window.KG = {"chapters":{"k:1":{"book":"kural","num":1,"title":"கடவுள் வாழ்த்து","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"An unnamed First One, known only through epithets, is made the ground of learning, freedom from anxiety and the crossing of the sea. The chapter's recurring claim is exclusive: without reaching those feet, these things are out of reach."},"k:2":{"book":"kural","num":2,"title":"வான்சிறப்பு","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Rain is the unacknowledged condition of everything: food, farming, the sea's life, worship, giving, austerity and conduct. The chapter makes virtue and the gods depend on a natural cause."},"k:3":{"book":"kural","num":3,"title":"நீத்தார் பெருமை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"The greatness of those who have renounced: immeasurable, grounded in mastery of the senses, and shown in the force of their word and anger. It ends by saying that the name அந்தணர் belongs to those whose conduct is cool grace toward all beings."},"k:4":{"book":"kural","num":4,"title":"அறன்வலியுறுத்தல்","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Virtue's power: nothing brings more gain, and forgetting it is the worst loss. The chapter defines it inwardly, as a clean mind free of four faults, and presses that it must be practised now and continually."},"k:5":{"book":"kural","num":5,"title":"இல்வாழ்க்கை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"The household life as the support of everyone else and as the fullest form of virtue. Having just exalted renunciants in chapter 3, the book now asks what they could gain by leaving home, and the commentators work hard to limit that challenge."},"k:6":{"book":"kural","num":6,"title":"வாழ்க்கைத் துணைநலம்","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"The wife as the household's real wealth. The chapter praises her steadfastness and even grants her power over the rain, yet it is told largely from the husband's side, and it measures her by what she makes possible for his life."},"k:7":{"book":"kural","num":7,"title":"புதல்வரைப் பெறுதல்","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Children are the greatest thing one can obtain: first for the fruit they bring beyond this life, then for sensuous joy in them, and finally for their worth in the world. It moves from possessing them to being known by them, and the last verse has the father judged by the son."},"k:8":{"book":"kural","num":8,"title":"அன்புடைமை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Love cannot be hidden, gives itself away to the bone, and is what makes a body alive. Without it, virtue burns a person and a life is a withered tree."},"k:9":{"book":"kural","num":9,"title":"விருந்தோம்பல்","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Hospitality is the reason for having a home and wealth at all. Hosting does not impoverish, refusing it makes a rich person poor, and it depends finally on the face with which a guest is met."},"k:10":{"book":"kural","num":10,"title":"இனியவைகூறல்","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Sweet speech is defined as loving, guileless and true, then ranked above generous giving. Its closing argument is almost one of common sense: why would anyone choose the unripe fruit?"},"k:11":{"book":"kural","num":11,"title":"செய்ந்நன்றி அறிதல்","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Gratitude: help is measured by its timing, its motive and the worth of the one who receives it, not by its size. Memory is ordered accordingly, so that good is kept and harm is let go, and ingratitude is called the one sin with no escape."},"k:12":{"book":"kural","num":12,"title":"நடுவு நிலைமை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Impartiality: holding to what is due, whoever is involved, and giving up any gain that requires leaving it. The chapter's central image is the balance, and it treats loss incurred by being fair as no real loss."},"k:13":{"book":"kural","num":13,"title":"அடக்கமுடைமை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Self-restraint is the treasure that decides one's end. It works through body, speech and mind, and the chapter is most vivid on the tongue, whose burn alone never heals."},"k:14":{"book":"kural","num":14,"title":"ஒழுக்கமுடைமை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Conduct is to be guarded above life itself. It is what high birth really consists of, it outranks study, and its final test is living in accord with the world."},"k:15":{"book":"kural","num":15,"title":"பிறனில் விழையாமை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Desire for another man's wife is the height of folly and a loss in every register at once. The chapter speaks to men, frames the woman as belonging to another, and calls restraint here the greatest manhood."},"k:16":{"book":"kural","num":16,"title":"பொறையுடைமை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Forbearance as strength: it wins the victory, it outlasts revenge's one day of pleasure, and for those who can bear harsh words it outranks fasting. Beyond it lies forgetting the wrong entirely."},"k:17":{"book":"kural","num":17,"title":"அழுக்காறாமை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Envy is a self-inflicted ruin that needs no enemy. The chapter insists that the envious never rise and the unenvious never fall, and yet it admits the world sometimes shows otherwise, which it says is to be pondered."},"k:18":{"book":"kural","num":18,"title":"வெஃகாமை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Coveting another's goods destroys one even in the wanting. Not wanting is presented as the real security of wealth, and it ends as a kind of pride and victory."},"k:19":{"book":"kural","num":19,"title":"புறங்கூறாமை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Backbiting is placed below open wrongdoing because it adds falsity to harm: the smile to the face after the slander behind the back. The remedy is simple and hard: to see one's own faults as one sees a stranger's."},"k:20":{"book":"kural","num":20,"title":"பயனில சொல்லாமை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Empty speech, especially before many, ruins the speaker's standing and even his claim to being fully human. The chapter ranks uselessness of speech as a graver failing than unkindness or lack of grace, and ends with a bare rule."},"k:21":{"book":"kural","num":21,"title":"தீவினையச்சம்","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Fear of evil deeds is presented as a mark of excellence and as enlightened self-interest. Harm done to others does not stay with them: it pursues the doer as surely as a shadow, so whoever loves himself will refrain."},"k:22":{"book":"kural","num":22,"title":"ஒப்புரவறிதல்","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"ஒப்புரவு is the obligation to make one's means serve the community, expecting nothing back as rain expects nothing. It is treated as the mark of being alive, and it holds even in poverty and at the cost of ruin."},"k:23":{"book":"kural","num":23,"title":"ஈகை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"True giving is giving to those who have nothing, and everything else is a kind of loan. The chapter ranks giving above reward, above ascetic endurance and even above life itself, and it argues from the giver's own joy and safety as well as from virtue."},"k:24":{"book":"kural","num":24,"title":"புகழ்","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Renown, earned above all through giving, is the one gain that outlasts the body. The chapter treats a life without it as a disgrace and a burden on the earth, indeed as no life at all."},"k:25":{"book":"kural","num":25,"title":"அருளுடைமை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"அருள், compassion extended to all living beings, is the highest wealth and the basis of other virtue. The chapter promises its possessor freedom from suffering and denies its lack any recovery, then closes by showing how it can be cultivated."},"k:26":{"book":"kural","num":26,"title":"புலான்மறுத்தல்","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Eating flesh is incompatible with அருள், and the chapter argues this like a debate, answering those who say that intent is what matters and those who say that the guilt lies only with the killer. It ends by exalting the one who neither kills nor eats."},"k:27":{"book":"kural","num":27,"title":"தவம்","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"தவம் is defined simply as bearing pain and harming no one, and is then credited with immense powers, over enemies, wishes, and even death. The chapter makes it the one truly self-regarding work and the reason so few prosper."},"k:28":{"book":"kural","num":28,"title":"கூடாவொழுக்கம்","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"The false renunciant, whose outward form hides an unrenounced heart, is exposed through a run of precise images. The chapter insists that conduct, not appearance, is the test, and ends by declaring the outward marks of renunciation unnecessary."},"k:29":{"book":"kural","num":29,"title":"கள்ளாமை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Theft begins as a thought, and its wish alone is an offence. The chapter sets களவு against அளவு, stealth against measure, as two settled dispositions that can't live together, and promises ruin to the one and heaven to the other."},"k:30":{"book":"kural","num":30,"title":"வாய்மை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Truthfulness is defined by what speech does rather than by whether it matches facts: speech without harm is truth, and a falsehood yielding faultless good counts as truth. The chapter then raises this virtue above austerity, giving, and every other good."},"k:31":{"book":"kural","num":31,"title":"வெகுளாமை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Anger is the self-destroying fire: it kills smiles and joy, burns the raft that would save a person, and returns on the one who holds it. Restraint counts only where anger could have had its way, and the one who drops it equals those who have renounced everything."},"k:32":{"book":"kural","num":32,"title":"இன்னாசெய்யாமை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Doing no hurt holds even when hurt would bring great wealth and even toward those who hurt first. The chapter's ground is self-knowledge, since a person who knows pain in his own life has no reason to inflict it, and it adds that hurt returns to its doer by the same day's end."},"k:33":{"book":"kural","num":33,"title":"கொல்லாமை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Not killing is the first of all goods, ranked explicitly above truthfulness and above all renunciation. It is absolute, holding even at the cost of one's own life and regardless of any gain, and the chapter ends with the fate of those who kill."},"k:34":{"book":"kural","num":34,"title":"நிலையாமை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Nothing lasts: wealth disperses like a theatre crowd, each day is a blade, and life is a lodger in the body. The error is mistaking this for permanence, and the practical conclusion is urgency: do good now."},"k:35":{"book":"kural","num":35,"title":"துறவு","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Renunciation is the removal of attachment to what is 'mine' and even to the 'I'. It begins with freedom from each thing's pain and reaches release, while those who keep any hold stay in the net. The chapter ends in the paradox that one must cling to get free of clinging."},"k:36":{"book":"kural","num":36,"title":"மெய்யுணர்தல்","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Seeing what is really so is what ends suffering and birth: mistaking the unreal for the real starts it, and stripping off appearances ends it. The chapter never says what மெய்ப்பொருள் is, and this silence is where its commentators divide most."},"k:37":{"book":"kural","num":37,"title":"அவாவறுத்தல்","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Craving is the seed of all birth and the sorrow of sorrows, and its removal, not any other act, is the direct cause of an unchanging state. The whole section on renunciation ends here, by locating release at the moment when wanting stops."},"k:38":{"book":"kural","num":38,"title":"ஊழ்","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Fate as the force behind wealth, understanding, and pleasure and pain. It reaches even the qualities a person might rely on against it, such as effort and intelligence."},"k:39":{"book":"kural","num":39,"title":"இறைமாட்சி","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"What makes a king excellent: not power alone but a set of resources and qualities that must be held without lapse, and above all how he is to the people who approach him. His standing is conferred from below, by those who praise him, shelter under him, and set him apart."},"k:40":{"book":"kural","num":40,"title":"கல்வி","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Learning as the thing that makes a person see, belong anywhere, and possess what can't be lost. It begins and is only complete in conduct: to learn is also to stand by what is learned."},"k:41":{"book":"kural","num":41,"title":"கல்லாமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"The emptiness of being unlearned: the unlearned person's speech, native wit, looks, wealth and rank all fail to count. The chapter is the harshest in the book on this point, and its contempt is largely about assemblies and public standing."},"k:42":{"book":"kural","num":42,"title":"கேள்வி","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Listening to the wise as a form of learning open even to those who haven't studied. The chapter treats the ear as an organ of nourishment and taste, rivalling the mouth, and it softens the verdict of the previous chapter."},"k:43":{"book":"kural","num":43,"title":"அறிவுடைமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Understanding as protection and as practice: it steers the mind, tests what is heard, moves with the world, foresees what is coming and knows what to fear. After three chapters on acquiring knowledge, this one asks what it is to have it."},"k:44":{"book":"kural","num":44,"title":"குற்றங்கடிதல்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"A ruler's real enemy is his own faults: arrogance, anger, desire, avarice, misplaced pride and self-regard. Small ones must be treated as large and dealt with before they arrive, and only one who has cleared his own is fit to judge others."},"k:45":{"book":"kural","num":45,"title":"பெரியாரைத் துணைக்கோடல்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"A ruler's power depends on those greater than himself whom he can win and keep, and above all on those who will correct him. The chapter counts this as the highest strength and calls its lack self-destruction."},"k:46":{"book":"kural","num":46,"title":"சிற்றினஞ்சேராமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Understanding and goodness are not sealed within a person; they take on the nature of the company one keeps, as water takes on its soil. The chapter gradually grants the mind its goodness while insisting that company still strengthens or spoils it."},"k:47":{"book":"kural","num":47,"title":"தெரிந்துசெயல்வகை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Acting well begins before the act: weighing cost and return, taking counsel, and matching the means to the person and to one's own position. The chapter's vocabulary is that of a careful ledger, and its fear is less of failure than of being laughed at for failing."},"k:48":{"book":"kural","num":48,"title":"வலியறிதல்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Knowing strength: the deed's, one's own, the opponent's and the allies', and treating wealth as a form of strength with a limit. The theme is limits, and most of the chapter warns against zeal, generosity and pride that outrun them."},"k:49":{"book":"kural","num":49,"title":"காலமறிதல்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Strength is not a fixed quantity but depends on when it is used. The chapter's real teaching is about waiting: holding back, even bowing and hiding one's anger, then striking without hesitation when the moment comes. Its stance is cold and tactical."},"k:50":{"book":"kural","num":50,"title":"இடனறிதல்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Power belongs to the ground it stands on. Through animals and vehicles, the chapter shows the strong undone off their own ground and the weak made strong on theirs, and it warns against attacking even people with nothing but their home."},"k:51":{"book":"kural","num":51,"title":"தெரிந்துதெளிதல்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"How a ruler comes to trust someone: test first and trust only after that, and once trust is settled, don't doubt it. The chapter doesn't expect the flawless. It looks at what predominates, and deeds are its final test."},"k:52":{"book":"kural","num":52,"title":"தெரிந்துவினையாடல்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Having tested someone, how to use them: match person, means, task and time, hand the work over fully, and don't resent the familiarity it brings, yet look at their conduct every day. Trust and scrutiny are both required, and the chapter doesn't entirely settle how."},"k:53":{"book":"kural","num":53,"title":"சுற்றந்தழால்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Holding kin close as a political and material matter: kin stay when wealth goes, their love multiplies gain, and wealth's use lies in being surrounded by them. They have to be kept through giving, soft speech and absence of anger, and they can be won back once estranged."},"k:54":{"book":"kural","num":54,"title":"பொச்சாவாமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Heedlessness, especially the forgetting that comes with joy and success, is more dangerous than rage. To keep in mind what one has resolved is the skill on which everything else depends, and it is an inward fort or an inward leak."},"k:55":{"book":"kural","num":55,"title":"செங்கோன்மை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Justice as the root of a king's power: the people live by his sceptre as the world lives by rain, and it wins him battles, brings rain and protects him in turn. The chapter ends by defending punishment, even death, as part of just rule."},"k:56":{"book":"kural","num":56,"title":"கொடுங்கோன்மை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Oppressive rule as a crime and a self-destruction: the king who extorts is a murderer and a highwayman, and his people's tears wear away his wealth. Its effects reach everything, turning wealth into a burden, and in the older reading failing the rain, the cows and the sacred books."},"k:57":{"book":"kural","num":57,"title":"வெருவந்தசெய்யாமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Punishment belongs to rule, but dread does not. Disproportion, inaccessibility, a harsh face and harsh words turn subjects' fear into the slow or sudden ruin of the one who causes it."},"k:58":{"book":"kural","num":58,"title":"கண்ணோட் டம்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Kindly regard is what eyes are for and what keeps the world's dealings going; eyes without it are wounds or dead wood. Yet the chapter also sets a limit on it, that it must not undo one's duty, and then praises enduring poison for its sake."},"k:59":{"book":"kural","num":59,"title":"ஒற்றாடல்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Intelligence-gathering is treated as the sight of the state and as a discipline of evidence: a spy must watch everyone and hold firm, and the king must not trust any one spy."},"k:60":{"book":"kural","num":60,"title":"ஊக்கமுடைமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Energy of mind is the only possession that truly belongs to a person. Wealth leaves and returns, but this inner drive sets how high one rises, and without it strength and even humanity count for little."},"k:61":{"book":"kural","num":61,"title":"மடியின்மை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Sloth is not a private weakness but the ruin of a lineage: it puts out the family's light, hands a person to enemies, and can be reversed only by the one who harbours it."},"k:62":{"book":"kural","num":62,"title":"ஆள்வினையுடைமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Sustained labour builds the doer, funds the virtue of giving and supports one's kin. Faced with fate, the chapter shifts the question: failure of fortune carries no blame, labour always earns its wage, and persistence can outlast what opposes it."},"k:63":{"book":"kural","num":63,"title":"இடுக்கணழியாமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Trouble need not break a person: it can be laughed at, thought away, outlasted, or expected as the body's lot, until suffering itself seems to suffer. The chapter is a series of stances, not one remedy, and it closes the book's treatment of the king."},"k:64":{"book":"kural","num":64,"title":"அமைச்சு","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"The minister is defined by what he can do: judge means and time, split and join alliances, unite innate acuity with learning and with the ways of the world. The chapter also assigns him a duty to speak even to a king who won't listen, and warns that a false minister is worse than a multitude of enemies."},"k:65":{"book":"kural","num":65,"title":"சொல்வன்மை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Speech is an excellence in its own right and a source of gain and ruin. Its mastery means fitting the hearer, being unanswerable, being brief when one can and expansive when one must; learning that can't be spoken has no fragrance."},"k:66":{"book":"kural","num":66,"title":"வினைத்தூய்மை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"The worth of an action lies in its cleanness: no gain, necessity or even a mother's hunger justifies what the worthy condemn, because tainted gain departs as it came and leaves ruin behind."},"k:67":{"book":"kural","num":67,"title":"வினைத்திட்பம்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Firmness in action is firmness of mind, and nothing else substitutes for it. Speaking is easy and doing is hard, but one who stays firm gets what he has thought of, however small he looks and whatever pain lies on the way."},"k:68":{"book":"kural","num":68,"title":"வினைசெயல்வகை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Deliberation ends in decision and decision must not stall; beyond that, the chapter gives a practical method of action and alliance, measured to whether one is stronger, an equal, or weaker than the other side."},"k:69":{"book":"kural","num":69,"title":"தூது","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"An envoy must combine love, knowledge, learning and presence with exact speech, courage and incorruptibility, and must carry his sovereign's interest to a foreign court even at the cost of his life."},"k:70":{"book":"kural","num":70,"title":"மன்னரைச் சேர்ந்தொழுதல்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Serving a king is like warming oneself at a fire: useful, dangerous, and demanding constant awareness of distance. The chapter gives rules for courtiers who can't presume on favour, age, kinship or old intimacy, and is candid about the danger behind the courtesy."},"k:71":{"book":"kural","num":71,"title":"குறிப்பறிதல்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Reading what is unspoken from face and eye is the highest social faculty. The chapter places this skill near the divine, treats the eyes that can't do it as useless, and holds that the face betrays what the heart holds."},"k:72":{"book":"kural","num":72,"title":"அவையறிதல்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Speech can't be judged apart from its hearers. One must read the assembly and adjust: shine among the bright, stay silent or whitewashed among the dull, step carefully among the great, and never spill nectar where it can't be received."},"k:73":{"book":"kural","num":73,"title":"அவையஞ்சாமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Fearlessness before an assembly is rarer than courage in battle, and it's the test of learning: knowledge that can't be spoken there is a sword in a frightened hand, and its holder counts for less than the unlearned and little more than the dead."},"k:74":{"book":"kural","num":74,"title":"நாடு","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"What makes a land deserve the name: people and yield that do not fail, resilience under burden and damage, and freedom from hunger, disease and faction. All of it is void if land and ruler are not in accord."},"k:75":{"book":"kural","num":75,"title":"அரண்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"The fort: what makes it hard to take and easy to hold, from terrain and walls to food, arms and fighters. Its last verse says all of this depends on the people inside knowing how to act."},"k:76":{"book":"kural","num":76,"title":"பொருள்செயல்வகை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"The making of wealth: it is the great social force that raises the despised, drives out enemies and brings virtue and pleasure within reach, but only wealth rightly gained deserves pursuit."},"k:77":{"book":"kural","num":77,"title":"படைமாட்சி","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"What makes an army: inherited courage, unity before death, honour, and discipline rather than numbers. It fails when the king neglects it or when it lacks leaders."},"k:78":{"book":"kural","num":78,"title":"படைச்செருக்கு","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"The warrior's pride, spoken largely from within: boast, disdain for easy prey, mercy as courage's edge, and death sought as honour. Its ethic is one of glory and is not softened by the rest of the Kural."},"k:79":{"book":"kural","num":79,"title":"நட்பு","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Friendship as a rare achievement and the strongest guard. It grows with use, lives in the heart rather than on the face, corrects as well as comforts, and moves to help as instinctively as a hand to a slipping garment."},"k:80":{"book":"kural","num":80,"title":"நட்பாராய்தல்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Testing friends before committing, because a friendship once formed should not be left. It asks what to test for, finds that ruin is the most reliable test, and accepts that bad friendships must sometimes be paid to end."},"k:81":{"book":"kural","num":81,"title":"பழைமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Old friendship as the freedom to take liberties with each other. It teaches welcoming what a friend presumes to do, reading hurt charitably, and staying even through harm the friend causes."},"k:82":{"book":"kural","num":82,"title":"தீ நட்பு","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"A catalogue of false friendship: the self-serving, the unreliable, fools, jesters and the two-faced. Its rhetoric of comparison ranks all of them below loneliness and even below enemies."},"k:83":{"book":"kural","num":83,"title":"கூடாநட்பு","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Friendship with one who is inwardly hostile: smiles, soft words, folded hands and even tears can hide a weapon. In the end the chapter tells the reader to answer such a person with the same outward show."},"k:84":{"book":"kural","num":84,"title":"பேதைமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Folly as choosing loss over gain. It is not ignorance as such: its worst form belongs to the learned who do not restrain themselves, and it spoils whatever the fool touches, whether work, wealth, friendship or an assembly."},"k:85":{"book":"kural","num":85,"title":"புல்லறிவாண்மை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Petty knowledge that thinks itself wise. It is the poverty that counts most, it does worse harm than any enemy, and it can't be taught, because the person who has it is already sure that he knows."},"k:86":{"book":"kural","num":86,"title":"இகல்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Hostility, as a disease of the disposition to quarrel. Giving it up is shown as gain and strength more than as saintliness, and pursuing it as a way to one's own ruin."},"k:87":{"book":"kural","num":87,"title":"பகைமாட்சி","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"The enemy worth having, seen coldly: pick the weak, and notice who makes himself easy to beat through anger, ignorance, stinginess and isolation. Read another way, its list of vices is a portrait of how not to become someone's easy enemy."},"k:88":{"book":"kural","num":88,"title":"பகைத்திறந்தெரிதல்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"How to handle enmity as a strategic matter. Don't seek it, avoid certain enemies above all, turn some into friends and others into neutrals, hide your weakness, and cut the rest down while they are still young."},"k:89":{"book":"kural","num":89,"title":"உட்பகை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"The enemy within the circle: kin and companions whose hostility is hidden. It is worse than an open foe because it is unseen, it wears a family down slowly, and even a sesame-sized amount of it holds ruin."},"k:90":{"book":"kural","num":90,"title":"பெரியாரைப் பிழையாமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Not offending the great: to slight those with real power is to call down one's own ruin, and no wealth, fort or ally protects against them. Who the great are is the key question, and the verses themselves mostly leave it open."},"k:91":{"book":"kural","num":91,"title":"பெண்வழிச்சேறல்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"A man who lets his wife's will or his desire for her govern him loses virtue, wealth, standing and even pleasure. The chapter treats household subordination as a failure of his own agency and of his duties to others, and it speaks throughout from the husband's side."},"k:92":{"book":"kural","num":92,"title":"வரைவின்மகளிர்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Women who sell intimacy are portrayed as a danger to men because their words and embrace are divided from their hearts. The chapter's argument is that the falseness is detectable, and that who touches them reveals the quality of the man. It gives no view from the women's side."},"k:93":{"book":"kural","num":93,"title":"கள்ளுண்ணாமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Drink destroys standing, shame and understanding, can't be hidden, and can't be argued away. The only exit the chapter offers is the drinker's own recognition during a sober moment."},"k:94":{"book":"kural","num":94,"title":"சூது","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Gambling is ruin even when it pays, because a win is the bait that holds the player. It takes wealth first, then truthfulness, compassion and the basics of living, and the attachment grows with each loss."},"k:95":{"book":"kural","num":95,"title":"மருந்து","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Medicine as a matter of measure. Illness comes from imbalance, the best remedy is eating with judgment so that no remedy is needed, and when illness does come, healing depends on careful diagnosis and on a whole system of people."},"k:96":{"book":"kural","num":96,"title":"குடிமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"High birth as a set of obligations and marks: integrity, shame, truthfulness, generous speech and giving, held to even in poverty. The chapter speaks as if birth produced these qualities, while its own tests make conduct the evidence of birth, and the commentators pull hard in opposite directions on that point."},"k:97":{"book":"kural","num":97,"title":"மானம்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Honour ranks above profit, fame and survival itself: one abandons even indispensable acts that would lower one, and one who has lost standing is as worthless as fallen hair. The chapter ends by praising those who would rather die than live degraded."},"k:98":{"book":"kural","num":98,"title":"பெருமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Greatness is not given by birth, rank or wealth. It consists of high aim, self-guarding, doing the rare thing, freedom from conceit and covering others' weakness, and smallness is the same set of features reversed."},"k:99":{"book":"kural","num":99,"title":"சான்றாண்மை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Fullness of character: the worthy hold all good qualities as their own, rest on five pillars, don't tell others' faults, and do good to those who harm them. This fullness doesn't depend on poverty or wealth, and the chapter finally places the earth's stability upon it."},"k:100":{"book":"kural","num":100,"title":"பண்புடைமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Courtesy of character, பண்பு, is what makes one fully human and keeps the world going. It reaches even to enemies, begins in being approachable, and without it sharp intelligence and great wealth come to nothing."},"k:101":{"book":"kural","num":101,"title":"நன்றியில்செல்வம்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Wealth that is neither enjoyed nor given is effectively no wealth. It does nothing for the holder, the needy or the holder's heirs, and it turns a person into something the world doesn't want. The chapter measures wealth entirely by its use."},"k:102":{"book":"kural","num":102,"title":"நாணுடைமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Shame over wrongdoing is the distinguishing mark of a human being and the seat of worth. It ranks above life, conduct and the whole earth, and those without it only imitate being alive."},"k:103":{"book":"kural","num":103,"title":"குடிசெயல்வகை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Raising one's people is the highest greatness. It requires ceaseless effort and knowledge, draws divine help and willing kin, ignores season and pride, and costs the one who does it his own bodily ease. A family with no one to carry it falls."},"k:104":{"book":"kural","num":104,"title":"உழவு","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Farming is the world's first occupation because everyone, including kings and renouncers, ends up depending on it. Those who live by it alone owe nothing to anyone, and the land itself responds to attention, as a wife or a mocking woman would."},"k:105":{"book":"kural","num":105,"title":"நல்குரவு","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Poverty as the worst suffering, one that has no comparison. It strips lineage, speech, learning and even a mother's regard, it returns day after day, and the chapter's last verse asks whether such a person should go on as they are."},"k:106":{"book":"kural","num":106,"title":"இரவு","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Asking is permitted, even graceful, when the giver is one who can't conceal. The chapter then goes further and says that the world needs askers, since without them giving, fame and even being alive would lose their point. It closes by telling the asker not to be angry at refusal."},"k:107":{"book":"kural","num":107,"title":"இரவச்சம்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Dread of begging: never asking, even from the best givers, is worth ten million times what asking could bring. The chapter's fury goes so far as a curse on whoever ordered the world so that begging is needed, and it holds the withholder guiltier still."},"k:108":{"book":"kural","num":108,"title":"கயமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"A bitterly ironic portrait of the base: they look perfectly human, are as free as gods, and are happier than the good because nothing troubles them. Only fear and greed move them, and at the first trouble they sell themselves. The chapter ends the whole Book of Wealth."},"k:109":{"book":"kural","num":109,"title":"தகையணங்குறுத்தல்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"The man's first sight of the woman, told as an injury: her beauty and returned gaze attack him like a goddess, an army, Death and a rutting elephant. The chapter treats love's beginning as the defeat of a strong man, and ends by claiming that love, unlike liquor, intoxicates through sight alone."},"k:110":{"book":"kural","num":110,"title":"குறிப்பறிதல்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"Reading desire that won't declare itself. The lovers communicate through lowered eyes, half-smiles and feigned indifference, until spoken words are declared useless."},"k:111":{"book":"kural","num":111,"title":"புணர்ச்சிமகிழ்தல்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"The man's joy in union, in which one person holds all five senses, cures the sickness she caused, and rivals heaven and the virtuous householder's meal. Its last verse compares this desire to knowledge, which only reveals how much more there is."},"k:112":{"book":"kural","num":112,"title":"நலம்புனைந்துரைத்தல்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"The man praises her beauty through deliberate hyperbole, defeating the standard comparisons: flowers, the moon, swan's down. As the praise goes on, it becomes a claim of exclusivity, and what is seen by many ceases to compare with what he alone sees."},"k:113":{"book":"kural","num":113,"title":"காதற்சிறப்புரைத்தல்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"Each lover declares what this love is. He makes it a bond of body and life, and she makes it an indwelling so complete that separation is impossible, which leaves the townspeople's talk of an absent, unloving man simply mistaken."},"k:114":{"book":"kural","num":114,"title":"நாணுத்துறவுரைத்தல்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"Declaring that shame has been given up. The man threatens to ride the palmyra horse, a public humiliation that forces the issue, while the woman, who may not do so, watches her love go into the streets on its own."},"k:115":{"book":"kural","num":115,"title":"அலரறிவுறுத்தல்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"The lovers turn the town's gossip around. Talk meant to shame and stop them feeds the love, keeps the man alive, and can even press for marriage or elopement. The chapter treats public scandal as an unintended gift."},"k:116":{"book":"kural","num":116,"title":"பிரிவாற்றாமை","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"The inability to bear parting. Mostly in the woman's voice, the chapter argues against his leaving before it happens: promises of a quick return are useless to one who won't survive, and a broken 'fear not' makes trust itself hard."},"k:117":{"book":"kural","num":117,"title":"படர்மெலிந்திரங்கல்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"The woman wastes away in grief through the night, caught between a love she can't hide and a shame that forbids telling him. Love is measured against the sea and against the night, and the only company that remains is the night itself."},"k:118":{"book":"kural","num":118,"title":"கண்விதுப்பழிதல்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"The woman puts her own eyes on trial. They showed her the man, so they caused the sickness; now they weep, can't sleep and betray her secret, and she answers with bitter laughter and a curse. Blaming a body part makes her suffering speakable."},"k:119":{"book":"kural","num":119,"title":"பசப்புறுபருவரல்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"The pallor of longing (பசலை), the woman's most visible symptom, personified as a creature that rides her body, moves in the instant he moves away, and earns her the town's blame while he escapes it. She ends by offering her body as the price of his good name."},"k:120":{"book":"kural","num":120,"title":"தனிப்படர்மிகுதி","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"The surfeit of lonely grief turns into an argument about reciprocity. Mutual love is the seedless fruit and the timely rain; one-sided love is a carrying pole weighted at one end. The chapter asks what love is worth if it isn't returned."},"k:121":{"book":"kural","num":121,"title":"நினைந்தவர்புலம்பல்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"Remembering the absent lover as both the only thing keeping one alive and a burning pain. The chapter starts with a man's claim that memory itself gives joy, and turns to a woman who wonders whether she exists in his thoughts at all."},"k:122":{"book":"kural","num":122,"title":"கனவுநிலையுரைத்தல்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"Dreams as the woman's way of staying alive. She argues that a lover who won't come when she is awake comes when she sleeps, and so waking life is the villain and the gossips are simply people who have never dreamed of a beloved."},"k:123":{"book":"kural","num":123,"title":"பொழுதுகண்டிரங்கல்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"Evening, once a friend, returns as an executioner. The woman speaks to the hour, quarrels with it and maps her sickness through the day, ending on the man whose nature, she says, has become wealth itself."},"k:124":{"book":"kural","num":124,"title":"உறுப்புநலனழிதல்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"The woman's body as a public record of his absence. Eyes, shoulders and forehead lose their beauty, and each speaker reads that record differently: the friend sees an accusation, the woman refuses to let it be one, and the man, far away and turning for home, sees a reason to hurry."},"k:125":{"book":"kural","num":125,"title":"நெஞ்சொடுகிளத்தல்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"The woman argues with her own heart about what to do: find a remedy, stop suffering, go to him, or forget him. The heart is a separate party that can be scolded, sent away and accused, and the chapter ends without any option having won."},"k:126":{"book":"kural","num":126,"title":"நிறையழிதல்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"The breaking of self-containment (நிறை). Desire is an axe against a door, a sneeze that can't be held back, a tyrant working her at midnight. The chapter ends with a woman who sets out to sulk and finds her heart has already gone over to him."},"k:127":{"book":"kural","num":127,"title":"அவர்வயின்விதும்பல்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"Impatience for reunion on both sides of the distance. She has worn down her fingers counting days and rehearses how she will greet him, and, on Parimelazhagar's reading, he is stuck on campaign and starting to fear he may come back too late."},"k:128":{"book":"kural","num":128,"title":"குறிப்பறிவுறுத்தல்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"Communicating what can't be said. Thread within crystal, scent within a bud, and the movement of her gaze from bangles to shoulders to feet: unspoken signs carry the fear of a new parting and the resolve to go with him. It is the book's mature counterpart to the first signs of chapter 110."},"k:129":{"book":"kural","num":129,"title":"புணர்ச்சிவிதும்பல்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"The eagerness for union overrides every plan to sulk. She can't see his faults once he is in view, and sets out to quarrel but her heart betrays her. The chapter's most quoted verse states why: love is more delicate than a flower, and few catch its moment."},"k:130":{"book":"kural","num":130,"title":"நெஞ்சொடுபுலத்தல்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"Quarrelling with one's own heart for taking the other side. The woman charges it with disloyalty for loving him when she wants to sulk, and the chapter ends in the man's voice, on Parimelazhagar's reading, with both lovers facing the same discovery: one's own heart may not be on one's side."},"k:131":{"book":"kural","num":131,"title":"புலவி","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"Sulking and its right measure. The quarrel is salt: just enough makes love tasty and a little more spoils it. Nothing holds it in check except that the other person cares, as the chapter's closing verses, in which the man finds her sulk has gone too far, make clear."},"k:132":{"book":"kural","num":132,"title":"புலவி நுணுக்கம்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"The fine points of sulking, a comedy of jealous logic. Every word he says, every sneeze, whether he hides it or not, whether he pleads or looks, is turned into evidence. It is the most purely entertaining chapter in the Kural, and it also shows how love trains two people in close reading."},"k:133":{"book":"kural","num":133,"title":"ஊடலுவகை","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"The joy of the quarrel, and the end of the Kural. Sulking is here set above heaven, and above union itself as digestion is above eating; the loser wins, and a man prays for the night to last. The whole work ends not on virtue or God but on a condition about an embrace."},"n:1":{"book":"naladiyar","num":1,"title":"செல்வம் நிலையாமை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Wealth does not stay with anyone: the feasting rich end up begging and kings lose their wives to enemies. Because death is near and certain, wealth should be shared and spent on virtue now, and the hoarder who neither enjoys nor gives has in effect already lost it."},"n:2":{"book":"naladiyar","num":2,"title":"இளமை நிலையாமை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Youth does not last: old age, the loss of friends and the decay of the beloved's body come to everyone, and death can take the young as well as the old. The wise therefore renounce early and do virtue while they have the means."},"n:3":{"book":"naladiyar","num":3,"title":"யாக்கை நிலையாமை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"The body does not last: kings die like everyone, wedding drums turn into funeral drums, and the body is a bubble, a passing cloud or dew on grass that the life within leaves behind. Because of this, the body should be used for its proper fruit, virtue and service, while it lasts."},"n:4":{"book":"naladiyar","num":4,"title":"அறன் வலியுறுத்தல்","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"An urging to virtue: human birth is rare and the body brief, so its fruit, virtue, must be gathered now. Those who live on the merit of past births without adding to it end at others' gates; those who have gathered it face death without grief, and even a small gift to the worthy grows vast."},"n:5":{"book":"naladiyar","num":5,"title":"தூய்தன்மை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"The body is impure: beneath skin, perfume and garlands it is a vessel of flesh, fluids and filth, and in death it is food for vultures and a grinning skull. Desire for a woman's body rests on not seeing this, and the renunciant who has seen it is not moved by the conventional praise of eyes and teeth."},"n:6":{"book":"naladiyar","num":6,"title":"துறவு","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Renunciation, argued from impermanence: the wise see that home, youth, wealth and pleasure do not last and that pleasure is mixed with pain, so they leave household life without delay, keep their vows under hardship, pity those who insult them, and master the senses to reach release."},"n:7":{"book":"naladiyar","num":7,"title":"சினம் இன்மை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Freedom from anger: the worthy let honour and insult pass alike, guard their tongues, bear harsh words from those beneath them and do not return harm, while their anger, when it comes, cools of itself. The base nurse and broadcast theirs."},"n:8":{"book":"naladiyar","num":8,"title":"பொறையுடைமை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Forbearance carried beyond insult into friendship and privation: avoid fools, bear inferiors, value a friend's harsh words, bear with or quietly leave an erring friend, starve rather than beg from the graceless, and refuse blameworthy pleasure, harm and falsehood even at the greatest cost."},"n:9":{"book":"naladiyar","num":9,"title":"பிறர்மனை நயவாமை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Not desiring another man's wife, argued mostly from consequences: constant fear, small pleasure, the king's punishment, family disgrace, hell and a degraded rebirth. The last quatrains turn from the act to desire itself, a fire worse than any outer fire, which the strong suppress in silence."},"n:10":{"book":"naladiyar","num":10,"title":"ஈகை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Giving: give gladly and daily within one's means, even when poor and even a grain's worth, to those who cannot repay, because death and age are near and wealth comes and goes by past merit, not by clutching. The fame of giving outlasts all else, and one who cannot give should at least not beg."},"n:11":{"book":"naladiyar","num":11,"title":"பழவினை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Old deeds (பழவினை) find their doer and settle each person's share of joy and suffering. Wishing, effort, holiness and learning cannot move that share, so the chapter ends by asking why anyone should grieve over it."},"n:12":{"book":"naladiyar","num":12,"title":"மெய்ம்மை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Things as they truly are (மெய்ம்மை): the hard facts of the world, as opposed to how people would like it to be. Natures do not change, kin come with fortune and leave with it, the rich are heard and the poor are not, and in the end only one's deeds go with one."},"n:13":{"book":"naladiyar","num":13,"title":"தீவினையச்சம்","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Dread of evil deeds (தீவினையச்சம்): harm done to creatures returns in kind, and bad company is itself a source of evil, turning even the upright. The chapter ends by urging the heart to give up household attachment, since only good done profits the soul."},"n:14":{"book":"naladiyar","num":14,"title":"கல்வி","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Learning (கல்வி) is the only true beauty, a medicine for delusion and the best legacy, and it outranks birth. Because life is short, one must choose what to learn, keep learned company, and prefer the books of wisdom that free one from confusion to merely worldly study."},"n:15":{"book":"naladiyar","num":15,"title":"குடிப்பிறப்பு","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Noble birth (குடிப்பிறப்பு) shows in conduct that holds through poverty: the well-born keep their principles, courtesy and generosity when they have nothing, while the low-born fail to do good even when they can. The chapter treats these qualities as belonging to birth."},"n:16":{"book":"naladiyar","num":16,"title":"மேன்மக்கள்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Portraits of the noble (மேன்மக்கள்): they cannot bear a stain, aim high whatever the outcome, stay within bounds in want, keep their speech clean under abuse and welcome those who come to ask. Wealth draws the base, but meeting the well-born is the real treasure."},"n:17":{"book":"naladiyar","num":17,"title":"பெரியாரைப் பிழையாமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Not wronging the great: their goodwill, once lost, cannot be restored, their anger reaches into any fortress, and their esteem is the only esteem worth having. Time with them is precious, and their bond grows where the bond of the small shrinks."},"n:18":{"book":"naladiyar","num":18,"title":"நல்லினம் சேர்தல்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Joining good company: it washes out the faults of youth, raises the lowly as river water sanctifies sewage, protects the weak and hides meanness. Life's pain makes the company of the wise the thing to seek, and bad company ruins even the pure."},"n:19":{"book":"naladiyar","num":19,"title":"பெருமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Greatness of character: it renounces when the season of giving and loving has passed, sees home and its supports as passing, and keeps giving even in decline. Its faults show more than others' crimes, it stays unshaken by slander, and it feeds others before itself."},"n:20":{"book":"naladiyar","num":20,"title":"தாளாண்மை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Energetic effort (தாளாண்மை): it makes a person, as a twig hardens into an elephant post, supports kin and an ageing father, and is what lineage really consists of. Manliness is persisting when things fail, and food won by one's own effort tastes better than a patron's feast."},"n:21":{"book":"naladiyar","num":21,"title":"சுற்றந்தழால்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Holding one's own close: the sympathy of kin dissolves suffering, the worthy bear everyone who leans on them without sorting people into ours and others, and plain food from those who love is sweeter than a feast from those who don't. The bond is proved when it goes into the fire with you."},"n:22":{"book":"naladiyar","num":22,"title":"நட்பாராய்தல்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Test a friend before taking him: by learning, by family, by loyalty under injury, by constancy, and by whether he actually helps. The reason is given at the end: once a bond is formed, parting hurts even when the friend was a snake."},"n:23":{"book":"naladiyar","num":23,"title":"நட்பிற் பிழை பொறுத்தல்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Once a friend has been chosen, bear their faults: everything has its husk, the patience of one keeps two in friendship, and one who exposes or pries into a friend's faults is worse than the friend."},"n:24":{"book":"naladiyar","num":24,"title":"கூடா நட்பு","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Friendships that don't fit: with those who stay only for their own ends, whose bond is shallow, who are of another nature, or who lack discernment. Such friendship fades, pains, and drags down even the discerning, while the company of the wise is like heaven."},"n:25":{"book":"naladiyar","num":25,"title":"அறிவுடைமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Understanding shown in conduct: restraint toward fallen foes and in poverty, judging people by mind and conduct rather than birthplace or kind, steadiness in friendship, and knowing that a person raises or lowers himself. It ends with the picture of a life that completes work, pleasure and virtue."},"n:26":{"book":"naladiyar","num":26,"title":"அறிவின்மை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Want of understanding (அறிவின்மை) is the real poverty. The unlearned and the base cannot take in good counsel, show themselves for what they are among the learned, and set up petty assemblies of their own, while the learned keep silent before those who would not absorb what they say."},"n:27":{"book":"naladiyar","num":27,"title":"நன்றியில் செல்வம்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Wealth that does no good (நன்றியில் செல்வம்): the riches of the base and ungiving are of no use to anyone near them, and the poor turn away to givers however far. The chapter also faces the scandal that wealth goes to the senseless while the good are poor, and explains it by former deeds."},"n:28":{"book":"naladiyar","num":28,"title":"ஈயாமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Not giving (ஈயாமை): to cook and eat is to share; the miser who neither gives nor enjoys is mocked by his own hoard, loses it to strangers and heirs, and is worse off than the poor. Giving must be done now, in whatever measure one can, and wealth held without giving is a house of suffering."},"n:29":{"book":"naladiyar","num":29,"title":"இன்மை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Poverty (இன்மை): the world honours a few coins over noble birth, kin and flatterers leave with wealth, and poverty strips away lineage, valour, learning, goodness and even sharpness of mind. For one who cannot help those who ask, the chapter says it is better to leave home and live as a guest or a beggar elsewhere."},"n:30":{"book":"naladiyar","num":30,"title":"மானம்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Honour (மானம்): those who have it burn at the arrogance and contempt of the rich, will not lay their need before the unworthy, keep away from patrons who demean them, and would rather die, or refuse heaven itself, than incur sin, blame or loss of honour. Honour is set in a scale of what people fear and is said to bring good in this world and the next."},"n:31":{"book":"naladiyar","num":31,"title":"இரவச்சம்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"The dread of begging (இரவச்சம்): a person of worth would rather go hungry and poor than ask, even of willing givers, because the mind melts at the thought of asking and a refusal kills. The only givers the chapter allows are those who welcome one in, and the last quatrain turns to the help owed to an old friend."},"n:32":{"book":"naladiyar","num":32,"title":"அவையறிதல்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Knowing the assembly (அவையறிதல்): where learning should be spoken and before whom. The worthy keep silent before the ignorant and the quarrelsome, while those who memorise texts without understanding them expose themselves. A book's meaning is harder than its letter, and only those who can explain it, by the full method of commentary, count as scholars."},"n:33":{"book":"naladiyar","num":33,"title":"புல்லறிவாண்மை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Petty understanding (புல்லறிவாண்மை): the small-minded scorn the teaching of virtue, see life slipping away and still do no good, nurse quarrels, hoard and put things off, until old age or death leaves them nothing. Folly is measured here by wasted time and wasted wealth."},"n:34":{"book":"naladiyar","num":34,"title":"பேதைமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Folly (பேதைமை): the fool enjoys a world in which death is already at work, puts off virtue, relies on birth and status, clings to the rich and the unworthy, praises himself and quarrels for nothing. Most quatrains are comparisons that make his position look absurd."},"n:35":{"book":"naladiyar","num":35,"title":"கீழ்மை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Baseness (கீழ்மை): the base keep their nature whatever is done for them. Teaching, invitation, honour, wealth and care do not change them, and the chapter sets them beside the worthy, whom prosperity does not change either."},"n:36":{"book":"naladiyar","num":36,"title":"கயமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"The base (கயமை): their nature is not changed by age, learning or wealth. They give only under force, remember insults and forget kindnesses, speak ill of others to their faces, and hold their wealth without softening."},"n:37":{"book":"naladiyar","num":37,"title":"பன்னெறி","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Various ways (பன்னெறி): a closing miscellany of the Book of Wealth. It weighs the wife as what makes a house a home or a ruin, ranks the lives a person may lead with austerity first, and observes that sons inherit their fathers' minds, that the world turns upside down, and that the unfeeling are better dead."},"n:38":{"book":"naladiyar","num":38,"title":"பொது மகளிர்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"Courtesans (பொது மகளிர்): their love lasts as long as a man's money and turns hostile when it ends. The chapter blames the men who trust them as much as the women, calling them beasts, dupes and people whose bodies are full of sin."},"n:39":{"book":"naladiyar","num":39,"title":"கற்புடை மகளிர்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"The chaste wife (கற்புடை மகளிர்): her worth is measured by self-guarding, hospitality in poverty, modesty that never wears off, and timely sulking. In the last four quatrains she speaks herself, as a wife wronged by a husband who goes to courtesans."},"n:40":{"book":"naladiyar","num":40,"title":"காமநுதலியல்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"The nature of love (காமநுதலியல்): love needs both union and the sulking that interrupts it, and its pain is separation, felt most at evening and in the rains. The chapter ends with a girl's flight with her lover, seen by her mother and herself, and a woman's grief at a lover gone to seek wealth."},"t:1":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1,"title":"கடவுள் வாழ்த்து","section":"பாயிரம்","theme":"Invocation of God (கடவுள் வாழ்த்து): Siva, often named Nandi, is one and many, beyond the gods who cannot measure him and without equal, yet near to those who love him; the chapter urges praise in any form and promises that grace answers it."},"t:2":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2,"title":"வேதச் சிறப்பு","section":"பாயிரம்","theme":"The greatness of the Veda (வேதச் சிறப்பு): there is no dharma outside the Veda, the Lord spoke it to show the true reality, and Vedanta teaches the one path that joins the guru's way to Siva's. Yet those who recite it seldom live by its qualities and turn it to gain."},"t:3":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":3,"title":"ஆகமச் சிறப்பு","section":"பாயிரம்","theme":"The greatness of the Agamas (ஆகமச் சிறப்பு): the Lord gave the Siva Agamas by grace, through a chain from Siva to Nandi; they are past counting, yet without the knowledge the Lord declared they are writing on water. He spoke them in Sanskrit and Tamil alike, and both languages make him known."},"t:4":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":4,"title":"குரு பாரம்பரியம்","section":"பாயிரம்","theme":"The line of gurus (குரு பாரம்பரியம்): Tirumular names the masters who received Nandi's grace, himself among eight, and the seven disciples who carry his mantra on; everything he has, he says, came by Nandi's grace, and the Lord charged the four Nandis with keeping the observances."},"t:5":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":5,"title":"திருமூலர் வரலாறு","section":"பாயிரம்","theme":"Tirumular's own story (திருமூலர் வரலாறு): with Nandi's feet on his head he came from Kailasa, saw the dance, served the Goddess, and stayed at Avaduthurai beneath the bodhi tree for ages, in this body. The Lord made him to sing the Lord in Tamil, and he wishes the bliss he gained on the whole world."},"t:6":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":6,"title":"அவையடக்கம்","section":"பாயிரம்","theme":"The poet's modesty before the assembly (அவையடக்கம்): no one knows the Lord's greatness or the root of the nameless flame, and Tirumular disclaims the skill of those who sing, dance and seek; yet the Lord can be known by those who contemplate him, though even sages and gods may miss the fruit of the knowledge of tattvas."},"t:7":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":7,"title":"திருமந்திரத் தொகைச் சிறப்பு","section":"பாயிரம்","theme":"The greatness of the book's number (திருமந்திரத் தொகைச் சிறப்பு): Mulan's three thousand Tamil verses are Nandi's grace given for the world to know, and those who recite them each morning with understanding reach the Lord."},"t:8":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":8,"title":"குரு மட வரலாறு","section":"பாயிரம்","theme":"The history of the guru mathas (குரு மட வரலாறு): seven lines of teaching descend in the true path down to Mulan's own, and he spoke the Agama in nine tantras and three thousand verses; the second verse names founders of the lines."},"t:9":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":9,"title":"திரு மும்மூர்த்திகளின் முறைமை","section":"பாயிரம்","theme":"The order of the three gods (மும்மூர்த்திகளின் முறைமை): Siva is the one source, and Brahma and Vishnu are his aspects or parts, holding their place from him; those who split the three into rival gods and quarrel over them have not understood."},"t:10":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":10,"title":"உபதேசம்","section":"முதல் தந்திரம்","theme":"Teaching (உபதேசம்): the Lord comes down as the guru and burns away the soul's impurity, which is beginningless like the soul; through grace and Siva-yoga the freed come to rest in Sivam, in a state of stillness, 'sleep' and silence that cannot be told. The means offered are the guru's form, name and word and Nandi's feet."},"t:11":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":11,"title":"யாக்கை நிலையாமை","section":"முதல் தந்திரம்","theme":"The impermanence of the body (யாக்கை நிலையாமை): the body is a clay pot, a thatched hut, a walled temple that falls apart; when the life that dwelt in it leaves, kin weep, burn it, bathe and forget, and only vows and knowledge go with the dead."},"t:12":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":12,"title":"செல்வம் நிலையாமை","section":"முதல் தந்திரம்","theme":"The impermanence of wealth (செல்வம் நிலையாமை): wealth wanes like the moon, sinks like a capsizing boat and is taken by the stronger, as honey is taken from bees; the lasting wealth is the Lord, and one who gives up his wealth and thinks of him need not fear Death."},"t:13":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":13,"title":"இளமை நிலையாமை","section":"முதல் தந்திரம்","theme":"The impermanence of youth (இளமை நிலையாமை): the sun's daily course and the calf grown old show that youth is passing, yet people do not take it in; the time to think of Siva and praise him is now, while life and youth remain."},"t:14":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":14,"title":"உயிர் நிலையாமை","section":"முதல் தந்திரம்","theme":"The impermanence of life (உயிர் நிலையாமை): the life dwells in the body as a king in a drum or a lord over five guardians of a field, and leaves when its summons comes; so praise the Lord, do good, and share your food while there is time."},"t:15":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":15,"title":"கொல்லாமை","section":"முதல் தந்திரம்","theme":"Not killing (கொல்லாமை) is the finest offering in worship, and those who order killing are dragged to hell."},"t:16":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":16,"title":"புலால் மறுத்தல்","section":"முதல் தந்திரம்","theme":"Refusing flesh (புலால் மறுத்தல்): those who eat flesh are thrown into hell, and those who give up killing, theft, lust and lying and reach Siva's feet are free of them in the bliss of knowledge."},"t:17":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":17,"title":"பிறன்மனை நயவாமை","section":"முதல் தந்திரம்","theme":"Not desiring another man's wife (பிறன்மனை நயவாமை): a man who leaves his own wife for another's trades a sweet fruit for a poor one and falls; and even the learned and the yogis, once deluded by women, cannot turn the mind back."},"t:18":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":18,"title":"மகளிர் இழிவு","section":"முதல் தந்திரம்","theme":"Against infatuation with women (மகளிர் இழிவு): their charm is like a fair-leaved poison tree, the pleasure is a dream and a whirlpool, their attachment shifts, and the sweetness at first turns bitter in the body."},"t:19":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":19,"title":"நல்குரவு","section":"முதல் தந்திரம்","theme":"Poverty (நல்குரவு) strips a man of livelihood, kin and standing; but the deeper want is a pit that goods cannot fill, filled only when impurity is gone, by praising the Lord and lighting the lamp of awareness."},"t:20":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":20,"title":"அக்கினி காரியம்","section":"முதல் தந்திரம்","theme":"The fire rite (அக்கினி காரியம்): the Brahmins' oblation sustains sky, earth and gods and burns away deeds, and the Lord is within the sacrificial fire as the light in the heart."},"t:21":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":21,"title":"அந்தண ரொழுக்கம்","section":"முதல் தந்திரம்","theme":"The conduct of Brahmins (அந்தணர் ஒழுக்கம்): a Brahmin is one who keeps the rites and through them reaches Vedanta, where craving ends; thread, tuft and birth without truth, knowledge and devotion don't make one."},"t:22":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":22,"title":"அரசாட்சி முறை (இராச தோடம்)","section":"முதல் தந்திரம்","theme":"The order of kingship (அரசாட்சி முறை): an unlearned king is worse than Death; the king must follow and guard the way of austerity, test and punish false holy men and the faithless, protect the helpless, and take his sixth."},"t:23":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":23,"title":"வானச் சிறப்பு","section":"முதல் தந்திரம்","theme":"The excellence of rain (வானச் சிறப்பு): rain flowing with nectar makes every tree, sweet and bitter, bear nectar, and the Lord's clear river springs within the heart like rain falling from a mountain."},"t:24":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":24,"title":"தானச் சிறப்பு","section":"முதல் தந்திரம்","theme":"The excellence of giving (தானச் சிறப்பு): give to anyone without discrimination, look for others before eating, don't hoard, and learn from the crow that calls its kin before it eats."},"t:25":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":25,"title":"அறஞ்செய்வான் திறம்","section":"முதல் தந்திரம்","theme":"The way of one who does virtue (அறஞ்செய்வான் திறம்): virtue is within everyone's reach, a leaf for God, a mouthful for a cow, a handful before eating, a kind word; it must be done before Death comes, and austerity and virtue are the boat across the sea of deeds."},"t:26":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":26,"title":"அறஞ்செயான் திறம்","section":"முதல் தந்திரம்","theme":"The way of one who does not do virtue (அறஞ்செயான் திறம்): the hoarder's wealth is poison fruit, the unvirtuous see bodies perish and still learn nothing, disease and hell await them; joy and sorrow come from past deeds, so give and praise the Lord instead of flattering the rich."},"t:27":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":27,"title":"அன்புடைமை","section":"முதல் தந்திரம்","theme":"Having love (அன்புடைமை): love and Siva are not two; austerity without melting love cannot reach him, but he stands in the love of those who praise him, dwells within love, and is the near support of those who live in it."},"t:28":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":28,"title":"அன்பு செய்வாரை அறியும் சிவன்","section":"முதல் தந்திரம்","theme":"Siva knows those who love him (அன்பு செய்வாரை அறியும் சிவன்): he sees what each has spurned and gained and returns love with grace; siddhas cannot find him but he stands before devotees; claims to know him through love ring hollow without true understanding, and mingling with him inwardly is the true worship."},"t:29":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":29,"title":"கல்வி","section":"முதல் தந்திரம்","theme":"Learning (கல்வி): true learning is learning to hold the Lord. It brings him into the heart, gives the learned an eye, cuts away sin when joined to worship, and goes with one as a companion, while clinging to one text or faction in delusion, or having no learning, leaves one without support."},"t:30":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":30,"title":"கேள்வி கேட்டமைதல்","section":"முதல் தந்திரம்","theme":"Being settled by listening (கேள்வி கேட்டமைதல்): hearing about the Lord, his deeds and his grace, then reciting and realising it, leads to the Siva-state and makes people like gods; but what counts is knowing him with longing, not hearing or reckoning alone."},"t:31":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":31,"title":"கல்லாமை","section":"முதல் தந்திரம்","theme":"Lack of learning (கல்லாமை), where learning means knowing Siva: the unlearned take the passing for the lasting, cannot see the Lord in their hearts and suffer their deeds, while those who know the true reckoning have truly learned; book learning without Siva-knowledge or love is itself a kind of unlearning."},"t:32":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":32,"title":"நடுவு நிலைமை","section":"முதல் தந்திரம்","theme":"Standing in the middle (நடுவு நிலைமை): those who stand in the middle gain knowledge, escape hell and become gods, jnanis and even the Lord, and the poet stands with them; the last verse defines them as those who cling to the Lord's feet and name."},"t:33":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":33,"title":"கள்ளுண்ணாமை","section":"முதல் தந்திரம்","theme":"Not drinking liquor (கள்ளுண்ணாமை): liquor and lust confuse, destroy truth and understanding and keep people from knowing soul and bond; the sects that drink for power lose it by forgetting themselves. The drink worth taking is the unceasing nectar of Siva's bliss, reached in samadhi, yoga and the union of Siva and Shakti."},"t:34":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":34,"title":"அகத்தியம்","section":"இரண்டாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Agastya (அகத்தியம்): when the world tilted and was failing, the Lord sent Agastya south to set it right, and Agastya, with sages in the west and north, keeps the fire rising so that the light gives plenty everywhere."},"t:35":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":35,"title":"பதிவலியில் வீரட்டம் எட்டு","section":"இரண்டாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The eight heroic deeds of the Lord's might (பதிவலியில் வீரட்டம் எட்டு): the slaying of the asura, the punishing of Prajapati and Brahma, the overthrow of Jalandhara, the three cities, the elephant from the fire, Death at Kadavur and Kama at Korukkai. From the three cities on, Tirumular reads the deeds inwardly: the cities are the work of the three impurities, Death is burned in yoga, Kama destroyed by restraint."},"t:36":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":36,"title":"இலிங்க புராணம்","section":"இரண்டாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Stories of the Linga Purana (இலிங்க புராணம்): the mountain's daughter, Vishnu and Brahma, Ravana, Chandesa and the gods each worshipped or submitted and received the Lord's grace; the Lord who destroyed the three cities is not hard to reach, and is not false to those who love him."},"t:37":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":37,"title":"தக்கன் வேள்வி","section":"இரண்டாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Daksha's sacrifice (தக்கன் வேள்வி): a rite performed without Siva breaks down when he is angered, and the gods who took part are maimed for lack of his grace; yet Siva is the power within the very fire of the sacrifice, and after his anger he gave grace, so the devotee need not waver."},"t:38":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":38,"title":"பிரளயம்","section":"இரண்டாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The deluge (பிரளயம்): when the flood covers the world and the gods contend, Siva alone stands as light and mountain above the waters, tells the gods not to fear, and keeps the sea within bounds; the gods praise him without knowing his purpose."},"t:39":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":39,"title":"சக்கரப்பேறு","section":"இரண்டாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Receiving the discus (சக்கரப்பேறு): Vishnu's discus comes from Siva, who made and owns all things; Vishnu could not bear it until he worshipped Siva, who divided its power between Vishnu, Shakti and himself; at Daksha's sacrifice the discus Vishnu hurled at Siva's side was useless."},"t:40":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":40,"title":"எலும்பும் கபாலமும்","section":"இரண்டாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Bone and skull (எலும்பும் கபாலமும்): Siva rises bearing bones and a skull, and it is his bearing them that keeps the gods' bones and skulls from crumbling into earth."},"t:41":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":41,"title":"அடிமுடி தேடல்","section":"இரண்டாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The search for the head and feet (அடிமுடி தேடல்): when Brahma and Vishnu each claim to be the Lord, Siva stands before them as a boundless column of fire whose crown and feet neither can find; he is flesh, life and awareness and beyond sun and moon, and is reached not by those who worship for gifts but by those who give themselves to him."},"t:42":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":42,"title":"படைத்தல்","section":"இரண்டாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Creation (படைத்தல்): from the beginningless Supreme come Shakti, nada and bindu, and from bindu, as Maya, the tattvas, elements and worlds. The gods who create and keep the worlds act within this order; the Lord alone, with his Shakti, is said to create, protect and remove the worlds and to be their life."},"t:43":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":43,"title":"காத்தல்","section":"இரண்டாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Protection (காத்தல்): the Lord keeps the world by entering and becoming it: space and darkness, praise and blame, body, life, elements and seasons, love and restraint. He shapes the world and its births as a potter shapes pots, and he holds each life in its body so that it is not cast off."},"t:44":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":44,"title":"அழித்தல்","section":"இரண்டாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Destruction (அழித்தல்): the Lord burns the world, the sea and the asuras with fire as his arrow, and the worlds are consumed like rice in the pot. Yet dissolution is also inward and saving: four kinds of saṃhāra run from daily sleep to the dissolution that is the truth of grace, and what returns to the void leaves the void itself unharmed."},"t:45":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":45,"title":"மறைத்தல்","section":"இரண்டாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Concealment (மறைத்தல்), the fourth of the Lord's five acts: the Lord dwells in the heart and in everything he has become, yet the heart does not know his form; he shuts the soul into a body of bone and flesh within the bond of pasa, and even the three gods do not know how he did it. Like the eye that sees all but not itself, he is present and unseen."},"t:46":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":46,"title":"அருளல்","section":"இரண்டாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Grace (அருளல்), the fifth of the Lord's five acts: the Lord who gladly made the worlds, the ages, the elements, the gods and the souls, and who binds and unbinds the body, stays near the mind of those who turn to him; he is in the body as milk is in water, and to see him there is bliss."},"t:47":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":47,"title":"கரு உற்பத்தி","section":"இரண்டாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The making of the embryo (கரு உற்பத்தி): the Lord, knowing each soul's due, builds its body in the womb from the tattvas and the breaths through the union of the parents; the state of breath and seed at conception decides sex, lifespan and defects; the parents do not know what they make, and the Lord, present though unseen in the small body, shapes and protects it by grace."},"t:48":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":48,"title":"மூவகைச்சீவ வர்க்கம்","section":"இரண்டாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The three classes of souls (மூவகைச் சீவ வர்க்கம்): souls are ranked by the impurities that bind them, the Vijnanakalas by anava alone, the Pralayakalas by anava and karma, the Sakalas by all three; Shakti and Siva yoke souls to maya in their play so as to lead them to Sivahood, and those who destroy the malas become Siva."},"t:49":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":49,"title":"பாத்திரம்","section":"இரண்டாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The worthy recipient (பாத்திரம்): a gift as small as a sesame seed to a knower of Siva yields release, powers and the highest joy, while gold as vast as the earth given to fools bears no fruit. The other verses describe those who know the Lord, and only loosely fit the title."},"t:50":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":50,"title":"அபாத்திரம்","section":"இரண்டாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The unworthy recipient (அபாத்திரம்): a gift to those without discipline, vow and love of the Lord is wasted, like milking a barren cow or a crop sown out of season, and can drag giver and receiver alike to hell; given to the Lord, the pure guru and the desireless, it saves even a great sinner."},"t:51":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":51,"title":"தீர்த்தம்","section":"இரண்டாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Holy water (தீர்த்தம்): the true sacred waters are within the heart, where the Lord already dwells, and those of deceitful mind who wander from river to tank to wash away their deeds are seeking in a pond what they lost in the sea."},"t:52":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":52,"title":"திருக்கோயில்","section":"இரண்டாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The sacred temple (திருக்கோயில்): moving a fixed lingam, taking a stone from a temple wall, letting its rites lapse or having them done by an unfit priest brings ruin on the king and the land: disease, failed rain, theft and famine. Each verse ends on Nandi's word or command."},"t:53":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":53,"title":"அதோமுக தெரிசனம்","section":"இரண்டாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The vision of the downward face (அதோமுக தெரிசனம்): Siva's sixth face, turned downward, sent the six-faced Skanda against the asura, bears the cosmos and its quarters, and is the Lord of the aeon and the endless Shakti; his dark throat has a meaning that those who say 'he drank poison' miss."},"t:54":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":54,"title":"சிவ நிந்தை","section":"இரண்டாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Reviling Siva (சிவ நிந்தை): those who scorn the Lord as easy fall as a parrot falls to a cat; gods and asuras who opposed him perished, and even Vedic Brahmins who dwell on pleasure forget him. Enmity toward him, even feigned, rebounds many times over."},"t:55":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":55,"title":"குரு நிந்தை","section":"இரண்டாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Reviling the guru (குரு நிந்தை): those who wound the teacher who gave a single syllable of mantra are reborn as dogs and worms; troubling the devotees, the chaste and the knowers ruins life, wealth, land and even Indra's throne; and to leave the guru's knowledge for ritual is to drop a gem and carry a stone."},"t:56":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":56,"title":"மயேசுர நிந்தை","section":"இரண்டாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Reviling the Lord's devotees (மயேசுர நிந்தை): the devotees stand apart from everyone and live on alms, and those who speak ill of them fall into low hell; when a jnani is reviled or worshipped, the deeds are divided and he enters Siva's bliss."},"t:57":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":57,"title":"பொறையுடைமை","section":"இரண்டாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Forbearance (பொறையுடைமை), on the title's word: in the attached heart lies a lizard that grips the senses, and checking it is great patience. The other verses turn to the Lord's goodness, the grace that kings and jnanis reach, and the dancer's endless rhythm; their link to forbearance is not plain."},"t:58":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":58,"title":"பெரியாரைத் துணைகோடல்","section":"இரண்டாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Taking the great as one's support (பெரியாரைத் துணைகோடல்): the poet resolves to keep company with those who run, sing, seek and join the Lord, bids his faltering heart come with him, and tells how, going with the devotees' devotees, he was announced at the Lord's gate and called in. Joining the great is supreme bliss."},"t:59":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":59,"title":"அட்டாங்க யோகம்","section":"மூன்றாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The eightfold yoga (அட்டாங்க யோகம்): Nandi set out yama and niyama and the rest; the poet will teach the way from them to samadhi with its rites of armour, placement and gesture. Without wavering between paths, one should follow the eight limbs into samadhi, for the path of sacrifice leads nowhere."},"t:60":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":60,"title":"இயமம்","section":"மூன்றாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Yama (இயமம்), the first limb: the Lord taught the four sages to keep the yamas in every direction even if the waters rise, and the man of yama is one who does not kill, lie or steal, is good and restrained, acts justly, shares his food, is stainless and free of drink and lust."},"t:61":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":61,"title":"நியமம்","section":"மூன்றாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Niyama (நியமம்), the second limb: the man of niyama knows the First, the meaning of the Veda, the light and the fire, with Parashakti in his half; he keeps ten disciplines of conduct, and ten of practice: austerity, mantra repetition, contentment, faith, giving, Siva's vow, hearing Siddhanta, sacrifice, Siva puja and clear mind."},"t:62":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":62,"title":"ஆதனம்","section":"மூன்றாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Asana (ஆதனம்): of the many postures, a few are named and described, the lotus, the auspicious, the cock and the lion, with svastika as the steady seat in which one becomes master; the closing verse lists the chief seven and counts the postures in the hundreds."},"t:63":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":63,"title":"பிராணாயாமம்","section":"மூன்றாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Pranayama (பிராணாயாமம்): the breath is a horse that throws the false and carries the true, mastered only by the guru's grace; held with the mind and measured in inhaling, holding and exhaling, it stops birth and death, keeps the body young, keeps Siva within and kicks away Death."},"t:64":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":64,"title":"பிரத்தியாகாரம்","section":"மூன்றாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Pratyahara (பிரத்தியாகாரம்): drawing the mind inward from the senses, the yogi sees within the one the Vedas searched for; the chapter locates the inner places, below the navel, between the lower openings, where kundalini and the light stand, and defines withdrawal as separating the light from what limits it."},"t:65":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":65,"title":"தாரணை","section":"மூன்றாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Dharana (தாரணை): fixing the mind and breath within, through the spine to the head, the yogi sees the dance and the light of bliss, rouses the power at the base, and holds life, body and time still; since all who came before have died, the work is now, and its end is holding the dissolved tattvas in the Supreme."},"t:66":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":66,"title":"தியானம்","section":"மூன்றாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Meditation (தியானம்), the seventh limb: the gaze turned inward to the light at the palate, between the brows or at the tip of the nose, the ten inner sounds heard only by the devoted, and Siva found at the end of nada. Done rightly it steadies mind and breath, keeps the body and, by grace, takes the devotee past the tattvas and maya."},"t:67":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":67,"title":"சமாதி","section":"மூன்றாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Samadhi (சமாதி), the eighth limb, is reached through the limbs from yama on: mind and breath dissolve together, bindu and nada rise, the gaze is turned back, and the yogi joins the Supreme in a cool absorption where Siva is the soul within. Yet for one who goes with the Lord no samadhi is needed."},"t:68":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":68,"title":"அட்டாங்கயோகப் பேறு","section":"மூன்றாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The attainment of eight-limbed yoga (அட்டாங்கயோகப் பேறு): those who love Siva's feet and practise reach the Siva-state, where the gods come out to meet them, hail them as their lord or as Hara himself, and they dwell in bliss by the Lord's grace. The chapter ends by defining samadhi as joining the Supreme beyond all limiting adjuncts."},"t:69":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":69,"title":"அட்டமா சித்தி","section":"மூன்றாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The eight great siddhis (அட்டமா சித்தி): powers such as becoming minute, light, heavy or all-pervading, and entering another body, said to come by eightfold yoga, by holding the breath in the central channel and by a year's union with a form of Shakti. The chapter frames them with grace: the siddhis come when Shakti grants them, the Lord who cuts off birth gives them, and walking the world finds nothing that love does not."},"t:70":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":70,"title":"கலை நிலை","section":"மூன்றாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The state of the kalas (கலை நிலை): by love turned toward Siva and by breath held steady along the kalas, the yogi can guard the inner faculties and the body, find the Lord and his Shakti dwelling within so that the body becomes Siva's temple, and by tests of the breath know when the body's end approaches and how to put it off."},"t:71":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":71,"title":"காயசித்தி உபாயம்","section":"மூன்றாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The means of perfecting the body (காயசித்தி உபாயம்): since the body is the ground of life and of true knowledge, and the Lord has made it his temple, it is to be nurtured, not despised; breath control, mantra, the raising of the vital airs, restraint of food and union with Shakti are given as the means by which the body is cleansed, made young and kept from death."},"t:72":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":72,"title":"கால சக்கரம்","section":"மூன்றாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The wheel of time (கால சக்கரம்): a life is measured out in naazhigai, days, lunar mansions and years, and most people perish without noticing the measure run out. The yogi who rises along the inner staff to the crown outlasts these measures, escapes Yama, and finds the Lord within as the syllable Om and in the seven circles."},"t:73":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":73,"title":"ஆயுள் பரிட்சை","section":"மூன்றாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Testing the span of life (ஆயுள் பரிட்சை): signs of the body and counts of the breath on its days are said to tell how many years, months or days remain. Around the counts stand verses on the Lord found beyond sound, on watching the failing breath, and on knowing the knower, by which the bond loosens and grace is given."},"t:74":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":74,"title":"வாரசரம்","section":"மூன்றாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The breath by weekday (வாரசரம்): on each day of the week the breath should flow through the left or the right nostril, and the yogi who knows the rule, and turns the breath that runs against it, keeps the body sound and comes to bliss."},"t:75":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":75,"title":"வாரசூலம்","section":"மூன்றாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The trident by weekday (வாரசூலம்): each day of the week has a direction in which the inauspicious trident stands, and a journey made with it behind or to the left brings no grief, while one made with it ahead or to the right brings trouble."},"t:76":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":76,"title":"கேசரி யோகம்","section":"மூன்றாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Khechari yoga (கேசரி யோகம்): the tongue turned up into the palate and the breath held there make nectar flow from above, so that death, age and evil deeds lose their hold; the body becomes a lit temple where the Lord and Shakti dwell, and the yogi gains powers such as far sight."},"t:77":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":77,"title":"பரியண்-க யோகம்","section":"மூன்றாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Paryanka yoga (பரியங்க யோகம், the yoga of the couch): sexual union of man and woman made a discipline, in which the breath is held and the bindu, the 'silver', is not let fall, so that union yields bliss, mastery and long life; Siva himself is its first practitioner. The chapter ends in the space, the light and the rootless lotus known within."},"t:78":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":78,"title":"அமுரிதாரணை","section":"மூன்றாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Amuri dharana (அமுரிதாரணை): a water held within the body, called the drinking-water of strength and Siva-water, is praised as a medicine without rival. Drunk, alone or with pepper, gooseberry, turmeric and neem, it is said to make the body golden and supple, restore the hair, end grey hair and wrinkles and keep Yama away; Nandi taught it as a medicine of light that cannot be told."},"t:79":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":79,"title":"சந்திர யோகம்","section":"மூன்றாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Moon yoga (சந்திர யோகம்): the body holds the kalas of moon, sun and fire, which with the stars make ninety-six, and the inner moon waxes and wanes as the outer one does. By knowing the fortnights, setting the breaths of sun and moon, joining moon, sun and fire, raising the power from the base through the central channel to the head and keeping bindu, the yogi gains bliss, an unharmed body and freedom from Yama."},"t:80":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":80,"title":"அசபை","section":"நான்காம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Under the heading Ajapa, the chapter praises the one letter and the five letters (சிவாயநம in its several orders) as the sound-form of Siva's dance in the golden hall: the dance, the letters and the mantra are one, Siva's body is mantra, and chanting the letters by the drawn chakra ends birth, removes the bonds, yields powers and turns copper to gold."},"t:81":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":81,"title":"திருஅம்பலச் சக்கரம்","section":"நான்காம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The chakra of the holy hall (திருஅம்பலச் சக்கரம்): diagrams of crossing lines and cells are filled with the five syllables na-ma-si-va-ya, Om, bindu and nada and the fifty-one letters, and the Dancer of the hall dwells in them. The five syllables are praised as the source of the elements, the Vedas and the Agamas, as what drives out the three impurities and ends birth, and are read letter by letter as Siva, Shakti, soul and bond; the chapter ends with drawn figures used for worldly rites of fixing, enchanting, driving away, killing and attracting."},"t:82":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":82,"title":"அருச்சனை","section":"நான்காம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Archana, worship with flowers, scented pastes, food offerings and lamps: offered with love it brings everything from wealth and the eight siddhis to release. Yet those given to Siva, the Siva-yogis and the desireless need no rites, and the chapter turns inward to the Lord known within, the syllables of the mantra placed in the body, and the nectar and light of Siva."},"t:83":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":83,"title":"நவகுண்டம்","section":"நான்காம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The nine fire-pits (navakundam) of the fire rite, each of its own shape, read at once as altars and as places in the body: the flame rising in them is the Lord's light, in which Siva, Shakti and Skanda are visualized in forms of fixed numbers. Those who see that fire within are freed from strong karma, live long in the guarded body and reach release."},"t:84":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":84,"title":"சத்தி பேதம் - திரிபுரை சக்கரம்","section":"நான்காம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The distinctions of Shakti (சத்தி பேதம்): the Goddess Tripura, under many names (Tripurasundari, Manonmani, Parashakti, Abhirami, Bhuvanapati, Varahi, Omkari), is the source of the worlds, the tattvas, nada and bindu, speech and mantra, never apart from Siva; worshipped, she enters the poet's heart, clears his deluded mind and shows him the way to Siva."},"t:85":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":85,"title":"வயிரவி மந்திரம்","section":"நான்காம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The Bhairavi mantra (வயிரவி மந்திரம்): the goddess Bhairavi, also called Tripura and Manonmani, is a mantra of twelve kalas to be laid out, worshipped and recited with its rites, and she is the Shakti in whom the gods have their cause, who enters the devotee's heart, wakes him, takes away his deeds and births, and reveals Siva."},"t:86":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":86,"title":"பூரண சக்தி","section":"நான்காம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Full Shakti (பூரண சக்தி): the goddess, Parashakti, is the Lord's own power and consort, mother of all within the bonds, the powers of knowledge, will and action, bindu and nada, and the light that rises through the lotuses of the yogic body. The chapter ends in praise of her names and a complaint that people speak of the Lord without knowing her."},"t:87":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":87,"title":"சுத்த சைவம்","section":"ஐந்தாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Pure Saivism (சுத்த சைவம்): the Lord who makes all things includes the four kinds of Saivism, and the pure Saiva is one who has discerned the real and the unreal, learned and practised in order, and passed through Vedanta and Siddhanta to the end of nada and the Supreme."},"t:88":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":88,"title":"அசுத்த சைவம்","section":"ஐந்தாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Impure Saivism (அசுத்த சைவம்): Saivas of service and rite, known by outward signs such as earrings, seals and rudraksha beads and by recitation; the chapter then turns to those who see further, the severe pure Saiva and the jnani."},"t:89":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":89,"title":"மார்க்க சைவம்","section":"ஐந்தாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Path Saivism (மார்க்க சைவம்): the means and the vision of the pure Saiva, who holds Vedanta and Siddhanta as one, reflects on the Lord, the soul, the bond and release, and by the feet of the Supreme power becomes Siva, while soul and bond cannot of themselves reach the Lord."},"t:90":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":90,"title":"கடுஞ் சுத்த சைவம்","section":"ஐந்தாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Severe pure Saivism (கடுஞ் சுத்த சைவம்): beyond outward signs, the severe pure Saiva destroys desire, bond and the state of a bound soul, strips away the tattvas and maya, and becomes pure Siva, until 'I' and 'He' can no longer be said and the senses are stilled in union."},"t:91":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":91,"title":"சரியை","section":"ஐந்தாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Chariyai (சரியை), the first of the four Saiva paths: seeking out temples and towns, singing and bowing to Siva until he takes the heart as his temple. The chapter sets chariyai in the ladder of chariyai, kiriyai, yoga and jnana, matches each to a kind of worship and a kind of devotee, and ends with the four grades of initiation."},"t:92":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":92,"title":"கிரியை","section":"ஐந்தாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Kiriyai (கிரியை), worship of the Lord by rite and service: refuge at the feet of the one God of all directions frees from the sea of deeds, but rich offerings reach him only from those who have given up flesh. Service done by humans is service to the Lord, and kiriyai with chariyai leads on through yoga and jnana, by the guru's grace, to becoming Siva."},"t:93":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":93,"title":"யோகம்","section":"ஐந்தாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Yoga as the third path: the yogi sits still as a post, unmoved by touch, and finds Siva blossoming within the soul like scent in a flower, the Lord whom Hari and Brahma sought for ages and who dwells in the crown. Learning and the arts do not end the bonds; the yogic way does, and the chapter ends with yoga's four grades."},"t:94":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":94,"title":"ஞானம்","section":"ஐந்தாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Jnana, the fourth and highest path: there is no dharma, religion or release above it. Its marks are going beyond sound and the faculties, loss of 'I' and 'mine', passing beyond merit and sin, and becoming the pure, freed Siva-siddha. Like yoga, jnana has the four paths within it and four grades, ending at the good guru's feet."},"t:95":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":95,"title":"சன்மார்க்கம்","section":"ஐந்தாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Sanmarga (சன்மார்க்கம்), the true path, which in Saiva usage is the path of jnana: laid down by Nandi as a guru-path, it removes the five impurities and the bond of soul and fetter, joins the soul to the Lord, melts the unmelting heart and ends birth. Without clarity there is no knowing Siva; the lower paths serve the simple, and sanmarga is becoming Him."},"t:96":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":96,"title":"சகமார்க்கம்","section":"ஐந்தாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Sahamarga (சகமார்க்கம்), the companion path, which the chapter identifies with yoga: sanmarga itself becomes sahamarga, and within yoga-samadhi are space, light and Shakti. Yoga gives the yogi both union with Siva and the enjoyments of the world; it purifies the centres and channels, cuts down the five senses with the sword of the mind, and the Lord stands within like a ripe fruit split open."},"t:97":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":97,"title":"சற்புத்திர மார்க்கம்","section":"ஐந்தாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The path of the good son (சற்புத்திர மார்க்கம்), traditionally kiriyai: worship, recitation, praise, chanting, truthfulness and purity offered to the Lord as a son serves a father. Bowing at Hara's feet is the fruit of birth, his feet are the shore for all souls, and he comes forth each time he is worshipped."},"t:98":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":98,"title":"தாச மார்க்கம்","section":"ஐந்தாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The servant's path (தாச மார்க்கம்), traditionally sariyai: the humble temple tasks of lamps, flowers, sweeping, smearing, bell and bathing the image. Seeking the Lord by the ordained way quiets the mind's 'this or that', but worship done without love leaves the mind a stone sunk in a mossy pond."},"t:99":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":99,"title":"சாலோகம்","section":"ஐந்தாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Salokam, dwelling in the Lord's world: the states of release named salokam, samipam and sarupam are gained through sariyai and the paths after it, and in kiriyai the mind becomes the temple and the grades of initiation end in samadhi."},"t:100":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":100,"title":"சாமீபம்","section":"ஐந்தாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Samipam, nearness to the Lord, set in a ladder of the four states by what becomes of the bond (பாசம்) in each, until in sayujyam the bond dissolves and the Lord alone remains."},"t:101":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":101,"title":"சாரூபம்","section":"ஐந்தாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Sarupam, taking the Lord's form: it is reached through the eighth limb of yoga and the true path, and as everything that comes to the golden mountain takes its colour, so one who enters the guru's abode takes on the radiant form of the Lord of Kailash."},"t:102":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":102,"title":"சாயுச்சியம்","section":"ஐந்தாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Sayujyam, union with Siva, the last of the four states: Saivam is relation with Siva, knowing oneself and reaching him, and its end is Siva's bliss; sayujyam is the state beyond waking, abiding in stillness and becoming Siva."},"t:103":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":103,"title":"சத்திநிபாதம்","section":"ஐந்தாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The descent of power (சத்திநிபாதம்): when the soul is ripe, the Lord's grace-power, figured as an old woman, a hill-girl or the Goddess with flowing hair, comes to the blinded soul, removes its darkness, cuts its deeds, comes as the guru and makes it Siva. Worship of the Goddess with the Lord ends birth."},"t:104":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":104,"title":"அருசமயப் பிணக்கம்","section":"ஐந்தாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The quarrel of the six sects (அறுசமயப் பிணக்கம்): the six systems seek the Lord outside and miss the God who stands within the body; he is not the substance of any sect, and those who argue that this one or that one is good stay deluded and are born and die. The way is Siva's path, the guru's word and the light within, where no quarrel remains."},"t:105":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":105,"title":"நிராசாரம்","section":"ஐந்தாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Beyond sectarian observance (நிராசாரம்): those who master a sect's scriptures claim to know, but the Lord dwells with the forbearing and with those who hold his feet; sect-bound people who never turn to the Lord within weep, are reborn and perish inside the sect they could not leave."},"t:106":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":106,"title":"உட்சமயம்","section":"ஐந்தாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The inner religion (உட்சமயம்): the six sects are roads to one city and the Lord made them all, so quarrelling over which is good is a dog barking at a hill; within them Nandi set one Saiva path of the guru, Hara's path, which knowers and gods cannot reach by examining alone but which opens to those who worship his feet."},"t:107":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":107,"title":"சிவகுரு தரிசினம்","section":"ஆறாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Seeing the Siva-guru (சிவகுரு தரிசனம்): the true guru is Siva himself come into the world, who tests, removes the bonds and shows the soul, the bonds and the Lord; siddhi, release and the knowledge of Vedanta cannot be known except by his grace, and fools who take him for an ordinary man miss it."},"t:108":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":108,"title":"திருவடிப் பேறு","section":"ஆறாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Attaining the holy feet (திருவடிப் பேறு): the guru, Nandi, lays his hand and his feet on the disciple's head, heart and eyes, removes the forms of the self and makes him Siva beyond words; the knowledge of the holy feet is itself Siva-hood, release and every mantra, medicine and path."},"t:109":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":109,"title":"ஞாதுரு ஞான ஞேயம்","section":"ஆறாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Knower, knowing and known (ஞாதுரு, ஞானம், ஞேயம்): for one who stands in Siva as the known, the bonds and the ego lose their hold and the two of 'I' and 'He' become one. Tirumular speaks of Nandi as the guru who reworked his tattvas, broke his ignorance and showed him a state beyond the senses; silence and the mudras complete the knower."},"t:110":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":110,"title":"துறவு","section":"ஆறாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Renunciation (துறவு): Siva, himself a renouncer who lives in the burning ground and eats alms, cuts the births of those who renounce and becomes their light. The renouncer refuses the senses' claims for the tapas of reaching his feet, keeps to the path whose thorns hurt only those who stray, and, in a run of riddling images, turns the body's energies inward."},"t:111":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":111,"title":"தவம்","section":"ஆறாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Tapas (தவம்): those who withdraw and let go of attachment are beyond fear, death and the turn of day and night; only those with the Lord's grace know what tapas is; it ends birth where asking only wins wealth; no threat shakes the true ascetic, and the Lord comes near only to such. Yet the chapter ends by saying tapas is needed only until the samadhi of knowledge is reached."},"t:112":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":112,"title":"தவ நிந்தை","section":"ஆறாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The limits of tapas (தவ நிந்தை): once the Life of life is within and samadhi is in hand, recitation, chant, purity rites and even tapas fall away; true tapas is knowing the outcome, cutting the senses with the sword of understanding, a single-pointed mind and the fellowship of those already in Siva's bliss. Those without tapas or learning only whirl around for food."},"t:113":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":113,"title":"அருளுடைமையின் ஞானம் வருதல்","section":"ஆறாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Knowledge comes from possessing grace (அருளுடைமையின் ஞானம் வருதல்): the Lord's grace is the source of good wealth, knowledge, greatness and divinity; it keeps deeds from clinging, removes birth and makes the soul one with Siva, and it is to be won in embodied life. Tirumular ends by vowing not to let go of Nandi until release."},"t:114":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":114,"title":"அவ வேடம்","section":"ஆறாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The futile garb (அவ வேடம்): those who put on the ascetic's guise for food and show do not reach Siva's feet; the land suffers when the ignorant wear it, so the ruler should correct his people and strip the base of it; false austerity leads to hell, and garb saves only those who have come to know."},"t:115":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":115,"title":"தவவேடம்","section":"ஆறாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The garb of austerity (தவ வேடம்): only those great in austerity can rightly bear the ascetic's garb, which the rest wear as hunters wear theirs; the chapter then lists the marks of the Siva-yogi, from holy ash and earrings to bowl, sandals and staff."},"t:116":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":116,"title":"திருநீறு","section":"ஆறாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Holy ash (திருநீறு): wearing the ash Siva himself wears, as armour, drives off deeds and brings the Siva state; ash from the sacred fire changes the form and lineage of the pure who wear it. The chapter opens with a verse on the true sacred thread and tuft."},"t:117":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":117,"title":"ஞான வேடம்","section":"ஆறாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The garb of knowledge (ஞான வேடம்): knowledge, not garb, decides; the ignorant in garb go to hell and the knower without it is released. For the Siva-gnani any garb serves, his own garb is Siva-yoga, and he lives as one dead while body and senses remain, silent, released and in Nandi's state."},"t:118":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":118,"title":"சிவ வேடம்","section":"ஆறாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The garb of Siva (சிவ வேடம்): the true Siva garb is not worn on the body, which sheds it at death, but is the state of those who by grace become Siva's servants, are free of darkness, delusion and action, and seek the Lord within; so rein in the running horse and seek Nandi rather than garb."},"t:119":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":119,"title":"அபக்குவன்","section":"ஆறாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The unripe (அபக்குவன்): the unready choose blind teachers and fall with them, look outward, do not wake to the light or answer the Lord's call, say one thing and do another; the king should drive out the great sinners; and the unworthy disciple, who neither removes his impurities nor fears birth, is not to be given the teaching."},"t:120":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":120,"title":"பக்குவன்","section":"ஆறாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The ripe (பக்குவன்): the worthy disciple seeks a flawless guru, gives him goods, body and life, and stays at his feet like a shadow; sattvic, fearing birth, discerning the real from the unreal and melting in devotion, he receives the guru's foot and the knowledge it brings, and the teaching is to be given to such as he alone."},"t:121":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":121,"title":"ஆறு ஆதாரம்","section":"ஏழாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The six adharas: the lotuses of four to sixteen petals rising from the root carry the fifty letters, and purifying them cleanses the nadis and the body; above them, through the sixteen kalas to the end of nada, the supreme Shakti stands and pure knowledge turns into the bliss of Siva."},"t:122":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":122,"title":"அண்டலிங்கம் (உலக சிவம்)","section":"ஏழாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The cosmic linga: the linga is the directions, the arts and the world itself, arising as Shakti and as Sadasiva, who gives enjoyment and release. The gods praise him without understanding; the temple, and many materials matched to each varna, can serve as the linga; and the Lord beyond all knowing makes the body his temple and the cosmos his place of worship."},"t:123":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":123,"title":"பிண்டலிங்கம் (உடற் சிவம்)","section":"ஏழாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The body linga: the form of the human body is the Sivalinga, Chidambaram, Sadasiva and the holy dance. The Lord enters the body as his temple, the five keep its gates, and the worship he takes there is made by setting the senses behind."},"t:124":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":124,"title":"சதாசிவ லிங்கம் (உலக முதற் சிவம்)","section":"ஏழாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The Sadasiva linga: Sadasiva, five-faced, fifteen-eyed and ten-armed, is the form shared by the gods, bindu, nada and Shakti; through Shakti the directions, Vedas, religions and tattvas unfold. His faces and limb-mantras are set on the body, the poet takes him into his heart, and the chapter ends with one's own body as the linga and the linga as the syllable Om."},"t:125":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":125,"title":"ஆத்மலிங்கம் (உயிர்ச்சிவம் )","section":"ஏழாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The lingam of the self (ஆத்மலிங்கம்): the letters A and U, bindu as pedestal and nada as shaft, and Shakti joined with Siva make up the lingam, fixed and moving, which is Sadasiva; and the Lord who wears the moon, whom the gods seek in the eight directions, makes the devotee's mind his temple."},"t:126":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":126,"title":"ஞான லிங்கம் (உணர்வுச் சிவம் )","section":"ஏழாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The lingam of knowledge (ஞான லிங்கம்): the supreme Siva takes form, formlessness and form-with-formlessness, the nine forms with Sadasiva in the middle, and comes as guru to souls; with him stands Parashakti, and the whole world is the play of Shakti and Siva, which enters the mind and makes it Siva."},"t:127":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":127,"title":"சிவலிங்கம் ( சிவகுரு )","section":"ஏழாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The Siva lingam (சிவலிங்கம்): the Lord pervades sea, wind and fire too widely to be walked round, yet there is a way to circle him, by water, flowers and his name; the nine forms together are the lingam, and restraining the breath through the inner flame leads to the supreme state."},"t:128":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":128,"title":"சம்பிரதாயம் ( பண்டை முறை )","section":"ஏழாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The ancient way of initiation (சம்பிரதாயம்): the guru takes the disciple's body, wealth and life with water, places hand and feet on him, cuts the clinging of deeds and births, and shows the Lord within; the disciple, exchanged for the guru himself, comes to know no difference between I and you, Siva and soul, as body and life are one."},"t:129":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":129,"title":"திருவருள் வைப்பு","section":"ஏழாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The placing of grace (திருவருள் வைப்பு): grace is what binds the soul and what frees it, the whole course of a life from birth to dissolution, and the five acts by which the Lord makes and pervades the worlds. The Lord cannot be reached by one's own knowing; he enters the heart of those he graces, and is unseen by those without grace."},"t:130":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":130,"title":"அருள் ஒளி","section":"ஏழாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The light of grace (அருள் ஒளி): those who stand in grace are freed of the bonds, and the poet tells how, seeking with song and tears, he was given Siva's feet, made spotless and fed nectar. Grace is a lamp that turns its bearer into light, and when light and darkness both pass the soul becomes Siva."},"t:131":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":131,"title":"சிவபூசை","section":"ஏழாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Worship of Siva (சிவபூசை): the body is the temple and the soul the linga; outer worship with flowers and water is open to anyone and the Lord answers it with grace, but rite without inner knowing does not reach him. The chapter moves between praise of offering, the inner rite of mantra and the heart, and the stages of initiation."},"t:132":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":132,"title":"குருபூசை","section":"ஏழாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Worship of the guru, Nandi: outer worship with sandal and flowers does not reach the Lord, while inner worship in knowledge, in the place beyond sun and moon and beyond day and night, is the great worship."},"t:133":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":133,"title":"மகேசுவர பூசை","section":"ஏழாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Worship of Siva in his devotees (மகேசுவர பூசை): what is given to the devotee, the walking temple, reaches the god, and feeding one knower of Siva outweighs gifts to thousands of Brahmins and the building of temples."},"t:134":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":134,"title":"அடியார் பெருமை","section":"ஏழாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The greatness of the Lord's devotees: where one lives the land prospers and rain falls, they attain the Lord in this body and this world, all things are held in their sight, and they are bound to Siva by a deed written before birth."},"t:135":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":135,"title":"போசன விதி","section":"ஏழாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The rule of eating: devotees are welcomed and fed everywhere as if with nectar, and those who know the grace within Siva beg at noon for the Lord in the inner temple, eat without distinction and sit apart, free of desire."},"t:136":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":136,"title":"பிட்சா விதி","section":"ஏழாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The rule of begging: Siva himself begged with Brahma's skull and made begging the devotee's way to his feet; begging from desire is faulted, while the true ascetic is free of hunger, craving and anger and the world comes to him."},"t:137":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":137,"title":"முத்திரை பேதம்","section":"ஏழாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The kinds of mudra: shambhavi, khechari, the grace-hand and the knowledge-mudra are distinguished, and the highest seal is the mudra of silence, in which speech and mind both cease and release is gained; the chapter ends by placing Vedanta and Siddhanta together in pure Saivam."},"t:138":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":138,"title":"பூரணக் குகை நெறிச் சமாதி","section":"ஏழாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"What becomes of yogis and jnanis when they leave the body depends on how far they have come: the yogi passes through other worlds and returns to finish his yoga, while the faultless jnani rests in silent samadhi, becomes Siva and is not born again. To die to the three impurities while still living is the real death and the real attainment."},"t:139":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":139,"title":"சமாதிக் கிரியை","section":"ஏழாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The body of a realized jnani must not be burned or left to animals: that is like setting fire to the Lord's temple and brings drought, famine and the fall of kings. It is to be buried seated in a cave-tomb, and the chapter prescribes the tomb's measures, sites, furnishings, offerings and the shrine set over it, which brings grace to the land."},"t:140":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":140,"title":"விந்துற்பனம்","section":"ஏழாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Bindu is both the cosmic seed, from which with nada the tattvas, the powers of speech and the worlds unfold as Mahamaya, and the bodily seed, distilled from food through the seven bodily elements. The Lord stands in the seed as a sprout in its seed; those who do not know how bindu is lost, or how to guard it, perish with the perishing body."},"t:141":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":141,"title":"விந்து ஜயம் - போக சரவோட்டம்","section":"ஏழாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Conquest of bindu (விந்து ஜயம்) and the practice of union governed by the breath (போக சரவோட்டம்): the yogi turns desire back to the root and, raising the inner fire, drinks the nectar of the moon instead of losing bindu; where union with a woman happens, it is timed by the lunar days and the breath and held so that bindu is not spilled. Kept bindu gives breath, strength, siddhi and the state of Sivoham."},"t:142":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":142,"title":"ஆதித்த நிலை - அண்டாதித்தன்","section":"ஏழாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The sun of the cosmos (அண்டாதித்தன்): the sun and the gods circle Meru in worship of the Lord's feet, and the Lord himself stands as the sun, the source of all lives, figured in a diagram of circles and petals; the way the sun travels beneath the earth is hidden from all but those who know."},"t:143":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":143,"title":"பிண்டாதித்தன்","section":"ஏழாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The sun in the body (பிண்டாதித்தன்): when the malams are destroyed and the inner current runs up the channel, a wide light shines; those who find where that sun settles know themselves and fall silent, and the poet's love melts toward those who know where it lies hidden."},"t:144":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":144,"title":"மன ஆதித்தன்","section":"ஏழாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The sun of the mind (மன ஆதித்தன்): sun, moon and fire are taken as the lights and breaths within. When the two rays become one the life-breath stops wandering and the clear-minded become Siva; beyond these lights shines one that the bounded cannot perceive, and without the First One's grace there is only darkness."},"t:145":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":145,"title":"ஞானாதித்தன்","section":"ஏழாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The sun of knowledge (ஞானாதித்தன்): bindu and nada dawn within the heart as the source of the kalas and of speech, and the Lord is a light beyond sun, moon and fire, travelling through all the worlds, shining at centres in the body, and removing a darkness the outer lights cannot."},"t:146":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":146,"title":"சிவாதித்தன்","section":"ஏழாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Siva as the sun (சிவாதித்தன்): his light of knowledge ends the darkness of bond and ignorance; he is in every body like the sun reflected in many pots, yet not confined by them; and he himself is the three lights, the gods, body and soul, and the elements."},"t:147":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":147,"title":"பசு இலக்கணம்","section":"ஏழாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The marks of the soul (பசு இலக்கணம்), given through the swan (அன்னம்): the Lord is known within as an unfading lamp, and soul and Lord are two swans that never part; the swan that holds itself a separate one does not reach its gain."},"t:148":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":148,"title":"புருடன்","section":"ஏழாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The soul as purusha and as anu (அணு, the atom): the Lord made speech, bonds and the tattvas from beginningless time; he is the atom within the atom and is in the soul as the soul is in him; the tiny soul, like a banyan seed, can be cleared of the dark of bonds and set on the good path."},"t:149":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":149,"title":"சீவன்","section":"ஏழாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The individual soul (சீவன்): it is unimaginably small, yet the Lord, greater than all, dwells within its flesh; bound by maya's adjuncts, it is stirred by the guru's grace, leaves them, enters the fourth state and becomes knowledge, and takes Siva's form within."},"t:150":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":150,"title":"பசு","section":"ஏழாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Souls as cattle (பசு, the bound soul): learned or powerful souls may bellow and graze, but only ripened ones give milk; and souls grazing in the field of the senses will not grasp the meaning until they are led past the boundary to the Lord's feet."},"t:151":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":151,"title":"போதன் (அறிஞன்)","section":"ஏழாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The knower (போதன்): soul and Siva are not two, and once the soul knows Siva it remains as Siva; the knower, taught the thirty-six tattvas by Nandi's grace, passes beyond them, while those who do not know the impurities pursue hair-tearing austerities, and those busy only in the Lord's daily work miss his daily grace."},"t:152":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":152,"title":"ஐந்து இந்திரியம் அடக்கும் அருமை","section":"ஏழாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"How hard it is to restrain the five senses (ஐந்து இந்திரியம் அடக்கும் அருமை): the senses are rutting elephants that tire the mahout, a horse no learning can steer, birds and lions feeding in five fields; the poet confesses he cannot conquer them, though cutting their claws and teeth would bring one to the Lord."},"t:153":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":153,"title":"ஐந்து இந்திரியம் அடக்கும் முறைமை","section":"ஏழாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The way of restraining the five senses (ஐந்து இந்திரியம் அடக்கும் முறைமை): the senses are fish, elephants and gnawing thieves, and the fort of knowledge alone does not hold them; yet Tirumular mocks those who only cry 'curb the five', since full suppression leaves one inert. What works is letting the senses subside as the mind settles on Nandi, is drawn in by worship and song, and is caught by the Lord's grace."},"t:154":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":154,"title":"அசற்குரு நெறி","section":"ஏழாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The way of the false guru (அசற்குரு நெறி): the false guru lacks inner feeling and true knowledge, does not see the Veda-Agama path, reviles others, is ruled by lust, and teaches for his food what he has only looked at. Such teaching harms the teacher, the king and the land, and teacher and pupil fall together like the blind leading the blind."},"t:155":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":155,"title":"சற்குரு நெறி","section":"ஏழாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The way of the true guru (சற்குரு நெறி): the true guru gives his feet, makes one know oneself, lifts the soul beyond the tattvas and quells its bonds; like alchemy turning iron to gold for good, his grace ends birth. He shows the real, the unreal and the real-unreal, burns away the malas, and is finally the Supreme himself acting through the five acts."},"t:156":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":156,"title":"கூடா ஒழுக்கம்","section":"ஏழாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Conduct that does not fit (கூடா ஒழுக்கம்): those who do wrong in secret forget that the Lord is an overseer present everywhere, and those who seek him in the north or across the land forget that he is within the body; those who see him give up their stealth. The later verses turn to grace that clears the mind, to the soul and its awareness, and to knowing oneself as the chief work."},"t:157":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":157,"title":"கேடு கண்டு இரங்கல்","section":"ஏழாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Grieving at people's ruin (கேடு கண்டு இரங்கல்): they spend a rare human birth, their youth and their days in toil, argument, lust and want, and fail to worship the Lord who is near them as light and honey, while death comes running."},"t:158":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":158,"title":"இதோபதேசம்","section":"ஏழாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Salutary teaching (இதோபதேசம்): leave the passing birth behind, hold the Lord in mind even at death, keep to the Siva way with truth and love, and worship the guru who shows how to reach him; for those who do, deeds and births end."},"t:159":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":159,"title":"உடலிற் பஞ்சபேதம்","section":"எட்டாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The kinds of body (உடலிற் பஞ்சபேதம்): the soul lives in a gross body of the seven bodily elements, a subtle body of eight tattvas and finer bodies still; the subtle body carries it from birth to birth to taste its deeds, while for the knower the body becomes Siva, so the body is not to be despised."},"t:160":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":160,"title":"உடல்விடல்","section":"எட்டாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Leaving the body (உடல்விடல்): the desires, speech, breath-sound, senses and mind that tie a person to the body fall away, and nothing, not even vows and gifts, is a sure support for the perishing flesh; the mind made firm stands before Siva."},"t:161":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":161,"title":"அவத்தை பேதம் - கீழால் அவத்தை","section":"எட்டாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The descending states of the soul (கீழால் அவத்தை): waking, dream, deep sleep, turiya and the beyond are placed at centres of the body from the brow downward, each with fewer instruments; bound by the five malas the soul is born and dies without end, yet the Lord rouses it from the stupor of anava through maya, and one who sees the malas plainly in full waking stands with the Supreme."},"t:162":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":162,"title":"மத்திய சாக்கிர அவத்தை","section":"எட்டாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The middle states within waking (மத்திய சாக்கிர அவத்தை): waking itself holds all five states, each tied to a mala or power, and the tattvas are counted differently by each school (96, 36, 28, 24 and 25). The soul in the sakala state gropes like a blind man, but by grace and Nandi's teaching it learns that it is the knower beyond the inert tattvas, sheds states, malas and tattvas, and stands as one."},"t:163":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":163,"title":"அத்துவாக்கள்","section":"எட்டாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The six adhvas (அத்துவாக்கள்), the paths of tattvas, mantras, letters, worlds, words and kalas through which the soul is bound: counting and searching them leaves seekers bewildered, but rising through the states to Siva's dance makes them all Sivaya."},"t:164":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":164,"title":"சுத்த நனவாதி பருவம்","section":"எட்டாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The pure states beginning with waking (சுத்த நனவாதி): waking, dream, deep sleep, turiya and the beyond are matched to the tattvas, the malas and places in the body, and each state is divided into all the others, as the soul drops its instruments and passes inward to turiya and the beyond where it becomes Siva. Among these sit verses on the malas that hide the soul (rice and husk), the one herdsman of the many cows, and knowledge that knows itself."},"t:165":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":165,"title":"கேவல சகல சுத்தம்","section":"எட்டாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The three states of the soul: kevala, alone with anava and knowing nothing; sakala, joined to body, maya and karma; and suddha, purified by grace. The chapter sorts beings by their impurities, subdivides each state into three and maps them onto waking, dream, deep sleep, turiya and the beyond, and holds that bondage and release alike are Hara's grace, by which the soul, with maya as nurse and Siva as father, comes to purity."},"t:166":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":166,"title":"பராவத்தை","section":"எட்டாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The supreme states (பராவத்தை): the soul's states of waking, dream, deep sleep and turiya are traced up through grades to Siva's own turiya, where, beyond the thirty-six tattvas and by grace, the bonds fall away and the soul becomes one with Siva. Images of gold and ornament, wood and elephant show how the Supreme and the world each hide the other."},"t:167":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":167,"title":"முக்குண நிர்க்குணம்","section":"எட்டாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The three gunas and the gunaless (முக்குண நிர்க்குணம்): the states of the soul are matched with the gunas, sattva with waking, rajas with dream, tamas with deep sleep, while turiya is the stainless state beyond all three."},"t:168":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":168,"title":"அண்டாதி பேதம்","section":"எட்டாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The divisions of the cosmic eggs (அண்டாதி பேதம்): the world-spheres are as many as the sands of the seven seas, all under the Lord seated on them, and the tattvas spread through them in fixed multiples."},"t:169":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":169,"title":"பதினோராம் தானமும் அவத்தையெனக் காணல்","section":"எட்டாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The eleventh station seen as a state (பதினோராம் தானமும் அவத்தையெனக் காணல்): counted centres in the body yield nectar, bliss and Om, and the eleven stations are themselves states of the soul; held firmly, they leave nothing but Nandi."},"t:170":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":170,"title":"கலவு செலவு","section":"எட்டாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Joining and going (கலவு செலவு): the soul's passage from the kevala condition, through the sakala, to removal of the bonds ends in the pure state beyond waking; give up anger, direct the mind, and rest in grace, and even stone splits into open space."},"t:171":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":171,"title":"நின்மல அவத்தை","section":"எட்டாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The pure states (நின்மல அவத்தை): when waking, dream and deep sleep subside into turiya and beyond, and grace removes malam, the soul knows itself as the knower, beyond the inert tattvas, and becomes one with Siva. The soul knows only as grace illumines it; one Shakti feeds desire and then withers it; the freed leave heaven and hell behind, and even what is vain, known through grace, is grace."},"t:172":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":172,"title":"அறிவுதயம்","section":"எட்டாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The dawning of knowledge (அறிவுதயம்): to know oneself is to come to no harm, and the self so known is knowledge in form, taught by Nandi's grace. Knowledge has no birth and no death and knows itself; passing beyond the world's knowing and not-knowing, the knower finds the Lord in the heart, ends the search for the distant lamp and, the malam removed, abides as Siva with no more birth."},"t:173":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":173,"title":"ஆறு அந்தம்","section":"எட்டாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The six ends (ஆறு அந்தம்): Vedanta, Siddhanta, the end of nada, the end of knowing, the end of yoga and the end of the kalas. Each is defined, and all subside into the one known where knower and knowing vanish in silence and Siva's bliss. Vedanta and Siddhanta are one; the Veda and the Agama are both the Lord's word; and tat tvam asi is read in Saiva terms as the bound soul, the Lord and their union, sayujya."},"t:174":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":174,"title":"பதி பசு பாசம் வேறின்மை","section":"எட்டாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Pati, pasu and pasam (the Lord, the soul and the bond) are all three beginningless; the bond is three, and when it is cut the soul clings to the Lord. The Lord sets the bond and maya to work through his five acts, so that the bond itself becomes the grace by which the soul is cleansed and reaches his feet."},"t:175":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":175,"title":"அடிதலை அறியும் திறங்கூறல்","section":"எட்டாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Knowing the Lord's foot and head: the foot is his grace, which shows the soul knowledge, and in the body foot and head lie at the root and the crown. The poet tells how Nandi's feet came onto his head, cut his bonds and took him, and how the Lord, like a parent, deals with each soul by its ripeness."},"t:176":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":176,"title":"முக்குற்றம்","section":"எட்டாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The three faults: they afflict wholly and keep souls dazed in darkness, and those who do not leave them perish in them. With lust, anger and delusion driven out, the poet reached the abode within the sound Om."},"t:177":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":177,"title":"முப்பதம்","section":"எட்டாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The three words of the great saying tat tvam asi, 'that thou art': 'thou' is the soul known apart from the tattvas, 'that' is Siva, and 'art', the word that joins them, is Shakti. When they mingle the soul becomes Siva in a joy beyond words, and the joining word is matched to the turiya state."},"t:178":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":178,"title":"முப்பரம்","section":"எட்டாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The three supremes: Siva as the Supreme, the supreme beyond the supreme, and the Supreme in oneself (tatpara), reached beyond the thirty-six tattvas and the states of the soul. He comes to the soul like a mother to her calf, and is reached by a clinging that ends clinging, in a bliss that knowledge makes."},"t:179":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":179,"title":"பரலட்சணம்","section":"எட்டாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The marks of the Supreme (பரலட்சணம்): Siva stands beyond the states and the tattvas and is not bound by adjuncts, yet with Shakti as his body and form he pervades the five elements and the worlds. The soul that leaves its darkness becomes one with him, and Nandi as guru cuts away fear and gives himself."},"t:180":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":180,"title":"முத்திரியம்","section":"எட்டாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The three turiyas: the soul's turiya, the Supreme's turiya and Siva's turiya, each traced through waking and the states below it, answer to the three words of tvam tat asi. Standing in turiya the soul reaches the Lord beyond reach, leaves the thirty-six tattvas and maya by grace, and Nandi gives the true reality."},"t:181":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":181,"title":"மும்முத்தி","section":"எட்டாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The three releases: the soul's release, the release in the Supreme and the release in Siva, which are the state beyond, stilled calm and bliss. Souls that do not know what truly is go to birth, those who know go to grace, and release is to leave all and become one with Siva, who is attainment, release and samadhi."},"t:182":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":182,"title":"முச்சொரூபம்","section":"எட்டாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The three forms (முச்சொரூபம்): the soul rises past the impurities into the knowledge of 'I am Siva' and release in three forms, while the Lord, beyond measure, becomes formless, form and both, comes as Shakti and guru, and holds the three forms as a gem holds its lustre."},"t:183":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":183,"title":"முக்கரணம்","section":"எட்டாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The three instruments (முக்கரணம்): as one man appears in three roles, so the difference of Siva, the Supreme and the soul may be called one; and when Om, breath and space are raised and the stupor of the three instruments is cleared, Nandi gives the inner samadhi of unmani."},"t:184":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":184,"title":"முச்சூனிய தொந்தத்தசி","section":"எட்டாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The three voids and tvam tat asi: the soul, the Supreme and their union, the three words of the saying, appear in turiya and are matched with maya, the beyond and peace, and with the letters of Om; when the impurities and qualities die Siva appears as darkness leaves before the moon, and the soul is known as Parasiva."},"t:185":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":185,"title":"முப்பாழ்","section":"எட்டாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The three voids (முப்பாழ்): letting go of the seven effects gives the void of maya, of the seven causes the void of knowledge, and of their traces the void of calm; these voids are the soul, the Supreme and Siva-Shakti, where speech ends. The speaker crosses the void to Nandi's feet, and at the last even void, heart and form are gone."},"t:186":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":186,"title":"காரிய காரண உபாதி","section":"எட்டாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The adjuncts of effect and cause (காரிய காரண உபாதி): the soul is bound by seven adjuncts and the Supreme by seven more; when these, with effect and cause, are cut off, the soul becomes Siva. The letters of Om and of சிவாய are matched with soul, Supreme and Siva, and anava leaves only through the Lord who is the life of life."},"t:187":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":187,"title":"உபசாந்தம்","section":"எட்டாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Upasantam (உபசாந்தம்), the stilling: the quiet that comes when the tendencies left by cause and effect fall away, beyond waking and turiya, as the soul is purified, pervaded by the Lord and steeped in Siva's bliss. It ends in union, like water settling clear after waves."},"t:188":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":188,"title":"புறங்கூறாமை","section":"எட்டாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Under the title 'not speaking ill' (புறங்கூறாமை), the verses rebuke those who claim full knowledge without seeing the Lord, tell the heart to drop its boasting and to stop quarrelling over who is God, and praise those who contemplate him within: they see him, are loved by the knowers, and become Siva-siddhas who set aside the lesser releases."},"t:189":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":189,"title":"எட்டிதழ்க் கமல முக்குண அவத்தை","section":"எட்டாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The eight-petalled lotus and the states of the three gunas: the eight directions with their guardian gods are set as the petals of an inner lotus, through whose stalk the sushumna rises to a light; the Lord stands in the eight, in the grades beyond them and in the ages, and is reached at the summit, where a tank fills without a river and a lotus blooms without mud."},"t:190":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":190,"title":"ஒன்பான் அவத்தை - ஒன்பான் அபிமானி","section":"எட்டாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The nine states and their nine presiding selves (ஒன்பான் அவத்தை, ஒன்பான் அபிமானி): waking, dream and sleep, each taken at three levels, have each a presiding self; passing through them, cut from mala and the gunas, the soul reaches turiya as its true form, and to become Siva beyond the three turiyas is the attainment of Siddhanta."},"t:191":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":191,"title":"சுத்தாஅசுத்தம்","section":"எட்டாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Pure and impure (சுத்தாஅசுத்தம்): what is truly hidden and what truly defiles are not where people look. The Lord sits a few fingers from the nose tip and within the body, yet is sought in far places; ritual impurity does not touch the disciplined, the worshipper, the fire-keeper or the knower; and true purity is the Lord's way, name and feet, while those bound to karma never learn its fruit."},"t:192":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":192,"title":"முத்திநிந்தை","section":"எட்டாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Reviling release (முத்திநிந்தை): those who deny a higher state, ignore the guru's sign, or take their own mind for God wander outside, unable to see the way shown them, while the Lord stands within and the short way is mantra and the inner fire."},"t:193":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":193,"title":"இலக்கணாத் திரயம்","section":"எட்டாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The three kinds of implication (இலக்கணாத் திரயம்), the Vedantic modes by which a word points beyond its literal sense, are placed in the states of the soul, and what lies beyond all implication is one's own true form; the senses fall like elephants to a well-aimed arrow."},"t:194":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":194,"title":"தத்துவமசி வாக்கியம்","section":"எட்டாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The great saying 'that you are' (தத்துவமசி): its three words, 'you', 'that' and 'are', are placed in three turiyas, of the soul, of the Supreme and of Siva, and read by implication until the soul, its bonds and instruments left behind, becomes Siva. Tirumular calls this Vedanta's saying and Siddhanta's conclusion at once, and ends with the Lord's grace making the speaker spotless and every soul dancing as he makes it dance."},"t:195":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":195,"title":"விசுவக் கிராசம்","section":"எட்டாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The swallowing of the universe (விசுவக் கிராசம்): the body dissolves into the Supreme like a bubble or camphor in fire, and Siva, who spreads through all and eats and spits out the world, draws soul, Supreme, turiya and cosmos into himself, as heated iron draws in water or a wood-apple is emptied while its shell stays whole."},"t:196":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":196,"title":"வாய்மை","section":"எட்டாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Truth (வாய்மை), taken as the true as against the false in the whole person: the Lord mingles with those who are mingled with truth and does not enter before the false; knowledge without the all-knowing is no gain; those who leave falsehood and the bewildering senses and follow the one who cut away confusion reach the Siva-state."},"t:197":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":197,"title":"ஞானி செயல்","section":"எட்டாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The jnani's action (ஞானி செயல்): the knowers of tattva who know themselves exhaust past karma by living it out and stop future karma from forming, by Siva's grace placed on their head; karma arises through mind, speech and body and does not last when mind and speech stand straight."},"t:198":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":198,"title":"அவா அறுத்தல்","section":"எட்டாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Cutting off craving (அவா அறுத்தல்): talk of yogic technique is useless while desire remains; the Lord is found not in buildings or garb but in the heart freed of longing, and desire must be cut even where it reaches toward the Lord, for desire multiplies sorrow and its loss is bliss."},"t:199":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":199,"title":"பக்தியுடைமை","section":"எட்டாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Having devotion (பக்தியுடைமை): the Lord, whom the seven worlds cannot know, is known only through the devotion of his devotees; the poet takes on servitude to the servants of his servants, calls to the Lord as a calf calls, and finds that in bondage and release alike the devotee has no task of his own, for grace does all."},"t:200":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":200,"title":"முத்தியுடைமை","section":"எட்டாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Having release (முத்தியுடைமை): those in release have the Father's full grace, purified tattvas and a devotion in which their deeds have fallen away, and know the supreme bliss; a second, obscure verse pictures the search for the heart's fruit."},"t:201":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":201,"title":"சோதனை","section":"எட்டாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The test (சோதனை): the seeker's search is completed when, having bathed in the ocean of grace and left all maya, he simply stays still; the Lord pervades all as ghee in milk, takes the heart as his temple, tests and leads the seeker, and gives him the sword of knowledge with which nothing can turn him aside."},"t:202":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":202,"title":"குருமட தரிசனம்","section":"ஒன்பதாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Seeing the guru's matha (குருமட தரிசனம்): those who see the guru's seat, where all offerings are understood as the Lord's, reach his feet; and the matha is turned inward, so that the Lord's only house is the person, and the seat, mountain, cave and forest of the yogi are found in the body's centres and the inward-turned face."},"t:203":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":203,"title":"ஞானகுரு தரிசனம்","section":"ஒன்பதாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Seeing the guru of knowledge (ஞானகுரு தரிசனம்): the guru's state lies beyond the tattvas and the turiyas and cannot be spoken, yet to fix the mind on his form clears the three malams, as the thought of Garuda clears poison. Nandi as guru takes the seeker's head, mouth, eyes and mind, enters the heart, cuts the bonds and makes even the smallest great, until the disciple no longer bows to gods but becomes what others bow to."},"t:204":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":204,"title":"பிரணவ சமாதி","section":"ஒன்பதாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The samadhi of the pranava (பிரணவ சமாதி): Om, gross and subtle, holds within it the one word, form and the formless and all distinctions; the elements, the moving and unmoving and the souls arise from it, and it is the form of both soul and Parasiva. Those on the true path pass beyond its measures and rest at the end of knowing and the known."},"t:205":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":205,"title":"ஒளி","section":"ஒன்பதாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Light (ஒளி): the Lord is light, the source of the light of sun, moon and fire, which are his eyes, and of the five elements; he stays within the body and mind as an inner light that gives life and drives out the darkness of maya and bonds. To know the light is to find form and light one, and those it reaches are joined to him as fragrance to a flower."},"t:206":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":206,"title":"தூல பஞ்சாக்கரம்","section":"ஒன்பதாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The gross five letters (தூல பஞ்சாக்கரம்): the fifty letters that make all the Vedas and Agamas resolve into the five letters of Nandi's name, which are placed in the body and recited aloud. Said with the mouth and held in the heart, they make nectar well up, show the way of grace past rebirth, and protect the soul at death; those who ignore them whirl like splashing water."},"t:207":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":207,"title":"சூக்கும பஞ்சாக்கரம்","section":"ஒன்பதாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The subtle five-letter mantra: the letters stand for Siva, his Shakti or grace, the soul, concealment and impurity, and chanting சிவாய, with நம (taken as the bonds) dropped, or simply சிவசிவ, removes the bonds and karma, turns the body to gold, and ends birth in becoming Siva."},"t:208":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":208,"title":"அதிசூக்கும பஞ்சாக்கரம்","section":"ஒன்பதாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The most subtle five letters: chanting சிவாய and சிவசிவ with a fixed mind brings bliss and a heart the Lord never leaves; the one letter of the Lord outweighs all Vedic and Agamic learning, and those who boast of knowing letters do not know the letter that masters them."},"t:209":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":209,"title":"திருக்கூத்து தரிசனம்","section":"ஒன்பதாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The vision of the holy dance: Siva dances everywhere and as everything, with Shakti, at Tillai and beyond all worlds, and his dancing sets the scriptures, the elements, the worlds and the mind dancing. The same dance is placed in the body, in the central channel, between the brows and in the heart, is read off the dancer's hands as the five acts and the five letters, and is known by its effects on those who see it: tears, melting, the end of self, and bliss."},"t:210":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":210,"title":"ஆகாசப் பேறு","section":"ஒன்பதாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The attainment of space (ஆகாசப் பேறு): the Lord who is Om, fire and right order within the heart turns the body into space; he stands as earth, cosmos and what lies beyond, and is known as light, cosmic, bodily and undivided, into which the knower's heart opens as open sky. The chapter ends by naming four spaces, outer, inner, in Siva and in the world, the last being the seat of samadhi."},"t:211":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":211,"title":"ஞானோதயம்","section":"ஒன்பதாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The dawning of knowledge (ஞானோதயம்): Siva is not known by his colour or form but in knowledge he himself gives, through the path of the worthy and the guru; the seeker who searches for 'I' and 'He' is shown they are not two. Images of a lamp lit within a lamp, of gems and of nectar mark the knowledge, which is called the path of nadanta, true yoga and release."},"t:212":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":212,"title":"சத்திய ஞானானந்தம்","section":"ஒன்பதாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Truth, knowledge and bliss (சத்திய ஞானானந்தம்): passing beyond the three voids and the states into the turiya of 'thou' and 'that', the knower sheds malam and the thirty-six tattvas and becomes Siva. The qualities of the Supreme, truth and the rest, stand together as a water lily's form, colour, scent and beauty; the bliss floods the body like milk and honey, and even the thought of the Lord is his grace."},"t:213":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":213,"title":"சொரூப உதயம்","section":"ஒன்பதாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The rising of the true form (சொரூப உதயம்): the Lord pervades the elements so wholly that he cannot be marked off and circled, is beyond form and formlessness, and is reached only through the guru. Those who meditate on his form receive light; the poet sinks into the supreme light, crosses birth, and can say the First is the 'I'."},"t:214":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":214,"title":"ஊழ்","section":"ஒன்பதாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Fate (ஊழ்): what befalls comes by Nandi's decree and one's own former deeds, so the knowers of truth are unchanged whether they are wounded or anointed. The devotee who has gained the Lord stays with him through thunder, fire, storm and wild beasts, ploughs the field of knowledge, and sees the loss of a body as moving to another house."},"t:215":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":215,"title":"சிவ தரிசனம்","section":"ஒன்பதாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The vision of Siva (சிவ தரிசனம்): Siva is not other than the mind, and appears within the mind made clear; he is the subtle hidden thing in which speech and mind vanish, without going, coming or ruin; beyond the Supreme and all dispute, he gives grace in the world as Hara."},"t:216":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":216,"title":"சிவ சொரூப தரிசனம்","section":"ஒன்பதாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Seeing the form of Siva (சிவ சொரூப தரிசனம்): the Lord is consciousness, life and all things yet beyond thought, close as scent in a flower; he wakes the seeker, and what is seen is bliss and light beyond turiya, named by every title and none, beyond words."},"t:217":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":217,"title":"முத்தி பேதம், கரும நிருவாணம்","section":"ஒன்பதாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Kinds of release and the nirvana of karma (முத்தி பேதம், கரும நிருவாணம்): when release is reached the soul attains Siva without distinction and the primal form becomes its own; those who cling to the one without attachments find him their kin and, gaining his light, fall silent."},"t:218":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":218,"title":"சூனிய சம்பாஷணை","section":"ஒன்பதாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The discourse of the void (சூனிய சம்பாஷணை): seventy riddle verses in the images of farming, cattle, trees, birds, trade and boats, which on their surface describe impossible or puzzling things and are traditionally read as a hidden language for the body, the senses, the breath, the tattvas and the way to Siva. The few plain threads are that the senses must be herded and not let run wild, that the pure path lies overgrown and inverted, and that the sweet is left for the bitter."},"t:219":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":219,"title":"மோன சமாதி","section":"ஒன்பதாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Silent samadhi (மோன சமாதி): the state beyond posture, speech and the grades of the Lord's forms, given only by the guru and known only by having it, as a taste is known by eating or a wife's joy by the wife; in it self, mind and the knower dissolve, and the one who stands united sees the Supreme and the ages."},"t:220":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":220,"title":"வரையுரை மாட்சி","section":"ஒன்பதாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The worth of speech about the limitless (வரையுரை மாட்சி): once one has lost one's limits and become the Lord, there is nothing left to bound, think of or choose; those who talk of the unspeakable are mutes, while the Lord is plain to a mind as clear as still water, and the truth is to rest in examining oneself once the mind's maya dies."},"t:221":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":221,"title":"அணைந்தோர் தன்மை","section":"ஒன்பதாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The nature of those who have reached (அணைந்தோர் தன்மை): told mostly in the first person, they are free of stain, pride, caste and bonds; the Lord has entered and ruled them, birth and karma are cut, Yama can be fought off, worldly sweetness turns sour beside his bliss, and the mind's spreading and drawing in, stilled with speech, is release."},"t:222":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":222,"title":"தோத்திரம்","section":"ஒன்பதாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Praise (தோத்திரம்): the Lord pervades the elements, the lights, the worlds and souls, is great beyond the gods' measure and small within the flesh, yet is found within the body and in his devotees' hearts; his feet are every heaven, and naming and praising him cut away deeds, desire and the sea of births."},"t:223":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":223,"title":"சர்வ வியாபி","section":"ஒன்பதாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"The all-pervader (சர்வ வியாபி): the Lord is the inner light in the body, the elements, the sun, the breath and the gods, spread through every direction, world and sea, near and far, many and yet one, and no one can trace his course. The chapter, and the book's last tantra, closes with a verse on Mulan's three thousand Tamil verses."},"t:224":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":224,"title":"வாழ்த்து","section":"ஒன்பதாம் தந்திரம்","theme":"Benediction (வாழ்த்து): the book closes by blessing the feet of Nandi, the guru who cut away impurity, and of the Lord of true knowledge who has none."},"p:1":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1,"title":"விநாயகர் துதி","section":"காப்பு, நூல்","theme":"Praise of Vinayaka (விநாயகர் துதி) opening the book: the elephant-faced god of the offerings, who drives off deeds and helped his younger brother win the hunter girl, and the poet's memory of Murugan's command to sing the Thiruppugazh."},"p:2":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":2,"title":"விநாயகர்","section":"காப்பு, நூல்","theme":"Songs to Vinayaka (விநாயகர்): prayers to the elephant-faced god for grace and a mind fixed on worship, one ritual offering ending in a battlefield, and a first confession of being sunk in women's love."},"p:3":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":3,"title":"நூல்","section":"காப்பு, நூல்","theme":"The opening song of the book proper (நூல்): Murugan named as Devayanai's lord, the seed of release and the guru who taught Siva the first of the Veda, with a plea for the day he will save the poet, and a battle in which demons are offered up."},"p:4":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":4,"title":"திருப்பரங்குன்றம்","section":"அறுபடை வீடு","theme":"Songs at Thiruparankundram (திருப்பரங்குன்றம்): the poet confesses his bondage to women, courtesans who take a man's wealth with false hearts, his neglect of worship, his life from the womb to disease, and his begging from patrons, and asks for Murugan's feet, while the praise turns on the hunter girl, the vel against Sura, Vishnu as uncle and Murugan as teacher of Siva."},"p:5":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":5,"title":"திருச்செந்தூர்","section":"அறுபடை வீடு","theme":"First songs at Thiruchendur (திருச்செந்தூர், 1 of 6): the poet confesses his bondage to courtesans who invite, rob and expel, and to the body that rots and dies, imagines the hour of death and Yama's summons, and asks for Murugan's feet; the praise turns on Valli, Vishnu as uncle, Siva and Uma, and the shore of Senthil."},"p:6":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":6,"title":"திருச்செந்தூர்","section":"அறுபடை வீடு","theme":"Songs at Thiruchendur (திருச்செந்தூர், 2 of 6): the poet confesses his bondage to women, many of them named as selling themselves for money, and to the body as a hut of guts and blood that ends on the bier, and asks for Murugan's feet, his company of devotees, and his coming on the peacock when Death's messengers arrive; the praise turns on the hunter girl, the vel against Sura, Vishnu as uncle and Murugan as Siva's teacher."},"p:7":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":7,"title":"திருச்செந்தூர்","section":"அறுபடை வீடு","theme":"Songs at Thiruchendur (திருச்செந்தூர், 3 of 6): the poet asks to be freed from infatuation with women and courtesans who seek money, from the body of the five elements, from the kin who crowd the deathbed, and from praising petty patrons, and asks for the feet, for daily worship and service, and for Murugan to face Death on his behalf; he also scorns poets who sing love-sickness without naming Senthil."},"p:8":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":8,"title":"திருச்செந்தூர்","section":"அறுபடை வீடு","theme":"Songs at Thiruchendur (திருச்செந்தூர், 4 of 6): the poet confesses bondage to courtesans who lure, rob and expel, the decay of the old body and Death's coming, and his own trade as a poet who praises patrons for gifts and prides himself on titles; he asks for the feet, the six-letter name and the gift of song, and the praise turns on Rama and Vishnu as uncle, Siva burning Kama, and Murugan as Siva's teacher."},"p:9":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":9,"title":"திருச்செந்தூர்","section":"அறுபடை வீடு","theme":"Songs at Thiruchendur (திருச்செந்தூர், 5 of 6): beside the confessions of bondage to courtesans and the decay of the old body, several songs ask for something higher, the One that the gods seek, the yogi's absorbed vision and the breath's ascent to the nectar of the moon, and others confess the poet's begging at patrons' doors; the praise turns on Valli, Siva taught by his son, and Vishnu as uncle."},"p:10":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":10,"title":"திருச்செந்தூர்","section":"அறுபடை வீடு","theme":"Last songs at Thiruchendur (திருச்செந்தூர், 6 of 6): the poet asks for Murugan's feet at the hour of death and at the fading of house, wife and child, confesses his capture by women's eyes and his voyages abroad for money to win them, and traces his body from seed through many burnings; one song speaks for a lovesick girl of the hills. The praise turns on Brahma imprisoned, the vel against Sura and the hill, Siva who drank the poison, and the shore of Alaivay and Senthil."},"p:11":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":11,"title":"பழநி","section":"அறுபடை வீடு","theme":"First songs at Palani (பழநி, 1 of 7): the poet confesses courtesans who sell love, take wealth and embrace without regard to caste, his begging from patrons, and a life run from birth through lust to the pyre, and asks for Murugan's feet, grace, the meaning of the mantra and the work of worship; the praise turns on Murugan as Siva's teacher, as the Tamil saint who defeated the Jains, as the all-pervading tradition, and on the goddess of Palani."},"p:12":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":12,"title":"பழநி","section":"அறுபடை வீடு","theme":"Songs at Palani (பழநி), second part: the poet confesses infatuation with women and courtesans who swear lasting love and take a man's wealth, the bonds of kin and body, neglected worship and the coming of Death, and asks for Murugan's feet; the praise names Murugan as the child born of Siva's grace, as Siva's teacher, and, in 126, as the boy Uruttiracanman who judged the Tamil of the Sangam poets."},"p:13":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":13,"title":"பழநி","section":"அறுபடை வீடு","theme":"Songs at Palani (பழநி), third part, two of them at Thiru Avinankudi at the hill's foot: the poet confesses a life spent on women and courtesans who sell themselves, begging at patrons' doors, the foul and diseased body, and the clamour of rival schools, and asks for Murugan's feet and teaching; the praise makes Murugan the Tamil child of Pukali whom Parvati fed, the lover of Valli, Siva's teacher, and the form of A, U and M."},"p:14":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":14,"title":"பழநி","section":"அறுபடை வீடு","theme":"Songs at Palani (பழநி, 4 of 7): the poet confesses bondage to courtesans who sell their bodies and take a man's wealth, the rotting body and its burning, and his own faults, and asks for Murugan's feet, grace and liberating knowledge; the praise turns on Murugan as Siva's teacher, as the Tamil saint who defeated the Jains, on Vishnu as uncle, and on the shrine's patron from Kalisai."},"p:15":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":15,"title":"பழநி","section":"அறுபடை வீடு","theme":"Songs at Palani (பழநி, 5 of 7): the poet confesses lust and courtesans who take a man's property, his faults and sins, disease and Death's rope, and the vain praise of patrons and court poets, and asks for Murugan's feet, service and knowledge; the praise turns on Murugan as Siva's child guru who spoke the pranava, the race for the fruit that brought him to Palani, the Tamil saint who cured the Pandya, and Vishnu as uncle."},"p:16":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":16,"title":"பழநி","section":"அறுபடை வீடு","theme":"Songs at Palani (பழநி, 6 of 7), many naming the shrine at its foot, Avinankudi: the poet confesses capture by women's charms and by courtesans who take a man's money, and asks for the Lord's feet, for jnana, or simply not to forget him; between these stand a namo-nama litany, a song of kundalini yoga, a positive plea to live by scripture in service, and a self-indictment that lists the virtues he lacks."},"p:17":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":17,"title":"பழநி","section":"அறுபடை வீடு","theme":"Last songs at Palani (பழநி, 7 of 7), several at Avinankudi: the poet sets knowing Murugan above alchemy, traces his life from the womb to ruin among women, lists the body's diseases and the cheats around it, sees women's beauty as maya in form, and asks for the feet, for tears of devotion and for the simple rite of pouring Ganges water on them."},"p:18":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":18,"title":"சுவாமி மலை","section":"அறுபடை வீடு","theme":"First songs at Swamimalai (சுவாமி மலை, also called Tiruverakam and Gurumalai; 1 of 3): pleas against craving and courtesans, against a life spent at patrons' gates and against Death's hour, and several prayers in Siddhanta vocabulary for jnana, union without difference and a silence beyond breath-yoga; the praise keeps to Murugan as guru, as Valli's lover, and as giver of Tamil verse."},"p:19":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":19,"title":"சுவாமி மலை","section":"அறுபடை வீடு","theme":"Swamimalai (சுவாமி மலை, 2 of 3): courtesans and the arts of 'Kama's scripture', the diseased and ageing body, poems wasted on patrons, a lovesick girl's song, and pleas for the release the Agamas teach, for servanthood among devotees, and, uniquely, for Murugan to be born as the poet's own child; the praise returns again and again to Murugan teaching his father Siva."},"p:20":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":20,"title":"சுவாமி மலை","section":"அறுபடை வீடு","theme":"The last songs at Swamimalai (சுவாமி மலை, also called Eragam): the poet confesses the diseases of a sinful life, his spending on women's jewels, and courtesans who dance in the street, drug a man and take his gold, and in the last song his refusal to share with the hungry before Death comes; the praise returns to Murugan as teacher of Siva and Brahma's humbler, and to the Rama story."},"p:21":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":21,"title":"திருத்தணிகை","section":"அறுபடை வீடு","theme":"The first songs at Thiruthani (திருத்தணிகை): beside the usual confessions of courtesans who take a man's wealth, the poet catalogues his diseases, the body as a house of filth, the begging poet's humiliation and a child's plea to his father, and three songs name the Thiruppugazh itself; the praise dwells on Murugan as teacher of Siva and on Thani's blue-lily pool and fields."},"p:22":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":22,"title":"திருத்தணிகை","section":"அறுபடை வீடு","theme":"Songs at Thiruttani (திருத்தணிகை, 2 of 5): the poet turns from women who deceive and rob, from the false pot of the body, and from sectarians who chase gods and gold back into the womb, and asks for the feet, release and the gift of praise; four songs are love laments for a girl wasting for Murugan, and one, 269, praises the Thiruppugazh itself as fire. The praise returns often to Murugan as teacher of Siva and as Sambandar who routed the Jains."},"p:23":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":23,"title":"திருத்தணிகை","section":"அறுபடை வீடு","theme":"Songs at Thiruttani (திருத்தணிகை, 3 of 5): the poet counts his numberless births and deaths, takes apart the body as a pot, bellows and bag of humours, turns from courtesans, flattering court poetry and barren book-learning, and asks for the feet as the raft over the seas of desire, birth and learning; the praise names Murugan as the southern guru who told Siva the meaning of Om, and Thiruttani as Sivaloka, where he is physician of the disease of birth."},"p:24":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":24,"title":"திருத்தணிகை","section":"அறுபடை வீடு","theme":"Songs at Thiruthanigai (திருத்தணிகை, 4 of 5): love-laments for a girl wasting with longing for Murugan, the poet's confessions of infatuation with women and courtesans, of book-babble and begging praise of patrons, and a death song that lets go of wife, children and goods before the buffalo comes; the praise turns on Valli, Vishnu as uncle, Parvati, Murugan as Siva's teacher and as Sambandar, and the hill of Thanigai."},"p:25":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":25,"title":"திருத்தணிகை","section":"அறுபடை வீடு","theme":"Last songs at Thiruthanigai (திருத்தணிகை, 5 of 5): two confessions of infatuation with women, one asking for Murugan's feet before knowledge and life are lost, the other for the art of singing his praise instead of falling into womb after womb; the praise dwells on the town of Thanigai, whose towers clear the deeds of those who worship from afar and where those who chant Thiruppugazh gather."},"p:26":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":26,"title":"குன்றுதோறாடல்","section":"அறுபடை வீடு","theme":"Songs of Kundruthoradal (குன்றுதோறாடல், 'dancing on every hill'), Murugan of all the hills: a lovesick plea in the poet's own voice and one for a girl wasting with longing, a short prayer for refuge, a plea for the samadhi beyond yogis and sages, and a renunciation of praising miserly patrons for one word from Murugan's mouth; the praise names Murugan as the poet of Sambandar's victory over the Jains and as the one who settled the meaning of Pandya Tamil."},"p:27":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":27,"title":"ஆறு திருப்பதி","section":"அறுபடை வீடு","theme":"A song of the six shrines (ஆறு திருப்பதி): the poet confesses his bondage to courtesans who sell their arts and ruin young men's families, and asks for Murugan's feet; the praise names him the guru, Siva's son, and the lord abiding in all six shrines."},"p:28":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":28,"title":"காஞ்சீபுரம்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"First songs at Kanchipuram (காஞ்சீபுரம், 1 of 3): in dense rhythms the poet asks to stand beyond the tattvas and the quarrels of the sects, confesses flattering patrons, the prison of the body, old age and his own worthlessness, and asks for the feet and a poet's speech; the praise dwells unusually long on the Goddess of Kanchi, who did penance by the Kampa and keeps the thirty-two charities, and on Siva and Vishnu worshipping Murugan at Kachi."},"p:29":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":29,"title":"காஞ்சீபுரம்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Songs at Kanchipuram (காஞ்சீபுரம், 2 of 3), nearly all closing on Murugan as the beautiful lord of Kachi (கச்சிச் சொக்கப் பெருமாளே): in tight doubled-consonant rhythms the poet confesses bondage to women, the body of bone and skin, old age, the heirs dividing his goods and the burning of the corpse, and asks for the feet, pure devotees and the gift of Tamil; two songs are love laments for a girl sick for Murugan."},"p:30":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":30,"title":"காஞ்சீபுரம்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Last songs at Kanchipuram (காஞ்சீபுரம், 3 of 3): the poet confesses infatuation with women and courtesans who take his wealth, the body's old age and death, and his own trade of praising unworthy patrons for money, and asks for the feet, a truth beyond the quarrelling sects, and a place among the devotees; the praise dwells on Uma's penance at Kanchi by the Kampa river, Krishna in the Bharata war, and Murugan as Siva's teacher."},"p:31":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":31,"title":"திருவானைக்கா","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Songs at Thiruvanaikka (திருவானைக்கா), the elephant forest by the Kaveri: the poet confesses bondage to courtesans who sell love by the measure of wealth, the body as a hut of filth, his faults and his trade of begging patrons, rejects the breath-holding yogi who would make the body last, and asks for the feet, Siva-knowledge beyond the stages of practice, and service; the praise names Ganesa, Uma as Akhilandeswari, the Ramayana, the spider and the jambu tree of the shrine, and Murugan as the Tamil child who won at Madurai."},"p:32":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":32,"title":"திருவருணை","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"First songs at Thiruvannamalai (திருவருணை, 1 of 6): the poet renounces women, sects, patron-poetry and birth, vows not to forget the word and the feet given him at Arunai, sees each charm of a woman as Death's weapon, and pictures the funeral and Yama's noose; three songs are laments of a lovesick girl tormented by the moon, the flute and the town's talk. The praise dwells on Arunai's tall tower, Murugan as teacher of Siva, and his love for Valli."},"p:33":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":33,"title":"திருவருணை","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"More songs at Thiruvannamalai (திருவருணை, 2 of 6): the poet confesses to deceitful women who call wealthy men home, to eyes as deadly as the churned poison, and to a body bent by age and bound for hell; he pictures Yama coming while wife, sons and kin weep, asks for the silent seal the sects cannot know, and in three songs speaks for a girl sick with love. The praise names the Goddess in long litanies, Unnamulai of Arunai, Siva who could not be found by Vishnu and Brahma, and Murugan who hid his form to follow Valli."},"p:34":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":34,"title":"திருவருணை","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Songs at Thiruvannamalai (திருவருணை, 3 of 6): the poet confesses embraces bought from courtesans whose love lasts as long as the money, the body's foulness and the ruin of old age, and asks for Murugan's feet; two songs speak for a girl sick with love; the praise centres on the tower and hill of Arunai, Murugan as Siva's guru, and stories of Siva for his devotees."},"p:35":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":35,"title":"திருவருணை","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Songs at Thiruvannamalai (திருவருணை, 4 of 6): the poet confesses infatuation with women, the body's birth, decay and corpse, and the hour of Death, and asks for Murugan's feet, for release and for a place among his devotees; one song is a love-lament in a girl's voice and one a chant of the five syllables joined to the name of Arunachala; the praise returns to the hill of fire, the tower, Murugan as Siva's teacher and as the saint who defeated the Jains."},"p:36":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":36,"title":"திருவருணை","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Songs at Thiruvannamalai (திருவருணை, 5 of 6): the poet confesses a counterfeit asceticism worn for gold, the trade of flattering patrons, and his bondage to courtesans who cheat and sell, sets out the diseased and maya-bound body, and asks for the feet, for release and for the gift of song; three love-laments in a woman's voice ask Murugan to come; the praise turns on the vel against Sura, Valli and Devayanai, and Murugan standing in the streets and at the tower of Arunai."},"p:37":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":37,"title":"திருவருணை","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Last songs at Thiruvannamalai (திருவருணை, 6 of 6): a life wasted by fate in women's craft that asks to reach the feet through Tamil song; a whole life traced from the drop in the womb to the pyre that leaves not a handful of ash; and a plea for the feet that are Jnana-Siva; the praise centres on Murugan coming on the peacock to the tower of Arunai, the hill of fire."},"p:38":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":38,"title":"திருக்காளத்தி","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Songs at Kalahasti (திருக்காளத்தி): the poet sets household life and the body that ends as carrion against Siva-knowledge and the golden feet, asks the Lord to rule him without bowing to others or clinging to the world, and asks, troubled in a body of five elements, for a steady mind; the praise names Murugan the guru on Siva's shoulders and Kalahasti as the southern Kailasa."},"p:39":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":39,"title":"சிதம்பரம்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"First songs at Chidambaram (சிதம்பரம், 1 of 5): two litanies of pure praise and short pleas for vision, Siva's nectar and the five syllables open the chapter; the rest confess bondage to courtesans who sell, take money in advance, cheat and leave disease and sores behind; the praise turns on Murugan as guru to his father, on Siva and Sivakami dancing, and on Murugan with Valli in the golden hall (கனகசபை, திருச்சிற்றம்பலம்)."},"p:40":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":40,"title":"சிதம்பரம்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Songs at Chidambaram (சிதம்பரம், 2 of 5): the poet confesses his capture by women, their arts, their magic and their price, and once by the five senses and once by kin, wealth and learning, and asks for Murugan's feet, love and release; two songs ask to hold Murugan's form in mind even in the women's embrace. The praise centres on the hall (அம்பலம்) where Siva dances, Sivakami, and Murugan's place beside them."},"p:41":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":41,"title":"சிதம்பரம்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Songs at Chidambaram (சிதம்பரம், 3 of 5), many sung at its towers (கோபுரம்): the poet confesses to women who demand money, plunder and cheat, catalogues the body and its diseases, imagines his death and cremation, and asks for the feet, wisdom and the company of devotees; the praise turns on Siva's dance at Puliyur, Murugan as Siva's teacher, and the gopurams of the temple."},"p:42":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":42,"title":"சிதம்பரம்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Songs at Chidambaram (புலியூர்): the poet confesses his fall to courtesans who sell themselves, the pomp of wealth that vanishes in a moment, the life that runs from the womb to Death, and the confusion of the Vedas and sects; he asks for the reality beyond form and the formless, for the lamp of knowledge rather than an ascetic's outward marks, and for the yogic ascent to the dancing Lord's hall."},"p:43":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":43,"title":"சிதம்பரம்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"The last songs at Chidambaram (புலியூர்): courtesans who take a man's wealth, the body as a pot of filth for dogs and flies, and the slide from lust to old age, death and the burning ground; against these the poet asks for teaching in the meaning of Siva's emblems, for release, for the yogic ascent through the letters of the five-syllable mantra, and he speaks to his own mind."},"p:44":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":44,"title":"கயிலைமலை","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Songs at Kailasa (கயிலைமலை): two yogic pleas for the self to become Siva's form in light, nada and bliss, three confessions of courtesans who take a man's wealth, a life from the womb, and a short prayer for the gift of song that Sambandar had; the praise dwells on Siva's dance, his swallowing of the poison and the demon who lifted Kailasa."},"p:45":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":45,"title":"ஸ்ரீ சைலம் திருமலை","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"The one song at Srisailam (திருமலை): the poet asks to know the constituents of the self, reach the feet within, and see the six-faced Lord, rather than wander the streets in idle talk."},"p:46":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":46,"title":"திருவேங்கடம்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Songs at Tiruvenkatam (வடமலை): the body's old age, the women's net, the fire of desire and the sea of sects, and Death taking the soul alone, against which the poet asks to serve the Lord's servants before the body ripens, to chant the six-syllable name and to escape hell; the praise, at Vishnu's hill, dwells on Murugan as nephew of Rama and Krishna."},"p:47":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":47,"title":"வள்ளிமலை","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Songs at Vallimalai (வள்ளிமலை), the hill where Murugan wooed Valli: the poet confesses the lure of women, the body as a bag of filth, sectarian wrangling and a household life that ends on the pyre, and asks for the feet before Death comes; the praise keeps returning to the hill itself, the valli creeper and the god who bowed at the hunter girl's feet."},"p:48":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":48,"title":"திருக்கழுக்குன்றம்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Songs at Thirukkazhukundram (திருக்கழுக்குன்றம்), the Vedagiri worshipped by eagles: the poet confesses a life spent on women, children and gold until grey age and Yama's rope, and the sea of birth sunk in passion; the praise centres on Murugan as Sambandar and as the teacher of the pranava who bowed at the hunter girl's feet."},"p:49":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":49,"title":"பேறைநகர்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"A single song at Peraiyur (பேறைநகர்): the poet asks whether he must wander, blind with infatuation, among women's hair, faces, eyes and breasts; the praise makes Murugan nephew of Vishnu as the Boar and the Man-Lion."},"p:50":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":50,"title":"மயிலம்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"A single song at Mayilam (மயிலம்): the poet, melting like wax before women's eyes, asks Murugan to come when Yama's messengers take his life; the praise turns on Murugan himself infatuated with the hunter girl and falling at her feet."},"p:51":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":51,"title":"திருசிராப்பள்ளி","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Songs at Thiruchirappalli (திருசிராப்பள்ளி, Trisira hill), the first fifteen: most confess bondage to courtesans who sell their arts and refuse a man without gold, one torments the poet with the tortures of Yama's servants, and a few turn inward to the mind, the guru's secret and the Reality beyond speech and yoga; the praise names Murugan as Sambandar, as the guru who took the poet publicly, and as the son of the Lord of the rock, often with Vayalur beside it."},"p:52":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":52,"title":"திருசிராப்பள்ளி","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"The last song at Thiruchirappalli (திருசிராப்பள்ளி): the poet confesses delusion by poisonous, deceitful women who look on a man with contempt once his money is gone, and yet vows never to forget, in dream or waking, the fragrant feet that gave release; the praise centres on the vel that saved Indra and the hunters' submission."},"p:53":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":53,"title":"திருக்கற்குடி","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Songs at Thirukkarkudi (திருக்கற்குடி): the poet confesses the sea of union with women whose breasts overpower even ascetics, and the wealth poured out on them, and fears Yama's messengers who will open his account and torture him by its total; he asks for the feet and for Murugan to come on the peacock, and praises him as the god who was himself caught by the hunter girl's eye."},"p:54":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":54,"title":"இரத்னகிரி","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Songs at Ratnagiri (இரத்னகிரி): the poet confesses he has lost goodness and sense to women's eyes, hair and breasts and to the five senses that cheat him toward death, and in a short song asks to sing the Thiruppugazh with devotion until he is freed; the praise names the vel against Sura and the jewel hill."},"p:55":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":55,"title":"விராலிமலை","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"First songs at Viralimalai (விராலிமலை, 1 of 2): the poet confesses bondage to courtesans who sell their bodies, to his own flattery of misers and his poems on women, and to the malas, and asks for the feet, for samadhi and for the teaching of jnana beyond the tattvas; the praise names Vayalur, where Murugan commanded the poet to sing, and identifies him with Tamil saints."},"p:56":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":56,"title":"விராலிமலை","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"The last song at Viralimalai (விராலிமலை, 2 of 2): drowned in the maya of women who deceive the body, the poet asks when Murugan will come running as guard before Yama's messengers bind his legs with their rope; the praise centres on Uma, one with Brahma, Vishnu and Siva, who gave Murugan."},"p:57":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":57,"title":"விநாயகமலை - பிள்ளையார்பட்டி","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"A song at Vinayakamalai, Pillaiyarpatti (விநாயகமலை - பிள்ளையார்பட்டி): a litany of salutations to Murugan by his names, born in Saravana, son of Uma, root of this world and the next, asking only for grace, with praise of the vel that shook the worlds and of Vishnu and Brahma worshipping his feet."},"p:58":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":58,"title":"திருச்செங்கோடு","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"First songs at Tiruchengodu (திருச்செங்கோடு, 1 of 2), also called Nagagiri: the poet confesses his infatuation with courtesans and their arts, his begging songs to hard-hearted patrons, and a life of births bound by malam, and asks for the feet, for union with the devotees and for the wisdom of Agastya; the praise names Murugan the teacher of Siva and gives the Ramayana in brief."},"p:59":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":59,"title":"திருச்செங்கோடு","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Last songs at Thiruchengode (திருச்செங்கோடு, 2 of 2), the serpent hill: the poet asks not to fall with his impure body into hell, to be freed from the tangle of wealth-lust and women, and from the illusion that a bubble-like life with wife, sons and house will last for aeons; he gives thanks for the grace that set him to sing the Thiruppugazh, and asks Murugan to come on the peacock to the battle in which his servant defeats Death."},"p:60":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":60,"title":"கொல்லிமலை","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Songs at the Kolli hills (கொல்லிமலை): the poet laments the jasmine-smiling women who took his gold and then said there was none, and asks for the bliss that stands beneath the whole ladder of being, from the one and the two to the Vedas, senses, tattvas, souls and bonds."},"p:61":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":61,"title":"ராஜகெம்பீரவளநாட்டு மலை","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"A song at the hill of Rajagembira Valanadu (ராஜகெம்பீரவளநாட்டு மலை): the poet, wasting with women whose every limb outdoes a thing of beauty and who seek coins, asks for his impurity to be removed and for the vision of the supreme truth in which the soul becomes one; the praise tells the wrecking of Daksha's sacrifice."},"p:62":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":62,"title":"ஞானமலை","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Songs at Gnanamalai (ஞானமலை): the poet rejects the diseased, bargaining women of the street who stand beside gamblers and killers, and, mocked by wife, town and kin, asks at death for the feet he was once given, the feet that melted him and gave the experience of yoga."},"p:63":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":63,"title":"ஊதிமலை","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Songs at Oothimalai (ஊதிமலை): the poet, ignorant of the Siva Agamas and given to the desire for women, asks for mercy, firm knowledge and instruction; the praise makes Murugan the teacher who spoke the Vedic mantra into Siva's ear and the light that is the life of the letter Om."},"p:64":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":64,"title":"குருடிமலை","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"A song at Kurudimalai (குருடிமலை): the poet says he cannot understand the ways of poets who flatter men of the world as Vishnu and patch together stolen verses for the ignorant; the praise is of the flooding river at the hill."},"p:65":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":65,"title":"தென்சேரிகிரி","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Songs at Thenserigiri (தென்சேரிகிரி): the poet asks to be freed from courtesans who give their lips and take a man's wealth and life, and from the infatuation of dancing women, and asks for the meaning once told to Siva; the praise dwells on Krishna's flute, the gopis' clothes and Kamsa."},"p:66":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":66,"title":"கொங்கணகிரி","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"A song at Konganagiri (கொங்கணகிரி): a litany of requests, for control of the senses, love in Tamil praise, knowledge from austerity and the way to release, a mind turned from women to Murugan, and worldly honour besides."},"p:67":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":67,"title":"தீர்த்தமலை","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"A song at Theerthamalai (தீர்த்தமலை): the poet rebukes his own mind, which neither melts at song nor fears the coming of Death, and urges it to cling to Murugan; the praise tells Siva's deeds and Sambandar's ordeals by water and fire."},"p:68":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":68,"title":"கநகமலை","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"A song at Kanagamalai (கநகமலை): the poet asks to give up the bewildering pleasure of women and to see Murugan's faces, shining like sun and moon, so that all sorrow leaves him and he bows at his feet."},"p:69":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":69,"title":"புகழிமலை","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"A song at Pugazhimalai (புகழிமலை): the poet fears drowning in the sea of women, possessions, wife and children, and asks Murugan to come on the peacock with the life of the lotus feet."},"p:70":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":70,"title":"பூம்பறை","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"A song at Poomparai (பூம்பறை): the poet asks whether a relationship with price-women who speak of kinship and seek out rich houses can be right; the praise names Krishna dancing on the serpent."},"p:71":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":71,"title":"கொடுங்குன்றம்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Songs at Kodunkundram (கொடுங்குன்றம்): the poet asks Murugan to look on his crippled service, bent by women, and promises never to forget the dancing feet even as the body perishes in the sea of lust; one song's praise is a long drum-syllable battle."},"p:72":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":72,"title":"குன்றக்குடி","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Songs at Kundrakudi (குன்றக்குடி), also named Mayuragiri, the peacock hill: the poet asks to leave the sorrow of women, the net of lovemaking that wastes his knowing mind, and the love-sickness of moon, cuckoo and Kama's arrows; the praise names Murugan as teacher of Siva and Agastya, Rama's and Krishna's nephew, and the vel at which Death's servants tremble."},"p:73":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":73,"title":"பொதியமலை","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Songs at Pothiyamalai (பொதியமலை), Agastya's mountain: the poet asks not to be debased by the beds of wicked women, nor to wither, diseased and laughed at by kin, but to be called near, given a place among the great ascetics and given the vel and peacock; the praise names Murugan as the one who told the word Om when Siva danced."},"p:74":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":74,"title":"கழுகுமலை","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Songs at Kazhugumalai (கழுகுமலை): the poet confesses the bitter infatuation with women who sell their breasts and seize men's wealth, refuses any longer to plead his poverty before base men, and asks to swim the wave of births to the pure feet; the praise names Rama, Murugan's vel given by Siva, and Murugan become a vengai tree for Valli."},"p:75":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":75,"title":"வள்ளியூர்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"A short song at Valliyur (வள்ளியூர்): the body is an illusion like lightning at night, and the poet asks whether he must suffer along every stony road; the praise names the vel that ruined the Suras' town and Murugan between his two consorts."},"p:76":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":76,"title":"கதிர்காமம்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Songs at Kathirkamam (கதிர்காமம்) in Ilam, the Lord of the hill among waterfalls, fruit trees and monkeys: the poet confesses courting women and flattering patrons, the household that eats up a life, and the hour of Death's torturers, and asks for bhakti, the feet and the stainless state of sayujya; two songs lament a lovesick girl, and two are praise alone."},"p:77":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":77,"title":"திருக்கோணமலை","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"The one song at Thirukkonamalai (திருக்கோணமலை) in Lanka: the poet confesses drowning in desire for women who sell their bodies, mocked by the world and sick in a body of filth, and asks to be saved by singing Murugan in verse; the praise makes Murugan the meaning that comes alive in the verses of the sages and Tamil poets."},"p:78":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":78,"title":"காசி","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Songs at Kasi (காசி) on the Ganga: a sinner and gambler who never wore the holy ash asks to see the feet and gain jnana; a life traced from conception in the womb to the women who ruin it; and a body gone hollow like a wood-apple, with Murugan as the victor over the Jains."},"p:79":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":79,"title":"மாயாபுரி","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"The one song at Mayapuri (மாயாபுரி): a short and obscure song that seems to read the letters சி, அ and ம as Sivajnana, scattered desire and the fall into maya, and asks for grace not to dwell on maya."},"p:80":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":80,"title":"வயிரவிவனம்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"The one song at Vayiravivanam (வயிரவிவனம்) on the Saraswati river: a plea for the jnana to bow at the feet of those who keep Murugan's feet in mind, with Murugan as the guru who told Brahma the first word."},"p:81":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":81,"title":"வெள்ளிகரம்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Songs at Vellikaram (வெள்ளிகரம்), the 'silver town' (வெள்ளிநகர்) among paddy fields near Valli's hill: the poet confesses his infatuation with women and his body ruled by the five senses, and asks for the silence beyond words, for the instruction that melts the heart, and for words to proclaim Murugan; the praise turns again and again on Valli and on Murugan himself made a lover."},"p:82":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":82,"title":"திருவல்லம்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"The one song at Thiruvallam (திருவல்லம்): the body as a walking hut of skin, flesh and bone, and a plea for the strength to proclaim Murugan before the devotees before the senses carry the life away."},"p:83":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":83,"title":"வேலூர்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Songs at Vellore (வேலூர்), a rich town of mango, honey and sugar presses: the poet confesses bondage to courtesans who welcome givers and drive off the rest, and to women who destroy young men, and asks for the feet and a truthful worship; the praise tells Sambandar's victory over the Jains at Madurai as Murugan's own."},"p:84":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":84,"title":"விரிஞ்சிபுரம்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Songs at Virinchipuram (விரிஞ்சிபுரம்), also named Thiruvirinchai and Karapuram in the songs: the poet confesses the courtesans who play their lovers against each other and take his wealth, his neglect of rites, austerity and giving, and the five elements he cannot cast off; he asks for the feet, for love that melts, and for the formless state, and the praise names Murugan as teacher of Siva and as the Tamil child who impaled the Jains."},"p:85":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":85,"title":"திருவாலங்காடு","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Songs at Thiruvalangadu (திருவாலங்காடு), where Siva danced with Kali: the poet confesses scheming women, the body as a nest of Death, the tattvas that make up the body, and Death's messenger with the noose, and asks for the feet, the supreme state and the gift of Tamil praise; one song, sung at Thiruvothur, confesses praising unworthy patrons and names Murugan as Sambandar."},"p:86":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":86,"title":"பாக்கம்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Two songs at Pakkam (பாக்கம்) on the body: food for crows, dogs and vultures at the pyre, carried by Death's trident, and the hut that wanders town to town in craving; the poet asks for the learning to string Murugan's names together and for the senses driven out so he can enter the house of release."},"p:87":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":87,"title":"திருவேற்காடு","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Two songs at Thiruverkadu (திருவேற்காடு): the poet confesses the women's eyes, breasts and waist that keep him taking bodies, and his lust as a great sinner, and asks when the round of births will end and whether Murugan will look on him, join him to the devotees and give him one word, as he rescued Nakkirar."},"p:88":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":88,"title":"வடதிருமுல்லைவாயில்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Songs at Vadathirumullaivayil (வடதிருமுல்லைவாயில்): the poet asks whether grace will leave him long in darkness, confesses courtesans who take a man home by a trick and rob him, and asks not to fall into the snare of women's eyes; the praise names Murugan as Siva's guru, the Goddess of the burning ground, and Siva the Masilamani who defeated Kali."},"p:89":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":89,"title":"திருவலிதாயம்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"One short song at Thiruvalithayam (திருவலிதாயம்): the poet asks not to wander deluded by women but to be given a mind able to honour Murugan's feet."},"p:90":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":90,"title":"திருவொற்றியூர்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Two songs at Thiruvotriyur (திருவொற்றியூர், Adhipuri): the poet asks for knowledge before the world mocks him as a maniac of love, and not to be born again in a woman's womb but to reach the Siva-state; the praise names Murugan the guru whom Nataraja praises."},"p:91":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":91,"title":"திருமயிலை","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Songs at Mylapore (திருமயிலை): the poet confesses the eyes, speech and breasts of women and of the courtesans who sell love, the bad company and the deceit of his life, and his failure to think of the feet, worship or keep silence, and asks to join the devotees and see Murugan; the praise names the Goddess in long litanies, Murugan as guru who whispered into Siva's ear, and Sambandar raising the merchant's daughter at Mylai."},"p:92":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":92,"title":"திருவான்மியூர்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"One song at Thiruvanmiyur (திருவான்மியூர்): the poet confesses he wandered in union with women of no virtue instead of seeking Murugan, and asks for the lotus feet; the praise names Murugan as the expert who gave knowledge into Siva's two ears."},"p:93":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":93,"title":"கோசைநகர்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"One song at Kosainagar (கோசைநகர்): the poet asks not to wander in desire for women's bodies but to think each day of the feet whose anklets resound with the hundred crore Vedas."},"p:94":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":94,"title":"பெருங்குடி","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"One song at Perungudi (பெருங்குடி) that tells a whole life in a row of verbs, from ten months in the womb to the burning, and asks whether the poet will end this birth that ends in fire."},"p:95":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":95,"title":"மாடம்பாக்கம்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Two songs at Madambakkam (மாடம்பாக்கம்) on the courtesans: their charms one by one, and the whole cycle of the bargain, the bed, the robbery, the beating and the new man; the poet asks to be ruled at the lotus feet, and the praise names Murugan as the light of the Agamas beyond maya and as teacher of Siva."},"p:96":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":96,"title":"கோடைநகர்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Songs at Kodainagar (கோடைநகர்): two twin songs trace a life from the womb through play, women and the search for wealth to the edge of hell, two short akam songs plead for a lovesick girl, one catalogues the brutes who fall into Yama's pit, and others ask for the feet at the hour of death and for the yogic stilling of breath that ends dying."},"p:97":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":97,"title":"திருப்பேரூர்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Songs at Tiruporur (the heading reads திருப்பேரூர், the songs திருப்போரூர்): three confessions of bondage to women, one naming the courtesans' messengers, betel and greed, end in a plea for grace or for the feet, and a fourth song is a litany of epithets for Murugan, the sun that clears the darkness of ego."},"p:98":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":98,"title":"உத்தரமேரூர்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Songs at Uttaramerur (உத்தரமேரூர், வடமேரு): the poet asks to know by inner knowledge the feet that all the gods and sages fail to know, to be rid of the body built of skin and phlegm, to be looked on despite a life spent chasing women and wealth and singing for worthless men, and to be freed from the sea of desire; one praise recalls Siva claiming Sundarar as his slave."},"p:99":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":99,"title":"மதுராந்தகம்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Songs at Madurantakam (மதுராந்தகம்), at its northern Chirrambalam: the poet asks to cling to the feet as a raft instead of the leaking body, asks to be taught the instruction Murugan once gave Siva, and asks to leave house, kin and wealth for the vastness of silence."},"p:100":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":100,"title":"சேயூர்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"A single song under the heading Seyur (சேயூர்): the poet asks whether he must perish in the beds of women who bewitch all with their eyes, and praises the vel that turned his two deeds to dust."},"p:101":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":101,"title":"திருவக்கரை","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Songs at Tiruvakkarai (திருவக்கரை): the poet asks to stop babbling scriptures without speaking of Murugan, to escape the hell the womb leads to and the round of births, and to be counted among his servants; and asks to be freed from courtesans who paint their faces and grasp at money while the world says 'shame'."},"p:102":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":102,"title":"சிறுவை","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Songs at Siruvai (சிறுவை, சிறுவாபுரி): a plea that Murugan come dancing with his peacock in sight of all the gods, a litany of salutations, a plea to escape birth through the feet that opened the way of silence, and a confession about courtesans with a long string of the goddess's names; the town is praised as the place where two boys fought Rama."},"p:103":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":103,"title":"திருவாமாத்தூர்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Songs at Tiruvamathur (திருவாமாத்தூர், மாதை): four confessions of bondage to women, seen as deceivers of the young, as Death in a woman's form, as common women singing to the measure of the coin, and as the snare that keeps the poet sunk in caste and clan; he asks for the feet and to stop swinging like a balance beam between devotion and desire."},"p:104":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":104,"title":"தச்சூர்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"A single song at Tachur (தச்சூர்): the courtesan's flattering speech, which ranks the client above Murugan himself, and the poet's question whether he will go mad with desire for such women; the praise recalls Murugan teaching the poet 'the eighth letter'."},"p:105":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":105,"title":"திருக்கோவலூர்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"A single song at Tirukkovalur (திருக்கோவலூர், கோவை மாநகர்): the poet asks whether he will cease to be caught by women's breasts, eyes, hair, lips and smiles, and praises the supreme lord whom the Vedas and the gods cannot see, in a town loud with Vedic chant and festival."},"p:106":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":106,"title":"தேவனூர்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Songs at Devanur (தேவனூர்), all three without a confession about women: the poet asks to pass beyond the counted tattvas and the scriptures into the silence where craving ends, asks to stand praising Murugan's names, and declares Murugan the one reality beyond form, speech, Veda, bindu and nada, turiya and beyond."},"p:107":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":107,"title":"திருவதிகை","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Songs at Tiruvathigai (திருவதிகை) on the Kedilam river, where Siva burned the three cities: two confessions about common women and their bed, one asking to be rid of the mind's whirling, the other to escape the sea of births and the two deeds by naming Murugan's twelve arms; one praise calls Murugan the poet who received his songs at the wish of the Lady of Kailasa."},"p:108":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":108,"title":"திருவர்முர்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"A short song at Thiruvamur (headed திருவர்முர்): a voice sick with love says the burning of the cool moon, Kama's bow and the beating sea leave the night as long as an aeon, and turns to Kumaresa on the peacock, life of his loving devotees, who makes Death turn his back."},"p:109":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":109,"title":"வடுகூர்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"A song at Vadugur (வடுகூர்): the poet praises Siva in his acts, the laugh that burned the cities, Ganga in his hair, the eye that felled Kama, and asks grace to worship Murugan's feet calling him the Kumara who gladdens Siva's heart."},"p:110":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":110,"title":"திருத்துறையூர்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Songs at Thiruthuraiyur (திருத்துறையூர்): the poet asks whether company with ornamented singing women befits him, and fears fate, Death's rope, the pyre and hell; he asks for the flower feet, and praises Murugan as the guru who taught and as lord of the shrine where gods and sages receive grace."},"p:111":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":111,"title":"திருநாவலூர்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"A love song at Thirunavalur (திருநாவலூர்): a speaker wasting with longing in the evening, under Kama's arrows, the cuckoo and the poisonous moon, asks Murugan to come daily to embrace; the praise names the vel and the shrine where he freed the gods."},"p:112":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":112,"title":"திருவெண்ணெய்நல்லூர்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"A yogic song at Thiruvennainallur (திருவெண்ணெய்நல்லூர்): the poet asks to burn the tattvas, still the breath and enter the space of Siva and the dance in the golden hall; the praise names Murugan as the poet who impaled the Jains and the old man who came to Valli."},"p:113":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":113,"title":"திருப்பாதிரிப்புலியூர்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"A song at Pathiripuliyur (திருப்பாதிரிப்புலியூர்): the poet describes the body as a diseased nine-holed hut of flesh and bone, confesses he has no austerity, japa or love, and asks Murugan to come on the peacock and make clear the Upanishads; the praise names Rama's arrow and the vel."},"p:114":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":114,"title":"திருமாணிகுழி","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"A song at Thirumanikuzhi (திருமாணிகுழி): the poet, calling himself a dog, confesses he has drowned in women's breasts and lips and has not sought Murugan's anklets, shoulders, weapons and peacock; the praise is a battle scene and the shrine's groves and ponds."},"p:115":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":115,"title":"திருவேட்களம்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Songs at Thiruvetkalam (திருவேட்களம்): in one the poet vows never to forget the vision of Murugan's feet, faces and gracious look that ended his impurity; in the other he confesses chasing gold for women and praising fools as gods, and asks whether he must wander so."},"p:116":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":116,"title":"திருநெல்வாயில்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"A song at Thirunelvayil (திருநெல்வாயில்): the poet confesses he sang fine Tamil to fools, misers and cheats and now lies in the fire of poverty like a writhing worm, and asks for pity; the praise sets the joy of the heavenly women against the grief of the demons' widows and names the vel that split the mango in the sea."},"p:117":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":117,"title":"விருத்தாசலம்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Songs at Viruthachalam (விருத்தாசலம், முதுகிரி): the poet rejects courtesans who sell themselves and strip men of wealth, asks for grace among the loveless, and describes old age with staff and failing eyes as a birth to be freed from; the praise names Murugan who taught Siva and impaled the Jains."},"p:118":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":118,"title":"வேப்பூர்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"A song at Veppur (வேப்பூர்): the poet asks Murugan to speak to him with his own word the form of the experience that shines to those who, with both kinds of deeds gone, sleep in awareness; the praise names the burning of the sea and the demons' widows entering the fire."},"p:119":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":119,"title":"நிம்பபுரம்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"A song at Nimbapuram (நிம்பபுரம்): the poet asks for the one release beyond elements, organs, day and night, beginning and end, and for delight in Tamil to sing the feet that went into the forest for the hunter girl."},"p:120":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":120,"title":"வேப்பஞ்சந்தி","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"A short song at Veppanchandhi (வேப்பஞ்சந்தி): the poet asks that his gaze not sink into women's breasts and praises the giver of lotus feet to devotees, the warrior vel, and Ganapati's younger brother."},"p:121":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":121,"title":"திருக்கூடலையாற்றூர்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"A song at Koodalaiyatrur (திருக்கூடலையாற்றூர்): the poet confesses that deeds, the three desires and women's charms brought disease, and asks that when Death's summons comes Kalan not approach; the praise names Siva at Madurai bearing sand, the goddess who burned Death, and Murugan who lives in the poet's heart."},"p:122":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":122,"title":"கடம்பூர்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"A song at Kadambur (கடம்பூர்): the poet describes dancing women who invite a man in, demand money and leave in spite when it is not enough, and asks not to go where they are; the praise names Vishnu as uncle and Siva's dance sung by Narada and Tumburu."},"p:123":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":123,"title":"திருவரத்துறை","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"A song at Thiruvarathurai (திருவரத்துறை): the poet asks whether he should fall to young women who flirt, steal hearts and wealth and exhaust a man on the bed; the praise makes Murugan the four-Veda child of Sirkazhi to whom the Lord of the shrine gave the pearl palanquin."},"p:124":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":124,"title":"யாழ்ப்பாணாயன்பட்டினம்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"A song at Yazhppanayanpattinam (யாழ்ப்பாணாயன்பட்டினம், Jaffna): the poet asks to be rid of desire for women whose eyes are Kama's arrows and Death's messengers and who bind the rich with illusion, and to gain knowledge by Murugan's word; the praise is a litany of the goddess and Murugan as guru and giver of Tamil."},"p:125":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":125,"title":"ஸ்ரீமுஷ்டம்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Songs at Srimushnam (ஸ்ரீமுஷ்டம், திருமுட்டம்): one asks Murugan to appear wearing every kind of pearl garland on the peacock with his consort and devotees; the other rejects courtesans who snatch money and ruin families; the praise names the goddess, Devayanai, and the daughter of Adi Varaha."},"p:126":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":126,"title":"திருநல்லூர்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"A yogic song at Thirunallur (திருநல்லூர்): the poet describes the ascent from the root centre through the three mandalas and bindu and nada, up the central pillar on the rope of knowledge, and asks whether he will meet the light of a hundred crore moons; the praise names the goddess and the saint who imposed the ash on the Jains."},"p:127":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":127,"title":"திருமயேந்திரம்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"A song at Thirumayendram (திருமயேந்திரம்): the poet describes women who rejoice at gold and, when it is gone, hand a man the begging bowl and staff and poison him, and asks whether he must wander as a dog; the praise names the vel that ground the asuras while Death swung his noose."},"p:128":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":128,"title":"சீகாழி","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Songs at Sirkazhi (சீகாழி, also called Pugali, Sanbai, Kochchai, Kazhumalam and Brahmapuram): the poet confesses to courtesans who take money, to the decaying body and to delusion, and two songs voice a lovesick girl; the praise again and again makes Murugan the child-saint of Sirkazhi who sang Tamil at Madurai, cured the Pandya with holy ash and sent the Jains to the stakes."},"p:129":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":129,"title":"கரியவனகர்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"The single song at Kariyavanagar (கரியவனகர்): a confession to courtesans who take money and turn away the penniless, a plea to be shown the feet, and praise of the battle and of Murugan who listens to songs in Tamil."},"p:130":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":130,"title":"வைத்தீசுரன் கோயில்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Songs at Vaitheeswaran Koyil (வைத்தீசுரன் கோயில், the Velur of the physician Lord): the poet confesses the darkness of mala, countless births in delusion and the lure of courtesans, and asks for the feet of wisdom, the company of devotees and the yogic ascent to nectar; the praise names Murugan as lord of the healer's shrine."},"p:131":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":131,"title":"திருக்கடவூர்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Songs at Thirukkadavur (திருக்கடவூர்), where Siva kicked Death: the poet pictures the fated body as food for vultures and asks Murugan to come on the peacock at the hour it falls, and asks for release through yoga; both songs name Siva's defeat of Death."},"p:132":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":132,"title":"திருப்படிக்கரை","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"The single song at Thiruppadikkarai (திருப்படிக்கரை): the poet asks to turn from courtesans who strip a man's wealth to praising the shrine and singing Murugan's Thiruppugazh; the praise is Rama's war and the father who praises Murugan's Tamil."},"p:133":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":133,"title":"மாயூரம்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"The single song at Mayuram (மாயூரம், the peacock town, Sikandiyur): a confession of infatuation with women whose lips feed lust, and a praise that names Murugan as the one who cleared the poet's mala, bade him sing and praised his verse."},"p:134":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":134,"title":"பாகை","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Songs at Pagai (பாகை): the poet asks to be spared Kama's arrows, women, the begging of patrons, the body's diseases and the burning ground, and asks for Sivayoga and the silent bliss; the praise names Murugan as the Tamil hero who straightened the Pandya's hunch, and describes the town's ponds, groves and towers."},"p:135":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":135,"title":"திருவிடைக்கழி","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Songs at Thiruvidaikkazhi (திருவிடைக்கழி), the shrine of the kura tree: the poet confesses useless learning, the body's hut, courtesans who take money and kick a man out, and the ocean of birth, and asks for the feet as a raft, the company of devotees and the gift of song; one song is a lovesick girl's plea for the kadamba garland, and the praise names Murugan teaching Siva and Brahma's prison."},"p:136":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":136,"title":"தான் தோன்றி","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"A single song at Thanthondri (தான் தோன்றி): the poet asks to understand that this body, a hut propped up by deeds, rots and ends as fine ash, and to be ruled so that, past burning and past deeds, he worships the feet; the praise plays on the shrine's name, the Lord who stands self-arisen."},"p:137":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":137,"title":"கந்தன்குடி","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"A single song at Kandankudi (கந்தன்குடி): the poet asks Murugan to come one day on the peacock like a light filling the worlds and give his feet, so that the bonds of his trembling body and the births of a deceitful heart end; the praise shows the child playing as Siva worships and Murugan coming to save a devotee who calls him guru."},"p:138":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":138,"title":"திலதைப்பதி","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Three songs at Thilathaippathi (திலதைப்பதி): a short plea not to be tossed by impurity and maya, a confession of wandering in waves of pleasure addressed to the Lord who knows it, and a plea to stop singing the praise of men and sing his; the praise includes the rescue of the elephant Gajendra."},"p:139":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":139,"title":"திருவம்பர்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"A single song at Thiruvambar (திருவம்பர்) in the Chola land: the poet, who did not seek the light, mantra and inner space, but gambled, raced horses and danced for pleasure until the senses failed, asks when the trouble of the burning ground will end; the praise sets the peacock leaping across the cosmic circles."},"p:140":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":140,"title":"திருமாகாளம்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"A single song at Thirumakalam (திருமாகாளம்): after a detailed scene of lovemaking, the poet asks that, without 'who am I, who are you', he hold to the feet that took his life and chant Murugan's names daily; the praise names Murugan as the guru who gave joy and understanding to Siva."},"p:141":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":141,"title":"இஞ்சிகுடி","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"A single song at Inchikudi (இஞ்சிகுடி): the courtesans who take a day's worth of coins and goods with false love, and the plea to sing Murugan and reach release instead; the praise is of Rama's march to Lanka with Hanuman, Sugriva and the monkey host."},"p:142":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":142,"title":"திருநள்ளாறு","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"A single song at Thirunallaru (திருநள்ளாறு) that turns the catalogue of a woman's body to the Goddess: the poet asks whether he will melt calling Murugan the son of Parvati, who with two measures of paddy gives charity to every house in the world; the praise names the six faces, the vel against Sura's kin and Vishnu as uncle."},"p:143":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":143,"title":"வழுவூர்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Two songs at Vazhuvur (வழுவூர்): a plea to be kept from women and Death and given true knowledge and the shade of the feet, and a song of thanksgiving recalling, stage by stage, the poet's initiation: teaching, Tamil, light, nada and the peacock's coming."},"p:144":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":144,"title":"கன்னபுரம்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"A single song at Kannapuram (கன்னபுரம்) on the Kaveri, against the sects: the poet asks to be rid of the pettiness of 'this is mine', of learning the books of yoga and other paths, and to be given a life that cannot be called 'this'; the praise shows Murugan waiting outside Valli's cave at night like a thief."},"p:145":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":145,"title":"திருவாஞ்சியம்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"A single song at Thiruvanchiyam (திருவாஞ்சியம்) on kings: lords of elephant armies and conquered directions, perfumed and jewelled, end as a handful of ash when the life slips away, yet they do not think to worship Murugan's feet; the praise names Vishnu of Venkatam and Murugan as Kama's kin."},"p:146":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":146,"title":"திருச்செங்காட்டங்குடி","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"A single song at Thiruchengattangudi (திருச்செங்காட்டங்குடி): against courtesans who are gamblers, killers of families and lovers of money, the poet asks for the teaching in his ear, with the nada sounding, the darkness of maya burnt and the root fire rising; the praise is a chain of 'namo nama' and names the shrine where Siva drank the curry of love."},"p:147":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":147,"title":"திருவிற்குடி","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"A single song at Thiruvirkudi (திருவிற்குடி): women who measure every mind and stand in the golden street like a rutting elephant, and the poet who fears fading in their net like an elephant in a pit; the praise is a battle in drum syllables and Brahma imprisoned."},"p:148":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":148,"title":"விஜயபுரம்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"A single song at Vijayapuram (விஜயபுரம்) on the body: a hut of gut, fat and bone with nine gates of filth, quicker to vanish than a water bubble, which the poet desired and tried to keep with medicines and yogas; he asks to reach the anklet-bearing feet; the praise names Siva burning the triple city and Murugan born of his eye."},"p:149":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":149,"title":"திருவாரூர்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Seven songs at Tiruvarur (திருவாரூர்): three in long syllables with a shared refrain (one on a wasted life, one in a lovesick girl's voice, one on a lullaby's endearments), a short plea for the feet of knowledge, two courtesan songs, one of which tells the story of Manu's justice, and a song that turns the questions of desire into questions about the inner light."},"p:150":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":150,"title":"பெரியமடம்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"A single song at Periyamadam (பெரியமடம்), a monastery: the poet confesses learning the arts of love from courtesans and wasting the body and its days, and asks for initiation to stand on the path; the praise is a litany of 'potri' that names Murugan the lord of the tower at Arunai and 'the father called Arunagirinathar'."},"p:151":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":151,"title":"சோமநாதன்மடம்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"A single song at Somanathan Madam (சோமநாதன்மடம்): the poet asks for the sword of teaching in the silent word, which strikes down Yama's servants, so that body, life and wandering heart melt; the praise honours Somanathan, the ascetic who worships the Lord of Arunai and founded the monastery, and Murugan who never grows angry with his devotees' many wants."},"p:152":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":152,"title":"த்ரியம்பகபுரம்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"A single song at Thiriyambakapuram (த்ரியம்பகபுரம்): courtesans who befriend men to seize their wealth, and the plea to be freed from that base work; the praise is a battle of drum syllables and ghosts drinking blood, and the gods bowing at the feet saying 'Kanda, given by Gauri'."},"p:153":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":153,"title":"சிக்கல்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Two songs at Sikkal (சிக்கல்): the courtesans who say 'put down whatever is in your hand', and a confession of singing false praise to misers to end poverty, with the plea to be looked on by Murugan instead; the praise names Singaravelan of Sikkal and its fragrant groves."},"p:154":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":154,"title":"நாகப்பட்டினம்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Three songs at Nagapattinam (நாகப்பட்டினம்): the poet repents of praising dull men as famous and the base as lords; he will not forget the feet even while he wanders in desire; and he asks for an unfailing boon against a life wasted in materialism and infatuation."},"p:155":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":155,"title":"எட்டிகுடி","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Four songs at Ettikudi (எட்டிகுடி), one of which names Kanjirangudi: the poet confesses abandoning penance for women's breasts, being ruined and made poor by desire, and wandering in shameless lust, and asks for the teaching of Om and for the Lord's mark on him; the praise names Murugan the master of three Tamils and lists his shrines."},"p:156":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":156,"title":"எண்கண்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"A single song at Enkan (எண்கண்): the poet asks Murugan to come on the peacock to the sound of drums and anklets, enter his mind and tell him to praise, know and bow to the red feet; the praise names Rama's war with Lanka, Kumbhakarna shaken, and Indra's place restored."},"p:157":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":157,"title":"திருக்குடவாயில்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Two songs at Thirukkudavayil (திருக்குடவாயில்): a plea to be freed from the price-women and from mala, maya and the gunas, and a song of wonder at Murugan as everything, the guru who made the poet speak of him, praised as the child of Uma who came as the Kauniya brahmin and sent the disputants to the stake."},"p:158":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":158,"title":"வலிவலம்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"One song at Valivalam (வலிவலம்): the poet confesses to common women who leave one man for another with more wealth, and to delighting in house, wife, kin and wealth as if they were permanent, and asks to be set among devotees who worship with flowers."},"p:159":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":159,"title":"வேதாரணியம்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Three songs at Vedaranyam (வேதாரணியம், Vedavanam): pleas against the bond of deeds, disease and lust, and against the women whose eyes conquer the land, with praise that dwells on Rama's story as the deeds of Murugan's uncle."},"p:160":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":160,"title":"கோடி - குழகர் கோயில்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"One song at Kodi, the Kuzhagar temple (கோடி - குழகர் கோயில்): a plea against women, land-greed and wealth-greed, before the body falls into Death's mouth like a frog in a snake's, asking to say 'Murugavel' once among the devotees."},"p:161":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":161,"title":"திருப்பெருந்துறை","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Three songs at Tirupperunthurai (திருப்பெருந்துறை): the body as a house of blood and bone that sickens, bends and becomes a corpse; the embrace of women and the great sea of birth; the memory of a night's lovemaking that the poet asks to have erased. Murugan is praised as guru to his father, beside Siva seated as guru under the kurunda tree."},"p:162":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":162,"title":"திருத்துருத்தி","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"One song at Tirutturutti (திருத்துருத்தி): the poet confesses to courtesans who are never satisfied with any sum and to the disease that follows, and recalls that Murugan called him, gave him the experience of release and told him to recite the Thiruppugazh."},"p:163":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":163,"title":"திருவீழிமிழலை","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"One song at Thiruveezhimizhalai (திருவீழிமிழலை): the endless round of births, from womb to crop to harvest, with mothers by the crore, and the plea to say once 'Muruga, save my life'."},"p:164":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":164,"title":"திருவாவடுதுறை","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"One song at Thiruvavaduthurai (திருவாவடுதுறை): the poet confesses that instead of praising Murugan he sank into courtesans and then into flattering wicked rich men in verse to escape poverty, and asks not to be left to that weariness."},"p:165":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":165,"title":"மருத்துவக்குடி","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"One song at Maruthuvakkudi (மருத்துவக்குடி): a plea against deceitful courtesans and the poet's own deafness to good counsel, asking for the feet that give realization of the meaning of the ancient sayings; Murugan is praised as the jnana guru of Siva."},"p:166":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":166,"title":"திருப்பந்தணை நல்லூர்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Seven songs at Tiruppandanainallur (திருப்பந்தணை நல்லூர்): courtesans of the street who ask first whether a man has money, the poet's mala and deeds, and pleas for the jnana teaching, the feet, the gift of rhythmic Tamil, and for remembering Murugan even in pleasure; the praise names the goddess as Sivai, Sambhavi and Parai, bindu and sound."},"p:167":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":167,"title":"திருப்பனந்தாள்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"One song at Thiruppanandal (திருப்பனந்தாள்): a plea against women whose speech is sugar, asking Murugan to hand over the unfading good thing; the praise shows the vel playing ball with Sura's head."},"p:168":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":168,"title":"திருவிடைமருதூர்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Four songs at Thiruvidaimarudur (திருவிடைமருதூர்): a whole life from a dewdrop of seed to the cremation fire, the miser who mocks the next world and dies despised, ending in a plea for union in which 'you and I' become one enjoyment; pleas for the gift of sweet Tamil, for the will to share and sing, and for the teaching of Siva-jnana against courtesans."},"p:169":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":169,"title":"திரிபுவனம்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"One song at Tribhuvanam (திரிபுவனம்): the poet caught in the net of union that he enjoys even in dreams asks for Murugan's small red lotus feet; the praise names Murugan who taught his father the one thing and is lord of the siddhas of the shrine."},"p:170":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":170,"title":"கும்பகோணம்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Seven songs at Kumbakonam (கும்பகோணம், Kudanthai): a yogic vision of the dancing Lord of eight qualities in the jewelled hall within, pleas against women and their union, for jnana teaching and the sight of the space of knowledge, and two songs on the body, one on its ageing and one on its filth, asking for Sivayoga and jnana."},"p:171":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":171,"title":"சோமீச்சுரம்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"One song at Someechuram (சோமீச்சுரம்): after a full description of lovemaking the poet asks for the jnana to recite Murugan's youth, beauty, arms, feet and faces even in union, in sleep and in every conversation; the praise tells Prahlada's story."},"p:172":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":172,"title":"கொட்டையூர்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"One song at Kottaiyur (கொட்டையூர்): the poet, called wicked, troublesome, mad and fallen for his surrender to women, asks for the golden lotus feet and a place among the purified devotees; the praise tells of Sita's abduction and Ravana's ten heads cut."},"p:173":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":173,"title":"சிவபுரம்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"A song at Sivapuram (சிவபுரம்): the poet asks that his mind, wandering in the joys and sorrows of a body made of the elements, be conquered and joined to grace on the path of supreme knowledge; the praise is a litany of Parvati and the Man-Lion as Murugan's uncle."},"p:174":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":174,"title":"திருநாகேச்சுரம்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"A song at Thirunageswaram (திருநாகேச்சுரம்): a catalogue of the wicked, from parent-revilers and adulterers to drunkards and hoarders, who will wander in the lowest births; the praise names Vayalur, where the Thiruppugazh was given, and Siva of the shrine as the father who praised his son as guru."},"p:175":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":175,"title":"கூந்தலூர்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"A song at Koonthalur (கூந்தலூர்): the poet heaps abuse on himself as a fool bound to women and asks that, even with one flower or leaf, he may fall at Murugan's feet in love; the praise names Rama, the peacock that lifts devotees, and Siva who calls Murugan guru."},"p:176":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":176,"title":"திருச்சத்திமுத்தம்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"A song at Thiruchaththimuththam (திருச்சத்திமுத்தம்): the poet describes women's breasts and wandering eyes and asks to stop suffering in their grip; the praise names Murugan who taught the meaning of the Veda to Siva and confounds the fools who live by lies and plunder of the simple."},"p:177":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":177,"title":"திருவலஞ்சுழி","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"A song at Thiruvalanjuzhi (திருவலஞ்சுழி): a long catalogue of questions about women's eyes, spear, lotus, Kama's arrow, fish, Death, deer, poison, ending in women who plunder like demons; the praise names Rama with Hanuman's army and the yogis and Saiva rites of the shrine."},"p:178":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":178,"title":"திருப்பழையாறை","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"A song at Thiruppazhaiyarai (திருப்பழையாறை): the poet, a slave to women he serves for love, asks Murugan to scold him like a mother and speak one word of teaching so he gains the feet; the praise names the hero of the sea-battle and the son of Siva who is half Uma, at the golden temple of Pazhaiyarai."},"p:179":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":179,"title":"திருச்சக்கிரப்பள்ளி","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"A song at Thiruchakkarappalli (திருச்சக்கிரப்பள்ளி): hyperbole on the breasts of women who sell them, and a plea to be freed from attachment to their false union; the praise, in Sanskrit epithets, identifies Murugan with Sambandar, the king of Pugali, and the teacher of Agastya."},"p:180":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":180,"title":"திருக்குரங்காடுதுறை","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Three songs at Thirukkurangaduthurai (திருக்குரங்காடுதுறை), where monkeys play in the fields: a song only of Murugan's beauty and dance, a catalogue of courtesans who make ascetics falter, and the bent, staff-bound body in which the poet asks the feet even if he is born again."},"p:181":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":181,"title":"காவ்ளுர்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"A song at Kavalur (காவளூர்): the poet describes lovemaking with women versed in Kama's arts and asks to escape the ruin of that delusion and reach Murugan; the praise tells Rama's war for Sita and the slaying of Vali."},"p:182":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":182,"title":"தஞ்சை","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Three songs at Thanjai (தஞ்சை): a short plea not to wander in women's maya, a plea to end the illusion and be given the true substance that is everywhere, and a courtesan scene with a plea for knowledge; the praise names Murugan as Siva's teacher and the great towered city."},"p:183":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":183,"title":"சப்தஸ்தானம்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"A song for the seven shrines (சப்தஸ்தானம்) around Thiruvaiyaru: the poet asks not to approach scheming courtesans and to be given the joy of singing again the Thiruppugazh he sang at Arunai; the praise names the seven shrines one by one."},"p:184":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":184,"title":"திருவையாறு","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"A song at Thiruvaiyaru (திருவையாறு): the poet asks whether the day will come when, leaving the sea of illusion where he praises women part by part, he joins the praise that jnanis sing and reaches the supreme release; the praise names Vishnu's avatars and Siva who is half woman and without caste."},"p:185":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":185,"title":"திருப்பூந்துருத்தி","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"A song at Thiruppoonthuruthi (திருப்பூந்துருத்தி): after a frank picture of lovemaking, the poet asks that the cravings of food, union and maya end and that he be given the conduct of true knowledge; the praise is a run of epithets for Murugan as commander, teacher and healer of devotees."},"p:186":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":186,"title":"திருநெய்த்தானம்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"A song at Thiruneyththanam (திருநெய்த்தானம்): after a picture of lovemaking, the poet vows that even sleeping in women's embrace he will not forget Murugan's feet; the praise is a battle scene in drum syllables and Siva's dance."},"p:187":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":187,"title":"திருப்பழுவூர்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"A song at Thiruppazhuvur (திருப்பழுவூர்): a catalogue of prostitutes with foul mouths, love-drugs in rice and dancing seductions, and the question whether kinship with them can be; the praise tells Krishna's part in the Bharata war."},"p:188":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":188,"title":"பெரும்புலியூர்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"A song at Perumpuliyur (பெரும்புலியூர்): the poet, sunk in women's beds, asks Murugan to pity him and give his two feet; the praise names him son of Chidambaram and of Sivakami."},"p:189":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":189,"title":"நெடுங்களம்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"A song at Nedungalam (நெடுங்களம்): the poet asks that the senses, past deeds and diseases burn like cotton, that he live among the old devotees, and that Murugan come on the peacock and give his feet; the praise describes Murugan's own lovemaking with his consort."},"p:190":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":190,"title":"குறட்டி","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Two songs at Kurattai (குறட்டி): the poet asks not to sink in the mud of women's bodies with a mind bound to maya, and not to wander in a ruined body of diseases, filth and carrion; the praise names Siva the beggar of alms and a long litany of Parvati."},"p:191":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":191,"title":"அத்திப்பட்டு","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"A song at Aththippattu (அத்திப்பட்டு): the poet follows a death through the bier, the pyre and the mourners' return home, where Kama soon has them laughing again, and asks for the way to heaven and the golden feet; the praise names Parvati's penance at Kanchi and Vishnu as uncle."},"p:192":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":192,"title":"அத்திக்கரை","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"A song at Aththikkarai (அத்திக்கரை): the poet asks to leave the vanity of adornment and mean company, remove the three faults, learn faultlessly, serve the freed who know themselves, and enter the shoreless sea of release; the praise names the shakti that pierced the Suras and Valli rescued from the elephant."},"p:193":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":193,"title":"கந்தனூர்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"A song at Kandanur (கந்தனூர்): after births in every form, the poet cries 'give me the state of jnana now' and asks whether Murugan will not come as father and mother run to embrace a leaping child; the praise names the vel against the fire-eyed demons and Siva with the Mother as his half."},"p:194":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":194,"title":"வாலிகொண்டபுரம்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"A song at Valikondapuram (வாலிகொண்டபுரம்): the poet scorns those who carry a carrion body yet swagger in wealth, with chowries, horns and palanquins, and asks that this desire burn and he be given love as a servant of the feet; the praise names Siva's dance and Murugan's own dance of victory."},"p:195":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":195,"title":"திருமாந்துறை","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"A song at Thirumanthurai (திருமாந்துறை): the poet pictures old age, bent body, loose teeth, cough, bedridden years and fading sense, and asks Murugan to come on the peacock before life goes and give his planted feet; the praise names Valli's millet field and the mango-rich fields of the shrine."},"p:196":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":196,"title":"வயலூர்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"First songs at Vayalur (வயலூர், வயலி, 1 of 2), the shrine near Trichy on the Kaveri where the poet places his own call: courtesans who show, sell and swear, the catalogue of a woman's charms, and the body and kin renounced with 'enough, enough'; against them the poet asks for the teaching of pati, pasu and pasam, for jnana beyond sects and rites, and recalls the Lord who called him at Viralimalai and ruled him in a dream."},"p:197":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":197,"title":"வயலூர்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Last songs at Vayalur (வயலூர், 2 of 2): the courtesans' showing and the dangerous beauty of women, answered by a litany of the Goddess's names and by the longest plea of the group, which sets the 'small pleasure' of union against the three impurities removed and the sea of Siva's release."},"p:198":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":198,"title":"திருத்தவத்துறை","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Songs at Thiruthavathurai (திருத்தவத்துறை, Lalgudi) on the Kaveri: two courtesan songs whose pleas ask to leave the women's delusion and to be raised among the devotees, with praise of the vel that taught Siva and of the shrine's goddess who fosters dharma."},"p:199":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":199,"title":"பூவாளூர்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"The one song at Poovalur (பூவாளூர்) in the rain-fed land of the Kaveri's channels: a catalogue of murderous, money-asking courtesans, and praise that makes Murugan the child-saint who defeated the Jains of Madurai and saved the Pandya king and queen."},"p:200":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":200,"title":"திருப்பராய்த்துறை","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"The one song at Thirupparaithurai (திருப்பராய்த்துறை): women who make the poet called vile and a body drowning in disease, and the plea that the Lord come to dwell in his breast; the praise centres on Murugan correcting Brahma's pride and giving the initiation of knowledge."},"p:201":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":201,"title":"தென்கடம்பந்துறை","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"The one song at Then Kadambanthurai (தென்கடம்பந்துறை) on the Kaveri: women's eyes that outdo every standard of comparison and the gold given to them, against a plea to reach the red feet at dawn and dusk; the praise lists the shrines and hearts Murugan dwells in and the river born from Agastya's water-pot."},"p:202":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":202,"title":"கருவூர்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Songs at Karuvur (கருவூர், also Karuvai, Garbhapuram and Vanji): pleas for Siva-jnana and for the experience of the Pati beyond pasu and pasam, a confession of the elemental body and its pride of 'I', love scenes the poet asks to leave, and a song on kings in their pomp who do not know their death, with the Mahabharata in the praise."},"p:203":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":203,"title":"நெருவூர்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"The one song at Neruvur (நெருவூர், Neruvai): the poet, lacking every virtue of the devotees who serve the guru and the Lord's servants, asks not to wander among priced women and vows never to forget the feet; the praise is a sequence of Krishna's deeds."},"p:204":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":204,"title":"திருவெஞ்சமாக்கூடல்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"The one song at Thiruvenjamakkoodal (திருவெஞ்சமாக்கூடல்): a short plea in three similes, the bee, the monkey and the ball, for grace that ends the mind; the Lord is seen by his servants and hidden from the stubborn."},"p:205":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":205,"title":"திருப்பாண்டிக்கொடுமுடி","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Songs at Thiruppandikkodumudi (திருப்பாண்டிக்கொடுமுடி): a short plea to escape the sea of births made by the two deeds, and a love scene in which the poet is drawn to women like a needle to a magnet."},"p:206":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":206,"title":"சேலம்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"The one song at Salem (சேலம்): an akam love poem in which a girl, pierced by the moon's fire, the wind, the cuckoo and the flute, wastes in longing, and the singer asks Murugan to come and embrace her."},"p:207":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":207,"title":"ராஜபுரம்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"The one song at Rajapuram (ராஜபுரம்) in the Kongu land: the poet lists his own honours as a court poet and asks whether one so gifted should praise base sinners as equal to the legendary patrons; the praise is of Vishnu who saved the elephant."},"p:208":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":208,"title":"விஜயமங்கலம்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"A single song at Vijayamangalam (விஜயமங்கலம்) in Kongu: a frank picture of lovemaking, ending not in a plea but in a vow never to forget Murugan's feet even while serving women."},"p:209":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":209,"title":"சிங்கை - காங்கேயம்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Two songs at Singai (சிங்கை, given with Kangeyam, காங்கேயம்): pleas to be freed from trusting women and wandering shamelessly after them, with praise of the vel, of Valli, and of Siva's dance that bowed Kali's head."},"p:210":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":210,"title":"பட்டாலியூர்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Three songs at Pattaliyur (பட்டாலியூர்) in Kongu: the killing eyes of women and the wish to serve before grey hair comes, a rejection of sectarian argument and even of mantra and yantra lore in favour of the highest state, and a long praise of the shrine's fierce goddess."},"p:211":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":211,"title":"திருமுருகன்பூண்டி","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"A single short song at Thirumuruganpoondi (திருமுருகன்பூண்டி): a plea for the feet through ripened penance and prayer, with praise of Murugan as beyond the six faiths."},"p:212":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":212,"title":"அவிநாசி","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Three songs at Avinasi (அவிநாசி) in Kongu: a plea to the guru to end death and birth, courtesans who take coins, and old age with the coming of Death's messengers; Murugan is also praised in the person of Sambandar."},"p:213":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":213,"title":"திருப்புக்கொளியூர்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Three songs at Thiruppukkoliyur (திருப்புக்கொளியூர், the old name of Avinasi): Murugan as the guru of the Siva-yogis, a praise that recalls Siva's acts for Sundarar, and the poet himself besieged by Kama's army at evening."},"p:214":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":214,"title":"§ப்ருர்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Two songs at Perur (பேரூர்) in Kongu: a plea for the one word of jnana that cures the incurable disease, and for desire, town and name to end as they do for yogis who have been taught."},"p:215":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":215,"title":"கொடும்பாளுர்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"A single song at Kodumbalur (கொடும்பாளூர், கொடும்பை in the text): a plea to leave the clamour of sects and arguments and know the knowledge that cannot be pointed to, with praise of Murugan as the merchant riding the peacock."},"p:216":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":216,"title":"கீரனூர்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"A single song at Keeranur (கீரனூர்): the courtesans' arts of luring and pricing, and the churning of the milk sea in praise of Vishnu as Murugan's uncle."},"p:217":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":217,"title":"குளந்தைநகர்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"A single short song at Kulandai (குளந்தைநகர்): fear of dying in the pit of women's delusion, and trust that no one but Murugan can give the boon."},"p:218":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":218,"title":"தனிச்சயம்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Two songs at Thanichayam (தனிச்சயம்) in Kongu: the poet's own lovemaking and a wish that this birth end, with Murugan as the one who commanded him to sing the Thiruppugazh; and old age with its contempt and the hour of dying."},"p:219":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":219,"title":"மதுரை","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Twelve songs at Madurai (மதுரை, also Koodal and the City of the Golden Lotus): courtesans and the wish for the feet, jnana that silences the sects, and praise that draws on Madurai's own stories, Siva's sports for Manikkavachakar and Sambandar's victory over the Jains."},"p:220":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":220,"title":"ஸ்ரீ புருஷமங்கை","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Three songs at Sri Purushamangai (ஸ்ரீ புருஷமங்கை): pleas against women's eyes and the waste of wealth on them, and for the supreme of Vedanta beyond the senses and the mind's maya, with praise of Rama and Krishna as Murugan's uncle."},"p:221":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":221,"title":"இலஞ்சி","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Four songs at Ilanji (இலஞ்சி): pleas that Murugan come on the peacock with Valli at the hour of Death, and that the poet be kept from women's embraces and given the feet; the last is a love-lament for a girl wasting at evening."},"p:222":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":222,"title":"திருக்குற்றாலம்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Three songs at Thirukkutralam (திருக்குற்றாலம்): the courtesans who dance for money and pull the ladder away, the diseased body bound for Yama's town and hell, a plea for the Absolute beyond Veda and knowledge, and the poet who writes on women's breasts as poets write on palm leaf; the shrine is set above a list of famous temples and among devotees and siddhas."},"p:223":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":223,"title":"ஆய்க்குடி","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"One song at Aykudi (ஆய்க்குடி): seeing that the glory of conquering kings ends in a moment at the burning ground, the poet asks, like the devotees who left household life, to be done with the dance of birth; the praise tells of Siva kicking Death for the boy."},"p:224":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":224,"title":"திருப்புத்தூர்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Two songs at Thiruppuththur (திருப்புத்தூர்): the poet regrets the base work of making praise-songs on women's breasts for patrons and longs for chariyai and kiriyai; and the courtesans who demand the coins promised and drive men out; the praise dwells on Siva as Bhairava and as the archer of the triple city."},"p:225":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":225,"title":"திருவாடனை","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"One song at Thiruvadanai (திருவாடனை): the diseased body dies, is wailed over, carried on a bier and burned to ash before it stinks, and the poet asks not to seek such a birth again; the praise names Siva who drank the poison, before whom Murugan taught the Agama of knowledge."},"p:226":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":226,"title":"உத்தரகோசமங்கை","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"One song at Uttarakosamangai (உத்தரகோசமங்கை): bowing first to Ganapati of the many sweets, the poet asks to sing Murugan's praise and so gain Siva's state, the vast space of knowledge and the nectar of refuge; the praise is a battle scene."},"p:227":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":227,"title":"இராமேசுரம்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Two songs at Rameswaram (இராமேசுரம்): a whole life from youth's trade and pleasures among price-women to old age with a stick and a list of diseases, and the endless sweetness of unions that hides the Lord's feet; the praise tells of Brahma beaten for not knowing Om and of Rama's deeds."},"p:228":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":228,"title":"இந்தம்பலம்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"One song at Inthampalam (இந்தம்பலம்): a yogic hymn asking for the Sivayoga of nectar in the sky's sushumna, the Absolute of the letters and of light, and for youth and grace at the feet; the praise is a battle in drum syllables."},"p:229":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":229,"title":"எழுகரைநாடு","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"One song for the lord of Ezhukarai Nadu (எழுகரைநாடு): the poet asks to weep, sing the six-syllable names and become a servant in love night and day, and be given the stainless meaning."},"p:230":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":230,"title":"ஒடுக்கத்துச் செறிவாய்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"One song at Odukkaththu Cherivay (ஒடுக்கத்துச் செறிவாய்): the courtesans' arts, love potions, smiles with stained teeth, the gold assayed in their hands and the quarrel that sends a man away; the praise names Vishnu at length and Murugan who came as a merchant to esteem Tamil."},"p:231":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":231,"title":"காமத்தூர்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"One song at Kamaththur (காமத்தூர்): a short love-lament for a girl scorched by Kama's arrows and the moon on her flower-bed, asking Murugan to come and embrace her."},"p:232":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":232,"title":"முள்வாய்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"One song at Mulvai (முள்வாய்): before the quarrelling kin and Death who hides in the sky can take his life, the poet asks Murugan to enter his brief life with anklets, sacred thread and kadamba."},"p:233":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":233,"title":"வாகைமாநகர்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"One song at Vagaimanagar (வாகைமாநகர்): the poet's infatuation with women's speech, eyes, breasts and gait, set against Murugan's own love for the hunter girl, whose hair he combed and whose feet he held."},"p:234":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":234,"title":"விசுவை","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"One song at Visuvai (விசுவை): a short plea not to fall into women's net with failing understanding, and to be given the feet with love."},"p:235":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":235,"title":"பொதுப்பாடல்கள்","section":"பொதுப்பாடல்கள்","theme":"The first of the general songs (பொதுப்பாடல்கள், 1 of 21), named for no shrine: litanies of 'namo namah' to Murugan and to the goddess, the poet's long self-condemnations, pleas for the supreme truth beyond kingly pomp and beyond the quarrels of sects, Ganapati's offerings, the funeral drum, and yogic and doctrinal hymns."},"p:236":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":236,"title":"பொதுப்பாடல்கள்","section":"பொதுப்பாடல்கள்","theme":"General songs (பொதுப்பாடல்கள், 2 of 21), named for no shrine: most confess the poet's bondage to women and their embraces and ask for the feet, two face the funeral pyre and Death's messengers, one sets the Lord's form beyond what all the sects and scriptures know, and one appears to give the lovesickness of women who watch the god pass in the street; the praise leans heavily on Vishnu's avatars as Murugan's uncle and on the ghouls feasting on the battlefield."},"p:237":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":237,"title":"பொதுப்பாடல்கள்","section":"பொதுப்பாடல்கள்","theme":"General songs (பொதுப்பாடல்கள், 3 of 21), mostly short and grouped in pairs or sets that share a closing refrain: the poet asks to be freed from the lure of women, from disease and from Death, and to sing and serve the feet; one song judges arguers and hoarders bound for Death's town, one is a love-lament, and one asks for the yoga that would bring back the departing breath."},"p:238":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":238,"title":"பொதுப்பாடல்கள்","section":"பொதுப்பாடல்கள்","theme":"General songs (பொதுப்பாடல்கள், 4 of 21): pleas against disease, birth and the decaying body, against flattering patrons and courtesans, and above all for the inner way (silence as temple, breath and the six centres, the self lost so that all becomes one, the abode of supreme wisdom that no fire, wind or weapon touches), set to praise that calls Murugan the Meru of compassion."},"p:239":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":239,"title":"பொதுப்பாடல்கள்","section":"பொதுப்பாடல்கள்","theme":"General songs (பொதுப்பாடல்கள், 5 of 21): the rotting body, the funeral and Death's men, women's eyes and the poet's wasted verse, false promisers, and the miser who gives not even bran, set against pleas for the abode of supreme wisdom, the word of grace already received, and the feet; the praise leans on the Mahabharata and Krishna as Murugan's uncle."},"p:240":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":240,"title":"பொதுப்பாடல்கள்","section":"பொதுப்பாடல்கள்","theme":"General songs (பொதுப்பாடல்கள், 6 of 21), sung in pairs that share a meter and a closing line: the poet fears the hour when Death's noose falls and kin carry the body to the fire, sees the body from womb to decay, renounces house, kin and wealth as passing, and confesses his bondage to women; the praise turns on the freeing of the imprisoned gods, Siva's dance in the burning ground, and Murugan as teacher of Siva and of Tamil to Agastya."},"p:241":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":241,"title":"பொதுப்பாடல்கள்","section":"பொதுப்பாடல்கள்","theme":"General songs (பொதுப்பாடல்கள், 7 of 21): the poet confesses the sea of lust and the women whose bodies he lists part by part, sees the body as a bag of gut, blood and worms, and fears Yama on his buffalo and the cremation fire; he asks for the feet, for song, and for the one Thing where speech and mind end, and rejects begging from patrons and the clamour of rival sects. The last five songs share a closing couplet on Murugan riding the peacock against the demons' elephants, chariots and horses."},"p:242":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":242,"title":"பொதுப்பாடல்கள்","section":"பொதுப்பாடல்கள்","theme":"General songs (பொதுப்பாடல்கள், 8 of 21): the poet confesses bondage to courtesans who sell themselves and take a man's wealth, the frank pleasures of love, the begging of patrons with verse, and the body as a tree or hut that dies and is burned, and asks for teaching (போதகம்), a melting heart and the Lord's feet; the praise turns on Siva's matted hair and his dance, Sambandar's victories over the Jains, and the fire-column of Annamalai."},"p:243":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":243,"title":"பொதுப்பாடல்கள்","section":"பொதுப்பாடல்கள்","theme":"General songs (பொதுப்பாடல்கள், 9 of 21): the poet asks for the yogi's ascent through the body's centres to silence, for the knowledge and mantra Murugan taught Siva, and for his feet at the hour when Death's men come and kin and wealth fall away; he confesses courtesans who want silk and money, his wandering for food, and his trade of praising worthless patrons, and the praise ends in a litany of the goddess's names."},"p:244":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":244,"title":"பொதுப்பாடல்கள்","section":"பொதுப்பாடல்கள்","theme":"General songs (பொதுப்பாடல்கள்), not tied to a shrine: most confess the lure of courtesans and their eyes, a few turn to old age, the funeral and Death's servants, one sets ritual pomp aside for a union where 'you and I' fall away, and one thanks Murugan for the grace that made the poet sing the Thiruppugazh; the praise draws on Siva's and the goddess's epithets, Vishnu's avatars and the war with Sura."},"p:245":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":245,"title":"பொதுப்பாடல்கள்","section":"பொதுப்பாடல்கள்","theme":"General songs (பொதுப்பாடல்கள்), not tied to a shrine: most confess the poet's bondage to courtesans and union with them and ask for Murugan's feet or teaching; others set out the body's frailty, a list of sinners bound for hell, the poet's past as a court poet of women's bodies, and a satire on the boasts of the Kali age turned into the poet's own modesty; the praise draws on the Ramayana, the Bharata war, Prahlada, Siva burning Kama and Murugan teaching Siva."},"p:246":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":246,"title":"பொதுப்பாடல்கள்","section":"பொதுப்பாடல்கள்","theme":"General songs (பொதுப்பாடல்கள், 12 of 21), sung at no named shrine: the poet confesses his bondage to women and courtesans who take a man's wealth, pictures old age, the funeral and Death's servants, traces a life from the womb to the pyre, and asks for the feet, for knowledge beyond the scriptures and sects, and for a place among the devotees; the praise turns on Krishna's boyhood, Siva's deeds, the freeing of the gods and Murugan as teacher of Siva."},"p:247":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":247,"title":"பொதுப்பாடல்கள்","section":"பொதுப்பாடல்கள்","theme":"General songs (பொதுப்பாடல்கள், 13 of 21), sung at no named shrine: most confess the poet's bondage to courtesans who rob, drug and discard a man, and ask Murugan to end the infatuation; others ask for the reality beyond sects and scriptures, for the feet before Death or the scribe's fate comes, and for knowledge that roots out the bonds; the praise turns on Krishna, Rama and Siva, Murugan's own love-sickness for Valli, and Murugan as teacher of Siva."},"p:248":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":248,"title":"பொதுப்பாடல்கள்","section":"பொதுப்பாடல்கள்","theme":"General songs (பொதுப்பாடல்கள், 14 of 21), named for no shrine: most confess the poet's bondage to courtesans and their embraces and ask for the feet, the gift of song or knowledge; others give the funeral and Death's crowd, a clause-by-clause inventory of old age, a mother's lament for her lovesick daughter, and in 1200 Murugan selling himself as Valli's slave. The praise turns on the churning of the ocean, the Mahabharata told almost whole, and Murugan as Siva's teacher."},"p:249":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":249,"title":"பொதுப்பாடல்கள்","section":"பொதுப்பாடல்கள்","theme":"General songs (பொதுப்பாடல்கள், 15 of 21), named for no shrine: most confess bondage to courtesans who seek money and ask for the feet, knowledge or release; others ask to be freed from the round of wombs, picture the corpse carried to the pyre and Yama's summons, and offer a garland of letters; a long litany of the Goddess, Sambandar's impaling of the Jains and Kannappar's offering appear in the praise."},"p:250":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":250,"title":"பொதுப்பாடல்கள்","section":"பொதுப்பாடல்கள்","theme":"General songs (பொதுப்பாடல்கள், 16 of 21): confessions of courtesans who price love, of praising patrons and of wealth hoarded without sharing, the funeral and the rotting body, set against pleas for the feet, for Saiva knowledge of Lord, soul and bond, and in 1221 the poet's disowning of every act as his own."},"p:251":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":251,"title":"பொதுப்பாடல்கள்","section":"பொதுப்பாடல்கள்","theme":"General songs (பொதுப்பாடல்கள், 17 of 21): the household, wealth and kin left at the burning ground or seized at Death's coming, courtesans who take money, and pleas for the name and feet at the last hour, beside songs of inner yoga, of Siva-knowledge as a bride, of experience over scripture and sect, and of the threefold Tamil."},"p:252":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":252,"title":"பொதுப்பாடல்கள்","section":"பொதுப்பாடல்கள்","theme":"General songs (பொதுப்பாடல்கள், 18 of 21): the poet asks to be lifted above the quarrels of sects and the lure of women, out of the body seen as self and the bubble of life, and to be given the true word, the teaching Murugan gave Siva, and the feet; one song is a love-lament, one a vow that in kingship or poverty he will speak only of the feet, and one a frank account of love answered by a plea for Vedanta's lamp."},"p:253":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":253,"title":"பொதுப்பாடல்கள்","section":"பொதுப்பாடல்கள்","theme":"General songs (பொதுப்பாடல்கள், 19 of 21): the poet traces the body from the womb to the burning ground, sets kin and Death's rope against the feet, confesses the lure of women and calls himself every name of fault, and asks for steady wisdom, the yogic ascent to the thousand-petalled lotus, the company of devotees and release; one song is a love-lament and one a vow not to forget the feet even in lovemaking."},"p:254":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":254,"title":"பொதுப்பாடல்கள்","section":"பொதுப்பாடல்கள்","theme":"General songs (பொதுப்பாடல்கள், 20 of 21): after two long songs on lovemaking and on women's eyes, the chapter turns to short songs that ask not to be born again, for the experience of the feet, for the word beyond the quarrelling scriptures, and against singing for deceitful patrons; Murugan appears as Sambandar who impaled the Jains, and three akam songs give the lovesick girl's plight under Kama, the sea, the cuckoo and the moon."},"p:255":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":255,"title":"பொதுப்பாடல்கள்","section":"பொதுப்பாடல்கள்","theme":"General songs (பொதுப்பாடல்கள், 21 of 21): short songs that ask for the knowledge that cuts 'I' and 'mine', for the experience (அநுபூதி) and dance of knowledge, for the vision of the feet beyond the river of births, the net of family and the women who want gold, and for knowledge no book can hold; three akam songs give the lovesick girl under Kama, the moon and the town's gossip, and the book ends on a fragmentary song of Murugan's hill and battle."},"p:256":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":256,"title":"க்ஷேத்திரக் கோவை","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"The catalogue of shrines (க்ஷேத்திரக் கோவை): a single song that strings together the names of Siva's and Murugan's shrines across the Tamil country, from Kumbakonam, Arur and Chidambaram to Senthil, Eragam, Avinankudi and Vayalur, and ends with Murugan dwelling in every temple in the world."},"p:257":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":257,"title":"பழமுதிர்சோலை","section":"அறுபடை வீடு","theme":"Pazhamudircholai (பழமுதிர்சோலை, 1 of 2): Murugan of the hill of Solaimalai is hymned as the letter A and the form above the three gods, and as the guru who teaches Siva; the poet confesses courtesans who sell their love and polish brass for gold, the life from womb to old age taken as eternal, the old body coughing over its medicines and the sick body fattened on food, and asks for the feet, for servanthood, for the yogic ascent and for the knowledge that he is Siva."},"p:258":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":258,"title":"பழமுதிர்சோலை","section":"அறுபடை வீடு","theme":"Pazhamudircholai (பழமுதிர்சோலை, 2 of 2): one song in which the poet, describing lovemaking, vows that even sunk in that old infatuation he will not forget Murugan's garlanded crown and the vel in his hand; the praise names Uma's son, the vel of knowledge thrown at Sura's hill and sea, and the lord of Kulagiri."},"p:259":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":259,"title":"புதிய பாடல்கள்","section":"பொதுப்பாடல்கள்","theme":"New songs (புதிய பாடல்கள்): two songs without a shrine. In one the poet confesses a life spent learning arts, singing poems and running after wealth and women; in the other, the courtesans' binding arts; the praise names Murugan the guru who spoke supreme knowledge into Siva's ears and the vel that cut down Singamukha."},"p:260":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":260,"title":"புனவாயில்","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Punavayil (புனவாயில்): one song of the death-bed, speech gone, eyes dim, milk spilling from the mouth, the physician giving up and wives and kin falling and wailing, with the plea that on the day Death comes Murugan appear with the peacock, his twelve arms and his two consorts; the praise tells of Hanuman bringing Sita's tokens to Rama, whose nephew dwells at Punavayil."},"p:261":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":261,"title":"திருவெழுகூற்றிருக்கை","section":"பிற தலங்கள்","theme":"Thiruvezhukutrirukkai (திருவெழுகூற்றிருக்கை): the closing number-poem, in which the numbers one to seven rise and fall through the lines while the praise tells Murugan's story: the one form of the Taraka Brahman, the release of Brahma, the circling of the world, the marriage to Devayanai, the teaching of Siva, the birth at Kazhumalam as master of three Tamils, the vel at Krauncha, and the lord of Eragam on the Kaveri's north bank."}},"concepts":{"aram":{"tamil":"அறம்","gloss":"virtue; moral order"},"oozh":{"tamil":"ஊழ்","gloss":"fate; what is apportioned"},"vinai":{"tamil":"வினை","gloss":"deed and its consequence"},"muyarchi":{"tamil":"முயற்சி","gloss":"effort"},"kalvi":{"tamil":"கல்வி","gloss":"learning"},"arivu":{"tamil":"அறிவு","gloss":"understanding, innate or acquired"},"selvam":{"tamil":"செல்வம்","gloss":"wealth, material or figurative"},"perumai":{"tamil":"பெருமை","gloss":"greatness of character"},"sirumai":{"tamil":"சிறுமை","gloss":"smallness of character"},"panivu":{"tamil":"பணிவு","gloss":"humility"},"pirappu":{"tamil":"பிறப்பு","gloss":"birth, as social origin"},"piravi":{"tamil":"பிறவி","gloss":"rebirth; the cycle of births"},"kudi":{"tamil":"குடி","gloss":"lineage; high birth"},"manam":{"tamil":"மனம்","gloss":"inner state of mind"},"kurippu":{"tamil":"குறிப்பு","gloss":"unspoken sign or intention"},"kaamam":{"tamil":"காமம்","gloss":"love and desire"},"samanilai":{"tamil":"சமநிலை","gloss":"equanimity"},"sutram":{"tamil":"சுற்றம்","gloss":"human kinship and fellowship"},"iraivan":{"tamil":"இறைவன்","gloss":"God; the Lord, in the Kural usually named only by epithet"},"idumbai":{"tamil":"இடும்பை","gloss":"suffering; affliction"},"pugazh":{"tamil":"புகழ்","gloss":"praise; renown"},"adakkam":{"tamil":"அடக்கம்","gloss":"self-restraint"},"ozhukkam":{"tamil":"ஒழுக்கம்","gloss":"conduct; a disciplined way of living"},"mazhai":{"tamil":"மழை","gloss":"rain, as the condition of life and of virtue"},"pasi":{"tamil":"பசி","gloss":"hunger"},"eegai":{"tamil":"ஈகை","gloss":"giving and sharing with others"},"thavam":{"tamil":"தவம்","gloss":"austerity; penance"},"thuravu":{"tamil":"துறவு","gloss":"renunciation"},"vekuli":{"tamil":"வெகுளி","gloss":"anger"},"arul":{"tamil":"அருள்","gloss":"compassion toward all living beings"},"avaa":{"tamil":"அவா","gloss":"craving; desire that binds"},"azhukkaaru":{"tamil":"அழுக்காறு","gloss":"envy"},"inbam":{"tamil":"இன்பம்","gloss":"joy; pleasure"},"pazhi":{"tamil":"பழி","gloss":"blame; what deserves blame"},"ilvaazhkkai":{"tamil":"இல்வாழ்க்கை","gloss":"household life"},"virundhu":{"tamil":"விருந்து","gloss":"the guest; hospitality"},"anbu":{"tamil":"அன்பு","gloss":"love; affection"},"porai":{"tamil":"பொறை","gloss":"forbearance; endurance"},"vaazhkkaithunai":{"tamil":"வாழ்க்கைத் துணை","gloss":"life's companion; the wife"},"karpu":{"tamil":"கற்பு","gloss":"a wife's steadfastness; chastity"},"makkal":{"tamil":"மக்கள்","gloss":"children, and the good of having them"},"inchol":{"tamil":"இன்சொல்","gloss":"pleasant speech"},"vaaimai":{"tamil":"வாய்மை","gloss":"truthfulness"},"nanri":{"tamil":"நன்றி","gloss":"a good done; gratitude for it"},"natpu":{"tamil":"நட்பு","gloss":"friendship"},"naduvu":{"tamil":"நடுவு","gloss":"impartiality; keeping to what is fair"},"piranil":{"tamil":"பிறனில்","gloss":"another man's wife, named through his home"},"vehkaamai":{"tamil":"வெஃகாமை","gloss":"not coveting what belongs to others"},"purangooral":{"tamil":"புறங்கூறல்","gloss":"backbiting; speaking ill of someone in their absence"},"sol":{"tamil":"சொல்","gloss":"speech; the spoken word and its effects"},"payan":{"tamil":"பயன்","gloss":"use; fruit; what something yields"},"nayan":{"tamil":"நயன்","gloss":"goodness of conduct or manner; variously justice, grace, liking"},"acham":{"tamil":"அச்சம்","gloss":"fear, especially fear of wrongdoing as a moral faculty"},"oppuravu":{"tamil":"ஒப்புரவு","gloss":"the obligation to serve the community with one's means"},"pulaal":{"tamil":"புலால்","gloss":"flesh as food; abstention from it"},"kollaamai":{"tamil":"கொல்லாமை","gloss":"not killing"},"koodaozhukkam":{"tamil":"கூடாவொழுக்கம்","gloss":"conduct at odds with one's professed state; hypocrisy of the renunciant"},"kalavu":{"tamil":"களவு","gloss":"stealth; taking another's goods by deceit"},"alavu":{"tamil":"அளவு","gloss":"right measure; proportion"},"innaa":{"tamil":"இன்னா","gloss":"what hurts; harm done to others"},"nilaiyaamai":{"tamil":"நிலையாமை","gloss":"impermanence"},"pattru":{"tamil":"பற்று","gloss":"attachment; clinging"},"meyyunarvu":{"tamil":"மெய்யுணர்வு","gloss":"perception of what is truly so"},"arasu":{"tamil":"அரசு","gloss":"kingship; the ruler and the office of ruling"},"maanam":{"tamil":"மானம்","gloss":"honour; self-respect one won't compromise"},"sengol":{"tamil":"செங்கோல்","gloss":"just rule; the straight sceptre"},"panpu":{"tamil":"பண்பு","gloss":"good character; nature that holds"},"kallaamai":{"tamil":"கல்லாமை","gloss":"lack of learning, as a condition of a person"},"kelvi":{"tamil":"கேள்வி","gloss":"learning by listening to the wise"},"thunai":{"tamil":"துணை","gloss":"support; those or that on which one can lean"},"kutram":{"tamil":"குற்றம்","gloss":"fault; flaw of character"},"ivaral":{"tamil":"இவறல்","gloss":"avarice; clinging to wealth when it should be used"},"vali":{"tamil":"வலி","gloss":"strength, one's own and others', to be weighed"},"therithal":{"tamil":"தெரிதல்","gloss":"examining and discerning before choosing or acting"},"inam":{"tamil":"இனம்","gloss":"the company one keeps"},"kaalam":{"tamil":"காலம்","gloss":"right timing; the season for acting"},"idam":{"tamil":"இடம்","gloss":"right place; the ground on which one acts"},"thelivu":{"tamil":"தெளிவு","gloss":"settled trust reached after examination"},"naanam":{"tamil":"நாண்","gloss":"modesty; shame that restrains"},"pochchaappu":{"tamil":"பொச்சாப்பு","gloss":"heedlessness; forgetting oneself and one's purpose"},"kolai":{"tamil":"கொலை","gloss":"killing, as crime or as punishment"},"kodungol":{"tamil":"கொடுங்கோல்","gloss":"oppressive rule; the bent sceptre"},"ali":{"tamil":"அளி","gloss":"tender regard for those in one's care"},"veruvu":{"tamil":"வெருவு","gloss":"dread; the fear a ruler instils"},"kol":{"tamil":"கோல்","gloss":"the sceptre; the quality of a ruler's rule, just or cruel"},"oruththal":{"tamil":"ஒறுத்தல்","gloss":"punishing; penalty as an act of rule"},"kadunjol":{"tamil":"கடுஞ்சொல்","gloss":"harsh speech"},"kannottam":{"tamil":"கண்ணோட்டம்","gloss":"the grace of the eye; kindly regard, especially for those one knows"},"aran":{"tamil":"அரண்","gloss":"fortress; protection, literal or figurative"},"otru":{"tamil":"ஒற்று","gloss":"spying; the spy; intelligence for the state"},"ookkam":{"tamil":"ஊக்கம்","gloss":"energy of mind; inner drive"},"velanmai":{"tamil":"வேளாண்மை","gloss":"helping others; liberality"},"madi":{"tamil":"மடி","gloss":"sloth; inertia"},"adimai":{"tamil":"அடிமை","gloss":"bondage; servitude"},"aalvinai":{"tamil":"ஆள்வினை","gloss":"sustained purposeful labour; manly exertion"},"idukkan":{"tamil":"இடுக்கண்","gloss":"trouble; hardship; suffering"},"amaichu":{"tamil":"அமைச்சு","gloss":"ministership; the minister"},"solvanmai":{"tamil":"சொல்வன்மை","gloss":"power of speech; eloquence"},"pagai":{"tamil":"பகை","gloss":"enmity; the enemy"},"avai":{"tamil":"அவை","gloss":"the assembly; those before whom one speaks"},"thooimai":{"tamil":"தூய்மை","gloss":"purity, especially of action and means"},"saandror":{"tamil":"சான்றோர்","gloss":"the worthy; those complete in character"},"thitpam":{"tamil":"திட்பம்","gloss":"firmness of mind and execution"},"soozhchi":{"tamil":"சூழ்ச்சி","gloss":"deliberation; strategic planning"},"thoothu":{"tamil":"தூது","gloss":"embassy; the envoy"},"mannar":{"tamil":"மன்னர்","gloss":"the ruler, seen from those who serve him"},"anjaamai":{"tamil":"அஞ்சாமை","gloss":"fearlessness"},"naadu":{"tamil":"நாடு","gloss":"land; realm as a living whole"},"vendhan":{"tamil":"வேந்தன்","gloss":"king; ruler"},"padai":{"tamil":"படை","gloss":"army; fighting force"},"maram":{"tamil":"மறம்","gloss":"martial valour; fierce courage"},"saakkaadu":{"tamil":"சாக்காடு","gloss":"death, as something faced or chosen"},"pedhaimai":{"tamil":"பேதைமை","gloss":"folly"},"idithal":{"tamil":"இடித்தல்","gloss":"rebuke that corrects"},"kedu":{"tamil":"கேடு","gloss":"ruin; loss of fortune"},"aaraaydhal":{"tamil":"ஆராய்தல்","gloss":"testing; careful inquiry into a person"},"pazhaimai":{"tamil":"பழைமை","gloss":"old friendship; a bond with long-standing claims"},"kizhamai":{"tamil":"கிழமை","gloss":"the liberty of intimacy; rightful claim on a friend"},"vanjam":{"tamil":"வஞ்சம்","gloss":"deceit; hidden hostility"},"pullarivu":{"tamil":"புல்லறிவு","gloss":"petty understanding that thinks itself wise"},"igal":{"tamil":"இகல்","gloss":"hostility; the disposition to quarrel"},"utpagai":{"tamil":"உட்பகை","gloss":"enmity from within one's own circle"},"penvazhi":{"tamil":"பெண்வழிச்சேறல்","gloss":"being led by a woman's will; a husband's subordination"},"aanmai":{"tamil":"ஆண்மை","gloss":"manliness; capacity to act and govern"},"varaivinmagalir":{"tamil":"வரைவின்மகளிர்","gloss":"women who draw no line; women who sell intimacy"},"kal":{"tamil":"கள்","gloss":"liquor; intoxication"},"soodhu":{"tamil":"சூது","gloss":"gambling"},"noy":{"tamil":"நோய்","gloss":"disease; affliction"},"marundhu":{"tamil":"மருந்து","gloss":"medicine; remedy"},"undi":{"tamil":"உண்டி","gloss":"food; eating"},"saandraanmai":{"tamil":"சான்றாண்மை","gloss":"fullness of character; moral completeness"},"uzhavu":{"tamil":"உழவு","gloss":"farming; ploughing as the base of society"},"iravu":{"tamil":"இரவு","gloss":"asking; begging from others"},"inmai":{"tamil":"இன்மை","gloss":"poverty; lack"},"karavu":{"tamil":"கரவு","gloss":"concealing what one has from one who asks; refusal"},"kayamai":{"tamil":"கயமை","gloss":"baseness; lack of any inner moral restraint"},"anangu":{"tamil":"அணங்கு","gloss":"a goddess or spirit that afflicts; beauty as a wounding power"},"kootru":{"tamil":"கூற்று","gloss":"Death personified"},"kan":{"tamil":"கண்","gloss":"the eyes as agents of love: they wound, steal, speak and suffer"},"nalam":{"tamil":"நலம்","gloss":"bodily beauty and wellbeing, as something love gives or takes"},"kaamanoy":{"tamil":"காமநோய்","gloss":"love as sickness; the pain of longing"},"kalaviyal":{"tamil":"களவியல்","gloss":"secret love before marriage"},"punarchi":{"tamil":"புணர்ச்சி","gloss":"the union of lovers, physical and emotional"},"uyir":{"tamil":"உயிர்","gloss":"life; the life-breath at stake in love"},"oodal":{"tamil":"ஊடல்","gloss":"lovers' quarrel; the sulking that love enjoys"},"nenju":{"tamil":"நெஞ்சு","gloss":"the heart as a separate party the lover speaks to"},"pirivu":{"tamil":"பிரிவு","gloss":"separation of lovers"},"ninaivu":{"tamil":"நினைவு","gloss":"remembering the absent lover; constant thought"},"alar":{"tamil":"அலர்","gloss":"public gossip about the lovers"},"madal":{"tamil":"மடல்","gloss":"riding the palmyra-stem horse in public to shame or force a beloved's kin"},"maalai":{"tamil":"மாலை","gloss":"evening as the hour when longing flowers"},"nirai":{"tamil":"நிறை","gloss":"self-containment; holding feeling within"},"melivu":{"tamil":"மெலிவு","gloss":"wasting of the body in longing, shown by slipping bangles and thinning arms"},"pasalai":{"tamil":"பசலை","gloss":"the pallor of longing, spreading over a woman's skin in separation"},"orutalai":{"tamil":"ஒருதலை","gloss":"one-sided love; love that isn't returned"},"kanavu":{"tamil":"கனவு","gloss":"dream, as a place where the absent lover can be met"},"parathai":{"tamil":"பரத்தை","gloss":"the courtesan or other woman; a man's straying, as a commentators' setting"},"ilamai":{"tamil":"இளமை","gloss":"youth, as a stage that passes"},"yaakkai":{"tamil":"யாக்கை","gloss":"the body, as a perishable and impure frame"},"veedu":{"tamil":"வீடு","gloss":"release; liberation from the cycle of births"},"azhagu":{"tamil":"அழகு","gloss":"beauty, outward or true"},"marumai":{"tamil":"மறுமை","gloss":"the next world or life, for which good deeds are provision"},"udanpokku":{"tamil":"உடன்போக்கு","gloss":"a girl's going away with her lover, against her family, through the wilderness"},"guru":{"tamil":"குரு","gloss":"the spiritual teacher, whose path and feet lead to release"},"naragam":{"tamil":"நரகம்","gloss":"hell, as the after-death punishment of the wicked"},"vazhipaadu":{"tamil":"வழிபாடு","gloss":"worship; ritual service with offerings"},"sivam":{"tamil":"சிவம்","gloss":"Siva, as the Lord and as the state the freed soul becomes"},"gnaanam":{"tamil":"ஞானம்","gloss":"liberating knowledge, and the ஞானி who has it"},"malam":{"tamil":"மலம்","gloss":"impurity; the bonds (traditionally three) that bind the soul"},"aaguthi":{"tamil":"ஆகுதி","gloss":"the oblation poured into the sacred fire; the fire rite (ஓமம்)"},"andhanar":{"tamil":"அந்தணர்","gloss":"the Brahmin, defined by rite, Vedanta and conduct; in the Kural, the virtuous one"},"maayai":{"tamil":"மாயை","gloss":"maya: the material cause of bodies and worlds; also illusion"},"naadham":{"tamil":"நாதம்","gloss":"nada: primal sound, paired with bindu as the first stage of manifestation"},"piranavam":{"tamil":"பிரணவம்","gloss":"the syllable Om"},"samaadhi":{"tamil":"சமாதி","gloss":"samadhi: the absorbed state of the yogi or jnani; also the tomb in which such a one's body is kept"},"siththi":{"tamil":"சித்தி","gloss":"attainment; the powers that come with yoga, and the siddha who has them"},"vedaantham":{"tamil":"வேதாந்தம்","gloss":"Vedanta: the end of the Veda, taken as the knowledge in which craving ends"},"thiruvarul":{"tamil":"திருவருள்","gloss":"the Lord's grace, as distinct from compassion between beings"},"yogam":{"tamil":"யோகம்","gloss":"yoga: the discipline of union with Siva, and the yogi who practises it"},"vindhu":{"tamil":"விந்து","gloss":"bindu: in cosmology the source of sound and form (mahamaya); in the body the seminal essence"},"kundalini":{"tamil":"குண்டலினி","gloss":"kundalini: the coiled power, cosmic and in the body"},"thaththuvam":{"tamil":"தத்துவம்","gloss":"tattva: one of the (traditionally 36) constituents of the soul's world"},"pasu":{"tamil":"பசு","gloss":"pasu: the bound soul, one of the three Saiva Siddhanta categories with pati (the Lord) and pasam (the bonds)"},"paasam":{"tamil":"பாசம்","gloss":"pasam: the bonds that hold the soul, one of the three Saiva Siddhanta categories with pati and pasu"},"pathi":{"tamil":"பதி","gloss":"Pati: the Lord, as the first of the three Saiva Siddhanta categories (Lord, soul, bond)"},"saththi":{"tamil":"சத்தி","gloss":"Shakti: the Lord's power, his consort, through whom he creates and acts"},"aindhozhil":{"tamil":"ஐந்தொழில்","gloss":"the Lord's five acts: creating, protecting, removing, concealing and granting grace"},"vedham":{"tamil":"வேதம்","gloss":"the Veda, as scripture recited and as the source of dharma"},"aagamam":{"tamil":"ஆகமம்","gloss":"the Agamas: the Saiva scriptures given by Siva, traditionally twenty-eight"},"thamizh":{"tamil":"தமிழ்","gloss":"Tamil, as a language of revelation beside Sanskrit, and the poet's medium"},"mandhiram":{"tamil":"மந்திரம்","gloss":"mantra: the sacred formula, received from a guru, and the verses of the Tirumanthiram itself"},"aanandham":{"tamil":"ஆனந்தம்","gloss":"bliss, especially the bliss of Siva (சிவானந்தம்) known in union with him, as distinct from worldly pleasure"},"samayam":{"tamil":"சமயம்","gloss":"a religious sect or system, often in Tirumular a source of delusion"},"naadi":{"tamil":"நாடி","gloss":"nadi: a channel of breath and life in the yogic body"},"aasanam":{"tamil":"ஆசனம்","gloss":"asana: a seated posture of yoga, the first bodily step of the eightfold discipline"},"praanan":{"tamil":"பிராணன்","gloss":"prana: the breath as life-force, and its control (pranayama) in yoga"},"kalai":{"tamil":"கலை","gloss":"kala: a phase or digit, of the moon and of the breath matched with it; in Siddhanta also a class of the tattvas"},"karu":{"tamil":"கரு","gloss":"the embryo, and its forming in the womb"},"thiyaanam":{"tamil":"தியானம்","gloss":"meditation: the seventh limb of eight-limbed yoga, contemplation held on light, sound or the Lord"},"theerththam":{"tamil":"தீர்த்தம்","gloss":"holy water; bathing at sacred rivers and tanks to remove sin, and the inner waters set against it"},"nindhai":{"tamil":"நிந்தை","gloss":"reviling or slighting what is holy: the Lord, the guru, his devotees or the jnani"},"adiyaar":{"tamil":"அடியார்","gloss":"the Lord's devotees, his servants, as a community to be revered"},"amudham":{"tamil":"அமுதம்","gloss":"nectar: in Tirumular's yoga the fluid held to flow from the head or palate, which preserves the body"},"thirukkoothu":{"tamil":"திருக்கூத்து","gloss":"Siva's holy dance in the hall (அம்பலம்) at Chidambaram, as the five acts, as letters and mantra, and as a dance within the body"},"panchaakkaram":{"tamil":"பஞ்சாக்கரம்","gloss":"the five-letter mantra of Siva (சிவாயநம, நமசிவாய and its other orders), the அஞ்செழுத்து"},"muththirai":{"tamil":"முத்திரை","gloss":"mudra: a seal or gesture of the hands (or of the body) used with mantra in worship and in yoga"},"chakkaram":{"tamil":"சக்கரம்","gloss":"chakra as a drawn diagram (yantra): lines and cells filled with letters and mantras for worship or rites"},"thiruvadi":{"tamil":"திருவடி","gloss":"the Lord's holy feet, as refuge, as what the guru places on the disciple's head, and as the attainment itself"},"saivam":{"tamil":"சைவம்","gloss":"Saivism: the religion of Siva, and in Tirumular its kinds (pure, impure, path, severe pure) and the Saiva who follows it"},"siththaantham":{"tamil":"சித்தாந்தம்","gloss":"Siddhanta: the settled conclusion, Saiva Siddhanta as the teaching and the Siddhantin who has reached it"},"sariyai":{"tamil":"சரியை","gloss":"chariyai: the first of the four Saiva paths, outward service of the Lord in temples (cleaning, gathering flowers, singing), the path of the devotee"},"kiriyai":{"tamil":"கிரியை","gloss":"kiriyai: the second of the four Saiva paths, ritual worship of Siva in a form, by Agamic rite"},"sanmaarkkam":{"tamil":"சன்மார்க்கம்","gloss":"sanmarga: the true or good path; in Saiva usage the path of jnana, the highest of the four paths, in which the soul becomes one with Siva"},"padhamukthi":{"tamil":"பதமுத்தி","gloss":"the graded states of release short of union: salokam (dwelling in the Lord's world), samipam (nearness to him) and sarupam (taking his form)"},"saayujjiyam":{"tamil":"சாயுச்சியம்","gloss":"sayujya: union with Siva, the highest of the four states of release, in which the soul becomes Siva"},"mounam":{"tamil":"மோனம்","gloss":"silence: the stillness of speech and mind of the sage, as a state rather than a vow"},"vesham":{"tamil":"வேடம்","gloss":"the ascetic's garb or guise: the outward marks of a holy life, true or assumed"},"thiruneeru":{"tamil":"திருநீறு","gloss":"holy ash, worn as Siva's emblem and armour"},"seedan":{"tamil":"சீடன்","gloss":"the disciple, worthy (சற்சீடன்) or unworthy (அசற்சீடன்), whose ripeness decides whether the teaching is given"},"ilingam":{"tamil":"இலிங்கம்","gloss":"the lingam: Siva's emblem, as the installed stone with its pedestal and as the forms it is said to be (letters, bindu and nada, Shakti and Siva, the self, the guru)"},"aadhaaram":{"tamil":"ஆதாரம்","gloss":"adhara: one of the six supports or centres in the body named in Tirumular's yoga (the chakras), pictured as lotuses rising from the root to the brow"},"aadhiththan":{"tamil":"ஆதித்தன்","gloss":"the sun: in Tirumular both the sun of the cosmos, worshipper and form of the Lord, and the sun within the yogic body"},"avaththai":{"tamil":"அவத்தை","gloss":"avastha: a state of the soul's consciousness, chiefly waking (நனவு, சாக்கிரம்), dream (கனவு), deep sleep (சுழுத்தி), turiya (துரியம்) and the beyond (அதீதம்), each in Tirumular divided into the others and matched to tattvas, malas and places in the body"},"mukkunam":{"tamil":"முக்குணம்","gloss":"the three gunas, sattva, rajas and tamas, the qualities of prakriti; in Tirumular matched with the states of waking, dream and deep sleep"},"andam":{"tamil":"அண்டம்","gloss":"the cosmic egg; a world-sphere, of which there are numberless ones within the outer egg"},"aththuvaa":{"tamil":"அத்துவா","gloss":"adhva: the six paths (tattva, mantra, varna, bhuvana, pada, kala) of Saiva Agamic teaching, through which the soul is bound and through which it is purified"},"aarandham":{"tamil":"ஆறந்தம்","gloss":"the six ends: Vedanta, Siddhanta, nadanta, bodhanta, yoganta and kalanta, the end points of six disciplines of knowledge, which Tirumular gathers into one"},"thonthaththasi":{"tamil":"தொந்தத்தசி","gloss":"tat tvam asi, 'that thou art': the Upanishadic great saying, its words தற்பதம் (that), தொம்பதம் (thou) and அசி (art) read by Tirumular as the Lord, the bound soul and their union"},"paazh":{"tamil":"பாழ்","gloss":"the void: the emptiness left when maya, knowledge and their traces are let go, in Tirumular three voids (முப்பாழ்) in which speech ends and the soul comes to rest"},"upaadhi":{"tamil":"உபாதி","gloss":"upadhi: a limiting adjunct, of effect or of cause, that clings to the soul (and is said to cling even to the Supreme) and must be cut away for release"},"upasaantham":{"tamil":"உபசாந்தம்","gloss":"upasanta: the stilling or quiescence in which the traces of action and the states fall away and the soul rests in Siva"},"thisai":{"tamil":"திசை","gloss":"the eight directions, with their guardian gods and elephants, set in Tirumular's yoga as the petals of the inner lotus"},"ilakkanai":{"tamil":"இலக்கணை","gloss":"lakshana: implication, the secondary sense by which a word points beyond its literal meaning; in Vedanta, the three modes (leaving, keeping, and both) used to read the great sayings"},"paththi":{"tamil":"பத்தி","gloss":"bhakti: devotion to the Lord, and the பத்தர் (devotees) who have it"},"madam":{"tamil":"மடம்","gloss":"matha: the guru's seat or monastery, where teaching and worship go on, and in Tirumular its inner counterpart in the body"},"oli":{"tamil":"ஒளி","gloss":"light: the Lord as light (சோதி), the inner light seen within the body and mind in meditation, and the lights of sun, moon and fire he gives"},"aakaasam":{"tamil":"ஆகாசம்","gloss":"space, the fifth element (also வெளி, விசும்பு), and in Tirumular the inner space of awareness and the space in Siva that the knower attains"},"viyaabagam":{"tamil":"வியாபகம்","gloss":"pervasion: the Lord's filling of all things, the elements, the worlds, the gods, the souls and the body, while remaining one and beyond them"},"mayal":{"tamil":"மயல்","gloss":"infatuation; the deluded fascination of desire, the poet's word for his own state before the Lord's grace"},"thondu":{"tamil":"தொண்டு","gloss":"devotional service to the Lord and his devotees; becoming his servant (அடிமை, வழியடிமை) by choice, as distinct from bondage"},"agandhai":{"tamil":"அகந்தை","gloss":"pride; the ego's self-importance"},"thalam":{"tamil":"தலம்","gloss":"a sacred site or shrine where the Lord dwells and is worshipped; the place itself as an object of devotion"}},"verses":{"k:1":{"book":"kural","num":1,"t":["அகர முதல எழுத்தெல்லாம் ஆதி","பகவன் முதற்றே உலகு"],"p":"As the letter A is the first of all letters, so the eternal God is first in the world.","r":"As all letters have 'a' as their first, the world has the Primal Lord as its first.","n":"The verse gives a simile of being first and says nothing of creating. Parimelazhagar reads it as establishing that the unseen deity exists, arguing from the visible world, which is more than the couplet states.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:1"},"k:2":{"book":"kural","num":2,"t":["கற்றதனால் ஆய பயனென்கொல் வாலறிவன்","நற்றாள் தொழாஅர் எனின்"],"p":"What Profit have those derived from learning, who worship not the good feet of Him who is possessed of pure knowledge ?","r":"What has learning achieved if the learned do not bow at the good feet of the one of pure knowledge?","n":"The question assumes that learning has a purpose beyond itself. It is the chapter's first conditional, and it is put to the learned, not to the ignorant.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","iraivan","panivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The one of pure knowledge is God, and scriptural knowledge bears fruit by worship that cuts off rebirth.","ஆகம அறிவிற்குப் பயன் அவன் தாளைத் தொழுது பிறவியறுத்தல்"],["kalaignar","The one meant is any person greater in knowledge, and the failing is a lack of humility before such people.","தன்னைவிட அறிவில் மூத்த பெருந்தகையாளரின் முன்னே வணங்கிநிற்கும் பண்பு"]],"ch":"k:1"},"k:3":{"book":"kural","num":3,"t":["மலர்மிசை ஏகினான் மாணடி சேர்ந்தார்","நிலமிசை நீடுவாழ் வார்"],"p":"They who are united to the glorious feet of Him who passes swiftly over the flower of the mind, shall flourish long above all worlds.","r":"Those who reach the noble feet of the one who walked upon the flower will live long upon the earth.","n":"The Tamil says நிலமிசை, on the earth, so reading it as a world above is an interpretation. Parimelazhagar also rejects an identification of 'the one who walked on the flower' with another deity, which shows the epithet was already disputed.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","piravi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The long life is deathless life in the world of release, above all worlds.","எல்லா உலகிற்கும் மேலாய் வீட்டு உலகின்கண் அழிவின்றி வாழ்வார்"],["manakkudavar","The general word 'land' covers this world and the next, with enjoyment named before release.","இவ்வுலகின்கண்ணும் மேலுலகின்கண்ணு மென்று கொள்ளப்படும்"],["kalaignar","The long life is the lasting renown of those who follow such a person.","புகழ்வாழ்வு, உலகில் நெடுங்காலம் நிலைத்து நிற்கும்."]],"ch":"k:1"},"k:4":{"book":"kural","num":4,"t":["வேண்டுதல் வேண்டாமை இலானடி சேர்ந்தார்க்கு","யாண்டும் இடும்பை இல"],"p":"To those who meditate the feet of Him who is void of desire or aversion, evil shall never come.","r":"Those who reach the feet of the one without liking or aversion have no suffering, anywhere or at any time.","n":"The one sought is defined by what he lacks. Parimelazhagar's explanation makes the benefit mimetic: those who reach him also lose liking and aversion, and so lose the suffering these cause.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","idumbai","samanilai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The suffering removed is that of rebirth, in its three classical kinds.","எக்காலத்தும் பிறவித் துன்பங்கள் உளவாகா."],["kalaignar","The one meant is a selfless human example, and following such a person keeps ordinary suffering away.","விருப்பு வெறுப்பற்றுத் தன்னலமின்றித் திகழ்கின்றவரைப் பின்பற்றி நடப்பவர்களுக்கு"]],"ch":"k:1"},"k:5":{"book":"kural","num":5,"t":["இருள்சேர் இருவினையும் சேரா இறைவன்","பொருள்சேர் புகழ்புரிந்தார் மாட்டு"],"p":"The two-fold deeds that spring from darkness shall not adhere to those who delight in the true praise of God.","r":"The two kinds of deed born of darkness do not cling to those devoted to the Lord's true praise.","n":"The verse says both kinds, so good deeds are also among what must not cling. That is a stronger claim than 'worship removes sin'.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","iraivan","pugazh"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Even good deeds cause birth, and the praise is true only of the one who has all divine qualities.","நல்வினையும் பிறத்தற்கு ஏதுவாகலான்"],["kalaignar","Those who understand what 'the Lord' means and seek renown meet good and bad with equal measure.","நன்மை தீமைகளை ஒரே அளவில் எதிர் கொள்வார்கள்."]],"ch":"k:1"},"k:6":{"book":"kural","num":6,"t":["பொறிவாயில் ஐந்தவித்தான் பொய்தீர் ஒழுக்க","நெறிநின்றார் நீடுவாழ் வார்"],"p":"Those shall long proposer who abide in the faultless way of Him who has destroyed the five desires of the senses.","r":"Those who stand firm on the truthful path of conduct of the one who quenched the five senses' desires will live long.","n":"This is the only verse in the chapter that asks for a way of living instead of reaching or praising. Parimelazhagar says the path was taught by the one who quenched the five, as a poem belongs to its poet, which makes him a teacher and not only an object of worship.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","adakkam","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:1"},"k:7":{"book":"kural","num":7,"t":["தனக்குவமை இல்லாதான் தாள்சேர்ந்தார்க் கல்லால்","மனக்கவலை மாற்றல் அரிது"],"p":"Anxiety of mind cannot be removed, except from those who are united to the feet of Him who is incomparable.","r":"Except for those who reach the feet of the incomparable one, it is hard to remove the mind's anxiety.","n":"The verse says அரிது, hard. Parimelazhagar explicitly takes this as impossibility, which hardens the claim beyond its plain wording.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:1"},"k:8":{"book":"kural","num":8,"t":["அறவாழி அந்தணன் தாள்சேர்ந்தார்க் கல்லால்","பிறவாழி நீந்தல் அரிது"],"p":"None can swim the sea of vice, but those who are united to the feet of that gracious Being who is a sea of virtue.","r":"Except for those who reach the feet of the gracious one who is a sea of virtue, the other seas are hard to swim.","n":"The sea of virtue set against 'other seas' is the image's whole point. Pope's gloss, 'sea of vice', is not in the Tamil.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","iraivan"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The other seas are wealth and pleasure, set apart from virtue in the three aims of life.","ஏனைப் பொருளும் இன்பமும் 'பிற' எனப்பட்டன."],["kalaignar","The அந்தணன் is a worthy human being, whose example carries others across seas of trouble.","அந்தணர் என்பதற்குப் பொருள் சான்றோர் என்பதால்,"]],"ch":"k:1"},"k:9":{"book":"kural","num":9,"t":["கோளில் பொறியின் குணமிலவே எண்குணத்தான்","தாளை வணங்காத் தலை"],"p":"The head that worships not the feet of Him who is possessed of eight attributes, is as useless as a sense without the power of sensation.","r":"A head that does not bow at the feet of the one of eight qualities is as useless as a sense organ that cannot sense.","n":"The verse never lists the eight. Parimelazhagar gives a list from Saiva scripture and records rival lists, so any particular list is the commentator's. Manakkudavar goes further and says such bodies lack life.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","panivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The eight qualities are those taught in the Saiva agamas, though others list them differently.","இவ்வாறு சைவாகமத்துக் கூறப்பட்டது."]],"ch":"k:1"},"k:10":{"book":"kural","num":10,"t":["பிறவிப் பெருங்கடல் நீந்துவர் நீந்தார்","இறைவன் அடிசேரா தார்"],"p":"None can swim the great sea of births but those who are united to the feet of God.","r":"Those who reach the Lord's feet swim the great sea of births, and those who do not reach them cannot.","n":"The chapter closes with both outcomes in one couplet. Verses 7 and 8 hedge with 'hard', and this one drops the hedge. Parimelazhagar's 'reach' comes from the verse's own closing negative, since the positive verb is left implied.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","iraivan"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The sea is the shoreless chain of cause and effect in rebirth.","காரண காரியத் தொடர்ச்சியாய் கரை இன்றி வருதலின்"],["kalaignar","The sea is life in this world, which is hard to cross without following a leading person.","வாழ்க்கை எனும் பெருங்கடலை நீந்திக் கடக்க"]],"ch":"k:1"},"k:11":{"book":"kural","num":11,"t":["வான்நின்று உலகம் வழங்கி வருதலால்","தான்அமிழ்தம் என்றுணரற் பாற்று"],"p":"By the continuance of rain the world is preserved in existence; it is therefore worthy to be called ambrosia.","r":"Because the world keeps going through the rain's constancy, rain deserves to be understood as amrita.","n":"The word உணரற்பாற்று makes this a matter of recognition, since rain is ordinary and easily taken for granted. Manakkudavar draws this out by saying it should not be thought of as a mere element.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["mazhai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:2"},"k:12":{"book":"kural","num":12,"t":["துப்பார்க்குத் துப்பாய துப்பாக்கித் துப்பார்க்குத்","துப்பாய தூஉம் மழை"],"p":"Rain produces good food, and is itself food.","r":"Rain produces the food that eaters eat, and rain is itself food to those who drink it.","n":"Five uses of துப்பு pack the couplet, and the wordplay enacts the idea: the same substance is the maker, the made and the eater's need.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mazhai","pasi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:2"},"k:13":{"book":"kural","num":13,"t":["விண்இன்று பொய்ப்பின் விரிநீர் வியனுலகத்து","உள்நின்று உடற்றும் பசி"],"p":"If the cloud, withholding rain, deceive (our hopes) hunger will long distress the sea-girt spacious world.","r":"If the sky fails, hunger stays and torments this wide world, though it is surrounded by spreading water.","n":"The irony is in விரிநீர்: the world is ringed by water and still starves. Parimelazhagar makes this explicit. பொய்ப்பின் also treats the sky as something that can break its word.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasi","mazhai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:2"},"k:14":{"book":"kural","num":14,"t":["ஏரின் உழாஅர் உழவர் புயல்என்னும்","வாரி வளங்குன்றிக் கால்"],"p":"If the abundance of wealth imparting rain diminish, the labour of the plough must cease.","r":"If the bounty of the rains declines, ploughmen will not plough.","n":"Parimelazhagar takes the verse as explaining why food runs out. The human labour is real but placed downstream of a yield the farmer doesn't control.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mazhai","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:2"},"k:15":{"book":"kural","num":15,"t":["கெடுப்பதூஉம் கெட்டார்க்குச் சார்வாய்மற் றாங்கே","எடுப்பதூஉம் எல்லாம் மழை"],"p":"Rain by its absence ruins men; and by its existence restores them to fortune.","r":"Rain is what ruins, and then it is rain again that supports and lifts up the ruined.","n":"The verse says only that rain ruins. The commentators here take the ruin as failure to fall, though a reader might expect flood too. The power to destroy and restore belongs to one impersonal agent, with no moral reason given.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mazhai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:2"},"k:16":{"book":"kural","num":16,"t":["விசும்பின் துளிவீழின் அல்லால்மற் றாங்கே","பசும்புல் தலைகாண்பு அரிது"],"p":"If no drop falls from the clouds, not even the green blade of grass will be seen.","r":"Unless drops fall from the sky, it is hard to see even the head of a green blade of grass.","n":"Parimelazhagar notes the implied 'even': if the grass is gone, not even a life with one sense remains. The argument descends to the smallest thing that lives.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mazhai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:2"},"k:17":{"book":"kural","num":17,"t":["நெடுங்கடலும் தன்நீர்மை குன்றும் தடிந்தெழிலி","தான்நல்கா தாகி விடின்"],"p":"Even the wealth of the wide sea will be diminished, if the cloud that has drawn (its waters) up gives them not back again (in rain).","r":"Even the vast sea would lose its nature if the cloud held back after taking from it.","n":"The source of rain needs rain, so the dependence runs in a circle. Parimelazhagar makes this the point, and Kalaignar turns the same cycle into a social lesson.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mazhai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","தடிந்து means the cloud scoops up the sea's water, and the sea's 'nature' is its living creatures and gems.","ஈண்டுக் 'குறைத்தல்' என்றது முகத்தலை."],["manakkudavar","He takes the word as the cloud flashing, and reports that others read it as dividing.","மின்னி மழையானது பெய்யாவிடின்"],["kalaignar","The verse also teaches that those who rise from a society must serve it for it to live.","மனித சமுதாயத்திலிருந்து புகழுடன் உயர்ந்தவர்களும் அந்தச் சமுதாயத்திற்கே பயன்பட்டால்தான் அந்தச் சமுதாயம் வாழும்."]],"ch":"k:2"},"k:18":{"book":"kural","num":18,"t":["சிறப்பொடு பூசனை செல்லாது வானம்","வறக்குமேல் வானோர்க்கும் ஈண்டு"],"p":"If the heaven dry up, neither yearly festivals, nor daily worship will be offered in this world, to the celestials.","r":"If the sky dries up, festival and worship will cease here, even for the gods.","n":"The word 'even' reverses the usual order: the celestials depend on what the sky provides through human offering. Kalaignar's comment adds scepticism with 'those said to live in the sky', which the verse does not express.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mazhai","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:2"},"k:19":{"book":"kural","num":19,"t":["தானம் தவம்இரண்டும் தங்கா வியன்உலகம்","வானம் வழங்கா தெனின்"],"p":"If rain fall not, penance and alms-deeds will not dwell within this spacious world.","r":"If the sky does not give, neither giving nor austerity will last in this wide world.","n":"Parimelazhagar maps giving broadly to the householder and austerity to the renunciant, so this verse quietly announces the two paths that chapters 3 and 5 go on to compare. Even austerity, which might seem independent of plenty, is included.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["eegai","thavam","mazhai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:2"},"k:20":{"book":"kural","num":20,"t":["நீர்இன்று அமையாது உலகெனின் யார்யார்க்கும்","வான்இன்று அமையாது ஒழுக்கு"],"p":"If it be said that the duties of life cannot be discharged by any person without water, so without rain there cannot be the flowing of water.","r":"If no one's world can do without water, then its flow can't exist without the sky.","n":"The whole dispute rests on ஒழுக்கு, which can mean a flow or conduct. On the second reading, the chapter ends by saying morality depends on rain.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["mazhai","ozhukkam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","ஒழுக்கு is the unceasing flow of water, and he records the conduct reading as someone else's.","அந்நீர் இடையறாது ஒழுகும் ஒழுக்கும் வானை இன்றி அமையாது."],["kalaignar","ஒழுக்கு is conduct, which may decay if there is no rain.","உலகில் மழையே இல்லையென்றால் ஒழுக்கமே கெடக்கூடும்"]],"ch":"k:2"},"k:21":{"book":"kural","num":21,"t":["ஒழுக்கத்து நீத்தார் பெருமை விழுப்பத்து","வேண்டும் பனுவல் துணிவு"],"p":"The end and aim of all treatise is to extol beyond all other excellence, the greatness of those who, while abiding in the rule of conduct peculiar to their state, have abandoned all desire.","r":"The settled view of the texts is to place the greatness of those who renounce within their discipline above all other excellence.","n":"The verse says only ஒழுக்கத்து, in discipline. Parimelazhagar's ladder from duties of varna to release is his own construction. The commentators also give very different reasons for the agreement among texts.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","perumai","ozhukkam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","This is a shared conclusion of all religious texts, though they conflict on everything else.","ஒன்றையொன்று ஒவ்வாத சமய நூல்கள் எல்லாவற்றிற்கும் இஃது ஒத்த துணிவு"],["manakkudavar","Even a false text praises renunciants, so that people will honour it.","யாதானுமொரு பொய்யைச் சொல்லும் நூலும்"]],"ch":"k:3"},"k:22":{"book":"kural","num":22,"t":["துறந்தார் பெருமை துணைக்கூறின் வையத்து","இறந்தாரை எண்ணிக்கொண் டற்று"],"p":"To describe the measure of the greatness of those who have forsaken the two-fold desires, is like counting the dead.","r":"Trying to state the measure of renouncers' greatness is like counting all the dead who have ever lived.","n":"It's a strange image of praise. The dead aren't only numerous but past counting in principle. My reading, offered tentatively, is that comparing renouncers to the dead also suits people who have left the world.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:3"},"k:23":{"book":"kural","num":23,"t":["இருமை வகைதெரிந்து ஈண்டுஅறம் பூண்டார்","பெருமை பிறங்கிற்று உலகு"],"p":"The greatness of those who have discovered the properties of both states of being, and renounced the world, shines forth on earth (beyond all others).","r":"The greatness of those who discern the two ways and take on virtue here stands highest in the world.","n":"ஈண்டு, here, is important: this choice is made in this life. Parimelazhagar uses an implied 'only' to place them above even emperors.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["thuravu","perumai","aram"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The two are birth and release, weighed for their suffering and joy, and the virtue taken up is renunciation.","பிறப்பு வீடு என்னும் இரண்டனது துன்ப இன்பக் கூறுபாடுகளை ஆராய்ந்து அறிந்து"],["kalaignar","The two are good and evil, and anyone who chooses the good is great.","நன்மை எது, தீமை எது என்பதை ஆய்ந்தறிந்து"]],"ch":"k:3"},"k:24":{"book":"kural","num":24,"t":["உரனென்னும் தோட்டியான் ஓரைந்தும் காப்பான்","வரனென்னும் வைப்பிற்கோர் வித்து"],"p":"He who guides his five senses by the hook of wisdom will be a seed in the world of heaven.","r":"One who guards the five with the goad of firmness is a seed for the finest ground.","n":"The goad implies the elephants, which the verse doesn't mention, and then the image shifts to farming. வரன் leaves the 'field' open: Parimelazhagar makes it release, while Kalaignar makes it renunciation itself. The word உரன் suggests strength, though Parimelazhagar says it refers here to understanding.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","thuravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:3"},"k:25":{"book":"kural","num":25,"t":["ஐந்தவித்தான் ஆற்றல் அகல்விசும்பு ளார்கோமான்","இந்திரனே சாலுங் கரி"],"p":"Indra, the king of the inhabitants of the spacious heaven, is himself, a sufficient proof of the strength of him who has subdued his five senses.","r":"Of the strength of one who has quenched the five, Indra, lord of the wide heavens, is witness enough.","n":"The verse does not say how Indra testifies. The commentators supply two opposed stories, one in which Indra failed and another in which he fears.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","thuravu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Indra, cursed for failing to quench the five, shows by his own failure what the power is worth.","தான் ஐந்து அவியாது சாபம் எய்தி நின்று, அவித்தவனது ஆற்றல் உணர்த்தினான்"],["manakkudavar","Indra trembles for his throne when anyone does great penance, so the ascetic is stronger than the gods.","அவன் தன் பதம் இழக்கின்றானாக நடுங்குமாதலான்"]],"ch":"k:3"},"k:26":{"book":"kural","num":26,"t":["செயற்கரிய செய்வார் பெரியர் சிறியர்","செயற்கரிய செய்கலா தார்"],"p":"The great will do those things which is difficult to be done; but the mean cannot do them.","r":"Those who do what is hard to do are great, and those who can't do it are small.","n":"The verse doesn't say what is hard, so it reads as general. In its context the commentators narrow it to yoga. Manakkudavar turns it into a warning within the chapter: renouncing alone does not make one great.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","sirumai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","What is hard is the eight limbs of yoga; what is easy is following the senses.","செயற்கு அரிய ஆவன, இமயம், நியமம் முதலாய எண்வகை யோக உறுப்புக்கள்."],["manakkudavar","Those who have renounced are still small if they can't do these hard things.","துறந்த மாத்திரத்தானே பெரியரென்று கொள் ளப்படார்"]],"ch":"k:3"},"k:27":{"book":"kural","num":27,"t":["சுவைஒளி ஊறுஓசை நாற்றமென ஐந்தின்","வகைதெரிவான் கட்டே உலகு"],"p":"The world is within the knowledge of him who knows the properties of taste, sight, touch, hearing and smell.","r":"The world lies within the one who discerns the kinds of the five: taste, light, touch, sound and smell.","n":"கட்டே is the crux. Pope's 'owns his sway' and Parimelazhagar's 'within his knowledge' suggest different kinds of mastery. Parimelazhagar's whole twenty-five principles are not in the couplet, which names only the five.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["arivu","adakkam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","This is knowledge of the twenty-five principles of Samkhya, beyond which there is no world.","சாங்கிய நூலுள் ஓதியவாற்றான் ஆராய்தல்."],["kalaignar","This is the ability to understand and master the senses, which the world honours.","ஐம்புலன்களின் இயல்பை உணர்ந்து அவற்றை அடக்கியாளும் திறன் கொண்டவனையே உலகம் போற்றும்."]],"ch":"k:3"},"k:28":{"book":"kural","num":28,"t":["நிறைமொழி மாந்தர் பெருமை நிலத்து","மறைமொழி காட்டி விடும்"],"p":"The hidden words of the men whose words are full of effect, will shew their greatness to the world.","r":"The greatness of those whose word is full is shown on earth by their hidden words.","n":"நிறைமொழி and மறைமொழி echo each other: a complete word and a concealed one. The verse doesn't say whether the proof is what the words do or what they teach.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","thuravu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Renunciants' words take effect, and their commanding mantras prove it visibly.","அவர் ஆணையாகச் சொல்லிய மந்திரங்களே கண்கூடாகக் காட்டும்."],["manakkudavar","'Full' describes people of complete learning, whose mantras circulate as proof.","நிரம்பிய கல்வியையுடைய மாந்தரது"],["kalaignar","The proof is the enduring ethical books of the worthy.","அவர்களது அறவழி நூல்களே எடுத்துக் காட்டும்."]],"ch":"k:3"},"k:29":{"book":"kural","num":29,"t":["குணமென்னும் குன்றேறி நின்றார் வெகுளி","கணமேயும் காத்தல் அரிது"],"p":"The anger of those who have ascended the mountain of goodness, though it continue but for a moment, cannot be resisted.","r":"The anger of those who have climbed the hill of good qualities, even for a moment, is hard to withstand.","n":"The crux is whose capacity காத்தல் refers to, and the commentators turn the verse in opposite directions. Pope sides with the threatening reading.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","perumai","thuravu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Their anger lasts only a moment, yet its target cannot ward it off.","தான் உள்ள அளவு கணமே ஆயினும், வெகுளப்பட்டாரால் தடுத்தல் அரிது."],["manakkudavar","The harm from their anger can't be avoided, as in Nahusha's becoming a serpent.","நகுஷன் பெரும்பாம்பாயினான்."],["kalaignar","Such people can't hold on to anger, even for a moment.","அந்தக் கோபம் அவர்கள் உள்ளத்தில் ஒரு கணம்கூட நிலைத்து நிற்காது."]],"ch":"k:3"},"k:30":{"book":"kural","num":30,"t":["அந்தணர் என்போர் அறவோர்மற் றெவ்வுயிர் க்கும்","செந்தண்மை பூண்டொழுக லான்"],"p":"The virtuous are truly called Anthanar; because in their conduct towards all creatures they are clothed in kindness.","r":"Those called அந்தணர் are the virtuous, because they live clothed in cool kindness toward every living being.","n":"The verse defines a status word by conduct, and its logic is a reason (ஒழுகலான்), so the name follows from the behaviour. Placed right after 29, its coolness is the counterweight to the sage's terrifying anger.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","aram","thuravu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The name's reason restricts it to those with such grace, namely those in renunciation.","அந்தணரென்று சொல்லப்படுவார் துறவறத்தில் நின்றவர்."],["manakkudavar","Having said some renunciants are small, the verse says some non-renunciants are great.","துற வாதாரினும் பெரியாருளரென்று கூறினார்."],["kalaignar","Any worthy person with love for all beings is an அந்தணர்.","சான்றோர் எவராயினும் அவர் அந்தணர் எனப்படுவார்."]],"ch":"k:3"},"k:31":{"book":"kural","num":31,"t":["சிறப்பு ஈனும் செல்வமும் ஈனும் அறத்தினூஉங்கு","ஆக்கம் எவனோ உயிர்க்கு"],"p":"Virtue will confer heaven and wealth; what greater source of happiness can man possess ?","r":"It yields distinction and it yields wealth: what gain is greater for a living being than virtue?","n":"The appeal is to self-interest, as virtue is praised for what it brings. Parimelazhagar takes சிறப்பு as release and செல்வம் as heaven, while Manakkudavar takes the second more plainly as wealth and sees the verse as answering those who say prosperity comes from wealth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:4"},"k:32":{"book":"kural","num":32,"t":["அறத்தினூஉங்கு ஆக்கமும் இல்லை அதனை","மறத்தலின் ஊங்கில்லை கேடு"],"p":"There can be no greater source of good than (the practice of) virtue; there can be no greater source of evil than the forgetfulness of it.","r":"No gain surpasses virtue, and no ruin surpasses forgetting it.","n":"The opposite of virtue here is மறத்தல், forgetting, not doing wrong. Ruin comes through inattention, so the danger is ordinary.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:4"},"k:33":{"book":"kural","num":33,"t":["ஒல்லும் வகையான் அறவினை ஓவாதே","செல்லும்வாய் எல்லாஞ் செயல்"],"p":"As much as possible, in every way, incessantly practise virtue.","r":"As far as you are able, without stopping, do the work of virtue wherever it can go.","n":"ஒல்லும் வகையான் sets the measure by capacity, so the demand is relentless in duration but not impossible in scale. Parimelazhagar gives 'wherever' as mind, speech and body, while Kalaignar turns it into a requirement that all one's acts be virtuous.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","vinai","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:4"},"k:34":{"book":"kural","num":34,"t":["மனத்துக்கண் மாசிலன் ஆதல் அனைத்து அறன்","ஆகுல நீர பிற"],"p":"Let him who does virtuous deeds be of spotless mind; to that extent is virtue; all else is vain show.","r":"To be spotless in mind is the whole of virtue, and everything else is noisy show.","n":"ஆகுல means clamour, so the charge against outward virtue is that it wants an audience. Parimelazhagar reads the verse with 33, so it concerns the mind of someone already doing good, not the mind in place of deeds. Manakkudavar's comment says the four faults were named earlier, which implies his text placed 35 before this verse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:4"},"k:35":{"book":"kural","num":35,"t":["அழுக்காறு அவாவெகுளி இன்னாச்சொல் நான்கும்","இழுக்கா இயன்றது அறம்"],"p":"That conduct is virtue which is free from these four things, viz, malice, desire, anger and bitter speech.","r":"Virtue is what proceeds without slipping into these four: envy, craving, anger and harsh speech.","n":"Virtue is defined by exclusion. The verse only lists the faults, and Parimelazhagar supplies a causal chain in which craving leads to anger and anger to harsh speech. Three of the four are inward and one is spoken.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","azhukkaaru","avaa","vekuli"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:4"},"k:36":{"book":"kural","num":36,"t":["அன்றறிவாம் என்னாது அறஞ்செய்க மற்றது","பொன்றுங்கால் பொன்றாத் துணை"],"p":"Defer not virtue to another day; receive her now; and at the dying hour she will be your undying friend.","r":"Do virtue without saying 'we'll see to it later': when you perish, it is a companion that doesn't perish.","n":"The pun பொன்றுங்கால் பொன்றா turns on one verb: you perish and it doesn't. The verse doesn't say how virtue survives, and that is where the commentators part.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","piravi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","When the body dies, virtue stays joined to the soul and travels into the next body.","செய்த உடம்பு அழியவும் உயிரோடு ஒன்றி ஏனை உடம்பினுட் சேறலின்"],["kalaignar","Virtue survives as lasting renown after death.","அது ஒருவர் இறந்தபின் கூட அழியாப் புகழாய் நிலைத்துத் துணை நிற்கும்."]],"ch":"k:4"},"k:37":{"book":"kural","num":37,"t":["அறத்தாறு இதுவென வேண்டா சிவிகை","பொறுத்தானோடு ஊர்ந்தான் இடை"],"p":"The fruit of virtue need not be described in books; it may be inferred from seeing the bearer of a palanquin and the rider therein.","r":"There is no need to say 'this is the way of virtue': look between the one who bears the palanquin and the one who rides in it.","n":"The couplet is very compressed, and it never says what difference we are to see. Taken at face value, it treats social rank as visible evidence of moral merit, which is one of the harder claims in the book.","x":1,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aram","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Rider and bearer are direct perceptual evidence of virtue's fruit, so scripture needn't be cited.","சிவிகையைக் காவுவானோடு செலுத்துவானிடைக் காட்சியளவை தன்னானே உணரப்படும்."],["kalaignar","This is an analogy of attitude: the virtuous ride through joy and sorrow lightly, and others carry life as a burden.","வாழ்க்கையில் வரும் இன்ப துன்பங்கள் இரண்டையும் எளியவாகக் கருதி மகிழ்வுடன் பயணத்தை மேற்கொள்வார்கள்."]],"ch":"k:4"},"k:38":{"book":"kural","num":38,"t":["வீழ்நாள் படாஅமை நன்றாற்றின் அஃதொருவன்","வாழ்நாள் வழியடைக்கும் கல்"],"p":"If one allows no day to pass without some good being done, his conduct will be a stone to block up the passage to other births.","r":"If you do good so that no day falls away unused, that is a stone blocking the road of your days.","n":"வீழ்நாள் and வாழ்நாள் are set against each other, so fallen days are contrasted with living days. The stone's job depends entirely on what வாழ்நாள் means here.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","piravi","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The stone blocks the road by which embodied days would return, which means it ends rebirth.","அச்செயல் அவன் யாக்கையோடு கூடும் நாள் வரும் வழியை வாராமல் அடைக்குங் கல்லாம்."],["kalaignar","The stone is a paving stone that smooths the road of this life.","வாழ்க்கைப் பாதையைச் சீராக்கி அமைத்துத் தரும் கல்லாக"]],"ch":"k:4"},"k:39":{"book":"kural","num":39,"t":["அறத்தான் வருவதே இன்பம் மற் றெல்லாம்","புறத்த புகழும் இல"],"p":"Only that pleasure which flows from domestic virtue is pleasure; all else is not pleasure, and it is without praise.","r":"Only what comes through virtue is joy; everything else lies outside it and has no renown either.","n":"Pope's gloss restricts this to 'domestic virtue', which follows Parimelazhagar and is not in the Tamil. The verse has a strong implication: joy is not separate from virtue and can't be got apart from it.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","aram","pugazh"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The joy is sensual enjoyment that accords with household life; illicit pleasure turns to pain.","இல்லறத்தோடு பொருந்தி வருவதே இன்பம் ஆவது"],["manakkudavar","All enjoyment of any kind comes through virtue.","இஃது எல்லாப் போக நுகர்ச்சியும் இதனானே வருமென்றது."],["kalaignar","The joy arises from the renown a virtuous life brings.","அறவழி வாழ்க்கையில் வருகின்ற புகழால் ஏற்படுவதே இன்பமாகும்."]],"ch":"k:4"},"k:40":{"book":"kural","num":40,"t":["செயற்பால தோரும் அறனே ஒருவற்கு","உயற்பால தோரும் பழி"],"p":"That is virtue which each ought to do, and that is vice which each should shun.","r":"What a person should do is virtue, and what a person should escape is blame.","n":"Virtue's counterpart here is பழி, blame, not sin. Parimelazhagar explains it as what is blamed, and takes it to mean bad deeds. The term gives virtue a social measure as well as a cosmic one.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","pazhi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:4"},"k:41":{"book":"kural","num":41,"t":["இல்வாழ்வான் என்பான் இயல்புடைய மூவர்க்கும்","நல்லாற்றின் நின்ற துணை"],"p":"He will be called a (true) householder, who is a firm support to the virtuous of the three orders in their good path.","r":"The one called a householder is the steady support, on their good path, of the three who keep their proper way.","n":"The verse doesn't name the three, and what is meant by இயல்புடைய decides who is included.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","eegai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The three are the other life-stages, and he says Valluvar takes the first two from other people's doctrine.","இவருள் முன்னை இருவரையும் பிறர் மதம் மேற்கொண்டு கூறினார்."],["kalaignar","The three are one's parents, spouse and children.","பெற்றோர், வாழ்க்கைத் துணை, குழந்தைகள் என இயற்கையாக அமைந்திடும் மூவர்க்கும்"]],"ch":"k:5"},"k:42":{"book":"kural","num":42,"t":["துறந்தார்க்கும் துவ்வாதவர்க்கும் இறந்தார்க்கும்","இல்வாழ்வான் என்பான் துணை"],"p":"He will be said to flourish in domestic virtue who aids the forsaken, the poor, and the dead.","r":"The householder is the support of those who have let go, those who have nothing and those who have died.","n":"A single support reaches even the dead. Chapter 3 uses துறந்தார் for renouncers, so Parimelazhagar's different reading here gives the same word a very different sense.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","eegai","thuravu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","துறந்தார் are people abandoned by those who should support them, and இறந்தார் are those who die alone at the door.","களைகண் ஆனவரால் துறக்கப்பட்டார்க்கும்"],["manakkudavar","துறந்தார் are renouncers who have given up caste and name.","வருணத்தினையும் நாமத்தினையுந் துறந்தார்க்கும்"],["kalaignar","They are ascetics, the hungry and the unprotected, so there is no duty to the dead here.","பற்றற்ற துறவிகட்கும், பசியால் வாடுவோர்க்கும், பாதுகாப்பற்றவர்க்கும்"]],"ch":"k:5"},"k:43":{"book":"kural","num":43,"t":["தென்புலத்தார் தெய்வம் விருந்தொக்கல் தானென்றாங்கு","ஐம்புலத்தாறு ஓம்பல் தலை"],"p":"The chief (duty of the householder) is to preserve the five-fold rule (of conduct) towards the manes, the Gods, his guests, his relations and himself.","r":"To tend properly the five, namely ancestors, gods, guests, kin and oneself, is the chief duty.","n":"The self is last on a list of duties. Parimelazhagar's reason is that one must exist in order to do any virtue, so self-care is a moral requirement. Kalaignar replaces ancestors and gods with memory of the dead and honour for those who live well.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","virundhu","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:5"},"k:44":{"book":"kural","num":44,"t":["பழியஞ்சிப் பாத்தூண் உடைத்தாயின் வாழ்க்கை","வழியெஞ்சல் எஞ்ஞான்றும் இல்"],"p":"His descendants shall never fail who, living in the domestic state, fears vice (in the acquisition of property) and shares his food (with others).","r":"If a life fears blame and shares its food, its line never fails at any time.","n":"Fear of blame comes before sharing because generosity can't launder what was wrongly got. Parimelazhagar gives a striking account of how merit and fault split in that case.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","pazhi","eegai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","If someone shares wealth that was wrongly taken, the merit goes to the owner and the sin stays with the sharer.","அறம் பொருளுடையார் மேலும், பாவம் தன் மேலுமாய் நின்று வழி எஞ்சும்"]],"ch":"k:5"},"k:45":{"book":"kural","num":45,"t":["அன்பும் அறனும் உடைத்தாயின் இல்வாழ்க்கை","பண்பும் பயனும் அது"],"p":"If the married life possess love and virtue, these will be both its duty and reward.","r":"If household life has love and virtue, these are its character and its fruit.","n":"The verse makes the reward internal: the fruit of having love and virtue is having them. Parimelazhagar directs love to the wife and virtue to sharing, whereas Manakkudavar directs love to everyone and says no further reward is needed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","aram","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:5"},"k:46":{"book":"kural","num":46,"t":["அறத்தாற்றின் இல்வாழ்க்கை ஆற்றின் புறத்தாற்றில்","போஒய்ப் பெறுவ தெவன்?"],"p":"What will he who lives virtuously in the domestic state gain by going into the other, (ascetic) state ?","r":"If one conducts household life in the way of virtue, what would one gain by going off along the outside way?","n":"The rhetorical question challenges chapter 3 directly. Everything depends on how far 'outside way' reaches.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","thuravu","aram"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The outside way is only the forest stage, which leaves full renunciation out of the comparison.","இல்லை விட்டு வனத்துச் செல்லும் நிலை."],["manakkudavar","The outside way is austerity in general, and the householder gets the fruit of austerity too.","புறநெறியாகிய தவத்திற் போய்ப் பெறுவது யாதோ?"]],"ch":"k:5"},"k:47":{"book":"kural","num":47,"t":["இயல்பினான் இல்வாழ்க்கை வாழ்பவன் என்பான்","முயல்வாருள் எல்லாம் தலை"],"p":"Among all those who labour (for future happiness) he is greatest who lives well in the household state.","r":"One who lives the household life according to its nature stands first among all who strive.","n":"The verse doesn't say what they strive for, so the comparison group changes with each commentator.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","muyarchi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Those striving are ascetics trying to give up the senses, and the householder gets the result without such effort.","புலன்களை விட முயல்வார் எல்லாருள்ளும் மிக்கவன்."],["manakkudavar","Those striving are people striving for wealth.","முயறல் - பொருட்கு முயறல்."]],"ch":"k:5"},"k:48":{"book":"kural","num":48,"t":["ஆற்றின் ஒழுக் கி அறனிழுக்கா இல்வாழ் க்கை","நோற்பாரின் நோன்மை உடைத்து"],"p":"The householder who, not swerving from virtue, helps the ascetic in his way, endures more than those who endure penance.","r":"A household life that keeps others on their way and doesn't itself slip from virtue has more endurance than those who do penance.","n":"The word chosen, நோன்மை, is the ascetic's own virtue claimed for the householder. Parimelazhagar's reason is that the ascetic bears only their own pain, while the householder also bears that of others.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","thavam","porai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The householder also endures the troubles that come upon others.","பிறரை உற்ற நோயும் பொறுத்தல் இன்மையின்"]],"ch":"k:5"},"k:49":{"book":"kural","num":49,"t":["அறன் எனப் பட்டதே இல்வாழ்க்கை அஃதும்","பிறன்பழிப்ப தில்லாயின் நன்று"],"p":"The marriage state is truly called virtue. The other state is also good, if others do not reproach it.","r":"What is called virtue is household life, and that too is good if there is nothing in it others can blame.","n":"The whole verse turns on what அஃதும் points to. On one reading it is a rebuke to renunciants, and on another it is a condition on householders. Manakkudavar's explanation also shows a caste assumption of his own.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","aram","pazhi","thuravu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","'That too' is renunciation, which is good only if it is free of hypocrisy.","ஏனைத் துறவறமோ எனின், அதுவும் பிறனால் பழிக்கப்படுவது இல்லையாயின்"],["manakkudavar","'That too' is household life, and the blame in question would be a wife of low birth.","பழிக்கப்படுவதென்றது இழிகுலத்தாளாகிய மனையாளை."],["kalaignar","A blameless household life is what is honoured as the household dharma.","பழிப்புக்கு இடமில்லாத இல்வாழ்க்கை இல்லறம் எனப் போற்றப்படும்."]],"ch":"k:5"},"k:50":{"book":"kural","num":50,"t":["வையத்துள் வாழ்வாங்கு வாழ்பவன் வான்உநற்யும்","தெய்வத்துள் வைக்கப் படும்"],"p":"He who on earth has lived in the conjugal state as he should live, will be placed among the Gods who dwell in heaven.","r":"One who lives on earth as life should be lived will be placed among the gods who dwell in the sky.","n":"வைக்கப்படும், 'will be placed', could mean a destiny or an estimation. Manakkudavar treats it as being honoured by all, while Parimelazhagar also expects actual godhood later on. The Tamil word for how to live, வாழ்வாங்கு, is left undefined.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:5"},"k:51":{"book":"kural","num":51,"t":["மனைக்தக்க மாண்புடையள் ஆகித்தற் கொண்டான்","வளத்தக்காள் வாழ்க்கைத் துணை"],"p":"She who has the excellence of home virtues, and can expend within the means of her husband, is a help in the domestic state.","r":"She who has the excellence the home needs, and lives within the means of the man who took her, is the companion of life.","n":"Income literacy is half the definition, and it comes before chastity is mentioned. Parimelazhagar makes her skills concrete: knowing what is needed, cooking and neighbourly help. Manakkudavar instead reads மனை as her birth family, which shifts the standard from her work to where she comes from.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["vaazhkkaithunai","selvam","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:6"},"k:52":{"book":"kural","num":52,"t":["மனைமாட்சி இல்லாள்கண் இல்லாயின் வாழ்க்கை","எனைமாட்சித் தாயினும் இல்"],"p":"If the wife be devoid of domestic excellence, whatever (other) greatness be possessed, the conjugal state, is nothing.","r":"If the wife lacks household excellence, the life has nothing, whatever other distinction it may have.","n":"The final word இல், nothing, is blunt. The verse doesn't say the life is worse, it says the life isn't there at all, and Parimelazhagar says this is because it bears no fruit.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaazhkkaithunai","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:6"},"k:53":{"book":"kural","num":53,"t":["இல்லதென் இல்லவள் மாண்பானால் உள்ளதென்","இல்லவள் மாணாக் கடை?"],"p":"If his wife be eminent (in virtue), what does (that man) not possess ? If she be without excellence, what does (he) possess ?","r":"What is lacking if the wife has worth, and what is present if she does not?","n":"Two questions with no answers given. Manakkudavar supplies the sharp version: though the man has nothing, nothing is lacking, and though he has everything, nothing is there.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaazhkkaithunai","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:6"},"k:54":{"book":"kural","num":54,"t":["பெண்ணின் பெருந்தக்க யாவுள கற்பென்னும்","திண்மைஉண் டாகப் பெறின்"],"p":"What is more excellent than a wife, if she possess the stability of chastity ?","r":"What is greater than a woman, if she has the firmness called கற்பு?","n":"The word திண்மை presents கற்பு as a strength, not as mere purity. Whether the verse is ranking a possession or a form of life is what divides the commentators.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","vaazhkkaithunai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","A wife is ranked among the things a man can obtain, and none is better.","ஒருவன் எய்தும் பொருள்களுள் இல்லாளின் மேம்பட்ட பொருள்கள் யாவை உள"],["manakkudavar","The comparison is about being born a woman, which nothing exceeds if it has this firmness.","பெண் பிறப்புப்போல் மேம்பட்டன யாவையுள்?"],["kalaignar","The firmness is womanhood's own strength of character.","பெண்மையின் உறுதிப் பண்பைப் பெற்றுவிட்டால்,"]],"ch":"k:6"},"k:55":{"book":"kural","num":55,"t":["தெய்வம் தொழாஅள் கொழுநன் தொழுதெழுவாள்","பெய்யெனப் பெய்யும் மழை"],"p":"If she, who does not worship God, but who rising worships her husband, say, \"let it rain,\" it will rain.","r":"She who rises worshipping her husband and not a god: if she says 'rain', it rains.","n":"The verse says flatly that she doesn't worship a god. Parimelazhagar softens this by adding 'other' gods. The line is both the strongest claim of a woman's power in the book and the most troubling, and Kalaignar's entry reads the rain as an image of fearful servitude, not of power.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","mazhai","iraivan"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Her husband is her own god, and her steadfastness is so powerful that the deities obey her.","பிற தெய்வம் தொழாது தன் தெய்வம் ஆகிய கொழுநனைத் தொழாநின்று துயிலெழுவாள்"],["manakkudavar","She regards all gods as being her husband.","எல்லாத் தெய்வ முந் தன்கணவனென்றே கருதி"],["kalaignar","Such a wife is like rain falling in fear at a command: she has made herself a slave.","அஞ்சி நடுங்கிப் பெய்கின்ற மழையைப் போலத் தன்னை அடிமையாக எண்ணிக் கொள்பவளாவாள்."]],"ch":"k:6"},"k:56":{"book":"kural","num":56,"t":["தற்காத்துத் தற்கொண்டாற் பேணித் தகைசான்ற","சொற்காத்துச் சோர்விலாள் பெண்"],"p":"She is a wife who unweariedly guards herself, takes care of her husband, and preserves an unsullied fame.","r":"She is a woman who guards herself, cares for the man who took her, keeps their good name and does not flag.","n":"பெண் here is a title that has to be earned, as the verse defines who counts as a woman. Parimelazhagar takes சொல் as a shared reputation for wife and husband, which means she is responsible for both.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","pugazh","vaazhkkaithunai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:6"},"k:57":{"book":"kural","num":57,"t":["சிறைகாக்கும் காப்பெவன் செய்யும் மகளிர்","நிறைகாக்கும் காப்பே தலை"],"p":"What avails the guard of a prison ? The chief guard of a woman is her chastity.","r":"What use is the guarding of women by confinement? The guard that their own steadfastness keeps is the best.","n":"The verse rejects confinement because it does not work, and it doesn't speak of the woman's rights. It leaves in place the assumption that she needs guarding, but it moves that guard inside her.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","manam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Walls and door-guards are useless without நிறை, the holding of her heart to chastity.","காவல் இரண்டினும் நிறைக் காவல் இல்வழி ஏனைச் சிறைக்காவலால் பயன் இல்லை"],["kalaignar","The verse is a charge against men: treating such women as slaves is ignorance.","அடிமைகளாக நடத்த எண்ணுவது அறியாமையாகும்."]],"ch":"k:6"},"k:58":{"book":"kural","num":58,"t":["பெற்றாற் பெறின்பெறுவர் பெண்டிர் பெருஞ்சிறப்புப்","புத்தேளிர் வாழும் உலகு"],"p":"If women shew reverence to their husbands, they will obtain great excellence in the world where the gods flourish.","r":"If women obtain the one who obtained them, they will gain great distinction in the world where the gods live.","n":"The word play பெற்றாற் பெறின் leaves open what 'obtaining' a husband means. Who has to be worthy depends on how the gap is filled.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","vaazhkkaithunai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The missing word is worship: women who serve their husbands are honoured by the gods.","தற்கொண்டாற் பேணிய மகளிர் புத்தேளிரால் பேணப்படுவர்"],["kalaignar","If a woman obtains a virtuous husband, household life becomes a new world for her.","நற்பண்பு பெற்றவனைக் கணவனாகப் பெற்றால்,"]],"ch":"k:6"},"k:59":{"book":"kural","num":59,"t":["புகழ்புரிந்த இல்லிலோர்க்கு இல்லை இகழ்வார்முன்","ஏறுபோல் பீடு நடை"],"p":"The man whose wife seeks not the praise (of chastity) cannot walk with lion-like stately step, before those who revile them.","r":"Those whose home does not seek renown cannot walk with a great beast's proud gait before those who deride them.","n":"The man's public bearing is said to depend on his wife's conduct. ஏறு needn't mean a lion as Pope and Parimelazhagar have it, since the word covers male animals generally, and Manakkudavar describes the gait without naming an animal.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","perumai","vaazhkkaithunai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:6"},"k:60":{"book":"kural","num":60,"t":["மங்கலம் என்ப மனைமாட்சி மற்று அதன்","நன்கலம் நன்மக்கட் பேறு"],"p":"The excellence of a wife is the good of her husband; and good children are the jewels of that goodness.","r":"They call a home's excellence its blessing, and good children its fine ornament.","n":"Parimelazhagar says this verse prepares for the next chapter by naming the wife's ornament. Children are ornaments here and do not yet have the value they get in chapter 7. Kalaignar takes மனைமாட்சி as the family's culture and not specifically the wife's.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaazhkkaithunai","makkal"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:6"},"k:61":{"book":"kural","num":61,"t":["பெறுமவற்றுள் யாமறிவது இல்லை அறிவறிந்த","மக்கட்பேறு அல்ல பிற"],"p":"Among all the benefits that may be acquired, we know no greater benefit than the acquisition of intelligent children.","r":"Of all that can be gained, we know of nothing else like children who come to know what should be known.","n":"The first-person யாம் அறிவது is unusual and it sounds personal. The word அறிவறிந்த makes this a conditional praise: not children as such, but children who come to understand.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The epithet of understanding restricts மக்கள் to sons, excluding daughters.","'மக்கள்' என்னும் பெயர் பெண் ஒழித்து நின்றது."],["kalaignar","The verse is about intelligent children in general, with no such exclusion.","அறிவில் சிறந்த நல்ல பிள்ளைகளைவிட"]],"ch":"k:7"},"k:62":{"book":"kural","num":62,"t":["எழுபிறப்பும் தீயவை தீண்டா பழிபிறங்காப்","பண்புடை மக்கட் பெறின்"],"p":"The evils of the seven births shall not touch those who abtain children of a good disposition, free from vice.","r":"Evils will not touch one for seven births, if one has children of good character who bring no blame.","n":"The verse assumes a child's character can protect a parent across births. Kalaignar's reading turns seven births into seven generations, which reverses the direction from the parent's future to the descendants', but no aligned Parimelazhagar or Manakkudavar comment is available to set against it here.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","piravi","pazhi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:7"},"k:63":{"book":"kural","num":63,"t":["தம்பொருள் என்பதம் மக்கள் அவர்பொருள்","தம்தம் வினையான் வரும்"],"p":"Men will call their sons their wealth, because it flows to them through the deeds which they (sons) perform on their behalf.","r":"People call their children their wealth, because the wealth of those children comes by way of their own deeds.","n":"தம்தம் வினை is the crux: whose deeds, and whose wealth? Pope's couplet and his own gloss take opposite sides of this.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","vinai","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Sons' good deeds done on the parents' behalf bring what they earned to the parents in the next world.","அப்புதல்வர் செய்த பொருள் தம்மை நோக்கி அவர் செய்யும் நல்வினையானே தம்பால் வரும்"],["kalaignar","Each child's wealth comes from that child's own actions.","அம்மக்களின் பொருள்கள் அவரவர் செயல்களின் விளைவாக வரக் கூடியவை."]],"ch":"k:7"},"k:64":{"book":"kural","num":64,"t":["அமிழ்தினும் ஆற்ற இனிதேதம் மக்கள்","சிறுகை அளாவிய கூழ்"],"p":"The rice in which the little hand of their children has dabbled will be far sweeter (to the parent) than ambrosia.","r":"Far sweeter than amrita is the plain gruel stirred by the small hands of one's own children.","n":"The food is கூழ், a humble gruel, and it is sweet because of the mess. Parimelazhagar illustrates the mess by citing Purananuru 188, which makes this verse a real thread across books.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:7"},"k:65":{"book":"kural","num":65,"t":["மக்கள்மெய் தீண்டல் உடற்கின்பம் மற்று அவர்","சொற்கேட்டல் இன்பம் செவிக்கு"],"p":"The touch of children gives pleasure to the body, and the hearing of their words, pleasure to the ear.","r":"Touching one's children is joy to the body, and hearing their words is joy to the ear.","n":"The chapter dwells on the senses here, after verses that praise restraining them. Parimelazhagar notes that சொல் is deliberately general, so it covers grown learned speech as well as baby talk.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:7"},"k:66":{"book":"kural","num":66,"t":["குழல் இனிது யாழ்இனிது என்பதம் மக்கள்","மழலைச்சொல் கேளா தவர்"],"p":"\"The pipe is sweet, the lute is sweet,\" say those who have not heard the prattle of their own children.","r":"'The flute is sweet, the lute is sweet', say those who haven't heard their own children's prattle.","n":"The verse never actually says prattle is sweeter. It leaves that to be inferred, and Parimelazhagar notes this implied ending. Such praise comes in the form of mild scorn for the musically refined.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["makkal","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:7"},"k:67":{"book":"kural","num":67,"t":["தந்தை மகற்காற்று நன்றி அவையத்து","முந்தி இருப்பச் செயல்"],"p":"The benefit which a father should confer on his son is to give him precedence in the assembly of the learned.","r":"The good a father does for a son is to make him sit at the front in the assembly.","n":"Parimelazhagar rules out nepotism: the son must actually surpass the assembly in learning. He adds that making him rich is no real good, since wealth brings pain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:7"},"k:68":{"book":"kural","num":68,"t":["தம்மின்தம் மக்கள் அறிவுடைமை மாநிலத்து","மன்னுயிர்க் கெல்லாம் இனிது"],"p":"That their children should possess knowledge is more pleasing to all men of this great earth than to themselves.","r":"Their children's understanding is sweet to all beings on the great earth, more than to themselves.","n":"தம்மின், than themselves, can attach to two different things. Pope's couplet and gloss again split, one on each side.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It is sweeter to all the world, especially the assembly, than it is to the parents.","மன்உயிர்க்கு எல்லாம் தம்மின் இனிது"],["kalaignar","It concerns children who are wiser than their parents, which gladdens everyone.","பெற்றோரைக் காட்டிலும் பிள்ளைகள் அறிவிற் சிறந்து விளங்கினால்,"]],"ch":"k:7"},"k:69":{"book":"kural","num":69,"t":["ஈன்ற பொழுதின் பெரிதுவக்கும் தன்மகனைச்","சான்றோன் எனக்கேட்ட தாய்"],"p":"The mother who hears her son called \"a wise man\" will rejoice more than she did at his birth.","r":"A mother who hears her son called worthy rejoices more than at the hour she bore him.","n":"The verse says she hears. Parimelazhagar's explanation is his own and is telling: he says women cannot know this for themselves. The verse may simply show that public recognition is what is wanted. It sets the birth's joy as the baseline and goes beyond it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","perumai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","She has to hear it because, by a woman's nature, she cannot judge it herself.","பெண்ணியல்பால் தானாக அறியாமையின் 'கேட்ட தாய்'"]],"ch":"k:7"},"k:70":{"book":"kural","num":70,"t":["மகன்தந்தைக்கு ஆற்றும் உதவி இவன்தந்தை","என்நோற்றான் கொல் எனும் சொல்"],"p":"(So to act) that it may be said \"by what great penance did his father beget him,\" is the benefit which a son should render to his father.","r":"The help a son gives his father is the saying, 'What penance did this one's father do?'","n":"The repayment is a sentence spoken by others. The son can't deliver it directly, only live so that others say it. The verse uses the language of austerity (நோற்றான்), so fatherhood is framed as a kind of penance.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","pugazh","thavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:7"},"k:71":{"book":"kural","num":71,"t":["அன்பிற்கும் உண்டோ அடைக்குந்தாழ் ஆர்வலர்","புன்கணீர் பூசல் தரும்"],"p":"Is there any fastening that can shut in love ? Tears of the affectionate will publish the love that is within.","r":"Is there any bolt that can shut in even love? The small tears of the loving will proclaim it.","n":"A tiny tear (புன்கணீர்) produces an uproar (பூசல்), and the contrast in scale is the point. Parimelazhagar specifies that the tears fall at the beloved's pain, and he treats them as logical inference: love is unseen but can be inferred.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:8"},"k:72":{"book":"kural","num":72,"t":["அன்பிலார் எல்லாம் தமக்குரியர் அன்புடையார்","என்பும் உரியர் பிறர்க்கு"],"p":"Those who are destitute of love appropriate all they have to themselves; but those who possess love cons ider even their bones to belong to others.","r":"The loveless belong to themselves in everything, and the loving belong to others even to their bones.","n":"Love is framed as a question of ownership. Parimelazhagar glosses the bones by citing Purananuru 43, on the king who gave his own flesh for a dove, which is a direct thread to the Sangam corpus.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:8"},"k:73":{"book":"kural","num":73,"t":["அன்போடு இயைந்த வழக்கென்ப ஆருயிர்க்கு","என்போடு இயைந்த தொடர்பு"],"p":"They say that the union of soul and body in man is the fruit of the union of love and virtue (in a former birth).","r":"They say the bond of the precious life with the bone is the way that came to be joined with love.","n":"The syntax is compressed, and the verse could give embodiment a purpose or a past cause. On Parimelazhagar's reading, the body exists for loving, which is an unusually positive view of embodiment. Pope's gloss follows the past-birth view.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","piravi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The soul is joined to a body for the purpose of love, since love can't be done without one.","உடம்போடு இயைந்தல்லது அன்பு செய்யலாகாமையின், அது செய்தற் பொருட்டு இத்தொடர்ச்சி உளதாயிற்று"],["manakkudavar","The present embodiment results from love practised in a previous birth.","முற்பிறப்பின்கண் அன்போடு பொருந்தச் சென்ற செலவென்று சொல்லுவர்"],["kalaignar","The verse is an analogy: love and action should fit as soul and body do.","உயிரும் உடலும்போல் அன்பும் செயலும் இணைந்திருப்பதே உயர்ந்த பொருத்தமாகும்."]],"ch":"k:8"},"k:74":{"book":"kural","num":74,"t":["அன்பு ஈனும் ஆர்வம் உடைமை அதுஈனும்","நண்பு என்னும் நாடாச் சிறப்பு"],"p":"Love begets desire: and that (desire) begets the immeasureable excellence of friendship.","r":"Love gives rise to eagerness for others, and that gives rise to the unsought distinction called friendship.","n":"Love is a seed with a sequence. Parimelazhagar widens the final term a great deal: love for one's own extends to everyone, until the person has neither enemies nor neutrals. நாடா, unsought, suggests this distinction arrives without being chased.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:8"},"k:75":{"book":"kural","num":75,"t":["அன்புற்று அமர்ந்த வழக்கென்ப வையகத்து","இன்புற்றார் எய்தும் சிறப்பு"],"p":"They say that the felicity which those who, after enjoying the pleasure (of the conjugal state) in this world, obtain in heaven is the result of their domestic state imbued with love.","r":"They say that the distinction won by those who have known joy in this world is the outcome of a life settled in love.","n":"The commentators disagree about the timeline. Manakkudavar puts the love in a past birth, Parimelazhagar puts the distinction in heaven after this life, and Kalaignar keeps both in this life. Parimelazhagar's contrast is a pointed one: ascetics reach heaven through pain, while householders reach it through joy.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","inbam","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:8"},"k:76":{"book":"kural","num":76,"t":["அறத்திற்கே அன்புசார் பென்ப அறியார்","மறத்திற்கும் அஃதே துணை"],"p":"The ignorant say that love is an ally to virtue only, but it is also a help to get out of vice.","r":"The ignorant say that love supports only virtue, but it is also the ally against, or of, மறம்.","n":"The word மறம் is the problem. It can mean sin or valour, and the ambiguous dative 'for மறம்' leaves open whether love helps to remove it or to do it.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","aram"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","மறம் is hostile feeling, and love helps remove it when one treats an enemy as a friend.","மறத்தை நீக்குதற்கும் துணையாம்"],["manakkudavar","Love also stands by those who do மறம்.","அவ்வன்பு மறஞ் செய்வார்க்குத் துணையாம்,"],["kalaignar","மறம் is heroism, and heroic action is also backed by love.","வீரச் செயல்களுக்கும் அன்பு துணையாகத் திகழ்கிறது"]],"ch":"k:8"},"k:77":{"book":"kural","num":77,"t":["என்பி லதனை வெயில்போலக் காயுமே","அன்பி லதனை அறம்"],"p":"So loveless beings virtue's power to nothing brings.","r":"Virtue burns what has no love, as the sun burns what has no bones.","n":"It is a chilling image, and on Parimelazhagar's explanation the sun bears no malice: it shines equally, and the boneless creature perishes by its own nature. Virtue is thus not vindictive, which makes this verse more unsettling.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["aram","anbu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","This is அறம் personified as a deity, impersonal like the sun, and the loveless soul perishes before it.","அன்பில்லாத உயிரை அறக்கடவுள்."],["kalaignar","What burns is one's own conscience, in someone who knows virtue but doesn't follow it.","அவரது மனச்சாட்சியே வாட்டி வதைக்கும்."]],"ch":"k:8"},"k:78":{"book":"kural","num":78,"t":["அன்பகத் தில்லா உயிர்வாழ்க்கை வன்பாற்கண்","வற்றல் மரந்தளிர்த் தற்று"],"p":"The domestic state of that man whose mind is without love is like the flourishing of a withered tree upon the parched desert.","r":"A life without love inside is like a dried tree putting forth shoots on hard ground.","n":"The image is an impossibility, as Parimelazhagar says and as Manakkudavar explains: there is nothing in the tree to cause sprouting. The verse says உயிர்வாழ்க்கை, a living being's life, and Parimelazhagar and Pope narrow it to household life.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:8"},"k:79":{"book":"kural","num":79,"t":["புறத்துறுப் பெல்லாம் எவன்செய்பு ம் யாக்கை","அகத்துறுப்பு அன்பி லவர்க்கு"],"p":"Of what avail are all the external members (of the body) to those who are destitute of love, the internal member.","r":"What use are all the outer parts to those without love, the inner part of the body?","n":"Love is classed as an organ, which is a striking way to make it non-optional. Parimelazhagar explicitly refutes the bodily-organs reading that Kalaignar's comment follows.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["anbu","manam","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Outer 'parts' are household means such as place, wealth and servants, because the body reading has no connection to household life.","புறத்து உறுப்பாவன: இடனும், பொருளும், ஏவல் செய்வாரும் முதலாயின."],["kalaignar","Outer parts are the body's beauty, which is pointless without love.","புறத்து உறுப்புகள் அழகாக இருந்து என்ன பயன்?"]],"ch":"k:8"},"k:80":{"book":"kural","num":80,"t":["அன்பின் வழியது உயிர்நிலை அஃதிலார்க்கு","என்புதோல் போர்த்த உடம்பு"],"p":"That body alone which is inspired with love contains a living soul: if void of it, (the body) is bone overlaid with skin.","r":"The seat of life goes by way of love; for those without it, a body is bones wrapped in skin.","n":"The final verse is about what counts as alive, and it isn't an exaggeration about ethics. Manakkudavar connects உயிர்நிலை to release, whereas Parimelazhagar connects it to the household virtue that makes a body live.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:8"},"k:81":{"book":"kural","num":81,"t":["இருந்தோம்பி இல்வாழ்வ தெல்லாம் விருந்தோம்பி","வேளாண்மை செய்தற் பொருட்டு"],"p":"The whole design of living in the domestic state and laying up (property) is (to be able) to exercise the","r":"All of staying at home and living by guarding one's goods is for the purpose of caring for guests and doing them good.","n":"The verse gives household life a purpose outside itself. Parimelazhagar draws the consequence: without this, the toil of home and earning has no fruit.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","ilvaazhkkai","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:9"},"k:82":{"book":"kural","num":82,"t":["விருந்து புறத்ததாத் தானுண்டல் சாவா","மருந்தெனினும் வேண் டற்பாற் றன்று"],"p":"It is not fit that one should wish his guests to be outside (his house) even though he were eating the food of immortality.","r":"Even if it were the medicine of immortality, eating it alone while the guest stays outside is not to be desired.","n":"Parimelazhagar offers a second, sharper reading as acceptable: if someone calls even one meal eaten without a guest a medicine of immortality, reject it. Either way, the argument sets a refusal to eat alone over the prospect of not dying.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["virundhu","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:9"},"k:83":{"book":"kural","num":83,"t":["வருவிருந்து வைகலும் ஓம்புவான் வாழ்க்கை","பருவந்து பாழ்படுதல் இன்று"],"p":"The domestic life of the man that daily entertains the guests who come to him shall not be laid waste by poverty.","r":"The life of one who daily tends the guest who comes will not suffer and fall into ruin.","n":"It answers the obvious worry that hosting every day would be ruinous. Parimelazhagar goes further than the verse's 'not ruined': he says wealth will keep branching out.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:9"},"k:84":{"book":"kural","num":84,"t":["அகனமர்ந்து செய்யாள் உறையும் முகனமர்ந்து","நல்விருந்து ஓம்புவான் இல்"],"p":"Lakshmi with joyous mind shall dwell in the house of that man who, with cheerful countenance, entertains the good as guests.","r":"The fair goddess dwells with a glad heart in the home of one who tends good guests with a glad face.","n":"A chiasm links the two gladnesses: the host's face and the goddess's heart. Parimelazhagar's reason is that she's pleased when her wealth goes the right way. He also takes நல், 'good', to limit the guests to those of high knowledge and conduct.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:9"},"k:85":{"book":"kural","num":85,"t":["வித்தும் இடல்வேண்டும் கொல்லோ விருந்தோம்பி","மிச்சில் மிசைவான் புலம்"],"p":"Is it necessary to sow the field of the man who, having feasted his guests, eats what may remain ?","r":"Must one even sow the field of the person who feeds guests and then eats what's left?","n":"மிச்சில், leftovers, is a low word for the host's own food, as if the guest's plate comes first. The rhetorical question doesn't say what the answer is, and the commentators answer it in opposite ways.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","No sowing is needed, since the field will yield by itself.","'தானே விளையும்' என்பது குறிப்பெச்சம்."],["kalaignar","Such a host would use even the seed set aside for the field to feed guests.","தன் நிலத்திற்குரிய விதையைக்கூட விருந்தோம்பலுக்குப் பயன்படுத்தாமல் இருப்பானா?"]],"ch":"k:9"},"k:86":{"book":"kural","num":86,"t":["செல்விருந்து ஓம்பி வருவிருந்து பார்த்திருப்பான்","நல்வருந்து வானத் தவர்க்கு"],"p":"He who, having entertained the guests that have come, looks out for others who may yet come, will be a welcome guest to the inhabitants of heaven.","r":"One who tends the guest who is leaving and watches for the guest to come will be a good guest to those in the sky.","n":"The host becomes the guest in heaven, and the verse has an elegant reciprocity. Manakkudavar gives 'watching' a concrete rule: not eating without a guest. Kalaignar's 'those in the sky of renown' removes the literal heaven that Parimelazhagar assumes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["virundhu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:9"},"k:87":{"book":"kural","num":87,"t":["இனைத்துணைத் தென்பதொன் றில்லை விருந்தின்","துணைத்துணை வேள்விப் பயன்"],"p":"The advantages of benevolence cannot be measured; the measure (of the virtue) of the guests (entertained) is the only measure.","r":"The fruit of this sacrifice has no set measure: it is as great as the guest's worth.","n":"Calling hospitality வேள்வி claims the language of ritual for a household meal. The measure is the guest and not the cost, and Parimelazhagar supports this with a Naladiyar verse. This also introduces a hierarchy among guests, which sits awkwardly with welcoming whoever comes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:9"},"k:88":{"book":"kural","num":88,"t":["பரிந்தோம்பிப் பற்றற்றேம் என்பர் விருந்தோம்பி","வேள்வி தலைப்படா தார்"],"p":"Who cherish not their guests, nor kindly help supply.","r":"Those who don't take up the sacrifice of tending guests guard their goods anxiously and then say 'we have no support'.","n":"It is a late regret: the wealth they guarded with such pain is gone. பற்றற்றேம் can also be read as the renouncer's boast, 'we're without attachment', and that echo seems to me to sharpen the irony, though no commentator here says so.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["virundhu","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:9"},"k:89":{"book":"kural","num":89,"t":["உடைமையுள் இன்மை விருந்தோம்பல் ஓம்பா","மடமை மடவார்கண் உண்டு"],"p":"That stupidity which excercises no hospitality is poverty in the midst of wealth. It is the property of the stupid.","r":"Poverty in the midst of having is the folly that neglects hospitality, and it is found only among fools.","n":"The verse redefines poverty as a failure of use, not a lack of goods. Parimelazhagar explains: since folly takes away the benefit one gets from wealth, it makes having into not having.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","selvam","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:9"},"k:90":{"book":"kural","num":90,"t":["மோப்பக் குழையும் அனிச்சம் முகந்திரிந்து","நோக்கக் குநழ்யும் விருந்து"],"p":"As the Anicham flower fades in smelling, so fades the guest when the face is turned away.","r":"The anicham flower wilts when smelled, and a guest wilts when the host's face changes on seeing them.","n":"Parimelazhagar's analysis has the guest more delicate than the flower, as the flower needs to be touched but the guest wilts at a distance. He gives a sequence of pleasant face, pleasant word and good deed, which leads directly into the next chapter.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["virundhu","inchol"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:9"},"k:91":{"book":"kural","num":91,"t":["இன்சொலால் ஈரம் அளைஇப் படிறுஇலவாம்","செம்பொருள் கண்டார்வாய்ச் சொல்"],"p":"Sweet words are those which imbued with love and free from deceit flow from the mouth of the virtuous.","r":"Sweet words are those from the mouths of people who see the true thing, moist with love and free of deceit.","n":"The definition rules out flattery. Sweetness needs truthfulness and love, and ஈரம், moisture, makes love a physical quality of the words. Whether it allows rebuke is what the commentators bring out.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["inchol","anbu","vaaimai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","செம்பொருள் is virtue, and the speakers are those who understand it.","அறத்தினை உணர்ந்தார் வாயிற்சொற்கள்."],["manakkudavar","The speakers judge disputes between two parties, and they must rebuke sweetly too.","இஃது ஒருவனைக் கடிந்து சொல்லவேண்டு மிடத்தும் இன்சொல்லாலே கடிய வேண்டுமென்றது."]],"ch":"k:10"},"k:92":{"book":"kural","num":92,"t":["அகன்அமர்ந்து ஈதலின் நன்றே முகனமர்ந்து","இன்சொலன் ஆகப் பெறின்"],"p":"Sweet speech, with a cheerful countenance is better than a gift made with a joyous mind.","r":"Better than giving with a glad heart is to be, with a glad face, a person of sweet speech.","n":"This is a surprising ranking in a book so committed to giving. Parimelazhagar's reasoning is that sweet speech is in one's own power, where a gift depends on wealth, yet it comes naturally only to a virtuous heart, so it's the rarer thing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inchol","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:10"},"k:93":{"book":"kural","num":93,"t":["முகத்தான் அமர்ந் துஇனிது நோக்கி அகத்தானாம்","இன்சொ லினதே அறம்"],"p":"Sweet speech, flowing from the heart (uttered) with a cheerful countenance and a sweet look, is true virtue.","r":"Virtue lies in sweet words from the heart, after looking kindly with a glad face.","n":"The sequence is face, then eye, then heart, then word, and அகத்தானாம் is what keeps it from being mere manners. Parimelazhagar takes the emphatic ending as 'not in giving'. That's a strong inference: the verse may only be emphasizing speech.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["inchol","aram","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:10"},"k:94":{"book":"kural","num":94,"t":["துன்புறூஉம் துவ்வாமை இல்லாகும் யார்மாட்டும்","இன்புறூஉம் இன்சொ லவர்க்கு"],"p":"Sorrow-increasing poverty shall not come upon those who use towards all, pleasure-increasing sweetness of speech.","r":"Grievous want won't come to those whose sweet speech gives everyone joy.","n":"The two forms உறூஉம் balance grief and joy in matching words. The promise seems to be social, not magical: Parimelazhagar reasons that one who has only friends comes to all wealth.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inchol","selvam","inbam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","துவ்வாமை is material poverty, in which the senses have nothing to enjoy.","துன்பத்தை மிகுவிக்கும் நல்குரவு இல்லையாம்."],["kalaignar","The want removed is a poverty of friendship.","'நட்பில் வறுமை' எனும் துன்பமில்லை."]],"ch":"k:10"},"k:95":{"book":"kural","num":95,"t":["பணிவுடையன் இன்சொலன் ஆதல் ஒருவற்கு","அணியல்ல மற்றுப் பிற"],"p":"Humility and sweetness of speech are the ornaments of man; all others are not (ornaments).","r":"Being humble and sweet-spoken is a person's ornament, and nothing else is.","n":"The verse states humility without limit. Parimelazhagar limits it: one is to be humble toward those who deserve deference and sweet-spoken toward everybody. That's a notable narrowing by the commentator.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inchol","panivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:10"},"k:96":{"book":"kural","num":96,"t":["அல்லவை தேய அறம்பெருகும் நல்லவை","நாடி இனிய சொலின்"],"p":"If a man, while seeking to speak usefully, speaks also sweetly, his sins will diminish and his virtue increase.","r":"If one seeks out good things and speaks them sweetly, what is not virtue wears away and virtue grows.","n":"நாடி, having sought out, makes sweetness conditional on content. Parimelazhagar adds the converse: good counsel spoken harshly isn't virtue. For him the effect is on the speaker's own merit, whereas Kalaignar makes it a method of guiding others.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inchol","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:10"},"k:97":{"book":"kural","num":97,"t":["நயன் ஈன்று நன்றி பயக்கும் பயன்ஈன்று","பண்பின் தலைப்பிரியாச் சொல்"],"p":"That speech which, while imparting benefits ceases not to please, will yield righteousness (for this world) and merit (for the next world).","r":"Speech that yields benefit and never parts from gentleness produces justice and brings forth good.","n":"பயன் and பண்பு are held together, so usefulness alone doesn't suffice and neither does charm. Parimelazhagar defines நயன் strikingly as being in accord with the world.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inchol","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:10"},"k:98":{"book":"kural","num":98,"t":["சிறுமையுவு நீங்கிய இன்சொல் மறுமையும்","இம்மையும் இன்பம் தரும்"],"p":"Sweet speech, free from harm to others, will give pleasure both in this world and in the next.","r":"Sweet speech free of pettiness gives joy in the next life and in this one.","n":"The next life is named first, and Parimelazhagar says this is because its joy is greater. Kalaignar reads this as renown during and after life, which removes the afterlife. Pope's and Parimelazhagar's 'harm to others' is more specific than சிறுமை, smallness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["inchol","inbam","sirumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:10"},"k:99":{"book":"kural","num":99,"t":["இன்சொல் இனிதீன்றல் காண்பான் எவன்கொலோ","வன்சொல் வழங்கு வது?"],"p":"Why does he use harsh words, who sees the pleasure which sweet speech yields ?","r":"Why would one who sees how sweetly sweet speech yields use harsh words?","n":"Parimelazhagar makes this an argument from experience: you know how others' sweet words feel to you, and harsh words are just as painful to them. The verse doesn't command, it only asks what the point is.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inchol"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:10"},"k:100":{"book":"kural","num":100,"t":["இனிய உளவாக இன்னாத கூறல்","கனிஇருப்பக் காய்கவர்ந் தற்று"],"p":"To say disagreeable things when agreeable are at hand is like eating unripe fruit when there is ripe.","r":"Speaking unpleasant things when pleasant ones are there is like grabbing raw fruit when ripe fruit is at hand.","n":"The final charge is stupidity, not wickedness. Parimelazhagar makes the stakes much higher by reading the fruit as Avvai's nelli, which is like amrita, and the raw one as poison. He adds that harsh speech ends up hurting the speaker, and this allusion is a thread to the Sangam story of Avvai and the king.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inchol"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:10"},"k:101":{"book":"kural","num":101,"t":["செய்யாமல் செய்த உதவிக்கு வையகமும்","வானகமும் ஆற்றல் அரிது"],"p":"(The gift of) heaven and earth is not an equivalent for a benefit which is conferred where none had been received.","r":"For help given when no help was given first, earth and heaven together are hardly repayment.","n":"Parimelazhagar gives the reason: all repayment has a cause, and this help has none, so nothing can match it. He also records a variant reading, in which the helped person could not return it, which changes the focus from the giver's freedom to the receiver's helplessness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nanri","eegai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","He reports another reading of the text, about help given to those unable to return it.","மறித்து உதவமாட்டாமை உள்ளவிடத்துச் செய்த உதவி என்று உரைப்பாரும் உளர்."]],"ch":"k:11"},"k:102":{"book":"kural","num":102,"t":["காலத்தி னாற்செய்த நன்றி சிறிதெனினும்","ஞாலத்தின் மாணப் பெரிது"],"p":"A favour conferred in the time of need, though it be small (in itself), is (in value) much larger than the world.","r":"Help given in its time, though small, is far greater than the earth.","n":"Value here is relative to the need of the moment. Parimelazhagar's phrase is striking: it's the moment of mortal crisis that is being weighed, not the gift.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nanri"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:11"},"k:103":{"book":"kural","num":103,"t":["பயன்தூக்கார் செய்த உதவி நயன்தூக்கின்","நன்மை கடலின் பெரிது"],"p":"If we weigh the excellence of a benefit which is conferred without weighing the return, it is larger than the sea.","r":"If you weigh the good of help given by those who don't weigh the return, it is greater than the sea.","n":"The verb தூக்கு is used twice: the giver doesn't weigh, and the recipient should. The three opening comparisons go from earth and heaven to earth to sea, and the verses seem interested in measure as such.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nanri","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:11"},"k:104":{"book":"kural","num":104,"t":["தினைத்துணை நன்றி செயினும் பனைத்துணையாக்","கொள்வர் பயன்தெரி வார்"],"p":"Though the benefit conferred be as small as a millet seed, those who know its advantage will consider it as large as a palmyra fruit.","r":"Though the good done is only as much as a millet, those who understand its fruit take it as a palm.","n":"The enlargement happens in the recipient's mind, and it isn't an error, it's a discipline. Parimelazhagar says the fruit in question is what comes to the one who regards it this way, so gratitude also benefits the grateful.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nanri","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:11"},"k:105":{"book":"kural","num":105,"t":["உதவி வரைத்தன்று உதவி உதவி","செயப்பட்டார் சால்பின் வரைத்து"],"p":"The benefit itself is not the measure of the benefit; the worth of those who have received it is its measure.","r":"Help is not bounded by the help given: its measure is the worth of those to whom it was done.","n":"The verse repeats உதவி three times and it isn't clear which help is being measured.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nanri","perumai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","This concerns repayment: what the receiver gives back is measured by the receiver's own worth, not by the original help.","கைம்மாறான உதவி, காரணத்தானும் பொருளானும் காலத்தானும் ஆகிய மூவகையானும் முன் செய்த உதவியளவிற்று அன்று;"],["kalaignar","This concerns the help itself: its value is judged by the quality of whoever receives it.","அந்த உதவியைப் பெறுபவரின் பண்பைப் பொறுத்தே அதன் அளவு மதிப்பிடப்படும்."]],"ch":"k:11"},"k:106":{"book":"kural","num":106,"t":["மறவற்க மாசற்றார் கேண்மை துறவற்க","துன்பத்துள் துப்பாயார் நட்பு"],"p":"Forsake not the friendship of those who have been your staff in adversity. Forget not be benevolence of the blameless.","r":"Do not forget the bond of the stainless, and do not abandon the friendship of those who were your strength in sorrow.","n":"Two different people are owed loyalty: those of good character and those who helped when it was needed. Parimelazhagar says the first serves the next life and the second this one. The second debt holds whatever the helper's character is.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","nanri"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:11"},"k:107":{"book":"kural","num":107,"t":["எழுமை எழுபிறப்பும் உள்ளுவர் தங்கண்","விழுமந் துடைத்தவர் நட்பு"],"p":"(The wise) will remember throughout their seven-fold births the love of those who have wiped away their affliction.","r":"For all seven births they will remember the friendship of those who wiped away their sorrow.","n":"துடைத்தவர், those who wiped, has a physical sense of wiping tears away. Parimelazhagar notes the past tense conveys speed, and he cites the lost Valaiyapathi for the phrase, which is a valuable thread.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","nanri","piravi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Seven births are meant literally: affection for the helper carries on in each birth.","பிறப்புதோறும் தொடர்ந்து அன்புடையராதல்."],["kalaignar","Seven births is a figure of exaggeration meaning that there is no time limit.","மிகைப்படுத்திச் சொல்லுவதுபோல,"]],"ch":"k:11"},"k:108":{"book":"kural","num":108,"t":["நன்றி மறப்பது நன்றன்று நன்றல்லது","அன்றே மறப்பது நன்று"],"p":"It is not good to forget a benefit; it is good to forget an injury even in the very moment (in which it is inflicted).","r":"It's not good to forget a good done; what's good is to forget, that very day, what isn't good.","n":"நன்று repeats through the verse, so 'good' is both the thing remembered and the act of letting go. Parimelazhagar says this is about one person who has done both good and harm, which is narrower than a general call to forget all wrongs.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nanri","porai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:11"},"k:109":{"book":"kural","num":109,"t":["கொன்றன்ன இன்னா செயினும் அவர்செய்த","ஒன்றுநன்று உள்ளக் கெடும்"],"p":"Though one inflict an injury great as murder, it will perish before the thought of one benefit (formerly) conferred.","r":"Even if they do something as painful as killing, it vanishes when one thinks of a single good they did.","n":"This is a hard saying, as it places the burden on the injured person. Parimelazhagar explains it through 104: the millet taken as a palm outweighs everything. Kalaignar makes it more therapeutic, a way to keep from being wounded inside.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nanri","porai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:11"},"k:110":{"book":"kural","num":110,"t":["எந்நன்றி கொன்றார்க்கும் உய்வுண்டாம் உய்வில்லை","செய்ந்நன்றி கொன்ற மகற்கு"],"p":"He who has killed every virtue may yet escape; there is no escape for him who has killed a benefit.","r":"Those who kill any good may have an escape, but there is no escape for one who kills a good done to him.","n":"Ingratitude is ranked as the sin with no remedy. Parimelazhagar's list of lesser crimes comes from Purananuru 34, whose speaker makes the same comparison, so this verse is a direct thread to that poem.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nanri","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Even the great crimes, cutting a cow's udder, destroying a womb and killing a Brahmin, can be expiated, as Purananuru 34 lists.","'ஆன்முலை அறுத்தலும், மகளிர் கருவினைச் சிதைத்தலும், பார்ப்பார்த்தபுதலும்' (புறநா 34)"]],"ch":"k:11"},"k:111":{"book":"kural","num":111,"t":["தகுதி எனவொன்று நன்றே பகுதியால்","பாற்பட்டு ஒழுகப் பெறின்"],"p":"That equity which consists in acting with equal regard to each of (the three) divisions of men [enemies, strangers and friends] is a pre-eminent virtue.","r":"That one thing called rightness is good if one can keep to it, part by part, in what is due.","n":"Parimelazhagar says பெறின் conveys how rare this is. The whole verse depends on what பகுதி divides.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naduvu","aram"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The parts are enemy, stranger and friend, toward each of whom one keeps the same rule.","பகை, நொதுமல், நட்பு எனும் பகுதிதோறும், தன் முறைமையை விடாது ஒழுகப் பெறின்,"],["manakkudavar","The parts are each person's own station, so what is due varies with position.","அவரவர் நிலைமைப்பகுதியோடே அறத்தின் பாற்பட்டு ஒழுகப் பெறுமாயின்,"]],"ch":"k:12"},"k:112":{"book":"kural","num":112,"t":["செப்பம் உடையவன் ஆக்கஞ் சிதைவின்றி","எச்சத்திற் கேமாப்பு உடைத்து"],"p":"The wealth of the man of rectitude will not perish, but will bring happiness also to his posterity.","r":"The wealth of a person of rectitude doesn't decay and is a strength to those who come after.","n":"The claim concerns durability, not amount. Parimelazhagar explains that it lasts because it comes together with virtue.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naduvu","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:12"},"k:113":{"book":"kural","num":113,"t":["நன்றே தரினும் நடுவிகந்தாம் ஆக்கத்தை","அன்றே யொழிய விடல்"],"p":"Forsake in the very moment (of acquisition) that gain which, though it should bring advantage, is without equity.","r":"Even if it brings nothing but good, let go that very day of gain that comes from leaving what is fair.","n":"The commentators differ on the force of 'even if'. For Parimelazhagar it's only a concession, because such gain can't really bring good. Kalaignar's wording allows that it may, which makes giving it up a costlier demand.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naduvu","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:12"},"k:114":{"book":"kural","num":114,"t":["தக்கார் தகவிலர் என்பது அவரவர்","எச்சத்தாற் காணப்ப படும்"],"p":"The worthy and unworthy may be known by the existence or otherwise of good offsprings.","r":"Whether people are worthy or unworthy will be seen from what each leaves behind.","n":"எச்சம், what remains, is left unspecified, and the choice has large consequences. Parimelazhagar's claim that the unworthy have no good children is a very confident generalisation, and it isn't in the verse.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["naduvu","pugazh","makkal"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","What remains is good offspring, whose presence or absence is a sure mark.","தக்கார்க்கு எச்சம் உண்டாதலும் தகவிலார்க்கு இல்லையாதலும் ஒரு தலையாகலின்,"],["kalaignar","What remains is the praise or blame left after a person is gone.","அவருக்குப் பின் எஞ்சி நிற்கப் போகும் புகழ்ச் சொல்லைக் கொண்டோ அல்லது பழிச் சொல்லைக் கொண்டோதான்"]],"ch":"k:12"},"k:115":{"book":"kural","num":115,"t":["கேடும் பெருக்கமும் இல்லல்ல நெஞ்சத்துக்","கோடாமை சான்றோர்க் கணி"],"p":"Loss and gain come not without cause; it is the ornament of the wise to preserve evenness of mind (under both).","r":"Loss and increase are not absent from life; not letting the heart bend is the ornament of the worthy.","n":"The verse doesn't explain where loss and gain come from, and Pope's 'not mere accident' assumes one view. The important move is that bias is presented as useless as well as wrong.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naduvu","samanilai","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","They are already laid down by past deeds, so bending to get or avoid them achieves nothing.","'அவற்றிற்குக் காரணம் பழவினையே; கோடுதல் அன்று'"],["kalaignar","Rise and fall are simply the nature of the world.","ஒருவர்க்கு வாழ்வும், தாழ்வும் உலக இயற்கை;"]],"ch":"k:12"},"k:116":{"book":"kural","num":116,"t":["கெடுவல்யான் என்பது அறிகதன் நெஞ்சம்","நடுவொரீஇ அல்ல செயின்"],"p":"Let him whose mind departing from equity commits sin well consider thus within himself, \"I shall perish.\"","r":"If one's heart leaves what is fair and does wrong, let one know: 'I will perish'.","n":"The subject is the heart, so the act is inward. Parimelazhagar draws this out: thinking is the same as doing, and the very thought is an omen to be read. The first-person கெடுவல் யான் makes the warning a sentence one says to oneself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naduvu","manam","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:12"},"k:117":{"book":"kural","num":117,"t":["கெடுவாக வையாது உலகம் நடுவாக","நன்றிக்கண் தங்கியான் தாழ்வு"],"p":"The great will not regard as poverty the low estate of that man who dwells in the virtue of equity.","r":"The world won't count as ruin the low state of one who remains in virtue by standing fair.","n":"Parimelazhagar glosses உலகம் as the noble, as commentators often do, and adds that they regard such poverty as wealth. That's a real narrowing, since the actual world may well despise it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naduvu","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:12"},"k:118":{"book":"kural","num":118,"t":["சமன்செய்து சீர்தூக்குங் கோல்போல் அமைந்தொருபால்","கோடாமை சான்றோர்க் கணி"],"p":"To incline to neither side, but to rest impartial as the even-fixed scale is the ornament of the wise.","r":"To be settled and lean to no side, like the balance that first makes itself level and then weighs, is the ornament of the worthy.","n":"A balance isn't indifferent: it first levels itself, then registers exactly what's put on it. Fairness here is a precise response, not neutrality. Parimelazhagar carries the image into assembly practice, with weighing as hearing both sides and not leaning as stating the result equally to all.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naduvu","samanilai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:12"},"k:119":{"book":"kural","num":119,"t":["சொற்கோட்டம் இல்லது செப்பம் ஒருதலையா","உட்கோட்டம் இன்மை பெறின்"],"p":"F reedom from obliquity of speech is rectitude, if there be (corresponding) freedom from bias of mind.","r":"Rectitude is speech without bend, provided one surely has no bend within.","n":"Parimelazhagar's corollary is subtle: a correct statement made from a biased mind isn't impartiality. Manakkudavar adds that it excludes clever words which suit both sides.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["naduvu","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:12"},"k:120":{"book":"kural","num":120,"t":["வாணிகம் செய்வார்க்கு வாணிகம் பேணிப்","பிறவும் தமபோல் செயின்"],"p":"The true merchandize of merchants is to guard and do by the things of others as they do by their own.","r":"For traders, true trade is caring for others' goods as if they were their own.","n":"Manakkudavar glosses வாணிகம் as profit, so honesty is what actually pays. Parimelazhagar's phrasing, taking no more and giving no less, closely resembles a description of merchants in Pattinappalai, as I recall it. That is my own observation, and it should be checked.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naduvu","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:12"},"k:121":{"book":"kural","num":121,"t":["அடக்கம் அமரருள் உய்க்கும் அடங்காமை","ஆரிருள் உய்த்து விடும்"],"p":"Self-control will place (a man) among the Gods; the want of it will drive (him) into the thickest darkness (of hell).","r":"Restraint leads one among the immortals, and lack of restraint drives one into thick darkness.","n":"Both verbs are of driving, so restraint is itself a force that carries a person. The final உய்த்துவிடும் has a sense of finality.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The destinations are literal: the gods' world and a particular hell called darkness.","'இருள்' என்பது ஓர் நரக விசேடம்."],["kalaignar","The results are this-worldly: lasting renown and a darkened life.","அடக்கம் அழியாத புகழைக் கொடுக்கும். அடங்காமை வாழ்வையே இருளாக்கி விடும்."]],"ch":"k:13"},"k:122":{"book":"kural","num":122,"t":["காக்க பொருளா அடக்கத்தை ஆக்கம்","அதனினூஉங் கில்லை உயிர்க்கு"],"p":"Let self-control be guarded as a treasure; there is no greater source of good for man than that.","r":"Guard restraint as your wealth, for life has no greater gain than it.","n":"Restraint is pictured as something to guard, as misers guard money, so the language of acquisition is turned to its opposite. Parimelazhagar notes this concerns humans, who can understand it and benefit.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:13"},"k:123":{"book":"kural","num":123,"t":["செறிவறிந்து சீர்மை பயக்கும் அறிவறிந்து","ஆற்றின் அடங்கப் பெறின்"],"p":"Knowing that self-control is knowledge, if a man should control himself, in the prescribed course, such self-control will bring him distinction among the wise.","r":"If one understands and keeps restrained in the right way, the restraint, recognised, will yield distinction.","n":"The syntax is knotted, and the commentators parse the repeated அறிந்து differently: Parimelazhagar says one learns that restraint is itself knowledge, and Manakkudavar says one learns the five objects and the five organs. I'm unsure which is closest.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:13"},"k:124":{"book":"kural","num":124,"t":["நிலையின் திரியாது அடங்கியான் தோற்றம்","மலையினும் மாணப் பெரிது"],"p":"More lofty than a mountain will be the greatness of that man who without swerving from his domestic state, controls himself.","r":"The stature of one who is restrained and doesn't stray from his station is much greater than a mountain's.","n":"தோற்றம், how one looks or appears, makes this a visual comparison. The dispute over நிலை decides whether the verse has a social hierarchy in it.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","perumai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The station is household life, and the restraint is exercised while enjoying sense objects.","பொறிகளால் புலன்களை நுகராநின்றே அடங்குதல்."],["manakkudavar","The station is the order of varna and stage of life.","நிலை - வன்னாச்சிரம தன்மம்."],["kalaignar","It's a matter of firmness of mind joined to unostentatious restraint.","உறுதியான உள்ளமும், அத்துடன் ஆர்ப்பாட்டமற்ற அடக்க உணர்வும்"]],"ch":"k:13"},"k:125":{"book":"kural","num":125,"t":["எல்லார்க்கும் நன்றாம் பணிதல் அவருள்ளும்","செல்வர்க்கே செல்வம் தகைத்து"],"p":"Humility is good in all; but especially in the rich it is (the excellence of) higher riches.","r":"Bowing is good for all, and among them, for the rich it is a further wealth.","n":"Parimelazhagar asks why the rich are singled out. Those with learning or birth may be humble without it being notable, but for the rich it stands out. Manakkudavar reads செல்வம் simply as abundance, which weakens the pun.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panivu","selvam","adakkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:13"},"k:126":{"book":"kural","num":126,"t":["ஒருமையுள் ஆமைபோல் ஐந்தடக்கல் ஆற்றின்","எழுமையும் ஏமாப் புடைத்து"],"p":"Should one throughout a single birth, like a tortoise keep in his five senses, the fruit of it will prove a safe-guard to him throughout the seven-fold births.","r":"If in one birth one can draw in the five like a tortoise, it is a protection for seven births.","n":"The tortoise withdraws out of self-protection, and the image suggests retreat from danger, not conquest. The numbers one and seven make for an argument about return on effort. Kalaignar translates the seven births as all time.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","piravi","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:13"},"k:127":{"book":"kural","num":127,"t":["யாகாவா ராயினும் நாகாக்க காவாக்கால்","சோகாப்பர் சொல்லிழுக்குப் பட்டு"],"p":"Whatever besides you leave unguarded, guard your tongue; otherwise errors of speech and the consequent misery will ensue.","r":"Whatever else they fail to guard, let them guard the tongue; if they don't, they'll suffer from the fault of their words.","n":"This is a rare concession in a demanding chapter: it sets priorities for someone who cannot do everything. The opening யாகாவாராயினும் accepts failure in advance.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","inchol"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:13"},"k:128":{"book":"kural","num":128,"t":["ஒன்றானுந் தீச்சொல் பொருட்பயன் உண்டாயின்","நன்றாகா தாகி விடும்"],"p":"If a man's speech be productive of a single evil, all the good by him will be turned into evil.","r":"If even one bad word has its effect, it turns out that nothing is good.","n":"The verse doesn't say what stops being good, and the commentators offer three scopes, from one's speech to one's whole moral record.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","inchol","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","One harm caused by evil words turns the good of the speaker's other virtues to evil.","அவனுக்குப் பிற அறங்களான் உண்டான நன்மை தீதாய்விடும்."],["manakkudavar","It concerns a word sweet to the hearer but harmful in effect, so flattery is also bad.","இது சால மொழி கூறினாலுந் தீதாமென்றது."],["kalaignar","One bad word in a speech spoils all of that speech's good words, like poison in milk.","ஒரு குடம் பாலில் துளி நஞ்சுபோல்,"]],"ch":"k:13"},"k:129":{"book":"kural","num":129,"t":["தீயினாற் சுட்டபுண் உள்ளாறும் ஆறாதே","நாவினாற் சுட்ட வடு"],"p":"The wound which has been burnt in by fire may heal, but a wound burnt in by the tongue will never heal.","r":"A wound burned by fire heals inside, but a scar burned by the tongue doesn't heal.","n":"புண் and வடு are carefully chosen: Parimelazhagar notes the first heals and is a wound, while the second stays and is a scar. For him the fire's mark may stay on the body but is healed in the mind, which reverses what one expects to be permanent.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:13"},"k:130":{"book":"kural","num":130,"t":["கதங்காத்துக் கற்றடங்கல் ஆற்றுவான் செவ்வி","அறம்பார்க்கும் ஆற்றின் நுழைந்து"],"p":"Virtue, seeking for an opportunity, will come into the path of that man who, possessed of learning and self-control, guards himself against anger.","r":"Virtue itself looks for its moment and enters by the path to the one who has guarded against anger and has learned restraint.","n":"The usual relation is reversed: virtue is a seeker waiting on a person. Parimelazhagar's gloss of செவ்வி pictures virtue as a petitioner awaiting a good moment to present its plea. Manakkudavar adds what must be restrained: the pride that comes from learning.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","vekuli","adakkam","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:13"},"k:131":{"book":"kural","num":131,"t":["ஒழுக்கம் விழுப்பந் தரலான் ஒழுக்கம்","உயிரினும் ஓம்பப் படும்"],"p":"Propriety of conduct leads to eminence, it should therefore be preserved more carefully than life.","r":"Since conduct gives excellence, conduct is to be guarded more than life.","n":"Parimelazhagar's reasoning is careful: life is the best of all things, but it doesn't confer distinction the way conduct does, so conduct gets greater care. He adds that this holds for high and low alike.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ozhukkam","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:14"},"k:132":{"book":"kural","num":132,"t":["பரிந்தோம்பிக் காக்க ஒழுக்கம் தெரிந்தோம்பித்","தேரினும் அஃதே துணை"],"p":"Let propriety of conduct be laboriously preserved and guarded; though one know and practise and excel in many virtues, that will be an eminent aid.","r":"Guard conduct with great effort; however much you search and weigh, it alone is the help.","n":"The three forms of ஓம்பி, தெரிந்து and தேர்ந்து make it sound like a long investigation with one result. Parimelazhagar makes it a survey of all virtues, of which only this one helps in both lives.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ozhukkam","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:14"},"k:133":{"book":"kural","num":133,"t":["ஒழுக்கம் உடைமை குடிமை இழுக்கம்","இழிந்த பிறப்பாய் விடும்"],"p":"Propriety of conduct is true greatness of birth, and impropriety will sink into a mean birth.","r":"Having conduct is what noble birth is, and failing in it becomes low birth.","n":"The verse redefines birth by behaviour. Even the conservative commentators allow that one's status can really change, though for Parimelazhagar it is within a varna frame that the verse doesn't state.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ozhukkam","kudi","pirappu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Status really changes: a low-born person of good conduct becomes high, and a high-born person who fails falls.","உயர்ந்த வருணத்துப் பிறந்தாராயினும் ஒழுக்கத்தில் தவறத் தாழ்ந்த வருணத்தராவர்"],["kalaignar","Conduct is the evidence of good birth, and failures are regarded as low-born whoever they are.","ஒழுக்கம் தவறுகிறவர்கள் யாவராயினும் அவர்கள் இழிந்த குடியில் பிறந்தவர்களாகவே கருதப்படுவர்."]],"ch":"k:14"},"k:134":{"book":"kural","num":134,"t":["மறப்பினும் ஓத்துக் கொளலாகும் பார் ப்பான்","பிறப்பொழுக்கங் குன்றக் கெடும்"],"p":"A Brahman though he should forget the Vedas may recover it by reading; but, if he fail in propriety of conduct even his high bi rth will be destroyed.","r":"Even if forgotten, the Veda can be learned again, but a Brahmin's birth is ruined when conduct declines.","n":"This is a pointed ranking, because study is what the Brahmin is supposed to be defined by. It makes birth a thing that can be lost. Parimelazhagar says the rule extends from this high example to the other varnas.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["ozhukkam","pirappu","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:14"},"k:135":{"book":"kural","num":135,"t":["அழுக்கா றுடையான்கண் ஆக்கம்போன்று இல்லை","ஒழுக்க மிலான்கண் உயர்வு"],"p":"Just as the envious man will be without wealth, so will the man of destitute of propriety of conduct be without greatness.","r":"As there is no prosperity in the envious, so there is no elevation in one who lacks conduct.","n":"The simile takes as given what chapter 17 has yet to argue, that envy destroys wealth. Parimelazhagar uses verse 166 to extend it: as an envious man's kin suffer, so do a person's kin when conduct fails.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ozhukkam","azhukkaaru","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:14"},"k:136":{"book":"kural","num":136,"t":["ஒழுக்கத்தின் ஒல்கார் உரவோர் இழுக்கத்தின்","ஏதம் படுபாக் கறிந்து"],"p":"Those firm in mind will not slacken in their observance of the proprieties of life, knowing, as they do, the misery that flows from the transgression from them.","r":"The strong don't slacken in conduct, knowing what harm arises from a lapse.","n":"The motive given is prudence, not love of virtue. Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar differ a little on உரவோர், as strength of mind and understanding respectively, the same question raised by உரன் in verse 24.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ozhukkam","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:14"},"k:137":{"book":"kural","num":137,"t":["ஒழுக்கத்தின் எய்துவர் மேன்மை இழுக்கத்தின்","எய்துவர் எய்தாப் பழி"],"p":"From propriety of conduct men obtain greatness; from impropriety comes insufferable disgrace.","r":"From conduct people attain high standing, and from lapses they attain blame not properly theirs.","n":"எய்தாப் பழி is the sharp part. Parimelazhagar sees that after one slip, even a false charge from an enemy is believed. Conduct is therefore a shield as well as a good.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ozhukkam","pazhi","perumai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","When enemies make false charges, the world believes them because of the earlier lapse.","பகை பற்றி அடாப்பழி கூறியவழி, அதனையும் இழுக்கம்பற்றி உலகம் அடுக்கும் என்று கொள்ளுமாகலின்,"]],"ch":"k:14"},"k:138":{"book":"kural","num":138,"t":["நன்றிக்கு வித்தாகும் நல்லொழுக்கம் தீயொழுக்கம்","என்றும் இடும்பை தரும்"],"p":"Propriety of conduct is the seed of virtue; impropriety will ever cause sorrow.","r":"Good conduct is a seed of good, and bad conduct always gives suffering.","n":"The two halves are asymmetrical: one is a seed with a future harvest, the other suffers 'always'. Parimelazhagar shows each half implies what the other leaves out. Manakkudavar makes the seed one of release, which the verse doesn't say.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ozhukkam","idumbai","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:14"},"k:139":{"book":"kural","num":139,"t":["ஒழுக்க முடையவர்க்கு ஒல்லாவே தீய","வழுக்கியும் வாயாற் சொலல்"],"p":"Those who study propriety of conduct will not speak evil, even forgetfully.","r":"Speaking evil with their mouths, even by a slip, is not possible for those who have conduct.","n":"It's a strong claim: conduct has become so ingrained that the slip itself can't occur. Parimelazhagar adds speech improper to one's varna to the list of evil words, which is his addition. He also says the redundant 'with the mouth' implies that it's a mouth used to good words.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ozhukkam","inchol"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:14"},"k:140":{"book":"kural","num":140,"t":["உலகத்தோடு ஒட்ட ஒழுகல் பலகற்றும்","கல்லார் அறிவிலா தார்"],"p":"Those who know not how to act agreeably to the world, though they have learnt many things, are still ignorant.","r":"Those who haven't learned to live in accord with the world have studied much and still lack understanding.","n":"All three commentators narrow உலகம் to the noble. Yet Parimelazhagar makes an unusual concession in explaining why the verse says 'world': scripture contains things that no longer suit the present, and it omits things that do.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ozhukkam","kalvi","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Living with the world includes setting aside what the moral texts say that doesn't suit this age, and taking up what suits it.","அறநூல் சொல்லியவற்றுள் இக்காலத்திற்கு ஏலாதன ஒழிந்து, சொல்லாதனவற்றுள் ஏற்பன கொண்டு வருதலான்"]],"ch":"k:14"},"k:141":{"book":"kural","num":141,"t":["பிறன்பொருளாள் பெட்டொழுகும் பேதைமை ஞாலத்து","அறம்பொருள் கண்டார்கண் இல்"],"p":"The folly of desiring her who is the property of another will not be found in those who know (the attributes of) virtue and (the rights of) property.","r":"The folly of desiring a woman who is another's possession doesn't exist in those who have seen virtue and wealth in this world.","n":"பிறன்பொருளாள், the verse's own word, treats the woman as property. Parimelazhagar explains why only two of the three aims are named: the science of pleasure has a term for this very act, so its experts can't be cited against it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piranil","aram","selvam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:15"},"k:142":{"book":"kural","num":142,"t":["அறன்கடை நின்றாருள் எல்லாம் பிறன்கடை","நின்றாரின் பேதையார் இல்"],"p":"Among all those who stand on the outside of virtue, there are no greater fools than those who stand outside their neighbour's door.","r":"Among all who stand outside virtue, none is a greater fool than those who stand outside another man's door.","n":"The repeated கடை sets one threshold on another: outside virtue and outside the door. Parimelazhagar's reason is telling: other sinners at least get their pleasure, whereas this man is too afraid even for that. So the charge is stupidity, and even pleasure is lost.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piranil","aram","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:15"},"k:143":{"book":"kural","num":143,"t":["விளிந்தா஡஢ன் வேறல்லர் மன்ற தெளிந்தாரில்","தீமை புரிந்து ஒழுகு வார்"],"p":"Certainly they are no better than dead men who desire evil towards the wife of those who undoubtingly confide in them.","r":"Those who take to evil in the home of people who trust them are surely no different from the dead.","n":"The wrong here is defined by trust, as தெளிந்தார் shows: Parimelazhagar points out that the act is only possible because the husband trusts him. It's betrayal of a friend as much as sexual wrongdoing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["piranil","natpu","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:15"},"k:144":{"book":"kural","num":144,"t":["எனைத்துணையர் ஆயினும் என்னாம் தினைத்துணையும்","தேரான் பிறனில் புகல்"],"p":"However great one may be, what does it avail if, without at all considering his guilt, he goes unto the wife of another ?","r":"However great they are, what does it come to, when one enters another's home without weighing it even a millet's worth?","n":"A millet's worth of thought would be enough to stop it. Parimelazhagar names Indra as the example of greatness lost. He also reports a reading of தேரான் பிறன் as 'the unsuspecting husband'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piranil","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:15"},"k:145":{"book":"kural","num":145,"t":["எளிதென இல்லிறப்பான் எய்துமெஞ் ஞான்றும்","விளியாது நிற்கும் பழி"],"p":"He who thinks lightly of going into the wife of another acquires guilt that will abide with him imperishably and for ever.","r":"One who crosses into a home thinking it easy gets blame that never dies.","n":"The contrast is in timescale: 'easy' describes one moment and the blame lasts forever. Parimelazhagar makes it the family's blame, so the cost falls on others too.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["piranil","pazhi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:15"},"k:146":{"book":"kural","num":146,"t":["பகைபாவம் அச்சம் பழியென நான்கும்","இகவாவாம் இல்லிறப்பான் கண்"],"p":"Hatred, sin, fear, disgrace; these four will never leave him who goes in to his neighbour's wife.","r":"Enmity, sin, fear and blame: these four never leave one who crosses into a home.","n":"The four cover different kinds of consequence: social, moral, psychological and reputational. 'Home' throughout this chapter stands for the wife, an equation that the Tamil title itself makes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["piranil","pazhi","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:15"},"k:147":{"book":"kural","num":147,"t":["அறனியலான் இல்வாழ்வான் என்பான் பிறனியலாள்","பெண்மை நயவா தவன்"],"p":"He who desires not the womanhood of her who should walk according to the will of another will be praised as a virtuous house-holder.","r":"The one called a householder in the way of virtue is he who doesn't desire the womanhood of the one who belongs to another's way.","n":"It's a negative definition of the householder, after chapter 5's positive ones. Parimelazhagar makes it exclusive: he alone is such a householder.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","piranil","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:15"},"k:148":{"book":"kural","num":148,"t":["பிறன்மனை நோக்காத பேராண்மை சான்றோர்க்கு","அறனொன்றோ ஆன்ற வொழுக்கு"],"p":"That noble manliness which looks not at the wife of another is the virtue and dignity of the great.","r":"The great manhood that doesn't look at another's wife is, for the worthy, both virtue and complete conduct.","n":"Parimelazhagar's explanation is a fine one: those who can subdue outer enemies can still find this inner enemy hard, so this is the greater manhood. He takes நோக்காத beyond looking, to not holding her in mind.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piranil","adakkam","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:15"},"k:149":{"book":"kural","num":149,"t":["நலக்குரியார் யாரெனின் நாமநீர் வைப்பின்","பிறர்க்குரியாள் தோள்தோயா தார்"],"p":"Is it asked, \"who are those who shall obtain good in this world surrounded by the terror-producing sea ?\" Those who touch not the shoulder of her who belongs to another.","r":"Who deserve good on this world ringed by the awesome sea? Those who don't touch the shoulders of a woman belonging to another.","n":"The question-and-answer form and the immense sea are grand for such a plain answer. தோள் is the vocabulary of love poetry. The commentators differ on நாமநீர், as Parimelazhagar has fearful water and Manakkudavar has water that grants wishes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["piranil","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:15"},"k:150":{"book":"kural","num":150,"t":["அறன்வரையான் அல்ல செயினும் பிறன்வரையாள்","பெண்மை நயவாமை நன்று"],"p":"Though a man perform no virtuous deeds and commit (every) vice, it will be well if he desire not the womanhood of her who is within the limit (of the house) of another.","r":"Even if one doesn't practise virtue and does what is wrong, it's good not to desire the womanhood of one within another's bounds.","n":"The verse sets a minimum for someone who has failed at everything else. Parimelazhagar says this quality alone stands out, and Manakkudavar says that it brings good even with no other virtue. It doesn't say the person is thereby virtuous.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["piranil","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:15"},"k:151":{"book":"kural","num":151,"t":["அகழ்வாரைத் தாங்கும் நிலம்போலத் தம்மை","இகழ்வார்ப் பொறுத்தல் தலை"],"p":"To bear with those who revile us, just as the earth bears up those who dig it, is the first of virtues.","r":"Like the earth that holds up those who dig into it, bearing with those who scorn you is the highest thing.","n":"Parimelazhagar's gloss brings out the image: the earth keeps the digger from falling, so the one injured is still supporting the one who injures. Forbearance here is more than not responding.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["porai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:16"},"k:152":{"book":"kural","num":152,"t":["பொறுத்தல் இறப்பினை என்றும் அதனை","மறத்தல் அதனினும் நன்று"],"p":"Bear with reproach even when you can retaliate; but to forget it will be still better than that.","r":"Always bear with excess against you; forgetting it is better still.","n":"Parimelazhagar explains why forgetting ranks higher: to forbear, one must still hold the wrong in mind. He adds that one should forbear even when one has the power to punish, which is what makes this more than helplessness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["porai","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:16"},"k:153":{"book":"kural","num":153,"t":["இன்நம்யுள் இன்மை விருந்தொரால் வன்மையுள்","வன்மை மடவார்ப் பொறை"],"p":"To neglect hospitality is poverty of poverty. To bear with the ignorant is might of might.","r":"The poverty of poverties is turning away a guest, and the strength of strengths is forbearance toward fools.","n":"The verse is an analogy, as Parimelazhagar notes: just as turning a guest away isn't wealth, bearing with fools isn't weakness. The first half is there to change what we think the second half means.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["porai","virundhu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","விருந்து means a guest, and turning one away is the worst poverty.","வறுமையுள் வைத்து வறுமையாவது விருந்தினரை ஏற்றுக் கொள்ளாது நீக்குதல்;"],["manakkudavar","விருந்து means novelty: the weakest weakness is failing to reject the strange and unheard-of.","புதுமை யென்றது கேட்டறியாதது."]],"ch":"k:16"},"k:154":{"book":"kural","num":154,"t":["நிறையுடைமை நீங்காமை வேண்டின் பொநற்யுடைமை","போற்றி யொழுகப் படும்"],"p":"If you desire that greatness should never leave, you preserve in your conduct the exercise of patience.","r":"If you want நிறை never to leave you, keep and live by forbearance.","n":"Forbearance is presented as the means, and நிறை is what is at stake. Parimelazhagar takes the latter as worth of character, and Manakkudavar as a guarded discipline: keeping what should be kept and rejecting what should be rejected.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["porai","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:16"},"k:155":{"book":"kural","num":155,"t":["ஒறுத்தாரை ஒன்றாக வையாரே வைப்பர்","பொறுத்தாரைப் பொன்போற் பொதிந்து"],"p":"(The wise) will not at all esteem the resentful. They will esteem the patient just as the gold which they lay up with care.","r":"They don't count at all those who retaliate, but those who forbear they keep wrapped up like gold.","n":"It's a claim about social estimation, which is why the motive is reputational. Parimelazhagar gives the reason: the person who retaliates has become the wrongdoer's equal. He reads 'wrapping up' as constantly bearing them in mind.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["porai","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:16"},"k:156":{"book":"kural","num":156,"t":["ஒறுத்தார்க்கு ஒருநாளை இன்பம் பொறுத்தார்க்குப்","பொன்றுந் துணையும் புகழ்"],"p":"The pleasure of the resentful continues for a day. The praise of the patient will continue until (the final destruction of) the world.","r":"Those who retaliate have one day's joy, and those who forbear have renown until they perish.","n":"The verse honestly concedes that revenge is a pleasure. Parimelazhagar plays this down with 'false joy'.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["porai","pugazh","inbam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The word 'world' is supplied, so the renown lasts until the world itself ends.","பொறுத்தார்க்கு உலகம் அழியுமளவும் புகழ் உண்டாம்."],["manakkudavar","The renown lasts as long as the forbearing person lives.","பொறுத்த வர்க்குத் தாம் சாமளவும் புகழுண்டாம்,"]],"ch":"k:16"},"k:157":{"book":"kural","num":157,"t":["திறனல்ல தற்பிறர் செய்யினும் நோநொந்து","அறனல்ல செய்யாமை நன்று"],"p":"Though others inflict injuries on you, yet compassionating the evil (that will come upon them) it will be well not to do them anything contrary to virtue.","r":"Though others do you wrong, it's good to feel the pain of it and not do what is contrary to virtue.","n":"நோநொந்து, grieving at the pain, doesn't say whose pain. On the first reading below, the wronged person pities the wrongdoer, which is a remarkable demand.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["porai","aram","arul"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","One grieves for the suffering that will come to the wrongdoer, who may fall into hell.","அவர்க்கு அதனால் வரும் துன்பத்திற்கு நொந்து,"],["kalaignar","One feels hurt by the base act done to oneself, and still does not take revenge.","பிறர் செய்திடும் இழிவான காரியங்களுக்காகத் துன்பமுற்று வருந்தி;"]],"ch":"k:16"},"k:158":{"book":"kural","num":158,"t":["மிகுதியான் மிக்கவை செய்தாரைத் தாந்தம்","தகுதியான் வென்று விடல்"],"p":"Let a man by patience overcome those who through pride commit excesses.","r":"Those who do wrong out of their excess, defeat them by your own rightness.","n":"The language is of winning. Manakkudavar says it most directly: forbearing isn't defeat, it is victory. மிகுதி and தகுதி, excess and fitness, are set against each other as rival kinds of power.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["porai","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:16"},"k:159":{"book":"kural","num":159,"t":["துறந்தாரின் தூய்மை உடையர் இறந்தார்வாய்","இன்னாச்சொல் நோற்கிற் பவர்"],"p":"Those who bear with the uncourteous speech of the insolent are as pure as the ascetics.","r":"Those who bear the harsh words from the mouths of those who go beyond bounds are as pure as those who have renounced.","n":"Parimelazhagar spells out the implication: this is someone still in household life. The purity of renunciation can therefore be reached without renouncing. Here இறந்தார் means those who overstep, not the dead.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["porai","thuravu","inchol"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:16"},"k:160":{"book":"kural","num":160,"t":["உண்ணாது நோற்பார் பெரியர் பிறர்சொல்லும்","இன்னாச்சொல் நோற்பாரின் பின்"],"p":"Those who endure abstinence from food are great, next to those who endure the uncourteous speech of others.","r":"Those who fast are great, but only after those who bear what others say in harshness.","n":"The verse first grants greatness and then takes the top rank away. Parimelazhagar's reasoning is subtle: bearing insult is done while one's sense of 'I' and 'mine' is still in place, the very things that make it hard.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["porai","thavam","inchol"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:16"},"k:161":{"book":"kural","num":161,"t":["ஒழுக்காறாக் கொள்க ஒருவன்தன் நெஞ்சத்து","அழுக்காறு இலாத இயல்பு"],"p":"Let a man esteem that disposition which is free from envy in the same manner as propriety of conduct.","r":"Let one take as one's rule of conduct a nature whose heart has no envy.","n":"ஒழுக்காறு and அழுக்காறு differ by one sound, so the sound of the verse sets conduct and envy against each other. Coming right after chapter 14, it makes an inner state into a rule of conduct.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhukkaaru","ozhukkam","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:17"},"k:162":{"book":"kural","num":162,"t":["விழுப்பேற்றின் அஃதொப்பது இல்லையார் மாட்டும்","அழுக்காற்றின் அன்மை பெறின்"],"p":"Amongst all attainable excellences there is none equal to that of being free from envy towords others.","r":"Among choice gains nothing equals it, if one can be free of envy toward anyone at all.","n":"யார்மாட்டும், toward anyone at all, is what makes it hard. Parimelazhagar says this includes one's enemies. It's a further 'nothing greater' claim to be read along with those for virtue, restraint and children.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhukkaaru","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:17"},"k:163":{"book":"kural","num":163,"t":["அறன்ஆக்கம் வேண்டாதான் என்பான் பிறனாக்கம்","பேணாது அழுக்கறுப் பான்"],"p":"Of him who instead of rejoicing in the wealth of others, envies it, it will be said \"he neither desires virtue not wealth.\"","r":"He who doesn't care for another's prosperity and envies it is called one who wants neither virtue nor gain.","n":"The verse argues that envy works against what the envier wants. பேணாது goes beyond 'not envying': Parimelazhagar glosses it as failing to help, so what's required is positive support for another's prosperity.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["azhukkaaru","aram","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:17"},"k:164":{"book":"kural","num":164,"t":["அழுக்காற்றின் அல்லவை செய்யார் இழுக்காற்றின்","ஏதம் படுபாக்கு அறிந்து"],"p":"(The wise) knowing the misery that comes from transgression will not through envy commit unrighteous deeds.","r":"They do no wrong out of envy, because they know the harm that arises from a wrong path.","n":"The second line nearly repeats verse 136, and here too the motive is prudence. Parimelazhagar's list of wrongs from envy covers thought, speech and action against those with wealth or learning.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhukkaaru","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:17"},"k:165":{"book":"kural","num":165,"t":["அழுக்காறு உடையார்க்கு அதுசாலும் ஒன்னார்","வழுக்காயும் கேடீன் பது"],"p":"To those who cherish envy that is enough. Though free from enemies that (envy) will bring destruction.","r":"For the envious that's enough: even without enemies, it brings about their ruin.","n":"The verse is sardonic, and அதுசாலும், 'that is enough', sounds almost like a verdict. The envious person's enemy is within, and no outside enemy is needed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["azhukkaaru"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:17"},"k:166":{"book":"kural","num":166,"t":["கொடுப்பது அழுக்கறுப்பான் சுற்றம் உடுப்பதூஉம்","உண்பதூஉம் இன்றிக் கெடும்"],"p":"He who is envious at a gift (made to another) will with his relations utterly perish destitute of food and rainment.","r":"The kin of one who envies what is given will perish, with nothing to wear or eat.","n":"The cost falls on those near him. Parimelazhagar says his own ruin needn't be stated. Manakkudavar makes the fault active, that he stops another's giving, whereas Parimelazhagar makes it the feeling itself. Either way, the verse punishes innocents, and it doesn't address this.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhukkaaru","eegai","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:17"},"k:167":{"book":"kural","num":167,"t":["அவ்வித்து அழுக்காறு உடையானைச் செய்யவள்","தவ்வையைக் காட்டி விடும்"],"p":"Lakshmi envying (the prosperity) of the envious man will depart and introduce him to her sister.","r":"The fair goddess, grudging the envious man, will show him to her elder sister.","n":"The verse describes the goddess's act with the word for envy's own attitude, அவ்வித்து, so the envier gets what he gives. The elder sister is misfortune. Kalaignar distances himself by saying this is how people describe it. Parimelazhagar also records a different reading of the verb.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhukkaaru","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The goddess too can't bear it, and leaves after showing him to her elder sister.","திருமகள் தானும் பொறாது, தன் தவ்வைக்கு காட்டி நீங்கும்."]],"ch":"k:17"},"k:168":{"book":"kural","num":168,"t":["அழுக்காறு எனஒரு பாவி திருச்செற்றுத்","தீயுழி உய்த்து விடும்"],"p":"Envy will destroy (a man's) wealth (in his world) and drive him into the pit of fire (in the world to come.)","r":"The one evil called envy destroys good fortune and drives one to the bad place.","n":"பாவி personifies a quality as a villain. Parimelazhagar acknowledges it's a figure of speech: a quality isn't a person, but this one is so cruel to its maker that it's called one. Kalaignar has 'evil path' where the older commentators have hell.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["azhukkaaru","selvam","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:17"},"k:169":{"book":"kural","num":169,"t":["அவ்விய நெஞ்சத்தான் ஆக்கமும் செவ்வியான்","கேடும் நினைக்கப் படும்"],"p":"The wealth of a man of envious mind and the poverty of the righteous will be pondered.","r":"The prosperity of the crooked-hearted and the ruin of the upright are matters to be pondered.","n":"This verse admits what the next one denies. It doesn't state a resolution, only that it must be thought about. Parimelazhagar cites Silappatikaram 15.91-93 as an example of someone pondering this in the story of Kovalan's suffering, a direct thread between the two works.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhukkaaru","selvam","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","These results can't come from crookedness or uprightness, so the wise ask what past deed caused them.","'இதற்கு ஏது ஆகிய பழவினை யாது?' என்று ஆராயப்படுதலின்,"],["kalaignar","This is simply a matter for astonishment, with no appeal to past deeds.","வியப்புக்குரிய செய்தியாகும்."]],"ch":"k:17"},"k:170":{"book":"kural","num":170,"t":["அழுக்கற்று அகன்றாரும் இல்லை அஃதுஇல்லார்","பெருக்கத்தில் தீர்ந்தாரும் இல்"],"p":"Never have the envious become great; never have those who are free from envy been without greatness.","r":"None have grown great through envy, and none free of it have lost out on increase.","n":"It's an absolute claim, with 'none' twice. Parimelazhagar takes this as the moral cause of gain and ruin, immediately after his past-deeds reading of 169, without explaining how the two fit.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["azhukkaaru","perumai","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:17"},"k:171":{"book":"kural","num":171,"t":["நடுவின்றி நன்பொருள் வெஃகின் குடிபொன்றிக்","குற்றமும் ஆங்கே தரும்"],"p":"If a man departing from equity covet the property (of others), at that very time will his family be destroyed and guilt be incurred.","r":"If one covets another's good things and leaves aside fairness, the family perishes, and faults come at once.","n":"The condition is only வெஃகின், if one covets, and ஆங்கே, right then, makes the penalty immediate. Parimelazhagar gives நடுவு a specific meaning: the recognition that what belongs to others isn't ours to take.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vehkaamai","naduvu","kudi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:18"},"k:172":{"book":"kural","num":172,"t":["படுபயன் வெஃகிப் பழிப்படுவ செய்யார்","நடுவன்மை நாணு பவர்"],"p":"Those who blush at the want of equity will not commit disgraceful acts through desire of the profit that may be gained.","r":"Those ashamed of unfairness won't do blameworthy things out of desire for the gain to be had.","n":"நாணு, shame, is the operative word and it means this person is restrained from inside. Parimelazhagar glosses it with fear, which is a less interior motive than the verse's word.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vehkaamai","naduvu","pazhi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:18"},"k:173":{"book":"kural","num":173,"t":["சிற்றின்பம் வெஃகி அறனல்ல செய்யாரே","மற்றின்பம் வேண்டு பவர்"],"p":"Those who desire the higher pleasures (of heaven) will not act unjustly through desire of the trifling joy. (in this life.)","r":"Those who want the other joy won't do what's contrary to virtue out of desire for a small joy.","n":"சிற்றின்பம் here refers to pleasure from taking others' goods, which isn't its common later meaning of sexual pleasure. The verse leaves 'the other joy' unnamed.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vehkaamai","inbam","aram"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The other joy is the lasting joy that comes from virtue.","அறத்தான் வரும் நிலையுடைய இன்பத்தைக் காதலிப்பவர்."],["manakkudavar","The other joy is the great joy of release.","பேரின்பமாகிய வீடுபேற்றைக் காமிப்பவர்,"],["kalaignar","It's a lasting benefit that one wants in place of an immediate one.","அறவழியில் நிலையான பயனை விரும்புகிறவர்"]],"ch":"k:18"},"k:174":{"book":"kural","num":174,"t":["இலமென்று வெஃகுதல் செய்யார் புலம்வென்ற","புன்மையில் காட்சி யவர்"],"p":"The wise who have conquered their senses and are free from crime, will not covet (the things of others), with the thought \"we are destitute.\"","r":"Those of faultless sight, who have conquered the senses, won't covet at the thought 'we have nothing'.","n":"Need is the hardest case, and the verse rules out that excuse. Parimelazhagar goes a step further and says that such people don't count as poor at all, which removes the case instead of answering it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vehkaamai","adakkam","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:18"},"k:175":{"book":"kural","num":175,"t":["அஃகி அகன்ற அறிவென்னாம் யார்மாட்டும்","வெஃகி வெறிய செயின்"],"p":"What is the advantage of extensive and accurate knowledge if a man through covetousness act senselessly towards all ?","r":"What is the use of fine and wide understanding if, coveting, one acts senselessly toward everyone?","n":"The sound of அஃகி, to be fine, is played against வெஃகி, to covet, and a learned mind is shown undone by the same sound. Parimelazhagar repeats a principle from 140: the purpose of understanding is what one refrains from.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vehkaamai","arivu","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:18"},"k:176":{"book":"kural","num":176,"t":["அருள்வெஃகி ஆற்றின்கண் நின்றான் பொருள்வெஃகிப்","பொல்லாத சூழக் கெடும்"],"p":"If he, who through desire of the virtue of kindness abides in the domestic state i.e., the path in which it may be obtained, covet (the property of others) and think of evil methods (to obtain it), he will perish.","r":"One standing on the path out of desire for grace will perish if, desiring wealth, he plots wrongs.","n":"வெஃகி is used twice: he desires grace and he desires wealth, in the one verb. The other notable word is சூழ, to plot: Parimelazhagar notes that it says the plotting alone destroys him, let alone the doing.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vehkaamai","arul","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The path is household life, called a path because one can't renounce until understanding matures in it.","இல்லற நெறியில் அறிவு முதிர்ந்துழி அல்லது துறக்கப் படாமையின், அதனைத் துறவறத்திற்கு 'ஆறு' என்றார்."],["manakkudavar","The path is simply the way of virtue, and even one with grace can fall.","அருளை விரும்பி யறனெறியிலே நின்றவனும்,"]],"ch":"k:18"},"k:177":{"book":"kural","num":177,"t":["வேண்டற்க வெஃகியாம் ஆக்கம் விளைவயின்","மாண்டற் கரிதாம் பயன்"],"p":"Desire not the gain of covetousness. In the enjoyment of its fruits there is no glory.","r":"Don't seek gain that comes by coveting: when it ripens, its fruit is hard to make good.","n":"The word விளைவயின் brings in a harvest image: the wrong shows only when the crop comes in, so the problem is one of delayed consequences.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vehkaamai","selvam","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:18"},"k:178":{"book":"kural","num":178,"t":["அஃகாமை செல்வத்திற்கு யாதெனின் வெஃகாமை","வேண்டும் பிறன்கைப் பொருள்"],"p":"If it is weighed, \"what is the indestructibility of wealth,\" it is freedom from covetousness.","r":"What keeps wealth from shrinking? Not coveting what's in another's hand, which that person wants.","n":"The chapter's sound play, அஃகாமை and வெஃகாமை, is itself the answer. It offers a security for wealth that the wealthy might not expect: leaving others' goods alone.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vehkaamai","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:18"},"k:179":{"book":"kural","num":179,"t":["அறனறிந்து வெஃகா அறிவுடையார்ச் சேரும்","திறன்அறிந் தாங்கே திரு"],"p":"Lakshmi, knowing the manner (in which she may approach) will immediately come to those wise men who, knowing that it is virtue, covet not the property of others.","r":"Fortune, knowing the way to come, approaches those who understand virtue and do not covet.","n":"Here the goddess has to work out the right way to approach, as if she were the supplicant. Parimelazhagar's gloss explains the 'way' as time, place and the right moment.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["vehkaamai","selvam","aram","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:18"},"k:180":{"book":"kural","num":180,"t":["இறலீனும் எண்ணாது வெஃகின் விறல்ஈனும்","வேண்டாமை என்னுஞ் செருக்கு"],"p":"To covet (the wealth of another) regardless of consequences will bring destruction. That greatness (of mind) which covets not will give victory.","r":"Coveting without thought yields destruction, and the pride called not-wanting yields victory.","n":"செருக்கு normally means arrogance, and here it's a virtue. Parimelazhagar explains why it's a victory: it puts beneath it all those who scramble after wealth. Not wanting is a form of power.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["vehkaamai","perumai","avaa"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:18"},"k:181":{"book":"kural","num":181,"t":["அறங்கூறான் அல்ல செயினும் ஒருவன்","புறங்கூறான் என்றல் இனிது"],"p":"Though one do not even speak of virtue and live in sin, it will be well if it be said of him \"he does not backbite.\"","r":"Though one doesn't speak of virtue and does wrong, it's pleasing if it is said, 'he doesn't backbite'.","n":"The merit here is what's said about a person, which fits the chapter: a backbiter is judged by talk, and this man is praised by it. Virtue need not even be spoken of, which sets a very low floor.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["purangooral","aram","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:19"},"k:182":{"book":"kural","num":182,"t":["அறனழீஇ அல்லவை செய்தலின் தீதே","புறனழீஇப் பொய்த்து நகை"],"p":"To smile deceitfully (in another's presence) after having reviled him to his destruction (behind his back) is a greater evil than the commission of (every other) sin and the destruction of (every) virtue.","r":"Worse than denying virtue and doing wrong is destroying someone behind their back and then smiling falsely.","n":"The ranking is what's striking. Parimelazhagar takes அறனழீஇ as openly saying there's no such thing as virtue, so the moral denier is rated above the smiling gossip. Honest wickedness is treated as less bad than a hidden betrayal.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["purangooral","aram","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:19"},"k:183":{"book":"kural","num":183,"t":["புறங்கூறிப் பொய்த்துயிர் வாழ்தலின் சாதல்","அறங்கூற்றும் ஆக்கத் தரும்"],"p":"Death rather than life will confer upon the deceitful backbiter the profit which (the treatises on) virtue point out.","r":"Dying brings more of the gain that virtue speaks of than living on by backbiting and falsehood.","n":"Death is compared favourably here, and the terms are moral accounting. Parimelazhagar's reason is that death at least stops the backbiting. Manakkudavar pictures a person who refuses to gossip and dies poor, which suggests that gossip may be a livelihood.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["purangooral","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:19"},"k:184":{"book":"kural","num":184,"t":["கண்ணின்று கண்ணறச் சொல்லினும் சொல்லற்க","முன்னின்று பின்நோக்காச் சொல்"],"p":"Though you speak without kindness before another's face speak not in his absence words which regard not the evil subsequently resulting from it.","r":"Though you speak harshly to a person's face, do not speak words that have no regard for what follows, when they are not there.","n":"The verse is a concession, as open harshness is the lesser evil, and it goes against chapter 10's standards. Manakkudavar takes care to add that this isn't permission for harsh speech.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["purangooral","inchol"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The forbidden word disregards the harm that will come later, in the person's absence.","அவன் எதிரின்றிப்பின் வரும் குற்றத்தை நோக்காத சொல்லைச் சொல்லாதொழிக."],["manakkudavar","The forbidden word is one that will leave you unable to look the person in the eye later.","பிற்காலத்து அவன் முன்னே நின்று எதிர்முகம் நோக்க வொண் ணாத சொல்லைச் சொல்லா தொழிக,"]],"ch":"k:19"},"k:185":{"book":"kural","num":185,"t":["அறஞ்சொல்லும் நெஞ்சத்தான் அன்மை புறஞ்சொல்லும்","புன்மையாற் காணப் படும்"],"p":"The emptiness of that man's mind who (merely) praises virtue will be seen from the meanness of reviling another behind his back.","r":"That someone's heart doesn't really speak virtue will be seen from the meanness of their backbiting.","n":"It's a test: talk about virtue is checked against talk about others. Parimelazhagar makes it specific: a backbiter can praise virtue, but because his mind is bad, what he says shouldn't be accepted.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["purangooral","manam","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:19"},"k:186":{"book":"kural","num":186,"t":["பிறன்பழி கூறுவான் தன்பழி யுள்ளும்","திறன்தெரிந்து கூறப் படும்"],"p":"The character of the faults of that man who publishes abroad the faults of others will be sought out and published.","r":"One who talks of another's faults will have his own faults picked out and talked about.","n":"The passive voice doesn't say who will do the talking. Parimelazhagar makes it the injured party, who picks out from the speaker's many faults the ones that would hurt most, and says them to his face.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["purangooral","pazhi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:19"},"k:187":{"book":"kural","num":187,"t":["பகச்சொல்லிக் கேளிர்ப் பிரிப்பர் நகச்சொல்லி","நட்பாடல் தேற்றா தவர்"],"p":"Those who know not to live in friendship with amusing conversation will by back-biting estrange even their relatives.","r":"Those who don't know how to make friends through words that delight will divide even their own people with words that divide.","n":"பகச்சொல்லி and நகச்சொல்லி are one sound apart, like divide and delight. The verse gives a diagnosis, that gossip is a social failure: those who can't please must separate. Parimelazhagar says even kin are affected, and strangers all the more.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["purangooral","natpu","inchol","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:19"},"k:188":{"book":"kural","num":188,"t":["துன்னியார் குற்றமும் தூற்றும் மரபினார்","என்னைகொல் ஏதிலார் மாட்டு"],"p":"What will those not do to strangers whose nature leads them to publish abroad the faults of their intimate friends ?","r":"Those whose way is to spread abroad even the faults of those closest to them, what would they do to strangers?","n":"மரபினார் suggests a settled habit or even a family tradition, not a lapse. Parimelazhagar reports a variant reading, 'what sort of people are they?'","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["purangooral","natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:19"},"k:189":{"book":"kural","num":189,"t":["அறன்நோக்கி ஆற்றுங்கொல் வையம் புறன்நோக்கிப்","புன்சொல் உரைப்பான் பொறை"],"p":"The world through charity supports the weight of those who reproach others observing their absence.","r":"Is it out of regard for virtue that the earth bears the weight of one who watches for a person's back and speaks mean words?","n":"The earth, the model of forbearance, finds this one hard. Parimelazhagar imagines the earth telling itself that bearing this is its duty. Manakkudavar adds darkly that otherwise it would shake him off.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["purangooral","porai","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:19"},"k:190":{"book":"kural","num":190,"t":["ஏதிலார் குற்றம்போல் தங்குற்றங் காண்கிற்பின்","தீதுண்டோ மன்னும் உயிர்க்கு"],"p":"If they observed their own faults as they observe the faults of others, would any evil happen to men ?","r":"If people could see their own faults as they see strangers' faults, what harm would come to any living being?","n":"The whole chapter resolves into a problem of seeing. காண்கிற்பின் means 'if they were able', and Parimelazhagar notes that this impartial comparison is rare.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["purangooral","arivu","samanilai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Seeing the fault ends it, so there's no sin, and thus no suffering in the births to come.","கண்டவழி ஒழிதலின் பாவம் இன்றாம், ஆகவே வரும் பிறவிகளினும் துன்பம் இல்லை என்பது நோக்கி,"],["kalaignar","The habit of backbiting goes and one's life becomes peaceful.","புறங்கூறும் பழக்கமும் போகும்; வாழ்க்கையும் நிம்மதியாக அமையும்."]],"ch":"k:19"},"k:191":{"book":"kural","num":191,"t":["பல்லார் முனியப் பயனில சொல்லுவான்","எல்லாரும் எள்ளப் படும்"],"p":"He who to the disgust of many speaks useless things will be despised by all.","r":"A person who talks emptily to the irritation of many ends up despised by all.","n":"The verse says only 'many'. Parimelazhagar narrows them to the discerning, which turns the move from 'many' to 'all' into a causal chain: once the wise are repelled, everyone else follows.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["sol","payan"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:20"},"k:192":{"book":"kural","num":192,"t":["பயனில பல்லார்முன் சொல்லல் நயனில","நட்டார்கண் செய்தலிற் றீது"],"p":"To speak useless things in the presence of many is a greater evil than to do unkind things towards friends.","r":"Saying useless things before many is worse than doing what is unwelcome to one's friends.","n":"The ranking is startling: a word to an audience outweighs a deed against a friend. Parimelazhagar's explanation is that the talk brings more disgrace, so his measure is the speaker's standing and not the harm done.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sol","payan","nayan"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:20"},"k:193":{"book":"kural","num":193,"t":["நயனிலன் என்பது சொல்லும் பயனில","பாரித் துரைக்கும் உரை"],"p":"That conversation in which a man utters forth useless things will say of him \"he is without virtue.\"","r":"Expansive talk about useless things itself declares that the speaker is one without நயன்.","n":"No one needs to accuse the talker, because the speech testifies against him. நயன் shifts through this chapter: Parimelazhagar takes it as liking in 192 and as justice here, so the word should not be given a single translation.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sol","nayan","payan"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:20"},"k:194":{"book":"kural","num":194,"t":["நயன்சாரா நன்மையின் நீக்கும் பயன்சாராப்","பண்பில்சொல் பல்லா ரகத்து"],"p":"The words devoid of profit or pleasure which a man speaks will, being inconsistent with virtue, remove him from goodness.","r":"Graceless words that serve no purpose, spoken among many, lack நயன் and take a person away from what is good.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sol","nayan","payan"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The words fall short of justice toward the listeners, and cost the speaker his good qualities.","அவை அவர்மாட்டு நீதியோடு படாவாய், அவனை நற்குணங்களின் நீக்கும்."],["manakkudavar","நயன் is even-handedness, and it is the speaker who loses that balance and falls from goodness.","அவன் நடு சாராது நன்மையி னீங்கும்"],["kalaignar","நயன் is the gladness of the gathering, and such words destroy it along with good.","மகிழ்ச்சியைக் குலைத்து, நன்மையை மாய்க்கும்."]],"ch":"k:20"},"k:195":{"book":"kural","num":195,"t":["சீர்மை சிறப்பொடு நீங்கும் பயனில","நீர்மை யுடையார் சொலின்"],"p":"If the good speak vain words their eminence and excellence will leave them.","r":"If those of gentle nature speak useless things, their worth departs and the regard it earned goes with it.","n":"The verse aims at the good, not the habitual babbler of 196. Standing already earned is not secure against this one lapse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sol","payan","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:20"},"k:196":{"book":"kural","num":196,"t":["பயனில் சொல் பராட்டு வானை மகன்எனல்","மக்கட் பதடி யெனல்"],"p":"Call not him a man who parades forth his empty words. Call him the chaff of men.","r":"Don't call a person who keeps up useless talk a man; call him chaff among people.","n":"For Parimelazhagar the image means a husk without the kernel of understanding, so the talk reveals emptiness and doesn't merely offend.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["sol","payan","sirumai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","பாராட்டுவான் is one who says such things repeatedly: the fault is the habit.","பயன் இல்லாத சொற்களைப் பலகாலுஞ் சொல்லுவானை"],["manakkudavar","He is one who prizes and celebrates useless speech: the fault is the relish.","பயனில்லாத சொல்லைக் கொண்டாடுவானை"],["kalaignar","He is one who hopes to profit from empty talk: the fault is the motive.","பயனற்றவைகளைச் சொல்லிப் பயன்பெற நினைப்பவனை"]],"ch":"k:20"},"k:197":{"book":"kural","num":197,"t":["நயனில சொல்லினுஞ் சொல்லுக சான்றோர்","பயனில சொல்லாமை நன்று"],"p":"Let the wise if they will, speak things without excellence; it will be well for them not to speak useless things.","r":"Let the worthy say what lacks நயன் if they must; it is good that they not say what lacks use.","n":"The concession is rhetorical, as Parimelazhagar observes: 'even if they say it' implies they should not. Its force is the comparison, which keeps the sting whichever sense of நயன் is taken.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sol","nayan","payan"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","நயன் is justice, so even speech at odds with justice is ranked less bad than useless speech.","நயன் இலவற்றினும் பயன் இல தீய என்பதாம்."],["kalaignar","நயன் is pleasantness, so the worthy may say what is unsweet, which is a much milder claim.","பண்பாளர்கள், இனிமையல்லாத சொற்களைக்கூடச் சொல்லி விடலாம்;"]],"ch":"k:20"},"k:198":{"book":"kural","num":198,"t":["அரும்பயன் ஆயும் அறிவினார் சொல்லார்","பெரும்பயன் இல்லாத சொல்"],"p":"The wise who seek after rare pleasures will not speak words that have not much weight in them.","r":"Those whose understanding searches out rare ends do not say what lacks great use.","n":"The bar rises here from useless to not greatly useful, and Parimelazhagar draws this out: words of small use are excluded too. His identification of the rare ends with release and higher destinies is his own; Manakkudavar says only 'rare matters'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","sol","payan"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The rare ends are release and passage to higher worlds.","அறிதற்கு அரிய பயன்களாவன: வீடு பேறும் மேற்கதிச் செலவும் முதலாயின."]],"ch":"k:20"},"k:199":{"book":"kural","num":199,"t":["பொருள்தீர்ந்த பொச்சாந்துஞ் சொல்லார் மருள்தீர்ந்த","மாசறு காட்சி யவர்"],"p":"Those wise men who are without faults and are freed from ignorance will not even forgetfully speak things that profit not.","r":"Those of stainless sight, freed from delusion, do not say meaningless things even by a lapse.","n":"பொச்சாந்தும் moves the claim from choice to nature: for such people the restraint survives inattention.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","sol"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:20"},"k:200":{"book":"kural","num":200,"t":["சொல்லுக சொல்லிற் பயனுடைய சொல்லற்க","சொல்லிற் பயனிலாச் சொல்"],"p":"Speak what is useful, and speak not useless words.","r":"Say what among words has use; do not say what among words has none.","n":"Parimelazhagar notes that சொல்லில் is grammatically surplus and kept for the figure of repeated words. It may be a quiet wit that a chapter against waste in speech ends on a deliberate surplus, though that is my reading and no commentator's.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sol","payan"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","சொல்லில் means 'among words': the verse sorts words into two kinds.","சொற்களில் பயன் உடைய சொற்களைச் சொல்லுக"],["manakkudavar","The first சொல்லில் means 'if you speak', which leaves silence as an option.","சொல்லுவனாயின் பயனுடைய சொற்களைச் சொல்லுக"]],"ch":"k:20"},"k:201":{"book":"kural","num":201,"t":["தீவினையார் அஞ்சார் விழுமியார் அஞ்சுவர்","தீவினை என்னும் செருக்கு"],"p":"Those who have experience of evil deeds will not fear, but the excellent will fear the pride of sin.","r":"Those given to evil deeds don't fear the intoxication called evil deed; the excellent do.","n":"Fear is the sign of moral standing here, not a weakness. Parimelazhagar adds an insight about habit: long practice has removed the fear in one group, and never having done such things keeps it alive in the other.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["acham","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","செருக்கு is delusion: the evil deed is called by the name of the confusion it brings on.","தீவினை என்று சொல்லப்படும் மயக்கத்தை"],["manakkudavar","செருக்கு is the elation of wrongdoing.","தீவினை யாகிய களிப்பை"],["kalaignar","செருக்கு is a pleasure the deed may yield, and the wise fear the deed even so.","தீவினையால் மகிழ்ச்சி ஏற்படுவதாயினும்"]],"ch":"k:21"},"k:202":{"book":"kural","num":202,"t":["தீயவை தீய பயத்தலால் தீயவை","தீயினும் அஞ்சப் படும்"],"p":"Because evil produces evil, therefore should evil be feared more than fire.","r":"Since evil deeds yield evil, they are to be feared more than fire.","n":"The verse's reason is only that evil begets evil, sounded in the play of தீயவை on தீ. Parimelazhagar supplies a further reason, that fire does not burn across time, place and bodies, which brings in rebirth that the verse doesn't state.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["acham","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Fire can't burn in another time, another place or another body, and an evil deed can.","பிறிதொரு காலத்தும், பிறிதொரு தேயத்தும், பிறிதோர் உடம்பினும் சென்று சுடுதல் தீக்கு இன்மையின்"]],"ch":"k:21"},"k:203":{"book":"kural","num":203,"t":["அறிவினுள் எல்லாந் தலையென்ப தீய","செறுவார்க்கும் செய்யா விடல்"],"p":"To do no evil to enemies will be called the chief of all virtues.","r":"They say the chief of all understanding is to leave off doing harm even to those who hate you.","n":"The word is அறிவு, not virtue as Pope's gloss has it. Forbearance appears here as the height of intelligence, and Parimelazhagar makes the logic prudential: refraining keeps suffering from coming back to oneself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","vinai","acham"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It is the chief understanding because one infers that refraining, even when able to strike, keeps suffering away.","செய்யத் தக்குழியுஞ் செய்யாது ஒழியவே தமக்குத் துன்பம் வாராது என உய்த்துணர்தலின்"]],"ch":"k:21"},"k:204":{"book":"kural","num":204,"t":["மறந்தும் பிறன்கேடு சூழற்க சூழின்","அறஞ்சூழம் சூழ்ந்தவன் கேடு"],"p":"Even though forgetfulness meditate not the ruin of another. Virtue will meditate the ruin of him who thus meditates.","r":"Don't plot another's ruin even by a lapse; if you do, அறம் plots the ruin of the plotter.","n":"The same verb, சூழ், serves both parties, so the plotting already sets the reply in motion and harm need never be carried out. Parimelazhagar draws the point: even thinking of it is forbidden.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","அறம் is a deity of virtue, and its 'plotting' is resolving to leave the man to his ruin.","அறக்கடவுள் எண்ணுதலாவது, அவன் கெடத் தான் நீங்க நினைத்தல்."],["manakkudavar","அறம் actively returns harm for harm, and gets in before the intended victim can respond.","தீமை செய்தார்க்குத் தீமை பயக்கும் அறந்தானே சூழும்"],["kalaignar","சூழும் means surrounds: moral order closes in on him like a siege.","அவனுக்குக் கேடு உண்டாக்க அவனை அறம் முற்றுகையிட்டு விடும்."]],"ch":"k:21"},"k:205":{"book":"kural","num":205,"t":["இலன் என்று தீயவை செய்யற்க செய்யின்","இலனாகும் மற்றும் பெயர்த்து"],"p":"Commit not evil, saying, \"I am poor\": if you do, you will become poorer still.","r":"Don't do evil saying 'I have nothing'; if you do, you'll have nothing all over again.","n":"The verse answers the strongest excuse for wrongdoing on its own terms: it doesn't defend the poor man's honour but says the plan won't work. Parimelazhagar also reports other glossators who take the second poverty as poverty of good qualities.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The poverty returns across lives, birth after birth.","அத் தீவினையால் பிறவிதோறும் இலன் ஆம் என்பதாம்."],["manakkudavar","The deed simply fails to produce wealth, with no appeal to rebirth.","பின்பும் நல்குரவினனாவன்; அது செல் வம் பயவாது"]],"ch":"k:21"},"k:206":{"book":"kural","num":206,"t":["தீப்பால தான்பிறர்கண் செய்யற்க நோய்ப்பால","தன்னை அடல்வேண்டா தான்"],"p":"Let him not do evil to others who desires not that sorrows should pursue him.","r":"One who doesn't want afflicting things to overpower him should not do evil things to others.","n":"The balance of தீப்பால and நோய்ப்பால gives the rule its shape: what one portions out as evil returns as a portion of pain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:21"},"k:207":{"book":"kural","num":207,"t":["எனைப்பகை யுற்றாரும் உய்வர் வினைப்பகை","வீயாது பின்சென்று அடும்"],"p":"However great be the enmity men have incurred they may still live. The enmity of sin will incessantly pursue and kill.","r":"However great the enmity they've incurred, people survive it; the enmity of deeds doesn't die and follows them to kill.","n":"The deed is made an enemy of a special kind, one that can't be outrun because it travels with the doer. Both Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar gloss it as entering wherever one enters.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:21"},"k:208":{"book":"kural","num":208,"t":["தீயவை செய்தார் கெடுதல் நிழல்தன்னை","வீயாது அஇஉறைந் தற்று"],"p":"Destruction will dwell at the heels of those who commit evil even as their shadow that leaves them not.","r":"The ruin of those who do evil is like a person's shadow, which never perishes and stays at the foot.","n":"Parimelazhagar sees a shadow that stretches far but comes back to rest at the foot, so a consequence can seem distant and still be right there. He expressly rejects another reading, in which the shadow stands for a deed latent until its time.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The image is the shadow's return to the foot, not a deed lying hidden until its season.","ஒருவன் நிழல் நொடிதாகப் போயும், அவன்றன்னை விடாது வந்து அடியின்கண் தங்கி தன்மைத்து."]],"ch":"k:21"},"k:209":{"book":"kural","num":209,"t":["தன்னைத்தான் காதல னாயின் எனைத்தொன்றும்","துன்னற்க தீவினைப் பால்"],"p":"If a man love himself, let him not commit any sin however small.","r":"If a person loves himself, let him not go near the portion of evil deeds, however small.","n":"The appeal rests on self-love, using காதலன், a word of real attachment. The verse doesn't ask a person to overcome self-regard: it asks him to think it through, and on Parimelazhagar's reading harm to others is harm to oneself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","acham"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:21"},"k:210":{"book":"kural","num":210,"t":["அருங்கேடன் என்பது அறிக மருங்கோடித்","தீவினை செய்யான் எனின்"],"p":"Know ye that he is freed from destruction who commits no evil, going to neither side of the right path.","r":"Know that one who doesn't stray aside and do evil is a person for whom ruin is rare.","n":"அருங்கேடன் on its face says ruin is 'rare' or 'hard'. Parimelazhagar argues that அருமை here means absence, which turns a strong probability into a guarantee. Kalaignar also says no ruin at all.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","aram"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","அருமை means absence, so the man has no ruin at all.","அருமை:இன்மை."]],"ch":"k:21"},"k:211":{"book":"kural","num":211,"t":["கைம்மாறு வேண்டா கடப்பாடு மாரிமாட்டு","என் ஆற்றுங் கொல்லோ உலகு"],"p":"Benevolence seeks not a return. What does the world give back to the clouds ?","r":"Obligation seeks no return: what does the world do for the rain in exchange?","n":"The word chosen is கடப்பாடு, what is owed, and not gift. Parimelazhagar points out that the name itself implies it can't be dropped. Giving with no return is therefore framed as debt, a nicely paradoxical idea.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oppuravu","payan"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:22"},"k:212":{"book":"kural","num":212,"t":["தாளாற்றித் தந்த பொருளெல்லாம் தக்கார்க்கு","வேளாண்மை செய்தற் பொருட்டு"],"p":"All the wealth acquired with perseverance by the worthy is for the exercise of benevolence.","r":"All the wealth got through exertion exists for doing வேளாண்மை, with தக்கார் standing in some relation to it.","n":"The grammar of தக்கார்க்கு decides whether this sets a limit on recipients or describes the owner, so I have left it open in the reading.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oppuravu","selvam","muyarchi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","தக்கார் are the owners: for those who are worthy, all wealth is for benevolence, not for private consumption or hoarding.","தக்கார்க்கு தகுதி உடையார்க்கு ஆயின்;"],["manakkudavar","தக்கார் are the recipients: the wealth is for helping those who deserve it.","ஒருவன் முயன்று ஈட்டிய பொருளெல்லாம் தகுதியுடையார்க்கு உபகரித்தற்காகவாம்"]],"ch":"k:22"},"k:213":{"book":"kural","num":213,"t":["புத்தே ளுலகத்தும் ஈண்டும் பெறலரிதே","ஒப்புரவின் நல்ல பிற"],"p":"It is difficult to obtain another good equal to benevolence either in this world or in that of the gods.","r":"Whether in the world of புத்தேள் or here, it is hard to find any other thing as good as ஒப்புரவு.","n":"Parimelazhagar offers a striking reason for the gods' world: all there are alike, with no giver and no receiver, so this good can't exist there. On that view the human condition of need is what makes the highest good possible.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oppuravu","aram"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","புத்தேள் உலகு is the world of the gods.","தேவர் உலகத்தும் இவ்வுலகத்தும்"],["kalaignar","It is a new world yet to come, which removes the gods from the verse.","இன்றைய உலகிலும், இனிவரும் புதிய உலகிலும் காண்பது அரிது."]],"ch":"k:22"},"k:214":{"book":"kural","num":214,"t":["ஒத்த தறவோன் உயிர்வாழ்வான் மற்றையான்","செத்தாருள் வைக்கப் படும்"],"p":"He truly lives who knows (and discharges) the proper duties (of benevolence). He who knows the m not will be reckoned among the dead.","r":"One who knows what is fitting truly lives; anyone else is counted among the dead.","n":"Knowing here includes doing: Parimelazhagar glosses it as knowing and acting, because the dead neither know nor act.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["oppuravu","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","What is fitting is the way of the world as a whole, and lapses in it can't be repaired as lapses in Vedic practice can.","உலகநடை வழு வேதநடை வழுப்போலத் தீர்திறன் உடைத்து அன்று"],["manakkudavar","What is fitting is specifically ஒப்புரவு, and those who lack it are like corpses.","ஒப்புரவறியாதார் பிணத்தோ டொப்ப ரென்றது."]],"ch":"k:22"},"k:215":{"book":"kural","num":215,"t":["ஊருணி நீர்நிறைந் தற்றே உலகவாம்","பேரறி வாளன் திரு"],"p":"The wealth of that man of eminent knowledge who desires to exercise the benevolence approved of by the world, is like the full waters of a city-tank.","r":"The wealth of a greatly wise person connected with the world's desiring is like water filling the village's drinking tank.","n":"The image puts the wealth in a common place, and the owner disappears from the picture: people simply draw from it. This verse calls such a person பேரறிவாளன, wise, not merely generous.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oppuravu","selvam","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","This person desires and practises the world's way.","உலகநடையை விரும்பிச் செய்யும் பெரிய அறிவினை யுடையவனது செல்வம்"],["manakkudavar","This person is the one desired by all the world.","உலகத்தா ரெல்லாராலும் நச்சப்படுகின்ற"]],"ch":"k:22"},"k:216":{"book":"kural","num":216,"t":["பயன்மரம் உள்ளூர்ப் பழுத்தற்றால் செல்வம்","நயனுடை யான்கண் படின்"],"p":"The wealth of a man (possessed of the virtue) of benevolence is like the ripening of a fruitful tree in the midst of a town.","r":"Wealth falling to one who has நயன் is like a fruit-giving tree ripening in the heart of the town.","n":"Parimelazhagar says that நயன் here is ஒப்புரவு as the chief of worldly justice, Manakkudavar makes it being loved by others, and Kalaignar a tender heart. The tree's public position matters as much as its fruit, since no one has to go looking for it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oppuravu","selvam","nayan"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:22"},"k:217":{"book":"kural","num":217,"t":["மருந்தாகித் தப்பா மரத்தற்றால் செல்வம்","பெருந்தகை யான்கண் படின்"],"p":"If wealth be in the possession of a man who has the great excellence (of benevolence), it is like a tree which as a medicine is an infallible cure for disease.","r":"Wealth falling to one of great nobility is like a tree that is medicine and never fails.","n":"This goes further than the fruit tree of 216. Parimelazhagar has every part of the tree serving as cure, with no regard for its own lack, which suggests giving that costs the giver something. He and Manakkudavar agree that தப்பா includes never being hidden from those who seek it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oppuravu","selvam","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:22"},"k:218":{"book":"kural","num":218,"t":["இடனில் பருவத்தும் ஒப்புரவிற்கு ஒல்கார்","கடனறி காட்சி யவர்"],"p":"The wise who know what is duty will not scant their benevolence even when they are without wealth.","r":"Even at a time without means, those whose sight knows what is due don't falter in ஒப்புரவு.","n":"The verse cuts ஒப்புரவு free from wealth. If it were a by-product of surplus it would lapse with the surplus.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oppuravu","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:22"},"k:219":{"book":"kural","num":219,"t":["நயனுடையான் நல்கூர்ந்தா னாதல் செயும்நீர","செய்யாது அமைகலா வாறு"],"p":"The poverty of a benevolent man, is nothing but his inability to exercise the same.","r":"Poverty, for one with நயன், is just the condition of being unable to rest while what should be done goes undone.","n":"Poverty is redefined from the inside. Parimelazhagar makes the contrast explicit: it isn't going without one's own enjoyments. The deprivation is of the power to give.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oppuravu","nayan","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:22"},"k:220":{"book":"kural","num":220,"t":["ஒப்புரவி னால்வரும் கேடெனின் அஃதொருவன்","விற்றுக்கோள் தக்க துடைத்து"],"p":"If it be said that loss will result from benevolence, such loss is worth being procured even by the sale of one's self.","r":"If ruin is said to come through ஒப்புரவு, that ruin is worth acquiring even by selling oneself.","n":"The verse deals with an objection: 'if it is said'. For Parimelazhagar nothing is normally worth selling oneself for, so this is meant to startle, and the reason he gives is the fame such loss yields.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["oppuravu","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Selling oneself is meant literally as the limit case: the loss is worth buying even at that price.","அக்கேடு தன்னை விற்றாயினும் கொள்ளும் தகுதியை உடைத்து."],["manakkudavar","It is a commercial image, selling one thing to obtain another, so the 'loss' is really a trade for later benefit.","ஒன்றை விற்று ஒன்றைக் கொள்ளுகின்ற வாணிகமாகக் கொள்ளுந் தகுதி புடைத்து"]],"ch":"k:22"},"k:221":{"book":"kural","num":221,"t":["வறியார்க்கொன்று ஈவதே ஈகைமற் றெல்லாம்","குறியெதிர்ப்பை நீர துடைத்து"],"p":"To give to the destitute is true charity. All other gifts have the nature of (what is done for) a measured return.","r":"Giving something to those who have nothing is what giving is; all the rest has the nature of a measured loan.","n":"குறியெதிர்ப்பு is a technical word: taking a stated measure and returning that same measure, as Parimelazhagar glosses it. All gifts among equals are thus reclassified, however generous they feel.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:23"},"k:222":{"book":"kural","num":222,"t":["நல்லாறு எனினும் கொளல்தீது மேலுலகம்","இல்லெனினும் ஈதலே நன்று"],"p":"To beg is evil, even though it were said that it is a good path (to heaven). To give is good, even though it were said that those who do so cannot obtain heaven.","r":"Even if it were called a good path, taking is bad; even if there were no higher world, giving alone is good.","n":"The verse cuts the value of giving loose from its reward by a hypothetical. How strong that hypothetical is, is exactly what the commentators disagree about.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","aram"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Both 'even ifs' are purely hypothetical: no one actually says such things, and heaven isn't in doubt.","'எனினும்' என்பது இரு வழியும் அங்ஙனம் கூறுவார் இன்மை விளக்கி நின்றது."],["kalaignar","Giving won't obtain any higher world, and the giving life is best regardless.","கொடை வழங்குவதால் மேலுலகம் என்று சொல்லப்படுவது கிட்டிவிடப் போவதில்லை;"]],"ch":"k:23"},"k:223":{"book":"kural","num":223,"t":["இலனென்னும் எவ்வம் உரையாமை ஈதல்","குலனுடையான் கண்ணே யுள"],"p":"(Even in a low state) not to adopt the mean expedient of saying \"I have nothing,\" but to give, is the characteristic of the mad of noble birth.","r":"Not uttering the misery of 'I have nothing', and giving, are found only in one of good family.","n":"Parimelazhagar lists several rival interpretations, including giving before the asker has to speak, and giving so that he need not go on to ask anyone else.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","kudi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","These are two virtues: not begging oneself, and not refusing those who beg.","யான் வறியன் என்று இரப்பான் சொல்லும் இளிவரவைத் தான் பிறர்கண் சொல்லாமையும்"],["manakkudavar","It is one act: giving without telling the suppliant 'I have nothing'.","இரந்து வந்தார்க்கு இல னென்னாகின்ற துன்பத்தைக் கூறாது ஈதலும்"],["kalaignar","It means giving without showing the distress of one's own poverty.","தமக்குள்ள வறுமைத் துன்பத்தைக் காட்டிக்கொள்ளாமல் பிறருக்கு ஈவது"]],"ch":"k:23"},"k:224":{"book":"kural","num":224,"t":["இன்னாது இரக்கப் படுதல் இரந்தவர்","இன்முகங் காணும் அளவு"],"p":"To see men begging from us in disagreeable, until we see their pleasant countenance.","r":"Being begged from is itself painful, until one sees the glad face of the one who begged.","n":"A candid observation: being asked is uncomfortable even for the generous. Parimelazhagar attributes this to fear of not being able to give all that is sought.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","இரக்கப்படுதல் is being the one asked, and resolving to give.","இரக்கப்படுதல்-'இரப்பார்க்கு ஈவல்' என்று இருத்தல்."],["kalaignar","It is feeling pity (இரக்கம்) for the supplicant, a different root.","அவருக்காக இரக்கப்படுவதும் ஒரு துன்பமாகவே தோன்றும்."]],"ch":"k:23"},"k:225":{"book":"kural","num":225,"t":["ஆற்றுவார் ஆற்றல் பசிஆற்றல் அப்பசியை","மாற்றுவார் ஆற்றலின் பின்"],"p":"The power of those who perform penance is the power of enduring hunger. It is inferior to the power of those who remove the hunger (of others).","r":"The strength of the strong is enduring hunger, and that ranks behind the strength of those who remove hunger.","n":"The verse says only ஆற்றுவார், those with strength. Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar both understand ascetics, which makes this a direct ranking of the giver over the renunciant. Parimelazhagar's reason is that the ascetic hungers and can't relieve others.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:23"},"k:226":{"book":"kural","num":226,"t":["அற்றார் அழிபசி தீர்த்தல் அஃதொருவன்","பெற்றான் பொருள்வைப் புழி"],"p":"The removal of the killing hunger of the poor is the place for one to lay up his wealth.","r":"Removing the ruinous hunger of the destitute is where a person who has come into wealth should store it.","n":"அழிபசி is hunger that destroys: Parimelazhagar says all goods, Manakkudavar says a person's qualities. The financial image is made pointed by Parimelazhagar: the wealth comes back to help the giver. He supplies 'for virtue's sake' himself, so the verse is plainer about the bank than he is.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:23"},"k:227":{"book":"kural","num":227,"t":["பாத்தூண் மரீஇ யவனைப் பசியென்னும்","தீப்பிணி தீண்டல் அரிது"],"p":"The fiery disease of hunger shall never touch him who habitually distributes his food to others.","r":"The evil disease called hunger is unlikely to touch one who is habituated to sharing his food.","n":"Manakkudavar reads this as a reply to the miser's fear that giving will lead to hunger. Understood that way, the verse is a piece of argument rather than a promise. மரீஇ makes the claim about habit, not about any one gift.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","selvam"],"pos":[["manakkudavar","It answers the fear that giving will reduce wealth and bring on hunger.","பகுத்துண்ணப் பசி வாராதென்று கூறிற்று."]],"ch":"k:23"},"k:228":{"book":"kural","num":228,"t":["ஈத்துவக்கும் இன்பம் அறியார்கொல் தாமுடைமை","வைத்திழக்கும் வன்க ணவர்"],"p":"Do the hard-eyed who lay up and lose their possessions not know the happiness which springs from the pleasure of giving ?","r":"Do those hard-eyed people who hoard what they have and then lose it not know the joy of giving and gladdening?","n":"The appeal is to pleasure, not to duty: the hoarder has simply missed out. 'Keep and lose' in one word, வைத்திழக்கும், makes the loss seem part of keeping. Parimelazhagar's own wording suggests the joy belongs to those with அருள், so this reaches forward to chapter 25.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:23"},"k:229":{"book":"kural","num":229,"t":["இரத்தலின் இன்னாது மன்ற நிரப்பிய","தாமே தமியர் உணல்"],"p":"Solitary and unshared eating for the sake of filling up one's own riches is certainly much more unpleasant than begging.","r":"Eating alone to fill up one's own store is surely more bitter than begging.","n":"Parimelazhagar's comparison: begging has only a moment's humiliation and no lasting poverty, while the solitary eater has both. The verse takes the worst thing most people can think of and names something worse.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","நிரப்பிய refers to filling a fixed hoard target, so the fault is accumulation.","ஒரோ எண்களைக்குறித்து அத்துணை ஈட்டுதும் என ஈட்டத்தையே மேற்கொண்டு இவறக் கூட்டுதல்."],["manakkudavar","It refers to the food one has obtained, and தமியர் to eating out of everyone's sight.","தேடின வுணவைத தாமே தமியராயிருந் துண்டல்"]],"ch":"k:23"},"k:230":{"book":"kural","num":230,"t":["சாதலின் இன்னாத தில்லை இனிததூஉம்","ஈதல் இயையாக் கடை"],"p":"Nothing is more unpleasant than death: yet even that is pleasant where charity cannot be exercised.","r":"Nothing is more bitter than dying, yet even that is sweet when giving is out of reach.","n":"The chapter's last ranking sets giving above life. Parimelazhagar's reason is that it frees one from a bodily burden no use to anyone. This is hyperbole about value, not advice to die, and Kalaignar softens it to a comparison of pains.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:23"},"k:231":{"book":"kural","num":231,"t":["ஈதல் இசைபட வாழ்தல் அதுவல்லது","ஊதியம் இல்லை உயிர்க்கு"],"p":"Give to the poor and live with praise. There is no greater profit to man than that.","r":"Give, and live so that renown arises: there is no other profit for a living being.","n":"ஊதியம is a word for profit, so the verse puts its ethics in the language of a balance sheet. Parimelazhagar notes that learning and valour can also win fame, and that giving is named as the highest route to it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pugazh","eegai","payan"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:24"},"k:232":{"book":"kural","num":232,"t":["உரைப்பார் உரைப்பவை எல்லாம் இரப்பார்க்கொன்று","ஈவார்மேல் நிற்கும் புகழ்"],"p":"Whatsoever is spoken in the world will abide as praise upon that man who gives alms to the poor.","r":"All that speakers have to say amounts to praise resting on those who give something to those who ask.","n":"Parimelazhagar extends speech to poets' song, and he dismisses a reading that lets others have praise too ('say what you will, fame stays with the giver'), because it misses fame's excellence. The verse's sweep ('all that speakers say') is a large claim, even as hyperbole.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:24"},"k:233":{"book":"kural","num":233,"t":["ஒன்றா உலகத்து உயர்ந்த புகழல்லால்","பொன்றாது நிற்பதொன் றில்"],"p":"There is nothing that stands forth in the world imperishable, except fame, exalted in solitary greatness.","r":"Apart from towering, unmatched renown, there is nothing in the world that stands undying.","n":"The verse doesn't say what makes fame unmatched. Parimelazhagar specifies gifts that are hardest to give, of life, limb and property, which raises the bar far above ordinary repute.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:24"},"k:234":{"book":"kural","num":234,"t":["நிலவரை நீள்புகழ் ஆற்றின் புலவரைப்","போற்றாது புத்தேள் உலகு"],"p":"If one has acquired extensive fame within the limits of this earth, the world of the Gods will no longer praise those sages who have attained that world.","r":"If someone achieves far-reaching renown within the earth's bounds, the world of the gods doesn't honour the புலவர்.","n":"The verse is elliptical: it doesn't say whom heaven honours in their place, and everything hinges on the identity of புலவர். I'm not confident any reading here is the verse's own. Parimelazhagar cites Purananuru 27 for the idea that the famous gain both worlds.","x":1,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["pugazh"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","புலவர் are sages who have reached that world, and it prefers the famous to them.","புத்தேள் உலகம் அவனையல்லது தன்னை எய்தி நின்ற ஞானிகளைப் பேணாது."],["manakkudavar","புலவர் are the gods themselves, so named because they have wisdom.","புலவரென்றார் தேவரை; அவர் புலனுடையாராதலான்."],["kalaignar","புலவர் are the learned and the world is a future one: it will not honour intellect alone over the selfless and famous.","அறிவாற்றல் உடையவரை மட்டும் போற்றிக் கொண்டிராது."]],"ch":"k:24"},"k:235":{"book":"kural","num":235,"t":["நத்தம்போல் கேடும் உளதாகும் சாக்காடும்","வித்தகர்க் கல்லால் அரிது"],"p":"Prosperity to the body of fame, resulting in poverty to the body of flesh and the stability to the former arising from the death of the latter, are achievable only by the wise.","r":"A ruin that is really growth and a death that is really existence are hard to come by except for the skilled.","n":"வித்தகர் praises skill, not goodness: for Parimelazhagar, the art of turning what doesn't last into what does. The reading depends on his derivation of நத்தம் from நந்து, growth, and on taking போல் as a filler, so its foundation is a philological argument.","x":1,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It is a doctrine of two bodies: the elemental body grows poor and dies while the body of fame grows rich and stands.","ஆக்கமாகும் கேடாவது, புகழ் உடம்பு செல்வம் எய்தப் பூத உடம்பு நல்கூர்தல்."],["kalaignar","It speaks of strength of character that grows fame amid hardship and establishes it even in dying, with no second body.","துன்பங்களுக்கிடையேகூட அவற்றைத் தாங்கும் வலிமையால் தமது புகழை வளர்த்துக் கொள்வதும்,"]],"ch":"k:24"},"k:236":{"book":"kural","num":236,"t":["தோன்றின் புகழொடு தோன்றுக அஃதிலார்","தோன்றலின் தோன்றாமை நன்று"],"p":"If you are born (in this world), be born with qualities conductive to fame. From those who are destitute of them it will be better not to be born.","r":"If you appear, appear with renown; for those without it, not appearing is better than appearing.","n":"Taken as birth, a command about how to be born can't be obeyed. Each commentator has to resolve that oddity, and how they do it shapes their positions.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","pirappu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It means being born human, and for those lacking the qualities that lead to fame, an animal birth is better, since no one reviles animals.","அக்குணம் இல்லாதார் மக்களாய்ப் பிறத்தலின் விலங்காய்ப் பிறத்தல் நன்று."],["manakkudavar","It means plainly that not being born is better.","அஃதிலார் பிறக்குமதிற் பிறவாமை நன்று"],["kalaignar","தோன்று means entering a field of activity: excel there, or stay out of it.","எந்தத் துறையில் ஈடுபட்டாலும் அதில் புகழுடன் விளங்கவேண்டும்;"]],"ch":"k:24"},"k:237":{"book":"kural","num":237,"t":["புகழ்பட வாழாதார் தந்நோவார் தம்மை","இகழ்வாரை நோவது எவன்?"],"p":"Why do those who cannot live with praise, grieve those who despise them, instead of grieving themselves for their own inability.","r":"Those who cannot live with renown: why do they resent the people who scorn them, instead of resenting themselves?","n":"The scorn is accepted as a fair verdict. Parimelazhagar's reasoning is that a good name was within reach, so the failure is one's own.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:24"},"k:238":{"book":"kural","num":238,"t":["வசையென்ப வையத்தார்க் கெல்லாம் இசையென்னும்","எச்சம் பெறாஅ விடின்"],"p":"Not to beget fame will be esteemed a disgrace by the wise in this world.","r":"They say it is a disgrace for everyone in the world not to obtain the legacy called renown.","n":"எச்சம் is what remains after one dies, and it also means offspring, which Pope's 'childless' seizes on. Manakkudavar and Parimelazhagar make a severe point: the lack of fame needs no other fault to justify disgrace.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:24"},"k:239":{"book":"kural","num":239,"t":["வசையிலா வண்பயன் குன்றும் இசையிலா","யாக்கை பொறுத்த நிலம்"],"p":"The ground which supports a body without fame will diminish in its rich produce.","r":"Land that bears a body without renown will decline in its blameless rich yield.","n":"யாக்கை, 'body', is pointed: for Parimelazhagar, what lacks renown is merely a body, a load the land must carry. Moral failure has an effect on the soil here, which Kalaignar weakens to how the land is regarded.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:24"},"k:240":{"book":"kural","num":240,"t":["வசையொழிய வாழ்வாரே வாழ்வார் இசையொழிய","வாழ்வாரே வாழா தவர்"],"p":"Those live who live without disgrace. Those who live without fame live not.","r":"Those who live free of blame are the ones who live; those who live free of renown are the ones who don't.","n":"The balanced form leaves no neutral ground, and Parimelazhagar states it outright: to lack blame is to have renown and to lack renown is to have blame. A quiet, harmless, obscure life has no place in this verse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:24"},"k:241":{"book":"kural","num":241,"t":["அருட்செல்வம் செல்வத்துள் செல்வம் பொருட்செல்வம்","பூரியார் கண்ணும் உள"],"p":"The wealth of kindness is wealth of wealth, in as much as the wealth of property is possessed by the basest of men.","r":"The wealth of அருள் is the wealth among wealths, because the wealth of goods is found even among the base.","n":"The argument is about distribution: what the worst can have can't be the best thing. It doesn't say goods are bad, and 247 will concede how much depends on them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["arul","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:25"},"k:242":{"book":"kural","num":242,"t":["நல்லாற்றாள் நாடி அருளாள்க பல்லாற்றால்","தேரினும் அஃதே துணை"],"p":"(Stand) in the good path, consider, and be kind. Even considering according to the conflicting tenets of the different sects, kindness will be your best aid, (in the acquisition of heavenly bliss.)","r":"Examine by sound means and hold to அருள்; however many ways you investigate, that alone is the companion.","n":"Compassion here is what inquiry concludes, not an alternative to it. Parimelazhagar reads பல்லாற்றால் as the competing religious systems, which disagree on means of knowledge and objects but converge here. The verse itself mentions no sects, so this ecumenical reading is his.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["arul","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Mutually opposed schools agree on this one point.","அன்னவாயினும், அருள் துணை என்றற்கண் ஒக்கும் என்பதாம்."]],"ch":"k:25"},"k:243":{"book":"kural","num":243,"t":["அருள்சேர்ந்த நெஞ்சினார்க் கில்லை இருள்சேர்ந்த","இன்னா உலகம் புகல்"],"p":"They will never enter the world of darkness and wretchedness whose minds are the abode of kindness.","r":"Those whose hearts are joined with அருள் will not enter the painful world joined with darkness.","n":"அருள் and இருள் rhyme across the verse, and the same word, சேர்ந்த, joins each to its noun.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","manam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It is a literal hell, a place in the lower world.","தன்கண்ணே புக்கார்க்குத் துன்பம் செய்வதோர் நரகத்தை"],["kalaignar","The darkness is ignorance, and the suffering is of this life.","அறியாமை எனும் இருள் சூழ்ந்த துன்ப உலகில்"]],"ch":"k:25"},"k:244":{"book":"kural","num":244,"t":["மன்னுயிர் ஓம்பி அருளாள்வார்க் கு இல்லென்ப","தன்னுயிர் அஞ்சும் வினை"],"p":"(The wise) say that the evils, which his soul would dread, will never come upon the man who exercises kindness and protects the life (of other creatures)","r":"They say that one who sustains living beings and holds to அருள் has no deeds his own life need fear.","n":"Care for other lives removes the fear for one's own. Parimelazhagar takes மன் as making souls eternal, a doctrine not required by the word, and gives a causal link: such a person won't kill, and hence won't go to hell.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["arul","vinai","acham"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:25"},"k:245":{"book":"kural","num":245,"t":["அல்லல் அருளாள்வார்க்கு இல்லை வளிவழங்கும்","மல்லன்மா ஞாலங் கரி"],"p":"This great rich earth over which the wind blows, is a witness that sorrow never comes upon the kind-hearted.","r":"Those who hold to அருள் have no distress, and the wide rich earth where the wind moves is the witness.","n":"Parimelazhagar's witness is negative: no one has ever seen such a person suffer, so the whole world can testify. Kalaignar instead makes the wind an analogy for அருள் moving the mind. The claim of no distress is bold, and should be read with the reservation of 169.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:25"},"k:246":{"book":"kural","num":246,"t":["பொருள்நீங்கிப் பொச்சாந்தார் என்பர் அருள்நீங்கி","அல்லவை செய்தொழுகு வார்"],"p":"(The wise) say that those who neglect kindness and practise cruelties, neglected virtue (in their former birth), and forgot (the sorrows which they must suffer.)","r":"Those who abandon அருள் and carry on doing what should not be done, they say, have parted from பொருள் and forgotten.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","aram","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","பொருள் is virtue: they failed to practise it before and have forgotten the suffering they are in as a result.","முன்னும் உறுதிப் பொருளைச் செய்யாது தாம் துன்புறுகின்றமையை மறந்தவர்"],["manakkudavar","பொருள் is wealth, and the verse spans lives: lacking அருள் in a former birth, they are now both poor and forgetful.","இப்பிறப்பின்கண் பொருளினின்று நீங்கி மறவியு முடையவ"],["kalaignar","With no former birth in view, the cruel become poor and forget their duty.","பொருளற்றவர்களாகவும், கடமை மறந்தவர்களாகவும் ஆவர்."]],"ch":"k:25"},"k:247":{"book":"kural","num":247,"t":["அருளில்லார்க்கு அவ்வுலகம் இல்லை பொருளில்லார்க்கு","இவ்வுலகம் இல்லாகி யாங்கு"],"p":"As this world is not for those who are without wealth, so that world is not for those who are without kindness.","r":"As this world is not for those without wealth, that world is not for those without அருள்.","n":"The comparison gets its force from its blunt premise about money, which the verse states as obvious fact. A chapter that opened by belittling goods grants them complete authority over this world.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","That world is release.","உயிர்கள்மேல் அருள் இல்லாதார்க்கு வீட்டுலகத்து இன்பம் இல்லை"],["manakkudavar","That world is heaven.","இஃது அருளில்லாதார் சுவர்க்கம் புகாரென்றது."],["kalaignar","It isn't another world at all: wealth is to the household life as compassion is to the renouncer's life.","கருணை உள்ளம் இல்லாதவர்களின் துறவற வாழ்க்கையும் சிறப்பாக அமையாது."]],"ch":"k:25"},"k:248":{"book":"kural","num":248,"t":["பொருளற்றார் பூப்பர் ஒருகால் அருளற்றார்","அற்றார்மற் றாதல் அரிது"],"p":"Those who are without wealth may, at some future time, become prosperous; those who are destitute of kindness are utterly destitute; for them there is no change.","r":"Those without wealth may flower one day; those without அருள் are finished, and it is hard for them to become anything again.","n":"Parimelazhagar sees this as withdrawing the parallel just drawn in 247, since the two lacks aren't alike after all. His addition of 'through fate' to the poor man's state is not in the verse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:25"},"k:249":{"book":"kural","num":249,"t":["தெருளாதான் மெய்ப்பொருள் கண்டற்றால் தேரின்","அருளாதான் செய்யும் அறம்"],"p":"If you consider, the virtue of him who is without kindness is like the perception of the true being by him who is without wisdom.","r":"Virtue done by one without அருள், if examined, is like the muddled person seeing the true reality.","n":"Parimelazhagar concludes that அருள் is the root of all other virtues. That goes beyond the comparison the verse draws, but it is a natural development.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","aram","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Such virtue can occur, as a passing glimpse can, but the person's own lack destroys it.","அருளாதான் இடையே அறஞ்செய்தால் அதனைத் தன் அருளாமையால் தானே அழித்துவிடும்"],["manakkudavar","Such people can't practise virtue at all.","இஃது அறஞ்செய்யவும் மாட்டாரென்றது,"]],"ch":"k:25"},"k:250":{"book":"kural","num":250,"t":["வலியார்முன் தன்னை நினைக்க தான் தன்னின்","மெலியார்மேல் செல்லு மிடத்து"],"p":"When a man is about to rush upon those who are weaker than himself, let him remember how he has stood (trembling) before those who are stronger than himself.","r":"When you go against those weaker than yourself, think of yourself before those stronger.","n":"The chapter's only instruction on how compassion arises doesn't appeal to a principle: it asks you to remember your own fear. Parimelazhagar calls this the means by which அருள் is born, and extends the weaker to animals too.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","acham"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:25"},"k:251":{"book":"kural","num":251,"t":["தன்னூன் பெருக்கற்குத் தான்பிறிது ஊனுண்பான்","எங்ஙனம் ஆளும் அருள்?"],"p":"How can he be possessed of kindness, who to increase his own flesh, eats the flesh of other creatures.","r":"How can one who eats another's flesh to swell his own hold to அருள்?","n":"ஊன் appears on both sides, so the eater is flesh too and the act is flesh feeding flesh. Parimelazhagar adds that fattening the body is itself useless, so the cruelty buys nothing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pulaal","arul"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:26"},"k:252":{"book":"kural","num":252,"t":["பொருளாட்சி போற்றாதார்க்கு இல்லை அருளாட்சி","ஆங்கில்லை ஊன்தின் பவர்க்கு"],"p":"As those possess no property who do not take care of it, so those possess no kindness who feed on flesh.","r":"Holding wealth isn't for those who don't guard it, and likewise holding அருள் isn't for those who eat flesh.","n":"ஆட்சி, holding and using, is the operative word: compassion is a possession that lapses through neglect. For Parimelazhagar the verse answers those who say a flesh-eater who wishes no creature harm hasn't compromised அருள்.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pulaal","arul","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The verse rebuts the claim that good intention makes flesh-eating harmless to அருள்.","ஊன் தின்றாராயினும் உயிர்கட்கு ஒரு தீங்கும் நினையாதார்க்கு அருள் ஆள்தற்கு இழுக்கு இல்லை என்பாரை மறுத்து,"]],"ch":"k:26"},"k:253":{"book":"kural","num":253,"t":["படைகொண்டார் நெஞ்சம்போல் நன்னூக்காது ஒன்றன்","உடல்சுவை உண்டார் மனம்"],"p":"Like the (murderous) mind of him who carries a weapon (in his hand), the mind of him who feasts with pleasure on the body of another (creature), has no regard for goodness.","r":"The mind of one who has relished another creature's body doesn't incline to good, like the heart of one holding a weapon.","n":"The argument concerns attention rather than guilt: what the hand holds, or the tongue relishes, takes over the mind. சுவை matters here, since the verse speaks of savouring and not merely of eating.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pulaal","arul","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:26"},"k:254":{"book":"kural","num":254,"t":["ருளல்லது யாதெனின் கொல்லாமை கோறல்","பொருளல்லது அவ்வூன் தினல்"],"p":"If it be asked what is kindness and what its opposite, the answer would be preservation and destruction of life; and therefore it is not right to feed on the flesh (obtained by taking away life).","r":"If asked what is அருள் and what isn't, it comes down to not killing and killing; eating that flesh is not பொருள்.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kollaamai","arul","pulaal"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","அருள் is not killing and its opposite is killing, and பொருள் here means virtue, so eating is sin. He also reports another division of the words.","அக்கோறலான் வந்த ஊனைத் தின்கை பாவம்."],["manakkudavar","Non-அருள் is destroying the vow of not killing, and பொருள் means gain, so eating harms அருள் and gets one nothing.","அருள் கெடுதலேயன்றி, பெறுவதொரு பயனுமில்லை என்றது."]],"ch":"k:26"},"k:255":{"book":"kural","num":255,"t":["உண்ணாமை உள்ளது உயிர்நிலை ஊனுண்ண","அண்ணாத்தல் செய்யாது அளறு"],"p":"Not to eat flesh contributes to the continuance of life; therefore if a man eat flesh, hell will not open its mouth (to let him escape out, after he has once fallen in).","r":"The standing of life lies in not eating flesh; if one eats, hell doesn't open its mouth.","n":"The image is of a mouth that has swallowed and won't open to release. Parimelazhagar offers a population argument for உயிர்நிலை: creatures that are eaten dwindle while others multiply. Kalaignar takes அளறு as a mud pit, not the hell of the older commentators.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pulaal","kollaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:26"},"k:256":{"book":"kural","num":256,"t":["தினற்பொருட்டால் கொல்லாது உலகெனின் யாரும்","விலைப்பொருட்டால் ஊன்றருவா ரில்"],"p":"If the world does not destroy life for the purpose of eating, then no one would sell flesh for the sake of money.","r":"If the world didn't kill for the sake of eating, nobody would bring flesh for the sake of a price.","n":"This is a demand-and-supply argument. The buyer who says he killed nothing is told that his purchase causes the killing. Parimelazhagar formalizes the reply as inference by necessary implication.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pulaal","kollaamai"],"pos":[["manakkudavar","It answers those who ask what fault there is in buying and eating without having killed.","கொன்று தின்னாது விலைக்குக் கொண்டு தின்பார்க்குக் குற்றமென்னை யென்றார்க்கு , அதனாலுங் கொலைப்பாவம் வருமென்று கூறிற்று."]],"ch":"k:26"},"k:257":{"book":"kural","num":257,"t":["உண்ணாமை வேண்டும் புலாஅல் பிறிதொன்றன்","புண்ணது உணர்வார்ப் பெறின்"],"p":"If men should come to know that flesh is nothing but the unclean ulcer of a body, let them abstain from eating it.","r":"If there were people who perceived flesh as another creature's wound, it would not be eaten.","n":"பெறின் suggests such perceivers are rare. The commentators stress impurity, yet 'another's wound' can equally appeal to fellow-feeling, as the wound belongs to someone. That second emphasis is my own reading.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pulaal","arul"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:26"},"k:258":{"book":"kural","num":258,"t":["செயிரின் தலைப்பிரிந்த காட்சியார் உண்ணார்","உயிரின் தலைப்பிரிந்த ஊன்"],"p":"The wise, who have freed themselves from mental delusion, will not eat the flesh which has been severed from an animal.","r":"Those whose vision has parted from fault don't eat flesh that has parted from life.","n":"The two lines mirror each other with தலைப்பிரிந்த: a person parted from defect faces flesh parted from life. Parimelazhagar says they see for themselves that it is a corpse, so no rule is needed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pulaal","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:26"},"k:259":{"book":"kural","num":259,"t":["அவிசொரிந் தாயிரம் வேட்டலின் ஒன்றன்","உயிர்செகுத் துண்ணாமை நன்று"],"p":"Not to kill and eat (the flesh of) an animal, is better than the pouring forth of ghee etc., in a thousand sacrifices.","r":"Better than pouring oblations in a thousand sacrifices is not taking one creature's life and eating.","n":"A thousand rites weigh less than one abstention. The commentators treat this as a comparison of merit, but the verse can also be read as a challenge to ritual, and that sharper reading isn't one they give.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","pulaal","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:26"},"k:260":{"book":"kural","num":260,"t":["கொல்லான் புலாலை மறுத்தானைக் கைகூப்பி","எல்லா உயிருந் தொழும்"],"p":"All creatures will join their hands together, and worship him who has never taken away life, nor eaten flesh.","r":"All living beings fold their hands and worship the one who doesn't kill and has refused flesh.","n":"The beings that have been spared do the worshipping, an image rather than a report. Parimelazhagar insists that the two abstentions are only fruitful together, which closes the loophole of the previous verses.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","pulaal","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:26"},"k:261":{"book":"kural","num":261,"t":["உற்றநோய் நோன்றல் உயிர்க்குறுகண் செய்யாமை","அற்றே தவத்திற் குரு"],"p":"The nature of religious discipline consists, in the endurance (by the ascetic) of the sufferings which it brings on himself, and in abstaining from giving pain to others.","r":"Bearing the pain that comes and causing no pain to living beings: such alone is the form of தவம்.","n":"The definition mentions no gods, rites or forest, and half of it is ethical. Parimelazhagar takes the pain as self-imposed through such things as reduced food, while Manakkudavar says only pain that has come to one. Kalaignar turns it into a heart that can bear anything, which would make tapas available in any life.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","arul"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:27"},"k:262":{"book":"kural","num":262,"t":["தவமும் தவமுடையார்க்கு ஆகும் அதனை","அஃதிலார் மேற்கொள் வது"],"p":"Austerities can only be borne, and their benefits enjoyed, by those who have practised them (in a former birth); it will be useless for those who have not done so, to attempt to practise them (now).","r":"தவம் too comes to those who have தவம்; for those without it, taking it up is futile.","n":"The form is circular, a discouraging one for a would-be beginner. The chapter's praise of effort in the following verses sits awkwardly with it.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["thavam","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","One needs தவம் from an earlier birth, which gives the familiarity needed to complete it.","தவந்தானும் உண்டாவது முன்தவம் உடையார்க்கே"],["manakkudavar","It requires earlier good deeds of any kind, not earlier austerity as such.","தவஞ்செய்தலும் முன்பு நல்வினை செய்தார்க்கு வரும்"],["kalaignar","It requires present firmness and self-control, with no former birth involved.","உறுதிப்பாடும், மன அடக்கமும் உடையவருக்கே தவத்தின் பெருமை வாய்க்கும்."]],"ch":"k:27"},"k:263":{"book":"kural","num":263,"t":["துறந்தார்க்குத் துப்புரவு வேண்டி மறந்தார்கொல்","மற்றை யவர்கள் தவம்"],"p":"It is to provide food etc, for the ascetics who have abandoned (the desire of earthly possessions) that other persons have forgotten (to practise) austerity ?","r":"Is it in wanting to provide for those who have renounced that the others have forgotten தவம்?","n":"The chiding question implies that தவம் isn't reserved for renunciants. Both older commentators draw the conclusion that தவம் is greater than giving, but the verse only implies it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","eegai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Householders, drawn by love of giving, have forgotten what is greater.","தானத்தினும் தவம் மிக்கது என்பது பெற்றாம்."]],"ch":"k:27"},"k:264":{"book":"kural","num":264,"t":["ஒன்னார்த் தெறலும் உவந்தாரை ஆக்கலும்","எண்ணின் தவத்தான் வரும்"],"p":"If (the ascetic) desire the destruction of his enemies, or the aggrandizement of his friends, it will be effected by (the power of) his austerities.","r":"Crushing foes and raising those who rejoice with one come through தவம், if one so intends.","n":"எண்ணின், 'if one thinks of it', is doing much here. Parimelazhagar takes it to imply that such people naturally don't think of it. An ascetic with enemies is a problem for him, and he solves it by moving the power to தவம் itself.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The foes are enemies of virtue, and the friends are those who delight in it.","அறத்திற்குப் பகையாய் அழிவு செய்தாரைக் கெடுத்தலும்"],["manakkudavar","These are ordinary enemies and friends, and the power comes from austerity done earlier.","பகைவரைத் தெறுதலும், நட்டோரையாக்குதலு மாகிய வலி ஆராயின், முன்செய்த தவத்தினாலே வரும்"]],"ch":"k:27"},"k:265":{"book":"kural","num":265,"t":["வேண்டிய வேண்டியாங் கெய்தலால் செய்தவம்","ஈண்டு முயலப் படும்"],"p":"Religious dislipline is practised in this world, because it secures the attainment of whatever one may wish to enjoy (in the world to come).","r":"Because whatever is wanted is obtained as it is wanted, தவம் is striven for here.","n":"The motive offered is getting what you want, in a chapter that condemns desire in the very next verse. Parimelazhagar infers from ஈண்டு, 'here', that the results come in the next life and include release, but the verse doesn't say so.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:27"},"k:266":{"book":"kural","num":266,"t":["தவஞ் செய்வார் தங்கருமஞ் செய்வார்மற் றல்லார்","அவஞ்செய்வார் ஆசையுட் பட்டு"],"p":"Those discharge their duty who perform austerities; all others accomplish their own destruction, through the entanglement of the desire (of riches and sensual pleasure).","r":"Those who do தவம் are doing their own work; the rest, caught in desire, do what is futile.","n":"தங்கருமம் is telling: austerity is presented as true self-interest, and worldly activity as neglect of one's real business. Parimelazhagar adds 'having renounced' and identifies 'the rest' as those pursuing wealth and pleasure, which sweeps in most of life.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","avaa"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:27"},"k:267":{"book":"kural","num":267,"t":["சுடச்சுடரும் பொன்போல் ஒளிவிடும் துன்பஞ்","சுடச்சுட நோற்கிற் பவர்க்கு"],"p":"Just as gold is purified as heated in the fire, will those shine, who have endured the burning of pain (in frequent austerities).","r":"For those able to endure, pain burning them makes them shine, as gold shines the more it is burned.","n":"The doubled சுடச்சுட appears in both lines and makes the image of repeated firing audible. What the shining stands for is left open: for Parimelazhagar sin leaves and knowledge grows, for Manakkudavar deeds leave, and for Kalaignar it is fame.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:27"},"k:268":{"book":"kural","num":268,"t":["தன்னுயிர் தான்அறப் பெற்றானை ஏனைய","மன்னுயி ரெல்லாந் தொழும்"],"p":"All other creatures will worship him who has attained the control of his own soul.","r":"All other living beings worship the one who has completely gained his own self.","n":"The disagreement is nearly a reversal: for one reading the person comes to own the self, and for the other he is rid of it.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["thavam","manam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","He has obtained his own self as truly his own, and அற intensifies this: nothing is harder to obtain.","தன் உயிரைத் தான் தனக்கு உரித்தாகப் பெற்றவனை"],["manakkudavar","அற marks cessation: he has got rid of the thought of 'I', and தான் is the ego.","தானறு தலாவது அகங்கார மறு தல்."],["kalaignar","He has neither attachment to 'my life' nor the pride of 'I'.","\"தனது உயிர்\" என்கிற பற்றும், \"தான்\" என்கிற செருக்கும் கொள்ளாதவர்களை"]],"ch":"k:27"},"k:269":{"book":"kural","num":269,"t":["கூற்றம் குதித்தலும் கைகூடும் நோற்றலின்","ஆற்றல் தலைப்பட் டவர்க்குல்"],"p":"Those who have attained the power which religious discipline confers, will be able also to pass the limit of Yama, (the God of death).","r":"Even leaping past death becomes possible for those who have attained the power of austerity.","n":"Parimelazhagar notes the 'even' can signal how nearly impossible it is. Manakkudavar illustrates with Markandeya, which is his example and isn't in the verse. Kalaignar takes it as conquering death through steadfastness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["thavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:27"},"k:270":{"book":"kural","num":270,"t":["இலர்பல ராகிய காரணம் நோற்பார்","சிலர்பலர் நோலா தவர்"],"p":"Because there are few who practise austerity and many who do not, there are many destitute and few rich in this world.","r":"The reason the have-nots are many: those who practise austerity are few and those who don't are many.","n":"The verse says just 'those who lack', without saying what they lack. As an explanation of poverty it is arresting and severe, and the commentators each soften or redirect it.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Wealth and poverty here include having and lacking understanding.","செல்வம் நல்குரவு என்பன ஈண்டு அறிவினது உண்மை இன்மைகளையும் குறித்து நின்றன;"],["manakkudavar","It is about those without goods, plainly.","பொருளில்லாதார் உலகத்துப் பலராதற்குக் காரணம், தவஞ் செய்வார் சிலராதல்;"],["kalaignar","It is about those lacking capability, and the few are simply those with firm resolve.","ஆற்றலற்றவர்கள் பலராக இருப்பதற்குக் காரணம், மன உறுதி கொண்டவர் சிலராக இருப்பதும்"]],"ch":"k:27"},"k:271":{"book":"kural","num":271,"t":["வஞ்ச மனத்தான் படிற்றொழுக்கம் பூதங்கள்","ஐந்தும் அகத்தே நகும்"],"p":"The five elements (of his body) will laugh within him at the feigned conduct of the deceitful minded man.","r":"The five elements laugh inwardly at the concealed conduct of one with a deceitful mind.","n":"A hypocrite can't escape witnesses, because his own body is one of them. Parimelazhagar identifies the hidden conduct as sexual, and he carries this through 273 and 274, but the verse doesn't specify.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["koodaozhukkam","manam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The elements themselves are the cosmic witnesses, mingled in his body and laughing without his knowing.","உடம்பாய் அவனோடு கலந்து நிற்கின்ற பூதங்கள் ஐந்தும் கண்டு தம்முள்ளே நகும்."],["manakkudavar","'Elements' stands for the senses, their products, which know what he does.","பூதங்களென்றது அவையிற்றின் காரியமாகிய பொறிகளை ."]],"ch":"k:28"},"k:272":{"book":"kural","num":272,"t":["வானுயர் தோற்றம் எவன்செய்யும் தன்னெஞ்சம்","தான்அறி குற்றப் படின்"],"p":"What avails an appearance (of sanctity) high as heaven, if his mind suffers (the indulgence) of conscious sin.","r":"What will an appearance as high as the sky do, if one's own heart sinks into a fault one knows to be a fault?","n":"தான்அறி, 'that one knows', is the crux. Parimelazhagar's point is that a fault done unknowingly can be washed away, but one done knowingly can't, so the outward habit only frightens others.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["koodaozhukkam","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:28"},"k:273":{"book":"kural","num":273,"t":["வலியில் நிலைமையான் வல்லுருவம் பெற்றம்","புலியின்தோல் போர்த்துமேய்ந் தற்று"],"p":"The assumed appearance of power, by a man who has no power (to restrain his senses and perform austerity), is like a cow feeding on grass covered with a tiger's skin.","r":"A person without strength taking on the form of the strong is like a cow grazing wrapped in a tiger's skin.","n":"Parimelazhagar presses the image hard. The guards don't chase the 'tiger' because tigers don't eat grass, and because they are afraid, and the cow leaves its own grass for another's crop. So the renouncer leaves his wife and pursues other men's. The strength lacking is mastery of one's own mind; the rest is his extrapolation.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["koodaozhukkam","thuravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:28"},"k:274":{"book":"kural","num":274,"t":["தவமறைந்து அல்லவை செய்தல் புதல்மறைந்து","வேட்டுவன் புள்சிமிழ்த் தற்று"],"p":"He who hides himself under the mask of an ascetic and commits sins, like a sportsman who conceals himself in the thicket to catch birds.","r":"Hiding in austerity and doing what isn't austerity is like a hunter hiding in a bush and snaring birds.","n":"With 273 this gives two opposed animals: the cow is harmless inside a predator's skin, and the hunter is a predator behind harmless leaves. The hypocrite is both, weak before his desire and dangerous to others. That pairing is my own observation. Manakkudavar gives Arjuna as an example.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["koodaozhukkam","thavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:28"},"k:275":{"book":"kural","num":275,"t":["பற்றற்றேம் என்பார் படிற்றொழுக்கம் எற்றெற்றென்று","ஏதம் பலவுந் தரும்"],"p":"The false conduct of those who say they have renounced all desire will one day bring them sorrows that will make them cry out, \"Oh! what have we done, what have we done.\"","r":"The hidden conduct of those who say 'we are free of attachment' brings many evils, with cries of 'what, what!'","n":"என்பார், 'those who say', quietly marks the claim as words only, and Parimelazhagar says that the attachment was only cut in speech.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["koodaozhukkam","thuravu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The cry belongs to the hypocrites, who later lament what they have done.","பின் 'என் செய்தோம்! என் செய்தோம்! என்று தாமே இரங்கும்வகை,"],["manakkudavar","The cry belongs to everyone, who exclaim at such a person, and எற்று is a regional word.","எல்லாரும் எற்றெற்றென்று சொல்லும்படி பலகுற்றமும் உண்டாக்கும்,"]],"ch":"k:28"},"k:276":{"book":"kural","num":276,"t":["நெஞ்சின் துறவார் துறந்தார்போல் வஞ்சித்து","வாழ்வாரின் வன்கணார் இல்"],"p":"Amongst living men there are none so hard-hearted as those who without to saking (desire) in their heart, falsely take the appearance of those who have forsaken (it).","r":"None are more hard-eyed than those who, unrenounced in heart, live by deceiving as though they had renounced.","n":"Why is deceit the height of cruelty? Parimelazhagar has a remarkable answer: the victims are donors, whose gifts to the unworthy earn them a low birth, so the fraud returns harm for help. He supports this from the Civakacintamani, not from the verse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["koodaozhukkam","thuravu","manam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","They harm those who feed them, since gifts to the unrestrained bring donors a low birth.","தமக்கு ஆவன செய்தார்க்கு ஆகாதன விளைத்தலின் 'வன்கணார் இல்' என்றார்."]],"ch":"k:28"},"k:277":{"book":"kural","num":277,"t":["புறங்குன்றி கண்டனைய ரேனும் அகங்குன்றி","முக்கிற் கரியார் உடைத்து"],"p":"(The world) contains persons whose outside appears (as fair) as the (red) berry of the Abrus, but whose inside is as black as the nose of that berry.","r":"The world holds those who, though looking outwardly like the red kunri seed, are inwardly black as its tip.","n":"The kunri is a closely observed choice, since its red and black belong to a single small seed. உடைத்து, 'it holds', says only that such people exist. Manakkudavar takes this as a warning to the wise not to judge ascetics by form.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["koodaozhukkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:28"},"k:278":{"book":"kural","num":278,"t":["மனத்தது மாசாக மாண்டார் நீராடி","மறைந்தொழுகு மாந்தர் பலர்"],"p":"There are many men of masked conduct, who perform their ablutions, and (make a show) of greatness, while their mind is defiled (with guilt).","r":"Many people, with impurity in their minds, bathe as though distinguished and go about concealed.","n":"பலர், 'many', comes right after the 'some' of 277 and is a social observation. Water cleans what isn't dirty and leaves what is. Parimelazhagar specifies the impurity as desire, anger and delusion.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["koodaozhukkam","manam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","They literally bathe, to appear distinguished by austerity, and hide behind this.","பிறர்க்குத் தவத்தான் மாட்சிமையுடையராய் நீரின் மூழ்கிக் காட்டி;"],["manakkudavar","நீராடி isn't bathing: they put on the நீர்மை, the nature, of the distinguished.","மாட்சிமைப் பட்டாரது நீர்மையைப் பூண்டு,"],["kalaignar","Water is an analogy: they hide under the name of greatness as a diver hides under water.","நீருக்குள் மூழ்கியோர் தம்மை மறைத்துக் கொள்வது போல,"]],"ch":"k:28"},"k:279":{"book":"kural","num":279,"t":["கணைகொடிது யாழ்கோடு செவ்விதுஆங் கன்ன","வினைபடு பாலால் கொளல்"],"p":"As, in its use, the arrow is crooked, and the curved lute is straight, so by their deeds, (and not by their appearance) let (the uprightness or crookedness of) men be estimated.","r":"The arrow is cruel and the curved lute is straight, so know people by the nature of their deeds.","n":"The verse makes shape deceive in both directions: the straight kills and the curved delights. It does more than warn that some who look good are bad, since it also suggests that some who look wrong are good.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["koodaozhukkam","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:28"},"k:280":{"book":"kural","num":280,"t":["மழித்தலும் நீட்டலும் வேண்டா உலகம்","பழித்தது ஒழித்து விடின்"],"p":"There is no need of a shaven crown, nor of tangled hair, if a man abstain from those deeds which the wise have condemned.","r":"Neither shaving nor long hair is needed, if one gives up what the world condemns.","n":"The test of renunciation is set by what the ordinary world condemns, and not by a special ascetic code. Parimelazhagar narrows உலகம் to the high-minded, but Manakkudavar leaves it as people generally. The verse removes the outward marks, not renunciation itself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["koodaozhukkam","thuravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:28"},"k:281":{"book":"kural","num":281,"t":["எள்ளாமை வேண்டுவான் என்பான் எனைத்தொன்றும்","கள்ளாமை காக்கதன் நெஞ்சு"],"p":"Let him, who desires not to be despised, keep his mind from (the desire of) defrauding another of the smallest thing.","r":"One who is called a person wanting not to be scorned should guard his own heart against stealing anything at all.","n":"The verse guards the heart, not the hand, and so the offence is relocated. Parimelazhagar uses எள்ளாமை for a long attack on Lokayata materialism, and this polemic is entirely his.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalavu","manam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It means not scorning release: the person wants liberation and is respected by ascetics for that.","வீட்டினை இகழாது விரும்புவான் இவன் என்று தவத்தோரான் நன்கு மதிக்கப்படுவான்;"],["manakkudavar","It means not being scorned by other people, a matter of ordinary repute.","பிறரா லிகழப்படாமையை வேண்டுவா னிவனென்று சொல் லப்படுமவன்,"]],"ch":"k:29"},"k:282":{"book":"kural","num":282,"t":["உள்ளத்தால் உள்ளலும் தீதே பிறன்பொருளைக்","கள்ளத்தால் கள்வேம் எனல்"],"p":"Even the thought (of sin) is sin; think not then of crafiily stealing the property of another.","r":"Even thinking it in the mind is wrong, saying 'we will steal another's goods by stealth'.","n":"Parimelazhagar limits this high standard to renunciants, whose minds he says are special, but the verse names no such group. The distinction matters, as a universal thought-crime is a stronger claim.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalavu","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:29"},"k:283":{"book":"kural","num":283,"t":["களவினால் ஆகிய ஆக்கம் அளவிறந்து","ஆவது போலக் கெடும்"],"p":"The property, which is acquired by fraud, will entirely perish, even while it seems to increase.","r":"Gain got by stealth will perish beyond all measure, even as it seems to grow.","n":"ஆவது போல, 'as if growing', places the ruin inside the appearance of success. Parimelazhagar gives அளவிறந்து a sharp sense: as it goes, stolen wealth leaves sin and blame behind and takes earlier virtue along with it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalavu","selvam","alavu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:29"},"k:284":{"book":"kural","num":284,"t":["களவின்கண் கன்றிய காதல் விளைவின்கண்","வீயா விழுமம் தரும்"],"p":"The eager desire of defrauding others will, when it brings forth its fruit, produce undying sorrow.","r":"Love that has ripened in stealth yields, at harvest, a sorrow that doesn't die.","n":"The verb and noun suggest a crop: கன்றிய, then விளைவு. Using காதல் for the urge to steal treats theft as an attachment, and Parimelazhagar makes the point that the craving breeds repetition, which is why the sorrow doesn't end.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalavu","avaa","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:29"},"k:285":{"book":"kural","num":285,"t":["அருள்கருதி அன்புடைய ராதல் பொருள்கருதிப்","பொச்சாப்புப் பார்ப்பார்கண் இல்"],"p":"The study of kindness and the exercise of benevolence is not with those who watch for another's forgetfulness, though desire of his property.","r":"Having a loving nature with அருள் in view isn't found in those who, with goods in view, watch for others' lapses.","n":"The rhyme of அருள்கருதி and பொருள்கருதி sets two objects of attention side by side as if a mind could hold only one. பொச்சாப்புப் பார்ப்பார் is a precise picture of the thief's patient watching.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalavu","arul"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:29"},"k:286":{"book":"kural","num":286,"t":["அளவின்கண் நின்றொழுகல் ஆற்றார் களவின்கண்","கன்றிய காத லவர்"],"p":"They cannot walk steadfastly, according to rule, who eagerly desire to defraud others.","r":"Those whose love has ripened in stealth can't keep to the path of அளவு.","n":"The rhyme அளவு and களவு runs through the next several verses, so the chapter has built its argument into one letter's difference.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalavu","alavu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","அளவு is reasoned inquiry measuring the soul, karma and release, which he identifies with a Jain term.","உயிர் முதலியவற்றை அளத்தலாகிய நெற்றியின்கண் நின்று அதற்கு ஏற்ப ஒழுகமாட்டார்;"],["manakkudavar","அளவு is simply rectitude.","களவின்கண்ணே மிக்க ஆசையையுடையவர் நேரின் கணின்று ஒழுகுதலைச் செய்யமாட்டார்,"],["kalaignar","அளவு is living within limits, and failing to do so leads to theft, which reverses the causal direction.","ஓர் எல்லைக்குட்பட்டு வாழ்வைச் செம்மையாக அமைத்துக் கொள்ளாதவர்கள்,"]],"ch":"k:29"},"k:287":{"book":"kural","num":287,"t":["களவென்னும் காரறி வாண்மை அளவென்னும்","ஆற்றல் புரிந்தார்கண்ட இல்"],"p":"That black-knowledge which is called fraud, is not in those who desire that greatness which is called rectitude.","r":"The dark knowledge called stealth isn't found in those who have set their love on the strength called அளவு.","n":"Stealth is granted to be a kind of intelligence, just a dark one, so cleverness alone is not a virtue in the Kural. Parimelazhagar says stealth and renunciation are opposed as dark and light.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kalavu","alavu","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:29"},"k:288":{"book":"kural","num":288,"t":["அளவறாந்தார் நெஞ்சத் தறம்போல நிற்கும்","களவறிந்தார் நெஞ்சில் கரவு"],"p":"Deceit dwells in the mind of those who are conversant with fraud, even as virtue in the mind s of those who are conversant with rectitude.","r":"Deceit stands in the heart of those who know stealth, just as virtue stands in the heart of those who know அளவு.","n":"The comparison gives vice the same stability as virtue. Manakkudavar draws the discouraging conclusion that once learned, stealth can't be given up.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kalavu","alavu","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:29"},"k:289":{"book":"kural","num":289,"t":["அளவல்ல செய்தாங்கே வீவர் களவல்ல","மற்றைய தேற்றா தவர்"],"p":"Those, who are acquainted with nothing but fraud, will perish in the very commission of transgression.","r":"Those who know nothing but stealth perish right there, having done what is beyond measure.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalavu","alavu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The ruin is immediate and inward: they are lost the moment they think such thoughts, and 'other things' are the renunciant's permitted food and lay gifts.","அவ்வளவல்லாத தீய நினைவுகளை நினைத்த பொழுதே கெடுவர்;"],["manakkudavar","The ruin is civil: kings execute thieves.","இது கள் வாரை அரசர் கொல்வரென்றது."]],"ch":"k:29"},"k:290":{"book":"kural","num":290,"t":["கள்வார்க்குத் தள்ளும் உயிர்நிலை கள்வார்க்குத்","தள்ளாது புத்தே ளுளகு"],"p":"Even their body will fail the fraudulent; but even the world of the gods will not fail those who are free from fraud.","r":"For those who steal, உயிர்நிலை falls away; for those who don't, the world of the gods doesn't fall away.","n":"The shared verb தள்ளு balances the near against the far, in Parimelazhagar's phrase: even what is closest fails the one, and even what is remote doesn't fail the other.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kalavu","piravi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","உயிர்நிலை is the body, lost when the king punishes.","களவினைப் பயில்வார்க்குத் தம்மின் வேறல்லாத உடம்பும் தவறும்;"],["manakkudavar","உயிர்நிலை is release, so thieves miss liberation just as non-thieves gain heaven.","பிறர் பொருளைக் கள்வார்க்கு உயிர்நிலையாகிய வீடு பெறுதல் தப்பும்;"],["kalaignar","It is life itself, and the gods' world becomes a world of fame.","களவை நினைத்தும் பார்க்காதவர்க்கோ, புகழுலக வாழ்க்கை தவறவே தவறாது."]],"ch":"k:29"},"k:291":{"book":"kural","num":291,"t":["வாய்மை எனப்படுவது யாதெனின் யாதொன்றும்","தீமை இலாத சொலல்"],"p":"Truth is the speaking of such words as are free from the least degree of evil (to others).","r":"If you ask what truth is, it is speech containing no evil at all.","n":"A surprising definition, because nothing here refers to facts. Parimelazhagar spells out the consequence: stating what happened isn't the definition, and even an accurate report is falsehood if it harms.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaaimai","sol"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Reporting what actually occurred is excluded as the definition of truth.","இதனான் நிகழ்ந்தது கூறல் என்பது நீக்கப்பட்டது."]],"ch":"k:30"},"k:292":{"book":"kural","num":292,"t":["பொய்மையும் வாய்மை யிடத்த புரைதீர்ந்த","நன்மை பயக்கும் எனின்"],"p":"Even falsehood has the nature of truth, if it confer a benefit that is free from fault.","r":"Even falsehood is in the place of truth, if it yields good that is free of fault.","n":"புரைதீர்ந்த does the limiting work: this excuses no convenient lie. Parimelazhagar's example is a falsehood that saves a creature from ruin or death, and he builds 291 and 292 into a four-way scheme crossing fact with effect.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["vaaimai","sol","aram"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Truth and falsehood are classified by effect: useful non-fact is truth, and harmful or useless fact is falsehood.","'தீங்கு பயவாத நிகழ்ந்தது கூறலும், நன்மை பயக்கும் நிகழாதது கூறலும் மெய்ம்மை'"]],"ch":"k:30"},"k:293":{"book":"kural","num":293,"t":["தன்நெஞ் சறிவது பொய்யற்க பொய்த்தபின்","தன்நெஞ்சே தன்னைச் சுடும்"],"p":"Let not a man knowingly tell a lie; for after he has told the lie, his mind will burn him (with the memory of his guilt).","r":"Don't lie about what your own heart knows; once you lie, your own heart will burn you.","n":"The harm returns by the same organ of knowing. Parimelazhagar has the heart as a witness that brings on the sin's result, and Manakkudavar has it as remorse on seeing what the lie did to others. Kalaignar's 'conscience' is a modern word for the same thing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaaimai","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:30"},"k:294":{"book":"kural","num":294,"t":["உள்ளத்தாற் பொய்யா தொழுகின் உலகத்தார்","உள்ளத்து ளெல்லாம் உளன்"],"p":"He who, in his conduct, preserves a mind free from deceit, will dwell in the minds of all men.","r":"If one conducts oneself without being false in one's heart, one lives in the hearts of all the world.","n":"உள்ளம் appears on both sides of the verse: inner truth gives a place in others' inner lives. Parimelazhagar narrows 'the world' to the elevated, and says it is speech that has to match the heart. Manakkudavar and Kalaignar make it not even thinking a lie.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaaimai","manam","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:30"},"k:295":{"book":"kural","num":295,"t":["மனத்தொடு வாய்மை மொழியின் தவத்தொடு","தானஞ்செய் வாரின் தலை"],"p":"He, who speaks truth with all his heart, is superior to those who make gifts and practise austerities.","r":"If one speaks truth together with one's heart, one is above those who practise austerity along with giving.","n":"Parimelazhagar's reason is about levels of the person: acts of body are outward, but acts of mind and speech are inward. Note that truth is ranked here above the two virtues that chapters 23 and 27 have just exalted.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaaimai","manam","thavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:30"},"k:296":{"book":"kural","num":296,"t":["பொய்யாமை அன்ன புகழில்லை எய்யாமை","எல்லா அறமுந் தரும்"],"p":"There is no praise like the praise of never uttering a falsehood: without causing any suffering, it will lead to every virtue.","r":"There is no renown like not lying, and it brings all virtue without எய்யாமை.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaaimai","pugazh","aram"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Without bodily strain: it spares the toil that wealth-gathering and fasting demand.","மறுமைக்கு மெய் வருந்தாமல் அவனுக்கு எல்லா அறங்களையும் தானே கொடுக்கும்."],["manakkudavar","Without his knowing: virtue accrues to him unawares.","பொய்யாமையானது அவனறியாமல் தானே எல்லா அறங்களையுங் கொடுக்குமாதலான்,"],["kalaignar","It brings benefits that never leave.","என்றும் நீங்காத அறவழி நலன்களை அளிப்பது அந்த வாழ்வேயாகும்."]],"ch":"k:30"},"k:297":{"book":"kural","num":297,"t":["பொய்யாமை பொய்யாமை ஆற்றின் அறம்பிற","செய்யாமை செய்யாமை நன்று"],"p":"If a man has the power to abstain from falsehood, it will be well with him, even though he practise no other virtue.","r":"If one keeps up not lying, not lying, then not doing other virtues, not doing them, is good.","n":"The commentators' opposed readings depend on where the doubled words are divided. Parimelazhagar knows and rejects a reading like Manakkudavar's.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaaimai","aram"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Truthfulness alone suffices, and it is better to skip other virtues than to attempt many and fail.","அவன் பிற அறங்களைச் செய்யாமையே செய்யாமையே நன்று."],["manakkudavar","The opposite stress: other virtues are good only if truthfulness is unfailing, and otherwise they turn bad.","பொய்யாமையைப் பொய்யாமல் செலுத்துவனாயின், பிற அறங் களைச் செய்தல் நன்றாம்; அல்லது தீதாம்,"]],"ch":"k:30"},"k:298":{"book":"kural","num":298,"t":["புறள்தூய்மை நீரான் அமையும் அகந்தூய்மை","வாய்மையால் காணப் படும்"],"p":"Purity of body is produced by water and purity of mind by truthfulness.","r":"Outer purity comes about through water; inner purity is seen through truth.","n":"காணப்படும் is more ambiguous than it looks. Parimelazhagar explains that what is seen exists, so truth produces purity, whereas Manakkudavar says purity is known by truthful speech, which makes truth its evidence. Parimelazhagar also infers that renunciants need both purities.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["vaaimai","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:30"},"k:299":{"book":"kural","num":299,"t":["எல்லா விளக்கும் விளக்கல்ல சான்றோர்க்குப்","பொய்யா விளக்கே விளக்கு"],"p":"All lamps of nature are not lamps; the lamp of truth is the lamp of the wise.","r":"Not all lamps are lamps; for the worthy, the unfailing lamp alone is the lamp.","n":"பொய்யா, 'unfailing' and also 'unlying', makes truth a kind of light that shows things as they are.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaaimai","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The rejected lamps are worldly lights such as sun, moon and fire, which dispel outer darkness only.","புறத்து இருள் கடியும் உலகத்தார் விளக்குகள் எல்லாம் விளக்கு ஆகா;"],["manakkudavar","விளக்கு is the radiance of a good person, and what is rejected is radiance earned by other virtues.","சான்றோர்க்கு எல்லாவறத்தினாலும் உண்டான ஒளியும் ஒளி யல்ல ;"]],"ch":"k:30"},"k:300":{"book":"kural","num":300,"t":["யாமெய்யாக் கண்டவற்றுள் இல்லை எனைத்தொன்றும்","வாய்மையின் நல்ல பிற"],"p":"Amidst all that we have seen (described) as real (excellence), there is nothing so good as truthfulness.","r":"Among the things we have seen as true, there is nothing at all better than truthfulness.","n":"The first person, யாம், is a rare moment when the poet speaks as himself. Parimelazhagar turns 'what we have seen' into the scriptures revealed by the Lord, but Manakkudavar keeps it simple as what we have found to be true, and nothing in the verse mentions texts.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:30"},"k:301":{"book":"kural","num":301,"t":["செல்லிடத்துக் காப்பான் சினங்காப்பான் அல்லிடத்துக்","காக்கின்என் காவாக்கால் என்?"],"p":"He restrains his anger who restrains it when it can injure; when it cannot injure, what does it matter whether he restrain it, or not ?","r":"One who guards his anger where it can take effect truly guards it; where it can't, what does guarding or not guarding matter?","n":"Virtue is located only where power is. Patience toward those who could crush you may be just caution, and the verse declines to count it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["vekuli","samanilai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:31"},"k:302":{"book":"kural","num":302,"t":["செல்லா இடத்துச் சினந்தீது செல்லிடத்தும்","இல்அதனின் தீய பிற"],"p":"Anger is bad, even when it cannot injure; when it can injure; there is no greater evil.","r":"Anger is bad where it can't take effect; where it can, nothing else is worse.","n":"This closes the gap left by 301, which could be misread as permission to rage where it doesn't matter. Parimelazhagar sets out why: at the strong it rebounds, and at the weak it brings both blame and sin.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["vekuli"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:31"},"k:303":{"book":"kural","num":303,"t":["மறத்தல் வெகுளியை யார்மாட்டும் தீய","பிறத்தல் அதனான் வரும்"],"p":"Forget anger towards every one, as fountains of evil spring from it.","r":"Forget anger at anyone at all; evils are born from it.","n":"The verb is மறத்தல், to forget, not to suppress: the instruction asks for anger to go out of mind and not to be held down. Pope's 'trespass' moves the forgetting from the anger to the offence, which the Tamil doesn't do.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["vekuli"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:31"},"k:304":{"book":"kural","num":304,"t":["நகையும் உவகையும் கொல்லும் சினத்தின்","பகையும் உளவோ பிற"],"p":"Is there a greater enemy than anger, which kills both laughter and joy ?","r":"Is there any other enemy than the anger that kills both the smile and the joy?","n":"Parimelazhagar assigns the smile to the face and the joy to the heart, so anger destroys both outer and inner. He adds that renunciants have no outer enemies, which is why this inner one stands alone.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:31"},"k:305":{"book":"kural","num":305,"t":["தன்னைத்தான் காக்கின் சினங்காக்க காவாக்கால்","தன்னையே கொல்லுஞ் சினம்"],"p":"If a man would guard himself, let him guard against anger; if he do not guard it, anger will kill him.","r":"If you would guard yourself, guard against anger; if you don't, it is yourself that anger kills.","n":"தன்னையே, 'yourself and no other', turns anger's target around. Parimelazhagar reads the verse in light of curses: the ascetic who spends his power cursing loses it, so 'kills' means extreme suffering. Manakkudavar takes it as a threat to life.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vekuli"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","'Kills' means intense suffering: the ascetic loses his austerity by cursing and is back in the misery of births.","கொலைச்சொல் ஈண்டுத் துன்பமிகுதி உணர்த்தி நின்றது."],["manakkudavar","Anger endangers life itself.","இஃது உயிர்க்கேடு வருமென்றது."]],"ch":"k:31"},"k:306":{"book":"kural","num":306,"t":["சினமென்னும் சேர்ந்தாரைக் கொல்லி இனமென்னும்","ஏமப் புணையைச் சுடும்"],"p":"The fire of anger will burn up even the pleasant raft of friendship.","r":"Anger, the fire that kills what it comes near, burns even the safe raft of one's own people.","n":"The name சேர்ந்தாரைக் கொல்லி is a coinage for fire, 'killer of those it joins'. Parimelazhagar finds a contrast with ordinary fire, which burns only where it touches. It also matters that a raft is being burned by someone who needs it to survive.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","sutram"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","இனம் means fellow renunciants who teach, the raft that crosses the sea of births to release.","முற்றத் துறந்து தவஞானங்களால் பெரியயராய்க் கேட்டார்க்கு உறுதி பயக்கும் மொழிகளை இனியவாகச் சொல்லுவாரை."],["manakkudavar","இனம் means one's friends, who bring one ashore from a sea of sorrow.","தன்னைக் கரையேற விடுகின்ற நட்டோராகிய புணையைச் சுடும்."],["kalaignar","இனம் means one's kin.","அவரைப் பாதுகாக்கும் தோணி போன்ற சுற்றத்தையும் அழித்துவிடும்."]],"ch":"k:31"},"k:307":{"book":"kural","num":307,"t":["சினத்தைப் பொருளென்று கொண்டவன் கேடு","நிலத்தறைந்தான் கைபிழையா தற்று"],"p":"Destruction will come upon him who ragards anger as a good thing, as surely as the hand of him who strikes the ground will not fail.","r":"The ruin of one who takes anger to be a thing of worth is like the hand of one who strikes the ground, which can't miss.","n":"பொருள் also varies: Parimelazhagar makes it a quality that displays one's power, citing Vaisesika usage, and Manakkudavar seems to take it as wealth when he speaks of loss of goods. The image is a fine one because the ground is untouched by the blow.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The image is about certainty: the ruin can no more miss than a hand can miss the ground.","நிலத்தின்கண் அறைந்த அவன் கை அந்நிலத்தையுறுத்தல் தப்பாதவாறு போலத் தப்பாது."],["kalaignar","The image is about rebound: it is the striker's own hand that hurts.","நிலத்தைக் கையால் அறைந்தவனுக்கு அவன் கைதான் வலிக்கும்."]],"ch":"k:31"},"k:308":{"book":"kural","num":308,"t":["இணர்எரி தோய்வன்ன இன்னா செயினும்","புணரின் வெகுளாமை நன்று"],"p":"Though one commit things against you as painful (to bear) as if a bundle of fire had been thrust upon you, it will be well, to refrain, if possible, from anger.","r":"Even if harm is done to you like the touch of massed flames, not being angry, if you can manage it, is good.","n":"புணரின், 'if it can be managed', is a rare concession in such a chapter. Parimelazhagar notes that such treatment would anger even sages. The commandment admits its own difficulty.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["vekuli","samanilai","innaa"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:31"},"k:309":{"book":"kural","num":309,"t":["உள்ளிய தெல்லாம் உடனெய்தும் உள்ளத்தால்","உள்ளான் வெகுளி எனின்"],"p":"If a man never indulges anger in his heart, he will at once obtain whatever he has thought of.","r":"All one has thought of comes together at once, if one doesn't think of anger in the heart.","n":"The root உள் runs through the whole verse: thought, heart, not thinking. It sets one kind of thinking against another. The promise is enormous, and Parimelazhagar assigns it to the ascetic, though the verse itself doesn't.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:31"},"k:310":{"book":"kural","num":310,"t":["இறந்தார் இறந்தார் அனையர் சினத்தைத்","துறந்தார் துறந்தார் துணை"],"p":"Those, who give way to excessive anger, are no better than dead men; but those, who are freed from it, are equal to those who are freed (from death).","r":"Those who go to excess in anger are like the dead; those who renounce anger are equal to those who have renounced.","n":"Wordplay carries the argument: இறந்தார் is both 'those who exceed' and 'those who have died'. துறந்தார் doubles in the same way, so a single renunciation is set equal to total renunciation.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","thuravu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","They equal those who have attained release, since knowledge is certain for them though they still have a mortal body.","'வீடு பெற்றாரோடு ஒப்பர்'"],["manakkudavar","They equal those who have given up all possessions.","அதனை யொழிந் தார் எல்லாப் பொருளையுந் துறந்தாரோடு ஒப்பர்,"]],"ch":"k:31"},"k:311":{"book":"kural","num":311,"t":["சிறப்பீனும் செல்வம் பெறினும் பிறர்க்கு இன்னா","செய்யாமை மாசற்றார் கோள்"],"p":"It is the determination of the spotless not to cause sorrow to others, although they could (by so causing) obtain the wealth which confers greatness.","r":"Even if it would bring wealth that gives eminence, not hurting others is the settled stand of the stainless.","n":"கோள் is a stance held, not a rule obeyed. The verse puts the temptation first and at its strongest, then refuses it in a word.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["innaa","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The prize is the yogic powers such as becoming minute, so the temptation is an ascetic one.","யோகமாகிய சிறப்புத் தரும் அணிமா முதலிய செல்வங்களைப்"],["manakkudavar","The prize is ordinary wealth, refused even when it would bring no blame.","இது பழி வாராத செல்வம் பெறினும் தவிர வேண்டு மென்றது."]],"ch":"k:32"},"k:312":{"book":"kural","num":312,"t":["கறுத்துஇன்னா செய்தவக் கண்ணும் மறுத்தின்னா","செய்யாமை மாசற்றார் கோள்"],"p":"It is the determination of the spotless not to do evil, even in return, to those who have cherished enmity and done them evil.","r":"Even when someone in hatred has done them hurt, not hurting in return is the settled stand of the stainless.","n":"Manakkudavar supplies a detail that alters the verse's pairing with 313: here the other person's anger was aroused by one's own fault, whereas 313 treats enmity with no cause. On his view, the two verses together cover both provoked and unprovoked enemies.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["innaa","samanilai"],"pos":[["manakkudavar","The attacker has been angered by a fault of one's own.","தாஞ்செய்த குற்றத்தினாலே வெகுண்டு,"]],"ch":"k:32"},"k:313":{"book":"kural","num":313,"t":["செய்யாமல் செற்றார்க்கும் இன்னாத செய்தபின்","உய்யா விழுமந் தரும்"],"p":"In an ascetic inflict suffering even on those who hate him, when he has not done them any evil, it will afterwards give him irretrievable sorrow.","r":"Doing hurt, even to those who hated you though you did nothing, brings sorrow there is no escaping.","n":"The verse chooses the case where retaliation looks most justified, an unprovoked enemy, and says it still rebounds. Parimelazhagar explains the sorrow by an ascetic's loss of austerity and takes the actor to be a renunciant, as Pope's gloss follows him in doing, but the verse doesn't name one.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["innaa","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:32"},"k:314":{"book":"kural","num":314,"t":["இன்னாசெய் தாரை ஒறுத்தல் அவர்நாண","நன்னயஞ் செய்து விடல்"],"p":"The (proper) punishment to those who have done evil (to you), is to put them to shame by showing them kindness, in return and to forget both the evil and the good done on both sides.","r":"The way to punish those who have hurt you is to shame them by doing them good, and then let it go.","n":"ஒறுத்தல், to punish, is kept: retribution isn't abolished but redefined, and shame remains its instrument, so this isn't quite unconditional love. விடல் adds that one should leave the matter there, without savouring the effect.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["innaa","nayan","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:32"},"k:315":{"book":"kural","num":315,"t":["அறிவினான் ஆகுவ துண்டோ பிறிதின்நோய்","தந்நோய்போல் போற்றாக் கடை"],"p":"What benefit has he derived from his knowledge, who does not endeavour to keep off pain from another as much as from himself ?","r":"What comes of understanding, if one doesn't guard against another's pain as one's own?","n":"பிறிதின், 'of another', is neuter and so can reach beyond humans. போற்று is active guarding, so understanding that doesn't produce care is judged to have no result at all.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","innaa","arul"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:32"},"k:316":{"book":"kural","num":316,"t":["இன்னா எனத்தான் உணர்ந்தவை துன்னாமை","வேண்டும் பிறன்கண் செயல்"],"p":"Let not a man consent to do those things to another which, he knows, will cause sorrow.","r":"What one has perceived to be painful, one must not come near doing to another.","n":"A negative golden rule, but with உணர்ந்தவை, what has been felt or perceived, as the basis rather than a principle. Pope's couplet says 'his own soul has felt', and Kalaignar too says known through experience, while Manakkudavar only says known.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["innaa","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:32"},"k:317":{"book":"kural","num":317,"t":["எனைத்தானும் எஞ்ஞான்றும் யார்க்கும் மனத்தானாம்","மாணாசெய் யாமை தலை"],"p":"It is the chief of all virtues not knowingly to do any person evil, even in the lowest degree, and at any time.","r":"Not doing ill of any degree, at any time, to anyone, with the mind, is the highest thing.","n":"Three universals pile up before the verse arrives at மனத்தானாம், which pulls in two directions. Manakkudavar and Kalaignar extend the ban to harm even in thought, while Pope's 'wilful' and 'knowingly' limit it to harm that is intended.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["innaa","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:32"},"k:318":{"book":"kural","num":318,"t":["தன்னுயிர்ககு ஏன்னாமை தானறிவான் என்கொலோ","மன்னுயிர்க்கு இன்னா செயல்"],"p":"Why does a man inflict upon other creatures those sufferings, which he has found by experience are sufferings to himself ?","r":"Why does one who knows what is hurtful to his own life do hurtful things to other living beings?","n":"This is posed as a real question and isn't answered. Manakkudavar's touch is that a person differs from unconscious objects in knowing his pain, which is exactly why this conduct is puzzling.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["innaa","arul","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:32"},"k:319":{"book":"kural","num":319,"t":["பிறர்க்கின்னா முற்பகல் செய்யின் தமக்கு இன்னா","பிற்பகல் தாமே வரும்"],"p":"If a man inflict sorrow upon others in the morning, it will come upon him unsought in the very evening.","r":"If you do hurt to others in the forenoon, hurts to you will come in the afternoon, of themselves.","n":"தாமே, 'of themselves', removes any agent: as Manakkudavar says, nobody else need do it. The speed is rhetorical, since the verse is not a report on timing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["innaa","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:32"},"k:320":{"book":"kural","num":320,"t":["நோயெல்லாம் நோய்செய்தார் மேலவாம் நோய்செய்யார்","நோயின்மை வேண்டு பவர்"],"p":"Sorrow will come upon those who cause pain to others; therfore those, who desire to be free from sorrow, give no pain to others.","r":"All pains rest on those who caused pain; those who want to be without pain cause none.","n":"நோய் occurs four times. Taken strictly, 'all pains' has a hard consequence: anyone who suffers must have caused suffering. Manakkudavar accepts it by a time scheme in which present pain derives from past harm, and in that way this chapter leads into the claim of 330.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["innaa","vinai"],"pos":[["manakkudavar","Present suffering comes from past harm, and harming no one now protects the future.","இக் காலத்து நுகர்கின்ற துன்பமெல்லாம் முற்காலத்துப் பிறர்க் குத் துன்பம் செய்தார் மாட்டே யுளவாம் ;"]],"ch":"k:32"},"k:321":{"book":"kural","num":321,"t":["அறவினை யாதெனின் கொல்லாமை கோறல்","பிறவினை எல்லாந் தரும்"],"p":"Never to destroy life is the sum of all virtuous conduct. The destruction of life leads to every evil.","r":"If you ask what virtuous action is, it is not killing; killing brings all the other kind of deed.","n":"பிறவினை, 'other deeds', leaves the wrongness to be inferred. Parimelazhagar takes it as a claim about results: the suffering killing yields exceeds that of all other sins combined.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","aram","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:33"},"k:322":{"book":"kural","num":322,"t":["பகுத்துண்டு பல்லுயிர் ஓம்புதல் நூலோர்","தொகுத்தவற்றுள் எல்லாந் தலை"],"p":"The chief of all (the virtues) which authors have summed up, is the partaking of food that has been shared with others, and the preservation of the mainfold life of other creatures.","r":"Sharing one's food and sustaining many lives is the chief of all that the authors have gathered.","n":"Inside a chapter on not killing, the crown goes to a positive act of feeding, so non-harm here isn't only refraining. Parimelazhagar assigns the summary to renunciants, Manakkudavar says it is good for all faiths, and Kalaignar reads an egalitarian creed into it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","eegai","arul"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:33"},"k:323":{"book":"kural","num":323,"t":["ஒன்றாக நல்லது கொல்லாமை மற்றதன்","பின்சாரப் பொய்யாமை நன்று"],"p":"Not to destroy life is an incomparably (great) good next to it in goodness ranks freedom from falsehood.","r":"Good beyond compare is not killing; following behind it, not lying is good.","n":"A rare moment of the Kural ranking two of its own highest claims. Parimelazhagar recognizes this: since 297 and 300 raised doubt about which is greater, this verse settles it, and it can do so because truth depends on results and therefore shifts.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","vaaimai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The verse exists to settle a doubt that the chapter on truth raised.","இரண்டு அறந்துள்ளும் யாது சிறந்தது என்று ஐயம் நிகழுமன்றே!"]],"ch":"k:33"},"k:324":{"book":"kural","num":324,"t":["நல்லாறு எனப்படுவது யாதெனின் யாதொன்றும்","கொல்லாமை சூழும் நெறி"],"p":"Good path is that which considers how it may avoid killing any creature.","r":"If you ask what is called the good path, it is the way that keeps in view not killing anything at all.","n":"The question form follows 291 and 321, as if the Kural is defining terms. யாதொன்றும் is wide, and Parimelazhagar says it takes in even one-sensed life. சூழும் makes it a matter of constant attention rather than a single vow.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:33"},"k:325":{"book":"kural","num":325,"t":["நிலைஅஞ்சி நீத்தாருள் எல்லாம் கொலைஅஞ்சிக்","கொல்லாமை சூழ்வான் தலை"],"p":"Of all those who, fearing the permanence of earthly births, have abandoned desire, he is the chief who, fearing (the guilt of) murder, considers how he may avoid the destruction of life.","r":"Among all who renounced out of fear of their condition, the one who fears killing and keeps not killing in view is chief.","n":"Two fears are set side by side with the repeated அஞ்சி, one about one's own state and the other about another's life. The second outranks the first.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kollaamai","thuravu","acham"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","He is the highest within the class of renunciants, who differ by sect.","கொலைப் பாவத்தை அஞ்சிக் கொல்லாமை ஆகிய அறத்தை மறவாதவன் உயர்ந்தவன்."],["manakkudavar","He may stay a householder and still be above all who renounce.","கொல்லாமையைச் சிந்திப்பான் தலைமை யுடையவன்; இல் வாழ்க்கையில் நிற்பினும்,"]],"ch":"k:33"},"k:326":{"book":"kural","num":326,"t":["கொல்லாமை மேற்கொண் டொழுகுவான் வாழ்நாள்மேல்","செல்லாது உயிருண்ணுங் கூற்று"],"p":"Yama, the destroyer of life, will not attack the life of him, who acts under the determination of never destroying life.","r":"Death, the devourer of life, does not advance on the days of one who lives by not killing.","n":"The symmetry is exact: the one who won't kill is not killed, and the killer of all refrains here.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","piravi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It promises a full allotted span without interruption, which gives time for knowledge and release.","இப் பேரறம்செய்தான் தானும் கொல்லப்படான்; படானாகவே, அடியிற்கட்டிய வாழ்நாள் இடையூறின்றி எய்தும்"],["manakkudavar","Death doesn't come because there will be no further birth.","பிறவாமை யுண்டாமாதலால் கூற்றுச் செல்லாது என்றார்."],["kalaignar","It is a tribute: death itself hesitates, in awe.","சாவுகூட அவர் உயிரைப் பறிக்கத் தயங்கி நிற்கும்."]],"ch":"k:33"},"k:327":{"book":"kural","num":327,"t":["தன்னுயிர் நீப்பினும் செய்யற்க தான்பிறிது","இன்னுயிர் நீக்கும் வினை"],"p":"Let no one do that which would destroy the life of another, although he should by so doing, lose his own life.","r":"Even if your own life should go, do not do the deed that takes away another's sweet life.","n":"இன்னுயிர் gives the other's life the word for sweetness, while one's own is just உயிர். This is the Kural's hardest statement of the rule, and it allows no exception of necessity.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The case is a creature that would kill you: even then, because killing increases sin. He rejects a reading about ritual expiation.","'தன்னை அது கொல்லினும் தான் அதனைக் கொல்லற்க'"],["manakkudavar","The case is killing for medicine in mortal illness, which some people hold is no fault.","உயிர்க்குக் கேடுவருங் காலத்து நோய்க்கு மருந்தாகக் கொல்லுதல் குற்ற மன்று என் பார்க்கு, இது கூறப்பட்டது."]],"ch":"k:33"},"k:328":{"book":"kural","num":328,"t":["நன்றாகும் ஆக்கம் பெரிதெனினும் சான்றோர்க் குக்","கொன்றாகும் ஆக்கங் கடை"],"p":"The advantage which might flow from destroying life in sacrifice, is dishonourable to the wise (who renounced the world), even although it should be said to be productive of great good.","r":"However great the gain that would bring good, gain that comes by killing is the lowest for the worthy.","n":"The verse mentions no sacrifice. That whole issue comes from the older commentators and through them Pope's gloss.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The issue is Vedic sacrifice, valid for householders seeking heaven and base only for those seeking release, so injunction and prohibition don't clash.","துறக்கம் எய்துவார்க்கு ஆம் ஆயினும், வீடு எய்துவார்க்கு ஆகாது"],["manakkudavar","Killing in sacrifice is also not acceptable, as the great seek release and let ritual go.","வேள்வி யின் வரும் கொலையும் ஆகாதென்றது."],["kalaignar","It is any killing that would produce great benefit, and sacrifice isn't mentioned.","பெரிதாக நன்மை தரக்கூடிய அளவுக்கு ஒரு கொலை பயன்படக் கூடுமெனினும்,"]],"ch":"k:33"},"k:329":{"book":"kural","num":329,"t":["கொலைவினைய ராகிய மாக்கள் புலைவினையர்","புன்மை தெரிவா ரகத்து"],"p":"Men who destroy life are base men, in the estimation of those who know the nature of meanness.","r":"People whose work is killing are of base occupation in the eyes of those who discern what baseness is.","n":"Baseness is assigned by work, not birth, as both Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar stress, though the word புலை itself carries caste weight. Parimelazhagar's claim here that sacrifice isn't killing at all fits awkwardly with his note on 328.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","sirumai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The phrase 'whose work is killing' shows that killing in sacrifice isn't killing.","'கொலை வினையர்' என்றதனான், வேள்விக் கண் கொலையன்மை அறிக."]],"ch":"k:33"},"k:330":{"book":"kural","num":330,"t":["உயிர் உடம்பின் நீக்கியார் என்ப செயிர் உடம்பின்","செல்லாத்தீ வாழ்க்கை யவர்"],"p":"(The wise) will say that men of diseased bodies, who live in degradation and in poverty, are those who separated the life from the body of animals (in a former birth).","r":"Those in wretched bodies, leading an evil and failing life, they say, are people who separated life from body.","n":"என்ப, 'they say', is the verse's own hedge. It doesn't mention a former birth, which Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar supply. Its logic runs backward from a sufferer to his guilt, and that makes it one of the more troubling claims in this section.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kollaamai","vinai","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:33"},"k:331":{"book":"kural","num":331,"t":["நில்லாத வற்றை நிலையின என்றுணரும்","புல்லறி வாண்மை கடை"],"p":"That ignorance which considers those things to be stable which are not so, is dishonourable (to the wise).","r":"The paltry understanding that takes what doesn't last for what lasts is the lowest.","n":"The fault is cognitive before it is moral: a mistake of knowledge, sarcastically dignified with ஆண்மை, mastery. Parimelazhagar limits 'lowest' to those seeking release, for whom this error feeds attachment, and the verse doesn't state that limit.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:34"},"k:332":{"book":"kural","num":332,"t":["கூத்தாட்டு அவைக் குழாத் தற்றே பெருஞ்செல்வம்","போக்கும் அதுவிளிந் தற்று"],"p":"The acquisition of wealth is like the gathering together of an assembly for a theatre; its expenditure is like the breaking up of that assembly.","r":"The coming of great wealth is like a crowd gathering at a dance hall, and its going is like that crowd breaking up.","n":"A crowd has its own reason for being there, and Parimelazhagar presses this: wealth comes because of good deeds, of its own accord, and leaves when that cause is spent. So the owner is no more than the hall. His addition that this includes heavenly wealth is his own.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:34"},"k:333":{"book":"kural","num":333,"t":["அற்கா இயல்பிற்றுச் செல்வம் அதுபெற்றால்","அற்குப ஆங்கே செயல்"],"p":"Wealth is perishable; let those who obtain it immediately practise those (virtues) which are imperishable.","r":"Wealth has the nature of not staying; if you get it, do straight away what stays.","n":"அற்கா and அற்குப play on one root, so what doesn't stay can be converted into what does. For Parimelazhagar 'what stays' means worship and giving without expecting fruit, leading to release, though the verse doesn't name them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["nilaiyaamai","selvam","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:34"},"k:334":{"book":"kural","num":334,"t":["நாளென ஒன்றுபோற் காட்டி உயிர் ஈரும்","வாளது உணர்வார்ப் பெறின்"],"p":"Time, which shows itself (to the ignorant) as if it were something (real) is in the estimation of the wise (only) a saw which cuts down life.","r":"It shows itself as a thing called a day, but it is a blade that saws through life, if one can find those who perceive it.","n":"Parimelazhagar: the fool rejoices that time is passing, and doesn't realize it is his own time. He grammatically dismisses a rival division of the words at length. உணர்வார்ப் பெறின் admits how few will ever see it this way.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:34"},"k:335":{"book":"kural","num":335,"t":["நாச்செற்று விக்குள்மேல் வாராமுன் நல்வினை","மேற்சென்று செய்யப் படும்"],"p":"Let virtuous deeds be done quickly, before the biccup comes making the tongue silent.","r":"Before the hiccup rises and stops the tongue, good deeds must be rushed to.","n":"The image is the plain physical detail of the death rattle, with no metaphor. Parimelazhagar notes that it is the tongue being stopped, meaning that one can't even instruct others to act, and that no one knows when it will come.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:34"},"k:336":{"book":"kural","num":336,"t":["நெருநல் உளனொருவன் இன்றில்லை என்னும்","பெருமை உடைத்துஇவ் வுலகு"],"p":"This world possesses the greatness that one who yesterday was is not today.","r":"This world has the greatness that one who was here yesterday isn't here today.","n":"பெருமை is bitter here. Parimelazhagar takes it as excess, an overflow of impermanence, while Manakkudavar simply repeats 'greatness'. Kalaignar makes it the world's arrogance, boasting of having killed. All three hear the irony, in different keys.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:34"},"k:337":{"book":"kural","num":337,"t":["ஒருபொழுதும் வாழ்வது அறியார் கருதுப","கோடியும் அல்ல பல"],"p":"Innumerable are the thoughts which occupy the mind of (the unwise), who know not that they shall live another moment.","r":"Those who can't know they will live one moment more have thoughts in the many crores.","n":"The disproportion, a moment against crores, is the joke. Parimelazhagar gives a delightful catalogue of what such people plan: acquiring, guarding, overcoming, and raising friends and ruining rivals.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","manam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The crores are thoughts and schemes.","கோடியளவும் அன்றி அதனினும் பலவாய நினைவுகளை நினையா நிற்பர் அறிவிலாதார்."],["manakkudavar","The crores are what they assume their lifespan to be.","தமது வாழ்நாளைக் கோடியுமல்ல, பலவாகக்கருதுவர் உலகத்தார்,"]],"ch":"k:34"},"k:338":{"book":"kural","num":338,"t":["குடம்பை தனித்து ஒழியப் புள்பறந் தற்றே","உடம்பொடு உயிரிடை நட்பு"],"p":"The love of the soul to the body is like (the love of) a bird to its egg which it flies away from and leaves empty.","r":"The friendship between body and life is like a bird flying off and leaving its குடம்பை alone.","n":"நட்பு, friendship, is heavily ironic: for Parimelazhagar there is none, only a union forced by deeds.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["nilaiyaamai","piravi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","குடம்பை is the egg, since a bird grows within it and never returns, and he explicitly rejects 'nest'.","முன் தனியாத முட்டை தனித்துக் கிடப்ப அதனுள் இருந்த புள்ளுப் பருவம் வந்துழிப் பறந்து போன தன்மைத்து,"],["manakkudavar","குடம்பை is the nest, and the point is that life leaves whenever it will.","கூடு தனியே கிடக்கப் புள்ளுப் பறந்து போனாற்போலும்;"],["kalaignar","It is the egg and the chick.","உடலுக்கும் உயிருக்கும் உள்ள உறவு முட்டைக்கும் பறவைக் குஞ்சுக்கும் உண்டான உறவு போன்றதுதான்."]],"ch":"k:34"},"k:339":{"book":"kural","num":339,"t":["உறங்கு வதுபோலுஞ் சாக்காடு உறங்கி","விழிப்பது போலும் பிறப்பு"],"p":"Death is like sleep; birth is like awaking from it.","r":"Death is like falling asleep, and birth is like waking after sleep.","n":"The verse is very short and calm, with nothing about what happens in between. Its tone can be taken as reassuring or as a further turn of the screw. Parimelazhagar says birth is included to show that impermanence is itself what is permanent.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","piravi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Death and birth alternate naturally and quickly, as sleep and waking do.","சாக்காடும் பிறப்பும் இயல்பாய்க் கடிதின் மாறிமாறி வரும்"],["manakkudavar","It is a statement of rebirth: the life that went is born again.","போன உயிர் மீண்டும் பிறக்கு மென்பதூஉம்,"],["kalaignar","There is no cycle: birth is the waking and death is a sleep with no waking.","திரும்ப விழிக்க முடியாத மீளா உறக்கம் கொள்வதே இறப்பு."]],"ch":"k:34"},"k:340":{"book":"kural","num":340,"t":["புக்கில் அமைந்தின்று கொல்லோ உடம்பினுள்","துச்சில் இருந்த உயிர்க்கு"],"p":"It seems as if the soul, which takes a temporary shelter in a body, had not attained a home.","r":"Has the life that sits as a lodger in the body not found a home of its own?","n":"துச்சில், a lodger's corner in another's house, is homely and sad, and the verse is a soft question rather than a declaration.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","piravi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","No body has ever been a permanent home to it, given how life comes and goes among bodies.","எஞ்ஞான்றும் இருப்பதோர் இல் இதுகாறும் அமைந்ததில்லை போலும்!"],["manakkudavar","The home is specifically the state of release.","புக்கில் என்பது முத்தி ஸ்தானம்."],["kalaignar","Once it leaves the body, life has no other shelter, which comes close to the reverse.","அதனைப் பிரிந்தால் வேறு புகலிடம் கிடையாது."]],"ch":"k:34"},"k:341":{"book":"kural","num":341,"t":["யாதனின் யாதனின் நீங்கியான் நோதல்","அதனின் அதனின் இலன்"],"p":"Whatever thing, a man has renounced, by that thing; he cannot suffer pain.","r":"From whatever, whatever one has left, from that, from that, one has no pain.","n":"The doubled words make this piecemeal, and Parimelazhagar accepts that: giving up everything at once is best, but each thing left removes its own pain. So renunciation isn't all or nothing in this verse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","pattru"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:35"},"k:342":{"book":"kural","num":342,"t":["வேண்டின் உண் டாகத் துறக்க துறந்தபின்","ஈண்டுஇயற் பால பல"],"p":"After a man has renounced (all things), there will still be many things in this world (which he may enjoy); if he should desire them, let him, while it is time abandon. (the world).","r":"If you want it, renounce while there is time; after renouncing, there are many things that come about here.","n":"The verse is elliptical. Parimelazhagar concedes that he has supplied both 'joys' and 'time', so much of what appears to be said is commentary. Its surprise, on his reading, is that renunciation brings joys in this life and not only the end of pain. It also starts with வேண்டின், 'if you want'.","x":1,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["thuravu","pattru"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Renounce in time, while young, so as to have these joys for longer.","அவ் இன்பங்களை வேண்டின், அவற்றைக் காலம் பெறத் துறக்க."],["manakkudavar","Renounce if you want gain for your own soul.","தன்னுயிர்க்கு ஆக்கம் உண்டாக வேண்டின்,"],["kalaignar","Renounce while everything is still there to give up.","எல்லாம் இருக்கும் போதே அவற்றைத் துறந்து விடுவானே யானால்,"]],"ch":"k:35"},"k:343":{"book":"kural","num":343,"t":["அடல்வேண்டும் ஐந்தன் புலத்தை விடல்வேண்டும்","வேண்டிய வெல்லாம் ஒருங்கு"],"p":"Let the five senses be destroyed; and at the same time, let everything be abandoned that (the ascetic) has (formerly) desired.","r":"The domain of the five must be crushed, and all that one has wanted must be let go at once.","n":"புலம் is the field of the senses, their enjoyment, and not the organs that Pope's gloss has destroyed. Parimelazhagar gives the reason for 'at once': sense enjoyment fed with objects only burns higher, like fire with fuel.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","pattru"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:35"},"k:344":{"book":"kural","num":344,"t":["இயல்பாகும் நோன்பிற்கொன்று இன்மை உடைமை","மயலாகும் மற்றும் பெயர்த்து"],"p":"To be altogether destitute is the proper condition of those who perform austerities; if they possess anything, it will change (their resolution) and bring them back to their confused state.","r":"Having nothing at all is natural to austerity; having anything brings back confusion all over again.","n":"Parimelazhagar's reading is psychological: keep one thing and everything given up returns through it. Manakkudavar links the confusion to further birth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","thavam","pattru"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:35"},"k:345":{"book":"kural","num":345,"t":["மற்றும் தொடர்ப்பாடு எவன்கொல் பிறப்பறுக்கல்","உற்றார்க்கு உடம்பும் மிகை"],"p":"What means the addition of other things those who are attempting to cut off (future) births, when even their body is too much (for them).","r":"For those set on cutting off births, even the body is excess; why then any further ties?","n":"The body is the one tool needed for the work and still counts as excess. Parimelazhagar divides it into gross and subtle bodies, a doctrine the verse doesn't state, and makes this the renunciation of the inner 'I'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","pattru","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:35"},"k:346":{"book":"kural","num":346,"t":["யான் எனது என்னும் செருக்கு அறுப்பான் வானோர்க்கு","உயர்ந்த உலகம் புகும்"],"p":"He who destroys the pride which says \"I\", \"mine\" will enter a world which is difficult even to the Gods to attain.","r":"One who cuts off the pride of saying 'I' and 'mine' enters a world higher than the gods'.","n":"செருக்கு is glossed as delusion by the older commentators, as Parimelazhagar also does at 201: for them, the problem is a false identification and not arrogant manners. The body isn't 'I' and goods aren't 'mine'.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","pattru"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","This is the world of release, beyond even the gods' reach.","வானோர்க்கும் எய்தற்கு அரிய வீட்டுலகத்தை எய்தும்."],["kalaignar","This is earthly fame greater than heaven-high fame.","வான்புகழையும் மிஞ்சுகின்ற உலகப் புகழுக்கு உரியவனாவான்."]],"ch":"k:35"},"k:347":{"book":"kural","num":347,"t":["பற்றி விடாஅ இடும்பைகள் பற்றினைப்","பற்றி விடாஅ தவர் க்கு"],"p":"Sorrows will never let go their hold of those who give not up their hold of desire.","r":"Griefs that hold and won't let go hold those who hold attachment and won't let go.","n":"The two lines use one verb for the person and the griefs, so that the grip one keeps and the grip one suffers are shown to be the same act.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pattru","thuravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:35"},"k:348":{"book":"kural","num":348,"t":["தலைப்பட்டார் தீரத் துறந்தார் மயங்கி","வலைப்பட்டார் மற்றை யவர்"],"p":"Those who have entirely renounced (all things and all desire) have obtained (absorption into God); all others wander in confusion, entangled in the net of (many) births.","r":"Those who have thoroughly renounced have got to the head; the rest, in confusion, are in the net.","n":"தலைப்பட்டார் doesn't say what has been reached, and the past tense suggests it is already done. Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar say release, Kalaignar says a high state, and Pope's gloss has 'absorption into God', which adds a theology the Tamil doesn't have.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","pattru","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:35"},"k:349":{"book":"kural","num":349,"t":["பற்றற்ற கண்ணே பிறப்பறுக்கும் மற்று","நிலையாமை காணப் படும்"],"p":"At the moment in which desire has been abandoned, (other) births will be cut off; when that has not been done, instability will be seen.","r":"The moment attachment ends it cuts off births; otherwise impermanence is what is seen.","n":"கண்ணே, 'at the very moment', makes release immediate, as an effect stops with its cause. Parimelazhagar takes மற்று as 'if not', and Pope's couplet and gloss diverge on it, the couplet making impermanence what is seen after the release, the gloss what is seen without it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pattru","piravi","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:35"},"k:350":{"book":"kural","num":350,"t":["பற்றுக பற்றற்றான் பற்றினை அப்பற்றைப்","பற்றுக பற்று விடற்கு"],"p":"Desire the desire of Him who is without desire; in order to renounce desire, desire that desire.","r":"Cling to the clinging of the one who has no clinging; cling to that clinging, in order to let clinging go.","n":"பற்று comes six times in seven words, and the chapter on letting go ends by commanding a holding on. A useful attachment is the ladder that lets one drop the others.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pattru","thuravu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The unattached one is the Lord, and what is held is the path to release that he taught.","எல்லாப் பொருளையும் பற்றி நின்றே பற்றற்ற இறைவன் ஓதிய வீட்டு நெறியை,"],["manakkudavar","What is held is a practice: meditative absorption.","பற்றற்றான் பற்றாவது தியான சமாதி."],["kalaignar","The unattached one is any person so unattached, and it is him one should hold to.","எதிலும் பற்றில்லாதவராக யார் இருக்கிறாரோ அவரிடம் மட்டும் பற்றுக் கொள்ள வேண்டும்."]],"ch":"k:35"},"k:351":{"book":"kural","num":351,"t":["பொருளல்ல வற்றைப் பொருளென்று உணரும்","மருளானாம் மாணாப் பிறப்பு"],"p":"Inglorious births are produced by the confusion (of mind) which considers those things to be real which are not real.","r":"A joyless birth comes from the confusion that perceives what isn't real as real.","n":"The verse doesn't say which things are unreal. Parimelazhagar fills this in as denying rebirth, the fruit of deeds, and God, adding the stump mistaken for a man and shell for silver. Kalaignar has no birth in view, only a life that goes poorly.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["meyyunarvu","piravi","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The false view is to deny rebirth, the fruit of deeds, and God.","அவ் விபரீத உணர்வாவது, மறுபிறப்பும், இருவினைப் பயனும், கடவுளும் இல்லை எனவும்,"]],"ch":"k:36"},"k:352":{"book":"kural","num":352,"t":["இருள்நீங்கி இன்பம் பயக்கும் மருள்நீங்கி","மாசறு காட்சி யவர்க்கு"],"p":"A clear, undimmed vision of things will deliver its possessors from the darkness of future births, and confer the felicity (of heaven).","r":"For those of stainless sight, who have left confusion behind, it removes darkness and brings joy.","n":"What is the darkness? Parimelazhagar says hell, Manakkudavar and Kalaignar say ignorance, and Pope's gloss says the darkness of future births. The verse leaves it as a word.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["meyyunarvu","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:36"},"k:353":{"book":"kural","num":353,"t":["ஐயத்தின் நீங்கித் தெளிந்தார்க்கு வையத்தின்","வானம் நணிய துடைத்து"],"p":"Heaven is nearer than earth to those men of purified minds who are freed from from doubt.","r":"For those who have left doubt and become clear, the sky is nearer than the earth they stand on.","n":"Doubt here is the obstacle in its own right. Parimelazhagar makes an unusually candid point: every school's texts refute the others, so which is true can only be settled through one's own experience. The image of the far as nearer than the near is very bold.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["meyyunarvu","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:36"},"k:354":{"book":"kural","num":354,"t":["ஐயுணர்வு எய்தியக் கண்ணும் பயமின்றே","மெய்யுணர்வு இல்லா தவர்க்கு"],"p":"Even those who have all the knowledge which can be attained by the five senses, will derive no benefit from it, if they are without a knowledge of the true nature of things.","r":"Even having gained the five-fold awareness, there is no benefit for those who lack true perception.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["meyyunarvu","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The five-fold awareness is the mind itself, gathered into concentration, and even this falls short.","ஐந்தாகிய உணர்வு: மனம்."],["manakkudavar","It is all that can be known by the five senses.","மெய் முதலாகிய பொறிகளைந்தினானும் அறியப்படுவனவெல்லாம் அறிந்தவிடத்தும்,"],["kalaignar","It is having subdued the five senses.","தமது ஐம்புலன்களையும் அடக்கி வெற்றி கண்டிருந்தாலும் கூட"]],"ch":"k:36"},"k:355":{"book":"kural","num":355,"t":["எப்பொருள் எத்தன்மைத் தாயினும் அப்பொருள்","மெய்ப்பொருள் காண்பது அறிவு"],"p":"(True) knowledge is the perception concerning every thing of whatever kind, that that thing is the true thing.","r":"Whatever a thing is and of whatever nature, seeing the true thing in that thing is understanding.","n":"One of the Kural's best-known definitions, and it is doctrinally open. Parimelazhagar removes a Chera king's rank, lineage, looks and names as conventions, down to the elements and beyond, while Kalaignar asks only that we not be fooled by appearances. It is both a metaphysical and a practical line.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["meyyunarvu","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","One discards the world's imputed conceptions and sees what remains.","பொருள் தோறும் உலகத்தார் கற்பித்துக் கொண்டு வழங்குகின்ற கற்பனைகளைக் கழித்து, நின்ற உண்மையைக் காண்பது"],["kalaignar","It is about not being taken in by outward looks.","வெளித்தோற்றத்தைப் பார்த்து ஏமாந்து விடாமல்,"]],"ch":"k:36"},"k:356":{"book":"kural","num":356,"t":["கற்றீண்டு மெய்ப்பொருள் கண்டார் தலைப்படுவர்","மற்றீண்டு வாரா நெறி"],"p":"They, who in this birth have learned to know the True Being, enter the road which returns not into this world.","r":"Those who have learned here and seen the true thing will reach the path of not coming back here.","n":"Here learning is an actual route to the truth, in contrast to 373. For Parimelazhagar this is hearing from an experienced teacher, the first of his three means.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["meyyunarvu","kalvi","piravi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","'Not coming back' means no return to birth.","மீண்டு இப்பிறப்பின்கண் வாராத நெறியை எய்துவர்."],["kalaignar","It means no return to the household life from renunciation.","மீண்டும் இல்லற வாழ்க்கையை விரும்ப மாட்டார்கள்."]],"ch":"k:36"},"k:357":{"book":"kural","num":357,"t":["ஓர்த்துள்ளம் உள்ளது உணரான் ஒருதலையாப்","பேர்த்துள்ள வேண்டா பிறப்பு"],"p":"Let it not be thought that there is another birth for him whose mind having thoroughly considered (all it has been taught) has known the True Being.","r":"If the mind ponders and knows with certainty what is, one needn't think again of birth.","n":"உள்ளது, 'what is', is the plainest of the chapter's names for the goal. This reading assumes the commentators' உணரின் over the verse text's உணரான், as noted in source_issues.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["meyyunarvu","piravi","manam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","There is no need to expect a further birth for such a person.","அவனுக்கு மாறிப் பிறப்புளதாக நினைக்க வேண்டா."],["kalaignar","Those who truly investigate won't believe in rebirth at all, which reverses the claim into a denial of the doctrine.","உண்மையை ஆராய்ந்து உறுதியாக உணர்பவர்கள் மீண்டும் பிறப்பு உண்டு எனக் கருத மாட்டார்கள்."]],"ch":"k:36"},"k:358":{"book":"kural","num":358,"t":["பிறப்பென்னும் பேதைமை நீங்கச் சிறப்பென்னும்","செம்பொருள் காண்பது அறிவு"],"p":"True knowledge consists in the removal of ignorance; which is (the cause of) births, and the perception of the True Being who is (the bestower of) heaven.","r":"Understanding is seeing the perfect thing called the excellent, so that the folly called birth departs.","n":"The verse calls birth itself a folly, an astonishing metaphor that all three commentators have to unpack, each differently. Pope's 'man's dignity' for சிறப்பு goes in a different direction from all of them.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["meyyunarvu","piravi","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It means the ignorance that is the first cause of birth.","பிறப்பிற்கு முதற்காரணமாய அவிச்சை கெட;"],["manakkudavar","The folly is thinking that oneself is born and dies, when one's real standing has neither.","தான் பிறந்தானாக வும் செத்தானாகவும் கருதுகின்ற அறியாமையை விட்டுத்,"],["kalaignar","The ignorance is the very belief in a next birth.","அடுத்த பிறப்பு எனக் கூறப்படும் அறியாமையைப் போக்கித்"]],"ch":"k:36"},"k:359":{"book":"kural","num":359,"t":["சார்புணர்ந்து சார்பு கெடஒழுகின் மற்றழித்துச்","சார்தரா சார்தரு நோய்"],"p":"He who so lives as to know Him who is the support of all things and abandon all desire, will be freed from the evils which would otherwise cleave to him and destroy (his efforts after absorption).","r":"If one knows the support and lives so that supports perish, the pains due to cling won't destroy that life and cling.","n":"One word, சார்பு, does double service. Parimelazhagar adds that the fruit even of good deeds counts as 'pain' here, because it causes birth, which is a hard saying.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["meyyunarvu","vinai","pattru"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The support to be known is the supreme reality on which all rests, and the supports to end are the attachments.","எல்லாப் பொருட்கும் சார்பாய அச்செம்பொருளை உணர்ந்து,"],["manakkudavar","The support is what attaches to oneself, the dependence on deeds.","சார்பு - வினைச் சார்பு;"],["kalaignar","It simply means finding out the causes of one's troubles and letting go of them.","அத்துன்பங்களுக்குக் காரணமானவற்றை உணர்ந்து"]],"ch":"k:36"},"k:360":{"book":"kural","num":360,"t":["காமம் வெகுளி மயக்கம் இநவ்முன்றன்","நாமம் கெடக்கெடும் நோய்"],"p":"If the very names of these three things, desire, anger, and confusion of mind, be destroyed, then will also perish evils (which flow from them).","r":"When even the names of the three, desire, anger and confusion, perish, pain perishes.","n":"நாமம், 'the name', is what must go, so they aren't merely suppressed. This triad resembles the three poisons in Buddhist teaching, a comparison that is mine and no commentator's. Parimelazhagar derives it by reducing a Sanskrit list of five faults.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["meyyunarvu","kaamam","vekuli"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The Sanskrit list of five faults reduces to these three, since ego falls under ignorance and craving under desire.","இவர் அவற்றுள் அகங்காரம் அவிச்சைக்கண்ணும் அவாவுதல் ஆசைக்கண்ணும் அடங்குதலான், 'மூன்று' என்றார்."]],"ch":"k:36"},"k:361":{"book":"kural","num":361,"t":["அவாஎன்ப எல்லா உயிர்க்கும் எஞ் ஞான்றும்","தவாஅப் பிறப்பீனும் வித்து"],"p":"(The wise) say that the seed, which produces unceasing births, at all times, to all creatures, is desire.","r":"They say craving is the seed that, for all beings at all times, produces unceasing birth.","n":"என்ப, 'they say', is read by Parimelazhagar as marking agreement among all schools. He supplies a mechanism: at death, craving carries the life to its next state. Kalaignar keeps the seed but removes birth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["avaa","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:37"},"k:362":{"book":"kural","num":362,"t":["வேண்டுங்கால் வேண்டும் பிறவாமை மற்றது","வேண்டாமை வேண்ட வரும்"],"p":"If anything be desired, freedom from births should be desired; that (freedom from births) will be attained by desiring to be without desire.","r":"If you must want, want not to be born; that comes when you want not to want.","n":"A paradox as neat as 350. The verb வேண்டு occurs in every clause, and the verse doesn't pretend desire can simply be stopped. It redirects desire until it undoes itself.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avaa","piravi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Non-birth comes of itself to one who wants to want nothing.","அப்பிறவாமைதான் ஒரு பொருளையும் அவாவாமையை வேண்ட அவனுக்குத் தானே உண்டாம்."],["kalaignar","It is a warning: the misery that makes one wish one had never been born comes if desires aren't eliminated.","விரும்புவதானால் பிறக்காமலே இருந்திருக்க வேண்டும் என்று ஒருவன் எண்ணுகிற அளவுக்கு ஏற்படுகிற துன்ப நிலை, ஆசைகளை ஒழிக்காவிடில் வரும்."]],"ch":"k:37"},"k:363":{"book":"kural","num":363,"t":["வேண்டாமை அன்ன விழுச்செல்வம் ஈண்டில்லை","ஆண்டும் அஃதொப்பது இல்"],"p":"There is in this world no excellence equal to freedom from desire; and even in that world, there is nothing like it.","r":"There is no fine wealth here like not wanting, and nothing there equals it either.","n":"It is pointed to name wanting nothing as wealth. Parimelazhagar explains why it is the top: human and divine wealth always have something above them, so only not wanting has no superior.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avaa","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:37"},"k:364":{"book":"kural","num":364,"t":["தூஉய்மை என்பது அவாவின்மை மற்றது","வாஅய்மை வேண்ட வரும்"],"p":"Purity (of mind) consists in freedom from desire; and that (freedom from desire) is the fruit of the love of truth.","r":"Purity is the absence of craving, and that comes by wanting வாய்மை.","n":"The word தூய்மை also differs: for Parimelazhagar it is release itself, as the soul's stains are gone, and for Manakkudavar it is the removal of defilement.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["avaa","vaaimai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","வாய்மை here stands for the Supreme, who has truth, and wanting it means continuous contemplation.","மெய்ம்மையுடைய பரத்தை ஆகுபெயரால் 'மெய்ம்மை' என்றும் கூறினார்."],["manakkudavar","வாய்மை means speaking truth, and those who speak truth come to have no desire.","அவ்வாசை யின்மை மெய் சொல்லுதலை விரும்ப வரும்,"]],"ch":"k:37"},"k:365":{"book":"kural","num":365,"t":["அற்றவர் என்பார் அவாஅற்றார் மற்றையார்","அற்றாக அற்றது இலர்"],"p":"They are said to be free (from future birth) who are freed from desire; all others (who, whatever else they may be free from, are not freed from desire) are not thus free.","r":"Those called 'freed' are those freed of craving; the rest are not so freed.","n":"அற்றார் is left without an object, and that is the wit. Being cut free of other things is a partial matter, which Parimelazhagar makes explicit: others have only avoided some pains.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["avaa","thuravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:37"},"k:366":{"book":"kural","num":366,"t":["அஞ்சுவ தோரும் அறனே ஒருவனை","வஞ்சிப்ப தோரும் அவா"],"p":"It is the chief duty of (an ascetic) to watch against desire with (jealous) fear; for it has power to deceive (and destroy) him.","r":"It is virtue alone to fear; what deceives a person is craving.","n":"Craving is a confidence trick: Manakkudavar has it stand in front like a benefactor and then lead one down. Parimelazhagar has it slip in through a moment's inattention, even for someone close to release. So vigilance has no end point.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["avaa","acham","aram"],"pos":[["manakkudavar","It deceives by acting first as a friend.","வஞ்சனை செய்தல் - நன்றி செய்வாரைப் போல முன்னே நின்று , பின்னே தீக்கதியுள் உய்த்தல்."]],"ch":"k:37"},"k:367":{"book":"kural","num":367,"t":["அவாவினை ஆற்ற அறுப்பின் தவாவினை","தான்வேண்டு மாற்றான் வரும்"],"p":"If a man thoroughly cut off all desire, the deeds, which confer immortality, will come to him, in the path in which he seeks them.","r":"If one cuts off craving thoroughly, the deeds that don't fail come in the way one wishes.","n":"Parimelazhagar makes a bold antinomian comment: once craving is fully cut, no other virtue need be done, and everything one does is virtue. The verse says only that the right deeds arrive.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avaa","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:37"},"k:368":{"book":"kural","num":368,"t":["அவாஇல்லார்க் கில்லாகுந் துன்பம் அஃதுண்டேல்","தவாஅது மேன்மேல் வரும்"],"p":"There is no sorrow to those who are without desire; but where that is, (sorrow) will incessantly come, more and more.","r":"Those without craving have no sorrow; if it exists, sorrow keeps coming, unfailing, on and on.","n":"The claim seems too simple, since those without craving still get sick. Parimelazhagar sees this and limits it: the suffering that came with the body is already earned, and the verse means suffering created now.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["avaa","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:37"},"k:369":{"book":"kural","num":369,"t":["இன்பம் இடையறா தீண்டும் அவாவென்னும்","துன்பத்துள் துன்பங் கெடின்"],"p":"Even while in this body, joy will never depart (from the mind, in which) desire, that sorrow of sorrows, has been destroyed.","r":"Joy will be unbroken ஈண்டும், if the sorrow of sorrows called craving perishes.","n":"Craving is not just the cause of sorrow but is itself the worst sorrow. Parimelazhagar explains that it hurts both at its source and in its fruit.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avaa"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","ஈண்டும் means 'even here', so joy comes while still in the body.","அவன் வீடு பெற்ற வழியே அன்றி உடம்போடு நின்ற வழியும் இன்பம் இடையறாது."],["manakkudavar","ஈண்டும் means 'it swells', so joy increases without break, and Parimelazhagar notes this view.","இன்ப மானது இடையறாமல் வந்து மிகும்,"]],"ch":"k:37"},"k:370":{"book":"kural","num":370,"t":["ஆரா இயற்கை அவாநீப்பின் அந்நிலையே","பேரா இயற்கை தரும்"],"p":"The removal of desire, whose nature it is never to be satisfied, will immediately confer a nature that can never be changed.","r":"If one gives up craving, whose nature is never to be filled, right then it gives a nature that never shifts.","n":"The rhyme of ஆரா and பேரா sets an insatiable nature against an immovable one, and அந்நிலையே makes it immediate. Parimelazhagar calls this person liberated while living and quotes the Tiruvaymoli. The verse says only that the nature doesn't move.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avaa","thuravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:37"},"k:371":{"book":"kural","num":371,"t":["ஆகூழால் தோன்றும் அசைவின்மை கைப்பொருள்","போகூழால் தோன்றும் மடி"],"p":"Perseverance comes from a prosperous fate, and idleness from an adverse fate.","r":"A fate of gain produces tireless effort and a fate of loss produces sloth.","n":"Effort is presented as fate's product, not its rival, so the verse removes the usual escape route from fate.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","muyarchi","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Fate itself generates the effort and sloth that help make or lose wealth.","முயற்சி மடிகளையும் தானே தோற்றுவிக்கும்"],["kalaignar","A natural condition, not fate, gives rise to enthusiasm or laziness.","ஆக்கத்திற்கான இயற்கை நிலை"]],"ch":"k:38"},"k:372":{"book":"kural","num":372,"t":["பேதைப் படுக்கும் இழவூழ் அறிவகற்றும்","ஆகலூழ் உற்றக் கடை"],"p":"An adverse fate produces folly, and a prosperous fate produces enlarged knowledge.","r":"A fate of loss makes a person foolish, and a fate of gain, when it arrives, enlarges their understanding.","n":"Intelligence is not a fixed possession here; circumstances of fortune change it.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Fate alters even the understanding one is born with.","இயற்கையானாகிய அறிவையும் வேறுபடுக்கும்"],["kalaignar","Natural conditions of ruin and growth produce ignorance and understanding.","அழிவுதரும் இயற்கை நிலை, அறியாமையை உண்டாக்கும்"]],"ch":"k:38"},"k:373":{"book":"kural","num":373,"t":["நுண்ணிய நூல்பல கற்பினும் மற்றுந்தன்","உண்மை யறிவே மிகும்"],"p":"Although (a man) may study the most polished treatises, the knowledge which fate has decreed to him will still prevail.","r":"However many subtle books a person studies, their own understanding still prevails.","n":"The verse doesn't say whose understanding wins or why. The commentators fill that gap differently, and தன் உண்மை அறிவு carries the dispute.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","arivu","kalvi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The verse concerns one fated to folly, whose fate-given foolishness overrides what study taught.","தன் ஊழான் ஆகிய பேதைமை உணர்வே மேற்படும்"],["manakkudavar","Learning may be present, but understanding that comes from fate is stronger.","கல்வியுண்டாயினும் ஊழானாய்வறிவு வலியுடைத் தென்றார்"],["kalaignar","Natural intelligence rises above study, with no appeal to fate.","அவரது இயற்கை அறிவே மேலோங்கி நிற்கும்."]],"ch":"k:38"},"k:374":{"book":"kural","num":374,"t":["இருவேறு உலகத்து இயற்கை திருவேறு","தெள்ளிய ராதலும் வேறு"],"p":"There are (through fate) two different natures in the world, hence the difference (observable in men) in (their acquisition of) wealth, and in their attainment of knowledge.","r":"The world has two distinct natures: being wealthy is one thing, and being wise is another.","n":"Parimelazhagar draws a pointed conclusion: since the wise can be poor and others rich, wealth does not need wisdom as a cause.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","selvam","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:38"},"k:375":{"book":"kural","num":375,"t":["நல்லவை எல்லாஅந் தீயவாம் தீயவும்","நல்லவாம் செல்வம் செயற்கு"],"p":"In the acquisition of property, every thing favourable becomes unfavourable, and (on the other hand) everything unfavourable becomes favourable, (through the power of fate).","r":"In making wealth, all that is favourable can turn out badly and all that is unfavourable can turn out well.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The 'good' and 'bad' are favourable conditions such as time, place, means and action, which fate overturns.","'நல்லவை' 'தீயவை' என்பன காலமும், இடனும், கருவியும், தொழிலும் முதலாயவற்றை."],["kalaignar","Good and bad actions can end in the opposite result, which is simply the natural course.","நல்ல செயல்களை ஆற்ற முற்படும்போது அவை தீமையில் போய் முடிந்துவிடுவதும்"]],"ch":"k:38"},"k:376":{"book":"kural","num":376,"t":["பரியினும் ஆகாவாம் பாலல்ல உய்த்துச்","சொரியினும் போகா தம"],"p":"Whatever is not conferred by fate cannot be preserved although it be guarded with most painful care; and that, which fate has made his, cannot be lost, although one should even take it and throw it away.","r":"What is not yours will not stay however carefully you guard it, and what is yours will not leave though you throw it away.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Possession and loss follow fate, not care or neglect.","பொருள்களின் நிலையும் போக்கும் ஊழினான் ஆவதல்லது, காப்பு இகழ்ச்சிகளான் ஆகா"],["kalaignar","The verse is about what one has a right to, not what fate allots.","தனக்கு உரிமையல்லாதவற்றை"]],"ch":"k:38"},"k:377":{"book":"kural","num":377,"t":["வகுத்தான் வகுத்த வகையல்லால் கோடி","தொகுத்தார்க்கு துய்த்தல் அரிது"],"p":"Even those who gather together millions will only enjoy them, as it has been determined by the disposer (of all things).","r":"Even those who have amassed millions can enjoy them only as the one who apportions has apportioned.","n":"வகுத்தான் gives fate a personal agent. This is one of the chapter's clearest points of disagreement about whether a cosmic agent is present.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","selvam","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The apportioner is a deity who gives each soul the fruit of its own deeds.","ஓர் உயிர் செய்த வினையின் பயன் பிறிதோர் உயிரின்கண் செல்லாமல் அவ்வுயிர்க்கே வகுத்தலின்"],["kalaignar","What is apportioned is an ordered way of life, and wealth is hard to enjoy without following it.","வகுத்து முறைப்படுத்திய வாழ்க்கை நெறியை ஒட்டி நடக்கா விட்டால்"]],"ch":"k:38"},"k:378":{"book":"kural","num":378,"t":["துறப்பார்மன் துப்புர வில்லார் உறற்பால","ஊட்டா கழியு மெனின்"],"p":"The destitute will renounce desire (and become ascetics), if (fate) do not make them suffer the hindrances to which they are liable, and they pass away.","r":"The destitute would renounce the world, if only what they are bound to suffer would pass without afflicting them.","n":"The irony is dry: the poor already lack what renunciation gives up, yet fate withholds even that peace.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","They could let go of what they lack anyway, but fate's power keeps them suffering through their attitude.","கருத்து வேறுபாட்டால் துன்பமுறுகின்றது ஊழின் வலியான்"],["kalaignar","If afflicting feelings left them, they would renounce; the obstacle is psychological.","தம்மை வருத்தக்கூடிய உணர்வுகள் வந்து வருத்தாமல் நீங்கிவிடுமானால்"]],"ch":"k:38"},"k:379":{"book":"kural","num":379,"t":["நன்றாங்கால் நல்லவாக் காண்பவர் அன்றாங்கால்","அல்லற் படுவ தெவன்?"],"p":"How is it that those, who are pleased with good fortune, trouble themselves when evil comes, (since both are equally the decree of fa te) ?","r":"Those who accept things as good when good comes: why do they grieve when it doesn't?","n":"After nine verses on fate's power, this one asks for a response. Fate here is the premise of equanimity.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","samanilai","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Both are one's own earlier deeds and can't be escaped, so accepting one and resisting the other is unwise.","தாமே முன் செய்து கொண்டமையானும், ஊட்டாது கழியாமையானும்"]],"ch":"k:38"},"k:380":{"book":"kural","num":380,"t":["ஊழிற் பெருவலி யாவுள மற்றொன்று","சூழினுந் தான்முந் துறும்"],"p":"What is stronger than fate ? If we think of an expedient (to avert it), it will itself be with us before (the thought).","r":"What is stronger than fate? Even when one devises another way, it gets there first.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","muyarchi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Fate can come through the very plan meant to avert it.","தான் அவ்வுபாயமேயானும் பிறிதொன்றானும் வழியாக வந்து"],["kalaignar","The natural state reasserts itself over artificial arrangements.","இயற்கை நிலையே முதன்மையாக வந்து நிற்பதால்"]],"ch":"k:38"},"k:381":{"book":"kural","num":381,"t":["படைகுடி கூழ்அமைச்சு நட்பரண் ஆறும்","உடையான் அரசருள் ஏறு"],"p":"He who possesses these six things, an army, a people, wealth, ministers, friends and a fortress, is a lion among kings.","r":"One who has all six, an army, a people, resources, ministers, allies and a fort, stands among kings as the male leads its herd.","n":"ஏறு names the male of a species, not specifically a lion. Pope's and Kalaignar's lion is an interpretive choice. Parimelazhagar presses ஆறும்: lacking even one limb, royal policy doesn't function.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["arasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:39"},"k:382":{"book":"kural","num":382,"t":["அஞ்சாமை ஈகை அறிவூக்கம் இந்நான்கும்","எஞ்சாமை வேந்தர்க் கியல்பு"],"p":"Never to fail in these four things, fearlessness, liberality, wisdom, and energy, is the kingly character.","r":"Fearlessness, giving, understanding and drive, these four never failing, are the nature of a king.","n":"The weight is on எஞ்சாமை. Parimelazhagar explains that when one quality surfaces in a person others recede, and a king can't afford that, so the verse asks for constancy as well as possession.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["arasu","eegai","arivu","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:39"},"k:383":{"book":"kural","num":383,"t":["தூங்காமை கல்வி துணிவுடைமை இம்மூன்றும்","நீங்கா நிலனான் பவர்க்கு"],"p":"These three things, viz., vigilance, learning, and bravery, should never be wanting in the ruler of a country.","r":"Promptness, learning and resolve: these three do not leave those who rule the land.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["arasu","kalvi","muyarchi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","துணிவு here stands for manly swiftness, acting quickly without regard to anything else.","ஆண்மையாவது, ஒன்றனையும் பாராது கடிதில் செய்வது"],["manakkudavar","துணிவு is a settled decision reached after examining the matter.","ஒரு பொருளை ஆராய்ந்து துணிதலுடைமையும்"]],"ch":"k:39"},"k:384":{"book":"kural","num":384,"t":["அறனிழுக்கா தல்லவை நீக்கி மறனிழுக்கா","மானம் உடைய தரசு"],"p":"He is a king who, with manly modesty, swerves not from virtue, and refrains from vice.","r":"A king does not stray from virtue, removes what is not virtue, and keeps an honour that does not stray from valour.","n":"The verse pairs அறன் with மறன், so virtue and martial valour stand side by side as parts of one honour. Parimelazhagar illustrates this honour with rules of war, such as not striking the young, the old or one who flees.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","aram","maanam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","What is removed is wrongdoing in his realm, such as killing and theft. It is a public duty.","அறனல்லவை தன் நாட்டின் கண்ணும் நிகழாமல் கடிந்து"],["manakkudavar","What is removed is desire and anger, the king's own inner faults.","அறமல்லாத காம வெகுளியைக் கடிந்து"]],"ch":"k:39"},"k:385":{"book":"kural","num":385,"t":["இயற்றலும் ஈட்டலுங் காத்தலும் காத்த","வகுத்தலும் வல்ல தரசு"],"p":"He is a king who is able to acquire (wealth), to lay it up, to guard, and to distribute it.","r":"A king is one able to create sources of wealth, gather it, guard it, and apportion what is guarded.","n":"Four verbs form a cycle that ends in distribution, not in holding. The verse doesn't say to whom. Parimelazhagar spreads the giving over virtue, wealth and pleasure, while Manakkudavar's example is equipping the army.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:39"},"k:386":{"book":"kural","num":386,"t":["காட்சிக் கெளியன் கடுஞ்சொல்லன் அல்லனேல்","மீக்கூறும் மன்னன் நிலம்"],"p":"The whole world will exalt the country of the king who is easy of access, and who is free from harsh language.","r":"If a king is easy to see and not harsh of speech, his land is spoken of above others.","n":"The grammar praises the land, not the king. Parimelazhagar notes that praising the land makes praising the king unnecessary, though he has to supply the world as the speaker.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","inchol"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:39"},"k:387":{"book":"kural","num":387,"t":["இன்சொலால் ஈத்தளிக்க வல்லார்க்குத் தன்சொலால்","தான்கண் டனைத்திவ் வுலகு"],"p":"The world will praise and submit itself to the mind of the king who is able to give with affability, and to protect all who come to him.","r":"For those who can give and protect with pleasant words, this world falls in with their word and their intent.","n":"The second line is compressed, and my reading of தன் சொலால் தான் கண்டனைத்து is tentative. Pleasant speech is named as what makes the giving work, not as an extra.","x":1,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["arasu","inchol","eegai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","சொல் means fame: the world, joined to his renown, becomes as much as he conceives.","இவ்வுலகம் தன் புகழோடு மேவித் தான் கருதிய அளவிற்றாம்"],["manakkudavar","சொல் means his command: by it the world lies in his control.","தன்னேவலாலே இவ்வுலகம் தான் கண்டாற்போலும் தன் வசத்தே கிடக்கும்"]],"ch":"k:39"},"k:388":{"book":"kural","num":388,"t":["முறைசெய்து காப்பாற்றும் மன்னவன் மக்கட்கு","இறையென்று வைக்கப் படும்"],"p":"That king, will be esteemed a God among men, who performs his own duties, and protects (his subjects).","r":"The ruler who does justice and protects is set apart as இறை for the people.","n":"வைக்கப்படும் is passive and conditional: the status is awarded for doing justice, not held by right of office.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","sengol"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Though a man by birth, by his deeds he is placed apart as a god for humans.","பிறப்பான் மகனேயாயினும், செய்வான் மக்கட்குக் கடவுள் என்று வேறு வைக்கப்படும்"],["manakkudavar","He is reckoned the lord of people, a political headship with no deification.","மனிதர்க்கு நாயக னென்று எண்ணப்படுவான்"],["kalaignar","He is honoured as the people's leader.","மக்களுக்குத் தலைவன் எனப் போற்றப் படுவான்."]],"ch":"k:39"},"k:389":{"book":"kural","num":389,"t":["செவிகைப்பச் சொற்பொறுக்கும் பண்புடை வேந்தன்","கவிகைக்கீழ்த் தங்கும் உலகு"],"p":"The whole world will dwell under the umbrella of the king, who can bear words that embitter the ear.","r":"The world rests under the parasol of a king with the character to bear words that are bitter to the ear.","n":"செவிகைப்ப moves a taste onto the ear, as Parimelazhagar points out. Tolerance of bitterness is presented as what brings the world under the parasol, which reverses the usual picture of power as not having to listen.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","panpu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The words are the rebukes of counsellors, borne for the good they will produce.","இடிக்கும் துணையாயினார் சொற்களைத் தன் செவி பொறாதாகவும்."],["manakkudavar","The words are reports of what others have done wrong, and the king forgives those offences. He also records a view that they are a priest's words.","பிறர் செய்த குற்றங்களைக் கேட்டு வைத்தும், அதனைப் பொறுக்கவல்ல குணமுடைய"]],"ch":"k:39"},"k:390":{"book":"kural","num":390,"t":["கொடையளி செங்கோல் குடியோம்பல் நான்கும்","உடையானாம் வேந்தர்க் கொளி"],"p":"He is the light of kings who has there four things, beneficence, benevolence, rectitude, and care for his people.","r":"One who has these four, giving, kindness, a straight sceptre and care of the people, is a light among kings.","n":"Manakkudavar reads all four as relief for struggling farmers, including seed and plough and deferred or waived dues. That makes the verse far more concrete than its abstract nouns suggest.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","sengol","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:39"},"k:391":{"book":"kural","num":391,"t":["கற்க கசடறக் கற்பவை கற்றபின்","நிற்க அதற்குத் தக"],"p":"Let a man learn thoroughly whatever he may learn, and let his conduct be worthy of his learning.","r":"Learn what is to be learned without flaw, and having learned, stand in keeping with it.","n":"நிற்க gives conduct a posture: the chapter's first verse makes learning unfinished until one stands by it. Parimelazhagar narrows கற்பவை to works on the aims of life, on the grounds that life is too short for the rest, but the verse leaves the scope open.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:40"},"k:392":{"book":"kural","num":392,"t":["எண்ணென்ப ஏனை எழுத்தென்ப இவ்விரண் டும்","கண்ணென்ப வாழும் உயிர்க்கு"],"p":"Letters and numbers are the two eyes of man.","r":"What are called numbers and what are called letters, these two, are called the eyes of living beings.","n":"The verse says வாழும் உயிர்க்கு and doesn't restrict it. Parimelazhagar limits it to beings of special understanding and treats the two as tools for seeing the aims of life; Kalaignar goes further and makes them the test of being alive at all.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:40"},"k:393":{"book":"kural","num":393,"t":["கண்ணுடையர் என்பவர் கற்றோர் முகத்திரண்டு","புண்ணுடையர் கல்லா தவர்"],"p":"The learned are said to have eyes, but the unlearned have (merely) two sores in their face.","r":"Those called people with eyes are the learned; the unlearned have two sores on their face.","n":"The insult works because the sore is in the same place and looks similar. Parimelazhagar adds that a merely physical eye gives pain through disease. Kalaignar draws an inference absent from the verse, that a blind person who is learned counts as having eyes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","kallaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:40"},"k:394":{"book":"kural","num":394,"t":["உவப்பத் தலைக்கூடி உள்ளப் பிரிதல்","அனைத்தே புலவர் தொழில்"],"p":"It is the part of the learned to give joy to those whom they meet, and on leaving, to make them think (Oh! when shall we meet them again.)","r":"To meet with delight and part leaving others thinking of you: such is the way of the learned.","n":"The measure of learning here is purely social, with no mention of knowledge. Manakkudavar makes it a comparison with two people who meet in joy and part in longing, an image close to love. Parimelazhagar's விளக்கம் opens the அத்தன்மைத்து to include right conduct and counsel.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","panpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:40"},"k:395":{"book":"kural","num":395,"t":["உடையார்முன் இல்லார்போல் ஏக்கற்றுங் கற்றார்","கடையரே கல்லா தவர்"],"p":"The unlearned are inferior to the learned, before whom they stand begging, as the destitute before the wealthy.","r":"Those who learned while yearning like the poor before the rich are the foremost; those who did not learn are the lowest.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","panivu","kallaamai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The learner stands humbled before the teacher and is thereby foremost. Those ashamed of that posture stay low for good, having lost true learning to false pride.","செல்வர்முன் நல்கூர்ந்தார் நிற்குமாறு போலத் தாமும் ஆசிரியர்முன் ஏக்கற்று நின்றும் கற்றார் தலையாயினார்"],["manakkudavar","It is the unlearned who stand yearning like the poor, and they rank beneath the learned.","அதனைக் காதலித்து நிற்றலுமன்றிக் கற்றாரிடத்தாவர் கல்லாதார்"]],"ch":"k:40"},"k:396":{"book":"kural","num":396,"t":["தொட்டனைத் தூறும் மணற்கேணி மாந்தர்க்குக்","கற்றனைத் தூறும் அறிவு"],"p":"Water will flow from a well in the sand in proportion to the depth to which it is dug, and knowledge will flow from a man in proportion to his learning.","r":"A well in sand gives water as far as it is dug; for people, understanding wells up as far as they have learned.","n":"ஊறும் matters: understanding seeps up from below rather than being poured in, so study opens something rather than simply filling a store. Parimelazhagar openly reconciles this with 373, saying it holds where fate doesn't oppose it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The verse holds where fate does not stand against it, and so it doesn't conflict with 373.","இஃது ஊழ் மாறு கொள்ளாவழியாகலின்"]],"ch":"k:40"},"k:397":{"book":"kural","num":397,"t":["யாதானும் நாடாமால் ஊராமால் என்னொருவன்","சாந்துணையுங் கல்லாத வாறு"],"p":"How is it that any one can remain without learning, even to his death, when (to the learned man) every country is his own (country), and every town his own (town) ?","r":"Any land becomes one's own, any town one's own; why then does a person stay unlearned until death?","n":"The verse states the belonging without naming who has it; the commentators supply the learned. Parimelazhagar's reason is reception: others' lands give the learned gifts and honour. A reader today may hear something more cosmopolitan than that.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","kallaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:40"},"k:398":{"book":"kural","num":398,"t":["ஒருமைக்கண் தான் கற்ற கல்வி ஒருவற்கு","எழுமையும் ஏமாப் புடைத்து"],"p":"The learning, which a man has acquired in one birth, will yield him pleasure during seven births.","r":"The learning one acquires in a single span serves as protection through seven.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","piravi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","ஒருமை and எழுமை are births: learning stays with the soul as deeds do, and follows it into each birth.","வினைகள்போல உயிரின்கண் கிடந்து அது புக்குழிப் புகும்"],["kalaignar","They are generations: what is learned in one generation protects seven generations of descendants.","ஒரு தலைமுறையில் பெறும் கல்வி அறிவனாது, ஏழேழு தலைமுறைக்கும் பாதுகாப்பாக அமையும்."]],"ch":"k:40"},"k:399":{"book":"kural","num":399,"t":["தாமின் புறுவது உலகின் புறக் கண்டு","காமுறுவர் கற்றறிந் தார்"],"p":"The learned will long (for more learning), when they see that while it gives pleasure to themselves, the world also derives pleasure from it.","r":"Seeing that what delights them delights the world, the learned come to love it all the more.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","What they desire further is learning itself, because it delights the world too. Unlike wealth, its pleasure gains from being shared.","கற்றறிந்தார் பின்னும் அதனையே விரும்புவர்"],["manakkudavar","The learned are glad when others enjoy what they enjoy. The verse is about freedom from envy, a virtue that learning brings.","இஃது அழுக்காறு செய்யாது இன்புறுதல் அறமாதலின், அது கல்வியானே வருமென்றது."]],"ch":"k:40"},"k:400":{"book":"kural","num":400,"t":["கேடில் விழுச்செல்வம் கல்வி யொருவற்கு","மாடல்ல மற்றை யவை"],"p":"Learning is the true imperishable riches; all other things are not riches.","r":"Learning is the imperishable, excellent wealth for a person; other things are not wealth.","n":"மாடு is a plain word for property and cattle, and the verse flatly denies that other possessions deserve it. Parimelazhagar defines the imperishability as more than safety from thieves and kings: learning doesn't decrease when given away.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:40"},"k:401":{"book":"kural","num":401,"t":["அரங்கின்றி வட்டாடி யற்றே நிரம்பிய","நூலின்றிக் கோட்டி கொளல்"],"p":"To speak in an assembly (of the learned) without fullness of knowledge, is like playing at chess (on a board) without squares.","r":"Holding forth in an assembly without complete learning is like playing a board game with no marked board.","n":"The image is exact: a game without a board isn't a bad game, because no move can mean anything. The fault is the absence of a shared frame, not lack of cleverness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kallaamai","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:41"},"k:402":{"book":"kural","num":402,"t":["கல்லாதான் சொற்கா முறுதல் முலையிரண்டும்","இல்லாதாள் பெண்காமுற் றற்று"],"p":"The desire of the unlearned to speak (in an assembly), is like a woman without breasts desiring (the enjoyment of ) woman-hood.","r":"For the unlearned to set their heart on speech is like desiring womanhood, or a woman, in one who lacks both breasts.","n":"A crude comparison that rests on a narrow view of women's bodies. It is one of the verses where the book's period shows, and it shouldn't be softened. The commentators disagree on who is desiring.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kallaamai","kalvi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The unlearned man himself longs to speak, and this is like such a woman longing for womanhood: it gets nowhere, or brings laughter.","கல்வியில்லாதவன் ஒருவன் அவையின்கண் ஒன்று சொல்லுதலை அவாவுதல்"],["kalaignar","The fault lies with the listener who wants to hear the unlearned, and this is like being infatuated with such a woman.","கல்லாதவனின் சொல்கேட்க விரும்புவது,"]],"ch":"k:41"},"k:403":{"book":"kural","num":403,"t":["கல்லா தவரும் நனிநல்லர் கற்றார்முன்","சொல்லா திருக்கப் பெறின்"],"p":"The unlearned also are very excellent men, if they know how to keep silence before the learned.","r":"Even the unlearned are very good, if only they can stay silent before the learned.","n":"Dry praise. Parimelazhagar takes பெறின் seriously: it marks the condition as hard to meet, since those who don't know themselves can't manage it. The verse's sting is that knowing when to be silent would itself take the knowledge they lack.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kallaamai","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:41"},"k:404":{"book":"kural","num":404,"t":["கல்லாதான் ஒட்பம் கழியநன் றாயினும்","கொள்ளார் அறிவுடை யார்"],"p":"Although the natural knowledge of an unlearned man may be very good, the wise will not accept for true knowledge.","r":"However excellent an unlearned person's brightness may be, the wise do not accept it.","n":"The verse grants the brightness and refuses it recognition, so this is a claim about what the wise will credit and not about what the person has.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kallaamai","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It isn't real understanding: any good result is a chance hit, like letters traced by accident.","ஏரலெழுத்துப் போல்வதோர் விழுக்காடு"],["kalaignar","The natural intelligence may be real. The wise simply won't accept him as distinguished in learning.","அவனைக் கல்வியில் சிறந்தோன் என்று அறிவுடையோர் ஒப்புக் கொள்ள மாட்டார்கள்."]],"ch":"k:41"},"k:405":{"book":"kural","num":405,"t":["கல்லா ஒருவன் தகைமை தலைப்பெய்து","சொல்லாடச் சோர்வு படும்"],"p":"The self-conceit of an unlearned man will fade away, as soon as he speaks in an assembly (of thelearned).","r":"An unlearned person's standing wilts once he meets and speaks.","n":"The verse doesn't say with whom; the commentators supply a learned person. தகைமை is taken three ways: Parimelazhagar as his self-estimate, Manakkudavar as real greatness, and Kalaignar as a false pose. The first and third make the loss self-delusion ending, while the second makes it a real loss.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kallaamai","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:41"},"k:406":{"book":"kural","num":406,"t":["உளரென்னும் மாத்திரையர் அல்லால் பயவாக்","களரனையர் கல்லா தவர்"],"p":"The unlearned are like worthless barren land: all that can be said of them is, that they exist.","r":"The unlearned can only be said to exist; beyond that they are like barren saline land that yields nothing.","n":"உளர் என்னும் மாத்திரையர் reduces a person to the bare fact of being said to exist. Kalaignar's walking corpses is stronger than the verse's image, which is about yield.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kallaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:41"},"k:407":{"book":"kural","num":407,"t":["நுண்மாண் நுழைபுலம் இல்லான் எழில்நலம்","மண்மாண் புனைபாவை யற்று"],"p":"The beauty and goodness of one who is destitute of knowledge by the study of great and exquisite works, is like (the beauty and goodness) of a painted earthen doll.","r":"The fine looks of one without subtle, penetrating understanding are like those of a well-made clay doll.","n":"Parimelazhagar concedes that a fine body is itself a rare fruit of birth, which makes the point less a scorn of beauty than a statement that it needs learning to be worth anything.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kallaamai","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:41"},"k:408":{"book":"kural","num":408,"t":["நல்லார்கண் பட்ட வறுமையின் இன்னாதே","கல்லார்கண் பட் ட திரு"],"p":"Wealth, gained by the unlearned, will give more sorrow than the poverty which may come upon the learned.","r":"Wealth lodged with the unlearned is more painful than poverty lodged with the good.","n":"The verse says நல்லார், the good, and opposes it to கல்லார், the unlearned. The pairing makes the learned and the good one group by sound and by implication. Parimelazhagar glosses நல்லார் as கற்றார் outright.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kallaamai","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Wealth ruins the unlearned owner, while poverty doesn't ruin the good. That is why it is more painful.","திருகல்லாரைக் கெடுக்க, வறுமை நல்லாரைக் கெடாது நிற்றலான்"],["manakkudavar","The harm falls on others: given wealth, the unlearned make other people suffer.","இது செல்வமுண்டாயின், பிறரைத் துன்பமுறுவிப்பரென்றது."]],"ch":"k:41"},"k:409":{"book":"kural","num":409,"t":["மேற்பிறந்தா ராயினும் கல்லாதார் கீழ்ப்பிறந்தும்","கற்றார் அனைத்திலர் பாடு"],"p":"The unlearned, though born in a high caste, are not equal in dignity to the learned; though they may have been born in a low caste.","r":"Though born high, the unlearned do not have the worth of those who are learned though born low.","n":"The verse overturns the ranking but keeps the words மேல் and கீழ், so birth rank remains as something to be overturned. The argument is not that the distinction is false, only that learning outweighs it.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","pirappu","kudi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Caste rank ends with the body while learning goes with the soul, so learning is superior. Caste rank itself is assumed.","உடலோடு ஒழியும் சாதி உயர்ச்சியினும், உயிரோடு செல்லும் கல்வி உயர்ச்சி சிறப்புடைத்து"],["kalaignar","The honour of learning removes the distinction of high and low altogether.","உயர்ந்தவர் தாழ்ந்தவர் என்ற வேறுபாட்டைப் போக்கிவிடும்."]],"ch":"k:41"},"k:410":{"book":"kural","num":410,"t":["விலங்கொடு மக்கள் அனையர் இலங்குநூல்","கற்றாரோடு ஏனை யவர்"],"p":"As beasts by the side of men, so are other men by the side of those who are learned in celebrated works.","r":"As beasts stand to people, so do others stand to those who have studied the shining books.","n":"The chapter ends by removing the unlearned from the human as such. Parimelazhagar's logic is that the surplus of awareness which marks humans over animals is found in the learned, so the two aren't of one birth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","kallaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:41"},"k:411":{"book":"kural","num":411,"t":["செல்வத்துட் செல்வஞ் செவிச்செல்வம் அச்செல்வம்","செல்வத்து ளெல்லாந் தலை"],"p":"Wealth (gained) by the ear is wealth of wealth; that wealth is the chief of all wealth.","r":"The wealth among wealths is the wealth of the ear; that wealth stands first among all wealth.","n":"The sound does the work, with செல்வம் in every half-line. After 400 has already denied other wealth the name, this verse places the ear at the head of that revalued wealth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kelvi","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:42"},"k:412":{"book":"kural","num":412,"t":["செவுக்குண வில்லாத போழ்து சிறிது","வயிற்றுக்கும் ஈயப் படும்"],"p":"When there is no food for the ear, give a little also to the stomach.","r":"When there is no food for the ear, a little may be given to the stomach too.","n":"The verse is witty, not ascetic. Parimelazhagar catches the two loaded words: சிறிது, and ஈயப்படும், which treats the stomach as a beggar to be given alms. It also says the food is given so that one can go on listening.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kelvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:42"},"k:413":{"book":"kural","num":413,"t":["செவியுணவிற் கேள்வி யுடையார் அவியுணவின்","ஆன்றாரோ டொப்பர் நிலத்து"],"p":"Those who in this world enjoy instruction which is the food of the ear, are equal to the Gods, who enjoy the food of the sacrifices.","r":"Those who have listening as food for the ear, though on earth, are equal to those fulfilled by the food of oblation.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kelvi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","ஆன்றார் here are the gods who receive sacrificial offerings. The listeners are their earthly equals.","நிலத்தின்கண்ணர் ஆயினும் அவியுணவினையுடைய தேவரொடு ஒப்பர்."],["kalaignar","ஆன்றார் are human sages who eat little and are full of understanding, with no sacrifice or gods.","குறைந்த உணவருந்தி நிறைந்த அறிவுடன் விளங்கும் ஆன்றோர்க்கு"]],"ch":"k:42"},"k:414":{"book":"kural","num":414,"t":["கற்றில னாயினுங் கேட்க அஃதொருவற்கு","ஒற்கத்தின் ஊற்றாந் துணை"],"p":"Although a man be without learning, let him listen (to the teaching of the learned); that will be to him a staff in adversity.","r":"Even if you haven't studied, listen; it will be a staff to lean on in one's faltering.","n":"Parimelazhagar notes that the உம்மை in ஆயினும் keeps study as the first duty. Listening is not offered as an equal path, only as what remains. He also makes the faltering inward: a mind shaken by poverty or ignorance.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kelvi","kalvi","thunai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:42"},"k:415":{"book":"kural","num":415,"t":["இழுக்கல் உடையுழி ஊற்றுக்கோல் அற்றே","ஒழுக்க முடையார்வாய்ச் சொல்"],"p":"The words of the good are like a staff in a slippery place.","r":"The words from the mouths of those of good conduct are like a staff on slippery ground.","n":"The authority comes from the speaker's conduct, not learning. Parimelazhagar makes the exclusion explicit: the learned without conduct are not to be heard at all. So the chapter's listening is listening to a kind of person.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kelvi","ozhukkam","thunai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:42"},"k:416":{"book":"kural","num":416,"t":["எனைத்தானும் நல்லவை கேட்க அனைத்தானும்","ஆன்ற பெருமை தரும்"],"p":"Let a man listen, never so little, to good (instruction), even that will bring him great dignity.","r":"Listen to good things, however little; that much too will give full greatness.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kelvi","perumai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Even a little brings full greatness. Like drops of rain, the listening accumulates, so don't despise the small.","அக்கேள்வி மழைத் துளைபோல வந்து ஈண்டு எல்லா அறிவுகளையும் உள ஆக்கலின்"],["kalaignar","Greatness comes in proportion: as much as one listens, that much greatness.","நல்லவற்றை எந்த அளவுக்குக் கேட்கிறோமோ அந்த அளவுக்குப் பெருமை கிடைக்கும்"]],"ch":"k:42"},"k:417":{"book":"kural","num":417,"t":["பிழைத் துணர்ந்தும் பேதைமை சொல்லா ரிழைத்துணர்ந்","தீண்டிய கேள்வி யவர்"],"p":"Not even when they have imperfectly understood (a matter), will those men speak foolishly, who have profoundly studied and diligently listened (to instruction).","r":"Those whose listening is gathered upon their own close investigation do not speak folly, even when they have misunderstood.","n":"A subtle claim: being wrong and speaking foolishly are different things. Such a person can err and still not talk nonsense. இழைத்துணர்ந்து comes before கேள்வி, and Parimelazhagar reads this as one's own investigation first, confirmed by consulting those who know.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kelvi","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:42"},"k:418":{"book":"kural","num":418,"t":["கேட்பினுங் கேளாத் தகையவே கேள்வியால்","தோட்கப் படாத செவி"],"p":"The ear which has not been bored by instruction, although it hears, is deaf.","r":"Ears not pierced by listening are deaf even though they hear.","n":"தோட்கப்படாத plays on pierced ears, ornament and all. Parimelazhagar's closing remark is apt: the old hole isn't a hole. Listening must make a new opening.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kelvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:42"},"k:419":{"book":"kural","num":419,"t":["நுணங்கிய கேள்விய ரல்லார் வணங்கிய","வாயின ராதல் அரிது"],"p":"It is a rare thing to find modesty, a reverend mouth- with those who have not received choice instruction.","r":"It is hard for those without fine listening to have a mouth that bows.","n":"The bowing is done by the mouth, so this is humility as a way of speaking. The causal claim is striking: humility comes from having listened, because those who haven't heard much admire themselves, as Parimelazhagar explains.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kelvi","panivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:42"},"k:420":{"book":"kural","num":420,"t":["செவியிற் சுவையுணரா வாயுணர்வின் மாக்கள்","அவியினும் வாழினும் என்?"],"p":"What does it matter whether those men live or die, who can judge of tastes by the mouth, and not by the ear ?","r":"What does it matter whether they die or live, these beings who know taste by the mouth and not by the ear?","n":"The verse doesn't say to whom it doesn't matter; both older commentators supply the world, which makes it a social judgment of uselessness, not a cosmic one. மாக்கள் rather than மக்கள் also seems to me a deliberate demotion. It may also echo அவி of 413, oblation there and perishing here, though that is my suggestion.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kelvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:42"},"k:421":{"book":"kural","num":421,"t":["அறிவற்றங் காக்குங் கருவி செறுவார்க்கும்","உள்ளழிக்க லாகா அரண்"],"p":"Wisdom is a weapon to ward off destruction; it is an inner fortress which enemies cannot destroy.","r":"Understanding is the tool that guards against அற்றம், and an inner fortress enemies can't destroy.","n":"The fort is one of the six limbs of a kingdom (381). This verse puts a fort inside the person, with உள் able to mean either inward or impenetrable from within, as Parimelazhagar notes.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","kutram"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","அற்றம் is final ruin, which understanding foresees and prevents.","அரசர்க்கு அறிவு என்பது இறுதி வாராமல் காக்கும் கருவியாம்"],["manakkudavar","அற்றம் is one's own weakness, and understanding covers one's faults, while the fort handles harm from others.","இது தனக்குள்ள குற்றத்தை மறைக்கு மென்றும், பிறரால் வரும் தீமை யைக் காக்குமென்றும்"]],"ch":"k:43"},"k:422":{"book":"kural","num":422,"t":["சென்ற இடத்தால் செலவிடா தீதொரீஇ","நன்றின்பால் உய்ப்ப தறிவு"],"p":"Not to permit the mind to go where it lists, to keep it from evil, and to employ it in good, this is wisdom.","r":"Understanding does not let one go wherever things have gone, but turns away from evil and steers toward good.","n":"The verse doesn't name what wanders. Both commentators supply the mind, but they disagree on what is held back and how far.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","manam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The mind itself is driven through the senses, as a groom drives a horse. Understanding is a separate quality of the soul.","குதிரையை நிலமறிந்து செலுத்தும் வாதுவன்போல வேறாக்கி மனத்தைப் புலமறிந்து செலுத்துவது அறிவு"],["manakkudavar","The mind may go, but the body must not follow it, and the verse chiefly concerns sexual pleasure.","உள்ளஞ் சென்ற விடத்தே உடம்பையுஞ் செல்லவிடாது"]],"ch":"k:43"},"k:423":{"book":"kural","num":423,"t":["எப்பொருள் யார்யார்வாய்க் கேட்பினும் அப்பொருள்","மெய்ப்பொருள் காண்ப தறிவு"],"p":"To discern the truth in every thing, by whomsoever spoken, is wisdom.","r":"Whatever is heard, from whoever's mouth, understanding is seeing the true substance of that very thing.","n":"The repeated யார்யார் removes the speaker from the test. Parimelazhagar gives the sharp consequence: high matters may come from low mouths, harmful ones from friends. He takes மெய்ப்பொருள் as the thing's true use, not its truth in the abstract, which Manakkudavar and Kalaignar prefer.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","kelvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:43"},"k:424":{"book":"kural","num":424,"t":["எண்பொருள வாகச் செலச்சொல்லித் தான்பிறர்வாய்","நுண்பொருள் காண்ப தறிவு"],"p":"To speak so as that the meaning may easily enter the mind of the hearer, and to discern the subtlest thought which may lie hidden in the words of others, this is wisdom.","r":"Understanding is saying things so they go in easily, while seeing the subtle meaning in what comes from others.","n":"The two directions are asymmetric: speech should make the hard simple, and listening should find the fine in the plain. Parimelazhagar goes further, saying to take the use from what others say even if it is flawed in expression.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","kelvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:43"},"k:425":{"book":"kural","num":425,"t":["உலகம் தழீஇய தொட்பம் மலர்தலும்","கூம்பலும் இல்ல தறிவு"],"p":"To secure the friendship of the great is true wisdom; it is (also) wisdom to keep (that friendship unchanged, and) not opening and closing (like the lotus flower).","r":"Embracing the world is brightness; not opening and closing is understanding.","n":"Parimelazhagar narrows உலகம் to the worthy and contrasts a pond flower that closes with a tree flower that stays open.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","samanilai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","What must not open and close is friendship with the worthy. He records another view that it is about wealth and poverty.","அந்நட்பின்கண் முன் மலர்தலும் பின் கூம்புதலும் இன்றி ஒரு நிலையினாவது அறிவாம்."],["kalaignar","What must not open and close is one's response to joy and sorrow, which is equanimity.","இன்பம் துன்பம் ஆகிய இரண்டையும் ஒரே நிலையாகக் கருதுவதே"]],"ch":"k:43"},"k:426":{"book":"kural","num":426,"t":["எவ்வ துறைவது உலகம் உலகத்தோடு","அவ்வ துறைவ தறிவு"],"p":"T o live as the world lives, is wisdom.","r":"As the world lives, so to live with the world is understanding.","n":"This verse needs reading with 423, because the same chapter tells the reader to test everything and to go along.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It restrains a king: he must not think that because he commands the world, no one commands him.","உலகத்தையெல்லாம் யான் நியமித்தலான் என்னை நியமிப்பாரில்லை,"],["manakkudavar","உலகம் means the elevated of earlier times, so this is following the worthy, not the crowd.","முற்பட்ட உயர்ந்தாரோடு பொருந்த ஒழுகுதல் அறிவு என்றார்."]],"ch":"k:43"},"k:427":{"book":"kural","num":427,"t":["அறிவுடையார் ஆவ தறிவார் அறிவிலார்","அஃதறி கல்லா தவர்"],"p":"The wise are those who know beforehand what will happen; those who do not know this are the unwise.","r":"Those with understanding know what will come to be; those without it cannot.","n":"ஆவது could be what will happen or what is good for oneself; Parimelazhagar records both. His gloss makes foreknowledge an act of reasoning ahead, எண்ணி அறிதல், and not prophecy. Not knowing is defined as finding out once it arrives.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:43"},"k:428":{"book":"kural","num":428,"t":["அஞ்சுவ தஞ்சாமை பேதைமை அஞ்சுவது","அஞ்சல் அறிவார் தொழில்"],"p":"Not to fear what ought to be feared, is folly; it is the work of the wise to fear what should be feared.","r":"Not fearing what is to be feared is folly; fearing what is to be feared is the work of the knowing.","n":"Fear is treated as a skill, தொழில். Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar both notice the conflict with the kingly fearlessness of 382 and resolve it. Parimelazhagar defines fear here as considering and refraining.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:43"},"k:429":{"book":"kural","num":429,"t":["எதிரதாக் காக்கும் அறிவினார்க் கில்லை","அதிர வருவதோர் நோய்"],"p":"No terrifying calamity will happen to the wise, who (foresee) and guard against coming evils.","r":"Those whose understanding guards against what is coming have no affliction that comes to shake them.","n":"அதிர வருவது is careful: the claim may be that no calamity shakes them, not that no calamity comes. Parimelazhagar's guarding means removing the cause.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","oozh"],"pos":[["manakkudavar","Even suffering from past deeds does not come harshly if it is guarded against early.","இது முன்னை வினையால் வருந் துன்பமும் முற்பட்டுக் காக்கின், கடிதாக வாராதென்றது."]],"ch":"k:43"},"k:430":{"book":"kural","num":430,"t":["அறிவுடையார் எல்லா முடையார் அறிவிலார்","என்னுடைய ரேனும் இலர்"],"p":"Those who possess wisdom, possess every thing; those who have not wisdom, whatever they may possess, have nothing.","r":"Those with understanding have everything; those without it have nothing, whatever they have.","n":"Parimelazhagar gives a practical reason, not a pious one: all wealth is made and kept by understanding, so the fool has no means of keeping what he has or rebuilding it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:43"},"k:431":{"book":"kural","num":431,"t":["செருக்குஞ் சினமும் சிறுமையும் இல்லார்","பெருக்கம் பெருமித நீர்த்து"],"p":"Truly great is the excellence of those (kings) who are free from pride, anger, and lust.","r":"The prosperity of those without arrogance, anger and smallness has a dignified quality.","n":"The verse puts the virtue in the quality of the prosperity: the same wealth is dignified or not, depending on who holds it.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kutram","selvam","perumai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","செருக்கு is intoxication from wealth, and சிறுமை is excessive desire, which he calls small because it is what small people do.","மதமும் வெகுளியும் காமமும் ஆகிய குற்றங்கள்"],["manakkudavar","செருக்கு is coveting another man's wife, and சிறுமை is any behaviour of petty people.","பிறர்மனை நயத்தலும், வெகுளியும், சிறியார் செய்வன செய் தொழுகுதலும்"]],"ch":"k:44"},"k:432":{"book":"kural","num":432,"t":["இவறலும் மாண்பிறந்த மானமும் மாணா","உவகையும் ஏதம் இறைக்கு"],"p":"Avarice, undignified pride, and low pleasures are faults in a king.","r":"Stinginess, honour that has passed beyond dignity, and joy that lacks dignity are faults in a ruler.","n":"The surprising items are positive feelings gone wrong. Parimelazhagar makes the undignified joy excessive leniency, கழிகண்ணோட்டம், and the wrong honour includes refusing to bow to elders and carrying a thing through just because one intended it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kutram","ivaral","maanam","arasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:44"},"k:433":{"book":"kural","num":433,"t":["தினைத்துணையாங் குற்றம் வரினும் பனைத்துணையாக்","கொள்வர் பழிநாணு வார்"],"p":"Those who fear guilt, if they commit a fault small as a millet seed, will consider it to be as large as a palmyra tree.","r":"Though a fault comes as small as a millet seed, those who are ashamed of blame take it to be as large as a palmyra.","n":"The motive is shame at blame, பழிநாணுவார், so this is social conscience. Parimelazhagar makes the scale practical: treating it as large is how they ensure it doesn't come again.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:44"},"k:434":{"book":"kural","num":434,"t":["குற்றமே காக்க பொருளாகக் குற்றமே","அற்றந் த்ரூஉம் பகை"],"p":"Guard against faults as a matter (of great consequence; for) faults are a deadly enemy.","r":"Guard against fault itself as your aim; fault alone is the enemy that brings ruin.","n":"The double ஏ, குற்றமே twice, refuses any other candidate. Parimelazhagar draws a strong conclusion from a Sanskrit view: external enemies can only bring ruin through such faults. பொருளாக is 'as your aim' for him; Pope's 'wealth' is a different and weaker reading.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:44"},"k:435":{"book":"kural","num":435,"t":["வருமுன்னர்க் காவாதான் வாழ்க்கை எரிமுன்னர்","வைத்தூறு போலக் கெடும்"],"p":"The prosperity of him who does not timely guard against faults, will perish like straw before fire.","r":"The life of one who doesn't guard before it comes will perish like straw set before a fire.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kutram","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","What is coming is fault, supplied from the chapter's subject; even a small fault burns away great wealth.","குற்றம் வரக் கடவதாகின்ற முற்காலத்திலே அதனைக் காவாத அரசன் வாழ்க்கை"],["manakkudavar","What is coming is suffering, and failing to prepare against it is itself the fault.","இது முந்துற்றுக் காவல் செய்வன செய்யாமையும் குற்ற மென்றது."]],"ch":"k:44"},"k:436":{"book":"kural","num":436,"t":["தன்குற்றம் நீக்கிப் பிறர்குற்றங் காண்கிற்பின்","என்குற்ற மாகும் இறைக்கு?"],"p":"What fault will remain in the king who has put away his own evils, and looks after the evils of others.","r":"If he removes his own fault and can then see others' faults, what fault can attach to a ruler?","n":"Parimelazhagar turns this into a condition of legitimacy: punishing others' faults is itself a fault until one has cleared one's own, after which it becomes justice. The verse's order, தன்... பிறர், supports him, though the claim about the right to judge is his.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kutram","arasu","sengol"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:44"},"k:437":{"book":"kural","num":437,"t":["செயற்பால செய்யா திவறியான் செல்வம்","உயற்பால தன்றிக் கெடும்"],"p":"The wealth of the avaricious man, who does not expend it for the purposes for which he ought to expend it will waste away and not continue.","r":"The wealth of one who clings to it, not doing what should be done with it, does not last but perishes.","n":"Stinginess doesn't keep wealth, on this view; it loses it. Parimelazhagar's explanation is that wealth left unused for virtue and growth has nothing to sustain it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ivaral","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:44"},"k:438":{"book":"kural","num":438,"t":["பற்றுள்ளம் என்னும் இவறன்மை எற்றுள்ளும்","எண்ணப் படுவதொன் றன்று"],"p":"Griping avarice is not to be reckoned as one among other faults; (it stands alone - greater than all).","r":"The miserliness called the clinging heart is not a thing to be counted among any.","n":"பற்றுள்ளம் uses a word close to the attachment renunciation speaks of, here applied to wealth in the Porul section.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ivaral","kutram"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It can't be counted among faults because it exceeds them all: it can dominate even when every good quality is present.","குற்றத் தன்மைகள் எல்லாவற்றுள்ளும் வைத்து எண்ணப்படுவது ஒன்று அன்று; மிக்கது."],["manakkudavar","It isn't counted because it is contemptible: useless to self and others, it is reckoned as nothing.","இஃது உலோபம் தனக்கும் பிறர்க்கும் பயன்படாமையால் ஒரு பொருளாக மதிக்கப்படாதென்றது."]],"ch":"k:44"},"k:439":{"book":"kural","num":439,"t":["வியவற்க எஞ்ஞான்றும் தன்னை நயவற்க","நன்றி பயவா வினை"],"p":"Let no (one) praise himself, at any time; let him not desire to do useless things.","r":"Never admire yourself, and never set your heart on a deed that brings no good.","n":"The two clauses could be separate, or the second a consequence of the first, as Manakkudavar and Kalaignar take it. Parimelazhagar connects them too: self-admiration blinds one to place, time and strength, the subjects of chapters 48 to 50.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kutram","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:44"},"k:440":{"book":"kural","num":440,"t":["காதல காதல் அறியாமை உய்க்கிற்பின்","ஏதில ஏதிலார் நூல்"],"p":"If (a king) enjoys, privately the things which he desires, the designs of his enemies will be useless.","r":"If you can enjoy what you love without others knowing of the love, your enemies' schemes come to nothing.","n":"A striking end. After a chapter on removing faults, the last verse manages one by concealing it. Parimelazhagar concedes that desire, anger and joy aren't to be wholly removed. The verse is a matter of security: a known desire is a door for the enemy.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kutram","arasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:44"},"k:441":{"book":"kural","num":441,"t":["அறனறிந்து மூத்த அறிவுடையார் கேண்மை","திறனறிந்து தேர்ந்து கொளல்"],"p":"Let (a king) ponder well its value, and secure the friendship of men of virtue and of mature knowledge.","r":"Knowing their worth and the way to go about it, choose the friendship of those of understanding who have matured in knowledge of virtue.","n":"மூத்த is not simply age: Parimelazhagar counts maturity in understanding, character and years. The verse doesn't say what office such people hold, though Manakkudavar takes them to be priests. For Parimelazhagar திறன் is the way of winning them, and for Manakkudavar it is their capacities.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thunai","aram","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:45"},"k:442":{"book":"kural","num":442,"t":["உற்றநோய் நீக்கி உறாஅமை முற்காக்கும்","பெற்றியார்ப் பேணிக் கொளல்"],"p":"Let (a king) procure and kindly care for men who can overcome difficulties when they occur, and guard against them before they happen.","r":"Cherish and take as support those who have the quality of removing the affliction that has come and guarding before against what has not.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thunai","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","These are priests and ministers: priests handle afflictions from the gods by rites, and ministers those from people by the four policies.","புரோகிதரையும் அமைச்சரையும் கூறியவாறாயிற்று."],["manakkudavar","These are ministers. He takes the verse's general word to refer to them, leaving priests to 441.","இது மந்திரிகளைக் கூட் மாறு கூறிற்று."]],"ch":"k:45"},"k:443":{"book":"kural","num":443,"t":["அரியவற்று ளெல்லாம் அரிதே பெரியாரைப்","பேணித் தமராக் கொளல்"],"p":"To cherish great men and make them his own, is the most difficult of all difficult things.","r":"To cherish the great and make them one's own is the rarest of all rare things.","n":"அரிது can mean hard to do, as Manakkudavar has it, or rare to attain, as Parimelazhagar does. The second adds his observation that a king who can have every rare thing still prizes this one, because the others come through it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thunai","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:45"},"k:444":{"book":"kural","num":444,"t":["தம்மிற் பெரியார் தமரா ஒழுகுதல்","வன்மையு ளெல்லாந் தலை"],"p":"So to act as to make those men, his own, who are greater than himself is of all powers the highest.","r":"To conduct oneself so that those greater than oneself become one's own is the chief of all strengths.","n":"A paradox of power: the supreme strength is standing under someone. Parimelazhagar's தாம் அவர் வழிநின்று makes the king the follower, and he ranks this above wealth, army and fortress on the ground that it reaches afflictions they can't.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thunai","vali","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:45"},"k:445":{"book":"kural","num":445,"t":["சூழ்வார்கண் ணாக ஒழுகலான் மன்னவன்","சூழ்வாரைக் சூழ்ந்து கொளல்"],"p":"As a king must use his ministers as eyes (in managing his kingdom), let him well examine their character and qualifications before he engages them.","r":"Since counsellors serve as his eyes, a ruler must take his counsellors after counsel.","n":"The verse plays on சூழ், which means to deliberate, to plot and to surround, and the commentators split along those senses.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thunai","arasu","therithal"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The king should examine his counsellors before taking them on, because they are his eyes.","அரசன் அத்தன்மையராய அமைச்சரை ஆராய்ந்து தனக்குத் துணையாகக் கொள்க."],["manakkudavar","The king should be able to outplot and kill those who plot to kill him.","அரசன் தன்னைச் சூழ்ச்சியாற் கொல்ல நினைப்பாரைத் தானுஞ் சூழ்ச்சியாற் கொல்லவல்லவனாதல்"],["kalaignar","The ruler benefits by keeping the wise around him.","அறிஞர் பெருமக்களைச் சூழ வைத்துக் கொண்டிருப்பதே"]],"ch":"k:45"},"k:446":{"book":"kural","num":446,"t":["தக்கா ரினத்தனாய்த் தானொழுக வல்லானைச்","செற்றார் செயக்கிடந்த தில்"],"p":"There will be nothing left for enemies to do, against him who has the power of acting (so as to secure) the fellowship of worthy men.","r":"There is nothing enemies can do against one able to carry himself as one of the company of the worthy.","n":"தான் ஒழுக வல்லான்: having good company isn't enough; he too must act accordingly. It is the ability that protects, not only the associates.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","thunai","arasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:45"},"k:447":{"book":"kural","num":447,"t":["இடிக்குந் துணையாரை யாள்வரை யாரே","கெடுக்குந் தகைமை யவர்"],"p":"Who are great enough to destroy him who has servants that have power to rebuke him ?","r":"Who has the standing to ruin those who keep as their support people who will rebuke them?","n":"ஆள்வாரை: such people must actually be employed and valued. Parimelazhagar adds that the rebuker needs to be free of the same faults and to love the king, so rebuke alone doesn't qualify.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thunai","arasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:45"},"k:448":{"book":"kural","num":448,"t":["இடிப்பாரை இல்லாத ஏமரா மன்னன்","கெடுப்பா ரிலானுங் கெடும்"],"p":"The king, who is without the guard of men who can rebuke him, will perish, even though there be no one to destroy him.","r":"A ruler without the guard of those who rebuke will perish even with no one to ruin him.","n":"Parimelazhagar's image is an elephant with no mahout, going off the path and ruining itself. கெடுப்பார் இலானும் makes enemies unnecessary to the story, and 447 and 448 form a pair, protection and exposure.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thunai","arasu","kutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:45"},"k:449":{"book":"kural","num":449,"t":["முதலிலார்க ஊதிய மில்லை மதலையாஞ்","சார்பிலார்க் கில்லை நிலை"],"p":"There can be no gain to those who have no capital; and in like manner there can be no permanence to those who are without the support of adherents.","r":"Those without capital have no profit; those without the support of a pillar have no standing.","n":"A merchant's image for a king's court. Allies are the principal rather than the return: everything else is earned on them. The verse doesn't limit itself to kings; the older commentators supply that.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thunai","arasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:45"},"k:450":{"book":"kural","num":450,"t":["பல்லார் பகை கொளலிற் பத்தடுத்த தீமைத்தே","நல்லார் தொடர்கை விடல்"],"p":"It is tenfold more injurious to abandon the friendship of the good, than to incur the hatred of the many.","r":"To let go one's tie with the good is ten times worse than taking on the enmity of many.","n":"Pope and Kalaignar read கைவிடல் as abandoning a tie, whereas Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar read it as failing to take the great as support. His reason is practical: there are ways out of many enemies, but none out of having no good company.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thunai","inam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:45"},"k:451":{"book":"kural","num":451,"t":["சிற்றினம் அஞ்சும் பெருமை சிறுமைதான்","சுற்றமாச் சூழ்ந்து விடும்"],"p":"(True) greatness fears the society of the base; it is only the low - minded who will regard them as friends.","r":"Greatness fears the small company; smallness at once takes it as kin.","n":"The verse gives the fear to greatness, not to a person lacking resolve. Parimelazhagar explains it through self-knowledge: the wise know their judgment can bend, while the small see only likeness and suspect nothing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","perumai","sirumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:46"},"k:452":{"book":"kural","num":452,"t":["நிலத்தியல்பால் நீர்திரிந் தற்றாகும் மாந்தர்க்கு","இனத்தியல்ப தாகும் அறிவு"],"p":"As water changes (its nature), from the nature of the soil (in which it flows), so will the character of men resemble that of their associates.","r":"As water changes by the nature of the land, so a person's understanding becomes the nature of their company.","n":"Note the subject: it is அறிவு that changes, not behaviour or reputation. Parimelazhagar sharpens the image, with water pure in the sky and only turning on contact, and so with understanding held alone.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:46"},"k:453":{"book":"kural","num":453,"t":["மனத்தானாம் மாந்தர்க் குணர்ச்சி இனத்தானாம்","இன்னான் எனப்படுஞ் சொல்"],"p":"The power of knowing is from the mind; (but) his character is from that of his associates.","r":"A person's perception comes from the mind; the word 'such a one' comes from their company.","n":"A clean division of labour: faculty is inner and reputation is social. Parimelazhagar assigns the mind only basic sense-awareness and gives all cultivated judgment to company, a step further than the verse says. Manakkudavar makes the word specifically blame.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","manam","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:46"},"k:454":{"book":"kural","num":454,"t":["மனத்து ளதுபோலக் காட்டி ஒருவற்கு","இனத்துள தாகும் அறிவு"],"p":"Wisdom appears to rest in the mind, but it really exists to a man in his companions.","r":"Understanding, while showing itself as though in the mind, lies for a person in their company.","n":"The verse is one of the Kural's most radical on the self: where understanding appears to be is not where it is. Parimelazhagar's 'the mind is where it shows, the company is its root' makes it explicit. The verse doesn't explain why.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","arivu","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:46"},"k:455":{"book":"kural","num":455,"t":["மனந்தூய்மை செய்வினை தூய்மை இரண்டும்","இனந்தூய்மை தூவா வரும்"],"p":"Chaste company is the staff on which come, these two things, viz, purity of mind and purity of conduct.","r":"Purity of mind and purity of deeds, these two, come leaning on purity of company.","n":"தூவா is a rare word, which Parimelazhagar glosses as a support and defends with a citation. The claim is causal: company is the thing the other two rest on, not a third kind of purity alongside them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","manam","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:46"},"k:456":{"book":"kural","num":456,"t":["மனந்தூயார்க் கெச்சம்நன் றாகும் இனந்தூயார்க்கு","இல்லைநன் றாகா வினை"],"p":"To the pure-minded there will be a good posterity. By those whose associates are pure, no deeds will be done that are not good.","r":"Those pure of mind have a good எச்சம்; for those pure of company there is no deed that doesn't turn out well.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["inam","manam","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","எச்சம் is children: effects don't differ from their causes.","மனம் தூயராயினார்க்கு மக்கட்பேறு நன்று ஆகும்;"],["manakkudavar","எச்சம் is the wealth that remains after one.","மனநல்லார்க்குப் பின்பு நிற்கும் காண முதலான பொருள்கள் நல்லவாம்;"],["kalaignar","எச்சம் is fame.","மனத்தின் தூய்மையால் புகழும்,"]],"ch":"k:46"},"k:457":{"book":"kural","num":457,"t":["மனநலம் மன்னுயிர்க் காக்கம் இனநலம்","எல்லாப் புகழும் தரும்"],"p":"Goodness of mind will give wealth, and good society will bring with it all praise, to men.","r":"Goodness of mind gains a person prosperity; goodness of company gives all praise as well.","n":"Parimelazhagar's reason for the second clause is neat: those able to give praise are the good themselves, and they are the company.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","manam","pugazh","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:46"},"k:458":{"book":"kural","num":458,"t":["மனநலம் நன்குடைய ராயினும் சான்றோர்க்கு","இனநலம் ஏமாப் புடைத்து"],"p":"Although they may have great (natural) goodness of mind, yet good society will tend to strengthen it.","r":"Though the worthy have goodness of mind in full, goodness of company is a protection to them.","n":"The verse makes its concession to the mind here, then refuses to let it stand alone. Even சான்றோர் are not exempt.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","manam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Good company keeps nurturing a goodness of mind that came from earlier good deeds.","அந் நல்வினை உள்வழியும் மனநலத்தை வளர்த்து வருதலின்"],["manakkudavar","The protection is social: good company keeps the worthy from being looked down on.","இனநலம் இகழ்ச்சி வாராமற் காக்குமென்றது."]],"ch":"k:46"},"k:459":{"book":"kural","num":459,"t":["மனநலத்தின் ஆகும் மறுமைமற் றஃதும்","இனநலத்தின் ஏமாப் புடைத்து"],"p":"Future bliss is (the result) of goodness of mind; and even this acquires strength from the society of the good.","r":"From goodness of mind comes the next world, and even that gains strength from goodness of company.","n":"The verse names மறுமை in plain terms in the book on polity. Parimelazhagar notes that goodness of mind can slip from time to time, and good company holds it steady.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","manam","piravi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The verse concerns joy in the next life, which the mind's goodness yields and good company secures.","ஒருவற்கு மனநன்மையானே மறுமை இன்பம் உண்டாம்;"],["kalaignar","He doesn't mention the next life. The verse is about the support that company gives to a firm mind.","அவர் சார்ந்த இனத்தின் உறுதியும் அவருக்கு வலிமையான துணையாக"]],"ch":"k:46"},"k:460":{"book":"kural","num":460,"t":["நல்லினத்தி னூங்குந் துணையில்லை தீயினத்தின்","அல்லற் படுப்பதூஉம் இல்"],"p":"There is no greater help than the company of the good; there is no greater source of sorrow than the company of the wicked.","r":"There is no greater support than good company and nothing that brings more affliction than bad company.","n":"Parimelazhagar says the chapter's title, avoiding small company, has been argued mainly from the reverse: by praising good company. Only this last verse states both sides together.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","thunai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:46"},"k:461":{"book":"kural","num":461,"t":["அழிவதூஉம் ஆவதூஉம் ஆகி வழிபயக்கும்","ஊதியமும் சூழ்ந்து செயல்"],"p":"Let a man reflect on what will be lost, what will be acquired and (from these) what will be his ultimate gain, and (then, let him) act.","r":"Weigh what will be lost, what will arise, and the gain that comes of it later, and then act.","n":"The verse separates the immediate return from later gain. Parimelazhagar draws out the consequence: a deed that only breaks even now may still be worth doing if the later gain is there. So this is not a plain cost-benefit rule.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["therithal","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:47"},"k:462":{"book":"kural","num":462,"t":["தெரிந்த இனத்தொடு தேர்ந்தெண்ணிச் செய்வார்க்கு","அரும்பொருள் யாதொன்றும் இல்"],"p":"There is nothing too difficult to (be attained by) those who, before they act, reflect well themselves, and thoroughly consider (the matter) with chosen friends.","r":"Nothing is out of reach for those who act after examining it with chosen company and considering it themselves.","n":"Parimelazhagar takes the two verbs in order: first with the company, then alone. So consulting doesn't replace judgment. தெரிந்த also makes the counsellors themselves the first thing chosen.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["therithal","inam","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:47"},"k:463":{"book":"kural","num":463,"t":["ஆக்கம் கருதி முதலிழக்கும் செய்வினை","ஊக்கார் அறிவுடை யார்"],"p":"Wise men will not, in the hopes of profit, undertake works that will consume their principal.","r":"Those with understanding don't undertake a deed that loses the capital for the sake of gain.","n":"Parimelazhagar's example is political: marching to take another's land and losing one's own. He also reads செய்வினை as covering even work already begun, so sunk effort is no excuse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["therithal","arivu","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:47"},"k:464":{"book":"kural","num":464,"t":["தெளிவி லதனைத் தொடங்கார் இளிவென்னும்","ஏதப்பாடு அஞ்சு பவர்"],"p":"Those who fear reproach will not commence anything which has not been (thoroughly considered) and made clear to them.","r":"Those who fear the affliction called disgrace don't begin what isn't clear to them.","n":"The stated motive is shame, not loss. Parimelazhagar explains தொடங்கார்: once begun, a thing can't be dropped midway, so scorn would be added to the failure.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["therithal","vinai","maanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:47"},"k:465":{"book":"kural","num":465,"t":["வகையறச் சூழா தெழுதல் பகைவரைப்","பாத்திப் படுப்பதோ ராறு"],"p":"One way to promote the prosperity of an enemy, is (for a king) to set out (to war) without having thoroughly weighed his ability (to cope with its chances).","r":"Rising up without thinking through every aspect is one way of bedding your enemy in good soil.","n":"பாத்தி is a garden bed. The sting is that the hasty attacker does the enemy's cultivating for them: a failed attack strengthens its target.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["therithal","vali"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:47"},"k:466":{"book":"kural","num":466,"t":["செய்தக்க அல்ல செயக் கெடும் செய்தக்க","செய்யாமை யானுங் கெடும்"],"p":"He will perish who does not what is not fit to do; and he also will perish who does not do what it is fit to do.","r":"One perishes by doing what is unfit to do, and perishes too by not doing what is fit.","n":"Omission gets the same verdict as commission. தக்க is not obviously moral: Parimelazhagar's list of the unfit includes the too-laborious, the useless and the doubtful, which makes it prudential.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","therithal"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:47"},"k:467":{"book":"kural","num":467,"t":["எண்ணித் துணிக கருமம் துணிந்தபின்","எண்ணுவம் என்பது இழுக்கு"],"p":"Consider, and then undertake a matter; after having undertaken it, to say \"We will consider,\" is folly.","r":"Think and then resolve on a task; to say 'we'll think after resolving' is a lapse.","n":"What is to be thought about isn't stated. For Parimelazhagar it is the means, the four strategies; for Manakkudavar it is the result. The first assumes the deed is already chosen.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["therithal","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:47"},"k:468":{"book":"kural","num":468,"t":["ஆற்றின் வருந்தா வருத்தம் பலர்நின்று","போற்றினும் பொத்துப் படும்"],"p":"The work, which is not done by suitable methods, will fail though many stand to uphold it.","r":"Effort not made in the right way will collapse, even if many stand by to protect it.","n":"The verse corrects the chapter 45 picture of allies as near-sufficient: support can't save effort that is on the wrong path. Parimelazhagar spells out which means suit which kind of person.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","muyarchi","thunai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:47"},"k:469":{"book":"kural","num":469,"t":["நன்றாற்ற லுள்ளுந் தவுறுண்டு அவரவர்","பண்பறிந் தாற்றாக் கடை"],"p":"There are failures even in acting well, when it is done without knowing the various dispositions of men.","r":"Even in doing good there is error, when it is done without knowing each person's character.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["therithal","panpu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","நன்று is the good stratagem, gifts and sweet words, used on rival kings, and the error is that the task fails.","வேற்று வேந்தர் மாட்டு நன்றான உபாயம் செய்தற்கண்ணும் குற்றம் உண்டாம்;"],["kalaignar","நன்று is kindness to anyone, and misjudged kindness comes back as harm.","ஒருவருடைய இயல்பைப் புரிந்து கொண்டுதான் நன்மையைக் கூடச் செய்ய வேண்டும்."]],"ch":"k:47"},"k:470":{"book":"kural","num":470,"t":["எள்ளாத எண்ணிச் செயல்வேண்டும் தம்மோடு","கொள்ளாத கொள்ளாது உலகு"],"p":"Let a man reflect, and do things which bring no reproach; the world will not approve, with him, of things which do not become of his position to adopt.","r":"Think and do what won't be scorned; the world doesn't accept what doesn't accord with oneself.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["therithal","pugazh","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The issue is tactical fit: the strong shouldn't use the means of the weak, such as gifts, and the weak shouldn't use force.","தாம் வலியராய் வைத்து மெலியார்க்கு உரிய கொடுத்தல் முதலிய மூன்றனைச் செய்தலும்,"],["manakkudavar","The issue is propriety: even if a thing can be accomplished, do only what others won't despise.","முடியுமாயினும், பிறராலிகழப்படாதவற்றை எண்ணிச் செய் தல் வேண்டும்;"]],"ch":"k:47"},"k:471":{"book":"kural","num":471,"t":["வினைவலியும் தன்வலியும் மாற்றான் வலியும்","துணைவலியும் தூக்கிச் செயல்"],"p":"Let (one) weigh well the strength of the deed (he purposes to do), his own strength, the strength of his enemy, and the strength of the allies (of both), and then let him act.","r":"Weigh the strength of the deed, one's own strength, the opponent's strength and the strength of allies, and then act.","n":"The first item is surprising: the deed itself has strength. Parimelazhagar adds a strict rule the verse doesn't state, that one should act only if one's own side is greater, and avoid even an equal contest, since victory there is doubtful.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vali","therithal"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:48"},"k:472":{"book":"kural","num":472,"t":["ஒல்வ தறிவது அறிந்ததன் கண்தங்கிச்","செல்வார்க்குச் செல்லாதது இல்"],"p":"There is nothing which may not be accomplished by those who, before they attack (an enemy), make themselves acquainted with their own ability, and with whatever else is (needful) to be known, and apply themselves wholly to their object.","r":"For those who know what they can do and what must be known, and proceed resting in that, nothing fails.","n":"The chapter's only verse of confidence, and it hangs on அதன்கண் தங்கி, where the commentators pull in nearly opposite directions.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vali","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Resting means total commitment: fixing mind, speech and body on the task while marching.","எப்பொழுதும் மன, மொழி, மெய்களை அதன் கண் வைத்துப் பகைமேல் செல்லும் அரசர்க்கு;"],["manakkudavar","Resting means restraint: staying within the measure of what one knows one can do.","அதன் பின்பு அவ்வளவிலே நின்று, ஒழுகுவராயின்,"]],"ch":"k:48"},"k:473":{"book":"kural","num":473,"t":["உடைத்தம் வலியறியார் ஊக்கத்தின் ஊக்கி","இடைக்கண் முரிந்தார் பலர்"],"p":"There are many who, ignoran t of their (want of) power (to meet it), have haughtily set out to war, and broken down in the midst of it.","r":"Many, not knowing the strength they have, rose on enthusiasm and were broken midway.","n":"A rare verse appealing to history, பலர். ஊக்கத்தின் ஊக்கி uses the very quality demanded of kings in 382 as the cause of the fall. Not knowing runs both ways too, as உடைத்தம் வலி means the strength they do have.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vali","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:48"},"k:474":{"book":"kural","num":474,"t":["அமைந்தாங் கொழுகான் அளவறியான் தன்னை","வியந்தான் விரைந்து கெடும்"],"p":"He will quickly perish who, ignorant of his own resources flatters himself of his greatness, and does not live in peace with his neighbours.","r":"One who doesn't live in accord, doesn't know his measure and admires himself perishes quickly.","n":"Parimelazhagar makes ஆங்கு அமைந்து about neighbouring kings: one must be either a friend or an enemy who knows his strength. Others read it as a general composure, or as respect for people.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vali","kutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:48"},"k:475":{"book":"kural","num":475,"t":["பீலிபெய் சாகாடும் அச்சிறும் அப்பண்டஞ்","சால மிகுத்துப் பெயின்"],"p":"The axle tree of a bandy, loaded only with peacocks' feathers will break, if it be greatly overloaded.","r":"Even a cart loaded with peacock feathers breaks its axle, if that load is piled too high.","n":"The two உம் make both the cart strong and the load light. The verse speaks only of the cart, and the allegory is supplied by the commentators.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vali"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The feathers are weak enemies: even the strong fail when many combine against them.","'எளியர்' என்று பலரோடு பகைகொள்வான். தான் வலியனே ஆயினும் அவர் தொக்க வழி வலியழியும்;"],["manakkudavar","The cart is a fortress: those who trust in strong forts learn that defences fail if the enemy grows too much.","இஃது அரண் மிகுதல் நன் றென்றிருப்பார்க்குப் பகைமிகின் அரண் நில்லா தென்று கூறிற்று."]],"ch":"k:48"},"k:476":{"book":"kural","num":476,"t":["நுனிக்கொம்பர் ஏறினார் அஃதிறந் தூக்கின்","உயிர்க்கிறுதி ஆகி விடும்"],"p":"There will be an end to the life of him who, having climbed out to the end of a branch, ventures to go further.","r":"Those who have climbed to the tip of a branch and strive further will end their life.","n":"The climber has done well to get there, and that is the danger: this is a warning to the successful. The verse is purely image. Parimelazhagar applies it to a king who has gone as far as he can on a campaign, and Manakkudavar has him turn back.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vali","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:48"},"k:477":{"book":"kural","num":477,"t":["ஆற்றின் அறவறிந்து ஈக அதுபொருள்","போற்றி வழங்கு நெறி"],"p":"Let a man know the measure of his ability (to give), and let him give accordingly; such giving is the way to preserve his property.","r":"Give in the proper way, knowing the measure; that is the way of guarding wealth and bestowing it.","n":"The verse fuses the two usually opposed, போற்றி and வழங்கு, into one way. Parimelazhagar's fixed ratio, two parts for spending, one for reserves and one for giving, comes from Tirikadugam, not the verse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","selvam","vali"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:48"},"k:478":{"book":"kural","num":478,"t":["ஆகாறு அளவிட்டி தாயினுங் கேடில்லை","போகாறு அகலாக் கடை"],"p":"Even though the income (of a king) be small, it will not cause his (ruin), if his outgoings be not larger than his income.","r":"Though the channel of income be narrow, there is no ruin, if the channel of outgo isn't wider.","n":"ஆறு is a path or channel, which gives wealth a hydraulic image. Income's size doesn't determine ruin; only the ratio does. Parimelazhagar adds that even exact balance is safe.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","vali"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:48"},"k:479":{"book":"kural","num":479,"t":["அளவற஧ந்து வாழாதான் வாழ்க்கை உளபோல","இல்லாகித் தோன்றாக் கெடும்"],"p":"The prosperity of him who lives without knowing the measure (of his property), will perish, even while it seems to continue.","r":"The life of one who doesn't live knowing the measure seems to exist, then is not, and perishes without a trace.","n":"The verse has three stages: seeming, not being and not even seeming. Parimelazhagar points out that ruin isn't visible at the start, which is why the first stage is a seeming and the warning needed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["selvam","vali"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:48"},"k:480":{"book":"kural","num":480,"t":["உளவரை தூக்காத ஒப்புர வாண்மை","வளவரை வல்லைக் கெடும்"],"p":"The measure of his wealth will quickly perish, whose liberality weighs not the measure of his property.","r":"Helpfulness that doesn't weigh the limit of what one has quickly ends the limit of one's wealth.","n":"Even the most praised social virtue gets a limit. Manakkudavar sees the objection coming: if spending must stay below income, how can one be helpful? This is presented as the answer.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oppuravu","selvam","vali"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:48"},"k:481":{"book":"kural","num":481,"t":["பகல்வெல்லும் கூகையைக் காக்கை இகல்வெல்லும்","வேந்தர்க்கு வேண்டும் பொழுது"],"p":"A crow will overcome an owl in the day time; so the king who would conquer his enemy must have (a suitable) time.","r":"By day the crow beats the owl; kings who would win a contest need the right time.","n":"The image makes strength relational, as the same two birds reverse by night, a symmetry Manakkudavar makes explicit. Parimelazhagar's definition of time is unexpectedly concrete: weather that lets an army travel without disease and with food and water.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","vali"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:49"},"k:482":{"book":"kural","num":482,"t":["பருவத்தோடு ஒட்ட ஒழுகல் திருவினைத்","தீராமை ஆர்க்குங் கயிறு"],"p":"Acting at the right season, is a cord that will immoveably bind success (to a king).","r":"Acting in accord with the season is the rope that binds fortune so that it doesn't leave.","n":"A rope only makes sense for something that tends to run. Parimelazhagar draws this from தீராமை: fortune's nature is to leave. Kalaignar takes திருவினை differently, as the success of a good deed, but the older commentators read wealth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:49"},"k:483":{"book":"kural","num":483,"t":["அருவினை யென்ப உளவோ கருவியான்","காலம் அறிந்து செயின்"],"p":"Is there anything difficult for him to do, who acts, with (the right) instruments at the right time ?","r":"Are there things said to be hard, if one acts with the right means and knows the time?","n":"Parimelazhagar notes that கருவியான் is there to say that even with the means, time is still needed: the tools don't make timing unnecessary.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:49"},"k:484":{"book":"kural","num":484,"t":["ஞாலம் கருதினுங் கைகூடுங் காலம்","கருதி இடத்தாற் செயின்"],"p":"Though (a man) should meditate (the conquest of) the world, he may accomplish it if he acts in the right time, and at the right place.","r":"Even the world will come to hand if one thinks of the time and acts by the place.","n":"This verse joins time and place and forms a bridge into the next chapter. The promise is grand, and the chapter's next verse immediately makes the same ambition the reason for doing nothing yet.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","idam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:49"},"k:485":{"book":"kural","num":485,"t":["காலம் கருதி இருப்பர் கலங்காது","ஞாலம் கருது பவர்"],"p":"They who thoughtfully consider and wait for the (right) time (for action), may successfully meditate (the conquest of) the world.","r":"Those who have their mind on the world wait, unshaken, with their mind on the time.","n":"Greater ambition means more waiting, not less. Parimelazhagar adds that this applies even if one's strength is already superior, and identifies இருப்பு as a recognised policy, one of six, the opposite of marching out. கலங்காது could go with either the waiting or the aim.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","samanilai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:49"},"k:486":{"book":"kural","num":486,"t":["ஊக்க முடையான் ஒடுக்கம் பொருதகர்","தாக்கற்குப் பேருந் தகைத்து"],"p":"The self-restraint of the energetic (while waiting for a suitable opportunity), is like the drawing back of a fighting-ram in order to butt.","r":"The holding back of the person of energy is like a fighting ram drawing back in order to charge.","n":"It reframes restraint as part of attack, so ஒடுக்கம் doesn't signal a lack of ஊக்கம். Kalaignar adds that this is silence in the face of cruelty and that it isn't from fear, which the verse doesn't say.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:49"},"k:487":{"book":"kural","num":487,"t":["பொள்ளென ஆங்கே புறம்வேரார் காலம்பார்த்து","உள்வேர்ப்பர் ஒள்ளி யவர்"],"p":"The wise will not immediately and hastily shew out their anger; they will watch their time, and restrain it within.","r":"The bright don't flare up outwardly at once; watching the time, they burn within.","n":"The anger isn't removed, only stored. Parimelazhagar gives the unusual reason that it must continue within: if it died away, one could not later stop the offender. Anger here is a resource.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kaalam","vekuli","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:49"},"k:488":{"book":"kural","num":488,"t":["செறுநரைக் காணின் சுமக்க இறுவரை","காணின் கிழக்காம் தலை"],"p":"If one meets his enemy, let him show him all respect, until the time for his destruction is come; when that is come, his head will be easily b rought low.","r":"Seeing those who would harm you, bear them; when you see their final limit, the head goes down.","n":"The verse doesn't say what is to be carried or whose head falls. Manakkudavar's தலையினாற் சுமக்க is the literal image behind Parimelazhagar's reading: carry them on the head, then tip them off.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","vali"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Actually bow to the enemy until his time comes. Your submission makes him careless, and that is why his head falls.","பணியவே, அக்காலம் வந்திறும் வழி அவர் தகைவின்றி இறுவர்."],["kalaignar","Bear one's own feelings of enmity patiently, and the enemy falls by himself.","தங்களின் பகையுணர்வைப் பொறுமையுடன் தாங்கிக் கொள்ள வேண்டும்."]],"ch":"k:49"},"k:489":{"book":"kural","num":489,"t":["எய்தற் கரியது இயைந்தக்கால் அந்நிலையே","செய்தற் கரிய செயல்"],"p":"If a rare opportunity occurs, while it lasts, let a man do that which is rarely to be accomplished (but for such an opportunity).","r":"When what is hard to get has come, do at that very point what is hard to do.","n":"The double அரிய pairs them: rare time, rare deed. Parimelazhagar makes the best observation on அந்நிலையே: the moment won't stay. All the patience of the preceding verses exists for this one instant.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:49"},"k:490":{"book":"kural","num":490,"t":["கொக்கொக்க கூம்பும் பருவத்து மற்றதன்","குத்தொக்க சீர்த்த இடத்து"],"p":"At the time when one should use self-control, let him restrain himself like a heron; and, let him like it, strike, when there is a favourable opportunity.","r":"When it is the season to fold up, be like the heron; when the moment is right, be like its strike.","n":"One image holds both modes, and the verse compares the person first to the bird and then only to the bird's stab. Parimelazhagar notes that the heron looks as though lifeless while it waits, which adds a touch of menace.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaalam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:49"},"k:491":{"book":"kural","num":491,"t":["தொடங்கற்க எவ்வினையும் எள்ளற்க முற்றும்","இடங்கண்ட பின்அல் லது"],"p":"Let not (a king) despise (an enemy), nor undertake any thing (against him), until he has obtained (a suitable) place for besieging him.","r":"Begin no deed and scorn nothing until you have seen the ground for it in full.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["idam","therithal"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","முற்றும் is a verb, to besiege: don't act against an enemy until you have ground for a siege.","பகைவரை முற்றுதற்கு ஆவதோர் இடம் பெற்றபின் அல்லது;"],["manakkudavar","முற்றும் is to complete: don't begin any deed until you see where it can be finished, and don't treat it as easy.","முடியுமிடங் கண்டாலல்லது, யாதொரு வினையுந் தொடங்கா தொழிக; எளிதென்றிகழாதொழிக,"],["kalaignar","முற்றும் is 'fully': choose a completely right place.","முற்றிலும் சரியான இடத்தைத் தேர்ந்தெடுத்து அச்செயலில் இறங்க வேண்டும்."]],"ch":"k:50"},"k:492":{"book":"kural","num":492,"t":["முரண்சேர்ந்த மொய்ம்பி னவர்க்கும் அரண்சேர்ந்தாம்","ஆக்கம் பலவுந் தரும்"],"p":"Even to those who are men of power and expedients, an attack in connection with a fortification will yield many advantages.","r":"Even for those of contentious strength, the gain that comes by joining a fortress gives many benefits.","n":"Even the strong: உம் concedes the objection. Manakkudavar takes the fort as one's own place, and Parimelazhagar, who thinks of a king on campaign, as a siege position. Either way, it is a protection for those who think they don't need it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idam","vali"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:50"},"k:493":{"book":"kural","num":493,"t":["ஆற்றாரும் ஆற்றி அடுப இடனறிந்து","போற்றார்கண் போற்றிச் செயின்"],"p":"Even the powerless will become powerful and conquer, if they select a proper field (of action), and guard themselves, while they make war on their enemies.","r":"Even those without strength become strong and win, if they know the place and act against foes while guarding themselves.","n":"The ஆற்றி in ஆற்றாரும் ஆற்றி plays on the same root: place does not merely help the weak but turns them into the strong.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idam","vali"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:50"},"k:494":{"book":"kural","num":494,"t":["எண்ணியார் எண்ணம் இழப்பர் இடனறிந்து","துன்னியார் துன்னிச் செயின்"],"p":"If they who draw near (to fight) choose a suitable place to approach (their enemy), the latter, will have to relinquish the thought which they once entertained, of conquering them.","r":"Those who had planned will lose their plan, if those who approach know the place and hold to it.","n":"The loss is of a thought: the enemy's victory has been defeated before fighting starts.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","துன்னியார் are the kings who have marched there and who stay close to their fort.","தாம் வினை செய்தற்கு ஏற்ற இடத்தினை அறிந்து சென்ற அரசர்;"],["manakkudavar","துன்னியார் are allies acting with one mind, so the verse is about concord among friends as well as place.","வினைசெய்யும் இடமறிந்து நட்டோரானவர் மனம் பொருந்திச் செய்வாராயின்."]],"ch":"k:50"},"k:495":{"book":"kural","num":495,"t":["நெடும்புனலுள் வெல்லும் முதலை அடும்புனலின்","நீங்கின் அதனைப் பிற"],"p":"In deep water, a crocodile will conquer (all other animals); but if it leave the water, other animals will conquer it.","r":"A crocodile wins in deep water; if it leaves the water, others kill it.","n":"The very same animal, and the reversal is total: பிற, anything at all. Parimelazhagar's maxim is that everyone is strong on their own ground, and the practical point is to fight where the enemy can't stand.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idam","vali"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:50"},"k:496":{"book":"kural","num":496,"t":["கடலோடா கால்வல் நெடுந்தேர் கடலோடும்","நாவாயும் ஓடா நிலத்து"],"p":"Wide chariots, with mighty wheels, will not run on the ocean; neither will ships that the traverse ocean, move on the earth.","r":"Long chariots with strong wheels don't run on the sea; ships that run on the sea don't run on land.","n":"The verse states an obvious fact without comment, and the plainness is the point. Parimelazhagar notes the qualities listed, strong wheels and great size, as just those that don't transfer. He and Manakkudavar read it as being about fitting equipment to the terrain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:50"},"k:497":{"book":"kural","num":497,"t":["அஞ்சாமை அல்லால் துணைவேண்டா எஞ்சாமை","எண்ணி இடத்தால் செயின்"],"p":"You will need no other aid than fearlessness, if you thoroughly reflect (on what you are to do), and select (a suitable) place for your operations.","r":"No support is needed but fearlessness, if you think everything through and act by the place.","n":"This verse suggests an answer to how 382's fearlessness can sit with 428's fear of what should be feared: fearlessness is what remains once the thinking is complete, எஞ்சாமை எண்ணி. Parimelazhagar glosses அஞ்சாமை as firmness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idam","therithal","thunai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:50"},"k:498":{"book":"kural","num":498,"t":["சிறுபடையான் செல்லிடம் சேரின் உறுபடையான்","ஊக்கம் அழிந்து விடும்"],"p":"The power of one who has a large army will perish, if he goes into ground where only a small army can act.","r":"If one with a large army enters where one with a small army can move, his zeal is destroyed.","n":"The large army's size becomes the problem. Parimelazhagar explains that it can't act together and has no practice on that ground. What is destroyed is ஊக்கம், the fighting spirit, not only the force.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idam","vali"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:50"},"k:499":{"book":"kural","num":499,"t":["சிறைநலனும் சீரும் இலரெனினும் மாந்தர்","உறைநிலத்தோடு ஒட்டல் அரிது"],"p":"It is a hazardous thing to attack men in their own country, although they may neither have power nor a good fortress.","r":"Even if they have no fortress or standing, it is hard to take on people on the land where they live.","n":"Parimelazhagar sees the reason in psychology: attacked at home, such people won't resolve to flee; they will resolve to die, and even a large army breaks on that. The verse says மாந்தர், people, not soldiers.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["idam","kudi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:50"},"k:500":{"book":"kural","num":500,"t":["காலாழ் களரில் நரியடும் கண்ணஞ்சா","வேலாள் முகத்த களிறு"],"p":"A fox can kill a fearless, warrior-faced elephant, if it go into mud in which its legs sink down.","r":"The jackal kills, in mud where feet sink, the unflinching elephant with a warrior's face.","n":"The chapter closes with its most extreme reversal, the jackal over the elephant. கண் அஞ்சா also splits the commentators, between a beast unafraid at a glance and one that won't obey its handlers.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idam","vali"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The elephant is one that strikes down spearmen. He rejects the variant வேலாழ், because a wounded elephant makes the jackal's success unremarkable.","'வேலாழ் முகத்த' என்று பாடம் ஓதுவாரும் உளர்; வேற்படை குளித்த முகத்தவாயின் அதுவும் நரி அடுதற்கு ஏதுவாய் முடிதலின், அது பாடம் அன்மை அறிக."],["manakkudavar","The elephant's face has spears sunk in it: he follows the variant reading.","கண்ணஞ்சாத வேலழுத்தப்பட்ட முகத்தினையுடைய களிற்றைக்"]],"ch":"k:50"},"k:501":{"book":"kural","num":501,"t":["அறம்பொருள் இன்பம் உயிரச்சம் நான்கின்","திறந்தெரிந்து தேறப் படும்"],"p":"Let (a minister) be chosen, after he has been tried by means of these four things, viz,-his virtue, (love of) money, (love of) sexual pleasure, and tear of (losing) life.","r":"A person is to be trusted after their nature has been examined through four things: virtue, wealth, pleasure and fear for life.","n":"My observation: the usual fourth term after virtue, wealth and pleasure is liberation, and here it is fear for life, the most worldly substitute possible. Parimelazhagar defends the wording against a variant, உயிரெச்சம், saying others changed it for not knowing the Sanskrit source.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["therithal","thelivu","aram","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","These are four entrapment tests from Sanskrit polity, in which agents tempt the minister with staged plots, wealth, a woman and threats. Passing shows he won't change in future.","உபதை நான்கின் திறத்தான் மனவியல்பு ஆராய்ந்தால் பின்பு தெளியப்படும்."],["kalaignar","These are qualities to look for: firm in virtue, honest with money, not lost in pleasure and unafraid for his life.","தன்னுயிருக்கு அஞ்சாதவனாகவும் இருப்பவனையே ஆய்ந்தறிந்து ஒரு பணிக்கு அமர்த்த வேண்டும்."]],"ch":"k:51"},"k:502":{"book":"kural","num":502,"t":["குடிப்பிறந்து குற்றத்தின் நீங்கி வடுப்பரியும்","நாணுடையான் சுட்டே தெளிவு"],"p":"(The king's) choice should (fall) on him, who is of good family, who is free from faults, and who has the modesty which fears the wounds (of sin).","r":"Trust lies in one born of good family, free of faults and with a shame that recoils from any blemish.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thelivu","kudi","kutram","naanam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","High birth is one of the qualifications in its own right, alongside the others.","உயர்ந்த குடியில் பிறந்து; குற்றத்தின் நீங்கி"],["kalaignar","He reverses the logic: one who is faultless and ashamed of wrong is the one to be considered high-born.","இருப்பவனையே உயர்குடியில் பிறந்தவன் எனத் தெளிவு கொள்ள வேண்டும்."]],"ch":"k:51"},"k:503":{"book":"kural","num":503,"t":["அரியகற்று ஆசற்றார் கண்ணும் தெரியுங்கால்","இன்மை அரிதே வெளிறு"],"p":"When even men, who have studied the most difficult works, and who are free from faults, are (carefully) examined, it is a rare thing to find them without ignorance.","r":"Even in those who have studied the hard books and are flawless, it is rare, when examined, to find no blankness.","n":"வெளிறு is whiteness or emptiness, a striking word for ignorance: a pale spot in a person otherwise solid. Coming right after the demand for faultlessness, this verse admits no one fully passes, which prepares 504. Parimelazhagar takes it as occasional, due to an unsteady mind.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["therithal","kalvi","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:51"},"k:504":{"book":"kural","num":504,"t":["குணம்நாடிக் குற்றமும் நாடி அவற்றுள்","மிகைநாடி மிக்க கொளல்"],"p":"Let (a king) consider (a man's) good qualities, as well as his faults, and then judge (of his character) by that which prevails.","r":"Examine the qualities, examine the faults too, examine which of these predominates, and take that.","n":"நாடி three times. Parimelazhagar's reasoning is what makes the verse humane: no one in the world is only good or only bad. Dominance can be either in weight or in number, so this isn't simple counting.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["therithal","kutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:51"},"k:505":{"book":"kural","num":505,"t":["பெருமைக்கும் ஏனைச் சிறுமைக்கும் தத்தம்","கருமமே கட்டளைக் கல்"],"p":"A man's deeds are the touchstone of his greatness and littleness.","r":"For greatness and for smallness alike, one's own deeds are the touchstone.","n":"The ஏ in கருமமே excludes other tests, which sits awkwardly with 502's demand for birth. Parimelazhagar says explicitly that birth, quality and understanding produce the greatness and that deeds are only the final test of it. Manakkudavar turns it into practice: give a small task first, then promote.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","perumai","sirumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:51"},"k:506":{"book":"kural","num":506,"t":["அற்றாரைத் தேறுதல் ஓம்புக மற்றவர்","பற்றிலர் நாணார் பழி"],"p":"Let (a king) avoid choosing men who have no relations; such men have no attachment, and thereforehave no fear of crime.","r":"Beware of trusting those who are cut off: they have no attachments and aren't ashamed of blame.","n":"The logic is that attachment makes a person accountable: without ties there is nothing that shame can work on.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thelivu","sutram","naanam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","அற்றார் are those with no kin. Without them a person lacks the worldly custom that restrains him from blame.","சுற்றம் இல்லாரைத் தெளிதலை ஒழிக;"],["manakkudavar","அற்றார் are those without moral conduct.","ஒழுக்கமற்றாரைத் தேறுதலைத் தவிர்க;"],["kalaignar","They are those without principles, not those without kin.","நெறியற்றவர்களை ஒரு பணிக்குத் தேர்வு செய்வது கூடாது."]],"ch":"k:51"},"k:507":{"book":"kural","num":507,"t":["காதன்மை கந்தா அறிவறியார்த் தேறுதல்","பேதைமை எல்லாந் தரும்"],"p":"To choose ignorant men, through partiality, is the height of folly.","r":"To trust the ignorant with fondness as your support brings every kind of folly.","n":"Manakkudavar shows this is an argument, naming Parasara as holding that a king may trust those who love him. The verse refutes that. Parimelazhagar notes the folly is the king's, not only the appointee's.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thelivu","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:51"},"k:508":{"book":"kural","num":508,"t":["தேரான் பிறனைத் தெளிந்தான் வழிமுறை","தீரா இடும்பை தரும்"],"p":"Sorrow that will not leave even his posterity will come upon him chooses a stranger whose character he has not known.","r":"One who trusts an outsider without examining him brings trouble that doesn't end, even down his line.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["thelivu","therithal","kudi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","வழிமுறை means descendants: the suffering passes to them, as the kin fall under the enemy.","அத்தெளிவு தன் வழிமுறையினும் நீங்காத துன்பத்தைக் கொடுக்கும்."],["manakkudavar","வழிமுறை means hereditary succession: the error is trusting an outsider as if he were of the ministerial line, and those from such lines may be trusted.","பிறனை ஆராயாதே வழிமுறையென்று தெளிந்தவனுக்கு அத் தெளிவு,"]],"ch":"k:51"},"k:509":{"book":"kural","num":509,"t":["தே றற்க யாரையும் தேராது தேர்ந்தபின்","தேறுக தேறும் பொருள்"],"p":"Let (a king) choose no one without previous consideration; after he has made his choice, let him unhesitatingly select for each such duties as are appropriate.","r":"Trust no one without examining them; having examined, trust what is to be trusted.","n":"The commentators limit the second half differently. For Parimelazhagar, trust comes for tasks suited to each person's capacity, while Manakkudavar has trust in one matter extend to all matters.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thelivu","therithal"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:51"},"k:510":{"book":"kural","num":510,"t":["தேரான் தெளிவும் தெளிந்தான்கண் ஐயுறவும்","தீரா இடும்பை தரும்"],"p":"To make choice of one who has not been examined, and to entertain doubts respecting one who has been chosen, will produce irremediable sorrow.","r":"Trusting without examining, and doubting one who has been trusted: both bring endless trouble.","n":"The second error is the unusual one. Parimelazhagar's reason is practical: the person senses it and thinks 'this won't last', so he slackens and becomes easy for enemies to detach. Doubt creates the very betrayal it fears.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thelivu","therithal"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:51"},"k:511":{"book":"kural","num":511,"t":["நன்மையும் தீமையும் நாடி நலம்புரிந்த","தன்மையான் ஆளப் படும்"],"p":"He should be employed (by a king), whose nature leads him to choose the good, after having weighed both the evil and the good in any undertaking.","r":"One whose nature, having looked at good and bad, has settled on the good is to be employed.","n":"Note the sequence: this person also looks at the bad. The verse doesn't ask for innocence. Parimelazhagar finds in the past tense புரிந்த a probationary stage: a trial task given first, then a more important one.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["therithal","vinai","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:52"},"k:512":{"book":"kural","num":512,"t":["வாரி பெருக்கி வளம்படுத்து உற்றவை","ஆராய்வான் செய்க வினை"],"p":"Let him do (the king's) work who can enlarge the sources (of revenue), increase wealth and considerately prevent the accidents (which would destroy it).","r":"Let him do the work who enlarges the sources, increases the yield and looks into what befalls them.","n":"A purely administrative definition of a good servant, with no moral term. உற்றவை includes, for Parimelazhagar, what comes from the king himself, whose interference counts among the hazards.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","selvam","therithal"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:52"},"k:513":{"book":"kural","num":513,"t":["அன்பறிவு தேற்றம் அவாவின்மை இந்நான்கும்","நன்குடையான் கட்டே தெளிவு"],"p":"Let the choice (of a king) fall upon him who largely possesses these four things, love, knowledge, a clear mind and freedom from covetousness.","r":"Trust lies in one who firmly has these four: love, understanding, clarity and freedom from desire.","n":"Love comes first, even after the warnings of 507. Parimelazhagar directs it to the king and places the absence of desire at the moment when money comes into his hands, which is a very specific temptation.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["thelivu","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:52"},"k:514":{"book":"kural","num":514,"t":["எனைவகையான் தேறியக் கண்ணும் வினைவகையான்","வேறாகும் மாந்தர் பலர்"],"p":"Even when (a king) has tried them in every possible way, there are many men who change, from the nature of the works (in which they may be employed).","r":"Even when trusted after every kind of test, many people turn different through the nature of the work.","n":"The cause of change is the job itself, வினைவகையான். Office alters the person. Parimelazhagar's example is Kattiyankaran, the treacherous minister of Jivaka Chintamani, and he says it is rare for people to resist the pleasures of royal power.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thelivu","vinai","kutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:52"},"k:515":{"book":"kural","num":515,"t":["அறிந்தாற்றிச் செய்கிற்பாற்கு அல்லால் வினைதான்","சிறந்தானென்று ஏவற்பாற் றன்று"],"p":"(A king's) work can only be accomplished by a man of wisdom and patient endurance; it is not of a nature to be given to one from mere personal attachment.","r":"Work itself isn't such as to be given to anyone except one who knows and endures and can do it, just because he is favoured.","n":"The grammar makes வினை the subject. Work has its own nature and doesn't admit of favourites. Parimelazhagar takes சிறந்தான் as someone dear to the king, and Kalaignar as someone merely thought excellent.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:52"},"k:516":{"book":"kural","num":516,"t":["செய்வானை நாடி வினைநாடிக் காலத்தோடு","எய்த உணர்ந்து செயல்"],"p":"Let (a king) act, after having considered the agent (whom he is to employ), the deed (he desires to do), and the time which is suitable to it.","r":"Examine the doer, examine the deed, and act understanding how they fit with the time.","n":"The doer comes before the deed, which is not the expected order. Parimelazhagar's last step is a judgment on the conjunction: at this time, one of this quality doing a deed of this nature will succeed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["therithal","vinai","kaalam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:52"},"k:517":{"book":"kural","num":517,"t":["இதனை இதனால் இவன்முடிக்கும் என்றாய்ந்து","அதனை அவன்கண் விடல்"],"p":"After having considered, \"this man can accomplish this, by these means\", let (the king) leave with him the discharge of that duty.","r":"Having reasoned 'this one will finish this by this means', leave that to him.","n":"The verse is all pointing, with இதனை, இதனால், இவன், then அதனை, அவன். The distal pronouns of the second line themselves enact the handing over: the thing moves away from the king. விடல் is a real letting go.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["therithal","vinai","thelivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:52"},"k:518":{"book":"kural","num":518,"t":["வினைக் குரிமை நாடிய பின்றை அவனை","அதற்குரிய னாகச் செயல்"],"p":"Having considered what work a man is fit for, let (the king) employ him in that work.","r":"After seeing a person is fit for the work, make him fully entitled to it.","n":"Recognition isn't enough: authority has to be conferred. Parimelazhagar makes it elevation so that he can carry the work out himself, and warns that without it the work fails. Kalaignar reads this as simply examining then appointing, which leaves it close to 517.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","thelivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:52"},"k:519":{"book":"kural","num":519,"t":["வினைக்கண் வினையுடையான் கேண்மைவே றாக","நினைப்பானை நீங்கும் திரு"],"p":"Prosperity will leave (the king) who doubts the friendship of the man who steadily labours in the discharge of his duties.","r":"Fortune leaves one who takes differently the closeness of a person devoted to the work.","n":"A rare observation about what success at work produces: familiarity. Parimelazhagar's setting is sharp: rivals tell the king this man acts as if he were family, and the king takes it as disrespect. If he punishes it, no one will commit themselves again.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","selvam","thelivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:52"},"k:520":{"book":"kural","num":520,"t":["நாடோ றும் நாடுக மன்னன் வினைசெய்வான்","கோடாமை கோடா துலகு"],"p":"Let a king daily examine the conduct of his servants; if they do not act crookedly, the world will not act crookedly.","r":"Let the ruler look into it daily: if the one doing the work doesn't bend, the world won't bend.","n":"Parimelazhagar sees the difficulty with trust and adds that the inspection should be done privately, without harming the person's entitlement.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","arasu","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The ruler inspects the officer's conduct. The officer is the one point through which the whole world can be checked.","வினை செய்வான் கோடாது ஒழிய உலகம் கோடாது;"],["kalaignar","The state checks on workers' condition. If those who labour don't wither, the world's prosperity doesn't wither.","உழைப்போர் உள்ளம் வாடாமல் இருக்கும் வரையில் உலகின் செழிப்பும் வாடாமல் இருக்கும்."]],"ch":"k:52"},"k:521":{"book":"kural","num":521,"t":["பற்றற்ற கண்ணும் பழைமைபா ராட்டுதல்","சுற்றத்தார் கண்ணே உள"],"p":"Even when (a man's) property is all gone, relatives will act towards him with their accustomed (kindness).","r":"Even when all is gone, to keep honouring old ties is found only among kin.","n":"பழைமை points back to shared history. Parimelazhagar takes it as help the fallen man gave when he had means, which turns kin loyalty into a kind of remembered debt. Kalaignar turns the description into a definition: those with this trait are kin.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:53"},"k:522":{"book":"kural","num":522,"t":["விருப்பறாச் சுற்றம் இயையின் அருப்பறா","ஆக்கம் பலவும் தரும்"],"p":"If (a man's) relatives remain attached to him with unchanging love, it will be a source of ever-increasing wealth.","r":"If kin whose love doesn't lapse come together, they yield many gains that keep on branching.","n":"Pope's flower and fadeless blossom come from reading அருப்பு as a bud. The commentators read it as branching, கிளைத்தல், which gives a neat play: கிளை also means kin. So kin make wealth put out kin of its own, though that last step is my reading.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:53"},"k:523":{"book":"kural","num":523,"t":["அளவளா வில்லாதான் வாழ்க்கை குளவளாக்","கோடின்றி நீர்நிறைந் தற்று"],"p":"The wealth of one who does not mingle freely with his relatives, will be like the filling of water in a spacious tank that has no banks.","r":"The life of one who doesn't mingle freely is like a broad tank filling with water, with no bank.","n":"The tank does fill: the failure isn't in earning. Without kin, the wealth has nothing to hold it and spreads away uselessly. அளவளாவு is intimacy, and Parimelazhagar explains it through mutual sharing of mind, so mere proximity isn't enough.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:53"},"k:524":{"book":"kural","num":524,"t":["சுற்றத்தால் சுற்றப் படஒழுகல் செல்வந்தான்","பெற்றத்தால் பெற்ற பயன்"],"p":"To live surrounded by relatives, is the advantage to be derived from the acquisition of wealth.","r":"The fruit of having gained wealth is to live surrounded by one's kin.","n":"It is striking how far this goes: a whole purpose for wealth in four words. Parimelazhagar's reason keeps it in Porul: it is what enables ruling free of enemies. Read without him, it is more tender than that.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["sutram","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:53"},"k:525":{"book":"kural","num":525,"t":["கொடுத்தலும் இன்சொலும் ஆற்றின் அடுக்கிய","சுற்றத்தால் சுற்றப் படும்"],"p":"He will be surrounded by numerous relatives who manifests generosity and affability.","r":"One who can give and speak pleasantly will be surrounded by kin in layer upon layer.","n":"Parimelazhagar identifies these with the Sanskrit policies of dāna and sāma. So the tender acts of the household are also two of the four instruments of statecraft, which he used in 467 against enemies.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["sutram","eegai","inchol"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:53"},"k:526":{"book":"kural","num":526,"t":["பெருங்கொடையான் பேணான் வெகுளி அவனின்","மருங்குடையார் மாநிலத்து இல்"],"p":"No one, in all the world, will have so many relatives (about him), as he who makes large gift, and does not give way to anger.","r":"No one on earth has more of a following than one who gives greatly and doesn't cherish anger.","n":"பேணான் doesn't mean he has no anger, only that he doesn't tend it. Parimelazhagar even takes it as not exceeding what a king needs. In 487 anger is nursed within against enemies. The verses may simply be about different audiences.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram","eegai","vekuli"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:53"},"k:527":{"book":"kural","num":527,"t":["காக்கை கரவா கரைந்துண்ணும் ஆக்கமும்","அன்னநீ ரார்க்கே உள"],"p":"The crows do not conceal (their prey), but will call out for others (to share with them) while they eat it; wealth will be with those who show a similar disposition (towards their relatives).","r":"Crows don't hide, they call and eat; gain too belongs only to those of that nature.","n":"A humble bird is the model for royal conduct. The verse's உம் says gain too comes this way, and Parimelazhagar concludes that sharing yields wealth as well as virtue and pleasure. Hiding, கரவா, is what is excluded, so a person's private hoard is the fault.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram","selvam","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:53"},"k:528":{"book":"kural","num":528,"t":["பொதுநோக்கான் வேந்தன் வரிசையா நோக்கின்","அதுநோக்கி வாழ்வார் பலர்"],"p":"Many relatives will live near a king, when they observe that he does not look on all alike, but that he looks on each man according to his merit.","r":"If a king looks at people not in common but by rank, many will live looking to that.","n":"A real problem: equal treatment drives away those who see themselves as higher. Parimelazhagar openly gives that as the reason.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram","arasu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Each kinsman is to be regarded according to his own standing; treating all alike would make the eminent leave.","எல்லாரையும் ஒரு தன்மையராக நோக்காது அரசன் தத்தம் தகுதிக்கு ஏற்ப நோக்குமாயின்"],["manakkudavar","The verse is about singling out one person: it is the advice to appoint a crown prince.","இஃது ஒருவனை இளவரசாக்க வேண்டுமென்றது."],["kalaignar","All people are equal, and regard should follow ability, not rank.","அனைத்து மக்களும் சமம் எனினும், அவரவர்க்குரிய ஆற்றலுக்கேற்ப அவர்களைப் பயன்படுத்திக் கொண்டால்,"]],"ch":"k:53"},"k:529":{"book":"kural","num":529,"t":["தமராகிக் தற்றுறந்தார் சுற்றம் அமராமைக்","காரணம் இன்றி வரும்"],"p":"Those who have been friends and have afterwards forsaken him, will return and join themselves (to him), when the cause of disagreement is not to be found in him.","r":"Those who were kin and left will return to kinship when the cause of discord is gone.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The cause was in the king. If he removes it, they come back by themselves, as natural affection returns without being won again.","அவ்வமராமைக் காரணம் தன் மாட்டு இல்லையாகத் தானே உளதாம்."],["kalaignar","The cause was their pretext. They come back on realising it doesn't hold up.","அந்தக் காரணம் பொருந்தாது என்று உணரும்போது மீண்டும் உறவு கொள்ள வருவார்கள்."]],"ch":"k:53"},"k:530":{"book":"kural","num":530,"t":["உழைப்பிரிந்து காரணத்தின் வந்தானை வேந்தன்","இழைத் திருந்து எண்ணிக் கொளல்"],"p":"When one may have left him, and for some cause has returned to him, let the king fulfil the object (for which he has come back) and thoughtfully receive him again.","r":"One who left and came back for a reason, the king should accept with care after attending to it.","n":"The contrast with 529 is subtle, as Parimelazhagar notes: there a reason for leaving is removed, and here there was none for leaving, only one for returning. He advises to grant what the person came for, but stay cautious: otherwise he may go to the enemy, but he has also shown no love.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram","arasu","therithal"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:53"},"k:531":{"book":"kural","num":531,"t":["இறந்த வெகுளியின் தீதே சிறந்த","உவகை மகிழ்ச்சியிற் சோர்வு"],"p":"More evil than excessive anger, is forgetfulness which springs from the intoxication of great joy.","r":"The slackness that comes of the elation of great joy is worse than excessive anger.","n":"A surprising ranking, because joy isn't usually the danger. சோர்வு is a letting go, and it comes when things go well. Parimelazhagar's reason is exact: anger sometimes at least kills an enemy, while this kills only oneself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pochchaappu","vekuli"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:54"},"k:532":{"book":"kural","num":532,"t":["பொச்சாப்புக் கொல்லும் புகழை அறிவினை","நிச்ச நிரப்புக் கொன் றாங்கு"],"p":"Forgetfulness will destroy fame, even as constant poverty destroys knowledge.","r":"Heedlessness kills fame as unremitting poverty kills understanding.","n":"The comparison is the striking claim. நிச்ச நிரப்பு is not poverty but chronic poverty, and Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar make it daily begging to fill the stomach. Understanding is treated as something that material conditions can destroy.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pochchaappu","pugazh","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:54"},"k:533":{"book":"kural","num":533,"t":["பொச்சாப்பார்க் கில்லை புகழ்மை அதுஉலகத்து","எப்பால்நூ லோர்க்கும் துணிவு"],"p":"Thoughtlessness will never acquire fame; and this tenet is upheld by all treatises in the world.","r":"The heedless have no fame: that is the settled view of writers of every kind in the world.","n":"The Kural rarely appeals to an all-schools consensus. Parimelazhagar explains why it can: every aim of life fails when one is heedless, so the claim isn't limited to polity.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pochchaappu","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:54"},"k:534":{"book":"kural","num":534,"t":["அச்ச முடையார்க்கு அரணில்லை ஆங்கில்லை","பொச்சாப் புடையார்க்கு நன்கு"],"p":"Just as the coward has no defence (by whatever fortifications ha may be surrounded), so the thoughtless has no good (whatever advantages he may possess).","r":"The fearful have no fortress; likewise the heedless have no good.","n":"A fort is useless to one whose fear is inside it: the verse places the failure in the mind, not in the walls. So the heedless may have resources, and they do them no good.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pochchaappu","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:54"},"k:535":{"book":"kural","num":535,"t":["முன்னுறக் காவாது இழுக்கியான் தன்பிழை","பின்னூறு இரங்கி விடும்"],"p":"The thoughtless man, who provides not against the calamities that may happen, will afterwards repent for his fault.","r":"One who slipped by not guarding beforehand will later, when struck, grieve over his own error.","n":"Regret is presented as the penalty. தன்பிழை matters: there is no one else to blame. Parimelazhagar adds that the things that could have been guarded against are enemies' moves, made by watching for such lapses.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pochchaappu","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:54"},"k:536":{"book":"kural","num":536,"t":["இழுக்காமை யார்மாட்டும் என்றும் வழுக்காமை","வாயின் அதுவொப்பது இல்"],"p":"There is nothing comparable with the possession of unfailing thoughtfulness at all times; and towards all persons.","r":"If not slipping, with anyone, at any time, is had without fail, nothing equals it.","n":"Three universals. Parimelazhagar assigns each: toward all people, in good times and bad, in every matter. The condition வாயின் concedes how seldom this is met. Manakkudavar takes it as not making errors in giving justice, a narrower sense.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pochchaappu","arasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:54"},"k:537":{"book":"kural","num":537,"t":["அரியஎன்று ஆகாத இல்லைபொச் சாவாக்","கருவியால் போற்றிச் செயின்"],"p":"There is nothing too difficult to be accomplished, if a man set about it carefully, with unflinching endeavour.","r":"Nothing fails to be done on the ground of being hard, if done with the tool of unforgetting care.","n":"Attention is called a கருவி, a tool. Parimelazhagar notes that it is an inner instrument, the mind, set beside the external means of 483.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pochchaappu","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:54"},"k:538":{"book":"kural","num":538,"t":["புகழ்ந்தவை போற்றிச் செயல்வேண்டும் செய்யாது","இகழ்ந்தார்க்கு எழுமையும் இல்"],"p":"Let (a man) observe and do these things which have been praised (by the wise); if he neglects and fails to perform them, for him there will be no (happiness) throughout the seven births.","r":"What has been praised must be carefully done; those who slight it and don't do it have nothing through seven births.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pochchaappu","piravi","pugazh"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","What is praised is what polity texts prescribe to kings, and lapsing means hell, as with neglect of caste duty.","நீதி நூலுடையார் இவை அரசர்க்கு உரியன என்று உயர்த்துக் கூறிய செயல்களைக் கடைப்பிடித்துச் செய்க;"],["manakkudavar","What is praised is virtue, as praised by the exalted, and the verse is about not neglecting it.","இஃது அறத்தின்கண் இகழாமற் செய்வது கூறிற்று."],["kalaignar","They are one's duties, and neglect means no rise in life. He doesn't mention births.","அப்படிச் செய்யாமல் புறக்கணிப்பவர்களுக்கு வாழ்க்கையில் உயர்வே இல்லை."]],"ch":"k:54"},"k:539":{"book":"kural","num":539,"t":["இகழ்ச்சியின் கெட்டாரை உள்ளுக தாந்தம்","மகிழ்ச்சியின் மைந்துறும் போழ்து"],"p":"Let (a king) think of those who have been ruined by neglect, when his mind is elated with joy.","r":"When swollen with one's own joy, think of those who were ruined by negligence.","n":"A practical remedy: history is used deliberately against one's own mood, at the moment it is hardest to think of it. Parimelazhagar notes that negligence and ruin arise together.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pochchaappu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The memory is a corrective to mood: thinking 'we too would fall that way' stops one exulting.","கெட்டாரை உள்ளவே, 'நாமும் அவ்வாறே கெடுதும்' என்று அதன்கண் மைந்துறார் என்பது கருத்து"],["manakkudavar","It leads to a specific precaution: a king should have what he eats, wears and smears checked, because past kings died that way.","இஃது உண்பவை, உடுப்பவை, பூசுபவை சோதித்துக் கொள்க என்றது."]],"ch":"k:54"},"k:540":{"book":"kural","num":540,"t":["உள்ளியது எய்தல் எளிதுமன் மற்றுந்தான்","உள்ளியது உள்ளப் பெறின்"],"p":"It is easy for (one) to obtain whatever he may think of, if he can again think of it.","r":"To gain what one has thought of is easy, if one can keep thinking of what one has thought of.","n":"The verse is built on repetition of உள்ளியது, which enacts its own lesson. Parimelazhagar catches the particle மன்: it hints what remains unsaid, that this is just what people can't do. So 'easy' is an ironic word. It is easy only in the way the conditional describes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pochchaappu","manam","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:54"},"k:541":{"book":"kural","num":541,"t":["ஓர்ந்துகண் ணோடாது இறைபுரிந்து யார்மாட்டும்","தேர்ந்துசெய் வஃதே முறை"],"p":"To examine into (the crimes which may be committed), to show no favour (to any one), to desire to act with impartiality towards all, and to inflict (such punishments) as may be wisely resolved on, constitute rectitude.","r":"To investigate, show no favour to anyone, hold to what is fitting for a ruler and act after consideration: that is justice.","n":"The definition is mainly procedural, four steps with no mention of outcome. கண்ணோடாது is a strong word for a book with a whole chapter praising கண்ணோட்டம். Parimelazhagar extends யார்மாட்டும் to those dearer than life, and Manakkudavar to one's own kin.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","therithal"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:55"},"k:542":{"book":"kural","num":542,"t":["வானோக்கி வாழும் உலகெல்லாம் மன்னவன்","கோல் நோக்கி வாழுங் குடி"],"p":"When there is rain, the living creation thrives; and so when the king rules justly, his subjects thrive.","r":"All the world lives looking to the sky; the people live looking to the ruler's sceptre.","n":"நோக்கி has the sense of dependence as well as looking up. Parimelazhagar narrows the point: the sky gives food and the sceptre gives security, and without security the food is no use to the people. So the sceptre is the more basic of the two for them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","arasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:55"},"k:543":{"book":"kural","num":543,"t":["அந்தணர் நூற்கும் அறத்திற்கும் ஆதியாய்","நின்றது மன்னவன் கோல்"],"p":"The sceptre of the king is the firm support of the Vedas of the Brahmin, and of all virtues therein described.","r":"The ruler's sceptre stands as the origin of the books of the அந்தணர் and of virtue.","n":"ஆதி is a bold word. It makes politics the source of learning and morals, the opposite of what might be expected. Parimelazhagar notices this, because the Vedas are eternal for him, and explains ஆதி as meaning they don't function without the sceptre.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","aram","arasu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","அந்தணர் நூல் is the Veda: beginningless itself, but it needs the sceptre in order to operate.","வேதமும் அறனும் - அநாதியாயினும், செங்கோல் இல்வழி நடவா ஆகலின்,"],["kalaignar","They are books of the virtuous, with no reference to the Veda or to Brahmins.","ஓர் அரசின் செங்கோன்மைதான் அறவோர் நூல்களுக்கும் அறவழிச் செயல்களுக்கும் அடிப்படையாக அமையும்."]],"ch":"k:55"},"k:544":{"book":"kural","num":544,"t":["குடிதழீஇக் கோலோச்சும் மாநில மன்னன்","அடிதழீஇ நிற்கும் உலகு"],"p":"The world will constantly embrace the feet of the great king who rules over his subjects with love.","r":"The world stands embracing the feet of the great ruler who embraces his people and wields the sceptre.","n":"The same word தழீஇ appears twice, and the reciprocity is the point: the king embraces his people and the world embraces his feet. The embrace from above must come first.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","arasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:55"},"k:545":{"book":"kural","num":545,"t":["இயல்புளிக் கோலோச்சும் மன்னவன் நாட்ட","பெயலும் விளையுளும் தொக்கு"],"p":"Rain and plentiful crops will ever dwell together in the country of the king who sways his sceptre with justice.","r":"Rain and harvest gather together in the land of the ruler who wields the sceptre according to its nature.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","arasu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It is a literal effect: sky and earth work together and yield abundance where rule is just.","வானும் நிலனும் சேரத் தொழிற்பட்டு வளம் சுரக்கும் என்பதாம்."],["kalaignar","It is a comparison: just government is like seasonal rain and a fine harvest.","பெய்யும் மழையினால் வளமான விளைச்சல் கிடைப்பதற்கு ஒப்பானதாகும்."]],"ch":"k:55"},"k:546":{"book":"kural","num":546,"t":["வேலன்று வென்றி தருவது மன்னவன்","கோலதூஉங் கோடா தெனின்"],"p":"It is not the javelin that gives victory, but the king's sceptre, if it do no injustice.","r":"It is not the spear that gives victory but the ruler's sceptre, if it doesn't bend.","n":"வேல் and கோல் rhyme and look alike as long shafts. The comparison also lets the sceptre be read as itself a weapon. The verse makes the claim without saying how justice wins battles. Parimelazhagar only says a spear strikes true when the sceptre is straight, and quotes a Sangam poem in support.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","vali","arasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:55"},"k:547":{"book":"kural","num":547,"t":["இறைகாக்கும் வையகம் எல்லாம் அவனை","முறைகாக்கும் முட்டாச் செயின்"],"p":"The king defends the whole world; and justice, when administered without defect, defends the king.","r":"The ruler guards all the world; justice guards him, if he doesn't falter in it.","n":"முட்டாச் செயின் means even when it costs. Parimelazhagar's examples are the most extreme ones in Tamil memory: the king who gave judgment on his own son and the king who cut off his own hand, both from the Silappatikaram. That shows what 'not faltering' is taken to require.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","arasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:55"},"k:548":{"book":"kural","num":548,"t":["எண்பதத்தான் ஓரா முறைசெய்யா மன்னவன்","தண்பதத்தான் தானே கெடும்"],"p":"The king who gives not facile audience (to those who approach him), and who does not examine and pass judgment (on their complaints), will perish in disgrace.","r":"A ruler who isn't easily approached and who doesn't examine and do justice falls low and perishes by himself.","n":"தானே கெடும் is close to 448: the ruin needs no enemy. Parimelazhagar's 'low state' is sin and blame, and he supports it by quoting that virtue is death to those who have done wrong.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","arasu","kutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:55"},"k:549":{"book":"kural","num":549,"t":["குடிபுறங் காத்தோம்பிக் குற்றம் கடிதல்","வடுவன்று வேந்தன் தொழில்"],"p":"In guarding his subjects (against injury from others), and in preserving them himself; to punish crime is not a fault in a king, but a duty.","r":"Guarding the people, caring for them and removing wrongdoing are no stain on a king: they are his work.","n":"வடு அன்று answers a charge that must have been in the air. Parimelazhagar makes it explicit: punishing one's subjects could seem against virtue, and he replies that purifying them of wrong is the king's caste duty.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","arasu","kutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:55"},"k:550":{"book":"kural","num":550,"t":["கொலையிற் கொடியாரை வேந்தொறுத்தல் பைங்கூழ்","களைகட் டதனொடு நேர்"],"p":"For a king to punish criminals with death, is like pulling up the weeds in the green corn.","r":"A king's dealing with the cruel by death is equal to weeding out the young crop.","n":"The weeding image makes killing seem an act of care for the crop. It says nothing about who decides which are the weeds, and the next chapter opens on a king who may be one.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","kolai","arasu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The king is to punish by death those who burn, poison, rob, and those who desire another's wife. Not doing so harms the world.","அரசன் கொடியவர்களைக் கொலையான் ஒறுத்துத் தக்கோரைக் காத்தல்;"],["manakkudavar","The cruel are a narrower list: thieves, highway robbers and plunderers.","கொடியாராவார் கள்வர், ஆறலைப்பார், சூறைகொள்வார்."],["kalaignar","கொலை is the crime and not the penalty: the state simply punishes those who commit murder and similar cruelty.","கொலை முதலிய கொடுமைகள் புரிவோரை, ஓர் அரசு தண்டனைக்குள்ளாக்குவது"]],"ch":"k:55"},"k:551":{"book":"kural","num":551,"t":["கொலைமேற்கொண் டாரிற் கொடிதே அலைமேற்கொண்டு","அல்லவை செய்தொழுகும் வேந்து"],"p":"The king who gives himself up to oppression and acts unjustly (towards his subjects) is more cruel than the man who leads the life of a murderer.","r":"A king who takes up harassing people and goes on doing wrong is crueller than those who have taken up murder.","n":"Both are given the same verb மேற்கொண்டு, taken up as a trade. Parimelazhagar's reason concerns duration: the killer's harm is for a moment, the oppressor's all the time. He adds the reason is wanting wealth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kodungol","kolai","arasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:56"},"k:552":{"book":"kural","num":552,"t":["வேலொடு நின்றான் இடுவென் றதுபோலும்","கோலொடு நின்றான் இரவு"],"p":"The request (for money) of him who holds the sceptre is like the word of a highway robber who stands with a weapon in hand and says \"give up your wealth\".","r":"The asking of one who stands with a sceptre is like one standing with a spear saying 'hand it over'.","n":"The cutting word is இரவு: the king is begging, or calls it that. A request backed by the power to punish isn't a request. Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar agree that this is about demands beyond the fixed dues. Manakkudavar adds it is like robbery even when worded as a request.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kodungol","selvam","arasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:56"},"k:553":{"book":"kural","num":553,"t":["நாடொறும் நாடி முறைசெய்யா மன்னவன்","நாடொறும் நாடு கெடும்"],"p":"The country of the king who does not daily examine into the wrongs done and distribute justice, will daily fall to ruin.","r":"A ruler who doesn't examine daily and do justice loses his land day by day.","n":"The repeated நாடொறும், and the play of நாடி and நாடு, make the decline as gradual as the neglect. Parimelazhagar notes two ways to read it: that the king loses the land's fruits or that the land itself decays.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kodungol","sengol","arasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:56"},"k:554":{"book":"kural","num":554,"t":["கூழுங் குடியும் ஒருங்கிழக்கும் கோல்கோடிச்","சூழாது செய்யும் அரசு"],"p":"The king, who, without reflecting (on its evil consequences), perverts justice, will lose at once both his wealth and his subjects.","r":"A ruler who bends the sceptre and acts without reflection loses resources and people together.","n":"சூழாது is the failure here, not malice. Pope's 'who counsel doth refuse' is more specific than the Tamil. Parimelazhagar makes the people the source of future wealth, so a bad king loses past and future income at a stroke.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kodungol","selvam","arasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:56"},"k:555":{"book":"kural","num":555,"t":["அல்லற்பட்டு ஆற்றாது அழுதகண் ணீரன்றே","செல்வத்தைத் தேய்க்கும் படை"],"p":"Will not the tears, shed by a people who cannot endure the oppression which they suffer (from their king), become a saw to waste away his wealth ?","r":"Are not the tears shed by those who can't bear their affliction the weapon that wears away wealth?","n":"தேய்க்கும் is slow wearing: tears work like an abrasive. Parimelazhagar sees a saw and a tree in it, and says the real agent is the sin of causing affliction, shifted onto the tears. So he supplies a moral mechanism the verse leaves unstated. Kalaignar has the tears destroy the regime, though the verse says wealth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kodungol","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:56"},"k:556":{"book":"kural","num":556,"t":["மன்னர்க்கு மன்னுதல் செங்கோன்மை அஃதின்றேல்","மன்னாவாம் மன்னர்க் கொளி"],"p":"Righteous government gives permanence to (the fame of) kings; without that their fame will have no endurance.","r":"For rulers, enduring is by just rule; without it, the lustre of rulers doesn't endure.","n":"The verse plays on மன்னர், மன்னுதல், மன்னா, ruler and enduring in one root, as if a ruler is by definition one who lasts. Parimelazhagar takes ஒளி as fame and adds the implication that the ruler himself doesn't endure.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","kodungol","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:56"},"k:557":{"book":"kural","num":557,"t":["துளியின்மை ஞாலத்திற்கு எற்றற்றே வேந்தன்","அளியின்மை வாழும் உயிர்க்கு"],"p":"As is the world without rain, so live a people whose king is without kindness.","r":"As the lack of rain is to the earth, so the king's lack of tender regard is to those who live.","n":"The negative of 542, as Parimelazhagar points out. But note the shift: 542 needs the sceptre, and 557 fears the lack of அளி, kindness. The failure of a king here is one of feeling as well as of law.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kodungol","ali","arasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:56"},"k:558":{"book":"kural","num":558,"t":["இன்மையின் இன்னாது உடைமை முறைசெய்யா","மன்னவன் கோற்கீழ்ப் படின்"],"p":"Property gives more sorrow than poverty, to those who live under the sceptre of a king without justice.","r":"Under the sceptre of a ruler who doesn't do justice, having is more painful than having nothing.","n":"Pope's couplet, adding a sting to poverty, misses this, though his gloss has it. Parimelazhagar spells out the reason with grim realism: only those who have something get shackled and tortured for it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kodungol","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:56"},"k:559":{"book":"kural","num":559,"t":["முறைகோடி மன்னவன் செய்யின் உறைகோடி","ஒல்லாது வானம் பெயல்"],"p":"If the king acts contrary to justice, rain will become unseasonable, and the heavens will withhold their showers.","r":"If the ruler bends justice in what he does, the sky, bending the rain, won't pour.","n":"கோடி is used of both king and cloud, so the same bending passes between them, and the grammar itself does the work of linking them.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kodungol","arasu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It is literal: rain in fact fails to come in season, and the next verse gives the cause.","அவன் நாட்டுப் பருவமழை இன்றாம் வகை மேகம் பொழிதலைச் செய்யாது."],["kalaignar","It is a practical chain: bad government lets reservoirs fall into ruin, so rain can't be stored.","நீரைத் தேக்கிப் பயனளிக்கும் இடங்கள் பாழ்பட்டுப் போகுமாதலால்,"]],"ch":"k:56"},"k:560":{"book":"kural","num":560,"t":["ஆபயன் குன்றும் அறுதொழிலோர் நூல்மறப்பர்","காவலன் காவான் எனின்"],"p":"If the guardian (of the country) neglects to guard it, the produce of the cows will fail, and the men of six duties viz., the Brahmins will forget the vedas.","r":"If the guardian doesn't guard, the yield of cows declines and those of the six occupations forget their books.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kodungol","kalvi","arasu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Those of the six duties are Brahmins. Without milk and mantras there is no sacrifice, and that is why rain fails, which completes 559.","பசுக்கள் பால் குன்றியவழி அவியின்மையானும், அது கொடுத்தற்குரியார் மந்திரம் கற்பம் என்பன ஓதாமையானும், வேள்வி நடவாதாம்;"],["kalaignar","They are not a caste: the verse means that productive work and key occupations dwindle.","ஆக்கப்பணிகள் எதுவும் நடக்காது; முக்கியமான தொழில்களும் தேய்ந்து விடும்."]],"ch":"k:56"},"k:561":{"book":"kural","num":561,"t":["தக்காங்கு நாடித் தலைச்செல்லா வண்ணத்தால்","ஒத்தாங்கு ஒறுப்பது வேந்து"],"p":"He is a king who having equitably examined (any injustice which has been brought to his notice), suitably punishes it, so that it may not be again committed.","r":"A king is one who weighs a case fairly and then punishes in proportion, so that the wrong does not go further.","n":"The chapter opens by defining the king through punishment, not against it. As I read it, dread is set apart from penalty by inquiry and proportion; the two matching words தக்காங்கு and ஒத்தாங்கு carry the whole standard.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oruththal","kol","veruvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:57"},"k:562":{"book":"kural","num":562,"t":["கடிதோச்சி மெல்ல எறிக நெடிதாக்கம்","நீங்காமை வேண்டு பவர்"],"p":"Let the king, who desires that his prosperity may long remain, commence his preliminary enquires wi th strictness, and then punish with mildness.","r":"Those who want their prosperity to last long should raise the rod sharply and bring it down lightly.","n":"The rod image gives two audiences to one act: the raising is seen by would-be wrongdoers and the light landing by everyone else. If Manakkudavar is right, this verse softens the exact proportion that 561 has just asked for.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oruththal","veruvu","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Begin as if going beyond measure, then act within measure: the penalty remains proportionate and only looks mild beside its start.","அளவிறப்பச் செய்வார்போல் தொடங்கி; மெல்ல எறிக-செய்யுங்கால் அளவிறவாமல் செய்க"],["manakkudavar","The penalty should actually be less than the offence deserves.","இது குற்றத்திற்குத் தக்க தண்டத்தைக் குறையச் செய்யவேண்டு மென்றது."],["kalaignar","The severity lies in admonishing to prevent wrongs, and the gentleness in the punishing itself.","கண்டிக்கும்போது கடுமை காட்டித், தண்டிக்கும் போது மென்மை காட்டுகிறவர்களின்"]],"ch":"k:57"},"k:563":{"book":"kural","num":563,"t":["வெருவந்த செய்தொழுகும் வெங்கோல னாயின்","ஒருவந்தம் ஒல்லைக் கெடும்"],"p":"The cruel-sceptred king, who acts so as to put his subjects in fear, will certainly and quickly come to ruin.","r":"A cruel-sceptred ruler who goes on doing what terrifies people will certainly and quickly perish.","n":"The verse supplies no mechanism: it asserts certainty (ஒருவந்தம்) and speed (ஒல்லை) and leaves the how to the verses that follow.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["veruvu","kol"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:57"},"k:564":{"book":"kural","num":564,"t":["இறைகடியன் என்றுரைக்கும் இன்னாச்சொல் வேந்தன்","உறைகடுகி ஒல்லைக் கெடும்"],"p":"The king who is spoken of as cruel will quickly perish; his life becoming shortened.","r":"A king about whom the painful words 'our lord is harsh' are spoken will quickly perish, his tenure cut short.","n":"The harmful speech here is arguably the subjects', not the king's. A phrase people say of him can do what no enemy has done.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["veruvu","kadunjol","kol"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The painful word is what wounded subjects say of the king, and உறை means his days: his life shrinks and his wealth goes.","ஆயுளும் குறைந்து செல்வமும் கடிதின் இழக்கும்"],["manakkudavar","உறை is where he dwells, which turns against him; even if land and residence bear it, divine force ruins him.","இது நாடும் தான் உறையும் இடமும் பொறுப்பினும் தெய்வத்தினாற் கெடுவ னென்றது."],["kalaignar","The harsh speech is the ruler's own, and what is lost is the regime's standing.","கடுஞ்சொல் உரைக்கும் கொடுங்கோல் என்று குடி மக்களால் கருதப்படும் அரசு"]],"ch":"k:57"},"k:565":{"book":"kural","num":565,"t":["அருஞ்செவ்வி இன்னா முகத்தான் பெருஞ்செல்வம்","பேஎய்கண் டன்னது உடைத்து"],"p":"The great wealth of him who is difficult of access and possesses a sternness of countenance, is like that which has been obtained by a devil.","r":"The great wealth of one who is hard to get an audience with and grim of face has something of the ghost about it.","n":"Inaccessibility and a grim face count as violence here, though no one has been touched.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["veruvu","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The wealth is as if a ghost had seized it: since no one approaches, it benefits neither him nor others.","அவனது செல்வம் தனக்கும் பிறர்க்கும் பயன்படாது"],["manakkudavar","The wealth is frightening, like seeing a ghost: no one will take from it, so no army gathers.","இது செல்வத்தை வாங்குவார் இன்மையின் படை சேராதென்றது."]],"ch":"k:57"},"k:566":{"book":"kural","num":566,"t":["கடுஞ்சொல்லன் கண்ணிலன் ஆயின் நெடுஞ்செல்வம்","நீடின்றி ஆங்கே கெடும்"],"p":"The abundant wealth of the king whose words are harsh and whose looks are void of kindness, will instantly perish instead of abiding long, with him.","r":"If a ruler is harsh of speech and without kindly regard, even vast wealth is lost on the spot instead of lasting.","n":"நெடுஞ்செல்வம் and ஆங்கே are set against each other: length of holdings gives no length of time. This verse's instant loss sits oddly beside the slow filing of 567, so the chapter seems to offer no single speed of ruin.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kadunjol","kannottam","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:57"},"k:567":{"book":"kural","num":567,"t":["கடுமொழியும் கையிகந்த தண்டமும் வேந்தன்","அடுமுரண் தேய்க்கும் அரம்"],"p":"Severe words and excessive punishments will be a file to waste away a king's power for destroying","r":"Harsh words and punishment past all bounds are a file that wears down a king's conquering strength.","n":"A file works by small repeated strokes, and here the king holds it against his own iron. Parimelazhagar assigns the harm: harsh words lose the army, and excessive punishment loses the country.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kadunjol","oruththal","veruvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:57"},"k:568":{"book":"kural","num":568,"t":["இனத்தாற்றி எண்ணாத வேந்தன் சினத்தாற்றிச்","சீறிற் சிறுகும் திரு"],"p":"The prosperity of that king will waste away, who without reflecting (on his affairs himself), commits them to his ministers, and (when a failure occurs) gives way to anger, and rages against them.","r":"The fortune of a king shrinks if he does not think matters through with his circle and then lashes out in anger.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","veruvu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","He leaves deliberation to his ministers without sharing it, then rages at them when it fails, and they withdraw from him in fear.","அமைச்சர்மேல் வைத்து அவரோடு தானும் எண்ணிச் செய்யாத அரசன்"],["manakkudavar","The context is justice: he does not examine others' offences together with his council, and acts from anger.","பிறர் செய்த குற்றத்தைத் தனக்கு இனமானாரோடே அமைந்து ஆராயாத அரசன்"],["kalaignar","The fault is a failure to consult, followed by anger and crooked conduct, and the regime falls by itself.","கூட்டாளிகளிடம் கலந்து பேசாமல் சினத்திற்கு ஆளாகிக்"]],"ch":"k:57"},"k:569":{"book":"kural","num":569,"t":["செருவந்த போழ்திற் சிறைசெய்யா வேந்தன்","வெருவந்து வெய்து கெடும்"],"p":"The king who has not provided himself with a place of defence, will in times of war be seized with fear and quickly perish.","r":"A king who has not made his defences will, when war comes, be seized by fear and quickly perish.","n":"வெரு returns here pointed the other way: in a chapter on causing dread, the king himself ends up dreading. Manakkudavar draws this out by saying that a king must also not do what brings fear on himself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["veruvu","aran"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:57"},"k:570":{"book":"kural","num":570,"t":["கல்லார்ப் பிணிக்கும் கடுங்கோல் அதுவல்லது","இல்லை நிலக்குப் பொறை"],"p":"The earth bears up no greater burden than ignorant men whom a cruel sceptre attaches to itself (as the ministers of its evil deeds).","r":"The harsh sceptre binds the unlearned to itself, and the earth carries no burden heavier than this.","n":"Cruelty is presented as having a staffing logic: it gathers those who lack the learning to resist it. Parimelazhagar puts the weight on the alliance and Manakkudavar on the king who forms it, which comes to much the same.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kol","kalvi","veruvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:57"},"k:571":{"book":"kural","num":571,"t":["கண்ணோட்டம் என்னும் கழிபெருங் காரிகை","உண்மையான் உண்டிவ் வுலகு"],"p":"The world exists through that greatest ornament (of princes), a gracious demeanour.","r":"Because that surpassingly great beauty called kindly regard exists, this world exists.","n":"A word of ornament (காரிகை) is given the weight of a cosmic condition. The chapter's elevated claim starts by calling kindness a beauty, not a duty.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kannottam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The beauty is the king's, and without it his subjects live like deer beside a tiger.","கண்ணோட்டம் என்று சொல்லப்படுகின்ற சிறப்பு உடைய அழகு அரசர்மாட்டு உண்டு ஆகலான்"],["kalaignar","The verse is about anyone who has this quality, and they bring the world its greatness.","பெரும் அழகைக் கொண்டவர்கள் இருப்பதால்தான் பெருமை அடைகிறது."]],"ch":"k:58"},"k:572":{"book":"kural","num":572,"t":["கண்ணோட்டத் துள்ளது உலகியல் அஃதிலார்","உண்மை நிலக்குப் பொறை"],"p":"The prosperity of the world springs from the kindliness, the existence of those who have no (kindliness) is a burden to the earth.","r":"The world's ordinary workings rest on kindly regard, and the mere existence of those without it is a weight on the earth.","n":"Parimelazhagar lists what உலகியல் consists of: mutual helpfulness, protection, and forgiving lapses. On that reading the grace of the eye is social infrastructure, not private sentiment.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kannottam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:58"},"k:573":{"book":"kural","num":573,"t":["பண்என்னாம் பாடற்கு இயைபின்றேல் கண்என்னாம்","கண்ணோட்டம் இல்லாத கண்"],"p":"Of what avail is a song if it be inconsistent with harmony ? what is the use of eyes which possess no kindliness.","r":"Of what use is melody that does not fit the song? Of what use is an eye with no kindly regard?","n":"The analogy treats kindness as attunement: the eye is related to regard as melody is related to singing, so it needs the other to fulfil itself. Parimelazhagar's long list of musical terms is a resource for the music tradition more than for the verse's argument.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kannottam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:58"},"k:574":{"book":"kural","num":574,"t":["உளபோல் முகத்தெவன் செய்யும் அளவினால்","கண்ணோட்டம் இல்லாத கண்"],"p":"Beyond appearing to be in the face, what good do they do, those eyes in which is no well-regulated kindness ?","r":"What do eyes that lack measured kindly regard do, beyond seeming to be there in the face?","n":"அளவினால் is easy to skip: even while condemning the lack, the verse measures the virtue, and Parimelazhagar takes this as excluding excess of it. This prepares for 578.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kannottam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:58"},"k:575":{"book":"kural","num":575,"t":["கண்ணிற்கு அணிகலம் கண்ணோட்டம் அஃதின்றேல்","புண்ணென்று உணரப் படும்"],"p":"Kind looks are the ornaments of the eyes; without these they will be considered (by the wise) to be merely two sores.","r":"Kindly regard is the eye's only jewel, and without it the eye is known for a wound.","n":"The wound is what the observer perceives (உணரப் படும்), so the verse judges the eye by how it meets others.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kannottam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:58"},"k:576":{"book":"kural","num":576,"t":["மண்ணோ டியைந்த மரத்தனையர் கண்ணோ","டியைந்துகண் ணோடா தவர்"],"p":"They resemble the trees of the earth, who although they have eyes, never look kindly (on others).","r":"Those who have eyes yet do not turn them kindly on others are like trees fixed in the soil.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kannottam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It means a real tree, which stands joined to earth: they walk about but are as unresponsive as trees. He rejects the plaster-puppet reading.","அதனைச் சுதைமண்ணோடு கூடிய மரப்பாவை என்று உரைப்பாரும் உளர். அஃது உரையன்மை"],["manakkudavar","It means a wooden doll made with plaster: people whose eyes have met another's and still show no regard are like the doll.","சுதைமண்ணோடு கூடச் செய்த மாப்பாவையோடு ஒப்பார்"]],"ch":"k:58"},"k:577":{"book":"kural","num":577,"t":["கண்ணோட் டம் இல்லவர் கண்ணிலர் கண்ணுடையார்","கண்ணோட்டம் இன்மையும் இல்"],"p":"Men without kind looks are men without eyes; those who (really) have eyes are also not devoid of kind looks.","r":"Those without kindly regard have no eyes, and those who truly have eyes do not lack it.","n":"The verse redefines possession of eyes by function. The second half turns the first around, closing off the idea of a sighted person who merely chooses not to look kindly.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kannottam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:58"},"k:578":{"book":"kural","num":578,"t":["கருமம் சிதையாமல் கண்ணோட வல்லார்க்கு","உரிமை உடைத்திவ் வுலகு"],"p":"The world is theirs (kings) who are able to show kindness, without injury to their affairs, (administration of justice).","r":"The world belongs to those able to extend kindly regard without damaging the task at hand.","n":"வல்லார் marks this as a skill and a difficult one. After seven verses of unqualified praise, the condition here gives the whole chapter its hard edge.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kannottam","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The task is the king's justice, which fails if he spares associates who have harmed others.","முறை செய்தல் ஆகிய தன் தொழில் அழியாமல்"],["manakkudavar","The task is the king's own interests: kindness must not cost him material loss.","அரசர்க்குப் பொருட்கேடு வாராமல் கண்ணோட வேண்டுமென்று கூறிற்று."]],"ch":"k:58"},"k:579":{"book":"kural","num":579,"t":["ஒறுத்தாற்றும் பண்பினார் கண்ணும்கண் ணோடிப்","பொறுத்தாற்றும் பண்பே தலை"],"p":"Patiently to bear with, and show kindness to those who grieve us, is the most excellent of all dispositions.","r":"The highest disposition is to extend kindly regard and bear with even those whose way is to hurt you.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kannottam","porai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","பண்பினார் implies familiarity: those meant are people close to the king, and this is the top quality in kings.","'பண்பினார்' என்றதனான், அவர் பயிற்சி பெற்றாம்."],["kalaignar","It extends to anyone at all who is inclined to destroy you.","அழிக்க நினைத்திடும் இயல்புடையவரிடத்திலும் பொறுமை காட்டுவது"]],"ch":"k:58"},"k:580":{"book":"kural","num":580,"t":["பெயக்கண்டும் நஞ்சுண் டமைவர் நயத்தக்க","நாகரிகம் வேண்டு பவர்"],"p":"Those who desire (to cultivate that degree of) urbanity which all shall love, even after swallowing the poison served to them by their friends, will be friendly with them.","r":"Those who want a refinement worth loving will see poison poured, drink it, and stay at ease.","n":"The verse names no pourer: the 'friends' in Pope and the associates in Parimelazhagar are supplied. Parimelazhagar's support comes from Natrinai 355, which offers a thread beyond the Kural, and it is where the identification of நாகரிகம with kindly regard rests.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kannottam","porai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","நாகரிகம is kindly regard, and the poison comes from associates whom one's eyes cannot refuse.","நாகரிகம் என்பது கண்ணோட்டமாதல்"],["manakkudavar","நாகரிகம is the broader possession of good qualities across virtue, wealth and pleasure.","நாகரிகம் - அறம் பொருள் இன்பத்தின்கண் நற்குணங்கள் பலவும் உடைமை"]],"ch":"k:58"},"k:581":{"book":"kural","num":581,"t":["ஒற்றும் உரைசான்ற நூலும் இவையிரண்டும்","தெற்றென்க மன்னவன் கண்"],"p":"Let a king consider as his eyes these two things, a spy and a book (of laws) universally esteemed.","r":"Let a ruler hold spies and the well-regarded treatise, these two, firmly as his eyes.","n":"Immediately after a chapter that defines the eye by kindness, the eye is redefined by information. Parimelazhagar's pairing makes the spy the eye of flesh, which sees what is happening, and the treatise the eye of wisdom, which says what to do about it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["otru","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:59"},"k:582":{"book":"kural","num":582,"t":["எல்லார்க்கும் எல்லாம் நிகழ்பவை எஞ்ஞான்றும்","வல்லறிதல் வேந்தன் தொழில்"],"p":"It is the duty of a king to know quickly (by a spy) what all happens, daily, amongst all men.","r":"To know quickly, at all times, everything happening to everyone is the king's work.","n":"Knowing is called the work itself. Parimelazhagar explains that this is a figure, since both favour and punishment depend on timely knowledge.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["otru","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","'Everyone' is the three classes named in 584, and what is known is their good and bad words and acts.","'எல்லார்க்கும்' என்றது மூன்று திறத்தாரையும்."],["manakkudavar","'Everyone' is the surrounding kings, whether enemy, ally, middle or neutral, and both their circle and his own.","பகைவராகியும் நட்டாராகியும் மத்திமராகியும் உதாசீனராகியும் இருக்கின்ற அரசர்க்கும்"]],"ch":"k:59"},"k:583":{"book":"kural","num":583,"t":["ஒற்றினான் ஒற்றிப் பொருள்தெரியா மன்னவன்","கொற்றங் கொளக்கிடந்தது இல்"],"p":"There is no way for a king to obtain conquests, who knows not the advantage of discoveries made by a spy.","r":"A ruler who does not gather through spies and discern what it all means has no victory lying ready to take.","n":"The verse has two conditions: spying and discerning (பொருள் தெரியா). Gathering reports without weighing them fails as well.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["otru","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:59"},"k:584":{"book":"kural","num":584,"t":["வினைசெய்வார் தம்சுற்றம் வேண்டாதார் என்றாங்கு","அனைவரையும் ஆராய்வது ஒற்று"],"p":"He is a spy who watches all men, to wit, those who are in the king's employment, his relatives, and his enemies.","r":"A spy is one who examines everyone: those doing the king's work, his kin, and those who wish him ill.","n":"The list begins with the king's own servants and relatives; enemies come last. Surveillance turns inward first, and Kalaignar draws out of this a professional ethic: the spy must not sort people by favour.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["otru","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:59"},"k:585":{"book":"kural","num":585,"t":["கடாஅ உருவொடு கண்ணஞ்சாது யாண்டும்","உகாஅமை வல்லதே ஒற்று"],"p":"A spy is one who is able to assume an appearance which may create no suspicion (in the minds of others), who fears no man's face, and who never reveals (his purpose).","r":"A spy is one who can take an appearance that raises no suspicion, not flinch before eyes that probe him, and never let anything spill.","n":"உகாஅமை is the image of not spilling. Parimelazhagar's gloss shows his own social world: the unsuspected forms are those of Brahmins, merchants and the like.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["otru"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:59"},"k:586":{"book":"kural","num":586,"t":["துறந்தார் படிவத்த ராகி இறந்தாராய்ந்து","என்செயினும் சோர்விலது ஒற்று"],"p":"He is a spy who, assuming the appearance of an ascetic, goes into (whatever place he wishes), examines into (all, that is needful), and never discovers himself, whatever may be done to him.","r":"A spy goes in the guise of renunciants, pushes through to examine what must be examined, and gives nothing away whatever is done to him.","n":"என்செயினும் quietly admits torture. Parimelazhagar sees this: having mentioned the four methods already, he says force is raised again because it is the hardest to bear.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["otru","thuravu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","He lives as complete renunciants and vow-keepers such as pilgrims do, and this commentator records separately that others read படிவம் as costume.","இதனுள் 'படிவம்' என்றதனை வேடமாக்கி, 'துறந்தார் வேடத்தாராகி' என்று உரைப்பாரும் உளர்."],["manakkudavar","He wears the costume of an ascetic and crosses the country's border to gather news.","தவஞ் செய்வார் வேடத்தராகி, நாட்டெல்லையைக் கடந்து போய்"],["kalaignar","No renunciant is involved: he simply seems unconnected with the affair.","நிகழ்வில் தொடர்பற்றவரைப் போலக் காட்டிக் கொண்டு"]],"ch":"k:59"},"k:587":{"book":"kural","num":587,"t":["மறைந்தவை கேட்கவற் றாகி அறிந்தவை","ஐயப்பாடு இல்லதே ஒற்று"],"p":"A spy is one who is able to discover what is hidden and who retains no doubt concerning what he has known.","r":"A spy is one able to hear what has been concealed and to be free of doubt about what he has learned.","n":"Parimelazhagar's reason is practical: a king can't act on a report delivered with doubt. The next verse then refuses to accept the spy's certainty, so in this chapter confidence is required of the source but not trusted by the user.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["otru","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:59"},"k:588":{"book":"kural","num":588,"t":["ஒற்றொற்றித் தந்த பொருளையும் மற்றுமோர்","ஒற்றினால் ஒற்றிக் கொளல்"],"p":"Let not a king receive the information which a spy has discovered and made known to him, until he has examined it by another spy.","r":"Even what a spy has found and brought back should be confirmed through another spy.","n":"The commentators explain why from a suspicion of corruption: Parimelazhagar thinks of collusion with those being watched, and Manakkudavar of a spy taking money from rival kings.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["otru","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:59"},"k:589":{"book":"kural","num":589,"t":["ஒற்றெற் றுணராமை ஆள்க உடன்மூவர்","சொற்றொக்க தேறப் படும்"],"p":"Let a king employ spies so that one may have no knowledge of the other; and when the information of three agrees together, let him receive it.","r":"Run spies so that one does not know of another, and accept what three say in agreement.","n":"This is a statement about independent evidence: agreement counts only among witnesses who could not have conferred. Parimelazhagar adds that agreement need only be in substance (பயனால்), and that if the accounts differ, the inquiry continues.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["otru","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:59"},"k:590":{"book":"kural","num":590,"t":["சிறப்பறிய ஒற்றின்கண் செய்யற்க செய்யின்","புறப்படுத்தான் ஆகும் மறை"],"p":"Let not a king publicly confer on a spy any marks of his favour; if he does, he will divulge his own secret.","r":"Do not honour a spy where others can see it; if the king does, he has himself let out the secret.","n":"The exposure comes from the patron, not the spy, and from gratitude, not carelessness: the honour itself is the leak. The verse does not forbid the reward, only its being seen.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["otru"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:59"},"k:591":{"book":"kural","num":591,"t":["உடையர் எனப்படுவது ஊக்கம் அஃ தில்லார்","உடையது உடையரோ மற்று"],"p":"Energy makes out the man of property; as for those who are destitute of it, do they (really) possess what they possess ?","r":"What makes someone 'one who has' is energy of mind; without it, do people really have even what they have?","n":"Ownership is put in question, not merely wealth's worth. Parimelazhagar supplies a practical reason: without the power to guard it, they will lose it too.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["ookkam","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:60"},"k:592":{"book":"kural","num":592,"t":["உள்ளம் உடைமை உடைமை பொருளுடைமை","நில்லாது நீங்கி விடும்"],"p":"The possession of (energy of) mind is true property; the possession of wealth passes away and abides not.","r":"Possession of the inner drive is real possession; possession of goods does not stay and slips away.","n":"The verse's word is உள்ளம், not ஊக்கம். Parimelazhagar makes the identification by metonymy, and Pope's '(energy of)' imports it, so the verse itself may be claiming somewhat more broadly that the mind is the true holding.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ookkam","selvam","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:60"},"k:593":{"book":"kural","num":593,"t":["க்கம் இழந்தேமென்று அல்லாவார் ஊக்கம்","ஒருவந்தம் கைத்துடை யார்"],"p":"They who are possessed of enduring energy will not trouble themselves, saying, \"we have lost our property.\"","r":"Those who hold steady energy as their cash in hand do not fret, 'We have lost our gains'.","n":"கைத்து is money in the hand, and the image is the verse's point: energy is liquid in a way that lost property is not. Their calm is a calculation of assets, not detachment, and this differs from the equanimity grounded in fate at 379.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["ookkam","selvam","samanilai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:60"},"k:594":{"book":"kural","num":594,"t":["க்கம் அதர்வினாய்ச் செல்லும் அசைவிலா","ஊக்க முடையா னுழை"],"p":"Wealth will find its own way to the man of unfailing energy.","r":"Prosperity itself asks the way and goes to the one whose energy does not waver.","n":"Wealth is personified as a traveller who asks directions, so the seeker has become the sought. Parimelazhagar glosses அசைவின்மை as not flagging in hardship.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["ookkam","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:60"},"k:595":{"book":"kural","num":595,"t":["வெள்ளத் தனைய மலர்நீட்டம் மாந்தர்தம்","உள்ளத் தனையது உயர்வு"],"p":"The stalks of water-flowers are proportionate to the depth of water; so is men's greatness proportionate to their minds.","r":"The length of a water-flower's stem matches the depth of the water, and a person's height matches their inner drive.","n":"Parimelazhagar notes the point of the image: the stem keeps lengthening as long as the water rises, so there is no fixed ceiling. The analogy has a gap, though: water is outside the plant, whereas the mind is within. 'Lotus' is Pope's and Kalaignar's addition; the verse says only மலர்.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ookkam","manam","perumai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The height gained is worldly: increase in wealth and military force.","உயர்தல் - பொருள் படைகளான் மிகுதல்."],["manakkudavar","What is measured by the mind is energy itself, so the verse says how energy arises.","மாந்தரது உள்ளத்தின் அளவினது ஊக்கம்"]],"ch":"k:60"},"k:596":{"book":"kural","num":596,"t":["உள்ளுவ தெல்லாம் உயர்வுள்ளல் மற்றது","தள்ளினுந் தள்ளாமை நீர்த்து"],"p":"In all that a king thinks of, let him think of his greatness; and if it should be thrust from him (by fate), it will have the nature of not being thrust from him.","r":"Whatever you think about, think of rising; even if it is thrust aside, it has the quality of not having been thrust aside.","n":"The verse names nothing that thrusts the aim aside. 'Fate' comes from Pope and from Parimelazhagar's பால்வகை, so this is less clearly a verse about fate than it appears in English.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ookkam","muyarchi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Even if fate denies kings the height, the aim stands: good people will not blame it, since their exertion did not fail.","அவ்வுயர்ச்சி பால்வகையாற் கூடிற்றில்லையாயினும், அக்கருத்துத் தள்ளாமை நீர்மையுடைத்து."],["manakkudavar","A failed high aim is equal to having achieved it, and is beyond blame.","முயன்று பெற்றதனோடு ஒக்கும்"],["kalaignar","This is an instruction: even if it does not succeed, do not give up the thought.","அதற்காக அந்த நினைப்பை விடக்கூடாது."]],"ch":"k:60"},"k:597":{"book":"kural","num":597,"t":["சிதைவிடத்து ஒல்கார் உரவோர் புதையம்பிற்","பட்டுப்பா டூன்றுங் களிறு"],"p":"The strong minded will not faint, even when all is lost; the elephant stands firm, even when wounded by a shower of arrows.","r":"The strong in mind do not give way when things fall apart, as a bull elephant holds its ground under a sheaf of arrows.","n":"Parimelazhagar reads a crossover: 'not giving way' belongs to the elephant and 'upholding one's greatness' to the strong, so that each half lends the other its verb.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ookkam","samanilai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:60"},"k:598":{"book":"kural","num":598,"t":["உள்ளம் இலாதவர் எய்தார் உலகத்து","வள்ளியம் என்னுஞ் செருக்கு"],"p":"Those who have no (greatness of) mind, will not acquire the joy of saying in the world, \"we have excercised liaberality\".","r":"Those without inner drive never reach, in this world, the proud joy of saying 'we have been generous'.","n":"செருக்கு is a fault in 431, yet here it is a joy worth wanting. Generosity is presented as something one may feel proud about, and this is not a usual picture of selfless giving.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["ookkam","velanmai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","உள்ளம் is energy: without it the chain of effort, wealth, giving and pride never starts.","ஊக்கம் இல்லையாகவே முயற்சி, பொருள், கொடை செருக்கு இவை முறையே இலவாம்"],["kalaignar","உள்ளம் is the eagerness to give: without it one can't take pride in being a giver.","அள்ளி வழங்கும் ஆர்வம் இல்லாத ஒருவர்"]],"ch":"k:60"},"k:599":{"book":"kural","num":599,"t":["பரியது கூர்ங்கோட்டது ஆயினும் யானை","஦வ்ருஉம் புலிதாக் குறின்"],"p":"Although the elephant has a large body, and a sharp tusk, yet it fears the attack of the tiger.","r":"Though its body is huge and its tusks are sharp, an elephant fears when a tiger attacks.","n":"The verse says nothing about energy or kings. Parimelazhagar names it an allegory (பிறிது மொழிதல்), and the chapter's setting is all that justifies the moral.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["ookkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:60"},"k:600":{"book":"kural","num":600,"t":["உரமொருவற்கு உள்ள வெறுக்கைஅஃ தில்லார்","மரம்மக்க ளாதலே வேறு"],"p":"Energy is mental wealth; those men who are destitute of it are only trees in the form of men.","r":"A person's real firmness is the abundance of the inner drive; those without it are trees, and being human is the only difference.","n":"Parimelazhagar sharpens the insult: trees bear fruit and serve in temples, houses, chariots and ships, so the only thing left separating such people from trees is that they are not as useful. He also reads உரம் as understanding, as in 24, which joins will and intelligence.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["ookkam","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:60"},"k:601":{"book":"kural","num":601,"t":["குடியென்னும் குன்றா விளக்கம் மடியென்னும்","மாசூர மாய்ந்து கெடும்"],"p":"By the darkness, of idleness, the indestructible lamp of family (rank) will be extinguished.","r":"The unfailing lamp called lineage goes dark and dies when the grime called sloth creeps over it.","n":"A lamp said to be unfailing fails. Parimelazhagar explains மாசு through tamas (தாமத குணம்); that is his Samkhya vocabulary, which the verse does not use.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["madi","kudi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:61"},"k:602":{"book":"kural","num":602,"t":["மடியை மடியா ஒழுகல் குடியைக்","குடியாக வேண்டு பவர்"],"p":"Let those, who desire that their family may be illustrious, put away all idleness from their conduct.","r":"Those who want their family to be a family should conduct themselves with sloth treated as sloth.","n":"The repetition is the verse's device: மடியை மடியா and குடியைக் குடியாக. To be a family is an achievement here, not a birth fact.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["madi","kudi","muyarchi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","மடியா means 'as sloth': regard it for what it is, as one says to treat fire as fire. He records the other reading.","நெருப்பிற் கொடியது பிறிதின்மை பற்றி, நெருப்பை நெருப்பாகவே கருதுக"],["manakkudavar","மடியா means killing: destroy the habit of sloth.","மடி செய்தலை மடித்து ஒழுகுக"]],"ch":"k:61"},"k:603":{"book":"kural","num":603,"t":["மடிமடிக் கொண்டொழுகும் பேதை பிறந்த","குடிமடியும் தன்னினும் முந்து"],"p":"The (lustre of the) family of the ignorant man, who acts under the influence of destructive laziness will perish, even before he is dead.","r":"The family of a fool who carries deadly sloth inside him dies before he does.","n":"The cruelty is in the order: he survives to see it. Parimelazhagar's reason is that the family depends on him for support.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["madi","kudi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:61"},"k:604":{"book":"kural","num":604,"t":["குடிமடிந்து குற்றம் பெருகும் மடிமடிந்து","மாண்ட உஞற்றி லவர்க்கு"],"p":"Family (greatness) will be destroyed, and faults will increase, in those men who give way to laziness, and put forth no dignified exertions.","r":"For those sunk in sloth without any excellent effort, the family declines and faults multiply.","n":"The contrast is not sloth against mere activity but against மாண்ட உஞற்று, refined exertion. The verse thus sets a higher bar than being busy; 607 repeats the phrase.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["madi","kudi","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:61"},"k:605":{"book":"kural","num":605,"t":["நெடுநீர் மறவி மடிதுயில் நான்கும்","கெடுநீரார் காமக் கலன்"],"p":"Procrastination, forgetfulness, idleness, and sleep, these four things, form the vessel which is desired by those destined to destruction.","r":"Delay, forgetting, sloth and sleep, these four, are the pleasure-craft of those bound for ruin.","n":"The image lies in காமக் கலன்: a boat you board because you want to, and which then drops you mid-sea. Parimelazhagar records but does not accept a reading of கலன் as a desired ornament. Manakkudavar reads the four as one sequence: delaying a task, forgetting it, slacking, and sleeping instead.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["madi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:61"},"k:606":{"book":"kural","num":606,"t":["படியுடையார் பற்றமைந்தக் கண்ணும் மடியுடையார்","மாண்பயன் எய்தல் அரிது"],"p":"It is a rare thing for the idle, even when possessed of the riches of kings who ruled over the whole earth, to derive any great benefit from it.","r":"Even when what world-rulers possess comes to them, the slothful rarely reach any great fruit.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["madi","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The wealth of world-rulers comes to them unearned, but without effort to guard it, it perishes. He records another reading, that such kings become their allies.","நிலம் முழுவதும் ஆண்டாரது செல்வம் தானே வந்து எய்திய இடத்தும்"],["manakkudavar","It is the kings of the earth themselves who, however well provided, lose out if they are slothful.","பூமியையுடைய வேந்தர் பல்பொருளினாலும் அமைந்த விடத் தும் மடியுடையாராயின்"],["kalaignar","படி means worthiness: even those who hold the affection of worthy people get nothing.","தகுதியுடையவரின் அன்புக்குப் பாத்திரமானவராக இருப்பினும்"]],"ch":"k:61"},"k:607":{"book":"kural","num":607,"t":["இடிபுரிந்து எள்ளுஞ் சொல் கேட்பர் மடிபுரிந்து","மாண்ட உஞற்றி லவர்"],"p":"Those who through idleness, and do not engage themselves in dignified exertion, will subject themselves to rebukes and reproaches.","r":"Those who cling to sloth and make no excellent effort end up hearing words of rebuke and scorn.","n":"கேட்பர் may hold the sting: they can only listen. Parimelazhagar builds a small story from இடி: friends scold first, see no result, and only then turn to contempt, and if friends do this, others need not be mentioned.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["madi","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:61"},"k:608":{"book":"kural","num":608,"t":["மடிமை குடிமைக்கண் தங்கின்தன் ஒன்னார்க்கு","அடிமை புகுத்தி விடும்"],"p":"If idleness take up its abode in a king of high birth, it will make him a slave of his enemies.","r":"If sloth settles in one of a family-building nature, it delivers him into slavery to his enemies.","n":"மடிமை, குடிமை and அடிமை rhyme in sequence, and the sound performs the fall from sloth through standing to bondage.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["madi","kudi","adimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:61"},"k:609":{"book":"kural","num":609,"t":["குடியாண்மை யுள்வந்த குற்றம் ஒருவன்","மடியாண்மை மாற்றக் கெடும்"],"p":"When a man puts away idleness, the reproach which has come upon himself and his family will disappear.","r":"When a person ends the rule of sloth over him, the faults that have come into his family and his manhood are gone.","n":"Sloth is a ruler here (மடியாண்மை), and one's own effort deposes it. This is the chapter's one verse of repair. Parimelazhagar makes the promise wide, since even faults from other causes vanish; Manakkudavar limits the faults to neglecting what the family needs.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["madi","kudi","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:61"},"k:610":{"book":"kural","num":610,"t":["மடியிலா மன்னவன் எய்தும் அடியளந்தான்","தாஅய தெல்லாம் ஒருங்கு"],"p":"The king who never gives way to idleness will obtain entire possession of (the whole earth) passed over by him who measured (the worlds) with His foot.","r":"A ruler free from sloth obtains, all at once, the whole expanse crossed by the one who measured with his foot.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["madi","muyarchi","kol"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The one who measured is the deity who spanned all worlds with his stride, and the verse leaves him unnamed.","தன் அடியளவானே எல்லா உலகையும் அளந்த இறைவன்"],["kalaignar","It is a tireless human king who brings every place he goes within his footstep's range.","சோர்வில்லாத ஒரு மன்னன், அவன் சென்ற இடமனைத்தையும்"]],"ch":"k:61"},"k:611":{"book":"kural","num":611,"t":["அருமை உடைத்தென்று அசாவாமை வேண்டும்","பெருமை முயற்சி தரும்"],"p":"Yield not to the feebleness which says, \"this is too difficult to be done\"; labour will give the greatness (of mind) which is necessary (to do it).","r":"One must not slacken by saying 'this is hard', because effort brings the greatness needed.","n":"Difficulty is measured against a self that labour itself enlarges, so the calculation 'this is too much for me' is wrong in its premise. Parimelazhagar makes this explicit.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["muyarchi","perumai","aalvinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:62"},"k:612":{"book":"kural","num":612,"t":["வினைக்கண் வினைகெடல் ஓம்பல் வினைக்குறை","தீர்ந்தாரின் தீர்ந்தன்று உலகு"],"p":"Take care not to give up exertion in the midst of a work; the world will abandon those who abandon their unfinished work.","r":"Guard against letting a task die in the middle of the doing, for the world's bond with a person turns on how they have dealt with the work's unmet need.","n":"A short verse gives opposite readings of தீர்ந்தார்: those who left the work or those who finished it. Parimelazhagar glosses குறை from Purananuru 188, which is a thread beyond the Kural.","x":1,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aalvinai","muyarchi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","தீர்ந்தார் are those who abandoned the needed work, and the world abandons them.","வினையாகிய குறையைச் செய்யாது விட்டாரை உலகம் விட்டது"],["manakkudavar","தீர்ந்தார் are those who completed it, and the world does not leave them.","வினைக் குறையை முடித்தாரினின்றும் உலகம் விடப்பட்டதன்று"]],"ch":"k:62"},"k:613":{"book":"kural","num":613,"t":["தாளாண்மை என்னும் தகைமைக்கண் தங்கிற்றே","வேளாண்மை என்னுஞ் செருக்கு"],"p":"The lustre of munificence will dwell only with the dignity of laboriousness or efforts.","r":"The proud distinction called helping others stands on the quality called labour.","n":"Manakkudavar draws the implication plainly: even doing virtue comes from this. A moral excellence has an economic foundation, and தாளாண்மை and வேளாண்மை rhyme to tie them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aalvinai","velanmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:62"},"k:614":{"book":"kural","num":614,"t":["தாளாண்மை இல்லாதான் வேளாண்மை பேடிகை","வாளாண்மை போலக் கெடும்"],"p":"The liberality of him, who does not labour, will fail, like the manliness of a hermaphrodite, who has a sword in its hand.","r":"The generosity of one who won't labour comes to nothing, like a sword in the hand of a பேடி.","n":"The image depends on a period contempt for a third-gender person, which commentators extend by saying the figure wishes to fight but is too afraid. Manakkudavar alone drops the word and says 'one who fears at the sight of arms'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aalvinai","velanmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:62"},"k:615":{"book":"kural","num":615,"t":["இன்பம் விழையான் வினைவிழைவான் தன்கேளிர்","துன்பம் துடைத்தூன்றும் தூண்"],"p":"He who desires not pleasure, but desires labour, will be a pillar to sustain his relations, wiping away their sorrows.","r":"One who desires the work and not pleasure is a pillar who wipes away his kin's troubles and bears them up.","n":"Parimelazhagar finds a principle of causes here: wanting the cause rather than the effect gets all effects, and wanting the effect without the cause gets nothing. The pillar stands under others' weight, so personal renunciation of pleasure is here for one's household, not for liberation.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aalvinai","sutram","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:62"},"k:616":{"book":"kural","num":616,"t":["முயற்சி திருவினை ஆக்கும் முயற்றின்மை","இன்மை புகுத்தி விடும்"],"p":"Labour will produce wealth; idleness will bring poverty.","r":"Effort builds prosperity, and the absence of effort ushers in the absence of everything.","n":"முயற்றின்மை இன்மை: absence brings absence. The verse's symmetry leaves no room for a third cause, and that is what 371 and 377 dispute.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["muyarchi","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","திருவினை means wealth, and specifically a king's six limbs of state.","அரசர்மாட்டு உளதாய முயற்சி அவரது செல்வத்தினை வளர்க்கும்"],["kalaignar","It yields excellent deeds and achievements, not wealth as such.","முயற்சிதான் சிறப்பான செயல்பாடுகளுக்குக் காரணமாக அமையும்."]],"ch":"k:62"},"k:617":{"book":"kural","num":617,"t":["மடியுளாள் மாமுகடி என்ப மடியிலான்","தாளுளான் தாமரையி னாள்"],"p":"They say that the black Mudevi (the goddess of adversity) dwells with laziness, and the Latchmi (the goddess of prosperity) dwells with the labour of the industrious.","r":"They say the dark goddess of misfortune lives in sloth, and she of the lotus lives in the labour of the unslothful.","n":"என்ப, 'they say', keeps a little distance from the claim: the verse reports it. தாள் can mean both foot and exertion, and so Lakshmi may be at the feet of the one who works.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["madi","aalvinai","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The goddesses are taken as they are, dwelling respectively in sloth and in effort, with sin's blackness assigned to the first.","பாவத்தின் கருமை அதன் பயனாக முகடிமேல் ஏற்றப்பட்டது."],["kalaignar","The names are mere words used to point at the energetic person and the idler.","சொற்கள் முறையே முயற்சியில் ஊக்கமுடையவரையும்,"]],"ch":"k:62"},"k:618":{"book":"kural","num":618,"t":["பொறியின்மை யார்க்கும் பழியன்று அறிவறிந்து","ஆள்வினை இன்மை பழி"],"p":"Adverse fate is no disgrace to any one; to be without exertion and without knowing what should be known, is disgrace.","r":"Lacking good fortune is no one's disgrace; failing to work, after learning what must be known, is the disgrace.","n":"The verse does not say effort defeats fate. It moves the question from causes to blame, and that is a different and more defensible claim than 620's. அறிவறிந்து adds a condition: blind labour isn't what is being asked for.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","aalvinai","arivu","pazhi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","பொறி is fate: people blame present faults, not fruits of past deeds.","உலகம் பழவினை பற்றிப் பழியாது, ஈண்டைக் குற்றமுடைமை பற்றியே பழிப்பது"],["manakkudavar","பொறி is religious merit: lacking it is no fault, and this answers those who say effort is useless without merit.","யார்க்கும் புண்ணியமின்மை குற்றமாகாது"],["kalaignar","The idea of blame from fate is itself wrong.","விதிப்பயனால் பழி ஏற்படும் என்பது தவறு"]],"ch":"k:62"},"k:619":{"book":"kural","num":619,"t":["தெய்வத்தான் ஆகா தெனினும் முயற்சிதன்","மெய்வருத்தக் கூலி தரும்"],"p":"Although it be said that, through fate, it cannot be attained, yet labour, with bodily exertion, will yield its reward.","r":"Though divine order may deny the end sought, effort still pays the wage of the body's strain.","n":"கூலி is a modest word: a labourer's wage, not the prize. The verse concedes the goal may be lost, and secures only a floor.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["muyarchi","oozh"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","தெய்வம் is fate: where it withholds, one gets a wage, and where it favours, much more. So don't wait on it.","முயன்ற வினை பால்வகையால் கருதிய பயனைத் தாராதாயினும்"],["manakkudavar","தெய்வம் stands for merit: without it there's no gain, yet effort pays in proportion to strain.","புண்ணியம் இன்மையால் ஆக்கம் இல்லையாயினும்"],["kalaignar","It refers to God and prayer: what crying out to God won't achieve, labour will.","கடவுளே என்று கூவி அழைப்பதால் நடக்காத காரியம்"]],"ch":"k:62"},"k:620":{"book":"kural","num":620,"t":["ஊழையும் உப்பக்கம் காண்பர் உலைவின்றித்","தாழாது உஞற்று பவர்"],"p":"They who labour on, without fear and without fainting will see even fate (put) behind their back.","r":"Those who toil on without flagging or delay will see even fate's back.","n":"உப்பக்கம் காண்பர் is a battle image: to see an enemy's back as it retreats. Pope's rendering, with fate put 'behind their back', turns the image around.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","aalvinai","muyarchi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Fate can obstruct once or twice but not always, so repeated effort outlasts it.","ஊழ் ஒருகாலாக இருகாலாக அல்லது விலக்கலாகாமையின், பலகால் முயல்வார் பயன் எய்துவர்"],["manakkudavar","They actually conquer fate, turning back the bad karma that blocks the fruit.","இஃது ஊழ்தன்னையும் வெல்வ ரென்றது."]],"ch":"k:62"},"k:621":{"book":"kural","num":621,"t":["இடுக்கண் வருங்கால் நகுக அதனை","அடுத்தூர்வது அஃதொப்ப தில்"],"p":"If troubles come, laugh; there is nothing like that, to press upon and drive away sorrow.","r":"When trouble comes, laugh; nothing else presses back on it as well.","n":"The laugh is treated as a tactic, not a sign of being unaffected. Parimelazhagar says it borrows the joy of a finished task in the middle of the task, and that the lift it gives supplies the strength to push through.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idukkan","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:63"},"k:622":{"book":"kural","num":622,"t":["வெள்ளத் தனைய இடும்பை அறிவுடையான்","உள்ளத்தின் உள்ளக் கெடும்"],"p":"A flood of troubles will be overcome by the (courageous) thought which the minds of the wise will entertain, even in sorrow.","r":"Even a flood of suffering is gone when a person of understanding merely thinks it through in the mind.","n":"The verse doesn't say which thought. That gap is the whole dispute, and Pope's '(courageous)' fills it with one more answer.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idukkan","arivu","manam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Suffering is nothing but a stance in the mind, and it vanishes when that stance is reversed.","இடும்பையாவது உள்ளத்து ஒரு கோட்பாடேயன்றிப் பிறிதில்லை"],["manakkudavar","The thought is that this is the fruit of one's deeds.","நெஞ்சினாலே வினைப்பய னென்று நினைக்கக் கெடும்"],["kalaignar","The thought is a practical one: how to defeat the trouble.","அதனை வெல்லும் வழி யாது என்பதை அறிவுடையவர்கள் நினைத்த மாத்திரத்திலேயே"]],"ch":"k:63"},"k:623":{"book":"kural","num":623,"t":["இடும்பைக்கு இடும்பை படுப்பர் இடும்பைக்கு","இடும்பை படாஅ தவர்"],"p":"They give sorrow to sorrow, who in sorrow do not suffer sorrow.","r":"Those who do not suffer at suffering make suffering suffer.","n":"The verse enacts its turn in four occurrences of one word. This reversal of subject and object recurs in 624 and 625, so the chapter's signature is grammatical. Parimelazhagar gives the mechanism: persisting finishes the task, and finishing ends all the troubles.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idukkan","porai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:63"},"k:624":{"book":"kural","num":624,"t":["மடுத்தவா யெல்லாம் பகடன்னான் உற்ற","இடுக்கண் இடர்ப்பாடு உடைத்து"],"p":"Troubles will vanish (i.e., will be troubled) before the man who (struggles against difficulties) as a buffalo (drawing a cart) through deep mire.","r":"Trouble that meets one who pulls through every obstruction like a draught ox itself has trouble.","n":"Nothing about this image is heroic: Parimelazhagar cites Civakacintamani's picture of the ox on its side, nose to the ground. It sits beside the elephant of 597 and shows a humbler kind of strength.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idukkan","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:63"},"k:625":{"book":"kural","num":625,"t":["அடுக்கி வரினும் அழிவிலான் உற்ற","இடுக்கண் இடுக்கட் படும்"],"p":"The troubles of that man will be troubled (and disappear) who, however thickly they may come upon him , does not abandon (his purpose).","r":"Though they come stacked one on another, the troubles of one who is not undone are themselves in trouble.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idukkan","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:63"},"k:626":{"book":"kural","num":626,"t":["அற்றேமென்று அல்லற் படுபவோ பெற்றேமென்று","ஓம்புதல் தேற்றா தவர்"],"p":"Will those men ever cry out in sorrow, \"we are destitute\" who, (in their prosperity), give not way to (undue desire) to keep their wealth.","r":"Would those who never knew how to clutch at wealth, saying 'we have got it', grieve, saying 'we have lost it'?","n":"Grief at losing is settled earlier, at the moment of having. The verse praises a lack of skill (தேற்றாதவர்), which is a wry way to describe a virtue.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idukkan","selvam","samanilai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:63"},"k:627":{"book":"kural","num":627,"t":["இலக்கம் உடம்பிடும்பைக் கென்று கலக்கத்தைக்","கையாறாக் கொள்ளாதாம் மேல்"],"p":"The great will not regard trouble as trouble, knowing that the body is the butt of trouble.","r":"Knowing that the body is a target for suffering, they do not take trouble as a thing to be shaken by.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idukkan","manam","samanilai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","மேல் means 'upon them', and the verse concerns bodies in all four states of birth, targets for a sword called suffering.","நாற்கதியினும் உள்ள உடம்புகள் இடும்பை என்னும் வாளுக்கு இலக்கு என்று தெளிந்து"],["manakkudavar","மேல் means 'the superior', and the context is the hardship taken on in austerity.","இது மேல் நன்மையாற் றவஞ் செய்யுங்கால் வருந் துன்பத்திற்கு அழியாதா ரைக் கூறிற்று."]],"ch":"k:63"},"k:628":{"book":"kural","num":628,"t":["இன்பம் விழையான் இடும்பை இயல்பென்பான்","துன்பம் உறுதல் இலன்"],"p":"That man never experiences sorrow, who does not seek for pleasure, and who considers distress to be natural (to man).","r":"One who does not chase pleasure and calls suffering natural does not come to pain.","n":"Pain is prevented by an expectation, not a defence. Parimelazhagar sees two conditions: wanting pleasure brings pain, and so does trying to ward off suffering as if it were not natural.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idukkan","samanilai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:63"},"k:629":{"book":"kural","num":629,"t":["இன்பத்துள் இன்பம் விழையாதான் துன்பத்துள்","துன்பம் உறுதல் இலன்"],"p":"He does not suffer sorrow, in sorrow who does not look for pleasure in pleasure.","r":"One who does not crave the pleasure within pleasure does not feel the pain within pain.","n":"The experience is not denied: the phrasing இன்பத்துள், துன்பத்துள் places a person within each. Parimelazhagar says they undergo them, yet do not desire or grieve, so that the deeds bear no fruit. That karmic consequence is his addition.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["samanilai","idukkan"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Pleasure and pain here are those arising through work and effort, and both are held in the same way.","இரண்டையும் ஒரு தன்மையாகக் கோடலின், பயன்களும் இலவாயின."],["manakkudavar","It concerns desire: one who doesn't lose himself in sensual pleasure isn't undone by the pain it brings.","இது காமத்தால் வரும் துன்பத்திற்கு அழியாதாரைக் கூறிற்று."]],"ch":"k:63"},"k:630":{"book":"kural","num":630,"t":["இன்னாமை இன்பம் எனக்கொளின் ஆகுந்தன்","ஒன்னார் விழையுஞ் சிறப்பு"],"p":"The elevation, which even his enemies will esteem, will be gained by him, who regards pain as pleasure.","r":"If a person takes what hurts as pleasure, there comes to him a distinction even his enemies admire.","n":"The reward is a rival's respect, which is the one kind that can't be flattery. Parimelazhagar's reason is philosophical: pain is no part of the self but a passing mental stance (கணிகம், momentary), which the verse does not claim.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idukkan","manam","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:63"},"k:631":{"book":"kural","num":631,"t":["கருவியும் காலமும் செய்கையும் செய்யும்","அருவினையும் மாண்டது அமைச்சு"],"p":"The minister is one who can make an excellent choice of means, time, manner of execution, and the difficult undertaking (itself).","r":"A minister is one excellent in means, timing, method and the difficult task itself.","n":"Manakkudavar makes this concrete in a military way: the hard task is seizing another's realm, the means elephants and horses, and the time a season with water and shade. The verse itself says nothing so specific.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:64"},"k:632":{"book":"kural","num":632,"t":["வன்கண் குடிகாத்தல் கற்றறிதல் ஆள்வினையோடு","ஐந்துடன் மாண்டது அமைச்சு"],"p":"The minister is one who in addition to the aforesaid five things excels in the possession of firmness, protection of subjects, clearness by learning, and perseverance.","r":"A minister is one excellent in five things together, including resolve, protecting the people, learned knowing and labour.","n":"வன்கண் is hard-heartedness when it condemns impostors in 276, but here it is a qualification for office. The word's moral value depends on where it is pointed.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","kalvi","aalvinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","This verse lists four, and 'the five' are those of 631. He criticises those who split items here to reach five.","மேற்சொல்லிய அங்கங்கள் ஐந்துடனே திருந்த உடையானே அமைச்சனாவான்"],["manakkudavar","The five are named in this verse, including the guarding of the senses, and are needed before one becomes a minister.","அஞ்சாமையும், குடிகாத்தலும், இந்திரியங்களைக் காத்தலும், நூல் முகத்தானறிதலும், முயற்சியும்"]],"ch":"k:64"},"k:633":{"book":"kural","num":633,"t":["பிரித்தலும் பேணிக் கொளலும் பிரிந்தார்ப்","பொருத்தலும் வல்ல தமைச்சு"],"p":"The minister is one who can effect discord (among foes), maintain the good-will of his friends and restore to friendship those who have seceded (from him).","r":"A minister is one able to divide others, keep his own side close, and bring back those who have broken away.","n":"The verse states three skills of division and joining, with no moral comment. Kalaignar's '(for the nation's good)' and his 'those who reformed' are moral framing added by him. Parimelazhagar links two of them to Sanskrit terms.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:64"},"k:634":{"book":"kural","num":634,"t":["தெரிதலும் தேர்ந்து செயலும் ஒருதலையாச்","சொல்லலும் வல்லது அமைச்சு"],"p":"The minister is one who is able to comprehend (the whole nature of an undertaking), execute it in the best manner possible, and offer assuring advice (in time of necessity).","r":"A minister is one able to discern, to act after weighing, and to speak in a way that settles the matter.","n":"ஒருதலையாச் சொல்லல் doesn't name its audience. For Parimelazhagar the settling speech is aimed at the parties being divided or joined, and for Manakkudavar it settles doubtful matters, so eloquence here is persuasion on one view and judgement on the other.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:64"},"k:635":{"book":"kural","num":635,"t":["அறனறிந்து ஆன்றமைந்த சொல்லான்எஞ் ஞான்றுந்","திறனறிந்தான் தேர்ச்சித் துணை"],"p":"He is the best helper (of the king) who understanding the duties, of the latter, is by his special learning, able to tender the fullest advice, and at all times conversant with the best method (of","r":"One who knows what is right, whose speech is rich and settled, and who always knows the ways of doing things is the companion for deliberation.","n":"Virtue comes first in the list, in a chapter otherwise about competence. But Parimelazhagar narrows it to 'virtues done by the king', which makes it knowledge of duties more than the minister's own goodness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["amaichu","aram","solvanmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:64"},"k:636":{"book":"kural","num":636,"t":["மதிநுட்பம் நூலோடு உடையார்க்கு அதிநுட்பம்","யாவுள முன்நிற் பவை"],"p":"What (contrivances) are there so acute as to resist those who possess natural acuteness in addition to learning ?.","r":"What over-subtle scheme can stand before those who have keen native intelligence together with treatises?","n":"Parimelazhagar names மதிநுட்பம் first since it must be given by the divine, leaving effort to the learning alone. His contrast of natural and artificial knowledge is a useful frame for the next verse. He also defends the text அதிநுட்பம் against அதினுட்பம், 'finer than that', the sense Manakkudavar's gloss gives; the verse's point survives either way.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["amaichu","arivu","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:64"},"k:637":{"book":"kural","num":637,"t":["செயற்கை அறிந்தக் கடைத்தும் உலகத்து","இயற்கை அறிந்து செயல்"],"p":"Though you are acquainted with the (theoretical) methods (of performing an act), understand the ways of the world and act accordingly.","r":"Even after knowing the method of doing a thing, act with knowledge of how the world goes.","n":"செயற்கை and இயற்கை are opposed: the made and the given. In this verse a treatise's knowledge is the artifice and the world is the nature, which is a surprising way to rank them.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","arivu","kalvi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Treatise methods must fit the prevailing ways of the world, or the world will blame them.","நூல் நெறியே ஆயினும் உலக நெறியோடு பொருந்தாதன செய்யற்க"],["manakkudavar","'The world' means the court: the character of kings and the standing of their retinue.","உலகநடை அறிதலாவது அரசர் சீலமும் பரிவாரத்திலுள்ளார் நிலைமையும் அறிதல்."]],"ch":"k:64"},"k:638":{"book":"kural","num":638,"t":["அறிகொன்று அறியான் எனினும் உறுதி","உழையிருந்தான் கூறல் கடன்"],"p":"Although the king be utterly ignorant, it is the duty of the minister to give (him) sound advice.","r":"Though the king destroys wise counsel and himself knows nothing, the one at his side is bound to tell him what is good for him.","n":"The word for the minister here is உழையிருந்தான், 'the one close by'. Duty arises from nearness, and Parimelazhagar adds that the world will lay the king's fall at the silent minister's door.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","அறிகொன்று means destroying the understanding of those who advise, even with contempt.","அறிந்து சொல்லியாரது அறிவையும் அழித்து அரசன் தானும் அறியானே ஆயினும்"],["manakkudavar","He splits the phrase differently: 'let the king know' what the minister said, and even if he knows nothing, the minister must speak.","அரசன் அமைச்சன் கூறிய பொருளை யறிக"]],"ch":"k:64"},"k:639":{"book":"kural","num":639,"t":["பழுதெண்ணும் மந்திரியின் பக்கததுள் தெவ்வோர்","எழுபது கோடி உறும்"],"p":"Far better are seventy crores of enemies (for a king) than a minister at his side who intends (his) ruin.","r":"Better seventy crore enemies than a minister at one's side who plots harm.","n":"Parimelazhagar's reason is that open enemies can be guarded against, but the one inside cannot. 'Seventy crore' is his example of a huge number, not a figure with meaning.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["amaichu","pagai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:64"},"k:640":{"book":"kural","num":640,"t":["முறைப்படச் சூழ்ந்தும் முடிவிலவே செய்வர்","திறப்பாடு இலாஅ தவர்"],"p":"Those ministers who are destitute of (executive) ability will fail to carry out their projects, although they may have contrived aright.","r":"Though they plan things properly, those without practical capacity do only what comes to no conclusion.","n":"A good plan is not enough. Parimelazhagar splits capacity into knowing remedies for obstacles and being firm in oneself, so this anticipates the chapter on firmness in action.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:64"},"k:641":{"book":"kural","num":641,"t":["நாநலம் என்னும் நலனுடைமை அந்நலம்","யாநலத்து உள்ளதூஉம் அன்று"],"p":"The possession of that goodness which is called the goodness of speech is (even to others) better than any other goodness.","r":"Having the goodness called goodness of tongue is a good that isn't contained in any other good.","n":"The verse is about the excellence of speech as such; it does not mention ministers. Parimelazhagar draws them in from the chapter's position and also argues that if others need it, ministers need it more.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["solvanmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:65"},"k:642":{"book":"kural","num":642,"t":["ஆக்கமுங் கேடும் அதனால் வருதலால்","காத்தோம்பல் சொல்லின்கட் சோர்வு"],"p":"Since (both) wealth and evil result from (their) speech, ministers should most carefully guard themselves against faultiness therein.","r":"Since gain and ruin both come from it, guard against any lapse in speech.","n":"சோர்வு is a slip, not malice. Parimelazhagar widens the stakes: a minister's lapse falls on all living beings at once.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["solvanmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:65"},"k:643":{"book":"kural","num":643,"t":["கேட்டார்ப் பிணிக்கும் தகையவாய்க் கேளாரும்","வேட்ப மொழிவதாம் சொல்"],"p":"The (minister's) speech is that which seeks (to express) elements as bind his friends (to himself) and is so delivered as to make even his enemies desire (his friendship).","r":"Speech is what is said so that it binds those who hear it and makes even those who have not heard want it.","n":"The second half is the striking one: speech is judged partly by its effect on those not present. Parimelazhagar lists good speech's qualities here as freedom from error, brevity, clarity, sweetness and valuable result.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["solvanmai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Hearers are allies who accepted him and non-hearers are hostile parties won over. He criticises all others for reading தகையவாய் as 'having worth'.","நட்பாய் ஏற்றுக் கொண்டாரைப் பின் வேறுபடாமல் பிணிக்கும் குணங்களை அவாவி"],["manakkudavar","Those are the ones who asked and the ones who did not ask.","வினாவினாரைப் பிணித்துக் கொள்ளுந் தகையவாய், வினாவாதா ரும் விரும்புமாறு"]],"ch":"k:65"},"k:644":{"book":"kural","num":644,"t":["திறனறிந்து சொல்லுக சொல்லை அறனும்","பொருளும் அதனினூஉங்கு இல்"],"p":"Understand the qualities (of your hearers) and (then) make your speech; for superior to it, there is neither virtue nor wealth.","r":"Speak with knowledge of what the moment and hearer require, for no virtue or gain goes beyond that.","n":"A surprising claim: fit speech ranks at the top of both virtue and gain. Parimelazhagar explains that the cause stands for the effect.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["solvanmai","aram","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The 'capacities' are differences of rank by birth, learning, conduct, wealth, form and age, and to speak according to them is the virtue.","குடிப்பிறப்பு, கல்வி, ஒழுக்கம், செல்வம், உருவம், பருவம் என்பவற்றான் வரும் தகுதி வேறுபாடுகள்."],["manakkudavar","It's the way of speaking: knowing this avoids slander, empty talk and lying, so it becomes virtue.","புறங்கூறாமையும் பயனில சொல்லாமையும் பொய்கூறா மையும் உளவாம்"],["kalaignar","It means knowing clearly the reason for saying something.","காரணத்தைத் தெளிவாக அறிந்து ஒன்றைச் சொல்ல வேண்டும்."]],"ch":"k:65"},"k:645":{"book":"kural","num":645,"t":["சொல்லுக சொல்லைப் பிறிதோர்சொல் அச்சொல்லை","வெல்லுஞ்சொல் இன்மை அறிந்து"],"p":"Deliver your speech, after assuring yourself that no counter speech can defeat your own.","r":"Speak your word once you know there is no other word that can defeat it.","n":"சொல் occurs five times, so the verse is a sort of closed argument. Parimelazhagar says winning means excelling in the qualities of speech, which is a broader standard than mere refutation.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["solvanmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:65"},"k:646":{"book":"kural","num":646,"t":["வேட்பத்தாஞ் சொல்லிப் பிறர்சொல் பயன்கோடல்","மாட்சியின் மாசற்றார் கோள்"],"p":"It is the opinion of those who are free from defects in diplomacy that the minister should speak so as to make his hearers desire (to hear more) and grasp the meaning of what he hears himself.","r":"It is the way of those without blemish in excellence to speak so that others want more and to take the fruit from what others say.","n":"Mastery of speech includes a discipline of hearing. Parimelazhagar adds generosity: a master doesn't scorn others' faulty words, and takes what is useful in them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["solvanmai","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:65"},"k:647":{"book":"kural","num":647,"t":["சொலல்வல்லன் சோர்விலன் அஞ்சான் அவனை","இகல்வெல்லல் யார்க்கும் அரிது"],"p":"It is impossible for any one to conquer him by intrique who possesses power of speech, and is neither faulty nor timid.","r":"It's hard for anyone to beat in a contest a person who can speak, doesn't slip, and isn't afraid.","n":"Parimelazhagar's gloss of 'speaking so that it is accepted' is uncomfortable: making people decide 'yes' even where the thing isn't actually in their interest. Eloquence, in that reading, isn't truthful by nature, and the chapter doesn't address this.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["solvanmai","avai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:65"},"k:648":{"book":"kural","num":648,"t":["விரைந்து தொழில்கேட்கும் ஞாலம் நிரந்தினிது","சொல்லுதல் வல்லார்ப் பெறின்"],"p":"If there be those who can speak on various subjects in their proper order and in a pleasing manner, the world would readily accept them.","r":"The world quickly responds if it gets those who can speak in order and sweetly.","n":"பெறின், 'if it gets', implies rarity. Parimelazhagar cites a Sanskrit saying that a true speaker is one in a hundred thousand.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["solvanmai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","தொழில் is what is being said, and the world accepts these matters readily.","சொல்லப்படும் காரியங்களை நிரல்படக் கோத்து"],["manakkudavar","தொழில் is work to do: people rush up and ask what they must do.","உலகத்தார் மேவி விரைந்து சென்று செய்யுந் தொழில் யாது என்று கேட்பர்"]],"ch":"k:65"},"k:649":{"book":"kural","num":649,"t":["பலசொல்லக் காமுறுவர் மன்றமா சற்ற","சிலசொல்லல் தேற்றா தவர்"],"p":"They will desire to utter many words, who do not know how to speak a few faultless ones.","r":"Those who don't know how to say a few faultless words surely long to say many.","n":"Loquacity is explained as a substitute for an ability, not an excess of it. Parimelazhagar takes மன்ற as a firm answer to those who actually admire nonstop speech as eloquence.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["solvanmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:65"},"k:650":{"book":"kural","num":650,"t":["இண்ருழ்த்தும் நாறா மலரனையர் கற்றது","உணர விரித்துரையா தார்"],"p":"Those who are unable to set forth their acquirements (before others) are like flowers blossoming in a cluster and yet without fragrance.","r":"Those who can't unfold what they've learned so that others understand are like flowers blooming in clusters without scent.","n":"Coming right after 649's praise of few words, this verse praises expansion. Manakkudavar notices this: brevity alone isn't enough, and one must also expand where needed. In the image, learning is already in bloom; what is missing is the part that travels to others.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["solvanmai","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:65"},"k:651":{"book":"kural","num":651,"t":["துணைநலம் ஆக்கம் த்ருஉம் வினைநலம்","வேண்டிய எல்லாந் தரும்"],"p":"The efficacy of support will yield (only) wealth; (but) the efficacy of action will yield all that is desired.","r":"Good companions bring prosperity, while good action brings everything one could want.","n":"The verse does not say what 'everything' includes. Parimelazhagar expands it to virtue, wealth and pleasure here and desired stations hereafter, and he frames the contrast as between the visible good of allies and the inferred good of deeds.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["thooimai","vinai","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:66"},"k:652":{"book":"kural","num":652,"t":["என்றும் ஒருவுதல் வேண்டும் புகழொடு","நன்றி பயவா வினை"],"p":"Ministers should at all times avoid acts which, in addition to fame, yield no benefit (for the future).","r":"One must at all times avoid action that yields neither renown nor good.","n":"என்றும் is pointed. Parimelazhagar says it holds in rising, shrinking and stable circumstances; Manakkudavar says it holds even when one has no means of doing anything else. The rule doesn't relax under pressure.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thooimai","vinai","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:66"},"k:653":{"book":"kural","num":653,"t":["ஒஓதல் வேண்டும் ஒளிமாழ்கும் செய்வினை","ஆஅதும் என்னு மவர்"],"p":"Those who say, \"we will become (better)\" should avoid the performance of acts that would destroy (their fame).","r":"Those who say 'we shall rise' must refrain from the deed that dims their light.","n":"Ambition itself is the ground of the restraint. Parimelazhagar's summary is that gain that dims one's light isn't gain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["thooimai","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:66"},"k:654":{"book":"kural","num":654,"t":["இடுக்கண் படினும் இளிவந்த செய்யார்","நடுக்கற்ற காட்சி யவர்"],"p":"Those who have infallible judgement though threatened with peril will not do acts which have brought disgrace (on former ministers).","r":"Even when caught in trouble, those of unshaken clarity won't do what is degrading.","n":"Pope's 'ever-during vision' suggests mysticism; the Tamil நடுக்கற்ற காட்சி is steadiness of judgement. Parimelazhagar's reasoning is a calculation: trouble passes but disgrace doesn't, and what must come will come anyway.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thooimai","idukkan","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:66"},"k:655":{"book":"kural","num":655,"t":["எற்றென்று இரங்குவ செய்யற்க செய்வானேல்","மற்றன்ன செய்யாமை நன்று"],"p":"Let a minister never do acts of which he would have to grieve saying, \"what is this I have done\"; (but) should he do (them), it were good that he grieved not.","r":"Do nothing that makes you cry out 'What have I done!'; but if you do, it's good not to do the like.","n":"The second line turns on what அன்ன points to. Pope gives both answers: his couplet says sin no more, and his gloss says do not grieve.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thooimai","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Once done in confusion, don't go on grieving: regret displays a lack of remedy and firmness and spreads one's own shame. He says all others miss the connection.","பின் இருந்து அவ்விரங்கல்களைச் செய்யாதொழிதல் நன்று."],["manakkudavar","If one acts at all, one should not do anything like such deeds.","அவைபோல்வனவுஞ் செய்யாமையே நல்லது."],["kalaignar","Having done it, the good thing is not to continue it.","ஒருகால் அப்படிச் செய்து விட்டாலும் அச்செயலை மீண்டும் தொடராதிருப்பதே நன்று."]],"ch":"k:66"},"k:656":{"book":"kural","num":656,"t":["ஈன்றாள் பசிகாண்பான் ஆயினுஞ் செய்யற் க","சான்றோர் பழிக்கும் வினை"],"p":"Though a minister may see his mother starve; let him do not act which the wise would (treat with contempt).","r":"Even if you must watch the mother who bore you go hungry, don't do what the worthy condemn.","n":"The verse picks the hardest case it can imagine and refuses the exception. Its reach is the dispute.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thooimai","saandror","pazhi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","This overrides a general dharma rule permitting wrong to feed aged parents, wife and infant, but only for ministers, who have standing and royal duties.","தீயன பலவுஞ் செய்தாயினும் புறந்தருக' என்னும் அறநூற்பொது விதி"],["kalaignar","It applies to anyone, with no exception: not even to relieve a mother in the throes of hunger.","பசியால் துடிக்கும் தனது தாயின் வேதனையைத் தணிப்பதற்காகக்கூட"]],"ch":"k:66"},"k:657":{"book":"kural","num":657,"t":["பழிமலைந்து எய்திய ஆக்கத்தின் சான்றோர்","கழிநல் குரவே தலை"],"p":"Far more excellent is the extreme poverty of the wise than wealth obtained by heaping up of sinful deeds.","r":"The utter poverty of the worthy is above wealth gained under a load of blame.","n":"Manakkudavar sees this as a reply to an objection: living by these rules will leave you poor. The verse concedes it. Poverty is not denied or softened by கழி, 'extreme'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saandror","pazhi","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:66"},"k:658":{"book":"kural","num":658,"t":["கடிந்த கடிந்தொரார் செய்தார்க்கு அவைதாம்","முடிந்தாலும் பீழை தரும்"],"p":"The actions of those, who have not desisted from doing deeds forbidden (by the great), will, even if they succeed, cause them sorrow.","r":"Even when they succeed, the forbidden things people do without shunning them bring anguish.","n":"முடிந்தாலும் answers the practical objection that the end might come off. Parimelazhagar goes further and doubts it: such acts won't usually succeed, and even if somehow they do, pain follows.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thooimai","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:66"},"k:659":{"book":"kural","num":659,"t":["அழக் கொண்ட எல்லாம் அழப்போம் இழப்பினும்","பிற்பயக்கும் நற்பா லவை"],"p":"All that has been obtained with tears (to the victim) will depart with tears (to himself); but what has been by fair means; though with loss at first, will afterwards yield fruit.","r":"All that is taken with tears leaves with tears; what comes from good ways, even if lost, pays later.","n":"The verse names no one in அழ. The commentators divide the tears, so the victim weeps on taking and the taker on losing, and this moral symmetry is part of the sound of the line. பின் covers the next birth for Parimelazhagar, which the verse does not specify.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","selvam","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:66"},"k:660":{"book":"kural","num":660,"t":["சலத்தால் பொருள்செய்தே மார்த்தல் பசுமண்","கலத்துள்நீர் பெய்திரீஇ யற்று"],"p":"(For a minister) to protect (his king) with wealth obtained by foul means is like preserving a vessel of wet clay by filling it with water.","r":"Making wealth by deceit and feeling secure in it is like pouring water into an unfired clay pot.","n":"Both pot and water are lost: the wrongly got wealth also destroys the one it was meant to protect.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["selvam","thooimai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","ஏமார்த்தல் means making the king safe with such wealth. He attacks those who read it as rejoicing, since that breaks the simile's form.","பிறரெல்லாம், 'ஏமாத்தல்' என்று பாடமோதி, அதற்கு மகிழ்தல் என்றும்"],["manakkudavar","It means delight: one enjoys what one gained by deception, and both person and wealth perish.","வஞ்சத்தாலே பொருள் தேடி மகிழ்ந்திருத்தல்"]],"ch":"k:66"},"k:661":{"book":"kural","num":661,"t":["வினைத்திட்பம் என்பது ஒருவன் மனத்திட்பம்","மற்றைய எல்லாம் பிற"],"p":"Firmness in action is (simply) one's firmness of mind; all other (abilities) are not of this nature.","r":"What is called firmness in action is one's firmness of mind; all else is something other.","n":"Parimelazhagar points to the word choice: மற்றைய admits these others are related, and பிற denies that they count here. Manakkudavar takes 'the others' to be means and methods.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thitpam","manam","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:67"},"k:662":{"book":"kural","num":662,"t":["ஊறொரால் உற்றபின் ஒல்காமை இவ்விரண்டின்","ஆறென்பர் ஆய்ந்தவர் கோள்"],"p":"Not to perform a ruinous act, and not to be discouraged by the ruinous termination of an act, are the two maxims which, the wise say, from the principles of those who have investigated the subject.","r":"Avoiding harm beforehand and not giving way once it has come are, those who have studied it say, the path of these two.","n":"Parimelazhagar traces the maxim to the ministers of gods and demons, Jupiter and Venus, and so explains why clean action slips in here. The verse itself mentions only 'those who have studied'.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thitpam","idukkan","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Avoid deeds likely to fail, and don't flag if fate spoils a deed. He rejects the variant ஓரார் as ungrammatical.","பழுதுபடும் வினைகளைச் செய்யாமையும், செய்யும் வினை தெய்வத்தான் பழுதுபட்ட வழி அதற்குத் தளராமையும்"],["manakkudavar","Reading ஓரார்: don't dwell on the suffering that action involves, and if it comes, don't weaken.","வினை செய்யுங்கால் உறும் துன்பத்தை ஓரார் ஆதலும்"],["kalaignar","Work to remove obstacles before they come, and don't lose heart if they still do.","இடையூறு வருவதற்கு முன்பே அதனை நீக்கிட முனைவது"]],"ch":"k:67"},"k:663":{"book":"kural","num":663,"t":["கடைக்கொட்கச் செய்தக்க தாண்மை இடைக்கொட்கின்","எற்றா விழுமந் தரும்"],"p":"So to perform an act as to publish it (only) at its termination is (true) manliness; for to announce it beforehand, will cause irremediable sorrow.","r":"True manliness is doing it so that it comes round only at the end; if it comes round midway, it brings unending trouble.","n":"The verb கொட்க carries the verse, and firmness comes out as secrecy on one reading and as persistence on the other. The two make different claims about what ஆண்மை is.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thitpam","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","கொட்க means becoming known: conceal the deed until its end, for enemies who learn of it midway will block it.","செய்யப்படும் வினையை முடிவின்க புலப்படும் வகை முன்னெல்லாம் மறைத்துச் செய்வதே திட்பமாவது"],["manakkudavar","கொட்க means turning back: go through to the end and return, for returning midway brings harm.","ஒரு வினையைத் தொடங்கினால், முடிவிலே சென்று மீளல் செய் வது ஆண்மையாவது"]],"ch":"k:67"},"k:664":{"book":"kural","num":664,"t":["சொல்லுதல் யார்க்கும் எளிய அரியவாம்","சொல்லிய வண்ணம் செயல்"],"p":"To say (how an act is to be performed) is (indeed) easy for any one; but far difficult it is to do according to what has been said.","r":"Speaking is easy for anyone; doing as one has spoken is hard.","n":"Pope's 'shape one's life' turns it into a general moral. Both early commentators restrict it to the plan: saying 'this is how we will do this'. After a full chapter on eloquence, this comes as a quiet correction.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thitpam","solvanmai","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:67"},"k:665":{"book":"kural","num":665,"t":["வீறெய்தி மாண்டார் வினைத்திட்பம் வேந்தன்கண்","ஊறெய்தி உள்ளப் படும்"],"p":"The firmness in action of those who have become great by the excellence (of their counsel) will, by attaining its fulfilment in the person of the king, be esteemed (by all).","r":"The firmness in action of those great in excellence, once it takes effect on the king, comes to be held in everyone's regard.","n":"On Parimelazhagar's reading the fruits lodge in the king, wealth and fame being his; the minister's firmness earns only regard. Kalaignar seems to have the firmness attract the ruler instead, so what ஊறெய்தி describes is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thitpam","amaichu","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:67"},"k:666":{"book":"kural","num":666,"t":["எண்ணிய எண்ணியாங்கு எய்து எண்ணியார்","திண்ணியர் ஆகப் பெறின்"],"p":"If those who have planned (an undertaking) possess firmness (in executing it) they will obtain what they have desired even as they have desired it.","r":"What people have thought of, they obtain as they thought of it, if those who thought it turn out firm.","n":"The first line sounds unconditional, and it is often quoted that way. The condition is in the second line: thinking must be backed by firmness. Parimelazhagar adds that such people don't think otherwise.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["thitpam","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:67"},"k:667":{"book":"kural","num":667,"t":["உருவுகண்டு எள்ளாமை வேண்டும் உருள்பெருந்தேர்க்கு","அச்சாணி அன்னார் உடைத்து"],"p":"Let none be despised for (their) size; (for) the world has those who resemble the linch-pin of the big rolling car.","r":"Do not scorn anyone for how they look: the great rolling chariot has its linchpin.","n":"The image answers the appearance: the pin bears great loads because it is placed exactly at the point of strain. Parimelazhagar applies this to small-bodied ministers while Manakkudavar applies it to everyone (யாவரையும்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thitpam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:67"},"k:668":{"book":"kural","num":668,"t":["கலங்காது கண்ட வினைக்கண் துளங்காது","தூக்கங் கடிந்து செயல்"],"p":"An act that has been firmly resolved on must be as firmly carried out without delay.","r":"With the deed one has looked at clearly and decided, act unwaveringly and without delay.","n":"Sequence matters: கலங்காது before துளங்காது. Clarity comes first, then speed, and Parimelazhagar notes that a confused mind makes what should be dropped seem worth doing. தூக்கம் here is delay, not sleep as in Pope's 'unslumbering'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thitpam","madi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:67"},"k:669":{"book":"kural","num":669,"t":["துன்பம் உறவரினும் செய்க துணிவாற்றி","இன்பம் பயக்கும் வினை"],"p":"Though it should cause increasing sorrow (at the outset), do with firmness the act that yield bliss (in the end).","r":"Even though pain closes in, do resolutely the deed that yields joy.","n":"Parimelazhagar uses technical language to weigh momentary pain of effort (கணிகம்) against the lasting pleasure of the result (பரிணாம இன்பம்). The verse only says 'joy-yielding'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thitpam","idukkan"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:67"},"k:670":{"book":"kural","num":670,"t":["எனைத்திட்பம் எய் தியக் கண்ணும் வினைத்திட்பம்","வேண்டாரை வேண்டாது உலகு"],"p":"The great will not esteem those who esteem not firmness of action, whatever other abilities the latter may possess.","r":"Whatever other firmness they have attained, the world doesn't want those who don't want firmness of action.","n":"வேண்டாரை வேண்டாது mirrors the rejection. Parimelazhagar reads உலகு as 'the high', and Pope's gloss has 'the great', but the verse's own word is the world.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thitpam","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:67"},"k:671":{"book":"kural","num":671,"t":["சூழ்ச்சி முடிவு துணிவெய்தல் அத்துணிவு","தாழ்ச்சியுள் தங்குதல் தீது"],"p":"Consultation ends in forming a resolution (to act); (but) delay in the execution of that resolve is an evil,”","r":"Deliberation ends in arriving at resolve, and it's a fault if that resolve lingers in delay.","n":"The verse gives thought a limit from both sides: it must run until resolve, as Parimelazhagar notes, and it has no right to run after. Manakkudavar supplies the risk: enemies learn of the delay and take precautions.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","vinai","thitpam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:68"},"k:672":{"book":"kural","num":672,"t":["தூங்குக தூங்கிச் செயற்பால தூங்கற்க","தூங்காது செய்யும் வினை"],"p":"Sleep over such (actions) as may be slept over; (but) never over such as may not be slept over.","r":"Sleep over what should be done slowly; do not sleep over what should be done without sleeping.","n":"தூங்கு means both to sleep and to delay. The verse repeats it so that the word itself has to be sorted, and it does not tell you how to tell the two kinds of task apart; Parimelazhagar says they are known through strength and timing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:68"},"k:673":{"book":"kural","num":673,"t":["ஙல்லும்வா யெல்லாம் வினைநன்றே ஒல்லாக்கால்","செல்லும்வாய் நோக்கிச் செயல்"],"p":"Whenever it is possible to overcome your enemy the act {of fighting) is certainly good; if not, endeavour to employ some more ‘successful method,","r":"Wherever it's possible, the deed is good; where it isn't, act by looking for a way that will go.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["vinai","pagai","soozhchi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","வினை here means war: where stronger, fight, since force is what is feared; otherwise use one of the other three means (conciliation, division, gifts).","இயலுமிடத்தெல்லாம் போராற் செய்தல் நன்று"],["manakkudavar","No reference to war: act when possible, and when not, keep it in mind and wait for an opening.","இயலாத காலத்து அதனை நினைந்திருந்து, இயலும் இடம் பார்த்துச் செய்க"]],"ch":"k:68"},"k:674":{"book":"kural","num":674,"t":["வினைபகை என்றிரண்டின் எச்சம் நினையுங்கால்","தீயெச்சம் போலத் தெறும்"],"p":"When duly considered, the incomplete execution of an undertaking and hostility will grow and destroy one like the {unextinguished} remnant of a fire,","r":"If you think about it, what is left over from a task or an enmity scorches like what is left over from a fire.","n":"நினையுங்கால் suggests that this only becomes clear on reflection: a remainder seems small at the moment. Parimelazhagar assigns this to the stronger party, for whom leaving things unfinished seems safe.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","pagai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:68"},"k:675":{"book":"kural","num":675,"t":["பொருள்கருவி காலம் வினையிடனொடு ஐந்தும்","இருள்தீர எண்ணிச் செயல்"],"p":"Do an act alter a due consideration of the {following} five, viz., Money, means, time, execution and place.","r":"Act after thinking through until the darkness clears these five: resources, means, time, the deed and the place.","n":"The list recalls that of 631, but both early commentators double each item: one's own and the enemy's. The verse doesn't say this, and Kalaignar's checklist has only one side. If they're right, this is a list about comparison.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:68"},"k:676":{"book":"kural","num":676,"t":["முடிவும் இடையூறும் முற்றியாங்கு எய்தும்","படுபயனும் பார்த்துச் செயல்"],"p":"An act is to be performed after considering the exertion required, the obstacles to be encountered, and the great to be gained {on its completion).","r":"Act after looking at how it would be finished, what will hinder it, and the great yield when complete.","n":"Parimelazhagar states it as a cost-benefit test: act if the yield outweighs the effort and obstacles. Manakkudavar reads முடிவு as the manner of ending, and Parimelazhagar as effort by metonymy.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","vinai","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:68"},"k:677":{"book":"kural","num":677,"t":["செய்வினை செய்வான் செயன்முறை அவ்வினை","உள்ளறிவான் உள்ளம் கொளல்"],"p":"The method of performance for one who has begun an act is to ascertain the mind of him who knows the secret thereof,","r":"The proper way for someone doing a task is to learn the mind of the one who knows it from inside.","n":"After the preceding checklists, this verse says they aren't enough, and that someone who has done it should be consulted. Parimelazhagar's உள் அறிவான் is the person who has done this before.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:68"},"k:678":{"book":"kural","num":678,"t":["வினையான் வினையாக்கிக் கோடல் நனைகவுள்","யானையால் யானையாத் தற்று"],"p":"To make one undertaking the means of accomplishing another (similar to it) is like making one rutting elephant the means of capturing arother.","r":"Using one task to accomplish another is like binding a rutting elephant with another elephant.","n":"The image takes for granted the practice of capturing elephants with an elephant, and பிணித்தல் is itself a skill of the deed. The elephants of 597 and 599 are about character; this one is about technique.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","soozhchi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Plan each task so that it becomes a means to finishing the next similar one.","தொடங்கிய வினையானே பிறிதும் ஓர் வினையை முடித்தற்கு உபாயம் ஆமாறு எண்ணிச் செய்க."],["manakkudavar","It means setting an enemy's enemies against him, so that enmity is used against enmity.","தமக்கு ஒரு பகைவர் தோன்றினால் அவர்க்குப் பகையாயினாரை அவ ரோடு பகைக்குமாறு"]],"ch":"k:68"},"k:679":{"book":"kural","num":679,"t":["நட்டார்க்கு நல்ல செயலின் விரைந்ததே","ஒட்டாரை ஒட்டிக் கொளல்"],"p":"One should rather hasten to secure the alliance of the foes (of one's foes} rather than perform good offices to one’s friends,","r":"Bringing the unallied over to one's side is more urgent than doing good to one's friends.","n":"Friends can wait because they are friends. This chilly ranking of urgency is about strategy, not love, and Parimelazhagar's reason is timing: it must be done before enemies see one's weakness.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","natpu","soozhchi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","ஒட்டார் are those not allied with one's enemy, i.e. the enemy's enemies, to be made friends. He allows another sense: keeping one's own foes from joining others.","தன் பகைவரோடு ஒட்டாரைத் தனக்கு நட்பாக்கிக்கோடல்."],["manakkudavar","ஒட்டார் are simply one's enemies, to be reconciled, and this is put ahead of doing good for one's own kin.","தஞ் சுற்றத்திற்கு நல்லவை செய்தலினும், பகைவரைப் பொருந்தி நட்பாகக் கொள்ளுதலை"]],"ch":"k:68"},"k:680":{"book":"kural","num":680,"t":["உறைசிறியார் உள்நடுங்கல் அஞ்சிக் குறைபெறின்","கொள்வர் பெரியார்ப் பணிந்து"],"p":"Ministers of smali states, afraid of their people being frightened will yield to and acknowledge thelr superior foes, it the latter offer them a chance of reconciliation","r":"Those whose domain is small, afraid of the trembling within, will bow to the great and accept what they can get if it is offered.","n":"Parimelazhagar sees the verse's tone: since bowing isn't what the honourable would choose, கொள்வர் reports what is done, without praising it. His reason is that if one's own side panics, all is lost.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pagai","panivu","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:68"},"k:681":{"book":"kural","num":681,"t":["அன்புடைமை ஆன்ற குடிப்பிறத்தல் வேந்தவாம்","பண்புடைமை தூதுரைப்பான் பண்பு"],"p":"The qualification of an ambassador are affection (for his relations) a fitting birth, and the possession of attributes pleasing to royalty.","r":"Love, birth in an honourable family, and qualities kings desire make up the nature of one who carries a message.","n":"Whatever அன்பு means here, the envoy's list begins from the affections, not the skills. Parimelazhagar's 'qualities kings desire' also serve abroad: foreign kings too come under his sway.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","kudi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The love is for one's own kin, which keeps them from harm, and the birth is in a family fit for ministry.","தன் சுற்றத்தார்மாட்டு அன்புடையனாதலும்"],["manakkudavar","The love is for the king, and 'qualities kings desire' means not desiring what the king desires.","வேந்தனால் விரும்பப்படும் குணமுடைமையாவது அவன் விரும்புவனவற் றைத் தான் விரும்பாமை."]],"ch":"k:69"},"k:682":{"book":"kural","num":682,"t":["அன்பறிவு ஆராய்ந்த சொல்வன்மை தூதுரைப்பார்க்கு","இன்றி யமையாத மூன்று"],"p":"Love (to his sovereign), knowledge (of his affairs), and a discriminating power of speech (before other sovereigns) are the three sine qua non qualifications of an ambassador.","r":"Love, knowledge and carefully considered eloquence are the three things an envoy can't do without.","n":"ஆராய்ந்த qualifies eloquence, and Parimelazhagar takes it as choosing words that will win assent. Parimelazhagar reads this verse's love as being for the king, so he has given the two adjacent verses two different objects of love.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","arivu","solvanmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:69"},"k:683":{"book":"kural","num":683,"t":["நூலாருள் நூல்வல்லன் ஆகுதல் வேலாருள்","வென்றி வினையுரைப்பான் பண்பு"],"p":"To be powerful in politics among those who are learned (in ethics) is the character of him who speaks to lance-bearing kings on matters of triumph (to his own sovereign).","r":"It is the mark of one who speaks of victory among spear-bearers to be strong in the treatises among those who know them.","n":"The two audiences are set side by side: those with treatises and those with spears. Parimelazhagar sharpens the first: the ministers there merely understand the texts, while the envoy must be able to set them out.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:69"},"k:684":{"book":"kural","num":684,"t":["அறிவுரு வாராய்ந்த கல்விஇம் மூன்றன்","செறிவுடையான் செல்க வினைக்கு"],"p":"He may go on a mission (to foreign rulers) who has combined in him all these three. viz., (natural) sense, an attractive bearing and well-tried learning.","r":"Let one who has these three together go on this work: understanding, presence and well-tested learning.","n":"உரு, bodily presence, counts as a requirement. Parimelazhagar says all three earn respect, on which the work depends, and that such a combination in one person is rare.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["thoothu","arivu","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:69"},"k:685":{"book":"kural","num":685,"t":["தொகச் சொல்லித் தூவாத நீக்கி நகச்சொல்லி","நன்றி பயப்பதாந் தூது"],"p":"He is an ambassador who (in the presence of foreign rulers) speaks briefly, avoids harshness, talks so as to make them smile, and thus brings good (to his own sovereign).","r":"The envoy is one who speaks in brief, removes what would offend, speaks so as to bring a smile, and thereby gets good done.","n":"Parimelazhagar gives each tactic a psychology: when told a compressed reason, hearers who would refuse many matters come round; when told in a pleasing way, they accept hard things without noticing they are hard. Courtesy is presented as technique.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","solvanmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:69"},"k:686":{"book":"kural","num":686,"t":["கற்றுக்கண் அஞ்சான் செலச்சொல்லிக் காலத்தால்","தக்கது அறிவதாம் தூது"],"p":"He is an ambassador who having studied (politics) talks impressively, is not afraid of angry looks, and knows (to employ) the art suited to the time.","r":"The envoy is one who is learned, does not fear hostile eyes, speaks so that it goes home, and knows what fits the time.","n":"Parimelazhagar notes that காலத்தால் தக்கது allows for ends reached by means other than what the treatises taught. Manakkudavar makes the learning very specific: how to turn aside the enemy king's anger.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","kalvi","solvanmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:69"},"k:687":{"book":"kural","num":687,"t":["கடனறிந்து காலங் கருதி இடனறிந்து","எண்ணி உரைப்பான் தலை"],"p":"He is chief (among ambassadors) who understands the proper decorum (before foreign princes), seeks the (proper) occasion, knows the (most suitable) place, and delivers his message after (due) consideration.","r":"The highest is the one who knows the protocol, considers the moment, knows the place, and speaks after thinking it through.","n":"Parimelazhagar's எண்ணி is rehearsal in chains: what I say, what they answer, what I say next. His இடம் is a place where one's allies are present, so a place is political, not physical.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:69"},"k:688":{"book":"kural","num":688,"t":["தூய்மை துணைமை துணிவுடைமை இம்மூன்றின்","வாய்மை வழியுரைப்பான் பண்பு"],"p":"The qualifications of him who faithfully delivers his (sovereign's) message are purity, the support (of foreign ministers), and boldness, with truthfulness in addition to the (aforesaid) three.","r":"Purity, support, resolve and truthfulness in these three mark the one who delivers the message faithfully.","n":"Parimelazhagar's purity is immunity to bribes of money and women, so that the message isn't altered. Truth here is a qualification for being believed. These are professional virtues as he explains them, not an inner holiness.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","thooimai","vaaimai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","துணைமை is having the foreign court's ministers as support, which can calm a king's anger.","தமக்கு அவரமைச்சர் துணையாந்தன்மையும்"],["manakkudavar","துணைமை is having one's own retinue, and purity is of body and mind.","தூய்மையுடைமையும், சுற்றமுடைமையும்"]],"ch":"k:69"},"k:689":{"book":"kural","num":689,"t":["விடுமாற்றம் வேந்தர்க்கு உரைப்பான் வடுமாற்றம்","வாய்சேரா வன்க ணவன்"],"p":"He alone is fit to communicate (his sovereign's) reply, who possesses the firmness not to utter even inadvertently what may reflect discredit (on the latter).","r":"One who conveys the king's reply to kings is a hard man whose mouth will not let slip a word that shames.","n":"வன்கண் is again a virtue, as in 632, and Parimelazhagar shows why: the temptation comes from fear for oneself. The slip would be a phrase softening his king's standing in order to save his skin.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["thoothu","solvanmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:69"},"k:690":{"book":"kural","num":690,"t":["இறுதி பயப்பினும் எஞ்சாது இறைவற் கு","உறுதி பயப்பதாம் தூது"],"p":"He is the ambassador who fearlessly seeks his sovereign's good though it should cost him his life (to deliver his message).","r":"The envoy is one who, even if it brings his end, doesn't hold back and secures what is good for his lord.","n":"இறுதி is the limit, and the chapter stops there. The ideal is unconditional, and the verse gives no reason why the envoy should owe so much; it's taken for granted.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:69"},"k:691":{"book":"kural","num":691,"t":["அகலாது அணுகாது தீக்காய்வார் போல்க","இகல்வேந்தர்ச் சேர்ந்தொழுகு வார்"],"p":"Ministers who serve under fickle-minded monarchs should, like those who warm themselves at the fire, be neither (too) far, nor (too) near.","r":"Those who attach themselves to quarrelsome kings should be like people warming at a fire: not going off, not going close.","n":"The image avoids praising or blaming the king: fire doesn't have intentions. Parimelazhagar spells out both halves: at a distance one gets nothing, and up close one is burned. இகல் doesn't say which kings, and may imply all of them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mannar","amaichu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:70"},"k:692":{"book":"kural","num":692,"t":["மன்னர் விழைப விழையாமை மன்னரால்","மன்னிய ஆக்கந் தரும்"],"p":"","r":"Not desiring what kings desire brings, through those kings, lasting prosperity.","n":"A kind of worldly detachment that works as strategy. Parimelazhagar gives the mechanism: kings fear those who would rival them in pleasures, finery and rank, and they reward one who doesn't. So the restraint is prudence and may not be virtue at all.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mannar","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:70"},"k:693":{"book":"kural","num":693,"t":["போற்றின் அரியவை போற்றல் கடுத்தபின்","தேற்றுதல் யார்க்கும் அரிது"],"p":"Ministers who would save themselves should avoid (the commission of) serious errors for if the king's suspicion is once roused, no one can remove it.","r":"If you would guard yourself, guard against the gravest things; once suspicion has arisen, nobody can clear it.","n":"Parimelazhagar's list of grave faults is betrayal, intimacy with royal women and seizing treasure. His comparison is memorable: like a debt that comes to mind at each sight of the debtor, a cleared suspicion recurs each time the king sees you.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mannar","amaichu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:70"},"k:694":{"book":"kural","num":694,"t":["செவிச்சொல்லும் சேர்ந்த நகையும் அவித்தொழுகல்","ஆன்ற பெரியா ரகத்து"],"p":"While in the presence of the sovereign, ministers should neither whisper to nor smile at others.","r":"In the presence of the great and established, suppress whispers and shared smiles.","n":"These small signs are dangerous because a ruler reads them: Parimelazhagar says he will assume they concern his own faults. The next chapter's interest in reading signs here falls on those who are read. The verse says 'great ones', and only commentators limit this to kings.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mannar","kurippu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:70"},"k:695":{"book":"kural","num":695,"t":["எப்பொருளும் ஓரார் தொடரார்மற் றப்பொருளை","விட்டக்கால் கேட்க மறை"],"p":"(When the king is engaged) in secret counsel (with others), ministers should neither over-hear anything whatever nor pry into it with inquisitive questions, but (wait to) listen when it is divulged (by the king himself).","r":"Don't listen in to anything or press for anything; hear a secret when it has been let go.","n":"Manakkudavar says that even the desire to hear is a fault. The whole risk is that curiosity about power looks like a threat to it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mannar","amaichu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:70"},"k:696":{"book":"kural","num":696,"t":["குறிப்பறிந்து காலங் கருதி வெறுப்பில","வேண்டுப வேட்பச் சொலல்"],"p":"Knowing the (king's disposition and seeking the right time, (the minister) should in a pleasing manner suggest things such as are desirable and not disagreeable.","r":"Knowing the mood and considering the time, say what is needed and not disliked, so that it is wanted.","n":"Each word is doing work: வெறுப்பில, 'not disliked', is a test of the hearer, while வேண்டுப, 'needed', is a test of the matter. Parimelazhagar explains that a thing a king doesn't accept won't get done, so the point is practical, and that during lust or anger advice is useless.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mannar","kurippu","solvanmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:70"},"k:697":{"book":"kural","num":697,"t":["வேட்பன சொல்லி வினையில எஞ்ஞான்றும்","கேட்பினும் சொல்லா விடல்"],"p":"Ministers should (always) give agreeable advice but on no occasion recommend useless actions, though requested (to do so).","r":"Say what is wanted, and never say what is useless even if you are asked.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mannar","solvanmai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Say what is of great benefit and desired, even if not asked, and don't say what is profitless, even if the king asks.","எஞ்ஞான்றும் பயனிலவாயவற்றைத் தானே கேட்டாலும் சொல்லாது விடுக."],["manakkudavar","Say what the king wants; and if an enemy king questions an envoy about the size of the army, evade: 'Don't you know?'","பகையரசன் வினாவினால், நீ அறியாததொன்றோ வென்று அளவு கூறாமை."]],"ch":"k:70"},"k:698":{"book":"kural","num":698,"t":["இளையர் இனமுறையர் என்றிகழார் நின்ற","ஒளியோடு ஒழுகப் படும்"],"p":"Ministers should behave in accordance with the (Divine) light in the person of kings and not despise them saying, \"He is our junior (in age) and connected with our family!\".","r":"Without belittling him as 'he's younger' or 'he's kin of such a degree', one should conduct oneself in keeping with the light he stands in.","n":"நின்ற ஒளி: the light is one he stands in, and this can be read as belonging to the office, whoever holds it. Pope's '(Divine)' comes from Parimelazhagar.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mannar","kudi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The light is the king's divinity: he guards the world while asleep, and they must act as men before gods.","அவர் கடவுளரும் தாம் மக்களுமாய் ஒழுகுதல்."],["manakkudavar","The light is simply the headship he has obtained.","அவர் பெற்று நின்ற தலைமையோடே பொருந்த ஒழுக வேண்டும்"],["kalaignar","It is the eminence of those in positions of government.","அவர்கள் அடைந்துள்ள பெருமைக் கேற்பப் பண்புடன் நடந்து கொள்ள வேண்டும்."]],"ch":"k:70"},"k:699":{"book":"kural","num":699,"t":["கொளப்பட்டேம் என்றெண்ணிக் கொள்ளாத செய்யார்","துளக்கற்ற காட்சி யவர்"],"p":"Those whose judgement is firm will not do what is disagreeable (to the sovereign) saying (within themselves) \"We are esteemed by the king\".","r":"Those of unshaken clarity won't, thinking 'we are in favour', do what is unacceptable.","n":"Parimelazhagar's diagnosis is one of changed self-image: those who fall think of themselves as new people after being favoured. Clarity, then, is seeing that one is the same person as before.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mannar","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:70"},"k:700":{"book":"kural","num":700,"t":["பழையம் எனக்கருதிப் பண்பல்ல செய்யும்","கெழுதகைமை கேடு தரும்"],"p":"The (foolish) claim with which a minister does unbecoming acts because of his (long) familiarity (with the king) will ensure his ruin.","r":"The intimacy that, thinking 'we go back a long way', does what is improper brings ruin.","n":"Parimelazhagar's explanation is the chapter's coldest note: when the king can no longer bear it, he takes the life without any kindly regard for the old bond. The chapter on kindly regard asks for exactly what the courtier here can't count on.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mannar","kannottam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:70"},"k:701":{"book":"kural","num":701,"t":["கூறாமை நோக்கிக் குறிப்பறிவான் எஞ்ஞான்றும்","மாறாநீர் வையக் கணி"],"p":"The minister who by looking (at the king) understands his mind without being told (of it), will be a perpetual ornament to the world which is surrounded by a never-drying sea.","r":"One who sees and knows what is meant before it is said is an ornament to the earth, whose waters never fail.","n":"The verse doesn't say whose mind is being read. Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar take it as the king's, Kalaignar as anyone's. After the previous chapter, a minister's skill seems likely, though it is not stated.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:71"},"k:702":{"book":"kural","num":702,"t":["ஐயப் படாஅது அகத்தது உணர்வானைத்","தெய்வத்தோ டொப்பக் கொளல்"],"p":"He is to be esteemed a god who is able to ascertain without a doubt what is within (one's mind).","r":"Regard as equal to a god one who knows, without doubt, what is within.","n":"ஐயப்படாஅது is the claim's high bar: accurate conjecture isn't enough. Parimelazhagar locates the divinity narrowly in this one capacity, while the person stays human in body. Kalaignar's phrase 'if it is said that this power is god's' hedges the verse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:71"},"k:703":{"book":"kural","num":703,"t":["குறிப்பிற் குறிப்புணர் வாரை உறுப்பினுள்","யாது கொடுத்தும் கொளல்"],"p":"The king should ever give whatever (is asked) of his belongings and secure him who, by the indications (of his own mind) is able to read those of another.","r":"Give whatever part of what you have, and gain those who read a sign by a sign.","n":"Parimelazhagar's reading contains a small theory of other minds: since inner workings are alike in all people, knowing one's own gives access to another's. The verse's குறிப்பிற் குறிப்பு is short enough to allow this but does not state it.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","amaichu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","They read another's sign through their own inner sign; 'parts' are outer possessions such as wealth, land, elephants.","உள் நிகழும் நெறி யாவர்க்கும் ஒத்தலின், பிறர் குறிப்பறிதற்குத் தம் குறிப்புக் கருவியாயிற்று."],["manakkudavar","They read the mind's intent through the sign in the face, and 'parts' can be limbs of the body or of the realm.","முகக் குறிப்பினாலே உள்ளக்கருத்தை அறியுமவர்களை"],["kalaignar","They read the face, and the offer is any post in government.","எந்தப் பொறுப்பைக் கொடுத்தாவது துணையாக்கிக் கொள்ள வேண்டும்."]],"ch":"k:71"},"k:704":{"book":"kural","num":704,"t":["குறித்தது கூறாமைக் கொள்வாரோ டேனை","உறுப்போ ரனையரால் வேறு"],"p":"Those who understand one's thoughts without being informed (thereof) and those who do not, may (indeed) resemble one another bodily; still are they different (mentally).","r":"Those who take what is meant without its being said and all the others are alike in limbs, yet they are different.","n":"Parimelazhagar completes the thought: lacking the highest knowing, the others are to be classed as beasts. The verse just says வேறு, 'different', and the understatement may be stronger than the gloss.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:71"},"k:705":{"book":"kural","num":705,"t":["குறிப்பிற் குறிப்புணரா வாயின் உறுப்பினுள்","என்ன பயத்தவோ கண்?"],"p":"Of what use are the eyes amongst one's members, if they cannot by their own indications dive those of another ?.","r":"If they can't read a sign by a sign, of what use, among one's limbs, are eyes?","n":"Eyes are asked to do what is not seeing in the ordinary sense. Parimelazhagar says awareness itself is being attributed to the eye here because of how much it helps. Manakkudavar says such people are like the blind.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:71"},"k:706":{"book":"kural","num":706,"t":["அடுத்தது காட்டும் பளிங்குபோல் நெஞ்சம்","கடுத்தது காட்டும் முகம்"],"p":"As the mirror reflects what is near so does the face show what is uppermost in the mind.","r":"As crystal shows what is near it, the face shows what is strongest in the heart.","n":"பளிங்கு is crystal, and it takes on a neighbouring colour. Pope and Kalaignar's 'mirror' changes the figure: a mirror shows what stands opposite, and crystal changes colour within itself. கடுத்தது is what is most intense, so the face shows peaks and not everything.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:71"},"k:707":{"book":"kural","num":707,"t":["முகத்தின் முதுக்குறைந்தது உண்டோ உவப்பினும்","காயினும் தான்முந் துறும்"],"p":"Is there anything so full of knowledge as the face ? (No.) it precedes the mind, whether (the latter is) pleased or vexed.","r":"Is there anything as knowing as the face? Whether one is pleased or angry, it gets there first.","n":"Parimelazhagar treats this as a rejoinder to those who say a face, made of elements, can't know anything, and reads the verse's question as a mock refutation. The point isn't that it knows, but that it arrives before the words do.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:71"},"k:708":{"book":"kural","num":708,"t":["முகம்நோக்கி நிற்க அமையும் அகம்நோக்கி","உற்ற துணர்வார்ப் பெறின்"],"p":"If the king gets those who by looking into his mind can understand (and remove) what has occurred (to him) it is enough that he stand looking at their face.","r":"If you have those who look within and understand what has happened, it's enough to stand and look them in the face.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kurippu","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It concerns a petitioner: he need only stand before those who will read his need and resolve it, since speaking shames both.","அவ்வெல்லையைக் கடந்து சொல்லுமாயின் இருவர்க்குஞ் சிறுமையாமாகலின்"],["manakkudavar","It's the reverse relation: the king, too, must read his ministers.","இஃது அமாத்தியர் குறிப்பை அரசனும் அறியவேண்டுமென்றது."],["kalaignar","It concerns the reader: if he has the skill, he only needs to stand in front of someone.","ஒருவரின் முகத்துக்கு எதிரில் நின்றாலே போதுமானது."]],"ch":"k:71"},"k:709":{"book":"kural","num":709,"t":["பகைமையும் கேண்மையும் கண்ணுரைக்கும் கண்ணின்","வகைமை உணர்வார்ப் பெறின்"],"p":"If a king gets ministers who can read the movements of the eye, the eyes (of foreign kings) will (themselves) reveal (to him) their hatred or friendship.","r":"The eye itself will tell of enmity and friendship, if you have those who understand the eye's kinds.","n":"The eye is said to speak (உரைக்கும்), and speech itself isn't needed. Parimelazhagar applies this to foreign kings as read by ministers, so this is diplomatic intelligence at its most subtle.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","pagai","natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:71"},"k:710":{"book":"kural","num":710,"t":["நுண்ணியம் என்பார் அளக்குங்கோல் காணுங்கால்","கண்ணல்லது இல்லை பிற"],"p":"The measuring-rod of those (ministers) who say \"we are acute\" will on inquiry be found to be their (own) eyes and nothing else.","r":"When you look into it, the measuring rod of those who call themselves subtle is nothing but the eye.","n":"என்பார் may carry a slight smile: 'those who say we are fine-minded'. Parimelazhagar explains why the eye: gesture, form, action and speech can all be hidden once one knows they're being read, but a gaze is mixed with the mind. He also records a variant reading, அலைக்குங்கோல்.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:71"},"k:711":{"book":"kural","num":711,"t":["அவையறிநது ஆராய்ந்து சொல்லுக சொல்லின்","தொகையறிந்த தூய்மை யவர்"],"p":"Let the pure who know the arrangement of words speak with deliberation after ascertaining (the nature of) the court (then assembled).","r":"Those whose purity lies in knowing the body of words should speak after knowing the assembly and examining the matter.","n":"Pope's 'pure in heart' gives the wrong impression. For Parimelazhagar, தூய்மை here is selection: discarding what doesn't suit and keeping what does. It's a purity of diction, not of character, though the verse doesn't rule out more.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["avai","solvanmai","thooimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:72"},"k:712":{"book":"kural","num":712,"t":["இடைதெரிந்து நன்குணர்ந்து சொல்லுக சொல்லின்","நடைதெரிந்த நன்மை யவர்"],"p":"Let the good who know the uses of words speak with a clear knowledge after ascertaining the time (suited to the court).","r":"Those with the goodness of knowing how words work should discern the opening, understand clearly, and then speak.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avai","solvanmai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","இடை is the moment: when the assembly is willing to hear.","செவ்வி; கேட்டற்கண் விருப்புடைமை."],["manakkudavar","இடை is the fault: think about what wrong a word could bring.","சொன்னால் அதற்கு வருங்குற்றத்தை ஆராய்ந்து"]],"ch":"k:72"},"k:713":{"book":"kural","num":713,"t":["அவையறியார் சொல்லல்மேற் கொள்பவர் சொல்லின்","வகையறியார் வல்லதூஉம் இல்"],"p":"Those who undertake to speak without knowing the (nature of the) court are ignorant of the quality of words as well as devoid of the power (of learning).","r":"Those who undertake to speak without knowing the assembly don't know the kinds of speech, and have no ability at all.","n":"A strong claim about what speech is. Parimelazhagar's reason is that kinds of speech follow from kinds of hearers' understanding, so if you don't know the hearer, you don't know the words.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avai","solvanmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:72"},"k:714":{"book":"kural","num":714,"t":["ஒளியார்முன் ஒள்ளிய ராதல் வெளியார்முன்","வான்சுதை வண்ணம் கொளல்"],"p":"Ministers should be lights in the assembly of the enlightned, but assume the pure whiteness of mortar (ignorance) in that of fools.","r":"Before the bright be bright; before the empty, take on the colour of pure white plaster.","n":"The verse plays on வெளி, the blank and white. Parimelazhagar connects it to வெளிறு, wood without heart, and says one should appear even whiter than they are so they will respect you. It's unusually candid advice to feign emptiness; the commentators don't soften it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avai","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:72"},"k:715":{"book":"kural","num":715,"t":["நன்றென்ற வற்றுள்ளும் நன்றே முதுவருள்","முந்து கிளவாச் செறிவு"],"p":"The modesty by which one does not rush forward and speak in (an assembly of) superiors is the best among all (one's) good qualities.","r":"The best of all things called good is the restraint of not speaking first among elders.","n":"Parimelazhagar notes the exact limit: only speaking first is forbidden, so speaking with them or after them remains allowed. Manakkudavar extends முதுவர் beyond age to austerity, family and learning, and this matters because the verse gives only the one word.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avai","panivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:72"},"k:716":{"book":"kural","num":716,"t":["ஆற்றின் நிலைதளர்ந் தற்றே வியன்புலம்","ஏற்றுணர்வார் முன்னர் இழுக்கு"],"p":"(For a minister) to blunder in the presence of those who have acquired a vast store of learning and know (the value thereof) is like a good man stumbling (and falling away) from the path (of virtue).","r":"A slip before those who have taken in and understood wide learning is like faltering from one's footing on the path.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avai","kalvi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The path is the path to liberation: it is like an ascetic falling away, with loss of fruit and scorn as well.","வீடு எய்தற்பொருட்டு நன்னெறிக்கண் நின்றானொருவன்"],["manakkudavar","It is simply someone who is on a path of life and loses his standing, and is scorned.","ஒரு நெறியின்கண் நின்றார் அந்நிலைமை குலைந்தாற்போல் இகழப்படும்"]],"ch":"k:72"},"k:717":{"book":"kural","num":717,"t":["கற்றறிந்தார் கல்வி விளங்கும் கசடறச்","சொல்தெரிதல் வல்லார் அகத்து"],"p":"The learning of those who have read and understood (much) will shine in the assembly of those who faultlessly examine (the nature of) words.","r":"The learning of those who have studied and understood shines where people can examine words faultlessly.","n":"Learning needs a qualified audience to appear at all. Manakkudavar puts this as a limit: only the learned can tell how good learning is.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["avai","kalvi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Those with the skill to examine words are the assembly: speak in such a gathering.","வழுப்படாமல் சொற்களை ஆராய்தல் வல்லார் அவைக்கண் சொல்லின்"],["kalaignar","They are the speakers themselves: learning shows in those who choose flawless words.","மாசற்ற சொற்களைத் தேர்ந்தெடுத்து உரை நிகழ்த்துவோரிடமே"]],"ch":"k:72"},"k:718":{"book":"kural","num":718,"t":["உணர்வ துடையார்முன் சொல்லல் வளர்வதன்","பாத்தியுள் நீர்சொரிந் தற்று"],"p":"Lecturing to those who have the ability to understand (for themselves) is like watering a bed of plants that are growing (of themselves).","r":"Speaking before those with understanding is like pouring water on a bed where the crop is already growing.","n":"The image could suggest redundancy, since the crop grows anyway. Parimelazhagar takes the opposite point: what would grow by itself grows all the more, and what grows is the speaker's own learning.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avai","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:72"},"k:719":{"book":"kural","num":719,"t":["புல்லவையுள் பொச்சாந்தும் சொல்லற்க நல்லவையுள்","நன்குசலச் சொல்லு வார்"],"p":"Those who are able to speak good things impressively in an assembly of the good should not even forgetfully speak them in that of the low","r":"Those able to speak well and effectively in a good assembly should not, even by forgetting, speak in a mean one.","n":"Parimelazhagar's reason is double: the good assembly scorns you for not knowing your audience, and the mean one for not grasping the matter. பொச்சாந்தும், 'even by forgetting', treats it as a slip one must be on guard against.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["avai","solvanmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:72"},"k:720":{"book":"kural","num":720,"t":["அங்கணத்துள் உக்க அமிழ்தற்றால் தங்கணத்தார்","அல்லார்முன் கோட்டி கொளல்"],"p":"To utter (a good word) in the assembly of those who are of inferior rank is like dropping nectar on the ground.","r":"Holding forth before those who aren't of one's own group is like nectar spilled in a dirty yard.","n":"The verse's division is தங்கணத்தார் அல்லார், those not of one's group, which is a matter of fit and not explicitly of worth. Pope's 'alien herd' and 'inferior rank' sharpen it. Parimelazhagar sees the waste as twofold: those who would know it as deathless food never get it, and the yard gains nothing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["avai","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:72"},"k:721":{"book":"kural","num":721,"t":["வகையறிந்து வல்லவை வாய்சோரார் சொல்லின்","தொகையறிந்த தூய்மை யவர்"],"p":"The pure who know the classification of words having first ascertained the nature (of the court) will not (through fear) falter in their speech before the powerful body.","r":"Those whose purity lies in knowing the body of words, having known the kinds, don't let their speech slip before the capable.","n":"The second line is identical to 711's. There the same people adjust to their hearers, and here they keep from faltering. Parimelazhagar brings in fear from the chapter's title; the verse only says வாய்சோரார்.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avai","anjaamai","solvanmai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","வகை is the kinds of assembly, and வல்லவை is a capable assembly. He records a reading as 'what they have mastered'.","கற்றுவல்ல அவை, அல்லா அவை என்னும் அவை வகையினை அறிந்து"],["manakkudavar","வகை is the kinds of harm that come from a slip.","தப்பினால் வருங் குற்றவகையை யறிந்து"]],"ch":"k:73"},"k:722":{"book":"kural","num":722,"t":["கற்றாருள் கற்றார் எனப்படுவர் கற்றார்முன்","கற்ற செலச்சொல்லு வார்"],"p":"Those who can agreeably set forth their acquirements before the learned will be regarded as the most learned among the learned.","r":"Those who make what they've learned go home before the learned are called learned among the learned.","n":"The verse is about reputation (எனப்படுவர்). Parimelazhagar's reasoning is that the world only knows the learned through such occasions, so public recognition depends on this one skill, not on how much is known.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avai","kalvi","solvanmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:73"},"k:723":{"book":"kural","num":723,"t":["பகையகத்துச் சாவார் எளியர் அரியர்","அவையகத்து அஞ்சா தவர்"],"p":"Many indeed may (fearlessly) die in the presence of (their) foes; (but) few are those who are fearless in the assembly (of the learned).","r":"Those who die amid enemies are easy to find; those who don't fear within an assembly are hard to find.","n":"பகையகத்து and அவையகத்து are parallel: the assembly is a battlefield with a higher casualty of nerve. This is one of the Kural's boldest reversals of martial values. Parimelazhagar reads எளியர் simply as 'many' and அரியர் as 'few', so the claim concerns numbers and isn't a ranking of worth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anjaamai","avai","pagai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:73"},"k:724":{"book":"kural","num":724,"t":["கற்றார்முன் கற்ற செலச்சொல்லித் தாம்கற்ற","மிக்காருள் மிக்க கொளல்"],"p":"(Ministers) should agreeably set forth their acquirements before the learned and acquire more (knowledge) from their superiors (in learning).","r":"Make what you have learned go home before the learned, and take the more from those who know more.","n":"Fearlessness has an epistemic pay-off. Parimelazhagar's reason: no one can learn everything, so speaking in a mixed assembly prompts others to speak. Manakkudavar goes so far as to define fearlessness of the assembly as this exchange.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["avai","kalvi","anjaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:73"},"k:725":{"book":"kural","num":725,"t":["ஆற்றின் அளவறிந்து கற்க அவையஞ்சா","மாற்றங் கொடுத்தற் பொருட்டு"],"p":"In order to reply fearlessly before a foreign court, (ministers) should learn logic according to the rules (of grammar).","r":"Learn by the proper path and with its measure, so as to give replies in the assembly without fear.","n":"Fear is treated as a gap in training, not of temperament. The verse's ஆற்றின் அளவு is brief; Parimelazhagar expands it into grammar followed by logic, listing Nyaya terms for disputation. Pope follows him. That learned apparatus isn't in the verse itself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","anjaamai","avai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:73"},"k:726":{"book":"kural","num":726,"t":["வாளொடென் வன்கண்ணர் அல்லார்க்கு நூலொடென்","நுண்ணவை அஞ்சு பவர்க்கு"],"p":"What have they to do with a sword who are not valiant, or they with learning who are afraid of an intelligent assembly ?","r":"What is a sword to those who are not hard-eyed? What are treatises to those who fear a subtle assembly?","n":"வன்கண் is once again positive, as in 632 and 689. Parimelazhagar's conclusion is that such people have no claim on the treatises, and this goes further than saying they fail to use them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["anjaamai","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:73"},"k:727":{"book":"kural","num":727,"t":["பகையகத்துப் பேடிகை ஒள்வாள் அவையகத்து","அஞ்சு மவன்கற்ற நூல்"],"p":"The learning of him who is diffident before an assembly is like the shining sword of an hermaphrodite in the presence of his foes.","r":"The treatise learned by one who fears the assembly is a bright sword in the hand of a பேடி in the midst of enemies.","n":"The image relies on a period contempt, and Manakkudavar again drops the word, saying only 'one who fears'. Parimelazhagar works the comparison out carefully: field and sword are both good, so the failure lies wholly in the holder, as with the assembly and the book.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anjaamai","kalvi","avai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:73"},"k:728":{"book":"kural","num":728,"t":["பல்லவை கற்றும் பயமிலரே நல்லவையுள்","நன்கு செலச்சொல்லா தார்"],"p":"Those who cannot agreeably speak good things before a good assembly are indeed unprofitable persons inspite of all their various acquirements.","r":"Though they learn many things, they are of no use if they can't speak well and tellingly in a good assembly.","n":"Parimelazhagar reads பயமிலர் as of no use to the world, and records another view, that they have no fruit from their learning. Who is deprived is therefore not settled, the world or the scholar. The verse's own 'good assembly' is the same condition as 719.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","avai","solvanmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:73"},"k:729":{"book":"kural","num":729,"t":["கல்லா தவரின் கடையென்ப கற்றறிந்தும்","நல்லா ரவையஞ்சு வார்"],"p":"They who, though they have learned and understood, are yet afraid of the assembly of the good, are said to be inferior (even) to the illiterate.","r":"They say that those who have studied and understood, and yet fear an assembly of the good, are lower than the unlearned.","n":"என்ப, 'they say', reports this as public opinion. Parimelazhagar makes it pointed: such people benefit neither themselves nor others and have obtained only the pain of study.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","anjaamai","avai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:73"},"k:730":{"book":"kural","num":730,"t":["உளரெனினும் இல்லாரொடு ஒப்பர் களன்அஞ்சிக்","கற்ற செலச்சொல்லா தார்"],"p":"Those who through fear of the assembly are unable to set forth their learning in an interesting manner, though alive, are yet like the dead.","r":"Those who, fearing the arena, can't make what they've learned go home are like the absent, even though they exist.","n":"The chapter ends with a social death: to Parimelazhagar they are like the dead since the world doesn't count them. களன் can be a battlefield as well, so the word may close the chapter on the same comparison as 723, though Parimelazhagar glosses it as those seated there.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["anjaamai","kalvi","avai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:73"},"k:731":{"book":"kural","num":731,"t":["தள்ளா விளையுளும் தக்காரும் தாழ்விலாச்","செல்வரும் சேர்வது நாடு"],"p":"A kingdom is that in which (those who carry on) a complete cultivation, virtuous persons, and merchants with inexhaustible wealth, dwell together.","r":"A land is where unfailing yield, worthy people and wealthy people whose wealth does not sink all come together.","n":"The definition opens with who and what gathers in a place, not with territory or borders. A land here is a convergence.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","selvam","aram"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","விளையுள் stands for the cultivators, தக்கார் for renunciants and brahmins, and செல்வர் for merchants, so the verse lists three kinds of people.","'விளையுள்' என்பது உழவர்மேல் நின்றது."],["manakkudavar","விளையுள் is land itself, fields that do not fail even when rain does.","தள்ளா விளையுள் - மழையில்லாத காலத்தினும் சாவிபோகாத நிலம்."],["kalaignar","The wealthy who count are those who do not spend their wealth on evil.","செல்வத்தைத் தீயவழியில் செலவிடாதவர்களும்"]],"ch":"k:74"},"k:732":{"book":"kural","num":732,"t":["பெரும்பொருளால் பெட்டக்க தாகி அருங்கேட்டால்","ஆற்ற விளைவது நாடு"],"p":"A kingdom is that which is desire for its immense wealth, and which grows greatly in prosperity, being free from destructive causes.","r":"A land is desirable for its great wealth and yields abundantly because ruin rarely touches it.","n":"The verse names no king. Parimelazhagar lists six calamities and credits their absence to the king's virtue and his might, which is his addition.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:74"},"k:733":{"book":"kural","num":733,"t":["பொறையொருங்கு மேல்வருங்கால் தாங்கி இறைவற்கு","இறையொருங்கு நேர்வது நாடு"],"p":"A kingdom is that which can bear any burden that may be pressed on it (from adjoining kingdoms) and (yet) pay the full tribute to its sovereign.","r":"A land bears burdens that fall on it all at once and still gives its ruler his full due.","n":"நேர்வது suggests consent as well as payment. Parimelazhagar's gloss உடம்பட்டு makes the land a willing payer, not a squeezed one.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","vendhan"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The burdens are refugees and herds driven from other lands by war, misrule or famine, whom this land absorbs.","பாரங்கள் - மக்கள் தொகுதியும் ஆன் எருமை முதலிய விலங்குத் தொகுதியும்."],["manakkudavar","The burdens are the land's own, the extra exactions laid on subjects beyond the fixed tax.","குடிமையாவது கடமையொழிய வருவது."]],"ch":"k:74"},"k:734":{"book":"kural","num":734,"t":["உறுபசியும் ஓவாப் பிணியும் செறுபகையும்","சேரா தியல்வது நாடு"],"p":"A kingdom is that which continues to be free from excessive starvation, irremediable epidemics, and destructive foes.","r":"A land is one that goes on without severe hunger, unceasing disease or destructive enemies coming to it.","n":"சேரா answers சேர்வது in 731. The chapter defines a land by what gathers in it and then by what does not. Each ill also carries an intensifier, so the verse excludes the crushing forms and not all hardship.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","pagai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:74"},"k:735":{"book":"kural","num":735,"t":["பல்குழுவும் பாழ்செய்யும் உட்பகையும் வேந்தலைக்கும்","கொல்குறும்பும் இல்லது நாடு"],"p":"A kingdom is that which is without various (irregular) associations, destructive internal enemies, and murderous savages who (sometimes) harass the sovereign.","r":"A land is free of many factions, of ruinous enemies within, and of murderous marauders who harass the king.","n":"Parimelazhagar says the factions are united by caste or by god, a striking gloss on பல்குழு, and his 'enemies within' include robbers, slanderers and even wild animals. Chapter 89 uses உட்பகை much more intimately.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","pagai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:74"},"k:736":{"book":"kural","num":736,"t":["கேடறியாக் கெட்ட இடத்தும் வளங்குன்றா","நாடென்ப நாட்டின் தலை"],"p":"The learned say that the best kingdom is that which knows no evil (from its foes), and, if injured (at all), suffers no diminution in its fruitfulness.","r":"The foremost of lands is one that does not know ruin, and whose richness does not fall even if ruin comes.","n":"The verse does not rest on safety alone. It adds a second test for after the first has failed, so the highest praise goes to resilience. The commentators disagree about the ruin: Parimelazhagar means enemies, Manakkudavar pests and storms.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:74"},"k:737":{"book":"kural","num":737,"t":["இருபுனலும் வாய்ந்த மலையும் வருபுனலும்","வல்லரணும் நாட்டிற்கு உறுப்பு"],"p":"The constituents of a kingdom are the two waters (from above and below), well situated hills and an undestructible fort.","r":"The limbs of a land are the two waters, a well-placed hill, the water that comes from it, and a strong fort.","n":"Three of the four limbs involve water or its source, and the fort comes last. Parimelazhagar's hill stores rain and releases it in summer, a piece of water reasoning.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","aran"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The two waters are ground water and surface water, and வருபுனல் is what flows down from the hill.","'கீழ் நீர், மேல் நீர்' எனப்பட்ட தன்கண் நீரும்"],["kalaignar","The two waters are river and sea, and வருபுனல் is rain.","ஆறு, கடல் எனும் இருபுனலும்"]],"ch":"k:74"},"k:738":{"book":"kural","num":738,"t":["பிணியின்மை செல்வம் விளைவின்பம் ஏமம்","அணியென்ப நாட்டிவ் வைந்து"],"p":"Freedom from epidemics, wealth, produce, happiness and protection (to subjects); these five, the learned, say, are the ornaments of a kingdom.","r":"Freedom from disease, wealth, yield, happiness and protection: these five, they say, are a land's ornaments.","n":"Health stands ahead of wealth. Calling these ornaments rather than foundations is odd, and Parimelazhagar's explanation is that such beauty makes people of other lands desire it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","selvam","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:74"},"k:739":{"book":"kural","num":739,"t":["நாடென்ப நாடா வளத்தன நாடல்ல","நாட வளந்தரு நாடு"],"p":"The learned say that those are kingdom whose wealth is not laboured for, and those not, whose wealth is only obtained through labour.","r":"They call those lands whose riches need no seeking; lands that give riches only when sought are no lands.","n":"The verse puns on நாடு as land and as seek. The verdict is on the land, not on the person who toils, but it still sits awkwardly with a text that honours labour. Parimelazhagar treats seeking as hardship, and the verse does not say no work at all is done.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","selvam","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:74"},"k:740":{"book":"kural","num":740,"t":["ஆங்கமை வெய்தியக் கண்ணும் பயமின்றே","வேந்தமை வில்லாத நாடு"],"p":"Although in possession of all the above mentioned excellences, these are indeed of no use to a country, in the absence of harmony between the sovereign and the sujects.","r":"Even with all these attained, a land is without use if it has no accord with its king.","n":"After nine verses of conditions, the tenth makes them insufficient.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","vendhan"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","வேந்து அமைவு is mutual: the people's love for the king and his grace toward them.","குடிகள் அவன்மாட்டு அன்புடையராதலும், அவன்தான் இவர்மாட்டு அருளுடையனாதலும் அடங்கின."],["manakkudavar","The requirement falls on the king, who must himself be of good character.","இது நாட்டுக்கு அரசனும் பண்புடையனாகல் வேண்டுமென்றது."],["kalaignar","The point is the absence of good government.","நல்ல அரசு அமையாத நாட்டில்"]],"ch":"k:74"},"k:741":{"book":"kural","num":741,"t":["ஆற்று பவர்க்கும் அரண்பொருள் அஞ்சித்தற்","போற்று பவர்க்கும் பொருள்"],"p":"A fort is an object of importance to those who march (against their foes) as well as to those who through fear (of pursuers) would seek it for shelter.","r":"A fort matters to those strong enough to march out, and it matters as much to those who, afraid, look to their own safety.","n":"The verse sets no shame on அஞ்சி. Fear is a normal condition of statecraft here. Parimelazhagar names the strong first because even they perish without a fort.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aran","pagai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:75"},"k:742":{"book":"kural","num":742,"t":["மணிநீரும் மண்ணும் மலையும் அணிநிழற்","காடும் உடைய தரண்"],"p":"A fort is that which has everlasting water, plains, mountains and cool shady forests.","r":"A fort has jewel-clear water, open ground, a hill, and a forest of dense shade.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aran"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The fort is ringed by all four defences, natural and built, and the ground is a bare waste left so enemies find no foothold.","நீரரண், நிலவரண், மலையரண், காட்டரண் என இயற்கையும் செயற்கையுமாய இந்நான்கு அரணும் சூழப்படுவது அரண் என்பதாம்."],["manakkudavar","Any one of these is enough, whether water, bare land, hill or forest.","மலையாயினும், அழகிய நிழலினையுடைய காடாயினும் உடையது அரணாம்"],["kalaignar","The water is a deep, wide moat.","ஆழமும் அகலமும் கொண்ட அகழ்"]],"ch":"k:75"},"k:743":{"book":"kural","num":743,"t":["உயர்வகலம் திண்மை அருமைஇந் நான்கின்","அமைவரண் என்றுரைக்கும் நூல்"],"p":"The learned say that a fortress is an enclosure having these four (qualities) viz., height, breadth, strength and inaccessibility.","r":"The treatises say a fort is a wall with height, breadth, strength and inaccessibility in full.","n":"The verse appeals openly to a technical literature (நூல்), which Valluvar is summarizing. Parimelazhagar fills in அருமை with a long catalogue of war machines from the Silappatikaram, which the verse doesn't list.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["aran"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:75"},"k:744":{"book":"kural","num":744,"t":["சிறுகாப்பிற் பேரிடத்த தாகி உறுபகை","ஊக்கம் அழிப்ப தரண்"],"p":"A fort is that which has an extensive space within, but only small places to be guarded, and such as can destroy the courage of besieging foes.","r":"A fort has little to guard and much room within, and it breaks the spirit of the enemy who comes against it.","n":"The final test is psychological. For Parimelazhagar the enemy who comes meaning to take it at once loses heart merely on seeing it, so a good fort wins some of its battle before it begins.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aran","pagai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:75"},"k:745":{"book":"kural","num":745,"t":["கொளற்கரிதாய்க் கொண்டகூழ்த் தாகி அகத்தார்","நிலைக்கெளிதாம் நீரது அரண்"],"p":"A fort is that which cannot be captured, which abounds in suitable provisions, and affords a position of easy defence to its inmates.","r":"A fort is hard to seize, holds stored food, and is easy for those inside to hold their ground in.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aran"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","நீரது means of such a nature: it is built so the defenders' position is easy, with their missiles reaching out and the enemy's not reaching in.","அகத்தாரது போர்நிலைக்கு எளிதாய நீர்மையையுடையதே அரணாவது."],["manakkudavar","நீரது means having water: there is water easy for those inside and scarce for those outside.","அகத்துறைவார்க்கு நிற்றற்கு எளிதாகும் நீரை யுடைத்தாயிருப்பது அரணாவது"]],"ch":"k:75"},"k:746":{"book":"kural","num":746,"t":["எல்லாப் பொருளும் உடைத்தாய் இடத்துதவும்","நல்லாள் உடையது அரண்"],"p":"A fort is that which has all (needful) things, and excellent heroes that can help it against destruction (by foes).","r":"A fort has every needed thing and good men who help at the critical place.","n":"நல்லாள் is not merely a strong man. Parimelazhagar reads the 'good' as love of the king, honour, valour and vigilance, so the defenders' character is already part of the fort's definition.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["aran","padai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:75"},"k:747":{"book":"kural","num":747,"t":["முற்றியும் முற்றா தெறிந்தும் அறைப்படுத்தும்","பற்றற் கரியது அரண்"],"p":"A fort is that which cannot be captured by blockading, assaulting, or undermining it.","r":"A fort is hard to take by encircling it, by storming it without encircling, or by working on those inside.","n":"Pope's 'mine beneath the ground' turns அறைப்படுத்தல் into tunnelling, while Parimelazhagar makes it subversion, turning trusted insiders so they open the gates. On his reading the third danger is treachery. He says stores answer the first threat and good men the other two, so walls answer none of them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["aran","pagai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:75"},"k:748":{"book":"kural","num":748,"t":["முற்றாற்றி முற்றி யவரையும் பற்றாற்றிப்","பற்றியார் வெல்வது அரண்"],"p":"That is a fort whose inmates are able to overcome without losing their ground, even abler men who have besieged it.","r":"A fort lets those who hold it stand fast and defeat even those strong enough to besiege it.","n":"Parimelazhagar stresses that a small force does not merely endure a large one but wins, which raises the claim from survival to victory. He also says others explain this verse differently, and his text doesn't give their view.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aran","padai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:75"},"k:749":{"book":"kural","num":749,"t":["முனைமுகத்து மாற்றலர் சாய வினைமுகத்து","வீறெய்தி மாண்ட தரண்"],"p":"A fort is that which derives excellence from the stratagems made (by its inmates) to defeat their enemies in the battlefield.","r":"A fort gains its distinction from the actions by which its people bring down enemies as soon as battle begins.","n":"The chapter's language shifts here from what the fort has to what is done in it, preparing for 750.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aran","padai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:75"},"k:750":{"book":"kural","num":750,"t":["எனைமாட்சித் தாகியக் கண்ணும் வினைமாட்சி","இல்லார்கண் இல்லது அரண்"],"p":"Although a fort may possess all (the above-said) excellence, it is, as it were without these, if its inmates possess not the excellence of action.","r":"Whatever excellences it has, a fort is nothing in the hands of those without excellence in action.","n":"Pope's 'craven souls' narrows this to cowardice. வினைமாட்சி is broader, and Parimelazhagar includes sitting idle, acting without measure and doing what is unsuitable. A brave incompetent would fail this verse as well.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aran","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:75"},"k:751":{"book":"kural","num":751,"t":["பொருளல் லவரைப் பொருளாகச் செய்யும்","பொருளல்லது இல்லை பொருள்"],"p":"Besides wealth there is nothing that can change people of no importance into those of (some) importance.","r":"Apart from wealth, nothing makes persons of no account into persons of account.","n":"பொருள் appears four times, playing on 'wealth' and 'something that counts'. Whether this is approval or a dry observation is left open. Parimelazhagar names the unlearned and low-born and concludes that wealth alone should be amassed, while Kalaignar calls those raised unworthy of regard, which has an edge.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","pirappu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:76"},"k:752":{"book":"kural","num":752,"t":["இல்லாரை எல்லாரும் எள்ளுவர் செல்வரை","எல்லாரும் செய்வர் சிறப்பு"],"p":"All despise the poor; (but) all praise the rich.","r":"Everyone scorns those who have nothing and everyone honours the rich.","n":"The verse states a social fact without saying it is just. Parimelazhagar sharpens it, adding that the poor are scorned though they have every good and the rich honoured though they have every evil, words which aren't in the verse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","sirumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:76"},"k:753":{"book":"kural","num":753,"t":["பொருளென்னும் பொய்யா விளக்கம் இருளறுக்கும்","எண்ணிய தேயத்துச் சென்று"],"p":"The imperishable light of wealth goes into regions desired (by its owner) and destroys the darkness (of enmity therein).","r":"The unfailing lamp called wealth goes to whatever country one has in mind and cuts away the darkness.","n":"Unlike a lamp, this one travels to where it is intended.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","pagai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The darkness is enmity, and the lamp reaches enemy lands on its owner's behalf.","தன்னைச் செய்தவர்க்கு அவர் நினைத்த தேயத்துச் சென்று பகை என்னும் இருளைக் கெடுக்கும்."],["manakkudavar","The darkness is enmity, and a wealthy king's command runs wherever he intends.","அரசன் பொரு ளுடையனானால், தான் கருதிய தேசமெல்லாம் தன்னாணை நடத்துவானென்றது."],["kalaignar","The darkness is ordinary suffering, which money in hand drives away anywhere one goes.","இருள் என்னும் துன்பத்தைத் துரத்தி விட முடிகிறது."]],"ch":"k:76"},"k:754":{"book":"kural","num":754,"t":["அறன்ஈனும் இன்பமும் ஈனும் திறனறிந்து","தீதின்றி வந்த பொருள்"],"p":"The wealth acquired with a knowledge of the proper means and without foul practices will yield virtue and happiness.","r":"Wealth that comes with knowledge of the right means and without wrong yields virtue and also pleasure.","n":"The commentators differ on the audience. Parimelazhagar makes 'without wrong' a matter of a king not being tyrannical, while Manakkudavar means no harm to anyone else, and the verse itself names no king.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["selvam","aram","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:76"},"k:755":{"book":"kural","num":755,"t":["அருளொடும் அன்பொடும் வாராப் பொருளாக்கம்","புல்லார் புரள விடல்"],"p":"(Kings) should rather avoid than seek the accumulation of wealth which does not flow in with mercy and love.","r":"Accumulation of wealth that does not come with grace and love should not be embraced, but let go.","n":"புல்லார் புரள விடல் is more than refusing. It is the gesture of letting something roll away. Coming right after 754 and before 756, it is the chapter's clearest limit.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","arul","anbu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Coming with grace and love means, for a king, taxing no more than the rightful one-sixth.","அவற்றொடு கூடி வருதலாவது, ஆறிலொன்றாய் வருதல்."],["manakkudavar","It applies to any earner: not profiting from others' distress and not losing affection for one's intimates while earning.","பொருள் தேடுங்கால் பிறர் வருத்தத்திற்கு உபகரியாதும், பயின்றார் மாட்டுக் காதலில்லாமலும்"]],"ch":"k:76"},"k:756":{"book":"kural","num":756,"t":["உறுபொருளும் உல்கு பொருளும்தன் ஒன்னார்த்","தெறுபொருளும் வேந்தன் பொருள்"],"p":"Unclaimed wealth, wealth acquired by taxes, and wealth (got) by conquest of foes are (all) the wealth of the king.","r":"Wealth that falls in on its own, wealth from customs, and wealth from crushing his enemies are the king's wealth.","n":"Parimelazhagar takes this as the income beyond the one-sixth of 755. Without that bridge, an income by force would sit oddly next to wealth that comes with grace.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["selvam","vendhan","pagai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","உறுபொருள் is ownerless wealth: long-buried treasure and estates without heirs.","வைத்தார் இறந்துபோக நெடுங்காலம் நிலத்தின்கண் கிடந்து பின் கண்டெடுத்தூஉம், தாயத்தார் பெறாததூஉமாம்."],["manakkudavar","உறுபொருள் is revenue connected with protection.","உறு பொருள் - காவற் பொருள்."]],"ch":"k:76"},"k:757":{"book":"kural","num":757,"t":["அருளென்னும் அன்பீன் குழவி பொருளென்னும்","செல்வச் செவிலியால் உண்டு"],"p":"The child mercy which is borne by love grows under the care of the rich nurse of wealth.","r":"The child called grace, born of love, is raised by the wealthy foster-mother called wealth.","n":"This gives a genealogy of compassion. Parimelazhagar distinguishes love, which runs along ties, from grace, which goes to any sufferer. The mother gives birth but cannot rear alone, so wealth is necessary without being the source.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","anbu","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:76"},"k:758":{"book":"kural","num":758,"t":["குன்றேறி யானைப் போர் கண்டற்றால் தன்கைத்தொன்று","உண்டாகச் செய்வான் வினை"],"p":"An undertaking of one who has wealth in one's hands is like viewing an elephant-fight from a hill-top.","r":"Undertaking a task with money in hand is like watching an elephant fight from a hilltop.","n":"The picture's point is the safety of distance. Parimelazhagar says the funded person commissions the able and finishes the work without fear or strain, which is a view of capital as insulation from risk.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:76"},"k:759":{"book":"kural","num":759,"t":["செய்க பொருளைச் செறுநர் செருக்கறுக்கும்","எஃகதனிற் கூரிய தில்"],"p":"Accumulate wealth; it will destroy the arrogance of (your) foes; there is no weapon sharper than it.","r":"Make wealth; there is no steel sharper than it for cutting down the pride of enemies.","n":"The target is செருக்கு and not the enemy's body. Parimelazhagar seizes on this: other blades can't cut an intangible. The verse is one of the chapter's few bald imperatives.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["selvam","pagai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:76"},"k:760":{"book":"kural","num":760,"t":["ஒண்பொருள் காழ்ப்ப இயற்றியார்க்கு எண்பொருள்","ஏனை இரண்டும் ஒருங்கு"],"p":"To those who have honestly acquired an abundance of riches, the other two, (virtue and pleasure) are things easy (of acquisition).","r":"For those who have made bright wealth in full measure, the other two are easily had, together.","n":"ஒண் does a lot of work: the claim is for bright wealth and not for any wealth. Parimelazhagar reads ஒண் as wealth gained by the right path, while Manakkudavar simply glosses it as bright. Everything else in the claim hinges on that adjective.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","aram","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:76"},"k:761":{"book":"kural","num":761,"t":["உறுப்பமைந்து ஊறஞ்சா வெல்படை வேந்தன்","வெறுக்கையுள் எல்லாம் தலை"],"p":"The army which is complete in (its) parts and conquers without fear of wounds is the chief wealth of the king.","r":"A conquering army complete in its parts and unafraid of wounds is the chief of all a king's wealth.","n":"வெறுக்கை calls the army wealth, directly after the chapter on wealth. Parimelazhagar's reason is that it guards the other limbs of the state and the king himself. The commentators count the parts differently: he has four, Manakkudavar five.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","selvam","vendhan"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:77"},"k:762":{"book":"kural","num":762,"t":["உலைவிடத்து ஊறஞ்சா வன்கண் தொலைவிடத்துத்","தொல்படைக் கல்லால் அரிது"],"p":"Ancient army can alone have the valour which makes it stand by its king at the time of defeat, fearless of wounds and unmindful of its reduced strength.","r":"The hardness that fears no wound when the king is faltering and the force itself is reduced is rare except in an ancient army.","n":"The verse locates courage in continuity. Only தொல் is the verse's own; Parimelazhagar makes it a technical term, ranking the ancestral force first among six kinds, above hired, allied and enemy-derived troops.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:77"},"k:763":{"book":"kural","num":763,"t":["ஒலித்தக்கால் என்னாம் உவரி எலிப்பகை","நாகம் உயிர்ப்பக் கெடும்"],"p":"What if (a host of) hostile rats roar like the sea ? They will perish at the mere breath of the cobra.","r":"What if a host of hostile rats roar like the sea? They perish when the cobra merely breathes.","n":"The verse doesn't mention armies at all. Parimelazhagar reads it as an allegory recommending one hero over many who are not. The cobra doesn't even strike: its breath is enough.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:77"},"k:764":{"book":"kural","num":764,"t":["அழிவின்றி அறைபோகா தாகி வழிவந்த","வன்க ணதுவே படை"],"p":"That indeed is an army which has stood firm of old without suffering destruction or deserting (to the enemy).","r":"An army is one that does not collapse, cannot be turned by subversion, and has courage that has come down to it.","n":"அறைபோகாது repeats the danger of 747: the same threat of being turned from inside. Parimelazhagar's supporting verse is a woman's boast of the men of her house who fell in battle, so the inheritance includes grief.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:77"},"k:765":{"book":"kural","num":765,"t":["கூற்றுடன்று மேல்வரினும் கூடி எதிர்நிற்கும்","ஆற்ற லதுவே படை"],"p":"That indeed is an army which is capable of offering a united resistance, even if Yama advances against it with fury.","r":"An army is one that, even when Death comes raging at it, has the power to stand together and face it.","n":"The operative word is கூடி. The courage asked for is collective. Parimelazhagar says the many are of one heart because they love the king, and that ஆற்றல் here is strength of mind.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:77"},"k:766":{"book":"kural","num":766,"t":["மறமானம் மாண்ட வழிச்செலவு தேற்றம்","எனநான்கே ஏமம் படைக்கு"],"p":"Valour, honour, following in the excellent-footsteps (of its predecessors) and trust-worthiness; these four alone constitute the safeguard of an army.","r":"Valour, honour, walking the proven path and தேற்றம் are the four things that protect an army.","n":"Parimelazhagar's 'path' includes not striking those who have turned their backs, so honour limits valour. He concludes that such men need no other fort.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram","maanam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","தேற்றம் is being trusted by the king.","அரசனால் தேறப்படுதலும்"],["manakkudavar","தேற்றம் is the army's own clarity, and the good path is steadiness in fighting.","நல்லவழிச்சேறலாவது மறஞ் செய்யுங் காலத்துக் கலக்கமின்மை."],["kalaignar","It is winning the trust of the leader, close to Parimelazhagar but placed on the leader.","தலைவனின் நம்பிக்கையைப் பெறுதல்"]],"ch":"k:77"},"k:767":{"book":"kural","num":767,"t":["தார்தாங்கிச் செல்வது தானை தலைவந்த","போர்தாங்கும் தன்மை அறிந்து"],"p":"That is an army which knowing the art of warding off an impending struggle, can bear against the dust-van (of a hostile force).","r":"An army is one that knows how to meet the battle coming at it and advances bearing the தார்.","n":"This is the chapter's one verse about skill. Parimelazhagar sends readers to Sanskrit treatises on battle formation and declines to describe them.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","தார் is the enemy's vanguard, held off before pressing forward.","அவர் தூசியைத் தன்மேல் வாராமல் தடுத்துத்தான் அதன்மேற்செல்வதே படையாவது."],["kalaignar","தார் is the garland of victory that the capable army carries.","வெற்றி மாலை தாங்கிச் செல்லக்கூடிய சிறந்த படையாகும்."]],"ch":"k:77"},"k:768":{"book":"kural","num":768,"t":["அடல்தகையும் ஆற்றலும் இல்லெனினும் தானை","படைத்தகையால் பாடு பெறும்"],"p":"Though destitute of courage to fight and strength (to endure), an army may yet gain renown by the splendour of its appearance.","r":"Even without striking power or endurance, an army gains stature through its martial bearing.","n":"A surprising concession that appearance has real force. Parimelazhagar's பாடு is the fear an army causes at first sight, so display works on the enemy's mind as 744's fort does.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Display gives some standing, though the wording itself shows striking power and endurance remain indispensable.","'இல்லெனினும்' எனவே, அவற்றது இன்றியமையாமை பெறப்பட்டது."],["kalaignar","The army's parade appearance should be more distinguished than either valour or endurance.","இரண்டையும் விட ஒரு படையின் அணிவகுப்புத் தோற்றம் சிறப்புடையதாக"]],"ch":"k:77"},"k:769":{"book":"kural","num":769,"t":["சிறுமையும் செல்லாத் துனியும் வறுமையும்","இல்லாயின் வெல்லும் படை"],"p":"An army can triumph (over its foes) if it is free from diminution; irremediable aversion and poverty.","r":"An army wins if it is free of shrinking, of a resentment that will not leave, and of poverty.","n":"The verse states an army's conditions, but Parimelazhagar assigns the blame upward. Desertion and poverty come from a king who doesn't pay, and resentment from outrages such as seizing their women. A verse on the army becomes a verse on the king's conduct.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","vendhan","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:77"},"k:770":{"book":"kural","num":770,"t":["நிலைமக்கள் சால உடைத்தெனினும் தானை","தலைமக்கள் இல்வழி இல்"],"p":"Though an army may contain a large number of permanent soldiers, it cannot last if it has no generals.","r":"Even with plenty of steadfast men, there is no army where there are no leaders.","n":"Parimelazhagar's explanation is striking. If leaders go, the men say there is no one to witness and leave too. On this view courage needs an audience.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","vendhan"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:77"},"k:771":{"book":"kural","num":771,"t":["என்னைமுன் நில்லன்மின் தெவ்விர் பலரென்னை","முன்நின்று கல்நின் றவர்"],"p":"O my foes, stand not before my leader; (for) many are those who did so but afterwards stood (in the shape of) statues.","r":"Enemies, do not stand before my lord; many who stood before him now stand in stone.","n":"The verse puns on நில்: to stand in battle and to stand as a hero-stone. The stone is both a threat and an honour, a fact that makes the boast double-edged. It is a voice in the Kural that isn't Valluvar's own, and Parimelazhagar notes the warrior places his valour on his king.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["maram","pugazh","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:78"},"k:772":{"book":"kural","num":772,"t":["கான முயலெய்த அம்பினில் யானை","பிழைத்தவேல் ஏந்தல் இனிது"],"p":"It is more pleasant to hold the dart that has missed an elephant than that which has hit hare in the forest.","r":"Carrying a spear that missed an elephant is sweeter than carrying an arrow that hit a forest hare.","n":"The verse measures an act by its target, not its result. Parimelazhagar supplies a dramatic setting, a warrior ashamed that the opposing force fled, but the verse itself gives only the comparison.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:78"},"k:773":{"book":"kural","num":773,"t":["பேராண்மை என்ப தறுகண்ஒன் றுற்றக்கால்","ஊராண்மை மற்றதன் எஃகு"],"p":"The learned say that fierceness (incontest with a foe) is indeed great valour; but to become a benefactor in case of accident (to a foe) is the extreme (limit) of that valour.","r":"They call fierceness great manhood; but when something befalls the foe, generous help is its cutting edge.","n":"The image is exact: the kindness isn't an exception to valour but its sharpest point. Parimelazhagar's example is Rama telling the defeated Ravana to go and come back tomorrow, an epic illustration that the verse doesn't state.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["maram","arul","pagai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:78"},"k:774":{"book":"kural","num":774,"t":["கைவேல் களிற்றொடு போக்கி வருபவன்","மெய்வேல் பறியா நகும்"],"p":"The hero who after casting the lance in his hand on an elephant, comes (in search of another) will pluck the one (that sticks) in his body and laugh (exultingly).","r":"Having sent his spear away with an elephant, he comes on seeking another, and laughs as he pulls one from his own body.","n":"The laugh is the verse's whole shock. For Parimelazhagar he is happy because he has found what he was seeking, and rage had kept him from noticing the wound. A wound registers as supply.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:78"},"k:775":{"book":"kural","num":775,"t":["விழித்தகண் வேல்கொண டெறிய அழித்திமைப்பின்","ஒட்டன்றோ வன்க ணவர்க்கு"],"p":"Is it not a defeat to the valiant to wink and destroy their ferocious look when a lance in cast at them (by their foe) ?","r":"When the eye that glares at the enemy gives way and blinks at a flung spear, is that not flight, to the fierce?","n":"Valour is carried to the reflex. Parimelazhagar's line is that even withdrawing the angry gaze counts as retreat.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","maanam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The spear is thrown at the man, and giving way means ending the glare by blinking.","அவர் வேலைக்கொண்டு எறிய அஃது ஆற்றாது அந்நோக்கை அழித்து இமைக்குமாயின்"],["manakkudavar","The spear is thrust into the eye itself, and blinking again at that is equal to defeat.","மாற்றாரோடு எதிர்த்துச் சிவந்து நோக்கின கண்ணிலே ஒரு வேலினாலே எறிய"]],"ch":"k:78"},"k:776":{"book":"kural","num":776,"t":["விழுப்புண் படாதநாள் எல்லாம் வழுக்கினுள்","வைக்கும்தன் நாளை எடுத்து"],"p":"The hero will reckon among wasted days all those on which he had not received severe wounds.","r":"Going over his days, he sets among the wasted all those on which he took no noble wound.","n":"விழுப்புண் is a wound of a certain kind. Parimelazhagar restricts it to face and chest, so that it marks one who did not turn. A ledger of days is a sober image for a very unsober ethic.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["maram","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:78"},"k:777":{"book":"kural","num":777,"t":["சுழலும் இசைவேண்டி வேண்டா உயிரார்","கழல்யாப்புக் காரிகை நீர்த்து"],"p":"The fastening of ankle-ring by those who disire a world-wide renown and not (the safety of) their lives is like adorning (themselves).","r":"For those who want fame that circles the world and do not want life, the tying on of the war-anklet is an adornment.","n":"The verse names only fame. Parimelazhagar adds heaven (துறக்கம்), saying fame remains encircling the earth while the hero goes on, so the afterlife is his reading. காரிகை turns a mark of danger into jewellery.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","maram","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:78"},"k:778":{"book":"kural","num":778,"t":["உறின்உயிர் அஞ்சா மறவர் இறைவன்","செறினும் சீர்குன்றல் இலர்"],"p":"The heroes who are not afraid of losing their life in a contest will not cool their ardour, even if the king prohibits (their fighting).","r":"Warriors who do not fear for their lives when battle comes will not lessen in their distinction even if their lord is angry.","n":"This verse makes a real space between warrior and king, which the army chapter's language of loyalty doesn't.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["maram","vendhan"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","They press on to battle even if the king angrily forbids it, because their ardour doesn't diminish.","தம் இறைவன் அது வேண்டா என்று முனியினும் அவ் வீரமிகுதி குன்றார்."],["kalaignar","The true warriors are those who go on doing their duty though their leader is angry with them.","தலைவன் சினந்தாலும் சிறப்புக் குறையாமல் கடமை ஆற்றுபவர்கள்தான்"]],"ch":"k:78"},"k:779":{"book":"kural","num":779,"t":["இழைத்தது இகவாமைச் சாவாரை யாரே","பிழைத்தது ஒறுக்கிற் பவர்"],"p":"Who would reproach with failure those who seal their oath with their death ?","r":"Who could reproach those who die so that what they vowed is not broken, for having failed?","n":"Parimelazhagar makes a bold move: dying in the attempt counts as fulfilling the vow. The verse's rhetorical question closes off blame for one who failed, without calling the failure a success.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam","saakkaadu","maram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:78"},"k:780":{"book":"kural","num":780,"t":["புரந்தார்கண் நீர்மல்கச் சாகிற்பின் சாக்காடு","இரந்துகோள் தக்கது உடைத்து"],"p":"If (heroes) can so die as to fill with tears the eyes of their rulers, such a death deserves to be obtained even by begging.","r":"If one can die so that the eyes of those who sustained one fill with tears, that death is worth begging for.","n":"The measure of the death is another's tears, and the one who weeps is the patron. Parimelazhagar sets it against dying sick at home among weeping kin, which only spends old deeds, whereas this earns heaven. The verse itself mentions neither of those.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saakkaadu","vendhan","maram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:78"},"k:781":{"book":"kural","num":781,"t":["செயற்கரிய யாவுள நட்பின் அதுபோல்","வினைக்கரிய யாவுள காப்பு"],"p":"What things are there so difficult to acquire as friendship ? What guards are there so difficult to break through by the efforts (of one's foes) ?","r":"What is as hard to make as friendship, and what guard is as hard for hostile deeds to get past?","n":"The two questions are linked: the difficulty of getting it is the reason for its value as protection. Parimelazhagar opens by classifying friendship as natural or made and says this chapter treats the made kind, kin being treated elsewhere.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","aran"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:79"},"k:782":{"book":"kural","num":782,"t":["நிறைநீர நீரவர் கேண்மை பிறைமதிப்","பின்னீர பேதையார் நட்பு"],"p":"The friendship of the wise waxes like the new moon; (but) that of fools wanes like the full moon.","r":"Friendship with the good grows like the crescent moon filling, and that of fools shrinks like the moon after it is full.","n":"The moon's phases make growth depend on the starting point: the good begin small and fools begin large. Parimelazhagar's reason is knowledge. Each pair at first doesn't know the other and later does, and it is that knowing which makes one friendship grow and the other shrink.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:79"},"k:783":{"book":"kural","num":783,"t":["நவில்தொறும் நூல்நயம் போலும் பயில்தொறும்","பண்புடை யாளர் தொடர்பு"],"p":"Like learning, the friendship of the noble, the more it is cultivated, the more delightful does it become.","r":"The more it is practised, the bond with people of character is like the more a book is studied, its nayam.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["natpu","kalvi","panpu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Nayam is delight: both friendship and study give increasing pleasure.","நூற் பொருள் கற்குந்தோறும் கற்றார்க்கு இன்பஞ் செய்தலை ஒக்கும்."],["manakkudavar","The gain is understanding: a friend of good character teaches as a book does.","இது குணவானோடு நட்புக் கொள்ளின், அறிவுண்டா மென்றது."]],"ch":"k:79"},"k:784":{"book":"kural","num":784,"t":["நகுதற் பொருட்டன்று நட்டல் மிகுதிக்கண்","மேற்செனறு இடித்தற் பொருட்டு"],"p":"Friendship is to be practised not for the purpose of laughing but for that of being beforehand in giving one another sharp rebukes in case of transgression.","r":"Friendship is not for laughing together, but for going ahead to rebuke when one oversteps.","n":"மேற்சென்று has timing in it. The rebuke goes out before the wrong is done, says Parimelazhagar, and it is harsh because sweet words will not turn the person back. This is not a claim that friends should never laugh, only that laughter isn't the purpose.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["natpu","idithal"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:79"},"k:785":{"book":"kural","num":785,"t":["புணர்ச்சி பழகுதல் வேண்டா உணர்ச்சிதான்","நட்பாங் கிழமை தரும்"],"p":"Living together and holding frequent intercourse are not necessary (for friendship); (mutual) understanding can alone create a claim for it.","r":"Neither meeting nor long acquaintance is needed; a shared feeling alone gives the claim of friendship.","n":"Parimelazhagar's example is Kopperuncholan and Pisirandaiyar, who never met and whose bond was so close that one died with the other. The verse itself offers no example and says nothing of dying together.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:79"},"k:786":{"book":"kural","num":786,"t":["முகநக நட்பது நட்பன்று நெஞ்சத்து","அகநக நட்பது நட்பு"],"p":"The love that dwells (merely in the smiles of the face is not friendship; (but) that which dwells deep in the smiles of the heart is true friendship.","r":"Friendship that makes the face smile is not friendship; friendship that makes the heart within smile is friendship.","n":"The verse sounds as if the face doesn't matter. Parimelazhagar says that an implied 'also' is present, so both should bloom together. That softens a sharper opposition in the Tamil.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:79"},"k:787":{"book":"kural","num":787,"t":["அழிவி னவைநீக்கி ஆறுய்த்து அழிவின்கண்","அல்லல் உழப்பதாம் நட்பு"],"p":"(True) friendship turns aside from evil (ways) makes (him) walk in the (good) way, and, in case of loss if shares his sorrow (with him).","r":"Friendship turns one away from what destroys, sets one on the path, and in ruin shares the suffering.","n":"The order is a plan of action: prevent, then guide, then, if all that fails, suffer alongside. Parimelazhagar adds that ruin in the third stage comes from a divine cause and so can't be averted. The verse names no such cause.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","kedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:79"},"k:788":{"book":"kural","num":788,"t":["உடுக்கை இழந்தவன் கைபோல ஆங்கே","இடுக்கண் களைவதாம் நட்பு"],"p":"(True) friendship hastens to the rescue of the afflicted (as readily) as the hand of one whose garment is loosened (before an assembly).","r":"Like the hand of a man whose garment slips, friendship clears away distress there and then.","n":"The simile is about speed and also about no deliberation. Parimelazhagar notes that the hand gets there before the mind does. The garment also implies that the friend's trouble is a shame to be covered, and that the friend is as close as one's own limb.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:79"},"k:789":{"book":"kural","num":789,"t":["நட்பிற்கு வீற்றிருக்கை யாதெனின் கொட்பின்றி","ஒல்லும்வாய் ஊன்றும் நிலை"],"p":"Friendship may be said to be on its throne when it possesses the power of supporting one at all times and under all circumstances, (in the practice or virtue and wealth).","r":"Where does friendship sit enthroned? In standing firm without wavering, supporting however one can.","n":"ஒல்லும்வாய் sets a realistic limit: as far as one is able. The throne is unwavering support, not unlimited power. Parimelazhagar specifies support in virtue and wealth, which Pope follows in parentheses, while the verse itself gives no object.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:79"},"k:790":{"book":"kural","num":790,"t":["இனையர் இவரெமக்கு இன்னம்யாம் என்று","புனையினும் புல்லென்னும் நட்பு"],"p":"Though friends may praise one another saying, \"He is so intimate with us, and we so much (with him)\"; (still) such friendship will appear mean.","r":"'They are this to us and we are that to them': even when so adorned, friendship looks shabby.","n":"A subtle point. For Parimelazhagar friendship has no 'I' and 'they', so that listing what each is to the other brings in a difference that did not exist. The praise itself is what damages it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:79"},"k:791":{"book":"kural","num":791,"t":["நாடாது நட் டலிற் கேடில்லை நட்டபின்","வீடில்லை நட்பாள் பவர்க்கு"],"p":"As those who are of a friendly nature will not forsake (a friend) after once loving (him), there is no evil so great as contracting a friendship without due inquiry.","r":"Nothing brings ruin like befriending without inquiry, since for those devoted to friendship there is no leaving once it is formed.","n":"The logic turns on faithfulness: the reason for caution is precisely the commitment to stay. Parimelazhagar adds that with no distinction between friends, the friend's sins and blame become one's own.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","aaraaydhal","kedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:80"},"k:792":{"book":"kural","num":792,"t":["ஆய்ந்தாய்ந்து கொள்ளாதான் கேண்மை கடைமுறை","தான்சாம் துயரம் தரும்"],"p":"The friendship contracted by him who has not made repeated inquiry will in the end grieve (him) to death.","r":"The friendship of one who does not inquire again and again ends by bringing grief that kills him.","n":"The doubled ஆய்ந்தாய்ந்து asks for repeated testing and not a single check. For Parimelazhagar no enemy is needed, for one brings it on oneself, and the bad friend's enemies become one's own.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","aaraaydhal","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:80"},"k:793":{"book":"kural","num":793,"t":["குணமும் குடிமையும் குற்றமும் குன்றா","இனனும் அறிந்தியாக்க நட்பு"],"p":"Make friendship (with one) after ascertaining (his) character, birth, defects and the whole of one's relations.","r":"Know his qualities, his family, his faults and his inan, and only then form a friendship.","n":"Faults are on the list to be known, not to be absent. Parimelazhagar's point is that no one is faultless, so the question is whether the faults can be borne.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["natpu","aaraaydhal","kudi","panpu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","குன்றா இனன் is a friend's undiminished kin, since those bound to kin bring that bond along with them.","சுற்றப் பிணிப்புடையார் நட்டாரோடும் பிணிப்புண்டு வருதலின்"],["kalaignar","The phrase means qualities that don't decline, not relatives.","குறையாத இயல்புகள் எவை?"]],"ch":"k:80"},"k:794":{"book":"kural","num":794,"t":["குடிப்பிறந்து தன்கண் பழிநாணு வானைக்","கொடுத்தும் கொளல்வேண்டும் நட்பு"],"p":"The friendship of one who belongs to a (good) family and is afraid of (being charged with) guilt, is worth even purchasing.","r":"One of good family who feels shame at blame on himself: that friendship should be had even by giving.","n":"பழிநாணுவான் is a person who is governed by shame. The commercial phrase is startling for something the previous chapter praises so highly. Kalaignar softens it to 'by any means', while Parimelazhagar says plainly: buy it for a price.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","kudi","naanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:80"},"k:795":{"book":"kural","num":795,"t":["அழச்சொல்லி அல்லது இடித்து வழக்கறிய","வல்லார்நடபு ஆய்ந்து கொளல்"],"p":"You should examine and secure the friendship of those who can speak so as to make you weep over a crime (before its commission) or rebuke you severely (after you have done it) and are able to teach you (the ways of) the world.","r":"Inquire and take the friendship of those able to speak until one weeps, to rebuke, and to teach what is proper.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","idithal","aaraaydhal"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The verse describes friends, who dissuade before the act, press hard after it, and make one do what is proper.","தாம் உலக வழக்கல்லது செய்யக் கருதின்சோகம் பிறக்கும்வகை சொல்லி விலக்கியும்"],["manakkudavar","The verse is about which ministers a ruler should befriend.","இது மந்திரிகளுள் நட்பாக்கற் பாலாரைக் கூறிற்று."]],"ch":"k:80"},"k:796":{"book":"kural","num":796,"t":["கேட்டினும் உண்டோ ர் உறுதி கிளைஞரை","நீட்டி அளப்பதோர் கோல்"],"p":"Even in ruin there is some good; (for) it is a rod by which one may measure fully (the affection of one's) relations.","r":"Even in ruin there is one benefit: it is a rod that measures one's people to full length.","n":"The benefit is knowledge rather than comfort. Parimelazhagar says the true extent of each friend's bond stays hidden in wealthy times, and that ruin measures it for choosing better afterward.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","kedu","aaraaydhal"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:80"},"k:797":{"book":"kural","num":797,"t":["ஊதியம் என்பது ஒருவற்குப் பேதையார்","கேண்மை ஒரீஇ விடல்"],"p":"It is indead a gain for one to renounce the friendship of fools.","r":"Gain for a person means giving up and leaving the friendship of fools.","n":"The verse calls a loss a gain. Parimelazhagar says one must also move away, because like sandal and vengai burning together, nearness alone brings harm.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:80"},"k:798":{"book":"kural","num":798,"t":["உள்ளற்க உள்ளம் சிறுகுவ கொள்ளற்க","அல்லற்கண் ஆற்றறுப்பார் நட்பு"],"p":"Do not think of things that discourage your mind, nor contract friendship with those who would forsake you in adversity.","r":"Do not think on what shrinks the spirit, and do not take the friendship of those who cut away in distress.","n":"The verse pairs a rule about thought and a rule about company, and Parimelazhagar treats the first as an illustration of the second. Fair-weather friends are thus a thing that shrinks the mind.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:80"},"k:799":{"book":"kural","num":799,"t":["கெடுங்காலைக் கைவிடுவார் கேண்மை அடுங்காலை","உள்ளினும் உள்ளஞ் சுடும்"],"p":"The very thought of the friendship of those who have deserted one at the approach of adversity will burn one's mind at the time of death.","r":"Friendship with those who forsake one in ruin burns the heart, even to think of, at the moment of death.","n":"Betrayal is measured against death and is what burns. Parimelazhagar goes further: the mere thought of it is worse than Death, and even for an unconnected person who thinks of it. He also records another reading in which the thought is of the harm one brought on oneself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","kedu","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:80"},"k:800":{"book":"kural","num":800,"t":["மருவுக மாசற்றார் கேண்மைஒன் றீத்தும்","ஒருவுக ஒப்பிலார் நட்பு"],"p":"Continue to enjoy the friendship of the pure; (but) renounce even with a gift, the friendship of those who do not agree (with the world).","r":"Keep close to the friendship of the stainless; give something if you must, but leave the friendship of those who are oppilar.","n":"The cost of a bad friendship is paid at the end as well. It balances 794: one may pay to get a friend and pay to be rid of one.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["natpu","panpu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","ஒப்பிலார் are those out of accord with the world.","உலகோடு ஒத்தலில்லார் நட்பினை அறியாது கொண்டாராயின்"],["manakkudavar","ஒப்பிலார் are those not one's equals.","தனக்கு நிகரில்லாதார் நட்பினின்று நீங்குக"],["kalaignar","They are simply those who carry a stain, the contrary of the first half.","மாசு உள்ளவர்களின் நட்பை, விலைகொடுத்தாவது விலக்கிடவேண்டும்."]],"ch":"k:80"},"k:801":{"book":"kural","num":801,"t":["பழைமை எனப்படுவது யாதெனின் யாதும்","கிழமையைக் கீழ்ந்திடா நட்பு"],"p":"Imtimate friendship is that which cannot in the least be injured by (things done through the) right (of longstanding intimacy).","r":"What is called old friendship? It is friendship that in no way cuts down the liberties that intimacy gives.","n":"The definition is not about time, and Parimelazhagar says so outright. His list of permitted liberties is concrete: acting without asking, even acting to one's harm, taking what one needs, showing neither deference nor fear.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pazhaimai","natpu","kizhamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:81"},"k:802":{"book":"kural","num":802,"t":["நட்பிற் குறுப்புக் கெழுதகைமை மற்றதற்கு","உப்பாதல் சான்றோர் கடன்"],"p":"The constituents of friendship are (things done through) the right of intimacy; to be pleased with such a right is the duty of the wise.","r":"Intimate liberty is a limb of friendship, and being as salt to it is the duty of the worthy.","n":"உப்பு: the worthy person doesn't merely tolerate liberties but gives them savour. Manakkudavar gives a homely comparison: a bitter gourd stays bitter however much seasoning goes in, so without consent no sweetness comes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kizhamai","natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:81"},"k:803":{"book":"kural","num":803,"t":["பழகிய நட்பெவன் செய்யுங் கெழுதகைமை","செய்தாங்கு அமையாக் கடை"],"p":"Of what avail is long-standing friendship, if friends do not admit as their own actions done through the right of intimacy ?","r":"What use is long-practised friendship if one does not accept a friend's liberty as if one had done it oneself?","n":"செய்தாங்கு is the core: treat the act as your own. Parimelazhagar adds that this holds even for what one wouldn't agree to, which is where the test really lies.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pazhaimai","kizhamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:81"},"k:804":{"book":"kural","num":804,"t":["விழைதகையான் வேண்டி இருப்பர் கெழுதகையாற்","கேளாது நட்டார் செயின்"],"p":"If friends, through the right of friendship, do (anything) without being asked, the wise will be pleased with them on account of its desirability.","r":"When friends act unasked out of intimacy, those who are wise welcome it for how desirable it is.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kizhamai","natpu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The act is one's own business done for one by a friend unasked, and nothing is better than finding it done.","ஒருவர்க்குத் தம் கருமம் தாம் அறியாமல் முடிந்திருத்தலின் ஊங்கு நன்மை யின்மையின்"],["manakkudavar","The act is something one doesn't approve, and one should still welcome it and not only permit it.","கேளாது இசைவில்லாதவற்றைச் செய்வாராயின், அதற்கு முனியாது"]],"ch":"k:81"},"k:805":{"book":"kural","num":805,"t":["பேதைமை ஒன்றோ பெருங்கிழமை என்றுணர்க","நோதக்க நட்டார் செயின்"],"p":"If friends should perform what is painful, understand that it is owing not only to ignorance, but also to the strong claims of intimacy.","r":"If friends do something that hurts, understand it as either folly or great intimacy.","n":"The verse is a rule of interpretation: two readings are allowed, and lack of love isn't one of them. Parimelazhagar brings in fate, saying the folly comes from a fate of loss. The verse does not. Pope's 'not only... but also' blurs the either-or that the commentators find.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kizhamai","pedhaimai","natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:81"},"k:806":{"book":"kural","num":806,"t":["எல்லைக்கண் நின்றார் துறவார் தொலைவிடத்தும்","தொல்லைக்கண் நின்றார் தொடர்பு"],"p":"Those who stand within the limits (of true friendship) will not even in adversity give up the intimacy of long-standing friends.","r":"Those who hold to the bounds will not give up the bond of those who stand in old friendship, even when ruin comes.","n":"For Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar the ruin comes from that friend, which makes this harder than loyalty in hard times. The verse's தொலைவிடத்தும் doesn't itself say who caused the ruin.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pazhaimai","natpu","kedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:81"},"k:807":{"book":"kural","num":807,"t":["அழிவந்த செய்யினும் அன்பறார் அன்பின்","வழிவந்த கேண்மை யவர்"],"p":"Those who have (long) stood in the path of affection will not give it up even if their friends cause (them) their ruin.","r":"Even when destructive things are done to them, those whose bond has come by the way of love do not cut off love.","n":"The verse does not say the friendship goes unaltered in every way, only that அன்பு does not end. It locates the constancy in love and not in the deeds.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","pazhaimai","natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:81"},"k:808":{"book":"kural","num":808,"t":["கேளிழுக்கம் கேளாக் கெழுதகைமை வல்லார்க்கு","நாளிழுக்கம் நட்டார் செயின்"],"p":"To those who understand that by which they should not listen to (tales about) the faults of their friends, that is a (profitable) day on which the latter may commit a fault.","r":"For those who have the strength of intimacy not to listen to reports of a friend's lapse, the day the friend lapses has its own meaning.","n":"The verse has a sting: the lapse is what reveals the strength. Parimelazhagar says the bond only shows when it is tested, so other days don't count.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kizhamai","natpu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The day a friend lapses is a day of real worth, for it alone shows such intimacy.","செய்து போந்துழியல்லது அவ்வுரிமை வெளிப்படாமையின், செய்யாதன நாளல்லவாயின."],["manakkudavar","Such a day is a good day.","நட்டோர் தப்புச் செய்யின், அந்த நாள் நல்ல நாளாம்"],["kalaignar","The reverse: if they do wrong to one so trusting, all the days of that friendship were wasted.","அவர்களுடன் நட்புக் கொண்டிருந்த நாளெல்லாம் வீணான நாளாகும்."]],"ch":"k:81"},"k:809":{"book":"kural","num":809,"t":["கெடாஅ வழிவந்த கேண்மையார் கேண்மை","விடாஅர் விழையும் உலகு"],"p":"They will be loved by the world, who have not forsaken the friendship of those with whom they have kept up an unbroken long-standing intimacy.","r":"The world loves those who do not give up the friendship of friends whose bond has come down unbroken.","n":"Parimelazhagar gives a motive: everyone reasons that such a person would be like this to them too, and comes to seek friendship. Loyalty to one is an advertisement to all. Manakkudavar's வழிவந்த points rather to friendship inherited through family.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pazhaimai","natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:81"},"k:810":{"book":"kural","num":810,"t":["விழையார் விழையப் படுப பழையார்கண்","பண்பின் தலைப்பிரியா தார்"],"p":"Even enemies will love those who have never changed in their affection to their long-standingfriends.","r":"Those who do not depart from their nature toward old friends are wanted even by those who do not want them.","n":"The commentators take விழையார் as enemies. The verse's own word is softer, 'those who don't desire', and the tight wordplay விழையார் விழையப் works without naming any foe.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pazhaimai","panpu","pagai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:81"},"k:811":{"book":"kural","num":811,"t":["பருகுவார் போலினும் பண்பிலார் கேண்மை","பெருகலிற் குன்றல் இனிது"],"p":"The decrease of friendship with those who look as if they would eat you up (through excess of love) while they are really destitute of goodness is far better than its increase.","r":"Though they seem to drink you up, the friendship of those without character is better shrinking than growing.","n":"பருகுவார் is the language of passion. Parimelazhagar cites a matching line of poetry and Manakkudavar adds 'with the eyes'. The verse warns that intensity is not evidence. The advice is modest too, since it only says that shrinking is better.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","panpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:82"},"k:812":{"book":"kural","num":812,"t":["உறின்நட்டு அறின்ஙருஉம் ஒப்பிலார் கேண்மை","பெறினும் இழப்பினும் என்?"],"p":"Of what avail is it to get or lose the friendship of those who love when there is gain and leave when there is none ?","r":"What does it matter whether one gains or loses the friendship of misfits who are friends when there is profit and leave when there is none?","n":"The rhetorical shrug is the point. Parimelazhagar concludes that one should simply be neutral toward them (நொதுமல்). They are neither to be sought nor fought, which sets them apart from the verses that say to run.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:82"},"k:813":{"book":"kural","num":813,"t":["உறுவது சீர்தூக்கும் நட்பும் பெறுவது","கொள்வாரும் கள்வரும் நேர்"],"p":"Friendship who calculate the profits (of their friendship), prostitutes who are bent on obtaining their gains, and thieves are (all) of the same character.","r":"Friends who weigh what they stand to gain, those who take what they get, and thieves are all alike.","n":"The middle term in the verse says only 'those who take what they get'. It is the commentators who identify them as prostitutes. In the verse's shape, the calculation of friends is the real target and the other two are comparisons.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:82"},"k:814":{"book":"kural","num":814,"t":["அமரகத்து ஆற்றறுக்கும் கல்லாமா அன்னார்","தமரின் தனிமை தலை"],"p":"Solitude is more to be desired than the society of those who resemble the untrained horses which throw down (their riders) in the fields of battle.","r":"Better solitude than having as one's own those like an untrained horse that throws its rider in battle.","n":"The horse has been carrying its rider well until the moment that counts. Parimelazhagar also notes the verse doesn't praise solitude, which is only less bad. Valluvar isn't recommending it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:82"},"k:815":{"book":"kural","num":815,"t":["செய்தேமஞ் சாராச் சிறியவர் புன்கேண்மை","எய்தலின் எய்தாமை நன்று"],"p":"It is far better to avoid that to contract the evil friendship of the base who cannot protect (their friends) even when appointed to do so.","r":"It is better not to get than to get the poor friendship of the small, who are no protection even after one has made them friends.","n":"ஏமம் brings in the guard of 781 again: a friend who doesn't protect has failed the definition.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","sirumai","aran"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The small are base people in general, whose failure is that they leave in ruin.","அரணாகாமை - தொலைவின்கண் விட்டு நீங்குதல்."],["manakkudavar","The small are particular kinds of people, gamblers, hunters and women among them, a gendered list which the verse doesn't contain.","சிறியார் - சூதர், வேட்டைக் காரர், பெண்டிர் போல்வார்."]],"ch":"k:82"},"k:816":{"book":"kural","num":816,"t":["பேதை பெருங்கெழீஇ நட்பின் அறிவுடையார்","ஏதின்மை கோடி உறும்"],"p":"The hatred of the wise is ten-million times more profitable than the excessive intimacy of the fool.","r":"Being a stranger to the wise is ten million times better than close friendship with a fool.","n":"ஏதின்மை is literally not-belonging. Pope and the commentators read it as enmity. Parimelazhagar's argument is that the enmity of the wise does no harm while the fool's friendship does every harm.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai","natpu","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:82"},"k:817":{"book":"kural","num":817,"t":["நகைவகைய ராகிய நட்பின் பகைவரால்","பத்தடுத்த கோடி உறும்"],"p":"What comes from enemies is a hundred million times more profitable than what comes from the friendship of those who cause only laughter.","r":"Ten crore times better than friendship of the kind that is all laughter is what comes from enemies.","n":"The multiplier goes up ten times from 816. Parimelazhagar says even the pains that enemies cause exceed the pleasures of these friends, identifies them as hangers-on who amuse for profit, and dismisses other commentators' readings as ungrammatical.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","pagai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:82"},"k:818":{"book":"kural","num":818,"t":["ஒல்லும் கருமம் உடற்று பவர்கேண்மை","சொல்லாடார் சோர விடல்"],"p":"Gradually abandon without revealing (beforehand) the friendship of those who pretend inability to carry out what they (really) could do.","r":"With those who make hard work of what they could easily do, let the friendship slip without a word.","n":"A practical subtlety. Parimelazhagar says speaking would only lead them to patch things up and go on, so the silence is tactical. It is the opposite of a formal break.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:82"},"k:819":{"book":"kural","num":819,"t":["கனவினும் இன்னாது மன்னோ வினைவேறு","சொல்வேறு பட்டார் தொடர்பு"],"p":"The friendship of those whose actions do not agree with their words will distress (one) even in (one's) dreams.","r":"Even in a dream, the bond with those whose deeds are one thing and words another is bitter.","n":"Parimelazhagar's gloss is exact: enemies in deed and friends in word. The dream adds that the harm follows into sleep, a place where in other verses love is what lives.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","vanjam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:82"},"k:820":{"book":"kural","num":820,"t":["எனைத்தும் குறுகுதல் ஓம்பல் மனைக்கெழீஇ","மன்றில் பழிப்பார் தொடர்பு"],"p":"Avoid even the least approach to a contraction of friendship with those who would love you in private but ridicule you in public.","r":"Take care not to come near at all to the bond of those who are friendly at home and defame you in the assembly.","n":"The contrast is between private and public, and it's the public that matters: a friend is tested by what is said in the மன்று. எனைத்தும் leaves no room for degree, unlike the 'better to shrink' of 811.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","vanjam","pazhi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:82"},"k:821":{"book":"kural","num":821,"t":["சீரிடம் காணின் எறிதற்குப் பட் டடை","நேரா நிரந்தவர் நட்பு"],"p":"The friendship of those who behave like friends without inward affection is a weapon that may be thrown when a favourable opportunity presents itself.","r":"The friendship of those who come close without inner consent is an anvil for striking, once they find the right place.","n":"A பட்டடை seems to hold a thing up and in fact is what makes the blow cut, so Pope's gloss 'weapon' loses the image. The false friend need not be the blade. The support he gives is what enables it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:83"},"k:822":{"book":"kural","num":822,"t":["இனம்போன்று இனமல்லார் கேண்மை மகளிர்","மனம்போல வேறு படும்"],"p":"The friendship of those who seem to be friends while they are not, will change like the love of women.","r":"The friendship of those who seem one's own but aren't will change the way women's minds change.","n":"The verse itself makes a general comparison about women, and the commentators' responses show how uneasy it is.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","natpu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Women's minds are changeable once there is an opportunity, and he supports it from another text.","இடம் பெற்றால் பெண்பாலார் மனம் போல வேறுபடும்."],["kalaignar","The comparison concerns only prostitutes, who pretend to the virtue that women have, so women in general are not the target.","நடிக்கும் விலை மகளிரின் மனம்போல உள்ளொன்றும் புறமொன்றுமாக"]],"ch":"k:83"},"k:823":{"book":"kural","num":823,"t":["பலநல்ல கற்றக் கடைத்து மனநல்லர்","ஆகுதல் மாணார்க் கரிது"],"p":"Though (one's) enemies may have mastered many good books, it will be impossible for them to become truly loving at heart.","r":"Though they have studied many good things, it is hard for the unworthy to become good at heart.","n":"The verse separates learning from moral change. This limit matters to a text that elsewhere praises study so highly.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["manam","kalvi","vanjam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","மாணார் are enemies, and learning won't make them friends, so don't trust them because they are learned.","அதனான் மனம் திருந்தி நட்பாதல் பகைவர்க்கு இல்லை"],["manakkudavar","மாணார் are simply those lacking excellence, and the point is about what learning can reveal.","மனநல்லாராகு தல் மாட்சிமையில்லார்க்கு அரிது"]],"ch":"k:83"},"k:824":{"book":"kural","num":824,"t":["முகத்தின் இனிய நகாஅ அகத்தின்னா","வஞ்சரை அஞ்சப் படும்"],"p":"One should fear the deceitful who smile sweetly with their face but never love with their heart.","r":"Fear the deceivers who smile sweetly with the face while within they are bitter.","n":"Parimelazhagar gives the reason for fear: the face is the instrument for reading intentions, and here the malice doesn't show on it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","kurippu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:83"},"k:825":{"book":"kural","num":825,"t":["மனத்தின் அமையா தவரை எனைத்தொன்றும்","சொல்லினால் தேறற்பாற்று அன்று"],"p":"In nothing whatever is it proper to rely on the words of those who do not love with their heart.","r":"Those not in accord with you in mind are not to be trusted on the strength of their words in anything.","n":"The verse states no authority, but Parimelazhagar supplies one: 'the treatises on policy' say so. He reads the line as counsel on whom to trust in practical affairs (கருமங்கள்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["manam","vanjam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:83"},"k:826":{"book":"kural","num":826,"t":["நட்டார்போல் நல்லவை சொல்லினும் ஒட்டார்சொல்","ஒல்லை உணரப் படும்"],"p":"Though (one's) foes may utter good things as though they were friends, once will at once understand (their evil, import).","r":"Though they say good things as friends do, the words of the unreconciled are quickly seen through.","n":"This verse is more optimistic than its neighbours about detection. Parimelazhagar reads the concession to mean they rarely say good things at all.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vanjam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:83"},"k:827":{"book":"kural","num":827,"t":["சொல்வணக்கம் ஒன்னார்கண் கொள்ளற்க வில்வணக்கம்","தீங்கு குறித்தமை யான்"],"p":"Since the bending of the bow bespeaks evil, one should not accept (as good) the humiliating speeches of one's foes.","r":"Do not take humility in an enemy's speech as humility, for the bending of a bow means harm.","n":"Parimelazhagar marks it as 'word-bowing' and not real bowing, since the man himself doesn't bow. The bend is what stores the force, so the humility may be the very preparation.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["vanjam","pagai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:83"},"k:828":{"book":"kural","num":828,"t":["தொழுதகை யுள்ளும் படையொடுங்கும் ஒன்னார்","அழுதகண் ணீரும் அனைத்து"],"p":"A weapon may be hid in the very hands with which (one's) foes adore (him) (and) the tears they shed are of the same nature.","r":"A weapon hides even inside hands folded in worship, and an enemy's shed tears are the same.","n":"The chapter moves from words to the body, and it picks the gestures hardest to doubt, reverence and grief. Parimelazhagar's line is that those able to read intent see the weapon even in the moment of reassurance.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["vanjam","pagai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:83"},"k:829":{"book":"kural","num":829,"t":["மிகச்செய்து தம்மெள்ளு வாரை நகச்செய்து","நட்பினுள் சாப்புல்லற் பாற்று"],"p":"It is the duty of kings to affect great love but make it die (inwardly); as regard those foes who shew them great friendship but despise them (in their heart).","r":"Those who overdo friendship and despise you inwardly should be made to smile, and embraced in friendship so that it dies.","n":"The verse itself gives no audience, but Parimelazhagar supplies 'royal policy' and says plainly that having one thing inside and another outside is wrong in general but proper toward enemies. He treats it as a licensed exception. சாப்புல்லல் is a hard phrase: this reading follows the commentators, and Pope's couplet ('slap') goes elsewhere.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","pagai","natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:83"},"k:830":{"book":"kural","num":830,"t":["பகைநட்பாம் காலம் வருங்கால் முகநட்டு","அகநட்பு ஒரீஇ விடல்"],"p":"When one's foes begin to affect friendship, one should love them with one's looks, and, cherishing no love in the heart, give up (even the former).","r":"When the time comes that an enemy turns friend, befriend with the face, let go in the heart, and then let it go altogether.","n":"Parimelazhagar narrows the permission to a moment when open rejection isn't possible, and stresses a final exit. So the mask is a temporary means, not a settled way of life.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pagai","natpu","vanjam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:83"},"k:831":{"book":"kural","num":831,"t":["பேதைமை என்பதொன்று யாதெனின் ஏதங்கொண்டு","ஊதியம் போக விடல்"],"p":"Folly is one (of the chief defects); it is that which (makes one) incur loss and forego gain.","r":"What is the one thing called folly? It is taking hold of harm and letting gain go.","n":"This is a definition by behaviour rather than by lack of knowledge. Parimelazhagar lists poverty, blame and sin as the harm and wealth, fame and virtue as the gain, so he reads the choice as ruining both worlds.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai","kedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:84"},"k:832":{"book":"kural","num":832,"t":["பேதைமையுள் எல்லாம் பேதைமை காதன்மை","கையல்ல தன்கட் செயல்"],"p":"The greatest folly is that which leads one to take delight in doing what is forbidden.","r":"Of all follies the worst is to set one's love on what is not for one's hand.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It means conduct forbidden by the treatises, loved and done anyway.","இருமைக்கும் ஆகாதென்று நூலோர் கடிந்த செயல்களை விரும்பிச் செய்தல் என்பதாம்."],["manakkudavar","It means something out of reach, loved even though effort won't get it.","தனக்குக் கைவாராத பொருளின்கண் காதன்மை செய்தல்"],["kalaignar","It means tasks beyond one's ability, and meddling in them.","தன்னால் இயலாத செயல்களை விரும்பி, அவற்றில் தலையிடுவது"]],"ch":"k:84"},"k:833":{"book":"kural","num":833,"t":["நாணாமை நாடாமை நாரின்மை யாதொன்றும்","பேணாமை பேதை தொழில்"],"p":"Shamelessness indifference (to what must be sought after), harshness, and aversion for everything (that ought to be desired) are the qualities of the fool.","r":"No shame, no searching, no tenderness, no care for anything: this is what a fool does.","n":"The fool is described by four absences, in a chain of words ending in -மை. தொழில் suggests something habitual: Parimelazhagar says these are his constant nature. நார் is a fibre, a bond, and also fellow-feeling.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai","naanam","panpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:84"},"k:834":{"book":"kural","num":834,"t":["ஓதி உணர்ந்தும் பிறர் க்குரைத்தும் தானடங்காப்","பேதையின் பேதையார் இல்"],"p":"There are no greater fools than he who, though he has read and understood (a great deal) and even taught it to others, does not walk according to his own teaching.","r":"There is no greater fool than one who studies, understands and teaches others but does not restrain himself.","n":"All three stages of learning are granted, and restraint alone is missing. Parimelazhagar makes it medical: this folly isn't cured by its own remedy, and no other remedy exists.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai","kalvi","adakkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:84"},"k:835":{"book":"kural","num":835,"t":["ஒருமைச் செயலாற்றும் பேதை எழுமையும்","தான்புக் கழுந்தும் அளறு"],"p":"A fool can procure in a single birth a hell into which he may enter and suffer through all the seven births.","r":"A fool can, in one life's doing, make the mire he will enter and sink into for seven.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai","piravi","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The mire is hell, and all future births are lived in hell's suffering because of one life's cruel deeds.","வரும் பிறவிகள் எல்லாம் தான் புக்கு அழுந்தும் நிரயத்தினை"],["kalaignar","ஒருமை is acting on one's own whim, and எழுமை simply means always. The mire is suffering with no births or hell involved.","எக் காலத்திலும் துன்பமெனும் சகதியில் அழுந்திக் கிடக்க நேரிடும்."]],"ch":"k:84"},"k:836":{"book":"kural","num":836,"t":["பொய்படும் ஒன்றோ புனைபூணும் கையறியாப்","பேதை வினைமேற் கொளின்"],"p":"If the fool, who knows not how to act undertakes a work, he will (certainly) fail. (But) is it all ? He will even adorn himself with fetters.","r":"If a fool who doesn't know how takes up a task, it comes to nothing, and more than that, he wears fetters.","n":"பூணும் is what one does with jewellery, so the fetters are worn like ornaments. Manakkudavar takes பொய்படும் to mean he himself gets called false, whereas Parimelazhagar means the work collapses inside.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:84"},"k:837":{"book":"kural","num":837,"t":["ஏதிலார் ஆரத் தமர்பசிப்பர் பேதை","பெருஞ்செல்வம் உற்றக் கடை"],"p":"If a fool happens to get an immense fortune, his neighbours will enjoy it while his relations starve.","r":"When a fool gets great wealth, strangers feast and his own people go hungry.","n":"The verse says உற்றக்கடை, 'when it has come to him'. Parimelazhagar takes this as wealth got by fate, on the argument that a fool couldn't have made it himself. The next verse's பெறின் gets the same treatment. Both are his additions.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pedhaimai","selvam","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:84"},"k:838":{"book":"kural","num":838,"t":["மையல் ஒருவன் களித்தற்றால் பேதைதன்","கையொன்று உடைமை பெறின்"],"p":"A fool happening to possess something is like the intoxication of one who is (already) giddy.","r":"When a fool gets something into his hand, it is like a man already deranged getting drunk.","n":"The image is of something added to an existing state. Wealth doesn't produce the folly but compounds it, and ஒன்று, 'some one thing', suggests it doesn't take much.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:84"},"k:839":{"book":"kural","num":839,"t":["பெரிதினிது பேதையார் கேண்மை பிரிவின்கண்","பீழை தருவதொன் றில்"],"p":"The friendship between fools is exceedingly delightful (to each other): for at parting there will be nothing to cause them pain.","r":"The friendship of fools is very sweet, for there is nothing painful about parting from them.","n":"பிரிவு is a word that carries great weight in the Book of Love, so the 'sweetness' measured by painless parting reads like the opposite of what love is.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai","natpu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It is mock praise, blame dressed as praise: the friendship wanes daily, so parting hurts no one.","புகழ்வார் போன்று பழித்தவாறு."],["manakkudavar","It concerns women's relations with foolish men, and how a fool enjoys desire.","இது பேதை காமந் துய்க்குமாறு கூறிற்று."],["kalaignar","He gives the literal sense, with no irony marked: it is sweet because parting brings no sorrow.","பிரியும்போது எந்தத் துன்பமும் ஏற்படுவதில்லை."]],"ch":"k:84"},"k:840":{"book":"kural","num":840,"t":["கழாஅக்கால் பள்ளியுள் வைத்தற்றால் சான்றோர்","குழாஅத்துப் பேதை புகல்"],"p":"The appearance of a fool in an assembly of the learned is like placing (one's) unwashed feet on a bed.","r":"A fool's entering an assembly of the worthy is like placing an unwashed foot on a bed.","n":"The harm passes to the place: the bed and the assembly are both defiled, not the foot or the fool. Parimelazhagar notes that 'unwashed' is a polite cover for what the foot has trodden, which Kalaignar spells out.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:84"},"k:841":{"book":"kural","num":841,"t":["அறிவின்மை இன்மையுள் இன்மை பிறிதின்மை","இன்மையா வையா துலகு"],"p":"The want of wisdom is the greatest of all wants; but that of wealth the world will not regard as such.","r":"Lack of understanding is the poverty of poverties; other lack the world does not count as lack.","n":"வையாது உலகு attributes the valuation to the world. Parimelazhagar's reason is that the wise keep this world's and the next world's fruit even in poverty, whereas the petty-minded don't even with wealth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pullarivu","arivu","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:85"},"k:842":{"book":"kural","num":842,"t":["அறிவிலான் நெஞ்சுவந்து ஈதல் பிறிதியாதும்","இல்லை பெறுவான் தவம்"],"p":"(The cause of) a fool cheerfully giving (something) is nothing else but the receiver's merit (in a former birth).","r":"When a person without understanding gives with a glad heart, the cause is nothing other than the receiver's merit.","n":"A strange and rather cold claim: a good act by such a person earns him nothing. Parimelazhagar raises and rebuts the objection that fools also do good. Kalaignar has simply good luck (பெற்றபேறு), which removes தவம் from the picture.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pullarivu","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:85"},"k:843":{"book":"kural","num":843,"t":["அறிவிலார் தாந்தம்மைப் பீழிக்கும் பீழை","செறுவார்க்கும் செய்தல் அரிது"],"p":"The suffering that fools inflict upon themselves is hardly possible even to foes.","r":"The suffering those without understanding do to themselves is hard even for their enemies to do.","n":"Parimelazhagar gives a clever reason: an enemy has to watch for a moment and knows only so much, whereas self-injury never pauses and reaches poverty, blame and sin all at once.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pullarivu","pagai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:85"},"k:844":{"book":"kural","num":844,"t":["வெண்மை எனப்படுவ தியாதெனின் ஒண்மை","உடையம்யாம் என்னும் செருக்கு"],"p":"What is called want of wisdom is the vanity which says, \"We are wise\".","r":"What is called வெண்மை? It is the pride that says 'we have brightness.'","n":"வெண்மை, whiteness, is rawness or unripeness. ஒண்மை is brightness. The two words sound alike and describe opposite things, and the fool can't tell whether he is lit or blank. Parimelazhagar calls it a delusion because such people persist knowing the world mocks them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pullarivu","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:85"},"k:845":{"book":"kural","num":845,"t":["கல்லாத மேற்கொண் டொழுகல் கசடற","வல்லதூஉம் ஐயம் தரும்"],"p":"Fools pretending to know what has not been read (by them) will rouse suspicion even as to what they have thoroughly mastered.","r":"Taking on what one hasn't learned brings doubt even on what one has mastered without flaw.","n":"The verse is about how credibility works. The pretence costs real knowledge too. This is not a moral claim but a practical one about reputation, and it grants that the pretender may have actual skill.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pullarivu","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:85"},"k:846":{"book":"kural","num":846,"t":["அற்றம் மறைத்தலோ புல்லறிவு தம்வயின்","குற்றம் மறையா வழி"],"p":"Even to cover one's nakedness would be folly, if (one's) faults were not covered (by forsaking them).","r":"Is covering one's nakedness not itself petty, when one's faults aren't covered?","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pullarivu","naanam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","To cover a fault means to get rid of it. Covering the lesser thing while leaving the greater and thinking oneself decent is the folly.","குற்றம் மறைத்தலாவது, அவற்றை இலவாக்குதல்."],["manakkudavar","To cover a fault means keeping it from others' notice, as clothing hides the body from view.","தம்பாலுள்ள குற்றத்தைப் பிறரறியாமல் தாம் மறையாத காலத் து"]],"ch":"k:85"},"k:847":{"book":"kural","num":847,"t":["அருமறை சோரும் அறிவிலான் செய்யும்","பெருமிறை தானே தனக்கு"],"p":"The fool who neglects precious counsel does, of his own accord, a great injury to himself.","r":"One without understanding who lets slip the precious மறை does great harm to himself by himself.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pullarivu","kedu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","மறை is rare teaching which the fool doesn't take in. He rejects the other reading explicitly, since leaking a secret would be carelessness and not this fault.","அது பேணாமை என்னும் பேதைமையாவதன்றிப் புல்லறிவாண்மையன்மை அறிக."],["manakkudavar","மறை is a closely held secret, and letting it slip means telling others.","சோரவிடுதல் - பிறர்க்குச் சொல்லுதல்."]],"ch":"k:85"},"k:848":{"book":"kural","num":848,"t":["ஏவவும் செய்கலான் தான்தேறான் அவ்வுயிர்","போஒம் அளவுமோர் நோய்"],"p":"The fool will not perform (his duties) even when advised nor ascertain them himself; such a soul is a burden (to the earth) till it departs (from the body).","r":"Not doing it when told, not seeing it himself, that life is a disease until it departs.","n":"The verse doesn't say for whom. Parimelazhagar says for the earth that bears him, Manakkudavar for the people of the world, and Kalaignar for himself. These are three very different diseases. The verse's two failures also cover both routes to knowledge, one's own and another's.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pullarivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:85"},"k:849":{"book":"kural","num":849,"t":["காணாதான் காட்டுவான் தான்காணான் காணாதான்","கண்டானாம் தான்கண்ட வாறு"],"p":"One who would teach a fool will (simply) betray his folly; and the fool would (still) think himself \"wise in his own conceit\".","r":"One who shows things to a person who doesn't see becomes himself unseeing, and the unseeing one goes on having seen what he saw.","n":"The compact wordplay on காண் is the content: the teacher fails and the pupil remains satisfied. Parimelazhagar says the teacher is blamed by the pupil and ends up the ignorant one, and concludes that good sense can't be instilled here by any means.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pullarivu","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:85"},"k:850":{"book":"kural","num":850,"t":["உலகத்தார் உண்டென்பது இல்லென்பான் வையத்து","அலகையா வைக்கப் படும்"],"p":"He who denies the existence of what the world believes in will be regarded as a demon on earth.","r":"One who says 'it isn't' to what the world says is will be set down on this earth as a demon.","n":"The verse names neither what's denied nor who 'the world' is, and everything turns on those blanks.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pullarivu","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","What is denied is God, rebirth, and the fruit of deeds, as affirmed by the eminent.","கடவுளும், மறுபிறப்பும், இருவினைப் பயனும் முதலாக அவர் உள என்பன பலவேனும்"],["manakkudavar","What is denied is what the many wise affirm, with no specific doctrine named.","உலகத்தில் அறிவுடையார் பலர் உண்டென்பதாகிய ஒரு பொருளை இல்லையென்று"],["kalaignar","What is denied is what's been shown clearly by evidence, and the denial is deliberate.","ஆதாரங்களைக் காட்டி இதுதான் உண்மை என்று தெளிவாகக் கூறப்படுகிற ஒன்றை"]],"ch":"k:85"},"k:851":{"book":"kural","num":851,"t":["இகலென்ப எல்லா உயிர்க்கும் பகலென்னும்","பண்பின்மை பாரிக்கும் நோய்"],"p":"The disease which fosters the evil of disunion among all creatures is termed hatred by the wise.","r":"They call hostility the disease that spreads, among all living beings, the ill nature of division.","n":"Hostility here is not an event but a disease that spreads a disposition. Parimelazhagar sees in எல்லா உயிர்க்கும் a judgement: quarrelling humans are being put with the animals.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["igal","panpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:86"},"k:852":{"book":"kural","num":852,"t":["பகல்கருதிப் பற்றா செயினும் இகல்கருதி","இன்னாசெய் யாமை தலை"],"p":"Though disagreeable things may be done from (a feeling of) disunion, it is far better that nothing painful be done from (that of) hatred.","r":"Even if someone does what you can't bear, intending division, it is best not to do harm in return, intending hostility.","n":"The reason Parimelazhagar gives is strategic rather than saintly. Retaliate and enmity grows and you sink, abstain and it dies down and you rise. This is non-retaliation argued in the Book of Wealth's terms.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["igal","pagai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:86"},"k:853":{"book":"kural","num":853,"t":["இகலென்னும் எவ்வநோய் நீக்கின் தவலில்லாத்","தாவில் விளக்கம் தரும்"],"p":"To rid one-self of the distressing dtsease of hatred will bestow (on one) a never-decreasing imperishable fame.","r":"If the painful disease called hostility is removed, it gives a light that doesn't diminish and has no flaw.","n":"Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar differ on விளக்கம்: fame for one, radiance for the other. His route to fame is a long chain. Everyone becomes a friend, then every wealth comes, then giving, then renown. The verse takes no such steps.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["igal","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:86"},"k:854":{"book":"kural","num":854,"t":["இன்பத்துள் இன்பம் பயக்கும் இகலென்னும்","துன்பத்துள் துன்பங் கெடின்"],"p":"If hatred which is the greatest misery is destroyed, it will yield the greatest delight.","r":"If the suffering of sufferings called hostility is destroyed, it brings the joy of joys.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["igal","inbam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The joy is worldly: with everyone a friend, one obtains every fruit.","யாவரும் நட்பாகலின் எல்லாப் பயனும் எய்தியுறுவது."],["manakkudavar","The joy is liberation, the highest of all joys.","எல்லா இன்பத்தின் மிக்க வீடுபேற்றின்பம் எய்தும் என்றவாறு."]],"ch":"k:86"},"k:855":{"book":"kural","num":855,"t":["இகலெதிர் சாய்ந்தொழுக வல்லாரை யாரே","மிக்லூக்கும் தன்மை யவர்"],"p":"Who indeed would think of conquering those who naturally shrink back from hatred ?","r":"Who would set out to defeat those who are able to lean away from hostility?","n":"வல்லாரை marks it as an ability, not a weakness. Manakkudavar faces the objection directly: wouldn't giving way be a defeat? The verse answers that no one can defeat them. Parimelazhagar adds it is particularly hard for kings.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["igal","pagai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:86"},"k:856":{"book":"kural","num":856,"t":["இகலின் மிகலினிது என்பவன் வாழ்க்கை","தவலும் கெடலும் நணித்து"],"p":"Failure and ruin are not far from him who says it is sweet to excel in hatred.","r":"The life of one who says it is sweet to excel in hostility is close to failure and to ruin.","n":"The problem is the relish: என்பவன் indicts the person who enjoys conflict and not one drawn into it. Kalaignar gives 'easy' (எளிது) for the verse's 'sweet' (இனிது), which changes the charge from pleasure to overconfidence.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["igal","kedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:86"},"k:857":{"book":"kural","num":857,"t":["மிகல்மேவல் மெய்ப்பொருள் காணார் இகல்மேவல்","இன்னா அறிவி னவர்"],"p":"Those whose judgement brings misery through its connection with hatred cannot understand the triumphant nature of truth.","r":"Those of bitter understanding who hold to hostility do not see the true thing that holds victory.","n":"Parimelazhagar's point: hostility itself clouds the mind, so that the very quarrel blinds one to how to win. A combative person is therefore bad at conflict.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["igal","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","மெய்ப்பொருள் here is the content of the treatises on policy, which brings success to those who follow them.","வெற்றி பொருந்துதலையுடைய நீதி நூற்பொருளை அறியமாட்டார்."],["manakkudavar","It is truth as such, which those in conflict can't perceive.","இது மாறுபடுவார்க்கு மெய்ப்பொருள் தோன்றாதென்றது."]],"ch":"k:86"},"k:858":{"book":"kural","num":858,"t":["இகலிற்கு எதிர்சாய்தல் ஆக்கம் அதனை","மிக்லூக்கின் ஊக்குமாம் கேடு"],"p":"Shrinking back from hatred will yield wealth; indulging in its increase will hasten ruin.","r":"Leaning away from hostility is gain; if one drives on to excel at it, ruin drives on to come.","n":"Ruin mirrors what one does: ஊக்கின் ஊக்கும். Parimelazhagar locates the turning point in one's own mind, at the moment when hostility arises and isn't taken up.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["igal","kedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:86"},"k:859":{"book":"kural","num":859,"t":["இகல்காணான் ஆக்கம் வருங்கால் அதனை","மிகல்காணும் கேடு தரற்கு"],"p":"At the approach of wealth one will not think of hatred (but) to secure one's ruin, one will look to its increase.","r":"When gain is coming one doesn't look at hostility, and to bring on ruin one looks to excel at it.","n":"This complicates 858. Hostility may not be the cause of ruin but the sign of it. Parimelazhagar says these are forerunners of gain and loss, and Manakkudavar links them to one's merit, which reduces how much the choice in 858 is really free.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["igal","kedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:86"},"k:860":{"book":"kural","num":860,"t":["இகலானாம் இன்னாத எல்லாம் நகலானாம்","நன்னயம் என்னும் செருக்கு"],"p":"All calamities are caused by hatred; but by the delight (of friendship) is caused the great wealth of good virtues.","r":"From hostility come all ills, and from friendly gladness comes the pride of good and fitting ways.","n":"செருக்கு is usually a fault in the Kural (844) and here it is praise. Parimelazhagar reads it as great wealth, and நன்னயம் as good policy. So the virtue is presented as a prosperous kind of wisdom.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["igal","natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:86"},"k:861":{"book":"kural","num":861,"t":["வலியார்க்கு மாறேற்றல் ஓம்புக ஓம்பா","மெலியார்மேல் மேக பகை"],"p":"Avoid offering resistance to the strong; (but) never fail to cherish enmity towards the weak.","r":"Avoid opposing the strong, and without holding back, favour enmity against the weak.","n":"Whatever this is, it is not a moral teaching, which is why the commentators' disagreement matters so much.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","vendhan"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It is a plain rule of statecraft: avoid the strong, and keep enmity with the weak, since beating them doesn't drain one's strength.","ஏனை மெலியார்க்குப் பகையாதலை ஒழியாது விரும்புக."],["manakkudavar","The weak in view are those who don't respect one, which narrows the rule.","தம்மைப் போற்றாத எளியார்மாட்டுப் பகைகோடலை மேவுக"],["kalaignar","The opposite: true excellence in enmity is leaving the weak alone and taking on the strong.","மெலியோரை விடுத்து, வலியோரை எதிர்த்துப் போரிட விரும்புவதே"]],"ch":"k:87"},"k:862":{"book":"kural","num":862,"t":["அன்பிலன் ஆன்ற துணையிலன் தான்துவ்வான்","என்பரியும் ஏதிலான் துப்பு"],"p":"How can he who is unloving, destitute of powerful aids, and himself without strength overcome the might of his foe ?","r":"With no love, no worthy allies and no strength of his own, how can he wear down an enemy's might?","n":"அன்பு appears in a list of military assets. For Parimelazhagar it is love of one's kin, so without it there's no kin, and for an attacker such a person only adds strength.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pagai","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:87"},"k:863":{"book":"kural","num":863,"t":["அஞ்சும் அறியான் அமைவிலன் ஈகலான்","தஞ்சம் எளியன் பகைக்கு"],"p":"In the estimation of foes miserably weak is he, who is timid, ignorant, unsociable and niggardly.","r":"He fears, doesn't know, doesn't fit in with others, doesn't give: he is utterly easy for an enemy.","n":"The list includes not giving, which is an ethical failing elsewhere and here is a military one. Kalaignar's 'compassion' softens the word. Parimelazhagar says அஞ்சும் means fearing what needn't be feared, the mirror of 428.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:87"},"k:864":{"book":"kural","num":864,"t":["நீங்கான் வெகுளி நிறையிலன் எஞ்ஞான்றும்","யாங்கணும் யார்க்கும் எளிது"],"p":"He who neither refrains from anger nor keeps his secrets will at all times and in all places be easily conquered by all.","r":"Not free from rage and without நிறை: at any time, in any place, he is easy for anyone.","n":"The commentators differ on நிறை. Parimelazhagar means keeping secrets and Kalaignar means self-control. Parimelazhagar's logic is neat: the enemy no longer needs to watch for the right time, place or strength because the angry man's leaked plans give it all away.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:87"},"k:865":{"book":"kural","num":865,"t":["வழிநோக்கான் வாய்ப்பன செய்யான் பழிநோக்கான்","பண்பிலன் பற்றார்க்கு இனிது"],"p":"(A) pleasing (object) to his foes is he who reads not moral works, does nothing that is enjoined by them cares not for reproach and is not possessed of good qualities.","r":"He doesn't look to the வழி, doesn't do what is suitable, doesn't look to blame, has no character: he is a pleasure to his enemies.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","panpu","pazhi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","வழி is the treatise on policy, handed down from the ancients, which he doesn't study.","ஒருவன் நீதிநூலை ஓதான்"],["manakkudavar","வழி is the consequence of an action, which he doesn't consider when he starts it.","ஒருவினை செய்யத் தொடங்குங்கால் பின் வருவன பாரான்"]],"ch":"k:87"},"k:866":{"book":"kural","num":866,"t":["காணாச் சினத்தான் கழிபெருங் காமத்தான்","பேணாமை பேணப் படும்"],"p":"Highly to be desired is the hatred of him whose anger is blind, and whose lust increases beyond measure.","r":"One of blinding anger and ever-growing lust: his not caring for you is something to care for.","n":"பேணாமை பேணப்படும் is the wordplay and the whole point. Such a person's hostility is an asset, since he will destroy himself. Parimelazhagar says all become his enemies through the anger, and in the lust he loses sight of his affairs. Kalaignar turns காமம் into greed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:87"},"k:867":{"book":"kural","num":867,"t":["கொடுத்தும் கொளல்வேண்டும் மன்ற அடுத்திருந்து","மாணாத செய்வான் பகை"],"p":"It is indeed necessary to obtain even by purchase the hatred of him who having begun (a work) does what is not conductive (to its accomplishment).","r":"One who stays close and does what is unbecoming: it is truly necessary to get his enmity, even by giving something.","n":"Even the enmity has a price worth paying. The verse's hardest word is அடுத்திருந்து, and the commentators each supply a different object.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","He is a ruler who has begun a task and does what doesn't fit it, so losing a little to fight him pays many times over.","வினையைத் தொடங்கியிருந்து அதற்கு ஏலாதன செய்வான் பகைமையை"],["manakkudavar","He has taken up enmity and does what doesn't serve it, so he will not actually hurt you.","இஃது இவன் பகைசெய்யமாட்டானாதலால், அப்பகை கோடலாமென்றது."],["kalaignar","He is someone staying at your side and doing wrong, and you must pay to make him an open enemy.","தன்னோடு இருந்துகொண்டே தனக்குப் பொருந்தாத காரியங்களைச் செய்து கொண்டிருப்பவனைப்"]],"ch":"k:87"},"k:868":{"book":"kural","num":868,"t":["குணனிலனாய்க் குற்றம் பலவாயின் மாற்றார்க்கு","இனனிலனாம் ஏமாப் புடைத்து"],"p":"He will become friendless who is without (any good) qualities. and whose faults are many; (such a character) is a help to (his) foes.","r":"With no qualities and many faults he has no one around him, and this is a protection to his enemies.","n":"ஏமாப்பு, the word for protection, has been turned around. A man's faults become his enemies' shield. Parimelazhagar lists what deserts him: kin, friends, wealth, army.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","panpu","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:87"},"k:869":{"book":"kural","num":869,"t":["செறுவார்க்குச் சேணிகவா இன்பம் அறிவிலா","அஞ்சும் பகைவர்ப் பெறின்"],"p":"There will be no end of lofty delights to the victorious, if their foes are (both) ignorant and timid.","r":"Joy doesn't go far from those fighting, if they get enemies who are unknowing and afraid.","n":"பெறின் sounds like luck. Parimelazhagar says that's because foes with neither knowledge nor resolve are rare.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","arivu","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:87"},"k:870":{"book":"kural","num":870,"t":["கல்லான் வெகுளும் சிறுபொருள் எஞ்ஞான்றும்","ஒல்லானை ஒல்லா தொளி"],"p":"The light (of fame) will never be gained by him who gains not the trifling reputation of having fought an unlearned (foe).","r":"Fame will never come to one who cannot manage the small thing of being angry at the unlearned.","n":"The syntax is hard and the commentaries don't converge. The reading given follows Parimelazhagar, who says others have not kept to the chapter's subject.","x":1,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pagai","pugazh","kalvi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Fighting the untaught is cheap in effort, and one who can't manage even that won't win fame.","நீதி அறியாதானை வேறல் எளிதாயிருக்கவும், அது மாட்டாதானை வெற்றியான் வரும் புகழ் கூடாது என்பதாம்"],["manakkudavar","The words describe one enemy who is unlearned, angry and poor, and it is he whom light never reaches.","கல்லாதானுமாய், வெகுளியுடையனுமாய், சிறு பொருளனுமாகிய பகைவனை எல்லா நாளும் ஒளி பொருந்தாது"]],"ch":"k:87"},"k:871":{"book":"kural","num":871,"t":["பகைஎன்னும் பண்பி லதனை ஒருவன்","நகையேயும் வேண்டற்பாற்று அன்று"],"p":"The evil of hatred is not of a nature to be desired by one even in sport.","r":"The thing called enmity, which has no good in it, shouldn't be wanted by anyone, even in jest.","n":"Parimelazhagar gives a psychological reason for நகையேயும்: desired in play, the resentment grows and it becomes real. The verse sits at odds with the chapter that comes just before it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pagai","panpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:88"},"k:872":{"book":"kural","num":872,"t":["வில்லேர் உழவர் பகைகொளினும் கொள்ளற்க","சொல்லேர் உழவர் பகை"],"p":"Though you may incur the hatred of warriors whose ploughs are bows, incur not that of ministers whose ploughs are words.","r":"Though you take on the enmity of ploughmen whose plough is the bow, do not take on that of ploughmen whose plough is the word.","n":"A famous line, often read now as about writers and poets, but the older commentators don't read it that way. Parimelazhagar means those with counsel and policy, whose harm reaches one's descendants, and Manakkudavar means ministers. Kalaignar's 'learned' is the broadest. Both groups are 'ploughmen', borrowing the honour chapter 104 gives to that work.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:88"},"k:873":{"book":"kural","num":873,"t":["ஏமுற் றவரினும் ஏழை தமியனாய்ப்","பல்லார் பகைகொள் பவன்"],"p":"He who being alone, incurs the hatred of many is more infatuated than even mad men.","r":"One who, being alone, takes on the enmity of many is more wretched than those who are ஏமுற்றவர்.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","pedhaimai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","ஏமுற்றவர் are the mad, who at least aren't harmed by their delusion, while this one is.","மயக்கத்தால் ஒப்பாராயினும் ஏமுற்றவர் அதனால் தீங்கு எய்தாமையின்"],["kalaignar","ஏமுற்றவர் are the arrogant, and this person is better called a fool than arrogant.","ஆணவம் பிடித்தவன் என்பதைவிட அறிவிலி என்பதே பொருத்தமாகும்."]],"ch":"k:88"},"k:874":{"book":"kural","num":874,"t":["பகைநட்பாக் கொண்டொழுகும் பண்புடை யாளன்","தகைமைக்கண் தங்கிற்று உலகு"],"p":"The world abides in the greatness of that good-natured man who behaves so as to turn hatred into friendship.","r":"The world rests in the greatness of a person of character who conducts himself taking enmity as friendship.","n":"நட்பாக் கொண்டு is ambiguous: making them friends, or treating them as friends? The whole verse turns on it.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","natpu","panpu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","A king who, when it is needed, wins enemies over, and whose power therefore holds the world.","வேண்டியவழிப் பகையை வேறுபடுத்துத் தனக்கு நட்பாகச் செய்து கொண்டொழுகும் இயல்பினையுடைய அரசனது"],["manakkudavar","One able to behave toward enemies as if they were friends.","பகைவரை நட்புபோலக் கொண்டொழுக வல்ல பண்புடையவன்"],["kalaignar","It is a moral nobility in anyone who thinks of enemies as friends, and the world praises it.","பகைவர்களையும் நண்பர்களாகக் கருதிப் பழகுகின்ற பெருந்தன்மையான பண்பை"]],"ch":"k:88"},"k:875":{"book":"kural","num":875,"t":["தன்துணை இன்றால் பகையிரண்டால் தான்ஒருவன்","இன்துணையாக் கொள்கவற்றின் ஒன்று"],"p":"He who is alone and helpless while his foes are two should secure one of them as an agreeable help (to himself).","r":"If you have no ally and two enemies, being alone, take one of them as a pleasing ally.","n":"The word is இன்துணை, a sweet ally, which is warm for what it describes. Parimelazhagar adds 'for that time', and picks the one suited to defeating the other. The temporariness is his addition, though it's probably correct as politics.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pagai","natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:88"},"k:876":{"book":"kural","num":876,"t":["தேறினும் தேறா விடினும் அழிவின்கண்","தேறான் பகாஅன் விடல்"],"p":"Though (one's foe is) aware or not of one's misfortune one should act so as neither to join nor separate (from him).","r":"Whether or not you have trusted him, when ruin comes neither join with him nor break from him; let him be.","n":"A rule for neutrality. Parimelazhagar explains the two halves separately: don't join, because he could harm from inside; don't break, because he could help your ruin along. In adversity an enemy is best left unprovoked.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","kedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:88"},"k:877":{"book":"kural","num":877,"t":["நோவற்க நொந்தது அறியார்க்கு மேவற்க","மென்மை பகைவர் அகத்து"],"p":"Relate not your suffering even to friends who are ignorant of it, nor refer to your weakness in the presence of your foes.","r":"Don't complain to those who don't know of your pain, and don't show softness in front of enemies.","n":"The pairing is the surprise. Parimelazhagar takes those who don't know to be friends who haven't noticed on their own, which is itself a judgement on them. Pain should be read and not announced, which ties this to the chapters on signs.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:88"},"k:878":{"book":"kural","num":878,"t":["வகையறிந்து தற்செய்து தற்காப்ப மாயும்","பகைவர்கண் பட்ட செருக்கு"],"p":"The joy of one's foes will be destroyed if one guards oneself by knowing the way (of acting) and securing assistance.","r":"Knowing the way, building oneself up and guarding oneself will end the swagger on the enemy's side.","n":"All three verbs are about oneself and none about the enemy, yet the enemy is what changes. Parimelazhagar's enemy is celebrating an expected win. Manakkudavar calls this 'the way to kill enemies', which shows how much he thinks self-strengthening does.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:88"},"k:879":{"book":"kural","num":879,"t":["இளைதாக முள்மரம் கொல்க களையுநர்","கைகொல்லும் காழ்த்த இடத்து"],"p":"A thorny tree should be felled while young, (for) when it is grown it will destroy the hand of the feller.","r":"Cut a thorn tree while it is young; grown hard, it will destroy the hand that cuts it.","n":"The verse itself doesn't mention enemies, and Parimelazhagar labels it allegory. Its logic of acting before a threat matures has no room in it for what a sapling might have become, or for whether it is really a thorn.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:88"},"k:880":{"book":"kural","num":880,"t":["உயிர்ப்ப உளரல்லர் மன்ற செயிர்ப்பவர்","செம்மல் சிதைக்கலா தார்"],"p":"Those who do not destroy the pride of those who hate (them) will certainly not exist even to breathe.","r":"Those who don't break the pride of those hostile to them are, surely, not alive even to breathe.","n":"Parimelazhagar adds that this is about one who could do it and neglects it out of contempt.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","saakkaadu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Their enemies will grow and destroy them, so they won't live even long enough to draw breath. He allows that it may be the enemy's breath that kills.","அவர் உயிர்த்த துணையானே தாம் இறப்பர் எனினும் அமையும்."],["manakkudavar","It is the enemy's breath that kills, and the lesson is not to take on the strong as enemies at all.","இது பகை கொள்ளுங்கால் வலியாரோடு பகைகோடலாகா தென்றது."],["kalaignar","They can't be said to live merely because they are breathing.","சுவாசிக்கிற காரணத்தினாலேயே, உயிரோடிருப்பதாக நிச்சயமாகச் சொல்ல முடியாது."]],"ch":"k:88"},"k:881":{"book":"kural","num":881,"t":["நிழல்நீரும் இன்னாத இன்னா தமர்நீரும்","இன்னாவாம் இன்னா செயின்"],"p":"Shade and water are not pleasant, (if) they cause disease; so are the qualities of (one's) relations not agreeable, (if) they cause pain.","r":"Even shade and water are bad if they do harm, and the நீர் of one's own people too is bad, if it does harm.","n":"நீர் does double work, as water and as nature: தமர்நீர் is the nature of kin, sitting right next to actual water. Parimelazhagar's kin are specifically the relatives who could become inner enemies, and his illnesses from bad water are quite concrete. Manakkudavar reads நிழல்நீர் as one thing, the water in the shade.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["utpagai","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:89"},"k:882":{"book":"kural","num":882,"t":["வாள்போல பகைவரை அஞ்சற்க அஞ்சுக","கேள்போல் பகைவர் தொடர்பு"],"p":"Fear not foes (who say they would cut) like a sword; (but) fear the friendship of foes (who seemingly act) like relations.","r":"Don't fear enemies who are like swords, but fear the bond with enemies who are like kin.","n":"The verse says to fear the தொடர்பு and not the person. Parimelazhagar explains that the danger already lies in the existing bond, the one that has been made, and that open foes can be guarded against because they are known.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["utpagai","pagai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:89"},"k:883":{"book":"kural","num":883,"t":["உட்பகை அஞ்சித்தற் காக்க உலைவிடத்து","மட்பகையின் மாணத் தெறும்"],"p":"Fear internal enmity and guard yourself; (if not) it will destroy (you) in an evil hour, as surely as the tool which cuts the potter's clay.","r":"Fear the enemy within and guard yourself; when you falter, it will cut surely, like the potter's cutter.","n":"A மட்பகை is what the potter uses to take a pot off the wheel. Parimelazhagar's and Manakkudavar's point is that the cut is clean and unnoticed. உலைவிடத்து adds patience, as this enemy doesn't need to attack until you are already down.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["utpagai","pagai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:89"},"k:884":{"book":"kural","num":884,"t":["மனமாணா உட்பகை தோன்றின் இனமாணா","ஏதம் பலவும் தரும்"],"p":"The secret enmity of a person whose mind in unreformed will lead to many evils causing disaffection among (one's) relations.","r":"If an inner enmity of unreformed mind appears, it brings many ills of a circle that has gone bad.","n":"The damage is secondary: it spoils the other relationships. Parimelazhagar's analysis is that the enemy works from within to turn others, the king's trust then fails too, and the whole thing compounds. Kalaignar places the mind that won't reform in the person himself, not in another.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["utpagai","sutram","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:89"},"k:885":{"book":"kural","num":885,"t":["உறல்முறையான் உட்பகை தோன்றின் இறல்முறையான்","ஏதம் பலவும் தரும்"],"p":"If there appears internal hatred in a (king's) family; it will lead to many a fatal crime.","r":"If an inner enmity appears that has the form of kinship, it brings many ills that have the form of death.","n":"The verse's two lines balance on முறையான், so the closer the tie, the closer to death.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["utpagai","sutram","saakkaadu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It is about a king: an outwardly related enemy who may kill him, aid outside foes, and erode his ministers.","புறப்பகைத் துணையாய் நின்றே அது தோன்றாமல் கோறல் முதலிய வஞ்சனை செய்தலும்"],["kalaignar","It is about enmity among close relatives in general, who all then suffer.","நெருங்கிய உறவினருக்கிடையே தோன்றும் உட்பகையானது"]],"ch":"k:89"},"k:886":{"book":"kural","num":886,"t":["ஒன்றாமை ஒன்றியார் கட்படின் எஞ்ஞான்றும்","பொன்றாமை ஒன்றல் அரிது"],"p":"If hatred arises among (one's) own people, it will be hardly possible (for one) to escape death.","r":"If not-being-one arises among those who are one, it is ever hard to reach not-dying.","n":"The wordplay runs through three lines: ஒன்றாமை, ஒன்றியார், ஒன்றல். Oneness is what is broken and also what is needed. Parimelazhagar's எஞ்ஞான்றும் means even when one is at the peak of power.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["utpagai","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:89"},"k:887":{"book":"kural","num":887,"t":["செப்பின் புணர்ச்சிபோல் கூடினும் கூடாதே","உட்பகை உற்ற குடி"],"p":"Never indeed will a family subject to internal hatred unite (really) though it may present an apparent union like that of a casket and its lid.","r":"Though joined like a casket and its lid, a family that has inner enmity is not joined.","n":"The image shows a perfect fit with a seam all the same. The lid and the box remain two objects.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["utpagai","kudi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The members look united but are not, and will split when an outside enemy comes. He notes other readings with the enmity itself as what doesn't join.","உட்பகையான் மனம் வேறுபட்டமையின், புறப்பகை பெற்றுழி வீற்றுவீற்றாவர் என்பதாம்."],["manakkudavar","The enmity and the family are what fit together, and the lid moves aside for the one who comes to take what's inside, as the insider assists the thief.","செப்பகத் துப் பொருள் வாங்குவார்க்கு மூடி துணையாய் அகன்று நிற்குமதுபோல அகன்று நிற்பரென்றவாறு."]],"ch":"k:89"},"k:888":{"book":"kural","num":888,"t":["அரம்பொருத பொன்போலத் தேயும் உரம்பொருது","உட்பகை உற்ற குடி"],"p":"A family subject to internal hatred will wear out and lose its strength like iron that has been filed away.","r":"As பொன் that is rubbed by a file wears away, a family with inner enmity wears down its strength by rubbing against itself.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["utpagai","kudi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","பொன் is iron, the hard metal worn down by a file, and the insider seems to help while slowly separating people.","அரத்தாற் பொரப்பட்ட இரும்பு போல அதனால் பொரப்பட்டு வலி தேயும்."],["manakkudavar","பொன் is gold, which is polished as it is filed. So inner enmity seems to beautify while it wastes strength.","இஃது உட்பகை அழகு செய்வது போலப் பலத்தைக் கெடுக்கு மென்றது."]],"ch":"k:89"},"k:889":{"book":"kural","num":889,"t":["எட்பக வன்ன சிறுமைத்தே ஆயினும்","உட்பகை உள்ளதாங் கேடு"],"p":"Although internal hatred be as small as the fragment of the sesamum (seed), still does destruction dwell in it.","r":"Though only as small as a split sesame seed, inner enmity has ruin within it.","n":"உள்ளதாம் lets the word play with உட்பகை: the ruin is inside what is inside. Parimelazhagar notes the smallness is relative to a king's greatness, and that the large ruin stays contained until its hour comes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["utpagai","kedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:89"},"k:890":{"book":"kural","num":890,"t":["உடம்பாடு இலாதவர் வாழ்க்கை குடங்கருள்","பாம்போடு உடனுறைந் தற்று"],"p":"Living with those who do not agree (with one) is like dwelling with a cobra (in the same) hut.","r":"Living with those not in accord with you is like living with a snake inside a hut.","n":"Pope's 'domestic life' narrows what the verse leaves open. It says only 'those not in accord', which could be anyone one lives with. Parimelazhagar's point is about smallness and familiarity: in a confined space with long proximity, the bite is a certainty.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["utpagai","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:89"},"k:891":{"book":"kural","num":891,"t":["ஆற்றுவார் ஆற்றல் இகழாமை போற்றுவார்","போற்றலுள் எல்லாம் தலை"],"p":"Not to disregard the power of those who can carry out (their wishes) is more important than all the watchfulness of those who guard (themselves against evil).","r":"Not slighting the power of those able to act is the highest of all safeguards for those who guard themselves.","n":"Parimelazhagar makes a large claim from this: fort, army, wealth and friends can all be undone by such people, so this safeguard outranks them. It closes a stretch of chapters that were all about those other safeguards.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","aran"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:90"},"k:892":{"book":"kural","num":892,"t":["பெரியாரைப் பேணாது ஒழுகிற் பெரியாரால்","பேரா இடும்பை தரும்"],"p":"To behave without respect for the great (rulers) will make them do (us) irremediable evils.","r":"Going on without regard for the great will, through the great, bring ills that do not leave.","n":"The grammar makes the conduct itself the agent, and the great are only the instrument. Parimelazhagar says this was meant to show people do it to themselves. He has the king as the one at fault, whereas Manakkudavar has a person failing to honour sages.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","kedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:90"},"k:893":{"book":"kural","num":893,"t":["கெடல்வேண்டின் கேளாது செய்க அடல்வேண்டின்","ஆற்று பவர்கண் இழுக்கு"],"p":"If a person desires ruin, let him not listen to the righteous dictates of law, but commit crimes against those who are able to slay (other sovereigns).","r":"If you want to be ruined, don't listen, and commit a fault against those able to destroy.","n":"A piece of grim irony, phrased as an instruction. The commentators differ on what isn't listened to: Parimelazhagar means the treatises on policy, and Manakkudavar means the great themselves. His Purananuru citation makes such kings swifter than Death, who at least waits for the time.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","kedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:90"},"k:894":{"book":"kural","num":894,"t":["கூற்றத்தைக் கையால் விளித்தற்றால் ஆற்றுவார்க்கு","ஆற்றாதார் இன்னா செயல்"],"p":"The weak doing evil to the strong is like beckoning Yama to come (and destroy them).","r":"For those who can't do harm to those who can is like beckoning Death with the hand.","n":"The word is விளி, to call, so the gesture does it. Parimelazhagar adds that Death would have come anyway, so the action only brings it sooner, and that the hand gesture itself carries a scornful tone.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:90"},"k:895":{"book":"kural","num":895,"t":["யாண்டுச்சென் றியாண்டு முளராகார் வெந்துப்பின்","வேந்து செறப்பட் டவர்"],"p":"Those who have incurred the wrath of a cruel and mighty potentate will not prosper wherever they may go.","r":"Wherever they go, they won't exist anywhere, those who've drawn the anger of a king of fierce might.","n":"Parimelazhagar is shrewdly political: other kings won't shelter the fugitive and may kill him to win favour or his goods. The verse's point is not the king's reach but what everyone around him will do because of it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","perumai","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:90"},"k:896":{"book":"kural","num":896,"t":["எரியால் சுடப்படினும் உய்வுண்டாம் உய்யார்","பெரியார்ப் பிழைத்தொழுகு வார்"],"p":"Though burnt by a fire (from a forest), one may perhaps live; (but) never will he live who has shown disrespect to the great (devotees).","r":"Even if burned by fire, there can be survival; those who go on offending the great will not survive.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","aran"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The great are ascetics, whose wrath strikes at the life itself in an instant where fire only reaches the body first.","தவத்தாற் பெரியாரைப் பிழைத்து ஒழுகுவார், எவ்வாற்றானும் உயிருய்யார்."],["manakkudavar","The great are sages, with the same conclusion.","இது முனிவரைப் பிழைத்தலினால் வருங்குற்றம் கூறிற்று."],["kalaignar","The great are simply those mighty in capacity, with no asceticism involved.","ஆற்றல் மிகுந்த பெரியோரிடம் தவறிழைப்போர் தப்பிப் பிழைப்பது முடியாது."]],"ch":"k:90"},"k:897":{"book":"kural","num":897,"t":["வகைமாண்ட வாழ்க்கையும் வான்பொருளும் என்னாம்","தகைமாண்ட தக்கார் செறின்"],"p":"If a king incurs the wrath of the righteous great, what will become of his government with its splendid auxiliaries and (all) its untold wealth ?","r":"What are a fully appointed life and lofty wealth, if worthy ones of great stature are angered?","n":"Parimelazhagar says தகை means greatness that can curse and bless. Note his remark that செறின் implies they usually don't get angry, which holds for the verses after this. The verse's question doesn't state a mechanism, while he describes royal wealth burning up in a moment.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:90"},"k:898":{"book":"kural","num":898,"t":["குன்றன்னார் குன்ற மதிப்பின் குடியொடு","நின்றன்னார் மாய்வர் நிலத்து"],"p":"If (the) hill-like (devotees) resolve on destruction, those who seemed to be everlasting will be destroyed root and branch from the earth.","r":"If hill-like people are held low or themselves turn to bringing low, those who seemed to stand forever will vanish from earth with their line.","n":"நின்றன்னார், those who 'seem to stand', and குன்றன்னார், those who 'are like hills': the verse compares two kinds of standing. குன்ற is also a verbal pun on the hill.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","kudi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","குன்ற மதித்தல் is what the hill-like ascetics do, resolving on someone's ruin, and the rich die with their kin at once.","குன்றத்தை ஒக்கும் அருந்தவர் கெட நினைப்பாராயின்"],["manakkudavar","It is done to them: someone thinks little of those who are great as hills.","குன்ற மதித்தல் - அவமதித்தல்."]],"ch":"k:90"},"k:899":{"book":"kural","num":899,"t":["ஏந்திய கொள்கையார் சீறின் இடைமுரிந்து","வேந்தனும் வேந்து கெடும்"],"p":"If those of exalted vows burst in a rage, even (Indra) the king will suffer a sudden loss and be entirely ruined.","r":"If those of exalted resolve flare up, even the வேந்தன் breaks midway and loses his kingship.","n":"ஏந்திய கொள்கை and வேந்தன் carry all the weight in this verse, and the disagreement could hardly be wider, from gods and vows to politics.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["perumai","vendhan"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The வேந்தன் is Indra, himself toppled by ascetics of great vows, with Nahusha's fall from Indra's seat through Agastya's anger in mind.","அவராற்றலான் இந்திரனும் இடைய தன் பதம் இழந்து கெடும்."],["manakkudavar","It is also Indra, who loses his realm, and this is a loss of wealth.","உயர்ந்த கோட்பாட்டையுடையார் வெகுள்வராயின், இந்திரனும் இடையிலே இற்றுத் தன்னரசு இழக்கும்"],["kalaignar","Those with firm principles rise up and a repressive regime collapses, with no god or vow in it.","அடக்குமுறை ஆட்சி நிலை குலைந்து அழிந்துவிடும்."]],"ch":"k:90"},"k:900":{"book":"kural","num":900,"t":["இறந்தமைந்த சார்புடையர் ஆயினும் உய்யார்","சிறந்தமைந்த சீரார் செறின்"],"p":"Though in possession of numerous auxiliaries, they will perish who are-exposed to the wrath of the noble whose penance is boundless.","r":"Even those with overwhelming support will not survive if those of supreme distinction are angered.","n":"சார்பு gets a precise list from Parimelazhagar: fort, army, wealth, friends, which are the chapters just passed through. His example is the burning of the three cities. The verse's own சிறந்தமைந்த சீரார் doesn't specify asceticism or any cause.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","aran"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:90"},"k:901":{"book":"kural","num":901,"t":["மனைவிழைவார் மாண்பயன் எய்தார் வினைவிழையார்","வேண்டாப் பொருளும் அது"],"p":"Those who lust after their wives will not attain the excellence of virtue; and it is just this that is not desired by those who are bent on acquiring wealth.","r":"Men who fix their desire on the wife do not reach the fine fruit of life, and this same desire is what men intent on action refuse to want.","n":"The verse says only மனை, 'home', and மாண்பயன், 'fine fruit', without naming either. Parimelazhagar supplies virtue as the fruit and wealth-making as the action, so the claim that the household's own goal is lost is partly his construction.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["penvazhi","kaamam","aram","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:91"},"k:902":{"book":"kural","num":902,"t":["பேணாது பெண்விழைவான் ஆக்கம் பெரியதோர்","நாணாக நாணுத் தரும்"],"p":"The wealth of him who, regardless (of his manliness), devotes himself to his wife's feminine nature will cause great shame (to ali men) and to himself;","r":"The prosperity of one who desires his woman without caring for something else brings him shame, and a great shame in the world besides.","n":"பேணாது has no object in the verse, so what is being neglected is exactly what the commentators must decide.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["penvazhi","naanam","selvam","aanmai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","What he abandons is his manliness, and his wealth shames all men because she controls its use.","தன் ஆண்மையை விட்டு மனையாளது பெண்மையை விழைவான்"],["manakkudavar","What he fails to tend is virtue and wealth, with no appeal to manliness.","அறத்தினையும் பொருளினையும் பேணாது மனையாளைக் காதலிப் பானது செல்வம்"],["kalaignar","What he neglects is the principle he adopted, as he trails after a woman.","ஏற்றுக்கொண்ட கொள்கையினைப் பேணிக் காத்திடாமல்"]],"ch":"k:91"},"k:903":{"book":"kural","num":903,"t":["இல்லாள்கண் தாழ்ந்த இயல்பின்மை எஞ்ஞான்றும்","நல்லாருள் நாணுத் தரும்"],"p":"The frailty that stoops to a wife will always make (her husband) feel ashamed among the good.","r":"The unnatural condition of stooping before one's wife brings a man shame among good people at all times.","n":"The shame is social and comparative. It arises among நல்லார், not in the house.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["penvazhi","naanam","aanmai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The fault is a husband's fear of any wife: fear is natural to her, so in him it is unnatural and leaves her uncontrolled.","அவள் தான் அஞ்சி ஒழுகுதல் இயல்பாகலின், அவளை அஞ்சுதல் இயல்பின்மையாயிற்று."],["kalaignar","The fault is yielding to a wife of bad character instead of trying to correct her.","நற்குணமில்லாத மனைவியைத் திருத்த முனையாமல் பணிந்து போகிற கணவன்"]],"ch":"k:91"},"k:904":{"book":"kural","num":904,"t":["மனையாளை அஞ்சும் மறுமையி லாளன்","வினையாண்மை வீறெய்த லின்று"],"p":"The undertaking of one, who fears his wife and is therefore destitute of (bliss), will never be applauded.","r":"A man who fears his wife and has no share in the other life gets no distinction from his capacity for action.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["penvazhi","vinai","aanmai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","மறுமை is the next world, whose fruit he forfeits because he cannot perform household virtue.","தன் மனையாளை அஞ்சி ஒழுகுகின்ற மறுமைப்பயன் இல்லாதானுக்கு"],["kalaignar","The man fears running a family and misses the fruit of the new life that marriage opens; no next world is involved.","மணம் புரிந்து புதுவாழ்வின் பயனை அடையாமல் குடும்பம் நடத்த அஞ்சுகின்றவனின்"]],"ch":"k:91"},"k:905":{"book":"kural","num":905,"t":["இல்லாளை அஞ்சுவான் அஞ்சுமற் றெஞ்ஞான்றும்","நல்லார்க்கு நல்ல செயல்"],"p":"He that fears his wife will always be afraid of doing good deeds (even) to the good.","r":"One who fears his wife will always be afraid to do good to good people.","n":"The harm named is the loss of generosity to outsiders, so fear in the house is treated as a public failure of giving.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["penvazhi","aram"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","He fears giving to guests, ascetics and elders, even out of wealth he earned himself.","தான் தேடிய பொருளே யாயினும் அதனால் நல்லார்க்கு நல்லன செய்தலை"],["kalaignar","He reverses the sense: a man anxious to do good without error heeds the wife who watches for error.","தவறு நேராமல் கண்காணிக்கும் மனைவிக்கு அஞ்சி நடப்பான்."]],"ch":"k:91"},"k:906":{"book":"kural","num":906,"t":["இமையாரின் வாழினும் பாடிலரே இல்லாள்","அமையார்தோள் அஞ்சு பவர்"],"p":"They that fear the bamboo-like shoulders of their wives will be destitute of manliness though they may flourish like the Gods.","r":"Even if they live like the gods, those who fear the bamboo-like shoulders of their wives have no worth.","n":"The bamboo epithet is usually praise, but here it makes the fear ridiculous: Parimelazhagar takes it to show how slight the object of terror is, and contrasts it with warriors' shoulders. Kalaignar again limits the verse, to a wife lacking wisdom and character.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["penvazhi","aanmai","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:91"},"k:907":{"book":"kural","num":907,"t":["பெண்ணேவல் செய்தொழுகும் ஆண்மையின் நாணுடைப்","பெண்ணே பெருமை உடைத்து"],"p":"Even shame faced womanhood is more to be esteemed than the shameless manhood that performs the behests of a wife.","r":"A woman who has shame holds more greatness than the manhood of one who goes about doing a woman's bidding.","n":"The verse uses gender as a rank and then overturns it: நாண் outranks manhood. Its force still depends on the assumption that being below a woman is a disgrace, so it cuts both ways.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","aanmai","perumai","penvazhi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:91"},"k:908":{"book":"kural","num":908,"t":["நட்டார் குறைமுடியார் நன்றாற்றார் நன்னுதலாள்","பெட் டாங்கு ஒழுகு பவர்"],"p":"Those who yield to the wishes of their wives will neither relieve the wants of (their) friends nor perform virtuous deeds.","r":"Those who act as the woman of the fine brow wishes neither meet their friends' needs nor do good.","n":"Parimelazhagar's reason is practical: she can neither issue the needed orders on her own nor hand over the wealth, so the household's decision-making stalls.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["penvazhi","sutram","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:91"},"k:909":{"book":"kural","num":909,"t":["அறவினையும் ஆன்ற பொருளும் பிறவினையும்","பெண்ஏவல் செய்வார்கண் இல்"],"p":"From those who obey the commands of their wives are to be expected neither deeds of virtue, nor those of wealth nor (even) those of pleasure.","r":"Deeds of virtue, ample wealth and the other pursuits are absent in those who do a woman's bidding.","n":"பிறவினை is left open. Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar take it as pleasure, which gives the verse its sting: the man who surrenders for the sake of pleasure ends up losing pleasure too.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["penvazhi","aram","selvam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:91"},"k:910":{"book":"kural","num":910,"t":["எண்சேர்ந்த நெஞ்சத் திடனுடையார்க்கு எஞ்ஞான்றும்","பெண்சேர்ந்தாம் பேதைமை இல்"],"p":"The foolishness that results from devotion to a wife will never be found in those who possess a reflecting mind and a prosperity (flowing) therefrom.","r":"Those whose minds hold deliberation and who have strength of heart will never have the foolishness that comes of attachment to a woman.","n":"The positive turns the chapter into a claim about mind, not women: the fault is called பேதைமை and is the man's own. Commentators split on இடன், which Parimelazhagar takes as wealth in kings and Manakkudavar as breadth of mind, while Kalaignar hears firmness of heart.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["penvazhi","arivu","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:91"},"k:911":{"book":"kural","num":911,"t":["அன்பின் விழையார் பொருள்விழையும் ஆய்தொடியார்","இன்சொல் இழுக்குத் தரும்"],"p":"The sweet words of elegant braceleted (prostitutes) who desire (a man) not from affection but from avarice, will cause sorrow.","r":"The sweet words of bangled women who desire a man for his wealth and not from love will bring him harm.","n":"The harm is located in the sweet speech, not the sex. The chapter opens as a chapter on deceptive language.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:92"},"k:912":{"book":"kural","num":912,"t":["பயன்தூக்கிப் பண்புரைக்கும் பண்பின் மகளிர்","நயன்தூக்கி நள்ளா விடல்"],"p":"One must ascertain the character of the ill-natured women who after ascertaining the wealth (of a man) speak (as if they were) good natured-ones, and avoid intercourse (with them).","r":"Weigh the conduct of faithless women who weigh the profit and then speak of their own virtue, and keep away from them.","n":"தூக்கி is repeated: she weighs the gain, so he must weigh her in return. Parimelazhagar calls this behaviour their சாதி தருமம், which is a commentator's social classification and not in the verse.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","நயன் is their conduct, examined so that one can leave them.","ஒழுகலாற்றினை ஆராய்ந்தறிந்து அவரைப் பொருந்தாது விடுக."],["manakkudavar","நயன் is the pleasure they offer, which on examination is not worth approaching.","இன்பத்தை யாராய்ந்து பார்த்து, அவரைச் சாராதொழிக"]],"ch":"k:92"},"k:913":{"book":"kural","num":913,"t":["பொருட்பெண்டிர் பொய்ம்மை முயக்கம் இருட்டறையில்","ஏதில் பிணந்தழீஇ அற்று"],"p":"The false embraces of wealth-loving women are like (hired men) embracing a strange corpse in a dark room.","r":"The false embrace of women of wealth is like embracing a stranger's corpse in a dark room.","n":"A quick reader takes the client to be holding a corpse, as Pope's couplet does. Parimelazhagar and Kalaignar make the woman the embracer: she is the hired bearer, and the unwanted client is the corpse she handles for pay. On that reading the disgust is hers, which complicates the chapter's usual stance.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","manam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The image describes her inner state: outwardly embracing, inwardly recoiling for the sake of money, like a paid corpse-bearer.","அகத்தால் அருவராநின்றும் பொருள் நோக்கிப் புறத்தால் தழுவுவர்"],["kalaignar","She embraces someone for money and acts out false love.","பணத்துக்காக மட்டுமே ஒருவரைத் தழுவிப் பொய்யன்பு காட்டி நடிப்பது"]],"ch":"k:92"},"k:914":{"book":"kural","num":914,"t":["பொருட்பொருளார் புன்னலந் தோயார் அருட்பொருள்","ஆயும் அறிவி னவர்"],"p":"The wise who seek the wealth of grace will not desire the base favours of those who regard wealth (and not pleasure) as (their) riches.","r":"Those whose understanding weighs the wealth of grace will not plunge into the base charms of women whose wealth is only wealth.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","selvam","varaivinmagalir","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","அருட்பொருள் is wealth joined to grace: the wise still pursue wealth, but examine how to use it graciously.","அருளொடு கூடிய பொருளை ஆராய்ந்து செய்யும் அறிவினையுடையார்"],["manakkudavar","அருட்பொருள் treats grace itself as the wealth one seeks.","அருளைப் பொருளாக ஆராயும் அறிவுடையார்"]],"ch":"k:92"},"k:915":{"book":"kural","num":915,"t":["பொதுநலத்தார் புன்னலம் தோயார் மதிநலத்தின்","மாண்ட அறிவி னவர்"],"p":"Those whose knowledge is made excellent by their (natural) sense will not covet the trffling delights of those whose favours are common (to all).","r":"Those whose knowledge has been refined by the goodness of their intellect will not plunge into the base charms of those whose goodness belongs to everyone.","n":"பொதுநலம், 'common goodness', is a pointed phrase, since நலம் elsewhere means personal excellence. Parimelazhagar explains மதிநலம் as clarity earned in former births; the verse names no births.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","arivu","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:92"},"k:916":{"book":"kural","num":916,"t":["தந்நலம் பாரிப்பார் தோயார் தகைசெருக்கிப்","புன்னலம் பாரிப்பார் தோள்"],"p":"Those who would spread (the fame of) their own goodness will not desire the shoulders of those,who rejoice in their accomplishments and bestow their despicable favours (on all who pay).","r":"Those who would spread their own goodness will not touch the shoulders of women who, proud of their charm, spread a base goodness.","n":"The same verb பாரிப்பார் is used for both parties: each is spreading a நலம், and the verse sets reputation against availability as two kinds of publicity.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:92"},"k:917":{"book":"kural","num":917,"t":["நிறைநெஞ்சம் இல்லவர் தோய்வார் பிறநெஞ்சிற்","பேணிப் புணர்பவர் தோள்"],"p":"Those who are destitute of a perfectly (reformed) mind will covet the shoulders of those who embrace (them) while their hearts covet other things.","r":"Those with no firmness of heart touch the shoulders of women who join with them while tending other things in their hearts.","n":"A divided heart on one side is paired with an unguarded heart on the other. The verse's பிற is unspecified; the commentators fill it with wealth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["varaivinmagalir","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:92"},"k:918":{"book":"kural","num":918,"t":["ஆயும் அறிவினர் அல்லார்க்கு அணங்கென்ப","மாய மகளிர் முயக்கு"],"p":"The wise say that to such as are destitute of discerning sense the embraces of faithless women are (as ruinous as those of) the celestail female.","r":"For those lacking discerning understanding, the embrace of deceiving women is said to be an அணங்கு.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","arivu","kaamam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","அணங்கு is a divine woman who takes life through desire, so the embrace is sweet and then fatal.","காமநெறியான் உயிர் கொள்ளும் தெய்வமகள்."],["manakkudavar","அணங்கு simply means affliction, with no divine being.","வருத்தமாமென்று சொல்லுவர்"]],"ch":"k:92"},"k:919":{"book":"kural","num":919,"t":["வரைவிலா மாணிழையார் மென்தோள் புரையிலாப்","பூரியர்கள் ஆழும் அளறு"],"p":"The delicate shoulders of prostitutes with excellent jewels are a hell into which are plunged the ignorant base.","r":"The soft shoulders of women of fine jewels who draw no line are the mire in which base men with no worth sink.","n":"வரைவு, 'limit', gives the chapter its title. Parimelazhagar says it means no line between high and low clients, and insists பூரியர் here means low in understanding, not in caste. அளறு means hell and mire at once, and softness of shoulder fits sinking.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","sirumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:92"},"k:920":{"book":"kural","num":920,"t":["இருமனப் பெண்டிரும் கள்ளும் கவறும்","திருநீக்கப் பட்டார் தொடர்பு"],"p":"Treacherous women, liquor, and gambling are the associates of such as have forsaken by Fortune.","r":"Double-minded women, liquor and dice are the company of those whom Fortune has cast off.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","kal","soodhu","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","These companions are signs by which one can tell that the goddess has already abandoned a man.","திரு நீக்கிப் பட்டமை இக்குறிகளான் அறியப்படும்"],["kalaignar","Consorting with them is what causes life's distinction to depart.","தொடர்பு கொண்டு உழல்வோரை விட்டு வாழ்வில் அமைய வேண்டிய சிறப்பு அகன்றுவிடும்."]],"ch":"k:92"},"k:921":{"book":"kural","num":921,"t":["உட்கப் படாஅர் ஒளியிழப்பர் எஞ்ஞான்றும்","கட்காதல் கொண்டொழுகு வார்"],"p":"Those who always thirst after drink will neither inspire fear (in others) nor retain the light (of their fame).","r":"Those who go on loving liquor are never held in awe, and they lose their lustre.","n":"Parimelazhagar reads this as a king's loss of enemies' fear, while Manakkudavar has ordinary esteem from others, so the same verse can be statecraft or general ethics.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kal","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:93"},"k:922":{"book":"kural","num":922,"t":["உண்ணற்க கள்ளை உணில்உண்க சான்றோரான்","எண்ணப் படவேண்டா தார்"],"p":"Let no liquor be drunk; if it is desired, let it be drunk by those who care not for esteem of the great.","r":"Do not drink; if anyone does, let it be those who don't wish to be counted by the worthy.","n":"The permission is a trap. It sounds like a concession but actually states the price.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kal","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:93"},"k:923":{"book":"kural","num":923,"t":["ஈன்றாள் முகத்தேயும் இன்னாதால் என்மற்றுச்","சான்றோர் முகத்துக் களி"],"p":"Intoxication is painful even in the presence of (one's) mother; what will it not then be in that of the wise ?","r":"Drunken joy is painful even in the sight of the woman who bore you; what then in the sight of the worthy?","n":"The argument runs from the most forgiving audience to the least, so the mother works as a limit case. களி, the word for drunken joy, is otherwise a word of delight.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kal","naanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:93"},"k:924":{"book":"kural","num":924,"t":["நாண்என்னும் நல்லாள் புறங்கொடுக்கும் கள்ளென்னும்","பேணாப் பெருங்குற்றத் தார்க்கு"],"p":"The fair maid of modesty will turn her back on those who are guilty of the great and abominable crime of drunkenness.","r":"Shame, the good woman, turns her back on those with the great and repugnant fault called liquor.","n":"புறங்கொடுக்கும் may carry a battlefield sense of turning one's back in flight; that is my suggestion, not a commentator's. Parimelazhagar explains the feminine personification as following a Sanskrit convention.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["naanam","kal"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:93"},"k:925":{"book":"kural","num":925,"t":["கையறி யாமை உடைத்தே பொருள்கொடுத்து","மெய்யறி யாமை கொளல்"],"p":"To give money and purchase unconsciousness is the result of one's ignorance of (one's own actions).","r":"To give up wealth and take self-oblivion in return must come from கையறியாமை.","n":"The irony is commercial: one pays good money to buy ignorance.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kal","arivu","selvam","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","கையறியாமை is helpless not-knowing what to do, itself the fruit of former deeds.","அவன் பழவினைப் பயனாய செய்வதறியாமையைத் தனக்குக் காரணமாக உடைத்து."],["manakkudavar","கையறியாமை is simply not seeing what benefit results.","பயன் அறியாமை யுடைத்து"],["kalaignar","It is indescribable stupidity, with no deed or fruit involved.","விவரிக்கவே முடியாத மூடத்தனமாகும்."]],"ch":"k:93"},"k:926":{"book":"kural","num":926,"t":["துஞ்சினார் செத்தாரின் வேறல்லர் எஞ்ஞான்றும்","நஞ்சுண்பார் கள்ளுண் பவர்"],"p":"They that sleep resemble the deed; (likewise) they that drink are no other than poison-eaters.","r":"Those asleep are no different from the dead, and those who drink are always eating poison.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kal","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","This is an analogy. Sleepers differ from the dead yet lack awareness just as they do; drinkers differ from poison-eaters yet lack awareness permanently. He rejects a rival crosswise pairing.","கள்ளுண்பார் நஞ்சுண்பாரின் வேறாதல் உடையரேனும், எக்காலத்தும் அறிவின்மையான் வேறு எனப்பட்டார்"],["kalaignar","Drinkers and poison-eaters are the same, and therefore the drinker's sleep is the same as death.","மது அருந்துவோர்க்கும் நஞ்சு அருந்துவோர்க்கும் வேறுபாடு கிடையாது"]],"ch":"k:93"},"k:927":{"book":"kural","num":927,"t":["உள்ளொற்றி உள்ளூர் நகப்படுவர் எஞ்ஞான்றும்","கள்ளொற்றிக் கண்சாய் பவர்"],"p":"Those who always intoxicate themselves by a private (indulgence in) drink; will have their secrets detected and laughed at by their fellow-townsmen.","r":"Those who always seek out liquor and sink with drooping eyes are seen through and laughed at by their own town.","n":"ஒற்றி occurs twice: he resorts to drink in hiding, and the town spies out what is within. Parimelazhagar says it is secret drinking and Manakkudavar seeking out where liquor is.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kal","naanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:93"},"k:928":{"book":"kural","num":928,"t":["களித்தறியேன் என்பது கைவிடுக நெஞ்சத்து","ஒளித்ததூஉம் ஆங்கே மிகும்"],"p":"Let (the drunkard) give up saying \"I have never drunk\"; (for) the moment (he drinks) he will simply betray his former attempt to conceal.","r":"Give up saying you have never been drunk, because what is hidden in the heart grows on that very spot.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kal","manam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The speaker is a secret drinker claiming sobriety when not drinking; as soon as he drinks, the hidden fault comes out larger.","மறைந்துண்டு வைத்து யான் கள்ளுண்டறியேன் என்று உண்ணாத பொழுது தம் ஒழுக்கங் கூறுதலையொழிக"],["manakkudavar","The speaker claims drink does not intoxicate him; drink itself makes the tongue slip and reveal the hidden things of the mind.","கள்ளுண்டால் களித்தறியே னென்பதனைக் கைவிடுக"]],"ch":"k:93"},"k:929":{"book":"kural","num":929,"t":["களித்தானைக் காரணம் காட்டுதல் கீழ்நீர்க்","குளித்தானைத் தீத்துரீஇ அற்று"],"p":"Reasoning with a drunkard is like going under water with a torch in search of a drowned man.","r":"To offer reasons to the drunk is like reaching with fire for one who has plunged beneath the water.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kal","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","துரீஇ is searching with a lamp: as light doesn't enter water, so reason doesn't enter his mind.","நீருள் மூழ்கினான் ஒருவனைப் பிறனொருவன் விளக்கினல் நாடுதலை யொக்கும்."],["manakkudavar","துரீஇ is burning him with fire: a submerged man can't be touched by it.","நீரின் கீழே முழுகினானைத் தீயினாற் சுட்டது போலும்"]],"ch":"k:93"},"k:930":{"book":"kural","num":930,"t":["கள்ளுண்ணாப் போழ்திற் களித்தானைக் காணுங்கால்","உள்ளான்கொல் உண்டதன் சோர்வு"],"p":"When (a drunkard) who is sober sees one who is not, it looks as if he remembered not the evil effects of his (own) drink.","r":"When sober, and seeing another man drunk, doesn't he reflect on the lapse of his own drinking?","n":"After 929's closed door the chapter leaves one open: self-recognition. Parimelazhagar names the logic as inference: what he sees in the other he could infer about himself. The question form suggests the chance is seldom taken.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kal","arivu","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:93"},"k:931":{"book":"kural","num":931,"t":["வேண்டற்க வென்றிடினும் சூதினை வென்றதூஉம்","தூண்டிற்பொன் மீன்விழுங்கி அற்று"],"p":"Though able to win, let not one desire gambling; (for) even what is won is like a fish swallowing the iron in fish-hook.","r":"Don't want gambling even if you can win, for what you win is like the metal of the hook swallowed by a fish.","n":"The verse attacks gambling at its strongest case, a win. Parimelazhagar adds that the winnings are a shackle keeping the gambler from leaving. பொன் here means the hook's metal, not gold, though the sound of 'gold' sharpens the image.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soodhu","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:94"},"k:932":{"book":"kural","num":932,"t":["ஒன்றெய்தி நூறிழக்கும் சூதர்க்கும் உண்டாங்கொல்","நன்றெய்தி வாழ்வதோர் ஆறு"],"p":"Is there indeed a means of livelihood that can bestow happiness on gamblers who gain one and lose a hundred ?","r":"Is there any way of living well for gamblers, who get one and lose a hundred?","n":"ஒன்றெய்தி நூறிழக்கும் is sequence as well as ratio. Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar hear the hope that follows the first gain, so the loss is driven by the win.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soodhu","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:94"},"k:933":{"book":"kural","num":933,"t":["உருளாயம் ஓவாது கூறின் பொருளாயம்","போஒய்ப் புறமே படும்"],"p":"If the king is incessantly addicted to the rolling dice in the hope of gain, his wealth and the resources thereof will take their departure and fall into other's hands.","r":"If one keeps calling the falling of the dice, the gains of wealth will leave and pass elsewhere.","n":"The chiming of உருளாயம் and பொருளாயம் sets one income against another: the income of the dice swallows the income of wealth. Parimelazhagar makes the subject a king and the destination his enemies, which the verse doesn't state.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soodhu","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:94"},"k:934":{"book":"kural","num":934,"t":["சிறுமை பலசெய்து சீரழிக்கும் சூதின்","வறுமை தருவதொன்று இல்"],"p":"There is nothing else that brings (us) poverty like gambling which causes many a misery and destroys (one's) reputation.","r":"There is nothing that gives poverty like gambling, which does many small harms and destroys distinction.","n":"சிறுமை elsewhere is smallness of character. The commentators read it here as miseries, but both senses are available, since gambling diminishes a person as well as afflicting him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soodhu","sirumai","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:94"},"k:935":{"book":"kural","num":935,"t":["கவறும் கழகமும் கையும் தருக்கி","இவறியார் இல்லாகி யார்"],"p":"Penniless are those who by reason of their attachment would never forsake gambling, the gambling-place and the handling (of dice).","r":"Those who, in pride, clung to the dice, the gaming hall and the skilled hand have come to nothing.","n":"The past tense makes this an appeal to history. Parimelazhagar names the example: the Pandavas losing their kingdom and going to the forest. The verse names no one, so this epic reference is his.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soodhu","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:94"},"k:936":{"book":"kural","num":936,"t":["அகடாரார் அல்லல் உழப்பர்சூ தென்னும்","முகடியான் மூடப்பட் டார்"],"p":"Those who are swallowed by the goddess called \"gambling\" will never have their hunger satisfied, but suffer the pangs of hell in the next world.","r":"Those covered by the goddess of misfortune named gambling will not fill themselves and will suffer distress.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soodhu","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","அகடு is the belly: they go hungry here, and suffer hell in the next world.","இம்மைக்கண் வயிறாரப் பெறார்; மறுமைக்கண் நிரயத் துன்பம் உழப்பர்."],["manakkudavar","அகடு is what lies within, the senses and the mind, which are never satisfied, and the covering hides their good qualities.","தமக்கு உள்ளாகிய இந்திரியங்களும் மனமும் இன்புற்று நிறை யப்பெறார்"]],"ch":"k:94"},"k:937":{"book":"kural","num":937,"t":["பழகிய செல்வமும் பண்பும் கெடுக்கும்","கழகத்துக் காலை புகின்"],"p":"To waste time at the place of gambling will destroy inherited wealth and goodness of character.","r":"If காலை enters the gaming hall, it will ruin long-held wealth and character.","n":"Whichever reading is taken, time is the stake, not money: ruin comes from what the hours would have been spent on.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soodhu","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","காலை means the time set apart for virtue, wealth and pleasure, especially a king's.","அறம் பொருள் இன்பங்கட்கு அடைத்த காலம் அரசனுக்குச் சூதாடு களத்தின்கண் கழியுமாயின்"],["manakkudavar","காலை means the morning hour.","சூது கழகத்தின்கண்ணே காலைப்பொழுது புகுவானாயின்"]],"ch":"k:94"},"k:938":{"book":"kural","num":938,"t":["பொருள் கெடுத்துப் பொய்மேற் கொளீஇ அருள்கெடுத்து","அல்லல் உழப்பிக்கும் சூது"],"p":"Gambling destroys property, teaches falsehood, puts an end to benevolence, and brings in misery (here and hereafter).","r":"Gambling destroys wealth, makes one take up lying, destroys compassion and drives one through misery.","n":"The list is a causal ladder, and the moral rungs come after the financial one. Parimelazhagar assigns the means: loss robs, the winner's position breeds lies, and the rancour of the game kills compassion. In his reading, then, wins and losses alike do damage.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soodhu","arul","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:94"},"k:939":{"book":"kural","num":939,"t":["உடைசெல்வம் ஊண்ஒளி கல்விஎன்று ஐந்தும்","அடையாவாம் ஆயங் கொளின்"],"p":"The habit of gambling prevents the attainment of these five: clothing, wealth, food, fame and learning.","r":"Clothing, wealth, food, lustre and learning, these five don't come near one who takes to gaming.","n":"அடையாவாம் says that they won't arrive, while Pope's 'depart' adds that they were held already. The list runs from bare needs to learning, and puts survival and culture under one head.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["soodhu","selvam","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:94"},"k:940":{"book":"kural","num":940,"t":["இழத்தொறூஉம் காதலிக்கும் சூதேபோல் துன்பம்","உழத்தொறூஉம் காதற்று உயிர்"],"p":"As the gambler loves (his vice) the more he loses by it, so does the soul love (the body) the more it suffers through it.","r":"As a gambler loves the game more with every loss, so the living being's love grows with every suffering it goes through.","n":"An unexpected turn to the human condition: the chapter's closing verse uses gambling to diagnose attachment to life itself. Parimelazhagar insists the real target is the gambler, with the soul there to drive the point home, but the verse's grammar makes the soul the thing explained.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soodhu","manam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The soul's love is for the body, through which it suffers; as the one quitting gambling is admired, so is the soul that spurns the body.","சூதினை வெறுத்து ஒழிவானை யொக்கும் உடம்பினை வெறுத்தொழியும் உயிர்"],["kalaignar","The love is for life itself, which grows as the body suffers; no renunciation is implied.","உடலுக்குத் துன்பம் தொடர்ந்து வரவர உயிர்மீது கொள்ளுகிற ஆசையும்"]],"ch":"k:94"},"k:941":{"book":"kural","num":941,"t":["மிகினும் குறையினும் நோய்செய்யும் நூலோர்","வளிமுதலா எண்ணிய மூன்று"],"p":"If (food and work are either) excessive or deficient, the three things enumerated by (medical) writers, flatulence, biliousness, and phlegm, will cause (one) disease.","r":"The three counted by the learned, starting with wind, cause disease whether they exceed or fall short.","n":"Deficiency is as dangerous as excess. The verse defers to the நூலோர் and doesn't claim the theory as its own.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["noy","alavu","kalvi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","What exceeds or falls short is food and activity, relative to one's constitution, and that disturbs the three.","உணவும் செயல்களும் ஒருவன் பகுதிக்கு ஒத்த அளவின் அன்றி"],["manakkudavar","What exceeds or falls short is food, sleep and sexual union.","உணவும் உறக்கமும் இணைவிழைச்சும் தன்னுடம்பின் அளவிற்கு மிகினும் குறையினும்"],["kalaignar","What exceeds or falls short is the three humours themselves.","மூன்றில் ஒன்று அளவுக்கு அதிகமானாலும் குறைந்தாலும் நோய் உண்டாகும்."]],"ch":"k:95"},"k:942":{"book":"kural","num":942,"t":["மருந்தென வேண்டாவாம் யாக்கைக்கு அருந்தியது","அற்றது போற்றி உணின்"],"p":"No medicine is necessary for him who eats after assuring (himself) that what he has (already) eaten has been digested.","r":"No medicine is called for by the body if one eats only after attending to the digestion of what was eaten.","n":"போற்றி, 'with care', turns digestion into something to watch. Parimelazhagar lists bodily signs of it, such as lightness and clean belching. His remark on உணின் concedes that this is hard to do.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["undi","marundhu","alavu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:95"},"k:943":{"book":"kural","num":943,"t":["அற்றால் அறவறிந்து உண்க அஃதுடம்பு","பெற்றான் நெடிதுய்க்கும் ஆறு"],"p":"If (one's food has been) digested let one eat with moderation; (for) that is the way to prolong the life of an embodied soul.","r":"When digestion is complete, eat knowing the measure: that is how one who has gained a body can carry it far.","n":"உடம்பு பெற்றான், 'one who has received a body', treats the body as a gift. Parimelazhagar pushes further: a human body is the only vehicle for this world, the next and liberation, so length of life serves those ends. The verse itself states no such purpose.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["undi","alavu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:95"},"k:944":{"book":"kural","num":944,"t":["அற்றது அறிந்து கடைப்பிடித்து மாறல்ல","துய்க்க துவரப் பசித்து"],"p":"(First) assure yourself that your food has been digested and never fail to eat, when very hungry, whatever is not disagreeable (to you).","r":"Knowing the food is digested, hold to what is not incompatible and eat it after real hunger.","n":"Parimelazhagar sets out three kinds of incompatibility: with one's constitution, with the season and hour, and between foods. His example is honey and ghee in equal amounts turning to poison, which Manakkudavar shares.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["undi","alavu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:95"},"k:945":{"book":"kural","num":945,"t":["மாறுபாடு இல்லாத உண்டி மறுத்துண்ணின்","ஊறுபாடு இல்லை உயிர்க்கு"],"p":"There will be no disaster to one's life if on e eats with moderation, food that is not disagreeable.","r":"If one eats with மறுத்து food that has no incompatibility, life takes no injury.","n":"I'm unsure of Manakkudavar's sense. His gloss, with its example of ginger after jackfruit, doesn't obviously fit the verse, and I record it without claiming to understand it fully.","x":1,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["undi","alavu","noy"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","மறுத்து means refusing the amount the heart wants and stopping at what brings no disease.","தன் உள்ளம் வேண்டிய அளவினான் அன்றிப் பிணிவாரா அளவினால்"],["manakkudavar","He takes it as eating by setting aside the food, with incompatibility illustrated by a compensating food.","சுவையும் வீரியமும் மாறுபாடில்லாத உணவை நீக்கி யுண்ப னாயின்"]],"ch":"k:95"},"k:946":{"book":"kural","num":946,"t":["இழிவறிந்து உண்பான்கண் இன்பம்போல் நிற்கும்","கழிபேர் இரையான்கண் நோய்"],"p":"As pleasure dwells with him who eats moderately, so disease (dwells) with the glutton who eats voraciously.","r":"As joy stays with the one who eats knowing how to stop short, disease stays with the one who eats enormously.","n":"இரையான், 'feeder', is a word for animals' feed. Parimelazhagar notes that this likens the glutton to a beast. The contrast is inbam and noy, not health and disease: moderation yields pleasure, so restraint isn't presented as a sacrifice.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["undi","noy","alavu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:95"},"k:947":{"book":"kural","num":947,"t":["தீயள வன்றித் தெரியான் பெரிதுண்ணின்","நோயள வின்றிப் படும்"],"p":"He will be afflicted with numberless diseases, who eats immoderately, ignorant (of the rules of health).","r":"If one eats hugely, not discerning the measure of the fire within, disease will arise beyond all measure.","n":"அளவு occurs on both sides: disregard the measure of the fire, and disease loses its measure. Manakkudavar and Kalaignar read தீ simply as hunger, while Parimelazhagar reads it as the belly's fire.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["undi","noy","alavu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:95"},"k:948":{"book":"kural","num":948,"t":["நோய்நாடி நோய்முதல் நாடி அதுதணிக்கும்","வாய்நாடி வாய்ப்பச் செயல்"],"p":"Let the physician enquire into the (nature of the) disease, its cause and its method of cure and treat it faithfully according to (medical rule).","r":"Investigate the disease, then its root, then the means that subdue it, and act so as to get it right.","n":"நாடி recurs three times and sets the order: identify, trace, select, then act. Kalaignar adds that this applies to the diseases of society, which is his extension. Parimelazhagar lists surgery and cautery among the means.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["noy","marundhu","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:95"},"k:949":{"book":"kural","num":949,"t":["உற்றான் அளவும் பிணியளவும் காலமும்","கற்றான் கருதிச் செயல்"],"p":"The learned (physician) should ascertain the condition of his patient; the nature of his disease, and the season (of the year) and (then) proceed (with his treatment).","r":"The learned one should act having considered the measure of the patient, the measure of the illness and the time.","n":"Learning alone isn't enough: the verse makes the patient's measure come first, so that treatment is individual. Parimelazhagar explicitly wants both the book's way and the physician's own discernment.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["noy","alavu","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:95"},"k:950":{"book":"kural","num":950,"t":["உற்றவன் தீர்ப்பான் மருந்துழைச் செல்வானென்று","அப்பால் நாற் கூற்றே மருந்து"],"p":"Medical science consists of four parts, viz., patient, physician, medicine and compounder; and each of these (again) contains four sub-divisions.","r":"The patient, the healer, the medicine and the one who attends: medicine has these four parts.","n":"The verse calls the whole arrangement மருந்து, so the patient is himself part of the medicine, not its recipient.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["marundhu","noy"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","உழைச்செல்வான் is the compounder, and each of the four has four sub-qualities of its own.","நான்கு பகுதியையுடைய நான்கு திறத்தது"],["kalaignar","உழைச்செல்வான் is one who stands by to help, and treatment simply has four parts.","அருகிருந்து துணைபுரிபவர் என மருத்துவமுறை நான்கு வகையாக அமைந்துள்ளது."]],"ch":"k:95"},"k:951":{"book":"kural","num":951,"t":["இற்பிறந்தார் கண்அல்லது இல்லை இயல்பாகச்","செப்பமும் நாணும் ஒருங்கு"],"p":"Consistency (of thought, word and deed) and fear (of sin) are conjointly natural only to the high-born.","r":"Only in those born to a good house do integrity and shame exist together, naturally.","n":"இயல்பாக and ஒருங்கு both limit the claim: it is about these qualities coming naturally and together, which leaves open whether others can acquire them.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kudi","naanam","pirappu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Birth decides: others can be taught these qualities but won't keep them for long.","பிறர்க்காயின் கற்பித்த வழியும் நெடிது நில்லா"],["kalaignar","Conduct defines birth: only those with these qualities can be regarded as high-born.","மற்றவர்களை உயர்ந்த குடியில் பிறந்தவர்களாகக் கருத முடியாது."]],"ch":"k:96"},"k:952":{"book":"kural","num":952,"t":["ஒழுக்கமும் வாய்மையும் நாணும் இம் மூன்றும்","இழுக்கார் குடிப்பிறந் தார்"],"p":"The high-born will never deviate from these three; good manners, truthfulness and modesty.","r":"Those of good birth don't slip in these three: conduct, truthfulness and shame.","n":"Parimelazhagar maps the three onto body, speech and mind, and adds that this comes without learning. Manakkudavar takes நாண் physically, as covering one's nakedness, while Kalaignar has honour instead and again makes these the test of who counts as high-born.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","ozhukkam","vaaimai","naanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:96"},"k:953":{"book":"kural","num":953,"t":["நகைஈகை இன்சொல் இகழாமை நான்கும்","வகையென்ப வாய்மைக் குடிக்கு"],"p":"A cheerful countenance, liberality, pleasant words, and an unreviling disposition, these four are said to be the proper qualities of the truly high-born.","r":"A smile, giving, sweet words and not scorning: these four, they say, belong to a truly good lineage.","n":"None of the four is about ancestry, and all are ways of treating someone else. Parimelazhagar specifies the setting: when the destitute come, for everyone scorns those who have nothing. That turns etiquette into a test.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kudi","eegai","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:96"},"k:954":{"book":"kural","num":954,"t":["அடுக்கிய கோடி பெறினும் குடிப்பிறந்தார்","குன்றுவ செய்தல் இலர்"],"p":"Though blessed with immense wealth, the noble will never do anything unbecoming.","r":"Even if they gain crores upon crores, the well-born do nothing that diminishes them.","n":"பெறினும் can mean holding wealth or being offered it. Parimelazhagar and Pope lean toward the first, and Kalaignar toward a bribe, which makes this a claim about being unbuyable.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","selvam","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:96"},"k:955":{"book":"kural","num":955,"t":["வழங்குவ துள்வீழ்ந்தக் கண்ணும் பழங்குடி","பண்பில் தலைப்பிரிதல் இன்று"],"p":"Though their means fall off, those born in ancient families, will not lose their character (for liberality).","r":"Even when what they can give has shrunk, an old family doesn't part from its nature.","n":"The verse measures their poverty by what they can give, not by what they have. Parimelazhagar explains பழங்குடி with the Chera, Chola and Pandya, excellent since creation, which ties this to royal houses.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","eegai","panpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:96"},"k:956":{"book":"kural","num":956,"t":["சலம்பற்றிச் சால்பில செய்யார்மா சற்ற","குலம்பற்றி வாழ்தும் என் பார்"],"p":"Those who seek to preserve the irreproachable honour of their families will not viciously do what is detrimental thereto.","r":"Those who say 'we will live holding to our spotless lineage' will not do unworthy things through deceit.","n":"என்பார் makes lineage a resolve: it applies to those who declare they will live by it. That moves lineage from something one has to something one takes on. Parimelazhagar adds 'even in poverty'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:96"},"k:957":{"book":"kural","num":957,"t":["குடிப்பிறந்தார் கண்விளங்கும் குற்றம் விசும்பின்","மதிக்கண் மறுப்போல் உயர்ந்து"],"p":"The defects of the noble will be observed as clearly as the dark spots in the moon.","r":"A fault in the well-born stands out high, like the spot on the moon in the sky.","n":"A cost of height: the fault is not larger, but more visible. Each part maps: lineage to sky, virtues to moon, fault to spot. Manakkudavar draws the practical moral.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:96"},"k:958":{"book":"kural","num":958,"t":["நலத்தின்கண் நாரின்மை தோன்றின் அவனைக்","குலத்தின்கண் ஐயப் படும்"],"p":"If one of a good family betrays want of affection, his descent from it will be called in question.","r":"If want of affection shows in one of a good house, people will have doubts about his lineage.","n":"The chapter's claim protects itself here: when a well-born man is cruel, the doubt falls on his birth and not on the rule that birth gives virtue. Reading conduct as evidence of birth also makes the doubt a slur on his parentage, which is not stated.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","pirappu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:96"},"k:959":{"book":"kural","num":959,"t":["நிலத்தில் கிடந்தமை கால்காட்டும் காட்டும்","குலத்தில் பிறந்தார்வாய்ச் சொல்"],"p":"As the sprout indicates the nature of the soil, (so) the speech of the noble indicates (that of one's birth).","r":"A sprout shows what lies in the ground; the words from the mouth show the lineage of the one born in it.","n":"Parimelazhagar notes the disguise: it looks as if the verse is teaching how to identify lineage, but it is really a command to speak sweetly. Note கால், a sprout: the evidence appears at the earliest moment. Kalaignar's version has the mature crop.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","pirappu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:96"},"k:960":{"book":"kural","num":960,"t":["நலம்வேண்டின் நாணுடைமை வேண்டும் குலம் வேண்டின்","வேண்டுக யார்க்கும் பணிவு"],"p":"He who desires a good name must desire modesty; and he who desires (the continuance of) a family greatness must be submissive to all.","r":"If you want goodness, want shame; if you want lineage, want humility toward all.","n":"வேண்டின் treats lineage as something one can want and go after. This is the verse most at odds with 951, since a thing given by birth need not be wanted.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","panivu","kudi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","யார்க்கும் means everyone who is due deference, such as priests, the worthy, ascetics and parents, shown by rising and going to meet them.","பணியப்படுவார் யாவர் மாட்டும் பணிதலை வேண்டுக"],["manakkudavar","யார்க்கும் means all people without limit.","யாவர்மாட்டும் தாழ்ந் தொழுகுதலை விரும்புக"]],"ch":"k:96"},"k:961":{"book":"kural","num":961,"t":["இன்றி அமையாச் சிறப்பின ஆயினும்","குன்ற வருப விடல்"],"p":"Actions that would degrade (one's) family should not be done; though they may be so important that not doing them would end in death.","r":"Even if they are so excellent as to be indispensable, give up things that would diminish you.","n":"Parimelazhagar makes this a deliberate polemic: he says it rejects a Sanskrit rule that one may do degrading things to survive. The verse names no rival.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam","kudi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The acts are ones whose omission would mean death, and they must still be refused.","'இறப்பவரும் வழி இளிவந்தன செய்தாயினும் உய்க' என்னும் வடநூல் முறையை மறுத்து"],["manakkudavar","What is given up is wealth and pleasure that would reduce one's nature, however great the amount.","இது பொருளும் இன்பமும் மிகினும், தன்மை குறைவன் செய்யற்க"],["kalaignar","They are obligatory actions, to be avoided if they lessen one's dignity; death is not raised.","கட்டாயமாகச் செய்து தீர வேண்டிய செயல்கள் என்றாலும்கூட"]],"ch":"k:97"},"k:962":{"book":"kural","num":962,"t":["சீரினும் சீரல்ல செய்யாரே சீரொடு","பேராண்மை வேண்டு பவர்"],"p":"Those who desire (to maintain their) honour, will surely do nothing dishonourable, even for the sake of fame.","r":"Those who want renown and great manliness together won't do unworthy things even for renown.","n":"சீர் is used against itself: renown bought with unworthy acts is not renown. பேராண்மை is explained by Parimelazhagar as a firmness that doesn't falter anywhere, and he takes it here as honour, not prowess.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam","pugazh","aanmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:97"},"k:963":{"book":"kural","num":963,"t":["பெருக்கத்து வேண்டும் பணிதல் சிறிய","சுருக்கத்து வேண்டும் உயர்வு"],"p":"In great prosperity humility is becoming; dignity, in great adversity.","r":"In abundance you must bow, and in slight and shrunken circumstances you must stand tall.","n":"The posture runs against the circumstances: humility when the world would permit pride, and height when the world would press one down. Humility and honour, often seen as rivals, are the same discipline at different times.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam","panivu","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:97"},"k:964":{"book":"kural","num":964,"t":["தலையின் இழிந்த மயிரனையர் மாந்தர்","நிலையின் இழிந்தக் கடை"],"p":"They who have fallen from their (high) position are like the hair which has fallen from the head.","r":"People who have fallen from their position are like hair that has fallen from the head.","n":"The verse says மாந்தர், people generally, and Parimelazhagar narrows it to the well-born. Nothing about the hair changes when it falls, only its place, so worth is presented as depending on where it stands.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam","kudi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:97"},"k:965":{"book":"kural","num":965,"t":["குன்றின் அனையாரும் குன்றுவர் குன்றுவ","குன்றி அனைய செயின்"],"p":"Even those who are exalted like a hill will be thought low, if they commit deeds that are debasing.","r":"Even those like a hill will sink, if they do a lowering thing the size of a kundri seed.","n":"குன்று works four ways: hill, sinking, lowering, and the tiny red seed குன்றி. The sound itself demonstrates how little separates the great from the small.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:97"},"k:966":{"book":"kural","num":966,"t":["புகழ்இன்றால் புத்தேள்நாட்டு உய்யாதால் என்மற்று","இகழ்வார்பின் சென்று நிலை"],"p":"Of what good is it (for the high-born) to go and stand in vain before those who revile him ? it only brings him loss of honour and exclusion from heaven.","r":"It brings no praise and doesn't carry one to the gods' land: why then go and stand behind those who scorn you?","n":"The argument is from utility, not pride: it weighs both possible rewards and finds neither.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["maanam","pugazh"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Heaven is a real reward that this conduct fails to gain.","ஏனைப் புத்தேளுலகத்துச் செலுத்தாது"],["kalaignar","Heaven is non-existent, and the line asks rhetorically whether it could be obtained this way.","இல்லாத சொர்க்கமா கிடைக்கும்?"]],"ch":"k:97"},"k:967":{"book":"kural","num":967,"t":["ஒட் டார்பின் சென்றொருவன் வாழ்தலின் அந்நிலையே","கெட்டான் எனப்படுதல் நன்று"],"p":"It is better for a man to be said of him that he died in his usual state than that he eked out his life by following those who disgraced him.","r":"Rather than live by following those who don't accept you, it is better to be called ruined in that very condition.","n":"கெட்டான் means ruined and can mean dead. Parimelazhagar and Kalaignar take death, while Manakkudavar stays with the word. Parimelazhagar says this replies to someone who would still go for wealth and survival. The verse's சொல் is about what is said of a person, so reputation is the stake.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:97"},"k:968":{"book":"kural","num":968,"t":["மருந்தோமற்று ஊன்ஓம்பும் வாழ்க்கை பெருந்தகைமை","பீடழிய வந்த இடத்து"],"p":"For the high-born to keep their body in life when their honour is gone will certainly not prove a remedy against death.","r":"When greatness comes to be stripped of its dignity, is a life nursing the flesh a medicine?","n":"The commentators complete the question: medicine against dying. Since death will come anyway, living on buys nothing. ஊன் reduces the body to meat, which Parimelazhagar notes is what a person is after honour goes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["maanam","marundhu","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:97"},"k:969":{"book":"kural","num":969,"t":["மயிர்நீப்பின் வாழாக் கவரிமா அன்னார்","உயிர்நீப்பர் மானம் வரின்"],"p":"Those who give up (their) life when (their) honour is at stake are like the yark which kills itself at the loss of (even one of) its hairs.","r":"Those who are like the yak that won't live once its hair is gone give up their lives when honour's moment comes.","n":"The image is presented as nature, which Parimelazhagar says shows that this is inborn in them. Kalaignar distances himself with என்பார்கள், 'people say'. The verse doesn't say whether the death is by one's own hand; Kalaignar's next gloss, on 970, does.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","மானம் வரின் means the point at which honour is won by giving up life.","உயிர் நீக்கத்தான் மானம் எய்தும் எல்லை வரின்"],["manakkudavar","மானம் வரின் means when honour is about to be destroyed.","மான முடையார், மானம் அழியவரின் உயிர்விடுவர்"]],"ch":"k:97"},"k:970":{"book":"kural","num":970,"t":["இளிவரின் வாழாத மானம் உடையார்","ஒளிதொழுது ஏத்தும் உலகு"],"p":"The world will (always) praise and adore the fame of the honourable who would rather die than suffer indignity.","r":"The world worships and praises the radiance of the honourable who won't live when disgrace comes.","n":"The reward is entirely posthumous and in others' mouths, and ஒளி is the fame that outlasts the person. Parimelazhagar adds heaven by citing Purananuru, which reverses 966's point only in appearance, since there heaven was what degrading service failed to earn.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:97"},"k:971":{"book":"kural","num":971,"t":["ஒளிஒருவற்கு உள்ள வெறுக்கை இளிஒருவற்கு","அஃதிறந்து வாழ்தும் எனல்"],"p":"One's light is the abundance of one's courage; one's darkness is the desire to live destitute of such (a state of mind.)","r":"One's radiance is the wealth of aspiration, and one's disgrace is saying 'we will live without it'.","n":"ஒளி and இளி close the previous chapter's last verse (970) and open this one, so the two chapters are stitched together by these words. வெறுக்கை means wealth, so aspiration is presented as a holding. Disgrace lies in the stated resolve to live without it, not in any failure.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["perumai","muyarchi","maanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:98"},"k:972":{"book":"kural","num":972,"t":["பிறப்பொக்கும் எல்லா உயிர்க்கும் சிறப்பொவ்வா","செய்தொழில் வேற்றுமை யான்"],"p":"All human beings agree as regards their birth but differ as regards their characteristics, because of the different qualities of their actions.","r":"Birth is alike for all living beings, and distinction is unequal because of the difference in the work they do.","n":"The verse says எல்லா உயிர்க்கும், wider than humanity. செய்தொழில் could be occupation or conduct, and the whole verse's social force depends on that word.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","pirappu","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Birth is alike only in being embodied through karma; the work that tests greatness itself differs by varna and individual.","தொழிற்பாகுபாடுகள் வருணந்தோறும் யாக்கைதோறும் வேறுபடுதலின்"],["manakkudavar","Greatness simply can't be recognized by lineage.","பெருமையாவது குலத்தினால் அறியப்படா தென்பதூஉம்"],["kalaignar","All are equal by birth, and difference appears only in the skill shown at one's work.","பிறப்பினால் அனைவரும் சமம்."]],"ch":"k:98"},"k:973":{"book":"kural","num":973,"t":["மேலிருந்தும் மேலல்லார் மேலல்லர் கீழிருந்தும்","கீழல்லார் கீழல் லவர்"],"p":"Though (raised) above, the base cannot become great; though (brought) low, the great cannot become base.","r":"Those who aren't high are not high even when placed high, and those who aren't low are not low even when placed low.","n":"A single word, மேல் or கீழ், names both position and worth, and the verse pries them apart. Pope's couplet takes the position as birth, but Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar take it as the seat that wealth gives, a high couch against bare ground.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","sirumai","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:98"},"k:974":{"book":"kural","num":974,"t":["ஒருமை மகளிரே போலப் பெருமையும்","தன்னைத்தான் கொண்டொழுகின் உண்டு"],"p":"Even greatness, like a woman's chastity, belongs only to him who guards himself.","r":"Greatness too, like single-minded women, exists only if one holds on to oneself.","n":"The model of masculine greatness is a woman. Conduct expected of wives becomes the standard for great men, a striking move given chapter 91. Parimelazhagar explains self-holding as restraint of mind, speech and body.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:98"},"k:975":{"book":"kural","num":975,"t":["பெருமை யுடையவர் ஆற்றுவார் ஆற்றின்","அருமை உடைய செயல்"],"p":"(Though reduced) the great will be able to perform, in the proper way, deeds difficult (for others to do).","r":"Those with greatness will accomplish in the right way what is hard to do.","n":"ஆற்றின் adds a limit: the rare deed must be done in the right way. Parimelazhagar adds 'even when poor', which the verse doesn't say. Kalaignar reverses the direction, so that those who do such things are the ones who become great.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:98"},"k:976":{"book":"kural","num":976,"t":["சிறியார் உணர்ச்சியுள் இல்லை பெரியாரைப்","பேணிக் கொள் வேம் என்னும் நோக்கு"],"p":"It is never in the nature of the base to seek the society of the great and partake of their nature.","r":"The aim of cherishing and taking on the great does not exist in the minds of the small.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sirumai","perumai","kalvi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","They don't seek to serve the great and acquire their nature, since those proud of birth, wealth or learning think they already have it.","பெரியாரை வழிபட்டு அவர் இயல்பினையாம் கோடும்"],["manakkudavar","They don't seek to take the great as support, so having great men as allies is itself greatness.","இது பெரியாரைப் பெறுதலும் பெருமையென்று கூறிற்று."]],"ch":"k:98"},"k:977":{"book":"kural","num":977,"t":["இறப்பே புரிந்த தொழிற்றாம் சிறப்புந்தான்","சீரல் லவர்கண் படின்"],"p":"Even nobility of birth, wealth and learning, if in (the possession of) the base, will (only) produce everincreasing pride.","r":"Even distinction, should it fall to the unworthy, turns entirely into acts of excess.","n":"Distinction is morally neutral and amplifies whoever has it. Manakkudavar takes சிறப்பு as wealth, while Parimelazhagar takes it as the surplus of birth, wealth and learning.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["sirumai","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:98"},"k:978":{"book":"kural","num":978,"t":["பணியுமாம் என்றும் பெருமை சிறுமை","அணியுமாம் தன்னை வியந்து"],"p":"The great will always humble himself; but the mean will exalt himself in self-admiration.","r":"Greatness always bows, and smallness adorns itself in admiration of itself.","n":"பணியும் and அணியும் rhyme, so what looks like opposite motions sound alike. Parimelazhagar reports another reading in which this is a wonder at the way of the world: the high go low and the low go high.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","sirumai","panivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:98"},"k:979":{"book":"kural","num":979,"t":["பெருமை பெருமிதம் இன்மை சிறுமை","பெருமிதம் ஊர்ந்து விடல்"],"p":"Freedom from conceit is (the nature of true) greatness; (while) obstinacy therein is (that of) meanness.","r":"Greatness is the absence of pride, and smallness is riding on pride and being done with it.","n":"பெருமிதம், 'pride', shares its root with பெருமை, so the verse defines greatness by the absence of what sounds like its own excess. Manakkudavar marks this as where the chapter turns from negative points to defining greatness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","sirumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:98"},"k:980":{"book":"kural","num":980,"t":["அற்றம் மறைக்கும் பெருமை சிறுமைதான்","குற்றமே கூறி விடும்"],"p":"The great hide the faults of others; the base only divulge them.","r":"Greatness hides another's weakness, and smallness only tells their faults.","n":"அற்றம் is literally a vulnerable exposure, so this hides a nakedness as much as a fault. Silence, not achievement, finishes the portrait of greatness that opened with high aim.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","sirumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:98"},"k:981":{"book":"kural","num":981,"t":["கடன்என்ப நல்லவை எல்லாம் கடன்அறிந்து","சான்றாண்மை மேற்கொள் பவர்க்கு"],"p":"It is said that those who are conscious of their duty and behave with a perfect goodness will regard as natural all that is good.","r":"For those who know what is fitting and take on fullness of character, all that is good is said to be கடன்.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["saandraanmai","aram"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","கடன் means what is natural: all good qualities are inherent, otherwise the word சான்றாண்மை wouldn't apply.","நல்லனவாய குணங்கள் எல்லாம் இயல்பாயிருக்கும்"],["kalaignar","கடன் means duty, and all efforts made to fulfil it count as good duties.","நல்ல கடமைகள் என்றே கொள்ளப்படும்."]],"ch":"k:99"},"k:982":{"book":"kural","num":982,"t":["குணநலம் சான்றோர் நலனே பிறநலம்","எந்நலத்து உள்ளதூஉம் அன்று"],"p":"The only delight of the perfect is that of their goodness; all other (sensual) delights are not to be included among any (true) delights.","r":"The goodness of the worthy is the goodness of character; any other goodness isn't counted among goodnesses at all.","n":"Parimelazhagar takes பிறநலம் as bodily good looks, and says it doesn't even appear in the lists of goods such as birth and learning. Kalaignar also has அழகு. The verse's own பிற is open, and a stronger reading dismisses everything other than character.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saandraanmai","panpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:99"},"k:983":{"book":"kural","num":983,"t":["அன்புநாண் ஒப்புரவு கண்ணோட்டம் வாய்மையொடு","ஐந்துசால் ஊன்றிய தூண்"],"p":"Affection, fear (of sin), benevolence, favour and truthfulness; these are the five pillars on which perfect goodness rests.","r":"Love, shame, fellow-feeling, kindly regard and truthfulness: these five are the pillars planted to bear worth.","n":"Four of the five are about how one regards other people. Parimelazhagar stretches two of them: love extends beyond kin, and truth holds everywhere. Their absence makes worth fall, not merely diminish.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saandraanmai","naanam","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:99"},"k:984":{"book":"kural","num":984,"t":["கொல்லா நலத்தது நோன்மை பிறர்தீமை","சொல்லா நலத்தது சால்பு"],"p":"Penance consists in the goodness that kills not , and perfection in the goodness that tells not others' faults.","r":"Austerity has the goodness of not killing, and worth has the goodness of not telling others' evil.","n":"The verse sets speech at the level of the ascetic's non-violence. Talking of another's fault is thus the moral equivalent of what killing is for a tapas. Kalaignar makes the silence cover harm done by others, which leaves open how it fits with exposing wrongdoing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saandraanmai","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:99"},"k:985":{"book":"kural","num":985,"t":["ஆற்றுவார் ஆற்றல் பணிதல் அதுசான்றோர்","மாற்றாரை மாற்றும் படை"],"p":"Stooping (to inferiors) is the strength of those who can accomplish (an undertaking); and that is the weapon with which the great avert their foes.","r":"The strength of those who accomplish is to bow; that is the weapon by which the worthy turn foes around.","n":"படை and மாற்றாரை மாற்றும் use the language of war, and the pun is that a foe (மாற்றார்) gets changed (மாற்றும்), not destroyed. Parimelazhagar makes the bowing concrete and strategic: stooping to gather allies.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panivu","saandraanmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:99"},"k:986":{"book":"kural","num":986,"t":["சால்பிற்குக் கட்டளை யாதெனின் தோல்வி","துலையல்லார் கண்ணும் கொளல்"],"p":"The touch-stone of perfection is to receive a defeat even at the hands of one's inferiors.","r":"If asked what the touchstone of worth is, it is accepting defeat even from those who aren't one's equal.","n":"Parimelazhagar specifies that one accepts it while able to win. Without that, the verse describes mere loss. The defeat then lifts one above the other person, an inversion of the usual sense of victory.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saandraanmai","panivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:99"},"k:987":{"book":"kural","num":987,"t":["இன்னாசெய் தார்க்கும் இனியவே செய்யாக்கால்","என்ன பயத்ததோ சால்பு"],"p":"Of what avail is perfect goodness if it cannot do pleasing things even to those who have pained (it) ?","r":"If you don't do what is sweet even to those who have done you harm, what has worth brought?","n":"A demanding test is framed as a question about use: worth is judged by what it produces in the hardest case.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saandraanmai","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:99"},"k:988":{"book":"kural","num":988,"t":["இன்மை ஒருவற்கு இளிவன்று சால்பென்னும்","திண்மை உண் டாகப் பெறின்"],"p":"Poverty is no disgrace to one who abounds in good qualities.","r":"Poverty is no disgrace for one who has the firmness called worth.","n":"திண்மை makes worth a hardness able to bear pressure. The verse doesn't call poverty good, only that it doesn't carry the disgrace it otherwise would.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saandraanmai","selvam","maanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:99"},"k:989":{"book":"kural","num":989,"t":["ஊழி பெயரினும் தாம்பெயரார் சான்றாண்மைக்கு","ஆழி எனப்படு வார்"],"p":"Those who are said to be the shore of the sea of perfection will never change, though ages may change.","r":"Those called the ஆழி of worth won't shift even when the age shifts.","n":"ஊழி and ஆழி chime. The cosmic dissolution is a scale reference for constancy.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saandraanmai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","ஆழி means shore: worth is the sea, and they are what holds it in when other seas overflow.","சால்புடைமையாகிய கடற்குக் கரை என்று சொல்லப்படுவார்"],["kalaignar","ஆழி means sea: they stand as the sea that doesn't change when all seas are overturned.","தம்நிலை மாறாத கடலாகத் திகழ்வார்கள்."]],"ch":"k:99"},"k:990":{"book":"kural","num":990,"t":["சான்றவர் சான்றாண்மை குன்றின் இருநிலந்தான்","தாங்காது மன்னோ பொறை"],"p":"If there is a defect in the character of the perfect, (even) the great world cannot bear (its) burden.","r":"If the worthy decline in worth, the great earth itself will not bear its load.","n":"Parimelazhagar says both events are impossible, so this is a thought experiment showing their dependence. He also reports a reading in which the worthy are the earth's helpers in bearing. Kalaignar reads பொறை as the world's forbearance: it won't patiently tolerate their decline.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saandraanmai","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:99"},"k:991":{"book":"kural","num":991,"t":["எண்பதத்தால் எய்தல் எளிதென்ப யார்மாட்டும்","பண்புடைமை என்னும் வழக்கு"],"p":"If one is easy of access to all, it will be easy for one to obtain the virtue called goodness.","r":"They say the practice called courtesy is easy to reach through being accessible to everyone.","n":"வழக்கு makes courtesy a public custom, not an inner trait. Being available turns out to be the whole method, and யார்மாட்டும் gives it no exception. Parimelazhagar calls it something that wins over the whole world, which adds a strategic note.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panpu","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:100"},"k:992":{"book":"kural","num":992,"t":["அன்புடைமை ஆன்ற குடிப்பிறத்தல் இவ்விரண்டும்","பண்புடைமை என்னும் வழக்கு"],"p":"Affectionateness and birth in a good family, these two constitute what is called a proper behaviour to all.","r":"Love and being born in a seasoned family: these two are the way called courtesy.","n":"This puts birth among courtesy's sources. Parimelazhagar softens it, saying குடிப்பிறத்தல் here means the conduct of those so born, and neither factor suffices alone. Kalaignar goes further, asking only that one have the marks of such birth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panpu","kudi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:100"},"k:993":{"book":"kural","num":993,"t":["உறுப்பொத்தல் மக்களொப்பு அன்றால் வெறுத்தக்க","பண்பொத்தல் ஒப்பதாம் ஒப்பு"],"p":"Resemblance of bodies is no resemblance of souls; true resemblance is the resemblance of qualities that attract.","r":"Likeness of limbs isn't likeness among people; true likeness is likeness of settled character.","n":"வெறுத்தக்க usually means hateful, and Parimelazhagar glosses it as செறியத்தக்க, 'to be drawn close', which is what makes the line work. Pope's gloss, 'qualities that attract', follows him. I can't confirm this sense from the verse alone.","x":1,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panpu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The issue is likeness to good people: bodily likeness doesn't amount to that, because the body doesn't last and character does.","செறியத்தகாத உடம்பால் ஒத்தல் ஒருவனுக்கு நன்மக்களோடு ஒப்பாகாமையின்"],["kalaignar","The issue is membership of humankind: those without good character shouldn't be counted as people.","நற்பண்புகளால் ஒத்திருப்பவர்களே மக்கள் எனப்படுவர்."]],"ch":"k:100"},"k:994":{"book":"kural","num":994,"t":["யனொடு நன்றி புரிந்த பயனுடையார்","பண்புபா ராட்டும் உலகு"],"p":"The world applauds the character of those whose usefulness results from their equity and charity.","r":"The world honours the character of those whose useful lives come from doing good with fairness.","n":"The world honours the பண்பு, not the deeds or their outcome, which makes deeds evidence of character. Parimelazhagar says that it is useful to oneself and to others alike.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panpu","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:100"},"k:995":{"book":"kural","num":995,"t":["நகையுள்ளும் இன்னா திகழ்ச்சி பகையுள்ளும்","பண்புள பாடறிவார் மாட்டு"],"p":"Reproach is painful to one even in sport; those (therefore) who know the nature of others exhibit (pleasing) qualities even when they are hated.","r":"Scorn hurts even in jest, so those who know how others feel have courtesy even in enmity.","n":"Parimelazhagar insists that the two clauses are one argument, and criticizes others who read them apart: knowing that scorn hurts is the reason for courtesy. பாடு here is what another person undergoes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panpu","sirumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:100"},"k:996":{"book":"kural","num":996,"t":["பண்புடையார்ப் பட்டுண்டு உலகம் அதுஇன்றேல்","மண்புக்கு மாய்வது மன்"],"p":"The (way of the) world subsists by contact with the good; if not, it would bury itself in the earth and perish.","r":"The world exists by resting upon those of courtesy; otherwise it would enter the earth and perish.","n":"Parimelazhagar makes உலகம் here the way of the world, not the planet. Kalaignar makes it a should: worldly practices ought to depend on such people. A claim about reality becomes a recommendation.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["panpu","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:100"},"k:997":{"book":"kural","num":997,"t":["அரம்போலும் கூர்மைய ரேனும் மரம்போல்வர்","மக்கட்பண்பு இல்லா தவர்"],"p":"He who is destitute of (true) human qualities (only) resembles a tree, though he may possess the sharpness of a file.","r":"Even though sharp as a file, those without human courtesy are like trees.","n":"The chime of அரம் and மரம் has the point in it. Parimelazhagar says the file wears down what it touches without wearing itself, and the tree has only one sense. Intelligence used without courtesy isn't merely inadequate, it is destructive.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panpu","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:100"},"k:998":{"book":"kural","num":998,"t":["நண்பாற்றார் ஆகி நயமில செய்வார்க்கும்","பண்பாற்றார் ஆதல் கடை"],"p":"It is wrong (for the wise) not to exhibit (good) qualities even towards those who bearing no friendship (for them) do only what is hateful.","r":"Failing in courtesy, even toward those who act without friendship and without kindness, is lowness.","n":"Parimelazhagar's reason is reflexive: respond in kind and you become like them. The insult harms the character of the one who returns it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panpu","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:100"},"k:999":{"book":"kural","num":999,"t":["நகல்வல்லர் அல்லார்க்கு மாயிரு ஞாலம்","பகலும்பாற் பட்டன்று இருள்"],"p":"To those who cannot rejoice, the wide world is buried darkness even in (broad) day light.","r":"For those who aren't capable of laughing, the great wide world lies in darkness even in the daytime.","n":"Laughter is treated as a skill (வல்லர்), and the lack of it as blindness. Parimelazhagar makes it the joy of mingling, and says such people don't understand the world because they can't mix with it. He reports a variant reading too.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panpu","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:100"},"k:1000":{"book":"kural","num":1000,"t":["பண்பிலான் பெற்ற பெருஞ்செல்வம் நன்பால்","கலந்தீமை யால்திரிந் தற்று"],"p":"The great wealth obtained by one who has no goodness will perish like pure milk spoilt by the impurity of the vessel.","r":"Great wealth obtained by one without courtesy is like good milk spoiled by the fault of its vessel.","n":"Wealth is good milk here, and the fault is the holder's alone. பெற்ற, 'obtained', suggests to Parimelazhagar that he couldn't have created it himself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panpu","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:100"},"k:1001":{"book":"kural","num":1001,"t":["வைத்தான்வாய் சான்ற பெரும்பொருள் அஃதுண்ணான்","செத்தான் செயக்கிடந்தது இல்"],"p":"He who does not enjoy the immense riches he has heaped up in his house, is (to be reckoned as) dead, (for) there is nothing achieved (by him).","r":"One who has stored up great wealth to the brim and doesn't eat it, is dead: there is nothing left for him to do.","n":"உண்ணான், 'doesn't eat', treats wealth as food. Parimelazhagar widens it to the five senses.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["selvam","eegai"],"pos":[["manakkudavar","The man who does not use it is himself dead, and has nothing further to do with it.","அதனை நுகரானாயின், செத்தான்; அவன் பின்பு செய்யக்கிடந்தது யாது மில்லை."],["parimelazhagar","An alternative he allows: once dead, one has no right over it, so enjoy it as soon as it comes.","வையாது பெற்றபொழுதே நுகர்க"],["kalaignar","It is about a literal death: what good is the wealth to one who dies without enjoying it?","அதனை அனுபவிக்காமல் செத்துப்போகிறவனுக்கு."]],"ch":"k:101"},"k:1002":{"book":"kural","num":1002,"t":["பொருளானாம் எல்லாமென்று ஈயாது இவறும்","மருளானாம் மாணாப் பிறப்பு"],"p":"He who knows that wealth yields every pleasure and yet is so blind as to lead miserly life will be born a demon.","r":"Through the delusion of clinging without giving, thinking all things come by wealth, comes a மாணாப் பிறப்பு.","n":"The delusion isn't the belief that wealth achieves everything, a point the Kural makes elsewhere: it lies in clinging while holding that belief.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["selvam","eegai","piravi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It means rebirth as a ghost that hungers amid food, matching those who watched others' hunger amid plenty.","நிறைதலில்லாத பேய்ப்பிறப்பு உண்டாம்."],["kalaignar","It describes a person: he becomes a low being with no distinction, with no rebirth involved.","எந்தச் சிறப்புமில்லாத இழி பிறவியாவான்."]],"ch":"k:101"},"k:1003":{"book":"kural","num":1003,"t":["ஈட்டம் இவறி இசைவேண்டா ஆடவர்","தோற்றம் நிலக்குப் பொறை"],"p":"A burden to the earth are men bent on the acquisition of riches and not (true) fame.","r":"The appearance on earth of men who cling to accumulation and don't want renown is a burden to the land.","n":"Parimelazhagar identifies the renown with virtue achieved by giving, so the choice isn't between wealth and vanity. Manakkudavar draws the harshest conclusion: it would be better if they hadn't been born.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:101"},"k:1004":{"book":"kural","num":1004,"t":["எச்சமென்று என்எண்ணுங் கொல்லோ ஒருவரால்","நச்சப் படாஅ தவன்"],"p":"What will the miser who is not liked (by any one) regard as his own (in the world to come) ?","r":"What does one wanted by no one imagine will remain behind him?","n":"The issue is what survives a person, and the question implies nothing does, since fame only rests on givers. Pope's gloss moves this to 'the world to come' while his couplet does not. The Tamil says எச்சம், what is left behind.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["selvam","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:101"},"k:1005":{"book":"kural","num":1005,"t":["கொடுப்பதூஉம் துய்ப்பதூஉம் இல்லார்க்கு அடுக்கிய","கோடியுண் டாயினும் இல்"],"p":"Those who neither give (to others) nor enjoy (their property) are (truly) destitute, though possessing immense riches.","r":"Those who neither give nor enjoy have nothing, even with crores upon crores.","n":"An ownership paradox: possession is defined by use. Parimelazhagar says giving is named first because virtue outranks pleasure. Kalaignar fuses the two: those not happy in giving.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:101"},"k:1006":{"book":"kural","num":1006,"t":["ஏதம் பெருஞ்செல்வம் தான்துவ்வான் தக்கார்க்கொன்று","ஈதல் இயல்பிலா தான்"],"p":"He who enjoys not (his riches) nor relieves the wants of the worthy is a disease to his wealth.","r":"One who doesn't use it himself and doesn't naturally give any of it to the deserving is a misfortune with great wealth.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","eegai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The man is a disease of the wealth, which could have done both things.","இரண்டும் செய்தற்கு இடனுடைத்தாய செல்வத்திற்கு ஒரு நோய்."],["manakkudavar","The great wealth obtained by such a person is itself faulty.","பெற்ற பெருஞ் செல்வம் குற்றமுடைத்து"]],"ch":"k:101"},"k:1007":{"book":"kural","num":1007,"t":["அற்றார்க்கொன்று ஆற்றாதான் செல்வம் மிகநலம்","பெற்றாள் தமியள்மூத் தற்று"],"p":"The wealth of him who never bestows anything on the destitute is like a woman of beauty growing old without a husband.","r":"The wealth of one who gives nothing to the destitute is like a woman of exceeding beauty growing old alone.","n":"The simile mourns what is wasted, and treats a woman's worth as realized through a husband. Parimelazhagar's gloss deepens it: she is unmarried for want of anyone to give her away, a giver being missing in both halves, and she herself loses something too.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","eegai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:101"},"k:1008":{"book":"kural","num":1008,"t":["நச்சப் படாதவன் செல்வம் நடுவூருள்","நச்சு மரம்பழுத் தற்று"],"p":"The wealth of him who is disliked (by all) is like the fruit-bearing of the etty tree in the midst of a town.","r":"The wealth of one who isn't wanted is a poison tree bearing fruit in the middle of town.","n":"நச்சப்படாதவன், 'unwanted', and நச்சு, 'poison', chime, and it is what makes the verse work. Being close by is what makes it worse: Parimelazhagar says he is near the needy and still gives nothing. Manakkudavar extends it, saying any who eat out of desire will die.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:101"},"k:1009":{"book":"kural","num":1009,"t":["அன்பொரீஇத் தற்செற்று அறநோக்காது ஈட்டிய","ஒண்பொருள் கொள்வார் பிறர்"],"p":"Strangers will inherit the riches that have been acquired without regard for friendship, comfort and charity.","r":"Others will take the bright wealth amassed by giving up love, by self-denial and by ignoring virtue.","n":"The list gives three prices paid, and the last word takes away the prize. Parimelazhagar says பிறர் excludes even his own line. Kalaignar makes ignoring virtue into unethical earning, which is a different charge from not giving.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:101"},"k:1010":{"book":"kural","num":1010,"t":["சீருடைச் செல்வர் சிறுதுனி மாரி","வறங்கூர்ந் தனையது உடைத்து"],"p":"The short-lived poverty of those who are noble and rich is like the clouds becoming poor (for a while).","r":"The சிறுதுனி of the renowned rich has the nature of rain-clouds going dry.","n":"As a closing verse it gives the positive pole of the chapter's whole contrast: the wealth that does give.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","eegai","pugazh"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","சிறுதுனி is brief poverty: those who gave everything soon recover, like a cloud. He rejects the other reading as not suited to this chapter.","நிற்கும் காலம் சிறிதாய வறுமை"],["manakkudavar","சிறுதுனி is slight displeasure that stops their giving, and the world suffers as with failed rain.","சீருடைய செல்வர் சிறுதுனியால் ஈயாதொழிதல், மாரி பெய் யாமை மிக்காற் போலும்"]],"ch":"k:101"},"k:1011":{"book":"kural","num":1011,"t":["கருமத்தால் நாணுதல் நாணுந் திருநுதல்","நல்லவர் நாணுப் பிற"],"p":"True modesty is the fear of (evil) deeds; all other modesty is (simply) the bashfulness of virtuous maids.","r":"Shame is being ashamed through one's actions; anything other is the shame of women of the bright brow.","n":"The verse separates moral shame from bodily modesty and assigns the second to women. Whichever women are meant, the distinction is gendered.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The women are honoured family women, and he rejects explicitly the reading that they are courtesans.","அழகிய நுதலினையுடைய குலமகளிர் நாண்கள்."],["manakkudavar","They are courtesans, good only in their brow, so that any other shame is merely like theirs.","அழகிய நுதலினாலே நல்லாராகிய கணிகையர் நாணத்தோ டொக்கும்."]],"ch":"k:102"},"k:1012":{"book":"kural","num":1012,"t":["ஊணுடை எச்சம் உயிர்க்கெல்லாம் வேறல்ல","நாணுடைமை மாந்தர் சிறப்பு"],"p":"Food, clothing and the like are common to all men but modesty is peculiar to the good.","r":"Food, clothing and the rest are no different for any living being, and the distinction of humans is having shame.","n":"The verse seems to separate humans from other life, but the commentators draw the line within humanity. Parimelazhagar contrasts all people with good people, and Manakkudavar contrasts base people with leaders. Parimelazhagar lists the rest of what is shared as sleep, fear and desire.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","panpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:102"},"k:1013":{"book":"kural","num":1013,"t":["ஊனைக் குறித்த உயிரெல்லாம் நாண்என்னும்","நன்மை குறித்தது சால்பு"],"p":"As the body is the abode of the spirit, so the excellence of modesty is the abode of perfection.","r":"As all lives are fixed on flesh, worth is fixed on the good called shame.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["naanam","saandraanmai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","ஊன் is the body: worth can't have effect without shame, as a soul can't without a body. He reports ஊண் as a variant.","உயிர் உடம்பொடு கூடியல்லது பயனெய்தாத வாறுபோலச் சால்பு நாணோடு கூடியல்லது பயன் எய்தாது"],["manakkudavar","The word is food: living things want food above all, and worth wants shame above all.","பலவகை உயிரும் மேற்கூறிய எல்லாவற்றினும் உண்டியைக் கருதிற்று"]],"ch":"k:102"},"k:1014":{"book":"kural","num":1014,"t":["அணிஅன்றோ நாணுடைமை சான்றோர்க்கு அஃதின்றேல்","பிணிஅன்றோ பீடு நடை"],"p":"Is not the modesty ornament of the noble ? Without it, their haughtiness would be a pain (to others).","r":"Isn't shame an ornament to the worthy? Without it, isn't a proud walk a disease?","n":"அணி and பிணி rhyme. Parimelazhagar makes this a disease for those who have to watch it. A walk, in other words, is public property and judged by the watcher.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["naanam","saandraanmai","noy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:102"},"k:1015":{"book":"kural","num":1015,"t":["பிறர்பழியும் தம்பழியும் நாணுவார் நாணுக்கு","உறைபதி என்னும் உலகு"],"p":"The world regards as the abode of modesty him who fear his own and other's guilt.","r":"The world calls those who feel shame for others' disgrace as for their own the dwelling of shame.","n":"This enlarges shame beyond guilt: one feels it for what one did not do. Moral concern here can't be reduced to self-regard.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:102"},"k:1016":{"book":"kural","num":1016,"t":["நாண்வேலி கொள்ளாது மன்னோ வியன்ஞாலம்","பேணலர் மேலா யவர்"],"p":"The great make modesty their barrier (of defence) and not the wide world.","r":"The high don't care for the wide world unless they have shame for a fence.","n":"The verse can mean they would take shame rather than the world, or that they won't want the world without it; Parimelazhagar holds the first and reports the second. Pope's couplet follows the second. Kalaignar has shame as the best of protections, with no choice involved.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:102"},"k:1017":{"book":"kural","num":1017,"t":["நாணால் உயிரைத் துறப்பர் உயிர்ப்பொருட்டால்","நாண்துறவார் நாணாள் பவர்"],"p":"The modest would rather lose their life for the sake of modesty than lose modesty for the sake of life.","r":"Those ruled by shame will give up life for shame, and won't give up shame for life.","n":"The syntax folds on itself like the choice it describes. நாணாள்பவர் can be 'those who wield shame', as a commander does, and not those who are ruled by it. Kalaignar glosses the object as மானம், showing how closely the two virtues run.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","maanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:102"},"k:1018":{"book":"kural","num":1018,"t":["பிறர்நாணத் தக்கது தான்நாணா னாயின்","அறம்நாணத் தக்கது உடைத்து"],"p":"Virtue is likely to forsake him who shamelessly does what others are ashamed of.","r":"If one is not ashamed of what makes others ashamed, it's a fault that virtue itself would be ashamed of.","n":"நாண recurs and virtue becomes a person who blushes. Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar read this as virtue leaving such a man, since virtue doesn't approach the shameless, so shame is a precondition of virtue and not one of its parts.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["naanam","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:102"},"k:1019":{"book":"kural","num":1019,"t":["குலஞ்சுடும் கொள்கை பிழைப்பின் நலஞ்சுடும்","நாணின்மை நின்றக் கடை"],"p":"Want of manners injures one's family; but want of modesty injures one's character.","r":"A lapse in principle burns one's lineage, and lasting shamelessness burns every good thing.","n":"Parimelazhagar draws the ranking out: destroying shame is worse than destroying conduct, which is significant given the rank chapter 14 gives conduct. Fire (சுடும்) is used for both.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","ozhukkam","kudi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:102"},"k:1020":{"book":"kural","num":1020,"t":["நாண்அகத் தில்லார் இயக்கம் மரப்பாவை","நாணால் உயிர்மருட்டி அற்று"],"p":"The actions of those who are without modesty at heart are like those of puppet moved by a string.","r":"The movements of those with no shame within are like a wooden puppet feigning life by its string.","n":"The pun is on நாண், which means both shame and string. The puppet has the one thing the man lacks, as Parimelazhagar notes, in a neat inversion. It leaves a man without shame to be moved from outside.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["naanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:102"},"k:1021":{"book":"kural","num":1021,"t":["கருமம் செயஒருவன் கைதூவேன் என்னும்","பெருமையின் பீடுடையது இல்"],"p":"There is no higher greatness than that of one saying. I will not cease in my effort (to raise my family).","r":"No greatness has more dignity than that of one who says 'I will not stay my hand from the work'.","n":"The verse says only கருமம், and family comes from the chapter's title. Parimelazhagar rejects two rival readings, one's own work and others' work, the second because serving others isn't leadership. Greatness here sits in the declaration (என்னும்), before any result.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kudi","muyarchi","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:103"},"k:1022":{"book":"kural","num":1022,"t":["ஆள்வினையும் ஆன்ற அறிவும் எனஇரண்டின்","நீள்வினையால் நீளும் குடி"],"p":"One's family is raised by untiring perseverance in both effort and wise contrivances.","r":"By long-continued work in the two things called effort and mature knowledge, the family is extended.","n":"நீள்வினையால் நீளும் is a chime, in which the long labour lengthens the line.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kudi","muyarchi","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","குடி is one's own family, and it rises.","ஒருவன் குடி உயரும்."],["kalaignar","குடி is the people attached to him, whose pride rises.","அவனைச் சேர்ந்துள்ள குடிமக்களின் பெருமை உயரும்."]],"ch":"k:103"},"k:1023":{"book":"kural","num":1023,"t":["குடிசெய்வல் என்னும் ஒருவற்குத் தெய்வம்","மடிதற்றுத் தான்முந் துறும்"],"p":"The Deity will clothe itself and appear before him who resolves on raising his family.","r":"For one who says 'I will raise my people', தெய்வம் tucks up its garment and goes ahead on its own.","n":"The image of tightened dress is homely and physical: the divine as a worker who gets ready to help.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","muyarchi","oozh"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The deity is said to lead, and leading is an act ascribed to destiny (நியதி).","முன் நடப்பார் செயல் நியதிமேல் ஏற்பட்டது."],["kalaignar","What is called divine action is the power of nature.","தெய்வச் செயல் எனக்கூறப்படும் இயற்கையின் ஆற்றல்கூட"]],"ch":"k:103"},"k:1024":{"book":"kural","num":1024,"t":["சூழாமல் தானே முடிவெய்தும் தம்குடியைத்","தாழாது உஞற்று பவர்க்கு"],"p":"Those who are prompt in their efforts (to better their family) need no deliberation, such efforts will of themselves succee d.","r":"For those who work without delay for their people, the end arrives by itself, without deliberation.","n":"The agent is unnamed. Parimelazhagar supplies the deity of 1023, while Manakkudavar supplies the man's good deeds (நல்வினை), and Kalaignar leaves success to come by itself. Each fills the gap to suit his view.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","muyarchi","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:103"},"k:1025":{"book":"kural","num":1025,"t":["குற்றம் இலனாய்க் குடிசெய்து வாழ்வானைச்","சுற்றமாச் சுற்றும் உலகு"],"p":"People will eagerly seek the friendship of the prosperous soul who has raised his family without foul means.","r":"The world gathers around one who lives faultlessly raising his people, as kin.","n":"The chime of சுற்றம் and சுற்றும் turns the noun into what kin do. Parimelazhagar's motive for this is unsentimental: people come for their own benefit.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:103"},"k:1026":{"book":"kural","num":1026,"t":["நல்லாண்மை என்பது ஒருவற்குத் தான்பிறந்த","இல்லாண்மை ஆக்கிக் கொளல்"],"p":"A man's true manliness consists in making himself the head and benefactor of his family.","r":"What's called good manliness is making oneself capable of ruling the house one was born to.","n":"நல்லாண்மை and இல்லாண்மை chime. Parimelazhagar says the adjective நல் is there to exclude the warrior's manliness. Kalaignar reverses the sense: one who rules well brings pride to his birth-family, so it isn't about ruling the family.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aanmai","kudi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:103"},"k:1027":{"book":"kural","num":1027,"t":["அமரகத்து வன்கண்ணர் போலத் தமரகத்தும்","ஆற்றுவார் மேற்றே பொறை"],"p":"Like heroes in the battle-field, the burden (of protection etc.) is borne by those who are the most efficient in a family.","r":"As with the stout-hearted in battle, the burden among kin falls on the capable.","n":"The commentators stress that many enter the field and many are born in a family: it isn't that others are forbidden, but that it falls to whoever can carry it. A question of ability, not of seniority.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","aanmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:103"},"k:1028":{"book":"kural","num":1028,"t":["குடிசெய்வார்க் கில்லை பருவம் மடிசெய்து","மானங் கருதக் கெடும்"],"p":"As a family suffers by (one's) indolence and false dignity there is to be so season (good or bad) to those who strive to raise their family.","r":"Those who raise their people have no season; if they idle and think about மானம், it will be ruined.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","maanam","muyarchi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","மானம் is the pride of asking 'why must I alone suffer while the rest are comfortable?'","'இக்குடியிலுள்ளார் யாவரும் இன்பமுற இக்காலத்துத் துன்பமுறுவேன் யானோ?'"],["kalaignar","It is being shaken by degrading attacks made on oneself.","தன்மீது நடத்தப்படும் இழிவான தாக்குதலைக் கண்டு கலங்கினாலோ"]],"ch":"k:103"},"k:1029":{"book":"kural","num":1029,"t":["இடும்பைக்கே கொள்கலம் கொல்லோ குடும்பத்தைக்","குற்ற மறைப்பான் உடம்பு"],"p":"Is it only to suffering that his body is exposed who undertakes to preserve his family from evil ?","r":"Is the body of one who covers his household against fault only a vessel for suffering?","n":"Parimelazhagar treats this as a pointed remark and not self-pity: the man accepts it, since the family's rise is his reward.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","noy"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","மறைத்தல் is shielding, as with a shield against spears: he protects them from the three kinds of suffering.","மூவகைத் துன்பமும் உறற்பாலதாய தன் குடியை அவை உறாமற்காக்க முயல்வானது உடம்பு"],["manakkudavar","மறைத்தல் is concealing the failings found among kin.","சுற்றத்தார் மாட்டு உளதாகிய குறையை மறைக்கக் கருதுவான் உடம்பு"]],"ch":"k:103"},"k:1030":{"book":"kural","num":1030,"t":["இடுக்கண்கால் கொன்றிட வீழும் அடுத்தூன்றும்","நல்லாள் இலாத குடி"],"p":"If there are none to prop up and maintain a family (in distress), it will fall at the stroke of the axe of misfortune.","r":"When trouble cuts it at the foot, a family with no good person to prop it will fall.","n":"A tree image is implied: Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar supply the axe. They take நல்லாள் as a good man (ஆண்மகன்), while Kalaignar has anyone with the ability. The same word names a personified good woman in 924 and 1040; here it divides as நல் ஆள், 'good person', which doesn't itself specify a man.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","noy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:103"},"k:1031":{"book":"kural","num":1031,"t":["சுழன்றும்ஏர்ப் பின்னது உலகம் அதனால்","உழந்தும் உழவே தலை"],"p":"Agriculture, though laborious, is the most excellent (form of labour); for people, though they go about (in search of various employments), have at last to resort to the farmer.","r":"Though it whirls about, the world follows the plough; therefore, though toilsome, farming is foremost.","n":"The verse concedes the toil and doesn't romanticize it (உழந்தும்). Its case is a structural one: all other paths end up at the same place. சுழன்றும் may also suggest the world turning like a wheel behind the plough, and Kalaignar's version keeps this.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uzhavu","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:104"},"k:1032":{"book":"kural","num":1032,"t":["உழுவார் உலகத்தார்க்கு ஆணிஅஃ தாற்றாது","எழுவாரை எல்லாம் பொறுத்து"],"p":"Agriculturists are (as it were) the linch-pin of the world for they support all other workers who cannot till the soil.","r":"Those who plough are the linchpin of the world's people, for they carry all those who can't do it and rise to other things.","n":"Parimelazhagar says உழுவார் extends to those who have others plough for them, citing a parallel, which brings landowners into the honour. The verse doesn't say so. He reports a variant, தொழுவாரே, in which those unable to plough cringe to their patrons, which anticipates 1033.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["uzhavu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:104"},"k:1033":{"book":"kural","num":1033,"t":["உழுதுண்டு வாழ்வாரே வாழ்வார்மற் றெல்லாம்","தொழுதுண்டு பின்செல் பவர்"],"p":"They alone live who live by agriculture; all others lead a cringing, dependent life.","r":"Only those who live by eating from their own ploughing truly live; all others bow to eat, and walk behind.","n":"Parimelazhagar's வாழ்வார் are those who belong to themselves (தமக்குரியராய்): the verse is about independence as much as food.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uzhavu","maanam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The rest bow to other patrons and live on what they are given.","பிறரைத் தொழுது, அவர் சில கொடுப்பத் தம் உயிரோம்பி"],["kalaignar","The rest bow to the farmers themselves.","மற்றவர்கள் அவர்களைத் தொழுதுண்டு வாழ வேண்டியிருக்கிறது."]],"ch":"k:104"},"k:1034":{"book":"kural","num":1034,"t":["பலகுடை நீழலும் தங்குடைக்கீழ்க் காண்பர்","அலகுடை நீழ லவர்"],"p":"Patriotic farmers desire to bring all other states under the control of their own king.","r":"Those with the shade of grain-ears will see many umbrellas' shade under their own umbrella.","n":"The royal umbrella is set against the grain's shade. Manakkudavar notices a pun: an 'umbrella-less shade'. The verse claims that farming, not armies, underwrites conquest.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uzhavu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The umbrella is that of their king, whose conquests their grain makes possible.","பலவேந்தர் - குடை நிழலதாய மண் முழுதினையும் தம் வேந்தர்குடைக்கீழே காண்பர்."],["kalaignar","Farmers themselves have the strength to bring all governments under them.","தமது குடைநிழலின் கீழ் கொண்டு வரும் வலிமை பெற்றவர்கள் உழவர்கள்."]],"ch":"k:104"},"k:1035":{"book":"kural","num":1035,"t":["இரவார் இரப்பார்க்கொன்று ஈவர் கரவாது","கைசெய்தூண் மாலை யவர்"],"p":"Those whose nature is to live by manual labour will never beg but give something to those who beg.","r":"Those whose way is to eat what their hands produce don't ask, and give to those who ask without hiding.","n":"The hand is the verse's key: it labours and it gives, and it never stretches out. Parimelazhagar restricts கைசெய் to ploughing, while Kalaignar makes it any self-earned work. The verse anticipates the chapters on begging, whose word கரவாது it uses.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uzhavu","eegai","iravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:104"},"k:1036":{"book":"kural","num":1036,"t":["உழவினார் கைம்மடங்கின் இல்லை விழைவதூஉம்","விட்டேம்என் பார்க்கும் நிலை"],"p":"If the farmer's hands are slackened, even the ascetic state will fail.","r":"If farmers fold their hands, even those who say they have given up desire won't be able to stay in that state.","n":"என்பார், 'those who say', carries a slight edge. Parimelazhagar extends the verse to all of this world's aims, the next world's and liberation. He and another reading differ on whether விழைவது is food or desire in general.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["uzhavu","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:104"},"k:1037":{"book":"kural","num":1037,"t":["தொடிப்புழுதி கஃசா உணக்கின் பிடித்தெருவும்","வேண்டாது சாலப் படும்"],"p":"If the land is dried so as to reduce one ounce of earth to a quarter, it will grow plentifully even without a handful of manure.","r":"If you dry the soil so that a measure of earth falls to a quarter, it will grow abundantly without even a handful of manure.","n":"A rare place where the Kural gives technical advice, with exact weights. Kalaignar reads the reduction as coming from repeated ploughing, while Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar have sun-drying. The moral of attention before input is left unstated.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uzhavu","alavu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:104"},"k:1038":{"book":"kural","num":1038,"t":["ஏரினும் நன்றால் எருவிடுதல் கட்டபின்","நீரினும் நன்றதன் காப்பு"],"p":"Manuring is better than ploughing; after weeding, watching is better than watering (it).","r":"Manuring is better than ploughing, and after weeding, guarding is better than watering.","n":"A ladder of care in which each stage beats the previous one, and watchfulness comes out above water, the thing plants most obviously need. Parimelazhagar sets out the sequence of five.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uzhavu","alavu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:104"},"k:1039":{"book":"kural","num":1039,"t":["செல்லான் கிழவன் இருப்பின் நிலம்புலந்து","இல்லாளின் ஊடி விடும்"],"p":"If the owner does not (personally) attend to his cultivation, his land will behave like an angry wife and yield him no pleasure.","r":"If the owner stays away and doesn't go, the land will resent and sulk like a wife.","n":"ஊடி is a term from love poetry, used here in farm advice. கிழவன், 'owner', also means husband, a double meaning that makes the comparison work. Parimelazhagar says that the owner must go himself and not send others, which is in some tension with his widening of உழுவார் in 1032.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uzhavu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:104"},"k:1040":{"book":"kural","num":1040,"t":["இலமென்று அசைஇ இருப்பாரைக் காணின்","நிலமென்னும் நல்லாள் நகும்"],"p":"The maiden, Earth, will laug h at the sight of those who plead poverty and lead an idle life.","r":"Seeing those who say 'we have nothing' and sit idle, the good woman called Land laughs.","n":"The Earth doesn't scold but laughs. Parimelazhagar notes this is the bridge to the next chapter on poverty, and he has a variant இரப்பாரை, 'those who beg', which Manakkudavar uses, pointing to the later chapters too.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uzhavu","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:104"},"k:1041":{"book":"kural","num":1041,"t":["இன்மையின் இன்னாதது யாதெனின் இன்மையின்","இன்மையே இன்னா தது"],"p":"There is nothing that afflicts (one) like poverty.","r":"If asked what is more painful than poverty, what's more painful than poverty is poverty.","n":"The sentence tries to find a comparison and fails. As Parimelazhagar says, if it has no equal, nothing can exceed it. Tautology is the argument.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:105"},"k:1042":{"book":"kural","num":1042,"t":["இன்மை எனவொரு பாவி மறுமையும்","இம்மையும் இன்றி வரும்"],"p":"When cruel poverty comes on, it deprives one of both the present and future (bliss).","r":"The sinner called poverty comes, with this world and the next both lost.","n":"பாவி personifies it as an evil actor, although Manakkudavar reads a poor person who has the sin. The next world is lost, in his reason, because one can't afford charity, so poverty denies access to virtue itself. That cuts against the assumption that merit is equally open to all.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:105"},"k:1043":{"book":"kural","num":1043,"t":["தொல்வரவும் தோலும் கெடுக்கும் தொகையாக","நல்குரவு என்னும் நசை"],"p":"Hankering poverty destroys at once the greatness of (one's) ancient descent and (the dignity of one's) speech.","r":"The desire called poverty destroys at once one's ancient descent and தோல்.","n":"The verse identifies poverty with desire, நசை. Parimelazhagar explains that where there's no craving there's no poverty, which is a remarkable reframing of an economic state as a mental one.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai","kudi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","தோல் means graceful speech, as Tolkappiyam uses it, and he rejects reading it as the body.","பழைய குடிவரவினையும் அதற்கு ஏற்ற சொல்லினையும் ஒருங்கே கெடுக்கும்."],["manakkudavar","தோல் means beauty of form.","குடிப்பிறப்பினையும் வடிவழகினையும் ஒருங்கு கெடுக்கும்"],["kalaignar","தோல் means fame, and the desire is a greed that poverty causes.","பரம்பரைப் பெருமையையும், புகழையும் ஒரு சேரக் கெடுத்துவிடும்."]],"ch":"k:105"},"k:1044":{"book":"kural","num":1044,"t":["இற்பிறந்தார் கண்ணேயும் இன்மை இளிவந்த","சொற்பிறக்கும் சோர்வு தரும்"],"p":"Even in those of high birth, poverty will produce the fault of uttering mean words.","r":"Even in the well-born, poverty brings the lapse from which degrading words are born.","n":"Parimelazhagar says what the words are: 'you must give to us'. He describes the lapse precisely: at the height of suffering, they forget their birth for a moment. The uncontrolled word is இளிவந்த, degrading, and that is the verdict of chapter 97 on such a person.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai","kudi","maanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:105"},"k:1045":{"book":"kural","num":1045,"t":["நல்குரவு என்னும் இடும்பையுள் பல்குரைத்","துன்பங்கள் சென்று படும்"],"p":"The misery of poverty brings in its train many (more) miseries.","r":"Within the suffering called poverty, many sufferings arrive and settle.","n":"Parimelazhagar lists a day's humiliations in order: going to the rich man's door, seeing him, being refused, accepting what he gives, and working out how to use it. One of the most vivid passages in his commentary, it is his own and not the verse's.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:105"},"k:1046":{"book":"kural","num":1046,"t":["நற்பொருள் நன்குணர்ந்து சொல்லினும் நல்கூர்ந்தார்","சொற்பொருள் சோர்வு படும்"],"p":"The words of the poor are profitless, though they may be sound in thought and clear in expression.","r":"Even if they speak of good matters, fully understood, the words and meaning of the poor go slack.","n":"Parimelazhagar gives a social mechanism and not a cognitive one: nobody listens, in case they come to feel obliged to help. So the meaning fails not in the speaker but in what the listeners are afraid of.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai","arivu","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:105"},"k:1047":{"book":"kural","num":1047,"t":["அறஞ்சாரா நல்குரவு ஈன்றதா யானும்","பிறன்போல நோக்கப் படும்"],"p":"He that is reduced to absolute poverty will be regarded as a stranger even by his own mother.","r":"One in poverty unconnected with virtue is looked at as a stranger, even by the mother who bore him.","n":"The mother is again the limit case, as in 923. Parimelazhagar's reason is unsentimental: she fears that he would be taking and not giving.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai","aram"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","அறஞ்சாரா describes the poverty: it doesn't come from virtue nor yield virtue.","காரண காரியங்களுள் ஒன்றானும் இயையாமை."],["kalaignar","It describes the man, who has left the path of virtue because of poverty.","அறநெறியிலிருந்து விலகி நிற்பவனை"]],"ch":"k:105"},"k:1048":{"book":"kural","num":1048,"t":["இன்றும் வருவது கொல்லோ நெருநலும்","கொன்றது போலும் நிரப்பு"],"p":"Is the poverty that almost killed me yesterday, to meet me today too ?","r":"Will it come today too, the poverty that as good as killed me yesterday?","n":"The verse switches to first-person speech. Parimelazhagar imagines the speaker: someone who with great difficulty filled his belly yesterday. In போலும் he survived what felt like death, which means he will face it again.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:105"},"k:1049":{"book":"kural","num":1049,"t":["நெருப்பினுள் துஞ்சலும் ஆகும் நிரப்பினுள்","யாதொன்றும் கண்பாடு அரிது"],"p":"One may sleep in the midst of fire; but by no means in the midst of poverty.","r":"One can even sleep in the middle of fire, but in the middle of poverty, there is no way of closing the eyes.","n":"Parimelazhagar explains the sleep in fire as a possibility through mantra and medicine, which rationalizes what looks like hyperbole. He hears the same speaker as in 1048.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai","noy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:105"},"k:1050":{"book":"kural","num":1050,"t":["துப்புர வில்லார் துவரத் துறவாமை","உப்பிற்கும் காடிக்கும் கூற்று"],"p":"The destitute poor, who do not renounce their bodies, only consume their neighbour's salt and water.","r":"If those without means don't fully renounce, they are death to salt and to gruel.","n":"The mockery falls on tiny things, salt and sour gruel: the poor person's mere continuing destroys these and nothing more.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai","piravi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","To renounce fully is to give up the body itself, for honour's sake; those left by kin have done everything else.","நின்ற தம் உடம்பினையும் துறத்தல்."],["manakkudavar","It is to give up attachment to wealth, which brings pleasure to the poor.","இது துறப்பாராயின், இன்ப முறலா மென்று கூறிற்று."]],"ch":"k:105"},"k:1051":{"book":"kural","num":1051,"t":["இரக்க இரத்தக்கார்க் காணின் கரப்பின்","அவர்பழி தம்பழி அன்று"],"p":"If you meet with those that may be begged of, you may beg; (but) if they withhold (their gift) it is their blame and not yours.","r":"Ask if you find those worth asking; if they withhold, the blame is theirs and not yours.","n":"The chapter starts by moving the moral burden from the asker to the withholder. Parimelazhagar says such givers understand before anything is said, and the blame sticks to them as a stain does on pure white cloth, because they're rare.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","karavu","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:106"},"k:1052":{"book":"kural","num":1052,"t":["இன்பம் ஒருவற்கு இரத்தல் இரந்தவை","துன்பம் உறாஅ வரின்"],"p":"Even begging may be pleasant, if what is begged for is obtained without grief (to him that begs).","r":"Even asking is pleasure to a person, if what is asked for comes without suffering.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","eegai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The suffering is the asker's: the courting, reading the giver's moods and being refused, which is spared if the giver anticipates.","அவையுறாமல் வருதலாவது, அவர் முன்னுணர்ந்து ஈயக்கோடல்."],["kalaignar","The suffering is that of both, giver and receiver.","வழங்குபவர், வாங்குபவர் ஆகிய இருவர் மனத்திற்கும் துன்பம் எதுவுமின்றி"]],"ch":"k:106"},"k:1053":{"book":"kural","num":1053,"t":["கரப்பிலா நெஞ்சின் கடனறிவார் முன்நின்று","இரப்புமோ ரேஎர் உடைத்து"],"p":"There is even a beauty in standing before and begging of those who are liberal in their gifts and understand their duty (to beggars).","r":"Even standing and asking before those of unconcealing heart who know கடன் has a certain beauty.","n":"முன்நின்று is significant: for Parimelazhagar merely standing is enough, and it's the absence of spoken words that keeps the grace.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["iravu","karavu","maanam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","கடன் is honour, and it is the asker's: he reads 963 to explain why such a giver can sense a poor person's dignity.","கரத்தல் இல்லாத நெஞ்சினையுடைய மானம் அறிவார்"],["manakkudavar","கடன் is social obligation, the duty of helping.","கரப்பிலாத நெஞ்சினை யுடைய ஒப்புரவறிவார்"],["kalaignar","கடன் is the giver's sense of duty.","உள்ளதை ஒளிக்காத உள்ளமும், கடமையுணர்வும் கொண்டவரிடத்தில்"]],"ch":"k:106"},"k:1054":{"book":"kural","num":1054,"t":["இரத்தலும் ஈதலே போலும் கரத்தல்","கனவிலும் தேற்றாதார் மாட்டு"],"p":"To beg of such as never think of withholding (their charity) even in their dreams, is in fact the same as giving (it oneself);","r":"Even asking is like giving, from those who don't conceive of concealment even in a dream.","n":"An extraordinary equivalence that erases the difference in rank. Parimelazhagar tempers it: asking brings no fame, but nor does it damage any you already have, so that is all the likeness. The strong statement is the verse's and the caveat is his.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","karavu","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:106"},"k:1055":{"book":"kural","num":1055,"t":["கரப்பிலார் வையகத்து உண்மையால் கண்ணின்று","இரப்பவர் மேற்கொள் வது"],"p":"As there are in the world those that give without refusing, there are (also) those that prefer to beg by simply standing before them.","r":"It's because there are people on earth who don't conceal that askers take up standing before them.","n":"Parimelazhagar adds the alternative: without such people they would give up their lives, since they can't give up honour. So the existence of generous people is what keeps them alive.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","karavu","maanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:106"},"k:1056":{"book":"kural","num":1056,"t":["கரப்பிடும்பை யில்லாரைக் காணின் நிரப்பிடும்பை","எல்லாம் ஒருங்கு கெடும்"],"p":"All the evil of begging will be removed at the sight of those who are far from the evil of refusing.","r":"If one sees those without the affliction of concealment, every affliction of poverty is destroyed at once.","n":"Concealing is called இடும்பை, the same word as poverty, so the verse treats the wealthy miser as a sufferer too. The cure comes merely at the sight (காணின்). Manakkudavar concludes one shouldn't ask from those who are merely rich.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karavu","inmai","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:106"},"k:1057":{"book":"kural","num":1057,"t":["இகழ்ந்தெள்ளாது ஈவாரைக் காணின் மகிழ்ந்துள்ளம்","உள்ளுள் உவப்பது உடைத்து"],"p":"Beggars rejoice exceedingly when they behold those who bestow (their alms) with kindness and courtesy.","r":"If one sees those who give without scorn and contempt, the heart is delighted and rejoices deep inside.","n":"The joy lies in being given to without being lowered. உள்ளுள் உவப்பது goes deeper than the material relief, and it's the manner of the gift that's being measured.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","iravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:106"},"k:1058":{"book":"kural","num":1058,"t":["இரப்பாரை இல்லாயின் ஈர்ங்கண்மா ஞாலம்","மரப்பாவை சென்றுவந் தற்று"],"p":"If there were no beggars, (the actions done in) the cool wide world would only resemble the movement of a puppet.","r":"If there were no askers, this wide cool world would be wooden puppets coming and going.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","eegai","pugazh"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Without someone to give to, others would earn no fame or merit, and they might as well be lifeless.","அவர்க்கு ஈதலைச் செய்து புகழும் புண்ணியமும் எய்தாமையின், உயிருடையரல்லார்"],["manakkudavar","Asking and giving are the order of the world, so asking shouldn't be treated as low.","இரத்தலும் ஈதலும் உலகியல்பாதலான், இரத்தல் இழிவென்று கொள்ளப்படா"],["kalaignar","He changes the subject: those who won't let askers near are the puppets.","தம்மை நெருங்கக் கூடாது என்கிற மனிதர்களுக்கும்"]],"ch":"k:106"},"k:1059":{"book":"kural","num":1059,"t":["ஈவார்கண் என்னுண்டாம் தோற்றம் இரந்துகோள்","மேவார் இலாஅக் கடை"],"p":"What (praise) would there be to givers (of alms) if there were no beggars to ask for and reveive (them).","r":"What renown could givers have, if there were none who want to ask and take?","n":"The logic has an uncomfortable side: if givers' renown requires askers, a world without poverty would be poorer in virtue. The verse doesn't address this, and it treats askers as a need, not as a problem.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","pugazh","iravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:106"},"k:1060":{"book":"kural","num":1060,"t":["இரப்பான் வெகுளாமை வேண்டும் நிரப்பிடும்பை","தானேயும் சாலும் கரி"],"p":"He who begs ought not to be angry (at a refusal); for even the misery of (his own) poverty should be a sufficient reason (for so doing).","r":"The one who asks shouldn't be angry; the affliction of his own poverty is proof enough.","n":"His poverty proves that wealth is hard to come by, so the refuser may have a real reason. This turns the sufferer's own case into a reason for patience. It is a counterweight to 1051, which puts blame on those who withhold.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","inmai","samanilai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:106"},"k:1061":{"book":"kural","num":1061,"t":["கரவாது உவந்தீயும் கண்ணன்னார் கண்ணும்","இரவாமை கோடி உறும்"],"p":"Not to beg (at all) even from those excellent persons who cheerfully give without refusing, will do immense good.","r":"Not asking, even from those precious as eyes who give gladly without concealing, is worth ten million times more.","n":"The verse picks the best case for asking, then refuses it. Parimelazhagar narrows this: it's about not asking even friends who already know of one's poverty, and he concludes one should try to live by hard effort.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","maanam","karavu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:107"},"k:1062":{"book":"kural","num":1062,"t":["இரந்தும் உயிர்வாழ்தல் வேண்டின் பரந்து","கெடுக உலகியற்றி யான்"],"p":"If the Creator of the world has decreed even begging as a means of livelihood, may he too go abegging and perish.","r":"If even by begging one must live, let the one who made the world wander and perish.","n":"The Kural's sharpest line against any cosmic order. It doesn't assert that a creator exists, since the curse is conditional. Kalaignar makes that explicit: the one 'said to be' the creator.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","oozh","inmai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The creator decrees each being's livelihood along with its karma at conception; if that includes begging, the evil returns on him and he should suffer the same.","சில உயிர்க்கு இரத்தலையும் ஒரு செய் தொழிலாகக் கற்பித்தானாயின், அத்தீவினையால் தானும் அத்துன்பமுறல் வேண்டும்"],["manakkudavar","The verse is about those who choose to beg to survive; the conclusion is that death is better than begging.","இஃது இரக்கு மதனின், இறத்தல் அமையு மென்றது."],["kalaignar","He distances himself from the idea: it is whoever is 'said' to have created the world.","உலகத்தைப் படைத்தவனாகச் சொல்லப்படுபவனும் கெட்டொழிந்து திரியட்டும்."]],"ch":"k:107"},"k:1063":{"book":"kural","num":1063,"t":["இன்மை இடும்பை இரந்துதீர் வாமென்னும்","வன்மையின் வன்பாட்ட தில்"],"p":"There is no greater folly than the boldness with which one seeks to remedy the evils of poverty by begging (rather than by working).","r":"Nothing is harder than the hardness that says 'we will end the misery of poverty by begging'.","n":"வன்மை, hardness, is the opposite of what one would expect of an asker's position. Begging isn't weakness here but a kind of obstinate insensitivity. Manakkudavar adds that it doesn't even work, since it won't end poverty.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","inmai","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:107"},"k:1064":{"book":"kural","num":1064,"t":["இடமெல்லாம் கொள்ளாத் தகைத்தே இடமில்லாக்","காலும் இரவொல்லாச் சால்பு"],"p":"Even the whole world cannot sufficiently praise the dignity that would not beg even in the midst of destitution.","r":"The worth that won't consent to asking even when no means remain is too great for all of space to contain.","n":"இடம் is used twice: as all of space, and as means or room. A man with no room left holds something that no room can hold.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saandraanmai","iravu","maanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:107"},"k:1065":{"book":"kural","num":1065,"t":["தெண்ணீர் அடுபுற்கை ஆயினும் தாள்தந்தது","உண்ணலின் ஊங்கினிய தில்"],"p":"Even thin gruel is ambrosia to him who has obtained it by labour.","r":"Though it is gruel thin as clear water, nothing is sweeter than eating what one's own effort brought.","n":"தாள், literally the foot, stands for effort. Sweetness has become a moral quality here and doesn't describe the taste. Parimelazhagar says it's equal to ambrosia, because it's one's own.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["muyarchi","undi","maanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:107"},"k:1066":{"book":"kural","num":1066,"t":["ஆவிற்கு நீரென்று இரப்பினும் நாவிற்கு","இரவின் இளிவந்த தில்"],"p":"There is nothing more disgraceful to one's tongue than to use it in begging water even for a cow.","r":"Even if one asks for water for a cow, nothing is more degrading to the tongue than asking.","n":"A limit case with the worthiest cause and the smallest request, and it's still degrading. Parimelazhagar: virtue itself should be done by effort, never by begging. He also notes that நாவிற்கு confines the disgrace to the spoken word, and that this still exceeds every other disgrace.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","aram","maanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:107"},"k:1067":{"book":"kural","num":1067,"t":["இரப்பன் இரப்பாரை எல்லாம் இரப்பின்","கரப்பார் இரவன்மின் என்று"],"p":"I beseech all beggars and say, \"If you need to beg, never beg of those who give unwillingly.\"","r":"I beg of all who beg: if you must beg, don't beg of those who conceal.","n":"The poet himself takes on the posture he has just condemned, begging beggars. The repetition of இரப்பன், இரப்பாரை and இரப்பின் is almost a chant.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","karavu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:107"},"k:1068":{"book":"kural","num":1068,"t":["இரவென்னும் ஏமாப்பில் தோணி கரவென்னும்","பார்தாக்கப் பக்கு விடும்"],"p":"The unsafe raft of begging will split when it strikes on the rock of refusal.","r":"The unsafe boat called asking will split apart when it strikes the rock called concealment.","n":"The sea of poverty is supplied by both Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar. The verse's point isn't the rock alone: the boat itself is flawed, and this is what ஏமாப்பில் says.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","karavu","inmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:107"},"k:1069":{"book":"kural","num":1069,"t":["இரவுள்ள உள்ளம் உருகும் கரவுள்ள","உள்ளதூஉம் இன்றிக் கெடும்"],"p":"To think of (the evil of) begging is enough to melt one's heart; but to think of refusal is enough to break it.","r":"Thinking of begging, the heart melts; thinking of concealment, even what remains is gone.","n":"The heart has two stages: it melts, and then there's nothing left to melt. Concealment is ranked as the worse.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","karavu","manam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The melting heart is that of an onlooker who imagines the cruelty; he notes that all others say it's the asker's.","இதற்குப் பிறரெல்லாம் 'இரக்கின்றவர் உள்ளம் உருகும்' என்று உரைத்தார்."],["manakkudavar","It concerns the asker, who has nothing to gain.","இஃது இரப்பார்க்கு ஆக்கமில்லை என்றது."]],"ch":"k:107"},"k:1070":{"book":"kural","num":1070,"t":["கரப்பவர்க்கு யாங்கொளிக்கும் கொல்லோ இரப்பவர்","சொல்லாடப் போஒம் உயிர்"],"p":"Saying \"No\" to a beggar takes away his life. (but as that very word will kill the refuser) where then would the latter's life hide itself ?","r":"As the asker's life goes out at the word of refusal, where does the life of the one who conceals hide?","n":"The rhetorical question is a charge: the word that kills the hearer ought to kill the speaker first. Parimelazhagar glosses 'life goes' as being stunned with helplessness, a less literal sense than the verse's own. He reports another reading as a dread-filled man's speech.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karavu","iravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:107"},"k:1071":{"book":"kural","num":1071,"t":["மக்களே போல்வர் கயவர் அவரன்ன","ஒப்பாரி யாங்கண்ட தில்"],"p":"The base resemble men perfectly (as regards form); and we have not seen such (exact) resemblance (among any other species).","r":"The base look just like human beings; we have never seen so close a resemblance.","n":"Bitter praise opens the portrait. The likeness is complete, and that is the whole problem, since there's no outward way of telling.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","panpu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It is a likeness of body between two different kinds, distinguished only by whether they have good qualities.","மக்கட் சாதிக்கும் கயச்சாதிக்கும் வடிவு ஒத்தலின்"],["kalaignar","It is hypocrisy within one person, who is base while acting good.","குணத்தில் ஒருவர் கயவராக இருப்பார். ஆனால், நல்லவரைப் போலக் காட்டிக் கொள்வார்."]],"ch":"k:108"},"k:1072":{"book":"kural","num":1072,"t":["நன்றறி வாரிற் கயவர் திருவுடையர்","நெஞ்சத்து அவலம் இலர்"],"p":"The low enjoy more felicity than those who know what is good; for the former are not troubled with anxiety (as to the good).","r":"The base are more fortunate than those who know what's good, for they have no anguish in their heart.","n":"A real observation under the sarcasm: moral knowledge has a psychological cost. Parimelazhagar spells it out: the good worry that they don't do enough, about obstacles to their good deeds, and about what might result. திரு here is mock praise, as he says.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","arivu","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:108"},"k:1073":{"book":"kural","num":1073,"t":["தேவர் அனையர் கயவர் அவருந்தாம்","மேவன செய்தொழுக லான்"],"p":"The base resemble the Gods; for the base ac t as they like.","r":"The base are like the gods, for they too act as they please.","n":"Having looked like humans in 1071, they now look like gods. Parimelazhagar says the difference between them goes unstated, since the gods act by understanding and the base by lowness. Kalaignar's gloss says 'the gods of the Puranas', which gives the barb a second target.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:108"},"k:1074":{"book":"kural","num":1074,"t":["அகப்பட்டி ஆவாரைக் காணின் அவரின்","மிகப்பட்டுச் செம்மாக்கும் கீழ்"],"p":"The base feels proud when he sees persons whose acts meaner than his own.","r":"Seeing அகப்பட்டி, the base outdo them and swagger with pride.","n":"அகப்பட்டி is hard to fix, and I've left it untranslated. The readings below differ so widely that I can't be confident of either.","x":1,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","sirumai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","These are people who behave as they please like strays, on a smaller scale than the base man, and he boasts by exceeding them.","தன்னிற் சுருங்கிய பட்டியாய் ஒழுகுவாரைக் கண்டானாயின்"],["manakkudavar","These are women at home with grace, and the base pride themselves on being more graceful still.","மனையகத்திருந்து பொற்புடையாராகிய பெண்டிரைக் காணின்"]],"ch":"k:108"},"k:1075":{"book":"kural","num":1075,"t":["அச்சமே கீழ்களது ஆசாரம் எச்சம்","அவாவுண்டேல் உண்டாம் சிறிது"],"p":"(The principle of) behaviour in the mean is chiefly fear; if not, hope of gain, to some extent.","r":"Fear is the conduct of the base, and beyond that there's some, if there's desire.","n":"A theory of external motivation: there's no inner source of good conduct in them, so it can only come from fear of punishment and hope of gain. Parimelazhagar names the king as what they fear, and Kalaignar reverses the order and makes it pretence.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:108"},"k:1076":{"book":"kural","num":1076,"t":["அறைபறை அன்னர் கயவர்தாம் கேட்ட","மறைபிறர்க்கு உய்த்துரைக்க லான்"],"p":"The base are like a drum that is beaten, for they unburden to others the secrets they have heard.","r":"The base are like a beaten drum, for they carry any secret they hear and proclaim it to others.","n":"Parimelazhagar notes that the drum only sounds when beaten, a working likeness. உய்த்து, 'carrying', suggests to him the secret is a heavy burden to such a man, so that he has to go and put it down.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:108"},"k:1077":{"book":"kural","num":1077,"t":["ஈர்ங்கை விதிரார் கயவர் கொடிறுடைக்கும்","கூன்கையர் அல்லா தவர்க்கு"],"p":"The mean will not (even) shake off (what sticks to) their hands (soon after a meal) to any but those who would break their jaws with their clenched fists.","r":"The base won't even shake their wet hands for any but those with bent fists who would break their jaws.","n":"The smallest gift is shaking drops of water off a washed hand. The gift is measured by what it takes to get it. Parimelazhagar sums up: they give nothing to the weak and everything to those who hurt them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:108"},"k:1078":{"book":"kural","num":1078,"t":["சொல்லப் பயன்படுவர் சான்றோர் கரும்புபோல்","கொல்லப் பயன்படும் கீழ்"],"p":"The great bestow (their alms) as soon as they are informed; (but) the mean, like the sugar-cane, only when they are tortured to death.","r":"The worthy are of use at a word, and the base, like sugar-cane, only when crushed.","n":"A sugar-cane is sweet, so the image concedes that the base do have something to give. But it comes out only under pressure. கொல்ல, 'to kill', is unusually strong.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","saandraanmai","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:108"},"k:1079":{"book":"kural","num":1079,"t":["உடுப்பதூஉம் உண்பதூஉம் காணின் பிறர்மேல்","வடுக்காண வற்றாகும் கீழ்"],"p":"The base will bring an evil (accusation) against others, as soon as he sees them (enjoying) good food and clothing.","r":"If they see others clothed and fed, the base are able to find a fault in them.","n":"Ordinary eating and dressing are enough to bring it on; no great success is required.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","sirumai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","They invent faults that don't exist, with skill enough to make them believable.","அவர்மாட்டு வடுவில்லையாகவும் உண்டாக்கவல்லனாம்."],["manakkudavar","They search out faults that do exist in such people.","அவர் மாட்டு உள்ள குற்றங்களை ஆராயவல்லாராவர் கயவர்"]],"ch":"k:108"},"k:1080":{"book":"kural","num":1080,"t":["எற்றிற் குரியர் கயவரொன்று உற்றக்கால்","விற்றற்கு உரியர் விரைந்து"],"p":"The base will hasten to sell themselves as soon as a calamity has befallen them. For what else are they fitted ?","r":"What are the base fit for? When something befalls them, they are fit to sell themselves, quickly.","n":"The Book of Wealth closes with a person as a thing sold. Parimelazhagar's reading of விரைந்து is grim: they hurry before buyers learn what they are. An answer to the question is the insult: this is the only use they have.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","maanam","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:108"},"k:1081":{"book":"kural","num":1081,"t":["அணங்குகொல் ஆய்மயில் கொல்லோ கனங்குழை","மாதர்கொல் மாலும் என் நெஞ்சு"],"p":"Is this jewelled female a celestial, a choice peahen, or a human being ? My mind is perplexed.","r":"The man, on first seeing her, cannot tell whether this woman in heavy earrings is a goddess, a rare peahen or a human being, and his heart reels.","n":"The three guesses aren't mere compliments. Parimelazhagar ties each to evidence: her indescribable form and his distress suggest a goddess, her grace and the grove suggest a peahen, and the way she looks back suggests a woman. Kalaignar's rendering drops the goddess and gives 'beauty that torments me' instead.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anangu","kaamam","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:109"},"k:1082":{"book":"kural","num":1082,"t":["நோக்கினாள் நோக்கெதிர் நோக்குதல் தாக்கணங்கு","தானைக்கொண் டன்ன துடைத்து"],"p":"This female beauty returning my looks is like a celestial maiden coming with an army to contend against me.","r":"The man says that when she answers his look with her own, it is as if a goddess who strikes on her own had brought an army with her.","n":"The returned look is the blow. Parimelazhagar says he has now settled that she is human, so the goddess of 1081 survives only as a figure, and his pain has grown from her beauty to her meaningful glance.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anangu","kan","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:109"},"k:1083":{"book":"kural","num":1083,"t":["பண்டறியேன் கூற்றென் பதனை இனியறிந்தேன்","பெண்டகையால் பேரமர்க் கட்டு"],"p":"I never knew before what is called Yama; I see it now; it is the eyes that carry on a great fight with (the help of) female qualities.","r":"The man says he never knew what Death was, and now he does: it has a woman's grace and large eyes that make war.","n":"Parimelazhagar glosses the first line as a contrast between hearsay and experience: Death was something known from what the learned say, and only now seen. He adds that the man calls her Death because the suffering outweighs the pleasure.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","kan","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:109"},"k:1084":{"book":"kural","num":1084,"t":["கண்டார் உயிருண்ணும் தோற்றத்தால் பெண்டகைப்","பேதைக்கு அமர்த்தன கண்"],"p":"These eyes that seem to kill those who look at them are as it were in hostilities with this feminine simplicity.","r":"The man says the eyes of this artless, womanly girl have a look that eats the life of those who see them, and he sets those eyes against, or alongside, her gentleness.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","kootru","kaamam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","அமர்த்தன means the eyes are at odds with her gentle nature; they are cruel where she is not.","அமர்த்தல்: மாறுபடுதல்."],["manakkudavar","The word means the eyes fit her: they suit the girl in the work they do.","அமர்தல் - மேவல்."],["kalaignar","The eyes are a puzzling contradiction in someone so womanly.","ஏனிந்த மாறுபாடு?"]],"ch":"k:109"},"k:1085":{"book":"kural","num":1085,"t":["கூற்றமோ கண்ணோ பிணையோ மடவரல்","நோக்கமிம் மூன்றும் உடைத்து"],"p":"Is it Yama, (a pair of) eyes or a hind ?- Are not all these three in the looks of this maid ?","r":"The man asks whether it is Death, an eye or a doe, since the young woman's look has the nature of all three.","n":"The list holds opposites: the look kills, it seeks him out, and it startles like a frightened animal. Parimelazhagar sums this up as pleasure and pain given together, which sets up the double look of 1091.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","kan","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:109"},"k:1086":{"book":"kural","num":1086,"t":["கொடும்புருவம் கோடா மறைப்பின் நடுங்கஞர்","செய்யல மன்இவள் கண்"],"p":"Her eyes will cause (me) no trembling sorrow, if they are properly hidden by her cruel arched eye-brows.","r":"The man says that if her curved brows would only stop bending and screen her eyes, those eyes could not cause him this trembling pain.","n":"The conceit turns on the word கோடா, 'unbending'. Parimelazhagar treats the brows as the eyes' close friends, who could restrain them only if they were straight themselves, because anyone who rebukes a friend must be upright. Manakkudavar reads a small scene into it: she has lowered her head and her brows now shade her eyes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kan","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:109"},"k:1087":{"book":"kural","num":1087,"t":["கடாஅக் களிற்றின்மேற் கட்படாம் மாதர்","படாஅ முலைமேல் துகில்"],"p":"The cloth that covers the firm bosom of this maiden is (like) that which covers the eyes of a rutting elephant.","r":"The man says the cloth over her firm breasts is like the face-cloth over the eyes of a rutting elephant.","n":"A covering usually suggests safety, but this one announces danger. Parimelazhagar makes modesty the reason for the cloth, while the man, seeing strength through it, thinks of what it holds back. Manakkudavar has the man address her directly as மாதரே, while Parimelazhagar has him speak about her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anangu","naanam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:109"},"k:1088":{"book":"kural","num":1088,"t":["ஒண்ணுதற் கோஒ உடைந்ததே ஞாட்பினுள்","நண்ணாரும் உட்குமென் பீடு"],"p":"On her bright brow alone is destroyed even th at power of mine that used to terrify the most fearless foes in the battlefield.","r":"The man laments that his might, feared in battle even by enemies who never met him there, has broken before her bright forehead alone.","n":"The ஓ marks wonder at the mismatch: so great a strength and so small a thing as a forehead. Parimelazhagar is careful to note that this isn't boasting, because the man is grieving for what he has lost.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:109"},"k:1089":{"book":"kural","num":1089,"t":["பிணையேர் மடநோக்கும் நாணும் உடையாட்கு","அணியெவனோ ஏதில தந்து"],"p":"Of what use are other jewels to her who is adorned with modesty, and the meek looks of a hind ?","r":"The man asks what use foreign ornaments are to one who already has a doe's shy look and modesty.","n":"It sounds like a pure compliment, but both older commentators hear a complaint. For Parimelazhagar, whoever adorned her lacks sense, because the jewels burden her and harm him; for Manakkudavar, he praises her while seeming to accuse her of cruelty.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","kan","nalam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:109"},"k:1090":{"book":"kural","num":1090,"t":["உண்டார்கண் அல்லது அடுநறாக் காமம்போல்","கண்டார் மகிழ்செய்தல் இன்று"],"p":"Unlike boiled honey which yields delight only when it is drunk, love gives pleasure even when looked at.","r":"Brewed liquor delights only those who drink it, not those who merely see it as love does; the speaker is the man for Parimelazhagar and the woman for Manakkudavar.","n":"Parimelazhagar supplies an unspoken close: love is better than liquor, yet he hasn't obtained it because he hasn't yet read her intent. He also takes காமம் here to mean the person in whom love is enjoyed.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kurippu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The man speaks as he sets out to read her intent.","தலைமகள் குறிப்பறிதல் உற்றான் சொல்லியது."],["manakkudavar","The woman thinks this, pained after seeing the man, which reverses the chapter's point of view.","இது தலைமகள் தலைமகனைக்கண்டுழி வருத்தமுற்றுக் கருதியது."]],"ch":"k:109"},"k:1091":{"book":"kural","num":1091,"t":["இருநோக்கு இவளுண்கண் உள்ளது ஒருநோக்கு","நோய்நோக்கொன் றந்நோய் மருந்து"],"p":"There are two looks in the dyed eyes of this (fair one); one causes pain, and the other is the cure thereof.","r":"The man sees two looks in her kohl-lined eyes: one gives him the sickness and the other is its cure.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","kaamanoy","kurippu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The painful look discloses desire in her mind and the healing look shows her love; he rejects calling the first a neutral look, since one-sided pain wouldn't be mutual love.","நோய் செய்யும் நோக்கு அவள் மனத்தனாய காமக்குறிப்பினை வெளிப்படுத்துகின்ற நோக்கு."],["manakkudavar","The two looks are successive: the first look that hurt him, then a later one mixed with shyness and concern for his pain.","நோய்நோக்கென்பது முதல் நோக்கின நோக்கம்"]],"ch":"k:110"},"k:1092":{"book":"kural","num":1092,"t":["கண்களவு கொள்ளும் சிறுநோக்கம் காமத்தில்","செம்பாகம் அன்று பெரிது"],"p":"A single stolen glance of her eyes is more than half the pleasure (of sexual embrace).","r":"The man says the brief look her eyes steal is not half of love's fulfilment but more than half.","n":"The arithmetic is odd until you see why it works. Parimelazhagar says the glance guarantees that union will follow, so its value lies in what it promises. Stealing also suits the setting: this is secret love, and the eyes are the first thieves.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","kalaviyal","kaamam","kurippu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:110"},"k:1093":{"book":"kural","num":1093,"t":["நோக்கினாள் நோக்கி இறைஞ்சினாள் அஃதவள்","யாப்பினுள் அட்டிய நீர்"],"p":"She has looked (at men) and stooped (her head); and that (sign) waters as it were (the corn of) our love.","r":"The man says she looked, and having looked lowered her head, and that gesture was water poured into the bond between them.","n":"The verse says only யாப்பு, a binding, and water poured into it. The growing crop of love that makes the image cohere comes from the commentators, and Parimelazhagar notes that the metaphor is only partly stated. Lowering her head is what waters the bond, so modesty here works for desire and not against it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","naanam","kurippu","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:110"},"k:1094":{"book":"kural","num":1094,"t":["யான்நோக்கும் காலை நிலன்நோக்கும் நோக்காக்கால்","தான்நோக்கி மெல்ல நகும்"],"p":"When I look, she looks down; when I do not, she looks and smiles gently.","r":"The man observes that when he looks she looks at the ground, and when he doesn't she looks at him and smiles softly.","n":"The whole verse is timing. Their eyes never meet, and that very avoidance tells him she is watching. Parimelazhagar takes மெல்ல நகும் as rejoicing inwardly without showing it, and mentions that others read it as a visible smile.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kan","naanam","kurippu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:110"},"k:1095":{"book":"kural","num":1095,"t":["குறிக்கொண்டு நோக்காமை அல்லால் ஒருகண்","சிறக்கணித்தாள் போல நகும்"],"p":"She not only avoids a direct look at me, but looks as it were with a half-closed eye and smiles.","r":"The man says that she doesn't look at him directly, but smiles as though narrowing one eye.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kan","kurippu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","She narrows one eye as she glances; the 'as though' is there because even this isn't openly done.","சிறக்கணித்தல்: சுருங்குதல்."],["manakkudavar","He reads the line as 'at one moment', and the verb as agreeing: she smiles as one who has consented.","ஒருகால் உடம்பட்டாள் போல"]],"ch":"k:110"},"k:1096":{"book":"kural","num":1096,"t":["உறாஅ தவர்போல் சொலினும் செறாஅர்சொல்","ஒல்லை உணரப் படும்"],"p":"Though they may speak harshly as if they were strangers, the words of the friendly are soon understood.","r":"The man reflects that the words of those without real anger are quickly seen through, even when they speak like strangers.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The harsh speaker is the friend, who is sending him away while really seeking to help; he says this to himself.","தோழி சேண்படுத்தவழி அவள் குறிப்பு அறிந்த தலைமகன் தன்னுள்ளே சொல்லியது."],["manakkudavar","The case is one where the body shows agreement and speech refuses, which is taken as consent.","உறுப்பினாலிசைவுகாட்டி, உரையினால் மறுப்பினும் உடன்படுதலா மென்றது."],["kalaignar","The harsh speaker is the woman herself, hiding love under a stranger's manner.","அவள் அகத்தில் கோபமின்றி அன்பு கொண்டிருப்பது"]],"ch":"k:110"},"k:1097":{"book":"kural","num":1097,"t":["செறாஅச் சிறுசொல்லும் செற்றார்போல் நோக்கும்","உறாஅர்போன்று உற்றார் குறிப்பு"],"p":"Little words that are harsh and looks that are hateful are (but) the expressions of lovers who wish to act like strangers.","r":"The man reasons that small harsh words without anger and looks like an enemy's are the signs of those who are close while seeming distant.","n":"1096 and 1097 give him a rule for treating refusal as disguise, and the verses rest it entirely on செறாஅ, the absence of real anger. In my reading that condition is doing all the work, and it is exactly what the man can't verify from outside. Parimelazhagar's conclusion is that he needn't fear such signs.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","kan"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:110"},"k:1098":{"book":"kural","num":1098,"t":["அசையியற்கு உண்டாண்டோ ர் ஏஎர்யான் நோக்கப்","பசையினள் பைய நகும்"],"p":"When I look, the pitying maid looks in return and smiles gently; and that is a comforting sign for me.","r":"The man says that when he looks, she softens and smiles gently, and there is something special about the swaying woman at that moment.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","nalam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","ஏர் here is the good sign that shows in her smile, a promise that won't fail him after his pleading look.","தோன்றுகின்றதோர் நன்மைக் குறிப்பு உண்டு."],["manakkudavar","ஏர் is beauty, the quality that surpasses her form at that moment.","அழகு - தன்வடிவினுள் மிக்க குணம்"]],"ch":"k:110"},"k:1099":{"book":"kural","num":1099,"t":["ஏதிலார் போலப் பொதுநோக்கு நோக்குதல்","காதலார் கண்ணே உள"],"p":"Both the lovers are capable of looking at each other in an ordinary way, as if they were perfect strangers.","r":"Looking at each other with a stranger's common glance is found only in lovers; Parimelazhagar gives this to the friend, who is working out the affair, while the other commentators name no speaker.","n":"A public pretence of indifference becomes the evidence. The couple's disguise gives them away to the one person placed to notice, so this is close observation from outside the romance.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","kalaviyal","kan"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:110"},"k:1100":{"book":"kural","num":1100,"t":["கண்ணொடு கண்இணை நோக்கொக்கின் வாய்ச்சொற்கள்","என்ன பயனும் இல"],"p":"The words of the mouths are of no use whatever, when there is perfect agreement between the eyes (of lovers).","r":"Once eyes agree with eyes in their look, spoken words have no use at all; the speaker is the friend for Parimelazhagar and the man for Manakkudavar.","n":"Parimelazhagar makes the claim very concrete: the useless words are the couple's cover stories, hers about guarding the millet field and his about hunting. Out of context it reads as a maxim about wordless love.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kan","kurippu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The friend, having heard both, speaks in general terms about what she has noticed.","இருவர் சொல்லும் கேட்டு உலகியல்மேல் வைத்துக் கூறியவாறு."],["manakkudavar","The man, about to approach her, speaks to his own heart.","இது சார்தலுறுகின்ற தலைமகன் தன்னெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது."]],"ch":"k:110"},"k:1101":{"book":"kural","num":1101,"t":["கண்டுகேட்டு உண்டுயிர்த்து உற்றறியும் ஐம்புலனும்","ஒண்தொடி கண்ணே உள"],"p":"The (simultaneous) enjoyment of the five senses of sight, hearing, taste, smell and touch can only be found with bright braceleted (women).","r":"The man says that all five senses, seeing, hearing, tasting, smelling and touching, find their objects in this one bright-braceleted woman.","n":"The claim is about concentration. Parimelazhagar's gloss is that pleasures usually taken from different things at different times are here had at once, from one person. He also folds in the erotic techniques named in Sanskrit treatises, which the verse doesn't mention.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["punarchi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:111"},"k:1102":{"book":"kural","num":1102,"t":["பிணிக்கு மருந்து பிறமன் அணியிழை","தன்நோய்க்குத் தானே மருந்து"],"p":"The remedy for a disease is always something different (from it); but for the disease caused by this jewelled maid, she is herself the cure.","r":"The man says the cure for a disease is usually something other than its cause, but this jewelled woman is herself the cure for the sickness she brings.","n":"Parimelazhagar sharpens பிற: ordinary medicine is opposite in nature to the disease's cause. Love therefore breaks a rule of medicine, not only of common sense.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kaamanoy","punarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:111"},"k:1103":{"book":"kural","num":1103,"t":["தாம்வீழ்வார் மென்றோள் துயிலின் இனிதுகொல்","தாமரைக் கண்ணான் உலகு"],"p":"Can the lotus-eyed Vishnu's heaven be indeed as sweet to those who delight to sleep in the delicate arms of their beloved ?","r":"The man asks whether the world of the lotus-eyed one can be sweeter than sleeping on the soft arms of the one you love.","n":"The question is left open, and the commentators answer it for him. Parimelazhagar has the man's friend object that one fit for supreme bliss shouldn't sink to this small pleasure, and takes the man's point to be that heaven requires hard austerities which this sleep does not.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","kaamam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The world is Vishnu's, reached by ascetics who renounce the senses; he rejects the Indra reading on the ground that lotus-eyed isn't Indra's name.","தாமரைக்கண்ணான் என்பது அவனுக்குப் பெயரன்மையின், அஃது உரையன்மையறிக."],["manakkudavar","The world is Indra's heaven, and the point is that heaven's pleasure is simply this.","இந்திரனது சுவர்க்கம்"]],"ch":"k:111"},"k:1104":{"book":"kural","num":1104,"t":["நீங்கின் தெறூஉம் குறுகுங்கால் தண்ணென்னும்","தீயாண்டுப் பெற்றாள் இவள்?"],"p":"From whence has she got this fire that burns when I withdraw and cools when I approach ?","r":"The man asks where she found this fire that burns when he moves away and cools when he comes near.","n":"Parimelazhagar observes a small sleight: the fire is the man's own desire, which he attributes to her because she is what draws it out.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:111"},"k:1105":{"book":"kural","num":1105,"t":["வேட் ட பொழுதின் அவையவை போலுமே","தோட் டார் கதுப்பினாள் தோள்"],"p":"The shoulders of her whose locks are adorned with flowers delight me as if they were the very sweets I have desired (to get).","r":"The man says her shoulders, with flowers in her hair, feel like each thing one craves at the moment of craving it.","n":"The point is freshness across repetition. For Parimelazhagar, they have already met several times and she still binds him as if new. Manakkudavar's text differs: where the verse has hair adorned with flowers, he has hair fallen loose on her shoulders during love, which changes the picture from adornment to its undoing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:111"},"k:1106":{"book":"kural","num":1106,"t":["உறுதோறு உயிர்தளிர்ப்பத் தீண்டலால் பேதைக்கு","அமிழ்தின் இயன்றன தோள்"],"p":"The shoulders of this fair one are made of ambrosia, for they revive me with pleasure every time I embrace them.","r":"The man says that since her touch makes his life put out fresh shoots each time they meet, this girl's shoulders must be made of ambrosia.","n":"Ambrosia is inferred from an effect, in the manner of a logical argument. உயிர்தளிர்ப்ப also makes his life a plant, withering between meetings, which Manakkudavar pushes further: without her that life would die.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uyir","punarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:111"},"k:1107":{"book":"kural","num":1107,"t":["தம்மில் இருந்து தமதுபாத்து உண்டற்றால்","அம்மா அரிவை முயக்கு"],"p":"The embraces of a gold-complexioned beautiful female are as pleasant as to dwell in one's own house and live by one's own (earnings) after distributing (a portion of it in charity).","r":"The man says the embrace of this lovely dark-gold woman is like living in your own home and eating your own food after sharing it.","n":"A pleasure is measured by a virtue. The comparison brings the whole ethic of the householder into the bedroom, as a kind of satisfaction that has nothing to hide or to owe.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["punarchi","aram"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Her friend has urged marriage, and he declines: this secret union already gives him the reward of the householder's life.","'இல்லறஞ்செய்தார் எய்தும் துறக்கத்து இன்பம் எனக்கு இப்புணர்ச்சியே தரும்' என வரைவு உடன்படான் கூறியவாறாயிற்று."],["kalaignar","The woman embraced is already his wife, so the verse describes married pleasure.","தனது அழகிய காதல் மனைவியைத் தழுவுகின்ற இன்பத்துக்கு"]],"ch":"k:111"},"k:1108":{"book":"kural","num":1108,"t":["வீழும் இருவர்க்கு இனிதே வளியிடை","போழப் படாஅ முயக்கு"],"p":"To ardent lovers sweet is the embrace that cannot be penetrated even by a breath of breeze.","r":"The man says an embrace so close that not even a breeze can part it is sweet to two who desire each other.","n":"Parimelazhagar finds an evasion in the word இருவர், 'two'. The friend has urged the uninterrupted union of marriage, and he answers that this holds for two people, whereas they are not two. Manakkudavar sees only the praise.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","kaamam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","He agrees in form but declines the marriage she urges, because here there aren't two.","'ஈண்டு இருவர் இல்லை இன்மையான், இஃது ஒவ்வாது' என்பது கருத்து."]],"ch":"k:111"},"k:1109":{"book":"kural","num":1109,"t":["ஊடல் உணர்தல் புணர்தல் இவைகாமம்","கூடியார் பெற்ற பயன்"],"p":"Love quarrel, reconciliation and intercourse - these are the advantages reaped by those who marry for lust.","r":"The man names quarrelling, making up and uniting as the rewards of those who have come together in love.","n":"The verse puts the quarrel inside love's rewards, and first in the list. That is the seed of the book's final chapter.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","punarchi","kaamam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","This is a dismissal: quarrels belong to married men who stray to courtesans, and a couple as one as they are wants no such 'reward'.","அப்பயன் இருதலைப் புள்ளிள் ஓருயிராய் உழுவலன்புடைய எமக்கு வேண்டா"],["manakkudavar","This is delight: the man has just ended her sulk and united with her, and celebrates the sequence.","ஊடற் குறிப்புத் தோன்ற நின்ற தலைமகளை ஊடல் தீர்த்துப் புணர்ந்த தலைமகன்"]],"ch":"k:111"},"k:1110":{"book":"kural","num":1110,"t":["அறிதோறு அறியாமை கண்டற்றால் காமம்","செறிதோறும் சேயிழை மாட்டு"],"p":"As (one's) ignorance is discovered the more one learns, so does repeated intercourse with a well-adorned female (only create a desire for more).","r":"The man says that closeness to this red-jewelled woman works like knowledge: each advance shows how much was lacking before.","n":"Parimelazhagar closes the chapter by noting that the woman feels this joy too, but it shows in her only through signs and never in speech. Nine verses of male voice therefore rest on a convention, and he says so.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","arivu","punarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:111"},"k:1111":{"book":"kural","num":1111,"t":["நன்னீரை வாழி அனிச்சமே நின்னினும்","மென்னீரள் யாம்வீழ் பவள்"],"p":"May you flourish, O Anicham! you have a delicate nature. But my beloved is more delicate than you.","r":"The man addresses the anicham flower, granting it delicacy and telling it that the woman he loves is more delicate still.","n":"வாழி sounds like a blessing, but for Parimelazhagar it is a polite way to end the flower's pride: stop thinking you are the softest. He also says the man has just touched her, so the praise comes from touch and not sight.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nalam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:112"},"k:1112":{"book":"kural","num":1112,"t":["மலர்காணின் மையாத்தி நெஞ்சே இவள்கண்","பலர்காணும் பூவொக்கும் என்று"],"p":"O my soul, fancying that flowers which are seen by many can resemble her eyes, you become confused at the sight of them.","r":"The man scolds his heart for reeling whenever it sees flowers, supposing that her eyes resemble what anyone can look at.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","kan","nalam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The flowers don't match her eyes, and the heart is rebuked for its false comparison now that he alone has seen them.","அம்மலர்கள் ஒவ்வாமை கண்டு, ஒப்புமை கருதிய நெஞ்சை இகழ்ந்து கூறியவாறு."],["manakkudavar","Her eyes match flowers in colour but not in quality.","கண் பூவினது நிறமொக்குமாயினும்"],["kalaignar","The verse is direct praise: her eyes are indeed a flower that many see and admire.","பலரும் கண்டு வியக்கும் மலராகவே திகழ்கிறது."]],"ch":"k:112"},"k:1113":{"book":"kural","num":1113,"t":["முறிமேனி முத்தம் முறுவல் வெறிநாற்றம்","வேலுண்கண் வேய்த்தோ ளவட்கு"],"p":"The complexion of this bamboo-shouldered one is that of a shoot; her teeth, are pearls; her breath, fragrance; and her dyed eyes, lances.","r":"The man describes her: bamboo shoulders, a body like young shoots, pearl teeth, fragrance, and kohl-lined eyes that are spears.","n":"Parimelazhagar offers a witty setting as an alternative: her friend claims not to know whom he means, so this list is a description for identifying her. Everything in it is soft except the eyes, the one weapon among the delights, as in chapter 109.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nalam","kan"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:112"},"k:1114":{"book":"kural","num":1114,"t":["காணின் குவளை கவிழ்ந்து நிலன்நோக்கும்","மாணிழை கண்ணொவ்வேம் என்று"],"p":"If the blue lotus could see, it would stoop and look at the ground saying, \"I can never resemble the eyes of this excellent jewelled one.\"","r":"The man says that if blue water-lilies could see, they would bow their heads and look at the ground, saying they can't match the eyes of this finely jewelled woman.","n":"The lilies would take the posture of a modest woman, which is the very gesture she makes in 1094. Parimelazhagar draws out the unstated corollary: lacking sight and shame, real lilies stand proudly staring at the sky.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kan","naanam","nalam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:112"},"k:1115":{"book":"kural","num":1115,"t":["அனிச்சப்பூக் கால்களையாள் பெய்தாள் நுகப்பிற்கு","நல்ல படாஅ பறை"],"p":"No merry drums will be beaten for the (tender) waist of her who has adorned herself with the anicham without having removed its stem.","r":"The man says that she has worn anicham flowers without removing their stems, so no good drums will sound for her waist.","n":"This is a mock funeral. As Parimelazhagar spells it out, the stems' weight will snap her waist, and then only the drum for the dead will sound. Praise by imagined catastrophe is one of the book's boldest jokes. He glosses கால் as the flower's bud or stalk end, and Manakkudavar as its stems.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["nalam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:112"},"k:1116":{"book":"kural","num":1116,"t":["மதியும் மடந்தை முகனும் அறியா","பதியின் கலங்கிய மீன்"],"p":"The stars have become confused in their places not being able to distinguish between the moon and the maid's countenance.","r":"The man says the stars have strayed from their places, unable to tell the moon from the young woman's face.","n":"This gives a fanciful explanation for why stars move at all: they are lost. Manakkudavar says so directly, that the poet presents their motion as confusion.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nalam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:112"},"k:1117":{"book":"kural","num":1117,"t":["அறுவாய் நிறைந்த அவிர்மதிக்குப் போல","மறுவுண்டோ மாதர் முகத்து"],"p":"Could there be spots in the face of this maid like those in the bright full moon ?","r":"The man asks whether a woman's face has spots, as the bright moon does when it fills out its lost parts.","n":"This undoes the previous verse. Waning and spots should have told the stars the difference, so Parimelazhagar hears mockery of the stars: they had every means of knowing, and still failed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nalam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:112"},"k:1118":{"book":"kural","num":1118,"t":["மாதர் முகம்போல் ஒளிவிட வல்லையேல்","காதலை வாழி மதி"],"p":"If you can indeed shine like the face of women, flourish, O moon, for then would you be worth loving ?","r":"The man tells the moon that if it could shine as her face does, it too could be loved.","n":"வாழி varies in force: Parimelazhagar calls it agreement when spoken to the flower in 1111 and contempt here, because the moon's spots make the condition impossible. Manakkudavar treats it as an empty particle. The tone of the verse depends on that small word.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","nalam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:112"},"k:1119":{"book":"kural","num":1119,"t":["மலரன்ன கண்ணாள் முகமொத்தி யாயின்","பலர்காணத் தோன்றல் மதி"],"p":"O moon, if you wish to resemble the face of her whose eyes are like (these) flowers, do not appear so as to be seen by all.","r":"The man tells the moon that if it wants to resemble the face of the flower-eyed woman, it must not appear where many can see it.","n":"The criterion has moved from brightness to privacy. For Parimelazhagar, what disqualifies the moon is that it shows itself to everyone, while he alone has fully enjoyed her face. Praise here shades into possession.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nalam","kan"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:112"},"k:1120":{"book":"kural","num":1120,"t":["அனிச்சமும் அன்னத்தின் தூவியும் மாதர்","அடிக்கு நெருஞ்சிப் பழம்"],"p":"The anicham and the feathers of the swan are to the feet of females, like the fruit of the (thorny) Nerunji.","r":"The man says anicham flowers and swan's down are to her feet what the nerunji fruit is.","n":"The verse says நெருஞ்சிப் பழம், the fruit, and Kalaignar and Pope turn it into a thorn. Parimelazhagar explains the choice of fruit, since it is hard at the tip.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nalam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","This is a covert refusal: her friend has proposed elopement, and feet like these couldn't cross the wilderness.","உடன் போக்கு உரைத்த தோழிக்கு அதனது அருமை கூறி மறுத்தது."],["manakkudavar","It is plain praise: her feet are softer than these.","இஃது அவையிற்றினும் மெல்லியது அடியென்று கூறிற்று."]],"ch":"k:112"},"k:1121":{"book":"kural","num":1121,"t":["பாலொடு தேன்கலந் தற்றே பணிமொழி","வாலெயிறு ஊறிய நீர்"],"p":"The water which oozes from the white teeth of this soft speeched damsel is like a mixture of milk and honey.","r":"The man says the moisture at the white teeth of this soft-spoken woman is like milk mixed with honey.","n":"Parimelazhagar notices why the comparison is a mixture and not one sweet thing: milk and honey each have a known taste, while their blend has a sweetness you can't name.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:113"},"k:1122":{"book":"kural","num":1122,"t":["உடம்பொடு உயிரிடை என்னமற் றன்ன","மடந்தையொடு எம்மிடை நட்பு"],"p":"The love between me and this damsel is like the union of body and soul.","r":"The man says the bond between him and this girl is what the bond between body and life is.","n":"நட்பு, the word for friendship, is what the verse uses. Parimelazhagar's reading is that these are words for her: she is privately wondering who he is, whether he loves her and whether they will meet again, and he answers what he reads in her face. He files the verse under fear of parting, and Manakkudavar agrees: I can't leave you.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","uyir","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:113"},"k:1123":{"book":"kural","num":1123,"t":["கருமணியிற் பாவாய்நீ போதாயாம் வீழும்","திருநுதற்கு இல்லை இடம்"],"p":"O you image in the pupil (of my eye)! depart; there is no room for (my) fair-browed beloved.","r":"The man tells the image in the pupil of his eye to leave, because there is no room there for the fair-browed woman he loves.","n":"The conceit depends on the Tamil word for the pupil's reflection, பாவை, a little figure living inside the eye. Parimelazhagar sets this at the moment she is leaving him: since he can't bear not seeing her, she must move in.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:113"},"k:1124":{"book":"kural","num":1124,"t":["வாழ்தல் உயிர்க்கன்னள் ஆயிழை சாதல்","அதற்கன்னள் நீங்கும் இடத்து"],"p":"My fair-jewelled one resembles the living soul (when she is in union with me), the dying soul when she leaves me.","r":"The man says the finely jewelled woman is to his life what living is when they are together, and what dying is when she goes.","n":"She isn't compared to life and death as things, but to what happens to a life, its staying in the body and its leaving. Parimelazhagar notes that the ideas of 'for me' and 'in union' have to be supplied, since the verse says only 'when she leaves'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uyir","punarchi","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:113"},"k:1125":{"book":"kural","num":1125,"t":["உள்ளுவன் மன்யான் மறப்பின் மறப்பறியேன்","ஒள்ளமர்க் கண்ணாள் குணம்"],"p":"If I had forgotten her who has bright battling eyes, I would have remembered (thee); but I never forget her. (Thus says he to her maid).","r":"The man, answering her friend's question whether he remembers them, says he would recall the qualities of the bright-eyed woman if he ever forgot them, but he doesn't know how to forget.","n":"The answer is a logical joke that turns the friend's question over: remembering presupposes forgetting, so no, he never thinks of her. Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar both mark this as the end of the man's speech.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:113"},"k:1126":{"book":"kural","num":1126,"t":["கண்ணுள்ளின் போகார் இமைப்பின் பருகுவரா","நுண்ணியர்எம் காத லவர்"],"p":"My lover would not depart from mine eyes; even if I wink, he would not suffer (from pain); he is so ethereal.","r":"The woman says her lover won't leave her eyes and isn't hurt even if she blinks, so subtle is he.","n":"Parimelazhagar gives this a strategic setting: she fears her friend will blame the absent man, and speaks to herself so that the friend will overhear. Her defence of him is that he hasn't gone anywhere. The invisible subtle body sounds like metaphysics, but he explains it as ceaseless thinking grown so ripe that the man is always before her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kan","ninaivu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:113"},"k:1127":{"book":"kural","num":1127,"t":["கண்ணுள்ளார் காத லவராகக் கண்ணும்","எழுதேம் கரப்பாக்கு அறிந்து"],"p":"As my lover abides in my eyes, I will not even paint them, for he would (then) have to conceal himself.","r":"The woman says that since her lover lives in her eyes, she doesn't even line them with kohl, knowing he would be hidden.","n":"A real sign of separation, an unadorned face, is given a loving explanation. Manakkudavar adds a second one: she is so busy watching him within that she has no time to dress.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","ninaivu","nalam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:113"},"k:1128":{"book":"kural","num":1128,"t":["நெஞ்சத்தார் காத லவராக வெய்துண்டல்","அஞ்சுதும் வேபாக் கறிந்து"],"p":"As my lover is in my heart, I am afraid of eating (anything) hot, for I know it would pain him.","r":"The woman says that since her lover lives in her heart, she is afraid to eat hot food, knowing he would be burned.","n":"Manakkudavar supplies the occasion: her friend has asked why she isn't eating. Loss of appetite is one of the visible symptoms, and this is a graceful evasion of the question.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:113"},"k:1129":{"book":"kural","num":1129,"t":["இமைப்பின் கரப்பாக்கு அறிவல் அனைத்திற்கே","ஏதிலர் என்னும் இவ் வூர்"],"p":"I will not wink, knowing that if I did, my lover would hide himself; and for this reason, this town says, he is unloving.","r":"The woman says she won't blink, knowing he would be hidden, and for this much the town calls him loveless.","n":"Parimelazhagar sees a quarrel behind 'this town': the friend has criticised the man to help her endure, and she, hurt, refuses to name the friend, calling her the town. Sleeplessness, the very evidence against him, she presents as her own act of care.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","kan","alar"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:113"},"k:1130":{"book":"kural","num":1130,"t":["உவந்துறைவர் உள்ளத்துள் என்றும் இகந்துறைவர்","ஏதிலர் என்னும் இவ் வூர்"],"p":"My lover dwells in my heart with perpetual delight; but the town says he is unloving and (therefore) dwells afar.","r":"The woman says he lives joyfully in her heart always, while this town says he is loveless and lives apart.","n":"The last words are identical to 1129's, and the refrain suggests how worn out she is by this argument. Whether her certainty is comfort or denial, the verse doesn't say.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["ninaivu","alar","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:113"},"k:1131":{"book":"kural","num":1131,"t":["காமம் உழந்து வருந்தினார்க்கு ஏமம்","மடலல்லது இல்லை வலி"],"p":"To those who after enjoyment of sexual pleasure suffer (for want of more), there is no help so efficient as the palmyra horse.","r":"The man says that for those who suffer in love there is no safeguard and no strength but the palmyra horse.","n":"ஏமம, 'safeguard', is a surprising word for a public humiliation. The safety he means is pressure: Parimelazhagar explains that the man has given up on the friend's help and intends to get his way as men of old did.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["madal","kaamam","kaamanoy"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","உழந்து assumes that he has already enjoyed love and has now lost access to it.","அரியராய மகளிரோடு காமத்தை அனுபவித்துப் பின் அது பெறாது துன்புற்ற ஆடவர்க்கு"],["manakkudavar","The word means only striving and suffering because of love, and says nothing of past union.","காமம் காரணமாக முயன்று வருந்தினார்க்கு"]],"ch":"k:114"},"k:1132":{"book":"kural","num":1132,"t":["நோனா உடம்பும் உயிரும் மடலேறும்","நாணினை நீக்கி நிறுத்து"],"p":"Having got rid of shame, the suffering body and soul save themselves on the palmyra horse.","r":"The man says his body and life, unable to bear it any longer, will mount the palmyra horse, once shame has been put aside.","n":"He says body and life will do this, and not 'I', as though his person had dissolved into what still suffers. Parimelazhagar adds that with the threat, the man is really looking for the friend to see his desperation and grant his plea quickly.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["madal","naanam","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:114"},"k:1133":{"book":"kural","num":1133,"t":["நாணொடு நல்லாண்மை பண்டுடையேன் இன்றுடையேன்","காமுற்றார் ஏறும் மடல்"],"p":"Modesty and manliness were once my own; now, my own is the palmyra horse that is ridden by the lustful.","r":"The man says he once had shame and sound manliness, and today he has the palmyra horse that lovers ride.","n":"The form is an inventory of property, then and now. Parimelazhagar defines the two lost goods: shame keeps one from base acts, and manliness is not giving way to anything. Notably, it is the man who claims shame here; நாண் in the Kural isn't only a woman's virtue.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","aanmai","madal"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:114"},"k:1134":{"book":"kural","num":1134,"t":["காமக் கடும்புனல் உய்க்கும் நாணொடு","நல்லாண்மை என்னும் புணை"],"p":"The raft of modesty and manliness, is, alas, carried-off by the strong current of lust.","r":"The man says that the fierce flood of love carries off the raft of shame and manliness.","n":"Parimelazhagar gives a neat debating context: she objects that shame and manliness are rafts against the flood, and so can't be lost. He concedes that they are rafts, and answers that this flood takes the raft.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","naanam","aanmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:114"},"k:1135":{"book":"kural","num":1135,"t":["தொடலைக் குறுந்தொடி தந்தாள் மடலொடு","மாலை உழக்கும் துயர்"],"p":"Has brought me 'horse of palm,' and pangs of eventide!","r":"The man says that the girl with bangles like a garland has given him the palmyra horse and the anguish of evening.","n":"Parimelazhagar's explanatory note gives the point: love exists at all times but flowers at evening, and the very name for her implies a young girl, who only echoes her friend. So the man tells the friend that what the girl gave, the friend can take away.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["madal","maalai","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:114"},"k:1136":{"book":"kural","num":1136,"t":["மடலூர்தல் யாமத்தும் உள்ளுவேன் மன்ற","படல்ஒல்லா பேதைக்கென் கண்"],"p":"Mine eyes will not close in sleep on your mistress's account; even at midnight will I think of mounting the palmyra horse.","r":"The man says that because of that simple girl his eyes can't close, so even at midnight he will surely think of mounting the palmyra horse.","n":"Parimelazhagar stresses that பேதை here refers to her foolishness and not her age. Manakkudavar has the friend teasing that he'll forget by morning, and his answer turns his insomnia into a guarantee.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["madal","kan","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:114"},"k:1137":{"book":"kural","num":1137,"t":["கடலன்ன காமம் உழந்தும் மடலேறாப்","பெண்ணின் பெருந்தக்க தில்"],"p":"There is nothing so noble as the womanly nature that would not ride the palmyra horse, though plunged a sea of lust.","r":"Nothing has greater worth than womanhood, which suffers a sea of desire and doesn't mount the palmyra horse; the speaker is the man for Parimelazhagar and the friend for Manakkudavar.","n":"The praise also rests on a restriction: she may not take the only remedy he has been boasting about. Who says it changes its force, since from him it is an excuse, and from the friend it is a demand.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["madal","kaamam","naanam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The man answers the friend's taunt that men ought to endure better: it is the birth of women that has this restraint, and he lacks it.","'பிறப்பு விசேடத்தால் அவ்வடக்கம் எனக்கு இல்லையாகா நின்றது; நீ அஃது அறிகின்றிலை,' என்பதாம்."],["manakkudavar","The friend tells the man that his beloved suffers just as much, and that women's lot is simply not to ride, to calm his distress.","தலைமகன் ஆற் றாமை நீங்குதற்பொருட்டுத் தோழி கூறியது."]],"ch":"k:114"},"k:1138":{"book":"kural","num":1138,"t":["நிறையரியர் மன்அளியர் என்னாது காமம்","மறையிறந்து மன்று படும்"],"p":"Even the Lust (of women) transgresses its secrecy and appears in public, forgetting that they are too chaste and liberal (to be overcome by it).","r":"Love doesn't stop to think that they are firm in restraint or deserving of pity, but breaks through secrecy and comes out in public; the speaker is the woman for Parimelazhagar and the friend for Manakkudavar.","n":"Parimelazhagar sees a hint under the generalisation: she seems to talk about all women, but means her own love has grown, and that the matter must now be disclosed to the family.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["nirai","alar","kaamam","kalaviyal"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The woman speaks, her guarded love having grown; desire isn't afraid of women's firmness, நிறை அரியர்.","'இவர் நிறையால் நாம் மீதூர்தற்கு அரியர்' என்று அஞ்சுதல் செய்யாது"],["manakkudavar","The friend speaks to refuse a daytime meeting because of scandal, and the woman lacks restraint.","இஃது அம்பலம் அலரும் ஆகாவென்று தோழி பகற்குறி மறுத்தது."]],"ch":"k:114"},"k:1139":{"book":"kural","num":1139,"t":["அறிகிலார் எல்லாரும் என்றேஎன் காமம்","மறுகின் மறுகும் மருண்டு"],"p":"My lust, feeling that it is not known by all, reels confused in the streets (of this town).","r":"The woman says that her love, thinking that not everyone knows, wanders bewildered round the streets.","n":"Desire is made a separate creature, resentful of her former self-control. She can't ride the horse, so, in effect, her love goes out into the street without her. Parimelazhagar assigns the two verbs to two stages of gossip, அம்பல் and அலர்.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kaamam","alar"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:114"},"k:1140":{"book":"kural","num":1140,"t":["யாம்கண்ணின் காண நகுப அறிவில்லார்","யாம்பட்ட தாம்படா ஆறு"],"p":"Even strangers laugh (at us) so as to be seen by us, for they have not suffered.","r":"The woman says fools laugh within her sight because they have not suffered as she has.","n":"Parimelazhagar names the unnamed fools: it is her friend, who has told her family and now teases her. He also reports, and rejects, a reading in which the man says this to the friend, because a man's speech wouldn't fit here at this point in the chapter.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","alar"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:114"},"k:1141":{"book":"kural","num":1141,"t":["அலரெழ ஆருயிர் நிற்கும் அதனைப்","பலரறியார் பாக்கியத் தால்"],"p":"My precious life is saved by the raise of rumour, and this, to my good luck no others are aware of.","r":"A precious life holds on because the gossip has risen, and by good fortune the many don't know this; for Parimelazhagar the man speaks of his own life, while Manakkudavar speaks of her life and leaves the speaker unnamed.","n":"The joke rests on the second line: if the gossips knew they were helping, they would stop. Commentators differ on the source of luck. Parimelazhagar says தெய்வத்தால் and Manakkudavar says புண்ணியத்தாலே; one is divine and the other is merit, for a verse whose word is the Sanskrit-derived பாக்கியம்.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alar","uyir"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The man, unable to reach her, is sustained by talk that makes her seem attainable.","அங்ஙனம் அரியாளை எளியளாக்கி எடுக்கின்றமையின், அஃது அவ்வாருயிர்க்குப் பற்றுக்கோடாக நின்றது"],["manakkudavar","It is her life that the gossip sustains.","அவளது ஆருயிர் நிற்கும்"],["kalaignar","It is the couple's life, which survives on hope that talk will help their love succeed.","எம் உயிர் போகாமல் இருக்கிறது"]],"ch":"k:115"},"k:1142":{"book":"kural","num":1142,"t":["மலரன்ன கண்ணாள் அருமை அறியாது","அலரெமக்கு ஈந்ததிவ் வூர்"],"p":"Not knowing the value of her whose eyes are like flowers this town has got up a rumour about me.","r":"The man says this town, not knowing how rare the flower-eyed woman is, has made him a present of gossip.","n":"ஈந்தது is the verb for a generous gift. Parimelazhagar takes it that way, as an ironic thanks. The rarity differs by commentator: for Parimelazhagar, she is hard to reach, and for Manakkudavar, it is her noble birth that the gossips ignore.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alar","nalam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:115"},"k:1143":{"book":"kural","num":1143,"t":["உறாஅதோ ஊரறிந்த கெளவை அதனைப்","பெறாஅது பெற்றன்ன நீர்த்து"],"p":"Will I not get a rumour that is known to the (whole) town ? For what I have not got is as if I had got it (already).","r":"The man asks whether the gossip the whole town knows isn't his to have, since it feels like having what he hasn't yet obtained.","n":"The gossip isn't true yet, and that is its use: being talked about as a couple gives a foretaste of being one. Manakkudavar gives a more practical reason, in which the man tells the friend that the talk will bring the families round.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["alar","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:115"},"k:1144":{"book":"kural","num":1144,"t":["கவ்வையால் கவ்விது காமம் அதுவின்றேல்","தவ்வென்னும் தன்மை இழந்து"],"p":"Rumour increases the violence of my passion; without it it would grow weak and waste away.","r":"The man says gossip makes his love flourish, and that without it the love would lose its nature and droop.","n":"The sound carries the argument: கவ்வை, கவ்விது, தவ்வென்னும். Parimelazhagar explains கவ்விது as a coinage from the noun, and தவ் as a word that imitates sagging. The claim is that love depends in part on being seen.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alar","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:115"},"k:1145":{"book":"kural","num":1145,"t":["களித்தொறும் கள்ளுண்டல் வேட்டற்றால் காமம்","வெளிப்படுந் தோறும் இனிது"],"p":"As drinking liquor is delightful (to one) whenever one is in mirth, so is lust delightful to me whenever it is the subject of rumour.","r":"Love grows sweeter each time it is exposed, as drinking becomes more desired each time one gets drunk; the speaker is the man for Parimelazhagar and the friend for Manakkudavar.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alar","kaamam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The man says that his own desire is so strong that gossip itself gives him pleasure.","வேட்கை மிகுதியால் அலரும் இன்பஞ் செய்யாநின்றது என்பதாம்."],["manakkudavar","The friend replies to the man, who has asked whether this gossip is hurting them.","நுமக்குத் துன்பமாயிற்றே இவ்வலரென்று வினாவிய தலைமகற்குத் தோழி கூறியது."]],"ch":"k:115"},"k:1146":{"book":"kural","num":1146,"t":["கண்டது மன்னும் ஒருநாள் அலர்மன்னும்","திங்களைப் பாம்புகொண் டற்று"],"p":"It was but a single day that I looked on (my lover); but the rumour thereof has spread like the seizure of the moon by the serpent.","r":"The woman says she saw him on only one day, and the gossip has spread like the serpent seizing the moon.","n":"An eclipse is an event no one can miss, and a tiny private act is set against it. Parimelazhagar has her speaking to her friend with the man in earshot, so the complaint is really a demand for marriage. Kalaignar pauses to mark the serpent as an imaginary notion, which the commentators before him don't do.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alar","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:115"},"k:1147":{"book":"kural","num":1147,"t":["ஊரவர் கெளவை எருவாக அன்னைசொல்","நீராக நீளும்இந் நோய்"],"p":"This malady (of lust) is manured by the talk of women and watered by the (harsh) words of my mother.","r":"The woman says this sickness grows long, with the town's gossip as its manure and her mother's words as its water.","n":"Whatever is meant to shrink the love feeds it, as Parimelazhagar spells out. The one verse in the chapter to name her mother shows where the gossip actually hurts: at home. Parimelazhagar takes ஊரவர் to mean the women of the town.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kaamanoy","alar"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:115"},"k:1148":{"book":"kural","num":1148,"t":["நெய்யால் எரிநுதுப்பேம் என்றற்றால் கெளவையால்","காமம் நுதுப்பேம் எனல்"],"p":"To say that one could extinguish passion by rumour is like extinguishing fire with ghee.","r":"The woman says that to think gossip can put out love is like thinking ghee can put out fire.","n":"The grammar frames this as an error in thought, 'to say we will extinguish'. Parimelazhagar places it in the same scene as 1147, so it is also her answer to the friend's advice that she should take the talk seriously and bear up.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["alar","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:115"},"k:1149":{"book":"kural","num":1149,"t":["அலர்நாண ஒல்வதோ அஞ்சலோம்பு என்றார்","பலர்நாண நீத்தக் கடை"],"p":"When the departure of him who said \"fear not\" has put me to shame before others, why need I be ashamed of scandal.","r":"The woman asks how she can be ashamed of gossip when the man who told her not to fear has left her, in a way that shames everyone who sees it.","n":"This verse is the turn in the chapter, with gossip now no longer a game. The promise 'fear not' is quoted against him. Shame has moved from what she feels about the town to what those watching feel on her behalf, so she has none left to spend on rumour.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alar","naanam","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:115"},"k:1150":{"book":"kural","num":1150,"t":["தாம்வேண்டின் நல்குவர் காதலர் யாம்வேண்டும்","கெளவை எடுக்கும்இவ் வூர்"],"p":"The rumour I desire is raised by the town (itself); and my lover would if desired consent (to my following him).","r":"The speaker, whom Parimelazhagar identifies as the friend talking to the woman while the man overhears, says the town is raising the very gossip they want, and the lover will grant it if asked or if he wishes.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["alar","kalaviyal"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","தாம் வேண்டின் means 'if we ask', and the friend is steering the man towards elopement, which the gossip now justifies.","இனிக் காதலர்தாமும்யாம் வேண்டியக்கால் அதனை இனிதின் நேர்வர்"],["kalaignar","The words mean 'if the lover also wishes', and his consent depends on his own desire.","காதலரும் விரும்பினால் அதை ஒப்புக் கொள்வார்."]],"ch":"k:115"},"k:1151":{"book":"kural","num":1151,"t":["செல்லாமை உண்டேல் எனக்குரை மற்றுநின்","வல்வரவு வாழ்வார்க் குரை"],"p":"If it is not departure, tell me; but if it is your speedy return, tell it to those who would be alive then.","r":"If you aren't leaving, tell me; if you mean to return quickly, tell that to those who will still be alive by then; the speaker is the friend for Parimelazhagar and the woman for Manakkudavar.","n":"One of the book's sharpest lines, a refusal to accept comfort. If the friend is the speaker, then Parimelazhagar's explanation for 'me' matters: she says எனக்கு for the woman because the two aren't distinct. He notes that some give the verse to the woman.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","uyir"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The friend answers the man, who has promised a quick return, and aims to stop his leaving.","'பிரிந்து கடிதின் வருவல்' என்ற தலைமகற்குத் தோழி சொல்லியது."],["manakkudavar","The woman answers the friend, who has told her he will come back soon.","இது கடித்து வருவாரென்ற தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் ஆற்றாமையாற் கூறி யது."]],"ch":"k:116"},"k:1152":{"book":"kural","num":1152,"t":["இன்கண் உடைத்தவர் பார்வல் பிரிவஞ்சும்","புன்கண் உடைத்தால் புணர்வு"],"p":"His very look was once pleasing; but (now) even intercourse is painful through fear of separation.","r":"The woman says that பார்வல் alone once gave her delight, and now union itself holds the pain of fearing separation.","n":"The cruelty is that the most that love can give is spoiled by what it anticipates. Parimelazhagar cites Kalittokai for the telling sign: he now fusses over her more than before, which is how she knows he is leaving.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","punarchi","kaamanoy"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","பார்வல் is his look back in courtship, sweet because it promised union.","அவர் நோக்கு மாத்திரமும் புணர்ச்சி குறித்தமையான் நமக்கு இன்ப முடைத்தாயிருக்கும்"],["manakkudavar","It is her own watching for his arrival, a present joy set against the anxiety of being with him.","நங்காதலரை வரவு பார்த்திருக்கு மது"]],"ch":"k:116"},"k:1153":{"book":"kural","num":1153,"t":["அரிதரோ தேற்றம் அறிவுடையார் கண்ணும்","பிரிவோ ரிடத்துண்மை யான்"],"p":"As even the lover who understands (everything) may at times depart, confidence is hardly possible.","r":"The woman says trust is hard, since parting sometimes happens even with those who understand.","n":"அறிவுடையார் isn't a compliment to his learning. Parimelazhagar's gloss is that he knows his own word and knows she can't bear it, which makes his going worse. The verse shifts from this man's fault to the general difficulty of trusting anyone.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:116"},"k:1154":{"book":"kural","num":1154,"t":["அளித்தஞ்சல் என்றவர் நீப்பின் தெளித்தசொல்","தேறியார்க்கு உண்டோ தவறு"],"p":"If he who bestowed his love and said \"fear not\" should depart, will it be the fault of those who believed in (his) assuring words ?","r":"The woman asks, if the one who was gracious and told her not to fear should leave, whether any fault lies with those who believed his assuring words.","n":"She refers to herself in the third person as தேறியார், 'those who trusted', which both commentators note. This turns the private complaint into a general moral question about a promise and who answers for it. Parimelazhagar names the fault as the mismatch between his word and deed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:116"},"k:1155":{"book":"kural","num":1155,"t":["ஓம்பின் அமைந்தார் பிரிவோம்பல் மற்றவர்","நீங்கின் அரிதால் புணர்வு"],"p":"If you would save (my life), delay the departure of my destined (husband); for if he departs, intercourse will become impossible.","r":"The woman tells her friend that if she wants to protect her, she must prevent the departure of the one who is fit for her, because once he goes, union will be hard.","n":"அமைந்தார் is a quiet word: the one who is fitting. Parimelazhagar adds divine ordering, saying he is the one fit by தெய்வம் to be her lord, and Pope follows with 'destined'. The verse itself doesn't mention destiny. He also explains why union would be hard: her life would have gone with him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","punarchi","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:116"},"k:1156":{"book":"kural","num":1156,"t":["பிரிவுரைக்கும் வன்கண்ணர் ஆயின் அரிதவர்","நல்குவர் என்னும் நசை"],"p":"If he is so cruel as to mention his departure (to me), the hope that he would bestow (his love) must be given up.","r":"The woman says that if he can be so hard as to speak of leaving, the hope that he would come back and be gracious is pointless.","n":"The offence is the announcement, even more than the journey. For Parimelazhagar, one who could plan this while still at her side isn't likely to soften at a distance.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:116"},"k:1157":{"book":"kural","num":1157,"t":["துறைவன் துறந்தமை தூற்றாகொல் முன்கை","இறைஇறவா நின்ற வளை"],"p":"Do not the rings that begin to slide down my fingers forebode the separation of my lord ?","r":"The woman asks if the bangles slipping past her wrist don't already announce that the lord of the shore has left her.","n":"தூற்றா is the verb for spreading gossip, so her own body has joined the town. Parimelazhagar sees a rebuke to the friend, who brings the news: 'you too only do what these bangles do', when she should have stopped him. The past tense 'has left' is anticipatory, since the news alone has made her thin.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["melivu","pirivu","alar"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:116"},"k:1158":{"book":"kural","num":1158,"t":["இன்னாது இனன்இல்ஊர் வாழ்தல் அதனினும்","இன்னாது இனியார்ப் பிரிவு"],"p":"Painful is it to live in a friendless town; but far more painful is it to part from one's lover.","r":"The woman says living in a town without one's own people is painful, and being parted from the one who is dear is more painful still.","n":"This sounds like a proverb, but Parimelazhagar says it is aimed. Her friend agreed to his leaving instead of preventing it, and so she speaks as if she has no companion here at all, while seeming only to speak about the way of the world.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pirivu","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:116"},"k:1159":{"book":"kural","num":1159,"t":["தொடிற்சுடின் அல்லது காமநோய் போல","விடிற்சுடல் ஆற்றுமோ தீ"],"p":"Fire burns when touched; but, like the sickness of love, can it also burn when removed ?","r":"Fire burns only when touched, and can it burn when one moves away, as the sickness of love does? The speaker is the woman for Parimelazhagar and the friend for Manakkudavar.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","pirivu","kaamam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The woman answers the friend, who has said love burns only where it is, like fire, and that she should therefore bear it.","'காமம் தீயே போன்று தான் நின்ற இடத்தைச் சுடுமாகலான் நீ ஆற்றல் வேண்டும்' என்ற தோழிக்குச் சொல்லியது."],["manakkudavar","The friend speaks, on seeing the woman's distress after the man has left.","தலைமகன் பிரிந்துழித் தலைமகளாற்றாமை கண்டு தோழிகூறியது."]],"ch":"k:116"},"k:1160":{"book":"kural","num":1160,"t":["அரிதாற்றி அல்லல்நோய் நீக்கிப் பிரிவாற்றிப்","பின்இருந்து வாழ்வார் பலர்"],"p":"As if there were many indeed that can consent to the impossible, kill their pain, endure separation and yet continue to live afterwards.","r":"The woman says that many agree to the impossible, remove their pain, endure the parting, and stay on alive afterwards.","n":"It is all in the tone. Taken flatly, as Pope's couplet takes it, this says many cope; read as Parimelazhagar does, 'many' means no one, and the unstated close is that she too will die. Kalaignar makes the turn explicit by adding, 'but I?'. The three verbs slow down the task of enduring.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","uyir","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:116"},"k:1161":{"book":"kural","num":1161,"t":["மறைப்பேன்மன் யானிஃதோ நோயை இறைப்பவர்க்கு","ஊற்றுநீர் போல மிகும்"],"p":"I would hide this pain from others; but it (only) swells like a spring to those who drain it.","r":"The woman says she would hide this pain, but, as water does in a spring when one draws from it, it only rises.","n":"The commentators differ quietly over what the drawing out is. Kalaignar makes concealing itself the action, so hiding is what increases it. For Parimelazhagar, the concealment simply fails, as water wells up for one who draws from a spring.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","nirai","naanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:117"},"k:1162":{"book":"kural","num":1162,"t":["கரத்தலும் ஆற்றேன்இந் நோயைநோய் செய்தார்க்கு","உரைத்தலும் நாணுத் தரும்"],"p":"I cannot conceal this pain, nor can I relate it without shame to him who has caused it.","r":"The woman says she cannot hide this sickness, and telling the one who caused it also brings her shame.","n":"Parimelazhagar gives a remarkable reason for the shame: if she sends a messenger, it will imply she is still alive. The grief that is meant to prove love would be disproved by being reported. Her friend has offered her two options, and she refuses both.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","naanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:117"},"k:1163":{"book":"kural","num":1163,"t":["காமமும் நாணும் உயிர்காவாத் தூங்கும்என்","நோனா உடம்பின் அகத்து"],"p":"(Both) lust and shame, with my soul for their shoulder pole balance themselves on a body that cannot bear them.","r":"The woman says love and shame hang from the ends of a carrying pole, which is her life, within her body that can't bear it.","n":"The verb தூங்கும் conveys the balance: as Parimelazhagar reads it, neither side outweighs the other, so she can't commit to either, and the pole will snap. Kalaignar shifts the image so that the body is the pole; the verse says the life is.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kaamam","naanam","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:117"},"k:1164":{"book":"kural","num":1164,"t":["காமக் கடல்மன்னும் உண்டே அதுநீந்தும்","ஏமப் புணைமன்னும் இல்"],"p":"There is indeed a flood of lust; but there is no raft of safety to cross it with.","r":"The woman says the sea of love certainly exists, and the safe raft for crossing it certainly doesn't.","n":"Parimelazhagar takes the missing raft as a reproach to the friend, who should have sent word and been the raft. Manakkudavar agrees that she means she has no helper. It is more personal than a cosmic complaint.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:117"},"k:1165":{"book":"kural","num":1165,"t":["துப்பின் எவனாவர் மன்கொல் துயர்வரவு","நட்பினுள் ஆற்று பவர்"],"p":"He who can produce sorrow from friendship, what can he not bring forth out of enmity ?","r":"The woman asks what those who can bring such grief in friendship would do in enmity.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","pirivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It is a rebuke to the friend, for not sending a messenger when she could have prevented the grief.","தூது விடாமை நோக்கித் தோழியோடு புலந்து சொல்லியது"],["kalaignar","It is about the lover, who gives her the grief of parting even while they are close.","நட்பாக இருக்கும்போதே பிரிவுத்துயரை நமக்குத் தரக்கூடியவர்"]],"ch":"k:117"},"k:1166":{"book":"kural","num":1166,"t":["இன்பம் கடல்மற்றுக் காமம் அஃதடுங்கால்","துன்பம் அதனிற் பெரிது"],"p":"The pleasure of lust is (as great as) the sea; but the pain of lu st is far greater.","r":"The woman says the pleasure of love is a sea, and when love afflicts her, the pain is greater than the sea.","n":"This rejects a consoling arithmetic. The friend has said that those who've had the joy should expect the pain, and the woman's point, as Parimelazhagar glosses it, is that an equal measure could be borne, but this isn't equal.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kaamanoy","punarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:117"},"k:1167":{"book":"kural","num":1167,"t":["காமக் கடும்புனல் நீந்திக் கரைகாணேன்","யாமத்தும் யானே உளேன்"],"p":"I have swam across the terrible flood of lust, but have not seen its shore; even at midnight I am alone; still I live.","r":"The woman says she swims the fierce flood of love and sees no shore, and even at midnight there is only herself.","n":"யானே உளேன் can mean 'I alone am here', and for Parimelazhagar also 'I am still alive', which he attributes to some evil deed's result. That idea of past deeds is his and isn't in the verse. Pope's prose follows him by adding 'still I live'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kaamanoy","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:117"},"k:1168":{"book":"kural","num":1168,"t":["மன்னுயிர் எல்லாம் துயிற்றி அளித்திரா","என்னல்லது இல்லை துணை"],"p":"The night which graciously lulls to sleep all living creatures, has me alone for her companion.","r":"The woman says the night, which puts all living beings to sleep, is to be pitied, as it has no companion but her.","n":"Everything turns on அளித்து. It is usually a word of pity or grace, and Parimelazhagar marks it as contempt: the night has foolishly chosen as its companion someone about to die. She isn't lamenting her solitude so much as mocking her only companion.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:117"},"k:1169":{"book":"kural","num":1169,"t":["> கொடியார் கொடுமையின் தாம்கொடிய இந்நாள்","நெடிய கழியும் இரா"],"p":"The long nights of these days are far more cruel than the heartless one who is torturing me.","r":"The woman says these long nights passing now are crueller than the cruelty of that cruel man.","n":"Parimelazhagar glosses the implicit contrast: nights that were short when they were happy have now grown long. The word கொடியார், 'the cruel one', replaces his name.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pirivu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:117"},"k:1170":{"book":"kural","num":1170,"t":["உள்ளம்போன்று உள்வழிச் செல்கிற்பின் வெள்ளநீர்","நீந்தல மன்னோஎன் கண்"],"p":"Could mine eyes travel like my thoughts to the abode (of my absent lord), they would not swim in this flood of tears.","r":"The woman says that if her eyes could go where he is as her heart does, they wouldn't be swimming in a flood of tears.","n":"For Parimelazhagar, the mind's journey is only thought, while eyes need a body to walk there, so what the heart can do isn't really a substitute for seeing. He says this verse opens the next chapter, and the eyes' complaint begins here.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kan","nenju","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:117"},"k:1171":{"book":"kural","num":1171,"t":["கண்தாம் கலுழ்வ தெவன்கொலோ தண்டாநோய்","தாம்காட்ட யாம்கண் டது"],"p":"As this incurable malady has been caused by my eyes which showed (him) to me, why should they now weep for (him).","r":"The woman asks why her eyes weep, when it was they who showed him to her, and so she came to know this unending sickness.","n":"The verse says 'we saw the sickness' with no mention of him, and the object of 'showed' has to be supplied, as Parimelazhagar says. In her account, seeing him and seeing the sickness are the same act.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kan","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:118"},"k:1172":{"book":"kural","num":1172,"t":["தெரிந்துணரா நோக்கிய உண்கண் பரிந்துணராப்","பைதல் உழப்பது எவன்?"],"p":"The dyed eyes that (then) looked without foresight, why should they now endure sorrow, without feeling sharply (their own fault).","r":"The woman asks why these kohl-lined eyes, which looked as they did, now suffer in misery without coming to terms with it.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","kaamanoy","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","தெரிந்துணரா means they looked without considering what would follow, so they should now bear what they caused.","மேல் விளைவதனை ஆராய்ந்தறியாது அன்று காதலரை நோக்கி நின்ற உண்கண்கள்"],["manakkudavar","It means the opposite: they looked after judging him good, and now they no longer recognise that goodness.","முன்பு அவர் நல்லரென்று தெரிந்து உணர்ந்து நோக்கிய உண்கண் கள்"]],"ch":"k:118"},"k:1173":{"book":"kural","num":1173,"t":["கதுமெனத் தாநோக்கித் தாமே கலுழும்","இதுநகத் தக்க துடைத்து"],"p":"They themselves looked eagerly (on him) and now they weep. Is not this to be laughed at ?","r":"The woman says it's laughable that her eyes looked at him so quickly on their own and now weep on their own.","n":"The repeated தாம் is the whole charge: they acted of their own accord both times. Manakkudavar pictures real laughter, a distracted woman laughing through her distress and being asked why, which makes this more disturbing than wit.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:118"},"k:1174":{"book":"kural","num":1174,"t":["பெயலாற்றா நீருலந்த உண்கண் உயலாற்றா","உய்வில்நோய் என்கண் நிறுத்து"],"p":"These painted eyes have caused me a lasting mortal disease; and now they can weep no more, the tears having dried up.","r":"The woman says her kohl-lined eyes, having fixed in her an inescapable, fatal sickness, have dried up and can no longer even weep.","n":"Dry eyes come after much weeping. What seems a relief is presented as the eyes' punishment: in Parimelazhagar's words, harm they did her has come back to them by itself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:118"},"k:1175":{"book":"kural","num":1175,"t":["படலாற்றா பைதல் உழக்கும் கடலாற்றாக்","காமநோய் செய்தஎன் கண்"],"p":"Mine eyes have caused me a lust that is greater than the sea and (they themselves) endure the torture of sleeplessness.","r":"The woman says her eyes, which gave her a sickness too large for the sea, now suffer torments and can't sleep.","n":"The commentators use the language of moral consequence with some wit: Parimelazhagar says அத் தீவினையால், 'by that evil deed', and Manakkudavar has harm returning to those who harm others. The verse just shows the sequence.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kan","kaamanoy","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:118"},"k:1176":{"book":"kural","num":1176,"t":["ஓஒ இனிதே எமக்கிந்நோய் செய்தகண்","தாஅம் இதற்பட் டது"],"p":"The eyes that have given me this disease have themselves been seized with this (suffering). Oh! I am much delighted.","r":"The woman exclaims how sweet it is that the eyes that gave her this sickness have fallen into it themselves.","n":"Vengeance against one's own organ can only hurt the avenger, and that absurdity is the point. Parimelazhagar's gloss, 'as if my suffering had ended', keeps the distance between the delight and any real relief.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:118"},"k:1177":{"book":"kural","num":1177,"t":["உழந்துழந் துள்நீர் அறுக விழைந்திழைந்து","வேண்டி அவர்க்கண்ட கண்"],"p":"The eyes that became tender and gazed intently on him, may they suffer so much as to dry up the fountain of their tears.","r":"The woman curses them: may the eyes that longed and melted and begged to see him suffer on until their inner water is gone.","n":"The verse echoes itself: உழந்துழந்து against விழைந்திழைந்து, suffering matching longing in the same rhythm. Pay-back is built into the sound.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","kaamanoy","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:118"},"k:1178":{"book":"kural","num":1178,"t":["பேணாது பெட்டார் உளர்மன்னோ மற்றவர்க்","காணாது அமைவில கண்"],"p":"He is indeed here who loved me with his lips but not with his heart but mine eyes suffer from not seeing him.","r":"The woman says there is one who desired without cherishing, and her eyes can't be at peace without seeing him.","n":"The syntax allows several readings, and the second one Parimelazhagar gives rests on a different text, கொண்கனை. I'm unsure which is best. The steady part is the last clause: whatever he is, the eyes can't do without him.","x":1,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kan","pirivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The friend says he hasn't really gone away. She replies: yes, someone who loved in words and not at heart is here, but what use is that if the eyes don't see him?","நெஞ்சால் விழையாது வைத்துச் சொல் மாத்திரத்தால் விழைந்தவர் இவ்விடத்தே உளர்"],["manakkudavar","It's a question, with no claim that he's near: does anyone desire what shouldn't be desired? He has gone without wishing to see them, and the eyes still can't settle.","விரும்பத்தகாததனை விரும்புவாரும் உளரோ?"]],"ch":"k:118"},"k:1179":{"book":"kural","num":1179,"t":["வாராக்கால் துஞ்சா வரின்துஞ்சா ஆயிடை","ஆரஞர் உற்றன கண்"],"p":"When he is away they do not sleep; when he is present they do not sleep; in either case, mine eyes endure unbearable agony.","r":"The woman says that when he doesn't come her eyes don't sleep, and when he does they still don't sleep, so either way they suffer terribly.","n":"Parimelazhagar supplies the two reasons: they stay up watching for him, and when he is there they stay up for fear he'll leave. Kalaignar's gloss reads this more generally, as the lot of all lovers' eyes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","pirivu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:118"},"k:1180":{"book":"kural","num":1180,"t":["மறைபெறல் ஊரார்க்கு அரிதன்றால் எம்போல்","அறைபறை கண்ணார் அகத்து"],"p":"It is not difficult for the people of this place to understand the secret of those whose eyes, like mine, are as it were beaten drums.","r":"The woman says it isn't hard for the town to learn the secret in the hearts of those like her, whose eyes are beaten drums.","n":"The eyes' final offence is treason. The friend has asked her to hide his cruelty so the town won't blame him, and she answers that the eyes make that impossible. Parimelazhagar notes the variant reading அறைபறைக் கண்ணார், which removes the poetic strain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","alar","nirai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:118"},"k:1181":{"book":"kural","num":1181,"t":["நயந்தவர்க்கு நல்காமை நேர்ந்தேன் பசந்தவென்","பண்பியார்க்கு உரைக்கோ பிற"],"p":"I who (then) consented to the absence of my loving lord, to whom can I (now) relate the fact of my having turned sallow.","r":"The woman, to herself, asks to whom she can describe her pallor, since she herself agreed to the absence of the man who loves her.","n":"Consent has closed off complaint. Parimelazhagar reads every word of this as her logic: having agreed, she feels it would be a further wrong even to tell anyone. 1181 is the first verse of the chapter, so the book's most visible symptom starts with her taking responsibility for it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:119"},"k:1182":{"book":"kural","num":1182,"t":["அவர்தந்தார் என்னும் தகையால் இவர்தந்தென்","மேனிமேல் ஊரும் பசப்பு"],"p":"","r":"The woman says the pallor climbs on and rides over her body, proud that he is the one who gave it.","n":"Parimelazhagar uses the chariot-and-rider sense of இவர்தல் and adds that this is a claim of right, like a retainer's, which she can't resist because it comes from him. By his reading she is also comforting her friend: it's riding me by right, so don't worry. For Manakkudavar, she is saying that it isn't her doing, so take it up with him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","melivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:119"},"k:1183":{"book":"kural","num":1183,"t":["சாயலும் நாணும் அவர்கொண்டார் கைம்மாறா","நோயும் பசலையும் தந்து"],"p":"He has taken (away) my beauty and modesty, and given me instead disease and sallowness.","r":"The woman says he took away her grace and modesty, and gave her sickness and pallor in exchange.","n":"The language is commercial: கைம்மாறா, 'in exchange'. Parimelazhagar notices the crossed pairing, in which sickness replaces modesty, since the more she tries to suppress it, the more it grows, and pallor replaces grace. He takes சாயல் as bodily beauty, and Manakkudavar as femininity (பெண்மை).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","naanam","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:119"},"k:1184":{"book":"kural","num":1184,"t":["உள்ளுவன் மன்யான் உரைப்பது அவர்திறமால்","கள்ளம் பிறவோ பசப்பு"],"p":"I think (of him); and what I speak about is but his excellence; still is there sallowness; and this is","r":"The woman says she thinks of him and speaks only of his merits, and still the pallor comes, a kind of cheating.","n":"Parimelazhagar makes this a small argument: body follows mind and speech, and both are faithfully his, so the body shouldn't have paled. The verse never accuses her of faithlessness; the pallor is the deceiver.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:119"},"k:1185":{"book":"kural","num":1185,"t":["உவக்காண்எம் காதலர் செல்வார் இவக்காண்என்","மேனி பசப்பூர் வது"],"p":"Just as my lover departed then, did not sallowness spread here on my person ?","r":"The woman says: look there, her lover is going, and look here, the pallor is spreading over her body.","n":"The verse is built only from the pointing words உவக்காண் and இவக்காண், which turn distance into simultaneity. Parimelazhagar compares his leaving and the pallor's coming to the changing of day and night. He treats it as a memory of a past parting, and Manakkudavar as happening right now.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pasalai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:119"},"k:1186":{"book":"kural","num":1186,"t":["விளக்கற்றம் பார்க்கும் இருளேபோல் கொண்கன்","முயக்கற்றம் பார்க்கும் பசப்பு"],"p":"Just as darkness waits for the failing light; so does sallowness wait for the laxity of my husband's intercourse.","r":"The woman says pallor watches for her lover's embrace to weaken, as darkness watches for a lamp to falter.","n":"Darkness isn't an enemy that attacks; it is simply what is there when the lamp fails. Read so, pallor is her natural state, and his embrace is what holds it off. Parimelazhagar's conclusion: if it waits for this tiny opening, what won't it do in a real separation?","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","punarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:119"},"k:1187":{"book":"kural","num":1187,"t":["புல்லிக் கிடந்தேன் புடைபெயர்ந்தேன் அவ்வளவில்","அள்ளிக்கொள் வற்றே பசப்பு"],"p":"I who was in close embrace just turned aside and the moment I did so, sallowness came on me like something to be seized on.","r":"The woman says she lay in his embrace and turned slightly, and at that the pallor was on her so thick it could be scooped up.","n":"This is the chapter's most vivid hyperbole, and it is almost comic in its domesticity. Manakkudavar's setting makes it sad: he has left without telling her, and the friend sees the change in her next morning and is told this.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pasalai","punarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:119"},"k:1188":{"book":"kural","num":1188,"t":["பசந்தாள் இவள்என்பது அல்லால் இவளைத்","துறந்தார் அவர்என்பார் இல்"],"p":"Besides those who say \"she has turned sallow\" there are none who say \"he has forsaken her\".","r":"The woman says people will say she has grown pale, and no one says he has left her.","n":"A flat remark about how blame is apportioned. The cause is invisible and the symptom is on her skin, so she bears the talk. Parimelazhagar reads the plural 'people' as aimed at the friend, whom she refuses to name in her anger.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pasalai","alar","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:119"},"k:1189":{"book":"kural","num":1189,"t":["பசக்கமன் பட்டாங்கென் மேனி நயப்பித்தார்","நன்னிலையர் ஆவர் எனின்"],"p":"If he is clear of guilt who has conciliated me (to his departure) let my body suffer its due and turn sallow.","r":"The woman says let her body go pale as it will, if the man who persuaded her is in a good state.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","pirivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","நன்னிலையர் means blameless in the friend's eyes: since he must be held faultless, what does her body matter? This is bitter.","முன் இப்பிரிவின் கொடுமையறியாத என்னை இதற்கு உடம்படுத்திப் பிரிந்தவர் தவறிலராகவே வேண்டுவது"],["manakkudavar","The condition means he should suffer as she does, which is close to a curse.","நம்மைக் காதலித்த வரும் நம்மைப் போலத் துன்பமுறுவராயின்"],["kalaignar","It means if he is well, and so it is a loving sacrifice.","காதலர் நலமாக இருப்பார் என்றால்"]],"ch":"k:119"},"k:1190":{"book":"kural","num":1190,"t":["பசப்பெனப் பேர்பெறுதல் நன்றே நயப்பித்தார்","நல்காமை தூற்றார் எனின்"],"p":"It would be good to be said of me that I have turned sallow, if friends do not reproach with unkindness him who pleased me (then).","r":"The woman says that being called pale is good, if they won't spread talk of how unkind the man who won her has been.","n":"Parimelazhagar pushes பசப்பெனப் பேர் to its limit: not 'she has pallor' but 'she is pallor', a loss of identity she says she will welcome. He also says she speaks in scorn at the friend, who has criticised him to help her. Her good name is what she is ready to give for his.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pasalai","alar","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:119"},"k:1191":{"book":"kural","num":1191,"t":["தாம்வீழ்வார் தம்வீழப் பெற்றவர் பெற்றாரே","காமத்துக் காழில் கனி"],"p":"The women who are beloved by those whom they love, have they have not got the stone-less fruit of sexual delight ?","r":"The woman says those who are loved by the ones they love have obtained the stoneless fruit of love.","n":"A fruit without a stone is enjoyment with nothing to spit out. The verse is general and ungendered, while Parimelazhagar makes it 'women loved by husbands', adds that this needs good deeds from before, and supplies the unstated close that she hasn't got this fruit.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","orutalai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:120"},"k:1192":{"book":"kural","num":1192,"t":["வாழ்வார்க்கு வானம் பயந்தற்றால் வீழ்வார்க்கு","வீழ்வார் அளிக்கும் அளி"],"p":"The bestowal of love by the beloved on those who love them is like the rain raining (at the proper season) on those who live by it.","r":"The woman says the grace shown by the beloved to the one who loves them is like the rain to those who live by it.","n":"The grace here is measured and timely, as rain is, and Parimelazhagar takes that to include a man who returns from duty when he should. He brings out the shadow: if the rain fails, those dependent on it die.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","orutalai","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:120"},"k:1193":{"book":"kural","num":1193,"t":["வீழுநர் வீழப் படுவார்க்கு அமையுமே","வாழுநம் என்னும் செருக்கு"],"p":"The pride that says \"we shall live\" suits only those who are loved by their beloved (husbands).","r":"The woman says the pride of saying 'we shall live' suits only those who are loved by those they love.","n":"செருக்கு, 'pride', is usually a vice in the Kural, and here it is a privilege she envies. Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar agree on the corollary: what suits her is death.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["orutalai","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:120"},"k:1194":{"book":"kural","num":1194,"t":["வீழப் படுவார் கெழீஇயிலர் தாம்வீழ்வார்","வீழப் படாஅர் எனின்"],"p":"Even those who are esteemed (by other women) are devoid of excellence, if they are not loved by their beloved.","r":"The woman says that those who are esteemed are still without something, if they aren't loved by the one they love.","n":"Manakkudavar ends with a proverb, கொண்டான் கயிற் கண்டான் காயும்: if the husband is angry at her, all who see her will be too. That gives a social reason why public esteem can't survive this loss.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["orutalai","kaamam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The esteemed one is honoured by chaste women, and கெழீஇயிலர் means she lacks good deeds, that is, she has bad karma.","கெழீஇயின்மை; நல்வினையின்மை"],["manakkudavar","The esteemed one is honoured by the world, and the word means she becomes one without being desired.","விருப்பமில்லா ராவர்"],["kalaignar","The verse is about the partner who withdraws love, who is then thought to lack the sense of friendship.","அந்தக் காதலர் நட்புணர்வு இல்லாதவராகவே கருதப்படுவார்."]],"ch":"k:120"},"k:1195":{"book":"kural","num":1195,"t":["நாம்காதல் கொண்டார் நமக்கெவன் செய்பவோ","தாம்காதல் கொள்ளாக் கடை"],"p":"He who is beloved by me, what will he do to me, if I am not beloved by him ?","r":"The woman asks what those she loves can do for her if they themselves don't love her.","n":"It answers a compliment. Parimelazhagar has the friend say that she bore this well because of her love; the reply is that love has brought her only pain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["orutalai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:120"},"k:1196":{"book":"kural","num":1196,"t":["ஒருதலையான் இன்னாது காமம்காப் போல","இருதலை யானும் இனிது"],"p":"Lust, like the weight of the KAVADI, pains if it lies in one end only but pleases if it is in both.","r":"The woman says love is painful when it is on one side only, and sweet on both sides, like the load of a carrying pole.","n":"It is a physical fact that a load at both ends is easier than half that load at one. The verse therefore doesn't claim only that shared love is pleasant; shared love is also lighter to carry.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["orutalai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:120"},"k:1197":{"book":"kural","num":1197,"t":["பருவரலும் பைதலும் காணான்கொல் காமன்","ஒருவர்கண் நின்றொழுகு வான்"],"p":"Would not cupid who abides and contends in one party (only) witness the pain and sorrow (in that party)?","r":"The woman asks whether Kaman, who works on one side alone, doesn't see her misery and grief.","n":"A rare appearance of a named love god in the Kural, and the verse makes him a partial combatant. The older commentators take the theology seriously: for Parimelazhagar, a god should know all and be impartial, and for Manakkudavar, he wouldn't hurt her if he saw, being a god. Kalaignar's version has him as simply indifferent.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["orutalai","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:120"},"k:1198":{"book":"kural","num":1198,"t":["வீழ்வாரின் இன்சொல் பெறாஅது உலகத்து","வாழ்வாரின் வன்கணார் இல்"],"p":"There is no one in the world so hard-hearted as those who can live without receiving (even) a kind word from their beloved.","r":"The woman says that no one in the world is harder than those who go on living without receiving a kind word from their beloved.","n":"வன்கண் is ambiguous between hardness and courage, and Pope chooses 'braver'. The commentators read it as an accusation she makes against herself. Parimelazhagar notes that any word at all from him would count as sweet.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uyir","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:120"},"k:1199":{"book":"kural","num":1199,"t":["நசைஇயார் நல்கார் எனினும் அவர்மாட்டு","இசையும் இனிய செவிக்கு"],"p":"Though my beloved bestows no love on one, still are his words sweet to my ears.","r":"The woman says that though the man she longs for gives no grace, even இசை from him is sweet to her ears.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","orutalai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","இசை means any word at all about him, even that he isn't coming, and she hasn't got even that.","'அவர் வாரார் என்னுஞ் சொல்லாயினும் அமையும்,' என்பதுபட நின்றது."],["kalaignar","The word means his fame, and words that praise him are honey to her.","அவரைப் பற்றிய புகழ் உரை என் செவிக்குச் செந்தேனாகும்."]],"ch":"k:120"},"k:1200":{"book":"kural","num":1200,"t":["உறாஅர்க்கு உறுநோய் உரைப்பாய் கடலைச்","செறாஅஅய் வாழிய நெஞ்சு"],"p":"Live, O my soul, would you who relate your great sorrow to strangers, try rather to fill up your own sea (of sorrow).","r":"The woman tells her heart that it wants to tell its pain to one who is not close, and that it may as well try to fill up the sea.","n":"வாழிய, 'may you live', is sarcasm here for Parimelazhagar. The chapter ends with her declining what she spent the chapter wanting, and the sea from 1137 and 1164 comes back as a job to be done with a basket. It is an image of utter futility.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["nenju","kaamanoy","orutalai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:120"},"k:1201":{"book":"kural","num":1201,"t":["உள்ளினும் தீராப் பெருமகிழ் செய்தலால்","கள்ளினும் காமம் இனிது"],"p":"Sexuality is sweeter than liquor, because when remembered, it creates a most rapturous delight.","r":"The man says love is sweeter than liquor, because even thinking of it brings a great joy that doesn't fade.","n":"Parimelazhagar's headnote has him speaking to the friend who has carried his message: his claim is also a report of loneliness and faithfulness. The older comparison with liquor gains a new term here, since liquor needs drinking while love needs only memory.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["ninaivu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:121"},"k:1202":{"book":"kural","num":1202,"t":["எனைத்தொனறு ஏனிதேகாண் காமம்தாம் வீழ்வார்","நினைப்ப வருவதொன்று ஏல்"],"p":"Even to think of one's beloved gives one no pain. Sexuality, in any degree, is always delightful.","r":"Love is sweet in every way, since thinking of the beloved brings no pain; the speaker is the man for Parimelazhagar and the woman for Manakkudavar.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["ninaivu","kaamam","pirivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The man says how he has coped: love is sweet in union and in parting alike.","தான் ஆற்றிய வகை கூறியவாறு."],["manakkudavar","The woman rejects the friend's warning that her inability to endure will bring pain.","இது நீ இவ்வாறு ஆற்றாயாகின்றது துன்பம் பயக்குமென்ற தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் மறுத்துக் கூறியது."]],"ch":"k:121"},"k:1203":{"book":"kural","num":1203,"t":["நினைப்பவர் போன்று நினையார்கொல் தும்மல்","சினைப்பது போன்று கெடும்"],"p":"I feel as if I am going to sneeze but do not, and (therefore) my beloved is about to think (of me) but does not.","r":"The woman wonders if he seems about to think of her and doesn't, since a sneeze seems to come and then dies away.","n":"She reads her own body for evidence about his mind, using a folk belief that a sneeze means a distant loved one is thinking of you. Manakkudavar notes that she is repeating what ordinary women say, a rare moment of commentary on popular custom. An aborted sneeze makes for deflating, near-comic evidence.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:121"},"k:1204":{"book":"kural","num":1204,"t":["யாமும் உளேங்கொல் அவர்நெஞ்சத்து எந்நெஞ்சத்து","ஓஒ உளரே அவர்"],"p":"He continues to abide in my soul, do I likewise abide in his ?","r":"The woman asks whether she too has a place in his heart, for, ah, he is always in hers.","n":"The verse uses the same indwelling that she argued for with such confidence in chapter 113, but now with doubt. Parimelazhagar extends the doubt into a tender either-or: is he kept away by unfinished work, or does he not remember her?","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["ninaivu","nenju","orutalai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:121"},"k:1205":{"book":"kural","num":1205,"t":["தம்நெஞ்சத்து எம்மைக் கடிகொண்டார் நாணார்கொல்","எம்நெஞ்சத்து ஓவா வரல்"],"p":"He who has imprisoned me in his soul, is he ashamed to enter incessantly into mine.","r":"The woman asks whether he, who has barred her from his heart, isn't ashamed to keep coming into hers.","n":"Her thoughts of him are treated as his actions, so she can rebuke him for trespassing. கடிகொண்டார் means he has guarded his heart against her; Pope's prose, 'imprisoned me in his soul', gets this backwards, though his couplet has it right.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["ninaivu","nenju","naanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:121"},"k:1206":{"book":"kural","num":1206,"t":["மற்றியான் என்னுளேன் மன்னோ அவரொடி யான்","உற்றநாள் உள்ள உளேன்"],"p":"I live by remembering my (former) intercourse with him; if it were not so, how could I live ?","r":"The woman asks what else she could live by, since she lives only by remembering the days she shared with him.","n":"Parimelazhagar sets it as a reply to the friend's advice to forget. He lists the things she lacks that would otherwise sustain her: his messenger, sending her own. Memory is what is left.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:121"},"k:1207":{"book":"kural","num":1207,"t":["மறப்பின் எவனாவன் மற்கொல் மறப்பறியேன்","உள்ளினும் உள்ளம் சுடும்"],"p":"I have never forgotten (the pleasure); even to think of it burns my soul; could I live, if I should ever forget it ?","r":"The woman asks what would become of her if she forgot, since she doesn't know forgetting, and even remembering burns.","n":"A precise bind: remembering burns her, and forgetting would kill her, as Parimelazhagar supplies. Compare 1206, where memory is what keeps her alive.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","uyir","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:121"},"k:1208":{"book":"kural","num":1208,"t":["எனைத்து நினைப்பினும் காயார் அனைத்தன்றோ","காதலர் செய்யும் சிறப்பு"],"p":"He will not be angry however much I may think of him; is it not so much the delight my beloved affords me ?","r":"The woman says that however much she thinks of him, he doesn't get angry; that much is the honour her lover does her.","n":"All she can claim is that he doesn't object to being thought of. Parimelazhagar hears the sarcasm directed at her friend's promise that he will come and bring her joy. Manakkudavar defines grace as not getting angry even at a fault, so the 'honour' is very small.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:121"},"k:1209":{"book":"kural","num":1209,"t":["விளியுமென் இன்னுயிர் வேறல்லம் என்பார்","அளியின்மை ஆற்ற நினைந்து"],"p":"My precious life is wasting away by thinking too much on the cruelty of him who said we were not different.","r":"The woman says her dear life is ending, as she thinks too much about the lack of grace in one who said they weren't separate.","n":"Parimelazhagar gives a key distinction: in her own words, it's not the parting that is killing her, but his lack of love.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","uyir","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:121"},"k:1210":{"book":"kural","num":1210,"t":["விடாஅது சென்றாரைக் கண்ணினால் காணப்","படாஅதி வாழி மதி"],"p":"May you live, O Moon! Do not set, that I mine see him who has departed without quitting my soul.","r":"The woman speaks to the moon, blessing it and asking about seeing with her eyes the man who went away without leaving her.","n":"விடாஅது சென்றாரை, 'who left without leaving', packs the whole chapter's paradox into two words.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","kan","pirivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","She begs the moon not to set, so that their eyes can meet on it while both look at it.","மதி இருவரானும் நோக்கப்படுதலின் இருவர் கண்ணும் அதன்கண்ணே சேர்தல்."],["manakkudavar","She reproaches the moon for not setting: if it set she could sleep and see him in dreams.","பட்டதாயின், என் கண் உறங்கும்; உறங்கினால், அவரைக் காணலாமென் பது கருத்து."],["kalaignar","She asks it not to set, to give her light to seek him out with her eyes.","நான் என் கண்களால் தேடிக் கண்டுபிடித்திடத் துணையாக நீ மறையாமல் இருப்பாயாக."]],"ch":"k:121"},"k:1211":{"book":"kural","num":1211,"t":["காதலர் தூதொடு வந்த கனவினுக்கு","யாதுசெய் வேன்கொல் விருந்து"],"p":"Where with shall I feast the dream which has brought me my dear one's messenger ?","r":"What feast can be given to the dream that came with a message from the lover? The speaker is the woman for Parimelazhagar and the friend for Manakkudavar.","n":"The Kural's great social duty of hospitality is turned on a dream guest. Parimelazhagar's little note gets the joke exactly: it is a guest for which one can find nothing to serve.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kanavu","pirivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The woman herself saw the dream and says this.","தலைமகன் தூது வரக் கண்டாள் சொல்லியது."],["manakkudavar","The friend says she dreamed that messengers came, in order to comfort the woman.","இது தலைமகளாற்றுதற் பொருட்டுக் காதலர் வாராநின்றாரென்று தூதர் வாக் கனாக் கண்டேனென்று தோழி சொல்லியது."]],"ch":"k:122"},"k:1212":{"book":"kural","num":1212,"t":["கயலுண்கண் யானிரப்பத் துஞ்சிற் கலந்தார்க்கு","உயலுண்மை சாற்றுவேன் மன்"],"p":"If my fish-like painted eyes should, at my begging, close in sleep, I could fully relate my sufferings to my lord.","r":"The woman says that if her carp-like kohl-lined eyes would sleep when she begs them to, she would tell the one who joined with her that she is surviving.","n":"She must beg her eyes for sleep, after cursing them in chapter 118. Parimelazhagar notes her reason for preferring this to a messenger: there are things kept back from messengers, or shortened, which she could tell him in full.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kanavu","kan","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:122"},"k:1213":{"book":"kural","num":1213,"t":["நனவினால் நல்கா தவரைக் கனவினால்","காண்டலின் உண்டென் உயிர்"],"p":"My life lasts because in my dream I behold him who does not favour me in my waking hours.","r":"The woman says her life continues because she sees in her dreams the man who gives no grace in waking life.","n":"Parimelazhagar takes it as a reassurance to the friend who worries: 'don't worry, I'm bearing up'. It may comfort the friend, but what it actually says is hardly comforting.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kanavu","uyir","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:122"},"k:1214":{"book":"kural","num":1214,"t":["கனவினான் உண்டாகும் காமம் நனவினான்","நல்காரை நாடித் தரற்கு"],"p":"There is pleasure in my dream, because in it I seek and obtain him who does not visit me in my wakefulness.","r":"The woman says there is pleasure in dreams, since they seek out and bring her the man who gives no grace in waking life.","n":"The dream becomes an agent, a go-between travelling to where he is. That is what she wanted from her friend. Manakkudavar has the friend ask what use a dream is without real enjoyment, and the verse is her answer.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kanavu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:122"},"k:1215":{"book":"kural","num":1215,"t":["நனவினால் கண்டதூஉம் ஆங்கே கனவுந்தான்","கண்ட பொழுதே இனிது"],"p":"I saw him in my waking hours, and then it was pleasant; I see him just now in my dream, and it is (equally) pleasant.","r":"The woman says that what she saw in waking life was sweet only then, and likewise the dream is sweet at the time it is seen.","n":"A strong philosophical move: both are equally over when over. Waking joy has no special standing in memory. Parimelazhagar's gloss is plain: 'the two are equal for me', and what she had before was no more than this.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kanavu","kaamam","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:122"},"k:1216":{"book":"kural","num":1216,"t":["நனவென ஒன்றில்லை ஆயின் கனவினால்","காதலர் நீங்கலர் மன்"],"p":"Were there no such thing as wakefulness, my beloved (who visited me) in my dream would not depart from me.","r":"The woman says that if there were no such thing as waking, her lover in the dream wouldn't leave.","n":"Parimelazhagar supplies a charged word, பாவி, 'sinner', for waking, and reads ஒன்று as pointing up its cruelty. Waking is what barges in and parts them, so the person responsible for leaving is no longer him but waking itself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kanavu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:122"},"k:1217":{"book":"kural","num":1217,"t":["நனவினால் நல்காக் கொடியார் கனவனால்","என்எம்மைப் பீழிப் பது"],"p":"The cruel one who would not favour me in my wakefulness, what right has he to torture me in my dreams?","r":"The woman asks why the cruel one who gives no grace in waking life torments her in dreams.","n":"A flat reversal of the last six verses, with the comfort now a torment. Parimelazhagar explains: on waking she doesn't find him, and because he vanishes from her shoulder, that too brings pain. He also tests a proverb against her case, that what isn't in waking isn't in dreams.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kanavu","kaamanoy","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:122"},"k:1218":{"book":"kural","num":1218,"t":["துஞ்சுங்கால் தோள்மேலர் ஆகி விழிக்குங்கால்","நெஞ்சத்தர் ஆவர் விரைந்து"],"p":"When I am asleep he rests on my shoulders, (but) when I awake he hastens into my soul.","r":"The woman says that when she sleeps he is on her shoulder, and when she wakes he quickly moves into her heart.","n":"In Parimelazhagar's reading she is defending him against her friend: he never leaves her at all. விரைந்து, 'quickly', gives the speed of a fugitive, and Manakkudavar hears the friend's tease that prompted it: where does he go when sleep ends?","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kanavu","nenju","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:122"},"k:1219":{"book":"kural","num":1219,"t":["நனவினால் நல்காரை நோவர் கனவினால்","காதலர்க் காணா தவர்"],"p":"They who have no dear ones to behold in their dreams blame him who visits me not in my waking hours.","r":"The woman says those who haven't seen their lovers in dreams are the ones who blame the man for not being gracious in waking life.","n":"This is an attack by pretending to speak about outsiders, as both commentators note. For Parimelazhagar she is hinting that the friend has no lover, which is a harsh thing to imply, and marks how angry the blaming makes her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kanavu","alar"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:122"},"k:1220":{"book":"kural","num":1220,"t":["நனவினால் நம்நீத்தார் என்பர் கனவினால்","காணார்கொல் இவ்வூ ரவர்"],"p":"The women of this place say he has forsaken me in my wakefulness. I think they have not seen him visit me in my dreams.","r":"The woman says that the people of this town say he has left her in waking life, and asks if they don't see him in dreams.","n":"A surreal question, since no one else could see her dream. Parimelazhagar makes sense of it by way of the unity between her and her friend: if they are truly one, what the woman saw, the friend has seen too, and in not seeing she has become an outsider, 'this town'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kanavu","alar","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:122"},"k:1221":{"book":"kural","num":1221,"t":["மாலையோ அல்லை மணந்தார் உயிருண்ணும்","வேலைநீ வாழி பொழுது"],"p":"Live, O you evening are you (the former) evening? No, you are the season that slays (married) women.","r":"The woman tells the hour, with a bitter blessing, that it isn't evening at all but வேலை, which eats the life of those once wed.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","uyir","pirivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","வேலை is a time word, the hour of ending, and he mentions but sets aside others who take it as 'you've become a spear'.","மகளிர் உயிரையுண்ணும் இறுதிக்காலமாய் இருந்தாய்."],["manakkudavar","The word is வேல் plus ஐ: you have become a spear that eats life. He adds evening is hot, so it's not really evening.","உயிரை உண்பதாகிய வொரு வேலாயிருந்தாய்"],["kalaignar","Also a spear that drinks life, with the blessing made explicit.","உயிரைக் குடிக்கும் வேலாக இருப்பதற்காக உனக்கோர் வாழ்த்து!"]],"ch":"k:123"},"k:1222":{"book":"kural","num":1222,"t":["புன்கண்ணை வாழி மருள்மாலை எம்கேள்போல்","வன்கண்ண தோநின் துணை"],"p":"A long life to you, O dark evening! You are sightless. Is your help-mate (also) as hard-hearted as mine.","r":"The woman says to the bewildered evening, may you live, you look sad-eyed; is your partner as hard-eyed as mine?","n":"The tender moment of the chapter: she stops fighting the hour and sees it as a fellow sufferer. Parimelazhagar explains that the pale evening's fading of light is what suggests its grief, and says she has put her own inner state onto it. The word மருள் means both the mixing of day and night and being confused.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["maalai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:123"},"k:1223":{"book":"kural","num":1223,"t":["பனிஅரும்பிப் பைதல்கொள் மாலை துனிஅரும்பித்","துன்பம் வளர வரும்"],"p":"The evening that (once) came in with trembling and dimness (now) brings me an aversion for life and increasing sorrow.","r":"The woman says that evening, which comes with chill and pale, now comes with revulsion budding and sorrow growing.","n":"Parimelazhagar glosses துனி strongly, as disgust with living. Evening used to tremble and pale before her when she was happy; now she trembles before it. Manakkudavar keeps the reversal smaller, with today's evening following yesterday's.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["maalai","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:123"},"k:1224":{"book":"kural","num":1224,"t":["காதலர் இல்வழி மாலை கொலைக்களத்து","ஏதிலர் போல வரும்"],"p":"In the absence of my lover, evening comes in like slayers on the field of slaughter.","r":"The woman says that when her lover is absent, evening comes like enemies coming to a killing ground.","n":"ஏதிலர் is the word she has been using of the town and of the man's accusers; Parimelazhagar glosses it here as 'those with no grace at all', executioners. That evening was once her friend is part of the pain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","pirivu","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:123"},"k:1225":{"book":"kural","num":1225,"t":["காலைக்குச் செய்தநன்று என்கொல் எவன்கொல்யான்","மாலைக்குச் செய்த பகை?"],"p":"What good have I done to morning (and) what evil to evening?","r":"The woman asks what good she has done to morning and what enmity she has shown to evening.","n":"It is framed as a legal puzzle about reward and punishment. Both commentators note a hidden reversal: morning used to be the hated hour, when they feared his leaving, and now it's the shore at the end of the flood of night. Evening made the opposite change.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:123"},"k:1226":{"book":"kural","num":1226,"t":["மாலைநோய் செய்தல் மணந்தார் அகலாத","காலை அறிந்த திலேன்"],"p":"Previous to my husband's departure, I know not the painful nature of evening.","r":"The woman says she didn't know that evening brings sickness, while he who wed her had not yet left.","n":"It answers the most painful question: why did you consent? Parimelazhagar spells out that she would have refused had she known this. So 1181's self-accusation gets its answer: she agreed in ignorance.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","kaamanoy","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:123"},"k:1227":{"book":"kural","num":1227,"t":["காலை அரும்பிப் பகலெல்லாம் போதாகி","மாலை மலரும்இந் நோய்"],"p":"This malady buds forth in the morning, expands all day long and blossoms in the evening.","r":"The woman says this sickness buds in the morning, ripens through the day and opens in the evening.","n":"A flower is made a sickness, in a lovely, cruel inversion. Parimelazhagar's reasons are as vivid as the verse: at morning she is still comforted by a dream, by day memory thins, and at evening she watches creatures and people returning to their mates.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kaamanoy","maalai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:123"},"k:1228":{"book":"kural","num":1228,"t":["அழல்போலும் மாலைக்குத் தூதாகி ஆயன்","குழல்போலும் கொல்லும் படை"],"p":"The shepherd's flute now sounds as a fiery forerunner of night, and is become a weapon that slays (me).","r":"The woman says the herdsman's flute is a messenger for the burning evening and a weapon that kills.","n":"The one sound in the chapter, pastoral and sweet in itself. Parimelazhagar reads it as a change from 'pleasant before' to deadly now. Evening has its own envoy and arms, like a king making war.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:123"},"k:1229":{"book":"kural","num":1229,"t":["பதிமருண்டு பைதல் உழக்கும் மதிமருண்டு","மாலை படர்தரும் போழ்து"],"p":"When night comes on confusing (everyone's) mind, the (whole) town will lose its sense and be plunged in sorrow.","r":"The woman says that when evening spreads, bewildering the mind, the whole town is bewildered and suffers.","n":"Parimelazhagar's gloss is missing in this source, but his note says the unstated thought is 'I shall die', and he treats evening as Death, implying that the town grieves for her. He adds that others read it as her own mind reeling, and with so little of his comment left, I can't say which he preferred in full.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maalai","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:123"},"k:1230":{"book":"kural","num":1230,"t":["பொருள்மாலை யாளரை உள்ளி மருள்மாலை","மாயும்என் மாயா உயிர்"],"p":"My (hitherto) unextinguished life is now lost in this bewildering night at the thought of him who","r":"The woman says that thinking of the one whose nature is wealth, in this confused evening, her life that wouldn't die is dying.","n":"The wordplay does much of the work: பொருள்மாலை, 'wealth-natured', against மருள்மாலை, 'confused evening', and மாயும் against மாயா. For Parimelazhagar, he has broken his word to keep to wealth, so he has lost his nature to it. Manakkudavar says she takes him to have neither love nor virtue.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["selvam","maalai","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:123"},"k:1231":{"book":"kural","num":1231,"t":["சிறுமை நமக்கொழியச் சேட்சென்றார் உள்ளி","நறுமலர் நாணின கண்"],"p":"While we endure the unbearable sorrow, your eyes weep for him who is gone afar, and shun (the","r":"The friend tells her that as she thinks of the man gone far away, leaving them this misery, her eyes are ashamed before fragrant flowers.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","nalam","pirivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","He left the pain with them when he went far away, and the friend is the speaker.","இவ்வாற்றாமை நம்கண்ணே நிற்பத் தாம் சேணிடைச் சென்ற காதலரை"],["manakkudavar","He went so that their hardship would end, which gives his journey a very different motive.","நமக்குத் துன்பம் ஒழிய வேண்டி, நெடு நெறிக்கண் சென்றாரை"]],"ch":"k:124"},"k:1232":{"book":"kural","num":1232,"t":["நயந்தவர் நல்காமை சொல்லுவ போலும்","பசந்து பனிவாரும் கண்"],"p":"The discoloured eyes that shed tears p rofusely seem to betray the unkindness of our beloved.","r":"The friend says that her eyes, pale and running with tears, seem to be telling others of the unkindness of the one they desired.","n":"போலும், 'seem', is doing precise work. Parimelazhagar calls it inference: others deduce what the eyes don't actually say. For the friend, the point is that weeping exposes him, so stop.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","pasalai","alar"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:124"},"k:1233":{"book":"kural","num":1233,"t":["தணந்தமை சால அறிவிப்ப போலும்","மணந்தநாள் வீங்கிய தோள்"],"p":"The shoulders that swelled on the day of our union (now) seem to announce our separation clearly (to the public).","r":"Her shoulders, which swelled on the day of union, seem to announce loudly that he has left; the speaker is the friend for Parimelazhagar and the woman for Manakkudavar.","n":"The record needs both moments. Parimelazhagar says those who saw the swelling and now the wasting will quickly work out what happened. It is a before-and-after that is hard to explain away.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["melivu","pirivu","alar"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The friend addresses her: your shoulders are doing what they shouldn't.","இன்ப மிகுதியால் பூரித்த நின் தோள்கள்"],["manakkudavar","The woman speaks to her friend, who has seen her shoulders waste.","பிரிவிடை யாற்றாளாகிய தலைமகள் தனது தோள் வாட்ட முற்றது கண்ட தோழிக்குச் சொல்லியது."]],"ch":"k:124"},"k:1234":{"book":"kural","num":1234,"t":["பணைநீங்கிப் பைந்தொடி சோரும் துணைநீங்கித்","தொல்கவின் வாடிய தோள்"],"p":"In the absence of your consort, your shoulders having lost their former beauty and fulness, your bracelets of pure gold have become loose.","r":"Her shoulders, their old beauty faded with her partner gone, have lost their fullness, and her bright bangles slip; the speaker is the friend for Parimelazhagar and the woman for Manakkudavar.","n":"தொல்கவின், 'old beauty', matters: for Parimelazhagar she has lost both the beauty he gave and the beauty she had before him. Commentators also differ over the bangle, gold for Parimelazhagar, emerald for Manakkudavar.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["melivu","pirivu","nalam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The friend says these shoulders are betraying his lack of love and his false word.","இவை இங்ஙனம் செயற்பாலவல்ல."],["manakkudavar","The woman tells her friend that her shoulders are now thinning as well as losing their beauty.","தோள் அழகழிதலே யன்றி மெலி வதுஞ் செய்யாநின்றதென்று தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது."]],"ch":"k:124"},"k:1235":{"book":"kural","num":1235,"t":["கொடியார் கொடுமை உரைக்கும் தொடியொடு","தொல்கவின் வாடிய தோள்"],"p":"The (loosened) bracelets, and the shoulders from which the old beauty has faded, relate the cruelty of the pitiless one.","r":"The friend says the shoulders, with bangles lost and old beauty faded, are declaring the cruelty of the cruel one.","n":"The friend now says openly what 1232-1234 only implied, with both commentators attributing it to her. Manakkudavar gives the purpose: she blames him to help the woman bear up. That prepares the next verse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["melivu","alar","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:124"},"k:1236":{"book":"kural","num":1236,"t":["தொடியொடு தோள்நெகிழ நோவல் அவரைக்","கொடியர் எனக்கூறல் நொந்து"],"p":"I am greatly pained to hear you call him a cruel man, just because your shoulders are reduced and your bracelets loosened.","r":"The woman tells her friend that with bangles and shoulders slackening, what hurts is hearing him called cruel.","n":"The friend's strategy has backfired. Parimelazhagar spells out the claim: it's not his absence I can't bear, but what you say. Kalaignar widens the accusers from the friend to anyone who sees her. It's the same defence as in 1190.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["melivu","pirivu","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:124"},"k:1237":{"book":"kural","num":1237,"t":["பாடு பெறுதியோ நெஞ்சே கொடியார்க்கென்","வாடுதோட் பூசல் உரைத்து"],"p":"Can you O my soul! gain glory by relating to the (so-called) cruel one the clamour of my fading shoulders?","r":"The woman asks her heart if it could win any glory by telling the 'cruel' one of the uproar over her wasting shoulders.","n":"A sardonic challenge. For Parimelazhagar, she says கொடியார் only in quotation, as in 'the one they call cruel'. He reads பூசல் as the friend's blame and her own distress, while Manakkudavar reads it as the town's gossip.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","melivu","alar"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:124"},"k:1238":{"book":"kural","num":1238,"t":["முயங்கிய கைகளை ஊக்கப் பசந்தது","பைந்தொடிப் பேதை நுதல்"],"p":"When I once loosened the arms that were in embrace, the forehead of the gold-braceleted women turned sallow.","r":"The man remembers that when he once loosened the arms that held her, the forehead of the girl with bright bangles turned pale.","n":"The voice changes suddenly: we have been with her, and now he is far away and remembering. The conclusion, as Parimelazhagar gives it: if that much did this, what will this absence do? I must go quickly.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","punarchi","pirivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","He loosened his arms out of tenderness, thinking the tight embrace would hurt her.","'இவட்கு நோம்' என்று கருதி ஒரு ஞான்று யான் நெகிழ்ந்தேனாக"],["manakkudavar","He loosened them because he was intending to leave, and she sensed this.","யான் பிரிவதாக நினைத்து, அவள் முயங்கியகைகளை நீக்கினே னாக"]],"ch":"k:124"},"k:1239":{"book":"kural","num":1239,"t":["முயக்கிடைத் தண்வளி போழப் பசப்புற்ற","பேதை பெருமழைக் கண்"],"p":"When but a breath of breeze penetrated our embrace, her large cool eyes became sallow.","r":"The man remembers that when a cool breeze slipped between them in their embrace, the girl's large cool eyes grew pale.","n":"Parimelazhagar draws the arithmetic: if a breeze could do this, what about mountains, forests and whole countries now between them? Manakkudavar says three verses of the man's speech begin here, which is a different division from Parimelazhagar's.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pasalai","punarchi","kan"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:124"},"k:1240":{"book":"kural","num":1240,"t":["கண்ணின் பசப்போ பருவரல் எய்தின்றே","ஒண்ணுதல் செய்தது கண்டு"],"p":"Was it at the sight of what the bright forehead had done that the sallowness of her eyes became sad?","r":"The man asks whether the pallor in her eyes itself grew distressed on seeing what her bright forehead had done.","n":"An odd and very ingenious image, and the commentary has to work hard. For Parimelazhagar, the eye's pallor is ashamed because the forehead paled faster, so her parts are competing to lose their beauty, and he should hurry. I find this plausible but can't be sure the verse means that much.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","kan"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:124"},"k:1241":{"book":"kural","num":1241,"t":["நினைத்தொன்று சொல்லாயோ நெஞ்சே எனைத்தொன்றும்","எவ்வநோய் தீர்க்கும் மருந்து"],"p":"O my soul, will you not think and tell me some medicine be it what it may, that can cure this incurable malady?","r":"The woman asks her heart to think and tell her of some medicine, of whatever kind, for this incurable sickness.","n":"Parimelazhagar takes எனைத்தொன்றும், 'of any kind', very far: even a remedy that means giving up shame, which is dearer than life. The heart is asked because she herself no longer has the power to know.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:125"},"k:1242":{"book":"kural","num":1242,"t":["காதல் அவரிலர் ஆகநீ நோவது","பேதைமை வாழியென் நெஞ்சு"],"p":"May you live, O my soul! While he is without love, for you to suffer is (simple) folly.","r":"The woman tells her heart, with a mocking blessing, that it's folly for it to suffer when he has no love.","n":"Parimelazhagar sees a surprising purpose in this: it isn't telling the heart to give up, but to stop waiting and go, since going would be wise. On this reading, it's the first move towards a woman going after a man.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["nenju","orutalai","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:125"},"k:1243":{"book":"kural","num":1243,"t":["இருந்துள்ளி என்பரிதல் நெஞ்சே பரிந்துள்ளல்","பைதல்நோய் செய்தார்கண் இல்"],"p":"O my soul! why remain (here) and suffer thinking (of him)? There are no lewd thoughts (of you) in him who has caused you this disease of sorrow.","r":"The woman asks her heart why it sits and suffers, thinking of him, when the one who caused this sickness has no thought of pity.","n":"இருந்து, 'sitting here', is the barb, because the alternatives are stated in Parimelazhagar's gloss: neither going to him nor dying. Waiting has become the worst option.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","kaamanoy","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:125"},"k:1244":{"book":"kural","num":1244,"t":["கண்ணும் கொளச்சேறி நெஞ்சே இவையென்னைத்","தின்னும் அவர்க்காணல் உற்று"],"p":"O my soul! take my eyes also with you, (if not), these would eat me up (in their desire) to see him.","r":"The woman tells her heart to take the eyes with it if it goes, since they are eating her alive with longing to see him.","n":"A practical request and one of the book's most physical: the body has parts she wants to send away. By Parimelazhagar's reading, this is a way to mean that she herself will go, since she can't part with her eyes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","kan","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:125"},"k:1245":{"book":"kural","num":1245,"t":["செற்றார் எனக்கை விடல்உண்டோ நெஞ்சேயாம்","உற்றால் உறாஅ தவர்"],"p":"O my soul! can he who loves not though he is beloved, be forsaken saying he hates me (now)?","r":"The woman asks her heart if one can really abandon him as an enemy, simply because he doesn't respond when she draws close.","n":"The commentators shift the emphasis subtly. For Parimelazhagar, she lacks the strength to abandon him; for Manakkudavar, the issue is whether it would be proper. One treats loyalty as weakness, and the other treats it as what's right.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["nenju","orutalai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:125"},"k:1246":{"book":"kural","num":1246,"t":["கலந்துணர்த்தும் காதலர்க் கண்டாற் புலந்துணராய்","பொய்க்காய்வு காய்திஎன் நெஞ்சு"],"p":"O my soul! when you see the dear one who remove dislike by intercourse, you are displeased and continue to be so. Nay, your displeasure is (simply) false.","r":"The woman tells her heart that when it sees the man who ends quarrels by union, it can't stay angry, and so its present anger is false.","n":"She exposes her own anger as fake, calling it பொய்க்காய்வு. The heart is angry only while he's absent, and is no use when it would count.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["nenju","oodal","punarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:125"},"k:1247":{"book":"kural","num":1247,"t":["காமம் விடுஒன்றோ நாண்விடு நன்னெஞ்சே","யானோ பொறேன்இவ் விரண்டு"],"p":"O my good soul, give up either lust or honour, as for me I can endure neither.","r":"The woman tells her good heart to give up either desire or shame, since she can't bear both.","n":"நன்னெஞ்சே, 'good heart', isn't flattery alone. For Parimelazhagar, the heart is good because holding on to both keeps her womanhood intact, but she can't afford that goodness, as her life is at stake. யானோ implies a split: 'you may be able to, I can't.'","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kaamam","naanam","nenju"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:125"},"k:1248":{"book":"kural","num":1248,"t":["பரிந்தவர் நல்காரென்று ஏங்கிப் பிரிந்தவர்","பின்செல்வாய் பேதைஎன் நெஞ்சு"],"p":"You are a fool, O my soul! to go after my departed one, while you mourn that he is not kind enough to favour you.","r":"The woman calls her heart a fool for following the man who has left, while lamenting that he isn't gracious.","n":"Parimelazhagar's surviving note gives the logic: he saw her distress and left anyway, so how would telling him at a distance change anything? This reverses 1242-1244, where she was urging the heart to go.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["nenju","orutalai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:125"},"k:1249":{"book":"kural","num":1249,"t":["உள்ளத்தார் காத லவரால் உள்ளிநீ","யாருழைச் சேறியென் நெஞ்சு"],"p":"O my soul! to whom would you repair, while the dear one is within yourself?","r":"The woman asks her heart whom it goes looking for, since her lover is within it.","n":"Parimelazhagar says it's laughable to go looking outside for one who is inside. To me, this resembles the later devotional complaint about seeking God outside, though this resemblance isn't something the verse or its commentators claim.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:125"},"k:1250":{"book":"kural","num":1250,"t":["துன்னாத் துறந்தாரை நெஞ்சத்து உடையேமா","இன்னும் இழத்தும் கவின்"],"p":"If I retain in my heart him who has left me without befriending me, I shall lose even the (inward) beauty that remains.","r":"The woman says that if she keeps in her heart the man who left without coming close, she will lose still more of her கவின்.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","nalam","pirivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","She has lost her outward beauty, and will now lose her inner beauty too, meaning her dignity and restraint.","முன் இழந்த புறக்கவினேயன்றி நின்ற அகக்கவினும் இழப்போம்."],["kalaignar","It is bodily beauty that will fade further as she wastes away.","மேலும் மேனியெழில் இழந்து மெலிந்து அழிய வேண்டியுள்ளது."]],"ch":"k:125"},"k:1251":{"book":"kural","num":1251,"t":["காமக் கணிச்சி உடைக்கும் நிறையென்னும்","நாணுத்தாழ் வீழ்த்த கதவு"],"p":"The axe of lust can break the door of chastity which is bolted with the bolt of modesty.","r":"The woman says that the axe of desire breaks the door of restraint, bolted with the bar of shame.","n":"The architecture is exact, as Parimelazhagar explains: restraint is the door that keeps what is inside from being taken, shame is the bolt, since restraint holds as long as shame does. Manakkudavar has அறிவு, 'understanding', as the door, though the verse says நிறை.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nirai","naanam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:126"},"k:1252":{"book":"kural","num":1252,"t":["காமம் எனவொன்றோ கண்ணின்றென் நெஞ்சத்தை","யாமத்தும் ஆளும் தொழில்"],"p":"Even at midnight is my mind worried by lust, and this one thing, alas! is without mercy.","r":"The woman says that this thing called desire has no pity, for it puts her heart to work even at midnight.","n":"The image is forced labour: everyone else's work stops at midnight, and hers doesn't. Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar identify the task as sending the heart to him, which, Parimelazhagar notes, isn't a proper task for a woman, and is why she says 'this thing called desire'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","nenju","nirai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:126"},"k:1253":{"book":"kural","num":1253,"t":["மறைப்பேன்மன் காமத்தை யானோ குறிப்பின்றித்","தும்மல்போல் தோன்றி விடும்"],"p":"I would conceal my lust, but alas, it yields not to my will but breaks out like a sneeze.","r":"The woman says she would hide her desire, but like a sneeze it comes out without her intending it.","n":"The comparison is deliberately undignified. குறிப்பின்றி means without intent, using a word that in chapter 110 named her coded signs, which were deliberate. Now the sign sends itself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kaamam","nirai","kurippu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:126"},"k:1254":{"book":"kural","num":1254,"t":["நிறையுடையேன் என்பேன்மன் யானோஎன் காமம்","மறையிறந்து மன்று படும்"],"p":"I say I would be firm, but alas, my malady breaks out from its concealment and appears in public.","r":"The woman says she used to say she had restraint, yet her desire passes beyond concealment and comes out in public.","n":"The tense matters, on Parimelazhagar's gloss: 'until today I thought of myself as restrained'. She has lost not only the restraint but also a belief about who she is.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["nirai","kaamam","alar"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:126"},"k:1255":{"book":"kural","num":1255,"t":["செற்றார்பின் செல்லாப் பெருந்தகைமை காமநோய்","உற்றார் அறிவதொன்று அன்று"],"p":"The dignity that would not go after an absent lover is not known to those who are sticken by love.","r":"The woman says the greatness of not going after one who has turned hostile is something those who are sick with love can't know.","n":"Honour isn't rejected, but it's placed out of reach. Parimelazhagar gives the other half: those who don't have the sickness can know it, and he calls them the people of honour (மானம் உடையார்). He also softens going after to continual thought, and softens செற்றார், 'enemy', as one who has made time painful.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","perumai","nirai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:126"},"k:1256":{"book":"kural","num":1256,"t":["செற்றவர் பின்சேறல் வேண்டி அளித்தரோ","எற்றென்னை உற்ற துயர்"],"p":"The sorrow I have endured by desiring to go after my absent lover, in what way is it excellent?","r":"The woman exclaims, in irony, how fine is the grief that has come to her, making her want to go after one who has turned hostile.","n":"Both older commentators hear sarcasm in அளித்து, 'sweet', as they did in the night verse 1168, meaning the opposite. Her comment on her own condition has become a dry detachment.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","nirai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:126"},"k:1257":{"book":"kural","num":1257,"t":["நாணென ஒன்றோ அறியலம் காமத்தால்","பேணியார் பெட்ப செயின்"],"p":"I know nothing like shame when my beloved does from love (just) what is desired (by me).","r":"The woman says she knows nothing of shame when the man she loves does in desire what she longs for.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","kaamam","punarchi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The context is that he has returned from courtesans; the shame she failed to feel is shame at touching a chest they have touched.","நாண் - பரத்தையர் தோய்ந்த மார்பைத் தோய்தற்கு நாணுதல்."],["manakkudavar","It's a general statement on women and desire, and doesn't mention any courtesans.","நாணென்பதொன்று அறியார் மகளிர், காமம் காரணமாக"]],"ch":"k:126"},"k:1258":{"book":"kural","num":1258,"t":["பன்மாயக் கள்வன் பணிமொழி அன்றோநம்","பெண்மை உடைக்கும் படை"],"p":"Are not the enticing words of my trick-abounding roguish lover the weapon that breaks away my feminine firmness?","r":"The woman says that the soft speech of that thief of many deceptions is the weapon that breaks her womanhood.","n":"The chapter's picture of siege ends here, with Parimelazhagar glossing பெண்மை as restraint, a fortress taken by words. She knows them to be deceptive, and that doesn't protect her. பணிமொழி was how he praised her, in 1121.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nirai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:126"},"k:1259":{"book":"kural","num":1259,"t":["புலப்பல் எனச்சென்றேன் புல்லினேன் நெஞ் சம்","கலத்தல் உறுவது கண்டு"],"p":"I said I would feign dislike and so went (away); (but) I embraced him the moment I say my mind began to unite with him!","r":"The woman says she went away meaning to sulk, and embraced him when she saw her heart beginning to join with him.","n":"She didn't yield to him but to her heart, and embracing is what's left when her instrument defects. Parimelazhagar says it exactly: the heart that is the means of sulking has itself turned into the means of joining.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","nenju","punarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:126"},"k:1260":{"book":"kural","num":1260,"t":["நிணந்தீயில் இட்டன்ன நெஞ்சினார்க்கு உண்டோ","புணர்ந்தூடி நிற்பேம் எனல்"],"p":"Is it possible for those whose hearts melt like fat in the fire to say they can feign a strong dislike and remain so?","r":"The woman asks whether those whose hearts are like fat thrown in a fire can ever say 'we will join and then hold out in a sulk'.","n":"An extraordinary image of unlovely and total melting: not wax or butter, but animal fat.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","nenju","punarchi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","புணர்ந்து here means simply coming close, or meeting; sulking fails at first encounter.","புணர்தல் - ஈண்டு மிக நணுகுதல்; எதிர்ப்படுதலுமாம்."],["kalaignar","The word means after union; one can't then keep up the quarrel.","கூடிக் களித்தபின் ஊடல் கொண்டு அதில் உறுதியாக இருக்க முடியுமா?."]],"ch":"k:126"},"k:1261":{"book":"kural","num":1261,"t":["வாளற்றுப் புற்கென்ற கண்ணும் அவர்சென்ற","நாளொற்றித் தேய்ந்த விரல்"],"p":"My finger has worn away by marking (on the wall) the days he has been absent while my eyes have lost their lustre and begin to fail.","r":"The woman says her eyes have lost their light and her fingers have worn down from marking the days since he left.","n":"Fingers wear down from touching marks on a wall, in Parimelazhagar's and Kalaignar's picture, and from folding to count, in Manakkudavar's. The verse itself just says they marked the days. Either way, this is the book's most concrete image of waiting.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kan","melivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:127"},"k:1262":{"book":"kural","num":1262,"t":["இலங்கிழாய் இன்று மறப்பின்என் தோள்மேல்","கலங்கழியும் காரிகை நீத்து"],"p":"O you bright-jewelled maid, if I forget (him) today, my shoulders will lose their beauty even in the other life and make my bracelets loose.","r":"The woman tells her bright-jewelled friend that if she forgets him today, her beauty will leave and the bangles will slip from her shoulders thereafter.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["ninaivu","melivu","piravi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","'Today' is the day of her death and மேல் is the next birth: remembering him at the moment of death is how she can reach him in the next life.","இவ்வெல்லைக்கண் நினைந்தால் மறுமைக்கண் அவரை எய்தி அன்புறலாம்;"],["manakkudavar","The verse is about this life: if she forgets, her body will fail, and the address means 'you who have no pain'.","தம்மழகினை நீக்கி வளை முதலான அணிகலங்களையும் கழலவிடும்"]],"ch":"k:127"},"k:1263":{"book":"kural","num":1263,"t":["உரன்நசைஇ உள்ளம் துணையாகச் சென்றார்","வரல்நசைஇ இன்னும் உளேன்"],"p":"I still live by longing for the arrival of him who has gone out of love for victory and with valour as his guide.","r":"The woman says she still lives by longing for the return of the man who left, longing for victory, with his own resolve as his companion.","n":"The pointed word is துணை, 'companion'. Parimelazhagar spells it out: he has scorned her as a partner and taken resolve instead. There are two longings set side by side, நசைஇ in each line, for victory and for him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","uyir","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:127"},"k:1264":{"book":"kural","num":1264,"t":["கூடிய காமம் பிரிந்தார் வரவுள்ளிக்","கோடுகொ டேறுமென் நெஞ்சு"],"p":"My heart is rid of its sorrow and swells with rapture to think of my absent lover returning with his love.","r":"The woman says that thinking of the return of the man who left with the love they shared, her heart கோடுகொடேறும்.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","ninaivu","kaamam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The heart puts out branches, swelling and rising as a tree does, with sorrow gone.","என் நெஞ்சு வருத்தமொழிந்து மேன்மேற் பணைத்தெழாநின்றது."],["manakkudavar","The heart climbs a tree to look for him, because from the top one can see someone coming from afar.","என்னெஞ்சம் மரத்தின் மேலேறிப் பாராநின்றது"]],"ch":"k:127"},"k:1265":{"book":"kural","num":1265,"t":["காண்கமன் கொண்கனைக் கண்ணாரக் கண்டபின்","நீங்கும்என் மென்தோள் பசப்பு"],"p":"May I look on my lover till I am satisfied and thereafter will vanish the sallowness of my slender shoulders.","r":"The woman says may she see her husband till her eyes are full, and after that the pallor of her soft shoulders will leave.","n":"Parimelazhagar notes what this is answering: the friend brings news that he's coming, and asks why she still pales. Her answer is that hearing won't do it, since the eyes must see. The news of the cure isn't the cure.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kan","pasalai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:127"},"k:1266":{"book":"kural","num":1266,"t":["வருகமன் கொண்கன் ஒருநாள் பருகுவன்","பைதல்நோய் எல்லாம் கெட"],"p":"May my husband return some day; and then will I enjoy (him) so as to destroy all this agonizing sorrow.","r":"The woman says may her husband come one day, and she'll drink him in so that all this agonising sickness is destroyed.","n":"Parimelazhagar makes பருகுவன் drinking through all five senses, recalling 1101, and says that in that single day she'll destroy not only all past pain but all that might still come. That one day is asked to carry a lot.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:127"},"k:1267":{"book":"kural","num":1267,"t":["புலப்பேன்கொல் புல்லுவேன் கொல்லோ கலப்பேன்கொல்","கண்அன்ன கேளிர் விரன்"],"p":"On the return of him who is as dear as my eyes, am I displeased or am I to embrace (him); or am I to do both?","r":"The woman asks, should he who is dear as her eyes return, whether she will sulk, embrace him or do both.","n":"The chapter's real interest here is the planning, with him not yet home. Parimelazhagar sets aside an alternative, that the third means to behave as if nothing happened: she wants to do all three, and that's her impatience.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["oodal","punarchi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:127"},"k:1268":{"book":"kural","num":1268,"t":["வினைகலந்து வென்றீக வேந்தன் மனைகலந்து","மாலை அயர்கம் விருந்து"],"p":"Let the king fight and gain (victories); (but) let me be united to my wife and feast the evening.","r":"Let the king fight and win, and let there be a home-joined feast for the evening; the speaker is the man for Parimelazhagar and Kalaignar and the woman for Manakkudavar.","n":"Parimelazhagar rejects the woman's reading at length, on the grounds that she wouldn't name the king rather than the husband. But Manakkudavar gives a reason for that: the king's unfinished war is what holds him back. Evening, the woman's executioner, is for the man a feast to come.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","maalai","punarchi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The man, stuck on campaign, says to himself that he wants to join his wife; மனை here stands for the wife.","வேந்தற்கு உற்றுழிப் பிரிந்த தலைமகன் வினை முடிவு நீட்டித் துழித் தலைமகளை நினைந்து தன்னுள்ளே சொல்லியது."],["manakkudavar","The woman speaks: let the king win, and I will prepare a feast at home, with மனை meaning the kitchen.","நம் காதலர்க்கு விருந்து செய்வே மாக"],["kalaignar","The man speaks: if the leader wins, he will feast with his wife.","என் மனைவியுடன் எனக்கு மாலைப்பொழுதில் இன்ப விருந்துதான்."]],"ch":"k:127"},"k:1269":{"book":"kural","num":1269,"t":["ஒருநாள் எழுநாள்போல் செல்லும்சேண் சென்றார்","வருநாள்வைத்து ஏங்கு பவர்க்கு"],"p":"To those who suffer waiting for the day of return of their distant lovers one day is as long as seven days.","r":"One day passes like seven for those who pine with an eye on the day when those far away return; Parimelazhagar gives it to the man, projecting her pain, and the other commentators name no speaker.","n":"Parimelazhagar uses the general phrasing as proof that it can't be the woman: she would say 'me', so the man is imagining how it is for her. If he's right, it's a rare moment in the book when a man grasps the other side of his absence. He takes 'seven' as meaning many.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:127"},"k:1270":{"book":"kural","num":1270,"t":["பெறின்என்னாம் பெற்றக்கால் என்னாம் உறினென்னாம்","உள்ளம் உடைந்துக்கக் கால்"],"p":"After (my wife) has died of a broken heart, what good will there be if she is to receive me, has received me, or has even embraced me?","r":"What use is it if she gets, or has got, or even touches, once the heart has broken and spilled? The speaker is the man, of her, for Parimelazhagar, and the woman, of herself, for Manakkudavar.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["uyir","pirivu","punarchi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The man fears that she may die before he gets back, and it can't be her voice, since then it would be a lament and not impatience.","இது தலைமகள் கூற்றாயவழி இரங்கலாவதல்லது விதுப்பாகாமை அறிக."],["manakkudavar","The woman answers the friend who says that he will come: what use, if my own heart has already broken?","இது வருவா ரென்ற தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் கூறியது."]],"ch":"k:127"},"k:1271":{"book":"kural","num":1271,"t":["கரப்பினுங் கையிகந் தொல்லாநின் உண்கண்","உரைக்கல் உறுவதொன் றுண்டு"],"p":"Though you would conceal (your feelings), your painted eyes would not, for, transgressing (their bounds), they tell (me) something.","r":"The man tells her that though she hides it, her kohl-lined eyes have gone beyond her control and have something they want to say.","n":"In Parimelazhagar's setting the man has returned and is lavishing affection on her, and she takes fright, which makes this a kind of tragic misunderstanding. Kalaignar has the eyes declare love that resists separation. The verse only says there is something.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kurippu","kan","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:128"},"k:1272":{"book":"kural","num":1272,"t":["கண்ணிறைந்த காரிகைக் காம்பேர்தோட் பேதைக்குப்","பெண்நிறைந்த நீர்மை பெரிது"],"p":"Unusually great is the female simplicity of your maid whose beauty fills my eyes and whose shoulders resemble the bamboo.","r":"The man says this artless girl who fills his eyes, with her bamboo shoulders, has a great measure of womanly nature.","n":"The compliment may be a rebuke. Parimelazhagar glosses நீர்மை as மடமை, 'naivety', and has him complain to her friend that she fears a separation he has no thought of. Kalaignar takes it as simple praise.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","nalam","kurippu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:128"},"k:1273":{"book":"kural","num":1273,"t":["மணியில் திகழ்தரு நூல்போல் மடந்தை","அணியில் திகழ்வதொன்று உண்டு"],"p":"There is something that is implied in the beauty of this woman, like the thread that is visible in a garland of gems.","r":"The man says that, like the thread that shows in strung gems, there is something that shows through this young woman's beauty.","n":"The gem image needs transparent stones, and Parimelazhagar says crystal: inside but visible. What is inside isn't compatible with the outer beauty, he says, but appears with it.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","nalam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It's an intent that he can't himself identify, and he asks her friend to find out what it is.","மடந்தையது அணியகத்துக் கிடந்து புறத்துப் புலனாகின்றதொரு குறிப்பு உண்டு."],["manakkudavar","It's a hidden sorrow, which shows though she conceals it.","இவள் மறைக்கவும் தோற்றுகின்றதொரு துன்பம் உண்டு"]],"ch":"k:128"},"k:1274":{"book":"kural","num":1274,"t":["முகைமொக்குள் உள்ளது நாற்றம்போல் பேதை","நகைமொக்குள் உள்ளதொன் றுண்டு"],"p":"There is something in the unmatured smile of this maid like the fragrance that is contained in an unblossomed bud.","r":"The man says there is something within the bud of this girl's smile, like the scent within a closed bud.","n":"It's a companion to 1273, except that here nothing shows: scent inside a bud can't be perceived, so his certainty about it is a matter of faith. Manakkudavar sets it in courtship, with the man telling his own heart that her smile promises joy, and Kalaignar says the smile holds the memory of her lover.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","nalam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:128"},"k:1275":{"book":"kural","num":1275,"t":["செறிதொடி செய்திறந்த கள்ளம் உறுதுயர்","தீர்க்கும் மருந்தொன்று உடைத்து"],"p":"The well-meant departure of her whose bangles are tight-fitting contains a remedy that can cure my great sorrow.","r":"The stealth with which the tight-bangled woman slipped away holds a medicine for great sorrow; the speaker is the man for Parimelazhagar and the friend for Manakkudavar.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kurippu","kaamanoy","kalaviyal"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The man tells the friend that she has hidden from him out of a false fear, and the cure is for the friend to explain that he isn't leaving.","என்கண் இல்லாத தொன்றனை உட்கொண்டு அது காரணமாக என்னை மறைத்துப் போன குறிப்பு"],["manakkudavar","The friend tells the man, in courtship, that the woman's slipping away coyly means that his plea will succeed.","எதிர்ப்பட்ட தலைமகற்கு நின்குறை முடியும்; நீ இவ்விடைச் செல்லென்று தோழி கூறியது."]],"ch":"k:128"},"k:1276":{"book":"kural","num":1276,"t":["பெரிதாற்றிப் பெட்பக் கலத்தல் அரிதாற்றி","அன்பின்மை சூழ்வ துடைத்து"],"p":"The embrace that fills me with comfort and gladness is capable of enduring (my former) sorrow and meditating on his want of love.","r":"The woman tells her friend that his great comfort and eager lovemaking contains in it her having to endure hardship again and to think about his lack of love.","n":"A sad piece of reading. His tenderness is the very thing that gives him away, since she reasons from excess of kindness to a coming parting. Both older commentators give it to her, speaking to the friend.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","punarchi","kurippu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:128"},"k:1277":{"book":"kural","num":1277,"t":["தண்ணந் துறைவன் தணந்தமை நம்மினும்","முன்னம் உணர்ந்த வளை"],"p":"My bracelets have understood before me the (mental) separation of him who rules the cool seashore.","r":"The woman says her bangles knew before she did that the lord of the cool shore had parted from her.","n":"Parimelazhagar makes the separation inward: he is still physically with her but has left in his mind. Her body has registered this before her understanding did. So bangles, the book's most public symptom, are here her private source of knowledge.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["melivu","pirivu","kurippu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:128"},"k:1278":{"book":"kural","num":1278,"t":["நெருநற்றுச் சென்றார்எம் காதலர் யாமும்","எழுநாளேம் மேனி பசந்து"],"p":"It was but yesterday my lover departed (from me); and it is seven days since my complexion turned sallow.","r":"The woman says that her lover left only yesterday, and her body has been pale for seven days.","n":"The sum doesn't add up, on purpose. The pain started before the event, because she read it coming.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pasalai","pirivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","He hasn't physically left: 'left yesterday' means he made up his mind then, shown by yesterday's tenderness.","'நெருநற்றுச் செய்த தலையளியாற் பிரிவு துணியப்பட்டது' என்பாள், 'நெருநற்றுச் சென்றார்' என்றும்"],["manakkudavar","He has actually left, and she was grieving before he went.","இஃது அவர் பிரிவதன் முன்னும் பிரிவரென் றேங்கி, இன்புற்றில் மென்று தலைமகள் கூறியது."]],"ch":"k:128"},"k:1279":{"book":"kural","num":1279,"t":["தொடிநோக்கி மென்தோளும் நோக்கி அடிநோக்கி","அஃதாண் டவள்செய் தது"],"p":"She looked at her bracelets, her tender shoulders, and her feet; this was what she did there (significantly).","r":"Parimelazhagar's speaker, the friend, tells the man that she looked at her bangles, looked at her soft shoulders and looked at her feet: this is what she did there.","n":"The whole verse is a silent gesture, and the commentators read it as a sentence: 'these will slip if you leave me; these will thin; so I'll go with you on these'. The verse itself doesn't state the conclusion. This is perhaps the most purely dramatic thing in the Kural, and the friend's 'that's what she did' leaves him to work it out.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kurippu","melivu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:128"},"k:1280":{"book":"kural","num":1280,"t":["பெண்ணினால் பெண்மை உடைத்தென்ப கண்ணினால்","காமநோய் சொல்லி இரவு"],"p":"To express their love-sickness by their eyes and resort to begging bespeaks more than ordinary female excellence.","r":"Those who know say that telling love's sickness with the eyes and begging adds more womanhood to womanhood; the speaker is the man for Parimelazhagar and the friend for Manakkudavar.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kurippu","kan","kaamanoy"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The man answers: he praises her decision to go with him, as a way of telling her friend he won't be leaving at all.","தன் பிரிவின்மைக் குறிப்பினை அறிவுறுப்பான், அவள் பெண்மையினைப் பிறர்மேலிட்டு வியந்து கூறியவாறு."],["manakkudavar","The friend speaks to the man, having seen the woman's response to news of his leaving.","இது பிரிவுணர்த்திய தலைமகள் குறிப்புக்கண்டு தலைமகற்குத் தோழி சொல்லியது."]],"ch":"k:128"},"k:1281":{"book":"kural","num":1281,"t":["உள்ளக் களித்தலும் காண மகிழ்தலும்","கள்ளுக்கில் காமத்திற் குண்டு"],"p":"To please by thought and cheer by sight is peculiar, not to liquor but lust.","r":"The woman says that being intoxicated at a thought and delighted at a look belong not to liquor but to love.","n":"Parimelazhagar's setting is pointed: the friend teases her about why she isn't angry at a man planning to leave. So the famous line is an excuse. He also distinguishes the words: களித்தல், with awareness intact, and மகிழ்தல், with awareness lost.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:129"},"k:1282":{"book":"kural","num":1282,"t":["தினைத்துணையும் ஊடாமை வேண்டும் பனைத் துணையும்","காமம் நிறைய வரின்"],"p":"If women have a lust that exceeds even the measure of the palmyra fruit, they will not desire (to feign) dislike even as much as the millet.","r":"The woman says that when love fills to the size of a palmyra tree, there should be no sulking, not even the size of a millet seed.","n":"The huge and tiny are the proverbial measures. Parimelazhagar says she seems to be giving others good advice, and is in fact describing her own eagerness. Manakkudavar has her say this to her own heart, which is thinking of sulking.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:129"},"k:1283":{"book":"kural","num":1283,"t":["பேணாது பெட்பவே செய்யினும் கொண்கனைக்","காணா தமையல கண்"],"p":"Though my eyes disregard me and do what is pleasing to my husband, still will they not be satisfied unless they see him.","r":"The woman says that though he disregards her and does only what pleases him, her eyes won't rest without seeing him.","n":"It's a clear admission, with no self-deception. He is at fault, she knows it, and the eyes don't care. Pope's prose wrongly makes the eyes the ones who disregard her; the commentators and his couplet agree it is the man.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:129"},"k:1284":{"book":"kural","num":1284,"t":["ஊடற்கண் சென்றேன்மன் தோழி அதுமறந்து","கூடற்கண் சென்றது என் னெஞ்சு"],"p":"O my friend! I was prepared to feign displeasure but my mind forgetting it was ready to embrace him.","r":"The woman tells her friend that she went there to sulk, but her heart forgot that and went there to unite.","n":"The same verb, சென்றேன் and சென்றது, is used for both: she and her heart set out together and went to different places. Parimelazhagar's word for this is அறைபோக, treason in a military sense.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","nenju","punarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:129"},"k:1285":{"book":"kural","num":1285,"t":["எழுதுங்கால் கோல்காணாக் கண்ணேபோல் கொண்கன்","பழிகாணேன் கண்ட இடத்து"],"p":"Like the eyes which see not the pencil that paints it, I cannot see my husband's fault (just) when I meet him.","r":"The woman says that, as the eye can't see the stick that lines it at the moment of lining, she doesn't see her husband's fault when she sees him.","n":"A wonderfully precise image: the object is at its closest and most intimate exactly when it can't be seen. Parimelazhagar notes that the eye knows the stick well enough at other times, and so she too knows his faults.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:129"},"k:1286":{"book":"kural","num":1286,"t":["காணுங்கால் காணேன் தவறாய காணாக்கால்","காணேன் தவறல் லவை"],"p":"When I see my husband, I do not see any faults; but when I do not see him, I do not see anything but faults.","r":"The woman says that when she sees him she doesn't see his faults, and when she doesn't see him she sees nothing but his faults.","n":"A perfectly symmetrical verse about what presence does to judgement. As with the previous verse, this isn't presented as an illusion that she can see through and correct; it's just how it is. Sight is what removes her power to judge him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","kaamam","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:129"},"k:1287":{"book":"kural","num":1287,"t":["உய்த்தல் அறிந்து புனல்பாய் பவரேபோல்","பொய்த்தல் அறிந்தென் புலந்து"],"p":"Like those who leap into a stream which they know will carry them off, why should a wife feign dislike which she knows cannot hold out long?","r":"The woman asks what use it is to sulk, knowing it will fail, like those who jump into a stream knowing it will carry them off.","n":"Parimelazhagar registers a strain in the syntax, and offers an alternate shape: 'I learned by trying once that sulking fails; I won't do it again'. Pope's couplet is rather different in having a rescue. The swimmer who knows she'll be swept away jumps in anyway, and the verse doesn't deny that this might be the fun of it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:129"},"k:1288":{"book":"kural","num":1288,"t":["இளித்தக்க இன்னா செயினும் களித்தார்க்குக்","கள்ளற்றே கள்வநின் மார்பு"],"p":"O you rogue! your breast is to me what liquor is to those who rejoice in it, though it only gives them an unpleasant disgrace.","r":"Thief, your chest is to us as liquor is to those who drink it, though it does them shameful harm; the speaker is the friend for Parimelazhagar and the woman for Manakkudavar.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","naanam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The friend tells the man that she has seen the woman's eagerness, and uses 'us' for them both.","தலைமகள் புணர்ச்சி? விதுப்பு அறிந்த தோழி, தலைமகற்குச் சொல்லியது."],["manakkudavar","The woman speaks to the man herself, having let go of the wish to sulk.","இது புலவிக்குறிப்பு நீங்கின தலை மகள் தலைமகற்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"ch":"k:129"},"k:1289":{"book":"kural","num":1289,"t":["மலரினும் மெல்லிது காமம் சிலர்அதன்","செவ்வி தலைப்படு வார்"],"p":"Sexual delight is more delicate than a flower, and few are those who understand its real nature.","r":"Love is softer than a flower, and few attain its moment; the speaker is the man for Parimelazhagar and the woman, to her heart, for Manakkudavar.","n":"The famous line has a practical use. Parimelazhagar lists what makes this moment rare: two people need to share intent, desire, enjoyment and pleasure at one time, in a fit place, moment and setting. He has the man saying this to end her sulk, so the maxim is also a plea.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kaamam","punarchi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The man speaks during a sulk that won't yield to his pleading, in order to end it.","உணர்ப்புவயின் வாரா ஊடற்கண் தலைமகன் சொல்லியது."],["manakkudavar","The woman, seeing that he desires union, tells her own heart that this isn't the moment to sulk.","இப்பொழுது ஊடுவையாயின், இக்காமஞ் செவ்விதப்பு மென்று புணர்ச்சி வேட்கையால் தலைமகள் நெஞ்சோடு கூறியது."]],"ch":"k:129"},"k:1290":{"book":"kural","num":1290,"t":["கண்ணின் துனித்தே கலங்கினாள் புல்லுதல்","என்னினும் தான்விதுப் புற்று"],"p":"She once feigned dislike in her eyes, but the warmth of her embrace exceeded my own.","r":"The man recalls that she sulked with her eyes alone and then, more eager than he for the embrace, was undone.","n":"Parimelazhagar gives this an edge: spoken during her present sulk, it means 'the woman who gave up so quickly was not this one'. Manakkudavar has him simply observing her now. A rare moment of him looking back on her eagerness, and one that might flatter him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","kan","punarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:129"},"k:1291":{"book":"kural","num":1291,"t":["அவர்நெஞ்சு அவர்க்காதல் கண்டும் எவன்நெஞ்சே","நீஎமக்கு ஆகா தது"],"p":"O my soul! although you have seen how his soul stands by him, how is it you do not stand by me?","r":"The woman asks her heart why it isn't hers, when it has seen how his heart belongs to him.","n":"An elegantly sharp opening. Two hearts are compared as servants, and his has a better idea of loyalty. Parimelazhagar adds the sting: even those who can't figure out what to do can copy someone else, and the heart won't do even that.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["nenju","oodal"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:130"},"k:1292":{"book":"kural","num":1292,"t":["உறாஅ தவர்க்கண்ட கண்ணும் அவரைச்","செறாஅரெனச் சேறியென் நெஞ்சு"],"p":"O my soul! although you have known him who does not love me, still do you go to him, saying \"he will not be displeased.\"","r":"The woman says that her heart, even after seeing that he isn't close to her, goes to him as if he weren't hostile.","n":"The heart's reason is the small hope, that he won't be angry. Parimelazhagar says it's relying on old familiarity, which is a thin basis, and calls it the greatest folly.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","orutalai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:130"},"k:1293":{"book":"kural","num":1293,"t":["கெட்டார்க்கு நட்டார்இல் என்பதோ நெஞ்சேநீ","பெட்டாங்கு அவர்பின் செலல்"],"p":"O my soul! do you follow him at pleasure under the belief that the ruined have no friends?","r":"The woman asks her heart if it follows him as it pleases, on the principle that the ruined have no friends.","n":"A piece of proverbial wisdom about false friends, turned against the heart. By using it she also admits that she is the one ruined. Parimelazhagar glosses கெட்டார் as her loss of honour, and the verse casts her heart as a fair-weather friend.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["nenju","natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:130"},"k:1294":{"book":"kural","num":1294,"t":["இனிஅன்ன நின்னொடு சூழ்வார்யார் நெஞ்சே","துனிசெய்து துவ்வாய்காண் மற்று"],"p":"O my soul! you would not first seem sulky and then enjoy (him); who then would in future consult you about such things?","r":"The woman tells her heart that it won't first sulk and then enjoy, so no one will plan such things with it any more.","n":"It's almost comic: she drops the heart from her team as an unreliable accomplice. Sulking here is a strategy with a sequence to it, sulk first and enjoy later, and the heart can't wait.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["nenju","oodal"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:130"},"k:1295":{"book":"kural","num":1295,"t":["பெறாஅமை அஞ்சும் பெறின்பிரிவு அஞ்சும்","அறாஅ இடும்பைத்தென் நெஞ்சு"],"p":"My soul fears when it is without him; it also fears when it is with him; it is sub ject to incessant sorrow.","r":"The woman says her heart fears not having him and, on having him, fears parting, so it is in endless grief.","n":"In Parimelazhagar's setting she says this within the hearing of a friend who has come as an envoy, so it also means she will let him in. He observes that there is no third state, which makes this grief complete.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","pirivu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:130"},"k:1296":{"book":"kural","num":1296,"t":["தனியே இருந்து நினைத்தக்கால் என்னைத்","தினிய இருந்ததென் நெஞ்சு"],"p":"My mind has been (here) in order to eat me up (as it were) whenever I think of him in my solitude.","r":"The woman says that when she sits alone and thinks of him, her heart is there only to eat her.","n":"Parimelazhagar notes the irony: it stays with her only to work on her, not to help her quarrel. Manakkudavar gives this a small domestic turn: she tells the friend, 'you've come now, and so I've escaped it'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","ninaivu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:130"},"k:1297":{"book":"kural","num":1297,"t":["நாணும் மறந்தேன் அவர்மறக் கல்லாஎன்","மாணா மடநெஞ்சிற் பட்டு"],"p":"I have even forgotten my modesty, having been caught in my foolish mind which is not dignified enough to forget him.","r":"The woman says that, stuck with this worthless, foolish heart that can't forget him, she has even forgotten shame.","n":"There are two forgettings in tension. Parimelazhagar's definition of shame is one of the finest things in his commentary: however long two have been together, to shrink as if seeing the other for the first time. His definition of the heart's foolishness: remembering when he's away, forgetting when he's there.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["naanam","nenju","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:130"},"k:1298":{"book":"kural","num":1298,"t":["எள்ளின் இளிவாம்என்று எண்ணி அவர்திறம்","உள்ளும் உயிர்க்காதல் நெஞ்சு"],"p":"My soul which clings to life thinks only of his (own) gain in the belief that it would be disgraceful for it to despise him.","r":"The woman says her heart, thinking it would be disgraceful to despise him, keeps thinking of what pertains to him, out of உயிர்க்காதல்.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["nenju","uyir","ninaivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","உயிர்க்காதல் means the heart loves its own life: it won't die, and so it plans reconciliation.","உயிர்க்காதல் நெஞ்சு - உயிர்மேல் காதலையுடைய என் நெஞ்சு"],["kalaignar","It's a heart whose love is as dear as life, and it thinks on his greatness.","அவருடைய பெருமையைப் பற்றியே என்னுயிர்க் காதல் நெஞ்சம்"]],"ch":"k:130"},"k:1299":{"book":"kural","num":1299,"t":["துன்பத்திற்கு யாரே துணையாவார் தாமுடைய","நெஞ்சந் துணையல் வழி"],"p":"Who would help me out of one's distress, when one's own soul refuses help to one?","r":"Who will be a companion in grief, when one's own heart is no companion? The speaker is the man for Parimelazhagar and the woman for Manakkudavar.","n":"If it's the man, then he has come to the state she has been in for ten verses. His heart is doing to him what hers did: urging union when he should be standing firm.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["nenju","oodal"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The man speaks, in a sulk that won't yield, whose heart still wants her.","உணர்ப்புவயின் வாரா ஊடற்கண் தலைமகன் சொல்லியது."],["manakkudavar","The woman replies to the friend who asks who would help.","இது தலைமகள் துணையாவார் யாரென்ற தோழிக்குக் கூறியது."]],"ch":"k:130"},"k:1300":{"book":"kural","num":1300,"t":["தஞ்சம் தமரல்லர் ஏதிலார் தாமுடைய","நெஞ்சம் தமரல் வழி"],"p":"It is hardly possible for strangers to behave like relations, when one's own soul acts like a stranger.","r":"The man, for Parimelazhagar, says that it's no wonder if strangers aren't one's own, when one's own heart isn't.","n":"Parimelazhagar gives a witty aim: 'stranger' here is the sulking woman, who no longer seems his beloved, so if his own heart pains him for her sake, it's a small thing if she, a stranger, sulks. The other commentators don't name a speaker, and read it as a maxim.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","oodal"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:130"},"k:1301":{"book":"kural","num":1301,"t":["புல்லா திராஅப் புலத்தை அவர் உறும்","அல்லல்நோய் காண்கம் சிறிது"],"p":"Let us witness awhile his keen suffering; just feign dislike and embrace him not.","r":"The friend tells the woman not to embrace him but to sulk, so that they may watch his sharp pain for a little while.","n":"It's mischief with a purpose. Parimelazhagar says the friend has actually come as his envoy, and sides with the woman, teasing, as a way of bringing her round. சிறிது, 'a little', is the word that matters: the quarrel mustn't go on long.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:131"},"k:1302":{"book":"kural","num":1302,"t":["உப்பமைந் தற்றால் புலவி அதுசிறிது","மிக்கற்றால் நீள விடல்"],"p":"A little dislike is like salt in proportion; to prolong it a little is like salt a little too much.","r":"The friend says that sulking is like the right amount of salt, and letting it go on a little too long is like a little too much salt.","n":"The image puts sulking in the kitchen, as an everyday and necessary ingredient without any drama. The second line is exact: just a little too much is enough to spoil it. Kalaignar makes the measure a time span, and Parimelazhagar makes it knowing when desire is about to fade.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:131"},"k:1303":{"book":"kural","num":1303,"t":["அலந்தாரை அல்லல்நோய் செய்தற்றால் தம்மைப்","புலந்தாரைப் புல்லா விடல்"],"p":"For men not to embrace those who have feigned dislike is like torturing those already in agony.","r":"The woman says that not embracing those who have sulked with you is like adding sharp pain to those already in distress.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","kaamanoy","parathai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","She means courtesans: go back to them, she tells the man returning from them, since they'll suffer without you. It's sarcasm.","நும்மைப் பெறாது புலந்தூடியிருக்கின்ற பரத்தையரைப் போய்ப் புலவி நீக்கிப் புல்லீராயின், அவராற்றார்"],["kalaignar","It's a general truth about lovers: leaving a sulking partner unreconciled is cruel.","ஊடல் கொண்டவரின் ஊடல் நீக்கித் தழுவாமல் விடுதல்"]],"ch":"k:131"},"k:1304":{"book":"kural","num":1304,"t":["ஊடி யவரை உணராமை வாடிய","வள்ளி முதலரிந் தற்று"],"p":"Not to reconcile those who have feigned dislike is like cutting a faded creeper at its root.","r":"The woman says that not reconciling those who have sulked is like cutting a withered creeper at its root.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","parathai","pirivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","She again means the other women, and she adds that she has no right to sulk, since she has their sons to look at; so go to them.","நும்மோடு ஊடிய பரத்தையரை ஊடலுணர்த்திக் கூடாதொழிதல்"],["kalaignar","It's about any partner who stays aloof from a sulking lover.","ஊடல்புரிந்து பிணங்கியிருப்பவரிடம் அன்பு செலுத்திடாமல் விலகியே இருப்பின்"]],"ch":"k:131"},"k:1305":{"book":"kural","num":1305,"t":["நலத்தகை நல்லவர்க்கு ஏஎர் புலத்தகை","பூஅன்ன கண்ணார் அகத்து"],"p":"An increased shyness in those whose eyes are like flowers is beautiful even to good and virtuous husbands.","r":"The grace of those good in their virtues is the depth of sulking in flower-eyed women; the speaker is the man for Parimelazhagar and the friend for Manakkudavar.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","nalam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The man, reconciled, reflects to himself that even faultless men find this sulking lovely.","தலைமகளைப் புலவி நீக்கிக் கூடிய தலைமகன் தன்னுள்ளே சொல்லியது."],["manakkudavar","The friend tells the woman that sulking suits courtesans, and that she shouldn't sulk at those not worth it.","இது பரத்தையரோடு புலந்து கூறிய தலைமகட்குத் தோழி கூறியது."]],"ch":"k:131"},"k:1306":{"book":"kural","num":1306,"t":["துனியும் புலவியும் இல்லாயின் காமம்","கனியும் கருக்காயும் அற்று"],"p":"Sexual pleasure, without prolonged and short-lived dislike, is like too ripe, and unripe fruit.","r":"Without serious and light quarrels, love is like over-ripe fruit and unripe fruit; the speaker is the man for Parimelazhagar and the woman for Manakkudavar.","n":"There are two kinds of quarrels for two kinds of failure, and the verse uses them in order: no deep quarrel, and love is fruit going off; no light quarrel, and love is unripe. Manakkudavar supplies the tastes, sour and astringent. Pope's couplet reads 'ripened fruit' as a good thing, and the commentators don't.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","kaamam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The man praises both kinds of quarrel after making up.","இவ்விரண்டும் வேண்டும் என்று வியந்து கூறியவாறு."],["manakkudavar","The woman speaks, saying that there is some good in the sulking that is then healed.","இஃது உணர்தற்கு நல்லது உளதென்று தலைமகள் கூறியது."]],"ch":"k:131"},"k:1307":{"book":"kural","num":1307,"t":["ஊடலின் உண்டாங்கோர் துன்பம் புணர்வது","நீடுவ தன்று கொல் என்று"],"p":"The doubt as to whether intercourse would take place soon or not, creates a sorrow (even) in feigned dislike.","r":"The man says there is one pain even within sulking: the worry about whether union will be delayed or not.","n":"This is where the game has its risk. Parimelazhagar takes it as the worry that union will be put off, and Kalaignar as a worry that the time for union will shrink. In either case, the player can't be sure the quarrel will end in time.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","punarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:131"},"k:1308":{"book":"kural","num":1308,"t":["நோதல் எவன்மற்று நொந்தாரென்று அஃதறியும்","காதலர் இல்லா வழி"],"p":"What avails sorrow when I am without a wife who can understand the cause of my sorrow?","r":"What use is it to suffer, when there is no lover who knows that this person is suffering? The speaker is the man for Parimelazhagar, and the friend, speaking to him, for Manakkudavar.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","orutalai","kaamanoy"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The man is himself sulking, because she won't yield to his pleading.","இவள் நம் காதலியல்லள்; அன்மையின், இந்நோவு அறியாள்"],["manakkudavar","The friend tells the man this when she has come inside, knowing about his sulking.","அதனை யறிந்து அகம்புக்க தோழி அவனுக்குச் சொல்லியது."]],"ch":"k:131"},"k:1309":{"book":"kural","num":1309,"t":["நீரும் நிழலது இனிதே புலவியும்","வீழுநர் கண்ணே இனிது"],"p":"Like water in the shade, dislike is delicious only in those who love.","r":"The man says water is sweet in the shade, and sulking too is sweet only with those who love.","n":"It's a reciprocal theory of sulking. A quarrel is a pleasure only inside a shared love, and without it there is only heat. Parimelazhagar applies this pointedly: she doesn't feel this for him, and so her sulk is unpleasant.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:131"},"k:1310":{"book":"kural","num":1310,"t":["ஊடல் உணங்க விடுவாரோடு என்நெஞ்சம்","கூடுவேம் என்பது அவா"],"p":"It is nothing but strong desire that makes her mind unite with me who can leave her to her own dislike.","r":"The man, in Parimelazhagar's reading, says that his heart's wish to join with someone who leaves a sulk to wither is simply craving.","n":"அவா, 'craving', is a strong word to use on one's own heart, with a long negative history in the Kural's ethics. He diagnoses himself exactly as the ascetic chapter might, and it doesn't stop him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["oodal","nenju","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:131"},"k:1311":{"book":"kural","num":1311,"t":["பெண்ணியலார் எல்லாரும் கண்ணின் பொதுஉண்பர்","நண்ணேன் பரத்தநின் மார்பு"],"p":"You are given to prostitution; all those who are born as womankind enjoy you with their eyes in an ordinary way. I will not embrace you.","r":"The woman calls him a philanderer and refuses his chest, because all women feast on him in common with their eyes.","n":"The offence is only that he was seen, and being seen is a form of being consumed. பரத்த is applied to him in the vocative, and is the verse's own word for his straying.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["oodal","kan","parathai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","பெண்ணியலார் means courtesans, who, lacking other virtues, have only the nature of women. They look at him as his procession passes.","பரத்தையர்க்குள்ளது பெண் இயற்கை மாத்திரமே என்னுங் கருத்தால், 'பெண் இயலார்' என்றாள்."],["manakkudavar","It simply means all women, who think of his chest as common property.","நின் மார்பைப் பெண்மை யுடையா ரெல்லாரும் தமக்குப் பொதுவாக நினைத்துக்"]],"ch":"k:132"},"k:1312":{"book":"kural","num":1312,"t":["ஊடி இருந்தேமாத் தும்மினார் யாம்தம்மை","நீடுவாழ் கென்பாக் கறிந்து"],"p":"When I continued to be sulky he sneezed and thought I would (then) wish him a long life.","r":"The woman tells her friend that while she was sulking he sneezed, knowing she would then say 'long life' and so break her silence.","n":"A real gem of observed domestic strategy. Parimelazhagar says what makes this a 'fine point': she takes a natural sneeze for a deliberate sign. We can't tell if he meant it, and that is the fun.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","kurippu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:132"},"k:1313":{"book":"kural","num":1313,"t":["கோட்டுப் பூச் சூடினும் காயும் ஒருத்தியைக்","காட்டிய சூடினீர் என்று"],"p":"Even if I were adorned with a garland of branch-flowers, she would say I did so to show it to another woman.","r":"The man tells her friend that even if he wears a wreath of bent flowers, she is angry and says he wore it to show some other woman.","n":"The word கோட்டுப்பூ is a puzzle. Parimelazhagar offers flowers woven in a curve or flowers from another landscape, Manakkudavar offers side-worn flowers, and Kalaignar flowers from a tree-branch. The offence doesn't depend on the answer, which is the point.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","parathai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:132"},"k:1314":{"book":"kural","num":1314,"t":["யாரினும் காதலம் என்றேனா ஊடினாள்","யாரினும் யாரினும் என்று"],"p":"When I said I loved her more than any other woman, she said \"more than others, yes, more than others,\" and remained sulky.","r":"The man says he told her he loved more than anyone, and she sulked: 'than whom, than whom?'","n":"A grammatical quarrel. He meant 'we love more than any other couple', and she took it to mean 'more than the other women I love'. The commentators spell this out, and the verse gives only her repeated words, one of the most natural bits of speech in the book.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:132"},"k:1315":{"book":"kural","num":1315,"t":["இம்மைப் பிறப்பில் பிரியலம் என்றேனாக்","கண்நிறை நீர்கொண் டனள்"],"p":"When I said I would never part from her in this life her eyes were filled with tears.","r":"The man says he told her they wouldn't part in this birth, and her eyes filled with tears.","n":"A promise made in the language of rebirth backfires on its own qualifier: 'in this birth' implies 'not in the next'. Both Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar explain it so. Kalaignar has her ask 'is there a next birth, then?', which is a modern touch that isn't in the verse. Rebirth is taken for granted, as material for a joke.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["oodal","pirivu","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:132"},"k:1316":{"book":"kural","num":1316,"t":["உள்ளினேன் என்றேன்மற் றென்மறந்தீர் என்றென்னைப்","புல்லாள் புலத்தக் கனள்"],"p":"When I said I had remembered her, she said I had forgotten her and relaxing her embrace, began to feign dislike.","r":"The man says he told her he had thought of her, and she said 'why did you forget?', and wouldn't embrace him, and went into a sulk.","n":"A strict logical inference, and Parimelazhagar uses a term from Indian logic for it, அருத்தாபத்தி: remembering presupposes forgetting. She is using the man's own argument from 1125.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:132"},"k:1317":{"book":"kural","num":1317,"t":["வழுத்தினாள் தும்மினேன் ஆக அழித்தழுதாள்","யாருள்ளித் தும்மினீர் என்று"],"p":"When I sneezed she blessed me, but at once changed (her mind) and wept, asking, \"At the thought of whom did you sneeze?\"","r":"The man says that when he sneezed she blessed him, and then undid it and wept: 'who were you thinking of, that you sneezed?'","n":"The blessing comes automatically and the suspicion comes a second later, and the verse catches that interval. Parimelazhagar notes that the belief behind this is a women's usage (மகளிர் வழக்கு) and that she's taking it as literal fact.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:132"},"k:1318":{"book":"kural","num":1318,"t":["தும்முச் செறுப்ப அழுதாள் நுமர்உள்ளல்","எம்மை மறைத்திரோ என்று"],"p":"When I suppressed my sneezing, she wept saying, \"I suppose you (did so) to hide from me your own people's remembrance of you\".","r":"The man says that when he suppressed a sneeze, she wept: 'are you hiding from me that your people are thinking of you?'","n":"It's the next stage of the farce. He has learned from 1317, and that's now the crime. Parimelazhagar sums it up: it's a fault to sneeze and a fault not to. So what is there to do?","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["oodal","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:132"},"k:1319":{"book":"kural","num":1319,"t":["தன்னை உணர்த்தினும் காயும் பிறர்க்கும்நீர்","இந்நீரர் ஆகுதிர் என்று"],"p":"Even when I try to remove her dislike, she is displeased and says, \"This is the way you behave towards (other women).\"","r":"The man says that even when he tries to appease her, she is angry and says that this is how he behaves with other women too.","n":"The cure has become evidence. Parimelazhagar notes the extra detail: he has even admitted to faults he didn't commit in order to placate her, and that confession is what now counts against him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["oodal","parathai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:132"},"k:1320":{"book":"kural","num":1320,"t":["நினைத்திருந்து நோக்கினும் காயும் அனைத்துநீர்","யாருள்ளி நோக்கினீர் என்று"],"p":"Even when I look on her contemplating (her beauty), she is displeased and says, \"With whose thought have you (thus) looked on my person?\"","r":"The man says that even when he just sits and gazes at her in thought, she is angry: 'whom are you thinking of as you look me over?'","n":"Silence and adoration are his last resort, and they fail too. For Parimelazhagar, her logic is very neat: as no one woman could match all her parts, he must be comparing each part with a different woman, so there must be several. So she wants them named.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["oodal","kan","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:132"},"k:1321":{"book":"kural","num":1321,"t":["இல்லை தவறவர்க்கு ஆயினும் ஊடுதல்","வல்லது அவர்அளிக்கு மாறு"],"p":"Although my husband is free from defects, the way in which he embraces me is such as to make me feign dislike.","r":"The woman says that even though he has no fault, the way he shows his grace has the power to bring on a sulk.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["oodal","kaamam","parathai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It is jealousy: his love is so delightful that others surely enjoy it too, and she can't stand the thought.","யான் எய்தற் பாலதாய இத்தலையளி ஒழிந்தாரும் எய்துவர் எனக்கருதி, அது பொறாமையான் ஊடல்நிகழா நின்றது"],["kalaignar","He reverses the direction: the quarrel, between faultless lovers, builds up their love.","அவர்களின் அன்பை மிகுதியாக வளர்க்கக் கூடியது."]],"ch":"k:133"},"k:1322":{"book":"kural","num":1322,"t":["ஊடலின் தோன்றும் சிறுதுனி நல்லளி","வாடினும் பாடு பெறும்"],"p":"His love will increase though it may (at first seem to) fade through the short-lived distress caused by (my) dislike.","r":"The woman says that even if his fine grace droops a little through the small misery sulking brings, it rises to greatness.","n":"She is justifying the risk to a friend who has pointed out she could have his kindness without quarrelling. வாடினும், 'though it droop', uses a word for a plant that comes back stronger. Parimelazhagar keeps the concession small: a quarrel with no real fault passes quickly.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:133"},"k:1323":{"book":"kural","num":1323,"t":["புலத்தலின் புத்தேள்நாடு உண்டோ நிலத்தொடு","நீரியைந் தன்னார் அகத்து"],"p":"Is there a celestial land that can please like the feigned dislike of those whose union resembles that of earth and water?","r":"The woman asks if there is any heaven to compare with sulking with one to whom one is joined as water to earth.","n":"Readers of Tamil poetry will recall Kuruntokai 40 here, with its rain and red earth. I offer that as an echo. In the sequence Manakkudavar's text gives, these are the last words of the Kural.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","punarchi","anbu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Water takes on the character of the ground it's in, so men take on the nature of the women they touch, which is the reason for her sulking. It's a surprisingly suspicious reading of a famous image.","நீர் தான் நின்ற நிலத்தியல்பிற்றாமாறு போலக் காதலரும் தாம் கூடிய மகளிரியல்பினராகலான்"],["manakkudavar","Earth and water together combine heat and coolness, and in the same way lovers experience joy and pain as one.","நிலத்தொடு நீரியை தலால் அவை வெப்பமுந்தட்பமுங் கூடியிருக்குமாறு போல்"],["kalaignar","புத்தேள்நாடு isn't a divine world but a new one, which removes heaven from the verse.","ஊடல் கொள்வதைவிடப் புதிய உலகம் வேறொன்று இருக்க முடியுமா?"]],"ch":"k:133"},"k:1324":{"book":"kural","num":1324,"t":["புல்லி விடாஅப் புலவியுள் தோன்றுமென்","உள்ளம் உடைக்கும் படை"],"p":"In prolonged dislike after an embrace there is a weapon that can break my heart.","r":"Within the sulk that follows an embrace and won't let go arises the weapon that breaks 'my' heart; the speaker is the woman for Parimelazhagar and the man for Manakkudavar.","n":"Both identify the weapon: his bows and soft words for Parimelazhagar, and soft words (பணிமொழி) for Manakkudavar. Being broken is exactly what she is after, so the battle imagery here is pure pleasure. Compare the same weapon in 1258.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","punarchi","nirai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The woman answers the friend's question about what will end this sulk.","'அப்புலவி இனி யாதான் நீங்கும்?' என்றாட்குச் சொல்லியது."],["manakkudavar","He calls the group at this point the man's speech.","இவையெட்டும் தலைமகன் கூற்று ."]],"ch":"k:133"},"k:1325":{"book":"kural","num":1325,"t":["தவறிலர் ஆயினும் தாம்வீழ்வார் மென்றோள்","அகறலின் ஆங்கொன் றுடைத்து"],"p":"Though free from defects, men feel pleased when they cannot embrace the delicate shoulders of those whom they love.","r":"The man, delighted after making up, says that though men may be faultless, being kept from the soft arms of those they love has a certain something.","n":"ஆங்கொன்று, 'a certain something', is an honest refusal to name it, and Parimelazhagar takes it that way: the pleasure is beyond words. Even the wrongly accused gets something from this. It matches 1321, in which she sulks at a man with no fault.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","punarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:133"},"k:1326":{"book":"kural","num":1326,"t":["உணலினும் உண்டது அறல்இனிது காமம்","புணர்தலின் ஊடல் இனிது"],"p":"To digest what has been eaten is more delightful than to eat more; likewise love is more delightful in dislike than intercourse.","r":"The man says digesting what you have eaten is sweeter than eating, and for love sulking is sweeter than union.","n":"Earthy and unusual, as digestion rarely comes into love poetry. The thought behind it is appetite: as Parimelazhagar says, hunger makes food better, and distance makes union better.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","punarchi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:133"},"k:1327":{"book":"kural","num":1327,"t":["ஊடலில் தோற்றவர் வென்றார் அதுமன்னும்","கூடலிற் காணப் படும்"],"p":"Those are conquerors whose dislike has been defeated and that is proved by the love (which follows).","r":"The man says that in a lovers' quarrel the one who loses wins, as can be seen when they come together.","n":"A paradox with a precise explanation in Parimelazhagar: the one who gives way first gets the great joy, and only later learns this. He adds the first-person point: I gave way, and so I got this joy.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","punarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:133"},"k:1328":{"book":"kural","num":1328,"t":["ஊடிப் பெறுகுவம் கொல்லோ நுதல்வெயர்ப்பக்","கூடலில் தோன்றிய உப்பு"],"p":"Will I enjoy once more through her dislike, the pleasure of that love that makes her forehead perspire?","r":"The man asks whether by sulking they might again win that savour which appeared in union, with her forehead wet with sweat.","n":"உப்பு, 'salt', stands for sweetness here, and Parimelazhagar glosses it as இனிமை, which he explains as all five senses at once, recalling 1101. It's a frankly physical detail near the end of the Kural, and Manakkudavar notes that sulking belongs to both of them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","punarchi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:133"},"k:1329":{"book":"kural","num":1329,"t":["ஊடுக மன்னோ ஒளியிழை யாமிரப்ப","நீடுக மன்னோ இரா"],"p":"May the bright-jewelled one feign dislike, and may the night be prolonged for me to implore her!","r":"The man wishes that the bright-jewelled woman would keep on sulking, and that the night would last, so that he may go on pleading.","n":"The pleading has become what he wants, and not merely a way to get to what he wants. யாமிரப்ப, 'that we may plead': the man who began the book with his might broken now prays for the chance to beg.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:133"},"k:1330":{"book":"kural","num":1330,"t":["ஊடுதல் காமத்திற்கு இன்பம் அதற்கின்பம்","கூடி முயங்கப் பெறின்"],"p":"Dislike adds delight to love; and a hearty embrace (thereafter) will add delight to dislike.","r":"The man says sulking is love's pleasure, and the pleasure of sulking is being able to come together and embrace.","n":"In the received order, the last verse of the entire work ends on பெறின், 'if one gets to'. So 1,330 verses on virtue, wealth and love close with a chained definition and an open condition. Parimelazhagar explains the condition: knowing how far to go is the hard part, so that the union will still be possible.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","punarchi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:133"},"n:1":{"book":"naladiyar","num":1,"t":["அறுசுவை யுண்டி அமர்ந்தில்லாள் ஊட்ட","மறுசிகை நீக்கியுண் டாரும் - வறிஞராய்ச்","சென்றிரப்பர் ஓரிடத்துக் கூழ்எனின், செல்வம்ஒன்று","உண்டாக வைக்கற்பாற் றன்று."],"p":"Those who ate erewhile, course after course, food of six flavours, supplied by their complaisant spouse, now roam as paupers and beg a mess of pottage here and there; if so, let wealth be counted as a thing of nought!","r":"Those who once ate six-flavoured food served by a willing wife, waving away a second helping, may go begging for gruel somewhere; so wealth is not a thing to be counted as lasting.","n":"The detail of refusing the second mouthful sets the excess of the past against the single bowl of gruel begged later; the argument rests on a picture, not on a general claim.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","selvam","iravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:1"},"n:2":{"book":"naladiyar","num":2,"t":["துகள்தீர் பெருஞ்செல்வம் தோன்றியக்கால் தொட்டுப்","பகடு நடந்தகூழ் பல்லாரோ டுண்க","அகடுற யார்மாட்டும் நில்லாது செல்வம்","சகடக்கால் போல வரும்."],"p":"When you own ample wealth acquired by blameless means, with many sharing eat the grain that steers have trodden out! In centre poised prosperity stands with no man, but revolves like the waggon's wheel.","r":"When blameless wealth comes, from that very day share its food with many, because wealth never stays level with anyone but turns like a cart wheel.","n":"துகள்தீர் (blameless) qualifies the wealth to be shared: the verse assumes it was rightly gained. Mudaliar reads the food trodden out by beasts as a hint that others have a share in it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","nilaiyaamai","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:1"},"n:3":{"book":"naladiyar","num":3,"t":["யானை எருத்தம் பொலியக் குடைநிழற்கீழ்ச்","சேனைத் தலைவராய்ச் சென்றோரும் - ஏனை","வினைஉலப்ப வேறாகி வீழ்வர்தாம் கொண்ட","மனையாளை மாற்றார் கொள."],"p":"Those who rode resplendent forth on the neck of an elephant, beneath the state umbrella's shade, as the leaders of the host, when 'other deeds' destroy, shallchange and fall, while foes lead away their wives as captives.","r":"Even kings who rode in state on elephants under the royal umbrella, at the head of armies, fall and are changed when their deeds turn against them, and enemies take the wives they married.","n":"Both commentators take ஏனை வினை as evil deeds that destroy; Pope keeps it as 'other deeds'. The verse itself names only 'the other deeds', so the cause of the fall is left in the vocabulary of karma rather than of war.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","vinai","arasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:1"},"n:4":{"book":"naladiyar","num":4,"t":["நின்றன நின்றன நில்லா எனவுணர்ந்து","ஒன்றின ஒன்றின வல்லே செயின்செய்க","சென்றன சென்றன வாழ்நாள் செறுத்துடன்","வந்தது வந்தது கூற்று."],"p":"The things of which you said, ‘they stand, they stand,' stand not; mark this, and perform what befits, yea! what befits, with all your power! Your days are gone, are gone! and death close pressing on is come, is come!","r":"Realise that the things you think will stand do not stand, and do what good you can at once, for your days are passing and Death is coming in anger.","n":"Mudaliar notes that the first two doublings express abundance and the last two continuity: the form of the verse enacts days going and Death arriving.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","kootru","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:1"},"n:5":{"book":"naladiyar","num":5,"t":["என்னானும் ஒன்றுதம் கையுறப் பெற்றக்கால்","பின்னாவ தென்று பிடித்திரா - முன்னே","கொடுத்தார் உயப்போவர் கோடில்தீக் கூற்றம்","தொடுத்தாறு செல்லும் சுரம்."],"p":"When you have gained and hold in hand any single thing, retain it not with the thought, ‘This will serve some other day!’ Those who have given betimes shall escape the desert road along which death, an unyielding foe, drags his captives away.","r":"Whatever comes into your hand, do not hold it back thinking it can be given later; those who gave first will escape the waste land through which impartial Death drags his captives.","n":"கோடில் makes Death impartial: it is the one force that does not favour the rich, which is why only what was given away counts.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","kootru","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:1"},"n:6":{"book":"naladiyar","num":6,"t":["இழைத்தநாள் எல்லை இகவா பிழைத்தெரிணஇக்","கூற்றம் குதித்துய்ந்தார் ஈங்கில்லை - ஆற்றப்","பெரும்பொருள் வைத்தீர், வழங்குமின், நாளைத்","தழீஇம் தழீஇம் தண்ணம் படும்."],"p":"Man's days pass not their assigned bound. None here on earth have ever escaped death's power, made off and got free. O hoarder of ample wealth, dispense it! On the morn the funeral drum will sound.","r":"The appointed span of days cannot be exceeded and no one here has leapt clear of Death and escaped; you who hold great wealth, give it away, for tomorrow the funeral drum may sound.","n":"Mudaliar glosses the 'appointed day' as the lifespan set by past deeds, and cites Kural 269 for the verb குதித்தல், the very verb the verse uses to deny that anyone escapes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","eegai","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:1"},"n:7":{"book":"naladiyar","num":7,"t":["தோற்றம்சால் ஞாயிறு நாழியா வைகலும்","கூற்றம் அளந்துநும் நாளுண்ணும் - ஆற்ற","அறஞ்செய் தருளுடையீர் ஆகுமின் யாரும்","பிறந்தும் பிறவாதா ரில்."],"p":"Death every day takes that fount of light the sun as a measure, metes out your days, and so devours.Do deeds of virtue full of kindly grace. Though all are born, none are exempt from death.","r":"Using the sun as a measure, Death daily measures out and eats your days, so do much virtue and be compassionate; the closing line is read in more than one way.","n":"The image makes each sunrise a scoop of grain taken from one's store of days. Pope reads the last line as 'none who are born escape death', which is a third reading beside the two commentators'.","x":1,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kootru","aram","arul","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[["mudaliar","Whoever fails to do this, though born human, is counted among the unborn, that is, with beasts.","அஃதாவது விலங்கொடு வைத்து எண்ணப்படுவர்."],["shanmuganar","Be compassionate like the great who have reached the state of no further birth.","பிறவா நிலை யெய்திய பெரியோர் போல அருள் உடையவராகுக!"]],"ch":"n:1"},"n:8":{"book":"naladiyar","num":8,"t":["செல்வர்யாம் என்றுதாம் செல்வுழி எண்ணாத","புல்லறி வாளர் பெருஞ்செல்வம் - எல்லில்","கருங்கொண்மூ வாய்திறந்த மின்னுப்போல் தோன்றி","மருங்கறக் கெட்டு விடும்."],"p":"The ample wealth of men of mean understanding, who say, ' We're rich,' yet ponder not their path and end, appears, and perishes, and leaves no trace; like the flash, when the black thunder-cloud by night opens its mouth.","r":"The great wealth of the petty-minded, who say 'we are rich' and never think of where they must go, appears like lightning from a dark cloud at night and vanishes without a trace.","n":"Both commentators take 'where they must go' as the next world, so the fault is not having wealth but forgetting the journey it cannot make.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","nilaiyaamai","pullarivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:1"},"n:9":{"book":"naladiyar","num":9,"t":["உண்ணான் ஒளிநிறான் ஓங்கு புகழ்செய்யான்","துன்னருங் கேளிர் துயர்களையான் - கொன்னே","வழங்கான் பொருள்காத் திருப்பானேல், அஆ","இழந்தான்என் றெண்ணப் படும்."],"p":"He eats not, sheds no light of splendour around, performs no deeds that merit lofty praise, soothes no sorrow that choice friends feel, spends nought, but hoards his wealth in vain : “Aha! he's lost it all,' shall men pronounce.","r":"One who neither eats, nor wins honour, nor does deeds of renown, nor relieves the distress of close kin, nor gives, but only guards his wealth in vain, will be thought of with pity as one who has lost it.","n":"The miser is pronounced to have lost his wealth while he still holds it: possession without use is already loss.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","eegai","pugazh","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:1"},"n:10":{"book":"naladiyar","num":10,"t":["உடாஅதும் உண்ணாதும் தம்உடம்பு செற்றும்","கெடாஅத நல்லறமும் செய்யார் - கொடாஅது","வைத்தீட்டி னார்இழப்பர், வான்தோய் மலைநாட","உய்த்தீட்டும் தேனீக் கா஢."],"p":"Those who stint in clothes and food, and mortify their bodies, yet do not deeds of deathless virtue, and bestow nothing, hoarding shall suffer loss:— Lord of the cloud-capped hills !--this the hoarding honey-bee attests. ---------","r":"Those who stint on clothing and food, wear down their bodies, do no lasting good and give nothing, will lose what they hoard, as the honey bee's store shows.","n":"Mudaliar spells out the comparison: as someone else takes the honey the bees gathered, thieves and others take the hoard. The verse names only the bee as witness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","eegai","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:1"},"n:11":{"book":"naladiyar","num":11,"t":["நரைவரும் என்றெண்ணி நல்லறி வாளர்","குழவி யிடத்தே துறந்தார் - புரைதீரா","மன்னா இளமை மகிழ்ந்தாரே கோல்ஊன்றி","இன்னாங் கெழுந்திருப் பார்."],"p":"\"Grey eld will come,’—the wise remembering this renounce the world even in tender age; but they who joy in youth, unstable, never free from fault, shall erewhile painfully rise up leaning on a staff.","r":"Knowing that grey hair will come, the wise renounced in childhood itself, while those who delighted in faulty, fleeting youth will later rise painfully leaning on a staff.","n":"குழவியிடத்தே ('in infancy') pushes renunciation as early as language allows; the verse treats the foresight of age, not age itself, as the mark of wisdom.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilamai","thuravu","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:2"},"n:12":{"book":"naladiyar","num":12,"t":["நட்புநார் அற்றன நல்லாரும் அஃகினார்","அற்புத் தளையும் அவிழ்ந்தன - உட்காணாய்","வாழ்தலின் ஊதியம் என்னுண்டாம்? வந்ததே","ஆழ்கலத் தன் ன கலி."],"p":"Served are the ties of friendship; minished are the pleasant ones; love's bonds are loosened too; then look within and say, what profit is there in this joyous life of thine ? The cry comes up as from a sinking ship!","r":"The cords of friendship are cut, those once close have drawn back, the bonds of love have come loose: look within and ask what profit there is in living on, for misery has come as on a sinking ship.","n":"The verse is spoken to an ageing person, perhaps the speaker himself (உட்காணாய், 'look within'). Pope's 'joyous life' is his addition; the Tamil asks only about the gain of living.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilamai","natpu","anbu","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[["mudaliar","நல்லார் are women, whose affection has waned.","நல்லாரும் - மாதர்களும்."],["shanmuganar","நல்லார் are the learned and good, who have lessened their closeness.","நல்லறிஞரும் நெருக்கம் குறைத்தனர்;"]],"ch":"n:2"},"n:13":{"book":"naladiyar","num":13,"t":["சொல்தளர்ந்து கோல்ஊன்றிச் சோர்ந்த நடையினராய்ப்","பல்கழன்று பண்டம் பழிகாறும் - இல்செறிந்து","காம நெறிபடரும் கண்ணினார்க்கு இல்லையே","ஏம நெறிபடரு மாறு."],"p":"Speech falters, they lean on a staff, and walk tottering, their teeth fall out; yet, till the vessel (the body) is scorned by all, they linger in the house, still indulging fond desires; to these no way of safety opens out.","r":"Those whose speech fails, who lean on a staff, walk unsteadily and lose their teeth, yet stay in the house with their eyes on desire until the body is scorned, have no way to the path of safety.","n":"Both commentators take the 'path of safety' as the way to release and இல்செறிந்து as clinging to household life, so the verse faults not old age but desire that outlives the body's capacity.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilamai","kaamam","ilvaazhkkai","thuravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:2"},"n:14":{"book":"naladiyar","num":14,"t":["தாழாத் தளராத் தலைநடுங்காத் தண்டுன்றா","வீழா இறக்கும் இவள்மாட்டும் - காழ்இலா","மம்மர்கொள் மாந்தர்க் கணங்காகும் தன்கைக்கோல்","அம்மனைக்கோல் ஆகிய ஞான்று."],"p":"To men that cherish weak desire for her that's doomed to droop and fail, supporting her palsied limbs with a staff, and then to fall and pass away, what anguish comes, when she grasps in her hands the staff her mother held!","r":"When a once-desired woman, bent, frail, head shaking and leaning on a staff, comes to hold the staff her mother held, she becomes a torment to men of unsteady mind who still desire her.","n":"அணங்கு is read by Mudaliar as an enchanting goddess and by Shanmuganar and Pope as pain; the line is hard, and the readings below differ on whose mother is meant.","x":1,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ilamai","kaamam","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[["mudaliar","அம்மனைக்கோல் is the staff of her own mother.","அம்மனைக் கோல் - அவள் தாயின் ஊன்றுகோல்."],["shanmuganar","Her staff looks to the men like the staff their own mother leaned on, and that pains them.","இவள் ஊன்றும் கைத்தடி, தம் தாய் ஊன்றிய தடிபோல் தெரியும்போது"]],"ch":"n:2"},"n:15":{"book":"naladiyar","num":15,"t":["எனக்குத்தாய் ஆகியாள் என்னைஈங் கிட்டுத்","தனக்குத்தாய் நாடியே சென்றாள் - தனக்குத்தாய்","ஆகி யவளும் அதுவானால் தாய்த்தாய்க்கொண்டு","ஏகும் அளித்திவ் வுலகு."],"p":"My mother bare me, left me here, and went to seek her mother, who in the selfsame manner has gone in search; and thus in ceaseless round goes on the mother-quest. Such is the grace this world affords !","r":"My mother left me here and went in search of her mother, and that mother did the same; this world goes on, mother seeking mother, a pitiable thing.","n":"Shanmuganar reads the 'seeking' as taking a new birth, so the chain is the cycle of births; Pope and Mudaliar keep it as each generation following the last into death. அளித்து is read as 'pitiable' by Mudaliar's gloss எளிமையுடைத்து; Pope renders it ironically as 'grace'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","piravi","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:2"},"n:16":{"book":"naladiyar","num":16,"t":["வெறியயர் வெங்களத்து வேல்மகன் பாணி","முறியார் நறங்கண்ணி முன்னர்த் தயங்க","மறிகுள குண்டன்ன மன்னா மகிழ்ச்சி","அறிவுடை யாளர்கண் இல்."],"p":"The lamb in the ruddy slaughter-house will crop the fragrant shoots that dangle from the garland in the slayer's hand; such transient gladness of the thoughtless, youthful hour is never found amid the wise.","r":"The fleeting joy of youth, like a sacrificial lamb nibbling the tender leaves of the garland held before it in the slaughter ground, is not found among the wise.","n":"The garland that feeds the lamb is the sign of its slaughter: youthful pleasure is enjoyed in the presence of what ends it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilamai","inbam","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:2"},"n:17":{"book":"naladiyar","num":17,"t":["பனிபடு சோலைப் பயன்மர மெல்லாம்","கனியுதிர்ந்து வீழ்ந்தற் றிளமை - நனிபெரிதும்","வேல்கண்ணள் என்றிவளை வெஃகன்மின் மற்றிவளும்","கோல்கண்ண ளாகும் குனிந்து."],"p":"The sweet fruit from every tree that bears in the dewy grove must fall to earth. Thus youth decays. Desire not her whose eyes gleam bright as darts. Full soon she too will walk bent down, with a staff to aid her dim sight.","r":"Youth falls as fruit drops from every tree in a cool grove; do not desire this woman for her spear-like eyes, for she too will stoop and walk with a staff for eyes.","n":"The wordplay turns வேல்கண்ணள் (spear-eyed) into கோல்கண்ணள் (staff-eyed): the same compound form names her beauty and her decline.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilamai","kaamam","nalam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:2"},"n:18":{"book":"naladiyar","num":18,"t":["பருவம் எனைத்துள பல்லின்பால் ஏனை","இருசிகையும் உண்டீரோ என்று - வரிசையால்","உண்ணாட்டம் கொள்ளப் படுதலால் யாக்கைக்கோள்","எண்ணார் அறிவுடை யார்."],"p":"’How old are you ?' 'How last your teeth ?' and, ‘Do you eat two courses yet?' men ask with kindly courtesy. By such close questions urged, the wise will learn to judge the body as a thing of nought.","r":"Because others ask in turn, 'How old are you? How are your teeth? Do you still eat twice?', and so examine one's body, the wise do not count on the body's vigour.","n":"The questions are polite and routine; the verse's point is that such solicitude already treats one as declining. Mudaliar records two readings of இருசிகை, a second mouthful or two meals a day.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","ilamai","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:2"},"n:19":{"book":"naladiyar","num":19,"t":["மற்றறிவாம் நல்வினை யாம்இளையம் என்னாது","கைத்துண்டாம் போழ்தே கரவா - தறம்செய்ம்மின்","முற்றி யிருந்த கனியொழியத் தீவளியால்","நற்காய் உதிர்தலும் உண்டு."],"p":"Say not, 'in after time we'll learn virtue, we're young;' but while wealth is yours conceal it not; do virtuous deeds. When evil tempests rage, not the ripe fruit alone, but the unripe fruit's fair promise also falls.","r":"Do not say 'we are young, we will learn good deeds later'; while wealth is in hand, do virtue without hiding it, for a harsh wind can drop good unripe fruit while the ripe stays on the tree.","n":"Mudaliar notes the alternative reading of கனி ஒழிய as 'the ripe fruit remaining on the tree', which sharpens the point: death may take the young and leave the old.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilamai","aram","kootru"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:2"},"n:20":{"book":"naladiyar","num":20,"t":["ஆட்பார்த் துழலும் அருளில்கூற் றுண்மையால்","தோட்கோப்புக் காலத்தால் கொண்டுய்ம்மின் - பீட்பிதுக்கிப்","பிள்ளையைத் தாய்அலறக் கோடலான் மற்றதன்","கள்ளம் கடைப்பிடித்தல் நன்று."],"p":"Relentless death is roaming round, and eyes his man! 'Tis true. Take up your wallet, scape betimes. He bears away the new-born babe, while the mother sorely laments. It is good to bear in mind his guile. --------","r":"Since merciless Death roams looking for his people, take provisions for the journey in good time; it is well to keep his stealth in mind, for he takes even the unborn child while its mother wails.","n":"Mudaliar reads தோட்கோப்பு, a bundle carried on the shoulder, as the good deeds one needs for the next world; Pope renders it 'wallet'. The verse leaves the provisions as a figure.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","aram","ilamai"],"pos":[["mudaliar","The shoulder-bundle is the stock of good deeds needed for the next world.","தோள் கோட்பு-தோளிற் கோக்கப்படுவது அஃதாவது கட்டுச்சோற்று மூட்டை; இங்கு மறுமைக்கும் வேண்டும். நல்வினைக்கு ஆயிற் றென்க."]],"ch":"n:2"},"n:21":{"book":"naladiyar","num":21,"t":["மலைமிசைத் தோன்றும் மதியம்போல் யானைத்","தலைமிசைக் கொண்ட குடையர் - நிலமிசைத்","துஞ்சினார் என்றெடுத்துத் தூற்றப்பட் டாரல்லால்","எஞ்சினார் இவ்வுலகத் தில்."],"p":"Even kings that rode on elephants beneath the state umbrella's shade, like the moon appearing over some bill, have had their names proclaimed on earth as dead ;— not any in this world have escaped. Time is fleeting,—use it As the measure of your days the shining orb each day unfailing rises; so before your joyous days have passed away, perform ye 'fitting deeds of grace'; for none abide on earth.","r":"Even kings who rode on elephants under umbrellas like the moon rising over a hill have been carried off and spoken of as dead; no one in this world has been left over.","n":"Mudaliar takes தூற்றப்பட்டார் as 'were disparaged by many', while Pope has 'had their names proclaimed' and Shanmuganar 'spoken of lightly'; whether the dead kings are mocked or merely announced is left open by the verb.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","nilaiyaamai","arasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:3"},"n:22":{"book":"naladiyar","num":22,"t":["வாழ்நாட் கலகா வயங்கொளி மண்டிலம்","வீழ்நாள் படாஅ தெழுதலால் - வாழ்நாள்","உலவாமுன் ஒப்புர வாற்றுமின் யாரும்","நிலவார் நிலமிசை மேல்."],"p":"","r":"Because the bright sun, as the measure of one's days, rises without missing a day, do ஒப்புரவு before your days run out; no one stays on the earth beyond them.","n":"ஒப்புரவு is the Kural's word for serving the community with one's means; Mudaliar cites Parimelazhagar's definition. He also says some read the last line in strained ways, without giving them. Pope's translation of this quatrain is attached to 21 in the source.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","oppuravu","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:3"},"n:23":{"book":"naladiyar","num":23,"t":["மன்றம் கறங்க மணப்பறை யாயின","அன்றவர்க் காங்கே பிணப்பறையாய்ப் - பின்றை","ஒலித்தலும் உண்டாமென்று உய்ந்துபோம் ஆறே","வலிக்குமாம் மாண்டார் மனம்."],"p":"The marriage drums that sounded out in the festive hall, there and that very day have served for him as funeral drums! Men of lofty minds will note that thus it haps, and will strive to gain the way to escape.","r":"The drums that sounded through the hall as wedding drums may sound that same day, in that same place, as funeral drums for the bridegroom; seeing this, the minds of the worthy hold fast to the way of escape.","n":"The same instruments serve both rites; the verse makes the reversal one of meaning, not of sound. Shanmuganar names the way of escape as virtue.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","yaakkai","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:3"},"n:24":{"book":"naladiyar","num":24,"t":["சென்றே எறிப ஒருகால் சிறுவரை","நின்றே எறிப பறையினை - நன்றேகாண்","முக்காலைக் கொட்டினுள் மூடித்தீக் கொண்டுஎழுவர்","செத்தாரைச் சாவார் சுமந்து."],"p":"They march and then strike once! A little while they wait, then strike the drum a second time. Behold, how fine! The third stroke sounds. They veil it, take the fire, and go forth :—the dying bear the deed!","r":"The drummers beat once on arrival, wait a little and beat again, and at the third beat the corpse is covered and the fire taken up; the dying carry the dead.","n":"நன்றேகாண் ('fine, see!') is sarcasm for Mudaliar and a question, 'is such a life good?', for Shanmuganar; either way the last line carries the point, that the bearers are already on the same road.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","nilaiyaamai","kootru"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:3"},"n:25":{"book":"naladiyar","num":25,"t":["கணம்கொண்டு சுற்றத்தார் கல்லென் றலறப்","பிணம்கொண்டு காட்டுய்ப்பார்க் கண்டும் - மணம் கொண்டீண்டு","உண்டுண்டுண் டென்னும் உணர்வினால் சாற்றுமே","டொண்டொண்டொ டென்னும் பறை."],"p":"To him, who, although he sees them bear the corpse to the burning ground, while friends in troops loudly lament, boldly asserts that wedded life is bliss on earth, the funeral drum speaks out, and mocks his vain utterance.","r":"To the one who sees kin wailing in crowds as a corpse is carried to the burning ground, yet marries and believes there is joy here, the funeral drum answers with its டொண் டொண் டொண்.","n":"The poem sets the man's 'உண்டு உண்டு உண்டு' (there is, there is) against the drum's 'டொண் டொண் டொண்'; Mudaliar calls the drum's reply a poetic fancy. Pope's 'mocks his vain utterance' paraphrases that echo.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","inbam","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:3"},"n:26":{"book":"naladiyar","num":26,"t":["நார்த்தொடுத் தீர்க்கிலென் நன்றாய்ந் தடக்கிலென்","பார்த்துழிப் பெய்யிலென் பல்லோர் பழிக்கிலென்","தோற்பையுள் நின்று தொழிலறச் செய்தூடும்","கூத்தன் புறப்பட்டக் கால்."],"p":"When the 'soul', that, taking its stand in this skin clad frame, has fully wrought its works, and partaken of life's experiences, has gone forth, what matters it whether you attach ropes to the body and drag it away, or carefully bury it, or throw it aside in any place you light upon, or if many revile the departed ?","r":"Once the dancer who stayed in this skin bag and did its work has left, what does it matter whether the body is dragged off with a rope, buried with care, thrown anywhere or reviled by many?","n":"The life is a performer and the body a costume; once it has gone, the treatment of the remains has no meaning. Mudaliar lists several readings of ஊட்டும், among them the life enjoying, or being made to enjoy, the fruit of its deeds.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","uyir","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:3"},"n:27":{"book":"naladiyar","num":27,"t":["படுமழை மொக்குளின் பல்காலும் தோன்றிக்","கெடுமிதோர் யாக்கையென் றெண்ணித் - தடுமாற்றம்","தீர்ப்பேம்யாம் என்றுணரும் திண்ணறி வாளரை","நேர்ப்பார்யார் நீணிலத்தின் மேல்."],"p":"'Like a bubble, that in pelting rain appears full oft, and disappears, is this our frame.' So sages have judged, steadfast in wisdom, and have decided to end this dubious strife. On this wide earth who equal these?","r":"Who on this wide earth equals those firm in understanding who see this body as a bubble in pelting rain that forms and bursts again and again, and resolve to end their wandering?","n":"'Again and again' (பல்காலும்) suggests repeated births as well as the bubble's quickness, and Mudaliar takes தடுமாற்றம் as the confusion of births. Shanmuganar reads it as worldly confusion.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","nilaiyaamai","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:3"},"n:28":{"book":"naladiyar","num":28,"t":["யாக்கையை யாப்புடைத்தாப் பெற்றவர் தாம்பெற்ற","யாக்கையா லாய பயன்கொள்க - யாக்கை","மலையாடு மஞ்சுபோல் தோன்றிமற் றாங்கே","நிலையாது நீத்து விடும்."],"p":"Those who've gained and held fast by this well-knit frame (a human body) should take the gain the body they have gained is intended to yield. Like a cloud that wanders over the hills, the body here appears, and abiding not, departs leaving no trace behind.","r":"Those who have a sturdy body should take the fruit that body can yield, for it appears like mist on a hill and departs there without staying.","n":"The verse does not despise the body here; it treats it as a means whose use must be taken quickly. Shanmuganar names the fruit as virtue.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","payan","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:3"},"n:29":{"book":"naladiyar","num":29,"t":["புல்நுனிமேல் நீர்போல் நிலையாமை என்றெண்ணி","இன்னினியே செய்க அறவினை - இன்னினியே","நின்றான் இருந்தான் கிடந்தான்தன் கேள்அலறச்","சென்றான் எனப்படுத லால்."],"p":"Considering that all things are transient as the dew-drop on the tip of a blade of grass, now, now at once, do virtuous deeds! \"Even now he stood, he sat, he fell—while his kindred cried aloud he died :' such is man's history!","r":"Knowing that the body lasts like water on the tip of a blade of grass, do virtuous deeds right now, for people say 'he stood, he sat, he lay down, and with his kin wailing he was gone'.","n":"The four verbs compress a death into one breath of speech, the way it will be reported; Mudaliar supplies 'the body' as the subject of நிலையாமை.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","aram","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:3"},"n:30":{"book":"naladiyar","num":30,"t":["கேளாதே வந்து கிளைகளாய் இல்தோன்றி","வாளாதே போவரால் மாந்தர்கள் - வாளாதே","சேக்கை மரன்ஒழியச் சேண்நீங்கு புள்போல","யாக்கை தமர்க்கொழிய நீத்து."],"p":"Unasked men come, appear in the home as kinsmen, and then silently go. As the bird silently deserts the tree where its nest yet remains, and goes far off, so these leave but their body to their friends. -----------","r":"Unasked they come and appear in the home as kin, and wordlessly they go, leaving the body to their own people as a bird flies far off leaving its nesting tree.","n":"The double வாளாதே (silently) frames a life with two unannounced events, arrival and departure; family is a place of passage.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","sutram","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:3"},"n:31":{"book":"naladiyar","num":31,"t":["அகத்தாரே வாழ்வார்என் றண்ணாந்து நோக்கிப்","புகத்தாம் பெறாஅர் பறங்கடை பற்றி","மிகத்தாம் வருந்தி இருப்பரே மேலைத்","தவத்தால் தவம்செய்யா தார்."],"p":"‘Yet those within are blest,' so saying, they look up, but obtain no entrance: their place is at the outer gate. There will they suffer much, who thro' lack of former penitence do no penance now. [In a former state penance won for them a human shape. As men they have now failed.]","r":"Those who, on the strength of earlier austerity, do no austerity now will look up at a house and say 'those inside are the ones who live', unable to enter, clinging to the outer door and grieving.","n":"The quatrain implies merit is a stock that is used up: present comfort is paid for by past austerity, and without renewal the next state is the beggar's at the gate. Pope's main text reads the cause as a lack of former penance, but his bracketed note agrees with Shanmuganar that past penance won their present state.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","vinai","iravu"],"pos":[["shanmuganar","Those who enjoyed the fruit of past virtue and do none in this birth will stand outside in their next birth.","முற்பிறப்பில் செய்த அறத்தால் (தவத்தால்) இன்பம் துய்த்து இப்பிறப்பில் அறம் செய்யாதவர்கள்,"]],"ch":"n:4"},"n:32":{"book":"naladiyar","num":32,"t":["ஆவாம்நாம் ஆக்கம் நசைஇ அறமறந்து","போவாம்நாம் என்னாப் புலைநெஞ்சே - ஓவாது","நின்றுஞற்றி வாழ்தி எனினும்நின் வாழ்நாள்கள்","சென்றன செய்வ துரை."],"p":"Say not, O silly soul, we will live desiring wealth and die forgetting virtue! We'll say that ceaselessly toiling thou shalt live long; but tell me, what wilt thou do when all thy happy days are over?","r":"Base heart, which says 'let us prosper, desiring wealth and forgetting virtue', even if you live on toiling without rest, your days are gone: tell me what you will do.","n":"The address is to one's own heart (புலைநெஞ்சே), and the closing question is left unanswered; the speaker is arguing with himself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","selvam","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:4"},"n:33":{"book":"naladiyar","num":33,"t":["வினைப்பயன் வந்தக்கால் வெய்ய உயிரா","மனத்தின் அழியுமாம் பேதை - நினைத்ததனைத்","தொல்லைய தென்றுணர் வாரே தடுமாற்றத்து","எல்லை இகந்தொருவு வார்."],"p":"When the ‘fruit of deeds' is come, the fool sighs heavily, and all his soul dies out; but those who reflect and say, ,'Tis old desert,' will pass beyond the bound of life's perplexity, and escape (by devoting themselves to virtue).","r":"When the fruit of past deeds arrives, the fool sighs hotly and his mind breaks; those who think of what comes as old desert pass beyond the bounds of confusion and are freed.","n":"Accepting suffering as the ripening of one's own past deeds is presented as the way out of the round itself, not only as consolation. Pope's 'by devoting themselves to virtue' is his addition.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","samanilai","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:4"},"n:34":{"book":"naladiyar","num":34,"t":["அரும்பெறல் யாக்கையைப் பெற்ற பயத்தால்","பெரும்பயனும் ஆற்றவே கொள்க - கரும்பூர்ந்த","சாறுபோல் சாலவும் பின்உதவி மற்றதன்","கோதுபோல் போகும் உடம்பு."],"p":"As the gain from the mortal frame now reached —and which is so hard to reach —with all thy might lay hold of virtue's lasting good. As the juice expressed from the sugar-cane 'twill afterwards be thine aid, when the body goes like refuse flung away.","r":"With the gain of having obtained this rare body, take its great fruit fully; like juice pressed from cane, it serves you afterwards, and the body goes like the crushed refuse.","n":"The body is valued only as the cane is: for what can be pressed out of it. The juice, virtue, outlasts the body and is what helps 'afterwards'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","aram","payan"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:4"},"n:35":{"book":"naladiyar","num":35,"t":["கரும்பாட்டிக் கட்டி சிறுகாலைக் கொண்டார்","துரும்பெழுந்து வேம்கால் துயராண் டுழவார்","வருந்தி உடம்பின் பயன்கொண்டார் கூற்றம்","வருங்கால் பரிவ திலர்."],"p":"Those who have pressed the sugar-cane, and early taken the juice, when the refuse heaped up burns, will suffer no grief: those who have toiled and gained the fruit won from embodied existence will feel no pangs when death shall come.","r":"As those who crushed their cane and took the jaggery early do not grieve when the refuse catches fire and burns, those who laboured to take the body's fruit feel no distress when Death comes.","n":"சிறுகாலை (early) carries the argument: the jaggery was made in time. The burning of refuse quietly suggests cremation.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","kootru","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:4"},"n:36":{"book":"naladiyar","num":36,"t":["இன்றுகொல் அன்றுகொல் என்றுகொல் என்னாது","பின்றையே நின்றது கூற்றமென் றெண்ணி","ஒருவுமின் தீயவை ஒல்லும் வகையால்","மருவுமின் மாண்டார் அறம்."],"p":"‘This day ?’ That day? What day? O question not the time! Bethink you death stands behind you ever waiting! Put from you every evil thing; and with all your powers embrace the virtue which sages teach.","r":"Without asking whether it is today, that day or some other day, think that Death stands right behind you; leave evil as far as you can and hold to the virtue the worthy practise.","n":"ஒல்லும் வகையால் (as far as you can) sets a measure within reach, unlike the absolute demands of renunciation elsewhere in these chapters.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","aram","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:4"},"n:37":{"book":"naladiyar","num":37,"t":["மக்களா லாய பொரும்பயனும் ஆயுங்கால்","எத்துணையும் ஆற்றப் பலவானால் - தொக்க","உடம்பிற்கே ஒப்புராவு செய்தொழுகா தும்பர்க்","கிடந்துண்ணப் பண்ணப் படும்."],"p":"When you examine closely the mighty gains to be acquired by birth in a human shape, if they seem manifold, perform not deeds which suit the body's frame alone, but deeds whose fruit is joy in the world to come!","r":"If the gains that come through human birth are many when examined, one should not live doing service only to the gathered-up body, but should act so as to enjoy them in the world above.","n":"The verse applies ஒப்புரவு, normally service to the community, to the body itself, ironically: serving the body is a misplaced kind of duty.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","aram","oppuravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:4"},"n:38":{"book":"naladiyar","num":38,"t":["உறக்கும் துணையதோர் ஆலம்வித் தீண்டி","இறப்ப நிழற்பயந் தாஅங்கு - அறப்பயனும்","தான்சிறி தாயினும் தக்கார்கைப் பட்டக்கால்","வான்சிறிதாப் போர்த்து விடும்."],"p":"The banyan seed, though it be minute as one might see in dreams, grows to a mighty tree of amplest shade; so gifts from a virtuous hand, received by a worthy hand, though small, will hide the diminish'd heavens.","r":"As a banyan seed of the smallest size grows and casts a vast shade, so the fruit of virtue, though small, when it reaches the hands of the worthy, spreads so that the sky seems small.","n":"The growth depends on the recipient: தக்கார் கைப்பட்டக்கால் (if it falls into worthy hands). Pope reads உறக்கும் துணைய as 'minute as one might see in dreams'; Shanmuganar renders the size differently, but both make the seed tiny.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","eegai","payan"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:4"},"n:39":{"book":"naladiyar","num":39,"t":["வைகலும் வைகல் வரக்கண்டும் அஃதுணரார்","வைகலும் வைகலை வைகுமென் றின்புறுவர்","வைகலும் வைகல்தம் வாழ்நாள்மேல் வைகுதல்","வைகலை வைத்துணரா தார்."],"p":"Daily they see the passing day added to the sum of the days gone by, as a day that is spent from out the store of their days, yet daily, as they see day dawn, they say joyously, 'This day will abide with us till the close of day?’","r":"Seeing day follow day, they do not grasp it, and rejoice each day that the day will pass well; they do not count each passing day as a day taken from their own lives.","n":"The quatrain plays வைகல் (day; passing) through eight forms, so the sound of repetition becomes the experience it describes. Mudaliar reports another reading of வைகுதல் as 'staying', and supplies the unstated conclusion: the wise would act on virtue at once.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:4"},"n:40":{"book":"naladiyar","num":40,"t":["மான அருங்கலம் நீக்கி இரவென்னும்","ஈன இளிவினால் வாழ்வேன்மன் - ஈனத்தால்","ஊட்டியக் கண்ணும் உறுதிசேர்ந் திவ்வுடம்பு","நீட்டித்து நிற்கும் எனின்."],"p":"Parting with honour's jewel I might still consent to live a suppliant's life of shame, if when maintained by such disgrace, this body could abide in strength and last for length of days. --------------","r":"I would put off the rare jewel of honour and live by the shame of begging, if a body fed by such disgrace would gain strength and last.","n":"The conditional is counterfactual: the body will not last, so honour is never worth selling for it. Mudaliar notes the particle மன் signals exactly this unfulfilled 'I would'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam","iravu","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:4"},"n:41":{"book":"naladiyar","num":41,"t":["மாக்கேழ் மடநல்லாய் என்றரற்றும் சான்றவர்","நோக்கார்கொல் நொய்யதோர் புக்கிலை-யாக்கைக்கோர்","ஈச்சிற கன்னதோர் தோல்அறினும் வேண்டுமே","காக்கை கடிவதோர் கோல்."],"p":"‘O gentle maiden, fair and good !' These paragons that thus rave, know they not 'the heavenly home invisible?’ Let a bit of skin be broken slight as an insect's wing, and you need the stick that drives a way the crows !","r":"The 'worthies' who cry 'O fair, lovely girl': do they not see the body's paltry dwelling? If skin the size of a fly's wing is torn from it, a stick is needed to drive away the crows.","n":"Both commentators take புக்கில் as the body, a temporary lodging of the life; Pope renders it 'the heavenly home invisible'. Mudaliar notes that சான்றவர் (worthies) is used ironically for the petty-minded. The crows show how little stands between the admired body and carrion.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","kaamam","pullarivu"],"pos":[["mudaliar","The புக்கில் is the body itself, a lodging where the life stays for a while.","துச்சிலை-(உயிரானது ஒதுக்குக் குடியிருக்கும்) வீடாகிய உடம்பின் தன்மையை."],["shanmuganar","The admirers do not know that the body is a paltry shelter.","உடம்பு அற்பமான ஓர் ஒதுக்கிடம் என்பதை அறியார் போலும் !"]],"ch":"n:5"},"n:42":{"book":"naladiyar","num":42,"t":["தோல்போர்வை மேலும் தொளைபலவாய்ப் பொய்ம்மறைக்கும்","மீப்போர்வை மாட்சித் துடம்பானால் - மீப்போர்வை","பொய்ம்மறையாக் காமம் புகலாது மற்றதனைப்","பைம்மறியாப் பார்க்கப் படும்."],"p":"If the body which, with a covering of skin above, possesses many apertures, owes its beauty only to that outward cloak that veils the false (the inner foulness), then is it fitting to say no word of lustful desire which hides the false from itself by the covering veil, but to regard that body as an undeveloped embryo.","r":"If the body, full of openings beneath its skin, is handsome only through an outer covering that hides its falsity, then without speaking desire one should look at it as a bag turned inside out.","n":"The commentators agree on the bag turned inside out; Pope instead renders பைம்மறி as 'an undeveloped embryo'. The verse asks for a change of viewpoint, not a change in the body.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","kaamam"],"pos":[["mudaliar","Look at the body as one looks at a bag turned so that the inside faces out.","பை மறியா-பையைப் போல,(உட்பக்கம் வெளிப் பக்கமாம்படி) மாற்றியதாக."],["shanmuganar","Turn the body inside out like a bag and its truth becomes plain.","பையைத் திருப்பிப் பார்ப்பதுபோல் அவ்வுடலைத் திருப்பிப் பார்த்தால் உண்மை விளங்கும்."]],"ch":"n:5"},"n:43":{"book":"naladiyar","num":43,"t":["தக்கோலம் தின்று தலைநிறையப் பூச் சூடி","பொய்க்கோலம் செய்ய ஒழியுமே - எக்காலும்","உண்டி வினையுள் உறைக்கும் எனப்பெரியோர்","கண்டுகை விட்ட மயல்."],"p":"Will impurity ever cease from the worthless body which the great have abandoned, knowing it to be reeking with odours from processes connected with nutrition, though aromatics be chewed, the head covered with garlands, and the body adornedwith false splendour?","r":"Will chewing fragrant spices, filling the head with flowers and putting on a false finery remove the foulness the great saw and abandoned, which is always strengthened by the work of eating?","n":"Mudaliar takes மயல் as the impurities of a woman's body; Shanmuganar applies it to the body in general. The verse says only that the great saw it and gave it up.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","undi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:5"},"n:44":{"book":"naladiyar","num":44,"t":["தெண்ணீர்க் குவளை பொருகயல் வேலென்று","கண்ணில்புன் மாக்கள் கவற்ற விடுவேனோ","உண்ணீர் களைந்தக்கால் நுங்குசூன் றிட்டன்ன","கண்ணீர்மை கண்டொழுகு வேன்."],"p":"Shall I abandon (my ascetic purpose) because blind, low men worry me, saying (that woman's eye is like) the water-lily in the clear stream, or the warring carp, or a javelin? I will pursue my virtuous way as having seen (that) the eye's real nature (is), like (that of) the palm-tree fruit (which is) scooped out, after the water has been drained off!","r":"Shall I give way when blind and petty men trouble me by calling eyes water-lilies in clear water, darting carp or spears? I will go on my way having seen what an eye is: like a palm fruit scooped out once its water is drained.","n":"The speaker is a renunciant guarding his resolve; the verse is personal resistance rather than general teaching. கண்ணில் (eyeless) turns the praisers' subject back on them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","yaakkai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:5"},"n:45":{"book":"naladiyar","num":45,"t":["முல்லை முகைமுறுவல் முத்தென் றிவைபிதற்றும்","கல்லாப்புன் மாக்கள் கவற்ற விடுவெனோ","எல்லாரும் காணப் புறங்காட் டுதிர்ந்துக்க","பல்லென்பு கண்டொழுகு வேன3"],"p":"Though worthless men untaught should fret my soul and rave of teeth like jasmine buds and pearls, shall I forego my fixed resolve, who have seen in the burning ground those bones—the fallen teeth —strewn round for all to see ?","r":"Shall I give way when untaught petty men chatter that smiles are jasmine buds and pearls? I will go on my way having seen the teeth and bones fallen and scattered in the burning ground for all to see.","n":"A companion to 44 with the same frame; the evidence here is not anatomy but the cremation ground, so impurity and impermanence meet.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","kaamam","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:5"},"n:46":{"book":"naladiyar","num":46,"t":["குடரும் கொழுவும் குருதியும் என்பும்","தொடரும் நரம்பொடு தோலும் - இடையிடையே","வைத்த தடியும் வழும்புமாம் மற்றிவற்றுள்","எத்திறத்தாள் ஈர்ங்கோதை யாள்."],"p":"(The body) is entrails, and marrow, and blood, and bone, and connecting tendons, and skin, and here and there flesh interposed, and fat. In the midst of these, what sort of a being is she who wears the fresh garlands?","r":"Entrails, fat, blood, bone, linking sinews, skin, and flesh and grease set here and there: among these, which part is the woman with the fresh garland?","n":"The question is a search for the person among the parts, and implies she is none of them; the verse uses the list, not argument, to dissolve the beloved.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:5"},"n:47":{"book":"naladiyar","num":47,"t":["ஊறி உவர்த்தக்க ஒன்பது வாய்ப்புலனும்","கோதிக் குழம்பலைக்கும் கும்பத்தைப் - பேதை","பெருந்தோளி பெய்வளாய் என்னுமீப் போர்த்த","கருந்தோலால் கண்விளக்கப் பட்டு."],"p":"The fool will address the earthen pot (the body), from which defilement oozes, which from nine disgusting outlets scatters pollution, and in which slimy liquids move to and fro—and say, 'O thou of the rounded arms,' ‘O thou with armlets decked, ‘—because it is made bright to his eyes by a covering of black skin.","r":"The fool calls out 'broad-shouldered one, bangle-wearer' to a pot that seeps filth from nine openings and churns with slime, because its covering of dark skin dazzles his eyes.","n":"The terms of address the fool uses are the stock epithets of love poetry, so the verse aims at a literary convention as well as at desire.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","kaamam","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:5"},"n:48":{"book":"naladiyar","num":48,"t":["பண்டம் அறியார் படுசாந்தும் கோதையும்","கண்டுபா ராட்டுவார் கண்டிலர்கொல் - மண்டிப்","பெடைச்சேவல் வன்கழுகு பேர்த்திட்டுக் குத்தும்","முடைச்சாகா டச்சிற் றுழி."],"p":"They know not what the body is; with sandal paste and flowers they make it fine. Have they not seen, I pray, the vultures and their mates in flocks with busy beaks devour the body foul when the chariot-axle is snapt?","r":"Those who praise sandal paste and garlands without knowing what the body is: have they not seen vultures, male and female, crowd in and tear at the stinking cart once its axle breaks?","n":"Mudaliar reads the axle as the life and the cart as the body. The cart image recurs from 2, where wealth turned like a wheel.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","kaamam","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:5"},"n:49":{"book":"naladiyar","num":49,"t":["கழிந்தார் இடுதலை கண்டார்நெஞ் சுட்கக்","குழிந்தாழ்ந்த கண்ணவாய்த் தோன்றி - ஒழிந்தாரைப்","போற்றி நெறிநின்மின் இற்றிதன் பண்பென்று","சாற்றுங்கொல் சாலச் சிரித்து."],"p":"The skulls of the dead, at the sight of which the gazer fears, with deep cavernous eyes appear, and grinning say to those who still survive, 'Guard well! In virtue's path stand fast. This is the body's grace and worth.''","r":"The skulls of the dead lying about, with deep sunken eye-holes that frighten those who see them, seem to laugh loudly and tell the living 'guard yourselves and keep to the path: this is what the body comes to'.","n":"The speech is a poetic fancy, as Mudaliar notes (தற்குறிப்பேற்றம்). He names the path as renunciation; Shanmuganar names it as virtue.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","nilaiyaamai","thuravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:5"},"n:50":{"book":"naladiyar","num":50,"t":["உயிர்போயார் வெண்டலை உட்கச் சிரித்துச்","செயிர்தீர்க்கும் செம்மாப் பவரைச் - செயிர்தீர்ந்தார்","கண்டிற் றிதன்வண்ண மென்பதனால் தம்மையோர்","பண்டத்துள் வைப்ப திலர்."],"p":"The skulls of the dead, grinning so as to excite disgust, cure the vain lovers of life of their folly. Those who are cured of this folly, seeing (the skulls in the burning ground), say “such is this body,' and so value themselves as nothing. --------------","r":"The white skulls of the dead, grinning so as to frighten, cure the infatuated of their fault; those so cured, seeing that this is the body's nature, do not count themselves as anything of worth.","n":"Mudaliar takes the infatuated as men besotted with women and 'themselves' as their bodies; Shanmuganar takes it as the proud, cured of arrogance. The verse's own words allow both.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","kaamam","panivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:5"},"n:51":{"book":"naladiyar","num":51,"t":["விளக்குப் புகஇருள் மாய்ந்தாங்கு ஒருவன்","தவத்தின்முன் நில்லாதாம் பாவம் - விளக்குநெய்","தேய்விடத்துச் சென்றிருள் பாய்ந்தாங்கு நல்வினை","தீர்விடத்து நிற்குமாம் தீது."],"p":"As when a lamp enters darkness dies, so sin stands not before man's penitence. As, when in a lamp the oil wastes, darkness rushes in, so evil takes its stand where deeds of virtue cease.","r":"As darkness dies when a lamp comes in, sin cannot stand before a person's austerity; and as darkness rushes back when the lamp's oil runs out, evil settles in once good deeds are exhausted.","n":"The second half limits the first: austerity protects only while the merit that feeds it lasts, so the lamp makes merit something burned up, not something owned.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","vinai"],"pos":[["mudaliar","Austerity here stands for the whole way of renunciation.","தவம் என்றது ஈண்டுத் துறவறத்தின் மேனின்றது."]],"ch":"n:6"},"n:52":{"book":"naladiyar","num":52,"t":["நிலையாமை நோய்மூப்புச் சாக்காடென் றெண்ணித்","தலையாயார் தங்கருமம் செய்வார் - தொலைவில்லாச்","சத்தமும் சோதிடமும் என்றாங் கிவைபிதற்றும்","பித்தரின் பேதையார் இல்."],"p":"The chief of men reflect on change, disease, old age, and death, and do their needful work. Those who raving teach the endless round of sevenfold science, and the lore of stars, are maddest of the foolish throng.","r":"The best of people reflect on impermanence, disease, old age and death and do the work that is truly theirs; none are more foolish than the madmen who prattle endlessly of சத்தம் and astrology.","n":"The target is not learning as such but study that never ends, pursued in place of what death makes urgent. 'Their own work' (தங்கருமம்) echoes Kural 266, which Mudaliar quotes.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","thavam","kalvi","pedhaimai"],"pos":[["mudaliar","'Their own work' is austerity, and the endless lore is grammar and mathematics or astrology, tool-sciences studied for a lifetime instead of the way of renunciation.","தங் கருமம்-தமக்கு உறுதியைச் செய்யுந் தொழிலாகிய தவத்தையே"],["shanmuganar","The wise do the good deeds fitting for them, and the endless lore is Vedic sastra and astrology.","மேலோர் தமக்கேற்ற நல்வினைகளைச் செய்வர். எனவே, வேத சாத்திரம், சோதிடம் என்றெல்லாம் பிதற்றுகிற"]],"ch":"n:6"},"n:53":{"book":"naladiyar","num":53,"t":["இல்லம் இளமை எழில்வனப்பு மீக்கூற்றம்","செல்வம் வலிஎன் றிவையெல்லாம் - மெல்ல","நிலையாமை கண்டு நெடியார் துறப்பர்","தலையாயார் தாம்உய்யக் கொண்டு."],"p":"The chief of men in quite thought discern how house, and youth, and beauty's grace, and high estate, and wealth, and strength, all pass away; and thus, to save themselves, prolonging not the time, renounce all these.","r":"Seeing that home, youth, beauty, high repute, wealth and strength all fail to last, the best of people, considering slowly, renounce them without delay so as to save themselves.","n":"மெல்ல and நெடியார் pull two ways: the examination is unhurried, the renouncing is not. Mudaliar keeps both, glossing one as deliberate inquiry and the other as acting without delay.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","nilaiyaamai","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:6"},"n:54":{"book":"naladiyar","num":54,"t":["துன்பம் பலநாள் உழந்தும் ஒருநாளை","இன்பமே காமுறுவர் ஏழையார் - இன்பம்","இடைதெரிந் தின்னாமை நோக்கி மனையாறு","அடைவொழிந்தார் ஆன்றமைந் தார்."],"p":"Though wretched men suffer afflictions many a day, yet one day's delight they eagerly desire. The men of calm and full wisdom, in pleasure's core see pain, and quit the pleasant household paths.","r":"Fools, after many days of suffering, still long for a single day's pleasure; the full and settled, discerning pleasure for what it is and seeing its pain, keep off the household way.","n":"The verse names household life (மனையாறு) as what the wise leave, which makes it a direct answer to the Kural's defence of the householder.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","inbam","ilvaazhkkai","idumbai"],"pos":[["mudaliar","The wise weigh, impartially, how small household pleasure is.","இன்பத்தின் சிறுமையை நடுவுநின்று ஆராய்ந்து அறிந்து"],["shanmuganar","The wise see that pleasure comes only now and then, a little at a time, and pain is the greater part.","இன்பம் இடையிடையே சிறிதளவு வருவதறிந்தும்"]],"ch":"n:6"},"n:55":{"book":"naladiyar","num":55,"t":["கொன்னே கழிந்தன் றிளமையும் இன்னே","பிணியொடு மூப்பும் வருமால் - துணிவொன்றி","என்னொடு சூழா தெழுநெஞ்சே போதியோ","நன்னெறி சேர நமக்கு."],"p":"In vain is my youth spent. Even now disease and old age will come. O soul ! be bold; wrangle no more with me, but rise! Wilt thou not go where both thou and I may gain virtue's path?","r":"The speaker tells his own heart that youth has passed to no purpose and disease and old age are coming now, and asks it to stop going its own way and set out with him on the good path.","n":"The heart is treated as a separate, wayward party to be persuaded, not commanded. Mudaliar takes the good path to be release through renunciation; Shanmuganar says only 'a good way'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","manam","thuravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:6"},"n:56":{"book":"naladiyar","num":56,"t":["மாண்ட குணத்தொடு மக்கட்பே றில்லெனினும்","பூண்டான் கழித்தற் கருமையால் - பூண்ட","மிடியென்னும் காரணத்தின் மேன்முறைக் கண்ணே","கடியென்றார் கற்றறிந் தார்."],"p":"Though your wife possess no excellence and bear no child, it is hard to get rid of the marriage bond. For this cause since he who weds puts sorrow on, in olden days the learned made marriage a synonym of dread.","r":"Even if a wife lacks good qualities and bears no children, the husband cannot put her away; because marriage brings this hardship, the learned gave it the name கடி.","n":"The argument turns on a pun, since கடி means both marriage and 'shun'. The verse finds no fault in marriage to a good wife; it argues from the worst case that the bond, once made, can't be undone.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaazhkkaithunai","ilvaazhkkai","idumbai","thuravu"],"pos":[["mudaliar","The word கடி has both senses, marriage and removal, and the poet invents a reason for the name, a figure Mudaliar calls நிருக்தி.","'கடி' என்னுஞ் சொற்கு 'மணம்' 'நீக்கு' ஆகிய இரண்டு பொருள்க ளுண்டு."]],"ch":"n:6"},"n:57":{"book":"naladiyar","num":57,"t":["ஊக்கித்தாம் கொண்ட விரதங்கள் உள்ளுடையத்","தாக்கருந் துன்பங்கள் தாந்தலை வந்தக்கால்","நீக்கி நிறூஉம் உரவோரே நல்லொழுக்கம்","காக்கும் திருவத் தவர்."],"p":"When troubles arise, hard to resist, to cause them to break the vows which their lofty spirits have pledged, the men of power set griefs aside and firmly fix their souls in right. These are the blessed, guarding decorum's rule.","r":"When unbearable troubles come that would break the vows they took on with zeal, those who push the troubles aside and hold the vows firm are the blessed ones who guard good conduct.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","porai","thitpam","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:6"},"n:58":{"book":"naladiyar","num":58,"t":["தம்மை யிகழ்ந்தமை தாம்பொறுப்ப தன்றிமற்று","எம்மை இகழ்ந்த வினைப்பயத்தால் - உம்மை","எரிவாய் நிரயத்து வீழ்வர்கொல் என்று","பரிவதூஉம் சான்றோர் கடன்."],"p":"To bear with those that speak contemptuous words; yea, more, to say, 'Ah, will these sink in the other world to hell, the place of fire, as fruit of their contemptuous words;' and to grieve, is duty of the perfect man.","r":"Bearing contempt is not enough: the worthy also grieve for those who scorned them, fearing that the deed will drop them into the fiery hell.","n":"The pity looks to the offender's future, not to one's own injury, so forbearance becomes a form of compassion. Parimelazhagar cites this quatrain when glossing Kural 157.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["porai","arul","vinai","saandror"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:6"},"n:59":{"book":"naladiyar","num":59,"t":["மெய்வாய்கண் மூக்குச் செவியெனப் பேர்பெற்ற","ஐவாய வேட்கை அவாவினைக் - கைவாய்","கலங்காமல் காத்துய்க்கும் ஆற்றல் உடையான்","விலங்காது வீடு பெறும்."],"p":"He who undisturbed, in the ordered way of right, has power to guard and guide the desires and lusts that find entrance by the five sense-gates, called 'body, mouth, eye, nose, and ear' - unfailing shall gain 'release.'","r":"One who has the strength to guard and steer, without wavering, the craving that enters through the five gates named body, mouth, eyes, nose and ears will surely reach release.","n":"The senses are called five mouths (ஐவாய) through which craving feeds; the verse asks for them to be steered, not destroyed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","avaa","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:6"},"n:60":{"book":"naladiyar","num":60,"t":["துன்பமே மீதூரக் கண்டும் துறவுள்ளார்","இன்பமே காமுறுவர் ஏழையார் - இன்பம்","இசைதொறும் மற்றதன் இன்னாமை நோக்கிப்","பசைதல் பரியாதாம் மேல்."],"p":"Though wretched men behold afflictions urge and press, renunciation is not in their thoughts; delight they eagerly desire. The great in every joy behold its pain, and seek it not. -----","r":"Fools, though they see suffering mount, have no thought of renouncing and long only for pleasure; the higher ones, whenever pleasure comes, look to the pain in it and do not cling to it.","n":"It reworks 54 almost line for line, but where 54 has the wise leave household life, this has them refuse attachment as each pleasure arrives: a discipline inside experience rather than a change of state.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","pattru","thuravu","idumbai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:6"},"n:61":{"book":"naladiyar","num":61,"t":["மதித்திறப் பாரும் இறக்க மதியா","மதித்திறப் பாரும் இறக்க - மதித்தேறி","ஈயும் தலைமேல் இருத்தலால் அஃதறிவார்","காயும் சுதமின்மை நன்று."],"p":"Who pass esteeming us, let them pass on! And those who contemn and trample on us as they pass, let them too pass on! If even a fly (especially unclean) should climb, trampling on their head, it is well that the wise who know its worth, should feel no wrath.","r":"Let those who honour us pass, and let those who trample us pass too; since even a fly can climb onto one's head, those who understand this do well to be free of burning anger.","n":"The fly deflates the insult: being stepped on is something even the smallest creature does, so it carries no weight. The printed second and third lines repeat மதித்- where both commentators read மிதித்- (trample), and the last line's wording looks corrupt.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","porai","samanilai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:7"},"n:62":{"book":"naladiyar","num":62,"t":["தண்டாச் சிறப்பின்தம் இன்னுயிரைத் தாங்காது","கண்டுழி யெல்லாம் துறப்பவோ - மண்டி","அடிபெயரா தாற்ற இளிவந்த போழ்தின்","முடிகிற்கும் உள்ளத் தவர்."],"p":"Although disgraces throng thickly, and may not be repulsed, will those whose minds are set upon finishing the work begun, renounce sweet life's unfailing worth in their impatience, whenever they see (evils)?","r":"Those whose minds can carry a task through will not throw away their precious life whenever they meet disgrace, even when it presses so hard they cannot move a step.","n":"Placed in a chapter on not being angry, the quatrain can be read as treating death at humiliation as anger turned on oneself. That is an interpretation from its placement; the verse itself argues only from the unfinished task.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["porai","thitpam","maanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:7"},"n:63":{"book":"naladiyar","num":63,"t":["காவா தொருவன்தன் வாய்திறந்து சொல்லும்சொல்","ஓவாதே தன்னைச் சுடுதலால் - ஓவாதே","ஆய்ந்தமைந்த கேள்வி அறிவுடையார் எஞ்ஞான்றும்","காய்ந்தமைந்த சொல்லார் கறுத்து."],"p":"If a man open his mouth and speak unguarded words, his words will ceaselessly burn his soul. The wise who ceaselessly hear, and ponder well and calmly, even in their wrath, will never give utterance to words of fire.","r":"Unguarded words from an open mouth burn the speaker without end, so the learned, who listen and reflect without end, never speak scorching words even when angry.","n":"The repeated ஓவாதே sets the endless burning of careless speech against endless listening as its cure. The burn falls on the speaker, not the hearer.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kadunjol","kelvi","vekuli","adakkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:7"},"n:64":{"book":"naladiyar","num":64,"t":["நேர்த்து நிகரல்லார் நீரல்ல சொல்லியக்கால்","வேர்த்து வெகுளார் விழுமியோர் - ஓர்த்ததனை","உள்ளத்தான் உள்ளி உரைத்துராய் ஊர்கேட்பத்","துள்ளித்தூண் முட்டுமாம் கீழ்."],"p":"When men who are beneath them confront them, and speak unseemly words, the excellent wax not hot with anger. The base man will brood over it, chafe and rave for all the town to hear, and leap, and dash his head against a post.","r":"The excellent do not grow hot with anger when people beneath them confront them with unfit words; the base brood on it, rant for the town to hear, leap about and butt a pillar.","n":"The comic last line makes anger self-injuring rather than wicked: the head that hits the pillar is the angry man's own.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","perumai","kayamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:7"},"n:65":{"book":"naladiyar","num":65,"t":["இளையான் அடக்கம் அடக்கம் கிளைபொருள்","இல்லான் கொடையே கொடைப்பயன் - எல்லாம்","ஒறுக்கும் மதுகை உரனுடை யாளன்","பொறுக்கும் பொறையே பொறை."],"p":"The young man's self-restraint is self-restraint. The gift of him who owns no stores of wealth is gift indeed. When wan has means and might to punish every fault, if he forbear, call him the patient man.","r":"Restraint in youth is true restraint, a gift from one without wealth is the true fruit of giving, and the patience of a strong person able to punish everything is true patience.","n":"Each virtue counts only where its opposite would be easy: youth tempts, poverty excuses, power permits.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["porai","adakkam","eegai","vali"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:7"},"n:66":{"book":"naladiyar","num":66,"t":["கல்லெறிந் தன்ன கயவர்வாய் இன்னாச்சொல்","எல்லாரும் காணப் பொறுத்துய்ப்பர் - ஓல்லை","இடுநீற்றால் பைஅவிந்த நாகம்போல் தத்தம்","குடிமையான் வாதிக்கப் பட்டு."],"p":"As stones the base shower down their bitter words: the noble bear, in sight of all, and let them pass, by sense of noble worth constrained : like serpent's crest at once by touch of sacred ash subdued.","r":"The well-born bear the stone-like bitter words of the base in front of everyone and let them pass, held back by their own lineage as a cobra's hood is quelled at once by sacred ash.","n":"The image grants that the noble have venom: their forbearance is power held in, not its absence.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["porai","kudi","kadunjol","kayamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:7"},"n:67":{"book":"naladiyar","num":67,"t":["மாற்றாராய் நின்றுதம் மாறேற்பார்க்கு ஏலாமை","ஆற்றாமை என்னார் அறிவுடையார் - ஆற்றாமை","நேர்த்தின்னா மற்றவர் செய்தக்கால் தாம்அவரைப்","பேர்த்தின்னா செய்யாமை நன்று."],"p":"When men stand forth as our enemies, and would begin the conflict, to decline the strife is not, in the language of the wise, lack of power. Even when men have confronted and done us intolerable evils, it is good not to do them evil in return.","r":"The wise do not call it weakness to decline a fight with those who come at you as enemies; even when they confront you and do unbearable harm, it is good not to harm them in return.","n":"The first half answers the charge of weakness before the second recommends restraint. With neither commentary aligned, this reading rests on the Tamil and Pope.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["porai","innaa","pagai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:7"},"n:68":{"book":"naladiyar","num":68,"t":["நெடுங்காலம் ஓடினும் நீசர் வெகுளி","கெடுங்காலம் இன்றிப் பரக்கும் - அடுங்காலை","நீர்கொண்ட வெப்பம்போல் தானே தணியுமே","சீர்கொண்ட சான்றோர் சினம்."],"p":"","r":"The anger of the low keeps spreading however long it runs and never dies, while the anger of the worthy cools of itself, like the heat water takes on when boiled.","n":"Water's heat is borrowed from the fire, so the simile can suggest that the worthy's anger comes from outside and leaves them as they were. That is my reading of the image, not the commentators'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","saandror","kayamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:7"},"n:69":{"book":"naladiyar","num":69,"t":["உபகாரம் செய்ததனை ஓராதே தங்கண்","அபகாரம் ஆற்றச் செயினும் - உபகாரம்","தாம்செய்வ தல்லால் தவற்றினால் தீங்கூக்கல்","வான்தோய் குடிப்பிறந்தார்க் கில்."],"p":"Though men think not of good received, and do much ill to men of family whose fame has touched the sky, these still do good; nor are they wont provoked by faults, to render evil to the thankless ones.","r":"Even when people forget the help they received and do great harm, those born in families whose fame touches the sky only keep doing good, and do not seek to repay the fault with harm.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["innaa","nanri","kudi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:7"},"n:70":{"book":"naladiyar","num":70,"t":["கூர்த்துநாய் கெளவிக் கொளக்கண்டும் தம்வாயால்","பேர்த்துநாய் கெளவினார் ஈங்கில்லை - ஈர்த்தன்றிக்","கீழ்மக்கள் கீழாய சொல்லியக்கால் சொல்பவோ","மேன்மக்கள் தம்வாயால் மீட்டு."],"p":"Though a dog, in range, should lay hold of them, there are none in the world who, in return, would lay hold of it with their mouth; and thus, when low men speak, not what is fitting, but low words, will high-minded men, in reply, utter such words with their mouth. ----------","r":"No one bitten by a raging dog bites it back; so when the low speak low words, will the high answer in kind with their own mouths?","n":"The analogy works by ranking the insulter with the dog: answering in kind would mean stooping to its level.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kadunjol","porai","kayamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:7"},"n:71":{"book":"naladiyar","num":71,"t":["கோதை யருவிக் குளர்வரை நன்னாட","பேதையொடு யாதும் உரையற்க - பேதை","உரைக்கிற் சிதைந்துரைக்கும் ஒல்லும் வகையான்","வழக்கிக் கழிதலே நன்று."],"p":"Lord of the pleasant land, where down the cool mountains the streams fall as garlands!— With a fool hold no converse. If you speak with him, in replying he will pervert your words. To slip away from him as best you can is well.","r":"Lord of the good cool hill country where streams fall like garlands: say nothing at all to a fool, who will twist what you say; slipping away as best you can is best.","n":"Forbearance here takes the form of withdrawal rather than endurance: the wise don't stay to bear the fool's talk.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai","sol","porai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:8"},"n:72":{"book":"naladiyar","num":72,"t":["நேரல்லார் நீரல்ல சொல்லியக்கால் மற்றது","தாரித் திருத்தல் தகுதிமற் - றோரும்","புகழ்மையாக் கொள்ளாது பொங்குநீர் ஞாலம்","சமழ்மையாக் கொண்டு விடும்."],"p":"When persons not our equals say unfitting things, to bear and to be still is worthy conduct. The world girt by foaming waters regards not a contrary course as praise-worthy, but as discreditable.","r":"When those not one's equals say unfit things, it is fitting to bear them in silence; the world does not count the opposite as praise but as disgrace.","n":"Mudaliar takes the 'world' that judges to be the worthy, not people at large.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["porai","pazhi","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:8"},"n:73":{"book":"naladiyar","num":73,"t":["காதலார் சொல்லும் கடுஞ்சொல் உவந்துரைக்கும்","ஏதிலார் இன்சொலின் தீதாமோ - போதெலாம்","மாதர்வண்டு ஆர்க்கும் மலிகடல் தண்சேர்ப்ப","ஆவ தறிவார்ப் பெறின்."],"p":"Lord of the swelling sea's cool shore, where bright insects hum around every flower!—Are severe words from loving lips harder to bear, if men can only rightly estimate their result, than pleasant words that strangers courteously speak?","r":"Lord of the cool shore of the teeming sea, where bees hum over every flower: for one who finds people who know what is good for him, are harsh words from those who love worse than pleasant words that strangers speak with a smile?","n":"The condition carries the weight: harsh words are worth more only if one finds friends who know what is good (ஆவது அறிவார்). Mudaliar reads பெறின் as stressing how rare such friends are.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","kadunjol","inchol","idithal"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:8"},"n:74":{"book":"naladiyar","num":74,"t":["அறிவ தறிந்தடங்கி அஞ்சுவ தஞ்சி","உறுவ துலகுவப்பச் செய்து - பெறுவதனால்","இன்புற்று வாழும் இயல்பினார் எஞ்ஞான்றும்","துன்புற்று வாழ்தல் அரிது."],"p":"Those who know what should be known and rule themselves thereby; who fear what should be feared ; who use all their faculties to bless the world; and whose nature rejoices in all good gained: are for ever free from woes.","r":"Those who know what must be known, keep restraint, fear what must be feared, do what is due so that the world rejoices, and live content with what they get, are hardly ever in suffering.","n":"Mudaliar glosses அடங்கி as 'being forbearing', which ties this broad portrait of contentment to the chapter's theme.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","adakkam","acham","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:8"},"n:75":{"book":"naladiyar","num":75,"t":["வேற்றுமை யின்றிக் கலந்திருவர் நட்டக்கால்","தேற்றா ஓழுக்கம் ஒருவன்கண் உண்டாயின்","ஆற்றும் துணையும் பொறுக்க பொறானாயின்","தூற்றாதே தூர விடல்."],"p":"When two with strict accord unite in friendship's bond, if one betray the other's confidence by unkind act, this latter should endure as best he may. And if he can't endure, he should not divulge it, but withdraw himself.","r":"When two have joined in friendship without any distinction and one of them shows conduct the other cannot make sense of, the other should bear it as far as he can, and if he can't, should withdraw without spreading it abroad.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","porai","purangooral"],"pos":[["mudaliar","The friend's lapse is an act done unknowingly, or one that can't be told to others.","தேற்றா ஒழுக்கம் - அறியாச் செய்கை; (பிறர்க்கு) அறிவிக்கக் கூடாச்செய்கை எனினுமாம்."],["shanmuganar","The friend is found in bad conduct.","ஒருவனிடம் தெளியாத தீயொழுக்கம் காணப்படின்"]],"ch":"n:8"},"n:76":{"book":"naladiyar","num":76,"t":["இன்னா செயினும் இனிய ஒழிகென்று","தன்னையே தான்நோவின் அல்லது - துன்னிக்","கலந்தாரைக் கைவிடுதல் கானக நாட","விலங்கிற்கும் விள்ளல் அரிது."],"p":"but never forsake him. Lord of the woodlands! Separation is hard even to beasts; therefore if friends do things that are unpleasant, think them pleasant. bid yourself cease (from wrath), and blame yourself alone; but forsake not those that have been joined to you in the intimacy of friendship.","r":"Lord of the forest land: even if friends do hurtful things, wish them to pass as if pleasant and blame only yourself, but do not abandon those joined to you closely, for parting is hard even for beasts.","n":"Blaming oneself is left open: Mudaliar records two readings, one moral and one karmic.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","porai","anbu"],"pos":[["mudaliar","One blames oneself either for the bad deeds of a former birth that caused the hurt, or for befriending without first examining the friend.","(அச் செய்கைக்குக் காரணமாகிய தீ வினையை முற்பிறப்பிற் செய்த) தன்னையே எனவும், (முன் நன்கு ஆராயாது அவரொடு நட்புகொண்ட) தன்னையே எனவும் பொருள் கூறுப."]],"ch":"n:8"},"n:77":{"book":"naladiyar","num":77,"t":["பெரியார் பெருநட்புக் கோடல்தாம் செய்த","அரிய பொறுப்பஎன் றன்றோ - அரியரோ","ஒல்லென் அருவி உயர்வரை நன்னாட","நல்லசெய் வார்க்குத் தமர்."],"p":"Is not the reason why the close friendship of the great is sought, that they will bear even with faults hard to endure? Lord of the good land of high mountains, with resounding waterfalls! — to good people are intimate friends rare?","r":"Is it not because the great bear with faults that are hard to bear that people seek their deep friendship? Lord of the good high hills with roaring falls, are close friends rare for those who do good?","n":"The closing question can be taken two ways: Mudaliar reads it as 'friends are not rare for the good', Shanmuganar as 'the great are not hard to win for those who do good without fault'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","porai","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:8"},"n:78":{"book":"naladiyar","num":78,"t":["வற்றிமற் றாற்றப் பசிப்பினும் பண்பிலார்க்கு","அற்றம் அறிய உரையற்க - அற்றம்","மறைக்கும் துணையார்க் குரைப்பவே தம்மைத்","துறக்கும் துணிவிலா தார்."],"p":"Though sore wasted with hunger, let not men tell out their destitution to ungracious churls. Those indeed who lack resolution to deny themselves may tell their wants to those who are able and willing to save them from destitution.","r":"Even if wasted and starving, do not tell your need to people without courtesy; those who lack the resolve to give up their life tell it only to those able to cover it.","n":"Both commentators take தம்மைத் துறக்கும் துணிவு as resolve to give up one's life, so the quatrain ranks starving in silence above asking even the right person; Pope reads it as resolve to deny oneself. மறைக்கும் can mean relieving the need or keeping it secret, and Mudaliar allows both.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","inmai","maanam","porai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:8"},"n:79":{"book":"naladiyar","num":79,"t":["இன்பம் பயந்தாங் கிழிவு தலைவரினும்","இன்பத்தின் பக்கம் இருந்தைக்க - இன்பம்","ஒழியாமை கண்டாலும் ஓங்கருவி நாட","பழியாகா ஆறே தலை."],"p":"Although shameful things may present themselves. as things that yield pleasure, flee from that pleasure's side! Though thou couldst see pleasures that cease not soon, — Lord of the land of fountains high! — the guiltless way is best.","r":"Lord of the land of high waterfalls: even if disgrace comes along the way pleasure opened, and even if the pleasure looks as if it would never end, the way that brings no blame comes first.","n":"The second concession is the stronger: not only brief pleasures but lasting ones are to be refused if they carry blame. The first two lines are knotted, and Mudaliar resolves இருந்தைக்க as addressed to the listener.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pazhi","inbam","porai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:8"},"n:80":{"book":"naladiyar","num":80,"t":["தான்கெடினும் தக்கார்கே டெண்ணற்க தன்உடம்பின்","ஊன்கெடினும் உண்ணார்கைத் துண்ணற்க - வான்கவிந்த","வையக மெல்லாம் பெறினும் உரையற்க","பொய்யோடு இடைமிடைந்த சொல்."],"p":"Though ruin seize you plan not ruin to the just! Though body's flesh should waste, eat not from hands unfit! Though the whole earth o'er-arched by heaven accrue as gain, never speak word with falsehood mixed !","r":"Though ruined, do not plan the ruin of the worthy; though your flesh wastes, do not eat from the hands of those you must not eat from; though you would gain the whole sky-roofed earth, do not speak words mixed with falsehood.","n":"Each refusal is set against the extreme loss that would excuse it, which is how the quatrain belongs to a chapter on forbearance.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["innaa","vaaimai","undi","porai"],"pos":[["mudaliar","Those whose food must not be eaten are the guru, the deity and the poor; he records others who say lower castes or those who serve without love.","உண்ணத்தாகாதார் குரு, தெய்வம், வறிய ராதியர்;"]],"ch":"n:8"},"n:81":{"book":"naladiyar","num":81,"t":["அச்சம் பெரிதால் அதற்கின்பம் சிற்றளவால்","நிச்சம் நினையுங்கால் கோக்கொலையால் - நிச்சலும்","கும்பிக்கே கூர்த்த வினையால் பிறன்தாரம்","நம்பற்க நாணுடை யார்."],"p":"The fear it brings is great ; its pleasure is brief ; each day if it is divulged death threatens by the king's decree; and ever it is a deed that tends to the pains of hell; O shamefast men, desire not your neighbour's wife.","r":"The fear is great and the pleasure small; thought through, it risks death at the king's hands and is a deed that daily sharpens toward hell, so those with a sense of shame should not set their hopes on another man's wife.","n":"The case is prudential from start to finish, weighing fear against pleasure, but the appeal at the end is to shame (நாணுடையார).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piranil","acham","naanam","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:9"},"n:82":{"book":"naladiyar","num":82,"t":["அறம்புகழ் கேண்மை பெருமைஇந் நான்கும்","பிறன்தாரம் நச்சுவார்ச் சேரா - பிறன்தாரம்","நச்சுவார்ச் சேரும் பகைபழி பாவம்என்று","அச்சத்தோடு இந்நாற் பொருள்."],"p":"Virtue, praise, friendship, greatness, all these four draw not anigh the men who covet their neighbour's wife. Hatred, disgrace and guilt, with fear, these four possessions abide with men who covet their neighbour's wife.","r":"Virtue, renown, friendship and greatness do not come to those who desire another man's wife; enmity, blame, sin and fear do.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piranil","pazhi","aram","acham"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:9"},"n:83":{"book":"naladiyar","num":83,"t":["புக்க விடத்தச்சம் போதரும் போதச்சம்","துய்க்கு மிடத்தச்சம் தோன்றாமல் காப்பச்சம்","எக்காலும் அச்சம் தருமால் எவன்கொலோ","உட்கான் பிறன்இல் புகல்."],"p":"In entering in there's fear; in home returning fear; during enjoyment is fear; in guarding the secret is fear; it evermore brings fear: why shuns he not with dread the entrance of neighbour's house?","r":"Fear on entering, fear on returning, fear while enjoying, fear in keeping it hidden: it brings fear at every moment, so why does a man enter another's house without dread?","n":"The repetition of அச்சம் enacts the condition it describes. The closing question turns on the same word: the one who lives in fear lacks the one fear that would have kept him out.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piranil","acham"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:9"},"n:84":{"book":"naladiyar","num":84,"t":["காணின் குடிப்பழியாம் கையுறின் கால்குறையும்","ஆணின்மை செய்யுங்கால் அச்சமாம் - நீள்நிரயத்","துன்பம் பயக்குமால் துச்சாரி, நீகண்ட","இன்பம் எனக்கெனைத்தால் கூறு."],"p":"If any one see, disgrace lights on the house; if any hand should seize, leg's maimed; in the doing of the shameful deed is dread; it yields as fruit vast hell's affliction : tell me, O profligate, what measure of delight is thine ?","r":"If seen, the family is disgraced; if caught, a leg is cut off; the unmanly act itself is fear; and it yields long suffering in hell: tell me, profligate, what pleasure did you find?","n":"Mudaliar reads ஆணின்மை as the absence of the qualities that make a man, so the adulterer's daring is renamed unmanliness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piranil","pazhi","acham","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:9"},"n:85":{"book":"naladiyar","num":85,"t":["செம்மையொன் றின்றிச் சிறியா ரினத்தராய்க்","கொம்மை வரிமுலையாள் தோள்மாணஇ - உம்மை","வலியால் பிறர்மனைமேல் சென்றாரே, இம்மை","அலியாகி ஆடிஉண் பார்."],"p":"Those who, in a former state, without any regard to right, becoming associates of the mean, enjoyed the embraces of beautiful women, and by violence approached their neighbour's wife, in this state will become eunuchs, and dancing shall earn their bread.","r":"Those who in a former birth, with no sense of right and in the company of the mean, went by force to other men's wives become eunuchs in this birth and live by dancing.","n":"The punishment mirrors the offence: those who used male force are reborn without it. This is the chapter's one claim about a past birth; the Kural's chapter on another's wife makes none.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piranil","piravi","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:9"},"n:86":{"book":"naladiyar","num":86,"t":["பல்லா ரறியப் பறையறைந்து நாள்கேட்டுக்","கல்யாணம் செய்து கடிபுக்க - மெல்லியல்","காதல் மனையாளும் இல்லாளா என்ஒருவன்","ஏதில் மனையாளை நோக்கு."],"p":"While his loving wife dwells in his home, the tender one whom he espoused, — seeking (out an auspicious) day, and sounding the drum, for many folks to know,—and 'whom he guards as his own, what means a man's glance at another's wife?","r":"When a man has at home a tender, loving wife, married on a day fixed by asking, with drums beaten for many to know, why should he look at a stranger's wife?","n":"The public wedding is the point: a bond announced to many and guarded (கடி) is set against one that has to be hidden.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piranil","vaazhkkaithunai","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:9"},"n:87":{"book":"naladiyar","num":87,"t":["அம்பல் அயல்எடுப்ப அஞ்சித் தமர்பாணஇ","வம்பலன் பெண்மாணஇ மைந்துற்று - நம்பும்","நிலைமைஇல் நெஞ்சத்தான் துப்புரவு பாம்பின்","தலைநக்கி யன்ன துடைத்து."],"p":"The enjoyment of the man of unstable heart, who under the influence of infatuation approaches his neighbour's wife and sets his affections upon her, while neighbours spread abroad his guilt, and kinsmen dread and mourn, is like (that of the person who takes pleasure in) licking a serpent's head.","r":"The pleasure of the unsteady-hearted man who, maddened, unites with another's wife while neighbours gossip and his own people tremble and grieve, is like licking a cobra's head.","n":"The image holds pleasure and lethal danger in a single act, so no separate weighing of costs is needed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piranil","kaamam","alar"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:9"},"n:88":{"book":"naladiyar","num":88,"t":["பரவா, வெளிப்படா, பல்லோர்கண் தங்கா","உரவோர்கண் காமநோய் ஓஓ.","கொடிதே. விரவாருள் நாணுப் படல்அஞ்சி யாதும்","உரையாதுஉள் ஆறி விடும்."],"p":"The disease of lust in men mighty (in wisdom) gains not ascendancy, is not revealed, does not remain fixed on many objects. O! it is a cruel conflict; but fearing to incur shame in the midst of their foes, they say nothing about it, and it is extinguished within them.","r":"In the strong, the sickness of desire does not spread, show itself or settle on many; fearing shame among those outside, they say nothing and it subsides within. Oh, how cruel it is!","n":"The strong are not free of desire, only able to contain it, and the cry 'how cruel' can fall on that containment. Mudaliar reads it so: desire that can't come out and must be suffered inwardly is the cruellest.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","nirai","naanam"],"pos":[["mudaliar","Because it doesn't come out but stays within and subsides only after great suffering, the desire of the wise is called very cruel.","வெளிப்பட்டொழியாது உள்ளே நின்று மிக வருந்தி அடங்குதலின், அறிவுடையார் காமம் மிகவுங் கொடிது எனப்படுத லுணர்க."]],"ch":"n:9"},"n:89":{"book":"naladiyar","num":89,"t":["அம்பும் அழலும் அவிர்கதிர் ஞாயிறும்","வெம்பிச் சுடினும் புறம்சுடும் - வெம்பிக்","கவற்றி மனத்தைச் சுடுதலால், காமம்","அவற்றினும் அஞ்சப் படும்."],"p":"Arrow and fire and sun with glistening rays may rage and burn; but these burn the outer man alone. Lust rages and distracts and burns the mind, and is more to be feared than they.","r":"Arrow, fire and the sun's bright rays, however they blaze, burn only the outside; desire, blazing and troubling the mind, burns the mind itself, so it is to be feared more than they.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","manam","acham"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:9"},"n:90":{"book":"naladiyar","num":90,"t":["ஊருள் எழுந்த உருகெழு செந்தீக்கு","நீருள் குளித்தும் உயலாகும் - நீருள்","குளிப்பினும் காமம் சுடுமேகுன் றேறி","ஒளிப்பினும் காமம் சுடும்."],"p":"From the ruddy fire that fiercely rises in the village you may scape by bathing in water. — Although you bathe in water, lust will burn; and though you climb the hill and hide you there, still lust will burn! -----------","r":"From a fierce red fire rising in the town one can escape by plunging into water; but desire burns even if one plunges into water, and burns even if one climbs a hill to hide.","n":"Water and the hilltop are the two refuges from fire, and both fail because this fire travels with the one who flees.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:9"},"n:91":{"book":"naladiyar","num":91,"t":["இல்லா விடத்தும் இயைந்த அளவினால்","உள்ள விடம்போல் பெரிதுவந்து - மெல்லக்","கொடையொடு பட்ட குணனுடைய மாந்தர்க்கு","அடையாவாம் ஆண்டைக் கதவு"],"p":"Even in their adverse hour, up to the measure of their means, as in the prosperous times, with large rejoicing heart to give is their nature still. To such good men heaven's gate is never closed.","r":"Those whose nature is to give gladly, as much as they can, even when they have little, as if they had plenty, will not find the doors of that world shut against them.","n":"Gladness is part of the qualification: the gift is measured against the giver's means and his spirit, not its size.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","inmai","manam"],"pos":[["mudaliar","The doors are those of the other world, which Pope calls heaven.","ஆண்டைக்கதவு-அவ்வுலகத்துக் கதவுகள்."],["shanmuganar","The doors are those of release (வீடுபேறு), not of a heaven.","வீடுபேற்றுக் கதவுகள் அடைக்கப்படாவாம்."]],"ch":"n:10"},"n:92":{"book":"naladiyar","num":92,"t":["முன்னரே சாம்நாள் முனிதக்க மூப்புள","பின்னரும் பீடழிக்கும் நோயுள - கொன்னே","பரவன்மின் பற்றன்மின் பாத்துண்மின் யாதும்","கரவன்மின் கைத்துண்டாம் போழ்து."],"p":"Before (you) are death's day and age detestable ; behind is disease that humbles pride. Discursive thoughts indulge not. Cling not to earth. Eat, sharing food; hide not your powers while wealth is in your hand.","r":"Death's day and hateful old age stand right before you, and disease that destroys strength comes after; don't run about in vain, don't clutch, share before you eat, and hide nothing while wealth is in your hand.","n":"The case for giving here is impermanence, not merit or fame: wealth is to be used while the hand still holds it. Mudaliar takes பரவன்மின் as 'don't roam after more wealth'; Pope has 'indulge not discursive thoughts'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","nilaiyaamai","ivaral"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:10"},"n:93":{"book":"naladiyar","num":93,"t":["நடுக்குற்றுத் தற்சேர்ந்தார் துன்பம் துடையார்","கொடுத்துத்தான் துய்ப்பினும் ஈண்டுங்கால் ஈண்டும்","மிடுக்குற்றுப் பற்றினும் நில்லாது செல்வம்","விடுக்கும் வினையுலந்தக் கால்."],"p":"Though dread of want they do not relieve the woes of men who as suppliants draw near! Yet although men enjoy and give, wealth grows in growing time: cling to it, and yet it flies when former deeds that brought wealth have lost their power.","r":"Whether one ignores the misery of those who come trembling or gives and enjoys, wealth gathers when its time to gather comes; and however tightly it is held, it leaves when the good deeds that sent it run out.","n":"The argument removes the miser's motive: giving can't lose wealth that merit holds, and clutching can't keep wealth that merit has stopped holding. Mudaliar notes the variant இடுக்குற்று (clutching tight) for மிடுக்குற்று (with force).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","vinai","oozh","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:10"},"n:94":{"book":"naladiyar","num":94,"t":["இம்மி யரிசித் துணையானும் வைகலும்","நும்மில் இயைவ கொடுத்துண்மின் - உம்மைக்","கொடாஅ தவரென்பர் குண்டுநீர் வையத்(து)","அடாஅ அடுப்பி னவர்."],"p":"Daily having given somewhat, though but the fraction of a grain of rice, according to your ability, eat ye (your own food). Those who gave not in that world, men say on this earth girt by deep waters are those on whose hearth nothing is cooked.","r":"Give daily, before eating, from what you can, even a grain of rice's worth; the quatrain backs this with a saying about those who do not give and those whose hearth is never lit.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","inmai","piravi","vinai"],"pos":[["mudaliar","உம்மை means 'in a former birth': those who did not give then are the poor with unlit hearths now.","உம்மை- முற்பிறப்பில். என்பர்-என்றிகழப்படுவர்;"],["shanmuganar","உம்மை means 'you': the poor with unlit hearths will scorn you as misers.","கொடாத கருமிகள் என்று உம்மை இழித்து உரைப்பர்."]],"ch":"n:10"},"n:95":{"book":"naladiyar","num":95,"t":["மறுமையும் இம்மையும் நோக்கி ஒருவற்கு","உறுமா றியைவ கொடுத்தல் - வறுமையால்","ஈதல் இசையா தெனினும் இரவாமை","ஈதல் இரட்டி யுறும்."],"p":"Regarding the other world and this world, give to any suppliant, in fitting way, according to your ability. And if on account of poverty giving is not possible, yet refraining from begging is twice as meritorious as giving.","r":"With an eye to the next world and this one, give what you can in a fitting way; and if poverty makes giving impossible, not begging is worth twice as much as giving.","n":"Mudaliar names the two rewards: heavenly joy in the next world and renown in this one. The last line keeps the poor inside the economy of giving: they give by not taking.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","iravu","inmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:10"},"n:96":{"book":"naladiyar","num":96,"t":["நடுவூருள் வேதிகை சுற்றுக்கோட் புக்க","படுபனை யன்னர் பலர்நச்ச வாழ்வார்","குடிகொழுத்தக் கண்ணுங்கொடுத்துண்ணா மாக்கள்","இடுகாட்டுள் ஏற்றைப் பனை."],"p":"Those who live desired of many are as a fertile palm entered in the altar's enclosure in midmost of the town. Those who, even when their house grows great, give not before they eat, are like the sterile palm in the burning ground.","r":"Those who live so that many desire them are like a fruit-bearing palm enclosed by a platform in the middle of town; those who do not give before eating, even when their household thrives, are like a barren male palm in the burning ground.","n":"Both are palms; fruit and place separate them. The giver stands where everyone gathers, the miser among the dead.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","selvam","payan"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:10"},"n:97":{"book":"naladiyar","num":97,"t":["பெயற்பால் மழைபெய்யாக் கண்ணும் உலகம்","செயற்பால செய்யா விடினும் - கயற்புலால்","புன்னை கடியும் பொருகடல் தண்சேர்ப்ப.","என்னை உலகுய்யு மாறு."],"p":"Even when the rain rains not in due season, if all the world should fail in virtuous actions that ought to be done, —Lord of the warring sea's cool shore, where acrid fume of fish in Punnai's perfume dies! - how scapes the world?","r":"Lord of the cool shore of the pounding sea, where the punnai's scent drives off the smell of fish: if the rain does not fall when it should, and the world does not do what it should, how will the world survive?","n":"Both commentators take the 'world' that must act to be its good or wise people, so their giving is set beside rain as a second support of life.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mazhai","eegai","oppuravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:10"},"n:98":{"book":"naladiyar","num":98,"t":["ஏற்றகைம் மாற்றாமை என்னானும் தாம்வரையாது","ஆற்றாதார்க் கீவதாம் ஆண்கடன் - ஆற்றின்","மலிகடல் தண்சேர்ப்ப மாறீவார்க் கீதல்","பொலிகடன் என்னும் பெயர்த்து."],"p":"Denying to no out-stretched hand, to give to needy men as he hath power, is duty of a man.—Lord of the swelling sea's cool shore !—A gift to those that can return the gift is usury !","r":"Never refusing an outstretched hand and holding nothing back, to give to those who can't repay is a man's duty; lord of the cool shore, giving to those who will give back earns only the name of a profitable loan.","n":"Mudaliar lists several ways to take தாம் வரையாது: excluding no gift, excluding no person, setting no rules, or keeping nothing back for oneself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","payan"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:10"},"n:99":{"book":"naladiyar","num":99,"t":["இறப்பச் சிறிதென்னா தில்லென்னா தென்றும்","அறப்பயன் யார்மாட்டும் செய்க - முறைப்புதவின்","ஐயம் புகூஉம் தவசி கடிஞைபோல்","பைய நிறைத்து விடும்."],"p":"Say not ‘ 'tis passing little,' nor ‘ ' tis nought I give;' on all confer thy boon of virtuous charity, Like the dish the mendicant presents from door to door, by frequent doles 'twill be filled full.","r":"Never say 'it is too little' or 'I have nothing'; always do the good of giving, to anyone, for like the ascetic's bowl filled at door after door, it fills slowly.","n":"The bowl belongs to the one who receives, but it stands for what fills in the giver: Mudaliar says the fruit of virtue fills his life, Shanmuganar that good deeds accumulate.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","aram","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:10"},"n:100":{"book":"naladiyar","num":100,"t":["கடிப்பிடு கண்முரசம் காதத்தோர் கேட்பர்","இடித்து முழங்கியதோர் யோசனையோர் கேட்பர்","அடுக்கிய மூவுலகும் கேட்குமே சான்றோர்","கொடுத்தா ரெனப்படும் சொல்."],"p":"The sound of beaten drum a katham off they'll hear; the thunder's voice through a whole yojanai will reach men's ears; the three successive worlds will hear the word that says, ‘Men excellent their gifts have given.' ------------","r":"A drum struck with a stick is heard a காதம் away and rolling thunder a யோசனை, but the word 'they gave', linked with the worthy, is heard through all three stacked worlds.","n":"Mudaliar records a third reading, 'they gave to the worthy'.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","pugazh","saandror"],"pos":[["mudaliar","The word is 'he gave', spoken by the worthy about a giver.","சான் றோரால் கொடுத்தார் எனப்படுஞ் சொல்லையோவெனில்;"],["shanmuganar","The word is praise that the worthy themselves gave.","பெரியார் கொடுத்தார் எனப்படும் புகழ்ச் சொல்லையோ,"]],"ch":"n:10"},"n:101":{"book":"naladiyar","num":101,"t":["பல்லாவுள் உய்த்து விடினும் குழக்கன்று","வல்லதாம் தாய்நாடிக் கோடலைத் - தொல்லைப்","பழவினையும் அன்ன தகைத்தேதற் செய்த","கிழவனை நாடிக் கொளற்கு."],"p":"Although you send forth the tender calf amid many cows, it has unerring skill to seek out its own mother. Deeds of old days have even so the power to search him out to whom their fruit pertains.","r":"A young calf let loose among many cows can still find its own mother, and an old deed has the same skill in seeking out the one who did it.","n":"The image makes the deed active: it searches, as the calf does, rather than waiting to be met. Neither the verse nor Mudaliar says the deed comes from a former birth; Shanmuganar adds that.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","oozh"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:11"},"n:102":{"book":"naladiyar","num":102,"t":["உருவும் இளமையும் ஒண்பொருளும் உட்கும்","ஒருவழி நில்லாமை கண்டும் - ஒருவழி","ஒன்றேயும் இல்லாதான் வாழ்க்கை உடம்(பு)இட்டு","நின்றுவீழ்ந் தக்க(து) உடைத்து."],"p":"Beauty and youth, and glittering wealth and reverence abide not in one stay. To him who, though he sees this, does no single virtuous act in this one stage of being, life's joy stands with the body and falls with it.","r":"Beauty, youth, fine wealth and the awe one commands do not stay; one who sees this and still does not one good thing has a life that merely takes a body, stands, and falls.","n":"The failure condemned is not vice but the absence of even one good act; the body is wasted rather than misused. Mudaliar offers two senses of ஒருவழி ஒன்றேயும்: any single good deed, or one deed of the kind that leads to the highest state.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","aram","payan"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:11"},"n:103":{"book":"naladiyar","num":103,"t":["வளம்பட வேண்டாதார் யார்யாரும் இல்லை","அளந்தன போகம் அவர்அவர் ஆற்றால்","விளங்காய் திரட்டினார் இல்லை, களங்கனியைக்","காரெனச் செய்தாரும் இல்."],"p":"Who would not see Prosperity? All seek her gifts; but as men's ways are, so each man's enjoyments are meted out. Who made the Vilam's apple round? Or who gave its dusky hue to the Kalam fruit ?","r":"Everyone wants prosperity, but each person's enjoyments are measured out according to their own course, just as no one rounded the vilam fruit or blackened the kalam berry.","n":"The fruits are chosen because their shape and colour belong to them by nature. The verse says only அவரவர் ஆற்றால்; both commentators take that 'way' to be one's former deeds.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","vinai","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:11"},"n:104":{"book":"naladiyar","num":104,"t":["உறற்பால நீக்கல் உறுவர்க்கும் ஆகா,","பெறற்பா லனையவும் அன்னவாம் மாரி","வறப்பின் தருவாரும் இல்லை, அதனைச்","சிறப்பின் தணிப்பாரும் இல்."],"p":"Not even saints can drive away predestined ills; and all the fated gain must needs accrue. In time of drought who can bestow the rain ? Or who can check its rich abundance when it falls?","r":"Not even the உறுவர் can remove what is bound to befall, and what is bound to be gained is the same; no one can bring rain in a drought or check it when it pours.","n":"Mudaliar records Shanmuganar's kind of reading of the second line as another view, under which the verse is a reversed parallel: rain withheld matches gains withheld, rain unstoppable matches pains unstoppable.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","vinai","mazhai"],"pos":[["mudaliar","The உறுவர் are sages, and destined pleasures can no more be prevented than destined pains.","அன்ன ஆம்-அத் தன்மையவேயாம்; 'நீக்கல் ஆகா' என்றபடி."],["shanmuganar","No strong person can stop what fate brings, and no one can obtain by their own effort what they wish to gain.","பெற விரும்புகிறவற்றைத் தாமாகப் பெறுவதென்பதும் அதுபோன்றே இயலாததாம்"]],"ch":"n:11"},"n:105":{"book":"naladiyar","num":105,"t":["தினைத்துணைய ராகித்தந் தேசுள் அடக்கிப்","பனைத்துணையார் வைகலும் பாடழிந்து வாழ்வர்","நினைப்பக் கிடந்த தெவனுண்டாம் மேலை","வினைப்பயன் அல்லால் பிற."],"p":"Those who lose like stately palms, when their greatness is gone, become small asthe millet seed, hiding their glory within, —and so they pass their days. This is the fruit of deeds of former days: when you think of it, what other cause can there be?","r":"Men once as great as palm trees shrink to the size of a millet seed, hide their brightness within and live on diminished day after day; think about it, and what cause is there but the fruit of former deeds?","n":"The verse turns a common sight, the fallen great, into an argument by elimination: no other cause can be thought of. Mudaliar specifies that the deeds are evil ones.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","perumai","kedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:11"},"n:106":{"book":"naladiyar","num":106,"t":["பல்லான்ற கேள்விப் பயனுணர்வார் வீயவும்","கல்லாதார் வாழ்வ தறிதிரேல் - கல்லாதார்","சேதனம் என்னுமச் சேறகத் தின்மையால்","கோதென்று கொள்ளாதாம் கூற்று."],"p":"Those that know the fruit of varied and profound learning die off, while the unlearned joyously live on. Would you know the cause ? — The unlearned posses within no 'sap of sapience'; — so death deems then refuse stalks, and takes them not!","r":"If you ask why those who know the fruit of deep learning die while the unlearned live on, it is because the unlearned have none of the sap called sense, so Death passes them over as husks.","n":"The verse is a joke with an edge: a long life for the unlearned is recast as Death's contempt. It doesn't say the learned die because of their learning; it supplies a fanciful cause, which Mudaliar names as a figure of imputed cause (ஏதுத் தற்குறிப்பேற்றவணி).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kallaamai","kalvi","kootru"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:11"},"n:107":{"book":"naladiyar","num":107,"t":["இடும்பைகூர் நெஞ்சத்தார் எல்லாரும் காண","நெடுங்கடை நின்றுழல்வ தெல்லாம் - அடம்பப்பூ","அன்னம் கிழிக்கும் அலைகடல் தண்சேர்ப்ப","முன்னை வினையாய் விடும்."],"p":"Lord of the sea's cool shore, where amid the wave swans sport, tearing to shreds the Adambu flowers ! When those whose hearts are sore with urgent need standbegging, and wander through the long street, in sight of all, this is the fruit of former deeds.","r":"When people with aching hearts stand at the gates of great houses, begging in sight of everyone, all of that is their former deeds, lord of the cool shore where swans tear the adambu flowers.","n":"The address to the lord of the shore frames the quatrain as words spoken to a chieftain. Both commentators make the deeds evil ones from a former birth; the verse says only முன்னை வினை.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","vinai","idumbai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:11"},"n:108":{"book":"naladiyar","num":108,"t":["அறியாரும் அல்லர் அறிவ தறிந்தும்","பழியோடு பட்டவை செய்தல் - வளியோடி","நெய்தல் நறவுயிர்க்கும் நீள்கடல் தண்சேர்ப்ப","செய்த வினையான் வரும்."],"p":"They are not ignorant; but, though what man should know they know, yet they do deeds that bring guilt to their souls. — Lord of the wide sea's pleasant shore, where breezes breathe the lily's fragrance round ! — This comes from former deeds.","r":"When people who are far from ignorant, who know what ought to be known, still do blameworthy things, that comes from the deeds they have done, lord of the long cool shore where the breeze carries the honey-scent of the neythal.","n":"The quatrain explains a moral puzzle, knowing the good and doing wrong, by pushing the cause back into earlier deeds. It explains the act; it does not excuse it, and pazhi still attaches.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","arivu","pazhi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:11"},"n:109":{"book":"naladiyar","num":109,"t":["ஈண்டுநீர் வையத்துள் எல்லாரும் எத்துணையும்","வேண்டார்மன் தீய விழைபயன் நல்லவை","வேண்டினும் வேண்டா விடினும் உற்றபால","தீண்டா விடுதல் அரிது."],"p":"On the earth begirt by gathering waters no men desire in anywise evil things, but choice fruit of good things. Yet whether they desire, or abhor, it is hard to shun the touch of what fate assigns.","r":"No one on this sea-girt earth wants evil in the least, and all want good results; but wanted or not, what is bound to come is hard to keep from touching them.","n":"Desire is shown to be idle in both directions: wanting good does not bring it, and not wanting evil does not prevent it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","vinai","avaa"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:11"},"n:110":{"book":"naladiyar","num":110,"t":["சிறுகா பெருகா முறைபிறழ்ந்து வாரா","உறுகாலத் தூற்றாகா ஆமிடத்தே யாகும்","சிறுகாலைப் பட்ட பொறியும் அதனால்","இறுகாலத் தென்னை பரிவூ."],"p":"The early fates diminish not, nor do they increase, they come not in order changedhelp in troublous times is none; what haps will happen, there and then; and so, when all things fail, why grieve? -----------","r":"What was set in the early mark does not shrink or grow, does not come out of order, gives no support in hard times, and arrives where it is due; so why grieve when the time of ruin comes?","n":"சிறுகாலைப் பட்ட பொறி is literally 'the mark set in the small time'; both commentators read it as what was fixed in the womb. The chapter ends not with fear but with an argument for calm, since a fixed sum cannot be changed by grieving.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","samanilai","idumbai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:11"},"n:111":{"book":"naladiyar","num":111,"t":["இசையா ஒருபொருள் இல்லென்றல் யார்க்கும்","வசையன்று வையத் தியற்கை - நசையழுங்க","நின்றோடிப் பொய்த்தல் நிரைதொடீஇ. செய்ந்நன்றி","கொன்றாரின் குற்ற முடைத்து."],"p":"O maid with many armlets graced! To answer 'no' disgraces no man, when the boon asked exceeds his means. It is the world's course. But to delay and cheat the soul faint with desire is a sin like his who 'slays' a benefit conferred.","r":"Saying 'I have none' about something one cannot give is no disgrace and is the way of the world; but keeping someone waiting till their hope dies and then failing them is a worse fault than killing a kindness received.","n":"The verse doesn't fault refusal; it faults the false promise that turns refusal into torment. Both commentators read கொன்றாரின் as a comparison, worse than ingratitude, where Pope says only 'like'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaaimai","eegai","nanri"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:12"},"n:112":{"book":"naladiyar","num":112,"t":["தக்காரும் தக்கவ ரல்லாரும் தந்நீர்மை","எக்காலும் குன்றல் இலராவர் - அக்காரம்","யாவரே தின்னினும் கையாதாம் கைக்குமாம்","தேவரே தின்னினும் வேம்பு."],"p":"Men of worth, and men unworthy too, retain their natures ever unchanged. Whoever they be that eat it, sugar can never grow bitter; and margosa is bitter even when eaten by gods.","r":"The worthy and the unworthy never fall away from their own natures: jaggery is not bitter whoever eats it, and neem is bitter even if gods eat it.","n":"The claim runs both ways: circumstances cannot spoil the good, and they cannot redeem the bad. The gods are named to say that even the highest eater does not change the taste.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panpu","perumai","sirumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:12"},"n:113":{"book":"naladiyar","num":113,"t":["காலாடு போழ்தில் கழிகிளைஞர் வானத்து","மேலாடு மீனின் பலராவர் - ஏலா","இடரொருவர் உற்றக்கால் ஈர்ங்குன்ற நாட","தொடர்புடையேம் என்பார் சிலர்."],"p":"When a man moves prosperonsly on, devoted kinsfolk are countless as shining stars that move in the upper heaven. But when grievous affliction haps,—Lord of the dripping hills !—few claim close alliance with him.","r":"When a man prospers, kin who cling to him are more numerous than the stars that move in the sky; when unbearable trouble strikes, few say 'we are related to him', lord of the cool hills.","n":"Mudaliar notes that கிளைஞர் means, by its root, those who cling like branches, which sharpens the irony of their leaving.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram","selvam","idumbai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:12"},"n:114":{"book":"naladiyar","num":114,"t":["விடுவிலா வையத்து மன்னிய மூன்றில்","நடுவண தெய்த இருதலையும் எய்தும்","நடுவண தெய்தாதான் எய்தும் உலைப்பெய்து","அடுவது போலும் துயர்."],"p":"Of the three things that endure upon this faultless earth, he who gains the midmost gains the two extremes also. He who gains not the midmost gains the cruel smart that the turtle feels when put into the pot to boil.","r":"Of the three that endure in this world, whoever gains the middle one gains both ends as well; whoever does not gains a suffering like being boiled in a furnace.","n":"The verse doesn't name the three; both commentators identify them as virtue, wealth and pleasure, with wealth in the middle. For a work that elsewhere calls wealth unreliable, this is a strong statement of its worth. Pope's turtle in the pot is his own; Mudaliar has a smith's furnace.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","aram","inbam","idumbai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:12"},"n:115":{"book":"naladiyar","num":115,"t":["நல்லாவின் கன்றாயின் நாகும் விலைபெறூஉம்","கல்லாரே யாயினும் செல்வர்வாய்ச் சொற்செல்லும்","புல்லீரப் போழ்தின் உழவேபோல் மீதாடிச்","செல்லாவாம் நல்கூர்ந்தார் சொல்."],"p":"A young heifer fetches a good price when it is the calf of a good cow; so the words of rich men though unlearned, pass current. Poor men's words, like the plough when moisture is scanty, merely graze the surface and are of no avail.","r":"Even a young calf fetches a price if its mother is a good cow, and the words of the rich are accepted even if they are unlearned; the words of the poor glance off like a plough in barely moist soil.","n":"The calf image says worth is borrowed from the source, not earned; the plough image says the poor man's words do not sink in. The verse reports this as how things are, not as how they should be.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai","selvam","sol"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:12"},"n:116":{"book":"naladiyar","num":116,"t":["இடம்பட மெய்ஞ்ஞானம் கற்பினும் என்றும்","அடங்காதார் என்றும் அடங்கார் - தடங்கண்ணாய்","உப்போடு நெய்பால் தயிர்காயம் பெய்திடினும்","கைப்பறா பேய்ச்சுரையின் காய்."],"p":"O wide-eyed one! though you cook the wild gourd pouring in salt, ghee, milk, curds, and spices, it never loses its bitterness. So those who never discipline themselves, though they may learn extensive works of true wisdom, never become disciplined.","r":"Those without restraint stay unrestrained even if they study true wisdom widely, as a wild bitter gourd stays bitter though cooked with salt, ghee, milk, curd and asafoetida.","n":"Mudaliar takes அடங்காதார் as those who are by nature not given to restraint, so the point is that learning cannot change a nature, not that learning is useless.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","kalvi","panpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:12"},"n:117":{"book":"naladiyar","num":117,"t":["தம்மை இகழ்வாரைத் தாமவரின் முன்னிகழ்க","என்னை அவரொடு பட்டது - புன்னை","விற்றபூங் கமழ்கானல் வீங்குநீர்ச் சேர்ப்ப","உற்றபால யார்க்கும் உறும்."],"p":"When men scorn you, before their faces scorn them too ! what has a man to do with them? — Lord of shore where beauteous Punnai-flowers perfume the glades that surround the swelling tide, — what fated comes to all!","r":"Scorn those who scorn you, before they do; what have you to do with them? What is bound to come comes to everyone, lord of the shore fragrant with punnai flowers.","n":"The link between the advice and the last line is not stated. One reading is that since what is fated will come anyway, there is nothing to lose by answering scorn. Mudaliar takes அவரின் முன் either as at the very moment they scorn or to their faces rather than behind their backs, and calls the verse a way to silence groundless slanderers.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["igal","oozh","maanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:12"},"n:118":{"book":"naladiyar","num":118,"t":["ஆவே றுருவின வாயினும் ஆபயந்த","பால்வே றுருவின அல்லவாம் - பால்போல்","ஒருதன்மைத் தாகும் அறநெறி ஆபோல்","உருவு பலகொளல் ஈங்கு."],"p":"Though cows in form are diverse, the milk they yield is not diverse. The way of virtue, like that milk, is one in nature, though the schools that teach it here are like those cows, of many forms.","r":"Cows differ in colour, but the milk they give does not; virtue, like milk, is of one nature, while its ways here take many forms, like the cows.","n":"Mudaliar takes the many ways as the many means of practising one virtue. Pope makes them the schools or sects that teach it, which turns the verse into a statement about religious diversity; the Tamil says only நெறி.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:12"},"n:119":{"book":"naladiyar","num":119,"t":["யாஅர் உலகத்தோர் சொல்லில்லார்? தேருங்கால்","யாஅர் உபாயத்தின் வாழாதார்? - யாஅர்","இடையாக இன்னாத தெய்தாதார்? யாஅர்","கடைபோகச் செல்வம்உய்த் தார்?"],"p":"Look well! of whom hath not the world found word to say ? And who have not by prudence prospered in life's way? Ah! who in life's mid course no bitter grief have known ? Ah! who to end of life have kept their wealth their own?","r":"Who in the world has escaped having a word said against them? Who has not lived by stratagem? Who has not met hardship midway? Who has kept their wealth to the end?","n":"The four questions expect the answer 'no one', so the verse states the common lot without saying it. Mudaliar gives two senses of உபாயம்: the four political stratagems, or the ordinary means of livelihood.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","pazhi","idumbai","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:12"},"n:120":{"book":"naladiyar","num":120,"t":["தாஞ்செய் வினையல்லால் தம்மொடு செல்வதுமற்று","யாங்கணும் தேரின் பிறிதில்லை - ஆங்குத்தாம்","போற்றிப் புனைந்த உடம்பும் பயமின்றே","கூற்றும்கொண் டோ டும் பொழுது."],"p":"Save a man's deed: nought goes with him, search where you will. The body which men cherish so, and adorn, is itself profitless indeed when death shall seize and hurry off with it. ------","r":"Search where you will, nothing goes with a person but the deeds they have done; even the body they guarded and adorned is of no use when Death seizes it and runs.","n":"வினை here is not only good deeds: what goes along can be a burden as well as a support.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","kootru","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:12"},"n:121":{"book":"naladiyar","num":121,"t":["துக்கத்துள் தூங்கித் துறவின்கட் சேர்கலா","மக்கள் பிணத்த சுடுகாடு - தொக்க","விலங்கிற்கும் புள்ளிற்கும் காடே புலன்கெட்ட","புல்லறி வாளர் வயிறு."],"p":"The burning ground is filled with the corpses of men that will not give themselves up to a self renouncing life, but oscillate amid sorrows; and the maws of perverted foolish men are a mere burning-ground for beasts and birds.","r":"The burning grounds are full of the corpses of people who linger in sorrow and do not turn to renunciation, and the bellies of senseless, petty-minded people are burning grounds for the beasts and birds they eat.","n":"The two halves mirror each other: people end in the burning ground, and the eater's belly becomes a burning ground for animals. The image says that the meat-eater carries a cremation ground inside.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","pulaal","pedhaimai"],"pos":[["mudaliar","The sorrow in which people linger is household life itself; Mudaliar also says the verse rejects meat-eating.","துக்கத்துள் - துன்பமாகிய இல்வாழ்க்கையில்."],["shanmuganar","The sorrow is the world's suffering in general, in which people are bewildered.","உலகத் துன்பத்துள் மயங்கித் துறவுநெறியைக் கொள்ளாத மக்களின்"]],"ch":"n:13"},"n:122":{"book":"naladiyar","num":122,"t":["இரும்பார்க்குங் காலராய் ஏதிலார்க் காளாய்க்","கரும்பார் கழனியுள் சேர்வர் - சுரும்பார்க்கும்","காட்டுளாய் வாழுஞ் சிவலும் குறும்பூமும்","கூட்டுளாய்க் கொண்டுவைப் பார்."],"p":"Their legs in iron bound, as slaves to alien lords they will till the black and barren soil, who snared and kept in cages partridges and quails, that dwell in wilds where beetles hum amid the flowers.","r":"Those who catch partridges and quails that live in the humming forest and keep them in cages will go, with irons clanking on their legs, as slaves to strangers into the fields.","n":"The punishment mirrors the crime: those who confined free birds are themselves confined and made to serve. Mudaliar reads கரும்பு ஆர் கழனி as sugarcane fields, and as an alternative as barren and fertile lands; Pope follows the barren reading. Shanmuganar places the punishment in the next birth; the verse doesn't say when.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["innaa","vinai","adimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:13"},"n:123":{"book":"naladiyar","num":123,"t":["அக்கேபோல் அங்கை யொழிய விரலழுகித்","துக்கத் தொழுநொய் எழுபவே - அக்கால்","அலவனைக் காதலித்துக் கான்முரித்துத் தின்ற","பழவினை வந்தடைந்தக் கால்."],"p":"Like fire their palms shall glow, their fingers rotaway, who loved in other times on crabs to feed, and broke their joints, what time the guilt of ‘olden deeds' comes home, and leprosy's fierce pangs assail.","r":"When the old deed of loving to eat crabs and breaking their legs comes home, people get the painful skin disease, white like conch beads, their fingers rotting away and only the palms left.","n":"Again the punishment copies the act: the crab's legs were broken, and the eater's fingers rot. Mudaliar and Shanmuganar read அக்கே போல் as like conch beads; Pope has palms that glow like fire.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","pulaal","noy"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:13"},"n:124":{"book":"naladiyar","num":124,"t":["நெருப்பழல் சேர்ந்தக்கால் நெய்போல் வதூஉம்","எரிப்பச்சுட் டெவ்வநோய் ஆக்கும் - பரப்பக்","கொடுவினைய ராகுவர் கோடாரும் கோடிக்","கடுவினைய ராகியார்ச் சார்ந்து."],"p":"Even things (soft and soothing) like ghee, when joined with the fierce heat of fire, will blaze and burn, and cause bitter anguish : so even upright men are perverted and give themselves up to deeds of utter evil, when they attach themselves to those whose deeds are evil.","r":"Even ghee, when it meets fire, burns and causes painful wounds; so even upright people turn and do widely cruel deeds when they join those of harsh deeds.","n":"Mudaliar notes the force of the image: ghee is what soothes burns, so the very thing that heals becomes what wounds. The verse makes company stronger than character.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","vinai","kutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:13"},"n:125":{"book":"naladiyar","num":125,"t":["பெரியவர் கேண்மை பிறைபோல நாளும்","வரிசை வரிசையா நந்தும் - வரிசையால்","வானூர் மதியம்போல் வைகலும் தேயுமே","தானே சிறியார் தொடர்பு."],"p":"Great men's intimate regard will daily grow in order due like the crescent moon. Mean men's alliance like the full moon that rides the sky daily by degrees dwindles away of itself.","r":"The friendship of the great grows day by day in due order like the crescent moon, while the bond with the small wanes by itself day by day like the full moon moving across the sky.","n":"The word தானே says the waning needs no cause: such a bond decays of itself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","perumai","sirumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:13"},"n:126":{"book":"naladiyar","num":126,"t":["சான்றோ ரெனமதித்துச் சார்ந்தாய்மன் சார்ந்தாய்க்குச்","சான்றாண்மை சார்ந்தார்கண் இல்லாயின் - சார்ந்தாய்கேள்","சாந்தகத் துண்டென்று செப்புத் திறந்தொருவன்","பாம்பகத்துக் கண்ட துடைத்து."],"p":"Thou didst attach thyself (to unworthy persons), saying, they are men of absolute integrity! If to thee who hast thus attached thyself, integrity in those thine intimates does not appear; hear, O thou who hast so attached thyself, it is as if one opened a casket, thinking it contained an odorous unguent, and saw a snake within.","r":"You attached yourself to someone, thinking him one of the worthy; if the worth is not in him, listen, it is like opening a casket expecting sandal paste and finding a snake inside.","n":"The repeated சார்ந்தாய் ('you who attached yourself') is a reproach: the fault lies partly with the one who did not examine before attaching.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","aaraaydhal","saandror"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:13"},"n:127":{"book":"naladiyar","num":127,"t":["யாஅர் ஒருவர் ஒருவர்தம் உள்ளத்தைத்","தேருந் துணைமை யுடையவர் - சாரல்","கனமணி நின்றிமைக்கும் நாடகேள் மக்கள்","மனம்வேறு செய்கையும் வேறு."],"p":"What single man has power to search and clearly know the inmost self of other men? Lord of the land where weighty gems glisten on mountain slope, O hear ! — Men's minds are otherwise, and otherwise their deeds.","r":"Who is there with the power to examine another's mind and know it? Listen, lord of the land where heavy gems glitter on the slopes: people's minds are one thing and their deeds another.","n":"The gap between mind and deed is offered as the reason minds cannot be known: behaviour is no evidence.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["manam","kurippu","aaraaydhal"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:13"},"n:128":{"book":"naladiyar","num":128,"t":["உள்ளத்தான் நள்ளா துறுதித் தொழிலராய்க்","கள்ளத்தான் நட்டார் கழிகேண்மை - தெள்ளிப்","புனற்செதும்பு நின்றலைக்கும் பூங்குன்ற நாட","மனத்துக்கண் மாசாய் விடும்."],"p":"Lord of the flowery hilly land where streams wash out and carry hither and thither (precious things) from the marshy land ! — The effusive friendship of those who do not attach themselves (to us) in heart, but perform certain friendly acts merely to strengthen their own position, and who form friendships guilefully, will issue in disappointment to the minds (of those who are intimate with them).","r":"The lavish friendship of those who do not join you at heart but act with an eye to their own gain, befriending you with guile, turns into a stain on the mind, lord of the flowering hills.","n":"Whose mind is stained is left open. Mudaliar says the friendship itself becomes a fault, Shanmuganar that it grows tainted and perishes, and Pope that it brings disappointment to those deceived.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","vanjam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:13"},"n:129":{"book":"naladiyar","num":129,"t":["ஓக்கிய ஒள்வாள்தன் ஒன்னார்கைப் பட்டக்கால்","ஊக்கம் அழிப்பதூஉம் மெய்யாகும் - ஆக்கம்","இருமையுஞ் சென்று சுடுதலால் நல்ல","கருமமே கல்லார்கண் தீர்வு."],"p":"If the glittering swords a man brandishes (be allowed to) fall into the hands of his foes, it will assuredly come about that he will thus destroy also his own power of action. So wealth (bestowed on the foolish) will go and burn up (merit accruing in) both worlds, and therefore the really good thing is to keep clear of the foolish.","r":"If a raised, gleaming sword falls into an enemy's hand it truly destroys one's strength; since ஆக்கம் among the unlearned burns one in both worlds, the good course is to get away from them.","n":"Pope offers a third sense, wealth bestowed on the foolish. On every reading the sword image says that what should protect you turns against you once it is in the wrong hands.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","kallaamai","natpu"],"pos":[["mudaliar","ஆக்கம் means joining the unlearned, and that association is what burns.","(கல்லார்கண்) ஆக்கம் - (பேதையரிடத்து) ஆகுதல், அஃதாவது சேர்தல்;"],["shanmuganar","ஆக்கம் is one's own growth, which friendship with the unlearned burns up in both worlds.","இம்மையிலும் மறுமையிலும் தம் வளர்ச்சியைச் சுட்டழிக்கு மாதலால்"]],"ch":"n:13"},"n:130":{"book":"naladiyar","num":130,"t":["மனைப்பாசம் கைவிடாய் மக்கட்கென் றேங்கி","எனைத்தூழி வாழ்தியோ, நெஞ்சே - எனைத்தும்","சிறுவரையே யாயினும் செய்தநன் றல்லால்","உறுபயனோ இல்லை உயிர்க்கு."],"p":"O mind! thou leavest not the bonds of home. How many cycles, pray, with thou live yearning still for children? Save the good he has done, however small its measure may be, there is no true result to any living soul.","r":"Heart, you won't let go of attachment to home, and you pine for the sake of children; for how many ages do you think you'll live? Apart from the good one has done, however small, there is no real gain for a living being.","n":"Both commentators read மனைப்பாசம் as attachment to one's wife. The verse doesn't call family evil; it says family is not the gain the soul carries.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pattru","makkal","aram","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:13"},"n:131":{"book":"naladiyar","num":131,"t":["குஞ்சி யழகும் கொடுந்தானைக் கோட்டழகும்","மஞ்சள் அழகும் அழகல்ல - நெஞ்சத்து","நல்லம்யாம் என்னும் நடுவு நிலைமையால்","கல்வி அழகே அழகு."],"p":"Beauty of locks, beauty of circling garments' folds, beauty of saffron tint: these are not beauty true. Integrity of soul that brings the conscience peace is learning's gift: that only is beauty true!","r":"The beauty of a man's hair, of a garment's curving border, of a saffron wash, is not beauty; the beauty of learning, with the fairness of heart that lets one say 'we are good', is beauty.","n":"Mudaliar notes that some read the three adornments as a man's (hair), both sexes' (garment) and a woman's (saffron), and so take the verse to commend learning for women as well as men; he says others give the verse other meanings too.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kalvi","naduvu"],"pos":[["mudaliar","True beauty is learning held together with conduct that lets one judge fairly that one has not strayed from virtue.","ஒழுக்கத்தோடு கல்வியுடைமையும் பெற்றிருத்தலே அழகு என்பது கருத்து."],["shanmuganar","Learning is true beauty because it produces the impartial quality of being good at heart.","நடுநிலைப் பண்பை உண்டாக்குவதால் கல்வி அழகே உண்மை அழகு"]],"ch":"n:14"},"n:132":{"book":"naladiyar","num":132,"t":["இம்மை பயக்குமால் ஈயக் குறைவின்றால்","தம்மை விளக்குமால் தாமுளராக் கேடின்றால்","எம்மை யுலகத்தும் யாம்காணேம் கல்விபோல்","மம்மர் அறுக்கும் மருந்து."],"p":"Since in this world it yields fruit; since given it grows not less ; since it makes men illustrious; since it perishes not as long as (its possessors) themselves exist; in any world we see not any medicine that, like learning, removes the delusions of sense.","r":"Learning yields fruit in this life, does not lessen when given, makes its owner shine, and does not perish while they live; in no world do we see a medicine like it for cutting away delusion.","n":"The disagreement is whether learning lasts as long as its owner or outlasts them through fame. Mudaliar notes that learning is called a medicine without delusion being called a disease, an incomplete metaphor.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","marundhu","pedhaimai"],"pos":[["mudaliar","Learning does not perish for as long as its owner lives.","தாம் உளர் ஆ - தாம் உயிருள்ளவராக வாழுமளவும். கேடு இன்று - அழிதலில்லை."],["shanmuganar","Learning makes its owner live on forever through fame and does not perish.","தம்மைப்புகழால் என்றும் உள்ளவராக்கி அழியாது நிற்கும்"]],"ch":"n:14"},"n:133":{"book":"naladiyar","num":133,"t":["களர்நிலத் துப்பிறந்த உப்பினைச் சான்றோர்","விளைநிலத்து நெல்லின் விழுமிதாக் கொள்வர்","கடைநிலத்தோ ராயினும், கற்றறிந் தோரைத்","தலைநிலத்து வைக்கப் படும்."],"p":"The excellent regard the salt produced in brackish ground as choicer than the Nel from fertile soil. It is fitting to place in the first rank the learned wise, though (sprung) from the lowest origin.","r":"The worthy prize salt born in brackish ground above rice grown in fertile fields; so the learned, though of the lowest origin, are to be set in the highest place.","n":"The image works because the place of origin is called low only by habit: salt from barren soil is worth more than grain from good soil. Mudaliar takes கடைநிலத்தோர் as those born in a low caste.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","pirappu","kudi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:14"},"n:134":{"book":"naladiyar","num":134,"t":["வைப்புழிக் கோட்படா வாய்த்தீயின் கேடில்லை","மிக்க சிறப்பின் அரசர் செறின்வவ்வார்","எச்சம் எனஒருவன் மக்கட்குச் செய்வன","விச்சைமற் றல்ல பிற."],"p":"It cannot be taken from its place of deposit; it does not perish anywhere by fire; if kings of surpassing grandeur are angry they cannot take it away; (and therefore) what any man should provide for his children as a legacy is learning. Other things are not (real wealth).","r":"It cannot be taken from where it is kept, it is not lost when given to a fit person, and kings angered by its eminence cannot seize it; so what a man should leave his children as their inheritance is learning and nothing else.","n":"Each clause is a way ordinary wealth is lost: theft, giving, a king's anger. Both commentators read வாய்த்து ஈயின் as giving to a fit recipient; Pope reads வாய்த் தீயின் and has learning not perish by fire.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","makkal","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:14"},"n:135":{"book":"naladiyar","num":135,"t":["கல்வி கரையில கற்பவர் நாள்சில","மெல்ல நினைக்கின் பிணிபல - தெள்ளிதன்","ஆராய்ந் தமைவுடைய கற்பவே நீரொழியப்","பாலுண் குருகின் தெரிந்து."],"p":"Learning hath no bounds, the learners' days are few. If you think calmly diseases many wait around! With clear discrimination learn what is meet for you, like the swan that leaving the water drinks the milk.","r":"Learning has no shore, learners have few days, and, thought over calmly, many illnesses; so the wise learn with clear discernment what is fitting, like the bird that drinks the milk and leaves the water.","n":"The argument for selection is arithmetic: boundless material, a short life, and illnesses that shorten it further. Mudaliar names the bird as the swan and notes the belief that it can separate milk from water.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","therithal","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:14"},"n:136":{"book":"naladiyar","num":136,"t":["தோணி இயக்குவான் தொல்லை வருணத்துக்","காணிற் கடைப்பட்டான் என்றிகழார் - காணாய்","அவன்துணையா ஆறுபோ யற்றேநூல் கற்ற","மகன்துணையா நல்ல கொளல்."],"p":"As none contemn the ferryman, by old caste rule to lowest rank assigned, but cross the stream by help he lends; so take thou teachings good and wise by help of him who is the learned man.","r":"No one despises the ferryman for being of the lowest of the old castes, but crosses the river with his help; so take what is good from a man who has learned books, whatever his birth.","n":"The argument is from ordinary practice: people already accept help from the low-born when they need it, so they should accept teaching on the same terms. The verse speaks of using the learned man's help, not of honouring him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","pirappu","kelvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:14"},"n:137":{"book":"naladiyar","num":137,"t":["தவலருந் தொல்கேள்வித் தன்மை யுடையார்","இகலிலர் எஃகுடையார் தம்முள் குழீஇ","நகலின் இனிதாயின் காண்பாம் அகல்வானத்(து)","உம்ப ருறைவார் பதி."],"p":"We shall see whether any greater bliss is found in the city inhabited by the dwellers in the ample heaven than is felt when men of natures formed by old imperishable lore, from rivalries exempt keen as tempered steel, meet together and laugh.","r":"If the city of those who live in the wide heaven is sweeter than when people of sound and ancient learning, free of rivalry and sharp of mind, gather and laugh together, then we'll go and see it.","n":"The conditional is a polite refusal: Mudaliar reads it as saying that such joy is found on earth and not in heaven, so there is no wish to go.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","inbam","inam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:14"},"n:138":{"book":"naladiyar","num":138,"t":["கனைகடல் தண்சேர்ப்ப கற்றறிந்தார் கேண்மை","நுனியின் கரும்புதின் றற்றே - நுனிநீக்கித்","துரி஢ன்தின் றன்ன தகைத்தரோ பண்பிலா","ஈரமி லாளர் தொடர்பு."],"p":"diminishes in sweetness. Lord of the cool shore of the resounding sea intimacy with learned people is like eating sugar cane from the (tender, juicy) tip; association with graceless. sapless men is like leaving the (tender) tip and eating it from the (hard, dry) root.","r":"Friendship with the learned is like eating sugarcane from the tip, sweeter as one goes; association with graceless, unfeeling people is like eating it from the root end, lord of the cool shore of the roaring sea.","n":"The cane is the same in both cases; only the direction differs. So the verse is about how a bond develops over time, not how it begins.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","kalvi","panpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:14"},"n:139":{"book":"naladiyar","num":139,"t":["கல்லாரே யாயினும் கற்றாரைச் சேர்ந்தொழுகின்","நல்லறிவு நாளுந் தலைப்படுவர் - தொல்சிறப்பின்","ஒண்ணிறப் பாதிரிப்பூச் சேர்தலால் புத்தோடு","தண்ணீர்க்குத் தான்பயந் தாங்கு."],"p":"","r":"Even the unlearned, if they keep company with the learned, gain good sense day by day, as a new pot gives its water the scent of the bright, long-prized pathiri flowers placed in it.","n":"Mudaliar explains the image: the new pot is the unlearned person, the flowers the learned, and the scent good sense. The water gains the fragrance without becoming flowers, so the change is real but borrowed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","kalvi","kallaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:14"},"n:140":{"book":"naladiyar","num":140,"t":["அலகுசால் கற்பின் அறிவுநூல் கல்லா(து)","உலகநூ லோதுவ தெல்லாம் - கலகல","கூஉந் துணையல்லால் கொண்டு தடுமாற்றம்","போஒம் துணையறிவா ரில்."],"p":"If men leaving works of wisdom, that contain well-weighed instruction, unstudied, devote themselves to the recitation of mere worldly literature, they will acquire a store of empty high-sounding words, but not that wisdom by means of which mental confusion (that treats unreal things as real) is removed. ------------","r":"Of all the many kinds of learning, studying worldly books while leaving the books of wisdom unread amounts only to clamour; there is no one who knows how to make confusion leave by that means.","n":"Mudaliar names the worldly books as logic and grammar, and takes தடுமாற்றம் as the stumbling through births; Pope takes it as the mind's confusion that treats unreal things as real. The verse does not say worldly learning is false, only that it cannot do this.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["meyyunarvu","kalvi","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:14"},"n:141":{"book":"naladiyar","num":141,"t":["உடுக்கை உலறி உடம்பழிந்தக் கண்ணும்","குடிப்பிறப் பாளர்தம் கொள்கையில் குன்றார்","இடுக்கண் தலைவந்தக் கண்ணும் அரிமா","கொடிப்புல் கறிக்குமோ மற்று."],"p":"Though their clothes may be old and their body worn with want, men of noble birth diminish nothing of their due observances. Will the lion nibble the creeping grass although sorest need should assail him?","r":"Even when their clothes are in rags and their bodies wasted, the well-born do not fall from their principles; would a lion, however hard pressed, chew creeping grass?","n":"The lion is chosen because grass is not merely beneath it but against its nature, so the verse makes principle a matter of what one is. Mudaliar records a variant reading, வரிமா, which would make the animal a tiger.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","ozhukkam","inmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:15"},"n:142":{"book":"naladiyar","num":142,"t":["சான்றாண்மை சாயல் ஒழுக்கம் இநவ்முன்றும்","வான்றோய் குடிப்பிறந்தார்க் கல்லது - வான்றோயும்","மைதவழ் வேற்ப. படாஅ பெருஞ்செல்வம்","எய்தியக் கண்ணும் பிறர்க்கு."],"p":"Lord of the hills traversed by clouds that touch the heavens! true excellence, and dignity, and good conduct, — these three things belong to men of race that touches heaven, and not to others, even though they may have acquired great wealth.","r":"Fullness of character, gentleness and good conduct belong only to those born of a family that touches the sky; they do not come to others even when they gain great wealth, lord of the hills where clouds creep.","n":"The point is aimed at wealth: money cannot buy these qualities. Mudaliar glosses சாயல் as mildness, restraint in mind, word and body, and notes that some take it as restraint from blame and sin.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","saandraanmai","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:15"},"n:143":{"book":"naladiyar","num":143,"t":["இருக்கை எழலும் எதிர்செலவும் ஏனை","விடுப்ப ஒழிதலோ டின்ன - குடிப்பிறந்தார்","குன்றா வொழுக்கமாக் கொண்டார் கயவரோடு","ஒன்றா வுணரற்பாற் றன்று."],"p":"","r":"Rising from one's seat, going out to meet, seeing a guest off, and the like are what the well-born have taken as unfailing conduct; they are not to be thought of as one with the base.","n":"Only Shanmuganar's commentary is aligned here, and the readings of விடுப்ப ஒழிதல் (as seeing off) and of whom the courtesies are for (elders who visit) are his.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kudi","ozhukkam","kayamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:15"},"n:144":{"book":"naladiyar","num":144,"t":["நல்லவை செய்யின் இயல்பாகும் தீயவை","பல்லவர் தூற்றம் பழியாகும் - எல்லாம்","உணரும் குடிப்பிறப்பின் ஊதிய மென்னோ,","புணரும் ஒருவர்க் கெனின்?"],"p":"If men (of noble birth) do good things it is natural to them. As to evil things (to commit these is impossible to them); for this would be guilt which many would bruit abroad. What greater good then can accrue to men than high-birth, if it be their lot, to which the perception of all (these things) belongs!","r":"If the well-born do good, it is called their nature; if they do evil, it becomes blame spread by many; what then does a person gain from the all-knowing high birth that falls to them?","n":"Pope reads the same question the other way, as praise: what greater good can come to anyone than high birth? So the two readings reach opposite conclusions. Mudaliar's commentary isn't aligned for this quatrain, so this can't be recorded as a contest between commentators.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kudi","pazhi","payan"],"pos":[["shanmuganar","The closing question is ironic: high birth brings no gain of its own, since good is credited to birth and evil is loudly blamed.","எல்லாம் உணர்ந்த உயர்குடியில் பிறந்ததால் அவருக்குச் சொந்த நன்மை என்னவோ?"]],"ch":"n:15"},"n:145":{"book":"naladiyar","num":145,"t":["கல்லாமை அச்சம் கயவர் தொழிலச்சம்","சொல்லாமை யுள்ளுமோர் சோர்வச்சம் - எல்லாம்","இரப்பார்க்கொன் றீயாமை அச்சம் மரத்தாரிம்","மாணாக் குடிப்பிறந் தார்."],"p":"Dread of unlearned ignorance; dread of the work that base men do; dread of forgetful slip in words which one must not utter; dread of not giving to those that ask: those who are born of a race not so distinguished by conscientious fears are as trees.","r":"Fear of being unlearned, fear of base work, fear of a slip in speech, and fear of not giving to those who ask: those born of an undistinguished family who lack all these are like wood.","n":"These fears are presented as marks of good birth, a moral sensitivity. The third fear is read two ways: Pope takes it as dread of a careless word, and Shanmuganar as the dismay of being unable to speak in an assembly of the good.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["acham","kudi","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:15"},"n:146":{"book":"naladiyar","num":146,"t":["இனநன்மை இன்சொல்ஒன் றீதல்மற் றேனை","மனநன்மை என்றிவை யெல்லாம் - கனமணி","முத்தோ டிமைக்கு முழங்குவரித் தண்சேர்ப்ப","இற்பிறந்தார் கண்ணே யுள."],"p":"Lord of the roaring salt-sea's cool shore, where gleam rare gems with pearls!Association with the good, pleasant speech, a liberal hand, and purity of mind, —all these are only found among the nobly born.","r":"Good company, sweet speech, giving, and goodness of mind are all found only among the well-born, lord of the cool shore of the roaring sea where great gems gleam with pearls.","n":"The claim is exclusive: these qualities are found only among the well-born, not merely more often.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","inchol","eegai","inam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:15"},"n:147":{"book":"naladiyar","num":147,"t":["செய்கை யழிந்து சிதல்மண்டிற் றாயினும்","பெய்யா ஒருசிறை பேரில் உடைத்தாகும்","எவ்வ முழந்தக் கடைத்துங் குடிப்பிறந்தார்","செய்வர் செயற்பா லவை."],"p":"Though its frame-work has perished, and thronging white ants infest it, in a spacious mansion some room will still afford shelter from the rain. Thus, although want annoys them, the nobly born even yet will do what should be done.","r":"A great house whose woodwork has decayed and which white ants have overrun still keeps one corner where the rain does not fall; so even when hardship presses, the well-born do what is theirs to do.","n":"The image does not claim the house is intact, only that some shelter remains; the well-born, too, can do less but not nothing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","inmai","oppuravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:15"},"n:148":{"book":"naladiyar","num":148,"t":["ஒருபுடை பாம்பு கொளினும் ஒருபுடை","அங்கண்மா ஞாலம் விளக்குறூஉம் - திங்கள்போல்","செல்லாமை செவ்வனேர் நிற்பினும் ஒப்புரவிற்கு","ஒல்கார் குடிப்பிறந் தார்."],"p":"Like the moon which affords light to the fair and spacious earth with one side, while the dragon holds the other, the nobly born do not become remiss in works of seemly benevolence, though poverty (inability) stand fronting them.","r":"Like the moon that lights the wide fair earth with one side while the serpent holds the other, the well-born do not slacken in serving others even when poverty stands squarely before them.","n":"The serpent is the eclipse, as Shanmuganar notes. The last line shares its words, ஒப்புரவிற்கு ஒல்கார், with Kural 218.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oppuravu","kudi","inmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:15"},"n:149":{"book":"naladiyar","num":149,"t":["செல்லா விடத்தும் குடிப்பிறந்தார் செய்வன","செல்லிடத்தும் செய்யார் சிறியவர் - புல்வாய்","பருமம் பொறுப்பினும் பாய்பரி மாபோல்","பொருமுரண் ஆற்றுதல் இன்று."],"p":"Men of mean descent, even when all goes well with them, will not perform the good deeds that the well-born will do, even when things do not go well with them. Though the antelope should bear a pillion, it rushes not to war like the prancing charger.","r":"What the well-born do even when they cannot, the small will not do even when they can; an antelope may bear a saddle, but it cannot do battle like a leaping horse.","n":"The saddle is outward equipment, so the verse says that giving the small the means does not give them the nature.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","sirumai","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:15"},"n:150":{"book":"naladiyar","num":150,"t":["எற்றொன்றும் இல்லா இடத்தும் குடிப்பிறந்தார்","அற்றத்தற் சேர்ந்தார்க் கசைவிடத் தூற்றாவர்","அற்றக் கடைத்தும் அகல்யா றகழ்ந்தக்கால்","தெற்றெனத் தெண்ணீர் படும்."],"p":"The nobly born, even when destitute, are props in time of feebleness to the needy ones that draw nigh to them. So, when the wide river is dry, if you dig in its bed, forth with clear waters gush out. -------------","r":"Even when they have nothing at all, the well-born are a prop to those who come to them in need, as a wide river, when dried up, yields clear water at once if you dig its bed.","n":"The river image implies that the means of giving is still there below the surface; the well-born have to be dug for, not asked from.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","eegai","inmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:15"},"n:151":{"book":"naladiyar","num":151,"t":["அங்கண் விசும்பின் அகனிலாப் பாரிக்கும்","திங்களும் சான்றோரும் ஒப்பர்மன் - திங்கள்","மறுவாற்றும், சான்றோரஃ தாற்றார் தெருமந்து","தேய்வர் ஒருமா சுறின்."],"p":"The moon that diffuses light through heaven's fair realms, and truly worthy men are alike: yet that endures a spot, while the truly worthy endure it. not; — perplexed and sad they pine away if but one stain appear.","r":"The noble resemble the moon that spreads its light across the sky, except that the moon carries its spot while the noble, if one stain falls on them, pine away and waste.","n":"The comparison turns into a contrast: the moon is the lesser because it puts up with its blemish. Sensitivity to stain is made the mark of superiority.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saandror","maanam","kutram"],"pos":[["mudaliar","Because the moon is specified as the moon in the wide sky, the 'noble' of the chapter title are to be taken as the high-born.","'மேன்மக்கள்' உயர் குடிப் பிறப்புடையார் என்பது குறிப்பிற் கொள்க"]],"ch":"n:16"},"n:152":{"book":"naladiyar","num":152,"t":["இசையும் எனினும் இசையா தெனினும்","வசைதீர எண்ணுவர் சான்றோர் - விசையின்","நா஢மா உளங்கிழித்த அம்பினின் தீதோ,","அரிமாப் பிழைப்பெய்த கோல்?"],"p":"Whether success attend, or do not attend the work, the excellent will even ponder blameless ends.—Is the shaft that missed the lion worse than the arrow sent forth, that with its impulse pierced the jackal's heart?","r":"Whether the aim succeeds or not, the worthy plan only what carries no blame: is the arrow that missed a lion worse than the one that tore open a jackal's breast?","n":"Two ideas sit together: the aim must be blameless, and it must be high. A noble failure outranks a mean success.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saandror","muyarchi","pazhi","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:16"},"n:153":{"book":"naladiyar","num":153,"t":["நரம்பெழுந்து நல்கூர்ந்தா ராயினும் சான்றோர்","குரம்பெழுந்து குற்றங்கொ ண்டேறார் - உரங்கவறா","உள்ளமெனும் நாரினால் கட்டி உளவரையால்","செய்வர் செயற்பா லவை."],"p":"The excellent though emaciated and poor, do not transgress the limits of virtue and commit evil. With wisdom for the pillar, with perseverance as the band, they bind (the mind); and as long as they live they do what it behoves them to do.","r":"Though poverty leaves them all sinew, the worthy do not cross the bounds into wrongdoing; binding the mind with understanding and resolve, they do what ought to be done as far as their means allow.","n":"The last phrase sets a measure: duty is scaled to what one has, not abandoned. 185 ends with the same words, செய்வர் செயற்பாலவை.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saandror","inmai","ozhukkam","ookkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:16"},"n:154":{"book":"naladiyar","num":154,"t":["செல்வுழிக் கண்ணொருநாள் காணினும் சான்றவர்","தொல்வழிக் கேண்மையிற் றோன்றப் புரிந்தியாப்பர்","நல்வரை நாட. சிலநாள் அடிப்படின்","கல்வரையும் உண்டாம் நெறி."],"p":"Lord of the land of goodly hills ! —If trodden for a few days a path is formed over even the craggy hill; so excellent persons, though they have seen (worthy) men only for one day, as they were travelling, will bind them to their soul, exhibiting all the marks of an ancient friendship.","r":"If the worthy see someone just once on the road, they bind that person to them as if in a friendship of long standing, just as a few days' treading makes a path even over rock.","n":"The image cuts two ways: a path on rock needs repeated treading, yet the worthy form the bond at one meeting. Pope adds that those so bound are themselves worthy; the Tamil does not say so.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saandror","natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:16"},"n:155":{"book":"naladiyar","num":155,"t":["புல்லா வெழுத்திற் பொருளில் வறுங்கோட்டி","கல்லா ஒருவன் உரைப்பவும் கண்ணோடி","நல்லார் வருந்தியும் கேட்பரே, மற்றவன்","பல்லாருள் நாணல் பரிந்து."],"p":"Even when one speaks who has an ungrammatical knowledge of the letter, but not of the meaning who is of a low (empty) school, and is unlearned the good with kindly compassion will listen, though it is pain to them, being grieved that he should be put to shame in the presence of many.","r":"When an unlearned man who knows neither grammar nor meaning speaks in an empty gathering, the good listen out of kindly regard, though it pains them, to spare him shame before many.","n":"Mudaliar glosses கண்ணோடி as தாட்சிண்யம், courteous indulgence. The good endure bad speech not for its sake but for the speaker's face.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kannottam","kallaamai","porai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:16"},"n:156":{"book":"naladiyar","num":156,"t":["கடித்துக் கரும்பினைக் கண்தகர நூறி","இடித்துநீர் கொள்ளினும் இன்சுவைத்தே யாகும்","வடுப்பட வைதிறந்தக் கண்ணும் குடிப்பிறந்தார்","கூறார்தம் வாயிற் சிதைந்து."],"p":"Although you bite the sugar-cane, crush it till its joints are broken, grind it, and express its juice, it still will be sweet. The highly born, even when men have passed by abusing them so as to wound, never lose their self-respect so as to utter from their mouth (words of abuse).","r":"Sugar-cane bitten, crushed at the joints and pounded for its juice is still sweet; so the well-born, even when abused so as to wound, do not lose themselves and speak in kind.","n":"Shanmuganar reads the close as positive, that they keep speaking sweetly; the Tamil says only that they do not speak as people undone.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","porai","inchol"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:16"},"n:157":{"book":"naladiyar","num":157,"t":["கள்ளார், கள் ளுண்ணார், கடிவ கடிந்தெரிணஇ,","எள்ளிப் பிறரை இகழ்ந்துரையார், - தள்ளியும்","வாயில்பொய் கூறார், வடுவறு காட்சியார்","சாயிற் பரிவ திலர்."],"p":"They defraud not, drink not palm-wine, shun what is forbidden, never despise and speak contemptuously of their neighbours, nor even forgetfully do they utter anything untrue with their mouth : these men of faultless vision grieve not though they fall into distress.","r":"Those of faultless vision do not steal, do not drink, shun what is forbidden, do not scorn others, and never lie even by a slip; and if they decline in fortune, they do not grieve.","n":"The list of abstentions ends in an inner state: equanimity in decline is presented as the fruit of clean conduct.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ozhukkam","kalavu","kal","samanilai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:16"},"n:158":{"book":"naladiyar","num":158,"t":["பிறர்மறை யின்கண் செவிடாய்த் திறனறிந்து","ஏதிலா ரிற்கண், குருடனாய்த் தீய","புறங்கூற்றின் மூகையாய் நிற்பானேல், யாதும்","அறங்கூற வேண்டா அவற்கு."],"p":"Deaf to others' secrets, blind to his neighbour's wife, dumb to evil backbiting—if, knowing what is befitting, a man thus abides, it is not necessary to teach him any virtuous precepts.","r":"One who is deaf to others' secrets, blind to a neighbour's wife and dumb in evil backbiting, knowing what is fitting, needs no teaching of virtue at all.","n":"Mudaliar reads 'if he stands so' as stressing how rare this is. Virtue here is a matter of restraint of the senses, not of instruction.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","piranil","purangooral","adakkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:16"},"n:159":{"book":"naladiyar","num":159,"t":["பன்னாளும் சென்றக்கால் பண்பிலார் தம்முழை","என்னானும் வேண்டுப என்றிகழ்ப - என்னானும்","வேண்டினும் நன்றுமற் றென்று விமுமியோர்","காண்டொறும் செய்வர் சிறப்பு."],"p":"They who are destitute of kindly courtesy, even after the lapse of many days, will contemn (those that visit them), saying, ‘they will ask something of us'. The excellent will treat them with distinction whenever they see them, saying, ‘if they ask something of us, it is well'.","r":"Those without good character scorn a frequent visitor, thinking he wants something from them; the excellent, thinking it good even if he asks, honour him every time they see him.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panpu","eegai","virundhu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:16"},"n:160":{"book":"naladiyar","num":160,"t":["உடையார் இவரென் றொருதலையாப் பற்றிக்","கடையாயார் பின்சென்று வாழ்வா - உடைய","பிலந்தலைப் பட்டது போலாதே, நல்ல","குலந்தலைப் பட்ட விடத்து."],"p":"The lowest sort of men say, there are men of property,’— cling to them steadily, follow them, and so flourish. Is it not as when a mine of treasure has been found, when men of good lineage have become our friends. --------------","r":"The base settle on the rich as their one hope and live by trailing after them; yet meeting people of good family is like coming upon a treasure cave.","n":"The contrast is between attaching oneself to money and to lineage. The verse prizes good birth as company; it does not say what the treasure consists of.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","sirumai","selvam","inam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:16"},"n:161":{"book":"naladiyar","num":161,"t":["பொறுப்பரென் றெண்ணிப் புரைதீர்ந்தார் மாட்டும்","வெறுப்பன செய்யாமை வேண்டும் - வெறுத்தபின்","ஆர்க்கும் அருவி யணிமலை நன்னாட","பேர்க்குதல் யார்க்கும் அரிது."],"p":"Lord of the pleasant land whose hills resounding water-falls adorn ! — You may not even to faultless men do things that enkindle wrath; for when their wrath is once kindled, it is hard for any one to change their mood.","r":"Do not do what offends even the faultless, counting on them to forgive; once they turn away, no one can undo it.","n":"The warning targets presumption: the very forbearance of the great is what tempts the offence. Pope reads the last line as the great one's mood being hard to change; both commentators read it as the resulting harm being impossible to remove.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","porai","kedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:17"},"n:162":{"book":"naladiyar","num":162,"t":["பொன்னே கொடுத்தும் புணர்தற் கா஢யாரைக்","கொன்னே தலைக்கூடப் பெற்றிருந்தும் - அன்னோ","பயனில் பொழுதாக் கழிப்பரே, நல்ல","நயமில் அறிவி னவர்."],"p":"Although those whose (pretended) wisdom is without moral excellence have gained access to men to whom approach is difficult even by gifts of gold alas! they pass away (the precious moments) as mere waste time.","r":"People of loveless cleverness who have freely gained the company of the great, whom others cannot reach even by paying gold, alas, let the time pass without profit.","n":"Mudaliar takes நயம் as delight or relish, so the fault is an inability to enjoy and draw on what the great offer, not ill will.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","pedhaimai","kelvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:17"},"n:163":{"book":"naladiyar","num":163,"t":["அவமதிப்பும் ஆன்ற மதிப்பும் இரண்டும்","மிகைமக்க ளான்மதிக்கற் பால - நயமுணராக்","கையறியா மாக்கள் இழிப்பும் எடுத்தேத்தும்","வையார் வடித்தநூ லார்."],"p":"Both disesteem and thorough esteem are in the class of things that depend on the estimation of the great. Those who have a discriminating knowledge of true science, lay no stress on either the abuse or the fulsome praise of man who comprehend not moral principles, and know not the rules of propriety.","r":"Disesteem and high esteem alike are for the superior to judge; those versed in refined learning take no account of the scorn or the extravagant praise of the base who know neither grace nor propriety.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","pugazh","kayamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:17"},"n:164":{"book":"naladiyar","num":164,"t":["விரிநிற நாகம் விடருள தேனும்","உருமின் கடுஞ்சினம் சேணின்றும் உட்கும்","அருமை யுடைய அரண்சேர்ந்தும் உய்யார்","பெருமை யுடையார் செறின்."],"p":"The serpent rich of hue, though it dwell in the mountain-cleft, from far is frighted by the thunderbolt's fierce wrath; so men escape not though hid in strongholds hard to reach, when those great in virtue are wroth.","r":"A brightly hooded snake living in a crevice still dreads the thunder's fury from afar; so those who have angered the great do not escape even inside a fortress hard to reach.","n":"Pope reads them as those great in virtue, closer to Shanmuganar. On Mudaliar's reading the fortress is a defence against royal power; on the others, against a moral force no wall can stop.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","aran","vekuli"],"pos":[["mudaliar","The 'great' whose anger cannot be escaped are kings.","பெருமையுடையார் செறின்- பெருமை யுடைய‌ அர‌ச‌ர் கோப்பிப்பாராயின்"],["shanmuganar","The great are elders in general, angered by someone who has wronged them.","பிழை செய்தவரைப் பெரியவர் சினந்தால்"]],"ch":"n:17"},"n:165":{"book":"naladiyar","num":165,"t":["எம்மை யறிந்திலிர் எம்போல்வார் இல்லென்று","தம்மைத்தாம் கொள்வது கோளன்று - தம்மை","அரியரா நோக்கி அறனறியும் சான்றோர்","பெரியராக் கொள்வது கோள்."],"p":"‘You know not all our worth, for equals have we none:’ when self thus estimates itself, this is not ‘esteem’ ! When perfect men, proficients in virtue, regard any as dear, and esteem them as great, this is ‘esteem’ !","r":"Saying 'you do not know us; there is none like us' is not esteem; esteem is when the worthy who know virtue see one as rare and accept one as great.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","saandror","panivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:17"},"n:166":{"book":"naladiyar","num":166,"t":["நளிகடல் தண்சேர்ப்ப. நாணிழல் போல","விளியும் சிறியவர் கேண்மை - விளிவின்றி","அல்கு நிழற்போல் அகன்றகன் றோடுமே","தொல்புக ழாளர் தொடர்பு."],"p":"Lord of the great sea’scool margin !—Friendships formed with mean men dwindling die like shadows of the morn; friendship of men of old renown as shadows of the after-day will lengthen out and grow.","r":"The friendship of the small dwindles like the morning shadow, while the bond of those of long renown keeps lengthening like the shadow of the declining day.","n":"Mudaliar offers two timings for the shadows (dawn against noon, or forenoon against afternoon); the point, shrinking against growing, is the same either way.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","sirumai","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:17"},"n:167":{"book":"naladiyar","num":167,"t":["மன்னர் திருவும் மகளிர் எழினலமும்","துன்னியார் துய்ப்பர் தகல்வேண்டா - துன்னிக்","குழைகொண்டு தாழ்ந்த குளிர்மர மெல்லாம்","உழைதங்கண் சென்றார்க் கொருங்கு."],"p":"Those who press their suit, fitness a part, enjoy the wealth of kings, and the charms of maidens fair; so all the cool (shady) trees that droop earthward with thick foliage, give room to those that thronging seek their shelter.","r":"Those who keep close enjoy the wealth of kings and the beauty of women, without needing to be worthy; just as cool trees heavy with leaf give shelter alike to all who come near.","n":"The verse states a fact about proximity rather than merit. Under this chapter's heading it may counsel staying near the great, but neither commentator draws that out.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mannar","selvam","nalam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:17"},"n:168":{"book":"naladiyar","num":168,"t":["தெரியத் தெரியும் தெரிவிலார் கண்ணும்","பிரியப் பெரும்படர் நோய்செய்யும் - பெரிய","உலவா இருங்கழிச் சேர்ப்பயார் மாட்டும்","கலவாமை கோடி யுறும்."],"p":"Severance from even those who have no under standing [lit. no understanding that understands (so as) to understand] causes great and spreading sorrow. — Lord of the shore of the great, unfailing, swelling bay !—To be intimate with none is ten million times the best.","r":"Parting even from those who lack discernment brings great, lingering sorrow; so, lord of the long brimming backwaters, not mixing with anyone at all is ten million times better.","n":"This is the renunciant's conclusion drawn from the pain of attachment, and it cuts against the next chapter's praise of good company. Mudaliar spells out the comparison: not joining is better than joining and then parting.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","pattru","thuravu","natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:17"},"n:169":{"book":"naladiyar","num":169,"t":["கல்லாது போகிய நாளும் பெரியவர்கண்","செல்லாது வைகிய வைகலும் - ஒல்வ","கொடாஅ தொழிந்த பகலும் உரைப்பின்","படாஅவாம் பண்புடையார் கண்."],"p":"Days gone by without learning, those passed without any intercourse with the great, those spent without giving what is fitting;— in the case of the excellent, if you tell them over, none such occur.","r":"Days passed without learning, without going to the great, without giving what one can: if you count them, none such are found among people of good character.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panpu","kalvi","eegai","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:17"},"n:170":{"book":"naladiyar","num":170,"t":["பெரியார் பெருமை சிறுதகைமை ஒன்றிற்","குரியா ருரிமை யடக்கம் - தெரியுங்கால்","செல்வ முடையாருஞ் செல்வரே தற்சேர்ந்தார்","அல்லல் களைப வெனின்."],"p":"The greatness of the great is [the quality of littleness (in their own sight), i. e.] humility; the (real) acquisition of those who have acquired (any). one (science) is modest self-restraint. If you rightly understand things, those possessors of wealth only are really wealthy who relieve the wants of those that approach them (as suppliants). ----------------","r":"The greatness of the great is humility; the fitting quality of one who owns any one excellence is restraint; and, rightly seen, the wealthy are wealthy only if they relieve the distress of those who come to them.","n":"Mudaliar takes ஒன்றிற்கு as any one of learning, wealth or manliness, and records another reading, 'to the Supreme'. Pope narrows it to one science.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panivu","adakkam","selvam","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:17"},"n:171":{"book":"naladiyar","num":171,"t":["அறியாப் பருவத் தடங்காரோ டொன்றி","நெறியல்ல செய்தொழுகி யவ்வும் - நெறியறிந்த","நற்சார்வு சாரக் கெடுமே வெயில்முறுகப்","புற்பனிப் பற்றுவிட் டாங்கு."],"p":"In youth unwise, though men consort with haughty ones and walk in lawless ways; yet, when they join with those that know the righteous path, their faults shall melt away as dew from off the grass when sunbeams scorch.","r":"Faults committed in thoughtless youth, keeping company with the unruly and going astray, vanish once one joins good people who know the right way, as dew lets go of the grass when the sun grows hot.","n":"Mudaliar names these faults the faults of association (சகவாச தோஷங்கள்). Company is both the cause of the wrong and its cure.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","kutram","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:18"},"n:172":{"book":"naladiyar","num":172,"t":["அறிமின் அறநெறி அஞ்சுமின் கூற்றம்","பொறுமின் பிறர்கடுஞ்சொல் போற்றுமின் வஞ்சம்","வெறுமின் வினைதீயார் கேண்மை எஞ்ஞான்றும்","பெறுமின் பெரியார்வாய்ச் சொல்."],"p":"Know virtue's path ! Dread death! Beat others' words severe! Beware ye practise no deceit! Friendship detest with men of evil deeds! Daily get gain of words that fall from greatmen's lips!","r":"Know the path of virtue; dread Death; bear the harsh words of others; guard against deceit; shun friendship with evildoers; and always take in the words of the great.","n":"Mudaliar reads போற்றுமின் வஞ்சம் as preventing deceit from arising in oneself, and allows வாய்ச்சொல் to mean the blessing of the great.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","kootru","porai","inam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:18"},"n:173":{"book":"naladiyar","num":173,"t":["அடைந்தார்ப் பிரிவும் அரும்பிணியும் கேடும்","உடங்குடம்பு கொண்டார்க் குறலால் - தொடங்கிப்","பிறப்பின்னா தென்றுணரும் பேரறிவி னாரை","உறப்புணர்க அம்மாஎன் நெஞ்சு."],"p":"Severance of close-joined friends and sore disease and death, all these combined hap to those that have assumed a human body: so the truly wise have felt that birth, from the very first, is bitterness. Ah! soul, cleave close to these!","r":"Since parting from those one is close to, grievous illness and ruin come together to all who take a body, O my heart, cling to those of great understanding who see that birth is bitter from its start.","n":"The speaker addresses his own heart. The reason for seeking the wise is not their virtue but their insight into birth as suffering.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","inam","nilaiyaamai","pirivu"],"pos":[["mudaliar","கேடு is harm or misfortune in general.","கேடும் - தீங்கும்"],["shanmuganar","கேடு is death, completing the list of separation, illness and death.","கொடிய பிணியும் சாவும்"]],"ch":"n:18"},"n:174":{"book":"naladiyar","num":174,"t":["இறப்ப நினையுங்கால் இன்னா தெனினும்","பிறப்பினை யாரும் முனியார் - பிறப்பினுள்","பண்பாற்றும் நெஞ்சத் தவர்களோ டெஞ்ஞான்றும்","நண்பாற்றி நட்கப் பெறின்."],"p":"Though when you ponder it, it is surpassing: bitterness, none hate (this mortal) birth, if in (this mortal) birth they ever perform friendly acts to men whose hearts are set on noble excellence, and gain their friendship.","r":"Though birth, when thought through, is extremely bitter, no one would resent it if within it they could keep lasting friendship with people whose hearts practise goodness.","n":"Mudaliar notes that 'if they can get it' (பெறின்) marks how rare such friendship is. The verse redeems birth without denying its pain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","natpu","panpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:18"},"n:175":{"book":"naladiyar","num":175,"t":["ஊரங் கணநீர் உரவுநீர் சேர்ந்தக்கால்","பேரும் பிறிதாகித் தீர்த்தமாம் - ஓருங்","குலமாட்சி இல்லாரும் குன்றுபோல் நிற்பர்","நலமாட்சி நல்லாரைச் சார்ந்து."],"p":"When water from the town-sewers has joined the great river its very name is changed, and it becomes a 'sacred stream’. Ponder well! Even those who have no greatness of clan, if they ally themselves with good men of goodly fame, shall stand firm as a hill.","r":"Town drain water that joins a great river loses its name and becomes holy water; consider: even those without lineage stand like a hill by joining the good.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","kudi","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:18"},"n:176":{"book":"naladiyar","num":176,"t":["ஒண்கதிர் வாள்மதியும் சேர்தலால் ஓங்கிய","அங்கண் விசும்பின் முயலும் தொழப்படுஉம்","குன்றிய சீர்மைய ராயினும் சீர்பெறுவர்,","குன்றன்னார் கேண்மை கொளின்."],"p":"Because it is in the bright-beamed moon the very hare in heaven's lofty fair expanse is worshipped. Though scant their worthiness, men obtain worth who share friendship with those firm as a hill.","r":"The hare in the bright moon is worshipped along with it in the sky; so those of slight worth gain worth by friendship with men as steady as a hill.","n":"Mudaliar explains the hare as the moon's dark mark: no one removes the spot before worshipping the moon, so the flaw shares the honour.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","natpu","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:18"},"n:177":{"book":"naladiyar","num":177,"t":["பாலோ டளாயநீர் பாலாகு மல்லது","நீராய் நிறம்தெரிந்து தோன்றாதாம் - தேரின்","சிறியார் சிறுமையும் தோன்றாதாம், நல்ல","பெரியார் பெருமையைச் சார்ந்து."],"p":"Commingled with milk water becomes milk; at least, it shows no more as water by its hue. Look close, the mean men's meanness shows not if they join themselves to greatness of the good and great.","r":"Water mixed with milk becomes milk and no longer shows its own colour; so, on inspection, the smallness of small people does not show when they join the greatness of the good.","n":"The verse says the meanness is hidden, not removed. That is a weaker claim than 171, where faults vanish.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","sirumai","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:18"},"n:178":{"book":"naladiyar","num":178,"t":["கொல்லை யிரும்புனத்துக் குற்றி யடைந்தபுல்","ஒல்காவே யாகும் உழவ ருழுபடைக்கு","மெல்லியரே யாயினும் நற்சார்வு சார்ந்தார்மேல்","செல்லாவாம் செற்றார் சினம்."],"p":"Grass close around the stump in the field adjoining the house will not be destroyed by the ploughshare of the farmer ; [thus] though men are weak, if they get under safe protection, the wrath of their foes will not reach them.","r":"Grass growing against a stump in a large field is not moved by the farmer's plough; so the anger of enemies cannot reach the weak who have joined good company.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","aran","pagai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:18"},"n:179":{"book":"naladiyar","num":179,"t":["நிலநலத்தால் நந்திய நெல்லேபோல் தத்தம்","குலநலத்தால் ஆகுவர் சான்றோர் - கலநலத்தைத்","தீவளி சென்று சிதைத்தாங்குச் சான்றாண்மை","தீயினம் சேரக் கெடும்."],"p":"As Nel that flourishes through the goodness of the soil, good men become illustrious by the excellence of their respective clanships. Goodness perishes when it comes near evil associates, as the tornado advances and destroys the excellence of the ship.","r":"As paddy thrives by the goodness of its soil, the worthy flourish by the goodness of their own group; and worth is ruined by joining evil company, as a gale wrecks a sound ship.","n":"Pope's 'clanships' leans toward birth. Which reading one takes decides whether this verse agrees or collides with 195.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","saandraanmai","kudi"],"pos":[["mudaliar","குலம் is the company one joins, keeping the verse within the chapter's theme.","த‌ம்த‌ம் குல‌ம் ந‌ல‌த்தால் - தாந்தாஞ் சேர்கிற‌ கூட்ட‌த்தின் நன்மையான்"],["shanmuganar","குலம் is the family one is born into; the worthy are formed by their birth.","தாம் பிறந்த குடியின் பெருமையால் உயர்ந்தோர் உருவாகின்றனர்"]],"ch":"n:18"},"n:180":{"book":"naladiyar","num":180,"t":["மனத்தான் மறுவில ரேனுந்தாம் சேர்ந்த","இனத்தால் இகழப் படுவர் - புனத்து","வெறிகமழ் சந்தனமும் வேங்கையும் வேமே","எறிபுனம் தீப்பட்டக் கால்."],"p":"Though themselves pure in mind, even good men incur contempt because of their associations. So in the woodland glade when a conflagration takes place (not the worthless brushWood only, but) the fragrant Sandal and Vengai too are consumed. --------------","r":"Though spotless in mind, people are scorned for the company they keep: when a cleared field is set on fire, the fragrant sandal and the vengai burn with the rest.","n":"The verse concerns reputation and fate together: the pure are blamed, and the fire also destroys them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","manam","pazhi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:18"},"n:181":{"book":"naladiyar","num":181,"t":["ஈத லிசையா திளமைசேண் நீங்குதலால்","காத லவரும் கருத்தல்லர் - காதலித்து","ஆதுநா மென்னு மவாவினைக் கைவிட்டுப்","போவதே போலும் பொருள்."],"p":"To give is no longer ours; and youth is gone far off ; our once beloved ones think of us no more! To depart, having abandoned 'desire' that bids us love and hope for future joys, appears the thing that's meet.","r":"We can no longer give, youth has gone far off, and those who once loved us no longer think of us; it seems the right course is to give up the craving that says 'we will love and prosper' and to leave.","n":"The reasons for renouncing here are practical and even rueful, not doctrinal. Mudaliar specifies the departure as going into renunciation, and the loved ones as women.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","avaa","inmai","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:19"},"n:182":{"book":"naladiyar","num":182,"t":["இற்சார்வின் ஏமாந்தேம் ஈங்கமைந்தேம் என்றெண்ணிப்","பொச்சாந் தொழுகுவர் பேதையார் - அச்சார்வு","நின்றன போன்று நிலையா எனவுணர்ந்தார்","என்றும் பரிவ திலர்."],"p":"Fools thinking 'we have found joy in the refuge of home,' and 'we are here in perfect repose, forgetful (of the instability of all things), go on their way. Those who have felt that 'refuge' is unstable, while it seems to stand fast, never fix their fond desire (on it).","r":"Fools think 'we found contentment by taking to home life; here we are complete', and go on heedless; those who see that such supports only seem to stand and do not last never grieve.","n":"Mudaliar allows பரிவது to mean either grieving or desiring; Pope takes the second. The verse targets complacency in home life; whether it condemns home life itself depends on the reader.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","ilvaazhkkai","pochchaappu","pattru"],"pos":[["mudaliar","The supports that fail are the attachments of household life.","அ சார்பு- (இல்வாழ்க்கையாகிய) அப் பற்றுக்கள்"]],"ch":"n:19"},"n:183":{"book":"naladiyar","num":183,"t":["மறுமைக்கு வித்து மயலின்றிச் செய்து","சிறுமைப் படாதேநீர் வாழ்மின் - அறிஞராய்","நின்றுழி நின்றே நிறம்வேறாம் காரணம்","இன்றிப் பலவு முள."],"p":"See that ye sow seed that in other world may germinate; free your lives from earth's bewilder-ment and meanness ; stand in your lot as wise men; the changing hue of things shall without cause fade, and many things be new.","r":"Without confusion sow the seed for the next life, and live wisely without being brought low; for standing just where it stands the body changes its colour without any cause, and many ills arise.","n":"Mudaliar supplies the subject: it is the body whose youth and qualities change unbidden. Pope's version speaks more vaguely of 'the changing hue of things'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","piravi","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:19"},"n:184":{"book":"naladiyar","num":184,"t":["உறைப்பருங் காலத்தும் ஊற்றுநீர்க் கேணி","இறைத்துணினும் ஊராற்றும் என்பர் - கொடைக்கடனும்","சாஅயக் கண்ணும் பெரியார்போல் மற்றையார்","ஆஅயக் கண்ணும் அரிது."],"p":"The well of springing water serves the town that draws and drinks, even in time when rains are scant; so great men in adverse hour dispense due gifts that others give not even in their best estate.","r":"They say a spring-fed well, even in a season of scant rain, serves the town that draws from it; the great keep their duty of giving even when they have declined, as others do not even when they have prospered.","n":"Only the Tamil and Pope are available for this quatrain; neither commentary is aligned to it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","perumai","oppuravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:19"},"n:185":{"book":"naladiyar","num":185,"t":["உறுபுனல் தந்துல கூட்டி அறுமிடத்தும்","கல்லூற் றுழியூறும் ஆறேபோல் - செல்வர்","பலர்க்காற்றிக் கெட்டுலந்தக் கண்ணும் சிலர்காற்றிச்","செய்வர் செயற்பா லவை."],"p":"The river pours forth a mighty stream and feeds the world; and when it is dried up, if men dig in its bed, streams gush out! So good men when rich, give to many; and, when ruined, give still at least to some, and do what should be done.","r":"Like a river that poured out abundant water to feed the world and, when dry, still yields water from springs dug in its bed, the generous who gave to many still give to a few when ruined, and do what ought to be done.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","selvam","inmai","oppuravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:19"},"n:186":{"book":"naladiyar","num":186,"t":["பெருவரை நாட பெரியோர்கட் டீமை","கருநரைமேற் சூடேபோல் தோன்றும் - கருநரையைக்","கொன்றன்ன இன்னா செயினும் சிறியார்மேல்","ஒன்றானும் தோன்றாக் கெடும்."],"p":"Lord of the lofty hills! Any evil in men of moral greatness shows like a brand on a mighty bull; but although mean men do painful deeds, like the slaughter of that same mighty bull, none of these attracts attention.","r":"Lord of the high hills, a fault in the great shows like a brand on a great white bull, while the small may do things as cruel as killing that bull and not one of them is noticed.","n":"This is an observation about visibility, not a defence of the small. The high standard applies because the great are watched.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","sirumai","kutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:19"},"n:187":{"book":"naladiyar","num":187,"t":["இசைந்த சிறுமை இயல்பிலா தார்கண்,","பசைந்த துணையும் பரிவாம் - அசைந்த","நகையேயும் வேண்டாத நல்லறிவி னார்கண்","பகையேயும் பாடு பெறும்."],"p":"In proportion to the degree of one's intimacy with men essentially mean and without good qualities sorrow accrues; but even the hostility of those who do not desire forbidden things even in jest will confer dignity.","r":"Intimacy with the inherently mean brings grief for as long as it lasts, but even enmity with the wise, who would not want harmful things even in jest, brings dignity.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","pagai","sirumai","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:19"},"n:188":{"book":"naladiyar","num":188,"t":["மெல்லிய நல்லாருள் மென்மை அதுவிறந்(து)","ஒன்னாருள் கூற்றுட்கும் உட்குடைமை - எல்லாம்","சலவருட் சாலச் சலவே நலவருள்","நன்மை வரம்பாய் விடல்."],"p":"With gentle fair ones use gentleness surpassing theirs; with foes display a wrath that death's self might dread; with persistent men show a persistency to match ; amongst men of good do good; let the law of life be thus laid down.","r":"Be gentler than the gentle good; toward foes, be a terror that Death itself would fear; toward the false, utterly false; toward the good, goodness itself: let these be one's bounds.","n":"A policy of matching each person in kind. Pope reads சலவர் as the persistent, to be met with persistence; Mudaliar reads them as liars, to be met with lies. Mudaliar also notes that others construe the verse differently.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pagai","vanjam","panpu"],"pos":[["mudaliar","Toward liars, one should be wholly a speaker of lies.","சால சலமே-மிகவும் பொய்யே பேசுதற்கும்"]],"ch":"n:19"},"n:189":{"book":"naladiyar","num":189,"t":["கடுக்கி யொருவன் கடுங்குறளைப் பேசி","மயக்கி விடினும் மனப்பிரிப் பொன்றின்றித்","துளக்க மிலாதவர் தூய மனத்தார்","விளக்கினுள் ஒண்சுடரே போன்று."],"p":"These are the pure in heart who, though any vex, and with use of slanderous words would fain perplex them, with calm unruffled mind abide unwavering, like the bright flame within the lamp.","r":"Though someone hurries in and tries to confuse them with cruel slander, the pure in heart stay unshaken, with no division in their mind, like the bright flame inside a lamp.","n":"The flame is steady because the lamp shields it; the image suggests an inner composure that slander cannot reach.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["manam","purangooral","samanilai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:19"},"n:190":{"book":"naladiyar","num":190,"t":["முற்றுற்றும் துற்றினை நாளும் அறஞ்செய்து","பிற்றுற்றுத் துற்றுவர் சான்றவர் - அத்துற்று","முக்குற்றம் நீக்கி முடியும் அளவெல்லாம்","துக்கத்துள் நீக்கி விடும்."],"p":"The excellent will daily give to the needy in charity their first-served food; they themselves will eat what is served after: such good conduct removes the three faults, and from sorrow sets men free, through all the days, till comes the end. ---------------","r":"The worthy daily give in charity the food served first and eat what is served after; that eating removes the three faults and keeps them from sorrow as long as life lasts.","n":"Shanmuganar names the three faults as desire, anger and delusion. Pope leaves them unnamed, and no Mudaliar gloss is aligned.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","undi","saandror","kutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:19"},"n:191":{"book":"naladiyar","num":191,"t":["கோளாற்றக் கொள்ளாக் குளத்தின்கீழ்ப் பைங்கூழ்போல்","கேளீவ துண்டு கிளைகளோ துஞ்சுப","வாளாடு கூத்தியர் கண்போல் தடுமாறும்","தாளாளர்க் குண்டோ தவறு."],"p":"As for kindred that feed upon what relatives give them they will die off like green grain below a tank, which does not hold a sufficient supply (of water). Is failure possible to men of energy that are quick in movement as the eyes of those who perform the sword-dance ?","r":"Like the green crop below a tank that does not hold back its water, kin live by what their relative gives and rest easy; can failure come to the energetic, who move as quickly as the eyes of women in the sword dance?","n":"Pope reads the first half in reverse: kin who live on handouts die off like crops under a tank that holds too little water. The Tamil supports Shanmuganar's 'rest' (துஞ்சுப) at least as well, so the verse probably praises the energetic as the one who feeds the family.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aalvinai","sutram","muyarchi"],"pos":[["shanmuganar","The other kin live without hardship on what the energetic one gives.","கொடுப்பதை உண்டு மற்ற சுற்றத்தார்கள் துன்பின்றி வாழ்வர்"]],"ch":"n:20"},"n:192":{"book":"naladiyar","num":192,"t":["ஆடுகோ டாகி அதரிடை நின்றதூஉம்","காழ்கொண்ட கண்ணே களிறணைக்கும் கந்தாகும்","வாழ்தலும் அன்ன தகைத்தே ஒருவன்றான்","தாழ்வின்றித் தன்னைச் செயின்."],"p":"What once stood by the wayside, a twig that bent to every touch, when its core is developed within, may become a stake to which they tie an elephant. Life too is thus, if man himself, unfailing make himself !","r":"A twig that swayed by the path, once its core has hardened, becomes a post to tether an elephant; life is like that too, if a person makes himself without slackening.","n":"Shanmuganar reads தாழ்வின்றி as not letting oneself sink; Pope as 'unfailing'. Either way the self is something made over time.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["muyarchi","aalvinai","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:20"},"n:193":{"book":"naladiyar","num":193,"t":["உறுபுலி ஊனிரை யின்றி ஒருநாள்","சிறுதேரை பற்றியும் தின்னும் - அறிவினால்","காற்றொழில் என்று கருதற்க கையினால்","மேற்றொழிலும் ஆங்கே மிகும்."],"p":"The huge tiger, when lacking flesh for food one day, may even seize a little frog and eat it. Think not 'By (all my) knowledge I only gain menial tasks;\" to the skillful hand nobler employments shall even there abound.","r":"A mighty tiger that finds no meat one day will catch and eat even a small frog; so do not think your knowledge fits you only for lowly work: through skill, higher work will come in that very place.","n":"Pope titles it 'Stoop to conquer'. Shanmuganar reads the advice as taking the humble job that is available, since better skilled work may follow.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["muyarchi","aalvinai","maanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:20"},"n:194":{"book":"naladiyar","num":194,"t":["இசையா தெனினும் இயற்றியோ ராற்றால்","அசையாது நிற்பதாம் ஆண்மை - இசையுங்கால்","கண்டால் திரையலைக்கும் கானலந் தண்சேர்ப்ப","பெண்டிரும் வாழாரோ மற்று."],"p":"Manliness is working on, in no wise faltering, remaining stedfast though the matter succeed not. When all is successful,— Lord of the cool lovely shore, in whose groves the waves agitate the scented thorn, — will not even women live and flourish?","r":"Manliness is standing firm in the way one has undertaken even when the thing does not succeed; lord of the cool seashore groves, when things succeed by themselves, don't even women manage to live?","n":"The taunt assumes that women cannot persist through failure. The point is that persistence, not success, is the measure of effort.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aanmai","muyarchi","thitpam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:20"},"n:195":{"book":"naladiyar","num":195,"t":["நல்ல குலமென்றும் தீய குலமென்றும்","சொல்லள வல்லால் பொருளில்லை - தொல்சிறப்பின்","ஒண்பொரு ளொன்றோ தவம்கல்வி யாள்வினை","என்றிவற்றான் ஆகும் குலம்."],"p":"When men speak of 'good caste' and 'bad caste' it is a mere form of speech, and has no real meaning. Not even by possessions, made splendid by ancient glories, but by self-denial, learning, and energy is Caste determined.","r":"'Good caste' and 'bad caste' are only words with nothing behind them; not long-famous wealth but austerity, learning and effort are what make a lineage.","n":"A strong claim from a Jain text that questions birth rank. Shanmuganar takes ஒண்பொருள் ஒன்றோ as asking whether fine wealth alone suffices; Pope reads it as 'not even by possessions'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","pirappu","thavam","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:20"},"n:196":{"book":"naladiyar","num":196,"t":["ஆற்றும் துணையும் அறிவினை உள்ளடக்கி","ஊக்கம் உரையார் உணர்வுடையார் - ஊக்கம்","உறுப்பினால் ஆராயும் ஒண்மை யுடையார்","குறிப்பின்கீழ்ப் பட்ட துலகு."],"p":"The world is subject to the nod of the brilliant (diplomatists) who search out (and know) men's designs from outward indications (lit. from) their members, i.e. from eye, gesture, tone, expression etc.).","r":"The perceptive keep their plans to themselves until the work is done and do not voice their drive; the world lies under the sign of those bright ones who read others' drive from their features.","n":"Mudaliar takes the first ஊக்கம் as one's own effort and the second as the stirring in others' minds, so the verse pairs concealment with insight. He also records a reading in which the world serves at their nod.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","ookkam","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:20"},"n:197":{"book":"naladiyar","num":197,"t":["சிதலை தினப்பட்ட ஆல மரத்தை","மதலையாய் மற்றதன் வீழுன்றி யாங்குக்","குதலைமை தந்தைகண் தோன்றிற்றான் பெற்ற","புதல்வன் மறைப்பக் கெடும்."],"p":"If the banyan's trunk be eaten by the gnawing ant, its 'branch root' bears it up, like a buttress; even so, when decay appears in the sire, the son he has begotten shall hide it, and weakness is no more.","r":"When termites have eaten the banyan, its aerial roots stand as pillars and hold it up; so when weakness shows in a father, the son he begot covers it and it disappears.","n":"Mudaliar notes that மதலை, the prop, also means a son, so the image and the claim share a word.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","kudi","aalvinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:20"},"n:198":{"book":"naladiyar","num":198,"t":["ஈனமாய் இல்லிருந் தின்றி விளியினும்","மானும் தலைவருவ செய்பவோ? - யானை","வரிமுகம் புண்படுக்கும் வள்ளுகிர் நோன்றாள்","அரிமா மதுகை யவர்."],"p":"The lion's pointed claw and mighty foot will wound the spotted face of an elephant; those who have power like his , — though bereft of all, they die in want within their home, — will they do deeds that bring disgrace?","r":"Those with the strength of a lion, whose sharp claws and mighty paws wound an elephant's spotted face, would sooner die in want and low estate at home than do anything that brings disgrace.","n":"Mudaliar reads மானம் here as dishonour (அவமானம்) and maps the lion's claws onto keen intelligence and resourceful effort.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam","inmai","aanmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:20"},"n:199":{"book":"naladiyar","num":199,"t":["தீங்கரும் பீன்று திரள்கால் உளையலா஢","தேங்கமழ் நாற்றம் இழந்தாஅங்கு - ஓங்கும்","உயர்குடி யுள்பிறப்பின் என்னாம் பெயர்பொறிக்கும்","பேராண்மை இல்லாக் கடை."],"p":"Like the flowers on a rounded stalk, with hair like filaments that sweet cane bare, when the sweet fragrance they breathed is lost, what gain accrues from birth in a lofty noble house when energy that makes the name noteworthy, is wanting?","r":"As the cane flower on its thick stalk, with its mane of filaments, loses its honeyed scent, what is birth in a lofty family worth without the great manliness that engraves one's name?","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","aanmai","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:20"},"n:200":{"book":"naladiyar","num":200,"t":["பெருமுத் தரையர் பெரிதுவந் தீயும்","கருனைச்சோ றார்வர் கயவர் - கருனையைப்","பேரும் அறியார் நனிவிரும்பு தாளாண்மை","நீரும் அமிழ்தாய் விடும்."],"p":"The base feed full of rice and savoury food, that men, great lords of the triple lands, with generous gladness give; but water won with willing strenuous toil by those who know, not savoury food by name even, will turn to nectar. -------------","r":"The base fill themselves on rice and fried dishes that the great Mutharaiyar give with delight; but for those who do not even know the names of such dishes, water won by their own eager effort becomes nectar.","n":"Pope makes the Mutharaiyar 'lords of the triple lands', one of the readings Mudaliar reports. The verse brands even the guests of a generous patron as base for living on another's food.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["muyarchi","undi","kayamai","iravu"],"pos":[["mudaliar","The Mutharaiyar were best taken as a generous patron of the poet's own time.","முத்தரையரென்பார் ஆசிரியர் காலத்திருந்த கொடை வள்ள லொருவ ரெனக் கோடலே சிறப்புடைத் தென்க"]],"ch":"n:20"},"n:201":{"book":"naladiyar","num":201,"t":["வயாவும் வருத்தமும் ஈன்றக்கால் நோவும்","கவாஅன் மகற்கண்டு தாய்மறந் தாஅங்கு","அசாஅத்தான் உற்ற வருத்தம் உசாஅத்தன்","கேளிரைக் காணக் கெடும்."],"p":"As a mother when she sees her son upon her lap forgets the languors, the pains, and the throes of birth; so trouble arising from weakness will die when a man sees his sympathizing kinsmen around him.","r":"As a mother forgets the cravings of pregnancy, the strain of carrying and the pain of birth when she sees her son on her lap, the trouble a person has suffered through weakness vanishes when he sees kin who ask after him.","n":"The kin do nothing but come and ask how he is (உசாவுதல்). The comfort the verse describes lies in being asked after, not in material help.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram","idumbai","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:21"},"n:202":{"book":"naladiyar","num":202,"t":["அழன்மண்டு போழ்தின் அடைந்தவர்கட் கெல்லாம்","நிழல்மரம்போல் நேரொப்பத் தாங்கிப் - பழுமரம்போல்","பல்லார் பயன்துய்ப்பத் தான்வருந்தி வாழ்வதே","நல்லாண் மகற்குக் கடன்."],"p":"To yield ready protection alike to all, as a tree affords shade to those that seek its shelter when the heat grows fierce; and to live toiling so that many may enjoy the gain, resembling thus a fruit producing tree, is the duty of the manly man.","r":"The duty of a good man is to shelter all who come to him alike, as a shade tree does in fierce heat, and to toil himself so that many enjoy what he yields, as a fruiting tree does.","n":"தான் வருந்தி makes the giver's own hardship part of the duty rather than a cost to avoid. Mudaliar takes பழுமரம் as the banyan and notes that others read it as a ripe fruiting tree, as Shanmuganar and Pope do.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oppuravu","naduvu","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:21"},"n:203":{"book":"naladiyar","num":203,"t":["அடுக்கல் மலைநாட. தற்சேர்ந் தவரை","எடுக்கல மென்னார் பெரியோர் - அடுத்தடுத்து","வன்காய் பலபல காய்ப்பினும் இல்லையோ","தன்காய் பொறுக்கலாக் கொம்பு."],"p":"Lord of the land where mountains piled on mountains rise! the great demur not to support their kith and kin ;—there is no bough but will support the fruit it bears, though clustered thick great fruits and many cling thereon.","r":"Lord of the land of piled-up hills, the great never say 'we will not support' those who have come to depend on them; is there any branch that cannot bear its own fruit, however many and heavy they hang?","n":"The image makes dependants the supporter's own fruit, so bearing them is part of his nature rather than an act of charity.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram","perumai","thunai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:21"},"n:204":{"book":"naladiyar","num":204,"t":["உலகறியத் தீரக் கலப்பினும் நில்லா","சிலபகலாம் சிற்றினத்தார் கேண்மை - நிலைதிரியா","நிற்கும் பெரியோர் நெறியடைய நின்றனைத்தால்","ஒற்கமி லாளர் தொடர்பு."],"p":"Though mingled in a complete intimacy so that all the world knows of it, the friendship of the little will last but little time. Connection with the firm unyielding men endures till the great one's path, who never swerve, is reached.","r":"The friendship of small-natured people does not last beyond a few days, even when they mingle so fully that the world knows it; the bond with those who never falter holds as steadily as the ascetics who keep to their path to the goal.","n":"Both commentators read the comparison as ascetics who never leave the discipline of renunciation, and Mudaliar names the goal as release (வீடு). Pope's translation makes the line say how long the bond lasts, which is less clear in the Tamil.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","sirumai","perumai","thavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:21"},"n:205":{"book":"naladiyar","num":205,"t":["இன்னர் இனையர் எமர்பிறர் என்னும்சொல்","என்னும் இலராம் இயல்பினால் - துன்னித்","தொலைமக்கள் துன்பம்தீர்ப் பாரேயார் மாட்டும்","தலைமக்க ளாகற்பா லார்."],"p":"‘Such are they and so many;’ ‘these are ours; those strange :’ those worthy to be classed as chief of men say nothing like this ; so to speak is not their nature ; for they relieve the distress of ALL that troubled come to them!","r":"Those fit to be counted chief among men have no trace of the talk that sorts people as 'of this kind, so many, ours, others', and they draw near and relieve the distress of anyone who is perishing.","n":"In a chapter on embracing kin, this verse removes the boundary of kinship: for the best people, anyone in distress counts as their own. Mudaliar glosses எமர் and பிறர் as those we want and those we don't.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naduvu","arul","perumai","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:21"},"n:206":{"book":"naladiyar","num":206,"t":["பொற்கலத்துப் பெய்த புலியுகிர் வான்புழுக்கல்","அக்காரம் பாலோ டமரார்கைத் துண்டலின்","உப்பிலிப் புற்கை உயிர்போல் கிளைஞர்மாட்டு","எக்காலத் தானு மினிது."],"p":"More sweet than rice, though white as tiger's claw with milk and sugar served on plate of gold, by loveless hands, is any tasteless mess, in any dish, when shared with kindred dear as life.","r":"Saltless millet gruel in any vessel, taken among kin as dear as life, is always sweeter than rice white as a tiger's claw, served with sugar and milk in a golden dish from the hands of those who are not one's own.","n":"The contrast is total on every count, the vessel, the grain, the salt and the giver, and only the giver decides the taste.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram","anbu","undi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:21"},"n:207":{"book":"naladiyar","num":207,"t":["நாள்வாய்ப் பெறினும்தந் நள்ளாதா ரில்லத்து","வேளாண்மை வெங்கருனை வேம்பாகும் - கேளாய்,","அபராணப் போழ்தின் அடகிடுவ ரேனும்","தமராயார் மாட்டே இனிது."],"p":"Most bitter (Margosa) is the bounteous meal of dainty food at early dawn in house of those who love us not. Hear thou! though not till evening given, the mess of herbs when eaten with our own is sweet.","r":"A hot fried feast given as hospitality at the proper hour in the house of those not close to one is bitter as neem; greens served at an untimely afternoon hour are sweet when they come from one's own people.","n":"Even the hour is reversed: the stranger's meal is punctual and the kin's is late, and the kin's is still the sweeter.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram","undi","virundhu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:21"},"n:208":{"book":"naladiyar","num":208,"t":["முட்டிகை போல முனியாது வைகலும்","கொட்டியுண் பாரும் குறடுபோல் கைவிடுவர்","சுட்டுக்கோல் போல எரியும் புகுவரே","நட்டார் எனப்படு வார்."],"p":"","r":"Those who pound at you every day like a hammer, eating off you, let go like tongs when the fire comes; those rightly called intimates enter the fire with you like the smith's poker.","n":"The three smithy tools sort people by how they behave in the heat. Mudaliar offers two readings of முனியாது, 'without tiring' or 'showing affection', and of கொட்டி, 'striking' or 'pestering'. Pope's translation is missing for this quatrain.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","sutram","kedu"],"pos":[["mudaliar","Those who enter the fire are loving kin.","ந‌ட்டார் - அன்புடை உற‌வின‌ர்."],["shanmuganar","Those who enter the fire are true friends.","உண்மை நண்பர் எனப்படுவார், பொருளோடு நெருப்பிலே நிற்கும் உலையாணிக்கோல் போல"]],"ch":"n:21"},"n:209":{"book":"naladiyar","num":209,"t":["நறுமலர்த் தண்கோதாய் நட்டார்க்கு நட்டார்","மறுமையும் செய்வதொன் றுண்டோ - இறுமளவும்","இன்புறுவ இன்புற் றெழீஇ அவரோடு","துன்புறுவ துன்புறாக் கால்."],"p":"O maid adorned with fresh garlands of fragrant flowers! is there one thing that even in other world, friends may perform for friends, if till they die, their joys they share, but shun to share their griefs ?","r":"Maiden with the cool garland of fragrant flowers, if friends share their friends' joys until death but not their sorrows, is there anything they could do for them even in the next world?","n":"The claim is negative: sharing only joys makes no bond at all. Mudaliar again glosses நட்டார் as kin, where Shanmuganar has friends. The verse is addressed to a woman, which Mudaliar notes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","idumbai","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:21"},"n:210":{"book":"naladiyar","num":210,"t":["விருப்பிலார் இல்லத்து வேறிருந் துண்ணும்","வெருக்குக்கண் வெங்கருனை வேம்பாம் - விருப்புடைத்","தன்போல்வா ரில்லுள் தயங்குநீர்த் தண்புற்கை","என்போ டியைந்த அமிழ்து."],"p":"The savoury fried curry, (in colour) like a cat's eye, which one eats seated a part in the house of those who are without affection, will be bitter as margosa; but cold gruel (weak and insipid), like clear water, in the house of affectionate equals, is ambrosia that cleaves to the bones. --------------","r":"A hot fried dish the colour of a cat's eye, eaten sitting apart in the house of those without affection, is bitter as neem; watery cold gruel in the house of loving equals is ambrosia that joins with one's bones.","n":"வேறிருந்து, sitting apart, names the slight: the food is served but the guest is kept separate.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","undi","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:21"},"n:211":{"book":"naladiyar","num":211,"t":["கருத்துணர்ந்து கற்றறிந்தார் கேண்மையெஞ் ஞான்றுங்","குருத்தின் கரும்புதின் ற்றறே - குருத்திற்கு","எதிர்செலத்தின் றன்ன தகைத்தரோ, என்றும்","மதுர மிலாளர் தொடர்பு."],"p":"Intimacy with those who understand the real intention (of one's words), and who have acquired wisdom by learning, will at all times be like eating sweet cane from the tender shoots; but attachment to those who have no sweetness of disposition is like eating it in a direction opposite to the tender shoot (it grows harder and less sweet).","r":"Friendship with those who have learned with understanding is always like eating sugarcane from the tip down, sweeter as one goes; the bond with those who lack sweetness is like eating it from the root toward the tip.","n":"The quatrain nearly repeats 138 in the chapter on learning, with a different refrain. Mudaliar explains that eating from the tip makes the cane grow sweeter as one goes, and from the root less sweet.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","kalvi","arivu"],"pos":[["mudaliar","The sweetness the second group lacks is the sweetness of learning.","கல்வியறி வாகிய இனிய தன்மை யில்லாதவர்களது நட்பு"],["shanmuganar","The sweetness they lack is a sweet disposition, as Pope also has it.","என்றும் இனிய பண்பு இல்லாதவாது நட்போ"]],"ch":"n:22"},"n:212":{"book":"naladiyar","num":212,"t":["இற்பிறப் பெண்ணி இடைதிரியா ரென்பதோர்","நற்புடை கொண்டமை யல்லது - பொற்கேழ்","புனலொழுகப் புள்ளிரியும் பூங்குன்ற நாட","மனமறியப் பட்டதொன் றன்று."],"p":"Lord of the land of flowery hills, where wild-fowl golden in hue fly, scared by the rush of the waterfall. Regarding the mobility of their birth—these will not swerve:—To say thus is a good ground of confidence—(a good position to take up); but to say ‘their minds are known,' is not any (i. e. real ground).","r":"Lord of the flowering hills, where birds scatter as golden water pours down, one may befriend people on the good ground that, mindful of their family, they will not change midway; apart from that, a person's mind is not something that can be known.","n":"The verse doesn't praise high birth for its own sake. It falls back on lineage because the mind can't be read. Mudaliar draws the rule: befriend those of high family.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","kudi","aaraaydhal","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:22"},"n:213":{"book":"naladiyar","num":213,"t":["யானை யனைவர் நண்பெரிணஇ நாயனையார்","கேண்மை கெழீஇக் கொளல்வேண்டும், - யானை","அறிந்தறிந்தும் பாகனையே கொல்லும், எறிந்தவேல்","மெய்யதா வால்குழைக்கும் நாய்."],"p":"Forsaking friendship with those who resemble the elephant, embrace and hold fast intimacy with those who are like the dog; for the elephant will slay even its keeper though it has long known him ; but the dog will wag its tail when it has in its body the javelin (hurled at it by its angry master).","r":"Give up the friendship of those who are like elephants and hold close those who are like dogs: the elephant may kill even its keeper after knowing him for years, while the dog wags its tail with its master's spear in its body.","n":"The test is loyalty under injury, not strength or rank: the elephant is the nobler animal and still loses.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","anbu","aaraaydhal"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:22"},"n:214":{"book":"naladiyar","num":214,"t":["பலநாளும் பக்கத்தா ராயினும் நெஞ்சில்","சிலநாளும் ஒட்டாரோ டொட்டார் - பலநாளும்","நீத்தா ரெனக்கை விடலுண்டோ , தந்நெஞ்சத்","தியாத்தாரோ டியாத்த தொடர்பு."],"p":"Though men dwell side by side for many days when their souls cleave not (are not congenial) for even a few days they retain not their friendship. But is it possible to let go attachment's well-knit ties, though those to whom one's soul is knit dwell many days afar?","r":"Those who are not joined in heart for even a few days do not become close, though they stay beside one for many days; and would anyone let go of the bond with those tied to their heart because they have been away for many days?","n":"Nearness and distance are both set aside: the heart's tie alone makes and keeps a friend. Shanmuganar supplies 'the great' as the subject.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","manam","pazhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:22"},"n:215":{"book":"naladiyar","num":215,"t":["கோட்டுப்பூப் போல மலர்ந்துபிற் கூம்பாது","வேட்டதே வேட்டதாம் நட்பாட்சி - தோட்ட","கயப்பூப்போல் முன்மலர்ந்து பிற்கூம்பு வாரை","நயப்பாரும் நட்பாரும் இல்."],"p":"The use and wont of friendship is that, what once it has loved it loves always,— like the flower on the tree-branch which having once unfolded afterwards closes not; but who will esteem, or make friends of those who are like flowers on the surface of the excavated tank, which unfold and afterwards close themselves?","r":"The true conduct of friendship is to go on loving what it first loved, like a tree's flower that opens and doesn't close again; no one esteems or befriends those who open first and close later, like the flowers of a dug pond.","n":"Pond flowers such as the lotus close each evening, so the fault condemned is changeability, not coldness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","panpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:22"},"n:216":{"book":"naladiyar","num":216,"t":["கடையாயார் நட்பிற் கமுகனையர் ஏனை","இடையாயார் தெங்கி னனையர் - தலையாயார்","எண்ணரும் பெண்ணைபோன் றிட்டஞான் றிட்டதே,","தொன்மை யுடையார் தொடர்பு."],"p":"The lowest sort of men in friendship are like the Arecanut tree. The middle sort are like the cocoa-nut tree. Attachments to the chief of men — who are old friends —is like the Palmyra tree of rare worth : what was given that first day was given once for all.","r":"In friendship the lowest are like the areca palm and the middle like the coconut; the bond of the highest, who keep old ties, is like the palmyra of untold worth: what was given on the first day stays given.","n":"Mudaliar explains the trees: the areca yields only with constant tending, the coconut with occasional tending, and the palmyra gives richly after a single watering. On that reading the grades measure how much a friend has to be given before the friendship yields.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","pazhaimai","perumai","sirumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:22"},"n:217":{"book":"naladiyar","num":217,"t":["கழுநீருள் காரட கேனும் ஒருவன்","விழுமிதாக் கொள்ளின் அமிழ்தாம் - விழுமிய","குய்த்துவையார் வெண்சோறே யாயினும் மேவாதார்","கைத்துண்டல் காஞ்சிரங் காய்."],"p":"If one receive you courteously, though what he gives is but rank herbs dressed in water in which rice had been washed, it is ambrosia. To eat from the hands of those who love us not, though it be white rice with rich spicy condiments, is nux vomica.","r":"Even dark greens cooked in rice-water are ambrosia if someone gives them with high regard; white rice with fine seasonings and relishes, taken from the hand of those not in accord with one, is the bitter strychnos fruit.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","natpu","undi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:22"},"n:218":{"book":"naladiyar","num":218,"t":["நாய்க்கால் சிறுவிரல்போல் நன்கணிய ராயினும்","ஈக்கால் துணையும் உதவாதார் நட்பென்னாம்?","சேய்த்தானும் சென்று கொளல்வேண்டும், செய்விளைக்கும்","வாய்க்கால் அனையார் தொடர்பு."],"p":"Of what value is the friendship of those who being very near like the little claw on a dog's leg, afford not help as much as a fly's foot? Though you go far to seek it, lay hold of the friendship of those who are like the water channel that causes the crops in the field to flourish.","r":"What use is the friendship of those as close as the little toe on a dog's foot who give not even a fly's foot of help? Go even far to gain the bond of those who are like the channel that makes the field yield.","n":"Nearness is set against usefulness, and the channel image implies that real help often comes from a distance.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","velanmai","thunai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:22"},"n:219":{"book":"naladiyar","num":219,"t":["தெளிவிலார் நட்பின் பகைநன்று சாதல்","விளியா அருநோயின் நன்றால் - அளிய","இகழ்தலின் கோறல் இனிதேமற் றில்ல","புகழ்தலின் வைதலே நன்று."],"p":"Better hate than friendship of the ignorant. Better death than disease which comes on yielding to no remedy. Sweeter is killing thin contempt that breaks the spirit. Better abuse than undeserved praise.","r":"Enmity is better than the friendship of those without clear understanding, death better than an incurable disease, killing kinder than contempt that wounds, and abuse better than praise of qualities one doesn't have.","n":"Only the first of the four comparisons concerns friendship; the other three give it company. Mudaliar's gloss of கோறல் is printed as 'சொல்லுதல்' (speaking), probably an error for கொல்லுதல் (killing), which Shanmuganar and Pope have.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","pedhaimai","pagai","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:22"},"n:220":{"book":"naladiyar","num":220,"t":["மாணஇப் பலரோடு பன்னாள் முயங்கிப்","பெரிணஇப் பொருட்டக்கார்க் கோடலே வேண்டும்","பாணஇ உயிர்செகுக்கும் பாம்பொடும் இன்னா","மாணஇஇப் பின்னைப் பிரிவு."],"p":"-----------","r":"Mix with many over many days, find who matches you, and take only those worth valuing; for it is painful to part after becoming close, even with a snake whose quick bite kills.","n":"The argument rests on the cost of parting: because breaking a bond hurts even when the friend was deadly, the testing must come first.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","aaraaydhal","idumbai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:22"},"n:221":{"book":"naladiyar","num":221,"t":["நல்லா ரெனத்தாம் நனிவிரும்பிக் கொண்டாரை","அல்லா ரெனினும் அடக்கிக் கொளல்வேண்டும்.","நெல்லுக் குமியுண்டு, நீர்க்கு நுரைஉண்டு","புல்லிதழ் பூவிற்கும் உண்டு."],"p":"When those to whom with strong desire we clung as good, prove otherwise, keep the sad secret bid, - cling to them still! The growing grain has husks; the water has its foam: flowers too have scentless outer sheath of leaves.","r":"Those one eagerly took as good should be kept close, with their faults held within, even if they prove otherwise; rice has its husk, water its foam, and flowers their rough outer petals.","n":"அடக்கிக் கொளல் can mean holding the friend close or keeping the fault to oneself. Shanmuganar takes the second, and Pope's version has both.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","kutram","porai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:23"},"n:222":{"book":"naladiyar","num":222,"t":["செறுத்தோ றுடைப்பினும் செம்புனலோ டுடார்,","மறுத்தும் சிறைசெய்வர் நீர்நசைஇ வாழ்நர்","வெறுப்ப வெறுப்பச் செயினும் பொறுப்பரே","தாம்வேண்டிக் கொண்டார் தொடர்பு."],"p":"If though they dam it up, the fresh flood should burst its bonds, men do not feel aggrieved; but straightway imprison it again, for by the precious stream they live: so though their friends again and again do very disagreeable things, men bear with those whose friendship is dear.","r":"Those who live by water don't rage at the fresh flood when it breaks the dam each time they build it, but dam it again; in the same way people bear with those whose bond they sought, even when they do one hateful thing after another.","n":"The friend is needed the way the farmer needs the stream, so here forbearance rests on dependence as much as on goodness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["porai","natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:23"},"n:223":{"book":"naladiyar","num":223,"t":["இறப்பவே தீய செயினுந்தந் நட்டார்","பொறுத்தல் தகுவதொன் றன்றோ - நிறக்கோங்கு","உருவவண் டார்க்கு முயர்வரை நாட","ஒருவர் பொறையிருவர் நட்பு."],"p":"Lord of the lofty hilly land, where the bees hum through all the flowery Gongu-glade ! — Though friends should work us surpassing ills, the only thing that is meet is forbearance: patience of one is friendship of the twain.","r":"Lord of the high hills where bees hum over the bright kongu flowers, isn't it right to bear with one's friends even when they do great wrong? The patience of one is the friendship of two.","n":"ஒருவர் பொறை இருவர் நட்பு is the maxim: a friendship survives if either party absorbs the injury, so it doesn't need both to be faultless.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["porai","natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:23"},"n:224":{"book":"naladiyar","num":224,"t":["மடிதிரை தந்திட்ட வான்கதிர் முத்தம்","கடுவசை நாவாய் கரையலைக்குஞ் சேர்ப்ப","விடுதற் கா஢யா ரியல்பிலரேல் நெஞ்சம்","சுடுதற்கு மூட்டிய தீ."],"p":"Lord of the shore where pearls of purest lustre are thrown up by circling waves, and where swift darting boats are borne through the surf !—when friends who we may not leave have alien hearts, it is as a scorching fire enkindled in the soul.","r":"Lord of the shore where folding waves cast up bright pearls and swift boats rock against the beach, if those one cannot let go lack good nature, they are a fire lit to burn one's heart.","n":"The verse doesn't say to leave them. It names the pain of a bond one cannot leave. Pope's title, about being angry with those we love, is not in the Tamil. Neither commentary is aligned to this quatrain, though Shanmuganar's comment on it appears under 223.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["natpu","idumbai","panpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:23"},"n:225":{"book":"naladiyar","num":225,"t":["இன்னா செயினும் விடற்பால ரல்லாரைப்","பொன்னாகப் போற்றிக் கொளல்வேண்டும் - பொன்னொடு","நல்லிற் சிதைத்ததீ நாடோ றும் நாடித்தம்","இல்லத்தில் ஆக்குத லால்."],"p":"Though those, from whom you may not part, do grievous things, O maid who art as Lakshmi! you must still cherish your chosen friends—fire destroys men's wealth and happy homes, yet is it sought there and kindled every day.","r":"Those one ought not to part from must be cherished like gold even when they do harm, since the fire that once destroyed a good house along with its gold is still sought out and kindled at home every day.","n":"Fire both destroys and is needed daily, so the friend is cherished because life needs them, not because the harm is forgotten. Pope takes பொன்னாக as an address to a maiden 'as Lakshmi'; Shanmuganar reads it as 'like gold'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["porai","natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:23"},"n:226":{"book":"naladiyar","num":226,"t":["இன்னா செயினும் விடுதற் கா஢யாரைத்","துன்னாத் துறத்தல் தகுவதோ - துன்னருஞ்சீர்","விண்குத்து நீள்வரை வெற்ப. களைபவோ","கண்குத்திற் றென்றுதங் கை."],"p":"When those from whom it is hard to part do evil things should men at once renounce them?—Lord of the lengthening hills that pierce the sky, whence rarest giftsdescend !—Do men cut off their hand because it pricked their eye?","r":"Lord of the sky-piercing hills of rare excellence, is it right to cast off those hard to part from, though they hurt you? Does anyone cut off their own hand because it poked their eye?","n":"The image makes the friend part of one's own body, so cutting them off harms oneself again and undoes nothing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["porai","natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:23"},"n:227":{"book":"naladiyar","num":227,"t":["இலங்குநீர்த் தண்சேர்ப்ப. இன்னா செயினும்","கலந்து பழிகாணார் சான்றோர் - கலந்தபின்","தீமை எடுத்துரைக்கும் திண்ணறி வில்லாதார்","தாமும் அவரிற் கடை."],"p":"Lord of the cool shore of the shining sea! The perfect ones when they have contracted an intimacy with any, see no faults in them, even if they do things that cause pain. Those who, being without stable wisdom, take up and tell out men's evil deeds after contracting an intimacy with them, are themselves worse than they.","r":"Lord of the cool shore of shining waters, once the worthy have joined with someone they see no fault in them even when hurt; those without firm understanding who bring out another's wrongs after joining with him are themselves lower than he is.","n":"The verse ranks exposing a friend's wrong as worse than the wrong itself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","kutram","saandror","purangooral"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:23"},"n:228":{"book":"naladiyar","num":228,"t":["ஏதிலார் செய்த திறப்பவே தீதெனினும்","நோதக்க தென்னுண்டாம் நோக்குங்கால் - காதல்","கழுமியார் செய்த கறங்கருவி நாட","விழுமிதாம் நெஞ்சத்துள் நின்று."],"p":"Lord of the land of resounding waterfalls ! Though what those alien to us have done may be surpassingly evil, what is there to be pained at, when you regard it? Things done by affectionate friends, will be excellent when so regarded by the mind.","r":"Lord of the land of roaring falls, what is there to grieve over, on reflection, when strangers do great wrong? And what those bound by love have done becomes good when held within the heart.","n":"The same harm is treated by who did it: from a stranger it is to be shrugged off, from one who loves it is to be turned into something good within.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","anbu","porai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:23"},"n:229":{"book":"naladiyar","num":229,"t":["தமரென்று தாங்கொள்ளப் பட்டவர் தம்மைத்","தமரன்மை தாமறிந்தா ராயின், - அவரைத்","தமா஢னும் நன்கு மதித்துத் தமரன்மை","தம்முள் அடக்கிக் கொளல்."],"p":"If we perceive those we have accepted as our own to be not really ours, we must pay even more respect to them than to our own, and keep concealed in our own mind the fact that they are not really ours.","r":"If you find that those you took as your own are not, honour them even more than your own and keep the knowledge that they are not yours shut within you.","n":"Pope's title says to love them the more; the verse says மதித்து, to value or honour.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","sutram","kutram"],"pos":[["mudaliar","One is to honour them more than one's real friends, with no suggestion of pretence.","த‌ம‌ரினும் ந‌ன்கு ம‌தித்து - ந‌ண்ப‌ரினும் மிக‌க் கௌர‌வித்து"],["shanmuganar","The honour is an outward show while the truth is kept inside.","தம் உறவினரினும் நன்கு மதிப்பது போல் வெளிக்குக் காட்டி"]],"ch":"n:23"},"n:230":{"book":"naladiyar","num":230,"t":["குற்றமும் ஏனைக் குணமும் ஒருவனை","நட்டபின் நாடித் திரிவேனேல் - நட்டான்","மறைகாவா விட்டவன் செல்வுழிச் செல்க","அறைகடல்சூழ் வையம் நக."],"p":"--------------","r":"If, after taking someone as my friend, I go about searching out his faults and his other qualities, may I go where the man who did not guard his friend's secret goes, while the world encircled by the roaring sea laughs.","n":"A curse spoken in the first person. Mudaliar names the destination as hell. Examining a friend belongs before the friendship (chapter 22); after it, examining is treated as betrayal.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","kutram","aaraaydhal","pazhi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:23"},"n:231":{"book":"naladiyar","num":231,"t":["செறிப்பில் பழங்கூரை சேறணை யாக","இறைத்துநீர் ஏற்றும் கிடப்பர், - கறைக்குன்றம்","பொங்கருவி தாழும் புனல்வரை நன்னாட","தங்கரும் முற்றுந் துணை."],"p":"Lord of the pleasant well-watered mountain land, where boiling waterfalls pour down from the dark hills! (Poor men) linger beneath the old roof that affords no shelter, baling out the water, and making mud embankments, and enduring the down-pour; (even so unreal friends stick by you) till their purpose has been attained.","r":"Lord of the well-watered hills where waterfalls pour from dark peaks, like people who stay under an old leaking roof, making mud dams and bailing out and catching the water, some stay by you only until their own business is done.","n":"The simile is of discomfort put up with for one's own sake: they stay as one stays under a bad roof, because they need it for now. Shanmuganar adds that they pretend to share one's troubles.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","vanjam","payan"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:24"},"n:232":{"book":"naladiyar","num":232,"t":["சீரியார் கேண்மை சிறந்த சிறப்பிற்றாய்","மாரிபோல் மாண்ட பயத்ததாம் - மாரி","வறந்தக்கால் போலுமே வாலருவி நாட","சிறந்தக்கால் சீரிலார் நட்பு."],"p":"Lord of the land of pure (white, forming) waterfalls! The friendship of the virtuous is of exceeding excellence, and yields glorious results — like (seasonable) rain; but the friendship of the vicious, even in the time of its exuberance, is as when the rain fails in the time of drought.","r":"Lord of the land of white waterfalls, the friendship of the excellent is of surpassing worth and fruitful like rain; that of the worthless, even at its height, is like rain that has failed.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","perumai","sirumai","mazhai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:24"},"n:233":{"book":"naladiyar","num":233,"t":["நுண்ணுணர்வி னாரொடு கூடி நுகர்வுடைமை","விண்ணுலகே யொக்கும் விழைவிற்றால் - நுண்ணு஡ல்","உணர்வில ராகிய ஊதியம் இல்லார்ப்","புணர்தல் நிரயத்துள் ஒன்று."],"p":"Enjoyment of the society of men of refinement resembles the heavenly world in the pleasure it affords. Closest contact with those destitute of fine perception of the value of learned pursuits —men who gain no wisdom from you, nor you from them—is one of the hells.","r":"Enjoying the company of people of fine understanding is as desirable as the heavenly world; being joined to profitless people without understanding of subtle learning is one of the hells.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","arivu","kalvi","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:24"},"n:234":{"book":"naladiyar","num":234,"t":["பெருகுவது போலத் தோன்றிவைத் தீப்போல்","ஒருபொழுதுஞ் செல்லாதே நந்தும் - அருகெல்லாம்","சந்தன நீள்சோலைச் சாரன் மலைநாட","பந்தமி லாளர் தொடர்பு."],"p":"Lord of the land where wide groves of Sandal cover the hilly slopes! Friendship with those who feel not its real obligation, like fire in the straw (suddenly) appears, seeming as though it would increase, but never advancing dies out.","r":"Lord of the hills whose slopes are lined with long sandal groves, the bond with those who have no real tie seems to grow, then, like fire in straw, dies without lasting even a moment.","n":"Mudaliar glosses பந்தம் as the bond of love; Shanmuganar as true attachment.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:24"},"n:235":{"book":"naladiyar","num":235,"t":["செய்யாத செய்துநாம் என்றலும் செய்வதனைச்","செய்யாது தாழ்த்துக்கொண் டோ ட்டலும் - மெய்யாக","இன்புறூஉம் பெற்றி இகழ்ந்தார்க்கும் அந்நிலையே","துன்புறூஉம் பெற்றி தரும்."],"p":"The promising to do what cannot be done, and the putting off and leaving undone through delay things that could be done, will forthwith bring experience of sorrow even to those who have condemned truthfully the pleasant experiences of life (ascetics and saints).","r":"Saying 'we will do it' of what cannot be done, and putting off what can be done, bring suffering at once even to those who have truly scorned the enjoyment of pleasure.","n":"'Even to ascetics' means these faults hurt whatever one's indifference to pleasure. The verse doesn't mention friends; only its place in this chapter suggests these are marks of an unfit friend.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vaaimai","madi","idumbai","thuravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:24"},"n:236":{"book":"naladiyar","num":236,"t":["ஒருநீர்ப் பிறந்தொருங்கு நீண்டக் கடைத்தும்","விரிநீர்க் குவளையை ஆம்பலொக் கல்லா","பெருநீரார் கேண்மை கொளினுநீர் அல்லார்","கருமங்கள் வேறு படும்."],"p":"The Ambal (water-lily) does not equalthe expanding Kuvalai though born and growing together with it in the same pool : though they attain to intimacy with those of generous instincts, the deeds of men in whom these instincts are lacking will be diverse.","r":"Though born in the same pool and growing up together, the water-lily never equals the spreading kuvalai; so though they gain the friendship of the large-natured, the deeds of those who lack that nature remain different.","n":"Pope's title speaks of a lotus; the Tamil compares the ஆம்பல் with the குவளை, the blue water-lily.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","panpu","perumai","sirumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:24"},"n:237":{"book":"naladiyar","num":237,"t":["முற்றற் சிறுமந்தி முற்பட்ட தந்தையை","நெற்றுக்கண் டன்ன விரலான் ஞெமிர்த்திட்டுக்","குற்றிப் பறிக்கும் மலைநாட இன்னாதே","ஒற்றுமை கொள்ளாதார் நட்பு."],"p":"Lord of the hilly land where the immature little monkey, with its finger like a bean-pod, will flip its father when it meets him, and poke him and snatch fruit from him! Afflictive indeed is friendship with the uncongenial.","r":"Lord of the hills where the unripe little monkey, with fingers like dry bean-pods, pokes and prises at the father who meets it to snatch his food, the friendship of those not of one mind with you is painful.","n":"The image is of closeness without regard: the young monkey takes from the one nearest to it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","manam","innaa"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:24"},"n:238":{"book":"naladiyar","num":238,"t":["முட்டுற்ற போழ்தின் முடுகியென் னாருயிரை","நட்டா னொருவன்கை நீட்டேனேல் - நட்டான்","கடிமனை கட்டழித்தான் செல்வுழிச் செல்க","நெடுமொழி வையம் நக."],"p":"If I hasten not to put forth my hand and offer my precious life to my friend when in distress, may I depart whither he goes who has violated the sanctity of his friend's wedded wife, while the far-famed world laughs!","r":"If, when my friend is in distress, I do not hurry to put my precious life in his hands, may I go where the man who violated his friend's guarded wife goes, while the world of lasting words laughs.","n":"The chapter on unfit friendship includes this vow of what a fit friend owes. Mudaliar names the destination as hell and gives three readings of நெடுமொழி வையம்.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","saakkaadu","pazhi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:24"},"n:239":{"book":"naladiyar","num":239,"t":["ஆன்படு நெய்பெய் கலனுள் அதுகளைந்து","வேம்படு நெய்பெய் தனைத்தரோ - தேம்படு","நல்வரை நாட நயமுணர்வார் நண்பெரிணஇப்","புல்லறிவி னாரொடு நட்பு."],"p":"Lord of the land of goodly hills where honey flows! To forsake the friendship of those who know the right, and cultivate that of shallow pretenders to knowledge, is like emptying out cow's ghee from a vessel and pouring into it margosa oil.","r":"Lord of the good hills where honey flows, leaving the friendship of those who know what is good to befriend the petty-minded is like emptying cow's ghee from a vessel and filling it with neem oil.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","pullarivu","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:24"},"n:240":{"book":"naladiyar","num":240,"t":["உருவிற் கமைந்தான்கண் ஊராண்மை யின்மை","பருகற் கமைந்தபால் நீரளா யற்றே","தெரிவுடையார் தீயினத்தா ராகுதல் நாகம்","விரிபெடையோ டாடிவிட் டற்று."],"p":"The absence of generosity in those whose exterior is pleasing, is like the mingling of water with the milk provided for food: when men of understanding take to bad company it is like the disporting of a Cobra with a female viper. -------------","r":"One fitted for good form who lacks generosity toward his town is like milk fit to drink mixed with water; the discerning taking up with bad company is like a cobra mating with a female viper.","n":"Mudaliar records the belief that a cobra mating with a viper dies, so the image predicts ruin.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["velanmai","inam","arivu","kedu"],"pos":[["mudaliar","உருவிற்கு அமைந்தான் is first one with some qualities fit for greatness; Mudaliar gives good looks only as another view.","பெருமைக்குரிய சில நற்குணங்களுடையா-னிடத்தே"],["shanmuganar","It is one handsome in form, as Pope also has it.","உருவத்தில் அழகாய் அமைந்திருப்பவனிடம்"]],"ch":"n:24"},"n:241":{"book":"naladiyar","num":241,"t":["பகைவர் பணிவிடம் நோக்கித் தகவுடையார்","தாமேயும் நாணித் தலைச்செல்லார் காணாய்","இளம்பிறை யாயக்கால் திங்களைச் சேராது","அணங்கருந் துப்பின் அரா."],"p":"Worthy men, when they behold where foes are foiled, themselves too feel sore abashed, and do not hasten on to crush them. Behold, the strong invulnerable dragon draws not near the moon (to swallow it) when it is in its tender crescent days!","r":"When the worthy see their enemies humbled, they themselves feel shame and do not advance on them; see, the snake whose strength nothing can trouble does not seize the moon while it is a young crescent.","n":"The shame belongs to the victor, not the defeated. Shanmuganar names the snake as Rahu, the eclipse serpent.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","naanam","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:25"},"n:242":{"book":"naladiyar","num":242,"t":["நளிகடல் தண்சேர்ப்ப நல்கூர்ந்த மக்கட்கு","அணிகல மாவ தடக்கம் - பணிவில்சீர்","மாத்திரை யின்றி நடக்குமேல் வாமுர்","கோத்திரம் கூறப் படும்."],"p":"Lord of the cool shore of the spreading sea ! To men in poverty a modest self-restraint is the chiefest ornament. If a man live in unbending pride, and in a manner unbefitting his position, his fellow-townsmen will revile his race.","r":"Lord of the cool shore of the vast sea, for the poor, restraint is the ornament; if one lives without humility and beyond measure, the town one lives in will speak ill of one's family.","n":"The cost of a poor man's lack of restraint falls on his lineage, not only on himself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","inmai","panivu","kudi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:25"},"n:243":{"book":"naladiyar","num":243,"t":["எந்நிலத்து வித்திடினும் காஞ்சிரங்காய் தெங்காகா","தென்னாட் டவருஞ் சுவர்க்கம் புகுதலால்","தன்னாற்றா னாகும் மறுமை வடதிசையும்","கொன்னாளர் சாலப் பலர்."],"p":"Whatever soil you sow it in, the Strychnos nut grows not into a cocoa-palm. Some of the Southern land have entered Paradise! It is man's way of life that decides his future state. Full many from the Northern land are denizens of hell.","r":"In whatever soil it is sown, a strychnos seed will not become a coconut; since even those of the south enter heaven, one's next world comes from oneself, and in the north too there are very many idle wasters.","n":"Pope calls the northerners 'denizens of hell'; கொன்னாளர் means those who spend their days to no purpose, which is milder.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","ozhukkam","pirappu","vinai"],"pos":[["mudaliar","The south is the Tamil land, and the verse rebuts a northern claim that those born there cannot reach a good state.","தென்னாடு மிலேச்ச தேசமென்றும், அங்குப் பிறந்தார்க்கு நற்கதியில்லை யென் றுங் கூறும் வடநாட்டார் கொள்கையை மேற்கொண்டு ஓராற்றான் மறுத்தவா றிது"],["shanmuganar","The south is Yama's quarter, the direction of death, rather than a region of people.","தென்னாட்டில் (யமதிசையில்) பிறந்த வரும் விண்ணுலகம் அடைவதால்"]],"ch":"n:25"},"n:244":{"book":"naladiyar","num":244,"t":["வேம்பின் இலையுள் கனியினும் வாழைதன்","தீஞ்சுவை யாதும் திரியாதாம் ஆங்கே","இனந்தீ தெனினும் இயல்புடையார் கேண்மை","மனந்தீதாம் பக்கம் அரிது."],"p":"Though ripened amid margosa leaves the fruit of the plantain loses no atom of its sweet flavour. Even so the friendship of men of noble mood, although their race be evil, can hardly work ill to the mind.","r":"Though ripened among neem leaves, the plantain keeps its sweet taste unchanged; so, though their company is bad, the friendship of those of good nature rarely turns to a bad mind.","n":"Pope reads இனம் as 'race'; both commentators read it as the company one keeps.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","panpu","natpu","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:25"},"n:245":{"book":"naladiyar","num":245,"t":["கடல்சார்ந்தும் இன்னீர் பிறக்கும், மலைசார்ந்தும்","உப்பீண் டுவரி பிறத்தலால் தத்தம்","இனத்தனையர் அல்லர் எறிகடற்றண் சேர்ப்ப","மனத்தனையர் மக்களென் பார்."],"p":"Though close by the sea, sweet waters oft-times spring up there; on the hill-side the waters often gush out all brine! Thus men are not as their race. — Lord of the dashing sea's cool shore! Men are as their minds.","r":"Lord of the cool shore of the dashing sea, since sweet water rises even beside the sea and brackish water even on the hills, people are not as their kind but as their minds.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","manam","kudi"],"pos":[["mudaliar","People are simply not like those of their own group; the mind decides.","தங்கள் தங்கள் இனத்தாரை யொத்தவ ரல்லர்"],["shanmuganar","People are judged not only by their group but by their minds as well, so group still counts.","தம்தம் இனத்திற்கு ஏற்பமட்டும் இருப்பவரல்லர்; தம்தம் மனத்திற்கு ஏற்பவும் மதிக்கப்படுவர்"]],"ch":"n:25"},"n:246":{"book":"naladiyar","num":246,"t":["பராஅரைப் புன்னை படுகடல் தண்சேர்ப்ப","ஒராஅலும் ஒட்டலுஞ் செய்பவோ? - நல்ல","ம்ருஉச்செய் தியார்மாட்டும் தங்கு மனத்தார்","விராஅஅய்ச் செய்யாமை நன்று."],"p":"Lord of the cool sea-shore, where flourishes the thick-stemmed laurel ! Men whose minds are good (constant), and who adhere to whomsoever they have formed an intimacy with, will not sometimes avoid men, and at other times be intimate. It is good not to have fits of alternating warmth and indifference.","r":"Lord of the cool shore where thick-trunked punnai trees grow, do people of steady mind, who stay with whomever they have formed a good bond, leave and then rejoin? It is good not to mix with people indiscriminately.","n":"Pope takes விராய் as alternating warmth and coldness; both commentators take it as mixing with people without examination.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","manam","aaraaydhal"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:25"},"n:247":{"book":"naladiyar","num":247,"t":["உணர உணரும் உணர்வுடை யாரைப்","புணரப் புணருமாம் இன்பம் - புணா஢ன்","தெரியத் தெரியும் தெரிவிலா தாரைப்","பிரியப் பிரியுமாம் நோய்."],"p":"Join the men who throughly feel true wisdom's inner sense, and forthwith joy joins you. Join yourself to men devoid of the accurate preception of knowledge, and then parting from them is parting from pain.","r":"Join those whose understanding grasps things as they should be grasped, and joy joins you; if you join those without discernment, the pain parts only when you part from them.","n":"The verse is built on repeated words (உணர உணரும், புணரப் புணரும், தெரியத் தெரியும், பிரியப் பிரியும்), a figure Mudaliar names.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","arivu","inbam","idumbai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:25"},"n:248":{"book":"naladiyar","num":248,"t":["நன்னிலைக்கண் தன்னை நிறுப்பானும், தன்னை","நிலைகலக்கிக் கீழிடு வானும் - நிலையினும்","மேன்மே லுயர்த்து நிறுப்பானும், தன்னைத்","தலையாகச் செய்வானும் தான்."],"p":"He that establishes a man in good, and he that disturbs that good position and casts him down, and he that more and more exalts a man and establishes him, and he that makes a man head (among men) is (the MAN) HIMSELF.","r":"The one who sets a person in a good state, the one who unsettles and casts him down, the one who raises him ever higher, and the one who makes him the head of others, is that person himself.","n":"Mudaliar names the cause as one's understanding or lack of it; the verse itself names none. It also names no fate, which sits uneasily with chapter 11 on old deeds, unless those deeds are counted as one's own doing too.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","sirumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:25"},"n:249":{"book":"naladiyar","num":249,"t":["கரும வரிசையால் கல்லாதார் பின்னும்","பெருமை யுடையாரும் சேறல் - அருமரபின்","ஓதம் அரற்றும் ஒலிகடல் தண்சேர்ப்ப","பேதைமை யன்ற தறிவு."],"p":"Lord of the cool shore of the sounding sea, where from old time the billows roar!— In the course of their affairs when even great men follow after the unlearned, this is not folly but wisdom.","r":"Lord of the cool shore of the roaring sea, when even the great follow behind the unlearned for the sake of a task, that is not folly but understanding.","n":"The verse allows status to be set aside for a purpose (கரும வரிசையால்); it doesn't say the great should follow the unlearned in general.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","perumai","kalvi","maanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:25"},"n:250":{"book":"naladiyar","num":250,"t":["கருமமு முட்படாப் போகமும் துவ்வாத்","தருமமும் தக்கார்க்கே செய்யா - ஒருநிலையே","முட்டின்றி மூன்று முடியமேல் அஃதென்ப","பட்டினம் பெற்ற கலம்."],"p":"If a man has wrought all fitting works, enjoyed all seemly pleasures, done deeds of charity to worthy men: if he shall have accomplished all these three unchecked, in this one state, of him men will say: ‘that is a ship that has gained the haren' -------------","r":"If one does one's work, enjoys one's pleasures, and does virtuous giving to the worthy, and all three are completed without hindrance in a single life, people call it a ship that has reached the port.","n":"A full worldly life is praised here, in a work that mostly urges renunciation, through the image of a trading voyage brought home. Pope reads ஒருநிலையே as 'in this one state'; Shanmuganar as 'all together'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","inbam","eegai","payan"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:25"},"n:251":{"book":"naladiyar","num":251,"t":["நுண்ணுணர் வின்மை வறுமை, அஃதுடைமை","பண்ணப் பணைத்த பெருஞ்செல்வம் - எண்ணுங்கால்","பெண்ணவாய் ஆணிழந்த பேடி யணியாளோ,","கண்ணவாத் தக்க கலம்."],"p":"The want of refined knowledge is poverty, its possession is very great and abundant wealth. When one considers, will not a sexless creature, more woman than man, adorn herself with the jewels that her eye desires ?","r":"Lack of fine understanding is poverty and having it is great wealth; when one thinks about it, a eunuch who longs to be a woman will still put on the jewels that catch every eye.","n":"The simile is left unexplained in the verse. Shanmuganar draws the conclusion that the wealth of the senseless is like the eunuch's jewels: an adornment on something that lacks the essential thing. Pope's heading puts it as 'mere externals are nothing'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","inmai","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:26"},"n:252":{"book":"naladiyar","num":252,"t":["பல்லான்ற கேள்விப் பயனுணர்வார் பாடழிந்து","அல்ல லுழப்ப தறிதிரேல் - தொல்சிறப்பின்","நாவின் கிழத்தி யுறைதலால் சேராளே","பூவின் கிழத்தி புலந்து."],"p":"Men of vast and varied lore are seen in low estate, and suffer want. Would you know the reason? The anciently renowned ‘Lady of the tongue' abides with them). ‘The Lady of the flowers' is jealous, and draws not near !","r":"If you would know why men of wide learning lose standing and suffer want: the ancient Lady of the Tongue lives with them, so the Lady of the Flower keeps away in a sulk.","n":"A playful explanation (Mudaliar names the figure தற்குறிப்பேற்றம், an imputed cause), not a theology of fortune. Mudaliar offers two reasons for the quarrel: Lakshmi and Sarasvati are mother-in-law and daughter-in-law, or two women do not share one man in peace.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","selvam","inmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:26"},"n:253":{"book":"naladiyar","num":253,"t":["கல்லென்று தந்தை கழற அதனையோர்","சொல்லென்று கொள்ளா திகழ்ந்தவன் - மெல்ல","எழுத்தோலை பல்லார்முன் நீட்ட விளியா","வழுக்கோலைக் கொண்டு விடும்."],"p":"He who, when his father urgently bade him learn, did not take it as a serious matter, but contemned it; when, before many men, some one gently presents a written palm-leaf, will in anger fetch a stick to beat him as guilty of an insult.","r":"One who scorned his father's urging to learn, not taking it as worth heeding, will grab a stick in anger when someone quietly holds out a written leaf to him before many people.","n":"The anger is the unlearned man's shame turned outward: he cannot read the leaf in public. Mudaliar records other readings of the last line, one in which he laughs in contempt, one in which he makes a noise to slip away.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kallaamai","kalvi","vekuli"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:26"},"n:254":{"book":"naladiyar","num":254,"t":["கல்லாது நீண்ட ஒருவன் உலகத்து","நல்லறி வாள ரிடைப்புக்கு - மெல்ல","இருப்பினும் நாயிருந் தற்றே, இராஅது","உரைப்பினும் நாய்குரைத் தற்று."],"p":"When a man who has grown up without learning enters the society of the wise, if he sit still, it is as if a dog sat there; and if he rise to speak, it is as though a dog barked.","r":"When a man who grew up without learning enters among the wise, if he sits quietly it is as if a dog sat there, and if he speaks it is as if a dog barked.","n":"Silence does not save him: Mudaliar allows மெல்ல இருப்பினும் to mean 'even if he sits with restraint', which makes the verse deny the unlearned any good way to be present.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kallaamai","avai","sol"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:26"},"n:255":{"book":"naladiyar","num":255,"t":["புல்லாப்புன் கோட்டிப் புலவ ரிடைப்புக்குக்","கல்லாத சொல்லும் கடையெல்லாம் - கற்ற","கடாஅயினும் சான்றவர் சொல்லார் பொருண்மேல்","படாஅ விடுபாக் கறிந்து."],"p":"All the baser sort consorting with scholars of a heterodox and low school, will utter illiterate rubbish; but men replete with learning, though urgently asked, utter not the results of their learning, knowing that (the asker) would fail to apply their minds to the import of what was said.","r":"The low all go among the poets of petty, senseless assemblies and say things they never learned; but the worthy, even when asked, do not speak of what they have learned, knowing it would not be taken in.","n":"Pope calls the assembly 'heterodox'; neither commentator says so. Mudaliar has it as an assembly not joined to understanding, and Shanmuganar's poets are false poets.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avai","saandror","sol","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:26"},"n:256":{"book":"naladiyar","num":256,"t":["கற்றறிந்த நாவினார் சொல்லார்தம் சோர்வஞ்சி","மற்றைய ராவார் பகர்வர் பனையின்மேல்","வற்றிய ஓலை கலகலக்கும் எஞ்ஞான்றும்","பச்சோலைக் கில்லை யொலி."],"p":"Men of learned tongues are silent, fearing some slip; others (ignorant men) will speak out; on the Palmyra tree the dried-up leaves make a loud rustling noise ; but evermore the green leaf gives forth no sound !","r":"The learned and eloquent hold back, afraid of a lapse, while others talk freely: on the palmyra the dry leaves rattle, but the green leaf never makes a sound.","n":"Mudaliar notes the images answer the statements in reverse order (green leaf to the learned, dry leaves to the rest). Silence here signals life and fullness, noise dryness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sol","kalvi","adakkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:26"},"n:257":{"book":"naladiyar","num":257,"t":["பன்றிக்கூழ்ப் பத்தரில் தேமா வடித்தற்றால்","நன்றறியா மாந்தர்க் கறத்தா றுரைக்குங்கால்","குன்றின்மேற் கொட்டுந் தறிபோல் தலைதகர்ந்து","சென்றிசையா வாகுஞ் செவிக்கு."],"p":"","r":"Telling the way of virtue to people who do not know the good is like pressing sweet mango juice into a pig's trough; like a stake driven on a rock, it splits at the head and does not go into their ears.","n":"Two similes for two failures: the teaching is wasted (the trough) and it cannot penetrate (the stake on rock). Pope's translation is missing for this quatrain, and Shanmuganar's commentary appears under 256.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","pedhaimai","sol"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:26"},"n:258":{"book":"naladiyar","num":258,"t":["பாலால் கழீஇப் பலநாள் உணக்கினும்","வாலிதாம் பக்கம் இருந்தைக் கிருந்தன்று","கோலால் கடாஅய்க் குறினும் புகலொல்லா","நோலா உடம்பிற் கறிவு."],"p":"Though you wash it with milk for many days and dry it, charcoal on no hypothesis becomes white ! So into the undisciplined body wisdom enters not, though you teach it, driving it in with a stick.","r":"Charcoal washed in milk and dried for many days never turns white, and understanding will not enter a body that has not earned it, though you drive it in with a stick.","n":"நோலா உடம்பு is the hinge. Both commentators take it as a body without merit from good deeds (Mudaliar: from a former birth, so without innate understanding); Pope reads 'undisciplined body', which moves the cause from past births to present discipline.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","vinai","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:26"},"n:259":{"book":"naladiyar","num":259,"t":["பொழிந்தினிது நாறினும் பூமிசைதல் செல்லாது","இழிந்தவை காமுறூஉம் ஈப்போல், - இழிந்தவை","தாங்கலந்த நெஞ்சினார்க் கென்னாகும் தக்கார்வாய்த்","தேன்கலந்த தேற்றச்சொல் தேர்வு."],"p":"To those whose minds are full of foul things, — like the fly which goes not to feed on the flower that pours forth sweetness and breathes perfume, but fixes its eager desire on ordure,—What clear comprehension can there be of the lucid words full of honied sweetness that issue from the mouths of the worthy?","r":"Like the fly that will not settle on a flower pouring honey and fragrance but craves filth, those whose minds are set on low things get nothing from the clear, honeyed words of the worthy.","n":"The fault is in desire, not capacity: the fly passes the flower by preference. Mudaliar notes that 'low things' is a polite way of naming filth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","sol","saandror"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:26"},"n:260":{"book":"naladiyar","num":260,"t":["கற்றா ருரைக்கும் கசடறு நுண்கேள்வி","பற்றாது தன்னெஞ் சுதைத்தலால் - மற்றுமோர்","தன்போ லொருவன் முகநோக்கித் தானுமோர்","புன்கோட்டி கொள்ளுமாம் கீழ்."],"p":"The base man does not apprehend the faultless words of accurate instruction which the learned utter. These pain his mind. He therefore looks in the face of some other one like himself (for encouragement), and convenes a wretched assembly of his own: i.e. He finds one like-minded, and the two set up a sect. -------------","r":"Because his heart kicks away the faultless, subtle teaching of the learned, the base man looks into the face of another like himself and sets up a petty assembly of his own.","n":"The rejection breeds a rival: two of a kind confirm each other. Pope reads it as founding a sect; Mudaliar's கோட்டி is the talk given there, Shanmuganar's a platform speech.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","kelvi","avai","pullarivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:26"},"n:261":{"book":"naladiyar","num":261,"t":["அருகல தாகிப் பலபழுத்தக் கண்ணும்","பொரிதாள் விளவினை வாவல் குறுகா","பெரிதணிய ராயினும் பீடிலார் செல்வம்","கருதும் கடப்பாட்ட தன்று."],"p":"The bat approaches not the Heronia, with its dry stem, though it be night at hand and bear abundant fruit; so although mean people are very close at hand, their wealth is not a thing that can be counted upon.","r":"Bats do not go to the wood-apple tree with its cracked trunk, though it stands near and bears much fruit; so the wealth of men without worth, however close they live, is not something anyone thinks of counting on.","n":"Nearness is the point: what is close but unapproachable is as good as far. Mudaliar specifies who does not count on it: the poor.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","sirumai","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:27"},"n:262":{"book":"naladiyar","num":262,"t":["அள்ளிக்கொள் வன்ன குறுமுகிழ வாயினும்","கள்ளிமேல் கைந்நீட்டார் சூடும்பூ அன்மையால்","செல்வம் பெரிதுடைய ராயினும் கீழ்களை","நள்ளார் அறிவுடை யார்."],"p":"Men reach not out their band to the Kalli (Cactus), though it bears delicate round buds by the handful, because these are not flowers they can weave into a garland to crown themselves withal; so wise people from no friendships with the base however great their wealth may be.","r":"No one reaches for the cactus, though its small buds look lovely enough to gather by the handful, because they are not flowers to wear; so the wise do not seek out the base, however rich.","n":"The cactus buds are attractive but unusable; wealth is the attraction, character the use. Shanmuganar's commentary on this quatrain appears under 261.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","kayamai","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:27"},"n:263":{"book":"naladiyar","num":263,"t":["மல்கு திரைய கடற்கோட் டிருப்பினும்,","வல்லூற் றுவரில் கிணற்றின்கண் சென்றுண்பர்","செல்வம் பெரிதுடைய ராயினும் சேண்சென்றும்","நல்குவார் கட்டே நசை."],"p":"Though men live on the curved shore of the sea with its multitudinous waves, they go and drink at the well, with its perennial fountain of fresh water from the rock; so even if those (who are neighbours) are very wealthy, the desire (of the poor) is towards the liberal, though these may be far to seek.","r":"Though they live on the shore of the wave-filled sea, people go and drink at a well with a strong, sweet spring; so though the rich are near, desire turns to the givers, even far away.","n":"The sea is huge and useless to drink; the small well gives. Size of wealth does not draw anyone, only its being given. Mudaliar's commentary is missing for this quatrain, and Shanmuganar's appears under 261.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","selvam","iravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:27"},"n:264":{"book":"naladiyar","num":264,"t":["புணர்கடல்சூழ் வையத்துப் புண்ணியமோ வேறே","உணர்வ துடையா ரிருப்ப - உணர்விலா","வட்டும் வழுதுணையும் போல்வாரும் வாழ்வரே","பட்டும் துகிலும் உடுத்து."],"p":"In the world surrounded by the (all-)embracing sea, merit is quite an indifferent matter! Understanding ones are (poor); and even those of no understanding—who are like mere palm tree tufts and brinjals—live prosperously, clad in silks and rich garments.","r":"In this sea-girt world merit is a thing apart: while men of understanding are there, even those with no understanding, like the dice-piece and the brinjal, live clad in silk and fine cloth.","n":"புண்ணியம் (merit) is set apart from understanding: prosperity follows merit, not sense. Mudaliar works out the two images: the dice-piece that returns to the board after being cut down (the shameless who come back to the assembly) and the brinjal that fruits hidden (those who hide their wealth). Shanmuganar's commentary is missing.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["selvam","arivu","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:27"},"n:265":{"book":"naladiyar","num":265,"t":["நல்லார் நயவர் இருப்ப நயமிலாக்","கல்லார்க்கொன் றாகிய காரணம் - தொல்லை","வினைப்பய னல்லது வேனெடுங் கண்ணாய்","நினைப்ப வருவதொன் றில்."],"p":"While pleasant folk and just abide (in poverty), you ponder why men unjust and ignorant have any joy. It is fruit of 'ancient deeds', thou whose long eyes are darts :—to thoughtful mind no other cause occurs.","r":"Long-eyed one, when the good and just remain poor, the only cause you will find on reflection for the unjust and unlearned having wealth is the fruit of old deeds.","n":"The verse answers the scandal of undeserved wealth with former deeds, and so denies that present wealth says anything about present worth. Mudaliar specifies good deeds of a former birth. Shanmuganar's commentary is missing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","selvam","oozh"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:27"},"n:266":{"book":"naladiyar","num":266,"t":["நாறாக் தகடேபோல் நன்மலர்மேற் பொற்பாவாய்","நீறாய் நிலத்து விளியரோ - வேறாய","புன்மக்கள் பக்கம் புகுவாய்நீ பொன்போலும்","நன்மக்கள் பக்கம் துறந்து."],"p":"O golden dame, that sittest like a scentless leaf on a beauteous flower! Die, and fall in ashes to the ground: thou enterest homes of worthless men of perverse mind, forsaking good men pure as gold.","r":"Golden lady seated on the fine flower like its scentless outer petal, may you die and turn to ash on the earth: you leave the good, who are like gold, and go to the side of worthless men.","n":"A curse on Lakshmi herself. Mudaliar explains the simile: as the outer petal lacks the flower's scent, the goddess who lives on the lotus has none of its softness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","kayamai","saandror"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:27"},"n:267":{"book":"naladiyar","num":267,"t":["நயவார்கண் நல்குரவு நாணின்று கொல்லோ","பயவார்கண் செல்வம் பரம்பப் பயின்கொல்","வியவாய்காண் வேற்கண்ணாய் இவ்விரண்டும் ஆங்கே","நயவாது நிற்கு நிலை."],"p":"Has poverty, that bides with men of righteous souls, no shame? Does wealth to ungenerous men stick like glue?O thou of dart-like eye, with wonder see that thus, no just discernment made, these two abide !","r":"Does poverty feel no shame in staying with the good? Is the wealth of the useless a glue that holds fast? See with wonder, dart-eyed one, how these two stay just where they ought not.","n":"Poverty and wealth are made into agents with manners: one shameless, the other sticky. The verse states the wonder without explaining it; 265 gives the explanation.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai","selvam","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:27"},"n:268":{"book":"naladiyar","num":268,"t":["வலவைக ளல்லாதார் காலாறு சென்று","கலவைகள் உண்டு கழிப்பர் - வலவைகள்","காலாறும் செல்லார் கருனையால் துய்ப்பவே","மேலாறு பாய விருந்து."],"p":"Men who are not void of shame will travel forth on foot, and feed on scraps, —so pass their days ; the shameless ones make no journeys on foot, but feed on dainties at home, perspiring over the feast.","r":"Those who are not shameless walk long roads and live on mixed scraps, while the shameless never stir a foot and feast at home on fried dishes as rivers of ghee and milk flow over them.","n":"Shame is what keeps the decent poor; its absence is what lets the others thrive. Mudaliar takes the scraps as begged food and the feasters as eating alone behind closed doors without giving; Shanmuganar names the feasters' fault as dishonesty and deceit.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","inmai","undi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:27"},"n:269":{"book":"naladiyar","num":269,"t":["பொன்னிறச் செந்நெல் பொதியொடு பீள்வாட","மின்னொளிர் வானங் கடலுள்ளுங் கான்றுகுக்கும்","வெண்மை யுடையார் விழுச்செல்வம் எய்தியக்கால்","வண்மையும் அன்ன தகைத்து."],"p":"While the red paddy's golden germ is parched within the ear and dies, the cloud gleaming with lightnings pours forth its treasures on the sea. When silly men gain ample wealth, even so are their liberal gifts bestowed !","r":"While the golden red paddy withers in the ear, the lightning-lit cloud pours its rain into the sea; the giving of fools who come into great wealth is of the same kind.","n":"The fault is not that fools never give but that they give where it is not needed. Mudaliar's 'also' (உம்மை) implies they otherwise do nothing good with wealth either.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","pedhaimai","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:27"},"n:270":{"book":"naladiyar","num":270,"t":["ஓதியும் ஓதார் உணர்விலார் ஓதாதும்","ஓதி யனையார் உணர்வுடையார் - தூய்தாக","நல்கூர்ந்தும் செல்வர் இரவாதார், செல்வரும்","நல்கூர்ந்தார் ஈயா ரெனின்."],"p":"Men void of understanding, though they learn, learn not! Men of understanding, though unlearned, are as men learned! They are rich, though utter paupers, who never beg; the rich are paupers if they bestow nothing ! -------------","r":"Those without understanding remain unlearned though they study, and those with understanding are as the learned though they have not studied; those who never beg are rich though poor, and the rich are poor if they do not give.","n":"Four reversals that make the inner quality, not the outward possession, decide the name. Mudaliar takes தூய்தாக as a pure mind, a condition on the poor who do not beg.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","kalvi","iravu","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:27"},"n:271":{"book":"naladiyar","num":271,"t":["நட்டார்க்கும் நள்ளா தவர்க்கும் உளவரையால்","அட்டது பாத்துண்டல் அட்டுண்டல் - ஆட்டது","அடைத்திருந் துண்டொழுகும் ஆவதின் மாக்கட்கு","அடைக்குமாம் ஆண்டைக் கதவு."],"p":"To eat your own meal, after sharing what you have cooked, to the extent of your ability, both with those who are friends and those who are not friends, is 'cooking and eating' (= is real house keeping). To the good-for-nothing human beings whose habit of life it is to shut themselves up and eat alone what they have cooked, the door of yonder world will be shut.","r":"To share what is cooked, as far as one has, with friends and with those who are not friends, and then eat, is what cooking and eating means; for the worthless who shut the door and eat inside, the door of the other world is shut.","n":"The punishment mirrors the fault: a shut door for a shut door. Mudaliar takes 'friends' as kin and 'not friends' as guests; Pope has 'friends and foes', which widens the duty further than either commentator.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","virundhu","undi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:28"},"n:272":{"book":"naladiyar","num":272,"t":["எத்துணை யானும் இயைந்த அளவினால்","சிற்றறஞ் செய்தார் தலைப்படுவர் - மற்றைப்","பெருஞ்செல்வம் எய்தியக்கால் பின்னறிதும் என்பார்","அழிந்தார் பழிகடலத் துள்."],"p":"Whatever the measure be, those who do even lesser acts of charity to the measure of their power shall attain to excellence. But those who, when they have obtained great wealth. say 'we will be wise (and give) by and by,' are lost in a sea of guilt.","r":"Those who do small acts of virtue, however little, within their means, rise; those who say they will think about it after they gain great wealth are lost in a sea of blame.","n":"The verse reverses the usual excuse: it is the small giver who is great and the one waiting for great wealth who drowns. Mudaliar notes that 'are lost' is past tense for certainty.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","eegai","kaalam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:28"},"n:273":{"book":"naladiyar","num":273,"t":["துய்த்துக் கழியான் துறவோர்க்கொன் றீகலான்","வைத்துக் கழியும் மடவோனை - வைத்த","பொருளும் அவனை நகுமே உலகத்து","அருளும் அவனை நகும்."],"p":"The senseless man who spends not time in enjoyment (of his wealth), and who gives nothing to pions devotees, but hoards and dies, —him his hoarded wealth derides; him all that is gracious in the world derides.","r":"The fool who neither enjoys his wealth nor gives anything to renunciants, but hoards it and dies, is laughed at by the very wealth he stored, and by the compassion in the world.","n":"The wealth itself is made to mock him: it outlives him and was never used. The gift named is to renunciants (துறவோர்), a pointer to the Jain setting. Shanmuganar's commentary is missing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ivaral","selvam","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:28"},"n:274":{"book":"naladiyar","num":274,"t":["கொடுத்தலும் துய்த்தலும் தேற்றா இடுக்குடை","உள்ளத்தான் பெற்ற பெருஞ்செல்வம் - இல்லத்து","உருவுடைக் கன்னியரைப் போலப் பருவத்தால்","ஏதிலான் துய்க்கப் படும்."],"p":"The great wealth obtained by the man of straitened soul, who knows not how either to give or to enjoy, shall be enjoyed by a stranger, in due season—like lovely virgins remaining unmarried in the dwelling.","r":"The great wealth of the tight-hearted man who knows neither how to give nor how to enjoy will in due season be enjoyed by a stranger, like a lovely daughter of the house.","n":"The daughter is raised in the house and goes to an outsider when she comes of age; the image makes the loss natural and certain, not a mishap.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ivaral","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:28"},"n:275":{"book":"naladiyar","num":275,"t":["எறிநீர்ப் பெருங்கடல் எய்தி யிருந்தும்","அறுநீர்ச் சிறுகிணற் றூறல்பார்த் துண்பர்","மறுமை யறியாதா ராக்கத்தின் சான்றோர்","கழிநல் குரவே தலை."],"p":"Though they have got the mighty sea with its dashing waves (to drink from), men wait for the stream slowly issuing from the little well, often dry, and drink there; so the exceeding poverty of the virtuous is preferable to the wealth of those who know not of the world to come.","r":"Though they live by the great sea with its dashing waves, people wait at a small, nearly dry well for its trickle and drink; so the deep poverty of the worthy is better than the wealth of those who know nothing of the next world.","n":"The sea is plenty that cannot be used; the small well is little that can. Shanmuganar takes 'knowing the next world' as knowing and doing the good deeds that belong to it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saandror","inmai","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:28"},"n:276":{"book":"naladiyar","num":276,"t":["எனதென தென்றிருக்கும் ஏழை பொருளை","எனதென தென்றிருப்பன் யானும் - தனதாயின்","தானும் அதனை வழங்கான் பயன்துவ்வான்","யானும் அதனை அது."],"p":"As to the property which the wretched churl claims saying 'It is mine, it is mine ,' I too chime in with \"It is mine, it is mine;' for if it is his, he himself spends it not, nor enjoys the benefit of it; and I, too, neither spend nor enjoy it!","r":"The wealth the fool keeps calling 'mine, mine', I too call 'mine, mine': if it is his, he neither gives it nor enjoys it, and neither do I.","n":"A joke with a sting: ownership that produces no use is no different from not owning. Shanmuganar's commentary on this quatrain appears under 275.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ivaral","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:28"},"n:277":{"book":"naladiyar","num":277,"t":["வழங்காத செல்வரின் நல்கூர்ந்தார் உய்ந்தார்","இழந்தா ரெனப்படுதல் உய்ந்தார் - உழந்ததனைக்","காப்புய்ந்தார் கல்லுதலும் உய்ந்தார்தங் கைந்நோவ","யாப்புயந்தார் உய்ந்த பல."],"p":"The poor have escaped much from which rich men that dispense not suffer. They have escaped the reputation of having lost (their substance). They have escaped the toil of saving it. They have escaped (the labour of) digging (to hide it). They have escaped the ache of hands securing it from powerful plunderers. Many are the (sorrows) they have escaped.","r":"The poor have escaped much that troubles the rich who do not give: being called ruined, the toil of guarding, the digging to bury it, the aching hands that clutch it; many are their escapes.","n":"The list is ironic consolation, not praise of poverty: the comparison is only with the miser. Pope reads the last escape as tying it up against powerful plunderers; Mudaliar as gripping it tight.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai","ivaral","idumbai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:28"},"n:278":{"book":"naladiyar","num":278,"t":["தனதாகத் தான்கொடான் தாயத் தவரும்","தமதாய போழ்தே கொடாஅர் - தனதாக","முன்னே கொடுப்பின் அவர்கடியார் தான்கடியான்","பின்னை அவர்கொடுக்கும் போழ்து."],"p":"While it was his he gave not; and his heirs, how it is theirs, give not. Before, while it was his, if he had given, they would not have reproved (him); and afterwards, if they had given, he would not have reproved them! Men's own avarice is the sole reason for the lack of charity.","r":"While it was his he did not give, and his heirs do not give once it is theirs; had he given first, they would not have stopped him, and when they give later, he cannot stop them.","n":"The argument removes every excuse: nobody prevented either party from giving. Pope's added sentence states the moral, that only one's own avarice stops giving.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ivaral","eegai","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:28"},"n:279":{"book":"naladiyar","num":279,"t":["இரவலர் கன்றாக ஈவார்ஆ வாக","விரகிற் சுரப்பதாம் வண்மை - விரகின்றி","வல்லவர் ஊன்ற வடிஆபோல் வாய்வைத்துக்","கொல்லச் சுரப்பதாம் கீழ்."],"p":"","r":"Liberality is to yield gladly, as a cow does to its calf, the askers being the calf and the givers the cow; the base yield, like a cow milked by force, only when strong men press and torment them.","n":"The same act, giving, is good or base by the way it comes out. Mudaliar records several readings of வாய் வைத்து: by many devices, by reviling, or with instruments. Pope's translation is missing for this quatrain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","kayamai","iravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:28"},"n:280":{"book":"naladiyar","num":280,"t":["ஈட்டலும் துன்பமற் றீட்டிய வொண்பொருளைக்","காத்தலும் ஆங்கே கடுந்துன்பம் - காத்தல்","குறைபடில் துன்பம் கெடில்துன்பம் துன்பக்கு","உறைபதி மற்றைப் பொருள்."],"p":"Gathering it together is trouble, and even so the guarding of resplendent wealth is severe trouble. If the guarded heap diminish, it is trouble. If it perish, it is trouble. Wealth is trouble's very dwelling-place! ---------------","r":"Gaining wealth is suffering, guarding it is bitter suffering, its dwindling is suffering and its loss is suffering: wealth is the very dwelling place of suffering.","n":"The verse speaks of wealth itself, not only the miser's, though the chapter setting is not giving. Every stage, from earning to loss, is suffering, so no stage is safe. Shanmuganar's commentary is missing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","idumbai","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:28"},"n:281":{"book":"naladiyar","num":281,"t":["அத்திட்ட கூறை அரைச்சுற்ற வாழினும்","பத்தெட் டுடைமை பலருள்ளும் பாடெய்தும்","ஒத்த குடிப்பிறந்தக் கண்ணுமொன் றில்லாதார்","செத்த பிணத்தின் கடை."],"p":"Though he wraps a cloth dyed red around his loins, a dozen coins or so, will gain (the wretch) respect among many men! The man devoid of wealth, though born of noble race, is viler (in the world's estimation) than a lifeless corpse !","r":"Even one who lives with a dyed cloth wrapped round his waist gains standing among many if he has ten or eight coins; those who have nothing, though born into a fitting family, are lower than a corpse.","n":"The ochre or patched cloth (Mudaliar gives both) marks someone of no standing; a handful of coins outweighs it, while noble birth without money counts for less than a dead body. The verse reports how the world judges, without endorsing it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai","selvam","kudi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:29"},"n:282":{"book":"naladiyar","num":282,"t":["நீரினும் நுண்ணிது நெய்யென்பார், நெய்யினும்","யாரும் அறிவர் புகைநுட்பம் - தேரின்","நிரப்பிடும்பை யாளன் புகுமே, புகையும்","புகற்கா஢ய பூழை நுழைந்து."],"p":"Where water cannot enter! the more insinuating ghi glides in; and smoke as a subtler power to penetrate than even ghi. If you look into it, the man debased by poverty will enter haunts where smoke scarce finds a way.","r":"Ghee is said to be finer than water and everyone knows smoke is finer than ghee; but if you consider it, the man in the affliction of want gets in through openings even smoke can hardly pass.","n":"A comic hyperbole of need pressing in anywhere for help. Mudaliar's first reading divides the phrase as இரப்பு இடும்பை, the affliction of begging, and gives நிரப்பு, poverty, as another; Shanmuganar and Pope take poverty.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai","iravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:29"},"n:283":{"book":"naladiyar","num":283,"t":["கல்லோங் குயர்வரைமேல் காந்தள் மலராக்கால்","செல்லாவாம் செம்பொறி வண்டினம் - கொல்லைக்","கலாஅற் கிளிகடியுங் கானக நாட","இலாஅஅர்க் கில்லை தமர்."],"p":"When on the high hill's crags the Kanthal blooms no more, the crimson-spotted beetle tribe seek not its boughs;—Lord of the hills wherefrom they scare parrots with stones! — the needy have no kin.","r":"Lord of the forest land where they scare parrots from the millet with stones: when the kanthal does not flower on the high rocky hill, the red-spotted bees do not go there; the poor have no kin.","n":"Kin are bees and wealth the flower: they come for what is given, not for the person. The address to a hill chief borrows the frame of the akam poems.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:29"},"n:284":{"book":"naladiyar","num":284,"t":["உண்டாய போழ்தின் உடைந்துழிக் காகம்போல்","தொண்டரா யிரவர் தொகுபவே - வண்டாய்த்","திரிதரும் காலத்துத் தீதிலிரோ என்பார்","ஒருவரும் இல்வுலகத் தில்."],"p":"When wealth is there, obsequious myriads will assemble, like crows around the fallen corpse. When wealth, as the beetle wheels its flight, is gone, no one in all the world will ask, ‘Is it well with you?'","r":"While there is wealth, a thousand gather as servants, like crows at a carcass; when one wanders like a bee, no one in the world asks 'are you well?'","n":"The crows at the carcass make the flatterers feeders on the rich man, not friends. Both commentators make the poor man the one wandering like a bee for food; Pope makes the wealth the thing that flies off like a beetle.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai","sutram","natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:29"},"n:285":{"book":"naladiyar","num":285,"t":["பிறந்த குலமாயும் பேராண்மை மாயும்","சிறந்ததங் கல்வியும் மாயும் - கறங்கருவி","கன்மேல் க்ழுஉங் கணமலை நன்னாட","இன்மை தழுவப்பட் டார்க்கு."],"p":"Lord of the pleasant land of clustering hills whose crags are washed by sounding waterfalls !—Their race is nought, their manly prowess is nought, their rare learning is nought, when men are held in poverty's embrace.","r":"Lord of the good land of clustered hills where loud waterfalls wash the rocks: for those whom poverty has seized, the family they were born into, great manliness and their excellent learning all perish.","n":"The three goods named are ones normally thought independent of wealth: birth, character and learning. Mudaliar lists the qualities of ஆண்மை as fullness, forbearance, discernment and firmness, or else as valour.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai","kudi","aanmai","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:29"},"n:286":{"book":"naladiyar","num":286,"t":["உள்கூர் பசியால் உழைநசைஇச் சென்றார்கட்கு","உள்ளூ ரிருந்துமோன் றாற்றாதான் - உள்ளூர்","இருந்துயிர் கொன்னே கழியாது தான்போய்","விருந்தினன் ஆதலே நன்று."],"p":"Although he dwells within the village, and sees the poor draw nigh with hungry soul desiring aid, he yields them none : why then in the village does he vainly pass his days? It were better he went to be a guest himself !","r":"One who lives in his own village but can give nothing to those who come to him driven by hunger had better go away and be a guest somewhere than waste his life staying there.","n":"Who is meant decides the tone. On Mudaliar's reading it is advice to a poor man, matching 288; on Shanmuganar's and Pope's it is scorn for a miser, and Shanmuganar lets 'guest' also mean a guest of the other world, that is, death.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","inmai","virundhu"],"pos":[["mudaliar","The man is poor and unable to help, and is advised to go and live as a guest elsewhere.","ஓர் உதவியுஞ் செய்ய மாட்டாத வறியவன்"],["shanmuganar","The man is a miser who gives nothing, and would do better to leave for another place or the other world.","உள்ளூரில் வாழ்ந்தும் ஒன்றும் கொடுக்காத கருமி"]],"ch":"n:29"},"n:287":{"book":"naladiyar","num":287,"t":["நீர்மையே யன்றி நிரம்ப எழுந்ததங்","கூர்மையும் எல்லாம் ஒருங்கிழப்பர் - கூர்மையின்","முல்லை அலைக்கும் எயிற்றாய். நிரப்பென்னும்","அல்லல் அடையப்பட் டார்."],"p":"O thou whose teeth vie in sharpness with jasmine buds! — When sharp distress of poverty assails, men lose all their attributes of goodness at once, with the mind's acuteness gained from amplest stores of wisdom.","r":"Girl whose teeth outdo the sharp jasmine buds: those overtaken by the affliction called want lose at once not only their good nature but their fully risen sharpness of mind and everything else.","n":"The verse plays on கூர்மை: the sharpness lost by the poor is held up against the sharpness of the girl's teeth. Mudaliar notes that நிரப்பு, literally fullness, is used here for its opposite. Shanmuganar's commentary appears under 286.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai","panpu","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:29"},"n:288":{"book":"naladiyar","num":288,"t":["இட்டாற்றுப் பட்டொன் றிரந்தவர்க் காற்றாது","முட்டாற்றுப் பட்டும் முயன்றுள்ளூர் வாழ்தலின்","நெட்டாற்றுச் சென்று நிரைமனையில் கைந்நீட்டும்","கெட்டாற்று வாழ்க்கையே நன்று."],"p":"","r":"Rather than live on in one's own village, straitened and blocked and struggling, unable to give to one who asks, it is better to go a long way and live the ruined life of holding out one's hand at row after row of houses.","n":"Begging is ranked above the shame of refusing an asker at home. Mudaliar says it repeats 286 and cites Kural 478 for இட்டு (narrow). Pope's translation is missing for this quatrain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai","iravu","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:29"},"n:289":{"book":"naladiyar","num":289,"t":["கடகம் செறிந்ததங் கைகளால் வாங்கி","அடகு பறித்துக்கொண் டட்டுக் - குடைகலனா","உப்பிலி வெந்தைதின் றுள்ளற்று வாழ்பவே,","துப்புரவு சென்றுலந்தக் கால்."],"p":"The hands once loaded with golden bracelets now cull the forest-herb and cook the meal; and then eat the mess unseasoned, from the palm-leaf for a dish! Thus sad at heart they live, when fortune is gone and ruin come.","r":"When their means are spent and gone, with the hands that once wore bracelets they pull down branches, pick greens, cook them, and eat the boiled leaves without salt from a folded palm-leaf, living on with broken spirit.","n":"The bracelets on the hands that now strip branches carry the whole fall in one image. Mudaliar cites Kalittokai for குடை as a palm-leaf vessel.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai","nilaiyaamai","undi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:29"},"n:290":{"book":"naladiyar","num":290,"t":["ஆர்த்த பொறிய அணிகிளர் வண்டினம்","பூத்தொழி கொம்பின்மேல் செல்லாவாம் - நீர்த்தருவி","தாழா உயர்சிறப்பின் தண்குன்ற நன்னாட","வாழாதார்க் கில்லை தமர்."],"p":"The humming spotted beetle tribes all bright in hue gather not on the branch that has ceased to blow. Lord of the good cool hilly land, of high renown, whence bounteous streams flow down unceasingly—the unprosperous have no kin! --------------","r":"Lord of the cool, good hill land whose waterfalls never fail: the bright, humming, spotted bees do not go to the branch that has flowered and finished; those who have lost their prosperity have no kin.","n":"Mudaliar notes it shares the thought of 283. The difference is small: here the branch has flowered and finished, so the stress falls on prosperity once had and lost.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:29"},"n:291":{"book":"naladiyar","num":291,"t":["திருமதுகை யாகத் திறனிலார் செய்யும்","பெருமிதம் கண்டக் கடைத்தும் - எரிமண்டிக்","கானந் தலைப்பட்ட தீப்போல் கனலுமே,","மான முடையார் மனம்."],"p":"The mind of those possessed of honourable feeling will kindle into flame, like the fire that has caught the forest when the conflagration rages, whenever they behold the haughty acts of those who are destitute of virtuous habits, and to whom their wealth is their only strength.","r":"Whenever they see the arrogance of the worthless, whose only strength is their wealth, the minds of men of honour blaze like fire raging through a forest.","n":"The fire is inward: the verse says the mind burns, not that they act on it. Mudaliar quotes a definition of மானம் as not falling from one's station and not living on when brought low by fate.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam","selvam","vekuli"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:30"},"n:292":{"book":"naladiyar","num":292,"t":["என்பாய் உகினும் இயல்பிலார் பின்சென்று","தம்பா டுரைப்பரோ தம்முடையார் - தம்பாடு","உரையாமை முன்னுணரும் ஒண்மை யுடையார்க்கு","உரையாரோ தாமுற்ற நோய்."],"p":"Will (honourable men who are) 'masters of themselves' follow graceless men to tell of their sufferings, tbough fallen away to mere skeletons ? Do they not (rather) tell the pain they have felt to those enlightened souls that understand their sufferings before they speak ?","r":"Will those who own themselves follow the graceless to tell of their plight, even if they waste to bone? Will they not tell their pain to the clear-sighted, who grasp it before it is spoken?","n":"Honour here does not forbid asking; it chooses whom to ask. The right patron reads the need before it is said, so the honourable never have to beg in words.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam","iravu","kurippu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:30"},"n:293":{"book":"naladiyar","num":293,"t":["யாமாயின் எம்மில்லம் காட்டுதும் தாமாயின்","காணவே கற்பழியும் என்பார்போல் - நாணிப்","புறங்கடை வைத்தீவர் சோறும் அதனால்","மறந்திடுக செல்வர் தொடர்பு."],"p":"As for ourselves we would introduce them (these friends) to our household : but as regards them, they are ashamed (of us), as though they said, as soon as they (the ladies) saw them (the friends,) it would be the destruction of their womanly reserve; and so they seat us at the backdoor and give us rice! Therefore let us dismiss all thought of rich men's friendship.","r":"We would bring our guests into our homes, but they, as if their wives' chastity would be lost if we so much as saw them, shame-faced, set rice before us at the back door; so forget the friendship of the rich.","n":"Both commentators take the poor speaker as one who would let his wife serve guests, while the rich hide theirs and feed the poor at the back. Mudaliar notes another reading in which இல்லம் is simply the house and கற்பு is firmness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam","natpu","selvam","virundhu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:30"},"n:294":{"book":"naladiyar","num":294,"t":["இம்மையும் நன்றாம் இயல்நெறியும் கைவிடாது","உம்மையும் நல்ல பயத்தலால் - செம்மையின்","நானம் கமழும் கதுப்பினாய். நன்றேகாண்","மான முடையார் மதிப்பு."],"p":"O thou whose locks diffuse the odour of pleasant musk! Behold, especially good is (it to cultivate) that which is esteemed by men of honourable mind; for in this world it is (obviously) good : and, since it leads to perseverance in the way of virtue, it will yield good things in yonder world also.","r":"Girl with hair fragrant with musk, see how good it is, the esteem of men of honour: it does good in this life and, never leaving the right way, yields good things in the next.","n":"Honour is justified by its fruit in both worlds, which makes it a part of virtue rather than pride. Mudaliar glosses மதிப்பு as the principle men of honour hold, and notes another reading: being well regarded by others.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:30"},"n:295":{"book":"naladiyar","num":295,"t":["பாவமும் ஏனைப் பழியும் படவருவ","சாயினும் சான்றவர் செய்கலார் - சாதல்","ஒருநாள் ஒருபொழுதைத் துன்பம் அவைபோல்","அருநவை ஆற்றுதல் இன்று."],"p":"The men 'fulfilled of excellence,' though death were the alternative, do not deeds that entail sin and guilt. Death is an affliction for one day, and for a little while. There is nothing that works irreparable ill like those (deeds).","r":"The worthy will not do what brings sin and blame, even if they must die: death is the pain of one day, one moment, and nothing does such lasting harm as those.","n":"The argument is a calculation of duration: death's pain is brief, sin and blame's are not. Mudaliar separates them: sin follows one into later lives, blame stays in this world.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saandror","pazhi","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:30"},"n:296":{"book":"naladiyar","num":296,"t":["மல்லன்மா ஞாலத்து வாழ்பவ ருள்ளெல்லாம்","செல்வ ரெனினும் கொடாதவர் நல்கூர்ந்தார்","நல்கூர்ந்தக் கண்ணும் பெருமுத் தரையரே,","செல்வரைச் சென்றிரவா தார்."],"p":"Poor are the men that give not, even though deemed wealthiest of all that flourish on the teeming apple earth! They who even when they are poor seek not as suppliants wealthy men are 'Lords of the three mighty lands.'","r":"Among all who live in this rich, wide world, those who do not give are poor even if they are rich; and those who, even in poverty, do not go and beg from the rich are the great Muttaraiyar.","n":"பெருமுத்தரையர் is a name of liberality: Mudaliar takes them as generous patrons of the author's time, also named in an earlier quatrain, and Shanmuganar as a royal line; Pope translates the name as 'lords of the three mighty lands'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","iravu","inmai","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:30"},"n:297":{"book":"naladiyar","num":297,"t":["கடையெலாம் காய்பசி அஞ்சுமற் றேனை","இடையெலாம் இன்னாமை அஞ்சும் - புடைபரந்த","விற்புருவ வேனெடுங் கண்ணாய் தலையெல்லாம்","சொற்பழி அஞ்சி விடும்."],"p":"Thou who hast long dart-like eyes with eye-brows extending far, like a bow! the lowest class of men dread burning hunger; the other (or middle class) dread what is unpleasant; all the chief of men fear words that impute crime.","r":"Girl of long dart-like eyes under wide bow-brows: the lowest all fear burning hunger, the middle fear hardship, and the highest fear only the blame in people's words.","n":"A scale of persons by what they fear. Shanmuganar adds what follows from each fear: the lower will do anything, the highest refrain from wrong.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pazhi","acham","pasi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:30"},"n:298":{"book":"naladiyar","num":298,"t":["நல்லர் பெரிதளியர் நல்கூர்ந்தார் என்றெள்ளிச்","செல்வர் சிறுநோக்கு நோக்குங்கால் - கொல்லன்","உலையூதும் தீயேபோல் உள்கனலும் கொல்லோ,","தலையாய சான்றோர் மனம்."],"p":"When the very worthy and thoroughly learned see the rich men's glance of disparagement, as they say contemptuously, 'These are good people —persons in greatly reduced circumstances—poor folks,' does not their mind kindle into flame within them, like the fire by the breath of the bellows on the blacksmith's forge?","r":"When the rich look down on them and say with contempt, 'good people, very kind, now poor', the minds of the foremost worthy may burn within like fire blown in a smith's forge.","n":"The insult is disguised as praise. Both commentators take அளியர் as kind or compassionate, and Shanmuganar makes the kindness the cause of the poverty; Pope takes it as pitiable, 'in greatly reduced circumstances'. Mudaliar glosses எற்றி where the verse has எள்ளி.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam","saandror","inmai","vekuli"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:30"},"n:299":{"book":"naladiyar","num":299,"t":["நச்சியார்க் கீயாமை நாணன்று நாணாளும்","அச்சத்தால் நாணுதல் நாணன்றாம் - எச்சத்தின்","மெல்லிய ராகித்தம் மேலாயார் செய்தது","சொல்லா திருப்பது நாண்."],"p":"It is no shame (disgrace) not to (be able to give to those who desire it of us. The shrinking on account of fear (felt) day by day is not shame (modesty). But to become reduced in other ways, and not to [dare to] tell what injuries those who love us not have inflicted on us is shame (disgrace, or self-respect).","r":"Not giving to those who come wanting is no shame, and shrinking each day from fear is no shame; the shame, when one is reduced by want, is to leave unspoken what one's superiors did to one.","n":"What the superiors did is left unnamed, so the verse can mean either keeping silent about their slights or failing to acknowledge their help, and the sense of நாண் shifts with it. Mudaliar gives both readings; Pope takes it as not daring to tell their injuries.","x":1,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naanam","maanam","nanri"],"pos":[["mudaliar","His main reading: what the richer did is the slight they showed, which one does not speak of; he also reports the reading that one fails to proclaim their help.","தம்மினும் மேலான செல்வர்கள் தமக்குச் செய்த எளிமையை"],["shanmuganar","The greatest shame is failing to speak gratefully of help one's superiors gave in one's poverty.","தம்மினும் மேலானவர் தமக்குச் செய்த உதவியை நன்றியுடன் வெளியில் சொல்லாதிருப்பதே"]],"ch":"n:30"},"n:300":{"book":"naladiyar","num":300,"t":["கடமா தொலைச்சிய கானுறை வேங்கை","இடம்வீழ்ந்த துண்ணா திறக்கும் - இடமுடைய","வானகம் கையுறினும் வேண்டார் விழுமியோர்","மானம் மழுங்க வரின்."],"p":"The jungle-haunting tiger that slays the wild cow, refuses to eat and passes by what has fallen (of itself) in its path (i. e. carrion); so the excellent, though the wide realms of heaven were within their reach, would not desire them, if to be obtained (only) by the loss of honour. --------------","r":"The forest tiger that has killed a great beast will starve to death rather than eat it if it falls on its left; so the excellent would not want even wide heaven placed in their hands if it came at the dulling of their honour.","n":"Both commentators take கடமா as the elephant (Mudaliar also gives wild cow) and இடம் வீழ்ந்தது as falling on the left, citing a Purananuru poem on the tiger's custom; Pope reads a wild cow and carrion fallen in its path. Heaven itself, not just wealth, is refused.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:30"},"n:301":{"book":"naladiyar","num":301,"t":["நம்மாலே யாவரிந் நல்கூர்ந்தார் எஞ்ஞான்றும்","தம்மாலாம் ஆக்கம் இலரென்று - தம்மை","மருண்ட மனத்தார்பின் செல்பவோ தாமும்","தெருண்ட அறிவி னவர்."],"p":"Will men themselves possessed of clear discerning knowledge follow after those who have yielded themselves up to mental bewilderment, and who (erroneously) say (of suppliants), ‘These poor folks depend entirely on us, evermore are they without resources in themselves ?'","r":"Would people of clear understanding follow after the self-deluded rich, who tell themselves that the poor live only through them and have no means of their own?","n":"The fault named is the patron's self-importance, not his stinginess. Mudaliar glosses தம்மை மருண்ட as being dazed by one's own estimate of oneself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","maanam","sirumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:31"},"n:302":{"book":"naladiyar","num":302,"t":["இழித்தக்க செய்தொருவன் ஆர உணலின்","பழித்தக்க செய்யான் பசித்தல் தவறோ?","விழித்திமைக்கு மாத்திரை யன்றோ ஒருவன்","அழித்துப் பிறக்கும் பிறப்பு."],"p":"Is not a man's dying and birth again measured by the twinkling of an eye? Is it then a fault if a man rather choose to suffer hunger, doing no blameworthy actions, than to feed full, doing things that entail disgrace ?","r":"Since dying and being born again takes no longer than opening the eyes and blinking, is it wrong for a man to go hungry without doing anything blameworthy rather than eat his fill by doing what degrades him?","n":"The argument rests on the shortness of a life: a full stomach bought with disgrace feeds a body that will be gone in a blink. Mudaliar gives three ways to take the eye image, one of them sleeping and waking, and cites Kural 339 for it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasi","pazhi","piravi","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:31"},"n:303":{"book":"naladiyar","num":303,"t":["இல்லாமை கந்தா இரவு துணிந்தொருவர்","செல்லாரும் அல்லர் சிறுநெறி - புல்லா","அகம்புகுமின் உண்ணுமின் என்பவர்மாட் டல்லான்","முகம்புகுதல் ஆற்றுமோ மேல்?"],"p":"There will never be wanting those who, with their destitution as their support (making it their excuse), will venture upon mendicancy, and tread the way of humiliation; but will the noble-minded man enter (as a suppliant) the presence of any save of those who will embrace him and say, 'Enter my dwelling, eat of my food ?'","r":"Some, leaning on their poverty, will dare to beg and take the mean road, but the worthy go before no one except those who embrace them and say 'come in, eat'.","n":"Mudaliar reads முகம் புகுதல் as putting up with a patron's frown, and Shanmuganar as showing one's own face to a patron. Either way, the only giver the worthy approach is one who receives them as a guest.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","maanam","virundhu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:31"},"n:304":{"book":"naladiyar","num":304,"t":["திருத்தன்னை நீப்பினும் தெய்வம் செறினும்","உருத்த மனத்தோ டுயர்வுள்ளி னல்லால்","அருத்தம் செறிக்கும் அறிவிலார் பின்சென்று","எருத்திறைஞ்சி நில்லாதாம் மேல்."],"p":"Though fortune forsake him, and fate frown, the man of lofty soul, dwelling with steadfast mind on things above, disdains to stand with bending neck in the train of the foolish who hoard their wealth.","r":"Though fortune leaves him and the gods turn against him, the man of worth keeps his mind on his own high standing and never stands with bowed neck behind fools who hoard their wealth.","n":"திரு and தெய்வம் are the two supports a person might count on, and the verse takes both away before asking for steadiness. The stance is independence from fortune, not defiance of it. Mudaliar takes உருத்த மனம் as a mind roused with energy.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam","ookkam","oozh","ivaral"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:31"},"n:305":{"book":"naladiyar","num":305,"t":["கரவாத திண்ணன்பின கண்ணன்னார் கண்ணும்","இரவாது வாழ்வதாம் வாழ்க்கை - இரவினை","உள்ளுங்கால் உள்ளம் உருகுமால் என்கொலோ","கொள்ளுங்கால் கொள்வார் குறிப்பு."],"p":"To live asking naught even from those dear as an eye, whose love is sure, and who never refuse, is happy life. Since the mind dissolves in shame at the very thought of beggary,— when men receive alms, what are the receivers' thoughts, I pray ?","r":"Real living is to live without asking even of those dear as one's eyes, whose love is firm and who hold nothing back; the heart melts at the mere thought of begging, so what must the asker feel at the moment of taking?","n":"The last line moves from the giver to what the receiver feels while taking. Mudaliar reads கொள்வார் குறிப்பு as the taker's state of mind; Shanmuganar names it as shame.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","maanam","manam","karavu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:31"},"n:306":{"book":"naladiyar","num":306,"t":["இன்னா இயைக இனிய ஒழிகென்று","தன்னையே தானிரப்பத் தீர்வதற் - கென்னைகொல்","காதல் கவற்றும் மனத்தினாற் கண்பாழ்பட்டு","ஏதி லவரை இரவு."],"p":"When a man himself, begs of himself, saying, ‘Let troubles come, let joys depart!' the sting of poverty is extracted; why then, for this purpose, should any one, his mind agitated with desire, and his eye wasted with weeping, beg of strangers ?","r":"When poverty can be ended by asking of oneself, 'let pain come, let pleasures go', why should anyone, with a mind racked by desire and eyes sunk, go begging of strangers?","n":"Mudaliar divides தானிரப்ப two ways: தான் நிரப்ப, filling oneself with contentment, or தான் இரப்ப, begging of oneself. Pope takes the second and Shanmuganar the first. On either reading the cure for poverty is inward: consenting to hardship removes the want that drives begging.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","inmai","avaa","samanilai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:31"},"n:307":{"book":"naladiyar","num":307,"t":["என்றும் புதியார் பிறப்பினும் இவ்வுலகத்","தென்று மவனே பிறக்கலான் - குன்றின்","பரப்பெலாம் பொன்னொழுகும் பாயருவி நாட","இரப்பாரை எள்ளா மகன்."],"p":"Lord of the land where gold is borne down by rushing waterfalls from every hilly slope! — Though in this world new (kinds of) men are continually born, that very man is never born that does not scorn mendicants.","r":"Though new people are born into this world every day, the man who does not scorn beggars has never been born, lord of the hill country where waterfalls carry gold down every slope.","n":"Mudaliar gives two reasons for the overstatement: such a man reaches the good state and is not born again, or he simply cannot be found. The first plays on பிறக்கலான் ('is not born').","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","sirumai","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:31"},"n:308":{"book":"naladiyar","num":308,"t":["புறுத்துத்தன் இன்மை நலிய அகத்துத்தன்","நன்ஞானம் நீக்கி நிறீஇ ஒருவனை","ஈயாய் எனக்கென் றிரப்பானேல் அந்நிலையே","மாயானோ மாற்றி விடின்."],"p":"Want wastes his outward frame; he lays aside wisdom, his inward being's good; and so resolving, begs of some stranger, saying, 'Give to me.’— If then the boon be refused, must he not that very instant die?","r":"With want wasting his body outside and his good sense put away inside, a man goes to another and says 'give to me'; if he is refused, will he not die on the spot?","n":"Two losses come before the refusal: the body already worn down by want, and the understanding set aside so that asking is possible at all. Shanmuganar adds that the death would come from shame.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","inmai","maanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:31"},"n:309":{"book":"naladiyar","num":309,"t":["ஒருவ ரொருவரைச் சார்ந்தொழுகல் ஆற்றி","வழிபடுதல் வல்லுத லல்லால் - பரிசழிந்து","செய்யீரோ என்னானும் என்னுஞ்சொற் கின்னாதே","பையத்தான் செல்லும் நெறி?"],"p":"Men form close intimacies with others and live as their dependants, and this is permissible ; but is it more painful quietly to go one's way (to the hermitage) than, lost to all sense of personal dignity, to say, 'Will ye not do aught for me?’","r":"It is proper to attach oneself to another and serve him with respect, but is the slow road of poverty more painful than losing one's dignity and saying 'won't you do something for me?'","n":"Both commentators allow dependence on a patron, with deference, and forbid only the words of asking, so the line is drawn in speech. Mudaliar derives பைய from பை (suffering); Shanmuganar takes it as 'slowly'. Pope's 'to the hermitage' is his own addition.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iravu","maanam","sol","inmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:31"},"n:310":{"book":"naladiyar","num":310,"t":["பழமைகந் தாகப் பசைந்த வழியே","கிழமைதான் யாதானும் செய்க - கிழமை","பொறார் அவரென்னின் பொத்தித்தம் நெஞ்சத்","தறாஅச் சுடுவதோர் தீ."],"p":"Relying on old friendship, in an affectionate manner, render such assistance as your intimacy Warrants. If he will not endure (the assistance), will it not be a fire heaped up and burning in his bosom? ----------------","r":"Where old acquaintance has grown into affection, one should do whatever help that intimacy calls for; if the claim of intimacy is not borne, it becomes a fire banked in the heart that burns without end.","n":"Whose heart burns is not settled. Shanmuganar says it is the old friend who came to ask and was turned away. Pope reverses the roles: help is offered, and the needy friend who cannot bear to accept it burns. Mudaliar says only that the one asked fails to give the fitting help.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pazhaimai","kizhamai","natpu","iravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:31"},"n:311":{"book":"naladiyar","num":311,"t":["மெய்ஞ்ஞானக் கோட்டி உறழ்வழி விட்டாங்கோர்","அஞ்ஞானந் தந்திட் டதுவாங் கறத்துழாய்க்","கைஞ்ஞானங் கொண்டொழுகுங் காரறி வாளர்முன்","சொன்ஞானஞ் சோர விடல்."],"p":"","r":"Before people of dark understanding, who leave the way of an assembly of true knowledge, put forward some piece of ignorance, spread it everywhere and live by petty learning, let the wise leave their wisdom unspoken.","n":"The advice is silence, not argument. Mudaliar records another reading of கைஞ்ஞானம் as 'skill of the hand', the habit of striking whoever objects, which would anticipate 312 and 315.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avai","pullarivu","solvanmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:32"},"n:312":{"book":"naladiyar","num":312,"t":["நாப்பாடம் சொல்லி நயமுணர்வார் போல்செறிக்கும்","தீப்புலவர் சேரார் செறிவுடையார் - தீப்புலவன்","கோட்டியுள் குன்றக் குடிப்பழிக்கும் அல்லர்க்கால்","தோட்புடைக் கொள்ளா எழும்."],"p":"Well-disciplined men come not near the heretic (=man of evil learning, the teacher of an evil system), who stores up (in his memory certain formulas, comp. 304), as though he understood their worth, and repeats them by rote (lit. as a tongue-lesson). The heretic, if worsted in the assembly, will abuse the family (of his opponents); or he will spring up and challenge them to fight.","r":"The disciplined keep away from the bad scholar who recites lessons by rote as if he knew their beauty; worsted in the assembly he will abuse their family, or else slap his shoulders and rise to fight.","n":"Pope calls the தீப்புலவன் a heretic, the teacher of an evil system. Mudaliar's gloss is simply one without learning, and Shanmuganar's a false poet. The danger here is social: the loser turns to insult and violence.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avai","pullarivu","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:32"},"n:313":{"book":"naladiyar","num":313,"t":["சொற்றாற்றுக் கொண்டு சுனைத்தெழுதல் காமுறுவர்,","கற்றாற்றல் வன்மையும் தாம்தேறார் - கற்ற","செலவுரைக்கும ஆறறியார் தோற்ப தறியார்","பலவுரைக்கும் மாந்தர் பலர்."],"p":"Many are the men that utter many things,—who long to raise up (in debate), from mere love of talking and an itching tongue; who do not themselves apprehend the power and might of learning; who know not the way to utter with penetrating force whatthey have learnt; and who know not what defeat means.","r":"Many people talk at length: they itch to rise and debate on the strength of a word, cannot judge the power of learning, do not know how to make what they learned reach the hearer, and do not know when they are beaten.","n":"Mudaliar gives several ways to divide the opening words, one of them 'with words for goads', which Shanmuganar follows. The last failure, not knowing defeat, is what keeps the talk going.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sol","solvanmai","pullarivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:32"},"n:314":{"book":"naladiyar","num":314,"t":["கற்றதூஉ மின்றிக் கணக்காயர் பாடத்தால்","பெற்றதாம் பேதையோர் சூத்திரம் - மற்றதனை","நல்லா ரிடைப்புக்கு நாணாது சொல்லித்தன்","புல்லறிவு காட்டி விடும்."],"p":"Without any learning of his own, the fool has obtained one formula from overhearing the lessons of a tutor (who was teaching others ), yet unabashed he enters the circle of the good, speaks it out, and makes exhibition of his mean understanding.","r":"With no learning of his own, a fool picks up a single sutra by overhearing a teacher's lesson, then walks unabashed into a gathering of the good, recites it, and shows how petty his understanding is.","n":"Mudaliar specifies that the fool has only the bare text (மூல பாடம்) of the sutra, overheard from lessons given to other students.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai","pullarivu","avai","naanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:32"},"n:315":{"book":"naladiyar","num":315,"t":["வென்றிப் பொருட்டால் விலங்கொத்து மெய்கொள்ளார்","கன்றிக் கறுத்தெழுந்து காய்வாரோ - டொன்றி","உரைவித் தகமெழுவார் காண்பவே, கையுள்","சுரைவித்துப் போலுந்தம் பல்."],"p":"They who arise to utter words of wisdom, having associated themselves with the assembly of those who rage and burn and fume with anger, like beasts, for the mere sake of victory, not understanding the truth, shall see their own teeth like pumpkin seeds in their hands!","r":"Those who join people who rage and burn like beasts for the sake of victory and will not accept the truth, and who rise among them to show off their eloquence, will see their own teeth in their hands like gourd seeds.","n":"Mudaliar names the figure: teeth in the hand say, without saying it, that they will be knocked out. The warning falls on the speaker who chooses that company, not on the brawlers.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avai","igal","solvanmai","vekuli"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:32"},"n:316":{"book":"naladiyar","num":316,"t":["பாடமே ஓதிப் பயன்தெரிதல் தேற்றாத","முடர் முனிதக்க சொல்லுங்கால் - கேடருஞ்சீர்ச்","சான்றோர் சமழ்த்தனர் நிற்பவே, மற்றவரை","ஈன்றாட் கிறப்பப் பரிந்து."],"p":"When foolish men chant their lesson, not knowing the fruit that lesson yields, but uttering words that gender wrath, the learned ones, whose fame dies not, will stand by ashamed, sorely pitying the mother that bare them.","r":"When fools who only recite the text and cannot make out its meaning say things that provoke anger, the worthy, whose renown does not decay, stand ashamed and grieve deeply for the mother who bore them.","n":"The shame belongs to the listeners, not the fool. Mudaliar quotes the kind of lament the worthy might make, that she bore him for others to laugh at, and names the figure as imputed cause.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai","kalvi","saandror","avai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:32"},"n:317":{"book":"naladiyar","num":317,"t":["பெறுவது கொள்பவர் தோள்போல் நெறிப்பட்டுக்","கற்பவர்க் கெல்லாம் எளியநூல் - மற்றம்","முறிபுரை மேனியர் உள்ளம்போன் றியார்க்கும்","அறிதற் கா஢ய பொருள்."],"p":"Like the charms of those who sell their love for what they gain, the sacred texts are easy to those who rightly learn then; but like the minds of these whose forms are soft as tender shoots, the meaning is hard to all.","r":"Books are easy for anyone who learns them in due order, like the shoulders of women who take whatever they are paid, but their meaning is hard for anyone to grasp, like the minds of those same women with skin like tender mango shoots.","n":"One simile does both halves of the work: access to the text is cheap and open, and understanding is closed. Pope's 'sacred texts' narrows நூல், which the commentators leave general.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","arivu","varaivinmagalir"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:32"},"n:318":{"book":"naladiyar","num":318,"t":["புத்தகமே சாலத் தொகுத்தும் பொருடெரியார்","உய்த்தக மெல்லா நிறைப்பினும் - மற்றவற்றைப்","போற்றும் புலவரும் வேறே பொருடெரிந்து","தேற்றும் புலவரும் வேறு."],"p":"Although men gather together books in abundance, and, not understanding their contents, fill the whole house with them; the sages who merely take care of books are of one sort, and the sages who understand their contents, and make them clear to others, are of another sort.","r":"Some collect books in great numbers and fill the whole house with them without understanding what they say; the scholars who keep books are one kind, and those who understand them and make others understand are another.","n":"Shanmuganar takes போற்றும் as keeping the books with worship (பூசை போட்டு) and calls the keepers false scholars. The verse itself says only that they are a different kind.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","arivu","solvanmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:32"},"n:319":{"book":"naladiyar","num":319,"t":["பொழிப்பகல நுட்பநூ லெச்சமிந் நான்கின்","கொழித்தகலங் காட்டாதார் சொற்கள் - பழிப்பில்","நிரையாமா சேர்க்கும் நெடுங்குன்ற நாட","உரையாமோ நூலிற்கு நன்கு?"],"p":"Lord of the land of long chains of hills where the wild cattle assemble in herds! Is that a good commentary to a faultless composition, which consists of the words of those who do not sift it thoroughly, in the four divisions of summary, amplification, minute exposition, and supplementary information, and thus exhibit the full import?","r":"Lord of the long hills where herds of wild cattle gather, can the words of those who do not sift a faultless work through the four modes of summary, breadth, subtlety and remainder, and so show its full extent, be a good commentary on it?","n":"A quatrain about commentary, on whose terms its commentators disagree. Mudaliar adds that others define the four modes differently again.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","arivu","solvanmai"],"pos":[["mudaliar","The four are a method of disputation: breadth raises objections, subtlety answers them, and the remainder states the conclusion they establish.","எச்சவுரையாவது இவ்வாறு தடைவிடைகளான் முடிந்த ஒரு பொருளை யுரைத்தல்."],["shanmuganar","The remainder is research that brings out what lies hidden and unsaid in the text.","நூலில் எஞ்சி மறைந்து கிடப்பதை எடுத்துக்கூறும் ஆராய்ச்சியுரை"]],"ch":"n:32"},"n:320":{"book":"naladiyar","num":320,"t":["இற்பிறப் பில்லா ரெனைத்துநூல் கற்பினும்","சொற்பிறரைக் காக்குங் கருவியரோ? - இற்பிறந்த","நல்லறி வாளர் நவின்றநூல் தேற்றாதார்","புல்லறிவு தாமறிவ தில்."],"p":"Will those who are not of high family, however they may study learned works, be fit instruments to guard others from faults of speech? And men of good family who are well learned will not (seem to) be aware of the deficiencies of those who do not comprehend the works they talk of. -------------","r":"However many books they learn, can those not born of a good house be the means of guarding others' words? And the well-born wise take no notice of the petty understanding of those who do not grasp the books they talk of.","n":"Pope offers a third reading of the first half: guarding others from faults of speech. The second half fits Mudaliar's reading best, since the well-born show the restraint the first half denies to the low-born by overlooking a fool's errors.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","kalvi","pullarivu","adakkam"],"pos":[["mudaliar","Those of low birth lack the restraint needed to protect others' words from scorn.","பிறருடைய சொற்களை (இகழாமற்) காத்தற்குரிய (அடக்கம் முதலிய) சாதனமுடையவரோ?"],["shanmuganar","Those of low birth cannot protect and care for others by what they say.","தம் சொல்லால், பிறரைப் போற்றிக் காக்கத்தக்க கருவி போன்றிருப்பரோ?"]],"ch":"n:32"},"n:321":{"book":"naladiyar","num":321,"t":["அருளின் அறமுரைக்கும் அன்புடையார் வாய்ச்சொல்","பொருளாகக் கொள்வர் புலவர் - பொருளல்லா","ஏழை அதனை இகழ்ந்துரைக்கும் பாற்கூழை","முழை சுவையுணரா தாங்கு."],"p":"Men of sense receive as a reality the words from the mouth of the loving ones who graciously teach virtue. The (ignorant)wretch, himself unreal, speaks (of virtue) with contempt. Just so the ladle distinguishes not the flavour of the rice boiled in milk.","r":"The learned take as real worth the words of loving people who teach virtue out of compassion; the fool, himself of no worth, mocks them, as a ladle cannot taste the milk-rice it stirs.","n":"The ladle is in the milk-rice all the time and tastes nothing: being near good teaching is not the same as taking it in. Mudaliar takes பொருளல்லா ஏழை as a fool of no account, and Shanmuganar as one who does not grasp the real meaning.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pullarivu","aram","kelvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:33"},"n:322":{"book":"naladiyar","num":322,"t":["அவ்வியம் இல்லார் அறத்தா றுரைக்குங்கால்","செவ்விய ரல்லார் செவிகொடுத்தும் கேட்கலார்","கவ்வித்தோல் தின்னும் குணுங்கர்நாய் பாற்சோற்றின்","செவ்விய கொளல்தேற்றா தாங்கு."],"p":"When men of ungrudging soul declare the way of virtue, those who are not rightly disposed, although they give ear, hear not. Even so the currier's dog seizes on and devours leather, not appreciating the flavour of milk and rice.","r":"When those free of crookedness explain the way of virtue, those not rightly disposed do not hear it even when they lend an ear, just as the tanner's dog that chews leather cannot take the taste of milk-rice.","n":"Mudaliar reads அவ்வியம் as crookedness of mind, or as envy standing for every bad trait. The image draws on a caste occupation: Mudaliar glosses குணுங்கர் as புலையர்.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pullarivu","kelvi","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:33"},"n:323":{"book":"naladiyar","num":323,"t":["இமைக்கும் அளவிற்றம் இன்னுயிர்போம் மார்க்கம்","எனைத்தானும் தாங்கண் டிருந்தும் - தினைத்துணையும்","நன்றி புரிகல்லா நாணின் மடமாக்கள்","பொன்றிலென் பொன்றாக்கா லென்?"],"p":"What matters it whether they live or die—the shameless stupid people who do no good, not even as much as a grain of millet, though they see by every species of example the way in which their life so sweet to them, passes away in the twinkling of an eye?","r":"What does it matter whether they die or live, those dull and shameless people who see in every way that their dear life may leave in the blink of an eye, and still do not a millet grain's worth of good?","n":"Mudaliar lets நன்றி mean any good deed, or specifically help given to others.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","aram","pullarivu","naanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:33"},"n:324":{"book":"naladiyar","num":324,"t":["உளநாள் சிலவால் உயிர்க்கேமம் இன்றால்,","பலர்மன்னுந் தூற்றும் பழியால், - பலருள்ளும்","கண்டாரோ டெல்லாம் நகாஅ தெவனொருவன்","தண்டித் தனிப்பகை கோள்."],"p":"Since being's days are few, and life no safeguardowns, and guilt by many blamed is rife, mid many men, why, laughing not with all they see, should any sulk apart, and nurse a sullen hate ?","r":"The days left are few, life has no safeguard, and the blame that many spread is everywhere; why then should anyone, among so many, refuse to smile with those he meets and stand apart nursing a private enmity?","n":"The case against sullen enmity rests on shortage of time rather than on virtue: with life brief and unsafe, hostility is a waste. Mudaliar lets ஏமம் mean pleasure as well as protection.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["igal","nilaiyaamai","panpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:33"},"n:325":{"book":"naladiyar","num":325,"t":["எய்தி யிருந்த அவைமுன்னர்ச் சென்றெள்ளி","வைதான் ஒருவன் ஒருவனை - வைய","வயப்பட்டான் வாளா இருப்பானேல், வைதான்","வியத்தக்கான் வாழும் எனின்."],"p":"A man has gone before the assembly that had gathered together, and contemning another has reviled him. Now if the reviled one remain silent, the reviler is to be wondered at if he survive; (for abuse is his very life, and the patience of the reviled one has closed his mouth.)","r":"If a man goes before a gathered assembly and reviles another, and the one reviled stays silent, it is a wonder if the reviler survives.","n":"The verse doesn't say how the reviler is undone. Pope supplies a reason, that abuse is the reviler's life and silence stops his mouth. The commentators say only that he will not prosper.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["porai","kadunjol","avai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:33"},"n:326":{"book":"naladiyar","num":326,"t":["மூப்புமேல் வாராமை முன்னே அறவினையை","ஊக்கி அதன்கண் முயலாதான் - நூக்கிப்","புறத்திரு போகென்னும் இன்னாச்சொல் இல்லுள்","தொழுத்தையால் கூறப் படும்."],"p":"He who before old age comes, has not undertaken and zealously carried out works of virtue, will be pushed about in the house addressed in harsh language, and bidden to get on one side or to go out, by even the maid-servants.","r":"One who does not eagerly take up virtuous deeds and work at them before old age comes on will be pushed aside in his own house and told 'sit outside, go away' even by the maidservant.","n":"The penalty is social, not karmic: humiliation in old age, inside one's own home.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","muyarchi","maanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:33"},"n:327":{"book":"naladiyar","num":327,"t":["தாமேயும் இன்புறார், தக்கார்க்கு நன்றாற்றார்","ஏமஞ்சார் நன்னெறியும் சேர்கலார் - தாமயங்கி","ஆக்கத்துள் தூங்கி அவத்தமே வாழ்நாளைப்","போக்குவார் புல்லறிவி னார்."],"p":"Men of scanty wisdom are those who do not themselves enjoy any sweets of life, bestow no benefactions on worthy persons, draw not nigh the good path that safeguards (the soul), but infatuated and absorbed in the acquisition of wealth, pass away their days of life in vain.","r":"People of petty understanding neither enjoy their wealth nor do good to the deserving nor join the good path that protects; dazed and sunk in their prosperity, they spend their days for nothing.","n":"Mudaliar names the cause of the daze: not knowing that body and wealth do not last.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pullarivu","selvam","eegai","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:33"},"n:328":{"book":"naladiyar","num":328,"t":["சிறுகாலை யேதமக்குச் செல்வுழி வல்சி","இறுகிறுகத் தோட்கோப்புக் கொள்ளார் - இறுகிறுகிப்","பின்னறிவாம் என்றிருக்கும் பேதையார் கைகாட்டும்","பொன்னும் புளிவிளிங்கா யாம்."],"p":"In the very earliest time (in early youth) they take not food for the journey which they must make (into another world), tying tightly the wallet on their shoulders ; but tying tightly (their treasure bags), they say, 'In after days we will learn wisdom;' the gold these idiots will indict with their hands (as legacies, when they are speechless, and in the grasp of death) will be as sour vilam fruit.","r":"Fools who do not tie on tightly, early in life, the provisions for the road they must take, but tighten their grip on their wealth and say 'we will see to it later', will find that the gold they point to with their hands turns into a sour vilam fruit.","n":"The commentators fill in a deathbed scene: the dying man, unable to speak, gestures for gold to give away, and his family takes the gesture to mean a sour vilam fruit. Mudaliar quotes a Civaka Cintamani verse telling this story. The quatrain gives only the gesture and the sour fruit.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","marumai","ivaral","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:33"},"n:329":{"book":"naladiyar","num":329,"t":["வெறுமை யிடத்தும் விழிப்பிணிப் போழ்தும்","மறுமை மனத்தாரே யாகி - மறுமையை","ஐந்தை யனைத்தானும் ஆற்றிய காலத்துச்","சிந்தியார் சிற்றறிவி னார்."],"p":"When poor, or when disease's deadly grasp they feel, to other world alone their minds are given ; but when wealth grows, no thought, small as a grain of mustard seed, give they to other world —these souls unwise!","r":"In poverty and in grave illness the small-minded think of the next world, but once they are able to act they give it not a mustard seed's worth of thought.","n":"The irony is that concern for the next life arrives only when one is least able to act on it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["marumai","aram","pullarivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:33"},"n:330":{"book":"naladiyar","num":330,"t":["என்னேமற் றிவ்வுடம்பு பெற்றும் அறம்நினையார்","கொன்னே கழிப்பர்தம் வாழ்நாளை - அன்னோ","அளவிறந்த காதற்றம் ஆருயி ரன்னார்க்","கொளஇழைக்கும் கூற்றமும் கண்டு."],"p":"Though they have gained a human frame, of virtue think they not! In vain they spent their days. Alas! and yet they see death eagerly hasting to snatch away those infinitely dear, like to their very souls! Why is this so? -------------","r":"Alas, though they have gained this human body they do not think of virtue and waste their days, even while they watch Death preparing to take those they love beyond measure, as dear as their own lives.","n":"Shanmuganar calls the human body hard to obtain; the verse's இவ்வுடம்பு implies as much without saying it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","nilaiyaamai","kootru"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:33"},"n:331":{"book":"naladiyar","num":331,"t":["கொலைஞர் உலையேற்றித் தீமடுப்ப ஆமை","நிலையறியா தந்நீர்ப் படிந்தாடி யற்றே","கொலைவல் பெருங்கூற்றம் கோள்பார்ப்ப ஈண்டை","வலையகத்துச் செம்மாப்பார் மாண்பு."],"p":"While the turtle's murderers have put it into the pot, and kindled fire beneath, it sports unconscious of its fate: such is their worth who joy entangled in life's not, while death, the mighty murderer, waits to seize them.","r":"As a turtle put in a pot by its killers, with the fire lit beneath, plays in the water without knowing its state, so are those who revel in this world's net while Death, the great killer, waits for the moment to seize them.","n":"The water the turtle enjoys is what will kill it, just as the world's pleasures are the net. Mudaliar notes that மாண்பு ('worth') is used ironically.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","pedhaimai","kootru"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:34"},"n:332":{"book":"naladiyar","num":332,"t":["பெருங்கட லாடிய சென்றார் ஒருங்குடன்","ஓசை அவிந்தபின் ஆடுது மென்றற்றால்","இற்செய் குறைவினை நீக்கி அறவினை","மற்றறிவாம் என்றிருப்பார் மாண்பு."],"p":"They went to bathe in the great sea, but cried 'we will wait till all its roar is hushed, then bathe!' Such is their worth who say, 'we will get rid of all our household toils and cares and then we will practise virtue and be wise.'","r":"Those who say they will see to virtue once the household's unfinished tasks are done are like people who went to bathe in the great sea and said they would bathe when its roar had died down.","n":"Household duties, like the noise of the sea, never stop, so the simile turns postponement into permanent refusal.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","ilvaazhkkai","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:34"},"n:333":{"book":"naladiyar","num":333,"t":["குலந்தவம் கல்வி குடிநம்முப் பைந்தும்","விலங்காமல் எய்தியக் கண்ணும் - நலஞ்சான்ற","மையறு தொல்சீர் உலகம் அறியாமை","நெய்யிலாப் பாற்சோற்றின் நேர்."],"p":"Though a man be of good caste, have performed deeds of ascetic virtue, acquired learning, is of good family and attained a ripe old age, so that in these five points he is faultless; yet his ignorance of what is thoroughly good, without stain, sanctioned by ancient precedent, and of good renown in the world's ways, will render all his advantages like thin, watery milk with boiled rice.","r":"Though one has all five, high caste, austerity, learning, standing and age, without lack, not knowing the good, stainless and anciently honoured உலகம் makes it all like milk-rice without ghee.","n":"The commentators also divide on குடி: household prosperity for Mudaliar, good family for Shanmuganar. Pope's 'thin, watery milk' departs from the Tamil, which has rice without ghee.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai","kudi","kalvi","ozhukkam"],"pos":[["mudaliar","உலகம் means the worlds to be reached after this life, heaven and release, so what the fool lacks is knowledge of what leads there.","உலகம்- (மறுமையில் அடையத்தக்க சுவர்க்க மோக்ஷம் என்னும்) உலகங்களின் தகுதியை."],["shanmuganar","உலகம் means the ways of the world, and what the fool lacks is knowledge of how to live by them.","பழம் பெருஞ் சிறப் புடைய உலக நடைமுறையை அறியாது ஒழுகுதல்"]],"ch":"n:34"},"n:334":{"book":"naladiyar","num":334,"t":["கன்னனி நல்ல கடையாய மாக்களின்","சொன்னனி தாமுணரா வாயினும் - இன்னினியே","நிற்றல் இருத்தல் கிடத்தல் இயங்குதலேன்","றுற்றவர்க்குத் தாமுதவ லான்."],"p":"Stones are much better than low men ; since, though those (too) are utterly without apprehension of your words, yet they (stones) are of assistance to those that employ them, inasmuch as they at once stand (where they are fixed), remain (where they are put), lie (where they are laid), and remove (when they are moved).","r":"Stones are far better than base people: though they cannot understand words either, they serve whoever comes to them, at once, for standing, sitting, lying and walking on.","n":"The comparison is not about intelligence, which both lack, but about use: the stone at least gives itself to others.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai","payan","kayamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:34"},"n:335":{"book":"naladiyar","num":335,"t":["பெறுவதொன் றின்றியும் பெற்றானே போலக்","கறுவுகொண் டேலாதார் மாட்டும் - கறுவினால்","கோத்தின்னா கூறி உரையாக்கால் பேதைக்கு","நாத்தின்னும் நல்ல சுனைத்து."],"p":"If the fool, though nothing is to be gained (by such conduct), acting as though he had gained something, waxing wroth against those who disdain (or are unable) to contend with him, cannot say and utter forth in his wrath a string of evil words a grievous itching will gnaw his tongue.","r":"Though nothing is gained by it, the fool, as if he had gained something, grows angry even with those who don't deserve it, and if he cannot string harsh words together and say them, a fierce itch gnaws at his tongue.","n":"The targets are read three ways: Mudaliar's ஏலாதார் are those for whom such anger is unfitting, Shanmuganar's (printed under 334) are superiors who are no match for the fool, and Pope's are those who will not or cannot contend with him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai","vekuli","kadunjol"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:34"},"n:336":{"book":"naladiyar","num":336,"t":["தங்கள் மரபில்லார் பின்சென்று தாமவரை","எங்கண் வணக்குதும் என்பவர் - புன்கேண்மை","நற்றளிர்ப் புன்னை மலருங் கடற்சேர்ப்ப","கற்கிள்ளிக் கையிழந் தற்று."],"p":"Lord of the sea-shore where the laurel with its bright foliage flowers! The poor (pretence of) intimacy enjoyed by those who follow after (wealthy but) utterly uncongenial men, and say 'we will bend them to our will,' is like loosing one's hand while digging into a rock.","r":"Those who follow people without good conduct, saying 'we will make them bow to us', have a poor kind of friendship, lord of the shore where the laurel with fine shoots flowers: it is like losing one's hand pinching a rock.","n":"Mudaliar calls the bond a one-sided friendship and cites a Pazhamozhi line saying no one pinches a rock and keeps their hand. Pope supplies 'wealthy' for the people followed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","pedhaimai","sirumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:34"},"n:337":{"book":"naladiyar","num":337,"t":["ஆகா தெனினும் அகத்துநெய் யுண்டாகின்","போகா தெறும்பு புறஞ்சுற்றும் - யாதும்","கொடாஅ ரெனினும் உடையாரைப் பற்றி","விடாஅர் உலகத் தவர்."],"p":"If there be ghi in the pot, though they cannot get at it, the ants will swarm without ceasing around the outside ; and so the people of the world will cling to and not leave the possessors of wealth, though these give them nothing.","r":"If there is ghee inside a pot, ants keep circling its outside without leaving, though they cannot get at it; so people cling to the wealthy and won't leave them, though they give nothing.","n":"The quatrain describes a habit of the world without blaming it in so many words; being placed in this chapter is what marks the habit as folly.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","pedhaimai","ivaral"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:34"},"n:338":{"book":"naladiyar","num":338,"t":["நல்லவை நாடொறும் எய்தார் அறஞ்செய்யார்","இல்லாதார்க் கியாதொன்றும் ஈகலார் - எல்லாம்","இனியார்தோள் சேரார் இசைபட வாழார்","முனியார்கொல் தாம்வாழும் நாள்."],"p":"No good each day they gain; nor deed of virtue do; nothing to needy men impart; nor know they joy of loved ones' sweet embrace ; devoid of fame they live: do such not loathe the days they live?","r":"They gain no good thing day by day, do no virtue, give nothing to the needy, never hold their beloved in their arms, and do not live with renown: will they not come to loathe the days they live?","n":"The list mixes virtue, giving, married love and fame, so the fool fails at every aim of life, not only at renunciation. Mudaliar allows நல்லவை to be read as நல் அவை, a good assembly, and Shanmuganar takes that reading.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","eegai","pugazh","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:34"},"n:339":{"book":"naladiyar","num":339,"t":["விழைந்தொருவர் தம்மை வியப்ப ஒருவர்","விழைந்திலேம் என்றிருக்கும் கேண்மை - தழங்குகுரல்","பாய்திரைசூழ் வையம் பயப்பினும் இன்னாதே","ஆய்நலம் இல்லாதார் மாட்டு."],"p":"Friendship with those who are destitute of exquisite goodness, and who, though you cultivate them with affectionate deference, (morosely) say we entertain affection for none,'—though it should yield as its fruit the earth, which the sea with its noisy chime of leaping billows girds around,—would be only affliction.","r":"When one person admires and seeks another who answers 'we don't want you', that friendship with those lacking discerning goodness is painful, even if it should yield the whole earth girdled by the roaring sea.","n":"Mudaliar calls the bond a one-sided friendship, or a false one. The fool is the one who rejects the regard offered, but the warning is for the one who keeps offering it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","pedhaimai","sirumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:34"},"n:340":{"book":"naladiyar","num":340,"t":["கற்றனவும் கண்ணகன்ற சாயலும் இற்பிறப்பும்","பக்கத்தார் பாராட்டப் பாடெய்தும் தானுரைப்பின்","மைத்துனர் பல்கி மருந்தின் தணியாத","பித்தனென் றெள்ளப் படும்."],"p":"The learning a man has acquired, his world - renowned excellence, and his nobility of birth, will gain for him respect when the bystanders celebrate them; but if he himself proclaim them, the young kinsmen crowding around will deride him as one afflicted with a mania not to be mitigated by any medicine. -----------","r":"Learning, wide gentleness and good birth win honour when others praise them; if a man praises them himself, his brothers-in-law multiply and mock him as a madman no medicine can cure.","n":"Mudaliar explains மைத்துனர் (brothers-in-law) as mockers: the joking relationship stands for ridicule.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","sirumai","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:34"},"n:341":{"book":"naladiyar","num":341,"t":["கப்பி கடவதாக் காலைத்தன் வாய்ப்பெயினும்","குப்பை கிளைப்போவாக் கோழிபோல் - மிக்க","கனம்பொதிந்த நூல்விரித்துக் காட்டினும் கீழ்தன்","மனம்புரிந்த வாறே மிகும்."],"p":"The fowl, though each morning duly you scatter broken grain into its very mouth, will ceaselessly scratch in the refuse-heap; so, though you expound and show the base man works of learning, full of weighty wisdom, he will but the more resolutely go on in the way in which his mind finds delight.","r":"As a hen keeps scratching the rubbish heap though grain is poured into its mouth each morning, so the base man, however you lay open books full of weighty meaning for him, goes further along the way his mind likes.","n":"மிகும் suggests excess, not just persistence: Mudaliar glosses it as going beyond bounds.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","kalvi","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:35"},"n:342":{"book":"naladiyar","num":342,"t":["காழாய கொண்டு கசடற்றார் தஞ்சாரல்","தாழாது போவாம் எனஉரைப்பின் - கீழ்தான்","உறங்குவாம் என்றெழுந்து போமாம், அஃதன்றி","மறங்குமாம் மற்றொன் றுரைத்து."],"p":"If you say to the base man, ‘Let us without delay go to seek refuge with faultless sages possessors of mature wisdom,' he will probably get up and make off, exclaiming, 'Let us go and slumber,!’ or he will perhaps demur, and change the subject.","r":"Say to the base man 'let us go without delay to the faultless who hold firm learning', and he gets up and leaves saying 'let's sleep', or else refuses with some other excuse.","n":"The comedy is in the excuses: sleep first, and failing that, anything else.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","saandror","madi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:35"},"n:343":{"book":"naladiyar","num":343,"t":["பெருநடை தாம்பெறினும் பெற்றி பிழையா","தொருநடைய ராகுவர் சான்றோர் - பெருநடை","பெற்றக் கடைத்தும் பிறங்கருவி நன்னாட","வற்றாம் ஒருநடை கீழ்."],"p":"Lord of the goodly land of swelling torrents! If thoroughly worthy men gain some added dignity, their nature knows no deviation, but they go on in the same even path of virtue ; and although the base obtain promotion, his conduct too changes not, (promotion betters him not.)","r":"Even if they gain great prosperity, the worthy keep one even course without falling from their nature; when the base gain it, the verse says only that they are 'able in one course', lord of the good land of shining waterfalls.","n":"Pope agrees with Shanmuganar that the base man's conduct does not change. Mudaliar takes பெருநடை as wealth and records that others read it as great distinction.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","saandror","selvam","panpu"],"pos":[["mudaliar","With prosperity the base man becomes adept at a conduct different from his earlier one.","வற்றாம் ஒரு நடை- (முன்னைய நடைக்கு வேறாகிய) ஒரு நடக்கையின் வல்லவனாவன்;"],["shanmuganar","With prosperity the base man keeps to his own low course.","தனது தாழ்ந்த ஒரு போக்கையே பின்பற்ற வல்லவனாம்."]],"ch":"n:35"},"n:344":{"book":"naladiyar","num":344,"t":["தினையனைத்தே யாயினும் செய்தநன் றுண்டால்","பனையனைத்தா உள்ளுவர் சான்றோர் - பளையனைத்","தென்றும் செயினும் இலங்கருவி நன்னாட","நன்றில நன்றறியார் மாட்டு."],"p":"Lord of the goodly land of glistening torrents ! The excellent will deem any favour done them, though small as a grain of millet, to be large as a palm tree. You may constantly confer favours huge as a palm tree, but they are not benefits, when conferred on those who are not grateful.","r":"If they have enjoyed a good done to them, though it is only the size of a millet grain, the worthy think of it as a palm tree; but goods the size of a palm, done again and again, are no good at all to those who do not know a good, lord of the land of bright waterfalls.","n":"Mudaliar cites Kural 104 and Parimelazhagar's remark that millet and palm are conventional measures of small and great.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nanri","saandror","kayamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:35"},"n:345":{"book":"naladiyar","num":345,"t":["பொற்கலத் தூட்டிப் புறந்தரினும் நாய்பிறர்","எச்சிற் கிமையாது பார்த்திருக்கும் - அச்சீர்","பெருமை யுடைத்தாக் கொளினுங்கீழ் செய்யும்","கருமங்கள் வேறு படும்."],"p":"The dog, though you tend it with care, feeding it from a golden vessel, will watch, eye fixed for others' leavings ; even so, although you receive the base as if they were possessed of greatness, the deeds they do will be otherwise (= their real character will be seen by their conduct).","r":"A dog, though reared on food from a golden bowl, watches unblinking for others' leftovers; so even if you treat the base as great, the deeds they do will be different.","n":"The golden bowl is the honour shown: it changes the setting, not the appetite.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","panpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:35"},"n:346":{"book":"naladiyar","num":346,"t":["சக்கரச் செல்வம் பெறினும் விழுமியோர்","எக்காலும் சொல்லார் மிகுதிச்சொல் - எக்காலும்","முந்திரிமேற் காணி மிகுவதேல் கீழ்தன்னை","இந்திரனா எண்ணி விடும்."],"p":"Though they obtain imperial wealth, the excellent never utter a vaunting word ; but if at any time the base see some small fraction added to his scanty store, he will deem himself great as Indra.","r":"Even with an emperor's wealth the excellent never speak a boastful word, but if a base man gains a காணி on top of a முந்திரி, he thinks himself Indra.","n":"The two words are tiny fractions. Shanmuganar gives முந்திரி as 1/320 and காணி as 1/80; Mudaliar gives காணி as 1/64 and முந்திரி as a quarter of that, and allows the words to be read in the other order.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","perumai","selvam","panivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:35"},"n:347":{"book":"naladiyar","num":347,"t":["மைதீர் பசும்பொன்மேல் மாண்ட மணியழுத்திச்","செய்த தெனினுஞ் செருப்புத்தன் காற்கேயாம்","எய்திய செல்வத்த ராயினும் கீழ்களைச்","செய்தொழிலால் காணப் படும்."],"p":"Though made of faultless yellow gold, enwrought with choicest pearls, the slipper serves but for one's foot; though the base be deemed prosperous through the wealth they have gained, their baseness will be seen by their actions.","r":"Though a sandal is made of flawless gold set with fine gems, it is still for the foot; so the base, though they have come into wealth, are known by the deeds they do.","n":"The sandal's material is the wealth and its place is the nature: costly making does not move it from the foot.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:35"},"n:348":{"book":"naladiyar","num":348,"t":["கடுக்கெனச் சொல்வற்றாம், கண்ணோட்டம் இன்றாம்","இடுக்கண் பிறர்மாட் டுவக்கும், - அடுத்தடுத்து","வேகம் உடைத்தாம், விறன்மலை நன்னாட","ஏகுமாம் எள்ளுமாம் கீழ்."],"p":"","r":"The base are quick to speak harshly, have no kindly regard, rejoice in others' troubles, fly into rage again and again, go where they please and scorn everyone, lord of the good land of mighty hills.","n":"A plain list with no image, which Mudaliar classes as straightforward description (தன்மை நவிற்சி).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","kadunjol","kannottam","vekuli"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:35"},"n:349":{"book":"naladiyar","num":349,"t":["பழைய ரிவரென்று பன்னாட்பின் நிற்பின்","உழையினிய ராகுவர் சான்றோர் - விழையாதே","கள்ளுயிர்க்கும் நெய்தற் கனைகடல் தண்சேர்ப்ப","எள்ளுவர் கீழா யவர்."],"p":"Lord of the resounding sea's cool shore, where the Neythal breathes honied fragrance round! The worthy, if men for many days stand waiting in their train will say 'these are old acquaintance,' and will make the place pleasant to them; the base in such circumstances feel no affection, but simply despise them.","r":"If people stand by them for many days, the worthy grow kind to them, thinking 'these are old friends'; the base feel no warmth and simply scorn them, lord of the cool shore of the roaring sea where the neydal breathes honey.","n":"Mudaliar takes standing by someone as being subordinate and obedient to them, so the test is how each kind treats long-serving dependants.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pazhaimai","kayamai","saandror","natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:35"},"n:350":{"book":"naladiyar","num":350,"t":["கொய்புல் கொடுத்துக் குறைத்தென்றும் தீற்றினும்","வையம்பூண் கல்லா சிறுகுண்டை - ஐயகேள்,","எய்திய செல்வத்த ராயினும் கீழ்களைச்","செய்தொழிலாற் காணப் படும்."],"p":"Hear, O Sir! The little bullock-calves will not learn to bear the yoke and draw the chariot, though you give them cut grass, clip them, and constantly rub them down; so, though they have gained wealth, the base will be seen (to be so) by the deeds they do. -----------------","r":"Hear, sir: small bullocks will never learn to wear the yoke and draw the cart however long you feed them cut grass, and the base, though they have come into wealth, are known by the deeds they do.","n":"Care cannot supply a capacity that is not there; the bullocks are well fed and still small.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:35"},"n:351":{"book":"naladiyar","num":351,"t":["ஆர்த்த அறிவினர் ஆண்டிளைய ராயினும்","காத்தோம்பித் தம்மை அடக்குப - மூத்தொறூஉம்","தீத்தொழிலே கன்றித் திரிதந் தெருவைபோல்","போத்தறார் புல்லறிவி னார்."],"p":"The thoroughly wise, though young in years, will guard themselves in stern self-repression. The low unwise as age comes on become mature in evil works, and are like reeds wind-shaken, hollow still!","r":"The fully wise restrain and guard themselves even when young in years, while the petty-minded grow more practised in wrongdoing the older they get and, like the எருவை, never lose their hollowness.","n":"Age is set aside as a cause: wisdom doesn't wait for years, and years don't cure folly. Mudaliar gives two senses for both எருவை and போத்து: a reed that never fills its hollow, or a kite that never sheds its faults. Shanmuganar takes the kite and the faults, and Pope the hollow reed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pullarivu","adakkam","kayamai","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:36"},"n:352":{"book":"naladiyar","num":352,"t":["செழும்பெரும் பொய்கையுள் வாழினும் என்றும்","வழும்பறுக்க கில்லாவாம் தேரை - வழும்பில்சீர்","நூல்கற்றக் கண்ணும் நுணுக்கமொன் றில்லாதார்","தேர்கிற்கும் பெற்றி அரிது."],"p":"Though frogs flourish long in a rich large lake, they never rid themselves of their slime; so it is hard for those void of fine perception, even when they have learned pure and excellent text-books, to acquire the gift of clear understanding.","r":"Frogs that live in a rich, large pond never rid themselves of their slime; in the same way, those without fine discernment rarely gain the power to examine what they read, even after studying flawless, excellent books.","n":"What is missing is not learning but நுணுக்கம், a subtlety that study doesn't supply. The pond's abundance matches the excellence of the books: neither reaches what is already in the creature. Shanmuganar's commentary on this quatrain appears under 351.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","arivu","kayamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:36"},"n:353":{"book":"naladiyar","num":353,"t":["கணமலை நன்னாட. கண்ணின் றொருவர்","குணனேயுங் கூற்ற கா஢தால், - குணனழுங்கக்","குற்றம் உழைநின்று கூறுஞ் சிறியவர்கட்கு","எற்றா லியன்றதோ நா."],"p":"Lord of the goodly land of mountain chains ! It is hard to stand before a man, and publish even his praise. How then can mean men's tongues depreciate a man's good qualities and proclaim his faults while standing before him?.","r":"Lord of the good land of clustered hills: it is hard even to speak of a man's virtues when he isn't present, so what are the tongues of petty people made of, that they stand at his side and proclaim his faults until his virtues are spoiled?","n":"Both commentators read கண் இன்று as 'not before his eyes', and Mudaliar rejects splitting it as கண் நின்று. The contrast is then between a decent reluctance to praise someone even behind his back and the brazenness of faulting him beside him. Pope takes the other split and has the praise spoken to the man's face.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","sol","kutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:36"},"n:354":{"book":"naladiyar","num":354,"t":["கோடேந் தகலல்குல் பெண்டிர்தம் பெண்ணீர்மை","சேடியர் போலச் செயல்தேற்றார் - கூடிப்","புதுப்பெருக்கம் போலத்தம் பெண்ணீர்மை காட்டி","மதித்திறப்பர் மற்றை யவர்."],"p":"Chaste women trick not out their charms of womanhood, as is as the wont of the wanton. Like fountains ever fresh those others make display of every charm, and vaunt their beauty as they pass.","r":"Household women do not know how to dress up their womanliness the way their maids do it for them; the other kind, like a fresh flood, put their charms on show themselves, think highly of themselves, and then pass on.","n":"In a chapter on the base the verse works as a figure, and Mudaliar names it பிறிது மொழிதல்: the wise don't praise themselves, and fools vaunt themselves to extract wealth. The verse itself speaks only of the two kinds of women.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","varaivinmagalir","panivu"],"pos":[["mudaliar","இறப்பர் means the courtesans leave a man once they have taken all his wealth; the verse is an allegory of fools who praise themselves to get money.","அறிவிலார் தம்மைப்பிறர் நன்கு மதிக்கும்படி தம்மைத்தாமே புகழ்ந்து பேசிப் பொருள் பறிப்பரென்பதூ"],["shanmuganar","இறப்பர் means they overstep all bounds, rating themselves highly; he reads the verse as a contrast between the women themselves.","தம்மைப் பெரிதாய் மதித்து வரம்பு கடந்து செல்வர்."]],"ch":"n:36"},"n:355":{"book":"naladiyar","num":355,"t":["தளிர்மேலே நிற்பினும் தட்டாமற் செல்லா","உளிநீரார் மாதோ கயவர் - அளிநீரார்க்","கென்னானும் செய்யார் எனைத்தானும் செய்பவே","இன்னாங்கு செய்வார்ப் பெறின்."],"p":"The base are like the chisel, that, though it is over the bud (to be sculptured), moves not without some one to strike it. On those who are courteous these confer no boon whatever. They will do everything for those who use violent means.","r":"The base are like a chisel that won't cut in, even set on a tender shoot, unless someone strikes it: they do nothing at all for the kind, and anything at all for those who treat them harshly.","n":"A tender shoot is the softest thing a chisel could meet, so the image says the base yield to nothing gentle. Mudaliar lets அளிநீரார் mean either the compassionate or those whose state calls for pity.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","eegai","arul"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:36"},"n:356":{"book":"naladiyar","num":356,"t":["மலைநலம் உள்ளும் குறவன் பயந்த","விளைநிலம் உள்ளும் உழவன் சிறந்தொருவர்","செய்தநன் றுள்ளுவர் சான்றோர் கயந்தன்னை","வைததை உள்ளி விடும்."],"p":"The hill-man thinks upon the beauty of his hills; the farmer thinks upon his fields that have yielded him rich crops; the good think on the boons bestowed by worthy men; the base man's thoughts are fixed on abuse he has received.","r":"The hill-dweller thinks of the wealth of his hills and the farmer of the field that yielded; the worthy think of a good someone did them, while the base dwell on the time someone abused them.","n":"The first two figures set up the rule that each mind returns to what it values; the last two say what the worthy and the base value. Mudaliar notes the verse calls the base man கயம், a neuter noun, to show how low he is.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nanri","kayamai","saandror"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:36"},"n:357":{"book":"naladiyar","num":357,"t":["ஒருநன்றி செய்தவர்க் கொன்றி யெழுந்த","பிழைநூறும் சான்றோர் பொறுப்பர் - கயவர்க்கு","எழுநூறு நன்றிசெய் தொன்றுதீ தாயின்","எழுநூறும் தீதாய் விடும்."],"p":"The good attach themselves to those that have done them one act of kindness, and forgive a hundred wrongs that arise. The base receive seven hundred benefits, but one wrong will turn them all to evil.","r":"The worthy bear a hundred faults, coming one after another, from someone who once did them a kindness; for the base, if one thing goes wrong after seven hundred kindnesses, all seven hundred turn into harm.","n":"Mudaliar takes the hundred and the seven hundred as standing for 'many' and 'very many'. The asymmetry is the point: for one kind of person a single kindness outweighs a hundred wrongs, and for the other a single wrong cancels seven hundred kindnesses.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nanri","porai","kayamai","saandror"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:36"},"n:358":{"book":"naladiyar","num":358,"t":["ஏட்டைப் பருவத்தும் இற்பிறந்தார் செய்வன்","மோட்டிடத்தும் செய்யார் முழுமக்கள் - கோட்டை","வயிரஞ் செறிப்பினும் வாட்கண்ணாய் பன்றி","செயிர்வேழ மாகுத லின்று."],"p":"Worthless persons even in high estate do not the deeds that men of noble birth perform even in their poverty. Okeen-eyed maid! Men may enring and arm the boar's tusks, but it will not even so become a warlike elephant.","r":"What those of good family do even when weakened by want, the utterly foolish don't do even at the height of their fortune; bright-eyed one, fit a boar's tusks with diamond rings and it still won't become an angry war elephant.","n":"The verse doesn't name the deeds. Pope supplies liberality, and Mudaliar says only 'good deeds'. The base are called முழுமக்கள், which Mudaliar glosses as fools, so the lack here is of sense as well as of birth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","kayamai","selvam","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:36"},"n:359":{"book":"naladiyar","num":359,"t":["இன்றாதும் இந்நிலையே ஆதும் இனிச்சிறிது","நின்றாதும் என்று நினைத்திருந் - தொன்றி","உரையின் மகிழ்ந்துதம் உள்ளம்வே றாகி","மரையிலையின் மாய்ந்தார் பலர்."],"p":"‘To-day, at once, a little hence, we shall gain our end!’ So many speak and think, and joyously tell it out among their friends ; but soon their eager minds are changed; and they have perished like a lotus leaf.","r":"Many people thought 'today we'll have it, right now, or in a little while', rejoiced as they said so to others, and then, their hopes overturned, perished like lotus leaves.","n":"The verse doesn't say what they expected, and names no base person. Mudaliar supplies both: they waited to become rich without doing good, and withered like the lotus leaf that flourishes and dies where it stands. Shanmuganar's commentary for this quatrain is missing.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","avaa","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:36"},"n:360":{"book":"naladiyar","num":360,"t":["நீருள் பிறந்து நிறம்பசிய தாயினும்","ஈரங் கிடையகத் தில்லாகும் - ஓரும்","நிறைப்பெருஞ் செல்வத்து நின்றக் கடைத்தும்","அறைப்பெருங்கல் அன்னா ருடைத்து."],"p":"Though born in the water, and its hue appear green, the Netti's pith knows no moisture; so in the world are men of amplest wealth whose hearts are hard as stones upon the rocky mountain fell. -------------","r":"Though it is born in water and is green, the netti reed has no moisture in its pith; in the same way the world holds men who stay hard as great rock slabs even while living in full, great wealth.","n":"The image is exact: the plant lives in water and holds none, as these men live among plenty and give none. Mudaliar's gloss on the rock is that it never softens (இளகுதல்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","selvam","arul"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:36"},"n:361":{"book":"naladiyar","num":361,"t":["மழைதிளைக்கு மாடமாய் மாண்பமைந்த காப்பாய்","இழைவிளக்கு நின்றிமைப்பின் என்னாம்? - விழைதக்க","மாண்ட மனையாளை இல்லாதான் இல்லகம்","காண்டற் கா஢யதோர் காடு."],"p":"The mansion meets the clouds. A stately band of warders keep watch around. Gems glisten therein like lamps. What then? Where the owner has not a wife of dainty excellence: the house within is a waste, hard to explore.","r":"What use is a mansion where the clouds play, under fine guard, with jewels shining like lamps, if its owner has no admirable wife? That house is a wilderness too grim to look at.","n":"Pope's 'hard to explore' reads காண்டற்கு அரிய as a trackless waste; Mudaliar reads it as a forest too harsh even to look at. Shanmuganar takes இழைவிளக்கு as a wick lamp that never goes out, where Mudaliar has jewels shining like lamps.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaazhkkaithunai","ilvaazhkkai","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:37"},"n:362":{"book":"naladiyar","num":362,"t":["வழக்கெனைத்து மில்லாத வாள்வாய்க் கிடந்தும்","இழுக்கினைத் தாம்பெறுவ ராயின் - இழுக்கெனைத்தும்","செய்குறாப் பாணி சிறிதே அச் சின்மொழியார்","கையுறாப் பாணி பெரிது."],"p":"Though compassed round with a faultless guard of swords, ifonce they gain freedom, in little time they are stained with every fault; and long is the time those soft-voiced once spurn every law of right","r":"Even when women are kept under a faultless guard of swords, the time in which soft-spoken women commit no fault is short and the time in which they don't keep to good conduct is long; the verse's condition on this is disputed.","n":"This is one of the collection's harshest lines on women, and its scope depends on the condition in the second line. Mudaliar cites Kural 57 with it, though 57 praises women's own guard rather than doubting it.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","manam"],"pos":[["mudaliar","He reads the condition as 'even if they get no chance to stray': strict guard can stop the deed, but most women's minds still go astray.","மகளிர் கடுங்காவலின் வைகிக் குற்றஞ் செய்தற்குச் சிறிது மிடம் பெறாராயினும் அவர் மனந் தீவழிச் செல்லுதலே பெரும்பான்மை"],["shanmuganar","He keeps the condition as written: the claim applies if women take the faulty way.","தாம் இழுக்கான வழியில் செல்வா ராயின்"]],"ch":"n:37"},"n:363":{"book":"naladiyar","num":363,"t":["எறியென் றெதிர்நிற்பாள் கூற்றம் சிறுகாலை","அட்டில் புகாதாள் அரும்பிணி - அட்டதனை","உண்டி யுதவாதாள் இல்வாழ்பேய் இம்மூவர்","கொண்டானைக் கொல்லும் படை."],"p":"Death is the wife that stands and dares her spouse to strike! Disease is she who enters not the kitchen betimes! Demon domestic is she who cooks and gives no alms! These three are swords to slay their lords !","r":"The wife who stands up to her husband saying 'strike me' is Death; the one who doesn't go into the kitchen at dawn is an incurable disease; the one who doesn't serve the food she has cooked is a demon in the house; these three are weapons that kill the man who took them.","n":"Pope's 'gives no alms' makes the third fault a failure of charity. Both commentators make it a failure to serve the husband, and Mudaliar adds that she eats the food herself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaazhkkaithunai","ilvaazhkkai","kootru"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:37"},"n:364":{"book":"naladiyar","num":364,"t":["கடியெனக் கேட்டுங் கடியான், வெடிபட","ஆர்ப்பது கேட்டும் அதுதெளியான் - பேர்த்துமோர்","இற்கொண் டினித஧ருஉம் ஏமுறுதல் என்பவே","கற்கொண் டெறியுந் தவறு."],"p":"He hears (that marriage is another name for) dread, and yet he dreads it not! He hears the fearful funeral drum, but it gives him no sense ! Again to take a wife and to dwell bewildered amidst domestic enjoyments is a fault that merits stoning; sosay (the wise).","r":"He hears marriage called a dread and doesn't give it up; he hears the funeral drum beat loud enough to split heads and still doesn't see; to take a wife again and live lost in pleasure, they say, is the fault of stoning oneself.","n":"Shanmuganar places the drum at the first wife's funeral, which makes this a verse about remarriage. Mudaliar points back to quatrain 56, where கடி names marriage as something to be dreaded. On either reading the verse goes further than a warning about remarrying: it treats household life itself as what the wise tell one to give up.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","ilvaazhkkai","pattru","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:37"},"n:365":{"book":"naladiyar","num":365,"t":["தலையே தவமுயன்று வாழ்தல் ஒருவர்க்","கிடையே இனியார்கண் தங்கல் - கடையே","புணராதென் றெண்ணிப் பொருள்நசையால் தம்மை","உணரார்பின் சென்று நிலை."],"p":"The best thing is a life spent in penitential practices. The middle course is to live with dear ones around. The worst of all is, with the thought that we have not enough, through desire of wealth subserviently to follow those who understand us not.","r":"The best way to live is to strive in austerity; the middle way is to dwell with those one loves; the lowest is to follow, out of desire for wealth, people who don't recognise one's worth, even thinking it won't come.","n":"இனியார் is general, 'the dear ones', and both commentators narrow it to wives. Mudaliar reads புணராது என்று எண்ணி as 'even while thinking it won't be gained', which makes the lowest life futile as well as servile; Pope's 'not enough' reads it as discontent.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","ilvaazhkkai","avaa","maanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:37"},"n:366":{"book":"naladiyar","num":366,"t":["கல்லாக் கழிப்பர் தலையாயார் நல்லவை","துவ்வாக் கழிப்பர் இடைகள் கடைகள்","இனிதுண்ணோம் ஆரப் பெறேமியாம் என்னும்","முனிவினாற் கண்பா டிலர்."],"p":"","r":"The best pass their time in learning, the middle in enjoying good things, and the lowest lie sleepless with resentment, saying 'we haven't eaten well, we haven't got enough'.","n":"கல்லா here means 'having learned', not 'not learning'. Mudaliar specifies books of spiritual knowledge and goods gained by right means; the verse says only learning and good things. The lowest are marked by craving rather than by poverty.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","inbam","avaa"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:37"},"n:367":{"book":"naladiyar","num":367,"t":["செந்நெல்லா லாய செழுமுளை மற்றுமச்","செந்நெல்லே யாகி விளைதலால் - அந்நெல்","வயனிறையக் காய்க்கும் வளவய லூர","மகனறிவு தந்தை யறிவு."],"p":"","r":"Lord of the rich-fielded town where the paddy fills the fields: since the sprout from red paddy grows again into that same red paddy, a son's understanding is his father's.","n":"The verse states inheritance as a law, the way a seed breeds true. It doesn't say whether the son learns from his father or is born like him. Mudaliar's parallel, 'the son tells the father's worth', reads the son as the sign of his lineage.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","makkal","kudi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:37"},"n:368":{"book":"naladiyar","num":368,"t":["உடைப்பெருஞ் செல்வரும் சான்றோரும் கெட்டுப்","புடைப்பெண்டிர் மக்களும் கீழும் பெருகிக்","கடைக்கால் தலைக்கண்ண தாகிக் குடைக்கால்போல்","கீழ்மேலாய் நிற்கும் உலகு."],"p":"The wealthy men of great possessions and the perfect have perished, while wantons' sons and base men multiply. The lowest takes chief place, and, like the umbrella's handle, this world is upside down!","r":"The wealthy of long standing and the worthy decline, while concubines' sons and base men flourish; what belongs at the feet comes to the head, and like an umbrella's handle the world stands upside down.","n":"Shanmuganar explains the umbrella: its handle points down when the umbrella is open and up when it is folded, so the same world can flip between orders. The complaint mixes moral and social rank, putting the worthy beside old wealth and the base beside concubines' sons.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","saandror","selvam","kudi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:37"},"n:369":{"book":"naladiyar","num":369,"t":["இனியார்தம் நெஞ்சத்து நோயுரைப்ப அந்நோய்","தணியாத உள்ளம் உடையார் - மணிவரன்றி","வீழும் அருவி விறன்மலை நன்னாட","வாழ்வின் வரைபாய்தல் நன்று."],"p":"Lord of the good land of mighty hills whence streams descend sweeping along pearls! Better men should jump down a precipice than live with a mind not disposed to assuage the pain of the dear ones who tell them of their heart's pain.","r":"Lord of the good land of mighty hills, whose falls sweep down gems: those whose hearts don't ease the pain their dear ones tell them of would do better to leap from a cliff than to live.","n":"The verse doesn't require them to remove the pain, only to be moved by it; a heart unmoved disqualifies a life. Mudaliar takes இனியார் as friends.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","arul","idumbai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:37"},"n:370":{"book":"naladiyar","num":370,"t":["புதுப்புனலும் பூங்குழையார் நட்பும் இரண்டும்","விதுப்புற நாடின்வே றல்ல - புதுப்புனலும்","மாரி அறவே அறுமே, அவரன்பும்","வாரி அறவே அறும்."],"p":"‘The mountain freshed, and the love of those adorned With pendant jewels rare examined calmly prove To differ nought: that fails when rains that feed it fail; And love of these fails too, when income fails !’ ------------","r":"Fresh floodwater and the friendship of women with fine earrings, examined calmly, are no different: the flood ends when the rain ends, and their love ends when the income ends.","n":"Mudaliar records a second reading of விதுப்பு அற, 'without desire', which would make the examiner a man no longer infatuated.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","selvam","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:37"},"n:371":{"book":"naladiyar","num":371,"t":["விளக்கொளியும் வேசையர் நட்பும் இரண்டும்","துளக்கற நாடின்வே றல்ல - விளக்கொளியும்","நெய்யற்ற கண்ணே அறுமே, அவரன்பும்","கையற்ற கண்ணே அறும்."],"p":"The lamp's light and harlot's love examined well are seen to differ not a whit; the lamp's light goes out when the feeding oil is consumed ! and the harlot's love is spent when the lavish hand has spent its wealth.","r":"The lamp's light and the friendship of harlots, examined steadily, are no different: the light ends when the oil runs out, and their love ends when the hand is empty.","n":"Mudaliar takes கை, the hand, as the money held in it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","selvam","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:38"},"n:372":{"book":"naladiyar","num":372,"t":["அங்கோட் டகலல்குல் ஆயிழையாள் நம்மோடு","செங்கோடு பாய்துமே என்றாள்மன் - செங்கோட்டின்","மேற்காணம் இன்மையான் மேவா தொழிந்தாளே","காற்கால்நோய் காட்டிக் கலுழ்ந்து."],"p":"She (of enticing beauty), adorned with choice jewels, said forsooth, 'I will leap with you down the steep precipice;' but on the very brow of the precipice, because I had no money, she, weeping and pointing to her aching feet, withdrew and left me alone!","r":"The woman with fine jewels said she would leap with us from the high peak; but at the peak, since we had no gold, she showed a pain in her feet, wept, and wouldn't come on.","n":"The speaker is the dupe himself, and the promise he recalls is a lovers' death pact. Mudaliar takes the foot pain as a lie and the tears as feigned; the verse says only that she showed the pain and wept.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:38"},"n:373":{"book":"naladiyar","num":373,"t":["அங்கண் விசும்பின் அமரர் தொழப்படுஞ்","செங்கண்மா லாயினும் ஆகமன்- தங்கைக்","கொடுப்பதொன் றில்லாரைக் கொய்தளி ரன்னார்","விடுப்பர்தங் கையால் தொழுது."],"p":"Though he be Mal, the fiery-eyed, whom in the heaven's fair homes immortals worship, if in hand he bring no gift the women tender as the buds men cull, will straight dismiss him, bowing low with folded hands.","r":"Even if he were the red-eyed Mal whom the gods of the wide sky worship, women tender as plucked shoots would join their hands in worship and send away a man with nothing in his hand to give.","n":"The joke lies in the gesture: hands joined as if in worship are the hands that dismiss him. Mudaliar takes மால் as Tirumal, with Indra as an alternative; Shanmuganar has Indra.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:38"},"n:374":{"book":"naladiyar","num":374,"t":["ஆணமில் நெஞ்சத் தணிநீலக் கண்ணார்க்குக்","காணமி லாதார் கடுவனையர் - காணவே","செக்கூர்ந்து கொண்டாரும் செய்த பொருளுடையார்","அக்காரம் அன்னார் அவர்க்கு."],"p":"To the damsels of loveless hearts, whose eyes are as beauteous as blue water-lilies, those who have no wealth are as poison! Even those who have turned the (oil) press, in sight of all men, if they have wealth, are as sugar to them.","r":"To women with loveless hearts and lovely blue-lily eyes, men without money are poison; but even a man seen by all driving an oil press is sugar to them if he has earned wealth.","n":"Mudaliar glosses the oil press as a low trade. The verse says money erases even that rank in their eyes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","selvam","kudi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:38"},"n:375":{"book":"naladiyar","num":375,"t":["பாம்பிற் கொருதலை காட்டி ஒருதலை","தேம்படு தெண்கயத்து மீன்காட்டும் - ஆங்கு","மலங்கன்ன செய்கை மகளிர்தோள் சேர்வர்","விலங்கன்ன வெள்ளறிவி னார்."],"p":"The silly ones, who are as beasts, seek the embraces of women who are like the eel, which shows one head to the snake, and another head to the fish, in the sweet clear lake : (are of a double and deceitful nature).","r":"Like the eel that shows one end to the snake and the other to the fish in the clear, sweet pond, women of double conduct are embraced by the dull-witted, who are like beasts.","n":"Mudaliar explains the eel's trick as matching each man's nature and showing him love. The verse turns part of the blame on the men, who are the beasts. Shanmuganar's commentary on this quatrain appears under 374.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:38"},"n:376":{"book":"naladiyar","num":376,"t":["பொத்தநூற் கல்லும் புணர்பிரியா அன்றிலும்போல்","நித்தலும் நம்மைப் பிரியலம் என்றுரைத்த","பொற்றொடியும் போர்த்தகர்க்கோ டாயினாள் நன்னெஞ்சே","நிற்றியோ போதியோ நீ."],"p":"‘We will never part, like the precious stone strung on its thread, or the Andril,' said the damsel with golden bracelets: she has now become the horn of a fighting ram (she angrily repels me). Dear heart! dost linger still, or wilt thou go?","r":"The woman of golden bangles who said 'we'll never part from you', like a gem strung on its thread and the anril birds that never separate, has turned away like a fighting ram's horn; my good heart, will you stay, or will you go?","n":"The speaker turns from her to his own heart, which is still with her. Both commentators take the last line as 'will you stay with her or come with me?', so the heart is a separate party the man has to win back.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","nenju","vanjam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:38"},"n:377":{"book":"naladiyar","num":377,"t":["ஆமாபோல் நக்கி அவர்கைப் பொருள்கொண்டு","சேமாபோல் குப்புறூஉஞ் சில்லைக்கண் அன்பினை","ஏமாந் தெமதென் றிருந்தார் பெறுபவே","தாமாம் பலரால் நகை."],"p":"Those who fondly reckon upon the devoted love of the worthless wanton, that, like the wild ox, licks the hand and despoils men of their wealth, and then, like the buffalo, bounds away, shall suffer the deserved ridicule of many.","r":"Those who are fooled into thinking theirs the love of a worthless woman who licks like a wild cow, takes the wealth from their hands, and then lies down like a balking ox, will be laughed at by many.","n":"Mudaliar cites a belief that the wild cow's lick pleases at first and kills later. குப்புறூஉம் is either lying face down, as Mudaliar takes it first and Shanmuganar takes it, or bounding away, as in Pope.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:38"},"n:378":{"book":"naladiyar","num":378,"t":["ஏமாந்த போழ்தின் இனியார்போன் றின்னாராய்த்","தாமார்ந்த போதே தகர்கோடாம் - மானோக்கின்","தந்நெறிப் பெண்டிர் தடமுலை சேராரே,","செந்நெறிச் சேர்துமென் பார்."],"p":"Those whose avowed purpose it is to walk in the way of rectitude seek not the embrace of the fascinating, fawn-eyed damsels, who walk in a way of their own' who are pleasant when gratified with gifts, and when they are filled( =when no more gifts are to be expected) are like the horn of the fighting ram.","r":"Those who mean to reach the right path won't embrace the large breasts of doe-eyed women who go their own way, women who seem kind while a man is infatuated and turn harsh as a ram's horn 'once they are full'; who is full is disputed.","n":"Mudaliar glosses செந்நெறி as the path to liberation, which makes avoiding these women a renunciant's discipline; Shanmuganar has only 'the good way'.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","ozhukkam","thuravu"],"pos":[["mudaliar","தாம் ஆர்ந்தபோது refers to the men: when, now poor, they come of their own desire, the women turn harsh.","தாம் ஆர்ந்தபோது - (வறுமையடைந்து) தாமே விரும்பிச் சென்ற போது."],["shanmuganar","It refers to the women: once they are filled with a man's wealth, they turn harsh.","தாம் செல்வம் நிரம்பியபோது இனிமையற்றவராய்"]],"ch":"n:38"},"n:379":{"book":"naladiyar","num":379,"t":["ஊறுசெய் நெஞ்சந்தம் உள்ளடக்கி ஒண்ணுதலார்","தேறா மொழிந்த மொழிகேட்டுத் - தேறி","எமரென்று கொள்வாரும் கொள்பவே யார்க்கும்","தமரல்லர் தம்உடம்பி னார்."],"p":"Let those accept (wantons' false love), who take them as their own believing the words uttered to inspire belief, by the bright-browed ones, who keep concealed within the cruelty that lurks in their heart. Those whose bodies are their sole wealth belong to none, (i. e. they have no souls to give!).","r":"Some do take bright-browed women as their own, trusting words spoken to be believed by women who hide their harmful intent; but such women belong to no one except their own bodies.","n":"Pope adds 'they have no souls to give'. Mudaliar's gloss is narrower: their attachment reaches only as far as their own bodies.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:38"},"n:380":{"book":"naladiyar","num":380,"t":["உள்ளம் ஒருவன் உழையதா ஒண்ணுதலார்","கள்ளத்தாற் செய்யுங் கருத்தெல்லாந் - தெள்ளி","அறிந்த விடத்தும் அறியாராம் பாவம்","செறிந்த உடம்பி னவர்."],"p":"Though the dupes clearly discern and know the guileful intentions entertained by the bright browed ones, even where their minds are (apparently) set upon some persons, they whose bodies are full of sin do not recognise it! ---------","r":"The scheming of bright-browed women whose minds are with another man is clearly known, and still those whose bodies are full of sin don't grasp it; who it is that knows is disputed.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","pedhaimai","vinai"],"pos":[["mudaliar","The dupes themselves examine and know the guile, yet don't grasp that the women should be shunned.","(அவர் பரிகரிக்கத் தக்காரென்று) உணரார்கள்."],["shanmuganar","The world knows the guile clearly; the sinful fools alone don't know it.","உலகினரால் தெளிவாக அறியப்பட்டிருந்தும் பாவம் நிறைந்த உடம்பை யுடைய மூடர் அறியமாட்டார்."]],"ch":"n:38"},"n:381":{"book":"naladiyar","num":381,"t":["அரும்பெறற் கற்பின் அயிராணி யன்ன","பெரும்பெயர்ப் பெண்டி ரெனினும் - விரும்பிப்","பெறுநசையால் பின்னிற்பா ரின்மையே பேணும்","நறுநுதலாள் நன்மைத் துணை."],"p":"Though women live famed as Ayirani for rarest gift of chastity, absence of men that stand enamoured of their charms is a help in way of good to those of fragrant brow who guard themselves.","r":"Even among women as famous as Indrani for a chastity hard to attain, the fragrant-browed woman who keeps the quality that leaves no man following her in desire is the good companion.","n":"Pope reads it differently: the absence of admirers is a help to women who guard themselves, as though even the chaste benefit from not being tempted. Both commentators make the absence her own doing. Indrani is the standard of the comparison, so the claim is strong: even such women are measured by this.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","vaazhkkaithunai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:39"},"n:382":{"book":"naladiyar","num":382,"t":["குடநீரட் டுண்ணும் இடுக்கட் பொழுதும்","கடனீ ரறவுண்ணும் கேளிர் வரினும்","கடனீர்மை கையாறாக் கொள்ளு மடமொழி","மாதர் மனைமாட்சி யாள்."],"p":"her duties with kindness of speech. When in the straitened time they cook and eat with but one pot of water on the hearth,— if relatives arrive enough to drain the sea, the soft-voiced dame, the glory of her home, well fulfils each seemly duty.","r":"Even in hard times, when the household cooks with a single pot of water, and even if kin arrive enough to drink the sea dry, the soft-spoken woman who keeps her duties as her settled way is the glory of the home.","n":"The two hyperboles push in opposite directions, one pot against a sea, and her duty holds between them. Shanmuganar names the duty as hospitality.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","vaazhkkaithunai","inmai","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:39"},"n:383":{"book":"naladiyar","num":383,"t":["நாலாறும் ஆறாய் நனிசிறிதாய் எப்புறனும்","மேலாறு மேலுறை சோரினும் - மேலாய","வல்லாளாய் வாழும்ஊர் தற்புகழு மாண்கற்பின்","இல்லாள் அமர்ந்ததே இல்."],"p":"On every side the narrow dwelling lies open, on every part the rain drips down; yet, if the dame has noble gifts, praised by townsfolk for her modest worth, such a housewife's blest abode is indeed a home! The wife : 'placens uxor.' She is sweet to the eye, and adorned in the way that a lover loves; she enforces awe: her virtue shames the village folk; she is submissive; but in fitting place is stern, yet sweetly relents :—such a soft-voiced dame is a wife.","r":"Though the house is open on all four sides, tiny, and dripping with rain from the roof everywhere, where the excellent wife lives, capable in the highest virtues and praised by the town she lives in, that alone is a home.","n":"Mudaliar draws the consequence the verse leaves unsaid, that other houses are forests, which completes the pair with 361.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaazhkkaithunai","karpu","inmai","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:39"},"n:384":{"book":"naladiyar","num":384,"t":["கட்கினியாள், காதலன் காதல் வகைபுனைவாள்,","உட்குடையாள், ஊர்நாண் இயல்பினாள், - உட்கி","இடனறிந் தூடி இனிதின் உணரும்","மடமொழி மாதராள் பெண்."],"p":"","r":"She is a wife who is lovely to the eye, adorns herself as her husband loves, holds him in awe, has the modesty that is shy before the town, and, in awe of him, knows when to sulk and relents sweetly: the soft-spoken woman.","n":"Mudaliar gives two senses of ஊர்நாண் இயல்பினாள்: one who is shy before the townsfolk, or one whose conduct puts the townsfolk to shame. The translation attached to 383 in Pope's text takes the second. Sulking is counted among a good wife's virtues, provided it is well timed and quickly ended.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaazhkkaithunai","naanam","oodal"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:39"},"n:385":{"book":"naladiyar","num":385,"t":["எஞ்ஞான்றும் எங்கணவர் எந்தோள்மேற் சேர்ந்தெழினும்","அஞ்ஞான்று கண்டேம்போல் நாணுதுமால் - எஞ்ஞான்றும்","என்னை கெழீஇயினர் கொல்லோ பெருள்நசையால்","பன்மார்பு சேர்ந்தொழுகு வார்."],"p":"‘Whenever our spouse takes us in his embrace, we feel a timid shame as if we saw him then for the first time and they (wantons) daily, through desire of gain, submit to the embraces of many! How can this be?'","r":"However often our husband rises from our shoulders, we feel as shy as if we were seeing him for the first time; how, then, do those who embrace many men's chests for gain do it every day?","n":"The wife speaks with wonder rather than scorn: she can't imagine the life she sets against her own.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","karpu","varaivinmagalir"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:39"},"n:386":{"book":"naladiyar","num":386,"t":["உள்ளத் துணர்வுடையான் ஓதிய நூலற்றால்","வள்ளன்மை பூண்டான்கண் ஒண்பொருள் - தெள்ளிய","ஆண்மகன் கையில் அயில்வாள் அனைத்தரோ","நாணுடையாள் பெற்ற நலம்."],"p":"As the scroll that he reads, whose inmost heart well understands it, is goodly wealth with him who is graced by a generous spirit. As a keen weapon in the hand of a clear-souled hero, is the loveliness that a modest woman owns.","r":"Like the learning of one with understanding in his heart, like bright wealth in the hands of a generous man, like a sharp sword in the hand of a clear-minded hero, is the beauty a modest woman has.","n":"The verse gives three similes and no explanation. Mudaliar supplies one: learning and a giver's wealth serve everyone, but a hero's sword destroys whoever stands against it, so a modest woman's beauty serves her husband alone and ruins any other who desires it. Shanmuganar pairs the first two similes as both useful and calls the sword only notable.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","nalam","karpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:39"},"n:387":{"book":"naladiyar","num":387,"t":["கருங்கொள்ளும் செங்கொள்ளும் தூணிப் பதக்கென்று","ஒருங்கொப்பக் கொண்டானாம் ஊரன் - ஒருங்கொவ்வா","நன்னுதலார்த் தோய்ந்த வரைமார்பன் நீராடாது","என்னையும் தோய வரும்."],"p":"'The lord of the town has bought, it seems, black gram and red gram, a tuni and a pathakku, as if they were all the same! He, whose chest is broad as a mountain, having associated with the beautiful browed ones , — inferior to me! — unpurified, seeks my society too!'","r":"The lord of the town has bought, it seems, black gram and red gram, a tuni and a pathakku, all as one; the mountain-chested man who embraced bright-browed women unlike me comes, without bathing, to embrace me too.","n":"The grain proverb is the wife's charge of indiscrimination: he treats the courtesans and his wife as one measure. Mudaliar says the verse shows the wife's own purity.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","karpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:39"},"n:388":{"book":"naladiyar","num":388,"t":["கொடியவை கூறாதி பாண. நீ கூறின்","அடிபைய இட்டொதுங்கிச் சென்று - துடியின்","இடக்கண் அனையம்யாம் ஊரற் கதனால்","வலக்கண் அனையார்க் குரை."],"p":"‘O minstrel, utter not cruel words, or if thou utter them, softly draw back thy step, and go to utter them to those who are like the lute's right side; for we, to the lord of the town, are as its left.'","r":"Minstrel, don't speak cruel words; if you must, step softly aside and go: to the lord of the town we are like the drum's left face, so tell it to those who are like its right face.","n":"The right face of the துடி is struck and the left isn't, as Mudaliar explains: the wife calls herself the side that is no longer played. The minstrel is the husband's go-between.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:39"},"n:389":{"book":"naladiyar","num":389,"t":["சாய்ப்பறிக்க நீர்திகழும் தண்வய லூரன்மீது","ஈப்பறக்க நொந்தேனும் யானேமன் - தீப்பறக்கத்","தாக்கி முலைபொருத தண்சாந் தணியகலம்","நோக்கி யிருந்தேனும் யான்."],"p":"'I am she, forsooth, that felt a pang when a fly alighted on the lord of the town, surrounded by cool rice-fields over which the waters gleam, where they pluck the rich grass! And I am she who have lived to look upon his breast adorned with cool sandal-wood paste, which has been warmly embraced by others.","r":"I am the one who grieved if a fly so much as flew over the lord of the cool town where water gleams once the reeds are pulled; and I am the one who has sat looking at his chest, its cool sandal paste smeared by breasts that struck it till sparks flew.","n":"The repeated 'I am the one' sets two times side by side without comment, and the grief lies in the gap between them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","pirivu","idumbai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:39"},"n:390":{"book":"naladiyar","num":390,"t":["அரும்பவிழ் தாரினான் எம்அருளும் என்று","பெரும்பொய் உரையாதி, பாண - கரும்பின்","கடைக்கண் அனையம்நாம் ஊரற் கதனால்","இடைக்கண் அனையார்க் குரை."],"p":"'Minstrel , utter not a gross falsehood, saying that he who wears a garland of opening buds will favour us!’ ‘To the lord of the town we are as the (tasteless) tip of the sugar-cane ; therefore, tell (such a tale) to them who are like its middle (sweet, juicy) joints.'","r":"Minstrel, don't tell the great lie that the man with the garland of opening buds will be kind to us; to the lord of the town we are like the end joints of the sugar-cane, so tell it to those who are like the middle joints.","n":"Mudaliar gives the situation: the husband, back from a courtesan and refused, sends the minstrel to soothe her, and she mocks the messenger.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:39"},"n:391":{"book":"naladiyar","num":391,"t":["முயங்காக்காற் பாயும் பசலைமற் றூடி","உயங்காக்கால் உப்பின்றாம் காமம் - வயங்கோதம்","நில்லாத் திரையலைக்கும் நீள்கழித் தண்சேர்ப்ப","புல்லாப் புலப்பதோர் ஆறு."],"p":"'Lord of the cool shore of the deep bay, where the gleaming ocean's restless billows beat! 'If there be no fond embrace, a sickly hue will spread itself (over her face); and, if there be no lovers' quarrels, love will lack its zest. ‘To embrace and disagree is the one way (of love).'","r":"Lord of the cool shore with long backwaters where the bright sea's restless waves beat: without embrace, pallor spreads; without sulking and pining, love has no salt; embracing and then sulking is one way.","n":"Mudaliar takes உப்பு as sweetness and compares the Sanskrit theorists, who call separation the sweeter mode. The verse sets pallor and saltlessness as twin dangers: love needs both union and its interruption.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","punarchi","pasalai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:40"},"n:392":{"book":"naladiyar","num":392,"t":["தம்மமர் காதலர் தார்சூழ் அணியகலம்","விம்ம முயங்கும் துணையில்லார்க் - கிம்மெனப்","பெய்ய எழிலி முழங்கும் திசையெல்லாம்","நெய்தல் அறைந்தன்ன நீர்த்து."],"p":"‘To those who were wont to strain in close embrace their own beloved whose broad breast was girt with garlands, when the rains patter down, it is as though one beat the funeral drum through all the regions where the muttering of the thunder is heard from out the clouds; since they are deprived of their loved one's society.'","r":"For women without the companion whose garlanded chest they would embrace till it swelled, the clouds thundering in every direction as the rain comes down sound like the beat of a funeral drum.","n":"The verse doesn't say why the rain hurts. Mudaliar does: the man promised to come back by the rainy season, so the rains arriving without him are the sign of a broken word.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kaamanoy","punarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:40"},"n:393":{"book":"naladiyar","num":393,"t":["கம்மஞ்செய் மாக்கள் கருவி ஒடுக்கிய","மம்மர்கொள் மாலை மலராய்ந்து பூத்தொடுப்பாள்","கைம்மாலை இட்டுக் கலுழ்ந்தாள் துணையில்லார்க்கு","இம்மாலை என்செய்வ தென்று."],"p":"At wildering eventide, when workmen all put by their tools, she culled choice flowers, and wove a gay garland ; — then let it fall from her hands, and wailed, 'What can this wreath avail to me who weep alone?’","r":"In the bewildering evening, when craftsmen have put away their tools, she was choosing flowers and weaving a garland; she threw down the garland in her hand and wept: 'What use is this garland to those without a companion?'","n":"The verse ends on இம்மாலை, which can mean 'this garland' or 'this evening'. The commentators take it as the garland, but the double sense may be intended. Mudaliar has the friend report the scene to the man.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","pirivu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:40"},"n:394":{"book":"naladiyar","num":394,"t":["செல்சுடர் நோக்கிச் சிதரா஢க்கண் கொண்டநீர்","மெல்விரல் ஊழ்தெறியா விம்மித்தன் - மெல்விரலின்","நாள்வைத்து நங்குற்றம் எண்ணுங்கொல், அந்தோதன்","தோள்வைத் தணைமேல் கிடந்து."],"p":"","r":"Watching the setting sun, flicking away again and again with soft fingers the tears in her red-streaked eyes, sobbing, does she, alas, count the days on her soft fingers and reckon up my faults, lying on the bed with her arm for a pillow?","n":"Mudaliar has the man speak this to his charioteer on the way home, imagining her grief, and cites Kural 1261. 'Our faults' turns her counting of days into a reckoning of his wrongs.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ninaivu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:40"},"n:395":{"book":"naladiyar","num":395,"t":["கண்கயல் என்னும் கருத்தினால் காதலி","பின்சென்றது அம்ம சிறுசிரல் - பின்சென்றும்","ஊக்கி யெழுந்தும் எறிகல்லா ஒண்புருவம்","கோட்டிய வில்வாக் கறிந்து."],"p":"‘The little kingfisher seeing the eyes of my beloved (as she was disporting in the tank) and taking them for carp, followed her; but though it followed, and poised itself aloft, it darted not down, recognising her gleaming brow bent above them as a bow!","r":"Taking my beloved's eyes for carp, the little kingfisher followed her; but though it followed and rose eager to strike, it didn't dive, recognising her bright brow as a drawn bow.","n":"Mudaliar calls it மயக்கவணி, the figure of mistaken identity, and has the man say it in wonder to his friend. Pope adds that the man is showing his own timid reverence.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","nalam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:40"},"n:396":{"book":"naladiyar","num":396,"t":["அரக்காம்பல் நாறும்வாய் அம்மருங்கிற் கன்னோ","பரற்கானம் ஆற்றின கொல்லோ - அரக்கார்ந்த","பஞ்சிகொண் டுட்டினும் பையெனப் பையெனவென்று","அஞ்சிப்பின் வாங்கும் அடி."],"p":"'When I applied the (softest) cotton soaked in the red dye to the foot of her whose waist is lovely, and whose mouth breathes the fragrance of the red water-lily, did she not cry \"gently, gently,\" and shrinking draw it back? And oh! has it endured the stony, desert path? '","r":"Feet that drew back in fear, crying 'gently, gently', even when red dye was applied with soft cotton: have they, alas, borne the stony desert path, the feet of my girl with lily-scented lips and lovely waist?","n":"Mudaliar identifies the speaker as the girl's mother, who has learned that her daughter has gone with her lover through the wilderness. The verse sets the softest touch the mother knows against the hardest ground she can imagine.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","makkal","idumbai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:40"},"n:397":{"book":"naladiyar","num":397,"t":["ஓலைக் கணக்கர் ஒலியடங்கு புன்செக்கர்","மாலைப் பொழுதில் மணந்தார் பிரிவுள்ளி","மாலை பரிந்திட் டழுதாள் வனமுலைமேல்","கோலஞ்செய் சாந்தந் திமிர்ந்து."],"p":"When those who con the palm leaf scrolls had ceased, in eve's din twilight hour, she thought of her absent spouse! and weeping plucked the flowers from out her wreath, and brushed the odorous sandal from her lovely breast!","r":"In the dim red evening, when the palm-leaf accountants fall silent, she thought of the parting of the man who married her, tore off her garland, and wept, rubbing away the sandal paste that adorned her lovely breasts.","n":"Mudaliar records two settings: the friend describing her grief in his absence, or the friend telling the man she won't consent to his going. The second makes the parting still to come. He also reports a view that the silenced voices are schoolchildren reciting, which Shanmuganar adopts.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","pirivu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:40"},"n:398":{"book":"naladiyar","num":398,"t":["கடக்கருங் கானத்துக் காளைபின் நாளை","நடக்கவும் வல்லையோ என்றி - சுடர்த்தொடீஇ","பெற்றா னொருவன் பெருங்குதிரை அந்நிலையே","கற்றான் அஃதூரும் ஆறு."],"p":"'Thou hast said, O maiden with burnished brace - lets ! have you strength to walk on the morrow after your beloved? When one (a warrior) has obtained a splendid horse, that very instant he has learned how to ride it! (his enthusiasm teaches him).","r":"You ask, friend of the bright bangles, whether I can walk tomorrow behind my young bull of a man through the forest that is hard to cross; a man who gets a great horse learns to ride it that very moment.","n":"The answer is a proverb: the ability comes with the thing. Mudaliar spells out the hint, that following one's husband is not hard. Pope's 'his enthusiasm teaches him' is his own explanation.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","ookkam","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:40"},"n:399":{"book":"naladiyar","num":399,"t":["முலைக்கண்ணும் முத்தும் முழுமெய்யும் புல்லும்","இலக்கணம் யாதும் அறியேன் - கலைக்கணம்","வேங்கை ஦வ்ருஉம் நெறிசெலிய போலும்என்","தீம்பாவை செய்த குறி."],"p":"‘My breast, my necklace of pearls, and my whole body she embraced ! I knew not what it portended. It was the sign, it seems, made by my lovely one that she was about to set out on the path where the herds of antelopes flee in fright from the tiger.'","r":"I didn't understand the sign at all when she embraced me so that my nipples, my pearls and my whole body were pressed; it seems the sign my sweet doll made meant she was about to take the path where herds of deer flee from the tiger.","n":"Mudaliar has the mother speak after her daughter's flight. The embrace was a farewell that only the leaving gave meaning to.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","makkal","kurippu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:40"},"n:400":{"book":"naladiyar","num":400,"t":["கண்மூன் றுடையானும் காக்கையும் பையரவும்","என்னீன்ற யாயும் பிழைத்ததென் - பொன்னீன்ற","கோங்கரும் பன்ன முலையாய் பொருள்வயின்","பாங்கனார் சென்ற நெறி."],"p":"The triple-eyed (Civan), the crow, the hooded snake; the mother that bare me: what have these done amiss ? Maiden whose bosom bears the Gongu buds, all gold! The way my lover went for wealth is my pain.’","r":"The three-eyed one, the crow, the hooded snake, the mother who bore me: what wrong did they do? Friend with breasts like golden kongu buds, it is the road my lover took for wealth.","n":"The verse names four suspects and acquits them without saying why. Both commentators supply the myths: Siva burned Kama and let him live again, the crow raised the koel's chick, the snake swallowed the moon and released it, and the mother let her live, so each lies behind one torment of separation. On that reading she refuses the easy targets and blames the one real cause, the journey for wealth. The verse closes the Naladiyar.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","selvam","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:40"},"t:1":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1,"t":["ஒன்றவன் தானே இரண்டவன் இன்னருள்","நின்றனன் மூன்றினுள் நான்குணர்ந் தான்ஐந்து","வென்றனன் ஆறு விரிந்தனன் ஏழும்பர்ச்","சென்றனன் தானிருந் தான்உணர்ந் தெட்டே."],"p":"","r":"He is one; he is two with his sweet grace; he stood within three, knew four, conquered five, spread out as six, went up beyond seven, and remains, knowing eight.","n":"The verse is a count from one to eight and names almost none of what is counted. Traditionally: two as Siva with Shakti (his grace), three as the three gods or the three gunas, four as the Vedas, five as the senses, six as the six adhvas or the Vedangas, seven as the worlds above, eight as the eight qualities or the eightfold form. Only ஐந்து வென்றனன் (conquered five) and இன்னருள் (sweet grace) are fairly secure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","iraivan","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:1"},"t:2":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2,"t":["போற்றிசைத்து இன்னுயிர் மன்னும் புனிதனை","நாற்றிசைக் கும்நல்ல மாதுக்கும் நாதனை","மேற்றிசைக் குள்தென் திசைக்கொரு வேந்தனாம்","கூற்றுதைத் தானையான் கூறுகின் றேனே."],"p":"","r":"Praising him, I tell of the pure one in whom dear life abides, the lord of the four directions and of the good lady, who became king even over the southern quarter by kicking Death.","n":"கூற்றுதைத்தான் recalls the legend of Siva kicking Yama to save Markandeya. The south is Yama's direction, which is why தென் திசைக்கொரு வேந்தன் is taken this way; மேற்றிசைக்குள் (within the western, or the upper, quarter) is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","kootru","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:1"},"t:3":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":3,"t":["ஒக்கநின் றானை உலப்பிலி தேவர்கள்","நக்கனென்று ஏத்திடும் நாதனை நாள்தொறும்","பக்கநின் றார்அறி யாத பரமனைப்","புக்குநின்று உன்னியான் போற்றிசெய் வேனே."],"p":"","r":"I enter in and meditate and praise every day the Supreme who stands alike in all, whom the undying gods hail as the naked one, and whom even those who stand at his side do not know.","n":"நக்கன் (the naked one) is a name of Siva as the bare ascetic. ஒக்க நின்றான் may mean 'stands equally in everything' or 'stands together' (with Shakti). The verse sets those near him who fail to know him against the poet who enters within (புக்கு நின்று).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","vazhipaadu","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:1"},"t:4":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":4,"t":["அகலிடத் தார்மெய்யை அண்டத்து வித்தைப்","புகலிடத்து என்றனைப் போதவிட் டானைப்","பகலிடத் தும்இர வும்பணிந் தேத்தி","இகலிடத் தேஇருள் நீங்கிநின் றேனே."],"p":"","r":"Bowing and praising day and night the truth of those on the wide earth, the seed of the cosmos, who sent me into refuge with him, I stood free of darkness in the very place of strife.","n":"போதவிட்டான் can be 'sent me forth' or, from போதம் (knowledge), 'made me understand'. இருள் (darkness) is traditionally the soul's root impurity (anava). இகலிடம், the place of strife, is taken as this world of birth.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","iraivan","malam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:1"},"t:5":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":5,"t":["சிவனொடுஒக் கும்தெய்வம் தேடினும் இல்லை","அவனொடுஒப் பார் இங்கு யாவரும் இல்லை","புவனம் கடந்தன்று பொன்னொளி மின்னும்","தவனச் சடைமுடித் தாமரை யானே."],"p":"","r":"Search as you may, there is no god equal to Siva and no one here like him; the lotus-bearer with burning matted hair flashes with golden light beyond the worlds.","n":"The claim is exclusive: not that Siva is highest among gods but that none compares. தாமரையான் is unusual for Siva and may mean one who wears the lotus, or one whose feet are lotuses.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:1"},"t:6":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":6,"t":["அவனை ஒழிய அமரரும் இல்லை","அவனன்றிச் செய்யும் அருந்தவம் இல்லை","அவனன்றி மூவரால் ஆவதொன் றில்லை","அவனன்றி ஊர்புகு மாறு அறியேனே."],"p":"","r":"Apart from him there are no gods, no hard penance, nothing the three can achieve, and I know no way to enter the city.","n":"The three (மூவர்) are usually Brahma, Vishnu and Rudra. ஊர் புகுமாறு, the way to enter the city, is traditionally taken as reaching release or Siva's world; a plain reading is possible but makes little sense here.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","thavam","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:1"},"t:7":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":7,"t":["முன்னைஒப் பாயுள்ள மூவர்க்கும் மூத்தவன்","தன்னைஒப் பாய்ஒன்றும் இல்லாத் தலைமகன்","தன்னைஅப் பாயெனில் அப்பனு மாயுளன்","பொன்னைஒப் பாகின்ற போதகத் தானே."],"p":"","r":"He is elder to the three who are ancient alike, the chief with nothing like him; call him father and he is father too, the one of knowledge who shines like gold.","n":"போதகத்தான் is taken as the one of knowledge (போதகம்), though போதகம் can also mean a young elephant. தன்னை அப்பா எனில் அப்பனுமாய் உளன: he becomes to the worshipper what the worshipper calls him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:1"},"t:8":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":8,"t":["தீயினும் வெய்யன் புனலினும் தண்ணியன்","ஆயினும் ஈசன் அருளறி வாரில்லை","சேயினும் நல்லன் அணியன்நல் அன்பர்க்குத்","தாயினும் நல்லன் தாழ்சடை யோனே."],"p":"","r":"He is hotter than fire and cooler than water, yet no one knows the Lord's grace; far off, yet near to those who love well, the one with hanging matted hair is kinder than a mother.","n":"சேயினும் நல்லன் can mean 'better than a child' or, with சேய் as 'distant', 'far beyond'; the second pairs with அணியன் (near). The opposites (hot and cool, far and near) say that grace is unknown not because it is hidden but because it is both at once.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","anbu","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:1"},"t:9":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":9,"t":["பொன்னால் புரிந்திட்ட பொற்சடை யென்னப்","பின்னாற் பிறங்க இருந்தவன் பேர்நந்தி","என்னால் தொழப்படும் எம்இறை மற்றவன்","தன்னால் தொழப்படு வாரில்லை தானே."],"p":"","r":"The one who sits with matted hair shining behind him like twisted gold is named Nandi; he is our Lord whom I worship, and there is none whom he worships.","n":"Nandi here is a name of Siva, as throughout the chapter; Tirumular also calls his teacher Nandi, and the two are not always easy to separate.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:1"},"t:10":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":10,"t":["தானே இருநிலம் தாங்கிவிண் ணாய்நிற்கும்","தானே சுடும்அங்கி ஞாயிறும் திங்களும்","தானே மழைபொழி தையலு மாய்நிற்கும்","தானே தடவரை தண்கட லாமே."],"p":"","r":"He himself bears the great earth and stands as the sky; he is the burning fire, the sun and the moon; he stands as the lady who pours rain; he is the great mountain and the cool sea.","n":"தானே opens every line: he is these things, not only their maker. மழைபொழி தையல் (the rain-pouring lady) is taken as Shakti, so his consort too is a form of him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","iraivan","mazhai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:1"},"t:11":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":11,"t":["அயலும் புடையும்எம் ஆதியை நோக்கில்","இயலும் பெருந்தெய்வம் யாதுமொன் றில்லை","முயலும் முயலில் முடிவும் மற் றாங்கே","பெயலும் மழைமுகிற் பேர்நந்தி தானே."],"p":"","r":"Look at our First One near and around, and there is no great god to compare; the end of every effort is there too; the great Nandi is the rain-pouring cloud.","n":"முயலும் முயலில் முடிவும் மற்றாங்கே is compressed: the goal of all striving is in him. அயலும் புடையும் (near and around) may also mean other gods and those beside him.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","muyarchi","mazhai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:1"},"t:12":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":12,"t":["கண்ணுத லான்ஒரு காதலின் நிற்கவும்","எண்ணிலி தேவர் இறந்தார் எனப்பலர்","மண்ணுறு வார்களும் வானுறு வார்களும்","அண்ணல் இவன் என்றுஅறியகி லார்களே."],"p":"","r":"While the god with the eye in his forehead remains in one love, many say countless gods have died; those who go to the earth and those who reach the sky do not know that he is the Lord.","n":"The contrast is between the gods who die and Siva who stays. ஒரு காதலின் நிற்க (stays in one love, or one desire) is unclear: perhaps his unchanging love for souls.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","nilaiyaamai","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:1"},"t:13":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":13,"t":["மண்ணளந் தான்மல ரோன்முதல் தேவர்கள்","எண்ணளந் தின்னும் நினைக்கிலார் ஈசனை","விண்ணளந் தாந்தன்னை மேலளந் தாரில்லை","கண்ணளந் தெங்குங் கடந்துநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"Vishnu who measured the earth, Brahma of the flower and the other gods, even measuring with thought, still cannot conceive the Lord; no one has measured above him who measured the sky; measured by the eye, he stands beyond everything.","n":"The verse plays on அளத்தல் (measuring) and recalls the legend of the column of fire whose top and bottom Brahma and Vishnu could not find. விண்ணளந்தான் may be Vishnu again (who measured the sky) or Siva.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:1"},"t:14":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":14,"t":["கடந்துநினின் றான்கம லம்மல ராதி","கடந்துநின் றான்கடல் வண்ணம்எம் மாயன்","கடந்துநின் றான்அவர்க்கு அப்புறம் ஈசன்","கடந்துநின் றான்எங்கும் கண்டுநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"He stands beyond the lotus-born one, beyond our dark sea-coloured Mayan, beyond even what lies past them; the Lord stands beyond everywhere and stands seeing all.","n":"கடந்துநின்றான் (stood beyond) opens every line. The last words, கண்டுநின்றான், balance transcendence with presence: beyond all, he still watches all.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:1"},"t:15":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":15,"t":["ஆதியு மாய்அர னாய்உட லுள்நின்ற","வேதியு மாய்விரிந்துஆர்ந்துஇருந் தான்அருள்","சோதியு மாய்ச்சுருங் காததோர் தன்மையுள்","நீதியு மாய்நித்த மாகிநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"As the First and as Hara, as the transformer standing within the body, he spread and filled and remained; as the light of grace and as justice in a nature that never shrinks, he stands eternal.","n":"வேதி is taken as the one who transforms (as alchemy changes metal), or as the Vedic one; the first fits உடலுள் நின்ற, within the body. The verse places the Lord in the body as well as beyond the world, a theme the later tantras develop.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","thiruvarul","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:1"},"t:16":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":16,"t":["கோது குலாவிய கொன்றைக் குழற்சடை","மாது குலாவிய வாள்நுதல் பாகனை","யாது குலாவி அமரரும் தேவரும்","கோது குலாவிக் குணம்பயில் வாரே."],"p":"","r":"The immortals and the gods, delighting in the one with konrai in his matted hair who shares half his body with the bright-browed lady, practise his qualities.","n":"கோது குலாவிய recurs in lines 1 and 4 and is obscure: கோது can mean dross, fault or a flower's sheath, so the last line may say the gods, for all their worship, still cling to faults. யாது குலாவி (delighting in what?) may be a question.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:1"},"t:17":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":17,"t":["காயம் இரண்டுங் கலந்து கொதிக்கினும்","மாயங் கத்தூரி யதுமிகும் அவ்வழி","தேசங் கலந்தொரு தேவனென் றெண்ணினும்","ஈசன் உறவுக் கெதிரில்லை தானே."],"p":"","r":"Even if asafoetida and the like are boiled together with it, musk's scent prevails; in the same way, even if one mixes with the gods of many lands and thinks of one as god, nothing equals kinship with the Lord.","n":"காயம் is read as asafoetida, set against கத்தூரி (musk); it can also mean the body. இரண்டும் and மாயம் are hard to fit, and the simile's point (the stronger scent wins) is a reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:1"},"t:18":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":18,"t":["அதிபதி செய்து அளகை வேந்தனை","நிதிபதி செய்த நிறைதவம் நோக்கி","அதுபதி ஆதரித்து ஆக்கமது ஆக்கின்","இதுபதி கொள்என்ற எம்பெரு மானே."],"p":"","r":"Seeing the full penance of the king of Alaka, our Lord made him overlord and lord of wealth, and said: if you cherish that lordship and make it prosper, take this lordship.","n":"The king of Alaka is Kubera, made lord of wealth by Siva for his penance. The speech in the last two lines is compressed and its sense uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","selvam","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:1"},"t:19":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":19,"t":["இதுபதி ஏலங் கமழ்பொழில் ஏழும்","முதுபதி செய்தவன் மூதறி வாளன்","விதுபதி செய்தவன் மெய்த்தவம் நோக்கி","அதுபதி யாக அமருகின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"The one of ancient wisdom, who made the seven groves fragrant with cardamom an ancient seat, seeing the true penance of the one he made lord, dwells there as that seat.","n":"The verse continues 18 (இதுபதி) and plays on பதி as lord and as place. விதுபதி may be the moon's lord or a misreading; who did the penance and which seat is meant are unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:1"},"t:20":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":20,"t":["முடிவும் பிறப்பையும் முன்னே படைத்த","அடிகள் உறையும் அறனெறி நாடில்","இடியும் முழக்கமும் ஈசர் உருவம்","கடிமலர்க் குன்ற மலையது தானே."],"p":"","r":"If you seek the path of virtue where the Lord dwells who first created death and birth, thunder and its roar are the Lord's form, and it is the mountain of fragrant flowers.","n":"The second half is obscure. கடிமலர்க் குன்ற மலை may be Kailasa or a particular hill; the thunder as his form may echo the rain imagery of 10 and 11.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aram","sivam","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:1"},"t:21":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":21,"t":["வானப் பெருங்கொண்டல் மாலயன் வானவர்","ஊனப் பிறவி ஒழிக்கும் ஒருவனைக்","கானக் களிறு கதறப் பிளந்தனம்","கோனைப் புகழுமின் கூடலு மாமே."],"p":"","r":"Praise the king who tore the forest elephant so that it screamed, the One who ends the flawed birth of Vishnu dark as a great cloud, of Brahma and of the gods, and you may join him.","n":"The elephant is the demon Siva flayed (Gajasura). ஊனப் பிறவி (flawed, or fleshly, birth) puts even the high gods inside the round of births from which only Siva frees.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","piravi","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:1"},"t:22":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":22,"t":["மனத்தில் எழுகின்ற மாயநன் நாடன்","நினைத்தது அறிவன் என்னில்தான் நினைக்கிலர்","எனக்குஇறை அன்பிலன் என்பர் இறைவன்","பிழைக்கநின் றார்பக்கம் பேணிநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"The good lord of the land of maya, who rises within the mind, knows what one thinks; yet people do not think of him and say 'the Lord has no love for me', while the Lord stands caring for those who would be saved.","n":"பிழைக்க நின்றார் can be 'those who stand to be saved' or 'those who stand in error'; on the second reading, his care reaches even the wrongdoers. The complaint that God has no love is answered: it is people who do not think of him.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","manam","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:1"},"t:23":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":23,"t":["வல்லவன் வன்னிககு இறையிடை வாரணம்","நில்லென நிற்பித்த நீதியுள் ஈசனை","இல்லென வேண்டா இறையவர் தம்முதல்","அல்லும் பகலும் அருளுகின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"Do not say there is no Lord: the mighty one, just Lord who made the elephant stop at his word, first of the gods, gives grace by night and day.","n":"The first line (வன்னிக்கு இறையிடை வாரணம்) refers to a legend I can't identify; வன்னி may be fire or the vanni tree. இல்லென வேண்டா, 'don't say he is not', is plain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:1"},"t:24":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":24,"t":["போற்றிசைத் தும்புகழ்ந் தும்புனி தன்அடி","தேற்றுமின் என்றும் சிவனடிக் கேசெல்வம்","ஆற்றிய தென்று மயலுற்ற சிந்தையை","மாற்றிநின் றார்வழி மன்னிநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"Praise and laud the holy one's feet and grow clear; the wealth is always Siva's feet; he stays firm in the way of those who have turned back the mind that was deluded.","n":"The middle is compressed: 'wealth is Siva's feet' revalues செல்வம். ஆற்றியதென்று மயலுற்ற சிந்தை may be the mind deluded by thinking it has achieved something.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","selvam","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:1"},"t:25":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":25,"t":["பிறப்பிலி பிஞ்ஞகன் பேரரு ளாளன்","இறப்பிலி யாவர்க்கும் இன்பம் அருளும்","துறப்பிலி தன்னைத் தொழுமின் தொழுதால்","மறப்பிலி மாயா விருத்தமும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"Worship the unborn, deathless, never-departing one, the bearer of the bow, great in grace, who gives joy to all; if you worship, the never-forgetting one becomes for you an undying greatness.","n":"A string of -இலி names (பிறப்பிலி, இறப்பிலி, துறப்பிலி, மறப்பிலி: without birth, death, leaving, forgetting). மாயா விருத்தம் is unclear: 'unperishing greatness' or 'the state free of maya'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","vazhipaadu","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:1"},"t:26":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":26,"t":["தொடர்ந்துனின் றானைத் தொழுமின் தொழுதால்","படர்ந்துநின் றான்பரி பாரக முற்றும்","கடந்துநின் றாம்கம லம்மலர் மேலே","உடந்திருந் தான்அடிப் புண்ணிய மாமே."],"p":"","r":"Worship the one who follows you; if you worship, he spreads through all the earth, stands beyond, above the lotus flower, and dwelling with him is the merit of his feet.","n":"தொடர்ந்துநின்றான் (who follows, or stays with, the soul) is a mark of grace. கடந்துநின்றாம் (we stood beyond) is probably கடந்துநின்றான் (he stood beyond), as in 14; the lotus may be Brahma's or the heart's.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:1"},"t:27":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":27,"t":["சந்தி எனத்தக்க தாமரை வாண்முகத்து","அந்தமில் ஈசன் அருள்நமக் கேயென்று","நந்தியை நாளும் வணங்கப் படும்அவர்","புந்தியி னுள்ளே புகுந்துநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"Those who bow daily to Nandi, saying 'the grace of the endless Lord with the bright lotus face, fit to be called the meeting-point, is for us', he enters and stays within their minds.","n":"சந்தி (twilight, or a junction) is unclear; it may be the dawn-and-dusk hour of worship. The verse's claim is plain: he enters the minds of those who bow to him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","thiruvarul","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:1"},"t:28":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":28,"t":["இணங்கிநின் றான் எங்கும் ஆகிநின் றானும்","பிணங்கிநின் றான்பின்முன் னாகிநின் றானும்","உணங்கிநின் றான்அம ராபதி நாதன்","வணங்கிநின் றார்க்கே வழித்துணை யாமே."],"p":"","r":"He stands in accord and has become everything; he stands apart and has become before and behind; the lord of Amaravati stands withered; to those who stand bowing he is a companion on the way.","n":"இணங்கி / பிணங்கி (agreeing, opposing) give the Lord both relations to the world. The lord of Amaravati is Indra; உணங்கி நின்றான் may say Indra stands humbled before Siva, or it may describe Siva, which is harder to make sense of.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","vazhipaadu","thunai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:1"},"t:29":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":29,"t":["காணநில் லாய்அடி யேற்குஉறவுஆருளர்","நாணநில் லேன்உன்னை நான்தழு விக்கொளக்","கோணநில் லாத குணத்தடி யார்மனத்து","ஆணியன் ஆகி அமர்ந்துநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"Stand where I can see you: who else is kin to me, your servant? I will not hold back in shame from embracing you. He has settled as the linchpin in the minds of devotees whose nature never bends.","n":"The first two lines address the Lord directly, the last two speak of him. ஆணி (nail, linchpin, or the finest gold) makes him what holds the devotee's mind in place.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","sivam","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:1"},"t:30":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":30,"t":["வான்நின்று அழைக்கும் மழைபோல் இறைவனும்","தானினறு அழைக்கும்கொல் என்று தயங்குவார்","ஆன்நின்று அழைக்கு மதுபோல்என் நந்தியை","நான்நின்று அழைப்பது ஞானம் கருதியே."],"p":"","r":"Some hesitate, wondering whether the Lord, like rain calling from the sky, will call them from where he stands; as the cow stands and calls, I stand and call my Nandi, seeking knowledge.","n":"The two images are set against each other: waiting to be called down from above, and calling out oneself as a cow lows for its calf (or a calf for its mother; ஆன் can be either). The poet calls ஞானம் கருதி, wanting knowledge, not reward.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","anbu","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:1"},"t:31":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":31,"t":["மண்ணகத் தான்ஒக்கும் வானகத் தான்ஒக்கும்","விண்ணகத் தான்ஒக்கும் வேதகத் தான்ஒக்கும்","பண்ணகத்து இன்னிசை பாடலுற் றானுக்கே","கண்ணகத் தேநின்று காதலித் தேனே."],"p":"","r":"He is the same on earth, in the sky, in the heavens and in the Veda; for him who took to singing sweet music in melody, I stood in my eye and loved.","n":"கண்ணகத்தே நின்று can be read as the Lord standing within the eye or the poet loving with his whole sight. Sengalvaraya notes the same phrase in Tiruvasagam (Tiruppalliyezhuchi 9).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:1"},"t:32":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":32,"t":["தேவர் பிரான்நம் பிரான்திசை பத்தையும்","மேவு பிரான்விரி நீருலகேழையும்","தாவு பிரான்தன்மை தானறி வாரில்லை","பாவு பிரான்அருட் பாடலு மாமே."],"p":"","r":"The lord of the gods, our lord, who fills the ten directions and strides across the seven worlds of spreading water: no one knows his nature, and to praise the all-pervading lord becomes a song of grace.","n":"பிரான் closes each half-line. The last line may mean that singing of him is itself grace, or that he is reached through songs of grace.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:1"},"t:33":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":33,"t":["பதிபல வாயது பண்டுஇவ் வுலகம்","விதிபல செய்தொன்றும் மெய்ம்மை உணரார்","துதிபல தோத்திரம் சொல்ல வல்லாரும்","மதியிலர் நெஞ்சினுள் வாடுகின் றாரே."],"p":"","r":"Of old this world had many lords; people perform many rites and do not see the one truth; even those who can recite many praises and hymns lack understanding and wither within.","n":"A check on the chapter's own call to praise: hymns and rites without understanding (மதி) leave the heart withered. பதிபல may be many gods or many cities and shrines; விதி பல may be rules or rites.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["meyyunarvu","vazhipaadu","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:1"},"t:34":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":34,"t":["சாந்து கமழுங் கவரியின் கந்தம்போல்","வேந்தன் அமரர்க்கு அருளிய மெய்ந்நெறி","ஆர்ந்த சுடரன்ன ஆயிர நாமமும்","போந்தும் இருந்தும் புகழுகின் றேனே."],"p":"","r":"Like the scent the sandal-fragrant yak-tail fan carries, the true way the king gave the gods and his thousand names, like a full flame, I praise whether going or sitting.","n":"The simile is hard to fix: the scent may be the true way, which spreads like the fan's fragrance, or the names. போந்தும் இருந்தும் (going and staying) means at all times.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:1"},"t:35":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":35,"t":["ஆற்றுகி லாவழி யாகும் இறைவனைப்","போற்றுமின் போற்றிப் புகழ்மின் புகழ்ந்திடில்","மேற்றிசைக் கும்கிழக் குத்திசை எட்டொடு","மாற்றுவன் அப்படி ஆட்டவு மாமே."],"p":"","r":"Praise the Lord who is the way when no way avails; praise and laud him, and if you laud him he will change you, through the eight directions with west and east, and set you dancing so.","n":"ஆற்றுகிலா வழி (the way for those unable to cope, or the way one cannot achieve oneself) and the last two lines are obscure. மாற்றுவன் may be 'transform you' or 'turn you about'; ஆட்டவுமாம் may speak of the soul made to dance as Siva dances.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:1"},"t:36":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":36,"t":["அப்பனை நந்தியை ஆரா அமுதினை","ஒப்பிலி வள்ளலை ஊழி முதல்வனை","எப்பரி சாயினும் ஏத்துமின் ஏத்தினால்","அப்பரி சீசன் அருள்பெற லாமே."],"p":"","r":"Praise in whatever way you can the father, Nandi, the nectar one never tires of, the peerless giver, the first of the aeon; if you praise him, you can win the Lord's grace in that same measure.","n":"எப்பரிசாயினும் ... அப்பரிசு: grace answers praise in kind and measure. The verse lowers the bar for worship: any form will do.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:1"},"t:37":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":37,"t":["நானும்நின் றேத்துவன் நாள்தொறும் நந்தியைத்","தானும்நின் றான்தழல் தான்ஒக்கும் மேனியன்","வானில்நின் றார்மதி போல்உடல் உள்ளுவந்து","ஊனில்நின் றாங்கே உயிர்க்கின்ற வாறே."],"p":"","r":"I too stand and praise Nandi every day; he too stands, his body like flame; like the moon in the sky he delights within the body and stands in the flesh, and so it breathes with life.","n":"உயிர்க்கின்றவாறே is read as 'that is how it lives'. The verse puts the Lord in the flesh as the source of its life, which the later tantras develop as the body as temple. The moon image may be yogic.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","yaakkai","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:1"},"t:38":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":38,"t":["பிதற்றொழி யேன்பெரி யான்அரி யானைப்","பிதற்றொழி யேன்பிற வாஉரு வானைப்","பிதற்றொழி யேன்எங்கள் பேர்நந்தி தன்னைப்","பிதற்றொழி யேன்பெரு மைத்தவன் தானே."],"p":"","r":"I will not stop babbling of the great and rare one, of the one whose form is unborn, of our great Nandi, of the one of greatness.","n":"பிதற்று is raving or babbling, the devotee's speech that does not care how it sounds; repeated at the head of each line, it enacts what it says.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","anbu","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:1"},"t:39":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":39,"t":["வாழ்த்தவல் லார்மனத் துள்ளுறு சோதியைத்","தீர்த்தனை அங்கே திளைக்கின்ற தேவனை","ஏத்தியும் எம்பெரு மான்என்றுஇறைஞ்சியும்","ஆத்தம் செய் தீசன் அருள்பெற லாமே."],"p":"","r":"By praising the light within the minds of those able to praise, the holy one, the god who delights there, by bowing to him as our Lord and holding him dear, one can win the Lord's grace.","n":"The light is already in the minds of those who praise; worship here is turning toward what is within. ஆத்தம் is taken as love or trust.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","thiruvarul","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:1"},"t:40":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":40,"t":["குறைந்துஅடைந் தீசன் குரைகழல் நாடும்","நிறைந்துஅடை செம்பொனின் நேர்ஒளி ஒக்கும்","மறைஞ்சடம் செய்யாது வாழ்த்தவல் லார்க்குப்","புறஞ்சடம் செய்வான் புகுந்துநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"The Lord's anklet-ringing feet, which the humbled come to seek, are like the light of pure red gold; for those who can praise without hidden guile, he enters and stays and works on the outer frame.","n":"மறைஞ்சடம் is taken as concealed deceit and புறஞ்சடம் as the outer body, so the last line may mean he transforms or removes the outer body; the second half is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","vanjam","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:1"},"t:41":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":41,"t":["சினஞ்செய்த நஞ்சுண்ட தேவர் பிரானைப்","புனஞ்செய்த நெஞ்சிடை போற்றவல் லார்க்குக்","கனஞ்செய்த வாள்நுதல் பாகனும் அங்கே","இனஞ்செய்த மான்போல் இணங்கிநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"For those who can praise in a heart tilled like a field the lord of the gods who swallowed the raging poison, the one who shares half his body with the bright-browed lady stays there in accord, like a deer among its herd.","n":"புனஞ்செய்த நெஞ்சு, the heart made into a cleared field, is a farming image for a mind prepared to receive him; the deer at ease with its kind says he dwells there naturally.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","manam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:1"},"t:42":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":42,"t":["போய்அரன் தன்னைப் புகழ்வார் பெறுவது","நாயக னான்முடி செய்தது வேநல்கும்","மாயகம் சூழ்ந்து வரவல்ல ராகிலும்","வேயன தோளிக்கு வேந்தொன்றும் தானே."],"p":"","r":"What those who go and praise Hara gain is what the Lord made the crown; even if they come wrapped in maya, the king of the bamboo-shouldered lady becomes one with them.","n":"நாயகனான் முடி செய்ததுவே நல்கும் is obscure: perhaps the highest state, or the crown he gave others. The point that maya is no bar to praise is fairly clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","maayai","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:1"},"t:43":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":43,"t":["அரனடி சொல்லி அரற்றி அழுது","பரனடி நாடியே பாவிப்ப நாளும்","உரன்அடி செய்துஅங்கு ஓதுங்கவல் லார்க்கு","நிரன்அடி செய்து நிறைந்துநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"For those who, speaking of Hara's feet, crying and weeping, seek and meditate daily on the feet of the Supreme and can settle there, he fills them, giving his feet in order.","n":"அரற்றி அழுது (crying out and weeping) is the devotional weeping of the Tamil saints. உரன் அடி செய்து and நிரன் அடி செய்து are obscure; the play on அடி runs through all four lines.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:1"},"t:44":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":44,"t":["போற்றிஎன் பார்அம ரர்புனி தன்அடி","போற்றிஎன் பார்அசு ரர்புனி தன்அடி","போற்றிஎன் பார்மனி தர்புனி தன்அடி","போற்றிஎன் அன்புள் பொலியவைத் தேனே."],"p":"","r":"The gods say 'praise' to the pure one's feet, the asuras say it, humans say it; I have set 'praise' to shine within my love.","n":"Gods, demons and humans alike praise him; what sets the poet apart is not the word but where it is placed, inside love.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:1"},"t:45":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":45,"t":["விதிவழி அல்லதுஇவ் வேலை உலகம்","விதிவழி இன்பம் விருத்தமும் இல்லை","துதிவழி நித்தலும் சோதிப் பிரானும்","பதிவழி காட்டும் பகலவ னாமே."],"p":"","r":"This sea-girt world runs only by ordinance, and outside the ordinance there is neither joy nor its opposite; by the way of daily praise, the Lord of light becomes the sun that shows the way to the Lord.","n":"விதி may be fate, divine ordinance or prescribed rule, and விருத்தம் the contrary of joy or something else. The verse sets the way of ordinance (விதிவழி) against the way of praise (துதிவழி) and the way to the Lord (பதிவழி).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oozh","vazhipaadu","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:1"},"t:46":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":46,"t":["அந்திவண் ணாஅர னேசிவ னேஎன்று","சிந்தைசெய் வண்ணம் திருந்தடி யார்தொழ","முந்திவண் ணாமுதல் வாபர னேஎன்று","புந்தி வண்ணன்எம் மனம்புகுந் தானே."],"p":"","r":"While devotees made worship, thinking 'you of twilight colour, Hara, Siva' and 'you of the first colour, First One, Supreme', the one whose colour is the intellect entered our mind.","n":"The play on வண்ணம் (colour, form, manner) runs through the verse: the red of evening, the first, and finally புந்தி வண்ணன், one whose form is understanding.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","sivam","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:1"},"t:47":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":47,"t":["மனையுள் இருந்தவர் மாதவர் ஒப்பர்","நினைவுள் இருந்தவர் நேசத்துள் நிற்பர்","பனையுள் இருந்த பருந்தது போல","நினையாத வர்க்கில்லை நின்இன்பந் தானே."],"p":"","r":"Those who stay in the household are equal to great ascetics; those who keep him in thought stand in his love; but like the kite perched in the palmyra, those who do not think of you have no joy of you.","n":"The first line read alone equates householders with ascetics; read with the second, it may mean householders who keep the Lord in mind. The kite in the palmyra is traditionally taken as sitting by the fruit and not tasting it, or as perched without settling.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","thavam","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:1"},"t:48":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":48,"t":["அடியார் பரவும் அமரர் பிரானை","முடியால் வணங்கி முதல்வனை முன்னிப்","படியால் அருளும் பரம்பரன் எந்தை","விடியா விளக்கென்று மேவிநின் றேனே."],"p":"","r":"Bowing my head to the lord of the immortals whom devotees extol, thinking of the First One, our father, the Supreme beyond the supreme who gives grace in measure, I stay with him as a lamp that needs no dawn.","n":"விடியா விளக்கு is a lamp that does not go out at dawn, or one that needs no daybreak; it may describe the Lord or the poet's steady devotion. படியால் may be 'on earth' or 'in measure'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:1"},"t:49":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":49,"t":["நரைபசு பாசத்து நாதனை உள்ளி","உரைபசு பாசததுஒருங்கவல் லார்க்குத்","திரைபசு பாவச் செழுங்கடல் நீந்திக்","கரைபசு பாசம் கடநது எய்த லாமே."],"p":"","r":"Those who think of the Lord of pasu and pasam and can gather themselves on the teaching of pasu and pasam will swim the rich sea of sin and reach the shore, passing beyond pasu and pasam.","n":"பசு பாசம் closes the first half of every line, with நரை, உரை, திரை, கரை before it. Traditionally pasu is the soul and pasam its bonds, under pati the Lord, the three categories of Saiva Siddhanta. நரை (grey, ancient) and திரை (wave) are hard to fit.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pasu","paasam","sivam","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:1"},"t:50":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":50,"t":["சூடுவன் நெஞ்சிடை வைப்பன் பிரான்என்று","பாடுவன் பன்மலர் தூவிப் பணிந்துநின்","றாடுவன் ஆடி அமரர்ப்பி ரான்என்று","நாடுவன் நான்இன் றறிவது தானே."],"p":"","r":"Calling him Lord I will wear him and keep him in my heart; I will sing, scatter many flowers, bow and dance, and dancing seek him as the lord of the immortals: this is what I know today.","n":"The verse lists the acts of worship (wearing, holding, singing, scattering flowers, bowing, dancing, seeking) and ends with the modest claim that this is all he knows today.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:1"},"t:51":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":51,"t":["வேதத்தை விட்ட அறமில்லை வேதத்தின்","ஓதத் தகும்அறம் எல்லாம் உளதர்க்க","வாதத்தை விட்டு மதிஞர் வளமுற்ற","வேதத்தை ஓதியே வீடுபெற் றார்க்களே."],"p":"","r":"There is no dharma that leaves out the Veda; all the dharma worth reciting is in it, and the wise, giving up disputation and argument, recited the rich Veda and gained release.","n":"The verse sets recitation against தர்க்க வாதம் (logic and debate): release comes from reciting the Veda, not from arguing about it. 'All dharma is in the Veda' is the verse's own claim, stated without qualification.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vedham","aram","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:2"},"t:52":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":52,"t":["வேதம் உரைத்தானும் வேதியன் ஆகிலன்","வேதம் உரைத்தானும் வேதா விளங்கிட","வேதம் உரைத்தானும் வேதியர் வேள்விக்காய்","வேதம் உரைத்தானும் மெய்ப்பொருள் காட்டவே."],"p":"","r":"The one who spoke the Veda is not himself a Vedic priest; he spoke it so that Brahma might shine, for the sacrifices of the Vedic people, and to show the true reality.","n":"The four lines all open வேதம் உரைத்தானும். வேதா is taken as Brahma, and the speaker as the Lord, who gives the Veda without belonging to the priestly order. Line 1 could also mean that merely uttering the Veda does not make one a வேதியன்; the verse allows both.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vedham","iraivan","aaguthi","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:2"},"t:53":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":53,"t":["இருக்குஉரு வாம்எழில் வேதத்தின் உள்ளே","உருக்குஉணர் வாயுணர் வேதத்துள் ஓங்கி","வெருக்குஉரு வாகிய வேதியர் சொல்லும்","கருக்குஉரு வாய்நின்ற கண்ணனும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"Within the beautiful Veda whose form is the Rig, rising within the Veda as the feeling that melts and is felt, he is also Kannan, standing as the inner form that the awe-inspiring Vedic seers speak of.","n":"The rhyme words இருக்கு, உருக்கு, வெருக்கு, கருக்கு drive the verse and blur its syntax. இருக்கு is the Rig Veda; கண்ணன் may be Vishnu or a name of the Lord as the eye (கண்) of the Veda. The point seems to be that the Lord is the inner content of the Veda.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vedham","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:2"},"t:54":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":54,"t":["திருநெறி யாவது சித்தசித் தன்றிப்","பெருநெறி யாய பிரானை நினைந்து","குருநெறி யாம்சிவ மாம்நெறி கூடும்","ஒருநெறி ஒன்றாக வேதாந்தம் ஓதுமே."],"p":"","r":"The holy path is to think of the Lord, the great path beyond the sentient and the insentient, and so join the guru's path that is Siva's path; Vedanta teaches this one path as one.","n":"சித்து அசித்து (the sentient and the insentient) is a standard pair of Siddhanta. The verse makes Vedanta the teacher of Tirumular's own path, guru and Siva together, rather than a rival school.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vedaantham","guru","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:2"},"t:55":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":55,"t":["ஆறங்க மாய்வரும் மாமறை ஓதியைக்","கூறங்க மாகக் குணம்பயில் வாரில்லை","வேறங்க மாக விளைவுசெய்து அப்புறம்","பேறங்க மாகப் பெருக்குகின் றாரே."],"p":"","r":"No one practises the qualities of the great Veda that comes with its six limbs as part of themselves; instead they make it yield other results and then enlarge it into a means of gain.","n":"ஆறங்கம் are the six auxiliary sciences of the Veda. The verse plays on அங்கம் (limb) four times: கூறங்கமாக (as a part of oneself), வேறங்கமாக (as something else), பேறங்கமாக (as gain). The reading of the last two lines, that they use the Veda for profit, is the plainest, not the only one.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vedham","ozhukkam","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:2"},"t:56":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":56,"t":["பாட்டும் ஒலியும் பரக்கும் கணிகையர்","ஆட்டும் அறாத அவனியில் மாட்டாதார்","வேட்டு விருப்பார் விரதமில் லாதவர்","ஈட்டும் இடஞ்சென்று இகலல்உற் றாரே."],"p":"","r":"In a land never free of song, music and the dancing of courtesans, those who cannot hold back, who perform sacrifices and desire, keeping no vows, go where wealth is heaped and fall to quarrelling.","n":"மாட்டாதார் (those unable) has no stated object, and வேட்டு may mean 'having sacrificed' or 'desiring'. In this chapter it is likely aimed at Vedic men who perform rites without the discipline of vows (விரதம்) and chase wealth, but the verse does not name them.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["igal","varaivinmagalir","selvam","thavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:2"},"t:57":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":57,"t":["அஞ்சன மேனி அரிவையோர் பாகத்தன்","அஞ்சொ டிருபத்து மூன்றுள ஆகமம்","அஞ்சலி கூப்பி அறுபத்து அறுவரும்","அஞ்சா முகத்தில் அரும்பொருள் கேட்டதே."],"p":"","r":"The twenty-eight Agamas are the rare meaning that the sixty-six, with joined palms, heard from the fearless face of the one who has the dark-bodied woman as half of himself.","n":"அஞ்சொடு இருபத்துமூன்று (five with twenty-three) gives the traditional count of 28 Saiva Agamas. அஞ்சா முகம் may be 'fearless face' or, punning, அஞ்சு ஆம் முகம், the five faces from which tradition says the Agamas came. Who the sixty-six hearers are the verse does not say.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aagamam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:3"},"t:58":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":58,"t":["அண்ணல் அருளால் அருளும் சிவாகமம்","எண்ணில் இருபத்தெண் கோடிநூ றாயிரம்","விண்ணவர் ஈசன் விழுப்பம் உரைத்தனர்","எண்ணிநின் றப்பொருள் ஏத்துவன் நானே."],"p":"","r":"The Siva Agamas the Lord gives by his grace number twenty-eight crore hundred-thousand; they told the greatness of the Lord of the gods, and I, keeping that meaning in mind, praise it.","n":"The number is a figure of vastness, not a count. The verse turns at the end to the poet's own task: what he praises is the meaning (அப்பொருள்), not the bulk.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aagamam","thiruvarul","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:3"},"t:59":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":59,"t":["பண்டிதர் ஆவார் பதினெட்டும் பாடையும்","கண்டவர் கூறும் கருத்தறி வார்என்க","பண்டிதர் தங்கள் பதினெட்டுப் பாடையும்","அண்ட முதலான் அறஞ்சொன்ன வாறே."],"p":"","r":"Say that the learned are those who know the intent of what the seers of the eighteen languages say; the eighteen languages of the learned are the way the First of the cosmos told dharma.","n":"பதினெட்டுப் பாடை is usually taken as the eighteen languages of the land. On that reading the verse makes every language a channel of the Lord's dharma and the learned those who grasp intent rather than words. Whether பதினெட்டும் pairs with பாடையும் or names eighteen other things is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","aram","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:3"},"t:60":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":60,"t":["அண்ணல் அருளால் அருளுந்திவ் யாகமம்","விண்ணில் அமரர் தமக்கும் விளங்கரி","தெண்ணில் எழுபது கோடிநூ றாயிரம்","எண்ணிலும் நீர்மேல் எழுத்தது ஆகுமே."],"p":"","r":"The divine Agamas the Lord gives by his grace are hard to understand even for the immortals in heaven; counted, they come to seventy crore hundred-thousand, and even that count is writing on water.","n":"The last line can mean that the number itself is meaningless, or, as 64 states outright, that the Agamas are writing on water for anyone who does not understand them. The number differs from 58 (twenty-eight crore), which suggests both are figures of vastness.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aagamam","thiruvarul","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:3"},"t:61":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":61,"t":["பரனாய் பராபரம் காட்டி உலகில்","தரனாய்ச் சிவதன்மந் தானேசொல் காலத்","தரனாய் அமரர்கள் அர்ச்சிக்கு நந்தி","உரனாகி ஆகமம் ஓங்கிநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"When the Supreme, showing the supreme-beyond, as support of the world, himself spoke the Siva dharma, Nandi, worshipped by the immortals as Hara, became its strength and stood high as the Agama.","n":"பரம் and பராபரம் are technical terms for the Supreme and what lies beyond it. தரனாய், அரனாய், உரனாகி rhyme and are hard to separate: Nandi may be Siva himself here, or the first receiver who becomes the Agama's support.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aagamam","guru","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:3"},"t:62":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":62,"t":["சிவமாம் பரத்தினில் சத்தி சதாசிவம்","உவமா மகேசர் உருத்திர தேவர்","தவமால் பிரமீசர் தம்மில்தாம் பெற்ற","நவஆ கமமெங்கள் நந்திபெற் றானே."],"p":"","r":"The nine Agamas that, from Siva as the Supreme, Shakti, Sadasiva, Mahesa, the Rudra gods, Vishnu, Brahma and the Lord received each from the other, our Nandi received.","n":"The verse gives a chain of transmission ending in Nandi, Tirumular's own teacher. உவமா and தவமால் பிரமீசர் may divide in other ways, so the exact list of links is not certain. The nine Agamas are named in 63.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aagamam","guru","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:3"},"t:63":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":63,"t":["பெற்றநல் ஆகமங் காரணம் காமிகம்","உற்றநல் வீரம் உயர்சிந்தியம் வாதுளம்","மற்றவ் வியாமளம் ஆகும்கா லோத்தரந்","துற்றநற் சுப்பிரம் சொல்லு மகுடமே."],"p":"","r":"The good Agamas he received are Karanam, Kamikam, the noble Viram, the lofty Sinthiyam, Vathulam, Yamalam, Kalottaram, the good Suppiram and Makutam.","n":"These are names from the traditional list of 28 Agamas (Karana, Kamika, Vira, Cintya, Vatula, Yamala, Kalottara, Suprabheda and Makuta). சுப்பிரம் is taken as Suprabheda.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aagamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:3"},"t:64":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":64,"t":["அண்ணல் அருளால் அருளும் சிவாகமம்","எண்ணிலி கோடி தொகுத்திடும் ஆயினும்","அண்ணல் அறைந்த அறிவுஅறி யாவிடின்","எண்ணிலி கோடியும் நீர்மேல் எழுத்தே."],"p":"","r":"Though the Siva Agamas the Lord gives by his grace are gathered in countless crores, if one does not know the knowledge the Lord declared, all those crores are writing on water.","n":"The verse repeats 58's first line and turns it against bulk: the measure of the Agamas is the knowledge (அறிவு) they carry, not their number. அண்ணல் அறைந்த அறிவு may be the Lord's teaching or the knowledge of the Lord.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aagamam","gnaanam","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:3"},"t:65":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":65,"t":["மாரியும் கோடையும் வார்பனி தூங்கநின்று","ஏரியும் நின்றங்கு இளைக்கின்ற காலத்து","ஆரிய முந்தமி ழும்உட னேசொலிக்","காரிகை யார்க்குக் கருணைசெய் தானே."],"p":"","r":"Standing while rain and summer and long dew pass, while even the lake wanes, he spoke Sanskrit and Tamil together and showed grace to the lady.","n":"காரிகையார் (the lady) is traditionally the Goddess, to whom Siva is said to have taught the Agamas. The first two lines may describe his standing through the seasons, or her long penance before he taught her. ஆரியம் here means Sanskrit, and the verse names it and Tamil as given together.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","thamizh","aagamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:3"},"t:66":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":66,"t":["அவிழ்கின்ற வாறும் அதுகட்டு மாறும்","சிமிட்டலைப் பட்டுயிர் போகின்ற வாறும்","தமிழ்ச்சொல் வடசொல் எனும்இவ் விரண்டும்","உணர்த்தும் அவனை உணரலு மாமே."],"p":"","r":"How the soul is loosed, how it is bound, and how life departs in the blink of an eye: the Tamil word and the northern word, these two, teach him, and through them one can come to know him.","n":"அவிழ்கின்ற, கட்டும் (loosing, binding) are read as release and bondage; the verse does not name the soul. The plain claim is that Tamil and Sanskrit equally lead to knowing the Lord.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thamizh","malam","veedu","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:3"},"t:67":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":67,"t":["நந்தி அருள்பெற்ற நாதரை நாடிடின்","நந்திகள் நால்வர் சிவயோக மாமுனி","மன்று தொழுத பதஞ்சலி வியாக்ரமர்","என்றிவர் என்னோ டெண்மரு மாமே."],"p":"","r":"If you seek the masters who received Nandi's grace: the four Nandis, the great sage Sivayoga, Patanjali and Vyaghra who worshipped at the hall, these with me make eight.","n":"மன்று is the hall where Siva dances, by tradition Chidambaram, where Patanjali and Vyaghrapada are said to have seen the dance. The count is four plus three plus Tirumular himself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["guru","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:4"},"t:68":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":68,"t":["நந்தி அருளாலே நாதனாம் பேர்பெற்றோம்","நந்தி அருளாலே மூலனை நாடினோம்","நந்தி அருளாவ தென்செயும் நாட்டினில்","நந்தி வழிகாட்ட நானிருந் தேனே."],"p":"","r":"By Nandi's grace we gained the name of master; by Nandi's grace we sought out Mulan; what can Nandi's grace not do? With Nandi showing the way, I stayed in this land.","n":"மூலனை நாடினோம் is traditionally taken of the body of Mulan the cowherd, which the sage entered and lived in; it may also mean seeking the root (மூலம்). The verse itself does not tell the story.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:4"},"t:69":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":69,"t":["மந்திரம் பெற்ற வழிமுறை மாலாங்கன்","இந்திரன் சோமன் பிரமன் உருத்திரன்","கந்துருக் காலாங்கி கஞ்ச மலையனோடு","இந்த எழுவரும் என்வழி யாமே."],"p":"","r":"Those who received the mantra in due succession, Malangan, Indran, Soman, Brahman, Rudran, Kalangi with Kanduru before his name, and Kanjamalaiyan, these seven are of my line.","n":"Malangan and Indran are addressed later in the preface (75, 77). To make seven, கந்துரு is taken as a qualifier of காலாங்கி rather than a separate name; it could be read as an eighth name, and its sense is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","mandhiram"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:4"},"t:70":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":70,"t":["நால்வரும் நாலு திசைக்கொன்று நாதர்கள்","நால்வரும் நானா விதப்பொருள் கைக்கொண்டு","நால்வரும் யான்பெற்ற தெல்லாம் பெறுகென","நால்வரும் தேவராய் நாதர் ஆனார்களே."],"p":"","r":"The four masters, one for each of the four directions, the four who took up many kinds of meaning, the four told 'may you gain all that I gained', the four became gods and became masters.","n":"The four are probably the four Nandis of 67. Who says 'may you gain all that I gained' is not stated: Nandi, the Lord, or Tirumular. The phrase recalls 85, where Tirumular wishes his bliss on the whole world.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:4"},"t:71":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":71,"t":["மொழிந்தது மூவர்க்கும் நால்வர்க்கும் ஈசன்","ஒழிந்த பெருமை இறப்பும் பிறப்பும்","செழுஞ்சுடர் மூன்றொளி யாகிய தேவன்","கழிந்த பெருமையைக் காட்டகி லானே."],"p":"","r":"What the Lord told the three and the four is a greatness beyond death and birth; the god who is the light of the three bright flames did not show that surpassing greatness.","n":"The three and the four are not named. The three flames are traditionally sun, moon and fire. காட்டகிலானே may mean 'could not show' or 'did not show', and so the second half may mean the greatness lies beyond showing. The verse is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","piravi","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:4"},"t:72":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":72,"t":["எழுந்துநீர் பெய்யினும் எட்டுத் திசையுஞ்","செழுந்தண் நியமங்கள் செய்யுமின் என்றண்ணல்","கொழுந்தண் பவளக் குளிர்சடை யோடே","அழுந்திய நால்வர்க்கு அருள்புரிந் தானே."],"p":"","r":"'Even if the flood rises and pours, keep the cool rich observances in all eight directions': so saying, the Lord with cool matted hair like tender coral gave grace to the four who were sunk deep in him.","n":"நியமங்கள் are observances, one of the limbs of yoga in Tirumular's later tantras. எழுந்துநீர் பெய்யினும் may mean 'even if a flood comes' or 'rising, pour water' (an ablution). அழுந்திய (steeped, sunk in) describes the four's devotion.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","ozhukkam","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:4"},"t:73":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":73,"t":["நந்தி திருவடி நான்தலை மேற்கொண்டு","புந்தியின் உள்ளே புகப்பெய்து போற்றிசெய்","தந்தி மதிபுனை அரனடி நாள்தொறும்","சிந்தைசெய் தாகமம் செப்பலுற் றேனே."],"p":"","r":"Placing Nandi's holy feet on my head, setting them within my mind and praising, thinking daily on the feet of Hara who wears the evening moon, I set out to tell the Agama.","n":"போற்றிசெய் தந்தி is read போற்றிசெய்து அந்தி: the moon of evening. The verse sets the guru's feet before the Lord's, as the preface does throughout.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["guru","aagamam","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:5"},"t:74":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":74,"t":["செப்புஞ் சிவாகமம் என்னும்அப் பேர்பெற்றும்","அப்படி நல்கும் அருள்நந்தி தாள்பெற்றுத்","தப்பிலா மன்றில் தனிக்கூத்துக் கண்டபின்","ஒப்பிலா எழுகோடி யுகமிருந் தேனே."],"p":"","r":"Gaining the feet of gracious Nandi, who grants the teaching that bears the name Siva Agama, and after seeing the matchless dance in the flawless hall, I remained for seven crore ages without equal.","n":"Line 1 may mean that the poet's work gained the name Siva Agama, or that he gained the Agama so named. எழுகோடி யுகம் is a figure of immense time. ஒப்பிலா may go with the ages or with himself.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aagamam","guru","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:5"},"t:75":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":75,"t":["இருந்தஅக் காரணம் கேள்இந் திரனே","பொருந்திய செல்வப் புவனா பதியாம்","அருந்தவச் செல்வியைச் சேவித்து அடியேன்","பரிந்துடன் வந்தனன் பத்தியி னாலே."],"p":"","r":"Hear the reason I stayed, Indra: having served the lady of rare austerity, rich in fortune, Bhuvanapati, I came with her out of love and devotion.","n":"Indra is the disciple named in 69. புவனாபதி (lady of the worlds) and அருந்தவச் செல்வி are taken as the Goddess. Whether he came with her or came because of her is not clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","anbu","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:5"},"t:76":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":76,"t":["சதாசிவந் தத்துவம் முத்தமிழ் வேதம்","மிதாசனி யாதிருந் தேன்இன்ற காலம்","இதாசனி யாதிருந் தேன்மனம் நீங்கி","உதாசனி யாதுடனே உணர்ந் தோமால்."],"p":"","r":"Through Sadasiva, the tattvas, the threefold Tamil and the Veda, I stayed till now keeping a measured diet, and with mind withdrawn and not indifferent, I came to know.","n":"The verse rhymes on Sanskrit forms: மிதாசனி (one who eats sparingly), இதாசனி and உதாசனி (indifferent), which do not yield a firm sense. முத்தமிழ் is Tamil of letters, music and drama. The reading is a guess at the shape of the verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","thamizh","vedham","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:5"},"t:77":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":77,"t":["மாலாங்க னேஇங்கு யான்வந்த காரணம்","நீலாங்க மேனியள் நேரிழை யாளொடு","மூலாங்க மாக மொழிந்த திருக்கூத்தின்","சீலாங்க வேதத்தைச் செப்பவந் தேனே."],"p":"","r":"Malangan, the reason I came here is to tell the Veda of noble limbs of the holy dance, spoken as root and limb with the fine-jewelled one of dark-blue body.","n":"Malangan is the disciple named in 69. மூலாங்கம் and சீலாங்கம் are coined compounds; மூல may pun on Mulan. The 'Veda' he has come to tell is probably his own book.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aagamam","sivam","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:5"},"t:78":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":78,"t":["நேரிழை யாவாள் நிரதிச யானந்தப்","பேருடை யாளென் பிறப்பறுத்து ஆண்டவள்","சீருடை யாள்சிவன் ஆவடு தண்டுறை","சீருடை யாள்பதம் சேர்ந்திருந் தேனே."],"p":"","r":"The fine-jewelled one is she whose name is unsurpassed bliss, who cut off my birth and took me as her own; she of glory is at Siva's Avaduthurai, and I reached her feet and stayed.","n":"ஆவடுதண்டுறை is Tiruvavaduturai, where tradition places Tirumular. Here the Goddess, not Nandi or Siva, is the one who ends his births.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","piravi","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:5"},"t:79":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":79,"t":["சேர்ந்திருந் தேன்சிவ மங்கைதன் பங்கனைச்","சேர்ந்திருந் தேன்சிவன் ஆவடு தண்டுறை","சேர்ந்திருந் தேன்சிவ போதியின் நீழலில்","சேர்ந்திருந் தேன்சிவன் நாமங்கள் ஓதியே."],"p":"","r":"I stayed joined to the one whose half is Siva's woman; I stayed at Siva's Avaduthurai; I stayed in the shade of Siva's bodhi tree; I stayed reciting Siva's names.","n":"சிவபோதி (Siva's bodhi tree) and நற்போதி in 82 use the word for the Buddha's tree, here for the sacred fig under which the sage sat.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:5"},"t:80":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":80,"t":["இருந்தேன் இக்காயத்தே எண்ணிலி கோடி","இருந்தேன் இராப்பகல் அற்ற இடத்தே","இருந்தேன் இமையவர் ஏத்தும் பதத்தே","இருந்தேன் என்நந்தி இணையடிக் கீழே."],"p":"","r":"I stayed in this body for countless crores; I stayed in the place where there is no night and day; I stayed at the feet the gods praise; I stayed beneath the twin feet of my Nandi.","n":"இராப்பகல் அற்ற இடம் is traditionally a yogic state beyond the alternation of the breaths (read as sun and moon, day and night); plainly it is a place outside time. Staying in this body (இக்காயத்தே) for ages is offered as attainment, not as bondage.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","yogam","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:5"},"t:81":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":81,"t":["பின்னைநின்று என்னே பிறவி பெறுவது","முன்னைநன் றாக முயல்தவம் செய்கிலர்","என்னைநன் றாக இறைவன் படைத்தனன்","தன்னைநன் றாகத் தமிழ்செய்யு மாறே."],"p":"","r":"Why be born again later? People do not strive at austerity well beforehand. The Lord made me well so that I would render him well in Tamil.","n":"தன்னை நன்றாகத் தமிழ் செய்யுமாறு is usually taken as 'to sing him well in Tamil'. The verse sets others' neglect of tapas against his own purpose. Sengalvaraya notes a parallel in Kandar Anubhuti 17, where the poet's learning is likewise the Lord's gift.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","piravi","thamizh"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:5"},"t:82":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":82,"t":["ஞானத் தலைவிதன் நந்தி நகர்புக்கு","ஊனமில் ஒன்பது கோடி யுகந்தனுள்","ஞானப்பா லாட்டி நாதனை அர்ச்சித்து","நானும் இருந்தேன்நற் போதியின் கீழே."],"p":"","r":"Entering the city of Nandi, consort of the lady of wisdom, I too stayed for nine flawless crore ages beneath the good bodhi tree, worshipping the Lord and bathing him in the milk of wisdom.","n":"ஞானப்பாலாட்டி may be 'bathing in the milk of wisdom' or a title of the Goddess (the nurse who gives wisdom's milk). The ages differ from 74's seven crore, which suggests figures of vastness.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:5"},"t:83":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":83,"t":["செல்கின்ற வாறறி சிவமுனி சித்தசன்","வெல்கின்ற ஞானத்து மிக்கேள் முனிவராய்ப்","பல்கின்ற தேவர் அசுரர்நரர் தம்பால்","ஒல்கின்ற வான்வழி யூடுவந் தானே."],"p":"","r":"A Siva-sage who knows the way to go, a siddha, eminent among sages in conquering wisdom, came by the sky-path that bends toward the many gods, asuras and humans.","n":"சித்தசன் is unclear: a siddha, a name, or Manmatha (சித்தஜன், born of mind) are all possible. Tradition reads the verse of Tirumular's own coming south from Kailasa through the sky.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["siththi","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:5"},"t:84":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":84,"t":["சித்தத்தின் உள்ளே சிறக்கின்ற நூல்களில்","உத்தம மாகவே ஓதிய வேதத்தின்","ஒத்த உடலையும் உள்நின்ற உற்பத்தி","அத்தன் எனக்குஇங்கு அருளால் அளித்ததே."],"p":"","r":"Among the books that excel in the mind, the Father here gave me by grace a body fitting the Veda recited as highest, and the origin that stands within.","n":"ஒத்த உடலையும் (a fitting body too) and உள்நின்ற உற்பத்தி (the origin within) are hard to construe. The plain sense is that the body was given by grace, for the teaching; that fits Tirumular's high view of the body.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","thiruvarul","vedham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:5"},"t:85":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":85,"t":["யான்பெற்ற இன்பம் பெறுக இவ் வையகம்","வான்பற்றி நின்ற மறைப்பொருள் சொல்லிடின்","ஊன்பற்றி நின்ற உணர்வுறு மந்திரம்","தான்பற்றப் பற்றத் தலைப்படுந் தானே."],"p":"","r":"May this world gain the bliss I gained: if one speaks the hidden meaning that holds the sky, the mantra felt within, that holds to the flesh, comes forward as one holds to it more and more.","n":"The first line is the most quoted in the book and is plain. The rest is not: ஊன்பற்றி நின்ற உணர்வுறு மந்திரம் is traditionally the mantra present in the body as breath or as consciousness, and தலைப்படும் may mean it is attained or that it rises up.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["inbam","mandhiram","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:5"},"t:86":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":86,"t":["பிறப்பிலி நாதனைப் பேர்நந்தி தன்னைச்","சிறப்பொடு வானவர் சென்றுகை கூப்பி","மறப்பிலர் நெஞ்சினுள் மந்திர மாலை","உறைப்பொடுங் கூடிநின்று ஓதலு மாமே."],"p":"","r":"The gods go and fold their hands to the unborn Lord, great Nandi; never forgetting him, they can recite the garland of mantras in their hearts, joined to it firmly.","n":"மந்திர மாலை (garland of mantras) is often taken as the Tirumanthiram itself. பிறப்பிலி (the unborn) is a title of Siva given here to Nandi.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","vazhipaadu","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:5"},"t:87":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":87,"t":["அங்கிமி காமைவைத் தானுடல் வைத்தான்","எங்குமி காமைவைத் தான்உலகு ஏழையும்","தங்குமி காமைவைத் தான் தமிழ்ச் சாத்திரம்","பொங்கிமி காமைவைத் தான்பொருள் தானுமே."],"p":"","r":"He set fire within its bounds and set the body, set the seven worlds so they keep within bounds, set the Tamil treatise so it keeps within bounds, and set meaning itself so it does not overflow.","n":"The refrain is மிகாமை (not exceeding) after வைத்தான் (he set). The Lord's ordering of fire, body and worlds is extended to the Tamil sastra and its meaning. எங்குமி and தங்குமி may also divide as இகாமை (not leaving).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","alavu","thamizh"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:5"},"t:88":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":88,"t":["அடிமுடி காண்பார் அயன்மால் இருவர்","படிகண் டிலர்மீண்டும் பார்மிசைக் கூடி","அடிகண் டிலேன் என்று அச்சுதன் சொல்ல","முடிகண்டேன் என்று அயன் பொய்மொழிந் தானே."],"p":"","r":"Brahma and Vishnu, seeking his foot and crown, did not see his form; meeting again on earth, Achyuta said 'I did not see the foot', and Brahma lied, 'I saw the crown'.","n":"The story of Siva as the endless column of fire. Vishnu admits failure and Brahma lies; the verse contrasts their honesty without stating a moral.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","vaaimai","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:5"},"t:89":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":89,"t":["பெற்றமும் மானும் மழுவும் பிரிவற்ற","தற்பரன் கற்பனை யாகும் சராசரத்து","அற்றமும் நல்கி அடியேன் சிரத்தினில்","நற்பத முமளித் தானெங்கள் நந்தியே."],"p":"","r":"Our Nandi, the Supreme never parted from bull, deer and axe, gave me release from the moving and unmoving world that is his imagining, and set his good feet on my head.","n":"அற்றம் may be 'end', 'release' or 'secret'. கற்பனை ஆகும் சராசரம் (the world that is his imagining) can be read as the world being his creation or as its being illusory; the verse does not settle which.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","thiruvarul","maayai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:5"},"t:90":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":90,"t":["நேயத்தை ஞானத்தை ஞாதுரு வத்தினை","மாயத்தை மாமாயை தன்னில் வரும்பரை","ஆயத்தை யச்சிவன் தன்னை யாகோசர","வீயத்தை முற்றும் விளக்கியிட் டேனே."],"p":"","r":"I have explained in full the known, knowledge and the knower, maya, the supreme power that comes from Mahamaya, the whole assembly, Siva himself, and the seed beyond the senses.","n":"நேயம், ஞானம், ஞாதுரு are the triad of knowable, knowledge and knower. ஆயம் (group, assembly) and அகோசர வீயம் (the imperceptible seed) are technical and unclear. The verse claims the whole Siddhanta scheme for his book.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","gnaanam","maayai","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:5"},"t:91":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":91,"t":["விளக்கிப் பரமாகும் மெய்ஞ்ஞானச் சோதி","அளப்பில் பெருமையன் ஆனந்த நந்தி","துளக்கறும் ஆனந்தக் கூத்தன்சொற் போந்து","வளப்பில் கயிலை வழியில்வந் தேனே."],"p":"","r":"Explaining, I came by the way of boundless Kailasa at the word of the unwavering dancer of bliss, Nandi of bliss and measureless greatness, the light of true wisdom that is the Supreme.","n":"விளக்கி takes up 90's last word. The verse places his coming from Kailasa under the dancer's command, which is how tradition tells his journey south.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["guru","gnaanam","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:5"},"t:92":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":92,"t":["நந்தி அருளாலே மூலனை நாடிப்பின்","நந்தி அருளாலே சதாசிவன் ஆயினேன்","நந்தி அருளால்மெய்ஞானத்துள் நண்ணினேன்","நந்தி அருளாலே நானிருந் தேனே."],"p":"","r":"By Nandi's grace I sought Mulan; then by Nandi's grace I became Sadasiva; by Nandi's grace I entered true wisdom; by Nandi's grace I stayed.","n":"The four lines repeat நந்தி அருளாலே as 68 does. சதாசிவன் ஆயினேன் (I became Sadasiva) is a strong claim of identity with a form of Siva, attributed wholly to the guru's grace.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","guru","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:5"},"t:93":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":93,"t":["இருக்கில் இருக்கும் எண்ணிலி கோடி","அருக்கின்ற மூலத்துள் அங்கே இருக்கும்","அருக்கனும் சோமனும் ஆரழல் வீச","உருக்கிய ரோமம் ஒளிவிடுந் தானே."],"p":"","r":"If one stays, countless crores stay there within the consuming root; as sun and moon blaze with fierce fire, the melted hair shines with light.","n":"மூலம் is traditionally the root centre of yoga, and அருக்கன், சோமன் (sun and moon) the two breath channels whose fire is raised. உருக்கிய ரோமம் (melted hair) is obscure; it may describe the yogi's body made radiant. இருக்கு may also pun on the Rig Veda. Both a plain and a yogic reading are uncertain here.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","siththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:5"},"t:94":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":94,"t":["பிதற்றுகின் றேனென்றும் பேர்நந்தி தன்னை","இயற்றுவன் நெஞ்சத்து இரவும் பகலும்","முயற்றுவன் ஓங்கொளி வண்ணன்எம் மானை","இயற்றிகழ் சோதி இறைவனு மாமே."],"p":"","r":"Always I babble of great Nandi; I hold him in my heart night and day; I strive toward my Lord of soaring light, who is the Lord, a light shining by its own nature.","n":"பிதற்றுகின்றேன் (I babble) is devotional self-belittling, preparing the chapter on modesty that follows. The last line runs Nandi and the Lord together.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["guru","vazhipaadu","panivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:5"},"t:95":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":95,"t":["ஆரறி வார் எங்கள் அண்ணல் பெருமையை","யாரறி வார்இந்த அகலமும் நீளமும்","பேரறி யாத பெருஞ்சுடர் ஒன்றதின்","வேரறி யாமை விளம்புகின் றேனே."],"p":"","r":"Who knows the greatness of our Lord? Who knows his breadth and length? I tell of my not knowing the root of the one great nameless flame.","n":"பேரறியாத may mean 'nameless' or 'whose name is unknown'. The great flame (பெருஞ்சுடர்) whose root is unknown recalls the fire column of 88. Sengalvaraya lists Tevaram parallels asking who knows his nature.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","panivu","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:6"},"t:96":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":96,"t":["பாடவல் லார்நெறி பாட அறிகிலேன்","ஆடவல் லார்நெறி ஆட அறிகிலேன்","நாடவல் லார்நெறி நாட அறிகிலேன்","தேடவல் லார்நெறி தேடகில் லேனே."],"p":"","r":"I do not know how to sing in the way of those who can sing, nor dance in the way of those who can dance, nor seek in the way of those who can seek, nor search in the way of those who can search.","n":"The four skills may be the forms of devotion (song and dance) and of inquiry (seeking and searching). The disclaimer is conventional in a preface, but here it covers both worship and knowledge.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panivu","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:6"},"t:97":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":97,"t":["மன்னிய வாய்மொழி யாலும் மதித்தவர்","இன்னிசை உள்ளே எழுகின்ற ஈசனைப்","பின்னை உலகம் படைத்த பிரமனும்","உன்னும் அவனை உணரலு மாமே."],"p":"","r":"Those who honour him with abiding words of truth, even Brahma who afterward made the world, can come to know the Lord who rises within sweet music by contemplating him.","n":"The syntax is loose: Brahma may be one of those who know him, or a contrast (even the creator must contemplate). உன்னும் (contemplating) is the verse's way to knowledge, against 95's 'who knows?'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","vaaimai","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:6"},"t:98":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":98,"t":["தத்துவ ஞானம் உரைத்தது தாழ்வரை","முத்திக்கு இருந்த முனிவரும் தேவரும்","இத்துடன் வேறா இருந்து துதிசெயும்","பத்திமை யால் இப் பயனறி யாரே."],"p":"","r":"The knowledge of the tattvas was told at the foot of the mountain; the sages and gods who waited there for release, staying apart and praising, do not know this fruit through devotion alone.","n":"தாழ்வரை (foothill, slope) may be the place where the teaching was given, perhaps Kailasa. The last line is the hard part: பத்திமையால் இப்பயன் அறியாரே may mean they do not know the fruit by devotion, or that because of their devotion they do not know it (they praise rather than understand). Either way it seems to rank knowledge of tattvas above praise alone.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","gnaanam","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:6"},"t:99":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":99,"t":["மூலன் உரைசெய்த மூவா யிரந்தமிழ்","ஞாலம் அறியவே நந்தி அருளது","காலை எழுந்து கருத்தறிந் தோதிடின்","ஞாலத் தலைவனை நண்ணுவர் அன்றே."],"p":"","r":"That the world should know the three thousand Tamil verses Mulan spoke is Nandi's grace; those who rise in the morning and recite them knowing their meaning will reach the Lord of the world.","n":"கருத்தறிந்து (knowing the meaning) is the condition: recitation alone is not promised the fruit, as 64 says of the Agamas. The verse speaks of Mulan in the third person.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thamizh","thiruvarul","vazhipaadu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:7"},"t:100":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":100,"t":["வைத்த பரிசே வகைவகை நன்னூலின்","முத்தி முடிவிது மூவா யிரத்திலே","புத்திசெய் பூர்வத்து மூவா யிரம்பொது","வைத்த சிறப்புத் தருமிவை தானே."],"p":"","r":"As arranged, among the many kinds of good books, this is the end that is release, within the three thousand; the three thousand, set out for those who apply their minds, are common to all and give the distinction placed in them.","n":"புத்திசெய் பூர்வத்து and மூவாயிரம் பொது are obscure. முத்தி முடிவு may mean that the book's goal is release or that it is the culmination of the teaching on release. The claim that the book is common (பொது) to all is a possible reading, not a sure one.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["veedu","kalvi","thamizh"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:7"},"t:101":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":101,"t":["வந்த மடம்ஏழும் மன்னும்சன் மார்க்கத்தின்","முந்தி உதிக்கின்ற மூலன் மடம்வரை","தந்திரம் ஒன்பது சார்வுமூ வாயிரம்","சுந்தர ஆகமச் சொல்மொழிந் தானே."],"p":"","r":"Of the seven mathas that came down in the abiding true path, up to Mulan's matha that arises first, he spoke the beautiful Agama in nine tantras, three thousand verses, as their support.","n":"சன்மார்க்கம் (the true path) in Siddhanta is the path of knowledge. முந்தி உதிக்கின்ற (arising first) may mean Mulan's matha is the earliest or the foremost. The verse speaks of Mulan in the third person; the count of nine tantras and three thousand verses is the book's own.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","aagamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:8"},"t:102":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":102,"t":["கலந்தருள் காலாங்கர் தம்பால்அ கோரர்","நலந்தரு மாளிகைத் தேவர்நா தாந்தர்","புலங்கொள் பரமானந் தர்போக தேவர்","நிலந்திகழ் மூவர் நிராமயத் தோரே."],"p":"","r":"From Kalangar, who mingles in grace, came Aghorar, Maligai Devar who gives good, Nathantar, Paramanandar of keen insight and Bhoga Devar; the three who shine on earth are of Niramaya.","n":"The verse lists founders or teachers of the mathas. Kalangar is likely the Kalangi of 69. மூவர் (the three) and நிராமயத்தோர் (those of Niramaya, or 'the flawless ones') are unclear, so the number and grouping of the names is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:8"},"t:103":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":103,"t":["அளவில் இளமையும் அந்தமும் ஈறும்","அளவியல் காலமும் நாலும் உணரில்","தளர்விலன் சங்கரன் தன்னடி யார்சொல்","அளவில் பெருமை அரியயற் காமே."],"p":"","r":"For one who understands the measureless youth, end and limit, measured time and the four, Sankara is unwearied, and the measureless greatness his devotees speak of is what reaches Hari and Brahma.","n":"The verse is compressed and its first two lines are obscure: நாலும் (the four) is unexplained, and அளவில் may mean 'measureless' or 'in measure'. The last line is commonly taken to mean that Hari and Brahma hold their greatness from Siva, which fits the chapter, but the syntax does not settle it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:9"},"t:104":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":104,"t":["ஆதிப் பிரானும் அணிமணி வண்ணனும்","ஆதிக் கமலத்து அலர்மிசை யானும்","சோதிக்கில் மூன்றும் தொடர்ச்சியில் ஒன்றெனார்","பேதித் துலகம் பிணங்குகின் றார்களே."],"p":"","r":"The first Lord, the one coloured like a fine jewel and the one on the petals of the first lotus are, on examination, one in their continuity; those who do not say so divide them and quarrel throughout the world.","n":"ஆதிப் பிரான் is Siva, அணிமணி வண்ணன் Vishnu and the one on the lotus Brahma. தொடர்ச்சியில் ஒன்று (one in continuity) does not say the three are identical; it may mean they form one sequence or one lineage from a single source. பேதித்து பிணங்குதல names sectarian quarrelling.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","sivam","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:9"},"t:105":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":105,"t":["ஈசன் இருக்கும் இருவினைககு அப்புறம்","பீசம் உலகல் பெருந்தெய்வம் ஆனது","ஈசன் அதுஇது என்பார் நினைப்பிலார்","தூசு பிடித்தவர் தூரறிந் தார்களே."],"p":"","r":"The Lord stands beyond the two kinds of deeds; the seed became the great god for the world; those who say the Lord is this or that have no understanding, while those who held fast knew the root.","n":"இருவினை is good and bad deeds. பீசம் (bija, seed) is unclear: the Lord as seed of the world, or a seed-syllable. தூசு பிடித்தவர் (those who held the cloth) is taken as those who clung to the Lord as a child holds a garment's hem; தூர் (root, base) may be the Lord as source.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","vinai","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:9"},"t:106":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":106,"t":["சிவன்முதல் மூவரோடு ஐவர் சிறந்த","அவைமுதல் ஆறிரண்டு ஒன்றோடு ஒன் றாகும்","அவைமுதல் விந்துவும் நாதமும் ஓங்கச்","சவைமுதற் சங்கரன் தன்பெயர் தானே."],"p":"","r":"With Siva first, the three and the five excel; from them come twelve, one joined with one; from them bindu and nada rise; and Sankara, first in the assembly, has these as his own name.","n":"The numbers (three, five, twelve, one with one) are not explained in the verse. Tradition reads them as groups of deities or powers emanating from Siva, but which groups is not fixed by the text. The plain point is that every emanation, up to bindu and nada, is Siva's own name and form.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","vindhu","naadham","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:9"},"t:107":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":107,"t":["பயன் அறிந்து அவ்வழி எண்ணும் அளவில்","அயனொடு மால்நமககு அன்னியம் இல்லை","நயனங்கள் மூன்றுடை நந்தி தமராம்","வயனம் பெறுவீர் அவ் வானவ ராலே."],"p":"","r":"Reckoning by that way, knowing the fruit, Brahma and Vishnu are not strangers to us; they are kin of three-eyed Nandi, and you will gain blessing through those gods.","n":"வயனம் is obscure; it may stand for வசனம் (word) or a blessing. தமர் (one's own people) makes Brahma and Vishnu Siva's kin and so worthy of regard, not rivals. The verse accepts their worship within Siva's order.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","sivam","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:9"},"t:108":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":108,"t":["ஓலக்கம் சூழ்ந்த உலப்பிலி தேவர்கள்","பாலொத்த மேனி பணிந்தடி யேன் தொழ","மாலுக்கும் ஆதிப் பிரமற்கும் ஒப்புநீ","ஞாலத்து நம்மடி நல்கிடுஎன் றானே."],"p":"","r":"As I bowed and worshipped the milk-white one surrounded by his court of deathless gods, he said: 'You are equal to Mal and to Brahma; give our feet to the world.'","n":"The speaker is taken to be Tirumular, and the verse as Siva's commission to him to teach. Who is 'equal to Mal and Brahma' is not wholly clear: it may be Tirumular, honoured as a teacher, or the phrase may compare Siva's gift. நம் அடி நல்கிடு, 'give our feet', makes the teaching the giving of Siva's feet.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","thiruvarul","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:9"},"t:109":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":109,"t":["வானவர் என்றும் மனிதர் இவர் என்றும்","தேனமர் கொன்றைச் சிவனருள் அல்லது","தானமர்ந்து ஓரும் தனித்தெய்வம் மற்றில்லை","ஊனமர்ந் தோரை உணர்வது தானே."],"p":"","r":"Whether gods or humans, there is no separate god to settle on and think of other than the grace of Siva of the honeyed konrai; it is that grace which knows those who dwell in flesh.","n":"The verse puts grace (அருள்) in the place of a god: the only deity is Siva's grace. The last line can also be read as 'it is that which those in the body must come to know'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","sivam","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:9"},"t:110":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":110,"t":["சோதித்த பேரொளி மூன்று ஐந்து எனநின்ற","ஆதிக்கண் ஆவது அறிகிலர் ஆதர்கள்","நீதிக்கண் ஈசன் நெடுமால் அயனென்று","பேதித் தவரைப் பிதற்றுகின் றாரே."],"p":"","r":"The ignorant do not know that the great light, examined, stood as three and as five in the beginning; they babble, dividing it into the Lord, tall Mal and Brahma by their functions.","n":"மூன்று ஐந்து may be the three gods and the five (adding Maheswara and Sadasiva), or the five acts of the Lord. நீதிக்கண் is read here as 'by their offices'. ஆதர் means the blind or the ignorant; பிதற்றுதல் is a strong word for babbling.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","pedhaimai","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:9"},"t:111":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":111,"t":["பரத்திலே ஒன்றாய் உள் ளாய்ப்புற மாகி","வரத்தினுள் மாயவ னாய்அய னாகித்","தரத்தினுள் தான்பல தன்மைய னாகிக்","கரத்தினுள் நின்று கழிவுசெய் தானே."],"p":"","r":"One in the supreme, inside and outside, becoming Vishnu and Brahma in their boons, taking many natures in their ranks, he stands concealed and works dissolution.","n":"The verse gives the one Lord the three functions: preserving as Vishnu, creating as Brahma, dissolving himself. கரத்தினுள் is taken as 'in concealment' (from கர, hide); 'in the hand' is also possible. வரம் and தரம் (boon, rank) are uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:9"},"t:112":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":112,"t":["தானொரு கூறு சதாசிவன் எம்மிறை","வானொரு கூறு மருவியும் அங்குளான்","கோனொரு கூறுஉடல் உள்நின்று உயிர்க்கின்ற","தானொரு கூறு சலமய னாமே."],"p":"","r":"One part of him is Sadasiva our Lord; one part joins and stays in the sky; one part as lord stands in the body and gives it breath; and one part is the one of the waters.","n":"சலமயன் (the one of water) is unclear: Vishnu lying on the waters, or Siva bearing the Ganga. The third line is the most important for the book as a whole: the Lord is the one who breathes in every body.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","uyir","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:9"},"t:113":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":113,"t":["விண்ணின்று இழிந்து வினைக்கீடாய் மெய்க்கொண்டு","தண்ணின்ற தாளைத் தலைக்காவல் முன்வைத்து","உண்ணின்று உருக்கியோர் ஒப்பிலா ஆனந்தக்","கண்ணின்று காட்டிக் களிம்பறுத் தானே."],"p":"","r":"Coming down from the sky, taking a body to answer my deeds, setting his cool feet on my head as a guard, melting me from within and showing me in the eye an incomparable bliss, he cut away the verdigris.","n":"களிம்பு is the green tarnish on copper; tradition takes it as the soul's root impurity (ஆணவம்), and the verse as the Lord descending as guru. வினைக்கு ஈடாய் may mean the body he took suits the disciple's deeds, so that the Lord meets the soul in a form it can receive.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["guru","thiruvarul","malam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:10"},"t:114":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":114,"t":["களிம்பறுத் தான்எங்கள் கண்ணுதல் நந்தி","களிம்பறுத் தான்அருள் கண்விழிப் பித்துக்","களிம்பணு காத கதிரொளி காட்டிப்","பளிங்கிற் பவளம் பதித்தான் பதியே."],"p":"","r":"He cut away the verdigris, our Nandi with the eye in his forehead; cutting it, he opened the eye of grace, showed a radiant light that tarnish cannot touch, and set coral in crystal: he is the Lord.","n":"பளிங்கில் பவளம் பதித்தான் (set coral in crystal) is symbolic: commonly read as the red Lord shining in the soul once it is clear like crystal. பதி, the verse's last word, is both 'Lord' and the first word of the next verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","malam","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:10"},"t:115":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":115,"t":["பதிபசு பாசம் எனப்பகர் மூன்றில்","பதியினைப் போற்பசு பாசம் அனாதி","பதியினைச் சென்றணு காப்பசு பாசம்","பதியணு கிற்பசு பாசம் நில் லாவே."],"p":"","r":"Of the three named Pati, Pasu and Pasam, Pasu and Pasam are beginningless like Pati; Pasu and Pasam cannot go and reach Pati, but when Pati draws near, Pasu and Pasam do not remain.","n":"The verse states the Saiva Siddhanta triad of Lord, soul and bond, and makes all three without beginning. The last line is traditionally read as the end of the soul's bondage (its being a பசு, a bound creature), not of the soul itself; the verse alone does not make that distinction. Approach is one-way: the soul cannot reach the Lord, the Lord must come.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pathi","pasu","paasam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:10"},"t:116":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":116,"t":["வேயின் எழுங்கனல் போலேஇம் மெய்யெனும்","கோயி லிருந்து குடிகொண்ட கோன்நந்தி","தாயினும் மும்மலம் மாற்றித் தயாஎன்னும்","தோயம தாய் எழுஞ் சூரிய னாமே."],"p":"","r":"Like fire that rises from bamboo, Nandi, the king who has made his home in the temple called this body, removes the three malas more tenderly than a mother and rises as a sun that is the water of compassion.","n":"Fire latent in bamboo, which comes out by friction, is the Lord hidden in the body. The body is called a temple (மெய் எனும் கோயில்), not a filthy frame. The last line mixes images: a sun that is the water (தோயம்) of mercy; tradition takes it as grace that both burns impurity and cools.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","malam","yaakkai","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:10"},"t:117":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":117,"t":["சூரிய காந்தமும் சூழ்பஞ்சும் போலவே","சூரிய காந்தம் சூழ்பஞ்சைச் சுட்டிடா","சூரியன் சந்நிதி யிற்சுடு மாறுபோல்","சூரியன் தோற்றமுன் அற்ற மலங்களே."],"p":"","r":"As a sunstone does not burn the cotton around it until it stands in the sun's presence, and then burns it, so the malas end before the sun appears.","n":"The sunstone is commonly taken as the soul, the cotton as the malas and the sun as the Lord or the guru. The point is that the soul has the power to burn its impurity only in the Lord's presence.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["malam","thiruvarul","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:10"},"t:118":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":118,"t":["மலங்கள்ஐந் தாமென மாற்றி அருளித்","தலங்கள்ஐந் தானற் சதாசிவ மான","புலங்களைந் தான்அப் பொதுவினுள் நந்தி","நலங்களைந் தான்உள் நயந்தான் அறிந்தே."],"p":"","r":"Making known that the malas are five, he removed them in grace; Nandi in that common hall, who is Sadasiva of the five stations, cleared away the five domains and, knowing them, desired the good within.","n":"Five malas are traditionally ஆணவம், கன்மம், மாயை, மாயேயம் and திரோதாயி, though the verse does not name them. தலங்கள் ஐந்து (five stations) and புலங்கள் (domains, senses) are unclear, and the last line is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["malam","thiruvarul","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:10"},"t:119":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":119,"t":["அறிவுஐம் புலனுட னேநான் றதாகி","நெறியறி யாதுற்ற நீர்ஆழம் போல","அறிவுஅறி வுள்ளே அழிந்தது போலக்","குறியறி விப்பான் குருபர னாமே."],"p":"","r":"When awareness, hanging on the five senses, was lost like water sunk in depths with no outlet, and was as if it had perished within awareness, the supreme guru makes known the sign.","n":"நான்றது (hung on, depended on) and நீர் ஆழம் (deep water) are the key images. அறிவு அறிவுள்ளே அழிந்தது is hard: perhaps the soul's knowing swallowed by the senses, or a positive merging. குறி (sign, mark) is left unexplained.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","arivu","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:10"},"t:120":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":120,"t":["ஆமேவு பால்நீர் பிரிக்கின்ற அன்னம்போல்","தாமே தனிமன்றில் தன்னந் தனிநித்தம்","தீமேவு பல்கர ணங்களுள் உற்றன","தாமேழ் பிறப்பெரி சார்ந்தவித் தாமே."],"p":"","r":"Like the swan that parts milk from water, he alone, eternally, in the solitary hall, makes what is lodged in the many fiery faculties into seeds that have touched the fire and will not sprout into the seven births.","n":"A roasted seed does not sprout: deeds burnt by grace no longer yield births. Who parts milk from water is not stated; it may be the Lord, the guru or the discerning soul. ஆமேவு, the opening, is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","vinai","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:10"},"t:121":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":121,"t":["வித்தைக் கெடுத்து வியாக்கிரத் தேமிகச்","சுத்தத் துரியம் பிறந்து துடக்கற","ஒத்துப் புலனுயிர் ஒன்றாய் உடம்பொடு","செத்திட் டிருப்பார் சிவயோகி யார்களே."],"p":"","r":"Having destroyed the seed, with the pure turiya state arising strongly in waking, bonds cut, senses and life become one, they remain with the body as if dead: these are the Siva-yogis.","n":"வியாக்கிரம் (literally tiger) is obscure; it is often taken as ஜாக்கிரம், the waking state, giving turiya in waking. 'Dead with the body' is taken as dead to the senses while alive. Sengalvaraya notes a parallel in Kandar Alangaram 19.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","samaadhi","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:10"},"t:122":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":122,"t":["சிவயோக மாவது சித்தசித் தென்று","தவயோகத் துள்புக்குத் தன்னொளி தானாய்","அவயோகஞ் சாராது அவன்பதி போக","நவயோக நந்தி நமக்களித் தானே."],"p":"","r":"Siva-yoga is to know the conscient and the non-conscient, enter the yoga of tapas, become one's own light, not take to false yoga, and go to his abode; Nandi of the nava-yoga gave it to us.","n":"சித்து, அசித்து are the conscient (souls) and the non-conscient (matter). அவயோகம் is read as wrong or lower yoga. நவயோகம் may mean 'the new yoga' or 'nine yogas'; the text doesn't decide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","sivam","thavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:10"},"t:123":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":123,"t":["அளித்தான் உலகெங்கும் தானான உண்மை","அளித்தான் அமரர் அறியா உலகம்","அளித்தான் திருமன்றுள் ஆடுந் திருத்தாள்","அளித்தான் பேரின்பத்து அருள்வெளி தானே."],"p":"","r":"He gave the truth that he is himself the whole world; he gave the world the gods do not know; he gave the holy feet that dance in the sacred hall; he gave the open space of grace in great bliss.","n":"The anaphora on அளித்தான் (he gave) builds a list of gifts that rise from knowledge to bliss. Sengalvaraya notes a parallel in Tirumurugatruppadai 293.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","sivam","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:10"},"t:124":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":124,"t":["வெளியில் வெளிபோய் விரவிய வாறும்","அளியில் அளிபோய் அடங்கிய வாறும்","ஒளியில் ஒளிபோய் ஒடுங்கிய வாறும்","தெளியும் அவரே சிவ சித்தர் தாமே."],"p":"","r":"Those who see clearly how space went into space and mingled, how sweetness went into sweetness and subsided, how light went into light and was absorbed: they are the Siva-siddhas.","n":"அளி can be honey, the bee or grace; whichever, the pattern is like merging into like. Three images of union without remainder.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["siththi","sivam","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:10"},"t:125":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":125,"t":["சித்தர் சிவலோகம் இங்கே தரிசித்தோர்","சத்தமும் சத்த முடிவுந்தம் முள்கொண்டோ ர்","நித்தர் நிமலர் நிராமயர் நீள்பர","முத்தர்தம் முத்தி முதல்முப்பத் தாறே."],"p":"","r":"Siddhas are those who have seen Sivaloka here, who hold within them sound and sound's end; eternal, stainless, free of affliction, the far-supreme freed ones, their release begins from the thirty-six.","n":"சத்தமும் சத்த முடிவும் are taken as nada and nadanta. முப்பத்தாறு is the thirty-six tattvas. 'Sivaloka here' (இங்கே) makes release something seen in this life.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["siththi","veedu","naadham","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:10"},"t:126":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":126,"t":["முப்பதும் ஆறும் படிமுத்தி ஏணியாய்","ஒப்பிலா ஆனந்தத் துள்ளொளி புக்குச்","செப்ப அரிய சிவங்கண்டு தான்தெளிந்து","அப்பரி சாக அமர்ந்திருந் தாரே."],"p":"","r":"Taking the thirty-six as the rungs of a ladder to release, entering the inner light of incomparable bliss, seeing the unspeakable Sivam and becoming clear, they remained settled in that state.","n":"The tattvas, which elsewhere unfold the world downward from Siva, are here climbed upward as rungs. Sengalvaraya notes parallels in Kandar Anubhuti 47 and Kandar Alangaram 55.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","veedu","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:10"},"t:127":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":127,"t":["இருந்தார் சிவமாகி எங்குந் தாமாகி","இருந்தார் சிவன்செயல் யாவையும் நோக்கி","இருந்தார் முக்காலத்து இயல்பைக் குறித்தங்கு","இருந்தார் இழவுவந்து எய்திய சோம்பே."],"p":"","r":"They remained having become Sivam and everything; they remained watching all of Siva's acts; they remained knowing the nature of the three times; they remained in the idleness that came when the loss had come.","n":"சோம்பு (idleness) is turned to praise: the stillness of one who has nothing left to do. இழவு (loss) is not said: loss of self, of the world, or of striving.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","samaadhi","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:10"},"t:128":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":128,"t":["சோம்பர் இருப்பது சுத்த வெளியிலே","சோம்பர் கிடப்பதும் சுத்த வெளியிலே","சோம்பர் உணர்வு சுருதி முடிந்திடஞ்","சோம்பர் கண் டார்அச் சுருதிக்கண் தூக்கமே."],"p":"","r":"The idle ones dwell in pure space, lie in pure space; their awareness is where the Veda ends, and what they saw is sleep within that Veda.","n":"The Kural lists sloth and sleep among the marks of those headed for ruin (605); Tirumular makes the same words name the freed. சுருதி முடிந்திடம் (where the Veda ends) is Vedanta, and சுருதிக்கண் தூக்கம் rest in it. The inversion is deliberate, but it is about stillness in Sivam, not worldly work.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["samaadhi","vedaantham","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:10"},"t:129":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":129,"t":["தூங்கிக்கண் டார்சிவ லோகமும் தம்முள்ளே","தூங்கிக்கண் டார்சிவ யோகமும் தம்முள்ளே","தூங்கிக்கண் டார்சிவ போகமும் தம்முள்ளே","தூங்கிக்கண் டார்நிலை சொல்வதெவ் வாறே."],"p":"","r":"Sleeping, they saw Sivaloka within themselves; sleeping, Siva-yoga within themselves; sleeping, Siva-bhoga within themselves; how could that state be told?","n":"The world, the union and the enjoyment of Siva are all found inside (தம்முள்ளே). The sleep is a sleep with awareness; Sengalvaraya notes Tiruppugazh 757, 'those who sleep with awareness'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["samaadhi","yogam","inbam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:10"},"t:130":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":130,"t":["எவ்வாறு காண்பான் அறிவு தனக்கெல்லை","அவ்வாறு அருட்செய்வன் ஆதிஅரன் தானும்","ஒவ்வாத மன்றுள் உமைகாண ஆடிடும்","செவ்வானிற் செய்ய செழுஞ்சுடர் மாணிக்கமே."],"p":"","r":"However far one's knowing reaches, so far the first Hara gives grace; he who dances in the matchless hall for Uma to see is a ruby redder than the red sky, a rich flame.","n":"The first line makes grace proportioned to the seeker's capacity. The red sky and ruby fix Siva's colour as red, as in 114.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","arivu","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:10"},"t:131":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":131,"t":["மாணிக்கத் துள்ளே மரகதச் சோதியாய்","மாணிக்கத் துள்ளே மரகத மாடமாய்","ஆணிப்பொன் மன்றில் ஆடுந் திருக்கூத்தைப்","பேணித் தொழுதென்ன பேறுபெற் றாரே."],"p":"","r":"Within the ruby an emerald light, within the ruby an emerald mansion: what a gain did they gain who cherished and worshipped the holy dance in the hall of finest gold!","n":"Ruby and emerald are traditionally Siva and Uma, the green one within the red. ஆணிப்பொன் is gold of the finest touch. The last line's question is answered in 132.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:10"},"t:132":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":132,"t":["பெற்றார் உலகிற் பிரியாப் பெருநெறி","பெற்றார் உலகிற் பிறவாப் பெரும்பயன்","பெற்றார் அம்மன்றில் பிரியாப் பெரும்பேறு","பெற்றார் உலகுடன் பேசாப் பெருமையே."],"p":"","r":"They gained in this world the great path that does not part from him, the great fruit of not being born, the great attainment that does not part from that hall, and the greatness of not speaking with the world.","n":"உலகுடன் பேசாப் பெருமை may be silence, or freedom from worldly converse and ties. All four gains are had 'in the world' (உலகில்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["veedu","piravi","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:10"},"t:133":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":133,"t":["பெருமை சிறுமை அறிந்தெம் பிரான்போல்","அருமை எளிமை அறிந்தறி வார்ஆர்","ஒருமையுள் ஆமைபோல் உள்ஐந்து அடக்கி","இருமையுங் கேட்டிருந் தார்புரை அற்றே."],"p":"","r":"Who but our Lord knows greatness and smallness, and knows the hard and the easy? Those who, in one birth (or in one-pointedness), drew the five within like a tortoise and heard both, remained without flaw.","n":"The third line nearly repeats Kural 126 (ஒருமையுள் ஆமைபோல் ஐந்தடக்கல்), where ஒருமை is usually taken as 'one birth'; here it may keep that sense or mean one-pointedness. இருமையும் கேட்டு is unclear: 'heard both' (this world and the next, or both teachings), or, if கேட்டு is a misreading of கெட்டு, 'with duality destroyed'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","iraivan","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:10"},"t:134":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":134,"t":["புரைஅற்ற பாலினுள் நெய்கலந் தாற்போல்","திரைஅற்ற சிந்தைநல் ஆரியன் செப்பும்","உரையற்று உணர்வோர் உடம்பிங்கு ஒழிந்தால்","கரையற்ற சோதி கலந்தசத் தாமே."],"p":"","r":"Like ghee mixed in pure milk, those who feel beyond words what the noble teacher of waveless mind speaks, once their body falls here, become the reality mixed in the shoreless light.","n":"ஆரியன் here is the noble teacher. Ghee in milk is present throughout yet unseen until churned; it may be the Lord in the soul or the teaching in the hearer. சத்து (being, reality) is what the knower becomes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["guru","veedu","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:10"},"t:135":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":135,"t":["சத்த முதல் ஐந்துந் தன்வழித் தான்சாரில்","சித்துக்குச் சித்தன்றி சேர்விடம் வேறுண்டோ","சுத்த வெளியிற் சுடரிற் சுடர்சேரும்","அத்தம் இதுகுறித் தாண்டுகொள் அப்பிலே."],"p":"","r":"If the five beginning with sound settle back by their own path, where else is there for the conscient to join except the Conscient? In pure space flame joins flame; grasp this meaning and be ruled, as in water.","n":"சித்துக்குச் சித்து: the conscient soul's only home is the conscient Lord. ஆண்டுகொள் அப்பிலே is obscure; அப்பு (water) leads into the salt image of 136.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","uyir","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:10"},"t:136":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":136,"t":["அப்பினில் கூர்மை ஆதித்தன் வெம்மையால்","உப்பெனப் பேர்ப்பெற்று உருச்செய்த அவ்வுரு","அப்பினிற் கூடிய தொன்றாகு மாறுபோல்","செப்பினிற் சீவன் சிவத்துள் அடங்குமே."],"p":"","r":"As salt, named and given form by the sun's heat on the water's sharpness, becomes one again in the water it meets, so, it is said, the soul subsides into Sivam.","n":"The salt was always of the water and returns to it. The image suggests non-difference; Siddhanta tradition reads it as union in which the soul does not perish, which the verse alone does not settle. அடங்கும் (subsides, is contained) is milder than 'is lost'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uyir","sivam","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:10"},"t:137":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":137,"t":["அடங்குபேர் அண்டத்து அணுஅண்டம் சென்றங்கு","இடங்கொண்டது இல்லை இதுவன்றி வேறுண்டோ","கடந்தொறும் நின்ற உயிர்க்கரை காணில்","திடம்பெற நின்றான் திருவடி தானே."],"p":"","r":"In the great universe that subsides, the atom-worlds go and find no room; is there anything other than this? If you see the shore of the life that stands in each body, it is the holy feet of the one who stood firm.","n":"கடம் (pot) stands for the body. The first two lines are obscure; the plain sense of the last two is that the limit of every soul is the Lord's feet.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","uyir","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:10"},"t:138":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":138,"t":["திருவடி யேசிவ மாவது தேரில்","திருவடி யேசிவ லோகஞ்சிந் திக்கில்","திருவடி யேசெல் கதியது செப்பில்","திருவடி யேதஞ்சம் உள்தெளி வார்க்கே."],"p":"","r":"Examine it and the holy feet are Sivam; think on it and the holy feet are Sivaloka; speak of it and the holy feet are the path to travel; the holy feet are refuge for those clear within.","n":"The feet are at once the Lord, his world and the way there. தஞ்சம் is refuge. The 'clear within' (உள் தெளிவார்) leads into 139, which defines clarity.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","veedu","thelivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:10"},"t:139":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":139,"t":["தெளிவு குருவின் திருமேனி காண்டல்","தெளிவு குருவின் திருநாமஞ் செப்பல்","தெளிவு குருவின் திருவார்த்தை கேட்டல்","தெளிவு குருவுரு சிந்தித்தல் தானே."],"p":"","r":"Clarity is seeing the guru's holy form, uttering the guru's holy name, hearing the guru's holy words and thinking on the guru's form.","n":"The four acts engage eye, tongue, ear and mind. Clarity (தெளிவு) is not reached by argument here but by devotion to the guru.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["guru","thelivu","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:10"},"t:140":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":140,"t":["தானே புலன்ஐந்துந் தன்வசம் ஆயிடும்","தானே புலன்ஐந்துந் தன்வசம் போயிடும்","தானே புலன்ஐந்துந் தன்னில் மடைமாறும்","தானே தனித்துஎம் பிரான்தனைச் சந்தித்தே."],"p":"","r":"Of themselves the five senses come under one's control; of themselves they go into his control; of themselves they turn their channels inward, once one has met our Lord alone.","n":"தன்வசம் can mean 'in one's own power' or 'in his (the Lord's) power', and the first two lines may differ on just that. மடைமாறும் (the sluice turns) is an irrigation image: the senses' flow is redirected. The key word is தானே: the control is not achieved by effort but follows from meeting the Lord.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","sivam","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:10"},"t:141":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":141,"t":["சந்திப் பதுநந்தி தன்திருத் தாளிணை","சிந்திப் பதுநந்தி செய்ய திருமேனி","வந்திப் பதுநந்தி நாமம்இன் வாய்மையால்","புந்திக்குள் நிற்பது நந்திபொற் பாதமே."],"p":"","r":"What one meets is Nandi's pair of holy feet; what one thinks on is Nandi's red holy form; what one praises in truth is Nandi's name; what stands within the mind is Nandi's golden feet.","n":"சந்திப்பது picks up the last word of 140. Nandi is both Siva and the guru-lineage; the verse leaves them as one.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","sivam","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:10"},"t:142":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":142,"t":["போதந் தரும்எங்கள் புண்ணிய நந்தியைப்","போதந் தனில்வைத்துப் புண்ணியர் ஆயினார்","நாதன் நடத்தால் நயனங் களிகூர","வேதந் துதித்திடப் போயடைந்தார் விண்ணே."],"p":"","r":"Those who placed our meritorious Nandi, who gives understanding, within their understanding became meritorious; with their eyes overflowing at the Lord's dance and the Veda praising, they went and reached the sky.","n":"போதம் (understanding) is played on: Nandi gives it, and is placed in it. விண் may be heaven or the space of Sivam the earlier verses speak of.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","sivam","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:10"},"t:143":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":143,"t":["மண்ணொன்று கண்டீர் இருவகைப் பாத்திரம்","திண்ணென்று இருந்தது தீவினைச் சேர்ந்தது","விண்ணின்று நீர்விழின் மீண்டுமண் ணானார்ப்போல்","எண்ணின்றி மாந்தர் இறக்கின்ற வாறே."],"p":"","r":"From one clay come two kinds of vessel, one that stays firm and one bound up with bad deeds; as the unfired returns to clay when rain falls from the sky, so people die past counting.","n":"The two vessels are most simply the fired and the unfired pot; line 2 (திண்ணென்று இருந்தது தீவினைச் சேர்ந்தது) can also mean that what seemed firm was itself joined to bad deeds, so that no body is truly fired. எண்ணின்றி may be 'without number' or 'without their thinking of it'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","nilaiyaamai","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:11"},"t:144":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":144,"t":["பண்டம்பெய் கூரை பழகி விழுந்தக்கால்","உண்ட அப் பெண்டிரும் மக்களும் பின்செலார்","கொண்ட விரதமும் ஞானமும் அல்லது","மண்டி அவருடன் வழிநட வாதே."],"p":"","r":"When the roof that held one's goods falls, worn with use, the women and children who ate from it do not follow; nothing goes along the road with the dead but the vows they kept and the knowledge they gained.","n":"The roof (கூரை) is the body, as in 146 and 161. விரதம் (vow, observance) and ஞானம் are the only companions named, not wealth or kin.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","sutram","gnaanam","thavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:11"},"t:145":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":145,"t":["ஊரெலாம் கூடி ஒலிக்க அழுதிட்டுப்","பேரினை நீக்கிப் பிணமென்று பேரிட்டுச்","சூரையங் காட்டிடைக் கொண்டுபோய்ச் சுட்டிட்டு","நீரினில் மூழ்கி நினைப்பொழிந் தார்களே."],"p":"","r":"The whole town gathers and wails aloud, takes away his name and calls him a corpse, carries him to the thorny burning ground and burns him, then bathes in water and stops thinking of him.","n":"The name is removed before the body: பேரினை நீக்கிப் பிணமென்று பேரிட்டு. The last act is not the burning but the forgetting (நினைப்பொழிந்தார்) that the bath completes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","sutram","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:11"},"t:146":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":146,"t":["காலும் இரண்டு முகட்டலகு ஒன்றுள","பாலுள் பருங்கழி முப்பத் திரண்டுள","மேலுள கூரை பிரியும் பிரிந்தால்முன்","போலுயிர் மீளப் புக அறி யாதே."],"p":"","r":"There are two posts, one ridge beam and thirty-two great rafters in the frame; the roof above comes apart, and once it has, the life cannot enter it again as before.","n":"The hut is the body: the two posts are the legs and the ridge beam is commonly taken as the spine. What the thirty-two rafters (பருங்கழி) stand for is not fixed; the ribs or the teeth are the usual guesses.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","uyir","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:11"},"t:147":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":147,"t":["சீக்கை விளைந்தது செய்வினை மூட்டிற்ற","ஆக்கை பிரிந்தது அலகு பழுத்தது","மூக்கினிற் கைவைத்து மூடிட்டுக் கொண்டுபோய்க்","காக்கைக் குப்பலி காட்டிய வாறே."],"p":"","r":"Sickness ripened, the deeds that were done ran out, the body came apart and its frame rotted; they put a hand to the nose, covered him, carried him off and set out an offering for the crows.","n":"சீக்கை and அலகு are unclear: சீக்கை is read as sickness or phlegm, அலகு as the beam of the frame. Death follows when the store of past deeds is used up (செய்வினை மூட்டிற்ற). The hand at the nose tests for breath; the crows' offering is the rice set out at the rites, or the body itself left to crows.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","vinai","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:11"},"t:148":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":148,"t":["அடப்பண்ணி வைத்தார் அடிசிலை உண்டார்","மடக்கொடி யாரொடு மந்தணங் கொண்டார்","இடப்பக்க மேஇறை நொந்தது என்றார்","கிடக்கப் படுத்தார் கிடந்தொழிந் தாரே."],"p":"","r":"He had food cooked and ate it, talked privately with his tender wife, said his left side ached a little, lay down, and lay there gone.","n":"The verse is a row of ordinary verbs ending in கிடந்தொழிந்தாரே, so that the death arrives as one more act of the day. இறை here means 'a little', not 'the Lord'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","yaakkai","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:11"},"t:149":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":149,"t":["மன்றத்தே நம்பி மாடம் எடுத்தது","மன்றத்தே நம்பி சிவிகைபெற் றேறினான்","மன்றத்தே நம்பி முக்கோடி வழங்கினான்","சென்றத்தா என்னத் திரிந்திலன் தானே."],"p":"","r":"In the public square the man raised a mansion, in the square he gained a palanquin and rode in it, in the square he gave away three crores; when the cry came that he had gone, he did not turn back.","n":"மன்றத்தே repeats three times: a whole public life of building, honour and giving. The palanquin may be a mark of honour, or, as in 153, the bier he rides at last. சென்றத்தா என்ன is unclear; it may be the mourners' cry 'he has gone, father'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","selvam","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:11"},"t:150":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":150,"t":["வாசந்தி பேசி மணம்புணர்ந்து அப்பதி","நேசந் தெவிட்டி நினைப்பொழி வார்பின்னை","ஆசந்தி மேல்வைத்து அமைய அழுதிட்டுப்","பாசந்தீச் சுட்டுப் பலியட்டி னார்களே."],"p":"","r":"With sweet talk they marry and live with that husband till their love is sated and they stop thinking of him; then they lay him on the bier, weep as is fitting, burn the bond in fire and make the offering.","n":"வாசந்தி in line 1 is obscure; it rhymes with ஆசந்தி (bier) in line 3, setting the wedding against the funeral. பாசம் may be the bond of love that is burned with him or simply the body. The verse makes the wife's mourning a matter of form (அமைய அழுதிட்டு).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","nilaiyaamai","pattru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:11"},"t:151":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":151,"t":["கைவிட்டு நாடிக் கருத்தழிந் தச்சற","நெய்யட்டிச் சோறுண்ணும் ஐவரும் போயினார்","மையிட்ட கண்ணாளும் மாடும் இருக்கவே","மெய்விட்டுப் போக விடைகொள்ளு மாறே."],"p":"","r":"The physician lets go the hand after feeling the pulse, the mind fails and the axle breaks, and the five who ate rice with ghee poured on it are gone; while the kohl-eyed wife and the wealth stay behind, this is how one takes leave and quits the body.","n":"The five (ஐவர்) are traditionally the five senses, fed well and the first to leave; the same five return in 188. அச்சற (the axle breaking) is read of the body as a cart. What stays (wife, wealth) and what goes (the man) are set side by side.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","nilaiyaamai","pattru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:11"},"t:152":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":152,"t":["பந்தல் பிரிந்தது பண்டாரங் கட்டற்ற","ஒன்பது வாசலும் ஒக்க அடைத்தன","துன்புறு காலந் துரிசுவர மேன்மேல்","அன்புடை யார்கள் அழுதகன் றார்களே."],"p":"","r":"The pandal has come apart, the storehouse has lost its fastenings, all nine gates are shut together; as the time of pain brings defilement on and on, the loving ones wept and went away.","n":"The nine gates (ஒன்பது வாசல்) are the body's nine openings, an image Tirumular uses again in 175. துரிசு (defilement, fault) may be the decay of the corpse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","sutram","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:11"},"t:153":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":153,"t":["நாட்டுக்கு நாயகன் நம்மூர்த் தலைமகன்","காட்டுச் சிவிகையொன்று ஏறிக் கடைமுறை","நாட்டார்கள் பின்செல்ல முன்னே பறைகொட்ட","நாட்டுக்கு நம்பி நடக்கின்ற வாறே."],"p":"","r":"The lord of the land, the chief of our town, mounts for the last time a palanquin of the burning ground; with the people following behind and drums beating in front, this is how the land's lord walks.","n":"Every mark of a royal procession is kept, the palanquin, the following crowd, the drums, but the palanquin is the bier (காட்டுச் சிவிகை). The verse lets the irony stand without comment.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","arasu","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:11"},"t:154":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":154,"t":["முப்பதும் முப்பதும் முப்பத் தறுவரும்","செப்ப மதிளுடைக் கோயிலுள் வாழ்பவர்","செப்ப மதிலுடைக் கோயில் சிதைந்தபின்","ஒப்ப அனைவரும் ஓட்டெடுத் தார்களே."],"p":"","r":"Thirty and thirty and thirty-six live in a temple with a well-built wall; once that walled temple fell to ruin, all of them alike ran off.","n":"30 + 30 + 36 makes ninety-six, the number of the body's constituents (tattvas) in a later Tamil count; that is how the verse is traditionally read, though it names no tattvas. The body is called a கோயில (temple) here, an idea Tirumular develops elsewhere.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","thaththuvam","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:11"},"t:155":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":155,"t":["மதுவூர் குழலியும் மாடும் மனையும்","இதுவூர் ஒழிய இதணம் தேறிப்","பொதுவூர் புறஞ்சுடு காடது நோக்கி","மதுவூர வாங்கியே வைத்தகன் றார்களே."],"p":"","r":"Leaving behind the woman with honeyed hair, the wealth and the house, he mounts the platform, and looking toward the common burning ground outside the town they lifted him, set him down and went away.","n":"இதணம் (a raised platform) is taken as the bier. மதுவூர வாங்கியே in line 4 is obscure; it echoes மதுவூர் in line 1 and may be corrupt.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","pattru","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:11"},"t:156":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":156,"t":["வைச்சகல் வுற்றது கண்டு மனிதர்கள்","அச்சக லாதென நாடும் அரும்பொருள்","பிச்சது வாய்ப்பின் தொடர்வுறு மற்றவர்","எச்சக லாநின் றிளைக்கின்ற வாறே."],"p":"","r":"Even seeing him set down and left, people think the rare wealth they seek will not leave them; those who chase it like madness grow weak without ever leaving off.","n":"The first line takes up வைத்தகன்றார் from 155. Lines 3 and 4 (பிச்சது வாய்ப்பின் தொடர்வுறு மற்றவர் எச்சகலா) are obscure, and this reading of them is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["selvam","pattru","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:11"},"t:157":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":157,"t":["ஆர்த்தெழு சுற்றமும் பெண்டிரும் மக்களும்","ஊர்த்துறைக் காலே ஒழிவர் ஒழிந்தபின்","வேர்த்தலை போக்கி விறகிட்கு எரிமூட்டி","நீர்த்தலை மூழ்குவர் நீதியி லோரே."],"p":"","r":"The kin who rise up wailing, the wives and the children, turn back at the edge of the town; after that they strip him, pile the wood and light the fire, then plunge headfirst into the water, people without justice.","n":"நீதியிலோர் (those without justice) is a harsh last word for the mourners; it may mean only that they do what custom requires and no more. வேர்த்தலை போக்கி is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sutram","nilaiyaamai","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:11"},"t:158":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":158,"t":["வளத்திடை முற்றத்தோர் மாநிலம் முற்றுங்","குளத்தின் மண்கொண்டு குயவன் வனைந்தான்","குடமுடைந் தால் அவை ஓடென்று வைப்பர்","உடலுடைந் தால்இறைப் போதும் வையாரே."],"p":"","r":"Over the whole wide land the potter shaped pots from the tank's clay; when a pot breaks people keep the pieces as shards, but when the body breaks they will not keep it even a moment.","n":"The broken pot is still worth something, the broken body nothing. Line 1 (வளத்திடை முற்றத்தோர்) is hard to construe.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:11"},"t:159":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":159,"t":["ஐந்து தலைப்பறி ஆறு சடையுள","சந்தவை முப்பது சார்வு பதினெட்டுப்","பந்தலும் ஒன்பது பந்தி பதினைந்து","வெந்து கிடந்தது மேலறி யோமே."],"p":"","r":"Five tufts, six braids, thirty joints, eighteen supports, nine pandals and fifteen rows: it lay burnt, and we know nothing beyond that.","n":"The numbers describe the body as a built thing, but what each stands for is not given and I can't fix it; tradition matches them to the elements, senses and other parts. The plain point is the last line: all this lay burnt, and what came after is unknown.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:11"},"t:160":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":160,"t":["அத்திப் பழமும் அறைக்கீரை நல்வித்தும்","கொத்தி உலைபெய்து கூழட்டு வைத்தனர்","அத்திப் பழத்தை அறைக்கீரை வித்துண்ணக்","கத்தி எடுத்தவர் காடுபுக் காரே."],"p":"","r":"They chopped fig fruit and good spinach seed, put them in the pot and cooked a gruel; when the spinach seed ate the fig, those who held the knife went to the burning ground.","n":"A riddle with no key in the verse. The fig and the seed are commonly taken as parts of the body, or as body and karma, and the gruel as the body's making; I can't fix the reference. Only the end, the cooks going to the burning ground, is plain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:11"},"t:161":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":161,"t":["மேலும் முகடில்லை கீழும் வடிம்பில்லை","காலும் இரண்டு முகட்டலக் கொன்றுண்டு","ஓலையான் மேய்ந்தவர் ஊடு வரியாமை","வேலையான் மேய்ந்ததோர் வெள்ளித் தளிகையே."],"p":"","r":"There is no ridge above and no eave below; there are two posts and one ridge beam; it is a silver dwelling thatched not by those who thatch with palm leaf but by a craftsman.","n":"The two posts and one beam repeat 146. வேலையான் may be the craftsman who made the body, taken as the Lord, and வெள்ளித் தளிகை (silver dish or dwelling) is unclear. Line 3 (ஊடு வரியாமை) is hard to construe.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:11"},"t:162":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":162,"t":["கூடம் கிடந்தது கோலங்கள் இங்கில்லை","ஆடும் இலையமும் அற்றது அறுதலும்","பாடுகின் றார்சிலர் பண்ணில் அழுதிட்டுத்","தேடிய தீயினில் தீயவைத் தார்க்களே."],"p":"","r":"The hall lies there but its adornments are gone; the dancing rhythm has ceased altogether; some sing and weep in tune, and they put it in the fire they gathered.","n":"The hall (கூடம்) is the body; the ceased rhythm (இலயம்) may be breath or pulse. தீயவைத்தார் plays on தீ (fire) and தீய (the bad thing), so the corpse becomes something unclean to be burned.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:11"},"t:163":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":163,"t":["முட்டை பிறந்தது முந்_று நாளினில்","இட்டது தானிலை ஏதேனும் ஏழைகாள்","பட்டது பார்மணம் பன்னிரண்டி ஆண்டினில்","கெட்டது எழுபதில் கேடறி யீரே."],"p":"","r":"The egg was born in three hundred days, though nothing was put there, poor people; at twelve years it knew marriage, at seventy it perished, and you do not see the ruin.","n":"The egg is the body, carried about three hundred days in the womb. இட்டது தானிலை ஏதேனும் is obscure: perhaps that no one can say who laid it. The verse compresses a whole life into three numbers.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","ilamai","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:11"},"t:164":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":164,"t":["இடிஞ்சில் இருக்க விளக்கெரி கொண்டான்","முடிஞ்ச தறியார் முழங்குவர் மூடர்","விடிஞ்சுஇரு ளாவது அறியா உலகம்","படிஞ்சு கிடந்து பதைக்கின்ற வாறே."],"p":"","r":"While the lamp bowl stays, he took away the flame; fools who don't know it is over cry aloud, and the world, not knowing that dawn turns to darkness, lies sunk and trembles.","n":"The bowl is the body and the flame the life. The taker (கொண்டான்) is not named; Death or the Lord both fit. The mourners are called fools for weeping over the bowl.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uyir","yaakkai","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:11"},"t:165":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":165,"t":["மடல்விரி கொன்றையன் மாயன் படைத்த","உடலும் உயிரும் உருவந் தொழாமல்","இடர்ப்படந்து ஏழா நரகிற் கிடப்பர்","குடர்ப்பட வெந்தமர் கூப்பிடு மாறே."],"p":"","r":"Those who do not worship the form of the one with spreading konrai blossoms, who made body and life, lie suffering in the seventh hell, where they cry out as their entrails burn.","n":"The konrai wearer is Siva. மாயன் may be another epithet of him (the wondrous one) or name Vishnu as maker; and the line may mean not worshipping him who made body and life, or not worshipping him with body and life. ஏழா நரகு is read as the seventh, lowest hell.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naragam","vazhipaadu","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:11"},"t:166":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":166,"t":["குடையும் குதிரையும் கொற்றவா ளுங்கொண்டு","இடையும்அக் காலம் இருந்தது நடுவே","புடையு மனிதனார் போக்கும்அப் போதே","அடையும் இடம்வலம் ஆருயி ராமே."],"p":"","r":"With umbrella, horse and victorious sword the time passed in between; when the man goes, at that moment the precious life reaches its place, left or right.","n":"The umbrella, horse and sword are the signs of rule, the life between birth and death. இடம் வலம் (left, right) is commonly taken as the bad or good destination the life reaches according to its deeds; it may also mean only that it goes wherever it goes. Lines 2 and 3 are hard to construe.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","arasu","uyir","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:11"},"t:167":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":167,"t":["காக்கை கவரிலென் கண்டார் பழிக்கிலென்","பாற்றுளி பெய்யிலென் பல்லோர் பழிச்சிலென்","தோற்பையுள் நின்று தொழிலறச் செய்தூட்டுங்","கூத்தன் புறப்பட்டுப் போன இக்கூட்டையே."],"p":"","r":"Once the dancer who stood in the skin bag, worked it to the end and fed it has left, what does it matter if crows carry off this nest, onlookers revile it, drops of milk are poured on it or many praise it?","n":"The dancer (கூத்தன்) is the life that moved the body; a theological reading makes it the dancing Lord. பாற்றுளி (drops of milk) is the milk poured in the funeral rites, and பழிச்சில் means 'if they praise', answering பழிக்கில் (if they revile). The body is called கூடு, a nest or cage.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","uyir","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:11"},"t:168":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":168,"t":["அருளும் அரசனும் ஆனையம் தேரும்","பொருளும் பிறர்கொள்ளப் போவதன் முன்னம்","தெருளும் உயிரொடும் செல்வனைச் சேரின்","மருளும் பினையவன் மாதவ மன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Before favour, king, elephants, chariots and wealth pass into others' hands, if with clear understanding, while life lasts, one joins the one who is true wealth, confusion ends; that is the great austerity.","n":"செல்வன் (the wealthy one) is taken as the Lord, the wealth that is not lost. அருள் at the head of the list is odd among worldly goods; it may mean a patron's favour. Line 4 (மருளும் பினையவன்) is hard to construe.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["selvam","nilaiyaamai","iraivan","thavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:12"},"t:169":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":169,"t":["இயக்குறு திங்கள் இரும்பிழப்பு ஒக்கும்","துயக்குறு செல்வத்தைச் சொல்லவும் வேண்டா","மயக்கற நாடுமின் வானவர் கோனைப்","பெயற்கொண்டல் போலப் பெருஞ்செல்வ மாமே."],"p":"","r":"Wealth that bewilders is like the moving moon with its great waning, and needs no more said of it; seek the king of the gods without confusion, and he becomes great wealth, like a rain cloud.","n":"The two images are paired: the moon that grows only to wane, and the rain cloud that pours. இரும்பிழப்பு is read as இரும் இழப்பு (great loss); வானவர் கோன் (king of the gods) is Siva here.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","nilaiyaamai","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:12"},"t:170":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":170,"t":["தன்னது சாயை தனக்குத வாதுகண்டு","என்னது மாடென்று இருப்பர்கள் ஏழைகள்","உன்னுயிர் போம்உடல் ஒக்கப் பிறந்தது","கண்ணது காணொளி கண்டுகொ ளீரே."],"p":"","r":"Seeing that even their own shadow is no help to them, the foolish still sit saying 'this wealth is mine'; your life will go, though it was born together with the body; see the light the eye sees.","n":"The shadow that cannot help its owner shows how little anything outside one is one's own. The last line (கண்ணது காணொளி) may mean simply 'see with your own eyes' or point to an inner light; both readings stand.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["selvam","pattru","nilaiyaamai","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:12"},"t:171":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":171,"t":["ஈட்டிய தேன்பூ மணங்கண் டிரதமும்","கூட்டிக் கொணர்ந்தொரு கொம்பிடை வைத்திடும்","ஓட்டித் துரந்திட்டு அதுவலி யார்கொளக்","காட்டிக் கொடுத்தது கைவிட்ட வாறே."],"p":"","r":"Bees find the scent of flowers, gather the honey and its sweetness and store it on a branch; the strong drive them off and take it, and this is how what was gathered was shown up and abandoned.","n":"The hoarder is the bee and the strong (வலியார்) are whoever takes the hoard: thieves, kings, heirs. The verse states the image and leaves the lesson to the reader.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","ivaral","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:12"},"t:172":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":172,"t":["தேற்றத் தெளிமின் தெளிந்தீர் கலங்கன்மின்","ஆற்றுப் பெருக்கிற் கலக்கி மலக்காதே","மாற்றிக் களைவீர் மறுத்துங்கள் செல்வத்தைக்","கூற்றன் வருங்கால் குதிக்கலு மாமே."],"p":"","r":"Be clear and settled, and once clear do not be troubled; do not churn about like a river in flood; turn your wealth away and get rid of it, and when Death comes you may even leap past him.","n":"மாற்றிக் களைவீர் மறுத்து may mean giving the wealth away or renouncing it; either way the hold on it is broken. குதிக்கலும் ஆமே (one may even leap) echoes the old phrase for escaping Death; whether that means not dying, or meeting death unafraid and free, the verse doesn't say.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["selvam","thuravu","kootru","thelivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:12"},"t:173":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":173,"t":["மகிழ்கின்ற செல்வமும் மாடும் உடனே","கவிழ்கின்ற நீர்மிசைச் செல்லும் கலம்போல்","அவிழ்கின்ற ஆக்கைக்கோர் வீடுபே றாகச்","சிமிழொன்று வைத்தமை தேர்ந்தறி யாரே."],"p":"","r":"The wealth and riches you delight in go like a boat on water that capsizes; people do not examine and see that a small casket has been set within the body that comes apart, as the means of release.","n":"சிமிழ் (a small casket) is unexplained; it is traditionally taken as the place within the body where the Lord is found, such as the heart. The verse contrasts the boat that sinks with a treasure already inside.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["selvam","nilaiyaamai","veedu","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:12"},"t:174":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":174,"t":["வாழ்வும் மனைவியும் மக்கள் உடன்பிறந்","தாரும் அளவு ஏது எமக்கென்பர் ஒண்பொருள்","மேவும் அதனை விரிவுசெய் வார்கட்குக்","கூவும் துணையொன்று கூடலு மாமே."],"p":"","r":"People say of their life, wife, children and brothers 'what limit is there to what is ours?'; to those who share out the bright wealth that comes to them, a helper who answers when called may come.","n":"Line 2 (அளவு ஏது எமக்கு) may be boasting ('our wealth has no limit') or worry ('what is enough for us?'). கூவும் துணை (the helper who answers a call) may be the Lord or the merit of giving.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["selvam","eegai","sutram","thunai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:12"},"t:175":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":175,"t":["வேட்கை மிகுத்தது மெய்கொள்வார் இங்கிலை","பூட்டுந் தறியொன்று போம்வழி ஒன்பது","நாட்டிய தாய்தமர் வந்து வணங்கிப்பின்","காட்டிக் கொடுத்தவர் கைவிட்ட வாறே."],"p":"","r":"Craving has grown great, and no one here holds to truth; there is one post to tie to and nine ways out; the mother and kin who set it up come and bow, and then, this is how they showed it up and abandoned it.","n":"The post (தறி) is the body where the life is tethered, the nine ways its openings, as in 152. The last line repeats 171's, so the kin who raised the body leave it as the strong leave the bees' hive. Why the verse sits in a chapter on wealth is unclear unless the body is the first wealth.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaa","yaakkai","sutram","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:12"},"t:176":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":176,"t":["உடம்போடு உயிரிடை விட்டோ டும் போது","அடும்பரிசு ஒன்றில்லை அண்ணலை எண்ணும்","விடும்பரி சாய்நின்ற மெய்ந்நமன் தூதர்","சுடும்பரி சத்தையுஞ் சூழகி லாரே."],"p":"","r":"When the life leaves the body and runs, there is no way to fight it; think of the Lord, and Death's true messengers, who stand ready to release, will not even think of burning you.","n":"அண்ணலை எண்ணும் can be a command ('think of the Lord') or describe those who do. The last two lines are hard to construe; the reading that Death's messengers spare the one who thinks of the Lord is the plainest but not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kootru","iraivan","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:12"},"t:177":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":177,"t":["கிழக்கெழுந்த் தோடிய ஞாயிறு மேற்கே","விழக்கண்டும் தேறார் விழியிலா மாந்தர்","குழக்கன்று மூத்தெரு தாய்ச்சில நாளில்","விழக்கண்டும் தேறார் வியனுல கோரே."],"p":"","r":"Even seeing the sun that rose in the east run its course and fall in the west, eyeless people do not grasp it; even seeing a young calf become an old bull in a few days and fall, people of the wide world do not grasp it.","n":"The two lines are built alike (விழக்கண்டும் தேறார்): the day and a life follow the same arc. விழியிலா (without eyes) is the blindness of those who see and do not understand.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilamai","nilaiyaamai","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:13"},"t:178":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":178,"t":["ஆண்டு பலவுங் கழிந்தன அப்பனைப்","பூண்டுகொண் டாரும் புகுந்தறி வாரில்லை","நீண்டன காலங்கள் நீண்டு கொடுக்கினும்","தூண்டு விளக்கின் சுடரறி யாரே."],"p":"","r":"Many years have gone by, and none have taken the Father to themselves, entered and known him; though the long ages give still longer time, they do not know the flame of the lamp whose wick is raised.","n":"தூண்டு விளக்கு (a lamp whose wick is pushed up to burn bright) is traditionally taken as the inner light, or the Lord within. The point stands either way: more time does not bring knowing.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ilamai","sivam","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:13"},"t:179":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":179,"t":["தேய்ந்தற் றொழிந்த இளமை கடைமுறை","ஆய்ந்தற்ற பின்னை அரிய கருமங்கள்","பாய்ந்தற்ற கங்கைப் படர்சடை நந்தியை","ஓர்ந்துற்று கொள்ளும் உயிருள்ள போதே."],"p":"","r":"Youth wears away and is gone at last, and after one's power for hard deeds is spent; so think on and hold to Nandi of the spreading locks into which the Ganga leapt, while life is still in you.","n":"The verb அற்ற (ended) runs through the first three lines: youth ended, strength ended, the Ganga's rush ended in his hair. The last line makes the urgency plain: உயிருள்ள போதே.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilamai","sivam","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:13"},"t:180":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":180,"t":["விரும்புவர் முன்என்னை மெல்லியன் மாதர்","கரும்பு தகர்த்துக் கடைக்கொண்ட நீர்போல்","அரும்பொத்த மென்முலை ஆயிழை யார்க்கும்","கரும்பொத்துக் காஞ்சிரங் காயுமொத் தேனே."],"p":"","r":"Before, tender women desired me as the juice pressed from crushed sugarcane; to those bejewelled women with breasts like buds, I who was like sugarcane am now like the bitter kaanjiram fruit too.","n":"The poet speaks of himself: the same man was cane and is now strychnine fruit (காஞ்சிரங்காய்) to the same women. The உம் in காயுமொத்தேன் may mean he was both in turn.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilamai","kaamam","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:13"},"t:181":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":181,"t":["பாலன் இளையன் விருத்தன் எனநின்ற","காலங் கழிவன கண்டும் அறிகிலார்","ஞாலம்கடந்து அண்டம் ஊடறுத் தான்அடி","மேலுங் கிடந்து விரும்புவன் நானே."],"p":"","r":"Seeing the times called child, youth and old man pass, people still do not understand; I lie at the feet of the one who passed beyond the world and cut through the cosmos, and desire them still more.","n":"ஞாலம் கடந்து அண்டம் ஊடறுத்தான் (who passed the world and pierced the cosmos) fits the tradition of Siva as the boundless column of fire. The verse turns from what others fail to see to the poet's own clinging.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilamai","nilaiyaamai","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:13"},"t:182":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":182,"t":["காலை ஏழுந்தவர் நித்தலும் நித்தலும்","மாலை படுவதும் வாணாள் கழிவதும்","சாலும்அவ் ஈசன் சலவிய னாகிலும்","ஏல நினைப்பவர்ககு இன்பம்செய் தானே."],"p":"","r":"For those who rise in the morning, day after day evening falls and the life span passes; though that Lord is hard to hold, he gives joy to those who think of him fittingly.","n":"சலவியன் is obscure: perhaps 'the changeful one', 'the one hard to approach' or 'the one who moves'. ஏல நினைப்பவர் may be those who think of him fittingly or those who think of him early.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","iraivan","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:13"},"t:183":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":183,"t":["பருவூசி ஐந்துமோர் பையினுள் வாழும்","பருவூசி ஐந்தும் பறக்கும் விருகம்","பருவூசி ஐந்தும் பனித்தலைப் பட்டால்","பருவூசிப் பையும் பறக்கின்ற வாறே."],"p":"","r":"Five thick needles live in one bag; the five needles are a flying beast; when frost falls on the five needles, the needle bag itself flies off.","n":"A riddle. The bag is commonly read as the body and the five needles as the five senses; பனித்தலைப் பட்டால் (when frost falls on the head) may be old age and grey hair. The four lines all begin பருவூசி, which may also play on பருவம் (season of life).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","ilamai","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:13"},"t:184":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":184,"t":["கண்ணதும் காய்கதி ரோனும் உலகினை","உண்ணின்று அளக்கின்றது ஒன்றும் அறிகிலார்","விண்ணுறு வாரையும் வினையுறு வாரையும்","எண்ணுறும் முப்பதில் ஈர்ந்தொழிந் தாரே."],"p":"","r":"People do not understand at all that the moon and the burning sun stand within and measure out the world; those bound for heaven and those bound by deeds alike are cut down at the counted thirty.","n":"கண்ணதும் is read as the moon, the other eye of the sky; yogic readings make sun and moon the two breaths. எண்ணுறும் முப்பதில் (at the counted thirty) may be the thirty days of a month or a span of years; I can't fix it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","kaalam","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:13"},"t:185":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":185,"t":["ஒன்றிய ஈரெண் கலையும் உடனுற","நின்றது கண்டு நினைக்கிலர் நீசர்கள்","கன்றிய காலன் கருக்குழி வைத்தபின்","சென்றதில் வீழ்வர் திகைப்பொழி யாரே."],"p":"","r":"Seeing the sixteen kalas joined and standing together, the base do not reflect; after harsh Death has put them in the pit of the womb, they fall into it again, their bewilderment not ending.","n":"The sixteen kalas (ஈரெண் கலை) are the moon's sixteen digits, full only for a moment before waning; in yoga they have an inner sense as well. கருக்குழி (the womb pit) names rebirth.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","kootru","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:13"},"t:186":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":186,"t":["எய்திய நாளில் இளமை கழியாமை","எய்திய நாளில் இசையினால் ஏத்துமின்","எய்திய நாளில் எறிவ து அறியாமல்","எய்திய நாளில் இருந்துகண் டேனே."],"p":"","r":"In the days you have, that youth may not pass in vain, praise him in song in the days you have; not knowing in the days I had what would be cast off, I stayed in the days I had and saw.","n":"எய்திய நாளில் (in the days one has reached) opens every line. எறிவது (what is thrown or cast) is unclear: the body, youth, or Death's blow. The object of கண்டேன் (I saw) is left unsaid, taken as the Lord.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ilamai","vazhipaadu","kaalam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:13"},"t:187":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":187,"t":["தழைக்கின்ற செந்தளிர்த் தண்மலர்க் கொம்பில்","இழைக்கின்றது எல்லாம் இறக்கின்ற கண்டும்","பிழைப்பின்றி எம்பெரு மானடி ஏத்தார்","அழைக்கின்ற போதுஅறி யாரவர் தாமே."],"p":"","r":"Even seeing that all that grows on the branch, with its spreading red shoots and cool flowers, comes to die, they do not without fail praise our Lord's feet; when they are called, they will not know.","n":"அழைக்கின்ற போது (when they are called) is the summons of death. அறியார் may mean they will not know what to do, or will not know him then, having not praised him before.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","iraivan","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:14"},"t:188":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":188,"t":["ஐவர்க்கு ஒருசெய் விளைந்து கிடந்தது","ஐவரும் அச்செய்யைக் காத்து வருவர்கள்","ஐவர்க்கு நாயகன் ஓலை வருதலால்","ஐவரும் அச்செய்யைக் காவல் விட்டாரே."],"p":"","r":"A field ripened for five, and the five keep guard over that field; because a summons comes from the master of the five, the five gave up guarding the field.","n":"The field is the body and the five are traditionally the senses, as in 151. The master of the five (ஐவர்க்கு நாயகன்) may be the life, or the Lord or Death who sends the ஓலை (palm-leaf summons); the verse does not say.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","uyir","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:14"},"t:189":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":189,"t":["மத்தளி ஒன்றுள தாளம் இரண்டுள","அத்துள்ளே வாழும் அரசனும் அங்குளன்","அத்துள்ளெ வாழும் அரசன் புறப்பட்டால்","மத்தளி மண்ணாய் மயங்கிய வாறே."],"p":"","r":"There is one drum and there are two cymbals, and the king who lives within is there; when the king who lives within goes out, the drum becomes clay and lies dazed.","n":"The drum is the body and the king the life. The two cymbals are not explained; the two breaths or the two legs are guesses. மண்ணாய் (becoming clay) recalls the clay pots of 143 and 158.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:14"},"t:190":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":190,"t":["வேங்கட நாதனை வேதாந்தக் கூத்தனை","வேங்கடத் துள்ளே விளையாடு நந்தியை","வேங்கடம் என்றே விரகுஅறி யாதவர்","தாங்கவல் லாருயிர் தாமறி யாரே."],"p":"","r":"Those who don't know the skill of calling it Venkatam do not know the precious life they could bear: the lord of Venkatam, the dancer of Vedanta, Nandi who plays within Venkatam.","n":"வேங்கடம் is the name of the sacred hill, but here it is traditionally broken as வேம் கடம் (the pot that burns, the body), so that Nandi plays within the body. விரகு (skill, the trick of it) suggests the name is a key to be understood. The verse is obscure and the wordplay reading is tradition's, not the verse's own statement.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","vedaantham","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:14"},"t:191":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":191,"t":["சென்றுணர் வாந்திசை பத்துந் திவாகரன்","அன்றுணர் வால் அளக் கின்ற தறிகிலர்","நின்றுண ரார்இந் நிலத்தின் மனிதர்கள்","பொன்றுணர் வாரிற் புணர்க்கின்ற மாயமே."],"p":"","r":"People do not know that the sun, travelling all ten directions, measures out their lives by its knowing; the people of this earth do not stop and perceive it: this is the illusion that holds them, though they know of perishing.","n":"The sun as the measure of life repeats 184. Line 4 (பொன்றுணர்வாரிற் புணர்க்கின்ற மாயம்) is hard to construe; it may say that maya binds even those who know they must die.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","maayai","kaalam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:14"},"t:192":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":192,"t":["மாறு திருத்தி வரம்பிட்ட பட்டிகை","பீறும் அதனைப் பெரிதுணர்ந் தாரிலை","கூறும் கருமயிர் வெண்மயி ராவது","ஈறும் பிறப்புமொ ராண்டெனும் நீரே."],"p":"","r":"No one has fully grasped that the strip of cloth, trimmed straight and bordered, will tear; you who see black hair turn white, say that end and birth lie within a single year.","n":"பட்டிகை (a strip, a border, or a written record) is taken as the body or the allotted life. The last line (ஈறும் பிறப்பும் ஓராண்டு) is unclear: perhaps that birth and death are as close as one year to the next.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","ilamai","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:14"},"t:193":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":193,"t":["துடுப்பிடு பானைக்கும் ஒன்றே அரிசி","அடுப்பிடு மூன்றிற்கும் அஞ்செரி கொள்ளி","அடுத்தெரி யாமற் கொடுமின் அரிசி","விடுத்தன நாள்களும் மேற்சென் றனவே."],"p":"","r":"For the pot with its ladle there is one measure of rice, and for the three hearth stones five firebrands; give out the rice before it burns too close; the days let go have already passed on.","n":"A riddle. The pot is commonly read as the body, the three stones and five brands as inner fires and the senses. On the plain reading the verse urges giving food while it can be given; a yogic reading is also traditional and I can't fix it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","eegai","undi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:14"},"t:194":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":194,"t":["இன்புறு வண்டிங்கு இனமலர் மேற்போய்","உண்பது வாச மதுபோல் உயிர்நிலை","இன்புற நாடி நினைக்கிலும் மூன்றொளி","கண்புற நின்ற கருத்துள்நில் லானே."],"p":"","r":"As a bee that delights here goes to clusters of flowers and drinks their scented honey, so with the state of the life: even if one seeks it with joy and thinks of it, the one who stands in thought beyond the eye of the three lights does not stay within.","n":"மூன்றொளி (three lights) are traditionally sun, moon and fire, and in yoga three centres of the body. Who does not stay (நில்லான்) is unclear: the life, which moves on like the bee, or the Lord beyond sight. The verse resists a confident reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["uyir","meyyunarvu","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:14"},"t:195":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":195,"t":["ஆம்விதி நாடி அறஞ்செய்மின் அந்நிலம்","போம்விதி நாடிப் புனிதனைப் போற்றுமின்","நாம்விதி வேண்டும் அதென்சொலின் மானிடர்","ஆம்விதி பெற்ற அருமைவல் லார்க்கே."],"p":"","r":"Considering how things are ordained to come, do good; considering how this earth is ordained to pass, praise the Pure One; if people ask why we need a destiny, it belongs to those able to win the rare gift of a good one.","n":"விதி (ordinance, destiny) runs through every line, set against what one does: ஆம் விதி (what will come) and போம் விதி (what will pass). Lines 3 and 4 are hard to construe and this reading is tentative.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aram","vazhipaadu","oozh"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:14"},"t:196":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":196,"t":["அவ்வியம் பேசி அறங்கெட நில்லன்மின்","வெவ்விய னாகிப் பிறர்ப்பொருள் வவ்வன்மின்","செவ்விய னாகிச் சிறந்துண்ணும் போதொரு","தவ்விகொ டுண்மின் தலைப்பட்ட போதே."],"p":"","r":"Do not stand speaking with envy so that virtue is ruined; do not turn harsh and snatch others' goods; be upright, and when you sit to eat well, give a ladleful first, when you have come into plenty.","n":"அவ்வியம் (envy) and செவ்வியன் (the upright) are the same pair the Kural sets side by side in 169. தவ்வி is a ladle; the gift asked is small and constant, given at every meal.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","azhukkaaru","vehkaamai","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:14"},"t:197":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":197,"t":["பற்றாய நற்குரு பூசைக்கும் பன்மலர்","மற்றோர் அணுக்களைக் கொல்லாமை ஒண்மலர்","நற்றார் நடுக்கற்ற தீபமும் சித்தமும்","உற்றாரும் ஆவி அமர்ந்திடம் உச்சியே."],"p":"","r":"In worship of the good guru one clings to, among the many flowers the bright one is not killing other small lives; with it go the good garland, the unwavering lamp and the mind, and the place where the life settles is the crown of the head.","n":"The verse turns the items of ritual worship (flower, garland, lamp) into inner ones, with not killing as the flower. அணுக்கள் may be small creatures or, in Siddhanta usage, souls. உச்சி is traditionally taken as the crown of the head where the yogi's awareness rests; a plainer reading of the last line isn't clear to me.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","vazhipaadu","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:15"},"t:198":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":198,"t":["கொல்லிடு குத்தென்று கூறிய மாக்களை","வல்லடிக் காரர் வலிக்கயிற் றாற்கட்டிச்","செல்லிடு நில்லென்று தீவாய் நரகிடை","நில்லிடும் என்று நிறுத்துவர் தாமே,"],"p":"","r":"Those brutish people who urged 'kill! stab!' are bound with strong ropes by Death's mighty servants, driven on with 'go! stop!', and made to stand in fire-mouthed hell.","n":"The guilt falls on those who said 'kill' (கூறிய மாக்களை), not only on those who struck; the commands of the guilty are echoed back to them by Yama's servants ('go', 'stop', 'stay').","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","kolai","naragam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:15"},"t:199":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":199,"t":["பொல்லாப் புலாலை நுகரும் புலையரை","எல்லாரும் காண இயமன்றன் தூதுவர்","செல்லாகப் பற்றித் தீவாய் நரகத்தில்","மல்லாக்கத் தள்ளி முறித்துவைப் பாரே."],"p":"","r":"Those vile ones who eat foul flesh are seized by Yama's messengers in sight of all, flung on their backs into fire-mouthed hell and broken.","n":"புலையர், a word for the lowest outcastes, is used here for flesh-eaters, so baseness is a matter of what one eats rather than one's birth. செல்லாக in the third line is obscure: 'as they go', or perhaps 'like a thunderbolt'; the sense of the verse doesn't depend on it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pulaal","naragam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:16"},"t:200":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":200,"t":["கொலையே களவுகள் காமம் பொய்கூறல்","மலைவான பாதகமாம் அவை நீக்கித்","தலையாம் சிவனடி சார்ந்தின்பம் சார்ந்தோர்க்கு","இலையாம் இவைஞானா னந்தத் திருத்தலே."],"p":"","r":"Killing, theft, lust and lying are sins as great as mountains; those who give them up, reach Siva's feet, which are the highest, and the joy there, have none of these, and that is the holy state of the bliss of knowledge.","n":"The chapter is on refusing flesh, but this verse widens it to a list of great sins in which killing comes first and flesh-eating is not named. The last line makes freedom from the sins a mark of the state reached, not only a precondition for it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kolai","kalavu","kaamam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:16"},"t:201":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":201,"t":["ஆத்த மனையாள் அகத்தில் இருக்கவே","காத்த மனையாளைக் காமுறுங் காளையர்","காய்ச்ச பலாவின் கனியுண்ண மாட்டாமல்","ஈச்சம் பழத்துக்கு இடருற்ற வாறே."],"p":"","r":"Young men who lust after another's guarded wife while their own wife is at home are like someone who won't eat the ripe jackfruit and toils for the fruit of the wild date.","n":"The image weighs effort against reward: a large, sweet fruit already at hand against a small fruit from a thorny palm. ஆத்த, of one's own wife, is an unusual form; it is usually taken as 'married' or 'one's own'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piranil","vaazhkkaithunai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:17"},"t:202":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":202,"t":["திருத்தி வளர்த்ததோர் தேமாங் கனியை","அருத்தமென் றெண்ணி அறையில் புதைத்துப்","பொருத்தம் இலாத புளிமாங் கொம்பேறிக்","கருத்தறி யாதவர் காலற்ற வாறே."],"p":"","r":"Those who don't grasp the point hide away the sweet mango they tended and raised as if it were a treasure, then climb the branch of an ill-matched sour mango and fall and break their legs.","n":"The sweet mango is the wife, kept locked in the house (அறையில் புதைத்து), and the sour tree another man's wife. The verse blames the man twice: for hoarding his own wife and for climbing elsewhere.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piranil","vaazhkkaithunai","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:17"},"t:203":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":203,"t":["பொருள்கொண்ட கண்டனும் போதத்தை யாளும்","இருள்கொண்ட மின்வெளி கொண்டுநின் றோரும்","மருள்கொணட மாதர் மயலுறு வார்கள்","மருள்கொண்ட சிந்தையை மாற்றகில் லாரே."],"p":"","r":"Even the man who has grasped the meaning, those who command knowledge, and those who stand holding the flash of light in the darkness are infatuated with women who bewilder, and cannot change a mind once bewildered.","n":"The three kinds of men are taken as the learned, the knowers and the yogis who see an inner light (இருள்கொண்ட மின்வெளி); that last phrase is a yogic term I can't fix. The verse speaks of women (மாதர்) in general, not only other men's wives, and its point is that attainment gives no protection.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","manam","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:17"},"t:204":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":204,"t":["இலைநல வாயினும் எட்டி பழுத்தால்","குலைநல வாங்கனி கொண்டுண லாகா","முலைநலங் கொண்டு முறுவல்செய் வார்மேல்","விலகுறு நெஞ்சினை வெய்துகொள் ளீரே."],"p":"","r":"As the fruit of the etti tree cannot be eaten however good its leaves and clusters look, do not let your heart, which ought to turn away, burn for women who smile and display the beauty of their breasts.","n":"The etti (strychnine tree) looks good and is poison, so the image is of an attractive surface over harm. The last line is unclear: வெய்துகொள்ளீர் may mean 'do not let it grow hot' or 'make it hard'; either way the heart is told to hold back.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu","adakkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:18"},"t:205":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":205,"t":["மனைபுகு வார்கள் மனைவியை நாடில்","சுனைபுகு நீர்போல் சுழித்துடன் வாங்கும்","கனவது போலக் கசிந்தெழும் இன்பம்","நனவது போலவும் நாடவொண் ணாதே."],"p":"","r":"For men who enter a home seeking the wife there, the pleasure swirls them down like water in a deep pool; it seeps up like a dream and cannot be had as things are had awake.","n":"Two images of the same pleasure: a whirlpool that drags one under, and a dream that leaves nothing. The grammar of the first two lines is loose, and I am not sure whether the whirling water is the pleasure or the woman.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piranil","kaamam","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:18"},"t:206":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":206,"t":["இயலுறும் வாழ்க்கை இளம்பிடி மாதர்","புயலுறும் புல்லின் புணர்ந்தவ ரேயினும்","மயலுறும் வானவர் சாரஇரும் என்பார்","அயலுறப் பேசி அகன்றொழிந் தாரே."],"p":"","r":"Women graceful as young she-elephants, though they cling like grass under a storm and say even the gods would be bewitched and bid a man stay near, turn to talk with strangers and are gone.","n":"The middle lines are hard to construe; I take மயலுறும் வானவர் as the women's boast that even gods are enchanted by them. The point that survives any construal is the turn to others (அயலுறப் பேசி) and the leaving.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:18"},"t:207":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":207,"t":["வையகத் தேமட வாரொடும் கூடியென்","மெய்யகத் தோடும் வைத்த விதியது","கையகத் தேகரும் பாலையின் சாறுகொள்","மெய்யகத் தேபெரு வேம்பது வாமே."],"p":"","r":"Joining with young women in this world, the lot set for the body, is sweet cane juice from the press in the hand and turns to bitter neem within the body.","n":"The contrast is in the place: sweet in the hand (கையகத்தே), bitter in the body (மெய்யகத்தே). The second line, மெய்யகத்தோடும் வைத்த விதியது, may mean that this is the fate attached to having a body; I am not sure of it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","yaakkai","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:18"},"t:208":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":208,"t":["கோழை ஒழுக்கம் குளமூடு பாசியில்","ஆழ நடுவர் அளப்புறு வார்களைத்","தாழத் துடக்கித் தடுக்ககில் லாவிடில்","பூழை நுழைந்தவர் போகின்ற வாறே."],"p":"","r":"Weak-willed conduct is like wading to the middle of a moss-covered pond to sound its depth; unless such men are tied down and held back, they go the way of those who crawl into a narrow hole.","n":"The moss-covered pond hides its depth, an image of danger under a smooth surface. பூழை is a narrow opening; it may be a plain trap image or a euphemism for the woman's body. The verse is obscure and my construal is tentative.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","adakkam","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:18"},"t:209":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":209,"t":["புடைவை கிழிந்தது போயிற்று வாழ்க்கை","அடையப்பட் டார்களும் அன்பில ராயினார்","கொடையில்லை கோளில்லை கொண்டாட்ட மில்லை","நடையில்லை நாட்டில் இயங்குகின் றார்கட்கே."],"p":"","r":"The cloth is torn, the living is gone, even those who depended on him have turned unloving; for those who drift about the land there is no giving, no receiving, no festivity and no standing.","n":"கோள் is taken as receiving, so the poor man is cut out of exchange both ways. நடை may be gait, standing or custom; I read it as standing among others.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai","sutram","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:19"},"t:210":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":210,"t":["பொய்க்குழி தூர்ப்பான் புலரி புலருதென்று","அக்குழி தூர்க்கும் அரும்பண்டம் தேடுவீர்","எக்குழி தூர்த்தும் இறைவனை ஏத்துமின்","அக்குழி தூரும் அழுக்கற்ற போதே."],"p":"","r":"You who, as each dawn breaks, hunt for scarce goods to fill the false pit, praise the Lord whatever pit you fill; that pit is filled only when impurity is gone.","n":"பொய்க்குழி, the false pit, is traditionally the belly, filled each day and empty again; it can also be read as the want that the body and its desires make. அழுக்கு is taken as the soul's impurity (மலம்). Both a plain and a spiritual reading of the pit stand.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["inmai","pasi","iraivan","malam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:19"},"t:211":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":211,"t":["கற்குழி தூரக் கனகமும் தேடுவர்","அக்குழி தூர்க்கை யாவர்க்கும் அரியது","அக்குழி தூர்க்கும் அறிவை அறிந்தபின்","அக்குழி தூரும் அழுக்கற்ற வாறே."],"p":"","r":"People seek even gold to fill the stony pit, and no one finds that easy; once one knows the understanding that fills it, the pit fills as impurity leaves.","n":"கற்குழி may be a pit dug in rock, so hard to fill, or a play on கல்வி. The verse repeats 210 but names knowledge rather than praise as the way.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","arivu","malam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:19"},"t:212":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":212,"t":["தொடர்ந்தெழு சுற்றம் வினையினுந் தீய","கடந்தோர் ஆவி கழிவதன் முன்னே","உடந்தொரு காலத்து உணர்விளக்கு ஏற்றித்","தொடர்ந்துநின்று அவ்வழி தூர்க்கலு ம் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"The kin who cling and follow are worse than one's deeds; before the life departs, light the lamp of awareness while in the body, keep to it, and that road can be closed.","n":"அவ்வழி may be the pit of 210 and 211 or the road of rebirth; கடந்தோர் in the second line is hard to construe. The claim that kin are worse than வினை is striking: kin hold one to the world as deeds do.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sutram","vinai","gnaanam","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:19"},"t:213":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":213,"t":["அறுத்தன ஆறினும் ஆனினம் மேவி","அறுத்தனர் ஐவரும் எண்ணிலி துன்பம்","ஒறுத்தன வல்வினை ஒன்றல்ல வாழ்வை","வெறுத்தனன் ஈசனை வேண்டிநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"The five senses, roaming like cattle over the paths, have inflicted endless suffering, and strong deeds have struck again and again; weary of such a life, he stood asking for the Lord.","n":"The first line is obscure: அறுத்தன ஆறினும் ஆனினம் மேவி may be cattle grazing in cleared fields or on the six paths. ஐவர், 'the five', is traditionally the senses. The chapter's poverty here becomes the soul's.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","vinai","idumbai","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:19"},"t:214":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":214,"t":["வசையில் விழுப்பொருள் வானும் நிலனும்","திசையும் திசைபெறு தேவர் குழாமும்","விசையும் பெருகிய வேத முதலாம்","அசைவிலா அந்தணர் ஆகுதி வேட்கிலே."],"p":"","r":"If steadfast Brahmins learned in the Veda pour the oblation, sky and earth, the quarters and the gods who rule them, and blameless great wealth all flourish.","n":"The verse makes the rite a support of the whole world. விசையும் in the third line is unclear to me; it may be 'force' or 'speed'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aaguthi","andhanar","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:20"},"t:215":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":215,"t":["ஆகுதி வேட்கும் அருமறை அந்தணர்","போகதி நாடிப் புறங்கொடுத்து உண்ணுவர்","தாம்விதி வேண்டித் தலைப்படு மெய்ந்நெறி","தாமறி வாலே தலைப்பட்ட வாறே."],"p":"","r":"The Brahmins who make the oblation seek the higher way, give to others before they eat, and reach the true path that the rule asks of them by their own understanding.","n":"புறங்கொடுத்து உண்ணுவர can be 'eat after giving to others' or 'turn their backs and eat'; போகதி is unclear. I take the first reading, but it is a choice.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aaguthi","andhanar","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:20"},"t:216":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":216,"t":["அணைதுணை அந்தணர் அங்கியுள் அங்கி","அணைதுணை வைத்ததின் உட்பொரு ளான","இணைதுணை யாமத்து இயங்கும் பொழுது","துணையணை யாயதோர் தூய்நெறி யாமே."],"p":"","r":"For the Brahmins, the fire within the fire, and the inner meaning set within it, become, when it moves in the paired hours, a pure path that supports them.","n":"அங்கியுள் அங்கி, the fire within the fire, is taken as an inner fire matching the outer one of the rite. இணைதுணை யாமம் may be the paired twilights or the paired breaths. I can't fix the sense.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aaguthi","andhanar","thooimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:20"},"t:217":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":217,"t":["போதிரண் டோ திப் புரிந்தருள் செய்திட்டு","மாதிரண் டாகி மகிழ்ந்துட னேநிற்குந்","தாதிரண் டாகிய தண்ணம் பறவைகள்","வேதிரண் டாகி வெறிக்கின்ற வாறே."],"p":"","r":"The verse speaks of reciting at two times and doing grace, of two that stand together in delight, and of cool birds that are two and grow heady; its sense is unclear.","n":"Every line turns on 'two' (இரண்டு). Pairs like the two breaths, the two daily rites or Siva and Sakti are possible keys, but the verse doesn't name one, and I won't choose.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aaguthi","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:20"},"t:218":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":218,"t":["நெய்நின்று எரியும் நெடுஞ்சுட ரேசென்று","மைநின்று எரியும் வகையறி வார்க்கட்கு","மைநின்று அவிழ்தரும் அத்தின மாம் என்றும்","செய்நின்ற செல்வம் தீயது வாமே."],"p":"","r":"For those who know how the tall flame fed by ghee reaches and burns the darkness, the darkness loosens that very day; and the wealth that lies in deeds becomes fire.","n":"மை can be soot, darkness or blackness of the self; taken as impurity, the flame of the rite burns the soul's dark. The last line may mean that wealth gained by deeds is fuel for the fire or that it is itself harmful (தீயது, 'fire' or 'evil').","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aaguthi","malam","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:20"},"t:219":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":219,"t":["பாழி அகலும் எரியும் திரிபோலிட்டு","ஊழி அகலும் உறுவினை நோய்பல","வாழிசெய்து அங்கி உதிக்க அவைவிழும்","வீழிசெய்து அங்கி வினைசுடு மாமே."],"p":"","r":"As the wick laid in a wide lamp burns, the many ills of deeds lasting through ages fall away when the fire is worshipped and rises; the fire burns up deeds.","n":"Deeds are called diseases (வினை நோய்), and the fire is their cure. வீழிசெய்து in the last line is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aaguthi","vinai","noy"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:20"},"t:220":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":220,"t":["பெருஞ்செல்வம் கேடென்று முன்னே படைத்த","வருஞ்செல்வம் தந்த தலைவனை நாடும்","வருஞ்செல்வதது இன்பம் வரஇருந் தெண்ணி","அருஞ்செல்வத்து ஆகுதி வேட்கநின் றாரே."],"p":"","r":"Knowing great wealth to be ruin, they seek the Lord who made and gave the wealth to come, and, reckoning on the joy it will bring, they keep to the oblation, the rare wealth.","n":"The verse sets two wealths against each other: the great wealth (பெருஞ்செல்வம்) that ruins and the rare wealth (அருஞ்செல்வம்) of the rite. Which wealth 'the wealth to come' is, worldly or the joy beyond, isn't clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["selvam","aaguthi","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:20"},"t:221":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":221,"t":["ஒண்சுட ரானை உலப்பிலி நாதனை","ஒண்சுட ராகிஎன் உள்ளத்து இருக்கின்ற","கண்சுட ரோன் உலகு ஏழும் கடந்த அத்","தண்சுடர் ஓமத் தலைவனு மாமே."],"p":"","r":"The Lord who is bright flame and without end, who as that flame dwells in my heart, the light in the eye who passes beyond the seven worlds, is also the cool flame that heads the fire offering.","n":"The verse joins three lights: the flame of the rite, the flame in the heart, and the light that passes beyond the worlds. 'Cool flame' (தண்சுடர்) is a deliberate paradox.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","aaguthi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:20"},"t:222":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":222,"t":["ஓமத்துள் அங்கியின் உள்ளுளன் எம்மிறை","ஈமத்துள் அங்கி இரதங்கொள் வானுளன்","வேமத்துள் அங்கி விளைவு வினைக்கடல்","கோமத்துள் அங்கி குரைகடல் தானே."],"p":"","r":"Our Lord is in the fire of the oblation, and in the fire of the funeral pyre as the one who takes its essence; another fire yields the sea of deeds, and another is the roaring sea itself.","n":"Four fires are named: ஓமம் (the rite), ஈமம் (the pyre), வேமம் and கோமம். The last two are obscure; கோமம் with the roaring sea may be the fire under the sea of Tamil tradition. My reading of lines 3 and 4 is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","aaguthi","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:20"},"t:223":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":223,"t":["அங்கி நிறுத்தும் அருந்தவர் ஆரணத்து","தங்கி இருக்கும் வகையருள்செய்தவர்","எங்கும் நிறுத்தி இளைப்பப் பெரும்பதி","பொங்கி நிறுத்தும் புகழது வாமே."],"p":"","r":"The austere ones who tend the fire, who granted the way of abiding in the Veda, set it everywhere; and the great Lord, rising, establishes them: that is their glory.","n":"The subject of the second half is unclear: the Lord may establish the fire or the ascetics. இளைப்ப is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","aaguthi","iraivan","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:20"},"t:224":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":224,"t":["அந்தணர் ஆவோர் அறுதொழில் பூண்டுளோர்","செந்தழல் ஓம்பிமுப் போதும் நியமஞ்செய்","தந்தவ நற்கரு மத்துநின்று ஆங்கிட்டுச்","சந்தியும் ஓதிச் சடங்கறுப் போர்களே."],"p":"","r":"Brahmins are those who keep the six duties, tend the red fire, keep the rules at the three times of day, stand in good works, recite the twilight prayers, and so cut away ritual.","n":"சடங்கறுப்போர் can mean those who complete the rites or those who end them; the second fits 232, where the rites are set aside, but the line doesn't settle it. The six duties are traditionally learning and teaching the Veda, sacrificing and having others sacrifice, giving and receiving.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["andhanar","aaguthi","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:21"},"t:225":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":225,"t":["வேதாந்தங் கேட்க விருப்பொடு முப்பதப்","போதாந்த மான பிரணவத் துள்புக்கு","நாதந்த வேதாந்த போதாந்த நாதனை","ஈதாந்தம் எனாதுகண்டு இன்புறு வோர்க்களே."],"p":"","r":"Brahmins are those who, eager to hear Vedanta, enter the Pranava in which the three states of knowing end, and see with joy the Lord who is the end of sound, of the Veda and of knowledge, without fixing any one thing as that end.","n":"The verse stacks technical 'ends': நாதாந்தம் (end of sound), வேதாந்தம் (end of the Veda), போதாந்தம் (end of knowing). முப்பதம் may be the three letters of Om or three states. ஈதாந்தம் எனாது, 'not saying this is the end', refuses to stop at any one of them.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["andhanar","vedaantham","piranavam","naadham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:21"},"t:226":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":226,"t":["காயத் திரியே கருதுசா வித்திரி","ஆய்தற்கு உவப்பர் மந்திரம் ஆங்கு உன்னி","நேயத் தேரேறி நினைவுற்று நேயத்தாய்","மாயத்துள் தோயா மறையோர்கள் தாமே."],"p":"","r":"The Vedic Brahmins are those glad to study the Gayatri and Savitri, who meditate on the mantra, ride the chariot of love into steady thought, and, become love itself, are not drowned in maya.","n":"நேயம் (love, devotion) appears twice: as the chariot one rides and as what one becomes. The verse makes devotion the means of reciting the mantra rightly.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["andhanar","anbu","maayai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:21"},"t:227":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":227,"t":["பெருநெறி யான பிரணவம் ஓர்ந்து","குருநெறி யாலுரை கூடிநால் வேதத்","திருநெறி யான கிரியை யிருந்து","சொரூபமது ஆனோர் துகளில்பார்ப் பாரே."],"p":"","r":"The faultless Brahmins are those who grasp the Pranava, the great path, join the word through the guru's way, keep to the rites of the four Vedas, and become one with the true form.","n":"சொரூபம், 'own form', is taken as the soul's true nature or Siva's; the verse doesn't say which.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["andhanar","piranavam","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:21"},"t:228":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":228,"t":["சத்திய மும்தவம் தானவன் ஆதலும்","எய்த்தரும் இந்தியம் ஈட்டியே வாட்டலும்","ஒத்த உயிர்கள் உண்டா யுணர்வுற்று","பெத்தம் அறுத்தலும் ஆகும் பிரமமே."],"p":"","r":"Truthfulness, austerity, becoming one with Him, wearing down the craving senses, knowing all lives as equal to oneself, and cutting off bondage: this is Brahman.","n":"The verse defines பிரமம், the Brahmin's own word, by conduct. தானவன் ஆதலும் could be 'becoming He' or 'being a giver'; I take the first. ஒத்த உயிர்கள் means all lives seen as alike.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vaaimai","thavam","adakkam","arul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:21"},"t:229":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":229,"t":["வேதாந்தங் கேட்க விரும்பிய வேதியர்","வேதாந்தங் கேட்டுந்தம் வேட்கை ஒழிந்திலர்","வேதாந்த மாவது வேட்கை ஒழிந்திடம்","வேதாந்தங் கேட்டவர் வேட்கை விட்டாரே."],"p":"","r":"Brahmins eager to hear Vedanta heard it and still kept their craving; but Vedanta is where craving ends, so those who have truly heard it have let craving go.","n":"The verse plays வேதாந்தம் against வேட்கை (craving): the end of the Veda is the end of wanting. Hearing that leaves craving intact wasn't hearing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vedaantham","avaa","andhanar"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:21"},"t:230":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":230,"t":["நூலும் சிகையும் நுவலிற் பிரமமோ","நூலது கார்ப்பாசம் நுண்சிகை கேசமாம்","நூலது வேதாந்தம் நுண்சிகை ஞானமாம்","நூலுடை அந்தணர் காணும் நுவலிலே."],"p":"","r":"Are the sacred thread and the tuft Brahman? The thread is cotton and the tuft is hair; the real thread is Vedanta and the real tuft knowledge, as thread-wearing Brahmins would see if they looked.","n":"The verse doesn't tell Brahmins to drop the thread; it says what the thread stands for. 241 goes further.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["andhanar","koodaozhukkam","vedaantham","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:21"},"t:231":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":231,"t":["சத்தியம் இன்றித் தனிஞானம் தானின்றி","ஒத்த விடயம்விட் டோ டும் உணர்வின்றிப்","பத்தியும் இன்றிப் பரன் உண்மை யின்றிப்","பித்தேறும் மூடர் பிராமணர் தாமன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Those mad fools without truth, without knowledge, without the sense that turns from the objects of the senses, without devotion and without knowing the Supreme are not Brahmins.","n":"Five absences are listed and none of them is birth, so the verse denies the name to the born Brahmin who lacks them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["andhanar","vaaimai","gnaanam","adakkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:21"},"t:232":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":232,"t":["திருநெறி யாகிய சித்தசித் தின்றிக்","குருநெறி யாலே குருபதம் சேர்ந்து","கரும நியமாதி கைவிட்டுக் காணும்","துரிய சமாதியாந் தூய்மறை யோர்க்கே."],"p":"","r":"For pure Brahmins, turiya samadhi is reaching the guru's feet by the guru's way, beyond the division of the conscious and the inert, and seeing once the rites and rules have been set aside.","n":"துரியம் is the fourth state beyond waking, dream and deep sleep; சித்தசித்து is the conscious and the inert. These are Siddhanta and Vedanta terms whose exact sense here I can't fix. The verse places the rites of 224 below the goal.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["andhanar","guru","samaadhi","thuravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:21"},"t:233":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":233,"t":["மறையோர் அவரே மறையவர் ஆனால்","மறையோர்தம் வேதாந்த வாய்மையினால் தூய்மை","குறையோர்தன் மற்றுள்ள கோலா கலமென்று","அறிவோர் மறைதெரிந்து அந்தண ராமே."],"p":"","r":"Brahmins are truly such when their purity comes from the truth of Vedanta; knowing that what lacks it is mere noise, they know the Veda and are Brahmins.","n":"The grammar of the second and third lines is loose; I take கோலாகலம் (noise, show) as the verdict on rite without Vedanta.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["andhanar","vedaantham","thooimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:21"},"t:234":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":234,"t":["அந்தண்மை பூண்ட அருமறை அந்தத்துச்","சிந்தைசெய் அந்தணர் சேரும் செழும்புவி","நந்துதல் இல்லை நரபதி நன்றாகும்","அந்தியும் சந்தியும் ஆகுதி பண்ணுமே."],"p":"","r":"The fertile land where Brahmins who wear cool kindness and meditate on the end of the Veda live does not decline and its king prospers; they make the oblation at dusk and dawn.","n":"அந்தண்மை பூண்ட echoes the Kural's செந்தண்மை பூண்டொழுகலான் (Kural 30), the edition's own parallel. Here the Brahmin's virtue benefits the land and king.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["andhanar","naadu","arasu","aaguthi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:21"},"t:235":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":235,"t":["வேதாந்த ஞானம் விளங்க விதியிலோர்","நாதாந்த போதம் நணுகிய போக்கது","போதாந்த மாம்பரன் பாற்புகப் புக்கதால்","நாதாந்த முத்தியும் சித்தியும் நண்ணுமே."],"p":"","r":"For those whose rule makes the knowledge of Vedanta shine, the course that approaches the awareness at the end of sound enters the Supreme beyond knowing, and so both release at the end of sound and powers are reached.","n":"Again the verse stacks Vedanta, நாதாந்தம் and போதாந்தம், as technical stages. It promises both முத்தி (release) and சித்தி (powers) without ranking them.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vedaantham","veedu","siththi","naadham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:21"},"t:236":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":236,"t":["ஒன்றும் இரண்டும் ஒருங்கிய காலத்து","நன்றும் இருந்தும் நலம்பல பேசினும்","வென்று விளங்கும் விகிர்தனை நாடுவர்","சென்று வணங்குந் திருவுடை யோரே."],"p":"","r":"When the one and the two are brought together, those blessed with grace, however well they live and however many good things they speak, seek out and go to worship the Lord who shines victorious.","n":"ஒன்றும் இரண்டும் is unexplained: it could be the soul and Siva, the self and its two bonds, or a number of breaths or syllables. The rest of the verse is clear: worship remains even for the accomplished.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","andhanar"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:21"},"t:237":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":237,"t":["தானே விடும்பற்று இரண்டும் தரித்திட","நானே விடப்படும் ஏதொன்றை நாடாது","பூமேவு நான்முகன் புண்ணிய போகனாய்","ஓமேவும் ஓர்ஆ குதிஅவி உண்ணவே."],"p":"","r":"When the two attachments fall away of themselves, the self is let go and nothing is sought; then Brahma on his flower, enjoying merit, receives the single oblation of Om.","n":"The two attachments are traditionally 'I' and 'mine'. The verse seems to make the giving up of self the true oblation, received by the gods; the third and fourth lines are hard to construe.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pattru","aaguthi","thuravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:21"},"t:238":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":238,"t":["கல்லா அரசனும் காலனும் நேரொப்பர்","கல்லா அரசனிற் காலன் மிகநல்லன்","கல்லா அரசன் அறம் ஓரான் கொல்லென்பான்","நல்லாரைக் காலன் நணுகநில் லானே."],"p":"","r":"An unlearned king and Death are alike, but Death is much the better: the unlearned king, blind to dharma, says 'kill', while Death does not come near the good.","n":"The comparison turns on discrimination: Death spares the good, the unlearned king doesn't.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","kallaamai","kootru","kolai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:22"},"t:239":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":239,"t":["நாள்தோறும் மன்னவன் நாட்டில் தவநெறி","நாள்தோறும் நாடி அவன்நெறி நாடானேல்","நாள்தோறும் நாடு கெடுமூட நண்ணுமால்","நாள்தோறும் செல்வம் நரபதி குன்றுமே."],"p":"","r":"If a king does not every day look to the way of austerity in his land and keep to it himself, the land declines daily, folly sets in, and his wealth shrinks day by day.","n":"நாள்தோறும் opens every line: the king's duty and the land's decline are both daily.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","thavam","naadu","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:22"},"t:240":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":240,"t":["வேட நெறிநில்லார் வேடம்பூண் டென்பயன்","வேட நெறிநிற்பார் வேடம்மெய் வேடமே","வேட நெறிநில்லார் தம்மை விறல்வேந்தன்","வேட நெறிசெய்தால் வீடது வாமே."],"p":"","r":"What use is the holy garb to those who don't live by it? For those who do, it is the true garb; and if a strong king makes those who don't live by it do so, that leads to release.","n":"It isn't clear whose release (வீடு) is meant: the false ascetics' once corrected, or the king's for correcting them.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["koodaozhukkam","arasu","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:22"},"t:241":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":241,"t":["மூடங் கெடாதோர் சிகைநூல் முதற்கொள்ளில்","வாடும் புவியும் பெருவாழ்வு மன்னனும்","பீடுஒன் றிலனாகும் ஆதலாற் பேர்த்துணர்ந்து","ஆடம் பரநூல் சிகையறுத் தால்நன்றே."],"p":"","r":"If those whose ignorance remains take up the tuft and sacred thread, the land withers and even a great king loses his standing; so, on reflection, it is good to cut off this showy thread and tuft.","n":"The verse doesn't name who cuts them off; in a chapter on kingship, the king is implied.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["koodaozhukkam","andhanar","arasu","naadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:22"},"t:242":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":242,"t":["ஞானமி லாதார் சடைசிகை நூல்நண்ணி","ஞானிகள் போல நடிக்கின் றவர் தம்மை","ஞானிக ளாலே நரபதி சோதித்து","ஞானமுண் டாக்குதல் நலமாகும் நாட்டிற்கே."],"p":"","r":"When those without knowledge put on matted hair, tuft and thread and act the part of the wise, it is good for the land that the king test them through the wise and bring them to knowledge.","n":"The remedy is correction, not only punishment: the king tests them through the wise and makes knowledge arise.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["koodaozhukkam","arasu","aaraaydhal","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:22"},"t:243":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":243,"t":["ஆவையும் பாவையும் மற்றுஅற வோரையும்","தேவர்கள் போற்றும் திருவேடத் தாரையும்","காவலன் காப்பவன் காவாது ஒழிவனேல்","மேவும் மறுமைக்கு மீளா நரகமே."],"p":"","r":"If the king, whose task is to protect cows, women, the virtuous and the holy ones the gods honour, fails to protect them, unending hell awaits him in the next world.","n":"பாவை (lit. doll, image) is taken as women. The punishment falls on the king himself, after death.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","naragam","marumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:22"},"t:244":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":244,"t":["திறந்தரு முத்தியும் செல்வமும் வேண்டின்","மறந்தும் அறநெறி யேஆற்றல் வேண்டும்","சிறந்தநீர் ஞாலம் செய்தொழில் யாவையும்","அறைந்திடில் வேந்தனுககு ஆறில் ஒன் றாமே."],"p":"","r":"If you want release and wealth, keep to the path of dharma without lapse even in forgetfulness; and of all that is produced in the well-watered world, the king's share, as is declared, is one sixth.","n":"The one-sixth share is the traditional royal tax. மறந்தும் means 'even by forgetting', that is, never. The two halves are loosely joined; திறந்தரு may be 'that gives strength' or 'excellent'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","arasu","veedu","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:22"},"t:245":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":245,"t":["வேந்தன் உலகை மிகநன்று காப்பது","வாய்ந்த மனிதர்கள் அவ்வழி யாநிற்பர்","போந்திவ் வுலகைப் பிறர்க்கொள்ளத் தாங்கொள்ளப்","பாய்ந்த புலியன்ன பாவகத் தானே."],"p":"","r":"When the king guards the world well, worthy people follow the same path; but one who comes to seize the world, for others or for himself, has the evil nature of a pouncing tiger.","n":"The second half is hard to construe: போந்திவ் வுலகைப் பிறர்க்கொள்ளத் தாங்கொள்ள may describe a king who lets others plunder or plunders himself. My reading is tentative.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","sengol","kodungol"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:22"},"t:246":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":246,"t":["கால்கொண்டு கட்டிக் கனல்கொண்டு மேலேற்றிப்","பால்கொண்டு சோமன் முகம்பற்றி உண்ணாதோர்","மால்கொண்டு தேறலை உண்ணும் மருளரை","மேல்கொண்டு தண்டஞ்செய் வேந்தன் கடனே."],"p":"","r":"It is the king's duty to punish those deluded people who drink liquor instead of drinking the nectar taken by binding the breath, raising the fire and holding the moon's face.","n":"கால் (breath), கனல் (fire) and சோமன் (the moon) are yogic terms: the breath held, the inner fire raised, and the nectar from the moon at the crown, traditionally. The verse sets the yogi's inner drink against toddy (தேறல்).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kal","arasu","oruththal"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:22"},"t:247":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":247,"t":["தத்தம்சமயத் தகுதிநில் லாதாரை","அத்தன் சிவன் சொன்ன ஆகம நூல்நெறி","எத்தண் டமுஞ்செயும் அம்மையில் இம்மைக்கே","மெய்த்தண்டம் செய்வதுஅவ் வேந்தன் கடனே."],"p":"","r":"Those who don't keep to the proper rules of their own faith suffer every punishment in the next life under the Agama law spoken by Siva; to punish them truly in this life is the king's duty.","n":"The verse divides punishment between the two worlds: Siva's Agama in the next, the king in this. Each faith is judged by its own rules (தத்தம் சமயத் தகுதி), not by one.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","oruththal","marumai","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:22"},"t:248":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":248,"t":["அமுதூறு மாமழை நீரத னாலே","அமுதூறும் பன்மரம் பார்மிசை தோற்றும்","கமுகூறு தெங்கு கரும்பொடு வாழை","அமுதூறுங் காஞ்சிரை ஆங்கது வாமே."],"p":"","r":"Through the great rain that flows with nectar, nectar-bearing trees spring up on earth, areca and coconut with sugarcane and banana; even the bitter kanjirai is fed by it.","n":"The bitter kanjirai (strychnine tree) in the last line may be named to show that rain feeds all alike, or the line may say the bitter tree too yields nectar. The verse doesn't judge between sweet and bitter.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mazhai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:23"},"t:249":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":249,"t":["வரையிடை நின்றிழி வான்நீர் அருவி","உரையில்லை உள்ளத் தகத்துநின் றூறும்","நுரையில்லை மாசில்லை நுண்ணிய தெண்ணீர்","கரையில்லை எந்தை கழுமணி யாறே."],"p":"","r":"Like a waterfall of rain descending from the mountain, my Father's clear river springs up within the heart, beyond words, foamless, stainless, fine and without banks.","n":"The rain becomes an image of the Lord's grace inside the heart; the yogic tradition may also read the mountain as the head and the stream as nectar from the crown. Both a devotional and a yogic reading stand.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mazhai","iraivan","thiruvarul","thooimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:23"},"t:250":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":250,"t":["ஆர்க்கும் இடுமின் அவரிவர் என்னன்மின்","பார்த்திருந்து உண்மின் பழம்பொருள் போற்றன்மின்","வேட்கை உடையீர் விரைந்தொல்லை உண்ணன்மின்","காக்கை கரைந்துண்ணும் காலம் அறிமினே."],"p":"","r":"Give to everyone without asking who they are; look out for others before you eat; don't hoard old goods; you who are hungry, don't hurry to eat alone; know the hour when the crow calls out and then eats.","n":"Five commands in four lines, closing on the crow that calls its flock to food, an old Tamil image of sharing. அவரிவர் என்னன்மின், 'don't say this one or that one', rules out choosing worthy recipients.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","virundhu","undi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:24"},"t:251":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":251,"t":["தாமறி வார்அண்ணல் தாள்பணி வார்அவர்","தாமறி வார்அறம் தாங்கிநின் றார்அவர்","தாமறி வார்சில தத்துவர் ஆவர்கள்","தாமறி வார்க்குத் தமர்பர னாமே."],"p":"","r":"Those who know themselves bow at the Lord's feet, stand upholding virtue and become the few who know the real; to such people the Supreme becomes their own kin.","n":"தாமறிவார் opens every line. It is read here as 'those who know themselves', but it can also be 'they who know (this)'. தத்துவர் is taken as knowers of the tattvas, the real constituents; தமர் (one's own people) makes the Supreme a kinsman rather than a distant judge.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aram","meyyunarvu","iraivan","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:25"},"t:252":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":252,"t":["யாவர்க்கு மாம்இறை வற்குஒரு பச்சிலை","யாவர்க்கு மாம்பசு வுக்கொரு வாயுறை","யாவர்க்கு மாம் உண்ணும் போதொரு கைப்பிடி","யாவர்க்கு மாம்பிறர்க்கு இன்னுரை தானே."],"p":"","r":"Anyone can give a green leaf to God, a mouthful of grass to a cow, a handful of food to another before eating, and a kind word to others.","n":"யாவர்க்குமாம், 'possible for all', is the verse's argument: each act is tiny, so no one can plead poverty. Worship, care of animals, sharing food and speech are set side by side as equal forms of virtue.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","eegai","inchol","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:25"},"t:253":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":253,"t":["அற்றுநின் றார்உண்ணும் ஊணே அறன்என்னும்","கற்றன போதம் கமழ்பவர் மானிடர்","உற்றுநின்று ஆங்கொரு கூவல் குளத்தினில்","பற்றிவந் துண்ணும் பயனறி யாரே."],"p":"","r":"Men fragrant with learned wisdom say that the food eaten by those who have nothing is virtue; those who do not feed them do not know the merit, as of one whose well or tank people come to and drink from.","n":"அற்றுநின்றார் can be the destitute or those who have cut off attachment, the renunciants. Lines 3 and 4 are compressed; the reading that the giver's merit is like that of one who dug a public well is traditional but not stated outright.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","undi","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:25"},"t:254":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":254,"t":["அழுக்கினை ஓட்டி அறிவை நிறையீர்","தழுக்கிய நாளில் தருமமும்செய்யீர்","விழித்திருந்து என்செய்வீர் வெம்மை பரந்து","விழிக்கஅன்று என்செய்வீர் ஏழைநெஞ்சீரே."],"p":"","r":"You who neither drive out your impurity and fill yourselves with knowledge nor do good while your days are full, what will you do when you lie awake as fever spreads, and what when your eyes no longer open, poor hearts?","n":"விழித்திருந்து and விழிக்க அன்று frame the deathbed: sleepless in the last fever, then unable to open the eyes. அழுக்கு (dirt) may be inner impurity in the sense of malam, but the verse does not use the technical word. தழுக்கிய நாள் is taken as days of plenty.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","nilaiyaamai","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:25"},"t:255":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":255,"t":["தன்னை அறியாது தான்நலன் என்னாதுஇங்கு","இன்மை அறியாது இளையர்என்று ஓராது","வன்மையில் வந்திடும்கூற்றம் வருமுன்னம்","தன்மையில் நல்ல தவஞ்செய்யும் நீரே."],"p":"","r":"Before Death comes in force, which does not ask whether you know yourself, whether you are good, whether you are poor or young, do good austerity in its proper way.","n":"The four negated verbs in lines 1 and 2 can describe Death, which takes no account of who one is, or the hearer, who goes on without knowing himself. Either way youth is no excuse for delay.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","kootru","nilaiyaamai","ilamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:25"},"t:256":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":256,"t":["துறந்தான் வழிமுதல் சுற்றமும் இல்லை","இறந்தான் வழிமுதல் இன்பமும் இல்லை","மறந்தான் வழிமுதல் வந்திலன் ஈசன்","அறந்தான் அறியும் அளவறி வாரே."],"p":"","r":"For one who has renounced there is no kin from then on, for one who has died no joy, and to one who has forgotten him the Lord has not come; those who know the measure of knowing know virtue.","n":"வழிமுதல் (from that point on) marks three things that end at a moment: kinship at renunciation, joy at death, the Lord's coming at forgetting. The last line can also be read 'virtue itself knows those who know the measure'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","aram","iraivan","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:25"},"t:257":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":257,"t":["தான்தவம் செய்வதாம் செய்தவத்து அவ்வழி","மான்தெய்வ மாக மதிக்கும் மனிதர்கள்","ஊன் தெய்வ மாக உயிர்க்கின்ற பல்லுயிர்","நான்தெய்வம் என்று நமன்வரு வானே."],"p":"","r":"Those who in their austerity honour the deer-bearing Lord as God are set against the many beings who live taking the flesh as their god; to them Yama comes, saying 'I am god'.","n":"மான் is taken as the deer Siva holds, so 'the deer-bearer' is Siva; line 1 is obscure. The plain point is ironic: those who serve the body get Death (நமன்) as the god who claims them.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","yaakkai","kootru","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:25"},"t:258":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":258,"t":["திளைக்கும் வினைக்கடல் தீர்வுறு தோணி","இளைப்பினை நீக்கும் இருவழி உண்டு","கிளைக்கும் தனக்கும் அக் கேடில் புகழோன்","விளைக்கும் தவமறம் மேற்றுணை யாமே."],"p":"","r":"There are two ways that end weariness and are a boat across the surging sea of deeds: austerity and virtue, which the Lord of unfailing renown makes fruitful, are the highest support for oneself and one's kin.","n":"கேடில் புகழோன் may be the Lord or the man of lasting fame who practises the two. The sea is of deeds (வினைக்கடல்), the stuff that keeps births going.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","aram","vinai","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:25"},"t:259":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":259,"t":["பற்றது வாய்நின்ற பற்றினைப் பார்மிசை","அற்றம் உரையான் அறநெறிக் கல்லது","உற்று உங்களால் ஒன்றும் ஈந்தது வேதுணை","மற்றண்ணல் வைத்த வழிகொள்ளு மாறே."],"p":"","r":"The Lord, the support worth clinging to, does not tell the secret of release on earth except to those on the path of virtue; whatever you have given will be your help, and that is the way to take the path he set.","n":"பற்றது வாய்நின்ற பற்று plays on பற்று (attachment, support) as Kural 350 does. அற்றம் can mean a secret, an end or release; line 3 (உற்று உங்களால் ஒன்றும் ஈந்தது வே துணை) is broken and the sense is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aram","eegai","pattru","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:25"},"t:260":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":260,"t":["எட்டி பழுத்த இருங்கனி வீழ்ந்தன","ஒட்டிய நல்லறம் செய்யா தவர்செல்வம்","வட்டிகொணடு ஈட்டியே மண்ணில் முகந்திடும்","பட்டிப் பதகர் பயன்அறி யாரே."],"p":"","r":"The wealth of those who do no clinging good is like the large fruit of the poisonous etti that ripens and falls; base wretches who heap it up by lending at interest and scoop it into the ground do not know what it is for.","n":"எட்டி is the strychnine tree, whose fruit looks ripe but cannot be eaten. ஒட்டிய நல்லறம் is taken as virtue that stays with one beyond death. Usury (வட்டி) is singled out as the way such wealth is gathered.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","eegai","aram","payan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:26"},"t:261":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":261,"t":["ஒழிந்தன காலங்கள் ஊழியும் போயின","கழிந்தன கற்பனை நாளுங் குறுகிப்","பிழிந்தன போலத்தம் பேரிடர் ஆக்கை","அழிந்தன கண்டும் அறம்அறி யாரே."],"p":"","r":"The ages have gone, aeons have passed, plans have run out and the days grow short; even seeing bodies, sources of great affliction, destroyed as if wrung out, they do not know virtue.","n":"கற்பனை may be imaginings and plans, or kalpa, the aeon, echoing ஊழி in line 1. பிழிந்தன போல (as if squeezed) gives the body as something wrung dry.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","aram","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:26"},"t:262":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":262,"t":["அறம்அறி யார்அண்ணல் பாதம் நினையுந்","திறம்அறி யார்சிவ லோக நகர்க்குப்","புறம்அறி யார்பலர் பொய்மொழி கேட்டு","மறம்அறி வார்பகை மன்னிநின் றாரே."],"p":"","r":"Those who do not know virtue do not know how to think of the Lord's feet and do not know even the outskirts of Siva's city; many of them, listening to false words, know only wrongdoing and stay fixed in enmity.","n":"மறம் here is the opposite of அறம், wrongdoing, not the martial valour it means in war poetry. The chain runs from not knowing virtue to not knowing the Lord: ethics comes first.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","sivam","pagai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:26"},"t:263":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":263,"t":["இருமலும் சோகையும் ஈளையும் வெப்பும்","தருமஞ்செய் யாதவர் தம்பால தாகும்","உருமிடி நாகம் உரோணி கழலை","தருமம்செய் வார்பக்கல் தாழகி லாவே."],"p":"","r":"Cough, wasting, phlegm and fever belong to those who do not do good; thunderbolt, snake, rohini and tumour do not come near those who do.","n":"The verse makes virtue a literal protection against illness and mishap. உரோணி is unclear: a disease of the throat in some lists, or the star Rohini as an evil influence.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","noy"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:26"},"t:264":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":264,"t":["பரவப் படுவான் பரமனை ஏத்தார்","இரவலர்க்கு ஈதலை யாயினும் ஈயார்","கரகத்தால் நீராட்டிக் காவை வளர்க்கார்","நரகத்தில் நிற்றிரோ நல்நெஞ்சி னீரே."],"p":"","r":"You do not praise the Supreme who is to be praised, do not give even a little to those who beg, do not water and raise groves with a pot; will you stand in hell, good-hearted ones?","n":"The groves (கா) are taken as flower gardens kept for worship. நல்நெஞ்சினீரே may be ironic or a sincere appeal to people capable of better. ஈதலை யாயினும் is unclear; read as 'even a little'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naragam","eegai","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:26"},"t:265":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":265,"t":["வழிநடப் பாரின்றி வானோர் உலகம்","கழிநடப் பார்நடந் தார்கருப் பாரும்","மழிநடக் கும்வினை மாசற ஓட்டிட்டு","வழிநடப் பார்வினை ஓங்கிநின் றாரே."],"p":"","r":"Those who stray from the path go back into the womb, while those who keep to it, driving off the stain of deeds, stand high; without walking the path no one reaches the world of the gods.","n":"The verse plays on வழி (path) and கழி (going astray). கருப்பு ஆரும் is read as entering the womb again; மழிநடக்கும் is obscure and the reading of lines 1 to 3 is a guess at the plain contrast.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ozhukkam","vinai","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:26"},"t:266":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":266,"t":["கனிந்தவர் ஈசன் கழலடி காண்பர்","துணிந்தவர் ஈசன் துறக்கமது ஆள்வர்","மலிந்தவர் மாளும் துணையும்ஒன் றின்றி","மெலிந்த சினத்தினுள் வீழ்ந்தொழிந் தாரே."],"p":"","r":"Those who have ripened see the Lord's feet, the resolute rule his heaven, but the swollen-headed die without a single support, fallen into a withering anger.","n":"கனிந்தவர் (ripened, softened) is commonly taken as those mellowed in love, துணிந்தவர் as those who boldly renounced, மலிந்தவர் as those swollen with wealth or pride. மெலிந்த சினம் is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","veedu","vekuli"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:26"},"t:267":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":267,"t":["இன்பம் இடரென்று இரண்டுற வைத்தது","முன்பவர் செய்கையி னாலே முடிந்தது","இன்பம் அதுகண்டும் ஈகிலாப் பேதைகள்","அன்பிலார் சிந்தை அறமறி யாரே."],"p":"","r":"Joy and sorrow, the two, are settled by what one did before; fools who see joy and still do not give, loveless in mind, do not know virtue.","n":"The argument is from cause: present joy is the fruit of past good, so withholding now spends it without renewal. Lack of giving is traced to lack of love (அன்பிலார்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","eegai","inbam","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:26"},"t:268":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":268,"t":["கெடுவதும் ஆவதும் கேடில் புகழோன்","நடுவல்ல செய்து இன்பம் நாடவும் ஒட்டான்","இடுவதும் ஈவதும் எண்ணுமின் இன்பம்","படுவது செய்யின் பசுவது வாமே."],"p":"","r":"The Lord of unfailing renown decides ruin and gain and will not let you seek pleasure by doing what is unfair; think of offering and giving, and if you do what brings joy it will be like a cow that yields.","n":"இடுவதும் ஈவதும் may be offering (to God or fire) and giving (to people). பசுவது வாமே is obscure: a cow that keeps giving milk, or பசு in the Siddhanta sense of the bound soul.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naduvu","eegai","iraivan","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:26"},"t:269":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":269,"t":["செல்வம் கருதிச் சிலர்பலர் வாழ்வெனும்","புல்லறி வாளரைப் போற்றிப் புலராமல்","இல்லங் கருதி இறைவனை ஏத்துமின்","வில்லி இலக்கெய்த விற்குறி யாமே."],"p":"","r":"Do not wither praising petty-minded men for their wealth in hope of a living; with your true home in mind, praise the Lord, as an archer fixes his mark to hit the target.","n":"இல்லம் கருதி may mean thinking of one's household or of the final home with the Lord. Line 1 (சிலர்பலர் வாழ்வெனும்) is compressed. The archer image asks for a single aim.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pullarivu","selvam","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:26"},"t:270":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":270,"t":["அன்பு சிவம் இரண்டு என்பர் அறிவிலார்","அன்பே சிவமாவது ஆரும் அறிகிலார்","அன்பே சிவமாவது ஆரும் அறிந்தபின்","அன்பே சிவமாய் அமர்ந்திருந் தாரே."],"p":"","r":"The ignorant say love and Siva are two; no one knows that love itself is Siva; once they know it, they rest as love that is Siva.","n":"அன்பே சிவம் is the verse's refrain and one of the best-known lines in Tamil. The last line makes knowing it transformative: those who know do not merely hold a doctrine but sit (அமர்ந்திருந்தார்) as that love.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","sivam","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:27"},"t:271":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":271,"t":["பொன்னைக் கடந்திலங் கும்புலித் தோலினன்","மின்னிக் கிடந்து மிளிரும் இளம்பிறை","துன்னிக் கிடந்த சுடுபொடி யாடிக்குப்","பின்னிக் கிடந்ததென் பேரன்பு தானே."],"p":"","r":"My great love is entwined with the one who wears the tiger skin that outshines gold, with the young crescent gleaming like lightning, who bathes in the ash heaped close about him.","n":"The tiger skin, crescent and burning-ground ash (சுடுபொடி) are Siva's standard marks. கிடந்து runs through the lines, so the love lies close to him as the skin, moon and ash do.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:27"},"t:272":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":272,"t":["என்பே விறகா இறைச்சி அறுத்திட்டுப்","பொன்போற் கனலிற் பெரிய வறுப்பினும்","அன்போடு உருகி அகங்குழை வார்க்கன்றி","என்போல் மணியினை எய்தஒண் ணாதே."],"p":"","r":"Even if one makes one's bones the firewood, cuts one's flesh and fries it in the fire till it glows like gold, none but those who melt with love and soften within can reach my gem.","n":"The extreme of bodily austerity is set aside in favour of love that melts (உருகி) and softens (குழை). என்போல் மணி is unclear: 'my gem' or 'the gem that is like me'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","thavam","sivam","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:27"},"t:273":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":273,"t":["ஆர்வம் உடையவர் காண்பார் அரன்தன்னை","ஈரம் உடையவர் காண்பார் இணையடி","பாரம் உடையவர் காண்பார் பவந்தன்னைக்","கோர நெறிகொடு கொங்குபுக் காரே."],"p":"","r":"Those with longing see Hara, those with tenderness see his feet, but those who carry a burden see only birth, as if they had taken a harsh road into Kongu.","n":"ஆர்வம், ஈரம், பாரம் rhyme as three states of the heart; பாரம் is taken as the weight of attachment or deeds, and பவம் as birth. Line 4 (கோர நெறிகொடு கொங்குபுக்காரே) is obscure: கொங்கு may be the Kongu country, or honey.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anbu","piravi","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:27"},"t:274":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":274,"t":["என்அன் புருக்கி இறைவனை ஏத்துமின்","முன்அன் புருக்கி முதல்வனை நாடுமின்","பின்அன் புருக்கிப் பெருந்தகை நந்தியும்","தன்அன் பெனக்கே தலைநின்ற வாறே."],"p":"","r":"Melt your love and praise the Lord; melt your love first and seek the First One; when I later melted with love, great Nandi made his love for me stand foremost.","n":"என் அன்பு, முன் அன்பு, பின் அன்பு, தன் அன்பு move from the hearer's love to the Lord's answering love. Nandi is Tirumular's teacher and also a name of Siva; the verse need not choose.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","vazhipaadu","guru","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:27"},"t:275":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":275,"t":["தானொரு காலம் சயம்பு என் றேத்தினும்","வானொரு காலம் வழித்துணை யாய்நிற்கும்","தேனொரு பால்திகழ் கொன்றை அணிசிவன்","தானொரு வண்ணம்என் அன்பில்நின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"Even if one praises him only once as the self-born, he stands then as a companion on the way to heaven; Siva, wearing the konrai with honey on one side, stood in my love in a way all his own.","n":"ஒரு runs through the lines (ஒரு காலம், ஒரு பால், ஒரு வண்ணம்): a single praise is enough to make him a companion. சயம்பு is svayambhu, the self-born.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","sivam","vazhipaadu","thunai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:27"},"t:276":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":276,"t":["முன்படைத்து இன்பம் படைத்த முதலிடை","அன்புஅடைத்து எம்பெரு மானை அறிகிலார்","வன்படைத்து இந்த அகலிடம் வாழ்வினில்","அன்புஅடைத் தான்தன் அகலிடத் தானே."],"p":"","r":"People do not know how to hold with love our Lord, the First who first created and created joy; yet he who locked love into life in this wide world stays in his own wide realm.","n":"அடைத்து (locking in, enclosing) is used twice: people fail to enclose the Lord in love, while he enclosed love in the world. வன்படைத்து and the second அகலிடம் are unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anbu","iraivan","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:27"},"t:277":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":277,"t":["கருத்துறு செம்பொன்செய் காய்கதிர்ச் சோதி","இருத்தியும் வைத்தும் இறைவன் என்று ஏத்தியும்","அருத்தியுள் ஈசனை யாரருள் வேண்டில்","விருத்தி கொடுத்திடும் விண்ணவர் கோனே."],"p":"","r":"If one sets in the mind the Lord who shines like the sun of fine red gold, keeps him there, praises him as Lord and seeks his grace with longing, the king of the gods grants increase.","n":"அருத்தி is longing or devotion. விருத்தி can be growth, livelihood or, in later usage, growth in grace; the verse does not say which.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","vazhipaadu","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:27"},"t:278":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":278,"t":["நித்தலும் துஞ்சும் பிறப்பையும் செய்தவன்","வைத்த பரிசு அறிந் தேயும் மனிதர்கள்","இச்சையு ளேவைப்பர் எந்தை பிரானென்று","நச்சியே அண்ணலை நாடுகி லாரே."],"p":"","r":"Though they know the order set by the one who made daily sleep and birth, men keep him within their wants, calling him 'our father, our Lord', and do not seek him with longing.","n":"நித்தலும் துஞ்சும் பிறப்பு may be 'daily sleep and birth' or 'the birth that daily dies'; sleep as a small death is an old figure (Kural 339). The complaint is that people call on the Lord for what they want (இச்சை) rather than out of love for him (நச்சி).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anbu","avaa","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:27"},"t:279":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":279,"t":["அன்பின் உள் ளான்புறத்தான் உட லாயுளான்","முன்பின்உள் ளான்முனி வர்க்கும் பிரானவன்","அன்பின்உள் ளாகி அமரும் அரும்பொருள்","அன்பினுள் ளார்க்கே அணைதுணை யாமே."],"p":"","r":"He is within love and outside it, within the body, before and after, lord even of the sages; the rare being who dwells within love is a close support only to those who dwell in love.","n":"அன்பின் உள் occurs four times. The Lord is everywhere (inside, outside, before, after), yet his nearness as support (அணைதுணை) is given only to those in love.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","sivam","thunai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:27"},"t:280":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":280,"t":["இகழ்ந்ததும் பெற்றதும் ஈசன் அறியும்","உகந்தருள் செய்திடும் உத்தம நாதன்","கொழுந்தன்பு செய்தருள் கூரவல் லார்க்கு","மகிழ்ந்தன்பு செய்யும் அருளது வாமே."],"p":"","r":"The Lord knows what we have spurned and what we have gained; the excellent Lord gives grace gladly, and to those who can love with tender, fresh love so that grace grows, his grace returns their love with delight.","n":"கொழுந்தன்பு is love like a young shoot, fresh and growing. The chapter's title idea opens here: the Lord knows (அறியும்), so love is not unseen.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","anbu","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:28"},"t:281":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":281,"t":["இன்பப் பிறவிக்கு இயல்வது செய்தவன்","துன்பப் பிறவித் தொழில்பல என்னினும்","அன்பிற் கலவிசெய்து ஆதிப் பிரான்வைத்த","முன்பிப் பிறவி முடிவது தானே."],"p":"","r":"Though the Lord made what suits a joyful birth and the toils of a sorrowful birth are many, this birth that the First Lord ordained by mingling in love is where births end.","n":"அன்பிற் கலவிசெய்து (mingling in love) and முன்பு இப்பிறவி are compressed. One reading is that a birth lived in love with the Lord is the last birth; முடிவது may also mean simply 'is fulfilled'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","anbu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:28"},"t:282":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":282,"t":["அன்புறு சிந்தையின் மேலெழும் அவ்வொளி","இன்புறு கண்ணியொடு ஏற்க இசைந்தன","துன்புறு கண்ணி ஐந் தாடும் துடக்கற்று","நண்புறு சிந்தையை நாடுமின் நீரே."],"p":"","r":"The light that rises above a loving mind consents to accept the joyful garland; free of the tangle of the five sorrowful snares, seek the mind that is friendly to him.","n":"கண்ணி means both garland and snare, and the verse uses both: the joyful garland of love offered to him, and the five snares that dance, traditionally the senses.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anbu","manam","pattru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:28"},"t:283":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":283,"t":["புணர்ச்சியுள் ஆயிழை மேல்அன்பு போல","உணர்ச்சியுள் ஆங்கே ஒடுங்கவல் லாருக்கு","உணர்ச்சியில் லாது குலாவி உலாவி","அணைத்தலும் இன்பம் அதுவிது வாமே."],"p":"","r":"For those who can subside into feeling as love settles on a woman in union, the joy of embracing him, delighting and moving beyond awareness, is like that.","n":"Sexual union is the image for union with the Lord. உணர்ச்சியில்லாது (without awareness) is taken as loss of separate self-awareness. அதுவிது (that is this) leaves open which joy is the measure of the other.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","anbu","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:28"},"t:284":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":284,"t":["உற்றுநின் றாரொடும் அத்தகு சோதியைச்","சித்தர்கள் என்றும் தெரிந்தறி வாரில்லை","பத்திமை யாலே பணிந்தடி யார்தொழ","முத்தி கொடுத்தவர் முன்புநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"The siddhas, even with those close to him, have never discerned that fitting light; but to devotees who bowed and worshipped him in devotion he stood before them and gave release.","n":"பத்திமை is bhakti, devotion. The contrast is pointed in a book that elsewhere honours siddhas: attainment does not find him, devotion does. உற்றுநின்றாரொடும் is unclear.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","siththi","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:28"},"t:285":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":285,"t":["கண்டேன் கமழ்தரு கொன்றையி னான்அடி","கண்டேன் கரியுரி யான்தன் கழலிணை","கண்டேன் கமல மலர்உறை வானடி","கண்டேன் கழலதென் அன்பினுள் யானே."],"p":"","r":"I saw the feet of him with fragrant konrai, the twin anklets of him who wears the elephant's hide, the feet of him who dwells in the lotus; I saw the anklet within my love.","n":"கண்டேன் opens every line. The one dwelling in the lotus is usually Brahma, but here may be Siva in the heart-lotus; the last line places the vision inside the poet's love.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:28"},"t:286":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":286,"t":["நம்பனை நானா விதப்பொரு ளாகுமென்று","உம்பரில் வானவர் ஓதுந் தலைவனை","இன்பனை இன்பத் திடைநின்று இரதிக்கும்","அன்பனை யாரும் அறியகி லாரே."],"p":"","r":"No one knows the Lover: Nambhan, whom the gods above hail as the leader who becomes things of many kinds, the joyful one who abides in joy and relishes it.","n":"அன்பன் can be 'the one who loves' or 'the one loved'. இரதிக்கும் (relishes) gives the Lord himself as enjoying joy.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","anbu","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:28"},"t:287":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":287,"t":["முன்பு பிறப்பும் இறப்பும் அறியாதார்","அன்பில் இறைவனை யாம்அறி வோம்என்பர்","இன்பப் பிறப்பும் இறப்பும் இலான்நந்தி","அன்பில் அவனை அறியகி லாரே."],"p":"","r":"Those who do not know their past births and deaths say 'we know the Lord through love', but they cannot know in love Nandi, who is joy and has no birth or death.","n":"The verse sets the claimants' bondage to birth and death against Nandi's freedom from it. Whether it rejects their claim outright or says love alone is not enough without knowledge of bondage is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anbu","piravi","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:28"},"t:288":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":288,"t":["ஈசன் அறியும் இராப்பக லுந்தன்னைப்","பாசத்துள் வைத்துப் பரிவுசெய் வார்களைத்","தேசுற்று இருந்து செயலற் றிருந்திடில்","ஈசன்வந்து எம்மிடை ஈட்டிநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"The Lord knows those who night and day keep themselves in the bond and show him tender care; if they abide radiant and cease from action, the Lord comes and stands gathered among us.","n":"பாசத்துள் வைத்து may mean in the bond of love, or, in Siddhanta language, while still in the fetters (பாசம்) that bind the soul. செயலற்றிருந்திடில் (becoming actionless) is taken as surrender or yogic stillness.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anbu","sivam","adakkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:28"},"t:289":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":289,"t":["விட்டுப் பிடிப்பதென் மேதகு சோதியைத்","தொட்டுத் தொடர்வன் தொலையாப் பெருமையை","எட்டும் என் ஆருயி ராய்நின்ற ஈசனை","மட்டுக் கலப்பது மஞ்சனம் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"Why let go of the excellent light and then grasp it again? I will hold to his endless greatness and follow it; to mingle in honey with the Lord who stands as my life is the sacred bath.","n":"மஞ்சனம் is the ritual bathing of the image; the verse makes inner union the true ablution. மட்டு may be honey (of love) or measure, giving 'mingling without measure'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anbu","vazhipaadu","sivam","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:28"},"t:290":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":290,"t":["குறிப்பறிந் தேன்உடல் உயிரது கூடிச்","செறிப்பறிந் தேன்மிகு தேவர் பிரானை","மறிப்பறி யாதுவந்துஉள்ளம் புகுந்தான்","கறிப்பறி யாமிகுங் கல்விகற் றேனே."],"p":"","r":"I came to know the sign by which body and life are joined and how to hold fast the Lord of the gods; he came unhindered and entered my heart, and I learned a learning that surpasses.","n":"குறிப்பு (sign, hidden meaning) is read as the secret of how life holds to the body, which tradition takes yogically. கறிப்பறியா in line 4 is obscure; it may mean learning that knows no lack or no bitterness.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","sivam","uyir","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:29"},"t:291":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":291,"t":["கற்றறி வாளர் கருதிய காலத்துக்","கற்றறி வாளர் கருத்திலோர் கண்ணுண்டு","கற்றறி வாளர் கருதி உரைசெய்யுங்","கற்றறி காட்டக் கயல்உள வாக்குமே."],"p":"","r":"When the learned reflect, there is an eye within their thought, and what they say after reflecting, their learning reveals, gives clear sight.","n":"கற்றறிவாளர் repeats in three lines. Line 4 (கற்றறி காட்டக் கயல் உளவாக்குமே) is obscure; கயல் (carp) is a stock comparison for the eye, so the line may say learning makes seeing eyes.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:29"},"t:292":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":292,"t":["நிற்கின்ற போதே நிலையுடை யான்கழல்","கற்கின்ற செய்மின் கழிந்தறும் பாவங்கள்","சொற்குன்றல் இன்றித் தொழுமின் தொழுதபின்","மற்றொன்று இலாத மணிவிளக் காமே."],"p":"","r":"While you still stand, learn to serve the feet of the Steady One and your sins will fall away and be cut off; worship without failing in word, and once you have, he becomes a gem lamp with no second.","n":"Learning is here something done with the Lord's feet: study and worship are one act. நிற்கின்ற போதே is taken as 'while alive'. The gem lamp (மணிவிளக்கு) gives a light that needs no oil.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","vazhipaadu","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:29"},"t:293":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":293,"t":["கல்வி யுடையார் கழிந்தோடிப் போகின்றார்","பல்லி யுடையார் பாம்பரிந்து உண்கின்றார்","எல்லியுன் காலையும் ஏத்தும் இறைவனை","வல்லியுள் வாதித்த காயமும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"The learned pass away and are gone, and those with the lizard eat cut snakes; but if you praise the Lord night and morning, the body becomes as if transmuted by alchemy in the herb.","n":"Lines 1 and 2 are obscure; பல்லி may be lizard (with its omens) or a form of பல் (tooth). வாதித்த is taken from வாதம் (alchemy) and வல்லி as a herb, so the claim may be that worship makes the body lasting where learning alone does not.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","yaakkai","vazhipaadu","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:29"},"t:294":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":294,"t":["துணையது வாய்வரும் தூயநற் சோதி","துணையது வாய்வரும் தூயநற் சொல்லாம்","துணையது வாய்வரும் தூயநற் கந்தம்","துணையது வாய்வரும் தூயநற் கல்வியே."],"p":"","r":"Pure good light comes as a companion, pure good word comes as a companion, pure good fragrance comes as a companion, pure good learning comes as a companion.","n":"The four lines are parallel and the verse does not say how light, word, fragrance and learning relate. Read in the chapter, learning is the last and summing item; the light, word and fragrance may be taken symbolically (the Lord, mantra, the scent of worship).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","thunai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:29"},"t:295":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":295,"t":["நூலொன்று பற்றி நுனியேற மாட்டாதார்","பாலொன்று பற்றினால் பண்பின் பயங்கெடும்","கோலொன்று பற்றினால் கூடாப் பறவைகள்","மாலொன்று பற்றி மயங்குகின் றார்க்களே."],"p":"","r":"Those who hold to one text and cannot climb to its end, or cling to one side and lose the fruit of good nature, are like birds scattered at one raised stick: holding to one delusion, they are bewildered.","n":"The verse puns on ஒன்று பற்றி (holding one thing) four times. நூல் is thread and book, பால் milk or side (faction). The reading against narrow, sectarian learning is a guess at a compressed verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","pedhaimai","panpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:29"},"t:296":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":296,"t":["ஆய்ந்துகொள் வார்க்குஅரன் அங்கே வெளிப்படும்","தோய்ந்த நெருப்பது தூய்மணி சிந்திடும்","ஏய்ந்த இளமதி எட்டவல் லார்கட்கு","வாய்ந்த மனமல்கு நூலேணி யாமே."],"p":"","r":"To those who search, Hara appears right there; the fire within sheds the light of a pure gem; and for those who can reach the young crescent, it becomes a thread-ladder for the ready mind.","n":"நூலேணி puns on rope ladder and ladder of texts. இளமதி may be the crescent on Siva's head or the moon centre of yogic practice; நெருப்பு and மணி may also be yogic. The plain sense is that study, rightly used, leads up to him.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","sivam","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:29"},"t:297":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":297,"t":["வழித்துணை யாய்மருந் தாயிருந் தார்முன்","கழித்துணை யாம்கற் றிலாதவர் சிந்தை","ஒழித்துணை யாம்உம் பராய்உல கேழும்","வழித்துணை யாம்பெருந் தன்மைவல் லானே."],"p":"","r":"Before the Lord who was companion on the way and medicine, the mind of the unlearned is cast aside; he of great nature, the one above, is the companion on the way through all seven worlds.","n":"வழித்துணை and மருந்து are epithets of Siva; Sengalvaraya notes Sundarar's 'வழித்துணை மருந்தே' (Tevaram 7.70.9). கழித்துணை and ஒழித்துணை are unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kallaamai","iraivan","thunai","marundhu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:29"},"t:298":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":298,"t":["பற்றது பற்றில் பரமனைப் பற்றுமின்","முற்றது எல்லா முதல்வன் அருள்பெறில்","கிற்ற விரகிற் கிளரொளி வானவர்","கற்றவர் பேரின்பம் உற்றுநின் றாரே."],"p":"","r":"If you would cling, cling to the Supreme; with the First One's grace all is fulfilled, and the learned, by the skill they have, stand in great bliss like the shining gods.","n":"பற்றது பற்றில் plays on பற்று as Kural 350 does: attachment redirected, not abolished. கிற்ற விரகு (the skill one has) is unclear, and so is whether the shining gods are a comparison or what the learned become.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pattru","thiruvarul","kalvi","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:29"},"t:299":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":299,"t":["கடலுடை யான்மலை யான்ஐந்து பூதத்து","உடலுடை யான்பல ஊழிதொ றூழி","அடல்விடை யேறும் அமரர்கள் நாதன்","இடமுடை யார்நெஞ்சத் தில்லிருந் தானே."],"p":"","r":"He who owns the sea and the mountain, whose body is the five elements, the lord of the immortals who rides the mighty bull through age after age, dwells in the hearts of those who have room for him.","n":"இடமுடையார் is those with room in their hearts; it may also pun on those who own land. The Lord who owns the sea and mountains chooses the heart as his dwelling.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:29"},"t:300":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":300,"t":["அறங்கேட்டும் அந்தணர் வாய்மொழி கேட்டும்","மறங்கேட்டும் வானவர் மந்திரங் கேட்டும்","புறங்கேட்டும் பொன்னுரை மேனிஎம் ஈசன்","திறங்கேட்டும் பெற்ற சிவகதி தானே."],"p":"","r":"Hearing of virtue, hearing the words of the andhanar, hearing of wrong, hearing the mantras of the gods, hearing of outer things, and hearing the ways of our gold-bodied Lord: by these the Siva-state is gained.","n":"The repeated கேட்டும் can be additive (by all these hearings) or concessive (though one hears all these, it is hearing the Lord's ways that gains the goal). மறம் is taken as wrong, the opposite of அறம், or as valour; புறம் as outer or worldly matters, or other doctrines.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kelvi","sivam","aram","andhanar"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:30"},"t:301":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":301,"t":["தேவர் பிரானைத் திவ்விய மூர்த்தியை","யாவர் ஒருவர் அறிவார் அறிந்தபின்","ஓதுமின் கேள்மின் உணர்மின் உணர்ந்தபின்","ஓதி உணர்ந்தவர் ஓங்கி நின்றாரே."],"p":"","r":"Who knows the Lord of the gods, the divine form? Once you know him, recite, listen and realise; those who recited and realised stood exalted.","n":"ஓதுமின் கேள்மின் உணர்மின் puts the three in order: recitation, listening, realisation, the last being the point. The first two lines can also be read 'whoever knows him'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kelvi","kalvi","sivam","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:30"},"t:302":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":302,"t":["மயன்பணி கேட்பது மாநந்தி வேண்டின்","அயன்பணி கேட்பது அரன்பணி யாலே","சிவன்பணி கேட்பவர் தேவரும் ஆவர்","பயன்பணி கேட்பது பற்றது வாமே."],"p":"","r":"If great Nandi wills, one hears Mayan's word; one hears Brahma's word through Hara's will; those who hear Siva's word become gods; to hear the word that bears fruit is the real hold.","n":"பணி may be word, command or service. மயன் may be Maya the divine artificer or Vishnu (மாயன்). பற்று in the last line may be the refuge one holds to or, negatively, attachment; I read it as refuge, but the line is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kelvi","sivam","pattru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:30"},"t:303":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":303,"t":["பெருமான் இவனென்று பேசி இருக்கும்","திருமானிடர் பின்னைத் தேவரும் ஆவர்","வருமா தவர்க்கு மகிழ்ந்தருள் செய்யும்","அருமா தவத்தெங்கள் ஆதிப் பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"Holy people who stay saying of him 'this is the Lord' will later become gods; our First Lord, of rare tapas, gladly grants grace to the great ascetics who come to him.","n":"The first half rewards speech about the Lord, the second tapas. அருமாதவத்து can describe the Lord or the tapas through which the ascetics come.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","thiruvarul","thavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:30"},"t:304":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":304,"t":["ஈசன் அருளும் இறப்பும் பிறப்பையும்","பேசி யிருந்து பிதற்றி மகிழ்வெய்தி","நேசமு மாகும் நிகழொளி யாய்நின்று","வாச மலர்க்கந்தம் மன்னிநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"Speaking of the Lord's grace, of death and of birth, babbling in delight, he becomes love to them and, standing as the living light, abides like the scent in a fragrant flower.","n":"பிதற்றி, babbling, is praise here: the talk of the enraptured devotee. The subject shifts between the devotees and the Lord; the scent in the flower is an image of his presence, felt and unseen.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","anbu","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:30"},"t:305":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":305,"t":["விழுப்பமும் கேள்வியும் மெய்நின்ற ஞானத்து","ஒழுக்கமும் சிந்தை உணர்கின்ற போது","வழுக்கி விடாவிடில் வானவர் கோனும்","இழுக்கின்றி எண்ணிலி காலம தாமே."],"p":"","r":"When the mind grasps excellence, listening and the conduct of true knowledge, if it does not slip from them, one becomes, without fault, even the king of the gods for countless ages.","n":"The last two lines are compressed: the one who holds fast may become the king of the gods, or the king of the gods may stay with him. வழுக்கி and இழுக்கு both carry the sense of slipping.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kelvi","ozhukkam","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:30"},"t:306":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":306,"t":["சிறியார் மணற்சோற்றில் தேக்கிடு மாபோல்","செறிவால் அனுபோகம் சித்திக்கும் என்னில்","குறியாதது ஒன்றைக் குறியாதார் தம்மை","அறியாது இருந்தார் அவராவார் அன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Like children who belch over rice made of sand, those who think enjoyment of the Real comes by mere closeness, and who do not fix on the One that cannot be fixed, have stayed without knowing; they are not what they claim.","n":"The sand-rice of children's play is food that cannot satisfy. குறியாதது, what cannot be marked or made an object, is traditionally the Supreme beyond every sign. செறிவு may be closeness, crowding or accumulation; the target of the verse is unclear beyond its mockery of pretended experience.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["meyyunarvu","pedhaimai","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:30"},"t:307":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":307,"t":["உறுதுணை யாவது உயிரும் உடம்பும்","உறுதுணை யாவது உலகுறு கேள்வி","செறிதுணை யாவது சிவனடிச் சிந்தை","பெறுதுணை கேட்கிற் பிறப்பில்லை தானே."],"p":"","r":"Life and body are a close support; listening that reaches the world is a close support; the mind on Siva's feet is the firm support; if one seeks the support to be gained, there is no birth.","n":"A graded list of supports, each repeating துணை: the bodily, the learned, and the mind fixed on Siva, which alone ends birth. The verse does not despise the first two.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thunai","kelvi","sivam","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:30"},"t:308":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":308,"t":["புகழநின் றார்க்கும் புராணன்எம் ஈசன்","இகழநின் றார்க்கும் இடும்பைக்கு இடமாம்","மகிழநின் றாதியை ஓதி உணராக்","கழியநின் றார்க்கொரு கற்பசு வாமே."],"p":"","r":"Our Lord, the Ancient One, is there for those who praise him; for those who scorn him he becomes the place of suffering; and for those who stand apart, not reciting and knowing the First with joy, he is a cow of stone.","n":"A stone cow looks like a cow and gives no milk. Read with the dative, the Lord is that to those who stand apart; it is also possible to read the stone cow as the people themselves.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","kelvi","idumbai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:30"},"t:309":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":309,"t":["வைத்துணர்ந் தான்மனத் தொடும்வாய் பேசி","ஒத்துணர்ந் தான்உரு ஒன்றொடொன்று ஒவ்வாது","அச்சுஉழன்று ஆணி கலங்கினும் ஆதியை","நச்சு உணர்ந் தார்க்கே நணுகலு மாமே."],"p":"","r":"Though one holds him in mind, speaks him aloud and knows him in accord, though forms do not match one another, and though the axle turns and the pin is shaken, the First comes near only to those who know him with longing.","n":"The axle and linchpin (அச்சு, ஆணி) are often taken as the body as a cart failing at death; நச்சு is longing, though it can also mean poison. The lines before the last are obscure, and the verse may be saying that mind and speech alone are not enough.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anbu","sivam","kelvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:30"},"t:310":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":310,"t":["கல்லா தவரும் கருத்தறி காட்சியை","வல்லார் எனில்அருட் கண்ணான் மதித்துளோர்","கல்லாதார் உண்மைபற் றாநிற்பர் கற்றோரும்","கல்லாதார் இன்பம் காணுகி லாரே."],"p":"","r":"Even the unlearned, if skilled in the vision that knows the meaning, are valued by the eye of grace; the unlearned hold to the truth, and even the learned cannot see the joy of the unlearned.","n":"The verse is hard to construe. பற்றா நிற்பர் can mean 'stand holding' or 'stand not holding' the truth, and the last line can mean that the learned cannot see the joy of the unlearned or that the learned, like the unlearned, do not see joy. Against the rest of the chapter it seems to allow a graced unlearning that sees what learning misses.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kallaamai","meyyunarvu","thiruvarul","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:31"},"t:311":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":311,"t":["வல்லார்கள் என்றும் வழியொன்றி வாழ்கின்றார்","அல்லா தவர்கள் அறிவு பலஎன்பார்","எல்லா இடத்தும் உளன்எங்கள் தம் இறை","கல்லா தவர்கள் கலப்பறி யாரே."],"p":"","r":"The able always live joined to the one way, while the others say knowledge is many; our God is in every place, but the unlearned do not know his being mingled in all.","n":"வல்லார், the able, stands for the truly learned. அறிவு பல, 'knowledge is many', is taken as the many doctrines of the sects. கலப்பு may be his pervasion of all or the soul's union with him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kallaamai","meyyunarvu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:31"},"t:312":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":312,"t":["நில்லா நிலையை நிலையாக நெஞ்சத்து","நில்லாக் குரம்பை நிலையென்று உணர்வீர்காள்","எல்லா உயிர்க்கும் இறைவனே ஆயினும்","கல்லாதார் நெஞ்சத்துக் காணஒண் ணாதே."],"p":"","r":"You who feel in your heart that the state which does not stay is lasting, and that this perishing hut is permanent: though he is the Lord of all lives, he cannot be seen in the hearts of the unlearned.","n":"குரம்பை, a hut or nest, is the body. The verse ties lack of learning to one specific error, taking the impermanent for the lasting.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kallaamai","nilaiyaamai","yaakkai","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:31"},"t:313":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":313,"t":["கில்லேன் வினைத்துய ராக்கும் மயலானேன்","கல்லேன் அரநெறி அறியாத் தகைமையின்","வல்லேன் வழங்கும் பொருளே மனத்தினுள்","கல்லேன் கழியநின்று ஆடவல் லேனே."],"p":"","r":"I could not; I fell into the delusion that makes the suffering of deeds; I did not learn, being one who did not know Hara's way; yet with the Giver in my mind, I, unlearned, can go beyond and dance.","n":"A first-person confession whose turn is from கல்லேன் (I did not learn) to வல்லேன் (I am able). I take 'the Giver' (வழங்கும் பொருள்) as the Lord whose grace makes up what learning did not; ஆடவல்லேன் may echo the Lord's name ஆடவல்லான், the dancer. The first word கில்லேன் and the second line's syntax are uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kallaamai","vinai","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:31"},"t:314":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":314,"t":["நில்லாது சீவன் நிலையன்று எனஎண்ணி","வல்லார் அறத்தும் தவத்துளும் ஆயினார்","கல்லா மனித்தர் கயவர் உலகினில்","பொல்லா வினைத்துயர் போகஞ்செய் வாரே."],"p":"","r":"Thinking that life does not stay and is not lasting, the able have entered virtue and tapas; the unlearned men, the base of the world, live out the suffering of evil deeds.","n":"The able here act on impermanence, by virtue and austerity; the unlearned simply undergo it. கயவர், the base, is the word of the Kural's chapter on baseness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kallaamai","nilaiyaamai","thavam","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:31"},"t:315":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":315,"t":["விண்ணினின் உள்ளே விளைந்த விளங்கனி","கண்ணினின் உள்ளே கலந்துஅங் கிருந்தது","மண்ணினின் உள்ளே மதித்து மதித்துநின்","றெண்ணி எழுதி இளைத்துவிட் டாரே."],"p":"","r":"The shining fruit that ripened within the sky was mingled within the eye and stayed there; yet those within the earth reckoned and reckoned, thought and wrote, and wore themselves out.","n":"The plain point is that what scholars seek in books is already near. Traditionally the sky is the inner space at the crown, the eye the point between the brows, and the fruit Siva's light seen in yoga; the verse does not say so, and both readings stand.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","sivam","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:31"},"t:316":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":316,"t":["கணக்கறிந் தார்க்கு அன்றிக் காணஒண் ணாதது","கணக்கறிந் தார்க்கு அன்றிக் கைகூடாக் காட்சி","கணக்கறிந்து உண்மையைக் கண்டுஅண்ட நிற்கும்","கணக்கறிந் தார்கல்வி கற்றறிந் தாரே."],"p":"","r":"It cannot be seen except by those who know the reckoning, and the vision is not won except by them; knowing the reckoning, they see the truth and stand to reach it; those who know the reckoning are the ones who have learned.","n":"கணக்கு, the word repeated in every line, means account, measure or method; it is traditionally taken as the right order of knowing (of the tattvas, or of the path), not arithmetic. அண்ட நிற்கும் is unclear: perhaps 'stand near', perhaps 'stand in the cosmos'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","meyyunarvu","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:31"},"t:317":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":317,"t":["கல்லாத மூடரைக் காணவும் ஆகாது","கல்லாத மூடர்சொல் கேட்கக் கடன்அன்று","கல்லாத மூடர்க்குக் கல்லாதார் நல்லராம்","கல்லாத மூடர் கருத்தறி யாரே."],"p":"","r":"One should not even look upon unlearned fools, nor is it one's duty to hear their words; beside unlearned fools the merely unlearned are good; unlearned fools do not know the meaning.","n":"The third line separates the unlearned fool (கல்லாத மூடர்) from the plainly unlearned, who are better. It can also be read 'to unlearned fools, the unlearned seem good', that is, fools prize their own kind.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kallaamai","pedhaimai","inam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:31"},"t:318":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":318,"t":["கற்றும் சிவஞானம் இல்லாக் கலதிகள்","சுற்றமும் வீடார் துரிசுஅறார் மூடர்கள்","மற்றும் பலதிசை காணார் மதியிலோர்","கற்றன்பில் நிற்போர் கணக்கறிந் தார்களே."],"p":"","r":"Wretches who have learned yet lack Siva-knowledge do not leave their kin or rid themselves of fault; they are fools, and the witless do not see the many directions; those who stand in love after learning are the ones who know the reckoning.","n":"The chapter's sharpest claim: learning without சிவஞானம் leaves one among the fools. சுற்றமும் வீடார் may also pun on வீடு, release. The last line joins love to learning and recalls 316's கணக்கறிந்தார்.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","gnaanam","anbu","pattru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:31"},"t:319":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":319,"t":["ஆதிப் பிரான்அம ரர்க்கும் பரஞ்சுடர்","சோதி அடியார் தொடரும் பெருந்தெய்வம்","ஓதி உணரவல் லோம்என்பர் உள்நின்ற","சோதி நடத்தும் தொடர்வறி யாரே."],"p":"","r":"He is the First Lord, the supreme flame even to the immortals, the great god whom the devotees of light follow; people say 'we can recite and know him', but they do not know the course of the light that stands within and moves them.","n":"சோதி comes three times: the supreme light, the light followed by devotees, and the light within that directs (நடத்தும்) the person. Claimed knowledge by recitation misses the last.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","kalvi","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:31"},"t:320":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":320,"t":["நடுவுநின் றார்க்கு அன்றி ஞானமும் இல்லை","நடுவுநின் றார்க்கு நரகமும் இல்லை","நடுவுநின் றார்நல்ல தேவரும் ஆவர்","நடுவுநின் றார்வழி நானும்நின் றேனே."],"p":"","r":"There is no knowledge except for those who stand in the middle; for those who stand in the middle there is no hell; they become good gods, and I too stood in their way.","n":"நடுவு is the Kural's word for fairness, keeping to no side. In Tirumular it is also traditionally taken as the yogic middle, the poise between the two breaths or the central channel; the verse itself does not decide, and both senses stand.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naduvu","gnaanam","naragam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:32"},"t:321":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":321,"t":["நடுவுநின் றான்நல்ல கார்முகில் வண்ணன்","நடுவுநின் றான்நல்ல நான்மறை யோதி","நடுவுநின் றார்ச்சிலர் ஞானிகள் ஆவோர்","நடுவுநின் றார்நல்ல நம்பனு மாமே."],"p":"","r":"The good cloud-coloured one stood in the middle; the good reciter of the four Vedas stood in the middle; some who stood in the middle became jnanis; those who stood in the middle became even the good Lord.","n":"The cloud-coloured one is Vishnu and the reciter of the four Vedas Brahma. நம்பன், the Lord, is a name of Siva; the last line may mean those in the middle become one with him or that the Lord himself stands in the middle.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naduvu","gnaanam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:32"},"t:322":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":322,"t":["நடுவுநின் றார்சிலர் ஞானிக ளாவார்","நடுவுநின் றார்சிலர் தேவரு மாவார்","நடுவுநின் றார்சிலர் நம்பனு மாவார்","நடுவுநின் றாரொடு நானும்நின் றேனே."],"p":"","r":"Some who stood in the middle became jnanis, some gods, some the Lord, and I too stood with those who stood in the middle.","n":"A near repetition of 320 and 321. The gradation (jnanis, gods, the Lord) suggests different ends for those who keep the same middle.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naduvu","gnaanam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:32"},"t:323":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":323,"t":["தோன்றிய எல்லாம் துடைப்பன் அவனன்றி","ஏன்றுநின் றாரென்றும் ஈசன் இணையடி","மூன்றுநின் றார்முதல் வன்திரு நாமத்தை","நான்றுநின் றார்நடு வாகிநின் றாரே."],"p":"","r":"He alone dissolves all that has come to be; those who stand always holding the Lord's two feet and cling to the holy name of the First are the ones who stand in the middle.","n":"The verse defines the middle by devotion, not by fairness. மூன்றுநின்றார் is obscure: perhaps standing in the three (thought, word, deed) or at the three times of day. நான்று, hanging, suggests clinging to the name.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naduvu","sivam","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:32"},"t:324":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":324,"t":["கழுநீர்ப் பசுப்பெறிற் கயந்தொறும் தேரா","கழுநீர் விடாய்த்துத்தம் காயம்சுருக்கும்","முழுநீர்க் கள்ளுண்போர் முறைமை அகன்றோர்","செழுநீர்ச் சிவன்தன் சிவானந்தத் தேறலே."],"p":"","r":"As a cow given rice-wash water seeks no pond yet, craving the slop, wastes its body, so those who drink their fill of liquor have left the right order; the true drink is the clear nectar of Siva's bliss.","n":"கழுநீர் is rice-washing water given to cattle, but can also be the water-lily; the cow image is my best reading of the first two lines. செழுநீர், rich water, describes Siva as the true source of drink.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kal","aanandham","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:33"},"t:325":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":325,"t":["சித்தம் உருக்கிச் சிவமாம் சமாதியில்","ஒத்த சிவானந்தம் ஓவாத தேறலைச்","சுத்த மதுவுண்ணச் சிவானந்தம் விட்டிடா","நித்தல் இருத்தல் கிடத்தல் கீழ்க் காலே."],"p":"","r":"Those who drink the pure honey, the unceasing nectar of Siva's bliss that comes in the samadhi where the mind melts and becomes Siva, do not leave that bliss, whether standing, sitting or lying down.","n":"சுத்த மது, pure honey or wine, keeps the vocabulary of drink for bliss. The last line (நித்தல் இருத்தல் கிடத்தல் கீழ்க் காலே) is obscure; I take it as the postures in which the bliss persists, but கீழ்க்கால் may be a yogic term for the lower breath.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["samaadhi","aanandham","sivam","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:33"},"t:326":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":326,"t":["காமமும் கள்ளும் கலதிகட் கேயாகும்","மாமல மும்சம யத்துள் மயலுறும்","போமதி யாகும் புனிதன் இணையடி","ஓமய ஆனந்தத் தேறல் உணர்வுண்டே."],"p":"","r":"Lust and liquor belong to the wretched, and within the sects the great impurity breeds delusion; the mind that goes to the twin feet of the Pure One finds there awareness of the nectar of bliss made of Om.","n":"போமதி may be 'the departing mind' or 'the mind that goes'; ஓமய is read as ஓம்-மய, made of Om, though it could relate to ஓமம், the fire rite. The verse pairs lust and liquor as the chapter does again in 327 and 330.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kal","kaamam","malam","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:33"},"t:327":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":327,"t":["வாமத்தோர் தாமும் மதுவுண்டு மாள்பவர்","காமத்தோர் காமக்கள் ளுண்டே கலங்குவர்","ஓமத்தோர் உள்ளொளிக் குள்ளே உணர்வர்கள்","நாமத்தோர் அன்றே நணுகுவர் தாமே."],"p":"","r":"Left-hand worshippers drink liquor and die; the lustful drink the liquor of lust and are confused; those of the fire rite know within the inner light; those of the Name draw near that very day.","n":"வாமத்தோர் are traditionally the followers of the left-hand (Vama) tantra, who use wine in ritual. ஓமத்தோர் may be those of the fire rite or those of Om, and நாமத்தோர் those who repeat the Name; the four groups are ranked.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kal","samayam","kaamam","aaguthi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:33"},"t:328":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":328,"t":["உள்ளுண்மை ஓரார் உணரார் பசுபாசம்","வள்ளன்மை நாதன் அருளினன் வாழ்வுறார்","தெள்ளுண்மை ஞானச் சிவயோகம் சேர்வுறார்","கள்ளுண்ணும் மாந்தர் கருத்தறி யாரே."],"p":"","r":"Those who drink liquor do not ponder the inner truth, do not know soul and bond, do not live in the grace the bountiful Lord gave, and do not reach the Siva-yoga of clear true knowledge; they do not know the meaning.","n":"பசு பாசம், soul and bond, are two of the three Siddhanta categories, the third being பதி, the Lord, who appears here as the bountiful நாதன். The charge against liquor is that it blocks Siddhanta knowledge, not only good conduct.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kal","paasam","gnaanam","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:33"},"t:329":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":329,"t":["மயக்கும் சமய மலமன்னு மூடர்","மயக்கு மதுவுண்ணும் மாமூடர் தேரார்","மயக்குறு மாமாயை மாயையின் வீடு","மயக்கில் தெளியின் மயக்குறும் அன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Fools in whom the impurity of deluding sects abides, and the great fools who drink deluding liquor, do not discern great maya's delusion or the release from maya; to feel clear inside delusion is itself delusion.","n":"மயக்கு opens every line. The last line can also be read 'if one grows clear of delusion, delusion ends'; I take it as the drunkard's false clarity.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kal","samayam","maayai","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:33"},"t:330":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":330,"t":["மயங்குந் தியங்கும் கள்வாய்மை அழிக்கும்","இயங்கும் மடவார்தம் இன்பமே எய்தி","முயங்கும் நயங்கொண்ட ஞானத்து முந்தார்","இயங்கும் இடையறா ஆனந்தம் எய்துமே."],"p":"","r":"Liquor bewilders, dulls and destroys truthfulness; drinkers reach only the pleasure of women and embrace them, and do not go forward into the good knowledge that attains unbroken bliss.","n":"Truthfulness (வாய்மை) is the first casualty named. The two kinds of pleasure, of women and of unbroken bliss, are set side by side through the repeated இயங்கும்.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kal","vaaimai","kaamam","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:33"},"t:331":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":331,"t":["இராப்பகல் அற்ற இடத்தே இருந்து","பராக்கற ஆனந்தத் தேறல் பருகார்","இராப்பகல் அற்ற இணையடி இன்பத்து","இராப்பகல் மாயை இரண்டிடத் தேனே."],"p":"","r":"They do not stay in the place where there is neither night nor day and drink, free of distraction, the nectar of bliss; in the joy of the twin feet where there is neither night nor day, night and day and maya are left behind.","n":"The place without night and day is traditionally the state beyond the two breaths, the moon and sun channels, or beyond waking and sleep. The last line (இராப்பகல் மாயை இரண்டிடத் தேனே) is obscure: தேன் may be honey or a first-person ending. The corpus notes a parallel in Kandar Alangaram 74, where staying where there is no night and day is called hard.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aanandham","yogam","maayai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:33"},"t:332":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":332,"t":["சத்தியை வேண்டிச் சமயத்தோர் கள்ளுண்பர்","சத்தி அழிந்தது தம்மை மறத்தலால்","சத்தி சிவஞானம் தன்னில் தலைப்பட்டுச்","சத்திய ஞானஆ னந்தத்திற் சார்தலே."],"p":"","r":"Sectarians drink liquor seeking power, but the power is destroyed because they forget themselves; real power is to reach Siva-knowledge and join the bliss of truth and knowledge.","n":"சத்தி is both power and the goddess Shakti, whom some sects worship with wine. சத்திய ஞான ஆனந்தம் echoes the formula of being, knowledge and bliss.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kal","samayam","saththi","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:33"},"t:333":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":333,"t":["சத்தன் அருள்தரின் சத்தி அருளுண்டாம்","சத்தி அருள்தரின் சத்தன் அருளுண்டாம்","சத்தி சிவமாம் இரண்டும் தன் உள்வைக்கச்","சத்தியம் எண்சித்தித் தன்மையு மாமே."],"p":"","r":"If the Lord of Shakti gives grace, Shakti's grace follows; if Shakti gives grace, his grace follows; when one holds both Shakti and Siva within, truth and the nature of the eight attainments come.","n":"சத்தன் is the possessor of Shakti, Siva. The mirror-image lines make their grace one. சத்தியம் may be 'truth' or 'it is certain'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","saththi","sivam","siththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:33"},"t:334":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":334,"t":["தத்துவம் நீக்கி மருள்நீக்கித் தானாகிப்","பொய்த்தவம் நீக்கிமெய்ப் போகத்துட் போகியே","மெய்த்த சகமுண்டு விட்டுப் பரானந்தச்","சித்திய தாக்கும் சிவானந்தத் தேறலே."],"p":"","r":"Removing the tattvas and delusion, becoming oneself, removing false tapas, entering true enjoyment, taking the world and leaving it: the nectar of Siva's bliss makes the attainment of supreme bliss.","n":"தானாகி, becoming oneself, is traditionally the soul knowing itself apart from the tattvas. மெய்த்த சகமுண்டு விட்டு is obscure; I read சகம் as the world.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aanandham","thaththuvam","siththi","thavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:33"},"t:335":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":335,"t":["யோகிகள் கால்கட்டி ஒண்மதி ஆனந்தப்","போத அமுதைப் பொசித்தவர் எண்சித்தி","மோகியர் கள்ளுண்டு மூடராய் மோகமுற்று","ஆகும் மதத்தால் அறிவழிந் தாரே."],"p":"","r":"Yogis who bind the breath taste the nectar of knowledge-bliss from the bright moon and gain the eight attainments; the deluded drink liquor, become fools, fall into infatuation and lose their understanding in intoxication.","n":"கால் is the breath, and ஒண்மதி the moon, traditionally the place in the head from which yogic nectar is said to flow when the breath is held. The two halves set yogic nectar against liquor, both bringing an altered state, one with siddhis and one without understanding.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","siththi","kal","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:33"},"t:336":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":336,"t":["உண்ணீர் அமுத முறும் ஊறலைத்திறந்து","எண்ணீர் குரவன் இணையடித் தாமரை","நண்ணீர் சமாதியின் நாடிநீ ரால்நலம்","கண்ணாற் றொடேசென்று கால்வழி காணுமே."],"p":"","r":"Open the spring where the nectar of the inner water wells, think on the guru's lotus feet, draw near in samadhi; then by the water of the channels the good flows with the eye's stream and is seen along the way of the breath.","n":"நாடிநீர், the water of the channels, கண்ணாறு, the eye's stream, and கால்வழி, the breath's way, are yogic terms whose exact reference I can't fix. நாடி can also be read as 'seeking'. The plain sense is a turn from outer drink to inner nectar under the guru.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","samaadhi","naadi","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:33"},"t:337":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":337,"t":["நடுவுநில் லாதிவ் வுலகஞ் சரிந்து","கெடுகின்ற தெம்பெரு மானென்ன ஈசன்","நடுவுள அங்கி அகத்திய நீபோய்","முடுகிய வையத்து முன்னிரென் றானே."],"p":"","r":"When the gods cried 'our Lord, this world does not stay level, it tilts and is ruined', the Lord said: 'Agastya, fire within the middle, go into the troubled world and stand first there.'","n":"The verse assumes the legend in which, at Siva's wedding, all beings gathered in the north and the earth tipped, and Agastya was sent south to balance it. நடுவுள அங்கி, the fire in the middle, may be an epithet of Agastya's tapas or a yogic term. Who speaks the second line is not stated.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","thavam","naduvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:34"},"t:338":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":338,"t":["அங்கி உதயம் வளர்க்கும் அகத்தியன்","அங்கி உதயஞ்செய் மேல்பா லவனொடு","மங்கி உதயஞ்செய் வடபால் தவமுனி","எங்கும் வளங்கொள் இலங்கொளி தானே"],"p":"","r":"Agastya, who makes the rising fire grow, with the one in the west who makes the fire rise and the tapas-sage of the north who makes it rise: through them the shining light holds plenty everywhere.","n":"அங்கி உதயம், the rising of fire, may be the sacrificial fire or the inner fire of yoga. The sages of the west and north are not named. Line 3 begins மங்கி, which may be a misreading of அங்கி.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","aaguthi","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:34"},"t:339":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":339,"t":["கருத்துறை அந்தகன் தன்போல் அசுரன்","வரத்தின் உலகத் துயிர்க்களை எல்லாம்","வருத்தஞ்செய் தானென்று வானவர் வேண்டக்","குருத்துயர் சூலங்கைக் கொண்டுகொன் றானே"],"p":"","r":"When the gods begged him, saying that an asura like Andhaka was tormenting all the lives of the world by the power of a boon, the Lord took the high-pointed trident in his hand and killed him.","n":"Andhaka's slaying is traditionally set at Tirukkovalur. அந்தகன் தன்போல் அசுரன் can be 'an asura like Andhaka' or Andhaka himself; கருத்துறை, in the first line, is unclear.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","kolai","maram"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:35"},"t:340":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":340,"t":["கொலையிற் பிழைத்த பிரசா பதியைத்","தலையைத் தடிந்திட்டுத் தானங்கி யிட்டு","நிலையுல குக்கிவன் வேண்டுமென் றெண்ணித்","தலையை யரிந்திட்டுச் சந்திசெய் தானே"],"p":"","r":"He struck off the head of Prajapati, who had erred in a matter of killing, and gave it to the fire; then, judging that the stable world needed him, he cut a head and joined it on.","n":"Prajapati here is taken as Daksha, whose head was burned and replaced, in the legend, with a goat's. கொலையிற் பிழைத்த may mean 'who erred by killing', perhaps the animal of his sacrifice, or 'who escaped death'. The punishment ends in restoration.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","oruththal","kolai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:35"},"t:341":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":341,"t":["எங்கும் பரந்தும் இருநிலந் தாங்கியுந்","தங்கும் படித்தவன் தாளுணர் தேவர்கள்","பொங்கும் சினத்துள் அயன்தலை முன்னற","அங்குஅச் சுதனை உதிரங்கொண் டானே"],"p":"","r":"The gods who know the feet of the one who pervades everything and bears the great earth so that it stays: in that surging anger Brahma's head was severed, and there he took blood from Achyuta.","n":"The legend, set at Kandiyur, is that Siva cut off Brahma's fifth head and filled the skull with Vishnu's blood. Whose anger surges (பொங்கும் சினம்) and how the gods in line 2 fit the sentence are unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","vekuli","kolai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:35"},"t:342":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":342,"t":["எங்குங் கலந்துமென் உள்ளத் தெழுகின்ற","அங்க முதல்வன் அருமறை யோதிபாற்","பொங்குன்ய் சலந்தரன் போர்ச்செய்ய நீர்மையின்","அங்கு விரற்குறித் தாழிசெய் தானே"],"p":"","r":"The First, who mingles everywhere and rises in my heart, when Jalandhara swelled to make war on the reciter of the Vedas, drew a wheel there with his toe in the water.","n":"In the legend, set at Virkudi, Siva drew a circle with his toe that became the discus which killed Jalandhara. நீர்மையின் is taken as 'in the water' or 'fittingly'. A variant reads யோகிபாற் (against the yogi) for யோதிபாற் (against the reciter).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","kolai","maram"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:35"},"t:343":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":343,"t":["அப்பணி செஞ்சடை ஆதி புராதனன்","முப்புரம் செற்றனன் என்பர்கள் மூடர்க்கள்","முப்புர மாவது மும்மல காரியம்","அப்புரம் எய்தமை யாரறி வாரே"],"p":"","r":"Fools say the Ancient One, with water in his red matted hair, burned the three cities; the three cities are the work of the three impurities, and who knows that it was these he shot?","n":"The chapter's turning point: the myth is not denied but read as allegory, the cities being the soul's three bonds (traditionally anava, karma and maya). Tirumular calls the literal reading foolish, not the myth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["malam","sivam","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:35"},"t:344":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":344,"t":["முத்தீ கொளுவி முழங்கெரி வேள்வியுள்","அத்தி யுரியர னாவ தறிகிலர்","சத்தி கருதிய தாம்பல தேவரும்","அத்தீயின் உள்ளெழுந் தன்று கொலையே"],"p":"","r":"At the sacrifice roaring with three fires lit, they did not know who Hara, wearer of the elephant's hide, was; and when the many gods who sought power were there, the killing arose from within that fire.","n":"The legend, set at Vazhuvur, is that the sages of the Darukavana raised an elephant from their sacrificial fire to kill Siva, who flayed it and wore its hide. The verse names the elephant's hide and the killing from the fire; who the many gods are and who seeks power (சத்தி) is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aaguthi","sivam","kolai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:35"},"t:345":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":345,"t":["மூலத் துவாரத்து மூளும் ஒருவனை","மேலைத் துவாரத்து மேலுற நோக்கிமுற்","காலுற்றுக் காலனைக் காய்ந்தங்கி யோகமாய்","ஞாலக் கடவூர் நலமாய் இருந்ததே"],"p":"","r":"Turning the One who blazes at the root opening upward to the upper opening, with the breath joined, he burned Death in the yoga of fire: so Kadavur of the world became good.","n":"The legend is of Siva kicking Death to save Markandeya at Tirukkadavur. Here it becomes yoga: the fire at the root opening (traditionally the muladhara) is raised by breath (கால்) to the upper opening, and Death is burned. The verse does not name kundalini; the reading is tradition's. கால் is also the leg, so காலுற்றுக் காலனை may pun on the kick.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","kundalini","kootru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:35"},"t:346":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":346,"t":["இருந்த மனத்தை இசைய இருத்திப்","பொருந்தி இலிங்க வழியது போக்கித்","திருந்திய காமன் செயலழித் தங்கண்","அருந்தவ யோகங் கொறுக்கை அமர்ந்ததே"],"p":"","r":"Making the mind that sat there settle in accord, turning back the way of the linga, destroying the work of Kama: that is the rare yoga of tapas seated at Korukkai.","n":"The burning of Kama at Korukkai becomes restraint. இலிங்க வழியது போக்கி is traditionally taken as reversing the way of the sexual organ, holding the seed; it may also refer to the linga of worship. கொறுக்கை is Korukkai, with a likely pun on curbing.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","kaamam","adakkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:35"},"t:347":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":347,"t":["அடிசேர்வன் என்னஎம் ஆதியை நோக்கி","முடிசேர் மலைமக னார்மக ளாகித்","திடமார் தவஞ்செய்து தேவர் அறியப்","படியார அர்ச்சித்துப் பத்திசெய் தாளே"],"p":"","r":"Resolving to reach his feet and looking to our First, she became the daughter of the crowned mountain-king, did firm tapas, and, as the gods knew, worshipped him fully on earth and gave him devotion.","n":"The goddess is unnamed; the mountain's daughter is Parvati. Even the Lord's consort reaches him by tapas and worship, the means open to devotees.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","thavam","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:36"},"t:348":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":348,"t":["திரிகின்ற முப்புரஞ் செற்ற பிரானை","அரியனென் றெண்ணி அயர்வுற வேண்டா","புரிவுடை யாளர்க்குப் பொய்யலன் ஈசன்","பரிவொடு நின்று பரிசறி வானே"],"p":"","r":"Do not grow weary thinking the Lord who destroyed the wandering three cities is hard to reach; the Lord is not false to those who love him, and he stands with tenderness and knows their worth.","n":"புரிவுடையார், those with புரிவு, may be those with love, with longing, or with understanding. பரிசறிவான் may be 'knows their worth' or 'knows what to give them'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","thiruvarul","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:36"},"t:349":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":349,"t":["ஆழி வலங்கொண் டயன்மால் இருவரும்","ஊழி வலஞ்செய்ய ஒண்சுட ராதியும்","ஆழி கொடுத்தனன் அச்சுதற்க் கவ்வழி","வாழி பிரமற்கும் வாள்கொடுத் தானே"],"p":"","r":"When Brahma and Vishnu circled the sea and did reverence through an age, the bright-flamed First gave the discus to Achyuta in that way, and gave a sword to Brahma as well.","n":"ஆழி may be the sea or the wheel, and வலம் circling or power; the first two lines are hard to fix. The gift of the discus to Vishnu is a known legend; the sword to Brahma is less familiar.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","thiruvarul","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:36"},"t:350":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":350,"t":["தாங்கி இருபது தோளுந் தடவரை","ஓங்க எடுத்தவன் ஒப்பில் பெருவலி","ஆங்கு நெரித்தம ராவென் றழைத்தபின்","நீங்கா அருள்செய்தான் நின்மலன் தானே"],"p":"","r":"He who bore twenty arms and lifted the great mountain high had a matchless strength; when the Lord crushed him there and he cried 'Hara!', the Stainless One gave him unending grace.","n":"Ravana is not named. The verse moves from pride in strength to a single cry and to grace that does not leave: the crushing is the first step of grace.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","vali","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:36"},"t:351":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":351,"t":["உறுவது அறிதண்டி ஒண்மணற் கூட்டி","அறுவகை ஆன்ஐந்தும் ஆட்டத்தன் தாதை","செறுவகை செய்து சிதைப்ப முனிந்து","மறுமழு வால்வெட்டி மாலைபெற் றானே"],"p":"","r":"Chandi, who knew what was coming, heaped fine sand and bathed it with the five products of the cow; when his father came in hostility and wrecked it, he grew angry, cut him with the axe, and received a garland.","n":"The legend of Chandesa, the cowherd boy who worshipped a sand linga and struck his father's leg when he kicked it over, and was made chief of the Lord's servants and given his garland. அறுவகை (six kinds) and உறுவது அறி are unclear. The verse reports the act and the reward without comment.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","anbu","kolai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:36"},"t:352":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":352,"t":["ஓடிவந் தெல்லாம் ஒருங்கிய தேவர்கள்","வாடி முகமும் வருத்தத்துத் தாஞ்சென்று","நாடி இறைவா நமேன்று கும்பிட","ஈடில் புகழோன் எழுகவென் றானே."],"p":"","r":"The gods all ran together, faces withered, went in distress, sought him and bowed saying 'Lord, we bow'; the One of matchless fame said 'Rise'.","n":"The occasion is not named; it is often taken as the poison rising from the churned ocean. நம என்று may be the 'namah' of worship.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","thiruvarul","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:36"},"t:353":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":353,"t":["தந்தைபி ரான்வெகுண் டாந்தக்கன் வேள்வியை","வெந்தழல் ஊடே புறப்பட விண்ணவர்","முந்திய பூசை முடியார் முறைகெட்டுச்","சிந்தினர் அண்ணல் சினஞ்செய்த போதே"],"p":"","r":"When the Father, the Lord, grew angry, fire burst out through the blazing flame of Daksha's sacrifice, and the gods, unable to finish the worship they had begun, its order broken, scattered.","n":"The verse does not tell the story's cause, Daksha's leaving Siva out of the rite; it assumes it. What it shows is a rite whose order (முறை) collapses the moment its true Lord is angered.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","aaguthi","vazhipaadu","vekuli"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:37"},"t:354":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":354,"t":["சந்தி செயக்கண் டெழுகின் றரிதானும்","எந்தை யிவனல்ல யாமே உலகினிற்","பந்தஞ்செய் பாசத்து வீழ்ந்து தவஞ்செய்ய","அந்தமி லானும் அருள்புரிந் தானே"],"p":"","r":"Seeing the rite performed, Hari rose and said 'this one is not our lord; we have fallen into the binding snare of the world', did penance, and the endless one gave him grace.","n":"Who says 'எந்தை இவன் அல்ல' and of whom is unclear: it may be Hari disowning Daksha, or a confession that they had taken the wrong lord. சந்தி may mean the twilight rite or simply the ritual. The penance-then-grace pattern is plain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","thavam","paasam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:37"},"t:355":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":355,"t":["அப்பரி சேயய நார்ப்பதி வேள்வியுள்","அப்பரி சேயங்கி அதிசய மாகிலும்","அப்பரி சேயது நீர்மையை யுள்கலந்","தப்பரி சேசிவன் ஆலிக்கின் றானே"],"p":"","r":"Just so, in the sacrifice of Brahma's son the Prajapati, though the fire was a marvel, Siva entered into its very nature and stood exulting within it.","n":"அப்பரிசே (in that way) opens every line, and the next verse repeats the device. அயனார்பதி is read as Daksha, a lord born of Brahma; ஆலிக்கின்றான் may mean exulting, roaring or dancing. The claim, as read, is that even a rite that excludes Siva burns only with his power.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","aaguthi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:37"},"t:356":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":356,"t":["அப்பரி சேஅயன் மால்முதல் தேவர்கள்","அப்பரி சேயவ ராகிய காரணம்","அப்பரி சங்கி யுளநாளும் உள்ளிட்","டப்பரி சாகி அலர்ந்திருந் தானே"],"p":"","r":"Just so he is the cause of Brahma, Vishnu and the other gods being what they are; just so, entering the fire on every day there is, he became that fire and stood blossomed forth.","n":"The variants அலந்திருந்தானே (stood suffering, troubled) and அமர்ந்திருந்தானே (stayed seated) give different last words; அலர்ந்திருந்தானே (stood blossomed) is taken up by the next verse's opening, which supports it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","aaguthi","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:37"},"t:357":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":357,"t":["அலர்ந்திருந் தானென் றமரர் துதிப்பக்","குலந்தரும் கீழங்கி கோளுற நோக்கிச்","சிவந்த பரமிது சென்று கதுவ","உவந்த பெருவழி யோடி வந்தானே"],"p":"","r":"While the gods praised him as the one who had blossomed forth, he looked hard at the low fire that confers lineage so that it was seized; the red Supreme went and caught it, and came running by the great way he delighted in.","n":"குலந்தரும் கீழங்கி (the lower fire that gives lineage) and the last two lines are obscure; they may describe Siva, as Virabhadra, seizing the sacrificial fire. I can't fix the sense beyond that.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","aaguthi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:37"},"t:358":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":358,"t":["அரிபிர மந்தக்கன் அருக்க னுடனே","வருமதி வாலை வன்னிநல் இந்திரன்","சிரமுக நாசி சிறந்தகை தோள்தான்","அரனருள் இன்றி அழிந்தநல் லோரே"],"p":"","r":"Hari, Brahma, Daksha, the sun, the rising moon, the young goddess, Agni and Indra lost head, face, nose, hand and shoulder: without Hara's grace even those good ones were ruined.","n":"The list follows the story in which each god at the sacrifice was maimed in one part, Daksha losing his head. The verse pairs gods and body parts only loosely. Its point is the last line: the gods are 'good' (நல்லோர்), and still fall without grace. வாலை is taken as a goddess, perhaps Sarasvati, whose nose was cut in the story.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:37"},"t:359":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":359,"t":["செவிமந் திரஞ்சொல்லுஞ் செய்தவத் தேவர்","அவிமந் திரத்தின் அடுக்களை கோலிச்","செவிமந் திரஞ்செய்து தாமுற நோக்குங்","குவிமந் திரங்கொல் கொடியது வாமே"],"p":"","r":"Gods who did penance and whisper mantras in the ear, who lay out the kitchen of the oblation with mantras and do ear-mantras looking to their own gain: such a gathered mantra is a cruel thing.","n":"செவிமந்திரம் (ear-mantra, the whispered initiation formula) and குவிமந்திரம் (gathered or concentrated mantra) are technical. கொல் may be a doubt particle or mean 'kills'. As read, the target is ritual turned to self-interest, not mantra itself.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aaguthi","vazhipaadu","avaa"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:37"},"t:360":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":360,"t":["நல்லார் நவகுண்டம் ஒன்பதும் இன்புறப்","பல்லார் அமரர் பரிந்தருள் செய்கென","வில்லாற் புரத்தை விளங்கெரி கோத்தவன்","பொல்லா அசுரர்கள் பொன்றும் படிக்கே"],"p":"","r":"When the many gods begged him to show grace, so that the nine fire-pits of the good might give joy, he who set blazing fire to the cities with his bow did so that the wicked asuras would perish.","n":"நவகுண்டம் (the nine fire-pits) is a ritual term. The verse sets Siva's destroying the three cities beside the rite: his destruction here protects worship rather than breaking it, the reverse of Daksha's case. The tie between the two halves is my reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","thiruvarul","aaguthi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:37"},"t:361":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":361,"t":["தெளிந்தார் கலங்கினும் நீகலங் காதே","அளித்தாங் கடைவதெம் ஆதிப் பிரானை","விளிந்தா னது தக்கன் வேள்வியை வீயச்","சுளிந்தாங் கருள்செய்த தூய்மொழி யானே"],"p":"","r":"Though even the clear-minded are shaken, do not you be shaken: the one we reach is our first Lord who sustains us, he of pure word who in anger destroyed Daksha's sacrifice and then gave grace.","n":"The address turns to the listener (நீ). சுளிந்து ஆங்கு அருள்செய்த, angered and then giving grace, holds the chapter's two sides together. The tradition that Daksha was restored with a goat's head is not in the verse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","thiruvarul","thelivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:37"},"t:362":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":362,"t":["கருவரை மூடிக் கலந்தெழும் வெள்ளத்","திருவருங் கோவென் றிகல இறைவன்","ஒருவனும் நீருற ஓங்கொளி யாகி","அருவரை யாய்நின் றருள் புரிந் தானே"],"p":"","r":"When the flood rose and covered the dark mountains and the two contended, each crying 'I am king', the Lord alone became a towering light reaching down into the water and stood as a mountain no one could scale, and gave grace.","n":"இருவர் (the two) are traditionally Brahma and Vishnu, and கோ (king) their claim to supremacy; the verse folds the column of fire story, told fully in chapter 41, into the deluge. ஒருவனும் stresses that he alone stands.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","thiruvarul","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:38"},"t:363":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":363,"t":["அலைகடல் ஊடறுத் தண்டத்து வானோர்","தலைவன் எனும்பெயர் தான்றலை மேற்கொண்டு","உலகார் அழற்கண் டுள்விழா தோடி","அலைவாயில் வீழாமல் அஞ்சலென் றானே"],"p":"","r":"The one who bears the name Lord of the gods of the universe cut through the surging sea and, seeing the world in its fire, ran without falling in and said 'Fear not' so that they would not fall into the mouth of the waves.","n":"உலகார் அழற்கண்டு may be 'seeing the fire that fills the world' or 'seeing the world's people in distress'. அஞ்சல் (fear not) is the gesture of protection, and the verse's centre.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:38"},"t:364":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":364,"t":["தண்கடல் விட்ட தமரருந் தேவரும்","எண்கடல் சூழெம் பிரானென் றிறைஞ்சுவர்","விண்கடல் செய்தவர் மேலெழுந் தப்புறங்","கண்கடல் செய்யுங் கருத்தறி யாரே."],"p":"","r":"The immortals and gods whom the cool sea let go bow to him as our Lord encircled by the eight seas, but they do not know the purpose of the one who rises above the sky-sea and beyond it.","n":"விண்கடல் (sky-sea) and கண்கடல் செய்யும் (makes a sea of the eye, or makes the sea vast) are obscure. The plain point is the gap between worship and knowing: the gods praise him and still don't grasp his intent.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","vazhipaadu","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:38"},"t:365":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":365,"t":["சமைக்கவல் லானைச் சயம்புவென் றேத்தி","அமைக்கவல் லாரிவ் வுலகத்து ளாரே","திகைத்தெண் ணீரிற் கடலொலி ஓசை","மிகைக்கொள அங்கி மிகாமைவைத் தானே"],"p":"","r":"Who in this world can order things as he does, the one praised as self-born and able to create? When the roar of the sea swelled over the bewildering waters, he set a fire within it so that it would not overflow.","n":"The fire set in the sea is traditionally the submarine mare's-head fire (வடவைத்தீ) that drinks the ocean and keeps it from flooding the land. The verse names only அங்கி (fire). சயம்பு (svayambhu) is often an epithet of Brahma, so the first line may contrast praise of Brahma with Siva's real ordering power.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:38"},"t:366":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":366,"t":["பண்பழி செய்வழி பாடுசென் றப்புறங்","கண்பழி யாத கமலத் திருக்கின்ற","நண்பழி யாளனை நாடிச்சென் றச்சிரம்","விண்பழி யாத விருத்திகொண் டானே."],"p":"","r":"Going beyond the worship that ruins good qualities, he sought out the friend seated in the lotus whose eye does not fail, took that head, and took up a way of living that heaven does not blame.","n":"The verse plays on பழி (blame, ruin) in every line. அச்சிரம் (that head) and the one in the lotus point to the story of Siva taking Brahma's fifth head and then going about begging with the skull; that is tradition's reading, and the verse's own sense is obscure. Its link to the deluge is not clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:38"},"t:367":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":367,"t":["மால்போ தகனென்னும் வண்மைக்கிங் காங்காரங்","கால்போதகங் கையினோ டந்தரச் சக்கர","மேல்போக வெள்ளி மலைஅம ராபதி","பார்ப்போக மேழும் படைத்துடை யானே"],"p":"","r":"The verse names things the Lord has made and owns: Mal and his gift, ahankara, the elephant, the discus in the sky, the silver mountain, Indra's city Amaravati and the enjoyments of the seven worlds.","n":"The first two lines are hard to parse: மால் போதகன் may be 'Mal the elephant' or 'Mal the one taught', and காங்காரம் is read as ahankara. The last line is clear: he made and possesses all. The verse sets up the chapter by making the discus one of Siva's possessions.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:39"},"t:368":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":368,"t":["சக்கரம் பெற்றுநல் தாமோ தரந்தானும்","சக்கரந் தன்னைத் தரிக்கவொண் ணாமையால்","மிக்கரன் தன்னை விருப்புடன் அர்ச்சிக்கத்","தக்கநற் சக்தியைத் தாங்கூறு செய்ததே"],"p":"","r":"Damodara, having received the discus but unable to bear it, worshipped Hara with longing, and the fitting good power was divided into a share for him.","n":"Who makes the share (கூறு செய்தது) is not said; the next verse makes it Siva. The variant திரிக்கவொண்ணாமை (could not spin it) for தரிக்கவொண்ணாமை (could not bear it) changes the difficulty from holding the weapon to wielding it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","sivam","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:39"},"t:369":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":369,"t":["கூறது வாகக் குறித்துநற் சக்கரங்","கூறது செய்து கொடுத்தனன் மாலுக்குக்","கூறது செய்து கொடுத்தனன் சத்திக்குக்","கூறது செய்து தரித்தனன் கோலமே"],"p":"","r":"Intending it in portions, he divided the good discus: a share he gave to Mal, a share he gave to Shakti, and a share he kept and bore as his own form.","n":"கூறது (a share) begins every line. The discus here seems to stand for a power (சக்தி) that can be divided rather than a single weapon; what Shakti's share and Siva's share mean is left unstated.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:39"},"t:370":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":370,"t":["தக்கன்றன் வேள்வி தகர்த்தநல் வீரர்பால்","தக்கன்றன் வேள்வியில் தாமோ தரந்தானுஞ்","சக்கரந் தன்னைச் சசிமுடி மேல்விட","அக்கி உமிழ்ந்தது வாயுக் கரத்திலே"],"p":"","r":"At Daksha's sacrifice, when Damodara loosed his discus at the moon-crowned one among the heroes who shattered the sacrifice, fire was spat out in the hand of Vayu.","n":"The last line (அக்கி உமிழ்ந்தது வாயுக் கரத்திலே) is obscure: it may mean the discus spat fire, or that it was rendered harmless. In one telling Virabhadra's side swallowed the discus. சசிமுடி (moon-crowned) is Siva or Virabhadra.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","aaguthi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:39"},"t:371":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":371,"t":["எலும்புங் கபாலமும் ஏந்தி எழுந்த","வலம்பன் மணிமுடி வானவ ராதி","எலும்புங் கபாலமும் ஏந்தில நாகில்","எலும்புங் கபாலமும் இற்றுமண் ணாமே"],"p":"","r":"The strong one, first of the jewel-crowned gods, rose bearing bones and a skull; had he not borne them, those bones and skulls would have broken and become earth.","n":"Tradition reads the bones and skulls as those of Brahma, Vishnu and the gods, which Siva wears after their deaths at the end of an aeon; by bearing them he keeps them. The verse says only that without his bearing them they would fall to dust. வலம்பன் is taken as 'the strong one'; it may be a corrupt or rare word.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","yaakkai","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:40"},"t:372":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":372,"t":["பிரமனும் மாலும் பிரானேநான் என்னப்","பிரமன்மால் தங்கள்தம் பேதைமை யாலே","பரமன் அனலாய்ப் பரந்துமுன் நிற்க","அரனடி தேடி அரற்றுகின் றாரே."],"p":"","r":"When Brahma and Mal, through their own folly, each said 'I am the Lord', the Supreme stood before them spread out as fire, and they searched for Hara's feet and cried out.","n":"பேதைமை (folly) is named as the cause of the claim, not of the search. அரற்றுகின்றார் (cry out, lament) ends the verse on their helplessness rather than on any finding.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","pedhaimai","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:41"},"t:373":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":373,"t":["ஆமே ழுலகுற நின்றேம் அண்ணலுந்","தாமே ழுலகில் தழற்பிழம் பாய்நிற்கும்","வானே ழுலகுறும் மாமணி கண்டனை","நானே அறிந்தேன் அவனாண்மை யாலே"],"p":"","r":"While they said 'we stand filling the seven worlds', the Lord himself stood as a mass of flame through the seven worlds; I came to know him, the great-gem-throated one who fills the seven heavens, by his own might.","n":"The first line may be the two gods' boast. The variant last line, நானே அறிந்தேன் என் ஆண்மையினாலே (I knew him by my own power), reverses the sense: the text makes knowledge of Siva his gift, the variant the knower's achievement. With the text, the speaker succeeds where the gods' self-claim failed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","meyyunarvu","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:41"},"t:374":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":374,"t":["ஊனாய் உயிராய் உணர்வங்கி யாய்முன்னஞ்","சேணாய்வா னோரங்கித் திருவுரு வாய் அண்டத்","தாணுவும் ஞாயிறுந் தண்மதி யுங்கடந்","தாண்முழு தண்டமு மாகிநின் றானே"],"p":"","r":"As flesh, as life, as awareness and as fire, as the far heaven and as the holy form of the gods' fire, he passed beyond the pillar of the universe, the sun and the cool moon, and stood as the whole universe that he rules.","n":"The verse moves from the body (ஊன்) outward to the cosmos, so that Siva is at once in the flesh and beyond the sun and moon. தாணு means pillar and is also a name of Siva; here it may be the axis of the cosmos.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","iraivan","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:41"},"t:375":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":375,"t":["நின்றான் நிலமுழு தண்டத்துள் நீளியன்","அன்றே யவன்வடி வஞ்சின ராய்ந்தது","சென்றார் இருவர் திருமுடி மேற்செல","நன்றாங் கழலடி நாடவொண் ணாதே."],"p":"","r":"He stood, the tall one, through all the earth and the universe; then the two who sought out his form were afraid; one went up toward the holy crown, and the good anklet-feet could not be found either.","n":"அஞ்சினர் (they feared) adds what the story often leaves out: the search ends in fear, not just failure. Tradition has Brahma fly up as a swan and Vishnu dig down as a boar; the verse names neither.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:41"},"t:376":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":376,"t":["சேவடி ஏத்துஞ் செறிவுடை வானவர்","மூவடி தாவென் றானும் முனிவரும்","பாவடி யாலே பதஞ்செய் பிரமனுந்","தாவடி யிட்டுத் தலைப்பெய்து மாறே"],"p":"","r":"The gods close in devotion who praise his red feet, the one who asked for three steps, the sages, and Brahma who makes the worlds by verse: this is how they stride and come to bow their heads.","n":"The verse plays on அடி (foot, step, metrical foot): சேவடி, மூவடி (Vamana's three steps), பாவடி, தாவடி. பாவடியாலே பதஞ்செய் பிரமன் may mean Brahma who creates by Vedic verse. The last line is read as the great at last bowing to Siva's feet; it may also be ironic.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","panivu","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:41"},"t:377":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":377,"t":["தானக் கமலத் திருந்த சதுமுகன்","தானக் கருங் கடல் வாழித் தலைவனும்","ஊனத்தின் உள்ளே உயிர்போல் உணர்கின்ற","தானப் பெரும்பொருள் தன்மைய தாமே"],"p":"","r":"The four-faced one seated in the lotus and the lord who dwells in the dark sea both share the nature of that great being, who is sensed within the body as its life.","n":"The syntax could equally say that Brahma and Vishnu are of his nature, or that they abide within it. The variant கடலூழித் தலைவன் (lord of the sea at the aeon's end) changes the epithet for Vishnu. The image of life felt within flesh brings the cosmic Lord inside the body.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","uyir","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:41"},"t:378":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":378,"t":["ஆலிங் கனஞ்செய் தெழுந்த பரஞ்சுடர்","மேலிங்ஙன் வைத்ததோர் மெய்ந்நெறி முங்கண்","டாலிங் கனஞ்செய் துலகம் வலம்வருங்","கோலிங் கமைஞ்சருள் கூடலு மாமே"],"p":"","r":"The supreme light that rose as an embrace set here a true path above; seeing it, the world embraces it and circles it, and grace may be gained by those who turn to it.","n":"ஆலிங்கனம் (embrace) puns on இலிங்கம் (linga); the verse is traditionally read as the column of fire becoming the linga that the world worships by circling. முங்கண் (three eyes, or 'the front') and கோலிங்கமைஞ்சு are obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","vazhipaadu","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:41"},"t:379":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":379,"t":["வாள்கொடுத் தானை வழிபட்ட தேவர்கள்","ஆள்கொடுத் தெம்போல் அரனை அறிகிலர்","ஆள்கொடுத் தின்பங் கொடுத்துக் கோளாகத்","தாள்கொடுத் தானடி சாரகி லாரே"],"p":"","r":"The gods who worshipped the one who gave the sword do not know Hara as we do who gave ourselves to him; they will not reach the feet of him who took us as his own, gave us joy and gave us his feet to hold.","n":"ஆள்கொடுத்து is used twice: the devotees give themselves (become his servants), and he takes them as his own. The gods worship for a gift (the sword given to Brahma) and so stay outside; the contrast is between worship for boons and self-giving.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","vazhipaadu","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:41"},"t:380":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":380,"t":["ஊழி வலஞ்செய்தங் கோரும் ஒருவற்கு","வாழி சதுமுகன் வந்து வெளிப்படும்","வீழித் தலைநீர் விதித்தது தாவென","ஊழிக் கதிரோன் ஒளியைவென் றானே"],"p":"","r":"To the one who circled through the aeon and asked of him, the four-faced one comes forth and appears; when he said 'give what was ordained, the water from the fallen head', he outshone the light of the sun at the aeon's end.","n":"The verse is obscure. ஊழி வலஞ்செய்து echoes 349, where Brahma and Vishnu circle Siva through the aeon, and வீழித் தலைநீர் may refer to the Ganga from Siva's head or to the skull of Brahma. Only the close is clear: Siva's light surpasses the sun of the final dissolution.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:41"},"t:381":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":381,"t":["ஆதியோ டந்தம் இலாத பராபரம்","போதம தாகப் புணரும் பராபரை","சோதி யதனிற் பரந்தோன்றத் தோன்றுமாந்","தீதில் பரையதன் பால்திகழ் நாதமே"],"p":"","r":"The supreme beyond the supreme, without beginning or end, unites as knowledge with its consort power; as light spreads in that, the flawless Para appears, and from her nada shines.","n":"பராபரம் and பராபரை are a male and female pair, traditionally Siva and his Shakti; the variant பராபரன் makes the first explicitly personal. The order Supreme, Shakti, Para, nada is the tradition's sequence of pure manifestation; the verse states it compactly and the steps are not fixed by it alone.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","saththi","naadham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:42"},"t:382":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":382,"t":["நாதத்தில் விந்துவும் நாதவிந் துக்களில்","தீதற் றகம்வந்த சிவன்சத்தி என்னவே","பேதித்து ஞானங் கிரியை பிறத்தலால்","வாதித்த விச்சையில் வந்தெழும் விந்துவே"],"p":"","r":"In nada is bindu, and in nada and bindu Siva and Shakti come within, flawless; as they divide, knowledge and action are born, and bindu rises out of the will that impels them.","n":"ஞானம், கிரியை and இச்சை are traditionally Shakti's three powers of knowing, acting and willing. வாதித்த (impelling, or arguing) is unusual here.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naadham","vindhu","saththi","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:42"},"t:383":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":383,"t":["இல்லது சத்தி இடந்தனில் உண்டாகிக்","கல்லொளி போலக் கலந்துள் ளிருந்திடும்","வல்லது ஆக வழிசெய்த அப்பொருள்","சொல்லது சொல்லிடில் தூராதி தூரமே"],"p":"","r":"What was not comes to be in Shakti's place and stays mingled within like light in a gem; that Being which opened the way for it to become mighty is, if one tries to put it into words, farther than far.","n":"இல்லது can mean 'what does not exist' or 'what has no abode'; the reading turns on it. கல்லொளி, light in a stone or gem, is an image of presence that is inside yet unmixed. The last line is plain: the source is beyond speech.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:42"},"t:384":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":384,"t":["தூரத்திற் சோதி தொடர்ந்தொரு சத்தியாய்","ஆர்வத்து நாதம் அணைந்தொரு விந்துவாய்ப்","பாரச் சதாசிவம் பார்முதல் ஐந்துக்கும்","சார்வத்து சத்திஓர் சாத்துமா னாமே"],"p":"","r":"From that farness the light becomes a Shakti, nada joins with longing and becomes a bindu, and Sadasiva becomes the support of the five beginning with earth, with Shakti as their binding.","n":"சாத்துமான் at the end is obscure; I take it loosely as 'that which binds or holds'. The five from earth are the elements. The verse continues 383's தூராதி தூரமே by opening with தூரத்தில்.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","naadham","vindhu","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:42"},"t:385":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":385,"t":["மானின்கண் வானாகி வாயு வளர்ந்திடும்","கானின்கண் நீருங் கலந்து கடினமாய்த்","தேனின்கண் ஐந்துஞ் செறிந்தைந்து பூதமாய்ப்","பூவின்கண் நின்று பொருந்தும் புவனமே"],"p":"","r":"In mahat space arises and air grows, water mingles and hardens, the five crowd together and become the five elements, and standing in the flower the worlds take form.","n":"மான் is taken as mahat, the great tattva, and the verse as the usual chain of elements from space to earth; கான் (forest, or fire) and தேன் (honey) are hard to fit to that chain and may be corrupt or symbolic. பூ may be the earth or Brahma's lotus.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","maayai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:42"},"t:386":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":386,"t":["புவனம் படைப்பான் ஒருவன் ஒருத்தி","புவனம் படைப்பார்க்குப் புத்திரர் ஐவர்","புவனம் படைப்பானும் பூமிசை யானாய்","புவனம் படைப்பானப் புண்ணியன் தானே"],"p":"","r":"One He and one She create the world; those who create it have five sons; the creator is also the one seated on the flower, and the one who creates the world is that holy one himself.","n":"The five sons are traditionally the five lords of the five acts (Brahma, Vishnu, Rudra, Maheshvara, Sadasiva). The verse repeats புவனம் படைப்பான் four times: creation is credited to the pair, to Brahma and to the holy one, and the last line gathers them into one.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aindhozhil","saththi","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:42"},"t:387":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":387,"t":["புண்ணியன் நந்தி பொருந்தி உலகெங்கும்","தண்ணிய மானை வளர்த்திடுஞ் சத்தியுஞ்","கண்ணியல் பாகக் கலவி முழுதுமாய்","மண்ணியல் பாக மலர்ந்தெழு பூவிலே"],"p":"","r":"The holy Nandi, joined with the Shakti who nurtures cool mahat in every world, in union wholly by nature of the eye, blossoms in the flower of earth's nature.","n":"கண்ணியல்பாக and மண்ணியல்பாக are paired but unclear; the sense of Siva and Shakti together flowering into the world is clear, the details are not.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:42"},"t:388":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":388,"t":["நீரகத் தின்பம் பிறக்கும் நெருப்பிடை","காயத்திற் சோதி பிறக்கும்அக் காற்றிடை","ஓர்வுடை நல்லுயிர்ப் பாதம் ஒலிசத்தி","நீரிடை மண்ணின் நிலைபிறப் பாமே"],"p":"","r":"In water pleasure is born, in fire light in the body, in air the sounding power of good life's breath, and between water and earth the state of birth.","n":"The verse seems to match elements to qualities (taste, form, sound, smell) and to the body, but its lines do not give a clean scheme; the variant காய்கதிர் for காயத்திற் would make 'the burning sun's light' instead of 'light in the body'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:42"},"t:389":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":389,"t":["உண்டுல கேழும் உமிழ்ந்தான் உடனாகி","அண்டத் தமரர் தலைவனும் ஆதியுங்","கண்டச் சதுர்முகக் காரணன் தன்னொடும்","பண்டிவ் வுலகம் படைக்கும் பொருளே"],"p":"","r":"With the one who swallowed and spat out the seven worlds, with the head of the gods and the first one, and with the four-faced cause, it is that Being who of old creates this world.","n":"The one who swallowed the worlds is Vishnu in his familiar myth, the four-faced one Brahma. The verse makes them companions of the creator, not the creator; whether அண்டத்தமரர் தலைவன் and ஆதி are Rudra or the Lord himself is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","aindhozhil"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:42"},"t:390":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":390,"t":["ஓங்கு பெருங்கடல் உள்ளுறு வானொடும்","பாங்கார் கயிலைப் பராபரன் தானும்","வீங்குங் கமல மலர்மிசை மேலயன்","ஆங்குயிர் வைக்கும் அதுவுணர்ந் தானே"],"p":"","r":"With the sky that lies within the vast ocean, the Supreme of Kailasa, and Brahma above on the swelling lotus, he knew how life is placed there.","n":"The three figures are usually taken as Vishnu (the one in the ocean), Siva of Kailasa and Brahma; the subject of உணர்ந்தானே is not named.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:42"},"t:391":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":391,"t":["காரணன் அன்பிற் கலந்தெங்கும் நின்றவன்","நாரணன் நின்ற நடுவுட லாய்நிற்கும்","பாரணன் அன்பிற் பதஞ்செய்யும் நான்முகன்","ஆரண மாஉல காயமர்ந் தானே"],"p":"","r":"The Cause stands everywhere, mingled in love; Narayana stands as the middle body; the four-faced one, from love of the Lord of the world, shapes its parts and is settled as the world of the Veda.","n":"காரணன், நாரணன், பாரணன் and ஆரணம் chime. பதஞ்செய்யும் may mean 'makes ready' or 'makes the stations'; நடுவுடல் (middle body) is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","aindhozhil"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:42"},"t:392":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":392,"t":["பயன்எளி தாம்பரு மாமணி செய்ய","நயன்எளி தாகிய நம்பன்ஒன் றுண்டு","அயஓளி யாயிருந் தங்கே படைக்கும்","பயனெளி தாம்வய ணந்தெளிந் தேனே"],"p":"","r":"There is a Lord whose kindness is easy to reach, who makes the great gem yield its fruit with ease; staying there as Brahma's light he creates, and I have understood clearly the easy way to the fruit.","n":"பருமாமணி (great gem) and அயஒளி (Brahma's light, or the light of the unborn) are not fixed by the verse. எளிது (easy) is said three times; the point that the Lord is easy of access is plain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","thiruvarul","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:42"},"t:393":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":393,"t":["போக்கும் வரவும் புனிதன் அருள்புரிந்","தாக்கமுஞ் சிந்தைய தாகின்ற காலத்து","மேக்கு மிகநின்ற எட்டுத் திசையொடுந்","தாக்குங் கலக்குந் தயாபரன் தானே"],"p":"","r":"When the pure one in grace grants going and coming, and growth becomes thought, the merciful one himself strikes and stirs the eight directions that stand high.","n":"போக்கும் வரவும, going and coming, is commonly taken as death and birth. Calling the one who stirs the directions தயாபரன், the supremely merciful, makes the stirring itself an act of grace.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:42"},"t:394":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":394,"t":["நின்றுயி ராக்கு நிமலன்என் னாருயிர்","ஒன்றுயி ராக்கும் அளவை உடலுற","முன்துய ராக்கும் உடற்குந் துணையதா","நன்றுயிர்ப் பானே நடுவுநின் றானே"],"p":"","r":"The spotless one who makes life stands in the middle: he measures my precious life into one life to fit a body, is help to the body that brings earlier suffering, and makes the life good.","n":"முன்துயர் (earlier suffering) may be the karma that the body brings with it. நடுவு நின்றான் may mean 'stood within' or 'stood impartial'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["uyir","yaakkai","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:42"},"t:395":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":395,"t":["ஆகின்ற தன்மையில் அக்கணி கொன்றையன்","வேகின்ற செம்பொனின் மேலணி மேனியன்","போகின்ற சீவன் புகுந்துட லாய்உளன்","ஆகின்ற தன்மைசெய் ஆண்டகை யானே"],"p":"","r":"In making things become, the Lord who wears bone beads and konrai, with a body adorned like burning red gold, enters the departing soul and is there as its body; he is the master who works becoming.","n":"அக்கணி is taken as அக்கு அணி, wearing bone or rudraksha beads, and the konrai flower and red-gold body are Siva's marks. போகின்ற சீவன் may be the soul leaving one body for the next.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","uyir","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:42"},"t:396":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":396,"t":["ஒருவன் ஒருத்தி விளையாடல் உற்றார்","இருவர் விளையாட்டும் எல்லாம் விளைக்கும்","பருவங்கள் தோறும் பயன்பல வான","திருவொன்றிற் செய்கை செகமுற்று மாமே"],"p":"","r":"One He and one She have set to play; the play of the two brings forth everything, many fruits in every season, and the work in one Tiru becomes the whole world.","n":"திரு is commonly taken as Shakti, so that the world is her work; it could also be read as fortune or holiness. விளையாடல் makes creation play, not labour or need.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","saththi","aindhozhil"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:42"},"t:397":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":397,"t":["புகுந்தறி வான்புவ னாபதி அண்ணல்","புகுந்தறி வான்புரி சக்கரத் தண்ணல்","புகுந்தறி வான்மலர் மேலுறை புத்தேள்","புகுந்தறி யும்முடிக் காகிநின் றாரே"],"p":"","r":"The Lord of the worlds enters and knows, the lord with the discus enters and knows, the god on the flower enters and knows; they stand as those who enter and know up to the crown.","n":"The three are usually Rudra, Vishnu and Brahma. The last line is obscure: முடிக்காகி may mean they know only so far, below the summit, which would set limits on these gods.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","aindhozhil"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:42"},"t:398":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":398,"t":["ஆணவச் சத்தியும் ஆம்அதில் ஐவருங்","காரிய காரண ஈசர் கடைமுறை","பேணிய ஐந்தொழி லால்விந்து விற்பிறந்து","ஆணவம் நீங்கா தவரென லாகுமே"],"p":"","r":"The power of anava and the five within it, the lords of effect and cause in their order, born of bindu through the five acts they tend, can be called those from whom anava has not left.","n":"ஆணவம் is the root impurity of Siddhanta. The verse's claim, as I read it, is that the five lords who carry out the five acts are themselves still bound souls, not the Lord: a strong ranking of the gods below Siva.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["malam","aindhozhil","vindhu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:42"},"t:399":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":399,"t":["உற்றமுப் பாலொன்று மாயாள் உதயமா","மற்றைய மூன்று மாயோ தயம்விந்து","பெற்றவன் நாதம் பரையிற் பிறத்தலால்","துற்ற பரசிவன் தொல்விளை யாட்டிதே"],"p":"","r":"Of the three parts that came, one is the rising of Maya; the other three are bindu risen from Maya; since nada, which begot it, is born of Para, this is the ancient play of the supreme Siva.","n":"The count (three parts, one, then 'the other three') does not add up as read; a word may be lost or முப்பால் may be meant differently. The variant பெற்றவள் makes the begetter female.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maayai","vindhu","naadham","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:42"},"t:400":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":400,"t":["ஆகாய மாதி சதாசிவ ராதியென்","போகாத சத்தியுட் போந்துடன் போந்தனர்","மாகாய ஈசன் அரன்மால் பிரமனாம்","ஆகாயம் பூமி காண அளித்தலே"],"p":"","r":"Space and the rest, and Sadasiva and the others, went out together within the unfailing Shakti: the great-bodied Isa, Hara, Mal and Brahma, so that space and earth are cared for.","n":"The last line is unclear; with the variant அளித்ததே it would say that this is what gave space and earth.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","aindhozhil","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:42"},"t:401":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":401,"t":["அளியார் முக்கோணம் வயிந்தவந் தன்னில்","அளியார் திரிபுரை யாமவள் தானே","அளியார் சதாசிவ மாகி அமைவாள்","அளியார் கருமங்கள் ஐந்துசெய் வாளே"],"p":"","r":"In the gracious triangle born of bindu she is Tripura; she settles as Sadasiva, and she performs the five acts.","n":"அளியார், repeated at each line's head, may mean 'full of grace' or 'like bees'. முக்கோணம் (triangle) and வயிந்தவம் (born of bindu) suggest a yantra. Here the five acts are hers, where 404 gives them to the One.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","aindhozhil","vindhu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:42"},"t:402":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":402,"t":["வாரணி கொங்கை மனோன்மணி மங்கலி","காரணி காரிய மாகக் கலந்தவள்","வாரணி ஆரணி வானவர் மோகினி","பூரணி போதாதி போதமு மாமே"],"p":"","r":"Manonmani with banded breasts, the auspicious one, mingled as cause and effect; she of the Veda, enchantress of the gods, the full one, is also the knowing at the root of all knowing.","n":"A string of names of the goddess. With the variant பூதாதி the last line reads 'source of the elements' rather than 'knowing at the root of knowing'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","maayai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:42"},"t:403":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":403,"t":["நின்றது தானாய் நிறைந்த மகேசுரன்","சென்றங் கியங்கும் அரந்திரு மாலவன்","மன்றது செய்யும் மலர்மிசை மேலயன்","என்றிவ ராக இசைந்திருந் தானே"],"p":"","r":"He agreed to be these: Maheshvara who stands full as himself, Hara and Tirumal who go and move there, and Brahma on the flower who makes.","n":"The gods are one Lord's forms, as the verse says, but மன்றது செய்யும் for Brahma is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","aindhozhil"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:42"},"t:404":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":404,"t":["ஒருவனு மேஉல கேழும் படைத்தான்","ஒருவனு மேஉல கேழும் அளித்தான்","ஒருவனு மேஉல கேழுந் துடைத்தான்","ஒருவனு மேஉல கோடுயிர் தானே"],"p":"","r":"One alone created the seven worlds, one alone protected them, one alone wiped them away, and one alone is the life with the worlds.","n":"The fourfold ஒருவனுமே insists on one agent where the chapter elsewhere names several gods and a Shakti. The variant உடலோடுயிர் makes the last line 'the life with the body'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","aindhozhil","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:42"},"t:405":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":405,"t":["செந்தா மரைவண்ணன் தீவண்ணன் எம் இறை","மைந்தார் முகில்வண்ணன் மாயஞ்செய் பாசத்தும்","கொந்தார் குழலியர் கூடிய கூட்டத்தும்","அந்தார் பிறவி அறுத்துநின் றானே"],"p":"","r":"Our Lord, red-lotus coloured, fire coloured and dark as the rain cloud, stands having cut off the birth that comes in Maya's bond and in union with women of flowered hair.","n":"The three colours are commonly matched to Brahma, Rudra and Vishnu, all as the one Lord. The variant last line ஐந்தார் பிறவி அமைத்து நின்றானே says the opposite: that he set up five-fold birth. In a chapter on creation the variant fits as well as the received text.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","piravi","maayai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:42"},"t:406":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":406,"t":["தேடுந் திசைஎட்டுஞ் சீவன் உடல்உயிர்","கூடும் பிறவிக் குணஞ்செய்த மாநந்தி","ஊடும் அவர்தம் துள்ளத்துள் ளேநின்று","நாடும் வழக்கமும் நான்அறிந் தேனே"],"p":"","r":"The great Nandi, who made the nature of the births in which soul, body and life come together in all eight directions people search, stands within those who seek him, and I know their way of seeking.","n":"ஊடும் may mean 'who sulk with him' or 'who pass through', so 'those who seek' is a guess. The verse recurs almost word for word as 414.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","piravi","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:42"},"t:407":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":407,"t":["ஓராய மேஉல கேழும் படைப்பதும்","ஓராய மேஉல கேழும் அளிப்பதும்","ஓராய மேஉல கேழுந் துடைப்பதும்","ஓராய மே உல கோடுயிர் தானே"],"p":"","r":"One host creates the seven worlds, one host protects them, one host wipes them away, and one host is the life with the worlds.","n":"The verse repeats 404 with ஓராயமே in place of ஒருவனுமே. ஆயம் can mean a company or a gathering, so the agent may be the Lord's assembled powers; tradition differs on the word, and it may be a corruption of 404's line.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aindhozhil","saththi","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:42"},"t:408":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":408,"t":["நாதன் ஒருவனும் நல்ல இருவருங்","கோது குலத்தொடுங் கூட்டிக் குழைத்தனர்","ஏது பணியென் றிசையும் இருவருக்","காதி இவனே அருளுகின் றானே"],"p":"","r":"The one Lord and the good two kneaded beings together with the dross of their kinds; to the two who stand ready asking what their task is, he, the first, gives grace.","n":"The two are usually taken as Brahma and Vishnu, and கோது குலம் as the impure classes of beings. The verse is compressed and the reading is tentative.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:42"},"t:409":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":409,"t":["அப்பரி செண்பத்து நான்குநூ றாயிரம்","மெய்ப்பரி செய்தி விரிந்துயி ராய்நிற்கும்","பொய்ப்பரி செய்திப் புகலும் மனிதர்கட்","கிப்பரி சேஇருள் மூடிநின் றானே"],"p":"","r":"In that way he spreads truly as life in eighty-four lakh kinds; to men who talk falsely he stands covered by darkness.","n":"The eighty-four lakh kinds of birth are a common count, also in the Tevaram, as Sengalvaraya notes. மெய்ப்பரிசு and பொய்ப்பரிசு play true against false manner: the Lord is present in all life, yet hidden from the false.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uyir","piravi","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:42"},"t:410":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":410,"t":["ஆதித்தன் சந்திரன் அங்கிஎண் பாலர்கள்","போதித்த வானொலி பொங்கிய நீர்ப்புவி","வாதித்த சத்தாதி வாக்கு மனாதிகள்","ஓதுற்ற மாயையின் விந்துவின் உற்றதே"],"p":"","r":"Sun, moon, fire, the eight guardians, sky with its sound, water and earth, the contending qualities beginning with sound, speech, mind and the rest all come from the bindu of Maya, as is taught.","n":"The list runs from cosmic bodies to the inner faculties, so that both the outer world and the mind come from one material source.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vindhu","maayai","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:42"},"t:411":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":411,"t":["புகுந்துநின் றான்வெளி யாய்இரு ளாகிப்","புகுந்துநின் றான்புகழ் வாய்இகழ் வாகிப்","புகுந்துநின் றான்உட லாய்உயி ராகிப்","புகுந்துநின் றான்புந்தி மன்னிநின் றானே"],"p":"","r":"He has entered and stands as open space and as darkness, as praise and as blame, as body and as life; he has entered and stays fixed in the mind.","n":"The pairs are opposites (light and dark, praise and blame, body and life): the Lord keeps the world by being both sides of it. புகுந்துநின்றான், repeated, makes his presence an entering, not only an encompassing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","uyir","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:43"},"t:412":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":412,"t":["தானே திசையொடு தேவரு மாய்நிற்குந்","தானே உடலுயிர் தத்துவ மாய்நிற்குந்","தானே கடல்மலை யாதியு மாய்நிற்குந்","தானே உலகில் தலைவனு மாமே"],"p":"","r":"He himself stands as the directions and the gods, as body, life and the tattvas, as ocean, mountain and the rest, and he is also the leader of the world.","n":"The last line adds rule to presence: he is the world and also its head.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","thaththuvam","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:43"},"t:413":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":413,"t":["உடலாய் உயிராய் உலகம தாகிக்","கடலாய் கார்முகில் நீர்ப்பொழி வானாய்","இடையாய் உலப்பிலி எங்குந்தா னாகி","அடையார் பெருவழி அண்ணல் நின்றானே"],"p":"","r":"As body, as life, as the world, as the ocean, as the dark cloud and the sky that pours rain, as what lies between, unfailing and everywhere himself, the Lord stands as the great way that those who do not come to him do not reach.","n":"அடையார் can mean 'those who do not reach' or 'enemies'. The variant பெருவெளி makes the last line 'the great open space' instead of 'the great way'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","mazhai","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:43"},"t:414":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":414,"t":["தேடுந் திசைஎட்டுஞ் சீவன் உடல்உயிர்","கூடு மரபிற் குணஞ்செய்த மாநந்தி","ஊடும் அவர்தம துள்ளத்து ளேநின்று","நாடும் வழக்கமும் நான்அறிந் தேனே"],"p":"","r":"The great Nandi, who made the nature of the line in which soul, body and life come together in all eight directions people search, stands within those who seek him, and I know their way of seeking.","n":"Nearly identical to 406; here மரபு (line, order) stands for 406's பிறவி (birth), and the variant restores பிறவி. ஊடும் is unclear, as in 406.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","piravi","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:43"},"t:415":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":415,"t":["தானொரு காலந் தனிச்சுட ராய்நிற்குந்","தானொரு கால்சண்ட மாருத மாய்நிற்குந்","தானொரு காலந் தண்மழை யாய்நிற்குந்","தானொரு காலந்தண் மாயனு மாமே"],"p":"","r":"At one time he stands as a lone flame, at another as a fierce gale, at another as cool rain, and at another he is the cool Mayan.","n":"மாயன் is usually Vishnu, the preserver, which suits a chapter on protection; it can also be read as the one of Maya. The changing forms run from fire and storm to cool rain, from harm to care.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","mazhai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:43"},"t:416":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":416,"t":["அன்பும் அறிவும் அடக்கமு மாய்நிற்கும்","இன்பமும் இன்பக் கலவியு மாய்நிற்கும்","முன்புறு காலமும் ஊழியு மாய்நிற்கும்","அன்புற ஐந்தில் அமர்ந்துநின் றானே"],"p":"","r":"He stands as love, knowledge and restraint, as pleasure and the joy of union, as time past and the aeon, and he sits, with love, in the five.","n":"ஐந்து (the five) is not named: the senses, the elements or the five acts are all possible. Putting love, restraint and the pleasure of union side by side makes the Lord present in both discipline and desire.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","anbu","adakkam","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:43"},"t:417":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":417,"t":["உற்று வனைவான் அவனே உலகினைப்","பெற்று வனைவான் அவனே பிறவியைச்","சுற்றிய சாலுங் குடமுஞ் சிறுதூதை","மற்றும் அவனே வனையவல் லானே"],"p":"","r":"He is the one who shapes the world, setting to it as a potter; he shapes births; the turning wheel, the pot, the small jar and all the rest he alone can shape.","n":"The verse makes the Lord the potter, the maker, not the clay; tradition reads this as the Siddhanta view that God is the efficient cause while Maya is the material. சுற்றிய சால் may be the potter's wheel or a large pot.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","piravi","aindhozhil"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:43"},"t:418":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":418,"t":["உள்ளுயிர்ப் பாயுட லாகிநின் றான்நந்தி","வெள்ளுயி ராகும் வெளியான் நிலங்கொளி","உள்ளுயிர்க் கும்உணர் வேயுட லுட்பரந்","தள்ளுயி ராவண்ணந் தாங்கிநின் றானே"],"p":"","r":"Nandi stands as the body, being the breath within; he who is open space, pure life and the light that earth holds, spreads in the body as the awareness of the inner life and holds it so the life is not cast off.","n":"வெள்ளுயிர் (white or pure life) and நிலங்கொளி are obscure. The last line is clear: the Lord's keeping is holding life in the body, which values the body as the place of life.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","uyir","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:43"},"t:419":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":419,"t":["தாங்கருந் தன்மையுந் தானவை பல்லுயிர்","வாங்கிய காலத்து மற்றோர் பிறிதில்லை","ஓங்கி எழுமைக்கும் யோகாந்த மவ்வழி","தாங்கிநின் றானும்அத் தாரணி தானே"],"p":"","r":"The nature that is hard to bear is also his; when he takes back the many lives there is nothing else; for all seven births that way is yoga's end, and he who stands supporting it is also that earth.","n":"தாரணி may be the earth, the bearer, or dharana, yogic holding of the mind; the pun with தாங்கி (holding) suits both. யோகாந்தம் is a technical term not fixed by the verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","uyir","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:43"},"t:420":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":420,"t":["அணுகினுஞ் சேயவன் அங்கியிற் கூடி","நணுகினும் ஞானக் கொழுந்தொன்று நல்கும்","பணுகினும் பார்மிசைப் பல்லுயி ராகித்","தணிகினும் மண்ணுடல் அண்ணல்செய் வானே"],"p":"","r":"Though near he is far; joining in fire and drawing close, he gives a single shoot of knowledge; though he bends low he becomes the many lives on earth, and though he is quiet the Lord makes the earthly body.","n":"பணுகினும் and தணிகினும் are uncertain words; ஞானக் கொழுந்து (the tender shoot of knowledge) is the clearest image. Fire (அங்கி) may be the sacrificial fire or the inner fire of yoga.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","iraivan","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:43"},"t:421":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":421,"t":["அங்கிசெய் தீசன் அகலிடஞ் சுட்டது","அங்கிசெய் தீசன் அலைகடற் சுட்டது","அங்கிசெய் தீசன் அசுரரைச் சுட்டது","அங்கியவ் வீசற்குக் கைஅம்பு தானே"],"p":"","r":"The Lord who made fire burned the wide world, burned the surging sea, burned the asuras; fire is that Lord's arrow in hand.","n":"Burning the asuras recalls the burning of the three cities, where the arrow is usually Vishnu with fire at its tip; here fire is the whole arrow. அங்கிசெய்து may also be 'wielding fire'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aindhozhil","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:44"},"t:422":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":422,"t":["இலயங்கள் மூன்றினும் ஒன்றுகற் பாந்த","நிலையன் றழிந்தமை நின்றுணர்ந் தேனால்","உலைதந்த மெல்லரி போலும் உலகம்","மலைதந்த மானிலந் தான்வெந் ததுவே"],"p":"","r":"In the three dissolutions I came to know that the order which stood as one aeon was undone; the world, like soft rice on the boil, and the great land the mountains bore were burnt.","n":"இலயங்கள் மூன்று and ஒன்றுகற்பாந்த are technical and not fixed by the verse. The image is plain: the world softens and is consumed like rice in the pot.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aindhozhil","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:44"},"t:423":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":423,"t":["பதஞ்செய்யும் பாரும் பனிவரை எட்டும்","உதஞ்செய்யும் ஏழ்கடல் ஓதம் முதலாங்","குதஞ்செய்யும் அங்கி கொளுவியா காசம்","விதஞ்செய்யும் நெஞ்சில் வியப்பில்லை தானே"],"p":"","r":"The earth that gives footing, the eight snowy mountains, the seven seas with their tides and all the rest, the fire takes hold of and burns, and space too; there is no wonder in the heart that knows it.","n":"பதஞ்செய்யும், உதஞ்செய்யும், குதஞ்செய்யும், விதஞ்செய்யும் rhyme but are hard to gloss; குதம் may be 'destroying' and விதம் 'forms'. The last line may mean the wise are not astonished at the world's end.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aindhozhil","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:44"},"t:424":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":424,"t":["கொண்டல் வரைநின் றிழிந்த குலக்கொடி","அண்டத்துள் ஊறி யிருந்தெண் டிசையாதி","ஒன்றின் பதந்செய்த ஓம்என்ற அப்புறக்","குண்டத்தின் மேலங்கி கோலிக் கொண்டானே"],"p":"","r":"The noble stream that came down from the cloud mountain, seeping through the cosmos to the eight directions, he set as fire upon the pit beyond Om.","n":"குலக்கொடி may be the Ganga or a power such as kundalini; குண்டம் is the fire pit of the rite. The verse seems to make cosmic burning a fire rite beyond the syllable Om, but every term here is open.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aindhozhil","piranavam","aaguthi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:44"},"t:425":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":425,"t":["நித்தசங் காரம் உறக்கத்து நீள்மூடம்","வைத்தசங் காரமுஞ் சாக்கிரா தீதமாஞ்","சுத்தசங் காரந் தொழிலற்ற கேவலம்","உய்த்தசங் காரம் பரன் அருள் உண்மையே"],"p":"","r":"Daily dissolution is sleep's long stupor; the held dissolution is the state beyond waking; pure dissolution is kevala where action ceases; the dissolution that lifts up is the truth of the Supreme's grace.","n":"The four names (நித்த, வைத்த, சுத்த, உய்த்த சங்காரம்) are technical. கேவலம் in Siddhanta usually names the soul's state wholly under anava, which sits oddly with 'pure'. Only the last term is constant across 425 to 428: the highest dissolution is grace.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aindhozhil","thiruvarul","malam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:44"},"t:426":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":426,"t":["நித்தசங் காரம் இரண்டுடல் நீவுதல்","வைத்தசங் காரமும் மாயாசங் காரமாஞ்","சுத்தசங் காரம் மனாதீதந் தோய்வுறல்","உய்த்தசங் காரஞ் சிவன் அருள் உண்மையே"],"p":"","r":"Daily dissolution is the putting off of the two bodies; the held dissolution is the dissolution of Maya; pure dissolution is immersion beyond the mind; the dissolution that lifts up is the truth of Siva's grace.","n":"இரண்டுடல் (two bodies) may be the gross and subtle bodies. The same names as 425 receive different definitions.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aindhozhil","maayai","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:44"},"t:427":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":427,"t":["நித்தசங் காரம் கருவிடர் நீக்கினால்","ஒத்தசங் காரமும் உடலுயிர் நீவுதல்","சுத்தசங் காரம் அதீதத்துட் டோ ய்வுறல்","உய்த்தசங் காரம் பரனருள் உண்மையே"],"p":"","r":"Daily dissolution is when the instruments' troubles are removed; the matching dissolution is shedding body and life; pure dissolution is immersion in what is beyond; the dissolution that lifts up is the truth of the Supreme's grace.","n":"ஒத்த stands where 425 and 426 have வைத்த; the variant line வைத்தசங் காரங் கேவலம் ஆன்மாவுக் restores வைத்த and ties it to kevala. கருவி (instruments) are the faculties.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aindhozhil","thiruvarul","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:44"},"t:428":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":428,"t":["நித்தசங் காரமும் நீடிளைப் பாற்றலின்","வைத்தசங் காரமும் மன்னும் அனாதியிற்","சுத்தசங் காரமுந் தோயாப் பரன்அருள்","உய்த்தசங் காரமும் நாலா மதிக்கிலே"],"p":"","r":"Daily dissolution is in relieving long weariness; held dissolution is in the abiding beginningless bond; pure dissolution is the Supreme's untouched grace; and there is the dissolution that lifts up, when the four are weighed.","n":"அனாதி (beginningless) is commonly taken as anava, the beginningless impurity. நாலா மதிக்கிலே closes the series by asking that the four be weighed together.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aindhozhil","malam","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:44"},"t:429":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":429,"t":["பாழே முதலா எழும்பயிர் அப்பயிர்","பாழாய் அடங்கினும் பண்டைப்பாழ் பாழாகா","வாழாச்சங் காரத்தின் மாலயன் செய்தியாம்","பாழாம் பயிராய் அடங்கும்அப் பாழிலே"],"p":"","r":"The crop that rises with waste ground as its source, though it sinks back into waste, does not make the old waste waste away; the doings of Mal and Ayan, in the dissolution that does not last, sink as crop of the waste back into that waste.","n":"பாழ் is barren ground and also the void; the verse plays on it six times. The plain sense is a field: what grows from it returns to it while the ground endures. Taken symbolically, the source (Maya or the void) outlasts what it yields, and even Vishnu's and Brahma's works end there.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aindhozhil","nilaiyaamai","maayai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:44"},"t:430":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":430,"t":["தீயவைத் தார்மிங்கள் சேரும் வினைதனை","மாயவைத் தான்வைத்த வன்பதி ஒன்றுண்டு","காயம்வைத் தாங்கலந் தெங்கும் நினைப்பதோர்","ஆயம்வைத் தானுணர் வாரவைத் தானே"],"p":"","r":"There is one strong Lord who set the deeds that reach evildoers to perish; he gave a body, mingled everywhere, set a company that thinks, and set those who understand.","n":"மாயவைத்தான் may be 'made to perish' or 'set in Maya', and the reading turns on it. On the first reading, destruction here is the destruction of bad karma, a mercy, not a punishment. ஆயம் recalls 407.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","iraivan","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:44"},"t:431":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":431,"t":["உள்ளத் தொருவனை உள்ளுறு சோதியை","உள்ளம்விட் டோ ரடி நீங்கா ஒருவனை","உள்ளமுந் தானும் உடனே இருக்கினும்","உள்ளம் அவனை உருவறி யாதே"],"p":"","r":"The One in the heart, the light dwelling within, the One who never leaves the heart by a single step: though the heart and he are together, the heart does not know his form.","n":"உள்ளம் opens every line, so the verse keeps returning to the heart that holds him and still cannot see him. That nearness itself is the concealment, which is why the verse opens this chapter. The variant line 3 (உள்ளமும் அவனும் உறவாயிருந்தும், though heart and he are kin) keeps the sense.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","manam","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:45"},"t:432":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":432,"t":["இன்பப் பிறவி படைத்த இறைவனுந்","துன்பஞ்செய் பாசத் துயருள் அடைத்தனன்","என்பிற் கொளுவி இசைந்துறு தோற்றசை","முன்பிற் கொளுவி முடிகுவ தாமே"],"p":"","r":"The Lord who made birth for joy also shut it up in the sorrow of the bond that causes suffering; fastening skin and flesh to bone and binding it to what went before, it runs on to its end.","n":"தோற்றசை is read as தோல் தசை (skin and flesh). முன்பிற் கொளுவி, fastening to what is before, is commonly taken as binding the body to past deeds; the verse does not say so. The point is that the same Lord who intends joy conceals the soul inside the body and its bonds. The variant அடைந்தனன் would say the Lord himself entered that sorrow.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["paasam","piravi","yaakkai","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:45"},"t:433":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":433,"t":["இறையவன் மாதவன் இன்பம் படைத்த","மறையவன் மூவரும் வந்துடன் கூடி","இறையவன் செய்த இரும்பொறி யாக்கை","மறையவன் வைத்த பரிசறி யாதே"],"p":"","r":"Though the Lord, Madhavan and the Vedic one who made pleasure, all three, come and gather together, they do not know how the Hidden One placed the great machine of a body that the Lord made.","n":"மறையவன் plays on மறை, which means both the Veda and hiding: in line 1 it most naturally names Brahma, in line 4 it can name Siva as the Concealer. Which god the first இறையவன் is (Rudra, or Siva himself) is unclear. இரும்பொறி யாக்கை, the body as a great mechanism, is a striking image. The variant பரிசறியாரே makes the three gods the subject explicitly.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","yaakkai","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:45"},"t:434":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":434,"t":["காண்கின்ற கண்ணொளி காதல்செய் தீசனை","ஆண்பெண் அலியுரு வாய்நின்ற ஆதியை","ஊண்படு நாவுடை நெஞ்சம் உணர்ந்திட்டுச்","சேண்படு பொய்கைச் செயலணை யாரே"],"p":"","r":"Those who come to know, with a heart and a tongue given to food, the Lord who loves the light of the seeing eye, the First who stands as male, female and neuter forms, will not come near the workings of the distant pool.","n":"சேண்படு பொய்கைச் செயல், the working of the far-off pool, is obscure. It may be the pool of births, which the knowers escape, or bathing at a distant sacred tank, which they no longer need. ஊண்படு நாவுடை நெஞ்சம் can also be read against the knowers, as the food-bound heart that cannot reach him; the syntax allows both.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","meyyunarvu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:45"},"t:435":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":435,"t":["தெருளும் உலகிற்குந் தேவர்க்கும் இன்பம்","அருளும் வகைசெய்யும் ஆதிப் பிரானுஞ்","சுருளுஞ் சுடருறு தூவெண் சுடரும்","இருளும் அறநின் றிருட்டறை யாமே"],"p":"","r":"The first Lord, who makes the ways by which joy is given to the world that grows clear and to the gods, and the pure white flame with its coiled radiance, stands where darkness ends and yet becomes a dark chamber.","n":"The paradox of the last line, beyond darkness yet a dark room, fits the chapter: the light is hidden from those he conceals it from. சுருளும் சுடர், the coiled flame, is traditionally taken as a yogic light, perhaps kundalini; the plain sense is simply a radiant fire.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","thiruvarul","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:45"},"t:436":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":436,"t":["அரைகின் றருள்தரும் அங்கங்கள் ஓசை","உரைக்கின்ற ஆசையும் ஒன்றோடொன் றொவ்வாப்","பரக்கும் உருவமும் பாரகந் தானாய்க்","கரகின் றவைசெய்த காண்டகை யானே."],"p":"","r":"The one worth beholding, who made the limbs that yield grace, the sound, the desire that speaks, the many unlike forms spreading out and the earth, becomes these himself and hides in them.","n":"அரைகின்று and கரகின்றவை are odd forms; கரக்கின்ற (hiding) is the likely sense of the second and carries the chapter's theme. அங்கங்கள் may be bodily limbs or the Vedangas. The verse is too compressed to fix beyond its drift: he hides by becoming his works.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","maayai","aindhozhil"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:45"},"t:437":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":437,"t":["ஒளித்துவைத் தேனுள் ளுறவுணர்ந் தீசனை","வெளிப்பட்டு நின்றருள் செய்திடு மீண்டே","களிப்பொடுங் காதன்மை என்னும் பெருமை","வெளிப்பட் டிறைஞ்சினும் வேட்சியு மாமே"],"p":"","r":"Knowing the kinship within, I hid the Lord inside me; coming forth, he grants grace again; and the greatness called joy and love, even when it comes out in open worship, becomes an offering too.","n":"The verse turns concealment round: here the devotee hides the Lord within, and the Lord comes out in grace. வேட்சி may mean desire or sacrifice (from வேட்டல்), so the last line may say open worship is still an offering, or that it turns into longing. The verse leads toward the next chapter, on grace.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","thiruvarul","anbu","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:45"},"t:438":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":438,"t":["நின்றது தானாய் நிறைந்த மகேசுரன்","சென்றங் கியங்கும் அரந்திரு மாலவன்","மன்றது செய்யும் மலர்மிசை மேலயன்","என்றிவ ராகி இசைந்திருந் தானே"],"p":"","r":"Maheshvara, who fills all and stands as himself, Hara and Tirumal who go and move there, and Ayan on the flower who does the work: becoming these, he abides in harmony with them.","n":"In Siddhanta, Maheshvara is traditionally the god of the concealing act, which may be why he is named first here. மன்றது செய்யும் (doing the work of the hall, or of the assembly) is unclear, and the variant கியங்கி யயந்திரு changes line 2. The verse makes the gods modes of the one Lord, as 386 and 404 do.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","aindhozhil","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:45"},"t:439":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":439,"t":["ஒருங்கிய பாசத்துள் உத்தமச் சித்தன்","இருங்கரை மேலிருந் தின்புற நாடி","வருங்கரை ஓரா வகையினிற் கங்கை","அருங்கரை பேணில் அழுக்கற லாமே"],"p":"","r":"Within the gathered bond, the excellent siddha sits on the great bank seeking joy; if one keeps to the rare bank of the Ganga in a way that does not reckon on the shore to come, impurity can be washed away.","n":"A plain reading is bathing in the Ganga; a yogic reading, common in tradition, places the Ganga within the body and the bank at the crown. வருங்கரை ஓரா (not reckoning the coming shore) and the variant சித்தின் (of consciousness) for சித்தன் leave the subject unclear. அழுக்கு is likely the soul's impurity rather than bodily dirt.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["paasam","malam","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:45"},"t:440":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":440,"t":["மண்ணொன்று தான்பல நற்கல மாயிடும்","உண்ணின்ற யோனிகட் கெல்லாம் ஒருவனே","கண்ணொன்று தான்பல காணுந் தனைக்காணா","அண்ணலும் இவ்வண்ண மாகிநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"One clay becomes many good vessels; for all the wombs within, there is one; one eye sees many things but does not see itself; the Lord too stands in this way.","n":"Two images close the chapter. The clay gives one source in many forms; the eye gives the seer who is not seen. The eye may stand for the Lord, unseen because he is the one who sees, or for the soul, which cannot see the Lord who is in it; both readings fit concealment.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","iraivan","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:45"},"t:441":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":441,"t":["எட்டுத் திசையும் எறிகின்ற காற்றொடு","வட்டத் திரையனல் மாநிலம் ஆகாயம்","ஒட்டி உயிர்நிலை என்னுமிக் காயப்பை","கட்டி அவிழ்ப்பான் கண்ணுதல் காணுமே"],"p":"","r":"The brow-eyed Lord sees to it: he ties and unties this bag of a body, which binds together the wind that blows in the eight directions, fire, the water of circling waves, the great earth and the sky, and is called the seat of life.","n":"காயப்பை (the body as a bag) is plain, not contemptuous: the stress is on the Lord's tying and untying, birth and death, under a chapter on grace. வட்டத் திரை, circling waves, stands for water among the five elements.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","sivam","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:46"},"t:442":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":442,"t":["உச்சியில் ஓங்கி ஒளிதிகழ் நாதத்தை","நச்சியே இன்பங்கொள் வார்க்கு நமன்இல்லை","விச்சும் விரிசுடர் மூன்றும் உலகுக்குத்","தச்சு மவனே சமைக்கவல் லானே"],"p":"","r":"For those who long for the nada that rises and shines at the crown and take their joy in it, there is no Yama; he alone is able to fashion, as a carpenter does, the sky and the three spreading lights for the world.","n":"உச்சியில் ஒளிதிகழ் நாதம், the shining sound at the crown, is a yogic term taken as the inner sound heard in meditation. விச்சும் is read as விசும்பு (sky), but may be வித்து (seed). The three lights are traditionally sun, moon and fire.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naadham","kootru","yogam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:46"},"t:443":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":443,"t":["குசவன் திரிகையில் ஏற்றிய மண்ணைக்","குசவன் மனத்துற்ற தெல்லாம் வனைவன்","குசவனைப் போல்எங்கள் கோன்நந்தி வேண்டில்","அசைவில் உலகம் அதுயிது வாமே"],"p":"","r":"From the clay set on his wheel the potter shapes whatever is in his mind; like the potter, if our lord Nandi wills it, the unmoving world becomes this or that.","n":"The potter image makes the world the Lord's to shape at will. அசைவில் உலகம் (the world without motion) suggests inert matter that moves only by his will; the variant அயைவில் is unclear.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:46"},"t:444":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":444,"t":["விரியுடை யான்விகிர் தன்மிகு பூதப்","படையுடை யான்பரி சேஉல காக்குங்","கொடையுடை யாங்குணம் எண்குண மாகுஞ்","சடையுடை யாஞ்சிந்தை சார்ந்துநின் றானே"],"p":"","r":"He of wide expanse, the one unlike all others, the lord of a host of many bhutas, who by his nature has the bounty of keeping the world, whose qualities are the eight qualities, the matted-haired one, abides close to the mind.","n":"எண்குணம், the eight qualities, is the term Kural 9 uses of the one to be worshipped. Here the eight belong to the matted-haired Siva. விரியுடையான் may mean the expansive one or the one with spread matted hair; விகிர்தன் is the one who is different, unlike the world.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","thiruvarul","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:46"},"t:445":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":445,"t":["உகந்துநின் றேபடைத் தான்உல கேழும்","உகந்துநின் றேபடைத் தான்பல ஊழி","உகந்துநின் றேபடைத் தான்ஐந்து பூதம்","உகந்துநின் றேஉயிர் ஊன்படைத் தானே"],"p":"","r":"Gladly he made the seven worlds; gladly he made the many ages; gladly he made the five elements; gladly he made life and flesh.","n":"உகந்து (gladly, delighting) opens every line. Placed under grace, the verse suggests that creation is itself an act of the Lord's goodwill toward souls, as tradition takes it: he makes bodies so that souls may work out their bonds.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","thiruvarul","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:46"},"t:446":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":446,"t":["படைத்துடை யான்பண் டுலகங்கள் ஏழும்","படைத்துடை யான்பல தேவரை முன்னே","படைத்துடை யான்பல சீவரை முன்னே","படைத்துடை யான்பர மாகிநின் றானே"],"p":"","r":"He made and owns the seven worlds of old; he made the many gods first; he made the many souls first; having made them, he stands as the Supreme.","n":"படைத்துடையான் joins making and owning: he is maker and possessor. The verse repeats முன்னே (first, of old) for both gods and souls, so neither is prior to his making; it does not say whether souls themselves are created or only embodied, a point Siddhanta settles by holding souls to be beginningless.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:46"},"t:447":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":447,"t":["ஆதி படைத்தனன் ஐம்பெரும் பூதம்","ஆதி படைத்தனன் ஆசில்பல் ஊழி","ஆதி படைத்தனன் எண்ணிலி தேவரை","ஆதி படைத்தவை தாங்கிநின் றானே"],"p":"","r":"The First made the five great elements; the First made the many faultless ages; the First made the countless gods; and the First stands bearing all he made.","n":"The last line adds keeping to making: தாங்கி நின்றான், he stands bearing them. The variant அனாதி (the beginningless) for ஆதி (the first) would sharpen the point that the maker has no beginning.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","aindhozhil"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:46"},"t:448":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":448,"t":["அகன்றான் அகலிடம் ஏழுமொன் றாகி","இவன்றா னெனநின் றெளியனும் அல்லன்","சிவன்றான் பலபல சீவனும் ஆகி","நவின்றான் உலகுறு நம்பனு மாமே"],"p":"","r":"He is vast, the seven wide realms becoming one in him; he is not easy to point to as 'this is he'; Siva himself becomes the many, many souls, and is the beloved one who spoke and came into the world.","n":"சிவன்றான் பலபல சீவனும் ஆகி, Siva becoming the many souls, reads on its face as identity. Siddhanta tradition takes ஆகி as 'pervading as' or 'standing inseparably with', keeping Lord and soul distinct; the verse alone does not settle it. நவின்றான் (who spoke) is often taken as giving the Vedas and Agamas.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","uyir","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:46"},"t:449":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":449,"t":["உண்ணின்ற சோதி உறநின்ற ஓருடல்","விண்ணின் றமரர் விரும்பும் விழுப்பொருள்","மண்ணின்ற வானோர் புகழ்திரு மேனியன்","கண்ணின்ற மாமணி மாபோத மாமே"],"p":"","r":"The light within, the one body that stands close, the excellent thing the immortals in heaven desire, the one of holy form praised by the gods on earth, the great gem in the eye: this is the great awareness.","n":"மண்ணின்ற வானோர், the gods who stand on earth, is usually taken as devotees or Brahmins. மாபோதம் (great awareness) and its variant மாபோதகம் (great teacher) give different endings: the Lord as knowledge itself, or as the teacher. கண்ணின்ற மாமணி may be the pupil of the eye, or a yogic light.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","gnaanam","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:46"},"t:450":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":450,"t":["ஆரும் அறியாத அண்டத் திருவுருப்","பார்முத லாகப் பயிலுங் கடத்திலே","நீரினிற் பால்போல நிற்கின்ற நேர்மையைச்","சோராமற் காணுஞ் சுகம்அறிந் தேனே"],"p":"","r":"I came to know the bliss of seeing, without slackening, how the holy form of the cosmos, which no one knows, stands rightly like milk in water within the vessel of the body, made from earth and the other elements.","n":"கடம் (pot, vessel) is the body. Milk in water is a presence everywhere in it and inseparable from it, unseen but not absent; this answers 431, where the heart could not see the one within. The poet ends in the first person: he has found it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","yaakkai","meyyunarvu","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:46"},"t:451":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":451,"t":["ஆக்குகின் றான்முன் பிரிந்த இருபத்தஞ்","சாக்குகின் றானவ னாதிஎம் ஆருயிர்","ஆக்குகின் றான்கர்ப்பக் கோளகை யுள்ளிருந்","தாக்குகின் றான்அவன் ஆவ தறிந்தே"],"p":"","r":"The Lord, the First, our precious life, makes the twenty-five that were set apart before; staying within the globe of the womb he forms the embryo, knowing what it is to become.","n":"இருபத்தஞ்சு (twenty-five) is traditionally taken as the tattvas from earth up to purusha that make up the soul's body. ஆவது அறிந்தே, knowing what it will become, is usually read as his knowing the soul's karma before shaping the body.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["karu","thaththuvam","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:47"},"t:452":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":452,"t":["அறிகின்ற மூலத்தின் மேல்அங்கி அப்புச்","செறிகின்ற ஞானத்துச் செந்தாள் கொளுவிப்","பொறைநின்ற இன்னுயிர் போந்துற நாடிப்","பறிகின்ற பத்தெனும் பாரஞ்செய் தானே"],"p":"","r":"Above the root he sets fire and water, joins the red foot of knowledge, and, so that the sweet life borne there may enter, he lays on it the burden of the ten.","n":"மூலம் (the root) is taken as the muladhara, அங்கி and அப்பு as fire and water, and பத்து as the ten vital airs, though ten months is also possible. செந்தாள் (red foot) and பறிகின்ற are obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["karu","praanan","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:47"},"t:453":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":453,"t":["இன்புறு காலத் திருவர்முன் பூறிய","துன்புறு பாசத் துயர்மனை வானுளன்","பண்புறு காலமும் பார்மிசை வாழ்க்கையும்","அன்புறு காலத் தமைத்தொழிந் தானே"],"p":"","r":"At the hour when the two join in pleasure, the Lord, present in the sorrowful house of bondage that went before them, fixes in that moment of love the span of time and the life on earth the child will have.","n":"The verse puts the fixing of the lifespan (காலமும்) and the earthly life (வாழ்க்கையும்) at the moment of conception. Whose bondage the துயர்மனை is, the parents' or the soul's, is not clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["karu","paasam","oozh"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:47"},"t:454":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":454,"t":["கருவை ஒழிந்தவர் கண்டநால் மூவேழ்","புருடன் உடலில் பொருந்துமற் றோரார்","திருவின் கருக்குழி தேடிப் புகுந்த","உருவம் இரண்டாக ஓடி விழுந்ததே"],"p":"","r":"Those freed from birth have seen that the 'four and three sevens' join in the man's body, which others do not know; the form that sought and entered the womb fell as two.","n":"நால் மூவேழ் is read either as 4 + 21 = 25, the tattvas of 451, or as some other count; I can't fix it. உருவம் இரண்டாக may mean the seed dividing, or twins, or the two parents' contributions.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["karu","thaththuvam","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:47"},"t:455":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":455,"t":["விழுந்தது லிங்கம் விரிந்தது யோனி","ஒழிந்த முதல்ஐந்தும் ஈரைந்தொ டேறிப்","பொழிந்த புனல்பூதம் போற்றுங் கரணம்","ஒழிந்த நுதல்உச்சி உள்ளே ஒளித்ததே"],"p":"","r":"The seed falls and the womb opens; the first five, joined by the ten, rise, and the poured water, the elements and the faculties hide within at the brow and the crown.","n":"லிங்கம் and யோனி are used plainly of the sexual organs here. The first five are taken as the elements and the ten as the senses or vital airs; நுதல் உச்சி (brow, crown) points to yogic centres where the faculties are said to lie latent.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["karu","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:47"},"t:456":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":456,"t":["பூவின் மணத்தைப் பொருந்திய வாயுவுந்","தாவி உலகில் தரிப்பித்த வாறுபோல்","மேவிய சீவனில் மெல்லநீள் வாயுவுங்","கூவி அவிழுங் குறிக்கொண்ட போதே"],"p":"","r":"As the wind that has taken a flower's scent spreads it and holds it in the world, so the long subtle breath settled in the soul, at the marked moment, calls it and lets it go.","n":"The plain image is clear: breath carries the soul as wind carries scent. What குறிக்கொண்ட போது (the marked time) and அவிழும் (opens, lets go) refer to, conception or the soul's leaving a former body, is not fixed by the verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["praanan","uyir","karu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:47"},"t:457":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":457,"t":["போகின்ற எட்டும் புகுகின்ற பத்தெட்டும்","மூழ்கின்ற முத்தனும் ஒன்பது வாய்தலும்","நாகமும் எட்டுடன் நாலு புரவியும்","பாகன் விடானெனிற் பன்றியு மாமே"],"p":"","r":"The eight that go out, the eighteen that come in, the freed one sunk within, the nine gates, the serpent with the eight and the four horses: if the driver does not let go, it becomes even a pig.","n":"A list of the body's parts as a chariot team. The numbers are traditionally matched with breaths, tattvas and senses, and the last line with birth in a low body, but I can't fix any of them. The same verse stands as 2926 with a different second line.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","piravi","karu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:47"},"t:458":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":458,"t":["ஏற எதிர்க்கில் இறையவன் றானாகும்","மாற எதிர்க்கில் அரியவன் றானாகும்","நேரொக்க வைக்கின் நிகர்ப்போதத் தானாகும்","பேரொத்த மைந்தனும் பேரர சாளுமே"],"p":"","r":"If the forces meet rising, the child will be like the Lord; if they meet reversed, like Hari; if they are kept level, of balanced understanding; and a son matching his name will rule a great kingdom.","n":"The subject of ஏற, மாற, நேரொக்க is unstated; it is usually taken as the breath or the seed at conception. The variant அரியயன் would make the second Brahma, or Hari and Brahma together.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["karu","praanan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:47"},"t:459":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":459,"t":["ஏயங் கலந்த இருவர்தஞ் சாயத்துப்","பாயுங் கருவும் உருவா மெனப்பல","காயங் கலந்தது காணப் பதிந்தபின்","மாயங் கலந்த மனோலய மானதே"],"p":"","r":"From the mingled essence of the two, the flowing seed takes form; once the body, compounded of many things, is settled, the mind sinks into a stupor mixed with maya.","n":"ஏயம் and சாயம் are unclear; சாயம் may be colour or essence. மனோலயம் mixed with maya is usually read as the soul losing awareness in the womb.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["karu","maayai","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:47"},"t:460":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":460,"t":["கர்ப்பத்துக் கேவல மாயாள் கிளைகூட்ட","நிற்குந் துரியமும் பேதித்து நினைவெழ","வற்புறு காமியம் எட்டாதல் மாயேயஞ்","சொற்புறு தூய்மறை வாக்கினாஞ் சொல்லே"],"p":"","r":"In the womb maya joins her brood to the soul in its kevala state; turiya is broken and thought arises; karma takes firm hold and maya's bonds begin, as the word of the pure Veda says.","n":"கேவலம் (the soul's isolated state, bound only by anava), துரியம், காமியம் (karma) and மாயேயம் (the products of maya) are Siddhanta terms; the verse seems to describe the soul passing from kevala to its embodied (sakala) state at conception. எட்டாதல் is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["karu","maayai","malam","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:47"},"t:461":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":461,"t":["என்பால் மிடைந்து நரம்பு வரிக்கட்டிச்","செம்பால் இறைச்சி திருந்த மனைசெய்து","இன்பால் உயிர்நிலை செய்த இறையோங்கும்","நன்பால் ஒருவனை நாடுகின் றேனே"],"p":"","r":"He wove bones together, bound them with nerves like lashings, made a fitting house of red flesh, and set in it, sweetly, the seat of life; I seek that good one, the Lord who rises there.","n":"The body is described as a house built by the Lord, bone for frame, nerve for cord, flesh for walls, and the poet seeks the builder who dwells in it. The variant நண்பால் would make the last line 'I seek him in friendship'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","iraivan","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:47"},"t:462":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":462,"t":["பதஞ்செய்யும் பால்வண்ணன் மேனிப் பகலோன்","இதஞ்செய்யு மொத்துடல் எங்கும் புகுந்து","குதஞ்செய்யும் அங்கியின் கோபந் தணிப்பான்","விதஞ்செய்யு மாறே விதித்தொழிந் தானே"],"p":"","r":"The milk-coloured one, bright as the sun, who brings things to ripeness, enters the whole body for its good, calms the rage of the fire below, ordains how it should be, and leaves.","n":"பால்வண்ணன் is an epithet of Siva. The 'fire' of குதம் is read as the heat of the lower body or womb, which would harm the embryo if not checked. The verse's subject may be the Lord or the sun as his agent.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["karu","iraivan","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:47"},"t:463":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":463,"t":["ஒழிபல செய்யும் வினையுற்ற நாளே","வழிபல நீராடி வைத்தெழு வாங்கிப்","பழிபல செய்கின்ற பாசக் கருவைச்","சுழிபல வாங்கிச் சுடாமல்வைத் தானே"],"p":"","r":"When the deeds that bring many endings came due, the Lord took the embryo of bondage, which does much that is blamed, through its many turns and kept it from being burned.","n":"Line 2 (வழிபல நீராடி வைத்தெழு வாங்கி) is obscure. The point taken is that the embryo, formed by karma, is protected in the heat of the womb by the Lord.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["karu","vinai","paasam","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:47"},"t:464":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":464,"t":["சுக்கில நாடியில் தோன்றிய வெள்ளியும்","அக்கிர மத்தே தோன்றுமவ் வியோனியும்","புக்கிடும் எண்விரல் புறப்பட்டு நால்விரல்","அக்கரம் எட்டும்எண் சாணது வாகுமே"],"p":"","r":"The white seed in its channel and the womb in its order: entering eight finger-breadths and coming out four, and with the eight letters, it becomes a body of eight spans.","n":"The body measuring eight of its own spans is an old teaching. எண்விரல், நால்விரல் (eight and four finger-breadths) are often read of the breath's reach, and அக்கரம் எட்டும் is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["karu","vindhu","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:47"},"t:465":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":465,"t":["போகத்துள் ஆங்கே புகுந்த புனிதனுங்","கோசத்துள் ஆகங்கொணர்ந்த கொடைத்தொழில்","ஏகத்துள் ஆங்கே இரண்டெட்டு மூன்றைந்து","மோகத்துள் ஆங்கொரு முட்டைசெய் தானே"],"p":"","r":"The pure one who entered there in the act of love, whose work of giving set a body in the sheath, there in that one act made an egg of two eights and three fives.","n":"இரண்டெட்டு மூன்றைந்து (sixteen and fifteen) is commonly matched with the kalas of the moon and sun, or other counts; I can't fix it. முட்டை (egg) names the embryo. The variant கோகத்துள் would replace கோசத்துள் (in the sheath).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["karu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:47"},"t:466":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":466,"t":["பிண்டத்தில் உள்ளுறு பேதைப் புலன்ஐந்தும்","பிண்டத்தி னூடே பிறந்து மரித்தது","அண்டத்தின் உள்ளுறு சீவனும் அவ்வகை","அண்டத்து நாதத் தமர்ந்திருந் தானே"],"p":"","r":"The five foolish senses within the body arise and die within the body; in the same way the soul within the cosmos is born and dies there, and the Lord rests in the cosmos's nada.","n":"பிண்டம் (body) and அண்டம் (cosmos) are set side by side as microcosm and macrocosm. Who அமர்ந்திருந்தான் refers to, the Lord or the soul, is not stated.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","uyir","naadham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:47"},"t:467":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":467,"t":["இலைபொறி யேற்றி யெனதுடல் ஈசன்","துலைப்பொறி யிற்கரு ஐந்துட னாட்டி","நிலைபொறி முப்பது நீர்மை கொளுவி","உலைப்பொறி ஒன்பதில் ஒன்றுசெய் தானே"],"p":"","r":"The Lord built my body like machines: raising the leaf-machine, planting the embryo with the five in the balance-machine, fixing the thirty in the standing machine, and making one thing of the nine in the forge-machine.","n":"பொறி (machine, contrivance) repeats in every line. The numbers five, thirty and nine are matched by tradition with elements, tattvas and openings, but the verse does not say what they are.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["karu","yaakkai","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:47"},"t:468":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":468,"t":["இன்புற் றிருவர் இசைவித்து வைத்தமண்","துன்பக் கலசம் அணைவான் ஒருவனே","ஒன்பது நீர்ச்சால் கலசம் பதினெட்டு","வெந்தது சூளை விளைந்தது தானே"],"p":"","r":"From the clay the two set in their pleasure, one alone forms the pot of sorrow; with nine water-jars and eighteen pots, the kiln fires and it is made.","n":"A potter's image: the parents supply clay, the Lord is the potter, the womb the kiln. ஒன்பது and பதினெட்டு may be the nine openings and some count of parts.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["karu","yaakkai","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:47"},"t:469":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":469,"t":["அறியீ ருடம்பினி லாகிய வாறும்","பிறியீ ரதனிற் பெருகுங் குணங்கள்","செறியீ ரவற்றினுட் சித்திகள் இட்ட","தறியவீ ரைந்தினு ளானது பிண்டமே"],"p":"","r":"You don't know how the body came to be, you don't set apart the qualities that grow in it, you don't go into them; the body is what took shape within the ten, so that what was set within it might be known.","n":"ஈரைந்து (ten) is usually the ten months in the womb. சித்திகள் இட்டது may be powers placed in the body or simply what was set there; the last line is compressed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","arivu","karu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:47"},"t:470":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":470,"t":["உடல்வைத்த வாறும் உயிர்வைத்த வாறும்","மடைவைத்த ஒன்பது வாய்தலும் வைத்துத்","திடம்வைத்த தாமரைச் சென்னியுள் அங்கிக்","கடைவைத்த ஈசனைக் கைகலந் தேனே"],"p":"","r":"The way he set the body and the way he set the life, placing the nine gates as sluices, and the Lord who set the fire at the end within the firm lotus at the head: I have joined hands with him.","n":"The nine gates are the body's openings, here called மடை, sluices built to purpose. The lotus at the head with fire is a yogic image of the crown. The verse treats the body's design as the Lord's work and the place where the poet meets him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","iraivan","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:47"},"t:471":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":471,"t":["கேட்டுநின் றேன்எங்குங் கேடில் பெருஞ்சுடர்","மூட்டுகின் றான்முதல் யோனி மயனவன்","கூட்டுகின் றான்குழம் பின்கரு வையுரு","நீட்டுநின் றாகத்து நேர்ப்பட்ட வாறே"],"p":"","r":"I have heard it: the great unfailing light everywhere is the one who kindles it; the first maker of wombs, the divine builder, joins the fluid into the embryo's form and draws it out until it comes right in the body.","n":"மயன் is the divine architect of the Puranas, here used of the Lord as maker of wombs (முதல் யோனி). The construction of line 1 is loose.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["karu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:47"},"t:472":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":472,"t":["பூவுடன் மொட்டுப் பொருந்த அலர்ந்தபின்","காவுடைத் தீபங் கலந்து பிறந்திடும்","நீரிடை நின்ற குமிழி நிழலதாய்ப்","பாருடல் எங்கும் பரந்தெட்டும் பற்றுமே"],"p":"","r":"Once flower and bud have joined and opened, a light within the grove mingles and is born; like a bubble standing in water, a shadow, it spreads through the earthly body, and the eight take hold.","n":"காவுடைத் தீபம் (the lamp of the grove) is obscure. The eight are taken in the next verse as five senses and three inner faculties, the subtle body (puriyattaka) of the tradition.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["karu","yaakkai","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:47"},"t:473":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":473,"t":["எட்டினுள் ஐந்தாகும் இந்திரி யங்களும்","கட்டிய மூன்று கரணமு மாய்விடும்","ஒட்டிய பாச உணர்வென்னுங் காயப்பை","கட்டி அவிழ்த்திடுங் கண்ணுதல் காணுமே"],"p":"","r":"Of the eight, five become the senses and three the bound inner faculties; this body-bag, which is the bond's awareness clinging to the soul, the Lord with the eye in his brow ties, unties and watches.","n":"கரணம் மூன்று are traditionally mind, intellect and ego. Calling the body பாச உணர்வு, the awareness of the bond, makes it the means by which the soul knows while bound.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","paasam","yaakkai","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:47"},"t:474":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":474,"t":["கண்ணுதல் நாமங் கலந்துடம் பாயிடைப்","பண்ணுதல் செய்து பசுபாசம் நீங்கிட","எண்ணிய வேதம் இசைந்த பரப்பினை","மண்முத லாக வகுத்துவைத் தானே"],"p":"","r":"Joining his name with it, the Lord with the brow eye fashions the body, and so that the soul's bond may fall away he lays out the whole range the Veda describes, starting from earth.","n":"The body is made so that பசுபாசம் நீங்கிட, the bond may leave the soul: embodiment is for release. மண்முதலாக is read as the tattvas from earth upward. கண்ணுதல் நாமம் கலந்து is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","pasu","paasam","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:47"},"t:475":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":475,"t":["அருளல்ல தில்லை அரனவன் அன்றி","அருளில்லை யாதலி னவ்வோர் உயிரைத்","தருகின்ற போதிரு கைத்தாயர் தம்பால்","வருகின்ற நண்பு வகுத்திடுந் தானே"],"p":"","r":"There is nothing but grace, and no grace apart from Hara; so when he gives a soul he also arranges the love that comes to it from its two mothers.","n":"இருகைத் தாயர் is taken as the mother who bears and the one who nurses, or the mother and the divine mother; I can't fix it. The point is that a child's being loved is itself the Lord's grace.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","anbu","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:47"},"t:476":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":476,"t":["வகுத்த பிறவியை மாதுநல் லாளுந்","தொகுத்திருள் நீக்கிய சோதி யவனும்","பகுத்துணர் வாக்கிய பல்லுயிர் எல்லாம்","வகுத்துள்ளும் நின்றதோர் மாண்பது வாமே"],"p":"","r":"The birth so arranged is the greatness of the good Lady and of the Light who gathered and removed the darkness: they set out all the many beings given discernment and stand within them too.","n":"மாது நல்லாள் is Shakti and சோதியவன் Siva. பகுத்துணர்வாக்கிய (made to discern) may refer to giving beings the faculties to know.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saththi","sivam","uyir","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:47"},"t:477":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":477,"t":["மாண்பது வாக வளர்கின்ற வன்னியுங்","காண்பது ஆண்பெண் அலியெனுங் கற்பனை","பூண்பது மாதா பிதாவழி போலவே","ஆம்பதி செய்தானச் சோதிதன் ஆண்மையே"],"p":"","r":"The fire that grows into greatness, the fashioning seen as male, female or neither, and the likeness borne down the mother's and father's line: all these were brought about by the Light's power.","n":"வன்னி (fire) is unclear: the vital heat of the embryo, or the Lord as fire. The variant வன்னியைக் makes it an object. கற்பனை here seems to mean form or fashioning, not fancy.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["karu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:47"},"t:478":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":478,"t":["ஆண்மிகில் ஆணாகும் பெண்மிகிற் பெண்ணாகும்","பூணிரண் டொத்துப் பொருந்தில் அலியாகும்","தாண்மிகு மாகில் தரணி முழுதாளும்","பாணவ மிக்கிடில் பாய்ந்ததும் இல்லையே"],"p":"","r":"If the male part prevails it becomes a male, if the female part a female, and if the two are equal neither; if the strength is great the child will rule the whole earth, and if the force is excessive nothing takes at all.","n":"The first two lines state an old belief about sex determination. தாண் and பாணவம் are obscure; தாண் may be a misreading of தாள் (strength).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["karu","vindhu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:47"},"t:479":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":479,"t":["பாய்ந்தபின் னஞ்சோடில் ஆயுளும் நூறாகும்","பாய்ந்தபின் நாலோடில் பாரினில் எண்பதாம்","பாய்ந்திடும் வாயுப் பகுத்தறிந் திவ்வகை","பாய்ந்திடும் யோகிக்குப் பாய்ச்சலு மாமே"],"p":"","r":"If after the flow the breath runs five, life will be a hundred years; if four, eighty on earth; the yogi who discerns the flowing breath this way can also direct its flow.","n":"Five and four are usually taken as the reach of the breath in finger-breadths at the moment of union. The last line turns the teaching toward breath control.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["praanan","karu","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:47"},"t:480":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":480,"t":["பாய்கின்ற வாயுக் குறையிற் குறளாகும்","பாய்கின்ற வாயு விளைக்கின் முடமாகும்","பாய்கின்ற வாயு நடுப்படிற் கூனாகும்","பாய்கின்ற வாயுமா தர்க்கில்லை பார்க்கிலே"],"p":"","r":"If the flowing breath falls short the child is a dwarf, if it is twisted lame, if it is caught midway hunchbacked; and this, looked at closely, does not lie with the woman's breath.","n":"விளைக்கின் is read as 'bends' or 'twists'. The last line is unclear: it may say that the woman's breath does not decide these things, or that women have none of this.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["praanan","karu","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:47"},"t:481":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":481,"t":["மாதா உதரம் மலமிகில் மந்தனாம்","மாதா உதரம் சலமிகில் மூங்கையாம்","மாதா உதரம் இரண்டொக்கில் கண்ணில்லை","மாதா உதரத்தில் வந்த குழவிக்கே."],"p":"","r":"If the mother's belly is heavy with faeces the child is dull, if with urine mute, and if both are equal blind: this for the child that has come into the mother's belly.","n":"மலம் and சலம் here are faeces and urine, not the Siddhanta impurity. The verse states a physiological belief about the mother's condition at conception or in pregnancy.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karu","yaakkai","undi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:47"},"t:482":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":482,"t":["குழவியும் ஆணாம் வலத்தது வாகில்","குழவியும் பெண்ணாம் இடத்தது வாகில்","குழவியும் இரண்டாம் அபான னெதிர்க்கில்","குழவி அலியாகுங் கொண்டகால் ஒக்கிலே"],"p":"","r":"The child is male if the breath is on the right, female if on the left, two if the downward breath opposes, and neither if the breaths taken are equal.","n":"The verse does not name its subject; tradition takes வலம், இடம் as the right and left nostril or channel. அபானன் is the downward breath of yoga physiology.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["karu","praanan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:47"},"t:483":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":483,"t":["கொண்டநல் வாயு இருவர்க்கும் ஒத்தெழில்","கொண்ட குழவியுங் கோமள மாயிடுங்","கொண்டநல் வாயு இருவர்க்குங் குழறிடில்","கொண்டதும் இல்லையாங் கோள்வளை யாட்கே"],"p":"","r":"If the good breath rises evenly in both, the child conceived will be fair; if it falters in both, the bangled woman conceives nothing.","n":"The variant ஒத்தேறில் (rises together) fits the sense.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karu","praanan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:47"},"t:484":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":484,"t":["கோள்வளை உந்தியிற் கொண்ட குழவியுந்","தால்வளை யுள்ளே தயங்கிய சோதியாம்","பால்வளர்ந் துள்ளே பகலவன் பொன்னுருப்","போல்வளர்ந் துள்ளே பொருந்துரு வாமே"],"p":"","r":"The child conceived in the bangled woman's belly is a light trembling within; nourished as with milk, it grows within like the sun's golden form and takes its fitting shape.","n":"தால்வளை (perhaps the curve of the womb) and பால் வளர்ந்து are obscure. The light image echoes 471.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["karu","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:47"},"t:485":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":485,"t":["உருவம் வளர்ந்திடும் ஒண்டிங்கள் பத்திற்","பருவம தாகவே பாரினில் வந்திடும்","மருவி வளர்ந்திடு மாயையி னாலே","அருவம தாவதிங் காரறி வாரே"],"p":"","r":"The form grows for ten bright months and comes into the world in its season; it grows mingled with maya, and who here knows how it comes to be without form?","n":"அருவமதாவது may mean how the embodied one becomes formless again at death, or how the formless Lord is in it; both readings stand.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["karu","maayai","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:47"},"t:486":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":486,"t":["இட்டான் அறிந்திலன் ஏற்றவள் கண்டிலள்","தட்டான் அறிந்தும் ஒருவர்க் குரைத்திலன்","பட்டாங்கு சொல்லும் பரமனும் அங்குளன்","கெட்டேன் இம்மாயையின் கீழ்மையெவ் வாறே"],"p":"","r":"He who planted the seed did not know, she who received it did not see, the smith who knew told no one, and the Supreme who tells things as they are is there too: I am lost; how low is this maya!","n":"தட்டான், the goldsmith who shapes, is taken as Brahma the fashioner or as the Lord himself; பரமன் then is the Supreme behind him. The point is that the parents have no knowledge of what they make.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["karu","maayai","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:47"},"t:487":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":487,"t":["இன்புற நாடி இருவருஞ் சந்தித்துத்","துன்புறு பாசத்தில் தோன்றி வளர்ந்தபின்","முன்புற நாடி நிலத்தின்முன் தோன்றிய","தொன்புற நாடிநின் றோதலு மாமே"],"p":"","r":"Born of two who met seeking pleasure and grown within painful bondage, once born into the world one can still seek the Ancient and stand there chanting.","n":"Lines 3 and 4 play on நாடி (seeking) and are loose; the variant சிந்தித்து (thinking) for சந்தித்து changes the first line slightly. The reading that birth from bondage still permits worship is mine.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","paasam","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:47"},"t:488":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":488,"t":["குயிற்குஞ்சு முட்டையைக் காக்கைக் கூட்டிட்டால்","அயிர்ப்பின்றிக் காக்கை வளர்க்கின் றதுபோல்","இயக்கில்லை போக்கில்லை ஏனென்ப தில்லை","மயக்கத்தால் காக்கை வளர்க்கின்ற வாறே"],"p":"","r":"If a cuckoo's egg is put in a crow's nest, the crow raises it without suspicion; it does not go, does not leave, does not ask why: the crow raises it out of delusion.","n":"The verse states only the image. Placed here it is taken as parents raising a child whose soul is not theirs, in the delusion that it is.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maayai","makkal","pattru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:47"},"t:489":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":489,"t":["முதற்கிழங் காய்முளை யாயம் முளைப்பின்","அதற்புத லாய்ப்பல மாய்நின் றளிக்கும்","அதற்கது வாயின்ப மாவதுபோல","அதற்கது வாய்நிற்கும் ஆதிப் பிரானே"],"p":"","r":"As the root-tuber becomes a sprout, shoots up, becomes a bush and then fruit and gives, and in each stage is the sweetness proper to it, so the First Lord stands as each thing to each thing.","n":"அதற்கது வாய் (being that to that) is the key phrase: the Lord is present in every stage of growth as what that stage is. The image continues the chapter's growth of the embryo.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:47"},"t:490":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":490,"t":["ஏனோர் பெருமைய னாகிலும் எம்மிறை","ஊனே சிறுமையுள் உட்கலந் தங்குளன்","வானோர் அறியும் அளவல்லன் மாதேவன்","தானே அறியுந் தவத்தினி னுள்ளே"],"p":"","r":"Though our Lord's greatness is beyond all others, he is mingled within the smallness of flesh; Mahadeva is beyond the measure of the gods' knowing, and is known within tapas.","n":"தானே அறியும் may mean that he alone knows himself, or that he is known of himself within tapas. ஊனே சிறுமையுள் sets the great Lord in the small body.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","yaakkai","thavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:47"},"t:491":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":491,"t":["பரத்திற் கரைந்து பதிந்தநற் காயம்","உருத்தரித் திவ்வுடல் ஓங்கிட வேண்டித்","திரைக்கடல் உப்புத் திரண்டது போலத்","திரித்துப் பிறக்குந் திருவரு ளாலே"],"p":"","r":"The subtle body that had dissolved into the Supreme takes form again so this body may grow, and by grace is shaped anew and born, as salt crystallizes out of the sea.","n":"The salt image is of something dissolved that takes form again from the same water. பரத்திற் கரைந்து பதிந்த நற்காயம் is read as the subtle body resting in the Supreme between births.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","thiruvarul","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:47"},"t:492":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":492,"t":["சத்தி சிவன்விளை யாட்டால் உயிராக்கி","ஒத்த இருமாயா கூட்டத் திடைப்பூட்டிச்","சுத்தம தாகுந் துரியம் புரிவித்துச்","சித்தம் புகுந்து சிவமய மாக்குமே"],"p":"","r":"Shakti and Siva in their play make souls, yoke them within the joining of the two mayas, have them enter the pure turiya, enter their minds and make them full of Siva.","n":"இருமாயை is traditionally pure and impure maya. விளையாட்டு (play) names creation as the Lord's sport, and the end of the process is that the soul becomes சிவமயம், filled with Siva.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","sivam","uyir","maayai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:48"},"t:493":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":493,"t":["விஞ்ஞானர் நால்வரு மெய்ப்பிரள யாகலத்","தஞ்ஞானர் மூவருந் தாங்கு சகலத்தின்","அஞ்ஞானர் மூவரு மாகும் பதின்மராம்","விஞ்ஞான ராதியர் வேற்றுமை தானே"],"p":"","r":"The Vijnanars are four, those in Pralayakala who 'know their own' three, and the unknowing in Sakala three; ten in all, these are the divisions of the Vijnanars and the rest.","n":"விஞ்ஞானர், தஞ்ஞானர், அஞ்ஞானர் are technical names; how the 4, 3 and 3 subdivisions are made is not stated, and 498 counts nine.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["uyir","malam","pasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:48"},"t:494":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":494,"t":["விஞ்ஞானர் கேவலத் தாரது விட்டவர்","தஞ்ஞானர் அட்டவித் தேசராஞ் சார்ந்துளோர்","எஞ்ஞானர் ஏழ்கோடி மந்திர நாயகர்","மெய்ஞானர் ஆணவம் விட்டுநின் றாரே"],"p":"","r":"The Vijnanars are those who have left the kevala state; the thanjnanars who draw near are the eight Vidyesvaras; the enjnanars are the seven crore Lords of mantra; the true knowers stand having left anava.","n":"The eight Vidyesvaras and the seventy million Mantresvaras are classes of liberated or near-liberated souls in Siddhanta. The play on -ஞானர் through all four lines makes the classes hard to separate.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["uyir","malam","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:48"},"t:495":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":495,"t":["இரண்டா வதில்முத்தி எய்துவர் அத்தனை","இரண்டாவ துள்ளே இருமல பெத்தர்","இரண்டாகு நூற்றெட்டு ருத்திரர் என்பர்","முரண்சேர் சகலத்தர் மும்மலத் தாரே"],"p":"","r":"In the second class all attain release; within the second are the bound with two malas, said to be the hundred and eight Rudras; the Sakalas, beset with conflict, have three malas.","n":"இரண்டாவது, the second, is taken as the Pralayakalas, bound by anava and karma. The 108 Rudras as a class of these souls is the tradition's reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["malam","uyir","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:48"},"t:496":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":496,"t":["பெத்தெத்த சித்தொடு பேண்முத்தச் சித்தது","ஒத்திட் டிரண்டிடை யூடுற்றார் சித்துமாய்","மத்தது மும்மலம் வாட்டுகை மாட்டாதார்","சத்தத் தமிழ்ந்து சகலத்து ளாரே"],"p":"","r":"Between the bound soul and the freed soul stand those who have become intermediate; those unable to wither the three malas, sunk in sound, are in the Sakala state.","n":"Line 1 (பெத்தெத்த சித்தொடு பேண்முத்தச் சித்தது) and சத்தத்து அமிழ்ந்து are obscure; the verse may be corrupt.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["malam","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:48"},"t:497":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":497,"t":["சிவமாகி ஐவகைத் திண்மலஞ் செற்றோர்","அவமாகார் சித்தர்முத் தாந்தத்து வாழ்வார்","பவமான தீர்வோர் பசுபாசம் அற்றோர்","நவமான தத்துவம் நாடிக்கண் டோ ரே"],"p":"","r":"Those who have become Siva and destroyed the five firm malas will not come to nothing; these siddhas live in the three ends, end their becoming, are free of soul-bondage and bond, and have sought and seen the ninefold tattva.","n":"The five malas are traditionally anava, karma, maya, mayeya and tirodhana. முத்தாந்தம் is taken as Vedanta, Siddhanta and Nadanta. நவமான may be 'ninefold' or 'new'; the variant நாடிக்கொண்டாரே changes little.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["malam","sivam","siththi","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:48"},"t:498":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":498,"t":["விஞ்ஞானர் ஆணவ கேவல மேவுவோர்","விஞ்ஞானர் மாயையில் தங்கும் இருமலர்","அஞ்ஞானர் அச்சக லத்தர் சகலராம்","விஞ்ஞான ராதிகள் ஒன்பான்வே றுயிர்களே"],"p":"","r":"The Vijnanars dwell in anava's kevala state; the (second) Vijnanars stay in maya with two malas; the unknowing are the Sakalas of Sakala; the Vijnanars and the rest are nine different kinds of soul.","n":"The second line repeats விஞ்ஞானர் where the scheme needs the Pralayakalas; this may be a slip in the text. The count of nine differs from 493's ten.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["uyir","malam","maayai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:48"},"t:499":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":499,"t":["விஞ்ஞான கன்மத்தால் மெய்யகங் கூடிய","அனையான கன்மத்தி நால்சுவர் யோனிபுக்","கெஞ்ஞான மெய்தீண்டி யேயிடை யிட்டுப்போய்","மெய்ஞ்ஞான ராகிச் சிவமேவல் உண்மையே"],"p":"","r":"By karma, the Vijnanars joined to a body enter wombs of four walls, touch some knowledge and a body, pass on through what lies between, become true knowers and attain Siva: this is the truth.","n":"Lines 2 and 3 (அனையான கன்மத்தி நால்சுவர் யோனி, எஞ்ஞான மெய்தீண்டி) are obscure and may be corrupt; the sense of a path from embodiment to Siva is clear only in outline.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["uyir","vinai","gnaanam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:48"},"t:500":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":500,"t":["ஆணவந் துற்ற வவித்தா நனவற்றோர்","காணிய விந்துவா நாத சகலாதி","ஆணவ மாதி யடைந்தோ ரவரன்றே","சேணுயர் சத்தி சிவதத் துவமாமே வரன்றே"],"p":"","r":"Those reached by anava and avidya, without waking; bindu, nada, Sakala and the rest; those who have reached anava and the others are not they; the Shakti and Siva tattvas rise far above.","n":"The syntax is broken and the verse may be corrupt; it seems to set the pure tattvas of Shakti and Siva above the states bound by anava.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["malam","vindhu","naadham","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:48"},"t:501":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":501,"t":["திலமத் தனைபொன் சிவஞானிக்கு ஈந்தால்","பலமுத்தி சித்தி பரபோக மும்","தரும்நிலமத் தனைபொன்னை நின்மூடர்க்கு ஈந்தால்","பலமும்அற் றெபர போகமும் குன்றுமே."],"p":"","r":"Gold the size of a sesame seed given to a knower of Siva yields its fruit: release, siddhi and the highest enjoyment; gold the size of the earth given to fools loses its fruit, and the highest enjoyment fails.","n":"The contrast is between திலம் (sesame) and நிலம் (earth), one letter apart. The worth of the gift lies in the recipient's knowledge, not in its size or the recipient's need.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","gnaanam","payan","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:49"},"t:502":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":502,"t":["கண்டிருந் தாருயிர் உண்டிடுங் காலனைக்","கொண்டிருந் தாருயிர் கொள்ளும் குணத்தனை","நன்றுணர்ந் தார்க்கருள் செய்திடு நாதனைச்","சென்றுணர்ந் தார்சிலர் தேவரு மாமே."],"p":"","r":"Some went and came to know the Lord who masters Death, who eats the lives of those who stand and watch, who has the power to take life, and who gives grace to those who understand rightly; they became even gods.","n":"The first two lines are ambiguous: காலனை and குணத்தனை may describe Death or the Lord who subdued him. Placed in this chapter, these knowers are presumably the worthy recipients.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","kootru","thiruvarul","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:49"},"t:503":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":503,"t":["கைவிட்டி லேன்கரு வாகிய காலத்து","மெய்விட்டி லேன்விகிர் தன்அடி தேடுவன்","பொய்விட்டு நானே புரிசடை யானடி","நெய்விட் டிலாத விடிஞ்சிலு மாமே."],"p":"","r":"I have not let go of him since I was an embryo, have not left the truth, and seek the feet of the Changeless; putting away falsehood, I seek the feet of the one with plaited matted hair, and become like a lamp that needs no ghee.","n":"விகிர்தன் is an epithet of Siva. The last line, நெய்விட்டிலாத இடிஞ்சில், is obscure: a lamp burning without ghee (an inner light) or an empty lamp; the reading is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","vaaimai","karu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:49"},"t:504":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":504,"t":["ஆவன ஆவ அழிவ அழிவன","போவன போவ புகுவ புகுவன","காவலன் பேர்நந்தி காட்டித்துக் கண்டவன்","ஏவன செய்யும் இளங்கிளை யோனே."],"p":"","r":"What is to become becomes, what is to perish perishes, what is to go goes and what is to come comes; the guardian named Nandi showed this and saw it, and the young kin does as he is bidden.","n":"The repetitions in lines 1 and 2 state that things take their course. இளங்கிளையோன் (the young kin) may be the disciple or the poet himself; காட்டித்துக் looks corrupt.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oozh","guru","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:49"},"t:505":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":505,"t":["கோல வறட்டைக் குனிந்து குளகிட்டுப்","பாலைக் கறந்து பருகுவதே ஒக்கும்","சீலமும் நோன்பும் இலாதவர்க்கு ஈந்தது","காலங் கழிந்த பயிரது ஆகுமே."],"p":"","r":"Giving to those without good conduct and vows is like stooping to feed fodder to a handsome barren cow and milking it for something to drink; it is a crop whose season has passed.","n":"Two images of wasted effort: the barren cow (வறட்டு) gives nothing however well fed, and the late crop yields nothing however well sown. சீலம் and நோன்பு are taken as conduct and religious vows; the verse judges the recipient, not the gift.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","ozhukkam","payan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:50"},"t:506":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":506,"t":["ஈவது யோக இயம நியமங்கள்","சார்வ தறிந்தன்பு தங்கு மவர்க்கன்றி","ஆவ தறிந்தன்பு தங்கா தவர்க்களுக்கு","ஈவ பெரும்பிழை என்றுகொள் ளீரே.."],"p":"","r":"Know it as a great fault to give to anyone except those who know how to keep to yoga, yama and niyama and in whom love dwells, and not to those in whom love does not dwell though they know what profits.","n":"ஈவது in line 1 can also be read as what is given: the teaching of yoga, yama and niyama is to be given only to those fit for it. On that reading the verse is about initiation rather than alms. Lines 2 and 3 repeat அறிந்து அன்பு தங்கு with a negation, which makes the contrast hard to fix.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","yogam","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:50"},"t:507":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":507,"t":["ஆமாறு அறியான் அதிபஞ்ச பாதகன்","தோமாறும் ஈசற்குந் தூய குரவற்கும்","காமாதி விட்டோ ர்க்குந் தரல்தந்து கற்பிப்போன்","போமா நரகில் புகான்போதங் கற்கவே."],"p":"","r":"Even one who does not know the way and has done the five great sins, if he gives to the Lord, to the pure guru and to those who have left desire behind and has them teach him, will not enter the hell where such people go, but will learn true knowledge.","n":"This is the reading in which புகான் (he will not enter) governs the whole: right giving outweighs even the five great sins (பஞ்ச பாதகம்). தரல்தந்து கற்பிப்போன் is compressed; it may instead mean one who gives and then presumes to teach such people, who goes to hell, but போதம் கற்கவே (so as to learn knowledge) fits the first reading better.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","guru","naragam","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:50"},"t:508":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":508,"t":["மண்மலை யத்தனை மாதனம் ஈயினும்","அண்ணல் இவனென்றே அஞ்சலி அத்தனாய்","எண்ணி இறைஞ்சாதார்ககு ஈந்த இருவரும்","நண்ணுவர் ஏழா நரகக் குழியிலே."],"p":"","r":"Though one gives great wealth as large as a mountain of earth, if it goes to those who do not bow with joined palms, thinking 'this is the Lord', giver and receiver both reach the pit of the seventh hell.","n":"ஏழா நரகம் is the seventh, lowest hell. The verse makes the giver share the fault: wealth is no excuse for an unworthy choice of recipient.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","iraivan","naragam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:50"},"t:509":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":509,"t":["உள்ளத்தின் உள்ளே உளபல தீர்த்தங்கள்","மெள்ளக் குடைந்துநின் றாடார் வினைகெடப்","பள்ளமும் மேடும் பரந்து திரிவரே","கள்ள மனமுடைக் கல்வியி லோரே."],"p":"","r":"There are many holy waters within the heart; those unlearned ones with deceitful minds who do not slowly plunge into them and bathe so that their deeds are destroyed wander over hollow and height.","n":"பள்ளமும் மேடும் (hollow and height) is the pilgrim's road over the land. கல்வியிலோர் (the unlearned) is pointed: learning here means knowing where the waters are.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["theerththam","manam","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:51"},"t:510":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":510,"t":["தளியறி வாளர்க்குத் தண்ணிதாய்த் தோன்றும்","குளியறி வாளர்க்குக் கூடவும் ஒண்ணான்","வளியறி வாளர்க்கு வாய்க்கினும் வாய்க்கும்","தெளியறி வாளர்தம் சிந்தையு ளானே."],"p":"","r":"To those who know the temple he appears gentle; those who know only bathing cannot join him; to those who know the breath he may or may not come; he is within the minds of those whose knowledge is clear.","n":"The verse rings on four words: தளி (temple), குளி (bathing), வளி (breath, yoga) and தெளி (clarity). It ranks them, with bathing lowest and clear knowledge highest; வாய்க்கினும் வாய்க்கும் leaves the yogi's success open.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["theerththam","vazhipaadu","yogam","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:51"},"t:511":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":511,"t":["உள்ளத்தின் உள்ளே உணரும் ஒருவனைக்","கள்ளத்தி னாரும் கலந்தறி வார்இல்லை","வெள்ளத்தை நாடி விடும்அவர் தீவினைப்","பள்ளத்தல் இட்டதோர் பத்துள் ளாமே."],"p":"","r":"None of the deceitful, however they mingle, know the One who is felt within the heart; those who seek out the flood of the river leave their evil deeds like something cast into a pit.","n":"The last line (பள்ளத்தல் இட்டதோர் பத்துள்ளாமே) is obscure. It may mean that river bathing only sinks the deeds where they stay, or that the deeds cling like a பற்று (attachment, read for பத்து).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["theerththam","manam","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:51"},"t:512":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":512,"t":["அறிவார் அமரர்கள் ஆதிப் பிரானைச்","செறிவான் உறைபதம் சென்று வலங்கொள்","மறியார் வளைக்கை வருபுனல் கங்கைப்","பொறியார் புனல்மூழ்கப் புண்ணிய ராமே."],"p":"","r":"The gods who know the First Lord go round the dense abode where he dwells, and plunging into the speckled water of the Ganga that flows on him who holds a deer and a bangled hand, they become meritorious.","n":"This verse seems to grant outer bathing its place, for the gods and at the Lord's own river. மறியார் வளைக்கை may be the deer in the Lord's hand and the bangled hand of Uma beside him; பொறியார் புனல் is unclear. Some read the Ganga here as an inner current, which would bring the verse in line with the chapter.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["theerththam","vazhipaadu","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:51"},"t:513":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":513,"t":["கடலில் கெடுத்துக் குளத்தினில் காண்டல்.","உடலுற்றுத் தேடுவார் தம்மைஒப் பாரிலர்","திடமுற்ற நந்தி திருவரு ளால்சென்று","உடலிற் புகுந்தமை ஒன்றறி யாரே."],"p":"","r":"Losing it in the sea and looking for it in a pond: none match those who search by tiring the body; they do not know at all that by firm Nandi's grace he went and entered the body.","n":"Line 1 is a proverb for searching in the wrong place. உடலுற்றுத் தேடுவார (variant உடலுற) may mean those who search with bodily toil, that is, by pilgrimage. The answer is that the Lord is already in the body.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["theerththam","yaakkai","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:51"},"t:514":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":514,"t":["கலந்தது நீரது உடம்பில் கறுக்கும்","கலந்தது நீரது உடம்பில் சிவக்கும்","கலந்தது நீரது உடம்பில் வெளுக்கும்","கலந்தது நீர்அனல் காற்றது வாமே."],"p":"","r":"The water mixed in the body turns black, turns red, turns white; what mixed is water, fire and wind.","n":"The three colours are not explained. They may be the body's three humours (wind, bile and phlegm) or three stages of a yogic practice; the plain point may only be that the body is itself made of the elements the pilgrim seeks outside.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:51"},"t:515":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":515,"t":["தாவர லிங்கம் பறித்தொன்றில் தாபித்தால்","ஆவதன் முன்னே அரசு நிலைகெடும்","சாவதன் முன்னே பெருநோய் அடுத்திடும்","காவலன் பேர்நந்தி கட்டுரைத் தானே."],"p":"","r":"If one pulls up a fixed lingam and sets it up elsewhere, the kingdom loses its stability before the deed is done, and great disease befalls him before he dies; so Nandi the guardian declared.","n":"தாவர லிங்கம் is the installed, immovable lingam, as against a movable one. The verse does not say whose disease is meant; it is usually taken as the one who moves it, while the king's realm suffers too.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","arasu","noy"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:52"},"t:516":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":516,"t":["கட்டுவித் தார்மதில் கல்லொன்று வாங்கிடில்","வெட்டுவிக் கும்அபி டேகத்து அரசரை","முட்டுவிக் கும்முனி வேதிய ராயினும்","வெட்டுவித் தேவிடும் விண்ணவன் ஆணையே."],"p":"","r":"If one takes away even a single stone from the wall of a temple someone built, the command of the Lord of heaven will have even an anointed king cut down, and will have him cut down even if he is a sage or a Vedic Brahmin.","n":"The verse spares no rank: anointed kings, sages and Brahmins alike. முட்டுவிக்கும் may mean 'brings to an end' or 'obstructs'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","arasu","kolai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:52"},"t:517":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":517,"t":["ஆற்றரு நோய்மிக்கு அவனி மழையின்றிப்","போற்றரு மன்னரும் போர்வலி குன்றுவர்","கூற்றுதைத் தான்திருக் கோயில்கள் எல்லாம்","சாற்றிய பூசைகள் தப்பிடில் தானே."],"p":"","r":"If the pujas ordained in all the temples of the one who kicked Death lapse, unbearable disease grows, the earth has no rain, and even praiseworthy kings lose their strength in war.","n":"கூற்றுதைத்தான் recalls Siva kicking Death to save Markandeya. The verse makes worship a cause of rain, not merely dependent on it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","mazhai","arasu","noy"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:52"},"t:518":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":518,"t":["முன்னவ னார்கோயில் பூசைகள் முட்டிடின்","மன்னர்க்குத் தீங்குள வாரி வளம்குன்றும்","கன்னம் களவு மிகுத்திடும் காசினி","என்னரு நந்தி எடுத்துரைத் தானே."],"p":"","r":"If the pujas in the temple of the First One stop, harm comes to kings, the bounty of the rains declines, and housebreaking and theft increase in the land; so our rare Nandi declared.","n":"The text reads வாரி (abundance, or the sea); the variant மாரி makes it the rains. கன்னம் is housebreaking, from the hole made in the wall.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","arasu","mazhai","kalavu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:52"},"t:519":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":519,"t":["பேர்கொண்ட பார்ப்பான் பிரான்தன்னை அர்ச்சித்தால்","போர்கொண்ட வேந்தர்க்குப் பொல்லா வியாதியாம்","பார்கொண்ட நாட்டுக்குப் பஞ்சமு மாம்என்றே","சீர்க்கொண்ட நந்தி தெரிந்துரைத் தானே."],"p":"","r":"If a Brahmin who has only the name worships the Lord, it becomes a vile disease for warring kings and famine for the land; so glorious Nandi declared after due thought.","n":"பேர்கொண்ட பார்ப்பான் can mean a Brahmin in name only, or a Brahmin of renown. Tradition takes it of one not qualified or initiated for temple worship, but the verse does not say what he lacks.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","andhanar","arasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:52"},"t:520":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":520,"t":["எம்பெரு மான்இறை வாமுறை யோஎன்று","வம்பவிழ் வானோர் அசுரன் வலிசொல்ல","அம்பவள மேனி அறுமுகன் போயவர்","தம்பகை கொல்லென்ற தற்பரன் தானே."],"p":"","r":"When the gods, their flowers blooming, cried 'Our great Lord, our God, is this just?' and told of the asura's might, the Supreme said to the six-faced one of beautiful coral body, 'go and kill their enemy.'","n":"The chapter title links this to the downward face; tradition says Skanda was born from it. The verse itself names only the six-faced one and the command.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:53"},"t:521":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":521,"t":["அண்டமொடு எண்டிசை தாங்கும் அதோமுகம்","கண்டங் கறுத்த கருத்தறி வாரில்லை","உண்டது நஞ்சென்று உரைப்பர் உணர்விலோர்","வெண்டலை மாலை விரிசடை யோற்கே."],"p":"","r":"No one knows the meaning of the darkened throat of the downward face that bears the cosmos and the eight directions; those without understanding say that what he ate was poison, of him who wears a garland of white skulls and spreading matted hair.","n":"The verse does not deny the poison story outright; it says the story is what people without understanding tell. What the throat means instead is left unstated: tradition reads it as the Lord's holding of the world within.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:53"},"t:522":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":522,"t":["செய்தான் அறியுஞ் செழுங்கடல் வட்டத்துப்","பொய்யே யுரைத்துப் புகழும் மனிதர்கள்","மெய்யே உரைக்கில் விண்ணோர் தொழச்செய்வன்","மைதாழ்ந்து இலங்கும் மிடறுடை யோனே."],"p":"","r":"He who made them knows the men of the sea-girt world who praise with lies; if they spoke the truth, he of the throat dark and gleaming would make the gods worship them.","n":"The falsehood may be false praise of the Lord or false speech in general. The reward for truth is put high: the gods themselves would bow.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaaimai","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:53"},"t:523":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":523,"t":["நந்தி எழுந்து நடுவுற ஓங்கிய","செந்தீக் கலந்துள் சிவனென நிற்கும்","முந்திக் கலந்தங்கு உலகம் வலம்வரும்","அந்தி இறைவன் அதோமுகம் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"Nandi rising, high in the middle, mingled with the red fire, stands within as Siva; mingling first, he goes round the world; the Lord of the twilight is the downward face.","n":"நடுவுற ஓங்கிய (rising in the middle) and செந்தீ (red fire) suggest a yogic ascent through the central channel; அந்தி (twilight) may be the juncture of day and night or of the breaths. All of this is tradition's reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:53"},"t:524":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":524,"t":["அதோமுகம் கீழண்ட மான புராணன்","அதோமுகம் தன்னொடும் எங்கும் முயலும்","சதோமுகத் து ஒண்மலர்க் கண்ணிப் பிரானும்","அதோமுகன் ஊழித் தலைவனு மாமே."],"p":"","r":"The downward face is the Ancient who is the lower cosmos; with the downward face he works everywhere; the Lord with the bright flower wreath of the hundred faces is also the downward-faced one, the Lord of the aeon.","n":"சதோமுகம் is unclear: perhaps 'hundred-faced', or a form of சதாமுகம். ஊழித் தலைவன் (Lord of the aeon) links the face to dissolution.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","aindhozhil"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:53"},"t:525":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":525,"t":["அதோமுகம் மாமல ராயது கேளும்","அதோமுகத் தால் ஒரு நூறாய் விரிந்து","அதோமுகம் ஆகிய அந்தமில் சத்தி","அதோமுகம் ஆகி அமர்ந்திருந் தானே."],"p":"","r":"Hear how the downward face became the great flower: by the downward face it spread out a hundredfold; the endless Shakti that became the downward face, he became the downward face and abides.","n":"The repetition of அதோமுகம் in every line is deliberate. The flower that opens a hundredfold may be a lotus of the body or of the cosmos; the verse identifies the face with Shakti and with Siva at once.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:53"},"t:526":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":526,"t":["தெளிவுறு ஞானத்துச் சிந்தையின் உள்ளே","அளிவுறு வார்அம ராபதி நாடி","எளியனென்று ஈசனை நீசர் இகழில்","கிளியொன்று பூஞையால் கீழது வாகுமே."],"p":"","r":"To those in whose minds clear knowledge has come, who melt in longing for the heavenly city, he is easy; if base people scorn the Lord for being easy, they go down like a parrot to a cat.","n":"எளியன் (easy, accessible) is both his gift to the devoted and the excuse of the scornful. கிளியொன்று பூஞையால் may also mean that the scorned Lord is as a parrot to the cat's eyes, but the fall of the scorner fits கீழதுவாகுமே.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nindhai","sivam","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:54"},"t:527":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":527,"t":["முளிந்தவர் வானவர் தானவர் எல்லாம்","விளிந்தவர் மெய்ந்நின்ற ஞானம் உணரார்","அளிந்தமுது ஊறிய ஆதிப் பிரானைத்","தளிந்தவர்ககு அல்லது தாங்கஒண் ணாதே."],"p":"","r":"Gods and asuras who withered have all perished, not knowing the knowledge that stands in truth; the First Lord, from whom ripe nectar wells up, cannot be borne except by those who have become calm.","n":"தளிந்தவர் is uncertain: perhaps 'those cooled and settled' or 'those who have drawn near'. The ripe nectar may recall the churning of the sea.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nindhai","gnaanam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:54"},"t:528":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":528,"t":["அப்பகை யாலே அசுரரும் தேவரும்","நற்பகை செய்து நடுவே முடிந்தனர்","எப்பகை யாகிலும் எய்தார் இறைவனைப்","பொய்ப்பகை செய்யினும் ஒன்றுபத் தாமே."],"p":"","r":"Through that enmity asuras and gods waged their good war and perished midway; whatever enmity they bear, they do not reach the Lord, and even a false enmity against him becomes one tenfold.","n":"நற்பகை (good enmity) is ironic or refers to the war of gods and asuras. The last line may mean the Lord answers even a pretended hostility tenfold, or that one false enmity grows into ten.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nindhai","pagai","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:54"},"t:529":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":529,"t":["போகமும் மாதர் புலவி அதுநினைந்து","ஆகமும் உள்கலந்து அங்குஉள ராதலில்","வேதிய ராயும் விகிர்தன்நாம் என்கின்ற","நீதியுள் ஈசன் நினைப்பொழி வாரே."],"p":"","r":"Because they dwell on pleasure and the sulking of women and have that mingled in their very bodies, even those who are Vedic Brahmins abandon thought of the Lord who abides in the rule that says 'we are Vikirta'.","n":"விகிர்தன் (the one unlike all, a name of Siva) and நாம் என்கின்ற நீதி are hard to fix: the line may refer to the Lord as known in the Veda or to a teaching of identity with him. The verse's target is plain: learning does not save those lost in pleasure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nindhai","andhanar","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:54"},"t:530":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":530,"t":["பெற்றிருந் தாரையும் பேணார் கயவர்கள்","உற்றிருந் தாரை உளைவன சொல்லுவர்","கற்றிருந் தார்வழி உற்றிருந் தாரவர்","பெற்றிருந் தார்அன்றி யார்பெறும் பேறே."],"p":"","r":"The base do not honour even those who have attained, and speak words that hurt those close to them; those who keep to the path of the learned are the ones who have attained, and who but they gain the true gain?","n":"பெற்றிருந்தார் may mean 'those who have attained' (the realised) or 'those who bore them' (parents). The verse turns on the word: the base do not honour those who have it, and only those who follow the learned get it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nindhai","guru","kayamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:55"},"t:531":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":531,"t":["ஓரெழுத்து ஒருபொருள் உணரக் கூறிய","சீரெழுத் தாளரைச் சிதையச் செப்பினோர்","ஊரிடைச் சுணங்கனாய்ப் பிறந்தங் கு ஓர்உகம்","வாரிடைக் கிருமியாய் மாய்வர் மண்ணிலே."],"p":"","r":"Those who speak so as to wound the excellent teacher who taught one syllable and its meaning so that it was understood will be born as a dog in the town for an age and then die as worms in the earth.","n":"ஓரெழுத்து (one letter) is usually taken as a mantra syllable, often Om, but may mean any teaching however small. The variant பாரிடைக் does not change the sense.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nindhai","guru","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:55"},"t:532":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":532,"t":["பத்தினி பத்தர்கள் தத்துவ ஞானிகள்","சித்தங் கலங்கச் சிதைவுகள் செய்தவர்","அத்தமும் ஆவியும் ஆண்டொன்றில் மாண்டிடும்","சத்தியம் ஈது சதாநந்தி ஆணையே."],"p":"","r":"Those who cause ruin that troubles the minds of a chaste wife, of devotees and of knowers of the tattvas will lose wealth and life within a year; this is truth, by the command of Sadananda.","n":"சதாநந்தி may be the Lord as ever-blissful Nandi. The pairing of the chaste wife with devotees and jnanis puts them in one class of the inviolable.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nindhai","karpu","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:55"},"t:533":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":533,"t":["மந்திரம் ஓரெழுத்து உரைத்த மாதவர்","சிந்தையில் நொந்திடத் தீமைகள் செய்தவர்","நுந்திய சுணங்கனாய்ப் பிறந்து நூறுரு","வந்திடும் புலையராய் மாய்வர் மண்ணிலே."],"p":"","r":"Those who did evil so that the great ascetics who taught the one-syllable mantra grieved in mind will be born as outcast dogs and come as outcastes in a hundred forms, and die on earth.","n":"This repeats 531 with மந்திரம் stated. நூறுரு may mean a hundred births or a hundred bodily forms.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nindhai","guru","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:55"},"t:534":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":534,"t":["ஈசன் அடியார் இதயம் கலங்கிடத்","தேசமும் நாடும் சிறப்பும் அழிந்திடும்","வாசவன் பீடமும் மாமன்னர் பீடமும்","நாசமது ஆகுமே நம்நந்தி ஆணையே."],"p":"","r":"If the hearts of the Lord's devotees are troubled, country, land and their glories are destroyed; Indra's throne and the thrones of great kings come to ruin, by the command of our Nandi.","n":"வாசவன் is Indra. The threat moves up from land to king to the king of the gods.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nindhai","adiyaar","arasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:55"},"t:535":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":535,"t":["சன்மார்க்க சற்குருச் சந்நிதி பொய்வரின்","நன்மார்க்க மும்குன்றி ஞானமும் தங்காது","தொன்மார்க்க மாய துறையும் மறந்திட்டுப்","பன்மார்க்க மும்கெட்டுப் பஞ்சமும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"If falsehood enters the presence of the true guru of the true path, the good path declines and knowledge does not stay; forgetting the ancient way, the many paths are ruined and famine comes too.","n":"சந்நிதி பொய்வரின் may mean lying before the guru, or the guru's presence proving false (a false guru). சன்மார்க்கம் is the path of knowledge in Saiva usage.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","gnaanam","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:55"},"t:536":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":536,"t":["கைப்பட்ட மாமணி தானிடை கைவிட்டு","மெய்ப்பட்ட கல்லைச் சுமப்போன் விதிபோன்றும்","கைப்பட்ட நெய்பால் தயிர்நிற்கத் தானறக்","கைப்பிட்டுண் பான்போன்றுங் கன்மிஞானிக் கொப்பே."],"p":"","r":"Like the fate of one who lets fall the great gem he held and carries a stone stuck to his body, and like one who eats bitter things while ghee, milk and curd stand at hand: such is the ritualist beside the knower.","n":"The last words கன்மிஞானிக்கு ஒப்பே are compressed: perhaps 'such is the karmi compared with the jnani', or 'such is one who leaves the jnani for the karmi'. The images make plain that something precious at hand is exchanged for something worthless.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","guru","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:55"},"t:537":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":537,"t":["ஆண்டான் அடியவர் ஆர்க்கும் விரோதிகள்","ஆண்டான் அடியவர் ஐயமேற் றுண்பவர்","ஆண்டான் அடியரை வேண்டாது பேசினோர்","தாம்தாம் விழுவது தாழ்நர காமே."],"p":"","r":"The Lord's devotees are at odds with everyone; the Lord's devotees eat what they receive as alms; those who speak unwanted words of the Lord's devotees fall, each of them, into low hell.","n":"ஆர்க்கும் விரோதிகள் (opponents of all) is taken as their standing apart from worldly ways, not as hostility; it may also be read with a negative understood, 'enemies to none'. The repeated ஆண்டான் அடியவர் opens three lines.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["adiyaar","nindhai","naragam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:56"},"t:538":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":538,"t":["ஞானியை நிந்திப் பவனும் நலன் என்றே","ஞானியை வந்திப் பவனுமே நல்வினை","யான கொடுவினை தீர்வார் அவன்வயம்","போன பொழுதே புகுஞ்சிவ போகமே."],"p":"","r":"The one who reviles the jnani and the one who worships the jnani as good take away his good and his cruel deeds; the moment he has passed into the Lord's keeping, Siva's bliss enters.","n":"This is the traditional reading: the worshipper takes the jnani's good deeds and the reviler his bad ones, leaving him free of both. The verse runs the two together (நல்வினையான கொடுவினை), so which goes to whom is inferred, not stated.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","nindhai","vinai","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:56"},"t:539":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":539,"t":["பற்றிநின் றார்நெஞ்சில் பல்லிதான் ஒன்றுண்டு","முற்றிக் கிடந்தது மூக்கையும் நாவையும்","தெற்றிக் கிடந்து சிதைக்கின்ற சிந்தையுள்","வற்றா தொழிவது மாகமை யாமே."],"p":"","r":"In the heart of those who cling there is a lizard; grown full, it lies tangled about the nose and tongue, and to keep it from flourishing in the mind it ravages is great forbearance.","n":"கமை is forbearance (Sanskrit kshama). The lizard is not named: it may be desire or anger, and the nose and tongue may be the senses, or breath and speech in a yogic reading. The variant நாவியும் for நாவையும் changes tongue to musk.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["porai","pattru","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:57"},"t:540":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":540,"t":["ஓலக்கம் சூழ்ந்த உலப்பிலி தேவர்கள்","பாலொத்த மேனியன் பாதம் பணிந்துய்ய","மாலுக்கும் ஆதி பிரமற்கும் மன்னவன்.","ஞாலத் திவன்மிக நல்லன்என் றாரே"],"p":"","r":"The countless gods ringed by their court, bowing at the feet of him whose body is like milk so that they might be saved, said: 'King even of Vishnu and of first Brahma, in the world this one is truly good.'","n":"The variants rewrite the verse: மேனி பணிந்தடியேன் தொழ makes the poet the worshipper, and ஞாலத்து நம்மடி நல்கிடென்றாலே makes the last line a plea, 'grant us your feet in the world'. Forbearance enters only if the Lord's goodness to supplicants is meant.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","sivam","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:57"},"t:541":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":541,"t":["ஞானம் விளைந்தவர் நம்மிடம் மன்னவர்","சேனை வளைந்து திசைதொரும் கைதொழ","ஊனை விளைத்திடும் உம்பர்தம் ஆதியை","ஏனை விளைந்தருள் எட்டலு மாமே."],"p":"","r":"Those in whom knowledge has ripened are kings at our side, worshipped with joined hands by armies ringed round from every quarter; they can reach the grace of the First of the gods, who makes the flesh grow.","n":"ஊனை விளைத்திடும் (who makes the flesh grow) may be the Lord as maker of bodies. ஏனை விளைந்தருள் (variant வளைந்தருள்) is unclear. The verse may also mean that kings with armies worship the jnanis.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","thiruvarul","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:57"},"t:542":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":542,"t":["வல்வகை யானும் மனையிலும் மன்றிலும்","பல்வகை யானும் பயிற்றி பதஞ்செய்யும்","கொல்லையி னின்று குதிகொள்ளும் கூத்தனுக்கு","எல்லையி லாத இலயம்உண் டாமே."],"p":"","r":"By strong ways and many ways, at home and in the hall, practising and setting the steps, the dancer who leaps from the field has a rhythm without limit.","n":"கூத்தன் is Siva the dancer and மன்று his hall, but மனையிலும் மன்றிலும் may also be the body and the inner space. கொல்லை (field, cremation ground) and இலயம் (rhythm, absorption) both carry yogic readings.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:57"},"t:543":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":543,"t":["ஓடவல் லார்தம ரோடு நடாவுவன்","பாடவல் லாரொளி பார்மிசை வாழ்குவன்","தேடவல் லார்க்கருள் தேவர் பிரானொடும்","கூடவல் லாரடி கூடுவன் யானே."],"p":"","r":"I will go along with those able to run to him and their kin; I will live on earth in the light of those able to sing; with the Lord of gods who grants grace to those able to seek, and with those able to join him, I will reach his feet.","n":"வல்லார் (those able to) ends each verb: run, sing, seek, join. ஓட may also be 'to run away', from the world; the sense of eager devotion seems more likely.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thunai","adiyaar","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:58"},"t:544":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":544,"t":["தாமிடர்ப் பட்டுத் தளிர்போல் தயங்கினும்","மாமனதது அங்குஅன்பு வைத்தது இலையாகும்","நீஇடர்ப் பட்டிருநது என்செய்வாய் நெஞ்சமே","போமிடத் து என்னொடும் போதுகண் டாயே."],"p":"","r":"Though they suffer and tremble like tender leaves, they have set no love in the great mind there; O heart, what will you do, staying in distress? When I go, come with me.","n":"The first two lines are obscure: it is not clear who suffers and whose mind is meant. The last two are an address to the heart, a device known from love poetry and the bhakti hymns.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nenju","anbu","thunai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:58"},"t:545":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":545,"t":["அறிவார் அமரர் தலைவனை நாடிச்","செறிவார் பெறுவர் சிலர்தத் துவத்தை","நெறிதான் மிகமிக நின்றருள் செய்யும்","பெரியார் உடன்கூடல் பேரின்ப மாமே."],"p":"","r":"Seeking the Lord of the immortals, a few who draw near gain the reality; the great ones who stand ever more firmly in the path give grace, and to join with them is supreme bliss.","n":"தத்துவம் (variant சிவதத்துவம்) here seems to mean the true reality rather than the 36 tattvas. பேரின்பம் is the bliss of release.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thunai","saandror","inbam","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:58"},"t:546":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":546,"t":["தார்சடை யான்தன் தமராய் உலகினில்","போர புகழா எந்தை பொன்னடி சேருவர்","வாயடை யாவுள்ளம் தேர்வார்ககு அருள்செய்யும்","கோவந்தடைந் து அந்நெறி கூடலு மாமே."],"p":"","r":"Becoming in the world the kin of him whose matted hair bears a garland, praising him, they reach our father's golden feet; coming to the King who grants grace to those who search the heart without shutting the mouth, one can join that path.","n":"வாயடையா உள்ளம் தேர்வார் is unclear: perhaps those who search their heart while still praising aloud. போர புகழா may be corrupt.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["adiyaar","thiruvarul","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:58"},"t:547":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":547,"t":["உடையான் அடியார் அடியா ருடன்போய்ப்","படையார் அழல்மேனிப் பதிசென்று புக்கேன்","கடையார நின்றவர் கண்டறி விப்ப","உடையான் வருகென ஓலம் என் றாரே."],"p":"","r":"I went with the devotees of the Lord's devotees and entered the city of him whose body is fire bearing weapons; those standing at the gate saw me and made me known, and the cry went up: 'The Lord says, let him come.'","n":"The poet gains entry not alone but in the company of devotees of devotees, the humblest rank. ஓலம் is the call announcing an arrival or answering one.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adiyaar","thunai","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:58"},"t:548":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":548,"t":["அருமைவல் லோன்கலை ஞானத்துள் தோன்றும்","பெருமைவல் லோன்பிற விச்சுழி நீந்தும்","உரிமைவல் லோன்உணர்ந்து ஊழி இருக்கும்","திருமைவல் லாரொடு சேர்ந்தனன் யானே.."],"p":"","r":"The one rare in skill appears in the knowledge of the arts; the one great in skill swims the whirlpool of births; the one with the rightful skill, understanding, abides for an aeon; I have joined those strong in holiness.","n":"The verse plays on வல்லோன் with அருமை, பெருமை, உரிமை and திருமை. The variant ஞாலத்துள் for ஞானத்துள் would make the first line 'appears in the world of the arts'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thunai","saandror","piravi","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:58"},"t:549":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":549,"t":["உரைத்தன வற்கரி ஒன்று மூடிய","நிரைத்த இராசி நிரைமுறை எண்ணிப்","பிரச்சதம் எட்டும் பேசியே நந்தி","நிரைத்த இயமம் நியமஞ்செய் தானே"],"p":"","r":"Nandi, having counted in order the arranged signs and spoken the eight, set out yama and niyama.","n":"The first line (உரைத்தன வற்கரி ஒன்று மூடிய, variant உரைத்த நவாக்கிரி) and பிரச்சதம் (variant பிரைச்சதம்) cannot be fixed. Only the last line is clear: Nandi taught yama and niyama, the first of the eight limbs.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:59"},"t:550":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":550,"t":["செய்த இயம நியமஞ் சமாதிசென்","றுய்யப் பராசக்தி உத்தர பூருவ","மெய்த கவச நியாசங்கள் முத்திரை","எய்த வுரைசெய்வன் இந்நிலை தானே"],"p":"","r":"I will tell, so that it is reached, this state: going by the practised yama and niyama to samadhi, and for deliverance Parashakti, the north and east, the true armour, the placings and the gestures.","n":"கவசம், நியாசம் and முத்திரை are mantra rites: protective formulas, placing mantras on the body and hand gestures. உத்தர பூருவ (north and east) may be directions to face or the latter and former parts of a practice.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","samaadhi","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:59"},"t:551":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":551,"t":["அந்நெறி இந்நெறி என்னாதட் டாங்கத்","தன்னெறி சென்று சமாதியி லேநின்மின்","நன்னெறி செல்வார்க்கு ஞானத்தி லேகலாம்","புன்னெறி யாகத்திற் போக்கில்லை யாகுமே"],"p":"","r":"Without saying 'that path, this path', go by the path of the eight limbs and stand in samadhi; those who go the good path can enter knowledge, but in the mean path of sacrifice there is no way through.","n":"The last line depends on யாகத்திற் (in sacrifice); the variant யாக்கத்திற் would point to the body instead. On the text as printed the verse ranks the eight limbs above the Vedic rite.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","samaadhi","gnaanam","aaguthi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:59"},"t:552":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":552,"t":["இயம நியமமே எண்ணிலா ஆதனம்","நயமுறு பிராணாயா மம் பிரத்தி யாகாரஞ்","சயமிகு தாரணை தியானஞ் சமாதி","அயமுறும் அட்டாங்க மாவது மாமே"],"p":"","r":"Yama, niyama, the countless postures, good breath control, withdrawal of the senses, victorious concentration, meditation and samadhi: these make up the eight limbs.","n":"The list is the standard one of the yoga tradition. எண்ணிலா ஆதனம் (countless postures) is Tirumular's touch; அயமுறும் in the last line is unclear.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yogam","samaadhi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:59"},"t:553":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":553,"t":["எழுந்துநீர் பெய்யினும் எட்டுத் திசையுஞ்","செழுந்த ணியமங்கள் செய்மினென் றண்ணல்","கொழுந்தண் பவளக் குளிர்சடை யோடே","அழுந்திய நால்வர்க் கருள்புரிந் தானே"],"p":"","r":"'Even if the waters rise and pour, in all eight directions keep the rich, cool yamas,' said the Lord with the cool coral-red matted hair, granting grace to the four who were absorbed in him.","n":"The four (நால்வர்) are traditionally the sages Sanaka and the others taught by Siva as Dakshinamurti. எழுந்துநீர் பெய்யினும் may mean 'even if the flood comes', that is, whatever happens.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yogam","guru","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:60"},"t:554":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":554,"t":["கொல்லான்பொய் கூறான் களவிலான் எண்குணன்","நல்லான் அடக்க முடையான் நடுச்செய்ய","வல்லான் பகுந்துண்பான் மாசிலான் கட்காமம்","இல்லான் இயமத் திடையில்நின் றானே"],"p":"","r":"One who does not kill, does not lie, does not steal, has good qualities, is good, has restraint, can act justly, shares before he eats, is stainless and has neither drink nor lust: he stands in yama.","n":"The list gives ten marks, matching the ten yamas of the yoga texts. எண்குணன் may be 'one of eight qualities' or 'one of esteemed qualities'; in the Kural the same word names the Lord. நடுச்செய்ய வல்லான் is fairness, and பகுந்துண்பான் sharing one's food.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yogam","kollaamai","vaaimai","adakkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:60"},"t:555":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":555,"t":["ஆதியை வேதத்தின் அப்பொரு ளானைச்","சோதியை ஆங்கே சுடுகின்ற அங்கியைப்","பாதியுள் மன்னும் பரசக்தி யோடுடன்","நீதி யுணர்ந்து நியமத்த னாமே"],"p":"","r":"He who knows what is right, together with the First, the one who is the meaning of the Veda, the Light, the fire that burns there, and Parashakti who abides in his half, is a man of niyama.","n":"பாதியுள் மன்னும் பரசக்தி is Shakti as the left half of Siva. The verse makes knowledge of the Lord, not only conduct, the ground of niyama. The variant யுணர்ந்த makes the line adjectival without changing the sense.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yogam","sivam","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:61"},"t:556":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":556,"t":["தூய்மை அருளூண் சுருக்கம் பொறைசெவ்வை","வாய்மை நிலைமை வளர்த்தலே மற்றிவை","காமங் களவு கொலையெனக் காண்பவை","நேமியீ ரைந்து நியமத்த னாமே"],"p":"","r":"Purity, compassion, little food, forbearance, uprightness, truthfulness and steadiness, cultivating these, and seeing lust, theft and killing for what they are: one who keeps these ten rules is a man of niyama.","n":"நேமி ஈரைந்து is read as 'ten rules'. Several of these (truthfulness, forbearance, uprightness, little food, purity) are counted among the yamas in the yoga texts, and avoiding lust, theft and killing repeats 554; tradition reads this verse as yama and the next as niyama, or the reverse. The variant அருளுண் would make it 'food of grace'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","thooimai","arul","porai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:61"},"t:557":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":557,"t":["தவஞ்செபஞ் சந்தோடம் ஆத்திகந் தானஞ்","சிவன்றன் விரதமே சித்தாந்தக் கேள்வி","மகஞ்சிவ பூசையொண் மதிசொல்லீர் ஐந்து","நிவம்பல செய்யின் நியமத்த னாமே"],"p":"","r":"Austerity, mantra repetition, contentment, faith, giving, Siva's vow, hearing the Siddhanta, sacrifice, Siva puja and clear mind: say these ten; one who does them many times is a man of niyama.","n":"ஆத்திகம் is faith in the scriptures and the Lord; சித்தாந்தக் கேள்வி is hearing the Saiva Siddhanta. நிவம்பல is obscure; 'many times' is a guess.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yogam","thavam","vazhipaadu","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:61"},"t:558":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":558,"t":["பங்கய மாதி பரந்தபல் ஆதனம்","அங்குள வாம்இரு நாலும் அவற்றினுள்","சொங்கில்லை யாகச் சுவத்திக மெனமிகத்","தங்க இருப்பத் தலைவனு மாமே"],"p":"","r":"Of the many postures that spread out from the lotus, eight are chief; among them, one who sits firmly in svastika without strain becomes master.","n":"இரு நாலும் (two fours) gives eight. சொங்கு is taken as flaw or strain; தலைவன் may mean master of the posture or of yoga itself. The verse names svastika (சுவத்திகம்) as the seat to settle in, though it then describes others.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aasanam","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:62"},"t:559":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":559,"t":["ஓரணை யப்பத மூருவின் மேலேறிட்","டார வலித்ததன் மேல்வைத் தழகுறச்","சீர்திகழ் கைகள் அதனைத்தன் மேல்வைக்கப்","பார்திகழ் பத்மா சனமென லாகுமே"],"p":"","r":"Setting one foot upon the opposite thigh, drawing the other firmly over it, and laying the hands beautifully upon them: this is called padmasana.","n":"ஓரணையப் பதம் is a compressed phrase, read as one foot set against the other thigh; the hands resting on the feet is one reading of அதனைத்தன் மேல்வைக்க.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aasanam","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:62"},"t:560":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":560,"t":["துரிசில் வலக்காலைத் தோன்றவே மேல்வைத்து","அரிய முழந்தாளி லங்கையை நீட்டி","உருசி யொடுமுடல் செவ்வே யிருத்திப்","பரிசு பெறுமது பத்திரா சனமே"],"p":"","r":"Placing the right foot clearly over the other, stretching the hands out on the knees and holding the body upright with ease: that is bhadrasana.","n":"உருசியொடு (with relish) suggests the posture is held with ease, not strain. Which foot goes under is implied, not stated.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aasanam","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:62"},"t:561":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":561,"t":["ஒக்க அடியிணை யூருவில் ஏறிட்டு","முக்கி யுடலை முழங்கை தனில்ஏற்றித்","தொக்க அறிந்து துளங்கா திருந்திடிற்","குக்குட ஆசனங் கொள்ளலு மாமே"],"p":"","r":"Setting both feet together on the thighs and lifting the body to rest upon the elbows, holding still without wavering: that is the cock posture.","n":"முக்கி (straining) and தொக்க அறிந்து are unclear; the description matches the cock posture of later manuals, where the arms pass through the crossed legs, but the verse doesn't say so.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aasanam","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:62"},"t:562":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":562,"t":["பாத முழந்தாளிற் பாணி களைநீட்டி","ஆதர வோடும்வாய் அங்காந் தழகுறக்","கோதில் நயனங் கொடிமூக்கி லேயுறச்","சீர்திகழ் சிங்கா தனமெனச் செப்புமே"],"p":"","r":"Stretching the hands to the knees over the feet, opening the mouth wide and fixing the clear eyes on the tip of the nose: that is called the lion posture.","n":"The open mouth and the gaze at the nose-tip (கொடிமூக்கு) are the marks of the posture; the placing of the feet is compressed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aasanam","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:62"},"t:563":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":563,"t":["பத்திரங் கோமுகம் பங்கயங் கேசரி","சொத்திரம் வீரஞ் சுகாதனம் ஓரேழு","முத்தம மாமுது ஆசனம் எட்டெட்டுப்","பத்தொடு நூறு பலஆ சனமே"],"p":"","r":"Bhadra, gomukha, the lotus, the lion, sothiram, vira and sukhasana are the seven best; the postures are many, counted as eight and eight, ten and a hundred.","n":"எட்டெட்டுப் பத்தொடு நூறு is traditionally summed as 126 postures (8 + 8 + 10 + 100), but it can also be read as 64 and 110; I can't fix the count. சொத்திரம் is taken as svastika (சொத்திகம்). The variant மயூரம் would add the peacock posture to the list.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aasanam","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:62"},"t:564":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":564,"t":["ஐவர்க்கு நாயகன் அவ்வூர்த் தலைமகன்","உய்யக்கொண்டேறுங் குதிரைமற் றொன்றுண்டு","மெய்யர்க்குப்பற்றுக்கொடுக்குங் கொடாதுபோய்ப்","பொய்யரைத் துள்ளி விழுத்திடுந் தானே"],"p":"","r":"The lord of the five, chief of that town, has another horse to ride to salvation; it gives a hold to the true but leaps and throws the false.","n":"The horse is traditionally the breath, the five the senses and the town the body, with the soul as their lord; none of this is named. மெய்யர் and பொய்யர் may be the truthful and the false, or those firm and unfirm in practice.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["praanan","yogam","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:63"},"t:565":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":565,"t":["ஆரிய னல்லன் குதிரை இரண்டுள","வீசிப் பிடிக்கும் விரகறி வாரில்லை","கூரிய நாதன் குருவின் அருள்பெற்றால்","வாரிப் பிடிக்க வசப்படுந் தானே"],"p":"","r":"There are two horses, though one is no noble rider, and none knows the skill to cast and catch them; if one gains the grace of the keen lord, the guru, they come under control.","n":"The two horses are traditionally the breaths of the left and right nostril (ida and pingala). ஆரியன் அல்லன் is obscure: it may say the rider is not skilled, or that no master is at hand.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["praanan","guru","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:63"},"t:566":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":566,"t":["புள்ளினும் மிக்க புரவியை மேற்கொண்டாற்","கள்ளுண்ண வேண்டாந் தானே களிதருந்","துள்ளி நடப்பிக்குஞ் சோம்பு தவிர்ப்பிக்கும்","உள்ளது சொன்னோம் உணர்வுடை யோருக்கே"],"p":"","r":"If you ride the horse swifter than a bird, it gives intoxication without drinking toddy, makes one move lightly and drives off sloth; we have told the truth to those who understand.","n":"Toddy (கள்) stands for a joy that needs nothing outside the body. The horse is the controlled breath, as in 564; the last line marks the teaching as for the prepared.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["praanan","madi","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:63"},"t:567":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":567,"t":["பிராணன் மனத்தொடும் பேரா தடங்கிப்","பிராண னிருக்கிற் பிறப்பிறப் பில்லை","பிராணன் மடைமாறிப் பேச்சறி வித்துப்","பிராண னடைபேறு பெற்றுண்டீர் நீரே"],"p":"","r":"If the breath stays with the mind without moving, there is no birth or death; the breath, turned in its channel, makes speech known, and you have gained the gift of the breath.","n":"மடைமாறி (changing course, as water at a sluice) and பேச்சறிவித்து are unclear: the line may mean the breath turned within gives knowledge of the inner sound or of mantra. The first two lines are the plain claim.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["praanan","manam","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:63"},"t:568":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":568,"t":["ஏறுதல் பூரகம் ஈரெட்டு வாமத்தால்","ஆறுதல் கும்பம் அறுபத்து நாலதில்","ஊறுதல் முப்பத் திரண்டதி ரேசகம்","மாறுதல் ஒன்றின்கண் வஞ்சக மாமே"],"p":"","r":"Drawing in is puraka, sixteen measures by the left; holding is kumbhaka, sixty-four; letting out is recaka, thirty-two; to change the order in one of these is deceit.","n":"The ratio 16:64:32 is the standard one. வஞ்சகம் in the last line is unclear: it may condemn changing the measures, or name the alternation of nostrils as the 'clever' method.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["praanan","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:63"},"t:569":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":569,"t":["வளியினை வாங்கி வயத்தில் அடக்கில்","பளிங்கொத்துக் காயம் பழுக்கினும் பிஞ்சாந்","தெளியக் குருவின் திருவருள் பெற்றால்","வளியினும் வேட்டு வளியனு மாமே"],"p":"","r":"If one draws in the air and holds it under control, a ripened body becomes unripe and clear as crystal; if one clearly gains the guru's grace, one becomes swifter than the wind.","n":"பழுக்கினும் பிஞ்சாம் (though ripe, it becomes unripe) promises a return to youth. வளியினும் வேட்டு வளியனும் is obscure; it may mean master of the wind. The variant வயிற்றில் (in the belly) places the holding in the abdomen.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["praanan","yaakkai","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:63"},"t:570":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":570,"t":["எங்கே இருக்கினும் பூரி இடத்திலே","அங்கே யதுசெய்ய ஆக்கைக் கழிவில்லை","அங்கே பிடித்தது விட்டள வுஞ்செல்லச்","சங்கே குறிக்கத் தலைவனு மாமே"],"p":"","r":"Wherever you are, fill the breath on the left; doing it there, the body does not decay; holding it there and releasing it in measure, one becomes master as the conch signals.","n":"இடம் may be 'the left' or 'the place'. சங்கே குறிக்க is unclear: the conch may be an inner sound heard in practice, or a sign of the master.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["praanan","yaakkai","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:63"},"t:571":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":571,"t":["ஏற்றி இறக்கி இருகாலும் பூரிக்குங்","காற்றைப் பிடிக்குங் கணக்கறி வாரில்லை","காற்றைப் பிடிக்குங் கணக்கறி வாளர்க்குக்","கூற்றை யுதைக்குங் குறியது வாமே"],"p":"","r":"No one knows the measure of holding the breath that one draws in and lets out through both nostrils; for those who know it, it is the means to kick Death.","n":"இருகால் may mean the two breaths or the two nostrils. Kicking Death (கூற்றை உதைக்கும்) echoes the story of Siva kicking Death for Markandeya; whether the verse promises a long life or release from death is left open.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["praanan","kootru","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:63"},"t:572":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":572,"t":["மேல்கீழ் நடுப்பக்க மிக்குறப் பூரித்துப்","பாலாம் இரேசகத் தாலுட் பதிவித்து","மாலாகி யுந்தியுட் கும்பித்து வாங்கவே","ஆலாலம் உண்டான் அருள்பெற லாமே"],"p":"","r":"Filling the breath fully above, below, in the middle and at the sides, settling it within by recaka, and holding it in the navel, one may gain the grace of the one who drank the poison.","n":"ஆலாலம் உண்டான் is Siva, who drank the halahala poison. பாலாம் இரேசகம் and மாலாகி are unclear: மால் may mean fullness, delight or Vishnu.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["praanan","thiruvarul","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:63"},"t:573":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":573,"t":["வாமத்தில் ஈரெட்டு மாத்திரை பூரித்தே","ஏமுற்ற முப்பத் திரண்டும் இரேசித்துக்","காமுற்ற பிங்கலைக் கண்ணாக இவ்விரண்","டோ மத்தால் எட்டெட்டுக் கும்பிக்க உண்மையே"],"p":"","r":"Fill sixteen measures through the left, let out thirty-two gladly with pingala as the eye, and hold sixty-four between the two: this is the truth.","n":"It repeats the measures of 568 and adds that exhaling is through pingala, the right channel. ஓமத்தால் is unclear: it may mean 'in between' or 'as an offering'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["praanan","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:63"},"t:574":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":574,"t":["இட்ட தவ்வீ டிளகா திரேசித்துப்","புட்டிப் படத்தச நாடியும் பூரித்துக்","கொட்டிப் பிராணன் அபானனுங் கும்பித்து","நட்டம் இருக்க நமனில்லை நமக்கே"],"p":"","r":"Without loosening the seat one has set, breathe out, fill all ten nadis, and hold prana and apana together; if one stays still, there is no Yama for us.","n":"இட்ட அவ்வீடு (that house one set) may be the posture or the body. நட்டம் may mean steadiness or dance. நமனில்லை (no Yama) can be read as long life or as freedom from death.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["praanan","naadi","kootru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:63"},"t:575":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":575,"t":["புறப்பட்டுப் புக்குத் திரிகின்ற வாயுவை","நெறிப்பட வுள்ளே நின்மல மாக்கில்","உறுப்புச் சிவக்கும் உரோமங் கறுக்கும்","புறப்பட்டுப் போகான் புரிசடை யோனே"],"p":"","r":"If the breath that wanders out and in is guided within and made pure, the limbs grow ruddy, the hair turns black, and the one with matted locks will not leave.","n":"The bodily signs are stated plainly: ruddy limbs and black hair, the marks of youth. புரிசடையோன் is Siva; the verse puts him in the purified body. The same last line returns in 586.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["praanan","yaakkai","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:63"},"t:576":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":576,"t":["கூடம் எடுத்துக் குடிபுக்க மங்கையர்","ஓடுவர் மீளுவர் பன்னிரண் டங்குலம்","நீடுவர் எண்விரல் கண்டிப்பர் நால்விரல்","கூடிக் கொளிற்கோல அஞ்செழுத் தாமே"],"p":"","r":"The women who built a house and moved in run out and come back twelve finger-breadths; they reach out eight fingers and cut back four; if gathered in, they become the five letters.","n":"The women are traditionally the breaths and the house the body; the breath goes out twelve finger-breadths, eight return and four are lost. அஞ்செழுத்து is the five-syllable mantra of Siva, so the held breath becomes mantra. All of this rests on a symbolic reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["praanan","yaakkai","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:63"},"t:577":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":577,"t":["பன்னிரண் டானை பகலஇர வுள்ளது","பன்னிரண் டானையைப் பாகன் அறிகிலன்","பன்னிரண் டானையைப் பாகன் அறிந்தபின்","பன்னிரண் டானைக்குப் பகல்இர வில்லையே"],"p":"","r":"Twelve elephants have day and night; the mahout does not know them; once the mahout knows the twelve elephants, they have no day and night.","n":"The twelve are traditionally the twelve finger-breadths of the breath (as in 576), the mahout the soul, and day and night the right and left breaths (sun and moon). Once the breath is known it no longer alternates. The riddle has other readings, such as the twelve signs.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["praanan","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:63"},"t:578":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":578,"t":["கண்டுகண் டுள்ளே கருத்துற வாங்கிடிற்","கொண்டுகொண் டுள்ளே குணம்பல காணலாம்","பண்டுகந் தெங்கும் பழமறை தேடியை","இன்றுகண் டிங்கே இருக்கலு மாமே"],"p":"","r":"If one draws the mind within, looking again and again, one may see many qualities inside, and see here, today, and stay with the one the ancient Veda gladly searched for everywhere.","n":"The contrast is between பண்டு (long ago), எங்கும் (everywhere) and இன்று, இங்கே (today, here): what scripture searched for outside is found within. The one sought is Siva, though not named.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yogam","manam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:64"},"t:579":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":579,"t":["நாபிக்குக் கீழே பன்னிரெண் டங்குலந்","தாபிக்கு மந்திரந் தன்னை அறிகிலர்","தாபிக்கு மந்திரந் தன்னை அறிந்தபின்","கூவிக்கொண் டீசன் குடியிருந் தானே"],"p":"","r":"They don't know the mantra set twelve finger-breadths below the navel; once they know it, the Lord, called, came and dwelt there.","n":"தாபிக்கும் மந்திரம் (the mantra that is established) is unnamed. The variant நாலிரண்டு (four and two, or eight) changes the measure; the variant குடிபுகுந்தானே (moved in) is close in sense.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:64"},"t:580":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":580,"t":["மூலத் திருவிரல் மேலுக்கு முன்நின்ற","பாலித்த யோனிக் கிருவிரற் கீழ்நின்ற","கோலித்த குண்டலி யுள்ளேழுஞ் செஞ்சுடர்","ஞாலத்து நாபிக்கு நால்விரற் கீழதே"],"p":"","r":"Two fingers above the root and two fingers below the yoni stands the coiled kundalini, with seven red flames within it; it lies four fingers below the navel.","n":"மூலம் is the base of the spine or the anus, and யோனி the genital place; the seven red flames are not explained and may be the seven centres or seven bodily elements. The two measures of the place do not obviously agree.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kundalini","yogam","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:64"},"t:581":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":581,"t":["நாசிக் கதோமுகம் பன்னிரண் டங்குலம்","நீசித்தம் வைத்து நினையவும் வல்லையேல்","மாசித்த மாயோகம் வந்து தலைப்பெய்துந்","தேகத்துக் கென்றுஞ் சிதைவில்லை யாமே"],"p":"","r":"If you can fix the mind and meditate twelve finger-breadths down from the nose, the great yoga comes to its height and the body never decays.","n":"நாசிக்கு அதோமுகம் may mean below the nose or the downward breath from the nose. மாசித்த is read as மா சித்த (great perfected). The variants யாகுமே and தானே don't change the sense.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","manam","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:64"},"t:582":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":582,"t":["சோதி இரேகைச் சுடரொளி தோன்றிடிற்","கோதில் பரானந்தம் என்றே குறிக்கொண்மின்","நேர்திகழ் கண்டத்தே நிலவொளி எய்தினால்","ஓதுவ துன்னுடல் உன்மத்த மாமே"],"p":"","r":"If a radiant light appears like a line, take it as flawless supreme bliss; if moonlight reaches the throat, the body that recites becomes ecstatic.","n":"The line of light and the moonlight at the throat are signs of practice I can't fix. உன்மத்தம் may be madness, ecstasy or the yogic state unmani; ஓதுவது உன்னுடல் may mean your body, as it recites.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aanandham","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:64"},"t:583":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":583,"t":["மூலத் துவாரத்தை மொக்கர மிட்டிரு","மேலைத் துவாரத்தின் மேல்மனம் வைத்திரு","வேலொத்த கண்ணை வெளியில் விழித்திரு","காலத்தை வெல்லுங் கருத்திது தானே"],"p":"","r":"Close the lower door firmly, set the mind upon the upper door, keep the spear-like eyes open to the void: this is the way to conquer time.","n":"The lower door is traditionally the anus and the upper the crown of the head. வெளி may be the outer world or the inner void. காலத்தை வெல்லும் may mean conquering death, as in 571.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","manam","kootru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:64"},"t:584":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":584,"t":["எருவிடும் வாசற் கிருவிரன் மேலே","கருவிடும் வாசற் கிருவிரற் கீழே","உருவிடுஞ் சோதியை உள்கவல் லார்க்குக்","கருவிடுஞ் சோதி கலந்துநின் றானே"],"p":"","r":"For those who can meditate on the light that gives form, two fingers above the door that lets out excrement and two below the door that lets out the embryo, the light that gives birth stays mingled with them.","n":"The place is the one given in 580 for kundalini, though this verse names only a light. கருவிடும் சோதி may be the light that ends births (கரு விடும், lets the womb go) as well as the light that gives birth; both readings stand.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","sivam","kundalini"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:64"},"t:585":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":585,"t":["ஒருக்கால் உபாதியை ஒண்சோதி தன்னைப்","பிரித்துணர் வந்த உபாதிப் பிரிவைக்","கரைத்துணர் வுன்னல் கரைதல்உள் நோக்கல்","பிரத்தியா காரப் பெருமைய தாமே"],"p":"","r":"To distinguish at once the bright light from its limiting adjuncts, to dissolve the separation those adjuncts brought, to meditate, to melt and to look within: that is the greatness of pratyahara.","n":"உபாதி (upadhi) is a philosophical term for what limits or conditions the self. The verse defines pratyahara as discrimination and inward looking, not only as drawing in the senses.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","meyyunarvu","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:64"},"t:586":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":586,"t":["புறப்பட்ட வாயுப் புகவிடா வண்ணந்","திறப்பட்டு நிச்சயஞ் சேர்ந்துடன் நின்றால்","உறப்பட்டு நின்றது உள்ளமும் அங்கே","புறப்பட்டுப் போகான் பெருந்தகை யானே"],"p":"","r":"If one keeps the breath that has gone out from re-entering, becomes skilled and stays firm in certainty, and the mind too is fixed there, the great one will not leave.","n":"புறப்பட்ட வாயுப் புகவிடா is odd as read: it may mean keeping the breath out, or not letting it wander in and out. The last line repeats 575's with பெருந்தகை for புரிசடையோன்.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["praanan","manam","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:64"},"t:587":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":587,"t":["குறிப்பினின் உள்ளே குவலயந் தோன்றும்","வெறுப்பிருள் நங்கி விகிர்தனை நாடுங்","சிறப்புறு சிந்தையைச் சிக்கென் றுணரில்","அறிப்புறு காட்சி அமரரு மாமே"],"p":"","r":"Within one's intention the world appears; with the darkness of aversion gone, one seeks the transcendent one; if one grasps firmly the excellent mind, the vision of knowing makes one equal to the gods.","n":"குறிப்பு is intention, or the fixed point of meditation. விகிர்தன் is a name of Siva as the one beyond change. நங்கி is obscure; I read it as நீங்கி (gone).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["manam","sivam","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:64"},"t:588":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":588,"t":["கோணா மனத்தைக் குறிக்கொண்டு கீழ்க்கட்டி","வீணாத்தண் டூடே வெளியுறத் தானோக்கிக்","காணாக்கண் கேளாச் செவியென் றிருப்பார்க்கு","வாணாள் அடைக்கும் வழியது வாமே"],"p":"","r":"For those who fix the unwavering mind, bind it below, look up through the spine into the space above and stay as eyes that do not see and ears that do not hear, this is the way that stops the days of life from running out.","n":"வீணாத்தண்டு (the vina's stem) is traditionally the spine. கோணா மனம் may be the mind that does not bend, or the crooked mind to be bound. வாணாள் அடைக்கும் can mean closing off lifespan, so the verse may promise long life or the end of embodied life.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","manam","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:65"},"t:589":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":589,"t":["மலையார் சிரத்திடை வானீர் அருவி","நிலையாரப் பாயும் நெடுநாடி யூடே","சிலையார் பொதுவில் திருநட மாடுந்","தொலையாத ஆனந்தச் சோதிகண் டேனே"],"p":"","r":"A heavenly waterfall flows steadily from the mountain's peak through the long nadi; in the hall the holy dance goes on, and I saw the light of unending bliss.","n":"The plain landscape and the symbolic body both stand: the mountain-top as the head, the waterfall as the nectar of the yogis, the hall (பொது) as the dance-hall of Siva. சிலையார் may mean of stone or of the bow.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aanandham","naadi","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:65"},"t:590":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":590,"t":["மேலை நிலத்தினாள் வேதகப் பெண்பிள்ளை","மூல நிலத்தில் எழுகின்ற மூர்த்தியை","ஏல எழுப்பி இவளுடன் சந்திக்கப்","பாலனும் ஆவான் பராநந்தி ஆணையே"],"p":"","r":"The girl of the upper region, the transmuting maiden, rouses the form that rises from the root region; when he meets her, he becomes a youth: this is Paranandi's command.","n":"Usually kundalini is the female power that rises from the base to Siva above; here the girl is above and the form rising from the root is male, so the roles may be reversed or my reading wrong. வேதகம் is alchemical transmutation. பாலனும் ஆவான் may mean he becomes a child, as in the return to youth of 569.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kundalini","saththi","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:65"},"t:591":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":591,"t":["கடைவாச லைக்கட்டிக் காலை எழுப்பி","இடைவாசல் நோக்கி இனிதுள் இருத்தி","மடைவாயிற் கொக்குப்போல் வந்தித் திருப்பார்க்","குடையாமல் ஊழி இருக்கலு மாமே"],"p":"","r":"Those who bind the last door, rouse the breath, look to the middle door, settle sweetly within and wait like a heron at the sluice-mouth may stay unbroken for an aeon.","n":"The last and middle doors are traditionally the anus and the central channel (sushumna). The heron at the sluice (மடைவாய்க் கொக்கு) waits motionless for its moment.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["praanan","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:65"},"t:592":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":592,"t":["கலந்த உயிருடன் காலம் அறியில்","கலந்த உயிரது காலின் நெருக்கங்","கலந்த உயிரது காலது கட்டிற்","கலந்த உயிருடல் காலமும் நிற்குமே"],"p":"","r":"If the life joined with the body knows time, the life so joined is the pressure of the breath; if that breath is bound, the life, body and time of the one so joined stand still.","n":"The verse plays on கால், which means both breath and time (காலம்). Each line begins with கலந்த உயிர் (the joined life). The reading is tentative.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["uyir","praanan","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:65"},"t:593":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":593,"t":["வாய்திற வாதார் மனத்திலோர் மாடுண்டு","வாய்திறப் பாரே வளியிட்டுப் பாய்ச்சுவர்","வாய்திற வாதார் மதியிட்டு மூட்டுவர்","கோய்திற வாவிடிற் கோழையுமாமே"],"p":"","r":"Those who don't open the mouth have a treasure in the mind; those who open it drive it out with breath; those who keep it closed kindle it with the moon; if the inner door is not opened, one becomes weak.","n":"Nearly every key word is unclear. மாடு may be wealth or an ox; மதி may be the moon or the mind; கோய் is obscure; கோழை may be phlegm or a coward. The verse seems to praise silence and inner practice, and I can't do more than that.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:65"},"t:594":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":594,"t":["வாழலு மாம்பல காலும் மனத்திடைப்","போழ்கின்ற வாயு புறம்படாப் பாய்ச்சுறில்","எழுசா லேகம் இரண்டு பெருவாய்தல்","பாழி பெரியதோர் பள்ளி அறையே"],"p":"","r":"One may live for many ages if the breath that splits within the mind is driven inward without escaping; the body has seven windows and two great doors, a large cave and a bedchamber.","n":"The second half is a riddle of the body as a house: seven openings in the head and two below, perhaps. பாழி and பள்ளியறை may be the inner space where Siva rests. எழுசாலேகம் is read as seven lattice windows.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["praanan","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:65"},"t:595":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":595,"t":["நிரம்பிய ஈரைந்தில் ஐந்திவை போனால்","இரங்கி விழித்திருந் தென்செய்வை பேதாய்","வரம்பினைக் கோலி வழிசெய்கு வார்க்குக்","குரங்கினைக் கொட்டை பொதியலு மாமே"],"p":"","r":"When five of the full ten have gone, what will you do, fool, waking and lamenting? For those who mark out a boundary and make a way, it is possible to bag the monkey.","n":"The ten are traditionally the ten vital airs, five of which leave at death. The monkey is the restless mind. கொட்டை பொதியலும் is obscure; the variant கோட்டை (fort) gives 'shut the monkey in a fort'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["praanan","manam","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:65"},"t:596":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":596,"t":["முன்னம் வந்தனர் எல்லாம் முடிந்தனர்","பின்னை வந்தவர்க் கென்ன பிரமாணம்","முன்னுறு கோடி உறுகதி பேசிடில்","என்ன மாயம் இடிகரை நிற்குமே"],"p":"","r":"All who came before have ended; what assurance is there for those who came after? If one talks of crores of states to come, what a trick: will a crumbling bank stand?","n":"The crumbling bank (இடிகரை) is the body. உறுகதி may be states or destinies hoped for after death, which the verse mocks as reasons to wait. The verse's place in the chapter suggests it urges the practice now.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:65"},"t:597":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":597,"t":["அரித்த வுடலைஐம் பூதத்தில் வைத்துப்","பொருத்தஐம் பூதஞ்சத் தாதியிற் போந்து","தெரித்த மனாதிசத் தாதியிற் செல்லத்","தரித்தது தாரணை தற்பரத் தோடே"],"p":"","r":"Placing the dissolved body in the five elements, the five elements going into sound and the other subtle elements, and the mind and the rest going into what lies beyond them: dharana is holding it all with the Supreme.","n":"The verse runs the tattvas backward, each into its cause. சத்தாதி appears twice, for the subtle elements and for something higher; the repetition may be a corruption. தற்பரம் is the Supreme.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","yogam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:65"},"t:598":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":598,"t":["வருமாதி யீரெட்டுள் வந்த தியானம்","பொருவாத புந்தி புலன்போக மேவல்","உருவாய சத்தி பரத்தியான முன்னுங்","குருவார் சிவத்தியானம் யோகத்தின் கூறே"],"p":"","r":"Meditation, which comes among the limbs counted from the first, is the unwavering intellect's dealing with the senses' enjoyment; meditation on Shakti in form, on the Supreme, and on Siva who comes as guru are the parts of yoga.","n":"ஈரெட்டு (two eights, sixteen) is unclear: it may count the limbs or a larger list of disciplines. புலன்போக மேவல் can be split to mean either settling in the senses' objects or the intellect not going after them; the second fits the chapter better.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiyaanam","yogam","saththi","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:66"},"t:599":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":599,"t":["கண்ணாக்கு மூக்குச் செவஞானக் கூட்டத்துட்","பண்ணாக்கி நின்ற பழம்பொருள் ஒன்றுண்(டு)","அண்ணாக்கின் உள்ளே அகண்ட ஒளிகாட்டிப்","புண்ணாக்கி நம்மைப் பிழைப்பித்த வாறே"],"p":"","r":"Within the gathering of eye, tongue, nose, ear and knowing there is one ancient Being who tunes them; showing an unbroken light within the palate, he wore us down and so saved us.","n":"The verse rhymes கண்ணாக்கு, பண்ணாக்கி, அண்ணாக்கு, புண்ணாக்கி. புண்ணாக்கி may mean 'wounding' us or, with the variant பிண்ணாக்கி, crushing us like oil-cake; either way the saving comes through being broken. The palate (அண்ணாக்கு) as a seat of inner light is a yogic teaching.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","thiyaanam","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:66"},"t:600":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":600,"t":["ஒண்ணா நயனத்தில் உற்ற ஒளிதன்னைக்","கண்ணாரப் பார்த்துக் கலந்தங் கிருந்திடில்","விண்ணாறு வந்து வெளிகண் டிடவோடிப்","பண்ணாமல் நின்றது பார்க்கலு மாமே"],"p":"","r":"If one looks to the full at the light in the eye that cannot be reached and stays merged there, the way of the sky opens, runs on until the open space is seen, and what stands unmade can be seen.","n":"ஒண்ணா நயனம் (the eye one cannot attain) is commonly taken as the inner or third eye. விண்ணாறு (sky-river or sky-path) and வெளி (open space) are yogic terms whose exact sense I can't fix.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiyaanam","meyyunarvu","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:66"},"t:601":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":601,"t":["ஒருபொழு துன்னார் உடலோ டுயிரை","ஒருபொழு துன்னார் உயிருட் சிவனை","ஒருபொழு துன்னார் சிவனுறை சிந்தையை","ஒருபொழு துன்னார் சந்திரப் பூவே"],"p":"","r":"Not once do people contemplate the life within the body, not once Siva within the life, not once the mind where Siva dwells, not once the moon-flower.","n":"The four lines step inward, from body to life to Siva to the mind that holds him. சந்திரப் பூ (moon-flower) is traditionally the moon region at the crown of the head; it cannot be fixed from the verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["uyir","sivam","manam","thiyaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:66"},"t:602":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":602,"t":["மனத்து விளக்கினை மாண்பட ஏற்றிச்","சினத்து விளக்கினைச் செல்ல நெருக்கி","அனைத்து விளக்குந் திரியொக்கத் தூண்ட","மனத்து விளக்கது மாயா விளக்கே"],"p":"","r":"Light the lamp of the mind to its full, press the lamp of anger until it goes out, and trim every lamp's wick alike: then the lamp of the mind becomes a lamp that does not die.","n":"மாயா விளக்கு means 'an undying lamp', and may also play on மாயை. Anger is itself called a lamp, one burning the wrong way, so meditation needs a moral step as well as a technique.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["manam","vekuli","thiyaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:66"},"t:603":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":603,"t":["எண்ணா யிரத்தாண்டு யோகம் இருக்கினும்","கண்ணார் அமுதினைக் கண்டறி வாரில்லை","உண்ணாடிக் குள்ளே ஒளியுற நோக்கினால்","கண்ணாடி போலக் கலந்துநின் றானே"],"p":"","r":"Though one sit in yoga eight thousand years, no one sees and knows the nectar in the eye; if one looks within the inner channel until light comes, he stands merged in it as in a mirror.","n":"உண்ணாடி can be உள் நாடி (the inner nadi) or 'seeking within'. The verse sets long practice against the right inward look: duration alone yields nothing. Sengalvaraya cites Kandar Alangaram 85 as a parallel.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiyaanam","naadi","yogam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:66"},"t:604":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":604,"t":["நாட்டமும் இரண்டும் நடுமூக்கில் வைத்திடில்","வாட்டமும் இல்லை மனைக்கும் அழிவில்லை","ஓட்டமும் இல்லை உணர்வில்லை தானில்லை","தேட்டமும் இல்லை சிவனவ நாமே"],"p":"","r":"If both gazes are set at the middle of the nose, there is no withering, no ruin for the house, no running, no feeling, no self, no seeking: we become Siva.","n":"மனை (house) is usually taken as the body. The run of 'no' clauses describes the stilling of breath, sense and ego; சிவனவ நாமே (we are he, Siva) states identity outright. The variant வேட்டமும் (desire) for தேட்டமும் (seeking) is close in sense.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiyaanam","yaakkai","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:66"},"t:605":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":605,"t":["நயனம் இரண்டும் நாசிமேல் வைத்திட்","டுயர்வெழா வாயுவை உள்ளே அடக்கித்","துயரற நாடியே தூங்கவல் லார்க்குப்","பயனிது காயம் பயமில்லை தானே"],"p":"","r":"Setting both eyes on the nose and holding within the breath that does not rise, for those who can rest in absorption, seeking the end of sorrow, this is the fruit: the body is without fear.","n":"தூங்க (to sleep) is taken in the yogic sense of absorbed stillness, as often in Tirumular. நாடியே can be 'seeking' or 'by the nadi'. உயர்வெழா வாயு is unclear: breath that is not let rise, or held so it rises within.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiyaanam","praanan","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:66"},"t:606":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":606,"t":["மணிகடல் யானை வார்குழல் மேகம்","அணிவண்டு தும்பி வளைபேரி கையாழ்","தணிந்தெழு நாதங்கள் தாமிவை பத்தும்","பணிந்தவர்க் கல்லது பார்க்கஒண் ணாதே"],"p":"","r":"Bell, sea, elephant, long flute, cloud, the bee, the humming beetle, conch, drum and lute: these ten softened inner sounds cannot be perceived except by those who have bowed.","n":"The list is the yogic ten sounds (dasa nada) heard in meditation. The condition the verse sets is humility or devotion (பணிந்தவர்), not technique.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadham","thiyaanam","panivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:66"},"t:607":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":607,"t":["கடலொடு மேகங் களிறொடும் ஓசை","அடவெழும் வீணை அண்டரண் டத்துச்","சுடர்மன்னு வேணுச் சுரிசங்கின் ஓசை","திடமறி யோகிக்கல் லாற்றெறி யாதே"],"p":"","r":"The sound of sea and clouds and elephant, the vina rising in the world of the gods, the flute with abiding light, and the curling conch: none but the yogi who knows steadiness can know them.","n":"A second list of inner sounds, this time conditioned on the yogi's firmness (திடம்), where 606 asked for humility. சுடர்மன்னு வேணு (flute with abiding light) has variants சுடர்மனு and சுடர்மணி and is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naadham","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:66"},"t:608":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":608,"t":["ஈசன் இயல்பும் இமையவர் ஈட்டமும்","பாசம் இயங்கும் பரிந்துய ராய்நிற்கும்","ஓசை யதன்மணம் போல விடுவதோர்","ஓசையாம் ஈசன் உணரவல் லார்க்கே"],"p":"","r":"The Lord's nature, the host of the gods and the working of the bonds all stand in sound; for those able to perceive it, the Lord is a sound that spreads like its own fragrance.","n":"Lines 2 and 3 are compressed and the syntax is uncertain. The comparison of sound to fragrance suggests a presence that pervades without being seen; the reading that all three stand in nada is the usual one, not the only one.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naadham","pathi","paasam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:66"},"t:609":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":609,"t":["நாத முடிவிலே நல்லாள் இருப்பது","நாத முடிவிலே நல்யோகம் இருப்பது","நாத முடிவிலே நாட்டம் இருப்பது","நாத முடிவிலே நஞ்சுண்ட கண்டனே"],"p":"","r":"At the end of nada the good lady dwells, at the end of nada is good yoga, at the end of nada is the aim, at the end of nada is the one whose throat held the poison.","n":"நாத முடிவு (nadanta, the end or limit of sound) is where sound subsides into its source. நல்லாள் is traditionally Shakti and நஞ்சுண்ட கண்டன் is Siva, so the verse places both beyond sound. நாட்டம் can be 'the gaze' or 'the goal'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadham","saththi","sivam","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:66"},"t:610":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":610,"t":["உதிக்கின்ற ஆறினும் உள்ளங்கி ஐந்துந்","துதிக்கின்ற தேசுடைத் தூங்கிருள் நீங்கி","அதிக்கின்ற ஐவருள் நாதம் ஒடுங்கக்","கதிக்கொன்றை ஈசன் கழல்சேர லாமே"],"p":"","r":"When, among the six that rise and the five inner fires, the deep darkness lifts and nada subsides among the five that oppress, one can reach the anklets of the Lord of the konrai, the goal.","n":"ஆறு (six) is commonly taken as the six centres, அங்கி ஐந்து as five fires and ஐவர் as the senses, but none is fixed by the verse. The variant ஐவர் அருள்நாதமோடும் gives a different syntax for line 3.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naadham","sivam","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:66"},"t:611":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":611,"t":["பள்ளி அறையிற் பகலே இருளில்லை","கொள்ளி அறையிற் கொளுந்தாமற் காக்கலாம்","ஒள்ளி தறியிலோ ரோசனை நீளிது","வெள்ளி அறையில் விடிவில்லை தானே"],"p":"","r":"In the bedchamber it is day and there is no darkness; in the chamber of fire one can keep it from blazing; in the bright pillar the way is a yojana long; in the silver chamber there is no dawn.","n":"The four chambers are taken as places in the yogic body, perhaps the heart, the fire at the base, the spinal channel and the moon region at the crown. ஒள்ளி தறியிலோ ரோசனை is obscure and the whole verse rests on symbols I can't fix.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:66"},"t:612":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":612,"t":["கொண்ட விரதங் குறையாமற் றானொன்றித்","தண்டுடன் ஓடித் தலைப்பட்ட யோகிக்கு","மண்டல மூன்றினும் ஒக்க வளர்ந்தபின்","பிண்டமும் ஊழி பிரியா திருக்குமே"],"p":"","r":"For the yogi who keeps his vow undiminished, becomes one, runs up the spine and reaches the head, once he has grown alike in all three mandalas, the body too stays an aeon without parting.","n":"தண்டு (staff) is usually the spinal channel and the three mandalas are traditionally fire, sun and moon. பிண்டமும் (the body too) implies the life stays with it. ஊழி may be literal (an aeon) or a way of saying 'very long'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","yaakkai","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:66"},"t:613":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":613,"t":["அவ்வவர் மண்டல மாம்பரி சொன்றுண்டு","அவ்வவர் மண்டலத் தவ்வவர் தேவராம்","அவ்வவர் மண்டலம் அவ்வவர்க் கேவரில்","அவ்வவர் மண்டல மாயமற் றோர்க்கே"],"p":"","r":"There is a way in which each one's mandala comes; in each one's mandala are that one's gods; when each one's mandala comes to that one, each one's mandala is illusion to others.","n":"மாயமற் றோர்க்கே can be split as மாயம் மற்றோர்க்கு (illusion to others) or மாயம் அற்றோர்க்கு (for those free of illusion). On the first, inner attainment is private; on the second, it belongs only to the undeluded. Who அவ்வவர் (each of them) are, practitioners or deities, is also open.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","maayai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:66"},"t:614":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":614,"t":["இளைக்கின்ற நெஞ்சத் திருட்டறை உள்ளே","முளைக்கின்ற மண்டலம் மூன்றினும் ஒன்றித்","துளைப்பெரும் பாசந் துருவிடு மாகில்","இளைப்பின்றி மார்க்கழி ஏற்றம தாமே"],"p":"","r":"If, inside the dark chamber of the weakening heart, one unites with the three mandalas that spring up there and pierces the great bond of the passage, the ascent comes without weariness.","n":"துளைப் பெரும் பாசம் (the great bond with an opening, or of the passage) and மார்க்கழி ஏற்றம் are obscure; மார்க்கழி may be the month or a form of மார்க்கம் (the way). The heart is called dark and weakening before the practice.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["paasam","yogam","nenju"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:66"},"t:615":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":615,"t":["முக்குண மூடற வாயுவை மூலத்தே","சிக்கென மூடித் திரித்துப் பிடித்திட்டுத்","தக்க வலமிடம் நாழிகை சாதிக்க","வைக்கும் உயர்நிலை வானவர் கோனே"],"p":"","r":"To end the covering of the three gunas, close the breath firmly at the base, turn it and hold it, and master the right and left by the measure of time: then the King of the gods grants the high state.","n":"வலமிடம் (right and left) is taken as the two nostrils or the two channels, and நாழிகை as a timed measure of breath. வானவர் கோன் here is presumably Siva, not Indra.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["praanan","yogam","naadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:66"},"t:616":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":616,"t":["நடலித்த நாபிக்கு நால்விரன் மேலே","மடலித்த வாணிக் கிருவிரல் உள்ளே","கடலித் திருந்து கருதவல் லார்கள்","சடலத் தலைவனைத் தாமறிந் தாரே"],"p":"","r":"Those who can stay four fingers above the navel and two fingers within the seat of speech, deep as the sea, and contemplate there have known the Lord of the body.","n":"நடலித்த, மடலித்த and கடலித்து are rare forms and may be corrupt; வாணி (speech, or its goddess) is usually taken as the throat. The finger measures locate centres in the yogic body. சடலத் தலைவன் (lord of the body) is Siva as indweller.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiyaanam","yaakkai","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:66"},"t:617":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":617,"t":["அறிவாய சத்தென்னு மாறா றகன்று","செறிவான மாயை சிதைத்தரு ளாலே","பிறியாத பேரரு ளாயிடும் பெற்றி","நெறியான அன்பர் நிலையறிந் தாரே"],"p":"","r":"Going beyond the thirty-six that are called real to knowledge, and shattering dense maya by grace, the devotees on the path have known the state in which great grace stays unparted.","n":"ஆறாறு (six sixes) is the 36 tattvas. The variant அறியா யசந்தென்னு would call them 'unknowing, unreal' (asat), which fits Siddhanta teaching better than 'real to knowledge'. The verse gives the last step to grace and to the devotee (அன்பர்), not to technique.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","maayai","thiruvarul","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:66"},"t:618":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":618,"t":["சமாதி யமாதியிற் றான்செல்லக் கூடும்","சமாதி யமாதியிற் றானெட்டுச் சித்தி","சமாதி யமாதியில் தங்கினோர்க் கன்றே","சமாதி யமாதி தலைப்படுந் தானே"],"p":"","r":"Samadhi can be reached by going through yama and the limbs that follow; in them lie the eight siddhis; to those who abide in yama and the rest, samadhi comes of itself.","n":"Each line turns on சமாதி யமாதி, a chime of samadhi with yama-adi (yama and the rest). The verse binds the highest limb to the first, the ethical one: there is no samadhi without yama.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["samaadhi","yogam","siththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:67"},"t:619":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":619,"t":["விந்துவும் நாதமும் மேருவில் ஓங்கிடிற்","சந்தியி லான சமாதியிற் கூடிடும்","அந்த மிலாத அறிவின் அரும்பொருள்","சுந்தரச் சோதியுந் தோன்றிடுந் தானே"],"p":"","r":"If bindu and nada rise high in Meru, they join in the samadhi where there is no juncture, and the rare substance of endless knowledge and a beautiful light appear.","n":"மேரு is traditionally the spine or the crown of the head. சந்தி இல்லாத (without juncture) may mean a state with no meeting of opposites, or one beyond the junctures of time. Both bindu and nada are technical terms here.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["samaadhi","vindhu","naadham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:67"},"t:620":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":620,"t":["மன்மனம் எங்குண்டு வாயுவும் அங்குண்டு","மன்மனம் எங்கில்லை வாயுவும் அங்கில்லை","மன்மனத் துள்ளே மகிழ்ந்திருப் பார்க்கு","மன்மனத் துள்ளே மனோலய மாமே"],"p":"","r":"Where the abiding mind is, there the breath is; where the mind is not, the breath is not; for those who rejoice within the abiding mind, within it comes the dissolving of mind.","n":"The verse states the yogic teaching that mind and breath move together, so stilling one stills the other. மன்மனம் may be 'the abiding mind' or simply a doubled மனம். மனோலயம் (the mind's dissolving) is the goal, reached inside the mind and not by force.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["manam","praanan","samaadhi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:67"},"t:621":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":621,"t":["விண்டலர் கூபமும் விஞ்சத் தடவியுங்-","கண்டுணர் வாகக் கருதி யிருப்பர்கள்","செண்டு வெளியிற் செழுங்கிரி யத்திடை","கொண்டு குதிரை குசைசெறுத் தாரே"],"p":"","r":"They contemplate the opened well and the dense forest until they know them by seeing; in the open space of the ball, between the rich hills, they reined the horse with the bridle.","n":"Every image is symbolic: the horse is commonly taken as the breath or mind and the rein as its control. கூபம் (well), விஞ்சத் தடவி (Vindhya forest, or dense wood) and செண்டு வெளி (open ground of the ball) are places in the yogic body that I can't fix.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","praanan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:67"},"t:622":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":622,"t":["மூல நாடி முகட்டல குச்சியுள்","நாலு வாசல் நடுவுள் இருப்பிர்காள்","மேலை வாசல் வெளியுறக் கண்டபின்","காலன் வார்த்தை கனாவிலும் இல்லையே"],"p":"","r":"You who stay in the middle of the four doors at the summit of the root channel: once you have seen the upper door open onto space, Death's word will not reach you even in dreams.","n":"மூல நாடி (root nadi) is usually the central channel and மேலை வாசல் (upper door) the opening at the crown. The last line can be read literally, that the yogi escapes death, or as freedom from fear of it; the variant முக்கடல் for முகட்டு changes line 1.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naadi","yogam","kootru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:67"},"t:623":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":623,"t":["மண்டலம் ஐந்து வரைகளும் ஈராறு","கொண்டிட நிற்குங் குடிகளும் ஆறெண்மர்","கண்டிட நிற்குங் கருத்து நடுவாக","உண்டு நிலாவிடும் ஓடும் பதத்தையே"],"p":"","r":"Five mandalas, twelve bounds and forty-eight dwellers holding them, with the aim seen in the middle: there the moon shines and flows on the moving step.","n":"The numbers (5, 12, six eights) map the yogic body in a way the verse does not explain; tradition takes the moon as the nectar flowing from the crown. ஓடும் பதம் is obscure. A short reading is all the Tamil supports.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:67"},"t:624":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":624,"t":["பூட்டொத்து மெய்யிற் பொறிபட்ட வாயுவைத்","தேட்டற்ற வந்நிலஞ் சேரும் படிவைத்து","நாட்டத்தை மீட்டு நயனத் திருப்பார்க்குத்","தோட்டத்து மாம்பழந் தூங்கலு மாமே"],"p":"","r":"For those who lock the breath that stirs in the body as with a bolt, settle it in that ground where seeking ends, and turn the gaze back to rest within the eye, the mango in the garden hangs within reach.","n":"The ripe mango hanging in one's own garden is the fruit of practice, near at hand. தேட்டற்ற நிலம் (ground without seeking) echoes 604's தேட்டமும் இல்லை. Which bodily ground is meant is not said.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["praanan","thiyaanam","samaadhi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:67"},"t:625":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":625,"t":["உருவறி யும்பரி சொன்றுண்டு வானோர்","கருவரை பற்றிக் கடைந்தமு துண்டார்","அருவரை யேறி அமுதுண்ண மாட்டார்","திருவரை யாமனந் தீர்ந்தற்ற வாறே"],"p":"","r":"There is a way to know the form: the gods seized the dark mountain, churned and drank the nectar, but they will not climb the rare mountain and drink the nectar there; so the mind that is the holy mountain ended and ceased.","n":"The contrast is between the gods' outer churning of the ocean with Mount Mandara and the inner mountain, taken as the crown of the head, where the yogi drinks nectar. Line 4 (திருவரை யாமனந் தீர்ந்தற்ற வாறே) is obscure and its word division uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:67"},"t:626":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":626,"t":["நம்பனை யாதியை நான்மறை ஓதியைச்","செம்பொனின் உள்ளே திகழ்கின்ற சோதியை","அன்பினை யாக்கி யருத்தி ஒடுக்கிப்போய்க்","கொம்பேறிக் கும்பிட்டுக் கூட்டமிட் டாரே"],"p":"","r":"Making love the means and subduing desire, they went to the Trusted One, the First, the chanter of the four Vedas, the light shining within red gold, climbed the branch, worshipped and gathered there.","n":"கொம்பேறி (having climbed the branch) is traditionally the ascent to the crown. அருத்தி may be desire (subdued) or a form of 'feeding'. The verse joins yogic ascent and devotional worship.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anbu","iraivan","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:67"},"t:627":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":627,"t":["மூலத்து மேலது முச்சது ரத்தது","காலத் திசையிற் கலக்கின்ற சந்தினில்","மேலைப் பிறையினில் நெற்றிநேர் நின்ற","கோலத்தின் கோலங்கள் வெவ்வேறு கொண்டதே"],"p":"","r":"Above the base is the three-sided form; at the juncture where the paths of time meet, at the upper crescent standing level with the forehead, the form takes many different forms.","n":"முச்சதுரம் may be a triangle or a figure of three squares, traditionally the seat of kundalini above the base. மேலைப் பிறை (upper crescent) is the moon at the brow or crown. The verse is a map I can't fix.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","kundalini"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:67"},"t:628":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":628,"t":["கற்பனை யற்றுக் கனல்வழி யேசென்று","சிற்பனை எல்லாஞ் சிருட்டித்த பேரொளிப்","பொற்பினை நாடிப் புணர்மதி யோடுற்றுத்","தற்பர மாகத் தகுந்தண் சமாதியே"],"p":"","r":"Free of imagining, going by the path of fire, seeking the splendour of the great light that made all forms and meeting it with the joined moon, one becomes the Supreme: that is cool samadhi.","n":"கனல் வழி (path of fire) and புணர் மதி (the moon joined) are traditionally the rising power meeting the moon at the crown. Samadhi is called தண் (cool) after the fire of the ascent. தற்பரம் is the Supreme, or the state of being it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["samaadhi","sivam","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:67"},"t:629":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":629,"t":["தலைப்பட் டிருந்திடத் தத்துவங் கூடும்","வலைப்பட் டிருந்திடும் மாதுநல் லாளுங்","குலைப்பட் டிருந்திடுங் கோபம் அகலுந்","துலைப்பட் டிருந்திடுந் தூங்கவல் லார்க்கே"],"p":"","r":"For those able to rest in absorption, when they reach the summit the tattvas gather, the fair good lady is caught as in a net, anger is broken and goes, and they stay in balance.","n":"மாதுநல்லாள் may be Shakti, held within the yogi, or worldly woman, no longer a snare; the verse does not say. துலை (balance, scales) gives the image of equanimity.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["samaadhi","thaththuvam","vekuli","samanilai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:67"},"t:630":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":630,"t":["சோதித் தனிச்சுட ராய்நின்ற தேவனும்","ஆதியும் உள்நின்ற சீவனு மாகுமால்","ஆதிப் பிரமன் பெருங்கடல் வண்ணனும்","ஆதி அடிபணிந் தன்புறு வாரே"],"p":"","r":"The God who stood as the single flame of light becomes both the First and the soul that stands within; the first Brahma and the one coloured like the great sea bow at the First's feet and gain love.","n":"The verse identifies the light seen in samadhi with the soul within (சீவன்), a strong claim on its face. The variant முன்நின்ற (standing before) for உள்நின்ற (standing within) would weaken it. Brahma and Vishnu are shown as Siva's worshippers.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","uyir","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:67"},"t:631":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":631,"t":["சமாதிசெய் வார்க்குத் தகும்பல யோகஞ்","சமாதிகள் வேண்டாம் இறையுட னேகிற்","சமாதிதா னில்லை தானவ னாகிற்","சமாதியில் எட்டெட்டுச் சித்தியும் எய்துமே"],"p":"","r":"Many yogas suit those who practise samadhi; if one goes with the Lord, no samadhis are needed; if one becomes him, there is no samadhi at all; yet in samadhi all sixty-four siddhis are attained.","n":"The verse ranks three states: practising samadhi, going with the Lord, and becoming him, where samadhi has no meaning. எட்டெட்டு (eight eights) is read as sixty-four; it could also mean the eight siddhis in each of their kinds. The last line restores samadhi's worth after relativizing it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["samaadhi","siththi","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:67"},"t:632":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":632,"t":["போதுகந் தேறும் புரிசடை யானடி","யாதுகந் தாரம ராபதிக் கேசெல்வர்","ஏதுகந் தானிவன் என்றருள் செய்திடு","மாதுகந் தாடிடு மால்விடை யோனே"],"p":"","r":"Those who delight in the feet of the matted-haired one who gladly rises into the flower will go to Amaravati; the one on the great bull, who dances with the lady, will grant grace, asking 'what did this one desire?'","n":"போது உகந்து ஏறும் (who delights to mount the flower) is traditionally the Lord rising in the heart's lotus; Sengalvaraya parallels Kural 3. அமராபதி is Indra's heaven, a lower goal than release, which the next verses go beyond. ஏதுகந்தான் இவன் may be 'what does he want?', a gracious question, as read here.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","thiruvarul","marumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:68"},"t:633":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":633,"t":["பற்றிப் பதத்தன்பு வைத்துப் பரன்புகழ்","கற்றிருந் தாங்கே கருது மவர்கட்கு","முற்றெழுந் தாங்கே முனிவர் எதிர்வரத்","தெற்றுஞ் சிவபதஞ் சேரலு மாமே"],"p":"","r":"For those who hold to his feet with love, learn the Supreme's praise and contemplate him where they are, sages come to meet them as they rise, and they can reach the clear Siva-state.","n":"The means named here are devotional (love, learning his praise, contemplation), though the chapter is the fruit of yoga. முற்றெழுந்தாங்கே may be 'as they rise in full' or 'as they rise at the end'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","sivam","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:68"},"t:634":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":634,"t":["வந்தித் தவஞ்செய்து வானவர் கோவாய்த்","திருந்தம ராபதிச் செல்வன் இவனெனத்","தருந்தண் முழவங் குழலும் இயம்ப","இருந்தின்பம் எய்துவர் ஈசன் அருளே"],"p":"","r":"Having worshipped and done austerity and become king of the gods, they are hailed as the lord of Amaravati, and with cool drums and flutes sounding they dwell in bliss: this is the Lord's grace.","n":"Whether the yogi literally becomes Indra or is welcomed like a king of the gods is open. The last words credit the bliss to grace, not to the austerity.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","thiruvarul","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:68"},"t:635":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":635,"t":["செம்பொற் சிவகதி சென்றெய்துங் காலத்துக்","கும்பத் தமரர் குழாம்வந் தெதிர்கொள்ள","எம்பொற் றலைவன் இவனா மெனச்சொல்ல","இன்பக் கலவி இருக்கலு மாமே"],"p":"","r":"At the time they go and reach the red-gold Siva-way, hosts of gods come out to meet them, saying 'this is our golden lord', and they may dwell in blissful union.","n":"கும்பத்து அமரர் is unclear: the gods of the dome of heaven, or of the summit in the body. இன்பக் கலவி (blissful union) is union with Siva. The gods now call the yogi their lord, a step beyond 634.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","veedu","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:68"},"t:636":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":636,"t":["சேருறு காலந் திசைநின்ற தேவர்கள்","ஆரிவன் என்ன அரனாம் இவனென்ன","ஏருறு தேவர்கள் எல்லாம் எதிர்கொள்ளக்","காருறு கண்டனை மெய்கண்ட வாறே"],"p":"","r":"When he arrives, the gods of the directions ask 'who is this?', are told 'this is Hara', and all the splendid gods come to meet him: so he truly saw the dark-throated one.","n":"The yogi is taken for Siva himself, which the verse gives as the proof that he truly saw Siva. மெய்கண்ட (truly saw) may also echo the name of the Siddhanta's Meykandar, but nothing in the verse requires it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:68"},"t:637":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":637,"t":["நல்வழி நாடி நமன்வழி மாற்றிடுஞ்","சொல்வழி யாளர் சுருங்காப் பெருங்கொடை","இல்வழி யாளர் இமையவர் எண்டிசைப்","பல்வழி எய்தினும் பார்வழி யாகுமே"],"p":"","r":"Those of the true word who seek the good way and turn aside Death's way, and householders whose great giving never shrinks, though they reach the gods' many ways in the eight directions, find it becomes the way of the earth.","n":"பார்வழி யாகுமே is the crux. Read as 'it leads back to the earth', the verse says merit wins heavens but not release, against the yogi's attainment. Read as 'it becomes the way for the world', it praises such people as a model. Both stand.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","ilvaazhkkai","marumai","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:68"},"t:638":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":638,"t":["தூங்க வல் லார்க்கும் துணையேழ் புவனமும்","வாங்கவல் லார்க்கும் வலிசெய்து நின்றிடுந்","தேங்க வல் லார்க்கும் திளைக்கும் அமுதமுந்","தாங்கவல் லார்க்குந் தன்னிட மாமே"],"p":"","r":"For those able to rest in absorption, and those able to draw in the seven worlds, it stands as strength; for those able to store it, it is nectar to revel in; for those able to bear it, it becomes their own dwelling.","n":"The subject is not stated; it may be the Lord, the attainment or the yogic power. Each line names a capacity (வல்லார்க்கு, those able to) and a matching gift. The variants alter case endings but not the sense.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","siththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:68"},"t:639":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":639,"t":["காரிய மான உபாதியைத் தாங்கடந்","தாரிய காரணம் ஏழுந்தன் பாலுற","ஆரிய காரண மாய தவத்திடைத்","தாரியல் தற்பரஞ் சேர்தல் சமாதியே"],"p":"","r":"Going beyond the limiting adjunct that is effect, so that the seven noble causes come to oneself, and in the austerity that is the noble cause joining the Supreme: that is samadhi.","n":"உபாதி (upadhi, limiting adjunct) and காரியம், காரணம் (effect, cause) are technical; the seven causes are not identified in the verse. தாரியல் may be 'garlanded' or a form of 'bearing'. The definition parallels 597's for dharana and 628's for samadhi.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["samaadhi","thavam","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:68"},"t:640":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":640,"t":["பணிந்தெண் திசையும் பரமனை நாடித்","துணிந்தெண் திசையுந் தொழுதெம் பிரானை","அணிந்தெண் திசையினும் அட்டமா சித்தி","தணிந்தெண் திசைசென்று தாபித்த வாறே"],"p":"","r":"Bowing and seeking the Supreme in all eight directions, worshipping our Lord there with resolve, the eight great siddhis were established in the eight directions.","n":"எண் திசை (eight directions) repeats in every line, matching the eight siddhis. Who established them, and what தணிந்து (calmed, subdued) qualifies, is unclear; the verse may be the poet's own account of how he received them.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["siththi","vazhipaadu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:641":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":641,"t":["பரிசறி வானவர் பண்பன் அடியெனத்","துரிசற நாடியே தூவெளி கண்டேன்","அரிய தெனக்கில்லை அட்டமா சித்தி","பெரிதருள் செய்து பிறப்பறுத் தானே"],"p":"","r":"Seeking without fault the feet of the Lord whose nature the gods know, I saw the pure space; the eight great siddhis are not hard for me, for he gave great grace and cut off my birth.","n":"தூவெளி (pure space) is a yogic term for the open expanse of awareness, as tradition takes it. The verse sets the siddhis beside the cutting of birth, and credits both to grace rather than to technique.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["siththi","thiruvarul","piravi","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:642":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":642,"t":["குரவன் அருளிற் குறிவழி மூலன்","பரையின் மணமிகு சங்கட்டம் பார்த்துத்","தெரிதரு சாம்பவி கேசரி சேரப்","பெரிய சிவகதி பேறெட்டாஞ் சித்தியே"],"p":"","r":"By the guru's grace, Mular, following the sign, looked to the fragrant ... of Parai; with the shambhavi and khechari seals joined, the great Siva-state is the gain of the eight siddhis.","n":"சாம்பவி and கேசரி are names of yogic seals (mudras), traditionally the inward gaze and the tongue turned back. சங்கட்டம் is obscure; a variant reads சக்கட்டம், perhaps the wheel or circle. The verse names the author, Mular.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["siththi","guru","yogam","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:643":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":643,"t":["காயாதி பூதங் கலைகால மாயையில்","ஆயா தகல அறிவொன் றனாதியே","ஓயாப் பதியதன் உண்மையைக் கூடினால்","வீயாப் பரகாயம் மேவலு மாமே"],"p":"","r":"If the one beginningless knowledge, which body, elements, kala, time and maya cannot reach, joins the truth of the unceasing Lord, entering another's body becomes possible.","n":"காயாதி பூதம் கலை காலம் மாயை lists tattvas from the body up to maya. பரகாயம் is usually entering another's body; it can also be read as the supreme body. ஆயாது அகல is unclear: 'parting without being examined' or 'separating without being caught'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["siththi","pathi","maayai","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:644":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":644,"t":["இருபதி நாயிரத் தெண்ணூறு பேதம்","மருவிய கன்ம மாமந்த யோகந்","தருமிவை காய உழைப்பாகுந் தானே","அருமிகு நான்காய் அடங்குமா சித்திக்கே"],"p":"","r":"The twenty thousand eight hundred kinds of karma, mantra and yoga only give the body toil; for siddhi they come down to four.","n":"The number and the four (a variant has அருமிரு, two) are not explained. The plain point is that many practices reduce to a few that matter.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["siththi","yogam","mandhiram"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:645":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":645,"t":["மதிதனில் ஈராறாய் மன்னுங் கலையின்","உதய மதுநா லொழியவோ ரெட்டுப்","பதியுமஈ ராறாண்டு பற்றறப் பார்க்கில்","திதமான ஈராறு சித்திக ளாமே"],"p":"","r":"Of the kalas that abide as twelve in the moon, when the rising of four is gone and eight remain, if one looks without attachment for twelve years, twelve firm siddhis arise.","n":"The moon's kalas are a figure for the breath and the inner moon in yogic reading; the arithmetic of twelve, four and eight is obscure. The twelve years open the sequence of powers by year in 646 to 648.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["siththi","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:646":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":646,"t":["நாடும் பிணியாகு நஞ்சனஞ் சூழ்ந்தக்கால்","நீடுங் கலைகல்வி நீள்மேதை கூர்ஞானம்","பீடொன்றி னால்வாயாச் சித்திபே தத்தின்","நீடுங் துரங்கேட்டல் நீண்முடி வீராறே"],"p":"","r":"Powers that come in order with the years: freedom when disease and poison surround, arts and learning, long intellect and keen wisdom, ... and hearing from afar, ending at the twelfth.","n":"The verse is a compressed list and its grammar is hard to follow; பீடொன்றினால் வாயா is unclear. The list overlaps with the powers in 705 and 706.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["siththi","kalvi","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:647":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":647,"t":["ஏழா னதிற்சண்ட வாயுவின் வேகியாந்","தாழா நடைபல யோசனை சார்ந்திடுஞ்","சூழான ஓரெட்டில் தோன்றா நரைதிரை","தாழான ஒன்பதிற் றான்பர காயமே"],"p":"","r":"In the seventh year one has the speed of a storm wind, an untiring pace that covers many yojanas; in the eighth grey hair and wrinkles do not appear; in the ninth comes entering another's body.","n":"The years are supplied from the ordinals (ஏழு, எட்டு, ஒன்பது) and the context of 645; they may count stages rather than years. நரைதிரை, grey hair and wrinkles, is the standard pair for old age.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["siththi","yaakkai","ilamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:648":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":648,"t":["ஈரைந்திற் பூரித்துத் தியான உருத்திரன்","ஏர்வொன்று பன்னொன்றில் ஈராறாம் எண்சித்தி","சீரொன்று மேலேழ் கீழேழ் புவிச்சென்று","ஏருன்று வியாபியாய் நிற்றல்ஈ ராறே"],"p":"","r":"In the tenth one is filled and becomes Rudra in meditation; in the eleventh the eight siddhis; and going through the seven worlds above and seven below, standing all-pervading, comes in the twelfth.","n":"The first word of lines 2 to 4 varies across copies (ஏர்வொன்று, நேரொன்று, ஓரொன்று), and the line division of years is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["siththi","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:649":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":649,"t":["தானே அணுவுஞ் சகத்துத்தன் நொய்ம்மையும்","மானாக் கனமும் பரகாயத் தேகமுந்","தானாவ தும்பர காயஞ்சேர் தன்மையும்","ஆனாத வுண்மையும் வியாபியு மாம்எட்டே"],"p":"","r":"The eight are: becoming atomic, lightness in the world, unyielding heaviness, the body entering another body, becoming whatever one wills, the nature of joining the supreme body, unfailing truth, and pervading all.","n":"The standard eight are anima, mahima, laghima, garima, prapti, prakamya, ishitva and vashitva. The verse's list does not map onto them cleanly, and variants (நோன்மையும், ககனமும்) change two items.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["siththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:650":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":650,"t":["தாங்கிய தன்மையுந் தானணுப் பல்லுயிர்","வாங்கிய காலத்து மற்றோர் குறையில்லை","யாங்கே எழுந்தோம் அவற்றுள் எழுந்துமிக்","கோங்கி வரமுத்தி முந்திய வாறே"],"p":"","r":"Holding all things, becoming atomic, drawing many lives in, there is no further lack; rising within those from which we rose and growing high is how the boon of release came first.","n":"The syntax is loose and the variant கோண்கிய வாமுத்தி is garbled. One reading is that the powers rest on release; another that release is reached through them.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["siththi","veedu","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:651":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":651,"t":["முந்திய முந்நூற் றறுபது காலமும்","வந்தது நாழிகை வான்முத லாயிடச்","சிந்தை செயச்செய மண்முதல் தேர்ந்தறிந்","துந்தியில் நின்று உதித்தெழு மாறே"],"p":"","r":"The former three hundred and sixty measures of time came as nazhigai beginning from space; as one meditates again and again, discerning from earth upward, it rises from the navel.","n":"முந்நூற்றறுபது (360) may count days, breaths or units of time; நாழிகை is a unit of twenty-four minutes. மண்முதல் (from earth) suggests meditating the elements in order, and உந்தி (navel) the seat from which the breath rises.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:652":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":652,"t":["சித்தந் திரிந்து சிவமய மாகியே","முத்தந் தெரிந்துற்ற மோனர் சிவமுத்தர்","சுத்தம் பெறலாம் ஐந்தில் தொடக்கற்றோர்","சித்தம் பரத்தில் திருநடத் தோரே"],"p":"","r":"Those whose mind turns and becomes full of Siva, who have discerned release and reached silence, are freed in Siva; free of the five bonds they gain purity, their mind in the Supreme, in the holy dance.","n":"சிவமுத்தர் (those freed in Siva) and மோனர் (the silent) name a state beyond the powers just listed. ஐந்தில் தொடக்கு, bond in the five, may be the five senses or a fivefold count of malas; the verse does not say.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","veedu","manam","paasam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:653":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":653,"t":["ஒத்தஇவ் வொன்பது வாயுவும் ஒத்தன","ஒத்தஇவ் வொன்பதின் மிக்க தனஞ்செயன்","ஒத்தஇவ் வொன்பதில் ஒக்க இருந்திட","ஒத்த வுடலும் உயிரும் இருந்ததே"],"p":"","r":"These nine vital airs are in accord; above them is dhananjaya; while it stays in accord within the nine, body and life stay together.","n":"The tradition counts ten airs (prana, apana, vyana, udana, samana, naga, kurma, krikara, devadatta, dhananjaya). The verse makes dhananjaya the tenth and the one on which body and life hold together.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["praanan","uyir","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:654":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":654,"t":["இருக்குந் தனஞ்செயன் ஒன்பது காலில்","இருக்கும் இருநூற் றிருபத்து மூன்றாய்","இருக்கு முடலி லிருந்தில வாகில்","இருக்கும் உடலது வீங்கி வெடித்ததே"],"p":"","r":"Dhananjaya stays in the nine airs, as two hundred and twenty-three; if it does not stay in the body, the body swells and bursts.","n":"இருநூற்றிருபத்து மூன்று (223) is not explained. Dhananjaya is traditionally the air that swells the corpse after death; here its failure in the living body causes swelling.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["praanan","yaakkai","noy"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:655":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":655,"t":["வீங்குங் கழலை சிரங்கொடு குட்டமும்","வீங்கும் வியாதிகள் சோகை பலவதாய்","வீங்கிய வாதமுங் கூனு முடமதாய்","வீங்கு வியாதிகள் கண்ணில் மருவியே"],"p":"","r":"Swellings, tumours, scabies and leprosy, many swelling diseases and anaemia, swollen wind-disease, hunchback and lameness, and diseases settling in the eye.","n":"The verse continues 654: these are the diseases of a disordered dhananjaya. வீங்கு (swell) opens every line.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["noy","praanan","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:656":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":656,"t":["கண்ணில் வியாதி யுரோகந் தனஞ்செயன்","கண்ணிலிவ் வாணிகள் காச மவனல்லன்","கண்ணினிற் கூர்மன் கலந்தில நாதலாற்","கண்ணினிற் சோதி கலந்ததும் இல்லையே"],"p":"","r":"Eye disease comes from dhananjaya, cough does not; because kurma is not mixed in the eye, the light is not mixed in the eye either.","n":"Kurma is traditionally the air that moves the eyelids. இவ்வாணிகள் is obscure, and the logic of the last two lines (no kurma, so no light) is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["praanan","noy"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:657":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":657,"t":["நாடியின் ஓசை நயனம் இருதயந்","தூடி யளவுஞ் சுடர்விடு சோதியைத்","தேவருள் ஈசன் திருமால் பிரமனும்","ஓவற நின்றங் குணர்ந்திருந் தாரே"],"p":"","r":"Isa among the gods, Tirumal and Brahma stood there without ceasing and knew the radiant light that reaches through the sound in the channel, the eye and the heart.","n":"நாடியின் ஓசை (the sound in the channel) and தூடி அளவு are obscure; தூடி may be a measure or a misreading. The point seems to be that even the gods reached this inner light by stillness.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naadi","naadham","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:658":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":658,"t":["ஒன்பது வாசல் உடையதோர் பிண்டத்துள்","ஒன்பது நாடி யுடையதோ ரோரிடம்","ஒன்பது நாடி ஒடுங்கவல் லார்கட்(கு)","ஒன்பது காட்சி யிலைபல வாமே"],"p":"","r":"In the body with nine gates is one place with nine channels; for those who can still the nine channels, the nine visions ... become many.","n":"The nine gates are the body's openings. The last line, ஒன்பது காட்சி இலை பல ஆமே, can be read 'the nine visions are not; many come', and a variant has உலைநலம் ஆமே. I can't fix it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naadi","yaakkai","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:659":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":659,"t":["ஓங்-கிய அங்கிக்கீழ் ஒண்சுழு னைச்செல்ல","வாங்கி இரவி மதிவழி ஓடிடத்","தாங்கி உலகங்கள் ஏழுந் தரித்திட","ஆங்கது சொன்னோம் அருவழி யோர்க்கே"],"p":"","r":"When the bright sushumna runs below the rising fire, drawing in, and the sun and moon run their way, holding up and bearing the seven worlds: this we told to those on the rare path.","n":"இரவி மதி (sun and moon) are taken in yoga as the right and left channels, pingala and ida; the fire below as the fire at the root where kundalini lies. The seven worlds may be the body's centres.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naadi","praanan","yogam","kundalini"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:660":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":660,"t":["தலைப்பட்ட வாறண்ணல் தையலை நாடி","வலைப்பட்ட பாசத்து வன்பிணை மான்போல்","துலைப்பட்ட நாடியைத் தூவழி செய்தால்","விலைக்குண்ண வைத்ததோர் வித்தது வாமே"],"p":"","r":"As the Lord, meeting her, sought the Lady, like a doe caught in a net of bonds, if one makes the balanced channel a pure path it becomes a seed set aside to be sold.","n":"துலைப்பட்ட நாடி, the channel that is balanced like a scale, is taken as the central channel between ida and pingala. The doe in the net and the seed kept for sale are images I can't place with confidence.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naadi","saththi","paasam","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:661":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":661,"t":["ஓடிச்சென் றங்கே ஒருபொருள் கண்டவர்","நாடியி னுள்ளாக நாதம் எழுப்புவர்","தேடிச்சென் றங்கே தேனை முகந்துண்டு","பாடியுள் நின்ற பகைவரைக் கட்டுமே"],"p":"","r":"Those who ran there and saw the one thing raise nada within the channel; seeking there, they scoop up honey and drink it, and bind the enemies who stood in the camp.","n":"பாடி (camp) is taken as the body, the enemies as the senses or passions, and the honey as the inner nectar. The plain image is of a raid that ends in a feast and a binding.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naadham","naadi","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:662":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":662,"t":["கட்டிட்ட தாமரை ஞாளத்தில் ஒன்பது","மட்டிட்ட கன்னியர் மாதுடன் சேர்ந்தனர்","கட்டிட்டு நின்று களங்கனி யூடுபோய்ப்","பொட்டிட்டு நின்று பூரண மானதே"],"p":"","r":"In the stalk of the bound lotus nine adorned maidens joined the Lady; standing bound, passing through the dark fruit and standing at the brow-mark, it became full.","n":"The lotus stalk is taken as the central channel and the nine maidens as nine shaktis. களங்கனி (the dark kalam fruit) and பொட்டு (the brow-mark) may be centres at the throat or palate and the brow. The variants in line 3 are garbled.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","naadi","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:663":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":663,"t":["பூரண சத்தி ஏழுமூன் றறையாக","ஏரணி கன்னியர் எழுநூற்றஞ் சாக்கினார்","நாரணன் நான்முக னாதிய ஐவர்க்குங்","காரண மாகிக் கலந்து விரிந்ததே"],"p":"","r":"The full Shakti, divided as seven and three, and the beautiful maidens who made seven hundred and five, became the cause of the five beginning with Narayana and Brahma, and mingled and spread.","n":"The five gods are traditionally the agents of the five acts. The numbers (ஏழுமூன்று, எழுநூற்றஞ்சு) are not explained.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","aindhozhil"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:664":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":664,"t":["விரிந்து குவிந்து விளைந்தஇம் மங்கை","கரந்துள் எழுந்து கரந்தங் கிருக்கிற்","பரந்து குவிந்தது பார்முதற் பூதம்","இரைந்தெழு வாயு விடத்தினில் ஒடுங்கே"],"p":"","r":"If this Lady, who expanded, contracted and ripened, rises hidden within and stays hidden there, the elements from earth that spread out are drawn in, and the roaring breath subsides in its place.","n":"The Lady (மங்கை) may be Shakti as kundalini. விரிந்து குவிந்து (expanding and contracting) suits both the cosmos and the breath.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","praanan","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:665":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":665,"t":["இடையொடு பிங்கலை என்னும் இரண்டு","மடைபடு வாயுவு மாறியே நிற்குந்","தடையவை யாறேழுந் தண்சுட ருள்ளே","மிடைவளர் மின்கொடி தன்னில் ஒடுங்கே"],"p":"","r":"The two airs called ida and pingala, flowing through their channels, stand reversed; their hindrances, six and seven, subside in the lightning creeper that grows in the cool light.","n":"மின்கொடி, the lightning creeper, is a common figure for kundalini or the central channel. ஆறேழு may be six and seven, or forty-two; a variant reads ஆறு எழும், the six that rise.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naadi","praanan","kundalini"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:666":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":666,"t":["ஒடுங்கி ஒருங்கி யுணர்ந்தங் கிருக்கில்","மடங்கி அடங்கிடும் வாயு வதனுள்","மடங்கி மடங்கிடு மன்னுயி ருள்ளே","நடங்கொண்ட கூத்தனும் நாடுகின் றானே"],"p":"","r":"If one stills and gathers oneself and stays aware there, the breath folds and subsides within; folding within the abiding life, the dancer who took up the dance seeks him.","n":"In the text the dancer (Siva) seeks the stilled one; the variant கூத்தனை நாடுகின்றேனே reverses this to 'I seek the dancer'. The reversal changes who moves first.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["praanan","adakkam","sivam","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:667":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":667,"t":["நாடியின் உள்ளே நாதத் தொனியுடன்","தேடி யுடன்சென்றத் திருவினைக் கைக்கொண்டு","பாடியுள் நின்ற பகைவரைக் கட்டிட்டு","மாடி ஒருகை மணிவிளக் கானதே"],"p":"","r":"Seeking within the channel with the sound of nada, going and taking hold of that sacred one, binding the enemies in the camp, it became a jewelled lamp in one hand.","n":"Line 3 repeats 661. திரு (the sacred, Shri) may be Shakti. மாடி ஒருகை is obscure; the lamp in the hand suggests a light that is now one's own.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naadham","naadi","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:668":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":668,"t":["அணுமாதி சித்திக ளானவை கூறில்","அணுவில் அணுவின் பெருமையில் நேர்மை","இணுகாத வேகார் பரகாய மேவல்","அணுவத் தனையெங்குந் தானாத லென்றெட்டே"],"p":"","r":"To name the siddhis beginning with anima: being an atom within the atom, greatness, lightness, unbending weight, entering another body, and being everywhere though an atom: these are the eight.","n":"This list is closer to the standard eight than 649's, but the mapping of each phrase is still uncertain: நேர்மை and இணுகாத ஏகார் are hard to fix.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["siththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:669":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":669,"t":["எட்டா கியசித்தி யோரெட்டி யோகத்தாற்","கிட்டாப் பிராணனே செய்தாற் கிடைத்திடும்","ஒட்டா நடுநாடி மூலத்த னல்பானு","விட்டான் மதியுண்ண வும்வரு மேலதே"],"p":"","r":"The eight siddhis come through eightfold yoga, by mastering the prana that is hard to master; when the fire at the root of the central channel drives the sun and lets the moon drink, the higher ones come too.","n":"எட்டி யோகம, eightfold yoga, is the limbs of yoga from restraints to samadhi. The fire, sun and moon are the usual yogic figures for the root fire and the right and left channels.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["siththi","yogam","praanan","naadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:670":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":670,"t":["சித்திக ளெட்டன்றிச் சேரெட்டி யோகத்தாற்","புத்திக ளானவை எல்லாம் புலப்படுஞ்","சித்திகள் எண்சித்தி தானாந் திரிபுரை","சத்தி அருள்தரத் தானுள வாகுமே"],"p":"","r":"Through eightfold yoga, beyond the eight siddhis, all kinds of understanding become clear; the siddhis, the eight, come into being when Shakti Tripurai grants grace.","n":"The verse puts the siddhis under Shakti's grace, not under the practice alone, and places understanding (புத்தி) above them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["siththi","yogam","saththi","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:671":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":671,"t":["எட்டிவை தன்னோ டெழிற்பரங் கைகூடப்","பட்டவர் சித்தர் பரலோகஞ் சேர்தலால்","இட்டம துள்ளே இறுக்கல் பரகாட்சி","எட்டு வரப்பு மிடந்தானின் றெட்டுமே"],"p":"","r":"Those who have gained these eight with the beautiful Supreme are siddhas, since they reach the supreme world; holding firm within what they love is the supreme vision, and the eight boundaries reach to where he stands.","n":"சித்தர் is defined here by reaching the supreme world, not by the powers alone. The last line is obscure; the variant இருக்கல் (staying) for இறுக்கல் (holding tight) changes little.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["siththi","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:672":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":672,"t":["மந்தர மேறு மதிபானு வைமாற்றிக்","கந்தாய்க் குழியிற் கசடற வல்லார்க்குத்","தந்தின்றி நற் கா மியலோகஞ் சார்வாகும்","அந்த வுலகம் அணிமாதி யாமே"],"p":"","r":"For those who can reverse the moon and sun that climb the mountain and be free of dross in the pit like a bulb, the good world of desire is reached without effort; that world is anima and the rest.","n":"மந்தரம் (the mountain) may be the spine; குழி (the pit) the root centre; கந்து, a bulb or post. The variant காய மிய for கா மிய changes 'world of desire' to something about the body.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["siththi","yogam","naadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:673":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":673,"t":["முடிந்திட்டு வைத்து முயங்கிலோ ராண்டில்","அணிந்த அணிமாகை தானாம் இவனுந்","தணிந்தவப் பஞ்சினுந் தானொய்ய தாகி","மெலிந்தங் கிருந்திடும் வெல்லவொண் ணாதே"],"p":"","r":"Binding it and practising for a year, he becomes the anima he puts on; lighter than carded cotton, he stays thin and cannot be overcome.","n":"The lightness of cotton suits laghima more than anima. முடிந்திட்டு வைத்து (binding and setting) is taken as holding the breath.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["siththi","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:674":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":674,"t":["ஆகின்ற வத்தனி நாயகி தன்னுடன்","போகின்ற தத்துவம் எங்கும் புகல தாய்ச்","சாகின்ற காலங்கள் தன்வழி நின்றிடின்","மாய்கின்ற தையாண்டின் மாலகு வாகுமே"],"p":"","r":"With that singular Lady who arises, the tattvas that go everywhere become a refuge; if the times of dying stay in one's own control, in five years comes great lightness.","n":"மாலகு is taken as great lightness (laghima). மாய்கின்றது ஐயாண்டின் might also mean 'what dies in five years'. The formula 'with the Lady' recurs through 694 to 702.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["siththi","saththi","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:675":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":675,"t":["மாலகு வாகிய மாயனைக் கண்டபின்","தானொளி யாகித் தழைத்தங் கிருந்திடும்","பாலொளி யாகிப் பரந்தெங்கு நின்றது","மேலொளி யாகிய மெய்ப்பொருள் காணுமே"],"p":"","r":"After seeing the wondrous one who has become great lightness, he stays flourishing as light; it stood spread everywhere as milk-white light, and he sees the true thing that is the higher light.","n":"மாயன் can mean Vishnu, the wondrous one, or the illusory; who becomes light (the yogi or the seen) is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["siththi","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:676":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":676,"t":["மெய்ப்பொருள் சொல்லிய மெல்லிய லாளுடன்","தற்பொரு ளாகிய தத்துவங் கூடிடக்","கைப்பொரு ளாகக் கலந்திடு மோராண்டின்","மைப்பொரு ளாகு மகிமாவ தாகுமே"],"p":"","r":"When one's own tattva joins the gentle Lady who spoke the truth and mingles with it like a thing held in the hand, in a year one becomes the dark thing, mahima.","n":"மகிமா (greatness) is one of the eight. மைப்பொருள் (the dark or black thing) is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["siththi","saththi","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:677":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":677,"t":["ஆகின்ற காலொளி யாவது கண்டபின்","போகின்ற காலங்கள் போவது மில்லையாம்","மேனின்ற காலம் வெளியுற நின்றன","தானின்ற காலங்கள் தன்வழி யாகுமே"],"p":"","r":"After seeing the light of the breath that rises, the passing times no longer pass; the time above stood open in space, and the times that remain go one's own way.","n":"கால் means both breath and time, and the verse plays on the two: mastering the breath is mastering time. 680 repeats much of it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["praanan","siththi","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:678":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":678,"t":["தன்வழி யாகத் தழைத்திடு ஞ்aனமுந்","தன்வழி யாகத் தழைத்திடும் வையகந்","தன்வழி யாகத் தழைத்த பொருளெல்லாந்","தன்வழி தன்னரு ளாகிநின் றானே"],"p":"","r":"Knowledge flourishes as his way, the world flourishes as his way, all things flourish as his way; he stands as his own way, as his own grace.","n":"தான் may be the Lord or the yogi who has gained the powers; the second fits the chapter, the first the closing words தன்னருள். Line 1 is garbled in the source (ஞ்aனமும், read as ஞானமும்).","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["gnaanam","thiruvarul","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:679":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":679,"t":["நின்றன தத்துவ நாயகி தன்னுடன்","கண்டன பூதப் படையவை யெல்லாங்","கொண்டவை யோராண்டு கூட இருந்திடில்","விண்டது வேநல்ல பிராத்தி யதாகுமே"],"p":"","r":"If one stays a year with the Lady of the tattvas, holding all the hosts of elements one has seen, what opens is good prapti.","n":"பிராத்தி is prapti, the power of reaching anything. The formula of a year with a Lady recurs in 682 and 684.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["siththi","saththi","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:680":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":680,"t":["ஆகின்ற மின்னொளி யாவது கண்டபின்","பாகின்ற பூவிற் பரப்பவை காணலா","மேகின்ற காலம் வெளியுற நின்றது","போகின்ற காலங்கள் போவது மில்லையே"],"p":"","r":"After seeing the light of lightning that rises, one can see the spreading worlds in the ripening flower; the moving time stood open in space, and the passing times do not pass.","n":"Lines 3 and 4 repeat 677. The flower (பூ) may be a lotus centre or the earth.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["siththi","yogam","praanan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:681":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":681,"t":["போவதொன் றில்லை வருவது தானில்லை","சாவதொன் றில்லை தழைப்பது தானில்லை","தாமத மில்லை தமரகத் தின்னொளி","யாவது மில்லை யறிந்துகொள் வார்க்கே"],"p":"","r":"For those who know, there is nothing that goes, nothing that comes, nothing that dies, nothing that grows, no delay, and no light at all in the inner cave.","n":"தமரகம் is the small inner cavity, traditionally of the heart or head. After the verses on seeing lights, this one denies even light: the knower's state is beyond change, and beyond the visions too.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["meyyunarvu","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:682":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":682,"t":["அறிந்த பராசத்தி யுள்ளே அமரில்","பறிந்தது பூதப் படையவை யெல்லாங்","குவிந்தவை யோராண்டு கூட இருக்கில்","விரிந்தது பரகாய மேவலு மாமே"],"p":"","r":"If one dwells within the known Parashakti, the hosts of elements fall away; if one stays a year with them gathered, entering another's body becomes possible.","n":"The formula follows 679 and 684. பரகாயம் மேவல் recurs from 643.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["siththi","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:683":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":683,"t":["ஆன விளக்கொளி யாவ தறிகிலர்","மூல விளக்கொளி முன்னே யுடையவர்","கான விளக்கொளி கண்டுகொள் வார்கட்கு","மேலை விளக்கொளி வீடெளி தாநின்றே"],"p":"","r":"People do not know what the lamp's light is; for those who already have the light of the root lamp and see the light of the lamp that is seen, the light of the higher lamp makes release easy.","n":"The four lamps (ஆன, மூல, கான, மேலை) may be stages of an inner light rising from the root centre to the crown, as tradition takes them. கான may be 'seen' or 'of the forest'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["veedu","yogam","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:684":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":684,"t":["நின்ற சதாசிவ நாயகி தன்னுடன்","கண்டன பூதப் படையவை எல்லாங்","கொண்டவை யோராண்டு கூடி யிருந்திடிற்","பண்டையவ் வீசன் தத்துவ மாகுமே"],"p":"","r":"If one stays a year with the Lady of Sadasiva, gathering all the hosts of elements one has seen, one becomes the ancient Lord's tattva.","n":"ஈசன் தத்துவம், the Lord's tattva, is taken as ishitva, lordship, one of the eight.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["siththi","saththi","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:685":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":685,"t":["ஆகின்ற சந்திரன் தன்னொளி யாயவன்","ஆகின்ற சந்திரன் தட்பமு மாயிடும்","ஆகின்ற சந்திரன் தன்கலை கூடிடில்","ஆகின்ற சந்திரன் தானவ நாமே"],"p":"","r":"He becomes the light of the rising moon and its coolness; if the moon's kala joins, he becomes the moon itself.","n":"The moon is a yogic figure for the cool nectar in the head. ஆகின்ற சந்திரன் opens every line. A variant reads தண்ணளி, cool grace.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["siththi","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:686":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":686,"t":["தானே படைத்திட வல்லவ னாயிடுந்","தானே யளித்திட வல்லவ னாயிடுந்","தானே சங்காரத் தலைவனு மாயிடுந்","தானே யிவனெனுந் தன்மைய னாமே"],"p":"","r":"He becomes able to create, able to protect, and lord of destruction; he becomes one of whom it is said, he is himself the one.","n":"In context the subject is the yogi who has gained ishitva, taking on the Lord's acts. The verse names three acts, not the five, and does not say whether the yogi becomes the Lord or only acts like him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aindhozhil","siththi","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:687":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":687,"t":["தன்மைய தாகத் தழைத்த கலையினுள்","பன்மைய தாகப் பரந்தஐம் பூதத்தை","வன்மைய தாக மறித்திடில் ஓராண்டின்","மென்மைய தாகிய மெய்பொருள் காணுமே"],"p":"","r":"If, within the kala that flourished in its nature, one firmly turns back the five elements spread out in many forms, in a year one sees the subtle true thing.","n":"மறித்திடில் (turning back) suggests withdrawing the elements into their source. The verse plays on தன்மை, பன்மை, வன்மை, மென்மை.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","yogam","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:688":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":688,"t":["மெய்ப்பொரு ளாக விளைந்தது வேதெனின்","நற்பொரு ளாகிய நல்ல வசித்துவங்","கைப்பொரு ளாகக் கலந்த உயிர்க்கெல்லாந்","தற்பொரு ளாகிய தன்மைய னாகுமே"],"p":"","r":"If you ask what ripened as the true thing, it is the good vashitva; he becomes the very self of all lives, mingled with them like a thing in hand.","n":"வசித்துவம் is vashitva, the power of bringing beings under one's will. The verse turns it into being the self of all lives, a gentler sense than control.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["siththi","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:689":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":689,"t":["தன்மைய தாகத் தழைத்த பகலவன்","மென்மைய தாகிய மெய்ப்பொருள் கண்டிடின்","பொன்மைய தாகப் புலன்களும் போயிட","நன்மைய தாகிய நற்கொடி காணுமே"],"p":"","r":"If the sun that flourished in its nature sees the subtle true thing, and the senses become golden and go, one sees the good creeper.","n":"The creeper (கொடி) is a figure for Shakti or kundalini, and the sun for the right channel. பொன்மையதாக (becoming golden) is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["siththi","saththi","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:690":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":690,"t":["நற்கொடி யாகிய நாயகி தன்னுடன்","அக்கொடி யாகம் அறிந்திடில் ஓராண்டு","பொற்கொடி யாகிய புவனங்கள் போய்வருங்","கற்கொடி யாகிய காமுக னாமே"],"p":"","r":"If one knows the body of that creeper with the Lady who is the good creeper, in a year one goes to the golden worlds and returns, and becomes the lover of the stone creeper.","n":"காமுகன் (lover, one who desires) may point to the power of having one's desires fulfilled (prakamya). கற்கொடி, the stone creeper, is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["siththi","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:691":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":691,"t":["காமரு தத்துவ மானது வந்தபின்","பூமரு கந்தம் புவனம தாயிடும்","மாமரு வுன்னிடை மெய்த்திடு மானனாய்","நாமரு வுமஒளி நாயக மானதே"],"p":"","r":"After the lovely tattva comes, the fragrance of the flower becomes the world; ... the light where the tongue dwells becomes lordship.","n":"Line 3 is obscure, and a variant reads உன்னிடம் எய்திடு, 'reaching your place'. நா (the tongue) may refer to the tongue turned back in khechari.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["siththi","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:692":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":692,"t":["நாயக மாகிய நல்லொளி கண்டபின்","தாயக மாகத் தழைத்தங் கிருந்திடும்","போயக மான புவனங்கள் கண்டபின்","பேயக மாகிய பேரொளி காணுமே"],"p":"","r":"After seeing the good light that is lordship, it stays flourishing as a mother's home; after seeing the worlds that went within, one sees the great light.","n":"பேயகம் is obscure, perhaps the cremation ground where Siva dances, or the great void.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["siththi","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:693":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":693,"t":["பேரொளி யாகிய பெரியஅவ் வேட்டையும்","பாரொளி யாகப் பதைப்புறக் கண்டவன்","தாரொளி யாகத் தரணி முழுதுமாம்","ஓரொளி யாகிய காலொளி காணுமே"],"p":"","r":"He who has seen with trembling that great ... which is the great light, as the light of the earth, sees the light of the breath, the one light that is the whole earth as a garland of light.","n":"வேட்டை (hunt) does not fit; the variant வெட்டை (open space, void) fits better. காலொளி, the breath's light, recurs from 677.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["praanan","siththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:694":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":694,"t":["காலோ டுயிருங் கலக்கும் வகைசொல்லிற்","காலது அக்கொடி நாயகி தன்னுடன்","காலது ஐஞ்நூற் றொருபத்து மூன்றையங்","காலது வேண்டிக் கொண்டஇவ் வாறே"],"p":"","r":"To tell how breath and life mingle: the breath is with that creeper Lady, and it is wanted in five hundred and thirteen, in this way.","n":"694 to 703 give breath counts for a series of Ladies or kalas. The numbers are not explained, and a variant in line 4 (பெண்மண்டி) is garbled.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["praanan","uyir","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:695":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":695,"t":["ஆறது வாகும் அமிர்தத் தலையினுள்","ஆறது ஆயிர முந்நூற் றொடைஞ்சுள","ஆறது வாயிர மாகு மருவழி","ஆறது வாக வளர்ப்ப திரண்டே"],"p":"","r":"Six is in the head of nectar; six is a thousand three hundred and five; six becomes a thousand on the rare path; what grows as six is two.","n":"ஆறு means both six and path, and the verse may play on both. The counts are obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["praanan","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:696":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":696,"t":["இரண்டினின் மேலே சதாசிவ நாயகி","இரண்டது கால்கொண் டெழுவகை சொல்லில்","இரண்டது ஆயிரம் ஐம்பதோ டொன்றாய்த்","திரண்டது காலம் எடுத்ததும் அஞ்சே"],"p":"","r":"Above the two is the Lady of Sadasiva; to tell how the two rise taking breath, two thousand and fifty-one gathered, and the time taken is five.","n":"The numbers are not explained; கால் is again both breath and time.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["praanan","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:697":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":697,"t":["அஞ்சுடன் அஞ்சு முகமுள நாயகி","அஞ்சுடன் அஞ்சது வாயுத மாவது","அஞ்சது வன்றி இரண்டது வாயிரம்","அஞ்சது காலம் எடுத்துளும் ஒன்றே"],"p":"","r":"The Lady with five and five faces: five with five is ayuta; besides that five, two thousand; the time taken is one.","n":"அஞ்சுடன் அஞ்சு முகம் (five and five faces) may be the ten faces of a goddess. ஆயுதம் may be ayuta (ten thousand) or 'weapon'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["praanan","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:698":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":698,"t":["ஒன்றது வாகிய தத்துவ நாயகி","ஒன்றது கால்கொண் டூர்வகை சொல்லிடில்","ஒன்றது வென்றிகொள் ஆயிரம் ஆயிரம்","ஒன்றது காலம் எடுத்துளும் முன்னே"],"p":"","r":"The Lady of the tattva that is one: to tell how the one moves taking breath, the one wins thousands upon thousands, and the time taken is before.","n":"ஒன்று opens every line; the counts are obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["praanan","saththi","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:699":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":699,"t":["முன்னெழும் அக்கலை நாயகி தன்னுடன்","முன்னுறு வாயு முடிவகை சொல்லிடின்","முன்னுறும் ஐம்பத் தொன்றுடன் அஞ்சுமாய்","முன்னுறு வாயு முடிவகை யாமே"],"p":"","r":"With the Lady of that kala who rises first, the measure where the breath ends is fifty-one with five.","n":"Fifty-one may be the letters of the Sanskrit alphabet, which mantra theory links to the kalas; the verse doesn't say so.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["praanan","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:700":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":700,"t":["ஆய்வரும் அத்தனி நாயகி தன்னுடன்","ஆய்வரு வாயு அளப்பது சொல்லிடில்","ஆய்வரும் ஐஞ்நூற்று முப்பதொ டொன்பது","மாய்வரு வாயு வளப்புள் ளிருந்ததே"],"p":"","r":"With that singular Lady hard to fathom, the measure of breath is five hundred and thirty-nine, and the breath stayed within its measure.","n":"ஆய்வரும் (hard to examine) opens each line. மாய்வரு வாயு may be 'the breath that ends' or 'the wondrous breath'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["praanan","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:701":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":701,"t":["இருநிதி யாகிய எந்தை யிடத்து","இருநிதி வாயு இயங்கு நெறியில்","இருநூற்று முப்பத்து மூன்றுடன் அஞ்சாய்","இருநிதி வாயு இயங்கும் எழுத்தே"],"p":"","r":"In the Father who is the twofold treasure, the way the breath moves is two hundred and thirty-three with five: these are the letters in which the breath moves.","n":"இருநிதி (twofold treasure) is obscure; எழுத்து (letter) suggests the counts are of syllables in a mantra or breaths matched to letters.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["praanan","iraivan","mandhiram"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:702":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":702,"t":["எழுகின்ற சோதியுள் நாயகி தன்பால்","எழுகின்ற வாயு இடமது சொல்லில்","எழுநூற் றிருபத் தொன்பா னதுநாலாய்","எழுந்துடன் அங்கி இருந்ததிவ் வாறே"],"p":"","r":"With the Lady in the rising light, the place of the breath is seven hundred and twenty-nine and four, and the fire rose and stayed with it in this way.","n":"The fire (அங்கி) may be the root fire of 659 and 669. The counts are obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["praanan","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:703":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":703,"t":["ஆறது கால்கொண் டிரதம் விளைத்திடும்","ஏழது கால்கொண் டிரட்டி இறக்கிட","எட்டது கால்கொண் டிடவகை யொத்தபின்","ஒன்பது மாநிலம் ஒத்தது வாயுவே"],"p":"","r":"Taking six breaths it produces essence; taking seven it doubles and brings it down; taking eight the places come into accord; and with nine the breath is even across the great ground.","n":"இரதம் may be rasa, the bodily essence or nectar. The verse reads like a practice of breath counts, but its terms can't be fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["praanan","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:704":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":704,"t":["சந்திரன் சூரியன் தற்பரன் தாணுவிற்","சந்திரன் தானுந் தலைப்படுந் தன்மையைச்","சந்தியி லேகண்டு தானாஞ் சகமுகத்","துந்திச் சமாதி யுடையொளி யோகியே"],"p":"","r":"The yogi of the light of samadhi sees at the juncture how the moon meets sun, Supreme and Sthanu, and becomes one with the world.","n":"சந்தி (juncture) may be the meeting of the channels or the twilight hour. தாணு (the pillar) is a name of Siva. சகமுகத்து உந்தி is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["samaadhi","yogam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:705":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":705,"t":["அணங்கற்ற மாதல் அருஞ்சன நீவல்","வணங்குற்ற கல்விமா ஞான மிகுத்தல்","சுணங்குற்ற வாயர் சித்திதூரங் கேட்டல்","நுணங்கற் றிரோதல்கால் வேகத்து நுந்தலே"],"p":"","r":"A list of powers: freedom from afflicting spirits, removing poison, learning that is revered and great wisdom, ... hearing from afar, becoming invisible, and moving with the speed of wind.","n":"அணங்கு is an afflicting spirit or goddess; அருஞ்சனம் may be poison or a rare collyrium. Line 3 is garbled in a variant. The list repeats much of 646.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["siththi","kalvi","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:706":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":706,"t":["மரணஞ் சரைவிடல் வண்பர காயம்","இரணஞ் சேர்பூமி இறந்தோர்க் களித்தல்","அரணன் திருவுற வாதன்மூ வேழாங்","கரனுறு கேள்வி கணக்கறிந் தோனே"],"p":"","r":"Escaping death and old age, entering another's body, giving the earth of battle to the dead, and gaining the form of Hara: one who has learned the count knows these as three sevens.","n":"மூவேழ் (three sevens, twenty-one) closes the list begun in 705. சரைவிடல் (leaving old age) has a variant சிறைவிடல் (release from prison). Line 2 is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["siththi","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:707":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":707,"t":["ஓதம் ஒலிக்கும் உலகை வலம்வந்து","பாதங்கள் நோவ நடந்தும் பயனில்லை","காதலில் அண்ணலைக் காண இனியவர்","நாதன் இருந்த நகரறி வாரே."],"p":"","r":"Circling the world where the sea roars and walking till the feet ache is of no use; those sweet ones who see the Lord in love know the city where the Lord dwells.","n":"The verse sets love (காதல்) against pilgrimage. The city (நகர்) is not named; tradition reads it as the body or the heart. Placed in a chapter on powers, it may also say that travel to the worlds, a siddhi, is not the point.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","vazhipaadu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:708":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":708,"t":["மூல முதல்வேதா மாலரன் முன்னிற்கக்","கோலிய ஐம்முகன் கூறப் பரவிந்து","சாலப் பரநாதம் விந்துத் தனிநாதம்","பாலித்த சத்தி பரைபரன் பாதமே"],"p":"","r":"With the root, the first one, Brahma, Vishnu and Hara before him, as the five-faced one declares: supreme bindu, ample supreme nada, bindu, the single nada, the sheltering Shakti and Parai are the feet of the Supreme.","n":"The five-faced one is Sadasiva. The list of bindu, nada, Shakti and Parai follows the stages of Saiva cosmology, as in chapter 140; how they divide is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vindhu","naadham","saththi","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:709":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":709,"t":["ஆதார யோகத் ததிதே வொடுஞ்சென்று","மீதான தற்பரை மேவும் பரனொடு","மேதாதி யீரெண் கலைசெல்ல மீதொளி","ஓதா அசிந்தமீ தானந்த யோகமே"],"p":"","r":"Going with the deity of adhara yoga, with Parai above, joining the Supreme, as the sixteen kalas from medha rise, the light above, unspoken and unthinkable, is the yoga of bliss.","n":"ஆதார யோகம் is yoga through the body's centres. மேதாதி ஈரெண் கலை, the sixteen kalas beginning with medha, are a mantra-theory series. ஆனந்த யோகம், the yoga of bliss, is the chapter's goal beyond the powers.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","inbam","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:710":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":710,"t":["மதியமும் ஞாயிறும் வந்துடன் கூடித்","துதிசெய் பவரவர் தொல்வா னவர்கள்","விதியது செய்கின்ற மெய்யடி யார்க்குப்","பதியது காட்டும் பரமன்நின் றானே"],"p":"","r":"When moon and sun come and join, the ancient gods praise; to the true devotees who do what is ordained, the Supreme stands and shows the Lord.","n":"பதி may be the Lord, the Pati of Siddhanta, or the place (abode). Moon and sun meeting is again the joining of the side channels.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pathi","vazhipaadu","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:711":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":711,"t":["பொட்டெழக் குத்திப் பொறியெழத் தண்டிட்டு","நட்டறி வார்க்கு நமனில்லை தானே","கட்டவல் லார்கள் கரந்தெங்குந் தானாவர்","மட்டவிழ் தாமரை யுள்ளே மணஞ்செய்து"],"p":"","r":"Those who pierce till the brow-spot rises, plant the staff till sparks rise, and know, have no Yama; those who can bind become hidden and are everywhere, wedded within the honey-opening lotus.","n":"பொட்டு (brow-spot), தண்டு (staff, taken as the spine) and பொறி (sparks) are yogic figures. நமனில்லை (no Yama) can mean freedom from death or from death's power over the soul, and the two readings differ greatly.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","saakkaadu","siththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:69"},"t:712":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":712,"t":["காதல் வழிசெய்த கண்ணுதல் அண்ணலைக்","காதல் வழிசெய்து கண்ணுற நோக்கிடிற்","காதல் வழிசெய்து கங்கை வழிதருங்","காதல் வழிசெய்து காக்கலு மாமே"],"p":"","r":"If one makes love the way and looks with the eye at the forehead-eyed Lord who made love the way, by love he opens the way of the Ganga, and by love one can guard oneself.","n":"காதல் வழிசெய்து is repeated in every line. கங்கை வழிதரும் is the obscure line: plainly the Lord who bears the Ganga, it is traditionally taken in yoga as a flow of nectar from the crown, or as a channel of breath. What is guarded (காக்கலும்) is not named; the next verse lists it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anbu","sivam","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:70"},"t:713":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":713,"t":["காக்கலு மாகுங் கரணங்கள் நான்கையுங்","காக்கலு மாகுங் கலைபதி நாறையுங்","காக்கலு மாகுங் கலந்தநல் வாயுவுங்","காக்கலு மாகுங் கருத்துற நில்லே"],"p":"","r":"One can guard the four inner instruments, the sixteen kalas and the good breath mingled within; one can guard them all if one stands with the mind fixed.","n":"கரணங்கள் நான்கு are traditionally manas, buddhi, chitta and ahankara. கலைபதி நாறையும் is read here as கலை பதினாறையும், the sixteen kalas (traditionally of the moon, matched with the breath); the printed word split hides this.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","praanan","kalai","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:70"},"t:714":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":714,"t":["நிலைபெற நின்றது நேர்தரு வாயு","சிலைபெற நின்றது தீபமும் ஒத்துக்","கலைவழி நின்ற கலப்பை அறியில்","அலைவற வாகும் வழியிது வாமே"],"p":"","r":"The breath that comes evenly stands steady, still like a lamp that does not flicker; if one knows the union that stands along the path of the kalas, this is the way to be free of wavering.","n":"சிலைபெற may mean 'firm as stone' or simply 'steady'. கலப்பு (union) is taken as the joining of the breaths or of breath and mind; the verse does not say which. The variant மலைவறி would give 'free of confusion' for அலைவற, a small change.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["praanan","kalai","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:70"},"t:715":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":715,"t":["புடையொன்றி நின்றிடும் பூதப் பிரானை","மடையொன்றி நின்றிட வாய்த்த வழியுஞ்","சடையொன்றி நின்றஅச் சங்கர நாதன்","விடையொன்றி லேறியே வீற்றிருந் தானே"],"p":"","r":"The Lord of the elements who stays close at one's side, and the way found by holding the breath as a sluice holds water: Sankara with matted hair sits enthroned upon the bull.","n":"மடையொன்றி (joined like a sluice-gate) is read as the breath held and released; the verse does not say breath. The last two lines are a plain image of Siva, and their link to the first two is left to the reader: perhaps the Lord who is reached by that way.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","praanan","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:70"},"t:716":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":716,"t":["இருக்கின்ற காலங்கள் ஏதும் அறியார்","பெருக்கின்ற காலப் பெருமையை நோக்கி","ஒருக்கின்ற வாயு வொளிப்பெற நிற்கத்","தருக்கொன்றி நின்றிடுஞ் சாதக னாமே"],"p":"","r":"People do not know how much time remains to them; one who looks to how that time can be lengthened and holds the gathered breath so that it shines stands firm, and he is the true practitioner.","n":"பெருக்கின்ற காலப் பெருமை is taken as the lengthening of life by breath control, a common claim of the yogic tantra. தருக்கு usually means pride; here it seems to mean firmness or strength.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["praanan","yogam","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:70"},"t:717":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":717,"t":["சாதக மானஅத் தன்மையை நோக்கியே","மாதவ மான வழிபாடு செய்திடும்","போதக மாகப் புகலுறப் பாய்ச்சினால்","வேதக மாக விளைந்து கிடக்குமே"],"p":"","r":"Seeing what that practice is, one performs worship that is great austerity; if it is directed as knowledge into the place of refuge, it ripens and lies there as a transmutation.","n":"வேதகம் is the alchemist's transmuting agent that turns base metal to gold; the image is traditionally of the body or soul transformed. போதகம் (instruction, knowledge) and புகலுற (into the refuge) are compressed and I can't fix what is being 'directed' (பாய்ச்சினால்), though breath is likely.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","vazhipaadu","siththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:70"},"t:718":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":718,"t":["கிடந்தது தானே கிளர்பயன் மூன்று","நடந்தது தானேஉள் நாடியுள் நோக்கிப்","படர்ந்தது தானே பங்கய மாகத்","தொடர்ந்தது தானேஅச் சோதியுள் நின்றே"],"p":"","r":"Of itself it lies there with its three rising fruits; of itself it moves, looking into the inner nadi; of itself it spreads out as a lotus; of itself it goes on, standing within that light.","n":"The subject of தானே is unnamed: it continues the 'transmutation' of 717, and may be the breath, the kundalini or the practice. The three fruits (பயன் மூன்று) are not named. The lotus (பங்கயம்) is traditionally a chakra and the light (சோதி) the vision at the crown.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naadi","yogam","siththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:70"},"t:719":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":719,"t":["தனே எழுந்தஅத் தத்துவ நாயகி","ஊனே வழிசெய்தெம் உள்ளே யிருந்திடும்","வானோர் உலகீன்ற அம்மை மதித்திடத்","தேனே பருகிச் சிவாலய மாகுமே"],"p":"","r":"The Lady of the tattvas, who rose of herself, makes her way through the flesh and dwells within us; when the Mother who bore the world of the gods regards us, the body drinks honey and becomes Siva's temple.","n":"The verse gives the body (ஊன்) a positive end: it becomes சிவாலயம் through Shakti, the claim 725 makes in the first person. தேனே பருகி (drinking honey) is traditionally the nectar of yoga; whether it is the body or the yogi that drinks is not stated.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","yaakkai","sivam","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:70"},"t:720":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":720,"t":["திகழும் படியே செறிதரு வாயு","அழியும் படியை அறிகில ராரும்","அழியும் படியை அறிந்தபின் நந்தி","திகழ்கின்ற வாயுவைச் சேர்தலு மாமே"],"p":"","r":"No one knows how the breath, which packs in so as to make one shine, is wasted away; once one knows how it is wasted, one can join with the breath in which Nandi shines.","n":"அழியும் படி is 'the way it perishes', of the breath and by implication of life. நந்தி is Siva (or Tirumular's guru); the verse can be read as joining the breath to Nandi or as reaching the breath in which he shines.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["praanan","yogam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:70"},"t:721":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":721,"t":["சோதனை தன்னில் துரிசறிக் காணலாம்","நாதனும் நாயகி தன்னிற் பிரியுநாள்","சாதன மாகுங் குருவை வழிபட்டு","மாதன மாக மதித்துக்கொள் ளீரே"],"p":"","r":"By testing you can see the flaw, the day when the Lord parts from the Lady; worship the guru, who is the means, and value him as great wealth.","n":"சோதனை is traditionally the testing of the breath to foretell the day of death, and the parting of நாதன் and நாயகி is taken as death, the separation of Siva and Shakti (or of life and body) in the person. The verse does not name the breath; the reading rests on the chapter. The variant gives சாதகமாகும் குருவழி பட்டு, 'following the guru's way that makes the practice', with much the same sense.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","saakkaadu","praanan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:70"},"t:722":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":722,"t":["ஈராறு கால்கொண் டெழுந்த புரவியைப்","பேராமற் கட்டிப் பெரிதுண்ண வல்லீரேல்","நீரா யிரமும் நிலமாயி ரத்தாண்டும்","பேராது காயம் பிரான்நந்தி ஆணையே"],"p":"","r":"If you can tie fast, so that it does not escape, the horse that rose with twelve legs, and drink deeply, the body will not leave for a thousand years of water and a thousand of earth; this is the Lord Nandi's command.","n":"The horse with twelve legs (ஈராறு கால்) is traditionally the breath, whose outward reach is reckoned as twelve finger-breadths; கால் means both leg and wind. நீர் ஆயிரமும் நிலம் ஆயிரத்து ஆண்டும் may be read as a huge span of years or as surviving through flood and the earth's ages; the sense is long life, the measure is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["praanan","yaakkai","siththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:70"},"t:723":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":723,"t":["ஓசையில் ஏழும் ஒளியிங்கண் ஐந்தும்","நாசியில் மூன்றும் நாவில் இரண்டுந்","தேசியுந் தேசனுந் தன்னிற் பிரியுநாள்","மாசறு சோதி வகுத்துவைத் தானே"],"p":"","r":"Seven in sound, five in sight here, three in the nose and two on the tongue: on the day the shining Lady and the shining Lord part, he has set out the stainless light.","n":"The numbers attached to hearing, sight, smell and taste are traditionally read as signs, counted in days, by which the failing senses foretell death; the verse names no days. தேசி and தேசன் (the radiant one, female and male) echo நாயகி and நாதன் in 721. The last line can be read as the Lord fixing the light the soul goes to, or fixing these signs.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saakkaadu","sivam","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:70"},"t:724":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":724,"t":["உடம்பார் அழியில் உயிரார் அழிவர்","திடம்பட மெய்ஞ்ஞானஞ் சேரவு மாட்டார்","உடம்பை வளர்க்கும் உபாயம் அறிந்தே","உடம்பை வளர்த்தேன் உயிர்வளர்த் தேனே"],"p":"","r":"If the body perishes, the life perishes, and one cannot firmly attain true knowledge; so, having learned the means of nurturing the body, I nurtured the body and with it nurtured life.","n":"The argument is practical: the body is kept because knowledge (மெய்ஞ்ஞானம்) needs time and a living frame to be won. உபாயம் is left unnamed here; the chapter supplies it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","uyir","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:71"},"t:725":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":725,"t":["உடம்பினை முன்னம் இழுக்கென் றிருந்தேன்","உடம்பினுக் குள்ளே யுறுபொருள் கண்டேன்","உடம்புளே உத்தமன் கோயில்கொண் டான் என்று","உடம்பினை யானிருந் தோம்புகின் றேனே"],"p":"","r":"I once held the body to be a thing of shame; then within the body I saw the true substance, and knowing that the Highest has made the body his temple, I now cherish it.","n":"The verse narrates a change of mind: இழுக்கு (a fault, something base) is the view Tirumular says he held first, the ascetic contempt for the body. உத்தமன் கோயில் கொண்டான் makes the body a temple, and ஓம்புதல் is the word for caring for something precious.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","sivam","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:71"},"t:726":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":726,"t":["சுழற்றிக் கொடுக்கவே சுத்திக் கழியுங்","கழற்றி மலத்தைக் கமலத்துப் பூரித்து","உழற்றிக் கொடுக்கும் உபாயம் அறிவார்க்கு","அழற்றித் தவிர்ந்துடல் அஞ்சன மாமே"],"p":"","r":"Turning the breath round cleanses and drives out; for those who know the means of loosening the impurity, filling the lotus and circulating it, the heat ceases and the body becomes like collyrium.","n":"Breath is not named; சுழற்றி (whirling) and கமலத்துப் பூரித்து (filling at the lotus) are traditionally taken as the inhalation (puraka) and circulation of breath. மலம் here may be bodily waste or the soul's impurity. அஞ்சனம் (black eye-salve) is unclear as an image: a dark, lustrous, unaging body is one reading, an invisible one (the salve of the magicians) another. The variant சுழித்து changes little.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["praanan","malam","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:71"},"t:727":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":727,"t":["அஞ்சனம் போன்றுட லையறு மந்தியில்","வஞ்சக வாத மறுமத்தி யானத்திற்","செஞ்சிறு காலையிற் செய்திடிற் பித்தறும்","நஞ்சறச் சொன்னோம் நரைதிரை நாசமே"],"p":"","r":"With the body like collyrium, practice in the evening removes phlegm, at midday the treacherous wind, and in the early morning bile; we have spoken so that the poison goes, and grey hair and wrinkles are destroyed.","n":"ஐ, வாதம் and பித்தம் are the three humours of Tamil medicine (kapha, vata, pitta), each matched to a time of day. What is to be done at those times (செய்திடில்) is not named; the chapter suggests breath practice. நரைதிரை (greying and wrinkling) are the marks of age the chapter promises to undo, as again in 734.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","noy","siththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:71"},"t:728":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":728,"t":["மூன்று மடக்குடைப் பாம்பிரண் டெட்டுள","வேன்ற வியந்திரம் பன்னிரண் டங்குலம்","நான்றவிம் முட்டை யிரண்டையங் கட்டியிட்டு","ஊன்றி யிருக்க உடம்பழி யாதே"],"p":"","r":"The serpents of three coils, the two and the eight, the yantra that holds, the twelve finger-breadths, and the two hanging eggs tied there: whoever stays fixed so will not lose the body.","n":"Almost every term is technical. The coiled serpent is traditionally kundalini, twelve finger-breadths the measure of the breath, and the two hanging eggs (முட்டை இரண்டு) are taken by some as the testes, tied in a yogic lock; பாம்பு இரண்டு எட்டு may be nadis or the eight-petalled lotus. I can't fix the diagram. The variant நான்றவிழ் (hanging loose) for நான்றவிம் may be the better reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kundalini","praanan","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:71"},"t:729":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":729,"t":["நூறும் அறுபதும் ஆறும் வலம்வர","நூறும் அறுபதும் ஆறும் இடம்வர","நூறும் அறுபதும் ஆறும் எதிரிட","நூறும் அறுபது மாறும் புகுவரே"],"p":"","r":"A hundred and sixty-six go round to the right, a hundred and sixty-six to the left, a hundred and sixty-six face each other, and a hundred and sixty-six enter.","n":"நூறும் அறுபதும் ஆறும் may be one number (166) or three counts (a hundred, sixty, six). The subject is unnamed; breaths through the right and left channels, meeting and entering the central channel, is the likely sense in this chapter, but it is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["praanan","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:71"},"t:730":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":730,"t":["சத்தியார் கோயி லிடம்வலஞ் சாதித்தான்","மத்தியா னத்திலே வாத்தியங் கேட்கலாந்","தித்தித்த கூத்துஞ் சிவனும் வெளிப்படுஞ்","சத்தியஞ் சொன்னோஞ் சதாநந்தி ஆணையே"],"p":"","r":"One who has mastered the left and right in Shakti's temple can hear music at midday; the sweet dance and Siva himself appear; we have spoken truth, by Sadananda's command.","n":"Shakti's temple is taken as the body, and left and right (இடம் வலம்) as the left and right breaths (ida and pingala). The music (வாத்தியம்) is traditionally the inner nada heard in yoga, and கூத்து Siva's dance. The variant வாக்கியம் (speech) for வாத்தியம் would change what is heard.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","naadham","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:71"},"t:731":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":731,"t":["திறத்திறம் விந்து திகழு மகார","முறப்பெற வேநினைந் தோதுஞ் சகார","மறிப்பது மந்திர மன்னிய நாத","மறப்பெற யோகிக் கறநெறி யாமே"],"p":"","r":"Bindu shining in its many forms as the letter ma, the letter sa recited in thought until it is attained, the mantra that turns back, and the abiding nada: when these are fully gained, they are the yogi's path of dharma.","n":"மகாரம் and சகாரம் are letters of a mantra, traditionally of the five-syllable mantra, and are matched here with bindu and nada; their roles are not explained. மறிப்பது (that which turns back or blocks) and the last line's division (நாதம் அறப்பெற or நாத மறப்பு) are uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","vindhu","naadham","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:71"},"t:732":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":732,"t":["உந்திச் சுழியி னுடனேர் பிராணனைச்","சிந்தித் தெழுப்பிச் சிவமந் திரத்தினால்","முந்தி முகட்டின் நிறுத்தி அபானனைச்","சிந்தித் தெழுப்பச் சிவனவ நாமே"],"p":"","r":"Raising by contemplation the prana that stands straight with the whorl of the navel, fixing it first at the summit with Siva's mantra, and raising the apana by contemplation, one becomes Siva.","n":"பிராணன் and அபானன் are the upward and downward vital airs; உந்திச் சுழி (the navel whorl) and முகடு (the summit, the crown) are their stations. சிவன் அவன் ஆமே, he becomes Siva, is the strongest claim in the chapter, and it rests on the technical practice.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["praanan","mandhiram","sivam","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:71"},"t:733":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":733,"t":["மாறா மலக்குதந் தன்மே லிருவிரற்","கூறா இலங்கத்தின் கீழே குறிக்கொண்மின்","ஆறா உடம்பிடை அண்ணலும் அங்குளன்","கூறா உபதேசங் கொண்டது காணுமே"],"p":"","r":"Mark the place two finger-breadths above the anus and below the linga; in this body the Lord too is there; see that this is the instruction that is not to be spoken.","n":"The place is traditionally the muladhara, the base of the yogic body. ஆறா உடம்பு (the body that does not cool, or does not rest) is unclear. கூறா உபதேசம் can be 'instruction not to be told' (secret, from a guru) or 'instruction that cannot be told'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","sivam","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:71"},"t:734":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":734,"t":["நீல நிறனுடை நேரிழை யாளொடுஞ்","சாலவும் புல்லிச் சதமென் றிருப்பார்க்கு","ஞாலம் அறிய நரைதிரை மாறிடும்","பாலனு மாவர் பராநந்தி ஆணையே"],"p":"","r":"Those who closely embrace the blue-hued lady with fine jewels and hold to her as lasting will, as the world can see, lose their grey hair and wrinkles and become young again; by the supreme Nandi's command.","n":"The plain reading is of union with a woman; the blue lady (நீல நிறனுடை நேரிழையாள்) is traditionally Shakti, or kundalini, and the embrace a yogic union. Both readings stand in the text. சதம் may mean 'lasting' or 'a hundred (years)'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","ilamai","siththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:71"},"t:735":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":735,"t":["அண்டஞ் சுருங்கில் அதற்கோ ரழிவில்லை","பிண்டஞ் சுருங்கிற் பிராணன் நிலைபெறும்","உண்டி சுருங்கில் உபாயம் பலவுள","கண்டங் கறுத்த கபாலியு மாமே"],"p":"","r":"If the cosmos contracts there is no ruin for it; if the body contracts the breath stays firm; if food is reduced there are many means; and one becomes the dark-throated Kapali.","n":"அண்டம் and பிண்டம் are the cosmos and the body, the macrocosm and microcosm of yoga. 'Contracting' is unexplained: shrinking, withdrawal or restraint. The last line names Siva as Kapali (the skull-bearer with the dark throat); the yogi becoming him is the sense taken, though it could be the means being his.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["undi","praanan","yaakkai","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:71"},"t:736":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":736,"t":["பிண்டத்துள் உற்ற பிழக்கடை வாசலை","அண்டத்துள் உற்று அடுத்தடுத் தேகிடில்","வண்டிச் சிக்கு மலர்க்குழல் மாதரார்","கண்டிச் சிக்குநற் காயமு மாமே"],"p":"","r":"If one goes step by step through the cosmos to the back door set in the body, women with flower-decked hair that bees seek will look at it with desire: it becomes a fine body.","n":"பிழக்கடை வாசல் (the back gate) is traditionally a passage of the yogic body, perhaps the rear channel up the spine; I can't fix it. The reward is outward beauty, desirable to women, which runs against the ascetic strain elsewhere in Tamil didactic verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","azhagu","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:71"},"t:737":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":737,"t":["சுழலும் பெருங்கூற்றுத் தொல்லைமுன் சீறி","அழலும் இரத்ததுள் அங்கியுள் ஈசன்","கழல்கொள் திருவடி காண்குறில் ஆங்கே","நிழலுளுந் தெற்றுளும் நிற்றலு மாமே"],"p":"","r":"Before the torment of great, whirling Death rages, if you would see the anklet-bearing holy feet of the Lord who is in the burning blood and in the fire, there you can stand in the shade and in the clear.","n":"அழலும் இரத்தம் and அங்கி (fire) are taken as the inner fire of the yogic body, where the Lord is seen. நிழலுளும் தெற்றுளும் is obscure: perhaps standing untouched in shade and in clear (light), or in any condition.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kootru","sivam","siththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:71"},"t:738":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":738,"t":["நான்கண்ட வன்னியும் நாலு கலையேழுந்","தான்கண்ட வாயுச் சரீர முழுதொடும்","ஊன்கண்டு கொண்ட வுணர்வு மருந்தாக","மாங்கன்று நின்று வளர்க்கின்ற வாறே"],"p":"","r":"The fire I saw, the four and seven kalas, the breath he saw with the whole body, and the awareness gained by seeing the flesh, taken as medicine: this is how the mango sapling stands and grows.","n":"The list is compressed and its grammar uncertain. The mango sapling (மாங்கன்று) is an image of the body tended to grow, but how fire, kalas and breath make the medicine is not explained.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["marundhu","praanan","kalai","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:71"},"t:739":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":739,"t":["ஆகுஞ் சனவேத சத்தியை அன்புற","நீகொள்ளின் நெல்லின் வளர்கின்ற நேர்மையைப்","பாகு படுத்திப் பல்கோடி களத்தினால்","ஊழ்கொண்ட மந்திரத் தன்னால் ஒடுங்கே"],"p":"","r":"If you take with love the Shakti of the arising Veda, with the straightness of rice as it grows, divide it into many crores of portions and be absorbed by the mantra that holds what is destined.","n":"Nearly every phrase is obscure: சனவேத சத்தி (perhaps the Shakti of the arising Veda, perhaps a corrupt word), the rice image (the variant நீர்க்கொள நெல்லின், rice taking water, suggests growth fed by water), and ஊழ்கொண்ட மந்திரம். The reading is a guess at the words, not the sense.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","mandhiram","oozh"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:71"},"t:740":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":740,"t":["மதிவட்ட மாக வரையைந்து நாடி","இதுவிட்டிங் கீரா றமர்ந்த அதனாற்","பதிவட்டத் துள்நின்று பாலிக்கு மாறு","மதுவிட்டுப் போமாறு மாயலுற் றேனே"],"p":"","r":"The nadis form a circle like the moon; taking up the twelve, I set out to examine how life is sustained standing within the Lord's circle and how the nectar slips away.","n":"மதிவட்டம் (the moon's circle), பதிவட்டம் (the Lord's circle) and மது (honey, nectar) are yogic terms I can't fix; ஈராறு (twelve) may be the twelve signs of the time wheel or a count of breath. The plain frame is the poet announcing his inquiry into how life is kept and lost.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naadi","yogam","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:72"},"t:741":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":741,"t":["உற்றறி வைந்தும் உணர்ந்தறி வாறேழுங்","கற்றறி வெட்டுங் கலந்தறி வொன்பதும்","பற்றிய பத்தும் பலவகை நாழிகை","அற்ற தறியா தழிகின்ற வாறே"],"p":"","r":"Whatever people know by the five senses, by understanding, by learning and by mixing these, they perish without noticing the many naazhigai of their life being cut away.","n":"The numbers (ஐந்து, ஆறேழு, எட்டு, ஒன்பது, பத்து) are attached to kinds of knowing and are traditionally taken as counts of the tattvas; I can't fix them. The last line is plain: the naazhigai, units of about twenty-four minutes, run out unnoticed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","arivu","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:72"},"t:742":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":742,"t":["அழிகின்ற ஆண்டவை ஐயைஞ்சு மூன்று","மொழிகின்ற முப்பத்து மூன்றென்ப தாகுங்","கழிகின்ற காலறு பத்திரண் டென்ப","தெழுகின்ற ஈரைம்ப தெண்ணற் றிருந்தே"],"p":"","r":"The years of a perishing life are counted in spans, twenty-five and three, thirty-three, sixty-two, and the hundred that rises beyond reckoning.","n":"ஐயைஞ்சு மூன்று can be read as twenty-five and three or as twenty-eight; what each span measures (a lifetime, a stage, a breath count) is not said.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:72"},"t:743":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":743,"t":["திருந்து தினமத் தினத்தி நொடுநின்","றிருந்தறி நாளொன் றிரண்டெட்டு மூன்று","பொருந்திய நாளொடு புக்கறிந் தோங்கி","வருந்துத லின்றி மனைபுக லாமே"],"p":"","r":"Keeping to the right day and its days, knowing the days one, two, eight and three, and rising by entering into them, one may enter the house without distress.","n":"மனை (house) may be a house of the zodiac, the body, or the inner seat the yogi reaches; the day numbers are not explained.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:72"},"t:744":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":744,"t":["மனைபுகு வீரும் மகத்திடை நீராடி","எனவிரு பத்தஞ்சும் ஈரா றதனால்","தனையறிந் தேறட்டுத் தற்குறி யாறு","வினையறி யாறு விளங்கிய நாலே"],"p":"","r":"Those who enter the house, bathing in the midst of Magam, reckon twenty-five and twelve; by that they know themselves and climb the eight, with six signs of their own, six of karma and the shining four.","n":"மகம் is the lunar mansion Magha, but bathing there may be literal or a yogic figure. The verse is a list of counts whose scheme I can't recover.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:72"},"t:745":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":745,"t":["நாலுங் கடந்தது நால்வரும் நாலைந்து","பாலங் கடந்தது பத்துப் பதினைந்து","கோலங் கடந்த குணத்தாண்டு மூவிரண்","டாலங் கடந்ததொன் றாரறி வாரே"],"p":"","r":"Beyond the four, the four beings and the twenty, beyond the ten and the fifteen, beyond forms and qualities and the six years, lies the one beyond the banyan: who knows it?","n":"Each line passes beyond (கடந்தது) a number. ஆலம் may be the banyan, under which Siva teaches, or poison, which he swallowed; பாலம் (bridge) is also unclear. The movement, counting up to what has no count, is plain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:72"},"t:746":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":746,"t":["ஆறும் இருபதுக் கையஞ்சு மூன்றுக்குந்","தேறு மிரண்டு மிருபத்தொ டாறிவை","கூறு மதியொன் றினுக்கிரு பத்தேழு","வேறு பதியங்க ணாள்விதித் தானே"],"p":"","r":"Six for the twenty, five for the three, two, twenty-six, and twenty-seven for the one moon: so he ordained the days in another station.","n":"Twenty-seven for the moon matches the twenty-seven lunar mansions. The other numbers, and who விதித்தான் (he who ordained) is, are not given; the Lord is the usual reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:72"},"t:747":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":747,"t":["விதித்த இருபத்தெட் டொடுமூன் றறையாகத்","தொகுத்தறி முப்பத்து மூன்று தொகுமின்","பதித்தறி பத்தெட்டும் பாரா திகணால்","உதித்தறி மூன்றிரண் டொன்றின் முறையே"],"p":"","r":"The ordained twenty-eight, with three as a half, gathered make thirty-three; the eighteen set in place, and three, two and one rising in order, are to be known.","n":"A count whose scheme I can't recover. பாரா திகணால் in line 3 is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:72"},"t:748":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":748,"t":["முறைமுறை யாய்ந்து முயன்றில ராகில்","இறையிறை யார்க்கும் இருக்க அரிது","மறையது காரண மற்றொன்று மில்லை","பறையறை யாது பணிந்து முடியே"],"p":"","r":"Unless one examines these orders step by step and practises, it is hard for anyone to remain even a little while; the scripture is the ground, there is no other, so complete it humbly, without beating a drum about it.","n":"மறை may be the Veda or simply the hidden teaching. பறையறையாது (without beating the drum) is a plain instruction against proclaiming one's practice. இருக்க (to remain) is taken here as staying in the body.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","panivu","vedham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:72"},"t:749":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":749,"t":["முடிந்த தறியார் முயல்கின்ற மூர்க்கர்","இட்ஞ்சில் இருக்க விளக்கெரி கொண்டு","கடிந்தனன் மூளக் கதுவவல் லார்க்கு","நடந்திடும் பாரினில் நண்ணலு மாமே"],"p":"","r":"Fools strive without knowing the end; but for those able to catch the flame as it kindles, like a lamp burning in its bowl, the attainment may be reached even while walking the earth.","n":"இட்ஞ்சில் is taken as இடிஞ்சில் (lamp bowl). கடிந்தனன் மூளக் கதுவ (to seize as it blazes) is obscure; the inner flame of yoga is the usual reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","pedhaimai","siththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:72"},"t:750":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":750,"t":["நண்ணு சிறுவிர னாணாக மூன்றுக்கும்","பின்னிய மார்பிடைப் பேராமல் ஒத்திடுஞ்","சென்னியின் மூன்றுக்குன்ய் சேரவே நின்றிடும்","உன்னி யுணர்ந்திடும் ஓவியந் தானே"],"p":"","r":"With the little finger as a cord for the three, held at the chest without moving, and the three at the head joined together, one who contemplates perceives the picture.","n":"This seems to describe a posture or a placing of the fingers, but I can't reconstruct it. ஓவியம் (picture) is taken up in 751 as an inner awareness.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:72"},"t:751":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":751,"t":["ஓவிய மான வுணர்வை அறிமின்கள்","பாவிக ளித்தின் பயனறி வாரில்லை","தீவினை யாமுடன் மண்டல மூன்றுக்கும்","பூவில் இருந்திடும் புண்ணியத் தண்டே"],"p":"","r":"Know the awareness that has become this picture; sinners do not know its use; for the three mandalas, the meritorious staff rests in the flower.","n":"தண்டு (staff) is traditionally the spinal channel, பூ (flower) a lotus centre, and the three மண்டலம் those of fire, sun and moon. A plain reading is not available.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","meyyunarvu","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:72"},"t:752":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":752,"t":["தண்டுடன் ஓடித் தலைப்பெய்த யோகிக்கு","மண்டல மூன்று மகிழ்ந்துடல் ஒத்திடுங்","கண்டவர் கண்டனர் காணார் வினைப்பயன்","பிண்டம் பிரியப் பிணங்குகின் றாரே"],"p":"","r":"For the yogi who has run up the staff to the head, the three mandalas gladly accord with the body; those who saw it have seen, and those who did not, the fruit of their deeds, struggle when the body parts.","n":"பிண்டம் பிரிய (when the body separates) is death; the contrast is between the yogi whose body is held in accord and those who die struggling. The variant மகிழ்ந்துடன் changes little.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","yaakkai","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:72"},"t:753":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":753,"t":["பிணங்கி அழிந்திடும் பேறது கேள்நீ","அணங்குட னாதித்த னாறு விரியின்","வணங்குட னேவந்த வாழ்வு குலைந்து","சுணங்கனுக் காகச் சுழல்கின்ற வாறே"],"p":"","r":"Hear what that struggle and ruin bring: when the sun with the goddess spreads through the six, the life that came with honour falls apart and goes whirling on, ending as food for dogs.","n":"அணங்குடன் ஆதித்தன் ஆறு விரியின் is obscure. சுணங்கன் (dog) is plain: the neglected body ends as carrion.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:72"},"t:754":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":754,"t":["சுழல்கின்ற வாறின் துணைமலர் காணான்","தழலிடைப் புக்கிடுந் தன்னு ளிலாமற்","கழல்கண்ட போம்வழி காணவல் லார்க்குக்","குழல்வழி நின்றிடுங் கூத்தனு மாமே"],"p":"","r":"Whirling so, a person never sees the paired flowers and goes into the fire with nothing within; but for those who can see the way to the anklet-feet, the Dancer stands in the channel.","n":"துணைமலர் (paired flowers) and கழல் (anklet) are usually the Lord's feet; தழல் (fire) is the cremation fire. குழல்வழி (the tube's way) is traditionally the central channel.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","yogam","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:72"},"t:755":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":755,"t":["கூத்தன் குறியிற் குணம்பல கண்டவர்","சாத்திரந் தன்னைத் தலைப்பெய்து நிற்பர்கள்","பார்த்திருந் துள்ளே அனுபோக நோக்கிடில்","ஆத்தனு மாகி யலர்ந்திரு மொன்றே"],"p":"","r":"Those who have seen many qualities in the Dancer's sign stand at the head of the sastras; if they look within for experience, the Lord too unfolds as one with them.","n":"ஆத்தன் (the trusted one, the Lord) may be the Dancer or the seer who becomes like him. The contrast between knowing scripture and inner experience (அனுபோகம்) is the verse's turn.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","gnaanam","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:72"},"t:756":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":756,"t":["ஒன்றில் வளர்ச்சி உலப்பிலி கேளினி","நன்றென்று மூன்றுக்கு நாளது சென்றிடுஞ்","சென்றிடு முப்பதுஞ் சேர இருந்திடிற்","குன்றிடைப் பொன்திகழ் கூத்தனு மாமே"],"p":"","r":"Hear now the unending growth in the one: the days go well for the three, and if one stays joined as the thirty pass, the Dancer gleaming like gold on the mountain appears.","n":"The three and the thirty may be mandalas and days of practice; குன்றிடைப் பொன் (gold on the mountain) may be the crown of the head.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:72"},"t:757":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":757,"t":["கூத்தவன் ஒன்றிடுங் கூர்மை அறிந்தங்கே","ஏத்துவர் பத்தினில் எண்டிசை தோன்றிடப்","பார்த்து மகிழ்ந்து பதுமரை நோக்கிடிற்","சாத்திடு நூறு தலைப்பெய்ய லாமே"],"p":"","r":"Knowing the fine point where the Dancer unites, they praise him there; when in ten the eight directions appear and one gazes on the lotus with delight, a hundred years may be attained.","n":"சாத்திடு நூறு (the hundred that is fixed) is taken as a hundred years of life; பதுமரை (lotus) as an inner centre. The numbers ten and eight are not explained.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","siththi","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:72"},"t:758":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":758,"t":["சாத்திடு நூறு தலைப்பெய்து நின்றவர்","காத்துடல் ஆயிரங் கட்டுறக் காண்பர்கள்","சேர்த்துடல் ஆயிரஞ் சேர இருந்தவர்","மூத்துடன் கோடி யுகமது வாமே"],"p":"","r":"Those who have reached the hundred guard the body and see it held for a thousand; those who stay joined a thousand age on into a crore of yugas.","n":"The plain claim is the long life of the yogi's body, rising in powers of ten. The variant கூடி for கோடி would change a crore of ages to 'joined with the age'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["siththi","yaakkai","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:72"},"t:759":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":759,"t":["உகங்கோடி கண்டும் ஒசிவற நின்று","அகங்கோடி கண்டு ளயலறக் காண்பர்கள்","சிவங்கோடி விட்டுச் செறிய இருந்தங்","குகங்கோடி கண்டல் குயருறு வாரே"],"p":"","r":"Seeing a crore of ages, they stand unwavering, see crores within without weariness, and staying close in Siva rise to the height.","n":"சிவங்கோடி விட்டு is hard: 'leaving crores for Siva' or 'Siva's crores'. The variant அயர்வற (without weariness) is followed here.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["siththi","sivam","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:72"},"t:760":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":760,"t":["உயருறு வாருல கத்தொடுங் கூடிப்","பயனுறு வார்பலர் தாமறி யாமற்","செயலுறு வார்சிலர் சிந்தையி லாமற்","கயலுறு கண்ணியைக் காணகி லாரே"],"p":"","r":"Of those who rise, many mingle with the world and take its fruits without knowing, and some act without a mind on it; they do not see the fish-eyed one.","n":"கயலுறு கண்ணி (she with fish-like eyes) is usually the goddess, the Lord's Shakti. Whether உயருறுவார் are failed yogis or simply people who prosper is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:72"},"t:761":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":761,"t":["காணகி லாதார் கழிந்தோடிப் போவர்கள்","நாணகி லாதார் நயம்பேசி விடுவர்கள்","காணகி லாதார் கழிந்த பொருளெலாங்","காணகி லாமற் கழிகின்ற வாறே"],"p":"","r":"Those who do not see run off and pass away; the shameless talk pleasantly and let it go; and for those who do not see, all that has passed passes on unseen.","n":"The verse repeats காணகிலாதார் (those who cannot see) to make blindness to passing time its subject; நயம்பேசி (talking nicely) may mean empty talk in place of practice.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:72"},"t:762":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":762,"t":["கழிகின்ற அப்பொருள் காணகி லாதார்","கழிகின்ற அப்பொருள் காணலு மாகுங்","கழிகின்ற வுள்ளே கருத்துற நோக்கிற்","கழியாத அப்பொருள் காணலு மாமே"],"p":"","r":"Those who cannot see the thing that passes away, yet that passing thing can be seen; and looking within the passing with the mind fixed, the thing that does not pass can be seen.","n":"The verse plays on கழிகின்ற (passing) and கழியாத (unpassing). The passing thing may be the breath, time or the body; the unpassing one is usually the Lord or the self. The turn from one to the other is plain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","meyyunarvu","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:72"},"t:763":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":763,"t":["கண்ணன் பிறப்பிலி காணந்தி யாயுள்ளே","எண்ணுந் திசையுடன் ஏகாந்த னாயிடுந்","திண்ணென் றிருக்குஞ் சிவகதி யாநிற்கும்","நண்ணும் பதமிது நாடவல் லார்கட்கே"],"p":"","r":"The unborn one with the eye, seeing within as Nandi, becomes the solitary one in whatever direction one thinks of; this firm Siva-state is the station reached by those able to seek.","n":"கண்ணன் is not Krishna here but is taken as the one with the (third) eye; காணந்தி may be காண் நந்தி (Nandi who sees). சிவகதி (the Siva-state) is plain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","veedu","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:72"},"t:764":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":764,"t":["நாடவல் லார்க்கு நமனில்லை கேடில்லை","நாடவல் லார்கள் நரபதி யாய்நிற்பர்","தேடவல் லார்கள் தெரிந்த பொருளிது","கூடவல் லார்கட்குக் கூறலு மாமே"],"p":"","r":"For those able to seek there is no Yama and no ruin; they stand as lords among men; this is the thing the seekers have discerned, and it may be told to those able to join it.","n":"நமன் is Yama, death. நரபதி (lord of men) may be literal kingship or mastery; the verse doesn't say.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","siththi","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:72"},"t:765":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":765,"t":["கூறும் பொருளி தகார வுகாரங்கள்","தேறும் பொருளிது சிந்தையுள் நின்றிடக்","கூறு மகாரங் குழல்வழி யோடிட","ஆறும் அமர்ந்திடும் அண்ணலு மாமே"],"p":"","r":"What is spoken is A and U, what is grasped while it stays in the mind; when the spoken M runs along the tube, the Lord settles in the six.","n":"அகார, உகார, மகாரம் are the parts of Om. குழல்வழி (the tube) is the central channel and ஆறு (six) probably the six centres; ஆறு may also mean the way.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piranavam","yogam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:72"},"t:766":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":766,"t":["அண்ணல் இருப்பிட மாரும் அறிகிலர்","அண்ணல் இருப்பிடம் ஆய்ந்துகொள் வார்களுக்","கண்ணல் அழிவின்றி உள்ளே அமர்ந்திடும்","அண்ணலைக் காணில் அவனிவ வாகுமே"],"p":"","r":"No one knows where the Lord dwells; for those who search it out he stays within without decay, and whoever sees the Lord becomes him.","n":"அவன் இவன் ஆகுமே can be 'he becomes this one' or 'this one becomes him'; the identity of seer and Lord is the point either way, but how far it goes is the reader's theology.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:72"},"t:767":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":767,"t":["அவனிவ நாகும் பரிசறி வாரில்லை","அவனிவ நாகும் பரிசது கேள்நீ","அவனிவ நோசை ஒளியினுள் ஒன்றிடும்","அவனிவன் வட்டம தாகிநின் றானே"],"p":"","r":"No one knows how he becomes this one; hear it: he unites in this one's sound and light, and stands as this one's circle.","n":"ஓசை (sound) and ஒளி (light) are taken as nada and the inner light; வட்டம் (circle) links to the seven circles of 768.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","naadham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:72"},"t:768":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":768,"t":["வட்டங்க ளேழு மலர்ந்திடும் உம்முளே","சிட்டன் இருப்பிடஞ் சேர அறிகிலீர்","ஒட்டி யிருந்துள் உபாயம் உணர்ந்திடக்","கட்டி இருப்பிடங் காணலு மாகுமே"],"p":"","r":"Seven circles blossom within you, yet you do not know how to reach the dwelling of the excellent one; staying attached within and learning the means, you can see his dwelling held firm.","n":"The seven circles (வட்டங்கள் ஏழு) are often taken as the bodily centres; சிட்டன் is the excellent or holy one, the Lord. கட்டி (firmly, or a lump) is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:72"},"t:769":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":769,"t":["காணலு மாகும் பிரமன் அரியென்று","காணலு மாகுங் கறைக்கண்டன் ஈசனைக்","காணலு மாகுஞ் சதாசிவ சத்தியங்","காணலு மாகுங் கலந்துடன் வைத்ததே"],"p":"","r":"One can see Brahma and Hari, the dark-throated Lord Isa, Sadasiva and Shakti, and all of them set together.","n":"The gods are the usual five presiding deities of the inner centres; the verse may mean they are seen within, in the circles of 768. கலந்துடன் வைத்ததே (what is set mingled together) is vague.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","saththi","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:72"},"t:770":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":770,"t":["வைத்தகை சென்னியில் நேரிதாய்த் தோன்றிடில்","உத்தம மிக்கிடில் ஓராறு தங்களா","மத்த மிகுத்திட் டிரட்டிய தாயிடில்","நித்தல் உயிர்க்கொரு திங்களில் ஓசையே"],"p":"","r":"If the hand placed on the head appears straight, the span is six months; if the sign is doubled in excess, it is one month, and a sound is heard daily by the life.","n":"The verse gives a test of the remaining span, but the sign (வைத்தகை சென்னியில் நேரிதாய்), உத்தமம் and மத்தம் can't be fixed; ஓசை (sound) may be an inner sound heard before death.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["uyir","nilaiyaamai","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:73"},"t:771":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":771,"t":["ஓசையும் ஈசனும் ஒக்கும் உணர்வின்கண்","ஓசை யிறந்தவர் ஈசனை உள்குவர்","ஓசை யிறந்தவர் நெஞ்சினுள் ஈசனும்","ஓசை யுணர்ந்த உணர்விது வாமே"],"p":"","r":"Sound and the Lord are alike in awareness; those who have gone beyond sound contemplate the Lord, and the Lord in their heart is the awareness that has known the sound.","n":"ஓசை is traditionally nada, the inner sound. The variant பிறந்தவர் (those in whom sound arose) reverses 'gone beyond sound' and changes the sense.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naadham","sivam","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:73"},"t:772":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":772,"t":["ஆமே அழிகின்ற வாயுவை நோக்கிடில்","நாமே உறைகின்ற நன்மை யளித்திடும்","பூமேல் உறைகின்ற போதகம் வந்திடுந்","தாமே யுலகில் தலைவனு மாமே"],"p":"","r":"If one watches the breath that is wasting away, it gives the good of our own dwelling, the teaching that dwells on the flower comes, and one becomes a master in the world.","n":"அழிகின்ற வாயு is the breath spent with each moment. பூமேல் உறைகின்ற போதகம் (the knowledge dwelling on the flower) may be an inner lotus or a god seated on one; போதகம் can also mean an elephant.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","uyir","siththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:73"},"t:773":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":773,"t":["தலைவ னிடம்வலஞ் சாதிப்பார் இல்லை","தலைவ னிடம்வல மாயிடில் தையல்","தலைவ னிடம்வலந் தன்வழி யஞ்சில்","தலைவ நிடம்வலந் தன்வழி நூறே"],"p":"","r":"None master the Lord's left and right; if the left and right turn, it is the woman's; if they keep to their own way, five; if they keep to their own way, a hundred.","n":"இடம் வலம் (left and right) are traditionally the breath in the left and right nostrils, and தலைவன் the breath or the Lord. தையல் (woman) and the numbers five and a hundred (perhaps years) can't be fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:73"},"t:774":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":774,"t":["ஏறிய வாறினில் எண்பது சென்றிடுந்","தேறிய ஏழிற் சிறக்கும் வகையெண்ணில்","ஆறொரு பத்தாய் அமர்ந்த இரண்டையுந்","தேறியே நின்று தெளியிவ் வகையே"],"p":"","r":"If the breath runs in the six that has risen, eighty go; reckoned by the seven, it is better; take the two that stand at sixteen, stand clear, and understand this kind.","n":"A count whose scheme I can't recover; ஆறொருபத்து may be sixteen or six and ten.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:73"},"t:775":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":775,"t":["இவ்வகை எட்டும் இடம்பெற ஓடிடில்","அவ்வகை ஐம்பதே யென்ன அறியலாஞ்","செவ்வகை ஒன்பதுஞ் சேரவே நின்றிடின்","முவ்வகை யாமது முப்பத்து மூன்றே"],"p":"","r":"If these eight run to their place, that kind is known as fifty; if the nine stand together, the threefold is thirty-three.","n":"The variants (ஐயொன்பது, ஒன்பது, அன்பது) change the number in line 2, so even the count is unsettled.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:73"},"t:776":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":776,"t":["மும்மூன்றும் ஒன்றும் முடிவுற நின்றிடிற்","எண்மூன்றும் நாலும் இடவகை யாய்நிற்கும்","ஐம்மூன்றும் ஓடி அகலவே நின்றிடிற்","பன்மூன்றொ டீராறு பார்க்கலு மாமே"],"p":"","r":"If three threes and one stand to the end, three eights and four stand as places; if three fives run and stand apart, three and twelve can be seen.","n":"The variant எண்முன்றினாலும் changes the number in line 2. What is counted is not said.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:73"},"t:777":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":777,"t":["பார்க்கலு மாகும் பகல்முப் பதுமாகில்","ஆக்கலு மாகுமவ் வாறிரண் டுள்ளிட்டுப்","போக்கலு மாகும் புகலற ஒன்றெனில்","தேக்கலு மாகுந் திருந்திய பத்தே"],"p":"","r":"If the day-breath runs thirty, the span can be seen; it can be made by putting twelve within; if it is one without refuge it can be sent off; the ten set right can be stored.","n":"பகல் (day) may be the daytime or the right breath, traditionally called the sun. The verbs (see, make, send off, store) suggest the breath can be governed, but the scheme is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:73"},"t:778":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":778,"t":["ஏயிரு நாளும் இயல்புற ஓடிடிற்","பாயிரு நாலும் பகையற நின்றிடும்","தேய்வுற மூன்றுந் திகழவே நின்றிடில்","ஆயுரு வாறென் றளக்கலு மாமே"],"p":"","r":"If the breath runs properly on two days, the two fours stand without hostility; if the three stand clear to wane, the form can be measured as six.","n":"ஏயிரு and பாயிரு are unclear; the units (days, months, years) are not given.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:73"},"t:779":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":779,"t":["அளக்கும் வகைநாலும் அவ்வழியே ஓடில்","விளக்கும் ஒருநாலு மெய்ப்பட நிற்கும்","துளக்கும் வகையைந்துந் தூய்நெறி ஓடில்","களக்க மறமூன்றிற் காணலு மாமே"],"p":"","r":"If the four ways of measuring run on that path, one four stands true; if the five that shake run on the pure way, it can be seen in three without stain.","n":"A count whose scheme I can't recover.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:73"},"t:780":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":780,"t":["காணலு மாகுங் கருதிய பத்தோடிற்","காணலு மாகுங் கலந்த இரண்டையும்","காணலு மாகுங் கலப்பற மூவைந்தேற்","காணலு மாகுங் கருத்துற ஒன்றே"],"p":"","r":"If the intended ten run, it can be seen; the two mixed can be seen; three fives without mixing can be seen; the one can be seen with the mind fixed.","n":"The refrain காணலுமாகும் (it can be seen) carries the verse. The variant கலப்புற மூவைந்தேழ் reverses 'without mixing' and adds a seven.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:73"},"t:781":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":781,"t":["கருதும் இருபதிற் காண ஆறாகும்","கருதிய ஐயைந்திற் காண்பது மூன்றாம்","கருதும் இருப துடனாறு காணிற்","கருதும் இரண்டெனக் காட்டலு மாமே"],"p":"","r":"Considered, in twenty it is six; in twenty-five it is three; in twenty-six it can be shown as two.","n":"Possibly breath counts against remaining days or months. The variants ஈராறு (twelve for six) and ஐந்திற் (five for twenty-five) change the numbers.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:73"},"t:782":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":782,"t":["காட்டலு மாகுங் கலந்திரு பத்தேழில்","காட்டலு மாகுங் கலந்தெழும் ஒன்றெனக்","காட்டலு மாகுங் கலந்திரு பத்தெட்டிற்","காட்டலு மாகுங் கலந்தஈ ரைந்தே"],"p":"","r":"In twenty-seven it can be shown as one rising mingled; in twenty-eight it can be shown as ten.","n":"The refrain காட்டலுமாகும் (it can be shown) continues 781's series; what is shown is not said.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:73"},"t:783":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":783,"t":["ஈரைந்தும் ஐந்தும் இருமூன்றும் எட்டுக்கும்","பாரஞ்சி நின்ற பகைபத்து நாளாகும்","வாரஞ்செய் கின்ற வகையாறஞ் சாமாகில்","ஓரஞ்சொ டொன்றொன் றெனவொன்று நாளே"],"p":"","r":"For ten, five, six and eight, the enemy that dreads the burden is ten days; if the weekly course is six and five, it is one day.","n":"The series seems to narrow toward a single remaining day. The variant வகையான்ய் சமாதியில் is garbled but would bring in samadhi.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","uyir","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:73"},"t:784":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":784,"t":["ஒன்றிய நாள்கள் ஒருமுப்பத் தொன்றாகிற்","கன்றிய நாலுங் கருத்துற மூன்றாகுஞ்","சென்றுயிர் நாலெட்டுஞ் சேரவே நின்றிடின்","மன்றியல் பாகு மனையில் இரண்டே"],"p":"","r":"If the joined days become thirty-one, the four become three; if the life goes and the four eights stand together, the measure in the house is two.","n":"மனை (house) and மன்றியல்பு are unclear; மன்று may be the hall where the Lord dances.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:73"},"t:785":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":785,"t":["மனையிலஒன் றாகும் மாதமு மூன்றுஞ்","சுனையில்ஒன் றாகத் தொனித்தனன் நந்தி","வினையற வோங்கி வெளிச்செய்து நின்றால்","தனையுற நின்ற தலைவனு மாமே"],"p":"","r":"In the house it is one and three months, Nandi declared it as one in the spring; one who rises beyond karma and opens out into the clear space becomes the Lord who stands joined with himself.","n":"சுனை (spring, pool) may be an inner centre. The second half turns from the counts to the yogi's goal: வினையற (with karma gone) and வெளிச்செய்து (making open, or entering the void).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","vinai","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:73"},"t:786":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":786,"t":["ஆரு மறியார் அளக்கின்ற வன்னியை","ஆரு மறியார் அளக்கின்ற வாயுவை","ஆரு மறியார் அழிகின்ற அப்பொருள்","ஆரு மறியார் அறிவறிந் தேனே"],"p":"","r":"No one knows the fire that measures, no one knows the breath that measures, no one knows the thing that perishes; but I have come to know the knowing.","n":"வன்னி (fire) and வாயு (breath) are the measurers of life the chapter has been counting. அறிவறிந்தேனே can be 'I knew by knowledge' or 'I knew the knower'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["meyyunarvu","yogam","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:73"},"t:787":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":787,"t":["அறிவது வாயுவொ டைந்தறி வாய","அறிவா வதுதான் உலகுயி ரத்தின்","பிறிவுசெய் யாவகை பேணியுள் நாடிற்","செறிவது நின்று திகழு மதுவே"],"p":"","r":"Knowing is with the breath and the five senses; if one searches within and keeps world and life from being parted, that knowledge stands dense and shines.","n":"உலகுயிரத்தின் is obscure, taken here as உலகு உயிர் அதன்; பிறிவுசெய்யாவகை (so as not to part) may mean keeping life in the body.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arivu","yogam","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:73"},"t:788":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":788,"t":["அதுவரு ளும்மரு ளான துலகம்","பொதுவரு ளும்புக ழாளர்க்கு நாளு","மதுவரு ளும்மலர் மங்கையர் செல்வி","இதுவருள் செய்யும் இறையவ னாமே"],"p":"","r":"That gives grace, and the world is its grace; it graces those of renown in common every day; it gives the honey, the lady of the flower; this is the Lord who grants grace.","n":"அது (that) may be the knowledge of 787 or the Lord. மலர் மங்கையர் செல்வி (the lady among flower-women) is taken as Lakshmi or as Shakti; மது (honey) may be bliss.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:73"},"t:789":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":789,"t":["பிறப்பது சூழ்ந்த பெருந்தகை நந்தி","குறிப்பது கூடிய கோலக் குரம்பைப்","பழப்பதி யாவது பற்றறும் பாசம்","அழப்படி செய்வார்க் ககலு மதியே"],"p":"","r":"Nandi, the great one who ordained birth, joined to his sign the fair hut of the body; for those who work so that the bond of the old dwelling, its attachment cut, gives way, understanding widens.","n":"குரம்பை (hut) is a common figure for the body and பழப்பதி (the old town) may be the same body or the round of births. அழப்படி and அகலும் மதியே (the mind, or the moon, widens or departs) are hard.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["paasam","yaakkai","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:73"},"t:790":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":790,"t":["வெள்ளிவெண் திங்கள் விளங்கும் புதனிடம்","ஒள்ளிய மந்தன் இரவிசெவ் வாய்வலம்","வள்ளிய பொன்னே வளரும் பிறையிடந்","தெள்ளிய தேய்பிறை தான்வல மாமே"],"p":"","r":"On Friday, Monday and Wednesday the breath runs on the left; on Saturday, Sunday and Tuesday on the right; on Thursday it runs on the left in the waxing fortnight and on the right in the waning.","n":"The days are named by their planets: வெள்ளி Venus, திங்கள் the moon, புதன் Mercury, மந்தன் Saturn, இரவி the sun, செவ்வாய் Mars, and பொன் (gold) for Jupiter. Left and right are taken as the left and right nostril, the channels இடை and பிங்கலை of 793.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["praanan","naadi","kaalam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:74"},"t:791":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":791,"t":["வெள்ளிவெண் திங்கள் விளங்கும் புதன்மூன்றுந்","தள்ளி இடத்தே தயங்குமே யாமாகில்","ஒள்ளிய காயத்துக் கூன மிலையென்று","வள்ளல் நமக்கு மகிழ்ந்துரைத் தானே"],"p":"","r":"If on Friday, Monday and Wednesday the breath moves steadily on the left, the bright body has no flaw: so the generous one gladly told us.","n":"வள்ளல் (the generous one) is usually taken as Nandi, Tirumular's teacher. The promise is bodily health, not release.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["praanan","yaakkai","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:74"},"t:792":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":792,"t":["செவ்வாய் வியாழஞ் சனிஞாயி றேஎன்னும்","இவ்வா றறிகின்ற யோகி இறைவனே","ஒவ்வாத வாயு வலத்துப் புரியவிட்","டவ்வா றறிவார்க்கவ் வானந்த மாமே"],"p":"","r":"The yogi who knows Tuesday, Thursday, Saturday and Sunday in this way is a lord; for those who know how to turn the breath that does not fit the day to the right, there is bliss.","n":"Thursday is here simply a right-hand day, while 790 splits it by fortnight. ஒவ்வாத வாயு வலத்துப் புரியவிட்டு is read as redirecting a breath that runs on the wrong side; how this is done is not said.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["praanan","yogam","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:74"},"t:793":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":793,"t":["மாறி வருமிரு பான்மதி வெய்யவன்","ஏறி இழியுந் இடைபிங் கலையிடை","ஊறும் உயிர்நடு வேயுயி ருக்கிரந்","தேறி அறிமின் தெரிந்து தெளிந்தே"],"p":"","r":"Moon and sun, alternating over the two fortnights, rise and fall through ida and pingala; life wells up in the middle between them: understand it, discern it and be clear.","n":"Moon and sun are the traditional names of the left and right channels, and the middle is taken as சுழுமுனை (sushumna), which the verse does not name. உயிருக்கு இரந்து in line 3 is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naadi","praanan","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:74"},"t:794":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":794,"t":["உதித்து வலத்திடம் போகின்ற போது","அதிர்த்தஞ்சி யோடுத லாமகன் றாரும்","உதித்தது வேமிக வோடிடு மாகில்","உதித்த விராசி யுணர்ந்துகொ ளுற்றே"],"p":"","r":"When the breath that has risen moves from the right side to the left, there is trembling, fear and going away; if the risen breath runs strongly, understand the sign in which it rose.","n":"Line 2 is obscure and has variants (அதிற்கஞ்சி, அதிற்றுஞ்சி) that change it; it may name the bad results of a breath on the wrong side. இராசி is taken as the zodiac sign of the moment, which ties the breath to astrological reckoning.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["praanan","kaalam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:74"},"t:795":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":795,"t":["நடுவுநில் லாமல் இடம்வலம் ஓடி","அடுகின்ற வாயுவை அந்தணன் கூடி","இடுகின்ற வாறுசென் றின்பணி சேர","முடிகின்ற தீபத்தின் முன்னுண்டென் றானே"],"p":"","r":"The wise one who joins the breath that will not keep to the middle but runs left and right and does harm, and leads it as it should go so that joy is gained, said there is something before the lamp that ends it.","n":"அந்தணன் is taken as Siva or the teacher. முடிகின்ற தீபத்தின் முன்னுண்டு is obscure: perhaps a light at the crown, reached when the breath enters the middle channel.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["praanan","naadi","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:74"},"t:796":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":796,"t":["ஆயும் பொருளும் அணிமலர் மேலது","வாயு விதமும் பதினா றுளவலி","போய மனத்தைப் பொருகின்ற வாதாரம்","ஆயவு நாளு முகுர்த்தமு மாமே"],"p":"","r":"The thing to be searched out is above the adorned flower; the kinds of breath and their sixteen strengths, and the support that wrestles with the straying mind, become the day and the hour.","n":"Almost every phrase is technical: அணிமலர் may be a lotus of the body, பதினாறு may be sixteen measures of breath, and ஆதாரம் a support centre. The last line seems to say the breath itself is the clock, but the reading is a guess. A variant reads முகுத்தமு.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["praanan","manam","kaalam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:74"},"t:797":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":797,"t":["வாரத்திற் சூலம் வரும்வழி கூறுங்கால்","நேரொத்த திங்கள் சனிகிழக் கேயாகும்","பாரொத்த சேய்புதன் உத்தரம் பானுநாள்","நேரொத்த வெள்ளி குடக்காக நிற்குமே"],"p":"","r":"Telling how the trident falls by weekday: on Monday and Saturday it is in the east, on Tuesday and Wednesday in the north, and on Sunday and Friday in the west.","n":"சேய் (the red one) names Mars, so Tuesday, and பானு the sun, so Sunday. The rule is travel lore of the kind still printed in almanacs; the verse gives no reason for it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","idam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:75"},"t:798":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":798,"t":["தெக்கண மாகும் வியாழத்துச் சேர்த்திசை","அக்கணி சூலமு மாமிடம் பின்னாகில்","துக்கமும் இல்லை வலமுன்னே தோன்றிடின்","மிக்கது மேல்வினை மேன்மேல் விளையுமே"],"p":"","r":"On Thursday it is in the south; if the trident is to the left or behind, there is no grief, but if it appears to the right or in front, trouble grows on trouble.","n":"மிக்கது மேல்வினை மேன்மேல் விளையுமே could also be read as 'further deeds grow greater and greater', a good result; the contrast with துக்கமும் இல்லை favours the bad sense, as the rule is usually given.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","idam","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:75"},"t:799":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":799,"t":["கட்டக் கழன்று கீழ்நான்று வீழாமல்","அட்டத்தைக் கட்டி அடுப்பை அணைகோலி","விட்டத்தைப் பூட்டி மேற்பையைத் தாட்கோத்து","நட்ட மிருக்க நமனில்லை தானே"],"p":"","r":"So that nothing comes loose and drops down, bind the eight, bank the hearth, lock the crossbeam and fasten the upper bag at its stem; when one stays firm thus, there is no Yama.","n":"The verse is a riddle in household words. அடுப்பு (hearth) is often taken as the fire at the base of the spine, மேற்பை (upper bag) as the head or the palate cavity, and what must not drop as the breath or the bindu; none of this is stated. The last line repeats 574.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","kootru","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:76"},"t:800":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":800,"t":["வண்ணான் ஒலிக்குஞ் சதுரப் பலகைமேற்","கண்ணாறு மோழை படாமற் கரைகட்டி","விண்ணாறு பாய்ச்சிக் குளத்தை நிரப்பினால்","அண்ணாந்து பார்க்க அழுக்கற்ற வாறே"],"p":"","r":"On the square plank where the washerman beats clothes, bank the edges so the channels do not leak, let the sky-river flow in and fill the tank, and on looking up one is free of dirt.","n":"The plank is taken as the palate, the sky-river as the nectar from the head, and அண்ணாந்து பார்க்க (looking upward) as the upturned gaze or tongue. அழுக்கு may be bodily or the soul's impurity. Variants read விண்ணாற்றைத் தேக்கி and நிரப்பிட்டு.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["amudham","yogam","malam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:76"},"t:801":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":801,"t":["இடக்கை வலக்கை இரண்டையும் மாற்றித்","துதிக்கையால் உண்பார்க்குச் சோரவும் வேண்டாம்","உறக்கத்தை நீக்கி உணரவல் லார்க்கட்","கிறக்கவும் வேண்டாம் இருக்கலு மாமே"],"p":"","r":"Those who exchange left hand and right and eat with the trunk need not grow weary; for those who can drive out sleep and stay aware, there is no need to die: they can remain.","n":"Left and right hand are read as the two breaths, and துதிக்கை (an elephant's trunk) as the tongue turned back to 'eat' the nectar. The promise to stay without dying may be literal or may mean freedom from birth and death.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","naadi","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:76"},"t:802":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":802,"t":["ஆய்ந்துரை செய்யில் அமுதநின் றூறிடும்","வாய்ந்துரை செய்யும் வருகின்ற காலத்து","நீந்துரை செய்யில் நிலாமண் டலமதாய்ப்","பாய்ந்துரை செய்தது பாலிக்கு மாறே"],"p":"","r":"If one practises with discernment, nectar wells up and stays; it speaks fittingly when its time comes; practised further it becomes the sphere of the moon, and flowing, it nourishes.","n":"உரை செய்யில் recurs in each line and may mean 'practise' or 'utter', perhaps a mantra. The moon's sphere (நிலாமண்டலம்) is traditionally placed in the head as the source of nectar.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["amudham","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:76"},"t:803":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":803,"t":["நாவின் நுனியை நடுவே சிவிறிடிற்","சீவனும் அங்கே சிவனும் உறைவிடம்","மூவரு முப்பத்து மூவருந் தோன்றுவர்","சாவதும் இல்லை சதகோடி யூனே"],"p":"","r":"If the tip of the tongue is pressed up into the middle, there the soul and Siva dwell; the three gods and the thirty-three appear, and the flesh does not die for a hundred crore of years.","n":"நடுவே is taken as the cavity above the soft palate. சதகோடி யூனே is obscure; 'a hundred crore' may qualify the flesh's life or the gods. The verse places the soul and the Lord together in the body, as 725 does.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","sivam","pasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:76"},"t:804":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":804,"t":["ஊனூறல் பாயும் உயர்வரை உச்சிமேல்","வானூறல் பாயும் வகையறி வாரில்லை","வானூறல் பாயும் வகையறி வாளர்க்குத்","தேனூறல் உண்டு தெளியலு மாமே"],"p":"","r":"On the peak of the high mountain where the body's juices flow, no one knows how the juice of the sky flows; for those who know it there is honey to drink, and clarity.","n":"ஊனூறல் (flesh-juice) and வானூறல் (sky-juice) set bodily secretion against the nectar from above; the mountain peak is taken as the head. தெளியலு மாமே can mean 'one gains clarity' or 'one may drink it clear'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["amudham","yogam","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:76"},"t:805":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":805,"t":["மேலையண் ணவில் விரைந்திரு காலிடிற்","காலனும் இல்லை கதவுந் திறந்திடும்","ந்யாலம் அறிய நரைதிரை மாறிடும்","பாலனு மாவான் பராநந்தி ஆணையே"],"p":"","r":"If the two breaths are set swiftly in the upper palate, there is no Death and the door opens; as the world sees, grey hair and wrinkles change and one becomes a child: by the command of supreme Nandi.","n":"இரு கால் is read as the two breaths, and the door that opens as the opening at the crown. The last two lines repeat 734 almost word for word, where the means is union with a woman.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["praanan","kootru","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:76"},"t:806":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":806,"t":["நந்தி முதலாக நாமேலே யேறிட்டுச்","சந்தித் திருக்கில் தரணி முழுதாளும்","பந்தித் திருக்கும் பகலோன் வெளியாகச்","சிந்தித் திருப்பவர் தீவினை யாளரே"],"p":"","r":"Raising the tongue up, beginning from Nandi, and staying joined there, one rules the whole earth; those who sit thinking of the bound sun as outside them are doers of evil.","n":"நந்தி முதலாக may mean 'with Nandi as the first' (invoking the teacher) or name a place in the body. Lines 3 and 4 are obscure; they may fault those who seek the light outside instead of within.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","vinai","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:76"},"t:807":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":807,"t":["தீவினை யாடத் திகைத்தங் கிருந்தவர்","நாவினை நாடின் நமனுக் கிடமில்லை","பாவினை நாடிப் பயனறக் கண்டவர்","தேவினை யாடிய தீங்கரும் பாமே"],"p":"","r":"Those who stood bewildered while evil deeds danced, if they seek the tongue, leave Yama no room; those who sought verse and saw its fruit through became sweet cane that danced the divine dance.","n":"The rhymes நாவினை, பாவினை, தேவினை drive the verse more than its sense. பாவினை may be verse or sin; தீங்கரும்பு (sweet cane) sets up the next verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","kootru","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:76"},"t:808":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":808,"t":["தங்கரும் பாகவே செய்தொழி லுள்ளவர்","ஆங்கரும் பாக அடையநா வேறிட்டுக்","கோங்கரும் பாகிய கோணை நிமிர்த்திட","ஊங்கரும் பாகியே ஊனீர் வருமே"],"p":"","r":"Those whose practice is to make it sweet cane raise the tongue fully to its shoot there, and as they straighten the bent bud, the body's water comes rising like cane.","n":"The cane image plays on கரும்பு and அரும்பு (bud). The bent thing straightened (கோணை நிமிர்த்திட) may be the curled tongue or the coiled kundalini; the verse does not say.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["amudham","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:76"},"t:809":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":809,"t":["ஊனீர் வழியாக வுண்ணாவை யேறிட்டுத்","தேனீர் பருகிச் சிவாய நமவென்று","கானீர் வரும்வழி கங்கை தருவிக்கும்","வானீர் வரும்வழி வாய்ந்தறி வீரே"],"p":"","r":"Raise the tongue by the way of the body's water, drink the honey-water and say Sivaya nama; the way the breath's water comes will bring the Ganga: know well the way the sky's water comes.","n":"சிவாய நம is the five-syllable mantra, here joined to the bodily practice. கானீர் is read as கால் நீர், water of the breath, and the Ganga as the nectar descending as it falls on Siva's head.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["amudham","mandhiram","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:76"},"t:810":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":810,"t":["வாய்ந்தறிந் துள்ளே வழிபாடு செய்தவர்","காய்ந்தறி வாகக் கருணை பொழிந்திடும்","பாய்ந்தறிந் துள்ளே படிக்கத வொன்றிட்டுக்","கூய்ந்தறிந் துள்ளுறை கோயிலு மாமே"],"p":"","r":"For those who know it rightly and worship within, grace pours down as purified knowledge; entering and closing one door of the steps, knowing it within, the place of his dwelling becomes a temple.","n":"Worship is moved inside the body. காய்ந்தறிவாக may mean knowledge burnt clean of impurity. The door (படிக்கதவு) recurs in 820; a variant reads கோய்ந்தறிந்.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","thiruvarul","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:76"},"t:811":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":811,"t":["கோயிலின் உள்ளே குடிசெய்து வாழ்பவர்","தாயினும் நல்லார் தரணி முழுதுக்குங்","காயினும் நல்லவர் காய்ந்தவர் தம்முளுந்","தீயினுந் தீயரத் தீவினை யாளர்க்கே"],"p":"","r":"Those who make their home inside the temple are better than a mother to the whole world, good as ripe fruit even among those who are hostile to them, and worse than fire to doers of evil.","n":"காயினும் நல்லவர் plays on காய் (unripe fruit, or to burn with anger): they are good even when bitter, or good even to those who burn against them. The verse makes the inward yogi a moral presence in the world, gentle and severe by turns.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","saandror","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:76"},"t:812":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":812,"t":["தீவினை யாளர்த்தஞ் சென்னியி லுள்ளவன்","பூவினை யாளர்த்தம் பொற்பதி யானவன்","பாவினை யாளர்த்தம் பாகவத் துள்ளவன்","மாவினை யாளர்த்தம் மதியிலுள் ளானே"],"p":"","r":"He is on the heads of the doers of evil, the golden abode of those who worship with flowers, within the devotion of those who sing verse, and in the minds of those of great deeds.","n":"சென்னியிலுள்ளவன் (on the head) may mean he stands over evildoers as their judge, or that he is present even in them. பாகவத்து and மதி (mind or moon) can each be read more than one way.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:76"},"t:813":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":813,"t":["மதியி நெழுங்கதிர் போலப் பதினாறாய்ப்","பதிமனை நூறு நூற் றிருபத்து நாலாய்க்","கதிமனை யுள்ளே கணைகள் பரப்பி","எதிர்மலை யாமல் இருந்தனன் தானே"],"p":"","r":"Like rays rising from the moon he is sixteen, and in the lord's house a hundred and a hundred and twenty-four; spreading his arrows within the house of motion, he remained without opposing.","n":"The sixteen are taken as the moon's kalas; the other numbers and the houses (பதிமனை, கதிமனை) have no fixed reading. A variant replaces lines 2 and 3 with a different count.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:76"},"t:814":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":814,"t":["இருந்தனள் சத்தியு மக்கலை சூழ","இருந்தனள் கன்னியு மந்நடு வாக","இருந்தனள் மானேர் முகநில வார","இருந்தனள் தானும் அமுதம் பொழிந்தே"],"p":"","r":"Shakti sat with those kalas around her; the maiden sat in their midst; she sat with a deer-like face shining like the moon; she sat pouring nectar.","n":"கன்னி (the maiden) is a name of Shakti. The kalas are those of 813, so she is the moon in the head from which nectar flows. இருந்தனள் opens every line.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","amudham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:76"},"t:815":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":815,"t":["பொழிந்த இருவெள்ளி பொன்மண் ணடையில்","வழிந்துள் ளிருந்தது வான்முத லங்குக்","கழிந்தது போகாமற் காக்கவல் லார்க்குக்","கொழுந்தது வாகுண்- குணமது தனே"],"p":"","r":"The two silvers poured into the golden earthen enclosure flowed down and stayed within, beginning from the sky; for those who can guard what has gone so that it does not leave, it becomes a shoot.","n":"Silver (வெள்ளி) is traditionally read as the white bindu or the nectar, and gold as the body or Shakti, as in the alchemical language of 834. The last line is garbled.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vindhu","adakkam","amudham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:76"},"t:816":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":816,"t":["குணமது வாகிய கோமள வல்லி","மணமது வாக மகிழ்ந்தங் கிருக்கில்","தனமது வாகிய தத்துவ ஞானம்","இனமது வாக இருந்தனன் தானே"],"p":"","r":"When the tender creeper who is that quality stays there gladly as fragrance, the knowledge of the tattvas, which is wealth, stays with him as kin.","n":"கோமளவல்லி (tender creeper) is a name of Shakti. Who 'he' is in இருந்தனன் is not said: the Lord or the yogi.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","gnaanam","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:76"},"t:817":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":817,"t":["இருந்த பிராணனும் உள்ளே எழுமாம்","பரிந்தஇத் தண்டுடன் அண்டம் பரிய","விரிந்தஅப் பூவுடன் மேலெழ வைக்கின்","மலர்ந்தது மண்டலம் வாழலு மாமே"],"p":"","r":"The breath that rested will rise within; as this staff and the cosmos above are joined, if one makes it rise with that blossomed flower, the circle blossoms and one may live.","n":"தண்டு (staff) is taken as the spine and அண்டம் as the head; the flower and the மண்டலம் may be a centre of the body. பரிந்த and பரிய are unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["praanan","yogam","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:76"},"t:818":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":818,"t":["மண்டலத் துள்ளே மனவொட்டி யாணத்தைக்","கண்டகத் தங்கே கருதியே கீழ்க்கட்டிப்","பண்டகத் துள்ளே பகலே ஒளியாகக்","குண்டலக் காதனுங் கூத்தொழிந் தானே"],"p":"","r":"Seeing the mind's girdle within the circle, binding it below with intent, so that daylight shines in the vessel within, the one with ear-rings ceased his dance.","n":"ஒட்டியாணம் (girdle) may point to the abdominal lock of hatha yoga (uddiyana). குண்டலக் காதன் (the one with ear-rings) is usually Siva, and his ceasing to dance is read as the stilling of movement; it may also be the jiva.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["manam","yogam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:76"},"t:819":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":819,"t":["ஒழிகின்ற வாயுவும் உள்ளே அமருங்","கழிகின்ற வாயுவுங் காக்கலு மாகும்","வழிகின்ற காலத்து வட்டக் கழலைப்","பழிக்கின்ற காலத்துப் பையகற் றீரே"],"p":"","r":"The breath that is passing away will settle within, and the breath that is going out can be guarded; when it flows, at the time it scorns the round anklet, drive off the bag.","n":"Lines 1 and 2 are plain; lines 3 and 4 are not. வட்டக் கழல் (round anklet) may be Siva's feet, and பை (bag) the body or a sheath.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["praanan","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:76"},"t:820":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":820,"t":["பையினி நுள்ளே படிக்கத வொன்றிடின்","மெய்யினிநுள்ளே விளங்கும் ஒளியதாங்","கையினுள் வாயுக் கதித்தங் கெழுந்திடின்","மையணி கோயில் மணிவிளக் காமே"],"p":"","r":"If one door of the steps is closed inside the bag, light will shine within the body; if the breath rises swiftly there, the temple daubed with black becomes lit with a gem lamp.","n":"பை (bag) and மையணி கோயில் (a temple smeared dark, perhaps with soot) are read as the body, which held breath turns into a lamp-lit shrine. Which door is closed is not said.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["praanan","yaakkai","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:76"},"t:821":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":821,"t":["விளங்கிடும் வாயுவை மேலெழ உன்னி","நலங்கிடுங் கண்டத்து நாபியி நுள்ளே","வணங்கிடு மண்டலம் வாய்த்திடக் கும்பிச்","சுணங்கிட நின்றவை சொல்லலு மாமே"],"p":"","r":"Willing the shining breath to rise, within the navel and in the throat where it is pressed, holding it so that the revered circle is gained, one can tell of what stood withering.","n":"கும்பி is read as கும்பகம், holding the breath. A variant replaces line 2 with one naming காமம் and நாடி. The last line is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["praanan","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:76"},"t:822":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":822,"t":["சொல்லலு மாயிடு மாகத்து வாயுவுஞ்f","சொல்லலு மாகு மண்ணீர்க் கடினமுஞ்f","சொல்லலு மாகும் இவையஞ்சுங் கூடிடிற்","சொல்லலு மாந்தூர தெரிசனந் தானே"],"p":"","r":"One can speak of the breath and the sky, and of the hardness of earth and water; when these five come together, one can speak of seeing far.","n":"The five are taken as the elements (earth, water, fire implied, air, sky). தூர தெரிசனம் (far sight) is one of the yogic powers. Lines 1 and 2 are marked corrupt in the source.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["siththi","thaththuvam","praanan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:76"},"t:823":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":823,"t":["தூர தெரிசனஞ் f சொல்லுவன் காணலாங்","காராருங் கண்ணி கடைன்யான முட்பெய்தி","ஏராருந் தீபத் தெழிற்சிந்தை வைத்திடிற்","பாரா ருலகம் பகன்முன்ன தாமே"],"p":"","r":"I shall tell of far sight, and it can be seen: placing the dark-eyed one within in meditation and setting the mind on the lovely lamp, the whole earth is before one as in daylight.","n":"காராருங் கண்ணி (the one with dark eyes) is taken as Shakti, and கடைன்யானம் as a garbled கடை தியானம் or ஞானம். Line 1 is marked corrupt.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["siththi","saththi","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:76"},"t:824":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":824,"t":["முன்னெழு நாபிக்கு முந்நால் விரற்கீழே","பன்னெழு வேதப் பகலொளி யுண்டென்னும்","நன்னெழு நாதத்து நற்றீபம் வைத்திடத்","தன்னெழு கோயில் தலைவனு மாமே"],"p":"","r":"Twelve finger-breadths below the navel that rises first there is, they say, the daylight of the many Vedas; set a good lamp in the rising nada there and one becomes lord of the temple that arises in oneself.","n":"முந்நால் (three fours) is read as twelve; whether the place is below or above the navel depends on how முன்னெழு and கீழே are taken. The body as a temple that rises within closes the chapter.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naadham","yogam","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:76"},"t:825":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":825,"t":["பூசு வனவெல்லாம் பூசிப் புலர்த்திய","வாச நறுங்குழல் மாலையுஞ்f சாத்திக்","காயக் குழலி கலவி யொடுங்கலந்","தூசித் துளையுறத் தூங்காது போகமே"],"p":"","r":"Having anointed with all the unguents, dried and dressed her fragrant hair with garlands, uniting in embrace with the woman so that the needle's eye is reached, the enjoyment is one without sleep.","n":"ஊசித் துளை (the needle's eye) is taken as the opening of the central channel, and தூங்காது (without sleeping, or without slackening) as the alertness of the practice. The last word has variants (யோகமே, போதமே, மோகமே) that turn the line toward yoga, knowledge or passion; line 2 is marked corrupt.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["punarchi","kaamam","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:77"},"t:826":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":826,"t":["போகத்தை யுன்னவே போகாது வாயுவு","மோகத்தை வெள்ளியு மீளும் வியாழத்தில்","சூதொத்த மென்முலை யாளுநற் சூதனுந்","தாதிற் குழைந்து தலைகண்ட வாறே"],"p":"","r":"When the enjoyment is willed, the breath does not leave and the silver turns back from passion at Jupiter; the woman with soft breasts like dice-boxes and the good man melted together in the essence and reached the head.","n":"வெள்ளி (silver, also Venus) is traditionally the semen and வியாழம் (Jupiter, also gold) the point of return; the planet names do double duty as code. சூதன் may mean the man or mercury, the alchemist's metal. தலைகண்ட is read as the essence rising to the head.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","vindhu","praanan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:77"},"t:827":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":827,"t":["கண்டனுங் கண்டியுங் காதல்செய் யோகத்து","மாண்டலங் கொண்டிரு பாலும் வெளிநிற்கும்","வண்டியை மேற்கொண்டு வானீர் உருட்டிடத்","தண்டொரு காலுந் தளராது அங்கமே"],"p":"","r":"In the yoga where man and woman love, holding the circle, both stand out on either side; taking up the cart and rolling the sky's water, the staff and the limbs never once slacken.","n":"வண்டி (cart), வானீர் (sky-water) and தண்டு (staff, the spine or the organ) are figures with no fixed reading. The plain claim is that the practice does not weaken the body.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","yogam","amudham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:77"},"t:828":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":828,"t":["அங்கப் புணர்ச்சியு மாகின்ற தத்துவ","மங்கத்தில் விந்து வருகின்ற போகத்துப்","பங்கப் படாமற் பரிகரித் துத்தம்மைத்","தங்கிக் கொடுக்கத் தலைவனு மாமே"],"p":"","r":"In the union of bodies, the tattva that forms, the bindu that comes in the enjoyment within the body: guarding it so that it is not spoiled, holding oneself and giving, one becomes lord.","n":"பங்கப்படாமல் பரிகரித்து (guarding so it is not ruined) is taken as keeping the bindu from being lost. தங்கிக் கொடுக்க (staying and giving) is unclear: perhaps restraint on one side and pleasure given on the other.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vindhu","punarchi","adakkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:77"},"t:829":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":829,"t":["தலைவனு மாயிடுந் தன்வழி ஞானந்","தலைவனு மாயிடுந் தன்வழி போகந்","தலைவனு மாயிடுந் தன்வழி யுள்ளே","தலைவனு மாயிடுந் தன்வழி அஞ்சே"],"p":"","r":"He becomes lord: knowledge goes his way, enjoyment goes his way, what is within goes his way, the five go his way.","n":"தன்வழி can mean 'under his own control' or 'by his own path'; the five are taken as the senses. The verse makes mastery, not pleasure, the fruit of the practice.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","gnaanam","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:77"},"t:830":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":830,"t":["அஞ்சு கடிகைமேல் ஆறாங் கடிகையில்","துஞ்சுவ தொன்றத் துணைவி துணைவன்பால்","நெஞ்சு நிறைந்தது வாய்கொளா தென்றது","பஞ்ச கடிகை பரியங்க யோகமே"],"p":"","r":"Past five nazhigai, in the sixth, when the two fall still together and the wife's heart toward her husband is so full that she says her mouth cannot hold it: the five nazhigai are paryanka yoga.","n":"A நாழிகை is twenty-four minutes, so five is two hours. துஞ்சுவது ஒன்ற is read as the two coming to stillness together. A variant swaps மேல் and இல், which changes which nazhigai is meant.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","yogam","kaalam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:77"},"t:831":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":831,"t":["பரியங்க யோகத்துப் பஞ்ச கடிகை","அரியஇவ் வியோகம் அடைந்தவர்க் கல்லது","சரிவளை முன்கைச்சி சந்தனக் கொங்கை","உருவித் தழுவ ஒருவற்கொண் ணாதே"],"p":"","r":"Except for those who have reached this rare yoga of the five nazhigai, no man can strip and embrace the woman with bangles on her forearm and sandal on her breasts.","n":"ஒண்ணாதே may be 'cannot' (it is beyond them) or 'must not' (it is forbidden them). Either way the union is restricted to the adept, not offered to all.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","yogam","adakkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:77"},"t:832":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":832,"t":["ஒண்ணாத யோகத்தை உற்றவ ராரென்னில்","விண்ணந்த கங்கை விரிசடை வைத்தவன்","பண்ணார் அமுதினைப் பஞ்ச கடிகையில்","எண்ணா மெனஎண்ணி இருந்தான் இருந்தே"],"p":"","r":"If you ask who has attained this impossible yoga: he who set the sky's Ganga in his spread locks sat steady in the five nazhigai, holding the melodious nectar in mind.","n":"Siva, the Ganga-bearer, is made the model practitioner, joined with Shakti. எண்ணா மென எண்ணி is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","yogam","amudham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:77"},"t:833":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":833,"t":["ஏய்ந்த பிராயம் இருபது முப்பதும்","வாய்ந்த குழலிக்கு மன்னர்க்கு மானந்தம்","வாய்ந்த குழலியோ டைந்து மலர்ந்திடச்","சோர்ந்தன சித்தமுன்ய் சோர்வில்லை வெள்ளிக்கே"],"p":"","r":"At the fitting ages of twenty and thirty there is bliss for the woman and for the man; as the five blossom with the woman the mind slackened, but there is no slackening of the silver.","n":"The ages are usually taken as twenty for the woman and thirty for the man. மன்னர் (kings) stands for the men. The contrast is that the mind may give way in pleasure while the bindu is held. Line 4 is garbled (சித்தமுன்ய்).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aanandham","vindhu","punarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:77"},"t:834":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":834,"t":["வெள்ளி யுருகிப் பொன்வழி ஓடாமே","கள்ளத்தட் டானார் கரியிட்டு மூடினார்","கொள்ளி பறியக் குழல்வழி யேசென்று","வள்ளியுண் ணாவில் அடக்கிவைத் தாரே"],"p":"","r":"So that the silver does not melt and run off by the path of the gold, the cunning goldsmiths covered it with charcoal; as the brand was pulled away they went by the tube and held it fast in Valli's tongue.","n":"Goldsmith's craft stands for the practice: silver as bindu, gold as the woman's part or the body, the tube (குழல்) as a channel. கள்ளத் தட்டார் (thieving goldsmiths) may praise their cunning. வள்ளியுண் ணாவில் has no settled reading; it may be the tongue of the fire.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vindhu","adakkam","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:77"},"t:835":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":835,"t":["வைத்த இருவருந் தம்மின் மகிழ்ந்துடன்","சித்தங் கலங்காது செய்கின்ற ஆனந்தம்","பத்து வகைக்கும் பதினெண் கணத்துக்கும்","வித்தக நாய்நிற்கும் வெங்கதி ரோனே"],"p":"","r":"The two who set it there, rejoicing in each other with minds undisturbed, make a bliss; for the ten kinds and the eighteen hosts the burning sun stands as the master.","n":"The ten kinds may be the ten breaths and the eighteen கணம் the classes of celestial beings. The sun (வெங்கதிரோன்) may be the right-hand breath or Siva. Line 1 is clear; the rest is not.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aanandham","punarchi","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:77"},"t:836":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":836,"t":["வெங்கதி ருக்குஞ் சனிக்கும் இடைநின்ற","நங்கையைப் புல்லிய நம்பிக்கோ ரானந்தந்","தங்களிற் பொன்னிடை வெள்ளிதா ழாமுனந்","தங்களிற் செவ்வாய் புதைத்திருந் தாரே"],"p":"","r":"For the man who embraced the woman standing between the hot sun and Saturn there is a bliss; before the silver sank into the gold between them, they hid Mars between them.","n":"Planet names again stand for bodily things: செவ்வாய் (Mars, the red one) may be the red essence, sun and Saturn the channels or body points. A variant ends புதனிருந் தானே. No reading is secure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","vindhu","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:77"},"t:837":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":837,"t":["திருத்திப் புதனைத் திருத்தல்செய் வார்க்குக்","கருத்தழ காலே கலந்தங் கிருக்கில்","வருத்தமு மில்லையா மங்கை பங்கற்குந்","துருத்தியுள் வெள்ளியஞ் சோரா தெழுமே"],"p":"","r":"For those who keep Mercury corrected, if they stay mingled with the beauty of intent, there is no strain even for the one who shares himself with a woman, and the silver in the bellows rises without flagging.","n":"புதன் (Mercury) may also be mercury the metal, as in alchemy. மங்கை பங்கன் (he who shares himself with a woman) is a name of Siva, here perhaps the practitioner. துருத்தி (bellows) is taken as the lungs or the body.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vindhu","adakkam","praanan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:77"},"t:838":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":838,"t":["எழுகின்ற தீயை முன்னே கொண்டு சென்றிட்டால்","மெழுகுரு கும்பரி செய்திடும் மெய்யே","உழுகின்ற தில்லை ஔயை அறிந்தபின்","விழுகின்ற தில்லை வெளியறி வார்க்கே"],"p":"","r":"If the rising fire is led on ahead, it truly works on the body as melting does on wax; after one knows the mother there is no more toil, and for those who know the space there is no falling.","n":"ஔயை is read as அவ்வை, the mother, Shakti. Falling (விழுகின்றது) may be the fall of the bindu or the fall into birth; வெளி (the open space) is taken as the void known in the head or the space of awareness.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","vindhu","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:77"},"t:839":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":839,"t":["வெளியை அறிந்து வெளியி னடுவே","ஒளியை அறியி நுளிமுறி யாமே","தெளிவை அறிந்து செழுநந்தி யாலே","வெளியை அறிந்தனன் மேலறி யேனே"],"p":"","r":"Knowing the space and, in the middle of the space, knowing the light, the chisel does not break; knowing clarity through gracious Nandi, I knew the space, and beyond that I know nothing.","n":"உளிமுறியாமே (the chisel will not break) is obscure, and a variant reads உளியை. மேலறியேனே can mean 'I know nothing more' in humility or 'there is nothing beyond to know'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["meyyunarvu","guru","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:77"},"t:840":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":840,"t":["மேலாந் தலத்தில் விரிந்தவ ராரெனின்","மாலாந் திசைமுகன் மாநந்தி யாயவர்","நாலா நிலத்தி நடுவான வப்பொருள்","மேலா யுரைத்தனர் மின்னிடை யாளுக்கே"],"p":"","r":"If you ask who spread out in the high place: Mal, the four-faced one and great Nandi; that thing in the middle of the four grounds they explained above to the woman with a lightning-thin waist.","n":"The four grounds (நாலா நிலம்) may be centres of the body or levels of being. மின்னிடையாள் is usually Shakti. The verse ties the teaching to the gods, but its sense is unsure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:77"},"t:841":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":841,"t":["மின்னிடை யாளுமின் னாளனுங் கூட்டத்துப்","பொன்னிடை வட்டத்தின் உள்ளே புகப்பெய்து","தன்னொடு தன்னை தலைப்பெய்ய வல்லாரேன்","மண்ணிடைப் பல்லூழி வாழலு மாமே"],"p":"","r":"If, in the union of the lightning-waisted woman and her man, they pour it into the golden circle and can join self with self, one may live on earth for many ages.","n":"பொன்னிடை வட்டம் (the golden circle) is unexplained. தன்னொடு தன்னை தலைப்பெய்ய (to join self with self) may be the self reaching itself or the two partners becoming one. A variant reads வல்லீரே, addressing the reader.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","vindhu","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:77"},"t:842":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":842,"t":["வாங்க லிறுதலை வாங்கலில் வாங்கிய","வீங்க வலிக்கும் விரகறி வாரில்லை","வீங்க வலிக்கும் விரகறி வாளரும்","ஓங்கிய தன்னை உதம்பண்ணி னாரே"],"p":"","r":"No one knows the skill of drawing it up so that it swells, at the drawing's end; those who know that skill raised themselves on high.","n":"வாங்கல் (drawing in) may be drawing up the bindu or the breath. உதம் is obscure; it is read here as rising. The verse is a riddle and this reading is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","vindhu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:77"},"t:843":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":843,"t":["உதமறிந் தங்கே ஒருசுழிப் பட்டாற்","கதமறிந் தங்கே கபாலங் கறுக்கும்","இதமறிந் தென்றும் இருப்பாள் ஒருத்தி","பதமறிந் தும்முளே பார்க்கடிந் தாளே"],"p":"","r":"Knowing the rising, if one is caught there in a whirl, and knowing the fury there, the skull grows dark; one woman who knows the good stays always, and knowing the moment, she drove off the world within you.","n":"சுழி (whirl) and கபாலம் கறுக்கும் (the skull darkens) may warn of danger in the practice. ஒருத்தி (one woman) is taken as Shakti. பார்க்கடிந்தாளே may mean she removed the world or she guarded you; the reading is unsure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:77"},"t:844":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":844,"t":["பாரில்லை நீரில்லை பங்கயம் ஒன்றுண்டு","தாரில்லை வேரில்லை தாமரை பூத்தது","ஊரில்லை காணும் ஒளியது ஒன்றுண்டு","கீழில்லை மேலில்லை கேள்வியிற் பூவே"],"p":"","r":"There is no earth and no water, yet there is a lotus; no stalk, no root, yet the lotus bloomed; there is no town, yet there is a light one sees; no below and no above: it is a flower in hearing.","n":"A riddle of negatives: a lotus without ground, water or root, taken as the lotus of the crown or of awareness. கேள்வியிற் பூ may be 'a flower known by hearing', from the teacher, or a flower in the sound of nada. A variant reads ஒன்றில்லை.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["meyyunarvu","yogam","kelvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:77"},"t:845":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":845,"t":["உடலிற் கிடந்த வுறுதிக் குடிநீர்க்","கடலிற் சிறுகிணற் றேற்றமிட் டாலொக்கும்","உடலில் ஒருவழி ஒன்றுக் கிறைக்கில்","நடலைப் படாதுயிர் நாடலு மாமே"],"p":"","r":"The strengthening water that lies in the body is like a well-sweep set to draw from the sea; if one draws it by the one way in the body for the one purpose, life is spared distress and can seek its end.","n":"அமுரி in the title is commonly taken as the body's own urine, and the chapter as a teaching on drinking it (amaroli); some readers take the water symbolically as the nectar of yoga. The sea and small well-sweep may mean the supply is ample beside the small draw, but the image is not explained. ஒருவழி ஒன்றுக்கு (one way, for one thing) is compressed, and உயிர் நாடலும் may mean 'life can seek' or 'one can attain life'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["marundhu","yaakkai","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:78"},"t:846":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":846,"t":["தெளிதரும் இந்தச் சிவநீர் பருகில்","ஒளிதரு மோராண்டில் ஊனமொன் றில்லை","வளியுறும் எட்டின் மனமும் ஒடுங்குங்","களிதருங் காயங் கனகம தாமே"],"p":"","r":"If one drinks this clarifying Siva-water, within a year the body shines without any defect; with breath held in the eight the mind too subsides, and the body, giving joy, becomes gold.","n":"சிவநீர் (Siva-water) is the chapter's reverent name for the water. வளியுறும் எட்டின் is obscure: the eight may be the eight limbs of yoga or a measure of breath. காயம் கனகம் (the body as gold) is the alchemists' image of the perfected, undecaying body.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["marundhu","yaakkai","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:78"},"t:847":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":847,"t":["நூறு மிளகு நுகருஞ் சிவத்தினீர்","மாறும் இதற்கு மருந்தில்லை மாந்தர்கள்","தேறில் இதனைத் தெளியுச்சி கப்பிடின்","மாறும் இதற்கு மறுமயி ராமே"],"p":"","r":"Siva's water taken with a hundred peppercorns has no medicine to rival it; if people understand it and apply it to the crown of the head, the hair changes and grows anew.","n":"மாறும் opens two lines in different senses: a rival (no medicine rivals this) and change (the hair changes). நுகரும் may mean the water is drunk with the pepper or that pepper is steeped in it; the verse doesn't say which. மறுமயிர் is read as new hair.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["marundhu","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:78"},"t:848":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":848,"t":["கரையரு கேநின்ற கானல் உவரி","வரைவரை என்பர் மதியிலா மாந்தர்","நுரைதிரை நீக்கி நுகரவல் லார்க்கு","நரைதிரை மாறு நமனுமங் கில்லையே"],"p":"","r":"Witless people say the salt water of the shore, near the bank, is to be avoided; for those who can drink it with the froth and scum removed, grey hair and wrinkles change, and Yama is not there either.","n":"கானல் உவரி (brackish water of the shore) is taken as the same water under a disguising name; வரைவரை is read as 'avoid it, avoid it'. நரைதிரை (grey hair and wrinkles) and நமன் (Yama) make the promise one of youth and deathlessness, as in the body-perfecting chapters of this tantra.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["marundhu","kootru","pedhaimai","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:78"},"t:849":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":849,"t":["அளக நன்னுத லாயோ ரதிசயங்","களவு காயங் கலந்தஇந் நீரிலே","மிளகு நெல்லியும் மஞ்சளும் வேம்பிடில்","இளகும் மேனி இருளுங் கபாலமே"],"p":"","r":"O you with curling hair and fair forehead, a wonder: if pepper, gooseberry, turmeric and neem are put into this water mixed with the hidden body, the body grows supple and the head grows dark.","n":"The address to a woman (அளக நன்னுதலாய்) is taken in the tradition as Siva speaking to Shakti, or as a conventional address. களவு காயம் is obscure: காயம் may be the body or a spice such as asafoetida, and களவு 'hidden' or a corrupt word. இருளும் கபாலம் (the skull darkens) is read as the hair turning black. A variant gives இளகிடு for இளகும், with no change of sense.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["marundhu","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:78"},"t:850":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":850,"t":["வீர மருந்தென்றும் விண்ணோர் மருந்தென்றும்","நாரி மருந்தென்றும் நந்தி அருள்செய்தான்","ஆதி மருந்தென் றறிவார் அகலிடஞ்","சோதி மருந்திது சொல்லவொண் ணாதே"],"p":"","r":"Nandi in his grace called it the hero's medicine, the gods' medicine and the woman's medicine; those who know it as the primal medicine know that across the wide earth this medicine of light cannot be told.","n":"The names are given without explanation; நாரி மருந்து (woman's medicine) is unclear, perhaps tied to Shakti. The closing line can mean the medicine is too great for words or that it is not to be told openly, a secret teaching; both stand.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["marundhu","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:78"},"t:851":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":851,"t":["எய்து மதிக்கலை சூக்கத்தி லேறியே","எய்துவ தூலம் இருவகைப் பக்கத்துள்","எய்துங் கலைபோல ஏறி இறங்குமாந்","துய்யது சூக்கத்து தூலத்த காயமே"],"p":"","r":"As the moon's kalas rise and fall through the two fortnights, so the body, pure in its subtle and its gross form, climbs from the subtle into the gross.","n":"மதிக்கலை (the moon's digits) sets the chapter's frame: an inner moon in the body that waxes and wanes like the outer one. How the subtle and gross bodies follow the moon is not explained. A variant reads மதிநிலை for மதிக்கலை.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:79"},"t:852":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":852,"t":["ஆகின்ற சந்திரன் சூரியன் அங்கியுள்","ஆகின்ற ஈரெட்டா டாறிரண் டீரைந்துள்","ஏகின்ற வக்கலை யெல்லா மிடைவழி","ஆகின்ற யோகி அறிந்த அறிவே"],"p":"","r":"The kalas that arise in moon, sun and fire, sixteen, twelve and ten, all go by the middle way; this is the knowledge known to the yogi who comes to be.","n":"இடைவழி may be 'the middle way' (the central channel) or the இடை (ida) channel. Fire is given ten kalas here, but sixty-four in 854 to 856; the two counts are not reconciled.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","naadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:79"},"t:853":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":853,"t":["ஆறாத தாங்கலை ஆதித்தன் சந்திரன்","நாறா நலங்கினார் ஞாலங் கவர்க்கொளப்","பேறாங் கலைமுற்றும் பெருங்கால் ஈரெட்டு","மாறாக் கதிர்க்கொள்ளு மற்றங்கி கூடவே"],"p":"","r":"Sun and moon have kalas that never cease; when fire joins, the unchanging sun takes in all the sixteen kalas of fulfilment through the great breath.","n":"Line 2 (நாறா நலங்கினார் ஞாலங் கவர்க்கொள) I can't construe, and the rest is read only roughly. பெருங்கால் is taken as the great breath.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","praanan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:79"},"t:854":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":854,"t":["பத்தும் இரண்டும் பகலோன் உயர்க்கலை","பத்தினொ டாறும் உயர்க்கலை பான்மதி","ஒத்தநல் அங்கிய தெட்டெட் டுயர்க்கலை","அத்திறன் நின்றமை ஆய்ந்துகொள் வீரே"],"p":"","r":"The sun's high kalas are ten and two, the moon's ten and six, and fitting fire's eight eights; examine how they stand.","n":"Twelve, sixteen and sixty-four, stated as sums and products in the manner of the chapter. The verse only counts; what the kalas are is left to the teacher.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:79"},"t:855":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":855,"t":["எட்டெட் டனிலின் கலையாகும் ஈராறுட்","சுட்டப் படுங்கதி ரோனுக்குஞ் சூழ்கலை","கட்டப் படுமீ ரெட்டா மதிக்கலை","ஒட்டப் படாஇவை ஒன்றோடொன் றாவே"],"p":"","r":"Fire's kalas are eight eights, the kalas round the sun are counted as twelve, and the moon's are bound at sixteen; these are not merged, yet each is joined with the other.","n":"அனிலின் (of the wind) is read as அனலின் (of fire), to agree with 854 and 856. The last line, distinct yet one with another, is compressed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:79"},"t:856":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":856,"t":["எட்டெட்டும் ஈராறும் ஈரெட்டுந் தீக்கதிர்","சுட்டிட்ட சோமனில் தோன்றுங் கலையெனக்","கட்டப் படுந்தார கைகதிர் நாலுள","கட்டிட்ட தொண்ணூற்றொ டாறுங் கலாதியே"],"p":"","r":"Fire's sixty-four, the sun's twelve and the moon's sixteen are the kalas, and with the four rays of the stars the kalas bound together number ninety-six.","n":"64 + 12 + 16 + 4 = 96. Ninety-six is also the number of tattvas in some Tamil Saiva lists, which may be why the total is stated, but the verse does not make that link. A variant reads ஈரெட்டுத் for ஈரெட்டும்.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yogam","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:79"},"t:857":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":857,"t":["எல்லாக் கலையும் இடைபிங் கலைநடுச்","சொல்லா நடுநாடி யூடே தொடர்மூலஞ்","செல்லா எழுப்பிச் சிரத்துடன் சேர்தலால்","நல்லோர் திருவடி நண்ணிநிற் போரே"],"p":"","r":"All the kalas run in ida and pingala, and through the unnamed middle channel between them; rousing the base and joining it with the head, the good attain the holy feet and stand there.","n":"இடை, பிங்கலை and the சொல்லா நடுநாடி (the middle channel not named, taken as sushumna) are the three main nadis. மூலம் is the base centre; rousing it to the head is the traditional account of the kundalini's ascent, though the verse does not name the power.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadi","kundalini","yogam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:79"},"t:858":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":858,"t":["அங்கியிற் சின்னக் கதிரிரண் டாட்டத்துத்","தங்கிய தாரகை யாகுஞ் சசிபானு","வங்கிய தாரகை யாகும் பரையொளி","தங்கு நவசக்ர மாகுந் தரணிக்கே"],"p":"","r":"In fire, the two small rays, sun and moon, with the star that stays in their play, become the light of the supreme power, which dwells as the nine wheels for the world.","n":"பரையொளி (Para's light) and நவசக்ரம் (nine wheels) are technical; the nine are perhaps those listed in 859. The syntax is loose and the reading is a guess at the joints.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:79"},"t:859":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":859,"t":["தரணி சலங்கனல் கால்தக்க வானம்","அரணிய பானு அருந்திங்கள் அங்கி","முரணிய தாரகை முன்னிய ஒன்பான்","பிரணவ மாகும் பெருநெறி தானே"],"p":"","r":"Earth, water, fire, air and fitting space, the guarding sun, the rare moon, fire and the contrary star: these nine become the pranava, the great way.","n":"Five elements, three lights and the star make nine, and the nine are said to be Om. The verse names them; it does not say how they make the syllable. முரணிய (contrary, opposed) of the star is unexplained.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piranavam","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:79"},"t:860":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":860,"t":["தாரகை மின்னுஞ் சசிதேயும் பக்கத்துத்","தாரகை மின்னா சசிவளர் பக்கத்துத்","தாரகை பூவிற் சகலத்தி யோனிகள்","தாரகைத் தாரகை தானான்ய் சொரூபமே"],"p":"","r":"The star shines in the fortnight when the moon wanes and does not shine in the fortnight when it waxes; the star is all the wombs on earth, and the star within the star is one's own true form.","n":"தாரகை can mean a star, the pupil of the eye, or (as tāraka) the mantra that carries one across, traditionally Om; each fits a different line. The last line, the star of the star as one's own form (சொரூபம்), moves from the sky to the self. தானான்ய் is an encoding slip for தானாம்.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["meyyunarvu","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:79"},"t:861":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":861,"t":["முற்பதி னைஞ்சின் முளைத்துப் பெருத்திடும்","பிற்பதி னைஞ்சிற் பெருத்துச் சிறுத்திடும்","அப்பதி னைஞ்சும் அறியவல் லார்க்கட்குச்","செப்பரி யாங்கழல் சேர்தலு மாமே"],"p":"","r":"In the first fifteen days it sprouts and grows, in the latter fifteen, having grown, it shrinks; for those who can know those fifteen, reaching the feet beyond words becomes possible.","n":"Plainly the moon's two fortnights; in the chapter they are the waxing and waning of the inner moon, which the yogi must learn. The verse does not name what grows and shrinks.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:79"},"t:862":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":862,"t":["அங்கி எழுப்பி யருங்கதிர் ஊட்டத்துத்","தங்குஞ் சசியால் தாமம்ஐந் தைந்தாகிப்","பொங்கிய தாரகை யாம்புலன் போக்கறத்","திங்கள் கதிரங்கி சேர்க்கின்ற யோகமே"],"p":"","r":"Rousing the fire and feeding the rare sun, with the moon that stays the garlands become five and five, and as the senses' going ceases at the swelling star, this is the yoga that joins moon, sun and fire.","n":"தாமம் ஐந்தைந்து (five and five garlands, or abodes) is obscure. The clear claim is the last line: the yoga of the chapter is the joining of the three lights, and it stops the senses' outward going.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","adakkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:79"},"t:863":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":863,"t":["ஒன்றிய ஈரெண் கலையும் உடலுற","நின்றது கண்டும் நினைக்கிலர் நீதர்கள்","கன்றிய காலன் கருத்துழி வைத்தபின்","சென்றதில் வீழ்வர் திகைப்பொழி யாரே"],"p":"","r":"Though they see the sixteen kalas joined and abiding in the body, the base do not think of it; once harsh Death has set them where he means, they fall into it and their bewilderment does not end.","n":"நீதர் is read as நீசர் (base men). For கருத்துழி (the place he intends) variants give கருக்குழி (the womb's pit), which would make the fall a rebirth, and கழுக்குறி.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kootru","pedhaimai","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:79"},"t:864":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":864,"t":["அங்கி மதிகூட வாகும் கதிரொளி","அங்கி கதிர்க்கூட வாகு மதியொளி","அங்கி சசிகதிர் கூடவத் தாரகை","தங்கி யதுவே சகலமு மாமே"],"p":"","r":"When fire joins the moon, the sun's light arises; when fire joins the sun, the moon's light; when fire, moon and sun join, that star arises, and what abides there becomes everything.","n":"A symmetrical riddle of the three lights; the joining of all three yields the star, which elsewhere in the chapter is one's own form (860). A variant gives சிவத்தினிற் in the last line, which would place what abides in Siva.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:79"},"t:865":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":865,"t":["ஈராறு பெண்கலை எண்ணிரண் டாண்கலை","பேராமற் புக்குப் பிடித்துக் கொடுவந்து","நேராகத் தோன்றும் நெருப்புற வேபெய்யில்","ஆராத ஆனந்தம் ஆனந்த மானதே"],"p":"","r":"Entering without straying, seizing the twelve female kalas and the sixteen male kalas and bringing them, if one pours them into the fire that appears straight ahead, insatiable bliss becomes bliss itself.","n":"Twelve and sixteen are the sun's and moon's counts from 854, but here twelve are called female and sixteen male, the reverse of the common pairing of moon with the female; the verse does not explain. The pouring into fire borrows the language of the fire rite for an inner act.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aanandham","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:79"},"t:866":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":866,"t":["காணும் பரிதியின் காலை இடத்திட்டு","மாணும் மதியதன் காலை வலத்திட்டுப்","பேணியே யிவ்வாறு பிழையாமற் செய்வீரேல்","ஆணி கலங்காதவ் வாயிரத் தாண்டே"],"p":"","r":"Set the breath of the visible sun on the left and the breath of the great moon on the right; if you do this with care and without error, the axle-pin will not shake for a thousand years.","n":"காலை may be கால் (breath) with the object ending, or 'morning'. The sun's breath usually runs on the right and the moon's on the left, so the verse prescribes a reversal, as tradition reads it. ஆணி (the axle-pin) is taken as the body or life that holds.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["praanan","naadi","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:79"},"t:867":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":867,"t":["பாலிக்கும் நெஞ்சம் பறையோசை ஒன்பதில்","ஆலிக்கும் அங்கே அமரர் பராபரன்","மேலைக்கு முன்னே விளக்கொளி யாய்நிற்குங்","காலைக்குச் சங்கு கதிரவன் தானே"],"p":"","r":"The nourishing heart resounds with the drum's sound in the nine; there the Supreme of the gods stands as lamplight before what is above, and for the dawn the sun itself is the conch.","n":"The inner sounds heard in yoga are often listed as drum, conch and others. ஒன்பது may be the nine gates or nine sounds. A variant gives பரையோசை (the sound of Para) for பறையோசை (the drum's sound).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naadham","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:79"},"t:868":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":868,"t":["கதிரவன் சந்திரன் காலம் அளக்கும்","பொதிரவ னுள்ளே பொழிமழை நிற்கும்","அதிரவ னண்டப் புறஞ்சென் றடர்ப்ப","எதிரவ நீச நிடமது தானே"],"p":"","r":"Sun and moon measure time; within the covering the pouring rain stays; when it thunders and presses beyond the shell of the cosmos, that is the place of the Lord.","n":"பொதிரவன், அதிரவன் and the last line (எதிரவ நீச நிடமது) look like puns on ரவம் (sound) and may be corrupt; ஈசன் இடம் is read from நீச நிடம். The inner rain is often taken as the nectar of yoga and the thunder as nada.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","sivam","naadham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:79"},"t:869":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":869,"t":["உந்திக் கமலத் துதித்தெழுஞ் சோதியை","அந்திக்கு மந்திர மாரும் அறிகிலார்","அந்திக்கு மந்திர மாரும் அறிந்தபின்","தந்தைக்கு முன்னே மகன்பிறந் தானே"],"p":"","r":"No one knows the mantra that meets the light rising from the navel lotus; once they know that mantra, the son is born before the father.","n":"அந்திக்கும் (meets, joins) also echoes அந்தி, the twilight rite. The son born before the father is a riddle of reversal that the verse doesn't solve, and I can't fix whom they stand for. Variants change lines 2 and 3 to 'they did not know by knowledge how it met' and the end to மகன் இருந்தானே (the son was there).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:79"},"t:870":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":870,"t":["ஊதியம் ஏதும் அறியார் உரைப்பினும்","ஓதியும் ஏதும் அறியாத ஊமர்கள்","ஆதியும் அந்தமும் அந்திக்க வல்லீரேல்","வேதியன் அங்கே வெளிப்படுந் தானே"],"p":"","r":"Even when they speak they know no gain, and even having recited they are mutes who know nothing; if you can join the beginning and the end, the Lord of the Veda will appear there.","n":"அந்திக்க plays on the twilight worship at dawn and dusk (சந்தி) and an inner joining of beginning and end, perhaps of the breaths or of the moon's course; the verse sets recitation against this inner act. வேதியன் may be the Lord of the Veda or a true Brahmin.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","sivam","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:79"},"t:871":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":871,"t":["பாம்பு மதியைத் தினலுறும் பாம்பினைத்","தங்கு கதிரையஞ் சோதித் தனலுறும்","பாம்பு மதியும் பகைதீர்த் துடங்கொளீஇ","நீண்-கல் கொடானே நெடுந்தகை யானே"],"p":"","r":"The serpent seeks to devour the moon, and the sun's light seeks to burn the serpent; the great one, ending the enmity of serpent and moon and holding them together, will not let them part.","n":"The eclipse serpent swallowing the moon is turned inward: the serpent is traditionally the kundalini and the moon the moon in the head. Line 2 and line 4 (நீண்-கல் கொடானே) are corrupt or obscure, and the variants (துடங்கொளின், கொடானெம்) make the great one either the Lord or the yogi who does this.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kundalini","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:79"},"t:872":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":872,"t":["அயின்றது வீழ்வள வுந்துயில் இன்றிப்","பயின்ற சசிவீழ் பொழுதில் துயின்று","நயந்தரு பூரணை உள்ள நடத்தி","வியந்தரு பூரணை மேவுன்ய் சசியே"],"p":"","r":"Not sleeping until what was eaten has settled, sleeping when the practised moon goes down, and carrying within the good fullness, the wondrous fullness reaches the moon.","n":"A regimen of sleep and food. Whether the moon is the outer moon or the inner one, and பூரணை the full moon or the filling of breath, both readings stand. மேவுன்ய் is an encoding slip, read as மேவும்.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","undi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:79"},"t:873":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":873,"t":["சசியுதிக் குமஅள வுந்துயி இன்றிச்","சசியுதித் தானேல் தனதூண் அருந்திச்","சசிசரிக் கின்றள வுந்துயி லாமற்","சசிசரிப் பிங்கட்டன் கண்டுயில் கொண்டதே"],"p":"","r":"Not sleeping until the moon rises; when the moon has risen, eating one's food; not sleeping while the moon moves across; and sleeping once the moon has gone down.","n":"The rule is clear in outline, but again the moon may be outer or inner. பிங்கட்டன் in the last line is obscure; a variant reads பிங்கட் டன்றுயில்.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","undi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:79"},"t:874":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":874,"t":["ஊழி பிரியா திருக்கின்ற யோகிகள்","நாழிகை யாக நமனை அளப்பர்க்கள்","ஊழி முதலாய் உயர்வார் உலகினில்","தாழவல் லார்.க் இச் சசிவன்ன ராமே"],"p":"","r":"Yogis who stay unparted through the aeon measure Yama by the nazhigai; they rise in the world as first of the aeon, and those able to humble themselves become of the moon's hue.","n":"நாழிகையாக நமனை அளப்பர் may mean they count Death's span as a mere nazhigai (twenty-four minutes), or that they hold Death by measured time. Line 4 is corrupt (தாழவல் லார்.க் இச்); a variant reads இன் வழிச் சைவராமே, 'become Saivas of this way'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","kootru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:79"},"t:875":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":875,"t":["தண்மதி பானுச் சரிபூமி யேசென்று","மண்மதி காலங்கள் மூன்றும் வழிகண்டு","வெண்மதி தோன்றிய நாளில் விளைந்தபின்","தண்மதி வீழ்வள விற்கண மின்றே"],"p":"","r":"As the cool moon and the sun move over the earth, one sees the way of earth's and moon's three times; after it ripens on the day the white moon appears, there is not a moment until the cool moon sets.","n":"The verse is too compressed for a firm reading. கணம் இன்று may mean 'not a moment is lost' or 'no moment of waking', continuing 873.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:79"},"t:876":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":876,"t":["வளர்க்கின்ற ஆதித்தன் தங்கலை யாறுந்","தளர்க்கின்ற சந்திரன் தங்கலை யாறு","மலர்ந்தெழு பன்னிரண் டங்குலம் ஓடி","அலர்ந்து விழுந்தமை யாரறி வாரே"],"p":"","r":"Who knows how the six kalas of the growing sun and the six of the waning moon blossom, run twelve finger-breadths, and opening out, fall?","n":"Twelve finger-breadths (பன்னிரண்டு அங்குலம்) is the traditional reach of the out-breath from the nostrils, so the verse is read of the breath, but it does not name it. A variant line 3 (மலர்ந்தேறு பன்னிரண்டோடு எட்டு நாலாம்) changes the count.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["praanan","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:79"},"t:877":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":877,"t":["ஆமுயிர்த் தேய்மதி நாளே யெனல்விந்து","போம்வழி எங்கணும் போகாது யோகிக்குக்","காமுற இன்மையிற் கட்டுண்ணு மூலத்தில்","ஓமதி யத்துள்விட் டுரையுணர் வாலே"],"p":"","r":"Though one says the waning moon's day is life's, for the yogi bindu does not go out by its way anywhere; free of desire, it is bound at the base, through awareness of the word set within the moon of Om.","n":"Line 1 and line 4 (ஓமதியத்துள் விட்டு உரையுணர்வாலே) are obscure; ஓமதி may be ஓம் with மதி (moon) or ஓமம் (fire rite). The clear claim is the middle: the yogi's bindu does not leave, because desire is absent and it is held at the base.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vindhu","adakkam","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:79"},"t:878":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":878,"t":["வேறுறச் செங்கதிர் மெய்க்கலை யாறொடுஞ்","சூறுற நாங்குந் தொடர்ந்துற வேநிற்கும்","ஈறிலி நங்கலை யீரைந்தொ டேமதித்","தாறுட் கலையுள் அகலுவா வாமே"],"p":"","r":"Joined with the six true kalas of the red sun and the four, the endless one's kalas, with ten, stand closely joined; considered, they pass into the six inner kalas.","n":"Nearly every word is technical or doubtful (சூறுற, ஈறிலி நங்கலை, அகலுவா); the reading only follows the numbers. No firm sense can be fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:79"},"t:879":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":879,"t":["உணர்விந்து சோணி உறவினன் வீசும்","புணர்விந்து வீசுங் கதிரிற் குறையில்","உணர்வும் உடம்பும் உவையொக்க நிற்கில்","உணர்வும் உடம்பும் ஒருகால் விடாவே"],"p":"","r":"Knowing bindu, in union with the blood, is cast forth, and the united bindu wanes in the rays it casts; if awareness and body stand matched, awareness and body never once leave each other.","n":"சோணி (shonita, the female blood) paired with bindu is the old account of conception. The first two lines are compressed; the last two are clear: when awareness and body are kept in balance they do not part, which is to say death does not come.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vindhu","yaakkai","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:79"},"t:880":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":880,"t":["விடாத மனம்பவ நத்தொடு மேவி","நடாவு சிவசங்கின் நாதங் கொளுவிக்","கடாவிடா ஐம்புலன் கட்டுண்ணும் வீடு","படாதன இன்பம் பருகார் அமுதமே"],"p":"","r":"The unwavering mind, joined with the breath and hooked to the sound of Siva's sounding conch, binds the five senses that no longer drive it: that is release, and they drink nectar, a joy that does not fade.","n":"பவனம் is the breath, and the conch's sound one of the inner nadas. பருகார் may be 'those who drink' or 'they do not drink'; the second would make the last line a reproach to those who do not do this, and both readings stand.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["praanan","naadham","adakkam","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:79"},"t:881":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":881,"t":["அமுதப் புனல்வரு மாற்றங் கரைமேற்","குமிழிக் குட்சுட ரைந்தையுங் கூட்டிச்","சமையத்தண் டோ ட்டித் தரிக்கவல் லார்க்கு","நமன்இகில்லை நற்கலை நாளனஇல்லை தானே"],"p":"","r":"On the bank of the river where the nectar-water comes, gathering the five lights in the bubble and driving them through the staff, those able to hold them have no Yama, and their good kalas have no ending day.","n":"தண்டு (the staff) is taken as the spine and its central channel, and the nectar as what flows from the moon in the head. குமிழி (the bubble) and the five lights are not explained. The line is garbled in the text (தண்டோ ட்டி, நமன்இகில்லை, நாளனஇல்லை).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kootru","yogam","naadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:79"},"t:882":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":882,"t":["உண்ணீ ரமுத முறுமூ றலைத்திறந்","தெண்ணீர் இணையடித் தாமரைக் கேசெலத்","தெண்ணீர்ச் சமாதி யமர்ந்துதீ ராநலங்","கண்ணாற் றொடேசென்று கால்வழி மாறுமே"],"p":"","r":"Opening the spring of nectar-water within and sending the clear water to the lotus of the two feet, settled in the samadhi of clear water, unending good goes on by the eye's stream and the breath's course changes.","n":"இணையடித் தாமரை (the lotus of the two feet) is the Lord's feet, here perhaps placed in the head. கண்ணாற்றொடே (by the eye's stream) is obscure; கால்வழி மாறும் is read as the breath's course changing, as in yogic stilling of the breath.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["samaadhi","sivam","praanan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:79"},"t:883":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":883,"t":["மாறு மதியும் மதித்திரு மாறின்றித்","தாறு படாமல் தண்டோ டே தலைப்படில்","ஊறு படாதுடல் வேண்டும் உபாயமும்","பாறு படாஇன்பம் பார்மிசை பொங்குமே"],"p":"","r":"If the moon and its partner, unchanging and without disorder, reach the head by the staff, the body comes to no harm, the means one seeks is won, and a joy that does not decay wells up on earth.","n":"The variant மதியும் ஆதித்தனும் மாறின்றி (moon and sun without exchange) makes the partner the sun; the printed line (மதித்திரு) is unclear. The end, joy here on earth (பார்மிசை), not only after death, fits the tantra's aim of a perfected body.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","aanandham","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:79"},"t:884":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":884,"t":["போற்றுகின் றேன்புகழ்ந் தும்புகல் ஞானத்தைத்","தேற்றுகின் றேன்சிந்தை நாயகன் சேவடி","சாற்றுகின் றேன்அறை யோசிவ யோகத்தை","ஏற்றுகின் றேன்நம் பிரான்ஓர் எழுத்தே."],"p":"","r":"I praise and extol the knowledge I take refuge in, I settle in my mind the red feet of the Lord, I proclaim Siva-yoga, and I raise up our Lord, the one letter.","n":"ஓர் எழுத்து (the one letter) is traditionally Om, or the syllable சி as Siva; the verse doesn't say which. அறையோ can be a call to listen ('hear this') rather than 'I proclaim'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","gnaanam","yogam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:80"},"t:885":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":885,"t":["ஓரெழுத் தாலே உலகெங்கும் தானாகி","ஈரெழுத் தாலே இசைந்துஅங்கு இருவராய்","மூவெழுத் தாலே முளைக்கின்ற சோதியை","மாவெழுத் தாலே மயக்கமே உற்றதே."],"p":"","r":"By one letter he became everything in the world, by two letters he joined there as two, by three letters the light springs up, and by the great letter delusion came.","n":"A traditional key: one letter is Om or சி, two letters சிவ as Siva and Shakti, three letters அ, உ, ம of Om. மாவெழுத்து (the great letter, or the letter ம) is taken as the ம of நமசிவாய, which tradition makes stand for malam, hence மயக்கம். All of this is symbolic; the verse gives only the counts.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","sivam","saththi","malam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:80"},"t:886":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":886,"t":["தேவர் உறைகின்ற சிற்றம் பலம்என்றுந்","தேவர் உறைகின்ற சிதம்பரம் என்றுந்","தேவர் உறைகின்ற திருஅம் பலமென்றுந்","தேவர் உறைகின்ற தென்பொது வாமே."],"p":"","r":"The place where the gods dwell is called Chirrambalam, Chidambaram, the holy Ambalam and the southern Podu.","n":"Four names for the one dance-hall, with தேவர் உறைகின்ற repeated in each line. தேவர் may be the gods who attend, or an honorific plural for the Lord himself. Tradition reads the hall also as a place within the body, but the verse names only the hall.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thirukkoothu","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:80"},"t:887":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":887,"t":["ஆமே பொன் னம்பலம் அற்புதம் ஆனந்தம்","ஆமே திருக்கூத்து அனவரத் தாண்டவம்","ஆமே பிரளயம் ஆகும்அத் தாண்டவம்","ஆமேசங் காரத்து அருந்தாண் டவங்களே."],"p":"","r":"The golden hall is wonder and bliss; it is the holy dance, the unceasing dance; that dance is the dissolution of the world, the rare dances of destruction.","n":"The verse makes one dance both bliss and destruction (பிரளயம், சங்காரம்). Destruction is one of the five acts; the dance of destruction is not presented as fearful but as rare (அரு).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thirukkoothu","aanandham","aindhozhil"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:80"},"t:888":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":888,"t":["தாண்டவ மான தனியெழுத்து ஓரெழுத்து","தாண்டவ மானது அனுக்கிரகத் தொழில்","தாண்டவக் கூத்துததனிநின்ற தற்பரம்","தாண்டவக் கூத்துத் தமனியந் தானே."],"p":"","r":"The dance is the single letter, the one letter; the dance is the work of grace; the dance is the Supreme who stood alone; the dance is gold itself.","n":"அனுக்கிரகத் தொழில் (the work of grace, anugraha) is the last of the five acts; 887 named destruction, so the dance holds both. தமனியம் is gold, perhaps the golden hall, perhaps the gold that copper becomes in 903 to 907.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thirukkoothu","mandhiram","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:80"},"t:889":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":889,"t":["தானே பரஞ்சுடர் தத்துவ மாய்நிற்கும்","தானே அகார உகாரம தாய்நிற்கும்","தானே பரஞ்சுடர் தத்துவக் கூத்துக்குத்","தானே தனக்குத் தராதலம் தானே."],"p":"","r":"He himself stands as the supreme light and as the tattva, himself stands as A and U, and for the tattva-dance of the supreme light he himself is the ground, ground to himself.","n":"தராதலம் (the earth, the ground) says the dance needs no stage but the dancer. அகார உகாரம் are traditionally Siva and Shakti, or the first two parts of Om. Line 2 recurs unchanged in 901.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thirukkoothu","sivam","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:80"},"t:890":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":890,"t":["தராதல மூலைக்குத் தற்பர மாபரன்","தராதலம் வெப்பு நமசி வாயந்","தராதலம் சொல்லில் தான்வா சியவாகும்","தராதல யோகம் தயாவாசி யாமே."],"p":"","r":"For the ground at the root the Supreme over all is the base; that ground is the heat and is நமசிவாய; spoken, the ground becomes வாசிய, and the yoga of the ground is தயாவாசி.","n":"Obscure throughout. மூலை may be the root centre (மூலாதாரம்), and வெப்பு its heat. வாசி is the breath in Siddha usage and is also a play on the syllables of சிவாய (வா, சி); தயா may be compassion. I can fix neither the syntax nor the reference.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panchaakkaram","yogam","praanan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:80"},"t:891":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":891,"t":["ஆமே சிவங்கள் அகார உகாரங்கள்","ஆமே பரங்கள் அறியா இடம்என்ப","ஆமே திருக்கூத்து அடங்கிய சிற்பரம்","ஆமே சிவகதி ஆனந்த மாமே."],"p":"","r":"A and U are the Siva-forms, the supreme ones are said to be the place no one knows, the supreme consciousness is where the holy dance comes to rest, and Siva's way is bliss.","n":"The plurals சிவங்கள் and பரங்கள் are unusual and their reference is unclear. அடங்கிய may mean the dance is contained in the supreme consciousness, or subsides into it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","thirukkoothu","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:80"},"t:892":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":892,"t":["ஆனந்த மூன்றும் அறிவுஇரண்டு ஒன்றாகும்","ஆனந்தம் சிவாய அறிவார் பலரில்லை","ஆனந்த மோடும் அறியவல் லார்கட்டு","ஆனந்தக் கூத்தாய் அகப்படும் தானே."],"p":"","r":"The three blisses and the two knowledges become one; few know that bliss is சிவாய; for those who can know it with bliss, it is caught as the dance of bliss.","n":"Which three blisses and two knowledges are meant is not said. அறியவல்லார் கட்டு can be read 'for those able to know' or as 'the bond of those able to know'; either way, the Lord or the bliss is 'caught' (அகப்படும்) as the dance.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aanandham","panchaakkaram","thirukkoothu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:80"},"t:893":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":893,"t":["படுவது இரண்டும் பலகலை வல்லார்","படுவது ஓங்காரம் பஞ்சாக் கரங்கள்","படுவது சங்காரத் தாண்டவப் பத்தி","படுவது கோணம் பரந்திடும் வாறே."],"p":"","r":"What the masters of many arts attain is the two: Omkara and the five letters; what they attain is devotion to the dance of destruction and the angles by which it spreads.","n":"படுவது (what befalls, what is attained) opens every line. கோணம் (angle) may point to the triangles of a drawn chakra, as in 904; this is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piranavam","panchaakkaram","thirukkoothu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:80"},"t:894":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":894,"t":["வாறே சதாசிவ மாறிலா ஆகமம்","வாறே சிவகதி வண்டுறை புன்னையும்","வாறே திருக்கூத்து ஆகம வசனங்கள்","வாறே பொதுவாகும் மன்றின் அமலமே."],"p":"","r":"This is the way: Sadasiva and the unchanging Agama, Siva's path, the punnai tree where bees dwell, the holy dance and the words of the Agama, and the stainless one of the hall common to all.","n":"வாறே (thus, this is the way) opens each line and continues 893's last word. The punnai tree with bees is unexplained here; it may be a sacred tree of a shrine or a symbol. பொது (common, public) is also a name of the dance-hall.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aagamam","thirukkoothu","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:80"},"t:895":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":895,"t":["அமலம் பதிபசு பாசங்கள் ஆகமம்","அமலம் திரோதாயி யாகுமா னந்தமாம்","அமலம் சொல் ஆணவம் மாயை காமியம்","அமலம் திருக்கூத்து ஆமிடம் தானே."],"p":"","r":"The stainless one is joined in turn to the Agama of Pati, Pasu and Pasam, to the concealing power that becomes bliss, to the impurities called anava, maya and karma, and to the place of the holy dance.","n":"அமலம் (the stainless) opens each line, and the verse does not say how it relates to each thing it is joined to. திரோதாயி is the Lord's concealing power, which tradition holds hides the Lord only until the soul is ripe, so it can 'become bliss'. காமியம் is the karma-impurity. The three named impurities are the standard Siddhanta three; the plain sense is that where the dance is, the stainless stands against them.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["malam","pathi","pasu","paasam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:80"},"t:896":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":896,"t":["தானே தனக்குத் தலைவனு மாய்நிற்கும்","தானே தனக்குத் தன்மலை யாய்நிற்கும்","தானே தனக்குத் தன்மய மாய்நிற்கும்","தானே தனக்குத் தலைவனும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"He is lord to himself, his own mountain, his own substance, and again lord to himself.","n":"The first and last lines are almost the same, closing the circle of a being who depends on nothing. தன்மலை (his own mountain) may be Kailasa, or a play on மலம் that I cannot confirm.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","pathi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:80"},"t:897":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":897,"t":["தலைவனு மாய்நின்ற தற்பரக் கூத்தனைத்","தலைவனு மாய்நின்ற சற்பாத் திரத்தைத்","தலைவனு மாய்நின்ற தாதவிழ் ஞானத்","தலைவனு மாய்நின்ற தாளிணை தானே."],"p":"","r":"The pair of feet is the Lord: of the supreme dancer, of the worthy vessel, and of the knowledge that opens as a flower releases its pollen.","n":"The verse builds four lines of தலைவனுமாய் நின்ற to land on தாளிணை (the two feet). சற்பாத்திரம் (a worthy vessel) is unclear here: perhaps the Lord as the fit recipient of worship, perhaps the soul fit to receive grace.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","thirukkoothu","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:80"},"t:898":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":898,"t":["இணையார் திருவடி எட்டெழுத் தாகும்","இணையார் கழலிணை ஈர்ஐஞ்ச தாகும்","இணையார் கழலிணை ஐம்பத் தொன்றாகும்","இணையார் கழலிணை ஏழா யிரமே."],"p":"","r":"The paired holy feet are the eight letters; the paired anklets are twice five, are fifty-one, are seven thousand.","n":"The feet are identified with letters and mantras. Fifty-one is the traditional count of letters of the Sanskrit alphabet, and seven thousand the count of mantras (900). The eight letters and twice five (perhaps the five letters doubled, or the ten letters of a longer formula) are not specified.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:80"},"t:899":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":899,"t":["ஏழா யிரமாய் இருபதாய் முப்பதாய்","ஏழா யிரத்தும் ஏழுகோடி தானாகி","ஏழா யிரத்துயிர் எண்ணிலா மந்திரம்","ஏழா யிரண்டாய் இருக்கின்ற வாறே."],"p":"","r":"Being seven thousand, twenty and thirty, in the seven thousand becoming seven crores, the countless mantras of the seven thousand stand as seven and two.","n":"A chain of numbers I cannot resolve. ஏழாயிரத்துயிர் may be 'the life of the seven thousand' or a misdivision. The point that survives is that the mantras multiply beyond counting and yet reduce to a few.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:80"},"t:900":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":900,"t":["இருக்கின்ற மந்திரம் ஏழா யிரமாம்","இருக்கின்ற மந்திரம் எத்திறம் இல்லை","இருக்கின்ற மந்திரம் சிவன்திரு மேனி","இருக்கின்ற மந்திரம் இவ்வண்ணம் தானே."],"p":"","r":"The mantras that exist number seven thousand, there is no kind of mantra outside them, the mantra is Siva's holy body, and this is the nature of mantra.","n":"சிவன் திருமேனி: that Siva's body is made of mantra is an Agamic teaching (the mantra-body), here stated plainly. எத்திறம் இல்லை may also mean 'there is nothing it cannot do'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:80"},"t:901":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":901,"t":["தானே தனக்குத் தகுநட்டம் தானாகும்","தானே அகார உகாரம தாய்நிற்கும்","தானே ரீங்காரத் தத்துவக் கூத்துக்குத்","தானே உலகில் தனிநடந் தானே."],"p":"","r":"He himself becomes his own fitting dance, himself stands as A and U, and for the tattva-dance of hrim he himself dances alone in the world.","n":"The verse reworks 889: the tattva-dance there is of the supreme light, here of ரீங்காரம், the seed syllable hrim, traditionally Shakti's.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thirukkoothu","mandhiram","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:80"},"t:902":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":902,"t":["நடம்இரண்டு ஒன்றே நளினம தாகும்","நடம்இரண்டு ஒன்றே நமன்செய்யும் கூத்துலயம்","நடம்இரண்டு ஒன்றே நகைசெயா மந்திரம்","நடம்சிவ லிங்கம் நலஞ்செம்பு பொன்னே."],"p":"","r":"The two dances are one: it becomes the lotus; the two dances are one: the dance of dissolution that Death performs; the two dances are one: the mantra that does not mock; the dance is the Sivalinga, the good copper turned gold.","n":"Which two dances are one is not said (perhaps Siva's and Shakti's, or creation and destruction). நமன் may be Death, or a fragment of நம from the five letters. நகைசெயா is obscure. The copper turned gold, repeated through 903 to 907, is an alchemical image; tradition takes copper's tarnish as the soul's impurity removed by the mantra.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thirukkoothu","mandhiram","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:80"},"t:903":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":903,"t":["செம்பொன் ஆகும் சிவாய நமஎன்னில்","செம்பொன் ஆகத் திரண்டது சிற்பரம்","செம்பொன் ஆகும் ஸ்ரீயும் கிரீயுமெனச்","செம்பொன் ஆன திருஅம் பலமே."],"p":"","r":"If one says சிவாயநம it becomes red gold; the supreme consciousness gathered as red gold; it becomes red gold as shrim and krim; red gold is the holy hall.","n":"ஸ்ரீ and கிரீ are taken as the seed syllables shrim and krim. The golden hall of Chidambaram and the gold of alchemy are both in play; whether the verse promises actual gold or uses gold for purity is not settled by the verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panchaakkaram","mandhiram","thirukkoothu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:80"},"t:904":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":904,"t":["திருஅம் பலமாகச் சீர்ச்சக் கரத்தைத்","திருஅம் பலமாக ஈராறு கீறித்","திருஅம் பலமாக இருபத்தைஞ் சாக்கித்","திருஅம் பலமாகச் செபிக்கின்ற வாறே."],"p":"","r":"To make the holy hall, draw the auspicious chakra by scoring twice six lines into twenty-five squares, and chant it as the holy hall: this is the way.","n":"Six lines each way make a five-by-five grid of twenty-five cells, so the arithmetic holds. Tradition fills the cells with the five letters in rotating order (the திருவம்பலச் சக்கரம்), which fits 912's changing orders; the verse itself gives only the grid.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","vazhipaadu","thirukkoothu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:80"},"t:905":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":905,"t":["வாறே சிவாய நமச்சி வாயநம","வாறே செபிக்கில் வரும்பேர் பிறப்பில்லை","வாறே அருளால் வளர்கூத்துக் காணலாம்","வாறே செபிக்கில் வரும்செம்பு பொன்னே."],"p":"","r":"The way is சிவாயநம, நமசிவாயநம: chant it so and no birth follows, by grace one may see the dance that grows, and copper becomes gold.","n":"Release from birth, the vision of the dance and the alchemical gold are promised in one breath; the verse doesn't rank them. அருளால் places the vision under grace, not under the chanting alone.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panchaakkaram","piravi","thiruvarul","thirukkoothu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:80"},"t:906":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":906,"t":["பொன்னான மந்திரம் புகலவும் ஒண்ணாது","பொன்னான மந்திரம் பொறிகிஞ்சு கத்தாகும்","பொன்னான மந்திரம் புகையுண்டு பூரிக்கிற்","பொன்னாகும் வல்லோர்க்கு உடம்பு பொற் பாதமே."],"p":"","r":"The golden mantra cannot be told; it is red like the kinjuka flower; for the capable who take in its smoke and are filled, the body becomes gold and they reach the golden feet.","n":"கிஞ்சுகம் is the red palasa flower. புகையுண்டு பூரிக்கில் may be alchemical fumigation or the in-breath (பூரகம்) of breath control; பொறி may be the senses or a spark. The body turned golden is both a siddha claim and an image of the purified body.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","yaakkai","siththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:80"},"t:907":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":907,"t":["பொற்பாதம் காணலாம் புத்திரர் உண்டாகும்","பொற்பாதத்து ஆணையே செம்புபொன் ஆயிடும்","பொற்பாதம் காணத் திருமேனி ஆயிடும்","பொற்பாத நன்னடம் சிந்தனை சொல்லுமே."],"p":"","r":"One may see the golden feet and sons will be born; by the command of the golden feet copper becomes gold; seeing the golden feet the body becomes holy; the good dance of the golden feet speaks to thought.","n":"Worldly fruits (sons, gold) and the holy body sit side by side. சிந்தனை சொல்லுமே is unclear: the dance may speak to the mind, or be what the mind dwells on.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["siththi","sivam","thirukkoothu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:80"},"t:908":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":908,"t":["சொல்லும் ஒருகூட்டில் புக்குச் சுகிக்கலாம்","நல்ல மடவார் நயத்துட னேவரும்","சொல்லினும் பாசச் சுடர்ப்பாம்பு நீங்கிடும்","சொல்லும் திருக்கூத்தின் சூக்குமம் தானே."],"p":"","r":"One may enter another body and take pleasure there, good women come with liking, even by speaking it the bright serpent of bonds departs; this is the subtle secret of the holy dance.","n":"ஒருகூட்டில் புக்கு is read as the yogic power of entering another body. The serpent of bonds (பாசச் சுடர்ப்பாம்பு) is the bond pictured as a serpent; சுடர் (bright, burning) could also suggest kundalini, but the verse calls it பாசம். Powers and liberation are again listed together.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["siththi","paasam","thirukkoothu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:80"},"t:909":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":909,"t":["சூக்குமம் எண்ணா யிரஞ்செபித் தாலும்மேல்","சூக்கும மான வழியிடைக் காணலாம்","சூக்கும மான வினையைக் கெடுக்கலாம்","சூக்கும மான சிவனதுஆ னந்தமே."],"p":"","r":"When one chants the subtle mantra eight thousand times, the subtle way can be seen above, subtle karma can be destroyed, and Siva's bliss is subtle.","n":"சூக்குமம் (subtle) opens each line and is the last word of 908. செபித்தாலும் literally 'even if one chants', but 'when one chants' fits the promise better. மேல் may mean above, in the head, or simply 'further'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","vinai","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:80"},"t:910":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":910,"t":["ஆனந்தம் ஆனந்தம் ஒன்றென்று அறைந்திட","ஆனந்தம் ஆனந்தம் ஆஈஊஏஓம் என்று அறைந்திடும்","ஆனந்தம் ஆனந்தம் அஞ்சுமது ஆயிடும்","ஆனந்தம் ஆனந்தம் அம்ஹ்ரீம்அம் க்ஷம் ஆம்ஆகுமே."],"p":"","r":"Declare bliss to be one; bliss declares ஆ ஈ ஊ ஏ ஓம்; bliss becomes the five; bliss becomes அம் ஹ்ரீம் அம் க்ஷம் ஆம்.","n":"The vowels and seed syllables are mantra forms I can't interpret from the verse; tradition matches the five vowels with the five letters or the five faces of Sadasiva. அஞ்சு (five) may be the five letters.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aanandham","mandhiram"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:80"},"t:911":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":911,"t":["மேனி இரண்டும் விலங்காமல் மேற்கொள்ள","மேனி இரண்டும் மிகார விகாரியாம்","மேனி இரண்டும் ஊஆஈஏஓ என்று","மேனி இரண்டும் ஈஓஊஆஏ கூத்தாமே."],"p":"","r":"So that the two bodies are taken up without parting, the two bodies are change and the changeless, sounded as ஊ ஆ ஈ ஏ ஓ and as ஈ ஓ ஊ ஆ ஏ, and that is the dance.","n":"மேனி இரண்டு (the two bodies) are commonly taken as Siva and Shakti; they may also be the gross and subtle bodies. மிகார விகாரி is obscure; I read it as விகாரி and அவிகாரி (changing and unchanging), which is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","thirukkoothu","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:80"},"t:912":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":912,"t":["கூத்தே சிவாய நமமசி வாயிடும்","கூத்தே ஈஊஆஏஓம் சிவாய நம வாயிடும்","கூத்தே ஈஊஆஏஓம் சிவயநம வாயிடும்","கூத்தே இஊஆஏஓம் நமசிவாய கோளொன்று மாறே."],"p":"","r":"The dance becomes சிவாயநம and நமசிவாய; with ஈ ஊ ஆ ஏ ஓம் it becomes சிவாயநம and சிவயநம; with இ ஊ ஆ ஏ ஓம் it becomes நமசிவாய: one way of holding it, in changed order.","n":"The five letters are rotated (சிவாயநம, நமசிவாய, சிவயநம), as in the chakra of 904. கோளொன்று மாறே may mean 'one formula, changed in order' or 'the planet/grasp changes'. The variant spellings may be deliberate forms or copying errors.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panchaakkaram","thirukkoothu","mandhiram"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:80"},"t:913":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":913,"t":["ஒன்றிரண்டு ஆடவோர் ஒன்றும் உடனாட","ஒன்றிரண்டு மூன்றாட ஓரேழும் ஒத்தாட","ஒன்றினில் ஆடவோர் ஒன்பதும் உடனாட","மன்றினில் ஆடனான் மாணிக்கக் கூத்தே."],"p":"","r":"As one and two dance, one dances with them; as one, two and three dance, all seven dance in accord; as he dances in one, the nine dance with him: he who dances in the hall, the ruby dance.","n":"The numbers carry the meaning and the verse doesn't fix them; tradition variously makes them Siva and Shakti, the three powers, the seven worlds or notes, and the nine forms of the Lord. The plain sense: everything joins the dance of the one in the hall. ஆடனான் is likely ஆடினான் or ஆடுவான்.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thirukkoothu","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:80"},"t:914":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":914,"t":["இருந்தஇவ் வட்டங்கள் ஈராறி ரேகை","இருந்த இரேகைமேல் ஈராறு இருத்தி","இருந்த மனைகளும் ஈராறு பத்தொன்று","இருந்த மனையொன்றில் எய்துவன் தானே."],"p":"","r":"The circle has twelve lines, and twelve more are set across them; the houses so formed number a hundred and twenty-one, and in one of those houses he himself arrives.","n":"ஈராறு பத்தொன்று is taken as 121, the eleven by eleven cells that twelve lines each way make. Who arrives (எய்துவன்) is left unnamed; in context it is the Lord, or the worshipper's letter.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["chakkaram","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:915":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":915,"t":["தான்ஒன்றி வாழிடம் தன்எழுத் தேயாகும்","தான்ஒன்றும் அந்நான்கும் தன்பே ரெழுத்தாகும்","தான்ஒன்று நாற்கோணம் தன்ஐந் தெழுத்தாகும்","தான்ஒன்றி லேஒன்றும் அவ்அரன் தானே."],"p":"","r":"The place where he dwells is his own letter; one letter with four makes the letters of his great name, the five letters set in the square, and the one who is the one within the one is Hara.","n":"தன் எழுத்து (his own letter) is commonly taken as சி, the one that with four others makes நமசிவாய. நாற்கோணம் is the square of the diagram.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panchaakkaram","chakkaram","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:916":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":916,"t":["அரகர என்ன அரியதொன்று இல்லை","அரகர என்ன அறிகிலர் மாந்தர்","அரகர என்ன அமரரும் ஆவர்","அரகர என்ன அறும்பிறப்பு அன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Nothing is as rare as saying 'Hara Hara'; people don't know to say it; by saying it they become as the gods, and by saying it birth is cut off.","n":"The plainest verse of the opening: the name alone, without diagram, is enough. அமரரும் ஆவர் can mean they become immortals or become like the gods.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","piravi","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:917":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":917,"t":["எட்டு நிலையுள எங்கோன் இருப்பிடம்","எட்டினில் ஒன்றும் இருமூன்றும் ஈரேழும்","ஒட்டிய விந்துவும் நாதமும் ஓங்கிடப்","பட்டது மந்திரம் பான்மொழி யாலே."],"p":"","r":"There are eight stations where my Lord dwells; in the eight, with one, six and fourteen, and bindu and nada joined and rising, the mantra was formed by the one of milk-sweet speech.","n":"The numbers (one, two threes, two sevens) are not explained, and பான்மொழி may name the goddess 'of milk-sweet speech' or describe the mantra's sound.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","vindhu","naadham","chakkaram"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:918":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":918,"t":["மட்டவிழ் தாமரை மாதுநல் லாளுடன்","ஒட்டி இருந்த உபாயம் அறிகிலர்","விட்ட எழுத்தை விடாத எழுத்துடன்","கட்டவில் லாருயிர் காக்கவல் லாரே."],"p":"","r":"People don't know the means by which he stays joined with the good lady of the opening lotus; those who unite the letter that is let go with the letter not let go can guard the precious life.","n":"விட்ட எழுத்து and விடாத எழுத்து are traditionally taken as the outgoing and the held breath, or the spoken and the unspoken syllable of the previous chapter's ajapa. கட்டவில்லாருயிர் is obscure; read here as கட்டு அவிழ் ஆருயிர், the life whose bond is loosed, or the precious life.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","mandhiram","praanan","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:919":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":919,"t":["ஆலய மாக அமர்ந்தபஞ் சாக்கரம்","ஆலய மாக அமர்ந்தஇத் தூலம்போய்","ஆலய மாக அறிகின்ற சூக்குமம்","ஆலய மாக அமர்ந்திருந் தானே."],"p":"","r":"The five syllables are seated as a temple; when this gross temple is passed, the subtle known as temple remains, and there he sits enshrined.","n":"தூலம் and சூக்குமம் (gross and subtle) may mean the gross and subtle body or the spoken and inner forms of the mantra; ஆலயம் (temple) carries the verse in all four lines.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panchaakkaram","vazhipaadu","yaakkai","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:920":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":920,"t":["இருந்த இவ்வட்டம் இருமூன்றுஇ ரேகை","இருந்த அதனுள் இரேகை ஐந்தாக","இருந்த அறைகள் இருபத்துஐஞ் சாக","இருந்த அறையொன்றில் எய்தும் அகாரமே."],"p":"","r":"This circle has six lines, within them lines making five, and the cells come to twenty-five; in one of these cells the letter A takes its place.","n":"Six lines each way give five by five, twenty-five cells, the smaller grid filled in 921 to 924.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["chakkaram","mandhiram"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:921":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":921,"t":["மகார நடுவே வளைத்திடும் சத்தியை","ஓகாரம் வளைத்திட்டு உம்பிளந்து ஏற்றி","அகாரம் தலையாய் இருகண் சிகாரமாய்","நகார வகாரநற் காலது நாடுமே."],"p":"","r":"Shakti is curved in the middle of MA, O curved around it, U split and raised; A is the head, SI the two eyes, NA and VA the good legs: so the figure is sought.","n":"The letters seem to be shaped into a figure with head, eyes and legs, traditionally taken as the form of the Dancer drawn from the syllables. The way the letter shapes are combined can't be recovered from the words.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panchaakkaram","piranavam","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:922":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":922,"t":["நாடும் பிரணவம் நடுஇரு பக்கமும்","ஆடும் அவர்வாய் அமர்ந்தங்கு நின்றது","நாடும் நடுவண் முகம்நம சிவாய","ஆடும் சிவாயநம புறவட்டத்து ஆயதே."],"p":"","r":"Om, the pranava, is set in the middle and on both sides; in the central face is NAMASIVAYA, and SIVAYANAMA runs in the outer circle.","n":"ஆடும் அவர் வாய் (the mouth of the one who dances) is obscure; it may tie the diagram to the Dancer of the hall.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piranavam","panchaakkaram","chakkaram"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:923":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":923,"t":["ஆயும் சிவாய நமமசி வாயந","ஆயும் நமசிவா யயநம சிவா","வாயுமே வாய நமசியெனும் மந்திரம்","ஆயும் சிகாரம் தொட்டநதத் தடைவிலே."],"p":"","r":"The mantra is examined in its rotations, SIVAYANAMA, MASIVAYANA, NAMASIVAYA, YANAMASIVA, VAYANAMASI, taken in order from SI.","n":"The five rotations of the five syllables, each beginning on a different letter, fill the rows of the grid. The last line (தொட்டநதத் தடைவிலே) is obscure.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panchaakkaram","chakkaram"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:924":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":924,"t":["அடைவினில் ஐம்பதும் ஐஐந்து அறையின்","அடையும் அறையொன்றுக்கு ஈரெழுத்து ஆக்கி","அடையும் மகாரத்தில் அந்தமாம் க்ஷவ்வும்","அடைவின் எழுத்துஐம் பத்தொன்றும் அமர்ந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"In order, the fifty letters go two to a cell in the twenty-five cells; with KSHA, the last, placed at MA, all fifty-one letters are set.","n":"Fifty-one is the count of the Sanskrit alphabet with க்ஷ added; the verse fits the whole alphabet into the five-syllable grid.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["chakkaram","mandhiram"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:925":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":925,"t":["அமர்ந்த அரகர வாம்புற வட்டம்","அமர்ந்த அரிகரி யாம் அதனுள் வட்டம்","அமர்ந்த அசபை யாம் அதனுள்வட்டம்","அமர்ந்தஇ ரேகையும் ஆகின்ற சூலமே."],"p":"","r":"The outer circle is set with 'Hara Hara', the circle within it with 'Hari Hari', the circle within that with the ajapa, and the lines become a trident.","n":"அசபை is the unspoken mantra of the breath, subject of the previous chapter. அரிகரி may be Hari or a variant of Hara.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["chakkaram","mandhiram"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:926":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":926,"t":["சூலத் தலையினில் தோற்றிடும் சத்தியும்","சூலத் தலையினில் சூழும்ஓங் காரத்தால்","சூலத்து இடைவெளி தோற்றிடும் அஞ்செழுத்து","ஆலப் பதிக்கும் அடைவதும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"At the trident's head Shakti appears, around it Om, in the space between its prongs the five letters appear, and this is also the way to reach the Lord of the banyan.","n":"ஆலப்பதி may be the Lord under the banyan or a place; the reading of the figure depends on how the trident of 925 is drawn.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","piranavam","panchaakkaram"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:927":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":927,"t":["அதுவாம் அகார இகார உகாரம்","அதுவாம் எகாரம் ஓகாரமது ஐந்தாம்","அதுவாகும் சக்கர வட்டமேல் வட்டம்","பொதுவாம் இடைவெளி பொங்குநம் பேரே."],"p":"","r":"That is A, I, U, E and O, five; they form circle upon circle of the chakra, and in the shared space between, our name swells.","n":"நம் பேரே may be 'our name' or the syllable நம.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["chakkaram","mandhiram"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:928":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":928,"t":["பேர்பெற் றதுமூல மந்திரம் பின்னது","சோர்வுற்ற சக்கர வட்டத்துள் சந்தியின்","நேர்பெற் றிருந்திட நின்றது சக்கரம்","ஏர்பெற் றிருந்த இயல்பிது வாமே."],"p":"","r":"What received the name is the root mantra; after it, the chakra stands set straight at the junction within the circle, and this is the nature of its form.","n":"சோர்வுற்ற (wearied, slack) of the circle is obscure and may be corrupt.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","chakkaram"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:929":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":929,"t":["இயலும் இம் மந்திரம் எய்தும் வழியின்","செயலும் அறியத் தெளிவிக்கு நாதன்","புயலும் புனலும் பொருந்துஅங்கி மண்விண்","முயலும் எழுத்துக்கு முன்னா இருந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"The Lord makes clear the working of the way this mantra is reached; wind, water, fire, earth and sky stood before the letters that are sounded.","n":"முயலும் எழுத்துக்கு முன்னா may mean the elements came before the letters, or stand in front of them as their places in the diagram.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","sivam","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:930":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":930,"t":["ஆறெட்டு எழுத்தின்மேல் ஆறும் பதினாலும்","ஏறிட்டு அதன்மேல் விந்துவும் நாதமும்","சீறிட்டு நின்று சிவாய நமவென்னக்","கூறிட்டு மும்மலம் கூப்பிட்டுப் போமே."],"p":"","r":"Above the forty-eight letters set six and fourteen, above them bindu and nada; standing fierce and saying SIVAYANAMA, the three impurities cry out and leave.","n":"The count (6 x 8, then 6 and 14) is not explained. கூப்பிட்டுப் போமே pictures the impurities fleeing with a cry; the claim that the mantra removes the three malams is plain whatever the numbers mean.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panchaakkaram","malam","vindhu","naadham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:931":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":931,"t":["அண்ணல் இருப்பது அவள்அக் கரத்துளே","பெண்ணின்நல் லாளும் பிரானக் கரத்துளே","எண்ணி இருவர் இசைந்துஅங்கு இருந்திடப்","புண்ணிய வாளர் பொருளறி வார்களே."],"p":"","r":"The Lord is within her letter and the good lady within the Lord's letter; those who think of the two dwelling there in accord are the virtuous who know the meaning.","n":"Tradition takes சி as Siva's letter and வ as Shakti's; the verse says only that each is within the other's.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","saththi","panchaakkaram"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:932":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":932,"t":["அவ்விட்டு வைத்தங்கு அரவிட்டு மேல்வைத்து","இவ்விட்டுப் பார்க்கில் இலிங்கம தாய்நிற்கும்","மவ்விட்டு மேலே வளியுறக் கண்டபின்","தொம்மிட்டு நின்ற சுடர்க்கொழுந்து ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"Set A, place ARA above it, set I, and it stands as a linga; set MA, and once the breath is seen rising above, it becomes a flame-tip sealed with 'thom'.","n":"அரவு may be the letters ர or ஹ, or the serpent. தொம் may be a seed syllable. The verse shapes letters into a linga and then a flame.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","sivam","praanan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:933":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":933,"t":["அவ்வுண்டு சவ்வுண்டு அனைத்தும் அங்கு உள்ளது","சவ்வுண்டு நிற்கும் கருத்தறி வார்இல்லை","கவ்வுண்டு நிற்கும் கருத்தறி வாளர்க்குச்","சவ்வுண்டு சத்தி சதாசிவன் தானே."],"p":"","r":"There is A, there is SA, everything is there; no one knows the purpose for which SA stands; for those who know the purpose of KA, SA is Shakti and Sadasiva.","n":"அ, ச and க are not explained; they may be letters of a seed mantra, or அ and ச Siva and Shakti as in 957.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","saththi","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:934":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":934,"t":["அஞ்செழுத் தாலே அமர்ந்தனன் நந்தியும்","அஞ்செழுத் தாலே அமர்ந்தபஞ் சாக்கரம்","அஞ்செழுத் தாகிய வக்கர சக்கரம்","அஞ்செழுத் துள்ளே அமர்ந்திருந் தானே."],"p":"","r":"By the five letters Nandi is seated; by the five letters the pancakshara is seated; the five letters make the letter-chakra, and within the five letters he is seated.","n":"The circling of அஞ்செழுத்து in every line makes the Lord, the mantra and the diagram one thing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panchaakkaram","chakkaram","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:935":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":935,"t":["கூத்தனைக் காணுங் குறிபல பேசிடில்","கூத்தன் எழுத்தின் முதலெழுத்து ஓதினார்","கூத்தனொடு ஒன்றிய கொள்கைய ராய்நிற்பர்","கூத்தனைக் காணும் குறியது வாமே."],"p":"","r":"Many signs are told for seeing the Dancer; those who recite the first of the Dancer's letters stand as one in creed with him, and that is the sign for seeing the Dancer.","n":"Which letter is first is not said: ந in the usual order, சி as the Lord's own letter, or Om.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panchaakkaram","sivam","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:936":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":936,"t":["அத்திசைக் குள்நின்ற அனலை எழுப்பிய","அத்திசைக் குள்நின்ற நவ்எழுத்து ஓதினால்","அத்திசைக் குள்நின்ற அந்த மறையனை","அத்திசைக் குள்ளுற வாக்கினன் தானே."],"p":"","r":"If one raises the fire within that direction and recites the letter NA within that direction, the Vedic one in that direction is made to dwell within that direction.","n":"அத்திசை (that direction) opens every line; directions of the diagram are assigned to letters and deities, but which is meant is lost. மறையன் may be Brahma, the Vedic one, or 'the hidden one'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","panchaakkaram"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:937":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":937,"t":["தானே அளித்திடும் தையலை நோக்கினால்","தானே அளித்திட்டு மேலுற வைத்திடும்","தானே அளித்த மகாரத்தை ஓதிடத்","தானே அளித்ததோர் கல்லொளி யாகுமே."],"p":"","r":"If one looks to the lady he himself gives, he gives and places her above; when one recites the MA he gave, it becomes a gem-light he has given.","n":"கல்லொளி (light of stone, a gem's light) links this verse to the next. The grammar of who gives and who is placed is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","mandhiram"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:938":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":938,"t":["கல்லொளி யேயென நின்ற வடதிசை","கல்லொளி யேயென நின்ற னன் இந்திரன்","கல்லொளி யேயென நின்ற சிகாரத்தைக்","கல்லொளி யேயெனக் காட்டிநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"The north stood as gem-light, Indra stood as gem-light, and he showed the letter SI, which stood as gem-light, as gem-light.","n":"Indra is usually the guardian of the east, not the north, so either the diagram's scheme differs or the line is disturbed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","panchaakkaram"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:939":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":939,"t":["தானே எழுகுணம் தண்சுட ராய்நிற்கும்","தானே எழுகுணம் வேதமு மாய்நிற்கும்","தானே எழுகுணம் ஆவதும் ஓதிடில்","தானே எழுந்த மறையவன் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"He himself stands as cool light with the seven qualities, as the Vedas with them; if one recites what becomes those qualities, he becomes the self-risen Vedic one.","n":"எழுகுணம் may be 'seven qualities' or 'the rising quality'. The last line may mean the reciter or the Lord becomes the Vedic one.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","vedham","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:940":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":940,"t":["மறைய வனாக மதித்த பிறவி","மறையவ னாக மதித்திக் காண்பர்","மறையவன் அஞ்செழுத்து உள்நிற்கப் பெற்ற","மறையவன் அஞ்செழுத்து தாம்அது வாகுமே."],"p":"","r":"Births esteemed as those of the Vedic man are seen as such, but the Vedic man in whom the five letters have come to stand becomes those five letters himself.","n":"One reading: the true மறையவன் is made by the five syllables dwelling in him, not by the birth people esteem. The first two lines are compressed and may only say the birth is held in honour.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panchaakkaram","andhanar","vedham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:941":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":941,"t":["ஆகின்ற பாதமும் அந்நாவாய் நின்றிடும்","ஆகின்ற நாபியுள் அங்கே மகாரமாம்","ஆகின்ற சீயுரு தோள்வவ்வாய்க் கண்டபின்","ஆகின்ற வச்சுடர் யவ்வியல் பாமே."],"p":"","r":"NA stands at the feet, MA at the navel, SI and VA are seen at the shoulders, and the light above is of the nature of YA.","n":"The syllables are placed on the body from foot to crown. சீயுரு தோள் is unclear: சி may be at the chest or with வ at the shoulders.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panchaakkaram","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:942":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":942,"t":["அவ்வியல் பாய இருமூன்று எழுத்தையும்","செவ்வியல் பாகச் சிறந்தனன் நந்தியும்","ஒவ்வியல் பாக ஒளியுற நோக்கிடில்","பவ்வியல் பாகப் பரந்துநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"Nandi excelled in giving the six letters of that nature their fine nature; if one looks at them alike and filled with light, he stands spread through them.","n":"இருமூன்று எழுத்து, six letters, is commonly Om with the five syllables. ஒவ்வியல், பவ்வியல் play on the letters ஒ and ப and are obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:943":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":943,"t":["பரந்தது மந்திரம் பல்லுயிர்க் கெல்லாம்","வரந்தரு மந்திரம் வாய்த்திட வாங்கித்","துரந்திடு மந்திரம் சூழ்பகை போக","உரந்தரு மந்திரம் ஓமென்று எழுப்பே."],"p":"","r":"The mantra spreads to all lives; it is the boon-giving mantra; take it so it fits; it is the mantra that drives away surrounding enemies, the mantra that gives strength: raise it with Om.","n":"சூழ்பகை (surrounding enmity) may be outward enemies or inner foes; both readings stand, and the chapter's closing rites (997 to 1002) show the outward sense was also in view.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","piranavam","pagai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:944":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":944,"t":["ஓமென்று எழுப்பிதன் உத்தம நந்தியை","நாமென்று எழுப்பி நடுவெழு தீபத்தை","ஆமென்று எழுப்பிஅவ் வாறுஅறி வார்கள்","மாமன்று கண்டு மகிழ்ந்திருந் தாரே."],"p":"","r":"Raising the excellent Nandi with Om, raising the lamp that rises in the middle with NAM, raising with AAM: those who know that way saw the great hall and rejoiced.","n":"மாமன்று is the great hall, the அம்பலம் of the chapter's title, here seen within. நாம், ஆம் may be syllables or words ('we', 'it is').","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piranavam","sivam","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:945":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":945,"t":["ஆகின்ற சக்கரத் துள்ளே எழுத்துஐந்தும்","பாகொன்றி நின்ற பதங்களில் வார்த்திக்கும்","ஆகின்ற ஐம்பத்து ஓரெழுத்து உள்நிற்கப்","யாகொன்றி நிற்கும் பராபரன் தானே."],"p":"","r":"Within the chakra the five letters are poured into the steps joined part by part; with the fifty-one letters within, the Supreme beyond stands joined to it.","n":"பாகொன்றி and வார்த்திக்கும் are obscure; யாகொன்றி may be a misprint for ஆகொன்றி.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["chakkaram","panchaakkaram","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:946":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":946,"t":["பரமாய அஞ்செழுத்து உள்நடு வாகப்","பரமாய நவசிவ பார்க்கில் மவயரசி","பரமாய சியநம வாம்பரத்து ஓதில்","பரமாய வாசி மயநமாய் நின்றே."],"p":"","r":"With the supreme five letters set in the middle, further orders of the syllables are given, ending in the form VASI-MAYANAMA.","n":"The strings (நவசிவ, மவயரசி, சியநம, வாசி மயநம) do not form the clean rotations of 923 and include a stray ர; they are likely corrupt.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panchaakkaram","chakkaram"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:947":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":947,"t":["நின்ற எழுத்துகள் நேர்தரு பூதமும்","நின்ற எழுத்துகள் நேர்தரு வண்ணமும்","நின்ற எழுத்துகள் நேர்தர நின்றிடில்","நின்ற எழுத்துள்ளும் நின்றனன் தானே."],"p":"","r":"The elements match the letters, the colours match the letters; when the letters stand matched, he stands within those letters.","n":"Each syllable is traditionally given an element and a colour; the verse doesn't list them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","thaththuvam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:948":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":948,"t":["நின்றது சக்கரம் நீளும் புவியெல்லாம்","மன்றது வாய்நின்ற மாயநன் னாடனைக்","கன்றது வாகக் கறந்தனன் நந்தியும்","குன்றிடை நின்றிடும் கொள்கையன் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"The chakra stands for the whole wide earth; Nandi, as a calf, drew milk from the good Lord of the land of maya who stands as the hall, and he is one whose way is to stand on the mountain.","n":"The calf milking its mother is a common image for drawing grace by love. Who is the calf and who is milked is not certain, and குன்று may be Kailasa or a yogic height.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["chakkaram","sivam","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:949":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":949,"t":["கொண்டஇச் சக்கரத் துள்ளே குணம்பல","கொண்டஇச் சக்கரத் துள்ளே குறிஐந்து","கொண்டஇச் சக்கரங் கூத்தன் எழுத்துஐந்தும்","கொண்டஇச் சக்கரத் துள்நின்ற கூத்தே."],"p":"","r":"Within this chakra are many qualities, five signs and the Dancer's five letters, and the dance stands within it.","n":"The dance (கூத்து) of the hall is set inside the drawn diagram; the five signs are not named.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["chakkaram","panchaakkaram","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:950":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":950,"t":["வெளியில் இரேகை இரேகையி லத்தலை","சுளியில் உகாரமாம் சுற்றிய வன்னி","நெளிதரும் கால்கொம்பு நோவிந்து நாதம்","தெளியும் பிரகாரம் சிவமந் திரமே."],"p":"","r":"Outside a line, on the line its head, in the curl U, fire around it, the bending stroke and horn, O, bindu and nada: this clear plan is the Siva mantra.","n":"The verse names the strokes of the written letters (கால், கொம்பு, the curl and the dot) as parts of the drawn mantra; the figure itself can't be reconstructed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","vindhu","naadham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:951":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":951,"t":["அகார உகார சிகார நடுவாய்","வகாரமோடு ஆறும் வளியுடன் கூடிச்","சிகார முடனே சிவன்சிந்தை செய்ய","ஓகார முதல்வன் உவந்துநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"With A, U and SI in the middle, VA and the six joined with the breath, and with SI contemplating Siva, the Lord of O stands pleased.","n":"A near-variant of 976 and 982. 'The six' (ஆறும்) is unexplained.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","praanan","piranavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:952":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":952,"t":["அற்ற இடத்தே அகாரமது ஆவது","உற்ற இடத்தே உறுபொருள் கண்டிடச்","செற்றம் அறுத்த செழுஞ்சுடர் மெய்ப்பொருள்","குற்றம் அறுத்த பொன்போலும் குளிகையே."],"p":"","r":"Where all ceases, A arises; where it is reached, the true object is seen; that bright light which cut away anger, the true object, is a pill like gold with its dross removed.","n":"குளிகை is the alchemist's pill that turns base metal to gold; the image makes the realised mantra a transforming pill. அற்ற இடம் may be the place where breath or thought stops.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","meyyunarvu","malam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:953":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":953,"t":["அவ்வென்ற போதினில் உவ்வெழுத் தாலித்தால்","உவ்வென்ற முத்தி உருகிக் கலந்திடும்","மவ்வென்று என்னுள்ளே வழிப்பட்ட நந்தியை","எவ்வணஞ் சொல்லுகேன் எந்தை இயற்கையே."],"p":"","r":"When A is sounded with U, the release that is U melts and mingles; how shall I tell the nature of my father, Nandi, who came into me as MA?","n":"அ, உ, ம are the parts of Om; the verse moves from the syllable to the speaker's experience of the Lord within.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piranavam","veedu","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:954":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":954,"t":["நீரில் எழுத்துஇவ் வுலகர் அறிவது","வானில் எழுத்தொன்று கண்டறிவார் இல்லை","யாரிவ் வெழுத்தை அறிவார் அவர்கள்","ஊனில் எழுத்தை உணர்கிலர் தாமே."],"p":"","r":"What the people of this world know is letters on water; no one sees and knows the one letter in the sky; and those who know this letter do not perceive the letter in the flesh.","n":"Writing on water is the stock image of what doesn't last. The last two lines can be read as a rebuke (even knowers miss the letter in the body) or as 'who knows this letter? they do not perceive it in the flesh'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","yaakkai","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:955":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":955,"t":["காலை நடுவுறக் காயத்தில் அக்கரம்","மாலை நடுவுற ஐம்பதும் ஆவன","மேலை நடுவுற வேதம் விளம்பிய","மூலம் நடுவுற முத்திதந் தானே."],"p":"","r":"At morning the letter is centred in the body, at evening the fifty letters are; with the root spoken by the Veda centred above, he gave release.","n":"நடுவுற (being centred) in every line suggests stages of practice through the day; மூலம் may be the root mantra or the root centre.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:956":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":956,"t":["நாவியின் கீழது நல்ல எழுத்தொன்று","பாவிகள் அத்தின் பயனறி வாரில்லை","ஓவிய ராலும் அறியவொண் ணாத\\து","தேவியும் தானும் திகழ்ந்திருந் தானே."],"p":"","r":"Below the navel is one good letter; sinners don't know its use; it can't be known even by painters; the Goddess and he shine there together.","n":"ஓவியர் may be painters (it can't be drawn) or those who have ceased (renouncers). நாவி may be the navel or musk. The centre below the navel is traditionally the base of kundalini.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["mandhiram","saththi","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:957":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":957,"t":["அவ்வொடு சவ்வென்ற தரனுற்ற மந்திரம்","அவ்வொடு சவ்வென்றது ஆரும் அறிகிலர்","அவ்வொடு சவ்வென்றது ஆரும் அறிந்தபின்","அவ்வொடு சவ்வும் அனாதியும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"The mantra Hara attained is A with SA; no one knows A with SA; once someone knows it, A with SA becomes the beginningless as well.","n":"அவ்வொடு சவ்வு is unexplained: perhaps Siva and Shakti, or the syllables of a seed mantra. அதரன் may be அ(த்)து அரன் or a variant.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","sivam","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:958":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":958,"t":["மந்திரம் ஒன்றுள் மலரால் உதிப்பது","உந்தியின் உள்ளே உதயம்பண் ணாநிற்குஞ்","சந்திசெய் யாநிற்பர் தாமது அறிகிலர்","அந்தி தொழுதுபோய் ஆர்த்துஅகன் றார்களே."],"p":"","r":"One mantra, springing from the flower, rises within the navel; people perform the twilight rites without knowing it, worship at dusk, and go off making noise.","n":"A rebuke of outer ritual: சந்தி (the twilight rite) done aloud without knowing the mantra that rises within. The flower (மலர்) is the inner lotus as tradition reads it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:959":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":959,"t":["சேவிக்கு மந்திரம் செல்லும் திசைபெற","ஆவிக்குள் மந்திரம் ஆதார மாவன","பூவிக்குள் மந்திரம் போக்கற நோக்கிடில்","ஆவிக்குள் மந்திரம் அங்குச மாமே."],"p":"","r":"The mantra of worship gives direction; the mantras within the life are its supports; if one looks at the mantra within the flower without letting it go, the mantra within the life is a goad.","n":"ஆதாரம் is the yogic support-centre or simply a base. The goad (அங்குசம்) anticipates 977.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","uyir","adakkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:960":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":960,"t":["அருவினில் அம்பரம் அங்கெழு நாதம்","பெருகு துடியிடை பேணிய விந்து","மருவி யகார சிகார நடுவாய்","உருவிட ஊறும் உறுமந் திரமே."],"p":"","r":"In the formless is the sky, where nada rises; in the swelling drum-shaped waist is the cherished bindu; joining in the middle of YA and SI, the true mantra wells up as it takes form.","n":"துடியிடை (the drum-shaped middle) may be the waist of the body or of Shakti. The verse places nada above and bindu below.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naadham","vindhu","mandhiram"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:961":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":961,"t":["விந்துவும் நாதமும் மேவி யுடன்கூடிச்","சந்திர னோடே தலைப்படு மாயிடில்","அந்தர வானத்து அமுதம்வந்து ஊறிடும்","அங்குதி மந்திரம் ஆகுதி யாமே."],"p":"","r":"When bindu and nada join and meet the moon, nectar comes and wells up in the inner sky, and the mantra rising there becomes the oblation.","n":"The moon is traditionally the centre at the head from which nectar flows in yoga. The fire rite is turned inward: the mantra itself is the oblation. அங்குதி is read as அங்கு உதி(த்த).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vindhu","naadham","amudham","aaguthi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:962":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":962,"t":["ஆறெழுத்து ஓதும் அறிவார் அறிகிலர்","ஆறெழுத்து ஒன்றாக ஓதி உணரார்கள்","வேறெழுத்து இன்றி விளம்பவல் லார்கட்கு","ஓரெழுத்தாலே உயிர்பெற லாமே."],"p":"","r":"Those who recite the six letters don't know them, nor recite the six as one and understand; for those who can utter it with no other letter, by one letter life can be won.","n":"The six letters are commonly Om with the five syllables, and the one letter Om or சி. உயிர்பெறல் may be gaining life (long life) or gaining the soul's release.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","piranavam","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:963":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":963,"t":["ஓதும் எழுத்தோடு உயிர்க்கலை மூவைஞ்சும்","ஆதி எழுத்தவை ஐம்பதோடு ஒன்றென்பர்","சோதி எழுத்தினில் ஐயிரு மூன்றுள","நாத எழுத்திட்டு நாடிக்கொள் ளீரே."],"p":"","r":"With the recited letters go the soul-letters, three fives; the first letters are said to be fifty-one; in the light-letters there are ten and three more; set down the nada letter and seek.","n":"உயிர்க்கலை மூவைஞ்சு (fifteen) and ஐயிரு மூன்று may count vowels and other letter groups; the arithmetic is not clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","naadham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:964":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":964,"t":["விந்துவி லும்சுழி நாதம் எழுந்திடப்","பந்தத் தலைவி பதினாறு கலையதாம்","சுந்தர வாகரங் கால்உடம்பு ஆயினாள்","அந்தமும் இன்றியே ஐம்பத்தொன்று ஆயதே."],"p":"","r":"As nada rises spiralling from bindu, the mistress of bonds becomes sixteen kalas; she took the beautiful letter-form with breath as her body, and without end became the fifty-one.","n":"பந்தத் தலைவி (mistress of bonds) is Shakti as the power that binds and releases. The sixteen kalas are traditionally the vowels or the moon's digits; the fifty-one are the letters.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","vindhu","naadham","kalai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:965":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":965,"t":["ஐம்பது எழுத்தே அனைத்தும்வே தங்களும்","ஐம்பது எழுத்தே அனைத்துஆக மங்களும்","ஐம்பது எழுத்தேயும் ஆவது அறிந்தபின்","ஐம்பது எழுத்தும்போய் அஞ்செழுத் தாமே."],"p":"","r":"The fifty letters are all the Vedas and all the Agamas; once one knows what the fifty letters become, the fifty go and become the five letters.","n":"The whole of scripture is reduced to its letters, and the letters to the five syllables. The verse recurs almost word for word as 2698.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panchaakkaram","vedham","aagamam","mandhiram"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:966":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":966,"t":["அஞ்செழுத் தால்ஐந்து பூதம் படைத்தனன்","அஞ்செழுத் தால்பல யோனி படைத்தனன்","அஞ்செழுத் தால்இவ் அகலிடம் தாங்கினன்","அஞ்செழுத் தாலே அமர்ந்து நின்றானே."],"p":"","r":"By the five letters he made the five elements, by them the many wombs of birth, by them he bears this wide world, and by them he stands seated.","n":"யோனி is the kinds of birth, traditionally 84 lakh. The five syllables are made the instrument of creation and support.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panchaakkaram","aindhozhil","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:967":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":967,"t":["வீழ்ந்தெழு லாம்விகிர் தன்திரு நாமத்தைச்","சோர்ந்தொழி யாமல் தொடங்கும் ஒருவற்குச்","சார்ந்த வினைத்துயர் போகத் தலைவனும்","போந்திடும் என்னும் புரிசடை யோனே."],"p":"","r":"To one who, falling and rising, begins the holy name of the Transcendent and does not tire and give it up, the Lord comes to take away the grief of the deeds that cling, says the one with coiled locks.","n":"வீழ்ந்தெழல் may be falling down in worship and rising, or falling and rising in life. The final line puts the promise in the Lord's own mouth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","vinai","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:968":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":968,"t":["உண்ணும் மருந்தும் உலப்பிலி காலமும்","பண்ணுறு கேள்வியும் பாடலு மாய்நிற்கும்","விண்ணின்று அமரர் விரும்பி அடிதொழ","எண்ணின்று எழுத்துஅஞ்சும் ஆகிநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"He stands as the food one eats and the medicine, endless time, learned hearing and song; while the gods in heaven long to worship his feet, he has become the five letters, beyond reckoning.","n":"எண்ணின்று may be 'beyond count' or 'standing in thought'. The gods worship from afar what is near in the syllables.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panchaakkaram","sivam","marundhu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:969":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":969,"t":["ஐந்தின் பெருமையே அகலிடம் ஆவதும்","ஐந்தின் பெருமையே ஆலயம் ஆவதும்","ஐந்தின் பெருமையே அறவோன் வழக்கமும்","ஐந்தின் வகைசெயப் பாலனும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"By the greatness of the five comes the wide world, by it the temple, by it the way of the virtuous one, and by working the five in their kinds one becomes the protector.","n":"ஐந்து (the five) is in context the five syllables, but could be the five acts or elements. பாலன் may be the protector or a child (one guarded).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panchaakkaram","vazhipaadu","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:970":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":970,"t":["வேரெழத் தாய்விண்ணாய் அப்புறமாய் நிற்கும்","நீரெழுத் தாய்நிலந் தாங்கியும் அங்குளன்","சீரெழுத் தாய்அங்கி யாய்உயி ராம்எழுத்து","ஓரெழுத்து ஈசனும் ஓண்சுட ராமே."],"p":"","r":"As the root letter he is sky and beyond; as the water letter he bears the earth too; as the fine letter he is fire; and the letter that is life, the one-letter Lord, is bright light.","n":"Each syllable is matched to an element; the verse's assignments don't plainly match the usual scheme (ந earth, ம water, சி fire, வ air, ய sky). வேரெழத்து looks like a misprint for வேரெழுத்து.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panchaakkaram","thaththuvam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:971":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":971,"t":["நாலாம் எழுத்துஓசை ஞாலம் உருவது","நாலாம் எழுத்தினுள் ஞாலம் அடங்கியது","நாலாம் எழுத்தே நவிலவல் லார்கட்டு","நாலாம் எழுத்தது நன்னெறி தானே."],"p":"","r":"The sound of the fourth letter is the world's form; within the fourth letter the world is contained; for those who can recite the fourth letter, it is the good path.","n":"The fourth letter is commonly taken as வ, fourth in நமசிவாய and traditionally Shakti's letter; it may also be a number for another syllable.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panchaakkaram","mandhiram"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:972":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":972,"t":["இயைந்தனள் ஏந்திழை என்னுளம் மேவி","நயந்தனள் அங்கே நமசிவ என்னும்","பயந்தனை யோரும் பதமது பற்றும்","பெயர்ந்தனன் மற்றும் பிதற்றுஅறுத் தேனே."],"p":"","r":"The bejewelled lady joined, entered my heart and loved there the word NAMASIVA; she holds the step that knows the fearful, she moved, and I cut away all other babble.","n":"The verse recurs as 1115 with அயன்தனை for பயந்தனை and with the lady, not the speaker, cutting the babble (பெயர்ந்தனள், அறுத்தாளே). பயந்தனை ஓரும் is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","panchaakkaram","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:973":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":973,"t":["ஆமத்து இனிதிருந்து அன்ன மயத்தினை","ஓமத்தி லேயுதம் பண்ணும் ஒருத்திதன்","நாம நமசிவ என்றிருப் பாருக்கு","நேமத் தலைவி நிலவிநின் றாளே."],"p":"","r":"For those who stay saying the name NAMASIVA, the lady of the rule shines: the one woman who stays sweetly in the stomach and in the fire raises up the sheath of food.","n":"ஆமம் (the stomach, or undigested food), அன்னமயம் (the food-sheath) and ஓமம் (the fire rite) suggest Shakti as the digestive fire that turns food into the body; this is a traditional reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","panchaakkaram","aaguthi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:974":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":974,"t":["பட்ட பரிசே பரமஞ் செழுத்ததின்","இட்டம் அறிந்திட்டு இரவு பகல்வர","நட்டமது ஆடும் நடுவே நிலையங்கொண்டு","அட்டதே சப்பொருள் ஆகிநின் றாளே."],"p":"","r":"Knowing the liking of the supreme five letters as is fitting, while night and day come, she takes her place in the middle where the dance is danced and stands as the substance of the eight directions.","n":"பட்ட பரிசே and பரமஞ் செழுத்ததின் are compressed. Night and day may be the two breaths; the middle, the central channel or the hall's centre.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","panchaakkaram"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:975":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":975,"t":["அகாரம் உயிரே உகாரம் பரமே","மகார மலமாய் வருமுப் பதத்தில்","சிகாரம் சிவமாய் வகாரம் வடிவமாய்","யகாரம் உயிரென்று அறையலும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"A is the soul, U the supreme and MA the impurity in the three-part word; SI is Siva, VA the form, and YA, one may say, is the soul.","n":"Both Om and the five syllables are read as the Siddhanta categories. Tradition takes வ as Shakti, the Lord's form (வடிவம்) of grace. A is made the soul here, where Kural 1 makes A the image of the Lord.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piranavam","panchaakkaram","pasu","malam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:976":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":976,"t":["நகார மகார சிகார நடுவாய்","வகாரம் இரண்டும் வளியுடன் கூடி","ஓகார முதற்கொண்டு ஒருக்கால் உரைக்க","மகார முதல்வன் மனத்தகத் தானே."],"p":"","r":"With NA, MA and SI in the middle, the two VAs joined with the breath, beginning with O and uttered once, the first Lord MA is within the mind.","n":"'The two VAs' and 'Lord MA' are unexplained; the verse is a near-doublet of 982 and close to 951.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panchaakkaram","piranavam","praanan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:977":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":977,"t":["அஞ்சுள வானை அடவியுள் வாழ்வன","அஞ்சுக்கும் அஞ்செழுத்து அங்குசம் ஆவன","அஞ்சையும் கூடத் தடுக்கவல் லார்கட்கே","அஞ்சாதி ஆதி அகம்புக லாமே."],"p":"","r":"Five elephants live in the forest; for the five, the five letters are the goad; for those who can hold all five together, the First beyond the five can enter within.","n":"The five elephants are the senses, though the verse doesn't name them. அஞ்சாதி ஆதி may be 'the First before the five' or 'the fearless First'; அகம் புகல் may be his entering the heart or their entering his.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panchaakkaram","adakkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:978":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":978,"t":["ஐந்து கலையில் அகராதி தன்னிலே","வந்த நகராதி மாற்றி மகராதி","நந்தியை மூலத்தே நாடிப் பரையொடும்","சந்திசெய் வார்க்குச் சடங்கில்லை தானே."],"p":"","r":"In the five kalas, beginning with A, the order beginning with NA is changed to begin with MA; for those who seek Nandi at the root and join with the Supreme Shakti, there is no ritual.","n":"சந்திசெய்வார் plays on the twilight rite (சந்தி) and joining: the inner union replaces the outer rite. மூலம் is traditionally the base centre.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panchaakkaram","kalai","saththi","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:979":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":979,"t":["மருவும் சிவாயமே மன்னும் உயிரும்","அருமந்த போகமும் ஞானமும் ஆகும்","தெருள்வந்த சீவனார் சென்றுஇவற் றாலே","அருள்தங்கி அச்சிவமம் ஆவது வீடே."],"p":"","r":"SIVAYA, joined, becomes the abiding soul, the precious enjoyment and knowledge; the soul that has come to clarity goes on by these, grace settles in it, and becoming that Siva is release.","n":"சிவாய without நம is traditionally the subtle form of the mantra, with the impurity (ம) and concealment (ந) dropped, as in the ninth tantra's subtle pancakshara.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panchaakkaram","gnaanam","thiruvarul","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:980":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":980,"t":["அஞ்சுக அஞ்செழுத்து உண்மை அறிந்தபின்","நெஞ்சுகத்து உள்ளே நிலையும் பராபரம்","வஞ்சகம் இல்லை மனைக்கும் அழிவில்லை","தஞ்சம் இதுவென்று சாற்றுகின் றேனே."],"p":"","r":"Once the truth of the five letters is known, the Supreme stays firm within the heart; there is no deceit and no ruin to the house; I declare that this is refuge.","n":"அஞ்சுக (opening) may be 'fear' (imperative) or a form of அஞ்சு, five; மனை (house) may be the household or the body.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panchaakkaram","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:981":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":981,"t":["சிவாயவொடு அவ்வே தெளிந்துஉளத்து ஓதச்","சிவாயவொடு அவ்வே சிவனுரு வாகும்","சிவாயவொடு அவ்வும் தெளியவல் லார்கள்","சிவாயவொடு அவ்வே தெளிந்திருந் தாரே."],"p":"","r":"Recited clearly in the heart, SIVAYA with A becomes Siva's form; those who can see SIVAYA with A clearly stayed in that clarity.","n":"அவ்வே may be the letter அ added to சிவாய, or simply 'that'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panchaakkaram","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:982":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":982,"t":["சிகார வகார யகார முடனே","நகார மகார நடுவுற நாடி","ஓகார முடனே ஒருகால் உரைக்க","மகார முதல்வன் மதித்துநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"With SI, VA and YA, and NA and MA sought in the middle, uttered once with O, the first Lord MA stands esteemed.","n":"A near-doublet of 976, beginning from சி rather than ந.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panchaakkaram","piranavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:983":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":983,"t":["நம்முதல் ஓர்ஐந்தின் நாடுங் கருமங்கள்","அம்முதல் ஐந்தில் அடங்கிய வல்வினை","சிம்முதல் உள்ளே தெளியவல் லார்கட்குத்","தம்முதல் ஆகும் சதாசிவந் தானே."],"p":"","r":"The acts sought in the five beginning with NA, the hard deeds contained in the five beginning with A: for those who can see clearly within the first SI, Sadasiva becomes their own First.","n":"The two groups of five (from ந and from அ) may be the five syllables and the five vowels; the point is that seeing சி first frees from deeds.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panchaakkaram","vinai","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:984":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":984,"t":["நவமும் சிவமும் உயிர்பர மாகும்","தவமொன்று இலாதன தத்துவம் ஆகும்","சிவம்ஒன்றி ஆய்பவர்ஆதர வால்அச்","சிவம்என்ப தானாம் எனும்தெளி வுற்றதே."],"p":"","r":"NAVA and SIVA become the soul and the Supreme; what lacks tapas is only tattva; those who search, at one with Siva, in devotion reached the clarity that they are what is called Siva.","n":"நவம் is obscure: perhaps ந and ம (soul and bond), or 'the new'. The closing claim, that the seeker becomes Siva, is the Siddhanta goal of oneness without loss of the soul, as tradition reads it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","pasu","thaththuvam","thavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:985":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":985,"t":["கூடிய எட்டும் இரண்டும் குவிந்தறி","நாடிய நந்தியை ஞானத்துள் ளேவைத்து","ஆடிய ஐவரும் அங்குஉறவு ஆவார்கள்","தேடி அதனைத் தெளிந்தறி யீரே."],"p":"","r":"Know by gathering the eight and the two; set Nandi whom you sought within knowledge, and the five who danced become kin there; seek it and know it clearly.","n":"In Tamil numerals அ is 8 and உ is 2, so எட்டும் இரண்டும் is traditionally read as the letters அ and உ. The five who danced are the senses, now made kin.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","sivam","adakkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:986":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":986,"t":["எட்டும் இரண்டும் இனிதுஅறி கின்றிலர்","எட்டும் இரண்டும் அறியாத ஏழையர்","எட்டும் இரண்டும் இருமூன்று நான்கெனப்","பட்டது சித்தாந்த சன்மார்க்க பாதமே."],"p":"","r":"They don't know the eight and two well; the poor who don't know them miss that the eight and two, as six and four, are the step of the true path of Siddhanta.","n":"இருமூன்று நான்கு (two threes and four, or six and four) is unexplained. சித்தாந்த சன்மார்க்கம் names the path explicitly.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:987":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":987,"t":["எட்டு வரையின்மேல் எட்டு வரைகீறி","இட்ட நடுவுள் இறைவன் எழுத்தொன்றில்","வட்டத்தி லேயறை நாற்பத்தெட் டும்இட்டுச்","சிட்டஞ் செழுத்தும் செபிசீக் கிரமே."],"p":"","r":"Draw eight lines across eight lines, set one letter of the Lord in the middle, place the forty-eight cells within the circle, and recite the five letters quickly.","n":"Eight lines each way make seven by seven, forty-nine cells: the centre for the Lord's letter and forty-eight around it. சிட்டஞ்செழுத்து is read as the prescribed (சிட்ட) five letters.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["chakkaram","panchaakkaram"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:988":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":988,"t":["தானவர் சட்டர் சதிரர் இருவர்கள்","ஆனஇம் மூவரோடு ஆற்றவர் ஆதிகள்","ஏனைப் பதினைந்தும் விந்துவும் நாதமும்","சேனையும் செய்சிவ சக்கரந் தானே."],"p":"","r":"Danavas, the Sattars, the two Chatirars, with these three the Atravar and the rest, the other fifteen, bindu and nada and the host: this is the Siva chakra.","n":"The names are classes of beings or letter groups placed in the cells; most can't be identified.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["chakkaram","vindhu","naadham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:989":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":989,"t":["பட்டனம் மாதவம் ஆறும் பராபரம்","விட்டனர் தம்மை விகிர்தா நமஎன்பர்","எட்டனை யாயினும் ஈசன் திறத்திறம்","ஒட்டுவன் பேசுவன் ஒன்றறி யேனே"],"p":"","r":"The six of great penance who suffered and the Supreme; those who gave up the self say 'to the Transcendent, nama'; however many the eight ways of the Lord, I join them and speak, I know nothing else.","n":"The first line is obscure. விகிர்தா நம (homage to the Transcendent) is the mantra form; the last line is the speaker's humility.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:990":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":990,"t":["சிவன்முதல் மூவரோடு ஐவர் சிறந்த","அவைமுதல் ஆறிரண்டு ஒன்றொடுஒன்று ஆன","அவைமுதல் விந்துவும் நாதமும் ஓங்கச்","சவைமுதல் சங்கரன் தன்பெயர் தானே."],"p":"","r":"Siva first, with the three and the five excelling; from them twelve, one with one; from them bindu and nada rising; first of the assembly is Sankara's own name.","n":"The counts likely place deities or letters in the 49-cell grid of 987; சவை (assembly) may be the hall.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["chakkaram","vindhu","naadham","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:991":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":991,"t":["வித்தாம் செகமய மாக வரைகீறி","நத்தார் கலைகள் பதினாறு நாட்டிப்பின்","உத்தாரம் பன்னிரண்டு ஆதி கலைதொரும்","பத்தாம் பிரம சடங்குபார்த்து ஓதிடே."],"p":"","r":"Draw lines making the world as seed, set the sixteen kalas of the conch, then twelve in each kala from the first, and recite looking to the tenth, the Brahma rite.","n":"நத்தார் கலை (kalas of the conch, or the moon's), உத்தாரம் and பத்தாம் பிரம சடங்கு are obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["chakkaram","kalai","mandhiram"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:992":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":992,"t":["கண்டெடுந் தேன்கம லம்மலர் உள்ளிடை","கொண்டெழுந் தேன்உடன் கூடிய காலத்துப்","பண்டழி யாத பதிவழி யேசென்று","நண்பழி யாமே நமவென வாமே."],"p":"","r":"I found the honey within the lotus flower and rose with it; at the time of joining, going by the Lord's way that never perished of old, without losing the bond, it becomes 'nama'.","n":"கண்டெடுந்தேன் puns on 'I found and took up' and 'honey'. பதிவழி may be the way of the Lord (பதி) or the settled way.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","pathi","thiyaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:993":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":993,"t":["புண்ணிய வானவர் பூமழை தூவிநின்று","எண்ணுவர் அண்ணல் இணையடி மந்திரம்","நண்ணுவர் நண்ணி நமஎன்று நாமத்தைக்","கண்ணென உன்னிக் கலந்துநின் றாரே."],"p":"","r":"The virtuous gods stand showering flowers and meditate on the mantra of the Lord's two feet; they draw near and, saying 'nama' with the name and holding it dear as their eyes, stand merged with it.","n":"கண்என உன்னி (thinking of it as one's eye) means holding it most dear.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","vazhipaadu","panchaakkaram"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:994":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":994,"t":["ஆறெழுத்தாவது ஆறு மந்திரங்கள்","ஆறுக்கு நாலே இருபத்து நாலென்பர்","சாவித் திரியில் தலையெழுத்து ஒன்றுள","பேதிக்க வல்லார் பிறவியற் றார்களே."],"p":"","r":"The six letters are six mantras; for the six, four each, twenty-four, they say; in the Savitri there is one head-letter, and those who can discern it have ended their births.","n":"The Savitri (Gayatri) has twenty-four syllables, which the verse matches to six times four. A variant reads சமயங்கள் for மந்திரங்கள்: the six letters would then be the six religious systems.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","piravi","samayam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:995":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":995,"t":["எட்டினில் எட்டறை யிட்டு அறையிலே","கூட்டிய ஒன்றெட்டாய்க் காண நிறையிட்டுச்","சுட்டி இவற்றைப் பிரணவம் சூழ்ந்திட்டு","மட்டும் உயிர்கட்டு உமாபதி யானுண்டே."],"p":"","r":"Set eight cells in eight, fill the cells so that each joined one shows as eight, mark them and surround them with Om: for lives in bondage, Umapati is there.","n":"The filling rule may be a magic square of eights. மட்டும் உயிர்கட்டு is obscure: perhaps 'for lives bound' or 'binding lives'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["chakkaram","piranavam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:996":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":996,"t":["நம்முதல் அவ்வொடு நாவினர் ஆகியே","அம்முதல் ஆகிய எட்டிடை யுற்றிட்டு","உம்முதல் ஆகவே உணர்பவர் உச்சிமேல்","உம்முதல் ஆயவன் உற்றுநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"Those who take NA as first with A and hold it on the tongue, reaching within the eight beginning with A, and perceive U as first: on their crown the one who is first in U comes and stays.","n":"The play is on letters as 'firsts'. உச்சி (crown) is the traditional head centre where the Lord is met.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panchaakkaram","piranavam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:997":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":997,"t":["நின்ற அரசம் பலகைமேல் நேராக","ஒன்றிட மவ்விட்டு ஓலையிற் சாதகம்","துன்று மெழுகையுள் பூசிச் சுடரிடைத்","தன்ற வெதுப்பிடத் தம்பனங் காணுமே."],"p":"","r":"On a pipal board, set MA in one place, draw the figure on palm-leaf, smear dense wax over it and warm it in the flame, and fixing (stambhana) is seen.","n":"997 to 1002 give rites for worldly ends: தம்பனம் (fixing or paralysing), மோகனம் (enchanting), உச்சாடனம் (driving away), மாரணம் (killing) and வசியம் (attracting), traditionally among the six acts (ஷட்கர்மம்) of mantra practice. The verses state technique without comment, and many words of material and direction are uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["chakkaram","mandhiram","siththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:998":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":998,"t":["கரண இரளிப் பலகை யமன்திசை","மரணம் இட்டு எட்டின் மகார எழுத்திட்டு","வரணமில் ஐங்காயம் பூசி அடுப்பிடை","முரணிற் புதைத்திட மோகன மாகுமே."],"p":"","r":"Set a board of karanai wood toward Yama's quarter, write 'death', set the letter MA in the eight, smear it with the five astringents and bury it by a hearth, and enchanting (mohana) follows.","n":"கரண இரளி may be two woods or one; மரணம் இட்டு may be writing the word or the name of the target. Yama's quarter is the south.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["chakkaram","mandhiram","siththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:999":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":999,"t":["ஆங்கு வடமேற்கில் ஐயனார் கோட்டத்தில்","பாங்கு படவே பலாசப் பலகையில்","காங்கரு மேட்டில் கடுப்பூசி விந்துவிட்டு","ஓங்காமல் வைத்திடும் உச்சாட னத்துக்கே."],"p":"","r":"There in the north-west, in Ayyanar's shrine, on a board of palasa wood, smear it with bitter stuff, set the bindu, and place it so it doesn't rise: this is for driving away (uchchatana).","n":"காங்கரு மேட்டில் is obscure; கடு may be a bitter or astringent paste.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["chakkaram","mandhiram","siththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:1000":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1000,"t":["உச்சியம் போதில் ஒளிவன்னி மூலையில்","பச்சோலை யில் பஞ்ச காயத்தைப் பாரித்து","முச்சது ரத்தின் முதுகாட்டில் வைத்திட","அச்சமற மேலோர் மாரணம் வேண்டிலே."],"p":"","r":"At noon, in the fire-corner, spread the five astringents on a green palm-leaf and place it in a triple square in the old cremation ground, if one wants killing (marana) without fear.","n":"The fire-corner is the south-east. முச்சதுரம் may be a triple square or triangle. அச்சமற மேலோர் may mean 'the great, without fear' or 'without fear, over the enemy'. The verse gives the rite without approving or condemning it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["chakkaram","mandhiram","kolai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:1001":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1001,"t":["ஏய்ந்த அரிதாரம் ஏட்டின்மே லேபூசி","ஏய்ந்த அகாரம் உகாரம் எழுத்திட்டு","வாய்ந்ததோர் வில்லம் பலகை வசியத்துக்கு","ஏய்ந்தவைத்து எண்பதி னாயிரம் வேண்டிலே."],"p":"","r":"Smear yellow orpiment on the leaf, write the letters A and U, place it fittingly on a bilva board for attracting (vasiya), and recite eighty thousand times.","n":"எண்பதினாயிரம் (eighty thousand) is the count of recitations, as tradition reads it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["chakkaram","mandhiram","siththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:1002":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1002,"t":["எண்ணாக் கருடனை ஏட்டில் உகாரமிட்டு","எண்ணாப் பொனefனாளிf எழுவெள்ளி பூசிடா","வெண்ணாவல் பலகையில் இட்டுமேற் கேநோக்கி","எண்ணா எழுத்தோடுஎண்ணாயிரம் வேண்டிலே."],"p":"","r":"For Garuda, write U on the leaf, smear it with gold and silver, place it on a white naval-wood board facing west, and recite eight thousand times with the letters.","n":"எண்ணாக் கருடன் is obscure: perhaps a rite against poison invoking Garuda. Line 2 is corrupt in the source.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["chakkaram","mandhiram","siththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:81"},"t:1003":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1003,"t":["அம்புயம் நீலம் கழுநீர் அணிநெய்தல்","வம்பவிழ் பூகமும் மாதவி மந்தாரம்","தும்பை வகுளம் சுரபுனனை மல்லிகை","செண்பகம் பாதிரி செவ்வந்தி சாத்திடே."],"p":"","r":"Offer lotus, blue lily, water lily, the fine neythal, fragrant areca blossom, madhavi, mandaram, thumbai, vakulam, surapunnai, jasmine, champak, pathiri and chrysanthemum.","n":"The verse is only a list of flowers ending in the command சாத்திடே (offer, adorn). It sets the outward rite that 1008 will say some need not perform.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:82"},"t:1004":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1004,"t":["சாங்கம தாகவே சந்தொடு சந்தனம்","தேங்கமழ் குங்குமம் கர்ப்பூரம் காரகில்","பாங்கு படப்பனி நீரால் குழைத்துவைத்து","ஆங்கே அணிந்துநீர் அர்ச்சியும் அன்பொடே."],"p":"","r":"Mix sandal with fragrant kunkumam, camphor and dark agil, blended properly with rosewater as part of the rite, and adorn and worship with love.","n":"சாங்கமதாகவே is taken as 'with its proper limbs', the rite done complete; சந்தொடு is unclear. The verse closes on அன்பொடே: the materials are listed, but love is the manner required.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:82"},"t:1005":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1005,"t":["அன்புடனே நின்று அமுதமும் ஏற்றியே","பொன்செய் விளக்கும் புகைதீபம் திசைதொறும்","துன்பம் அகற்றித் தொழுவோர் நினையுங்கால்","இன்புட னேவந்து எய்திடும் முத்தியே."],"p":"","r":"Standing in love, offering the food offering and setting golden lamps and incense and lights in every direction, when worshippers who have put away sorrow think on him, release comes to them with joy.","n":"துன்பம் அகற்றி can be what the worshippers do (putting sorrow aside) or what the worship does (removing their sorrow). அமுது here is the food offered (naivedya).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","anbu","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:82"},"t:1006":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1006,"t":["எய்தி வழிப்படில் எய்தா தனஇல்லை","எய்தி வழிப்படில் இந்திரன் செல்வமுன்","எய்தி வழிப்படில் எண்சித்தி உண்டாகும்","எய்தி வழிப்படில் எய்திடும் முத்தியே."],"p":"","r":"If you come and worship there is nothing you cannot reach: first Indra's wealth, then the eight siddhis, and at last release.","n":"Each line opens with எய்தி வழிப்படில், and the fruits climb from worldly wealth to powers to release. எய்தி may mean coming near the Lord or having attained the means.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","siththi","veedu","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:82"},"t:1007":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1007,"t":["நண்ணும் பிறதார நீத்தார் அவித்தார்","மண்ணிய நைவேத் தியம்அனு சந்தான","நண்ணிய பஞ்சாங்கம் நண்ணும் செபமென்னும்","மன்னும் மனம்பவ னத்தோடு வைகுமே."],"p":"","r":"Those who have left desire for others' wives and quenched the senses, with pure food offerings, attentive contemplation, the five-limbed prostration and repetition of the mantra, have minds that settle with the breath.","n":"அவித்தார் has no stated object; the senses are the usual reading. பஞ்சாங்கம் may be the five-limbed prostration or a five-part rite, and அனுசந்தானம் steady inner attention. மனம் பவனத்தோடு வைகும் is a yogic note: the mind abiding with the breath.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","piranil","manam","praanan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:82"},"t:1008":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1008,"t":["வேண்டார்கள் கன்மம் விமலனுக்கு ஆட்பட்டோ ர்","வேண்டார்கள் கன்மம் அதில்இச்சை அற்றபேர்","வேண்டார்கள் கன்மம் மிகுசிவ யோகிகள்","வேண்டார்கள் கன்மம் மிகுதியோர் ஆய்ந்தன்பே."],"p":"","r":"Those who have become servants of the Stainless One, those who have no desire for it, the great Siva-yogis and the eminent who have searched it out do not want ritual works; they want love.","n":"கன்மம் here is most naturally the ritual work the chapter has been prescribing, though it can also mean deeds and their fruit. The last line is compressed: ஆய்ந்து அன்பே, 'having examined, love', taken as love being what they keep.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","anbu","yogam","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:82"},"t:1009":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1009,"t":["அறிவரு ஞானத்து எவரும் அறியார்","பொறிவழி தேடிப் புலம்புகின்றார்கள்","நெறிமனை யுள்ளே நிலைபெற நோக்கில்","எறிமணி யுள்ளே இருக்கலும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"No one knows him through the knowledge that is hard to reach; people seek through the senses and lament, but if they look steadily within the house of the way, he can be found inside the struck bell.","n":"பொறிவழி (the way of the senses) is set against looking within. நெறிமனை, the house of the path, is taken as the body; எறிமணி, the struck bell, is traditionally the inner sound heard in meditation, though it could also be the bell of temple worship.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","meyyunarvu","naadham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:82"},"t:1010":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1010,"t":["இருளும் வெளியும்போல் இரண்டாம் இதயம்","மருளறி யாமையும் மன்னும் அறிவு","மருளிவை விட்டெறி யாமை மயங்கும்","மருளும் சிதைத்தோர் அவர்களாம் அன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The heart is twofold, like darkness and light: delusion and ignorance, and abiding knowledge; ignorance that does not cast off these delusions stays bewildered, and only those who have broken delusion are the knowers.","n":"The pairing of இருள் and வெளி (darkness and open light) for ignorance and knowledge in the one heart is the image. The last line is elliptical: அவர்களாம் supplies 'they are the ones' without saying what.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","meyyunarvu","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:82"},"t:1011":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1011,"t":["தான்அவ னாக அவனேதான் ஆயிட","ஆன இரண்டில் அறிவன் சிவமாகப்","போனவன் அன்பிது நாலாம் மரபுறத்","தான்அவன் ஆகும்ஓ ராசித்த தேவரே."],"p":"","r":"When the self becomes He and He becomes the self, the knower of the two becomes Siva; this is the love of one who has reached the fourth way, and he becomes Him.","n":"நாலாம் மரபு is traditionally the fourth of the four paths (charya, kriya, yoga, jnana). The verse calls union a form of love (அன்பு). ஓராசித்த தேவரே is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","gnaanam","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:82"},"t:1012":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1012,"t":["ஓங்காரம் உந்திக்கீழ் உற்றிடும் எந்நாளும்","நீங்கா வகாரமும் நீள்கண்டத்து ஆயிடும்","பாங்கார் நகாரம் பயில்நெற்றி உற்றிடும்","வீங்காகும் விந்துவும் நாதமே லாகுமே."],"p":"","r":"Om always rests below the navel, the unfailing syllable va is in the long throat, na abides in the forehead, and swelling bindu and nada are above.","n":"This is a placing of mantra syllables in the body (nyasa). Only Om, va and na are named, and why they sit where they do is not explained; the usual placings of the five syllables differ.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","piranavam","vindhu","naadham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:82"},"t:1013":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1013,"t":["நமவது ஆசனம் ஆன பசுவே","சிவமது சித்திச் சிவமாம் பதியே","நமவற ஆதி நாடுவது அன்றாம்","சிவமாகும் மாமோனம் சேர்தல்மெய் வீடே."],"p":"","r":"Nama, the seat, is the soul; Sivam, the attainment, is Siva the Lord; when nama falls away there is no more seeking the First; to join the great silence that is Siva is true release.","n":"The verse splits நமசிவாய: நம is the பசு, the bound soul, as the seat (ஆசனம்), and சிவ the பதி. Line 3 (நமவற ஆதி நாடுவது அன்றாம்) is obscure; it may mean that once the soul's part ceases, the search ends. மாமோனம் is the great silence.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pasu","pathi","mandhiram","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:82"},"t:1014":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1014,"t":["தெளிவரு நாளில் சிவஅமுது ஊறும்","ஒளிவரு நாளில் ஓர்ஏட்டில் உகளும்","ஒளிவரும் அப்பதத்து ஓர் இரண்டு ஆகில்","வெளிதரு நாதன் வெளியாய் இருந்தே."],"p":"","r":"On the day clarity comes, Siva's nectar wells up; on the day light comes, it leaps in a single petal; if in that state the two become one, the Lord who gives the open space remains as that space.","n":"ஓர்ஏட்டில் (in one petal or leaf) may refer to a lotus centre in yogic anatomy. ஓர் இரண்டு ஆகில் can be 'if the two become one' or 'if one becomes two'. வெளி, open space, is the Lord's own nature here.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","aanandham","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:82"},"t:1015":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1015,"t":["நவகுண்டம் ஆனவை நான்உரை செய்யின்","நவகுண்டத்து உள்ளெழும் நற்றீபம் தானும்","நவகுண்டத்து உள்ளெழும் நன்மைகள் எல்லாம்","நவகுண்டம் ஆனவை நான்உரைப் பேனே."],"p":"","r":"I will tell what the nine kundas are, the good flame that rises in them and all the good that rises in them.","n":"குண்டம் is the pit for the sacred fire; nine kundas of different shapes belong to the Agamic fire rite. The chapter treats them also as places within, so a plain and an inner reading stand throughout.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aaguthi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:83"},"t:1016":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1016,"t":["உரைத்திடும் குண்டத்தின் உள்ளே முக்காலும்","நகைத்தெழு நாற்கோணம் நன்மை கள்ஐந்தும்","பகைத்திடு முப்புரம் பாரங்கி யோடே","மிகைத்தெழு கண்டங்கள் மேலறி யோமே."],"p":"","r":"Within the kunda spoken of are three parts, the four-cornered pit, five good things and the hostile triple city with fire; the parts that rise beyond these we do not know.","n":"முக்கால் (three legs, three times or three parts), நாற்கோணம் (square) and பாரங்கி (perhaps earth and fire) are unclear. முப்புரம், the three cities Siva burned, are traditionally read as the three malas. The last line may be corrupt.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aaguthi","malam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:83"},"t:1017":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1017,"t":["மேலெறிந்து உள்ளே வெளிசெய்த அப்பொருள்","கால்அறிந்து உள்ளே கருத்துற்ற செஞ்சுடர்","பார்அறிந்து அண்டம் சிறகற நின்றது","நான்அறிந்து உள்ளே நாடிக்கொண் டேனே."],"p":"","r":"That which, thrown upward, made space within, the red flame that knew the breath and entered thought within, which knew the earth and stood with the cosmos unbounded: I knew it and sought it within.","n":"கால் is taken as breath, as elsewhere in Tirumular, and the red flame as the inner fire. சிறகற (without wings or edges) is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["praanan","meyyunarvu","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:83"},"t:1018":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1018,"t":["கொண்டஇக் குண்டத்தின் உள்ளெழு சோதியாய்","அண்டங்கள் ஈரேழும் ஆக்கி அழிக்கலாம்","பண்டையுள் வேதம் பரந்த பரப்பெலாம்","இன்றுசொல் நூலாய் எடுத்துரைத் தேனே."],"p":"","r":"As the light rising within this kunda one can create and destroy the fourteen worlds; all the spread of the ancient Veda I have set out today as a spoken text.","n":"The subject of ஆக்கி அழிக்கலாம் is open: the light itself, the Lord, creates and destroys, or the one who knows it gains that power. Line 4 claims this teaching restates the Veda.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aaguthi","vedham","siththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:83"},"t:1019":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1019,"t":["எடுத்தஅக் குண்டத் திடம்பதி னாறில்","பதித்த கலைகளும் பாலித்து நிற்கும்","கதித்தனல் உள்ளெழக் கண்டுகொள் வார்க்கே","கொதித்தெழும் வல்லினை கூடகி லாவே."],"p":"","r":"In the sixteen places of that kunda the kalas set there stand guarding; for those who see the fire rising within, strong karma, however it boils up, cannot join them.","n":"பதினாறு கலை are traditionally the sixteen digits, as of the moon. உள்ளெழ (rising within) is what turns the rite inward.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalai","vinai","aaguthi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:83"},"t:1020":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1020,"t":["கூடமுக் கூடத்தின் உள்ளெழு குண்டத்துள்","ஆடிய ஐந்தும் அகம்புறம் பாய்நிற்கும்","பாடிய பன்னீர் இராசியும் அங்குஎழ","நாடிக்கொள் வார்கட்கு நற்சுடர் தானே."],"p":"","r":"In the kunda rising within the triple peak, the five that dance stand within and without, and the twelve signs of the zodiac rise there; for those who seek it, it is the good flame.","n":"கூடம் முக்கூடம் (peak, three peaks) and ஆடிய ஐந்து (the five that dance: elements, senses or the five acts) are not fixed by the verse. The twelve signs may be the twelve-part breath or a diagram.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aaguthi","yogam","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:83"},"t:1021":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1021,"t":["நற்சுட ராகும் சிரமுக வட்டமாம்","கைச்சுட ராகும் கருத்துற்ற கைகளிற்","பைச்சுடர் மேனி பதைப்பற்று இலிங்கமும்","நற்சுட ராய்எழு நல்லதென் றாளே."],"p":"","r":"The good flame forms the circle of head and face, flames are the hands held in thought, the body is flame and the linga stands unshaken; she rises as the good flame and is the Good.","n":"This begins the visualization of a deity's form in the fire. நல்லதென்றாள் is read as 'she who is called the Good', the goddess, which 1022 takes up.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","sivam","aaguthi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:83"},"t:1022":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1022,"t":["நல்லதென் றாளே நமக்குற்ற நாயகம்","சொல்லதென் றாளே சுடர்முடி பாதமோ","மெல்லநின் றாளை வினவகில் லாதவர்","கல்லதென் றாளையும் கற்றும் வின் வாளே."],"p":"","r":"She who is called the Good is the sovereign who has come to us; she who is called the Word, is she the flame-crowned head or the feet? Those who do not ask her who stands gently think her stone, even though they have learned.","n":"The last line (கல்லதென் றாளையும் கற்றும் வின் வாளே) is unclear and may be corrupt; read here as the learned taking the goddess for stone, the image only.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:83"},"t:1023":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1023,"t":["வின்னா விளம்பிறை மேவிய குண்டத்துச்","சொன்னால் இரண்டும் சுடர்நாகம் திக்கென்று","பன்னாலு நாகம் பரந்த பரஞ்சுடர்","என்ஆகத் துள்ளே இடங்கொண்ட வாறே."],"p":"","r":"In the kunda shaped like the young crescent of the sky, two flaming serpents point to the directions, many serpents spread, and the supreme flame took its place within my body.","n":"விளம்பிறை names the crescent-shaped kunda, one of the nine shapes. The serpents (நாகம்) may be ornaments of the form or the coiled power; பன்னாலு (fourteen, or many fours) is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","yaakkai","aaguthi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:83"},"t:1024":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1024,"t":["இடங்கொண்ட பாதம் எழிற்சுடர் ஏக","நடங்கொண்ட பாதங்கள் நண்ணீர் அதற்குச்","சுகங்கொண்ட கையிரண்டு ஆறும் தழைப்ப","முகங்கொண்ட செஞ்சுடர் முக்கண னார்க்கே."],"p":"","r":"The feet that took their place are a single beautiful flame, the feet are those that took up the dance, two hands and six flourish in ease, and the red flame of the face belongs to the three-eyed Lord.","n":"A form of Siva in the fire, given by parts. நண்ணீர் அதற்கு (approach it) and கையிரண்டு ஆறும் (two hands and six, or two and the six) are unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","aaguthi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:83"},"t:1025":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1025,"t":["முக்கணன் தானே முழுச்சுடர் ஆயவன்","அக்கணன் தானே அகிலமும் உண்டவன்","திக்கணன் ஆகித் திகைஎட்டும் கண்டவன்","எக்கணன் தானுக்கும் எந்தை பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The three-eyed one is himself the whole flame; that one swallowed all the world; with eyes in every direction he saw the eight quarters; to every god of whatever eyes he is our father and Lord.","n":"The verse plays on கணன் (the eyed one): முக்கணன், அக்கணன், திக்கணன், எக்கணன். 'Swallowing the world' is usually said of Vishnu; here it is Siva's, and எக்கணன் may glance at the many-eyed gods, Brahma and Indra.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:83"},"t:1026":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1026,"t":["எந்தை பிரானுக்கு இருமூன்று வட்டமாய்த்","தந்தைதன் முன்னே சண்முகம் தோன்றலால்","கந்தன் சுவாமி கலந்தங்கு இருந்தலான்","மைந்தன் இவனென்று மாட்டிக்கொள் ளீரே."],"p":"","r":"For our father the Lord there are six circles, and because the six-faced one appears before his father and Lord Skanda stays joined there, hold him fast as the son.","n":"The six-sided kunda is matched with six-faced Murugan. மாட்டிக்கொள்ளீரே (fix, hold fast) is read as a command to the meditator.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","aaguthi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:83"},"t:1027":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1027,"t":["மாட்டிய குண்டத்தின் உள்ளெழு வேதத்துள்","ஆட்டிய காலொன்றும் இரண்டும் அலர்ந்திடும்","வாட்டிய கையிரண்டு ஒன்று பதைத்தெழு","நாட்டும் சுரரிவர் நல்லொளி தானே."],"p":"","r":"In the Veda rising within the kunda, one foot moving and two unfolding, two bent hands and one rising trembling: these gods set there are good light.","n":"The verse lists limbs of forms seen in the fire, but whose forms and how they are counted is not clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aaguthi","vedham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:83"},"t:1028":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1028,"t":["நல்லொளி யாக நடந்துல கெங்கும்","கல்லொளி யாகக் கலந்துள் இருந்திடும்","சொல்லொளி யாகத் தொடர்ந்த உயிர்க்கெலாம்","கல்லொளி கண்ணுள மாகிநின் றாளே."],"p":"","r":"As good light she moves through all the world, as the light of a gem she stays mingled within, as the light of the word she follows all lives, and as the gem's light she stands in the eye.","n":"கல்லொளி (light of stone, a gem's light) is repeated; it may be the inner light seen in meditation. The feminine நின்றாளே makes the subject Shakti.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:83"},"t:1029":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1029,"t":["நின்றஇக் குண்டம் நிலையாது கோணமாய்ப்","பண்டையில் வட்டம் பதைத்தெழு மாறாறும்","கொண்டஇத் தத்துவம் உள்ளே கலந்தெழ","விண்ணுளம் என்ன எடுக்கலு மாமே."],"p":"","r":"This kunda does not stay fixed but becomes angled; in the ancient circle the six sixes rise, and as these tattvas mingle within it can be raised, like a heart of space.","n":"மாறாறும் is read as ஆறாறு, the thirty-six tattvas. விண்ணுளம் (sky-heart, or 'as if the heart of the sky') and the last line are obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","aaguthi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:83"},"t:1030":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1030,"t":["எடுக்கின்ற பாதங்கள் மூன்றது எழுத்தைக்","கடுத்த முகம்இரண்டு ஆறுகண் ஆகப்","படித்துஎண்ணும் நாவெழு கொம்பொரு நாலும்","அடுத்தெழு கண்ணான தந்தமி லாற்கே."],"p":"","r":"Three lifting feet, the letter, two fierce faces with six eyes, a tongue that reads and counts, four horns, and eyes rising close by: these belong to the one without end.","n":"Another numbered form. கொம்பு and தந்தம் can mean horn or tusk, which might suggest an elephant form, but தந்தமிலாற்கே is read with 1031 as அந்தம் இலாற்கு, the endless one.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","aaguthi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:83"},"t:1031":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1031,"t":["அந்தமில் லானுக்கு அகலிடம் தானில்லை","அந்தமில் லானை அளப்பவர் தாமில்லை","அந்தமில் லானுக்கு அடுத்தசொல் தானில்லை","அந்தமில் லானை அறிந்துகொள் பத்தே."],"p":"","r":"The endless one has no place wide enough, no one to measure him and no word to reach him; know the endless one through devotion.","n":"பத்தே may be devotion (பத்தி) or the number ten, which 1032 opens with. Lines 1 to 3 are the chapter's clearest statement that no form contains the Lord the other verses describe.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","iraivan","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:83"},"t:1032":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1032,"t":["பத்திட்டுஅங்கு எட்டிட்டு ஆறிட்டு நாலிட்டு","மட்டிட்ட குண்டம் மலர்ந்தெழு தாமரை","கட்டிட்டு நின்று கலந்தமெய் யாகவும்","பட்டிட்டு நின்றது பார்ப்பதி பாலே."],"p":"","r":"In the kunda measured out with ten, eight, six and four, the lotus blooms; bound there it stands as the mingled truth, and settled on Parvati's side.","n":"The petal counts may be those of lotus centres in the body or of the kunda's drawing. மெய் may be truth or body.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aaguthi","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:83"},"t:1033":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1033,"t":["பார்ப்பதி பாகன் பரந்தகை நால்ஐஞ்சு","காற்பதி பத்து முகம்பத்துக் கண்களும்","பூப்பதி பாதம் இரண்டு சுடர்முடி","நாற்பது சோத்திரம் நல்லிரு பத்தஞ்சே."],"p":"","r":"Parvati's partner has twenty hands spread, ten feet, ten faces with their eyes, two flower feet, a flaming crown, forty ears and a good twenty-five.","n":"The counts do not cohere as given: ten feet (காற்பதி பத்து) then two feet. சோத்திரம் may be ears (srotra) or hymns (stotra); இருபத்தஞ்சு may be the twenty-five tattvas.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:83"},"t:1034":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1034,"t":["அஞ்சிட்ட கோலம் அளப்பன ஐஐந்தம்","மஞ்சிட்ட குண்டம் மலர்ந்தங்கு இருத்தலால்","பஞ்சிட்ட சோதி பரந்த பரஞ்சுடர்","கொஞ்சிட்ட வன்னியைக் கூடுதல் முத்தியே."],"p":"","r":"The form of five measures five fives; because the kunda blooms there, the supreme flame spreads as light, and to join the cooing fire is release.","n":"ஐஐந்து (twenty-five) may be the tattvas. மஞ்சிட்ட (cloud-laid) and பஞ்சிட்ட (cotton-laid, or five-laid) are obscure. வன்னி, fire, is named as what one joins for release.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aaguthi","veedu","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:83"},"t:1035":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1035,"t":["முத்திநற் சோதி முழுச்சுடர் ஆயவன்","கற்றற்று நின்றார் கருத்துள் இருந்திடும்","பற்றற நாடிப் பரந்தொளி யூடு போய்ச்","செற்றற்து இருந்தவர் சேர்ந்திருந் தாரே."],"p":"","r":"He who is the whole flame, the good light of release, abides in the thought of those who stand beyond learning; those who seek without attachment, pass through the spreading light and are free of hostility have joined him.","n":"கற்றற்று is read as 'with learning ended' but may hide another word; செற்றற்து in line 4 is taken as செற்றற்று, 'without enmity'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["veedu","pattru","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:83"},"t:1036":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1036,"t":["சேர்ந்த கலையஞ்சும் சேரும்இக் குண்டமும்","ஆர்ந்த திசைகளும் அங்கே அமர்ந்திடும்","பாய்ந்தஐம் பூதமும் பார்க்கின்ற வன்னியைக்","காய்ந்தவர் என்றும் கலந்தவர் தாமே."],"p":"","r":"The five kalas joined, this kunda and all the directions settle there; those who have kindled the fire that the five elements watch are ever joined with it.","n":"The five kalas are traditionally nivritti, pratishtha, vidya, shanti and shantyatita. காய்ந்தவர் may be those who kindled or heated the fire, or those who overcame it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalai","aaguthi","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:83"},"t:1037":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1037,"t":["மெய்கண்ட மாம்விரி நீருல கேழையும்","உய்கண்டம் செய்த ஒருவனைச் சேருமின்","செய்கண்ட ஞானம் திருந்திய தேவர்கள்","பொய்கண்டம் இல்லாப் பொருள்கலந் தாரே."],"p":"","r":"Join the One who made the seven worlds of the wide waters a region of truth and of rescue; the gods made perfect by knowledge seen in practice have mingled with the reality that has no false part.","n":"The verse plays on கண்டம் (region, part): மெய்கண்டம், உய்கண்டம், செய்கண்ட, பொய்கண்டம்.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","gnaanam","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:83"},"t:1038":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1038,"t":["கலந்திரு பாதம் இருகர மாகும்","மலர்ந்திரு குண்ட மகாரத்தார் Yமூக்கு","மலர்ந்தெழு செம்முகம் மற்றைக்கண் நெற்றி","உணர்ந்திரு குஞ்சி அங்கு உத்தம னார்க்கே."],"p":"","r":"The two feet joined become two hands, the kunda opens at the syllable ma as the nose, the red face blooms with the other eye and forehead, and the tuft of hair is there for the Supreme.","n":"Line 2 is corrupt (a stray Latin Y). The verse seems to map a form, and perhaps mantra syllables, onto the kunda, but the mapping cannot be fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["sivam","mandhiram","aaguthi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:83"},"t:1039":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1039,"t":["உத்தமன் சோதி உளனொரு பாலனாய்","மத்திம னாகி மலர்ந்தங்கு இருந்திடும்","பச்சிம திக்கும் பரந்து குழிந்தன","சத்திமா னாகத் தழைத்த கொடியே."],"p":"","r":"The Supreme's light is there as a child on one side, blooms as one in the middle, spreads to the west and hollows, and flourishes as the creeper, Shakti.","n":"பாலன், மத்திமன் (child, middle one) may be stages of the Lord's form or positions. பச்சிம திக்கு, the west, in yogic usage can mean the back channel. கொடி (creeper) is a common image for Shakti.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:83"},"t:1040":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1040,"t":["கொடியாறு சென்று குலாவிய குண்டம்","அடியிரு கோணமாய் அந்தமும் ஒக்கும்","படிஏழ் உலகும் பரந்த சுடரை","மடியாது கண்டவர் மாதன மாமே."],"p":"","r":"The kunda where the creeper ran its six courses and played is two-angled at the base and alike at the end; those who see unfailingly the flame spread through the seven worlds become a great treasure.","n":"கொடியாறு (six courses of the creeper, or the creeper's river) may be the six centres of the body. மாதனம் (great wealth) is taken up in 1041.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aaguthi","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:83"},"t:1041":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1041,"t":["மாதன மாக வளர்கின்ற வன்னியைச்","சாதன மாகச் சமைந்த குருவென்று","போதன மாகப் பொருந்த உலகாளும்","பாதன மாகப் பரிந்தது பார்த்தே."],"p":"","r":"Taking as his means the fire that grows as great wealth, the guru stands perfected and teaches it fittingly, and the feet that rule the world, seeing it, are moved with love.","n":"The verse rhymes on -தனம்: மாதனம், சாதனம், போதனம், பாதனம். Who sees and is moved in the last line, the Lord or the guru, is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","aaguthi","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:83"},"t:1042":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1042,"t":["பார்த்திடம் எங்கும் பரந்தெழு சோதியை","ஆத்தம தாகவே ஆய்ந்தறி வார் இல்லை","காத்துடல் உள்ளே கருதி இருந்தவர்","மூத்துடல் கோடி யுகங்கண்ட வாறே."],"p":"","r":"No one examines and knows as their own the light that spreads wherever one looks; those who guarded the body and dwelt in thought within it have grown old in that body and seen a crore of ages.","n":"ஆத்தம் (one's own, or trustworthy) is unclear. The claim of long life in the guarded body is plain and fits Tirumular's teaching of nurturing the body.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","yogam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:83"},"t:1043":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1043,"t":["உகங்கண்ட ஒன்பது குண்டமும் ஒக்க","அகங்கண்ட யோகியுள் நாடி எழுப்பும்","பகங்கண்டு கொண்டஇப் பாய்கரு வொப்பச்","சகங்கண்டு கொண்டது சாதன மாமே."],"p":"","r":"The nine kundas known over the ages are alike; the yogi who has seen within raises them in the nadi, and as the womb conceives on union, seeing the world through this is the practice.","n":"The verse makes the nine kundas inner and raised through the channels. The third line (பகங்கண்டு கொண்டஇப் பாய்கரு) is read as an image of conception; சகம் கண்டு may be seeing or creating the world.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","naadi","karu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:83"},"t:1044":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1044,"t":["சாதனை நாலு தழல்மூன்று வில்வயம்","வேதனை வட்டம் விளையாறு பூநிலை","போதனை போதுஐஞ்சு பொய்கய வாரணம்","நாதனை நாடு நவகோடி தானே."],"p":"","r":"Four practices, three fires, the bilva, the circle of pain, six that grow, the flower-stage, teaching, five flowers, the false elephant: seeking the Lord in these is nine crore.","n":"The verse is a compressed list whose items cannot be fixed; தழல் மூன்று are traditionally the three sacred fires. நவகோடி may be nine crore or nine kinds. Some words may be corrupt.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aaguthi","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:83"},"t:1045":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1045,"t":["மாமாயை மாயை வயிந்தவம் வைகரி","ஓமாயை உள்ளொளி ஓராறு கோடியில்","தாமான மந்திரம் சத்திதன் மூர்த்திகள்","ஆமாய் அலவாந் திரிபுரை யாங்கே."],"p":"","r":"Mahamaya, maya, the bindu-born, the spoken word, the Om-maya and the inner light, and the mantras that are herself among the six crores, are the forms of Shakti; being and not being, there is Tripura.","n":"வயிந்தவம் (baindava, born of bindu) and வைகரி (vaikhari, speech at its spoken level) are terms of Saiva cosmology and mantra theory; ஓமாயை and ஓராறு கோடி (six crores, taken of the mantras) are obscure. ஆமாய் அலவாம் can be read 'she is and is not' or 'she is all and none of these'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","maayai","vindhu","mandhiram"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:84"},"t:1046":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1046,"t":["திரிபுரை சுந்தரி அந்தரி சிந்துரப்","பரிபுரை நாரணி யாம்பல வன்னத்தி","இருள்புரை ஈசி மனோன்மனி என்ன","வருபல வாய்நிற்கும் மாமது தானே."],"p":"","r":"Tripura, Sundari, Antari, the vermilion one, Paripurai, Narani, the one of many colours, Isi who removes darkness, Manonmani: by these names the great one stands as many.","n":"A string of the Goddess's names; several (அந்தரி, of the sky; நாரணி, the female Narayana; மனோன்மனி, she who lifts the mind beyond mind) carry traditional meanings, others (பரிபுரை) are unclear. மாமது in the last line is obscure: 'the great one' or 'great honey'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:84"},"t:1047":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1047,"t":["தானா அமைந்தஅம் முப்புரம் தன்னிடைத்","தானான மூவுரு ஓருருத் தன்மையுள்","தானான பொன்செம்மை வெண்ணிறத் தாள்கல்வி","தானான போகமும் முத்தியும் நல்குமே."],"p":"","r":"In the three cities she formed herself, three forms of one nature, she who is gold, red and white is learning, and she herself grants enjoyment and release.","n":"The three colours are traditionally matched with three goddesses (gold with Lakshmi, red with Durga or Shakti, white with Sarasvati) who are one Tripura; the verse only says three forms are one. Granting both போகம் and முத்தி, worldly enjoyment and release, is the claim Srividya makes for this goddess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","kalvi","inbam","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:84"},"t:1048":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1048,"t":["நல்குந் திரிபுரை நாதநா தாந்தங்கள்","பல்கும் பரவிந்து பாரண்ட மானவை","நல்கும் பரைஅபி ராமி அகோசரி","புல்கும் அருளும்அப் போதந்தந் தாளுமே."],"p":"","r":"Tripura grants nada and nada's end; the supreme bindu, which multiplies, becomes the earth and the worlds; Parai, Abhirami, she who is beyond the senses, embraces, gives grace, gives that wisdom and rules.","n":"நாதாந்தம் (the end of nada) and பரவிந்து (supreme bindu) are stages of manifestation in Saiva cosmology. அகோசரி is 'she who is not an object of the senses'. போதம் is taken as wisdom; the last line is compressed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","naadham","vindhu","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:84"},"t:1049":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1049,"t":["தாளணி நூபுரம் செம்பட்டுத் தானுடை","வாரணி கொங்கை மலர்க்கன்னல் வாளிவில்","ஏரணி அங்குச பாசம் எழில்முடி","காரணி மாமணிக் குண்டலக் காதிக்கே."],"p":"","r":"Anklets on her feet, red silk her garment, breasts bound with a band, the sugarcane bow and flower arrows, the goad and the noose, a fine crown, and earrings of dark great gems: such is she.","n":"A portrait of Tripurasundari with her standard emblems. Tradition reads the noose as desire or attachment, the goad as restraint, the sugarcane bow as the mind and the five flower arrows as the senses; the verse gives the emblems without their meanings.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:84"},"t:1050":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1050,"t":["குண்டலக் காதி கொலைவிற் புருவத்தள்","கொண்ட அரத்த நிறமன்னு கோலத்தன்","கண்டிகை ஆரம் கதிர் முடி மாமதிச்","சண்டிகை நாற்றிசை தாங்கிநின் றாளே."],"p":"","r":"She with earrings, with brows like a killing bow, with a form of lasting blood-red colour, with beads and necklace, rayed crown and the great moon, Chandika stands holding up the four directions.","n":"The gentle portrait of 1049 turns fierce here: கொலைவில் (killing bow) and சண்டிகை (Chandika, the fierce goddess) show the same goddess in her terrible aspect. கண்டிகை is usually a bead string, often rudraksha.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:84"},"t:1051":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1051,"t":["நின்ற திரிபுரை நீளும் புராதனி","குன்றலில் மோகினி மாதிருக் கும்சிகை","நன்றறி கண்டிகை நாற்கால் கரீடணி","துன்றிய நற்சுத்த தாமரைச் சுத்தையே."],"p":"","r":"Tripura who stands, the ancient one who extends, the undiminishing Mohini, the one of the crest, who knows the good, who wears beads, Karidani of four limbs, the pure one of the pure lotus.","n":"Another list of names. மாதிருக்கும் சிகை and நாற்கால் கரீடணி are obscure; the second may be a corrupt form of a Sanskrit name. Only புராதனி (the ancient), மோகினி (the enchantress) and சுத்தை (the pure) are clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:84"},"t:1052":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1052,"t":["தத்துவம் பாரத தனத்தி சுகோதயள்","வத்துவம் ஆய்ஆ ளும்மாசத்தி மாபரை","அத்தகை யாவும் அணோரணி தானுமாய்","வைத்தஅக் கோல மதியவள் ஆகுமே."],"p":"","r":"She is the tattvas, the heavy-breasted, the rising of bliss; as the substance she rules, the great Shakti, the great Parai; all those things, and the smaller than the atom, she is, and she is the mind of the form she set up.","n":"பாரத தனத்தி is taken as பார தனத்தி (of heavy breasts); சுகோதயள் is 'rising of bliss' and வத்துவம் is vastu, substance or reality. அணோரணி echoes the Upanishadic 'smaller than the small'. The last line (கோல மதியவள்) can also mean she is the moon of that form.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","thaththuvam","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:84"},"t:1053":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1053,"t":["அவளை அறியா அமரரும் இல்லை","அவளன்றிச் செய்யும் அருந்தவம் இல்லை","அவளன்றி ஐவரால் ஆவதொன்று இல்லை","அவளன்றி ஊர்புகும் ஆறறி யேனே."],"p":"","r":"There are no gods who do not know her, no rare austerity done without her, nothing the five can achieve without her, and without her I know no way of entering the city.","n":"The plainest verse of the chapter, built on the repeated அவளன்றி (without her). ஐவர் (the five) are traditionally the five gods who perform the five acts, but may be the five senses; ஊர் (the town) is traditionally the city of release or Siva's realm.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","thavam","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:84"},"t:1054":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1054,"t":["அறிவார் பராசத்தி ஆனந்தம் என்பர்","அறிவா ரருவுரு வாம்அவள் என்பர்","அறிவார் கருமம் அவள்இச்சை என்பர்","அறிவார் பரனும் அவளிடத் தானே."],"p":"","r":"The knowers say Parashakti is bliss; they say she is both formless and with form; they say action is her will; and the knowers say the Supreme too is in her.","n":"The verse reports what knowers (அறிவார்) say rather than asserting it in the poet's voice. பரனும் அவளிடத்தான் can mean the Supreme is within her or is on her side, as in the half-woman form; either way Siva and Shakti are not apart.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saththi","aanandham","pathi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:84"},"t:1055":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1055,"t":["தான்எங்கு உளன்அங்கு உளதுதையல் மாதேவி","ஊன்எங் குள அங்கு உள்ளுயிர்க் காலவன்","வான் எங் குளஅங் குளேவந்தும் அப்பாலாம்","கோன் எங்கும் நின்ற குறிபல பாரே."],"p":"","r":"Wherever he is, there is the lady Mahadevi; wherever there is flesh, he is there as the breath of the life within; wherever the sky is, he is there and beyond it: see the many signs of the king who stands everywhere.","n":"உள்ளுயிர்க் காலவன் is taken as கால் (wind, breath) of the inner life; it could also be read with காலம் (time). The verse moves from his inseparability from the Goddess to his presence in bodies and beyond the sky.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","saththi","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:84"},"t:1056":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1056,"t":["பராசத்தி மாசத்தி பல்வகை யாலும்","தராசத்தி யாய்நின்ற தன்மை யுணராய்","உராசத்தி ஊழிகள் தோறும் உடனே","பராசத்தி புண்ணிய மாகிய போகமே."],"p":"","r":"You do not grasp how Parashakti, Mahashakti, stands in many ways as the power of the earth, as the strong power through every age; Parashakti is the enjoyment that is merit.","n":"தராசத்தி (the earth's power) is fairly clear; உராசத்தி is obscure, taken as 'strong power' or 'the power in the breast'. The last line makes the Goddess herself the fruit of merit (புண்ணியம்) enjoyed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:84"},"t:1057":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1057,"t":["போகஞ்செய் சத்தி புரிகுழ லாளொடும்","பாகஞ்செய்து ஆங்கே பராசத்தி யாய்நிற்கும்","ஆகஞ்செய்து ஆங்கே அடியவர் நாள்தொறும்","பாகஞ்செய் ஞானம் படர்கின்ற கொம்பே."],"p":"","r":"Sharing half his body with the curl-haired Shakti who brings enjoyment, he stands there as Parashakti; taking a body there, she is the branch along which the knowledge that ripens devotees spreads each day.","n":"பாகஞ்செய்து is read first as sharing a half (the half-woman form) and then, in பாகஞ்செய் ஞானம், as ripening; the pun is the verse's hinge. கொம்பு (branch, creeper's support) prepares the next verse's கொம்பனையாள. Who is subject of the first two lines, Siva or Shakti, is not fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","gnaanam","adiyaar"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:84"},"t:1058":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1058,"t":["கொம்புஅனை யாளைக் குவிமுலை மங்கையை","வம்பவிழ் கோதையை வானவிர் நாடியைச்","செம்பவ ளத்திரு மேனிச் சிறுமியை","நம்பி என் உள்ளே நயந்துவைத் தேனே."],"p":"","r":"Trusting, I desired and placed within me the one like a creeper, the maiden of full breasts with a fragrant opening garland, the one of the channel shining to the sky, the little girl whose holy body is red coral.","n":"The love lyric's vocabulary turned on the Goddess. வானவிர் நாடி is taken as the nadi that shines up to the sky, traditionally the central channel; a plain reading is less clear. நம்பி may be 'trusting' or an address to a man ('O Nambi').","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","anbu","naadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:84"},"t:1059":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1059,"t":["வைத்த பொருளும் மருவுயிர்ப் பன்மையும்","பத்து முகமும் பரையும் பராபரைச்","சித்தக் கரணச் செயல்களும் செய்திடும்","சத்தியும் வித்தைத் தலையவ ளாமே."],"p":"","r":"The things set in place, the many lives that join bodies, the ten faces, Parai and Paraparai, the workings of the mind and its instruments, and the Shakti who makes them act: of all these she is the head of knowledge.","n":"பத்து முகம் (ten faces) may be the ten directions or ten forms of the Goddess. வித்தைத் தலையவள் is read as the head of vidya (knowledge), and is a name in Srividya; it may also be the head of all the listed things.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","uyir","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:84"},"t:1060":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1060,"t":["தலைவி தடமுலை மேல்நின்ற தையல்","தொலைவில் தவம்செய்யும் தூய்நெறித் தோகை","கலைபல வென்றிடும் கன்னியென் உள்ளம்","நிலைபெற இங்கே நிறைந்துநின் றாளே."],"p":"","r":"The leader, the lady of broad breasts, the peacock of the pure path who does unending austerity, the maiden who conquers many arts, has filled my heart and stands here so that it is steady.","n":"தொலைவில் தவம் is read as தொலைவு இல், austerity without end; it could also be austerity done far off. The Goddess as one who herself does tapas is a well-known image (Parvati's penance for Siva). The verse's point is the last line: her filling the heart makes it stable.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saththi","thavam","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:84"},"t:1061":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1061,"t":["நின்றவள் நேரிழை நீள்கலை யோடுற","என்றன் அகம்படிந்து ஏழுல கும்தொழ","மன்றது ஒன்றி மனோன்மனி மங்கலி","ஒன்றெனொடு ஒன்றிநின்று ஒத்துஅடைத்தாளே."],"p":"","r":"She who stood, the finely jewelled one, joined with the long kala, settled into my inner self while the seven worlds worship her; at one with the hall, Manonmani the auspicious became one with me and, matching me, closed it.","n":"நீள்கலை (the long kala) is technical and unclear. மன்று (the hall) may be the hall of Siva's dance at Tillai or its inner counterpart. ஒத்து அடைத்தாள் ('matching, she closed') may mean she shut the doors of the senses or closed the way of return; the verse does not say.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","kalai","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:84"},"t:1062":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1062,"t":["ஒத்தடங் குங்கம லத்திடை ஆயிழை","அத்தகை செய்கின்ற ஆய பெரும்பதி","மத்தடை கின்ற மனோன்மனி மங்கலி","சித்தடைக் கும்வழி தேர்ந்துண ரார்களே."],"p":"","r":"People do not discern how Manonmani, the auspicious, the jewelled one in the lotus where all subsides alike, who does all this as the great Lord, closes off the mind that churns.","n":"The word order is compressed and the reading is tentative. மத்து (churning stick) is taken of the agitated mind; சித்தடைக்கும் வழி is read as the way she stops the mind (சித்தம்), in keeping with மனோன்மனி, she who lifts beyond mind.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:84"},"t:1063":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1063,"t":["உணர்ந்துட னேநிறகும் உள்ளொளி யாகி","மணங்கமழ் பூங்குழல் மங்கையும் தானும்","புணர்ந்துட னேநிற்கும் போதருங் காலைக்","கணிந்தெழு வார்க்குக் கதியளிப் பாளே."],"p":"","r":"Knowing, she stands at once as the inner light; when the lady of fragrant flower-hair and he stand joined, she grants the goal to those who rise, ripened.","n":"கணிந்தெழுவார் is taken as கனிந்து எழுவார், those who rise ripened; it could also be 'those who reckon and rise'. The union of Siva and Shakti is the condition of grace in this reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","sivam","thiruvarul","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:84"},"t:1064":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1064,"t":["அளியொத்த பெண்பிள்ளை ஆனந்த சுந்தரி","புளியுறு புன்பழம் போலுள்ள நோக்கித்","தெளியுறு வித்துச் சிவகதி காட்டி","ஒளியுற வைத்தென்னை உய்யவுண் டாளே."],"p":"","r":"The girl gentle as grace, Ananda Sundari, looked at my inner self, like a mean fruit gone sour, made it clear, showed me the way to Siva, set me in light and took me into herself so that I would be saved.","n":"அளி is grace or the bee; 'bee-like girl' is also possible. The fruit image runs to the end: the sour fruit is made clear and sweet, and உண்டாள் (she ate) completes it, the soul consumed by the Goddess as its rescue.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saththi","thiruvarul","sivam","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:84"},"t:1065":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1065,"t":["உண்டில்லை என்றது உருச்செய்து நின்றது","வண்தில்லை மன்றினுள் மன்னி நிறைந்தது","கண்டிலர் காரண காரணி தம்மொடு","மண்டல முன்றுற மன்னிநின் றாளே."],"p":"","r":"What was called 'is' and 'is not' took form and stood, settled and filled the hall of rich Tillai; they have not seen it: with the cause and she who causes, she stands settled so the three spheres are joined.","n":"தில்லை மன்று is the hall at Chidambaram where Siva dances. காரண காரணி may be 'the cause and the causing one', Siva and Shakti. மண்டலம் முன்றுற is read as மூன்று, the three spheres traditionally of sun, moon and fire; the line is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:84"},"t:1066":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1066,"t":["நின்றாள் அவன்தன் உடலும் உயிருமாய்ச்","சென்றான் சிவகதி சேரும் பராசத்தி","ஒன்றாக என்னுள் புகுந்துணர் வாகியே","நின்றான் பரஞ்சுடர் ஏடங்கை யாளே."],"p":"","r":"She stood as his body and life; Parashakti, who joins the way to Siva, went in and, entering me as one, became my awareness; the supreme light stood with her whose hand holds the leaf.","n":"சென்றான் and நின்றான் are masculine while the subject seems to be Shakti; the verse may move between the two as one. ஏடங்கையாள் is taken as 'she with the palm-leaf book in her hand', a Sarasvati attribute; ஏடு may also be a petal.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","sivam","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:84"},"t:1067":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1067,"t":["ஏடங்கை நங்கை இறைஎங்கள் முக்கண்ணி","வேடம் படிகம் விரும்பும்வெண் தாமரை","பாடும் திருமுறை பார்ப்பதி பாதங்கள்","சூடுமின் சென்னிவாய்த் தோத்திரம் சொல்லுமே."],"p":"","r":"The lady with the leaf in her hand, our sovereign, the three-eyed one, whose guise is crystal, who loves the white lotus: wear Parvati's feet, sung in the holy hymns, on your head, and let your mouth speak her praise.","n":"Crystal, the white lotus and the book are Sarasvati's marks, given here to Parvati, so the verse makes the goddesses one. திருமுறை may mean the sacred hymns generally or the Saiva canon by name.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saththi","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:84"},"t:1068":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1068,"t":["தோத்திரம் செய்து தொழுது துணையடி","வாய்த்திட ஏத்தி வழிபடு மாறிடும்","பார்த்திடும் அங்குச பாசம் பசுங்கரும்","பார்த்திடும் பூம்பிள்ளை ஆருமாம் ஆதிக்கே."],"p":"","r":"Praising and worshipping her paired feet so that they are gained, the way of worship is set; she who looks out with goad, noose and green sugarcane, the flower-child, is everything to the Primal One.","n":"Lines 3 and 4 repeat பார்த்திடும் and are obscure; பசுங்கரும்பு may be the green sugarcane bow of 1049. வழிபடு மாறிடும் may mean 'the way of worship' or 'worship changes (one)'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:84"},"t:1069":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1069,"t":["ஆதி விதமிகுத் தண்தந்த மால்தங்கை","நீதி மலரின்மேல் நேரிழை நாமத்தைப்","பாதியில் வைத்துப் பல்காற் பயில்விரல்","சோதி மிகுந்துமுக் காலமும் தோன்றுமே."],"p":"","r":"Placing in the middle the name of the jewelled one on the flower, the younger sister of Mal who in many ways made the world, and practising it many times on the fingers, light grows and all three times appear.","n":"The Goddess as Vishnu's (மால்) sister is a common Saiva identification. The practice looks like repeating her name or mantra counted on the fingers, with knowledge of past, present and future (முக்காலம்) as its fruit; பாதியில் வைத்து (placing in the half or middle) and நீதி மலர் are unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","saththi","siththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:84"},"t:1070":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1070,"t":["மேதாதி ஈரெட்டும் ஆகிய மெல்லியல்","வேதாதி நூலின் விளங்கும் பராபரை","ஆதார மாகியே ஆயந்த பரப்பினள்","நாதாதி நாதத்து நல்லரு ளாளே."],"p":"","r":"The gentle one who is the sixteen beginning with Medha, Paraparai who shines in the texts beginning with the Veda, she who is the support and spreads as the examined expanse, is the good grace of nada and what precedes nada.","n":"மேதாதி ஈரெட்டும் (sixteen beginning with Medha) is traditionally taken of sixteen kalas or powers; the list is not given. ஆதாரம் may be the bodily centres (ādhāras) as well as 'support'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","kalai","naadham","vedham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:84"},"t:1071":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1071,"t":["அருள்பெற்றவர் சொல்ல வாரீர் மனிதர்","பொருள்பெற்ற சிந்தைப் புவனா பதியார்","மருளுற்ற சிந்தையை மாற்றி யருமைப்","பொருளுற்ற சேவடி போற்றுவன் நானே."],"p":"","r":"Come, you people, and hear it from those who have received grace: I praise the red feet, which hold the rare true thing, of Bhuvanapati, who turns the deluded mind into a mind that has found the true thing.","n":"The verse turns on பொருள (the true thing, meaning) and மருள் (delusion). Read this way, பொருள்பெற்ற சிந்தை describes the mind she gives, not her own; the line also allows 'Bhuvanapati of the mind that holds the truth'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saththi","thiruvarul","manam","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:84"},"t:1072":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1072,"t":["ஆன வராக முகத்தி பதத்தினள்","ஈன வராகம் இடிக்கும் முசலத்தோடு","ஏனை உழுபடை ஏந்திய வெண்ணகை","ஊன மறஉணர்ந் தாரஉளத்து ஓங்குமே."],"p":"","r":"She of the boar's face and her station, with the pestle that pounds the base boar and holding the other weapon, the plough, the white-smiling one rises high in the hearts of those who know her without flaw.","n":"A portrait of Varahi, the boar-faced goddess, whose traditional weapons are the pestle and plough. ஈன வராகம் (the base boar) in line 2 is obscure; it may be a boar-demon or a corruption.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:84"},"t:1073":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1073,"t":["ஓங்காரி என்பாள் அவளொரு பெண்பிள்ளை","நீங்காத பச்சை நிறத்தை உடையவள்","ஆங்காரி யாகியே ஐவரைப் பெற்றிட்டு","ரீங்காரத் துள்ளே இனிதிருந் தாளே."],"p":"","r":"She is called Omkari, a girl of unfading green; becoming Ahankari she bore the five, and she sits sweetly within Hrinkara.","n":"ஓங்காரி (she who is Om), ஆங்காரி (she who is ahamkara, the sense of I) and ரீங்காரம் (the seed syllable hrim, sacred to the Goddess) play on one sound. ஐவர் may be the five gods or the five elements, which in Samkhya arise through ahamkara.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","piranavam","mandhiram"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:84"},"t:1074":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1074,"t":["தானே தலைவி எனநின்ற தற்பரை","தானே உயிர்வித்துத் தந்த பதினாலும்","வானோர் தலமும் மனமும்நற் புத்தியும்","தானே சிவகதி தன்மையும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"Tatparai, who stood as the leader herself, herself sowed lives and gave the fourteen worlds, the place of the gods, the mind and good intellect; she herself is also the nature of the way to Siva.","n":"The repeated தானே (she herself) gathers the chapter: creation, the inner faculties and the path to release are all hers. பதினாலும் is taken as the fourteen worlds, which the verse doesn't name.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saththi","uyir","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:84"},"t:1075":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1075,"t":["பன்னிரண் டாங்கலை ஆதி பயிரவி","தன்னில் ஆகாரமும் மாயையும் கற்பித்துப்","பன்னிரண்டு ஆதியோடு அந்தம் பதினாலும்","சொல்நிலை சோடம் அந்தம் என்று ஓதிடே."],"p":"","r":"Take Bhairavi as the first, with twelve kalas, conceive in her the letter A and maya, and with the twelve from the start and the fourteen at the end recite the place of speech as the end of sixteen.","n":"The numbers (பன்னிரண்டு, பதினாலு, சோடம் for sixteen) belong to the arrangement of the mantra and are not explained. ஆகாரம் may be the long vowel ஆ or 'form'. The verse spells the name பயிரவி; the heading has வயிரவி.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","kalai","saththi","maayai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:85"},"t:1076":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1076,"t":["அந்தம் பதினாலும் அதுவே வயிரவி","முந்து நடுவும் முடிவும் முதலாகச்","சிந்தை கமலத்து எழுகின்ற மாசத்தி","அந்தமும் ஆதியும் ஆகின்றாளே."],"p":"","r":"The fourteen at the end is Bhairavi herself; the great Shakti who rises in the lotus of the mind, as beginning, middle and end, becomes both the end and the beginning.","n":"சிந்தை கமலம், the lotus of the mind, is usually taken as the heart-lotus of meditation. The point that she is end and beginning at once is plain; what the fourteen count is not.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","mandhiram","kalai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:85"},"t:1077":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1077,"t":["ஆகின்ற மூவரும் அங்கே அடங்குவர்","போகின்ற பூதம் பொருந்து புராதரர்","சார்கின்ற சாரவுழிச் சாரார் கதிர்பெறப்","போகுந் திரிபுரை புண்ணியத் தோரே."],"p":"","r":"The three who come into being are absorbed in her there; Tripura goes with those of merit so that they gain light, while the ancient ones who cling to the passing elements and those who do not reach the refuge do not.","n":"ஆகின்ற மூவர் are traditionally Brahma, Vishnu and Rudra. Lines 2 and 3 (புராதரர், சாரவுழிச் சாரார்) are obscure, and the contrast between them and the புண்ணியத்தோர் is my reading of the syntax.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:85"},"t:1078":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1078,"t":["புண்ணிய நந்தி புனிதன் திருவாகும்","எண்ணிய நாட்கள் இருபத்தேழ் சூழ்மதி","பண்ணிய வன்னி பகலோன் மதியீறு","திண்ணிய சிந்தைதன் தென்னனும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"Nandi, holy and pure, becomes the goddess; with the moon that circles through the reckoned twenty-seven days, and the fire, the sun and the moon's end that were set, the one of firm mind becomes the Southern one too.","n":"திரு may be the goddess, Sri or simply holiness. The twenty-seven days are the lunar asterisms, so the verse seems to set the practice by the moon. தென்னன், the Southerner or the Pandya, is taken as a name of Siva; the whole verse is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","saththi","kalai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:85"},"t:1079":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1079,"t":["தென்னன் திருநந்தி சேவகன் தன்னொடும்","பொன்னங் கிரியில் பூதலம் போற்றிடும்","பன்னும் பரிபிடி அந்தம் பகவனோடு","உன்னும் திரிபுரை ஓதிநின் றானுக்கே."],"p":"","r":"For the one who stands reciting Tripura, the earth on the golden mountain praises the Southern one, holy Nandi, with his attendant, and with the Lord at the end of the mantra.","n":"பரிபிடி அந்தம் is obscure; it may name the closing of the mantra or a seat of worship. பொன்னங்கிரி, the golden mountain, is usually Meru or Kailasa.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","sivam","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:85"},"t:1080":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1080,"t":["ஓதிய நந்தி உணரும் திருவருள்","நீதியில் வேத நெறிவந்து உரைசெய்யும்","போதம் இருபத்து எழுநாள் புணர்மதி","சோதி வயிரவி சூலம்வந்து ஆளுமே."],"p":"","r":"Nandi who taught knows grace and declares it rightly by the way of the Veda: when the knowing joined with the moon runs twenty-seven days, the trident of radiant Bhairavi comes to rule.","n":"The twenty-seven days again tie the practice to the lunar month. The trident's 'ruling' is commonly taken as her protection or possession of the practiser; the verse does not say which.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","saththi","vedham","kalai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:85"},"t:1081":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1081,"t":["சூலம் கபாலம் கை ஏந்திய சூலிக்கு","நாலாங் கரமுள நாகபா சாங்குச","மாலங் லயனறி யாத வடிவுக்கு","மேல்அங்க மாய்நின்ற மெல்லிய லாளே."],"p":"","r":"The trident-bearer who holds trident and skull has a fourth pair of hands holding noose and goad; she is the gentle one who stands as the upper limb of the form that Vishnu and Brahma could not know.","n":"The form Vishnu and Brahma could not know is Siva's column of fire; the goddess is placed as part of it (மேல்அங்கம்), which is read as her being half of Siva's body. நாலாங் கரம் can also be read as four hands in all.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saththi","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:85"},"t:1082":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1082,"t":["மெல்லியல் வஞ்சி விடமி கலைஞானி","சொல்லிய கிஞ்சுக நிறமன்னு சேயிழை","கல்லியல் ஒப்fபது காணும் திருமேனி","பல்லியல் ஆடையும் பன்மணி தானே."],"p":"","r":"The gentle vine-like one, knower of the kalas and of wisdom, jewelled and of the colour of the kinjuka flower, has a holy body like stone and wears garments of many kinds and many gems.","n":"கிஞ்சுகம், the palasa flower, makes her red. விடமி (she of poison?) is obscure, and line 3 is corrupt in the text, so 'like stone' is a guess at கல்லியல் ஒப்பது.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["saththi","kalai","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:85"},"t:1083":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1083,"t":["பன்மணி சந்திர கோடி திருமுடி","சொன்மணி குண்டலக்காதி உழைக்கண்ணி","நன்மணி சூரிய சோம நயனத்தள்","பொன்மணி வன்னியும் பூரிக்கின் றாளே."],"p":"","r":"Her crown has many gems, like ten million moons; she wears gem earrings and has a doe's eyes; her good gem-eyes are sun and moon, and she brims with golden gems and with fire.","n":"சூரிய சோம நயனம் with வன்னி gives the three eyes of sun, moon and fire, the third eye shared with Siva. The verse is a description for visualising her, as 1082 is.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:85"},"t:1084":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1084,"t":["பூரித்த பூவிதழ் எட்டினுக்கு உள்ளேயோர்","ஆரியத் தாள்உண்டுஅங்கு எண்மர் கன்னியர்","பாரித்த பெண்கள் அறுபத்து நால்வரும்","சாரித்துத் சத்தியைத் தாங்கள் கண்டாரே."],"p":"","r":"Within the eight petals of the full-blown flower there is a noble lady; there are eight maidens there, and the sixty-four women spread around came near and saw the Shakti.","n":"The eight-petalled lotus is a worship diagram. The eight maidens and sixty-four women are traditionally her attendant powers (the eight mothers and sixty-four yoginis), but the verse names none of them.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:85"},"t:1085":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1085,"t":["கண்ட சிலம்பு வளைசங்கு சக்கரம்","எண்டிசை யோகி இறைவி பராசக்தி","அண்டமோடு எண்டிசை தாங்கும் அருட்செல்வி","புண்டரி கத்தினுள் பூசனை யாளே."],"p":"","r":"With anklets, bangles, conch and discus seen on her, the yogini of the eight directions, the Goddess, Parashakti, the gracious one who bears the cosmos and the eight directions, is worshipped within the lotus.","n":"Conch and discus are Vishnu's emblems; here they are hers, as the verses after say that the gods are forms of her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saththi","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:85"},"t:1086":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1086,"t":["பூசனை கெந்தம் புனைமலர் மாகொடி","யோசனை பஞ்சத்து ஒலிவந்து உரைசெய்யும்","வாசம்இ லாத மணிமந் திரயோகம்","தேசம் திகழும் திரிபுரை காணே."],"p":"","r":"With worship, scent, adorned flowers and great banners, in contemplation the sound of the five comes and speaks; in the gem-mantra yoga that needs no dwelling, see Tripura shining in her radiance.","n":"பஞ்சத்து ஒலி, the sound of the five, may be the five-syllable mantra or five inner sounds. வாசம் இலாத may mean without scent or without abode. The verse moves from outer worship to inner hearing and sight.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","mandhiram","yogam","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:85"},"t:1087":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1087,"t":["காணும் பலபல தெய்வங்கள் வெவ்வேறு","பூணும் பலபல பொன்போலத் தோற்றிடும்","பேணும் சிவனும் பிரமனும் மாயனும்","காணும் தலைவிநற் காரணி காணே."],"p":"","r":"The many gods one sees are each different, just as the many ornaments worn appear many though they are gold; see that the Mistress is the good cause of Siva, Brahma and Vishnu whom people honour.","n":"The gold and ornaments image is the classic one for one substance in many forms. Here even Siva is among the forms caused by her, which the hymn later balances by calling him her husband and the light (1119, 1123).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saththi","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:85"},"t:1088":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1088,"t":["காரணி மந்திரம் ஓதுங் கமலத்துப்","பூரண கும்ப விரேசம் பொருந்திய","நாரணி நந்தி நடுஅங்கு உரைசெய்த","ஆரண வேதநூல் அந்தமும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"The Karani mantra recited in the lotus, joined with filling, holding and emptying the breath, and Narani, set in the middle as Nandi taught, are the end of the Vedic books too.","n":"பூரண கும்ப விரேசம் is read as பூரகம், கும்பகம், இரேசகம், the three phases of breath control. காரணி and நாரணி are names of the goddess. ஆரண வேதநூல் அந்தம் may simply mean Vedanta.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","praanan","saththi","vedaantham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:85"},"t:1089":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1089,"t":["அந்த நடுவிரல் ஆதி சிறுவிரல்","வந்த வழிமுறை மாறி உரை செய்யும்","செந்தமிழ் ஆதி தெளிந்து வழிபடு","நந்தி இதனை நவம் உரைத்தானே."],"p":"","r":"From the end, the middle finger, to the beginning, the little finger, recite reversing the order in which it came; understand clearly the start of fine Tamil and worship: Nandi taught this as new.","n":"The verse describes placing the mantra's syllables on the fingers. செந்தமிழ் ஆதி may be the Tamil letters from அ, or Tamil as the first speech. நவம் can mean new or nine; 1090 begins with நவசத்தி, the nine Shaktis.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","thamizh","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:85"},"t:1090":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1090,"t":["உரைத்த நவசத்தி ஒன்று முடிய","நிரைத்த இராசி நெடுமுறை எண்ணிப்","பிரைச்சதம் எட்டுமுன் பேசிய நந்தி","நிரைத்து நியதி நியமம்செய் தானே."],"p":"","r":"When the nine Shaktis spoken of are finished one by one, counting the long order of the zodiac signs, Nandi, who earlier spoke of the eight, set them in order and fixed rule and observance.","n":"பிரைச்சதம் எட்டு is obscure. நியதி நியமம் may be the rule of practice or the second limb of yoga. The verse seems to tie the nine powers to the cycle of signs.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:85"},"t:1091":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1091,"t":["தாமக் குழலி தயைக்கண்ணி உள்நின்ற","ஏமத்து இருளற வீசும் இளங்கொடி","ஓமப் பெருஞ்சுடர் உள்எழு நுண்புகை","மேவித்து அழுதொடு மீண்டது காணே."],"p":"","r":"She of garlanded tresses and merciful eyes, the young vine who stands within and scatters the darkness of the night: see the fine smoke rise inside the great flame of the fire rite, mingle and return with nectar.","n":"அழுதொடு is read as அமுதொடு, with nectar; as written it could mean 'with weeping'. ஏமத்து இருள் may be the darkness of night or of delusion. The fire rite may be outward or the inner fire.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","aaguthi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:85"},"t:1092":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1092,"t":["காணும் இருதய மந்திர முங்கண்டு","பேணும் நமஎன்று பேசும் தலைமேலே","வேணு நடுவு மிகநின்ற ஆகுதி","பூணு நடுஎன்ற அந்தம் சிகையே."],"p":"","r":"Seeing the heart mantra, say 'nama' in honour; upon the head the oblation set in the middle; and the end said to be worn in the middle is for the topknot.","n":"The verse runs through the limbs of the mantra placed on the body (அங்க நியாசம்): heart with namah, head with the oblation word (ஆகுதி, svaha), topknot (சிகை) with its ending. Lines 3 and 4 are compressed and the endings are the tradition's, not named.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:85"},"t:1093":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1093,"t":["சிகைநின்ற அந்தக் கவசங்கொண்டு ஆதிப்","பகைநின்ற அங்கத்தைப் பாரென்று மாறித்","தொகைநின்ற நேத்திர முத்திரை சூலம்","வகைநின்ற யோனி வகுத்தலும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"With the armour at the end of the topknot, then turning to look at the limb that stands as enemy, and then the eye, the mudras, the trident, and the yoni in its kind can be formed.","n":"கவசம் (armour), நேத்திரம் (eye) and the limb 'that stands as enemy' continue the limbs of the mantra; the last is commonly taken as the weapon (astra). Then the hand-seals: trident and yoni.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["muththirai","mandhiram"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:85"},"t:1094":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1094,"t":["வருத்தம் இரண்டும் சிறுவிரன் மாறிப்","பொருந்தி அணிவிரல் சுட்டிப் பிடித்து","நெரித்தொன்ற வைத்து நெடிது நடுவே","பெருத்த விரல்இரண்டு உள்புக்குப் பேசே."],"p":"","r":"Crossing the two little fingers, joining them, pointing and holding with the ring fingers, pressing them together and setting the two thumbs within the middle, speak.","n":"A hand-seal described finger by finger, taken as the yoni mudra of 1093. வருத்தம் இரண்டும் is obscure, and which fingers அணிவிரல் and பெருத்த விரல் are is the usual reading, not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["muththirai","mandhiram"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:85"},"t:1095":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1095,"t":["பேசிய மந்திரம் இராகம் பிரித்துரை","கூசமி லாத சகாரத்தை முன்கொண்டு","வாசிப் பிராணன் உபதேசம் ஆகைக்குக்","கூசியவிந்து வுடன் கொண்டு கூவே."],"p":"","r":"Recite the spoken mantra dividing it by its sounds; taking the sa-letter first without hesitation, for the teaching of the breath and prana, take it with the shrinking bindu and call.","n":"சகாரம் and the bindu (the nasal dot) build a seed-syllable. இராகம் may be tone or the mantra's parts; வாசி is the breath. The exact syllable is not recoverable from the verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","praanan","vindhu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:85"},"t:1096":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1096,"t":["கூவிய சீவன் பிராணன் முதலாகப்","பாவிய சவ்வுடன் பண்ணும் யகாரத்தை","மேவிய மாயை விரிசங்கு முத்திரை","தேவி நடுவுள் திகழ்ந்துநின் றாளே."],"p":"","r":"With the soul that was called and prana first, with the spread 'sau' and the ya-letter made, with maya joined and the wide conch mudra, the Goddess shone in the middle.","n":"சவ் and யகாரம் are seed-letters; சவ் is the familiar bija of Tripura worship. The verse ends the rite with the goddess present in the middle of the diagram or of the body.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","muththirai","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:85"},"t:1097":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1097,"t":["நின்ற வயிரவி நீலி நிசாசரி","ஒன்றும் இரண்டும் ஒருங்கிய உள்ளத்துச்","சென்றருள் நாயகி தேவர் பிரானுக்கே","நன்றருள் ஞாலத்து நாடிடும் சாற்றியே."],"p":"","r":"Bhairavi who stands, the dark one, the night-walker, the Lady who goes in grace into the heart where one and two have joined, for the Lord of the gods: seek her, proclaiming her in the world she graces well.","n":"நீலி and நிசாசரி are fierce names. ஒன்றும் இரண்டும் ஒருங்கிய உள்ளம் may be the heart where the soul and the Lord, or Siva and Shakti, are one. From here the chapter becomes a hymn.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:85"},"t:1098":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1098,"t":["சாற்றிய வேதம் சராசரம் ஐம்பூதம்","நாற்றிசை முக்கண்ணி நாடும் இருள்வெளி","தோற்றும் உயிர்ப்பன்மை சோதி பராபரை","ஆற்றலொடு ஆய்நிற்கும் ஆதி முதல்வியே."],"p":"","r":"The first mother stands with her power as the Veda that was proclaimed, the moving and unmoving, the five elements, the four directions, the three-eyed one, darkness and open space, the many lives that appear, the light and Parapara.","n":"A list identifying her with everything, from scripture and the elements to Siva himself (முக்கண்ணி can be she of three eyes) and the supreme (பராபரை).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saththi","uyir","vedham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:85"},"t:1099":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1099,"t":["ஆதி வயிரவி கன்னித் துறைமன்னி","ஓதி உணரில் உடலுயிர் ஈசனாம்","பேதை உலகிற் பிறவிகள் நாசமாம்","ஓத உலவாக் கோலம் ஒன்று ஆகுமே."],"p":"","r":"If one recites and realises primal Bhairavi abiding at the virgin's shore, body and life become the Lord, births in this foolish world are destroyed, and an unending form becomes one.","n":"கன்னித்துறை is often taken as Kanyakumari, where the goddess is a virgin; it may also be an inner place. உடலுயிர் ஈசனாம் can mean body and soul become the Lord, or that the Lord is the soul of the body.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","piravi","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:85"},"t:1100":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1100,"t":["கோலக் குழவி குலாய புருவத்துள்","நீலக் குவளை மலரன்ன கண்ணினாள்","ஆலிக்கும் இன்னமுது ஆனந்த சுந்தரி","மேலைச் சிவத்தை வெளிப்படுத் தாளே."],"p":"","r":"The lovely girl, with eyes like the blue water-lily under curving brows, Ananda Sundari, the sweet nectar that delights, revealed the Siva above.","n":"Shakti reveals Siva: the hymn's recurring claim that the way to Siva is through her. மேலைச் சிவம் may be Siva beyond, or Siva at the crown of the head.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saththi","sivam","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:85"},"t:1101":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1101,"t":["வெளிப்படு வித்து விளைவுஅறி வித்துத்","தெளிப்படு வித்துஎன் சிந்தையின் உள்ளே","களிப்படு வித்துக் கதிர்ப்படு சோதி","ஒளிப்படு வித்துஎன்னை உய்ய்க்கொண்டாளே."],"p":"","r":"She revealed, made me know the fruit, made it clear within my mind, made me rejoice, made the rayed light shine, and took me so that I was saved.","n":"Five lines of the causative -வித்து (made to) give a sequence: revelation, understanding, clarity, joy, light, rescue. The poet speaks in the first person, as through most of the hymn.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","saththi","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:85"},"t:1102":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1102,"t":["கொண்டனள் கோலங் கோடி அநேகங்கள்","கண்டனள் எண்ணென் கலையின் கண் மாலைகள்","விண்டனள் மேலை விரிகதிர் மூன்றையும்","தண்டலை மேல்நின்ற தையல் நல் லாளே."],"p":"","r":"She took countless millions of forms, she saw the garlands in the eye of the sixty-four kalas, and she opened the three wide rays above: the fine woman who stands above the head.","n":"எண்ணெண் கலை are the sixty-four arts or kalas. The three rays are traditionally sun, moon and fire. தண்டலை may be the top of the spine (தண்டு) or a grove; the yogic sense is likely but not fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","kalai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:85"},"t:1103":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1103,"t":["தையல் நல் லாளைத் தவத்தின் தலைவியை","மையலை நோக்கும் மனோன்மணி மங்கையைப்","பையநின் றேத்திப் பணிமின் பணிந்தபின்","வெய்ய பவம்இனி மேவகி லாவே."],"p":"","r":"Stand and praise slowly and bow to the fine woman, the leader of austerity, Manonmani who looks on delusion; once you have bowed, cruel births will not come again.","n":"மையலை நோக்கும் can be 'who looks upon (and so ends) delusion'. Bowing as the end of births is an old pattern; here it is to the goddess.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saththi","piravi","thavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:85"},"t:1104":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1104,"t":["வேயன தோளி விரையுறு மென்மலர்","ஏய குழலி இளம்பிறை ஏந்திழை","தூய கடைமுடிச் சூலினி சுந்தரி","ஏயெனது உள்ளத்து இனிதுஇருந் தாளே."],"p":"","r":"She of bamboo-like shoulders, with tresses set with fragrant soft flowers, wearing the young crescent, the beautiful trident-bearer with the pure crown of hair, has sat sweetly in my heart.","n":"The crescent and trident are Siva's marks, worn by her. The verse turns from description to her dwelling in the poet, which the following verses develop.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saththi","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:85"},"t:1105":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1105,"t":["இனியதென் மூலை இருக்குங் குமரி","தனியொரு நாயகி தானே தலைவி","தனிப்படு வித்தனள் சார்வு படுத்து","நனிப்படு வித்துள்ளம் நாடிநின் றாளே."],"p":"","r":"The maiden who dwells in the sweet southern corner, the sole lady and leader herself, set me apart, brought me to refuge, filled me, and stands seeking my heart.","n":"தென் மூலை is the southern corner, perhaps Kanyakumari again (1099); மூலை may also hint at மூலம், the base of the spine. தனிப்படுவித்தனள் may be 'made me single' or 'made me unique'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:85"},"t:1106":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1106,"t":["நாடிகள் மூன்று நடுஎழ ஞானத்துக்","கூடி யிருந்த குமரி குலக்கன்னி","பாடகச் சீறடிப் பைம்பொற் சிலம்பொலி","ஊடக மேவி உறங்குகின் றாளே."],"p":"","r":"The maiden of the clan, joined with wisdom where the three channels rise through the middle, sleeps within, the fresh gold anklets on her small feet sounding.","n":"The sleeping maiden in the middle of the three channels is traditionally kundalini, coiled until woken. The verse does not use the word; குலக்கன்னி may echo kula, the base. Why she sleeps while the channels rise is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kundalini","naadi","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:85"},"t:1107":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1107,"t":["உறங்கும் அளவில் மனோன்மணி வந்து","கறங்கும் வளைக்கைக் கழுத்தாரப் புல்லிப்","பிறங்கொளித் தம்பலம் வாயில் உமிழ்ந்திட்டு","உறங்கல்ஐ யாஎன்று உபாயம்செய் தாளே."],"p":"","r":"While I slept, Manonmani came, embraced my neck with her jingling bangled arms, spat her shining betel into my mouth, said 'Do not sleep, sir', and gave me the means.","n":"உறங்கும் அளவில் may be the poet's sleep or follow on from her sleep in 1106. Giving her chewed betel is an image of intimate grace known from devotional legend. உபாயம், the means, is taken as the yogic method of waking.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","saththi","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:85"},"t:1108":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1108,"t":["உபாயம் அளிக்கும் ஒருத்தியென் உள்ளத்து","அபாயம் அறக்கெடுத்து அன்பு விளைத்துச்","சுவாவை விளக்கும் சுழியாகத் துள்ளே","அவாவை அடக்கிவைத்து அஞ்சல்என் றாளே."],"p":"","r":"The one who gives the means rooted out all danger from my heart, made love grow, held craving down inside the whirl that lights the breath, and said 'Fear not'.","n":"சுவா is read as the breath (சுவாசம்), and சுழி as a whirl or centre in the body; both are uncertain. அஞ்சல் is the gesture and word of reassurance.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","avaa","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:85"},"t:1109":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1109,"t":["அஞ்சொல் மொழியாள் அருந்தவப் பெண்பிள்ளை","செஞ்சொல் மடமொழி சீருடைச் சேயிழை","தஞ்சமென்று எண்ணித்தன் சேவடி போற்றுவார்க்கு","இன்சொல் அளிக்கும் இறைவியென் றாரே."],"p":"","r":"They say that she of sweet speech, the girl of rare austerity, of true and gentle words, richly jewelled, is the goddess who gives sweet words to those who praise her red feet, thinking her their refuge.","n":"The verse repeats sweet speech in her and as her gift. என்றாரே, 'they said', presents it as the word of others, not the poet's own.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saththi","vazhipaadu","inchol"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:85"},"t:1110":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1110,"t":["ஆருயி ராயும் அருந்தவப் பெண்பிள்ளை","காரியல் கோதையுள் காரணி நாரணி","ஊரும் உயிரும் உலகும் ஒடுக்கிடும்","கோரியென் உள்ளம் குலாவிநின் றாளே."],"p":"","r":"The girl of rare austerity who is dear life, Karani and Narani within the dark-tressed one, the fierce one who withdraws towns, lives and worlds, plays in my heart.","n":"ஒடுக்கிடும், withdraws, gives her the act of dissolution, one of the five acts. Fierce and tender names sit together through the hymn.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saththi","uyir","aindhozhil"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:85"},"t:1111":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1111,"t":["குலாவிய கோலக் குமரியென் னுள்ளம்","நிலாவி யிருந்து நெடுநாள் அணைந்தும்","உலாவி இருந்துணர்ந்து உச்சியின் உள்ளே","கலாவி இருந்த கலைத்தலை யாளே."],"p":"","r":"The lovely maiden who played shone in my heart, joined it for many days, moved there and came to know it, and is the one at the head of the kalas who mingles within the crown of the head.","n":"The movement from heart to crown (உச்சி) suggests the yogic rising of Shakti. கலைத்தலை may be the summit of the kalas or of the moon's digits at the head.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","kalai","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:85"},"t:1112":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1112,"t":["கலைத்தலை நெற்றியோர் கண்ணுடைக் கண்ணுள்","முலைத்தலை மங்கை முயங்கி இருக்கும்","சிலைத்தலை யாய தெரிவினை நோக்கி","அலைத்தபூங் கொம்பினள் அங்கிருந் தாளே."],"p":"","r":"In the eye of the forehead that has one eye, at the head of the kalas, the breasted woman stays embraced; looking to the discernment at the bow's head, the swaying flower-branch stayed there.","n":"The forehead eye and சிலைத்தலை (the head of the bow, the brow) point to the place between the eyebrows. Whom she embraces is left unsaid; it is usually taken as Siva.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","yogam","kalai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:85"},"t:1113":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1113,"t":["இருந்தனள் ஏந்திழை என்னுள்ளம் மேவிப்","பொருந்திய நால்விரல் புக்கனள் புல்லித்","திருந்திய தாணுவில் சேர்ந்துடன் ஒன்fறி","அருந்தவம் எய்தினள் ஆதியி னாளே."],"p":"","r":"The jewelled one sat, entering my heart; she entered the four fingers' space and embraced, joined with the perfected pillar and became one with it, and the primal one attained rare austerity.","n":"தாணு, the pillar, is a name of Siva. நால்விரல் may be a measure of four finger-breadths in the body. Line 3 is corrupt in the text; ஒன்றி is read.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["saththi","sivam","thavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:85"},"t:1114":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1114,"t":["ஆதி அனாதி அகாரணி காரணி","சோதிய சோதி சுகபர சந்தரி","மாது சமாதி மனோன்மணி மங்கலி","ஓதிஎன் உள்ளத்து உடன்இயைந் தாளே."],"p":"","r":"The beginning and the beginningless, the causeless and the cause, the light of light, Sundari of supreme bliss, the woman, samadhi, Manonmani, the auspicious one, recited, joined my heart.","n":"A string of names held together in paradox: beginning and beginningless, causeless and cause. Line 3 recurs in 1120, and line 1 in 1124.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saththi","samaadhi","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:85"},"t:1115":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1115,"t":["இயைந்தனள் ஏந்திழை என்உள்ளம் மேலி","நயந்தனள் அங்கே நமசிவ என்னும்","அயன்தனை ஓரும் பதமது பற்றும்","பெயர்ந்தனள் மற்றும் பிதற்றுஅறுத் தாளே."],"p":"","r":"The jewelled one joined my heart and raised it; she desired there the words 'namasiva'; she grasps the state that knows Brahma; she moved and cut away all my other babble.","n":"நமசிவ is the five-syllable mantra. அயன்தனை ஓரும் பதம் is obscure: perhaps the word that knows the creator, or the state beyond him. பிதற்று, babble, is the idle speech the mantra replaces. மேலி is probably மேவி.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","saththi","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:85"},"t:1116":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1116,"t":["பிதற்றிக் கழிந்தனர் பேதை மனிதர்","முயற்றியின் முற்றி அருளும் முதல்வி","கயற்றிகழ் முக்கண்ணுங் கம்பலைச் செவ்வாய்","முகத்தருள் நோக்கமும் முன்னுள்ள தாமே."],"p":"","r":"Foolish people babbled and passed away; before those whose effort is complete stand the first mother who grants grace, her three fish-bright eyes, her red mouth that speaks, and the gracious look of her face.","n":"முயற்றியின் முற்றி may mean 'when effort is complete' or 'complete in effort'. கம்பலை (clamour, sound) with செவ்வாய் is unusual.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","muyarchi","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:85"},"t:1117":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1117,"t":["உள்ளத்து இதயத்து நெஞ்சத்தொரு மூன்றுள்","பிள்ளைத் தடம்உள்ளே பேசப் பிறந்தது","வள்ளல் திருவின் வயிற்றுனுள் மாமாயைக்","கள்ள ஒளியின் கருத்தாகுங் கன்னியே."],"p":"","r":"Within the three, mind, heart and breast, a child's path was born as it was spoken; in the womb of the bountiful Tiru, the virgin is the intent of the stealthy light of Mahamaya.","n":"The verse is obscure throughout. உள்ளம், இதயம், நெஞ்சம் are three inner seats. கள்ள ஒளி, stealthy light, fits maya as what both shows and hides. It prepares the virgin mother of 1118.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","maayai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:85"},"t:1118":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1118,"t":["கன்னியுங் கன்னி அழிந்தனள் காதலி","துன்னியங fகைவரைப் பெற்றனள் தூய்மொழி","பன்னிய நன்னூற் பகவரும் அங்குள","என்னேஇம் மாயை இருளது தானே."],"p":"","r":"The virgin, and the virgin undone, the beloved of pure words, bore the five; the lords of the well-made books are there too: what is this? This maya is darkness itself.","n":"The paradox of a virgin who is mother. ஐவர, the five, are traditionally the five gods of the five acts. Line 2 is corrupt in the text. Whether 'this maya is darkness' rebukes those who cannot grasp the paradox or names her as maya is open.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["saththi","maayai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:85"},"t:1119":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1119,"t":["இருளது சத்தி வெளியதுஎம் அண்ணல்","பொருளது புண்ணியர் போகத்துள் இன்பம்","தெருளது சிந்தையைத் தெய்வம்என்று எண்ணில்","அருளது செய்யும்எம் ஆதிப் பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"Darkness is Shakti and light is our Lord; the substance is the joy in the enjoyment of the meritorious; if one, in clarity, thinks of the mind as god, our primal Lord grants grace.","n":"Shakti as darkness and Siva as light can be read as concealing and revealing, two of the five acts. Line 4 may also be read 'if one thinks of god with a clear mind'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","sivam","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:85"},"t:1120":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1120,"t":["ஆதி அனாதியும் ஆய பராசக்தி","பாதிபராபரை மேலுறை பைந்தொடி","மாது சமாதி மனோன்மணி மங்கலி","ஓதும்என் உள்ளத்து உடன்முகிழ்த் தாளே."],"p":"","r":"Parashakti, who is beginning and beginningless, the green-bangled one dwelling above as Parapara and as the half, the woman, samadhi, Manonmani, the auspicious one, blossomed together in my reciting heart.","n":"பாதி, the half, is her place as the left half of Siva. The verse repeats 1114 nearly line for line but ends 'blossomed' (முகிழ்த்தாள்) instead of 'joined'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saththi","sivam","samaadhi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:85"},"t:1121":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1121,"t":["ஓதிய வண்ணம் கலையின் உயர்கலை","ஆதியில் வேதமே யாம்என்று அறிகிலர்","சாதியும் பேதமும் தத்துவ மாய்நிற்பன்","ஆதியென்று ஓதினள் ஆவின் கிழத்தியே."],"p":"","r":"As it is recited, people do not know that the highest of the kalas is the Veda from the beginning; the mistress of the cow declared that he stands as caste, difference and the tattvas, and is the first.","n":"ஆவின் கிழத்தி, mistress of the cow, also reads as mistress of souls, since ஆ (cow) and pasu are one word; 1122 ties her to Tiruvavaduturai. Who 'stands as caste and difference' is unclear; perhaps the Lord, perhaps her.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","vedham","thaththuvam","pasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:85"},"t:1122":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1122,"t":["ஆவின் கிழத்திநல் ஆவடு தண்துறை","நாவின் கிழத்தி நலம்புகழ்ந்து ஏத்திடும்","தேவின் கிழத்தி திருவாம் சிவமங்கை","மேவும் கிழத்தி வினைகடிந் தாளே."],"p":"","r":"The mistress of the cow, the mistress of the cool shore of good Avaduturai, the mistress of speech whom they praise, the mistress of the gods, Siva's woman who is Tiru, the mistress who abides, drove out my deeds.","n":"ஆவடுதண்துறை is Tiruvavaduturai, where tradition places Tirumular's life and his entering the cowherd's body; the cow names gain local weight here. நாவின் கிழத்தி may be Sarasvati as a form of her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saththi","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:85"},"t:1123":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1123,"t":["வினைகடிந் தார்உள்ளத்து உள்ளொளி மேவித்","தனைஅடைந் தோர்க்கெல்லாம் தத்துவ மாய்நிற்பள்","எனைஅடிமை கொண்ட ஏந்திழை ஈசன்","கணவனைக் காண அனாதியும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"Abiding as the inner light in the hearts of those whose deeds are driven out, she stands as the tattva for all who reach her; the jewelled one who made me her servant becomes the beginningless too, so that one may see the Lord her husband.","n":"Picking up வினைகடிந்தாள் from 1122. Line 4 is compressed: she is the way to seeing Siva, and is called beginningless as he is.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","vinai","thaththuvam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:85"},"t:1124":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1124,"t":["ஆதி அனாதி அகாரணி காரணி","வேதமது ஆய்ந்தனள் வேதியர்க் காய்நின்ற","சோதி தனிச்சுடர் சொரூபமாய் நிற்கும்","பாதி பராபரை பன்னிரண்டு ஆதியே."],"p":"","r":"The beginning and the beginningless, the causeless and the cause, she searched out the Veda; the light that stood for the Vedic ones, standing in the form of a lone flame, is Parapara, the half, the first of the twelve.","n":"பன்னிரண்டு ஆதி returns to the twelve kalas of the opening verse, closing the chapter as a ring. Line 1 repeats 1114.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saththi","vedham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:85"},"t:1125":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1125,"t":["அளந்தேன் அகலிடத்து அந்தமும் ஈறும்","அளந்தேன் அகலிடத்து ஆதிப் பிரானை","அளந்தேன் அகலிடத்து ஆணொடு பெண்ணும்","அளந்தேன் அவனருள் ஆய்ந்துணர்ந் தேனே."],"p":"","r":"Tirumular says he has measured the bounds of the wide world, its first Lord, and male and female in it, and that he measured them by examining and coming to know the Lord's grace.","n":"அளந்தேன் (I measured) opens all four lines. ஆணொடு பெண் may be the male and female of the world or Siva and Shakti; in a chapter on Shakti the second is likely, and the grace (அருள்) by which he measured is traditionally Shakti herself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","iraivan","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:86"},"t:1126":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1126,"t":["உணர்ந்திலர் ஈசனை ஊழிசெய் சக்தி","புணர்ந்தது பூரணம் புண்ணியர் தங்கள்","கணங்களைத் தன்னருள் செய்கின்ற கன்னி","கொணர்ந்த வழிகொண்டு கும்பகமாமே."],"p":"","r":"People have not known the Lord; the Shakti who brings about the ages joined him and that is fullness, and by the path the maiden who graces the companies of the meritorious brings, breath retention comes.","n":"பூரணம் (fullness) gives the chapter its title and is also the yogic term for drawing the breath in; with கும்பகம் (holding it) at the end, the verse seems to play on the stages of pranayama. கன்னி, the maiden, is a name of Shakti, ever virgin though consort. How the clauses connect is not clear from the verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","iraivan","praanan","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:86"},"t:1127":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1127,"t":["கும்பக் களிறுஐந்தும் கோலொடு பாகனும்","வம்பில் திகழும் மணிமுடி வண்ணனும்","இன்பக் கலவி இனிதுறை தையலும்","அன்பிற் கலவியுள் ஆயொழிந் தாரே."],"p":"","r":"The five elephants, their mahout with his goad, the jewel-crowned Lord and the lady who dwells sweetly in blissful union all merge and are lost in the union of love.","n":"கும்பக் களிறு ஐந்து, five elephants with swelling temples, are traditionally the five senses and the mahout the mind or the soul; கும்பம் picks up கும்பகம் from 1126. ஆயொழிந்தாரே, they became and ended, is read as absorption. The image is symbolic and the verse does not name its terms.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anbu","saththi","sivam","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:86"},"t:1128":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1128,"t":["இன்பக் கலவியில் இட்டொழு கின்றதோர்","அன்பிற் புகவல்ல னாம்எங்கள் அப்பனும்","துன்பக் குழம்பில் துயருறும் பாசத்துள்","என்பிற் பாரசக்தி என்னம்மை தானே."],"p":"","r":"Our father can enter only into the love set in blissful union, and within the bond where people suffer in a mire of sorrow, Parashakti, my mother, is there in the very bones.","n":"The verse divides the parents: the father reached through love, the mother present inside bondage. என்பிற் is read here as 'in the bone', the body; it could also be a form of என்பது. The rhyme of அன்பு and என்பு is old in Tamil.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","sivam","paasam","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:86"},"t:1129":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1129,"t":["என்னம்மை என்னப்பன் என்னும் செருக்கற்று","உன்னம்மை ஊழித் தலைவனும் அங்குளன்","மன்னம்மை யாகி மருவி உரைசெய்Yயும்","பின்னம்மை யாய்நின்ற பேர்நந்தி தானே."],"p":"","r":"Once the conceit of saying 'my mother, my father' is gone, your mother and the Lord of the ages are both there; the great Nandi, joined with her as the abiding mother, stands as the mother after.","n":"The first line is clear: claiming the divine parents as one's own is a pride (செருக்கு) to be cut. The rest is obscure: பின்னம்மை may be 'the later mother' or பின்னம் (division) with அம்மை, and the subject of the last two lines may be Nandi or Shakti. The point the tradition draws is that the Lord is both father and mother.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["saththi","sivam","adakkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:86"},"t:1130":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1130,"t":["தார்மேல் உறைகின்ற தண்மலர் நான்முகன்","பார்மேல் இருப்பதொரு நூறு தானுள","பூமேல் உறைகின்ற போதகம் வந்தனள்","நாமேல் உறைகின்ற நாயகி ஆணையே."],"p":"","r":"The four-faced Brahma of the cool flower, the hundred on the earth and the teacher who dwells on the flower all come by the command of the lady who dwells on the tongue.","n":"நாமேல் உறைகின்ற நாயகி, the lady on the tongue, is the goddess of speech, here taken as Shakti. போதகம் may be 'teaching' or a 'teacher', and ஒரு நூறு (a hundred) is unexplained. The verse is nearly repeated as 1141.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","sol"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:86"},"t:1131":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1131,"t":["ஆணையமாய்வருந் தாதுள் இருந்தவர்","மாணைய மாய மனத்தை ஒருக்கிப்பின்","பாணைய மாய பரத்தை அறிந்தபின்","தாணைய மாய தானதனன் தானே."],"p":"","r":"Those who stay within, gathering the deluding mind into one and then knowing the Supreme, become that one themselves.","n":"Each line turns on a compound ending in -ஐயமாய் (ஆணையம், மாணையம், பாணையம், தாணையம்) whose senses are obscure; ஆணையம் picks up ஆணை from 1130. Only the outline (one-pointed mind, knowing the Supreme, becoming it) is fairly clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["manam","yogam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:86"},"t:1132":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1132,"t":["தானே எழுந்தஇத் தத்துவ நாயகி","வானோர் எழுந்து மதியை விளக்கினள்","தேனார் எழகின்ற தீபத்து ஒளியுடன்","மானே நடமுடை மன்றறி யீரே."],"p":"","r":"The mistress of the tattvas, arising of herself, lit the moon as the gods rose; with the light of a lamp that wells with honey, know the hall of the dance.","n":"மதியை விளக்கினள் can mean she lit the moon or she made the mind clear. மன்று is the hall where Siva dances, outward at Chidambaram or inward in the yogi's heart or head. மானே (O deer) addresses a woman, or names the goddess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","thaththuvam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:86"},"t:1133":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1133,"t":["அறிவான மாயையும் ஐம்புலக் கூட்டத்து","அறிவான மங்கை அருளது சேரில்","பிரியா அறிவறி வார்உளம் பேணும்","நெறியாய சித்த நிறைந்திருந் தாளே."],"p":"","r":"When the grace of the lady who is knowledge, within maya and within the five senses, comes to one, she stays filling the mind of those who know the knowledge that does not part, as their path.","n":"The verse makes Shakti knowledge both in maya and in the senses, so even bound knowing is hers. பிரியா அறிவு, knowledge that does not part, is traditionally knowledge inseparable from the Lord.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","thiruvarul","arivu","maayai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:86"},"t:1134":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1134,"t":["இரவும் பகலும் இலாத இடத்தே","குரவம் செய்கின்ற குழலியை நாடி","அரவம்செய் யாமல் அருளுடன் தூங்கப்","பருவம்செய் யாததோர் பாலனும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"One who seeks out the long-haired lady dancing in the place where there is neither night nor day, and sleeps there with grace in silence, becomes a child that age does not touch.","n":"இரவும் பகலும் இலாத இடம் is traditionally the state beyond waking and sleep, or where the breaths called sun and moon cease. அரவம் is 'noise' but also 'serpent', so a reading in terms of kundalini is possible. பருவம் செய்யாத பாலன், a child that does not age, is the yogi's youth; பாலன் opens 1135.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","yogam","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:86"},"t:1135":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1135,"t":["பாலனும் ஆகும் பராசத்தி தன்னோடு","மேலனு காவிந்து நாதங்கள் விட்டிட","மூலம தாமெனும் முத்திக்கு நேர்படச்","சாலவு மாய்நின்ற தற்பரத் தானே."],"p":"","r":"He becomes a child, and with Parashakti, leaving bindu and nada behind, he stands as the Supreme itself, meeting the release that is the root.","n":"Bindu and nada are passed beyond, not reached, so they are here stages below release. தற்பரம் is the Supreme in itself. Whether the subject is the yogi of 1134 or the Lord is not settled by the verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","vindhu","naadham","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:86"},"t:1136":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1136,"t":["நின்ற பராசக்தி நீள்பரன் தன்னோடு","நின்றறி ஞானமும் இச்சையு மாய் நிற்கும்","நன்றறி யும்கிரி யாசக்தி நண்ணவே","மற்றன வற்றுள் மருவிடுந் தானே."],"p":"","r":"Parashakti, standing with the Supreme, becomes the power of knowledge and the power of will, and when the power of action arrives she enters into all the rest.","n":"ஞானம், இச்சை and கிரியா are the three Shaktis of Saiva doctrine: knowing, willing and acting. The verse gives them an order, action last, through which she reaches everything else.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saththi","sivam","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:86"},"t:1137":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1137,"t":["மருவொத்த மங்கையும் தானும் உடனே","உருவொத்துநின்றமை ஒன்றும் உணரார்","கருவொத்து நின்று கலக்கின போது","திருவொத்த சிந்தைவைத்து எந்தைநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"No one perceives that the lady, like fragrance in a flower, and he stand together in one form; when they mingled, like an embryo forming, our father stood with his thought set on her as on fortune.","n":"மருவொத்த can be 'like fragrance' (inseparable from the flower) or 'fitting'. கருவொத்து may be 'like an embryo' or 'in accord with the cause'. திரு may be Lakshmi, fortune or simply the sacred. The plain point, that Siva and Shakti are one form, is clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","sivam","karu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:86"},"t:1138":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1138,"t":["சிந்தையின் உள்ளே திரியும் சிவசத்தி","விந்துவும் நாதமும் ஆயே விரிந்தனள்","சந்திர பூமி சடாதரி சாத்தவி","அந்தமொடு ஆதிய தாம்வண் ணத்தாளே."],"p":"","r":"The Siva-Shakti who moves within thought spread out as bindu and nada; she is the moon-ground, the one with matted hair, the pure one, of the nature of both end and beginning.","n":"சந்திர பூமி, சடாதரி and சாத்தவி are names of the goddess; சாத்தவி may be 'the sattvic one' or a form of Shambhavi. சிந்தையின் உள்ளே places the cosmic bindu and nada in the mind.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","vindhu","naadham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:86"},"t:1139":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1139,"t":["ஆறி யிருந்த அமுத பயோதரி","மாறி யிருந்த வழியறி வாரில்லை","தேறி யிருந்துநல் தீபத்து ஒளியுடன்","ஊறி யிருந்தனள் உள்ளுடை யார்க்கே."],"p":"","r":"No one knows the reversed way of the nectar-breasted lady who stays quiet; steady, with the light of a good lamp, she wells up for those who have the inner sight.","n":"அமுத பயோதரி, she whose breasts are nectar, is a name of the goddess. மாறி இருந்த வழி, the turned-back way, is traditionally the reversed course of breath or kundalini. உள்ளுடையார் may be those with inner vision or those who hold her within.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:86"},"t:1140":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1140,"t":["உடையவன் அங்கி உருத்திர சோதி","விடையவன் ஏறி விளங்கி இருக்கும்","கடையவர் போயிடும் கண்டவர் நெஞ்சத்து","அடையது வாகிய சாதகர் தாமே."],"p":"","r":"The Lord who owns all, the fire, the light of Rudra, rides the bull and shines; the base fall away, and the practitioners who see him become his dwelling place in their hearts.","n":"சாதகர் are practitioners of a discipline (sadhakas). The last two lines are compressed and the relation of கடையவர் போயிடும் to what follows is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:86"},"t:1141":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1141,"t":["தாமேல் உறைவிடம் ஆறிதழ் ஆனது","பார்மேல் இதழ்பழி னெட்டிரு நூறுன","பூமேல் உறைகின்ற புண்ணியம் வந்தனள்","பார்மேல் உறைகின்ற பைந்தொடி யாளே."],"p":"","r":"Her dwelling above is the six-petalled lotus; above the earth the petals number in hundreds; the merit that dwells on the flower has come, the lady of fresh bangles who dwells above the earth.","n":"The petalled lotuses are traditionally the chakras of the yogic body; the counts (ஆறிதழ், பழினெட்டு, இருநூறு) can't be fixed from this text. The verse repeats the frame of 1130 almost line for line, with petals in place of Brahma.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","yogam","kundalini"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:86"},"t:1142":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1142,"t":["பைந்தொடி யாளும் பரமன் இருந்திடத்","திண்கொடி யாகத் திகழ்திரு சோதியாம்","விண்கொடி யாகி விளங்கி வருதலால்","பெண்கொடி யாக நடந்தது உலகே."],"p":"","r":"Because the lady of fresh bangles stays where the Supreme is, as his firm banner and holy light, and comes shining as a creeper across the sky, the world moves on as the woman's creeper.","n":"கொடி means banner, creeper and, of a woman, slenderness; the verse plays on all three. The claim is that the world runs by her, as the creeper grows from its root in the Lord.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saththi","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:86"},"t:1143":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1143,"t":["நடந்தது அம்மலர் நாலுடன் அஞ்சாய்","இருந்தனர் கன்னிகள் எட்டுடன் ஒன்றாய்ப்","படர்ந்தது தன்வழி பங்கயத் துள்ளே","தொடர்ந்தது உள்வழி சோதி யடுத்தே."],"p":"","r":"The flower moved as four and five, the maidens sat as eight and one, it spread its own way inside the lotus and followed the inner way, close to the light.","n":"Both pairs make nine; the maidens are commonly taken as the nine Shaktis seated in the heart lotus, eight round one. What the subject of படர்ந்தது and தொடர்ந்தது is (the flower, the light, the breath) is left open.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:86"},"t:1144":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1144,"t":["அடுக்குத் தாமரை ஆதி இருப்பிடம்","எடுக்கும் தாமரை இல்லகத்து உள்ளது","மடுக்கும் தாமரை மத்தகச் தேசெல","முடுக்கும் தாமரை முச்சது ரத்தே."],"p":"","r":"The stacked lotuses are the seat of the Primal one: one rises within the house of the body, one goes up to the crown of the head, and one presses upward in the triple square.","n":"முச்சதுரம் (triple square) is traditionally the base centre, drawn as a square; மத்தகம் is the head. The four kinds of lotus (அடுக்கும், எடுக்கும், மடுக்கும், முடுக்கும்) are a sound pattern whose technical sense is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","kundalini","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:86"},"t:1145":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1145,"t":["முச்சது ரத்தே எழுந்த முளைச்சுடர்","எச்சது ரத்தும் இடம்பெற ஓடிடக்","கைச்சது ரத்துக் கடந்துள் ஒளிபெற","எச்சது ரத்தும் இருந்தனள் தானே."],"p":"","r":"The sprouting flame that rose in the triple square ran until it had a place in every square, and passing the square of the hand to gain the inner light, she stayed in all of them.","n":"முளைச்சுடர், the sprout-flame, rising from the base square is traditionally kundalini. கைச்சதுரம் (square of the hand) is obscure. சதுரம் closes every line, picking up முச்சதுரம் from 1144.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kundalini","yogam","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:86"},"t:1146":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1146,"t":["இருந்தனள் தன்முகம் ஆறொடு நாலாய்ப்","பரந்தன வாயு திசை தோறும்","குவிந்தன முத்தின் முகவொளி நோக்கி","நடந்தது தேறல் அதோமுகம் அம்பே."],"p":"","r":"She stayed with faces six and four; the breaths spread to every quarter, drew together to look at the pearl-bright face, and the nectar flowed down.","n":"ஆறொடு நாலாய் may be ten faces, one to each quarter, or six and four. தேறல் (honey, nectar) flowing அதோமுகம் (face downward) is traditionally the nectar from the crown. The final அம்பே may be 'arrow', 'water' or 'mother'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","praanan","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:86"},"t:1147":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1147,"t":["அம்பன்ன கண்ணி அரிவை மனோன்மணி","கொம்பன்ன நுண்ணிடை கோதை குலாவிய","செம்பொன்செய் யாக்கை செறிகமழ் நாடொறும்","நம்பனை நோக்கி நவிலுகின் றா\\ளே."],"p":"","r":"Manonmani, with eyes like arrows and a waist slender as a twig, garlanded, her body like worked red gold and fragrant, speaks every day with her eyes on the Lord.","n":"மனோன்மணி is a name of Shakti in Saiva tantra, the power beyond mind. நவிலுகின்றாள் is 'speaks' or 'recites'; what she says is not given. The praise of her beauty follows the conventions of love poetry.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["saththi","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:86"},"t:1148":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1148,"t":["நவிலும் பெருந்தெய்வம் நான்மறைச் சத்தி","துகிலுடை யாடை நிலம்பொதி பாதம்","அகிலமும் அண்ட முழுதும் செம்மாந்தும்","புகலும்முச் சோதி புனையநிற் பாளே."],"p":"","r":"The great goddess of whom the four Vedas speak, clothed in garments, her feet covering the ground, stands in majesty over the world and the whole cosmos, adorned with the three lights.","n":"முச்சோதி, the three lights, are traditionally sun, moon and fire. நான்மறைச் சத்தி may mean the Shakti of whom the Vedas speak or the Shakti who is the Vedas. The second line's sense is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","vedham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:86"},"t:1149":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1149,"t":["புனையவல் லாள் புவனத்துஇறை எங்கள்","வனையவல் லாள் அண்டகோடிகள் உள்ளே","புனையவல் லாள்மண் லடத்தொளி தன்னைப்","புனையவல் லாளையும் போற்றியென் பேனே."],"p":"","r":"I say praise to her who can adorn our Lord of the worlds, who can shape the countless cosmic eggs within, and who can adorn the light of the mandala.","n":"புனைதல் (to adorn, compose) and வனைதல் (to shape, as a potter shapes clay) carry the verse: she is both the Lord's ornament and the maker of worlds.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saththi","vazhipaadu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:86"},"t:1150":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1150,"t":["போற்றியென் பேன்புவ னாபதி அம்மையென்","ஆற்றலுள் நிற்கும் அருந்தவப் பெண்பிள்ளை","சீற்றங் கடிந்த திருநுதற் சேயிழை","கூற்றம் துரக்கின்ற கொள்பைந் தொடியே."],"p":"","r":"I hail the mother, mistress of the worlds, the young girl of rare penance who stands within my strength, the jewelled one of fair brow who put away anger, the one of fresh bangles who drives Death away.","n":"அருந்தவப் பெண்பிள்ளை recalls the goddess as a girl doing penance to win Siva. என் ஆற்றலுள் நிற்கும, standing within my strength, makes her the poet's own power. சீற்றங் கடிந்த can mean she has put away anger or that she quells it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saththi","thavam","kootru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:86"},"t:1151":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1151,"t":["தொடியார் தடக்கைச் சுகோதய சுந்தரி","வடிவார் திரிபுரை யாமங்கை கங்கைச்","செடியார் வினைகெடச் சேர்வரை என்றென்","அடியார் வினைகெடுத்து ஆதியும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"The bangled beauty from whom joy rises, the lady Tripura of fair form, Ganga, destroys the deeds of her devotees, thick as brushwood, and is also the Primal one.","n":"சுகோதய சுந்தரி and திரிபுரை are names of the goddess. செடியார் வினை may be deeds thick as a bush or sinful deeds (செடி, sin). The third line (சேர்வரை என்றென்) is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","vinai","adiyaar"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:86"},"t:1152":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1152,"t":["மெல்லிசைப் பாவை வியோமத்தின் மென்கொடி","பல்லிசைப் பாவை பயன்தரு பைங்கொடி","புல்லிசைப் பாவை யைப் போகத் துரந்திட்டு","வல்லிசைப் பாவை மனம்புகுந் தானே."],"p":"","r":"She of soft sound, a tender creeper of the sky, she of many sounds, a fruitful creeper, drove out the doll of mean sound and entered the mind as the doll of mighty sound.","n":"இசை is sound and also fame, and பாவை a doll or a woman. The four kinds of sound may be the levels of speech from subtle to gross, as tradition reads such lists; புல்லிசை may equally be ill repute driven out of the mind. புகுந்தானே has a masculine ending where புகுந்தாளே would be expected.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","naadham","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:86"},"t:1153":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1153,"t":["தாவித தவப்பொருள் தான்அவன் எம்இறை","பாவித்து உலகம் படைக்கின்ற காலத்து","மேவிப் பராசக்தி மேலொடு கீழ்தொடர்ந்து","ஆவிக்கும் அப்பொருள் தானது தானே."],"p":"","r":"Our Lord is himself the goal of penance; when he conceives and makes the world, Parashakti joins him and runs through it above and below, and that very one becomes the life of all.","n":"பாவித்து, conceiving or contemplating, makes creation an act of the Lord's thought, with Shakti as its extension. ஆவிக்கும் may be 'for the soul' or 'that gives life'. The opening word தாவித is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","iraivan","thavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:86"},"t:1154":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1154,"t":["அதுஇது என்பர் அவனை அறியார்","கதிவர நின்றதோர் காரணம் காணார்","மதுவிரி பூங்குழல் மாமங்கை நங்கை","திதமது உன்னார்கள் தேர்ந்துஅறி யாரே."],"p":"","r":"Not knowing him, people call him this or that; they do not see the cause that stands for release to come, and they neither consider the constancy of the great lady with flowered tresses nor examine and know.","n":"The chapter closes with a reproach: speaking of the Lord without knowing his Shakti is to miss the cause through which release (கதி) comes. திதம் is steadiness or standing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saththi","iraivan","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:86"},"t:1419":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1419,"t":["ஊரும் உலகமும் ஓக்கப் படைக்கின்ற","பேரறி வாளன் பெருமை குறித்திடின்","மேருவும் மூவுல காளி யிலங்கெழுந்","தாரணி நால்வகைச் சைவமு மாமே."],"p":"","r":"To consider the greatness of the all-knowing one who makes town and world alike is to see that Meru, the three worlds and the earth are his, and so are the four kinds of Saivism.","n":"நால்வகைச் சைவம், the four kinds of Saivism, are taken as the subjects of this chapter and the next three (pure, impure, path and severe pure). Line 3 (மூவுலகாளி யிலங்கெழுந்) is obscure, and how Meru and the worlds bear on the four kinds is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saivam","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:87"},"t:1420":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1420,"t":["சத்தும் அசத்துஞ் சதசத்துந் தான்கண்டு","சித்தும் அசித்துஞ் சேர்வுறா மேநீத்த","சுத்தம் அசுத்தமுந் தோய்வுறா மேநின்று","நித்தம் பரஞ்சுத்த சைவர்க்கு நேயமே."],"p":"","r":"Pure Saivas, having discerned the real, the unreal and what is both, keeping clear of the sentient and the insentient and untouched by pure and impure, hold the eternal Supreme dear.","n":"சத்து, அசத்து and சதசத்து are in Siddhanta usually the Lord (real), the bonds (unreal) and the soul (real-unreal), and சித்து, அசித்து the sentient and insentient; that is the tradition's mapping, which the verse does not spell out. The pure Saiva is defined by discernment and by not being touched, not by outward marks.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saivam","meyyunarvu","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:87"},"t:1421":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1421,"t":["கற்பன கற்றுக் கலைமன்னு மெய்யோகம்","முற்பத ஞான முறைமுறை நண்ணியே","சொற்பத மேவித் துரிசற்று மேலான","தற்பரங் கண்டுளோர் சைவசித் தாந்தரே."],"p":"","r":"Those who learn what is to be learned, go step by step through the true yoga in which the kalas abide and the knowledge of the earlier stages, reach the stage of the word and, rid of fault, see the Supreme beyond: they are the Saiva Siddhantins.","n":"முறைமுறை (in due order) makes the way a sequence: learning, yoga, knowledge, then the Supreme. முற்பத ஞானம் and சொற்பதம் are technical and can't be fixed here; சொற்பதம் may be the stage of the word or mantra, or the stage words can reach.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["siththaantham","kalvi","yogam","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:87"},"t:1422":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1422,"t":["வேதாந்தஞ் சுத்தம் விளங்கிய சித்தாந்த","நாதாந்தங் கண்டோர் நடுக்கற்ற காட்சியர்","புதாந்த போதாந்த மாதுப் புனஞ்செய்ய","நாதாந்த பூரணர் ஞானநே யத்தரே."],"p":"","r":"Those who have seen pure Vedanta, the clear Siddhanta and the end of nada have unwavering sight; those full in the end of nada, past the ends of the elements and of knowing, are lovers of knowledge.","n":"The verse is built on -அந்தம் (end): Vedanta, Siddhanta, Nadanta, Bhutanta, Bodhanta, traditionally a series of limits each passed in turn. Here Vedanta and Siddhanta stand together, not opposed. மாதுப் புனஞ்செய்ய is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["siththaantham","vedaantham","naadham","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:87"},"t:1423":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1423,"t":["இணையார் திருவடி ஏத்துஞ் சீரங்கத்","தினையார் இணைக்குழை யீரணை முத்திரை","குணமா ரிணைக்கண்ட மாலையுங் குன்றா","தணைவாஞ் சரியை கிரியையி னார்க்கே."],"p":"","r":"For those on the paths of service and rite it is fitting to praise the paired holy feet and, without lapse, to wear the signs: paired earrings, the two seals and the paired rudraksha garland at the neck.","n":"சரியை and கிரியை are the first two of the four Saiva paths (with yoga and jnana): temple service and ritual worship. The signs are outward, which is why this is called impure Saivism, meaning mixed with form rather than wrong. தினையார், ஈரணை முத்திரை and இணைக்கண்ட மாலை are taken by tradition as earrings, seals and a rudraksha garland; the exact objects are uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saivam","vazhipaadu","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:88"},"t:1424":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1424,"t":["காதுப்பொ னார்ந்த கடுக்கன் இரண்டுசேர்த்","தோதுந் திருமேனி யுட்கட் டிரண்டுடன்","சோதனை செய்து துவாதெச மார்க்கராய்","ஓதி யிருப்பார் ஒருசைவ ராகுமே."],"p":"","r":"One who wears two gold earrings, keeps the two inner bonds on the body, tests himself, follows the twelvefold way and keeps reciting is one kind of Saiva.","n":"துவாதச மார்க்கம் (the twelvefold way) may be the breath's reach of twelve finger-breadths beyond the head, or a twelve-syllable mantra; the verse doesn't say. உட்கட்டு இரண்டு (two inner bindings) is obscure. ஒருசைவர், 'one kind of Saiva', places such a person within Saivism without praise.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saivam","vazhipaadu","mandhiram"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:88"},"t:1425":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1425,"t":["கண்டங்கள் ஒன்பதுங் கண்டவர் கண்டனர்","கண்டங்கள் ஒன்பதுங் கண்டாய் அரும்பொருள்","கண்டங்கள் ஒன்பதுங் கண்டவர் கண்டமாங்","கண்டங்கள் கண்டோர் கடுஞ்சுத்த சைவரே."],"p":"","r":"Those who have seen the nine divisions have seen the rare thing, and those who see the divisions are the severely pure Saivas.","n":"கண்டம் is a division or region (the nine regions of the earth) and also the throat, and the verse puns on it and கண்ட (saw) in every line. What the nine divisions are, in the world or in the body, can't be fixed from the verse. It names the severe pure Saiva of chapter 90, not the impure one.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saivam","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:88"},"t:1426":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1426,"t":["ஞானி புவியெழு நன்னூ லனைத்துடன்","மோன திசையும் முழுஎண்ணெண் சித்தியும்","ஏனை நிலமும் எழுதா மறையீறுங்","கோனொடு தன்னையுங் காணுங் குணத்தனே."],"p":"","r":"The jnani is one who comes to see all the good books of the earth, the way of silence, all sixty-four attainments, the other planes, the end of the unwritten Veda, and himself together with the Lord.","n":"எழுதா மறை, the unwritten scripture, is the Veda kept by mouth, and its end (ஈறு) Vedanta. எண்ணெண் is sixty-four. The list climbs from books to silence to seeing oneself with the Lord (கோனொடு தன்னையும்), which is the last and highest item.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","siththi","vedham","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:88"},"t:1427":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1427,"t":["பொன்னாற் சிவசாத னம்பூதி சாதனம்","நன்மார்க்க சாதனம் மாஞான சாதனந்","துன்மார்க்க சாதனந் தோன்றாத சாதனஞ்","சன்மார்க்க சாதன மாஞ்சுத்த சைவர்க்கே."],"p":"","r":"For pure Saivas the means are Siva's emblem of gold, the sacred ash, the good path, great knowledge, the means by which the wrong path does not arise, and the true path.","n":"சாதனம் is both a means of practice and an emblem worn, and the verse may mean either or both. பூதி is sacred ash. சன்மார்க்கம், the true path, is in Siddhanta the path of knowledge. பொன்னாற் சிவசாதனம் may be a gold emblem or Siva's emblem made precious.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saivam","gnaanam","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:89"},"t:1428":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1428,"t":["கேடறு ஞானி கிளர்ஞான பூபதி","பாடறு வேதாந்த சித்தாந்த பாகத்தின்","ஊடுறு ஞானோ தயனுண்மை முத்தியோன்","பாடுறு சத்தசை வப்பத்த நித்தனே."],"p":"","r":"The faultless knower, lord of rising knowledge, in whom knowledge dawns within the joined portion of Vedanta and Siddhanta, who has true release, is the eternal devotee of pure Saivism.","n":"வேதாந்த சித்தாந்த, written as one compound, treats the two as a single body of teaching. சத்தசைவம் is read as சுத்த சைவம்; சத்த could also be 'true'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","saivam","siththaantham","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:89"},"t:1429":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1429,"t":["ஆகமம் ஒன்பான் அதிலான நாலேழு","மோகமில் நாலேழு முப்பேத முற்றுடன்","வேகமில் வேதாந்த சித்தாந்த மெய்மேயொன்","றாக முடிந்த வருஞ்சுத்த சைவமே."],"p":"","r":"The Agamas are nine, and within them the twenty-eight, free of delusion, in their three divisions; pure Saivism is the rare whole in which they and the calm truth of Vedanta and Siddhanta end as one.","n":"Twenty-eight (நாலேழு, four sevens) is the standard count of Saiva Agamas; the nine are those Tirumular names in his preface (63). The two நாலேழு and the three divisions (முப்பேதம்) are not clear, and the counting may be compressed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aagamam","saivam","vedaantham","siththaantham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:89"},"t:1430":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1430,"t":["சுத்தம் அசுத்தந் துரியங்கள் ஓரேழுஞ்","சத்தும் அசத்துந் தணந்த பராபரை","உய்த்த பராபரை யுள்ளாம் பராபரை","அத்தன் அருட்சத்தி யாய்எங்கு மாமே."],"p":"","r":"The Supreme power who stands apart from the pure and the impure, the seven turiya states, the real and the unreal, she who impels and she who is within, is the Father's power of grace and is everywhere.","n":"பராபரை is the feminine of பராபரன், the Supreme as Shakti; it is repeated three times with different qualifiers. துரியங்கள் ஓரேழு, seven turiyas, can't be fixed from the verse. அருட்சத்தி names grace as the Lord's own power.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","thiruvarul","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:89"},"t:1431":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1431,"t":["சத்தும் அசுத்துந் தணந்தவர் தானாகிச்","சித்தும் அசித்துந் தெரியாச் சிவோகமாய்","முத்தியுள் ஆனந்த சத்தியுள் மூழ்கினார்","சித்தியு மங்கே சிறந்துள தானே."],"p":"","r":"Those who have passed beyond the real and the unreal become that very one, in the state of 'I am Siva' where sentient and insentient are no longer told apart, and sink into the power of bliss in release; attainment too is at its height there.","n":"சிவோகம் (I am Siva) is a contemplative formula; Siddhanta tradition takes it as union in which the soul does not cease, a reading the verse alone does not settle. தானாகி may mean they become themselves or become Him.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","aanandham","veedu","siththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:89"},"t:1432":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1432,"t":["தன்னைப் பரனைச் சதாசிவன் என்கின்ற","மன்னைப் பதிபசு பாசத்தை மாசற்ற","முன்னைப் பழமல முன்கட்டை வீட்டினை","உன்னத் தகுஞ்சுத்த சைவர் உபாயமே."],"p":"","r":"What pure Saivas are to reflect on is the self, the Supreme called Sadasiva, the abiding triad of Lord, soul and bond, the ancient stainless-making impurity and the bondage from before, and release.","n":"The list is the syllabus of Siddhanta: self, Lord, the three categories, impurity, bondage and release. மாசற்ற may qualify what follows (the soul made stainless) or the whole list; பழமலம், the ancient impurity, is traditionally anava, beginningless as 115 says.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pathi","pasu","paasam","malam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:89"},"t:1433":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1433,"t":["பூரணம் தன்னிலே வைத்தற்ற வப்போதே","மாரண மந்த மதித்தானந் தத்தோடு","நேரென ஈராறு நீதி நெடும் போகங்","காரண மாஞ்சுத்த சைவர்க்குக் காட்சியே."],"p":"","r":"For pure Saivas the vision is this: when all is set in the fullness and cut away, death comes to its end, and with bliss there is the long enjoyment of the twelve.","n":"மாரணம் may be 'death' or 'great death'; ஈராறு (twelve) and நீதி are unexplained, and the second and third lines are obscure. Only the frame, a vision of fullness and bliss, is fairly sure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aanandham","saivam","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:89"},"t:1434":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1434,"t":["மாறாத ஞான மதிப்பற மாயோகந்","தேறாத சிந்தையைத் தேற்றிச் சிவமாக்கிப்","பேறான பாவனை பேணி நெறிநிற்றல்","கூறாகு ஞானி சரிதை குறிக்கிலே."],"p":"","r":"To hold to unchanging knowledge and great yoga without pride, settle the unsettled mind and make it Siva, and keep to the path by cherishing the contemplation one has gained: that, marked out, is the jnani's charya.","n":"சரிதை is சரியை, service, here named as the service proper to the jnani: tradition nests each of the four paths within the others. மதிப்பற may be 'without pride' or 'beyond measure'. பாவனை, contemplation, is what the jnani keeps up as his service.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","yogam","manam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:89"},"t:1435":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1435,"t":["வேதாந்தங் கண்டோர் பிரமமித் தியாதரர்","நாதாந்தங் கண்டோர் நடுக்கற்ற யோகிகள்","வேதாந்த மல்லாத சித்தாந்தங் கண்டுளோர்","சாதா ரணமன்ன சைவர் உபாயமே."],"p":"","r":"Those who have seen Vedanta hold that Brahman alone is real and the world false; those who have seen the end of nada are yogis who do not waver; and the Siddhanta that is not other than Vedanta is the way Saivas share.","n":"பிரமமித்தியாதரர் is read as those who hold Brahman (பிரமம்) and falsity (மித்யா), the Advaita thesis that the world is false; that division is the tradition's. வேதாந்தம் அல்லாத சித்தாந்தம் can mean a Siddhanta not other than Vedanta or one that is not Vedanta, and the two readings pull opposite ways. சாதாரணமன்ன is likewise 'common' or 'like the common'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vedaantham","siththaantham","yogam","saivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:89"},"t:1436":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1436,"t":["விண்ணினைச் சென்றணு காவியன் மேகங்கள்","கண்ணினைச் சென்றணு காப்பல காட்சிகள்","எண்ணினைச் சென்றணு காம லெணப்படும்","அண்ணலைச் சென்றணு காபசு பாசமே."],"p":"","r":"As clouds do not reach the sky and the many sights do not reach the eye, the Lord, who is thought of yet beyond the reach of thought, is not reached by soul and bond.","n":"The images make the relation one-way: the sky holds the clouds, the eye takes in the sights, and neither is reached by what it holds. The last line repeats 115's பதியினைச் சென்றணுகா பசு பாசம் with அண்ணல் for பதி.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pathi","pasu","paasam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:89"},"t:1437":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1437,"t":["ஒன்றும் இரண்டும் இலதுமாய் ஒன்றாக","நின்று சமய நிராகார நீங்கியே","நின்று பராபரை நேயத்தைப் பாதத்தாற்","சென்று சிவமாதல் சித்தாந்த சித்தியே."],"p":"","r":"To be neither one nor two nor nothing, yet one; to leave the sects and the formless; and to go by her feet to the love of the Supreme power and so become Siva: that is the attainment of Siddhanta.","n":"ஒன்றும் இரண்டும் இலதுமாய் is the Siddhanta formula of non-duality, neither simple oneness nor two, as tradition reads it. நிராகாரம் may be the formless as a doctrine held by some sect. பாதத்தால் may be by her feet or on foot.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["siththaantham","sivam","saththi","samayam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:89"},"t:1438":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1438,"t":["வேடம் கடந்து விகிர்தன்தன் பால்மேனி","ஆடம் பரமின்றி ஆசாபா சம்செற்றுப்","பாடொன்று பாசம் பசுத்துவம் பாழ்படச்","சாடும் சிவபோ தகர்சுத்த சைவரே."],"p":"","r":"Those who go beyond outward garb to Siva's side without show, crush the bonds of desire and lay waste bondage and the state of a bound soul, are the pure Saivas who know Siva.","n":"வேடம், garb, is the outward dress and signs of a sect, such as those of 1423 and 1424. பசுத்துவம் is the soul's state of being bound, not the soul itself. விகிர்தன், a name of Siva, is the one unlike all others. சிவபோதகர் may be those who know Siva or those who teach him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saivam","paasam","pasu","avaa"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:90"},"t:1439":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1439,"t":["உடலான ஐந்தையும் ஓராறும் ஐந்து","மடலான மாமாயை மற்றுள்ள நீவப்","படலான கேவல பாசந் துடைத்துத்","திடமாய்த் தனையுறல் சித்தாந்த மார்க்கமே."],"p":"","r":"To strip away the five that make the body, the six and the five, great maya in its layers and the rest, wipe away the bond of the isolated state and firmly reach oneself: that is the path of Siddhanta.","n":"ஐந்து, ஆறு and ஐந்து are traditionally groups of tattvas (elements, and the higher categories), stripped from the grossest upward. கேவலம் is in Siddhanta the soul's state wrapped in anava alone, before it takes a body; tradition supplies that sense. தனையுறல் may be reaching oneself or reaching Him.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["siththaantham","thaththuvam","maayai","paasam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:90"},"t:1440":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1440,"t":["சுத்தச் சிவனுரை தானத்தில் தோயாமல்","முத்தர் பதப்பொருள் முத்திவித் தாமூலம்","அத்தகை யான்மா அரனை அடைந்தற்றாற்","சுத்த சிவமாவ ரேசுத்த சைவரே."],"p":"","r":"Those who, without dwelling on words about pure Siva, reach the root that is the seed of release and the goal of the freed, and as souls so reach Hara that the separate self is gone, become pure Siva: they are the pure Saivas.","n":"அற்றால் (when it ceases) is read of the soul's separateness; tradition holds that the soul is not destroyed, and the verse does not say. Line 1 (சுத்தச் சிவனுரை தானத்தில் தோயாமல்) is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","veedu","saivam","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:90"},"t:1441":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1441,"t":["நானென்றும் தானென்றும் நாடிநான் சாரவே","தானென்று நானென் றிரண்டிலாத் தற்பதந்","தானென்று நானென்ற தத்துவ நல்கலால்","தானென்று நானென்றுஞ் சாற்றகில் லேனே."],"p":"","r":"Searching out what 'I' is and what 'He' is, I came to the state of That where there are not two, He and I; since that truth was granted, I can no longer speak of He and I.","n":"The verse is a report of experience in the first person. தான் is the Lord here, and தற்பதம் may be 'the That-state' (tat) or 'his own state'. நல்கலால் (because it was given) makes the knowledge a gift, not an achievement.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","meyyunarvu","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:90"},"t:1442":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1442,"t":["சாற்றரி தாகிய தத்துவஞ் சித்தித்தால்","ஆற்றரி தாகிய ஐந்தும் அடங்கிடும்","மேற்றிகழ் ஞானம் விளக்கொளி யாய்நிற்கும்","பாற்பர சாயுச்சிய மாகும் பதியே."],"p":"","r":"When the truth beyond speech is attained, the five that are hard to curb subside, knowledge shines above as the light of a lamp, and union with the Supreme becomes one's station.","n":"சாற்றரிதாகிய (hard to speak of) picks up சாற்றகில்லேன் from 1441. The five are traditionally the senses. சாயுச்சியம் is union, the last of the four stations (salokya, samipya, sarupya, sayujya); பதி may be 'station' or 'the Lord'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","adakkam","sivam","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:90"},"t:1443":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1443,"t":["நேர்ந்திடு மூல சரியை நெறியிதென்","றாய்ந்திடுங் காலாங்கி கஞ்ச மலையமான்","ஓர்ந்திடுங் கந்துரு கேண்மின்கள் பூதலத்","தோர்ந்திடுஞ் சுத்த சைவத் துயிரதே."],"p":"","r":"This is the way of the root chariyai, as Kalangi, Kanjamalaiyan and Kandhuru examined it; listen: on earth it is the life of pure Saivam.","n":"காலாங்கி, கஞ்சமலையமான் and கந்துரு are taken as disciples of Tirumular named in the lineage verses; the readings of the names, especially கந்துரு, are not secure. மூல சரியை may mean the root or first chariyai, or the chariyai of Mulan.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sariyai","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:91"},"t:1444":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1444,"t":["உயிர்க்குயி ராய்நிற்றல் ஒண்ஞான பூசை","உயிர்க்கொளி நோக்கல் மகாயோக பூசை","உயிர்ப்பெறு மாவா கனம்புறப் பூசை","செயிற்கடை நேசஞ் சிவபூசை யாமே."],"p":"","r":"Standing as the life of life is the bright jnana-puja; looking at the light in the life is the great yoga-puja; invoking the deity into a form is outer puja; love at the end of all fault is Siva-puja.","n":"ஆவாகனம் is the ritual invocation of the deity into an image, the work of kiriyai. செயிற்கடை நேசம் (love at the end of fault, or love free of fault) is obscure; it is usually taken as the devotee's pure love, the puja of chariyai. The verse ranks inner over outer worship without dismissing the outer.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","gnaanam","yogam","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:91"},"t:1445":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1445,"t":["நாடு நகரமும் நற்றிருக் கோயிலுந்","தேடித் திரிந்து சிவபெரு மான்என்று","பாடுமின் பாடிப் பணிமின் பணிந்தபின்","கூடிய நெஞ்சத்துக் கோயிலாய்க் கொள்வனே."],"p":"","r":"Seek through lands, towns and holy temples, and sing 'Siva Peruman'; having sung, bow; and when you have bowed, he will take your joined heart as his temple.","n":"The outward wandering of chariyai ends inward: the temple sought outside becomes the heart. கூடிய நெஞ்சம் may be the heart that has come together in devotion, or the heart joined to him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sariyai","vazhipaadu","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:91"},"t:1446":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1446,"t":["பத்தர் சரிதை படுவோர் கிரியையோர்","அத்தகு தொண்டர் அருள்வேடத் தாகுவோர்","சுத்த வியமாதி சாதகர் தூயோகர்","சித்தர் சிவஞானஞ் சென்றெய்து வோர்களே."],"p":"","r":"The devotees are those in chariyai; those of kiriyai are servants who take the garb of grace; the practisers of pure yama and the rest are pure yogis; the siddhas are those who go and reach Siva-jnana.","n":"The verse matches the four paths to four kinds of people: பத்தர், தொண்டர், யோகர், சித்தர். வியமாதி is read as இயமாதி (yama and the other limbs); அருள்வேடம் (the garb of grace) may be holy ash and rudraksha or the guise of devotees.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sariyai","kiriyai","yogam","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:91"},"t:1447":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1447,"t":["சார்ந்தமெய்ஞ் ஞானத்தோர் தானவ னாயினோர்","சேர்ந்தவெண் யோகத்தர் சித்தர் சமாதியோர்","ஆய்ந்த கிரியையோர் அருச்சனை தப்பாதோர்","நேர்ந்த சரியையோர் நீள்நிலத் தோரே."],"p":"","r":"Those of true jnana who have become Him, those of the eightfold yoga, the siddhas in samadhi, those of kiriyai who never fail in archana, and those of chariyai: all are of this wide earth.","n":"The list runs down from jnana to chariyai, the reverse of 1446. வெண் யோகம் is read as எண் யோகம், eight-limbed yoga. நீள்நிலத்தோர் (those of the wide earth) may honour all four as living on earth, or may mark them all as still within the world.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","yogam","kiriyai","sariyai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:91"},"t:1448":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1448,"t":["கிரியை யோகங்கள் கிளர்ஞான பூசை","அரிய சிவனுரு அமரும் அரூபந்","தெரியும் பருவத்துத் தேர்ந்திடும் பூசை","உரியன நேயத் துயர்பூசை யாமே."],"p":"","r":"Kiriyai, the yogas and the rising jnana-puja; the rare form of Siva and the formless in which he dwells; the puja chosen at the stage when one can discern: these are the high puja of love.","n":"Tradition matches kiriyai to worship of Siva in form (உரு), yoga to form-and-formless, and jnana to the formless (அரூபம்); the verse names form and formless but doesn't spell out the match. தெரியும் பருவம் (the stage of discerning) suggests worship fitted to one's ripeness.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","kiriyai","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:91"},"t:1449":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1449,"t":["சரியாதி நான்குந் தருஞான நான்கும்","விரிவான வேதாந்த சித்தாந்த மாறும்","பொருளா னதுநந்தி பொன்னகர் போந்து","மருளாகு மாந்தர் வணங்கவைத் தானே."],"p":"","r":"The four paths beginning with chariyai, the four jnanas they give, and the six of wide Vedanta and Siddhanta: Nandi, who is their meaning, came to the golden city and set them for deluded men to worship.","n":"தருஞான நான்கும் is taken as the four fruits of the paths, or the fourfold division of each path. ஆறும் (the six) is unclear: six schools, or six divisions of Vedanta-Siddhanta. பொன்னகர் (the golden city) may be Tillai, Kailasa or the world. Nandi is both teacher and the meaning taught.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sariyai","gnaanam","vedaantham","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:91"},"t:1450":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1450,"t":["சமையம் பலசுத்தித் தன்செயல் அற்றிடும்","அமையும் விசேடமும் அரன்மந் திரசுத்தி","சமைய நிருவாணங் கலாசுத்தி யாகும்","அமைமன்னு ஞானமார்க் கம்அபிடேகமே."],"p":"","r":"Samaya initiation is purity of many kinds in which one's own action ceases; visesha is purification by Hara's mantra; nirvana is purification of the kalas; abhisheka is the path of settled jnana.","n":"சமயம், விசேடம், நிருவாணம் and அபிடேகம் are the four grades of Saiva initiation (தீட்சை) in the Agamas, though the verse does not use the word தீட்சை. Here சமயம் is the first initiation, not a sect. கலாசுத்தி, the purifying of the kalas, is a technical rite.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","mandhiram","kalai","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:91"},"t:1451":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1451,"t":["பத்துத் திசையும் பரமொரு தெய்வமுண்","டெத்திக் கிலரில்லை என்பதின் அமலர்க்","கொத்துத் திருவடி நீழல் சரணெனத்","தத்தும் வினைக்கடல் சாராது காணுமே."],"p":"","r":"In all ten directions there is one supreme God, and there is no direction where he is not; take refuge in the shade of the Pure One's holy feet, clustered like flowers, and the leaping sea of deeds will not reach you.","n":"கொத்து (a cluster, of flowers) pictures the feet as a bunch of blossoms; the shade (நீழல்) is the refuge. தத்தும் (leaping, surging) makes வினை a sea with waves. எத்திக்கு இலர் இல்லை may also mean 'there is no one in any direction who is without him'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","vinai","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:92"},"t:1452":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1452,"t":["கானுறு கோடி கடிகமழ் சந்தனம்","வானுறு மாமல ரிட்டு வணங்கினும்","ஊனினை நீக்கி உண்பவர்க் கல்லது","தேனமர் பூங்கழல் சேரவொண் ணாதே."],"p":"","r":"Even if one worships with crores of fragrant forest sandal and great flowers that reach the sky, none but those who eat having put away flesh can reach his flower-anklets where the honey sits.","n":"The verse sets the scale of offering against one abstention. ஊனினை நீக்கி may also be taken as 'removing the body', but in a chapter on worship and with உண்பவர் (those who eat) it reads most naturally as giving up meat.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pulaal","vazhipaadu","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:92"},"t:1453":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1453,"t":["கோனக்கன் றாயே குரைகழல் ஏத்துமின்","ஞானக்கன் றாகிய நடுவே யுழிதரும்","வானக்கன் றாகிய வானவர் கைதொழு","மானக்கன் றீசன் அருள்வள்ள மாமே."],"p":"","r":"Praise his sounding anklets as the king's calf; the Lord, calf of jnana who roams in the middle, calf of the sky whom the gods worship with joined hands, and holder of the deer's calf, is a vessel of grace.","n":"Each line turns on கன்று (calf, young one): கோனக்கன்று, ஞானக்கன்று, வானக்கன்று, மானக்கன்று. Only the last is clear, the deer Siva holds in his hand. Whether the worshipper or the Lord is the calf in line 1, and what நடுவே உழிதரும் (roams in the middle) refers to, I can't fix. வள்ளம் may be a vessel, a boat or a measure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","sivam","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:92"},"t:1454":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1454,"t":["இதுபணிந் தெண்டிசை மண்டிலம் எல்லாம்","அதுபணி செய்கின் றவளொரு கூறன்","இதுபணி மானுடர் செய்பணி யீசன்","பதிபணி செய்வது பத்திமை காணே."],"p":"","r":"Bow to this: she who serves all the regions of the eight directions is a part of him; the service humans do is service to the Lord; serving the Lord is devotion.","n":"அவள் ஒரு கூறன் is the Lord who has her (Shakti) as half his body. The words இது, அது and பணி are repeated so densely that the grammar is unclear; பதிபணி may be service to the Lord (பதி) or service at his shrine.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","saththi","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:92"},"t:1455":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1455,"t":["பத்தன் கிரியை சரியை பயில்வுற்றுச்","சுத்த அருளால் துரிசற்ற யோகத்தில்","உய்த்த நெறியுற் றுணர்கின்ற ஞானத்தாற்","சித்தங் குருவரு ளாற்சிவ மாகுமே."],"p":"","r":"The devotee, practising kiriyai and chariyai, moves by pure grace into flawless yoga, and through the jnana realised on the path it leads to, by the guru's grace, the mind becomes Siva.","n":"The verse gives the whole ladder in one sentence: chariyai and kiriyai, then yoga, then jnana, then Sivam. Each step is credited to grace (சுத்த அருள், குருவருள்), not only to practice. It names kiriyai before chariyai, perhaps for metre.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kiriyai","yogam","gnaanam","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:92"},"t:1456":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1456,"t":["அன்பின் உருகுவ நாளும் பணிசெய்வன்","செம்பொன்செய் மேனி கமலத் திருவடி","முன்புநின் றாங்கே மொழிவ தெனக்கருள்","என்பினுட் சோதி இலங்குகின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"Melting in love, I serve him every day; standing before his body of red gold and his lotus feet, I ask him to grant me words to speak there, and he shines as a light within my bones.","n":"The grammar of lines 2 and 3 is loose: மொழிவது எனக்கருள் may be 'grant me what to say' or 'grant me grace, I say'. என்பினுள் சோதி, the light within the bone, answers the image of bones melting in love (compare 272).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anbu","vazhipaadu","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:92"},"t:1457":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1457,"t":["நெறிவழி யேசென்று நேர்மையுள் ஒன்றித்","தறியிருந் தாற்போல் தம்மை யிருத்திச்","சொறியினுந் தாக்கினுந் துண்ணென் றுணராக்","குறியறி வாளர்க்குக் கூடலு மாமே."],"p":"","r":"Going by the path and becoming one within rectitude, setting themselves like a planted post, for those who know the sign and do not start whether scratched or struck, union becomes possible.","n":"தறி இருந்தாற்போல் (like a post standing) is the image of the motionless seated yogi; not flinching at scratch or blow marks withdrawal from the senses. குறி (the sign, or the goal) is a recurring yogic term in Tirumular whose exact referent the verse leaves open.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yogam","samaadhi","adakkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:93"},"t:1458":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1458,"t":["ஊழிதோ றூழிஉணர்ந்தவர்க் கல்லால்","ஊழிதோ றூழி உணரவுந் தானொட்டான்","ஆழி அமரும் அரியயன் என்றுளார்","ஊழி முயன்றும் ஒருச்சியு ளானே."],"p":"","r":"He lets himself be perceived age after age only by those who perceive him; Hari who rests on the ocean and Brahma strove for ages, and he is in the crown.","n":"ஒருச்சி is read as உச்சி, the crown of the head, where yoga places the meeting with Siva; it may also be the summit beyond the gods' reach. The verse recalls the story of Hari and Brahma seeking his foot and crown (chapter 41).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","yogam","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:93"},"t:1459":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1459,"t":["பூவினிற் கந்தம் பொருந்திய வாறுபோற்","சீவனுக் குள்ளே சிவமணம் பூத்தது","ஓவியம் போல உணர்ந்தறி வாளர்க்கு","நாவி யணைந்த நடுதறி யாமே."],"p":"","r":"As scent dwells in a flower, the fragrance of Siva has blossomed within the soul; for the knowers who perceive it steady as a painting, it is the central post joined with musk.","n":"The first two lines are plain: Siva in the soul as scent in the flower. ஓவியம் போல (like a painting) may describe the still yogi or the stable vision. நாவி அணைந்த நடுதறி (the planted middle post joined with musk) is taken by tradition as the central channel, sushumna; that is symbolic and uncertain. Sengalvaraya notes a parallel in Tiruppugazh 239 on the soul as Siva's form.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","uyir","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:93"},"t:1460":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1460,"t":["உய்ந்தனம் என்பீர் உறுபொருள் காண்கிலீர்","கந்த மலரிற் கலக்கின்ற நந்தியைச்","சிந்தை யுறவே தெளிந்திருள் நீங்கினால்","முந்தைப் பிறவிக்கு மூலவித் தாமே."],"p":"","r":"You say 'we are saved' but do not see the thing to be reached; if you see clearly in your mind Nandi who mingles in the fragrant flower, and darkness leaves, he is the root seed of your former births.","n":"The flower and scent carry on from 1459. The last line can mean the Lord was always the root seed of one's births, or that clear seeing destroys the seed of birth; the verse doesn't say which.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","meyyunarvu","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:93"},"t:1461":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1461,"t":["எழுத்தோடு பாடலும் எண்ணெண் கலையும்","பழித்தலைப் பாசப் பிறவியும் நீங்கா","வழித்தலைச் சோமனோ டங்கி யருக்கன்","வழித்தலைச் செய்யும் வகையுணர்ந் தேனே."],"p":"","r":"Letters, songs and the sixty-four arts do not remove birth bound in fetters with blame at its head; I have come to know the way of sending the moon, fire and sun up the path.","n":"The plain point is that learning (எழுத்து, பாடல், எண்ணெண் கலை) does not end the bonds. சோமன், அங்கி, அருக்கன் (moon, fire, sun) are traditionally the three channels or the three zones of the yogic body, and வழித்தலை செய்யும் (turning them up the path) a yogic method; that reading is tradition's.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","paasam","piravi","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:93"},"t:1462":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1462,"t":["விரும்பிநின் றேசெயில் மேய்த்தவ ராகும்","விரும்பிநின் றேசெயின் மெய்யுரை யாகும்","விரும்பிநின் றேசெயின் மெய்த்தவ மாகும்","விரும்பிநின் றேசெயில் விண்ணவ னாகுமே."],"p":"","r":"If done standing in longing, one becomes a true ascetic; done in longing, it becomes true speech; done in longing, true tapas; done in longing, one becomes a dweller of heaven.","n":"The verse does not name what is done; under the heading it is yoga. மேய்த்தவர் is probably மெய்த்தவர் (true ascetics). The fourfold repetition of விரும்பி நின்றே செயின் puts the weight on desire held steadily, not on the practice.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","thavam","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:93"},"t:1463":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1463,"t":["பேணிற் பிறவா உலகருள் செய்திடுங்","காணில் தனது கலவியு ளேநிற்கும்","நாணில் நகர நெறிக்கே வழிசெயும்","ஊனிற் சுடுமங்கி யுத்தமன் றானே."],"p":"","r":"If cherished, he grants the world of no birth; if seen, he stands within his own union; if without shame, he opens the way to the path of hell: the excellent one is the fire that burns in the flesh.","n":"ஊனில் சுடும் அங்கி (the fire that burns in the flesh) is traditionally the inner fire of yoga, and the verse calls it உத்தமன், the Lord. நாணில் may be 'if shamed' or 'without shame', and நகர நெறி may be the path to hell (நரகம்) or to the city (of Siva). So the third line either warns or promises; I have taken it as a warning, with the plain contrast of a fire that frees or harms.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","veedu","naragam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:93"},"t:1464":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1464,"t":["ஒத்தசெங் கோலார் உலப்பிலி மாதவர்","எத்தனை யாயிரம் வீழ்ந்தனர் எண்ணிலி","சித்தர்கள் தேவர்கள் மூவர் பெருமையாய்","அத்த னிவனென்றே அன்புறு வார்களே."],"p":"","r":"Kings of just sceptre and endless great ascetics, countless thousands, have fallen; the siddhas, the gods and the three in their greatness come in love, saying 'this one is the Father'.","n":"The two halves are not joined by any word. They may contrast the many who fell, for all their rule and austerity, with those who reached him by love; or say that even siddhas and gods only love him. மூவர் (the three) is usually Brahma, Vishnu and Rudra.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","sivam","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:93"},"t:1465":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1465,"t":["யோகிக்கு யோகாதி மூன்றுள கொண்டுற்றோர்","ஆகத் தருகிரி யாதி சரியையாந்","தாகத்தை விட்ட சரியையொன் றாம்ஒன்றுள்","ஆதித்தன் பத்தியுள் அன்புவைத் தேனே."],"p":"","r":"For the yogi there are three, yoga and the rest, taken up; kiriyai and the rest belong to the body as chariyai; chariyai that has left thirst is one; within that one I placed my love in devotion to the Sun.","n":"The verse seems to subdivide yoga by the four paths (chariyai within yoga and so on), as 1474 does for jnana, but the lines are too compressed to fix. ஆதித்தன் (the Sun) may be the Lord as light, or the sun of the yogic body. Reading tentative throughout.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","kiriyai","sariyai","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:93"},"t:1466":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1466,"t":["யோகச் சமயமே யோகம் பலவுன்னல்","யோக விசேடமே அட்டாங்க யோகமாம்","யோகநிர் வாணமே யுற்ற பரோதயம்","யோக அபிடேகமே ஒண்சித்தி யுற்றலே."],"p":"","r":"Yoga-samaya is contemplating the many yogas; yoga-visesha is eight-limbed yoga; yoga-nirvana is the rising of the Supreme that is reached; yoga-abhisheka is gaining bright siddhi.","n":"The four stages repeat the four initiations of 1450 (samaya, visesha, nirvana, abhisheka) as grades within yoga. பரோதயம் (the rising of the Supreme) is technical. The verse makes siddhi the crown of yoga, where 1476 makes the guru's feet the crown of jnana.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","siththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:93"},"t:1467":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1467,"t":["ஞானத்தின் மிக்க அறநெறி நாட்டில்லை","ஞானத்தின் மிக்க சமயமும் நன்றன்று","ஞானத்தின் மிக்கவை நன்முத்தி நல்காவா","ஞானத்தின் மிக்கார் நரரின்மிக் காரே."],"p":"","r":"There is no path of dharma in the land higher than jnana, no religion beyond jnana is good, what goes beyond jnana does not give good release, and those who excel in jnana excel among men.","n":"ஞானத்தின் மிக்க opens every line, but its sense shifts: in the first three it is 'higher than jnana', in the last 'abounding in jnana'. The verse ranks dharma and religion below jnana without rejecting them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","aram","samayam","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:94"},"t:1468":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1468,"t":["சத்தமுஞ் சத்த மனனும் தகுமனம்","உய்த்த வுணர்வு முணர்த்தும் அகந்தையுஞ்","சித்தமென் றிம்மூன்றுஞ் சிந்திக்குஞ் செய்கையுஞ்","சத்தங் கடந்தவர் பெற்றசன் மார்க்கமே."],"p":"","r":"Sound and reflection on sound, the fitting mind, directed awareness, the ego that makes aware, and these three with the chitta, and the act of thinking itself: going beyond sound, those who pass them gain the true path.","n":"The list looks like the inner instruments (manas, buddhi, ahankara, chitta), with சத்தம் and சத்த மனனம் perhaps standing for hearing and reflecting on teaching (sravana, manana). How the items group into 'these three' is unclear. சன்மார்க்கம் here is the path of jnana, the subject of the next chapter.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","manam","thaththuvam","sanmaarkkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:94"},"t:1469":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1469,"t":["தன்பால் உலகுந் தனக்கரு காவதும்","அன்பா லெனக்கரு ளாவது மாவன","என்பார்கள் ஞானமும் எய்துஞ் சிவோகமும்","பின்பாலின் நேயமும் பெற்றிடுந் தானே."],"p":"","r":"That the world is in him and what is near him, and what becomes grace to me through love: those who can say these gain jnana, reach Sivoham, and receive the love that follows.","n":"சிவோகம் (I am Siva) is a contemplative formula of identity. The first two lines are compressed and the subject of தன்பால் (in himself) may be the Lord or the knower. The verse has love both before jnana (அன்பால்) and after it (பின்பாலின் நேயம்).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","thiruvarul","anbu","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:94"},"t:1470":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1470,"t":["இருக்குஞ் சேம இடம்பிரமமாகும்","வருக்கஞ் சராசர மாகும் உலகந்","தருக்கிய ஆசார மெல்லாந் தருமே","திருக்கிலாஞானத்தைத் தேர்ந்துணர்ந் தோர்க்கே."],"p":"","r":"For those who have examined and understood the jnana without crookedness, the safe place to dwell is Brahman, all classes of being make up the moving and unmoving world, and it gives the fruit of all the proud observances.","n":"திருக்கிலா ஞானம் is jnana without twist or crookedness. தருக்கிய ஆசாரம் may be observances that people take pride in, or observances that are reasoned out; the claim is that jnana yields what they aim at. The grammar of the first two lines is loose.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:94"},"t:1471":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1471,"t":["அறிவும் அடக்கமும் அன்பும் உடனே","பிறியா நகர்மன்னும் பேரரு ளாளன்","குறியுங் குணமுங் குரைகழல் நீங்கா","நெறியறி வார்க்கிது நீர்த்தொனி யாமே."],"p":"","r":"The greatly gracious one dwells in the city that knowledge, restraint and love never leave; his sign, his qualities and his sounding feet do not leave it; for those who know the way, this is plain as the sound of water.","n":"The 'city' (நகர்) is probably the knower's own heart or body. நீர்த்தொனி (the sound of water) is obscure; it may mean something clearly heard, or something heard in still water. The pairing of knowledge, restraint and love is the verse's clear teaching.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arivu","adakkam","anbu","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:94"},"t:1472":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1472,"t":["ஞானம் விளைந்தெழு கின்றதோர் சிந்தையுள்","ஏனம் விளைந்தெதி ரேகாண வழிதோறுங்","கூனல் மதிமண் டலத்தெதிர் நீர்கண்டு","ஊனம் அறுத்துநின் றொண்சுட ராகுமே."],"p":"","r":"In a mind where jnana ripens and rises, a boar springs up to be seen ahead at every turn; seeing the water facing the curved moon's circle, cutting off all defect, one stands as a bright flame.","n":"ஏனம் (boar) may recall Vishnu as the boar who dug for Siva's foot, or ஏனம் may mean a vessel; கூனல் மதி மண்டலம் (the circle of the bent moon) and the water before it are traditionally the moon-region in the head and its nectar. The ending is plain: defect cut away, the knower becomes light. The rest is symbolic and I can't fix it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","yogam","malam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:94"},"t:1473":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1473,"t":["ஞானிக் குடன்குண ஞானத்தில் நான்குமா","மோனிக் கிவையொன்றுங் கூடாமுன் மோகித்து","மேனிற்ற லாஞ்சத்தி வித்தை விளைத்திடுந்","தானிக் குலத்தோர் சரியை கிரியையே."],"p":"","r":"For the jnani the four are in jnana with its qualities; for the silent one none of these join; before that, deluded, Shakti stands above and makes knowledge grow; those of this kind are of chariyai and kiriyai.","n":"The verse seems to grade the jnani, for whom the four paths are within jnana, above the silent one (மோனி) who is past all of them, and below both those still in chariyai and kiriyai. The middle lines (மோகித்து மேல் நிற்றல் ஆம் சத்தி வித்தை) are obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","saththi","sariyai","kiriyai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:94"},"t:1474":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1474,"t":["ஞானத்தின் ஞானாதி நான்குமா ஞானிக்கு","ஞானத்தின் ஞானமே நானென தென்னாமல்","ஞானத்தில் யோகமே நாதாந்த நல்லொளி","ஞானக் கிரியையே நன்முத்தி நாடலே."],"p":"","r":"For the jnani the four beginning with jnana are within jnana: jnana in jnana is not saying 'I' and 'mine'; yoga in jnana is the good light at the end of nada; kiriyai in jnana is seeking good release.","n":"Each path is said to contain all four (jnana in jnana, yoga in jnana and so on); the verse gives three of the four within jnana and leaves out chariyai. நாதாந்தம் (the end of nada) is technical. The clearest line is the first: jnana's own core is the end of 'I' and 'mine'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","yogam","naadham","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:94"},"t:1475":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1475,"t":["நண்ணிய ஞானத்தின் ஞானாதி நண்ணுவோன்","புண்ணிய பாவங் கடந்த பிணக்கற்றோன்","கண்ணிய நேயங் கரைஞானங் கண்டுளோன்","திண்ணிய சுத்தன் சிவமுத்தன் சித்தனே."],"p":"","r":"He who reaches jnana-in-jnana has passed beyond merit and sin, is free of strife, has seen the beloved jnana of the far shore, and is the firm, pure, Siva-freed siddha.","n":"புண்ணிய பாவம் கடந்த says the knower is beyond both good and bad deeds, not only bad ones. கரைஞானம் (shore-jnana) may be the knowledge of the far shore or the knowledge at the limit; கண்ணிய நேயம் (the cherished, or the intended, object of love) is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","vinai","veedu","siththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:94"},"t:1476":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1476,"t":["ஞானச் சமயமே நாடுந் தனைக் காண்டல்","ஞான விசேடமே நாடு பரோதய","ஞானநிர் வாணமே நன்றறி வானருள்","ஞானாபி டேகமே நற்குறு பாதமே."],"p":"","r":"Jnana-samaya is seeing the self one seeks; jnana-visesha is the rising of the Supreme one seeks; jnana-nirvana is the grace of the one who knows the good; jnana-abhisheka is the good guru's feet.","n":"நற்குறு பாதம் is read as நற்குரு பாதம், the good guru's feet. The sequence runs from seeing the self, to the Supreme rising, to grace, to the guru's feet: the summit of jnana is placed at the guru.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","guru","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:94"},"t:1477":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1477,"t":["சாற்றுஞ்சன் மார்க்கமாந் தற்சிவ தத்துவத்","தோற்றங் களான சுருதிச் சுடர்கண்டு","சீற்றம் ஒழிந்து சிவயோக சித்தராய்க்","கூற்றத்தை வென்றார் குறிப்பறிந் தார்களே."],"p":"","r":"Those who saw the flame of scripture that is the manifestation of the Siva-tattva, the declared sanmarga, lost their anger, became Siva-yoga siddhas, conquered death and knew the sign.","n":"சுருதிச் சுடர் (the flame of the shruti) may be the light that scripture reveals or scripture itself as light. Losing anger (சீற்றம் ஒழிந்து) is set beside conquering death, so moral and yogic attainments stand together. குறிப்பு (the sign) is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sanmaarkkam","vekuli","siththi","kootru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:95"},"t:1478":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1478,"t":["சைவப் பெருமைத் தனிநா யகன்நந்தி","உய்ய வகுத்த குருநெறி ஒன்றுண்டு","தெய்வச் சிவநெறி நன்மார்க்கஞ் சேர்ந்துய்ய","வையத்துள் ளார்க்கு வகுத்தவைத் தானே."],"p":"","r":"Nandi, the sole leader of Saiva greatness, has laid down one guru-path for salvation; he laid it down so that those in the world may join the divine Siva-way, the good path, and be saved.","n":"நன்மார்க்கம் (the good path) glosses சன்மார்க்கம். The verse makes the true path a teacher's path (குருநெறி), handed down, not found alone.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["guru","sanmaarkkam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:95"},"t:1479":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1479,"t":["தெரிசிக்கப் பூசிக்கச் சிந்தனை செய்யப்","பரிசிக்கக் கீர்த்திக்கப் பாதுகஞ் சூடக்","குருபத்தி செய்யுங் குவலயத் தோர்க்குத்","தருமுத்திச் சார்பூட்டுஞ் சன்மார்க்கந் தானே."],"p":"","r":"Seeing, worshipping and contemplating the guru, touching and praising him, wearing his sandals on the head: for those on earth who serve the guru so, sanmarga is what brings them to release.","n":"The six acts (தெரிசித்தல், பூசித்தல், சிந்தித்தல், பரிசித்தல், கீர்த்தித்தல், பாதுகம் சூடல்) are all directed to the guru as to the Lord. The verse ties the path of knowledge to devotion to the person of the teacher.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["guru","sanmaarkkam","vazhipaadu","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:95"},"t:1480":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1480,"t":["தெளிவறி யாதார் சிவனை யறியார்","தெளிவறி யாதார் சீவனு மாகார்","தெளிவறி யாதார் சிவமாக மாட்டார்","தெளிவறி யாதவர் தீரார் பிறப்பே."],"p":"","r":"Those who do not know clarity do not know Siva, do not even become the soul, cannot become Siva, and do not end their births.","n":"தெளிவு here is spiritual clarity, settled knowledge of what is real. சீவனும் ஆகார் (they do not become even the soul) is striking: without clarity one does not truly know oneself as soul, let alone as Siva. The four lines climb from knowing Siva to ending birth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["meyyunarvu","thelivu","sivam","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:95"},"t:1481":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1481,"t":["தானவ னாகித் தானைந்தா மலஞ்செற்று","மோனம தாம்மொழிப் பான்முத்த ராவது","மீனமில் ஞானானு பூதியில் இன்பமுந்","தாவை னாயுறலானசன் மார்க்கமே."],"p":"","r":"Becoming Him, destroying the five impurities, being freed by a speech that is silence, the bliss in flawless jnana-experience, and coming to be Him oneself: this is sanmarga.","n":"ஐந்து மலம் (five impurities) is an extended count of the usual three (anava, kanma, maya), adding maya's products and the concealing power. மோனமதாம் மொழி (speech that is silence) is the guru's silent teaching or the knower's silence. Line 4 is corrupt as printed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sanmaarkkam","malam","aanandham","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:95"},"t:1482":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1482,"t":["சன்மார்க்கத் தார்க்கு முகத்தொடு பீடமுஞ்","சன்மார்க்கத் தார்க்கும் இடத்தொடு தெய்வமுஞ்","சன்மார்க்கத் தார்க்கு வருக்கந் தரிசனம்","எம்மார்க்கத் தார்க்கும் இயம்புவன் கேண்மினோ."],"p":"","r":"For those of sanmarga there are a face and a seat, a place and a deity, a class and a vision; I shall tell those of every path: listen.","n":"The verse announces items (முகம், பீடம், இடம், தெய்வம், வருக்கம், தரிசனம்) without explaining them; they may be the marks of each path, given in verses not here. Read alone, it is only a preface.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sanmaarkkam","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:95"},"t:1483":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1483,"t":["சன்மார்க்க சாதனந் தான்ஞான ஞேயமாம்","பின்மார்க்க சாதனம் பேதையர்க்காய்நிற்கும்","துன்மார்க்கம் விட்ட துரியத் துரிசற்றார்","சன்மார்க்கந் தானவ னாகுஞ்சன் மார்க்கமே."],"p":"","r":"The means of sanmarga is knowledge and the known; the means of the later paths stands for the simple; those who have left the evil path and are flawless in the fourth state become Him: that is sanmarga.","n":"ஞான ஞேயம் is knowledge and its object (jnana, jneya). பின்மார்க்கம் (the later or lower paths) are chariyai, kiriyai and yoga, said to suit பேதையர், the simple; it is a ranking, not a dismissal. துரியம் is the fourth state beyond waking, dream and sleep.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sanmaarkkam","gnaanam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:95"},"t:1484":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1484,"t":["சன்மார்க்க மெய்த வருமருஞ் சீடர்க்குப்","பின்மார்க்க மூன்றும் பெறவியல் பாமென்றால்","நன்மார்க்கந் தானே சிவனொடு நாடலே","சொன்மார்க்க மென்னச்சுருதிகைக்கொள்ளுமே."],"p":"","r":"If the three lower paths come naturally to the rare disciples who reach sanmarga, then the good path itself is seeking with Siva; the scriptures accept it as the path of the word.","n":"The verse seems to say the lower three are taken up into sanmarga for the ripe disciple, but the conditional (என்றால்) makes the logic hard to fix. சொன்மார்க்கம் may be the path of the word (scripture) or a pun on சன்மார்க்கம்.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sanmaarkkam","guru","vedham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:95"},"t:1485":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1485,"t":["அன்னிய பாசமும் ஆகுங் கருமமும்","முன்னும் அவத்தையும் மூலப் பகுதியும்","பின்னிய ஞானமும் பேதாதி பேதமுந்","தன்னொடுங் கண்டவர் சன்மார்க்கத் தோரே."],"p":"","r":"Those who have seen, with themselves, the alien bond, the karma that comes, the states that follow, the root prakriti, the entwined knowledge, and difference upon difference, are of sanmarga.","n":"அன்னிய பாசம் (the bond that is other, alien to the soul), அவத்தை (the soul's states), மூலப் பகுதி (root nature, mula-prakriti) are technical terms. தன்னொடும் கண்டவர் (who saw them along with themselves) suggests knowing the self and its bonds together, as distinct.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sanmaarkkam","paasam","vinai","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:95"},"t:1486":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1486,"t":["பசுபாச நீக்கிப் பதியுடன் கூட்டிப்","கசியாத நெஞ்சங் கசியக் கசிவித்","தொசியாத வுண்மைச் சொரூபோ தயத்துற்","றசைவான தில்லாமை யானசன் மார்க்கமே."],"p":"","r":"Removing pasu and pasam and joining the soul with Pati, making the unmelting heart melt, reaching the rising of the true form that does not bend, and the stillness in which there is no moving: that is sanmarga.","n":"The three Siddhanta categories appear together: removing the soul's bound state (பசு) and its bonds (பாசம்), and joining it to the Lord (பதி). கசியாத நெஞ்சம் கசிய puts melting love at the centre of the path of knowledge. அசைவு இல்லாமை is stillness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sanmaarkkam","pathi","pasu","paasam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:95"},"t:1487":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1487,"t":["மார்க்கஞ்சன் மார்க்கிகள் கிட்ட வகுப்பது","மார்க்கஞ்சன் மார்க்கமே யன்றிமற் றொன்றில்லை","மார்க்கஞ்சன் மார்க்க மெனுநெறி வைகாதோர்","மார்க்கஞ்சன் மார்க்க மாஞ்சித்த யோகமே."],"p":"","r":"The path the sanmargis lay down for reaching is sanmarga and nothing else; for those who do not rest in the path called sanmarga, the path is sanmarga as siddha-yoga.","n":"Every line opens with மார்க்கம் சன்மார்க்க and the sense turns on how the repeated words divide. The last line may mean that siddha-yoga is the sanmarga for those not yet settled in it, or that sanmarga itself is siddha-yoga; the verse leads into the next chapter on yoga.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sanmaarkkam","yogam","siththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:95"},"t:1488":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1488,"t":["சன்மார்க்கந் தானே சகமார்க்க மானது","மனமார்க்க மாமுத்தி சித்திக்குள் வைப்பதாம்","பின்மார்க்க மானது பேராப் பிறந்திறந்","துன்மார்க்க ஞானத் துறதியு மாமே."],"p":"","r":"Sanmarga itself is what became sahamarga; it is the mind-path that places release within attainment; the later path is unending birth and death and the ruin of wrong-path knowledge.","n":"சகமார்க்கம் (the companion path) is in Saiva tradition the yoga path, standing with the Lord as a companion; the verse doesn't define it but joins it to sanmarga. பின்மார்க்கம் here seems to mean a fallen path, unlike 1483 where it is the lower paths. துறதி is obscure, perhaps துர்கதி (an evil end).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","sanmaarkkam","siththi","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:96"},"t:1489":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1489,"t":["மருவுந் துவாதச மார்க்கமில் லாதார்","குருவுஞ் சிவனுஞ் சமயமுங் கூடாம்","வெருவுந் திருமகள் வீட்டில்லை யாகும்","உருவுங் கிளையும் ஒருங்கிழப் பாரே."],"p":"","r":"For those without the twelve-path that joins, guru, Siva and religion do not come together; fearful Lakshmi will not be in their house, and they lose form and kin together.","n":"துவாதச மார்க்கம் (the twelve-path) is unexplained; it is taken as the breath reaching the dvadasanta, twelve finger-breadths above the head, or as twelve divisions of practice. The threatened loss is worldly as well as spiritual: wealth (திருமகள்), body (உரு) and kin (கிளை).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","guru","selvam","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:96"},"t:1490":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1490,"t":["யோகச் சமாதியின் உள்ளே யகலிடம்","யோகச் சமாதியின் உள்ளே யுளரொளி","யோகச் சமாதியின் உள்ளே யுளசத்தி","யோகச் சமாதி யுகந்தவர் சித்தரே."],"p":"","r":"Within yoga-samadhi is the wide space, within it is light, within it is Shakti; those who delight in yoga-samadhi are siddhas.","n":"அகலிடம் (the wide place) may be the world or the inner sky (ஆகாசம்); ஒளி and சத்தி are the light and power traditionally experienced there. The refrain யோகச் சமாதியின் உள்ளே makes samadhi a place one enters.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["samaadhi","yogam","saththi","siththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:96"},"t:1491":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1491,"t":["யோகமும் போகமும் யோகியர்க் காகுமால்","யோகஞ் சிவரூபம் உற்றிடும் உள்ளத்தோர்","போகம் புவியிற் புருடார்த்த சித்திய","தாகும் இரண்டும் அழியாத யோகிக்கே."],"p":"","r":"Both yoga and enjoyment come to yogis: those whose minds reach Siva's form through yoga have, as enjoyment, the attainment of the aims of life on earth; both belong to the yogi who does not perish.","n":"போகம் (enjoyment) is not renounced here but given to the yogi along with yoga. புருடார்த்தம் are the aims of life, traditionally dharma, wealth, pleasure and release. அழியாத யோகி may be the yogi whose body does not decay, in keeping with Tirumular's care for the body.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","inbam","siththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:96"},"t:1492":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1492,"t":["ஆதார சோதனை யானாடி சுத்திகள்","மேதாதி யீரெண் கலாந்தத்து விண்ணொளி","போதா லயத்துப் புலன்கர ணம்புந்தி","சாதா ரணங்கெட லாஞ்ச மார்க்கமே."],"p":"","r":"Purifying the centres and the channels, the sky-light at the end of the sixteen kalas beginning with medha, and in the abode of awareness the ordinary working of senses, instruments and intellect ceasing: this is sahamarga.","n":"ஆதாரம் (the centres, chakras), நாடி சுத்தி (cleansing the channels) and மேதாதி ஈரெண் கலை (the sixteen kalas from medha, in mantra-yoga the stages of the syllable up to the end of nada) are technical. சாதாரணம் கெடல் is read as the ordinary working of the faculties ending.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","naadi","kalai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:96"},"t:1493":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1493,"t":["பிணங்கிநிற் கின்றவை ஐந்தையும் பின்னை","அணங்கி யெறிவ னயிர்மன வாளாற்","கணம்பதி னெட்டுங் கருதும் ஒருவன்","வணங்கவல் லான் சிந்தை வந்துநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"The five that stand rebelling he will then torment and hurl down with the sharp sword of the mind; the one the eighteen hosts contemplate has come and stood in the mind of him who can bow.","n":"The five are the senses. அயில் மன வாள் (the sharp sword of the mind) makes the mind the weapon, not the thing subdued. கணம் பதினெட்டு (the eighteen hosts) are the traditional eighteen classes of celestial beings. Who 'he' is in the first half, the yogi or the Lord, is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","manam","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:96"},"t:1494":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1494,"t":["வளங்கனி யொக்கும் வளநிறத் தார்க்கும்","வளங்கனி யொப்பதோர் வாய்மைய னாகும்","உளங்கனிந் துள்ள முகந்திருப் பார்க்குப்","பழங்கனிந் துள்ளே பகுந்துநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"To those of rich colour he is true like a rich ripe fruit; for those whose hearts have ripened and who hold him gladly in the heart, he, the ripe fruit, splits open and stands within.","n":"The play is on கனி (fruit, to ripen): the Lord is the fruit, the devotee's heart ripens, and the fruit opens within. வளநிறத்தார் (those of rich colour) is obscure; it may be the radiant or the fortunate. பகுந்து (split open, or shared out) suggests the Lord giving himself.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","anbu","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:96"},"t:1495":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1495,"t":["மேவிய சற்புத்திர மார்க்க மெய்த்தொழில்","தாவிப்ப தாஞ்சக மார்க்கம் சகத்தொழில்","ஆவ திரண்டும் அகன்று சகமார்க்கத்","தேவியோ டொன்றல் சன்மார்க்கத் தெளிவதே."],"p":"","r":"The true work of the good son's path is one thing, the saha path's is another; passing beyond both, to become one with the Goddess of the saha path is the clarity of sanmarga.","n":"சகமார்க்கம் (the companion's path) is traditionally yoga, and சன்மார்க்கம் (the true path) is jnana, so the verse ranks kiriyai, yoga and jnana. தாவிப்பதாம் and சகத்தொழில் are obscure: 'what leaps' or 'what raises', and 'the companion's work' or 'worldly work'. The union with the Goddess (தேவியோடு ஒன்றல்) as the mark of jnana is unusual and may be read of Shakti as grace.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kiriyai","yogam","gnaanam","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:97"},"t:1496":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1496,"t":["பூசித்தல் வாசித்தல் போற்றல் செபித்திடல்","ஆசற்ற நற்றவம் வாய்மை அழுக்கின்மை","நேசித்திட் டன்னமும் நீசுத்தி செய்தன்மர்","றாசற்ற சற்புத் திரமார்க்க மாகுமே."],"p":"","r":"Worshipping, reciting, praising, chanting, faultless good austerity, truthfulness, freedom from impurity, offering food with love and performing the purifications: this is the faultless path of the good son.","n":"The list mixes ritual acts (pūjā, recitation, japa, food offering, purification) with moral ones (truthfulness, purity), so kiriyai here is not ritual alone. நீசுத்தி is read as the purifications done before worship; the line is compressed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kiriyai","vazhipaadu","vaaimai","thooimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:97"},"t:1497":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1497,"t":["அறுகாற் பறவை அலர்தேர்ந் துழலும்","மறுகா நரையன்னந் தாமரை நீலங்","குறுகா நறுமலர் கொய்வன கண்டுஞ்","சிறுகால் அறநெறி சேர்கி லாரே."],"p":"","r":"Even seeing the six-legged bee roam choosing blossoms and the grey swan gather fragrant lotus and blue lily, people do not join even a small path of virtue.","n":"The plain sense contrasts creatures that gather flowers with people who will not gather them for worship. மறுகா (unwavering) and குறுகா (not shrinking, unfading) are uncertain in what they qualify, and சிறுகால் may mean 'a small path' or 'even for a little while'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","aram","pochchaappu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:97"},"t:1498":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1498,"t":["அருங்கரை யாவது அவ்வடி நீழற்","பெருங்கரை யாவது பிஞ்ஞக னாணை","வருங்கரை யேகின்ற மன்னுயிர்க் கெல்லாம்","ஒருங்கரை யாயுல கேழினொத் தானே."],"p":"","r":"The rare shore is the shade of his feet, the great shore is Pinakan's command, and for all souls who come toward the shore he became the one shore, equal to the seven worlds.","n":"கரை (shore) is said four times; the sea it implies is birth, which the verse doesn't name. பிஞ்ஞகன் is a name of Siva. Making his ஆணை (command, rule) a shore puts obedience beside refuge.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","piravi","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:97"},"t:1499":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1499,"t":["உயர்ந்தும் பணிந்தும் முகந்துந் தழுவி","வியந்தும் அரனடிக் கேமுறை செய்மின்","பயந்தும் பிறவிப் பயனது வாகும்","பயந்து பிரிக்கிலப் பான்மையு னாமே."],"p":"","r":"Rising, bowing, taking up, embracing and marvelling, do service in due order to Hara's feet; done in awe, that is the fruit of birth, and if you part from it in fear, that nature is yours.","n":"The first line is a string of bodily acts of devotion; முகந்து may be 'taking up' (flowers or water) or 'facing'. The last line, பயந்து பிரிக்கில் அப்பான்மை உனாமே, is obscure: perhaps a warning that whoever separates himself out of fear keeps his bound nature.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","sivam","piravi","panivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:97"},"t:1500":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1500,"t":["நின்றுதொழுவன் கிடந்தெம் பிரான்தன்னை","என்றுந் தொழுவன் எழிற்பரஞ் சோதியைத்","துன்று மலர்தூவித் தொழுமின் தொழுந்தோறுஞ்","சென்று வெளிப்படுந் தேவர் பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"Standing I worship, lying down I worship our Lord, always I worship the beautiful supreme light; worship him strewing flowers thick, and each time you worship the Lord of the gods comes forth and appears.","n":"நின்று, கிடந்து (standing, lying) cover every posture of the day, so worship is unbroken. The promise is plain: he appears (வெளிப்படும்) at each act of worship, not only at its end.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","sivam","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:97"},"t:1501":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1501,"t":["திருமன்னுஞ் சற்புத் திரமார்க்கச் சரியை","உருமன்னி வாழும் உலகத்தீர்கேண்மின்","கருமன்னு பாசங் கைகூம்பத் தொழுது","இருமன்னு நாடோறும் இன்புற் றிருந்தே."],"p":"","r":"You who live in the world in bodies, hear the sariyai of the good son's path where fortune abides: worship so that the bond that fixes you in the womb shrinks, and stay in joy every day.","n":"சற்புத்திர மார்க்கச் சரியை reflects the tradition that each path has four grades (the sariyai within kiriyai and so on). கைகூம்ப may mean the bond 'folds' or shrinks, or that one worships with hands folded. கருமன்னு பாசம் is read as the bond that keeps one returning to the womb.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kiriyai","sariyai","paasam","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:97"},"t:1502":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1502,"t":["எளியனல் தீப miடல்மலர் கொய்தல்","அளிதின் மெழுக லதுதூர்த்தல் வாழ்த்தல்","பளிமணி பற்றல் பன்மஞ் சனமாதி","தளிதொழில் செய்வது தான்தாசமார்க்கமே."],"p":"","r":"Lighting lamps, gathering flowers, smearing the floor with care, sweeping, praising, holding the bell, the many bathings of the image and the rest: doing the work of the temple is the servant's path.","n":"தளி is the temple. These are the menial tasks of a temple servant, which tradition ranks as the first of the four paths. The first words are garbled (see source issues), but the list of tasks is clear.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["sariyai","vazhipaadu","panivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:98"},"t:1503":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1503,"t":["அதுவிது வாதிப் பரமென் றகல்வர்","இதுவழி யென்றங் கிறைஞ்சின ரில்லை","விதிவழி யேசென்று வேந்தனை நாடு","மதுவிது நெஞ்சில் தணிக்கின்ற வாறே."],"p":"","r":"People wander off saying this or that is the supreme, and none bows saying 'this is the way'; go by the ordained way and seek the King: that is how the mind's 'that or this' is stilled.","n":"அதுவிது (that-this) is used twice: first of the rival claims about the supreme, then of the wavering they leave in the mind. விதிவழி (the ordained way) is not specified; in the chapter it is the servant's worship.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["samayam","sariyai","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:98"},"t:1504":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1504,"t":["அந்திப்பன் திங்க ளதன்பின்பு ஞாயிறு","சிந்திப்பன் என்றும் ஒருவன் செறிகழல்","வந்திப்ப வானவர் தேவனை நாடோறும்","வந்திப்ப தெல்லாம் வகையின் முடிந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"I worship at the twilights of the moon and then the sun, and always think on the One's close-fitting anklet; worshipping daily the God of gods whom the celestials worship, all worship is completed in its due order.","n":"அந்திப்பன் திங்கள் அதன்பின்பு ஞாயிறு may be the twilight worship at evening and morning, or, symbolically, the moon and sun breaths of yoga. வகையின் முடிந்ததே may mean worship is complete when done by rule, or that all forms of worship end in him.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sariyai","vazhipaadu","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:98"},"t:1505":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1505,"t":["அண்ணலை வானவர் ஆயிரம் பேர்சொல்லி","உன்னுவர் உள்மகிழ்ந்துண்ணின் றடிதொழக்","கண்ணவ னென்று கருது மவர்கட்குப்","பண்ணவன் பேரன்பு பற்றிநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"The celestials call the Lord by a thousand names, think of him rejoicing within and worship his feet standing inside them; for those who hold him as their own eye, the Lord stands held by great love.","n":"பண்ணவன் is a name for the Lord (perhaps 'master', or 'Lord of melody'). The point turns on பற்றி நின்றான்: it is the Lord who is held, caught by love, rather than the worshipper by the Lord. The thousand names are praised, but the love is what holds him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","vazhipaadu","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:98"},"t:1506":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1506,"t":["வாசித்தும் பூசித்தும்மாமலர் கொய்திட்டும்","பாசிக் குளத்தில்வீழ் கல்லா மனம்பார்க்கின்","மாசற்ற சோதி மணிமிடற் றாண்ணலை","நேசத் திருந்த நினைவறி யாரே."],"p":"","r":"Though they recite, worship and gather great flowers, look at their mind, a stone dropped in a mossy pond: they do not know the remembrance that dwells in love for the stainless light, the Lord with the dark-gem throat.","n":"The stone in the moss pond is the mind that sinks without being changed, or round which the moss closes again at once. The verse names the same acts the chapter commends (recitation, worship, flowers) and denies them value without நேசம் (love).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","anbu","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:98"},"t:1507":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1507,"t":["சாலோக மாதி சரியாதி யிற்பெறுஞ்","சாலோகஞ் சாமீபந் தங்குஞ் சரியையால்","மாலோகஞ் சேரில் வழியாகுஞ் சாரூபம்","பாலோகம் இல்லாப் பரனுரு வாமே."],"p":"","r":"Salokam and the rest are gained through sariyai and the rest: salokam and samipam come with sariyai, and on reaching the great world sarupam becomes the way, the form of the Supreme who is beyond the worlds.","n":"The traditional scheme pairs sariyai with salokam (his world), kiriyai with samipam (nearness), yoga with sarupam (his form) and jnana with sayujyam (union); the verse compresses it and seems to give two states to sariyai. மாலோகம் may be 'the great world' or Mal's (Vishnu's) world, and பாலோகம் இல்லா is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padhamukthi","sariyai","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:99"},"t:1508":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1508,"t":["சமயங் கிரியையிற் றன்மனங் கோயில்","சமய மனுமுறை தானே விசேடஞ்","சமயத்து மூலந் தனைத்தேறன் மூன்றாஞ்","சமயாபி டேகந் தானாஞ் சமாதியே."],"p":"","r":"In kiriyai one's own mind is the temple; the samaya rule of mantra, then the special stage, then the third, coming to know the root, and the samaya consecration itself is samadhi.","n":"சமயம், விசேடம் and the third (traditionally நிருவாணம்) are read as the three grades of Saiva initiation (diksha), with அபிடேகம் as the consecration of a teacher. The verse names only சமயம், விசேடம், மூன்றாம் and சமயாபிடேகம், so this key is the tradition's. தன்மனங் கோயில் (the mind as temple) is the plainest claim.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kiriyai","mandhiram","samaadhi","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:99"},"t:1509":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1509,"t":["பாசம் பசுவான தாகும்இச் சாலோகம்","பாச மருளான தாகும்இச் சாமீபம்","பாசஞ் சிரமான தாகும்இச் சாரூபம்","பாசங் கரைபதி சாயுச் சியமே."],"p":"","r":"In salokam the bond is still the soul's; in samipam the bond turns into grace; in sarupam the bond becomes the crown; with the bond dissolved, the Lord is sayujyam.","n":"Each line turns on what பாசம் becomes: பசுவானது (of the soul, or the soul's own state), அருளானது (grace), சிரமானது (the head or crown; possibly 'toilsome', or a corrupt word), and கரை (dissolves, or reaches its shore). The scheme uses the three Siddhanta categories, pati, pasu and pasam, to rank the states; the exact sense of each line is the tradition's.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["paasam","padhamukthi","saayujjiyam","pasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:100"},"t:1510":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1510,"t":["தங்கிய சாரூபந் தானெட்டாம் யோகமாந்","தங்குஞ்சன்மார்க்கந் தனிலன்றிக் கைகூடா","அங்கத் துடல்சித்தி சாதன ராகுவர்","இங்கிவ ராக விழிவற்ற யோகமே."],"p":"","r":"Abiding sarupam is the eighth limb of yoga and is not attained except in the abiding sanmarga; those who practise gain the body's attainment in its limbs, and here, as such, it is a yoga without fall.","n":"எட்டாம் யோகம் is read as samadhi, the eighth limb. அங்கத்து உடல் சித்தி may be bodily perfection or powers in the limbs; இங்கிவராக இழிவற்ற யோகமே is obscure. That sarupam needs sanmarga sits awkwardly with the scheme that gives it to yoga, unless சன்மார்க்கம் here means the true path in general.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["padhamukthi","yogam","siththi","samaadhi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:101"},"t:1511":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1511,"t":["சயிலலோ கத்தினைச் சார்ந்த பொழுதே","சயிலம தாகுஞ் சராசரம் போலப்","பயிலுங் குருவின் பதிபுக்க போதே","கயிலை இறைவன் கதிர்வடி வாமே."],"p":"","r":"As everything moving and still that comes to the golden mountain becomes that mountain's colour, so the moment one enters the abode of the guru one serves, one becomes the radiant form of the Lord of Kailash.","n":"சயிலம் (mountain) is Meru, whose gold the old image says turns all that nears it gold; the verse needs that image but names only the mountain. சாரூபம் (taking his form) is reached here through the guru, not through practice: entry into his abode (பதி) is enough.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["guru","padhamukthi","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:101"},"t:1512":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1512,"t":["சைவஞ் சிவனுடன் சம்பந்த மாவது","சைவந் தனையறிந் தேசிவஞ் சாருதல்","சைவஞ் சிவந்தன்னைச் சாராமல் நீங்குதல்","சைவஞ் சிவானந்தஞ் சாயுச் சியமே."],"p":"","r":"Saivam is relation with Siva; Saivam is knowing oneself and reaching Siva; Saivam is leaving what does not reach Siva; Saivam is Siva's bliss, sayujyam.","n":"சைவந் தனையறிந்து may be 'knowing Saivam' or 'knowing oneself (தன்னை)'. Line 3, சிவந்தன்னைச் சாராமல் நீங்குதல், can mean leaving all that is not Siva, or (less likely) parting without clinging even to Siva. The verse defines Saivam not as a sect but as a relation that ends in union.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saayujjiyam","sivam","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:102"},"t:1513":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1513,"t":["சாயுச் சியஞ்சாக் கிராதீதஞ் சாருதல்","சாயுச் சியமுப சாந்தத்துத் தங்குதற்","சாயுச் சியஞ்சிவ மாதல் முடிவிலாச்","சாயுச் சியமனத் தானந்த சத்தியே."],"p":"","r":"Sayujyam is reaching the state beyond waking; sayujyam is abiding in stillness; sayujyam is becoming Siva; endless sayujyam is the power of bliss in the mind.","n":"சாக்கிராதீதம் (beyond waking) and உபசாந்தம் (complete stilling) are technical terms for the soul's highest states in Siddhanta. மனத்து ஆனந்த சத்தி may be 'the bliss-shakti in the mind' or a name of the Goddess as bliss.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saayujjiyam","sivam","aanandham","samaadhi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:102"},"t:1514":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1514,"t":["இருட்டறை மூலை யிருந்த கிழவி","குருட்டுக் கிழவனைக் கூடல் குறித்துக்","குருட்டினை நீக்கிக் குணம்பல காட்டி","மருட்டி யவனை மணம்புரிந் தாளே."],"p":"","r":"The old woman who sat in the corner of the dark room, meaning to unite with the blind old man, removed his blindness, showed him many good qualities, charmed him and married him.","n":"A riddle verse. Tradition takes the old woman as the Lord's grace-power, hidden and ancient, the dark room as the soul's state under malam, and the blind old man as the soul blinded since beginningless time. Nothing in the verse names them; the chapter title and 1518 support the reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","thiruvarul","pasu","malam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:103"},"t:1515":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1515,"t":["தீம்புல னான திசையது சிந்திக்கில்","ஆம்புல னாயறிவார்க்கமு தாய்நிற்குந்","தேம்புல னான தெளிவறி வார்கட்குக்","கோம்புல னாடிய கொல்லையு மாமே."],"p":"","r":"If one thinks on the direction of the sweet sense, it stands as nectar to those who know it as the true sense, and for those who know the clarity of the honeyed sense it becomes the field where the senses roam.","n":"புலன் (sense, or region) is played on in every line: தீம்புலன், ஆம்புலன், தேம்புலன், கோம்புலன். The words resist a firm reading; கோம்புலன் நாடிய கொல்லை is especially obscure. The plain thread is that grace becomes nectar to those who know.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["amudham","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:103"},"t:1516":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1516,"t":["இருள்நீக்கி எண்ணில் பிறவி கடத்தி","அருள்நீங்கா வண்ணமே யாதியருளும்","மருள்நீங்கா வானவர் கோனொடுங் கூடிப்","பொருள்நீங்கா இன்பம் புலம்பயல் தானே."],"p":"","r":"Removing darkness and carrying one past countless births, the First grants grace so that grace never leaves; joined with the Lord of the celestials, one has a joy that never leaves the true thing.","n":"நீக்கி, நீங்கா repeats through the verse: grace removes and then does not leave. மருள்நீங்கா வானவர் கோன் is awkward ('the Lord of gods whose delusion is not removed' makes no sense of the Lord), and புலம்பயல் is obscure; the last two lines may be corrupt.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","piravi","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:103"},"t:1517":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1517,"t":["இருள்சூ ழறையில் இருந்தது நாடிற்","பொருள்சூழ் விளக்கது புக்கெரிந் தாற்போன்","மருள்சூழ் மயக்கத்து மாமலர் நந்தி","அருள்சூழ் இறைவனும் அம்மையு மாமே."],"p":"","r":"As a lamp brought into a room wrapped in darkness burns and shows the things there, so into the confusion of delusion come Nandi of the great flower, the Lord wrapped in grace, and the Mother.","n":"The dark room recalls 1514. The verse makes the coming of grace the coming of a pair, the Lord and the Mother (அம்மை), as the chapter's title, the descent of Shakti, would lead one to expect. மாமலர் நந்தி is read as Nandi (Siva) seated in the heart's lotus.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","saththi","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:103"},"t:1518":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1518,"t":["மருட்டிப் புணர்ந்து மயக்கமும் நீக்கி","வெருட்டி வினையறுத் தின்பம் விளைத்துக்","குருட்டினை நீக்கிக் குணம்பல காட்டி","அருட்டிகழ் ஞான மதுபுரிந் தானே."],"p":"","r":"Charming and uniting, removing confusion, driving off and cutting away deeds, bringing forth joy, removing blindness and showing many good qualities, he granted the knowledge that shines with grace.","n":"Line 3 is 1514's line 3 word for word, but here the subject is masculine (புரிந்தானே), the Lord, where in 1514 it was the old woman. Together the two verses make the Lord and his power one agent. வெருட்டி (frightening, driving away) shows grace as also forceful.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","gnaanam","vinai","malam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:103"},"t:1519":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1519,"t":["கன்னித் துறைபடிந் தாடிய ஆடவர்","கன்னித் துறைபடிந் தாடுங் கருத்திலர்","கன்னித் துறைபடிந் தாடுங் கருத்துண்டேற்","பின்னைப் பிறவி பிறிதில்லை தானே."],"p":"","r":"The men who bathed at the Kanni ford have no thought of bathing in the Kanni ford; if they had the thought of bathing in the Kanni ford, there would be no other birth after.","n":"கன்னித் துறை is the sacred bathing place of the Virgin (Kanyakumari) and, by the verse's turn, an inner ford of the Goddess as Kanni. Those who bathe outwardly miss the bathing that ends birth. What the inner bathing is, yogic or devotional, the verse doesn't say.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["theerththam","saththi","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:103"},"t:1520":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1520,"t":["செய்யன் கரியன் வெளியன் நற் பச்சையன்","எய்த வுணர்ந்தவர் எய்வர் இறைவனை","மைவென் றகன்ற பகடுரி போர்த்தவெங்","கைய னிவனென்று காதல்செய் வீரே."],"p":"","r":"He is red, dark, white and good green; those who fully understand will reach the Lord: love him, saying 'this is the one whose hand wears the hide of the huge elephant dark as kohl.'","n":"The four colours may be the colours of Siva's faces, of the elements, or of the Lord with Shakti (green is often hers); the verse gives no key. மைவென்று may be 'outdoing kohl in blackness'. The elephant-hide is the familiar emblem of Siva who flayed the elephant demon.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","anbu","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:103"},"t:1521":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1521,"t":["எய்திய காலங்கள் எத்தனை யாயினுந்","தையலுந் தானுந் தனிநா யகமென்பர்","வைகலுந் தன்னை வணங்கு மவர்கட்குக்","கையிற் கருமஞ்செய் காட்டது வாமே."],"p":"","r":"However many ages pass, they say the woman and he are the sole Lord; for those who worship him every day it becomes something shown, like a deed done in the hand.","n":"தையலும் தானும் தனிநாயகம் makes the pair one sovereign. கையிற் கருமஞ்செய் காட்டது is obscure: perhaps 'as plain as a thing done in one's hand', the sense taken here.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","sivam","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:103"},"t:1522":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1522,"t":["கண்டுகொண்டோமிரண்டுந்தொடர்ந் தாங்கொளி","பண்டுபண் டோயும் பரமன் பரஞ்சுடர்","வண்டுகொண் டாடு மலர்வார் சடையண்ணல்","நின்றுகண் டார்க்கிருள் நீக்கிநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"We have seen it: the light that followed both, the supreme light of the Supreme who rests from of old, the Lord with long matted locks where bees revel among flowers, stood removing darkness for those who stood and saw.","n":"இரண்டும் (both) may be the Lord and Shakti, or the two eyes, or sun and moon; பண்டுபண்டோயும் is unclear. The verse speaks as a witness (கண்டுகொண்டோம்), and the darkness removed is for those who stood and looked.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","thiruvarul","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:103"},"t:1523":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1523,"t":["அண்ணிக்கும் பெண்பிள்ளை அப்பனார் தோட்டத்தில்","எண்ணிக்கும் ஏழேழ் பிறவி யுணர்விக்கும்","உண்ணிற்ப தெல்லாம் ஒழிய முதல்வனைக்","கண்ணுற்று நின்ற கனியது வாகுமே."],"p":"","r":"The sweet girl in her father's garden makes one know seven times seven births, and when all that stands within is gone, she becomes the ripe fruit that has beheld the First.","n":"The girl in her father's garden is taken as the Goddess, daughter of the mountain king, or as grace growing in the Lord's own field. அண்ணிக்கும் (who is sweet, or who draws near) and எண்ணிக்கும் are uncertain forms. கனி (fruit) suggests the ripeness that 1527 calls the balance of deeds.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","piravi","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:103"},"t:1524":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1524,"t":["பிறப்பை யறுக்கும் பெருந்தவம் நல்கும்","மறப்பை யறுக்கும் வழிபட வைக்குங்","குறப்பெண் குவிமுலை கோமள வல்லி","சிறப்பொடு பூசனை செய்யநின் றார்க்கே."],"p":"","r":"For those who stand worshipping her with due honour, the hill-girl with rounded breasts, the tender creeper, cuts birth, grants great austerity, cuts forgetfulness and sets them to worship.","n":"குறப்பெண் (girl of the hill tribes) is a name for the Goddess as mountain-born, though it also recalls Valli. Worship of her leads back into worship (வழிபட வைக்கும்): grace both rewards devotion and produces it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saththi","vazhipaadu","piravi","thavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:103"},"t:1525":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1525,"t":["தாங்குமின் எட்டுத் திசைக்குந் தலைமகன்","பூங்கமழ் கோதைப் புரிகுழ லாளொடும்","ஆங்கது சேரும் அறிவுடை யார்கட்குத்","தூங்கொளி நீலந் தொடர்தலு மாமே."],"p":"","r":"Hold fast to the Lord of the eight directions with her of flower-fragrant garland and curling hair; for those of knowledge who join there, it becomes possible to follow the steady blue light.","n":"தூங்கொளி நீலம் (the hanging or steady blue light) is traditionally the Goddess, often described as blue, or a light seen in meditation. ஆங்கது சேரும் (who join there) leaves the place unnamed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","sivam","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:103"},"t:1526":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1526,"t":["நணுகினு ஞானக் கொழுந்தொன்று நல்கும்","பணிகிலும் பன்மலர் தூவிப் பணிவன்","அணுகிய தொன்றறி யாத வொருவன்","அணுகும் உலகெங்கு மாவியு மாமே."],"p":"","r":"If one draws near she gives a shoot of knowledge; even in bowing I bow strewing many flowers; the One whom nothing that approaches can know becomes the life of every world that approaches.","n":"The subject of line 1 is unstated, and அணுகிய தொன்றறியாத ஒருவன் may be the Lord who is unknown to what approaches, or the one who knows nothing of what has come near. The verse plays on அணுகு (approach).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","vazhipaadu","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:103"},"t:1527":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1527,"t":["இருவினை நேரொப்பில் இன்னருட் சத்தி","குருவென வந்து குணம்பல நீக்கித்","தருமெனு ஞானத்தால் தன்செய லற்றால்","திரிமலந் தீர்ந்து சிவனவ னாமே."],"p":"","r":"When the two deeds stand evenly balanced, the sweet grace-power comes as the guru and removes many qualities; when one's own doing ends through the knowledge it gives, the three impurities end and one becomes Siva.","n":"The chapter's doctrine stated plainly: இருவினை ஒப்பு (balance of good and bad deeds) is the sign of ripeness for the descent of power, the power comes in the form of the guru, and தன்செயல் அற்றால் (when one's own agency ends) the three malas go. குணம்பல நீக்கி is read as removing the soul's bound qualities. Sengalvaraya notes a parallel in Kumaraguruparar's Kandar Kalivenba.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","guru","malam","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:103"},"t:1528":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1528,"t":["இரவும் பகலும் இலாத இடத்தே","குரவஞ் செய்கின்ற குழலியை உன்னி","அரவஞ்செய் யாமல் அவளுடன் சேரப்","பரிவொன்றி லாளும் பராபரை தானே."],"p":"","r":"Thinking of her with flowing hair who makes fragrance in the place where there is neither night nor day, join her without a sound, and the Supreme, with no other care, will rule you.","n":"இரவும் பகலும் இலாத இடம் (the place of no night or day) is traditionally a yogic state beyond the moon and sun breaths, or the state beyond waking and sleep. குரவம் may be the kuravam flower or a sound; அரவஞ்செய்யாமல் (without noise) may mean silently, or without the serpent (kundalini) stirring. பராபரை is the supreme Goddess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","yogam","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:103"},"t:1529":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1529,"t":["மாலை விளக்கும் மதியமும் ஞாயிறுஞ்","சாலை விளக்குந் தனிச்சுடர் அண்ணலுள்","ஞானம் விளக்கிய நாதன்என் உள்புகுந்(து)","ஊனை விளக்கி யுடனிருந் தானே."],"p":"","r":"The evening lamp, the moon and the sun, and the lone flame, the Lord who lights the hall: the Lord who lit up knowledge entered me, lit up my flesh and stayed with me.","n":"விளக்கு (lamp, to light) runs through every line, from outer lights to the light that lights the body. ஊனை விளக்கி (lit up the flesh) makes the body itself the place grace shines, not something to be left behind. Sengalvaraya notes Appar's verse beginning மாசில் வீணையும் மாலை மதியமும், which also opens with the evening moon.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","gnaanam","yaakkai","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:103"},"t:1530":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1530,"t":["ஆயத்துள் நின்ற அறுசம யங்களுங்","காயத்துள் நின்ற கடவுளைக் காண்கிலர்","மாயக் குழியில் விழுவர் மனைமக்கள்","பாசத்தில் உற்றுப் பதைக்கின்ற வாறே."],"p":"","r":"The six sects that stand in the assembly do not see the God who stands within the body; caught in the bond of house and children, they fall writhing into the pit of maya.","n":"ஆயத்துள் (in the assembly) is set against காயத்துள் (in the body), a near rhyme that carries the argument: the sects look in the crowd and miss the body. Which six sects are meant the verse doesn't say; tradition gives several lists.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["samayam","yaakkai","maayai","paasam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:104"},"t:1531":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1531,"t":["உள்ளத்து ளேதான் கரந்தெங்கும் நின்றவன்","வள்ளல் தலைவன் மலருறை மாதவன்","பொள்ளற் குரம்பைப் புகுந்து புறப்படுங்","கள்ளத் தலைவன் கருத்தறி யார்களே."],"p":"","r":"They do not know the purpose of the One who hid within the heart and stands everywhere, the generous chief, the great ascetic dwelling in the flower, the thief-chief who enters and leaves the hollow hut of the body.","n":"பொள்ளற் குரம்பை (the hut full of holes) is the body with its nine openings. கள்ளத் தலைவன் (the thief-chief) is the Lord who comes and goes unseen. மலருறை மாதவன் may be the great ascetic in the heart's lotus or a name of Vishnu given to the one Lord; the verse doesn't settle it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","yaakkai","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:104"},"t:1532":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1532,"t":["உள்ளத்தும் உள்ளன் புறத்துள்ளன் என்பவர்க்","குள்ளத்தும் உள்ளன் புறத்துள்ளன் எம்மிறை","உள்ளத்தும் இல்லை புறத்தில்லை என்பவர்க்","குள்ளத்தும் இல்லை புறத்தில்லை தானே."],"p":"","r":"For those who say he is within and without, our Lord is within and without; for those who say he is neither within nor without, he is neither within nor without.","n":"A mirror verse. It can be read as the Lord appearing to each as each conceives him, or as an irony at the sects: what they say decides what they find. It doesn't say which view is right.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","meyyunarvu","samayam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:104"},"t:1533":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1533,"t":["ஆறு சமயமும் கண்டவர் கண்டிலர்","ஆறு சமயப் பொருளும் அவனலன்","தேறுமின் தேறித் தெளிமின் தெளிந்தபின்","மாறுதல் இன்றி மனைபுக லாமே."],"p":"","r":"Those who saw the six sects did not see him; he is not the thing the six sects hold; be clear, and having become clear, settle, and once settled you may enter the house without wavering.","n":"தேறுமின், தேறித் தெளிமின், தெளிந்தபின் chains clarity to clarity. மனை (house, home) is read as release, the home one does not leave; it may also be plain household life lived without wavering.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["samayam","thelivu","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:104"},"t:1534":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1534,"t":["சிவமல்ல தில்லை யறையே சிவமாந்","தவமல்ல தில்லை தலைப்படு வார்க்கிங்(கு)","அவமல்ல தில்லை அறுசம யங்கள்","தவம்வல்ல நந்திதாள் சார்ந்துய்யு நீரே."],"p":"","r":"There is nothing but Siva, I declare; for those who reach him here there is nothing but the austerity that is Siva; the six sects are nothing but waste: cling to the feet of Nandi, strong in austerity, and be saved.","n":"சிவம், தவம், அவம் rhyme through three lines. அறையே may be 'I proclaim' or a call. நந்தி may be Siva himself or Tirumular's teacher; the tradition uses the name for both. The line breaks are uncertain (யறையே சிவமாந் / தவம்).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","samayam","thavam","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:104"},"t:1535":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1535,"t":["அண்ணலை நாடிய ஆறு சமயமும்","விண்ணவ ராக மிகவும் விரும்பியே","முண்ணின் றழியு முயன்றில ராதலான்","மண்ணின் றொழியும் வகையறி யார்களே."],"p":"","r":"The six sects that sought the Lord, longing greatly to become gods, perish where they stand because they did not strive within, and so do not know the way to leave the earth.","n":"முண்ணின் றழியு முயன்றிலர் is unclear: perhaps உண்ணின்று (within) with a misprint, as read here. விண்ணவராக (to become gods) faults the sects for seeking heaven rather than release from birth.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["samayam","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:104"},"t:1536":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1536,"t":["சிவகதி யேகதி மற்றுள்ள எல்லாம்","பவகதி பாசப் பிறவியொன் றுண்டு","தவகதி தன்னொடு நேரொன்று தோன்றில்","அவகதி மூவரும் அவ்வகை யாமே."],"p":"","r":"Siva's path is the only path; all others are paths of becoming, and in them is a birth in bonds; if the path of austerity shows itself straight, the three of the wasted path are of that same kind.","n":"சிவகதி, பவகதி, தவகதி, அவகதி play on கதி (path, end). Lines 3 and 4 are obscure: அவகதி மூவரும் may be the three gods (Brahma, Vishnu, Rudra) as ends in becoming, or three classes of the lost.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","piravi","paasam","thavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:104"},"t:1537":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1537,"t":["நூறு சமயம் உளவா நுவலுங்கால்","ஆறு சமயமவ் ஆறுட் படுவன","கூறு சமயங்கன் கொண்டநெறிநில்லா","ஈறு பரநெறி யில்லா நெறியன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Counted out, there are a hundred sects, and they fall within the six; the sects that are spoken of do not stay in the path they took; the end, the supreme path, is no path of theirs.","n":"ஆறு சமயமவ் ஆறுட் படுவன may also read 'the six sects fall within those six' (the six inner sects of Saivism). The last line, ஈறு பரநெறி யில்லா நெறியன்றே, can be parsed as 'the supreme path is not a path without an end' or as read here; it is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["samayam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:104"},"t:1538":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1538,"t":["கத்துங் கழுதைகள் போலுங் கலதிகள்","சுத்த சிவமெங்குந் தோய்வற்று நிற்கின்றான்","குற்றம் தெளியார் குணங்கொண்டு கோதாட்டிப்","பித்தேறி நாளும் பிறந்திறப் பாரே."],"p":"","r":"The wretched are like braying donkeys; pure Siva stands everywhere untouched, but not understanding the one who removes faults, takes up good qualities and cherishes, they go mad and are born and die every day.","n":"The third line echoes Tiruvasagam (Ammanai), where குற்றங்கள் நீக்கிக் குணங்கொண்டு கோதாட்டி describes the Lord, which fixes its sense: the Lord is the one who removes faults and cherishes, and the deluded don't know him. தோய்வற்று (untouched) says Siva pervades without being stained.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["samayam","sivam","piravi","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:104"},"t:1539":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1539,"t":["மயங்குகின் றாரு மதிதெளிந் தாரும்","முயங்கி யிருவினை முழைமுகப் பாச்சி","இயங்கிப் பெறுவரே லீறது காட்டிற்","பயங்கெட் டவர்க்கோர் பரநெறி யாமே."],"p":"","r":"Whether bewildered or clear of mind, if people unite and drive the two deeds into the cave's mouth and, moving on, attain, then when the end is shown there is a supreme path for those whose fear is gone.","n":"முழைமுகப் பாச்சி (driving into the mouth of the cave) is obscure; it may be yogic, the breath driven into an inner opening. The point that both the confused and the clear can reach the path is the plainest part.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","meyyunarvu","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:104"},"t:1540":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1540,"t":["சேயன் அணியன் பிணியிலன் பேர்நந்தி","தூயன் துளக்கற நோக்கவல் லார்கட்கு","மாயன் மயக்கிய மானுட ராமவர்","காயம் விளைக்குங் கருத்தறி யார்களே."],"p":"","r":"Far and near, without affliction, named Nandi, pure to those who can look without wavering: people bewildered by the deceiver do not know the purpose the body serves.","n":"மாயன் may be Vishnu, often named the deluder, or maya personified. காயம் விளைக்குங் கருத்து (the intent the body produces) is read as the purpose for which the body is given; it may also mean the thoughts the body breeds.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","maayai","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:104"},"t:1541":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1541,"t":["வழியரண் டுக்குமோர் வித்தது வான","பழியது பார்மிசை வாழ்தல் உறுதல்","சுழியறி வாளன்றன் சொல்வழி முன்னின்","றழிவழி வார்நெறி நாடநில் லாரே."],"p":"","r":"There is one seed for both ways: the blame of living on earth and clinging to it; those on the ruinous path do not stand before the word of the one who knows the whirl to seek the way.","n":"வழி இரண்டு (both ways) is unexplained. சுழியறிவாளன் (the one who knows the whirl) is taken as the guru who knows the inner point (சுழி) of yoga. The verse is compressed and its grammar uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["samayam","guru","pattru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:104"},"t:1542":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1542,"t":["மாதவர் எல்லாம் மாதேவன் பிரான்என்பர்","நாதம தாக அறியப்படுநந்தி","பேதஞ்செய் யாதே பிரான்என்று கைதொழில்","ஆதியும் அந்நெறி யாகிநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"All the great ascetics say Mahadeva is the Lord; Nandi is to be known as nada; if, without making distinctions, you worship with folded hands calling him Lord, the First himself stands as that path.","n":"பேதஞ்செய்யாதே (without making distinctions) answers the chapter's quarrel: the remedy is not one sect's victory but worship that does not divide. நாதமதாக அறியப்படு (known as nada) gives the Lord a mantra-form.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","naadham","vazhipaadu","samayam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:104"},"t:1543":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1543,"t":["அரநெறி யப்பனை யாதிப் பிரானை","உரநெறி யாகி யுளம்புகுந் தானைப்","பரநெறி தேடிய பத்தர்கள் சித்தம்","பரனெறி யாவிடிற் பல்வகைத் தூரமே."],"p":"","r":"Hara's path, the Father, the First Lord, who became the strong path and entered the heart: if the minds of devotees who seek the supreme path do not become the Supreme's path, they are many kinds of distance away.","n":"நெறி (path) ends every line: Hara's path, the strong path, the supreme path, the Supreme's path. The claim is that devotion itself must become the path, not merely seek it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","adiyaar","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:104"},"t:1544":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1544,"t":["பரிசறி வானவன் பண்பன் பகலோன்","பெரிசறி வானவர் பேற்றில் திகழுந்","துரிசற நீநினை தூய்மணி வண்ணன்","அரிதவன் வைத்த அறநெறி தானே."],"p":"","r":"He knows each one's nature, he is good, he is the sun; he shines in the attainment of those of great knowledge; think of him without fault, the pure gem-coloured one: rare is the path of virtue he set.","n":"பரிசறிவான் may be 'he who knows the measure (of each)' or 'he who knows the gift'. அரிது (rare, hard) in the last line may say his path is hard to walk or precious.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","aram","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:104"},"t:1545":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1545,"t":["ஆன சமயம் அதுஇது நன்றெனும்","மாய மனிதர் மயக்க மதுவொழி","கானங் கடந்த கடவுளை நாடுமின்","ஊனங் கடந்த வுருவது வாமே."],"p":"","r":"Give up the delusion of deluded men who say this sect or that one is good; seek the God beyond the forest, whose form is beyond all lack.","n":"கானம் may be the forest (the wild of worldly life) or, as some take it, song; ஊனம் (lack, flaw) also means flesh, so ஊனங் கடந்த உரு may be a form beyond the flesh. The verse rejects the ranking of sects, not worship.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["samayam","iraivan","maayai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:104"},"t:1546":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1546,"t":["அந்நெறி நாடி அமரரு முனிவருஞ்","செந்நெறி கண்டார் சிவனெனப் பெற்றார்பின்","முன்னெறி நாடி முதல்வன் அருளிலார்","சென்னெறி செல்லார் திகைக்கின்ற வாறே."],"p":"","r":"Seeking that path, the immortals and sages saw the red path and were made Siva; those without the First's grace, who seek the earlier paths, do not go on the red path, and so are bewildered.","n":"செந்நெறி (the red, straight path) is Siva's path. The verse makes grace the condition: those without it keep to முன்னெறி, the paths they began with. சிவனெனப் பெற்றார் (gained the state of being called Siva) anticipates sayujyam.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","thiruvarul","samayam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:104"},"t:1547":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1547,"t":["உறுமா றறிவதும் உள்நின்ற சோதி","பெறுமா றறியிற் பிணக்கொன்றும் இல்லை","அறுமா றதுவான வங்கியு ளாங்கே","இறுமா றறிகிலர் ஏழைகள் தாமே."],"p":"","r":"If one knows how to reach and how to gain the light standing within, there is no quarrel at all; the poor in mind do not know how to end there, in the fire that is the way of cutting off.","n":"பிணக்கொன்றும் இல்லை (no quarrel at all) answers the chapter's title: the sects' quarrel ends in the inner light, not in argument. வங்கி (fire, vahni) in line 3 is obscure; tradition may read it as the inner fire of yoga.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["meyyunarvu","samayam","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:104"},"t:1548":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1548,"t":["வழிநடக் கும்பரி சொன்றுண்டு வையங்","கழிநடக் குண்டவர் கற்பனை கேட்பர்","சுழிநடக் குந்துய ரம்மது நீக்கிப்","பழிநடப் பார்க்குப் பரவலு மாமே."],"p":"","r":"There is one way to walk the path; the world listens to the fancies of those who walk astray; removing the grief that walks in whirlpools, even those who walk in blame may come to praise.","n":"நட (walk) runs through every line. கழிநடக்குண்டவர் (those who walk to excess, or along the backwaters) and பரவல் (praise, or spreading) are uncertain; the last line may also mean that the grief spreads to those who walk in blame.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["samayam","idumbai","pazhi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:104"},"t:1549":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1549,"t":["வழிசென்ற மாதவம் வைகின்ற போது","பழிசெல்லும் வல்வினைப் பற்றறுத் தாங்கே","வழிசெல்லும் வல்வினை யாம்திறம் விட்டிட்","டுழிசெல்லில் உம்பர் தலைவன்முன் னாமே."],"p":"","r":"When the great austerity that went the right way abides, cutting away the clinging of strong deeds that bring blame, and leaving the course of the strong deeds that follow, if one goes in due order, one stands before the Lord of the celestials.","n":"வழி (way) is repeated, and வழிசெல்லும் வல்வினை may be 'the deeds that go with one on the way'. உழி is read as 'in due order' or 'at the right time'. The end is to stand before the Lord, not to become him.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","vinai","pattru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:104"},"t:1550":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1550,"t":["இமையங்க ளாய்நின்ற தேவர்கள் ஆறு","சமையங்கள் பெற்றனர் சாத்திரம் ஓதி","யமையறிந் தோமென்ப ராதிப் பிரானுங்","கமையறிந் தாருட் கலந்துநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"Those who stand as gods gained the six sects and, reciting the shastras, say 'we have understood'; but the First Lord stands mingled among those who know forbearance.","n":"இமையங்களாய் நின்ற தேவர்கள் is obscure: இமையவர் are the unblinking gods, but இமையங்கள் may hide another word. யமை in line 3 is read as அமை (it is settled, we know); கமை is taken as Sanskrit kshama, forbearance. The contrast between scripture-learning and forbearance is the verse's point on this reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["samayam","porai","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:105"},"t:1551":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1551,"t":["பாங்கமர் கொன்றைப் படர்சடை யானடி","தாங்கு மனிதர் தரணியில் நேரொப்பர்","நீங்கிய வண்ணம் நினைவுசெய் யாதவர்","ஏங்கி உலகில் இருந்தழு வாரே."],"p":"","r":"People who bear the feet of the Lord with spreading matted hair and konrai blossom are without equal on earth; those who never think how to be freed stay in the world grieving and weeping.","n":"நேர் ஒப்பர் can mean 'they are equal' (to the gods, or to one another) or 'they are incomparable'. நீங்கிய வண்ணம் is taken as the way of release from bonds; it could also mean the way the Lord stays apart.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvadi","sivam","idumbai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:105"},"t:1552":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1552,"t":["இருந்தழு வாரும் இயல்புகெ ட்டாரும்","அருந்தவ மேற்கொண்டங் கண்ணலை எண்ணில்","வருந்தா வகைசெய்து வானவர் கோனும்","பெருந்தன்மை நல்கும் பிறப்பில்லை தானே."],"p":"","r":"If those who weep and those whose nature has gone wrong take up rare austerity and think of the Lord there, the king of the gods keeps them from suffering, grants them greatness, and there is no more birth.","n":"The verse picks up 1551's weepers and offers them a way back: even the fallen (இயல்பு கெட்டார்) are not shut out. வானவர் கோன் here is the Lord, not Indra.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","piravi","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:105"},"t:1553":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1553,"t":["தூரறி வாளர் துணைவர் நினைப்பிலர்","பாரறி வாளர் படுபயன் றானுண்பர்","காரறி வாளர் கலந்து பிறப்பர்கள்","நீரறி வார்நெடு மாமுகி லாமே."],"p":"","r":"Those of deep knowledge do not think of any helper; those of worldly knowledge eat the fruit that falls to them; those of dark knowledge are born again, mixed in the world; those who know the water become the great tall cloud.","n":"The four kinds of knower turn on puns I can't fix: தூர் (root, depth, or far), பார் (the earth), கார் (darkness, or the rain-season) and நீர் (water, or one's true nature, நீர்மை). The cloud of the last line may be one who, knowing, gives to all as rain does; that is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arivu","piravi","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:105"},"t:1554":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1554,"t":["அறிவுடன் கூடி அழைத்தோர் தோணி","பறியுடன் பாரம் பழம்பதி சிந்துங்","குறியது கண்டுங் கொடுவினை யாளர்","செறிய நினைக்கிலர் சேவடி தானே."],"p":"","r":"Though they see the sign of the boat, called up with knowledge, spilling its load with its mast and its old home, the people of cruel deeds do not think to cling to the red feet.","n":"The boat is commonly taken as the body that carries the soul; பறி (mast, or a fish-basket, or plunder) and பழம்பதி (the old dwelling) are unclear. The plain point stands: people see the body break up and still don't turn to the feet.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","yaakkai","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:105"},"t:1555":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1555,"t":["மன்னும் ஒருவன் மருவு மனோமயன்","என்னின் மனிதர் இகழ்வரிவ் வேழைகள்","துன்னி மனமே தொழுமின் துணையிலி","தன்னையும் அங்கே தலைப்பட லாமே."],"p":"","r":"If I say the one who abides is the one who dwells in the mind, people scorn it, these poor fools; mind, draw near and worship, and there you can reach even the one who has no companion.","n":"மனோமயன், he who is made of or dwells in the mind, is the claim people scorn. துணையிலி, the one with no companion or equal, recalls the Kural's தனக்குவமை இல்லாதான். The verse turns from the scorners to address its own mind.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["manam","nindhai","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:105"},"t:1556":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1556,"t":["ஓங்காரத் துள்ளொளி உள்ளே உதயமுற்","றாங்கார மற்ற அனுபவங் கைகூடார்","சாங்கால முன்னார் பிறவாமை சார்வுறார்","நீங்காச் சமயத்துள் நின்றொழிந் தார்களே."],"p":"","r":"Though the light within Om rises within them, they do not gain the experience free of the ego; before death they do not reach freedom from birth; they stood inside the sect they never left and perished.","n":"ஓங்காரத்துள் ஒளி is a yogic term for the light seen in meditation on Om. ஆங்காரம் அற்ற அனுபவம is the experience of Siva without the 'I'. The verse's fault is not the sect as such but never moving beyond it (நீங்காச் சமயம்).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["samayam","piranavam","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:105"},"t:1557":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1557,"t":["இமையவர் தம்மையும் எம்மையும் முன்னம்","அமைய வகுத்தவன் ஆதி புராணன்","சமயங்க ளாறும்தன் றாளிணை நாட","அமையங் குழல்கின்ற ஆதிப் பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The ancient First One who long ago shaped both the gods and us is the First Lord who moves among the six sects so that they seek his pair of feet.","n":"அமையங்கு உழல்கின்ற is unclear: 'roams there among them' or 'is fitly there'. On either reading the six sects are his arrangement, not rivals to him.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["samayam","sivam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:106"},"t:1558":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1558,"t":["ஒன்றது பேரூர் வழியா றதற்கு","என்றது போல இருமுச் சமயமும்","நன்றிது தீதிது என்றுரை யாளர்கள்","குன்று குரைத்தெழு நாயையொத் தார்களே."],"p":"","r":"As one great city has six roads leading to it, so are the six sects; those who say 'this is good, that is bad' are like a dog that rises barking at a hill.","n":"The hill is unmoved by the barking: the quarrel harms only the quarreller. The verse does not say every road is equally short; 1556 and 1567 show Tirumular still ranks a Saiva path.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["samayam","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:106"},"t:1559":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1559,"t":["சைவப் பெருமைத் தனிநா யகன்தன்னை","உய்ய வுயிர்க்கின்ற ஒண்சுடர் நந்தியை","மெய்ய பெருமையர்க் கன்பனை இன்பஞ்செய்","வையத் தலைவனை வந்தடைந் துயமினே."],"p":"","r":"Come and reach, and be saved by, the unique Lord of Saiva greatness, Nandi the bright flame who gives life so that souls are saved, loving to those of true greatness, lord of the world who gives bliss.","n":"சைவப் பெருமை names the Saiva path in a chapter that has just told sects not to rank one another; the inner religion is offered as fulfilment, not as a rival sect.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","guru","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:106"},"t:1560":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1560,"t":["சிவனவன் வைத்ததோர் தெய்வ நெறியிற்","பவனவன் வைத்த பழிவழி நாடி","இவனவன் என்ப தறியவல் லார்கட்","கவனவ னங்குள தாங்கட னாமே."],"p":"","r":"For those able to know, by seeking the way he laid down within the divine path Siva set, that 'this one is he', he is there, and it becomes a duty.","n":"பவன் may be Bhava, a name of Siva, and பழிவழி (the way of blame) may be a corruption of பழவழி (the old way). இவன் அவன் is often taken as recognizing the soul as one with the Lord, or the guru as the Lord; the verse does not settle which.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:106"},"t:1561":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1561,"t":["ஆமா றுரைக்கும் அறுசம யாதிக்குப்","போமாறு தானில்லை புண்ணிய மல்லதங்","காமாம் வழியாக்கும் அவ்வே றுயிர்கட்கும்","போமா றவ்வாதாரப் பூங்கொடி யாளே."],"p":"","r":"For the six sects that teach how to become, there is no way to go except merit; for other souls, the way there is the flowering creeper of the supports.","n":"ஆதாரப் பூங்கொடியாள், the flower-creeper lady of the ādhāras, is taken as Shakti or kundalini rising through the body's centres. The verse seems to set the sects' road of merit against an inner yogic road, but the syntax is loose.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["samayam","saththi","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:106"},"t:1562":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1562,"t":["அரன்நெறி யாவ தறிந்தேனும் நானுஞ்","சிலநெறி தேடித் திரிந்தஅந் நாளும்","உரநெறி யுள்ளக் கடல்கடந் தேறுந்","தரநெறி நின்ற தனிச்சுடர் தானே."],"p":"","r":"Even in the days when I knew Hara's path and still wandered searching other paths, the solitary flame stood on the strong path by which one crosses the sea of the heart and climbs.","n":"The verse is autobiographical: the Lord was already waiting on the path while the speaker strayed. உரநெறி and தரநெறி (the strong path, the path of rank or crossing) are unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","guru","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:106"},"t:1563":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1563,"t":["தேர்ந்த அரனை அடைந்த சிவநெறி","பேர்ந்தவர் உன்னிப் பெயர்ந்த பெருவழி","ஆர்ந்தவர் அண்டத்துப் புக்க அருள்நெறி","போந்து புனைந்து புணர்நெறி யாமே."],"p":"","r":"The Siva path that reached the discerned Hara, the great way by which those who turned away went in contemplation, the path of grace by which the fulfilled entered the beyond, coming and adorning it, becomes the path of union.","n":"The verse piles up names for one path; பேர்ந்தவர் (those who moved away, from the world or from births) and போந்து புனைந்து are uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","thiruvarul","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:106"},"t:1564":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1564,"t":["ஈரு மனத்தை யிரண்டற வீசுமின்","ஊருஞ் சகாரத்தை ஓதுமின் னோதியே","வாரு மரநெறி மன்னியே முன்னியத்","தூருஞ் சுடரொளி தோன்றலு மாமே"],"p":"","r":"Cast off the divided mind so that its twoness ends; recite the moving letter ச, and reciting, abide in Hara's path and contemplate; then the flame-light that fills will appear.","n":"சகாரம், the letter ச, is traditionally the first letter of சிவாய and so stands for Siva in the five-letter mantra; ஊரும் (moving) may describe the breath carrying it. ஈரும் மனம் is the mind that splits or cuts.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","manam","panchaakkaram"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:106"},"t:1565":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1565,"t":["மினற்குறி யாளனை வேதியர் வேதத்","தனற்குறி யாளனை ஆதிப் பிரான்தன்னை","நினைக்குறி யாளனை ஞானக் கொழுந்தி","னயக்குறி காணில் அரநெறி யாமே."],"p":"","r":"If you see the Lord whose sign is lightning, whose sign in the Brahmins' Veda is fire, the First Lord whose sign is thought, the dear sign at the tip of the shoot of knowledge, that is Hara's path.","n":"The four குறி (signs) may be forms of the inner light; மினல் is read as மின்னல், lightning. நயக்குறி and ஞானக் கொழுந்து (the tip, or flame, of knowledge) are obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","vedham","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:106"},"t:1566":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1566,"t":["ஆய்ந்துண ரார்களின் ஆன்மாச் சதுர்பல","வாய்ந்துண ராவகை நின்ற அரனெறி","பாய்ந்துணர் வார் அரன் சேவடி கைதொழு","தேய்ந்துணர் செய்வதோர் இன்பமு மாமே."],"p":"","r":"Hara's path stands so that souls, many though they are, cannot grasp it by examination; those who plunge in, worship his red feet with folded hands and, being worn away, understand, find it a bliss.","n":"ஆன்மாச் சதுர்பல is obscure: perhaps 'souls of four and many kinds'. தேய்ந்துணர் may be 'understand as the self wears away'. The contrast between examining and plunging in is clear and recurs in 1571.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvadi","vazhipaadu","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:106"},"t:1567":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1567,"t":["சைவ சமயத் தனிநா யகன்நந்தி","உய்ய வகுத்த குருநெறி ஒன்றுண்டு","தெய்வச் சிவநெறி சன்மார்க்கஞ் சேர்ந்துய்ய","வையத் துளார்க்குவகுத்துவைத் தானே."],"p":"","r":"Nandi, the unique Lord of the Saiva religion, laid down one guru's path for salvation: the divine Siva path, the true way, which he set out for those in the world to join and be saved.","n":"சன்மார்க்கம், the true path, is in later Siddhanta the highest of four paths (after charya, kriya and yoga); whether Tirumular uses it in that technical sense here can't be fixed from this verse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["guru","sivam","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:106"},"t:1568":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1568,"t":["இத்தவம் அத்தவம் என்றிரு பேரிடும்","பித்தரைக் காணின் நகுமெங்கள் பேர்நந்தி","எத்தவ மாகிலென் எங்குப் பிறக்கிலென்","ஒத்துணர் வார்க்கொல்லை யூர்புக லாமே."],"p":"","r":"Our great Nandi laughs when he sees madmen who give two names, 'this penance' and 'that penance'; whatever the penance, wherever one is born, those who understand alike can quickly enter the city.","n":"ஒல்லை ஊர் is 'quickly, the city', echoing the one city of 1558. எங்குப் பிறக்கிலென் sets birth aside as well as sect.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["samayam","thavam","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:106"},"t:1569":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1569,"t":["ஆமே பிரான்முகம் ஐந்தொடு மாருயிர்","ஆமே பிரானுக் கதோமுக மாறுள","தாமே பிரானுக்குந் தன்சிர மாலைக்கும்","நாமே பிரானுக்கு நரரியல் பாமே."],"p":"","r":"The Lord has five faces and is the precious soul; the Lord has a sixth face turned downward; for the Lord and his garland of skulls, we ourselves are the Lord's human nature.","n":"The five faces are Sadasiva's; அதோமுகம், the downward face, is a sixth in some Agamic accounts. சிரமாலை may be a garland of heads (skulls) or a garland on his head. The last line is very obscure; I read it as the Lord taking human nature in us, but that is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:106"},"t:1570":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1570,"t":["ஆதிப்பிரானுல கேழும் அளந்தவன்","ஓதக் கடலும் உயிர்களு மாய்நிற்கும்","பேதிப் பிலாமையின் நின்ற பராசத்தி","ஆதிக்கட் டெய்வமும் அந்தமு மாமே."],"p":"","r":"The First Lord who measured the seven worlds stands as the tidal sea and as all lives; Parasakti, standing undivided, is the deity of the beginning and the end.","n":"உலகேழும் அளந்தவன், who measured the worlds, is Vishnu's epithet as Trivikrama; applied to the First Lord it fits the chapter's case that other sects' gods are his. Whether ஆதிப்பிரான் here is Vishnu, Siva or both can't be fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","saththi","samayam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:106"},"t:1571":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1571,"t":["ஆய்ந்தறி வார்கள் அமரர்வித் தியாதரர்","ஆய்ந்தறி யாவண்ணம் நின்ற அரனெறி","ஆய்ந்தறிந் தேனவன் சேவடி கைதொழ","ஆய்ந்தறிந் தேனிம்மை அம்மைகண் டேனே."],"p":"","r":"Gods and vidyadharas examine and know, yet Hara's path stands beyond their examining; I examined and knew to worship his red feet, and examining and knowing, I saw this life and the next.","n":"ஆய்ந்தறி opens every line: the same examining that fails the gods succeeds when it ends in worship. இம்மை அம்மை is this life and the other.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvadi","vazhipaadu","marumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:106"},"t:1572":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1572,"t":["அறியவொண் ணாதவ் வுடம்பின் பயனை","அறியவொண் ணாத அறுவகை யாக்கி","அறியவொண் ணாத அறுவகைக் கோசத்","தறியவொண் ணாததோர் அண்டம் பதிந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"The use of the body, which cannot be known, he made into six unknowable kinds; in the six unknowable sheaths an unknowable cosmos is set.","n":"அறியவொண்ணாத (cannot be known) opens every line. கோசம் is kosha, a sheath of the self; the usual count is five, so the six may be a different scheme. The point that the body holds a whole hidden cosmos is clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:106"},"t:1573":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1573,"t":["பத்திப் பணித்துப் பரவு மடிநல்கிச்","சுத்த வுரையால் துரிசறச் சோதித்துச்","சத்தும் அசத்துஞ் சதசத்துங் காட்டலாற்","சித்தம் இறையே சிவகுரு வாமே."],"p":"","r":"Making one bow in devotion, giving the feet to praise, testing with pure teaching so that fault is removed, and showing the real, the unreal and the real-unreal: because he does this, the guru who is mind and Lord is the Siva-guru.","n":"சத்து, அசத்து, சதசத்து (sat, asat, sadasat) are traditionally the Lord, the bonds and the soul, which is neither wholly real nor unreal but takes the nature of what it joins. சித்தம் இறையே is unclear: 'the mind is the Lord' or 'the guru whose mind is the Lord'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","pathi","paasam","pasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:107"},"t:1574":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1574,"t":["பாசத்தைக் கூட்டியே கட்டிப் பறித்திட்டு","நேசித்த காயம் விடிவித்து நேர்நேரே","கூசற்ற முத்தியிற் கூட்டலா நாட்டத்த","தாசற்ற சற்குரு அம்பலமாமே."],"p":"","r":"Gathering the bonds, binding them and plucking them out, freeing one from the cherished body, and directly joining one to release without shame: the faultless true guru with this aim is the hall itself.","n":"அம்பலம், the hall, is the dance hall of Chidambaram, so the guru is equated with the place of the Lord's dance; it may also be read as the open space of consciousness. கூசற்ற முத்தி is release without shrinking or shame.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","paasam","veedu","thirukkoothu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:107"},"t:1575":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1575,"t":["சித்திகள் எட்டோடுந் திண்சிவ மாக்கிய","சுத்தியும் எண்சத்தித் தூய்மையும் யோகத்துச்","சத்தியும்மந்திர சாதக போதமும்","பத்தியும் நாதன் அருளிற் பயிலுமே."],"p":"","r":"The eight siddhis, the purification that makes one firmly Siva, the purity of the eight powers, the power in yoga, the knowledge of mantra practice and devotion: all are learned by the Lord's grace.","n":"எண்சத்தி, the eight powers, may be the Lord's eight shaktis or the eight siddhis again. நாதன் here is the Lord, who in this chapter is the guru.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["siththi","thiruvarul","mandhiram","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:107"},"t:1576":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1576,"t":["எல்லா உலகிற்கும் அப்பாலோன் இப்பாலாய்","நல்லார் உள்ளத்து மிக்கருள் நல்கலால்","எல்லாரும் உய்யக்கொண் டிங்கே அளித்தலாற்","சொல்லார்ந்த நற்குருச் சுத்த சிவமே."],"p":"","r":"Because he who is beyond all the worlds comes here, gives abundant grace in the hearts of the good, and takes everyone to save them and protects them, the good guru praised in words is pure Siva.","n":"அப்பாலோன் இப்பாலாய், the one beyond becoming the one here, is the chapter's whole doctrine of the guru in four words.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["guru","sivam","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:107"},"t:1577":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1577,"t":["தேவனுஞ் சுத்த குருவும் உபாயத்துள்","யாவையும் மூன்றா யுனக்கண் டுரையாலே","மூவாப் பசுபாச மாற்றியே முத்திப்பால்","யாவையும் நல்குங் குருபரன் அன்புற்றே."],"p":"","r":"God, the pure guru and the means: by teaching you to see everything as these three, and removing the unaging bond of soul and fetter, the supreme guru in love gives everything that belongs to release.","n":"தேவன், சுத்த குரு, உபாயம் (God, guru, means) is one triad; the verse may also mean seeing everything as the three categories of Pati, pasu and pasam. மூவாப் பசுபாசம் is the soul's beginningless bondage.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","pasu","paasam","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:107"},"t:1578":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1578,"t":["சுத்த சிவன்குரு வாய்வந்து தூய்மைசெய்","தத்தனை நல்கருள் காணா அதிமூடர்","பொய்த்தகு கண்ணான் நமரென்பர் புண்ணியர்","அத்தன் இவனென் றடிபணிவாரே."],"p":"","r":"The utter fools who do not see the grace pure Siva gives by coming as guru and purifying say 'he is one of us, with eyes that deceive'; the meritorious bow at his feet, saying 'this is the Father'.","n":"பொய்த்தகு கண்ணான் நமர் is obscure: perhaps 'a man of ours, seen by deceived eyes', the fools mistaking the guru for an ordinary man. The verse sets two ways of seeing the same person.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","pedhaimai","nindhai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:107"},"t:1579":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1579,"t":["உண்மையிற் பொய்மை ஒழித்தலும் உண்மைப்பார்","திண்மையும் ஒண்மைச் சிவமாய அவ்வரன்","வண்மையும் எட்டெட்டுச் சித்தி மயக்கமும்","அண்ணல் அருளன்றி யாரறி வாரே."],"p":"","r":"Removing falsehood by truth, the firmness of the world of truth, the bounty of Hara who is bright Siva, and the bewilderment of the sixty-four siddhis: who knows these except by the Lord's grace?","n":"எட்டெட்டுச் சித்தி may be sixty-four or eight and eight. மயக்கம் may mean the siddhis' power to bewilder, a warning against them, or the bewildering wonder of them.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","siththi","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:107"},"t:1580":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1580,"t":["சிவனே சிவஞானி யாதலாற் சுத்த","சிவனே யெனஅடி சேரவல் லார்க்கு","நவமான தத்துவ நன்முத்தி நண்ணும்","பவமான தின்றிப் பரலோக மாமே."],"p":"","r":"Because Siva himself is the Siva-jnani, those who can reach his feet saying 'pure Siva' gain the good release of the new tattva; there is no more becoming, and there is the supreme world.","n":"சிவஞானி is the guru as knower of Siva, here said to be Siva himself. நவமான தத்துவம் may be 'the new tattva' or 'the ninefold tattva'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","sivam","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:107"},"t:1581":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1581,"t":["குருவே சிவமெனக் கூறினன் நந்தி","குருவே சிவமென் பதுகுறித் தோரார்","குருவே சிவனுமாய்க் கோனுமாய் நிற்கும்","குருவே யுரையுணர் வற்றதோர் கோவே."],"p":"","r":"Nandi said the guru is Siva; people do not grasp that the guru is Siva; the guru stands as Siva and as king; the guru is a king beyond word and thought.","n":"குருவே opens every line. The teaching is given on Nandi's authority, the guru of Tirumular's own line.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["guru","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:107"},"t:1582":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1582,"t":["சித்த யாவையுஞ் சிந்தித் திருந்திடும்","அத்தம் உணர்த்துவ தாகும் அருளாலே","சித்தம் யாவையுந் திண்சிவ மானக்கால்","அத்தனும் அவ்விடத் தேயமர்ந் தானே."],"p":"","r":"All that is thought abides in thinking; by grace one is made to know the meaning; when the whole mind becomes firmly Siva, the Father settles right there.","n":"சித்த யாவையும் is unclear and may be a slip for சித்தம் யாவையும், which opens line 3. அத்தம் is 'meaning' and அத்தன் 'the Father': the verse plays on the two.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["manam","thiruvarul","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:107"},"t:1583":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1583,"t":["தாநந்தி சீர்மையுட் சந்தித்த சீர்வைத்த","கோனந்தி யெந்தை குறிப்பறி வாரில்லை","வானந்தி யென்று மகிழும் ஒருவற்குத்","தானந்தி யங்கித் தனிச்சுட ராமே."],"p":"","r":"No one knows the intent of Nandi, the king and our father, who set the glory met within his own glory; to one who rejoices calling him the Nandi of the sky, he becomes the solitary flame of fire at dusk.","n":"The verse plays on நந்தி and அந்தி (dusk) and is largely opaque. அங்கித் தனிச்சுடர் (the lone flame of fire) recalls the inner light of yoga; the sense I give is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:107"},"t:1584":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1584,"t":["திருவாய சித்தியும் முத்தியும் சீர்மை","மருளா தருளும் மயக்கறும் வாய்மைப்","பொருளாய வேதாந்த போதமும் நாதன்","உருவாய் அருளாவிடிலோர ஒண்ணாதே."],"p":"","r":"The holy siddhi and release, the grace that grants glory without bewilderment, and the knowledge of Vedanta that is the truthful meaning ending delusion cannot be known unless the Lord takes form and gives grace.","n":"நாதன் உருவாய், the Lord taking form, is the guru: knowledge of Vedanta is not reached by study alone.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","vedaantham","guru","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:107"},"t:1585":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1585,"t":["பத்தியும் ஞானவை ராக்கிய மும்பர","சித்திக்கு வித்தாஞ் சிவோகமெ சேர்தலான்","முத்தியின் ஞான முளைத்தலால் அம்முளை","சத்தி யருள்தரில் தானெளி தாமே."],"p":"","r":"Devotion and the knowledge that is dispassion are the seed of the supreme siddhi; because one joins 'I am Siva', the knowledge of release sprouts; and if Shakti gives grace, that sprout grows easily.","n":"சிவோகம், 'I am Siva', is a meditation in which the self is identified with Siva; whether this is a practice or the state reached is unclear. The seed and sprout image echoes 1932.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","thuravu","saththi","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:107"},"t:1586":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1586,"t":["பின்னெய்த வைத்ததோர் இன்பப் பிறப்பினை","முன்எய்த வைத்த முதல்வனை எம்மிறை","தன்எய்துங் காலத்துத் தானே வெளிப்படு","மன்னெய்த வைத்த மனமது தானே."],"p":"","r":"Our Lord, the First One, who set before me the blissful birth to be attained later, reveals himself when his time comes; it is the mind he set to be held steady.","n":"இன்பப் பிறப்பு, a blissful birth, may be the new birth given by the guru's initiation. The last line is unclear: perhaps the Lord appears as, or in, the mind made steady.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","manam","kaalam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:107"},"t:1587":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1587,"t":["சிவமான ஞானந் தெளியவொண் சித்தி","சிவமான ஞானந் தெளியவொண் முத்தி","சிவமான ஞானஞ் சிவபரத்தே யேகச்","சிவமான ஞானஞ் சிவானந்த நல்குமே."],"p":"","r":"Clear Siva-knowledge is bright siddhi; clear Siva-knowledge is bright release; Siva-knowledge goes as one into the supreme Siva; Siva-knowledge gives Siva's bliss.","n":"சிவமான ஞானம் opens every line. The verse makes Siva-knowledge itself the attainments that 1575 and 1584 list separately.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","siththi","veedu","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:107"},"t:1588":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1588,"t":["அறிந்துணர்ந் தேனிவ் வகலிட முற்றுஞ்","செறிந்துணர்ந் தோதித் திருவருள் பெற்றேன்","மறந்தொழிந் தேன்மதி மாண்டவர் வாழ்க்கை","பிறந்தொழிந் தேனிப் பிறவியை நானே."],"p":"","r":"I have known and understood this whole wide world; understanding closely and reciting, I gained holy grace; I have forgotten and left the life of those whose wits are dead; I was born and have ended this birth.","n":"பிறந்தொழிந்தேன் means 'I was born and am done', so this birth is the last. மதி மாண்டவர் (those whose understanding has died) are the worldly the speaker has left behind.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","piravi","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:107"},"t:1589":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1589,"t":["தரிக்கின்ற பல்லுயிர்க் கெல்லாந் தலைவன்","இருக்கின்ற தன்மையை ஏதும் உணரார்","பிரிக்கின்ற இந்தப் பிணக்கறுத் தெல்லாங்","கருக்கொண்ட ஈசனைக் கண்டுகொண் டேனே."],"p":"","r":"People do not understand at all how the Lord of all the many lives he sustains is present; cutting away this dividing quarrel, I saw the Lord who holds everything as in a womb.","n":"பிரிக்கின்ற பிணக்கு, the quarrel that divides, may be the sense of separation between soul and Lord or the sects' disputes of chapter 106. கருக்கொண்ட, having conceived, makes the Lord the womb of all.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pathi","uyir","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:107"},"t:1590":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1590,"t":["இசைந்தெழும் அன்பில் எழுந்த படியே","பசைந்தெழும் ஈசரைப் பாசத்துள் ஏகச்","சிவந்த குருவந்து சென்னிகை வைக்க","உவந்த குருபதம் உள்ளத் துவந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"As love rose in harmony, the guru red as Siva came and placed his hand on my head, so that I went to the Lord who rises with tenderness within the bond, and the guru's joyful feet came gladly into my heart.","n":"சென்னி கை வைக்க, placing the hand on the head, is traditionally taken as initiation by touch (hasta-diksha). பாசத்துள் ஏக is unclear: going within the bond, or the bond going.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","thiruvadi","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:108"},"t:1591":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1591,"t":["தாடந்த போதே தலைதந்த எம்மிறை","வாள்தந்த ஞான வலியையுந் தந்திட்டு","வீடந்த மின்றியே யாள்கென விட்டருட்","பாடின் முடிவைத்துப் பார்வந்து தந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"When he gave his feet, our Lord took my head; he gave me the strength of knowledge that is a sword and, leaving me with 'rule without end of release', set a crown on me in grace's glory and gave me the world.","n":"தலைதந்த can mean the Lord gave headship or took the head (the ego) in exchange; the exchange of feet for head is a common devotional image. பாடு and பார்வந்து தந்தது are unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvadi","gnaanam","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:108"},"t:1592":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1592,"t":["தானவ னாகிச் சொரூபத் துவந்திட்டு","ஆன சொரூபங்கள் நான்கும் அகற்றின","ஏனைய முத்திரை ஈந்தாண்ட நன்நந்தி","தானடி முற்சூட்டித் தாபித்த துண்மையே."],"p":"","r":"Becoming himself, coming into my true form, he removed the four forms that had arisen; good Nandi, who ruled me giving the other seal, first crowned me with his feet and established me: this is true.","n":"சொரூபம், the self's own form, and the four சொரூபங்கள் are technical; they may be the four states preceding the true form. முத்திரை may be a ritual gesture given in initiation or a seal of identity.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","thiruvadi","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:108"},"t:1593":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1593,"t":["உரையற் றுணர்வற் றுயிர்பர மற்றுத்","திரையற்ற நீர்போல் சிவமாதல் தீர்த்துக்","கரையற்ற சத்தாதி நான்குங் கடந்த","சொரூபத் திருத்தனன் சொல்லிறந் தோமே."],"p":"","r":"Word ended, feeling ended, soul and supreme ended; like water without waves, he did away even with becoming Siva and set me in the true form beyond the shoreless four beginning with being; we passed beyond words.","n":"சிவமாதல் தீர்த்து, ending even the becoming-Siva, goes past any state still named. சத்தாதி நான்கு (the four beginning with sat) are unclear. Sengalvaraya notes a near-identical opening in Kandar Alangaram 61.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","veedu","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:108"},"t:1594":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1594,"t":["குரவன் உயிர்முச் சொரூபமுங் கைக்கொண்","டரிய பொருள்முத் திரையாகக் கொண்டு","பெரிய பிரானடி நந்தி பேச்சற்","றுருகிட என்னையங் குய்யக்கொண் டானே."],"p":"","r":"The guru took the soul's three forms and took the rare reality as a seal; Nandi, the feet of the great Lord, wordless, took me there and saved me so that I melted.","n":"உயிர் முச்சொரூபம், the soul's three forms, and பொருள் முத்திரை are technical terms I can't fix.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","uyir","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:108"},"t:1595":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1595,"t":["பேச்சற்ற இன்பத்துப் பேரானந் தத்திலே","மாச்சற்ற என்னைச் சிவமாக்கி மாள்வித்துக்","காச்சற்ற சோதி கடன்மூன்றுங் கைக்கொண்டு","வாச்ச புகழ்மாளத் தாள்தந்து மன்னுமே."],"p":"","r":"In the great bliss beyond speech, he made me, freed of stain, Siva and made me die; the flawless light took over the three debts and, so that the fame that had come died away, gave his feet and abides.","n":"மாள்வித்து, causing to die, is the death of the self. கடன் மூன்று (three debts) may be the three malas or the three traditional debts; வாச்ச புகழ் மாள, the fame that came dying away, is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aanandham","sivam","malam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:108"},"t:1596":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1596,"t":["இதயத்தும் நாட்டத்தும் என்றன் சிரத்தும்","பதிவித்த பாதப் பராபரன் நந்தி","கதிவைத்த வாறும் மெய்காட் டியவாறும்","விதிவைத்த வாறும் விளம்பவொண் ணாதே."],"p":"","r":"How Nandi, the supreme, who set his feet in my heart, my eyes and my head, gave me the goal, how he showed the truth and how he set the rule cannot be spoken.","n":"Heart, eyes and head are the places of the guru's touch and gaze in initiation as tradition reads it; the verse ends, like 1593, in what cannot be said.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["guru","thiruvadi","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:108"},"t:1597":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1597,"t":["திருவடி வைத்தென் சிரத்துருள் நோக்கிப்","பெருவடி வைத்தந்த பேர்நந்தி தன்னைக்","குருவடி விற்கண்ட கோனையெங் கோவைக்","கருவழி வாற்றிடக் கண்டுகொண் டேனே."],"p":"","r":"Placing his holy feet on my head and looking with grace, I saw great Nandi who took a great form, the king, our king, in the form of the guru, so that the path to the womb was drained away.","n":"கருவழி வாற்றிட, draining the womb-path, is ending rebirth. The guru's form and Nandi's great form are one in what the speaker saw.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvadi","guru","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:108"},"t:1598":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1598,"t":["திருவடி ஞானஞ் சிவமாக்கு விக்குந்","திருவடி ஞானஞ் சிவலோகஞ் சேர்க்குந்","திருவடி ஞானஞ் சிறைமல மீட்குந்","திருவடி ஞானமே திண்சித்தி முத்தியே."],"p":"","r":"Knowledge of the holy feet makes one Siva; knowledge of the holy feet brings one to Sivaloka; knowledge of the holy feet frees one from the prison of impurity; knowledge of the holy feet is itself firm siddhi and release.","n":"திருவடி ஞானம் opens every line. It may mean knowledge of the feet or the knowledge that the feet are; the verse lets both stand.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvadi","gnaanam","malam","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:108"},"t:1599":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1599,"t":["மேல்வைத்த வாறுசெய் யாவிடின் மேல்வினை","மால்வைத்த சிந்தையை மாயம தாக்கிடும்","பால்வைத்த சென்னிப் படரொளி வானவன்","தாள்வைத்த வாறு தரிப்பித்த வாறே."],"p":"","r":"If one does not do as he set above, the deeds to come will turn a mind dazed by delusion into illusion; this is how the sky-dweller of spreading light, with milk on his head, set his feet and made me bear them.","n":"மேல்வைத்தவாறு (as he set above, or as set out above) and மேல்வினை (later karma, or karma on top) play on மேல். பால் on the head may be the moon or the Ganga. The link between the two halves is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","maayai","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:108"},"t:1600":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1600,"t":["கழலார் கமலத் திருவடி என்னும்","நிழல்சேரப் பெற்றேன் நெடுமால் அறியா","அழல்சேரும் அங்கியுள் ஆதிப் பிரானுங்","குழல்சேரும் என்னுயிர்க் கூடுங் குலைத்தே."],"p":"","r":"I gained the shade called the anklet-bearing lotus holy feet; the First Lord within the fire that tall Mal could not know will also break apart the nest of my life where the coil dwells.","n":"நெடுமால் அறியா அழல் recalls the story of the column of fire whose end Vishnu could not find. குழல் சேரும் கூடு (the nest where the coil or tube joins) is taken as the body with kundalini or the central channel; breaking it may mean release from the body.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvadi","uyir","kundalini"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:108"},"t:1601":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1601,"t":["முடிமன்ன ராகிமூ வுலகம தாள்வர்","அடிமன்னர் இன்பத் தளவில்லை கேட்கின்","முடிமன்ன ராய்நின்ற தேவர்கள் ஈசன்","குடிமன்ன ராய்க்குற்ற மற்றுநின் றாரே."],"p":"","r":"Crowned kings rule the three worlds, but the bliss of those who are kings of the feet has no measure, if you ask; the gods who stood as crowned kings stood free of fault as subjects of the Lord.","n":"முடிமன்னர் and அடிமன்னர், kings of the crown and kings of the feet, set head against feet: kingship at the Lord's feet outranks the gods' own crowns.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvadi","inbam","arasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:108"},"t:1602":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1602,"t":["வைத்தேன் அடிகள் மனத்தினுள் ளேநான்","பொய்த்தே யெரியும் புலன்வழி போகாமல்","எய்த்தேன் உழலும் இருவினை மாற்றிட்டு","மெய்த்தேன் அறிந்தே னவ்வேதத்தின் அந்தமே."],"p":"","r":"I set his feet within my mind so as not to go by the senses that burn falsely; worn out, I turned away the two whirling deeds and knew the true honey, the end of the Veda.","n":"இருவினை, the two deeds, are good and bad karma, both binding. புலன் வழி, the way of the senses, is the opposite of keeping the feet in mind. மெய்த்தேன் puns on true honey and 'I attained truth'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvadi","vinai","adakkam","vedaantham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:108"},"t:1603":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1603,"t":["அடிசார லாம்அண்ண ல்பாத மிரண்டும்","முடிசார வைத்தனர் முன்னை முனிவர்","படிசார்ந்த இன்பப் பழவடி வெள்ளக்","குடிசார் நெறிகூடி நிற்பவர் கொள்கையே."],"p":"","r":"The sages of old placed on their heads the Lord's two feet, to which one may cling; this is the principle of those who stand joined to the path of those who dwell in the ancient flood of bliss on earth.","n":"Lines 3 and 4 are compressed: படிசார்ந்த (come down to earth, or in due order) and குடிசார் நெறி (the path of the household of devotees) are uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvadi","adiyaar","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:108"},"t:1604":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1604,"t":["மந்திரமாவதும் மாமருந் தாவதுந்","தந்திர மாவதுந் தானங்க ளாவதுஞ்","சுந்தர மாவதுந் தூய்நெறி யாவதும்","எந்தை பிரான்தன் இணையடி தானே."],"p":"","r":"What is mantra, what is great medicine, what is tantra, what are gifts, what is beauty and what is the pure path: it is our Father Lord's pair of feet.","n":"தானங்கள் may be gifts (dana) or holy places (sthana). The verse folds every religious means, ritual and textual, into the feet.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvadi","mandhiram","marundhu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:108"},"t:1605":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1605,"t":["நீங்காச் சிவானந்த ஞேயத்தே நின்றிடப்","பாங்கான பாசம் படரா படரினும்","ஆங்கார நீங்கி யதநிலை நிற்கவே","நீங்கா அமுத நிலைபெற லாமே."],"p":"","r":"For one who stands in the known, the unfailing bliss of Siva, the bonds that cling do not spread, and even if they spread, once the ego is gone and one holds that state, the unfailing state of nectar is reached.","n":"ஞேயம், what is to be known, is here Siva himself as the bliss that does not leave. The verse allows that bonds may still arise (படரினும்) and makes the loss of ஆங்காரம், the ego, the condition. அமுத நிலை may be the deathless state or the yogic nectar of earlier chapters; both readings stand.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aanandham","paasam","amudham","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:109"},"t:1606":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1606,"t":["ஞேயத்தே நின்றோர்க்கு ஞானாதி நின்றிடும்","ஞேயத்தின் ஞாதுரு ஞேயத்தில் வீடாகும்","ஞேயத்தின் ஞேயத்தை ஞேயத்தை யுற்றவர்","ஆயத்தில் நின்ற அறிவறி வாரே."],"p":"","r":"For those who stand in the known, knowledge and the rest follow; the knower of the known finds release in the known, and those who reach the known of the known know the knowledge that stands in the gathering.","n":"The verse turns ஞேயம் over five times, and the phrases (ஞேயத்தின் ஞாதுரு, ஞேயத்தின் ஞேயம்) resist a fixed sense. ஆயம் may be the assembly of the tattvas, the soul's faculties, or the company of the knowers; I cannot fix which.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","veedu","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:109"},"t:1607":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1607,"t":["தானென் றவனென் றிரண்டாகும் தத்துவந்","தானென் றவனென் றிரண்டுந் தனிற்கண்டு","தானென்ற பூவை யவனடி சாத்தினால்","நானென் றவனென்கை நல்லதொன் றன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The reality appears as two, 'self' and 'He'; seeing both within oneself and offering the flower called 'self' at His feet, one finds that saying 'I' and 'He' is no longer right.","n":"The self offered as a flower turns outer worship into an inner act. The last line, நல்லது ஒன்று அன்றே, can be read as 'is no good thing' (duality is wrong) or as the rhetorical 'is it not the one good thing'; the first fits the verse's movement from two to one, but the second is not ruled out.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pasu","pathi","vazhipaadu","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:109"},"t:1608":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1608,"t":["வைச்சன வாறாறு மாற்றியெனவைத்து","மெச்சப் பரன்றன் வியாத்துவ மேலிட்டு","நிச்சய மாக்கிச் சிவமாக்கி ஞேயத்தால்","அச்சங் கெடுத்தென்னை யாண்டனன் நந்தியே."],"p":"","r":"Nandi changed the thirty-six tattvas set in me and set me anew, laid the Supreme's all-pervading nature over me to be praised, made it sure, made me Siva, and through the known destroyed my fear and took me as his own.","n":"வாறாறு is read as ஆறாறு, six sixes, the thirty-six tattvas. வியாத்துவம் is vyaptva, pervasion. Nandi is both Tirumular's guru and a name of Siva; the verse lets the two stand together.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["guru","thaththuvam","sivam","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:109"},"t:1609":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1609,"t":["முன்னை யறிவறியாதஅம் மூடர்போற்","பின்னை யறிவுஅறி யாமையைப் பேதித்தான்","தன்aன யறியப் பரனாக்கித் தற்சிவத்து","தென்னை யறிவித் திருந்தனன் நந்தியே."],"p":"","r":"Nandi broke through the ignorance in my later knowledge, which was like that of fools who never knew, made me the Supreme so that I might know myself, and stayed, making me know myself in his own Siva-state.","n":"The first two lines are hard: முன்னை அறிவு and பின்னை அறிவு may be the soul's knowledge before and after the guru's work, or before and after birth. தற்சிவம் is taken as Siva's own state. The third line is misprinted in the source.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["guru","pedhaimai","sivam","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:109"},"t:1610":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1610,"t":["காணாத கண்ணுடன் கேளாத கேள்வியுங்","கோணாத போகமுங் கூடாத கூட்டமும்","நாணாத நாணமும் நாதாந்த போதமுங்","காணா யெனவந்து காட்டினன் நந்தியே."],"p":"","r":"Nandi came and showed me, saying 'see': an eye that does not see, a hearing that does not hear, an enjoyment that does not swerve, a union that is no joining, a modesty that feels no shame, and the awareness at nada's end.","n":"Each phrase pairs a faculty with its negation, so the state shown is one where seeing, hearing, enjoying and joining go on without the senses or a second party. நாதாந்தம், the end of nada, is a technical term for the stage beyond primal sound.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","naadham","gnaanam","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:109"},"t:1611":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1611,"t":["மோனங்கை வந்தோர்க்கு முத்தியும் கைகூடும்","மோனங்கை வந்தோர்க்குச் சித்தியும் முன்னிற்கும்","மோனங்கை வந்தூமை யாமொழி முற்றுங்காண்","மோனங்கை வந்தைந் கருமமும் முன்னுமே."],"p":"","r":"For those to whom silence has come, release comes too, the powers stand before them, all speech ends in a mute word, and the five acts come forward.","n":"மோனம் கைவந்தோர் is those for whom silence has become natural, not merely those who keep a vow of speech. Line 3 (வந்தூமை யாமொழி) is read as ஊமையாம் மொழி, a speech that is mute; it can also be split to mean they become mute and speech ends. That the five acts, the Lord's work, 'come forward' for them is a strong claim and may mean they share in it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mounam","veedu","siththi","aindhozhil"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:109"},"t:1612":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1612,"t":["முத்திரை மூன்றின் முடிந்தது மூன்றன்பால்","வைத்த கலைகால் நான்மடங் கால்மாற்றி","உய்த்தவத் தானந்தத் தொண்குரு பாதத்தே","பெத்த மறுத்தோர் பிறந்திற வாரே."],"p":"","r":"Those who, through the three mudras, turned back the kala and breath set in the three by a fourfold measure, and cut off their bondage at the bright guru's feet in that bliss, are neither born nor die.","n":"The three mudras, the three in which they end, and the fourfold measure (நான்மடங்கு) of kala and breath are technical and unnamed. பெத்தம் is baddha, the bound state, which the verse sets against release. Only the conclusion, freedom from birth and death at the guru's feet, is plain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["muththirai","praanan","guru","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:109"},"t:1613":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1613,"t":["மேலைச் சொரூபங்கள் மூன்று மிகுசத்தி","பாலித்த முத்திரை பற்றும் பரஞானி","ஆலித்த நட்டமே ஞேயம் புகுந்தற்ற","மூலச் சொரூபன் மொழிஞா துருவனே."],"p":"","r":"The supreme knower holds the mudra nurtured by abundant Shakti, the three higher forms; entering the known, the resounding dance, he stands as the root-form, the one called the knower.","n":"மேலைச் சொரூபங்கள் மூன்று are not named; they may be three forms of Siva or three states beyond the waking ones. ஆலித்த நட்டம் (the roaring or resounding dance) is made the known itself, so the dance is what the knower enters. மூலச் சொரூபன் may be the knower or Siva; the verse ends by naming him ஞாதுரு.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","muththirai","thirukkoothu","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:109"},"t:1614":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1614,"t":["இறப்பும் பிறப்பும் இருமையும் நீங்கித்","துறக்குந் தவங்கண்ட சோதிப் பிரானை","மறப்பில ராய்நித்தம் வாய்மொழி வார்கட்","கறப்பதி காட்டும் அமரர் பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"To those who, never forgetting, daily speak of the radiant Lord who saw the tapas of renouncing free of death, birth and the two deeds, the Lord of the immortals shows the abode of dharma.","n":"இருமை, the two, is usually the two kinds of deed, good and bad, though it can mean this world and the next. அறப்பதி may be the abode of dharma or Siva's realm as dharma's seat. The verse joins renunciation to unforgetting speech of the Lord rather than to bodily austerity.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","sivam","piravi","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:110"},"t:1615":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1615,"t":["பிறந்தும் இறந்தும்பல் பேதைமை யாலே","மறந்து பலஇருள் நீங்க மறைந்து","சிறந்த சிவனருள் சேர்பரு வத்துத்","துறந்த வுயிர்க்குச் சுடரொளி யாமே."],"p":"","r":"The soul that is born and dies again through much folly, forgetting, finds that once it has renounced, at the ripe time for joining Siva's grace, its many darknesses vanish and grace becomes its blazing light.","n":"சேர்பருவம், the season of joining, is traditionally the soul's ripeness for grace, when its malas are mature. The verse makes renunciation the soul's part and the light grace's, without saying which comes first.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","thiruvarul","piravi","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:110"},"t:1616":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1616,"t":["அறவன் பிறப்பிலி யாரும் இலாதான்","உறைவது காட்டகம் உண்பது பிச்சை","துறவனுங் கண்டீர் துறந்தவர் தம்மைப்","பிறவி யறுத்திடும் பித்தன்கண் டீரே"],"p":"","r":"The righteous one, unborn, with no one of his own, dwells in the burning ground and eats alms; see, he too is a renouncer, the mad one who cuts off the births of those who renounce.","n":"காட்டகம் is the burning ground or the wild; பிச்சை and பித்தன் are Siva's familiar epithets as beggar and madman. The verse makes Siva the model renouncer, so the renouncer imitates the Lord rather than merely obeying him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","thuravu","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:110"},"t:1617":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1617,"t":["நெறியைப் படைத்தான் நெருஞ்சில் படைத்தான்","நெறியில் வழுவின் நெருஞ்சில் முட்பாயும்","நெறியில் வழுவா தியங்கவல் லார்க்கு","நெறியின் நெருஞ்சில்முட் பாயகி லாவே."],"p":"","r":"He made the path and made the thorn; if you slip from the path the thorn pierces, but for those who can walk without slipping, the path's thorns do not pierce.","n":"நெருஞ்சில் is the caltrop, a low creeper with spiked burrs that lame the barefoot walker beside a path. The plain reading is that suffering comes from straying, not from the path itself; the verse does not say whether the Lord sends the thorn as punishment or merely set it there.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ozhukkam","iraivan","idumbai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:110"},"t:1618":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1618,"t":["கேடும் கடமையுங் கேட்டுவந் தைவரும்","நாடி வளைந்தது நான்கட வேனலேன்","ஆடல் விடையுடை அண்ணல் திருவடி","கூடுந் தவஞ்செய்த கொள்கையன் தானே."],"p":"","r":"The five came demanding ruin and their dues and closed in on me; I owe them nothing, for mine is the resolve of tapas that reaches the holy feet of the lord whose mount is the dancing bull.","n":"The image is of creditors or tax-collectors: கடமை is a due or levy, and நான் கடவேன் அலேன் 'I am not a debtor'. The five (ஐவர்) are traditionally the five senses, which the verse does not name.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","thavam","adakkam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:110"},"t:1619":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1619,"t":["உழவன் உழஉழ வானம் வழங்க","உழவன் உழவினிற் பூத்த குவளை","உழவன் உழத்தியர் கண்ணொக்கும் என்றிட்","டுழவன் அதனை யுழவொழிந் தானே."],"p":"","r":"The ploughman ploughed and the sky gave rain; a blue lily bloomed in his furrows, and saying it was like the eyes of the ploughwomen, the ploughman gave up ploughing.","n":"A riddle with no key in the verse. Read symbolically, the ploughman is the seeker, the ploughing his discipline, the rain grace and the lily the vision that makes further effort needless. Read plainly, he is distracted by a reminder of women's eyes and abandons his work. Its place in a chapter on renunciation favours the first, but both stand.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","uzhavu","thavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:110"},"t:1620":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1620,"t":["மேல்துறந் தண்ணல் விளங்கொளி கூற்றுவன்","நாள்துறந் தார்க்கவன் நண்ப னவாவிலி","கார்துறந் தார்க்கவன் கண்ணுத லாய்நிற்கும்","பார்துறந் தார்க்கே பதஞ்செய லாமே."],"p":"","r":"For those who renounce what is above, the Lord is a shining light; for those who renounce the day set by Death, he is a friend without desire; for those who renounce darkness, he stands as the one with an eye in his forehead; only those who renounce the earth can reach his state.","n":"The verse grades renunciations by what is given up (heaven, death's day, darkness, the earth) and pairs each with an aspect of Siva. மேல் and கார் are not fixed: மேல் may be the heavens, கார் dark ignorance or the rain cloud. The climax puts giving up the earth highest.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","sivam","avaa","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:110"},"t:1621":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1621,"t":["நாகமும் ஒன்று படம்ஐந்து நாலது","போகமுட் புற்றிற் பொருந்தி நிறைந்தது","ஆக மிரண்டும் படம்விரித் தாட்டொழிந்","தேகப் படம்செய் துடம்பிட மாமே."],"p":"","r":"There is one serpent with five hoods, settled and filling the anthill of enjoyment; when its two bodies stop spreading their hoods and dancing and it makes a single hood, the body becomes its proper dwelling.","n":"The serpent is traditionally the soul with its five senses as hoods, living in the anthill of the body; the 'two bodies' (ஆகம் இரண்டு) may be the gross and subtle bodies, or the two breaths. A yogic reading takes the serpent as kundalini. நாலது in line 1 is unclear. Either way the image is of many hoods drawn into one.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pasu","yaakkai","adakkam","kundalini"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:110"},"t:1622":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1622,"t":["அகன்றார் வழிமுதல் ஆதிப் பிரானும்","இவன்றா னெனநின் றெளியனும் அல்லன்","சிவன்றாள் பலபல சீவனு மாகும்","நயன்றான் வரும்வழி நாமறி யோமே."],"p":"","r":"The primal Lord, the source of the way of those who have left all, is not so easy that one can stand and say 'this is he'; Siva's feet become many, many souls, and we do not know the way his goodness comes.","n":"அகன்றார் is those who have gone away, the renouncers. சிவன் தாள் பலபல சீவனும் ஆகும் is hard: it may mean that Siva's feet are present in many souls, or that many souls reach them. நயன், goodness or grace, is left unexplained, which is the verse's point.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","thuravu","pasu","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:110"},"t:1623":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1623,"t":["தூம்பு திறந்தன ஒன்பது வாய்தலும்","ஆம்பற் குழலியின் கஞ்சுளிப் பட்டது","வேம்பேறி நோக்கினன் மீகாமன் கூரையிற்","கூம்பேறிக் கோயிலிற் பூக்கின்ற வாறே."],"p":"","r":"The nine gateways opened their channels and something was caught in the wallet of the one with lily-like tresses; the helmsman climbed the neem tree and looked: this is how it flowers in the temple, climbing the mast on the roof.","n":"Only the nine gates are clear: the nine openings of the body. ஆம்பல் குழலி (one whose tresses are like the water-lily, or a pipe), கஞ்சுளி (a mendicant's bag), the helmsman (மீகாமன்) on the neem, and the mast climbing to the temple are traditionally read as the breath or the yogi's awareness rising to the crown of the head, but the verse gives no key.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","yogam","praanan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:110"},"t:1624":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1624,"t":["ஒடுங்கி நிலைபெற்ற வுத்தமர் உள்ளம்","நடுங்குவ தில்லை நமனுமங் கில்லை","இடும்பையும் இல்லை இராப்பகல் இல்லை","படும்பயன் இல்லை பற்றுவிட் டோர்க்கே."],"p":"","r":"For those excellent ones who have withdrawn and become steady and let go of attachment, the heart does not tremble, Yama is not there, nor suffering, nor night and day, nor any fruit of deeds to undergo.","n":"ஒடுங்கி is withdrawal, of the senses into the mind or the mind into stillness. The list of absences is built so that letting go of பற்று, named only in the last word, is the cause of all of them. இராப்பகல் இல்லை may also be a yogic reference to a state beyond the sun and moon breaths.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","pattru","kootru","adakkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:111"},"t:1625":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1625,"t":["எம்மா ருயிரும் இருநிலத் தோற்றமுஞ்","செம்மா தவத்தின் செயலின் பெருமையும்","அம்மான் திருவருள் பெற்றவர்க் கல்லா(து)","இம்மா தவத்தின் இயல்பறி யாரே."],"p":"","r":"None but those who have received the Lord's holy grace know the nature of this great tapas, or all the precious lives and the appearing of the wide earth, or the greatness of what true tapas does.","n":"The verse makes grace prior to tapas: even knowing what tapas is depends on it. The first line, all lives and the appearing of the world, can be read as more things grace reveals or as the effects of tapas itself; the grammar allows both.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","thiruvarul","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:111"},"t:1626":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1626,"t":["பிறப்பறி யார்பல பிச்சைசெய் மாந்தர்","சிறப்பொடு வேண்டிய செல்வம் பெறுவர்","மறப்பில ராகிய மாதவஞ் செய்வார்","பிறப்பினை நீக்கும் பெருமைபெற் றாரே."],"p":"","r":"People who ask for many things, not knowing what birth is, get the wealth they wanted with honour; those who do great tapas without forgetting win the greatness of ending birth.","n":"பிச்சைசெய் மாந்தர் may be beggars in the plain sense or worshippers who beg favours of the Lord; the contrast is between asking for goods and the tapas that seeks no goods but an end to birth. The verse grants that asking works; it ranks it lower.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","piravi","selvam","iravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:111"},"t:1627":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1627,"t":["இருந்து வருந்தி எழிறவஞ் செய்யும்","பெருந்தன்மை யாளரைப் பேதிக்க வென்றே","இருந்திந் திரனே யெவரே வரினுந்","திருந்துந்தஞ் சிந்தை சிவனவன் பாலே."],"p":"","r":"Whoever comes to unsettle the great ones who sit enduring hardship in their fair tapas, even Indra himself, their minds are set right upon Siva.","n":"Indra sending distractions to break an ascetic's tapas is a familiar puranic motif; the verse answers it by placing the ascetic's steadiness in Siva rather than in his own strength.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","thitpam","sivam","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:111"},"t:1628":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1628,"t":["கரந்துங் கரந்திலன் கண்ணுக்குந் தோன்றான்","பரந்த சடையன் பசும்பொன் நிறத்தன்","அருந்தவர்க் கல்லால் அணுகலு மாகான்","விரைந்து தொழப்படும் வெண்மதி யானே."],"p":"","r":"Hidden, yet not hidden, he does not appear to the eye: he of the spreading matted hair and the colour of fresh gold, wearing the white moon, cannot be approached except by those of rare tapas, and is to be worshipped without delay.","n":"கரந்தும் கரந்திலன், hiding yet not hidden, is a paradox common in Saiva hymns: present everywhere but not seen. The verse gives a full iconographic image while denying that the eye sees it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","thavam","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:111"},"t:1629":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1629,"t":["பின்னெய்த வைத்ததோர் இன்பப் பிறப்பினை","முன்னெய்த வைத்த முதல்வனை எம்மிறை","தன்னெய்துங் காலத்துத் தானே வெளிப்படும்","மன்னெய்த வைத்த மனமது தானே."],"p":"","r":"The First, our Lord, who set the blissful birth to be reached later and set what must come first, reveals himself when the time to reach him comes; it is the mind, set to reach him firmly, that does this.","n":"இன்பப் பிறப்பு, a birth of joy, may be a heavenly birth or the birth in which release comes; what is 'set to come first' (முன்னெய்த வைத்த) is not named, and in this chapter may be tapas. The last line's மன் எய்த may be 'to reach firmly' or 'to reach the king'. The chain of 'set' (வைத்த) makes the Lord the arranger of the soul's sequence.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","manam","kaalam","thavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:111"},"t:1630":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1630,"t":["அமைச்சரும் ஆனைக் குழாமும் அரசும்","பகைத்தெழும் பூசலுட் பட்டார் நடுவே","அமைத்ததோர் ஞானமும் ஆத்தமும் நோக்கி","இமைத்தழி யாதிருந் தார்தவத் தாரே."],"p":"","r":"Caught in the midst of the uproar when ministers, troops of elephants and kings rise in enmity, those who kept their gaze on the knowledge they had set up and on the trustworthy one, and stayed unblinking and unharmed, are the people of tapas.","n":"ஆத்தம் is āpta, the trustworthy one or the trustworthy word, traditionally the Lord or the scripture he gives. The scene is literal war, and the claim is that the tapasvin's stillness holds even there. இமைத்தழியாது can be 'without blinking' or 'without perishing in a blink'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","thitpam","gnaanam","anjaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:111"},"t:1631":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1631,"t":["சாத்திரம் ஓதுஞ் சதுர்களை விட்டுநீர்","மாத்திரைப் போது மறித்துள்ளே நோக்குமின்","பார்த்தவப் பார்வை பசுமரத் தாணிபோல்","ஆர்த்த பிறவி அகலவிட் டோடுமே."],"p":"","r":"Leave the cleverness of reciting the shastras and, for the space of a moment, turn back and look within; that look, fixed like a nail driven into green wood, drives off the births that bind.","n":"மாத்திரைப் போது is the length of a single measure, the shortest unit of time. பசுமரத்து ஆணி, a nail in green wood, is the proverbial image of what goes in easily and holds firm. The verse sets a moment's inward look against a lifetime of scriptural talk.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","piravi","kalvi","thiyaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:111"},"t:1632":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1632,"t":["தவம்வேண்டு ஞானந் தலைபட வேண்டில்","தவம்வேண்டா ஞான சமாதிகை கூடில்","தவம்வேண்டா மச்ச கசமார்க்கத் தோர்க்கு","தவம்வேண்டா மாற்றந் தனையறி யாரே."],"p":"","r":"Tapas is needed if one is to reach knowledge, but not needed once the samadhi of knowledge is in hand; tapas is not needed by those on the path of the companion, and they do not know the word that says 'no tapas'.","n":"Lines 1 and 2 are clear: tapas is a means that falls away. Line 3 (மச்ச கசமார்க்கத்தோர்க்கு) is probably corrupt; if it is சகமார்க்கம், it is the 'path of the companion', traditionally the yoga stage of the Saiva fourfold path. Line 4 is obscure: it may mean they don't need tapas because they are past the debate, or that they never learned the teaching that tapas is unneeded.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","gnaanam","samaadhi","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:111"},"t:1633":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1633,"t":["ஓதலும் வேண்டாம் உயிர்க்குயி ருள்ளுற்றாற்","காதலும் வேண்டாமெய்க் காய மிடம்கண்டாற்","சாதலும் வேண்டாஞ் சமாதிகை கூடினாற்","போதலும் வேண்டாம் புலன்வழி போகார்க்கே."],"p":"","r":"Recitation is not needed if the Life of life has come within; desire is not needed if one sees the true body as its place; dying is not needed if samadhi is in hand; going about is not needed for those who do not follow the senses.","n":"Each line pairs a practice or urge with the attainment that makes it needless. மெய்க்காயம் இடம் கண்டால் may mean seeing the body as the Lord's dwelling, in line with Tirumular's body-as-temple teaching. சாதலும் வேண்டாம் may be that death is overcome or that one need not seek death; போதல் may be pilgrimage.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["samaadhi","kalvi","yaakkai","adakkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:112"},"t:1634":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1634,"t":["கத்தவும் வேண்டாங் கருத்தறிந் தாறினாற்","சத்தமும் வேண்டாஞ் சமாதிகை கூடினாற்","சுத்தமும் வேண்டாந் துடக்கற்று நிற்றலாற்","சித்தமும் வேண்டாஞ் செயலற்றிருக்கிலே."],"p":"","r":"No need to cry out once one has grasped the meaning and grown calm; no need of sounded chant once samadhi is in hand; no need of purification for one who stands free of pollution; no need even of the mind for one who rests without action.","n":"சத்தம் may be audible chant or sound in general. துடக்கு is ritual pollution (as from birth or death) and also attachment, so line 3 can be ritual or moral. The last line goes furthest: the mind itself is put among the things that become needless.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["samaadhi","thooimai","vazhipaadu","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:112"},"t:1635":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1635,"t":["விளைவறி வார்பண்டை மெய்த்தவஞ் செய்வார்","விளைவறி வார்பண்டை மெய்யுரை செய்வார்","விளைவறி வார்பண்டை மெய்யறஞ் செய்வார்","விளைவறி வார்விண்ணின் மண்ணின்மிக் காரே."],"p":"","r":"Those who know the outcome do the true tapas of old, speak the true word of old and do the true dharma of old; those who know the outcome surpass those of heaven and of earth.","n":"விளைவு, what grows from an act, is the fruit of deeds; to know it is to see where each act leads. பண்டை, of old, presents the right way as ancient rather than new.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","vinai","vaaimai","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:112"},"t:1636":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1636,"t":["கூடித் தவஞ்செய்து கண்டேன் குரைகழல்","தேடித் தவஞ்செய்து கண்டேன் சிவகதி","வாடித் தவஞ்செய்வ தேதவம் இவைகளைந்","தூடிற் பலவுல கோரெத் தவரே."],"p":"","r":"Doing tapas in company, I saw the sounding anklets; doing tapas in search, I saw the Siva-state; is wasting oneself away tapas? If they cast these off and quarrel, what tapas have the many worldly people?","n":"Line 3 (வாடித் தவஞ்செய்வதே தவம்) can be read as a statement, 'tapas done in wasting is tapas', or as a question, which fits the chapter's criticism of mortification. கூடி may be 'in the company of devotees' or 'united with the Lord'. Line 4 is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","sivam","yaakkai","adiyaar"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:112"},"t:1637":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1637,"t":["மனத்துரை மாகடல் ஏழுங் கைநீந்தித்","தவத்திடை யாளர்தஞ் சார்வத்து வந்தார்","பவத்திடை யாளர் அவர்பணி கேட்கின்","முகத்திடை நந்தியை முந்தலு மாமே."],"p":"","r":"Those who have swum with their arms across the seven great seas of the mind come into the shelter of the people of tapas; if those still in the round of birth listen to them and serve them, they may come face to face with Nandi.","n":"The seven seas are not explained; they may be the mind's restless states, seven births or the seven bodily centres. The verse gives a chain of mediation: the tapasvins reach shelter, and the worldly reach Nandi through serving them.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","inam","adiyaar","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:112"},"t:1638":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1638,"t":["மனத்திடை நின்ற மதிவாள் உருவி","இனத்திடை நீக்கி இரண்டற வீர்த்துப்","புனத்திடை அஞ்சும் போகாமல் மறித்தால்","தவத்திடை யாறொளி தன்னொளி யாமே."],"p":"","r":"Draw the sword of understanding that stands in the mind, cut yourself from the crowd, cleave it so that no two remain, and turn back the five so they do not stray into the field: then, within tapas, the light of the way becomes your own light.","n":"புனம், the millet field, is the field of sense objects and the five are the senses, as tradition reads them. இனம் may be the senses as a band or bad company. ஆறொளி may be the light of the path or the light of the six centres; the plain sense is that discipline makes the light one's own.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","thavam","manam","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:112"},"t:1639":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1639,"t":["ஒத்து மிகவு நின்றானை யுரைப்பது","பத்தி கொடுக்கும் பணிந்தடி யார்தொழ","முத்தி கொடுக்கும் முனிவன் னெனும்பதஞ்","சத்தான செய்வது தான்தவந் தானே."],"p":"","r":"To speak of him who stands equal to all and beyond all gives devotion; the sage gives release to the devotees who bow and worship; to act in that state as one who is real is itself tapas.","n":"ஒத்து மிகவும் நின்றான், one who is equal and also exceeds, is taken as the Lord who is in all and beyond all. சத்தான செய்வது (doing what is sat, real) is the verse's definition of tapas, but its sense is not fixed: it may be acting as the real self, or doing what is true.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","adiyaar","veedu","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:112"},"t:1640":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1640,"t":["இலைதொட்டுப் பூப்பறித் தெந்தைக்கென் றெண்ணி","மலர்தொட்டுக் கொண்டேன் வரும்புனல் காணேன்","தலைதொட்ட நூல்கண்டு தாழ்ந்ததென் உள்ளந்","தலைதொட்டுக் கண்டேன் தவங்கொண்ட வாறே."],"p":"","r":"Touching leaves and plucking flowers, I gathered them thinking 'for my father', but did not see the water that comes; seeing the scripture that reaches the summit my heart bowed, and touching the summit I saw how tapas is taken up.","n":"The verse moves from outer worship (leaves, flowers, water for the Lord's bath) to something reached at the தலை, head or summit. தலை தொட்ட நூல் may be the highest scripture, and தலை தொட்டு may be touching one's head in reverence or, yogically, reaching the crown. வரும் புனல் காணேன் may be a confession of incomplete outer worship or a hint at inner water.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","thavam","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:112"},"t:1641":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1641,"t":["படர்சடை மாதவம் பற்றிய பத்தர்க்","கிடரடை யாவண்ணம் ஈசன் அருளும்","இடரடை செய்தவர் மெய்த்தவ நோக்கில்","உடரடை செய்வ தொருமனத் தாமே."],"p":"","r":"The Lord grants that no trouble reaches the devotees who hold to the great tapas of matted hair; looked at truly, the tapas of those who take on hardship lies in a single mind that holds the body in check.","n":"Line 3 (இடரடை செய்தவர்) can be those who take on hardship or those who cause it; line 4's உடரடை is read as உடல் அடை, holding the body. The verse seems to move the worth of tapas from outward marks to one-pointedness of mind.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","thiruvarul","manam","idumbai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:112"},"t:1642":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1642,"t":["ஆற்றிக் கிடந்த முதலைகண் டஞ்சிப்போய்","ஈற்றுக் கரடிக் கெதிர்ப்பட்ட தன்னொக்கும்","நோற்றுத் தவஞ்செய்யார் நூலறி யாதவர்","சோற்றுக்கு நின்று சுழல்கின்ற வாறே."],"p":"","r":"Like one who, frightened by a crocodile lying in the river, runs off and meets a she-bear that has just given birth, so are those who do no tapas and do not know the books, standing and whirling around for rice.","n":"ஈற்றுக் கரடி, a bear that has just whelped, is at its fiercest; fleeing one danger, the man meets a worse. The verse's target is a life spent chasing food, where each escape leads to a new danger.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","kalvi","pasi","idumbai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:112"},"t:1643":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1643,"t":["பழுக்கின்ற வாறும் பழமுண்ணு மாறுங்","குழக்கன்று துள்ளியக் கோணியைப் பல்காற்","குழக்கன்று கொட்டிலிற் கட்டவல் லார்க்குள்","இழுக்காது நெஞ்சத் திடவொன்று மாமே."],"p":"","r":"Those who can tie up in its stall the frisking young calf that keeps butting at the sack will find in the heart, without slipping, how the fruit ripens and how the fruit is eaten.","n":"The calf is traditionally the mind and the stall the place it is held; கோணி may be a sack of fodder or something bent. The ripening and eating of the fruit is the fruit of discipline coming to fulfilment, but the verse does not name the fruit.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["manam","adakkam","payan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:112"},"t:1644":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1644,"t":["சித்தஞ் சிவமாகச் செய்தவம் வேண்டாவால்","சித்தஞ் சிவானந்தஞ் சேர்ந்தோர் உறவுண்டால்","சித்தஞ் சிவமாக வேசித்தி முத்தியாஞ்","சித்தஞ் சிவமாதல் செய்தவப் பேறே."],"p":"","r":"No tapas is needed to make the mind Siva if one has kinship with those whose minds have joined Siva's bliss; when the mind becomes Siva, siddhi and release follow; the mind becoming Siva is the fruit of tapas done.","n":"The verse gives two routes to one end: fellowship (உறவு) with those in Siva's bliss, which makes tapas unnecessary, and tapas itself, whose fruit is the same. Sengalvaraya notes a parallel in Tiruvasagam (அச்சோப் பதிகம் 1), where the Lord removes the mind's malas and makes it Siva.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","adiyaar","aanandham","siththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:112"},"t:1645":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1645,"t":["பிரானருள் உண்டெனில் உண்டுநற் செல்வம்","பிரானருள் உண்டெனில் உண்டுநன் ஞானம்","பிரானரு ளிற்பெருந் தன்மையும் உண்டு","பிரானரு ளிற்பெருந் தெய்வமு மாமே."],"p":"","r":"If the Lord's grace is there, good wealth is there; if the Lord's grace is there, good knowledge is there; in the Lord's grace there is greatness, and in the Lord's grace one becomes the great deity.","n":"The last line, பெருந்தெய்வமும் ஆமே, can mean that grace makes one divine or that the great God himself is reached in it. The chapter title borrows the Kural's chapter name அருளுடைமை but means the Lord's grace, not compassion between beings.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","selvam","gnaanam","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:113"},"t:1646":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1646,"t":["தமிழ்மண் டலம்ஐந்துந் தாவிய ஞானம்","உமிழ்வது போல உலகந் திரிவார்","அவிழு மனமும்எம் ஆதியறிவுந்","தமிழ்மண் டலம்ஐந்துந் தத்துவ மாமே."],"p":"","r":"People wander the world as if spitting out the knowledge that spread across the five Tamil lands; yet the loosening mind, the knowledge of our Primal One and the five Tamil lands are all tattva.","n":"தமிழ் மண்டலம் ஐந்து is usually taken as the five Tamil regions, where the teaching spread; some read the five mandalas yogically, as regions of the body. உமிழ்வது போல (as if spitting out) may mean rejecting the knowledge or pouring it out. The verse's claim that all of it is tattva is clear; its tone toward the wanderers is not.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thamizh","gnaanam","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:113"},"t:1647":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1647,"t":["புண்ணிய பாவம் இரண்டுள பூமியில்","நண்ணும் பொழுதறி வார்சில ஞானிகள்","எண்ணி இரண்டையும் வேர்அறத் தப்புறத்","தண்ணல் இருப்பிடம் ஆய்ந்துகொள் வீரே."],"p":"","r":"Merit and sin are two that come with one to the earth, and a few knowers see this when it happens; weigh them, cut both at the root, and seek out the Lord's dwelling beyond them.","n":"The verse asks for both merit and sin to be uprooted, since both bind to birth; it does not rank merit above sin. தப்பு அப்புறத்து is read as 'beyond, escaping both'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","gnaanam","iraivan","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:113"},"t:1648":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1648,"t":["முன்னின் றருளு முடிகின்ற காலத்து","நன்னின் றுலகில் நடுவுயி ராய்நிற்கும்","பின்னின் றருளும் பிறவியை நீக்கிடும்","முன்னின் றெனக்கொரு முத்திதந் தானே."],"p":"","r":"Grace that goes before at the time of ending stands in the world as the life at the centre; grace that stays behind removes birth; standing before me, it gave me a release.","n":"The subject, grace, is supplied from the chapter; the verse names only what 'stands before' and 'stands behind'. முடிகின்ற காலம் may be death, the dissolution of the world or the end of a soul's bonds, and நடுவுயிர் may be the life within all or the soul held between births.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","piravi","veedu","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:113"},"t:1649":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1649,"t":["சிவனரு ளாற்சிலர் தேவரு மாவர்","சிவனரு ளாற்சிலர் தெய்வத்தோ டொப்பர்","சிவனரு ளால்வினை சேரகி லாமை","சிவனருள் கூடின்அச் சிவலோக மாமே."],"p":"","r":"By Siva's grace some become gods, by Siva's grace some equal the deity, by Siva's grace deeds do not cling, and if Siva's grace is joined, that is Siva's world.","n":"The grades (gods, equal to the deity, free of deeds, Siva's world) mount toward the last, which makes Sivaloka not a place reached after death but the state of being joined to grace.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","sivam","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:113"},"t:1650":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1650,"t":["புண்ணியன் எந்தை புனிதன் இணையடி","நண்ணி விளக்கென ஞானம் விளைந்தது","மண்ணவ ராவதும் வானவர் ஆவதும்","அண்ணல் இறைவன் அருள்பெற்ற போதே."],"p":"","r":"Drawing near the paired feet of the meritorious one, our father, the pure one, knowledge grew like a lamp; becoming one of earth or one of heaven happens only when one receives the Lord's grace.","n":"விளக்கென ஞானம் விளைந்தது, knowledge grew like a lamp, ties the chapter's title to an image. The last two lines put even ordinary human and heavenly births under grace, not only release. The father (எந்தை) may be Siva or the guru.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","gnaanam","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:113"},"t:1651":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1651,"t":["காயத்தே ரேறி மனப்பாகன் கைகூட்ட","மாயத்தே ரேறி மயங்கு மவையுணர்","நேயத்தே ரேறி நிமலன் அருள்பெற்றால்","ஆயத்தே ரேறி யவனிவ னாமே."],"p":"","r":"Mounting the chariot of the body, driven by the mind as charioteer, one mounts the chariot of maya and is bewildered; know these; mounting the chariot of love and receiving the stainless one's grace, one mounts the chariot of the assembly, and He becomes this one.","n":"The image of the body as chariot and the mind as charioteer is old (it is in the Katha Upanishad). Tirumular stacks four chariots: body, maya, love (நேயம்) and ஆயம், which may be the assembly of the Lord's company or the tattvas brought under control. அவன் இவன் ஆமே, 'He becomes this one', is traditionally the soul's union with Siva.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","maayai","anbu","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:113"},"t:1652":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1652,"t":["அவ்வுல கத்தே பிறக்கில் உடலொடும்","அவ்வுல கத்தே யருந்தவர் நாடுவர்","அவ்வுல கத்தே யரனடி கூடுவர்","அவ்வுல கத்தே யருள்பெறு வாரே."],"p":"","r":"If one is born in that world with a body, it is in that world that one seeks those of rare tapas, in that world that one joins Hara's feet, and in that world that one receives grace.","n":"அவ்வுலகம், that world, is not named; it may be this earth, stressing that grace is won in embodied life, or a higher world in which one is reborn. The repetition makes the embodied birth the condition for every step that follows.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","yaakkai","thavam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:113"},"t:1653":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1653,"t":["கதிர்கண்ட காந்தங் கனலின் வடிவாம்","மதிகண்ட காந்தம் மணிநீர் வடிவாஞ்","சதிகொண்ட சாக்கி யெரியின் வடிவாம்","எரிகொண்ட ஈசன் எழில்வடி வாமே."],"p":"","r":"The sunstone that sees the sun takes the form of fire, the moonstone that sees the moon takes the form of clear water, the witness that holds the spark takes the form of flame, and the Lord who holds the fire takes on beautiful form.","n":"The sunstone and moonstone each take on the nature of what they face, and tradition reads the soul as taking on Siva's form when it faces him by grace. சதிகொண்ட சாக்கி is obscure: சாக்கி may be sakshi, the witnessing soul, or a flint (as in சக்கிமுக்கி); the last line may also mean that Siva, who holds fire, shines in the soul so lit.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","sivam","pasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:113"},"t:1654":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1654,"t":["நாடும் உறவும் கலந்தெங்கள் நந்தியைத்","தேடுவன் தேடிச் சிவபெரு மான்என்று","கூடுவன் கூடிக் குரைகழற் கேசெல்ல","வீடும் அளவும் விடுகின் றிலெனே."],"p":"","r":"Seeking him with all kinship, I search for our Nandi; having searched, I join him as Siva Peruman; having joined, so as to reach his sounding anklets, I do not let go until release.","n":"நாடும் உறவும் கலந்து can be 'with seeking and kinship mixed' or 'mingling land and kin', i.e. while still living among one's people. The chain தேடுவன், தேடி; கூடுவன், கூடி links each step to the next, and the verse ends on a clinging that is itself the way to release.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","anbu","veedu","pattru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:113"},"t:1655":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1655,"t":["ஆடம் பரங்கொண் டடிசிலுண் பான்பயன்","வேடங்கள் கொண்டு வெருட்டிடும் பேதைகாள்","ஆடியும் பாடியும் அழுதும் அரற்றியுந்","தேடியுங் காணீர் சிவனவன் தாள்களே."],"p":"","r":"Fools who put on guises and frighten people so as to eat their food in pomp: dance, sing, weep, wail and search as you will, you will not see Siva's feet.","n":"வெருட்டிடும் (who frighten) suggests ascetics who overawe householders into feeding them. The dancing, singing and weeping of line 3 are the outward marks of devotion; the verse says that performed for show they find nothing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vesham","koodaozhukkam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:114"},"t:1656":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1656,"t":["ஞானமில் லேர்வேடம் பூண்டிருந்த நாட்டிடை","ஈனம தேசெய் திரந்துண் டிருப்பினும்","மான நலங்கெடும் வையகம் பஞ்ச்மாம்","ஈனவர் வேடங் கழிப்பித்தல் இன்பமே."],"p":"","r":"If men without knowledge wear the garb in the land, do base things and live by begging, the land's honour and good decay and famine comes; to make the base put off the garb is a joy.","n":"The harm is public, not only the pretender's own: the land loses its standing and suffers famine. Who is to strip the garb is left unsaid; the next verses and 241 put it on the ruler.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vesham","gnaanam","naadu","koodaozhukkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:114"},"t:1657":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1657,"t":["இன்பமும் துன்பமும் நாட்டா ரிடத்துள்ள","நன்செயல் புன்செய லாலந்த நாட்டிற்காம்","என்ப இறைநாடி நாடோறும் நாட்டினின்","மன்பதை செப்பம் செயின்வையம் வாழுமே."],"p":"","r":"Joy and sorrow in a land come to it from the good and bad deeds of its people; so it is said that if the ruler looks into it every day and sets the people of his land right, the world will flourish.","n":"இறை here is the king, not the Lord, though the word allows both. The verse makes the king answerable for his subjects' conduct, not only for his own; in this chapter that means dealing with false ascetics.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","sengol","vinai","naadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:114"},"t:1658":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1658,"t":["இழிகுலத் தோர்வேடம் பூண்பர்மே லெய்த","வழிகுலத் தோர்வேடம் பூண்பர்தே வாகப்","பழிகுலத் தாகிய பாழ்சண்ட ரானார்","கழிகுலத் தோர்கள் களையப்பட் டோரே."],"p":"","r":"Men of low birth put on the garb to rise, men of the right line put it on to become gods; those who became ruined, base men of a blameworthy line, the outcast ones, are to be weeded out.","n":"Every line turns on a compound with குலம் (இழி, வழி, பழி, கழி), and their sense is unsure: வழிகுலம் may be 'those of the proper line' or 'those who follow the way', and சண்டர் may mean the fierce or the outcast. களையப்பட்டோர் (weeded out) echoes the ruler's duty of 1657; whether the verse ranks garb-wearers by birth or uses birth words for conduct can't be fixed from the Tamil.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vesham","kudi","pirappu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:114"},"t:1659":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1659,"t":["பொய்த்தவஞ் செய்வார் புகுவர் நரகத்துப்","பொய்த்தவஞ் செய்தவர் புண்ணிய ராகாரேற்","பொய்த்தவம்மெய்த்தவம் போகத்துட்போக்கியஞ்","சத்திய ஞானத்தால் தங்குந் தவங்களே."],"p":"","r":"Those who practise false austerity go to hell, and if they do not become men of merit, false and true austerity alike are things to be undergone among experiences; austerities hold only through true knowledge.","n":"Line 3, போகத்துள் போக்கியம், is obscure: it may mean that both kinds of austerity only yield experiences to be consumed, so neither frees without சத்திய ஞானம். The first line's claim is plain; the rest is my best reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","naragam","gnaanam","koodaozhukkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:114"},"t:1660":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1660,"t":["பொய்வேடம் பூண்பர் போசித்தல் பயனாக","மெய்வேடம் பூண்போர்மிகு பிச்சைகைக்கொள்வர்","பொய்வேடம் மெய்வேடம் போலவே பூணினும்","உய்வேட மாகும் உணர்ந்தறிந் தோர்க்கே."],"p":"","r":"The false garb is worn for the sake of eating; those who wear the true garb receive alms; but though the false garb be worn just like the true, a garb becomes one that saves only for those who have understood and known.","n":"மிகு பிச்சை கைக்கொள்வர் may mean the true wearers take alms as the highest thing, or simply that they live by alms rather than by feasting. The last two lines can be read as saying even an outwardly false garb saves the knower, or that no garb saves anyone but the knower; either way knowledge, not the garb, decides.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vesham","gnaanam","iravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:114"},"t:1661":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1661,"t":["தவமிக் கவரே தலையான வேடர்","அவமிக் கவரே யதிகொலை வேடர்","அவமிக் கவர்வேடத் தாகாரவ் வேடந்","தவமிக் கவர்க்கன்றித் தாங்கவொண் ணாதே."],"p":"","r":"Those great in austerity are the foremost wearers of the garb; those great in futility are hunters bent on killing; those great in futility cannot bear that garb, which none but those great in austerity can carry.","n":"வேடர் means both 'wearers of garb' and 'hunters', and the verse turns on it: the false ascetic is a hunter in disguise. தவம் and அவம் (austerity and its empty counterfeit) are set against each other as in Kural 262.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","vesham","koodaozhukkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:115"},"t:1662":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1662,"t":["பூதி யணிவது சாதன மாதியிற்","காதணி தாம்பிர குண்டலங் கண்டிகை","ஓதி யவர்க்கும் உருத்திர சாதனந்","தீதில் சிவயோகி சாதனந் தேரிலே."],"p":"","r":"Wearing holy ash is the first of the emblems; earrings of copper and the rudraksha string are Rudra's emblems for those who recite too; these are the emblems of the flawless Siva-yogi, if you examine.","n":"சாதனம் here means an emblem or outward means, not a practice. கண்டிகை is traditionally the rudraksha string. ஓதியவர்க்கும் may mean those who recite the scriptures, or those who have been instructed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vesham","thiruneeru","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:115"},"t:1663":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1663,"t":["யோகிக் கிடுமது வுட்கட்டுக் கஞ்சுளி","தோகைக்குப் பாசத்துச் சுற்றுஞ் சடையதொன்று","றாகத்து நீறனி யாங்கக் கபாலஞ்","சீகந்த மாத்திரை தின்பிரம் பாகுமே."],"p":"","r":"For the yogi there are the inner loincloth and the cloak, a single matted lock wound with cord, ash on the body, the skull-bowl, and the cane staff.","n":"The list is compressed and several words are unclear: உட்கட்டு, கஞ்சுளி, தோகைக்குப் பாசத்து, சீகந்தம், மாத்திரை. I take கஞ்சுளி as a cloak and பிரம்பு as a cane staff; the line divisions of the list are guesses.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vesham","yogam","thiruneeru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:115"},"t:1664":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1664,"t":["காதணி குண்டலங் கண்டிகை நாதமும்","ஊதுநற் சங்கும் உயர்கட்டி கப்பரை","ஏதமில் பாதுகம் யோகாந்த மாதனம்","ஏதமில் யோகபட் டந்தண்டம் ஈரைந்தே."],"p":"","r":"Earrings, rudraksha beads, the horn, the good conch to blow, the high bowl, flawless sandals, the seat at the end of yoga, the flawless yoga band and the staff: these are the ten.","n":"நாதம் is taken as a horn or other sounding instrument, and கட்டி may be a separate item or part of the begging bowl (கப்பரை); the count of ten depends on which. யோகபட்டம் is the band that holds the knees in a seated posture.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vesham","yogam","aasanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:115"},"t:1665":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1665,"t":["நூலுஞ் சிகையும் உணரார்நின் மூடர்கள்","நூலது வேதாந்தம் நுண்சிகை ஞானமாம்","பாலொன்றும் அந்தணர் பார்ப்பார் பரமுயிர்","ஓரொன் றிரண்டெனில் ஓங்காரம் ஓதிலே."],"p":"","r":"Fools, you do not understand thread and tuft: the thread is Vedanta and the fine tuft is knowledge; Brahmins who keep to the one part will see the supreme soul, whether it is one or two, if they recite Om.","n":"The first two lines restate 230 plainly. பாலொன்றும் and ஓரொன்று இரண்டெனில் are obscure: perhaps 'whether the soul and the Supreme are one or two', a question Om is said to settle. Why the verse stands under holy ash is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["andhanar","vedaantham","gnaanam","piranavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:116"},"t:1666":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1666,"t":["கங்காளன் பூசுng கவசத் திருநீற்றை","மங்காமல் பூசி மகிழ்வரே யாமாகில்","தங்கா வினைகளுஞ் சாருஞ் சிவகதி","சிங்கார மான திருவடி சேர்வரே."],"p":"","r":"If they wear with gladness, never letting it fade, the holy ash that is armour, which Kankala wears, deeds will not stay, the Siva state will come, and they will reach the beautiful holy feet.","n":"கங்காளன் is Siva bearing the skeleton, and கவசம் (armour) makes the ash a protection. மங்காமல் can mean wearing it without letting it fade, or without fail.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["thiruneeru","vinai","sivam","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:116"},"t:1667":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1667,"t":["அரசுட னாலத்தி யாகும்அக் காரம்","விரவுகனலில் வியனுரு மாறி","நிரவயன் நின்மலன் தாள்பெற்ற நீதர்","உருவம் பிரமன் உயர்குலம் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"The ash from peepal, banyan and fig wood, its form changed in the spreading fire, makes the form of the pure, who have gained the feet of the partless and stainless one, into a high lineage like Brahma's.","n":"அரசுடன் ஆலத்தி is taken as peepal (அரசு), banyan (ஆல்) and fig (அத்தி), the woods of the sacrificial fire, and அக்காரம் as the ash; both readings are tradition's guess. The last line may mean the wearer gains Brahmin status, or that his form becomes like Brahma's.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruneeru","kudi","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:116"},"t:1668":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1668,"t":["ஞானமி லார்வேடம் பூண்டும் நரகத்தர்","ஞானமுள்ளார்வேடம்இன்றெனில்நன்முத்தர்","ஞானமுளதாக வேண்டுவோர் நக்கன்பால்","ஞானமுள வேட நண்ணிநிற் பாரே."],"p":"","r":"Those without knowledge belong to hell even if they wear the garb; those with knowledge are the good released even without it; those who want knowledge go to the Naked One and stand in the garb that has knowledge.","n":"நக்கன் (the naked one) is Siva, the ascetic beyond all garb, which sharpens the point. The verse does not reject garb: for seekers it is the garb joined to knowledge.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","vesham","naragam","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:117"},"t:1669":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1669,"t":["புன்ஞானத் தோர்வேடம் பூண்டும் பயனில்லை","நன்ஞானத்தோர்வேடம் பூணார் அருள்நண்ணித்","துன்ஞானத் தோர்சம யத்துரி சுள்ளோர்","பின்ஞானத் தோரொன்றும் பேசுகில்லாரே."],"p":"","r":"There is no use in those of petty knowledge wearing the garb; those of good knowledge, without wearing it, attain grace; those of evil knowledge, with the stain of sect, and those of lesser knowledge, have nothing to say.","n":"The four grades (புன், நன், துன், பின் ஞானம்) are a play on one word. பின்ஞானத்தோர் may be those whose knowledge comes later or is secondary, and the last line may mean they cannot speak of the truth at all.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","vesham","thiruvarul","samayam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:117"},"t:1670":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1670,"t":["சிவஞானி கட்குஞ் சிவயோகி கட்கும்","அவமான சாதனம் ஆகாது தேரில்","அவமா மவர்க்கது சாதன நான்கும்","உவமான மில்பொருள் உள்ளுற லாமே."],"p":"","r":"For Siva-gnanis and Siva-yogis the emblems are not futile, if you examine; for the others they are futile; through the four means the peerless reality can come to dwell within.","n":"அவமாம் அவர்க்கு may refer back to the gnanis (for whom emblems are empty, being unneeded) or to others; the plain and the opposite readings both stand. சாதனம் நான்கும் may be four emblems or the four paths (சரியை, கிரியை, யோகம், ஞானம்), as Siddhanta tradition counts them.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","yogam","vesham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:117"},"t:1671":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1671,"t":["சுத்தித் திரிவர் கழுவடி நாய்போற்","கொத்தித் திரிவர் குரக்களி ஞானிகள்","ஒத்துப் பொறியும் உடலும் இருக்கவே","செத்துத் திரிவர் சிவஞானி யோர்களே."],"p":"","r":"The ritual purifiers wander like a dog at the foot of the stake, the monkey-glad knowers wander pecking about, but the Siva-gnanis wander as men dead while their senses and body remain intact.","n":"கழுவடி நாய் is read as a dog hanging about the impaling stake for scraps, though கழு may mean washing; குரக்களி ஞானிகள் as knowers with a monkey's restless glee. 'Dead while living' (செத்துத் திரிவர்) is the classic image of the released one whose attachments are gone.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","thuravu","pattru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:117"},"t:1672":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1672,"t":["அடியா ரவரே யடியா ரலாதார்","அடியாரு மாகார்அவ் வேடமு மாகார்","அடியார் சிவஞான மானது பெற்றோர்","அடியா ரலாதார் அடியார்கள் அன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Devotees are devotees; those who are not cannot become devotees nor become that garb; devotees are those who have gained Siva-knowledge, and those who have not are not devotees.","n":"The verse defines அடியார் by Siva-knowledge, not by garb or by profession of devotion; the repetition of அடியார் in every line drives it home.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adiyaar","gnaanam","vesham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:117"},"t:1673":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1673,"t":["ஞானிக்குச் சுந்தர வேடமும் நல்லவாந்","தானுற்ற வேடமுந் தற்சிவ யோகமே","ஆனவவ் வேடம் அருண்ஞான சாதனம்","ஆனது மாமொன்றும் ஆகா தவனுக்கே."],"p":"","r":"For the gnani even a handsome garb is fitting; the garb he has taken is itself Siva-yoga; that garb becomes a means to the knowledge of grace, and for one who is not so, nothing of it avails.","n":"சுந்தர வேடம் (a beautiful garb) may mean that even rich, unascetic dress does not harm the gnani. The last line can also be read 'for him, whatever comes is fitting and nothing binds'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","vesham","yogam","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:117"},"t:1674":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1674,"t":["ஞானத்தின் னாற்பத நண்ணுஞ் சிவஞானி","தானத்தில் வைத்த தனியால யத்தனாம்","மோனத்த னாதலின் முத்தனாஞ் சித்தனாம்","ஏனைத் தவசி இவனென லாகுமே."],"p":"","r":"The Siva-gnani who reaches the state through knowledge holds a lone temple set in its place; being one of silence he is released and a siddha, and he may be called the other kind of ascetic.","n":"தானத்தில் வைத்த தனி ஆலயத்தன் may mean his own body or heart is the temple in which the Lord is set, as in 1823. ஏனைத் தவசி could mark him as an ascetic of a different kind from the garb-wearers, or the verse may say only that he too can be called an ascetic.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","veedu","siththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:117"},"t:1675":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1675,"t":["தானன்ற தன்மையுந் தானவ னாதலும்","ஏனைய வச்சிவ மான இயற்கையுந்","தானுறு சாதக முத்திரை சாத்தலு","மேனமும் நந்தி பதமுத்தி பெற்றதே."],"p":"","r":"The state of not being oneself, the becoming Him, the nature that is Siva, the wearing of the seal of attainment and silence: these belong to the one who has gained release at Nandi's feet.","n":"தான் அன்ற தன்மை and தான் அவன் ஆதல (losing the self and becoming Him) are Siddhanta terms for the soul's union. மேனமும் is unknown; I read it as மோனமும் (silence), which fits 1674.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","veedu","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:117"},"t:1676":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1676,"t":["அருளால் அரனுக் கடிமைய தாகிப்","பொருளாந் தனதுடற் பொற்பதி நாடி","இருளான தின்றி யிருஞ்செயல் அற்றோர்","தெருளாம் அடிமைச் சிவவேடத் தோரே."],"p":"","r":"Those who by grace become servants of Hara, seek the golden Lord in their own body as the true thing, are without darkness and have ceased from their great doings: these are of the Siva garb of clear servanthood.","n":"பொருளாம் தனது உடல் பொற்பதி நாடி can be read as seeking the golden Lord within one's body, or as regarding one's body as His property. இருஞ்செயல் அற்றோர் (free of great action) may mean free of deeds that bind.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["adiyaar","thiruvarul","yaakkai","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:118"},"t:1677":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1677,"t":["உடலில் துவக்கிய வேடம் உயிர்க்காகா","உடல்கழன் றால்வேடம் உடனே கழலும்","உடலுயிர் உண்மையென் றோர்ந்துகொள்ளாதார்","கடலில் அகல்பட்ட கட்டையொத் தாரே."],"p":"","r":"A garb begun on the body is not for the soul; when the body falls away the garb falls with it; those who have not grasped what body and soul truly are are like a log cast adrift on the sea.","n":"The argument is plain: garb belongs to the body and dies with it, so it cannot serve the soul. The log adrift is a common image of the soul tossed in the sea of births.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vesham","yaakkai","uyir","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:118"},"t:1678":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1678,"t":["மயலற் றிருளற்று மாமன மற்றுக்","கயலுற்ற கண்ணியர் கையிணைக் கற்றுத்","தயலற் றவரோடும் தாமே தாமாகிச்","செயலற் றிருப்பார் சிவவேடத் தாரே."],"p":"","r":"Free of delusion and darkness, the great mind gone, having learned to escape the hands of carp-eyed women, being simply themselves with those free of desire, they rest without action: these are of the Siva garb.","n":"கையிணைக் கற்று is obscure: perhaps having learned (to be free of) the embrace of women. தயலற்றவர் may be those without women (from தையல்) or those without longing. தாமே தாமாகி (being themselves alone) suggests the soul standing in its own nature.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","manam","kaamam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:118"},"t:1679":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1679,"t":["ஒடுங் குதிரைக் குசைதிண்ணம் பற்றுமின்","வேடங்கொண் டென்செய்வீர் வேண்டாமனிதரே","நாடுமின் நந்தியை நம்பெரு மான்தன்னைத்","தேடுமின் பப்பொருள் சென்றெய்த லாமே."],"p":"","r":"Hold the reins of the running horse firmly; people, what will you do by putting on a garb? You don't need it; seek Nandi, our great Lord, search for him, and you can go and reach the thing that is bliss.","n":"The running horse is traditionally the mind or the senses, and some read it of the breath; the verse does not say. The closing contrast is between taking on garb and reining oneself in.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","manam","vesham","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:118"},"t:1680":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1680,"t":["குருட்டினை நீக்குங் குருவினைக் கொள்ளார்","குருட்டினை நீக்காக் குருவினைக் கொள்வார்","குருடுங் குருடுங் குருட்டாட்டம் ஆடிக்","குருடுங் குருடுங் குழிவிழு மாறே."],"p":"","r":"They will not take the guru who removes blindness but take one who does not; blind and blind, playing a blind game, both fall into the pit.","n":"குருடு (blindness) is ignorance, and the repetition of குரு and குருடு makes the line a pun. The blind leading the blind into a pit is a widespread image, known also from the Upanishads and the Gospels.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["guru","pedhaimai","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:119"},"t:1681":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1681,"t":["மனத்தில் எழுந்ததோர் மாயக்கண் ணாடி","நினைப்பின் அதனின் நிழலையுங் காணார்","வினைப்பயன் போக விளக்கியுங் கொள்ளார்","புறக்கடை இச்சித்துப் போகின்ற வாறே."],"p":"","r":"A magic mirror has arisen in the mind, yet they do not see even its reflection in their thought; they will not polish it so the fruit of deeds departs, but go off desiring what lies outside.","n":"The mirror in the mind is read as the inner faculty in which the Lord or the self could be seen if it were cleaned; விளக்கியும் (polishing) supports this. புறக்கடை (the outer gate or back door) is taken as the outward world of the senses.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["manam","maayai","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:119"},"t:1682":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1682,"t":["ஏயெனில் என்னென மாட்டார் பிரசைகள்","வாய்முலை பெய்ய மதுரநின் றூறிடுந்","தாய்முலை யாவ தறியார் தமருளோர்","ஊனிலை செய்யும் உருவிலி தானே."],"p":"","r":"When called they do not answer 'what is it?'; those among kin do not know the mother's breast that flows sweet when the mouth is put to it; the formless one is the one who keeps the body.","n":"பிரசைகள் may be people, subjects or children; the verse seems to say the unripe do not answer the Lord's call nor know him as a mother's breast that gives without asking. உருவிலி (the formless one) is the Lord; ஊன்நிலை செய்யும் may mean he sustains the body. The line connections are my guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","thiruvarul","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:119"},"t:1683":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1683,"t":["வாயென்று சொல்லி மனமொன்று சிந்தித்து","நீயொன்று செய்யல் உறுதி நெடுந்தகாய்","நீயென்றிங் குன்னைத் தெளிவன் தெளிந்தபின்","பேயென்றிங் கென்னைப் பிறர்தெளி யாரே."],"p":"","r":"Saying one thing with the mouth and thinking another in the mind, don't do yet a third, great one; I will know you here as you are, and once I have known you, others will not take me for a madman.","n":"The first two lines are a plain demand that word, thought and deed agree. The last two are obscure: பேய் may mean a madman or a ghost, and who speaks to whom is unclear; it may be the teacher sizing up a would-be disciple.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vaaimai","manam","koodaozhukkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:119"},"t:1684":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1684,"t":["பஞ்சத் துரோகத்திப் பாதகர் தம்மை","யஞ்சச் சமயத்தோர் வேந்தன் அருந்தண்டம்","விஞ்சச்செய் திப்புவி வேறே விடாவிடிற்","பஞ்சத்து ளாய்புவி முற்றும்பா ழாகுமே."],"p":"","r":"If the king of the faithful does not inflict hard punishment on these sinners of the five betrayals so that they fear, and drive them off this earth, the whole earth will fall into famine and lie waste.","n":"பஞ்சத் துரோகம் is traditionally taken as the five great sins (killing, theft, liquor, adultery, and wronging the guru, in one list). அஞ்சச் சமயத்தோர் வேந்தன் may be 'the king, so that those of the sects fear' or 'the king of the faith'. பஞ்சம் (famine) puns with பஞ்ச (five).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arasu","oruththal","naadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:119"},"t:1685":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1685,"t":["தவத்திடை நின்றவர் தாமுண்ணும் கன்மஞ்","சிவத்திடை நின்றது தேவர் அறியார்","தவத்திடை நின்றறி யாதவர் எல்லாம்","பவத்திடை நின்றதோர் பாடது வாமே."],"p":"","r":"The karma that those who stand in austerity undergo rests in Siva, and even the gods do not know it; for all who stand in austerity without knowing this, it is suffering that stays in births.","n":"The sense may be that the ascetic who knows offers his deeds to Siva and is freed, while austerity without that knowledge only adds to births. பவத்திடை நின்றதோர் பாடு (suffering that stands in birth) is my reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","vinai","sivam","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:119"},"t:1686":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1686,"t":["கன்றலுங் கருதலுங் கருமஞ் செய்தலும்","தின்றலுஞ் சுவைத்தலுந் தீமைசெய்தலும்","பின்றலும் பிறங்கலும் பெருமை கூறலும்","என்றிவை இறைபால் இயற்கை அல்லவே."],"p":"","r":"Burning with anger, scheming, doing deeds, eating, relishing, doing harm, falling back, putting oneself forward and boasting of greatness: these are not the nature of one near the Lord.","n":"இறைபால் இயற்கை அல்லவே can mean 'not the nature of the Lord' or 'not the nature of those who are with the Lord'. Placed in this chapter, the list reads as the marks of the unripe; the first reading would make it a statement about the Lord's own freedom from these.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","vekuli","vinai","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:119"},"t:1687":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1687,"t":["விடிவ தறியார் வெளிகாண மாட்டார்","விடியில் வெளியில் விழிக்கவு மாட்டார்","கடியதோ ருண்ணிமை கட்டுமின் காண்மின்","விடியாமை காக்கும் விளக்கது வாமே."],"p":"","r":"They do not know the dawning and cannot see the open space; even when it dawns they will not open their eyes in it; bind fast the strong inner eyelid and see: that is the lamp that guards against the night not ending.","n":"விடிவு (dawn) and வெளி (open space) are traditionally read as the dawning of knowledge and the inner space seen in yoga. உண்ணிமை (inner eyelid) is obscure; it may mean closing the outer eyes to see within.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["meyyunarvu","yogam","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:119"},"t:1688":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1688,"t":["வைத்த பசுபாசம் மாற்று நெறிவைகிப்","பெத்த மறமுத்த னாகிப் பிறழ்வுற்றுத்","தத்துவ முன்னித் தலைப்படா தவ்வாறு","பித்தான சீடனுக் கீயப் பெறாதானே."],"p":"","r":"One who keeps to the path that changes the soul's set bonds, then, released from bondage, wavers, and does not think his way through the tattvas to reach the goal: to such a deluded disciple the teaching is not to be given.","n":"பெத்த மற முத்தனாகிப் பிறழ்வுற்று is hard: it may describe one who seems freed but slips back. The verse ends without naming what is not to be given; the teaching or initiation is implied.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["seedan","pasu","paasam","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:119"},"t:1689":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1689,"t":["மன்னும் மலம்ஐந்தும் மாற்றும் வகையோரான்","துன்னிய காமாதி தோயும் தொழில்நீங்கான்","பின்னிய பொய்யன் பிறப்பிறப் பஞ்சாதான்","அன்னிய னாவன் அசற்சீட னாமே."],"p":"","r":"He who does not know the way to remove the five abiding impurities, does not give up dipping into lust and the rest, a tangled liar who does not fear birth and death, is an outsider: he is the unworthy disciple.","n":"மலம் ஐந்து, five impurities, extends the usual three (ஆணவம், கன்மம், மாயை); tradition adds மாயேயம் and திரோதாயி. அசற்சீடன் (the unreal or unworthy disciple) is set against சற்சீடன் of the next chapter.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["seedan","malam","kaamam","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:119"},"t:1690":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1690,"t":["தொழுதறி வாளர் கருதிகண் ணாகப்","பழுதறியாத பரம குருவை","வழியறி வார்நல் வழியறி வாளர்","அழிவறி வார்மற்றை யல்லா தவரே."],"p":"","r":"The wise who worship, with scripture as their eye, know the flawless supreme guru; those who know the way are those who know the good way, and the others know only ruin.","n":"கருதி is taken as ச்ருதி, the revealed scripture, so scripture is the eye by which the true guru is recognised. அழிவறிவார் may mean they come to ruin.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["guru","vedham","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:120"},"t:1691":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1691,"t":["பதைதொழிந் தேன்பர மாவுனை நாடி","யதைத்தொழிந் தேன்இனி யாரொடுங் கூடேன்","சிதைத்தடி யேன்வினை சிந்தனை தீர","உதைத்துடை யாயுகந் தாண்டரு ளாயே."],"p":"","r":"Seeking you, Supreme One, I have stopped my trembling and given that up; from now I will join no one else; shatter the deeds of me your servant so that my thinking ends; kick me, my master, and take me gladly into your grace.","n":"The verse is a prayer, the only one in the chapter in the first person. உதைத்து (kicking) may recall Siva's foot that kicked Death, or the guru's foot placed on the disciple in initiation (1698); the plain sense is a plea to be taken by force.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","vinai","iraivan","adiyaar"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:120"},"t:1692":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1692,"t":["பதைக்கின்ற போதே பரமென்னும் வித்தை","விதைக்கின்ற வித்தினை மேல்நின்று நோக்கிச்","சிதைக்கின்ற சிந்தையைச் செவ்வே நிறுத்தி","இசைக்கின்ற அன்பருக் கீயலு மாமே."],"p":"","r":"Even while one trembles, the seed called the Supreme can be given to devoted singers by one who, looking from above at the sowing of the seed, sets their scattering minds straight.","n":"பரம் என்னும் வித்தை may be read as 'the knowledge (வித்தை, vidya) called the Supreme' or 'the seed called the Supreme', and the verse may play on both. Who gives is not named; the guru is implied.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","gnaanam","manam","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:120"},"t:1693":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1693,"t":["கொள்ளினும் நல்ல குருவினைக் கொள்ளுக","உள்ள பொருளுடல் ஆவி யுடன்ஈக","எள்ளத் தனையும் இடைவிடா தேநின்று","தெள்ளி யறியச் சிவபதந் தானே."],"p":"","r":"If you take one, take a good guru; give him the goods you have, your body and your life; stand without break even for a sesame seed's while and know clearly: that is Siva's state itself.","n":"உள்ள பொருள் உடல் ஆவி (goods, body, life) is the classic threefold self-offering to the guru in Saiva tradition. The verse makes unbroken attention, not the gift alone, the way to Siva's state.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["guru","sivam","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:120"},"t:1694":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1694,"t":["சோதி விசாகந் தொடர்ந்திரு தேள்நண்டு","ஓதிய நாளே உணர்வது தானென்று","நீதியுள் நேர்மை நினைந்தவர்க் கல்லது","ஆதியும் ஏதும் அறியகி லானே."],"p":"","r":"Jyoti, Visakha, then the two stars of the Scorpion and the Crab: to all but those who keep uprightness within justice, thinking the named day is when one comes to know, he knows neither the Primal nor anything.","n":"The first line names lunar asterisms (சோதி is Svati) and signs, perhaps days thought fit for initiation. The verse may be setting the upright mind above the auspicious day, or prescribing those days; the grammar of lines 2 to 4 is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","kaalam","naduvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:120"},"t:1695":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1695,"t":["தொழிலார மாமணித் தூய்தான சிந்தை","எழிலால் இறைவன் இடங்கொண்ட போதே","விழலார் விறலாம் வினையது போகக்","கழலார் திருவடி கண்டரு ளாமே."],"p":"","r":"When the Lord, for its beauty, takes his place in a mind made pure like a great jewel polished by work, deeds strong as weeds depart and one sees his anklet-bearing holy feet, and grace comes.","n":"தொழிலார மாமணி, a gem made bright by the jeweller's work, implies effort in purifying the mind. விழலார் விறலாம் வினை (deeds with the strength of weeds) is my reading of an unclear phrase.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["manam","thooimai","vinai","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:120"},"t:1696":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1696,"t":["சாத்திக னாய்ப்பர தத்துவவந் தானுன்னி","ஆத்திக பேத நெறிதோற்ற மாகியே","ஆர்த்த பிறவியி னஞ்சி யறநெறி","சாத்தவல் லானவன் சற்சீட னாமே."],"p":"","r":"He who, being sattvic, thinks on the supreme tattva, comes forth on the path of the faithful, fears the binding round of births, and can keep to the way of dharma: he is the true disciple.","n":"சாத்திகன் is one in whom the quality of sattva rules. ஆத்திக பேத நெறி (the path of the distinctions of belief) is unclear: perhaps the graded paths of the believer as against the unbeliever.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["seedan","thaththuvam","piravi","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:120"},"t:1697":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1697,"t":["சத்தும் அசத்துமெவ் வாறெனத் தானுன்னிச்","சித்தை யுருக்கிக் சிவனருள் கைகாட்டப்","பத்தியின் ஞானம் பெறப்பணிந் தானந்தச்","சத்தியில் இச்சை தகுவோன்சற் சீடனே."],"p":"","r":"He who considers how the real and the unreal stand, melts his mind, bows when Siva's grace points the way so as to gain knowledge through devotion, and whose desire is fit for the Shakti of bliss: he is the true disciple.","n":"சத்து and அசத்து are taken in Siddhanta as the Lord (real) and the bonds (unreal), with the soul between. The verse joins discernment, devotion (பத்தி) and knowledge in one disciple.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["seedan","thiruvarul","anbu","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:120"},"t:1698":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1698,"t":["அடிவைத் தருளுதி யாசானின் றுன்னா","அடிவைத்த மாமுடி மாயப் பிறவி","அடிவைத்த காய அருட்சத்தி யாலே","அடிபெற்ற ஞானத்த னாசற்று ளோனே."],"p":"","r":"'Place your foot on me and grant grace': thinking of this, the teacher places his foot; the deceptive birth of the great head on which the foot is placed ends, and through the gracious Shakti of that body he who has received the foot becomes a knower free of fault.","n":"அடிவைத்து (placing the foot) opens every line. It is traditionally read as the guru's initiation by touch of the foot on the head (திருவடி தீக்கை). Lines 2 and 3 are compressed and my joins are guesses; ஆசு அற்றுளோன் is read as 'free of fault'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","thiruvarul","saththi","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:120"},"t:1699":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1699,"t":["சீராரு ஞானத்தின் இச்சை செலச்செல்ல","வாராத காதல் குருபரன் பாலாகச்","சாராத சாதக நான்குந்தன் பாலுற்றோன்","ஆராயும் ஞானத்த னாமடி வைக்கவே."],"p":"","r":"As the desire for glorious knowledge grows and grows and a love that never came before turns to the supreme guru, he in whom the four means not yet possessed have come becomes a seeker of knowledge, fit for the foot to be placed on him.","n":"சாதகம் நான்கு may be the four qualifications of Vedanta (discrimination, dispassion, the six virtues, longing for release) or the four Siddhanta paths; the verse does not say. அடிவைக்கவே ties the verse to the foot-initiation of 1698.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["seedan","guru","gnaanam","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:120"},"t:1700":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1700,"t":["உணர்த்து மதிபக் குவர்க்கே யுணர்த்தி","இணக்கிற் பராபரத் தெல்லையுள் இட்டுக்","குணக்கொடு தெற்குத் தரபச்சி மங்கொண்","டுணர்த்துமி னாவுடை யாள்தன்னை யுன்னியே."],"p":"","r":"Teach only those of ripe mind; setting them within the bound of the Supreme beyond, and facing east, south, north and west, teach them while meditating on the Lady of the tongue.","n":"நாவுடையாள் may be the goddess of speech or Shakti as the power of the word. The four directions may refer to the seating for initiation or to teaching in every quarter; பராபரத்து எல்லை is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","seedan","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:120"},"t:1701":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1701,"t":["இறையடி தாழ்ந்தை வணக்கமும் எய்திக்","குறையது கூறிக் குணங்கொண்டு போற்றச்","சிறையுடல் நீயறக் காட்டிச் சிவத்தோ","டறிவுக் கறிவிப்போன் சன்மார்க்கி யாமே."],"p":"","r":"When the disciple bows at the Lord's feet with the fivefold salutation, tells his want and praises with good heart, the one who shows him how to be rid of the prison of the body and makes knowledge known to the knower, together with Siva, is the follower of the true way.","n":"ஐ வணக்கம் may be the five-limbed prostration or simply 'the salutation'. சன்மார்க்கி (one on the true way) seems to name the teacher; the verse could also be read of the disciple. சிறையுடல் makes the body a prison.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","vazhipaadu","yaakkai","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:120"},"t:1702":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1702,"t":["வேட்கை விடுநெறி வேதாந்த மாதலால்","வாழ்க்கைப் புனல்வழி மாற்றிச்சித் தாந்தத்து","வேட்கை விடுமிக்க வேதாந்தி பாதமே","தாழ்க்குந் தலையினோன் சற்சீட னாமே."],"p":"","r":"Since Vedanta is the path of giving up desire, the one who turns aside the stream of worldly life and bows his head to the feet of the great Vedantin who gives up desire in Siddhanta is the true disciple.","n":"Tirumular joins the two: the teacher is a Vedantin whose renunciation is grounded in Siddhanta (சித்தாந்தத்து). வாழ்க்கைப் புனல் (the flood of worldly life) is to be diverted, not dammed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["seedan","vedaantham","avaa","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:120"},"t:1703":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1703,"t":["சற்குணம் வாய்மை தயாவிவே கந்தண்மை","சற்குரு பாதமே சாயைபோல் நீங்காமே","சிற்பர ஞானந் தெளியத் தெளிவோர்தல்","அற்புத மேதோன்ற லாகுஞ்சற் சீடனே."],"p":"","r":"Good quality, truth, compassion, discernment and gentleness, never leaving the true guru's feet, like a shadow, and gaining clear sight so that the supreme knowledge of consciousness is clear and wonder appears: such is the true disciple.","n":"The list of virtues (சற்குணம், வாய்மை, தயா, விவேகம், தண்மை) is ethical before it is spiritual. The shadow at the feet is the disciple's closeness; அற்புதம் (wonder) is the dawning of the supreme knowledge.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["seedan","guru","vaaimai","arul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:120"},"t:1704":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1704,"t":["நாலும் இருமூன்றும் ஈரைந்தும் ஈராறும்","கோவிமேல் நின்ற குறிகள் பதினாறும்","மூலம் கண்டு ஆங்கே முடிந்து முதல் இரண்டும்","காலங்கண் டான்அடி காணலும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"Four, six, ten and twelve, the sixteen marks standing above, and the first two: seeing them from the root upward and reaching their end, one may see the feet of the one who has seen time.","n":"The numbers (நாலும் இருமூன்றும் ஈரைந்தும் ஈராறும் ... பதினாறும்) are traditionally the petals of the lotuses from the root (மூலம்) to the throat, with முதல் இரண்டும் as the two petals at the brow. கோவிமேல் is obscure. காலம் கண்டான் may be Siva who mastered time (or Death), or a form of காலகண்டன், the dark-throated one.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aadhaaram","yogam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:121"},"t:1705":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1705,"t":["ஈராறு நாதத்தில் ஈரெட்டாம் அந்தத்தின்","மேதாதி நாதாந்த மீதாம் பாராசக்தி","போதா லயத்துஅ விகாரந்தனிற்போத","மேதாதி ஆதார மீதான உண்மையே."],"p":"","r":"Beyond the twelve of nada and at the end of the sixteen, from medha up to the end of nada, the supreme Shakti stands above, and in the changeless abode of knowing is the truth that lies above the adharas.","n":"மேதாதி நாதாந்தம் and ஈரெட்டு are taken in mantra-yoga as the sixteen kalas of the ascent above the brow, from medha to the end of nada; ஈராறு may be the twelve-finger span above the head. போதாலயம் (abode of knowing) and the third line's division are unclear, and the line may be corrupt.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","aadhaaram","naadham","kalai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:121"},"t:1706":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1706,"t":["மேல்என்றும் கீழ்என்று இரண்டற் காணுங்கால்","தான்என்றும் நான்என்றும் தன்மைகள் ஓராறும்","பார்எங்கும் ஆகிப் பரந்த பராபரம்","கார்ஒன்று கற்பகம் ஆகிநின்றானே."],"p":"","r":"When one sees without the two, above and below, and without the sixfold nature of 'he' and 'I', the Supreme of supremes, spread over all the earth, stands as a wishing tree, generous as a rain cloud.","n":"மேல், கீழ் may be the upper and lower centres or high and low in general; தன்மைகள் ஓராறு (six natures) may be the six adharas or six marks of the ego, and தான், நான் set the self against the other. கார் ஒன்று கற்பகம் joins the wishing tree to the rain cloud, both images of giving without stint.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","meyyunarvu","aadhaaram"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:121"},"t:1707":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1707,"t":["ஆதார சோதனை யால்நாடி சுத்திகள்","மேதாதி ஈரெண் காலந்தத்து விண்ணொளி","போதா லயத்துப் புலன்கர ணம் புத்தி","சாதா ரணங்கெட்டான் தான்சக மார்க்கமே."],"p":"","r":"Purifying the adharas cleanses the nadis, and with the light of the sky reached beyond the sixteen kalas from medha, the senses, the instruments and the intellect lose their ordinary working in the abode of knowing: this is the companion's path.","n":"சகமார்க்கம் (the path of the companion) is the traditional name of yoga as the third of the four Saiva paths. சாதாரணம் கெட்டான் may mean the senses lose their common working, or that the yogi loses what is common to ordinary people. The verse recurs as 1492.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aadhaaram","naadi","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:121"},"t:1708":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1708,"t":["மேதாதி யாலே விடாதுஓம் எனத்தூண்டி","ஆதார சோதனை அத்துவ சோதனை","தாதுஆர மாசுவே தானெழச் சாதித்தால்","ஆதாரஞ் செய்போக மாவது காயமே."],"p":"","r":"Rousing Om without pause from medha onward, purifying the adharas and the paths, and practising until the dross in the bodily elements is driven out, the body itself becomes the fruit the adharas yield.","n":"அத்துவா are traditionally the six paths of Saiva cosmology (of mantra, word, letter, world, tattva and kala). தாது ஆர மாசு may be impurity filling the bodily elements or a reference to semen. The last line, ஆதாரம் செய் போகம் ஆவது காயமே, can mean the body is the fruit of the practice, in the spirit of Tirumular's teaching on perfecting the body, or that the body is what the adharas serve.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piranavam","aadhaaram","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:121"},"t:1709":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1709,"t":["ஆறந்த மும்கூடி யாரும் உடம்பினில்","கூறிய ஆதார மற்றும் குறிக்கொண்மின்","ஆறிய அக்கரம் ஐம்பதின் மேலே","ஊறிய ஆதாரத்து ஓரெழுத்து ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"The six ends meet in every body, so mark the adharas that have been taught: above the fifty letters set in them, in the adhara where it wells up, stands the one letter.","n":"ஆறந்தம் is traditionally a set of six 'ends' (vedanta, siddhanta, nadanta and others). The fifty letters of the alphabet are distributed in tradition over the petals of the adharas, and ஓரெழுத்து (the one letter) is usually taken as Om. ஆறிய (settled, or cooled) is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aadhaaram","mandhiram","piranavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:121"},"t:1710":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1710,"t":["ஆகும் உடம்பும் அழிகின்ற அவ்வுடல்","போகும் உடம்பும் பொருந்திய வாறுதான்","ஆகிய அக்கரம் ஐம்பது தத்துவம்","ஆகும் உடம்புக்கும் ஆறந்த மாமே."],"p":"","r":"However the body that forms, the body that perishes and the body that departs are joined together, the fifty letters, which are tattvas, are the six ends even for the body that is coming to be.","n":"The three bodies may be the gross, subtle and causal bodies, or the body born, the body dying and the subtle body that goes on. The verse makes the letters the stuff of every body; how its two halves connect is not clear from the Tamil.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","thaththuvam","mandhiram"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:121"},"t:1711":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1711,"t":["ஆயு மலரின் அணிமலர் மேலது","ஆய இதழும் பதினாறும் அங்குள","தூய அறிவு சிவானந்த மாகிப்போய்","மேய அறிவாய் விளைந்தது தானே."],"p":"","r":"On the lovely flower above the one that is sought are sixteen petals, and there pure knowledge becomes the bliss of Siva and ripens into abiding knowledge.","n":"A sixteen-petalled lotus is traditionally the throat centre, though the sixteen may be the kalas above the brow named in 1705 and 1707. ஆயும் may mean examined, chosen or fine. The verse recurs as 2359, which ends with the Lord joining (புணர்ந்திருந்தானே) where this ends with knowledge ripening (விளைந்தது).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aanandham","gnaanam","aadhaaram"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:121"},"t:1712":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1712,"t":["இலிங்கம தாவது யாரும் அறியார்","இலிங்கம தாவது எண்டிசை எல்லாம்","இலிங்கம் தாவது எண்ணெண் கலையும்","இலிங்கம தாக எடுத்தது உலகே."],"p":"","r":"No one knows what the linga is: the linga is the eight directions, the linga is the sixty-four arts, and it is the world that has taken form as the linga.","n":"எண்ணெண் கலை (eight eights) are traditionally the sixty-four arts, though கலை may also mean the kalas of cosmology. The last line can be read either way round: the world rose as the linga, or the linga took up the world.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilingam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:122"},"t:1713":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1713,"t":["உலகில் எடுத்தது சத்தி முதலா","உலகில் எடுத்தது சத்தி வடிவாய்","உலகில் எடுத்தது சத்தி குணமாய்","உலகில் எடுத்த சதாசிவன் தானே."],"p":"","r":"What arises in the world arises first as Shakti, in Shakti's form and with Shakti's qualities, and what so arises in the world is Sadasiva himself.","n":"The verse picks up 1712's last word (உலகே) and repeats உலகில் எடுத்தது in every line. In Siddhanta, Sadasiva is the form of Siva where formed and formless meet, the one the linga signifies; the verse makes the world's arising Shakti's and Sadasiva's at once.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saththi","sivam","ilingam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:122"},"t:1714":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1714,"t":["போகமும் முத்தியும் புத்தியும் சித்தியும்","ஆகமும் ஆறாறு தத்துவத்து அப்பாலாம்","ஏகமும் நல்கி இருக்கும் சதாசிவம்","ஆகம அத்துவா ஆறும் சிவமே."],"p":"","r":"Sadasiva remains giving enjoyment, release, understanding and powers, and the oneness beyond the body and the thirty-six tattvas; the six paths of the Agamas too are Siva.","n":"ஆகமும் may be the body (ஆகம்) or the Agama, which changes what lies beyond the tattvas. ஆகம அத்துவா ஆறும் are the six paths (adhvas) of Agamic cosmology, here declared to be Siva himself.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","veedu","thaththuvam","aagamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:122"},"t:1715":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1715,"t":["ஏத்தினர் எண்ணிலி தேவர்எம் ஈசனை","வாழ்த்தினர் வாசப் பசுந்தென்றல் வள்ளலென்று","ஆர்த்தினர் அண்டங் கடந்து அப் புறநின்று","காத்தனன் என்னும் கருத்தறி யாரே."],"p":"","r":"Countless gods praised our Lord and hailed him as the fragrant fresh south breeze and the bountiful one, yet for all their clamour they do not grasp that he stands beyond the worlds and guards them.","n":"The south breeze (தென்றல்) is the gentle wind of Tamil poetry. The point is the gap between praise and understanding: the gods worship loudly (ஆர்த்தினர்) but miss what he is.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","vazhipaadu","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:122"},"t:1716":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1716,"t":["ஒண்சுட ரோன் அயன் மால்பிர சாபதி","ஒண்சுட ரான இரவியோடு இந்திரன்","கண்சுட ராகிக் கலந்தெங்கும் தேவர்கள்","தண்சுட ராய்எங்கும் தற்பரம் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"The bright fire, Brahma, Vishnu, Prajapati, the shining sun and Indra, the gods who mingle everywhere as the light of the eye, are all, as a cool light everywhere, the Supreme.","n":"ஒண்சுடரோன் may be Agni or Rudra. The play on சுடர் (flame) runs through every line, ending in தண்சுடர் (cool flame). The verse can mean the gods are the Supreme, or that the Supreme pervades them as their light.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:122"},"t:1717":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1717,"t":["தாபரத் துள்நின்று அருளவல் லான்சிவன்","மாபரத் துண்மை வழிபடு வாரில்லை","மாபரத் துண்மை வழிபடு வாளர்க்கும்","பூவகத்து உள்நின்ற பொற்கொடி யாகுமே."],"p":"","r":"Siva can give grace from within the fixed form, yet none worship the truth of the great Supreme; and for those who do, it becomes the golden creeper standing within the flower.","n":"தாபரம் (the fixed) is traditionally the installed linga, and மாபரம் the Supreme beyond form. பொற்கொடி (golden creeper) is a name for Shakti, and பூவகம் may be the heart lotus. One reading: grace comes through the installed form though few seek the formless, and those who do find Shakti within.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","ilingam","vazhipaadu","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:122"},"t:1718":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1718,"t":["தூவிய விமானமும் தூலமது ஆகுமால்","ஆய சதாசிவம் ஆகுநற் சூக்குமம்","ஆய பலிபீடம் பத்திர லிங்கமாம்","ஆய அரன்நிலை ஆய்ந்து கொள்வார்கட்கே."],"p":"","r":"To those who study Hara's state, the raised temple tower is the gross linga, Sadasiva is the fine subtle one, and the altar of offerings is the bhadra linga.","n":"The verse reads the temple itself as linga in grades: the vimana (தூலம், gross), Sadasiva (சூக்குமம், subtle), and the bali-pitha (பத்திர லிங்கம்), whose name is technical. Its point is that the whole temple is Siva's form.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilingam","vazhipaadu","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:122"},"t:1719":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1719,"t":["முத்துடன் மாணிக்கம் மொய்த்த பவளமும்","கொத்தும்அக்கொம்பு சிலைநீறு கோமளம்","அத்தன்தன் ஆகமம் அன்னம் அரிசியாம்","உய்த்த்தின் சாதனம் பூமண லிங்கமே."],"p":"","r":"Pearl, ruby and coral, sacred ash and other substances, cooked and raw rice, flowers and sand: for one who seeks to be saved, any of these can be made the linga, the Father's body.","n":"The verse lists materials for lingas, many of them temporary ones made for a single worship in Agamic practice. கொத்தும்அக்கொம்பு and கோமளம் are obscure; அத்தன்தன் ஆகமம் may be the Father's body or his Agama. The list says the material matters less than the worship.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ilingam","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:122"},"t:1720":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1720,"t":["துன்றும் தயிர்நெய் பால்துய்ய மெழுகுடன்","கன்றிய செம்பு கனல்இர தம்சலம்","வன்திறல் செங்கல் வடிவுடை வில்வம்பொன்","தென்தியங்கு ஒன்றை தெளிசிவ லிங்கமே."],"p":"","r":"Thick curd, ghee, milk, pure wax, refined copper, fire, mercury, water, strong brick, bilva and gold: any one of these, clearly known, can be the Sivalinga.","n":"A second list of materials. தென்தியங்கு ஒன்றை is unclear; I take ஒன்றை as 'any one'. இரதம் is usually taken as mercury, used for lingas in later Saiva practice.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ilingam","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:122"},"t:1721":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1721,"t":["மறையவர் அர்ச்சனை வண்படி கந்தான்","இறையவர் அர்ச்சனை யேயபொன் னாகும்","குறைவிலா வசியர்க்குக் கோமள மாகும்","துறையடைச் சூத்திரர் தொல்வாண லிங்கமே."],"p":"","r":"For Brahmins' worship the linga is of crystal, for kings' worship of gold, for prosperous Vaishyas of komalam, and for Shudras the ancient bana linga.","n":"The verse assigns materials by varna; it doesn't say one worship is worth more than another. கோமளம் recurs from 1719 and is unclear (perhaps a gem). The bana linga is traditionally a naturally formed stone from a river.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ilingam","vazhipaadu","pirappu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:122"},"t:1722":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1722,"t":["அது வுணர்ந் தோன்ஒரு தன்மையை நாடி","எதுஉண ராவகை நின்றனன் ஈசன்","புதுஉணர் வான புவனங்கள் எட்டும்","இது உணர்ந்து என்னுடல் கோயில்கொண்டானே."],"p":"","r":"The Lord stood where nothing could know him, even for one who knew and sought his single nature; and he who is the eight worlds of new awareness has, as I have come to know, made my body his temple.","n":"The demonstratives அது, எது, இது in the first and last lines carry the argument and are hard to fix. புவனங்கள் எட்டும் may be the eight worlds or the eightfold form of Siva. Sengalvaraya compares the last line with Tiruvachakam's Pidiththa Paththu 10, where the Lord enters the body as a golden temple.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","yaakkai","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:122"},"t:1723":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1723,"t":["அகலிட மாய்அறி யாமல் அடங்கும்","உகலிட மாய்நின்ற ஊனதன் உள்ளே","பகலிட மாம்முனம் பாவ வினாசன்","புகலிட மாய்நின்ற புண்ணியன் தானே."],"p":"","r":"Inside the flesh, a place made to fall away, he who is the wide world settles unknown; before the light came, the destroyer of sin, the holy one, stood there as refuge.","n":"The verse rhymes அகலிடம் (wide place), உகலிடம் (place of falling away), பகலிடம் (place of day) and புகலிடம் (refuge). பகலிடம் ஆம் முனம் may mean before the light of knowledge dawns, or before the day ends.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","yaakkai","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:122"},"t:1724":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1724,"t":["போது புனைசூழல் பூமிய தாவது","மாது புனைமுடி வானக மாவது","நீதியுள் ஈசன் உடல்விசும் பாய்நிற்கும்","ஆதியுற நின்றது அப்பரி சாமே."],"p":"","r":"The earth is his flower-decked feet, the sky his crown that bears the woman, and the Lord's body stands as space: that is how he stands as the origin.","n":"சூழல் in line 1 is read as கழல் (anklet, feet), which fits the head-to-foot pairing with முடி (crown). The woman on the crown is traditionally Ganga. நீதியுள் may mean 'in right order' or 'the Lord of justice'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","ilingam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:122"},"t:1725":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1725,"t":["தரையுற்ற சத்தி தனிலிங்கம் விண்ணாம்","திரைபொரு நீரது மஞ்சன சாலை","வரைதவழ் மஞ்சுநீர் வானுடு மாலை","கரையற்ற நந்திக் கலையும்திக்காமே."],"p":"","r":"The earth is Shakti as the base and the sky the linga; the wave-beaten sea is its bathing hall, the cloud water on the hills and the stars of the sky its garland, and the directions are the garment of boundless Nandi.","n":"The cosmos is pictured as a linga in worship: Shakti as the pedestal, the sea for its bath, stars for its garland. கலை is read as garment, the directions clothing Siva, as his name திகம்பரன் says; it can also mean digit or kala.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilingam","saththi","vazhipaadu","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:122"},"t:1726":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1726,"t":["மானுடர் ஆக்கை வடிவு சிவலிங்கம்","மானுடர் ஆக்கை வடிவு சிதம்பரம்","மானுடர் ஆக்கை வடிவு சதாசிவம்","மானுடர் ஆக்கை வடிவு திருக்கூத்தே."],"p":"","r":"The form of the human body is the Sivalinga, the form of the human body is Chidambaram, the form of the human body is Sadasiva, and the form of the human body is the holy dance.","n":"The refrain மானுடர் ஆக்கை வடிவு claims the body's very shape (வடிவு), not only what dwells in it. Chidambaram is the hall where Siva dances, and the verse puts the hall, the dancer, the dance and the linga all in the human frame.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","ilingam","thirukkoothu","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:123"},"t:1727":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1727,"t":["உலந்திலர் பின்னும் உளரென நிற்பர்","நிலந்திரு நீர்தெளி யூனவை செய்யப்","புலந்திரு பூதங்கள் ஐந்தும் ஒன்றாக","வலந்தரு தேவரை வந்திசெய் யீரே."],"p":"","r":"Those who have not wasted away stand as though they live on; so, as earth, water and the rest that make the flesh become one with the five elements and the senses, worship the Lord who gives strength.","n":"The first two lines are obscure. உலந்திலர் may be those who have not died, perhaps the perfected who keep their bodies, and நிலந்திரு நீர்தெளி ஊன் may list the elements that make the flesh. வலந்தரு may mean 'who gives strength' or 'circumambulated'. The plain thrust is that the body made of the five elements is where worship is done.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","vazhipaadu","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:123"},"t:1728":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1728,"t":["கோயில்கொண் டன்றே குடிகொண்ட ஐவரும்","வாயில்கொண் டாங்கே வழிநின் றருளுவர்","தாயில்கொண் டாற்போல் தலைவன்என் உட்புக","வாயில்கொண் டு ஈசனும் ஆளவந் தானே."],"p":"","r":"When he took the body as his temple the five took up dwelling there, and keeping its gates they guide and give grace; the Master entered me as if taken into a mother, took the gate, and came to rule me.","n":"The five (ஐவர்) may be the five deities who govern the body in Siddhanta or the five senses turned to the Lord's service. தாயில் கொண்டாற்போல் is unclear: as a child is held in its mother, or as a mother takes in her child. வாயில் (gate) recurs in 1729.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","thiruvarul","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:123"},"t:1729":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1729,"t":["கோயில்கொண் டான்அடி கொல்லைப் பெருமறை","வாயில்கொண் டான்அடி நாடிகள் பத்துள","பூசைகொண் டான்புலன் ஐந்தும் பிறகிட்டு","வாயில்கொண் டான் எங்கள் மாநந்தி தானே."],"p":"","r":"Our great Nandi took the body as his temple, with the great Veda as the ground of his feet and the ten nadis as its gates, and took its worship once the five senses were put behind.","n":"கொல்லைப் பெருமறை (the great Veda of the field) is obscure. The ten nadis are traditionally the body's main channels of breath. புலன் ஐந்தும் பிறகிட்டு (the five senses set behind) makes the worship itself an act of restraint.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","vazhipaadu","naadi","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:123"},"t:1730":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1730,"t":["கூடிய பாதம் இரண்டும் படிமிசை","பாடிய கையிரண்டு எட்டுப் பரந்தெழும்","தேடு முகம்ஐந்து செங்கையின் மூவைந்து","நாடும் சதாசிவம் நல்லொளி முத்தே."],"p":"","r":"Sadasiva, a pearl of good light, stands with his two feet together on the pedestal, with arms spreading two and eight, five faces to be sought, and three fives more.","n":"A meditation image. The two and eight arms make ten, as 1736 says; செங்கையின் மூவைந்து (three fives with the red hands) is unclear, but 1736 gives fifteen eyes, so the fifteen are probably his eyes.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","thiyaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:124"},"t:1731":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1731,"t":["வேதா நெடுமால் உருத்திரன் மேலீசன்","மீதான ஐம்முகன் விந்துவும் நாதமும்","ஆதார சத்தியும் அந்தச் சிவனொடும்","சாதா ரணமாம் சதாசிவந் தானே."],"p":"","r":"Brahma, tall Vishnu, Rudra, Maheshvara above them and the five-faced one above him, bindu and nada, and the supporting Shakti with Siva: Sadasiva is what is common to them all.","n":"The list follows the traditional ascending order of the deities who govern the levels of the cosmos. சாதாரணம் (common) may mean Sadasiva is shared by all of them, or is the general form that includes them.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","vindhu","naadham","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:124"},"t:1732":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1732,"t":["ஆகின்ற சத்தியின் உள்ளே கலைநிலை","ஆகின்ற சத்தியின் உள்ளே கதிரெழ","ஆகின்ற சத்தியின் உள்ளே அமர்ந்தபின்","ஆகின்ற சத்தியுள் அத்திசை பத்தே."],"p":"","r":"Within the Shakti that comes to be, the kalas take their place and the rays rise; once he is seated within her, the ten directions are within that Shakti.","n":"ஆகின்ற சத்தி opens every line. கலை may be the kalas of cosmology, and கதிர் the rays of sun and moon. Who is seated (அமர்ந்த) is not said; Sadasiva is the natural subject.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","kalai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:124"},"t:1733":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1733,"t":["அத்திசைக் குள்ளே அமர்ந்தன ஆறங்கம்","அத்திசைக் குள்ளே அமர்ந்தன நால்வேதம்","அத்திசைக் குள்ளே அமர்ந்த சரியையோடு","அத்திசைக் குள்ளே அமர்ந்த சமயமே."],"p":"","r":"Within those directions are settled the six limbs of the Veda, the four Vedas, the path of chariyai, and the religious systems.","n":"The verse takes up 1732's last words (அத்திசை) and hands its own last word (சமயம்) to 1734. Placing the Vedas, chariyai and the religions within Shakti's directions makes them all products of her unfolding.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vedham","samayam","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:124"},"t:1734":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1734,"t":["சமயத்து எழுந்த அவத்தையீர் ஐந்துள","சமயத்து எழுந்த இராசி ஈராறுள","சமயத்து எழுந்த சரீரம்ஆ றெட்டுள","சமயத்து எழுந்த சதாசிவந் தானே."],"p":"","r":"From that religious order arise the ten states, the twelve signs of the zodiac and the forty-eight bodies, and from it arises Sadasiva himself.","n":"சமயம் may here mean religious system, as in 1733, or appointed time and order. அவத்தை ஈரைந்து (ten states of the soul) and சரீரம் ஆறெட்டு (six eights of bodies) are technical and I can't fix them.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["samayam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:124"},"t:1735":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1735,"t":["நடுவு கிழக்குத் தெற்குஉத் தரமேற்கு","நடுவு படிகநற் குங்குமவன்னம்","அடைவுள அஞ்சனம் செவ்வரத் தம்பால்","அடியேற்கு அருளிய முகம்இவை அஞ்சே."],"p":"","r":"The five faces he gave me, his servant, face the zenith, east, south, north and west, and are coloured crystal, saffron, dark collyrium, red and milk-white.","n":"The faces are traditionally Isana, Tatpurusha, Aghora, Vamadeva and Sadyojata, matched in order with the colours. அடியேற்கு அருளிய (granted to me, the servant) turns an iconographic list into a vision given in grace.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","thiruvarul","thiyaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:124"},"t:1736":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1736,"t":["அஞ்சு முகமுள ஐம்மூன்று கண்ணுள","அஞ்சினொ டுஅஞ்சு கரதலம் தானுள","அஞ்சுடன் அஞ்சா யுதமுள நம்பியென்","நெஞ்சு புகுந்து நிறைந்துநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"He has five faces, fifteen eyes, ten hands and ten weapons, and this Lord has entered my heart and stands filling it.","n":"The play on அஞ்சு (five) fills three lines and gives way in the fourth to நெஞ்சு (heart), so the counted form ends inside the devotee.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","thiyaanam","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:124"},"t:1737":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1737,"t":["சத்தி தராதலம் அண்டம் சதாசிவம்","சத்தி சிவமிக்க தாபர சங்கமம்","சத்தி உருவம் அருவம் சதாசிவம்","சத்தி சிவதத்துவ முப்பத் தாறே."],"p":"","r":"Shakti and Sadasiva are the earth and the cosmos; Shakti and Siva fill all that is fixed and all that moves; Shakti and Sadasiva are form and the formless; Shakti and Siva are the thirty-six tattvas.","n":"Each line opens with சத்தி and pairs her with Siva or Sadasiva, so the verse never names one without the other.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saththi","sivam","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:124"},"t:1738":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1738,"t":["தத்துவ மாவது அருவம் சராசரம்","தத்துவ மாவது உருவம் சுகோதயம்","தத்துவம் எல்லாம் சகலமு மாய்நிற்கும்","தத்துவம் ஆகும் சதாசிவம் தானே."],"p":"","r":"The tattvas are the formless and all that moves and does not move, they are form and the rising of joy; all tattvas stand as everything, and Sadasiva is the tattva itself.","n":"தத்துவம் opens every line, taking up 1737's last word. சுகோதயம் (the rising of joy) is unclear here; it may name a state or the pleasures the tattvas give.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:124"},"t:1739":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1739,"t":["கூறுமின் ஊறு சதாசிவன் எம்இறை","வேறோர் உரைசெய்து மிகைப்பொரு ளாய்நிற்கும்","ஏறுரை செய்தொழில் வானவர் தம்மொடு","மாறுசெய் வான் என் மனம்புகுந் தானே."],"p":"","r":"Declare that Sadasiva, welling up within, is our Lord: describe him in other words and he stands beyond them; apart from the gods whose work is lofty speech, he has entered my mind.","n":"மிகைப்பொருள் (surplus meaning) suggests he exceeds any other description. Line 3 (ஏறுரை செய்தொழில் வானவர்) is unclear: it may be the gods who make proud claims, or ஏறு may be Siva's bull. மாறுசெய்வான் may mean he opposes or he differs from them.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","sol","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:124"},"t:1740":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1740,"t":["இருளார்ந்த கண்டமும் ஏந்து மழுவும்","சுருளார்ந்த செஞ்சடைச் சோதிப் பிறையும்","அருளார்ந்த சிந்தையெம் ஆதிப் பிரானைத்","தெருளார்ந்தென் உள்ளே தெளிந்திருந் தேனே."],"p":"","r":"Clear in understanding, I have held steady within me the first Lord whose mind is full of grace, with his dark throat, raised axe and bright crescent in his curling red hair.","n":"The dark throat (from the poison he drank), the axe and the crescent are Siva's usual marks. The verse moves from seeing the form to holding it within (தெளிந்திருந்தேனே), which is meditation.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","thiyaanam","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:124"},"t:1741":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1741,"t":["சத்திதான் நிற்கின்ற ஐம்முகம் காற்றிடில்","உத்தமம் வாமம் உரையத்து இருந்திடும்","தத்துவம் பூருவம் தற்புரு டன்சிரம்","அத்தரு கோரம் மருடத்துஈ சானனே."],"p":"","r":"Telling the five faces in which Shakti stands, the verse places Vamadeva as the highest, Tatpurusha in the east as the head, and Aghora and Isana after them.","n":"The verse is hard to divide. காற்றிடில் may be for கூற்றிடில் (if one tells); உரையத்து, தத்துவம் பூருவம் and அத்தரு கோரம் மருடத்து do not divide clearly. The five faces are also the five Brahma mantras of Agamic worship.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","saththi","mandhiram"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:124"},"t:1742":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1742,"t":["நாணுநல் ஈசானன் நடுவுச்சி தானாகும்","தாணுவின் தன்முகம் தற்புருட மாகும்","காணும் அகோரம் இருதயம் குய்யமாம்","மாணுற வாமம்ஆம் சத்திநற் பாதமே."],"p":"","r":"Isana is the crown of the head, Tatpurusha the face, Aghora the heart, Vamadeva the secret parts, and Shakti the feet.","n":"This is the placing (nyasa) of the five faces on the worshipper's body. The feet traditionally belong to Sadyojata, so சத்தி here may name that face or the power in it. தாணுவின் தன்முகம் (Sthanu's own face) is read as the face.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","mandhiram","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:124"},"t:1743":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1743,"t":["நெஞ்சு சிரம்சிகை நீள்கவ சம்கண்ணாம்","வஞ்சமில் விந்து வளர்நிறம் பச்சையாம்","செஞ்சுறு செஞ்சுடர் சேகரி மின்னாகும்","செஞ்சுடர் போலும் தெசாயுதம் தானே."],"p":"","r":"Heart, head, tuft, long armour and eye are the limbs; the flawless bindu is green, the crest flashes like lightning, and the ten weapons are like a red flame.","n":"நெஞ்சு, சிரம், சிகை, கவசம், கண் are the limb-mantras (anga mantras) of Agamic worship, with the weapon (astra) completing them. The colours belong to the tradition of meditation on them; சேகரி may mean the crest or a name of Shakti.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","vindhu","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:124"},"t:1744":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1744,"t":["எண்ணில் இதயம் இறைஞான சத்தியாம்","விண்ணிற் பரைசிரம் மிக்க சிகையாதி","வண்ணங் கவசம் வனப்புடை இச்சையாம்","பண்ணுங் கிரியை பரநேந் திரத்திலே."],"p":"","r":"The heart is the Lord's power of knowledge, the head the supreme Shakti, the tuft and the armour his power of will, and his power of action is in the supreme eye.","n":"The limbs of 1743 are matched with the Lord's powers of knowledge, will and action (ஞான, இச்சை, கிரியை). How சிகையாதி and வண்ணம் divide between tuft and armour is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","mandhiram","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:124"},"t:1745":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1745,"t":["சத்திநாற் கோணம் சலமுற்று நின்றிடும்","சத்திஅறு கோண சயனத்தை உற்றிடும்","சத்தி வட்டம் சலமுற்று இருந்திடும்","சத்தி உருவாம் சதாசிவன் தானே."],"p":"","r":"Shakti stands moving in the square, rests in the hexagon, and stays moving in the circle; Sadasiva is Shakti's form.","n":"சலம் may be water or movement. The square, hexagon and circle may be the shapes of a linga's pedestal or the diagrams of worship. சயனம் (lying, bed) is unclear here.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","sivam","ilingam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:124"},"t:1746":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1746,"t":["மான் நந்தி எத்தனை காலம் அழைக்கினும்","தான் நந்தி அஞ்கின் தனிச்சுடை ராய்நிற்கும்","கால் நந்தி உந்தி கடந்து கமலத்தில்","மேல் நந்தி ஒன்பதின் மேவிநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"However long one calls on Nandi who bears the deer, he stands in the five as a single flame; rising past the navel to the lotus, Nandi stands above, in the nine.","n":"நந்தி ends a word in every line. மான் நந்தி may be Siva holding the deer or 'great Nandi'. The five, the navel, the lotus and the nine are yogic places in the body, but which ones is not fixed; கால் நந்தி may be the breath.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:124"},"t:1747":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1747,"t":["ஒன்றிய வாறும் உடலின் உடன்கிடந்து","என்றும்எம் ஈசன் நடக்கும் இயல்பது","தென்தலைக்கு ஏறத் திருந்து சிவனடி","நின்று தொழுதேன் என் நெஞ்சத்தின் உள்ளே."],"p":"","r":"Our Lord stays joined with the body and always moves within it; when it rose to the head and was set right, I stood and worshipped Siva's feet within my heart.","n":"What rises to the head (தென்தலைக்கு ஏற) is unstated; tradition takes it as the breath or kundalini. தென்தலை may be 'the fair head' or a corruption. The worship is inner: the feet are found in the heart.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","yaakkai","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:124"},"t:1748":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1748,"t":["உணர்ந்தேன் உலகினில் ஒண்பொரு ளானைக்","கொணர்ந்தேன் குவலயம் கோயிலென் நெஞ்சம்","புணர்ந்தேன் புனிதனும் பொய்யல்ல மெய்யே","பணிந்தேன் பகலவன் பாட்டும் ஒலியே."],"p":"","r":"I came to know the bright reality in the world, brought him in and made my heart his temple on earth; I have joined the Pure One, and this is truth, not falsehood; I bowed, and the sun's song is the sound.","n":"The verbs run in sequence: knew, brought, joined, bowed. குவலயம் கோயில் என் நெஞ்சம் can mean my heart is a temple on earth, or the earth and my heart are both his temple. The last phrase, பகலவன் பாட்டும் ஒலியே, is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","meyyunarvu","manam","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:124"},"t:1749":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1749,"t":["ஆங்கவை Yமூன்றினும் ஆரழல் வீசிடத்","தாங்கிடும் ஈரேழு தான்நடு வானதில்","ஓங்கிய ஆதியும் அந்தமும் ஆமென","ஈங்கிவை தம்முடல் இந்துவும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"When fierce fire blazes through those three, the fourteen bear it, and in their midst rise the beginning and the end; these here become one's body and the moon.","n":"The three may be the sun, moon and fire, or three nadis, and ஈரேழு (fourteen) may be the worlds or the body's regions. இந்து (moon) is traditionally the cooling nectar at the head. I can't fix the sense with any confidence.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["yogam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:124"},"t:1750":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1750,"t":["தன்மேனி தற்சிவ லிங்கமாய் நின்றிடும்","தன்மேனி தானும் சதாசிவ மாய்நிற்கும்","தன்மேனி தற்சிவன் தற்சிவா னந்தமாய்","தன்மேனி தானாகும் தற்பரம் தானே."],"p":"","r":"One's own body stands as one's Sivalinga, one's body stands as Sadasiva, one's body is one's own Siva and Siva-bliss, and one's body becomes the Supreme itself.","n":"தன்மேனி (one's own body) opens every line, and தற்- (one's own) is prefixed to linga, Siva and bliss. The verse draws the chapter from Sadasiva's form to the worshipper's own body.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","ilingam","sivam","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:124"},"t:1751":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1751,"t":["ஆரும் அறியார் அகாரம் அவனென்று","பாரும் உகாரம் பரந்திட்ட நாயகி","தாரம் இரண்டும் தரணி முழுதுமாய்","மாறி எழுந்திடும் ஓசையதாமே."],"p":"","r":"No one knows that he is the letter A and that she, the Lady spread through the world, is U; the two together become the whole earth and rise, transformed, as sound.","n":"தாரம் may mean consort, so the two are Siva and Shakti, or the sacred syllable (tara, Om), of which A and U are parts. Sengalvaraya compares the first line with Kural 1.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","saththi","piranavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:124"},"t:1752":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1752,"t":["இலிங்கநற் பீடம் இசையும்ஓங் காரம்","இலிங்கநற் கண்டம் நிறையும் மகாரம்","இலிங்கத்து உள் வட்டம் நிறையும் உகாரம்","இலிங்கம் அகாரம் நிறைவிந்து நாதமே."],"p":"","r":"In the linga, Om is the good pedestal, M fills its neck, U fills its inner circle, and A is the linga itself, filled with bindu and nada.","n":"The syllable Om is mapped onto the parts of the linga: pedestal, neck (கண்டம்), inner circle and shaft, with bindu and nada, the dot and resonance of Om, in the shaft. இலிங்கம் opens every line.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilingam","piranavam","vindhu","naadham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:124"},"t:1753":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1753,"t":["அகார முதலா அனைத்துமாய் நிற்கும்","உகார முதலா உயிர்ப்பெய்து நிற்கும்","அகார உகாரம் இரண்டும் அறியில்","அகார உகாரம் இலிங்கம் தாமே."],"p":"","r":"With A first he stands as everything, with U first he stands having entered the lives; if one knows both A and U, A and U are themselves the lingam.","n":"அகாரம் and உகாரம் are the first two parts of the syllable Om, traditionally matched with Siva and Shakti, or with the Lord as all things and the Lord within souls. The verse names no subject; the reading supplies 'he'. Its first line closely echoes the Kural's first verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ilingam","piranavam","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:125"},"t:1754":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1754,"t":["ஆதாரம் ஆதேயம் ஆகின்ற விந்துவும்","மேதாதி நாதமும் மீதே விரிந்தன","ஆதார விந்து ஆதிபீட நாமே","போதாஇ லிங்கப் புணர்ச்சிய தாமே."],"p":"","r":"Bindu, which is both the support and what it supports, and nada with medha and the rest spread out above; the supporting bindu is the first pedestal, and their union is the lingam.","n":"ஆதாரம் and ஆதேயம் (support and supported) are technical terms; மேதாதி is taken as medha and the other kalas of the syllable above nada. போதா இலிங்கப் புணர்ச்சி is obscure; the plain sense is that the pedestal and the shaft together make the lingam.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ilingam","vindhu","naadham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:125"},"t:1755":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1755,"t":["சத்தி சிவமாம் இலிங்கமே தாபரம்","சத்தி சிவமாம் இலிங்கமே சங்கமம்","சத்தி சிவமாம் இலிங்கம் சதாசிவம்","சத்தி சிவமாகும் தாபரம் தானே."],"p":"","r":"The lingam that is Shakti and Siva is the fixed one, the lingam that is Shakti and Siva is the moving one, the lingam that is Shakti and Siva is Sadasiva, and Shakti becoming Siva is itself the fixed lingam.","n":"தாபரம் (the installed, immovable lingam) and சங்கமம் (the moving one, traditionally the devotee or the guru as a living lingam) are paired. The repetition makes one point: whatever its form, the lingam is Shakti joined with Siva.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilingam","saththi","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:125"},"t:1756":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1756,"t":["தானேர் எழுகின்ற சோதியைக் காணலாம்","வானேர் எழுகின்ற ஐம்பது அமர்ந்திடம்","பூரேர் எழுகின்ற பொற்கொடி தன்னுடன்","தானேர் எழுகின்ற அகாரமது ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"One can see the light that rises straight up of itself, the place where the fifty rising toward the sky dwell; together with the golden creeper rising in the body, what rises straight up of itself is the letter A.","n":"ஐம்பது (fifty) is taken as the fifty letters, and பொற்கொடி (the golden creeper) as Shakti or kundalini. பூரேர் is obscure, perhaps a form of பூர் or a misreading; I take it as the body. The verse ends where 1753 began, with A as the lingam.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ilingam","kundalini","piranavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:125"},"t:1757":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1757,"t":["விந்துவும் நாதமும் மேவும் இலிங்கமாம்","விந்துவ தேபீட நாதம் இலிங்கமாம்","அந்த இரண்டையும் ஆதார தெய்வமாய்","வந்து கருஐந்தம் செய்யும் அவைஐந்தே."],"p":"","r":"The lingam is where bindu and nada meet: bindu is the pedestal and nada the lingam; these two, as the supporting deities, come and perform the five acts of creation, and those are five.","n":"கருஐந்தம் is unclear; read as the five acts beginning with creation (கரு, the embryo or making). Tradition matches bindu with Shakti and nada with Siva, as the pedestal and the shaft.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ilingam","vindhu","naadham","aindhozhil"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:125"},"t:1758":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1758,"t":["சத்திநற் பீடம் தகுநல்ல ஆன்மா","சத்திநற் கண்டம் தகுவித்தை தானாகும்","சத்திநல் லிங்கம் தகும்சிவ தத்துவம்","சத்திநல் ஆன்மாச் சதாசிவம் தானே."],"p":"","r":"Shakti as the good pedestal is the soul; Shakti as the good middle part is vidya; Shakti as the good lingam is the Siva tattva; and Shakti with the good soul is Sadasiva.","n":"The three parts of the installed lingam (base, middle and shaft) are matched with the three classes of tattvas, atma, vidya and Siva; கண்டம் is taken as the middle section. The last line, joining Shakti and soul as Sadasiva, is less clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ilingam","thaththuvam","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:125"},"t:1759":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1759,"t":["மனம்புகுந்து என்னுயிர் மன்னிய வாழ்க்கை","மனம்புகுந்து இன்பம் பொழிகின்ற போது","நலம்புகுந்து என்னொடு நாதனை நாடும்","இனம்புகுந்து ஆதியும் மேற்கொண்டவாறே."],"p":"","r":"When he entered my mind, my life became settled, and when he entered my mind and poured down bliss, goodness entered and sought the Lord with me, and the First One entered among his own and took me over.","n":"The subject is unstated until ஆதி (the First) in the last line. இனம்புகுந்து may mean entering the company of devotees or joining his own kind. The repeated புகுந்து (entered) carries the verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["manam","aanandham","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:125"},"t:1760":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1760,"t":["பராபரன் எந்தை பனிமதி சூடி","தராபரன் தன்னடி யார்மனக் கோயில்","சிராபரன் தேவர்கள் சென்னியின் மன்னும்","அராபரன் மன்னி மனத்துஉறைந் தானே."],"p":"","r":"The Supreme, my Father who wears the cool moon, the support of the world, whose temple is the minds of his devotees, the Lord who abides on the heads of the gods, the one who wears the snake, has settled and dwells in my mind.","n":"Each line turns on a word ending in -பரன் (Lord): பராபரன், தராபரன், சிராபரன், அராபரன். The devotee's mind as the Lord's temple is the chapter's turn from the outer lingam to the inner one.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","adiyaar","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:125"},"t:1761":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1761,"t":["பிரான்அல்ல நாம்எனில் பேதை உலகம்","குரால்என்னும் என்மனம் கோயில்கொள் ஈசன்","அராநின்ற செஞ்சடை அங்கியும் நீரும்","பொராநின் றவர்செய்அப் புண்ணியன் தானே."],"p":"","r":"If I say 'we are not the Lord's', the foolish world will call my mind a ruined thing; the Lord who has made my mind his temple, with the snake on his red matted hair where fire and water contend, is that holy one.","n":"குரால் is obscure: it may mean something dull or faded, or a decaying thing, and the first two lines may be read differently. The image of fire and the Ganga contending on Siva's hair is a common one.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","manam","adiyaar"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:125"},"t:1762":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1762,"t":["அன்று நின் றான்கிடந் தான்அவன் என்று","சென்றுநின்று எண்டிசை ஏத்துவர் தேவர்கள்","என்றுநின்று ஏத்துவன் எம்பெரு மான்தன்னை","ஒன்றியென் உள்ளத்தின் உள்ளிருந் தானே."],"p":"","r":"The gods go about the eight directions and praise him, saying 'there he stood, there he lay'; I stand and praise my Lord at all times, and he is one with me and dwells within my heart.","n":"நின்றான் கிடந்தான் (he stood, he lay) is often taken as the standing and reclining forms worshipped in temples, which the gods seek outwardly. The contrast is between seeking him abroad and finding him within; அன்று and என்று may also mean 'then' and 'always'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","vazhipaadu","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:125"},"t:1763":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1763,"t":["உருவும் அருவும் உருவோடு அருவும்","மருவு பரசிவன் மன்பல் உயிர்க்கும்","குருவு மெனநிற்கும் கொள்கையன் ஆகும்","தருவென நல்கும் சதாசிவன் தானே."],"p":"","r":"The supreme Siva, who takes form, formlessness and form joined with formlessness, stands for the many souls with the purpose of being their guru too, and Sadasiva gives like the wishing tree.","n":"உருவும் அருவும் உருவோடு அருவும் (form, the formless, and form with the formless) is the Siddhanta division of the Lord's nine manifestations, set out in the next verse. தரு is taken as the wishing tree of the gods (karpaka).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","guru","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:126"},"t:1764":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1764,"t":["நாலான கீழது உருவம் நடுநிற்க","மேலான நான்கும் அருவம் மிகுநாப்பண்","நாலான ஒன்றும் அருவுரு நண்ணலால்","பாலாம் இவையாம் பரசிவன் தானே."],"p":"","r":"The four below are form, the four above are formless, and the one in the middle, standing among them, is form and formless; these are his parts, and they are the supreme Siva himself.","n":"The verse gives only the numbers. Tradition names the four forms as Brahma, Vishnu, Rudra and Mahesvara, the four formless as bindu, nada, Shakti and Siva, and the middle as Sadasiva, who is worshipped as the lingam; 1776 lists the same nine.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","thaththuvam","ilingam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:126"},"t:1765":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1765,"t":["தேவர் பிரானைத் திசைமுக நாதனை","நால்வர் பிரானை நடுவுற்ற நந்தியை","ஏவர் பிரான்என்று இறைஞ்சுவர் அவ்வழி","யாவர் பிரானடி அண்ணலும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"Those who bow to him as the lord of the gods, the lord of the four-faced one, the lord of the four, Nandi who stands in the middle, saying 'lord of whom?', find by that path that the Great One is the lord of all at whose feet they are.","n":"நால்வர் (the four) is traditionally Sanaka and the three other sages taught by Siva as Dakshinamurti. ஏவர் பிரான் என்று is ambiguous: a question, or an exclamation ('lord of every one!').","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","iraivan","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:126"},"t:1766":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1766,"t":["வேண்டிநின் றேதொழு தேன்வினை போயற","ஆண்டொரு திங்களும் நாளும் அளக்கின்ற","காண்டகை யானொடும் கன்னி உணரினும்","மூண்டகை மாறினும் ஒன்றது வாமே."],"p":"","r":"I stood longing and worshipped so that my deeds would leave me; whether the Virgin is known together with the visible one who measures out the year, the month and the day, or the kindled one changes, it is one.","n":"The second half is obscure. The one who measures years, months and days is perhaps the sun, or the Lord as time; கன்னி (the Virgin) is taken as Shakti; மூண்டகை மாறினும் is unclear. The last words, that it is one, suggest the Lord and Shakti are one under every form.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","vinai","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:126"},"t:1767":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1767,"t":["ஆதி பரந்தெய்வம் அண்டத்து நல்தெய்வம்","சோதி அடியார் தொடரும் பெருந்தெய்வம்","நீதியுள் மாதெய்வம் நின்மலர் எம்இறை","பாதியுள் மன்னும் பராசத்தி யாமே."],"p":"","r":"The first, supreme God, the good God of the cosmos, the great God whom the devotees of light follow, the great God within justice, the stainless one, our Lord: Parashakti abides in his half.","n":"The list of epithets ends by placing Parashakti in the Lord's half (பாதியுள்), the image of Siva as half woman. சோதி அடியார் may be devotees who see the light, or the devotees of the one who is light.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saththi","sivam","adiyaar"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:126"},"t:1768":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1768,"t":["சத்திக்கு மேலே பராசத்தி தன்னுள்ளே","சுத்த சிவபதம் தோயாத தூவொளி","அத்தன் திருவடிக்கு அப்பாலைக்கு அப்பாலாம்","ஒத்தவும் ஆம்ஈசன் தானான உண்மையே."],"p":"","r":"Above Shakti is Parashakti, and within her is the pure state of Siva, an untouched clear light, beyond the beyond of the Father's holy feet; this is the truth of the Lord being himself, and it is also what accords.","n":"The grades Shakti, Parashakti and pure Siva state are technical. ஒத்தவும் ஆம் in the last line is unclear: perhaps that Siva and Shakti are equal, or that this matches the teaching.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","sivam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:126"},"t:1769":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1769,"t":["கொழுந்தினைக் காணில் குவலயம் தோன்றும்","எழுந்திடம் காணில் இருக்கலும் ஆகும்","பரந்திடம் காணில் பார்ப்பதி மேலே","திரண்டெழக் கண்டவன் சிந்தையு ளானே."],"p":"","r":"If one sees the tip, the world appears; if one sees the place where it rises, one can abide; if one sees where it spreads, it is above Parvati; the one who saw it gather and rise is in my thought.","n":"கொழுந்து (tip, sprout or flame) has no stated subject. It may be the lingam's top, the flame of light in yoga, or kundalini rising; பார்ப்பதி மேலே may mean above Parvati or above what is seen. A symbolic reading is the only one that makes sense here.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","saththi","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:126"},"t:1770":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1770,"t":["எந்தை பரமனும் என்னம்மை கூட்டமும்","முந்த உரைத்து முறைசொல்லின் ஞானமாம்","சந்தித்து இருந்த இடம்பெருங் கண்ணியை","உந்தியின் மேல்வைத்து உகந்து இருந்தானே."],"p":"","r":"Speaking first of the union of my Father, the Supreme, and my Mother, and then telling it in order, is jnana; he set the great-eyed one above the navel at the place where they met, and dwelt there rejoicing.","n":"பெருங் கண்ணி (the great-eyed woman, Shakti, or a great garland) and உந்தியின் மேல் (above the navel, a centre of the yogic body) are taken symbolically; tradition reads it as the Lord holding Shakti in a bodily centre.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","saththi","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:126"},"t:1771":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1771,"t":["சத்தி சிவன்விளை யாட்டாம் உயிராகி","ஒத்த இருமாயா கூட்டத்து இடையூட்டிச்","சுத்தம தாகும் துரியம் பிறிவித்துச்","சித்தம் புகுந்து சிவம்அகம் ஆக்குமே."],"p":"","r":"In the play of Shakti and Siva, becoming life and feeding it amid the joined two mayas, then separating out the pure turiya, it enters the mind and makes the self Siva.","n":"இருமாயா is taken as pure and impure maya; துரியம் is the fourth state of consciousness. The subject that becomes life and enters the mind is unstated: the play, or Shakti and Siva.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","maayai","uyir","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:126"},"t:1772":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1772,"t":["சத்தி சிவன்தன் விளையாட்டுத் தாரணி","சத்தி சிவமுமாம் சிவன்சத் தியுமாகும்","சத்தி சிவமன்றித் தாபரம் வேறில்லை","சத்திதான் என்றும் சமைந்துரு வாகுமே."],"p":"","r":"The earth is the play of Shakti and Siva; Shakti becomes Siva and Siva becomes Shakti; there is no fixed thing other than Shakti and Siva, and it is always Shakti who takes shape as form.","n":"தாபரம் here can mean the fixed world or the installed lingam, as in 1755; both readings say the same: nothing stands apart from Shakti and Siva. The last line gives form to Shakti, leaving Siva formless.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saththi","sivam","ilingam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:126"},"t:1773":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1773,"t":["குரைக்கின்ற வாரிக் குவலய நீரும்","பரக்கின்ற காற்றுப் பயில்கின்ற தீயும்","நிரைக்கின்ற வாறிவை நீண்டகன் றானை","வரைத்து வலம்செயு மாறுஅறி யேனே."],"p":"","r":"The roaring sea, the water of the earth, the spreading wind and the fire that dwells in things: he stretches long and wide in their ordered array, and I do not know how to bound him and walk round him.","n":"வலம்செய்தல் is circumambulation, walking round a shrine in worship. The difficulty is that the Lord is the elements themselves, so there is no outside from which to circle him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:127"},"t:1774":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1774,"t":["வரைத்து வலஞ்செய்யு மாறுஇங்குஒன்று உண்டு","நிரைத்து வருகங்கை நீர்மலர் ஏந்தி","உரைத்து அவன் நாமம் உணரவல் லார்க்குப்","புரைத்துஎங்கும் போகான் புரிசடை யோனே."],"p":"","r":"There is one way here to bound him and walk round him: for those who bear the flowing Ganga water and flowers and can utter his name and know him, the one with twisted matted hair does not go off anywhere and hide.","n":"The answer to 1773 is worship with water, flowers and the name, joined to knowing (உணரவல்லார்). புரைத்து எங்கும் போகான் may also mean that he does not slip away through any gap.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","sivam","mandhiram"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:127"},"t:1775":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1775,"t":["ஒன்றெனக் கண்டோ ம் ஈசன் ஒருவனை","நன்றென்று அடியிணை நான்அவனைத்தொழ","வென்றுஐம் புலமும் மிகக்கிடந்து இன்புற","அன்றுஎன்று அருள்செய்யும் ஆதிப் பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"We have seen the Lord as one alone; as I worship his pair of feet as the good, the First Lord gives grace then and there, so that the five senses are conquered and lie subdued and one rejoices.","n":"அன்று என்று is unclear: 'on that day', or 'saying it is not so'. மிகக்கிடந்து may describe the senses lying overcome or the devotee lying prostrate.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvadi","thiruvarul","adakkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:127"},"t:1776":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1776,"t":["மலர்ந்த அயன்மால் உருத்திரன் மகேசன்","பலந்தரும் ஐம்முகன் பரவிந்து நாதம்","நலந்தரும் சத்தி சிவன்வடி வாகிப்","பலந்தரு லிங்கம் பராநந்தி யாமே."],"p":"","r":"Brahma who blossomed, Vishnu, Rudra, Mahesa, the five-faced one who gives fruit, supreme bindu and nada, Shakti who gives good and Siva: taking these forms, the lingam that gives fruit is the supreme Nandi.","n":"These are the nine forms of 1764, named. ஐம்முகன் (the five-faced) is Sadasiva. Calling the lingam பராநந்தி makes the guru's name, Nandi, the name of the whole.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilingam","sivam","vindhu","naadham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:127"},"t:1777":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1777,"t":["மேவி எழுகின்ற செஞ்சுடர் ஊடுசென்று","ஆவி எழும்அள வன்றே உடலுற","மேவப் படுவதும் விட்டு நிகழ்வதும்","பாவித்து அடக்கிற் பரகதி தானே."],"p":"","r":"Going through the red flame that rises, while the breath rises only as far as the body allows, if one contemplates what is to be joined and what goes on leaving it, and restrains it, that is the supreme state.","n":"செஞ்சுடர் (red flame) is taken as the inner fire or kundalini, and ஆவி as the breath. The second and third lines are hard to construe: they may contrast the breath that is held with the breath that goes out.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["praanan","yogam","thiyaanam","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:127"},"t:1778":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1778,"t":["உடல்பொருள் ஆவி உதகத்தாற் கொண்டு","படர்வினை பற்றறப் பார்த்துக்கை வைத்து","நொடியின் அடிவைத்து நுண்ணுணர் வாக்கி","கடியப் பிறப்பறக் காட்டினன் நந்தியே."],"p":"","r":"Taking body, wealth and life with water, looking so that the clinging of spreading deeds is cut, placing his hand, setting his feet in an instant and making the awareness subtle, Nandi showed the way that cuts off birth.","n":"உதகத்தால் கொண்டு (taking with water) is the gesture of a gift sealed by pouring water; the disciple's body, wealth and life (tanu, artha, prana) are given over to the guru. The look, the hand and the feet are traditionally the initiations by sight, touch and the feet.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["guru","thiruvadi","vinai","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:128"},"t:1779":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1779,"t":["உயிரும் சரீரமும் ஒண்பொரு ளான","வியவார் பரமும்பின் மேவும் பிராணன்","செயலார் சிவமும் சிற்சத்தி ஆதிக்கே","உயலார் குருபரன் உய்யக் கொண்டானே."],"p":"","r":"Life and body as the bright possession, the wondrous Supreme, the breath that follows after, Siva who acts and Chit-Shakti: the supreme guru took me, unable to save myself, so that I might be saved for the First One.","n":"The list in the first three lines is hard to construe; it may name what the guru took over or the levels through which he raised the disciple. உயலார் (those who cannot save themselves) is read as the disciple.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","uyir","praanan","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:128"},"t:1780":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1780,"t":["பச்சிம திக்கலே வைத்தஆ சாரியன்","நிச்சலும் என்னை நினையென்ற அப்பொருள்","உச்சிக்கும் கீழது உள்நாக்கு மேலது","வைச்ச பதமிது வாய்திற வாதே."],"p":"","r":"The thing the teacher set in the western direction and told me to think on every day lies below the crown and above the uvula; this is the state he set, with the mouth not opened.","n":"பச்சிம திக்கு (the western direction) is taken as the back of the head or the hidden rear path of the breath. The place between crown and palate and the closed mouth point to a yogic practice, but the verse does not spell it out.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","thiyaanam","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:128"},"t:1781":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1781,"t":["பிட்டடித்து எங்கும் பிதற்றித் திரிவோனை","ஒட்டடித்து உள்ளமர் மாசெலாம் வாங்கித்","தட்டொக்க மாறினன் தன்னையும் என்னையும்","வட்டமது ஒத்தது வாணிபம் வாய்த்ததே."],"p":"","r":"Me, who wandered babbling everywhere, he swept clean, drew out all the dirt that sat within, and exchanged himself and me as on a balance: the rate matched and the trade turned out well.","n":"The image is a merchant's: தட்டொக்க (the scale pans level), வட்டம் (the rate or commission in an exchange), வாணிபம் (trade). The guru takes the disciple and gives himself. பிட்டடித்து is obscure; it may mean beating about, or be corrupt.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","malam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:128"},"t:1782":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1782,"t":["தரிக்கின்ற பல்லுயிர்க்கு எல்லாம் தலைவன்","இருக்கின்ற தன்மையை ஏதும் உணரார்","பிரிக்கின்ற விந்து பிணக்கறுத்து எல்லாம்","கருக்கொண்ட ஈசனைக் கண்டுகொண்டேனே."],"p":"","r":"People do not know at all how the Lord of all the many lives that are borne abides; cutting through the strife of the dividing bindu, I have found the Lord who holds everything as in a womb.","n":"பிரிக்கின்ற விந்து (the bindu that divides) is taken as maya, which sets things apart; பிணக்கு is the conflict or tangle it makes. கருக்கொண்ட may mean holding all as a womb holds, or holding the seed of all.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pathi","vindhu","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:128"},"t:1783":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1783,"t":["கூடும் உடல்பொருள் ஆவி குறிக்கொண்டு","நாடி அடிவைத்து அருள்ஞான சத்தியால்","பாடல் உடலினில் பற்றற நீக்கியே","கூடிய தானவ னாம்குளிக் கொண்டே."],"p":"","r":"Taking note of the body, wealth and life that come together, seeking and setting his feet, with the power of grace and jnana he removed all clinging to the body of suffering, and became one with what he had joined, having plunged into it.","n":"This repeats the rite of 1778. பாடல் உடல் is read as பாடு (suffering) with the body; குளிக்கொண்டே (having plunged, or having immersed) is obscure, perhaps the disciple immersed in the Lord.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","thiruvadi","thiruvarul","pattru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:128"},"t:1784":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1784,"t":["கொண்டான் அடியேன் அடிமை குறிக்கொள்ளக்","கொண்டான் உயிர்பொருள் காயக் குழாத்தினைக்","கொண்டான் பலமுற்றும் தந்தவன் கோடலால்","கொண்டான் எனஒன்றும் கூறகி லானே."],"p":"","r":"He took my servitude as his own, he took my throng of life, wealth and body, and since he who gave all fruit has taken them, there is nothing I can say he took.","n":"கொண்டான் (he took) opens every line. The turn is that what he took was his own gift, so it cannot be counted as taken; the disciple's surrender gives nothing the guru did not already hold.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["guru","adiyaar","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:128"},"t:1785":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1785,"t":["குறிக்கின்ற தேகமும் தேகியும் கூடி","நெறிக்கும் பிராணன் நிலைபெற்ற சீவன்","பறிக்கின்ற காயத்தைப் பற்றியநேர்மை","பிறக்க அறியாதார் பேயுடன் ஒப்பரே."],"p":"","r":"Those who do not come to know the truth of how the body and the one in the body join, the breath that guides, the soul that stays firm, and what holds the body that is taken away, are like ghosts.","n":"தேகம் and தேகி (body and the embodied one) are a pair; பறிக்கின்ற காயம் may be the body that is snatched away at death. The comparison with ghosts is that they have form without understanding.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","uyir","praanan","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:128"},"t:1786":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1786,"t":["உணர்வுடை யார்கட்கு உலகமும் தோன்றும்","உணர்வுடை யார்கட்கு உறுதுயர் இல்லை","உணர்வுடை யார்கள் உணர்ந்தஅக் காலம்","உணர்வுடை யார்கண் உணர்ந்துகண் டாரே."],"p":"","r":"To those with awareness the world appears as it is; those with awareness have no suffering that befalls them; the time when those with awareness came to know, those with awareness knew it and saw.","n":"உணர்வுடையார் (those who have awareness) opens every line. The last two lines are circular as written and their point is unclear; perhaps that the moment of knowing is known only by the knower.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["meyyunarvu","idumbai","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:128"},"t:1787":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1787,"t":["காயப் பரப்பில் அலைந்து துரியத்துச்","சால விரிந்து குவிந்து சகலத்தில்","ஆயஅவ் ஆறாறு அடைந்து திரிந்தோர்க்குத்","தூய அருள்தந்த நந்திக்கு என் சொல்வதே."],"p":"","r":"What can I say to Nandi, who gave pure grace to those who wandered in the expanse of the body, spread out and then contracted in turiya and in the sakala state, and roamed through the thirty-six?","n":"ஆறாறு (six sixes) is the thirty-six tattvas; துரியம் and சகலம் are states of the soul (the fourth state, and the state of full embodiment). The movement described is the soul's wandering through the states and the tattvas before grace.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","thaththuvam","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:128"},"t:1788":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1788,"t":["நானென நீயென வேறில்லை நண்ணுதல்","ஊனென ஊனுயிர் என்ன உடனின்று","வானென வானவர் நின்று மனிதர்கள்","தேனென இன்பம் திளைக்கின்ற வாறே."],"p":"","r":"Nearing him, there is no difference such as I and you, standing together as flesh and the life in the flesh; as the gods abide in heaven, humans revel in bliss as in honey.","n":"The image of body and life (ஊன், ஊனுயிர்) for the union recurs in 1790 and 1791. The verse claims non-difference while keeping the image of two that live together.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","uyir","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:128"},"t:1789":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1789,"t":["அவனும் அவனும் அவனை அறியார்","அவனை அறியில் அறிவானும் இல்லை","அவனும் அவனும் அவனை அறியில்","அவனும் அவனும் அவனிவன் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"He and he do not know him; if one knows him, there is no knower left; if he and he know him, he and he become this one.","n":"A riddle built on அவன் (he) and இவன் (this one). It is traditionally read as the soul and the bond (or the soul and its awareness) that do not know the Lord, and the knower lost in knowing him; the referents are not fixed by the verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pathi","pasu","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:128"},"t:1790":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1790,"t":["நானிது தானென நின்றவன் நாடோ றும்","ஊனிது தானுயிர் போலுணர் வானுளன்","வானிரு மாமுகில் போற்பொழி வானுளன்","நானிது அம்பர நாதனும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"He who stood saying 'I am this' every day is there as the awareness within, as life in this flesh; he is there pouring like the great cloud of the sky; and I become the Lord of the space as well.","n":"நான் இது தான் is unclear: the Lord identifying with the soul, or the soul with the Lord. அம்பர நாதன் may be the Lord of the sky or of the hall (அம்பலம்); the last line claims the speaker becomes him.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","uyir","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:128"},"t:1791":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1791,"t":["பெருந்தன்மை தானென யானென வேறாய்","இருந்ததும் இல்லைஅது ஈசன் அறியும்","பொருந்தும் உடல்உயிர் போல்உமை மெய்யே","திருந்தமுன் செய்கின்ற தேவர் பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The great Lord's nature and mine were never separate as 'he' and 'I', and the Lord knows it; Uma is truly joined as body and life, and the lord of the gods makes it right beforehand.","n":"The last two lines are unclear: உமை மெய்யே may mean Uma is the body (மெய்) while Siva is life, or 'Uma, truly'. திருந்த முன் செய்கின்ற may be the Lord preparing the soul beforehand.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","saththi","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:128"},"t:1792":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1792,"t":["இருபத மாவது இரவும் பகலும்","உருவது ஆவது உயிரும் உடலும்","அருளது ஆவது அறமும் தவமும்","பொருவது உள்நின்ற போகமது ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"The two states are night and day, form is soul and body, grace is virtue and austerity, and what stands within matching them is enjoyment.","n":"The verse is a list of pairs and its key words are unclear: இருபதம் may be 'two states' or 'two feet', and பொருவது may mean 'what matches' or 'what contends'. Read as a whole it seems to set grace among the pairs that shape embodied life, but I can't fix the last line.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","aram","thavam","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:129"},"t:1793":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1793,"t":["காண்டற்கு அரியன் கருத்திலன் நந்தியும்","தீண்டற்கும் சார்தற்கும் சேயனாத் தோன்றிடும்","வேண்டிக் கிடந்து விளக்கொளி யான்நெஞ்சம்","ஈண்டிக் கிடந்தங்கு இருளறும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"Nandi is hard to see and beyond thought, and appears far off to those who would touch or approach him; but when the heart lies longing and gathered there, as the light of a lamp, the darkness ends.","n":"விளக்கொளி யான்நெஞ்சம் can be split as 'my heart, a lamp's light' or read with விளக்கொளியான் as the Lord who is the lamp's light, coming into the heart. Either way the darkness ends inside, not by reaching out.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","thiruvarul","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:129"},"t:1794":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1794,"t":["குறிப்பினின் உள்ளே குவலயம் தோன்றும்","வெறுப்பிருள் நீங்கில் விகிர்தனும் நிற்கும்","செறிப்புறு சிந்தையைக் சிக்கென நாடில்","அறிப்புறு காட்சி அமரரும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"Within the inner intent the world appears; if the darkness of aversion goes, the Lord stands there; if one searches the gathered mind steadily, one gains a knowing vision and becomes like the immortals.","n":"குறிப்பு (sign, intent, focus) and விகிர்தன் (the one who is other, a name of Siva) carry the verse. குறிப்பு may be the meditator's point of focus or the Lord's own will; the world 'appearing' in it is open to both.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["manam","gnaanam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:129"},"t:1795":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1795,"t":["தேர்ந்தறி யாமையின் சென்றன காலங்கள்","பேர்ந்தறி வான் எங்கள் பிஞ்ஞகன் எம்இறை","ஆர்ந்தறி வார்அறி வேதுணை யாமெனச்","சார்ந்தறி வான்பெருந் தன்மைவல் லானே."],"p":"","r":"Ages went by in not discerning; our Lord Pinnakan knows apart from us, and as the one who knows that knowledge is the companion of those who know fully, he draws near and knows, master of great nature.","n":"The play on அறி (know) runs through every line. Who knows what is hard to fix in lines 2 to 4; the plain point is that our ages of not knowing are met by a Lord who knows and comes near.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","arivu","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:129"},"t:1796":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1796,"t":["தானே அறியும் வினைகள் அழிந்தபின்","நானே அறிகிலன் நந்தி அறியுங்கொல்","ஊனே உருகி உணர்வை உணர்ந்தபின்","தேனே யனையன் நம் தேவர் பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"Once the deeds that run of themselves are destroyed, I no longer know; perhaps Nandi knows. When the flesh melts and one perceives awareness itself, our Lord of the gods is like honey.","n":"நானே அறிகிலன் may be humility or the claim that the 'I' as knower is gone once karma ends. ஊனே உருகி, the flesh melting, is the devotional image of the melting body found across Saiva hymns.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","sivam","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:129"},"t:1797":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1797,"t":["நான் அறிந்து அன்றே இருக்கின்றது ஈசனை","வான்அறிந் தார் அறி யாது மயங்கினர்","ஊன்அறிந்து உள்ளே உயிர்க்கின்ற ஒண்சுடர்","தான்அறி யான்பின்னை யார்அறி வாரே?"],"p":"","r":"The Lord does not exist because I know him; those who knew the heavens were bewildered and did not know him; if the bright flame that knows the flesh and breathes within does not know him, who else will?","n":"The flame within (ஒண்சுடர்) may be the soul or the Lord himself. On the first reading only the soul's inner light can know him; on the second, only he knows himself.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","arivu","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:129"},"t:1798":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1798,"t":["அருள்எங்கு மான அளவை அறியார்","அருளை நுகர்அமு தானதும் தேரார்","அருள்ஐங் கருமத்து அதிசூக்கம் உன்னார்","அருள்எங்கும் கண்ணானது ஆர்அறி வாரே."],"p":"","r":"People do not know how far grace reaches everywhere, do not discern that grace is nectar to be enjoyed, do not consider grace's great subtlety in the five acts; who knows that grace is the eye everywhere?","n":"Each line opens with அருள். The five acts (ஐங்கருமம்) are named here as grace's work, not only as power, which the chapter goes on to say outright (1805, 1809).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","amudham","aindhozhil"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:129"},"t:1799":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1799,"t":["அறிவில் அணுக அறிவது நல்கிப்","பொறிவழி யாசை புகுத்திப் புணர்ந்திட்டு","அறிவது ஆக்கி அடியருள் நல்கும்","செறிவொடு நின்றார் சிவம்ஆயி னாரே."],"p":"","r":"He grants knowledge so that the soul may draw near by knowing, lets desire in through the senses and joins with it, turns it into knowledge and gives the grace of his feet; those who stood close to him became Siva.","n":"அணு (the atom) is a Siddhanta name for the bound soul. The verse seems to say that even desire through the senses is used by grace, but who 'joins' (புணர்ந்திட்டு) is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","thiruvadi","pasu","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:129"},"t:1800":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1800,"t":["அருளில் பிறந்திட்டு அருளில் வளர்ந்திட்டு","அருளில் அழிந்துஇளைப் பாறி மறைந்திட்டு","அருளான ஆனந்தத்து ஆரமுது ஊட்டி","அருளால் என்நந்தி அகம்புகுந் தானே."],"p":"","r":"Born in grace, grown in grace, wearing out, resting and vanishing in grace, fed the full nectar of bliss that is grace, by grace my Nandi entered my heart.","n":"The whole course of a life, birth, growth, decay, rest and disappearance, is set inside grace. இளைப்பாறி (resting) may point to the rest the soul has in dissolution before the next creation.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","aanandham","amudham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:129"},"t:1801":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1801,"t":["அருளால் அமுதப் பெருங்கடல் ஆட்டி","அருளால் அடிபுணைந்து ஆர்வமும் தந்திட்டு","அருளானஆனந்தந்து ஆரமுது ஊட்டி","அருளால் என்நந்தி அதும்புகுந் தானே."],"p":"","r":"By grace he bathed me in the great sea of nectar, by grace joined me to his feet and gave me longing, fed me the full nectar of bliss that is grace, and by grace my Nandi entered there.","n":"Lines 3 and 4 nearly repeat 1800. அடிபுணைந்து can be 'joined to his feet' or 'his feet became my raft' (புணை), which suits the sea of line 1.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","amudham","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:129"},"t:1802":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1802,"t":["பாசத்தில் இட்டது அருள்அந்தப் பாசத்தின்","நேசத்தை விட்டது அருள்அந்தநேசத்தில்","கூசற்ற முத்தி அருள்அந்தக் கூட்டத்தின்","நேசத்துத் தோன்றா நிலையரு ளாமே."],"p":"","r":"It was grace that put the soul into bonds, grace that let go its love of those bonds, grace that gives release without shame in that love, and grace is the state that does not appear in the love of that union.","n":"The striking claim is that binding too is grace. Siddhanta tradition reads it so: the soul is joined to bonds so that its root impurity may ripen and fall. Line 4 is compressed and I can't fix it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","paasam","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:129"},"t:1803":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1803,"t":["பிறவா நெறிதந்த பேரரு ளாளன்","மறவா அருள் தந்த மாதவன் நந்தி","அறவாழி அந்தணன் ஆதிப்பராபரன்","உறவாகி வந்துஎன் உளம்புகுந் தானே."],"p":"","r":"The great gracious one who gave the way of no birth, the great ascetic Nandi who gave grace that does not forget, the Brahmin who is a sea of virtue, the first Supreme, came as kin and entered my heart.","n":"அறவாழி அந்தணன் is the Kural's own phrase for the Lord (Kural 8). மாதவன் here is 'great ascetic', not Vishnu. Nandi in Tirumular is both the Lord and the first guru, so the verse may also speak of the teacher.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","piravi","guru","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:129"},"t:1804":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1804,"t":["அகம்புகுந் தான்அடி யேற்குஅரு ளாலே","அகம்புகுந் தும்தெரி யான்அருள் இல்லோர்க்கு","அகம்புகுந்து ஆனந்த மாக்கிச் சிவமாய்","அகம்புகுந் தான்நந்தி ஆனந்தி யாமே."],"p":"","r":"By grace he entered the heart of me, his servant; though within, he is not seen by those without grace; entering, making bliss and becoming Siva, Nandi entered as the blissful one.","n":"அகம்புகுந்தான் opens every line, carried over from the end of 1800. Line 2 says the same indwelling Lord is in everyone but is not perceived without grace.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","aanandham","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:129"},"t:1805":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1805,"t":["ஆயும் அறிவோடு அறியாத மாமாயை","ஆய கரணம் படைக்கும் ஐம்பூதமும்","ஆய பலஇந் திரியம் அவற்றுடன்","ஆய அருள்ஐந்து மாம் அருட் செய்கையே."],"p":"","r":"Great maya, which does not know, with the knowing soul; the instruments it makes, the five elements and the many senses with them: all these are the fivefold work of grace.","n":"ஆயும் அறிவோடு அறியாத மாமாயை is taken here as the soul (which knows) beside maya (which does not), the sentient and insentient. Calling the whole apparatus of body and senses grace's work follows 1802.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maayai","thaththuvam","aindhozhil","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:129"},"t:1806":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1806,"t":["அருளே சகலமும் ஆய பவுதிகம்","அருளே சராசர மாய அமலமே","இருளே வெளியே யெனும்எங்கும் ஈசன்","அருளே சகளத்தின் அன்றிஇன் றாமே."],"p":"","r":"Grace is all the elemental world, grace is the Pure one become the moving and unmoving; the Lord who is called darkness and open space, everywhere, is grace, and there is none apart from embodied form.","n":"சகளம் is the Lord's form with parts, as against the formless. Line 4 (அருளே சகளத்தின் அன்றிஇன் றாமே) is unclear: it may mean grace is known only through form, or that nothing exists apart from it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:129"},"t:1807":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1807,"t":["சிவமொடு சத்தி திகழ்நாதம் விந்து","தவமான ஐம்முகன் ஈசன் அரனும்","பவமுறும் மாலும் பதுமத்தோன் ஈறா","நவம்அவை யாகி நடிப்பவன் தானே."],"p":"","r":"Siva with Shakti, shining nada, bindu, the five-faced Sadasiva of austerity, Isa, Hara, Mal who enters births and the lotus-born at the end: he dances, having become these nine.","n":"These are the nine forms (நவந்தரு பேதம்) of Siva in Siddhanta. Placing Vishnu (மால்) and Brahma among Siva's own forms ranks them under him. பவமுறும், 'who meets birth', may refer to Vishnu's avatars.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","saththi","naadham","vindhu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:129"},"t:1808":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1808,"t":["அருட்கண்இ லாதார்க்கு அரும்பொருள் தோன்றா","அருட்கண்உ ளோர்க்குஎதிர் தோன்றும் அரனே","இருட்கண்ணி னோர்க்குஅங்கு இரவியும் தோன்றாத்","தெருட்கண்ணி னோர்க்குஎங்கும் சீரொளி யாமே."],"p":"","r":"To those without the eye of grace the rare thing does not appear; to those with it Hara appears before them. To the dark-eyed even the sun does not appear; to the clear-eyed it is glorious light everywhere.","n":"The sun is visible to all but the blind; so the Lord is present to all but seen only with grace's eye. The fault lies in the seer, not in the light.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","gnaanam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:129"},"t:1809":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1809,"t":["தானே படைத்திடும் தானே அளித்திடும்","தானே துடைத்திடும் தானே மறைத்திடும்","தானே இவைசெய்து தான்முத்தி தந்திடும்","தானே வியாபித் தலைவனும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"He himself creates, protects, destroys and conceals; doing these he himself grants release; he himself pervades and is the head.","n":"The five acts, with release (முத்தி) taking the place of the fifth, grace. தானே (he himself) opens every line: no other agent is involved.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aindhozhil","sivam","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:129"},"t:1810":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1810,"t":["தலையான நான்கும் தனதுஅரு வாகும்","அலையா அருவுரு வாகும் சதாசிவம்","நிலையான கீழ்நான்கு நீடுரு வாகும்","துலையா இறைமுற்று மாய் அல்லது ஒன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The upper four are his formless forms; Sadasiva, unwavering, is form and formless; the lower four are lasting forms; the Lord, beyond measure, is all of them and yet one.","n":"This sorts the nine of 1807: Siva, Shakti, nada and bindu formless; Sadasiva both; Maheswara, Rudra, Vishnu and Brahma with form. துலையா (unweighable, or unfailing) and the last phrase அல்லது ஒன்றே are not fully clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:129"},"t:1811":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1811,"t":["ஒன்றது வாலே உலப்பிலி தானாகி","நின்றது தான்போல் உயிர்க்குயி ராய்நிலை","துன்றி அவைஅல்ல வாகும் துணையென்ன","நின்றது தான்விளை யாட்டென்னுள் நேயமே."],"p":"","r":"Being one, he is the imperishable; standing as if he were the soul, he is the life of life; pressing close, he is also not those things, as a companion; his play stands in me as love.","n":"The verse seems to state the Siddhanta relation of Lord and soul: one with it, other than it, and with it as companion. The syntax is compressed and the reading is tentative.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","uyir","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:129"},"t:1812":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1812,"t":["நேயத்தே நின்றிடும் நின்மலன் சத்தியோடு","ஆயக் குடிலைகள் நாதம் அடைந்திட்டுப்","போயக் கலைபல வாகப் புணர்ந்திட்டு","வீயத் தகாவிந்து வாக விளையுமே."],"p":"","r":"The Spotless one, standing in love with Shakti, reaching kutila and nada, joining to become many kalas, ripens as a bindu that cannot be lost.","n":"குடிலை (kutila, the curved power), நாதம் and கலை are technical terms of the cosmology chapter 140 develops. வீயத்தகா, 'not fit to perish', may describe bindu as imperishable or as not to be spent.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vindhu","naadham","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:129"},"t:1813":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1813,"t":["விளையும் பரவிந்து தானே வியாபி","விளையும் தனிமாயை மிக்கமா மாயை","கிளையொன்று தேவர் கிளர்மனு வேதம்","அளவொன் றிலாஅண்ட கோடிக ளாமே."],"p":"","r":"The supreme bindu that ripens is itself all-pervading; from it ripen pure maya and the vast great maya, the branch of the gods, the rising mantras and Veda, and countless crores of worlds.","n":"தனிமாயை may be சுத்தமாயை (pure maya) and மாமாயை the great maya, the two levels of maya in Siddhanta; மனு here is mantra. The chain (1812 to 1813) links back to grace: what grace places is the whole cosmos.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vindhu","maayai","mandhiram","vedham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:129"},"t:1814":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1814,"t":["அருளில் தலைநின்று அறிந்துஅழுந் தாதார்","அருளில் தலைநில்லார் ஐம்பாசம் நீங்கார்","அருளின் பெருமை அறியார் செறியார்","அருளில் பிறந்திட்டு அறிந்துஅறி வாரே."],"p":"","r":"Those who stand first in grace know and do not sink; those who do not stand in grace do not leave the five bonds, know not grace's greatness and do not draw near; born in grace, they know by knowing.","n":"ஐம்பாசம் (the five bonds) is traditionally anava, karma, maya, mayeya and the concealing power; the verse does not list them. Who is 'born in grace' in line 4 is unclear: perhaps those of line 1, perhaps everyone, as in 1800.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","paasam","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:130"},"t:1815":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1815,"t":["வாரா வழிதந்த மாநந்தி பேர்நந்தி","ஆரா அமுதளித்து ஆனந்தி பேர்நந்தி","பேரா யிரமுடைப் பெம்மான்பேர் ஒன்றினில்","ஆரா அருட்கடல் ஆடுகென் றானே."],"p":"","r":"Great Nandi gave the way of no return; Nandi the blissful gave nectar that never sates; the Lord of a thousand names said, in a single name, 'Bathe in the insatiable sea of grace.'","n":"வாரா வழி, the road from which one does not come back, is release from rebirth. பேர் ஒன்றினில் may mean 'by one of his names' or that the thousand names are summed in one, which tradition may take as the five-letter mantra.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","amudham","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:130"},"t:1816":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1816,"t":["ஆடியும் பாடியும் அழுதும் அரற்றியும்","தேடியும் கண்டேன் சிவன்பெரும் தன்மையைக்","கூடிய வாறே குறியாக் குறிதந்தென்","ஊடுநின் றான்அவன் தன்னருள் உற்றே."],"p":"","r":"Dancing, singing, weeping and crying out, seeking, I found Siva's great nature; as he joined me he gave me a sign that is no sign and stood within me, and I gained his grace.","n":"The first line is the vocabulary of ecstatic devotion. குறியாக் குறி, 'a sign that is not a sign', is traditionally the Lord beyond form given in a form, or the guru's teaching; the verse leaves it open.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","anbu","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:130"},"t:1817":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1817,"t":["உற்ற பிறப்பும் உறுமலம் ஆனதும்","பற்றிய மாயாப் படலம் எனப் பண்ணி","அத்தனை நீயென்று அடிவைத்தேன் பேர்நந்தி","கற்றன விட்டேன் கழல்பணிந் தேனே."],"p":"","r":"Treating the birth I came into and the impurity that came with it as a film of maya I had clung to, I set my feet on your way saying 'you are my father', great Nandi; I left what I had learned and bowed at your anklet.","n":"அத்தனை நீயென்று may be 'you are the father' (அத்தன்) or 'you are all that' (அத்தனை); அடிவைத்தேன் may be 'I stepped forward' or 'I placed myself at your feet'. கற்றன விட்டேன் is a strong phrase: learning is put down, not completed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["malam","maayai","thiruvadi","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:130"},"t:1818":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1818,"t":["விளக்கினை யேற்றி வெளியை அறிமின்","விளக்கினை முன்னே வேதனை மாறும்","விளக்கை விளக்கும் விளக்குடை யார்கள்","விளக்கில் விளங்கும் விளக்கவர் தாமே."],"p":"","r":"Light the lamp and know the open space; before the lamp suffering changes; those who hold the lamp that lights the lamp become themselves the lamp that shines in the lamp.","n":"விளக்கு (lamp) occurs in every line, and விளக்கும் also means 'explains' or 'makes clear'. வெளி (open space) may be the yogic inner sky. Tradition reads the lamp that lights the lamp as grace or the guru kindling knowledge.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","thiruvarul","idumbai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:130"},"t:1819":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1819,"t":["ஒளியும் இருளும் ஒருகாலும் தீரா","ஒளியு ளோர்க்குஅன்றோ ஒழியாது ஒளியும்","ஒளியுருள் கண்டகண் போலவே றாயுள","ஒளியிருள் நீங்க உயிர்சிவம் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"Light and darkness never end once and for all; only for those in the light does the light not fail; light and darkness are as different as an eye that has seen them; when both light and darkness go, the soul becomes Siva.","n":"Line 3 is obscure. The last line goes past the usual image: release is not light defeating darkness but both passing, which tradition may read as going beyond knowledge and ignorance alike.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","malam","uyir","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:130"},"t:1820":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1820,"t":["புறமே திரிந்தேனைப் பொற்கழல் சூட்டி","நிறமே புகுந்தென்னை நின்மலன் ஆக்கி","அறமே புகுந்தெனக்கு ஆரமுது ஈந்த","திறம்ஏதென்று எண்ணித் திகைத்திருந் தேனே."],"p":"","r":"Me, who wandered outside, he crowned with his golden anklets, entered my very nature and made me spotless, entered as dharma and gave me nectar; I sat dazed, wondering how he did it.","n":"பொற்கழல் சூட்டி, crowning with the anklets, is placing the feet on the head, the gesture tradition calls திருவடி தீட்சை. நிறமே may be 'my colour' or 'my very being'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","thiruvadi","amudham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:130"},"t:1821":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1821,"t":["அருளது என்ற அகலிடம் ஒன்றும்","பொருளது என்ற புகலிடம் ஒன்றும்","மருளது நீங்க மனம்புகுந் தானைத்","தெருளுறும் பின்னைச் சிவகதி தாமே."],"p":"","r":"There is one wide place called grace and one refuge called the true thing; when the one who entered the mind to remove delusion is grasped with clarity, the way to Siva follows.","n":"மருள் (delusion) and தெருள் (clarity) are paired. Grace and the true thing may be two names of one refuge or two stages, grace leading to the real.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","gnaanam","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:130"},"t:1822":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1822,"t":["கூறுமின் நீர்முன் பிறந்திங்கு இறந்தமை","வேறொரு தெய்வத்தின் மெய்ப்பொருள் நீக்கிடும்","பாறணி யும்உடல் வீழலிட்டு ஆருயிர்","தேறுஅணிவோம்இது செப்பவல் லீரே."],"p":"","r":"Tell us how you were born before and died here; another god's version of the truth is set aside; casting off the body that vultures adorn, we shall clothe the precious soul in clarity: can you say this?","n":"The verse reads as a challenge to those who cannot account for their own births and deaths. Line 2 (வேறொரு தெய்வத்தின் மெய்ப்பொருள் நீக்கிடும்) and தேறுஅணிவோம் are obscure, and the reading is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","yaakkai","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:130"},"t:1823":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1823,"t":["உள்ளம் பெருங்கோயில் ஊனுடம்பு ஆலயம்","வள்ளற் பிரானார்க்கு வாய்கோ புரவாசல்","தெள்ளத் தெளிந்தார்க்குச் சீவன் சிவலிங்கம்","கள்ளப் புலன்ஐந்தும் காளா மணிவிளக்கே."],"p":"","r":"For the generous Lord the heart is the great temple, the body of flesh the shrine and the mouth the tower gate; for those clear through and through, the soul is the Sivalinga, and the five thieving senses are the bright lamps.","n":"Each part of the temple is matched to the person. The senses, called thieves (கள்ளப் புலன்), become its lamps, which implies they are mastered rather than cut off. காளாமணி is unclear, perhaps 'unflickering jewel'. Only 'for those clear through' (தெள்ளத் தெளிந்தார்க்கு) is the soul the linga.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","vazhipaadu","sivam","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:131"},"t:1824":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1824,"t":["வேட்டவி யுண்ணும் விரிசடை நந்திக்குக்","காட்டவும் நாம்இலம் காலையும் மாலையும்","ஊட்டவி யாவன உள்ளம் குளிர்விக்கும்","பாட்டவி காட்டுதும் பால்அவி யாமே."],"p":"","r":"We have nothing to show, morning and evening, to Nandi of spreading matted hair who eats the oblation of sacrifice; as the food offering we offer the song that cools the heart, and that is our milk offering.","n":"The verse plays on அவி (oblation): வேட்டவி of the fire rite, ஊட்டவி fed to the deity, பாட்டவி of song and பால்அவி of milk. It seems to put sung praise in the place of the sacrificial offering, but the first two lines are compressed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","aaguthi","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:131"},"t:1825":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1825,"t":["பான்மொழி பாகன் பராபரன் தானாகும்","ஆன சதாசிவன் தன்னைஆ வாகித்து","மேன்முகம் ஈசான மாகவே கைக்கொண்டு","சீன்முகம் செய்யச் சிவனவன் ஆகுமே."],"p":"","r":"Invoking Sadasiva, who is the Supreme with the sweet-spoken goddess as his half, and taking the upper face as Isana, when the gesture is made, he becomes Siva.","n":"ஆவாகித்து is avahana, invoking the deity into the image; ஈசானம் is the upward face of five-faced Sadasiva. சீன்முகம் is obscure, perhaps a mudra. 'He becomes Siva' may mean the image becomes Siva or the worshipper does.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","sivam","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:131"},"t:1826":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1826,"t":["நினைவதும் வாய்மை மொழிவதும் அல்லால்","கனைகழல் ஈசனைக் காண அரிதாம்","கனைகழல் ஈசனைக் காண்குற வல்லார்","புனைமலர் நீர்கொண்டு போற்றவல் லாரே."],"p":"","r":"Without right thought and truthful speech it is hard to see the Lord of the sounding anklets; those who can see him are the ones who can truly praise him with flowers and water.","n":"The order matters: inner truth comes first, and only then is outer offering real praise. நினைவதும் may be 'thinking of him' or 'thinking rightly'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","vaaimai","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:131"},"t:1827":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1827,"t":["மஞ்சன மாலை நிலாவிய வானவர்","நெஞ்சினுள் ஈசன் நிலைபெறு காரணம்","அஞ்சமு தாம்உப சாரம்எட்டு எட்டோ டும்","அஞ்சலி யோடும் கலந்துஅர்ச்சித் தார்களே."],"p":"","r":"The reason the Lord stays fixed in the hearts of the gods who bathe and garland him is that they worshipped him with the five nectars, the services eight and eight, and joined palms.","n":"அஞ்சமுது is the five nectars of temple ablution (panchamrita), not the yogic amudham. உபசாரம் எட்டு எட்டு may be sixteen services (the usual count) or sixty-four.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","adiyaar"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:131"},"t:1828":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1828,"t":["புண்ணியம் செய்வார்க்குப் பூவுண்டு நீருண்டு","அண்ணல் அதுகண்டு அருள்புரி யாநிற்கும்","எண்ணிலி பாவிகள் எம்இறை ஈசனை","நண்ணறி யாமல் நழுவுகின் றாரே."],"p":"","r":"For those who would do good there are flowers and there is water; the Lord sees it and goes on granting grace; countless sinners slip away without knowing how to approach our Lord.","n":"The point is how little worship needs: flowers and water are always to hand, so failing to worship is not lack of means. நழுவுகின்றார் (they slip away) makes the failure an evasion.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","thiruvarul","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:131"},"t:1829":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1829,"t":["அத்தன் நவதீர்த்தம் ஆடும் பரிசுகேள்","ஒத்தமெய்ஞ் ஞானத்து உயர்ந்தார் பதத்தைச்","சுத்தம தாக விளக்கித் தெளிக்கவே","முத்தியாம் என்று நம்மூலன் மொழிந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"Hear how the Father bathes in the nine holy waters: washing clean and sprinkling the feet of those raised high in true knowledge is release, so our Mulan said.","n":"The nine sacred waters are relocated to the water that washes a jnani's feet, and the Lord is said to bathe in that. Tirumular names himself (நம்மூலன்) as the authority.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["theerththam","gnaanam","adiyaar","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:131"},"t:1830":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1830,"t":["மறப்புற்று இவ்வழி மன்னிநின் றாலும்","சிறப்பொடு பூநீர் திருந்தமுன் ஏந்தி","மறப்பின்றி யுன்னை வழிபடும் வண்ணம்","அறப்பெற வேண்டும் அமரர் பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"Lord of the gods, even if I remain on this path forgetful, grant that I may hold flowers and water rightly before you and worship you fully, without forgetting.","n":"A prayer, not a teaching: the worshipper asks for the unforgetting worship that he admits he cannot keep by himself. மறப்பு opens and returns in line 3.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","pochchaappu","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:131"},"t:1831":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1831,"t":["ஆரா தனையும் அமரர் குழாங்களும்","தீராக் கடலும் நிலத்துஉம தாய்நிற்கும்","பேரா யிரமும் பிரான்திரு நாமமும்","ஆரா வழியெங்கள் ஆதிப் பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"Worship, the hosts of gods, the endless sea and all on the land stand as yours; the thousand names and the Lord's holy name are a way that never sates, our first Lord.","n":"The syntax is loose: ஆரா வழி may be 'the insatiable way' or 'the way no one completes'. The verse seems to make worship, the cosmos and the names all the Lord's own.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","sivam","mandhiram"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:131"},"t:1832":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1832,"t":["ஆன்ஐந்தும் ஆட்டி அமரர் கணம்தொழத்","தான்அந்த மில்லாத் தலைவன் அருளது","தேன்உந்து மாமலர் உள்ளே தெளிந்ததோர்","பார்ஐங் குணமும் படைத்துநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"Bathed in the five products of the cow while the hosts of gods worship, the endless Lord's grace, clear within the great flower that drips honey, stands having made the five qualities of the earth.","n":"ஆன்ஐந்து is the panchagavya used in ablution. பார் ஐங்குணம் are the five qualities of earth (sound, touch, form, taste, smell). The honeyed flower may be a temple flower or the lotus of the heart.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","thiruvarul","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:131"},"t:1833":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1833,"t":["உழைக்கொண்ட பூநீர் ஒருங்குடன் ஏந்தி","மழைக்கொண்ட மாமுகில் மேற்சென்று வானோர்","தழைக்கொண்ட பாசம் தயங்கிநின்று ஏத்தப்","பிழைப்பின்றி எம்பெரு மான்அரு ளாமே."],"p":"","r":"Holding together flowers and water gathered with toil, the gods, going above the rain clouds, praise him, wavering with their bonds still thick on them; our Lord's grace comes without fail.","n":"உழைக்கொண்ட may be 'gathered with toil' or 'held at one's side'. If the gods worship with their bonds still on them, the verse says grace answers worship even before the bonds fall.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","thiruvarul","paasam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:131"},"t:1834":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1834,"t":["வெள்ளக் கடலுள் விரிசடை நந்திக்கு","உள்ளக் கடற்புக்கு வார்சுமை பூக்கொண்டு","கள்ளக் கடல்விட்டுக்கைதொழ மாட்டாதார்","அள்ளக் கடலுள் அழுந்துகின் றாரே."],"p":"","r":"Those who will not enter the sea of the heart, carry loads of flowers to Nandi of spreading matted hair in the flood-sea, leave the sea of deceit and join hands in worship sink in the sea that drags them down.","n":"Four seas: the flood-sea (perhaps the Ganga in his hair or the cosmic deluge), the sea of the heart, the sea of deceit (the senses) and the sea that swallows, taken as the sea of births. The syntax of line 2 is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","piravi","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:131"},"t:1835":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1835,"t":["கழிப்படுந் தண்கடற் கௌவை யுடைத்து","வழிப்படு வார்மலர் மொட்டுஅறி யார்கள்","பழிப்படு வார்பல ரும்பழி வீழ","வெளிப்படு வோர்உச்சி மேவிநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"The cool sea of the backwaters is full of uproar; those who go by the way do not know the flower bud; while the many who earn blame fall into blame, he stands at the crown of those who come into the open.","n":"The first two lines are obscure. வெளிப்படுவோர் may be those who become manifest in knowledge, and உச்சி the crown of the head as in yoga; neither can be fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","pazhi","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:131"},"t:1836":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1836,"t":["பயனறிவு ஒன்றுண்டு பன்மலர் தூவிப்","பயனறி வார்க்குஅரன் தானே பயிலும்","நயனங்கள் மூன்றுடை யான்அடி சேர","வயனங்க ளால்என்றும் வந்துநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"There is one knowledge of the fruit: to those who strew many flowers knowing that fruit, Hara himself keeps near; so that they may reach the feet of the three-eyed one, he has come and stands for ever by his own ways.","n":"பயனறிவு (knowing the fruit) separates worship that understands what it seeks from mere strewing of flowers. வயனங்கள் is unclear: perhaps 'means' or 'ways', perhaps a variant of நயனங்கள் (eyes).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","payan","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:131"},"t:1837":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1837,"t":["ஏத்துவர் மாமலர் தூவித் தொழுதுநின்று","ஆர்த்தெமது ஈசன் அருட்சே வடியென்றன்","மூர்த்தியை மூவா முதலுறு வாய்நின்ற","தீர்த்தனை யாரும் துதித்துஉண ராரே."],"p":"","r":"They praise, strewing great flowers and standing in worship, crying 'our Lord's gracious red feet'; yet none of them, though they praise, come to know the holy one who stands as form and as the unageing first.","n":"The last line turns against the first: praise and offering do not by themselves bring knowing. தீர்த்தன் may be 'the holy one' or 'the one who ends' (births).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","thiruvadi","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:131"},"t:1838":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1838,"t":["தேவர்க ளோடுஇசை வந்துமண் ணோடுறும்","பூவொடு நீர்சுமந்து ஏத்திப் புனிதனை","மூவரிற் பன்மை முதல்வனாய் நின்றருள்","நீர்மையை யாவர் நினைக்கவல் லாரே."],"p":"","r":"With the gods, music comes down and joins the earth; carrying flowers and water, they praise the Pure one; who can conceive the nature of his grace, standing as the first among the three and in many forms?","n":"மூவரிற் பன்மை முதல்வன் is taken as first of the three gods (Brahma, Vishnu, Rudra) and present in many; the syntax allows other readings.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","thiruvarul","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:131"},"t:1839":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1839,"t":["உழைக்கவல் லோர்நடு நீர்மலர் ஏந்திப்","பிழைப்பின்றி ஈசன் பெருந்தவம் பேணி","இழைக்கொண்ட பாதத்து இனமலர் தூவி","மழைக்கொண்டல் போலவே மன்னிநில் லீரே."],"p":"","r":"You who can labour, holding water and flowers between your hands, keeping the Lord's great austerity without fault, strewing flowers of every kind on the jewelled feet, stand firm like the rain cloud.","n":"The rain cloud (மழைக்கொண்டல்) may stand for unfailing, generous giving, matched to the steady showering of flowers. ஈசன் பெருந்தவம் may be austerity done for the Lord rather than his own.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","thiruvadi","thavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:131"},"t:1840":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1840,"t":["வென்று விரைந்து விரைப்பணி என்றனர்","நின்று பொருந்த இறைபணி நேர்படத்","துன்று சலமலர் தூவித் தொழுதிடில்","கொண்டிடும் நித்தலும் கூறியஅன்றே."],"p":"","r":"They said: overcome, hurry, serve with fragrance; if, standing fittingly as the Lord's service comes right, you strew dense water and flowers and worship, he will accept it every day, as was said.","n":"Line 1 (வென்று விரைந்து விரைப்பணி) is obscure, with a pun on விரை (hurry; fragrance). The second half is clearer: daily offering is accepted.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:131"},"t:1841":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1841,"t":["சாத்தியும் வைத்தும் சயம்புஎன்று ஏத்தியும்","ஏத்தியும் நாளும் இறையை அறிகிலார்","ஆத்தி மலக்கிட்டு அகத்துஇழுக்கு அற்றக்கான்","மாத்திக்கே செல்லும் வழியது வாமே."],"p":"","r":"Adorning him, setting him up, praising him as Svayambhu and praising him every day, they do not know the Lord; when one removes the impurity and the inner stain is gone, that is the way that leads to the great goal.","n":"சயம்பு is the self-manifest linga. Lines 3 and 4 (ஆத்தி மலக்கிட்டு அகத்துஇழுக்கு அற்றக்கான் மாத்திக்கே) are obscure; ஆத்தி may be the Bauhinia flower sacred to Siva. The contrast of outer rite and inner purity seems clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","malam","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:131"},"t:1842":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1842,"t":["ஆவிக் கமலத்தில் அப்புறத்து இன்புற","மேவித் திரியும் விரிசடை நந்தியைக்","கூவிக் கருதிக் கொடுபோய்ச் சிவத்திடைத்","தாவிக்கு மந்திரம் தாமறி யாரே."],"p":"","r":"They do not know the mantra by which to call on, contemplate and lead Nandi of spreading hair, who roams in delight beyond the lotus of the breath, and establish him in Siva.","n":"ஆவிக் கமலம் may be the lotus of the soul or of the breath, the heart-lotus of inner worship. கூவி, கருதி, தாவிக்கும் echo the outer rite's invoking, meditating and establishing (avahana, sthapana) done inwardly.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","yogam","praanan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:131"},"t:1843":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1843,"t":["காண்ஆகத் துள்ளேஅழுந்திய மாணிக்கம்","காணும் அளவும் கருத்தறி வாரில்லை","பேணிப் பெருக்கிப் பெருக்கி நினைவோர்க்கு","மாணிக்க மாலை மனம்புகுந் தானே."],"p":"","r":"Until they see the ruby sunk within the visible body, no one knows its worth; to those who cherish him and swell their thought of him again and again, the one who is a ruby garland enters the mind.","n":"The jewel is hidden in the body, not outside it, as in 1823. காண் ஆகம் may be 'the body that is seen' or 'look: within the body'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","yaakkai","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:131"},"t:1844":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1844,"t":["பெருந்தன்மை நந்தி பிணங்கிருள் நேமி","இருந்தன்மை யாலும் என் நெஞ்சுஇடங் கொள்ள","வருந்தன்மை யாளனை வானவர் தேவர்","தருந்தன்மை யாளனைத் தாங்கிநின் றாரே."],"p":"","r":"Nandi of great nature, a wheel against the clinging dark, by his two natures took my heart as his place; the gods held fast to the one whose nature is to come and whose nature is to give.","n":"பிணங்கிருள் நேமி and இருந்தன்மை are obscure; இருந்தன்மை may be 'two natures' or 'a great nature'. The reading is tentative.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:131"},"t:1845":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1845,"t":["சமைய மலசுத்தி தன்செயல் அற்றிடும்","அமையும் விசேடமும் ஆனமந் திரசுத்தி","சமையநிர் வாணம் கலாசுத்தி யாகும்","அமைமன்று ஞானம் ஆனார்க்கு அபிடேகமே."],"p":"","r":"Samaya initiation purifies impurity and one's own acting ceases; vishesha that follows is purification by mantra; nirvana initiation is purification of the kalas; for those become knowledge in the hall there is abhisheka.","n":"The four are traditionally the grades of Saiva initiation given by a guru: samaya, vishesha, nirvana and acharya abhisheka. The verse does not name தீட்சை. The match of each grade to a purification is the verse's own, but its grammar is compressed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","malam","mandhiram","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:131"},"t:1846":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1846,"t":["ஊழிதோ றூழி உணர்ந்தவர்க்கு அல்லது","ஊழில் உயிரை உணரவும் தான்ஒட்டா","ஆழி அமரும் அரிஅயன் என்றுளோர்","ஊழி கடந்தும் ஓர்உச்சியு ளானே."],"p":"","r":"Except to those who have perceived him age after age, he does not let the soul in its fate know him; beyond the ages, beyond those called Hari of the ocean and Ayan, he is at the one summit.","n":"ஊழி (aeon) and ஊழ் (fate) are played against each other. Hari and Brahma failing to find the summit recalls the myth of the column of fire. ஓர் உச்சி may be that summit or the crown of the head in yoga.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","oozh","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:131"},"t:1847":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1847,"t":["ஆகின்ற நந்தி அடித்தா மரைபற்றிப்","போகின்றுபதேசம் பூசிக்கும் பூசையும்","ஆகின்ற ஆதாரம் ஆறாறு அதனின்மேல்","போகின்ற பொற்பையும் போற்றுவன் யானே."],"p":"","r":"Holding to the lotus feet of Nandi, I praise the worship that honours the teaching he gives, and the splendour that rises above the six-and-six supports.","n":"போகின்றுபதேசம் is read as போகின்ற உபதேசம், the teaching that goes forth. ஆதாரம் ஆறாறு can be the six bodily supports (adharas) or six times six, the thirty-six tattvas; either way the splendour is above them.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","thiruvadi","vazhipaadu","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:132"},"t:1848":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1848,"t":["கானுறு கோடி கடிகமழ் சந்தனம்","வானுற மாமலர் இட்டு வணங்கினும்","ஊனினை நீக்கி உணர்பவர்க்கு அல்லது","தேனமர் புங்குழல் சேரஒண் ணாதே."],"p":"","r":"Though one worships with ten million loads of fragrant forest sandal and great flowers heaped to the sky, the one with honey-filled flowered tresses cannot be reached except by those who put away the flesh and perceive.","n":"தேனமர் பூங்குழல் names the goddess by her hair; whether she stands here for Shakti alone or for the Lord with her is not said. ஊனினை நீக்கி is usually taken as leaving identification with the body rather than abstaining from meat. Sengalvaraya cites Appar's 'ஊனையே கழிக்கவேண்டில்' for the same thought.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","meyyunarvu","saththi","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:132"},"t:1849":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1849,"t":["மேவிய ஞானத்தின் மிக்கிடின் மெய்ப்பரன்","ஆவயின் ஞான நெறிநிற்றல் அர்ச்சனை","ஓவற உட்பூ சனைசெய்யில் உத்தமம்","சேவடி சேரல் செயலறல் தானே."],"p":"","r":"If one rises high in the knowledge one has reached, the true Supreme is there; standing in the path of knowledge is the true offering, ceaseless inner worship is the best, and reaching the red feet is the ceasing of one's own acts.","n":"The verse redefines archana (அர்ச்சனை) as standing in knowledge, not as an outer rite. செயலறல், the end of acting, is traditionally the stillness in which the soul no longer claims its acts.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","vazhipaadu","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:132"},"t:1850":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1850,"t":["உச்சியும் காலையும் மாலையும் ஈசனை","நச்சுமின் நச்சி நமவென்று நாமத்தை","விச்சிமின் விச்சு விரிசுடர் மூன்றினும்","நச்சுமின் பேர்நந்தி நாயகன் ஆகுமே."],"p":"","r":"Long for the Lord at noon, morning and evening; longing, sow his name with 'nama'; sow it, and long for him in the three spreading lights, and Nandi, the great one, will become your master.","n":"நச்சுமின் (long for) and விச்சுமின் (sow, from வித்து) alternate through the verse. Naming the Lord with நம is taken as the five-letter mantra. The three lights are usually sun, moon and fire, which yogic readings place in the body.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","panchaakkaram","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:132"},"t:1851":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1851,"t":["புண்ணிய மண்டலம் பூசைநா றாகுமாம்","பண்ணிய மேனியும் பத்துநூ றாகுமாம்","எண்ணிலிக்கு ஐயம் இடில்கோடி யாகுமால்","பண்ணிடில் ஞானிஊண் பார்க்கில் விசேடமே."],"p":"","r":"Worship in a holy mandala counts a hundredfold, worship of a fashioned image a thousandfold, and alms given to the countless ten-millionfold; but food prepared for a jnani, if you consider it, is beyond all these.","n":"பூசைநா றாகுமாம் is read as பூசை நூறு. எண்ணிலிக்கு ஐயம் may be alms to countless people or to one of countless worth. The verse ranks offerings by merit and puts feeding a knower of Siva at the top, as the next chapter will at length.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","gnaanam","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:132"},"t:1852":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1852,"t":["இந்துவும் பானுவும் இலங்கும் தலத்திடை","வந்தித்த தெல்லாம் அசுரர்க்கு வாரியாம்","இந்துவும் பானுவும் இலங்காத் தலத்திடை","வந்தித்தல் நந்திக்கு மாபூசை யாமே."],"p":"","r":"All worship offered in the place where moon and sun shine goes to the asuras as their share; worship in the place where moon and sun do not shine is the great worship of Nandi.","n":"Plainly read, worship done in the light of day and night is lost and worship beyond them is true. In yogic tradition moon and sun are the left and right breath channels, and the place where they do not shine is the central channel or the crown above them. Both readings stand.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","naadi","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:132"},"t:1853":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1853,"t":["இந்துவும் பானுவும் என்றெழு கின்றதோர்","விந்துவும் நாதமும் ஆகிமீ தானத்தே","சிந்தனை சாக்கிரா தீதத்தே சென்றிட்டு","நந்தியைப் பூசிக்க நற்பூசை யாமே."],"p":"","r":"What rises as moon and sun becomes bindu and nada in the canopy above; there, with thought gone beyond waking, worship Nandi, and that is good worship.","n":"மீதானம் (the canopy above) is traditionally the space at the crown of the head. சாக்கிராதீதம் is the state beyond waking in Siddhanta's scheme of states. The verse turns the moon and sun of 1852 into bindu and nada.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vindhu","naadham","vazhipaadu","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:132"},"t:1854":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1854,"t":["மனபவ னங்களை மூலத்தான் மாற்றி","அனித உடல்பூத மாக்கி அகற்றிப்","புனிதன் அருள்தனில் புக்கிருந்து இன்பத்","தனியுறு பூசை சதாசிவற்கு ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"Turning mind and breath back at the root, making the impermanent body into its elements and setting it aside, entering the Pure One's grace and staying in bliss: this solitary worship is Sadasiva's.","n":"மனபவனம் is mind and breath, and மூலம் the root support at the base of the spine. உடல் பூதமாக்கி அகற்றி is taken as the rite of purifying the elements, in which the body is dissolved in thought before worship.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["praanan","yogam","thiruvarul","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:132"},"t:1855":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1855,"t":["பகலும் இரவும் பயில்கின்ற பூசை","இயல்புடை ஈசர்க்கு இணைமல ராகப்","பகலும் இரவும் பயிலாத பூசை","சகலமும் தான்கொள்வன் தாழ்சடை யோனே."],"p":"","r":"Worship practised by day and night becomes paired flowers for the Lord as he is by nature; worship not practised by day and night the one of hanging matted hair takes wholly.","n":"The second half may mean worship that is not bound to times, or worship in the state where day and night (the two breaths, or waking and sleep) have ceased. இணைமலர் may also be the Lord's paired feet.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:132"},"t:1856":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1856,"t":["இராப்பகல் அற்ற இடத்தே இருந்து","பராக்குஅற ஆனந்தத் தேறல் பருகி","இராப்பகல் அற்ற இறையடி இன்பத்து","இராப்பகல் மாயை இரண்டுஇடத் தேனே."],"p":"","r":"Staying in the place where night and day are not, drinking without distraction the clear honey of bliss, in the joy of the Lord's feet where there is no night or day, I have set the night and day of maya in their two places.","n":"இராப்பகல் அற்ற இடம் is the place beyond the alternation of states. The last line is obscure: it may mean the speaker has left maya's day and night behind, or holds them apart while staying between.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aanandham","thiruvadi","maayai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:132"},"t:1857":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1857,"t":["படமாடக் கோயில் பகவற்குஒன்று ஈயில்","நடமாடக் கோயில் நம்பர்க்கு அங்கு ஆகா","நடமாடக் கோயில் நம்பர்க்கு ஒன்று ஈயில்","படமாடக் கோயில் பகவற்கு அது ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"If you give something to the god of the temple of images, it does not reach the Lord in the walking temple; if you give something to the Lord in the walking temple, it reaches the god of the temple of images.","n":"படமாடக் கோயில் is the built temple with its images (படம் may also be its banners); நடமாடக் கோயில் is the living devotee, whose body houses the Lord. The verse does not forbid temple offerings; it says they flow one way only.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adiyaar","eegai","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:133"},"t:1858":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1858,"t":["தண்டுஅறு சிந்தை தபோதனார் தாம்மகிழ்ந்து","உண்டது மூன்று புவனமும் உண்டது","கொண்டது மூன்று புவனமும் கொண்டதுஎன்று","எண்திசை நந்தி எடுத்துரைத் தானே."],"p":"","r":"What the ascetics of steady mind eat with delight, the three worlds have eaten, and what they receive, the three worlds have received: so Nandi of the eight directions declared.","n":"தண்டு அறு சிந்தை is a mind without obstruction or wavering. The claim is that feeding one such ascetic feeds the whole world, and it is given Nandi's authority.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adiyaar","eegai","thavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:133"},"t:1859":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1859,"t":["மாத்திரை ஒன்றினில் மன்னி அமர்ந்துறை","ஆத்தனுக்கு ஈந்த அரும்பொரு ளானது","மூர்த்திகள் மூவர்க்கும் மூவேழ் குரவர்க்கும்","தீர்த்தம தாம்அது தேர்ந்துகொள் வீரே."],"p":"","r":"A rare gift made to the realised one who stays settled in a single measure becomes holy water for the three gods and for the thrice seven elders; understand this.","n":"ஆத்தன் is taken as the trustworthy, realised one. மாத்திரை ஒன்றினில் may be one moment, one measure of breath, or one syllable. மூவேழ் குரவர் is commonly read as twenty-one generations of forebears whom the gift sanctifies.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","adiyaar","theerththam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:133"},"t:1860":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1860,"t":["அகரம் ஆயிரம் அந்தணர்க்கு ஈயில் என்","சிகரம் ஆயிரம் செய்து முடிக்கில்என்","பகரு ஞானி பகல்ஊண் பலத்துக்கு","நிகரில்லை என்பது நிச்சயம் தானே."],"p":"","r":"What if one gives a thousand villages to Brahmins, what if one builds a thousand temple towers? Nothing equals the merit of giving a midday meal to a jnani; that is certain.","n":"அகரம் is the agraharam, a village granted to Brahmins, and சிகரம் the tower of a temple: the two great religious gifts of a king, both set below one meal.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","eegai","andhanar","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:133"},"t:1861":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1861,"t":["ஆறிடும் வேள்வி அருமறை நூலவர்","கூறிடும் அந்தணர் கோடிபேர் உண்பதில்","நீறிடும் தொண்டர் நினைவின் பயனிலை","பேறெனில் ஓர்பிடி பேறது வாகுமே."],"p":"","r":"Feeding ten million Brahmins learned in the rare Veda who perform the six duties does not bring the fruit of thinking of the ash-wearing servant of the Lord; if it is gain one wants, a single handful given to him is the gain.","n":"ஆறிடும் வேள்வி is taken as the Brahmin's six duties (studying and teaching, sacrificing and having sacrifice done, giving and receiving). The syntax of line 3 is compressed; the sense of comparison is clear but the exact construction is not.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["adiyaar","eegai","andhanar","thiruneeru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:133"},"t:1862":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1862,"t":["ஏறுடை யாய்இறை வாஎம்பி ரான்என்று","நீறிடு வார்அடி யார்நிகழ் தேவர்கள்","ஆறணி செஞ்சடை அண்ணல் இவர்என்று","வேறுஅணி வார்க்கு வினையில்லை தானே."],"p":"","r":"Those who wear the ash crying 'Lord who rides the bull, our master' are devotees, gods walking on earth; those who honour them apart, saying 'this is the Lord with the river on his red locks', have no karma.","n":"வேறு அணிவார் is unclear: those who honour the devotees specially, or who adorn them as the Lord. Either way the verse treats seeing the Lord in his devotees as what frees from karma.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["adiyaar","thiruneeru","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:133"},"t:1863":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1863,"t":["சீர்நந்தி கொண்டு திருமுக மாய்விட்ட","பேர்நந்தி என்னும் பிறங்கு சடையனை","நான்நொந்து நொந்து வருமளவுஞ் சொல்லப்","பேர்நந்தி என்னும் பிதற்குஒழி யேனே."],"p":"","r":"Glorious Nandi took me and sent me as his holy word; until I come back, though I ache and ache, I will not stop babbling 'Great Nandi' of the one with shining matted hair.","n":"திருமுகம் can be a royal letter or the holy face. On the first reading Tirumular is Nandi's message sent into the world, which fits the tradition that he was sent south from Kailasa.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","sivam","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:133"},"t:1864":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1864,"t":["அழிதகவு இல்லா அரன்அடி யாரைத்","தொழுகை ஞாலத்துத் தூfங்கிருள் நீங்கும்","பழுது படாவண்ணம் பண்பனை நாடித்","தொழுதெழ வையகத்து ஓர்இன்பம் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"By worshipping Hara's devotees, who know no decline, the world's thick darkness leaves; seeking the gracious one so as not to fall short, and rising after worship, there is a joy unlike any on earth.","n":"பண்பன் (the one of goodness) may be the Lord or the devotee; the verse moves from honouring devotees to seeking him without a break.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["adiyaar","vazhipaadu","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:133"},"t:1865":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1865,"t":["பகவற்குஏதா கிலும் பண்பில ராகிப்","புகுமத்த ராய்நின்று பூசனை செய்யும்","முகமத்தோடு ஒத்துநின்று ஊழிதோ றூழி","அகமத்த ராகிநின்று ஆய்ந்தொழிந் தா ரே"],"p":"","r":"Those who worship the Lord without the fitting disposition, entering as if possessed and matching only in outward face, stay deluded within age after age and, for all their inquiry, come to nothing.","n":"The verse plays on மத்தர் (the frenzied): புகுமத்தர், முகமத்து, அகமத்தர். Its target may be worshippers of the temple image who neglect the devotees, given the chapter's argument, but the verse does not say so.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","koodaozhukkam","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:133"},"t:1866":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1866,"t":["வித்தக மாகிய வேடத்தர் உண்டஊன்","அத்தன் அயன்மால் அருந்திய வண்ணமாம்","சித்தம் தெளிந்தவர் சேடம் பருகிடின்","முத்தியாம் என்றுநம் மூலன் மொழிந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"The food eaten by those in the wise guise of devotees is as if the Father, Brahma and Vishnu had eaten it; if the clear-minded drink what they leave, it is release: so our Mulan has said.","n":"ஊன் here is food, not flesh. சேடம் is the remnant of the devotee's meal, which tradition treats as sanctified. Tirumular names himself (நம் மூலன்) as the authority.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adiyaar","eegai","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:133"},"t:1867":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1867,"t":["தாழ்விலர் பின்னும் முயல்வர் அருந்தவம்","ஆழ்வினை ஆழ அவர்க்கே அறஞ்செய்யும்","ஆழ்வினை நீக்கி அருவினை தன்னொடும்","போழ்வினை தீர்க்கும் அப் பொன்னுலகு ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"Those who never fall still strive at rare austerity; charity done to them alone sinks deep karma, removes it along with hard and cleaving karma, and becomes the golden world.","n":"The kinds of karma (ஆழ்வினை, அருவினை, போழ்வினை) are not clearly distinguished; the repetition may be for sound. The subject of தீர்க்கும் may be the charity or the ascetics.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aram","vinai","thavam","adiyaar"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:133"},"t:1868":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1868,"t":["திகைக்குரி யானொரு தேவனை நாடும்","வகைக்குரி யானொரு வாது இருக்கில்","பகைக்குரி யாரில்லைப் பார்மழை பெய்யும்","அகக்குறை கேடில்லை அவ்வுல குக்கே."],"p":"","r":"If there is one who seeks the god of all directions in the fitting way, there are no enemies, rain falls on the earth, and that world suffers no inner want or ruin.","n":"ஒரு வாது இருக்கில் is odd; it is probably ஒருவன் இருக்கில், 'if there is one'. On the plain sense a single seeker of the Lord brings peace and rain to his whole land.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["adiyaar","mazhai","naadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:134"},"t:1869":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1869,"t":["அவ்வுல கத்தே பிறந்துஅவ் உடலோணடும்","அவ்வுல கத்தே அருந்தவம் நாடுவர்","அவ்வுல கத்தே அரனடி கூடுவர்","அவ்வுல கத்தே அருள்பெறு வாரே."],"p":"","r":"Born in that world, with that very body they seek rare austerity in that world, join Hara's feet in that world and gain grace in that world.","n":"அவ்வுலகம் may be the land of 1868 where the devotee lives, or this world as against another. Either way the stress falls on attaining in the same body and the same world one is born in, not after death.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["adiyaar","thavam","thiruvadi","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:134"},"t:1870":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1870,"t":["கொண்ட குறியும் குலவரை உச்சியும்","அண்டரும் அண்டத்து அமரரும் ஆதியும்","எண்டிசை யோரும்வந்து என்கைத் தலத்தினுள்","உண்டெனில் நாம்இனி உய்ந்தொழிந் தோமே."],"p":"","r":"If the sign I have taken, the summit of the great mountain, the gods, the immortals of the cosmos, the Primal one and those of the eight directions all come into the palm of my hand, then we are saved for good.","n":"கொண்ட குறி (the sign taken) may be the linga or the form of the Lord meditated on, and குலவரை உச்சி Kailasa. The verse may be a condition or a claim: the devotee who holds the Lord holds everything.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["adiyaar","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:134"},"t:1871":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1871,"t":["அண்டங்கள் ஏழும் அகண்டமும் ஆவியும்","கொண்ட சராசரம் முற்றும் குணங்களும்","பண்டை மறையும் படைப்பளிப்பு ஆதியும்","கண்டசிவனும்என் கண்ணன்றி இல்லையே."],"p":"","r":"The seven worlds, the undivided, the soul, all the moving and unmoving, the qualities, the ancient Veda, creation, protection and the rest, and Siva who beholds them: none is apart from my eye.","n":"என் கண் can be the eye of knowledge that sees all, or the devotee's eye in which all is contained. கண்ட சிவன் may also mean 'the Siva I have seen'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","meyyunarvu","aindhozhil"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:134"},"t:1872":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1872,"t":["பெண்ணல்ல ஆணல்ல பேடல்ல மூடத்துள்","உள்நின்ற சோதி ஒருவர்க்கு அறியொணாக்","கண்ணின்றிக் காணும் செவியின்றிக் கேட்டிரும்","அண்ணல் பெருமையை ஆய்ந்தது மூப்பே."],"p":"","r":"The light within the dark, neither woman nor man nor neuter, cannot be known by anyone; he sees without eyes and hears without ears, and to have searched out the Lord's greatness is true maturity.","n":"மூடத்துள் may be the body, ignorance, or the hidden heart. மூப்பு (age, seniority) is taken as ripeness of wisdom; the line could also mean that inquiring into his greatness only ends in old age.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","meyyunarvu","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:134"},"t:1873":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1873,"t":["இயங்கும் உலகினில் ஈசன் அடியார்","மயங்கா வழிசெல்வர் வானுலகு ஆள்வர்","புயங்களும் எண்டிசை போதுபா தாள","மயங்காப் பகிரண்ட மாமுடி தானே."],"p":"","r":"In this moving world the Lord's devotees go by the undeluded way and rule the world of the sky; their arms are the eight directions, their feet the netherworld, and their great crown the outer cosmos.","n":"The second half gives the devotee the cosmic body usually given to the Lord. போது பாதாளம் is read as 'the feet reach the netherworld'; பகிரண்டம் is the outer cosmos.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["adiyaar","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:134"},"t:1874":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1874,"t":["அகம்படி கின்றநம் ஐயனை ஒரும்","அகம்படி கண்டவர் அல்லலில் சேரார்","அகம்படி உட்புக்கு அறிகின்ற நெஞ்சம்","அகம்படி கண்டுஆம் அழிக்கலும் எட்டே."],"p":"","r":"Contemplate our Lord who dwells in the inner chamber; those who have seen the inner chamber will not come to grief; the heart that enters within and knows, seeing the inner chamber, can destroy the eight.","n":"அகம்படி is the inner apartment of a palace and those who serve in it, and also simply 'within'; the verse uses it in every line. The eight (எட்டு) to be destroyed are not named; the eight bodily constituents or eight bonds are possible.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["adiyaar","manam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:134"},"t:1875":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1875,"t":["கழிவும் முதலும் காதல் துணையும்","அழிவும் தாய்நின்ற ஆதிப் பிரானைப்","பழியும் புகழும் படுபொருள் முற்றும்","ஒழியும்என் ஆவி உழவுகொண் டானே"],"p":"","r":"The first Lord, who stood as end and beginning, as love's companion and as destruction, has taken my soul as his field to plough; blame and praise and all that befalls me now fall away.","n":"உழவு கொண்டான் pictures the Lord tilling the soul. Taking கழிவும் முதலும் as end and beginning is the usual reading; காதல் துணை may also be his consort.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","samanilai","pazhi","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:134"},"t:1876":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1876,"t":["என்தாயோடு என்அப்பன் ஏழ்ஏழ் பிறவியும்","அன்றே சிவனுக்கு எழுதிய ஆவணம்","ஒன்றாய் உலகம் படைத்தான் எழுதினான்","நின்றான் முகில்வண்ணன் நேர்எழுத் தாமே."],"p":"","r":"For my mother, my father and seven times seven births, a deed of bond to Siva was written that very day; he who alone created the world wrote it, and the cloud-coloured one stood as witness.","n":"ஆவணம் is a legal deed, here a deed of servitude, and நேர்எழுத்து the attesting signature, so Brahma writes it and Vishnu witnesses it. The image of hereditary bondage to Siva by deed resembles the story told of Sundarar. Sengalvaraya cites Appar's 'முந்தி உலகம் படைத்தான்'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adiyaar","adimai","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:134"},"t:1877":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1877,"t":["துணிந்தார் அகம்படி துன்னி உறையும்","பணிந்தார் அகம்படி பால்பட்டு ஒழுகும்","அணிந்தார் அகம்படி ஆதிப் பிரானைக்","கணிந்தார் ஒருவர்க்கு கைவிடலாமே."],"p":"","r":"He dwells close within the resolute, flows like milk within the humble, and stands as the Primal Lord within those who wear his signs; for one who has reckoned him so, can he be let go?","n":"The last line is unclear: it may ask whether such a devotee could ever be abandoned, or whether he could ever give the Lord up. அணிந்தார் may be those who wear ash and rudraksha, or those who adorn the Lord.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["adiyaar","panivu","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:134"},"t:1878":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1878,"t":["தலைமிசை வானவர் தாழ்சடை நந்தி","மிலைமிசை வைத்தனன் மெய்ப்பணி செய்யப்","புலைமிசை நீங்கிய பொன்னுலகு ஆளும்","பலமிசை செய்யும் படர்சடை யோனே."],"p":"","r":"Nandi of the hanging locks, above the heads of the gods, placed his devotees on his head as a garland so that they might do true service; the one of spreading locks rules the golden world free of baseness and grants its fruit.","n":"மிலை is a garland worn on the head; who is placed there is not said, and some take it as the Lord placing his feet on the devotee's head. The golden world (பொன்னுலகு) recurs from 1867.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["adiyaar","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:134"},"t:1879":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1879,"t":["அறியாப் பருவத்து அரன்அடி யாரைக்","குறியால் அறிந்தின்பம் கொண்டது அடிமை","குறியார் சடைமுடி கட்டி நடப்பார்","மறியார் புனல்மூழ்க மாதவம் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"To recognise Hara's devotees by their signs and delight in them even at an age that does not yet understand is true servitude; those who walk with the matted crown of his sign, bathing in wave-filled waters, make great austerity.","n":"அறியாப் பருவம் is childhood, the time before understanding; the verse may be thinking of child saints like Sambandar. மறியார் புனல் may also be the waters of the Ganga in the locks of the one who holds the deer (மறி).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["adiyaar","adimai","thavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:134"},"t:1880":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1880,"t":["அவன்பால் அணுகியே அன்புசெய் வார்கள்","சிவன்பால் அணுகுதல் செய்யவும் வல்லன்","அவன்பால் அணுகியே நாடும் அடியார்","இவன்பால் பெருமை இலயமது ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"Those who approach him and love him are able to approach Siva too; for the devotees who draw near and seek him, greatness in him becomes absorption.","n":"அவன் is most likely the devotee or the guru, so that approaching him is approaching Siva; இலயம் is absorption. The referents of அவன் and இவன் are not fixed by the verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["adiyaar","anbu","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:134"},"t:1881":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1881,"t":["முன்னிருந் தார்முழுது எண்கணத் தேவர்கள்","எண்ணிறந்து அன்பால் வருவர் இருநிலத்து","எண்இரு நாலு திசைஅந் தரம் ஒக்கப்","பன்னிரு காதம் பதஞ்செய்யும் பாரே."],"p":"","r":"The eight classes of gods who were there before come in countless numbers with love to this wide earth, and across the directions and the sky alike the land for twelve leagues around is made fit.","n":"The verse continues the theme of the place where a devotee lives. எண்இரு நாலு திசை and பன்னிரு காதம் are numbers whose exact sense is unclear; பதஞ்செய்யும் may mean 'becomes ripe' or 'becomes a holy station'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["adiyaar","naadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:134"},"t:1882":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1882,"t":["சிவயோகி ஞானி செறிந்தஅத் தேசம்","அவயோகம் இன்றி அறிவோர் உண்டாகும்","நவயோகம் கைகூடும் நல்லியல் காணும்","பவயோகம் இன்றிப் பரலோகம் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"The land where a Siva-yogi and jnani dwell will have knowers free of false yoga; the ninefold yoga will be gained, good order will be seen, and without the yoga of births it becomes the higher world.","n":"அவயோகம் (bad yoga), நவயோகம் (nine yogas or new yoga) and பவயோகம் (union with birth) form a play on யோகம். The nine yogas are not specified.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","gnaanam","naadu","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:134"},"t:1883":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1883,"t":["மேலுணர் வான்மிகு ஞாலம் படைத்தவன்","மேலுணர் வான்மிகு ஞாலம் கடந்தவன்","மேலுணர் வார்மிகு ஞாலத்து அமரர்கள்","மேலுணுர் வார்சிவன் மெய்யடி யார்களே."],"p":"","r":"He who created the vast world knows from above; he who transcends the vast world knows from above; the immortals of the vast world know from above; and Siva's true devotees know from above.","n":"The four lines repeat மேலுணர், so the devotees end a ladder beginning with the Creator and Siva. மேலுணர்வான் may also mean 'the one known as highest', which would make the verse a list of who is supreme.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["adiyaar","meyyunarvu","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:134"},"t:1884":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1884,"t":["எட்டுத் திசையும் இறைவன் அடியவர்க்கு","கட்ட அடிசில் அழுதென்று எதிர்கொள்வர்","ஒட்டி ஒருநிலம் ஆள்பவர் அந்நிலம்","விட்டுக் கிடக்கில் விருப்பறி யாரே."],"p":"","r":"In all eight directions people welcome the Lord's devotees with prepared food, calling it nectar; those who cling to ruling a piece of land would not know desire for it if they left it lying.","n":"கட்ட அடிசில் may be food made ready or food bundled for the road. The second half is obscure; it may contrast the devotee, fed everywhere, with the king who clings to his land, or say that a king who gave up his land for this life would not miss it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["adiyaar","undi","virundhu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:135"},"t:1885":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1885,"t":["அச்சிவன் உள்நின்ற அருளை அறிந்தவர்","உச்சியம் போதாக உள்ளமர் கோவிற்குப்","பிச்சை பிடித்துண்டு பேதம் அறநினைந்து","இச்சைவிட்டு ஏகாந்தத்து ஏறி இருப்பரே."],"p":"","r":"Those who know the grace standing within Siva beg alms at noon for the Lord in the temple within, eat, think of all without distinction, give up desire and sit raised in solitude.","n":"உள்ளமர் கோவில் is the temple within, the heart where the Lord dwells, so the alms are begged for him and eating is an offering. பேதம் அற நினைந்து may mean without distinguishing donors or without the sense of self and other.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","adiyaar","thuravu","avaa"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:135"},"t:1886":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1886,"t":["விச்சுக் கலம் உண்டு வேலிச்செய் ஒன்றுண்டு","உச்சிக்கு முன்னே உழவு சமைந்தது","அச்சம்கெட்டு அச்செயல் அறுத்துண்ண மாட்டாதார்","இச்சைக்குப் பிச்சை இரக்கின்ற வாறே."],"p":"","r":"There is a seed pot and there is one fenced field, and the ploughing was finished before noon; those who cannot lose their fear, reap that crop and eat, go begging alms for their desires.","n":"The seed pot and the fenced field are symbolic; tradition takes them as bindu and the body, or as the grace and the soul, with the work already done for the devotee. The plain point is that those who do not use what they have beg out of desire.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iravu","avaa","uzhavu","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:136"},"t:1887":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1887,"t":["பிச்சையது ஏற்றான் பிரமன் தலைதன்னில்","பிச்சையது ஏற்றான் பிரியா அறஞ்செய்யப்","பிச்சையது ஏற்றான் பிரமன் சிரங்காட்டிப்","பிச்சையது ஏற்றான் பிரமன் பரமாகவே."],"p":"","r":"He took alms in Brahma's skull; he took alms to do unfailing dharma; he took alms showing Brahma's head; he took alms so that Brahma might become supreme.","n":"The verse draws on the story of Siva as the divine beggar who cut off Brahma's fifth head and begged with its skull. பிரமன் பரமாகவே may mean to raise Brahma, to show Brahma's pride humbled, or to show himself as the supreme beyond Brahma; it is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iravu","sivam","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:136"},"t:1888":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1888,"t":["பரந்துலகு ஏழும் படைத்த பிரானை","இரந்துணி என்பர்கள் எற்றுக்கு இரக்கும்","நிரந்தக மாக நினையும் அடியார்","இரந்துண்டு தன்கழல் எட்டச்செய் தானே."],"p":"","r":"People call the Lord who spread out and created the seven worlds a beggar; why does he beg? For the devotees who think of him without end, he begged so that by begging and eating they might reach his feet.","n":"The verse makes Siva's begging a model: by begging himself he made begging a path for his devotees. இரந்துண்டு may describe the Lord or the devotees; both readings lead to the same point.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","sivam","adiyaar","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:136"},"t:1889":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1889,"t":["வரஇருந் தான்வழி நின்றிடும் ஈசன்","தரஇருந் தான்தன்னை நல்லவர்க்கு இன்பம்","பொரஇருந் தான்புக லேபுக லாக","வரஇருந் தால்அறி யான்என்ப தாமே."],"p":"","r":"The Lord who stands on the way waits ready to come, waits ready to give himself as joy to the good, waits to contend as their only refuge; yet if he waits to come, they say he does not know.","n":"The verse repeats இருந்தான் (he stayed, he waited). Its last line is obscure; it may mean that people think the Lord is unaware while he only waits for them to turn to him.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:136"},"t:1890":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1890,"t":["அங்கார் பசியும் அவாவும் வெகுளியும்","தங்கார் சிவனடி யார்சரீரத்திடைப்","பொங்கார் புவனத்தும் புண்ணிய லோகத்தும்","தங்கார் சிவனைத் தலைப்படு வாரே."],"p":"","r":"Fiery hunger, craving and anger do not stay in the bodies of Siva's devotees; those who reach Siva do not stay even in the abundant worlds or the worlds of merit.","n":"The verse pairs two kinds of not staying: the passions do not stay in the devotee, and the devotee does not stay in any heaven earned by merit. Hunger stands with the passions here, which suits a chapter on living by alms.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adiyaar","pasi","avaa","vekuli"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:136"},"t:1891":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1891,"t":["மெய்யக ஞானம் மிகத்தெளிந் தார்களும்","கையதும் நீண்டார் கடைத்தலைக் கேசெல்வர்","ஐயம் புகாமல் இருந்த தவசியார்","வையகம் எல்லாம் வரஇருந்தாரே."],"p":"","r":"Even those very clear in true inner knowledge go with outstretched hand to the doorstep; the ascetics who stayed without going for alms found the whole world coming to them.","n":"ஐயம் is alms, but also doubt, so ஐயம் புகாமல் may mean 'without entering for alms' or 'without doubt entering'. On the first reading the verse allows two ways for the ascetic and gives the higher place to staying put.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iravu","gnaanam","thavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:136"},"t:1892":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1892,"t":["நாலேழு மாறவே நண்ணிய முத்திரை","பாலான மோன மொழியில் பதிவித்து","மேலான நந்தி திருவடி மீதுய்யக்","கோலா கலங்கெட்டுக் கூடுநன் முத்தியே."],"p":"","r":"When the four-sevens cease, the mudra attained is fixed in the speech of silence, and saved at the holy feet of supreme Nandi, with all tumult gone, one gains good release.","n":"நாலேழு may be twenty-eight, or four and seven; what ceases is not said, perhaps the senses and faculties or the tattvas. பாலான மோன மொழி is the 'speech' of silence, a paradox the chapter returns to.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["muththirai","mounam","thiruvadi","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:137"},"t:1893":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1893,"t":["துரியங்கள் மூன்று சொருகுஇட னாகி","அரிய உரைத்தாரம் அங்கே அடக்கி","மருவிய சாம்பவி கேசரி உண்மை","பெருகிய ஞானம் பிறழ்முத் திரையே."],"p":"","r":"The three turiyas become the place of closing; restraining there the hard-to-utter sound, shambhavi and khechari join, and the truth is knowledge growing: these are the distinct mudras.","n":"துரியங்கள் மூன்று (three fourth-states), சொருகு இடம் (the place of closing or inserting) and உரைத்தாரம் are technical and obscure. Shambhavi (gaze fixed within) and khechari (the tongue turned up, moving in space) are named mudras of yoga.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["muththirai","yogam","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:137"},"t:1894":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1894,"t":["சாம்பவி நந்தி தன்னருள் பார்வையாம்","ஆம்பவம் இல்லா அருட்பாணி முத்திரை","ஓம்பயில் ஒங்கிய உண்மைய கேசரி","நாம்பயில் நாதன்மெய்ஞ் ஞானமுத் திரையே."],"p":"","r":"Shambhavi is Nandi's gracious glance; the grace-hand mudra is free of births; khechari is the truth that rises in practising Om; and the knowledge-mudra is the true knowledge of the Lord we practise.","n":"The verse reads each mudra as an inner reality rather than a posture: the gaze becomes the Lord's glance, the gesture of the hand his grace. அருட்பாணி may be the hand gesture of grace or the grace of the Lord's hand.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["muththirai","thiruvarul","piranavam","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:137"},"t:1895":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1895,"t":["தானத்தின் உள்ளே சதாசிவன் ஆயிடும்","ஞானத்தின் உள்ளே நற்சிவம் ஆதலால்","ஏனைச் சிவமாம் சொரூபம் மறைந்திட்ட","மோனத்து முத்திரை முத்தாந்த முத்தியே."],"p":"","r":"Within the station he becomes Sadasiva, within knowledge he is the good Sivam; so the mudra of silence, in which the form of Sivam otherwise seen is hidden, is release beyond release.","n":"தானம் may be a yogic station or a place of worship; ஏனைச் சிவமாம் சொரூபம் is unclear. முத்தாந்த முத்தி, the release at the end of release, is a Siddhanta phrase for the final state.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["muththirai","mounam","sivam","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:137"},"t:1896":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1896,"t":["வாக்கு மனமும் இரண்டும் மவுனமாம்","வாக்கு மவுனத்து வந்தாலும் மூங்கையாம்","வாக்கு மனமும் மவுனமாம் சுத்தரே","ஆக்கும் அச் சுத்த்ததை யார்அறி வார்களே."],"p":"","r":"When speech and mind are both still, that is silence; if only speech falls silent, one is merely mute; the pure are those in whom speech and mind are silent, and who knows the purity they bring about?","n":"The verse separates mauna from muteness (மூங்கை): silence of the tongue alone is nothing. The last line may also be read 'who can know that pure state which makes them?'","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mounam","manam","sol","thooimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:137"},"t:1897":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1897,"t":["யோகத்தின் முத்திரை ஓர்அட்ட சித்தியாம்","ஏகத்த ஞானத்து முத்திரை எண்ணுங்கால்","ஆகத் தகும்வேத கேசரி சாம்பவி","யோகத்துக் கேசரி யோகமுத் திரையே."],"p":"","r":"The mudra of yoga is the eight siddhis; considering the mudra of knowledge that is one, Veda-khechari and shambhavi are fitting; for yoga, khechari is the yoga mudra.","n":"The verse sorts mudras by path, yoga and knowledge, but the assignments in lines 2 and 3 are compressed and the term வேத கேசரி is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["muththirai","yogam","siththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:137"},"t:1898":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1898,"t":["யோகிஎண் சித்தி அருளொலி வாதனை","போகி தன் புத்தி புருடார்த்த நன்னெறி","ஆகும்நன் சத்தியும் ஆதார சோதனை","ஏகமும் கண்டொன்றில் எய்திநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"The yogi, the eight siddhis, the sound of grace, the traces of past impressions, the enjoyer, his intellect, the good path of the aims of life, the good shakti and the testing of the supports: seeing them as one, he reaches the One and stands.","n":"The verse is a list of terms (வாதனை for vasana, புருடார்த்தம் for the human aims, ஆதார சோதனை for examining the bodily supports) whose links are not stated. The point seems to be that all are gathered into one vision.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","siththi","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:137"},"t:1899":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1899,"t":["துவாதச மார்க்கமென் கோடச மார்க்கமாம்","அவாஅறும் ஈர்ஐ வகைஅங்கம் ஆறும்","தவாஅறு வேதாந்த சித்தாந்தத் தன்மை","நவாஅக மோடுஉன்னல் நற்சுத்த சைவமே."],"p":"","r":"The twelvefold path and the sixteenfold path, the ten kinds that end desire and the six limbs, the unfailing nature of Vedanta and Siddhanta, considered together with the nine Agamas: that is pure Saivam.","n":"துவாதசம் (twelve) and கோடசம் (sixteen) may be the paths of breath reaching twelve and sixteen finger-breadths, or kalas. நவாஅகமோடு is read as the nine Agamas Tirumular names elsewhere, but நவ may also mean new. The verse makes pure Saivam hold Vedanta and Siddhanta together.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saivam","vedaantham","siththaantham","aagamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:137"},"t:1900":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1900,"t":["மோனத்து முத்திரை முத்தர்க்கு முத்திரை","ஞானத்து முத்திரை நாதர்க்கு முத்திரை","தேனிக்கும் முத்திரை சித்தாந்த முத்திரை","கானிக்கும் முத்திரை கண்ட சமயமே."],"p":"","r":"The mudra of silence is the seal of the liberated, the mudra of knowledge the seal of the lords, the mudra that sweetens is the Siddhanta mudra, and the mudra that is displayed belongs to the religion seen outwardly.","n":"தேனிக்கும் and கானிக்கும் are obscure; they are read here as 'makes sweet' and 'is shown', which ranks inner mudras above those seen in a sect's practice. The ranking is my reading of the sequence, not stated.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["muththirai","mounam","siththaantham","samayam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:137"},"t:1901":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1901,"t":["தூநெறி கண்ட சுவடு நடுவுஎழும்","பூநெறி கண்டுஅது பொன்னக மாய்நிற்கும்","மேல்நெறி கண்டது வெண்மதி மேதினி","நீல்நெறி கண்டுள நின்மலன் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"The trace seen on the pure path rises in the middle; the flower path seen there stands as the golden place; the higher path seen is the white moon's land; and the one who has seen the blue path becomes the Spotless One.","n":"The four paths, pure, flowered, higher and blue, are coloured stages of yogic ascent through the central channel, but which centres or lights they name is not fixed by the verse. நின்மலன் may be the Lord or the yogi who becomes like him.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naadi","yogam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:137"},"t:1902":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1902,"t":["வளர்பிறை யில்தேவர் தம்பாலின் முன்னி","உளரொளி பானுவின் உள்ளே ஒடுங்கித்","தளர்வில் பிதிர்பதம் தங்கிச் சசியுள்","உளதுறும் யோகி உடல்விட்டால் தானே."],"p":"","r":"A yogi who leaves the body in the waxing fortnight goes, it seems, by the gods' way into the sun's light, rests in the realm of the fathers and comes to what lies in the moon.","n":"வளர்பிறை (the waxing fortnight), தேவர் (gods), பானு (sun), பிதிர்பதம் (the fathers' station) and சசி (moon) recall the old teaching of two after-death paths, the gods' way through the sun and the fathers' way through the moon, and of death in the bright fortnight as auspicious. That is the traditional frame, not something the verse spells out; whether these are stages of one journey or alternatives is not clear from the Tamil.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","saakkaadu","marumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:138"},"t:1903":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1903,"t":["தான்இவை ஒக்கும் சமாதிaக கூடாது","போன வியோகி புகலிடம் போந்துபின்","ஆனவை தீர நிரந்தர மாயோகம்","ஆனவை சேர்வார் அருளின் சார் வாகியே."],"p":"","r":"A yogi who dies without reaching a samadhi equal to these goes to a world of refuge, then returns and, practising unbroken great yoga until what remains is spent, reaches the goal by relying on grace.","n":"The first line is corrupt, so 'without reaching samadhi' is a reconstruction. வியோகி may mean simply the yogi who has departed, or one fallen from yoga. The point that survives is that unfinished yoga is resumed in a later life rather than lost.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["yogam","samaadhi","piravi","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:138"},"t:1904":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1904,"t":["தான்இவ் வகையே புவியோர் நெறிதங்கி","ஆன சிவயோகத்து ஆமாறுஆம் அவ்விந்து","தானதில் அந்தச் சிவயோகி ஆகுமுன்","ஊனத்தோர் சித்திவந்து ஓர்காயம் ஆகுமே."],"p":"","r":"One who keeps in this way to the path open to people on earth advances through Siva-yoga by way of the bindu; if he is still imperfect before he becomes a full Siva-yogi, powers come to him and he takes on one body.","n":"விந்து (bindu), ஊனத்தோர் (the defective) and ஓர்காயம் (one body) are technical and unclear. Tradition takes the last line to mean that attainments short of the goal yield siddhis and a perfected or further body; it may equally mean one more embodiment. The verse places powers below full Siva-yoga.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","vindhu","siththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:138"},"t:1905":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1905,"t":["சிவயோகி ஞானி சிதைந்துடல் விட்டால்","தவலோகம் சேர்ந்துபின் தான்வந்து கூடிச்","சிவயோக ஞானத்தால் சேர்ந்தவர் நிற்பர்","புவலோகம் போற்றும்நற் புண்ணியத்தோரே."],"p":"","r":"When a Siva-yogi who has knowledge leaves a worn-out body, he reaches the world of austerity, then comes back and, through the knowledge of Siva-yoga, stands united; such people of merit are honoured by the worlds.","n":"தவலோகம் and புவலோகம் are taken as names of the upper worlds (tapoloka, bhuvarloka) in the Puranic scheme. 'Comes back' marks this as a step short of the jnani's case in the next verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","gnaanam","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:138"},"t:1906":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1906,"t":["ஊனமில் ஞானிநல் யோகி உடல்விட்டால்","தானற மோனச் சமாதியுள் தங்கியே","தானவன் ஆகும் பரகாயம் சாராதே","ஊனமில் முத்தராய் மீளார் உணர்வுற்றே."],"p":"","r":"When the faultless jnani who is also a true yogi leaves the body, he rests in a silent samadhi where the self is gone, becomes That, takes no other body, and as one freed without fault does not return.","n":"தானற ... தானவன் ஆகும் plays on self: the self ends and he becomes He. பரகாயம் usually names the siddhi of entering another's body; here the jnani needs no body at all. The verse sets this against the returns of 1903 to 1905.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["samaadhi","veedu","gnaanam","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:138"},"t:1907":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1907,"t":["செத்தார் பெறும் பயன் ஆவது ஏதெனில்","செத்துநீர் சேர்வது சித்தினைக் கூடிடில்","செத்தார் இருந்தார் செகத்தில் திரிமலம்","செத்தார் சிவமாகி யேசித்தர் தாமே."],"p":"","r":"If asked what the dead gain, it is to join pure consciousness; those who, still living in the world, have died to the three impurities become Siva and are the true siddhas.","n":"The verse turns on செத்தார் (the dead) used four times: bodily death in the first line, death to the three மலம் in the last two. சித்து may mean consciousness (chit) or Siva as consciousness, and the second line's syntax is loose. 'Dying while alive' is the plain point.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["malam","sivam","saakkaadu","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:138"},"t:1908":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1908,"t":["உன்னக் கருவிட்டு உரவோன் அரன்அருள்","பன்னப் பரமே அருட்குலம் பாலிப்பன்","என்னப் புதல்வர்க்கும் வேண்டி யிடுஞானி","தன்இச்சைக்கு ஈசன் உருச்செய்யும் தானே."],"p":"","r":"The strong one who has left birth behind protects, by Hara's grace, a lineage of grace, and for a jnani who asks even for sons the Lord gives form to what he wills.","n":"Most of this verse is obscure. கருவிட்டு is taken as leaving the womb, that is birth; அருட்குலம் as a lineage or community of grace, perhaps disciples; the last line as the Lord shaping things to the jnani's wish. Whether புதல்வர் means bodily sons or spiritual heirs cannot be settled from the Tamil.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","gnaanam","piravi","makkal"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:138"},"t:1909":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1909,"t":["எங்கும் சிவமாய் அருளாம் இதயத்துத்","தங்கும் சிவஞானிக்கு எங்குமாம் தற்பரம்","அங்காங்கு எனநின்று சகமுண்ட வான்தோய்தல்","இங்கே இறந்துஎங்கு மாய்நிற்கும் ஈசனே."],"p":"","r":"For the Siva-jnani in whose heart grace dwells, Siva is everywhere and the Supreme is present everywhere; having died here, he stands everywhere as the Lord.","n":"The last line can be read with the jnani as subject (he becomes the all-pervading Lord) or with ஈசன் as subject (the Lord stands everywhere). The third line, on the sky that swallowed the world, is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","gnaanam","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:138"},"t:1910":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1910,"t":["அந்தமில் ஞானிதன் ஆகம் தீயினில்","வெந்திடின் நாடெலாம் வெப்புத் தீயினில்","நொந்து நாய்நரி நுகரின் நுண்செரு","வந்துநாய் நரிக்கு உணவாகும் வையகமே."],"p":"","r":"If the body of a boundless jnani is burned, the whole land burns with fever; if dogs and jackals feed on it, strife comes and the world itself becomes food for dogs and jackals.","n":"The punishment mirrors the wrong: fire for fire, dogs and jackals for dogs and jackals. நுண்செரு, read here as strife, is an unusual word.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["samaadhi","gnaanam","yaakkai","naadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:139"},"t:1911":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1911,"t":["எண்ணிலா ஞானி உடல்எரி தாவிடில்","அண்ணல்தம் கோயில் அழல்இட்டது ஆங்கு ஒக்கும்","மண்ணில் மழைவிழா வையகம் பஞ்சமாம்","எண்ணரு மன்னர் இழப்பார் அரசே."],"p":"","r":"If fire touches the body of a jnani beyond reckoning, it is as if the Lord's own temple were set alight; rain fails, the world falls into famine and countless kings lose their kingdoms.","n":"அண்ணல்தம் கோயில் makes the jnani's body the Lord's temple, a claim Tirumular makes of the body generally elsewhere. The harm is to the whole land and its rulers, not only to those who burn the body.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["samaadhi","yaakkai","mazhai","arasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:139"},"t:1912":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1912,"t":["புண்ணிய மாம்அவர் தம்மைப் புதைப்பது","நண்ணி அனல்கோக்கில் நாட்டில் அழிவாகும்","மண்ணில் அழியில் அலங்கார பங்கமாம்","மண்ணுலகு எல்லாம் மயங்கும் அனல்மண்டியே."],"p":"","r":"Burying such people is an act of merit; heaping fire on them brings ruin to the land, letting the body decay in the open disgraces it, and the whole world is thrown into confusion by the spreading fire.","n":"The third line, மண்ணில் அழியில் அலங்கார பங்கமாம், is taken as decay on open ground spoiling the body's dignity, but it could also mean harm to the land's order. The verse names burial as புண்ணியம், merit, and not just as the lesser evil.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["samaadhi","aram","naadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:139"},"t:1913":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1913,"t":["அந்தமில் ஞானி அருளை அடைந்தக்கால்","அந்த உடல்தான் குகைசெய்து இருந்திடில்","சுந்தர மன்னரும் தொல்புவி உள்ளோரும்","அந்தமில் இன்ப அருள்பெறு வாரே."],"p":"","r":"When the boundless jnani has reached grace, if that body is kept in a cave-tomb, fine kings and all who live on the ancient earth receive grace and joy without end.","n":"This is the reverse of 1910 to 1912: where burning harms land and kings, the kept body blesses them. குகை, cave, is the chapter's word for the tomb.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["samaadhi","thiruvarul","arasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:139"},"t:1914":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1914,"t":["நவமிகு சாணாலே நல்லாழம் செய்து","குவைமிகு சூழஐஞ் சாணாகக் கோட்டித்","தவமிகு குகைமுக் கோணமுச் சாணாக்கிப்","பவமறு நற்குகை பத்மா சனமே."],"p":"","r":"The pit is dug a good nine spans deep, its surround measured at five spans, the inner cave made triangular at three spans; in this birth-ending cave the body sits in the lotus posture.","n":"The measures nine, five and three are clear, but what குவைமிகு சூழ (the surround) refers to is not. பவமறு (ending birth) gives the tomb a part in the jnani's release, or marks it as fit for one already released.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["samaadhi","piravi","alavu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:139"},"t:1915":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1915,"t":["தன்மனை சாலை குளங்கரை ஆற்றிடை","நன்மலர்ச் சோலை நகரின்நற் பூமி","உன்னரும் கானம் உயர்ந்த மலைச்சாரல்","இந்நிலம் தான்குகைக்கு எய்தும் இடங்களே."],"p":"","r":"One's own house, a hall, a tank bank, the land between rivers, a flower grove, good ground in a town, a forest beyond thought and a high mountain slope are the places fit for the cave-tomb.","n":"The list runs from the household to the wilderness, so the tomb is not confined to remote or sacred ground.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["samaadhi","idam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:139"},"t:1916":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1916,"t":["நற்குகை நால்வட்டம் பஞ்சாங்க பாதமாய்","நிற்கின்ற பாதம் நவபாதம் நேர்விழப்","பொற்பமா ஓசமும் மூன்றுக்கு மூன்றுஅணி","நிற்பவர் தாம் செய்யும் நேர்மைய தாமே."],"p":"","r":"The verse gives further proportions for the cave: a fourfold enclosure, a base of five parts and of nine set true, and an arrangement three by three, done exactly by those who perform the rite.","n":"பஞ்சாங்க பாதம், நவபாதம் and பொற்பமா ஓசமும் are obscure, and the arithmetic cannot be fixed from the Tamil. Only the stress on exact measure is clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["samaadhi","alavu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:139"},"t:1917":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1917,"t":["பஞ்ச லோகங்கள் நவமணி பாரித்து","விஞ்சப் படுத்துஅதன் மேல்ஆ சனம்இட்டு","முஞ்சிப் படுத்துவெண் ணீறு இட்ட தன்மேலே","பொன்செய் நற்சுண்ணம் பொதியலும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"Five metals and nine gems are spread out abundantly, a seat placed over them, munja grass laid on it and white ash on that, and a pouch of fine gold powder may also be set there.","n":"பஞ்ச லோகம் and நவமணி are the standard five metals and nine gems of temple consecration, which treats the tomb like a shrine.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["samaadhi","vazhipaadu","thooimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:139"},"t:1918":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1918,"t":["நள்குகை நால்வட்டம் படுத்துஅதன் மேல்சாரக்","கள்ளவிழ தாமம் களபம்கத் தூரியும்","தெள்ளிய சாந்து புழுகுபன் னீர்சேர்த்து","ஒள்ளிய தூபம் உவந்திடு வீரே."],"p":"","r":"With the fourfold enclosure laid in the cave, put on it a garland dripping honey, sandal paste, musk, clear sandal, civet and rose water, and offer bright incense gladly.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["samaadhi","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:139"},"t:1919":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1919,"t":["ஓதிடும் வெண்ணீற்றால் உத்தூளம் குப்பாயம்","மீதினில் இட்டுஆ சனத்தினின் மேல் வைத்துப்","போதறு கண்ணமும் நறும் பொலிவித்து","மீதில் இருத்தி விரித்திடு வீரே."],"p":"","r":"The body is dusted with sacred ash and clothed, set on the seat, adorned with fragrant powder, seated on top, and then things are spread over it.","n":"போதறு கண்ணமும் is unclear: perhaps powder free of buds, or a corrupt form of சுண்ணம். What is spread in the last line, flowers or cloth, is not said.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["samaadhi","vazhipaadu","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:139"},"t:1920":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1920,"t":["விரித்தபின் நாற்சாரும் மேவுதல் செய்து","பொரித்த கறிபோ னகம் இள நீரும்","குருத்தலம் வைத்துஓர் குழைமுகம் பார்வை","தரித்தபின் மேல்வட்டம் சாத்திடு வீரே."],"p":"","r":"After spreading, the four sides are filled in, fried vegetables, cooked rice and tender coconut set down, and after a further act the cover above is closed.","n":"குருத்தலம் வைத்துஓர் குழைமுகம் பார்வை தரித்தபின் is obscure: it may refer to a guru's place, a face with ear-rings or a ritual gaze. The food offerings echo those made to a deity.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["samaadhi","vazhipaadu","undi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:139"},"t:1921":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1921,"t":["மீது சொரிந்திடும் வெண்ணீறும் கண்ணமும்","போது பலகொண்டு தர்ப்பைப்புல் வில்வமும்","பாத உதகத்தான் மஞ்சனம் செய்துபார்","மீதுமூன் றுக்குமூன்று அணிநிலம் செய்யுமே."],"p":"","r":"White ash, powder, many flowers, darbha grass and bilva are strewn over it, it is bathed with water of the feet, and the ground above is laid out three by three.","n":"பாத உதகம் is usually water that has washed a holy person's or deity's feet; whose feet is not said. The three-by-three ground repeats 1916.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["samaadhi","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:139"},"t:1922":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1922,"t":["ஆதன மீதில் அரசு சிவலிங்கம்","போதும் இரண்டினில் ஒன்றைத் தாபித்து","மேதரு சந்நிதி மேவுத் தரம்பூர்வம்","காதலில் சோடசம் காண்உப சாரமே."],"p":"","r":"On the platform above, install one of two, a peepal tree or a Siva linga, with the shrine facing north or east, and perform with love the sixteen services.","n":"அரசு here is the peepal tree, not kingship. சோடச உபசாரம் is the standard sixteenfold worship of a deity, so the tomb becomes a temple.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["samaadhi","vazhipaadu","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:139"},"t:1923":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1923,"t":["உதயத்தில் விந்துவில் ஓங்குகுண் டலியும்","உதயக் குடிலில் வயிந்தவம் ஒன்பான்","விதியில் பிரமாதி கள்மிகு சத்தி","கதியில் கரணம் கலைவை கரியே."],"p":"","r":"From bindu as it arises come kundalini and, in order, the nine bindu-born powers, the gods beginning with Brahma, the shaktis, the instruments, the kalas and speech.","n":"குடில் (kutila, the curved one), வயிந்தவம் (baindava, born of bindu), கரணம் and கலை are technical terms of Saiva cosmology, and கலைவை கரியே is taken as கலை, வைகரி (the spoken level of speech). The list order is the tradition's and the verse is compressed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vindhu","kundalini","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:140"},"t:1924":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1924,"t":["செய்திடும் விந்துபே தத்திறன் ஐ ஐந்தும்","செய்திடும் நாதபேதத்திற னால் ஆறும்","செய்திடும் மற்றவை ஈர்இரண்டில்திறம்","செய்திடும் ஆறுஆறு சேர்தத் துவங்களே."],"p":"","r":"The distinctions of bindu give twenty-five, those of nada six, and the rest come in twos and twos; together they make the thirty-six tattvas.","n":"ஐ ஐந்தும் (five fives), ஆறும் (six) and ஈர்இரண்டில் (two twos) do not simply add to ஆறுஆறு (six sixes, 36) as read here; the grouping may be different, or a number may have dropped. The standard Siddhanta count is 36 tattvas.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","vindhu","naadham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:140"},"t:1925":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1925,"t":["வந்திடு பேத மெலாம்பர விந்துமேல்","தந்திடு மாமாயை வாகேசி தற்பரை","உந்து குடிலையோடு ஏமுறு குண்டலி","விந்துவில் இந்நான்கும் மேவா விளங்குமே."],"p":"","r":"All the distinctions that arise rest above the supreme bindu; Mahamaya, the goddess of speech, the supreme power and kundalini with kutila, these four shine within bindu.","n":"மாமாயை, வாகேசி, தற்பரை and குண்டலி are traditionally taken as names of bindu or of powers in it. Whether குடிலை is a fifth name or one with குண்டலி is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vindhu","maayai","kundalini"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:140"},"t:1926":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1926,"t":["விளங்கு நிவர்த்தாதி மேலக ராதி","வளங்கொள் உகாரம் மகாரத் துள்விந்து","களங்கமில் நாதாந்தம் கண்ணினுள் நண்ணி","உளங்கொள் மனாதியுள் அந்தமும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"The kalas beginning with nivritti, the letters A and U and M with bindu within M, and the spotless end of nada reached in the eye extend through the mind and its faculties to their end.","n":"அகர, உகாரம், மகாரம் with விந்து and நாதாந்தம் are the parts of the syllable Om as analysed in mantra theory. கண்ணினுள் (in the eye) and மனாதியுள் அந்தமும் are obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vindhu","naadham","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:140"},"t:1927":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1927,"t":["அந்தமும் ஆதியும் ஆகிப் பராபரன்","வந்த வியாபி எனலாய அந்நெறி","கந்தம தாகிய காரண காரியம்","தந்துஐங் கருமமும் தான்செய்யும் வீயமே."],"p":"","r":"As end and beginning, the Supreme who pervades all is the seed, the root of cause and effect, and performs the five acts.","n":"வீயம் (seed, bija) and கந்தம் (root, bulb) can be read of the Lord or of bindu as his instrument; the verse runs them together. ஐங்கருமம் are traditionally creation, preservation, dissolution, concealment and grace.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vindhu","iraivan","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:140"},"t:1928":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1928,"t":["வீயம தாகிய விந்துவின் சத்தியால்","ஆய அகண்டமும் அண்டமும் பாரிப்பக்","காயஐம் பூதமும் காரிய மாயையில்","ஆயிட விந்து அகம்புறம் ஆகுமே."],"p":"","r":"By the power of bindu, which is the seed, the undivided and the cosmos spread out; when the five elements of the body arise in maya as effect, bindu is both inside and outside.","n":"அகண்டம் (the undivided) and காரிய மாயை (maya as effect) are technical. The verse links cosmic and bodily bindu, which the chapter later treats as one.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vindhu","maayai","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:140"},"t:1929":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1929,"t":["புறம்அகம் எங்கும் புகுந்துஒளிர் விந்து","நிறமது வெண்மை நிகழ்நாதம் செம்மை","உறமகிழ் சத்தி சிவபாதம் ஆயுள்","திறனொடு வீடுஅளிக் கும்செயல் கொண்டே."],"p":"","r":"Bindu enters and shines through everything within and without; its colour is white and nada's red, and joined with Shakti and Siva it has power over life and grants release.","n":"White bindu and red nada are traditionally matched with Siva and Shakti and, in the body, with semen and female blood. சிவபாதம் and ஆயுள் may also be taken as Siva's feet and length of life.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vindhu","naadham","uyir","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:140"},"t:1930":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1930,"t":["கொண்டஇவ் விந்து பரமம்போல் கோதற","நின்ற படம்கட மாய்நிலை நிற்றலின்","கண்டக லாதியின் காரண காரியத்து","அண்டம் அனைத்துமாய் மாமாயை ஆகுமே."],"p":"","r":"This bindu, flawless like the Supreme, stays in its effects as thread stays in cloth and clay in a pot, and as cause and effect of the kalas and the rest becomes all the worlds: it is Mahamaya.","n":"படம், கடம் (cloth, pot) are the classic examples of a material cause present in its effect. The verse makes bindu the material cause of the worlds, distinct from the Supreme it resembles.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vindhu","maayai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:140"},"t:1931":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1931,"t":["அதுவித்தி லேநின்று அங்கு அண்ணிக்கும் நந்தி","இதுவித்தி லேஉள வாற்றை உணரார்","மதுவித்தி லேமலர் அன்னம தாகிப்","பொதுவித்திலே நின்ற புண்ணியன் தானே."],"p":"","r":"Nandi stands in that seed and is sweet there, but people do not perceive how he is in this seed; the holy one stands in the seed common to all, as flower and swan in the seed of honey.","n":"The play on வித்து (seed) runs through every line. மதுவித்திலே மலர் அன்னம் அதாகி is obscure: மலர் and அன்னம் may be the lotus and swan of Brahma, or அன்னம் may mean food. The plain sense is that the Lord is in the seed of things unseen.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","vindhu","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:140"},"t:1932":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1932,"t":["வித்தினில் அன்றி முளையில்லை அம்முளை","வித்தினில் அன்றி வெளிப்படு மாறில்லை","வித்தும் முளையும் உடனன்றி வேறில்லை","அத்தன்மை யாரும் அரன்நெறி காணுமே."],"p":"","r":"There is no sprout without the seed, no way for the sprout to appear except from the seed, and seed and sprout exist only together; those who see this see Hara's way.","n":"The verse does not say what seed and sprout stand for. They are commonly taken as Siva and the world, or Siva and Shakti: cause and effect inseparable. Placed here, it may also bear on bodily bindu and the body.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","vindhu","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:140"},"t:1933":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1933,"t":["அருந்திய அன்னம் அவைமூன்று கூறாம்","பொருந்தும் உடல்மனம் போம்மலம் என்னத்","திருந்தும் உடன்மன மாம் கூறு சேர்ந்திட்டு","இருந்தன முன்னாள் இரதமது ஆகுமே."],"p":"","r":"Food eaten divides into three parts, the body's, the mind's and the waste that leaves; the body's and the mind's parts join, and what was there before becomes chyle.","n":"The threefold division of food into body, mind and waste is an old physiological teaching. இருந்தன முன்னாள் is unclear: perhaps what lay in the stomach the day before.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["undi","yaakkai","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:140"},"t:1934":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1934,"t":["இரதம் முதலான ஏழ்தாது மூன்றில்","உரிய தினத்தில் ஒருபுல் பனிபோல்","அரிய துளிவிந்து வாகும்ஏழ் மூன்றின்","மருவிய விந்து வளரும்கா யத்திலே."],"p":"","r":"Of the seven bodily elements beginning with chyle, each forming in three days, a rare drop of bindu arises like dew on a single blade of grass, and over seven threes of days the gathered bindu grows in the body.","n":"ஏழ் மூன்றின் is read as seven times three, twenty-one days. The dew on a grass blade marks smallness and rarity. Sengalvaraya notes a parallel in Tiruppugazh 862, where a drop like dew on a grass tip grows into a body.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vindhu","yaakkai","undi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:140"},"t:1935":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1935,"t":["காயத்தி லேமூன்று நாளில் கலந்திட்டுக்","காயத்துள் தன்மனம் ஆகும் கலாவிந்து","நேயத்தே நின்றோர்க்கு நீங்கா விடாமையின்","மாயத்தே செல்வோர் மனத்தோடு அழியுமே."],"p":"","r":"Within three days it mingles in the body and the kala-bindu becomes one's own mind; for those who hold steady it is not lost, but those who follow illusion perish with their mind.","n":"கலாவிந்து and நேயத்தே நின்றோர் (those who stand in love, or in what is fitting) are unclear. The claim that bindu becomes mind ties bodily continence to mental steadiness, as tradition reads it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vindhu","manam","maayai","adakkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:140"},"t:1936":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1936,"t":["அழிகின்ற விந்து அளவை அறியார்","கழிகின்ற தன்னையுட் காக்கலும் தேரார்","அழிகின்ற காயத்து அழிந்துஅயர் உற்றோர்","அழிகின்ற தன்மை அறிந்தொழி யாரே."],"p":"","r":"People do not know how much bindu is being lost, nor how to guard within themselves what is leaving; worn out in a perishing body, they perish, knowing the perishing yet not stopping it.","n":"அழி (perish) opens three lines and the last, so the verse sounds what it describes. அறிந்தொழியாரே can mean 'they do not know and desist' or 'they do not stop though they know'. Unlike the poets who answer the body's perishing by turning from it, Tirumular calls for guarding it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vindhu","yaakkai","nilaiyaamai","pochchaappu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:140"},"t:1937":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1937,"t":["பார்க்கின்ற மாதரைப் பாராது அகன்றுபோய்","ஓர்க்கின்ற உள்ளம் உருக அழல்மூட்டிப்","பார்க்கின்ற கண்ணாசை பாழ்பட மூலத்தே","சேர்க்கின்ற யோகி சிவயோகி தானே."],"p":"","r":"The yogi who leaves without looking at the women he sees, kindles a fire so the reflecting heart melts, lays waste the eye's desire and joins it at the root, is the Siva-yogi.","n":"பார்க்கின்ற opens lines 1 and 3: the women seen and the eye that sees. மூலத்தே சேர்க்கின்ற is taken as turning the force of desire back to the muladhara, the root centre, rather than suppressing it; the verse does not name what is joined.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","kaamam","adakkam","kundalini"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:141"},"t:1938":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1938,"t":["தானே அருளால் சிவயோகம் தங்காது","தானேஅக் காமாதி தங்குவோ னும் உட்கும்","தானே அதிகாரம் தங்கில் சடங்கெடும்","ஊனே அவற்றுள் உயிர்ஒம்பா மாயுமே."],"p":"","r":"Without grace Siva-yoga does not stay; one who harbours lust and its kin lives in fear; if the will to rule stays, the body is ruined, and in the flesh, if the life is not tended among these, it dies.","n":"தானே opens three lines. அதிகாரம் may be worldly power, the ego's claim to command, or in Siddhanta the stage of the soul's bondage; சடம் is the inert body. The verse puts grace before technique in a chapter mostly of technique.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","yogam","kaamam","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:141"},"t:1939":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1939,"t":["மாயாள் வசத்தே சென்றிவர் வேண்டில்","ஓயா இருபக்கத்து உள்வளர் பக்கத்துள்","ஏயாஎண் நாள்இன்ப மேல்பனி மூன்றிரண்டு","ஆயா அபரத்துள் ஆதிநாள் ஆறாமே."],"p":"","r":"If they wish to go into a woman's power, then of the two fortnights, in the waxing one, eight days are for pleasure; and in the waning one it is the sixth from the first day.","n":"மாயாள் may simply be 'the woman' or carry the sense of maya, the one who deludes. மேல்பனி மூன்றிரண்டு and the day counts are obscure; the verse fixes lunar days for union, as in the traditional rules for conception, but the exact days cannot be fixed from the text.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","kaalam","vindhu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:141"},"t:1940":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1940,"t":["ஆறுஐந்து பன்னொன்றும் அன்றிச் சகமார்க்கம்","வேறுஅன்பு வேண்டுவோர் பூவரில் பின்னம்தோடு","ஏறும் இருபத் தொருநாள் இடைத்தோங்கும்","ஆறின் மிகுந்தோங்கும் அக்காலம் செய்யவே."],"p":"","r":"Apart from the sixth, fifth and eleventh, those who want a love other than the common way should, after the woman's flowering, take the twenty-one days in which it rises, and do it at the time when the sixth rises highest.","n":"பூவரில் is taken as the woman's menses. சகமார்க்கம் (the common way) and the numbers are obscure; the verse continues 1939's calendar.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","kaalam","vindhu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:141"},"t:1941":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1941,"t":["செய்யும் அளவில் திருநான் முகூர்த்தமே","எய்யும் கலைகாலம் இந்து பருதிகால்","நையுமிடத்து ஓடி நன்கா நூல்நெறி","செய்க வலம் இடம் தீர்ந்து விடுக்கவே."],"p":"","r":"When it is done, the holy fourth muhurta, the time when the kala shoots, when the moon and sun breaths weaken, run there and act by the treatise's good way; leaving right and left, release.","n":"இந்து பருதிகால் are the moon and sun breaths, the left and right channels. வலம் இடம் தீர்ந்து (leaving right and left) is taken as the breath moving in the central channel. What is released (விடுக்க) is not said; the next verses suggest it is not bindu itself.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","praanan","kalai","naadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:141"},"t:1942":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1942,"t":["விடுங்காண் முனைந்துஇந் திரியங்க ளைப் போல்","நடுங்காது இருப்பானும் ஐஐந்தும் நண்ணப்","படுங்காதல் மாதின்பால் பற்றற விட்டுக்","கடுங்காற் கரணம் கருத்துறக் கொண்டே."],"p":"","r":"Release, and see: one who stays unshaken as the senses strain, as the five-times-five draw near, letting go all clinging to the woman of desire, holding the fierce breath and the faculties to the mind.","n":"ஐஐந்து may be the twenty-five tattvas or five times the five senses. கடுங்கால் (fierce wind) is taken as the breath held hard. The verse asks for detachment in the very act, not abstention from it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["praanan","pattru","adakkam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:141"},"t:1943":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1943,"t":["கொண்ட குணனே நலமேநற் கோமளம்","பண்டை உருவே பகர்வாய் பவளமே","மிண்டு தனமே மிடைய விடும் போதில்","கண்ட கரணம் உட் செல்லக்கண் டேவிடே."],"p":"","r":"With words like 'nature I have taken, goodness, tender one, form of old, coral-lipped, firm-breasted', when the moment of close release comes, see the faculty you have seen go within, then release.","n":"The first lines are a lover's endearments. கண்ட கரணம் உட் செல்ல (the faculty seen going within) is taken as the breath or the bindu drawn inward before release; the sense is that the yogi keeps the inner turning even at the climax.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","praanan","vindhu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:141"},"t:1944":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1944,"t":["விட்டபின் கர்ப்பஉற் பத்தி விதியிலே","தொட்டுறுங் காலங்கள் தோன்றக் கருதிய","கட்டிய வாழ்நாள் சாம்நாள் குணம் கீழ்மைசீர்ப்","பட்ட நெறியிதுஎன்று எண்ணியும் பார்க்கவே."],"p":"","r":"After release, in the rules of conception, consider the times that came into play and look to the life span bound, the day of death, low qualities and high, thinking 'this is the way it went'.","n":"The verse seems to say that the time of union fixes the child's length of life, death day and character, as in the astrological lore of conception. சீர்ப்பட்ட நெறி may be 'the proper way' or 'the way it turned out'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["karu","kaalam","vindhu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:141"},"t:1945":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1945,"t":["பார்த்திட்டு வையத்துப் பரப்பற்று உருப்பெற்று","வார்ச்செற்ற கொங்கை மடந்தையை நீக்கியே","சேர்த்துற்று இருதிங்கள் சேராது அகலினும்","மூப்புற்றே பின்னாளில் ஆம்எல்லாம் உள்ளவே."],"p":"","r":"Having looked to this, free of the world's spread and gaining form, keeping away from the woman, and even if one parts and keeps apart for two months after union, in later days of age all will remain.","n":"The verse is obscure. உருப்பெற்று may mean gaining a firm body. One reading: restraint after union keeps strength into old age. Another: the effects of the union remain even if one later abstains.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","punarchi","vindhu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:141"},"t:1946":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1946,"t":["வித்திடு வோர்க்கு அன்றி மேலோர் விளைவில்லை","வித்திடு வோர்க்கு அன்றி மிக்கோர் அறிவில்லை","வித்தினில் வித்தை விதற உணர்வரேல்","மத்தில் இருந்ததோர் மாங்கனி யாமே."],"p":"","r":"There is no harvest for the high except for those who sow, no knowledge for the eminent except for those who sow; if they come to know the seed within the seed, it is a mango in the churning-stick.","n":"வித்து (seed) is bindu, so 'those who sow' may be those who give bindu its proper end, or those who plant it upward in yoga. வித்தினில் வித்து (the seed in the seed) is the Lord, as in 1931. மத்தில் இருந்ததோர் மாங்கனி is obscure; it may mean something sweet hidden in an unlikely place.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vindhu","arivu","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:141"},"t:1947":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1947,"t":["கருத்தினில் அக்கரம் ஆயுவும் யாவும்","கருத்துளன் ஈசன் கருஉயிரோடும்","கருத்தது வித்தாய்க் காரண காரியம்","கருத்தறு மாறுஇவை கற்பனை தானே."],"p":"","r":"In thought are letters, life span and everything; the Lord is in thought with the womb and the life; thought becomes seed, cause and effect; and when thought is cut off, all these are imagination.","n":"கருத்து (thought, intention) puns on கரு (womb, embryo) in every line. The last line makes the world of seed and birth a construct of thought, which ends when thought ends; this is a reading, not the only one.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["manam","iraivan","karu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:141"},"t:1948":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1948,"t":["ஒழியாத விந்து வுடன்நிற்க நிற்கும்","அழியாப் பிராணன் அதிபலஞ் சத்தி","ஒழியாத புத்தி தபஞ்செப மோனம்","அழியாத சித்தியுண் டாம்விந்து வற்றிலே."],"p":"","r":"When bindu stays unspent, there stay the undying breath, great strength, power, unfailing intellect, tapas, japa and silence; and when bindu is dried up within, undying siddhi comes.","n":"ஒழியாத and அழியா alternate down the lines. விந்து வற்றிலே may mean 'when bindu is dried up', burnt within as 1949 describes, not 'when it runs dry' by loss; the first reading fits the chapter.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vindhu","praanan","siththi","thavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:141"},"t:1949":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1949,"t":["வற்ற அனலைக் கொளுவி மறித்தேற்றித்","துற்ற சுழியனல் சொருகிக் சுடருற்று","முற்று மதியத்து அமுதை முறைமுறை","செற்றுண் பவரே சிவயோகி யாரே."],"p":"","r":"To dry it, kindling the fire, turning it back and raising it, plugging the whirling fire and reaching the light, those who drink, step by step, the nectar of the full moon are the Siva-yogis.","n":"The fire at the root is raised (மறித்தேற்றி), the whirl (சுழி) is sealed, and the moon at the head yields nectar: the standard sequence of Tirumular's kundalini yoga as tradition reads it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","amudham","vindhu","kundalini"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:141"},"t:1950":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1950,"t":["யோகியும் ஞானியும் உத்தம சித்தனும்","யோகியும் ஞான புரந்தரன் ஆவோனும்","மோகம் உறினும் முறைஅமிர்து உண்போனும்","ஆகிய விந்து அழியாத அண்ணலே."],"p":"","r":"Yogi, jnani, excellent siddha, the yogi who is lord of jnana, and one who, though passion comes, drinks the nectar in due order: such is the noble one whose bindu does not perish.","n":"மோகம் உறினும் (even if passion arises) includes the householder who unites but keeps bindu by method, alongside the yogi and the jnani.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vindhu","yogam","amudham","siththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:141"},"t:1951":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1951,"t":["அண்ணல் உடலாகி அவ்வனல் விந்துவும்","மண்ணிடை மாய்க்கும் பிராணனாம் விந்துவும்","கண்ணும் கனலிடைக் கட்டிக் கலந்தெரித்து","உண்ணில் அமிர்தாகி யோகிக்கு அறிவாமே."],"p":"","r":"With the Lord as the body, the fire-bindu and the breath-bindu that dies in the earth, bound and mixed in the fire with the eye and burnt, become nectar when eaten and knowledge to the yogi.","n":"The two bindus are named again in 1965 and 1966 as food and breath. மண்ணிடை மாய்க்கும் may mean the breath that dies in the earth element at the root, or the bindu lost on the ground; கண்ணும் is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vindhu","praanan","amudham","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:141"},"t:1952":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1952,"t":["அறியாது அழிகின்ற ஆதலால் நாளும்","பொறியால் அழிந்து புலம்புகின் றார்கள்","அறிவாய் நனவில் அதீதம் புரியச்","செறிவாய் இருந்து சேரவே வாயுமே."],"p":"","r":"Because they perish without knowing, daily they are ruined through the senses and lament; as knowledge, staying gathered in waking so as to reach the beyond, union will come.","n":"நனவில் அதீதம் (the beyond-state in waking) is the Siddhanta turiyatita reached while awake. வாயுமே may be the breath or the verb 'it will be attained'; both are possible.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","adakkam","praanan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:141"},"t:1953":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1953,"t":["மாதரை மாய வரும் கூற்றம் என்றுன்னக்","காதலது ஆகிய காமம் கழிந்திடும்","சாதலும் இல்லை சதகோடி ஆண்டினும்","சோதியின் உள்ளே துரிசறும் காலமே."],"p":"","r":"If one thinks of women as Death come to kill, the desire that grew from love falls away; there is no dying even in a hundred crore years, and within the light comes the time free of fault.","n":"The verse prescribes a way of seeing, not a judgment on women as such: the meditation turns desire off. Sengalvaraya notes a Tiruppugazh parallel where young women's charms take lives as Death does.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kootru","adakkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:141"},"t:1954":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1954,"t":["காலம் கடந்தவன் காண்விந்து செற்றவன்","காலம் கடந்தழிந் தான்விந்து செற்றவன்","காலங் களின்விந்து செற்றுற்ற காரிகை","காலின்கண் வந்த கலப்பறி யாரே."],"p":"","r":"The one who has mastered bindu, see, has passed beyond time; the one who wasted bindu passed through time and perished; those who waste bindu through the ages do not know the mingling that came by the breath.","n":"செற்றவன் means both 'one who subdued' and 'one who destroyed'; the verse uses the same word for the yogi and the waster, and the reading depends on the contrast. காரிகை (woman) and காலின்கண் (by the breath, or at the feet) are unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vindhu","kaalam","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:141"},"t:1955":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1955,"t":["கலக்கு நாள் முன்னாள் தன்னிடைக் காதல்","நலத்தக வேண்டில் அந் நாரி யுதரக்","கலத்தின் மலத்தைத்தண் சீதத்தைப் பித்தை","விலக்கு வனசெய்து மேலணை வீரே."],"p":"","r":"If you want love's good on the day before union, first clear the woman's belly of waste, cold and bile by fitting remedies, and then embrace.","n":"A medical prescription in the humoral terms of Siddha medicine: சீதம் (phlegm, cold) and பித்தம் (bile). Tirumular addresses the man and treats the woman's health as part of the rite.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","yaakkai","marundhu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:141"},"t:1956":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1956,"t":["மேலா நிலத்தெழு விந்துவும் நாதமும்","கோலால் நடத்திக் குறிவழி யேசென்று","பாலாம் அமிர்துண்டு பற்றறப் பற்றினால்","மாலா னதுமான மாளும் அவ்விந்துவே."],"p":"","r":"Driving bindu and nada, rising to the upper place, with the rod along the marked path, drinking the nectar that is milk, if you hold on without clinging, the pride of delusion dies, and that bindu too.","n":"கோல் (rod) is taken as the spine or the central channel. பற்றறப் பற்றினால் (holding without holding) is a paradox. The last line can also mean that bindu dies to its ordinary course.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vindhu","naadham","amudham","pattru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:141"},"t:1957":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1957,"t":["விந்து விளைவும் விளைவின் பயன்முற்றும்","அந்த அழிவும் அடக்கத்தில் ஆக்கமும்","நந்திய நாசமும் நாதத்தால் பேதமும்","தந்துணர் வோர்க்குச் சயமாகும் விந்துவே."],"p":"","r":"Bindu's growth, the full fruit of that growth, its end and loss, the increase that comes by restraint, its gladdening loss and its division by nada: to those who grasp these, bindu becomes victory.","n":"The verse gives the chapter's title term சயம் (victory) its sense: bindu is conquered by knowing its whole course, not only by holding it. நந்திய நாசம் (a gladdening loss) is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vindhu","adakkam","naadham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:141"},"t:1958":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1958,"t":["விந்துஎன் வீசத்தை மேவிய மூலத்து","நந்திய அங்கிய னாலே நயந்தெரிந்து","அந்தமில் பானுஅதிகண்ட மேலேற்றிச்","சந்திரன் சார்புறத் தண்ணமு தாமே."],"p":"","r":"Bindu, the seed in the root centre, knowing its way by the fire there, raising the endless sun above the throat, becomes cool nectar when it reaches the moon.","n":"வீசம் is bija, seed. பானு (sun) and சந்திரன் (moon) are the hot and cool poles of the yogic body, and the cool nectar is the moon's. அதிகண்டம் is taken as above the throat.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vindhu","amudham","kundalini","aadhiththan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:141"},"t:1959":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1959,"t":["அமுதச் சசிவிந்து வாம்விந்து மாள","அமுதப் புனலோடி அங்கியின் மான","அமுதச் சிவயோகம் ஆதலால் சித்தி","அமுதப் பலாவனம் ஆங்குறும் யோகிக்கே."],"p":"","r":"When bindu, the moon of nectar, is consumed, the stream of nectar runs like fire; since this is the Siva-yoga of nectar, siddhi follows, and the jackfruit grove of nectar comes to the yogi.","n":"அமுதம் opens every line. அங்கியின் மான (like fire, or the fire's measure) is unclear. பலாவனம் (jackfruit grove) is an image of abundant sweetness.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["amudham","vindhu","siththi","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:141"},"t:1960":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1960,"t":["யோகம் அவ் விந்து ஒழியா வகையுணர்ந்து","ஆகம்இரண்டும் கலந்தாலும் ஆங்குறாப்","போகம் சிவபோகம் போகிநற் போகமா","மோகங் கெடமுயங் கார்மூடர் மாதர்க்கே."],"p":"","r":"Knowing the yoga by which bindu does not leave, even when two bodies mingle, the pleasure that does not go there is Siva's pleasure, the true pleasure of the enjoyer; fools embrace women but not so that infatuation dies.","n":"சிவபோகம் (Siva's enjoyment) is the verse's claim: union in which bindu is kept is itself a spiritual bliss. The last line is compressed; it may also be read 'fools do not embrace with infatuation ended'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","vindhu","yogam","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:141"},"t:1961":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1961,"t":["மாதர் இடத்தே செலுத்தினும் அவ்விந்து","காதலி னால்விடார் யோகம் கலந்தவர்","மாதர் உயிராசை கைக்கொண்ட வாடுவர்","காதலர் போன்றங்ஙன் காதலாம் சாற்றிலே."],"p":"","r":"Those joined in yoga, though they turn to women, do not release bindu out of love; those who take hold of women's craving for life waste away, though they seem lovers there, their love a matter of juice.","n":"உயிராசை (craving of life) may be the woman's desire or the man's lust for her. சாற்றிலே (in the juice) is unclear; it may be the bodily fluids, or a pun on சாற்று (declare).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vindhu","yogam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:141"},"t:1962":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1962,"t":["சாற்றிய விந்து சயமாகும் சத்தியால்","ஏற்றிய மூலத் தழலை எழமூட்டி","நாற்றிசை ஓடா நடுநாடி நாதத்தோடு","ஆற்றி அமுதம்அருந்தவித் தாமே."],"p":"","r":"The bindu spoken of becomes victory by Shakti: kindling the fire raised at the root, with nada through the middle channel that does not run in four directions, holding it, drinking the nectar, one becomes the seed.","n":"நடுநாடி is the central channel. The last word may be read அருந்த வித்தாம் (drinking it becomes seed) or அருந்தவம் (rare tapas); both fit.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vindhu","kundalini","naadi","amudham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:141"},"t:1963":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1963,"t":["விந்துவும் நாதமும் மேலக் கனல்மூல","வந்த அனல் மயிர்க் கால்தோறும் மன்னிடச்","சிந்தனை மாறச் சிவம்அக மாகவே","விந்துவும் மாளும்மெய்க் காயத்தில் வித்திலே."],"p":"","r":"Bindu and nada above, the fire at the root: when the fire that came settles in every hair-root, thought changes and Sivam becomes the self, and bindu dies into seed in the true body.","n":"மயிர்க்கால் தோறும் (in every hair-root) pictures the fire pervading the whole body. சிவம் அகமாக may be 'Sivam as the self' or 'Sivam within'. மெய்க்காயம் (true body) is taken as the body made imperishable.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vindhu","naadham","sivam","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:141"},"t:1964":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1964,"t":["வித்துக்குற் றுண்பான் விளைவுஅறி யாதவன்","வித்துக்குற் றுண்ணாமல் வித்துச் சுட்டு உண்பான்","வித்துகுற் றுண்பானில் வேறலன் ஈற்றவன்","வித்துக்குற் றுண்ணாமல் வித்துவித்தான் அன்றே."],"p":"","r":"One who pounds the seed and eats it does not know the harvest; one who, without pounding it, roasts the seed and eats is no different from him in the end; one who, not pounding and eating, sowed the seed is the other.","n":"A riddle on வித்து. Plainly: to eat the seed is to lose the crop. Symbolically, spending bindu in pleasure or burning it without method both waste it, while the sower turns it to growth. Which practice each figure stands for cannot be fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vindhu","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:141"},"t:1965":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1965,"t":["அன்னத்தில் விந்து அடங்கும் படிகண்டு","மன்னப் பிராணனாம் விந்து மறித்திட்டு","மின்னொத்த விந்துநா தாந்தத்து விட்டிட","வன்னத் திருவிந்து மாயும் கா யத்திலே."],"p":"","r":"Seeing how bindu is contained in food, turning back the bindu that is the abiding breath, releasing the lightning-like bindu into the end of nada, the coloured holy bindu dies in the body.","n":"அன்னம் may mean food or the swan (the breath, hamsa); with பிராணன் it gives the two bindus of 1966. நாதாந்தம் is the end of nada. 'Dies in the body' is taken as being absorbed, not lost.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vindhu","praanan","undi","naadham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:141"},"t:1966":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1966,"t":["அன்னம் பிராணன்என் றார்க்கும் இருவிந்து","தன்னை அறிந்துண்டு சாதிக்க வல்லார்க்குச்","சொன்ன மாம்உருத் தோன்றும்எண் சித்தியாம்","அன்னவர் எல்லாம் அழிவற நின்றதே."],"p":"","r":"For those who know the two bindus called food and breath, take them in and can practise, a golden form appears with the eight siddhis, and all such stand beyond destruction.","n":"சொன்னமாம் உரு (a golden form) is the perfected body of the siddhas. எண்சித்தி are the eight great powers. As in 1965, அன்னம் may be food or the hamsa breath.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vindhu","praanan","siththi","undi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:141"},"t:1967":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1967,"t":["நின்ற சிகாரம் நினைக்கும் பிராணனாய்","ஒன்றும் மகாரம் ஒருமூன்றோடு ஒன்றவை","சென்று பராசக்தி விந்து சயந்தன்னை","ஒன்ற உரைக்க உபதேசம் தானே."],"p":"","r":"The letter Si standing as the breath one thinks on, the letter Ma joining, these one with the three, go to Parashakti; to tell how to join with bindu's victory is the teaching.","n":"சிகாரம் and மகாரம் are letters of the five-syllable mantra. ஒருமூன்றோடு ஒன்ற (one with the three) may be the three remaining letters or the three bodies. உபதேசம் is the guru's teaching.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panchaakkaram","mandhiram","vindhu","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:141"},"t:1968":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1968,"t":["தானே உபதேசம் தானல்லாது ஒன்றில்லை","வானே உயர்விந்து வந்த பதினான்கு","மானேர் அடங்க அதன்பின்பு புத்தியும்","தானே சிவகதி தன்மையும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"It is itself the teaching, and there is nothing apart from it; when the fourteen that came from bindu, high as the sky, subside like deer, then intellect and the nature of the Siva-state come.","n":"பதினான்கு (fourteen) may be the fourteen worlds or the fourteen instruments (ten senses and four inner faculties). மானேர் (like deer) is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","vindhu","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:141"},"t:1969":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1969,"t":["விந்துவும் நாதமும் விளைய விளைந்தது","வந்தஇப் பல்லுயிர் மன்னுயி ருக்கெலாம்","அந்தமும் ஆதியும் ஆம்மந் திரங்களும்","விந்து அடங்க விளையும் சிவோகமே."],"p":"","r":"As bindu and nada bore fruit, these many lives came forth; for all abiding lives the end and the beginning, and the mantras too, are that; when bindu is contained, Sivoham grows.","n":"சிவோகம் (I am Siva) is the experience of identity with Siva. The verse joins the cosmic bindu that brings forth lives (1923) to the bodily bindu whose containment brings Sivoham.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vindhu","naadham","mandhiram","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:141"},"t:1970":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1970,"t":["வறுக்கின்ற வாறும் மனத்துலா வெற்றி","நிறுக்கின்ற வாறும் அந் நீள்வரை ஒட்டிப்","பொறிக்கின்ற வாறும்அப் பொல்லா வினையை","அறுக்கின்ற வாறும்அப் பொல்லா வினையை"],"p":"","r":"The way of roasting, the way of weighing the mind's victory, the way of sticking to that long mountain and sealing it, and the way of cutting that evil karma.","n":"Line 4 repeats the end of line 3 and the verse has no main verb, so the conclusion is lost. வறுக்கின்ற (roasting) echoes 1964's roasted seed; நீள்வரை (the long mountain) may be the spine.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","manam","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:141"},"t:1971":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1971,"t":["விந்துவும் நாதமும் மேவியுடன் கூடிப்","சந்திர னோடே தலைப்படு மாயிடில்","சுந்தர வானத்து அமுதம்வந்து ஊறிடும்","அங்குஉதி மந்திரம் ஆகுதி யாகுமே."],"p":"","r":"If bindu and nada join together and meet the moon, the nectar of the beautiful sky will spring there, and the mantra uttered there becomes the oblation.","n":"சுந்தர வானம் (the beautiful sky) is taken as the space in the head. The last line turns the inner act into the fire rite: mantra and oblation happen within.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vindhu","naadham","amudham","aaguthi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:141"},"t:1972":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1972,"t":["மனத்தொடு சத்து மனஞ்செவி யென்ன","இனத்தெழு வார்கள் இசைந்தன நாடி","மனத்தில் எழுகின்ற வாக்கு வசனம்","கனத்த இரதம் அக் காமத்தை நாடிலே."],"p":"","r":"Mind with its essence, mind and ear: those of one kind rise and agree in the channel; the word rising in the mind is speech, and if one seeks that desire, it is heavy juice.","n":"The verse is obscure. It may trace sound from mind to speech, and then say that desire turns this power into gross fluid (இரதம்).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["manam","naadi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:141"},"t:1973":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1973,"t":["சத்தமும் சத்த மனமும் மனக்கருத்து","ஒத்துஅறி கின்ற இடமும் அறிகிலர்","மெய்த்து அறிகின்ற இடம்அறி வாளர்க்கு","அத்தன் இருப்பிடம் அவ்விடம் தானே."],"p":"","r":"They do not know the place where sound, the mind of sound and the mind's thought agree and know; for those who know truly the place where knowing happens, that very place is the Father's abode.","n":"அத்தன் (Father) is the Lord. The verse places the Lord at the point where knowing itself takes place, not in an outer object of knowledge.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["meyyunarvu","manam","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:141"},"t:1974":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1974,"t":["உரம்அடி மேதினி உந்தியில் அப்பாம்","விரவிய தன்முலை மேவிய கீழ்அங்கி","கருமலை மீமிசை கைக்கீழிற் காலாம்","விரவிய சுந்தரம் மேல்வெளி யாமே."],"p":"","r":"From chest to feet is earth, at the navel water, below the breast fire, below the hands above the dark mountain air, and the beauty mingled above is space.","n":"The five elements are placed in regions of the body. The boundaries (உரம் அடி, கருமலை, கைக்கீழ்) are not clear, and the order of regions in the verse may be disturbed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:141"},"t:1975":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1975,"t":["செஞ்சுட ரோன்முத லாகிய தேவர்கள்","மஞ்சுடை மேரு வலம்வரு காரணம்","எஞ்சுடர் ஈசன் இறைவன் இணையடி","தஞ்சுட ராக வணங்கும் தவமே."],"p":"","r":"The reason the gods, the red-rayed sun first among them, circle cloud-topped Meru is their tapas of bowing to the twin feet of the Lord, light of all lights, as their own light.","n":"The sun's daily round of Meru, an astronomical fact in Puranic cosmology, is read as circumambulation in worship. எஞ்சுடர் may be 'all lights' or 'the light that remains'; either way the sun's light is borrowed from the Lord's.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aadhiththan","thiruvadi","vazhipaadu","thavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:142"},"t:1976":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1976,"t":["பகலவன் மாலவன் பல்லுயிர்க்கு எல்லாம்","புகலவ னாய்நின்ற புண்ணிய நாதன்","இகலற ஏழுல கும்உற வோங்கும்","பகலவன் பல்லுயிர்க்கு ஆதியும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"The sun, the holy Lord who stands as refuge for all lives, rising so that the seven worlds flourish without strife, is the origin of all lives.","n":"பகலவன் மாலவன் may name the sun and Vishnu side by side, or call the sun 'the great one'. The verse does not say plainly whether the புண்ணிய நாதன் is the sun or the Lord who stands as the sun; the chapter's drift favours the second.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aadhiththan","iraivan","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:142"},"t:1977":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1977,"t":["ஆதித்தன் அன்பினோடு ஆயிர நாமமும்","சோதியின் உள்ளே சுடரொளி யாய்நிற்கும்","வேதியர் வேண்டினும் விண்ணவர் சொல்லினும்","ஆதியில் அன்பு பழுக்கின்ற வாறே."],"p":"","r":"The sun, with love and a thousand names, stands as the radiant light within the light; whatever the Vedic priests ask and the gods say, this is how love ripens toward the First One.","n":"ஆயிர நாமமும் (a thousand names) recalls the hymns of a thousand names. The last two lines can be read as 'whatever they ask, it is love that ripens', setting love above ritual request, but the syntax is loose.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aadhiththan","anbu","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:142"},"t:1978":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1978,"t":["தானே உலகுக்குத் தத்துவனாய் நிற்கும்","தானே உலகுக்குத் தையலு மாய்நிற்கும்","தானே உலகுக்குச் சம்புவு மாய்நிற்கும்","தானே உலகுக்குத் தண்சுட ராகுமே."],"p":"","r":"He himself stands to the world as its principle, as the woman, as Shambhu, and as the cool light.","n":"தானே opens every line. தையல் (the woman) is Shakti and சம்பு is Siva, so the one who stands as the sun holds both. தண்சுடர் (cool light) is the moon, a surprise in a sun chapter: the Lord is both lights.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","saththi","thaththuvam","aadhiththan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:142"},"t:1979":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1979,"t":["வவையமுக் கோணம் வட்டம் அறுகோணம்","துலையிரு வட்டம் துய்ய விதம்எட்டில்","அலையுற்ற வட்டத்தில் ஈர்எட்டு இதழாம்","மலைவுற்று உதித்தனன் ஆதித்தன் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"Triangle, circle, hexagon, two balanced circles, the pure eight, and in the outer circle sixteen petals: the sun rose in this distinct order.","n":"The verse describes a diagram (yantra) for the sun, or the centres of the body in yogic reading. வவைய is probably a misprint, perhaps for வளைய; துலை and மலைவுற்று are unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["chakkaram","aadhiththan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:142"},"t:1980":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1980,"t":["ஆதித்தன் உள்ளி லானமுக் கோணத்தில்","சோதித்து இலங்கும்நற் சூரியன் நாலாம்","கேத முறுங்கேணி சூரியன் எட்டில்","சோதிதன் நீட்டில் சோடசம் தானே."],"p":"","r":"Within the sun, in the triangle, the good sun shines as four; the sun is in the eight; and in the light's spreading it is sixteen.","n":"The numbers continue 1979's diagram and are often taken as the sun's kalas. கேத முறுங்கேணி is obscure: கேணி (well) and கேதம் (grief) give no clear sense here.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aadhiththan","chakkaram","kalai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:142"},"t:1981":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1981,"t":["ஆதித்த னோடே அவனி இருண்டது","பேதித்த நாலும் பிதற்றிக் கழிந்தது","சோதிக்குள் நின்று துடியிடை செய்கின்ற","வேதப் பொருளை விளங்குகி லீரே."],"p":"","r":"With the sun the earth went dark; the four that were divided babbled and passed away; you do not see the Vedic truth that stands within the light and beats like a drum.","n":"பேதித்த நாலும் may be the four Vedas, the four religions or the four states; துடியிடை (drum-waisted) is also an epithet of a woman. The plain sense is a reproach: the truth is in the light, and talk misses it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aadhiththan","vedham","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:142"},"t:1982":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1982,"t":["பாருக்குக் கீழே பகலோன் வரும்வழி","யாருக்கும் காணஒண் ணாத அரும்பொருள்","நீருக்கும் தீக்கும் நடுவே உதிப்பவன்","ஆருக்கும் எட்டாத ஆதித்தன் தானே."],"p":"","r":"The way the sun comes beneath the earth is a rare thing no one can see; the one who rises between water and fire is the sun beyond everyone's reach.","n":"Plainly the sun's passage under the earth at night. Symbolically the earth, water and fire are taken as centres of the body, so the hidden sun is the inner light rising in yoga. Both readings stand.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aadhiththan","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:142"},"t:1983":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1983,"t":["மண்ணை இடந்துஅதின் கீழொடும்","விண்ணை இடந்து வெளிசெய்து நின்றிடும்","கண்ணை இடந்து களிதந்த ஆனந்தம்","எண்ணும் கிழமைக்கு இசைந்து நின்றானே."],"p":"","r":"Cleaving the earth and what is below it, cleaving the sky and making space, cleaving the eye and giving bliss, he stood suited to the reckoned day.","n":"இடந்து (cleaving, digging through) repeats. கிழமை may be the day of the week, named after the sun, or 'what is rightfully his'. Line 1 is a foot short.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aadhiththan","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:142"},"t:1984":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1984,"t":["பாரை இடந்து பகலோன் வரும்வழி","யாரும் அறியார் அருங்கடை நூலவர்","தீரன் இருந்த திருமலை சூழ்என்பர்","ஊரை உணர்ந்தார் உணர்ந்திருந் தாரே."],"p":"","r":"No one knows the way the sun comes cleaving the earth; those of the rare final texts say it circles the holy mountain where the brave one sits; those who knew the town knew and remained.","n":"அருங்கடை நூலவர் (those of the rare final books) may be Vedantins or Agama teachers. The mountain is Meru, or in yogic reading the head. ஊர் (the town) may be the Lord's abode or the body.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aadhiththan","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:142"},"t:1985":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1985,"t":["நின்றும் இருந்தும் கிடந்தும் நடந்தும்","கன்றாய நந்திக் கருத்துள் இருந்தனன்","கொன்று மலங்கன் குழல்வழி ஓடிட","வென்று விளங்கும் விரிசுடர் காணுமே."],"p":"","r":"Standing, sitting, lying and walking, he kept within the thought of Nandi; when the malams are destroyed and the current runs up the channel, the wide light is seen shining in victory.","n":"The four postures say the practice is constant, not set to a seat. கன்றாய is unclear (become a calf, or worn); மலங்கன் is likely மலங்கள். குழல்வழி (the tube's way) is traditionally the central channel, and the spreading light the inner sun the chapter is named for.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aadhiththan","malam","naadi","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:143"},"t:1986":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1986,"t":["ஆதித்தன் ஓடி அடங்கும் இடங்கண்டு","சாதிக்க வல்லவர் தம்மை யுணர்ந்தவர்","பேதித்து உலகம் பிதற்றும் பிதற்றெல்லாம் ஆதித்த","னோடே அடங்குகின் றாரே."],"p":"","r":"Those who have found the place where the sun runs and settles and can practise it know themselves; all the babble the divided world utters, they put to rest with that sun.","n":"ஆதித்தன் ஓடி அடங்கும் இடம் is taken as the point where the solar breath (the right channel) comes to rest. The last line can also mean that the babblers themselves subside with the setting sun, that is, die.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aadhiththan","yogam","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:143"},"t:1987":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1987,"t":["உருவிப் புறப்பட்டு உலகை வலம்வந்து","சொருகிக் கிடக்கும் துறையறி வார்இல்லை","சொருகிக் கிடக்கும் துறையறி வாளர்க்கு","உருகிக் கிடக்கும்என் உள்ளன்பு தானே."],"p":"","r":"No one knows the ford where it lies plugged after drawing out, going forth and circling the world; for those who know that ford, my inmost love lies melting.","n":"The subject is not named: in this chapter it is the inner sun, and many take it as the breath, which goes out, ranges and is then locked in (சொருகி). உருகிக் கிடக்கும் echoes சொருகிக் கிடக்கும்: the light lies locked, the poet's love lies melted, for those who know.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aadhiththan","anbu","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:143"},"t:1988":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1988,"t":["எறிகதிர் ஞாயிறு மின்பனி சோரும்","எறிகதிர் சோமன் எதிர்நின்று எறிப்ப","விரிகதிர் உள்ளே இயங்கும் என் ஆவி","ஒருகதிர் ஆகில் உலாஅது ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"As the glittering dew melts before the darting rays of the sun and the moon shines facing it, my life-breath, moving within the spreading rays, will stop wandering once it becomes a single ray.","n":"Sun and moon are traditionally the right and left breaths (pingala and ida), and the 'single ray' their union in the central channel. ஆவி may be breath or life; உலாஅது is 'will not roam'. A plain reading of dawn and moonlight also stands, but the last two lines push toward the yogic one.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["praanan","kalai","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:144"},"t:1989":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1989,"t":["சந்திரன் சூரியன் தான்வரின் பூசனை","முந்திய பானுவில் இந்துவந்து ஏய்முறை","அந்த இரண்டும் உபய நிலத்தில்","சிந்தை தெளிந்தார் சிவமாயி னரே."],"p":"","r":"Worship when moon and sun come together, in the way the moon comes to meet the sun that went before; those whose minds grow clear in the place of the two become Siva.","n":"பானு (sun) and இந்து (moon) may be the new-moon conjunction or, as tradition takes it, the two breaths meeting. உபய நிலம் ('the ground of both') is commonly placed between the brows or in the central channel; the verse does not say where.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalai","praanan","sivam","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:144"},"t:1990":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1990,"t":["ஆகும் கலையோடு அருக்கன் அனல்மதி","ஆகும் கலையிடை நான்குஎன லாம்என்பர்","ஆகும் அருக்கன் அனல்மதி யோடுஒன்ற","ஆகும்அப் பூரணை யாம்என்று அறியுமே."],"p":"","r":"Sun, fire and moon come with their kalas, and between the kalas, they say, there are four; know that when the sun joins fire and moon, that is the fullness.","n":"அருக்கன் அனல் மதி are the three lights, traditionally matched with three breaths or regions of the body. What the 'four' between the kalas are is not stated. பூரணை is the full moon and, figuratively, fullness or completion.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalai","praanan","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:144"},"t:1991":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1991,"t":["ஈர் அண்டத்து அப்பால் இயங்கிய அவ்வொளி","ஓர் அண்டத் தார்க்கும் உணரா உணர்வது","பேர்அண்டத்து ஊடே பிறங்கொளி யாய்நின்று","ஆர் அண்டத் தக்கார் அறியத்தக் காரே."],"p":"","r":"That light which moves beyond the two spheres is an awareness that those of a single sphere cannot perceive; it stands shining through the great sphere, and only those fit to approach it are fit to know it.","n":"அண்டம் is the cosmic egg or world-sphere; ஈர் அண்டம், ஓர் அண்டம் and பேர் அண்டம் are not explained and may be read cosmically or of regions in the body. The last line plays on அண்டு (approach) and அண்டம் (sphere).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","meyyunarvu","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:144"},"t:1992":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1992,"t":["ஒன்பதின் மேவி உலகம் வலம்வரும்","ஒன்பதும் ஈசன் இயல்அறி வார்இல்லை","முன்புஅதின் மேவி முதல்வன் அருளிலார்","இன்பம் இலார்இருள் சூழநின் றாரே."],"p":"","r":"Dwelling in the nine it circles the world, yet no one knows the nature of the Lord in the nine; those who dwelt in it before but lack the First One's grace are without bliss and stand surrounded by darkness.","n":"ஒன்பது is unexplained: the nine planets that circle the world fit the chapter's suns and moons, and the body's nine gates are another traditional reading. The contrast between dwelling in it and having grace is the clear point.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","sivam","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:144"},"t:1993":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1993,"t":["விந்து அபரம் பரம்இரண் டாய்விரிந்து","அந்த அபரம் பரநாத மாகியே","வந்தன தம்மில் பரங்கலை யாதிவைத்து","உந்தும் அருணோ தயமென்ன உள்ளத்தே."],"p":"","r":"Bindu spread into two, lower and higher; the lower became the higher nada, and with the higher kala and the rest set within them they rise in the heart like the dawn.","n":"அபரம், பரம் (lower, higher) and பரநாதம் are terms of Siddhanta cosmology. அருணோதயம் (sunrise) gives the chapter its sun: the unfolding of bindu is pictured as an inner dawn.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vindhu","naadham","kalai","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:145"},"t:1994":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1994,"t":["உள்ள அருணோ தயத்தெழும் ஓசைதான்","தெள்ளும் பரநாதத் தின்செயல் என்பதால்","வள்ளல் பரவிந்து வைகரி யாதிவாக்கு","உள்ளன ஐங்கலைக்கு ஒன்றாம் உதயமே."],"p":"","r":"The sound that rises at that inner dawn is the working of clear higher nada, so the bounteous higher bindu and the speech that begins with vaikhari are one rising for the five kalas.","n":"வைகரி is the spoken level of speech, traditionally the last of four (with madhyama, pashyanti and sukshma). ஐங்கலை are taken as the five kalas beginning with nivritti. The verse ties sound and speech back to one source.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naadham","vindhu","kalai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:145"},"t:1995":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1995,"t":["தேவர் பிரான்திசை பத்துஉத யஞ்செய்யும்","மூவர் பிரான்என முன்னொரு காலத்து","நால்வர் பிரான்நடு வாயுரை யாநிற்கும்","மேவு பிரான்என்பர் விண்ணவர் தாமே."],"p":"","r":"The celestials say he is the Lord of the gods who rises in the ten directions, who of old was called Lord of the three, the Lord of the four who goes on teaching from the centre, the Lord who abides.","n":"மூவர் are traditionally Brahma, Vishnu and Rudra, and நால்வர் the four sages taught by Siva as Dakshinamurti; நடுவாய் உரையாநிற்கும் may refer to that teaching. Neither identification is in the verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:145"},"t:1996":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1996,"t":["பொய்யிலன் மெய்யன் புவனா பதிஎந்தை","மையிருள் நீக்கும் மதிஅங்கி ஞாயிறு","செய்யிருள் நீக்கும் திருவுடை நந்திஎன்","கையிருள் நீங்கக் கலந்தெழுந் தானே."],"p":"","r":"My father, without falsehood and true, lord of the worlds: moon, fire and sun remove the black darkness, but Nandi, holy one who removes the darkness that deeds make, rose mingled in me so that the darkness close at hand left.","n":"The contrast is between outer lights and the inner one. செய்யிருள் (darkness of deeds, or dense darkness) and கையிருள் (darkness at hand, or within one's grasp) are both open; tradition takes the inner darkness as the soul's impurity.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","thiruvarul","malam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:145"},"t:1997":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1997,"t":["தனிச்சுடர் ஏற்றித் தயங்கிருள் நீங்க","அனித்திடும் மேலை அருங்கனி ஊறல்","கனிச்சுட ராய்நின்ற கயிலையில் ஈசன்","நனிச்சுடர் மேல்கொண்ட வண்ணமும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"Lighting the single flame so that the wavering darkness leaves, the juice of the rare fruit above comes near; and that is also the form of the great flame the Lord of Kailash, who stands as a ripened flame, took on above.","n":"மேலை அருங்கனி ஊறல் (the juice of the rare fruit above) is commonly read as the nectar said to flow from the head in yoga, and கயிலை as the crown of the head. அனித்திடும் is obscure. The verse is compressed and the reading is a guess at its order.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","amudham","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:145"},"t:1998":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1998,"t":["நேரறி வாக நிரம்பிய பேரொளி","போரறி யாது புவனங்கள் போய்வரும்","தேரறி யாத திசையொளி யாயிடும்","ஆரறி வாரிது நாயக மாமே."],"p":"","r":"The great light that fills as true knowledge goes to and returns from the worlds without strife, and becomes the light of a quarter no chariot knows; who knows this? It is the Lord.","n":"தேரறியாத may be 'that no chariot knows', the sun's chariot not reaching it, so a light beyond the sun; or 'that inquiry cannot know', from தேர் (to examine). Both readings stand.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","gnaanam","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:145"},"t:1999":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":1999,"t":["மண்டலத் துள்ளே மலர்ந்தெழும் ஆதித்தன்","கண்டிதத் துள்ளே கதிரொளி ஆயிடும்","சென்றிடத்து எட்டுத் திசையெங்கும் போய்வரும்","நின்றிடத் தேநிலை நேரறி வார்க்கே."],"p":"","r":"The sun that blossoms within the circle becomes the light of rays within the region; going out it travels to all eight directions, and for those who know rightly it stays in its place.","n":"மண்டலம் and கண்டிதம் are probably regions of the yogic body (கண்டம் may be the throat), but the verse does not fix them. The contrast is between the light going out everywhere and standing still for the one who knows.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","gnaanam","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:145"},"t:2000":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2000,"t":["நாபிக்கண் நாசிநயன நடுவினும்","தூபியோடு ஐந்தும் சுடர்விடு சோதியைத்","தேவர்கள் ஈசன் திருமால் பிரமனும்","மூவரு மாக உணர்ந்திருந் தாரே."],"p":"","r":"They perceived the flame that shines in five places, the navel, the nose, the eyes, their middle and the crown, as the three: the Lord of the gods, Tirumal and Brahma.","n":"The five places are centres of contemplation; நாசி நயன நடுவு may be one place, between nose and eyes, and தூபி the crown of the head. Who perceives (the gods, or the sages) is not clear in the verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiyaanam","yogam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:145"},"t:2001":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2001,"t":["அன்றிய பாச இருளும்அஞ் ஞானமும்","சென்றிடு ஞானச் சிவப்பர காசத்தால்","ஒன்றும் இருசட ராம்அரு ணோதயம்","துன்றிருள் நீங்குதல் போலத் தொலைந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"The darkness of bond and the ignorance that stood against me vanished through the supreme light of Siva which is knowledge, as dense darkness leaves at the dawn where two lights become one.","n":"ஒன்றும் இருசுடர் ('the two lights that join') may be sun and moon at dawn or, as in chapter 144, the two breaths united. The main claim, that knowledge as Siva's light ends bond and ignorance, is plain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paasam","gnaanam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:146"},"t:2002":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2002,"t":["கடம் கடம் தோறும் கதிரவன் தோன்றில்","அடங்கிட மூடில் அவற்றில் அடங்கான்","விடங்கொண்ட கண்டனும் மேவிய காயத்து","அடங்கிட நின்றதும் அப்பரி சாமே."],"p":"","r":"When the sun appears in pot after pot, closing them does not shut him in; the poison-throated Lord who stands as though confined in the body he dwells in is of the same kind.","n":"The sun reflected in water-filled pots is a classic image for one presence in many bodies. Here the point is that Siva is in each body without being held by it; விடங்கொண்ட கண்டன் is Siva who swallowed the poison.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:146"},"t:2003":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2003,"t":["தானே விரிசுடர் மூன்றும்ஒன்றாய் நிற்கும்","தானே அயன்மால் எனநின்று தாபிக்கும்","தானே உடலுயிர் வேறன்றி நின்றுளன்","தானே வெளியொளி தானிருட் டாமே."],"p":"","r":"He himself is the spreading light that stands as the three lights in one; he himself establishes things, standing as Brahma and Vishnu; he himself stands not other than body and soul; he himself is open space, light and darkness.","n":"The fourfold தானே insists on one agent behind all. உடலுயிர் வேறன்றி ('not other than body and soul') states Siva's presence in them; how far it means identity is left open.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","iraivan","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:146"},"t:2004":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2004,"t":["தெய்வச் சுடர்அங்கி ஞாயிறும் திங்களும்","வையம் புனல்அனல் மாருதம் வானகம்","சைவப் பெரும்பதி தாங்கிய பல்லுயிர்","ஐவர்க்கு இடம்இடை ஆறங்கம் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"The divine lights, fire, sun and moon, the earth, water, fire, wind and sky, and the many souls the great Lord of the Saivas bears: these are the six limbs and the place, in between, for the five.","n":"The first three lines name Siva's traditional eight forms (the five elements, sun, moon and the soul), though fire is named twice. The last line, ஐவர்க்கு இடம் இடை ஆறங்கம், is obscure: ஐவர் may be the five deities and ஆறங்கம் six limbs (of the Veda, or six paths).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","uyir","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:146"},"t:2005":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2005,"t":["உன்னும் அளவில் உணரும் ஒருவனைப்","பன்னு மறைகள் பயிலும் பரமனை","என்னுள் இருக்கும் இளையா விளக்கினை","அன்ன மயமென்று அறிந்துகொண் டேனே."],"p":"","r":"The One who is perceived as far as one contemplates him, the Supreme whom the recited Vedas study, the unfading lamp within me: I came to know him as of the nature of the swan.","n":"அன்ன மயம் may be 'of the swan' (hamsa, the soul and the breath in yogic usage) or 'made of food' (annamaya, the body's sheath). The next verse's swans favour the first; the second would mean he is known in the body.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pasu","sivam","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:147"},"t:2006":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2006,"t":["அன்னம் இரண்டுள ஆற்றம் கரையினில்","துன்னி இரண்டும் துணைப்பிரி யாதுஅன்னம்","தன்னிலை அன்னம் தனியொன்றது என்றக்கால்","பின்ன மடஅன்னம் பேறணு காதே."],"p":"","r":"There are two swans on the river bank; the two keep close and never leave each other; if a swan holds that its state is a single one apart, that deluded, separated swan will not reach the gain.","n":"The two swans are commonly taken as the soul and the Lord, like the two birds on one tree of the Upanishads; they may also be read as the two breaths. பின்ன மட அன்னம் is the swan that is foolishly divided off, or that thinks itself apart.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pasu","pathi","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:147"},"t:2007":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2007,"t":["வைகரி யாதியும் மாயா மலாதியும்","பொய்கரி யான புருடாதி பேதமும்","மெய்கரி ஞானம் கிரியா விசேடத்துச்","செய்கரி ஈசன் அனாதியே செய்ததே."],"p":"","r":"Speech beginning with vaikhari, the impurities beginning with maya, the distinctions beginning with purusha that bear false witness, and the particular workings of knowledge and action that bear true witness: the Lord who acts made all these from beginningless time.","n":"கரி (witness) closes three compounds, பொய்கரி, மெய்கரி, செய்கரி, a word game hard to fix. புருடாதி பேதம் are the tattvas from purusha down. The claim that the Lord made even the bonds 'without beginning' (அனாதியே) is central to Siddhanta, as tradition reads it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","maayai","malam","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:148"},"t:2008":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2008,"t":["அணுவில் அணுவினை ஆதிப் பிரானை","அணுவில் அணுவினை ஆயிரங் கூறிட்டு","அணுவில் அணுவை அணுகவல் லார்கட்கு","அணுவில் அணுவை அணுகலும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"The first Lord is the atom within the atom; for those able to approach the atom within the atom, by dividing that atom of atoms into a thousand parts, approaching the atom within the atom becomes possible.","n":"அணு is both 'atom' and, in Siddhanta, the soul (anu), so 'the atom within the atom' may be the Lord within the soul. The verse repeats அணுவில் அணுவினை in every line; the thousand parts mark extreme subtlety.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","pasu","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:148"},"t:2009":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2009,"t":["படர்கொண்ட ஆலதின் வித்தது போலச்","சுடர்கொண்டு அணுவினைத் தூவழி செய்ய","இடர்கொண்ட பாச இருளற ஒட்டி","நடர்கொண்ட நல்வழி நாடலும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"If one makes the soul pure by the light, as with the seed of the spreading banyan, driving out the painful darkness of bonds, one can seek the good path that leads on.","n":"The banyan seed is tiny but holds a great tree, a traditional image for the soul's hidden capacity. சுடர் (the light) is unnamed; it is commonly taken as grace or knowledge. நடர்கொண்ட is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pasu","paasam","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:148"},"t:2010":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2010,"t":["அணுவுள் அவனும் அவனுள் அணுவும்","கணுஅற நின்ற கலப்பது உணரார்","இணையிலி ஈசன் அவன்எங்கும் ஆகித்","தணிவற நின்றான் சராசரம் தானே."],"p":"","r":"People do not perceive the seamless mingling in which he is in the soul and the soul is in him; the peerless Lord, being everywhere, stood without lessening as all that moves and does not move.","n":"கணு அற (without joint or seam) stresses that the mingling has no join; the verse holds both indwelling (he in the soul) and containment (the soul in him).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","pasu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:148"},"t:2011":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2011,"t":["மேவிய சீவன் வடிவது சொல்லிடில்","கோவின் மயிர்ஒன்று நூறுடன் கூறிட்டு","மேவிய கூறது ஆயிரம் ஆயினால்","ஆவியின் கூறுநா றாயிரத்து ஒன்றே."],"p":"","r":"To tell the form of the soul: split a cow's hair into a hundred and each part into a thousand, and the soul's part is one in a hundred thousand.","n":"A similar measure of the soul by dividing a hair-tip is found in Upanishadic texts. The point is the soul's subtlety, not a literal size.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasu","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:149"},"t:2012":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2012,"t":["ஏனோர் பெருமையின் ஆயினும் எம்மிறை","ஊனே சிறுமையின் உட்கலந்து அங்குளன்","வானோர் அறியும் அளவல்லன் மாதேவன்","தானே அறியும் தவத்தின் அளவே."],"p":"","r":"Though our Lord is greater than all others, he is mingled within the flesh in smallness and is there; Mahadeva is not of a measure the gods can know; his measure is known by tapas.","n":"The last line can be 'he alone knows the measure of tapas' or 'his measure is known only by tapas'. The verse balances greatness and smallness, answering 2011's tiny soul with the Lord who dwells in it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","yaakkai","thavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:149"},"t:2013":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2013,"t":["உண்டு தெளிவன் உரைக்க வியோகமே","கொண்டு பயிலும் குணமில்லை யாயினும்","பண்டு பயிலும் பயில்சீவ னார்பின்னைக்","கண்டு சிவனுருக் கொள்வர் கருத்துளே."],"p":"","r":"He is, and he is clear; to speak of it, though souls lack the quality of practising separation, the souls who practised of old will later see and take Siva's form within their thought.","n":"வியோகம் (separation, or detachment) and the syntax of the first two lines are unclear; உண்டு தெளிவன் may also be 'having experienced, I am clear'. The last line, taking Siva's form in thought, is the firm part.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pasu","sivam","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:149"},"t:2014":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2014,"t":["மாயா உபாதி வசத்ததாகும் சேதனத்து","ஆய குருஅரு ளாலே அதில்தூண்ட","ஓயும் உபாதியோடு ஒன்றின் ஒன் றாது உயிர்","ஆய துரியம் புகுந்தறி வாகவே."],"p":"","r":"The conscious soul is under the sway of maya's adjuncts; when it is stirred there by the grace of the guru, the adjuncts cease, and the soul, not becoming one with them, enters turiya and becomes knowledge.","n":"உபாதி (limiting adjunct) and துரியம் (the fourth state, beyond waking, dream and deep sleep) are technical terms. ஒன்றின் ஒன்றாது may be 'not merging with what it joined'. The guru's grace as the trigger is plain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pasu","maayai","guru","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:149"},"t:2015":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2015,"t":["கற்ற பசுக்கள் கதறித் திரியினும்","கொற்ற பசுக்கள் குறிகட்டி மேயினும்","முற்ற பசுக்கள் ஒருகுடம் பால்போலும்","மற்றைப் பசுக்கள் வறள்பசு தானே."],"p":"","r":"Though the learned cows wander bellowing and the mighty cows graze tethered to a mark, the ripened cows are like a pot full of milk; the other cows are dry cows.","n":"பசு is both cow and the bound soul, so the verse sorts souls. கற்ற (learned) and கொற்ற (mighty, ruling) souls make noise or keep to marks, perhaps rites or sectarian signs; முற்ற (ripened) is traditionally read as souls whose impurity has ripened for grace. The categories are the reader's to fill.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pasu","kalvi","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:150"},"t:2016":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2016,"t":["கொல்லையின் மேயும் பசுக்களைச் செய்வதென்","எல்லைக் கடப்பித்து இறைவன் அடிகூட்டி","வல்லசெய்து ஆற்ற மதித்தபின் அல்லது","கொல்லை செய் நெஞ்சம் குறிப்பறி யாதே."],"p":"","r":"What can be done with cows that graze in the field? Unless they are led across the boundary, joined to the Lord's feet, made strong and come to value it, the heart that makes its field will not know the sign.","n":"Who leads the cows across is not named: the Lord, or the guru. கொல்லை (field, cleared land) is commonly taken as the field of the senses, and குறிப்பு as the hidden meaning or goal.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pasu","thiruvadi","manam","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:150"},"t:2017":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2017,"t":["சீவன் எனச்சிவன் என்னவே றில்லை","சீவ னார்சிவ னாரை அறிகிலர்","சீவ னார்சிவ னாரை அறிந்தபின்","சீவ னார்சிவ னாயிட்டு இருப்பரே."],"p":"","r":"There is no difference called soul and Siva; souls do not know Siva, and once souls know Siva, souls remain as Siva.","n":"One of the best-known verses of the Tirumanthiram. It can be read as identity (the soul is Siva, unaware of it) or, as Siddhanta reads it, as non-separation in which the freed soul becomes Siva-like without ceasing to be; the verse uses both வேறில்லை and ஆயிட்டு இருப்பர், which pull in each direction.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasu","sivam","meyyunarvu","saayujjiyam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:151"},"t:2018":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2018,"t":["குணவிளக் காகிய கூத்தப் பிரானும்","மனவிளக் காகிய மன்னுயிர்க் கெல்லாம்","பணவிளக் காகிய பல்தலை நாகம்","கணவிளக் காகிய கண்காணி யாகுமே."],"p":"","r":"The dancing Lord, the lamp of qualities, is the overseeing witness, a lamp in the eye, for all abiding souls whose lamp is the mind, as the many-headed serpent's lamp is the jewel in its hood.","n":"The verse stacks four lamps (குண, மன, பண, கண விளக்கு), and how they relate is not fixed. பணவிளக்கு is the jewel the serpent is said to carry in its hood. கண்காணி is an overseer or witness.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","thirukkoothu","uyir","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:151"},"t:2019":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2019,"t":["அறிவாய் அறியாமை நீங்கி யவனே","பொறிவாய் ஒழிந்தெங்கும் தானான போதன்","அறிவாய் அவற்றினுள் தானாய் அறிவன்","செறிவாகி நின்றஅச் சீவனும் ஆகுமே."],"p":"","r":"The knower who, being knowledge, is freed of ignorance, whose sense-doors are closed and who is himself everywhere, knows as knowledge within them all, and is also that soul which stood gathered in.","n":"போதன் may be the freed soul or the Lord, and the verse's subject shifts; பொறிவாய் ஒழிந்து (the doors of the senses ceasing) and செறிவாகி (concentrated, or densely present) carry the sense.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","pasu","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:151"},"t:2020":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2020,"t":["ஆறாறின் தன்மை அறியாது இருந்தேனுக்கு","ஆறாறின் தன்மை அறிவித்தான் பேர்நந்தி","ஆறாறின் தன்மை அருளால் அறிந்த பின்","ஆறாறுக்கு அப்புறம் ஆகி நின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"To me, who did not know the nature of the thirty-six, Nandi made known the nature of the thirty-six; after I knew it by his grace, he stood beyond the thirty-six.","n":"ஆறாறு (six sixes) is the thirty-six tattvas. நின்றானே is third person, so the one beyond them is Nandi; some take it of the knower, who passes beyond them with him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","guru","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:151"},"t:2021":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2021,"t":["சிவமா கியஅருள் நின்றுஅறிந்து ஓரார்","அவமாம் மலம்ஐந்தும் ஆவது அறியார்","தவமான செய்து தலைப்பறி கின்றார்","நவமான தத்துவம் நாடாகி லாரே."],"p":"","r":"Those who do not stand in and know the grace that is Siva, and do not know what the five ruinous impurities are, perform austerities and tear out their hair; they never seek the new truth.","n":"தலைப்பறிகின்றார் (they pluck the hair from their heads) is traditionally taken as aimed at Jain ascetics. மலம் ஐந்து is a fivefold count of impurities; நவமான தத்துவம் may be 'new', 'excellent' or 'ninefold' tattva.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["malam","thavam","thiruvarul","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:151"},"t:2022":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2022,"t":["நாடோ றும் ஈசன் நடத்தும் தொழில்உள்ளார்","நாடோ றும் ஈசன் நயந்தூட்டல் நாடிடார்","நாடோ றும் ஈசன்நல் லோர்க்கருள் நல்கலால்","நாடோ றும் நாடார்கள் நாள்வினை யாளரே."],"p":"","r":"Those who live daily in the work the Lord carries on do not daily seek the Lord's loving nourishment; since the Lord gives grace daily to the good, those who do not seek it daily are mere day-labourers.","n":"Every line begins நாடோறும் (daily), and நாடிடார், நாடார் play on நாடு (seek). நாள்வினையாளர் may be day-labourers or those bound to each day's deeds. Who 'lives in the Lord's work' is unclear: all beings, or ritual workers.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","vinai","aindhozhil"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:151"},"t:2023":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2023,"t":["ஆக மதத்தன ஐந்து களிறுள","ஆக மதத்தறி யோடுஅணை கின்றில","பாகனும் எய்த்துஅவை தாமும் இளைத்தபின்","யோகு திருந்துதல் ஒன்றிஅறி யோமே."],"p":"","r":"There are five elephants in rut within the body; they do not come to the post of the Agamas' teaching; once the mahout is spent and they too have weakened, we do not know how to set yoga right by uniting.","n":"ஆக மதத்தன puns on ஆகம் (body) and மதம் (rut), and ஆக மதத் தறி on ஆகமம் (scripture) and தறி (tethering post). The mahout is commonly taken as the mind or the self. The last line admits defeat rather than prescribing.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","yogam","aagamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:152"},"t:2024":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2024,"t":["கருத்தின்நன் னூல்கற்று கால்கொத்திப் பாகன்","திருத்தலும் பாய்மாத் திகைத்தன்றிப் பாயா","எருத்துற ஏறி இருக்கிலும் ஆங்கே","வருத்தினும் அம்மா வழிநட வாதே."],"p":"","r":"Though the mahout has learned good books in his mind and jabs with his foot to correct it, the leaping beast only leaps in confusion; even if he climbs onto its neck and sits there and goads it, that beast will not keep to the road.","n":"பாய்மா (the leaping beast) is usually a horse; எருத்து (neck) suits an elephant or bull, so the beast may continue 2023's elephants. The point is that book learning does not tame the senses.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","kalvi","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:152"},"t:2025":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2025,"t":["புலம் ஐந்து புள்ஐந்து புள்சென்று மேயும்","நிலம்ஐந்து நீர்ஐந்து நீர்மையும் ஐந்து","குலம் ஒன்று கோல்கொண்டு மேய்ப்பான் ஒருவன்","உலம்வந்து போம்வழி ஒன்பது தானே."],"p":"","r":"Five fields, five birds, and the birds go and feed there; five lands, five waters, five natures; one family, and one herdsman tends them with a staff; and the ways by which they come and go are nine.","n":"The fives are traditionally the senses, their objects and their qualities, and the nine ways the body's nine openings. The herdsman may be the soul or the Lord. The verse lays out the scheme without stating a moral.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:152"},"t:2026":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2026,"t":["அஞ்சுள சிங்கம் அடவியல் வாழ்வன","அஞ்சும்போய் மேய்ந்துதம் அஞ்சுஅக மேபுகும்","அஞ்சின் உகிரும் எயிரும் அறுத்திட்டால்","எஞ்சாது இறைவனை எய்தலும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"Five lions live in the forest; the five go out to feed and come back into their five dens; if the claws and teeth of the five are cut, reaching the Lord fully becomes possible.","n":"The lions are the senses and their dens the sense organs, as the image is usually read. Cutting claws and teeth disarms the senses without killing them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","sivam","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:152"},"t:2027":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2027,"t":["ஐவர் அமைச்சருள் தொண்ணூற்று அறுவர்கள்","ஐவரும் மைந்தரும் ஆளக் கருதுவர்","ஐவரும் ஐந்த சினத்தொட நின்றிடில்","ஐவர்க்கு சிறைஇறுத்து ஆற்றகி லோமே."],"p":"","r":"Under the five ministers are ninety-six; the five and their sons mean to rule; if the five stand with their anger, we cannot pay the tribute to the five.","n":"தொண்ணூற்று அறுவர் is traditionally the ninety-six tattvas of an extended count. சிறை இறுத்து may be 'paying the levy' or 'breaking the prison'; the second would reverse the sense of the last line.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:152"},"t:2028":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2028,"t":["சொல்லகில் லேன்சுடர்ச் சோதியை நாடொறும்","சொல்லகில் லேன்திரு மங்கையும் அங்குள","வெல்லகில் லேன்புலன் ஐந்துடன் தன்னையும்","கொல்லநின் றோடும் குதிரைஒத் தேனே."],"p":"","r":"Day after day I cannot speak of the radiant light, nor of the holy lady who is there too; I cannot conquer the five senses and myself; I have become like a horse that bolts when one stands to strike it.","n":"திருமங்கை (the holy lady) is commonly taken as Shakti, with the light as Siva. கொல்ல நின்று ஓடும் குதிரை may also be a horse that runs on to its death. A rare first-person confession of failure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","sivam","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:152"},"t:2029":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2029,"t":["எண்ணிலி இல்லி அடைத்துஅவ் இருட்டறை","எண்ணிலி இல்லியோடு ஏகில் பிழைதரும்","எண்ணிலி இல்லியோடு ஏகாமை காக்குமேல்","எண்ணிலி இல்லதோடு இன்பமது ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"Shut that pot of countless holes in the dark room; going along with it brings error; if one guards against going along with it, there is bliss.","n":"எண்ணிலி இல்லி is read here as a pot pierced with countless holes, an old image for the leaking body or mind; this is a guess. The verse repeats எண்ணிலி இல்லி in every line and the last line (இல்லதோடு) may mean 'with the one who has no holes'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","adakkam","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:152"},"t:2030":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2030,"t":["விதியின் பெருவலி வேலைசூழ் வையம்","துதியின் பெருவலி தொல்வான் உலகம்","மதியின் பெருவலி மானுடர் வாழ்க்கை","நிதியின் பெருவலி நீர்வலி தானே."],"p":"","r":"The great strength of fate is the sea-girt earth; the great strength of praise is the ancient heaven; the great strength of the mind is human life; the great strength of wealth is the strength of water.","n":"Each line pairs a power with its domain, but the logic is unclear, and மதி may be mind or the moon. The verse seems to stand apart from the chapter's senses.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oozh","manam","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:152"},"t:2031":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2031,"t":["குட்டம் ஒருமுழம் உள்ளது அரைமுழம்","வட்டம் அமைந்ததோர் வாவியுள் வாழ்வன","பட்டன மீன்பல பரவன் வகைகொணர்ந்து","இட்டனன் யாம்இனி ஏதம்இ லோமே."],"p":"","r":"In a round pool a cubit deep, holding half a cubit of water, the many fish that lived there have been caught; the fisherman brought his gear and cast it, and now we are free of harm.","n":"The verse names no meaning for its image. Placed here, the pool is usually taken as the body or the heart, the fish as the senses or desires, and the fisherman (பரவன்) as the Lord or the guru who catches them. The measures (ஒருமுழம், அரைமுழம்) may be symbolic and I can't fix them.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","thiruvarul","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:153"},"t:2032":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2032,"t":["கிடக்கும் உடலின் கிளர்இந் திரியம்","அடக்க லுறும் அவன்தானே அமரன்","விடக்கிண்டு இன்புற மேவுறு சிந்தை","நடக்கின் நடக்கும் நடக்கும் அளவே."],"p":"","r":"He who sets about curbing the restless senses of this lying body is himself immortal; the mind that goes to the flesh for pleasure runs only as far as it is let run.","n":"விடக்கு is flesh; விடக்கிண்டு is unclear and may be corrupt. The last line (நடக்கின் நடக்கும் நடக்கும் அளவே) can also mean that the mind goes wherever it is allowed to go, so the curbing lies in not allowing it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","manam","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:153"},"t:2033":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2033,"t":["அஞ்சும் அடக்குஅடக்கு என்பர் அறிவிலார்","அஞ்சும் அடக்கும் அமரரும் அங்கிலை","அஞ்சும் அடக்கிய அசேதன மாம்என்றிட்டு","அஞ்சும் அடக்கா அறிவு அறிந்தேனே."],"p":"","r":"The ignorant say 'curb all five, curb them'; even among the immortals none has curbed all five; knowing that to curb all five is to become inert, I learned the knowledge that does not curb them.","n":"அசேதனம் is the insentient: a mind with all five senses shut down is no better than matter. The verse is commonly taken as rejecting forced suppression, not restraint itself, since the same chapter praises the senses subsiding (2035) and the senses caught by grace (2031). The word அடக்கு is the one the Kural uses for self-restraint.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","arivu","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:153"},"t:2034":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2034,"t":["முழக்கி எழுவன மும்மத வேழம்","அடக்க அறிவென்றும் கோட்டையை வைத்தேன்","பிழைத்தன ஓடிப் பெருங்கேடு மண்டிக்","கொழுத்தன வேழம் குலைக்கின்ற வாறே."],"p":"","r":"Elephants in triple rut rise trumpeting; I built a fort called knowledge to hold them, but they broke out and ran, rushed into great ruin, and that is how the fattened elephants wreck everything.","n":"The elephants are usually taken as the senses, as in 977; மும்மதம், rut flowing from three places, is sometimes read as the three malas. The point is that knowledge by itself, as a wall, does not hold them.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","arivu","kedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:153"},"t:2035":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2035,"t":["ஐந்தில் ஒடுங்கில் அகலிடம் ஆவது","ஐந்தில் ஒடுங்கில் அருந்தவம் ஆவது","ஐந்தில் ஒடுங்கில் அரன்பதம் ஆவது","ஐந்தில் ஒடுங்கில் அருளுடை யாரே."],"p":"","r":"If one subsides within the five, that is the wide world; if one subsides within the five, that is rare tapas; if one subsides within the five, that is Hara's state; those who subside within the five are the ones who have grace.","n":"ஒடுங்குதல் is to draw in or subside, not to beat down (அடக்குதல்), and the chapter seems to set the one against the other. ஐந்தில் ஒடுங்கில் can mean the senses settling within their five, or the self drawing back from them. அகலிடம் (the wide place) may be the world or the vast inner space.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","thavam","sivam","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:153"},"t:2036":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2036,"t":["பெருக்கப் பிதற்றிலென் பேய்த்தேர் நினைந்தென்","விரித்த பொருட்கெல்லாம் வித்தாவது உள்ளம்","பெருக்கிற் பெருக்கம் சுருக்கிற் சுருக்கம்","அருத்தமும் அத்தனை ஆராய்ந்துகொள் வார்க்கே."],"p":"","r":"What use in babbling at length, what use in thinking of a mirage? The mind is the seed of everything that spreads out; enlarge it and it grows, shrink it and it shrinks; for those who examine, that is all the meaning there is.","n":"பேய்த்தேர், literally the demon's chariot, is the ordinary word for a mirage. The verse makes the mind, not the senses, the root: whatever world one lives in grows or shrinks with it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["manam","arivu","maayai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:153"},"t:2037":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2037,"t":["இளைக்கின்ற வாறுஅறிந்து இன்னுயிர் வைத்த","கிளைக்குஒன்றும் ஈசனைக் கேடில் புகழோன்","தளைக்கொன்ற நாகம்அஞ் சாடல் ஒடுக்கத்","துளைக்கொண்டது அவ்வழி தூங்கும் படைத்தே."],"p":"","r":"The Lord of unfailing fame, who knows how life wanes and is one with the kin in whom he set the sweet life, makes the five's dancing subside; the serpent that has slain its bonds takes the opening and rests in that path.","n":"The syntax is loose and the reading is a guess. நாகம் (serpent) and துளை (hole, opening) may be kundalini entering the central channel, as yogic readings take it, with அஞ்சாடல் ஒடுக்க as the stilling of the five senses' play.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","kundalini","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:153"},"t:2038":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2038,"t":["பாய்ந்தன பூதங்கள் ஐந்தும் படரொளி","சார்ந்திடும் ஞானத் தறியினில் பூட்டிட்டு","வாய்ந்துகொள் ஆனந்தம் என்னும் அருள் செய்யில்","வேய்ந்துகொள் மேலை விதியது தானே."],"p":"","r":"Tie the five elements that leapt about to the post of knowledge where the spreading light rests; if grace grants what is called bliss, that becomes the higher destiny that covers one over.","n":"பூதங்கள் ஐந்தும் are the five elements, here probably standing for the senses built from them, tied like cattle to a post (தறி). வேய்ந்துகொள் (to roof, to cover) and மேலை விதி (the higher rule or fate) are unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","aanandham","thiruvarul","adakkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:153"},"t:2039":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2039,"t":["நடக்கின்ற நந்தியை நாடோ றும் உன்னில்","படர்க்கின்ற சிந்தையப் பைய ஒடுக்கிக்","குறிக்கொண்ட சிந்தை குறிவழி நோக்கில்","வடக்கொடு தெற்கு மனக்கோயி லாமே."],"p":"","r":"If you think daily on Nandi who moves within, gently drawing in the spreading thought, and the mind fixed on its mark looks along the mark's path, north and south become the temple of the mind.","n":"பைய (gently) again sets drawing in against forcing. குறி (mark, sign) is a yogic term for the point of concentration. வடக்கொடு தெற்கு may mean the whole world or, in the body, the line from crown to base; either way the shrine is the mind.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiyaanam","manam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:153"},"t:2040":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2040,"t":["சென்றன நாழிகை நாள்கள் சிலபல","நின்றது நீள்பொருள் நீர்மேல் எழுத்துஒத்து","வென்று புலன்கள் விரைந்து விடுமின்கள்","குன்று விழவதில் தாங்கலும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"Some many nazhigais and days have gone; the wealth that seemed lasting is like writing on water; conquer the senses and let them go at once, and even if a mountain falls you can bear it.","n":"நீள்பொருள் may be lasting wealth or the long-lived body. விடுமின்கள் can mean 'let the senses go' or 'free yourselves'. The last line promises steadiness, not escape: the mountain still falls.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","adakkam","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:153"},"t:2041":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2041,"t":["போற்றிசைத் துப்புனி தன்திரு மேனியைப்","போற்றிசெய் மீட்டே புலன்ஐந்தும் புத்தியால்","நாற்றிசைக் கும்பின்னை யாருக்கும் நாதனை","ஊற்றுக உள்ளத்து ஒருங்கலும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"Praise and sing the holy form of the Pure One and worship it; then, turning back the five senses with the intellect, pour into the heart the Lord of the four directions and of all who come after, and gathering into one becomes possible.","n":"The order is worship first, then the senses turned back by புத்தி (intellect), then the Lord poured (ஊற்றுக) into the heart. ஒருங்கல் is the mind gathered into one.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","adakkam","thiyaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:153"},"t:2042":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2042,"t":["தரிக்கின்ற நெஞ்சம் சகளத்தின் உள்ளே","அரிக்கின்ற ஐவரை யாரும் உணரார்","சிரிக்கின்ற வாறு சிலபல பேசில்","வரிக்கின்ற மைசூழ் வரையது வாமே."],"p":"","r":"No one perceives the five who gnaw within the embodied state where the heart is held; if one speaks of many things as if in jest, it becomes a mountain wrapped in streaked darkness.","n":"சகளம் is the state with form, the embodied soul or the Lord in form. The second half is obscure: மைசூழ் வரை, a mountain surrounded by black, may be delusion piling up from idle talk.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","nenju","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:153"},"t:2043":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2043,"t":["கைவிட லாவது ஒன்று இல்லை கருத்தினுள்","எய்தி அவனை இசையினால் ஏத்துமின்","ஐவருடைய அவாவினில் தோன்றிய","பொய்வ ருடைய புலன்களும் ஐந்தே."],"p":"","r":"There is nothing one must give up; reaching him in your thought, praise him with song; the senses that arose from the craving of the five false ones are only five.","n":"The first line seems to answer the call to renounce: nothing needs abandoning if the Lord is held within and sung. The last two lines are unclear; ஐந்தே may belittle the senses (they are only five) once their root, craving, is seen.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","avaa","adakkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:153"},"t:2044":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2044,"t":["உணர்வுஒன்று இலாமூடன் உண்மைஒ ராதோன்","கணுவின்றி வேதா கமநெறி காணான்","பணிஒன்று இலாதோன் பரநிந்தை செய்வோன்","அணுவின் குணத்தோன் அசற்குரு வாமே."],"p":"","r":"A fool without inner feeling, who does not ascertain the truth, never sees even a part of the Veda-Agama path, does no service, reviles others and has the nature of a bound soul: he is a false guru.","n":"பரநிந்தை can be reviling others or reviling the Supreme. அணுவின் குணத்தோன், one with the quality of the anu, is taken as one still in the bound soul's state, who cannot free others. கணுவின்றி is read as 'not even a joint's length'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["guru","pedhaimai","nindhai","aagamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:154"},"t:2045":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2045,"t":["மந்திர தந்திர மாயோக ஞானமும்","பந்தமும் வீடும் தரிசித்துப் பார்ப்பவர்","சிந்தனை செய்யாத் தெளிவியாது ஊண்பொருட்டு","அந்தகர் ஆவோர் அசற்குரு வாமே."],"p":"","r":"Those who have looked over mantra, tantra, great yoga and jnana, bondage and release, yet do not reflect on them or make others clear, and go blind for the sake of food: they are false gurus.","n":"தரிசித்துப் பார்ப்பவர் suggests acquaintance by sight, without having thought it through (சிந்தனை செய்யா). ஊண்பொருட்டு names the motive: teaching as a living.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["guru","mandhiram","yogam","undi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:154"},"t:2046":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2046,"t":["ஆமாது அறியாதோன் மூடன் அதிமூடன்","காமாதி நீங்காக் கலதி கலதிகட்கு","ஆமாறு அசத்துஅறி விப்போன் அறிவிலோன்","கோமான் அலன்அசத் தாகும் குரவனே."],"p":"","r":"He who does not know what is, a fool and a great fool, a wretch not free of lust and the rest, who teaches the unreal to wretches as the way, is without knowledge: he is no master but an unreal teacher.","n":"அசத்து (the unreal) is a Siddhanta term for what is not abiding; the unreal teacher passes on the unreal. கலதி (wretch, the luckless) is applied to both teacher and pupils.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["guru","pedhaimai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:154"},"t:2047":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2047,"t":["கற்பாய கற்பங்கள் நீக்காமற் கற்பித்தால்","தற்பாவங் குன்றும் தனக்கே பகையாகும்","நற்பால் அரசுக்கும் நாட்டுக்கும் கேடென்றே","முற்பால நந்தி மொழிந்துவைத் தானே."],"p":"","r":"If one teaches without removing the fancies that pose as learning, one's own state declines and one becomes one's own enemy; it is ruin to the good king and to the land, as Nandi said of old.","n":"கற்பாய கற்பங்கள் is unclear: கற்பம் may be kalpana (imaginings), ritual prescriptions (kalpa), or a play on கற்பு (learning). தற்பாவம் may be one's own condition or the state of becoming That. The claim that bad teaching ruins king and realm echoes the first tantra's concern with rule.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","arasu","kedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:154"},"t:2048":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2048,"t":["குருடர்க்குக் கோல்காட்டிச் செல்லும் குருடர்","முரணும் பழங்குழி வீழ்வர்கள் முன்பின்","குருடனும் வீழ்வர்கள் முன்பின் அறவே","குருடரும் வீழ்வார் குருடரோடு ஆகியே."],"p":"","r":"The blind who go showing the stick to the blind fall, front and back, into the old pit in their way; the blind leader falls, front and back, altogether; the blind fall together with the blind.","n":"குருடு repeats in every line, so the verse stumbles as its walkers do. The guru who is himself blind is meant, not the pupil alone.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["guru","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:154"},"t:2049":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2049,"t":["தாள்தந்து அளிக்கும் தலைவனே சற்குரு","தாள்தந்து தன்னை அறியத் தரவல்லோன்","தாள்தந்து தத்துவா தீதத்துச் சார்சீவன்","தாள்தந்து பாசம் தணிக்கும் அவன்சத்தே."],"p":"","r":"The true guru is the lord who gives his feet and protects; giving his feet he can grant self-knowledge; giving his feet he brings the soul beyond the tattvas; giving his feet he quells the bonds: he is the Real.","n":"தாள்தந்து opens every line: the gift of the feet, a form of initiation, is the guru's single act with four effects. சத்து (the Real) in the last word makes the true guru (சற்குரு) one with the Real he shows.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["guru","thiruvadi","thaththuvam","paasam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:155"},"t:2050":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2050,"t":["தவிரவைத் தான்வினை தன்னடி யார்கோள்","தவிரவைத் தான்சிரத் தோடுதன் பாதம்","தவிரவைத் தான்நமன் தூதுவர் கூட்டம்","தவிரவைத் தான் பிற வித்துயர் தானே."],"p":"","r":"He ended the deeds and the afflictions that seize his devotees; he set his feet upon their heads; he kept away the band of Yama's messengers; he ended the sorrow of birth.","n":"தவிரவைத்தான் (he made it cease) opens each line; in the second it seems to mean he placed the feet without fail, so the refrain bends in sense. கோள் may be the planets' evil influence or anything that seizes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["guru","vinai","thiruvadi","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:155"},"t:2051":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2051,"t":["கறுத்த இரும்பே கனகமது ஆனால்","மறித்துஇரும் பாகா வகையது போலக்","குறித்தஅப் போதே குருவருள் பெற்றான்","மறித்துப் பிறவியல் வந்தணு கானே."],"p":"","r":"As black iron, once turned to gold, does not turn back into iron, so one who has received the guru's grace at the very moment marked will not come near birth again.","n":"The alchemical image stresses that the change is final. குறித்த அப்போதே, the appointed moment, may be the moment of initiation or the time ripe for grace.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["guru","thiruvarul","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:155"},"t:2052":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2052,"t":["பாசத்தை நீக்கிப் பரனோடு தன்னையும்","நேசத்து நாடி மலமற நீக்குவோர்","ஆசற்ற சற்குரு வாவோர் அறிவற்றுப்","பூசற்கு இரங்குவோர் போதக் குருவன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Those who remove the bond, seek with love the Supreme and themselves, and drive out impurity completely are faultless true gurus; those who, lacking knowledge, are moved by clamour are not gurus who give understanding.","n":"பூசற்கு இரங்குவோர் is unclear: பூசல் is clamour or strife, so it may be teachers stirred by quarrels and show; a reading as பூசை (ritual worship) is possible. போதக் குரு is a guru who teaches understanding.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","paasam","malam","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:155"},"t:2053":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2053,"t":["நேயத்தே நிற்கும் நிமலன் மலமற்ற","நேயத்தை நல்கவல் லோன்நித்தன் சுத்தனே","ஆயத்த வர்தத் துவம் உணர்ந் தாங்குஅற்ற","நேயர்க்கு அளிப்பவன் நீடும் குரவனே."],"p":"","r":"The stainless one who stands in love, able to give a love free of impurity, eternal and pure, is the lasting guru who gives to the loving ones who have grasped the tattvas as they are and are freed of them.","n":"நேயம் (love) repeats in three lines. The opening phrase matches Tiruvachakam, Sivapuranam 13 (நேயத்தே நின்ற நிமலன்), as Sengalvaraya notes. Line 3 (ஆயத்தவர் தத்துவம் உணர்ந்து ஆங்கு அற்ற) is compressed and my reading of it is tentative.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","anbu","malam","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:155"},"t:2054":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2054,"t":["பரிசன வேதி பரிசித்தது எல்லாம்","வரிசை தரும்பொன் வகையாகு மாபோல்","குருபரி சித்த குவலயம் எல்லாம்","திரிமலம் தீர்ந்து சிவகதி யாமே."],"p":"","r":"As everything the philosopher's stone touches becomes gold of rank, so all the world the guru touches is rid of the three malas and reaches the Siva-state.","n":"பரிசன வேதி is the stone that transmutes by touch; பரிசித்த (touched) ties the image to initiation by the guru's touch (ஸ்பர்ச தீக்ஷை in later manuals). திரிமலம் are traditionally anava, karma and maya.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["guru","malam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:155"},"t:2055":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2055,"t":["தானே எனநின்ற சற்குரு சந்நிதி","தானே எனநின்ற தன்மை வெளிப்படில்","தானே தனைப்பெற வேண்டும் சதுர்பெற","ஊனே எனநினைந்து ஓர்ந்துகொள் உன்னிலே."],"p":"","r":"When the presence of the true guru who stands as the very self, and that nature of being the self, are revealed, the self must win itself; to gain this, consider within you that the body is only flesh.","n":"தானே என நின்ற can be 'standing as oneself' or 'standing as He alone'. சதுர் may be skill or the fourth state (turiya). ஊனே என நினைந்து may be 'thinking it is mere flesh' or 'thinking of him as intimate as one's flesh'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","meyyunarvu","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:155"},"t:2056":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2056,"t":["வரும்வழி போம்வழி மாயா வழியைக்","கருவழி கண்டவர் காணா வழியைக்","பெரும்வழி யாநந்தி பேசும் வழியைக்","குருவழியே சென்று கூடலும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"The path of coming and going, the path of maya, the path that those who saw the womb's path do not see, the great path Nandi speaks of: going by the guru's path one can reach it.","n":"வழி ends every half-line. The first two paths are birth and death under maya; the last two are the path of release, unseen by those who know only birth, and taught by Nandi. The guru's path joins them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["guru","maayai","piravi","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:155"},"t:2057":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2057,"t":["குருஎன் பவனே வேதாக மங்கூறும்","பரஇன்ப னாகிச் சிவயோகம் பாவித்து","ஒருசிந்தை யின்றி உயிர்பாசம் நீக்கி","வருநல் குரவன்பால் வைக்கலும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"The guru is the one of supreme bliss whom the Veda and Agama describe, who practises Siva-yoga, has no thought of his own and removes the soul's bonds; one may place oneself with such a good guru when he comes.","n":"ஒருசிந்தையின்றி may mean 'without any other thought' or 'without a separate thought of his own'. வைக்கலும் ஆமே leaves unclear who is placed: the pupil entrusting himself, or the soul set with the guru.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","yogam","paasam","aagamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:155"},"t:2058":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2058,"t":["சத்தும் அசத்தும் சதசத்தும் தான்காட்டிச்","சித்தும் அசித்தும் சிவபரத் தேசேர்த்துச்","சுத்தம் அசுத்தம் அறச்சுக மானசொல்","அத்தன் அருட்குரு வாம்அவன் கூறிலே."],"p":"","r":"Showing the real, the unreal and the real-unreal, setting the sentient and the insentient in Siva the Supreme, speaking the word that is bliss beyond pure and impure: when he speaks, he is the Father's grace-guru.","n":"சத்து, அசத்து and சதசத்து are traditionally the Lord, the bonds, and the soul that can turn to either; the verse lists them without assigning them. சித்து and அசித்து are the sentient and the insentient.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","pathi","pasu","paasam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:155"},"t:2059":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2059,"t":["ஊற்றிடும் ஐம்மலம் பாச உணர்வினை","பற்றறு நாதன் அடியில் பணிதலால்","சுற்றிய பேதம் துரியம் மூன் றால்வாட்டித்","தற்பரம் மேவுவோர் சாதகர் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"By bowing at the feet of the Lord who is free of attachment, those who wither the five malas that well up and the awareness of bondage, scorch the surrounding divisions by the three turiyas, and reach the Supreme are the true adepts.","n":"ஐம்மலம் are traditionally anava, karma, maya, mayeya and tirodhana. துரியம் மூன்று (three turiyas) is a technical term I can't fix; it may be the fourth state in the three avasthas of soul, Shakti and Siva. சாதகர் are practitioners who have reached the goal.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["malam","paasam","thiruvadi","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:155"},"t:2060":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2060,"t":["எல்லாம் இறைவன் இறைவி யுடன்இன்பம்","வலலார் புலனும் வருங்கால் உயிர்தோன்றிச்","சொல்லா மலம்ஐந்து அடங்கிட்டு ஓங்கியே","செலலாச் சிவகதி சேர்தல்விளை யாட்டே."],"p":"","r":"All is the Lord's bliss with the Lady; when the strong senses come, the soul appears; the five unspoken malas subside, and rising to reach the Siva-state that does not pass is play.","n":"விளையாட்டு (play) is read of the Lord: the soul's embodiment and release are his sport with the Lady. சொல்லா மலம் may be malas beyond words or ones not to be named. செல்லா may mean 'not passing away' or 'where none goes'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","saththi","malam","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:155"},"t:2061":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2061,"t":["ஈனப் பிறவியில் இட்டது மீட்டுட்டித்","தானத்துள் இட்டுத் தனையூட்டித் தாழ்த்தலும்","ஞானத்தின் மீட்டலும் நாட்டலும் வீடுற்று","மோனத்துள் வைத்தலும் முத்தன்தன் செய்கையே."],"p":"","r":"Drawing back the soul he had placed in a lowly birth, setting it in its place and feeding it, humbling it, recovering it by knowledge, establishing it, and placing it in silence once it reaches release: these are the doings of the Freed One.","n":"The list may map onto the Lord's acts toward the soul, ending in grace. மீட்டுட்டி is obscure, perhaps மீட்டு ஊட்டி. முத்தன் (the Freed One) is the Lord, or the guru in whom he acts.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","mounam","veedu","aindhozhil"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:155"},"t:2062":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2062,"t":["அத்தன் அருளின் விளையாட் டிடம்சடம்","சித்தொடு அசித்துஅறத் தெளிவித்த சீவனைச்","சுத்தனும் ஆக்கித் துடைத்து மலத்தினைச்","சத்துடன் ஐங்கரு மத்திடும் தன்மையே."],"p":"","r":"The insentient world is the playground of the Father's grace; the soul he has made discern the sentient from the insentient he makes pure, wipes away its mala and sets with the Real: such is the way of his five acts.","n":"சடம் (insentient matter) is not illusion here but the field where grace works. The five acts are presented as all serving the soul's purification.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","aindhozhil","malam","pasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:155"},"t:2063":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2063,"t":["ஈசத்து வங்கடந்து இல்லையென்று அப்புறம்","பாசத்து ளேயென்றும் பாவியும் அண்ணலை","நேசத்து ளேநின்ற நின்மலன் எம்மிறை","தேசத்தை எல்லாம் தெளியவைத் தானே."],"p":"","r":"Our stainless Lord, who stood in love for those who pass beyond the Isa tattva and say there is nothing beyond, and for those who, still in bondage, ever contemplate him, has made all the realms clear.","n":"ஈசத்துவம் is the Isvara tattva, one of the pure tattvas. The first two lines are obscure; பாவியும் may be 'contemplating' (பாவித்தல்) or 'even the sinner'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","thaththuvam","paasam","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:155"},"t:2064":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2064,"t":["மாணிக்க மாலை மலர்ந்தெழு மண்டலம்","ஆணிப்பொன் நின்றங்கு அமுதம் விளைந்தது","பேணிக்கொண்டு உண்டார் பிறப்பற்று இருந்த","ஊனுக்கு இருந்தார் உணராத மாக்களே."],"p":"","r":"In the mandala that rises blossoming like a garland of rubies, pure gold stood and nectar ripened there; those who guarded it and drank lived free of birth, and the brutes who did not perceive it lived for the flesh.","n":"The mandala, the rubies and the pure gold (ஆணிப்பொன்) are symbolic and taken in yogic readings as a region of the head where nectar forms. மாக்கள் (brutes) is the word for those who live only for the body.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["amudham","yogam","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:155"},"t:2065":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2065,"t":["அசத்தொடு சத்தும் அசத்சத்து நீங்க","இசைத்திடு பாசப்பற்று ஈங்குஅறு மாறே","அசைத்துஇரு மாயை அனுத்தானும் ஆங்கே","இசைத்தானும் ஒன்றறி விப்போன் இறையே."],"p":"","r":"So that the real with the unreal and the real-unreal are set apart and the clinging attachment of bondage is cut here, the Lord moves the two mayas and the soul there and makes the one thing known.","n":"இரு மாயை are traditionally pure and impure maya, and அணு is the bound soul. The syntax is hard and my reading is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pathi","maayai","paasam","pasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:155"},"t:2066":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2066,"t":["ஏறு நெறியே மலத்தை எரித்தலால்","ஈறில் உரையால் இருளை அறுத்தலான்","மாறில் பசுபாசம் வாட்டலால் வீடுக","கூறு பரனே குருவாம் இயம்பிலே."],"p":"","r":"Because he burns mala by the ascending path, cuts away darkness by the endless word and withers the soul's bond so that release comes, the Supreme himself, if you would name him, is the guru.","n":"The chapter ends by collapsing the distinction: the guru's three acts (burning, cutting, withering) are the Supreme's, so the true guru is the Supreme. ஈறில் உரை (the endless word) may be the teaching or the mantra.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["guru","malam","pasu","paasam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:155"},"t:2067":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2067,"t":["கண்காணி இல்லென்று கள்ளம் பலசெய்வார்","கண்காணி இல்லா இடமில்லை காணுங்கால்","கண்காணி யாகக் கலந்தெங்கும் நின்றானைக்","கண்காணி கண்டார் களஒழிந் தாரே."],"p":"","r":"Saying there is no overseer, people commit many thefts; when you look, there is no place without an overseer; those who saw the one who stands mingled everywhere as overseer have given up stealth.","n":"கண்காணி, a supervisor or watchman, opens every line. கள்ளம் and களவு cover hidden wrongdoing generally, not only theft. The cure is not fear of being caught but seeing the overseer, after which stealth simply ends.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalavu","koodaozhukkam","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:156"},"t:2068":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2068,"t":["செய்தான் அறியும் செழுங்கடல் வட்டத்துப்","பொய்தான் மிகவும் புலம்பும் மனிதர்கள்","மெய்தான் உரைக்கில்விண் ணோர் தொழச் செய்வான்","மைதாழ்ந்து இலங்கும் மிடறுடை யோனே."],"p":"","r":"He knows what was done; the men of this sea-girt world who cry out great falsehoods, if they speak truth, will be made worshipped by the gods by the one whose throat glows dark.","n":"செய்தான் அறியும் may also mean 'the doer himself knows'. The dark throat (மைதாழ்ந்து இலங்கும் மிடறு) is Siva's, from swallowing the poison.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaaimai","koodaozhukkam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:156"},"t:2069":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2069,"t":["பத்துவிற் றுண்டு பகலைக் கழிவிடும்","மத்தகர்க்கு அன்றோ மறுபிறப்பு உள்ளது","வித்துக்குற் றுண்டு விளைபுலம் பாழ்செய்யும்","பித்தர்கட்கு என்றும் பிறப்பில்லை தானே."],"p":"","r":"Rebirth is for the drunken who pass the day selling ten and eating; for the madmen who pound the seed-grain, eat it and lay waste the field, there is never any birth.","n":"A riddle. பத்து (ten) may be the ten organs of sense and action, spent for daily living. The 'madmen' who eat the seed and ruin the field are taken as those who destroy the seed of karma, so nothing grows into a new birth; பித்தர் is also a word for Siva's devotees.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:156"},"t:2070":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2070,"t":["வடக்கு வடக்கென்பர் வைத்ததுஒன்று இல்லை","நடக்க உறுவரே ஞானமி ல்லாதார்","வடக்கில் அடங்கிய வையகம் எல்லாம்","அகத்தில் அடங்கும் அறிவுடை யோர்க்கே."],"p":"","r":"They say 'north, north', but nothing has been placed there; those without knowledge set out to walk; all the world contained in the north is contained within, for those who know.","n":"Going north may be pilgrimage to Kailas or the vow of facing north until death (வடக்கிருத்தல்). The verse does not say which; its point is that what is sought there lies within.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","theerththam","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:156"},"t:2071":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2071,"t":["காயக் குழப்பனைக் காயநன் னாடனைக்","காயத்தி னுள்ளே கமழ்கின்ற நந்தியைத்","தேயத்து ளேஎங்கும் தேடித் திரிவர்கள்","காயத்துள் நின்ற கருத்தறி யாரே."],"p":"","r":"They wander everywhere in the land seeking Nandi, the one who stirs the body, the lord of the body's good land, who is fragrant within the body; they do not know the purpose that stands in the body.","n":"காயம் (body) recurs in every line. காயக் குழப்பன் is unclear: one who kneads or works the body, or one who stirs within it. Against the poets who scorn the body, Tirumular makes it the land where the Lord lives.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","sivam","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:156"},"t:2072":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2072,"t":["கண்காணி யாகவே கையகத் தேயெழும்","கண்காணி யாகக் கருத்துள் இருந்திடும்","கண்காணி யாகக் கலந்து வழிசெய்யும்","கண்காணி யாகிய காதலன் தானே."],"p":"","r":"As overseer he rises in the hand; as overseer he stays in the mind; as overseer he mingles and makes the way; the beloved who has become overseer is he.","n":"கையகத்தே எழும் may echo the proverbial gooseberry in the palm: present and plain. The last line turns the watchman into the lover (காதலன்), so the overseer of 2067 is not a threat.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","anbu","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:156"},"t:2073":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2073,"t":["கன்னி ஒருசிறை கற்றோர் ஒருசிறை","மன்னிய மாதவம் செய்வோர் ஒருசிறை","தன்னியல்பு உன்னி உணர்ந்தோர் ஒருசிறை","என்னிது ஈசன் இயல்புஅறி யாரே."],"p":"","r":"Virgins in one enclosure, the learned in another, those doing great tapas in another, those who have known their own nature by contemplation in another: what is this? They do not know the Lord's nature.","n":"சிறை may be an enclosure, a side or a prison: each group shut in its own discipline. கன்னி is unclear here; it may be the celibate. The verse faults division rather than any one practice.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["samayam","iraivan","thavam","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:156"},"t:2074":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2074,"t":["காணாத கண்ணில் படலமே கண்ணொளி","காணாத வர்கட்கும் காணாதது அவ்வொளி","காணாத வர்கட்கும் கண்ணாம் பெருங்கண்ணைக்","காணாது கண்டார் களவொழிந் தாரே."],"p":"","r":"In the eye that cannot see, the film is what hides the eye's light; that light is unseen too by those who do not see; those who have seen, without seeing, the great Eye that is eye even to the unseeing have given up stealth.","n":"படலம் is a cataract or film over the eye, traditionally read as mala. காணாது கண்டார், seeing without seeing, is seeing not with the eye. The last words repeat 2067's ending (களவொழிந்தாரே).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["meyyunarvu","sivam","kalavu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:156"},"t:2075":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2075,"t":["பித்தன் மருந்தால் தெளிந்து பிரகிருதி","உய்த்தொன்று மாபோல் விழியும் தன் கண்ணொளி","அத்தன்மை யாதல்போல் நந்தி அருள்தரச்","சித்தம் தெளிந்தோன் செயல் ஒழிந்தேனே."],"p":"","r":"As a madman cleared by medicine returns to his nature, and as an eye regains its own light, so when Nandi gave grace I, clear in mind, ceased from doing.","n":"பிரகிருதி here is one's natural state. செயல் ஒழிந்தேன், I ceased from action, is the end of acting from one's own will, not idleness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","marundhu","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:156"},"t:2076":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2076,"t":["பிரான்பல மாகப் பெயர்ந்தன எட்டும்","பராமயம் என்றெண்ணிப் பள்ளி யுணரார்","சுராமயம் முன்னிய சூழ்வினை யாளர்","நிராமய மாக நினைப் பொழிந் தாரே."],"p":"","r":"The eight moved as the Lord's power; not knowing them to be filled with the Supreme, people do not wake; the schemers intent on intoxication gave up thinking of the One free of ill.","n":"The eight (எட்டும்) are traditionally Siva's eight forms: the five elements, sun, moon and the soul. சுராமயம் may be 'full of liquor' or 'of the gods (sura)'. பள்ளியுணரார் may be 'they do not wake from sleep'. நிராமயம் (free of disease) is an epithet of the Lord.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","pedhaimai","kal"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:156"},"t:2077":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2077,"t":["ஒன்றுஇரண் டாகிநின்று ஒன்றிஒன் றாயினோர்க்கு","ஒன்றும் இரண்டும் ஒருகாலும் கூடிடா","ஒன்றுஇரண்டு என்றே உரைதரு வோர்க்கெலாம்","ஒன்றுஇரண் டாய் நிற்கும் ஒன்றோடுஒன் றானதே."],"p":"","r":"For those who were one and two and have become one by uniting, one and two never come together; for all who keep saying 'one or two', the One that has become one with one stands as one and two.","n":"The verse plays on the debate whether soul and Lord are one or two. It seems to say those who have united no longer think in those terms, while for the debaters the truth appears as both; the Siddhanta term for this is advaita as inseparable union.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","pasu","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:156"},"t:2078":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2078,"t":["உயிரது நின்றால் உணர்வுஎங்கு நிற்கும்","அயர்அறி வில்லையால் ஆருடல் வீழும்","உயிரும் உணலும் ஒருங்கிக் கிடக்கும்","பயிரும் கிடந்துள்ளப் பாங்கு அறி யாரே."],"p":"","r":"If the soul stands still, where will awareness stand? Without knowing, the body falls; soul and food lie together, and people do not know how the crop lies within.","n":"அயர் அறிவு may be weary or absent knowing. பயிர் (crop) may be the body that grows from food, or what grows in the soul. The verse is compressed and may be damaged.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["uyir","yaakkai","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:156"},"t:2079":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2079,"t":["உயிரது வேறாய் உணர்வுஎங்கும் ஆகும்","உயிரை அறியில் உணர்வுஅறி வாகும்","உயிர்அன்று உடலை விழுங்கும் உணர்வை","அயரும் பெரும்பொருள் ஆங்கறி யாரே."],"p":"","r":"When the soul is seen as distinct, awareness spreads everywhere; if one knows the soul, awareness becomes knowledge; people do not know the great Being that then swallows body and awareness.","n":"உணர்வு (awareness) and அறிவு (knowledge) are set apart. Line 3 (உயிர் அன்று உடலை விழுங்கும் உணர்வை) is hard; அன்று may be 'then' or 'not'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["uyir","arivu","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:156"},"t:2080":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2080,"t":["உலகாணி ஒண்சுடர் உத்தம சித்தன்","நிலவாணி ஐந்தினுள் தேருற நிற்கும்","சிலவாணி யாகிய தேவர் பிரானைத்","தலைவாணி செய்வது தன்னை அறிவதே."],"p":"","r":"The linchpin of the world, the bright flame, the highest Siddha, stands to be known as the linchpin of the land in the five; the chief work toward the Lord of the gods is knowing oneself.","n":"ஆணி (linchpin, nail, also the finest gold) plays through the verse: உலகாணி, நிலவாணி, சிலவாணி, தலைவாணி. Only the last line is clear: self-knowledge is the principal thing.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["meyyunarvu","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:156"},"t:2081":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2081,"t":["தான்அந்த மாம்என நின்ற தனிச்சுடர்","ஊன்அந்த மாய்உல காய்நின்ற ஒண்சுடர்","தேன்அந்த மாய்நின்று சிற்றின்பம் நீஒழி","கோன்அந்தம் இல்லாக் குணத்தரு ளாமே."],"p":"","r":"The lone flame that stands as 'He is the end', the bright flame that stands as the end of the body and as the world, standing as the fullness of sweetness: give up petty pleasure; this is the grace of the King of endless qualities.","n":"அந்தம் recurs: end, limit, and in தேன்அந்தம் perhaps 'beauty' or 'fullness'. சிற்றின்பம் (petty pleasure), usually sensual pleasure, is set against the Lord's sweetness.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","inbam","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:156"},"t:2082":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2082,"t":["உன்முத லாகிய ஊன்உயிர் உண்டெனும்","கல்முதல் ஈசன் கருத்தறி வார்இல்லை","நல்முதல் ஏறிய நாமம் அறநின்றால்","தன்முதல் ஆகிய தத்துவம் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"None knows the purpose of the Lord, first in all from stone up, who says the flesh and life that are yours exist; if one stands so that the name that rose upon the good First is gone, one becomes the tattva that is one's own first.","n":"கல்முதல் and நாமம் அற நின்றால் are obscure. The drift may be that when name and identity drop away one reaches one's source; I can't fix more than that.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","thaththuvam","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:156"},"t:2083":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2083,"t":["இந்தியம் அந்தக் கரணம் இவைஉயிர்","வந்தன சூக்க உடலன்று மானது","தந்திடும் ஐவிதத் தால்தற் புருடனும்","முந்துளம் மன்னும் ஆறாறு முடிவிலே."],"p":"","r":"The senses and the inner organs came to the soul as the subtle body, not the gross; through the five kinds they give, the Purusha too abides in the heart at the end of the thirty-six.","n":"சூக்கும உடல் (the subtle body), அந்தக்கரணம் (inner organs) and ஆறாறு (36, the tattvas) are technical. ஐவிதம் may be the five kanchukas; the verse is a compressed tattva statement.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","uyir","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:156"},"t:2084":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2084,"t":["வித்துப் பொதிவார் விரைவிட்டு நாற்றுவார்","அற்றதம் வாணாள் அறிகிலாப் பாவிகள்","உற்ற வினைத்துயர் ஒன்றும் அறிகிலார்","முற்றொளி தீயின் முனிகின்ற வாறே."],"p":"","r":"Sinners who store seed, sow it and plant out seedlings, not knowing that their span of life is running out, know nothing of the suffering their deeds bring; they are like those scorched in a full-blazing fire.","n":"The plain reading is of people absorbed in the farming year; வித்து (seed) could also bear the yogic sense of bindu, as in chapter 140, but nothing in the verse forces it. முனிகின்ற in the last line (be angry, or be consumed) is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pochchaappu","vinai","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:157"},"t:2085":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2085,"t":["போது சடக்கெனப் போகின் றதுகண்டும்","வாதுசெய் தென்னோ மனிதர் பெறுவது","நீதியு ளேநின்று நின்மலன் தாள்பணிந்து","ஆதியை அன்பில் அறியகில் லார்களே."],"p":"","r":"Though they see time slipping away in a moment, what do people gain by arguing? They cannot stand in right conduct, bow at the feet of the Stainless one and know the First in love.","n":"வாது (disputation) is set against அன்பு: knowing the Lord is said to come through love and bowing, not argument.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvadi","anbu","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:157"},"t:2086":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2086,"t":["கடன்கொண்டு நெற்குத்துக் கையரை","உடம்பினை ஓம்பி உயிராத் திரிவார்","தடங்கொண்ட சாரல் தழல்முரு டேறி","இடங்கொண்டு உடலார் கிடக்கின்ற வாறே."],"p":"","r":"Those who borrow, pound paddy and tend the body as if it were their life end up lying in their place, bodies mounted on a pyre of rough logs on a wide slope.","n":"கையரை in line 1 is obscure (perhaps the wretched, or the hands at work). The irony is that labour spent only on keeping the body ends on the cremation ground.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","nilaiyaamai","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:157"},"t:2087":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2087,"t":["விரைந்தன்று நால்வர்க்கு மெய்ப்பதி சூழ்ந்து","புரந்தகல் லால்நிழல் புண்ணியன் சொன்ன","பரந்தன்னை ஓராப் பழிமொழி யாளர்","உரந்தன்மை யாக ஒருங்கிநின் றார்களே."],"p":"","r":"People of blameworthy speech, who do not grasp the Supreme that the holy one under the banyan's shade taught the four long ago, stand gathered together as if that were strength.","n":"கல்லால் நிழல் புண்ணியன் teaching நால்வர் is traditionally Siva as Dakshinamurti teaching the four sages. உரந்தன்மையாக ஒருங்கி நின்றார் is unclear: perhaps they band together in obstinacy.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pathi","guru","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:157"},"t:2088":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2088,"t":["நின்ற புகழும் நிறைதவத்து உண்மையும்","என்றுஎம் ஈசன் அடியவர்க் கேநல்கும்","அன்றி உலகம் அதுஇது தேவுஎன்று","குன்றுகை யாலே குறைப்பட்ட வாறே."],"p":"","r":"Lasting fame and the truth of full austerity our Lord always gives to his devotees; but the world, saying 'that god, this god', has come to loss by its own diminishing.","n":"அது இது தேவு என்று points at running from one god to another; the verse contrasts it with steady devotion to one Lord rather than condemning a named sect.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adiyaar","thavam","samayam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:157"},"t:2089":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2089,"t":["இன்பத்து ளேபிறந்து இன்பத்து ளேவளர்ந்து","இன்பத்து ளேநினைக் கின்றது இதுமறந்து","துன்பத்து ளேசிலர் சோறொடு கூறையென்று","துன்பத்து ளேநின்று தூங்குகின் றார்களே."],"p":"","r":"Born in joy, grown in joy, forgetting the joy that is there to think on, some cry out for food and clothing and, standing in sorrow, sleep in sorrow.","n":"The repeated இன்பத்துளே and துன்பத்துளே frame the verse. The joy may be the bliss of Siva in which beings are held, as tradition reads it, or simply the good of life; either way the fault is forgetting it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aanandham","idumbai","pochchaappu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:157"},"t:2090":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2090,"t":["பெறுதற்கு அரிய பிறவியைப் பெற்றும்","பெறுதற்கு அரிய பிரானடி பேணார்","பெறுதற்கு அரிய பிராணிகள் எல்லாம்","பெறுதற்கு அரியதோர் பேறுஇழந் தாரே."],"p":"","r":"Having gained a birth that is hard to gain, they do not cherish the Lord's feet, hard to gain; all these creatures, hard to come by, have lost a gain that is hard to gain.","n":"பெறுதற்கு அரிய opens every line. Sengalvaraya notes Arunagirinathar's Kandar Alangaram 67 takes up the same first line.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","thiruvadi","pochchaappu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:157"},"t:2091":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2091,"t":["ஆர்வ மனமும் அளவில் இளமையும்","ஈரமும் நல்லஎன்று இன்புறு காலத்துத்","தீர வருவதோர் காமத் தொழில்நின்று","மாதவன் இன்பம் மறந்தொழிந் தார்களே."],"p":"","r":"With eager minds, boundless youth and a kindness they think good, in the time of pleasure they stand in the work of lust that soon runs out and forget the joy of the great ascetic.","n":"மாதவன் is read here as the great ascetic, Siva; it can also name Vishnu, or mean great austerity. தீர வருவது may mean lust that ends, or that exhausts the one who follows it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","ilamai","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:157"},"t:2092":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2092,"t":["இப்பரி சேஇள ஞாயிறு போலுரு","அப்பரிசு அங்கியின் உள்ளுறை அம்மானை","இப்பரி சேகம லத்துறை ஈசனை","மெய்ப்பரி சேவினை வாதுஇருந் தோமே."],"p":"","r":"He is in this way a form like the young sun, in that way the Lord dwelling within fire, in this way the Lord dwelling in the lotus; yet in truth we sat arguing over deeds.","n":"Sun, fire and lotus may be the sun, fire and moon regions of the yogic body with the heart lotus, or outward forms of worship. வினைவாது is read as dispute about deeds or ritual; the last line is compressed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","vinai","pochchaappu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:157"},"t:2093":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2093,"t":["கூடகில் லார்குரு வைத்த குறிகண்டு","நாடகில் லார்நயம் பேசித் திரிவர்கள்","பாடகில் லார்அவன் செய்த பரிசறிந்து","ஆடவல் லார்அவர் பேறெது வாமே."],"p":"","r":"They cannot reach the sign the guru set, nor seek it; they wander making pleasant talk; they cannot sing, knowing what he has done; what gain is there for them?","n":"குறி (sign, mark) the guru set is not specified. ஆடவல்லார் may be the Lord who dances, whose deeds they do not sing, or the people themselves, able only to dance; the question at the end is rhetorical.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","pochchaappu","thirukkoothu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:157"},"t:2094":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2094,"t":["நெஞ்சு நிறைந்தங்கு இருந்த நெடுஞ்சுடர்","நம்செம் பிரான்என்று நாதனை நாடொறும்","துஞ்சும் அளவும் தொழுமின் தொழாவிடில்","அஞ்சுஅற்று விட்டதோர் ஆனையும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"Worship daily, until you sleep in death, the tall flame that has filled your heart, the Lord, calling him 'our red Lord'; if you do not, you become like an elephant that has thrown off its fear and broken loose.","n":"அஞ்சு அற்று may be 'free of fear', an elephant no longer held by the goad, or 'free of the five', a pun on the senses. Either way worship is what keeps the self in check.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","sivam","adakkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:157"},"t:2095":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2095,"t":["மிருக மனிதர் மிக்கோர் பறவை","ஒருவர்செய்து அன்புவைத்து உன்னாதது இல்லை","பருகுவர் ஓடுவர் பார்ப்பயன் கொள்வர்","திருமருவு மாதவம் சேர்ந்துஉணர்ந் தோரே."],"p":"","r":"Beasts, humans, the great and birds: there is nothing the One made and set his love on that he does not think of; those who joined the great austerity where grace abides and came to know drink, run and take the fruit of the earth.","n":"The syntax is loose. Line 3 may describe the freedom of the realized, or the ordinary acts of every creature that the Lord sustains. திருமருவு may mean where Lakshmi (fortune) abides or where grace abides.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","thavam","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:157"},"t:2096":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2096,"t":["நீதியி லோர்பெற்ற பொன்போல் இறைவனைச்","சோதியி லாரும் தொடர்ந்துஅறி வாரில்லை","ஆதி பயனென்று அமரர் பிரான்என்ற","நாதியே வைத்தது நாடுகின் றேனே."],"p":"","r":"Like gold that the unjust have got, the Lord is not known by those without the light, and none follows him to know him; I seek the refuge who set him as the first fruit, the Lord of the immortals.","n":"The gold of the unjust is perhaps hidden away and of no use, as the Lord is for those without inner light. நாதியே வைத்தது is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:157"},"t:2097":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2097,"t":["இருந்தேன் மலர்அளைந்து இன்புற வண்டு","பெருந்தேன் இழைக்கின்ற பெற்றிமை ஓரார்","வருந்தேன் நுகராது வாய்புகு தேனை","அருந்தேனை யாரும் அறியகி லாரே."],"p":"","r":"They do not grasp how the bee, delighting among flowers full of honey, builds a great store of honey; and no one knows the honey that enters the mouth unsought, to be drunk without toil.","n":"தேன் (honey) plays through every line, with வருந்தேன் (I do not toil) inside it. The honey is commonly read as the bliss of Siva, or in yogic terms the nectar (அமுதம்) that flows within; the verse names neither.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","aanandham","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:157"},"t:2098":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2098,"t":["கருத்தறி யாது கழிந்தன காலம்","அருத்தியுள் ளான்அம ராபதி நாதன்","ஒருத்தன்உள் ளான் உல கத்துயிர்க்கு எல்லாம்","வருத்திநில் லாது வழுக்கின் றாரே."],"p":"","r":"Time has passed without their knowing the purpose; the Lord of Amaravati is full of longing for them, there is One for every life in the world, yet not striving and standing firm, they slip.","n":"அருத்தியுள்ளான் can mean he has desire (for them) or is one to be desired. அமராபதி நாதன் is the lord of the gods' city, here an epithet of Siva.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","pochchaappu","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:157"},"t:2099":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2099,"t":["குதித்தோடிப் போகின்ற கூற்றமும் சார்வாய்","விதித்தென நாள்களும் வீழ்ந்து கழிந்த","விதிர்திருந்து என்செய்தீர் ஆறுதிர் ஆகில்","கொதிக்கின்ற கூழில் துடுப்பிட லாமே."],"p":"","r":"Death comes leaping and running close, and the days allotted have fallen away; what will you do, trembling? If you mean to be at peace, stir the porridge while it boils.","n":"The proverb in line 4 is read here as 'act now, while there is heat and time'; it may instead mean that the effort must be made in the thick of things. விதிர் திருந்து is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kootru","nilaiyaamai","pochchaappu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:157"},"t:2100":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2100,"t":["கரைஅருகு ஆறாக் கழனி வளைந்த","திரைஅரு காமுன்னம் சேர்ந்தின்பம் எய்தும்","வரைஅருகு ஊறிய மாதவ நோக்கின்","நரைஉரு வாச்செல்லும் நாள்கில வாமே."],"p":"","r":"Before the waves reach the field that the river curves around near its bank, join and gain joy; if you look to the great austerity that springs near the mountain, the days left before grey hair are few.","n":"திரை means both a wave and a wrinkle, and நரை is grey hair, so the field before the flood is youth before age. The mountain may be Kailasa or a yogic centre; that is not fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ilamai","thavam","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:157"},"t:2101":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2101,"t":["வரவுஅறி வானை மயங்கிருள் ஞாலத்து","இரவுஅறி வானை எழுஞ்சுடர்ச் சோதியை","அரவுஅறி வார்முன் ஒருதெய்வம் என்று","விரவுஅறி யாமலே மேல்வைத்த வாறே."],"p":"","r":"The One who knows coming, who knows the night in this dark, bewildered world, the rising radiant light: before those who know the serpent, people set him above as one god, without knowing him as mingled with them.","n":"அரவு அறிவார் may be yogis who know the serpent power, or those who know Siva who wears the snake. விரவு அறியாமலே is read as not knowing his union with the soul; the verse seems to fault worship that keeps him apart.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","meyyunarvu","kundalini"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:157"},"t:2102":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2102,"t":["மறந்தொழி மண்மிசை மன்னாப் பிறவி","இறந்தொழி காலத்தும் ஈசனை உள்கும்","பறந்துஅல மந்து படுதுயர் தீர்ப்பான்","சிறந்த சிவநெறி சிந்தைசெய் யீரே."],"p":"","r":"Forget and leave the birth that does not last on this earth; even at the time of dying, think of the Lord; contemplate the excellent Siva way that ends the suffering of fluttering in distress.","n":"இறந்தொழி காலத்தும் ஈசனை உள்கும் returns almost word for word in 2108: the thought held at death matters for what follows.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","saivam","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:158"},"t:2103":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2103,"t":["செல்லும் அளவு செலுத்துமின் சிந்தையை","வல்ல பரிசால் உரைமின்கள் வாய்மையை","இல்லை யெனினும் பெரிதுளன் எம்மிறை","நல்ல அரநெறி நாடுமின் நீரே."],"p":"","r":"Direct your mind as far as it will go; speak the truth as well as you can; even if you have nothing, our Lord is there in fullness; seek the good way of Hara.","n":"இல்லை எனினும் can be 'even if you lack means' or 'even if some say he is not'; both readings make the Lord's presence independent of the seeker's state.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["manam","vaaimai","saivam","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:158"},"t:2104":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2104,"t":["ஒன்றே குலமும் ஒருவனே தேவனும்","நன்றே நினைமின் நமனில்லை நாணாமே","சென்றே புகுங்கதி இல்லைநும் சித்தத்து","நின்றே நிலைபெற நீர்நினைந்துய் மினே."],"p":"","r":"Kin is one and God is one; think only good; then there is no death for you and no shame; there is no refuge to go to outside your own mind; stand there, think of him firmly and be saved.","n":"ஒன்றே குலம் is now usually read as the one kinship of all people; it may also mean all souls are of one kind. நமன் இல்லை நாணாமே can be 'there is no Yama, without shame', or 'there is no Yama, be unashamed'; line 3 may be 'there is no state you will enter' rather than 'no refuge outside the mind'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","pirappu","kootru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:158"},"t:2105":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2105,"t":["போற்றிசெய் அந்தண் கயிலைப் பொருப்பனை","நாற்றிசைக் கும்நடு வாய்கின்ற நம்பனைக்","காற்றிசைக் கும் கமழ்ஆக்கையைக் கைக்கொண்டு","கூற்றுதைத் தான்தன்மைக் கூறிநின்று உய்ம்மினே."],"p":"","r":"Praise the lord of the cool Kailasa mountain, the Nambi who is the centre of the four directions, and, taking up the body that wafts its fragrance to the winds, stand telling the nature of the one who kicked Death, and be saved.","n":"கூற்று உதைத்தான் is Siva who kicked Yama to save Markandeya. Line 3 (காற்றிசைக்கும் கமழ் ஆக்கை) is obscure: perhaps the body made fragrant by worship, or the body of breath.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","sivam","kootru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:158"},"t:2106":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2106,"t":["இக்காயம் நீக்கி இனியொரு காயத்தில்","புக்கும் பிறவாமல் போய்வழி நாடுமின்","எக்காலத்து இவ்வுடல் வந்துஎமக்கு ஆனதென்று","அக்காலம் உன்ன அருள்பெற லாமே."],"p":"","r":"Seek the way to go so that, leaving this body, you do not enter another body and be born; if you think on that time, asking when this body came to be ours, grace may be gained.","n":"அக்காலம் உன்ன may be reflecting on the body's origin, or holding the Lord in mind at the time of leaving it, as in 2102 and 2108.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","yaakkai","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:158"},"t:2107":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2107,"t":["போகின்ற வாறே புகுகின்ற அப்பொருள்","ஆகின்ற போதும் அரன்அறிவான்உளன்","சாகின்ற போதும் தலைவனை நாடுமின்","ஆகின்ற அப்பொருள் அக்கரை ஆகுமே."],"p":"","r":"Hara is there as knower of that thing that enters as it leaves, even as it comes to be; even when dying, seek the Lord, and that thing which comes to be will reach the farther shore.","n":"அப்பொருள் (that thing) is read as the soul, which passes from body to body; அக்கரை (the other shore) is release from that passing.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["uyir","sivam","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:158"},"t:2108":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2108,"t":["பறக்கின்ற ஒன்று பயனுற வேண்டின்","இறக்கின்ற காலத்தும் ஈசனை உள்கும்","சிறப்பொடு சேரும் சிவகதி பின்னைப்","பிறப்பொன்றும் இலாமையும் பேருல காமே."],"p":"","r":"If the one that flies is to reach its goal, think of the Lord even at the time of dying; then the state of Siva joins with distinction, and after it comes freedom from all birth, the great world.","n":"பறக்கின்ற ஒன்று (the thing that flies) is the soul, as a bird leaving its nest.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uyir","veedu","piravi","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:158"},"t:2109":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2109,"t":["கூடியும் நின்றும் தொழுதுஎம் இறைவனைப்","பாடியுளே நின்று பாதம் பணிமின்கள்","ஆடியு ளேநின்று அறிவுசெய் வார்கட்கு","நீடிய ஈற்றுப் பசுவது ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"Gathering and standing, worship our Lord; singing, stand within and bow at his feet; for those who stand within the mirror and come to know, he becomes like a cow long in milk after calving.","n":"ஆடி (mirror) may also be read as 'dancing'. பசு is both cow and the soul; the image of the milch cow is usually taken as the Lord giving freely, but whether the soul or the Lord is the cow is not fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","thiruvadi","pasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:158"},"t:2110":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2110,"t":["விடுகின்ற சிவனார் மேல்எழும் போது","நடுநின்று நாடுமின் நாதன்தன் பாதம்","கெடுகின்ற வல்வினை கேடில் புகழோன்","இடுகின்றான் உம்மை இமையவ ரோடே."],"p":"","r":"When the releasing one rises upward, stand in the middle and seek the Lord's feet; the one of unfailing fame, by whom strong deeds perish, will place you among the celestials.","n":"விடுகின்ற சிவனார் may be a slip for சீவனார், the soul leaving the body; நடுநின்று may be the middle channel of yoga or the space between the brows. Read either as death or as yogic ascent.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvadi","vinai","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:158"},"t:2111":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2111,"t":["ஏறுடை யாய்இறை வாஎம்பி ரான்என்று","நீறிடு வார்அடி யார்நிகழ் தேவர்கள்","ஆறணி செஞ்சுடை அண்ணல் திருவடி","வேறுஅணி வார்க்கு வினையில்லை தானே."],"p":"","r":"Those who wear the holy ash, crying 'you who ride the bull, our Lord, our master', are devotees and living gods; those who wear as their ornament the holy feet of the great one with the river in his red locks have no deeds left to bind them.","n":"வேறு அணிவார் may mean they wear the feet as a distinct ornament, or wear nothing else.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruneeru","adiyaar","thiruvadi","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:158"},"t:2112":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2112,"t":["இன்புறு வீர்அறிந் தேஎம் இறைவனை","அன்புறு விர்தவம் செய்யும்மெய்ஞ் ஞானத்து","பண்புறு வீர்பிற வித்தொழி லேநின்று","துன்புறு பாசத்து உழைத்துஒழிந் தீரே."],"p":"","r":"You who would have joy, know our Lord and be loving, take on the quality of the true knowledge that austerity yields; standing in the work of birth, you have toiled in the bond that brings pain and been ruined.","n":"The verse moves from what one may become to what one has been: the same addressees, offered love and knowledge, are told they have so far only laboured in பாசம்.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","gnaanam","paasam","thavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:158"},"t:2113":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2113,"t":["மேற்கொள்ள லாவதோர் மெய்த்தவம் ஒன்றுண்டு","மேற்கொள்ள லாவதோர் மெய்த்தாளும் ஒன்றுண்டு","மேற்கொள்ள லாவதோர் மெய்ந்நெறி ஒன்றுண்டு","மேற்கொள்ள லாம்வண்ணம் வேண்டிநின்றோர்க்கே."],"p":"","r":"There is one true austerity to take up, one true foot to take hold of, one true path to take up, for those who stand wishing to take them up.","n":"மெய்த்தாள் is read as the Lord's true feet. The last line puts the condition on the seeker's wanting: the way exists for whoever desires it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","thiruvadi","sanmaarkkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:158"},"t:2114":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2114,"t":["சார்ந்தவர்க்கு இன்பம் கொடுக்கும் தழல்வண்ணன்","பேர்ந்தவர்க்கு இன்னாப் பிறவி கொடுத்திடும்","கூர்ந்தவர்க்கு அங்கே குரைகழல்காட்டிடும்","சேர்ந்தவர் தேவரைச் சென்றுணர் வாரே."],"p":"","r":"The fire-coloured one gives joy to those who come to him and painful birth to those who turn away; to the keen he shows his ringing anklets there, and those who join him come to know the God.","n":"The verse states a simple reciprocity: drawing near gives joy, turning away gives birth again.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","piravi","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:158"},"t:2115":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2115,"t":["முத்தியை ஞானத்தை முத்தமிழ் ஓசையை","எத்தனை காலமும் ஏத்துவர் ஈசனை","நெய்த்தலைப் பால்போல் நிமலனும் அங்குளன்","அத்தகு சோதி அதுவிரும் பாரே."],"p":"","r":"They praise for all time release, knowledge, the sound of threefold Tamil and the Lord; the Stainless one is there as ghee within milk, yet they do not desire that light.","n":"The ghee in milk is the Lord present throughout but not seen until churned out. The last line may fault those who praise but do not seek the light, or (taking விரும்பார் as 'want nothing else') praise them; the reading here takes the first.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["veedu","thamizh","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:158"},"t:2116":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2116,"t":["நியமத்த னாகிய நின்மலன் வைத்த","உகம்எத் தனையென்று ஒருவரும் தேறார்","பவமத்தி லேவந்து பாய்கின்றது அல்லால்","சிவமத்தை ஒன்றும் தெளியகில் லாரே."],"p":"","r":"No one can tell how many ages the Stainless one, lord of order, has set; except that they come and plunge into birth, they cannot understand anything of Siva.","n":"நியமத்தன் may be the lord of cosmic order or the one who keeps the observances. பவமத்து and சிவமத்து are unusual forms.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","sivam","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:158"},"t:2117":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2117,"t":["இங்கித்தை வாழ்வும் எனைத்தோர் அகிதமும்","துஞ்சொத்த காலத்துத் தூய்மணி வண்ணனை","விஞ்சத்து உறையும் விகிர்தா எனநின்னை","நஞ்சுஅற் றவர்க்குஅன்றி நாடஒண் ணாதே."],"p":"","r":"Whatever the life here and whatever its ills, at the time like sleep, none but those free of venom can seek you, pure gem-coloured one, the Vikirta dwelling in excellence.","n":"துஞ்சு ஒத்த காலம் is read as death, likened to sleep. நஞ்சு அற்றவர் (those without poison) may be those free of malice or of the soul's impurity. The verse is obscure and the reading tentative.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","thooimai","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:158"},"t:2118":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2118,"t":["பஞ்சமும் ஆம்புவி சற்குறுபால்முன்னி","வஞ்சகர் ஆனவர் வைகில் அவர்தம்மை","அஞ்சுவன் நாதன் அருநர கத்துஇடும்","செஞ்சநிற் போரைத் தெரிசிக்கச் சித்தியே."],"p":"","r":"If deceivers stay in the land before the true guru, famine comes to it; the Lord fears them and casts them into hard hell, while for those with upright hearts, attainment is there to be seen.","n":"The text is doubtful throughout: சற்குறுபால் is probably சற்குரு, அஞ்சுவன் (he fears) is odd for the Lord, and தெரிசிக்க சித்தியே may mean seeing them is itself an attainment. Only the broad contrast of deceivers and the upright is secure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","guru","naragam","siththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:158"},"t:2119":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2119,"t":["சிவனை வழிபட்டார் எண்ணிலாத் தேவர்","அவனை வழிபட்டங்கு ஆமாறுஒன் றில்லை","அவனை வழிபட்டங்கு ஆமாறு காட்டும்","குருவை வழிபடின் கூடலும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"Countless gods worshipped Siva, yet by worshipping him there is no way to become him there; if one worships the guru who shows how to become him by that worship, union is possible.","n":"ஆமாறு (the way of becoming) is the hinge: worship of Siva alone gave the gods no way to become one with him; the guru shows it. The verse makes the guru the means, not a rival object of worship.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["guru","vazhipaadu","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:158"},"t:2120":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2120,"t":["நரரும் சுரரும் பசுபாசம்நண்ணிக்","கருமங்க ளாலே கழிதலில் கண்டு","குருஎன் பவன்ஞானி கோதிலன் ஆனால்","பரம்என்றல் அன்றிப் பகர்ஒன்றும் இன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Seeing that men and gods, held by soul and bond, pass away through their deeds, if the one called guru is a knower without flaw, there is nothing to call him but the Supreme.","n":"The condition matters: only the flawless jnani is to be named பரம். Men and gods both fall under பசு and பாசம், so neither can be the guru by status alone.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["guru","gnaanam","pasu","paasam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:158"},"t:2121":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2121,"t":["ஆட்கொண் டவர்தனி நாயகன் அன்புற","மேற்கொண்டவர்வினை போயற நாடொறும்","நீர்க்கின்ற செஞ்சுடை நீளன் உருவத்தின்","மேற்கொண்ட வாறுஅல்லை வீவித்து ளானே."],"p":"","r":"The sole Lord who took them as his servants, so that love may grow and the deeds of those who took him up may go day by day, has ended them, not as one who took on the form of the tall one with water in his red locks.","n":"Lines 3 and 4 are obscure. They may mean the Lord destroys his servants' deeds through the guru rather than in his own form, which would continue 2119 and 2120, but that is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","adiyaar","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:158"},"t:2122":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2122,"t":["காயப்பை ஒன்று சரக்குப் பலவுள","மாயப்பை ஒன்றுண்டு மற்றுமோர் பையுண்டு","காயப்பைக்கு உள்நின்ற கள்வன் புறப்பட்டால்","மாயப்பை மண்ணா மயங்கிய வாறே."],"p":"","r":"There is one bag of a body with many wares in it; there is one bag of maya and another bag besides; when the thief who stood inside the body-bag goes out, the maya-bag turns to earth: such is the bewilderment.","n":"The three bags are traditionally the gross, subtle and causal bodies, and the thief the soul, which slips out unseen. The verse names only the body-bag and the maya-bag clearly.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","maayai","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:159"},"t:2123":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2123,"t":["அத்தன் அமைத்த உடல்இரு கூறினில்","சுத்தம தாகிய சூக்குமம் சொல்லுங்கால்","சத்த பரிச ரூப ரசகந்தம்","புத்திமான் ஆங்காரம் புரியட்ட காயமே."],"p":"","r":"Of the body the Father fashioned in two parts, the pure subtle one, when spoken of, is sound, touch, form, taste, smell, intellect, mind and ego: the eightfold body.","n":"புரியட்டகம் (puryashtaka) is the subtle body of eight in Siddhanta: the five subtle elements with buddhi, manas and ahankara. The two parts are the gross and the subtle body.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:159"},"t:2124":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2124,"t":["எட்டினில் ஐந்தாகும் இந்திரி யங்களும்","கட்டிய மூன்று கரணமும் ஆயிடும்","ஒட்டிய பாசம் உணர்வுஅது வாகவே","கட்டி அவிழ்ந்திடும் கண்ணுதல் காணுமே."],"p":"","r":"Within the eight, the five senses and the three bound instruments arise; as the clinging bond becomes awareness, the one with the eye in his forehead binds and unbinds.","n":"This verse nearly repeats 473. The eight are the eight of 2123; the three கரணம் are traditionally mind, intellect and ego. Siva both binds the soul in the body and releases it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["paasam","thaththuvam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:159"},"t:2125":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2125,"t":["இரதம் உதிரம் இறைச்சிதோல் மேதை","மருவிய அத்தி வழும்பொடு மச்சை","பரவிய சுக்கிலம் பாழாம் உபாதி","உருவ மலால்உடல் ஒன்றென லாமே."],"p":"","r":"Chyle, blood, flesh, skin, fat, bone with its grease, marrow and the semen that spreads: this is the perishing adjunct, and but for its form, the body may be called one.","n":"The list is the seven or more bodily elements (dhatus) of the gross body. உபாதி (adjunct) marks the gross body as something laid over the soul. The last line is unclear: perhaps the body is one thing, differing only in form.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:159"},"t:2126":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2126,"t":["ஆரே அறிவார் அடியின் பெருமையை","யாரே அறிவார் அங்கவர் நின்றது","யாரே அறிவார் அறுபத்தெட்டு ஆக்கையை","யாரே அறிவார் அடிக்காவல் ஆனதே."],"p":"","r":"Who knows the greatness of the feet? Who knows where they stood? Who knows the sixty-eight bodies? Who knows what became the guard of the feet?","n":"அறுபத்தெட்டு ஆக்கை (sixty-eight bodies) has no settled meaning; it may be a count of bodily parts or of kinds of body. அடி may be the Lord's feet or the base of the body.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvadi","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:159"},"t:2127":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2127,"t":["எண்சாண் அளவால் எடுத்த உடம்புக்குள்","கண்கால் உடலில் சுரக்கின்ற கைகளில்","புண்கால் அறுபத்தெட்டு ஆக்கை புணர்கின்ற","நண்பால் உடம்பு தன் னால் உடம் பாமே."],"p":"","r":"In the body built to eight spans, in eye, foot and body and in the hands that flow, the sixty-eight bodies join, and by that union the body becomes a body of itself.","n":"எண்சாண் (eight spans) is the traditional measure of the human body, each by its own hand. புண்கால் and the sixty-eight are obscure, and the reading is tentative.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:159"},"t:2128":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2128,"t":["உடம்புக்கும் நாலுக்கும் உயிராகிய சீவன்","ஒடுங்கும் பரனோடு ஒழியாகப் பிரமம்","கடந்தொறும் நின்ற கணக்கது காட்டி","அடங்கியே அற்றது ஆரறி வாறே."],"p":"","r":"The soul that is life to the body and the four inner faculties subsides into the Supreme; Brahman, showing the measure that stands in each pot, is stilled and gone: who knows this?","n":"கடந்தொறும் (in each pot) recalls the Vedanta image of one space in many pots, the one Brahman in many bodies. நாலு is read as the four inner instruments. How the lines fit together is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["uyir","vedaantham","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:159"},"t:2129":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2129,"t":["ஆறுஅந்த மாகி நடுவுடன் கூடினால்","தேறிய மூவாறும் சிக்கென்று இருந்திடும்","கூறுங் கலைகள் பதினெட்டும் கூடியே","ஊறும் உடம்பை உயிருடம்பு எண்ணுமே."],"p":"","r":"When the six ends join with the middle, the clear three sixes hold firm; the eighteen kalas spoken of join together, and the life counts the welling body as its own body.","n":"ஆறு அந்தம் may be the six ends (the antas of Saiva doctrine) or 'the six as end'; மூவாறு and the eighteen kalas are counts whose content the verse does not give.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalai","thaththuvam","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:159"},"t:2130":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2130,"t":["மெய்யினில் தூல மிகுந்த முகத்தையும்","பொய்யினில் சூக்கம் பொருந்தும் உடலையும்","கையினில் துல்லியம் காட்டும் உடலையும்","ஐயன் அடிக்குள் அடங்கும் உடம்பே."],"p":"","r":"The body whose gross face abounds in flesh, the body in which the subtle sits in what is unreal, the body that shows the finest as if in the hand: all are bodies held within the Lord's feet.","n":"தூலம், சூக்கம், துல்லியம் name gross, subtle and finest bodies. மெய் and பொய் pun on body and truth, falsehood; the sense of the middle lines is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:159"},"t:2131":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2131,"t":["காயும் கடும்பரி கால்வைத்து வாங்கல் போல்","சேய இடம்அண்மை செல்லவும் வல்லது","காயத் துகிர் போர்வை ஒன்றுவிட்டு ஆங்குஒன்றிட்டு","ஏயும் அவரென்ன ஏய்ந்திடும் காயமே."],"p":"","r":"As a fierce galloping horse sets down one foot and lifts another, the body can go far and near; leaving one coat of flesh and putting on another there, it fits itself to whoever it becomes.","n":"The subject is read as the subtle body, which passes from one gross body to the next. The coat image (போர்வை) is the gross body worn and shed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","yaakkai","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:159"},"t:2132":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2132,"t":["நாகம் உடல்உரி போலும்நல் அண்டச","மாக நனாவில் கானாமறந் தல்லது","போகலும் ஆகும் அரன்அரு ளாலே சென்று","ஏகும் இடம்சென்று இருபயன் உண்ணுமே."],"p":"","r":"Like a snake leaving its slough, it may go, and by Hara's grace it goes to the place it must go and eats the two fruits.","n":"The two fruits are good and bad deeds' results, as tradition reads them. Line 2 (நனாவில் கானா மறந்து) seems to speak of waking and dream but is obscure and may be corrupt.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","vinai","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:159"},"t:2133":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2133,"t":["உண்டு நரக சுவர்கத்தில் உள்ளன","கண்டு விடும்சூக்கம் காரண மாச்செலப்","பண்டு தொடரப் பரகாய யோகிபோல்","பிண்டம் எடுக்கும் பிறப்பு இறப்பு எய்தியே."],"p":"","r":"Having eaten what is in hell and heaven, the subtle body leaves and goes on as the cause; with its old deeds following, like a yogi who enters another's body, it takes up a body and meets birth and death.","n":"பரகாய யோகி is the yogi with the power to enter another body, one of the attainments in the siddhi chapter; here it is a simile for the subtle body taking a new birth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","vinai","naragam","siththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:159"},"t:2134":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2134,"t":["தான்அவ னாகிய தற்பரம் தாங்கினோன்","ஆனவை மாற்றிப் பரமத்து அடைந்திடும்","ஏனை உயிர்வினைக்கு எய்தும் இடம்சென்றும்","வானும் நிலனும் புகுந்தும் வருந்துமே."],"p":"","r":"One who has borne the Supreme and become Him changes what has come and reaches the highest; other souls go to the place their deeds bring them and suffer, entering sky and earth.","n":"தான் அவன் ஆகிய (he who became He) states union plainly. ஆனவை மாற்றி may mean undoing the bodies and deeds that came to him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saayujjiyam","vinai","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:159"},"t:2135":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2135,"t":["ஞானிக்குக் காயம் சிவமாகும் நாட்டிடில்","ஞானிக்குக் காயம் உடம்பே அதுவாகும்","மேனிக்கும் யோகிக்கும் விந்துவும் நாதமும்","மோனிக்குக் காயம்முப் பாழ்கெட்ட முத்தியே."],"p":"","r":"For the knower, the body, once established, becomes Siva; for the knower the body itself becomes That; for the yogi it is bindu and nada; for the silent sage the body is release in which the three voids are destroyed.","n":"மேனி in line 3 may be another class of seeker or simply 'body'; முப்பாழ் (the three voids) is a technical term, traditionally taken as maya, the soul's state and the void beyond, but this is not fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","yogam","mounam","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:159"},"t:2136":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2136,"t":["விஞ்ஞானத் தோர்க்குஆ ணவமே மிகுதனு","எஞ்ஞானத் தோர்க்குத் தனுமாயை தான்என்ப","அஞ்ஞானத் தோர்க்குக் கன்மம் தனுவாகும்","மெஞ்ஞானத் தோர்க்குச் சிவதனு மேவுமே."],"p":"","r":"For the vijnanakalas the body is anava itself; for those of the next kind, maya, it is said, is the body; for those without knowledge, karma becomes the body; those of true knowledge take on a body of Siva.","n":"The classes follow 494: விஞ்ஞானர் (bound by anava alone), எஞ்ஞானர் (taken as the pralayakalas), அஞ்ஞானர் (the sakalas, bound by all three malas). Each one's 'body' is the impurity that holds it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["malam","pasu","gnaanam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:159"},"t:2137":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2137,"t":["மலமென்று உடம்பை மதியாத ஊமர்","தலமென்று வேறு தரித்தமை கண்டீர்","நலமென்று இதனையே நாடி இருக்கில்","பலமுள்ள காயத்தில் பற்றும்இவ் அண்டத்தே."],"p":"","r":"See the dull ones who, calling the body filth, do not value it and hold some other place to be sacred; if one seeks and holds to this body as good, one grasps this whole cosmos in the strong body.","n":"மலம் may be filth or the soul's impurity; தலம் என்று வேறு தரித்தமை is read as taking another place (a shrine, or another body) as holy. The last line rests on the body as microcosm (அண்டம்).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","malam","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:159"},"t:2138":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2138,"t":["நல்ல வசனத்து வாக்கு மனாதிகள்","மெல்ல விளையாடும் விமலன் அகத்திலே","அல்ல செவிசத்த மாதி மனத்தையும்","மெல்ல தரித்தார் முகத்தார் பசித்தே."],"p":"","r":"Speech, mind and the rest, in good utterance, play gently within the Stainless; the others, the ear, sound and the rest and the mind as well, those who have gently held them see Him, hungering.","n":"The verse is obscure and the reading a guess. It seems to set the faculties at play in the Lord against the senses gently held in check; முகத்தார் பசித்தே is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["manam","adakkam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:159"},"t:2139":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2139,"t":["பண்ணாக்கும் காமம் பயிலும் வசனமும்","விண்ணாம் பிராணன் விளங்கிய சத்தமும்","புண்ணாம் உடலில் பொருந்தும் மனத்தையும்","அண்ணாந்து பார்க்க அழியும் உடம்பே."],"p":"","r":"The desire that shapes a person, the speech one keeps up, the sound that shines in the breath, and the mind that clings to the sore-like body: when one looks upward, the body perishes.","n":"அண்ணாந்து பார்க்க carries the verse. Plainly it is the upturned gaze of the dying; in the yogic tradition it is taken as turning attention up to the crown, at which the body-bound faculties fall away. Both readings stand. பண்ணாக்கும் may mean 'that makes song' or 'that fashions'; புண்ணாம் உடல் calls the body a wound.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","kaamam","praanan","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:160"},"t:2140":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2140,"t":["அழிகின்ற ஓர் உடம்பு ஆகும் செவிகள்","கழிகின்ற காலவ் விரதங்கள் தானம்","மொழிகின்ற வாக்கு முடிகின்ற நாடி","ஒழிகின்ற ஊனுக்கு உறுதுணை இல்லையே."],"p":"","r":"The ears of the perishing body, the vows and gifts of passing time, the speech that is spoken and the nadis that come to their end: none of these is a sure support for the flesh that is passing away.","n":"The participles அழிகின்ற, கழிகின்ற, மொழிகின்ற, முடிகின்ற, ஒழிகின்ற march the verse toward its end. The verse says vows and gifts do not hold up the body (ஊன்); it does not say whether they help the soul.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","nilaiyaamai","eegai","naadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:160"},"t:2141":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2141,"t":["இலையாம் இடையில் எழுகின்ற காமம்","முலைவாய நெஞ்சத்து மூழ்கும் உளத்துத்","தலையாய மின்னுடல் தாங்கித் திரியும்","சிலையாய சித்தம் சிவமுன் இடைக்கே."],"p":"","r":"Desire rising at the leaf-slender waist and a mind sunk at the breast send one wandering, carrying a body brief as lightning; but the mind made firm as stone stands before Siva.","n":"மின்னுடல் may be the lightning-brief body or, in yogic reading, a body of light. சிலையாய சித்தம் may be a mind steady as stone or bent like a bow (சிலை is both), and சிவமுன் இடைக்கே is obscure, so the contrast drawn here is one reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","manam","yaakkai","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:160"},"t:2142":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2142,"t":["ஐஐந்து மத்திமை யானது சாக்கிரம்","கைகண்ட பன்னான்கில் கண்டம் கனாஎன்பர்","பொய்கண் டிலாத புருடன்இத யம் சுழுனை","மெய்கண் டவன் உந்தி ஆகும் துரியமே."],"p":"","r":"Waking, with its twenty-five, is at the middle place; dream, with its fourteen, they say is at the throat; deep sleep is in the heart of the purusha who sees no falsehood; and turiya is at the navel of the one who has seen the truth.","n":"மத்திமை is traditionally the brow. ஐஐந்து may be twenty-five or five and five, and the usual Siddhanta count for waking is thirty-five; சுழுனை here seems to stand for deep sleep (சுழுத்தி) rather than the channel sushumna.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","thaththuvam","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:161"},"t:2143":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2143,"t":["முப்பதோடு ஆறின் முதல்நனா ஐந்தாகச்","செப்புஅதில் நான்காய்த் திகழ்ந்திரண்டு ஒன்றாகி","அப்பதி யாகும் நியதி முதலாகச்","செப்பும் சிவம்ஈறாய்த் தேர்ந்துகொள் ளீரே."],"p":"","r":"Counting from the thirty-six, the states run from first waking as five, then four, then two and one; understand them as reaching from niyati up to Siva.","n":"The numbers (ஐந்து, நான்கு, இரண்டு, ஒன்று) are compressed and their objects are not named; நியதி and சிவம் are the ends of the tattva list, so the verse seems to map the states onto the tattvas. அப்பதி is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","thaththuvam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:161"},"t:2144":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2144,"t":["இந்தியம் ஈரைந்து ஈரைந்து மாத்திரை","மந்திர மாய்நின்ற மாருதம் ஈரைந்தும் அந்தக்","கரணம் ஒருநான்கும் ஆன்மாவும்","பந்தஅச் சாக்கரப் பாலது ஆகுமே."],"p":"","r":"The ten senses, their ten objects, the ten vital airs that stand as mantra, the four inner instruments and the soul make up the bound state of waking.","n":"The sum is thirty-five, the traditional count of waking's instruments. மந்திரமாய் நின்ற மாருதம் (breath standing as mantra) is usually linked to the unspoken mantra of the breath. பந்த names waking itself as bondage.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","thaththuvam","praanan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:161"},"t:2145":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2145,"t":["பாரது பொன்மை பசுமை உடையது","நீரது வெண்மை செம்மை நெருப்பது","காரது மாருதம் கறுப்பை உடையது","வானகம் தூமம் மறைந்துநின் றாரே."],"p":"","r":"Earth is golden, water white, fire red, air black and the sky smoke-coloured, and those who stand in them remain hidden.","n":"பசுமை (green) in line 1 has no clear element of its own; it may qualify earth or be a slip. The ones who stand hidden (மறைந்துநின்றார்) are traditionally the deities presiding over the elements.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:161"},"t:2146":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2146,"t":["பூதங்கள் ஐந்தும் பொறியவை ஐந்துளும்","ஏதம் படஞ்செய்து இருந்து புறநிலை","ஓதும் மலம்குணம் ஆகும்ஆ தாரமொடு","ஆதி அவத்தைக் கருவிதொண் ணூற்றாறே."],"p":"","r":"The five elements, the five senses, the outer tattvas that work harm, the malas, the gunas and the adharas: the instruments of the first state number ninety-six.","n":"Ninety-six is the larger Siddhanta count of the tattvas and their sub-parts (2179 calls it common to all). ஏதம் படஞ்செய்து is obscure; it may say the instruments cause harm or that they spread out.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","avaththai","malam","aadhaaram"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:161"},"t:2147":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2147,"t":["இடவகை சொல்லில் இருபத்தஞ்சு ஆனை","படுபர சேனையும் பாய்பரி ஐந்தும்","உடையவன் மத்திமை உள்ளுறும் நால்வர்","அடைய நெடுங்கடை ஐந்தொடு நான்கே."],"p":"","r":"Told by their places: twenty-five elephants, a great army, five leaping horses, the lord in the middle with four who dwell within, and at the long gate five with four.","n":"The body is pictured as a king's camp. Tradition takes the elephants as the tattvas, the horses as the senses or breaths, the lord as the soul and the four as the inner instruments; the closing five with four is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","thaththuvam","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:161"},"t:2148":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2148,"t":["உடம்பும் உடம்பும் உடம்பைத் தழுவி","உடம்பிடை நின்ற உயிரை அறியார்","உடம்பொடு உயிரிடை நட்புஅறி யாதார்","மடம்புகு நாய்போல் மயங்குகின் றாரே."],"p":"","r":"People cling to body after body and do not know the life that stands within the body; not knowing the bond between body and life, they wander bewildered like a dog that has strayed into a monastery.","n":"உடம்பும் உடம்பும் உடம்பைத் தழுவி may be bodies embracing in desire or the soul taking body after body. The phrase உடம்பொடு உயிரிடை நட்பு is the Kural's (338). The dog in the மடம் is a stray in a place not meant for it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uyir","yaakkai","pedhaimai","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:161"},"t:2149":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2149,"t":["இருக்கின்ற வாறுஒன்று அறிகிலர் ஏழைகள்","மருக்கும் அசபையை மாற்றி முகந்து","கருக்கொண்டு காமாரி சார முகந்தேர்ந்து","உருக்கொண்டு தொக்க உடல்ஒழி யாதே."],"p":"","r":"Fools do not know the one way things stand: if one turns back the ajapa breath, draws it up, holds it as a seed and turns toward Siva the foe of Kama, the body that has taken form will not perish.","n":"அசபை is the mantra said by the breath without utterance; மாற்றி (reversing) is taken as turning it back inward. மருக்கும் and கருக்கொண்டு are unclear. The promise that the body will not perish is the yogic claim of 724, not a statement about the soul.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["praanan","mandhiram","yaakkai","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:161"},"t:2150":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2150,"t":["ஒளித்திட்ட டிருக்கும் ஒருபதி னாலை","அளித்தவன் என்னுள்ளே ஆரியன் வந்து","அளிக்கும் கலைகளி னால்அறு பத்து","ஒளித்திட்டு வைத்தான் ஒடுங்கிய சித்தே."],"p":"","r":"The noble teacher who gave the fourteen came within me, and by the kalas he grants he hid away the sixty and set my mind in stillness.","n":"ஆரியன் is the guru. The fourteen may be the instruments of dream (2154); அறுபத்து (sixty) and ஒடுங்கிய சித்து are not explained in the verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","kalai","avaththai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:161"},"t:2151":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2151,"t":["மண்ணினில் ஒன்று மலர்நீரும் மருங்காகும்","பொன்னினில் அங்கி புகழ்வளி ஆகாயம்","மன்னும் மனோபுத்தி ஆங்காரம் ஓர்ஒன்றாய்","உன்னின் முடிந்தது ஒருபூத சயமே."],"p":"","r":"Earth, water, fire, air and sky, and the abiding mind, intellect and ego, each contemplated one by one: so mastery of the elements is won.","n":"பூதசயம் is bhuta-jaya, mastery of the elements, a yogic attainment. The words linked to each element (மலர், பொன்) may name their colours or seats; their exact sense is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","yogam","thiyaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:161"},"t:2152":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2152,"t":["முன்னிக்கு ஒருமகன் மூர்த்திக்கு இருவர்","வன்னிக்கு மூவர் வதுவைக்கு நால்வர்","கன்னிக்கு பிள்ளைகள் ஐவர் முனாள்இல்லை","கன்னியைக் கன்னியே காதலித் தானே."],"p":"","r":"One son to the first, two to the form, three to fire, four to the marriage, five children to the maiden, none before; and the maiden loved the maiden.","n":"A riddle. The counts one to five are traditionally taken as the qualities of the elements, from sky (sound alone) to earth (all five). The last line, a maiden loving a maiden, is obscure; it may speak of Shakti turning to herself.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:161"},"t:2153":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2153,"t":["கண்டன ஐந்தும் கலந்தனதான் ஐந்தும்சென்று","உண்டன நான்கும் ஒருங்கே உணர்ந்தபின்","பண்டைய தாகிப் பரந்து வியாக்கிரத்து","அண்டமும் தானாய் அமர்ந்துநின்றானே."],"p":"","r":"Once the five senses, the five objects they mingle with and the four inner instruments are understood together, he becomes as he was of old and abides spread out as the whole cosmos.","n":"It is unclear whether the subject is the soul regaining its original state or the Lord. வியாக்கிரத்து is obscure, perhaps for வியாபகம் (pervasion).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","thaththuvam","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:161"},"t:2154":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2154,"t":["நின்றவன் நிற்கப் பதினாலில் பத்துநீத்து","ஒன்றிய அங்தக் கரணங்கள் நான்குடன்","மன்று கலந்த மனைவாழ்க்கை வாதனை","கன்றிய கண்டத்தில் கண்டான் கனவதே."],"p":"","r":"As ten of the fourteen fall away and the four inner instruments remain, the soul at the throat sees dream, made from the impressions of household life.","n":"The ten that leave are taken as the outer senses, so dream works with the inner instruments alone. வாதனை (impressions) makes dream the replay of waking life. மன்று கலந்த is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","kanavu","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:161"},"t:2155":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2155,"t":["தானம் இழந்து தனிபுக்கு இதயத்து","மானம் அழிந்து மதிகெட்டு மாலாகி","ஆன விரிவுஅறி யாஅவ் வியத்தத்தில்","மேனி அழிந்து கழுத்தியது ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"Leaving its place and entering the heart alone, its self-regard gone, its understanding gone and dazed, in the unmanifest where it knows nothing spread out, the body forgotten, it is deep sleep.","n":"அவ்வியத்தம் is the unmanifest. கழுத்தியது, read here as deep sleep, looks like a misprint for சுழுத்தியது; it could also be read with கழுத்து (throat), which would not fit the heart of line 1.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:161"},"t:2156":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2156,"t":["கழுமுனையைச் சேர்ந்துள மூன்றுடன் காட்சி","கெழுமிய சித்தம் பிராணன்தன் காட்சி","ஒழுகக் கமலத்தின் உள்ளே யிருந்து","விழுமப் பொருளுடன் மேவிநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"In deep sleep three remain, the chitta, the breath and the soul's own seeing; dwelling within the lotus, the soul stands joined with the excellent reality.","n":"கழுமுனை may be deep sleep, as in 2142, or the channel sushumna. The lotus is traditionally the heart. விழுமப் பொருள் (the excellent thing) may be the Lord, present even in deep sleep.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","praanan","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:161"},"t:2157":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2157,"t":["தானத்து எழுந்து தருக்கு துரியத்தின்","வானத்து எழுந்துபோய் வையம் பிறகிட்டுக்","கானத்து எழுந்த கருத்தின் தலையிலே","ஊனத்து அவித்தைவிட்டு ஊமன்நின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"Rising from its place into the sky of turiya and leaving the world behind, at the summit of thought the soul casts off the ignorance of the flesh and stands silent as a mute.","n":"ஊமன் (the mute) gives the silence of the state beyond speech. கானத்து எழுந்த (risen in the forest, or in song) is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","mounam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:161"},"t:2158":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2158,"t":["Yஊமை எழுத்தொடு பேசும் எழுத்துறில்","ஆமை அகத்தினில் அஞ்சம் அடங்கிடும்","ஓமயம் உற்றுஅது உள்ளொளி பெற்றது","நாமயம் அற்றது நாம்அறி யோமே."],"p":"","r":"When the silent letter meets the spoken letter, the five draw in as a tortoise draws in its limbs; reaching Om it gains an inner light, and it is beyond names, beyond our knowing.","n":"ஊமை எழுத்து and பேசும் எழுத்து are traditionally the unspoken and spoken parts of a mantra, or bindu and nada. The tortoise is the Kural's image (126) for restraint of the senses; here the drawing-in follows the mantra rather than effort.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["piranavam","adakkam","mounam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:161"},"t:2159":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2159,"t":["துரியம் இருப்பதும் சாக்கிரத்து உள்ளே","நரிகள் பதினாலு நஞ்சுண்டு செத்தன","பரிய புரவியும் பாறிப் பறந்தது","துரியம் இறந்திடம் சொல்லஒண் ணாதே."],"p":"","r":"Turiya lies within waking itself; the fourteen jackals ate poison and died, the great horse bolted and was gone, and where turiya ends cannot be told.","n":"The fourteen jackals are taken as the fourteen instruments and the horse as the breath or mind. The first line makes turiya a state reached awake, not a separate sleep.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","mounam","praanan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:161"},"t:2160":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2160,"t":["மாறா மலம்ஐந்தால் மன்னும் அவத்தையில்","வேறாய மாயா தநுகர ணாதிக்குஇங்கு","ஈறாகா தேஎவ்வுயிரும் பிறந்துஇறந்து","ஆறாத வல்வினை யால்அடி யுண்ணுமே."],"p":"","r":"Held in the states by the five unyielding malas and given body and instruments from maya that are other than itself, every soul is born and dies without end and is beaten by strong deeds that do not abate.","n":"Tirumular counts five malas (2183 names anava first); tradition lists anava, karma, maya, the concealing power and mayeya. வேறாய stresses that body and instruments are not the soul.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["malam","piravi","vinai","maayai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:161"},"t:2161":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2161,"t":["உண்ணும்தன் ஊடாடது ஊட்டிடு மாயையும்","அண்ணல் அருள்பெற்ற முத்தியது ஆவது","நண்ணல் இலாஉயிர் ஞானத்தி னால்பிறந்து","எண்னுறு ஞானத்தின் நேர்முத்தி எய்துமே."],"p":"","r":"Maya makes the soul eat the fruit of its deeds without itself being part of the soul, and release comes by the Lord's grace: the soul, born through knowledge, reaches release directly through the knowledge it contemplates.","n":"Line 1 and நண்ணல் இலா உயிர் (the soul that does not approach, or the soul not yet near) are compressed. The verse joins grace and knowledge as the means of release without saying how they relate.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maayai","veedu","gnaanam","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:161"},"t:2162":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2162,"t":["அதிமூட நித்திரை ஆணவம் நந்த","அதனால் உணர்வோன் அருங்கன்மம் முன்னி","நிதமான கேவலம் இத்திறம் சென்று","பரமாகா ஐஅவத் தைப்படு வானே."],"p":"","r":"In the kevala state anava is a sleep of utter stupor; as it gives way, the knowing soul, prompted by its karma, leaves that steady kevala and, not yet the Supreme, falls into the five states.","n":"கேவலம் is the soul's state alone with anava, before embodiment. நந்த can mean to wane or to flourish, so whether anava weakens or prevails here is open; the reading takes it as waning.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["malam","avaththai","vinai","pasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:161"},"t:2163":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2163,"t":["ஆசான்முன் னேதுயில் மாண வகரைத்","தேசாய தண்டால் எழுப்பும் செயல்போல்","நேசாய ஈசனும் நீடுஆண வத்தரை","ஏசாத மாயாள்தன் னாலே எழுப்புமே."],"p":"","r":"As a teacher wakes with a shining rod the pupils sleeping in front of him, so the loving Lord wakes those long sunk in anava through blameless Maya.","n":"The simile makes maya the Lord's instrument of grace, not a fault: embodiment wakes the soul from the deeper sleep of anava. ஏசாத (blameless) defends maya against the charge the word often carries.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maayai","malam","pathi","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:161"},"t:2164":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2164,"t":["மஞ்சொடு மந்தா கினிகுட மாம்என","விஞ்சுஅறி வில்லோன் விளம்பும் மிகுமதி","எஞ்சலில் ஒன்றெனு மாறுஎன இவ்வுடல்","அஞ்சணும் மன்னன்அன் றேபோம் அளவே."],"p":"","r":"As the undiscerning speak of the moon seen in the clouds and in a pot of Ganga water as though it were many, though the great moon is one and undiminished, so with this body: its king departs on the day it goes.","n":"The moon reflected in many vessels is an old image for one reality seen in many bodies. The reading of line 4 (அஞ்சணும் மன்னன் அன்றே போம் அளவே) is a guess; whether the one is the soul or the Lord is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["uyir","yaakkai","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:161"},"t:2165":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2165,"t":["படியுடை மன்னவன் பாய்பரி ஏறி","வடிவுடை மாநகர் தான்வரும் போது","அடியுடை ஐவரும் அங்குஉறை வோரும்","துடியில்லம் பற்றித் துயின்றனர் தாமே."],"p":"","r":"When the king of the land rides his leaping horse into his fair city, the five who serve him and all who live there take to the drum-house and fall asleep.","n":"Traditionally the king is the soul, the horse the breath, the city the body and the five the senses, so the verse pictures the senses falling still as the soul withdraws inward. துடியில்லம் (house of the drum, or of the pulse) is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","uyir","praanan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:161"},"t:2166":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2166,"t":["நேரா மலத்தை நீடுஐந்து அவத்தையின்","நேரான வாறுஉன்னி நீடு நனவினில்","நேரா மலம்ஐந்தும் நேரே தரிசித்து","நேராம் பரத்துடன் நிற்பது நித்தமே."],"p":"","r":"Considering how the five stubborn malas work through the five long states, one who sees all five plainly while fully awake stands always with the Supreme.","n":"நேர் (straight, direct, facing) opens every line: the stubborn (நேரா) malas, seen directly (நேரே) in waking, give way to the true (நேராம்) Supreme. The verse places release in waking, not in withdrawal from it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["malam","avaththai","sivam","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:161"},"t:2167":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2167,"t":["சாக்கிர சாக்கிரம் தன்னில் திரோதாயி","சாக்கிர சொப்பனம் தன்னிடை மாமாயை","சாக்கிரம் தன்னில் அழுத்திதற் காமியம்","சாக்கிரம் தன்னில் துரியத்து மாயையே."],"p":"","r":"Within waking, waking-of-waking belongs to the concealing power, waking-dream to Mahamaya, the pressing stage of waking to one's karma, and waking-turiya to maya.","n":"திரோதாயி (the concealing power), மாமாயை, காமியம் (karma) and மாயை are four of the five malas or bonds; the verse names four sub-states. அழுத்தி is obscure, perhaps for deep sleep (சுழுத்தி) within waking.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","malam","maayai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:162"},"t:2168":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2168,"t":["மாயை எழுப்பும் கலாதியை மற்றதின்","நேய இராகாதி ஏய்ந்த துரியத்துத்","தோயும் கழுமுனை கனாநனா வும்துன்னி","ஆயினன் அந்தச் சகலத்துஉ ளானே."],"p":"","r":"Maya rouses kala and the tattvas after it, among them raga; passing through turiya, deep sleep, dream and waking, the soul comes to be in the sakala state.","n":"கலாதி and இராகாதி are the impure tattvas of kala, vidya, raga and the rest, which maya produces to rouse the soul. சகலம் is the embodied state between kevala and suddha.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maayai","thaththuvam","avaththai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:162"},"t:2169":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2169,"t":["மேவிய அந்த கண் விழிகண் குருடனாம்","ஆவயின் முன்அடிக் காணு மதுகண்டு","மேவும் தடிகொண்டு சொல்லும் விழிபெற","மூவயின் ஆன்மா முயலும் கருமமே."],"p":"","r":"Like a blind man whose eyes are open, who sees only the step ahead and goes feeling with a stick, the soul in its three states strives by its deeds.","n":"The image makes the embodied soul partly sighted: instruments let it see the next step only. சொல்லும் விழிபெற (to gain the eye that is spoken of) may hint at the guru's word as the cure; மூவயின் (three places) is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pasu","vinai","avaththai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:162"},"t:2170":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2170,"t":["மத்திமம் ஒத்த சிலந்தி வலயத்துள்","ஒத்துஅங் கிருந்து உயிருண்ணு மாறுபோல்","அத்தனும் ஐம்பொறி ஆடகத்துள் நின்று","சத்த முதல்ஐந்தும் தான்உண்ணு மாறே."],"p":"","r":"As a spider sits at the centre of its web and feeds on the life it catches, so the master sits in the golden house of the five senses and consumes the five beginning with sound.","n":"அத்தன் usually names the Lord, but the one who enjoys sense objects is in Siddhanta the soul; the verse can be read of either, or of the Lord as the soul's inner ruler.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pasu","thaththuvam","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:162"},"t:2171":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2171,"t":["வைச்சன வச்சு வகையிரு பத்தஞ்சும்","உச்சம் உடன்அணை வான்ஒரு வன்உளன்","பிச்சன் பெரியன் பிறப்பிலி என்றென்று","நச்சி அவனருள் நான்உய்ந்த வாறே."],"p":"","r":"Beyond the twenty-five tattvas set each in its place there is One who reaches to the summit; calling him the mad one, the great one, the unborn, and longing for him, I was saved by his grace.","n":"பிச்சன் (the mad one) is a loving epithet of Siva, the wandering beggar. The verse breaks the run of counting with personal devotion: the tattvas are named, but grace saves.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","sivam","thaththuvam","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:162"},"t:2172":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2172,"t":["நாலா றுடன்புருடன் நல்தத் துவமுடன்","வேறான ஐ ஐந்து மெய்ப்புரு டன்பரன்","கூறா வியோமம் பரம்எனக் கொண்டனன்","வேறான நாலேழு வேதாந்த தத்துவமே."],"p":"","r":"Twenty-four with purusha and its true tattva, twenty-five again with the true purusha and the Supreme, and taking the undivided void as the Supreme: so are counted the twenty-eight tattvas of Vedanta.","n":"The arithmetic is compressed; the verse seems to build the Vedanta count of twenty-eight from the Samkhya twenty-four and twenty-five by adding purusha, the Supreme and the void (வியோமம்).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","vedaantham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:162"},"t:2173":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2173,"t":["ஏலங்கொண்டு ஆங்கே இடையொடு பிங்கலை","கோலங்கொண்டு ஆங்கே குணத்துடன் புக்கு","மூலங்கொண்டு ஆங்கே முறுக்கிமுக் கோணிலும்","காலங்கொண் டான்அடி காணலும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"Taking up ida and pingala, entering with the gunas, and twisting at the root in the triangle, one may see the feet of the Lord who holds time.","n":"இடை and பிங்கலை are the left and right channels; மூலம் and முக்கோண் are the root centre and its triangle. ஏலம் is obscure, and காலம் கொண்டான் may be the master of time or the one who holds the breath (கால்).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naadi","aadhaaram","thiruvadi","praanan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:162"},"t:2174":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2174,"t":["நாடிகள் பத்தும் நலந்திகழ் வாயுவும்","ஓடிய காலில் ஒடுங்கி யிருந்திடும்","கூடிய காமம் குளிக்கும் இரதமும்","நாடிய நல்ல மனமும் உடலிலே."],"p":"","r":"The ten nadis and the vital airs settle in the running breath, and desire, the fluid that bathes, and the mind that seeks the good all lie within the body.","n":"இரதம் may be the body's essential fluid, often taken as bindu. The verse places breath, desire and mind together in the body, which the yogic chapters treat as the field of practice.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naadi","praanan","yaakkai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:162"},"t:2175":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2175,"t":["ஆவன ஆக அழிவ அழிவன","போவன போவ புகுவ புகுவன","காவலன் பேர்நந்தி காட்டித்துக் கண்டவன்","ஏவன செய்யும் இலங்கிழை யோனே."],"p":"","r":"What is to come comes, what is to perish perishes, what is to go goes and what is to enter enters; Nandi, the guardian, showed this and saw it, and the one with shining ornaments does as he bids.","n":"The doubled verbs state that things run their course. இலங்கிழையோன் (one with shining ornaments) may be Shakti or the soul; ஏவன செய்யும் (does what is commanded) is open as to who commands whom.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","nilaiyaamai","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:162"},"t:2176":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2176,"t":["பத்தொடு பத்துமோர் மூன்றும் பகுதியும்","உய்த்த துரியமும் உள்ளுணர் காலமும்","மெய்த்த வியோமமும் மேலைத் துரியமும்","தத்துவ நாலேழ் எனஉன்னத் தக்கதே."],"p":"","r":"Twenty-three with prakriti, turiya, the time known within, the true void and the higher turiya: so the twenty-eight tattvas are to be counted.","n":"This is a second reckoning of the twenty-eight of 2172; the added items differ. பத்தொடு பத்துமோர் மூன்றும் (ten and ten and three) with பகுதி gives twenty-four.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","vedaantham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:162"},"t:2177":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2177,"t":["விளங்கிடு முந்நூற்று முப்பதோடு ஒருபான்","தளங்கொள் இரட்டிய தாறு நடந்தால்","வணங்கிடும் ஐம்மலம் வாயு எழுந்து","விளங்கிடும் அவ்வழி தத்துவம் நின்றே."],"p":"","r":"If the breath runs in the measure of three hundred and thirty and ten, doubled in its seats, the five malas bow, the breath rises, and in that way the tattvas stand clear.","n":"The numbers are a breath count whose total is unclear; தளங்கொள் இரட்டியது ஆறு may be the six centres doubled. Only the claim that measured breath subdues the malas is plain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["praanan","malam","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:162"},"t:2178":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2178,"t":["நாலொரு கோடியே நாற்பத்தொண் ணாயிரம்","மேலுமோர் ஐந்நூறு வேறாய் அடங்கிடும்","பாலவை தொண்ணூறோடு ஆறுள் படும்அவை","கோலிய ஐ ஐந்துள் ஆகும் குறிக்கிலே."],"p":"","r":"The countless tattvas, numbered in crores and thousands and hundreds, are contained in the ninety-six, and those, when examined, in the twenty-five.","n":"The exact figure (நாலொரு கோடியே நாற்பத்தொண்ணாயிரம் மேலுமோர் ஐந்நூறு) cannot be fixed; நாற்பத்தொண் may be forty-one or forty-nine. The point is that the many reduce to the few.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:162"},"t:2179":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2179,"t":["ஆகின்ற தொண்ணூறோடு ஆறும் பொதுஎன்பர்","ஆகின்ற ஆறாறு அருஞ்சைவர் தத்துவம்","ஆகின்ற நாலேழ் வேதாந்தி வயிணவர்க்கு","ஆகின்ற நாலாறுஐ ஐந்துமாயா வாதிக்கே."],"p":"","r":"The ninety-six are called common to all; the thirty-six are the tattvas of the Saivas; the twenty-eight are for the Vedantins and Vaishnavas; the twenty-four and twenty-five are for the Mayavadins.","n":"The verse sets the schools' counts side by side without condemning any; அருஞ்சைவர் (the rare Saivas) marks the Saiva count as the one it values. The twenty-four and twenty-five are usually Samkhya numbers, here given to the Mayavadins.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","samayam","saivam","vedaantham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:162"},"t:2180":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2180,"t":["தத்துவ மானது தன்வழி நின்றிடில்","வித்தக னாகி விளங்கி யிருக்கலாம்","பொய்த்துவ மாம்அவை போயிடும் அவ்வழி","தத்துவம் ஆவது அகார எழுத்தே."],"p":"","r":"If the tattvas stay in their own course one may live wise and shining, and the false ones fall away; the tattva that is real is the letter A.","n":"பொய்த்துவம் plays on தத்துவம்: the false against the real. The letter A (அகாரம்) is traditionally Siva, as the first sound in all letters and in Om.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","gnaanam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:162"},"t:2181":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2181,"t":["அறிவொன் றிலாதன ஐஏழும் ஒன்றும்","அறிகின்ற என்னை அறியாது இருந்தேன்","அறிகின்றாய் நீஎன்று அருள்செய்தார் நந்தி","அறிகின்ற நானென்று அறிந்து கொண்டேனே."],"p":"","r":"The thirty-five and one are without knowledge; I sat not knowing myself, the one who knows, until Nandi graciously said 'you are the one who knows', and I came to know that I am the knower.","n":"ஐஏழும் ஒன்றும் (five sevens and one) are the thirty-six tattvas, inert (அறிவொன்றிலாதன). The verse repeats அறி in every line: the soul is set apart from the tattvas as the knower, and learns it only from the guru's word.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["guru","pasu","meyyunarvu","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:162"},"t:2182":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2182,"t":["சாக்கிர சாக்கிர மாதி தனில்ஐந்தும்","ஆக்கும் மலாவத்தை ஐந்து நனவாதி","போக்கி இவற்றொடும் பொய்யான ஆறாறு","நீக்கி நெறிநின்றுஒன்று ஆகியே நிற்குமே."],"p":"","r":"Putting away the five states from waking-of-waking, the five mala-states from waking that they produce, and with them the false thirty-six, keeping to the path, the soul stands as one.","n":"ஒன்றாகியே நிற்கும் (stands having become one) is read here as union; the verse does not name with what.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","malam","thaththuvam","saayujjiyam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:162"},"t:2183":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2183,"t":["ஆணவ மாதி மலம்ஐந்து அவரோனுக்கு","ஆணவ மாதிநான் காம்மாற்கு அரனுக்கு","ஆணவ மாதிமூன்று ஈசர்க்கு இரண்டென்ப","ஆணவம் ஒன்றே சதாசிவற்கு ஆவதே."],"p":"","r":"The five malas from anava belong to the lowest beings, four to Mal, three to Haran, two to the Isvaras, and anava alone to Sadasiva.","n":"அவரோனுக்கு is obscure (perhaps the lowest, or a slip for Brahma), and நான்காம் மாற்கு is read as நான்கு ஆம் மாற்கு. The grading makes the gods themselves bound souls of higher rank, as Siddhanta holds.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["malam","pasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:162"},"t:2184":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2184,"t":["தத்துவம் ஆறாறு தன்மனு ஏழ்கோடி","மெய்த்தரு வன்னம்ஐம் பான்ஒன்று மேதினி","ஒத்துஇரு நூற்றுஇரு பான்நான்குஎண் பான்ஒன்று","வைத்த பதம்கலை ஓர்ஐந்தும் வந்தவே."],"p":"","r":"Thirty-six tattvas, seven crore mantras, fifty-one letters, two hundred and twenty-four worlds, eighty-one words and five kalas: so the paths came to be.","n":"The numbers match the six adhvas of Saiva Agamic tradition: tattva, mantra (மனு), varna (வன்னம்), bhuvana (மேதினி), pada (பதம்) and kala. The verse lists them without the word அத்துவா, which is the chapter title.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aththuvaa","thaththuvam","mandhiram","kalai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:163"},"t:2185":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2185,"t":["நாடிய மண்டலம் மூன்றும் நலந்தெரிந்து","ஓடும் அவரோடு உள்இரு பத்துஐஞ்சும்","கூடுவர் கூடிக் குறிவழி யேசென்று","தேடிய பின்னர்த் திகைத்திருந் தார்களே."],"p":"","r":"Those who study the three regions well, run along with the twenty-five within, gather together and follow the path by its marks are left bewildered after all their searching.","n":"மண்டலம் மூன்றும் is traditionally taken as the regions of fire, sun and moon in the body, and இருபத்துஐஞ்சு as the twenty-five tattvas. Whether the bewilderment is failure or the stunned arrival of the search is not plain; திகைத்திருந்தார் allows both.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aththuvaa","thaththuvam","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:163"},"t:2186":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2186,"t":["சாக்கிர சாக்கிர மாதித் தலையாக்கி","ஆக்கிய தூலம் அளவாக்கி அதீதத்துத்","தாக்கிய அன்பான தாண்டவம் சார்ந்துஅது","தேக்கும் சிவமாதல் ஐந்தும் சிவாயவே."],"p":"","r":"Beginning from waking-within-waking and taking the gross body as the measure, one passes into the beyond and reaches the dance of love, becoming Siva, and then all five are Sivaya.","n":"ஐந்தும் சிவாயவே is traditionally taken as the five letters நமசிவாய losing ந and ம (taken as the malas) so that only சிவாய remains; the five may also be the five states. தாக்கிய (that strikes) with தாண்டவம் is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thirukkoothu","panchaakkaram","avaththai","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:163"},"t:2187":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2187,"t":["நானவாதி தூலமே சூக்கப் பகுதி","அனதான ஐஐந்தும் விந்துவின் சத்தி","தனதாம் உயிர்விந்து தான்நின்று போந்து","கனவா நனவில் கலந்ததுஇவ் வாறே."],"p":"","r":"The gross states beginning with waking and subtle prakriti, the twenty-five, are the power of bindu; the soul's own bindu stands and goes forth, and in this way dream mingles into waking.","n":"நனவாதி (waking and the rest) opens the chapter's subject. ஐஐந்து (five fives) is taken as the twenty-five lower tattvas; how bindu, the soul and the states fit together here is not clear from the verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","vindhu","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:164"},"t:2188":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2188,"t":["நனவில் அதீதம் பிறந்தார் கிடந்தார்","நனவில் துரியம் நிகழ்ந்தார் தவழ்ந்தார்","நனவில் சுழுத்தி நடந்தார் வளர்ந்தார்","நனவில் கனவுஓட நன்செய்தி யானதே."],"p":"","r":"Within waking, those in the beyond-state are like the newborn lying still, those in turiya like infants crawling, those in deep sleep like children walking and growing; when dream runs within waking, the work is done well.","n":"The verse seems to match the states within waking to a child's growth, lying, crawling, walking. The direction of the comparison, and what நன்செய்தி (good affair) means in the last line, are uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:164"},"t:2189":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2189,"t":["செறியுங் கிரியை சிவதத் துவமாம்","பிறிவில் சுகயோகம் பேரருள் கல்வி","குறிதல் திருமேனி குணம்பல வாகும்","அறிவில் சராசரம் அண்டத் தளவே."],"p":"","r":"Close-knit ritual is the Siva tattva; with it come undivided blissful yoga, great grace and learning, the sacred form one fixes on with its many qualities, and in knowledge all that moves and stays, as far as the cosmos.","n":"The verse lists without connecting words, so how கிரியை, சுகயோகம், பேரருள் and கல்வி relate is a guess. It may describe what the pure (சுத்த) states open on to.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kiriyai","thaththuvam","yogam","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:164"},"t:2190":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2190,"t":["ஆதி பரஞ்சிவம் சத்தி சதாசிவம்","ஏதம்இல் ஈசன்நல் வித்தியா தத்துவம்","போதம் கலைகாலம் நியதிமா மாயை","நீதிஈ றாக நிறுத்தினன் என்னே."],"p":"","r":"From the first, the Lord set the tattvas in place: supreme Siva, Shakti, Sadasiva, flawless Isa, pure vidya, then bodha, kala, time, niyati and mahamaya, down to the last.","n":"The list runs from the five pure tattvas (Siva, Shakti, Sadasiva, Isvara, Suddhavidya) to the impure ones begun by maya. போதம் and நீதி are not standard names in this list; நீதி ஈறாக may mean 'down to the end, in order'. என்னே may be wonder or 'what is this'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","sivam","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:164"},"t:2191":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2191,"t":["தேச திகழ்சிவம் சத்தி சதாசிவம்","ஈசன் அனல்வித்தை இராகம் கலைகாலம்","மாசகல் வித்தை நியதி மகாமாயை","ஆசில் புருடாதி ஆன்மாஈ றாறே."],"p":"","r":"Siva shining in radiance, Shakti, Sadasiva, Isa, the fiery vidya, raga, kala, time, stainless vidya, niyati, mahamaya, and the flawless purusha and the rest, with the soul at the end, make thirty-six.","n":"ஆறாறு (six sixes) is the standard count of thirty-six tattvas. The order differs from 2190 and from the usual Siddhanta order, and அனல்வித்தை and மாசகல் வித்தை look like two vidyas, pure and impure; ஆன்மா ஈறு may be the soul at the end of the list or the atma tattvas.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","sivam","pasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:164"},"t:2192":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2192,"t":["ஆணவம் மாயையும் கன்மமும் ஆம்மலம்","காணும் முளைக்குத் தவிடுஉமி ஆன்மாவும்","தாணுவை ஒவ்வாமல் தண்டுலமாய் நிற்கும்","பேணுவாய் மற்றுநின் பாசம் பிரித்தே."],"p":"","r":"Anava, maya and karma are the malas; as bran and husk cover the sprouting grain, the soul stands as the rice, unlike Sthanu who has none: cherish it by separating your bonds.","n":"The rice image is a classic of Siddhanta: the husk and bran are with the grain from the start, as the malas are with the soul, yet are not the grain and can be removed. தாணுவை ஒவ்வாமல் sets the bound soul apart from Siva, who is free of them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["malam","paasam","pasu","maayai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:164"},"t:2193":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2193,"t":["பசுக்கள் பலவண்ணம் பாலொரு வண்ணம்","பசுக்களை மேய்க்கின்ற ஆயன் ஒருவண்ணம்","பசுக்களை மேய்க்கின்ற ஆயன்கோல் போடில்","பசுக்கள் தலைவனைப் பற்றி விடாவே."],"p":"","r":"The cows are of many colours but their milk is of one colour, and the herdsman who grazes them is one; if the herdsman lays down his staff, the cows will not let go of their master.","n":"பசு means both cattle and the bound soul, so the verse is read of souls, many in form and one in nature, and of the one Lord who tends them. கோல் போடில் may mean laying the staff aside or striking with it; traditionally it is the Lord's act of grace, after which the souls cling to him.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pasu","pathi","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:164"},"t:2194":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2194,"t":["உடல்இந் தியம்மனம் ஒண்புத்தி சித்தம்","அடலொன்று அகந்தை அறியாமை மன்னிக்","கெடும்அவ் வுயிர்மயல் மேலும் கிளைத்தால்","அடைவது தான்ஏழ் நரகத்து ளாயே."],"p":"","r":"Body, senses, mind, bright intellect, chitta, the mighty ego and ignorance remaining, that soul perishes; and if its delusion branches further, it reaches the seven hells.","n":"The list is the soul's instruments, ending in அறியாமை, which here stands for the mala of ignorance. மயல் (delusion) that grows is what leads down; the verse does not say the instruments themselves are bad.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","malam","naragam","pasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:164"},"t:2195":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2195,"t":["தன்தெரியாத அதீதம்தற்கு ஆணவம்","சொல்தெரி கின்ற துரியம்சொல் காமியம்","பெற்ற சுழுத்திப்பின் பேசுறும் காதலாம்","மற்றது உண்டிக் கனவுநன வாதலே."],"p":"","r":"The beyond-state that does not know itself is anava; turiya, where words are known, is karma; deep sleep that follows is the desire spoken of; and the rest, of food, is dream and waking.","n":"The verse seems to match the states to the malas: atita to anava, turiya to காமியம் (karma), sushupti to desire, and dream and waking to the grosser bonds. உண்டி (food) in the last line is obscure; the matching is the tradition's reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","malam","maayai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:164"},"t:2196":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2196,"t":["நனவில் கனவுஇல்லை ஐந்து நனவில்","கனவிலாச் சூக்குமம் காணும் சுழுத்தி","தனலுண் பகுதியே தற்கூட்டும் மாயை","நனவில் துரியம் அதீதம் தலைவந்தே."],"p":"","r":"In waking there is no dream; in the five of waking, the subtle without dream is seen as deep sleep; prakriti that eats fire, and maya that gathers itself; in waking, turiya and atita come to the fore.","n":"Each line is compressed and the syntax uncertain. தனலுண் பகுதி (prakriti that eats fire) is obscure and may be corrupt.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","maayai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:164"},"t:2197":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2197,"t":["ஆறாறில் ஐஐந்து அகல நனாநனா","ஆறாம் அவைவிட ஆகும் நனாக்கனா","வேறான ஐந்தும் விடவே நனாவினில்","ஈறாம் சுழுத்தி இதில்மாயை தானே."],"p":"","r":"When twenty-five of the thirty-six withdraw, there is waking in waking; when six more leave, waking in dream; when the other five also go, at the end of waking is deep sleep; in this, there is maya alone.","n":"The counts follow the tradition that each state is marked by fewer tattvas in play. The arithmetic and which tattvas are meant are not stated, and the states named may be read differently.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","thaththuvam","maayai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:164"},"t:2198":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2198,"t":["மாயையில் வந்த புருடன் துரியத்தில்","ஆய முறைவிட்டு அதுவும்தான் அன்றாகிச்","சேயதே வலவித் துடன் செல்லச் சென்றக்கால்","ஆய தனுவின் பயனில்லை யாமே."],"p":"","r":"The purusha who came in maya, in turiya, leaving the order that arose and becoming not even that, goes far off with the kevala seed; then the body that was taken has no use.","n":"சேயதே வலவித்துடன் is obscure; read as கேவல வித்து, the seed of the kevala state, it fits the next chapter's subject, but the reading is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pasu","avaththai","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:164"},"t:2199":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2199,"t":["அதீதத் துரியத்து அறிவனாம் ஆன்மா","அதீதத் துரியம் அதனால் புரிந்தால்","அதீதத்து எழுந்து அறிவாகிய மானன்","முதிய அனலில் துரியத்து முற்றுமே."],"p":"","r":"The soul is the knower in atita and turiya; if it practises atita-turiya by that, the one who rose in atita and became knowledge is completed in turiya, in the ripe fire.","n":"மானன் and முதிய அனல் (ripe fire) are obscure. The verse seems to say the soul, as knower, matures through the higher states.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","pasu","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:164"},"t:2200":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2200,"t":["ஐஐந்து பத்துடன் ஆனது சாக்கிரம்","கைகண்ட ஐஐந்தில் கண்டம் கனாஎன்பர்","பொய்கண்ட மூவர் புருடன் சுழுனையின்","மெய்கண்டவன்உந்தி மேவல் இருவரே."],"p":"","r":"Waking has twenty-five with ten; dream, with the twenty-five at hand, is in the throat, they say; the three who saw falsehood, with purusha, are deep sleep; the one who saw the truth, settled in the navel, is two.","n":"This is a variant of 2142: the states descend through places in the body (brow, throat, heart, navel) with fewer instruments in each. சுழுனையின் may be சுழுத்தி (deep sleep) or சுழுமுனை (the central channel). The counts differ from 2142.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","thaththuvam","aadhaaram"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:164"},"t:2201":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2201,"t":["புரியட் டகமே பொருந்தல் நனவு","புரியட் டகந்தன்னில் மூன்று கனவு","புரியட் டகத்தில் இரண்டு கழுத்தி","புரியட் டகத்தொன்று புக்கல் துரியமே."],"p":"","r":"Union with the eightfold subtle body is waking; three in it is dream; two in it is deep sleep; entering one in it is turiya.","n":"புரியட்டகம் is the subtle body of eight (the five subtle elements with mind, intellect and ego, as tradition counts it). The numbers mark how much of it stays active in each state.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","yaakkai","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:164"},"t:2202":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2202,"t":["நனவில் நனவு புனலில் வழக்கம்","நனவிற் கனவு நினைத்தல் மறத்தல்","நனவிற் சுழுத்திஉள் நாடல் இலாமை","நனவில் துரியம் அதீதத்து நந்தியே."],"p":"","r":"Waking in waking is the ordinary run of things, like flowing water; dream in waking is remembering and forgetting; deep sleep in waking is not searching within; turiya in waking is Nandi in the beyond.","n":"From here to 2206 each state is divided into the states within it. புனலில் வழக்கம் may mean the habitual flow of life or of the waters of the body; the reading is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:164"},"t:2203":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2203,"t":["கனவில் நனவுபோல் காண்டல் நனவாம்","கனவினில் கண்டு மறத்தல் கனவாம்","கனவில் சுழுத்தியும் காணாமை காணல்","அனுமாதி செய்தலில் ஆன துரியமே."],"p":"","r":"Seeing in dream as if awake is waking in dream; seeing in dream and forgetting is dream in dream; seeing not-seeing is deep sleep in dream; and turiya comes in inferring and the like.","n":"அனுமாதி (inference and the other means of knowing) attached to turiya is unexpected; the line may be corrupt or mean that judging what was dreamt is the highest level within dream.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:164"},"t:2204":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2204,"t":["சுழுத்தி நனவுஒன்றும் தோன்றாமை தோன்றல்","சுழுத்தி கனவுஅதன் உண்மை சுழுத்தியில்","சுழுத்தி அறிவுஅறி வாலே அழிகை","சுழுத்தித் துரியமாம் சொல்லறும் பாழே."],"p":"","r":"Waking in deep sleep is the appearing that nothing appears; dream in deep sleep is its truth in deep sleep; deep sleep in deep sleep is knowledge destroyed by knowledge; turiya in deep sleep is a void beyond words.","n":"பாழ் (void, waste) is a term Tirumular uses for states beyond form. The lines read as pointers rather than definitions.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:164"},"t:2205":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2205,"t":["துரிய நனவாம் இதமுணர் போதம்","துரியக் கனவாம் அகமுணர் போதம்","துரியச் சுழுத்தி வியோமம் துரியம்","துரியம் பரமெனத் கோன்றிடும் தானே."],"p":"","r":"Waking in turiya is the awareness that knows what is good; dream in turiya is the awareness that knows the inner; deep sleep in turiya is the void; turiya in turiya appears itself as the supreme.","n":"இதம் may be 'the good' or 'this, the present'. வியோமம் (space, void) is a technical term for the pure expanse. தோன்றிடும் is printed கோன்றிடும்.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","gnaanam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:164"},"t:2206":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2206,"t":["அறிவுஅறி கின்ற அறிவு நனவாம்","அறிவுஅறி யாமை அடையக் கனவாம்","அறிவுஅறி அவ்அறி யாமை சுழுத்தி","அறிவுஅறி வாகும் ஆன துரியமே."],"p":"","r":"Knowledge knowing knowledge is waking; knowledge reaching unknowing is dream; that unknowing of knowledge is deep sleep; knowledge becoming knowledge of knowledge is turiya.","n":"The verse plays on அறிவு and அறியாமை in every line, so the sense rests on wordplay. It reads as the four states seen as states of the soul's knowing.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","arivu","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:164"},"t:2207":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2207,"t":["தான் எங்கும் ஆயவன் ஐம்மலம் தான்விட்டு","ஞானம் தனதுரு வாகி நயந்தபின்","தான்எங்கு மாய்நெறி நின்றது தான்விட்டு","மேல்நந்தச் சூக்கம் அவைவண்ணம் மேலிட்டே."],"p":"","r":"He who is everywhere, having left the five malas, with knowledge as his form, after delighting in it, leaves even the way of being everywhere and rises above, beyond the subtle and its hues.","n":"ஐம்மலம் (five malas) extends the usual three. Whether தான் is the freed soul or the Lord is unclear; read here of the soul whose pervasiveness was hidden by the malas.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["malam","gnaanam","pasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:164"},"t:2208":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2208,"t":["ஐஐந்தும் ஆறும்ஓர் ஐந்தும் நனாவினில்","எய்யும் நனவு கனவு சுழுத்தியாம்","மெய்யும்பின் சூக்கமும் மெய்ப்பகுதி மாயை","ஐயமும் தான்அவன் அத்துரி யத்தனே."],"p":"","r":"Twenty-five, six and five more are in waking, aimed at as waking, dream and deep sleep; body, then the subtle, true prakriti, maya and doubt: he is one in that turiya.","n":"The counts echo 2197. அவன் அத்துரியத்தன் may say that the soul beyond these is in turiya, or that the Lord is; the verse does not settle it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","thaththuvam","maayai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:164"},"t:2209":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2209,"t":["ஈதென்று அறிந்திலன் இத்தனை காலமும்","ஈதென்று அறிந்தபின் ஏதும் அறிந்திலேன்","ஈதென்று அறியும் அறிவை அறிந்தபின்","ஈதென்று அறியும் இயல்புடை யோனே."],"p":"","r":"All this time he did not know what this is; after knowing it he knew nothing else; after knowing the knowledge that knows 'this is it', he is one whose nature is to know it.","n":"ஈதென்று (as 'this') opens every line. The verse moves from first person (அறிந்திலேன்) to third (இயல்புடையோனே), which may be the speaker describing himself or the knower in general. Knowing it ends other knowing: the knower becomes what knows.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","arivu","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:164"},"t:2210":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2210,"t":["உயிர்க்குஉயி ராகி உருவாய் அருவாய்","அயல்புணர் வாகி அறிவாய்ச் செறிவாய்","நயப்புறு சத்தியும் நாதன் உலகாதி","இயற்பின்றி எல்லாம் இருண்மூட மாமே."],"p":"","r":"As life of life, with form and without, as union with others, as knowledge and as fullness, the loving Shakti and the Lord are the world and all; without them, all is the delusion of darkness.","n":"இருண்மூடம் (dark delusion) is read as the soul's state under anava without the Lord's presence. இயற்பின்றி may mean 'without their nature' or 'without their working'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","pathi","uyir","malam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:164"},"t:2211":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2211,"t":["சத்தி இராகத்தில் தான்நல் உயிராகி","ஒத்துறி பாச மலம்ஐந்தோடு ஆறாறு","தத்துவ பேதம் சமைத்துக் கருவியும்","வைத்தனன் ஈசன் மலம்அறு மாறே."],"p":"","r":"Making the soul good through the power in raga, the Lord fashioned the fitting bonds, the five malas and the thirty-six distinctions of tattva, and set the instruments, so that the malas may be cut.","n":"The second half states a Siddhanta teaching: bodies and instruments are given not as further bondage but so that, working through them, the soul can be rid of mala. The first line, சத்தி இராகத்தில், is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","malam","pathi","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:164"},"t:2212":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2212,"t":["சாக்கிரா தீதத்தில் ஆணவம் தன்னுண்மை","சாக்கிரா தீதம் துரியத்தில் தானுறச்","சாக்கிரா தீதத்தில் ஆணவம் தான்விடாச்","சாக்கிரா தீதம் பரன்உண்மை தங்குமே."],"p":"","r":"In waking-atita anava is in its own truth; when waking-atita reaches turiya, and anava does not leave in waking-atita, the truth of the Supreme abides in waking-atita.","n":"சாக்கிராதீதம் opens every line. Tradition distinguishes the atita of the bound soul, a blank under anava, from the atita of the freed, the Supreme's presence. The third line (ஆணவம் தான்விடா) seems to contradict this reading and may be meant as 'when anava has gone'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","malam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:164"},"t:2213":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2213,"t":["மலக்கலப் பாலே மறைந்தது சத்தி","மலக்கலப் பாலே மறைந்தது ஞானம்","மலக்கலப் பாலே மறைந்தனன் தாணு","மலக்கலப்பு அற்றால் மதியொளி யாமே."],"p":"","r":"By the mixing of mala, Shakti was hidden, knowledge was hidden, Sthanu was hidden; when the mixing of mala ends, there is the light of the moon.","n":"மலக்கலப்பு (mixture with mala) opens three lines, each naming something hidden. மதியொளி is the moon's light, or the light of understanding; both readings stand.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["malam","saththi","gnaanam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:164"},"t:2214":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2214,"t":["திகைக்கின்ற சிந்தையுள் சிங்கங்கள் மூன்று","நகைக்கின்ற நெஞ்சுள் நரிக்குட்டி நான்கு","வகைக்கின்ற நெஞ்சினுள் ஆனைக்கன்று ஐந்து","பகைக்கின்ற நெஞ்சுக்குப் பாலிரண் டாமே."],"p":"","r":"In the bewildered mind are three lions, in the mocking heart four fox cubs, in the dividing heart five elephant calves, and to the hostile heart the milk is two.","n":"The animals are symbolic and the verse does not gloss them. Commonly the three lions are taken as the three malas, the four foxes as the four inner instruments, and the five elephant calves as the five senses. பாலிரண்டு (milk, or share, of two) is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["manam","malam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:164"},"t:2215":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2215,"t":["கதறு பதினெட்டுக் கண்களும் போகச்","சிதறி எழுந்திடுஞ் சிந்தையை நீரும்","விதறு படாமுன்னம் மெய்வழி நின்றால்","அதிர வருவதோர் ஆனையும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"Before the mind, scattering and rising as the eighteen roaring eyes leave, is shaken, if you stand in the true way, there will be an elephant coming with a roar.","n":"பதினெட்டுக் கண்கள் (eighteen eyes) is unexplained; it may be a count of faculties or of sects. ஆனையும் ஆமே can mean that one becomes like a charging elephant in strength, or that the elephant (the senses or mind) is won; both readings stand.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["manam","adakkam","sanmaarkkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:164"},"t:2216":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2216,"t":["நனவகத் தேயொரு நாலைந்தும் வீடக்","கனவகத் தேஉள் கரணங்க ளோடு","முனவகத் தேநின்று உதறியுட் புக்கு","நினைவகத்து இன்றிச் சுழுத்திநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"In waking, twenty fall away; in dream, with the inner instruments; standing at the front, he shook them off and entered within, and without thought stood in deep sleep.","n":"நாலைந்து (four fives) is the twenty, presumably tattvas. முனவகம் is obscure and may be corrupt. The verse describes the soul passing inward through the states as it drops its instruments.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:164"},"t:2217":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2217,"t":["நின்றவன் ஆசான் நிகழ்துரி யத்தனாய்","ஒன்றி உலகின் நியமாதிகள் உற்றுச்","சென்று துரியாதீ தத்தே சிலகாலம்","நின்று பரனாய் நின்மல னாமே."],"p":"","r":"He who stood so, the teacher, being in turiya, united, taking on the world's rules of discipline, went to the turiya-atita and staying there a while became the Supreme, the stainless one.","n":"ஆசான் may be the soul as master of itself or the guru; நியமாதி is the yogic niyama and the limbs after it. சிலகாலம் (a while) suggests a stage rather than an end.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","guru","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:164"},"t:2218":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2218,"t":["ஆனஅவ் ஈசன் அதீதத்தில் வித்தையாத்","தான்உலகு உண்டு சதாசிவ மாசத்தி","மேனிகள் ஐந்தும்போல் விட்டுச் சிவமாகி","மோனம் அடைந்தொளி மூலத் னாமே."],"p":"","r":"That Isa, as vidya in the atita, swallows the world; leaving Sadasiva, the great Shakti and the five forms, he becomes Siva, reaches silence, and is the light of the root.","n":"The verse may describe the soul rising through the pure tattvas (vidya, Isvara, Sadasiva, Shakti) to Siva. உலகுஉண்டு (having eaten the world) and மூலத்தன் are read here as absorbing the world and being at the source.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","mounam","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:164"},"t:2219":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2219,"t":["மண்டலம் மூன்றினுள் மாயநன் நாடனைக்","கண்டுகொண்டு உள்ளே கருதிக் கழிகின்ற","விண்டவர் தாமரை மேலொன்றும் கீழாக","அண்டமும் தானாய் அகத்தினுள் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"Those who have seen within the three regions the good lord of the realm of maya, and pass on contemplating him inwardly, open out; on the lotus, the one above is below, and the cosmos itself is within.","n":"மண்டலம் மூன்று are traditionally the regions of fire, sun and moon in the body. The syntax of the second and third lines is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","aadhaaram","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:164"},"t:2220":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2220,"t":["போதறி யாது புலம்பின புள்ளினம்","மாது அறி யாவகை நின்று மயங்கின","வேதுஅறி யாவணம் நின்றனன் எம்இறை","சூதறி வாருச்சி சூடிநின் றாரே."],"p":"","r":"The flock of birds cried, not knowing the time; women stood confused, not knowing; our Lord stood so that none could know him; those who know the secret wear him on their crown.","n":"The birds and women may stand for the senses and their objects, or be plain images of not-knowing. வேதுஅறியா may be வேதம் அறியா (the Vedas not knowing). சூது (secret, subtlety) and உச்சி (crown) may point to the crown of the head in yoga.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:164"},"t:2221":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2221,"t":["கருத்தறிந்து ஒன்பது கண்டமும் ஆங்கே","பொருந்தறிந் தேன்புவ னாபதி நாடித்","திருத்தறிந் தேன்மிகு தேவர் பிரானை","பருத்தறிந் தேன்மனம் மன்னிநின் றேனே."],"p":"","r":"Knowing the intent, I saw the nine regions fit together there; seeking the Lord of the worlds I knew the great Lord of the gods truly, knew him in his fullness, and stood with mind fixed.","n":"ஒன்பது கண்டம் may be the nine parts of the earth or nine places in the body. The verse is a first-person testimony, its repeated அறிந்தேன் building to the mind standing still.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","manam","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:164"},"t:2222":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2222,"t":["ஆன விளக்கொளி தூண்டும் அவன் என்னத்","தான விளக்கொளி யாம்மூல சாதனத்து","ஆன விதிமூலத் தானத்தில் அவ்விளக்கு","ஏனை மதிமண்ட லம்கொண்டு எரியுமே."],"p":"","r":"As he who kindles the lamp's flame, the lamp's flame is the root practice; in the place of the root, by the rule there, that lamp burns, taking the other, the moon region.","n":"மூல சாதனம் and மூலத்தானம் point to the muladhara. The lamp lit at the root and fed from the moon region above is traditionally read as the fire of kundalini meeting the moon's nectar; that reading is the tradition's.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","aadhaaram","kundalini"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:164"},"t:2223":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2223,"t":["உள்நாடும் ஐவர்க்கு மண்டை ஓதுங்கிய","விண்நாட நின்ற வெளியை வினவுறில்","அண்ணாந்து பார்த்துஐவர் கூடிய சந்தியில்","கண்நாடி காணும் கருத்ததுஎன் றானே."],"p":"","r":"If you ask about the open space where the skull of the five who seek inward is gathered, he said: look upward, and at the junction where the five meet the eye's glass sees; that is the intent.","n":"The five (ஐவர்) are probably the senses drawn inward. அண்ணாந்து பார்த்து (looking up) and the சந்தி (junction) are read as the gaze to the point between or above the brows. கண்நாடி may be the eye as a mirror or a seeking eye.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","thiyaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:164"},"t:2224":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2224,"t":["அறியாத வற்றை அறிவான் அறிவான்","அறிவான் அறியாதான் தன்னறிவு ஆகான்","அறியா தவத்தை அறிவானைக் கூட்டி","அறியாது அறிவானை யார்அறிவாரே."],"p":"","r":"The knower knows what is not known; one who does not know is not the knower of himself; who can know the knower who knows without knowing, joining the unknowing state to the knower?","n":"Built on அறி in every phrase, like 2206 and 2209. அறியாதவத்தை may be அறியாத அவத்தை (the unknowing state) or அறியாத தவத்தை. The last line ends in a question that tradition reads as the knower known only by being it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arivu","gnaanam","pasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:164"},"t:2225":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2225,"t":["துரிய தரிசனம் சொற்றோம் வியோமம்","அரியன தூடணம் அந்தண வாதி","பெரியன கால பரம்பின் துரியம்","அரிய அதீதம் அதீதத்த தாமே."],"p":"","r":"We have told the vision of turiya: the void, the rare faults, the Brahmin and the rest; beyond great time is turiya, and the rare atita is in the atita itself.","n":"தூடணம் (faults) and அந்தணவாதி are obscure here; the line may be corrupt. The verse reads as a closing summary of the turiya teaching.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:164"},"t:2226":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2226,"t":["மாயையிற் சேதனன் மன்னும் பகுதியோன்","மாயையின் மற்றது நீவுதல் மாயையாம்","கேவல மாகும் சகலமா யோனியுள்","தோயும் மனிதர் துரியத்துள் சீவனே."],"p":"","r":"The sentient one in maya is bound to prakriti; removing that other part of maya is itself maya; it becomes kevala; in the womb of sakala maya, the jiva of men immersed there is in turiya.","n":"கேவலம் and சகலம், the soul's isolated and embodied states, lead into the next chapter (கேவல சகல சுத்தம்). The syntax is uncertain and the last line may mean that even immersed souls have a turiya.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maayai","pasu","avaththai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:164"},"t:2227":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2227,"t":["தன்னை அறிசுத்தன் தற்கேவ லன்தானும்","பின்னம் உறநின்ற பேத சகலனும்","மன்னிய சத்தசத் துச்சத சத்துடன்","துன்னவர் தத்தம் தொழில்கள் வாகவே."],"p":"","r":"The pure soul that knows itself, the kevala soul that is alone in itself, and the sakala soul that stands in division and difference are each joined to the real, the unreal and the real-unreal, according to their own work.","n":"The chapter's three names arrive at once: கேவலன் (alone, with anava only), சகலன் (joined to body and world) and சுத்தன் (pure). சத்து, அசத்து and சதசத்து are traditionally the Lord, the bonds and the soul between them, but line 3 is compressed and I can't fix how each soul is paired with each.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","pasu","malam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:165"},"t:2228":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2228,"t":["தானே தனக்குப் பகைவனும் நட்டானும்","தானே தனக்கு மறுமையும் இம்மையும்","தானே தான்செய்த வினைப்பயன் துய்ப்பானும்","தானே தனக்குத் தலைவனும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"A person is his own enemy and his own friend, his own next world and this one, the one who eats the fruit of the deeds he did, and his own master.","n":"The fourfold தானே makes the soul answerable for its own state. Placed in a chapter that later says bondage and release both come by Hara's grace (2256), the last line (தனக்குத் தலைவனும்) reads as the soul's responsibility within its bound state, not as a denial of the Lord; that is my reading.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasu","vinai","marumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:165"},"t:2229":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2229,"t":["ஆமுயிர் கேவலம் மாமாயை யின்நடந்து","ஆம்உயிர் மாயை எறிப்ப அறிவுற்று","காமியம் மாமேய மும்கல வாநிற்பத்","தாம்உறு பாசம் சகலத்து ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"The soul in kevala moves into mahamaya; as maya lights it, it comes to knowledge, and as karma-born and maya-born impurity mingle with it, the bonds it takes on make the sakala state.","n":"காமியம் and மாமேயம் are taken as காமிய மலம் (the impurity of deeds) and மாயேய மலம் (the impurity of maya's products), which with anava make the three malas. எறிப்ப (strikes, or shines on) leaves open whether maya wakes the soul or wounds it; tradition takes maya as a lamp that gives the soul limited knowledge.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","malam","maayai","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:165"},"t:2230":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2230,"t":["சகல அவத்தையில் சார்ந்தோர் சகலர்","புகலும் மலம்மூ வகையும் புணர்ந்தோர்","நிகரில் மலரோன்மால் நீடுபல் தேவர்கள்","நிகழ்நரர் சீடம் அந்தமும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"Those who enter the sakala state are the sakalas, joined to all three named impurities: the matchless lotus-born Brahma, Vishnu, the many long-lived gods, humans and everything down to the worm.","n":"The verse puts the high gods among bound souls with men and insects; status in the cosmos does not lift the three impurities. சீடம் is read as கீடம் (worm, insect), as in 2244.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","malam","pasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:165"},"t:2231":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2231,"t":["தாவிய மாயையில் தங்கும் பிரளயம்","மேவிய மற்றது உடம்பாய்மிக் குள்ளன","ஓவல் இலக்கணர் ஒன்றிய சீகண்டர்","ஆவயின் நூற்றெட்டு உருத்திர ராமே."],"p":"","r":"Those who stay in all-spreading maya are the pralayakalas; of those in the other condition, with bodies, the ones of unfailing marks are Srikantha and, with him, the hundred and eight Rudras.","n":"பிரளயம் is taken as பிரளயாகலர், souls with two impurities. Srikantha and the 108 Rudras as a class of such beings is a Siddhanta teaching; line 2 (மேவிய மற்றது உடம்பாய்மிக் குள்ளன) is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","maayai","pasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:165"},"t:2232":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2232,"t":["ஆகின்ற கேவலத்து ஆணவத்து ஆனவர்","ஆகின்ற வித்தேச ராம்அனந் தாதியர்","ஆகின்ற எண்மர் எழுகோடி மந்திரர்","ஆகின்ற ஈசர் அநேகரும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"Those in kevala who have anava alone are the vidyesvaras, Ananta and the others, the eight, the seventy million mantra beings and the many lords.","n":"These are the விஞ்ஞானகலர், souls with only anava left. எழுகோடி மந்திரர் are mantras as beings, not formulas. The repeated ஆகின்ற (that become) keeps the list a list of states, not of persons.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","malam","pasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:165"},"t:2233":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2233,"t":["ஆம்அவ ரில்சிவ னார்அருள் பெற்றுளோர்","போம்மலந் தன்னால் புகழ்விந்து நாதம்விட்டு","ஒம்மய மாகி ஒடுங்கலின் நின்மலம்","தோம்அறும் சுத்த அவத்தைத் தொழிலே."],"p":"","r":"Among them, those who have received Siva's grace leave behind the praised bindu and nada as their impurity departs, become one with Om and are absorbed; being stainless, that is the work of the flawless suddha state.","n":"Bindu and nada, elsewhere the source of manifestation, are here something to be left behind. ஒம்மயம் is read as ஓம்மயம் (made of Om); ஒடுங்கல் is absorption or subsidence.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","avaththai","vindhu","naadham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:165"},"t:2234":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2234,"t":["ஒரினும் மூவகை நால்வகை யும்உள","தேரில் இவைகே வலம்மாயை சேர்இச்சை","சார்இய லாயவை தாமே தணப்பவை","வாரிவைத்து ஈசன் மலம்அறுத் தானே."],"p":"","r":"On inquiry there are three kinds and four kinds; these are kevala, maya and joined desire, which by their nature depart, and the Lord, gathering them up and setting them aside, cut away the impurity.","n":"மூவகை நால்வகை and சேர்இச்சை are obscure: perhaps three classes of souls and four impurities, with இச்சை standing for karma. The last line is clear: the Lord removes the impurity.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["malam","iraivan","avaththai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:165"},"t:2235":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2235,"t":["பொய்யான போதாந்தம் ஆறாறும் விட்டகன்று","எய்யாமை நீங்கவே எய்தவன் தானாகி","மெய்யாம் சராசர மாய்வெளி தன்னுட்புக்கு","எய்தாமல் எய்தும்சுத் தாவத்தை என்பதே."],"p":"","r":"Leaving the false end of knowing and the thirty-six behind, so that ignorance departs, becoming the one he reached, entering the space that is the real moving and unmoving world, reaching without reaching: that is called the suddha state.","n":"ஆறாறு is the thirty-six tattvas. பொய்யான போதாந்தம் (the false end of knowing) may be knowledge that ends in the instruments, as against Siva-knowledge. எய்தாமல் எய்தும் is a deliberate paradox: union without arriving anywhere.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","thaththuvam","sivam","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:165"},"t:2236":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2236,"t":["அனாதி பசுவியாத்தி யாகும் இவனை","அனாதியில் வந்த மலம்ஐந்தால் ஆட்டி","அனாதியில் கேவலம் அச்சக லத்திட்டு","அனாதி பிறப்புறச் சுத்தத்துள் ஆகுமே."],"p":"","r":"This soul, beginningless and bound, is driven by the five impurities that came without beginning; from beginningless kevala it is placed in sakala, and through beginningless births it comes into the suddha state.","n":"அனாதி begins every line: soul, impurities, kevala and births are all without beginning, yet the movement ends in purity. The five impurities are counted again in 2253 and 2257; tradition adds மாயேயம் and திரோதாயி to the three. பசுவியாத்தி is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pasu","malam","avaththai","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:165"},"t:2237":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2237,"t":["அந்தரம் சுத்தாவத் தைகே வலத்தாறு","தந்தோர்தம் சுத்தகே வலத்தற்ற தற்பரத்","தின்பால் துரியத் திடையே அறிவுறத்","தன்பால் தனையறி தத்துவந் தானே."],"p":"","r":"Within the suddha state, beyond the kevala, as knowledge arises in the midst of turiya toward the supreme, one knows oneself by oneself, and that is the reality.","n":"Lines 1 and 2 (அந்தரம் ... தற்பரத்தின்பால்) are obscure and I have kept only their drift. தத்துவம் here seems to mean the truth itself, not a tattva in the list.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","gnaanam","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:165"},"t:2238":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2238,"t":["ஐ ஐந்து ஒடுங்கும் ஆன்மாவில் ஆன்மாவும்","மெய்கண்டு சுத்த அவத்தையில் வீடாகும்","துய்யஅவ் வித்தை முதன்மூன்றும் தொல்சத்தி","ஐயன் சிவன்சத்தி யாம்தோற்றம் அவ்வாறே."],"p":"","r":"The twenty-five subside in the soul, and the soul too, seeing the truth, finds release in the suddha state; the three pure tattvas beginning with vidya, the ancient Shakti and the lord Siva appear in that order.","n":"ஐஐந்து (five fives) is taken as twenty-five lower tattvas. The pure tattvas are traditionally suddha vidya, isvara, sadakhya, shakti and siva, which fits வித்தை முதன்மூன்றும் with Shakti and Siva.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","avaththai","veedu","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:165"},"t:2239":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2239,"t":["ஐஐந்தும் ஆன்மாவில் ஆறோடு அடங்கியும்","மெய்கண்ட மேல்மூன்றும் மேவுமெய் யோகத்தில்","கைகண்ட சத்தி சிவபாகத் தேகாண","எய்யும் படியடங்கும் நாலேழ் எய்தியே."],"p":"","r":"The twenty-five and the six subside into the soul, the three above are reached in true yoga, and seeing the Shakti held in Siva's portion, one subsides as one reaches the four-sevens.","n":"25 + 6 + 3 approaches the thirty-six with Shakti and Siva, but நாலேழ் (four sevens, twenty-eight, or four and seven) does not fit any count I can fix. சிவபாகம் may be Siva's half, the Shakti who is his left side.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","yogam","saththi","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:165"},"t:2240":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2240,"t":["ஆணவத் தார்ஒன்று அறியாத கேவலர்","பேணிய மாயைப் பிரளயா கலராகும்","காணும் உருவினர் காணாமை காண்பவே","பூணும் சகலர்முப் பாசமும் புக்கோரே."],"p":"","r":"Those with anava alone, who know nothing, are the kevalas; those who hold maya are the pralayakalas; and those who take on all three bonds are the sakalas, who have visible form and see without seeing.","n":"காணும் உருவினர் காணாமை காண்பவே may mean the sakalas see only with bodily eyes and so do not truly see. The verse is a key to the chapter's classes.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","malam","paasam","pasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:165"},"t:2241":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2241,"t":["ஆணவம் ஆகும் விஞ்ஞான கலருக்குப்","பேணிய மாயை பிரளயா கலருக்கே","ஆணவ மாயையும் கன்மம் மூன்றுமே","காணும் சகலர்க்குக் காட்டும் மலங்களே"],"p":"","r":"For the vijnanakalas the impurity is anava; for the pralayakalas, maya; for the sakalas, anava, maya and karma, all three, are the impurities shown.","n":"Later Siddhanta texts give the pralayakalas anava and karma, without maya, since maya is dissolved at pralaya. This verse, with 2240 and 2243, ties them to maya instead. Whether that is an older scheme or a looser use of மாயை I can't say.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["malam","avaththai","maayai","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:165"},"t:2242":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2242,"t":["கேவலம் தன்னில் கிளர்ந்தவிஞ் ஞாகலர்","கேவலம் தன்னில் கிளர்விந்து சத்தியால்","பூவயின் கேவலத்து அச்சக லத்தையும்","மேவிய மந்திர மாமாயை மெய்ம்மையே."],"p":"","r":"The vijnanakalas rise within kevala by the power of bindu that rises there, and the mantra beings who pass from kevala into sakala in the world have mahamaya as their truth.","n":"பூவயின் (in the world, or in the flower) and the syntax of lines 3 and 4 are unclear. The tradition makes mahamaya (pure maya, bindu) the field of the higher beings, as impure maya is of the sakalas.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","vindhu","maayai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:165"},"t:2243":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2243,"t":["மாயையில் மன்னும் பிரளயா கலர்வந்து","மாயையும் தோன்றா வகைநிற்க ஆணவ","மாய சகலத்துக் காமிய மாமாயை","ஏயமன் நூற்றெட்டு உருத்திரர் என்பவே."],"p":"","r":"The pralayakalas who dwell in maya come so that maya does not appear, and those in the maya-sakala of anava, karma and mahamaya are called the hundred and eight Rudras.","n":"ஏயமன் is obscure, and the relation of the 108 Rudras to 2231 is not settled by the verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","maayai","malam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:165"},"t:2244":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2244,"t":["மும்மலம் கூடி முயங்கி மயங்குவோர்","அம்மெய்ச் சகலத்தர் தேவர் சுரர்நரர்","மெய்ம்மையில் வேதா விரிமிகு கீடாந்தத்து","அம்முறை யோனிபுக்கு ஆர்க்கும் சகலரே."],"p":"","r":"Those whom the three impurities join, embrace and bewilder are the sakalas of that body: gods, devas and men, and truly, from Brahma down to the many insects, sakalas enter wombs in due order and are bound.","n":"யோனிபுக்கு (entering the womb) makes sakala the state of birth itself. The list from வேதா to கீடம் matches 2230.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["malam","avaththai","piravi","pasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:165"},"t:2245":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2245,"t":["சுத்த அவத்தையில் தோய்ந்தவர் மும்மலச்","சத்துஅசத்து ஓடத் தனித்தனி பாசமும்","மத்த இருள்சிவ னான கதிராலே","தொத்தற விட்டிடச் சுத்தஆ வார்களே."],"p":"","r":"Those steeped in the suddha state become pure when the three impurities, real and unreal, run off, and each bond in turn, the maddening darkness, is let go entirely by the ray that is Siva.","n":"மத்த இருள் (intoxicating darkness) is traditionally anava, and சிவனான கதிர் the light of grace. சத்துஅசத்து may name the impurities as both real and unreal, or the soul's two sides.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","malam","sivam","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:165"},"t:2246":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2246,"t":["தற்கே வலம்முத்தி தானே தனிமையாம்","பிற்பால் சகலம் கலாதிப் பிறிவதாம்","சொற்பால் புரிசுத்த கேவலம் சாக்கிரம்","தற்பால் புரிவது தற்சுத்தம் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"Tat-kevala is release, solitude itself; afterward sakala is separation from kala and the rest; the suddha-kevala named in words is waking, and what one does within oneself is one's own purity.","n":"Each line pairs a technical name with a gloss I can't fix. The verse seems to begin the subdivision of each state that 2249 to 2252 set out.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:165"},"t:2247":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2247,"t":["அறிவின்றி முத்தன் அராகாதி சேரான்","குறியொன்றி லாநித்தன் கூடான் காலதி","செறியும் செயலிலான் தினங்கற்ற வல்லோன்","கிறியன் மலவியாபி கேவலம் தானே."],"p":"","r":"Kevala is the soul without knowledge, not joined to raga and the other tattvas, without any mark, eternal, unjoined to kala, without action, and pervaded by impurity.","n":"முத்தன் (freed) at the start seems ironic: the kevala soul is 'free' of the tattvas only because it has nothing. தினங்கற்ற வல்லோன் கிறியன் is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","pasu","malam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:165"},"t:2248":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2248,"t":["விந்துவும் மாயையும் மேவும் கிரியையும்","சந்தத ஞான பரையும் தனுச்சுத்தி","விந்துவின் மெய்ஞ்ஞான மேவும் பிரளயர்","வந்த சகலசுத் தான்மாக்கள் வையத்தே."],"p":"","r":"Bindu and maya, the kriya that comes with them, the ever-present supreme power of knowledge and the purifying of the body; the pralayakalas who gain the true knowledge of bindu; and the sakala and suddha souls who come into the world.","n":"The verse is a list without a main verb, and I can't tell how its items relate. It may be naming what belongs to each class of soul.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vindhu","maayai","avaththai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:165"},"t:2249":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2249,"t":["கேவல மாதியின் பேதம் கிளக்குறில்","கேவல மூன்றும் கிளரும் சகலத்துள்","ஆவயின் மூன்று மதிசுத்த மூடவே","ஓவலில் லாஒன்பான் ஒற்றுணர் வோர்கட்கே."],"p":"","r":"If the divisions of kevala and the others are told, the three kevalas rise within sakala, and there the three of suddha too, so that the unceasing nine are known to those who perceive them.","n":"The next three verses set out the nine: kevala, sakala and suddha, each within each. Line 3 (மதிசுத்த மூடவே) is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:165"},"t:2250":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2250,"t":["கேவலத்தில் கேவலம் அதீதா தீதம்","கேவலத் தில்சகலங்கள் வயிந்தவம்","கேவத் திறசுத்தம் கேடில்விஞ் ஞாகலர்க்கு","ஆவயின் நாதன் அருண்மூர்த்தி தானே."],"p":"","r":"Kevala within kevala is the beyond-beyond; sakala within kevala is the bindu-born state; suddha within kevala belongs to the faultless vijnanakalas, and there the Lord is the form of grace.","n":"அதீதாதீதம் and வயிந்தவம் are technical. Line 3 reads கேவத் திறசுத்தம், taken as கேவலத்தில் சுத்தம்.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","vindhu","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:165"},"t:2251":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2251,"t":["சகலத்தில் கேவலம் சாக்கிரா தீதம்","சகல சகலமே சாக்கிர சாக்கிரம்","சகலத்திற் சுத்தமே தற்பரா வத்தை","சகலத்தில் இம்மூன்று தன்மையும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"Kevala within sakala is the state beyond waking; sakala within sakala is waking within waking; suddha within sakala is the state of the supreme; these three natures are within sakala.","n":"Here the three states meet the five of waking, dream, deep sleep, turiya and beyond. சாக்கிரசாக்கிரம் (waking in waking) is ordinary bound life at its fullest.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:165"},"t:2252":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2252,"t":["சுத்தத்தில் சுத்தமே தொல்சிவ மாகுதல்","சுத்தத்தில் கேவலம் தொல்லுப சாந்தமாம்","சுத்த சகலம் துரிய விலாசமாம்","சுத்தத்தில் இம்மூன்றும் சொல்லலும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"Suddha within suddha is becoming the ancient Siva; kevala within suddha is the ancient stillness; sakala within suddha is the play of turiya; these three too can be told within suddha.","n":"உபசாந்தம் (stillness) and துரிய விலாசம் (turiya's play) are technical. The highest of the nine is becoming Siva, which I have marked as union.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","sivam","saayujjiyam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:165"},"t:2253":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2253,"t":["சாக்கிர சாக்கிரம் தன்னில் கனவொடுஞ்","சாக்கிரம் தன்னில் சுழுத்தி துரியமே","சாக்கிரா தீதம் தனிற்சுகா னந்தமே","ஆக்கு மறையாதி ஐம்மல பாசமே."],"p":"","r":"Within waking-in-waking there is also dream; within waking there is deep sleep and turiya; in the state beyond waking there is easeful bliss; and the five impurity-bonds, concealment and the rest, make these.","n":"The waking states are nested inside waking, which is the jagrat-avastha scheme of Siddhanta as tradition gives it. மறையாதி (concealing and the rest) is taken as the concealing power, திரோதாயி, among the five.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","malam","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:165"},"t:2254":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2254,"t":["சாக்கிரா தீதத்தில் தானறும் ஆணவம்","சாக்கிரா தீதம் பிராவத்தை தங்காது","ஆக்கு பரோபதி யாஉப சாந்தத்தை","நோக்கும் மலங்குணம் நோக்குதல் ஆகுமே."],"p":"","r":"In the state beyond waking, anava itself is cut off; that state does not remain in the para state; the stillness it brings, transcending, looks on the impurities and qualities and sees them.","n":"Lines 2 to 4 are obscure. பரோபதி may be 'beyond adjuncts'. The sense may be that the freed state witnesses the impurities without being bound by them.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","malam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:165"},"t:2255":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2255,"t":["பெத்தமும் முத்தியும் பேணும் துரியமும்","சுத்த அதீதமும் தோன்றாமல் தானுணும்","அத்தன் அருள்என்று அருளால் அறிந்தபின்","சித்தமும் இல்லை செயல்இல்லை தானே."],"p":"","r":"Once one knows by grace that it is the Father's grace that, unseen, consumes bondage and release, the turiya that holds them and pure atita, there is no mind of one's own and no action of one's own.","n":"தோன்றாமல் தானுணும் (consumes them unseen) makes even the states of the soul the working of grace. அருளால் அறிந்தபின்: the knowing too is by grace. The end of mind and action is the sign, not the means.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","avaththai","veedu","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:165"},"t:2256":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2256,"t":["எய்திய பெத்தமும் முத்தமும் என்பன","எய்தும் அரன்அரு ளேவிளை யாட்டோ டு","எய்திடு உயிர்சுத்தத் திடுநெறி என்னவே","எய்தும் உயிர்இறை பால்அறி வாமே."],"p":"","r":"Bondage and release, as they are called, both come by Hara's grace, as play and as the way that brings the soul to purity; so the soul comes to know in the Lord.","n":"The verse calls bondage itself grace (அருளே) and play (விளையாட்டு). In Siddhanta the impurities are bound to the soul so that they may ripen and fall; that is the tradition's account of why bondage could be called grace.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","veedu","pasu","pathi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:165"},"t:2257":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2257,"t":["ஐம்மலத் தாரும் மதித்த சகலத்தர்","ஐம்மலத் தாரும் அருவினைப் பாசத்தார்","ஐம்மலத் தார்சுவர்க் கந்நெறி யான்பவர்","ஐம்மலத் தார்அர னார்க்குஅறி வோரே."],"p":"","r":"Those with five impurities are the sakalas reckoned; those with five impurities are bound by hard deeds; those with five impurities go on the path to heaven; those with five impurities are those who know Hara.","n":"The repeated ஐம்மலத்தார் places even heaven-goers under the impurities. The last line can mean that those still bound come to know Hara, or that they are known to him; neither reading is settled by the verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["malam","avaththai","vinai","paasam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:165"},"t:2258":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2258,"t":["கருவில் அதீதம் கலப்பிக்கும் மாயை","அரிய துரியம் அதிலுண்ணும் ஆசையும்","உரிய கழுனை முதல்எட்டும் சூக்கத்து","அரிய கனாத்துலம் அந்தன வாமே."],"p":"","r":"In the womb maya mixes in the beyond state; rare turiya and the desire that feeds in it, the sushumna and the first eight in the subtle body, and rare dream and the gross body are those.","n":"The verse maps the states onto the embryo and the body. கழுனை is read as சுழுனை (sushumna, or deep sleep), and எட்டும் சூக்கத்து as the eight of the subtle body; the last line is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","karu","maayai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:165"},"t:2259":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2259,"t":["ஆணவம் ஆகும் அதிதம்மேல் மாயையும்","பூணும் துரியம் சுழுத்திபொய்க் காமியம்","பேணும் கனவும் மாமாயை திரோதாயி","காணும் நனவில் மலக்கலப்பு ஆகுமே."],"p":"","r":"Anava is in the beyond state; above it maya is borne in turiya; deep sleep has false karma; dream holds mahamaya; and the concealing power is seen in waking, where the impurities mix.","n":"Each of the five states is matched with one of five impurities. Where திரோதாயி belongs, with dream or with waking, depends on how line 3 is divided.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","malam","maayai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:165"},"t:2260":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2260,"t":["அரன்முத லாக அறிவோன் அதீதத்தன்","அரன்முத லாமாயை தங்கிச் சுழுனை","கருமம் உணர்ந்து மாமாயைக் கைகொண்டோ ர்","அருளும் அறைவார் சகலத்துற் றாரே."],"p":"","r":"The knower beginning from Hara is in the beyond state; maya, from Hara on, stays in deep sleep; those who knew karma and took up mahamaya, and those who tell of grace, are in sakala.","n":"I can't make the syntax of this verse sure. It seems to place kinds of knowers and impurities across the states, as 2258 and 2259 do.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","maayai","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:165"},"t:2261":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2261,"t":["உருவுற்றுப் போகமே போக்கியம் துற்று","மருவுற்றுப் பூதம னாதியான் மன்னி","வரும்அச் செயல்பற்றிச் சத்தாதி வைகிக்","கருவுற் றிடுஞ் சீவன் காணும் சகலத்தே."],"p":"","r":"In sakala the soul takes a body, meets experience and what is experienced, settles among the elements, mind and the rest, holds to their working, dwells among sound and the other objects, and comes into the womb.","n":"போகம் and போக்கியம் are the enjoying and the enjoyed. The verse lists the sakala soul's equipment in order, ending at birth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","yaakkai","thaththuvam","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:165"},"t:2262":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2262,"t":["இருவிடை ஒத்திட இன்னருள் சத்தி","மருவிட ஞானத்தில் ஆதனம் மன்னிக்","குருவினைக்கொண்டருள் சத்திமுன் கூட்டிப்","பெருமலம் நீங்கிப் பிறவாமை சுத்தமே."],"p":"","r":"When the two deeds balance and sweet grace-power enters, establishing a seat in knowledge and taking the guru, grace-power joins one to itself, the great impurity goes, and purity is non-birth.","n":"இருவினை ஒப்பு (balance of the two deeds) and the descent of grace-power (சத்திநிபாதம்) are traditionally the conditions for the guru's coming. The verse nearly repeats 1527. Line 1 reads இருவிடை, taken as இருவினை.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","guru","vinai","malam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:165"},"t:2263":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2263,"t":["ஆறாறும் ஆறதின் ஐ ஐந்து அவத்தையோடு","ஈறாம் அதீதத் துரியத்து இவன்எய்தப்","பேறான ஐவரும் போம்பிர காசத்து","நீறார் பரம்சிவம் ஆதேய மாகுமே."],"p":"","r":"When the soul reaches the end, the beyond-turiya, past the thirty-six, the six and the twenty-five with their states, the five who were his gain fade in the light, and ash-adorned supreme Siva comes to dwell in him.","n":"ஐவர் are traditionally the five deities presiding over the states, who withdraw. ஆதேயம் (what is held or placed in something) is taken as Siva becoming the content of the soul; the numbers in line 1 are uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","thaththuvam","sivam","saayujjiyam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:165"},"t:2264":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2264,"t":["தன்னை அறியாது உடலைமுன் தான்என்றான்","தன்னைமுன் கண்டான் துரியம் தனைக்கண்டான்","உன்னும் துரியமும் ஈசனோடு ஒன்றாக்கால்","பின்னையும் வந்து பிறந்திடும் தானே."],"p":"","r":"Not knowing himself, he first took the body as himself; when he first saw himself, he saw turiya; but if the turiya he contemplates is not made one with the Lord, he will be born again.","n":"Three steps: body taken for self, self seen as turiya, turiya joined to the Lord. The verse warns that self-knowledge stopped at the second step still leads to birth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["meyyunarvu","yaakkai","avaththai","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:165"},"t:2265":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2265,"t":["சாக்கிரத் தன்னில் அதீதம் தலைப்படில்","ஆக்கிய அந்த வயிந்தவம் ஆனந்தம்","நோக்கும் பிறப்புஅறும் நோன்முத்தி சித்தியாம்","வாக்கும் மனமும் மருவல்செய் யாவே."],"p":"","r":"If the beyond state is reached within waking, the bindu-born state it brings is bliss; looming birth is cut off, firm release is attained, and speech and mind do not reach it.","n":"சாக்கிராதீதம் (beyond in waking) is traditionally taken as release while living in the body. வயிந்தவம் (bindu-born) here seems to name a pure state, not a bond.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","aanandham","veedu","vindhu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:165"},"t:2266":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2266,"t":["அப்பும் அனலும் அகலத்து ளேவரும்","அப்பும் அனலும் அகலத்து ளேவாரா","அப்பும் அனலும் அகலத்துள் ஏதெனில்","அப்பும் அனலும் கலந்ததுஅவ் வாறே."],"p":"","r":"Water and fire come within the breast; water and fire do not come within the breast; if you ask what water and fire are within the breast, they are water and fire mingled, that way.","n":"A riddle. அகலம் is breast or expanse, and அகல் a lamp, which holds oil and flame. Water and fire are taken symbolically as moon and sun, Shakti and Siva, or white bindu and red nada (compare 1929); none of these is stated.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","sivam","vindhu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:165"},"t:2267":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2267,"t":["அறுநான்கு அசுத்தம் அதிசுத்தா சுத்தம்","உறும்ஏழு மாயை உடன்ஐந்தே சுத்தம்","பெறுமாறு இவைமூன்றும் கண்டத்தால் பேதித்து","உறும்மாயை மாமாயை ஆன்மாவி னோடே."],"p":"","r":"Twenty-four are impure, seven pure-impure belong with maya, and five are pure; divided in these three parts, they are maya, mahamaya and what goes with the soul.","n":"The count 24 + 7 + 5 = 36 is the standard Siddhanta grouping of the tattvas. Which group line 4 assigns to the soul is not clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","maayai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:165"},"t:2268":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2268,"t":["மாயைகைத் தாயாக மாமாயை ஈன்றிட","ஆய பரசிவன் தந்தையாய் நிற்கவே","ஏயும் உயிர்க்கே வலசகலத்து எய்தி","ஆய்தரு சுத்தமும் தான்வந்து அடையுமே."],"p":"","r":"With maya as foster mother, mahamaya giving birth, and supreme Siva standing as father, the soul passes through kevala and sakala, and the purity it seeks comes to it of itself.","n":"கைத்தாய் is the nurse or foster mother. The family image gathers the chapter: the soul is raised through impure maya, born of pure maya and fathered by Siva, and the bound states are its upbringing. தான்வந்து அடையுமே: purity comes, it is not seized.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maayai","sivam","avaththai","pasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:165"},"t:2269":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2269,"t":["அஞ்சும் கடந்த அனாதி பரன்தெய்வம்","நெஞ்சம தாய நிமலன் பிறப்பிலி","விஞ்சும் உடலுயிர் வேறு படுத்திட","வஞ்சத் திருந்த வகையறிந் தேனே."],"p":"","r":"I came to know how the beginningless supreme God beyond the five, the stainless unborn one who became the heart, stayed hidden so as to set the body and the soul apart.","n":"அஞ்சும் கடந்த (beyond the five) is not explained: the five senses, the five states or the five malas are all possible. வஞ்சத்து இருந்த (stayed in concealment) suggests the Lord's hiding act, but the verse doesn't name it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pathi","uyir","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:166"},"t:2270":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2270,"t":["சத்தி பராபரம் சாந்தி தனிலான","சத்தி பரானந்தம் தன்னில் சுடர்விந்து","சத்திய மாயை தனுச்சத்தி ஐந்துடன்","சத்தி பெறுமுயிர் தான்அங்கத்து ஆறுமே."],"p":"","r":"The verse lists Shakti in her grades, as Paraparai, in peace (சாந்தி), in supreme bliss, as radiant bindu, as true maya and with the body's five powers, and the soul that receives Shakti makes the sixth in the limbs.","n":"The line divisions and the count (ஐந்துடன், ஆறுமே) are obscure; சாந்தி may be one of the kalas and சத்திய மாயை is perhaps pure maya. Only the general sense, Shakti descending in grades to the embodied soul, is secure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","vindhu","maayai","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:166"},"t:2271":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2271,"t":["ஆறாறுக்கு அப்பால் அறிவார் அறிபவர்","ஆறாறுக்கு அப்பால் அருளார் பெறுபவர்","ஆறாறுக்கு அப்பால் அறிவாம் அவர்கட்கே","ஆறாறுக்கு அப்பால் அரன்இனி தாமே."],"p":"","r":"Those who know are beyond the thirty-six; those who receive grace are beyond it; for them knowledge is beyond it, and beyond it Hara is sweet.","n":"ஆறாறு (six sixes) is the usual way of naming the thirty-six tattvas. The refrain makes the point by repetition: knowing, grace and the sweetness of Siva all lie past the constituents of body and world.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","thiruvarul","gnaanam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:166"},"t:2272":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2272,"t":["அஞ்சொடு நான்கும் கடந்துஅக மேபுக்குப்","பஞ்சணி காலத்துப் பள்ளி துயில்கின்ற","விஞ்சையர் வேந்தனும் மெல்லிய லாளொடு","நஞ்சுற நாடி நயம்செய்யு மாறே."],"p":"","r":"Passing the five and the four and entering within, the king of the vidyadharas, asleep on his soft bed, seeks out the gentle woman with longing and takes his pleasure.","n":"A plain erotic image with a symbolic sense: the five and the four are usually taken as the five senses and the four inner instruments, the sleeping king as the soul in the yogic sleep of turiya, and the woman as Shakti. நஞ்சுற (so that poison enters) is hard to fit; it may be a corruption, perhaps of நெஞ்சுற (with the heart).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","saththi","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:166"},"t:2273":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2273,"t":["உரிய நனாத்துரி யத்தில் இவளாம்","அரிய துரிய நனவாதி மூன்றில்","பரிய பரதுரி யத்தில் பரனாம்","திரிய வரும்துரி யத்தில் சிவமே."],"p":"","r":"In the waking proper to turiya she (Shakti) is present; across waking and the two after it lies the rare turiya; in the vast para-turiya is the Supreme; and in the turiya reached by turning back, Siva.","n":"நனாத்துரியம், பரதுரியம் and the turiya reached by turning (திரிய) are technical grades of the states, assigned here to Shakti, the Supreme and Siva. How the second line fits the scheme is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","saththi","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:166"},"t:2274":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2274,"t":["பரமாம் அதீதமே பற்றறப் பற்றப்","பரமாம் அதீதம் பயிலப் பயிலப்","பரமாம் அதீதம் பயிலாத் தபோதனார்","பரமாகார் பாசமும் பற்றொன்றுஅ றாதே."],"p":"","r":"The Supreme, the state beyond (அதீதம்), is grasped as attachment ends and becomes one's own as one practises it again and again; ascetics who don't practise it don't become the Supreme, since not one of their bonds and attachments ends.","n":"பற்றறப் பற்ற plays on பற்று: one grasps the Supreme by letting go of grasping. The verse is aimed at ascetics (தபோதனர்) whose austerity lacks this practice.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pattru","paasam","thavam","avaththai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:166"},"t:2275":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2275,"t":["ஆயும்பொய்ம் மாயை அகம்புற மாய்நிற்கும்","வாயு மனமும் கடந்துஅம் மயக்கறின்","தூய அறிவு சிவானந்த மாகிப்போய்","வேயும் பொருளாய் விளைந்தது தானே."],"p":"","r":"False maya, which one examines, stands inside and outside; if one passes beyond breath and mind and that confusion ends, pure knowledge becomes the bliss of Siva and itself grows into the reality that covers all.","n":"வேயும் பொருள் (the thing that covers or roofs) is obscure; it may mean the reality that pervades everything. வாயு is the breath, stilled with the mind in yoga.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maayai","praanan","gnaanam","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:166"},"t:2276":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2276,"t":["துரியப் பரியில் இருந்தஅச் சீவனைப்","பெரிய வியாக்கிரத் துள்ளே புகவிட்டு","நரிகளை ஓடத் துரத்திய நாதர்க்கு","உரிய வினைகள் நின்று ஓலமிட் டன்றே."],"p":"","r":"The Lord set the soul that rode the horse of turiya inside the great tiger and drove the jackals running, and the deeds bound to that soul stood there and wailed.","n":"Wholly symbolic: the jackals are usually taken as the senses or the malas, the tiger as Siva or his grace, and the wailing deeds as karma left without a hold. The verse does not name any of these.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","avaththai","pasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:166"},"t:2277":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2277,"t":["நின்றஇச் சாக்கிர நீள்துரி யத்தினின்","மன்றனும் அங்கே மணம்செய்ய நின்றிடும்","மன்றன் மணம்செய்ய மாயை மறைந்திடும்","அன்றே இவனும் அவன்வடி வாமே."],"p":"","r":"In this waking that stands in long turiya, the Lord of the hall stands ready to marry; when he marries, maya vanishes, and that day this soul takes his form.","n":"மன்றன் is Siva of the hall (அம்பலம்) where he dances. Marriage stands for the soul's union with him, and அவன்வடிவாமே for the soul taking Siva's form. சாக்கிர நீள்துரியம் is a technical state.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","saayujjiyam","maayai","avaththai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:166"},"t:2278":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2278,"t":["விரிந்திடில் சாக்கிரம் மேவும் விளக்காய்","இருந்த இடத்திடை ஈடான மாயை","பொருந்தும் துரியம் புரியல்தா னாகும்","தெரிந்த துரியத்துத் தீதுஅக லாதே."],"p":"","r":"When waking spreads out it becomes like a lamp; where it stays, maya stands to match it; the turiya one enters becomes one's own working, and in the turiya one comes to know, evil does not remain.","n":"The last words தீதுஅகலாதே can mean 'evil does not leave' as easily as 'evil leaves without fail', and the two give opposite senses; the context of the chapter favours the second, but the text does not decide it. ஈடான and புரியல்தானாகும் are also obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","maayai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:166"},"t:2279":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2279,"t":["உன்னை அறியாது உடலைமுன் நான்ஒன்றாய்","உன்னை அறிந்து துரியத்து உறநின்றாய்","தன்னை அறிந்தும் பிறவி தணவாதால்","அன்ன வியாத்தன் அமலன் என்று அறிதியே."],"p":"","r":"Not knowing you, I once took myself to be one with the body; knowing you, you stood with me in turiya; and since birth does not end merely by knowing oneself, know that the one who pervades is the Stainless.","n":"The verse separates knowing oneself, which does not end rebirth, from knowing the pervading Lord (வியாத்தன்). The shifts of person (I, you, oneself) make the speaker and addressee hard to fix.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","piravi","avaththai","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:166"},"t:2280":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2280,"t":["கருவரம்பு ஆகிய காயம் துரியம்","இருவரும் கண்டீர் பிறப்புஇறப்பு உற்றார்","குருவரம் பெற்றவர் கூடிய பின்னை","இருவரும் இன்றிஒன் றாகி நின் றாரே."],"p":"","r":"While the body, bounded by the womb, and turiya are two, the two pass through birth and death; once those who have the guru's boon unite, there are no longer two, and they stand as one.","n":"இருவரும் (the two) is read here as the body and turiya, but it can also be the soul and the Lord, which makes the last line a statement of union. Either way the guru's grace (குருவரம்) is what ends the duality.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","piravi","saayujjiyam","avaththai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:166"},"t:2281":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2281,"t":["அணுவின் துரியத்தில் ஆன நனவும்","அணுஅசை வின்கண் ஆனகனவும்","அணுஅசை வில்பரா தீதம் கழுத்தி","பணியில் பரதுரி யம்பர மாமே."],"p":"","r":"In the soul's turiya there is waking, in the soul's stirring there is dream, in its stillness the para-atita as deep sleep, and in service para-turiya is the Supreme.","n":"அணு is the soul as atom-like, the bound soul. The verse maps the ordinary states onto higher ones, and பணியில் (in service, or in humility) is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","pasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:166"},"t:2282":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2282,"t":["பரதுரி யத்து நனவும் பரந்து","விரிசகம் உண்ட கனவும்மெய்ச் சாந்தி","உருவுறு கின்ற சுழுத்தியும் ஓவத்","தெரியும் சிவதுரி யத்தனு மாமே."],"p":"","r":"In para-turiya there is a waking that spreads, a dream that has swallowed the wide world, a true peace and a deep sleep that takes form; as these cease, one is seen to be in Siva's turiya.","n":"The verse sets out the four states again at the higher level of para-turiya. Each name (சாந்தி, சுழுத்தி) is technical, and the exact grades are the tradition's.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:166"},"t:2283":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2283,"t":["பரமா நனவின்பின் பால்சக முண்ட","திரமார் கனவும் சிறந்த சுழுத்தி","உரமாம் உபசாந்தம் உற்றல் துறவே","தரனாம் சிவதுரி யத்தனும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"After the supreme waking come the steady dream that has eaten the world, the excellent deep sleep and the strong upasanta calm; to reach these is renunciation, and one becomes the bearer of Siva's turiya.","n":"A near-variant of 2282. உபசாந்தம் is a technical name for a state of calm; துறவே may mean renunciation itself or 'by renouncing'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","thuravu","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:166"},"t:2284":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2284,"t":["சீவன் துரியம் முதலாகச் சீரான","ஆவ சிவன்துரி யாந்தம் அவத்தைபத்தும்","ஓவும் பராநந்தி உண்மைக்குள் வைகியே","மேவிய நாலேழ் விடுவித்துநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"From the soul's turiya up to Siva's turiya-end, the ten states cease in their order; dwelling in the truth of supreme Nandi, he stood having freed the soul from the twenty-eight.","n":"அவத்தைபத்தும் (the ten states) and நாலேழ் (four sevens, twenty-eight, or four and seven) are counts from the tradition's scheme that the verse doesn't spell out.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","sivam","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:166"},"t:2285":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2285,"t":["பரம்சிவன் மேலாம் பரமம் பரத்தில்","பரம்சிவன் மேலாம் பரநனவாக","விரிந்த கனாவிடர் வீட்டும் கழுமுனை","உரந்தரும் மாநந்தி யாம்உண்மை தானே."],"p":"","r":"Above Paramasiva is the Paramam; in that Para, Paramasiva above becomes the supreme waking; the sushumna that ends the distress of the spreading dream gives strength: this is the truth of great Nandi.","n":"கழுமுனை is the sushumna, the central channel of yoga. The ordering of Paramasiva and Paramam is the tradition's and the syntax of the verse is loose.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","naadi","avaththai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:166"},"t:2286":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2286,"t":["சார்வாம் பரம்சிவன் சத்தி பரநாதம்","மேலாய விந்து சதாசிவம் மிக்கோங்கிப்","பாலாய்ப் பிரமன் அரிஅம ராபதி","தேவாம் உருத்திரன் ஈசனாம் காணிலே."],"p":"","r":"When seen, the order runs: Paramasiva who is the support, Shakti, supreme nada, bindu above it and Sadasiva rising high, then below them Brahma, Hari, the god of the immortals' city, Rudra and Isa.","n":"A list of Siva's descending forms in Siddhanta. பாலாய் may mean 'below' or 'as milk'; அமராபதி தேவாம் (the god of Amaravati) would usually be Indra, an odd entry here, so it may describe one of the others.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","saththi","naadham","vindhu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:166"},"t:2287":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2287,"t":["கலப்புஅறி யார்கடல் சூழ்உல கேழும்","உலப்புஅறி யார்உட லோடுஉயிர் தன்மை","அலப்புஅறிந்து இங்குஅர சாளகி லாதார்","குறிப்பது கோலம் அடலது வாமே."],"p":"","r":"Those who don't know the union, nor the end of the seven sea-girt worlds, nor how the soul stands with the body, and cannot rule here having understood the turmoil, aim only at outward show and force.","n":"கலப்பு (mingling) may be the soul's union with Siva or the mixing of soul and body. அலப்பு, அரசாளகிலாதார் and the last line (குறிப்பது கோலம் அடலது வாமே) are obscure; this reading is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["meyyunarvu","uyir","yaakkai","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:166"},"t:2288":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2288,"t":["பின்னை அறியும் பெருந்தவத்து உண்மைசெய்","தன்னை அறியில் தயாபரன் எம்இறை","முன்னை அறிவு முடிகின்ற காலமும்","என்னை அறியலுற்று இன்புற்ற வாறே."],"p":"","r":"If one knows oneself through the truth of great austerity that is known afterwards, the compassionate Supreme is our Lord; and when former knowledge comes to its end, I set out to know myself and so came to joy.","n":"The traditional saying that one who knows oneself knows the Lord fits this verse, but the first line is hard to construe. முன்னை அறிவு (former knowledge) may be the bound soul's ordinary knowing.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","pathi","thavam","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:166"},"t:2289":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2289,"t":["பொன்னை மறைத்தது பொன்னணி பூடணம்","பொன்னின் மறைந்தது பொன்னணி பூடணம்","தன்னை மறைத்தது தன்கர ணங்களாம்","தன்னின் மறைந்தது தன்கர ணங்களே."],"p":"","r":"The gold ornament hides the gold, and the gold ornament disappears into the gold; one's own instruments hide oneself, and one's instruments disappear into oneself.","n":"Seen as an ornament, the gold is not noticed; seen as gold, the ornament is not. So too the soul and its faculties (கரணங்கள்): attention to one hides the other. தன் is usually taken as the soul.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uyir","thaththuvam","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:166"},"t:2290":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2290,"t":["மரத்தை மறைத்தது மாமத யானை","மரத்தின் மறைந்தது மாமத யானை","பரத்தை மறைத்தது பார்முதல் பூதம்","பரத்தை மறைந்தது பார்முதல் பூதமே."],"p":"","r":"The great rutting elephant hides the wood, and the elephant disappears into the wood; the elements beginning with earth hide the Supreme, and the elements disappear into the Supreme.","n":"The image is a wooden elephant: one who sees the elephant doesn't see the wood, and one who sees the wood doesn't see the elephant. Seeing the world hides the Lord in it; seeing the Lord, the world vanishes into him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pathi","meyyunarvu","maayai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:166"},"t:2291":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2291,"t":["ஆறாறு ஆகன்று நமவிட்டு அறிவாகி","வேறான தானே யகாரமாய் மிக்கோங்கி","ஈறார் பரையின் இருளற்ற தற்பரன்","பேறார் சிவாய அடங்கும் பின் முத்தியே."],"p":"","r":"Passing beyond the thirty-six and leaving 'na-ma', the soul becomes knowledge and, standing apart, rises as 'ya'; reaching the Tatpara free of darkness beyond Para, release follows, gathered in 'Si-va-ya'.","n":"The verse reads the five-letter mantra (நமசிவாய) as a path: dropping நம, traditionally the syllables of the bonds, leaves சிவாய, in which ய is the soul. The letter symbolism is the tradition's and the syntax is compressed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panchaakkaram","thaththuvam","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:166"},"t:2292":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2292,"t":["துரியத்தில் ஓரைந்தும் சொல்அக ராதி","விரியப் பரையில் மிகும்நாதம் அந்தம்","புரியப் பரையில் பராவத்தா போதம்","திரிய பரமம் துரியம் தெரியவே."],"p":"","r":"In turiya the five are the letters beginning with A; in Para spreading out, nada and its end prevail; in Para there is the awareness of the supreme state, and as it turns, the supreme turiya becomes clear.","n":"அகராதி (the letters from A) may be the five letters of the mantra or the parts of Om. பராவத்தா போதம் names the chapter's subject, but the verse gives no clear account of it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","naadham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:166"},"t:2293":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2293,"t":["ஐந்தும் சகலத்து அருளால் புரிவற்றுப்","பந்திடும் சுத்த அவத்தைப் பதைப்பினில்","நந்தி பராவத்தை நாடச் சுடர்முனம்","அந்தி இருள்போலும் ஐம்மலம் மாறுமே."],"p":"","r":"By grace the five states in the sakala condition cease to work; in the stirring of the pure state, as one seeks Nandi's supreme state, the five malas leave like evening darkness before a flame.","n":"சகலம் (the embodied condition) and சுத்தம் (the pure) are two of the soul's three conditions in Siddhanta. ஐம்மலம் counts five malas, where the tradition often counts three. The darkness-and-light simile is plain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","malam","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:166"},"t:2294":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2294,"t":["ஐஐந்து மட்டுப் பகுதியும் மாயையும்","பொய்கண்ட மாமாயை தானும் புருடன்கண்டு","எய்யும் படியாய் எவற்றுமாய் அன்றாகி","உய்யும் பராவத்தை உள்ளுதல் சுத்தமே."],"p":"","r":"When the purusha sees through the twenty-five, measured prakriti, maya and the great maya seen to be false, and becomes all of them and none of them, the saving supreme state is reached: to contemplate it is purity.","n":"ஐஐந்து (five fives) is taken as the twenty-five lower tattvas. எய்யும் படியாய் (so as to shoot through, or to attain) is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","maayai","avaththai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:166"},"t:2295":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2295,"t":["நின்றான் அருளும் பரமும்முன் நேயமும்","ஒன்றாய் மருவும் உருவும் உபாதியும்","சென்றான் எனைவிடுத்து ஆங்கிச் செல்லாமையும்","நன்றான ஞானத்தின் நாதப் பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The Lord of nada in good knowledge is the one in whom his grace, the Supreme and the love that came first become one, in whom form and limiting adjunct merge, and who left me and went there yet did not go.","n":"உபாதி (limiting adjunct) is a technical term. The paradox of going and not going (சென்றான், செல்லாமை) may be the Lord's transcendence and immanence; the verse's construction is loose.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","thiruvarul","naadham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:166"},"t:2296":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2296,"t":["சாத்திகம் எய்தும் நனவெனச் சாற்றுங்கால்","வாய்ந்த இராசதம் மன்னும் கனவென்ப","ஒய்த்திடும் தாமதம் உற்ற சுழுத்தியாம்","மாய்த்திடும் நிற்குணம் மாசில் துரியமே."],"p":"","r":"When sattva is said to belong to waking, rajas is held to be dream and tamas deep sleep; the gunaless state that puts an end to them is stainless turiya.","n":"The mapping of the gunas onto the states is the verse's own. ஒய்த்திடும் is an unusual form, perhaps 'that wears down' or 'that stills', and மாய்த்திடும் (that destroys) can belong either to the gunaless state or to turiya.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mukkunam","avaththai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:167"},"t:2297":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2297,"t":["பெறுபகி ரண்டம் பேதித்த அண்டம்","எறிகடல் ஏழின் மணல்அள வாகப்","பொறியொளி பொன்னணி யென்ன விளங்கிச்","செறியும் அண் டாசனத் தேவர் பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The outer cosmic egg and the eggs divided within it, as many as the grains of sand of the seven wave-tossed seas, shine like sparks of light and gold ornaments, and the Lord of the gods sits on them as his seat.","n":"பகிரண்டம் is the outer egg enclosing the worlds. அண்டாசனம் (the egg as seat) is taken here as the Lord enthroned over the cosmos; the sand of the seas is a conventional figure for numberless worlds.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["andam","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:168"},"t:2298":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2298,"t":["ஆனந்த தத்துவம் அண்டா சலத்தின்மேல்","மேனிஐந்தாக வியாத்தம்முப் பத்தாறாய்க்","தான்அந்த மில்லாத தத்துவம் ஆனவை","ஈனமி லா அண்டத்து எண்மடங்கு ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"Above the cosmic mountain the bliss tattva, with five bodies, pervades as the thirty-six; the endless tattvas it becomes are eightfold in the flawless egg.","n":"ஆனந்த தத்துவம், அண்டாசலம் (cosmic mountain, or perhaps அண்டாசனம் again), the five bodies and the eightfold multiple are cosmological terms the verse doesn't explain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["andam","thaththuvam","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:168"},"t:2299":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2299,"t":["அஞ்சில் அமுதும்ஓர் ஏழின்கண் ஆனந்தம்","முஞ்சில்ஓங் காரம் ஓர் ஒன்பான் பதினொன்றில்","வஞ்சக மேநின்று வைத்திடில் காயமாம்","கிஞ்சுகக் செவ்வாய்க் கிளிமொழி கேளே."],"p":"","r":"In the fifth there is nectar, in the seventh bliss, in the ninth Om, and in the eleventh, if one sets it and holds it there by craft, it becomes the body: listen, you with lips red as kinchuka flowers and the speech of a parrot.","n":"The numbers are taken as stations or centres in the yogic body, which the verse does not name. முஞ்சில் is obscure, and வஞ்சகமே நின்று (standing by guile, or by craft) and காயமாம் (becomes the body, or becomes firm) are uncertain. The addressee is a woman, often taken as Shakti.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["amudham","aanandham","piranavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:169"},"t:2300":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2300,"t":["புருட னுடனே பொருந்திய சித்தம்","அருவமொ டாறும் அதீதத் துரியம்","விரியும் சுழுத்தியின் மிக்குள்ள எட்டும்","அரிய பதினொன்று மாம்அவ் அவத்தையே."],"p":"","r":"The mind joined with purusha, the formless with the six, turiya beyond, and the eight that abound in spreading deep sleep: these are the rare eleven states.","n":"The verse names the eleven stations as states, but how its items add up to eleven is not clear from the text.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:169"},"t:2301":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2301,"t":["காட்டும் பதினொன்றும் கைகலந் தால்உடல்","நாட்டி அழுத்திடின் நந்திஅல்லால் இல்லை","ஆட்டம்செய் யாத அதுவிதி யேநினை","ஈட்டு மதுதிடம் எண்ணலும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"When the eleven that are shown come together and one fixes and presses the body down, there is nothing but Nandi; think on that rule which does not waver: what it gathers is firmness, and thought becomes possible.","n":"உடல் நாட்டி அழுத்திடின் (fixing and pressing the body) sounds like a yogic posture or lock. The last line is loose; ஈட்டும் அது திடம் may mean the practice gains steadiness.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","sivam","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:169"},"t:2302":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2302,"t":["கேவலம் தன்னில் கலவச் சகலத்தின்","மேவும் செலவு விடவரு நீக்கத்துப்","பாவும் தனைக்கண்டால் மூன்றும் படர்வற்ற","தீதறு சாக்கிரா தீதத்தில் சுத்தமே."],"p":"","r":"Joining in the kevala condition, going in the sakala, and the removal that comes as they leave: when one sees oneself spread through these, the three cease to spread, and in the faultless waking-beyond there is purity.","n":"கேவலம், சகலம் and சுத்தம் are the soul's three conditions in Siddhanta: alone with its mala, embodied, and pure. Whether மூன்றும் is these three conditions or the three malas is not settled by the verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","malam","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:170"},"t:2303":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2303,"t":["வெல்லும் அளவில் விடுமின் வெகுளியைச்","செல்லும் அளவும் செலுத்துமிஹ் சிந்தையை","அல்லும் பகலும் அருளுடன் தூங்கினால்","கல்லும் பிளந்து கடுவெளி யாமே."],"p":"","r":"While you can still win, let go of anger; as far as it will go, direct this mind; if night and day you rest in grace, even stone will split open and become the vast space.","n":"வெல்லும் அளவில் (while you can prevail) puts the test of giving up anger where it has power, as the Kural does. தூங்கினால் (if you sleep, or rest, or hang on) may point to yogic sleep in grace. கல் may be the hard heart, and கடுவெளி the open space taken as Siva's. Sengalvaraya notes a near-parallel line in Kandar Alankaram 15.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","manam","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:170"},"t:2304":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2304,"t":["ஊமைக் கிணற்றகத் துள்ளே உறைவதோர்","ஆமையின் உள்ளே அழுவைகள் ஐந்துள","வாய்மையின் உள்ளே வழுவாது ஒடுங்குமேல்","ஆமையின் மேலும்ஓர் ஆயிரத்து ஆண்டே."],"p":"","r":"Inside a silent well lives a tortoise with five hollows within it; if it withdraws into truth without slipping, it lives a thousand years beyond a tortoise's span.","n":"ஊமைக் கிணறு (the dumb or silent well) is commonly taken as the body or its inner cavity, and the tortoise drawing in its five as the soul withdrawing the five senses. அழுவைகள் is obscure (hollows, depths). The last line may promise long life to the yogi; the plain and the symbolic readings both stand.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","vaaimai","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:171"},"t:2305":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2305,"t":["காலங்கி நீர்பூக் கலந்தஆ காயம்","மாலங்கி ஈசன் பிரமன் சதாசிவன்","மேலஞ்சும் ஓடி விரவவல் லார்கட்குக்","காலனும் இல்லை கருத்தில்லை தானே."],"p":"","r":"For those who can run past the five above, the elements of air, fire, water and earth with space and the gods Mal, Isan, Brahma and Sadasivan, and mingle beyond them, there is no Death and no thought of their own.","n":"The gods are traditionally the rulers of the five elements; அங்கி (fire) in line 2 may be a god's name or the element repeated. மேலஞ்சும் ஓடி (running over the five) is taken as passing through the centres of the elements in the body. கருத்தில்லை may mean freedom from the thinking mind or simply 'no concern'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","kootru","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:171"},"t:2306":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2306,"t":["ஆன்மாவே மைந்தன் ஆயினன் என்பது","தான்மா மறையறை தன்மை அறிகிலர்","ஆன்மாவே மைந்தன் அரனுக்கு இவன்என்றால்","ஆன்மாவும் இல்லையால் ஐஐந்தும் இல்லையே."],"p":"","r":"People do not grasp what the great Veda declares in saying the self became the son; if the self is called Hara's son, then the self as separate is gone and so are the twenty-five.","n":"The first line seems to echo the Vedic saying that the self is born as one's son. ஐஐந்து (five fives) is taken as the twenty-five tattvas. The argument is compressed and I can't fix whether it affirms or refutes the Vedic claim.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vedham","pasu","thaththuvam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:171"},"t:2307":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2307,"t":["உதயம் அழுங்கில் ஒடுங்கல்இம் மூன்றின்","கதிசாக் கிரங்கன வாதி சுழுத்தி","பதிதரு சேதனன் பற்றாம் துரியத்து","அதிசுப னாய்அனந் தான் அந்தி யாகுமே."],"p":"","r":"When rising ceases, the three states of waking, dream and deep sleep subside; the conscious soul, held by the Lord in turiya, becomes supremely auspicious and endless at its close.","n":"சாக்கிரம் (waking), கனவு (dream), சுழுத்தி (deep sleep) and துரியம் (the fourth) are the standard states of the soul. உதயம் அழுங்கில் (when rising is checked) and அந்தி (the close, or twilight) are unclear; பதிதரு சேதனன் may be 'the conscious one given by the Lord' or 'the conscious one who attains the Lord'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","pasu","pathi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:171"},"t:2308":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2308,"t":["எல்லாம்தன் னுட்புக யாவுளும் தானாகி","நல்லாம் துரியம் புரிந்தக்கால் நல்லுயிர்","பொல்லாத ஆறாறுள் போகாது போதமாய்ச்","செல்லாச் சிவகதி சென்றுஎய்தும் அன்றே."],"p":"","r":"When everything enters into oneself and one becomes all, and one dwells in good turiya, the good soul does not go into the evil thirty-six but becomes awareness and reaches Siva's state, from which there is no going.","n":"ஆறாறு is the thirty-six tattvas, called பொல்லாத (evil) as what binds. செல்லாச் சிவகதி may be 'the Siva-state not reached by going' or 'from which one does not leave'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","thaththuvam","sivam","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:171"},"t:2309":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2309,"t":["காய்ந்த இரும்பு கனலை அகன்றாலும்","வாய்ந்த கனலென வாதனை நின்றாற்போல்","ஏய்ந்த கரணம் இறந்த துரியத்துத்","தோய்ந்த கருமத் துரிசுஅக லாதே."],"p":"","r":"As heated iron, even after the fire is taken away, keeps a heat like fire, so in turiya, where the instruments have died away, the stain of karma steeped in does not leave.","n":"வாதனை (vasana) is the impression a deed leaves behind. The verse warns that reaching turiya does not by itself remove karma's residue; I read it as a check on the confidence of 2308.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","avaththai","malam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:171"},"t:2310":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2310,"t":["ஆன மறையாதி யாம் உரு நந்திவந்து","ஏனை அருள்செய் தெரிநனா அவத்தையில்","ஆன வகையை விடும்அடைத் தாய்விட","ஆன மாலதீதம் அப்பரந் தானே."],"p":"","r":"Nandi, whose form is the Veda and the rest, came and gave grace in the clear waking state; when the states as they came are closed and let go, that state beyond delusion is the Supreme.","n":"தெரிநனா அவத்தை (the clear or discerning waking state) is taken as the waking state of the freed soul. ஆன மாலதீதம் is obscure: மால் அதீதம் (beyond delusion) or a form of மலாதீதம் (beyond malam).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","thiruvarul","avaththai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:171"},"t:2311":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2311,"t":["சுத்த அதீதம் சகலத்தில் தோய்வுறில்","அத்தன் அருள்நீங்கா ஆங்கணில் தானாகச்","சித்த சுகத்தைத் தீண்டாச் சமாதிசெய்து","அத்தனோடு ஒன்றற்கு அருள்முத லாமே."],"p":"","r":"If the pure state beyond touches the embodied state, the Father's grace does not leave; there, being oneself, practising a samadhi that does not touch the mind's pleasure, grace becomes the first step to being one with the Father.","n":"சுத்த அதீதம் (the pure beyond) and சகலம் (the soul's embodied state) are technical terms. சித்த சுகம் is taken as the pleasure of the mind, which this samadhi does not seek.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","samaadhi","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:171"},"t:2312":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2312,"t":["வேறுசெய் தான்இரு பாதியின் மெய்த்தொகை","வேறுசெய் தான்என்னை எங்கணும் விட்டுய்த்தான்","வேறுசெய் யாஅருள் கேவலத் தேவிட்டு","வேறுசெய் யாஅத்தன் மேவிநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"He divided the whole of the body into two halves, set me apart and sent me everywhere; then, leaving me in kevala with a grace that does not divide, the Father who makes no division came and stayed joined with me.","n":"வேறுசெய் opens every line: twice the Lord separates, twice he does not. இரு பாதியின் மெய்த்தொகை may be the body's two halves or the two kinds of bond. கேவலம் is the soul's isolated state, bound only by anava.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","pasu","avaththai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:171"},"t:2313":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2313,"t":["கறங்குஓலை கொள்ளிவட் டம்கட லில்திரை","நிறஞ்சேர் ததிமத்தன் மலத்தே நின்றங்கு","அறங்காண் சுவர்க்க நரகம் புவிசேர்த்து","கிரங்கா உயிர்அரு ளால்இவை நீங்குமே."],"p":"","r":"Like a whirling leaf-wheel, a firebrand swung in a circle, waves on the sea or a churning stick in curds, the soul stays in its malam and is turned through heaven, hell and earth according to its deeds; by grace it is freed of these.","n":"All four images are of endless turning in one place. அறங்காண் (seen by deeds) and கிரங்கா (perhaps 'not grieving' or 'unsteady') are unclear, so who is freed and how exactly is not fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["malam","piravi","thiruvarul","naragam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:171"},"t:2314":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2314,"t":["தானே சிவமான தன்மை தலைப்பட","ஆன மலமும்அப் பாச பேதமும்","ஆன குணமும் பரான்மா உபாதியும்","பானுவின் முன்மதி போல்பலராவே."],"p":"","r":"When the state of oneself becoming Siva is reached, malam, the kinds of bond, the gunas and the soul's limiting adjuncts fade like the moon before the sun.","n":"உபாதி is the limiting adjunct. The last word பலராவே is unclear; the sun and moon image needs a sense like 'grow faint', and I take it so.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","malam","paasam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:171"},"t:2315":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2315,"t":["நெருப்புண்டு நீருண்டு வாயுவும் உண்டங்கு","அருக்கனும் சோமனும் அங்கே அமரும்","திருத்தக்க மாலும் திசைமுகன் தானும்","உருத்திர சோதியும் உள்ளத்து ளாரே."],"p":"","r":"Fire, water and air are there; the sun and the moon dwell there; worthy Mal, four-faced Brahma and Rudra's light are all within the heart.","n":"Plainly, the elements, the lights and the gods are within. Tradition also reads the sun and moon as the right and left breath channels and the gods as rulers of centres in the body; that reading is symbolic and not fixed by the verse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","aadhiththan","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:171"},"t:2316":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2316,"t":["ஆனைகள் ஐந்தம் அடங்கி அறிவென்னும்","ஞானத் திரியைக் கொளுவி அதனுட்புக்கு","ஊனை இருளற நோக்கும் ஒருவற்கு","வானகம் ஏற வழிஎளி தாமே."],"p":"","r":"For one who tames the five elephants, lights the wick of knowledge, enters it and looks at the flesh until its darkness is gone, the way to climb to the sky is easy.","n":"The five elephants are the senses. The flesh (ஊன்) is not despised but looked into until it is lit. வானகம் may be heaven, release, or the space at the crown of the head of yogic practice.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","gnaanam","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:171"},"t:2317":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2317,"t":["ஆடிய காலில் அசைக்கின்ற வாயுவும்","தாடித் தெழுந்த தமருக ஓசையும்","பாடி எழுகின்ற வேதாக மங்களும்","நாடியின் உள்ளாக நான்கண்ட வாறே."],"p":"","r":"The breath that sways the dancing foot, the sound of the damaru rising as it is struck, and the Vedas and Agamas rising in song: this is how I saw them within the nadi.","n":"Siva's dance with its drum and the scriptures it sounds are placed within the body's channel. Whether this is a vision in practice or an image for the dance in the body is not fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thirukkoothu","naadi","praanan","naadham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:171"},"t:2318":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2318,"t":["முன்னை அறிவினில் செய்த முதுதவம்","பின்னை அறிவினைப் பெற்றால் அறியலாம்","தன்னை அறிவது அறிவாம் அஃ தன்றிப்","பின்னை அறிவது பேயறி வாகுமே."],"p":"","r":"The ancient austerity done in an earlier understanding can be known once the later understanding is gained; knowing oneself is knowledge, and anything else known afterwards is a ghoul's knowledge.","n":"முன்னை அறிவு and பின்னை அறிவு play on earlier and later knowing; one reading is that tapas of past lives bears fruit as present insight. பேயறிவு (a ghoul's knowledge) is blunt contempt for knowing that leaves out the self.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","meyyunarvu","thavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:171"},"t:2319":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2319,"t":["செயலற் றிருக்கச் சிவானந்த மாகும்","செயலற் றிருப்பார் சிவயோகம் தேடார்","செயலற் றிருப்பார் செகத்தோடுங் கூடார்","செயலற் றிருப்பார்க்கே செய்தியுண் டாமே."],"p":"","r":"Being without action is Siva's bliss; those who rest without action do not seek Siva-yoga, do not mingle with the world, and only they have the work done.","n":"செயலற்றிருத்தல் is traditionally taken as giving up one's own agency to the Lord, not idleness. செய்தியுண்டாம் is open: 'there is achievement' or 'there are tidings'. Even seeking Siva-yoga is counted as action.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aanandham","thuravu","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:171"},"t:2320":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2320,"t":["தான்அவ னாகும் சமாதிகை கூடினால்","ஆன மலம்அறும் அப்பசுத் தன்மைபோம்","ஈனமில் காயம் இருக்கும் இருநிலத்து","ஊனங்கள் எட்டும் ஒழித்தொன்று வோர்கட்கே."],"p":"","r":"If the samadhi in which oneself becomes Him is reached, malam is cut off and that bound-soul nature goes; for those who remove the eight defects and become one, the flawless body stays on the great earth.","n":"The body staying on earth after malam is cut is read as release while living (jivanmukti). ஊனங்கள் எட்டு (eight defects) are not named; they may be the eightfold subtle body or eight bonds.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["samaadhi","malam","pasu","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:171"},"t:2321":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2321,"t":["தொலையா அரனடி தோன்றும் அம் சத்தி","தொலையா இருளொளி தோற்ற அணுவும்","தொலையாத் தொழின்ஞானம் தொன்மையில் நண்ணித்","தொலையாத பெத்தம்முத் திக்கிடை தோயுமே."],"p":"","r":"Hara's unfailing feet and his Shakti, unfailing darkness and light, the soul that appears, and unfailing action and knowledge meet from of old, and the soul is steeped between unfailing bondage and release.","n":"தொலையா (unfailing, unending) opens every line. அணு (the atom) is the Siddhanta name for the soul; பெத்தம் is bondage. The grammar is loose and the reading is a guess at the joints.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvadi","saththi","pasu","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:171"},"t:2322":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2322,"t":["தோன்றிய பெத்தமும் முத்தியும் சூழ்சத்தி","மான்றும் தெருண்டும் உயிர்பெறும் மற்றவை","தான்தரு ஞானம் தன் சத்திக்குச் சாதனாம்","ஊன்றல்இல் லாஉள் ளொளிக்கு ஒளி யாமே."],"p":"","r":"Through the Shakti who encompasses bondage and release as they arise, the soul is now bewildered and now clear; the knowledge she gives becomes the means to her, and a light to the unsupported light within.","n":"மான்றும் தெருண்டும் (bewildered and cleared) is the soul's state in bondage and in release, both under the same Shakti. ஊன்றல் இல்லா உள்ளொளி may be the soul's own light, which has no footing of its own.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","gnaanam","pasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:171"},"t:2323":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2323,"t":["அறிகின்றி லாதன ஐஏழும் ஒன்றும்","அறிகின்ற என்னை அறியா திருந்தேன்","அறிகின்றாய் நீயென்று அருள்செய்தான் நந்தி","அறிகின்ற நானென்று அறிந்துகொண் டேனே."],"p":"","r":"The thirty-five and the one do not know, and I did not know myself, the one who knows; Nandi graced me, saying 'You are the one who knows', and I came to know that I am the knower.","n":"ஐஏழும் ஒன்றும் (five sevens and one) is taken as the thirty-six tattvas, which in Siddhanta are inert. The guru does not give the soul knowledge but shows it that it is the knower.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["guru","pasu","thaththuvam","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:171"},"t:2324":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2324,"t":["தான்அவ னாகிய ஞானத் தலைவனை","வானவ ராதியை மாமணிச் சோதியை","ஈனமில் ஞானத்து இன்னருள் சத்தியை","ஊனமிலாள்தன்னை ஊனிடைக் கண்டதே."],"p":"","r":"What was seen within the flesh: the lord of the knowledge in which oneself becomes Him, first of the gods, the light of a great gem, and the flawless Shakti of sweet grace in faultless knowledge.","n":"The verse is a list of objects with one verb at the end, கண்டதே (it was seen). Siva and Shakti are both seen within the flesh (ஊனிடை).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","saththi","yaakkai","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:171"},"t:2325":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2325,"t":["ஒளியும் இருளும் பரையும் பரையுள்","அளியது எனலாகும் ஆன்மாவை யன்றி","அளியும் அருளும் தெருளும் கடந்து","தெளிய அருளே சிவானந்த மாமே."],"p":"","r":"Light and darkness and Parai, and what is called tender regard within Parai, are apart from the soul; passing beyond tenderness, grace and clarity, the grace that makes clear is Siva's bliss.","n":"பரை is the supreme Shakti; அளி (tender regard), அருள் and தெருள் are close words, and the distinctions between them are not explained. The verse seems to rank stages of grace below a final one.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","saththi","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:171"},"t:2326":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2326,"t":["ஆனந்த மாகும் அரனருட் சத்தியில்","தான் அந்த மாம்உயிர் தானே சமாதிசெய்து","ஊன்அந்த மாய் உணர் வாய்உள் உணர்வுறில்","கோன்அந்தம் வாய்க்கும் மகாவா கியமாமே."],"p":"","r":"In Hara's grace-Shakti, which is bliss, the soul that has come to its end does samadhi itself; if the flesh ends and one feels within as awareness, the Lord's end is reached: this is the great saying.","n":"The verse plays on அந்தம் (end): ஆனந்தம், தான் அந்தம், ஊன் அந்தம், கோன் அந்தம். மகாவாக்கியம் (the great saying) is the Vedantic term for the identity statements, here given a Saiva sense; which saying is meant is not stated.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aanandham","samaadhi","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:171"},"t:2327":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2327,"t":["அறிவிக்க வேண்டாம் அறிவற்று அயர்வோர்க்கும்","அறிவிக்க வேண்டாம் அறிவிற் செறிவோர்க்கும்","அறிவுற்று அறியாமை எய்திநிற் போர்க்கே","அறிவிக்கத் தம்அறி வார்அறி வோரே."],"p":"","r":"There is no need to teach those who are sunk without knowledge, nor those who are full of knowledge; the wise who know teach only those who, having gained knowledge, stand in unknowing.","n":"The third group is the fit disciple. அறிவுற்று அறியாமை எய்தி may mean those who have learned enough to know they do not know, or those who have passed through knowledge into a higher unknowing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["guru","seedan","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:171"},"t:2328":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2328,"t":["சத்தும் அசத்தும் சதசத்தும் தான்கூடிச்","சித்தும் அசித்தும் சிவசித்த தாய்நிற்கும்","சுத்தம் அசுத்தம் தொடங்காத துரியத்துச்","சுத்தரா மூன்றுடன் சொல்லற் றவர்களே."],"p":"","r":"Sat, asat and sadasat come together; the conscious and the inert stand as Siva's consciousness; in turiya, where pure and impure do not begin, the pure with the three are beyond words.","n":"In Siddhanta சத்து (the real) is the Lord, அசத்து the bond and சதசத்து the soul, which takes the nature of what it joins. மூன்றுடன் (with the three) may be these three or three states.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pathi","pasu","paasam","avaththai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:171"},"t:2329":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2329,"t":["தானே அறியான் அறிவிலோன் தானல்லன்","தானே அறிவான் அறிவு சதசத்தென்று","ஆனால் இரண்டும் அரனரு ளாய்நிற்கத்","தானே அறிந்து சிவத்துடன் தங்குமே."],"p":"","r":"The soul does not know by itself, yet it is not without knowing; it knows, and its knowing is sadasat; so when both stand as Hara's grace, it knows of itself and abides with Siva.","n":"This is the Siddhanta account of the soul as சதசத்து: it can know, but only when something illumines it. The verse places that illumining in grace.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasu","arivu","thiruvarul","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:171"},"t:2330":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2330,"t":["தத்துவ ஞானம் தலைப்பட் டவர்க்கே","தத்துவ ஞானம் தலைப்பட லாய்நிற்கும்","தத்துவ ஞானத்துத் தான்அவ னாகவே","தத்துவ ஞானானந் தந்தான் தொடங்குமே."],"p":"","r":"Only for those who have reached tattva-knowledge does tattva-knowledge stand reached; in tattva-knowledge, as oneself becomes Him, the bliss of tattva-knowledge begins.","n":"தத்துவ ஞானம் opens every line. It may mean knowledge of the tattvas or knowledge of the real; the verse moves from attaining it to its bliss.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","thaththuvam","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:171"},"t:2331":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2331,"t":["தன்னை அறிந்து சிவனுடன் தானாக","மன்னும் மலம்குணம் மாளும் பிறப்பறும்","பின்அது சன்முத்தி சன்மார்க்கப் பேரொளி","நன்னது ஞானத்து முத்திரை நண்ணுமே."],"p":"","r":"When one knows oneself and becomes oneself with Siva, the abiding malam and gunas die and birth ends; then follows true release, the great light of sanmarga, and the good seal of knowledge is reached.","n":"சன்முத்தி is taken as true release. ஞானத்து முத்திரை may be a literal hand-seal of the jnani or the sealing of knowledge.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","malam","sanmaarkkam","muththirai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:171"},"t:2332":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2332,"t":["ஞானம்தன் மேனி கிரியை நடுஅங்கம்","தானுறும் இச்சை உயிராகத் தற்பரன்","மேனிகொண்டு ஐங்கரு மத்தவித் தாதலான்","மோனிகள் ஞானத்து முத்திரை பெற் றார்களே."],"p":"","r":"Knowledge is his body, action his middle limbs and the will that reaches him his life; since the Supreme takes this form and is the seed of the five acts, the silent ones obtained the seal of knowledge.","n":"Jnana, kriya and iccha (knowledge, action, will) are the Lord's three Shaktis; here they are his body, limbs and life. மோனிகள் are the silent sages.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","aindhozhil","mounam","muththirai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:171"},"t:2333":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2333,"t":["உயிர்க்குஅறி உண்மை உயிர்இச்சை மானம்","உயிர்க்குக் கிரியை உயிர்மாயை சூக்கம்","உயிர்க்குஇவை ஊட்டுவோன் ஊட்டும் அவனே","உயிர்ச்சொல் அன்றி அவ்வுளத்து ளானே."],"p":"","r":"Knowledge is the soul's truth, will its measure, action the soul's, and maya the subtle for the soul; he who feeds these to the soul is the feeder, and he is within that mind, not within the soul's word.","n":"மானம் (measure) and சூக்கம் (the subtle) are unclear here. The plain point is that the Lord gives the soul its powers and is within it beyond what the soul can say.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pasu","maayai","pathi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:171"},"t:2334":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2334,"t":["தொழில்இச்சை ஞானங்கள் தொல்சிவசீவர்","கழிவற்ற மாமாயை மாயையின் ஆகும்","பழியற்ற காரண காரியம் பாழ்விட்டு","அழிவற்ற சாந்தாதீ தன்சிவ னாமே."],"p":"","r":"The action, will and knowledge of the ancient Siva and of the souls arise from undying Mahamaya and maya; leaving the void of flawless cause and effect, Siva is beyond the undying Santa.","n":"சாந்தாதீதன் (beyond Santa) names Siva above the highest kala, சாந்தி. That Siva's own powers should come from maya is surprising, and the line may mean only that their workings appear through maya.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maayai","sivam","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:171"},"t:2335":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2335,"t":["இல்லதும் உள்ளதும் யாவையும் தானாகி","இல்லதம் உள்ளது மாய்அன்றாம் அண்ணலைச்","சொல்வது சொல்லிடில் தூராகி தூரமென்று","ஒல்லை உணர்ந்தால் உயிர்க்குயி ராகுமே."],"p":"","r":"The Lord becomes all, what is not and what is, yet is neither not-being nor being; whatever one says of him, he is far beyond far; but understood quickly, he becomes the life of life.","n":"The verse turns on the gap between saying and understanding: in speech the Lord is ever farther, in understanding the nearest.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","uyir","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:171"},"t:2336":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2336,"t":["உயிரிச்சை யூட்டி உழிதரும் சத்தி","உயிரிச்சை வாட்டி ஒழித்திடும் ஞானம்","உயிரிச்சை யூட்டி யுடனுறலாலே","உயிரிச்சை வாட்டி உயர்பதஞ் சேருமே."],"p":"","r":"Shakti feeds the soul's desire and wanders with it; knowledge withers the soul's desire and ends it; because she feeds it and stays with it, by withering the soul's desire she brings it to the high state.","n":"உயிரிச்சை (the soul's desire) opens every line. The same Shakti first feeds desire and then withers it; tradition reads this as concealment followed by grace.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saththi","gnaanam","avaa","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:171"},"t:2337":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2337,"t":["சேரும் சிவமானார் ஐம்மலம் தீர்ந்தவர்","ஓர்ஒன்றி லார் ஐம் மலஇருள் உற்றவர்","பாரின்கண் விண்நர கம்புகும் பான்மையர்","ஆருங்கண் டோ ரார் அவையருள் என்றே."],"p":"","r":"Those freed of the five malams become Siva and join him; those not at one remain in the darkness of the five malams, of the kind that go from earth to heaven and hell; no one sees that these too are grace.","n":"ஐம்மலம் (five malams) is traditionally anava, karma, maya, mayeya and tirodhayi. The last line is taken to say that heaven and hell are themselves grace, a ripening, but the grammar is unsure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["malam","sivam","naragam","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:171"},"t:2338":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2338,"t":["எய்தினர் செய்யும் இருமாயா சத்தியின்","எய்தினர் செய்யும் இருஞான சத்தியின்","எய்தினர் செய்யும் இருஞால சத்தியின்","எய்தினர் செய்யும் இறையருள் தானே."],"p":"","r":"What those who have reached do through the great maya-Shakti, through the great Shakti of knowledge and through the great Shakti of the world: all of it is the Lord's grace.","n":"இருமாயா may be 'great maya' or 'the two mayas', pure and impure. எய்தினர் (those who have reached) are the freed, whose acts are counted as the Lord's.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","saththi","maayai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:171"},"t:2339":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2339,"t":["திருந்தினர் விட்டார் திருவில் நரகம்","திருந்தினர் விட்டார் திருவார் சுவர்க்கம்","திருந்தினர் விட்டார் செறிமலர் கூட்டம்","திருந்தினர் விட்டார் சிவமாய் அவமே."],"p":"","r":"The reformed have left wretched hell, left splendid heaven, left the gathering of crowded flowers, and, becoming Siva, left what is vain.","n":"திருந்தினர் (those set right) opens every line. செறிமலர் கூட்டம் is obscure: the pleasures of the senses, the company of women adorned with flowers, or the world of births. அவம் (the vain) answers சிவம் in sound.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["veedu","naragam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:171"},"t:2340":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2340,"t":["அவமும் சிவமும் அறியார் அறியார்","அவமும் சிவமும் அறிவார் அறிவார்","அவமும் சிவமும் அருளால் அறிந்தால்","அவமும் சிவமும் அவனரு ளாமே."],"p":"","r":"Those who do not know the vain and Siva do not know; those who know the vain and Siva know; if they know the vain and Siva through grace, both the vain and Siva become his grace.","n":"அவமும் சிவமும் opens every line, pairing the futile with the auspicious. The final claim is that even what is vain, known through grace, is his grace.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","thiruvarul","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:171"},"t:2341":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2341,"t":["அருளான சத்தி அனல் வெம்மை போல","பொருள் அவனாகத்தான் போதம் புணரும்","இருள் ஒளியாய் மீண்டு மும்மல மாகும்","திருவருள் ஆனந்தி செம்பொருளாமே."],"p":"","r":"Grace-Shakti is like heat in fire; with the substance being He, it joins awareness; darkness turned to light returns as the three malams; the holy grace, the blissful one, is the true substance.","n":"Heat in fire is the classic image for Shakti inseparable from Siva. Line 3 is unclear: perhaps light can fall back into the three malams, or grace works even through them.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","thiruvarul","malam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:171"},"t:2342":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2342,"t":["ஆதித்தன் தோன்ற வரும்பது மாதிகள்","பேதித்த தவ்வினை யாற்செயல் சேதிப்ப","ஆதித்தன் தன்கதி ரால்அவை சேட்டிப்பப்","பேதித்தப் பேதியா வாறுஅருட் பேதமே."],"p":"","r":"When the sun rises, the lotus and the rest differ in what they do by their deeds; the sun with its rays sets them moving; that it divides without dividing is the distinction of grace.","n":"One sun opens some flowers and closes others; tradition reads this as one grace working differently on souls by their karma. பதுமாதிகள் is taken as 'lotuses and others'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aadhiththan","thiruvarul","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:171"},"t:2343":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2343,"t":["பேதம் அபேதம் பிறழ்பேதா பேதமும்","போதம் புணர்போதம் போதமும் நாதமும்","நாத முடன்நாக நாதாதி நாதமும்","ஆதன் அருளின் அருள்இச்சை யாமே."],"p":"","r":"Difference, non-difference and shifting difference-in-non-difference, awareness and joined awareness, awareness and nada, and nada with naga-nada and the nadas that follow: all are the will of the Lord's grace.","n":"பேதம், அபேதம், பேதாபேதம் are the three ways of relating soul and Lord argued among the schools. நாகநாதம் and the other nadas are technical and I can't fix them. ஆதன் may be the Lord or the soul.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","naadham","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:171"},"t:2344":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2344,"t":["மேவிய பொய்க்கரி யாட்டும் வினையெனப்","பாவிய பூதம்கொண்டு ஆட்டிப் படைப்பாதி","பூவியல் கூட்டத்தால் போதம் புரிந்தருள்","ஆவியை நாட்டும் அரன்அரு ளாமே."],"p":"","r":"Like the deed that makes a false puppet dance, he makes the elements he spread dance, creating and the rest; through the gathering of earthly things he brings awareness and settles the soul: that is Hara's grace.","n":"பொய்க்கரி may be a puppet elephant or a false image moved by strings. பூவியல் கூட்டம் is taken as the assembly of earthly things.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","aindhozhil","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:171"},"t:2345":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2345,"t":["ஆறாது அகன்று தனையறிந் தானவன்","ஈறாகி யாவினும் இயலாவும் தனில்எய்த","வேறாய் வெளிபுக்கு வீடுற்றான் அம்மருள்","தேறாத் தெளிவுற்றுத் தீண்டச் சிவமாமே."],"p":"","r":"He who knew himself, going off without stopping, becoming the end, reaching in himself all that is and is not, separate and entering space, gained release; that confusion reaching a clarity not yet clear, at the touch becomes Siva.","n":"The grammar is very loose. அம்மருள் தேறாத் தெளிவுற்று (that confusion gaining a clarity not reached by reasoning) is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["veedu","sivam","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:171"},"t:2346":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2346,"t":["தீண்டற்குரிய அரிய திருவடி நேயத்தை","மீண்டுற்று அருளால் விதிவழியே சென்று","தூண்டிச் சிவஞான மாவினைத் தானேறித்","தாண்டிச் சிவனுடன் சாரலும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"Turning again to love of the rare holy feet that are worthy to touch, going by grace along the ordained way, spurring and mounting the great horse of Siva-knowledge and leaping across, one can reach Siva.","n":"மா here is taken as horse, so Siva-knowledge is a mount one rides across. விதிவழி (the ordained way) may be scriptural rule or the course set by grace.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvadi","thiruvarul","gnaanam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:171"},"t:2347":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2347,"t":["சார்ந்தவர் சாரணர் சித்தர் சமாதியர்","சார்ந்தவர் மெய்ஞ்ஞான தத்துவ சாத்தியர்","சார்ந்தவர் நேயந் தலைப்ட்ட ஆனந்தர்","சார்ந்தவர் சத்த அருள்தன்மை யாரே."],"p":"","r":"Those who have reached him are the charanas, the siddhas and those in samadhi; those who achieve true knowledge of the tattvas; the blissful in whom love comes first; and those whose nature is pure grace.","n":"சாரணர் are heavenly wanderers, சித்தர் the perfected. சத்த அருள் may be 'pure grace' (சுத்த) or 'the grace of sound'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["siththi","samaadhi","aanandham","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:171"},"t:2348":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2348,"t":["தான்என்று அவன்என்று இரண்டென்பர் தத்துவம்","தான்என்று அவன்என்று இரண்டற்ற தன்மையத்","தான்என்று இரண்டுஉன்னார் கேவலத் தானவர்","தான்இன்றித் தானாகத் தத்துவ சுத்தமே."],"p":"","r":"The tattvas speak of two, oneself and He; in the state where self and He are not two, those in kevala do not think 'I'; to become oneself without a self is the purity of the tattva.","n":"தான் and அவன் open three lines. கேவலத்தானவர் may be those in the soul's isolated state, who cannot think at all, or those in a pure solitude; the verse seems to set duality, non-duality and a selfless self in order.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","avaththai","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:171"},"t:2349":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2349,"t":["தன்னினில் தன்னை அறியும் தலைமகன்","தன்னினில் தன் ஐ அறியத் தலைப்படும்","தன்னினில் தன்னைச் சார்கிலன் ஆகில்","தன்னினில் தன்ஐயும் சார்தற்கு அரியவே."],"p":"","r":"The chief who knows himself within himself sets out to know his Lord within himself; if he does not rest in himself, his Lord within him is hard to reach as well.","n":"தன் ஐ (his master) is taken as the Lord, or the guru; ஐ also suggests the five senses, but 'lord' fits better. Self-knowledge is the way to the Lord, not a rival to him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["meyyunarvu","sivam","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:171"},"t:2350":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2350,"t":["அறியகி லேன்என்று அரற்றாதே நீயும்","நெறிவழி யேசென்ற நேர்பட்ட பின்னை","இருசுட ராகி இயற்றவல் லானும்","ஒருசுட ராவந்துஎன் உள்ளத்துள் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"Do not cry 'I cannot know'; once you have gone the way of the path and met him face to face, he who can work as the two lights comes as one light within my heart.","n":"இருசுடர் (two lights) is traditionally the sun and moon, and in yoga the two breaths. The shift from 'you' to 'my heart' is the poet speaking from his own case.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","aadhiththan","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:171"},"t:2351":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2351,"t":["மண்ஒன்று தான்பல நற்கலன் ஆயிடும்","உள்கின்ற யோனிகட்டு எல்லாம் ஒருவனே","கண்ஒன்று தான்பல காணும் தனைக்காணா","அண்ணலும் அவ்வண்ணம் ஆகிநின்றானே."],"p":"","r":"One clay becomes many fine vessels, and in all the wombs one thinks of there is one; one eye sees many things but does not see itself; the Lord stands in just that way.","n":"Two images: the clay present in all its pots, and the eye that sees all but itself. ஒருவனே may be the Lord in all wombs or the one soul passing through them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","uyir","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:171"},"t:2352":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2352,"t":["ஓம்புகின் றான்உலகு ஏழையும் உள்நின்று","கூம்புகின் றார்குணத் தின்னொடும் கூறுவர்","தேம்புகின்றார்சிவன் சிந்தைசெய் யாதவர்","கூம்பகில் லார்வந்து கொள்ளலும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"He guards the seven worlds from within; some close up and speak of him through qualities; those who do not think of Siva waste away; those who do not close up can come and receive him.","n":"கூம்பு is to close like a flower, or to fold the hands in worship. Whether closing is narrowing the mind or worshipping is unclear, and the verse's contrast depends on it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","manam","thiyaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:171"},"t:2353":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2353,"t":["குறிஅறி யார்கள் குறிகாண மாட்டார்","குறிஅறி யார்கடம் கூடல் பெரிது","குறிஅறி யாவகை கூடுமின் கூடி","அறிவறி யாஇருந்து அன்னமும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"Those who do not know the mark cannot see the mark; for those who do not know it, union is a great matter; unite in a way the mark is not known, and having united, stay without knowing knowledge, and you become the swan too.","n":"குறி (mark, sign, target) is unexplained; it may be the object of meditation or the Lord's form. அன்னம் may be the swan (hamsa, the breath or the freed soul) or food. கூடல் may be union or yogic joining.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","arivu","thiyaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:171"},"t:2354":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2354,"t":["ஊனோ உயிரோ உறுகின்றது ஏதுஇன்பம்","வானோர் தலைவி மயக்கத்து உறநிற்கத்","தானோ பெரிதுஅறி வோம் என்னும் மானுடர்","தானே பிறப்போடு இறப்பறி யாரே."],"p":"","r":"Is it the flesh or the life that has the joy? While the Lady of the gods stands bewildering them, people who say 'we know, it is we who are great' do not know birth and death themselves.","n":"வானோர் தலைவி (Lady of the gods) is taken as Shakti in her veiling work, or as maya. The question in line 1 is left for the reader.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["inbam","maayai","piravi","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:171"},"t:2355":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2355,"t":["தன்னை அறியத் தனக்கொரு கேடில்லை","தன்னை அறியாமல் தானே கெடுகின்றான்","தன்னை அறியும் அறிவை அறிந்தபின்","தன்னையே அர்ச்சிக்கத் தானிருந் தானே."],"p":"","r":"Knowing oneself, one comes to no harm; not knowing oneself, one ruins oneself; once one knows the knowledge by which one knows oneself, one stays worshipping oneself.","n":"தன்னை (oneself) opens every line. தன்னையே அர்ச்சிக்க, worshipping oneself, is traditionally taken as worshipping the Lord found as or within one's own self, not as self-regard; the verse does not name the Lord, so that reading comes from the rest of the chapter.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["meyyunarvu","arivu","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:172"},"t:2356":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2356,"t":["அங்கே அடற்பெரும் தேவரெல் லாம்தொழச்","சிங்கா தனத்தே சிவன்இருந் தானென்று","சங்குஆர் வளையும் சிலம்பும் சரேலெனப்","பொங்குஆர் குழலியும் போற்றிஎன் றாளே."],"p":"","r":"There, as all the mighty great gods bowed, saying that Siva sat on the lion throne, she of the full, rich hair, her conch bangles and anklets sounding at once, said 'Praise'.","n":"அங்கே (there) does not say where. Placed after 2355 it is taken as the inner scene that opens once the self is known, Siva enthroned and Shakti (பொங்குஆர் குழலி) praising him; read plainly it is a picture of the Lord's court.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","saththi","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:172"},"t:2357":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2357,"t":["அறிவு வடிவென்று அறியாத என்னை","அறிவு வடிவென்று அருள்செய்தான் நந்தி","அறிவு வடிவென்று அருளால் உணர்ந்தே","அறிவு வடிவென்று அருந்திருந் தானே."],"p":"","r":"Nandi graciously taught me, who did not know it, that knowledge is my form; realising through grace that knowledge is my form, I remained as one whose form is knowledge.","n":"அறிவு வடிவு (knowledge as form) opens every line. Whose form it is goes unsaid: the self's, as Siddhanta takes the soul to be of the nature of knowing, or the Lord's. Sengalvaraya cites Tiruppugazh 1019, which speaks of knowledge as a holy body beyond knowing and not-knowing. The last word அருந்திருந்தானே is odd (see source issues).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","arivu","thiruvarul","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:172"},"t:2358":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2358,"t":["அறிவுக்கு அழிவில்aல ஆக்கமும் இல்லை","அறிவுக்கு அறிவல்லது ஆதாரம் இல்லை","அறிவே அறிவை அறிகின்றது என்றிட்டு","அறைகின் றனமறை ஈறுகள் தாமே."],"p":"","r":"Knowledge has no destruction and no making; knowledge has no support but knowledge; the ends of the Vedas proclaim that knowledge itself knows knowledge.","n":"ஆதாரம் here is the plain word 'support', not the bodily centres. மறை ஈறுகள் (the ends of the Vedas) are taken as the Upanishads, so the verse claims their authority for knowledge that is self-luminous.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["arivu","vedaantham","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:172"},"t:2359":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2359,"t":["ஆயு மலரின் அணிமலர் தன்மேலே","பாய இதழ்கள் பதினாறும் அங்குள","தூய அறிவு சிவானந்த மாகியே","போய அறிவாய்ப் புணர்ந்திருந் தானே."],"p":"","r":"Above the fine flower of the flower one examines there are sixteen spreading petals; there, pure knowledge became Siva's bliss and, as knowledge that had gone beyond, he stayed in union.","n":"The sixteen-petalled flower is usually taken as a lotus of the yogic body, commonly placed at the throat, though 'above the flower' may point higher. போய அறிவு (knowledge that has gone) is read as knowledge whose separate knowing has ceased. Who is joined (புணர்ந்திருந்தான்) is not said.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","aanandham","arivu","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:172"},"t:2360":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2360,"t":["மன்னிநின் றாரிடை வந்தருள் மாயத்து","முன்னிநின் றாமை மொழிந்தேன் முதல்வனும்","பொன்னின்வந் தானோர் புகழ்திரு மேனியைப்","பின்னிநின் றேன்நீ பெரியையென் றானே."],"p":"","r":"Among those who stood firm I spoke of how I had stood intent in the wondrous grace that came; the First One came like gold, I clung to his praised holy form, and he said, 'You are great.'","n":"The syntax is compressed and முன்னிநின்றாமை and மாயத்து (wondrous, or of maya) are unclear. The scene is taken as the Lord coming in a golden form, perhaps as guru, and acknowledging the devotee; the plain sense of the last two lines is fairly sure, the first two are not.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","sivam","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:172"},"t:2361":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2361,"t":["அறிவுஅறி வாக அறிந்துஅன்பு செய்மின்","அறிவுஅறி வாக அறியும்இவ் வண்ணம்","அறிவுஅறி வாக அணிமாதி சித்தி","அறிவுஅறி வாக அறிந்தணன் நந்தியே."],"p":"","r":"Know knowledge as knowledge and love; this is how knowledge is known as knowledge; when knowledge is known as knowledge the powers beginning with anima come; Nandi knew knowledge as knowledge.","n":"அறிவுஅறிவாக opens every line and can mean knowing the knower as knowledge, or knowing (the Lord) as knowledge through knowledge. The command to love (அன்பு செய்மின்) sits beside knowing, not after it. அறிந்தணன் may be அறிந்தனன் (knew) or contain அணன் (one who is near).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arivu","anbu","siththi","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:172"},"t:2362":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2362,"t":["அறிவுஅறி வென்று அங்கு அரற்றும் உலகம்","அறிவுஅறி யாமை யாரும் அறியார்","அறிவுஅறி யாமை கடந்துஅறி வானால்","அறிவுஅறி யாமை அழகிய வாறே."],"p":"","r":"The world cries 'knowledge, knowledge', yet no one knows what knowledge and ignorance are; if one passes beyond knowledge and ignorance and becomes knowledge, that is the fine way of knowledge and ignorance.","n":"The last line, அறிவுஅறியாமை அழகியவாறே, may be plain ('that is the fine way') or ironic ('how fine, this knowing and not-knowing'). The verse's point is that true knowledge is not the opposite of ignorance but lies past both the world's knowledge and its ignorance.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arivu","pedhaimai","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:172"},"t:2363":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2363,"t":["அறிவுஅறி யாமையை நீவி யவனே","பொறிவாய் ஒழிந்துஎங்கும் தானான போது","அறிவாய் அவற்றினுள் தானாய் அறிவின்","செறிவாகி நின்றவன் சிவனும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"He who removes knowing and not-knowing, when the senses cease and he becomes himself everywhere, stands as knowledge within them, as knowledge made dense; he is Siva too.","n":"The subject can be Siva, who clears away the soul's knowing and not-knowing, or the freed soul, who, the senses ceased, becomes everywhere and so is Siva too. சிவனும் ஆமே (is Siva as well) leans toward the second, but the verse allows both.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","arivu","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:172"},"t:2364":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2364,"t":["அறிவுடை யார்நெஞ்சு அகலிடம் ஆவது","அறிவுடை யார்நெஞ்ச அருந்தவம் ஆவது","அறிவுடை யார்நெஞ்சொடு ஆதிப் பிரானும்","அறிவுடை யார்நெஞ்சத்து அங்குநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"The heart of those with knowledge is the wide world; the heart of those with knowledge is rare austerity; and the first Lord stays there, with the heart of those with knowledge and in it.","n":"அறிவுடையார் நெஞ்சு opens every line. அகலிடம் (the wide earth) makes the knower's heart as large as the world, holding all; the verse then makes it the place of austerity and of the Lord.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","thavam","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:172"},"t:2365":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2365,"t":["மாயனும் ஆகி மலரோன் இறையுமாய்க்","காயநன் னாட்டுக் கருமுதல் ஆனவன்","சேயன் அணியன் தித்திக்கும் தீங்கரும்","பாய்அமு தாகிநின்று அண்ணிக்கின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"Being Vishnu and the Lord of the flower too, the first cause of the womb in the good land of the body, far and near, he stands as sweet sugarcane and nectar and tastes sweet.","n":"மாயன் and மலரோன் are Vishnu and Brahma: the one Lord takes their roles. காயநன்னாட்டுக் கருமுதல் may be the first cause of every embryo in the body, or the root seed within the body. Sengalvaraya cites Tiruvasagam and Appar for Siva as sweeter than cane juice; the sugarcane image is shared Saiva devotional language.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","karu","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:172"},"t:2366":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2366,"t":["என்னை அறிந்திலேன் இத்தனை காலமும்","என்னை அறிந்தபின் ஏதும் அறிந்திலேன்","என்னை அறிந்திட்டு இருத்தலும் கைவிடாது","என்னையிட்டு என்னை உசாவுகின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"All this time I did not know myself; once I knew myself I knew nothing else; knowing myself and never letting go of that abiding, he questions me through myself.","n":"ஏதும் அறிந்திலேன் can mean that nothing else remained to be known, or that the knower no longer knows objects at all. The last line, என்னையிட்டு என்னை உசாவுகின்றானே, is obscure: 'he' is taken as the Lord within, who inquires of the self by the self.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["meyyunarvu","arivu","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:172"},"t:2367":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2367,"t":["மாய விளக்கது நின்று மறைந்திடும்","தூய விளக்கது நின்று சுடர்விடும்","காய விளக்கது நின்று கனன்றிடும்","சேய விளக்கினைத் தேடுகின் றேனை."],"p":"","r":"The lamp of maya stays a while and goes out; the pure lamp stays and sends out its flame; the lamp of the body stays and smoulders; I am the one who seeks the distant lamp.","n":"Three lamps are set against a fourth. The pure lamp is taken as knowledge and the distant lamp (சேய விளக்கு) as Siva, but the verse does not say so. The last word தேடுகின்றேனை (me who seeks) hangs and is completed by 2368, which opens with தேடுகின்றேன்.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maayai","yaakkai","gnaanam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:172"},"t:2368":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2368,"t":["தேடுகின் றேன்திசை எட்டோ டு இரண்டையும்","நாடுகின் றேன்நல மேஉடை யானடி","பாடுகின் றேன்பர மேதுணை யாமெனக்","கூடுகின் றேன்குறை யாமனைத் தாலே."],"p":"","r":"I search the eight directions and the two besides; I look for the feet of the one who owns all good; I sing that the Supreme is my aid; I join him with a mind that never fails.","n":"The two beyond the eight directions are taken as above and below, so the search is everywhere. Four verbs ending in -கின்றேன் give the stages: search, look, sing, join. குறையாமனைத்தாலே is read as குறையா மனத்தாலே, with an unfailing mind.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvadi","vazhipaadu","sivam","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:172"},"t:2369":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2369,"t":["முன்னை முதல்விளை யாட்டத்து முன்வந்தோர்","பின்னை பெருமலம் வந்தவர் பேர்த்திட்டுத்","தன்னைத் தெரிந்துதன் பண்டைத் தலைவன்தன்","மன்னிச் சிவமாக வாரா பிறப்பே."],"p":"","r":"Those who came forth first in the Lord's first play, to whom afterwards the great malam came, remove it, know themselves, abide in their ancient Lord and become Siva, and birth does not come again.","n":"விளையாட்டு (play) is the Lord's sport of creation. பின்னை பெருமலம் வந்தவர் reads as if the malam came after the souls; Siddhanta, as in 2401 (தன் ஆதி மலம்), holds the soul's malam to be beginningless, so பின்னை may mean 'then' rather than 'later'. The close draws the chapter back to its start: knowing oneself ends birth.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["malam","meyyunarvu","saayujjiyam","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:172"},"t:2370":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2370,"t":["வேதத்தின் அந்தமும் மிக்கசித் தாந்தமும்","நாதத்தின் அந்தமும் நற்போத அந்தமும்","ஓதத் தகும்எட்டு யோகாநந்த அந்தமும்","ஆதிக்க லாந்தமும் ஆறந்தம் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"The end of the Veda, the excellent Siddhanta, the end of nada, the good end of knowing, the end of the bliss of the eightfold yoga that is worth reciting, and the end of the primal kalas: these are the six ends.","n":"The list gives the chapter's six terms: வேதாந்தம், சித்தாந்தம், நாதாந்தம், போதாந்தம், யோகாந்தம், கலாந்தம். Calling Siddhanta an அந்தம் beside Vedanta puts the two on a level, which the chapter goes on to argue.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aarandham","vedaantham","siththaantham","naadham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:173"},"t:2371":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2371,"t":["அந்தம்ஓர் ஆறும் அறிவார் அதிசுத்தர்","அந்தம்ஒர் ஆறும் அறிவார் அமலத்தர்","அந்தம்ஓர் ஆறும் அறியார் அவர்தமக்கு","அந்தமோடு ஆதி அறியஒண் ணாதே."],"p":"","r":"Those who know the six ends are the most pure; those who know the six ends are free of malam; those who do not know the six ends cannot know the end and the beginning.","n":"The last line plays on அந்தம்: not knowing the ends, one cannot know 'end and beginning', taken as the Lord who is both.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aarandham","malam","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:173"},"t:2372":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2372,"t":["தானான வேதாந்தம் தான்என்னும் சித்தாந்தம்","ஆனாத் துரியத்து அணுவன் தனைக்கண்டு","தேனார் பராபரம் சேர்சிவ யோகமாய்","ஆனா மலமற்று அரும்சித்தி யாதலே."],"p":"","r":"Vedanta, which has become the self, and Siddhanta, which says it is the self: seeing the soul in unending turiya and joining the honeyed Supreme as Siva-yoga, the malam that does not end is removed and one comes to the rare attainment.","n":"தானான and தான்என்னும் separate the two only slightly: Vedanta as having become the self, Siddhanta as declaring it. துரியம் is the fourth state beyond waking, dream and sleep, and அணுவன் the soul as anu, both technical. ஆனா மலம் may be 'the malam that does not end of itself'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vedaantham","siththaantham","yogam","malam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:173"},"t:2373":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2373,"t":["நித்தம் பரனோடு உயிருற்று நீள்மனம்","சத்தம் முதல்ஐந்தும் தத்துவத் தால்நீங்கச்","சுத்தம் அசுத்தம் தொடரா வகைநினைந்து","அத்தன் பரன்பால் அடைதல்சித் தாந்தமே."],"p":"","r":"Siddhanta is this: the soul ever joined with the Supreme, the restless mind and the five beginning with sound removed by knowing the tattvas, holding in thought that neither pure nor impure follows it, and reaching the Father, the Supreme.","n":"சத்தம் முதல் ஐந்து are the five subtle elements beginning with sound. சுத்தம் அசுத்தம் may be pure and impure maya, or good and bad deeds; the verse does not say.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["siththaantham","pasu","thaththuvam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:173"},"t:2374":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2374,"t":["மேவும் பிரமனே விண்டு உருத்திரன்","மேவுசெய் ஈசன் சதாசிவன் மிக்கு அப்பால்","மேவும் பரவிந்து நாதம் விடாஆறாறு","ஓவும் பொழுதுஅணு ஒன்றுஉள தாமே."],"p":"","r":"Brahma, Vishnu, Rudra, the acting Isa, Sadasiva, and beyond them the supreme bindu and nada: when the thirty-six that do not leave cease, the soul alone remains.","n":"The five deities and bindu and nada are the ascending ranks of the tattvas; ஆறாறு is the thirty-six. அணு is the Siddhanta term for the soul. Placed after 2373, this is taken as the account of nadanta, though the verse does not name it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","vindhu","naadham","pasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:173"},"t:2375":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2375,"t":["உள்ள உயிர்ஆறாற தாகும் உபாதியைத்","தெள்ளி அகன்றுநா தாந்தத்தைச் செற்றுமேல்","உள்ள இருள்நீங்க ஓர்iஉணர் வாகுமேல்","எள்ளலின் நாதாந்தத்து எய்திடும் போதமே."],"p":"","r":"Sifting away the adjunct of the thirty-six that the living soul bears, going past the end of nada, when the darkness above is removed it becomes one awareness: that is the knowing reached at the end of nada.","n":"The closing போதம் suggests this is bodhanta, the end of knowing. உபாதி (limiting adjunct) is a Vedanta term. செற்று (having destroyed, or pressed past) and எள்ளலின் in the last line are unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["aarandham","thaththuvam","naadham","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:173"},"t:2376":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2376,"t":["தேடும் இயம நியமாதி சென்றகன்று","ஊடும் சமாதியில் உற்றுப் படர்சிவன்","பாடுறச் சீவன் பரமாகப் பற்றறக்","கூடும் உபசாந்தம் யோகாந்தக் கொள்கையே."],"p":"","r":"Passing beyond yama, niyama and the rest that are sought, entering samadhi, Siva spreading through, the soul becoming the Supreme with attachment gone, the union in perfect calm: that is the doctrine of yoganta.","n":"இயம நியமாதி are the first limbs of eightfold yoga, so the end of yoga is reached by going beyond its steps. உபசாந்தம் is a technical term for complete stillness. படர்சிவன் பாடுற is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aarandham","yogam","samaadhi","pattru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:173"},"t:2377":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2377,"t":["கொள்கையில் ஆன கலாந்தம் குறிக்கொள்ளில்","விள்கையில் ஆன நிவிர்த்தாதி மேதாதிக்கு","உள்ளன வாம்விந்து உள்ளே ஓடுங்கலும்","தெள்ளி அதனைத் தெளிதலும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"If one marks kalanta in this doctrine: it is bindu, which belongs to the kalas beginning with nivritti and medha that appear as things unfold, withdrawing within, and clearly discerning it.","n":"நிவிர்த்தாதி names the five kalas from nivritti, a standard Siddhanta set; மேதாதி is less clear and may be a set of kalas of the syllable Om. விள்கை (unfolding) and ஒடுங்கல் (withdrawing) make kalanta the reversal of the kalas back into bindu.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aarandham","kalai","vindhu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:173"},"t:2378":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2378,"t":["தெளியும் இவையன்றித் தேர்ஐங் கலைவேறு","ஒளியுள் அமைந்துள்ளது ஓரவல் லார்கட்கு","அளியவ னாகிய மந்திரம் தந்திரம்","தெளிஉப தேசஞா னத்தொடுஐந் தாமே."],"p":"","r":"Besides these discerned kalas there are five other kalas, set within light, for those able to examine: given by the gracious one, they are mantra, tantra, clear teaching and knowledge, five in all.","n":"The count is unclear: mantra, tantra, upadesa and jnana make four, and the fifth may be the gracious one (அளியவன்) himself or something the text has lost. 2379 goes on to define them.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalai","mandhiram","guru","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:173"},"t:2379":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2379,"t":["ஆகும் அனாதி கலைஆ கமவேதம்","ஆகும்அத் தந்திரம் அந்நூல் வழிநிற்றல்","ஆகும் அனாதி உடல்அல்லா மந்திரம்","ஆகும் சிவபோ தகம்உப தேசமே."],"p":"","r":"The beginningless kala is the Agama and the Veda; tantra is standing in the way those scriptures lay down; mantra is the beginningless one who is not body; upadesa is the teaching that is knowledge of Siva.","n":"The verse defines the five of 2378 in Tirumular's own terms: tantra is practice according to scripture, not a separate text. உடல்அல்லா மந்திரம் may mean mantra as the bodiless Lord, or the Lord whose body is mantra, as the Agamas say.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aagamam","vedham","mandhiram","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:173"},"t:2380":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2380,"t":["தேசார் சிவமாகும் தன்ஞானத் தின்கலை","ஆசார நேய மறையும் கலாந்தத்துப்","பேசா உரையுணர் வற்ற பெருந்தகை","வாசா மகோசர மாநந்தி தானே."],"p":"","r":"The kala of one's own knowledge that becomes radiant Siva, and conduct, love and the Veda too, end in kalanta; the great one beyond speech, word and feeling, beyond speech and mind, is great Nandi himself.","n":"வாசாமகோசரம் is taken as the Sanskrit phrase for what speech and mind cannot reach. ஆசார நேய மறையும் may mean conduct, love and Veda all end there, or that conduct and love 'disappear' (மறையும்) in kalanta.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalai","sivam","mounam","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:173"},"t:2381":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2381,"t":["தான்அவ னாகும் சமாதி தலைப்படில்","ஆன கலாந்தநா தாந்தயோ காந்தமும்","ஏனைய போதாந்தம் சித்தாந்த மானது","ஞான மென்ஞேய ஞாதுரு வாகுமே."],"p":"","r":"If one reaches the samadhi in which oneself becomes He, kalanta, nadanta and yoganta, and the others, bodhanta and Siddhanta, become knowledge, the known and the knower.","n":"ஞானம், ஞேயம், ஞாதுரு are the triad of knowing, known and knower. The verse does not say which end becomes which, and Vedanta is not named, perhaps because it is one with Siddhanta.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["samaadhi","aarandham","gnaanam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:173"},"t:2382":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2382,"t":["ஆறந்த மும்சென்று அடங்கும்அந் நேயத்தே","ஆறந்த ஞேயம் அடங்கிடும் ஞாதுரு","கூறிய ஞானக் குறியுடன் வீடவே","தேறிய மோனம் சிவானந்த மாமே."],"p":"","r":"The six ends all go and subside in that known; the knower too subsides in the known of the six ends; when even the stated mark of knowledge departs, the clear silence becomes Siva's bliss.","n":"The triad of 2381 collapses in order: the ends into the known, the knower into the known, then knowledge itself. What remains is மோனம், silence, as a state, and it is named as bliss.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aarandham","mounam","aanandham","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:173"},"t:2383":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2383,"t":["உண்மைக் கலைஆறுஓர் ஐந்தான் அடங்கிடும்","உண்மைக் கலாந்தம் இரண்டுஐந்தோடு ஏழ்அந்தம்","உண்மைக் கலைஒன்றில் ஈறாய நாதாந்தத்து","உண்மைக் கலைசொல்ல ஓர்அந்தம் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"The true kalas, six, are held in one five; true kalanta is two with five, seven ends; and in nadanta, the end within one kala, to speak the true kala is one end.","n":"The verse is a count of kalas and ends whose scheme I can't recover; உண்மைக் கலை (true kala) opens every line. The numbers are given as read, not reconciled.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalai","aarandham","naadham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:173"},"t:2384":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2384,"t":["ஆவுடை யானை அரன்வந்து கொண்டபின்","தேவுடை யான்எங்கள் சீர்நந்தி தாள்தந்து","வீவற வேதாந்த சித்தாந்த மேன்மையைக்","கூளி யருளிய கோனைக் கருதுமே."],"p":"","r":"After Hara came and took hold of the one who has the soul, our glorious divine Nandi gave his feet; think on the king who taught, unfailing, the greatness of Vedanta and Siddhanta.","n":"ஆவுடையான் is unclear: ஆ (cow) is a Saiva figure for the soul, so 'the one who owns the soul', but it may also echo ஆவுடையார, the lingam's pedestal. கூளி in line 4 gives no fitting sense and may be கூறி (having told).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","thiruvadi","vedaantham","siththaantham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:173"},"t:2385":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2385,"t":["கருதும் அவர்தம் கருத்தினுக்கு ஒப்ப","அரனுரை செய்தருள் ஆகமந் தன்னில்","வருசமயப் புற மாயைமா மாயை","உருவிய வேதாந்த சித்தாந்த உண்மையே."],"p":"","r":"In the Agama that Hara spoke and gave in grace to fit the thought of those who think on him lies the truth of Vedanta and Siddhanta, which passes through the outer sects, maya and great maya.","n":"Line 1 says the Lord's teaching is shaped to its hearers. உருவிய can mean 'piercing through' or 'drawn out from', so the truth either goes beyond the outer sects and the two mayas or is drawn out of them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aagamam","siththaantham","samayam","maayai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:173"},"t:2386":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2386,"t":["வேதாந்தம் சித்தாந்தம் வேறிலா முத்திரை","போதாந்த ஞானம் யோகாந்தம் பொதுஞேய","நாதாந்தம் ஆனந்தம் சீரோ தயமாகும்","மூதாந்த முத்திரை மோனத்து மூழ்கவே."],"p":"","r":"Vedanta and Siddhanta are a seal without difference; the knowledge of bodhanta, yoganta, the shared known of nadanta and bliss become a rising of glory when one sinks into the silence of the seal of the ancient end.","n":"முத்திரை here is a seal or mark of identity more than a hand gesture; மூதாந்த முத்திரை, the seal of the ancient end, is unclear. The verse gathers the ends again and ends them in silence, as 2382 does.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["muththirai","vedaantham","siththaantham","mounam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:173"},"t:2387":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2387,"t":["வேதாந்தம் தன்னில் உபாதிமே வேழ்விட","நாதாந்த பாசம் விடுநல்ல தொம்பதம்","மீதாந்த காரணோ பாதியேழ் மெய்ப்பரன்","போதாந்த தற்பதம் போமசி என்பவே."],"p":"","r":"In Vedanta, when the seven adjuncts are left, the good 'thou' is what leaves the bond at the end of nada; beyond, with the seven causal adjuncts gone, the true Supreme is the 'that' of bodhanta; and their joining, they say, is 'art'.","n":"தொம்பதம், தற்பதம் and அசி are the three words of tat tvam asi ('that thou art'). The verse assigns 'thou' to the soul freed at nadanta and 'that' to the Lord at bodhanta. The two sets of seven adjuncts are not identified, and போமசி may be போம் அசி (the joining is asi).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thonthaththasi","vedaantham","pasu","pathi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:173"},"t:2388":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2388,"t":["அண்டங்கள் ஏழும் கடந்துஅகன்று அப்பாலும்","உண்டென்ற பேரொளிக் குள்ளாம் உளஒளி","பண்டுறு நின்ற பராசக்தி என்னவே","கொண்டவன் அன்றிநின் றான்தங்கள் கோவே."],"p":"","r":"Beyond the seven worlds and further, within the great light said to be there, is an inner light called the ancient Parashakti; and our King stood there as the one who holds her, yet not as her.","n":"கொண்டவன் அன்றி நின்றான் can mean he stood as the one who holds her, or stood apart from what he holds; Siddhanta's Lord is one with his Shakti yet not reduced to her. Sengalvaraya cites Appar 6-97-1 on what lies beyond the worlds.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","sivam","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:173"},"t:2389":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2389,"t":["கோஉணர்ந் தும்சத்தி யாலே குறிவைத்துத்","தேவுணர்த் துங்கரு மஞ்செய்தி செய்யவே","பாவனைத் தும்படைத் தர்ச்சனை பாரிப்ப","ஓஅனைத் துண்டுஒழி யாத ஒருவனே."],"p":"","r":"The King knows through Shakti and, setting the aim, has the acts done that make the gods know; creating all that is imagined and spreading worship, he is the One who is in all and never ceases.","n":"The syntax is broken into odd units (கோஉணர்ந் தும், ஓஅனைத் துண்டு), which makes the reading tentative. அனைத்துண்டு may be 'is in all' or 'has consumed all', the latter pointing to dissolution.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","saththi","aindhozhil","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:173"},"t:2390":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2390,"t":["ஒருவனை உன்னார் உயிர்தனை உன்னார்","இருவினை உன்னார் இருமாயை உன்னார்","ஒருவனு மேயுள் உணர்ந்திநின் றூட்டி","அருவனு மாகிய ஆதரத் தானே."],"p":"","r":"They do not think of the One, nor of the soul, nor of the two kinds of deed, nor of the two mayas; yet the One alone, knowing within, stands and feeds them, the formless one who is their support.","n":"Line 1 names the categories: the Lord, the soul, deeds and maya. Whether it faults the ignorant who ignore them or describes the freed who no longer think of them is open. ஊட்டி (feeding) is taken as making souls experience the fruit of their deeds.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pathi","pasu","vinai","maayai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:173"},"t:2391":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2391,"t":["அரன்அன்பர் தானம தாகிச் சிவத்து","வருமவை சத்திகள் முன்னா வகுத்திட்டு","உரனுறு சந்நிதி சேட்டிப்ப என்றும்","திரனுறு தோயாச் சிவாநந்தி யாமே."],"p":"","r":"Hara becomes the place of his devotees; setting out first the powers that arise in Siva so that his mighty presence acts, he is ever the steady one of Siva's bliss whom nothing touches.","n":"தானம் may be 'place' or 'gift'. சந்நிதி சேட்டிப்ப (the presence acting) and தோயா (untouched, not soaked) are taken in the Siddhanta sense that the Lord acts through his powers by mere presence and is not stained by the acts. The verse is compressed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","saththi","adiyaar"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:173"},"t:2392":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2392,"t":["வேதாந்த தொம்பதம் மேவும் பசுஎன்ப","நாதாந்த பாசம் விடநின்ற நன்பதி","போதாந்த தற்பதம் போய்இரண்டு ஐக்கியம்","சாதா ரணம்சிவ சாயுச் சிய மாமே."],"p":"","r":"They say the 'thou' of Vedanta is the bound soul; the good Lord is what stands when the bond at the end of nada is let go, the 'that' of bodhanta; going on, the union of the two, held in common, is Siva-sayujya.","n":"The verse maps tat tvam asi onto the Siddhanta categories: 'thou' is pasu, 'that' is pati, 'art' is sayujya. சாதாரணம் (common) is unclear: perhaps the union shared by both schools, or the state common to soul and Lord.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thonthaththasi","pasu","pathi","saayujjiyam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:173"},"t:2393":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2393,"t":["சிவமாதல் வேதாந்த சித்தாந்த மாகும்","அவம்அவம் ஆகும் அவ்வவ் இரண்டும்","சிவமாம் சதாசிவன் செய்துஒன்றான் ஆனால்","நவமான வேதாந்தம் ஞானசித் தாந்தமே."],"p":"","r":"Becoming Siva is Vedanta and Siddhanta; apart from that, each of the two is in vain; if Sadasiva, who is Siva, made them one, the new Vedanta is the Siddhanta of knowledge.","n":"அவம் அவம் ஆகும் அவ்வவ் இரண்டும் may mean either is futile unless it leads to becoming Siva, or that the two taken apart are futile. நவமான may be 'new' or 'ninefold'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vedaantham","siththaantham","sivam","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:173"},"t:2394":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2394,"t":["சித்தாந்த தேசீவன் முத்திசித் தித்திலால்","சித்தாந்தத் தேநிற்போர் முத்திசித் தித்திவர்","சித்தாந்த வேதாந்தம் செம்பொருள் ஆதலால்","சிந்தாந்த வேதாந்தம் காட்டும் சிவனையே."],"p":"","r":"Since in Siddhanta the soul attains release while living, those who stand in Siddhanta attain release and siddhi; since Siddhanta and Vedanta are the perfect meaning, Siddhanta and Vedanta show Siva.","n":"Lines 1 and 2 (சித்தித்திலால், சித்தித்திவர்) are a play on சித்தி whose exact form is unclear; சீவன் முத்தி is release while living. செம்பொருள் (the perfect meaning) is the Kural's word in 358.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["siththaantham","vedaantham","veedu","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:173"},"t:2395":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2395,"t":["சிவனைப் பரமனுள் சீவனுள் காட்டும்","அவமற்ற வேதாந்த சித்தாந்தம் ஆனால்","நவமுற்று அவத்தையில் ஞானம் சிவமாம்","தவமிக்கு உணர்ந்தவர் தத்துவத் தாரே."],"p":"","r":"If Vedanta and Siddhanta, free of waste, show Siva within the Supreme and within the soul, then knowledge, made new in the states, becomes Siva; those who have realised this, rich in austerity, are the people of truth.","n":"அவத்தை are the states of the soul (waking, dream and the rest), a Siddhanta term. நவமுற்று may be 'made new' or 'reaching the ninth'. தத்துவத்தார் plays forward into 2396, which opens with தத்துவம்.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vedaantham","siththaantham","gnaanam","thavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:173"},"t:2396":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2396,"t":["தத்துவம் ஆகும் சகள அகளங்கள்","தத்துவ மாம்விந்து நாதம் சதாசிவம்","தத்துவ மாகும் சீவன் தன் தற்பரம்","தத்துவ மாம்சிவ சாயுச் சியமே."],"p":"","r":"Tattva is the formed and the formless; tattva is bindu, nada and Sadasiva; tattva is the soul and its Supreme; tattva is Siva-sayujya.","n":"தத்துவம் opens every line. It can be the tattvas as constituents of the world, which fits bindu and nada, or 'truth, reality', which fits the soul, the Supreme and sayujya; the verse seems to use both. சகளம், அகளம் are the Lord with parts and without.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","vindhu","naadham","saayujjiyam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:173"},"t:2397":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2397,"t":["வேதமோடு ஆகமம் மெய்யாம் இறைவன்நூல்","ஓதும் பொதுவும் சிறப்பும்என்று உள்ளன","நாதன் உரையவை நாடில் இரண்டந்தம்","பேதமது என்பர் பெரியோர்க்கு அபேதமே."],"p":"","r":"The Veda and the Agama are the scriptures of the true Lord; what they teach is called the general and the special; examined, both are the Lord's word; people call the two ends different, but for the great they are not different.","n":"The Veda as பொது (general, for all) and the Agama as சிறப்பு (special, for the qualified) is a well-known Saiva position, and this verse is often cited for it. The 'two ends' are taken as Vedanta and Siddhanta.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vedham","aagamam","vedaantham","siththaantham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:173"},"t:2398":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2398,"t":["பராநந்தி மன்னும் சிவானந்தம் எல்லாம்","பரானந்தம் மேல்மூன்றும் பாழுறா ஆனந்தம்","விராமுத்தி ரானந்தம் மெய்நடன ஆனந்தம்","பொராநின்ற உள்ளமே பூரிப்பி யாமே."],"p":"","r":"All the bliss of Siva in which Paranandi abides: the supreme bliss, the three above, the bliss that never goes to waste, the bliss of the seal of rest, the bliss of the true dance; the heart that stands without strife swells with them.","n":"The verse lists kinds of bliss whose number and order are unclear; விராமுத்திரானந்தம் is read as the bliss of the mudra of cessation (விராம). பொராநின்ற உள்ளம் may be 'the heart that does not contend' or 'the heart that strives'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aanandham","thirukkoothu","muththirai","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:173"},"t:2399":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2399,"t":["ஆகுங் கலாந்தம் இரண்டந்த நாதாந்தம்","ஆகும் பொழுதிற் கலைஐந்தாம் ஆதலில்","ஆகும் அரனேபஞ் சாந்தகன் ஆம் என்ற","ஆகும் மறைஆ கமம்மொழிந் தான்அன்றே."],"p":"","r":"Kalanta, the two ends and nadanta: when they come about, the kalas are five; so Hara is the Lord of the five ends, as he said in the Veda and the Agama.","n":"Here the ends are counted as five (பஞ்சாந்தம்), not six; 2400 suggests why, taking Vedanta and Siddhanta as one. இரண்டந்தம் (the two ends) may be those two, as in 2397.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aarandham","kalai","vedham","aagamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:173"},"t:2400":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2400,"t":["அன்றாகும் என்னாதுஐ வகைஅந்தம் அன்னை","ஒன்றான வேதாந்த சித்தாந்தம் உள்ளிட்டு","நின்றால் யோகாந்தம் நேர்படும் நேர்பட்டால்","மன்றாடி பாதம் மருவலும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"Not rejecting the five kinds of end, and holding them together with Vedanta and Siddhanta made one, yoganta comes right; when it comes right, one reaches the feet of the dancer in the hall.","n":"அன்றாகும் என்னாது is read as 'without saying it is not so'. அன்னை (mother) after ஐவகை அந்தம் is unclear: the Shakti, or a misreading. மன்றாடி is the dancer in the hall at Chidambaram.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aarandham","yogam","thirukkoothu","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:173"},"t:2401":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2401,"t":["அனாதி சீவன்ஐம் மலமற்றுஅப் பாலாய்","அனாதி அடக்கித் தனைக்கண்டு அரனாய்த்","தன்ஆதி மலம்கெடத் தத்துவா தீதம்","வினாவுநீர் பாலாதல் வேதாந்த உண்மையே."],"p":"","r":"The beginningless soul, free of the five malas and gone beyond, restraining what is beginningless, sees itself, becomes Hara, its own primal malam destroyed, beyond the tattvas, as water becomes milk: this is the truth of Vedanta.","n":"ஐம்மலம் is traditionally the five bonds (anava, karma, maya, mayeya, tirodhana). Water mixed into milk is a classic image of union in which the two can no longer be told apart. That the verse calls this Vedanta's truth matches the chapter's claim that Vedanta and Siddhanta agree. வினாவு (asking) in line 4 is unclear and may be a misprint for விரவு (mixing).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pasu","malam","sivam","vedaantham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:173"},"t:2402":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2402,"t":["உயிரைப் பரனை உயிர்சிவன் தன்னை","அயர்வற்று அறிதொந் தத்தசி அதனால்","செயலற்று அறிவாகி யும்சென்று அடங்கி","அயர்வற்ற வேதாந்த சித்தாந்தம் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"Knowing without weariness the soul and the Supreme, the soul and Siva, is 'thou art that'; so, action ceasing, becoming knowledge and going to subside there, is the unwearying Vedanta and Siddhanta.","n":"தொந்தத்தசி is tvam tat asi, the great saying with its words in this order. Line 3 gives the sequence: action ends, the soul becomes knowledge, then subsides in Siva.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thonthaththasi","pasu","sivam","vedaantham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:173"},"t:2403":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2403,"t":["மன்னிய சோகமாம் மாமறை யாளர்தம்","சென்னிய தான சிவயோகமாம் ஈதென்ன","அன்னது சித்தாந்த மாமறை யாய்பொருள்","துன்னிய ஆகம நூலெனத் தோன்றுமே."],"p":"","r":"The abiding 'So'ham' is the crowning Siva-yoga of those who know the great Veda; and that same thing, as the meaning of the great scripture of Siddhanta, appears as the Agama joined with it.","n":"சோகம் (so'ham, 'I am He') is a meditation formula. The verse makes one teaching appear as Siva-yoga for Veda-knowers and as Agama for Siddhantins; the syntax of lines 3 and 4 is loose.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","vedham","aagamam","siththaantham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:173"},"t:2404":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2404,"t":["முதலாகும் வேத முழுதுஆ கமம்அகப்","பதியான ஈசன் பகர்ந்தது இரண்டு","முதிதான வேதம் முறைமுறை யால்அமர்ந்து","அதிகாதி வேதாந்த சித்தாந்தம் ஆகவே."],"p":"","r":"The Veda that comes first and the whole Agama are the two that Isa, the Lord within, spoke; the ancient Veda, set in its due order, they become the foremost Vedanta and Siddhanta.","n":"அகப் பதி may be the Lord within, or பதி alone as the Siddhanta Lord. அதிகாதி in line 4 is unclear: perhaps 'foremost' or அதிகாரி (one qualified).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vedham","aagamam","pathi","vedaantham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:173"},"t:2405":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2405,"t":["அறிவுஅறிவு என்ற அறிவும் அனாதி","அறிவுக்கு அறிவாம் பதியும் அனாதி","அறிவினைக் கட்டிய பாசம் அனாதி","அறிவு பதியில் பிறப்பறுந் தானே."],"p":"","r":"The knowing soul is beginningless, the Lord who is knowledge to that knowledge is beginningless, the bond that binds the knowing soul is beginningless, and when the soul rests in the Lord, birth is cut off.","n":"அறிவு (knowledge) names the soul itself, and அறிவுக்கு அறிவு the Lord as the one by whom the soul knows. The three beginningless realities stated line by line are the Siddhanta position in its plainest form.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pathi","pasu","paasam","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:174"},"t:2406":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2406,"t":["பசுப்பல கோடி பிரமன் முதலாய்ப்","பசுக்களைக் கட்டிய பாசம்மூன் றுண்டு","பசுத்தன்மை நீக்கிஅப் பாசம் அறுத்தால்","பசுக்கள் தலைவனைப் பற்றி விடாவே."],"p":"","r":"Souls are many crores, from Brahma down, and the bonds that bind them are three; if their soul-state is removed and the bond is cut, the souls hold to their Lord and do not let go.","n":"The three bonds are traditionally anava, karma and maya. Brahma is counted among the bound souls, which places the gods below Siva. பசுத்தன்மை (the state of being pasu) is what goes, not the soul.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasu","paasam","malam","pathi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:174"},"t:2407":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2407,"t":["கிடக்கின்ற வாறே கிளர்பயன் மூன்று","நடக்கின்ற ஞானத்தை நாடோ றும் நோக்கித்","தொடக்குஒன்றும் இன்றித் தொழுமின் தொழுதால்","குடக்குன்றில் இட்ட விளக்கது வாமே."],"p":"","r":"As things lie there are three stirring fruits; look every day to the knowledge that moves, and worship without any entanglement, for when you worship it becomes like a lamp set on the western hill.","n":"கிளர்பயன் மூன்று (three stirring fruits) is obscure; it may mean the three categories as they are, or three results. குடக்குன்றில் இட்ட விளக்கு, a lamp on the western hill, suggests a light seen from far off; whether the western hill has a further sense is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","vazhipaadu","pathi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:174"},"t:2408":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2408,"t":["பாசம்செய் தானைப் படர்சடை நந்தியை","நேசம்செய்து ஆங்கே நினைப்பர் நினைத்தாலும்","கூசம் செய்து உன்னிக் குறிக்கொள்வது எவ்வண்ணம்","வாசம்செய் பாசத்துள் வைக்கின்ற வாறே."],"p":"","r":"People love Nandi of the spreading matted hair, who made the bond, and think of him there; yet even thinking of him, how could they shrink back and hold him in their aim, so does he keep them within the bond they live in.","n":"பாசம்செய்தான், 'he who made the bond', makes the Lord the author of bondage; in Siddhanta he does not create malam but sets maya and karma working on it. கூசம் செய்து உன்னி (thinking with shrinking) and the turn of the last line are hard to construe.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["paasam","sivam","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:174"},"t:2409":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2409,"t":["விட்ட விடம்ஏறா வாறுபோல் வேறாகி","விட்ட பசுபாசம் மெய்கண்டோ ன் மேவுறான்","சுட்டிய கேவலம் காணும் சகலத்தைச்","சுட்டு நனவில் அதீதத்துள் தோன்றுமே."],"p":"","r":"As poison once drawn out does not rise again, the one who has seen the truth does not return to the soul-state and bond he has left; the kevala state pointed to sees the sakala, and burning that away, he appears in the state beyond while still awake.","n":"கேவலம் and சகலம் are Siddhanta's states of the soul: alone with anava and inert, and embodied with maya and karma. நனவில் அதீதம், the beyond-state in waking, is taken as the condition of one freed while living. மெய்கண்டோன் means 'one who saw the truth'; it is also the name of the later Siddhanta teacher Meykandar, but nothing in the verse requires that.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["paasam","avaththai","gnaanam","pasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:174"},"t:2410":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2410,"t":["நாடும் பதியுடன் நற்பசு பாசமும்","நீடுமாம் நித்தன் நிலையறி வார்இல்லை","நீடிய நித்தம் பசுபாச நீக்கமும்","நாடிய சைவர்க்கு நந்தி அளித்ததே."],"p":"","r":"The Lord who is sought, and the good soul and the bond, all endure; none knows the state of the Eternal, and that lasting eternity and the removal of soul-state and bond Nandi gave to the Saivas who sought them.","n":"நீடுமாம் says all three endure, but the last line speaks of removing pasu and pasam; the verse holds both together, as Siddhanta does, by taking removal as of the soul's bound state rather than of the soul.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pathi","pasu","paasam","saivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:174"},"t:2411":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2411,"t":["ஆய பதிதான் அருட்சிவ லிங்கமாம்","ஆய பசுவும் அடலே றெனநிற்கும்","ஆய பலிபீடம் ஆகுநற் பாசமாம்","ஆய அரனிலை ஆய்ந்துகொள் வார்கட்கே."],"p":"","r":"The Lord is the lingam of Siva's grace, the soul stands as the mighty bull, and the good bond is the altar of offerings: this is Hara's state, for those who examine it.","n":"The verse reads the temple's axis as the three categories: the lingam in the sanctum, the Nandi bull facing it, and the balipitha nearest the entrance. Calling the bond நற்பாசம், the good bond, anticipates 2422.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pathi","ilingam","pasu","paasam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:174"},"t:2412":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2412,"t":["பதிபசு பாசம் பயில்வியா நித்தம்","பதிபசு பாசம் பகர்வோர்க்கு ஆறாக்கிப்","பதிபசு பாசத்தைப் பற்றற நீக்கும்","பதிபசு பாசம் பயில நிலாவே."],"p":"","r":"Pati, pasu and pasam are eternal and not taught into being; to those who speak of them they become a path; that knowledge removes them so clinging ends; and when they are practised they do not stay.","n":"Each line opens with பதிபசு பாசம், so the formula itself is the subject. பயில்வியா (unlearnt, not brought about) and நிலாவே (do not stay, or shine like the moon) are both open; the reading above is one way to make the lines cohere.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pathi","pasu","paasam","pattru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:174"},"t:2413":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2413,"t":["பதியும் பசுவொடு பாசமும் மேலைக்","கதியும் பசுபாச நீக்கமும் காட்டி","மதிதந்த ஆனந்த மாநந்தி காணும்","துதிதந்து வைத்தனன் சத்தசை வத்திலே."],"p":"","r":"Showing the Lord with soul and bond, the higher goal and the removal of soul-state and bond, great Nandi of bliss, who gave understanding, set them with praise in true Saivism.","n":"சத்தசைவம் may be சத்த (sat, true) or a variant of சுத்தசைவம் (pure Saivism), a kind of Saivism named elsewhere in Tirumular. துதிதந்து (giving praise) is loose in the line.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pathi","saivam","guru","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:174"},"t:2414":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2414,"t":["அறிந்தணு மூன்றுமே யாங்கணும் ஆகும்","அறிந்தணு மூன்றுமெ யாங்கணும் ஆக","அறிந்த அனாதி வியாத்தனும் ஆவன்","அறிந்த பதிபடைப் பான்அங்கு அவற்றையே."],"p":"","r":"The three kinds of souls are known to be everywhere, and so the knowing, beginningless Lord pervades them, and as knowing Pati he creates for them there.","n":"அணு மூன்று is taken as the three classes of souls in Siddhanta (bound by one, two or three malas), but it could be read otherwise. Lines 1 and 2 are nearly the same words.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pasu","pathi","aindhozhil"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:174"},"t:2415":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2415,"t":["படைப்புஆதி யாவது பரம்சிவம் சத்தி","இடைப்பால உயிர்கட்கு அடைத்துஇவை தூங்கல்","படைப்பாதி சூக்கத்தைத் தற்பரன் செய்ய","படைப்பாதி தூய மலம்அப் பரத்திலே."],"p":"","r":"Creation and the other acts belong to Parasiva and Shakti and are closed off from the souls between; the Supreme performs creation in its subtle form, and that beginning of creation, pure maya, lies in the Supreme.","n":"தூய மலம் (pure malam) is taken as suddha maya, the pure material of the higher worlds. இடைப்பால உயிர்கட்கு அடைத்துஇவை தூங்கல் is hard to construe; it may instead say the acts are fitted to the souls.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aindhozhil","sivam","saththi","maayai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:174"},"t:2416":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2416,"t":["ஆகிய சூக்கத்தை அவ்விந்து நாதமும்","ஆகிய சத்தி சிவபர மேம்ஐந்தால்","ஆகிய சூக்கத்தில் ஐங்கரு மம்செய்வோன்","ஆகிய தூயஈ சானனும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"In that subtle order, through bindu and nada, Shakti, Siva and the Supreme, these five, the one who performs the five acts in the subtle is also the pure Isana.","n":"ஈசானன் is one of Siva's five faces; here it may name the Lord of the pure order. The five through which he acts are listed, but their roles are not.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aindhozhil","vindhu","naadham","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:174"},"t:2417":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2417,"t":["மேவும் பரசிவம் மேற்சத்தி நாதமும்","மேவும் பரவிந்து ஐம்முகன் வேறுஈசன்","மேவும் உருத்திரன் மால்வேதா மேதினி","ஆகும் படிபடைப் போன்அர னாமே."],"p":"","r":"Parasiva, and above Shakti and nada, the supreme bindu, the five-faced one, the Lord as distinct, Rudra, Vishnu, Brahma and the earth: the one who creates so that all these come to be is Hara.","n":"ஐம்முகன் (the five-faced) is traditionally Sadasiva and வேறு ஈசன் Maheshvara; the list runs down the pure order to the three gods and the earth. The verse makes all of them Hara's creation.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","thaththuvam","vindhu","naadham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:174"},"t:2418":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2418,"t":["படைப்பும் அளிப்பும் பயில்இளைப் பாற்றும்","துடைப்பும் மறைப்பும்முன் தோன்ற அருளும்","சடத்தை விடுத்த அருளும் சகலத்து","அடைத்த அனாதியை ஐந்தென லாமே."],"p":"","r":"Creation, protection, the removal that gives rest, concealment, and the grace that comes forth, the grace that frees from inert matter: the beginningless one who shut souls in the embodied state may be called by these five.","n":"இளைப்பாற்றும் துடைப்பு, removal that gives rest, frames dissolution as relief for weary souls rather than destruction. The last act is grace that frees from சடம், inert matter. The five are named by what they do for souls.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aindhozhil","thiruvarul","pathi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:174"},"t:2419":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2419,"t":["ஆறாறு குண்டலி தன்னின் அகத்திட்டு","வேறாகு மாயைiல் முப்பால் மிகுத்திட்டுஅங்கு","ஈறாம் கருவி இவற்றால் வகுத்திட்டு","வேறாம் பதிபசு பாசம்வீ டாகுமே."],"p":"","r":"Setting the thirty-six tattvas within kundalini, increasing maya's three parts, and dividing them through the instruments down to the last, pati, pasu and pasam stand distinct, and that is release.","n":"ஆறாறு (six sixes) is the 36 tattvas; முப்பால் (three parts) of maya may be pure, mixed and impure maya. Whether the last line means that knowing the three as distinct is release, or something else, is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["thaththuvam","kundalini","maayai","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:174"},"t:2420":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2420,"t":["வீட்கும் பதிபசு பாசமும் மீதுற","ஆட்கும் இருவினை ஆங்குஅவற் றால் உணர்ந்து","ஆட்கு நரசு சுவர்க்கத்தில் தானிட்டு","நாட்குற நான்தங்கு நற்பாசம் நண்ணுமே."],"p":"","r":"With pati, pasu and pasam set above, the two kinds of deeds that govern a person are known through them; he is placed in hell and in heaven, and for his allotted days the good bond in which the 'I' dwells comes to him.","n":"இருவினை are good and bad deeds. வீட்கும் at the start and நான்தங்கு near the end are obscure; the 'I' that dwells in the bond leads into 2421, where saying 'I' is named anava.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","naragam","paasam","pasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:174"},"t:2421":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2421,"t":["நண்ணிய பாசத்தில் நான்எனல் ஆணவம்","பண்ணிய மாயையில் ஊட்டல் பரிந்தனன்","கண்ணிய சேதனன் கண்வந்த பேரருள்","அண்ணல் அடிசேர் உபாயமது ஆகுமே."],"p":"","r":"In the bond that comes, saying 'I' is anava; through the maya he set working the Lord kindly feeds the soul its experience; and the great grace that comes into the sentient soul's sight is the means of reaching the Lord's feet.","n":"The verse names ஆணவம் as the sense of 'I'. ஊட்டல், feeding, is the making of souls experience the fruit of deeds through maya, and பரிந்தனன் (in kindness) makes that an act of care. கண்ணிய and கண்வந்த are not fully clear.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["malam","maayai","thiruvarul","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:174"},"t:2422":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2422,"t":["ஆகும் உபாயமே யன்றி அழுக்கற்று","மோசு மறச்சுத்தன் ஆதற்கு மூலமே","ஆகும் அறுவை அழுக்கேற்றி ஏற்றல்போல்","ஆகுவ தெல்லாம் அருட்பாச மாகுமே."],"p":"","r":"More than a means, it is the root by which the soul, its dirt gone and its delusion ended, becomes pure; as a washer loads dirt on cloth to clean it, all that comes to the soul is the bond of grace.","n":"அறுவை அழுக்கேற்றி ஏற்றல் is taken as the washer applying fuller's earth or dung to cloth to draw out its dirt; tradition reads maya and karma as given to remove anava in the same way. அருட்பாசம், the bond of grace, is the chapter's turn. மோசு is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["paasam","thiruvarul","malam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:174"},"t:2423":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2423,"t":["பாசம் பயிலுயிர் தானே பரமுதல்","பாசம் பயிலுயிர் தானே பசுவென்ப","பாசம் பயிலப் பதிபர மாதலால்","பாசம் பயிலப் பதிபசு வாகுமே."],"p":"","r":"The soul that undergoes the bond is called pasu and is itself, the verse says, the supreme first; and since through the bond's working the Lord is supreme, through that working Lord and soul become one, or the Lord becomes as the soul.","n":"பதிபசு வாகுமே can mean 'the Lord becomes pasu' or 'pati and pasu become (one)'; either may explain the chapter title, வேறின்மை, non-difference. The verse is built on four repetitions of பாசம் பயில, and the readings above are guesses at its logic.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pasu","paasam","pathi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:174"},"t:2424":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2424,"t":["அத்தத்தில் உத்தரம் கேட்ட அருந்தவர்","அத்தத்தில் உத்தர மாகும் அருள்மேனி","அத்தத்தி னாலே அணையப் பிடித்தலும்","அத்தத்தில் தம்மை அடைந்து நின்றாரே."],"p":"","r":"The rare ascetics who heard the answer in the hand found the answer was the body of grace in the hand, and held close by that hand, they reached themselves within it and stood.","n":"அத்தம் can be hand (hasta), meaning (artha) or half, and உத்தரம் answer or north. A traditional reading takes it as the guru's touch in initiation, or the teaching gesture of the silent Siva; a plainer reading, that the answer lay in the meaning itself, also stands.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","thiruvarul","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:174"},"t:2425":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2425,"t":["காலும் தலையும் அறியார் கலதிகள்","கால்அந்தச் சத்தி அருள்என்பர் காரணம்","பாலொன்று ஞானமே பண்பார் தலைஉயிர்","கால்அந்த ஞானத்கைக் காட்டவீ டாகுமே."],"p":"","r":"The wretched do not know foot and head: the foot is Shakti, grace and the cause, the head is knowledge, and when the foot shows the soul that knowledge, release comes.","n":"The equation of the foot with Shakti as grace is stated; பாலொன்று ஞானமே பண்பார் தலைஉயிர் is hard to divide, and may make the soul the head, or place it under knowledge. கலதிகள் (the wretched, the ill-fated) is sharp.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvadi","saththi","gnaanam","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:175"},"t:2426":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2426,"t":["தலைஅடி யாவது அறியார் காயத்தில்","தலைஅடி உச்சியில் உள்ளது மூலம்","தலைஅடி யான அறிவை அறிந்தோர்","தலைஅடி யாகவே தான்இருந் தாரே."],"p":"","r":"People do not know what head and foot are; in the body they are at the crown and at the root, and those who know the knowledge that is head and foot remain as head and foot themselves.","n":"உச்சி (crown) and மூலம் (root) are taken in the yogic sense as the top of the head and the lowest centre; தலைஅடி can also mean the foot placed on the head, as in 2427. The last line says the knower becomes what he knows.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvadi","yaakkai","aadhaaram","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:175"},"t:2427":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2427,"t":["நின்றான் நிலமுழுது அண்டமும் மேலுற","வன்தாள் அசுரர் அமரரும் உய்ந்திடப்","பின்தான் உலகம் படைத்தவன் பேர்நந்தி","தன்தாள் இணைஎன் தலைமிசை ஆனதே."],"p":"","r":"Nandi, who stood so that the whole earth and the cosmos rose up, so that the strong-footed asuras and the gods were saved, and who afterwards created the world: his pair of feet came upon my head.","n":"நின்றான் ... மேலுற may recall Siva standing as the column whose head and foot Brahma and Vishnu could not find, which fits a chapter on head and foot; the verse does not name the story. The feet on the head are traditionally the guru's initiation by the feet (திருவடி தீட்சை).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvadi","sivam","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:175"},"t:2428":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2428,"t":["சிந்தையின் உள்ளே எந்தை திருவடி","சிந்தையின் எந்தை திருவடிக் கீழது","எந்தையும் என்னை அறியகி லான்ஆகில்","எந்தையை யானும் அறியகி லேனே."],"p":"","r":"My father's holy feet are within my mind, and my mind is beneath my father's holy feet; if my father cannot know me, I cannot know my father either.","n":"Lines 1 and 2 reverse each other: the feet in the mind and the mind under the feet. The last two lines make the soul's knowing of the Lord depend on his knowing it first. Sengalvaraya notes a close parallel in Appar's Tevaram (5.91.8).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvadi","manam","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:175"},"t:2429":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2429,"t":["பன்னாத பாரொளிக்கு அப்புறத்து அப்பால்","என்நா யாகனார் இசைந்தங்கு இருந்திடம்","உன்னா ஒளியும் உரைசெய்யா மந்திரம்","சொன்னான்கழலினை சூடிநின் றேனே."],"p":"","r":"Beyond the unspoken light of the world and beyond that, where my Lord consented to dwell, he told me the light that cannot be thought and the mantra that cannot be spoken, and I stand wearing his anklet feet.","n":"உரைசெய்யா மந்திரம் is a mantra not spoken aloud or one beyond speech, yet he 'told' it: the verse holds the paradox of teaching the unsayable. சூடி, wearing as a garland, is the feet on the head again.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvadi","mandhiram","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:175"},"t:2430":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2430,"t":["பதியது தோற்றும் பதமது வைம்மின்","மதியது செய்து மலர்ப்பதம் ஓதும்","நதிபொதி யும்சடை நாரியோர் பாகன்","கதிசெயும் காலங்கள் கண்டுகொ ளீரே."],"p":"","r":"Set in yourselves the feet that reveal the Lord, make your understanding steady and recite the flower-feet, and see the times when the one with the river in his matted hair and the woman as his half grants the goal.","n":"மதியது செய்து may be 'use your understanding' or, in the yogic sense, 'make the moon', the inner lunar breath. கதிசெயும் காலங்கள் (times he grants the goal) is unexplained.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvadi","pathi","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:175"},"t:2431":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2431,"t":["தரித்துநின்றானடி தன்னிட நெஞ்சில்","தரித்து நின்றான் அமராபதி நாதன்","கரித்துநின் றான்கரு தாதவர் சிந்தை","பரித்துநின் றான்அப் பரிபாகத் தானே."],"p":"","r":"The one who sustains stood with his feet in my heart; the lord of the gods' city stood sustaining; he stood scorching the minds of those who do not think of him, and he stood bearing souls according to their ripeness.","n":"பரிபாகம் is the soul's ripeness, a Siddhanta term for readiness to receive grace. கரித்து may be 'burning, blackening' or 'witnessing'; its sense against those who do not think of him is unclear. அமராபதி நாதன் uses the name of Indra's city for Siva as lord over the gods.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvadi","sivam","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:175"},"t:2432":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2432,"t":["ஒன்றுண்டு தாமரை ஒண்மலர் மூன்றுள","தன்தாதை தாளும் இரண்டுள காயத்துள்","நன்றாகக் காய்ச்சிப் பதஞ்செய வல்லார்கட்கு","இன்றேசென்று ஈசனை எய்தலும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"There is one lotus with three bright flowers, and one's father's feet, two, are in the body; for those who can heat it well and bring it to the right state, it is possible to go today and reach the Lord.","n":"காய்ச்சிப் பதஞ்செய is a cooking image, boiling down to the right consistency, taken as yogic heat or the ripening of the soul; பதம் also means foot and state. The lotus and its three flowers are not identified; tradition reads them as centres of the yogic body.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aadhaaram","thiruvadi","yogam","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:175"},"t:2433":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2433,"t":["கால்கொண்டுஎன் சென்னியிற் கட்டறக் கட்டற","மால்கொண்ட நெஞ்சின் மயக்கிற் றுயக்கறப்","பால்கொண்ட என்ணைப் பரன்கொள்ள நாடினான்","மேல்கொண்டென் செம்மை விளம்ப ஒண்ணாதே."],"p":"","r":"He set his foot on my head so that my bonds were cut and cut, so that the confusion of my bewildered heart's delusion was cut, and he sought to take me, who was his portion; what my state became after he took me cannot be told.","n":"கட்டறக் கட்டற doubles the cutting of bonds. பால்கொண்ட என்னை may be 'me, who belonged to his side' or 'me, who had taken a side'. The verse ends in the ineffable (விளம்ப ஒண்ணாதே).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvadi","paasam","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:175"},"t:2434":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2434,"t":["பெற்ற புதல்வர்போல் பேணிய நாற்றமும்","குற்றமுங் கண்டு குணங்குறை செய்யவோர்","பற்றைய ஈசன் உயிரது பான்மைக்குச்","செற்றமி லாச் செய்கைக்கு எய்தின செய்யுமே."],"p":"","r":"Like a parent who has seen both the tendencies and the faults of the sons he begot, the Lord who holds to souls deals with each by its nature, doing without anger what fits.","n":"நாற்றம் is usually smell; here perhaps disposition, or நாற்று (seedling) as what has been raised. குணங்குறை செய்ய (to make up for virtues and lacks) and the third line are loosely built. The point that the Lord's dealings are without hatred (செற்றமிலா) is clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pathi","thiruvarul","makkal"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:175"},"t:2435":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2435,"t":["மூன்றுள குற்றம் முழுதும் நலிவன","மான்றுஇருள் தூங்கி மயங்கிக் கிடந்தன","மூன்றினை நீங்கினர் நீக்கினர் நீங்காதார்","மூன்றினுள் பட்டு முடிகின்ற வாறே."],"p":"","r":"There are three faults that afflict wholly; dazed, they lay sleeping in darkness and bewildered; those who left the three have removed them, and those who did not are caught in the three and perish.","n":"The verse does not name the three. 2436 names lust, anger and delusion, the Kural's three; the darkness and sleep here could also suit the three malas. நீங்கினர் நீக்கினர் (they left, they removed) pairs leaving and removing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kutram","malam","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:176"},"t:2436":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2436,"t":["காமம் வெகுளி மயக்கம் இவைகடிந்து","ஏமம் பிடித்திருந் தேனுக்கு எறிமணி","ஓமெனும் ஓசையின் உள்ளே உறைவதோர்","தாமம் அதனைத் தலைப்பட்ட வாறே."],"p":"","r":"Having driven off lust, anger and delusion and held fast to safety, I reached the abode that dwells within the sound Om, as of a struck bell.","n":"The first line is nearly Kural 360's. எறிமணி (a struck bell) and ஓமெனும் ஓசை (the sound Om) are taken as an inner sound heard in yoga, and தாமம் as the abode or light within it; that yogic reading is the tradition's.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","vekuli","piranavam","naadham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:176"},"t:2437":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2437,"t":["தோன்றிய தொம்பதம் தற்பதம் சூழ்தர","ஏன்ற அசிபதம் இம்மூன்றோடு எய்தனோன்","ஆகின்ற பராபர மாகும் பிறப்பற","ஏன்றனன் மாளச் சிவமாய் இருக்குமே."],"p":"","r":"One who reaches 'thou' as it appears, 'that' surrounding it, and 'art' that receives them, becomes the transcendent-immanent so that birth ends, and as the self that took this on dies away, stays as Siva.","n":"தொம்பதம், தற்பதம், அசிபதம் are the Sanskrit tvam, tat and asi. பராபரம் is the Lord as beyond and within. ஏன்றனன் மாள, 'he who accepted dying', is hard to construe; it may mean the individual self ends in Siva.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thonthaththasi","sivam","piravi","saayujjiyam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:177"},"t:2438":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2438,"t":["போதந் தனையுன்னிப் பூதாதி பேதமும்","ஓதுங் கருவிதொண் ணூறுடன் ஓராறு","பேதமும் நாதாந்தப் பெற்றியில் கைவிட்டு","வேதம்சொல் தொம்பத மாகுதல் மெய்ம்மையே."],"p":"","r":"Contemplating awareness, and letting go, in the way of nada's end, the distinctions of the elements and the ninety-six instruments that are spoken of, to become the 'thou' that the Veda speaks of is the truth.","n":"தொண்ணூறுடன் ஓராறு (ninety with six) is the count of 96 tattvas common in Tamil Siddha writing, beside the 36 used elsewhere in the Tirumanthiram. 'Thou' is the soul known in itself once the instruments are set aside.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thonthaththasi","thaththuvam","naadham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:177"},"t:2439":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2439,"t":["தற்பதம் என்றும் துவம்பதம் தான்என்றும்","நிற்பது அசியத்துள் நேரிழை யாள்பதம்","சொற்பதத் தாலும் தொடரஒண் ணாச்சிவன்","கற்பனை யின்றிக் கலந்துநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"Whether called 'that' or 'thou', what stands in 'art' is the station of the finely jewelled lady, and Siva, whom no word can follow, stood mingled there without any fancy.","n":"The verse makes the joining word, asi, Shakti (நேரிழையாள், the woman of fine jewels): the bond between soul and Lord is his power. கற்பனை யின்றி (without imagining) says the mingling is real, not a construct of thought.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thonthaththasi","saththi","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:177"},"t:2440":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2440,"t":["அணுவும் பரமும் அசிபதத்து ஏய்ந்த","கணுஒன் றிலாத சிவமும் கலந்தால்","இணையறு பால்தேன் அமுதென இன்பத்","துணையது வாயுரை யற்றிடத் தோன்றுமே."],"p":"","r":"When the soul and the Supreme, joined in 'art', mingle with Siva who has no seam, it appears, once words cease, as a joy without equal, like milk, honey and nectar.","n":"அணு is the soul. கணு ஒன்று இலாத, without a single node (as on a cane), is an image of seamless wholeness. உரையற்றிட, when speech ends, is the condition for the joy to appear.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thonthaththasi","aanandham","sivam","pasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:177"},"t:2441":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2441,"t":["தொம்பதம் தற்பதம் தோன்றும் அசிபதம்","நம்பிய சீவன் பரன்சிவ னாய்நிற்கும்","அம்பத மேலைச் சொரூபமா வாக்கியம்","செம்பொருள் ஆண்டருள் சீர்நந்தி தானே."],"p":"","r":"With 'thou', 'that' and 'art' appearing, the trusting soul stands as the Supreme and as Siva; that word is the saying whose form is the highest, and glorious Nandi rules and gives its true meaning.","n":"சீவன் பரன் சிவன் may be read as matching the three words to soul, Supreme and Siva. வாக்கியம் (the saying) is the Upanishadic great saying; Tirumular makes Nandi, his guru, the one who gives its sense.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thonthaththasi","sivam","guru","pasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:177"},"t:2442":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2442,"t":["ஐம்பது அறியா தவரும் அவர்சிலர்","உம்பனை நாடி உரைமுப்ப தத்திடைச்","செம்பர மாகிய வாசி செலுத்திடத்","தம்பரயோகமாய்த் தான்அவன் ஆகுமே."],"p":"","r":"Even some who do not know the fifty letters, seeking the Highest within the three-word teaching and driving the breath that is the true Supreme, become, by that supreme yoga, He himself.","n":"ஐம்பது (the fifty) is taken as the fifty letters of the mantra alphabet, or learning in general; வாசி is the breath in Siddha yoga. உம்பனை is obscure, perhaps 'the one on high'. The verse opens the way to those without learning.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thonthaththasi","praanan","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:177"},"t:2443":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2443,"t":["நந்தி அறிவும் நழுவில் அதீதமாம்","இந்தியும் சத்தாதி விடவிய னாகும்","நந்திய மூன்றுஇரண்டு ஒன்று நலம்ஐந்து","நந்தி நனவாதி மூட்டும் அனாதியே."],"p":"","r":"When knowledge, extinguished, slips away it is the state beyond; when the senses and sound and the rest are let go it becomes vast; and the beginningless one sets going the states of waking and the rest, the three, two, one and five.","n":"நந்தி may be the Lord's name or the verb 'extinguished' (நந்துதல்), and நந்திய in line 3 likewise; the reading changes with it. The numbers மூன்றுஇரண்டு ஒன்று ஐந்து are traditionally the instruments active in each state, but the verse does not say which.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","thaththuvam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:177"},"t:2444":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2444,"t":["பரதுரி யத்து நனவு படியுண்ட","விரிவிற் கனவும் இதன்உப சாந்தத்து","துரிய கழுமுனையும் ஓவும் சிவன்பால்","அரிய துரியம் அசிபதம் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"In the supreme turiya, waking is absorbed; in its spreading is dream; in its stilling is turiya, with the sushumna, and cessation at Siva; and that rare turiya is 'art'.","n":"கழுமுனை is the sushumna, the central channel. The verse maps the joining word asi onto the turiya state, so the three words and the states of consciousness become one scheme. Its step-by-step order is compressed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","thonthaththasi","naadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:177"},"t:2445":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2445,"t":["தோன்றிஎன் உள்ளே சுழன்றுஎழு கின்றதோர்","மூன்று படிமண் டலத்து முதல்வனை","ஏன்றெய்தி இன்புற்று இருந்தே இளங்கொடி","நான்று நலம்செய் நலந்தரு மாறே."],"p":"","r":"Having reached the First One of the three-tiered circle, who appeared within me and rises whirling, and dwelling there in joy, the young creeper hangs down and does good: this is how good is given.","n":"மூன்று படி மண்டலம் is traditionally the three circles of fire, sun and moon in the yogic body. இளங்கொடி (the young creeper) is taken as Shakti, perhaps kundalini. The verse is built on images that can't be fixed from it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","saththi","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:178"},"t:2446":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2446,"t":["மன்று நிறைந்தது மாபர மாயது","நின்று நிறைந்தது நேர்தரு நந்தியும்","கன்று நினைந்தெழு தாயென வந்தபின்","குன்று நிறைந்த குணவிளக்கு ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"What filled the hall became the great Supreme, and Nandi stood filling it and came face to face; once he came like a mother rising at the thought of her calf, he became a lamp of virtue filling the hill.","n":"மன்று is the hall of the dance. The cow who rises at the very thought of her calf (கன்று நினைந்தெழு தாய்) makes grace come unbidden. குன்று நிறைந்த விளக்கு, a lamp filling the hill, echoes 2407.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","thiruvarul","thirukkoothu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:178"},"t:2447":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2447,"t":["ஆறாறு தத்துவத்து அப்புறத்து அப்புரம்","கூறா உபதேசம் கூறில் சிவபரம்","வேறாய் வெளிப்பட்ட வேதப் பசுவனார்","பேறாக ஆனந்தம் பேறும் பெருகவே."],"p":"","r":"Beyond the thirty-six tattvas and beyond that is the teaching that cannot be told, and if told, it is Siva the Supreme; the Lord of the Veda appeared as distinct, so that bliss and attainment grow.","n":"கூறா உபதேசம் கூறில் (if the untellable teaching is told) is the familiar paradox. வேதப் பசுவனார் is obscure: perhaps the Lord of souls (Pasupati) of the Veda, or the Veda as a cow.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","sivam","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:178"},"t:2448":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2448,"t":["பற்றறப் பற்றில் பரம்பதி யாவது","பற்றறப் பற்றில் பரனறி வேபரம்","பற்றறப் பற்றினில் பற்றவல் லோர்கட்கே","பற்றறப் பற்றில் பரம்பர மாமே."],"p":"","r":"If one clings so that clinging ends, that is the supreme Lord; the knowledge of the Supreme is the Supreme; and for those who can cling in that clinging so that clinging ends, it is the supreme beyond the supreme.","n":"பற்றறப் பற்றில் opens every line: 'if one clings so that clinging ends', or 'in the clinging free of clinging'. பரம்பரம் is the supreme above the supreme, the second of the chapter's three.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pattru","pathi","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:178"},"t:2449":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2449,"t":["பரம்பர மான பதிபாசம் பற்றாப்","பரம்பர மாகும் பரஞ்சிவ மேவப்","பரம்பர மான பரசிவா னந்தம்","பரம்பர மாகப் படைப்பது அறிவே."],"p":"","r":"The supreme beyond the supreme, which Lord and bond do not hold, becomes Parasiva to be reached; and the bliss of Parasiva, the supreme beyond the supreme, is made as such by knowledge.","n":"பதிபாசம் பற்றா, which pati and pasam do not hold, sets the highest beyond even the category of Lord. The last line makes knowledge the maker of the bliss; that may mean realisation is the knowing itself.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","aanandham","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:178"},"t:2450":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2450,"t":["நனவில் கலாதியாம் நாலொன்று அகன்று","தனியுற்ற கேவலம் தன்னில் தானாகி","நினைவுற்று அகன்ற அதீதத்துள் நேயந்","தனையுற்று இடத்தானே தற்பர மாமே."],"p":"","r":"In waking, leaving the five beginning with kala, becoming itself in the lone kevala state, going beyond thought into the state beyond, and reaching what is known there, the soul itself is the Supreme within.","n":"கலாதி நாலொன்று (kala and the rest, four and one) is taken as the five sheaths that limit the soul (kala, vidya, raga, niyati, kala). நேயம் may be the object of knowing (jneya) or love. தற்பரம் is the third supreme, the Supreme as one's own.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","thaththuvam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:178"},"t:2451":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2451,"t":["தற்கண்ட தூயமும் தன்னில் விசாலமும்","பிற்காணும் தூடணம் தானும் பிறழ்வுற்றுத்","தற்பரன் கால பரமும் கலந்தற்ற","நற்பரா தீதமும் நாடுஅக ராதியே."],"p":"","r":"The purity one sees in oneself, the vastness in oneself, the fault seen afterwards, all falter, and tatpara and the supreme of time mingle and end; that good state beyond the Supreme is to be sought through the letters beginning with A.","n":"தூடணம் is dushana (fault), and கால பரம் perhaps the supreme as time. அகராதி, the letters beginning with A, may mean the parts of Om or the mantra letters. The verse is compressed and its steps are not clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","mandhiram","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:178"},"t:2452":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2452,"t":["அதீதத்து ளாகி அகன்றவன் நந்தி","அதீதத்து ளாகி அறிவிலோன் ஆன்மா","மதிபெற் றிருள்விட்ட மன்னுயிர் ஒன்றாம்","பதியிற் பதியும் பரவுயிர் தானே."],"p":"","r":"Nandi, in the state beyond, stands apart from all; the soul in that beyond-state is without knowing; the abiding soul that gains understanding and leaves its darkness becomes one, and as the supreme soul it settles in the Lord.","n":"அதீதம் (the state beyond) is said of both: for Nandi it is transcendence, for the soul it is traditionally taken as its state of utter unknowing before grace. பதியிற் பதியும் plays on பதி, the Lord, and பதிதல, to settle or be imprinted.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","pasu","avaththai","saayujjiyam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:179"},"t:2453":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2453,"t":["ஆதியும் அந்தமும் இல்லா அரும்பதி","சோதிப் பரஞ்சுடர் தோன்றத்தோன் றாமையின்","நீதிய தாய்நிற்கும் நீடிய அப்பர","போதம் உணர்ந்தவர் புண்ணியத் தோரே."],"p":"","r":"The rare Lord without beginning or end, the supreme flame of light, appears and yet does not appear, and stands as the right order; those who have known that long supreme knowledge are the blessed.","n":"தோன்றத் தோன்றாமையின் can be read as his appearing without appearing, or as the world not appearing once his light appears. நீதி is order or justice; புண்ணியத்தோர் are those whose merit has ripened.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pathi","gnaanam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:179"},"t:2454":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2454,"t":["துரியங் கடங்கு துரியா தீதத்தே","அரிய வியோகங்கொண்டு அம்பலத் தாடும்","பெரிய பிரானைப் பிரணவக் கூபத்தே","துரியவல் லார்க்குத் துரிசில்லை தானே."],"p":"","r":"For those who can enter the well of Om and reach the great Lord who dances in the hall, in the state beyond turiya where turiya itself is contained, by a rare yoga, there is no flaw.","n":"துரியாதீதம் is the state beyond turiya. வியோகம் can be viyoga, separation from the bonds, or a heightened yoga. துரிய in the last line chimes with turiya and is taken as 'to enter or plunge'; துரிசு is fault.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","thirukkoothu","piranavam","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:179"},"t:2455":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2455,"t":["செம்மைமுன் னிற்பச் சுவேதம் திரிவபோல்","அம்மெய்ப் பரத்தோடு அணுவன்உள் ளாயிடப்","பொய்மைச் சகமுண்ட போத வெறும்பாழில்","செம்மைச் சிவமேரு சேர்கொடி யாகுமே."],"p":"","r":"As whiteness changes when red stands before it, so the soul, coming within that true Supreme, in the empty void of knowledge that has swallowed the false world, becomes the flag that joins the red Siva-Meru.","n":"The image is the crystal that takes the colour of what is near it, a classic Siddhanta figure for the soul taking on what it stands beside. மேரு is traditionally the crown of the yogic body; கொடி may be flag or creeper.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pasu","sivam","paazh","saayujjiyam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:179"},"t:2456":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2456,"t":["வைச்ச கலாதி வருதத்து வங்கெட","வெச்ச இருமாயை வேறாக வேரறுத்து","உச்ச பரசிவ மாம்உண்மை ஒன்றவே","அச்சம் அறுத்தென்னை ஆண்டவன் நந்தியே."],"p":"","r":"So that the tattvas from kala onward that were set on me perish, cutting at the root the two mayas as things apart, so that I become one with the truth that is the highest Parasiva, Nandi cut away my fear and took me as his own.","n":"இருமாயை are traditionally pure and impure maya. The verse credits release to Nandi's act, not to the speaker's effort: ஆண்டவன், he who ruled me.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["guru","maayai","thaththuvam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:179"},"t:2457":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2457,"t":["என்னை அறிய இசைவித்த என்நந்தி","என்னை அறிந்து அறி யாத இடத்துய்த்துப்","பின்னை ஒளியிற் சொரூபம் புறப்பட்டுத்","தன்னை அளித்தான் தற்பர மாகவே."],"p":"","r":"My Nandi, who made me fit to know myself, knew me and led me to the place beyond knowing, then came forth in light as his own form and gave himself to me as the Supreme beyond.","n":"என்னை அறிந்து may be Nandi knowing me or me knowing myself under him; அறியாத இடம், the place not known, is the state beyond ordinary knowing. தன்னை அளித்தான், he gave himself, is the climax.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["guru","thiruvarul","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:179"},"t:2458":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2458,"t":["பரந்தும் சுருங்கியும் பார்புனல் வாயு","நிரந்தர வளியொடு ஞாயிறு திங்கள்","அரந்த அறநெறி யாயது வாகித்","தரந்த விசும்பொன்று தாங்கிநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"Expanding and contracting, he becomes earth, water and air, the unceasing wind, the sun and the moon, and the way of dharma, and stands bearing the one sky.","n":"The list should run through the five elements, but வாயு and வளி both mean air and fire is missing; one may be a corruption. அரந்த may be Hara's, or what removes sorrow.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","aram","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:179"},"t:2459":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2459,"t":["சத்தின் நிலையினில் தானான சத்தியும்","தற்பரை யாய்நிற்கும் தானாம் பரற்கு உடல்","உய்த்தரும் இச்சையில் ஞானாதி பேதமாய்","நித்தம் நடத்தும் நடிக்கும்மா நேயத்தே."],"p":"","r":"Shakti, who in the state of being became one with him, stands as Tatpara; as the body of the Supreme who is himself, she takes on the distinctions of will, knowledge and the rest that she brings forth, and daily moves and dances in great love.","n":"That Shakti is Siva's body (உடல்) is a Siddhanta teaching; இச்சை, ஞானம் and the unstated கிரியை are her three powers. நடத்தும் நடிக்கும் may be 'conducts and dances' or one act said twice.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","sivam","thirukkoothu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:179"},"t:2460":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2460,"t":["மேலொடு கீழ்பக்கம் மெய்வாய்கண் நாசிகள்","பாலிய விந்து பரையுள் பரையாகக்","கோலிய நான்சுவை ஞானம் கொணர் விந்து","சீலமி லாஅணுச் செய்திய தாமே."],"p":"","r":"Bindu, nursed within Para as Para, reaches above, below and the sides, and the body, mouth, eyes and nose; the bindu that brings the knowing of taste becomes the doing of the undisciplined soul.","n":"The verse is obscure. நான்சுவை may be 'four tastes' or 'I-taste', and சீலமிலா அணு, the soul without discipline, may mean the bound soul in general. The plain sense is that bindu, born in Shakti, runs through the senses and so into the soul's acts.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vindhu","saththi","pasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:179"},"t:2461":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2461,"t":["வேறாம் அதன்தன்மை போலும்இக் காயத்தில்","ஆறாம் உபாதி அனைத்தாகும் தத்துவம்","பேறாம் பரவொளி தூண்டும் பிரகாசமாய்","ஊறாய் உயிர்த்துண்டு உறங்கிடும் மாயையே."],"p":"","r":"In this body, as with that separate nature, the adjuncts become all the tattvas; maya, lit as brightness by the supreme light that stirs it, becomes sensation, breathes, eats and sleeps.","n":"ஆறாம் may be 'sixth' or 'that becomes'. The point, as tradition reads it, is that maya is inert and acts only as lit by Siva's light, so the body's breathing, eating and sleeping belong to maya.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maayai","upaadhi","thaththuvam","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:179"},"t:2462":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2462,"t":["தற்பரம் மன்னும் தனிமுதல் பேரொளி","சிற்பரம் தானே செகமுண்ணும் போதமும்","தொற்பதம் தீர்பாழில் சுந்தரச் சோதிபுக்கு","அப்புறம் மற்றதுஇங்கு ஒப்பில்லை தானே."],"p":"","r":"The great light, sole and first, abiding as Tatpara, is itself Chitpara; the knowledge that swallowed the world enters the beautiful light in the void where the pada ends, and beyond that there is nothing here to compare it with.","n":"தற்பரம் and சிற்பரம் are the Supreme as itself and as pure consciousness. தொற்பதம் is probably தொம்பதம் (the soul's pada) or தொல்பதம் (the ancient pada).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","paazh","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:179"},"t:2463":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2463,"t":["பண்டை மறைகள் பரவான் உடலென்னும்","துண்ட மதியோன் துரியாதீ தந்தன்னைக்","கண்டு பரனும்அக் காரணோ பாதிக்கே","மிண்டின் அவன்சுத்தம் ஆகான் வினவிலே."],"p":"","r":"The ancient Vedas speak of the supreme space as his body; even one who has seen the turiyatita of the crescent-wearer, if he clings to that causal adjunct, is not pure, if you ask.","n":"Who is meant by பரனும் is unclear: the Supreme himself, said to be impure if bound to a causal adjunct, or the seer who has reached the Supreme. மிண்டின், if he clings or presses, is read either way.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["upaadhi","avaththai","vedham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:179"},"t:2464":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2464,"t":["வெளிகால் கனல்அப்பு மேவுமண் நின்ற","தனியா இயதற் பரங்காண் அவன்தான்","வெளிகால் கனல்அப்பு மேவுமண் நின்ற","வெளியாய சத்தி அவன்வடி வாமே."],"p":"","r":"See him as the lone Tatpara standing within space, wind, fire, water and earth; and the Shakti that is the open space standing in those same five is his form.","n":"Lines 1 and 3 are the same, so the verse places Siva and Shakti in the same five elements: he as what stands alone within them, she as the form they take.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","saththi","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:179"},"t:2465":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2465,"t":["மேருவி னோடே விரிகதிர் மண்டலம்","ஆர நினையும் அருந்தவ யோகிக்குச்","சீரார் தவம்செய்யில் சிவனருள் தானாகும்","பேரவும் வேண்டாம் பிறிதில்லை தானே."],"p":"","r":"For the yogi of rare austerity who thinks fully on Meru and the disc of spreading rays, if he does glorious tapas, Siva's grace becomes his own; he need not go elsewhere, for there is nothing other.","n":"Meru and the rayed disc are traditionally the crown of the yogic body and the inner sun. சிவனருள் தானாகும் may mean grace comes of itself or the yogi becomes grace. பேரவும் வேண்டாம்: no need to move or leave.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","thavam","thiruvarul","aadhiththan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:179"},"t:2466":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2466,"t":["நனவாதி மூன்றினில் சீவ துரியம்","தனதுஆதி மூன்றினில் பரதுரி யந்தான்","நனவாதி மூன்றி னில்சிவ துரியமாம்","இனதாகும் தொந்தத் தசிபதத் துஈடே."],"p":"","r":"In the three states from waking is the soul's turiya; in the three from his own is the Supreme's turiya; in the three from waking is Siva's turiya; these together match the padas of tvam tat asi.","n":"The scheme is technical: each turiya is reached through its own triad of waking, dream and sleep, and the three are traditionally matched with தொம் (the soul), தத் (the Supreme) and அசி (their union).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","thonthaththasi","pasu","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:180"},"t:2467":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2467,"t":["தானாம் நனவில் துரியம்தன் தொம்பதம்","தானாம் துரியம் நனவாதி தான்மூன்றில்","ஆனாப் பரபதம் அற்றது அருநனா","வானான மேல்மூன்றில் துரியம் அணுகுமே."],"p":"","r":"Turiya in one's own waking is one's tvam-pada; the same turiya runs through waking and the other two; the unending supreme pada is beyond; and in the three higher states, like the sky, Siva's turiya draws near.","n":"ஆனாப் பரபதம் அற்றது அருநனா is obscure and the reading of line 3 is a guess. வானான மேல் மூன்று, the upper three that are sky, are taken as the higher grades of the states.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","thonthaththasi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:180"},"t:2468":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2468,"t":["அணுவின் துரியத்து நான்கும துஆகிப்","பணியும் பரதுரி யம்பயில் நான்கும்","தணிவில் பரமாகிச் சாரமுந் துரியக்","கணுவில் இந் நான்கும் கலந்தார் ஐந்தே."],"p":"","r":"The four states in the soul's turiya, and the four practised in the Supreme's turiya, ripening into the Supreme that does not abate, meet at the node of turiya, and combined they make five.","n":"The arithmetic (four, four, five) depends on how the states are counted; கணு, node or joint, is taken as the point where the soul's states meet the Supreme's. The sense is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","pasu","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:180"},"t:2469":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2469,"t":["ஈர்ஐந்து அவத்தை இசைமுத் துரியத்துள்","நேர்அந்த மாகநெறிவழி யேசென்று","பார்அந்த மான பராபத்து அயிக்கியத்து","ஓர்அந்த மாம்இரு பாதியைச் சேர்த்திடே."],"p":"","r":"Go straight along the path through the ten states within the three turiyas to their end, and in the union with the Supreme that is the end of all, join the two halves into one end.","n":"ஈர்ஐந்து அவத்தை, ten states, are traditionally the five lower and five pure states. இரு பாதி, the two halves, may be the soul and the Supreme or the tvam and tat of the saying.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","saayujjiyam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:180"},"t:2470":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2470,"t":["தொட்டே இருமின் துரிய நிலத்தினை","எட்டாது எனின்நின்று எட்டும் இறைவனைப்","பட்டாங்கு அறிந்திடில் பன்னா உதடுகள்","தட்டாது ஒழிவதோர் தத்துவந் தானே."],"p":"","r":"Hold fast to the ground of turiya; though the Lord seems out of reach, standing there you reach him, and when he is known as he truly is, the lips no longer meet to recite: that is the truth of it.","n":"பன்னா உதடுகள் தட்டாது, lips that do not strike together, is read as silence or as the mantra repeated without the lips moving. எட்டாது எனின் நின்று எட்டும் plays on reaching what does not reach.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","iraivan","mounam","mandhiram"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:180"},"t:2471":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2471,"t":["அறிவாய் அசத்தென்னும் ஆறாறு அகன்று","செறிவாய மாயை சிதைத்துஅரு ளாலே","பிறியாத பேரருள் ஆயிடும் பெற்றி","நெறியான அன்பர் நிலையறிந் தாரே."],"p":"","r":"Those devotees on the path who knew the thirty-six as unreal before knowledge and left them, and broke dense maya by grace, came to know the state that becomes the great grace never parted.","n":"அசத்து is the Siddhanta term for what is inert and passing, set against the conscious soul. Here the breaking of maya is by grace, and the knowers are அன்பர், those who love.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","maayai","thiruvarul","adiyaar"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:180"},"t:2472":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2472,"t":["நனவின் நனவாகி நாலாம் துரியம்","தனதுயிர் தெம்பதம் ஆமாறு போல","வினையறு சீவன் நனவாதி யாகத்","தனைய பரதுரி யந்தற் பதமே."],"p":"","r":"As the fourth, turiya, becoming the waking within waking, is the soul's tvam-pada, so for the soul freed of deeds, becoming waking and the rest, the Supreme's turiya is the tat-pada.","n":"தெம்பதம் is read as தொம்பதம். The verse makes the two padas parallel grades of the same scheme: the soul's turiya for tvam, the Supreme's for tat, reached by the soul freed of deeds.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","thonthaththasi","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:180"},"t:2473":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2473,"t":["தொம்பதம் தற்பதம் சொன்முத் துரியம்பொல்","நம்பிய மூன்றாம் துரியத்து நல்நாமம்","அம்புலி யுன்னா அதிசூக்கம் அப்பாலைச்","செம்பொருள் ஆண்டருள் சீர்நந்தி தானே."],"p":"","r":"As tvam-pada and tat-pada are named as turiyas, the good name belongs to the trusted third turiya; beyond the moon and beyond the very subtle, glorious Nandi, the true reality, took me and gave grace.","n":"The third turiya is the asi of the saying, the union. அம்புலி உன்னா, not thinking the moon, is taken as beyond the moon of the yogic body. செம்பொருள், the true reality, is a word the Kural also uses.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thonthaththasi","avaththai","guru","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:180"},"t:2474":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2474,"t":["சீவன்தன் முத்தி அதீதம் பரமுத்தி","ஓய்உப சாந்தம் சிவமுத்தி ஆனந்தம்","Yமூவயின் முச்சொரூப முத்திமுப் பாலதாய்","ஓவுறு தாரத்தில் உள்ளும்நா தாந்தமே."],"p":"","r":"Release of the soul is the state beyond, release in the Supreme is the stilled calm, release in Siva is bliss; these three releases of three forms dwell within Om, at the end of nada.","n":"அதீதம், உபசாந்தம், ஆனந்தம் name the three releases. தாரம் is taken as Om and நாதாந்தம் as the end of nada. Line 3 is printed with a stray letter.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["veedu","avaththai","aanandham","piranavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:181"},"t:2475":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2475,"t":["ஆவது அறியார் உயிர்பிறப் பாலுறும்","ஆவது அறியும் உயிர்அருட் பாலுறும்","ஆவது ஒன்றில்லை அகம்புறத் தென்றுஅகன்று","ஓவு சிவனுடன் ஒன்றாதல் முத்தியே."],"p":"","r":"Souls that do not know what truly is go to birth; souls that know it go to grace; seeing that nothing inside or outside is anything in itself, and leaving it, to become one with Siva beyond is release.","n":"ஆவது, 'what is' or 'what avails', opens three lines. ஓவு is cessation, and ஓவு சிவன் may be Siva who is the ceasing or Siva beyond.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["veedu","piravi","thiruvarul","saayujjiyam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:181"},"t:2476":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2476,"t":["சிவமாகி மும்மலம் முக்குணம் செற்றுத்","தவமான மும்முத்தி தத்துவத்து அயிக்கியத்","துவம்ஆ கியநெறி சோகம்என் போர்க்குச்","சிவமாம் அமலன் சிறந்தனன் தானே."],"p":"","r":"To those who become Siva, destroying the three impurities and three gunas, and say 'I am he' on the path of union with the three releases, the stainless one, Siva, stands forth.","n":"சோகம் is so'ham, 'I am he'; it also means sorrow, but the context fixes the first sense. தவமான, which is tapas, may qualify the three releases or the path.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["malam","saayujjiyam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:181"},"t:2477":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2477,"t":["சித்தியும் முத்தியும் திண்சிவ மாகிய","சுத்தியும் முத்தீ தொலைக்கும் சுகானந்த","சத்தியும் மேலைச் சமாதியும் ஆயிடும்","பெத்தம் அறுத்த பெரும்பெரு மானே."],"p":"","r":"The great Lord who cut away bondage becomes attainment, release, the purity that is firm Siva, the Shakti of bliss that ends the three fires, and the highest samadhi.","n":"பெத்தம் (baddha) is the bound state, opposite of release. முத்தீ, three fires, are taken as the three impurities or the three torments; the ritual three fires are another reading.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","siththi","veedu","samaadhi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:181"},"t:2478":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2478,"t":["ஏறிய வாறே மலம்ஐந் திடைஅடைந்து","ஆறிய ஞானச் சிவோகம் அடைந்திட்டு","வேறும் எனமுச் சொரூபத்து வீடுற்று அங்கு","ஈறதில் பண்டைப் பரன்உண்மை செய்யுமே."],"p":"","r":"Rising stage by stage past the five impurities, reaching the stilled knowledge of 'I am Siva', and gaining release in the three forms as if they were separate, at the end the ancient Supreme makes the truth plain.","n":"மலம் ஐந்து are traditionally anava, karma, maya, mayeya and tirodhana. வேறும் என, 'as if separate', suggests the three forms are distinguished only on the way.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["malam","gnaanam","saayujjiyam","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:182"},"t:2479":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2479,"t":["மூன்றுள மாளிகை மூவர் இருப்பிடம்","மூன்றினில் முப்பத் தாறும் உதிப்புள","மூன்றினின் உள்ளே முளைத்தெழும் சோதியைக்","காண்டலும் காயக் கணக்கற்ற வாறே."],"p":"","r":"Three mansions are the seat of the three; in the three the thirty-six arise; on seeing the light that sprouts and rises within the three, the body's reckoning ceases.","n":"The three mansions may be the three bodies (gross, subtle, causal) or three regions of the yogic body; the three who dwell there are traditionally Brahma, Vishnu and Rudra. காயக் கணக்கு, the body's account, may be its debts of deeds.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","yaakkai","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:182"},"t:2480":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2480,"t":["உலகம் புடைபெயர்ந்து ஊழியம் போன","நிலவு சுடரொளி மூன்றும் ஒன்றாய","பலவும் பரிசொடு பான்மையுள் ஈசன்","அளவும் பெருமையும் ஆரறி வாரே."],"p":"","r":"When the world is moved from its place and the ages are gone, and the three abiding lights become one, who can know the measure and greatness of the Lord within the many things and their natures?","n":"The three lights are traditionally sun, moon and fire. The verse sets the Lord against dissolution: when all else merges, he remains past measure.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pathi","sivam","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:182"},"t:2481":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2481,"t":["பெருவாய் முதலெண்ணும் பேதமே பேதித்து","அருவாய் உருவாய் அருவுரு வாகிக்","குருவாய் வரும்சத்தி கோன்உயிர்ப் பன்மை","உருவாய் உடனிருந்து ஒன்றாய்அன் றாமே."],"p":"","r":"Dividing the distinctions that start from the great one, the Lord becomes formless, form and form-and-formless; comes as Shakti, as guru, as the many souls; and dwelling with them in form, is one with them and not one.","n":"பெருவாய் may be mahat, the great principle, or something else. ஒன்றாய் அன்றாம், one and not one, states the relation of Lord and souls as Siddhanta takes it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","saththi","guru","pasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:182"},"t:2482":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2482,"t":["மணிஒளி சோபை இலக்கணம் வாய்த்து","மணிஎன வாய்நின்ற வாறுஅது போலத்","தணிமுச் சொருபாதி சத்தியாதி சாரப்","பணிவித்த பேர்நந்தி பாதம்பற்றாயே."],"p":"","r":"As a gem's light and lustre are its own marks and it stands as the gem, so the gentle three forms and the Shaktis rest in him: hold to the feet of great Nandi who made them serve.","n":"The gem and its light is the classic image of a quality inseparable from what has it. பணிவித்த, who made them bow or serve, may also mean who made me bow.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvadi","saththi","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:182"},"t:2483":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2483,"t":["கல்லொளி மாநிறம் சோபை கதிர்தட்ட","நல்ல மணியொன்றின் நாடிஒண் முப்பதும்","சொல்லறும் முப்பாழில் சொல்லறு பேருரைத்து","அல்அறும் முத்திராந் தத்துஅனு பூதியே."],"p":"","r":"As one good gem holds its stone-light, colour, lustre and the strike of its rays, so in the three voids where words cease, speaking the great word beyond speech, is the experience at the end of the three, free of darkness.","n":"நாடி ஒண் முப்பதும் is obscure (perhaps the bright thirty, or a misreading of முப்பதம், the three padas), as is முத்திராந்தம், the end of the mudras or of the three. The gem image continues 2482.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["paazh","mounam","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:182"},"t:2484":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2484,"t":["உடந்தசெந் தாமரை உள்ளுறு சோதி","நடந்தசெந் தாமரை நாதம் தகைந்தால்","அடைந்த பயோதரி அட்டி அடைத்தஅவ்","விடம்தரு வாசலை மேல்திற வீரே."],"p":"","r":"Restrain the nada of the moving red lotus that holds a light within, and open from above the doorway that she who holds the waters has sealed shut.","n":"The red lotus is a centre of the yogic body. பயோதரி, she who holds water or milk, is taken as kundalini or a nadi that blocks the way up; விடம் may be 'place' or 'poison'. A plain reading is not available.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aadhaaram","naadham","kundalini"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:182"},"t:2485":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2485,"t":["இடனொரு மூன்றில் இயைந்த ஒருவன்","கடன் உறும் அவ்வுரு வேறெனக்காணும்","திடமது போலச் சிவபர சீவர்","உடனுரை பேதமும் ஒன்றென லாமே."],"p":"","r":"As one man, joined to three places, is seen in a different form as each duty requires, so the difference among Siva, the Supreme and the soul, spoken of together, may be called one.","n":"The image is one person in three roles. ஒன்றெனலாம், 'may be called one', is guarded: it allows the difference while naming it one.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","pasu","pathi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:183"},"t:2486":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2486,"t":["ஒளியைஒளிசெய்து ஓம்என்று எழுப்பி","வளியை வளிசெய்து வாய்த்திட வாங்கி","வெளியை வெளிசெய்து மேலெழ வைத்துத்","தெளியத் தெளியும் சிவபதம் தானே."],"p":"","r":"Make the light shine and raise it as Om, make the breath breath and draw it in fittingly, make space space and set it rising upward, and as clarity grows, Siva's state grows clear.","n":"Each line doubles its noun (ஒளியை ஒளிசெய்து, வளியை வளிசெய்து, வெளியை வெளிசெய்து), so each is made purely itself. The three are traditionally taken as the mantra, the breath and the inner space of the yogic practice.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piranavam","praanan","yogam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:183"},"t:2487":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2487,"t":["முக்கர ணங்களின் மூர்ச்சைதீர்த்து ஆவதுஅக்","கைக்கா ரணம் என்னத் தந்தனன் காண்நந்தி","மிக்க மனோன்மணி வேறே தனித்துஏக","ஒக்குமது உன்மணி ஓதுஉள் சமாதியே."],"p":"","r":"Nandi gave, as a thing held in the hand, what comes when the stupor of the three instruments is cleared; great Manonmani then goes on alone, and that unmani is the inner samadhi.","n":"முக்கரணம் are traditionally mind, speech and body, or the inner instruments. மனோன்மணி is a name of Shakti and a stage of yoga; உன்மணி (unmani) is the state beyond the mind. அக்கைக்காரணம் is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["manam","samaadhi","saththi","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:183"},"t:2488":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2488,"t":["தற்பதம் தொம்பதம் தானாம் அசிபதம்","தொல்பதம் மூன்றும் துரியத்துத் தோற்றலே","நிற்பது உயிர்பரன் நிகழ்சிவ மும்மூன்றின்","சொற்பத மாகும் தொந்தத் தசியே."],"p":"","r":"Tat-pada, tvam-pada and asi-pada, which is itself: these three ancient padas appear in turiya; what stands in them is the soul, the Supreme and Siva who comes forth, and in these three threes the word-padas become tvam tat asi.","n":"The verse names the three words of the saying and matches them with soul, Supreme and Siva. Which pada goes with which is the tradition's reading of a compressed line.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thonthaththasi","avaththai","pasu","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:184"},"t:2489":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2489,"t":["தொந்தத் தசிமூன்றில் தொல்கா மியமாதி","தொந்தத் தசிமூன்றில் தொல்கா மதமாதி","வந்த மலம்குணம் மாளச் சிவம்தோன்றின்","இந்துவின் முன்இருள் ஏகுதல் ஒக்குமே."],"p":"","r":"In the three of tvam tat asi lie the old impurities beginning with kamya and the old qualities, and when these impurities and qualities die and Siva appears, it is like darkness going before the moon.","n":"காமியம் is the impurity of desire-born deeds. காமதம் in line 2 is doubtful; 2494 has தாமதம், and line 2 may name the qualities beginning with tamas. இந்து is the moon.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thonthaththasi","malam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:184"},"t:2490":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2490,"t":["தொந்தத் தசியைஅவ் வாசியில் தோற்றியே","அந்த முறைஈர் ஐந்தாக மதித்திட்டு","அந்தம் இல்லாத அவத்தைஅவ் வாக்கியத்து","உந்து முறையில் சிவன்முன்வைத்து ஓதிடே."],"p":"","r":"Bring forth tvam tat asi in the breath, reckon its order as tenfold, and recite the saying with its endless states before Siva in the order that moves it.","n":"வாசி may be the breath, as in the yoga of that name, or the utterance. ஈர்ஐந்து, ten, recalls the ten states of 2469. The practice described cannot be fixed from the verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thonthaththasi","praanan","avaththai","mandhiram"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:184"},"t:2491":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2491,"t":["வைத்துச் சிவத்தை மதிசொரு பானந்தத்து","உய்த்துப் பிரணவ மாம்உப தேசத்தை","மெய்த்த இதயத்து விட்டிடு மெய்யுணர்ந்து","அத்தற்கு அடிமை அடைந்துநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"Setting Siva within the bliss of one's own nature, and letting the teaching that is Om sink into a true heart, he knew the truth and stood, having become the Father's servant.","n":"மதி may be 'the mind' or 'the moon'. The verse ends in servitude (அடிமை), not in identity, which is worth noticing in a chapter on the soul becoming Parasiva.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piranavam","aanandham","meyyunarvu","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:184"},"t:2492":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2492,"t":["தொம்பதம் மாயையுள் தோன்றிடும் தற்பதம்","அம்புரை தன்னில் உதிக்கும் அசிபதம்","நம்புறு சாந்தியில் நண்ணும்அவ் வாக்கியம்","உம்பர் உரைதொந்தத் தசிவாசி யாமே."],"p":"","r":"Tvam-pada appears within maya, tat-pada rises in the beyond, asi-pada is reached in trusted peace: this is the saying tvam tat asi, spoken by the heavenly ones.","n":"அம்புரை is obscure; it is read here as the beyond. உம்பர் உரை may be the word of the gods or the word from above.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thonthaththasi","maayai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:184"},"t:2493":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2493,"t":["ஆகிய அச்சோயம் தேவதத் தன்இடத்து","ஆகிய வைவிட்டால் காயம் உபாதானம்","ஏகிய தொந்தத் தசியென்ப மெய்யறிவு","ஆகிய சீவன் பரசிவன் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"As in 'this is that Devadatta' the qualities that came between are let go, so when the body and its material cause are set aside, the true knowledge called tvam tat asi is that the soul is Parasiva.","n":"அச்சோயம் தேவதத்தன் is so'yam devadattah, 'this is that Devadatta', the standard example by which the saying's identity is explained: the same man seen in another time and place. Tirumular uses the Vedanta example within a Saiva frame.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thonthaththasi","upaadhi","pasu","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:184"},"t:2494":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2494,"t":["தாமதம் காமியம் ஆசித் தகுணம்","மாமலம் மூன்றும் அகார உகாரத்தோடு","ஆம்அறும் மவ்வும் அவ் வாய்உடல் மூன்றில்","தாமாம் துரியமும் தொந்தத் தசியதே."],"p":"","r":"The three great impurities, tamas, kamya and the dark quality, go with A, U and the vanishing M through the three bodies, and the turiya in which they are themselves is tvam tat asi.","n":"ஆசித்த குணம் is unclear. அகார, உகார, மகாரம் are the letters of Om; ஆம் அறும் மவ்வும், 'M that ends', and உடல் மூன்று, the three bodies, follow the mantra analysis. The line-by-line pairing is the tradition's.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["malam","piranavam","thonthaththasi","avaththai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:184"},"t:2495":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2495,"t":["காரியம் ஏழ்கண் டறும்மாயப் பாழ்விடக்","காரணம் ஏழ்கண் டறும்போதப் பாழ்விடக்","காரிய காரண வாதனை கண்டறும்","சீர்உப சாந்தமுப் பாழ்விடத் தீருமே."],"p":"","r":"Seeing and leaving the seven effects, the void of maya; seeing and leaving the seven causes, the void of knowledge; seeing and ending the traces of effect and cause, the bond ends in the glorious void of calm.","n":"The seven effects and seven causes recur in 2501 and 2505 and are not listed. வாதனை are the latent traces (vasana). உபசாந்தம் is the stilled calm named in 2474.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["paazh","upaadhi","maayai","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:185"},"t:2496":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2496,"t":["மாயப்பாழ் சீவன் வியோமப்பாழ் மன்பரன்","சேயமுப் பாழெனச் சிவசத்தி யில் சீவன்","ஆய வியாப்தம் எனும்முப்பா ழாம்அந்தத்","தூய சொரூபத்தில் சொல்முடி வாகுமே."],"p":"","r":"The void of maya is the soul, the void of space is the abiding Supreme, and the far third void is the soul pervaded in Siva-Shakti; in that pure form speech comes to its end.","n":"The three voids are matched to soul, Supreme and union, as the padas were in 2488. வியாப்தம் is pervasion; சொல் முடிவு, the end of words, fits the wordless void of 2498.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["paazh","pasu","saththi","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:185"},"t:2497":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2497,"t":["எதிர்அற நாளும் எருதுஉவந்து ஏறும்","பதியெனும் நந்தி பதமது கூடக்","கதியெனப் பாழை கடந்து அந்தக் கற்பனை","உதறிய பாழில் ஒடுங்குகின் றேனே."],"p":"","r":"To join the feet of Nandi, the Lord who rides his bull gladly every day and has no rival, I take the void as my way, cross it, and come to rest in the void that has shaken off even that imagining.","n":"There are two voids here: the one crossed, and the one beyond imagining where the speaker comes to rest. The bull-rider gives a personal Lord in a chapter otherwise abstract.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvadi","paazh","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:185"},"t:2498":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2498,"t":["துரியம் அடங்கிய சொல்லறும் பாழை","அரிய பரம்பரம் என்பர்கள் ஆதர்","அரிய பரம்பரம் என்றே துதிக்கும்","அருநிலம் என்பதை யார்அறி வாரே."],"p":"","r":"The wordless void in which turiya subsides, the foolish call the rare Paramparam; but who knows the rare ground they praise by that name?","n":"ஆதர் usually means the ignorant, which makes the verse a rebuke to those who name it without knowing; if it is taken as 'those who love', the last line is a wondering question instead.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["paazh","avaththai","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:185"},"t:2499":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2499,"t":["ஆறாறு நீங்க நமவாதி அகன்றிட்டு","வேறா கயபரை யாவென்று மெய்ப்பரன்","ஈறான வாசியில் கூட்டும் அதுவன்றோ","தேறாச் சிவாய நமவெனத் தேறிலே."],"p":"","r":"When the thirty-six are gone and 'nama' with the rest has withdrawn, the true Supreme, standing apart, joins one in the final breath; is this not what comes when one comes clear in 'Sivayanama', which the unclear cannot grasp?","n":"In the five letters tradition reads ந and ம as concealment and impurity, so dropping நம leaves சிவாய, Siva, grace and soul. Line 2 (வேறா கயபரை யா) is obscure, and வாசி may be breath or utterance.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panchaakkaram","thaththuvam","praanan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:185"},"t:2500":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2500,"t":["உள்ளம் உருவென்றும் உருவம் உளமென்றும்","உள்ள பரிசறித் தோரும் அவர்கட்குப்","பள்ளமும் இல்லைத் திடர்இல்லை பாழ்இல்லை","உள்ளமும் இல்லை உருவில்லை தானே."],"p":"","r":"For those who know the true nature, that the heart is form and form is the heart, there is no hollow, no mound, no void, no heart and no form.","n":"The verse closes a chapter on voids by denying the void too. பள்ளம், திடர் (hollow, mound) are the uneven ground of distinctions.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["meyyunarvu","manam","paazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:185"},"t:2501":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2501,"t":["செற்றிடும் சீவ உபாதித் திறன்ஏழும்","பற்றும் பரோபதி ஏழும் பகருரை","உற்றிடும் காரிய காரணத் தோடற","அற்றிட அச்சிவ மாகும் அணுவனே."],"p":"","r":"When the seven powers of the soul's adjuncts that must be destroyed, and the seven adjuncts of the Supreme that cling, are cut off together with effect and cause, the soul becomes that Siva.","n":"The seven and seven are not listed. That the Supreme too has adjuncts (பரோபாதி) recalls 2463, where even the Supreme clinging to a causal adjunct is called impure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["upaadhi","pasu","sivam","saayujjiyam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:186"},"t:2502":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2502,"t":["ஆறாறு காரியோ பாதி அகன்றிட்டு","வேறாய் நனவு மிகுந்த கனாநனா","ஆறாறு அகன்ற கழுத்தி அதில் எய்தாப்","பேறா நிலத்துயிர் தொம்பதம் பேசிலே."],"p":"","r":"When the thirty-six effect-adjuncts have gone, the soul, passing apart through waking, the dream within waking and the deep sleep beyond the thirty-six, and standing in a ground it has not yet reached, is what is called tvam-pada.","n":"பேறா நிலம் may be 'the ground not yet gained' or 'the ground of gain'. கனாநனா is dream within waking, one of the graded states.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["upaadhi","avaththai","thonthaththasi","pasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:186"},"t:2503":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2503,"t":["அகாரம் உயிரே உகாரம் பரமே","மகாரம் சிவமாய் வருமுப் பதத்துச்","சிகாரம் சிவமே வகாரம் பரமே","யகாரம் உயிரென்று அறையலும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"A is the soul, U is the Supreme, M is Siva in the three padas; one may also say SI is Siva, VA is the Supreme and YA is the soul.","n":"The verse matches the letters of Om and of சிவாய with the same three. The order reverses: in Om the soul comes first, in சிவாய Siva does.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piranavam","panchaakkaram","thonthaththasi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:186"},"t:2504":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2504,"t":["உயிர்க்குயி ராகி ஒழிவற்று அழிவற்று","அயிர்ப்புஅறும் காரணோ பாதி விதிரேகத்து","உயிர்ப்புறும் ஈசன் உபமிதத் தால்அன்றி","வியர்ப்புறும் ஆணவம் வீடல்செய் யாவே."],"p":"","r":"The Lord is the life of life, unceasing and undying, and lives in the doubt-free distinction from the causal adjunct; except through him, the anava that wearies will not leave.","n":"ஆணவம் is the root impurity of Siddhanta. விதிரேகம் (vyatireka, distinction) and உபமிதம் (comparison, or what is shown by likeness) are technical terms of logic, and the second line's sense is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["malam","iraivan","upaadhi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:186"},"t:2505":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2505,"t":["காரியம் ஏழில் கலக்கும் கடும்பசு","காரணம் ஏழில் கலக்கும் பரசிவன்","காரிய காரணம் கற்பனை சொற்பதப்","பாரறும் பாழில் பராபரத் தானே."],"p":"","r":"The harsh bound soul mixes in the seven effects, Parasiva mixes in the seven causes; in the void beyond the world of imagined effect and cause and of word and pada, he is the Supreme beyond the supreme.","n":"கடும்பசு, the harsh or tightly bound soul. பராபரம் is the Supreme beyond the supreme. The verse returns to the voids of 2495 to place the Supreme beyond both effect and cause.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pasu","upaadhi","paazh","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:186"},"t:2506":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2506,"t":["முத்திக்கு வித்து முதல்வன்தன் ஞானமே","பத்திக்கு வித்துப் பணிந்துற்றப் பற்றலே","சித்திக்கு வித்துச் சிவபரம் தானாதல்","சத்திக்கு வித்துத் தனதுப சாந்தமே."],"p":"","r":"The seed of release is the Lord's own knowledge, the seed of devotion is bowing and clinging to him, the seed of attainment is becoming supreme Siva oneself, and the seed of power is one's own stilling.","n":"The four lines are built alike on வித்து (seed). சத்தி in the last line can be the Lord's Shakti or simply strength; read either way, upasantam is put last, as the ground of the others' power.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["upasaantham","gnaanam","veedu","siththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:187"},"t:2507":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2507,"t":["காரியம் ஏழும் கரந்திடும் மாயையுள்","காரணம் ஏழும் கரக்கும் கடுவெளி","காரிய காரண வாதனைப் பற்றறப்","பாரண வும் உப சாந்தப் பரிசிதே."],"p":"","r":"The seven effects hide within maya and the seven causes are hidden in the fierce void; when attachment to the traces left by effect and cause is cut away, that is the nature of upasantam.","n":"Which seven effects and seven causes are meant is not said; tradition reads them as groups of tattvas. வாதனை (vasana) is the trace or tendency that remains after action. கடுவெளி (the fierce void) and பாரணவும் are obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["upasaantham","maayai","thaththuvam","pattru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:187"},"t:2508":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2508,"t":["அன்ன துரியமே ஆத்தும சுத்தியும்","முன்னிய சாக்கிரா தீதத் துறுபுரி","மன்னும் பரங்காட்சி யாவது உடனுற்றுத்","தன்னின் வியாத்தி தனில்உப சாந்தமே."],"p":"","r":"That turiya is the purifying of the soul; the state beyond waking that it reaches, the lasting vision of the Supreme that comes with it, and upasantam lie in the Lord's pervading of oneself.","n":"சாக்கிராதீதம் (beyond waking) and ஆத்தும சுத்தி (purifying of the soul) are Siddhanta terms for the higher states. தன்னின் வியாத்தி may be the Lord's pervading of the self or the self's pervading; the syntax is loose.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["upasaantham","avaththai","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:187"},"t:2509":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2509,"t":["ஆறாதுஅமைந்துஆண வத்தையுள் நீக்குதல்","பேறான தன்னை அறிதல் பின் தீர்சுத்தி","கூறாத சாக்கிரா தீதம் குருபரன்","பேறாம் வியாத்தம் பிறழ்உப சாந்தமே."],"p":"","r":"Removing anava from the states, knowing oneself as the thing to be gained, then complete purity, the unspoken state beyond waking, the guru-Supreme, and the pervasion that is gained: this is upasantam.","n":"The verse lists stages without connecting words. ஆணவம் is the root mala. ஆறாது அமைந்து (settled, without cooling, or without six) and பிறழ் (flashing, or swerving) in the last line are unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["upasaantham","malam","avaththai","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:187"},"t:2510":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2510,"t":["வாய்ந்த உபசாந்த வாதனை உள்ளப் போய்","ஏய்ந்த சிவமாத லின்சிவா னந்தத்துத்","தோய்ந்தறல் மோனச் சுகானுபவத் தோடே","ஆய்ந்துஅதில் தீர்க்கை யானதுஈர் ஐந்துமே."],"p":"","r":"When the traces of upasantam leave the heart, one has become Siva, steeped in Siva's bliss with the joy of silence, and what is settled in it after inquiry is the ten.","n":"ஈர்ஐந்து (two fives) is taken by tradition as the ten stages of Siddhanta practice (seeing, purifying and knowing the tattvas, the self and Siva), but the verse does not name them. Even the trace of stillness is said to go.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["upasaantham","sivam","aanandham","mounam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:187"},"t:2511":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2511,"t":["பரையின் பரவ பரத்துடன் ஏகமாய்த்","திரையின்நின்று ஆகிய தெண்புனல் போலவுற்று","உரையுணர்ந்து ஆரமுது ஒக்க உணர்ந்துளோன்","கரைகண் டானுரை அற்ற கணக்கிலே."],"p":"","r":"Made one with the Supreme beyond Parai, settled like clear water that had been in waves, he who has known the word and fully tasted the rare nectar has seen the shore, in a reckoning beyond words.","n":"The water image carries the chapter: waves are the soul's movement, clear water its stilled state, and the water was never other than the sea. பரையின் பரம் is taken as Siva beyond his Shakti. கரைகண்டான் (one who has seen the shore) is the one who has crossed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saayujjiyam","saththi","amudham","upasaantham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:187"},"t:2512":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2512,"t":["பிறையுள் கிடந்த முயலை எறிவான்","அறைமணி வாள்கொண் டவர்தமைப் போலக்","கறைமணி கண்டனக் காண்குற மாட்டார்","நிறையறி வோம்என்பர் நெஞ்சிலர் தாமே."],"p":"","r":"Like men who take up a ringing, jewelled sword to strike the hare that lies in the crescent moon, the heartless who cannot see the one with the stained, jewelled throat say, 'We know it fully.'","n":"The hare is the mark on the moon, out of reach of any sword; the claim to full knowledge without sight of Siva is as idle. கறைமணி கண்டன் is Siva, his throat dark with the poison he held. நெஞ்சிலர் (without heart) may mean without feeling or without understanding.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pullarivu","sivam","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:188"},"t:2513":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2513,"t":["கருந்தாள் கருடன் விசும்பூடு இறப்பக்","கருந்தாள் கயத்தில் கரும்பாம்பு நீங்க","பெருந்தன்மை பேசுதி நீஒழி நெஞ்சே","அருந்தர அலைகடல் ஆறசென் றாலே."],"p":"","r":"As the black-legged eagle passes through the sky and the black snake leaves the dark-bottomed pool, O heart, give up your talk of your greatness; when you reach the rare, wave-tossed sea it will grow calm.","n":"The eagle's passing that makes the snake leave may stand for the Lord's coming driving off the bonds, or simply for a lesser power giving way to a greater. The last line is obscure; ஆற may mean 'to grow calm' or 'like a river'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nenju","perumai","pullarivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:188"},"t:2514":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2514,"t":["கருதலர் மாளக் கருவாயில் நின்ற","பொருதலைச் செய்வது புல்லறி வாண்மை","மருவலர் செய்கின்ற மாதவம் ஒத்தால்","தருவலர் கேட்ட தனியும்ப ராமே."],"p":"","r":"To stand at the gate of the womb and fight so that one's foes may die is petty knowledge; if it matched the great penance that the unfriendly do, the one who asked the giver would become a lone celestial.","n":"புல்லறிவாண்மை is the Kural's own chapter title, here applied to hostile striving bound to birth (கருவாயில், the womb's gate). The second half is obscure: தருவலர் கேட்ட and தனியும்பர் admit several readings, perhaps of a celestial gift-tree.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pullarivu","thavam","karu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:188"},"t:2515":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2515,"t":["பிணங்கவும் வேண்டாம் பெருநில முற்றும்","இணங்கிஎம் ஈசனே ஈசன்என்று உன்னில்","கணம்பதி னெட்டும் கழலடி காண","வணங்ககெழு நாடி அங்கு அன்புற லாமே."],"p":"","r":"There is no need to quarrel: if the whole wide world agrees in thinking 'our Lord is the Lord', the eighteen hosts seek to bow and see his anklet-bound feet, and there love can be had.","n":"The verse is closest to the title: the quarrel is over whose god is God. கணம் பதினெட்டு are the eighteen classes of celestial beings. The point is that agreement, not dispute, brings even the gods to his feet.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["samayam","thiruvadi","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:188"},"t:2516":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2516,"t":["என்னிலும் என்னுயி ராய இறைவனைப்","பொன்னிலும் மாமணி யாய புனிதனை","மின்னிய எவ்வுய ராய விகிர் தனை","உன்னிலும் உன்னும் உறும்வகை யாலே."],"p":"","r":"If you think on the Lord who is my life more than I am, the pure one who is a great gem more than gold, the changeless one who is the life of all shining lives, he will come in the very way you think of him.","n":"The comparisons (more than myself, more than gold) rank him above what one values most. உன்னும் உறும் வகையாலே says he is reached in the form one contemplates.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","uyir","thiyaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:188"},"t:2517":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2517,"t":["நின்றும் இருந்தும் கிடந்தும் நிமலனை","ஒன்றும் பொருள்கள் உரைப்பல ராகிலும்","வென்றுஐம் புலனும் விரைந்து பிணக்கறுவந்து","ஒன்றாய் உணரும் ஒருவனும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"Though many speak of the things to join with the Pure One, standing, sitting or lying, the one who conquers the five senses, quickly ends dispute and comes to know him as one becomes the one.","n":"நின்றும் இருந்தும் கிடந்தும் may be postures of practice or 'at all times'. The last line can mean that the knower becomes one with him or that such a knower is rare, only one.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","sivam","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:188"},"t:2518":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2518,"t":["நுண்ணறி வாய்உல காய்உலகு ஏழுக்கும்","எண்ணறி வாய்நின்ற எந்தை பிரான்தன்னைப்","பண்அறி வாளனைப் பாவித்த மாந்தரை","விண்அறி வாளர் விரும்புகின் றாரே."],"p":"","r":"Those who meditate on our father and Lord, who stands as subtle knowledge, as the world and as the knowledge that reckons the seven worlds, the knower of melody, are cherished by the knowers of heaven.","n":"பண் அறிவாளன் (the one who knows melody) may point to Siva as lord of music or to his knowing the songs of devotees. விண் அறிவாளர் are the celestials or the wise who know the higher world.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adiyaar","sivam","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:188"},"t:2519":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2519,"t":["விண்ணவ ராலும் அறிவுஅறி யான்தன்னைக்","கண்ணற வுள்ளே கருதிடின் காலையில்","எண்உற வாசமுப் போதும் இயற்றிநீ","பண்ணிடில் தன்மை பராபர னாமே."],"p":"","r":"If you hold within, with eyes closed, the one whom even the celestials cannot know, and do this in the morning, with thought fixed, at all three times, your nature becomes the Parapara.","n":"முப்போதும் is the three junctures of the day, when worship is done. எண் உற வாசம் is obscure: perhaps dwelling with thought fixed, or a reckoning of breaths. The claim is that the contemplator takes the nature of the Supreme.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiyaanam","sivam","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:188"},"t:2520":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2520,"t":["ஒன்றாய் உலகுடன் ஏழும் பரந்தவன்","பின்தான் அருள்செய்த பேரருள் ஆளவன்","கன்றா மனத்தார்தம் கல்வியுள் நல்லவன்","பொன்றாத போது புனைபுக ழானே."],"p":"","r":"He who spread as one with the seven worlds, the lord of great grace who gave grace afterward, good within the learning of those whose minds are unsoured, is adorned with praise in a time that does not perish.","n":"கன்றா மனத்தார் (those of unsoured mind) makes goodness of heart, not learning alone, the place where he is found. பொன்றாத போது may mean 'in the time that does not end' or 'when one does not perish'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","thiruvarul","kalvi","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:188"},"t:2521":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2521,"t":["போற்றியென் றேன்எந்தை பொன்னான சேவடி","ஏற்றியே தென்றும் எறிமணி தான்அகக்","காற்றின் விளக்கது காயம் மயக்குறும்","அற்றலும் கேட்டது மன்றுகண் டேனே."],"p":"","r":"I said 'Praise!' and raised my father's golden red feet on my head for ever; the ringing bell is the lamp of the inner breath, the body falls into a daze, I heard the sound and saw the hall.","n":"The bell (எறிமணி) and the inner breath's lamp are traditionally read as the inner sound and light heard and seen in yoga, and மன்று as the hall of Siva's dance within. அற்றலும் is obscure; it may be a misreading of அரற்றலும் (the outcry).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvadi","praanan","naadham","thirukkoothu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:188"},"t:2522":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2522,"t":["நேடிக்கொண் டென்னுள்ளே நேர்தரு நந்தியை","ஊடுபுக் காரும் உணர்ந்தறி வாரில்லை","கூடுபுக் கேறலுற் றேனவன் கோலங்கண்","மூடிக்கண் டேனுல கேழுங்க்ண் டேனே."],"p":"","r":"None who have entered within truly know Nandi, who comes before me when sought within me; I entered the nest and climbed, closed my eyes and saw his form, and I saw the seven worlds.","n":"கூடு (nest) is commonly the body, and climbing within it the yogic ascent. Seeing the seven worlds with eyes closed claims that the inner vision holds the whole.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","thiyaanam","yaakkai","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:188"},"t:2523":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2523,"t":["ஆன புகழும் அமைந்த தோர் ஞானமுந்","தேனு மிருக்குஞ் சிறுவரை யொன்றுடண்","டூனமொன் றின்றி யுணர்வுசெய் வார்கட்கு","வானகஞ் செய்யு மறவனு மாமே."],"p":"","r":"To those who without flaw contemplate the fame that has come, the settled knowledge, and a little hill where honey is, he becomes the hunter who makes heaven.","n":"The small hill with honey may be an inner place (the head, where nectar is held to flow) and மறவன் (hunter) Siva as the Kirata who hunts and wins the soul. The text of lines 2 and 3 is run together and partly garbled.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","sivam","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:188"},"t:2524":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2524,"t":["மாமதி யாமதி யாய்நின்ற மாதவர்","தூய்மதி யாகுஞ் சுடர்பர மானந்தந்","தாமதி யாகச் சகமுணச் சாந்திபுக்","காமல மற்றார் அமைவுபெற் றாரே."],"p":"","r":"The great ascetics whose mind became the great moon, whose pure mind is the radiant supreme bliss, entered peace that swallows the world and, their malas gone, reached rest.","n":"மாமதி plays on மதி as moon and as mind; the moon is often the cool inner light of yoga. சகம் உண (to swallow the world) is read as the world ceasing to appear. சாந்தி links this verse to the stilling of chapter 187.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","aanandham","malam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:188"},"t:2525":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2525,"t":["பதமுத்தி மூன்றும் பழுதென்று கைவிட்","டிதமுற்ற பாச இருளைத் துரந்து","மதமற் றெனதியான் மாற்றிவிட் டாங்கே","திதமுற் றவர்கள் சிவசித்தர் தாமே."],"p":"","r":"Those who cast off the three graded releases as flawed, drive out the darkness of the bonds that had seemed pleasant, lose their pride, change 'mine' and 'I' right there and stand firm are the Siva-siddhas.","n":"பதமுத்தி மூன்றும் are the three releases short of union (dwelling in his world, nearness, his form). இதம் உற்ற (agreeable) is a sharp touch: the bonds are dark yet pleasant. எனது யான் is the pair 'mine' and 'I'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padhamukthi","paasam","siththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:188"},"t:2526":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2526,"t":["சித்தர் சிவத்தைக் கண்டவர் சீருடன்","சுத்தாசுக் தத்துடன் தோய்ந்துந்தோ யாதவர்","முத்தரம் முத்திக்கு மூலத்தர் மூலத்துச்","சத்தர் சதாசிவத் தன்மையர் தாமே."],"p":"","r":"The siddhas are those who have seen Siva; with excellence they are steeped in the pure and impure yet unsteeped, freed ones rooted at the root of release, powerful in that root, of the nature of Sadasiva.","n":"தோய்ந்தும் தோயாதவர் (steeped yet unsteeped) is the jivanmukta who lives among pure and impure things untouched by them. மூலம் (root) may also point to the muladhara, the root centre of yoga.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["siththi","sivam","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:188"},"t:2527":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2527,"t":["உதிக்கின்ற இந்திரன் அங்கி யமனும்","துதிக்கும் நிருதி வருணன்நல் வாயு","மதிக்கும் குபேரன் வடதிசை யீசன்","நிதித்தெண் டிசையு நிறைந்துநின் றாரே."],"p":"","r":"Indra in the east where the sun rises, Agni, Yama, the praised Nirriti, Varuna, good Vayu, honoured Kubera and Isana of the north stand filling the eight directions.","n":"The list is the standard eight guardians of the quarters, in order round the compass. Placed here, they are traditionally read as presiding over the eight petals of the heart lotus. நிதித்து (keeping guard?) is unclear.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thisai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:189"},"t:2528":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2528,"t":["ஒருங்கிய பூவுமோர் எட்டித ழாகும்","மருங்கிய மாயா புரியத னுள்ளே","சுருங்கிய தண்டின் சுழுனையி னூடே","ஒருங்கிய சோதியை ஒர்ந்தெழும் உய்ந்தே."],"p":"","r":"The gathered flower has eight petals, and close within it is the city of maya; through the sushumna inside the narrowed stalk, perceive the gathered light and rise, and be saved.","n":"The eight-petalled lotus is traditionally placed at the heart; தண்டு (stalk) is the spinal column and சுழுனை the central channel. மாயாபுரி (city of maya) may be the body or the lotus itself.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aadhaaram","naadi","maayai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:189"},"t:2529":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2529,"t":["மொட்டலர் தாமரை மூன்றுள மூன்றினும்","விட்டலர் கின்றனன் சோதி விரிசுடர்","எட்டல ருள்ளே இரண்டலர் உள்ளுறிற்","பட்டலர் கின்றதோர் பண்டங் கனாவே."],"p":"","r":"There are three lotuses in bud, and in all three the light, a spreading flame, opens them; if one enters two petals within the eight, an old thing blooms and is a dream.","n":"The three lotuses may be the three gunas or three centres; the title suggests the gunas. The last line seems to tie entering two of the petals to the dream state, but what பண்டம் (old thing) is cannot be fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aadhaaram","mukkunam","avaththai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:189"},"t:2530":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2530,"t":["ஆறே யருவி யகங்குளம் ஒன்றுண்டு","நூறே சிவகதி நுண்ணிது வண்ணமும்","கூறே குவிமுலைக் கொண்பனை யாளொடும்","வேறே யிருக்கும் விழுபொருள் தானே."],"p":"","r":"There is a river, and a tank within; Siva's way is a hundred, its colour subtle; the precious Thing stays apart, with her of full breasts as his share.","n":"The river and inner tank are read as the flow of nectar to a reservoir in the head, as in 2535. நூறே (a hundred) and கொண்பனையாள் are obscure. கூறு (share) recalls Shakti as half of Siva's body, yet he is said to stay apart (வேறே).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","saththi","amudham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:189"},"t:2531":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2531,"t":["திகையெட்டும் தேரேட்டும் தேவதை எட்டும்","வகையெட்டு மாய்நின்ற ஆதிப் பிரானை","வகையெட்டு நான்குமற் றாங்கே நிறைந்து","முகையெட்டும் உள்நின் றுதிக்கின்ற வாறே."],"p":"","r":"The first Lord, who stands as the eight directions, the eight chariots, the eight deities and the eight kinds, fills there the eight and the four, and this is how he rises within the eight buds.","n":"The verse multiplies eights to place the Lord in every petal. தேர் எட்டும் (eight chariots) and வகை எட்டு நான்கும் (eight and four, or eight fours) are unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thisai","sivam","aadhaaram"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:189"},"t:2532":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2532,"t":["ஏழுஞ் சகளம் இயம்பு கடந்தெட்டில்","வாழும் பரமென் றதுகடந் தொன்பதில்","ஊழி பராபரம் ஓங்கிய பத்தினில்","தாழ்வது வான தனித்தன்மை தானே."],"p":"","r":"Seven are with form; passing what is spoken, in the eighth lives the Supreme; passing that, in the ninth the Parapara of the ages; in the high tenth, the lone nature that descends as the sky.","n":"சகளம் (with parts, with form) set against higher, formless grades is Siddhanta language, but which seven, and what the eighth to tenth are, the verse does not say. தாழ்வது வான may also be read 'that is lower than the sky' or 'that bows'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:189"},"t:2533":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2533,"t":["பல்லூழி பண்பன் பகலோன் இறையவன்","நல்லூழி ஐந்தினுள் ளேநின்ற வூழிகள்","செல்லூழி அண்டத்துக் சென்றவவ் வூழியுள்","அவ்வூழி யுச்சியு ள்ஒன்றிற் பகவனே"],"p":"","r":"The one of many ages, the sun, the Lord; the ages within the five good ages, the age that passes into the cosmos; in that age, at its summit, in the One, is Bhagavan.","n":"ஊழி (aeon) repeats in every line. The five ages may be cosmic cycles or the five states; the summit of the age may be the end of dissolution or the top of the head. The reading is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","aadhiththan","andam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:189"},"t:2534":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2534,"t":["புரியும் உலகினிற் பூண்டவெட் டானை","திரியுங் களிற்றொடு தேவர் குழாமும்","எரியு மழையும் இயங்கும் வெளியும்","பரியுமா காசத்திற் பற்றது தானே."],"p":"","r":"The eight elephants that bear the world, the roaming tusker with the throngs of gods, fire, rain and the moving air all have their hold in the vast space.","n":"The eight elephants are those of the quarters, and the tusker Indra's Airavata. பரியும் ஆகாசம் may be the vast space or the space that bears; tradition may read it as the inner space where the Lord is.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thisai","andam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:189"},"t:2535":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2535,"t":["ஊறு மருவி யுயர்வரை யுச்சிமேல்","ஆறின்றிப் பாயும் அருங்குளம் ஒன்றுண்டு","சேறின்றிப் பூத்த செழுங்கொடித் தாமரைப்","பூவின்றிச் சூடான் புரிசடை யோனே."],"p":"","r":"On the summit of a high hill where springs rise there is a rare tank that fills without a river; the one with twisted locks wears the rich lotus that bloomed without mud, without a flower.","n":"Each line holds a paradox: a tank with no river, a lotus with no mud, a flower worn with no flower. The hill is commonly the head, the tank the nectar there and the lotus the thousand-petalled one; plainly, the verse says the Lord's things need none of the world's causes.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["amudham","sivam","aadhaaram"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:189"},"t:2536":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2536,"t":["ஒன்றும் இரண்டும் ஒருங்கிய காலத்து","நின்றும் இருந்தும் நிலம்பல பேசினும்","வென்று மிருந்து விகிர்தனை நாடுவர்","சென்றும் இருந்தும் திருவடை யோரே."],"p":"","r":"When one and two have joined, though people stand and sit and talk of many lands, those who conquer and stay seeking the changeless one, going or staying, are the ones who reach the sacred.","n":"ஒன்றும் இரண்டும் (one and two) may be the soul with Siva and Shakti, or the breath and mind, or the two side channels with the central one. திருவடையோர் may be 'those who reach the sacred' or 'those who reach the holy feet'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","adakkam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:189"},"t:2537":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2537,"t":["தொற்பத விசுவன் றைசதன் பிராஞ்ஞன்","நற்பத விராட்டன்பொன் கர்ப்பனவ் யாகிர்தன்","பிற்பதஞ் சொலிதையன் பிரசா பத்தியன்","பொற்புவி சாந்தன் பொருதபி மானியே."],"p":"","r":"Under the first word are Visva, Taijasa and Prajna; under the good word Virat, Hiranyagarbha and Avyakrita; under the last word the one called Ojita(?), Prajapatya and Visanta: these are the presiding selves.","n":"தொற்பதம், நற்பதம், பிற்பதம் are taken as the three words of 'tat tvam asi' (tvam, tat, asi). Visva, Taijasa and Prajna are the Vedantic selves of waking, dream and sleep, Virat, Hiranyagarbha and Avyakrita their cosmic counterparts; the third group's names are garbled and the reading is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","pasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:190"},"t:2538":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2538,"t":["நவமாம் அவத்தை நன்வாதி பற்றிற்","பவமா மலங்குணம் பற்றற்றுப் பற்றாத்","தவமான சத்திய ஞானப் பொதுவிற்","றுவமார் துரியஞ் சொருபம தாமே."],"p":"","r":"When the nine states beginning with waking are let go, unattached to the birth-bound malas and gunas, in the common ground of penance, truth and knowledge the twofold turiya becomes the true form.","n":"பற்றில் may be 'if one holds' or 'when one releases'; the reading follows the second. துவமார் துரியம் (twofold turiya?) and சொருபம் (svarupa, true form) are technical and unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","malam","mukkunam","pattru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:190"},"t:2539":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2539,"t":["சிவமான சிந்தையிற் சீவன் சிதைய","பவமான மும்மலம் பாறிப் பறிய","நவமான அந்தத்தின் நற்சிவ போதந்","தவமான மவையாகித் தானல்ல வாகுமே."],"p":"","r":"In a mind become Siva, the self as jiva breaks up and the three birth-bound malas scatter and are uprooted; at the end of the nine, good Siva-knowledge takes their place, and the self becomes itself and not itself.","n":"சீவன் சிதைய (the jiva breaks up) is the end of the bound self, not of the soul. தான் அல்லவாகுமே may be the soul being neither itself nor other than Siva, the Siddhanta 'not one, not two'. தவமான மவை is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","malam","pasu","avaththai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:190"},"t:2540":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2540,"t":["முன்சொன்ன வொன்பானின் முன்னுறு தத்துவந்","தன்சொல்லில் எண்ணத்தகாவொன்பான் வேறுள","பின்சொல்ல லாகுமிவ் வீரொன்பான் பேர்த்திட்டுத்","தன்செயத வாண்டவன் றான்சிறந் தானே."],"p":"","r":"Before the nine already told there are tattvas, and nine others not to be counted in his word; these two nines can be told later, and setting them aside, the one who rules his own doing excels.","n":"Which two sets of nine are meant is not said. The last line may praise the Lord as sovereign over his acts or the knower who is master of himself.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","avaththai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:190"},"t:2541":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2541,"t":["உகந்த ஒன்பதும் ஐந்தும் உலகம்","பகர்ந்த பிரானென்னும் பண்பினை நாடி","அகந்தெம் பிரானென்பன் அல்லும் பகலும்","இகந்தன வல்வினை யோடறுத் தானே."],"p":"","r":"Seeking the nature of the Lord who spoke the nine and the five and the world, I say 'my Lord' within, night and day, and he cut away the strong karma and removed it.","n":"ஒன்பதும் ஐந்தும் may be the nine states and five states, or fourteen worlds. The plain claim is that constant inward remembrance of the Lord cuts karma.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","vinai","thiyaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:190"},"t:2542":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2542,"t":["நலம்பல காலந் தொகுத்தன நீளங்","குலம்பல வண்ணங் குறிப்பொடுங் கூடும்","பலம்பல பன்னிரு கால நினையும்","நிலம்பல வாறின் நீர்மையன் றானே."],"p":"","r":"Over many long ages he gathered goods; with many clans and colours and marks he joins; with many fruits and twelve times he thinks; with many lands and ways, he is the one whose nature is in them.","n":"The verse is a list of manifolds (பல, many, in every line) of which the Lord is the nature. பன்னிரு காலம் (twelve times) may be the twelve months or twelve breaths; much of the verse cannot be fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","kaalam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:190"},"t:2543":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2543,"t":["ஆதி பராபர மாகும் பராபரை","சோதி பரமுயிர் சொல்லுநற் றத்துவம்","ஓதுங் கமைமாயே யோரிரண் டோ ரமுத்தி","நீதியாம் பேதமொன் பானுடன் ஆதியே."],"p":"","r":"The first Parapara becomes Parapara the Shakti; light, the Supreme, the soul and the good tattva that is spoken, maya and the two, and release: with the nine distinctions in order, he is the first.","n":"The verse seems to list nine grades from Siva and Shakti down through soul and maya. Line 3 (ஓதுங் கமைமாயே யோரிரண் டோ ரமுத்தி) is corrupt, and the count cannot be checked.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","saththi","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:190"},"t:2544":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2544,"t":["தேராத சிந்தை தெளியத் தெளிவித்து","வேறாத நரக சுவர்க்கமும் மேதினி","ஆறாப் பிறப்பும் உயிர்க்கரு ளால்வைத்தான்","வேறாத் தெளியார் வினையுயிர் பெற்றதே."],"p":"","r":"Making the unclear mind clear, he set by grace for souls hell and heaven, not different from the earth, and endless births; those who do not see clearly what is separate are the souls that took on karma.","n":"The verse makes even hell and births the work of grace, set for the soul's clarifying, a Siddhanta claim. வேறாத (not different) and வேறாத் தெளியார் (who do not see as separate) play on one word and admit several readings.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","piravi","naragam","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:190"},"t:2545":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2545,"t":["ஒன்பான் அவத்தையுள் ஒன்பான் அபிமானி","நன்பாற் பயிலு நவதத் துவமாதி","ஒன்பானில் நிற்பதோர் முத்துரி யத்துறச்","செம்பாற் சிவமாதல் சித்தாந்த சித்தியே."],"p":"","r":"In the nine states are the nine presiding selves, with the nine tattvas practised in good measure; to pass beyond the three turiyas that stand in the nine and become Siva in the fair part is the attainment of Siddhanta.","n":"முத்துரியம் (three turiyas) is taken as the turiya of each of the three levels in 2537. செம்பால் (the fair part) may mean rightly, or in the red (Shakti's) part.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","siththaantham","sivam","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:190"},"t:2546":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2546,"t":["நாசி நுனியினின் நான்குமூ விரலிடை","ஈசன் இருப்பிடம் யாரும் அறிகிலர்","பேசி யிருக்கும் பெருமறை யம்மறை","கூசி யிருக்குங் குணமது வாமே."],"p":"","r":"No one knows the Lord's dwelling, between four and three finger-breadths from the tip of the nose; the great Veda that speaks of him shrinks back before it: such is its nature.","n":"The measure from the nose is a yogic placement for meditation; whether it points outward from the nose or inward is not said. கூசியிருக்கும் (shrinks, is abashed) puts the Lord beyond what scripture can say.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","thiyaanam","vedham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:191"},"t:2547":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2547,"t":["கருமங்கள் ஒன்று கருதுங் கருமத்","துரிமையுங் கன்மமும் முன்னும் பிறவிப்","கருவினை யாவது கண்டகன் றன்பின்","புரிவன கன்மக் கயத்துட் புகுமே."],"p":"","r":"Deeds are one; the claim on the deed one intends, the karma, and the seed of earlier births: once one sees these and departs from them, what one does after enters the destruction of karma.","n":"கன்மக் கயம் is taken as karma-kshaya, the wearing out of karma. The first two lines are compressed; they may distinguish the deed, the agency claimed in it, and inherited karma. The claim is that after seeing, action no longer binds.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","piravi","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:191"},"t:2548":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2548,"t":["மாயை மறைக்க மறைந்த மறைப்பொருள்","மாயை மறைய வெளிப்படும் அப்பொருள்","மாயை மறைய மறையவல் லார்கட்குக்","காயமும் இல்லை கருத்தில்லை தானே."],"p":"","r":"The hidden Thing is hidden because maya hides it; when maya is hidden, that Thing appears; for those able to hide maya away there is no body and no thought.","n":"The verse turns on மறை, to hide, and on மறைப்பொருள, which can also mean the Veda's meaning. Hiding the hider reverses the concealment. 'No body, no thought' is taken as freedom from the sense of being the body and mind, not their end.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maayai","meyyunarvu","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:191"},"t:2549":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2549,"t":["மோழை யடைந்து முழைதிறந் துள்புக்குக்","கோழை யடைகின்ற தண்ணற் குறிப்பினில்","ஆழ அடைந்தங் கனலிற் புறஞ்செய்து","தாழ அடைப்பது தன்வலி யாமே."],"p":"","r":"Closing the blunt one, opening the cave and entering within, at the sign of the cool one where phlegm collects, reaching deep there, putting it outside in the fire and shutting it down is one's own strength.","n":"A technical yogic verse. மோழை, முழை (cave), கோழை (phlegm) and அனல் (fire) are traditionally read of closing the lower opening, opening the passage at the head, the cool region of the palate and the fire of kundalini, but none is fixed by the verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","praanan","kundalini"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:191"},"t:2550":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2550,"t":["காயக் குழப்பனைக் காயநன் னாடனைக்","காயத்தி னுள்ளே கமழ்கின்ற நந்தியைத்","தேயத்து ளேயெங்குந் தேடித் திரிவர்கள்","காயத்துள் நின்ற கருத்தறி யாரே."],"p":"","r":"People wander searching everywhere in the land for the one who stirs the body, the good lord of the body, Nandi who is fragrant within the body; they do not know the meaning that stands within the body.","n":"காயம் (body) comes four times, closing every turn back on the body. கருத்து may be the meaning, the intent, or the Lord himself as the thought within. Sengalvaraya notes a parallel in Appar's Tevaram, 'I searched and found him within me'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","yaakkai","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:191"},"t:2551":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2551,"t":["ஆசூசம் ஆசூசம் என்பார் அறிவிலார்","ஆசூச மாமிடம் ஆரும் அறிகிலார்","ஆசூச மாமிடம் ஆரும் அறிந்தபின்","ஆசூச மானிடம் ஆசூச மாமே."],"p":"","r":"The ignorant cry 'impurity, impurity', but no one knows where impurity lies; once one knows where impurity lies, the place of impurity is itself what is impure.","n":"ஆசூசம் is ritual impurity, as from birth or death. The last line is circular; it is commonly taken to say the impurity is in the mind or in the body's bonds, not in the events people avoid. Another division of the words is possible.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thooimai","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:191"},"t:2552":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2552,"t":["ஆசூச மில்லை அருநிய மத்தருக்கு","ஆசூச மில்லை அரனை அர்ச் சிப்பவர்க்கு","ஆசூச மில்லையாம் அங்கி வளர்ப்போர்க்கு","ஆசூச மில்லை அருமறை ஞானிக்கே."],"p":"","r":"There is no impurity for those who keep strict discipline, none for those who worship Hara, none for those who tend the sacred fire, and none for the knower of the rare Veda.","n":"The four are the disciplined (நியமத்தர்), the worshipper, the fire-ritualist and the jnani. Tirumular does not abolish ritual purity but exempts those whose practice already purifies; the fire rite itself is among them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thooimai","vazhipaadu","aaguthi","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:191"},"t:2553":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2553,"t":["வழிபட்டு நின்று வணங்கு மவர்ககுச்","சுழிபட்டு நின்றதோர் தூய்மை தொடங்கும்","குழிபட்டு நின்றவர் கூடார் குறிகள்","கழிபட் டவர்க்கன்றிக் காணவொண் ணாதே."],"p":"","r":"For those who stand in worship and bow, a purity bound in the whorl begins; those who stand in the pit cannot join it, and its signs cannot be seen except by those who are freed.","n":"சுழி (whorl) may be a yogic place, such as the point between the brows, or the turning of worship itself; குழி (pit) is perhaps the pit of births or of sense. கழிபட்டவர் (those who have passed out) are read as the freed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thooimai","vazhipaadu","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:191"},"t:2554":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2554,"t":["தூய்மணி தூயனல் தூய ஒளிவிடும்","தூய்மணி தூயனல் தூரறி வாரில்லை","தூய்மணி தூயனல் தூரறி வார்கட்குத்","தூய்மணி தூயனல் தூயவு மாமே."],"p":"","r":"The pure gem and the pure fire send out pure light; none know the root of the pure gem and pure fire; for those who know the root, the pure gem and pure fire become pure indeed.","n":"The gem and fire may be the inner light of yoga, or Siva and Shakti, or the soul and the Lord. தூர் (root, base) is a guess for தூரறிவார்; it could also be தூய் அறிவார், 'those who know purely'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thooimai","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:191"},"t:2555":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2555,"t":["தூயது வாளா வைத்தது தூநெறி","தூயது வாளா நாதன் திருநாமம்","தூயது வாளா அட்டமா சித்தியும்","தூயது வாளா தூயடிச் சொல்லே."],"p":"","r":"What is pure is the pure way he set freely, the Lord's holy name, the eight great attainments, and the word of the pure feet.","n":"வாளா (freely, without cause) recurs in every line, which the reading takes as 'what is pure of itself'. அட்டமா சித்தி are the eight great powers of yoga. தூயடிச் சொல் may be the word of the Lord's feet or the pure word of scripture.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thooimai","mandhiram","siththi","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:191"},"t:2556":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2556,"t":["பொருளது வாய்நின்ற புண்ணியன் எந்தை","அருளது போற்றும் அடியவ ரன்றிச்","சுருளது வாய்நின்ற துன்பச் சுழியின்","மருளது வாச்சிந்தை மயங்குகின் றாரே."],"p":"","r":"Apart from the devotees who praise the grace of our father, the holy one who stands as the true Thing, others' minds are bewildered by delusion in the coiled whirl of suffering.","n":"The four lines rhyme on பொருள், அருள், சுருள், மருள் (the Real, grace, coil, delusion), setting the Real and grace against the coil and delusion of the rest.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adiyaar","thiruvarul","idumbai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:191"},"t:2557":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2557,"t":["வினையா மசத்து விளைவ துணரார்","வினைஞானந் தன்னில் வீடலுந் தேரார்","வினைவிட வீடென்னும் வேதமும் ஓதார்","வினையாளார் மிக்க விளைவறி யாரே."],"p":"","r":"They do not know what grows from karma, which is unreal; they do not see release through the knowledge of karma; they do not recite the Veda that says release is when karma leaves; the servants of karma do not know its great fruit.","n":"அசத்து (asat, the unreal) is the Siddhanta term for what does not endure. வினைஞானம் may be knowledge of karma's working or a knowledge that is itself action; the line is unclear. வினை opens every line.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","veedu","gnaanam","vedham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:191"},"t:2558":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2558,"t":["பரகதி யுண்டென இல்லையென் போர்கள்","நரகதி செல்வது ஞாலம் அறியும்","இரகதி செய்திடு வார்கடை தோறும்","துரகதி யுண்ணத் தொடங்குவர் தாமே."],"p":"","r":"The world knows that those who say there is no higher state go the way of hell; going from door to door, they begin to taste an evil end.","n":"பரகதி (the higher state) is set against நரகதி (the way to hell). Line 1 can be read of those who say 'there is' and those who say 'there is not', that is of all who argue over it, or only of the deniers. இரகதி and துரகதி are obscure: இரகதி is taken here with இரத்தல் (begging), துரகதி as துர்கதி (an evil end).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["veedu","nindhai","naragam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:192"},"t:2559":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2559,"t":["கூடகில் லார்குரு வைத்த குறிகண்டு","நாடகில் லார்நயம் பேசித் திரிவர்கள்","பாடகில் லாரவன் செய்த பரிசறிந்","தாடவல் லாரவர் பேறெது வாமே."],"p":"","r":"Those who cannot join the mark the guru set, and do not seek it, wander about talking fine words; they cannot sing of what he gave, so what gain can they have beside those who know it and can dance?","n":"குறி (the mark, the sign) is what the guru points to, taken as the goal of practice. The last two lines are compressed: they may contrast the talkers with those who know the gift and dance, or ask what gain either has.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","nindhai","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:192"},"t:2560":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2560,"t":["புறப்பட்டுப் போகும் புகுதுமென் னெஞ்சில்","திறப்பட்ட சிந்தையைப் தெய்வமென் றெண்ணி","அறப்பட்ட மற்றப் பதியென் றழைத்தேன்","இறப்பற்றி னேன்இங் கிதென்னென்கின் றானே"],"p":"","r":"Something goes out and comes into my heart; taking the opened mind for God, I called it that other Lord beyond all, and 'I am free of death; what is this here?' he says.","n":"In this chapter the verse reads most naturally as the self-deluded seeker who mistakes his own mind (சிந்தை) for God and declares himself deathless, and the speaker's 'I' as dramatic. அறப்பட்ட may mean 'beyond dharma' or 'cut off'; the voice of the last line is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["manam","nindhai","pathi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:192"},"t:2561":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2561,"t":["திடரடை நில்லாத நீர்போல் ஆங்கே","உடலிடை நில்லா உறுபொருள் காட்டிக்","கடலிடை நில்லா கலஞ்சேரு மாபோல்","அடலிடை வண்ணனும் அங்குநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"As water does not stay on raised ground and a ship does not stay at sea but comes to harbour, the mighty Lord, showing the real thing that does not stay in the body, stood there.","n":"Both images are of things that move on to where they belong. உறுபொருள் (the real thing) that does not stay in the body may be the soul or the truth, and அடலிடை வண்ணன் (the one of mighty form) is taken as Siva.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","yaakkai","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:192"},"t:2562":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2562,"t":["தாமரை நூல்போல் தடுப்பார் பரந்தொடும்","போம்வழி வேண்டிப் புறமே யுழிதர்வர்","காண்வழி காட்டக்கண் காணாக் கலதிகள்","தீநெறி செல்வான் திரிகின்ற வாறே."],"p":"","r":"Their hindrance is as weak as lotus thread, yet it spreads; wanting a way out they wander outside, and the wretched, shown the way to see, cannot see, and so wander on the evil path.","n":"தாமரை நூல்போல் தடுப்பார் may mean those who would hold back the Supreme with a lotus fibre, a futile effort, or a bond as fine as lotus fibre that still holds them. கலதி (the wretched) and காணாக் (unseeing) are plain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai","nindhai","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:192"},"t:2563":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2563,"t":["மூடுதல் இன்றி முடியும் மனிதர்கள்","கூடுவர் நந்தி யவனைக் குறித்துடன்","காடும் மலையுங் கழனி கடந்தோறும்","ஊடும் உருவினை யுன்னிகி லாரே."],"p":"","r":"People who reach their end without concealment join Nandi by fixing on him; the others cannot conceive the form that runs through forest, hill, field and every place.","n":"மூடுதல் இன்றி (without covering) is taken as without the veil of ignorance; the contrast in the last two lines is supplied, since the verse does not name the second group.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","veedu","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:192"},"t:2564":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2564,"t":["ஆவது தெற்கும் வடக்கும் அமரர்கள்","போவார் குடக்கும் குணக்கும் குறுவழி","நாவினின் மந்திர மென்று நடுவங்கி","வேவது செய்து விளங்கிடு வீரே."],"p":"","r":"While the gods go south and north, west and east, the short way is to hold the mantra on the tongue and burn the fire in the centre, and so you will shine.","n":"குறுவழி (the short way) is set against the gods' journeys in all directions. நடு அங்கி (the fire in the middle) is traditionally taken as the inner fire of yoga in the central channel; it may also be the fire of a rite.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","yogam","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:192"},"t:2565":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2565,"t":["மயக்குற நோக்கினும் மாதவஞ் செய்யார்","தமக்குறப் பேசின தாரணை கொள்ளார்","சினக்குறப் பேசின தீவினை யாளர்","தமக்குற வல்லினை தாங்கிநின் றாரே."],"p":"","r":"Though they look on in bewilderment they do no great tapas, and they do not hold to the concentration taught for their good; the evildoers who speak to provoke anger stood bearing heavy karma for themselves.","n":"தாரணை (dharana, fixing the mind) is the sixth limb of yoga. The verse turns the scorners' words back on them: what they say in anger becomes their own load of deeds.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","vinai","nindhai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:192"},"t:2566":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2566,"t":["விட்ட விலக்கணைதான்போம் வியோமத்துத்","தொட்டு விடாத துபசாந்தத் தேதொகும்","விட்டு விடாதது மேவுஞ்சத் தாதியிற்","சுட்டு மிலக்கணா தீதஞ் சொருபமே."],"p":"","r":"Implication that leaves the literal sense goes into the void; implication that touches it without leaving gathers in the stilled state; implication that both leaves and keeps reaches as far as sat and the rest; what is pointed to beyond implication is the true form.","n":"விட்ட இலக்கணை, விடாத இலக்கணை and விட்டுவிடாத இலக்கணை are taken as the three lakshanas of Vedantic exegesis (jahat, ajahat and jahad-ajahat). வியோமம் (space, void), உபசாந்தம் (the stilled state) and சத்தாதி are taken as stages of the soul's ascent; where each mode is placed is the verse's own scheme and is not clear to me. இலக்கணாதீதம் சொரூபம் is the true form beyond them all.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ilakkanai","avaththai","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:193"},"t:2567":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2567,"t":["வில்லின் விசைநாணிற்கோத்திலக்கெய்தபின்","கொல்லுங் களிறைந்துங் கோலொடு சாய்ந்தன","வில்லு ளிருந்தெறி கூரும் ஒருவற்குக்","கல்கலன் என்னக் கதிரெதி யாமே."],"p":"","r":"When the arrow, fitted to the string and driven by the bow, struck its mark, the five killing elephants fell with the shaft; for the one who sits in the bow and shoots keenly, the light rises to meet him like a jewel set in stone.","n":"The five elephants are traditionally the five senses, and the arrow the aimed mind or the mantra; the reading is symbolic throughout. கல்கலன் என்னக் கதிர் எதிர் ஆமே is obscure: கல்கலன் may be a jewel of stone or a stone vessel. How the verse bears on the three implications is not stated.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","yogam","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:193"},"t:2568":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2568,"t":["சீவ துரியத்துத் தொம்பதஞ் சீவனார்","தாவு பரதுரி யத்தனில் தற்பதம்","மேவு சிவதுரி யத்தசி மெய்ப்பத","மோவி விடும் தத் துவமசி உண்மையே."],"p":"","r":"The word 'you' is the soul in the soul's turiya, the word 'that' is in the supreme turiya to which it leaps, and the true word 'are' is in Siva's turiya; the truth of 'that you are' is that these fall away.","n":"தொம்பதம், தற்பதம் and அசிபதம் are the three words of tat tvam asi (tvam, tat, asi). சீவதுரியம், பரதுரியம் and சிவதுரியம் are the three turiyas of Tirumular's scheme. ஓவி விடும் (cease, fall away) is read as the three distinctions dissolving into one.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thonthaththasi","avaththai","pasu","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:194"},"t:2569":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2569,"t":["ஆறா றகன்ற அணுத்தொம் பதஞ்சுத்தம்","ஈறான தற்பதம் எய்துப சாந்தத்துப்","பேறா கியசீவன் நீங்கிப்பிர சாதத்து","வீறான தொந்தத் தசிதத்வ மசியே."],"p":"","r":"The soul beyond the thirty-six tattvas is the pure 'you'; 'that' is the end it reaches in the stilled state; when the soul that has gained this departs, in grace the glorious union of 'you', 'that' and 'are' is 'that you are'.","n":"ஆறாறு (six sixes) is taken as the thirty-six tattvas and அணு as the soul. உபசாந்தம் and பிரசாதம் (grace) are technical stages; the order of the steps is compressed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thonthaththasi","thaththuvam","pasu","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:194"},"t:2570":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2570,"t":["ஆகிய வச்சோயம் தேவகத் தன்னிடத்து","ஆகிய விட்டு விடாத விலக்கணைத்து","ஆருப சாந்தமே தொந்தத் தசியென்ப","ஆகிய சீவன் பரன்சிவ னாமே."],"p":"","r":"As in 'this is that Devadatta', the implication that both leaves and keeps applies; they call the stilled state 'you, that, are', and the soul so reached becomes the Supreme, Siva.","n":"அச்சோயம் தேவகத்தன் is read as sa ayam devadattah, the Vedantic example: the man seen now is the man seen before, so 'this' and 'that' keep the person and drop the time and place. The same mode is applied to 'that you are'. The reading of தேவகத்தன் as Devadatta is an inference.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thonthaththasi","vedaantham","pasu","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:194"},"t:2571":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2571,"t":["துவந்தத் தசியே தொந்தத் தசியும்","அவைமன்னா வந்து வயத்தேகமான","தவமுறு தத்துவ மசிவே தாந்த","சிவமா மதுஞ்சித் தாந்தவே தாந்தமே."],"p":"","r":"'You, that, are' becoming one is the 'that you are' of Vedanta, reached by tapas; it is Siva, and that is Siddhanta-Vedanta.","n":"The last line joins சித்தாந்தம் and வேதாந்தம் into one word, a claim that the two conclusions meet. Line 2 (அவைமன்னா வந்து வயத்தேகமான) is obscure; the reading of துவந்தத்தசி and தொந்தத்தசி as forms of 'you-that-are' is the usual one.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thonthaththasi","vedaantham","siththaantham","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:194"},"t:2572":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2572,"t":["துரியம் அடங்கிய சொல்லறும் பாழை","அரிய பரமென்ப ராகாரி தன்றென்னார்","உரிய பரம்பர மாமொன் றுதிக்கும்","அருநிலம் என்பதை யாரறி வாரே."],"p":"","r":"They call the wordless void in which turiya subsides the rare Supreme, and do not deny it form; but who knows the rare ground where the one, the highest of the high, arises?","n":"பாழ் (void, waste) for the highest state is a recurring term in Tirumular. ஆகாரி தன்று என்னார் may mean 'they do not say it is formless' or the reverse; the verse may be correcting those who stop at the void.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","avaththai","mounam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:194"},"t:2573":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2573,"t":["தொம்பதந் தற்பதஞ் சொல்லும் அசிபதம்","நம்பிய முத்துரி யத்துமே னாடவே","யும்பத மும்பத மாகும் உயிர்பரன்","செம்பொரு ளான சிவமென லாமே."],"p":"","r":"If one seeks the words 'you', 'that' and 'are' above the three trusted turiyas, that state becomes the state where soul and Supreme can be called Siva, the true thing.","n":"முத்துரியம் recalls the three turiyas of 2568. உம்பதம் உம்பதம் ஆகும் is obscure; it may mean 'that state becomes your state'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thonthaththasi","avaththai","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:194"},"t:2574":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2574,"t":["வைத்த துரிய மதிற்சொரு பானந்தத்","துய்த்த பிரணவ மாமுப தேசத்தை","மெய்த்த விதயத்து விட்டிடு மெய்யுணர்","வைத்த படியே யடைந்து நின்றானே."],"p":"","r":"In the turiya so placed, in the bliss of one's own form, he took the teaching of Om he had received, released it in the true heart, and stood having reached true awareness just as it was given.","n":"சொரூபானந்தம் (bliss of one's own form) and பிரணவ உபதேசம் (the teaching of Om) are technical; who 'he' is, the disciple or the Lord, is not stated.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piranavam","avaththai","aanandham","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:194"},"t:2575":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2575,"t":["நனவாதி ஐந்தையும் நாதாதியில் வைத்துப்","பினமா மலத்தைப் பின்வைத்துப் பின்சுத்தத்","தனதாஞ் சிவகதி சத்தாதி சாந்தி","மனவா சகங்கெட்ட மன்னனை நாடே."],"p":"","r":"Place the five states beginning with waking in nada and the rest, put the corpse-like mala behind, and then seek the king who is beyond mind and speech, the pure Siva-state of being and peace.","n":"நனவாதி ஐந்து are waking, dream, deep sleep, turiya and the beyond. பிணமா மலம் (mala like a corpse) is taken as anava, inert and binding. சத்தாதி சாந்தி may be a list of states (being, and the rest, peace) rather than one phrase.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","malam","naadham","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:194"},"t:2576":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2576,"t":["பூரணி யாது புறம்பொன்றி லாமையின்","பேரணி யாதது பேச்சொன்றி லாமையின்","ஓரணை யாததுவொன்றுமி லாமையிற்","காரண மின்றியே காட்டுந் தகைமைத்தே."],"p":"","r":"It is not filled up, since nothing is outside it; it takes no name, since no speech reaches it; it joins nothing, since there is nothing else; without any cause it shows itself, such is its nature.","n":"The verse is built as three negations each with its reason (இலாமையின்), then a positive. பூரணியாது and பேரணியாது are uncertain forms; 'not filled' and 'not named' are guesses from the reasons given.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","mounam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:194"},"t:2577":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2577,"t":["நீயது வானா யெனநின்ற பேருரை","ஆயது நானானேன் என்னச் சமைந்தறச்","சேய சிவமாக்குஞ் சீர்நந்தி பேரருள்","ஆயது வாயனந் தானந்தி யாகுமே."],"p":"","r":"The great saying that stood as 'you are that', once it ripens into 'I have become that', is the great grace of glorious Nandi that makes one Siva; then bliss itself becomes Nandi.","n":"The verse turns the second-person saying (நீ அது ஆனாய்) into a first-person realization (அது நான் ஆனேன்) and credits the change to Nandi's grace, not to understanding alone. The last line is uncertain: அனந்தான் may be ஆனந்தம் or அனந்தன் (the endless).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thonthaththasi","thiruvarul","sivam","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:194"},"t:2578":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2578,"t":["உயிர்பர மாக உயர்பர சீவன்","அரிய சிவமாக அச்சிவ வேதத்து","இரியிலுஞ் சீராம் பராபரன் என்ன","உரிய உரையற்ற வோமய மாமே."],"p":"","r":"The soul becoming the Supreme, the high supreme soul becoming rare Siva, as the Siva-Veda calls him the highest of the high, is the form of Om beyond any fitting word.","n":"இரியிலும் in line 3 is obscure. ஓமயம் is taken as 'made of Om'; it may also be read as 'Om-pervaded'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pasu","sivam","piranavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:194"},"t:2579":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2579,"t":["வாய்நாசி யேபுரு மத்தகம் உச்சியில்","ஆய்நாசி யுச்சி முதலவை யாய்நிற்கும்","தாய்நாடி யாதிவாக் காதி சகலாதி","சேய்நா டொளியெனச் சிவகதி யைந்துமே."],"p":"","r":"Mouth, nose, the crown of the head and the top: they stand as the first places; nadi and the rest, speech and the rest, sakala and the rest, and the far-reaching light are the five Siva-states.","n":"The verse lists places in the head and categories (நாடி, வாக்கு, சகலம்) in the compressed manner of the yogic chapters; the five Siva-states (சிவகதி ஐந்து) are not named, and I can't fix how the lists map onto each other.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naadi","sivam","avaththai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:194"},"t:2580":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2580,"t":["அறிவறி யாமை இரண்டும் அகற்றிப்","செறிவறி வாய்எங்கும் நின்ற சிவனைப்","பிறிவறி யாது பிரானென்று பேணுங்","குறியறி யாதவர் கொள்ளறி யாரே."],"p":"","r":"Those who do not know the sign of putting away both knowing and not-knowing, and tending as Lord, without knowing separation, the Siva who stands everywhere as dense knowledge, cannot grasp him.","n":"அறிவு அறியாமை இரண்டும் அகற்றி: both the soul's limited knowledge and its ignorance are to be set aside, since Siva is செறிவு அறிவு, knowledge itself. பிறிவு அறியாது (not knowing separation) is the condition of worship here. Sengalvaraya marks a parallel for line 1.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","gnaanam","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:194"},"t:2581":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2581,"t":["அறிவார் அறிவன அப்பும் அனலும்","அறிவார் அறிவன அப்புங் கலப்பும்","அறிவான் இருந்தங் கறிவிக்கி னல்லால்","அறிவான் அறிந்த அறிவறி யோமே."],"p":"","r":"The knowers know water and fire, and know water and its mixing; but unless the Knower who is there makes us know, we do not know the knowledge the Knower knows.","n":"The repeated அறிவு makes the point by sound: the soul can know the elements, but knowledge of the Lord comes only as he makes it known (அறிவிக்கின்). This is the Siddhanta claim that the soul knows only with the Lord's help, as tradition takes it. அப்புங் கலப்பும் is unclear.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","sivam","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:194"},"t:2582":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2582,"t":["அதீதத்துள் ளாகி அகன்றவன் நந்தி","அதீதத்துள் ளாகி அறிவிலோன் ஆன்மா","மதிபெற் றுருள்விட்ட மன்னுயி ரொன்றாம்","பதியிற் பதியும் பரவுயிர் தானே."],"p":"","r":"Nandi is within the beyond-state and yet transcends it; the soul within the beyond-state is without knowledge; the abiding soul that gains understanding and leaves its rolling becomes one, the supreme soul settling into the Lord.","n":"அதீதம் is the state beyond turiya. The contrast is between the Lord, who is in it and beyond it, and the soul, which in it is knowledgeless until it gains மதி. உருள் (rolling) is taken as the round of births. பதியிற் பதியும் plays on பதி as Lord and as 'settle'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pasu","pathi","avaththai","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:194"},"t:2583":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2583,"t":["அடிதொழ முன்னின் றமரர்க ளத்தன்","முடிதொழ ஈசனும் முன்னின் றருளிப்","படிதொழ நீபண்டு பாவித்த தெல்லாங்","கடிதொழ காணன்னுங் கண்ணுத லானே."],"p":"","r":"As the gods stood before him to worship his feet, the Lord stood before me in grace so that I could worship his crown, and the brow-eyed one said: see now all that you once imagined in worship on earth.","n":"The contrast of feet for the gods and crown for the speaker is a reading; பண்டு பாவித்தது (what you imagined before) suggests that earlier worship in imagination is now fulfilled in sight. கடிதொழ காணன்னும் is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","sivam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:194"},"t:2584":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2584,"t":["நின்மல மேனி நிமலன் பிறப்பிலி","என்னுளம் வந்திவன் என்னடி யானென்று","பொன்வளர் மேனி புகழ்கின்ற வானவன்","நின்மல மாகென்று நீக்கவல் லானே."],"p":"","r":"The spotless one with a spotless body, the unborn, came into my heart and said 'this one is my servant'; the gold-bodied one whom the gods praise could take away my impurity, saying 'be spotless'.","n":"நின்மலம் opens the verse and returns in the Lord's command, so the Lord's purity becomes the speaker's. The Lord claims the speaker as his servant (அடியான்) before he cleanses him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","malam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:194"},"t:2585":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2585,"t":["துறந்துபுக் கொள்ளொளி சோதியைக் கண்டு","பறந்ததென் உள்ளம் பணிந்து கிடந்தே","மறந்தறி யாவென்னை வான்வர் கோனும்","இறந்து பிறவாமல் ஈங்குவைத் தானே."],"p":"","r":"Renouncing, I entered and saw the light that holds the flame; my heart flew up and lay prostrate, and the king of the heavens, whom I had never forgotten, set me here so that I would not die and be born again.","n":"பறந்தது என் உள்ளம் பணிந்து கிடந்தே joins rapture and surrender in one line. இறந்து பிறவாமல் ஈங்கு வைத்தான் places release here, in this life, which suits the chapter's claim that the soul becomes Siva.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","piravi","thuravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:194"},"t:2586":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2586,"t":["மெய்வாய் கண்மூக்குச் செவியென்னும் மெய்த் தோற்றத்","தவ்வாய அந்தக் கரணம் அகில்மும்","எவ்வா யியுரும் இறையாட்ட ஆடலாற்","கைவா யிலாநிறை எங்குமெய் கண்டதே."],"p":"","r":"Body, mouth, eyes, nose and ears, the inner instruments and all else: since every soul dances as the Lord makes it dance, the fullness without hands or mouth is seen everywhere as truth.","n":"இறை ஆட்ட ஆடலால் (dancing as the Lord makes it dance) makes all the soul's action his. Sengalvaraya notes the parallel in Appar's Thevaram, ஆட்டுவித்தால் ஆரொருவர் ஆடாதாரே. கைவாயிலா நிறை (fullness with no hands or mouth) is the Lord who acts through all without organs of his own.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","uyir","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:194"},"t:2587":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2587,"t":["அழிகின்ற சாயா புருடனைப் போலக்","கழிகின்ற நீரிற் குமிழியைக் காணில்","எழுகின்ற தீயிற்கர்ப் பூரத்தை யொக்கப்","பொழிகின்ற இவ்வுடற் போமப் பரத்தே."],"p":"","r":"Like the shadow-man who vanishes, like a bubble on flowing water, like camphor in a rising flame, this streaming body goes into that Supreme.","n":"The three images are of disappearance without remainder: camphor in fire leaves no ash. சாயாபுருடன் (shadow-man) is taken as one's shadow, or as the shadow-figure some yogic texts describe; the plain sense holds either way. The body's ending is framed as merging into the Supreme, not as mere loss.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","nilaiyaamai","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:195"},"t:2588":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2588,"t":["உடலும் உயிரும் ஒழிவற ஒன்றிற்","படருஞ் சிவசத்தி தாமே பரமாம்","உடலைவிட் டிந்த உயிரெங்கு மாகிக்","கடையுந் தலையுங் கரக்குஞ் சிவத்தே."],"p":"","r":"When body and soul become wholly one, Siva-Shakti spreading through them is itself the Supreme; the soul, leaving the body, becomes everywhere, and end and beginning vanish in Siva.","n":"ஒழிவற ஒன்றில் (become one without remainder) may describe the union of body and soul in the yogi or their joint absorption. கடையுந் தலையும் (end and head) may be the ends of time or the soul's lowest and highest states.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","sivam","uyir","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:195"},"t:2589":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2589,"t":["செவிமெய்வாய் கண்மூக்குச் சேரிந் திரியம்","அவியின் றியமன மாதிகள் ஐந்துங்","குவிவொன் றிலாமல் விரிந்து குவிந்து","தவிர்வொன் றிலாத சராசரந் தானே."],"p":"","r":"The senses joined in ear, body, mouth, eyes and nose, and the five beginning with mind, unextinguished, without contracting, expanding and contracting, are themselves the moving and unmoving world without end.","n":"The verse seems to say that the soul's senses and inner faculties, in the swallowed state, are no longer its own but the whole world (சராசரம்). மனம் ஆதிகள் ஐந்து is unusual; the inner instruments are usually counted four. The reading is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","manam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:195"},"t:2590":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2590,"t":["பரனெங்கு மாரப் பரந்துற்று நிற்கும்","திரனெங்கு மாகிச் செறிவெங்கு மெய்தும்","உரனெங்கு மாயுல குண்டு உமிழ்க்கும்","வரமிங்ஙன் கண்டியான் வாழ்ந்துற்ற வாறே."],"p":"","r":"The Supreme stands spread fully everywhere, strong everywhere and dense everywhere; mighty everywhere, he eats the world and spits it out; this is how I saw the gift and came to live.","n":"உலகு உண்டு உமிழ்க்கும் (eats and spits out the world) gives dissolution and emanation as one act, the chapter's swallowing. The last line turns to the speaker's experience.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","aindhozhil","andam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:195"},"t:2591":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2591,"t":["அளந்து துரியத் தறிவினை வாங்கி","உளங்கொள் பரஞ்சகம் உண்ட தொழித்துக்","கிளர்ந்த பரஞ்சிவஞ்சேரக் கிடைத்தால்","விளங்கிய வெட்ட வெளியனு மாமே."],"p":"","r":"Measuring and drawing in the knowledge that is in turiya, discarding the world the mind had taken in, if one reaches the risen Supreme Siva, one becomes the open space itself.","n":"வெட்டவெளி (open space) is a term for the formless Supreme. பரஞ்சகம் உண்டது may mean 'what the world has eaten' or 'the world eaten by the mind'; the sense of discarding the world is clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","sivam","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:195"},"t:2592":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2592,"t":["இரும்பிடை நீரென என்னையுள் வாங்கிப்","பரம்பர மான பரமது விட்டே","உரம்பெற முப்பாழ் ஒளியை விழுங்கி","இருந்தஎன் நந்தி இதயத்து ளானே."],"p":"","r":"Drawing me in as heated iron draws in water, going beyond even the highest Supreme, swallowing the light of the three voids to gain strength, my Nandi stayed in my heart.","n":"இரும்பிடை நீர் (water on hot iron) is a classic image of total absorption: the water vanishes into the iron. முப்பாழ் (the three voids) is a technical term, traditionally the voids of maya, of the soul and of the Supreme, but the verse does not say.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","thiruvarul","pasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:195"},"t:2593":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2593,"t":["கரியுண் விளவின் கனிபோல் உயிரும்","உரிய பரமுமுன் னோதுஞ் சிவமும்","அரிய துரியமேல் அகிலமும் எல்லாம்","திரிய விழுங்குஞ் சிவபெரு மானே."],"p":"","r":"Like the wood-apple fruit eaten by the elephant, Lord Siva swallows, so that they spin, the soul, the Supreme, the Siva spoken of before, the rare turiya and the world above, all of it.","n":"கரியுண் விளவின் கனி is the wood-apple that keeps its shell while its inside is gone, said to happen when an elephant swallows it whole or when a blight called கரி strikes it. Sengalvaraya notes the image in Civaka Cintamani 1122 and Tiruppugazh 57. The point is that all remains in outward form while its substance is taken into Siva. திரிய (whirling) is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","avaththai","andam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:195"},"t:2594":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2594,"t":["அந்தமும் ஆதியும் ஆகும் பராபரன்","தந்தம் பரம்பரன் தன்னிற் பரமுடன்","நந்தமை யுண்டுமெய்ஞ் ஞானநே யாந்தத்தே","நந்தி யிருந்தனன் நாமறி யோமே."],"p":"","r":"The highest of the high, who is end and beginning, the supreme beyond the supreme in himself, with the Supreme, swallowed us, and Nandi stayed at the end of what is known in true knowledge; we do not know it.","n":"நேயாந்தம் is taken as jneya-anta, the end of the knowable. நாம் அறியோமே (we do not know) may mean that the swallowed self no longer knows as a separate knower, or simply that the state is beyond knowing.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:195"},"t:2595":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2595,"t":["அற்ற துரைக்கில் அருளுப தேசங்கள்","குற்ற மறுத்தபொன் போலுங் கனலிடை","அற்றற வைத்திறை மாற்றற ஆற்றிடில்","செற்றம் அறுத்த செழுஞ்சுட ராகுமே."],"p":"","r":"Put plainly, the teachings of grace are like gold refined of its flaw in fire: if one holds to them until all is cut away, without the least loss of fineness, one becomes a bright flame with enmity cut off.","n":"மாற்று is the fineness of gold as tested on the touchstone, so இறை மாற்று அற is read as 'without the slightest loss of fineness'; it might instead mean 'with the Lord's change' or 'with no exchange'. அற்றது உரைக்கில் may mean 'if one speaks of what is detached'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","guru","thooimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:196"},"t:2596":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2596,"t":["எல்லாம் அறியும் அறிவு தனைவிட்டு","எல்லாம் அறிந்தும் இலாபமங் கில்லை","எல்லாம் அறிந்த அறிவினை நானென்னில்","எல்லாம் அறிந்த இறையென லாமே."],"p":"","r":"Leaving aside the knowledge that knows everything, even knowing everything there is no gain; if I say that the knowledge that knows everything is 'I', it can be called the all-knowing Lord.","n":"எல்லாம் opens every line. Lines 1 and 2 are clear: all knowledge apart from the all-knowing is fruitless. Lines 3 and 4 may say that one who takes that knowledge as oneself is one with the Lord, or warn against claiming it; I read the first, in keeping with the neighbouring chapters on 'that you are'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arivu","sivam","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:196"},"t:2597":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2597,"t":["தலைநின்ற தாழ்வரை மீது தவஞ்செய்து","முலைநின்ற மாதறி மூர்த்தியை யானும்","புலைநின்ற பொல்லாப் பிறவி கடந்து","கலைநின்ற கள்வனை கண்டுகொண் டேனே."],"p":"","r":"Doing tapas on the high mountain, I too passed beyond the base, wretched cycle of birth and found the form known to the goddess, the thief who stands in the kalas.","n":"முலைநின்ற மாது is taken as Uma, and கலைநின்ற கள்வன் (the thief who stands in the kalas) as Siva, hidden in the phases of the moon or of the breath, or in the arts. கள்வன் for the Lord who steals the heart is an old devotional usage.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","piravi","sivam","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:196"},"t:2598":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2598,"t":["தானே யுலகில் தலைவ னெனத்தகும்","தானே யுலகுக்கோர் தத்துவ மாய்நிற்கும்","வானே மழைபொழி மாமறை கூர்ந்திடும்","ஊனே யுருகிய வுள்ளமொன் றாமே."],"p":"","r":"He alone is fit to be called lord in the world; he alone stands as the principle of the world; as the sky pours rain, the great Veda abounds; and the heart whose flesh has melted becomes one with him.","n":"Line 3 is compressed: வானே மழைபொழி மாமறை கூர்ந்திடும் may compare the Lord's gift of the Veda to rain, or name him as sky, rain and Veda. ஊனே உருகிய உள்ளம் (a heart melted to the flesh) is the language of devotional love.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pathi","vedham","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:196"},"t:2599":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2599,"t":["அருள்பெற்ற காரணம் என்கொல் அமரில்","இருளற்ற சிந்தை இறைவனை நாடி","மருளுற்ற சிந்தையை மாற்றி அருமைப்","பொருளுற்ற சேவடி போற்றுவோர் தாமே."],"p":"","r":"What is the cause of their gaining grace? It is that, with minds free of darkness, they seek the Lord, change their deluded minds, and praise the red feet that hold the rare substance.","n":"The verse answers its own question. அமரில் in line 1 is unclear: it may mean 'in desire', 'among the gods' or 'in the struggle'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","thiruvadi","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:196"},"t:2600":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2600,"t":["மெய்கலந் தாரொடு மெய்கலந் தான்தன்னை","பொய்கலந் தார்முன் புகுதா ஒருவனை","உய்கலந் தூழித் தலைவனுமாய் நிற்கும்","மெய்கலந் தின்பம் விளைந்திடும் மெய்யர்க்கே."],"p":"","r":"The one who mingles in truth with those who mingle in truth and does not enter before those mingled with falsehood stands as the saving Lord of the ages, and for the true, joy grows from mingling in truth.","n":"மெய் means truth and also the body, so மெய்கலந்தார் can be those joined in truth or those in whom the Lord has entered bodily; the chapter title favours truth. The Lord's presence follows the person's truthfulness: he goes in where there is truth and not where there is falsehood.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaaimai","iraivan","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:196"},"t:2601":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2601,"t":["மெய்கலந் தாரொடு மெய்கலந் தான்மிகப்","பொய்கலந் தாருட் புகுதாப் புனிதனை","கைகலந் தாவி எழும்பொழு தண்ணலைக்","கைகலந் தார்க்கே கருத்துற லாமே."],"p":"","r":"The pure one who mingles in truth with those who mingle in truth and does not go in among those mingled with falsehood: only those who have joined hands with him can fix that Lord in their thought when the breath rises to leave.","n":"ஆவி எழும்பொழுது (when the breath rises) is taken as the moment of death, when only those already joined to the Lord can hold him in mind. கைகலந்தார் (those who joined hands) may also mean those who joined him in worship with folded hands.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vaaimai","iraivan","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:196"},"t:2602":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2602,"t":["எய்திய காலத் திருபொழு துஞ்சிவன்","மெய்செயின் மேலை விதியது வாய்நிற்கும்","பொய்யும் புலனும் புகலொன்று நீத்திடில்","ஐயனும் அவ்வழி யாகிநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"If, when the time comes, one performs Siva's truth at both times of day, it stands as the higher rule; if one gives up falsehood and the senses as refuge, the Lord himself becomes that way.","n":"இருபொழுதும் (both times) is taken as morning and evening worship. மெய்செயின் may be 'do truth' or 'do Siva's worship with the body'; புகல் ஒன்று நீத்திடில் is read as giving up the refuge one takes in falsehood and the senses.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vaaimai","vazhipaadu","adakkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:196"},"t:2603":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2603,"t":["எய்துவ தெய்தா தொழிவ திதுவருள்","உய்ய அருள்செய்தான் உத்தமன் சீர்நந்தி","பொய்செய்புலன் நெறியொன்பதுந்தாட்கொளின்","மெய்யென் புரவியை மேற்கொள்ள லாமே."],"p":"","r":"What comes and what fails to come: this is grace; the excellent, glorious Nandi gave grace so that one may be saved; if one masters the nine paths of the lying senses, one can mount the horse called truth.","n":"நெறி ஒன்பது (nine paths) may be the nine openings of the body; புரவி (horse) is the vehicle of ascent. மெய் என் புரவி may mean 'the horse called truth' or 'the horse called the body'; both suit Tirumular.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","adakkam","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:196"},"t:2604":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2604,"t":["கைகலந் தானை கருத்தினுள் நந்தியை","மெய்கலந் தான்தன்னை வேத முதல்வனைப்","பொய்கலந் தார்முன் புகுதாப் புனிதனைப்","பொய்யொழிந் தார்க்கே புகலிட மாமே"],"p":"","r":"The one who joined hands with me, Nandi in my thought, the one who mingled in truth, the first of the Veda, the pure one who does not enter before the false, is a refuge only to those who have cast off falsehood.","n":"The verse gathers the chapter's epithets and ends where 2600 and 2601 point: the Lord is refuge (புகலிடம்) to those who have left falsehood, not to all.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaaimai","iraivan","vedham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:196"},"t:2605":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2605,"t":["மெய்த்தாள் அகம்படி மேவிய நந்தியைக்","கைத்தாள் கொண்டாருந் திறந்தறி வாரில்லை","பொய்த்தாள் இடும்பையைப் பொய்யற நீவிட்டாம்","கத்தாள் திறக்கில் அரும் பேற தாமே."],"p":"","r":"No one knows how to open, with their own hand, the bolt of Nandi who dwells in the inner shrine of the true feet; if you put away the false bolt of suffering so that falsehood ends, the opening of the bolt is the rare gain.","n":"தாள் means both foot and door-bolt, and the verse plays on both: மெய்த்தாள் (true feet, or true bolt), கைத்தாள் (hand-bolt), பொய்த்தாள் (false bolt), கத்தாள் (perhaps a corruption). The Lord is pictured behind a locked inner door that effort cannot open but the ending of falsehood can.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvadi","vaaimai","idumbai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:196"},"t:2606":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2606,"t":["உய்யும் வகையால் உணர்வில் ஏத்துமின்","மெய்யன் அரனெறி மேலுண்டு திண்ணெனப்","பொய்யொன்று மின்றிப் புறம்பொலி வார்நடு","ஐயனும் அங்கே அமர்ந்துநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"Praise him in your awareness, in the way that saves; the true path of Hara stands firm above; in the midst of those who shine without any falsehood, the Lord sits and stands.","n":"புறம் பொலிவார் (those who shine outward) suggests people whose outward life shows their inner truth; the Lord's seat is among them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaaimai","vazhipaadu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:196"},"t:2607":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2607,"t":["வம்பு பழுத்த மலர்ப்பழம் ஒன்றுண்டு","தம்பாற் பறவை புகுந்துணத் தானொட்டா","தம்புகொண் டெய்திட் டகலத் துரத்திடிற்","செம்பொற் சிவகதி சென்றெய்த லாமே."],"p":"","r":"There is a fruit of the fragrant flower; he does not let the bird that comes to it enter and eat; if you shoot an arrow and drive the bird far away, you can reach the red-gold Siva-state.","n":"The image is symbolic and not explained. The fruit may be the bliss or nectar of yoga, and the bird the senses or the ego that would consume it; the arrow, as in 2567, the aimed mind. Who 'he' is in line 2 is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","sivam","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:196"},"t:2608":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2608,"t":["மயக்கிய ஐம்புலப் பாசம் அறுத்துத்","துயக்கறுத்தானைத் தொடர்மின்தொடர்ந்தால்","தியக்கஞ் செய்யாதே சிவனெம் பெருமான்","உயப்போ எனமனம் ஒன்றுவித் தானே."],"p":"","r":"Cut the bond of the five bewildering senses and follow the one who cut away confusion; when I followed, Siva our Lord, without wavering, made my mind one, saying 'go and be saved'.","n":"ஐம்புலப் பாசம் names the senses as a பாசம், a bond in the Siddhanta sense. துயக்கறுத்தான் (the one who cut away confusion) may be the guru or Siva himself; the verse moves from the first to the second.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","paasam","guru","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:196"},"t:2609":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2609,"t":["மனமது தானே நினையவல் லாருக்குக்","கினமெனக் கூறு மிருங்காய மேவற்","றனிவினி னாதன்பால் தக்கன செய்யில்","புனிதன் செயலாகும் போதப் புவிக்கே."],"p":"","r":"For those who can think with the mind alone, if they do what is fitting toward the Lord in solitude, the body called a heavy burden becomes the act of the pure one, knowledge for the world.","n":"Lines 2 and 3 (கினமெனக் கூறு மிருங்காய மேவற் றனிவினி) are obscure and the word division is uncertain; இருங்காயம் (the great body) and தனி (alone) are the clearest words. The reading is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["manam","yaakkai","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:196"},"t:2610":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2610,"t":["முன்னை வினைவரின் முன்னுண்டே நீங்குவர்","பின்னை வினைக்கணார் பேர்ந்தறப் பார்ப்பர்கள்","தன்னை யறிந்திடுந் தததுவ ஞானிகள்","நன்மையில் ஐம்புலன் நாடலி னாலே."],"p":"","r":"When former karma arrives, the knowers of tattva who know themselves experience it first and are rid of it; later karma they watch closely so as to drive it off, because they direct the five senses toward the good.","n":"முன்னை வினை and பின்னை வினை are taken as karma already made, which must be lived out (உண்டே நீங்குவர், 'having eaten it they are free'), and karma not yet made, which can be prevented. The verse does not say the jnani escapes former karma, only that he exhausts it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","vinai","adakkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:197"},"t:2611":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2611,"t":["தன்னை யறிந்திடும் தத்துவ ஞானிகள்","முன்னை வினையின் முடிச்சை யவிழ்பவர்கள்","பின்னை வினையைப் பிடித்து பிசைவர்கள்","சென்னியின் வைத்த சிவனரு ளாலே."],"p":"","r":"The knowers of tattva who know themselves untie the knot of former karma and seize and crush later karma, by the grace of Siva placed on their head.","n":"The verse repeats 2610's first line and adds the cause: சென்னியில் வைத்த சிவனருள், grace placed on the head, recalling the guru placing the Lord's feet on the disciple's head. முடிச்சை அவிழ் (untie the knot) may mean that former karma loses its binding power rather than that it is not experienced.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","vinai","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:197"},"t:2612":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2612,"t":["மனவாக்குக் காயத்தால் வல்வினை மூளும்","மனவாக்கு நேர்நிற்கில் வல்வினை மன்னா","மனவாக்கு கெட்டவர் வாதனை தன்னால்","தனைமாற்றி யாற்றத் தகுஞானி தானே."],"p":"","r":"Strong karma kindles through mind, speech and body; if mind and speech stand straight, strong karma does not last; the jnani is the one fit to change himself and act, free of the latent tendencies of those whose mind and speech have gone astray.","n":"The first two lines are clear and set mind, speech and body as the three doors of karma. The last two are compressed: மனவாக்கு கெட்டவர் may be those whose mind and speech are corrupt or those in whom mind and speech have ceased, and வாதனை (vasana, latent impressions) may be what the jnani escapes or what he works through.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","manam","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:197"},"t:2613":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2613,"t":["வாசியு மூசியும் பேசி வகையினால்","பேசி இருந்து பிதற்றிப் பயனில்லை","ஆசையும் அன்பும் அறுமின் அறுத்தபின்","ஈசன் இருந்த இடம் எளிதாமே."],"p":"","r":"There is no use sitting and babbling in all manner of ways about the breath and the needle; cut away desire and attachment, and once they are cut, the place where the Lord dwells becomes easy to reach.","n":"வாசி is traditionally the controlled breath of yoga; ஊசி (needle) is obscure, taken as the needle-fine central channel or the needle-point of concentration. அன்பு here, paired with ஆசை, is attachment to people and things rather than the love of the Lord Tirumular praises elsewhere.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaa","pattru","praanan","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:198"},"t:2614":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2614,"t":["மாடத்து ளானலன் மண்டபத் தானலன்","கூடத்து ளானலன் கோயிலுள் ளானலன்","வேடத்து ளானலன் வேட்கைவிட் டார்நெஞ்சில்","மூடத்து ளேநின்று முத்திதந் தானே."],"p":"","r":"He is not in the mansion, not in the hall, not in the courtyard, not in the temple, not in the holy garb; standing within the hidden place in the heart of those who have given up longing, he granted release.","n":"The list sweeps away both secular and sacred buildings and the ascetic's garb (வேடம்). மூடத்துள்ளே is unusual: மூடம் can mean dullness or a covered place, and is usually taken as the hidden inner space of the heart.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avaa","vesham","veedu","nenju"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:198"},"t:2615":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2615,"t":["ஆசை யறுமின்கள் ஆசை யறுமின்கள்","ஈசனோ டாயினும் ஆசை யறுமின்கள்","ஆசை படப்பட ஆய்வருந் துன்பங்கள்","ஆசை விடவிட ஆனந்த மாமே."],"p":"","r":"Cut off desire, cut off desire; cut off desire even if it is for the Lord; the more desire grows, the more sorrows come, and the more desire is let go, the more it becomes bliss.","n":"ஈசனோடாயினும் (even with the Lord) is the striking phrase. It is often taken as desire for gains or powers from the Lord, or even desire to be joined with him, which still keeps the self that wants; the verse itself doesn't narrow it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avaa","idumbai","aanandham","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:198"},"t:2616":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2616,"t":["அடுவன பூதங்கள் ஐந்தும் உடனே","படுவழி செய்கின்ற பற்றற வீசி","விடுவது வேட்கையை மெய்ந்நின்ற ஞானம்","தொடுவது தம்மைத் தொடர்தலு மாமே."],"p":"","r":"Fling away the attachment that, together with the five oppressing elements, makes the path of suffering; letting go of longing is the knowledge that stands in truth, and what it reaches is the following of one's own self.","n":"அடுவன பூதங்கள் ஐந்தும் (the five elements that oppress) echoes Appar's அடுவன அஞ்சு பூதம், as Sengalvaraya notes. The last line, தொடுவது தம்மைத் தொடர்தலும் ஆமே, is compressed; it may mean that true knowledge turns one toward the self, or that what one clings to then follows one.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pattru","avaa","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:198"},"t:2617":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2617,"t":["உவாக்கடல் ஒக்கின்ற வூழியும் போன","துவாக்கட லுட்பட்டுத் துஞ்சினர் வானோர்","அவாக்கட லுட்பட் டழுந்தினர் மண்ணோர்","தவாக்கடல் ஈசன் தரித்து நின்றானே."],"p":"","r":"The ages, like the swelling sea at full moon, have passed; the gods slept, caught in one sea, and the people of the earth sank, caught in the sea of desire; the Lord stands holding the sea that never fails.","n":"The verse plays on four seas: உவா (the full-moon swell), துவா, அவா (desire) and தவா (unfailing). துவாக்கடல் is obscure; it may be the sea of duality or of the twofold deeds, or a corrupt word. Even the gods drown, only differently from humans.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaa","iraivan","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:198"},"t:2618":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2618,"t":["நின்ற வினையும் பிணியும் நெடுஞ்செயல்","துந்தொழி லற்றுச் சுத்தம தாகலும்","பின்றைங் கருமமும் பேர்த்தருள் நேர்பெற்றுத்","துன்ற அழுத்தலும் ஞானிகள் தூய்மையே."],"p":"","r":"The remaining deeds, disease and long labours ceasing and becoming pure, then even the five acts being turned back and grace gained directly, pressing close and sinking in: this is the purity of the knowers.","n":"நின்ற வினை may be the deeds still to be lived out (prarabdha). பின்றைங் கருமமும் is read as பின் ஐங்கருமமும், the five acts, perhaps the Lord's five acts or the soul's own five; பேர்த்து may mean they are reversed or set aside. துந்தொழில் is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","vinai","thooimai","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:198"},"t:2619":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2619,"t":["உண்மை யுணர்ந்துற ஒண்சித்தி முத்தியாம்","பெண்மயற் கெட்டறப் பேறட்ட சித்தியாம்","திண்மையின் ஞானி சிவகாயம் கைவிட்டால்","வண்மை யருள்தான் அடைந்தபின் ஆறுமே."],"p":"","r":"Realising and attaining truth, the bright attainment is release; when infatuation with women is destroyed, the gain is the eight powers; if the firm knower gives up the Siva-body, after he reaches bountiful grace he comes to rest.","n":"The verse ranks attainments: the eight siddhis follow from mastering desire for women, but release follows only from truth. சிவகாயம் may be the body made Siva's through yoga, which the knower lays down; ஆறுமே may mean he is stilled or that his way is made.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["siththi","veedu","kaamam","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:198"},"t:2620":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2620,"t":["அவனிவன் ஈசனென் றன்புற நாடிச்","சிவனிவன் ஈசனென் றுண்மையை யோரார்","பவனிவன் பல்வகை யாமிப் பிறவி","புவனிவன் போவது பொய்கண்ட போதே."],"p":"","r":"Seeking in love, saying 'that one is the Lord', they do not grasp the truth that Siva here within is the Lord; this one of becoming is this birth of many kinds, and this one's world goes when its falseness is seen.","n":"The verse is built on a chain of pronouns and names: அவன், இவன், சிவன், பவன், புவன். The contrast between அவன் (that one, far off) and இவன் (this one, near) is the plainest point: people look for the Lord out there. பவன் and புவன் may be names of Siva or plays on becoming (bhava) and world (bhuvana).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","piravi","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:198"},"t:2621":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2621,"t":["கொதிக்கின்ற வாறுங் குளிர்கின்ற வாறும்","பதிக்கின்ற வாறிந்தப் பாரக முற்றும்","விதிக்கின்ற ஐவரை வேண்டா துலகம்","நொதிக்கின்ற காயத்து நூலொன்று மாமே."],"p":"","r":"The way things boil and the way they cool, the way they are set across this whole earth: the world does not seek the five who ordain it, and in this fermenting body there is a single thread.","n":"விதிக்கின்ற ஐவர் may be the five deities of the five acts or the five senses that command the body; நூல் ஒன்று may be one thread, one rule or one scripture. How the verse bears on desire is not clear from the Tamil.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","aindhozhil","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:198"},"t:2622":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2622,"t":["உய்ந்தனம் என்பீர் உறுபொருள் காண்கிலீர்","கந்த மலரிற் கலக்கின்ற நந்தியைச்","சிந்தையில் வைத்துத் தெளிவுறச் சேர்த்திட்டால்","முந்தைப் பிறவிக்கு மூலவித் தாமே."],"p":"","r":"You say 'we are saved' yet do not see the thing to be reached; if you set Nandi, who mingles in the fragrant flower, in your thought and join him with clarity, he is the root seed even before birth.","n":"கந்த மலர் (the fragrant flower) is traditionally the heart-lotus. முந்தைப் பிறவிக்கு மூலவித்து can mean the Lord is the primal seed prior to all birth, or that joining him reaches the root seed of births and so ends them.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","thelivu","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:198"},"t:2623":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2623,"t":["முத்திசெய் ஞானமும் கேள்வியு மாய்நிற்கும்","அத்தனை மாயா அமரர் பிரான்தனைச்","சுத்தனை தூய்நெறி யாய்நின்ற சோதியைப்","பத்தர் பரசும் பசுபதி தானென்றே."],"p":"","r":"The devotees praise as Pasupati the Father who stands as the knowledge that grants release and as what is heard, the undying Lord of the immortals, the pure one, the light that stands as the pure path.","n":"பசுபதி (Lord of the pasus, the bound souls) names the Siddhanta relation of Pati to pasu. கேள்வி is learning by hearing, here the teaching heard from the guru or scripture.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paththi","pathi","gnaanam","kelvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:199"},"t:2624":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2624,"t":["அடியார் அடியார் அடியார்க் கடிமைக்","கடியவனாய் நல்கிட் டடினையும் பூண்டேன்","அடியார் அருளால் அவனடி கூட","அடியா னிவனென் றடிமைகொண் டானே."],"p":"","r":"I took on the servitude of being servant to the servants of the servants of his servants; by the grace of the devotees, so that I might reach his feet, he took me as his servant, saying 'this one is my servant'.","n":"The repeated அடியார் stacks degrees of humility, a common form in Tamil Saiva hymns. The grace that brings the poet to the Lord comes through the devotees (அடியார் அருளால்), not directly.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adiyaar","adimai","thiruvadi","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:199"},"t:2625":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2625,"t":["நீரிற் குளிரும் நெருப்பினிற் சுட்டிடும்","ஆரிக் கடனந்தி யாமா ரறிபவர்","பாரிற் பயனாரைப் பார்க்கிலும் நேரியர்","ஊரில் உமாபதி யாகிநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"He is cool in water and burns in fire; who can know Nandi? He is subtler than anyone one could find in the world, and he stands in the town as Uma's consort.","n":"Line 2, ஆரிக் கடனந்தி யாமா ரறிபவர், is hard to divide; it may be ஆர் இக்கடன் (who [knows] this duty) or a question about who can know Nandi. நேரியர் may mean subtle or upright. The verse sets the Lord's pervading power beside his form with Uma.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","saththi","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:199"},"t:2626":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2626,"t":["ஒத்துல கேழும் அறியா ஒருவனென்","ற்த்தன் இருந்திடம் ஆரறிவார்சொல்லப்","பத்தர்தம் பத்தியிற் பாற்படில் அல்லது","முத்தினை யார்சொல்ல முந்துநின் றாரே."],"p":"","r":"Who can say where the Father dwells, the One whom all seven worlds together cannot know? Unless one comes to share in the devotion of his devotees, who could speak of that pearl? Those who did stood first.","n":"முத்து (pearl) is a name for the Lord. The last phrase, முந்துநின்றாரே, may mean the devotees stand ahead of all others, or that they alone came forward to speak.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["paththi","adiyaar","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:199"},"t:2627":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2627,"t":["ஆன்கன்று தேடி யழைக்கு மதுபோல்","நான்கன்றாய் நாடி யழைத்தேனென் நாதனை","வான்கன்றுக் கப்பாலாய் நின்ற மறைப்பொருள்","ஊன்கன்றா னாடிவந் துள்புகுந் தானே."],"p":"","r":"As a cow seeks and calls its calf, I, as a calf, sought and called my Lord; and the hidden meaning of the Vedas, who stood beyond the heavens, sought me out, came and entered into this body of flesh.","n":"The image turns: the poet calls as a calf, but the Lord comes searching like the cow. வான்கன்றுக்கு அப்பால் and ஊன்கன்றால் are unclear; கன்று may be repeated for the wordplay, the second perhaps 'the calf in flesh', the poet.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["paththi","anbu","iraivan","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:199"},"t:2628":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2628,"t":["பெத்தத்துந் தன்பணி இல்லை பிறத்தலான்","முத்தத்துந் தன்பணி இல்லை முறைமையால்","அத்தற் கிரண்டும் அருளால் அளித்தலாற்","பத்திப்பட் டோ ர்க்குப் பணியொன்றும் இல்லையே."],"p":"","r":"In bondage there is no task of one's own, since one is born into it; in release there is no task of one's own, by its nature; since the Father gives both through grace, those caught in devotion have nothing to do.","n":"பெத்தம் and முத்தம் are the Siddhanta terms for the soul's bound and released states. தன்பணி (one's own work) is traditionally taken to mean that the soul is not the agent of either; whether this rules out effort or only claims of merit is not settled by the verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["paththi","thiruvarul","veedu","pasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:199"},"t:2629":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2629,"t":["பறவையிற் கற்பமும் பாம்புமெய் யாகப்","குறவஞ் சிலம்பக் குளிர்வரை யேறி","நறவார் மலர்கொண்டு நந்தியை யல்லால்","இறைவனென் றென்மனம் ஏத்தகி லாவே."],"p":"","r":"Climbing the cool mountain where the hill-folk shout, with honey-laden flowers, my mind will praise none as God but Nandi, whose body bears the serpent.","n":"Line 1, பறவையிற் கற்பமும் பாம்புமெய் யாக, is obscure: கற்பம் may be the kalpa tree, an elixir or an age, and பாம்பு மெய்யாக may mean the serpent on his body or the body as serpent in a yogic reading. The mountain may be Kailasa or a hill shrine.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","paththi","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:199"},"t:2630":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2630,"t":["உறுதுணை நந்தியை உம்பர் பிரானை","பெறுதுணை செய்து பிறப்பறுத் துய்மின்","செறிதுணை செய்து சிவனடி சிந்தித்","துறுதுணை யாயங்கி யாகிநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"Make Nandi, the close companion, the Lord of the gods, your support and cut off birth and be saved; when one makes him close support and thinks on Siva's feet, he stands as one's support, as fire.","n":"The verse runs on துணை (support, companion) four times. அங்கியாகி (as fire) may be the inner fire of yoga or the fire that burns away bonds.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thunai","thiruvadi","piravi","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:199"},"t:2631":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2631,"t":["வானவர் தம்மை வலிசெய் திருக்கின்ற","தானவர் முப்புரஞ் செற்ற தலைவனைக்","கானவன் என்றுங் கருவரை யானென்றும்","ஊனத னுள்நினைந் தொன்றுபட் டாரே."],"p":"","r":"The Lord who destroyed the three cities of the demons who oppressed the gods: calling him the forest-dweller and the one of the dark mountain, thinking of him within the flesh, they became one with him.","n":"The famous deed (the burning of the three cities) and the homely names (கானவன், கருவரையான்) are both ways of naming him; the union comes from thinking of him inside the body (ஊனதனுள்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paththi","sivam","saayujjiyam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:199"},"t:2632":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2632,"t":["நிலைபெறு கேடென்று முன்னே படைத்த","தலைவனை நாடித் தயங்குமென் உள்ளம்","மலையுளும் வானகத் துள்ளும் புறத்தும்","உலையுளும் உள்ளத்து மூழ்கிநின் றேனே."],"p":"","r":"My heart wavers as it seeks the Leader who first made both what endures and what perishes; in the mountain, in the sky, outside, in the forge and in the heart, I stood immersed.","n":"தயங்கும் can mean wavers or shines. The list of places may be where the poet searched or where he finds himself immersed in the Lord; உலை (forge) is unexpected and may be the body's inner fire.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["paththi","iraivan","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:199"},"t:2633":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2633,"t":["முத்தியில் அத்தன் முழுத்த அருள்பெற்றுத்","தத்துவ சுத்தி தலைப்பட்டுத் தன்பணி","மெய்த்தவஞ் செய்கை வினைவிட்ட மெய்யுண்மைப்","பத்தியி லுற்றோர் பரானந்த போதரே."],"p":"","r":"Those who, in release, have received the Father's full grace, reached the purification of the tattvas, and come into true devotion, in which their own service, true penance and action have left deeds behind, are knowers of the supreme bliss.","n":"தத்துவ சுத்தி (purification of the tattvas) is a Siddhanta stage in which the soul sees the constituents of its world as not itself. The syntax of line 3 is loose; வினைவிட்ட may qualify the devotion or the people. பரானந்த போதர் may also be 'those awakened to supreme bliss'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["veedu","thaththuvam","paththi","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:200"},"t:2634":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2634,"t":["வளங்கனி தேடிய வன்றாட் பறவை","உளங்கனி தேடி யுழிதரும் போது","களங்கனி யங்கியிற் கைவிளக் கேற்றி","நலங் கொண்ட நால்வரும் நாடுகின்றாரே."],"p":"","r":"When the strong-legged bird that sought rich fruit wanders seeking the fruit of the heart, the four who have gained the good light a lamp in their hand in the fire of the throat-fruit and search.","n":"The verse is symbolic and its key terms can't be fixed from the Tamil. The bird may be the soul; களங்கனி may be the dark kala fruit or the 'fruit in the throat', a yogic centre; நால்வர் may be the followers of the four paths or four sages. I give only the surface.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["veedu","gnaanam","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:200"},"t:2635":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2635,"t":["பெம்மான் பெருநந்தி பேச்சற்ற பேரின்பத்து","அம்மா நடிதந் தருட்கடல் ஆடினோம்","எம்மாய மும்விடுத் தெம்மைக் கரந்திட்டுச்","சும்மா திருந்திடஞ் சோதனை யாகுமே."],"p":"","r":"Our Lord, great Nandi, danced in the great bliss beyond speech, and we bathed in the ocean of grace; leaving every maya and hiding ourselves away, to stay simply still is the test.","n":"சோதனை can mean testing or searching, and some take it with சோதி (light). சும்மா இருத்தல் (simply being still) is a phrase of Tamil mystical tradition for the end of striving. அம்மா may be an exclamation or address the Mother.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mounam","aanandham","thiruvarul","maayai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:201"},"t:2636":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2636,"t":["அறிவுடை யானரு மாமறை யுள்ளே","செறிவுடை யான்மிகு தேவர்க்குந் தேவன்","பொறியுடை யான்புலன் ஐந்துங் கடந்த","குறியுடை யானொடுங் கூடுவன் நானே."],"p":"","r":"I will join with the one who has all knowledge, who dwells close within the rare great Veda, the god of the gods, who holds the senses and has the mark beyond the five senses.","n":"பொறியுடையான் may mean master of the sense organs or the one with the spark or mark; குறி (mark, sign) beyond the senses may be his formless sign or a yogic object of meditation.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","vedham","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:201"},"t:2637":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2637,"t":["அறிவறி வென்றங் கரற்றும் உலகம்","அறிவறி யாமையை யாரும் அறியார்","அறிவறி யாமை கடந்தறி வானால்","அறிவறி யாமை யழகிய வாறே."],"p":"","r":"The world cries 'knowledge, knowledge', yet no one knows what knowledge and ignorance are; when knowledge goes beyond both knowledge and ignorance, how fine knowledge and ignorance then become!","n":"The verse plays on அறிவு and அறியாமை six times. The last line may be admiring (once both are transcended they are seen rightly) or ironic (how strange that is); the tone isn't fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arivu","gnaanam","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:201"},"t:2638":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2638,"t":["குறியாக் குறியினிற் கூடாத கூட்டத்","தறியா அறிவில் அவிழ்ந்தேக சித்தமாய்","நெறியாம் பராநந்தி நீடருள் ஒன்றுஞ்","செறியாச் செறிவே சிவமென லாமே."],"p":"","r":"In the sign that is no sign, in the union that does not join, in the knowledge that does not know, loosened into a single mind, the path is supreme Nandi; his long grace is one; that close union which is no union may be called Sivam.","n":"The verse defines its goal only by negations (குறியாக் குறி, கூடாத கூட்டம், அறியா அறிவு, செறியாச் செறிவு). ஏகசித்தம் is the one-pointed or single mind. சிவமெனலாம் (may be called Sivam) is guarded: a name, not a description.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","gnaanam","thiruvarul","saayujjiyam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:201"},"t:2639":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2639,"t":["காலினில் ஊருங் கரும்பினில் கட்டியும்","பாலினுள் நெய்யும் பழத்துள் இரதமும்","பூவினுள் நாற்றமும் போலுளன் எம்மிறை","காவலன் எங்குங் கலந்துநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"Like what moves in the wind, jaggery in the sugarcane, ghee in milk, juice in fruit and fragrance in the flower, so is our Lord; the guardian stands mingled everywhere.","n":"Each image is of something present throughout a thing but not visible in it. காலினில் ஊரும் is unclear: it may be scent carried on the wind, or the wind itself. Sengalvaraya notes Appar's பாலின் நெய்யாம் பழத்தின் இரதமாம் as a parallel.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:201"},"t:2640":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2640,"t":["விருப்பொடு கூடி விகிர்த்னை நாடிப்","பொருப்பகஞ் சேர்தரு பொற்கொடி போல","இருப்பர் மனத்திடை எங்கள் பிரானார்","நெருப்புரு வாகி நிகழ்ந்துநின் றாரே."],"p":"","r":"Like a golden creeper that, joined with longing and seeking the Transcendent, reaches up a mountain, our Lord dwells in the mind, appearing as a form of fire.","n":"The creeper may be the devoted soul climbing toward the Lord, or Uma, the mountain's daughter, reaching Siva; the verse's subject shifts from the creeper to the Lord. விருப்பொடு கூடி (joined with longing) treats longing for the Lord as good.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["manam","sivam","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:201"},"t:2641":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2641,"t":["நந்தி பெருமான் நடுவுள் வியோமத்து","வந்தென் அகம்படி கோயில்கொண் டான்கொள்ள","எந்தைவந் தானென் றெழுந்தேன் எழுதலுஞ்","சிந்தையி லுள்ளே சிவனிருந் தானே."],"p":"","r":"Lord Nandi came in the space at the centre and took my inner self as his temple; as he did so I rose up saying 'my Father has come', and as I rose, Siva was within my thought.","n":"நடுவுள் வியோமம் (the space at the centre) is traditionally the space of the heart or the space between the brows; the verse doesn't say which. அகம்படி may be the inner self or the inner precinct of a temple, kept as a pun.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","manam","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:201"},"t:2642":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2642,"t":["தன்மைவல் லோனைத் தவத்துள் நலத்தினை","நன்மைவல் லோனை நடுவுறை நந்தியைப்","புன்மைபொய் யாதே புனிதனை நாடுமின்","பன்மையில் உம்மைப் பரிசுசெய் வானே."],"p":"","r":"Without meanness or falsehood, seek the pure one, master of his nature, the good within penance, the one able in goodness, Nandi who dwells at the centre; he will shape you in many ways.","n":"பரிசுசெய்வான் may mean he will fit you, form you or reward you; பன்மையில் (in manyness) may be 'in many ways' or 'out of the many', marking the seeker out.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","thooimai","thavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:201"},"t:2643":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2643,"t":["தொடர்ந்துநின் றானென்னைச் சோதிக்கும் போது","தொடர்ந்துநின் றானல்ல நாதனும் அங்கே","படர்ந்துநின் றாதிப் பராபரன் எந்தை","கடந்துநின் றவ்வழி காட்டுகின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"When he tested me he stood close behind me, and yet the Lord did not stand following there; spreading through all, the First, the Supreme beyond the supreme, my Father, stands beyond and shows that path.","n":"The first two lines seem to say opposite things (he followed me; he did not follow), which may contrast the Lord as near guide and as transcendent, or the guru and the Lord. சோதிக்கும் (tests) gives the chapter its title.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","thiruvarul","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:201"},"t:2644":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2644,"t":["அவ்வழி காட்டும் அமரர்க் கரும்பொருள்","இவ்வழி தந்தை தாய் கேளியான் ஒக்குஞ்","செவ்வழி சேர்சிவ லோகத் திருந்திடும்","இவ்வழி நந்தி இயல்பது தானே."],"p":"","r":"The thing rare even for the gods, who shows that path, is on this path like father, mother and kin; he sets those on the right path in Siva's world; this way is the nature of Nandi.","n":"சிவலோகம் (Siva's world) is the first of the graded states of release (salokam) in Siddhanta, though the verse may use it loosely for release. செவ்வழி (the straight or right path) can be read either with the seekers or with Siva's world.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","sutram","padhamukthi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:201"},"t:2645":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2645,"t":["எறிவது ஞானத் துறைவாள் உருவி","அறிவது னோடேயவ் வாண்டகை யானைச்","செறிவது தேவர்க்குத் தேவர் பிரானைப்","பறிவது பல்கணப் பற்றுவி டாரே."],"p":"","r":"Drawing and striking with the sword of the ford of knowledge, knowing that noble one by it and joining the Lord of the gods of gods, is what those who do not give up attachment to the many hosts have torn from them.","n":"The first three lines describe the work of knowledge; the last line, பறிவது பல்கணப் பற்றுவிடாரே, is hard to join to them: it may say that those who do not let go of the many (senses, people, or goblin hosts) lose all this, or that what is cut away is that attachment. துறை may be ford, path or field.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","pattru","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:201"},"t:2646":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2646,"t":["ஆதிப் பிரான்தந்த வாள்ங்கைக்கொண்டபின்","வேதித்து என்னை விலக்கவல் லாரில்லை","சோதிப்பன் அங்கே சுவடு படாவண்ணம்","ஆதிக்கட் டெய்வ மவனிவ னாமே."],"p":"","r":"Once I took in hand the sword the First Lord gave, no one can pierce me or turn me aside; I will test there so that no trace is left; the God of the beginning has become this one.","n":"The sword is traditionally knowledge given by the Lord or guru; Sengalvaraya compares Arunagirinathar's sword of knowledge-flame in Kandar Alankaram. அவன் இவன் ஆமே may mean the Lord has become the speaker, or simply that this is the primal god. வேதித்து may mean piercing or transforming.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","sivam","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:201"},"t:2647":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2647,"t":["அந்தக் கருவை யருவை வினைசெய்தற்","பந்தம் பணியச்சம் பல்பிறப் பும்வாட்டிச்","சிந்தை திருத்தலுஞ் சேர்ந்தாரச் சோதனை","சந்திக்கத் தற்பர மாகுஞ் சதுரர்க்கே."],"p":"","r":"Wasting away that embryo and the formless, the bond of making deeds, fearful servitude and the many births, and setting the mind right: for the skilled who meet that test, it becomes the Supreme itself.","n":"அந்தக் கருவை யருவை may be 'that embryo, the formless' or a play on கரு and அரு (form and formless). பணியச்சம் may be servitude and fear, or fear that makes one bow. தற்பரம் is the Supreme in itself.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","piravi","karu","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:201"},"t:2648":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2648,"t":["உரையற்ற தொன்றை யுரைத்தான் எனக்குக்","கரையற் றெழுந்த கலைவேட் டறுத்துத்","திரையொத்த என்னுடல் நீங்கா திருத்திப்","புரையற்ற என்னுட் புகுந்தற் பரனே."],"p":"","r":"He told me the one thing beyond telling; cutting off the craving for the arts that rose without bound, keeping my wave-like body from leaving me and setting it right, the Supreme entered into me, now flawless.","n":"கலைவேட்டு may be craving for learning and the arts, or for the kalas, the phases of the yogic breath. திரையொத்த உடல் (body like a wave, or wrinkled) is kept, not discarded: the Lord enters the embodied person.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","avaa","yaakkai","mounam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:201"},"t:2649":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2649,"t":["பலியும் அவியும் பரந்து புகையும்","ஒலியும் ஈசன் தனக்கென்ற உள்கிக்","குவியும் குருமடம் கண்டவர் தாம்போய்த்","தளிரும் மலரடி சார்ந்துநின் றாரே."],"p":"","r":"Those who have seen the guru's matha, where people gather knowing that the offerings, the oblations, the spreading smoke and the sounds are all for the Lord, went on and stood joined to his feet, tender as young leaves and flowers.","n":"பலி, அவி, புகை and ஒலி are the offerings, oblations, incense and chanting of ritual worship; the matha is valued because there they are known to be the Lord's. குவியும் may be the gathering of people or the folding of hands and minds.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["madam","guru","vazhipaadu","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:202"},"t:2650":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2650,"t":["இவன்இல்லம் அல்லது அவனுக்கு அங்கு இல்லை","அவனுக்கும் வேறு இல்லம் உண்டா அறியின்","அவனுக்கு இவனில்லம் என்றென்று அறிந்தும்","அவனைப் புறம்புஎன்று அரற்றுகின் றாரே."],"p":"","r":"Apart from this one's house he has no house there; if one asks whether he has some other house, they know again and again that this one's house is his, and yet they wail that he is outside.","n":"அவன் is the Lord and இவன் the person; இல்லம் (house) is usually read as the body or the heart. Line 2 can also be a statement: if one knows, he has no other house.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","iraivan","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:202"},"t:2651":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2651,"t":["நாடும் பெருந்துறை நான்கண்டு கொண்டபின்","கூடும் சிவனது கொய்மலர்ச் சேவடி","தேட அரியன் சிறப்பிலி எம்இறை","ஓடும் உலகுயிர் ஆகிநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"After I found the great ford that all seek, Siva's red feet, like freshly picked flowers, come to me; our Lord, hard to search out, who has no special mark, stands as the life of the moving world.","n":"பெருந்துறை (the great ford) may be the shrine of Tirupperunturai or a figure for the place of crossing, the guru or his matha; the verse doesn't settle it. சிறப்பிலி may mean the one without distinguishing marks or without equal.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvadi","sivam","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:202"},"t:2652":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2652,"t":["இயம்புவன் ஆசனத் தோடு மலையும்","இயம்புவன் சித்தக் குகையும் இடமும்","இயம்புவன் ஆதாரத் தோடு வனமும்","இயம்புவன் ஈராறு இருநிலத் தோர்க்கே."],"p":"","r":"I will tell of the seat and the mountain, of the cave of the mind and the place, of the centres and the forest, to the people of the twelve great lands.","n":"The places a yogi might seek outside (seat, mountain, cave, forest) are matched with inner ones (சித்தக் குகை, ஆதாரம்). ஈராறு இருநிலத்தோர் may be the people of the twelve lands, the wide earth, or those at the twelfth centre above the head; it can't be fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aasanam","aadhaaram","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:202"},"t:2653":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2653,"t":["முகம்பீடம் மாமடம் முன்னிய தேயம்","அகம்பர வர்க்கமே ஆசில்செய் காட்சி","அகம்பர மாதனம் எண்எண் கிரியை","சிதம்பரம் தற்குகை ஆதாரம் தானே."],"p":"","r":"The face is the seat, the great matha and the land one seeks; the inner self is the supreme order and the faultless vision; the inner self is the supreme seat and the sixty-four rites; Chidambaram, the cave of the self, is the centre itself.","n":"Each outward thing of a matha (its seat, building, land, rites) is set equal to something in the person. எண்எண் கிரியை may be sixty-four rites or eight-and-eight. சிதம்பரம் is both the shrine and, by its meaning 'space of consciousness', the inner space; தற்குகை is the cave of the self.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["madam","aadhaaram","yaakkai","kiriyai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:202"},"t:2654":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2654,"t":["அகமுக மாம்பீடம் ஆதார மாகும்","சகமுக மாம்சத்தி யாதன மாகும்","செகமுக மாம்தெய்வ மேசிவ மாகும்","அகமுகம் ஆய்ந்த அறிவுடை யோர்க்கே."],"p":"","r":"For the knowing who have examined the inward face, the inward face is the seat and the centre; the world-ward face is Shakti's seat; and the face toward the world, the deity, is Siva.","n":"The verse plays on அகமுகம் (face turned inward), சகமுகம் and செகமுகம் (both read as face turned to the world, from ஜகம்); the difference between the second and third is unclear and one may be a variant. The general sense is that the inward turn is the true seat.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aadhaaram","saththi","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:202"},"t:2655":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2655,"t":["மாயை இரண்டும் மறைக்க மறைவுறும்","காயம்ஓர் ஐந்தும் கழியத்தா னாகியே","தூய பரஞ்சுடர் தோன்றச் சொரூபத்துள்","ஆய்பவர் ஞானாதி மோனத்த ராமே."],"p":"","r":"When the two mayas veil and are themselves veiled, and the five bodies pass away and one becomes oneself, and the pure supreme flame appears, those who search within their own form are the silent ones who begin from knowledge.","n":"மாயை இரண்டும் are traditionally pure and impure maya; காயம் ஓர் ஐந்தும் may be the five sheaths or the five bodies of Siddhanta. ஞானாதி மோனத்தர் may be 'silent ones with knowledge first' or 'silent ones of knowledge and the rest'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maayai","mounam","gnaanam","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:202"},"t:2656":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2656,"t":["ஆறொடு முப்பதும் அங்கே அடங்கிடில்","கூறக் குருபரன் கும்பிடு தந்திடும்","வேறே சிவபதம் மேலாய் அளித்திடும்","பேறாக ஆனந்தம் பேணும் பெருகவே."],"p":"","r":"When the thirty-six subside there, the supreme guru grants what is to be worshipped, gives Siva's state above all else, and bliss swells and is kept as the gain.","n":"ஆறொடு முப்பதும் (six with thirty) is taken as the thirty-six tattvas. கும்பிடு தந்திடும் is unclear: the guru may give the act of worship, or give himself as the one to be worshipped.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","thaththuvam","sivam","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:203"},"t:2657":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2657,"t":["துரியங்கள் மூன்றும் கடந்தொளிர் சோதி","அரிய பரசிவம் யாவையும் ஆகி","விரிவு குவிவுஅற விட்ட நிலத்தே","பெரிய குருபதம் பேசஒண் ணாதே."],"p":"","r":"The light that shines beyond the three turiyas, rare Parasiva, having become all things, in the ground left where expanding and contracting have ceased: the great guru's state cannot be spoken.","n":"துரியங்கள் மூன்றும் (the three turiyas) is traditionally the turiya of the soul, of the Shakti and of Siva, but the verse does not say. விரிவு குவிவு (expanding and contracting) may be the outward and inward movement of the mind or of the world.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","avaththai","sivam","mounam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:203"},"t:2658":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2658,"t":["ஆயன நந்தி அடிக்குஎன்தலைபெற்றேன்","வாயன நந்தியை வாழ்த்தஎன் வாய்பெற்றேன்","காயன நந்தியைக் காணஎன் கண்பெற்றேன்","சேயன நந்திக்குஎன் சிந்தைபெற் றேனே."],"p":"","r":"I received my head for the feet of Nandi, my mouth to praise him, my eyes to see him, and my mind for him.","n":"Each line gives a part of the body its use. The epithets ஆயன, வாயன, காயன, சேயன rhyme and are hard to fix: perhaps 'who has become', 'who is in speech', 'who has a body' (the guru in visible form) and 'who is far'. The point is that the faculties were given for him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["guru","thiruvadi","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:203"},"t:2659":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2659,"t":["கருடன் உருவம் கருதும் அளவில்","பருவிடம் தீர்ந்து பயம்கெடு மாபோல்","குருவின் உருவம் குறித்த அப் போதே","திரிமலம் தீர்ந்து சிவன்அவன் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"As the heavy poison clears and fear ends as soon as one thinks of Garuda's form, so the moment one fixes on the guru's form the three malams clear and he becomes Siva.","n":"The image is of the snake-bite cure in which Garuda, the snakes' enemy, is held in mind. சிவன் அவன் ஆமே can mean the disciple becomes Siva, or that the guru is Siva; both readings are made.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["guru","malam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:203"},"t:2660":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2660,"t":["அண்ணல் இருப்பிடம் ஆரும் அறிகிலர்","அண்ணல் இருப்பிடம் ஆய்ந்துகொள் வார்களுக்கு","அண்ணல் அழிவின்றி உள்ளே அமர்ந்திடும்","அண்ணலைக் காணில் அவன்இவன் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"No one knows where the Lord dwells; for those who search out his dwelling he settles within, never perishing, and if one sees the Lord, he becomes this one.","n":"அண்ணல் opens all four lines. அவன் இவன் ஆமே (he becomes this one) is the same turn as the close of 2659: the seer and the seen become one, which can be read either way round.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","meyyunarvu","saayujjiyam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:203"},"t:2661":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2661,"t":["தோன்ற அறிதலும் தோன்றல் தோன்றாமையும்","மான்ற அறிவு மறிநன வாதிகள்","மூன்றவை நீங்கும் துரியங்கள் மூன்றற","ஊன்றிய நந்தி உயர்மோனத் தானே."],"p":"","r":"Knowing as things appear, appearing and not appearing, deluded knowledge, and the waking and the other states: these three go, and when the three turiyas end, Nandi stands fixed in the high silence.","n":"Which three are meant by மூன்றவை is unclear: the three kinds of knowing in lines 1 and 2, or waking, dream and sleep. மறி நனவாதிகள் may be the waking and the rest turned back. மோனம் here is the silent state of the guru.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","mounam","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:203"},"t:2662":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2662,"t":["சந்திர பூமிக் குள்தன்புரு வத்திடைக்","கந்த மலரில் இரண்டிதழ்க் கன்னியும்","பந்தம் இலாத பளிங்கின் உருவினள்","பந்தம் அறுத்த பரம்குரு பற்றே."],"p":"","r":"In the land of the moon, between one's brows, in a fragrant flower, is the maiden of two petals, crystal-formed and free of bond; she is the hold of the supreme guru who cut the bonds.","n":"The two-petalled flower between the brows is traditionally the ajna centre, and the maiden there is taken as Shakti. சந்திர பூமி (the land of the moon) is a yogic term I can't fix. பற்று may mean that she is what the guru holds, or the refuge he gives.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","aadhaaram","guru","paasam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:203"},"t:2663":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2663,"t":["மனம்புகுந் தான்உலகு ஏழும் மகிழ","நிலம்புகுந் தான்நெடு வானிலம் தாங்கிச்","சினம்புகுந் தான்திசை எட்டும்நடுங்க","வனம்புகுந் தான்ஊர் வடக்கென்பது ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"He entered the mind so the seven worlds rejoiced, entered the earth bearing up the long sky, entered into wrath so the eight directions shook, and entered the forest: this is what is meant by 'his town is to the north'.","n":"The verse is obscure. The wrath may allude to Siva's angry deeds, the forest to the ascetic's retreat, and the northern town to Kailasa, or in a yogic reading to the crown of the head. Who 'he' is (the Lord or the guru) is not said.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:203"},"t:2664":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2664,"t":["தானான வண்ணமும் கோசமும் சார்தரும்","தானாம் பறவை வனமெனத் தக்கன","தானான சோடச மார்க்கந்தான் நின்றிடில்","தாமாம் தசாங்கமும் வேறுள்ள தானே."],"p":"","r":"The colours and the sheaths that are oneself draw near, bird and forest are fit to be called oneself, and if one stands in the sixteen-fold path, the ten parts that seemed oneself become something other.","n":"கோசம் is taken as the five sheaths of the self, சோடச மார்க்கம் as the path through sixteen kalas, and தசாங்கம் as ten parts or limbs. The bird and forest may be the soul in the body. I can't fix the argument; the verse seems to say that what one took for oneself is seen as other on the path.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","kalai","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:203"},"t:2665":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2665,"t":["மருவிப் பிரிவுஅறி யாஎங்கள் மாநந்தி","உருவம் நினைக்க நின்று உள்ளே உருக்கும்","கருவில் கரந்துஉள்ளம் காணவல் லார்க்குஇங்கு","அருவினை கண்சோரும் அழிவார் அகத்தே."],"p":"","r":"Our great Nandi, who joins and knows no parting, stays and melts one within when his form is thought of; for those who can see the heart hidden in its source, hard karma swoons away here, within those who dissolve.","n":"கருவில் கரந்து may mean hidden in the source, or hidden in the womb, that is, in the body. அழிவார் அகத்தே is unclear: 'within those who are undone' or 'they perish within'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","vinai","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:203"},"t:2666":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2666,"t":["தலைப்பட லாம்எங்கள் தத்துவன் தன்னைப்","பலப்படு பாசம் அறுத்துஅறுத் திட்டு","நிலைப்பெற நாடி நினைப்பற உள்கில்","தலைப்பட லாகும் தருமமும் தானே."],"p":"","r":"One can reach our Lord of the tattvas: cutting and cutting the many bonds, seeking to stand firm and contemplating him beyond thought, one reaches him, and he himself is dharma.","n":"தத்துவன் is the Lord as the real, or as master of the tattvas. நினைப்பற உள்கில் (if one contemplates with thought gone) holds a paradox the chapter returns to. The last line may also mean that reaching him is itself dharma.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pathi","paasam","thiyaanam","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:203"},"t:2667":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2667,"t":["நினைக்கின் நினைக்கும் நினைப்பவர் தம்மைச்","சுனைக்குள் விளைமலர்ச் சோதியி னானைத்","தினைப்பிளந் தன்ன சிறுமைய ரேனும்","கனத்த மனத்தடைந் தால்உயர்ந் தாரே."],"p":"","r":"He thinks of those who think of him, he of the light of the flower that grows in the spring; even those as small as a split millet seed, if they reach him with a weighty mind, become high.","n":"The flower in the spring (சுனை) is commonly taken as the heart lotus. கனத்த மனம் may be a full, firm or devoted mind. The verse sets the measure of greatness in the mind's turning to him, not in one's station.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","manam","sirumai","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:203"},"t:2668":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2668,"t":["தலைப்படும் காலத்துத் தத்துவம் தன்னை","விலக்குறின் மேவை விதியென்றும் கொள்க","அனைத்துஉல காய் நின்ற ஆதிப் பிரானை","நினைப்புறு வார்பத்தி தேடிக் கொள்வாரே."],"p":"","r":"At the time of reaching him, if one sets aside the tattvas, take that too as the rule of attaining; those who think of the first Lord who stands as all the worlds will seek and find devotion.","n":"மேவை விதி is obscure, perhaps 'the rule for reaching'. பத்தி may be devotion, or, less likely, order. The verse seems to make letting go of the tattvas part of the method, not its failure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","anbu","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:203"},"t:2669":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2669,"t":["நகழ்வுஒழிந் தார்அவர் நாதனை யுள்கி","நிகழ்வுஒழிந் தார்எம் பிரானொடும் கூடித்","திகழ்வொழிந் தார்தங்கள் சிந்தையின் உள்ளே","புகழ்வழி காட்டிப் புகுந்துநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"Contemplating the Lord they ceased to waver; joined with our Lord they ceased from what happens; within their own minds they ceased from display; and he entered and stayed, showing the way of glory.","n":"The three ceasings are built on நகழ்வு, நிகழ்வு and திகழ்வு. நகழ்வு is an unusual word, taken here as wavering; திகழ்வு may be shining or display. The plain sense is a growing stillness ended by the Lord's entry.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiyaanam","saayujjiyam","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:203"},"t:2670":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2670,"t":["வந்த மரகத மாணிக்க ரேகைபோல்","சந்திடு மாமொழிச் சற்குரு சன்மார்க்கம்","இந்த இரேகை இலாடத்தின் மூலத்தே","சுந்தரச் சோதியுள் சோதியும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"Like a line of emerald and ruby that appears, the true path of the true guru of the great word joins; this line, at the root of the forehead, becomes the light within the beautiful light.","n":"இலாடம் is the forehead, and the line of green and red there is taken as a light seen between the brows in meditation, perhaps the meeting of Shakti and Siva. சந்திடு (joins) may describe the colours meeting or the disciple joining the path.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","sanmaarkkam","oli"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:203"},"t:2671":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2671,"t":["உண்ணும் வாயும் உடலும் உயிருமாய்க்","கண்ணுமா யோகக் கடவுள் இருப்பது","மண்ணு நீரனல் காலொடு வானுமாய்","விண்ணு மின்றி வெளியானோர் மேனியே."],"p":"","r":"The yogic god dwells as the mouth that eats, the body, the life and the eye; he is earth, water, fire, air and sky, and the form of those who have become the open expanse is beyond even the sky.","n":"யோகக் கடவுள் may be Siva as yogi or the realised guru. The last line, வெளியானோர் மேனி, seems to say that the body of those who have become the void is no longer bound to the elements; the syntax is loose.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","yaakkai","thaththuvam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:203"},"t:2672":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2672,"t":["பரசு பதியென்று பார்முழு தெல்லாம்","பரசிவன் ஆணை நடக்கும் பாதியால்","பெரிய பதிசெய்து பின்னாம் அடியார்க்கு","உரிய பதியும்பா ராக்கி நின்றானே."],"p":"","r":"Proclaiming him Pasupati, Parasiva's command runs through the whole earth by its half; having made the great seat, he made the earth too a fitting seat for the devotees who come after.","n":"The verse plays on பதி as Lord and as dwelling place. பாதியால் (by the half) may refer to Shakti as Siva's half, through whom his command works. பின்னாம் அடியார் may be later devotees or those who follow.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pathi","adiyaar","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:203"},"t:2673":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2673,"t":["அம்பர நாதன் அகலிடம் நீள்பொழில்","தம்பர மல்லது தாமறியோம் என்பர்","உம்பருள் வானவர் தானவர் கண்டிலர்","எம்பெரு மான்அருள் பெற்றிருந் தாரே."],"p":"","r":"Of the Lord of space, the wide earth and its long groves, they say: we know nothing but that they are his; the gods in heaven and the asuras did not see him, but those who received our Lord's grace remained in it.","n":"Line 2 is compressed: தம்பரம் அல்லது may be 'other than his supremacy' or 'other than his own'. The contrast between the gods who did not see and the graced who did is a common Saiva turn.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","sivam","adiyaar"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:203"},"t:2674":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2674,"t":["கோவணங் கும்படி கோவண மாகிப்பின்","நாவணங் கும்படி நந்தி அருள்செய்தான்","தேவணங் கோம்இனிச் சித்தம் தெளிந்தனம்","போய்வணங் கும்பொரு ளாயிருந் தோமே."],"p":"","r":"Taking the loincloth so that kings would bow, and then by Nandi's grace so that tongues would bow too, we will bow to gods no more: our mind is clear, and we have become what others go to worship.","n":"The verse puns: கோ வணங்கும் (kings bow) and கோவணம் (the loincloth), நா வணங்கும் (tongues bow), தே வணங்கோம் (we will not bow to gods). Whether the loincloth is Nandi's or the speaker's own renunciant garb is not said. The boast that the graced become an object of worship is strong and is offered as the fruit of the guru's grace.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","thiruvarul","vesham","thelivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:203"},"t:2675":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2675,"t":["தூலப் பிரணவம் சொரூபானந்தப் பேருரை","பாலித்த சூக்கும மேலைப் சொரூபப்பெண்","சூலித்த முத்திரை ஆங்கதிற்காரணம்","மேலைப் பிரணவம் வேதாந்த வீதியே."],"p":"","r":"The gross pranava is the great word of the bliss of one's own form; the subtle that sustains it is the woman of the higher form; the seal she bore is its cause there; and the higher pranava is the street of Vedanta.","n":"தூல (gross), சூக்கும (subtle) and மேலை (higher) pranava are grades of Om in mantra theory. The woman (பெண்) is taken as Shakti and சூலித்த முத்திரை (the seal she conceived) is obscure. The verse ends by placing the highest Om on the road of Vedanta.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piranavam","saththi","muththirai","vedaantham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:204"},"t:2676":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2676,"t":["ஓம்எனும் ஓங்காரத் துள்ளே ஒருமொழி","ஓம்எனும் ஓங்காரத் துள்ளே உருஅரு","ஓம்எனும் ஓங்காரத் துள்ளே பலபேதம்","ஓம்எனும் ஓங்காரம் ஒண்முத்தி சித்தியே."],"p":"","r":"Within the omkara called Om is the one word; within it are form and the formless; within it are many distinctions; and the omkara called Om is bright release and attainment.","n":"ஓம்எனும் ஓங்காரம் opens every line. ஒருமொழி (the one word) may be Om itself as the single source of speech, or the great saying of identity. உருஅரு (form and the formless) is a standard pair for the manifest and unmanifest Lord.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piranavam","veedu","siththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:204"},"t:2677":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2677,"t":["ஓங்காரத் துள்ளே உதித்தஐம் பூதங்கள்","ஓங்காரத் துள்ளே உதித்த சராசரம்","ஓங்கார தீதத்து உயிர்மூன்றும் உற்றன","ஓங்கார சீவ பரசிவ ரூபமே."],"p":"","r":"The five elements arose within omkara, the moving and unmoving arose within it, beyond omkara the three kinds of souls came to be, and omkara is the form of both soul and Parasiva.","n":"உயிர் மூன்று is traditionally the three classes of souls by their malams (vijnanakala, pralayakala, sakala). ஓங்காரா தீதத்து (beyond omkara) sits oddly between lines that place everything within it; it may mean the souls stand at Om's further edge.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piranavam","thaththuvam","pasu","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:204"},"t:2678":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2678,"t":["வருக்கம் சுகமாம் பிரமமும் ஆகும்","அருக்கம் சராசரம் ஆகும் உலகில்","தருக்கிய ஆதாரம் எல்லாம்தன் மேனி","சுருக்கம்இல் ஞானம் தொகுத் துணர்ந் தோரே."],"p":"","r":"The classes of letters become bliss and Brahman, the sun becomes the moving and unmoving in the world, and all the supports reasoned out are his body: so those who gathered and grasped knowledge without lack have known.","n":"வருக்கம் (class of letters) and அருக்கம் (the sun, or the small) are obscure here, and சுகமாம் may be 'is bliss' or a name. I take the verse as listing what Om becomes; the reading is tentative.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piranavam","aadhaaram","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:204"},"t:2679":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2679,"t":["மலையும் மனோபவம் மருள்வன ஆவன","நிலையில் தரிசனம் தீப நெறியாம்","தலமும் குலமும் தவம்சித்த மாகும்","நலமும்சன் மார்க்கத்து உபதேசம் தானே."],"p":"","r":"The mountain-like confusions born of the mind, the steady vision that is the way of the lamp, the holy place, lineage and austerity made one's own, and the good: all this is the teaching of the true path.","n":"மனோபவம் (mind-born) can also be a name of Kama, and மலையும் may be 'mountain' or 'that confuse'. தீப நெறி (the way of the lamp) may be the inner light of meditation. The list is loosely joined, and whether the confusions are to be taught or removed is not said.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sanmaarkkam","manam","thavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:204"},"t:2680":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2680,"t":["சோடச மார்க்கமும் சொல்லும்சன்மார்க்கிகட்கு","ஆடிய ஈராறின் அந்தமும் ஈரேழிற்","கூடிய அந்தமும் கோதண்ட மும்கடந்து","ஏறியே ஞானஞே யாந்தத்து இருக்கவே."],"p":"","r":"For the sanmargis, who speak of the sixteen-fold path, passing beyond the end of the twelve that moves, the end joined in the fourteen, and the bow, they climb and abide at the end of knowledge and the known.","n":"ஈராறு (twelve) and ஈரேழ் (fourteen) are numbered stages, perhaps kalas of the moon and sun or finger-breadths of breath; கோதண்டம் (the bow) is traditionally the brow region in the yogic body. ஞானஞேயாந்தம் is the end where knowledge and its object no longer differ.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sanmaarkkam","gnaanam","kalai","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:204"},"t:2681":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2681,"t":["ஒளியை அறியில் உருவும் ஒளியும்","ஒளியும் உருவம் அறியில் உருவாம்","ஒளியின் உருவம் அறியில் ஒளியே","ஒளியும் உருக உடனிருந் தானே."],"p":"","r":"If one knows the light, there are form and light; if one knows light as form, it is form; if one knows the form of the light, it is light alone; and he stayed with one as the light itself melted.","n":"A riddle built on ஒளி and உரு (form). It is commonly taken to say that the Lord is known first as form and light, then as light alone. ஒளியும் உருக (the light melting) may also be read as the light and the knower dissolving together.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oli","sivam","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:205"},"t:2682":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2682,"t":["புகல்எளி தாகும் புவனங்கள் எட்டும்","அகல்ஒளி தாய்இருள் ஆசற வீசும்","பகல்ஒளி செய்தும் அத்தா மரையிலே","இகல்ஒளி செய்துஎம் பிரான்இருந் தானே."],"p":"","r":"The eight worlds become easy to reach, the wide light spreads so that darkness leaves without trace, and making the light of day in that lotus, our Lord stayed there shining with a light that outdoes all.","n":"அத்தாமரை (that lotus) is commonly taken as the heart lotus, so the light of the worlds is also the light within. இகல் ஒளி is a light that rivals or surpasses.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oli","sivam","andam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:205"},"t:2683":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2683,"t":["விளங்கொளி அங்கி விரிகதிர் சோமன்","துளங்கொளி பெற்றன சோதி யருள","வளங்கொளி பெற்றதே பேரொளி வேறு","களங்கொளி செய்து கலந்து நின்றானே."],"p":"","r":"Fire with its bright light, the sun of spreading rays and the moon received their wavering light by the grace of that light; the rich light they received is the great light, which is other, and making light in the throat he stayed mingled with them.","n":"விரிகதிர் சோமன் may be 'the sun with spreading rays and the moon' or only 'the moon of spreading rays'. களங்கொளி may be the light of Siva's dark throat or simply 'light in its place'. The verse distinguishes the great light from the lights it lends.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oli","sivam","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:205"},"t:2684":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2684,"t":["இளங்கொளி ஈசன் பிறப்பொன்றும் இல்லி","துளங்கொளி ஞாயிறும் திங்களும் கண்கள்","வளங்கொளி அங்கியும் மற்றைக்கண் நெற்றி","விளங்கொளி செய்கின்ற மெய்காய மாமே."],"p":"","r":"Isan of young light has no birth at all; the wavering-lit sun and moon are his eyes, the rich-lit fire is the other eye in his forehead, and this is the true body that makes light shine.","n":"Sun, moon and fire as Siva's three eyes is standard Saiva iconography. மெய்காயம் (the true body) may be the Lord's body of light, or the yogi's body in which sun, moon and fire are the breaths and the inner fire.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oli","sivam","aadhiththan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:205"},"t:2685":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2685,"t":["மேல்ஒளி கீழ்அதன் மேவிய மாருதம்","பால்ஒளி அங்கி பரந்தொளி ஆகாசம்","நீர்ஒளி செய்து நெடுவிசும்பு ஒன்றிலும்","மேல்ஒளி ஐந்தும் ஒருங்கொளி யாமே."],"p":"","r":"Light above, and below it the wind that dwells there; fire of milky light; space spread out as light; water making light; in the one long sky the five lights above become one light.","n":"The five are the elements, each called a light, gathered into one; earth is not named unless மேல் ஒளி (light above) stands for it. The order and grouping are unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oli","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:205"},"t:2686":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2686,"t":["மின்னிய தூவொளி மேதக்க செவ்வொளி","பன்னிய ஞானம் பரந்து பரத்தொளி","துன்னிய ஆறுஒளி தூய்மொழி நாடொறும்","உன்னிய வாறுஒளி ஒத்தது தானே."],"p":"","r":"The flashing pure light, the excellent red light, knowledge spoken of spreading as the supreme light, the six lights joined: by pure words said daily, the light matches the way one has contemplated it.","n":"The six lights (ஆறு ஒளி) may be the lights of the six adharas or the colours named in the verse; they are not listed. தூய்மொழி (pure word) is taken as mantra. The last line says the light answers to meditation.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oli","gnaanam","mandhiram"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:205"},"t:2687":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2687,"t":["விளங்கொளி மின்னொளி யாகிக் கரந்து","துளங்கொளி ஈசனைச் சொல்லும்எப் போதும்","உளங்கொளி ஊனிடை நின்றுயிர்க் கின்ற","வளங்கொளி எங்கும் மருவிநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"The shining light, hiding itself as the light of lightning, the wavering light, always speaks of Isan; the light in the heart, standing in the flesh, gives life, and as that rich light he pervades everything.","n":"The rhyming light-words make the syntax loose: whether விளங்கொளி, துளங்கொளி and வளங்கொளி are three lights or one under three names is not clear. The verse places the life-giving light inside the body.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oli","sivam","uyir","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:205"},"t:2688":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2688,"t":["விளங்கொளி அவ்வொளி அவ்விருள் மன்னும்","துளங்கொளி யான்தொழு வார்க்கும் ஒளியான்","அளங்கொளி ஆரமு தாகநஞ் சாரும்","களங்கொளி ஈசன் கருத்தது தானே."],"p":"","r":"That shining light and that darkness both abide in him; he is the wavering light and the light even of those who worship; to take as nectar the poison that came from the salt sea is the intent of Isan with the shining throat.","n":"The last two lines recall Siva swallowing the poison from the churned sea, which stayed in his throat. அளங்கொளி ஆரமுதாக நஞ்சு ஆரும் can be read in several ways; I take அளம் as the salt sea. That light and darkness both abide in him is said, not explained.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oli","sivam","adiyaar"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:205"},"t:2689":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2689,"t":["இயலங்கியது எவ்வொளி அவ்வொளி ஈசன்","துலங்கொளி போல்வது தூங்கருட் சத்தி","விளங்கொளி மூன்றே விரிசுடர் தோன்றி","உளங்கொளி யுள்ளே ஒருங்கிகின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"Whatever light moves is the light of Isan; what is like a shining light is the abiding Shakti of grace; three shining lights appear as spreading flames, and within the heart's light he gathers into one.","n":"The three lights are taken as sun, moon and fire, as in 2684. தூங்கருட் சத்தி may be the grace-Shakti who abides or who hangs over all. The last word ஒருங்கிகின்றானே looks corrupt (see source_issues).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oli","saththi","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:205"},"t:2690":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2690,"t":["உலங்கொளி யாவதுஎன் உள்நின்ற சீவன்","வளங்கொளி யாய்நின்ற மாமணிச் சோதி","விளங்கொளி யாய்மின்னி விண்ணில் ஒடுங்கி","வளங்கொளி ஆயத்து ளாகிநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"The light within me is the soul that stands there; the great gem's flame that stood as rich light, flashing as shining light and subsiding into the sky, he stood as rich light in the gathering.","n":"உலங்கொளி is unclear: perhaps உளங்கொளி, the heart's light, as in 2689. மாமணிச் சோதி (the great gem's flame) is the Lord. ஆயத்துள் (in the gathering, or in what has come to be) is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oli","uyir","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:205"},"t:2691":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2691,"t":["விளங்கொளி யாய்நின்ற விகிர்தன் இருந்த","துளங்கொளி பாசத்துள் தூங்கிருள் சேராக்","களங்கிருள் நட்டமே கண்ணுதல் ஆட","விளங்கொளி உள்மனத்து ஒன்றிநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"The strange one who stands as shining light dwells where the hanging darkness does not reach within the wavering light and its bonds; as the brow-eyed one dances the dance of darkness, he stands as one with the shining light in the inner mind.","n":"விகிர்தன் (the strange or different one) and கண்ணுதல் (forehead-eyed) are names of Siva. களங்கிருள் நட்டம் may be the dance in the dark of dissolution. The syntax of line 2 is loose.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oli","thirukkoothu","paasam","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:205"},"t:2692":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2692,"t":["போது கருங்குழற் போனவர் தூதிடை","ஆதி பரத்தை அமரர் பிரானொடும்","சோதியும் அண்டத்துஅப் பாலுற்ற தூவொளி","நீதியின் நல்லிருள் நீக்கிய வாறே."],"p":"","r":"In the message carried to her of the flowered dark tresses, the primal supreme with the Lord of the immortals, the light and the pure light beyond the cosmic egg: this is how, rightly, the good darkness was removed.","n":"The verse is very obscure. போனவர் தூது (the message of the one who went) and பரத்தை might recall a story of the Lord going as a messenger to a woman, but that would be a guess and I do not adopt it. நல்லிருள் (good darkness) is a puzzling phrase.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oli","andam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:205"},"t:2693":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2693,"t":["உண்டில்லை என்னும் உலகத்து இயல்பிது","பண்டில்லை என்னும் பரங்கதி யுண்டுகொல்","கண்டில்லை மானுடர் கண்ட கருத்துறில்","விண்டில்லை உள்ளே விளக்கொளி யாமே."],"p":"","r":"Saying 'it is' and 'it is not' is the way of this world; is there a supreme goal of which it is said 'it was not before'? People have not seen it; if they come to the meaning seen, there is no gap, and within it is the lamp's light.","n":"The verse sets the world's arguments of existence and non-existence against an inner seeing that leaves no gap. விண்டு இல்லை may be 'no split' or 'not spoken'. Who has 'seen the meaning' (கண்ட கருத்து) is not said: perhaps the sages.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oli","meyyunarvu","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:205"},"t:2694":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2694,"t":["சுடருற ஒங்கிய ஒள்ளொளி ஆங்கே","படருறு காட்சிப் பகலவன் ஈசன்","அடருறு மாயையின் ஆரிருள் வீசில்","உடலுறு ஞாலத் துறவியின் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"The bright light that rose to touch the flame is there, Isan, the sun of a vision that spreads; if the dense darkness of maya is cast off, it comes by the renunciation of the world that clings to the body.","n":"The last line can also be read as 'it becomes the renunciant's, in this embodied world'. The sun here is an image of the Lord's light dispelling maya's darkness.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oli","maayai","thuravu","aadhiththan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:205"},"t:2695":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2695,"t":["ஒளி பவ ளத்திரு மேனிவெண் ணீற்றன்","அளிபவ ளச்சொம்பொன் ஆதிப் பிரானும்","களிபவ ளத்தினன் காரிருள் நீங்கி","ஒளிபவ ளத்தென்னோடு ஈசன் நின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"The first Lord, with a holy body of shining coral and white ash, gracious coral and fine gold, of coral joy: the black darkness gone, Isan stood with me in coral light.","n":"பவளம் (coral, red) recurs in every line for Siva's red body under white ash. The verse ends in the first person: the light stands with the speaker.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oli","sivam","thiruneeru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:205"},"t:2696":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2696,"t":["ஈசன்நின் றான்இமை யோர்கள் நின் றார்நின்ற","தேசம்ஒன் றின்றித் திகைத்துஇழைக் கின்றனர்","பாசம்ஒன் றாகப் பழவினை பற்றற","வாசம்ஒன் றாமலர் போன்றது தானே."],"p":"","r":"Isan stood, the gods stood, and with no ground to stand on they are bewildered and wear away; when the bonds become one and old karma's hold ends, it is like a flower and its single fragrance.","n":"பாசம் ஒன்றாக (the bonds becoming one) is unclear: perhaps the bonds gathering into one and falling away together. The flower and its single scent is an image of the soul and the Lord inseparable.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","paasam","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:205"},"t:2697":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2697,"t":["தானே யிருக்கும் அவற்றில் தலைவனும்","தானே யிருக்கும் அவனென நண்ணிடும்","வானாய் இருக்கும்இம் மாயிரு ஞாலத்துப்","பானாய் இருக்கப் பரவலும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"He alone is, the chief among them; approach him as the one who alone is; he is like the sky, and in this vast dark world, praising him is possible as he stands as light.","n":"பானாய் may be 'as light' (from பா, light, or பானு, the sun) or 'as a vessel'. மாயிரு ஞாலம் may be the great dark world or simply the great wide world.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","vazhipaadu","oli"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:205"},"t:2698":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2698,"t":["ஐம்பது எழுத்தே அனைத்துவே தங்களும்","ஐம்பது எழுத்தே அனைத்துஆக மங்களும்","ஐம்பது எழுத்தின் அடைவை அறிந்தபின்","ஐம்பது எழுத்தே அஞ்செழுத் தாமே."],"p":"","r":"The fifty letters are all the Vedas, the fifty letters are all the Agamas, and once one knows the order of the fifty letters, the fifty letters are the five letters.","n":"ஐம்பது எழுத்து is the full alphabet of mantra theory (fifty or fifty-one letters). அடைவு (order, arrangement) suggests the letters set out in a diagram, as in the earlier chakra chapters. The claim is that all scripture reduces to the five letters.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panchaakkaram","mandhiram","vedham","aagamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:206"},"t:2699":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2699,"t":["அகார முதலாக ஐம்பத்தொன்று ஆகி","உகார முதலாக ஓங்கி உதித்து","மகார இறுதியாய் மாய்ந்துமாய்ந்து ஏறி","நகார முதலாகும் நந்திதன் நாமமே."],"p":"","r":"Beginning with A it becomes fifty-one, rising from U, ending in M and dying away again and again as it climbs; Nandi's name begins with Na.","n":"A, U and M are the parts of Om, and the verse seems to trace speech from A through Om to its dying away, then to the five letters beginning with ந (நமசிவாய). மாய்ந்துமாய்ந்து ஏறி may describe the sound fading as it rises in the body.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panchaakkaram","piranavam","mandhiram"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:206"},"t:2700":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2700,"t":["அகராதி ஈரெண் கலந்த பரையும்","உகராதி தன்சத்தி உள்ளொளி ஈசன்","சிகராதி தான்சிவ வேதமே கோணம்","நகராதி தான்மூலமந்திரம் நண்ணுமே."],"p":"","r":"Para joined with the sixteen from A; Isan, the inner light, with his Shakti from U; Siva from Si, the Veda and the triangle; and from Na the root mantra is reached.","n":"ஈரெண் (the sixteen) are taken as the sixteen vowels from அ. கோணம் (angle, triangle) may be a diagram in which the letters are set. மூலமந்திரம் (root mantra) is taken as the five letters beginning with ந. The correspondences are technical and I can't fix them.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panchaakkaram","saththi","mandhiram","chakkaram"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:206"},"t:2701":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2701,"t":["வாயொடு கண்டம் இதயம் மருவுந்தி","ஆய இலிங்கம் அவற்றின்மேல் அவ்வாய்த்","தூயதோர் துண்டம் இருமத் தகம்செல்லல்","ஆயதுஈ றாம்ஐந்தோடு ஆம்எழுத்து அஞ்சுமே."],"p":"","r":"Mouth, throat, heart and navel, then the linga, above them that opening, a pure segment, and going to the crown of the head: these are the places, five with five, of the five letters.","n":"The verse maps the five letters onto places in the body, some of them the adharas. இலிங்கம் here is taken as the genital centre. அவ்வாய் and தூயதோர் துண்டம் are obscure, and ஈறாம் ஐந்தோடு ஆம் may mean the five places with five more, or the five letters at the five ends.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panchaakkaram","aadhaaram","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:206"},"t:2702":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2702,"t":["கிரணங்கள் ஏழும் கிளர்ந்தெரி பொங்கிக்","கரணங்கள் விட்டுயிர் தானெழும் போது","மரணம்கை வைத்துஉயிர் மாற்றிடும் போதும்","அரணம்கை கூட்டுவது அஞ்செழுத் தாமே."],"p":"","r":"When the seven rays blaze up and the soul rises leaving its instruments, and when Death lays hand on it to change its life, it is the five letters that bring protection.","n":"The seven rays (கிரணங்கள் ஏழும்) are unclear: perhaps the seven bodily elements burning up at death, or seven inner lights. கரணங்கள் are the instruments of the soul (mind and the rest). அரணம் is the fortress or shelter the mantra gives.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panchaakkaram","kootru","uyir","aran"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:206"},"t:2703":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2703,"t":["ஞாயிறு திங்கள் நவின்றெழு காலத்தில்","ஆயுறு மந்திரம் ஆரும் அறிகிலார்","சேயுறு கண்ணி திருஎழுத்து அஞ்சையும்","வாயுறு ஓதி வழுத்தலும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"At the time when sun and moon rise and are spoken of, no one knows the fitting mantra; but one can recite with the mouth and praise the holy five letters of the red-eyed one.","n":"Sun and moon are traditionally the right and left breaths, so the first line may refer to the moments of breath rather than the sky. சேயுறு கண்ணி (she of red or distant eyes) may be Shakti, whose letters these are. The verse sets plain recitation against hidden lore.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panchaakkaram","praanan","mandhiram"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:206"},"t:2704":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2704,"t":["தெள்ளமுது ஊறச் சிவாய நமஎன்று","உள்ளமுது ஊற ஒருகால் உரைத்திடும்","வெள்ளமுது ஊறல் விரும்பிஉண் ணாதவர்","துள்ளிய நீர்போல் சுழல்கின்ற வாறே."],"p":"","r":"Say 'Sivaya nama' once, so that clear nectar wells up and nectar wells in the heart; those who do not wish to drink that welling flood of nectar whirl about like splashing water.","n":"சிவாய நம is one of the orders of the five letters. The welling nectar can be read devotionally as the sweetness of the name or yogically as the amrita that flows from the head. The whirling of those who refuse is commonly taken as the round of births.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panchaakkaram","amudham","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:206"},"t:2705":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2705,"t":["குருவழி யாய குணங்களில் நின்று","கருவழி யாய கணக்கை அறுக்க","வரும்வழி மாள மறுக்கவல் லார்கட்கு","அருள்வழி காட்டுவது அஞ்செழுத் தாமே."],"p":"","r":"For those who, standing in the qualities that are the guru's way, can cut the reckoning that is the womb's way and refuse so that the coming way dies, it is the five letters that show the way of grace.","n":"The verse plays on வழி (way) four times: the guru's way, the womb's way, the way of coming (into birth) and the way of grace. கணக்கு (the reckoning) is the account of karma that leads to rebirth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panchaakkaram","guru","piravi","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:206"},"t:2706":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2706,"t":["வெறிக்க வினைத்துயிர் வந்திடும் போது","செறிக்கின்ற நந்தி திருஎழுத்து ஓதும்","குறிப்பது உன்னில் குரைகழல் கூட்டும்","குறிப்பறி வான்தவம் கோன்உரு வாமே."],"p":"","r":"When the soul comes, stunned by the frenzy of karma, recite Nandi's holy letters, which press close; if you dwell on what they point to, they join you to his ringing anklets, and one who knows that pointing takes the form of the king of austerity.","n":"வெறிக்க வினைத்து is obscure: perhaps the soul dazed by karma, or coming into birth by karma. குறிப்பு (what is pointed to) is the meaning behind the letters. தவம் கோன் உரு may mean Siva's form or the form of a great ascetic.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panchaakkaram","vinai","thiruvadi","thavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:206"},"t:2707":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2707,"t":["நெஞ்சு நினைந்துதம் வாயாற் பிரான்என்று","துஞ்சும் பொழுதுன் துணைத்தாள் சரண்என்று","மஞ்சு தவழும் வடவரை மீதுரை","அஞ்சில் இறைவன் அருள்பெற லாமே."],"p":"","r":"Thinking in the heart, saying 'Lord' with one's mouth, and at the time of sleep saying 'your paired feet are my refuge', one can win the grace of the Lord of the five, who dwells on the northern mountain where clouds crawl.","n":"துஞ்சும் பொழுது may be the time of sleep or of death; in this chapter, after 2702, death is likely. அஞ்சில் இறைவன் may be the Lord in the five letters or the Lord of the five elements. The northern mountain is Kailasa.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","thiruvadi","panchaakkaram","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:206"},"t:2708":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2708,"t":["பிரான்வைத்த ஐந்தின் பெருமை யுணராது","இராமாற்றம் செய்வார்கொல் ஏழை மனிதர்","பராமுற்றும் கீழோடு பல்வகை யாலும்","அராமுற்றும் சூழ்ந்த அகலிடம் தானே."],"p":"","r":"Not grasping the greatness of the five the Lord set down, will poor people go on with their empty talk? The wide earth, above and below and in every way, is wholly encircled by the serpent.","n":"இராமாற்றம் is obscure: it may be 'talk of the night' or 'unsteady words'. The last two lines may mean that the earth is bound by the serpent (Adisesha), or by the serpent of bondage; how they answer the first two is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panchaakkaram","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:206"},"t:2709":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2709,"t":["எளிய வாதுசெய் வார்எங்கள் ஈசனை","ஒளியை உன்னி உருகும் மனத்தராய்த்","தெளிய ஒதிச்சிவாயநம என்னும்","குளிகை யிட்டுப் பொன் னாக்குவன் கூட்டையே."],"p":"","r":"For those whose minds melt as they contemplate the Lord as light and who clearly recite சிவாயநம, the mantra works as an alchemist's pill and he turns their bodily cage to gold.","n":"குளிகை is the alchemist's pill that turns base metal to gold, and கூடு (the cage, the nest) is the body; the image may mean a body made immortal or simply made holy. Line 1 (எளிய வாதுசெய்வார்) is hard to place: it may set those who argue lightly about the Lord against those who melt, or say the Lord is easy for those who melt.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panchaakkaram","mandhiram","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:207"},"t:2710":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2710,"t":["சிவன்சத்தி சீவன் செறுமல மாயை","அவஞ்சேர்த்த பாச மலம்ஐந்து அகலச்","சிவன்சத்தி தன்னுடன் சீவனார் சேர","அவம்சேர்த்த பாசம் அணுககி லாவே."],"p":"","r":"Siva, Shakti, the soul, the choking impurity and maya: when the five bonds that bring ruin depart and the soul joins Siva with his Shakti, those bonds can no longer come near.","n":"The verse does not name the letters, but the five it lists are traditionally the meanings of the five letters (சி Siva, வா Shakti, ய the soul, ம impurity, ந concealment or maya). பாச மலம் ஐந்து may be 'the five impure bonds' or 'the bonds and impurity of the five'; the count is unclear, since only the last two are bonds.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panchaakkaram","paasam","malam","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:207"},"t:2711":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2711,"t":["சிவன்அரு ளாய சிவன்திரு நாமம்","சிவன்அருள் ஆன்மா திரோதம் மலமாயை","சிவன்முத லாகச் சிறந்து நிரோதம்","பவமது அகன்று பரசிவன் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"Siva's holy name is his grace, and holds Siva, grace, the soul, concealment and impurity with maya; when Siva stands first and the rest are stilled, birth departs and the soul becomes the supreme Siva.","n":"திரோதம் is concealment, the Lord's veiling power, and நிரோதம் restraint or cessation; the verse seems to play the two against each other. The reading of the five as the letters of the name is the tradition's; சிவன் முதலாக (with Siva first) may refer to the order beginning with சி.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panchaakkaram","thiruvarul","piravi","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:207"},"t:2712":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2712,"t":["ஓதிய நம்மலம் எல்லாம் ஒழித்திட்டு அவ்","ஆதி தனைவிட்டு இறையருள் சத்தியால்","தீதில் சிவஞான யோகமே சித்திக்கும்","ஓதும் சிவாயமலமற்ற உண்மையே."],"p":"","r":"When the impurity called நம is wholly removed and that beginning left behind, by the power of the Lord's grace the faultless yoga of Siva-knowledge is attained; what one then chants is சிவாய, the truth free of impurity.","n":"நம்மலம் is read as நம + மலம், the syllables நம taken as the bonds, so that dropping the opening syllables of நமசிவாய leaves the subtle form சிவாய. It could also be read 'our impurity'. அவ் ஆதி (that beginning) is most naturally those first syllables.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panchaakkaram","malam","gnaanam","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:207"},"t:2713":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2713,"t":["நமாதி நனாதி திரோதாயி யாகித்","தம்ஆதிய தாய்நிற்கத் தான்அந்தத் துற்றுச்","சமாதித் துரியம் தமதுஆகம் ஆகவே","நமாதி சமாதி சிவமாதல் எண்ணவே."],"p":"","r":"Beginning with ந and with waking, concealment acts; when the soul stands as its own origin and reaches the end, samadhi and the fourth state become its own body, and then one may think of the syllables from ந, and samadhi, as becoming Siva.","n":"Very compressed. நமாதி (from ந-ம) and நனாதி (from waking, நனவு) are set side by side, perhaps matching the letters to the states of consciousness; துரியம் is the fourth state. The syntax of lines 2 and 4 is my guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panchaakkaram","avaththai","samaadhi","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:207"},"t:2714":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2714,"t":["அருள்தரும் ஆயமும் அத்தனும் தம்மில்","ஒருவனை ஈன்றவர் உள்ளுறும் மாயை","திரிமலம் நீங்கிச் சிவாயஎன்று ஓதும்","அருவினை தீர்fப்பதும் அவ்வெழுத் தாமே."],"p":"","r":"The grace-giving mother and the father who together bore the one: when the maya within and the three impurities go and one chants சிவாய, it is those letters that end hard-to-remove karma.","n":"ஆயம் is taken here as the mother (Shakti) beside அத்தன், the father (Siva), and ஒருவனை ஈன்றவர் as their bringing forth the soul or a son; the line may also mean the mother and father of the devotee. திரிமலம் is the three impurities.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["panchaakkaram","vinai","malam","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:207"},"t:2715":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2715,"t":["சிவசிவ என்றே தெளிகிலர் ஊமர்","சிவசிவ வாயுவும் தேர்ந்துள் அடங்கச்","சிவசிவ ஆய தெளிவின் உள் ளார்கள்","சிவசிவ ஆகும் திருவருள் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"Those who do not come to clarity by saying Siva Siva are mutes; when the breath too, as Siva Siva, is understood and stilled within, those who stand in the clarity of Siva Siva find that Siva Siva becomes holy grace.","n":"ஊமர் (the dumb) are those who have tongues but do not use them for the name. சிவசிவ வாயுவும் may mean chanting matched with the breath, a yogic practice, or the breath itself becoming the name; I can't fix which.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panchaakkaram","praanan","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:207"},"t:2716":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2716,"t":["சிவசிவ என்கிலர் தீவினை யாளர்","சிவசிவ என்றிடத் தீவினை மாளும்","சிவசிவ என்றிடத் தேவரும் ஆவர்","சிவசிவ என்னச் சிவகதி தானே."],"p":"","r":"Those of evil deeds do not say Siva Siva; when one says Siva Siva evil deeds die, one becomes divine, and the state of Siva itself follows.","n":"The plainest verse of the chapter, built on fourfold repetition. It runs both ways: evil-doers do not say the name, and saying it ends evil deeds. தேவரும் ஆவர் is 'they become gods' or 'even the gods become (so)'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panchaakkaram","vinai","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:207"},"t:2717":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2717,"t":["நவமென்னும் நாமத்தை நாவில் ஒடுக்கிச்","சிவமென்னும் நாமத்தைச் சிந்தையுள் ஏற்றப்","பவமது தீரும் பரிசும்அது அற்றால்","அவதி தீரும் அறும்பிறப்பு அன்றோ."],"p":"","r":"Hold the name நவம் still on the tongue and raise the name சிவம் in the mind, and birth ends; when even that touch is gone, the limit ends, and is not birth cut off?","n":"நவம் may stand for நம, so that the bonds' syllables are stilled on the tongue while Siva rises in thought; it can also mean 'new'. பரிசு (touch, or manner) and அவதி (limit, or affliction) are uncertain; the sense seems to be that even the act of chanting is finally left behind.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panchaakkaram","piravi","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:207"},"t:2718":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2718,"t":["சிவாய நமவெனச் சித்தம் ஒருக்கி","அவாயம் அறவே அடிமைய தாக்கிச்","சிவாய சிவசிவ என்றென்றே சிந்தை","அவாயம் கெடநிற்க ஆனந்தம் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"Fixing the mind with சிவாய நம and making oneself wholly a servant so that harm is gone, then thinking சிவாய சிவசிவ again and again, the mind stands with its harm destroyed, and that is bliss.","n":"The verse moves from the full mantra (சிவாய நம) to its subtle forms (சிவாய, சிவசிவ). அவாயம் is apaya, harm or danger, and also echoes அவா, desire, so 'harm' may be the craving that troubles the mind.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panchaakkaram","aanandham","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:208"},"t:2719":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2719,"t":["செஞ்சுடர் மண்டலத்து ஊடுசென்று அப்புறம்","அஞ்சண வும்முறை ஏறிவழிக் கொண்டு","துஞ்சும் அவன்சொன்ன காலத்து இறைவனை","நெஞ்சென நீங்கா நிலைபெற லாகுமே."],"p":"","r":"Passing through the orb of red flame and beyond it, climbing by the five in their order and taking the way, at the time he spoke of one may attain the state in which the Lord never leaves, as one's own heart.","n":"செஞ்சுடர் மண்டலம் (the orb of red flame) is traditionally a centre in the yogic body, the sun's or fire's region; அஞ்சு அணவும் முறை may be the five letters placed in order along the body or the five states. துஞ்சும் அவன் சொன்ன காலத்து is obscure: துஞ்சு can mean sleep or death, so it may be the time of death the Lord appointed or the yogic sleep.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panchaakkaram","yogam","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:208"},"t:2720":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2720,"t":["அங்கமும் ஆகம வேதமது ஓதினும்","எங்கள் பிரான்எழுத்து ஒன்றில் இருப்பது","சங்கைகெட்டு அவ்எழுத்து ஒன்றையும் சாதித்தால்","அங்கரை சேர்ந்த அருங்கலம் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"Though one recites the Vedas with their limbs and the Agamas, what is ours lies in one letter of our Lord; master that one letter with doubt destroyed and one becomes a precious vessel that has reached the farther shore.","n":"அங்கம் are the six limbs of the Veda. The one letter is not named; tradition takes it as சி or as the five letters held as one, or as Om. அருங்கலம் can be a rare ship or a rare jewel; with அங்கரை (that shore) the ship fits better, with the shore as the end of births.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panchaakkaram","vedham","aagamam","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:208"},"t:2721":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2721,"t":["பழுத்தன ஐந்தும் பழமறை யுள்ளே","விழித்துஅங்கு உறங்கும் வினைஅறி வாரில்லை","எழுத்துஅறி வோம்என்று உரைப்பார்கள் ஏதர்","எழுத்தை அழுத்தும் எழுத்துஅறி யாரே."],"p":"","r":"The five that ripened lie within the ancient Veda; none know the karma that wakes there and sleeps; fools say 'we know the letters', but they do not know the letter that masters the letters.","n":"பழுத்தன ஐந்தும் பழமறையுள்ளே is traditionally taken as the five letters set at the centre of the Veda (in the Sri Rudram of the Yajur Veda). Line 2 is obscure: விழித்து அங்கு உறங்கும் வினை may be karma that seems to sleep but wakes. எழுத்தை அழுத்தும் எழுத்து (the letter that presses down the letters) plays on letters as script and as mantra.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panchaakkaram","vedham","pedhaimai","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:208"},"t:2722":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2722,"t":["எங்கும் திருமேனி எங்கும் சிவசத்தி","எங்கும் சிதம்பரம் எங்கும் திருநட்டம்","எங்கும் சிவமாய் இருத்தலால் எங்கெங்கும்","தங்கும் சிவனருள் தன்விளை யாட்டதே."],"p":"","r":"Everywhere is his holy form, everywhere Siva and Shakti, everywhere Chidambaram, everywhere the holy dance; because Siva is everywhere, the grace of Siva that abides everywhere is his own play.","n":"The verse opens the chapter by freeing the dance from one place: Chidambaram and the dance are wherever Siva is. விளையாட்டு (play) names the whole of his grace as sport, the idea tradition calls lila.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thirukkoothu","sivam","thiruvarul","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2723":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2723,"t":["சிற்பரஞ் சோதி சிவானந்தக் கூத்தனைச்","சொற்பத மாம்அந்தச் சுந்தரக் கூத்தனைப்","பொற்பதிக் கூத்தனைப் பொன்தில்லைக் கூத்தனை","அற்புதக் கூத்தனை யார்அறி வாரே."],"p":"","r":"Who can know the dancer of Siva's bliss, the supreme light of consciousness, the beautiful dancer who is the state of the word, the dancer of the golden city, of golden Tillai, the wondrous dancer?","n":"சொற்பதம் (the word-state) may be the state reached through words or the one beyond them; the verse does not settle it. பொற்பதி and பொன்தில்லை both name Chidambaram's golden hall.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thirukkoothu","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2724":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2724,"t":["தான்அந்தம் இல்லாச் சதானந்த சத்திமேல்","தேன்உந்தும் ஆனந்த மாநடம் கண்டீர்","ஞானம் கடந்து நடஞ்செய்யும் நம்பிக்கு அங்கு","ஆனந்தக் கூத்தாட ஆடரங்கு ஆனதே."],"p":"","r":"See the great dance of bliss, streaming honey, above the endless Shakti of eternal bliss; for the Lord who dances beyond knowledge, she became the stage for his dance of bliss.","n":"Shakti is the stage (ஆடரங்கு) and Siva the dancer: the pair is figured as dance and floor. ஞானம் கடந்து says the dance lies beyond knowledge, even liberating knowledge. தான்அந்தம் இல்லா (without end) returns as wordplay in 2796.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thirukkoothu","aanandham","saththi","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2725":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2725,"t":["ஆனந்தம் ஆடரங்கு ஆனந்தம் பாடல்கள்","ஆனந்தம் பல்லியம் ஆனந்தம் வாச்சியம்","ஆனந்தம் ஆக அகில சராசரம்","ஆனந்தம் ஆனந்தக் கூத்துஉகந் தானுக்கே."],"p":"","r":"For the one who delights in the dance of bliss, the stage is bliss, the songs are bliss, the instruments and their music are bliss, and the whole world of moving and unmoving things is bliss.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aanandham","thirukkoothu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2726":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2726,"t":["ஒளியாம் பரமாம் உளதாம் பரமும்","அளியார் சிவகாமி யாகும் சமயக்","களியார் பரமும் கருதுறை யந்தக்","தெளிவாம் சிவானந்த நட்டத்தின் சித்தியே."],"p":"","r":"Light, the Supreme, the Supreme that is, the Supreme of gracious Sivakami and of the joy of the creeds: the clarity that is reached in contemplation is the attainment of the dance of Siva's bliss.","n":"Lines 2 and 3 are broken in syntax (சமயக் களியார் பரமும் கருதுறை யந்தக்), and the reading here only joins what can be joined. சிவகாமி is the name of the goddess at Chidambaram.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thirukkoothu","aanandham","saththi","samayam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2727":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2727,"t":["ஆன நடம்ஐந்து அகள சகளத்தர்","ஆன நடமாடி ஐங்கரு மத்தாக","ஆன தொழில்அரு ளால்ஐந் தொழில்செய்தே","தேன்மொழி பாகன் திருநட மாடுமே."],"p":"","r":"The dances are five; the one who is without form and with form dances them as the five acts, and by grace doing the five acts, the consort of her of honeyed speech dances the holy dance.","n":"அகள சகளத்தர் (the formless and formed one) is Siddhanta's term for Siva as both. The five dances are matched to the five acts, which 2799 assigns one by one to parts of the dancer's body. தேன்மொழி பாகன் is Siva as sharing his body with Uma.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aindhozhil","thirukkoothu","thiruvarul","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2728":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2728,"t":["பூதாண்ட பேதாண்ட போகாண்ட யோகண்ட","மூதாண்ட முத்தாண்ட மோகாண்ட தேகாண்ட","தாகாண்ட ஐங்கரு மாத்தாண்ட தற்பரத்து","ஏகாந்த மாம்பிர மாண்டத்த என்பவே."],"p":"","r":"The worlds of the elements, of distinction, of enjoyment, of yoga, of the ancients, of release, of delusion, of the body, of thirst and of the five acts: beyond them, in the Supreme, is the solitary great Brahmanda, they say.","n":"The verse is a list of compounds in -அண்டம் whose sense is mostly guessable from their first parts; தாகாண்ட (of thirst?) and the exact force of each are uncertain. The dance is not named; the verse sets the scale for the cosmic dance of the chapter.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["andam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2729":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2729,"t":["வேதங்கள் ஆட மிகுஆ கமம் ஆடக்","கீதங்கள் ஆடக் கிளர்அண்டம் ஏழாடப்","பூதங்கள் ஆடப் புவனம் முழுதாட","நாதம்கொண் டாடினான் ஞானாந்தக் கூத்தே."],"p":"","r":"The Vedas dance, the great Agamas dance, songs dance, the seven bright worlds dance, the elements dance, the whole world dances: with nada he danced the dance at the end of knowledge.","n":"His dance sets everything else dancing; the verb ஆட runs through the verse as a refrain, and 2751, 2756, 2789 and 2791 repeat the pattern. ஞானாந்தக் கூத்து (the dance at knowledge's end) sits beside நாதாந்தம் in 2751 as names for where the dance is.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thirukkoothu","naadham","vedham","aagamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2730":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2730,"t":["பூதங்கள் ஐந்தில் பொறியில் புலன்ஐந்தில்","வேதங்கள் ஐந்தின் மிகும்ஆ கமந்தன்னில்","ஓதும் கலைகாலம் ஊழியுடன் அண்டப்","போதங்கள் ஐந்தில் புணர்ந்தாடும் சித்தனே."],"p":"","r":"The Siddha dances joined with the five elements, the senses, the five sense-objects, the five Vedas, the great Agama, the kalas that are recited, time and the aeons, and the five knowledges of the worlds.","n":"வேதங்கள் ஐந்து (five Vedas) is unusual, and அண்டப் போதங்கள் ஐந்து (five knowledges of the worlds) is obscure; both may follow the verse's run of fives rather than a fixed list. சித்தன் here names Siva.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thirukkoothu","thaththuvam","vedham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2731":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2731,"t":["தேவர் சுரர்நரர் சித்தர்வித் தியாதரர்","மூவர்கள் ஆதியின் முப்பத்து மூவர்கள்","தாபதர் சத்தர் சமயம் சராசரம்","யாவையும் ஆடிடும் எம்மிறை யாடவே."],"p":"","r":"Gods, suras, men, siddhas, vidyadharas, the three, the thirty-three from the beginning, ascetics, Shaktas, the creeds and all moving and unmoving things dance as our Lord dances.","n":"The creeds (சமயம்) are listed among the dancers: whatever their differences, they move by his movement.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thirukkoothu","samayam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2732":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2732,"t":["அண்டங்கள் ஏழினிக்கு அப்புறத்து அப்பால்","உண்டென்ற சத்தி சதாசிவத்து உச்சிமேல்","கண்டம் கரியான் கருணை திருவுருக்","கொண்டுஅங்கு உமைகாணக் கூத்துஉகந் தானே."],"p":"","r":"Beyond and beyond the seven worlds, above the crown of Shakti and Sadasiva who are said to be there, the dark-throated one took a holy form of compassion and delighted in the dance there for Uma to see.","n":"The dance is placed above even Sadasiva, the highest form in the usual Siddhanta ordering, and is done so that Uma may watch: the dance as a form taken out of compassion (கருணை திருவுரு).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thirukkoothu","andam","saththi","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2733":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2733,"t":["கொடிகட்டி பாண்டுரங் கோடுசங் காரம்","நடம் எட்டோ டு ஐந்துஆறு நாடியுள் நாடும்","திடம்உற்று ஏழும்தேவ தாருவும் தில்லை","வடம் உற்ற மாவனம் மன்னவன் தானே."],"p":"","r":"Kodukotti, Pandarangam, Kodu and Samharam, dances eight, and five and six sought within the nadis; firm and seven; the deodar forest, Tillai and the great banyan forest: he is the king of these.","n":"கொடுகொட்டி, பாண்டரங்கம் and சங்காரம் are traditionally names of Siva's dances, known also from the Silappatikaram; தேவதாரு, தில்லை and வடம் (the banyan, taken as Tiruvalangadu) are traditionally places of his dance. The numbers and the syntax of lines 2 and 3 are unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thirukkoothu","naadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2734":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2734,"t":["பரமாண்டத்து ஊடே பராசத்தி பாதம்","பரமாண்டத்து ஊடே படரொளி ஈசன்","பரமாண்டத்து ஊடே படர்தரு நாதம்","பரமாண்டத்து ஊடே பரன்நடம் ஆடுமே."],"p":"","r":"Within the supreme cosmos are the feet of Parashakti, within it the Lord of spreading light, within it the spreading nada, and within it the Supreme dances.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thirukkoothu","saththi","naadham","andam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2735":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2735,"t":["அங்குசம் என்ன எழுமார்க்கம் போதத்தில்","தங்கிய தொந்தி எனும்தாள ஒத்தினில்","சங்கரன் மூலநா டிக்குள் தரித்தாடல்","பொங்கிய காலம் புகும்போகல் இல்லையே."],"p":"","r":"With the path that rises like a goad in awakening and the beat of the rhythm called thonthi, Sankara dances held within the root nadi; when that time swells, there is no entering and no going.","n":"அங்குசம் (goad), தொந்தி and மூலநாடி are yogic terms whose exact sense is not fixed by the verse; தொந்தி may be a rhythm term or a name for a bodily centre. புகும் போகல் இல்லை is commonly taken as the breath's coming and going ceasing, and may also be read as an end of birth and death.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thirukkoothu","naadi","praanan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2736":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2736,"t":["ஆன்நந்தி யாடிபின் நவக் கூத்தாடிக்","கான்நந்தி யாடிக் கருத்தில் தரித்தாடி","மூனச் சுழுனையுள் ஆடி முடிவில்லா","ஞானத்துள் ஆடி முடித்தான் என் நாதனே."],"p":"","r":"My Lord danced as Nandi, then danced the new (or nine) dances, danced as Nandi of the forest, held it in thought and danced, danced within the threefold sushumna, danced within endless knowledge, and completed it.","n":"ஆன்நந்தி and கான்நந்தி are obscure and may be wordplay on ஆனந்தம் (bliss); நவக் கூத்து is either new or nine dances. The verse traces the dance inward, from outer forms to the central channel and then to knowledge.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thirukkoothu","naadi","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2737":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2737,"t":["சத்திகள் ஐந்தும் சிவபேதம் தான்ஐந்தும்","முத்திகள் எட்டும் முதலாம் பதம் எட்டும்","சித்திகள் எட்டும் சிவபதம் தான்எட்டும்","சுத்திகள் எட்டுஈசன் தொல்நடம் ஆடுமே."],"p":"","r":"The five Shaktis, the five distinctions of Siva, the eight releases, the eight first states, the eight attainments, the eight states of Siva and the eight purities: the Lord dances his ancient dance through these.","n":"The sets of five and eight are named without their members, and the traditional lists cannot be checked against the verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thirukkoothu","saththi","siththi","padhamukthi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2738":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2738,"t":["மேகங்கள் ஏழும் விரிகடல் தீவேழும்","தேகங்கள் சூழும் சிவபாற் கரன் ஏழும்","தாகங்கள் ஏழும் சாந்திகள் ஏழும்","ஆகின்ற நந்தி அடிக்கீழ் அடங்குமே."],"p":"","r":"The seven clouds, the seven islands of the wide sea, the bodies that surround them, the seven Siva-suns, the seven thirsts and the seven peaces: all that comes to be is contained beneath Nandi's feet.","n":"சிவபாற்கரன் (Siva-sun) and the sevens of thirst and peace are obscure. The point that holds is the last line: the whole ordered cosmos lies under the dancer's feet.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvadi","andam","aadhiththan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2739":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2739,"t":["தெற்கு வடக்குக் கிழக்குமேற்கு உச்சியில்","அற்புத மானதோர் அஞ்சு முகத்திலும்","ஒப்பில்பே ரின்பத்து உபய உபயத்துள்","தற்பரன் நின்று தனிநடம் செய்யுமே."],"p":"","r":"In the south, north, east, west and zenith, in the five wondrous faces, within the twofold pair of matchless great bliss, the Supreme stands and does his unique dance.","n":"The four directions and the zenith match the five faces of Sadasiva in Saiva iconography. உபய உபயம் (twofold of the twofold) is obscure; it may mean Siva and Shakti.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thirukkoothu","aanandham","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2740":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2740,"t":["அடிஆர் பவரே அடியவர் ஆமால்","அடியார்பொன் அம்பலத்து ஆடல்கண்டாரே","அடியார் அரனடி ஆனந்தம் கண்டோ ர்","அடியார் ஆனவர் அத்தருள் உற்றோர்."],"p":"","r":"Those who reach his feet are his devotees; devotees are those who saw the dance in the golden hall; they are those who saw bliss at Hara's feet; those who have become devotees have received the Father's grace.","n":"The verse plays on அடி (foot) and அடியார் (devotee, one of the foot): to be a devotee is to have reached the feet, and the feet are those of the dancer.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adiyaar","thiruvadi","thirukkoothu","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2741":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2741,"t":["அடங்காத என்னை அடக்கி அடிவைத்து","இடம்காண் பரானநத்தத் தேஎன்னை இட்டு","நடந்தான் செயும்நந்தி நன்ஞானக் கூத்தன்","படம்தான்செய்து உள்ளுள் படிந்திருந் தானே."],"p":"","r":"Nandi, the dancer of good knowledge, subdued me who would not be subdued, set his foot on me, placed me in the place of supreme bliss, walked on, and settled deep within me as a picture.","n":"அடிவைத்து (placing the foot) is traditionally the guru's initiation by the feet. படம்தான் செய்து (making a picture, or a cloth, or a hood) is unclear; the image may be of the dancer imprinted within. இடம்காண் பரானநத்தத்தே looks garbled for பரானந்தத்தே (in supreme bliss).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","thiruvadi","thirukkoothu","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2742":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2742,"t":["உம்பரில் கூத்தனை உத்தமக் கூத்தனைச்","செம்பொன் திருமன்றுள் சேவகக் கூத்தனைச்","சம்பந்தக் கூத்தனைத் தற்பரக் கூத்தனை","இன்புற நாடிஎன் அன்பில்வைத் தேனே."],"p":"","r":"The dancer in the heavens, the excellent dancer, the valiant dancer in the hall of red gold, the dancer of relationship, the dancer who is the Supreme: seeking him with joy, I placed him in my love.","n":"சம்பந்தக் கூத்தன் may mean the dancer who binds himself to souls; it could also allude to the saint Sambandar, which would be out of keeping with the rest of the list.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","thirukkoothu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2743":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2743,"t":["மாணிக்கக் கூத்தனை வண்தில்லைக் கூத்தனைப்","பூணுற்ற மன்றுள் புரிசடைக் கூத்தனைச்","சேணுற்ற சோதிச் சிவானந்தக் கூத்தனை","ஆணிப்பொற் கூத்தனை யாருரைப் பாரே."],"p":"","r":"Who can describe the ruby dancer, the dancer of bountiful Tillai, the dancer with twisted locks in the adorned hall, the dancer of Siva's bliss in the far light, the dancer of pure gold?","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thirukkoothu","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2744":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2744,"t":["விம்மும் வெருவும் விழும்எழும் மெய்சோரும்","தம்மையும் தாமறி யார்கள் சதுர்கெடும்","செம்மை சிறந்த திருஅம் பலக்கூத்துள்","அம்மலர்ப் பொற்பாதத்து அன்புவைப் பார்கட்கே."],"p":"","r":"Those who set their love on the beautiful golden flower-feet in the dance of the holy hall, perfect in its rightness, sob, tremble, fall, rise, grow faint in body, do not know themselves, and lose their cleverness.","n":"The signs are those of devotional rapture, and the corpus records a close parallel in the Tevaram. சதுர்கெடும் (cleverness is lost) presents the loss of composure as gain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","thiruvadi","thirukkoothu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2745":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2745,"t":["தேட்டறும் சிந்தை திகைப்பறும் பிண்டத்துள்","வாட்டறும் கால்புந்தி யாகி வரும்புலன்","ஓட்டறும் ஆசை அறும்உளத்து ஆனந்த","நாட்ட முறுக்குறும் நாடகங் காணவே."],"p":"","r":"To see the drama, seeking ends, the mind's bewilderment ends, the breath in the body no longer withers, the senses that come through the intellect stop running, desire ends, and the gaze of bliss in the heart is held taut.","n":"வாட்டறும் கால் (the breath that no longer withers) and நாட்டம் முறுக்குறும் (the gaze is twisted tight) carry a yogic sense of stilled breath and fixed gaze. The verse can be read as the effects of seeing the dance or as the conditions for seeing it; காணவே allows both.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aanandham","avaa","praanan","thirukkoothu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2746":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2746,"t":["காளியோடு ஆடிக் கனகா சலத்துஆடிக்","கூளியோடு ஆடிக் குவலயத் தேஆடி","நீடிய நீர்தீகால் நீள்வான் இடையாடி","நாளுற அம்பலத் தேயாடும் நாதனே."],"p":"","r":"He danced with Kali, danced on the golden mountain, danced with the goblins, danced on the earth, danced amid water, fire, wind and the wide sky, and the Lord dances in the hall for all days.","n":"The dance with Kali is traditionally the contest at Tiruvalangadu, and கனகாசலம் (golden mountain) is Meru. The verse gathers the dance's mythic sites and ends with the hall, where it goes on.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thirukkoothu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2747":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2747,"t":["மேரு நடுநாடி மிக்கிடை பிங்கனல்","கூரும்இவ் வானின் இலங்கைக் குறியுறும்","சாரும் திலைவனத் தண்மா மலயத்தூடு","ஏறும் கழுமுனை இவைசிவ பூமியே."],"p":"","r":"Meru is the central nadi, ida and pingala are marked by the Himalaya of this sky and by Lanka, and sushumna rises through the Tillai forest and the cool Malaya: these are Siva's ground.","n":"The verse maps the body's channels onto the land: the central channel onto Meru and onto Tillai, the side channels onto places north and south. Which channel goes with which place is not clear in the text, and 2754 gives the scheme again. இவைசிவ பூமியே makes the yogic body itself holy ground.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naadi","thirukkoothu","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2748":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2748,"t":["பூதல மேருப் புறத்தான தெக்கணம்","ஓதும் இடைபிங் கலைஒண் சுழுமுனையாம்","பாதி மதியோன் பயில்திரு அம்பலம்","ஏதமில் பூதாண்டத்து எல்லையின் ஈறே."],"p":"","r":"South of Meru on the earth are, it is said, ida, pingala and the bright sushumna; the holy hall where the bearer of the half-moon dwells is the end of the boundary of the flawless world of the elements.","n":"Chidambaram, in the south, is set at the place matching sushumna; பூதாண்டத்து எல்லையின் ஈறு (the end of the boundary of the elemental world) places the hall at the limit of the material cosmos.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naadi","thirukkoothu","andam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2749":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2749,"t":["அண்டங்கள் ஓரேழும் அம்பொற் பதியாகப்","பண்டைஆ காசங்கள் ஐந்தும் பதியாகத்","தெண்டினில் சத்தி திருஅம் பலமாகக்","கொண்டு பரஞ்சோதி கூத்துகந் தானே."],"p":"","r":"Taking the seven worlds as his golden city, the five ancient spaces as his city, and Shakti in the staff as his holy hall, the supreme light delighted in the dance.","n":"தெண்டு (staff) is taken here as தண்டு, the spine or central channel, as in 2771, which ends with the same line; then the hall is Shakti within the body's central staff. The five spaces (ஆகாசங்கள் ஐந்து) are a yogic list not given.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thirukkoothu","andam","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2750":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2750,"t":["குரானந்த ரேகையாய்க் கூர்ந்த குணமாம்","சிரானந்தம் பூரித்துத் தென்திசை சேர்ந்து","புரானந்த போகனாய்ப் பூவையும் தானும்","நிரானந்த மாகி நிருத்தஞ் செய் தானே."],"p":"","r":"As a line of bliss and keen quality, filling the head with bliss and reaching the southern quarter, he became the enjoyer of bliss with his lady, passed beyond bliss, and danced.","n":"The compounds குரானந்த, சிரானந்தம், புரானந்த and நிரானந்த form a chain of bliss words whose first parts are unclear; குரானந்த may be குரு + ஆனந்தம். நிரானந்தம் (bliss beyond bliss, or no-bliss) is a known term for the state past bliss.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aanandham","thirukkoothu","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2751":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2751,"t":["ஆதி பரன்ஆட அங்கைக் கனலாட","ஓதும் சடையாட உன்மத்த முற்றாடப்","பாதி மதியாடப் பாரண்ட மீதாட","நாதமோடு ஆடினான் நாதாந்த நட்டமே."],"p":"","r":"As the first Supreme dances, the fire in his palm dances, his celebrated matted hair dances, the datura dances, the half-moon dances, all above the earth and worlds dances: with nada he danced the dance at the end of nada.","n":"உன்மத்தம் is the datura flower Siva wears and also means frenzy; both senses may be in play. நாதாந்த நட்டம் puts the dance where sound itself ends.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thirukkoothu","naadham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2752":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2752,"t":["கும்பிட அம்பலத்து ஆடிய கோன்நடம்","அம்பரன் ஆடும் அகிலாண்ட நட்டமாம்","செம்பொருள் ஆகும் சிவலோகம் சேர்ந்துற்றால்","உம்பரம் மோனஞா ஞானந்தத்தில் உண்மையே."],"p":"","r":"The king's dance in the hall where we bow is the cosmic dance of the Lord of the sky; when one reaches Sivaloka, the true thing, the truth lies in the supreme silence at the end of knowledge.","n":"Line 4 (உம்பரம் மோனஞா ஞானந்தத்தில்) looks garbled; read as silence and ஞானாந்தம் (knowledge's end). The verse moves from the dance one bows to in the hall to a silence beyond it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thirukkoothu","mounam","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2753":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2753,"t":["மேதினி மூவேழ் மிகும்அண்டம் ஓரேழு","சாதக மாகும் சமயங்கள் நூற்றெட்டு","நாதமொடு அந்தம் நடானந்தம் நாற்பதம்","பாதியோடு ஆடிடும் பரன்இரு பாதமே."],"p":"","r":"The worlds thrice seven, the seven spheres, the hundred and eight creeds that are means, nada with its end, the bliss of dance and the four states: with his half, the Supreme's two feet dance.","n":"The numbers are given without their members; நாற்பதம் may be the four states of release or the four paths. பாதியோடு (with the half) is taken as with Uma, who is half his body.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvadi","samayam","naadham","thirukkoothu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2754":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2754,"t":["இடைபிங் கலைஇம வானோடு இலங்கை","நடுநின்ற மேரு நடுவாம் சுழுமுனை","கடவும் திலைவனம் கைகண்ட மூலம்","படர்பொன்றி என்னும் பரமாம் பரமே."],"p":"","r":"Ida and pingala are the Himalaya and Lanka, Meru standing in the middle is sushumna in the middle, and the Tillai forest one goes to is the root seen at hand, called the spreading one, the supreme of supremes.","n":"The mapping of 2747 again, more plainly: the side channels to the northern and southern limits, the central one to Meru and to Tillai. Lines 3 and 4 (கைகண்ட மூலம், படர்பொன்றி) are obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naadi","thirukkoothu","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2755":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2755,"t":["ஈறான கன்னி குமரியே காவிரி","வேறாம் நவதீர்த்தம் மிக்குள்ள வெற்புஏழுள்","பேறான வேதா கமமே பிறத்தலான்","மாறாத தென்திசை வையகம் சுத்தமே."],"p":"","r":"With Kumari, the maiden at the land's end, the Kaveri, nine distinct holy waters and seven hills, and because the Vedas and Agamas, the great gain, were born there, the unchanging southern land is pure.","n":"A praise of the south, set among verses on the southern hall (2748, 2758). பிறத்தலான் (because born there) may claim that the Vedas and Agamas were revealed in the south, which tradition says of the Agamas; it may also mean that they flourish there.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["theerththam","vedham","aagamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2756":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2756,"t":["நாதத்தினில் ஆடி நாற்பதத் தேயாடி","வேதத்தில் ஆடித் தழல் அந்தம் மீதாடி","போதத்தில் ஆடி புவனம் முழுதாடும்","தீதற்ற தேவாதி தேவர் பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"Dancing in nada, dancing in the four states, dancing in the Veda, dancing above the end of fire, dancing in knowledge, the flawless Lord of the gods dances through the whole world.","n":"நாற்பதம் (the four states) and தழல் அந்தம் (fire's end) are technical and unglossed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thirukkoothu","naadham","vedham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2757":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2757,"t":["தேவரோடு ஆடித் திருஅம்பலத்து ஆடி","மூவரோடு ஆடி முனிசனத் தோடு ஆடிப்","பாவினுள் ஆடிப் பராசத் தியில் ஆடிக்","கோவினுள் ஆடிடும் கூத்தப் பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"Dancing with the gods, dancing in the holy hall, dancing with the three, dancing with the company of sages, dancing within verse, dancing in Parashakti, the Lord of the dance dances within the king.","n":"கோவினுள் (within the king) may mean within the ruler, within the soul as lord of the body, or within the cow (the soul as பசு); the verse does not say.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thirukkoothu","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2758":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2758,"t":["ஆறு முகத்தில் அதிபதி நான்என்றும்","கூறு சமயக் குருபரன் நானென்றும்","தேறினர் தெற்குத் திருஅம்ப லத்துளே","வேறின்றி அண்ணல் விளங்கிநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"In the southern holy hall the great one shines without difference, as those who have come to clarity know him: the one who says 'I am lord over the six faces' and 'I am the supreme guru of the declared creeds'.","n":"ஆறு முகம் (six faces) is commonly taken as the six creeds, so that the dancer at Chidambaram is head and teacher of them all; it can also suggest Murugan. Who speaks the 'I am' is not fixed by the syntax.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["samayam","guru","thirukkoothu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2759":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2759,"t":["அம்பலம் ஆடரங் காக அதன்மீதே","எம்பரன் ஆடும் இருதாளின் ஈரொளி","உம்பர மாம்ஐந்து நாதத்து ரேகையுள்","தம்பத மாய்நின்று தான்வந் தருளுமே."],"p":"","r":"With the hall as his stage, the two lights of the two feet of our Supreme who dances on it stand as his own state within the line of the five supreme nadas, and he comes himself and grants grace.","n":"ஐந்து நாதத்து ரேகை (the line of the five nadas) is unexplained. The two feet as two lights may be read as sun and moon, or as the two feet of the dance, one planted and one raised.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvadi","thiruvarul","naadham","thirukkoothu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2760":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2760,"t":["ஆடிய காலும் அதிற்சிலம்பு ஓசையும்","பாடிய பாட்டும் பலவான நட்டமும்","கூடிய கோலம் குருபரன் கொண்டாடத்","தேடியு ளேகண்டு தீர்ந்தற்ற வாறே."],"p":"","r":"The dancing foot, the sound of its anklet, the songs sung, the many dances and the form they made: as the supreme guru celebrated them, I sought, saw them within, and so came to an end of seeking.","n":"தீர்ந்தற்றவாறு (how it ended and ceased) leaves open what ended: the search, the bonds, or the self. The dance is seen within (உளே), not in the hall.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thirukkoothu","guru","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2761":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2761,"t":["இருதயம் தன்னில் எழுந்த பிராணன்","கரசர ணாதி கலக்கும் படியே","அரதன மன்றினில் மாணிக்கக் கூத்தன்","குரவனயாய் எங்கணும் கூத்துகந் தானே."],"p":"","r":"As the breath rising in the heart spreads through hands, feet and the rest, so the ruby dancer in the jewelled hall, as guru, delighted in dancing everywhere.","n":"The breath pervading the limbs is the image for the dancer pervading all; குரவனாய் makes the pervading dancer also the teacher.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["praanan","thirukkoothu","guru","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2762":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2762,"t":["குருவுரு வன்றிக் குனிக்கும் உருவம்","அருவுரு வாவது அந்த அருவே","திரிபுரை யாகித் திகழ்தரு வாளும்","உருவரு வாகும் உமையவள் தானே"],"p":"","r":"The form that dances, other than the guru's form, that form-formless is the formless one itself; she who shines as Tripura is Uma, who is form and formless.","n":"குனிக்கும் (bending) is a verb for dancing. The verse seems to distinguish the dancing form seen in the guru from the formless, and to make Uma as Tripura both; the logic is compressed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","guru","thirukkoothu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2763":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2763,"t":["திருவழி யாவது சிற்றம் பலத்தே","குருவடி வுள்ளாக்குனிக்கும் உருவே","உருஅரு வாவதும் உற்றுணர்ந் தோர்க்கு","அருள்வழி யாவதும் அவ்வழி தானே."],"p":"","r":"The holy way is in the small hall, in the form that dances within the guru's form; for those who have perceived it closely, the way by which the form becomes formless is the way of grace itself.","n":"சிற்றம்பலம் (the small hall) is Chidambaram's shrine and in this chapter also a place in the body (2770). The dance is seen within the guru's form, which makes the guru the way to it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","thiruvarul","thirukkoothu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2764":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2764,"t":["நீரும் சிரிசிடைப் பன்னிரண்டு அங்குலம்","ஓடும் உயிர்எழுந்து ஓங்கி உதித்திட","நாடுமின் நாதாந்த நம்பெரு மான்உகந்து","ஆடும் இடந்திரு அம்பலந் தானே."],"p":"","r":"Where the life-breath rises and swells, running twelve finger-breadths, seek it: the place where our Lord of nada's end dances in delight is the holy hall itself.","n":"பன்னிரண்டு அங்குலம் (twelve finger-breadths) is the yogic measure of the breath's reach outside the body. Line 1 (நீரும் சிரிசிடை) looks corrupt.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["praanan","thirukkoothu","naadham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2765":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2765,"t":["வளிமேகம் மின்வில்லு வானகஓசை","தெளிய விசும்பில் திகழ்தரு மாறுபோல்","களிஒளி ஆறும் கலந்துடன் வேறாய்","ஒளியுரு வாகி ஒளித்துநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"As wind, cloud, lightning, rainbow and thunder shine clearly in the sky, so the six of joy and light mingle together and yet stay distinct; he stands as a form of light, hidden.","n":"The sky image says the many appear in the one space without ceasing to be many. களிஒளி ஆறும் (the six of joy and light) is not glossed and may be six centres or six powers.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2766":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2766,"t":["தீமுதல் ஐந்தும் திசை எட்டும் கீழ்மேலும்","ஆயும் அறிவினுக்கு அப்புறம் ஆனந்தம்","மாயைமா மாயை கடந்துநின் றார்காண","நாயகன் நின்று நடஞ்செய்யும் ஆறே."],"p":"","r":"Beyond the knowledge that examines the five beginning with fire, the eight directions, below and above, there is bliss; this is how the Lord stands and dances, for those who have passed beyond maya and great maya to see.","n":"மாயை and மாமாயை are traditionally impure and pure maya; the dance is visible only past both. The bliss is set beyond the examining mind, not as its result.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maayai","aanandham","thirukkoothu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2767":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2767,"t":["கூத்தன் கலந்திடும் கோல்வளை யாளொடும்","கூத்தன் கலந்திடும் கோதிலா ஆனந்தம்","கூத்தன் கலந்திடும் கோதிலா ஞானத்துக்","கூத்தனும் கூத்தியும் கூத்ததின் மேலே."],"p":"","r":"The dancer joins with her of rounded bangles, the dancer joins with flawless bliss, the dancer joins with flawless knowledge: the dancer and his dancing partner are above the dance.","n":"கூத்தி (the female dancer) is Shakti. கூத்ததின் மேலே (above the dance) can mean that the pair are beyond the dance they perform, or simply engaged in it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saththi","aanandham","gnaanam","thirukkoothu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2768":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2768,"t":["இடம்கொண்ட சத்தியும் எந்தை பிரானும்","நடங்கொண்டு நின்றமை நானும் அறிந்தேன்","படங்கொடு நின்றஇப் பல்லுயிர்க் கெல்லாம்","அடங்கலும் தாமாய்நின்று ஆடுகின் றாரே."],"p":"","r":"I too have come to know that the Shakti who took the left side and our Father stand in dance; for all these many lives that stand with bodies, they dance, standing as the whole of them.","n":"படங்கொடு நின்ற (standing with a படம், a cloth, picture or body) is taken here of embodied lives. The dancers in each body are Siva and Shakti.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saththi","thirukkoothu","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2769":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2769,"t":["சத்தி வடிவு சகல ஆனந்தமும்","ஒத்த ஆனந்தம் உமையவள் மேனியாம்","சத்தி வடிவு சகளத்து எழுந்துஇரண்டு","ஒத்த ஆனந்தம் ஒருநட மாமே."],"p":"","r":"Shakti's form is all bliss; the matching bliss is Uma's body; Shakti's form rising as two in the embodied, the matched bliss becomes one dance.","n":"சகளம் is the state with form; இரண்டு may be Siva and Shakti, whose two blisses make one dance. The verse is compressed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","aanandham","thirukkoothu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2770":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2770,"t":["நெற்றிக்கு நேரே புருவத்து இடைவெளி","உற்றுற்றுப் பார்க்க ஒளிவிடு மந்திரம்","பற்றுக்குப் பற்றாற்ப் பரமன் இருந்திடம்","சிற்றம் பலமென்று சேர்ந்துகொண் டேனே."],"p":"","r":"Gazing steadily at the space between the brows, straight from the forehead, the mantra shines out; the place where the Supreme sits as support of supports I took as Chirrambalam and joined.","n":"A practice of fixing the gaze between the brows; the yogi names that place the small hall. பற்றுக்குப் பற்றாய் may mean the support of all supports, or the one to cling to in place of every clinging.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thirukkoothu","thiyaanam","mandhiram","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2771":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2771,"t":["அண்டங்கள் தத்துவ மாகிச் சதாசிவம்","தண்டினில் சாத்தவி சாம்பவி ஆதனம்","தெண்டினில் ஏழும் சிவாசன மாகவே","கொண்டு பரஞ்சோதி கூத்துகந் தானே."],"p":"","r":"The worlds becoming tattvas, Sadasiva, the seats called Sattvi and Sambhavi in the staff, the seven in the staff becoming Siva's seat: taking these, the supreme light delighted in the dance.","n":"சாத்தவி and சாம்பவி are taken as names of seats or powers, and தண்டு as the spine. The last line repeats 2749's.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aasanam","thaththuvam","thirukkoothu","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2772":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2772,"t":["மன்று நிறைந்த விளக்கொளி மாமலர்","நன்றிது தான்இதழ் நாலொடு நூறவை","சென்றுஅது தான்ஒரு பத்திரு நூறுள","நின்றது தான்நெடு மண்டல மாமே."],"p":"","r":"The great flower, the lamp-light filling the hall, is good: its petals are four and a hundred; going on, there are twelve hundred; what stands is the long mandala.","n":"The lotus with its petal-counts is traditionally a centre in the body, the thousand-petalled one at the crown, but the numbers given (நாலொடு நூறு, பத்திரு நூறு) do not match a standard list and may be corrupt.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aadhaaram","thirukkoothu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2773":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2773,"t":["அண்டம் எழுகோடி பிண்டம் எழுகோடி","தெண்டிரை சூழ்ந்த திசைகள் எழுகோடி","எண்டிசை சூழ்ந்த இலிங்கம் எழுகோடி","அண்ட நடஞ்செயும் ஆலயம் தானே."],"p":"","r":"Seven crores of worlds, seven crores of bodies, seven crores of directions ringed by clear waves, seven crores of lingas ringed by the eight directions: these are the temple where he dances the cosmic dance.","n":"அண்டம் and பிண்டம் (worlds and bodies) are paired as macrocosm and microcosm; both are his temple.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thirukkoothu","andam","ilingam","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2774":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2774,"t":["ஆகாச மாம்உடல் அங்கார் முயலகன்","ஏகாச மாம்திசை எட்டும் திருக்கைகண்","மோகாய முக்கண்கள் மூன்றொளி தானாக","மாகாய மன்றுள் நடஞ்செய்கின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"Space is his body, Muyalakan lies there, the eight directions are his holy hands, the three lights are his three eyes; in the hall of the great body he dances.","n":"முயலகன் is the dwarf under the dancer's foot, traditionally taken as ignorance (ஆணவம்). The three lights are traditionally sun, moon and fire. அங்கார் and மோகாய are obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thirukkoothu","andam","aadhiththan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2775":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2775,"t":["அம்பல மாவது அகில சராசரம்","அம்பல மாவது ஆதிப் பிரானடி","அம்பல மாவது அப்புத்தீ மண்டலம்","அம்பல மாவது அஞ்செழுத் தாமே."],"p":"","r":"The hall is the whole moving and unmoving world; the hall is the first Lord's feet; the hall is the region of water and fire; the hall is the five letters.","n":"The hall (அம்பலம்) is given four identities, cosmic, devotional, yogic and mantric. அப்புத்தீ மண்டலம் is commonly taken as the moon and fire regions of the yogic body.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thirukkoothu","panchaakkaram","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2776":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2776,"t":["கூடிய திண்முழ வம்குழல் ஓமென்று","ஆடிய மானுடர் ஆதிப் பிரான் என்ன","நாடிய நற்கணம் ஆரம்பல் பூதங்கள்","பாடிய வாறுஒரு பாண்டரங் காமே."],"p":"","r":"Firm drums and flutes joined, sounding 'Om'; people danced crying 'the first Lord'; the good hosts who sought him, and the many goblins, sang: so it became a Pandarangam.","n":"பாண்டரங்கம் is a named dance of Siva (see 2733); here it is the whole festive scene of music and the dancing and singing of his hosts.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thirukkoothu","piranavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2777":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2777,"t":["அண்டத்தில் தேவர்கள் அப்பாலைத் தேவர்கள்","தெண்டிசை சூழ்புவிக் குள்ளுள்ள தேவர்கள்","புண்டரி கப்பதப் பொன்னம் பலக்கூத்துக்","கண்டுசே வித்துக் கதிபெறு வார்களே."],"p":"","r":"The gods in the world, the gods beyond it, and the gods within the earth ringed by the clear directions see and worship the dance in the golden hall on the lotus feet, and so reach their goal.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thirukkoothu","vazhipaadu","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2778":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2778,"t":["புளிக்கண்ட வர்க்குப் புனலூறு மாபோல்","களிக்கும் திருக்கூத்துக் கண்டவர்க்கு எல்லாம்","துளிக்கும் அருட் கண்ணீர் சோர்நெஞ் சுருக்கும்","ஒளிக்குள்ஆ னந்தத்து அமுதூறும் உள்ளத்தே."],"p":"","r":"As water springs in the mouth of those who see tamarind, for all who see the joyous holy dance tears of grace drip, the faint heart melts, and the nectar of bliss in the light wells up in the heart.","n":"The simile makes the point that the effect comes from seeing alone, as the mouth waters at the sight of tamarind. அமுது here is the bliss of the vision, without the yogic sense of nectar from the head that it has elsewhere.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thirukkoothu","anbu","amudham","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2779":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2779,"t":["திண்டாடி வீழ்கை சிவானந்த மாவது","உண்டார்க்கு உணவுண்டால் உன்மத்தம் சித்திக்கும்","கொண்டாடு மன்றுள் குனிக்கும் திருக்கூத்துக்","கண்டார் வருங்குணம் கேட்டார்க்கும் ஒக்குமே."],"p":"","r":"To stagger and fall is Siva's bliss; for those who have drunk, drinking more brings frenzy; the quality that comes to those who have seen the holy dance bending in the celebrated hall comes also to those who have heard of it.","n":"உண்டார்க்கு உணவுண்டால் is obscure; read here as drink upon drink, the image of intoxication for the bliss. The last line extends the dance's effect from seeing to hearing.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aanandham","thirukkoothu","kal"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2780":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2780,"t":["அங்கி தமருகம் அக்குமா லைபாசம்","அங்குசம் சூலம் கபாலம் உடன்ஞானம்","தங்குஉ பயந்தரு நீல மும்உடன்","மங்கையோர் பாகமாய் மாநடம் ஆடுமே."],"p":"","r":"With fire, drum, rosary, noose, goad, trident, skull and knowledge, and the blue that gives both, with the woman as one half of him, he dances the great dance.","n":"The list is of what the dancer holds in his hands, as in images of the many-armed form. உபயந்தரு நீலம் (the blue that gives both) is obscure; it may be the blue throat or Uma's colour.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thirukkoothu","saththi","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2781":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2781,"t":["ஆடல் பதினோர் உறுப்பும் அடைவாகக்","கூடிய பாதம் சிலம்புகைக் கொள்துடி","நீடிய நாதம் பராற்பர நேயத்தே","ஆடிய நந்தி புறம்அகந் தானே."],"p":"","r":"With the eleven parts of dance in order, the joined feet, the anklets, the drum held in the hand, and the long nada, Nandi danced in love of the beyond-supreme, outside and inside.","n":"ஆடல் பதினோர் உறுப்பு may be the eleven parts of dance in Tamil dance theory or the eleven dances of tradition. புறம் அகம் (outside and inside) puts the dance both in the world and in the body.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thirukkoothu","naadham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2782":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2782,"t":["ஒன்பதும் ஆட ஒருபதி னாறுஆட","அன்புறு மார்க்கங்கள் ஆறும் உடனாட","இன்புறும் ஏழினும் ஏழுஐம்பத் தாறுஆட","அன்பதும் ஆடினான் ஆனந்தக் கூத்தே."],"p":"","r":"As the nine dance, as the sixteen dance, as the six paths of love dance with them, as in the delightful seven the seven and fifty-six dance, he danced the fifty too: the dance of bliss.","n":"The numbers are given without their members. மார்க்கங்கள் ஆறு may be the six adhvas; அன்பதும் is probably ஐம்பதும் (the fifty letters), corrupted under the influence of அன்பு in line 2.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thirukkoothu","aththuvaa","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2783":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2783,"t":["ஏழினில் ஏழாய் இகழ்ந்தெழுந்து ஏழதாய்","ஏழினில் ஒன்றாய் இழிந்துஅமைந்து ஒன்றாகி","ஏழினில் சன்மார்க்கம் எங்கள் பரஞ்சோதி","ஏழிசை நாடகத் தேஇசைந் தானே."],"p":"","r":"As seven in the seven, rising as seven, as one in the seven, descending and settling as one, our supreme light, the true path in the seven, joined in the drama of the seven notes.","n":"ஏழிசை are the seven notes of music; the other sevens are unglossed and may be the seven centres or the seven worlds.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sanmaarkkam","thirukkoothu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2784":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2784,"t":["மூன்றினில் அஞ்சாகி முந்நூற்று அறுபதாய்","மூன்றினில் ஆறாய் முதற்பன்னீர் மூலமாய்","மூன்றின்இலக்கம் முடிவாகி முந்தியே","மூன்றிலும் ஆடினான் மோகாந்தக் கூத்தே."],"p":"","r":"As five in the three, as three hundred and sixty, as six in the three, as the first twelve at the root, as the three lakhs at the end, he danced first in all three the dance at delusion's end.","n":"Three hundred and sixty and twelve suggest the days and months of the year, and the three may be sun, moon and fire, which would make this the dance as time; that is a guess. மோகாந்தம் (the end of delusion) parallels நாதாந்தம் and ஞானாந்தம் elsewhere in the chapter.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thirukkoothu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2785":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2785,"t":["தாமுடி வானவர் தம்முடி மேலுறை","மாமணி ஈசன் மலரடித் தாளினை","வாமணி அன்புடை யார்மனத் துள்ளெழுங்","காமணி ஞாலம் கடந்துநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"The great jewel that dwells on the crowns of the crowned gods, the pair of the Lord's flower feet, the fair jewel that rises in the hearts of those with love: he stands beyond the world.","n":"The verse plays on மணி (jewel) through all four lines: the feet are the jewel on the gods' heads and in the lovers' hearts.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvadi","anbu","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2786":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2786,"t":["புரிந்தவன் ஆடில் புவனங்கள் ஆடும்","தெரிந்தவன் ஆடும் அளவுஎங்கள் சிந்தை","புரிந்தவன் ஆடில்பல்பூதங்கள் ஆடும்","எரிந்தவன் ஆடல்கண்டு இன்புற்ற வாறே."],"p":"","r":"If he who willed it dances, the worlds dance; our thought dances to the measure that he who knows dances; if he dances, the many elements dance: this is how I rejoiced seeing the dance of the fiery one.","n":"The mind is made one of the things that move with his dance, like the worlds and the elements. எரிந்தவன் (the fiery one, or one who burned) is taken as Siva of fire.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thirukkoothu","manam","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2787":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2787,"t":["ஆதி நடஞ்செய்தான் என்பர்கள் ஆதர்கள்","ஆதி நடஞ்செய்கை யாரும் அறிகிலர்","ஆதி நடமாடல் ஆரும் அறிந்தபின்","ஆதி நடமாட லாம் அருட் சத்தியே."],"p":"","r":"The ignorant say that the First danced; no one knows the First's dancing; once someone knows the First's dancing, the dancing of the First is the Shakti of grace.","n":"To say that he danced, as a past event, is the ignorant view; known rightly, the dance is his gracious power at work. The verse shares its pattern with 2796.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thirukkoothu","saththi","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2788":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2788,"t":["ஒன்பதோடு ஒன்பதாம் உற்ற இருபதத்து","அன்புறு கோணம் அதிபதந்து ஆடிடத்","துன்புறு சத்தியுள் தோன்றிநின்று ஆடவே","அன்புறு எந்தை நின்று ஆடலுற் றானே."],"p":"","r":"In the two states of nine and nine, as the angle of love stands at the head and dances, appearing within the Shakti that suffers, our loving Father stood and began to dance.","n":"கோணம் (angle) suggests the triangles of a drawn diagram, and the nines may be its cells or lines; the verse cannot be pinned down. துன்புறு சத்தி (the Shakti that suffers, or that brings suffering) is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thirukkoothu","saththi","chakkaram"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2789":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2789,"t":["தத்துவம் ஆடச் சதாசிவம் தானாடச்","சித்தமும் ஆடச் சிவசத்தி தானாட","வைத்த சராசரம் ஆட மறையாட","அத்தனும் ஆடினான் ஆனந்தக் கூத்தே."],"p":"","r":"As the tattvas dance, Sadasiva dances, the mind dances, Siva's Shakti dances, the placed world of moving and unmoving things dances and the Veda dances, the Father too danced the dance of bliss.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thirukkoothu","thaththuvam","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2790":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2790,"t":["இருவருங் காண எழில்அம் பலத்தே","உருவோடு அருவோடு ஒருபர ரூபமாய்த்","திருவருள் சத்திக்குள் சித்தன்ஆ னந்தன்","அருளுரு வாகிநின்று ஆடலுற் றானே."],"p":"","r":"For the two to see, in the beautiful hall, as one supreme form both with form and formless, within the Shakti of holy grace, the Siddha, the blissful one, stood as a form of grace and began to dance.","n":"இருவர் (the two) are traditionally the sages Patanjali and Vyaghrapada, for whom the dance was shown at Tillai; the verse does not name them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thirukkoothu","thiruvarul","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2791":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2791,"t":["சிவமாட சத்தியும் ஆடச் சகத்தில்","அவமாட ஆடாத அம்பரம் ஆட","நவமான தத்துவம் நாதாந்தம் ஆடச்","சிவமாடும் வேதாந்தச் சித்தாந்தத் துள்ளே."],"p":"","r":"As Siva dances and Shakti dances, as the ruin in the world dances, as the sky that does not dance dances, as the nine tattvas and nada's end dance, Siva dances within Vedanta-Siddhanta.","n":"வேதாந்தச் சித்தாந்தம் joins the two systems as the place of the dance, a pairing Tirumular makes elsewhere. நவமான may be nine or new. அவம் (ruin, waste) dances too: the dance takes in what is harmful.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","saththi","vedaantham","siththaantham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2792":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2792,"t":["நாதத்தின் அந்தமும் நாற்போத அந்தமும்","வேதத்தின் அந்தமும் மெய்ச்சிவா னாந்தமும்","தாதற்ற நல்ல சதாசிவா னந்தத்து","நாதப் பிரமம் சிவநாட மாமே."],"p":"","r":"The end of nada, the end of the four knowledges, the end of the Veda, the true bliss of Siva: in the good bliss of Sadasiva, free of elements, the Nada-Brahman is Siva's dance.","n":"நாற்போதம் (the four knowledges) and தாதற்ற (without dhatu, element or root) are not fixed. நாதப் பிரமம் (sound as Brahman) is identified with the dance.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naadham","vedaantham","aanandham","thirukkoothu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2793":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2793,"t":["சிவமாதி ஐவர்திண் டாட்டமும் தீரத்","தவமார் பசுபாசம் ஆங்கே தனித்துத்","தவமாம் பரன்எங்கும் தானாக ஆடும்","தவமாம் சிவானந்தத் தோர் ஞானக் கூத்தே."],"p":"","r":"So that the struggle of the five beginning with Siva ends, and pasu and pasa stand apart there in austerity, the Supreme who is austerity dances, being himself everywhere: it is the dance of knowledge of those in Siva's bliss.","n":"சிவமாதி ஐவர் (the five beginning with Siva) are commonly taken as the five lords who govern the five acts. தனித்து (standing apart) may mean that soul and bonds are separated. தவம் is repeated in every line and seems to name both practice and the Lord.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pasu","paasam","thavam","thirukkoothu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2794":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2794,"t":["கூடிநின் றானொடு காலத்துத் தேவர்கள்","வீடநின் றான்விகிர் தா என்னும் நாமத்தைத்","தேடநின் றான்திக ழுஞ்சுடர் மூன்றொளி","ஆடநின் றான்என்னை ஆட்கொண்ட வாறே."],"p":"","r":"Standing together with the gods of time, standing for release, standing to be sought under the name Vikirta, standing so that the three bright lights dance: this is how he took me as his own.","n":"Lines 1 and 2 are obscure; விகிர்தா (the one who is other, unlike all) is a Tevaram name of Siva. The three lights are traditionally sun, moon and fire.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thirukkoothu","thiruvarul","aadhiththan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2795":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2795,"t":["நாதத் துவம்கடந்து ஆதி மறைநம்பி","பூதத் துவத்தே பொலிந்தின்பம் எய்தினர்","நேதத் துவமும் அவற்றோடு நேதியும்","பேதப் படாவண்ணம் பின்னிநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"Passing beyond nada-tattva and trusting the first scripture, they found bliss shining in the elements themselves; he wove together the 'not that' and its negation so that they are not divided.","n":"நேதத்துவம் and நேதி echo the Upanishadic 'neti' (not this). The verse may mean that the way of negation and what it negates are held together by him, so that those beyond nada find bliss in the elements too; this is an interpretation of compressed lines.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","vedaantham","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2796":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2796,"t":["ஆனந்தம் ஆனந்தம் என்பர் அறிவிலர்","ஆனந்த மாநடம் ஆரும் அறிகிலர்","ஆனந்த மாநடம் ஆரும் அறிந்தபின்","தான் அந்தம் அற்றிடம் ஆனந்த மாமே."],"p":"","r":"The ignorant say 'bliss, bliss'; no one knows the great dance of bliss; once someone knows the great dance of bliss, the place where the self comes to an end is bliss.","n":"The last line puns on ஆனந்தம்: தான் அந்தம் அற்று (the self's end gone, or the self ended) is heard inside the word. Bliss is not a thing to name but the ending of the self in the dance.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aanandham","thirukkoothu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2797":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2797,"t":["திருந்துநல் சீஎன்று உதறிய கையும்","அருந்தவர் வாஎன்று அணைத்த மலர்க்கையும்","பொருந்த அமைப்பில் அவ்வென்ற பொற்கையும்","திருந்தநல் தீயாகும் திருநிலை மவ்வே."],"p":"","r":"The hand that shook off, saying 'Si', the flower hand that embraced the ascetics, saying 'Va', the golden hand set fittingly as 'A', and the fire: the holy state is 'Ma'.","n":"The dancer's hands are read as syllables of the five-letter mantra: the drum-hand or the shaking hand as சி, the embracing hand as வா, the protecting hand as அ (perhaps ய), and fire or the foot as ம. The exact matching is traditional and the verse does not give all five clearly.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panchaakkaram","thirukkoothu","muththirai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2798":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2798,"t":["மருவும் துடியுடன் மன்னிய வீச்சு","மருவிய அப்பும் அனலுடன் கையும்","கருவின் மிதித்த கமலப் பதமும்","உருவில் சிவாய நமவென வோதே."],"p":"","r":"The drum, the outstretched sweeping arm, the water and the hand with fire, and the lotus foot that trod on the embryo: in this form, recite 'Sivayanama'.","n":"Again the dancer's form spells the mantra. கருவின் மிதித்த (that trod on the embryo, or on birth) is taken as the foot crushing rebirth, or as the foot on Muyalakan; அப்பு (water) may be the Ganga in his hair.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panchaakkaram","thirukkoothu","karu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2799":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2799,"t":["அரன்துடி தோற்றம் அமைத்தல் திதியாம்","அரன் அங்கி தன்னில் அறையிற் சங் காரம்","அரன் உற்று அணைப்பில் அமரும் திரோதாயி","அரனடி என்றும் அனுக்கிரகம் என்னே."],"p":"","r":"Hara's drum is creation; the setting hand is preservation; Hara's fire is destruction; the pressing foot on which he rests is concealment; Hara's raised foot is grace for ever.","n":"This is the classic reading of the dancer's body as the five acts. அமைத்தல் and உற்று அணைப்பு are taken as the reassuring hand and the planted foot by tradition; the verse names the drum, fire and foot plainly.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aindhozhil","thirukkoothu","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2800":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2800,"t":["தீத்திரன் சோதி திகழ்ஒளி உள்ஒளி","கூத்தனைக் கண்டஅக் கோமளக் கண்ணினள்","மூர்த்திகள் மூவர் முதல்வன் இடைசெல்லப்","பார்த்தனன் வேதங்கள் பாடினள் தானே."],"p":"","r":"The fierce one's light, the shining light, the inner light: she of tender eyes who saw the Dancer, as the first of the three murtis went between, looked on and sang the Vedas.","n":"The tender-eyed one is most likely Uma watching the dance, as in 2732; இடைசெல்ல (going between) is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","thirukkoothu","vedham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2801":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2801,"t":["நந்தியை எந்தையை ஞானத் தலைவனை","மந்திரம் ஒன்றுள் மருவி அதுகடந்து","அந்தர வானத்தின் அப்புறத்து அப்பர","சுந்தரக் கூத்தனை என்சொல்லு மாறே."],"p":"","r":"How can I speak of Nandi, my Father, the Lord of knowledge, the beautiful dancer beyond and beyond the inner sky, reached by dwelling in the one mantra and passing beyond it?","n":"The mantra is the way in and must then be passed beyond: மருவி அது கடந்து.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["guru","mandhiram","thirukkoothu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2802":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2802,"t":["சீய குருநந்தி திருஅம்ப லத்திலே","ஆயுறு மேனியை யாரும் அறிகிலர்","தீயுறு செம்மை வெளுப்பொடும் அத்தன்மை","ஆயுறு மேனி அணைபுக லாமே."],"p":"","r":"No one knows the body to be examined of the guru Nandi in the holy hall; red like fire with white, such is the body in which one may take refuge.","n":"சீய is obscure. Red and white together may be Siva and Shakti, or nada and bindu, as in 1929; the verse gives the colours without a key.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","thirukkoothu","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2803":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2803,"t":["தானான சத்தியும் தற்பரை யாய்நிற்கும்","தானாம் பரற்கும் உயிர்க்கும் தரும் இச்சை","ஞானாதி பேதம் நடத்தும் நடித்தருள்","ஆனால் அரனடி நேயத்த தாமே."],"p":"","r":"Shakti, who is himself, stands as the Tatpara; she gives will to the Supreme who is himself and to souls; she conducts the distinctions beginning with knowledge; when she dances and grants grace, souls come to love Hara's feet.","n":"Shakti is made the source of will (இச்சை) both in the Lord and in souls, and the dancer of the grace that turns souls to his feet. The subject of the last line is supplied.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","thiruvarul","thiruvadi","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:209"},"t:2804":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2804,"t":["உள்ளத்துள் ஓம்என்ற ஈசன் ஒருவனை","உள்ளத்து ளேயங்கி யாய ஒருவனை","உள்ளத்து ளேநீதி யாய ஒருவனை","உள்ளத்து ளேயுடல் ஆகாய மாமே."],"p":"","r":"The one Lord who is Om within the heart, who is fire within it and who is right order within it: within the heart the body itself becomes space.","n":"Each line opens with உள்ளத்துள் (within the heart), and the last turns from the Lord to the body: உடல் ஆகாயம் ஆமே. It can be read as the body becoming spacious or void in realisation, or as the heart-space (the inner ஆகாசம் of 2812) being where the body is known. நீதி (justice, right order) is an unusual name for the Lord here and I can't fix its sense.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aakaasam","piranavam","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:210"},"t:2805":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2805,"t":["பெருநில மாய் அண்ட மாய்அண்டத்து அப்பால்","குருநில மாய்நின்ற கொள்கையன் ஈசன்","பெருநில மாய்நின்று தாங்கிய தாளோன்","அருநிலை யாய்நின்ற ஆதிப் பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The Lord's nature is to stand as the great earth, as the cosmic sphere and as the ground beyond the sphere; standing as the great earth and bearing it with his feet, the first Lord stands as the rare state.","n":"குருநிலம் (the guru-ground, or the ground of light, since குரு can mean brightness) names what lies beyond the அண்டம், and I can't fix which. தாங்கிய தாளோன் (he of the feet that bear) joins the cosmic image to the feet the devotee seeks.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","andam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:210"},"t:2806":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2806,"t":["அண்ட ஒளியும் அகண்ட ஒளியுடன்","பிண்ட ஒளியால் பிதற்றும் பெருமையை","உண்ட வெளிக்குள் ஒளிக்குள் ஒளித்தது","கொண்ட குறியைக் குலைத்தது தானே."],"p":"","r":"The light of the cosmos, with the undivided light and the light of the body, makes a greatness people babble about; hidden in light within the space that has swallowed all, it undid of itself the mark it had taken.","n":"அண்டம் and பிண்டம் are the cosmos and the body, the large and the small worlds, here each with its light, and அகண்டம் the undivided beyond both. கொண்ட குறி (the mark taken) may be the bodily form, the soul's sense of itself, or the yogi's point of concentration; the subject of குலைத்தது is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oli","aakaasam","andam","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:210"},"t:2807":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2807,"t":["பயனறு கன்னியர் போகத்தின் உள்ளே","பயனுறும் ஆதி பரஞ்சுடர்ச் சோதி","அயனொடு மால்அறி யாவகை நின்றிட்டு","உயர்நெறி யாய்ஒளி ஒன்றது வாமே."],"p":"","r":"Within the enjoyment of the maidens that yields no fruit is the first supreme flame of light that gives fruit; standing beyond the knowing of Brahma and Mal, the one light becomes the high path.","n":"பயனறு கன்னியர் போகம் is obscure: it may be the enjoyment of virgin powers (the Lord's shaktis) that produces no birth, or a yogic union without issue, set against the light that does yield fruit. Brahma and Mal failing to find the end of the light is the familiar story of the column of fire.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oli","saththi","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:210"},"t:2808":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2808,"t":["அறிவுக்கு அறிவாம் அகண்ட ஒளியும்","பிறிவா வலத்தினில் பேரொளி மூன்றும்","அறியாது அடங்கிடும் அத்தன் அடிக்குள்","பிறியாது இருக்கில் பெரும்காலம் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"The undivided light that is knowledge to knowledge, and the three great lights in their inseparable circuit, sink unknowing into the Father's feet; if one stays there without parting, it becomes the great time.","n":"The three great lights (பேரொளி மூன்று) are traditionally sun, moon and fire, also matched with the channels and centres of the yogic body. பிறிவா வலம் and பெரும் காலம் (great time: long life, or timelessness) are not fixed by the verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oli","thiruvadi","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:210"},"t:2809":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2809,"t":["ஆகாச வண்ணன் அமரர் குலக்கொழுந்து","ஏகாச மாசுணம் இட்டுஅங்கு இருந்தவன்","ஆகாச வண்ணம் அமர்ந்துநின்று அப்புறம்","ஆகாச மாய்அங்கி வண்ணனும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"The sky-coloured one, the shoot of the race of the gods, who sat there wearing a great serpent as his upper cloth, settles into the hue of space, and beyond that becomes space and the one of the colour of fire.","n":"ஆகாச வண்ணன் (sky-coloured) is usually an epithet of Mal, but ஏகாசம் (upper cloth) of மாசுணம் (great serpent) points to Siva, who wears snakes; the verse may be fusing the two or using the epithet of Siva. The movement is from a coloured form to space itself and then to fire.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aakaasam","sivam","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:210"},"t:2810":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2810,"t":["உயிர்க்கின்ற வாறும் உலகமும் ஒக்க","உயிர்க்கின்ற உள்ளொளி சேர்கின்ற போது","குயில்கொண்ட பேதை குலாவி உலாவி","வெயில்கொண்டு என்உள்ளம் வெளியது ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"When the inner light that gives life to beings and to the world alike comes to join, the girl with the koel's voice frolics and roams, and my heart, taking in sunlight, becomes open space.","n":"குயில்கொண்ட பேதை (the simple girl who has the koel's voice) may be Shakti, the kundalini, or the poet's own heart as a lovesick girl; the image is from love poetry and I can't fix its referent. வெயில்கொண்டு (taking in the sun) and வெளி (open space) carry the verse's claim that the heart becomes light and space.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oli","aakaasam","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:210"},"t:2811":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2811,"t":["நணுகில் அகல்கிலன் நாதன் உலகத்து","அணுகில் அகன்ற பெரும்பதி நந்தி","நணுகிய மின்னொளி சோதி வெளியைப்","பணியின் அமுதம் பருகலும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"Approach him and the Lord does not move away; Nandi, the great Lord, spreads wide even in the atom of the world; if one bows to the space of lightning-light that has drawn near, one may drink nectar.","n":"அணுகில் அகன்ற may be 'wide even in the atom' or 'far even when near'; both fit the paradox of the first two lines. Drinking nectar after seeing the light is the yogic sequence of the earlier tantras (nectar from the crown).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oli","amudham","aakaasam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:210"},"t:2812":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2812,"t":["புறத்துளா காசம் புவனம் உலகம்","அகத்துளா காசம்எம் ஆதி அறிவு","சிவத்துளா காசம் செழுஞ்சுடர் சோதி","சகத்துளா காசம் தானம்ச மாதியே."],"p":"","r":"The outer space is the regions and the worlds; the inner space is the knowing of our First; the space in Siva is the rich flame of light; and the space in the world is the seat of samadhi.","n":"A scheme of four spaces (ஆகாசம்), each named with துள் (within). The last line, சகத்துளாகாசம் தானம் சமாதியே, is the least clear: தானம் may be 'place, seat' (sthana) or 'giving', and why the space within the world should be samadhi is not explained.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aakaasam","arivu","oli","samaadhi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:210"},"t:2813":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2813,"t":["மனசந் தியில்கண்ட மனநன வாகும்","கனவுற ஆனந்தம் காண்டல் அதனை","வினவுற ஆனந்தம் மீதொழிவுஎன்ப","இனமுற்றான் நந்தி ஆனந்தம் இரண்டே."],"p":"","r":"What the mind sees at its juncture is the mind's waking; seeing bliss as dream comes, and, on inquiry, the ceasing beyond it, are called bliss; Nandi, who has reached his own, says bliss is twofold.","n":"மனசந்தி (the juncture of the mind), மனநனவு (the mind's waking) and மீதொழிவு (the ceasing above) are technical terms of the states of consciousness that I can't fix. Which two blisses are meant (perhaps the bliss seen in a state and the bliss of its ceasing) is not stated plainly; இனமுற்றான் is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aanandham","avaththai","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:211"},"t:2814":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2814,"t":["கரியட்ட கையன் கபாலம்கை யேந்தி","எரியும் இளம்பிறை சூடும்எம் மானை","அரியன் பெரியன் என்று ஆட்பட்டது அல்லால்","கரியன்கொல் சேயன்கொல் காண்கின்றி லேனே."],"p":"","r":"My Lord, whose hand killed the elephant, who holds a skull in his hand and wears the burning young crescent: I only became his servant, calling him rare and great; whether he is dark or red I cannot see.","n":"The first two lines give Siva's familiar marks (the flayed elephant, the skull-bowl, the crescent), and the last line refuses to describe him: devotion came without knowing his colour. கரியன்கொல் சேயன்கொல் (is he dark, is he red) may also hint that he is neither Mal nor any visible form.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","adiyaar","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:211"},"t:2815":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2815,"t":["மிக்கார் அமுதுண்ண நஞ்சுண்ட மேலவன்","தக்கார் உரைத்த தவநெறியே சென்று","புக்கால் அருளும் பொன்னுரை ஞானத்தை","நக்கார் சுழல்வழி நாடுமின் நீரே."],"p":"","r":"The exalted one who drank the poison so that the great might drink nectar grants, if you enter by the path of austerity the worthy have taught, a knowledge like refined gold; seek it by the way of the Naked One's feet.","n":"நக்கார் சுழல்வழி is obscure. I take நக்கர் as the Naked One (a name of Siva) and சுழல் as a variant of கழல் (anklet, the feet), but it may be 'the whirling way of those who laugh'. தக்கார் (the worthy) points to the guru and the line of teachers.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","thavam","thiruvarul","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:211"},"t:2816":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2816,"t":["விளக்கைப் பிளந்து விளக்கினை ஏற்றி","விளக்கினுக் குள்ளே விளக்கினைத் தூண்டி","விளக்கில் விளக்கை விளக்கவல் லார்க்கு","விளக்குடை யான்கழல் மேவலும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"For those who can split the lamp, light the lamp, trim the lamp within the lamp and make the lamp shine in the lamp, reaching the feet of the Lord who owns the lamp becomes possible.","n":"The whole verse plays on விளக்கு (lamp), also 'to make clear'. It is traditionally read of the soul's light kindled by the guru's knowledge until it shows the Lord's light within; splitting the lamp may be the separation of soul from bonds. The plain riddle and the symbolic reading both stand.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oli","gnaanam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:211"},"t:2817":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2817,"t":["தத்துவம் எங்குண்டு தத்துவன் அங்குண்டு","தத்துவம் எங்கில்லை தத்துவன் அங்கில்லை","தத்துவ ஞானத்தின் தன்மை அறிந்தபின்","தத்துவன் அங்கே தலைப்படுந் தானே."],"p":"","r":"Where the tattvas are, the Lord of the tattvas is there; where the tattvas are not, he is not there; once one knows the nature of knowledge of the tattvas, the Lord himself appears there.","n":"தத்துவன் may be the Lord who rules the tattvas, or 'the Real' as against தத்துவம் (the real things). The second line seems to deny the Lord where there are no tattvas, which may mean he is reached only through knowing them, or be wordplay that the last line resolves.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","gnaanam","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:211"},"t:2818":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2818,"t":["விசும்பொன்று தாங்கிய மெய்ஞ்ஞானத் துள்ளே","அசும்பினின்று ஊறியது ஆர்அமுது ஆகும்","பசும்பொன் திகழும் படர்சடை மீதே","குசும்ப மலர்க்கந்தம் கூடிநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"Within the true knowledge that holds up the sky, what wells up from the spring becomes rare nectar; on his spreading matted hair, shining like fresh gold, he stands joined with the scent of the safflower.","n":"அசும்பு (oozing, a spring) and the nectar it yields are traditionally the fluid felt to flow from the crown in yoga. விசும்பு ஒன்று தாங்கிய may be knowledge that bears the inner sky. The link between the nectar and Siva's fragrant hair is not stated.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["amudham","gnaanam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:211"},"t:2819":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2819,"t":["முத்தின் வயிரத்தின் முந்நீர்ப் பவளத்தின்","கொத்தும் பசும்பொன்னின்தூவொளி மாணிக்கம்","ஒத்துஉயிர் அண்டத் துள் அமர் சோதியை","எத்தன்மை வேறென்று கூறுசெய் வீரே."],"p":"","r":"The light that dwells in the cosmos of souls, like pearl, diamond, coral of the sea, clustered fresh gold and pure-shining ruby: in what way will you call it different and divide it?","n":"The gems are not alike, yet each shines; the question argues that the one light is not to be split by the many forms it shines in. ஒத்து உயிர் அண்டத்துள் can also be read 'matching, within soul and cosmos'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oli","sivam","andam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:211"},"t:2820":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2820,"t":["நான்என்றும் தான்என்றும் நாடினேன் நாடலும்","நான்என்றும் தான்என்றும் இரண்டில்லை என்பது","நான்என்ற ஞான முதல்வனே நல்கினான்","நான்என்ற நானும் நினைப்பு ஒழிந்தேனே."],"p":"","r":"I searched, calling it 'I' and calling it 'He', and as I searched, the First of knowledge who is the 'I' granted me that 'I' and 'He' are not two; and I, the one who said 'I', ceased to think.","n":"நான் and தான் (I and He, or I and the self) are the two terms; the knowledge that they are not two is given (நல்கினான்), not reached. The verse can be read as plain non-duality or, as Siddhanta reads such verses, as inseparability without identity; it says இரண்டில்லை and no more.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","pasu","pathi","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:211"},"t:2821":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2821,"t":["ஞானத்தின் நன்னெறி நாதாந்த நன்னெறி","ஞானத்தின் நன்னெறி நானென்று அறிவோர்தல்","ஞானத்தின் நல்யோக நன்னிலை யேநிற்றல்","ஞானத்தின் நன்மோக நாதாந்த வேதமே."],"p":"","r":"The good path of knowledge is the good path of nadanta; the good path of knowledge is knowing it as 'I'; the good yoga of knowledge is standing in the good state; the good release of knowledge is the Veda of nadanta.","n":"நாதாந்தம் (the end of nada) is one of the six ends (ஆறந்தம்). நன்மோகம் is odd: மோகம் is delusion or longing, and I read it as standing for மோக்கம் (release); the text may be corrupt. நானென்று அறிவோர்தல் echoes 2820.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","aarandham","yogam","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:211"},"t:2822":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2822,"t":["உய்யவல் லார்கட்கு உயிர்சிவ ஞானமே","உய்யவல் லார்கட்கு உயிர்சிவ தெய்வமே","உய்யவல் லார்கட்கு ஒடுக்கம் பிரணவம்","உய்யவல் லார்அறி வுள்அறி வாமே."],"p":"","r":"For those who can be saved, Siva-knowledge is their life; Siva the god is their life; Om is where they dissolve; and they become the knowing within knowing.","n":"ஒடுக்கம் (withdrawal, dissolution) makes the syllable Om the place into which the saved are drawn back, not only a sound they recite.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","sivam","piranavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:211"},"t:2823":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2823,"t":["காணவல் லார்க்குஅவன் கண்ணின் மணியொக்கும்","காணவல் லார்க்குக் கடலின் அமுதொக்கும்","பேணவல் லார்க்கப் பிழைப்பிலன் பேர்நந்தி","ஆணவல் லார்க்கே அவன்துணை யாமே."],"p":"","r":"To those who can see, he is like the pupil of the eye; to those who can see, like nectar from the sea; to those who can cherish him, Nandi, the great name, never fails; to those who can hold fast, he becomes their support.","n":"ஆணவல்லார் is obscure: it may mean those who can hold fast or rule themselves, or, by a pun, those free of ஆணவம் (the root impurity). The nectar of the sea recalls the churning, when Siva drank the poison (2815).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","thunai","amudham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:211"},"t:2824":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2824,"t":["ஓம்என்றும் எழுத் துள்நின்ற ஓசைபோல்","மேல்நின்ற தேவர் விரும்பும் விழுப்பொருள்","சேய்நின்ற செஞ்சுடர் எம்பெரு மானடி","ஆய்கின்ற தேவர் அகம்படி யாமே."],"p":"","r":"Like the sound that stands within the letter Om, the excellent thing the gods above desire is the feet of our Lord, the red flame standing far off; the gods who seek them become his inner retinue.","n":"The sound within Om is traditionally the nada beyond its letters. அகம்படி (inner retinue, palace attendants) may also mean that the feet become the seekers' inner dwelling.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piranavam","thiruvadi","oli"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:211"},"t:2825":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2825,"t":["எப்பாழும் பாழும் யாவுமாய் அன்றாகி","முப்பாழும் கீழுள முப்பாழும் முன்னியே","இப்பாழும் இன்னாவாறு என்பதில்லா இன்பத்துத்","தற்பரஞா னானந்தர் தானது வாகுமே."],"p":"","r":"Becoming every void, void and all things and yet not them, attending to the three voids and the three voids below, the one who is supreme knowledge-bliss becomes That, in a bliss where there is no saying 'this void is so'.","n":"பாழ் (void, emptiness) and முப்பாழ் (the three voids) are technical terms in the ninth tantra; they are traditionally taken as voids of the soul, of maya and of the Supreme (or of the three padas of 'that thou art'), but the verse does not list them. கீழுள முப்பாழ் (the three voids below) suggests two sets of three.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["paazh","aanandham","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:212"},"t:2826":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2826,"t":["தொம்பதம் தற்பதஞ் சொன்ன துரியம்போல்","நம்பிய மூன்றாம் துரியத்து நன்றாகும்","அம்புவி யுன்னா அதிசூக்கம் அப்பாலைச்","செம்பொருள் ஆண்டருள் சீர்நந்தி தானே."],"p":"","r":"Like the turiya spoken of 'thou' and 'that', it becomes good in the trusted third turiya; beyond the beautiful earth and the unthought super-subtle is the perfect thing, glorious Nandi, who rules and gives grace.","n":"தொம்பதம் (thou) and தற்பதம் (that) are the words of the great saying 'that thou art', each given its own turiya in the earlier chapters; the third turiya is traditionally that of அசி (art), their union. அம்புவி யுன்னா may be divided differently.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thonthaththasi","avaththai","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:212"},"t:2827":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2827,"t":["மன்னும் சத்தியாதி மணியொளி மாசோயை","அன்னதோடு ஒப்பம் இடல்ஒன்றா மாறது","இன்னிய உற்பலம் ஒண்சீர் நிறமணம்","பன்னிய சோபை பகர்ஆறும் ஆனதே."],"p":"","r":"To compare the lasting truth and the rest with the stainless light of a gem does not fit; rather, like the fresh water lily with its form, bright colour, fragrance and beauty, they are spoken of as six.","n":"சத்தியாதி (truth and the rest) is taken as truth, knowledge, bliss and the qualities that go with them; the count of six (ஆறும்) is not explained. மாசோயை is obscure. The point seems to be that the qualities are distinct yet inseparable, like a flower's form, colour and scent, not one undivided glow.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aanandham","sivam","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:212"},"t:2828":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2828,"t":["சத்தி சிவன்பர ஞானமும் சாற்றுங்கால்","உய்த்த அனந்தம் சிவமுயர் ஆனந்தம்","வைத்த சொருபத்த சத்தி வருகுரு","உய்த்த உடல்இவை உற்பலம் போலுமே."],"p":"","r":"When Shakti, Siva and supreme knowledge are spoken of, the endless one given, Siva who is high bliss, the Shakti of his own form, the guru who comes and the body given are like the water lily.","n":"The list is compressed and its grouping uncertain. The water lily (உற்பலம்) again stands for distinct aspects that cannot be separated; that the guru and the body are among them suggests the Lord's coming as guru in a body is one with his nature.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","sivam","aanandham","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:212"},"t:2829":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2829,"t":["உருஉற் பலநிறம் ஒண்மணம் சோபை","தரநிற்ப போல்உயிர் தற்பரன் தன்னில்","மருவச் சிவம்என்ற மாமுப் பதத்தின்","சொரூபத்தின் சத்தியாதி தோன்றநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"As the water lily's form, colour, bright fragrance and beauty stand together, so when the soul merges in the Supreme, he stands so that truth and the rest appear in the very form of the great three padas called Siva.","n":"மாமுப்பதம் (the great three padas) are taken as 'thou', 'that' and 'art'. The verse makes the water lily image explicit and applies it to the soul's union, not to the Lord alone.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pasu","sivam","thonthaththasi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:212"},"t:2830":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2830,"t":["நினையும் அளவில் நெகிழ வணங்கிப்","புனையில் அவனைப் பொதியலும் ஆகும்","எனையும் எங்கோன்நந்தி தன்னருள் கூட்டி","நினையும் அளவில் நினைப்பித் தனனே."],"p":"","r":"If one bows with a melting heart as far as one can think, and adorns him, one can hold him within; Nandi my king, joining his grace even to me, made me think as far as I think.","n":"The verse turns on நினையும் அளவில் (as far as one thinks), used twice: first as the measure of the devotee's worship, then as the measure of what the Lord's grace made him think. Even the thinking is given. பொதியல் (to contain, to wrap) may also mean to enshrine.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","vazhipaadu","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:212"},"t:2831":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2831,"t":["பாலொடு தேனும் பழத்துள் இரதமும்","வாலிய பேரமு தாகும் மதுரமும்","போலும் துரியம் பொடிபடி உள்புகச்","சீல மயிர்க்கால் தொறும்தேக் கிடுமே."],"p":"","r":"Like honey with milk, the juice within fruit and the sweetness of the great pure nectar, turiya, when it enters, floods the virtuous through every hair-root.","n":"பொடிபடி is obscure: perhaps 'shattering' or 'settling like fine dust'. சீல (virtuous) may qualify the person or be part of a phrase about the body. The bliss is felt in the body, at every மயிர்க்கால், not only in the mind.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","aanandham","amudham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:212"},"t:2832":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2832,"t":["அமரத் துவம்கடந்து அண்டம் கடந்து","தமரத்து நின்ற தனிமையன் ஈசன்","பவளத்து முத்தும் பனிமொழி மாதர்","துவளற்ற சோதி தொடர்ந்துநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"Passing beyond the immortal state and beyond the cosmos, the Lord who stands alone in the beyond stood following the unwavering light of the woman with pearls in coral and cool speech.","n":"The woman with pearl teeth in coral lips and cool words is taken as Shakti. தமரம் (sound, or a hollow) is obscure, as is whether the light is hers or his own that she follows.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","saththi","oli","andam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:212"},"t:2833":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2833,"t":["மத்திமம் ஆறாறு மாற்றி மலநீக்கிச்","சுத்தம தாகும் துரியத் துரிசற்றுப்","பெத்த மறச்சிவ மாகிப் பிறழ்வுற்றும்","சத்திய ஞா னானந்தம் சார்ந்தனன் ஞானியே."],"p":"","r":"Changing the middle thirty-six, removing malam, becoming pure and free of flaw in turiya, losing bondage and becoming Siva, and so transformed, the jnani reaches truth, knowledge and bliss.","n":"ஆறாறு is the thirty-six tattvas; மத்திமம் (middle) may refer to the middle state or channel. பெத்தம் is bondage (baddha), the opposite of release. பிறழ்வுற்றும் (even turning aside, changing) is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","malam","thaththuvam","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:212"},"t:2834":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2834,"t":["சிவமாய் அவமான மும்மலம் தீரப்","பவமான முப்பாழைப் பற்றறப் பற்றத்","தவமான சத்திய ஞானானந் தத்தே","துவமார் துரியம் சொரூபம் தாமே."],"p":"","r":"Becoming Siva so that the three disgraceful malas end, holding the three voids of birth so as to cling no more, in the truth, knowledge and bliss that is austerity, the turiya full of 'thou' is the true form itself.","n":"பவமான முப்பாழ் (the three voids that are birth) and பற்றறப் பற்ற (to hold so as to cling no more) are paradoxical and technical. துவம் is taken as tvam, 'thou'. The last line seems short of a syllable or two.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["malam","paazh","aanandham","avaththai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:212"},"t:2835":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2835,"t":["பரம குரவன் பரம்எங்கு மாகித்","திரமுற எங்கணும் சேர்ந்துஒழி வற்று","நிரவும் சொரூபத்துள் நீடும் சொரூபம்","அரிய துரியத்து அணைந்துநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"The supreme guru, becoming the Supreme everywhere, joining everywhere firmly and without gap, the form that endures within the all-pervading form, stood having reached rare turiya.","n":"சொரூபம் (own form, true nature) is used twice: the guru's own form is said to endure within the form that pervades all. Whether the guru here is the Lord as guru or the human teacher become one with him is not fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","sivam","avaththai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:213"},"t:2836":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2836,"t":["குலைக்கின்ற நீரின் குவலய நீரும்","அலைக்கின்ற காற்றும் அனலொடு ஆகாசம்","நிலத்திடை வானிடை நீண்டகன் றானை","வரைந்து வலம்செயு மாறுஅறி யேனே."],"p":"","r":"The water of the world in the surging sea, the tossing wind, fire and space: I do not know how to mark off the one who stretches long and wide through earth and sky and walk around him in worship.","n":"வலம்செய்தல் (circumambulation) needs a bounded object; the verse's point is that the Lord, spread through all five elements, cannot be bounded (வரைந்து) to be circled.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","vazhipaadu","aakaasam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:213"},"t:2837":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2837,"t":["அங்குநின் றான்அயன் மால்முதல் தேவர்கள்","எங்குநின் றாரும் இறைவன் என்று ஏத்துவர்","தங்கிநின் றான்தனிநாயகன் எம்இறை","பொங்கிநின் றான்புவ னாபதி தானே."],"p":"","r":"He stood there; the gods from Brahma and Mal on, and all who stand anywhere, praise him as Lord; our Lord, the sole master, abides, and swells forth as Lord of the worlds.","n":"The verbs run from standing there (அங்கு நின்றான்) to dwelling (தங்கி நின்றான்) to swelling forth (பொங்கி நின்றான்): the one Lord is both settled and overflowing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","pathi","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:213"},"t:2838":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2838,"t":["சமயச் சுவடும் தனையறி யாமல்","சுமையற்ற காமாதி காரணம் எட்டும்","திமிரச் செயலும் தெளிவுடன் நின்றோர்","அமரர்க்கு அதிபதி யாகிநிற் பாரே."],"p":"","r":"Those who stand in clarity, without knowing even the trace of the sects in themselves, beyond the eight causes beginning with desire and the workings of darkness, become lords over the gods.","n":"காமாதி காரணம் எட்டும் (the eight beginning with desire) are traditionally the faults such as desire, anger, greed, delusion, pride and envy, but the list of eight is not given. சுமையற்ற (burdenless) is unclear in its place. Sects (சமயம்) are again something to leave behind, as often in Tirumular.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["samayam","thelivu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:213"},"t:2839":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2839,"t":["மூவகைத் தெய்வத்து ஒருவன் முதல்உரு","வாய்அது வேறாம் அதுபோல் அணுப்பரன்","சேய சிவமுத் துரியத்துச் சீர்பெற","ஏயும் நெறியென்று இறைநூல் இயம்புமே."],"p":"","r":"One of the three kinds of gods is the first form and becomes different from it; so too the soul and the Supreme; that the far-off Siva may be gloriously reached in the three turiyas is the fitting path, say the books of the Lord.","n":"The analogy is compressed: as one god is both the source of the three gods and one of them, so the soul (அணு) and the Supreme are one and different. இறைநூல் (the Lord's books) is taken as the Agamas. The three turiyas are those of 'thou', 'that' and 'art' (2826).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pasu","sivam","avaththai","aagamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:213"},"t:2840":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2840,"t":["உருவன்றி யேநின்று உருவம் புணர்க்கும்","கருவன்றி யேநின்று தான்கரு வாகும்","அருவன்றி யேநின்ற மாயப் பிரானைக்","குருவன்றி யாவர்க்கும் கூடஒண் ணாதே."],"p":"","r":"Standing without form, he joins himself to forms; not being a womb, he himself becomes the womb; the wondrous Lord who stands without even formlessness cannot be reached by anyone without the guru.","n":"உரு, அரு (form, formless) and கரு (womb, embryo) rhyme through the verse. மாயப் பிரான் may be 'the wondrous Lord' or 'the Lord of maya'; Mal is also called மாயன், but here the subject is the one reached through the guru.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["guru","iraivan","karu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:213"},"t:2841":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2841,"t":["உருவம் நினைப்பவர்க்கு உள்ளுறும் சோதி","உருவம் நினைப்பவர் ஊழியும் காண்பர்","உருவம் நினைப்பவர் உம்பரும் ஆவர்","உருவம் நினைப்பவர் உலகத்தில் யாரே."],"p":"","r":"Those who think on his form find light rising within; those who think on his form see the aeons; those who think on his form become celestials; who in the world are equal to those who think on his form?","n":"The last line, உருவம் நினைப்பவர் உலகத்தில் யாரே, can mean 'who in the world is like them' or 'who in the world are such people' (they are rare). உருவம் may be the Lord's form or the guru's. After 2840 on the formless, the verse affirms meditation on form.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiyaanam","oli","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:213"},"t:2842":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2842,"t":["பரஞ்சோதி யாகும் பதியினைப் பற்றாப்","பரஞ்சோதி என்னுள் படிந்துஅதன் பின்னைப்","பரஞ்சோதி யுள்நான் படியப் படியப்","பரஞ்சோதி தன்னைப் பறையக் கண்டேனே."],"p":"","r":"As I held to the Lord who is the supreme light, the supreme light sank into me; and as I sank and sank within the supreme light, I saw the supreme light declare itself.","n":"The movement is reciprocal: the light sinks into the poet, then the poet into the light. பறைய may be 'proclaim, speak' or 'wear away, vanish'; under the second reading the light itself fades from view in union.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oli","pathi","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:213"},"t:2843":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2843,"t":["சொரூபம் உருவம் குணம்தொல் விழுங்கி","அரியன உற்பலம் ஆமாறு போல","மருவிய சத்தியாதி நான்கும் மதித்த","சொரூபக்குரவன் சுகோதயத் தானே."],"p":"","r":"As form, shape and quality swallow the ancient and become the rare water lily, so the guru of the true form, who weighs the four joined to him, truth and the rest, dwells in the dawning of bliss.","n":"The water lily image of the previous chapter (2827 to 2829). சுகோதயம் (the dawning of bliss) echoes the chapter title. தொல் விழுங்கி (swallowing the ancient) is obscure; it may refer to the old bonds.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","aanandham","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:213"},"t:2844":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2844,"t":["உரையற்ற ஆனந்த மோன சொரூபத்தின்","கரையற்ற சத்தியாதி காணில் அகார","மருவுற்று உகாரம் மகாரம தாக","உரையற்ற காரத்தில் உள்ளொளி யாமே."],"p":"","r":"In the true form of speechless bliss and silence, if one sees truth and the rest without bound, A merges and becomes U and M, and in the speechless letter it is the inner light.","n":"அகாரம், உகாரம், மகாரம் are the three parts of Om; உரையற்ற காரம் (the speechless letter) is taken as what lies beyond them, the point or nada. The verse ties the analysis of Om to the silence (மோனம்) of the true form.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piranavam","mounam","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:213"},"t:2845":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2845,"t":["தலைநின்ற தாழ்வரை மீது தவஞ்செய்து","முலைநின்ற மாதறி மூர்த்தியை யானும்","புலைநின்ற பொல்லாப் பிறவி கடந்து","கலைநின்ற கள்வனில் கண்டுகொண் டேனே."],"p":"","r":"The form known to the woman with breasts who did penance on the peak of the high mountain, I too, crossing vile and wicked birth, came to see in the thief who stands in the kalas.","n":"The woman doing penance on the mountain is taken as Uma, who won Siva by austerity on the Himalaya. கலைநின்ற கள்வன் (the thief who stands in the kalas) may be Siva hidden in the moon's digits, in the arts, or in the kalas of the yogic body; கள்வன் for the Lord who steals the heart is a devotional usage.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","piravi","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:213"},"t:2846":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2846,"t":["ஆமாறு அறிந்தேன் அகத்தின் அரும்பொருள்","போமாறு அறிந்தேன் புகுமாறும் ஈதென்றே","ஏமாப்ப தில்லை இனியோர் இடமில்லை","நாமாம் முதல்வனும் நான்என லாமே."],"p":"","r":"I came to know how the rare thing within comes about, how it leaves and that this is how it enters; there is no being misled and no other place now, and the First who is ourselves can be called 'I'.","n":"ஏமாப்பது இல்லை may mean 'there is no being deceived' or 'no other refuge is needed' (ஏமம், protection). The last line, நாமாம் முதல்வனும் நான் எனலாம், states the non-dual claim of 2820 in the poet's own voice.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","sivam","pasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:213"},"t:2847":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2847,"t":["செற்றிலென் சீவிலென் செஞ்சாந்து அணியலென்","மத்தகத் தேயுளி நாட்டி மறிக்கிலென்","வித்தகன் நந்தி விதிவழி யல்லது","தத்துவ ஞானிகள் தன்மைகுன் றாரே."],"p":"","r":"Whether they are struck or pared away, or anointed with red sandal paste, or a chisel is driven into their heads and they are thrown down, the knowers of truth, who go only by the decree of Nandi the wise, do not decline from their nature.","n":"விதிவழி அல்லது can be read as 'except by Nandi's decree' (nothing touches them unless he wills it) or as 'going by no way but his decree'. Setting anointing beside the chisel echoes the old ideal of treating the one who wounds and the one who honours alike.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","gnaanam","samanilai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:214"},"t:2848":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2848,"t":["தான்முன்னம் செய்த விதிவழி தானல்லால்","வான்முன்னம் செய்தங்கு வைத்ததோர் மாட்டில்லை","கோன்முன்னம் சென்னி குறிவழி யேசென்று","நான்முன்னம் செய்ததே நன்னில மானதே."],"p":"","r":"Nothing was made beforehand by the heavens and laid by for one except by the way of the decree one has made oneself; going before the king by the way of the mark on the head, what I did before has become good ground.","n":"The first two lines make the decree one's own (தான் முன்னம் செய்த விதி), not something heaven stores up. Line 3, கோன் முன்னம் சென்னி குறிவழியே சென்று, is obscure: it may be the mark written on the head (the fate written on the forehead) or the Lord's sign. The poet's former deeds are said to have become நன்னிலம், good ground for the Lord's grace.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oozh","vinai","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:214"},"t:2849":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2849,"t":["ஆறிட்ட நுண்மணல் ஆறே சுமவாதே","கூறிட்டுக் கொண்டு சுமந்தறி வாரில்லை","நீறிட்ட மேனி நிமிர்சடை நந்தியைப்","பேறிட்டுஎன் உள்ளம் பிரியகில் லாவே."],"p":"","r":"The river does not carry the fine sand it has laid down, and no one knows how to divide it up and carry it; my heart, having won Nandi of the ash-smeared body and upright matted hair, cannot part from him.","n":"The image of the first two lines is not explained and the link to the last two is left to the reader. One possible reading: as the river leaves its sand behind and no one else can carry a share of it, each bears what his own deeds have laid down; another is that what is deposited stays, as the poet's heart stays with Nandi.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oozh","vinai","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:214"},"t:2850":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2850,"t":["வான்நின்று இடிக்கில்என் மாகடல் பொங்கிலன்","கான்நின்ற செந்தீக் கலந்துடல் வேகில்என்","தான்ஒன்றி மாருதம் சண்டம் அடிக்கிலென்","நான்ஒன்றி நாதனை நாடுவன் நானே."],"p":"","r":"What if the sky thunders, what if the great sea surges, what if the red fire of the forest mingles and the body burns, what if the wind strikes fiercely: I, at one with him, will seek the Lord.","n":"The editor's parallel notes that the opening closely matches a verse of Appar's Thevaram (4.112.8), which uses the same 'what if' (என்) formula for the devotee's fearlessness. தான் ஒன்றி and நான் ஒன்றி both use ஒன்றி (joining): the wind joins its force, the poet joins the Lord.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anjaamai","iraivan","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:214"},"t:2851":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2851,"t":["ஆனை துரக்கிலென் அம்பூடு அறுக்கிலென்","கானத்து உழுவை கலந்து வளைக்கிலென்","ஏனைப் பதியினில் என்பெரு மான்வைத்த","ஞானத்து உழவினை நான்உழு வேனே."],"p":"","r":"What if an elephant charges, what if an arrow cuts through, what if a tiger of the forest closes in around: in that other field my Lord has given, I will plough the ploughing of knowledge.","n":"ஏனைப் பதி (the other place or field) may be the inner field as against the outer world, or the Lord's own realm. The image of ploughing knowledge turns the farmer's labour into spiritual practice.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anjaamai","gnaanam","uzhavu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:214"},"t:2852":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2852,"t":["கூடு கெடின்மற்றோர் கூடுசெய் வான்உளன்","நாடு கெடினும் நமர்கெடு வாரில்லை","வீடு கெடின்மற்றோர் வீடுபுக் கால்ஒக்கும்","பாடது நந்தி பரிசறி வார்க்கே."],"p":"","r":"If the nest perishes, there is one who will make another nest; even if the land perishes, our people do not perish; if the house perishes, it is like entering another house: such is the state of those who know Nandi's nature.","n":"கூடு (nest, also the body) and வீடு (house, also release) both carry a second sense; the verse's surface sense is that losing a body is like moving house, since the Lord will make another. நமர் (our people) is taken as the Lord's devotees. Whether வீடு here also hints at release is not clear.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","piravi","adiyaar","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:214"},"t:2853":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2853,"t":["சிந்தையது என்னச் சிவனென்ன வேறில்லை","சிந்தையின் உள்ளே சிவனும் வெளிப்படும்","சிந்தை தெளியத் தெளியவல் லார்கட்குச்","சிந்தையின் உள்ளே சிவனிருந்தானே."],"p":"","r":"There is no difference between what is called the mind and what is called Siva; Siva appears within the mind; for those who can clear the mind more and more, Siva abides within the mind.","n":"Every line begins with சிந்தை (mind, thought). The first line states identity, the rest presence: Siva appears in, and dwells in, the mind as it is cleared. Read together, the identity may hold only of the cleared mind; the verse leaves both readings open.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","manam","thelivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:215"},"t:2854":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2854,"t":["வாக்கும் மனமும் மறைந்த மறைபொருள்","நோக்குமின் நோக்கப் படும்பொருள் நுண்ணிது","போக்கொன்றும் இல்லை வரவில்லை கேடில்லை","யாக்கமும் அத்தனை ஆய்ந்துகொள் வார்க்கே."],"p":"","r":"Look at the hidden thing in which speech and mind disappear: the thing to be seen is subtle; for those who examine and grasp it there is no going, no coming and no ruin, and even its gain is the Father.","n":"போக்கு, வரவு, கேடு (going, coming, ruin) can describe the hidden thing itself or the knower, for whom birth and death end. The last line, யாக்கமும் அத்தனை, is obscure: it may be 'ஆக்கமும் அத்தனை' (even its growth is that much) or point to அத்தன், the Father.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","meyyunarvu","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:215"},"t:2855":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2855,"t":["பரனாய்ப் பராபர னாகிஅப் பால்சென்று","உரனாய் வழக்கற ஒண்சுடர் தானாய்","தரனாய் தனதென ஆறுஅறி வொண்ணா","அரனாய் உலகில் அருள்புரிந் தானே."],"p":"","r":"Being the Supreme, becoming the Supreme beyond the supreme and going further, being strength and the bright flame itself beyond all dispute, being the bearer whom no one can know as their own, he gave grace in the world as Hara.","n":"பரன், பராபரன், உரன், தரன் and அரன் rhyme through the verse, rising to the transcendent and returning as Hara, who gives grace in the world. வழக்கற (beyond dispute, or beyond usage and speech) and தனதென ஆறு அறிவொண்ணா are compressed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","thiruvarul","oli"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:215"},"t:2856":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2856,"t":["ஓதும் மயிரக்கால் தோறும் அமு தூரிய","பேதம் அபேதம் பிறழாத ஆனந்தம்","ஆதி சொரூபங்கள் மூன்றுஅகன்று அப்பாலை","வேதம் ஓதும் சொரூபிதன் மேன்மையே."],"p":"","r":"Nectar wells up at every hair-root, a bliss that does not waver between difference and non-difference; the greatness of the one whose form the Veda chants lies beyond, past the three primal forms.","n":"பேதம் அபேதம் (difference and non-difference) are the terms of the debate on how the soul stands to the Lord; the bliss here does not tip to either. ஆதி சொரூபங்கள் மூன்று (the three primal forms) is not fixed by the verse; it may be taken as the three aspects of being, knowing and bliss, or as three forms of the Lord. ஓதும் may go with the nectar ('the nectar spoken of') or simply open the verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","amudham","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:216"},"t:2857":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2857,"t":["உணர்வும் அவனே உயிரும் அவனே","புணரும் அவனே புலவி அவனே","இணரும் அவன்தன்னை எண்ணலும் ஆகான்","துணரின் மலர்க்கந்தம் துன்னிநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"He is awareness and he is life; he is the union and he is the lovers' sulking; though bound up with all, he cannot be reached by thought, and he stays close as the scent stays in a cluster of flowers.","n":"புணரும் and புலவி are the love-poetry pair of union and sulking, so the Lord is both moments of love; read so, the line borrows akam language for the soul and God. இணரும் may mean 'joined (with all)' or 'clustered', echoing இணர் (cluster) and துணர் (bunch) in the last line. The scent in the flower is Tirumular's recurrent image for a presence that pervades without being seen.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","uyir","punarchi","oodal"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:216"},"t:2858":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2858,"t":["துன்னிநின் றான்தன்னை உன்னிமுன் னாஇரும்","முன்னி அவர்தம் குறையை முடித்திடும்","மன்னிய கேள்வி மறையவன் மாதவன்","சென்னியுள் நின்றதோர் தேற்றத்தின் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"Keep in mind first the one who stays close; when you think of him he will end your lack; the one of lasting scripture, the Vedic one, the great ascetic, is known through a certainty that stands within the head.","n":"The opening துன்னிநின்றான் takes up the last word of 2857, linking the verses. மறையவன் மாதவன் can be epithets of Siva (the Vedic one, the great ascetic) or names of Brahma and Vishnu (மாதவன் is a name of Vishnu), which would change the line's sense. சென்னியுள் (within the head) is traditionally taken as the crown centre of yoga; plainly it may mean only 'in the mind'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiyaanam","sivam","thelivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:216"},"t:2859":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2859,"t":["மின்னுற்ற சிந்தை விழித்தேன் விழித்தலும்","தன்னுற்ற சோதி தலைவன் இணையிலி","பொன்னுற்ற மேனிப் புரிசடை நந்தியும்","என்னுற்று அறிவன்நான் என்விழித் தானே."],"p":"","r":"With my mind lit as by lightning I woke, and in that waking the peerless Lord, light of himself, Nandi of golden body and coiled matted hair, opened my eyes; how am I to know what came to me?","n":"நந்தி is both Siva and Tirumular's teacher, so the waking is at once grace and initiation. The last line is compressed: என்னுற்று அறிவன் may be 'how shall I know what befell me' or 'what shall I know of myself', and என்விழித்தான் 'he opened my eyes' or 'he looked upon me'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","thiruvarul","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:216"},"t:2860":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2860,"t":["சத்திய ஞானத் தனிப்பொருள் ஆனந்தம்","சித்தத்தின் நில்லாச் சிவானந்தப் பேரொளி","சுத்தப் பிரம துரியம் துரியத்துள்","உய்த்த துரியத்து உறுபே ரொளியே."],"p":"","r":"Truth, knowledge, the one substance, bliss; the great light of Siva's bliss that does not stay in the mind; pure Brahman, turiya; and within turiya, the great light reached in the turiya beyond it.","n":"சத்திய ஞான ... ஆனந்தம் matches the Vedantic triad of being, knowledge and bliss, and சுத்தப் பிரம is Vedanta's word; the verse sets them beside சிவானந்தம், as Tirumular elsewhere joins Vedanta and Siddhanta. துரியத்துள் உய்த்த துரியம் (a turiya brought within turiya) is usually taken as துரியாதீதம், the state beyond turiya.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aanandham","avaththai","sivam","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:216"},"t:2861":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2861,"t":["பரன்அல்ல நீடும் பராபரன் அல்ல","உரன்அல்ல மீதுணர் ஒண்சுடர் அல்ல","தரன்அல்ல தான்அவை யாய்அல்ல வாகும்","அரன்அல்ல ஆனந்தத்து அப்புறத்தானே."],"p":"","r":"It is not Para, nor the enduring Paraparan, nor strength, nor the bright flame known above, nor the bearer; being all these it is none of them; it is not even Hara, but lies beyond bliss.","n":"A string of denials in the manner of 'not this, not this': each title of the Lord, even அரன், falls short. உரன் (strength, or knowledge) and தரன் (bearer, or one who holds a rank) are uncertain. தான் அவையாய் அல்லவாகும் keeps the denial from being a simple absence: it is all of them and not them. Even bliss (ஆனந்தம்), which 2860 named, is passed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:216"},"t:2862":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2862,"t":["முத்தியும் சித்தியும் முற்றிய ஞானத்தோன்","பத்தியுள் நின்று பரந்தன்னுள் நின்றுமா","சத்தியுள் நின்றோர்க்குத் தத்துவங் கூடலால்","சுத்தி யகன்றொர் சுகானந்த போதரே."],"p":"","r":"One whose knowledge is complete, holding both release and attainment, stands within devotion and within the Supreme; those who stand within the great Shakti, as the tattvas join, pass beyond purification and become knowers of blissful joy.","n":"முத்தியும் சித்தியும் sets release beside yogic attainment. தத்துவங் கூடலால் (because the tattvas join, or are reached) and சுத்தி யகன்றோர் (those who have left purification behind, or those beyond the purified state) are obscure; the line may mean they no longer need the purifying rites. சுகானந்த போதர் (knowers of joy-bliss) is a compound of Sanskrit terms.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","veedu","saththi","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:216"},"t:2863":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2863,"t":["துரிய அதீதம் சொல்லறும் பாழாம்","அரிய துரியம் அதீதம் புரியில்","விரியும் குவியும் விள்ளா மிளிரும்தன்","உருவும் திரியும் உரைப்பது எவ்வாறே."],"p":"","r":"The state beyond turiya is the void where words cease; if one grasps the rare turiya and the beyond, how can one tell of one's own form, which spreads, contracts, does not break open, gleams and turns?","n":"பாழ் (void, barren waste) is Tirumular's word for the state beyond form. தன் உருவும் may be the form of the self or of the Lord, and the five verbs (spread, contract, not open, shine, turn) may describe its play or simply its being past description; விள்ளா may also mean 'cannot be uttered'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","sivam","sol"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:216"},"t:2864":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2864,"t":["ஓதிய முத்தியடைவே உயிர்பர","பேத மில் அச்சிவம் எய்தும் துரியமோடு","ஆதி சொரூபம் சொரூபத்த தாகவே","ஏதமி லாநிரு வாணம் பிறந்ததே."],"p":"","r":"When the release spoken of is reached, the soul attains the Supreme, that Siva, without distinction, through turiya; the primal form becoming its own form, faultless nirvana is born.","n":"உயிர்பர may be உயிர் பரம் (the soul, the Supreme) or உயிர் பர(ம்) எய்தும் (the soul reaches the Supreme). ஆதி சொரூபம் சொரூபத்தது ஆக is taken as the soul taking on the Lord's own nature. நிருவாணம் is both the name of the highest initiation and a word for the freed state; the chapter title (கரும நிருவாணம்) points to the ending of karma.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["veedu","sivam","uyir","avaththai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:217"},"t:2865":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2865,"t":["பற்றற் றவர்பற்றி நின்ற பரம்பொருள்","சுற்றற் றவர்சுற்றுக் கருதிய கண்ணுதல்","சுற்றற் றவர்சுற்றி நின்றான் சோதியைப்","பெற்றுற் றவர்கள் பிதற்றொழிந் தாரே."],"p":"","r":"The supreme being whom those without attachments hold to, the forehead-eyed one whom those without kin regard as kin and who stands around those with no one around them: those who have gained his light and reached him have ceased their babbling.","n":"The verse plays on பற்று (attachment) and சுற்று (kin, surrounding): having let go of both, the renouncer finds them again in the Lord. பிதற்று (babbling) may be empty talk or the devotee's restless praise; either way, the one who has gained the light falls silent.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pattru","sivam","sutram","thuravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:217"},"t:2866":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2866,"t":["காயம் பலகை கவறைந்து கண்மூன்றாய்","ஆயம் பொருவதோர் ஐம்பத்தோர் அக்கரம்","ஏய பெருமான்இருந்து பொருகின்ற","மாயக் கவற்றின் மறைப்பறி யேனே."],"p":"","r":"With the body as the board, five dice and three eyes, the game is played with the fifty-one letters; the Lord sits there and plays, and I do not know the secret of these magic dice.","n":"ஐம்பத்தோர் அக்கரம் (fifty-one letters) is the Sanskrit alphabet as mantra theory counts it. கவறு ஐந்து may be the five senses or elements and கண் மூன்று the three lights (sun, moon, fire); these are traditional guesses. The plain sense is that life is a game the Lord plays in the body, and its rule is hidden.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","sivam","maayai","mandhiram"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:218"},"t:2867":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2867,"t":["தூறு படர்ந்து கிடந்தது தூநெறி","மாறிக் கிடக்கும் வகையறி வாரில்லை","மாறிக் கிடக்கும் வகையறி வாளர்க்கு","ஊறிக் கிடந்ததென் உள்ளன்பு தானே."],"p":"","r":"The pure path lies overgrown with brush and no one knows how it lies turned about; for those who do know how it lies turned about, my inner love wells up.","n":"மாறிக் கிடக்கும் (lies reversed) is repeated and is the key: the path runs against the ordinary direction. Tradition takes it as the yogic turning inward of breath and sight, but the verse only says it is reversed. The verse could stand as a preface to the chapter's riddles.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","anbu","adiyaar"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:218"},"t:2868":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2868,"t":["ஆறு தெருவில் அகப்பட்ட சந்தியில்","சாறுபடுவன நான்கு பனையுள","ஏறற்கு அரியதோர் ஏணியிட்டு அப்பனை","ஏற்றலு றேன்கடல் ஏழுங்கண் டேனே."],"p":"","r":"At a junction where six streets meet there are four palmyras that yield juice; setting a ladder hard to climb, I climbed that palm and saw all seven seas.","n":"The six streets are traditionally the six adharas, the palm and ladder the central channel climbed by breath, and the seven seas what is seen at the top. None of this is stated; the verse reads as a vision report in farming-village images.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aadhaaram","yogam","naadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:218"},"t:2869":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2869,"t":["வழுதலை வித்திடப் பாகன் முளைத்தது","புழுதியைத் தோண்டினேன் பூசணி பூத்தது","தொழுதுகொண்டு ஓடினோர் தோட்டக் குடிகண்","முழுதும் பழுத்தது வாழைக் கனியே."],"p":"","r":"I sowed brinjal and bitter gourd sprouted; I dug the dust and a pumpkin flowered; the garden-folk ran off worshipping, and the banana ripened fully.","n":"The verse is built on harvests that do not match the sowing. I can't fix what each plant stands for; one reading is that practice yields a fruit other than the one aimed at, and that the full banana ripens after the workers have turned to worship.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","payan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:218"},"t:2870":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2870,"t":["ஐஎன்னும் வித்தினில் ஆனை விளைப்பதோர்","செய்யுண்டு செய்யின் தெளிவுஅறி வாரில்லை","மையணி கண்டன் மனம்பெறின் அந்நிலம்","பொய்யொன்றும் இன்றிப் புகஎளி தாமே."],"p":"","r":"There is a field where an elephant grows from the seed called ஐ, and none know that field's clarity; if one gains the mind of the dark-throated Lord, that land is easy to enter without any falsehood.","n":"ஐ may be a seed-syllable (ஐம் is a known bija), or the word for the subtle or for the master. The elephant from a tiny seed marks the vast fruit of something minute. மையணி கண்டன் is Siva of the dark throat; entry depends on his favour, not on effort.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","sivam","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:218"},"t:2871":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2871,"t":["பள்ளச்செய் ஒன்றுண்டு பாடச்செய் இரண்டுள","கள்ளச்செய் அங்கே கலந்து கிடந்தது","உள்ளச்செய் அங்கே உழவுசெய் வார்கட்கு","வெள்ளச்செய் யாகி விளைந்தது தானே."],"p":"","r":"There is one low field and two high fields, and a thieving field lies mixed among them; for those who plough the field of the heart, it grew of itself into a flooded field.","n":"The one low and two high fields are sometimes taken as the central and the two side channels, and the thief-field as what steals the yield. உள்ளச்செய், the heart's field, is the one plain term: inner cultivation brings the abundance.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["manam","yogam","naadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:218"},"t:2872":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2872,"t":["மூவணை ஏரும் உழுவது முக்காணி","தாமணி கோலித் தறியுறப் பாய்ந்திடும்","நாஅணைகோலி நடுவில் செறுஉழார்","காலணை கோலிக்களர்உழு வாரே."],"p":"","r":"A plough of three yokes ploughs three measures of land and strikes deep to the stake; those who do not yoke the tongue and plough the middle field yoke the breath and plough barren soil.","n":"நா அணை (tongue-yoke) may mean mantra or a yogic placing of the tongue, கால் அணை (breath-yoke) breath control, and நடு the central channel. களர் (saline, barren land) suggests the breath-ploughers waste their labour, but the verse could also be praising a harder way. I can't settle which.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["praanan","yogam","mandhiram"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:218"},"t:2873":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2873,"t":["ஏற்றம் இரண்டுள ஏழு துரவுள","மூத்தான் இறைக்க இளையான் படுத்தநீர்","பாத்தியிற் பாயாது பாழ்ப்பாய்ந்து போயிடில்","கூத்தி வளர்த்ததோர் கோழிப்புள் ளாமே."],"p":"","r":"There are two water-lifts and seven wells; if the water the elder bails and the younger channels does not flow into the beds but runs to waste, it is like a hen raised by a dancing-woman.","n":"The two lifts are commonly read as the two breaths and the wasted water as breath or vital fluid lost. கூத்தி வளர்த்த கோழி, a dancer's hen, seems proverbial for something raised to no use, but I can't fix the saying.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["praanan","naadi","vindhu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:218"},"t:2874":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2874,"t":["பட்டிப் பசுக்கள் இருபத்து நாலுள","குட்டிப் பசுக்களோர்ஏழுளு ஐந்துள","குட்டிப் பசுக்கள் குடப்பால் சொரியினும்","பட்டிப் பசுவே பனவற்கு வாய்த்ததே."],"p":"","r":"There are twenty-four penned cows and calves of seven and five; though the calves pour pots of milk, it is the penned cow that suits the Brahmin.","n":"24, 7 and 5 add to 36, the Siddhanta count of tattvas, which tradition divides as 24 soul-tattvas, 7 vidya-tattvas and 5 pure tattvas; that fit makes the tattva reading likely. Why the penned cow is preferred, and who the பனவன் is, the verse does not say.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","pasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:218"},"t:2875":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2875,"t":["ஈற்றுப் பசுக்கள் இருபத்து நாலுள","ஊற்றுப் பசுக்கள் ஒரு குடம் பால்போதும்","காற்றுப் பசுக்கள் கறந்துண்ணுங் காலத்து","மாற்றுப் பசுக்கள் வரவுஅறி யோமே."],"p":"","r":"There are twenty-four calving cows, and of the spring-cows one pot of milk is enough; when the wind-cows are milked and drunk, we do not know the coming of the other cows.","n":"காற்றுப் பசுக்கள் (wind-cows) points to the breath; milking them may be breath control, after which மாற்றுப் பசுக்கள் (the other cows, perhaps births or changes) no longer come. The twenty-four again suggests the tattvas.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["praanan","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:218"},"t:2876":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2876,"t":["தட்டான் அசுத்தில் தலையான மச்சின்மேல்","மொட்டாய் எழுந்தது செம்பால் மலர்ந்தது","வட்டம் படவேண்டி வாய்மை மடித்திட்டுத்","தட்டான் அதனைத் தகைந்துகொண் டானே."],"p":"","r":"On the top floor of the goldsmith's house a bud rose and bloomed red; wanting it round, folding away the truth, the goldsmith seized it.","n":"The goldsmith is a stock figure of the cheat, and may be the mind; the bud at the top of the house may be a centre in the head. அசுத்தில் is probably a misreading of அகத்தில், as in 2924. I can't give a confident reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:218"},"t:2877":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2877,"t":["அரிக்கின்ற நாற்றங்கால் அல்லல் கழனி","திரிக்கின்ற ஒட்டம்சிக்கெனக் கட்டி","வரிக்கின்ற நல்ஆன் கறவையைப் பூட்டில்","விரிக்கின்ற வெள்ளரி வித்துவித் தாமே."],"p":"","r":"If, in the sifting seed-bed and the field of trouble, one ties fast the turning water-lift and yokes the good striped milch cow, the cucumber seed spreads and seeds.","n":"The images (seed-bed, field of trouble, water-lift, cow) are farm work turned to an unstated end; the field of trouble may be the body. The lines are too compressed for a confident reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:218"},"t:2878":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2878,"t":["இடாக்கொண்டு தூவி எருவிட்டு வித்திக்","கிடாக் கொண்டு பூட்டிக் கிளறி முளையை","மிடாக் கொண்டு சோறட்டு மெள்ள விழுங்கார்","கிடாக்கொண்டு செந்நெல் அறுக்கின்ற வாறே."],"p":"","r":"They scatter with a scoop, manure and sow, yoke the ram, stir up the sprout, yet do not cook rice in the big pot and slowly swallow it; that is how they reap red paddy with a ram.","n":"The rhymes இடா, கிடா, மிடா (scoop, ram, pot) carry the verse. One reading is that they do all the labour and never eat the harvest; the closing line may mock reaping with the wrong tool. Both readings are guesses.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","payan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:218"},"t:2879":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2879,"t":["விளைந்து கிடந்தது மேலைக்கு வித்தது","விளைந்து கிடந்தது மேலைக்குக் காதம்","விளைந்து விளைந்து விளைந்துகொள் வார்க்கு","விளைந்து கிடந்தது மேவுமுக் காதமே."],"p":"","r":"The crop lies ripe, seed for what is above; it lies ripe a league above; for those who reap it again and again, the ripe crop spreads over three leagues.","n":"விளைந்து (ripened) is repeated seven times. மேலை may be 'above' (the head, in yoga) or 'the hereafter'. காதம் is a measure of distance, and the growth from one to three leagues marks a yield that increases with repeated reaping.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["payan","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:218"},"t:2880":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2880,"t":["களர்உழு வார்கள் கருத்தை அறியோம்","களர்உழு வார்கள் கருதலும் இல்லைக்","களர்உழு வார்கள் களரின் முளைத்த","வளர்இள வஞ்சியின்மாய்தலும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"We do not know the intent of those who plough barren land, and they have no intent at all; the young creeper that sprouts in barren land may also wither.","n":"This takes up களர் உழுவார் from 2872. The verse seems to blame aimless labour on soil that cannot hold a crop, so that even what sprouts dies; what the barren land stands for is not said.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:218"},"t:2881":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2881,"t":["கூப்பிடு கொள்ளாக் குறுநரி கொட்டகத்து","ஆப்பிடு பாசத்தை அங்கியுள் வைத்திட்டு","நாட்பட நின்று நலம்புகுந்து ஆயிழை","ஏற்பட இல்லத்து இனிதிருந்தானே."],"p":"","r":"Placing in the fire the wedging noose for the little jackal in the shed that won't come when called, standing long and entering into good, he lived sweetly in the house with the jewelled woman.","n":"The jackal that won't come when called may be the unruly mind, and burning the noose the ending of bonds. ஆயிழை (the jewelled woman) is often taken as Shakti. The syntax is loose and the reading tentative.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["paasam","manam","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:218"},"t:2882":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2882,"t":["மலைமேல் மழைபெய்ய மான்கன்று துள்ளக்","குலைமேல் இருந்த கொழுங்கனி வீழ","உலைமேல் இருந்த உறுப்பெனக் கொல்லன்","முலைமேல் அமிர்தம் பொழியவைத் தானே."],"p":"","r":"When rain falls on the mountain the fawn leaps and the ripe fruit on the bunch falls; the smith set the nectar to pour onto the breast as onto a piece on the forge.","n":"Tradition reads the mountain as the head, the rain as the nectar that flows from it in yoga, and the smith as the Lord or the yogi. The forge image (உலை) suggests heat that releases the flow. The fawn and the fruit are unexplained.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["amudham","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:218"},"t:2883":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2883,"t":["பார்ப்பான் அகத்திலே பாற்பசு ஐந்துண்டு","மேய்ப்பாரும் இன்றி வெறித்துத் திரிவன","மேய்ப்பாரும் உண்டாய் வெறியும் அடங்கினால்","பார்ப்பான் பசுஐந்தும் பாலாச் சொரியுமே."],"p":"","r":"In the Brahmin's house there are five milch cows that roam wild with no one to herd them; if a herdsman comes and their wildness is checked, the Brahmin's five cows will pour milk.","n":"The five cows are widely taken as the five senses, and the closer chapter on restraining them (2025) uses the same herdsman. The point is that the senses, once herded, give milk: restraint makes them yield, not die. The herdsman may be the guru or the disciplined mind.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","manam","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:218"},"t:2884":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2884,"t":["ஆமாக்கள் ஐந்தும் அரியேறு முப்பதும்","தேமா இரண்டொடு திப்பிலி ஒன்பதும்","தாமாக் குரங்கொளில் தம்மனத் துள்ளன","மூவாக் கடாவிடின் மூட்டுகின் றாரே."],"p":"","r":"Five wild cows, thirty lions, two sweet mangoes with nine long peppers: if one holds them as one's own monkey, they are within one's mind; if the unageing buffalo is let loose, they set it ablaze.","n":"The numbers suggest a count of tattvas or bodily powers, but I can't map them. குரங்கொளில் and மூவாக் கடா (the unageing buffalo, perhaps death or the mind) are obscure, and the last line may be corrupt.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:218"},"t:2885":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2885,"t":["எழுதாத புத்தகத்து ஏட்டின் பொருளைத்","தெருளாத கன்னி தெறிந்திருந்து ஓத","மலராத பூவின் மணத்தின் மதுவைப்","பிறவாத வண்டு மணமுண்ட வாறே."],"p":"","r":"As an unschooled maiden reads aloud the meaning of the leaves of an unwritten book, so an unborn bee drank the honey from the scent of an unopened flower.","n":"Four negatives (எழுதாத, தெருளாத, மலராத, பிறவாத) make a knowing that comes before every means of knowing. It is commonly read as the soul's experience of Siva, beyond book, learning and sense; the verse states only the paradox.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:218"},"t:2886":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2886,"t":["போகின்ற பொய்யும் புகுகின்ற பொய்வித்தும்","கூகின்ற நாவலின் கூழைத் தருங்கனி","ஆநின்ற வைங்கூழ் அவையுண்ணும் ஐவரும்","வேகின்ற கூரை விருத்திபெற் றாரே."],"p":"","r":"The falsehood that goes, the false seed that enters, the fruit of the naval tree that gives gruel, the green crop standing and the five who eat it: under a burning roof they prospered.","n":"The five who eat are probably the senses, and the burning roof the body, but the first two lines (போகின்ற பொய், புகுகின்ற பொய்வித்து) may mean deeds that leave and deeds that come in as seed for birth. Very uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","vinai","adakkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:218"},"t:2887":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2887,"t":["மூங்கில் முளையில் எழுந்ததோர் வேம்புண்டு","வேம்பினில் சார்ந்து கிடந்த பனையிலோர்","பாம்புண்டு பாம்பைத் துரத்தித்தின் பார்இன்றி","வேம்பு கிடந்து வெடிக்கின்ற வாறே."],"p":"","r":"A neem rose from a bamboo shoot; on a palm leaning on the neem there is a snake; with no one to chase the snake and eat it, the neem lies there bursting.","n":"Tradition takes the bamboo as the spine and the snake as kundalini. The bursting neem, bitter and unfruitful, may be the body wasted when the serpent power is not roused. This is the tradition's key, not the verse's.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kundalini","naadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:218"},"t:2888":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2888,"t":["பத்துப் பரும்புலி யானை பதினைந்து","வித்தகர் ஐவர் வினோகர் ஈ ரெண்மர்","அத்தகு மூவர் அறுவர் மருத்துவர்","அத்தலை ஐவர் அமர்ந்து நின் றாரே."],"p":"","r":"Ten great tigers, fifteen elephants, five skilled ones, sixteen entertainers, three worthy ones, six physicians and five at the head sit and stand there.","n":"A census of a body's or a town's inhabitants, probably standing for tattvas, breaths, senses and organs. I can't match the numbers to a known list.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:218"},"t:2889":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2889,"t":["இரண்டு கடாவுண்டு இவ்வூரின் உள்ளே","இரண்டு கடாவுக்கும் ஒன்றே தொழும்பின்","இரண்டு கடாவும் இருத்திப் பிடிக்கில்","இரண்டு கடாவும் ஒருகடா வாமே."],"p":"","r":"There are two buffaloes within this town and one service for both; if both are held fast, the two buffaloes become one.","n":"The two are usually taken as the two breaths, or the right and left channels, which become one when held. The town is the body. The pattern of two becoming one is the clearest thing in the verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["praanan","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:218"},"t:2890":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2890,"t":["ஒத்த மனக்கொல்லை உள்ளே சமன்கட்டிப்","பத்தி வலையில் பருத்தி நிறுத்தலால்","முத்தம் கயிறாக மூவர்கள் ஊரினுள்","நித்தம் பொருது நிரம்பநின் றாரே."],"p":"","r":"Setting a balance within the field of the even mind and weighing cotton in the net of devotion, with pearl as the rope, the three in the town fight daily and stand full.","n":"ஒத்த மனம் (the even mind) and பத்தி (devotion) are plain; the cotton, pearl rope and the three who fight are not. The three may be the gunas, kept in balance.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["manam","anbu","mukkunam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:218"},"t:2891":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2891,"t":["கூகையும் பாம்பும் கிளியொடு பூஞையும்","நாகையும் பூழும் நடுவில் உறைவன","நாகையைக் கூகை நணுகல் உறுதலும்","கூகையைக் கண்டெலி கூப்பிடு மாறே."],"p":"","r":"The owl, the snake, the parrot and the cat, the naagai and the quail live in the middle; when the owl draws near the naagai, the rat seeing the owl cries out.","n":"A household of creatures that prey on each other, probably faculties or passions in the body. நாகை is unclear (perhaps a bird or a cobra). I can't fix the code.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["manam","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:218"},"t:2892":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2892,"t":["குலைக்கின்ற நன்னகை யாம்கொங்கு உழக்கின்","நிலைக்கின்ற வெள்ளெலி மூன்று கொணர்ந்தான்","உலைக்குப் புறமெனில் ஓடும் இருக்கும்","புலைக்குப் பிறந்தவை போகின்ற வாறே."],"p":"","r":"He brought three white rats that stay in the laughing, scented measure; if told to leave the forge they run and they stay; that is how those born of baseness go.","n":"The three white rats may be the three malas or the three gunas, and the forge the body or the discipline; the first line is obscure and may be corrupt. Only the three and the forge stand out.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["malam","mukkunam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:218"},"t:2893":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2893,"t":["காடுபுக் கார்இனிக் காணார் கருவெளி","கூடுபுக்கு ஆனது ஐந்து குதிரையும்","மூடுபுக்கு ஆனது ஆறுள ஒட்டகம்","மூடு புகாவிடின் மூவனை யாமே."],"p":"","r":"Those who have entered the forest will no longer see the space of the womb; the five horses have entered the nest and the six camels the cover; if they do not enter the cover, there are the three bonds.","n":"கருவெளி (the womb-space) suggests that those who have gone in are free of birth. The five horses may be the senses and the six camels the six adharas or the inner organs; மூவணை (three yokes or three bonds) is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","adakkam","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:218"},"t:2894":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2894,"t":["கூறையும் சோறும் குழாயகத்து எண்ணெயும்","காறையும் நாணும் வளையலும் கண்டவர்","பாறையில் உற்ற பறக்கின்ற சீலைபோல்","ஆறைக் குழியில் அழுந்துகின் றாரே."],"p":"","r":"Those who set their eyes on clothing and food, oil in the tube, necklace, marriage thread and bangles sink into the pit like a fluttering cloth caught on a rock.","n":"The list is the goods of household life and of a wife's adornment, so the warning is against being taken by them. The cloth on the rock is plain enough as an image of being caught; ஆறைக் குழி (the pit, perhaps a river-pit) is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pattru","ilvaazhkkai","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:218"},"t:2895":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2895,"t":["துருத்தியுள் அக்கரை தோன்றும் மலைமேல்","விருத்திகண் காணிக்கப் போவார்முப் போதும்","வருத்திஉள் நின்ற மலையைத் தவிர்ப்பாள்","ஒருத்திஉள் ளாள்அவர் ஊர்அறி யோமே."],"p":"","r":"On the mountain where the far shore appears within the bellows, people go three times a day to offer what they have earned; there is one woman who removes the mountain that stood within afflicting them; we do not know their town.","n":"துருத்தி is a bellows and so the breath, and also the name of the shrine Tiruthuruthi; both may be meant. The one woman (ஒருத்தி) is taken as Shakti, who removes the inner obstacle. The three times a day may be the three breath-turns or the three daily rites.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","praanan","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:218"},"t:2896":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2896,"t":["பருந்துங் கிளியும் படுபறை கொட்டத்","திருந்திய மாதர் திருமணப் பட்டார்","பெருந்தவப் பூதம் போலுரு வாகும்","இருந்திய பேற்றினில் இன்புறு வாரே."],"p":"","r":"As the kite and the parrot beat the drum, well-adorned women were married; taking form like a spirit of great penance, they rejoice in the fortune they gained.","n":"A wedding with birds for drummers. The kite and parrot may be two breaths or two powers, and the marriage a union in yoga; பூதம் may mean the elements rather than a goblin. Very uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:218"},"t:2897":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2897,"t":["கூடும் பறவை இரைகொத்தி மற்றதன்","ஊடுபுக்கு உண்டி அறுக்குறில் என்ஒக்கும்","சூடுஎறி நெய்யுண்டு மைகான்று இடுகின்ற","பாடுஅறி வார்க்குப் பயன்எளி தாமே."],"p":"","r":"What is it like when a flocking bird pecks its food and, entering into it, cuts off its food? For those who know the way of the lamp, which drinks ghee thrown on the heat and spits out soot, the fruit is easy.","n":"The second half describes a lamp: it takes in ghee and gives off black soot (மை) while giving light. Tradition may read it as the yogi who takes in and burns away. The first image is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","payan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:218"},"t:2898":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2898,"t":["இலைஇல்லை பூவுண்டு இனவண்டு இங்கில்லை","தலைஇல்லை வேர்உண்டு தாள்இல்லை பூவின்","குலைஇல்லை கொய்யும் மலர்உண்டு சூடும்","தலைஇல்லை தாழ்ந்த கிளைபுல ராதே."],"p":"","r":"No leaf, yet there is a flower; no swarm of bees here; no top, yet a root; no stalk, no cluster of flowers, yet a flower to pluck; no head to wear it; and the low-hanging branch never withers.","n":"A plant described by what it lacks, like 2885. It is commonly read as the formless reality, or the flower of inner worship that needs no head to wear it. The unwithering branch suggests what does not perish.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:218"},"t:2899":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2899,"t":["அக்கரை நின்றதோர் ஆல மரங்கண்டு","நக்கரை வாழ்த்தி நடுவே பயன்கொள்வர்","மிக்கவர் அஞ்சு துயரமும் கண்டுபோய்த்","தக்கவர் தாழ்ந்து கிடக்கின்ற வாறே."],"p":"","r":"Seeing a banyan standing on the far shore, they praise the Naked One and take its fruit in the middle; the eminent, seeing the five sorrows, go there, and the worthy lie prostrate.","n":"நக்கர் is Siva as the naked one, and the banyan recalls Siva as teacher under the banyan. The far shore is release, the middle perhaps the central channel. அஞ்சு துயரம் (five sorrows) are not named.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","vazhipaadu","idumbai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:218"},"t:2900":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2900,"t":["கூப்பிடும் ஆற்றிலே வன்காடு இருகாதம்","காப்பிடு கள்ளர் கலந்துநின் றார்உளர்","காப்பிடு கள்ளரை வெள்ளர் தொடர்ந்திட்டுக்","கூப்பிட மீண்டதோர் கூரை கொண் டாரே."],"p":"","r":"By the calling river there is a hard forest two leagues long, where guarding thieves have mingled; the honest ones, chasing the guarding thieves, took back a roof that had turned back at the call.","n":"The guarding thieves (காப்பிடு கள்ளர்) are likely the senses, which seem to guard but steal, and வெள்ளர் the pure-minded who pursue them. The roof (கூரை) may be the body. Very tentative.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:218"},"t:2901":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2901,"t":["கொட்டியும் ஆம்பலும் பூத்த குளத்திடை","எட்டியும் வேம்பும் இனியதோர் வாழையும்","கட்டியும் தேனும் கலந்துண்ண மாட்டாதார்","எட்டிப் பழத்துக்கு இளைக்கின்ற வாறே."],"p":"","r":"In a pond where kotti and water-lily bloom stand the bitter etti and neem and a sweet banana; those who will not eat jaggery and honey mixed together waste away for the etti fruit.","n":"எட்டி is the poisonous strychnine tree. The verse does not name what the sweet and the bitter stand for; read as a riddle, the sweet may be the Lord or true knowledge and the bitter the pleasures of sense. The plain point is choosing the bitter with the sweet at hand.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai","pattru","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:218"},"t:2902":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2902,"t":["பெடைவண்டும் ஆண்வண்டும் பீடிகை வண்ணக்","குடைகொண்ட பாசத்துக் கோலம் உண் டானும்","கடைவண்டு தான் உண்ணும் கண்கலந் திட்ட","பெடைவண்டு தான்பெற்றது இன்பமும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"The female bee and the male bee, and the one who enjoyed the form of the bond under a canopy on a coloured pedestal: the last bee itself drinks, and the female bee whose eyes met it gained joy.","n":"The pair of bees may be Shakti and Siva, or the soul and the Lord; பீடிகை (pedestal) suggests an image in worship. The syntax is unclear and the reading is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","sivam","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:218"},"t:2903":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2903,"t":["கொல்லையில் மேயும் பசுக்களைச் செய்வதன்","எல்லை கடப்பித்து இறையடிக் கூட்டியே","வல்லசெய்து ஆற்றல் மதித்தபின் அல்லது","கொல்லசெய் நெஞ்சம் குறியறி யாதே."],"p":"","r":"Unless one drives the cows grazing in the field past its boundary, gathers them to the Lord's feet, works strongly and weighs one's strength, the heart that kills will not know the mark.","n":"The cows may be the senses, as in 2883, or the souls (பசு in the Siddhanta sense), led past the bounds of the world to the Lord's feet. கொல்லசெய் நெஞ்சம் (the killing heart) and குறி (the mark, the goal) are unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pasu","thiruvadi","adakkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:218"},"t:2904":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2904,"t":["தட்டத்து நீரிலே தாமரை பூத்தது","குட்டத்து நீரில் குவளை எழுந்தது","விட்டத்தின் உள்ளே விளங்கவல் லார்கட்குக்","குட்டத்தில் கிட்டதோர் கொம்மட்டி யாமே."],"p":"","r":"A lotus bloomed in the water on a plate; a blue lily rose in the water of a deep pond; for those who can shine within the beam, a colocynth is near in the pond.","n":"Lotus and lily are common names for centres in the body; விட்டம் (beam, crossbar, or diameter) may be the space in the head. கொம்மட்டி is a bitter gourd, so the last line may be ironic. I can't fix it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aadhaaram","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:218"},"t:2905":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2905,"t":["ஆறு பறவைகள் ஐந்தக்து உள்ளன","நூறு பறவை நுனிக்கொம்பின் மேலன","ஏறும் பெரும்பதி ஏழுங் கடந்தபின்","மாறுதல் இன்றி மனைபுக லாமே."],"p":"","r":"Six birds are within five, and a hundred birds sit on the topmost branch; after crossing the seven great cities that rise, one may enter the house without returning.","n":"The seven cities are commonly taken as the six adharas and the crown, after which one reaches the home (மனை) without change or return (மாறுதல் இன்றி). The six, five and hundred are unexplained. ஐந்தக்து is a garbled form.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aadhaaram","veedu","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:218"},"t:2906":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2906,"t":["கொட்டனஞ் செய்து குளிக்கின்ற கூவலுள்","வட்டனப் பூமி மருவிவந் தூறிடுங்","கட்டனஞ் செய்து கயிற்றால் தொழுமியுள்","ஒட்டனஞ் செய்தொளி யாவர்க்கு மாமே."],"p":"","r":"In the well one bathes in, the round earth comes and seeps; tying it with rope and keeping it close, it becomes a light to all.","n":"கொட்டனம், வட்டனம், கட்டனம், ஒட்டனம் rhyme across the lines and their senses are unclear. I can give only the outline.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:218"},"t:2907":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2907,"t":["ஏழு வளைகடல் எட்டுக் குலவரை","யாழும் விசும்பினில் அங்கி மழைவளி","தாழு மிருநிலந் தன்மை ய்துகண்டு","வாழ நினைக்கில தாலய மாமே."],"p":"","r":"The seven encircling seas, the eight great mountain ranges, the deep sky with its fire, rain and wind, and the low wide earth: seeing their nature, if one thinks to live, it becomes a temple.","n":"The verse may mean that the whole cosmos is the Lord's temple, or that the body, which holds these seas, mountains and elements, is. The last line (வாழ நினைக்கில் அது ஆலயம் ஆமே) is compressed, and the third line is garbled (தன்மை ய்துகண்டு).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["andam","yaakkai","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:218"},"t:2908":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2908,"t":["ஆலிங் கனஞ்செய்து அகம்சுடச் சூலத்துச்","சால்இங்கு அமைத்துத் தலைமை தவிர்த்தனர்","கோல்இங்கு அமைத்தபின் கூபப் பறவைகள்","மால்இங்கன் வைத்துஅது முன்பின் வழியே."],"p":"","r":"Embracing, burning the inner self, setting the furrow at the trident, they put away pride of place; after setting the staff here, the well-birds, placing their confusion here, it is the way before and after.","n":"சூலம் (trident) and கோல் (staff) may be the central channel; burning within (அகம் சுட) suggests yogic heat. The syntax of the last two lines is unclear and I can't give a reliable reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","kundalini"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:218"},"t:2909":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2909,"t":["கொட்டுக்கும் தாலி இரண்டே இரண்டுக்கும்","கொட்டுக்கும் தாலிக்கும் பாரை வலிதென்பர்","கொட்டுக்கும் தாலிக்கும் பாரைக்கும் மூன்றுக்கும்","இட்டம் வலிதென்பர் ஈசன் அருளே."],"p":"","r":"The drum and the marriage thread are two, and they say the crowbar is stronger than both; than all three, drum, thread and crowbar, they say the Lord's grace, his love, is stronger.","n":"Drum and tali are the marks of a wedding; the crowbar (பாரை) may be force or effort. The closing is plain: இட்டம் (love, favour) and ஈசன் அருள் outweigh all. What the three stand for is the tradition's guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:218"},"t:2910":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2910,"t":["கயலொன்று கண்டவர் கண்டே இருப்பர்","முயலொன்று கண்டவர் மூவரும் உய்வர்","பறையொன்று பூசல் பிடிப்பான் ஒருவன்","மறையொன்று கண்ட துருவம் போ லாமே."],"p":"","r":"Those who have seen a carp stay seeing; those who have seen a hare, all three are saved; one man holds the drum and the clamour, like the form a Veda has seen.","n":"கயல் (carp) is a stock image for the eye and முயல் (hare) for the moon; these may point to inner sight and the moon of yoga. The second half is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:218"},"t:2911":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2911,"t":["கோரை எழுந்து கிடந்த குளத்தினில்","ஆரை படர்ந்து தொடர்ந்து கிடந்தது","நாரை படுக்கின்றாற் போலல்ல நாதனார்","பாரை கிடக்கப் படிகின்ற வாறே."],"p":"","r":"In a pond where sedge rose, the water-clover spread and clung; not as a heron lies in wait, the Lord settles there while the rock lies.","n":"The heron waiting for fish is a stock image of feigned stillness; the Lord's stillness is said not to be that. What the pond, sedge and rock stand for is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:218"},"t:2912":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2912,"t":["கொல்லைமுக் காதமும் காடுஅரைக் காதமும்","எல்லை மயங்கிக் கிடந்த இருநெறி","எல்லை மயங்காது இயங்க வல் லார்கட்கு","ஒல்லை கடந்துசென்று ஊர்புக லாமே."],"p":"","r":"A field of three leagues and a forest of half a league: two paths lie with their boundary confused; for those who can move without confusing the boundary, passing quickly, they may enter the town.","n":"The two paths may be the two breaths or the two ways of the world and of release; keeping their boundary clear lets one reach the town, which tradition takes as Siva's abode. The measures are unexplained.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","veedu","naadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:218"},"t:2913":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2913,"t":["உழவொன்று வித்து ஒருங்கின காலத்து","எழுமழை பெய்யாது இருநிலச் செல்வி","தழுவி வினைசெய்து தான்பய வாது","வழுவாது போவன் வளர்சடை யோனே."],"p":"","r":"When ploughing and sowing are done together the rain does not fall; the lady of the wide earth embraces and works yet yields nothing; the one of growing matted hair goes on without fail.","n":"The plain image is labour without rain, effort without grace. The lady of the earth may be Shakti or the body; வளர்சடையோன் is Siva. Whether the last line means he stays unmoved or passes by is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","vinai","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:218"},"t:2914":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2914,"t":["பதுங்கிலும் பாய்புலி பன்னிரு காதம்","ஒதுங்கிய தண்கடல் ஓதம் உலவ","மதுங்கிய வார்களி ஆரமுது ஊறப்","பொதுங்கிய ஐவரைப் போய்வளைத் தானே."],"p":"","r":"The leaping tiger, even while crouching, covers twelve leagues; the tide of the cool sea that drew back flows again; the honeyed nectar wells up, and he went and surrounded the five common ones.","n":"Twelve is the traditional measure, in finger-breadths, of the outgoing breath, so the tiger may be the breath; the welling nectar is a known yogic image. The five are probably the senses, hemmed in. These are traditional keys.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["praanan","amudham","adakkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:218"},"t:2915":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2915,"t":["தோணியொன்று ஏறித் தொடர்ந்து கடல்புக்கு","வாணிபம் செய்து வழங்கி வளர்மகன்","நீலிக்கு இறையுமே நெஞ்சின் நிலைதளர்ந்து","ஆலிப் பழம்போல் அளிக்கின்ற அப்பே."],"p":"","r":"A son grew by boarding a boat, going to sea, trading and giving; when his heart falters before Neeli, the father gives like a hailstone fruit.","n":"நீலி may be the dark goddess or a deceiving woman; ஆலிப்பழம் (hailstone) melts at once. The son may be the soul trading in the sea of births, and the father the Lord. The text may be corrupt (நீலிக்கு இறையுமே).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["uyir","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:218"},"t:2916":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2916,"t":["முக்காதம் ஆற்றிலே மூன்றுள வாழைகள்","செக்குப் பழுத்த திரிமலம் காய்த்தன","பக்கனார் மிக்கார் படங்கினார் கன்னியர்","நக்கு மலருண்டு நடுவுநின் றாரே."],"p":"","r":"In a river three leagues long there are three banana trees, ripened in the oil-press, which bore the three impurities as fruit; and, in an obscure line, the maidens, laughing, ate the flowers and stood in the middle.","n":"திரிமலம் (the three impurities) is named outright, so the three bananas are probably the soul's three bonds. The third line (பக்கனார் மிக்கார் படங்கினார்) is obscure and may be corrupt.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["malam","mukkunam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:218"},"t:2917":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2917,"t":["அடியும் முடியும் அமைந்ததோர் ஆத்தி","முடியும் நுனியின் கண் முத்தலை மூங்கில்","கொடியும் படையும் கோட்சரன் ஐஐந்து","மடியும் வலம்புரி வாய்த்தது அவ் வாறே."],"p":"","r":"An aaththi tree complete from root to crown, at whose tip is a three-headed bamboo; flag, weapon and the planets' arrows, five fives; the right-whorled conch came that way.","n":"ஆத்தி (bauhinia) is a garland flower of Siva, and the three-headed bamboo may be the trident or the three channels. ஐஐந்து (twenty-five) may be a tattva count. I can't give a reliable reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:218"},"t:2918":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2918,"t":["பன்றியும் பாம்பும் பசுமுசு வானரம்","தென்றிக் கிடந்த சிறுநரிக் கூட்டத்துக்","குன்றாமை கூடித் தராசின் நிறுத்தபின்","குன்றி நிறையைக் குறைக்கின்ற வாறே."],"p":"","r":"For the herd of little jackals lying scattered, with the pig, the snake, the cow and the monkey, after weighing them on a balance without loss, the weight is brought down to a kunri seed.","n":"The beasts may be passions or faculties, weighed and reduced to the weight of a குன்றி seed, the smallest jeweller's weight. The reading of the beasts is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:218"},"t:2919":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2919,"t":["மொட்டித்து எழுந்ததோர் மொட்டுண்டு மொட்டினைக்","கட்டுவிட்டு ஓடின் மலர்தலும் காணலாம்","பற்றுவிட்டு அம்மனை பாழ்பட நோக்கினால்","கட்டுவிட் டார்க்கு அன்றிக் காணஒண் ணாதே."],"p":"","r":"There is a bud that rose budding; if its binding is loosed and it runs free, one can see it bloom; looking with attachment let go, so that that house falls to waste, it cannot be seen except by those who have loosed the bond.","n":"கட்டுவிட்டு (loosing the bond) frames the verse: the bud opens when untied, and only the unbound see. அம்மனை பாழ்பட நோக்கினால் is unclear; it may mean seeing the house of the body or household as empty.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pattru","paasam","thuravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:218"},"t:2920":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2920,"t":["நீரின்றிப் பாயும் நிலத்தினிற் பச்சையாம்","யாவரும் என்றும் அறியவல் லார்இல்லை","கூரும் மழைபொழி யாது பொழிபுனல்","தேரின் இந் நீர்மை திடரின்நில் லாதே."],"p":"","r":"There is green in a land watered without water, and no one can ever know it; the water that pours where the sharp rain does not fall, if you search, does not stay on high ground.","n":"Water without rain may be the inner nectar of yoga, which greens the body; that it does not stay on the high ground may mean it must be held. Very uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["amudham","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:218"},"t:2921":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2921,"t":["கூகை குருந்தமது ஏறிக் குணம் பயில்","மோகம் உலகுக்கு உணர்கின்ற காலத்து","நாகமும் ஒன்று நடுவுரை செய்திடும்","பாகனும் ஆகின்ற பண்பனும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"When the owl climbs the kurundham tree and practises its nature, at the time the world perceives delusion, a snake too speaks in the middle; he becomes both the mahout and the one of good nature.","n":"The snake speaking in the middle may be kundalini in the central channel and the mahout the controller of the mind. குருந்தம் is also the tree under which Siva is said to have taught Manikkavasagar. I can't fix the code.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["manam","kundalini"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:218"},"t:2922":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2922,"t":["வாழையும் சூரையும் வந்திடம் கொண்டன","வாழைக்குச் சூரை வலிது வலிதென்பர்","வாழையும் சூரையும் வன்துண்டம் செய்திட்டு","வாழை இடம்கொண்டு வாழ்கின்ற வாறே."],"p":"","r":"The banana and the thorny soorai came and took the place, and they say the soorai is stronger than the banana; but cutting both into hard pieces, the banana takes the place and lives.","n":"The plain image is a fruitful plant and a thorny weed competing for ground, the weed thought stronger yet the banana winning. Tradition may read them as knowledge and impurity, or good and evil deeds; the verse does not say.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["malam","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:218"},"t:2923":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2923,"t":["நிலத்தைப் பிளந்து நெடுங்கடல் ஓட்டிப்","புனத்துக் குறவன் புணர்ந்த கொழுமீன்","விளக்குமின் யாவர்க்கும் வேண்டிற் குறையாது","அருத்தமும் இன்றி அடுவதும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"Splitting the land and driving out the long sea, the plump fish the hill-hunter of the field caught: light it up for all; if wanted it will not lessen, and it cooks without cost.","n":"The images of hunter, field and fish are mixed and the syntax is unclear. அருத்தமும் இன்றி may mean without cost or without meaning. I can give only a tentative outline.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:218"},"t:2924":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2924,"t":["தளிர்க்கும் ஒருபிள்ளை தட்டான் அகத்தில்","விளிப்பதோர் சங்குண்டு வேந்தணை நாடிக்","களிக்கும் குசவர்க்கும் காவிதி யார்க்கும்","அளிக்கும் பதத்தொன்று ஆய்ந்து கொள் வார்க்கே."],"p":"","r":"In the goldsmith's house there is a child that sprouts and a conch that calls, seeking the king's bed; to the potters who rejoice and the titled men, it gives a measure, to those who examine and take it.","n":"The goldsmith's house recurs from 2876; the calling conch may be inner sound. The rest is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:218"},"t:2925":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2925,"t":["குடைவிட்டுப் போந்தது கோயில் எருமை","படைகண்டு மீண்டது பாதி வழியில்","உடையவன் மந்திரி உள்ளலும் ஊரார்","அடையா நெடுங்கடை ஐந்தொடு நான்கே."],"p":"","r":"The temple buffalo left the canopy and came out; seeing the army, it turned back halfway; the owner, the minister and the townsfolk: the long gates that cannot be shut are five and four.","n":"Five and four are nine, the body's nine openings, which tradition calls its gates; the town is then the body, its owner the soul and its minister the mind. The buffalo may be the mind or death. Tentative.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:218"},"t:2926":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2926,"t":["போகின்ற எட்டும் புகுகின்ற பத்தெட்டும்","ஆகிப் படைத்தன ஒன்பது வாய்தலும்","நாகமும் எட்டொடு நாலு புரவியும்","பாகன் விடாவிடில் பன்றியும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"Eight that go out and eighteen that come in made the nine gates; the snake with the eight and the four horses: if the mahout does not let go, it may even become a pig.","n":"The going and coming numbers suggest breath measures, and the nine gates the body's openings, as in 2925. பத்தெட்டு may be eighteen or ten and eight. The last line seems to warn of a low birth; I can't be sure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["praanan","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:218"},"t:2927":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2927,"t":["பாசி பாடர்ந்து கிடந்த குளத்திடைக்","கூசி யிருக்கும் குருகு இரைதேர்ந்துண்ணும்","தூசி மறவன் துணைவழி எய்திடப்","பாசங் கிடந்து பதைக்கின்ற வாறே."],"p":"","r":"In a pond overgrown with moss, a shy heron seeks its food and eats; when the warrior of the vanguard reaches it with his companion, the bond lies there trembling.","n":"The heron fishing in a mossy pond may be the soul feeding in the world, and the vanguard warrior death, at whose coming the bond (பாசம்) trembles. பாசம் is also Yama's noose. Tentative; பாடர்ந்து is a misprint for படர்ந்து.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["paasam","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:218"},"t:2928":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2928,"t":["கும்ப மலைமேல் எழுந்ததோர் கொம்புண்டு","கொம்புக்கும் அப்பால் அடிப்பதோர் காற்றுண்டு","வம்பாய் மலர்ந்ததோர் பூவுடைப் பூவக்குள்","வண்டாய்க் கிடந்து மணங்கொள்வன் ஈசனே."],"p":"","r":"On the pot-mountain a branch has risen, and beyond the branch a wind blows; within a flower that bloomed freshly, Isan lies as a bee and takes its fragrance.","n":"கும்பம் (pot) may be the head, or கும்பகம், the held breath; the wind beyond is the breath and the flower a centre in the head. The last line is plain: the Lord dwells as a bee in the flower. பூவக்குள் is a misprint for பூவுக்குள்.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","praanan","aadhaaram"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:218"},"t:2929":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2929,"t":["வீணையும் தண்டும் விரவி இசைமுரல்","தாணுவும் மேவித் தருதலைப் பெய்தது","வாணிபம் சிக்கென்று அதுஅடை யாமுன்னம்","காணியும் அங்கே கலக்கின்ற வாறே."],"p":"","r":"The vina and its staff mingle and hum music, and the pillar too joined and filled the head; before the trade was settled, the holding there is mixed in.","n":"The vina's staff (தண்டு) is a known image for the spine, and its music for inner sound; தாணு (the pillar) is a name of Siva. The trade and holding in the last two lines are unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naadham","naadi","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:218"},"t:2930":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2930,"t":["கொங்குபுக் காரொடு வாணிபம் செய்தது","வங்குபுக் காலன்றி ஆய்ந்தறி வாரில்லை","திங்கள்புக்கால் இருளாவது அறிந்திலர்","தங்குபுக் கார்சிலர் தாபதர் தாமே."],"p":"","r":"It traded with those who entered Kongu; none examine and know it except when it enters the ship; they did not know that when the moon enters it becomes dark; some who entered and stayed are ascetics.","n":"திங்கள் (the moon) entering and darkness following may refer to the moon-breath or a phase of breath control. கொங்கு and வங்கு (Kongu or pollen; ship) are puns I can't resolve.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalai","yogam","thavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:218"},"t:2931":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2931,"t":["போதும் புலர்fந்தது பொன்னிறங் கொண்டது","தாதவிழ் புன்னை தயங்கும் இருகரை","ஏதமில் ஈசன்இயங்கு நெறியிது","மாதர் இருந்ததோர் மண்டலம் தானே."],"p":"","r":"Dawn broke and turned golden; the two banks where punnai trees open their pollen shimmer; this is the path where the flawless Isan moves, the circle where the lady dwells.","n":"The two banks may be the two side channels with the path between them the central one, and the மண்டலம் where the lady dwells a centre of Shakti. The first line reads புலர்fந்தது, a corruption of புலர்ந்தது.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["sivam","saththi","naadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:218"},"t:2932":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2932,"t":["கோமுற்று அமரும் குடிகளும் தம்மிலே","காமுற்று அகத்தி இடுவர் கடைபொறும்","வீவற்ற எல்லை விடாது வழிகாட்டி","யாமுற்ற அதட்டினால் ஐந்துண்ண லாமே."],"p":"","r":"The people who sit with their king, desiring among themselves, set things within and bear at the door; the guide who does not leave the deathless boundary shows the way, and at our rebuke the five may be eaten.","n":"The king and people may be the soul and its faculties, the guide the guru, and the five the senses brought under rebuke. ஐந்துண்ணலாம் is unclear: the five may be enjoyed, or consumed. Very tentative.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:218"},"t:2933":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2933,"t":["தோட்டத்தில் மாம்பழம் தோண்டி விழுந்தக்கால்","நாட்டின் புறத்தில் நரியழைத்து என்செயும்","மூட்டிக் கொடுத்து முதல்வனை முன்னிட்டு","காட்டிக் கொடுத்தவர் கைவிட்ட வாறே."],"p":"","r":"When the mango in the garden has been dug out and fallen, what will calling the jackal on the edge of the land do? That is how those who kindled it, set the First One before them and showed it, let it go.","n":"காட்டிக் கொடுத்தல் is to betray or to point out; கைவிடுதல் to abandon. The verse may speak of those who showed the Lord and then forsook him, or of a teacher who shows and lets go. I can't settle it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:218"},"t:2934":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2934,"t":["புலர்ந்தது போதென்று புட்கள் சிலம்பப்","புலர்ந்தது போதென்று பூங்கொடி புல்லிப்","புலம்பின் அவளொடும் போகம் நுகரும்","புலம்பனுக்கு என்றும் புலர்ந்திலை போதே."],"p":"","r":"Birds cry out that dawn has come and the flowering creeper clings saying dawn has come; for the lonely man who enjoys pleasure with her in his longing, dawn has never broken.","n":"புலர்ந்தது போது (dawn has come) is repeated, and the last line denies it: புலர்ந்திலை போதே. Read as a riddle, dawn is awakening, which the pleasure-taker never meets. புலம்பன் is also the Sangam name for the hero of the seashore, so the verse borrows the love-poetry frame.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","pochchaappu","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:218"},"t:2935":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2935,"t":["தோணி ஒன்று உண்டு துறையில் விடுவது","வாணி மிதித்துநின்று ஐவர்கோல் ஊன்றலும்","வாணிபம் செய்வார் வழியிடை ஆற்றிடை","ஆணி கலங்கில் அதுஇது வாமே."],"p":"","r":"There is a boat to be let out at the ferry; with the woman standing pressing it and the five planting their poles, for traders on the way in the river, if the pin is shaken, this becomes that.","n":"The boat is commonly the body, the five the senses who pole it, and the ஆணி (pin, linchpin) what holds it together. வாணி may be the goddess of speech or a woman. The last phrase (அது இது வாமே) is open: the boat may become something else, or the world be lost.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","adakkam","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:218"},"t:2936":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2936,"t":["நின்றார் இருந்தார் கிடந்தார் எனஇல்லை","சென்றார்தம் சித்தம் மோன சமாதியாம்","மன்றுஏயும் அங்கே மறைப்பொருள் ஒன்றுண்டு","சென்றாங்கு அணைந்தவர் சேர்கின்ற வாறே."],"p":"","r":"For those who have gone on there is no saying that they stood, sat or lay: their mind is silent samadhi; there is one hidden thing that dwells in the hall, and this is how those who went there and drew near join it.","n":"The first line denies the postures of the body: the state isn't a matter of how one sits. மன்று (the hall) is traditionally Siva's dance-hall at Chidambaram, taken also as the inner space; மறைப்பொருள் is a hidden or secret thing, or the meaning of the Veda (மறை).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mounam","samaadhi","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:219"},"t:2937":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2937,"t":["காட்டும் குறியும் கடந்த அக் காரணம்","ஏட்டின் புறத்தில் எழுதிவைத்து என்பயன்","கூட்டும் குருநந்தி கூட்டிடின் அல்லது","ஆட்டின் கழுத்தில் அதர்கிடந் தற்றே."],"p":"","r":"What use is it to write down on the outside of a palm leaf that cause which lies beyond every sign that points and every mark? Unless guru Nandi joins one to it, it is like the hanging teat on a goat's neck.","n":"ஏட்டின் புறத்தில் is book learning, writing on the leaf's surface. The goat's-neck image, ஆட்டின் கழுத்தில் அதர், is usually taken as the fleshy tags under a goat's throat, which look like teats and give no milk: knowledge on the page looks like the real thing and feeds no one. அதர் for these tags is the traditional gloss.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["guru","kalvi","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:219"},"t:2938":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2938,"t":["உணர்வுடை யார்கட்கு உலகமும் தோன்றும்","உணர்வுடை யார்கட்கு உறுதுயர் இல்லை","உணர்வுடை யார்கள் உணர்ந்த அக்காலம்","உணர்வுடை யார்கள் உணர்ந்து கண் டாரே."],"p":"","r":"To those who have awareness the world appears; to those who have awareness no sorrow comes; at the moment those who have awareness realised, they realised and saw.","n":"Every line opens with உணர்வுடையார், and the last two lines turn back on themselves, perhaps to say that realising and seeing are one act. உலகமும் தோன்றும் may mean the world is seen as it is, or that a further world (Siva's) shows itself; the verse doesn't settle which.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["meyyunarvu","gnaanam","idumbai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:219"},"t:2939":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2939,"t":["மறப்பது வாய்நின்ற மாயநன் னாடன்","பிறப்பினை நீங்கிய பேரரு ளாளன்","சிறப்பிடை யான்திரு மங்கையும் தானும்","உறக்கமில் போகத்து உறங்கிடுந் தானே."],"p":"","r":"The good lord of the wondrous land, who stands as forgetting itself, the great gracious one free of birth, the glorious one, sleeps with the holy lady in an enjoyment that has no sleep.","n":"மறப்பது வாய்நின்ற may be the Lord as concealment, one of his five acts, or as the forgetting of the world in samadhi. மாய நன்னாடன் is the lord of maya's land or of a wondrous land. The last line is a paradox, a sleep that is no sleep, the tradition's name for the yogic sleep of union.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","saththi","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:219"},"t:2940":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2940,"t":["துரியங்கள் மூன்றும் கடந்தொளிர் சோதி","அரிய துரியம் அதன்மீது மூன்றாய்","விரிவு குவிவு விழுங்கி உமிழ்ந்தே","உரையில் அநுபூ திகத்தினுள் ளானே."],"p":"","r":"The light that shines beyond the three turiyas, and beyond the rare turiya above them as a third, swallowing and spitting out expansion and contraction, is within the experience beyond speech.","n":"துரியம் is the fourth state of consciousness; the three turiyas are traditionally the soul's, Shakti's and Siva's, but the count in lines 1 and 2 is hard to fix. விரிவு குவிவு (spreading and drawing in) may be the world's emanation and withdrawal or the mind's going out and coming back, as in 2981. உரையில் அநுபூதி is direct experience that words don't reach.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaththai","sivam","mounam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:219"},"t:2941":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2941,"t":["உருவிலி ஊனிலி ஊனம்ஒன்று இல்லி","திருவிலி தீதிலி தேவர்க்கும் தேவன்","பொருவிலி பூதப் படையுடை யாளி","மருவிலி வந்துஎன் மனம்புகுந் தானே."],"p":"","r":"The formless one, without flesh, without any lack, without wealth, without evil, god of the gods, without equal, master of the army of goblins, the one who clings to nothing, came and entered my mind.","n":"A string of negatives in -இலி. திருவிலி is odd in praise: it may mean he needs no fortune or ornament, or that he is without Tiru (Lakshmi), unlike Vishnu. மருவிலி may be 'without attachment' or 'without mixture'. The ending turns the list of absences into a personal arrival.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","iraivan","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:219"},"t:2942":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2942,"t":["கண்டறி வார்இல்லைக் காயத்தின் நந்தியை","எண்டிசை யோரும் இறைவன் என்று ஏத்துவர்","அண்டங் கடந்த அளவிலா ஆனந்தத்","தொண்டர் முகந்த துறையறி யோமே."],"p":"","r":"No one has seen and known Nandi who is in the body, yet those of the eight directions praise him as the Lord; we do not know the ford where his servants scooped up the measureless bliss beyond the worlds.","n":"துறை is a bathing ghat or landing place, the way down to the water; முகந்த is scooping water in the hands. The speaker includes himself among those who don't know, perhaps in modesty, perhaps to say the way can't be shown.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","aanandham","yaakkai","adiyaar"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:219"},"t:2943":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2943,"t":["தற்பரம் அல்ல சதாசிவன் தான்அல்ல","நிட்களம் அல்ல சகள நிலையல்ல","அற்புத மாகி அனுபோகக் காமம்போல்","கற்பனை இன்றிக் கலந்துநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"He is not the Supreme, not Sadasiva, not the formless, not the state with form; becoming a wonder, like the desire of lovers in their enjoyment, he stands mingled without imagining.","n":"தற்பரம், சதாசிவன், நிட்களம் and சகளம் are the Siddhanta names for the grades of the Lord's being, formless to embodied; the verse denies each. அனுபோகக் காமம் போல் compares union to sexual joy known only in the act, an image the chapter repeats in 2944 and 2950. கற்பனை இன்றி may mean without mental construction on the knower's side.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","aanandham","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:219"},"t:2944":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2944,"t":["முகத்திற் கண்கொண்டு காண்கின்ற மூடர்கள்","அகத்திற் கண்கொண்டு காண்பதே ஆனந்தம்","மகட்குத் தாய்தன் மணாளனோடு ஆடிய","சுகத்தைச் சொல்லென்றால் சொல்லுமாறு எங்ஙனே."],"p":"","r":"Fools see with the eyes in their faces; seeing with the eye within is bliss; if a daughter says 'tell me', how can a mother tell her the joy she had with her husband?","n":"The marriage image makes the claim concrete: the bliss is real and private, and the one who has it can't hand it on in words, as the next verse but one also argues.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aanandham","meyyunarvu","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:219"},"t:2945":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2945,"t":["அப்பினில் உப்பென அத்தன் அணைந்திட்டுச்","செப்பு பராபரம் சேர்பர மும்விட்டுக்","கப்புறு சொற்பதம் மாயக் கலந்தமை","எப்படி அப்படி என்னும் அவ்வாறே."],"p":"","r":"The Father joining like salt in water, leaving both the Parapara that is spoken of and the Supreme one reaches, the clinging word and its state dissolving in that mingling: as it is, so it is, that is the way.","n":"Salt dissolved in water is the classic image of union without remainder. பராபரம் and பரம் are grades of the Supreme. கப்புறு is obscure, perhaps 'branching' or 'clinging'; சொற்பதம் may be word and meaning or the levels of speech. எப்படி அப்படி, 'how, so', gives up description.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","saayujjiyam","mounam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:219"},"t:2946":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2946,"t":["கண்டார்க்கு அழகிது காஞ்சிரத் தின்பழம்","தின்றார்க்கு அறியலாம் அப்பழத் தின்சுவை","பெண்தான் நிரம்பி மடவியள் ஆனால்","கொண்டான் அறிவன் குணம்பல தானே."],"p":"","r":"The nux vomica fruit is lovely to those who look at it, but its taste is known to those who eat it; when a girl has come to full womanhood and is a young wife, the man who took her knows her many qualities.","n":"காஞ்சிரம் is the strychnine tree, whose fruit is handsome and bitter. Both images say the same thing, that only the one who has it knows a thing's nature; the chapter applies this to samadhi. The first image may also warn that a pleasing look deceives.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["meyyunarvu","vaazhkkaithunai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:219"},"t:2947":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2947,"t":["நந்தி யிருந்தான் நடுவுத் தெருவிலே","சந்தி சமாதிகள் தாமே ஒழிந்தன","உந்தியின் உள்ளே உதித்தெழும் சோதியைப்","புந்தியி னாலே புணர்ந்துகொண் டேனே."],"p":"","r":"Nandi sat in the middle street, and the junctions and the samadhis ceased of themselves; I joined, with my understanding, the light that rises and springs up within the navel.","n":"நடுவுத் தெரு is traditionally the central channel of the yogic body (sushumna); சந்தி may be the junctions of the breaths or of the day kept in sandhya worship. உந்தி (the navel) is a seat of fire in the body. The plain sense is that, once the Lord is at the centre, rites and practised samadhis fall away.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","samaadhi","naadi","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:219"},"t:2948":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2948,"t":["விதறு படாவண்ணம் வேறிருந்து ஆய்ந்து","பதறு படாதே பழமறை பார்த்துக்","கதறிய பாழைக் கடந்ததக் கற்பனை","உதறிய பாழில் ஒடுங்குகின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"Staying apart without being shaken and examining, studying the ancient Veda without haste, he subsides into the void that shook off that imagining which passed beyond the void that cried out.","n":"கதறிய பாழ் (the void that cried out) and உதறிய பாழ் (the void that shook off) are obscure; they may be grades of the three voids of earlier chapters. The subject of ஒடுங்குகின்றான் may be the seeker or the Lord.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["paazh","vedham","aaraaydhal"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:219"},"t:2949":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2949,"t":["வாடா மலர்புனை சேவடி வானவர்","கூடார் அறநெறி நாடொறும் இன்புறச்","சேடார் கமலச் செழுஞ்சுடர் உட்சென்று","நாடார் அமுதுற நாடார் அமுதமே."],"p":"","r":"The red feet adorned with unfading flowers, which the gods do not reach: for those on the path of dharma to rejoice day by day, going within the bright flame in the full lotus, the nectar flows for those who seek, and it is the nectar the seekers seek.","n":"நாடார் can be 'those who seek' or 'those who do not seek', and the last line plays on both; it could also say the nectar is not for those who don't seek. சேடார் கமலம், the lotus full of petals, is traditionally a centre of the yogic body, often the crown. வானவர் கூடார் may also be read as the gods who gather.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvadi","amudham","aadhaaram"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:219"},"t:2950":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2950,"t":["அதுக்கென்று இருவர் அமர்ந்த சொற் கேட்டும்","பொதுக்கெனக் காமம் புலப்படு மாபோல்","சதுக்கென்று வேறே சமைந்தாரைக் காண","மதுக்கொன்றைத் தாரான் வளந்தரும் அன்றே."],"p":"","r":"As desire shows itself suddenly even on hearing the words of two who sat together for that purpose, so the Lord with the honeyed konrai garland gives his wealth, then and there, to look upon those who have suddenly made themselves ready apart.","n":"பொதுக்கென and சதுக்கென are sound-words for suddenness. The comparison is again sexual: desire is kindled at once by hearing of it, and grace comes as fast. அன்றே may be 'that very day' or an emphatic ending.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","sivam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:219"},"t:2951":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2951,"t":["தானும் அழிந்து தனமும் அழிந்துநீடு","ஊனும் அழிந்து உயிரும் அழிந்துடன்","வானும் அழிந்து மனமும் அழிந்துபின்","நானும் அழிந்தமை நானறி யேனே."],"p":"","r":"Self destroyed, wealth destroyed, the long-lasting flesh destroyed, life destroyed with it, the sky destroyed and the mind destroyed, I do not know that I too was destroyed.","n":"The list runs from outer to inner and ends in a paradox: the one who would know of his own undoing is gone. தான் and நான் may be distinguished, the self as agent and the knower. வான் may be the element space or the heavens.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["samaadhi","manam","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:219"},"t:2952":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2952,"t":["இருளும் வெளியும் இரண்டையும் மாற்றிப்","பொருளிற் பொருளாய்ப் பொருந்தவுள் ளாகி","அருளால் அழிந்திடும் அத்தன் அடிக்கே","உருளாத கன்மனம் உற்றுநின் றேனே."],"p":"","r":"Turning away both darkness and the open light, becoming the thing within the thing and fitted within, I stood with a stone-like mind that no longer rolls, joined to the feet of the Father by whose grace things are undone.","n":"இருளும் வெளியும் may be ignorance and knowledge, or the states of the soul alone and in the body. உருளாத கன்மனம் can be a mind fixed and steady, or the hard heart that would not melt, offered in humility. What அருளால் அழிந்திடும் destroys isn't said.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvadi","thiruvarul","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:219"},"t:2953":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2953,"t":["ஒன்றிநின் றுள்ளே உணர்ந்தேன் பராபரம்","ஒன்றிநின் றுள்ளே உணர்ந்தேன் சிவகதி","ஒன்றிநின் றுள்ளே உணர்ந்தேன் உணர்வினை","ஒன்றிநின் றேபல ஊழிகண் டேனே."],"p":"","r":"Standing united, I knew the Parapara within; standing united, I knew the way of Siva within; standing united, I knew awareness itself within; standing united, I have seen many ages.","n":"ஒன்றிநின்று (standing as one) opens every line. பல ஊழி கண்டேன் may be the timelessness of the state, or, in tradition, Tirumular's own long life in yoga.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","meyyunarvu","samaadhi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:219"},"t:2954":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2954,"t":["தான்வரைவு அற்றபின் ஆரை வரைவது","தான்அவ னானபின் ஆரை நினைவது","காமனை வென்றகண் ஆரை உகப்பது","தூமொழி வாசகம் சொல்லுமின் நீரே."],"p":"","r":"Once one's own limits are gone, whom is there to limit? Once one has become He, whom is there to think of? Whom does the eye that conquered Kama favour? Tell me that word, you of pure speech.","n":"வரைவு is limit or bounding, and in love poetry also the choosing of a bride, so the first line may also ask 'whom is there to wed?' The eye that conquered Kama is Siva's third eye, which burned the god of love, or the jnani's eye that has mastered desire. The closing line is likely ironic, addressed to those who think the state can be spoken.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","saayujjiyam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:220"},"t:2955":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2955,"t":["உரையற்றது ஒன்றை உரைசெய்யும் ஊமர்காள்","கரையற்றது ஒன்றைக் கரைகாண லாகுமோ","திரையற்ற நீர்போல் சிந்தை தெளிவார்க்குப்","புரையற்று இருந்தான் புரிசடை யோனே."],"p":"","r":"You mutes who speak of the one that is beyond speech, can the shore of what has no shore be seen? To those whose thought grows clear like water without waves, the one with twisted matted hair stays without any veil.","n":"The talkers are called mutes because their words reach nothing. புரையற்று can mean without fault or without any hiding place; with the image of clear, still water, being seen plainly fits better.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mounam","sivam","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:220"},"t:2956":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2956,"t":["மனமாயை மாயைஇம் மாயை மயக்கம்","மனமாயை தான்மாய மற்றொன்றும் இல்லை","பினைமாய்வது இல்லை பிதற்றவும் வேண்டா","தனைஆய்ந்து இருப்பது தத்துவந் தானே."],"p":"","r":"The mind's maya is maya, and this maya is delusion; when the mind's maya itself dies there is nothing else, nothing dies after that, and no need to babble: to remain having examined oneself is the truth.","n":"தத்துவம் here likely means the real or the truth rather than one of the thirty-six tattvas, though the technical sense may also be heard. பிதற்றவும் வேண்டா echoes the previous verse's mutes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maayai","manam","thaththuvam","aaraaydhal"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:220"},"t:2957":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2957,"t":["மலமில்லை மாசில்லை மானாபி மானம்","குலமில்லை கொள்ளும் குணங்களும் இல்லை","நலமில்லை நந்தியை ஞானத்தி னாலே","பலமன்னி அன்பில் பதித்துவைப் போர்க்கே."],"p":"","r":"For those who by knowledge set Nandi firmly in love, holding steady, there is no impurity, no stain, no pride or self-regard, no caste, no qualities to take on, no good to seek.","n":"குலமில்லை denies caste or lineage to the one who has reached. நலமில்லை is surprising after a list of faults; it may mean no worldly good is needed, or that such a one is past good and bad alike. பலமன்னி is obscure, perhaps 'abiding in strength' or 'with the fruit secured'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["malam","gnaanam","anbu","kudi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:221"},"t:2958":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2958,"t":["ஒழிந்தேன் பிறவி உறவென்னும் பாசம்","கழிந்தேன் கடவுளும் நானும்ஒன் றானேன்","அழிந்தாங்கு இனிவரும் ஆக்கமும் வேண்டேன்","செழுஞ்சார் புடைய சிவனைக் கண் டேனே."],"p":"","r":"I have left birth and the bond called kinship; I am free; God and I have become one; I want no more gain that comes and perishes; I have seen Siva, the rich refuge.","n":"உறவு என்னும் பாசம் makes kinship itself a bond. கடவுளும் நானும் ஒன்றானேன் is a plain statement of union, though how 'one' is meant (identity or inseparability) is the long dispute between Vedanta and Siddhanta, which the verse doesn't enter.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saayujjiyam","paasam","piravi","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:221"},"t:2959":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2959,"t":["ஆலைக் கரும்பும் அமுதும்அக் காரமும்","சோலைத் தண்ணீரும் உடைத்தெங்கள் நாட்டிடைப்","பீலிக்கண் அன்ன வடிவுசெய் வாளொரு","கோலப்பெண் ணாட்குக் குறை யொன்றும் இல்லையே."],"p":"","r":"Our land has cane in the mill, nectar, sugar and the cool water of groves; the lovely woman who makes a form like the eye of a peacock's feather lacks for nothing.","n":"The verse reads at first as praise of a fertile land and a fortunate woman. In this chapter it is likely symbolic: the land as the body or the state reached, its sweets and waters as bliss and nectar, the woman as Shakti or the soul. பீலிக்கண் (the eye of a peacock feather) is sometimes taken as a light seen in meditation. None of this can be fixed from the verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","amudham","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:221"},"t:2960":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2960,"t":["ஆராலும் என்னை அமட்டஒண் ணாதிதினிச்","சீரார் பிரான்வந்தென் சிந்தை புகுந்தனன்","சீராடி அங்கே திரிவதால் லால் இனி","யார்படுஞ் சாரா அறிவறிந் தேனே."],"p":"","r":"From now no one can harm me; the glorious Lord came and entered my mind; I have come to know the knowledge in which, apart from wandering there in joy, I go near no one now.","n":"Lines 3 and 4 are hard: சீராடி may be playing like a cherished child, and யார்படும் சாரா may be 'not approaching anyone' or 'what no one reaches'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","manam","anjaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:221"},"t:2961":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2961,"t":["பிரிந்தேன் பிரமன் பிணித்ததோர் பாசம்","தெரிந்தேன் சிவகதி செல்லும் நிலையை","அரிந்தேன் வினையை அயில்மன வாளால்","முரிந்தேன் புரத்தினை முந்துகின் றேனே."],"p":"","r":"I have parted from the bond that Brahma tied; I have learned the state that goes on the way of Siva; I have cut down karma with the sharp sword of the mind; I have broken the city and press on ahead.","n":"Brahma's bond is birth into a body. புரம் (the city) may be the body, or may echo Siva's burning of the three cities, taken as the three malas. அயில் மன வாள் (the sharp sword of mind) parallels the sword of knowledge in 2968.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["paasam","vinai","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:221"},"t:2962":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2962,"t":["ஒன்றுகண் டீர்உல குக்கொரு தெய்வமும்","ஒன்றுகண் டீர்உல குக்குஉயி ராவது","நன்றுகண் டீர்இனி நமசிவா யப்பழம்","தின்றுகண் டேற்கிது தித்தித்த வாறே."],"p":"","r":"See, there is one God for the world; see, the one is what is life to the world; see that it is good: now, having eaten the fruit நமசிவாய, how sweet it has been to me!","n":"Each line opens with a call to 'see' (கண்டீர்) and the last turns to the speaker's own tasting (கண்டேற்கு). The five-letter mantra as a fruit makes knowing it a matter of taste, as in 2946.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panchaakkaram","iraivan","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:221"},"t:2963":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2963,"t":["சந்திரன் பாம்பொடும் சூடும் சடாதரன்","வந்தென்னை யாண்ட மணிவிளக்கு ஆனவன்","அந்தமும் ஆதியும் இல்லா அரும்பொருள்","சிந்தையின் மேவித் தியக்கு அறுத் தானே."],"p":"","r":"The one with matted hair who wears the moon with the snake came and ruled me and became a jewel-lamp, the rare thing with neither end nor beginning; he settled in my mind and cut away my confusion.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","thiruvarul","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:221"},"t:2964":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2964,"t":["பண்டுஎங்குள் ஈசன் நெடுமால் பிரமனைக்","கண்டுஅங்கு இருக்கும் கருத்தறி வாரில்லை","விண்டு அங்கே தோன்றி வெறுமனம் ஆயிடில்","துண்டு அங்கு இருந்ததோர் தூறுஅது வாமே."],"p":"","r":"No one knows the purpose of the Lord who was within us of old, who stays there seeing tall Mal and Brahma; if he breaks forth and appears there and the mind becomes empty, what stood there as a fragment becomes a mere thicket.","n":"The verse is obscure. One reading takes Vishnu and Brahma as lesser lords the inner Lord oversees, and the last line as saying that, to a mind emptied, the separate gods are as worthless as scrub. விண்டு may be 'splitting open' or a form of Vishnu's name.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","manam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:221"},"t:2965":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2965,"t":["அன்னையும் அத்தனும் அன்புற்றது அல்லது","அன்னையும் அத்தனும் ஆரறி வார்என்னை","அன்னையும் அத்தனும் யானும் உடனிருந்து","அன்னையும் அத்தனை யான்புரிந் தேனே."],"p":"","r":"Apart from the love that Mother and Father bore, who knows Mother and Father, and who knows me? With Mother and Father and me together, I came to serve the Mother and the Father.","n":"Mother and Father are traditionally Shakti and Siva. என்னை may be 'me' or 'what'. புரிந்தேன் can be served, desired or understood, so the last line may also say the speaker came to understand them.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","sivam","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:221"},"t:2966":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2966,"t":["கொண்ட சுழியும் குலவரை உச்சியும்","அண்டரும் அண்டத் தலைவரும் ஆதியும்","எண்டிசை யோரும்வந்து என்கைத் தலத்துளே","உண்டனர் நானினி உய்ந்தொழிந் தேனே."],"p":"","r":"The whorl it took, the summit of the great mountain, the gods, the lord of the gods, the First and those of the eight directions came and are in the palm of my hand; now I am saved and done.","n":"கொண்ட சுழி may be the coil of kundalini or the crown's whorl, and குலவரை உச்சி the summit of Meru, often taken as the top of the head in yogic reading. The whole cosmos held in the hand is a claim to mastery, or to the cosmos being found within. உண்டனர் is read as 'they are there'; it could also be 'they ate'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["siththi","yogam","andam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:221"},"t:2967":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2967,"t":["தானே திசையொடு தேவருமாய் நிற்கும்","தானே வடவரை ஆதியுமாய நிற்கும்","தானே உடலுயிர் தத்துவமாய் நிற்கும்","தானே உலகில் தலைவனும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"He himself stands as the directions and the gods; he himself stands as the northern mountain and the origin; he himself stands as body, life and tattvas; he himself is also the Lord in the world.","n":"தானே may be the Lord, as in many such lists, or, in this chapter, the one who has reached and become all, following 2966. The verse doesn't say which.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","thaththuvam","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:221"},"t:2968":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2968,"t":["நமன்வரின் ஞானவாள் கொண்டே எறிவன்","சிவன்வரின் நானுடன் போவது திண்ணம்","பவம்வரும் வல்வினை பண்டே யறுத்தேன்","தவம்வரும் சிந்தைத் தான்எதிராரே."],"p":"","r":"If Yama comes I will strike him with the sword of knowledge; if Siva comes, it is sure I will go with him; I cut long ago the strong karma that brings birth; who can stand against a mind into which tapas has come?","n":"The two comings set death and Siva side by side: one is driven off, the other followed. பவம் is birth or worldly becoming.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","gnaanam","vinai","thavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:221"},"t:2969":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2969,"t":["சித்தம் சிவமாய் மலமூன்றும் செற்றவர்","சுத்தச் சிவமாவர் தோயார் மலபந்தம்","சுத்தும் சிலகும் கலகமும் கைகாணார்","சத்தம் பரவிந்து தானாம்என்று எண்ணியே."],"p":"","r":"Those whose minds have become Siva and who have destroyed the three malas become pure Siva and do not touch the bondage of malam; they see no turning, no wavering, no strife, holding that sound and the supreme bindu are themselves.","n":"The three malas are traditionally anava, karma and maya. சுத்தும் சிலகும் is obscure and may be corrupt. The last line may say that nada and parabindu are themselves, or that they are He; either way it places these states within, not as things to reach.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["malam","sivam","naadham","vindhu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:221"},"t:2970":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2970,"t":["நினைப்பும் மறப்பும் இலாதவர் நெஞ்சம்","வினைப்பற்று அறுக்கும் விமலன் இருக்கும்","வினைப்பற்று அறுக்கும் விமலனைத் தேடி","நினைக்கப் பெறில்அவன் நீளியன் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"In the heart of those without thinking and forgetting dwells the Pure One who cuts away karma's clinging; if one goes seeking that Pure One and gets to think of him, he becomes far off.","n":"The paradox: he dwells where neither thinking nor forgetting is, so to think of him is to set him at a distance. நீளியன் may instead mean 'vast' or 'great', which would make the last line praise rather than warning.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["manam","vinai","sivam","pattru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:221"},"t:2971":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2971,"t":["சிவபெரு மான்என்று நான்அழைத்து ஏத்தத்","தவப்பெரு மான்என்று தான்வந்து நின்றான்","அவபெரு மான்என்னை யாளுடை நாதன்","பவபெரு மானைப் பணிந்துநின் றேனே."],"p":"","r":"When I called and praised him as Siva Peruman, he came and stood as the Lord of tapas; the Lord of undoing, the master who owns me: I stood bowing to the Lord of becoming.","n":"The verse plays on names: சிவ, தவ, அவ and பவ before பெருமான். பவன் (Bhava) is a name of Siva; அவ may be 'the destroyer', from அவம் (undoing), though its sense is unsure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","thavam","panivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:221"},"t:2972":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2972,"t":["பணிந்துநின் றேன்பர மாதி பதியைத்","துணிந்துநின் றேன்இனி மற்றொன்றும் வேண்டேன்","அணிந்துநின் றேன்உடல் ஆதிப் பிரானைத்","தணிந்துநின் றேன்சிவன் தன்மைகண் டேனே."],"p":"","r":"I stood bowing to the Lord, first and supreme; I stood resolved, wanting nothing else from now; I stood having put on the primal Lord as my body; I stood calmed, having seen the nature of Siva.","n":"Each line opens a stance with நின்றேன். அணிந்து நின்றேன் உடல் ஆதிப்பிரானை may be wearing the Lord as one's body (taking his form), or adorning the body with him, as with ash and emblems.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pathi","panivu","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:221"},"t:2973":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2973,"t":["என்நெஞ்சம் ஈசன் இணையடி தாம் சேர்ந்து","முன்னம்செய்து ஏத்த முழுதும் பிறப்பறும்","தன்நெஞ்சம் இல்லாத் தலைவன் தலைவிதி","பின்னம்செய்து என்னைப் பிணக்கறுத் தானே."],"p":"","r":"When my heart joined the Lord's two feet and I praised him beforehand, birth was cut away entirely; the Lord, who has no mind of his own, broke up the fate written for me and ended my discord.","n":"தலைவிதி is fate, the writing on the head. தன் நெஞ்சம் இல்லாத் தலைவன் is obscure: the Lord beyond mind, or one without self-interest. பிணக்கு can be discord, entanglement or the strife of rival creeds.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvadi","piravi","oozh","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:221"},"t:2974":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2974,"t":["பிணக்கறுத் தான்பிணி மூப்பறுத்து எண்ணும்","கணக்கறுத் தாண்டவன் காண்நந்தி என்னைப்","பிணக்கறுத்து என்னுடன் முன்வந்த துன்பம்","வணக்கலுற் றேன்சிவன் வந்தது தானே."],"p":"","r":"Nandi ended my discord, cut away disease, old age and the reckoning that is counted, and ruled me; when he ended my discord and the sorrow that had come along with me from before, I fell to bowing: it was Siva who had come.","n":"The verse picks up பிணக்கறுத்தான் from 2973. எண்ணும் கணக்கு may be the counting of one's days or the account of karma; முன்வந்த துன்பம் (sorrow come with me from before) is likely the karma of past births.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","noy","vinai","panivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:221"},"t:2975":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2975,"t":["சிவன்வந்து தேவர் குழாமுடன் கூடப்","பவம்வந் திடநின்ற பாசம் அறுத்திட்டு","அவன்எந்தை ஆண்டருள் ஆதிப் பெருமான்","அவன்வந்தென் னுள்ளே அகப்பட்ட வாறே."],"p":"","r":"Siva came with the throng of gods, cut off the bond that stood so that birth would come, and he, my Father, the First Lord who ruled and graced me, came and was caught within me.","n":"அகப்பட்டவாறு (how he got caught) turns the usual picture round: the soul seized by the Lord here holds the Lord.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","paasam","thiruvarul","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:221"},"t:2976":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2976,"t":["கரும்பும் தேனும் கலந்ததோர் காயத்தில்","அரும்பும் கந்தமும் ஆகிய ஆனந்தம்","விரும்பியே உள்ளம் வெளியுறக் கண்டபின்","கரும்பும் கைத்தது தேனும் புளித்ததே."],"p":"","r":"In a body where cane and honey are mixed, the bliss that is bud and fragrance: once the heart, longing, saw it laid open, the cane turned bitter and the honey sour.","n":"Cane and honey are first the body's sweet pleasures, then spoiled by the greater bliss. வெளியுற may be 'openly' or 'in the inner space'. The verse sets Siva's bliss against worldly pleasure, as the concept's gloss has it, rather than condemning the body.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aanandham","yaakkai","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:221"},"t:2977":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2977,"t":["உள்ள சரியாதி ஒட்டியே மீட்டென்பால்","வள்ளல் அருத்தியே வைத்த வளம்பாடிச்","செய்வன எல்லாம் சிவமாகக் காண்டலால்","கைவளம் இன்றிக் கருக்கடந் தேனே."],"p":"","r":"Having followed chariyai and the rest and then turned back, singing the riches the Generous One set in me in his love, since I see all that is done as Siva, I have passed beyond the womb with nothing in my hands.","n":"Chariyai and the rest are the four Saiva paths. மீட்டு may mean turning back from them or being brought back. கைவளம் இன்றி may be 'without any wealth in hand' (without merit of my own) or 'without the skill of my hands'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sariyai","sivam","karu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:221"},"t:2978":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2978,"t":["மீண்டார் கமலத்துள் அங்கி மிகச்சென்று","தூண்டா விளக்கிக் தகளிசெய் சேர்தலும்","பூண்டாள் ஒருத்தி புவன சூடாமணி","மாண்டான் ஒருவன்கை வந்தது தானே."],"p":"","r":"When, turned back, the fire went deep into the lotus and was set in the bowl as a lamp that needs no trimming, one woman, crown-jewel of the worlds, took it on, and it came into the hand of a man who had died.","n":"Very obscure. The lamp that needs no trimming (தூண்டா விளக்கு) is a common image of the self-lit inner light; the lotus is a centre of the yogic body; the woman is likely Shakti; the man who died may be the one who has died to self. Any fuller reading would be invented.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aadhaaram","saththi","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:221"},"t:2979":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2979,"t":["ஆறே அருவி அகங்குளம் ஒன்றுண்டு","நூறே சிவகதி நுண்ணிது வண்ணமும்","கூறே குவிமுலை கொம்பனை யாளொடும்","வேறே யிருக்கும் விழுப்பொருள் தானே."],"p":"","r":"There is a river, a waterfall and a tank within; Siva's way is fine, and so is its colour; the excellent thing is a share with the lady slender as a creeper, with rounded breasts, and also stays apart.","n":"ஆறு may be river or six, the six centres of the yogic body; the waterfall and inner tank are likely the nectar's flow. நூறே may be 'fine as powder' or 'a hundred'. The last lines give Siva as half of the Goddess and yet distinct, the Siddhanta position.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","saththi","naadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:221"},"t:2980":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2980,"t":["அன்புள் உருகி அழுவன் அரற்றுவன்","என்பும் உருக இராப்பகல் ஏத்துவன்","என்பொன் மணியை இறைவனை ஈசனைத்","தின்பன் கடிப்பன் திருத்துவன் தானே."],"p":"","r":"Melting in love I weep and cry out; with my very bones melting I praise him night and day; my golden gem, the Lord, Isa: I will eat him, bite him, and he sets me right.","n":"The eating and biting are a devotee's hunger for the Lord, as in 2962's fruit. திருத்துவன் may be the Lord correcting the devotee or the devotee savouring him; the subject is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anbu","iraivan","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:221"},"t:2981":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2981,"t":["மனம்வி ரிந்து குவிந்தது மாதவம்","மனம்வி ரிந்து குவிந்தது வாயு","மனம்வி ரிந்து குவிந்தது மன்னுயிர்","மனம்வி ரிந்துரை மாண்டது முத்தியே."],"p":"","r":"The mind spread out and drew in: that is great tapas; the mind spread out and drew in: that is the breath; the mind spread out and drew in: that is the abiding soul; the mind that spread out, and speech, died: that is mukti.","n":"The same movement is named three ways, as tapas, breath and life, and the fourth line ends it. மாதவம் is read as மா தவம், great tapas. The link of mind and breath echoes earlier yogic chapters, where where the mind goes the breath goes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["manam","praanan","veedu","thavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:221"},"t:2982":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2982,"t":["மாயனை நாடி மனநெடும் தேரெறிப்","போயின நாடறி யாதே புலம்புவர்","தேயமும் நாடும் திரிந்தெங்கள் செல்வனைக்","காயம்மின் நாட்டிடைக் கண்டுகொண் டேனே."],"p":"","r":"Those who drove the long chariot of the mind in search of the elusive one went off without knowing the land and lament; I, having wandered through countries and lands, found our treasured Lord within the land of the body.","n":"மாயன் is usually Vishnu, but here may simply mean the elusive one whom seekers chase. காயம் (body) is the key word: the search outward fails and the find is within. காயம்மின் is odd; it may be காயம் with an emphatic or a misdivided word, and some take மின் as the flash of light seen within.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","yaakkai","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:222"},"t:2983":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2983,"t":["மன்னு மலைபோல் மதவா ரணத்தின்மேல்","இன்னிசை பாட இருந்தவர் ஆரெனில்","முன்னியல் கால முதல்வனார் நாமத்தைப்","பன்னினர் என்றே பாடறி வீரே."],"p":"","r":"If you ask who sat on the mountain-like rutting elephant while sweet music was sung, know from the songs that they were those who had recited the name of the First One of the time before.","n":"The verse promises honour, pictured as a royal elephant ride with music, to those who repeat the Lord's name. Later tradition might see Sundarar's ride to Kailasa on a white elephant here, but that would make the verse later than Tirumular is usually placed; read it as a general image.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","sivam","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:222"},"t:2984":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2984,"t":["முத்தினின் முத்தை முகிழிள ஞாயிற்றை","எத்தனை வானோரும் ஏத்தும் இறைவனனை","அத்தனைக் காணாது அரற்றுகின் றேனையோர்","பித்தன் இவனென்று பேசுகின் றாரே."],"p":"","r":"They say of me, crying out because I cannot see the Father, the pearl of pearls, the young budding sun, the Lord whom all the gods praise: 'this one is a madman'.","n":"பித்தன் (madman) is also a name of Siva himself, so the devotee mocked as mad shares a name with the Lord he longs for. The verse turns on longing for one not seen (காணாது அரற்றுகின்றேன்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","sivam","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:222"},"t:2985":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2985,"t":["புகுந்துநின் றான்எங்கள் புண்ணிய மூர்த்தி","புகுந்துநின் றான்எங்கள் போதறி வாளன்","புகுந்துநின் றானடி யார்தங்கள் நெஞ்சம்","புகுந்துநின் றானையே போற்றுகின் றேனே."],"p":"","r":"Our holy form has entered and stays; our knower who knows has entered and stays; he has entered and stays in the hearts of his devotees; it is the one who entered and stays that I praise.","n":"புகுந்துநின்றான் (entered and stays) opens every line. போதறிவாளன் may mean the one who knows the right time, or the one whose knowledge is pure awareness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adiyaar","sivam","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:222"},"t:2986":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2986,"t":["பூதக்கண் ணாடியில் புகுந்திலன் போதுளன்","வேதக்கண் ணாடியில் வேறே வெளிப்படு","நீதிக்கண் ணாடி நினைவார் மனத்துளன்","கீதக்கண் ணாடியில் கேட்டுநின் றேனே."],"p":"","r":"He has not entered the mirror of the elements, yet he is there in awareness; in the mirror of the Veda he appears otherwise; in the mirror of justice he is in the minds of those who think of him; in the mirror of song I heard him and stood.","n":"கண்ணாடி (mirror) structures the verse: the elements do not reflect him, the Veda shows him in a different form, right conduct and thought hold him, and song lets him be heard. போதுளன் may mean 'he is in awareness' or 'he is in the flower' (of the heart); both are traditional.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","vedham","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:222"},"t:2987":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2987,"t":["நாமம் ஓர் ஆயிரம் ஓதுமின் நாதனை","ஏமம் ஓர் ஆயிரத் துள்ளே இசைவீர்கள்","ஓமம்ஓர் ஆயிரம் ஓதவல் லார்அவர்","காமம் ஆயிரம் கண்டொழிந் தாரே."],"p":"","r":"Recite the Lord's thousand names and you will be held within a thousand protections; those who can perform a thousand fire offerings have seen their thousand desires and are done with them.","n":"ஏமம் is protection or delight. கண்டொழிந்தார் can mean they saw their desires through and were done with them, or that they saw desire and left it; the plain promise is release from desire by naming and offering.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","aaguthi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:222"},"t:2988":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2988,"t":["போற்றுகின் றேன்புகழ்ந் தும்புகழ் ஞானத்தைத்","தேற்றுகின் றேன்சிந்தை நாயகன் சேவடி","சாற்றுகின் றேன்அறை யோசிவ யோகத்தைப்","போற்றுகின் றேன்எம் பிரானென்று நானே."],"p":"","r":"I praise, and praising I make known the renowned knowledge; I make sure the red feet of the Lord of the mind; I proclaim Siva-yoga, hear it; I praise, calling him our Lord.","n":"அறையோ is a call for attention, as a crier's or a challenge's. The four verbs (போற்று, தேற்று, சாற்று, போற்று) frame the work as a teacher's proclamation as well as a devotee's praise.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","thiruvadi","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:222"},"t:2989":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2989,"t":["நானா விதஞ்செய்து நாடுமின் நந்தியை","ஊனார் கமலத்தின் ஊடுசென்று அப்புறம்","வானோர் உலகம் வழிபட மீண்டபின்","தேனார உண்டு தெவிட்டலும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"Seek Nandi in many ways: passing through the lotus of the flesh and beyond it, coming back after the heavenly world has worshipped, you may drink honey to your fill and be sated.","n":"ஊனார் கமலம் (lotus of the flesh) is traditionally a centre of the yogic body and தேன் (honey) the nectar the yogi tastes. A plain devotional reading also stands: seek him by every means and the sweetness comes.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","aadhaaram","amudham","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:222"},"t:2990":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2990,"t":["வந்துநின் றான்அடி யார்கட்கு அரும்பொருள்","இந்திரன் ஆதி இமையவர் வேண்டினும்","சுந்தர மாதர் துழனியொன்று அல்லது","அந்தர வானத்தின் அப்புறம் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"The rare treasure has come and stands for his devotees; even if Indra and the other gods ask for him, he is beyond the sky of space, and they get nothing but the sound of beautiful women.","n":"The contrast is between devotees, to whom he comes, and the gods, whose pleasures (the clamour, துழனி, of heavenly women) are all they have. The construction of line 3 is loose and the reading is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["adiyaar","thiruvarul","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:222"},"t:2991":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2991,"t":["மண்ணிற் கலங்கிய நீர்போல் மனிதர்கள்","எண்ணிற் கலங்கி இறைவன் இவன்என்னார்","உண்ணிற் குளத்தின் முகந்தொரு பால்வைத்துத்","தெண்ணீர்ப் படுத்த சிவன்அவன் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"Like water muddied by earth, people muddied in their thoughts do not say 'this is the Lord'; Siva is the one who scoops from the pond within, sets the mud to one side, and makes the water clear.","n":"The image is of clearing muddy water: the mind is the pond, and the Lord does the settling. உண்ணிற் குளம் (the pond within) is read as the inner mind; the phrasing of line 3 is compressed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["manam","sivam","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:222"},"t:2992":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2992,"t":["மெய்த்தவத் தானை விரும்பும் ஒருவர்க்குக்","கைத்தலம் சேர்தரு நெல்லிக் கனியொக்கும்","சுத்தனைத் தூய்நெறி யாய்நின்ற தேவர்கள்","அத்தனை நாடி அமைந் தொழிந் தேனே."],"p":"","r":"To one who longs for the one of true penance he is like a gooseberry come into the palm; seeking the pure one, the Father of the gods who stood as the pure way, I have settled and come to rest.","n":"கைத்தலம் சேர் நெல்லிக்கனி (a gooseberry in the hand) is proverbial for what is plainly seen and surely held. அமைந்தொழிந்தேன் (I settled and ceased) opens the chain of linked verses that follows.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","sivam","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:222"},"t:2993":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2993,"t":["அமைந்தொழிந் தேன்அள வில்புகழ் ஞானம்","சமைந் தொழிந் தேன்தடு மாற்றம்ஒன் றில்லை","புகைந் தெழும் பூதலம் புண்ணியன் நண்ணி","வகைந்து கொடுக்கின்ற வள்ளலும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"I have settled; I have been made complete in a knowledge of measureless renown; there is no wavering at all; the holy one, coming near the smoking earth, is the bountiful one who apportions and gives.","n":"தடுமாற்றம் ஒன்றில்லை (no wavering) states the settled state. புகைந்தெழும் பூதலம் (the earth rising in smoke) is obscure: it may be the earth as the burning ground, or the world of distress.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","thelivu","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:222"},"t:2994":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2994,"t":["வள்ளல் தலைவனை வானநன் னாடனை","வெள்ளப் புனற்சடை வேதமுதல்வனைக்","கள்ளப் பெருமக்கள் காண்பர்கொலோஎன்று","உள்ளத்தின் உள்ளே ஒளிந்திருந்து ஆளுமே."],"p":"","r":"The bountiful chief, lord of the good heavenly land, first of the Veda with flood-water in his matted hair, wondering whether the deceitful will see him, hides within the heart and rules there.","n":"கள்ளப் பெருமக்கள் (deceitful folk) are those with hidden guile; the Lord hides from them, yet rules from within the heart. The verse may also play on the Lord as himself the hidden thief of hearts.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","vanjam","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:222"},"t:2995":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2995,"t":["ஆளும் மலர்ப்பதம் தந்த கடவுளை","நாளும் வழிபட்டு நன்மையுள் நின்றவர்","கோளும் வினையும் அறுக்கும் குரிசிலின்","வாளும் மனத்தொடும் வைத்தொழிந் தேனே."],"p":"","r":"Those who worship daily the God who gave his ruling flower-feet stand in goodness; I have laid with my mind the sword of the lord who cuts away the planets and deeds.","n":"கோள் (the planets, their bad influence) and வினை (deeds) are what the Lord cuts. வாளும் மனத்தொடும் வைத்தொழிந்தேன் can mean that I set his sword in my mind, or that I laid down my sword and my mind before him in surrender.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvadi","vinai","vazhipaadu","oozh"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:222"},"t:2996":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2996,"t":["விரும்பில் அவனடி வீர சுவர்க்கம்","பொருந்தில் அவனடி புண்ணிய லோகம்","திருந்தில் அவனடி தீர்த்தமும் ஆகும்","வருந்தி அவனடி வாழ்த்தவல் லார்க்கே."],"p":"","r":"For those who can praise his feet with effort: if they desire it, his feet are the heroes' heaven; if they join them, the world of merit; if they are reformed, the holy water too.","n":"The feet stand in for every reward promised elsewhere: the heaven of warriors fallen in battle, the world earned by merit, and the bathing places that wash away sin. வருந்தி (with effort, with longing) qualifies the praise.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvadi","theerththam","marumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:222"},"t:2997":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2997,"t":["வானகம் ஊடறுத் தான்இவ் வுலகினில்","தானகம் இல்லாத் தனியாகும் போதகன்","கானக வாழைக் கனிநுகர்ந்து உள்ளுறும்","பானகச் சோதியைப் பற்றிநின் றேனே."],"p":"","r":"He who cut through the heavens is in this world a solitary teacher with no house of his own; I have held to the light, sweet as a drink, that comes within as one eats the forest plantain fruit.","n":"போதகன் (teacher) and தானகம் இல்லா (with no dwelling of his own) suggest the Lord as a wandering teacher. கானக வாழைக்கனி and பானகச் சோதி (a light like a sweet drink) are images of sweetness taken within; their exact reference is not clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","guru","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:222"},"t:2998":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2998,"t":["விதியது மேலை அமரர் உறையும்","பதியது பாய்புனல் கங்கையும் உண்டு","துதியது தொல்வினைப் பற்றறு விக்கும்","பதியது வவ்விட்டது அந்தமும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"That is the destiny where the gods above dwell; that is the place where the flowing Ganga is; that praise removes the clinging of old deeds; that place, having seized them, is also the end.","n":"பதி may be a holy place (some think of Kasi on the Ganga) or the Lord himself, which the word also means in Siddhanta. Line 4 (வவ்விட்டது அந்தமும்) is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","vinai","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:222"},"t:2999":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":2999,"t":["மேலது வானவர் கீழது மாதவர்","தானிடர் மானுடர் கீழது மாதுஅனங்","கானது கூவிள மாலை கமழ்சடை","ஆனது செய்யும்என் ஆருயிர் தானே."],"p":"","r":"Above are the gods, below the great ascetics, below them humans in distress; the one whose fragrant matted hair wears the bilva garland does all this and is my dear life.","n":"Line 2 (மாதுஅனங் கானது) is broken and could not be read; the ranking of beings above and below is the plain outline. The last line makes the Lord the life of the speaker's life.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:222"},"t:3000":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":3000,"t":["சூழுங் கருங்கடல் நஞ்சுண்ட கண்டனை","ஏழும் இரண்டிலும் ஈசன் பிறப்பிலி","யாழுஞ் சுனையும் அடவியும் அங்குளன்","வாழும் எழுந்தைந்து மன்னனும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"He whose throat swallowed the poison of the encircling dark sea is the Lord unborn in the seven and the two; he is there in the lute, the spring and the forest, and is also the king who lives in the seven and five.","n":"ஏழும் இரண்டும் and எழுந்தைந்து are counts (seven and two; seven and five) whose referents, worlds or regions or tattvas, the verse does not fix.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","andam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:222"},"t:3001":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":3001,"t":["உலகமது ஒத்துமண் ஒத்ததஉயர் காற்றை","அலர்கதிர் அங்கிஓத்து ஆதிப் பிரானும்","நிலவிய மாமுகில் நீர்ஒத்து மீண்டுஅச்","செலவுஒத்து அமர்திகைத் தேவர் பி ரானே."],"p":"","r":"The First Lord is like the world and the earth, like the high wind, like the fire of spreading rays, like the water of the great clouds, and like their passing away again: he is the lord of the gods of the directions.","n":"The list runs through the elements: earth, wind, fire and water, with செலவு (going) perhaps standing for space or for dissolution. ஒத்து (being like) is weaker than 'being': the verse compares rather than identifies.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:222"},"t:3002":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":3002,"t":["பரிசறிந்து அங்குளன் அங்கி அருக்கன்","பரிசறிந்து அங்குளன் மாருதத்து ஈசன்","பரிசறிந்து அங்குளன் மாமதி ஞானப்","பரிசறிந்து அந்நிலம் பாரிக்கும் ஆறே."],"p":"","r":"Knowing the right manner, he is there as fire and sun; knowing it, he is there as the Lord in the wind; knowing it, as the great moon; and knowing the manner of knowledge, that is how he spreads that land.","n":"பரிசறிந்து (knowing the manner) opens each line. The last line is compressed; பாரிக்கும் may mean spreads out or sustains.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","aadhiththan","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:222"},"t:3003":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":3003,"t":["அந்தம் கடந்தும் அதுஅது வாய்நிற்கும்","பந்த உலகினில் கீழோர் பெரும் பொருள்","தந்த உலகெங்கும் தானே பாராபரன்","வந்து படைக்கின்ற மாண்பது வாமே."],"p":"","r":"Even beyond the end he stands as each thing that is; in the bound world he is the great treasure of those below; the Supreme himself pervades all the worlds he gave: this is the glory of his coming to create.","n":"பந்த உலகு (the bound world) and கீழோர் (those below) suggest bound souls, for whom he is the treasure. அதுஅதுவாய் நிற்கும் (stands as each thing) is a phrase of pervasion that Siddhanta reads as indwelling rather than identity.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","aindhozhil","pasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:222"},"t:3004":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":3004,"t":["முத்தண்ண ஈரண்ட மேமுடி ஆயினும்","அத்தன் உருவம் உலகுஏ ழெனப்படும்","அத்தனின் பாதாளம் அளவுள்ள சேவடி","மத்தர் அதனை மகிழ்ந்துஉண ராரே."],"p":"","r":"Though the three eggs and the two eggs are his crown, the Father's form is said to be the seven worlds, and his red feet reach as far as the underworld; the intoxicated do not know this and rejoice in it.","n":"முத்தண்ண ஈரண்டம் (three and two world-eggs) is a cosmological count that cannot be fixed here. மத்தர் (the intoxicated, the deluded) are those who miss the cosmic form.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["andam","thiruvadi","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:222"},"t:3005":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":3005,"t":["ஆதிப் பிரான்நம் பிரான்அவ் அகலிடச்","சோதிப் பிரான்சுடர் மூன்றுஒளி யாய்நிற்கும்","ஆதிப் பிரான்அண்டத்து அப்புறம் கீழவன்","ஆதிப் பிரான்நடு வாகிநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"The First Lord, our Lord, Lord of light of the wide world, stands as the light of the three lights; the First Lord is beyond the cosmos and below it; the First Lord stands in the middle.","n":"சுடர் மூன்று (the three lights) are traditionally sun, moon and fire. ஆதிப்பிரான் (the First Lord) opens three of the four lines.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","aadhiththan","andam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:222"},"t:3006":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":3006,"t":["அண்டம் கடந்துஉயர்ந்து ஓங்கும் பெருமையன்","பிண்டம் கடந்த பிறவிச் சிறுமையன்","தொண்டர் நடந்த கனைகழல் காண்டொறும்","தொண்டர்கள் தூய்நெறி தூங்கிநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"He is great, rising high beyond the cosmos, and small in a birth beyond the body; each time his servants see the ringing anklet that walked, he stands as the pure way, hanging there for them.","n":"The pairing of பெருமையன் and சிறுமையன் (great and small) is the point, as in 3024. பிண்டம் கடந்த பிறவிச் சிறுமையன் and தூங்கிநின்றான் (stood hanging, or stood intent) are hard to fix.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","andam","adiyaar"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:222"},"t:3007":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":3007,"t":["உலவுசெய் நோக்கம் பெருங்கடல் சூழ","நிலம்முழுது எல்லாம் நிறைந்தனன் ஈசன்","பலம்முழுது எல்லாம் படைத்தனன் முன்னே","புலம்முழு பொன்னிற மாகிநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"The Lord, whose roving gaze is ringed by the great sea, has filled the whole land; he first created all its strength, and stands as the whole field of the senses in golden colour.","n":"உலவுசெய் நோக்கம் (the roaming gaze) and புலம் (the field, or the senses) are uncertain; பலம் may be fruit or strength.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","aindhozhil"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:222"},"t:3008":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":3008,"t":["பராபர னாகிப் பல்லூழிகள் தோறும்","பராபர னாய்இவ் அகலிடம் தாங்கித்","தராபர னாய்நின்ற தன்மை யுணரார்","நிராபர னாகி நிறைந்துநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"As the supreme and the lower through many aeons, as the Supreme bearing this wide world, people do not grasp how he stood as bearer of the earth; he stands full as one who needs no support.","n":"The verse plays on -பரன்: பராபரன் (supreme and lower), தராபரன் (bearer of the earth) and நிராபரன் (without support, or without ornament).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","pathi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:222"},"t:3009":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":3009,"t":["போற்றும் பெருந்தெய்வம் தானே பிறரில்லை","ஊற்றமும் ஓசையும் ஓசை ஒடுக்கமும்","வேற்றுடல் தானென்றும் அதுபெருந் தெய்வமாம்","காற்றது ஈசன் கலந்து நின்றானே."],"p":"","r":"The great god who is praised is he alone, there is no other; he is the force and the sound and the subsiding of sound; he is always the body that is other too; that is the great god; the Lord stands mingled as the breath.","n":"ஓசை ஒடுக்கம் (the subsiding of sound) may point to the resolution of nada in yoga. வேற்றுடல் (the other body) is obscure. காற்று (wind, breath) is taken as the life-breath.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","naadham","praanan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:222"},"t:3010":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":3010,"t":["திகையனைத் தும்சிவ னேஅவ னாகின்","மிகையனைத் தும்சொல்ல வேண்டா மனிதரே","புகையனைத் தும்புறம் அங்கியிற் கூடு","முகையனைத் தும்எங்கள் ஆதிப் பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"If all the directions are Siva, he alone, there is no need, men, to say anything more; as all smoke gathers outside in fire, all buds are our First Lord.","n":"The image of smoke and fire in line 3 is unclear: it may mean that every rising thing goes back to its source in the Lord. முகை (bud) may stand for all that is yet to unfold.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:222"},"t:3011":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":3011,"t":["அகன்றான் அகலிடம் ஏழும்ஒன்றாகி","இவன்தான் எனநின்று எளயனும் அல்லன்","சிவன்தான் பலபல சீவனு மாகி","நவின்றான் உலகுறு நம்பனும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"He spread out, the seven wide worlds becoming one; he stands so that one may say 'this is he' and yet is not easily had; Siva himself, becoming many, many souls, is also the trusted one who spoke and lives in the world.","n":"சிவன்தான் பலபல சீவனுமாகி (Siva himself becoming many souls) sounds like identity of the Lord and souls. Siddhanta tradition takes such lines as his pervading souls without being one with them; both readings stand on the words.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","pasu","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:222"},"t:3012":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":3012,"t":["கலையொரு மூன்றும் கடந்தப்பால் நின்ற","தலைவனனை நாடுமின் தத்துவ நாதன்","விலையில்லை விண்ணவ ரோடும் உரைப்ப","உரையில்லை உள்ளுறும் உள்ளவன் தானே."],"p":"","r":"Seek the chief who stands beyond the three kalas, the lord of the tattvas; he has no price; even speaking with the gods there are no words for him; he is the one who dwells within.","n":"கலை ஒரு மூன்றும் (the three kalas) may be the kalas of sun, moon and fire or three of the five kalas of Siddhanta cosmology; the verse does not say.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","thaththuvam","kalai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:222"},"t:3013":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":3013,"t":["படிகால் பிரமன்செய் பாசம் அறுத்து","நெடியான் குறுமைசெய் நேசம் அறுத்துச்","செடியார் தவத்தினில் செய்தொழில் நீக்கி","அடியேனை உய்யவைத்து அன்புகொண் டானே."],"p":"","r":"Cutting the bond made by Brahma of the earth, cutting the attachment to smallness made by the tall one, removing the deeds done in thicket-like penance, he saved me his servant and took me in love.","n":"நெடியான் (the tall one) is Vishnu; Brahma's bond is taken as birth and Vishnu's as attachment to sustained life. செடியார் தவம் (penance thick as a thicket) may be austerities that themselves bind; the verse says the Lord removes even these.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["paasam","thavam","thiruvarul","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:222"},"t:3014":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":3014,"t":["ஈசனென்று எட்டுத் திசையும் இயங்கின","ஓசையில் நின்றெழு சத்தம் உலப்பிலி","தேசமொன்று ஆங்கே செழுங்கண்டம் ஒன்பதும்","வாச மலர்போல் மருவி நின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"The eight directions moved crying 'Lord'; the unfailing one rises as sound from sound; there one land and its nine rich regions: he dwells in them as fragrance in a flower.","n":"ஓசையில் நின்றெழு சத்தம் (sound rising from sound) may refer to nada; செழுங்கண்டம் ஒன்பதும் (the nine regions) is a traditional division of the earth.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","naadham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:222"},"t:3015":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":3015,"t":["இல்லனும் அல்லன் உளன் அல்லன் எம்இறை","நல்லது நெஞ்சம் பிளந்திடும் காட்சியன்","தொல்லையன் தூயன் துளக்கிலன் தூய்மணி","சொல்லரும் சோதி தொடர்ந்துநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"Our Lord is neither non-existent nor existent; his vision splits open the good heart; ancient, pure, unshaken, a pure gem, the light beyond words, he stands, continuing.","n":"இல்லனும் அல்லன் உளன் அல்லன் (not non-being, not being) places him beyond both categories. நெஞ்சம் பிளந்திடும் காட்சி (a vision that splits the heart) is overwhelming sight.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:222"},"t:3016":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":3016,"t":["உள்ளத் தொடுங்கும் புறத்துளும் நானெனும்","கள்ளத் தலைவன் கமழ்சடை நந்தியும்","வள்ளற்பெருமை வழக்கஞ்செய் வார்கள்தம்","அள்ளற் கடலை அறுத்துநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"The thief-chief called 'I', within the heart where it subsides and outside too, and Nandi of the fragrant matted hair: for those who proclaim the greatness of his bounty, he has cut through the sea of mire.","n":"நானெனும் கள்ளத் தலைவன் (the thief-chief called I) is read as the ego. அள்ளற் கடல் (the sea of mire) is taken as the sea of births or of sin. The syntax joining the ego and Nandi in lines 1 and 2 is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","piravi","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:222"},"t:3017":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":3017,"t":["மாறெதிர் வானவர் தானவர் நாடொறும்","கூறுதல் செய்து குரைகழல் நாடுவர்","ஊறுவர் உள்ளத்து அகத்தும் புறுத்துளும்","வேறுசெய்து ஆங்கே விளக்கொளி யாமே."],"p":"","r":"The gods and the asuras, opposed to each other, every day recount and seek his ringing anklet; he wells up within their hearts and outside, and, making them distinct, there becomes the light of a lamp.","n":"வேறுசெய்து (making distinct) may mean that he separates them from their bonds or that he stands apart from them; the verse does not settle it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","thiruvadi","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:222"},"t:3018":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":3018,"t":["விண்ணிலும் வந்த வெளியிலன் மேனியன்","கண்ணிலும் வந்த புலனல்லன் காட்சியன்","பண்ணினில் வந்த பயனல்லன் பான்மையன்","எண்ணில் ஆ னந்தமும் எங்கள் பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"He is not the open space that came in the sky, yet has a form; not an object that came to the eye, yet is seen; not the fruit that came from melody, yet of its nature: our Lord is bliss beyond reckoning.","n":"Each line denies a sensory category and then grants the Lord a likeness to it (மேனியன், காட்சியன், பான்மையன்).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:222"},"t:3019":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":3019,"t":["உத்தமன் எங்கும் உகக்கும் பெருங்கடல்","நித்திலச் சோதியன் நீலக் கருமையன்","எத்தனை காலமும் எண்ணுவர் ஈசனைச்","சித்தர் அமரர்கள் தேர்ந்தறி யாரே."],"p":"","r":"The highest one, the great sea that delights everywhere, of pearl's light and of blue-black, the siddhas and gods think on the Lord through all time and still do not know him fully.","n":"தேர்ந்தறியார் (they do not know by searching) sets even the siddhas and the gods short of him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","siththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:222"},"t:3020":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":3020,"t":["நிறம்பல எவ்வண்ணம் அவ்வண்ணம் ஈசன்","அறம்பல எவ்வண்ணம் அவ்வண்ணம் இன்பம்","மறம்பல எவ்வண்ணம் அவ்வண்ணம் பாவம்","புறம்பல காணினும் போற்றகி லாரே."],"p":"","r":"As many colours as there are, so is the Lord; as many virtues, so much joy; as many wrongs, so much sin; though people see many outward things, they do not praise him.","n":"மறம் here is the opposite of அறம் (wrongdoing), not valour. The middle lines pair virtue with joy and wrong with sin as if to measure them one for one.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","aram","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:222"},"t:3021":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":3021,"t":["இங்குநின் றான்அங்கு நின்றனன் எங்குளன்","பொங்கிநின் றான்புவ னாபதி புண்ணியன்","கங்குல்நின் றான்கதிர் மாமதி ஞாயிறு","எங்குநின் றான்மழை போல்இறை தானே."],"p":"","r":"He stood here, he stood there: where is he? He rose up, the lord of the worlds, the holy one; he stood in the night as the sun and the great moon; the Lord stands everywhere like rain.","n":"The verse answers its own question (எங்குளன், where is he) with the rain, which falls on every place.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","mazhai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:222"},"t:3022":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":3022,"t":["உணர்வது வாயுவே உத்தம மாயும்","உணர்வது நுண்ணறிவு எம்பெரு மானைப்","புணர்வது வாயும் புல்லிய தாயும்","உணர்வுடல் அண்டமும் ஆகிநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"What perceives is the breath, being the highest; what perceives our Lord is subtle knowledge; what joins him is breath, and is slight; he stands as perception, body and cosmos.","n":"வாயு (breath) and நுண்ணறிவு (subtle knowledge) are both named as means of knowing; the yogic sense of breath as what reaches the Lord is traditional. புல்லியதாயும் (being slight, or clinging) is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["praanan","arivu","sivam","andam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:222"},"t:3023":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":3023,"t":["தன்வலி யால்உல கேழும் தரித்தவன்","தன்வலி யாலே அணுவினும் தான்நொய்யன்","தன்வலி யால்மலை எட்டினும் தான்சாரான்","தன்வலி யாலே தடம்கட லாமே."],"p":"","r":"By his own strength he bore the seven worlds; by his own strength he is lighter than the atom; by his own strength he leans on none of the eight mountains; by his own strength he is the wide sea.","n":"தன்வலியால் (by his own strength) opens every line: nothing holds him up, while he holds all. The eight mountains are the traditional mountains at the quarters that bear the earth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","andam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:222"},"t:3024":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":3024,"t":["ஏனோர் பெருமையன் ஆகிலும் எம்இறை","ஊனே சிறுமையும் உட்கலந்து அங்குளன்","வானோர் அறியும் அளவல்லன் மாதேவன்","தானே அறியும் தவத்தின் அளவே."],"p":"","r":"Though our Lord is great beyond all others, he is there mingled within the flesh in smallness too; the great god is not of the measure the gods know; he himself knows the measure of penance.","n":"The last line can be read as 'he alone knows how much penance is done' or 'he is known only in the measure of one's penance'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","thavam","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:222"},"t:3025":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":3025,"t":["பிண்டாலம் வித்தில் எழுந்த பெருமுளைக்","குண்டாலம் காயத்துக் குதிரை பழுத்தது","உண்டனர் உண்டார் உணர்விலா மூடர்கள்","பிண்டத்துஉட் பட்டுப் பிணங்குகின்றார்களே."],"p":"","r":"The great sprout risen from the seed of the banyan of the body: in the pot-like body the horse has ripened; the senseless fools ate it, and caught within the body they quarrel.","n":"This is a riddle verse in the style of Tirumular's hidden-meaning songs. பிண்டாலம் (body-banyan), குண்டாலம் and குதிரை (horse, perhaps the breath or the mind) cannot be fixed; the plain sense is only that fools caught in the body consume its fruit and quarrel.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:222"},"t:3026":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":3026,"t":["ஏயும் சிவபோகம் ஈதன்றி ஓரொளி","ஆயும் அறிவையும் மாயா உபாதியால்","ஏய பரிய புரியும் தனதுஎய்தும்","சாயும் தனது வியாபகம் தானே."],"p":"","r":"This is the Siva-enjoyment that fits; apart from it, the one light and the knowing awareness, through the limiting adjunct of maya, take on what is great and what is made, and his pervasion is his own.","n":"சிவபோகம் (the enjoyment of Siva), மாயா உபாதி and வியாபகம் are technical, and lines 3 and 4 (ஏய பரிய புரியும் தனதுஎய்தும் சாயும்) are too compressed to construe with confidence. The verse seems to say that the pervasion is the Lord's own, while its appearance as many comes through maya's adjunct; that is a reading, not the verse's plain words.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["viyaabagam","upaadhi","maayai","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:223"},"t:3027":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":3027,"t":["நான்அறிந்து அப்பொருள் நாடஇடம் இல்லை","நான்அறிந்து அங்கே வழியுற விம்மிடும்","ஊன்அறிந்து உள்ளே உயிர்க்கின்ற ஒண்சுடர்","தான்அறிந்து அங்கும் தலைப்பட லாமே"],"p":"","r":"There is no room to seek that reality by my own knowing, and knowing it there one swells on the way; when the bright flame that breathes within, knowing the flesh, itself knows, one can reach him there too.","n":"The first line denies that 'I' can find him by searching; the last puts the knowing in the inner flame (ஒண்சுடர்), usually taken as the soul lit by grace, or as the Lord within. விம்மிடும் (swells, overflows) may be the swelling of joy or of weeping.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","arivu","uyir","oli"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:223"},"t:3028":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":3028,"t":["கடலிடை வாழ்கின்ற கௌவை உலகத்து","உடலிடை வாழ்வுகொண்டு உள்ளொளி நாடி","உடலிடை வைகின்ற உள்ளுறு தேவனைக்","கடலின் மலிதிரைக் காணலும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"In this noisy world ringed by the sea, living in the body and seeking the light within, one can see the god who dwells inside the body as plainly as the crowding waves of the sea.","n":"கடல் frames the verse: the sea round the world in line 1 and the sea's crowding waves in line 4. Whether the waves are an image of plainness, of the Lord's countless forms, or of the restless mind through which he is seen is not fixed by the verse. கௌவை may be 'noise' or 'scandal'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","yaakkai","oli"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:223"},"t:3029":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":3029,"t":["பெருஞ்சுடர் மூன்றினும் உள்ளொளி யாகித்","தெரிந்துட லாய் நிற்கும் தேவர் பிரானும்","இருஞ்சுடர் விட்டிட்டு இகலிடம் எல்லாம்","பரிந்துடன் போகின்ற பல்கோரை யாமே."],"p":"","r":"The lord of the gods, who is the inner light in the three great lights and stands knowingly as the body, leaves the great flame and goes along with every place of strife, as sedge spreads far and wide.","n":"The three great lights are traditionally sun, moon and fire. பல்கோரை (many sedges) and இகலிடம் (places of strife) are obscure; the sedge that spreads and goes with the water may picture a pervasion that reaches even the world's conflicts.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["viyaabagam","oli","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:223"},"t:3030":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":3030,"t":["உறுதியின் உள்வந்த உள்வினைப் பட்டு","இறுதியின் வீழ்ந்தார் இரணமது ஆகும்","சிறுதியின் உள்ளொளி திப்பிய மூர்த்தி","பெறுதியின் மேலோர் பெருஞ்சுட ராமே."],"p":"","r":"Those who fell in the end, caught in the inner karma that came in with their resolve, are a wound; the divine form is the inner light in the small one, and for those who gain it the higher beings become the great flame.","n":"Each line turns on a -தியின் form (உறுதியின், இறுதியின், சிறுதியின், பெறுதியின்) made for the rhyme, and their senses are guesses. இரணம் may be 'wound' or 'debt'. The plain contrast is between those who fall by karma and those who gain the inner light.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","oli","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:223"},"t:3031":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":3031,"t":["பற்றி னுள்ளே பரமாய பரஞ்சுடர்","முற்றினும் முற்றி முளைக்கின்ற மூன்றொளி","நெற்றியின் உள்ளே நினைவாய் நிலைதரு","மற்றவ னாய்னி ன்ற மாதவன் தானே."],"p":"","r":"The supreme flame, supreme within what one holds, the threefold light that ripens in all things and sprouts, gives a steady place as thought within the forehead; that is the great ascetic who stood as the other one.","n":"நெற்றியின் உள்ளே நினைவாய் is taken by tradition as meditation fixed between the brows. மூன்றொளி is again the three lights, and மாதவன் may be 'the great ascetic' (Siva) or Mal (Vishnu); மற்றவனாய் நின்ற (who stood as the other) allows either.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oli","thiyaanam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:223"},"t:3032":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":3032,"t":["தேவனும் ஆகும் திசைதிசை பத்துளும்","ஏவனும் ஆம்விரி நீருலகு ஏழையும்","ஆவனு மாம் அமர்ந்து எங்கும் உலகினும்","நாவனும் ஆகி நவிற்றுகின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"He is the god in each of the ten directions, the one who commands the seven worlds of wide waters, the one who comes to be, seated in every world, and as the one with the tongue he speaks.","n":"Each line ends on a -வன் noun (தேவன், ஏவன், ஆவன், நாவன்), so the verse makes its claim by naming him in four roles; நாவனும் ஆகி நவிற்றுகின்றான் makes the Lord the speaker in all speech, and perhaps in this book.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["viyaabagam","sivam","sol"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:223"},"t:3033":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":3033,"t":["நோக்கும் கருடன் நொடிஏழ் உலகையும்","காக்கும் அவனித் தலைவனும் அங்குள","நீக்கும் வினைஎன் நிமலன் பிறப்பிலி","போக்கும் வரவும் புணரல் லானே."],"p":"","r":"The watching Garuda and the lord of the earth who guards the seven worlds in an instant are in him; my spotless one, the unborn, removes karma, and does not join in going and coming.","n":"Garuda is traditionally the one whose glance draws out snake poison, and the lord who guards the worlds is taken as Vishnu, whom Garuda bears. Read so, the verse sets the gods' protection beside the Lord's removal of karma; போக்கும் வரவும் (going and coming) is birth and death.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","vinai","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:223"},"t:3034":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":3034,"t":["செழுஞ்சடை யன் செம்பொ னேயொக்கும் மேனி","ஒழிந்தன னாயும் ஒருங்குடன் கூடும்","கழிந்திலன் எங்கும் பிறப்பிலன் ஈசன்","ஒழிந்திலகு ஏழுலகு ஒத்துநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"The one of rich matted hair, whose body is like red gold, though withdrawn joins with everything together; the Lord is gone from nowhere and born nowhere, and stands equally with the seven shining worlds.","n":"ஒழிந்தனனாயும் (though withdrawn, or though he has ceased) against ஒருங்குடன் கூடும் gives the paradox of the one apart who is with all. ஒழிந்திலகு in line 4 is unclear: perhaps ஒளிர்ந்திலகு (shining).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["viyaabagam","sivam","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:223"},"t:3035":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":3035,"t":["உணர்வும் அவனே உயிரும் அவனே","புணர்வும் அவனே புலனும் அவனே","இணரும் அவன்தன்னை எண்ணலும் ஆகான்","துணரின் மலர்க்கந்தம் துன்னிநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"He is awareness and he is life, he is union and he is the senses; though bound up with all he cannot be reached by thought, and he stays close as scent in a cluster of flowers.","n":"A repeat of 2857 with two different words: புணர்வும் (union) for புணரும் and புலனும் (the senses) for புலவி (lovers' sulking). Here the pair is no longer love's union and quarrel but union and the senses, which fits this chapter's pervasion of all the faculties. The scent in the flower is the one image for being in all and unseen.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","uyir","arivu","viyaabagam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:223"},"t:3036":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":3036,"t":["புலமையின் நாற்றமில் புண்ணியன் எந்தை","நலமையின் ஞான வழக்கமும் ஆகும்","விலமையில் வைத்துள் வேதியர் கூறும்","பலமையில் எங்கும் பரந்துநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"Our father, the holy one without the scent of sense-knowledge, is in goodness the practice of the way of knowledge, and spreads everywhere in the many forms the Vedic seers speak of, keeping within.","n":"Each line opens with a -மையின் or -மையில் form (புலமை, நலமை, விலமை, பலமை); விலமை is not an ordinary word and line 3 cannot be construed with confidence. புலமையின் நாற்றமில் may mean 'untouched by the senses' or 'not smelling of learning'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["viyaabagam","gnaanam","vedham"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:223"},"t:3037":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":3037,"t":["விண்ணவ னாய்உலகு ஏழுக்கு மேலுளன்","மண்ணவ னாய்வலம் சூழ்கடல் ஏழுக்கும்","தண்ணவன் ஆயது தன்மையின் நிற்பதோர்","கண்ணவ னாகிக் கலந்துநின் றானே."],"p":"","r":"As the heavenly one he is above the seven worlds, as the earthly one he is for the seven seas that circle; as the cool one he stands in his own nature, and becoming the eye he stands mingled.","n":"The refrain of -அவன் names (விண்ணவன், மண்ணவன், தண்ணவன், கண்ணவன்) moves from heaven to earth to his own nature to the eye that sees. தண்ணவன் (the cool one) may hint at the moon he wears, and கண்ணவன் at his being the seer within all seeing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["viyaabagam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:223"},"t:3038":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":3038,"t":["நின்றனன் மாலொடு நான்முகன் தானாகி","நின்றனன் தான்நிலம் கீழொடு மேலென","நின்றனன் தான்நெடு மால்வரை ஏழ்கடல்","நின்றனன் தானே வளங்கனி யாயே."],"p":"","r":"He stood as Mal and as the four-faced one, stood as the earth below and above, stood as the tall great mountains and the seven seas, and stood as rich fruit.","n":"நின்றனன் opens every line, the chapter's main refrain. He stands as the other gods of creation and preservation and not only above them; the last line's ripe fruit (வளம் கனி) may be the fruit of the world or the sweetness he is to those who reach him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["viyaabagam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:223"},"t:3039":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":3039,"t":["புவனா பதிமிகு புண்ணியன் எந்தை","அவனே உலகில் அடர்பெரும் பாகன்","அவனே அரும்பல சீவனும் ஆகும்","அவனே இறையென மாலுற்ற வாறே."],"p":"","r":"Our father, the great holy one, is lord of the worlds; he is the mighty driver who presses on in the world, he becomes the many rare souls, and that is how people came to be bewildered into calling him alone God.","n":"அரும்பல சீவனும் ஆகும் (he becomes the many souls) is as strong a statement of oneness as the book makes; within Siddhanta it is taken as his pervading the souls, not being them. மாலுற்றவாறே is unclear: மால் may be bewilderment, love, or Mal (Vishnu), giving 'how Mal came to call him God' or 'how love made us call him God'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","uyir","pathi"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:223"},"t:3040":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":3040,"t":["உண்ணின்று ஒளிரும் உலவாப் பிராணனும்","விண்ணின்று இயங்கும் விரிகதிர்ச் செல்வனும்","மண்ணின்று இயங்கும் வாயுவு மாய் நிற்கும்","கண்ணின்று இயங்கும் கருத்தவன் தானே."],"p":"","r":"He stands as the unwasting breath that shines within, as the lord of spreading rays who moves in the sky, and as the wind that moves over the earth; he is the thought that moves out from the eye.","n":"The verse matches places to powers: within, the breath; in the sky, the sun; on earth, the wind; in the eye, thought or meaning (கருத்து). பிராணன் and வாயு are both the breath, the one inside and the one outside.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["viyaabagam","praanan","aadhiththan"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:223"},"t:3041":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":3041,"t":["எண்ணும் எழுத்தும் இனஞ்செயல் அவ்வழிப்","பண்ணும் திறனும் படைத்த பரமனைக்","கண்ணிற் கவரும் கருத்தில் அதுஇது","உண்ணின்று உருக்கியோர் ஆயமும் ஆமே."],"p":"","r":"The Supreme who made number and letter, the kinds of action, the music and the skill in that way: in the thought that the eye takes in, this and that, he stands within, melts one, and becomes one's company.","n":"The first two lines make the arts of knowledge his creation. Lines 3 and 4 are obscure: அதுஇது (that, this) may be the things perceived, and ஓர் ஆயமும் ஆமே may mean he becomes a companion, a crowd, or a gain (ஆயம் as income).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","kalvi","viyaabagam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:223"},"t:3042":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":3042,"t":["இருக்கின்ற எண்டிசை அண்டம்பா தாளம்","உருக்கொடு தன்னடு ஒங்கஇவ்வண்ணம்","கருக்கொடு எங்கும் கலந்திருந் தானே","திருக்கொன்றை வைத்த செழுஞ்சடை யானே."],"p":"","r":"He who wears the holy konrai on his rich matted hair dwelt mingled everywhere, with the seed, so that the eight directions, the cosmic sphere and the underworld that exist rise up with form together with him.","n":"கருக்கொடு (with the embryo or seed) suggests that he is in things as their seed, as in the bindu chapter, but the verse doesn't say so. Line 2 (உருக்கொடு தன்னடு ஓங்க) reads தன்னடு, probably for தன்னொடு.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["viyaabagam","andam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:223"},"t:3043":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":3043,"t":["பலவுடன் சென்றஅப் பார்முழுது ஈசன்","செலவுஅறி வார்இல்லை சேயன் அணியன்","அலைவிலன் சங்கரன் ஆதிஎம் ஆதி","பலவில தாய் நிற்கும் பான்மைவல் லானே."],"p":"","r":"No one knows the course of the Lord through the whole world that has gone along with many things; he is far and near, the unwavering Sankara, the first, our first, skilled in standing as the one that is not many.","n":"சேயன் அணியன் (far one, near one) is a common pairing in Saiva hymns. பலவிலதாய் நிற்கும் closes the verse that opened with பலவுடன்: present with many things, he stays not-many.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sivam","viyaabagam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:223"},"t:3044":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":3044,"t":["அதுஅறி வானவன் ஆதிப் புராணன்","எதுஅறி யாவகை நின்றவன் ஈசன்","பொதுஅது வான புவனங்கள் எட்டும்","இதுஅறி வானநந்தி எங்கள் பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"He who is that knowledge is the primal ancient one; the Lord stood so that nothing could know him; Nandi, who knows this, is our lord, and he is the eight worlds common to all.","n":"Close to 1722: lines 2 and 3 share most words, but 1722 ends with the Lord taking the poet's body as his temple and this verse ends with Nandi, the guru, as the one who knows. பொதுஅது வான (become common) for 1722's புதுஉணர்வான (of new awareness) changes the epithet of the eight worlds.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","gnaanam","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:223"},"t:3045":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":3045,"t":["நீரும் நிலனும் விசும்புஅங்கி மாருதம்","தூரும் உடம்புறு சோதியு மாய் உளன்","பேரும் பராபரன் பிஞ்ஞகன் எம்இறை","ஊரும் சகலன் உலப்பிலி தானே."],"p":"","r":"He is water, earth, sky, fire and wind, and the light that dwells in the body; his name is the Supreme beyond the supreme, Pinakan, our Lord, and he who rides is the all-containing, the unwasting.","n":"The five elements and the light in the body echo the old list of the Lord's eight forms, though the verse names only six. தூரும் and ஊரும் சகலன் are unclear: ஊரும் may be 'who rides (the bull)' or 'whose town'; சகலன் may mean the all-containing or, in Siddhanta usage, the one in the sakala state.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["viyaabagam","yaakkai","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:223"},"t:3046":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":3046,"t":["மூலன் உரைசெய்த மூவா யிரந்தமிழ்","மூலன் உரைசெய்த முன்னூறு மந்திரம்","மூலன் உரைசெய்த முப்பது உபதேசம்","மூலன் உரைசெய்த மூன்றும் ஒன்றாமே."],"p":"","r":"The three thousand Tamil verses Mulan spoke, the three hundred mantras Mulan spoke, the thirty teachings Mulan spoke: the three that Mulan spoke are one.","n":"The verse speaks of Mulan in the third person, like 99, and may be a later colophon. It doesn't say what the three hundred mantras and thirty teachings are; they are sometimes taken as other works ascribed to Tirumular and sometimes as parts of this one. The claim is their unity, not their contents.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thamizh","mandhiram","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:223"},"t:3047":{"book":"tirumanthiram","num":3047,"t":["வாழ்கவே வாழ்கஎன் நந்தி திருவடி","வாழ்கவே வாழ்க மலமறுத் தான்பதம்","வாழ்கவே வாழ்கமெய்ஞ் ஞானத் தவன்தாள்","வாழ்கவே வாழ்க மலமிலான் பாதமே."],"p":"","r":"May they live, may they live: my Nandi's holy feet, the feet of the one who cut away impurity, the feet of the one of true knowledge, the feet of the one without impurity.","n":"Each line repeats வாழ்கவே வாழ்க and names a different holder of the feet. மலமறுத்தான் (who cut away impurity, for others) and மலமிலான் (who has none) can be read as the guru and Siva, or as one being under four names; Tirumular often makes Nandi both.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvadi","guru","malam","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"t:224"},"p:1":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1,"t":["கைத்தல நிறைகனி அப்பமொ டவல்பொரி","கப்பிய கரிமுகன் ...... அடிபேணிக்","கற்றிடும் அடியவர் புத்தியில் உறைபவ","கற்பகம் எனவினை ...... கடிதேகும்","மத்தமு மதியமும் வைத்திடும் அரன்மகன்","மற்பொரு திரள்புய ...... மதயானை","மத்தள வயிறனை உத்தமி புதல்வனை","மட்டவிழ் மலர்கொடு ...... பணிவேனே","முத்தமிழ் அடைவினை முற்படு கிரிதனில்","முற்பட எழுதிய ...... முதல்வோனே","முப்புரம் எரிசெய்த அச்சிவன் உறைரதம்","அச்சது பொடிசெய்த ...... அதிதீரா","அத்துய ரதுகொடு சுப்பிர மணிபடும்","அப்புன மதனிடை ...... இபமாகி","அக்குற மகளுடன் அச்சிறு முருகனை","அக்கண மணமருள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet worships with honey-dripping flowers the elephant-faced, pot-bellied son of Hara and Uma, who holds fruit, appam, aval and puffed rice in his hand and dwells like the wish-granting tree in the minds of devotees, so that their deeds flee. He praises him as the first to write Tamil on the first mountain, the hero who broke the axle of the chariot Siva rode to burn the three cities, and the one who became an elephant in the millet field so that little Murugan wed the hunter girl at once.","n":"கற்பகம் எனவினை கடிதேகும்: for those who keep him in mind he is the kalpaka tree and deeds (வினை) leave quickly. முத்தமிழ் ... கிரிதனில் முற்பட எழுதிய points to the tradition that Ganapati wrote the epic on Meru with his tusk; the text says only that he first wrote the threefold Tamil on the mountain. The last lines name the hunter girl (குறமகள்) and the millet field, so Valli's story, in which the elephant drives her to Murugan.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","vinai","thamizh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:1"},"p:2":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":2,"t":["பக்கரைவி சித்ரமணி பொற்கலணை யிட்டநடை","பட்சியெனு முக்ரதுர ...... கமுநீபப்","பக்குவம லர்த்தொடையும் அக்குவடு பட்டொழிய","பட்டுருவ விட்டருள்கை ...... வடிவேலும்","திக்கதும திக்கவரு குக்குடமும் ரட்சைதரு","சிற்றடியு முற்றியப ...... னிருதோளும்","செய்ப்பதியும் வைத்துயர்தி ருப்புகழ்வி ருப்பமொடு","செப்பெனஎ னக்கருள்கை ...... மறவேனே","இக்கவரை நற்கனிகள் சர்க்கரைப ருப்புடனெய்","எட்பொரிய வற்றுவரை ...... இளநீர்வண்","டெச்சில்பய றப்பவகை பச்சரிசி பிட்டுவெள","ரிப்பழமி டிப்பல்வகை ...... தன஧முலம்","மிக்கஅடி சிற்கடலை பட்சணமெ னக்கொளொரு","விக்கிநச மர்த்தனெனும் ...... அருளாழி","வெற்பகுடி லச்சடில விற்பரம ரப்பரருள்","வித்தகம ருப்புடைய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says he will not forget the grace by which Murugan told him to sing, with love, an exalted Thiruppugazh that sets in it the saddled peacock, the kadamba garland, the vel that pierced the hill, the cock, the small feet, the twelve shoulders and the town of Seyppathi. The second half lists the offerings Ganapati takes as his own, sugarcane, fruits, sugar, dal, ghee, sesame, tender coconut, raw rice, pittu, cucumber and more, and praises him as the remover of obstacles, the son of the Lord with matted hair, the master with the tusk.","n":"This is the book naming itself: திருப்புகழ் ... செப்பென எனக்கருள்கை, the grace of being told to sing it. The emblems listed (peacock, vel, cock, feet, shoulders, the shrine) are the furniture of almost every later song. செய்ப்பதி is usually taken as Vayalur. விக்கிந சமர்த்தன் is the one able against obstacles (vighna). One word in the offering list is corrupt.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["pugazh","thamizh","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:1"},"p:3":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":3,"t":["உம்பர்தருத் தேநுமணிக் ...... கசிவாகி","ஒண்கடலிற் றேனமுதத் ...... துணர்வூறி","இன்பரசத் தேபருகிப் ...... பலகாலும்","என்றனுயிர்க் காதரவுற் ...... றருள்வாயே","தம்பிதனக் காகவனத் ...... தணைவோனே","தந்தைவலத் தாலருள்கைக் ...... கனியோனே","அன்பர்தமக் கானநிலைப் ...... பொருளோனே","ஐந்துகரத் தானைமுகப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks Ganapati to melt for him like the wish-granting tree, cow and gem of the gods, to let him drink the nectar of bliss many times, and to give his life grace. He praises him as the one who went to the forest for his younger brother's sake, who won the fruit by circling his father, the substance of those who love him, the five-handed elephant face.","n":"தம்பிதனக்காக வனத்தணைவோனே recalls the elephant in Valli's forest (see 1). தந்தைவலத்தால் அருள் கைக்கனியோனே is the fruit won by going round the father, the story in which Murugan circles the world (30).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:2"},"p:4":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":4,"t":["நினது திருவடி சத்திம யிற்கொடி","நினைவு கருதிடு புத்திகொ டுத்திட","நிறைய அமுதுசெய் முப்பழ மப்பமு ...... நிகழ்பால்தேன்","நெடிய வளைமுறி இக்கொடு லட்டுகம்","நிறவில் அரிசிப ருப்பவல் எட்பொரி","நிகரில் இனிகத லிக்கனி வர்க்கமும் ...... இளநீரும்","மனது மகிழ்வொடு தொட்டக ரத்தொரு","மகர சலநிதி வைத்தது திக்கர","வளரு கரிமுக ஒற்றைம ருப்பனை ...... வலமாக","மருவு மலர்புனை தொத்திர சொற்கொடு","வளர்கை குழைபிடி தொப்பண குட்டொடு","வனச பரிபுர பொற்பத அர்ச்சனை ...... மறவேனே","தெனன தெனதென தெத்தென னப்பல","சிறிய அறுபத மொய்த்துதி ரப்புனல்","திரளும் உறுசதை பித்தநி ணக்குடல் ...... செற஧முளை","செரும உதரநி ரப்புசெ ருக்குடல்","நிரைய அரவநி றைத்தக ளத்திடை","திமித திமிதிமி மத்தளி டக்கைகள் ...... செகசேசே","எனவெ துகுதுகு துத்ததென ஒத்துகள்","துடிகள் இடிமிக ஒத்துமு ழக்கிட","டிமுட டிமுடிமு டிட்டிமெ னத்தவில் ...... எழுமோசை","இகலி அலகைகள் கைப்பறை கொட்டிட","இரண பயிரவி சுற்றுந டித்திட","எதிரு நிசிசர ரைப்பெலி யிட்டருள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says he will never forget to worship the elephant-faced one with the single tusk, setting out fruits, appam, milk, honey, sugarcane, laddus, rice, dal, aval, sesame, plantains and tender coconut, with hymns, holding his ears, sit-ups and knocks on the temples, before the anklet-bearing lotus feet, so that he is given a mind that keeps the Lord's feet in thought. The second half is a battlefield: flies swarm on rivers of blood, flesh, bile, fat and brains, drums and tabors thunder, ghouls clap, Bhairavi dances in a ring, and the Lord offers the night-walking demons as sacrifice.","n":"குழைபிடி தொப்பண குட்டொடு names the gestures still made before Ganapati: holding the ears, sit-ups (தோப்புக்கரணம்) and knocking the temples. The battle half is sung in drum syllables (தெனன, திமித, டிமுட), a device used again in 6 and 11. Whether the closing பெருமாளே is Ganapati, as the heading implies, or Murugan, the song does not settle.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["vazhipaadu","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:2"},"p:5":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":5,"t":["விடமடைசு வேலை அமரர்படை சூலம்","விசையன்விடு பாண ...... மெனவேதான்","விழியுமதி பார விதமுமுடை மாதர்","வினையின் விளை வேதும் ...... அறியாதே","கடியுலவு பாயல் பகலிரவெ னாது","கலவிதனில் மூழ்கி ...... வறிதாய","கயவனறி வீனன் இவனுமுயர் நீடு","கழலிணைகள் சேர ...... அருள்வாயே","இடையர்சிறு பாலை திருடிகொடு போக","இறைவன்மகள் வாய்மை ...... அறியாதே","இதயமிக வாடி யுடையபிளை நாத","கணபதியெ னாம ...... முறைகூற","அடையலவர் ஆவி வெருவஅடி கூர","அசலுமறி யாமல் ...... அவரோட","அகல்வதென டாசொல் எனவுமுடி சாட","அறிவருளும் ஆனை ...... முகவோனே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses that, not knowing what deeds yield, he has drowned day and night in lovemaking on fragrant beds with women whose eyes are like the poison-filled sea, the gods' weapons, the trident and Vijaya's arrow, a base and ignorant wretch; he asks that he too be granted the Lord's anklets. The praise tells a story in which cowherds steal a little milk, a lord's daughter, not knowing the truth, cries out to 'my child, Ganapati', and the elephant-faced god gives understanding.","n":"வினையின் விளைவு ஏதும் அறியாதே: the fault confessed is ignorance of what deeds bring. The second half's story is not one I can identify, and the lines (அகல்வதென் டாசொல் எனவுமுடி சாட) are hard to divide; the reading of the praise is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","vinai","pedhaimai","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:2"},"p:6":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":6,"t":["முத்தைத்தரு பத்தித் திருநகை","அத்திக்கிறை சத்திச் சரவண","முத்திக்கொரு வித்துக் குருபர ...... எனவோதும்","முக்கட்பர மற்குச் சுருதியின்","முற்பட்டது கற்பித் திருவரும்","முப்பத்துமு வர்க்கத் தமரரும் ...... அடிபேணப்","பத்துத்தலை தத்தக் கணைதொடு","ஒற்றைக்கிரி மத்தைப் பொருதொரு","பட்டப்பகல் வட்டத் திகிரியில் ...... இரவாகப்","பத்தற்கிர தத்தைக் கடவிய","பச்சைப்புயல் மெச்சத் தகுபொருள்","பட்சத்தொடு ரட்சித் தருள்வதும் ...... ஒருநாளே","தித்தித்தெய ஒத்தப் பரிபுர","நிர்த்தப்பதம் வைத்துப் பயிரவி","திக்கொட்கந டிக்கக் கழுகொடு ...... கழுதாடத்","திக்குப்பரி அட்டப் பயிரவர்","தொக்குத்தொகு தொக்குத் தொகுதொகு","சித்ரப்பவு ரிக்குத் த்ரிகடக ...... எனவோதக்","கொத்துப்பறை கொட்டக் களமிசை","குக்குக்குகு குக்குக் குகுகுகு","குத்திப்புதை புக்குப் பிடியென ...... முதுகூகை","கோட்புற்றெழ நட்பற் றவுணரை","வெட்டிப்பலி யிட்டுக் குலகிரி","குத்துப்பட ஒத்துப் பொரவல ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet calls Murugan the Shakti of the pearl-smiling daughter of the elephant's lord, Saravana, the one seed of release, the supreme guru, who taught the three-eyed Lord what comes before the Veda and whose feet Brahma, Vishnu and the thirty-three gods worship. He asks whether there will be a day when Murugan protects him with the meaning that Vishnu praises, Vishnu who loosed the arrow at the ten heads, churned with the mountain, made night at midday with his discus, and drove his devotee's chariot. The second half is a battle: Bhairavi dances, vultures and ghouls, the eight Bhairavas chant, owls cry, and the demons are cut down and offered as sacrifice while the great hill is pierced.","n":"முக்கட் பரமற்குச் சுருதியின் முற்பட்டது கற்பித்து: what precedes the Veda is taken as Om, which the son taught the father. அத்திக்கிறை is the lord of the elephant, Indra, and his daughter is Devayanai. Vishnu's deeds are named by their details: Ravana's ten heads, the churning, the discus that hid the sun at midday, and the charioteer of Arjuna (பத்தற்கு இரதத்தைக் கடவிய). What 'the meaning Vishnu praises' (மெச்சத் தகு பொருள்) is, the song leaves open.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","piranavam","veedu","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:3"},"p:7":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":7,"t":["அருக்கு மங்கையர் மலரடி வருடியெ","கருத்த றிந்துபின் அரைதனில் உடைதனை","அவிழ்த்தும் அங்குள அரசிலை தடவியும் ...... இருதோளுற்","றணைத்தும் அங்கையின் அடிதொறும் நகமெழ","உதட்டை மென்றுபல் இடுகுறி களுமிட","அடிக்க ளந்தனில் மயில்குயில் புறவென ...... மிகவாய்விட்","டுருக்கும் அங்கியின் மெழுகென உருகிய","சிரத்தை மிஞ்சிடும் அநுபவம் உறுபலம்","உறக்கை யின்கனி நிகரென இலகிய ...... முலைமேல்வீழ்ந்","துருக்க லங்கிமெய் உருகிட அமுதுகு","பெருத்த உந்தியின் முழுகிமெ யுணர்வற","உழைத்தி டுங்கன கலவியை மகிழ்வது ...... தவிர்வேனோ","இருக்கு மந்திரம் எழுவகை முநிபெற","உரைத்த சம்ப்ரம சரவண பவகுக","இதத்த இங்கிதம் இலகிய அறுமுக ...... எழில்வேளென்","றிலக்க ணங்களும் இயலிசை களுமிக","விரிக்கும் அம்பல மதுரித கவிதனை","இயற்று செந்தமிழ் விதமொடு புயமிசை ...... புனைவோனே","செருக்கும் அம்பல மிசைதனில் அசைவுற","நடித்த சங்கரர் வழிவழி அடியவர்","திருக்கு ருந்தடி அருள்பெற அருளிய ...... குருநாதர்","திருக்கு ழந்தையு மெனஅவர் வழிபடு","குருக்க ளின்திற மெனவரு பெரியவ","திருப்ப ரங்கிரி தனிலுறை சரவண ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes, frankly and at length, lovemaking with proud women, from stroking their feet and loosening their clothes to biting, nail marks and the cries of birds, and asks whether he will ever give up delighting in it. He praises Murugan as Saravanabhava who told the Rig mantra to the seven sages, who wears on his shoulders Tamil poems that spread grammar and music, and as the holy child of the guru who gave grace under the kuruntham tree to the devotees of Sankara who danced in the hall.","n":"தவிர்வேனோ, 'will I give it up?', is the whole plea: the confession is the bulk of the song. திருக்குருந்தடி அருள்பெற அருளிய குருநாதர் recalls Siva as the guru under the kuruntham tree, a story told of Manikkavasagar; the following lines (குருக்களின் திறம்) make Murugan the guru of gurus, and their syntax is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","punarchi","thamizh","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:4"},"p:8":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":8,"t":["உனைத்தி னந்தொழு திலனுன தியல்பினை","உரைத்தி லன்பல மலர்கொடுன் அடியிணை","உறைப்ப ணிந்திலன் ஒருதவ மிலனுன ...... தருள்மாறா","உளத்து ளன்பினர் உறைவிடம் அறிகிலன்","விருப்பொ டுன்சிக ரமும்வலம் வருகிலன்","உவப்பொ டுன்புகழ் துதிசெய விழைகிலன் ...... மலைபோலே","கனைத்தெ ழும்பக டதுபிடர் மிசைவரு","கறுத்த வெஞ்சின மறலிதன் உழையினர்","கதித்த டர்ந்தெறி கயிறடு கதைகொடு ...... பொருபோதே","கலக்கு றுஞ்செயல் ஒழிவற அழிவுறு","கருத்து நைந்தல முறுபொழு தளவைகொள்","கணத்தில் என்பய மறமயில் முதுகினில் ...... வருவாயே","வினைத்த லந்தனில் அலகைகள் குதிகொள","விழுக்கு டைந்துமெய் உகுதசை கழுகுண","விரித்த குஞ்சியர் எனுமவு ணரைஅமர் ...... புரிவேலா","மிகுத்த பண்பயில் குயில்மொழி அழகிய","கொடிச்சி குங்கும முலைமுக டுழுநறை","விரைத்த சந்தன ம்ருகமத புயவரை ...... உடையோனே","தினத்தி னஞ்சதுர் மறைமுநி முறைகொடு","புனற்சொ ரிந்தலர் பொதியவி ணவரொடு","சினத்தை நிந்தனை செயுமுநி வரர்தொழ ...... மகிழ்வோனே","தெனத்தெ னந்தன எனவரி யளிநறை","தெவிட்ட அன்பொடு பருகுயர் பொழில்திகழ்","திருப் பரங்கிரி தனிலுறை சரவண ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses that he has not worshipped Murugan daily, told his nature, bowed at his feet with flowers, done any austerity, sought out his devotees, gone round his hill or praised him with joy; and asks that when Death's men come on the bellowing buffalo with noose and club and his mind fails, Murugan come at that moment on the peacock and end his fear. The praise names the vel that slew the demons while ghouls leapt and vultures fed, the hunter girl's kumkum on his shoulders, and the sages of the four Vedas who worship him daily at Parangiri.","n":"The confession is a list of omitted worship (தொழுதிலன், பணிந்திலன், வருகிலன்), and the plea is for one moment only, the hour of death. மறலிதன் உழையினர் are Death's servants, with the noose (கயிறு) and club (கதை) and Yama's buffalo (பகடு). This hour-of-death plea recurs through the book.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","vazhipaadu","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:4"},"p:9":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":9,"t":["கருவடைந்து பத்துற்ற திங்கள்","வயிறிருந்து முற்றிப்ப யின்று","கடையில்வந்து தித்துக்கு ழந்தை ...... வடிவாகிக்","கழுவியங்கெ டுத்துச்சு ரந்த","முலையருந்து விக்கக்கி டந்து","கரறியங்கை கொட்டித்த வழ்ந்து ...... நடமாடி","அரைவடங்கள் கட்டிச்ச தங்கை","இடுகுதம்பை பொற்சுட்டி தண்டை","அவையணிந்து முற்றிக்கி ளர்ந்து ...... வயதேறி","அரியபெண்கள் நட்பைப்பு ணர்ந்து","பிணியுழன்று சுற்றித்தி ரிந்த","தமையுமுன்க்ரு பைச்சித்தம் என்று ...... பெறுவேனோ","இரவிஇந்த்ரன் வெற்றிக்கு ரங்கி","னரசரென்றும் ஒப்பற்ற உந்தி","யிறைவன்எண்கி னக்கர்த்த னென்றும் ...... நெடுநீலன்","எரியதென்றும் ருத்ரற்சி றந்த","அநுமனென்றும் ஒப்பற்ற அண்டர்","எவரும்இந்த வர்க்கத்தில் வந்து ...... புனமேவ","அரியதன்ப டைக்கர்த்த ரென்று","அசுரர்தங்கி ளைக்கட்டை வென்ற","அரிமுகுந்தன் மெச்சுற்ற பண்பின் ...... மருகோனே","அயனையும்பு டைத்துச்சி னந்து","உலகமும்ப டைத்துப்ப ரிந்து","அருள்பரங்கி ரிக்குட்சி றந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet traces his life from the embryo ten months in the womb, through birth, washing, nursing, crawling and the child's ornaments, to youth, friendship with women and wandering in disease, and asks when he will gain the mind of Murugan's grace. The praise tells how the gods came to earth as Rama's monkey and bear army, the sun and Indra as monkey kings, Brahma as lord of the bears, Agni as Nila and Rudra as Hanuman, and names Murugan as nephew of Hari who defeated the asuras, and as the one who beat Brahma in anger and himself created the world.","n":"The life is told as one breathless sequence that runs from womb to women to disease with no pause, so that youth and lust lead straight to sickness. அயனையும் புடைத்துச் சினந்து உலகமும் படைத்து is the story in which Murugan punished Brahma and took over creation.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karu","ilamai","yaakkai","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:4"},"p:10":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":10,"t":["கறுக்கும் அஞ்சன விழியிணை அயில்கொடு","நெருக்கி நெஞ்சற எறிதரு பொழுதொரு","கனிக்குள் இன்சுவை அழுதுகும் ஒருசிறு ...... நகையாலே","களக்கொ ழுங்கலி வலைகொடு விசிறியெ","மனைக்கெ ழுந்திரும் எனமனம் உருகஒர்","கவற்சி கொண்டிட மனைதனில் அழகொடு ...... கொடுபோகி","நறைத்த பஞ்சணை மிசையினில் மனமுற","அணைத்த கந்தனில் இணைமுலை எதிர்பொர","நகத்த ழுந்திட அமுதிதழ் பருகிய ...... மிடறு஡டே","நடித்தெ ழுங்குரல் குமுகுமு குமுவென","இசைத்து நன்கொடு மனமது மறுகிட","நழுப்பு நஞ்சன சிறுமிகள் துயரற ...... அருள்வாயே","நிறைத்த தெண்டிரை மொகுமொகு மொகுவென","உரத்த கஞ்சுகி முடிநெறு நெறுவென","நிறைத்த அண்டமு கடுகிடு கிடுவென ...... வரைபோலும்","நிவத்த திண்திகழ் நிசிசர ருரமொடு","சிரக்கொ டுங்குவை மலைபுரை தரஇரு","நிணக்கு ழம்பொடு குருதிகள் சொரிதர ...... அடுதீரா","திறற்க ருங்குழல் உமையவள் அருளுறு","புழைக்கை தண்கட கயமுக மிகவுள","சிவக்கொ ழுந்தன கணபதி யுடன்வரும் ...... இளையோனே","சினத்தொ டுஞ்சமன் உதைபட நிறுவிய","பரற்கு ளன்புறு புதல்வநன் மணியுகு","திருப்ப ரங்கிரி தனிலுறை சரவண ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes how courtesans wound with kohl-dark eyes, catch a man with a smile and a net of talk, take him home with 'come in', and make love to him, and asks to be freed from the sorrow these deceiving young women cause. The praise is a battle in which the sea churns, the serpent's crowns crack and the demons' heads are heaped like mountains in fat and blood, and names Murugan as the younger brother of Ganapati and the son of the one who kicked Yama, at Parangiri.","n":"சமன் உதைபட நிறுவிய பரன் is Siva who kicked Yama, the story of Markandeya; the song places it beside the poet's own fear without drawing the line. நஞ்சன சிறுமிகள் calls the women poisonous girls.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["varaivinmagalir","kaamam","vanjam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:4"},"p:11":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":11,"t":["கனகந்திரள் கின்றபெ ருங்கிரி","தனில்வந்துத கன்தகன் என்றிடு","கதிர்மிஞ்சிய செண்டைஎ றிந்திடு ...... கதியோனே","கடமிஞ்சி அநந்தவி தம்புணர்","கவளந்தனை உண்டுவ ளர்ந்திடு","கரியின்றுணை என்றுபி றந்திடு ...... முருகோனே","பனகந்துயில் கின்றதி றம்புனை","கடல்முன்புக டைந்தப ரம்பரர்","படரும்புயல் என்றவர் அன்புகொள் ...... மருகோனே","பலதுன்பம்உழன்றுக லங்கிய","சிறியன்புலை யன்கொலை யன்புரி","பவமின்றுக ழிந்திட வந்தருள் ...... புரிவாயே","அனகன்பெயர் நின்றுரு ளுந்திரி","புரமுந்திரி வென்றிட இன்புடன்","அழலுந்தந குந்திறல் கொண்டவர் ...... புதல்வோனே","அடல்வந்துமு ழங்கியி டும்பறை","டுடுடுண்டுடு டுண்டுடு டுண்டென","அதிர்கின்றிட அண்டநெ ரிந்திட ...... வருசூரர்","மனமுந்தழல் சென்றிட அன்றவர்","உடலுங்குட லுங்கிழி கொண்டிட","மயில்வென்றனில் வந்தரு ளுங்கன ...... பெரியோனே","மதியுங்கதி ருந்தட வும்படி","உயர்கின்றவ னங்கள்பொ ருந்திய","வளமொன்றுப ரங்கிரி வந்தருள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A song mostly of praise: Murugan threw the blazing ball at the great golden mountain, was born as companion of the elephant, is nephew of the one who sleeps on the serpent and churned the sea, and son of the one who burned the three cities; with drums sounding he tore the bodies of the Suras and came on the peacock. In its middle the poet, small, base and a killer, troubled by many sorrows, asks Murugan to come and end his birth today, at Parangiri.","n":"சிறியன் புலையன் கொலையன் is the self-description of the book: small, outcast, killer. The ball thrown at the mountain (செண்டை எறிந்திடு) is an allusion I cannot place with certainty.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","kutram","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:4"},"p:12":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":12,"t":["காதட ருங்கயல் கொண்டிசைந் தைம்பொறி","வாளிம யங்கம னம்பயந் தந்திருள்","கால்தர விந்துவி சும்பிலங் கும்பொழு ...... தொருகோடி","காய்கதி ரென்றொளிர் செஞ்சிலம் புங்கணை","யாழியு டன்கட கந்துலங் கும்படி","காமனெ டுஞ்சிலை கொண்டடர்ந் தும்பொரு ...... மயலாலே","வாதுபு ரிந்தவர் செங்கைதந் திங்கித","மாகந டந்தவர் பின்திரிந் துந்தன","மார்பில ழுந்தஅ ணைந்திடுந் துன்பம ...... துழலாதே","வாசமி குந்தக டம்பமென் கிண்கிணி","மாலைக ரங்கொளும் அன்பர்வந் தன்பொடு","வாழநி தம்புனை யும்பதந் தந்துன ...... தருள்தாராய்","போதிலு றைந்தருள் கின்றவன் செஞ்சிர","மீதுத டிந்துவி லங்கிடும் புங்கவ","போதவ ளஞ்சிவ சங்கரன் கொண்டிட ...... மொழிவோனே","பூகமு டன்திகழ் சங்கினங் கொண்டகி","஡ணவம டந்தைபு ரந்தரன் தந்தருள்","பூவைக ருங்குற மின்கலந் தங்குப ...... னிருதோளா","தீதக மொன்றினர் வஞ்சகந் துஞ்சியி","டாதவர் சங்கரர் தந்ததென் பும்பல","சேர்நிரு தன்குலம் அஞ்சமுன் சென்றடு ...... திறலோனே","சீதள முந்தும ணந்தயங் கும்பொழில்","சூழ்தர விஞ்சைகள் வந்திறைஞ் சும்பதி","தேவர்ப ணிந்தெழு தென்பரங் குன்றுறை ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet tells how Kama, with eyes like carp, the five senses and the moon, attacks with his bow and how, deluded, he follows women and suffers in their embrace; he asks instead for the feet that devotees adorn with kadamba garlands. The praise names Murugan as the one who struck the lotus-dweller's head and fettered him and told the wealth of knowledge to Siva, whose twelve shoulders the conch-necked daughter of Indra and the hunter girl embrace, and who destroyed the clan of Nirudan, at south Parangundram.","n":"மயல் (delusion) is the poet's word for his state. Brahma (போதிலுறைந்தருள்கின்றவன்) is struck and bound, and the knowledge he lacked is given to Siva (போதவளம் சிவசங்கரன் கொண்டிட மொழிவோனே), the same story as 6. One word is corrupt; கிரீவ, neck, is the likely reading.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kaamam","guru","gnaanam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:4"},"p:13":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":13,"t":["சந்ததம் பந்தத் ...... தொடராலே","சஞ்சலந் துஞ்சித் ...... திரியாதே","கந்தனென் றென்றுற் ...... றுனைநாளும்","கண்டுகொண் டன்புற் ...... றிடுவேனோ","தந்தியின் கொம்பைப் ...... புணர்வோனே","சங்கரன் பங்கிற் ...... சிவைபாலா","செந்திலங் கண்டிக் ...... கதிர்வேலா","தென்பரங் குன்றிற் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A short song: the poet asks whether, no longer wandering anxious in the chain of bonds, he will one day call on Kanda again and again, see him and love him. He names Murugan as the one joined to the elephant's daughter, the son of Sivai on Sankara's side, the vel at Senthil and Kandi, the lord at south Parangundram.","n":"பந்தத் தொடர், the chain of bonds, and சஞ்சலம், agitation, are the condition; the cure is only the repeated name கந்தன். கண்டிக் கதிர்வேலா may point to Kathirkamam in Lanka; the reading is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["paasam","anbu","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:4"},"p:14":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":14,"t":["சருவும்படி வந்தனன் இங்கித","மதனின்றிட அம்புலி யுஞ்சுடு","தழல்கொண்டிட மங்கையர் கண்களின் ...... வசமாகிச்","சயிலங்கொளு மன்றல்பொ ருந்திய","பொழிலின்பயில் தென்றலும் ஒன்றிய","தடவஞ்சுனை துன்றியெ ழுந்திட ...... திறமாவே","இரவும்பகல் அந்தியு நின்றிடு","குயில்வந்திசை தெந்தன என்றிட","இருகண்கள்து யின்றிட லின்றியும் ...... அயர்வாகி","இவணெஞ்சுப தன்பதன் என்றிட","மயல்கொண்டுவ ருந்திய வஞ்சகன்","இனியுன் றன்ம லர்ந்தில கும்பதம் ...... அடைவேனோ","திருவொன்றிவி ளங்கிய அண்டர்கள்","மனையின்தயிர் உண்டவன் எண்டிசை","திகழும்புகழ் கொண்டவன் வண்டமிழ் ...... பயில்வோர்பின்","திரிகின்றவன் மஞ்சுநி றம்புனை","பவன்மிஞ்சுதி றங்கொள வென்றடல்","செயதுங்கமு குந்தன்ம கிழ்ந்தருள் ...... முருகோனே","மதியுங்கதி ரும்புய லுந்தின","மறுகும்படி அண்டம் இலங்கிட","வளர்கின்றப ரங்கிரி வந்தருள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet, sick with love, says that Kama's arrows, the burning moon, women's eyes, the south wind from the grove and the cuckoo singing night and day have kept his eyes from sleep, and asks whether a deceiver like him, wasted by infatuation, can now reach Murugan's blossoming feet. The praise names Vishnu who ate curd in the cowherds' homes and walked behind those who study Tamil, and Murugan as his nephew, at Parangiri where moon, sun and cloud move.","n":"The love-sickness is told with the stock machinery of Tamil love poetry (moon, south wind, cuckoo, sleepless eyes), but its object is women in general and the speaker calls himself a deceiver (வஞ்சகன்). வண்டமிழ் பயில்வோர் பின் திரிகின்றவன் is Vishnu following a Tamil poet.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","kaamam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:4"},"p:15":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":15,"t":["தடக்கைப் பங்கயம் கொடைக்குக் கொண்டல்தண்","டமிழ்க்குத் தஞ்சமென் ...... றுலகோரைத்","தவித்துச் சென்றிரந் துளத்திற் புண்படுந்","தளர்ச்சிப் பம்பரந் ...... தனையூசற்","கடத்தைத் துன்பமண் சடத்தைத் துஞ்சிடுங்","கலத்தைப் பஞ்சஇந் ...... த்ரியவாழ்வைக்","கணத்திற் சென்றிடந் திருத்தித் தண்டையங்","கழற்குத் தொண்டுகொண் ...... டருள்வாயே","படைக்கப் பங்கயன் துடைக்கச் சங்கரன்","புரக்கக் கஞ்சைமன் ...... பணியாகப்","பணித்துத் தம்பயந் தணித்துச் சந்ததம்","பரத்தைக் கொண்டிடுந் ...... தனிவேலா","குடக்குத் தென்பரம் பொருப்பிற் றங்குமங்","குலத்திற் கங்கைதன் ...... சிறியோனே","குறப்பொற் கொம்பைமுன் புனத்திற் செங்கரங்","குவித்துக் கும்பிடும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses that he has gone begging to people, praising them as lotus-handed, clouds in giving and the refuge of Tamil, his heart wounded and spinning like a top, and calls his body a swinging pot, a mass of suffering clay, a ship that sinks, a life of five senses; he asks Murugan to correct him in a moment and take him into service at his anklets. The praise names him the lone vel who set Brahma to create, Siva to destroy and Vishnu to protect, and ended the gods' fear, the son of Ganga at Parangundram, who folded his hands to the hunter girl in the millet field.","n":"The confession is of a poet's trade: praising patrons in Tamil for gifts (தண்டமிழ்க்குத் தஞ்சம்). The plea is to exchange that service for தொண்டு, service at the Lord's feet. The body images (ஊசல் கடம், துன்ப மண் சடம், துஞ்சிடும் கலம்) stress its frailty. The praise also reverses rank: Murugan folds his hands before Valli.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","yaakkai","thamizh","thondu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:4"},"p:16":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":16,"t":["பதித்தசெஞ் சந்தப் பொற்குட நித்தம்","பருந்துயர்ந் தண்டத் திற்றலை முட்டும்","பருப்பதந் தந்தச் செப்பவை ஒக்குந் ...... தனபாரம்","படப்புயங் கம்பற் கக்குக டுப்பண்","செருக்குவண் டம்பப் பிற்கயல் ஒக்கும்","பருத்தகண் கொண்டைக் கொக்குமி ருட்டென் ...... றிளைஞோர்கள்","துதித்துமுன் கும்பிட் டுற்றது ரைத்தன்","புவக்கநெஞ் சஞ்சச் சிற்றிடை சுற்றுந்","துகிற்களைந் தின்பத் துர்க்கம் அளிக்கும் ...... கொடியார்பால்","துவக்குணும் பங்கப் பித்தன வத்தன்","புவிக்குளென் சிந்தைப் புத்திம யக்கந்","துறக்கநின் தண்டைப் பத்மமெ னக்கென் ...... றருள்வாயே","குதித்துவெண் சங்கத் தைச்சுற வெற்றுங்","கடற்கரந் தஞ்சிப் புக்கஅ ரக்கன்","குடற்சரிந் தெஞ்சக் குத்திவி திர்க்குங் ...... கதிர்வேலா","குலக்கரும் பின்சொற் றத்தையி பப்பெண்","தனக்குவஞ் சஞ்சொற் பொச்சையி டைக்குங்","குகுக்குகுங் குங்குக் குக்குகு குக்குங் ...... குகுகூகூ","திதித்திதிந் திந்தித் தித்தியெ னக்கொம்","பதிர்த்துவெண் சண்டக் கட்கம்வி திர்த்துந்","திரட்குவிந் தங்கட் பொட்டெழ வெட்டுங் ...... கொலைவேடர்","தினைப்புனஞ் சென்றிச் சித்தபெ ணைக்கண்","டுருக்கரந் தங்குக் கிட்டிய ணைத்தொண்","திருப்பரங் குன்றிற் புக்குளி ருக்கும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes the breasts, eyes and dark hair of women whom young men worship and confide in, women who undress and give the stronghold of pleasure, and calls himself a fool and madman caught by them; he asks for the lotus of Murugan's anklets to end the delusion of his mind. The praise names the vel that tore out the guts of the demon who hid in fear in the sea, the hunters' din in the millet field, and Murugan's embrace of the girl there, at Thiruparankundram.","n":"இளைஞோர்கள் துதித்து முன் கும்பிட்டு: the young men worship the women, a reversal of worship that the plea sets right. The hunters' drum and horn syllables (குகுக்குகு, திதித்தி) fill two lines.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","pedhaimai","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:4"},"p:17":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":17,"t":["பொருப்புறுங் கொங்கையர் பொருட்கவர்ந் தொன்றிய","பிணக்கிடுஞ் சண்டிகள் ...... வஞ்சமாதர்","புயற்குழன் றங்கமழ் அறற்குலந் தங்கவிர்","முருக்குவண் செந்துவர் ...... தந்துபோகும்","அருத்திடுஞ் சிங்கியர் தருக்கிடுஞ் செங்கயல்","அறச்சிவந் தங்கையில் ...... அன்புமேவும்","அவர்க்குழன் றங்கமும் அறத்தளர்ந் தென்பயன்","அருட்பதம் பங்கயம் ...... அன்புறாதோ","மிருத்தணும் பங்கயன் அலர்க்கணன் சங்கரர்","விதித்தெணுங் கும்பிடு ...... கந்தவேளே","மிகுத்திடும் வன்சம ணரைப்பெருந் திண்கழு","மிசைக்கிடுஞ் செந்தமிழ் ...... அங்கவாயா","பெருக்குதண் சண்பக வனத்திடங் கொங்கொடு","திறற்செழுஞ் சந்தகில் ...... துன்றிநீடு","தினைப்புனம் பைங்கொடி தனத்துடன் சென்றணை","திருப்பரங் குன்றுறை ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of deceitful courtesans with mountainous breasts who take a man's wealth, quarrel and leave, and asks what he gains by wearing out his body after them, and whether Murugan's lotus feet will not love him. The praise names him Kandavel whom Brahma, Vishnu and Siva worship, the one of the Tamil mouth who set the hard Jains on great stakes, and the one who went to the hunter girl in the millet field, at Thiruparankundram.","n":"பொருட்கவர்ந்து, taking wealth, marks them as courtesans. வன்சமணரைப் பெருந்திண்கழு மிசைக்கிடும் செந்தமிழ் அங்கவாயா identifies Murugan with the Tamil saint who defeated the Jains at Madurai (Sambandar, by tradition), a claim the book repeats.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","selvam","thamizh","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:4"},"p:18":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":18,"t":["மன்றலங் கொந்துமிசை தெந்தனத் தெந்தனென","வண்டினங் கண்டுதொடர் ...... குழல்மாதர்","மண்டிடுந் தொண்டையமு துண்டுகொண் டன்புமிக","வம்பிடுங் கும்பகன ...... தனமார்பில்","ஒன்றஅம் பொன்றுவிழி கன்றஅங் கங்குழைய","உந்தியென் கின்றமடு ...... விழுவேனை","உன்சிலம் புங்கனக தண்டையுங் கிண்கிணியும்","ஒண்டகம் பும்புனையும் ...... அடிசேராய்","பன்றியங் கொம்புகம டம்புயங் கஞ்சுரர்கள்","பண்டையென் பங்கமணி ...... பவர்சேயே","பஞ்சரங் கொஞ்சுகிளி வந்துவந் தைந்துகர","பண்டிதன் தம்பியெனும் ...... வயலு஡ரா","சென்றுமுன் குன்றவர்கள் தந்தபெண் கொண்டுவளர்","செண்பகம் பைம்பொன்மலர் ...... செறிசோலை","திங்களுஞ் செங்கதிரு மங்குலுந் தங்குமுயர்","தென்பரங் குன்றிலுறை ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of women whose hair the bees follow and whose lips and breasts draw him until he falls into the pool of the navel, and asks to be joined to Murugan's feet with their anklets and bells. The praise names him the son of Siva who wears the boar's tusk, the tortoise shell, serpents and the gods' bones, the younger brother of the five-handed scholar, the lord of Vayalur who took the girl the hill people gave, at south Parangundram.","n":"உந்தி என்கின்ற மடு, the pool called the navel, is the image of drowning. One word is corrupt; வயலூரா is clear from context.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kaamam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:4"},"p:19":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":19,"t":["வடத்தை மிஞ்சிய புளகித வனமுலை","தனைத்த றந்தெதிர் வருமிளை ஞர்களுயிர்","மயக்கி ஐங்கணை மதனனை ஒருஅரு ...... மையினாலே","வருத்தி வஞ்சக நினைவொடு மெலமெல","நகைத்து நண்பொடு வருமிரும் எனஉரை","வழுத்தி அங்கவ ரொடுசரு வியுமுடல் ...... தொடுபோதே","விடத்தை வென்றிடு படைவிழி கொடுமுள","மருட்டி வண்பொருள் கவர்பொழு தினில்மயல்","விருப்பெ னும்படி மடிமிசை யினில்விழு ...... தொழில்தானே","விளைத்தி டும்பல கணிகையர் தமதுபொய்","மனத்தை நம்பிய சிறியனை வெறியனை","விரைப்ப தந்தனில் அருள்பெற நினைகுவ ...... துளதோதான்","குடத்தை வென்றிரு கிரியென எழில்தள","தளத்த கொங்கைகள் மணிவடம் அணிசிறு","குறக்க ரும்பின்மெய் துவள்புயன் எனவரு ...... வடிவேலா","குரைக்க ருங்கடல் திருவணை எனமுனம்","அடைத்தி லங்கையின் அதிபதி நிசிசரர்","குலத்தொ டும்பட ஒருகணை விடுமரி ...... மருகோனே","திடத்தெ திர்ந்திடும் அசுரர்கள் பொடிபட","அயிற்கொ டும்படை விடுசர வணபவ","திறற்கு கன்குரு பரனென வருமொரு ...... முருகோனே","செழித்த தண்டலை தொறுமில கியகுட","வளைக்கு லந்தரு தரளமு மிகுமுயர்","திருப்ப ரங்கிரி வளநகர் மருவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes courtesans who bewitch young men with breasts and smiles, invite them home, and while robbing them of wealth make them fall into their laps, and calls himself the small fool who trusted their false minds; he asks whether there is any thought of granting such a one grace. The praise names the vel, the hunter girl whom Murugan embraced, Rama who bridged the sea and slew Lanka's demons with one arrow as his uncle, and Murugan as Saravanabhava and guru, at Parangiri.","n":"கணிகையர் தமதுபொய் மனத்தை நம்பிய: the fault is trusting, and the women's deceit and greed (வண்பொருள் கவர்பொழுதினில்) are the stock charge of the Kural and Naladiyar chapters on courtesans.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","selvam","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:4"},"p:20":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":20,"t":["வரைத்தடங் கொங்கை யாலும்","வளைப்படுஞ் செங்கை யாலும்","மதர்த்திடுங் கெண்டையாலும் ...... அனைவோரும்","வடுப்படுந் தொண்டை யாலும்","விரைத்திடுங் கொண்டை யாலும்","மருட்டிடுஞ் சிந்தை மாதர் ...... வசமாகி","எரிப்படும் பஞ்சு போல","மிகக்கெடுந் தொண்ட னேனும்","இனற்படுந் தொந்த வாரி ...... கரையேற","இசைத்திடுஞ் சந்த பேதம்","ஒலித்திடுந் தண்டை சூழும்","இணைப்பதம் புண்ட ஡ணகம் ...... அருள்வாயே","சுரர்க்குவஞ் சஞ்செய் சூரன்","இளக்ரவுஞ் சந்த னோடு","துளக்கெழுந் தண்ட கோளம் ...... அளவாகத்","துரத்தியன் றிந்த்ர லோகம்","அழித்தவன் பொன்று மாறு","சுடப்பருஞ் சண்ட வேலை ...... விடுவோனே","செருக்கெழுந் தும்பர் சேனை","துளக்கவென் றண்ட மூடு","தெழித்திடுஞ் சங்க பாணி ...... மருகோனே","தினைப்புனஞ் சென்று லாவு","குறத்தியின் பம்ப ராவு","திருப்பரங் குன்ற மேவு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says that through women's breasts, bangled hands, eyes, lips and hair he has fallen into their power and is perishing like cotton in fire, and asks for Murugan's lotus feet with tinkling anklets so that he may cross the sea of bonds. The praise names the vel thrown at Sura, who harmed the gods and destroyed Indra's world, and at Krauncha, Vishnu with the conch as his uncle, and the hunter girl, at Thiruparankundram.","n":"தொந்த வாரி, the sea of bonds, and கரையேற, to reach the shore: the feet are a crossing. One word is corrupt; புண்டரீகம் (lotus) is clear from context.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kaamam","paasam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:4"},"p:21":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":21,"t":["அங்கை மென்குழ லாய்வார் போலே","சந்தி நின்றய லோடே போவா","ரன்பு கொண்டிட நீரோ போறீ ...... ரறியீரோ","அன்று வந்தொரு நாள்நீர் போனீர்","பின்பு கண்டறி யோநா மீதே","அன்று மின்றுமொர் போதோ போகா ...... துயில்வாரா","எங்க ளந்தரம் வேறா ரோர்வார்","பண்டு தந்தது போதா தோமே","லின்று தந்துற வோதா னீதே ...... னிதுபோதா","திங்கு நின்றதென் வீடே வா஡ண","ரென்றி ணங்கிகள் மாயா லீலா","இன்ப சிங்கியில் வீணே வீழா ...... தருள்வாயே","மங்கு லின்புறு வானாய் வானு஡","டன்ற ரும்பிய காலாய் நீள்கால்","மண்டு றும்பகை நீறா வீறா ...... எரிதீயாய்","வந்தி ரைந்தெழு நீராய் நீர்சூழ்","அம்ப ரம்புனை பாராய் பாரேழ்","மண்ட லம்புகழ் நீயாய் நானாய் ...... மலரோனாய்","உங்கள் சங்கரர் தாமாய் நாமார்","அண்ட பந்திகள் தாமாய் வானாய்","ஒன்றி னுங்கடை தோயா மாயோன் ...... மருகோனே","ஒண்த டம்பொழில் நநடுர் கேர்டுர்","செந்தி லம்பதி வாழ்வே வாழ்வோர்","உண்ட நெஞ்சறி தேனே வானோர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet gives the street talk of courtesans who stand at the crossroads pretending to fix their hair: 'Going without love? You came once and never again; what you gave before isn't enough, give today; come to my house', and asks not to fall in vain into these deceitful women's play. The praise names Murugan the nephew of Mayon, who became sky, wind, fire, water, earth, the seven worlds, you, I, Brahma, your Sankara and the gods, yet touches none of them, and the honey known to the hearts of those who live at Senthil.","n":"The first half is reported speech, an unusual dramatic device: the courtesans' words are themselves the confession. In the praise, ஒன்றினும் கடை தோயா மாயோன, Vishnu who becomes everything without being touched by anything, is the pervasion of the Lord, here given to Vishnu as Murugan's uncle. Two words in the second half are garbled.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","viyaabagam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:5"},"p:22":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":22,"t":["அந்தகன் வருந்தினம் பிறகிடச்","சந்ததமும் வந்துகண் டரிவையர்க்","கன்புருகு சங்கதந் தவிரமுக் ...... குணமாள","அந்திபக லென்றிரண் டையுமொழித்","திந்திரிய சஞ்சலங் களையறுத்","தம்புய பதங்களின் பெருமையைக் ...... கவிபாடிச்","செந்திலை யுணர்ந்துணர்ந் துணர்வுறக்","கந்தனை யறிந்தறிந் தறிவினிற்","சென்றுசெரு குந்தடந் தெளிதரத் ...... தணியாத","சிந்தையு மவிழ்ந்தழிந் துரையொழித்","தென்செய லழிந்தழிந் தழியமெய்ச்","சிந்தைவர என்றுநின் தெரிசனைப் ...... படுவேனோ","கொந்தவிழ் சரண்சரண் சரணெனக்","கும்பிடு புரந்தரன் பதிபெறக்","குஞ்சரி குயம்புயம் பெறஅரக் ...... கருமாளக்","குன்றிடிய அம்பொனின் திருவரைக்","கிண்கிணி கிணின்கிணின் கிணினெனக்","குண்டல மசைந்திளங் குழைகளிற் ...... ப்ரபைவீசத்","தந்தன தனந்தனந் தனவெனச்","செஞ்சிறு சதங்கைகொஞ் சிடமணித்","தண்டைகள் கலின்கலின் கலினெனத் ...... திருவான","சங்கரி மனங்குழைந் துருகமுத்","தந்தர வருஞ்செழுந் தளர்நடைச்","சந்ததி சகந்தொழுஞ் சரவணப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks when, with Death turned back and the craving for women gone, the three gunas ended, day and night left behind and the senses' agitation cut, singing poems on the lotus feet and knowing Senthil and Kandan ever deeper, his mind will melt and dissolve, his speech end and his own action perish, so that he gains the vision of Murugan. The praise shows Indra crying 'refuge' and regaining his city, Devayanai gaining Murugan's arms, the demons dying, and the child Murugan toddling with bells and anklets ringing until Sankari's heart melts.","n":"The plea moves by repeated doubled words (உணர்ந்துணர்ந்து, அறிந்தறிந்து, அழிந்தழிந்து), as if enacting the deepening it asks for. உரையொழித்து என் செயல் அழிந்து: the end of speech and of one's own agency is the goal, not escape from women alone. The praise ends with Murugan as a toddler (தளர்நடைச் சந்ததி), a tender picture rare in the battle-heavy praise.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mukkunam","mounam","gnaanam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:5"},"p:23":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":23,"t":["அமுதுததி விடமுமிழு செங்கட் டிங்கட்","பகவினொளிர் வெளிறெயிறு துஞ்சற் குஞ்சித்","தலையுமுடை யவனரவ தண்டச் சண்டச் ...... சமனோலை","அதுவருகு மளவிலுயி ரங்கிட் டிங்குப்","பறைதிமிலை திமிர்தமிகு தம்பட் டம்பற்","கரையவுற வினரலற உந்திச் சந்தித் ...... தெருவூடே","எமதுபொரு ளெனுமருளை யின்றிக் குன்றிப்","பிளவளவு தினையளவு பங்கிட் டுண்கைக்","கிளையுமுது வசைதவிர இன்றைக் கன்றைக் ...... கெனநாடா","திடுக்கடி தெனுமுணர்வு பொன்றிக் கொண்டிட்","டுடுடுடுடு டுடுடுடுடு டுண்டுட் டுண்டுட்","டெனவகலு நெறிகருதி நெஞ்சத் தஞ்சிப் ...... பகிராதோ","குமுதபதி வகிரமுது சிந்தச் சிந்தச்","சரணபரி புரசுருதி கொஞ்சக் கொஞ்சக்","குடிலசடை பவுரிகொடு தொங்கப் பங்கிற் ...... கொடியாடக்","குலதடினி அசையஇசை பொங்கப் பொங்கக்","கழலதிர டெகுடெகுட டெங்கட் டெங்கத்","தொகுகுகுகு தொகுகுகுகு தொங்கத் தொங்கத் ...... தொகுதீதோ","திமிதமென முழவொலிமு ழங்கச் செங்கைத்","தமருகம ததிர்சதியொ டன்பர்க் கின்பத்","திறமுதவு பரதகுரு வந்திக் குஞ்சற் ...... குருநாதா","திரளுமணி தரளமுயர் தெங்கிற் றங்கிப்","புரளஎறி திரைமகர சங்கத் துங்கத்","திமிரசல நிதிதழுவு செந்திற் கந்தப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"When the palm-leaf summons of Death, with his fanged and serpent-crowned retinue, arrives, the life faints, drums beat, kin wail, and the body is pushed through the streets; the poet asks whether his heart will not take fright at that departing path and, without the delusion 'this is ours', share even half a kunri seed or a grain of millet, instead of saying 'today, that day'. The praise shows Siva dancing with Uma, the crescent dripping nectar, the matted hair whirling and the drum sounding, and names Murugan the true guru whom that master of dance worships, at Senthil by the sea.","n":"The remedy the plea draws from death is giving, not devotion: குன்றிப் பிளவளவு தினையளவு பங்கிட்டு உண்கை, to eat sharing even a split kunri seed or a millet grain. சமனோலை is Yama's written summons. In the praise Siva the dancer (பரதகுரு) worships Murugan the true guru (சற்குருநாதா).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kootru","eegai","nilaiyaamai","thirukkoothu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:5"},"p:24":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":24,"t":["அம்பொத் தவிழித் தந்தக் கலகத்","தஞ்சிக் கமலக் ...... கணையாலே","அன்றிற் குமனற் றென்றற் குமிளைத்","தந்திப் பொழுதிற் ...... பிறையாலே","எம்பொற் கொடிமற் றுன்பக் கலனற்","றின்பக் கலவித் ...... துயரானாள்","என்பெற் றுலகிற் பெண்பெற் றவருக்","கின்பப் புலியுற் ...... றிடலாமோ","கொம்புக் கரிபட் டஞ்சப் பதுமக்","கொங்கைக் குறவிக் ...... கினியோனே","கொன்றைச் சடையற் கொன்றைத் தெரியக்","கொஞ்சித் தமிழைப் ...... பகர்வோனே","செம்பொற் சிகரப் பைம்பொற் கிரியைச்","சிந்தக் கறுவிப் ...... பொரும்வேலா","செஞ்சொற் புலவர்க் கன்புற் றதிருச்","செந்திற் குமரப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"In the voice of a mother, the singer laments that her golden girl, wounded by Kama's lotus arrow, by the anril bird, the south wind and the crescent at evening, has given up all other joy and suffers from love; she asks whether those who bear daughters in this world should meet such a tiger of pleasure. The praise names Murugan the sweet one of the hunter girl, the one who prattled Tamil to explain the one thing to Siva of the konrai-hair, the vel that struck the golden peak, and the lord at Senthil dear to poets of fine speech.","n":"This is akam poetry, a foster-mother's or mother's lament for a girl in love with the god, and there is no confession of the poet's own. The machinery is classical: the paired anril bird, the south wind, the crescent at dusk. கொன்றைச் சடையற்கு ஒன்றைத் தெரியக் கொஞ்சித் தமிழைப் பகர்வோனே: Murugan explains 'the one' to Siva in Tamil, again the teaching of 6 and 12.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","maalai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:5"},"p:25":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":25,"t":["அருணமணி மேவு பூஷித ம்ருகமத படீர லேபன","அபிநவ விசால பூரண","அம்பொற் கும்பத் ...... தனமோதி","அளிகுலவு மாதர் லீலையின் முழுகியபி ஷேக மீதென","அறவுமுற வாடி நீடிய","அங்கைக் கொங்கைக் ...... கிதமாகி","இருணிறைய மோதி மாலிகை சருவி யுறவான வேளையி","லிழைகலைய மாத ரார்வழி","யின்புற் றன்புற் ...... றழியாநீள்","இரவுபகல் மோக னாகியெ படியில்மடி யாமல் யானுமுன்","இணையடிகள் பாடி வாழஎ","னெஞ்சிற் செஞ்சொற் ...... றருவாயே","தருணமணி யாட ராவணி குடிலசடி லாதி யோதிய","சதுர்மறையி னாதி யாகிய","சங்கத் துங்கக் ...... குழையாளர்","தருமுருக மேக சாயலர் தமரமக ராழி சூழ்புவி","தனைமுழுதும் வாரி யேயமு","துண்டிட் டண்டர்க் ...... கருள்கூரும்","செருமுதலி மேவு மாவலி யதிமதக போல மாமலை","தெளிவினுடன் மூல மேயென","முந்தச் சிந்தித் ...... தருள்மாயன்","திருமருக சூரன் மார்பொடு சிலையுருவ வேலை யேவிய","ஜெயசரவ ணாம னோகர","செந்திற் கந்தப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes the bejewelled, musk-scented women in whose play he bathed as if in ritual, their hair loosened and ornaments slipping in the dark, and asks that, no longer deluded night and day, he be given fine words in his heart to sing Murugan's paired feet and live. The praise names Siva of the serpent and matted hair, source of the four Vedas, and Vishnu, the cloud-hued one who answered the elephant that first thought of him crying 'Root!', and calls Murugan the victorious Saravana whose vel pierced Sura's chest and the hill, at Senthil.","n":"அபிஷேக மீதென: the poet calls his bathing in women's play an abhisheka, a sacred bath, an irony. The plea is for the gift of song (செஞ்சொல்), as in 2. The elephant crying மூலமே is the story of Gajendra. The lines on Vishnu gathering up the sea-girt earth and sharing nectar with the gods are hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","pugazh","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:5"},"p:26":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":26,"t":["அவனிபெ றுந்தோட் டம்பொற்","குழையட ரம்பாற் புண்பட்","டரிவையர் தம்பாற் கொங்கைக் ...... கிடையேசென்","றணைதரு பண்டாட் டங்கற்","றுருகிய கொண்டாட் டம்பெற்","றழிதரு திண்டாட் டஞ்சற் ...... றொழியாதே","பவமற நெஞ்சாற் சிந்தித்","திலகுக டம்பார்த் தண்டைப்","பதயுக ளம்போற் றுங்கொற் ...... றமுநாளும்","பதறிய அங்காப் பும்பத்","தியுமறி வும்போய்ச் சங்கைப்","படுதுயர் கண்பார்த் தன்புற் ...... றருளாயோ","தவநெறி குன்றாப் பண்பிற்","றுறவின ருந்தோற் றஞ்சத்","தனிமல ரஞ்சார்ப் புங்கத் ...... தமராடி","தமிழினி தென்காற் கன்றிற்","றிரிதரு கஞ்சாக் கன்றைத்","தழலெழ வென்றார்க் கன்றற் ...... புதமாகச்","சிவவடி வங்காட் டுஞ்சற்","குருபர தென்பாற் சங்கத்","திரள்மணி சிந்தாச் சிந்துக் ...... கரைமோதும்","தினகர திண்டேர்ச் சண்டப்","பரியிட றுங்கோட் டிஞ்சித்","திருவளர் செந்தூர்க் கந்தப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks that he not end in the false play of embraces with women whose ear-ornaments wound like spears, and that, with birth ended, he think in his heart on the kadamba-adorned, anklet-bearing feet; he asks Murugan to look with love on the sorrow of one whose love and understanding have gone. The praise calls him the true guru who wondrously showed the form of Siva to the one who conquered with fire the Kama whose flower arrows frightened even ascetics, and the lord of Senthil where the sea casts conch-pearls and the sun's chariot stumbles.","n":"The praise appears to tell of Siva burning Kama (தழலெழ வென்றார்) and of Murugan showing Siva his own form (சிவவடிவம் காட்டும் சற்குருபர); the phrases in between (தென்காற் கன்றில் திரிதரு கஞ்சாக் கன்று) are obscure and the reading is tentative.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","piravi","thiruvadi","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:5"},"p:27":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":27,"t":["அளக பாரம லைந்துகு லைந்திட","வதனம் வேர்வுது லங்கிந லங்கிட","அவச மோகம் விளைந்துத ளைந்திட ...... அணைமீதே","அருண வாய்நகை சிந்திய சம்ப்ரம","அடர்ந காநுதி பங்கவி தஞ்செய்து","அதர பானம ருந்திம டுங்கிற ...... முலைமேல்வீழ்ந்","துளமும் வேறுப டும்படி ஒன்றிடு","மகளிர் தோதக இன்பின்மு யங்குதல்","ஒழியு மாறுதெ ளிந்துளம் அன்பொடு ...... சிவயோகத்","துருகு ஞானப ரம்பர தந்திர","அறிவி னோர்கரு தங்கொள்சி லம்பணி","உபய சீதள பங்கய மென்கழல் ...... தருவாயே","இளகி டாவளர் சந்தன குங்கும","களப பூரண கொங்கைந லம்புனை","இரதி வேள்பணி தந்தையும் அந்தண ...... மறையோனும்","இனது றாதெதிர் இந்திரன் அண்டரும்","ஹரஹ ராசிவ சங்கர சங்கர","எனமி காவரு நஞ்சினை யுண்டவர் ...... அருள்பாலா","வளர்நி சாசரர் தங்கள்சி ரம்பொடி","படவி ரோதமி டுங்குல சம்ப்ரமன்","மகர வாரிக டைந்தநெ டும்புயல் ...... மருகோனே","வளரும் வாழையு மஞ்சளும் இஞ்சியும்","இடைவி டாதுநெ ருங்கிய மங்கல","மகிமை மாநகர் செந்திலில் வந்துறை ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes lovemaking with women, hair loosened, faces sweating, nails and lips, and asks that, seeing clearly how to leave that embrace, he be given the cool lotus feet with anklets that those of Sivayoga who melt in jnana contemplate. The praise names Siva, son-giver, who drank the poison when Brahma, Vishnu the father of Kama, Indra and the gods cried 'Hara Hara Siva Sankara', and Vishnu who churned the sea as Murugan's uncle, at Senthil, a town of plantain, turmeric and ginger.","n":"சிவயோகத்து உருகு ஞான பரம்பர தந்திர அறிவினோர்: the feet are those that yogis and jnanis hold in mind, so the plea sets the yoga of Siva against the embrace. இரதி வேள் பணி தந்தை is Vishnu, father of Kama (Rati's lord).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","punarchi","yogam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:5"},"p:28":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":28,"t":["அறிவழிய மயல்பெருக வுரையுமற விழிசுழல","அனலவிய மலமொழுக ...... அகலாதே","அனையுமனை யருகிலுற வெருவியழ வுறவுமழ","அழலினிகர் மறலியெனை ...... யழையாதே","செறியுமிரு வினைகரண மருவுபுல னொழியவுயர்","திருவடியி லணுகவர ...... மருள்வாயே","சிவனைநிகர் பொதியவரை முநிவனக மகிழஇரு","செவிகுளிர இனியதமிழ் ...... பகர்வோனே","நெறிதவறி யலரிமதி நடுவன்மக பதிமுளரி","நிருதிநிதி பதிகரிய ...... வனமாலி","நிலவுமறை யவனிவர்க ளலையஅர சுரிமைபுரி","நிருதனுர மறஅயிலை ...... விடுவோனே","மறிபரசு கரமிலகு பரமனுமை யிருவிழியு","மகிழமடி மிசைவளரு ...... மிளையோனே","மதலைதவ ழுததியிடை வருதரள மணிபுளின","மறையவுயர் கரையிலுறை ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks that Death, like fire, not call him while his mind fails, delusion grows, speech goes, eyes roll, the body's fire dies and its filth flows, and his mother and kin weep in fear by the bed; and that, with the two deeds, the instruments and the senses gone, he be given the boon of drawing near the high feet. The praise names Murugan the one who spoke sweet Tamil to delight the sage of Pothiyai, equal to Siva, the vel thrown at Nirudan whose rule harried the sun, moon, Yama, Indra, Agni, Nirruti, Kubera, Vishnu and Brahma, and the child who grew in the laps of Siva and Uma, at the shore of Senthil.","n":"The death is told from the body outward, eyes rolling and filth flowing, then to the weeping mother at the bedside. இருவினை கரணம் மருவுபுலன் ஒழிய names what must fall away. பொதியவரை முநிவன் is Agastya, whom Murugan taught Tamil.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","saakkaadu","vinai","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:5"},"p:29":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":29,"t":["அனிச்சங் கார்முகம் வீசிட மாசறு","துவட்பஞ் சானத டாகம்வி டாமட","அனத்தின் தூவிகு லாவிய சீறடி ...... மடமானார்","அருக்கன் போலொளி வீசிய மாமர","கதப்பைம் பூணணி வார்முலை மேல்முகம்","அழுத்தும் பாவியை யாவி யிடேறிட ...... நெறிபாரா","வினைச்சண் டாளனை வீணணை நீணிதி","தனைக்கண் டானவ மானநிர் மூடனை","விடக்கன் பாய்நுகர் பாழனை யோர்மொழி ...... பகராதே","விகற்பங் கூறிடு மோகவி காரனை","அறத்தின் பாலொழு காதமு தேவியை","விளித்துன் பாதுகை நீதர நானருள் ...... பெறுவேனோ","முனைச்சங் கோலிடு நீலம கோததி","அடைத்தஞ் சாதஇ ராவண னீள்பல","முடிக்கன் றோர்கணை யேவுமி ராகவன் ...... மருகோனே","முளைக்குஞ் சீதநி லாவொட ராவிரி","திரைக்கங் காநதி தாதகி கூவிள","முடிக்குஞ் சேகரர் பேரரு ளால்வரு ...... முருகோனே","தினைச்செங் கானக வேடுவ ரானவர்","திகைத்தந் தோவென வேகணி யாகிய","திறற்கந் தாவளி நாயகி காமுறும் ...... எழில்வேலா","சிறக்குந் தாமரை யோடையில் மேடையில்","நிறக்குஞ் சூல்வளை பால்மணி வீசிய","திருச்செந் தூர்வரு சேவக னேசுரர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of the tender-footed women with gem-set breasts and calls himself the sinner who presses his face to them, a wretch of deeds, an insulted fool who saw great wealth, a ruin who loves flesh, a deluded man who does not walk in dharma, and asks whether he will gain grace if Murugan calls him and gives him his sandals. The praise names Rama who bridged the sea and cut Ravana's heads with one arrow as his uncle, Siva who wears the moon, the serpent, Ganga and flowers as his father, and Murugan as Valli's lover, the champion at Thiruchendur.","n":"The heap of self-abuse (வினைச் சண்டாளன், வீணன், நிர்மூடன், பாழன், மோக விகாரன், மூதேவி) is a set feature of the book. உன் பாதுகை நீ தர, 'that you give your sandals', recalls Rama's sandals, given just before in the praise. விடக்கு அன்பாய் நுகர் may mean loving to eat flesh, or the flesh of the body enjoyed in lust.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kutram","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:5"},"p:30":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":30,"t":["அனைவரு மருண்ட ருண்டு கடிதென வெகுண்டி யம்ப","அமரஅடி பின்தொ டர்ந்து ...... பிணநாறும்","அழுகுபிணி கொண்டு விண்டு புழுவுட னெலும்ப லம்பு","மவலவுட லஞ்சு மந்து ...... தடுமாறி","மனைதொறு மிதம்ப கர்ந்து வரவர விருந்த ருந்தி","மனவழி திரிந்து மங்கும் ...... வசைதீர","மறைசதுர் விதந்தெ ரிந்து வகைசிறு சதங்கை கொஞ்ச","மலரடி வணங்க என்று ...... பெறுவேனோ","தினைமிசை சுகங்க டிந்த புனமயி லிளங்கு ரும்பை","திகழிரு தனம்பு ணர்ந்த ...... திருமார்பா","ஜெகமுழுது முன்பு தும்பி முகவனொடு தந்தை முன்பு","திகிரிவலம் வந்த செம்பொன் ...... மயில்வீரா","இனியகனி மந்தி சிந்து மலைகிழவ செந்தில் வந்த","இறைவகுக கந்த என்று ...... மிளையோனே","எழுகடலு மெண்சி லம்பும் நிசிசரரும் அஞ்ச அஞ்சு","மிமையவரை யஞ்ச லென்ற ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of carrying this wretched body, which stinks like a corpse, rots in disease, splits and rattles with worms and bones, while all who see it flee and Death follows behind, and of going from house to house speaking sweetly and eating whatever feast comes; he asks when, freed from that disgrace and knowing the four Vedas, he will bow at the flower feet with tinkling bells. The praise names the girl guarding the millet who chased away parrots, Murugan who circled the whole world on his golden peacock before the elephant-faced one and his father, and who told the frightened gods not to fear.","n":"பிணநாறும் அழுகு பிணி ... புழுவுடன் எலும்பு அலம்பும் அவல உடல்: the body is already a corpse while alive. ஜெகமுழுது ... திகிரி வலம் வந்த is the circling of the world in the contest for the fruit (see 3).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","kootru","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:5"},"p:31":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":31,"t":["இயலிசையி லுசித வஞ்சிக் ...... கயர்வாகி","இரவுபகல் மனது சிந்தித் ...... துழலாதே","உயர்கருணை புரியு மின்பக் ...... கடல்மூழ்கி","உனையெனது ளறியு மன்பைத் ...... தருவாயே","மயில்தகர்க லிடைய ரந்தத் ...... தினைகாவல்","வனசகுற மகளை வந்தித் ...... தணைவோனே","கயிலைமலை யனைய செந்திற் ...... பதிவாழ்வே","கரிமுகவ னிளைய கந்தப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A short song: the poet asks not to tire himself and wander thinking night and day over a fitting woman praised in poetry and song, but to bathe in the sea of bliss of Murugan's high compassion and be given the love that knows him within. The praise names Murugan who bowed to and embraced the hunter girl guarding the millet field, the life of Senthil that is like Kailasa, the younger brother of the elephant-faced one.","n":"உனை எனதுள் அறியும் அன்பு: the love asked for is one that knows the Lord inside the self. இயலிசையில் உசித வஞ்சி may also mean the deceit of poetry and song, so whether the first line speaks of a woman or of art is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anbu","thiruvarul","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:5"},"p:32":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":32,"t":["இருகுழை யெறிந்த கெண்டைகள் ஒருகுமி ழடர்ந்து வந்திட","இணைசிலைநெ ரிந்தெ ழுந்திட ...... அணைமீதே","இருளளக பந்தி வஞ்சியி லிருகலையு டன்கு லைந்திட","இதழமுத ருந்த சிங்கியின் ...... மனமாய","முருகொடுக லந்த சந்தனஅளருபடு குங்கு மங்கமழ்","முலைமுகடு கொண்டெ ழுந்தொறு ...... முருகார","முழுமதிபு ரிந்த சிந்துர அரிவையரு டன்க லந்திடு","முகடியுந லம்பி றந்திட ...... அருள்வாயே","எரிவிடநி மிர்ந்த குஞ்சியி னிலவொடு மெழுந்த கங்கையு","மிதழியொட ணிந்த சங்கரர் ...... களிகூரும்","இமவரைத ருங்க ருங்குயில் மரகதநி றந்த ருங்கிளி","யெனதுயிரெ னுந்த்ரி யம்பகி ...... பெருவாழ்வே","அரைவட மலம்பு கிண்கிணி பரிபுர நெருங்கு தண்டைக","ளணிமணிச தங்கை கொஞ்சிட ...... மயில்மேலே","அகமகிழ்வு கொண்டு சந்ததம் வருகுமர முன்றி லின்புறம்","அலைபொருத செந்தில் தங்கிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes lovemaking with women whose eyes dart to their earrings and whose breasts are scented with sandal and kumkum, and makes an obscure request about the ill-fortune (முகடி) of mingling with them. The praise names Murugan the great treasure of Uma, the dark cuckoo of the Himalaya and green parrot whom Sankara loves, and the child who comes on the peacock with jingling bells, at the sea-washed courtyard of Senthil.","n":"முகடியும் நலம் பிறந்திட அருள்வாயே: முகடி is the goddess of ill fortune (மூதேவி); the line may ask that even this ill fortune turn to good, or be divided otherwise. The praise centres on Uma (த்ரியம்பகி) more than on Murugan.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","punarchi","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:5"},"p:33":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":33,"t":["இருள்விரி குழலை விரித்துத் தூற்றவு","மிறுகிய துகிலை நெகிழ்த்துக் காட்டவு","மிருகடை விழியு முறுக்கிப் பார்க்கவு ...... மைந்தரோடே","இலைபிள வதனை நடித்துக் கேட்கவு","மறுமொழி பலவு மிசைத்துச் சாற்றவு","மிடையிடை சிறிது நகைத்துக் காட்டவு ...... மெங்கள்வீடே","வருகென வொருசொ லுரைத்துப் பூட்டவும்","விரிமல ரமளி யணைத்துச் சேர்க்கவும்","வருபொரு ளளவி லுருக்கித் தேற்றவு ...... நிந்தையாலே","வனைமனை புகுதி லடித்துப் போக்கவு","மொருதலை மருவு புணர்ச்சித் தூர்த்தர்கள்","வசைவிட நினது பதத்தைப் போற்றுவ ...... தெந்தநாளோ","குருமணி வயிர மிழித்துக் கோட்டிய","கழைமட வுருவு வெளுத்துத் தோற்றிய","குளிறிசை யருவி கொழித்துத் தூற்றிய ...... மண்டுநநருர்","குழிபடு கலுழி வயிற்றைத் தூர்த்தெழு","திடர்மண லிறுகு துருத்திக் காப்பொதி","குளிர்நிழ லருவி கலக்கிப் பூப்புனை ...... வண்டலாடா","முருகவிழ் துணர்க ளுகுத்துக் காய்த்தினை","விளைநடு விதணி லிருப்பைக் காட்டிய","முகிழ்முலை யிளைய குறத்திக் காட்படு ...... செந்தில்வாழ்வே","முளையிள மதியை யெடுத்துச் சாத்திய","சடைமுடி யிறைவர் தமக்குச் சாத்திர","முறையருள் முருக தவத்தைக் காப்பவர் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet lists the courtesans' arts, loosening their hair, slipping their garments, looking from the corners of their eyes, asking for betel, laughing, calling 'come to our house', taking a man to bed and melting him while his wealth lasts, then, when it ends, beating him and driving him out if he enters; he asks when he will praise Murugan's feet and be rid of the blame of these one-sided unions. The praise is a long picture of the mountain stream in Valli's millet field, and names Murugan the life of Senthil, who gave the shastras to Siva with the crescent in his hair, the lord of ascetics.","n":"ஒருதலை மருவு புணர்ச்சித் தூர்த்தர்கள்: the classical term for one-sided love is turned on the courtesan's bed, where only one side loves. வருபொருள் அளவில் ... வனைமனை புகுதில் அடித்துப் போக்கவும்: love lasting exactly as long as the money is the stock charge against courtesans. One word in the praise (மண்டுநநருர்) is garbled.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","selvam","orutalai","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:5"},"p:34":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":34,"t":["உததியறல் மொண்டு சூல்கொள்கரு","முகிலெனஇ ருண்ட நீலமிக","வொளிதிகழு மன்றல் ஓதிநரை ...... பஞ்சுபோலாய்","உதிரமெழு துங்க வேலவிழி","மிடைகடையொ துங்கு பீளைகளு","முடைதயிர்பி திர்ந்த தோஇதென ...... வெம்புலாலாய்","மதகரட தந்தி வாயினிடை","சொருகுபிறை தந்த சூதுகளின்","வடிவுதரு கும்ப மோதிவளர் ...... கொங்கைதோலாய்","வனமழியு மங்கை மாதர்களின்","நிலைதனையு ணர்ந்து தாளிலுறு","வழியடிமை யன்பு கூருமது ...... சிந்தியேனோ","இதழ்பொதிய விழ்ந்த தாமரையின்","மணவறைபு குந்த நான்முகனும்","எறிதிரைய லம்பு பாலுததி ...... நஞ்சராமேல்","இருவிழிது யின்ற நாரணனும்","உமைமருவு சந்த்ர சேகரனும்","இமையவர்வ ணங்கு வாசவனும் ...... நின்றுதாழும்","முதல்வசுக மைந்த பீடிகையில்","அகிலசக அண்ட நாயகிதன்","மகிழ்முலைசு ரந்த பாலமுத ...... முண்டவேளே","முளைமுருகு சங்கு வீசியலை","முடுகிமைத வழ்ந்த வாய்பெருகி","முதலிவரு செந்தில் வாழ்வுதரு ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet pictures the women once desired as they age: hair dark as a rain cloud turns grey like cotton, the spear-like eyes gather rheum like curdled sour curd and smell of flesh, the breasts that rivalled an elephant's temples become skin; he asks whether, understanding the state of women whose beauty perishes, he will not think of servanthood at Murugan's feet. The praise shows Brahma, Vishnu, Siva and Indra bowing to Murugan, who drank the milk-nectar from the breast of the Mother of all worlds, at Senthil where the conches roll in on the waves.","n":"The argument against desire is decay rather than deceit: each charm is matched to its ruin (ஓதி நரை பஞ்சுபோல், விழி பீளை, கொங்கை தோல்). The plea names the remedy as வழியடிமை, hereditary servitude to the feet.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","ilamai","yaakkai","thondu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:5"},"p:35":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":35,"t":["உருக்கம் பேசிய நீலியர் காசுகள்","பறிக்குந் தோஷிகள் மோகவி காரிகள்","உருட்டும் பார்வையர் மாபழி காரிகள் ...... மதியாதே","உரைக்கும் வீரிகள் கோளர வாமென","வுடற்றுந் தாதியர் காசள வேமனம்","உறைக்குந் தூரிகள் மீதினி லாசைகள் ...... புரிவேனோ","அருக்கன் போலொளி வீசிய மாமுடி","யணைத்துந் தானழ காய்நல மேதர","அருட்கண் பார்வையி னாலடி யார்தமை ...... மகிழ்வோடே","அழைத்துஞ் சேதிகள் பேசிய காரண","வடிப்பந் தானென வேயெனை நாடொறும்","அதிக்கஞ் சேர்தர வேயரு ளாலுட ...... னினிதாள்வாய்","இருக்குங் காரண மீறிய வேதமும்","இசைக்குஞ் சாரமு மேதொழு தேவர்கள்","இடுக்கண் தீர்கன னேயடி யார்தவ ...... முடன்மேவி","இலக்கந் தானென வேதொழ வேமகிழ்","விருப்பங் கூர்தரு மாதியு மாயுல","கிறுக்குந் தாதகி சூடிய வேணிய ...... னருள்பாலா","திருக்குந் தாபதர் வேதிய ராதியர்","துதிக்குந் தாளுடைய நாயக னாகிய","செகச்செஞ் சோதியு மாகிய மாதவன் ...... மருகோனே","செழிக்குஞ் சாலியு மேகம ளாவிய","கருப்பஞ் சோலையும் வாழையு மேதிகழ்","திருச்செந் தூர்தனில் மேவிய தேவர்கள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether he will set his desire on women who talk tenderly, snatch coins, rage with rolling eyes, carry great blame, wound like serpents and measure the heart by the coin; he asks Murugan to rule him sweetly each day by grace, as one who calls his devotees with gracious eyes and talks with them. The praise names Siva who wears the aththi flower as his father, Vishnu the red light of the world whom ascetics and Vedic priests praise as his uncle, and the lord at Thiruchendur of rice fields, cane groves and plantains.","n":"காசளவே மனம் உறைக்கும் தூரிகள்: their hearts are weighed exactly to the coin. The plea pictures the Lord's grace as intimacy, calling devotees and talking with them (அழைத்துஞ் சேதிகள் பேசிய).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","selvam","thiruvarul","adiyaar"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:5"},"p:36":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":36,"t":["ஏவினை நேர்விழி மாதரை மேவிய","ஏதனை மூடனை ...... நெறிபேணா","ஈனனை வீணனை ஏடெழு தாமுழு","ஏழையை மோழையை ...... அகலாநீள்","மாவினை மூடிய நோய்பிணி யாளனை","வாய்மையி லாதனை ...... யிகழாதே","மாமணி நூபுர சீதள தாள்தனி","வாழ்வுற ஈவது ...... மொருநாளே","நாவலர் பாடிய நூலிசை யால்வரு","நாரத னார்புகல் ...... குறமாதை","நாடியெ கானிடை கூடிய சேவக","நாயக மாமயி ...... லுடையோனே","தேவிம நோமணி ஆயிப ராபரை","தேன்மொழி யாள்தரு ...... சிறியோனே","சேணுயர் சோலையி னீழலி லேதிகழ்","சீரலை வாய்வரு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet heaps names on himself, one who joined women with arrow-like eyes, faulty, a fool who keeps to no path, base, wasted, wholly ignorant, dull, sick with diseases wrapped in great deeds, without truth, and asks Murugan not to scorn him but to give him one day his cool feet with jewelled anklets so that he may have a life. The praise names the leader on the peacock who sought out and joined in the forest the hunter girl of whom Narada spoke, the little son of the Devi, the supreme mother with honeyed speech, at Thiruchiralaivay (Senthil) under its high groves.","n":"The plea is almost entirely a list of accusative self-names (ஏதனை மூடனை ... ஈனனை வீணனை ... ஏழையை மோழையை ... வாய்மையிலாதனை), each one the object of இகழாதே, 'do not scorn'. ஏடெழுதா முழு ஏழை may mean one who cannot write a single leaf, i.e. unlettered. Narada (நாரதனார் புகல் குறமாதை) is named as the one who told of Valli.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kutram","pedhaimai","vinai","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:6"},"p:37":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":37,"t":["ஓரா தொன்றைப் பாரா தந்தத்","தோடே வந்திட் ...... டுயிர்சோர","ஊடா நன்றற் றார்போல் நின்றெட்","டாமால் தந்திட் ...... டுழல்மாதர்","கூரா வன்பிற் சோரா நின்றக்","கோயா நின்றுட் ...... குலையாதே","கோடார் செம்பொற் றோளா நின்சொற்","கோடா தென்கைக் ...... கருள்தாராய்","தோரா வென்றிப் போரா மன்றற்","றோளா குன்றைத் ...... தொளையாடீ","சூதா யெண்டிக் கேயா வஞ்சச்","சூர்மா அஞ்சப் ...... பொரும்வேலா","சீரார் கொன்றைத் தார்மார் பொன்றச்","சேவா றெந்தைக் ...... கினியோனே","தேனே யன்பர்க் கேயா மின்சொற்","சேயே செந்திற் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"In a run of clipped two-syllable words the poet speaks of wandering women who, without thinking or looking, come with their charms until life faints, sulk as if without goodness and give an unreachable delusion, and asks not to be shaken within by them but to be given grace without Murugan's word swerving. The praise names the unbeaten warrior who pierced the hill, the vel that made deceitful Sura flee to the eight directions, the one dear to the konrai-wearing father who rides the bull, and the sweet-spoken son easy to his lovers, at Senthil.","n":"எட்டாமால் தந்திட்டு உழல்மாதர்: the women give a delusion (மால்) that cannot be reached or ended. The plea நின்சொல் கோடாது என் கைக்கு அருள் தாராய் may ask that the Lord's promise not bend; the clipped rhythm makes most of the first half hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","mayal"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:6"},"p:38":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":38,"t":["கட்டழகு விட்டுத் தளர்ந்தங் கிருந்துமுனம்","இட்டபொறி தப்பிப் பிணங்கொண்ட தின்சிலர்கள்","கட்டணமெ டுத்துச் சுமந்தும் பெரும்பறைகள் ...... முறையோடே","வெட்டவிட வெட்டக் கிடஞ்சங் கிடஞ்சமென","மக்களொரு மிக்கத் தொடர்ந்தும் புரண்டும்வழி","விட்டுவரு மித்தைத் தவிர்ந்துன் பதங்களுற ...... வுணர்வேனோ","பட்டுருவி நெட்டைக் க்ரவுஞ்சம் பிளந்துகடல்","முற்றுமலை வற்றிக் குழம்புங் குழம்பமுனை","பட்டஅயில் தொட்டுத் திடங்கொண் டெதிர்ந்தவுணர் ...... முடிசாயத்","தட்டழிய வெட்டிக் கவந்தம் பெருங்கழுகு","நிர்த்தமிட ரத்தக் குளங்கண் டுமிழ்ந்துமணி","சற்சமய வித்தைப் பலன்கண்டு செந்திலுறை ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet pictures the end: beauty gone, the body slack, the senses fail and it becomes a corpse; some lift the bier, great drums beat in order, the children follow rolling on the ground, and then leave it on the road; he asks whether he will escape this false life and know how to reach Murugan's feet. The praise is a battle scene: Krauncha split, the sea dried, the demons' crowns falling, headless trunks and vultures dancing in pools of blood, and Murugan at Senthil who saw the fruit of the knowledge of the true faith.","n":"மித்தை is mithya, the false or unreal: the whole sequence of death and mourning is named as the illusion to escape. கிடஞ்சம் கிடஞ்சம் is the drum's own sound. சற்சமய வித்தைப் பலன் கண்டு, 'having seen the fruit of the learning of the true religion', may refer to Murugan's victory over other faiths.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saakkaadu","nilaiyaamai","yaakkai","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:6"},"p:39":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":39,"t":["கண்டுமொழி கொம்பு கொங்கை வஞ்சியிடை யம்பு நஞ்சு","கண்கள்குழல் கொண்டல் என்று ...... பலகாலும்","கண்டுவளம்வ ருந்தி நொந்து மங்கையர்வ சம்பு ரிந்து","கங்குல்பகல் என்று நின்று ...... விதியாலே","பண்டைவினை கொண்டு ழன்று வெந்துவிழு கின்றல் கண்டு","பங்கயப தங்கள் தந்து ...... புகழோதும்","பண்புடைய சிந்தை யன்பர் தங்களினு டன்க லந்து","பண்புபெற அஞ்ச லஞ்ச ...... லெனவாராய்","வண்டுபடு கின்ற தொங்கல் கொண்டறநெ ருங்கி யிண்டு","வம்பினைய டைந்து சந்தின் ...... மிக்முழ்கி","வஞ்சியை முனிந்த கொங்கை மென்குறம டந்தை செங்கை","வந்தழகு டன்க லந்த ...... மணிமார்பா","திண்டிறல்பு னைந்த அண்டர் தங்களப யங்கள் கண்டு","செஞ்சமர்பு னைந்து துங்க ...... மயில்மீதே","சென்றசுரர் அஞ்சவென்று குன்றிடை மணம்பு ணர்ந்து","செந்தில்நகர் வந்த மர்ந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses calling women's speech sugar-candy, their waists creepers and their eyes arrows and poison, and living night and day under their sway, burning in old deeds by fate; he asks Murugan, seeing him fall, to give his lotus feet and come saying 'fear not, fear not', so that he may join the loving devotees who sing his praise and gain their nature. The praise names the jewelled chest that the hunter girl embraced, the one who, seeing the gods' plea for refuge, rode the peacock to war, conquered the demons, married on the hill and came to Senthil.","n":"The remedy asked is company: பண்புடைய சிந்தை யன்பர் தங்களினுடன் கலந்து பண்புபெற, to mix with devotees and so take on their nature. விதியாலே பண்டைவினை கொண்டு names both fate and old deeds as the force behind his bondage. குன்றிடை மணம் புணர்ந்து may be the marriage to Devayanai on a hill.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adiyaar","oozh","kaamam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:6"},"p:40":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":40,"t":["கமல மாதுடன் இந்திரை யுஞ்சரி","சொலவொ ணாதம டந்தையர் சந்தன","களப சீதள கொங்கையில் அங்கையில் ...... இருபோதேய்","களவு நூல்தெரி வஞ்சனை அஞ்சன","விழியின் மோகித கந்தசு கந்தரு","கரிய ஓதியில் இந்துமு கந்தனில் ...... மருளாதே","அமல மாகிய சிந்தைய டைந்தகல்","தொலைவி லாதஅ றம்பொருள் இன்பமும்","அடைய ஓதியு ணர்ந்துத ணந்தபின் ...... அருள்தானே","அறியு மாறுபெ றும்படி அன்பினின்","இனிய நாதசி லம்புபு லம்பிடும்","அருண ஆடக கிண்கிணி தங்கிய ...... அடிதாராய்","குமரி காளிப யங்கரி சங்கரி","கவுரி நீலிப ரம்பரை அம்பிகை","குடிலை யோகினி சண்டினி குண்டலி ...... எமதாயி","குறைவி லாள்உமை மந்தரி அந்தரி","வெகுவி தாகம சுந்தரி தந்தருள்","குமர மூஷிக முந்திய ஐங்கர ...... கணராயன்","மமவி நாயகன் நஞ்சுமிழ் கஞ்சுகி","அணிக ஜானன விம்பனொர் அம்புலி","மவுலி யானுறு சிந்தையு கந்தருள் ...... இளையோனே","வளரும் வாழையு மஞ்சளும் இஞ்சியும்","இடைவி டாது நெருங்கிய மங்கல","மகிமை மாநகர் செந்திலில் வந்துறை ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks not to be deluded by women whom even Lakshmi cannot equal, with sandal on their breasts, eyes skilled in stealth and fragrant dark hair, but to reach a pure mind, learn and understand virtue, wealth and pleasure fully, and having left them, know grace itself; for this he asks the feet with ringing anklets. The praise is a long litany of the Goddess (Kumari, Kali, Sankari, Gauri, Ambika, Kundali, Uma) and of Ganapati with the mouse, and names Murugan as the younger brother whom the moon-crowned Siva delights in, at Senthil of plantain, turmeric and ginger.","n":"அறம் பொருள் இன்பமும் அடைய ஓதி உணர்ந்து தணந்தபின் அருள்தானே அறியுமாறு: the three aims of life are to be learnt fully and then left, and only then is grace known, an order that sets the Lord's grace above and after the three. The Goddess's names include குடிலை and குண்டலி, Tantric names of her power.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","thiruvarul","saththi","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:6"},"p:41":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":41,"t":["கரிக்கொம்பந் தனித்தங்கங்","குடத்தின்பந் தனத்தின்கண்","கறுப்புந்தன் சிவப்புஞ்செம் ...... பொறிதோள்சேர்","கணைக்கும்பண் டுழைக்கும்பங்","களிக்கும்பண் பொழிக்குங்கண்","கழுத்துஞ்சங் கொளிக்கும்பொன் ...... குழையாடச்","சரக்குஞ்சம் புடைக்கும்பொன்","றுகிற்றந்தந் தரிக்குந்தன்","சடத்தும்பண் பிலுக்குஞ்சம் ...... பளமாதர்","சலித்தும்பின் சிரித்துங்கொண்","டழைத்துஞ்சண் பசப்பும் பெண்","தனத்துன்பந் தவிப்புண்டிங் ...... குழல்வேனோ","சுரர்ச்சங்கந் துதித்தந்தஞ்","செழுத்தின்பங் களித்துண்பண்","சுகத்துய்ந்தின் பலர்ச்சிந்தங் ...... கசுராரைத்","துவைத்தும்பந் தடித்துஞ்சங்","கொலித்துங்குன் றிடித்தும்பண்","சுகித்துங்கண் களிப்புங்கொண் ...... டிடும்வேலா","சிரப்பண்புங் கரப்பண்புங்","கடப்பந்தொங் கலிற்பண்புஞ்","சிவப்பண்புந் தவப்பண்புந் ...... தருவோனே","தினைத்தொந்தங் குறப்பெண்பண்","சசிப்பெண்கொங் கையிற்றுஞ்சுஞ்","செழிக்குஞ்செந் திலிற்றங்கும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women with breasts like elephant tusks and golden pots, swinging earrings and conch-like throats, whom he calls women for hire, who tire, then laugh, call a man and feign longing, and asks whether he must wander here suffering for their breasts. The praise names the vel that crushed the demons and broke the hill while the gods praised, the one who gives his devotees the virtue of the bowed head, the joined hands, the kadamba garland, Siva and austerity, and the lord at Senthil who sleeps on the breasts of the hunter girl and of Indra's daughter.","n":"சம்பளமாதர், 'women of wages', names the courtesans as paid, and சண் பசப்பும் is their feigned pallor of love. சிரப்பண்பும் கரப்பண்பும் ... சிவப்பண்பும் தவப்பண்பும் தருவோனே: Murugan gives the marks of worship of head and hands as well as inner qualities. The chiming short-syllable rhythm makes many divisions uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","thavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:6"},"p:42":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":42,"t":["கருப்பந்தங் கிரத்தம்பொங்","கரைப்புண்கொண் டுருக்கும்பெண்","களைக்கண்டங் கவர்ப்பின்சென் ...... றவரோடே","கலப்புண்டுஞ் சிலுப்புண்டுந்","துவக்குண்டும் பிணக்குண்டுங்","கலிப்புண்டுஞ் சலிப்புண்டுந் ...... தடுமாறிச்","செருத்தண்டந் தரித்தண்டம்","புகத்தண்டந் தகற்கென்றுந்","திகைத்தந்திண் செகத்தஞ்சுங் ...... கொடுமாயும்","தியக்கங்கண் டுயக்கொண்டென்","பிறப்பங்கஞ் சிறைப்பங்கஞ்","சிதைத்துன்றன் பதத்தின்பந் ...... தருவாயே","அருக்கன்சஞ் சரிக்குந்தெண்","டிரைக்கண்சென் றரக்கன்பண்","பனைத்தும்பொன் றிடக்கன்றுங் ...... கதிர்வேலா","அணிச்சங்கங் கொழிக்குந்தண்","டலைப்பண்பெண் டிசைக்குங்கொந்","தளிக்குஞ்செந் திலிற்றங்குங் ...... குமரேசா","புரக்குஞ்சங் கரிக்குஞ்சங்","கரர்க்குஞ்சங் கரர்க்கின்பம்","புதுக்குங்கங் கையட்குந்தஞ் ...... சுதனானாய்","புனைக்குன்றந் திளைக்குஞ்செந்","தினைப்பைம்பொன் குறக்கொம்பின்","புறத்தண்கொங் கையிற்றுஞ்சும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of the womb-born body of blood and sores, melted by women whom he follows, mingles with, quarrels with and is tangled with, reeling, until Yama comes with his staff and the world takes fright and he dies; he asks Murugan to see his confusion, save him, break the prison of the body of birth, and give the joy of his feet. The praise names the bright vel that killed the demon in the clear sea where the sun moves, Kumaresa at Senthil where waves cast up conches, the son of Sankari, of Sankara and of Ganga, who sleeps on the hunter girl's breasts.","n":"பிறப்பங்கம் சிறைப்பங்கம் சிதைத்து: the body given by birth is a prison to be broken, not reformed. Ganga is named as one of his parents (கங்கையட்கும் சுதன்), from the story of Siva's sparks carried to the reed pool. The middle lines (செருத்தண்டம் தரித்தண்டம் ...) are hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","yaakkai","kaamam","kootru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:6"},"p:43":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":43,"t":["களபம் ஒழுகிய புளகித முலையினர்","கடுவும் அமிர்தமும் விரவிய விழியினர்","கழுவு சரிபுழு கொழுகிய குழலினர் ...... எவரோடுங்","கலகம் இடுகயல் எறிகுழை விரகியர்","பொருளில் இளைஞரை வழிகொடு மொழிகொடு","தளர விடுபவர் தெருவினில் எவரையும் ...... நகையாடிப்","பிளவு பெறிலதி லளவள வொழுகியர்","நடையில் உடையினில் அழகொடு திரிபவர்","பெருகு பொருள்பெறில் அமளியில் இதமொடு ...... குழைவோடே","பிணமும் அணைபவர் வெறிதரு புனலுணும்","அவச வனிதையர் முடுகொடும் அணைபவர்","பெருமை யுடையவர் உறவினை விடஅருள் ...... புரிவாயே","அளையில் உறைபுலி பெறுமக வயிறரு","பசுவின் நிரைமுலை யமுதுண நிரைமகள்","வசவ னொடுபுலி முலையுண மலையுடன் ...... உருகாநீள்","அடவி தனிலுள உலவைகள் தளிர்விட","மருள மதமொடு களிறுகள் பிடியுடன்","அகல வெளியுயர் பறவைகள் நிலம்வர ...... விரல்சேரேழ்","தொளைகள் விடுகழை விரல்முறை தடவிய","இசைகள் பலபல தொனிதரு கருமுகில்","சுருதியுடையவன் நெடியவன் மனமகிழ் ...... மருகோனே","துணைவ குணதர சரவண பவநம","முருக குருபர வளரறு முககுக","துறையில் அலையெறி திருநகர் உறைதரு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes the courtesans: breasts flowing with sandal, eyes of poison mixed with nectar, quarrelsome with anyone, leading moneyless youths astray and leaving them spent, mocking all in the street, giving love by exactly the measure of the money, melting in bed for great wealth, ready to embrace even a corpse, drunk on liquor; he asks for grace to leave all ties with them. The praise is a picture of Krishna's flute, at whose music the tiger cub drinks the cow's milk and the calf the tigress's, mountains melt, dry trees sprout and birds come down, and names Murugan the nephew who delights that cloud-dark one, at the shore of Senthil.","n":"பிளவு பெறில் அதில் அளவளவு ஒழுகியர்: given a scrap, they give just that much. பிணமும் அணைபவர், 'they would embrace even a corpse', turns the courtesan's indifference into an image of death. The Krishna passage, with its reversal of nature (கழை = flute, ஏழ் தொளை = seven holes), is the longest single myth in these songs and is given to Vishnu, not Murugan.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","selvam","kal","vanjam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:6"},"p:44":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":44,"t":["கனங்கள் கொண்ட குந்த ளங்க ளுங்கு லைந்த லைந்து விஞ்சு","கண்க ளுஞ்சி வந்த யர்ந்து ...... களிகூரக்","கரங்க ளுங்கு விந்து நெஞ்ச கங்க ளுங்க சிந்தி டுங்க","றங்கு பெண்க ளும்பி றந்து ...... விலைகூறிப்","பொனின்கு டங்க ளஞ்சு மென்த னங்க ளும்பு யங்க ளும்பொ","ருந்தி யன்பு நண்பு பண்பு ...... முடனாகப்","புணர்ந்து டன்பு லர்ந்து பின்க லந்த கங்கு ழைந்த வம்பு","ரிந்து சந்த தந்தி ரிந்து ...... படுவேனோ","அனங்க னெந்து நைந்து வெந்து குந்து சிந்த அன்று கண்தி","றந்தி ருண்ட கண்டர் தந்த ...... அயில்வேலா","அடர்ந்த டர்ந்தெ திர்ந்து வந்த வஞ்ச ரஞ்ச வெஞ்ச மம்பு","ரிந்த அன்ப ரின்ப நண்ப ...... உரவோனே","சினங்கள் கொண்டி லங்கை மன்சி ரங்கள் சித்த வெஞ்ச ரத்தெ","ரிந்த வன்ப ரிந்த இன்ப ...... மருகோனே","சிவந்த செஞ்ச தங்கை யுஞ்சி லம்பு தண்டை யும்பு னைந்து","செந்தில் வந்த கந்த எங்கள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women born to name their price, hair loosened, eyes reddened, hands joined, hearts melting, joining with seeming love, friendship and kindness, uniting, tiring and mingling again, and asks whether he must forever suffer wandering so. The praise names the vel given by the dark-throated Siva who opened his eye and burnt Kama, Murugan the friend of devotees who fought the deceivers, the nephew of Rama whose arrow burnt the heads of Lanka's king, and Kandha who came to Senthil in red anklets.","n":"பிறந்து விலைகூறி: the women are 'born to quote a price', and the love, friendship and kindness (அன்பு நண்பு பண்பு) they show are part of the sale. The praise sets Siva burning Kama (அனங்கன்) beside the plea against lust.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","punarchi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:6"},"p:45":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":45,"t":["கன்றிலுறு மானை வென்றவிழி யாலே","கஞ்சமுகை மேவு ...... முலையநலே","கங்குல்செறி கேச மங்குல்குலை யாமை","கந்தமலர் சூடு ...... மதனாலே","நன்றுபொருள் தீர வென்றுவிலை பேசி","நம்பவிடு மாத ...... ருடனாடி","நஞ்சுபுசி தேரை யங்கமது வாக","நைந்துவிடு வேனை ...... யருள்பாராய்","குன்றிமணி போல்வ செங்கண்வரி போகி","கொண்டபடம் வீசு ...... மணிகூர்வாய்","கொண்ட மயிலேறி அன்றசுரர் சேனை","கொன்றகும ரேச ...... குருநாதா","மன்றல்கமழ் பூக தெங்குதிரள் சோலை","வண்டுபடு வாவி ...... புடைசூழ","மந்திநட மாடு செந்தினகர் மேவு","மைந்தஅம ரேசர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of women whose eyes defeat the young deer, with lotus-bud breasts and night-dark hair, who bargain to strip a man of all his wealth and make him trust them, and asks Murugan to look with grace on one who, playing with them, wastes away like a frog that has eaten poison. The praise names Kumaresa, the guru, who rode the peacock with the red-eyed serpent in its beak and killed the demon army, at Senthil of areca, coconut and ponds where monkeys dance.","n":"நன்று பொருள் தீர என்று விலை பேசி: the bargaining is aimed at the man's wealth running out. நஞ்சுபுசி தேரை: the frog that has eaten poison and wastes away is a fresh image for the lover.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","selvam","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:6"},"p:46":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":46,"t":["காலனார் வெங்கொடுந் தூதர்பா சங்கொடென்","காலினார் தந்துடன் ...... கொடுபோகக்","காதலார் மைந்தருந் தாயரா ருஞ்சுடுங்","கானமே பின்தொடர்ந் ...... தலறாமுன்","சூலம்வாள் தண்டுசெஞ் சேவல்கோ தண்டமுஞ்","சூடுதோ ளுந்தடந் ...... திருமார்பும்","தூயதாள் தண்டையுங் காணஆர் வஞ்செயுந்","தோகைமேல் கொண்டுமுன் ...... வரவேணும்","ஆலகா லம்பரன் பாலதா கஞ்சிடுந்","தேவர்வா ழன்றுகந் ...... தமுதீயும்","ஆரவா ரஞ்செயும் வேலைமேல் கண்வளர்ந்","தாதிமா யன்றனன் ...... மருகோனே","சாலிசேர் சங்கினம் வாவிசூழ் பங்கயஞ்","சாரலார் செந்திலம் ...... பதிவாழ்வே","தாவுசூ ரஞ்சிமுன் சாயவே கம்பெறுந்","தாரைவே லுந்திடும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks that before Death's cruel messengers bind his feet with the noose and drag him off, and before loving sons and mothers follow wailing to the burning ground, Murugan come before him on the peacock, with trident, sword, club, red cock, bow, shoulders, chest and pure anklets, so he may see him with longing. The praise names Vishnu, who gave nectar to the gods when they feared the poison Siva took and who sleeps on the roaring sea, as his uncle, and Murugan the life of Senthil who hurled the vel so that Sura fell.","n":"The plea is for sight at the hour of death (காண ஆர்வம் செயும்), and it lists the Lord's weapons and body one by one as what he wants to see. The cock (சேவல்) appears here among the weapons, as the banner.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","saakkaadu","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:6"},"p:47":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":47,"t":["குகர மேவுமெய்த் துறவினின் மறவாக்","கும்பிட் டுந்தித் ...... தட்முழ்கிக்","குமுத வாயின்முற் றமுதினை நுகராக்","கொண்டற் கொண்டைக் ...... குழலாரோ","டகரு தூளிகர்ப் புரதன இருகோட்","டன்புற் றின்பக் ...... கடலு஡டே","அமிழு வேனைமெத் தெனவொரு கரைசேர்த்","தம்பொற் றண்டைக் ...... கழல்தாராய்","ககன கோளகைக் கணவிரு மளவாக்","கங்கைத் துங்கப் ...... புனலாடும்","கமல வாதனற் களவிட முடியாக்","கம்பர்க் கொன்றைப் ...... புகல்வோனே","சிகர கோபுரத் தினுமதி ளினுமேற்","செம்பொற் கம்பத் ...... தளமீதும்","தெருவி லேயுநித் திலமெறி யலைவாய்ச்","செந்திற் கந்தப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses bowing to women, sinking in the pool of the navel, drinking the nectar of lily lips, and drowning in a sea of pleasure among those with cloud-dark hair and breasts scented with aloe and camphor, and asks Murugan gently to bring him to shore and give his golden anklets. The praise names him the one who told the one thing to Siva, whose measure Brahma could not find and who bathes in Ganga reaching to the sky's sphere, and the lord of Senthil where waves throw pearls onto towers, walls and streets.","n":"The plea uses the sea of births image (இன்பக் கடலூடே அமிழுவேனை ... கரைசேர்த்து) for the sea of pleasure. கம்பர்க்கு ஒன்றைப் புகல்வோனே is the teaching of Siva (here as கம்பர், the lord of Kanchi), though Om is not named. The opening, குகர மேவு மெய்த் துறவினின் மறவா, seems to set the true renunciation of cave-dwellers against the poet's bowing to women, but its sense is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["kaamam","punarchi","guru","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:6"},"p:48":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":48,"t":["குடர்நிண மென்பு சலமல மண்டு","குருதிந ரம்பு ...... சீயூன் பொதிதோல்","குலவு குரம்பை முருடு சுமந்து","குனகிம கிழ்ந்து ...... நாயேன் தளரா","அடர்மத னம்பை யனையக ருங்க","ணரிவையர் தங்கள் ...... தோடோ ய்ந் தயரா","அறிவழி கின்ற குணமற வுன்றன்","அடியிணை தந்து ...... நீயாண் டருள்வாய்","தடவியல் செந்தில் இறையவ நண்பு","தருகுற மங்கை ...... வாழ்வாம் புயனே","சரவண கந்த முருகக டம்ப","தனிமயில் கொண்டு ...... பார்சூழ்ந் தவனே","சுடர்படர் குன்று தொளைபட அண்டர்","தொழவொரு செங்கை ...... வேல்வாங் கியவா","துரிதப தங்க இரதப்ர சண்ட","சொரிகடல் நின்ற ...... சூராந் தகனே."],"p":"","r":"The poet calls his body a rough hut of guts, fat, bone, water, filth, blood, nerves, pus and flesh wrapped in skin, which he carries about with fondness, wearying himself among women whose dark eyes are Kama's arrows until his understanding fails, and asks Murugan to remove that nature, give his paired feet and rule him. The praise names Murugan the lord of Senthil on whose arms the hunter girl lives, who circled the world on the peacock, who took up the vel in one hand to pierce the hill, and the destroyer of Sura who stood in the sea.","n":"The list குடர் நிணம் என்பு ... குருதி நரம்பு ... தோல் is the stock anatomy of disgust, set against the poet's own fondness (குனகி மகிழ்ந்து) for this hut. The song ends on சூராந்தகனே, 'ender of Sura', without the usual பெருமாளே.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","kaamam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:6"},"p:49":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":49,"t":["குழைக்குஞ்சந் தனச்செங்குங்","குமத்தின்சந் தநற்குன்றங்","குலுக்கும்பைங் கொடிக்கென்றிங் ...... கியலாலே","குழைக்குங்குண் குமிழ்க்குஞ்சென்","றுரைக்குஞ்செங் கயற்கண்கொண்","டழைக்கும்பண் தழைக்குஞ்சிங் ...... கியராலே","உழைக்குஞ்செங் கடத்துன்பன்","சுகப்பண்டஞ் சுகித்துண்டுண்","டுடற்பிண்டம் பருத்தின்றிங் ...... குழலாதே","உதிக்குஞ்செங் கதிர்ச்சிந்தும்","ப்ரபைக்கொன்றுஞ் சிவக்குந்தண்","டுயர்க்குங்கிண் கிணிச்செம்பஞ் ...... சடிசேராய்","தழைக்குங்கொன் றையைச்செம்பொன்","சடைக்கண்டங் கியைத்தங்குந்","தரத்தஞ்செம் புயத்தொன்றும் ...... பெருமானார்","தனிப்பங்கின் புறத்தின்செம்","பரத்தின்பங் கயத்தின்சஞ்","சரிக்குஞ்சங் கரிக்கென்றும் ...... பெருவாழ்வே","கழைக்குங்குஞ் சரக்கொம்புங்","கலைக்கொம்புங் கதித்தென்றுங்","கயற்கண்பண் பளிக்குந்திண் ...... புயவேளே","கறுக்குங்கொண் டலிற்பொங்குங்","கடற்சங்கங் கொழிக்குஞ்செந்","திலிற்கொண்டன் பினிற்றங்கும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of women with sandal and kumkum on their breasts and red carp eyes that call, and of himself toiling in the sorrowful pot of the body, eating and enjoying until the body-lump grows fat, and asks not to wander so but to be joined to the red feet with bells brighter than the rising sun. The praise names Siva with konrai on his golden hair, Sankari who shares his side, Murugan whose strong arms delight the elephant's daughter and the deer's daughter, and the lord at Senthil where conches roll from the sea.","n":"உடற்பிண்டம் பருத்து: the body fattened by feasting is the image of waste. குஞ்சரக் கொம்பும் கலைக் கொம்பும் appear to be Devayanai, raised by the elephant, and Valli, born of a doe. Like 41, the song is in a chiming short-syllable rhythm and many divisions are guesses.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","yaakkai","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:6"},"p:50":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":50,"t":["கொங்கைகள்கு லுங்கவளை செங்கையில்வி ளங்கஇருள்","கொண்டலைய டைந்தகுழல் ...... வண்டுபாடக்","கொஞ்சியவ னங்குயில்கள் பஞ்சநல்வ னங்கிளிகள்","கொஞ்சியதெ னுங்குரல்கள் ...... கெந்துபாயும்","வெங்கயல்மி ரண்டவிழி அம்புலிய டைந்தநுதல்","விஞ்சையர்கள் தங்கள்மயல் ...... கொண்டுமேலாய்","வெம்பிணியு ழன்றபவ சிந்தனைநி னைந்துனது","மின்சரண பைங்கழலொ ...... டண்டஆளாய்","சங்கமுர சந்திமிலை துந்தமித தும்பவளை","தந்தனத னந்தவென ...... வந்தசூரர்","சங்கைகெட மண்டிதிகை யெங்கிலும டிந்துவிழ","தண்கடல்கொ ளுந்தநகை ...... கொண்டவேலா","சங்கரனு கந்தபரி வின்குருவெ னுஞ்சுருதி","தங்களின்ம கிழ்ந்துருகு ...... மெங்கள்கோவே","சந்திரமு கஞ்செயல்கொள் சுந்தரகு றம்பெணொடு","சம்புபுகழ் செந்தில்மகிழ் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women with shaking breasts, bangles, bee-haunted hair, voices like cuckoos and parrots and eyes like fish, who infatuate even the vidyadharas, and asks that he, suffering in sickness and thoughts of birth, be ruled so as to reach Murugan's shining feet. The praise is a battle with drums and conches in which the Suras fall and the sea burns at the vel's smile, and names Murugan the king in whom the Vedas delight as the guru Sankara loved, who delights in the hunter girl at Senthil praised by Sambhu.","n":"சங்கரன் உகந்த பரிவின் குரு எனும் சுருதி: the Vedas themselves call him the guru Siva loved, a reference to the teaching myth without naming Om. The song ends on தம்பிரானே rather than பெருமாளே.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","mayal","guru","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:6"},"p:51":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":51,"t":["கொங்கைப் பணையிற் செம்பொற் செறிவிற்","கொண்டற் குழலிற் ...... கொடிதான","கொன்றைக் கணையொப் பந்தக் கயலிற்","கொஞ்சுக் கிளியுற் ...... றுறவான","சங்கத் தொனியிற் சென்றிற் கடையிற்","சந்திப் பவரைச் ...... சருவாதே","சந்தப் படியுற் றென்றற் றலையிற்","சந்தப் பதம்வைத் ...... தருள்வாயே","அங்கப் படைவிட் டன்றைப் படுகைக்","கந்திக் கடலிற் ...... கடிதோடா","அந்தப் பொழிலிற் சந்துத் தலையுற்","றஞ்சப் பொருதுற் ...... றொழியாதே","செங்கைக் கதிருற் றொன்றக் கடலிற்","சென்றுற் றவர்தற் ...... பொருளானாய்","சிந்தைக் கனிவைத் தந்தப் பொழிலிற்","செந்திற் குமரப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"In clipped rhythm the poet speaks of women with breasts, cloud-dark hair, eyes like konrai arrows and carp and parrot speech, who meet men at the ends of streets, and asks not to be entangled with them but to have Murugan set his feet on his head. The praise is obscure: it seems to tell of the demon who left his army and fled into the sea, and names the lord at Senthil who keeps tenderness in his heart.","n":"என்றன் தலையில் சந்தப் பதம் வைத்தருள்வாயே: the feet placed on the head, the gesture of the guru's initiation. சந்தப்படி may also mean 'in the rhythm of the song'. Most of the praise cannot be divided with confidence.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:7"},"p:52":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":52,"t":["கொடியனைய இடைதுவள அங்கமும் பொங்கஅங்","குமுதஅமு திதழ்பருகி யின்புறுஞ் சங்கையன்","குலவியணை முகிலளக முஞ்சரிந் தன்பினின் ...... பண்புலாவக்","கொடியவிரல் நகநுதியில் புண்படுஞ் சஞ்சலன்","குனகியவ ருடனினிது சம்ப்ரமங் கொண்டுளங்","குரலழிய அவசமுறு குங்குணன் கொங்கவிழ்ந் ...... தொன்றுபாய்மேல்","விடமனைய விழிமகளிர் கொங்கையின் பன்புறும்","வினையனியல் பரவுமுயிர் வெந்தழிந் தங்கமும்","மிதமொழிய அறிவில்நெறி பண்பிலண் டுஞ்சகன் ...... செஞ்செநீடும்","வெகுகனக வொளிகுலவும் அந்தமன் செந்திலென்","றவிழவுள முருகிவரும் அன்பிலன் தந்திலன்","விரவுமிரு சிறுகமல பங்கயந் தந்துகந் ...... தன்புறாதோ","படமிலகும் அரவினுடல் அங்கமும் பங்கிடந்","துதறுமொரு கலபிமிசை வந்தெழுந் தண்டர்தம்","பகையசுர ரனைவருடல் சந்துசந் துங்கதஞ் ...... சிந்தும்வேலா","படியவரும் இமையவரும் நின்றிறைஞ் செண்குணன்","பழையஇறை யுருவமிலி அன்பர்பங் கன்பெரும்","பருவரல்செய் புரமெரிய விண்டிடுஞ் செங்கணண் ...... கங்கைமான்வாழ்","சடிலமிசை அழகுபுனை கொன்றையும் பண்புறுந்","தருணமதி யினகுறைசெய் துண்டமுஞ் செங்கையொண்","சகலபுவ னமுமொழிக தங்குறங் கங்கியும் ...... பொங்கிநீடும்","சடமருவு விடையரவர் துங்கஅம் பங்கினின்","றுலகுதரு கவுரியுமை கொங்கைதந் தன்புறுந்","தமிழ்விரக உயர்பரம சங்கரன் கும்பிடுந் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes himself in a long series of names: one who drinks women's lips, wounded by their nails, a sinner, without knowledge, path or virtue, a man who never melts with love crying 'Senthil' and has given nothing, and asks whether Murugan will not give him his two small lotus feet with love. The praise names the peacock that tears the serpent, the vel that splits the demons joint by joint, and Siva the eight-qualitied, formless ancient Lord who burnt the three cities, with Ganga, konrai and crescent in his hair and the bull for his mount; and Murugan as the master of Tamil to whom Uma gave her breast, whom Sankara worships.","n":"கவுரி உமை கொங்கை தந்து அன்புறும் தமிழ் விரக: the Tamil master who drank Uma's breast milk is Sambandar's story, here given to Murugan. எண்குணன் for Siva is the Kural's epithet for the Lord. Several words in the first half (செஞ்செநீடும், அண்டுஞ்சகன்) are unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kutram","kaamam","thamizh","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:7"},"p:53":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":53,"t":["கொம்பனை யார்காது மோதிரு","கண்களி லாமோத சீதள","குங்கும பாடீர பூஷண ...... நகமேவு","கொங்கையி னீராவி மேல்வளர்","செங்கழு நீர்மாலை சூடிய","கொண்டையி லாதார சோபையில் ...... மருளாதே","உம்பர்கள் ஸ்வாமி நமோநம","எம்பெரு மானே நமோநம","ஒண்டொடி மோகா நமோநம ...... எனநாளும்","உன்புக ழேபாடி நானினி","அன்புட னாசார பூசைசெய்","துய்ந்திட வீணாள்ப டாதருள் ...... புரிவாயே","பம்பர மேபோல ஆடிய","சங்கரி வேதாள நாயகி","பங்கய சீபாத நூபுரி ...... கரசூலி","பங்கமி லாநீலி மோடிப","யங்கரி மாகாளி யோகினி","பண்டுசு ராபான சூரனொ ...... டெதிர்போர்கண்","டெம்புதல் வாவாழி வாழியெ","னும்படி வீறான வேல்தர","என்றுமு ளானேம நோகர ...... வயலு஡ரா","இன்சொல்வி சாகாக்ரு பாகர","செந்திலில் வாழ்வாகி யேயடி","யென்றனை யீடேற வாழ்வருள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks not to be dazzled by women's breasts with sandal, kumkum and ornaments and their hair with water-lily garlands, but to sing daily 'Lord of the gods, namo nama; my Lord, namo nama; lover of the bangled one, namo nama', do proper worship with love and not waste his days. The praise names the Goddess, who danced like a spinning top, queen of ghouls, Kali, Yogini, holding the trident, who saw the war with drink-sodden Sura and blessed her son with the victorious vel, and Murugan as lord of Vayalur, Visakha, and the life of Senthil who saves the poet.","n":"The plea contains its own litany: the நமோநம lines are the praise the poet asks to be able to sing. The vel is given by the Goddess (எம்புதல்வா வாழி வாழி எனும்படி வீறான வேல்தர), which ties it to Shakti. Vayalur, another shrine, is named among the epithets.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["vazhipaadu","pugazh","saththi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:7"},"p:54":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":54,"t":["கொலைமத கரியன ம்ருகமத தனகிரி","கும்பத் ...... தனமானார்","குமுதஅ முதஇதழ் பருகியு ருகிமயல்","கொண்டுற் ...... றிடுநாயேன்","நிலையழி கவலைகள் கெடவுன தருள்விழி","நின்றுற் ...... றிடவேதான்","நினதிரு வடிமல ரிணைமன தினிலுற","நின்பற் ...... றடைவேனோ","சிலையென வடமலை யுடையவர் அருளிய","செஞ்சொற் ...... சிறுபாலா","திரைகட லிடைவரும் அசுரனை வதைசெய்த","செந்திற் ...... பதிவேலா","விலைநிகர் நுதலிப மயில்குற மகளும்வி","ரும்பிப் ...... புணர்வோனே","விருதணி மரகத மயில்வரு குமரவி","டங்கப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet calls himself a dog who drank the lips of women with breasts like a killer elephant's temples and melted in infatuation, and asks whether, with the cares that destroy him ended and the eye of grace on him, he will hold Murugan's flower feet in mind and come to cling to him. The praise names the child of red words graced by Siva whose bow is the northern mountain, the vel that killed the demon from the sea, the lover of the hunter girl and the rider of the emerald peacock.","n":"நின் பற்று அடைவேனோ: the plea is for attachment, but attachment to the Lord, after attachment to women. செஞ்சொல் சிறுபாலா may point to Sambandar, again identified with Murugan, but that is uncertain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mayal","pattru","thiruvarul","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:7"},"p:55":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":55,"t":["சங்குபோல் மென்கழுத் தந்தவாய் தந்தபற்","சந்தமோ கின்பமுத் ...... தெனவானிற்","றங்குகார் பைங்குழற் கொங்கைநீள் தண்பொருப்","பென்றுதாழ் வொன்றறுத் ...... துலகோரைத்","துங்கவேள் செங்கைபொற் கொண்டல்நீ யென்றுசொற்","கொண்டுதாய் நின்றுரைத் ...... துழலாதே","துன்பநோய் சிந்தநற் கந்தவே ளென்றுனைத்","தொண்டினா லொன்றுரைக் ...... கருள்வாயே","வெங்கண்வ்யா ளங்கொதித் தெங்கும்வெ மென்றெடுத்","துண்டுமே லண்டருக் ...... கமுதாக","விண்டநா தன்திருக் கொண்டல்பா கன்செருக்","குண்டுபே ரம்பலத் ...... தினிலாடி","செங்கண்மால் பங்கயக் கண்பெறா தந்தரத்","தின்கணா டுந்திறற் ...... கதிராழித்","திங்கள்வா ழுஞ்சடைத் தம்பிரா னன்புறச்","செந்தில்வாழ் செந்தமிழ்ப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks not to wander praising women's conch-necks, coral mouths, pearl teeth and mountain breasts, nor to go to the men of the world telling them like a mother 'you are the noble Vel, you are the golden cloud of red hands', but, his sorrow and disease scattered, to say one word of service: 'good Kanda Vel'. The praise names Siva who took the poison and gave the gods nectar and dances in the great hall, before whom red-eyed Vishnu won the bright discus, and Murugan the red-Tamil lord of Senthil whom that moon-crowned Siva loves.","n":"The patron is flattered as Murugan himself (துங்கவேள்) and as a cloud for his giving: the poet asks to keep those names for the Lord alone. செங்கண்மால் பங்கயக் கண் ... கதிராழி appears to be Vishnu offering his lotus eye to Siva and receiving the discus, though the line is compressed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iravu","pugazh","thondu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:7"},"p:56":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":56,"t":["சங்கைதா னொன்றுதா னின்றியே நெஞ்சிலே","சஞ்சலா ...... ரம்பமாயன்","சந்தொடே குங்குமா லங்க்ருதா டம்பரா","சம்ப்ரமா ...... நந்தமாயன்","மங்கைமார் கொங்கைசே ரங்கமோ கங்களால்","வம்பிலே ...... துன்புறாமே","வண்குகா நின்செர்ரு பம்ப்ரகா சங்கொடே","வந்துநீ ...... யன்பிலாள்வாய்","கங்கைசூ டும்பிரான் மைந்தனே அந்தனே","கந்தனே ...... விஞ்சையூரா","கம்பியா திந்த்ரலோ கங்கள்கா வென்றவா","கண்டலே ...... சன்சொல்வீரா","செங்கைவேல் வென்றிவேல் கொண்டுசூர் பொன்றவே","சென்றுமோ ...... தும்ப்ரதாபா","செங்கண்மால் பங்கஜா னன்தொழா நந்தவேள்","செந்தில்வாழ் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet calls himself a deceiver without shame, his heart full of agitation, puffed with the pomp of sandal and kumkum, and asks not to suffer in vain from the body's infatuation with women's breasts, but that Guha come with the splendour of his own form and rule him with love. The praise names the son of the Ganga-bearing lord, Kandha, who saved Indra's worlds from trembling, the hero of Indra's cause, the vel that killed Sura, and the lord at Senthil whom Vishnu and Brahma worship.","n":"நின் சொரூப ப்ரகாசம் கொடே வந்து: the plea asks for the Lord's own form shining, a vision rather than a gift. The opening (சங்கைதான் ஒன்றுதான் இன்றியே) could mean without doubt or without shame, and the self-description is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","mayal","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:7"},"p:57":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":57,"t":["சத்தமிகு மேழுகட லைத்தேனை","யுற்றமது தோடுகணை யைப்போர்கொள்","சத்திதனை மாவின்வடு வைக்காவி ...... தனைமீறு","தக்கமணம் வீசுகம லப்பூவை","மிக்கவிளை வானகடு வைச்சீறு","தத்துகளும் வாளையடு மைப்பாவு ...... விழிமாதர்","மத்தகிரி போலுமொளிர் வித்தார","முத்துவட மேவுமெழில் மிக்கான","வச்சிரகி ஡ணடநிகர் செப்பான ...... தனமீதே","வைத்தகொடி தானமயல் விட்டான","பத்திசெய ஏழையடி மைக்காக","வஜ்ரமயில் மீதிலினி யெப்போது ...... வருவாயே","சித்ரவடி வேல்பனிரு கைக்கார","பத்திபுரி வோர்கள்பனு வற்கார","திக்கினுந டாவுபுர விக்கார ...... குறமாது","சித்தஅநு ராககல விக்கார","துட்டஅசு ரேசர்கல கக்கார","சிட்டர்பரி பாலலளி தக்கார ...... அடியார்கள்","முத்திபெற வேசொல்வச னக்கார","தத்தைநிகர் தூயவநி தைக்கார","முச்சகர்ப ராவுசர ணக்கார ...... இனிதான","முத்தமிழை யாயும்வரி சைக்கார","பச்சைமுகில் தாவுபுரி சைக்கார","முத்துலவு வேலைநகர் முத்தேவர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet heaps comparisons on women's eyes, the seven roaring seas, honey, arrows, the shakti spear, the mango slice, the lily, lotus, poison and the leaping fish, and on their breasts, like a rutting elephant's temples, hung with pearls; he asks when, the delusion laid on them gone, Murugan will come on his diamond peacock for this poor servant to do him devotion. The praise is a chain of epithets ending -க்கார: holder of the vel in twelve hands, lord of the devotees' hymns, lover of the hunter girl, scourge of the demons, protector of the good, speaker of words that free devotees, expert in the three Tamils, lord at sea-girt Senthil.","n":"The plea is a catalogue: piling the conventional comparisons is the infatuation itself, which the poet then asks to leave (வைத்த கொடிதான மயல் விட்டு). அடியார்கள் முத்திபெற வேசொல் வசனக்கார: Murugan is the one whose word gives release to devotees.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["mayal","paththi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:7"},"p:58":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":58,"t":["சந்தனச வாதுநிறை கற்பூர","குங்குமப டீரவிரை கத்தூரி","தண்புழுக ளாவுகள பச்சீத ...... வெகுவாச","சண்பகக லாரவகு ளத்தாம","வம்புதுகி லாரவயி ரக்கோவை","தங்கியக டோ ரதர வித்தார ...... பரிதான","மந்த்ரம தானதன மிக்காசை","கொண்டுபொருள் தேடுமதி நிட்டூர","வஞ்சகவி சாரஇத யப்பூவை ...... யனையார்கள்","வந்தியிடு மாயவிர கப்பார்வை","அம்பிலுளம் வாடுமறி வற்றேனை","வந்தடிமை யாளஇனி யெப்போது ...... நினைவாயே","இந்த்ரபுரி காவல்முதன் மைக்கார","சம்ப்ரமம யூரதுர கக்கார","என்றுமக லாதஇள மைக்கார ...... குறமாதின்","இன்பஅநு போகசர சக்கார","வந்தஅசு ரேசர்கல கக்கார","எங்களுமை சேயெனரு மைக்கார ...... மிகுபாவின்","செந்தமிழ்சொல் நாலுகவி தைக்கார","குன்றெறியும் வேலின்வலி மைக்கார","செஞ்சொலடி யார்களெளி மைக்கார ...... எழில்மேவும்","திங்கள்முடி நாதர்சம யக்கார","மந்த்ரவுப தேசமகி மைக்கார","செந்தினகர் வாழுமரு மைத்தேவர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women scented with sandal, civet, camphor, kumkum and musk, in silk and diamonds, who seek money with great desire, cruel and deceitful at heart, whose glance of feigned longing withers his mind, and asks when Murugan will think to come and take him into service. The praise, again a chain of -க்கார epithets, names the guardian of Indra's city, the rider of the peacock, the ever-young, the lover of the hunter girl, Uma's son, the master of the four kinds of verse, the strength of the vel, the one easy for devotees, the glory of mantra teaching, at Senthil.","n":"பொருள் தேடுமதி நிட்டூர வஞ்சக விசார இதய: money-seeking, cruel and deceitful hearts, the courtesan named by her motive. நாலுகவிதைக்கார, master of the four kinds of poetry, and மந்த்ர உபதேச மகிமைக்கார, glory of teaching the mantra, make Murugan both poet and guru.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","selvam","vanjam","thondu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:7"},"p:59":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":59,"t":["சேமக் கோமள பாதத் தாமரை","சேர்தற் கோதும ...... நந்தவேதா","தீதத் தேயவி ரோதத் தேகுண","சீலத் தேமிக ...... அன்புறாதே","காமக் ரோதவு லோபப் பூதவி","காரத் தேயழி ...... கின்றமாயா","காயத் தேபசு பாசத் தேசிலர்","காமுற் றேயும ...... தென்கொலோதான்","நேமிச் சூரொடு மேருத் தூளெழ","நீளக் காளபு ...... யங்ககால","நீலக் ஡ணபக லாபத் தேர்விடு","நீபச் சேவக ...... செந்தில்வாழ்வே","ஓமத் தீவழு வார்கட் கூர்சிவ","லோகத் தேதரு ...... மங்கைபாலா","யோகத் தாறுப தேசத் தேசிக","வூமைத் தேவர்கள் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"Rather than a plea, the poet asks a question: why do some, without loving the lotus feet the endless Vedas proclaim and without care for virtue, waste away in lust, anger and greed, in the changes of the elements, in the body of maya, in the bonds of soul and fetter? The praise names the warrior of the kadamba on the blue-necked peacock, whose war raised dust from Meru, son of the Mother who gives Sivaloka to those who keep the fire rite, the master who taught the way of yoga, lord of the silent gods.","n":"The vocabulary is Saiva Siddhanta: பசு பாசம், மாயாகாயம், and the three faults காம க்ரோத லோப. The song asks a question (அது என்கொலோ தான்) instead of confessing. ஓமத் தீ வழுவார்கட்கு ஊர் சிவலோகத்தே தரு names the fire rite as a path to Sivaloka. ஊமைத் தேவர்கள் is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["pasu","paasam","maayai","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:7"},"p:60":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":60,"t":["தகரநறை பூண்ட விந்தைக்","குழலியர்கள் தேய்ந்த இன்பத்","தளருமிடை யேந்து தங்கத் ...... தனமானார்","தமைமனதில் வாஞ்சை பொங்கக்","கலவியொடு சேர்ந்து மந்த்ரச்","சமயஜெப நீங்கி யிந்தப் ...... படிநாளும்","புகலரிய தாந்த்ரி சங்கத்","தமிழ்பனுவ லாய்ந்து கொஞ்சிப்","புவியதனில் வாழ்ந்து வஞ்சித் ...... துழல்மூடர்","புநிதமிலி மாந்தர் தங்கட்","புகழ்பகர்தல் நீங்கி நின்பொற்","புளகமலர் பூண்டு வந்தித் ...... திடுவேனோ","தகுடதகு தாந்த தந்தத்","திகுடதிகு தீந்த மிந்தித்","தகுகணக தாங்க ணங்கத் ...... தனதான","தனனதன தாந்த னந்தத்","தெனநடன மார்ந்த துங்கத்","தனிமயிலை யூர்ந்த சந்தத் ...... திருமார்பா","திசையசுரர் மாண்ட ழுந்தத்","திறலயிலை வாங்கு செங்கைச்","சிமையவரை யீன்ற மங்கைக் ...... கொருபாலா","திகழ்வயிர மேந்து கொங்கைக்","குறவனிதை காந்த சந்த்ரச்","சிகரமுகி லோங்கு செந்திற் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of craving women with fragrant hair and golden breasts, joining them and leaving mantra, faith and prayer, and asks whether, ceasing to praise impure fools who wander the earth deceiving, he will adorn himself with Murugan's flower feet and worship. The praise is given in drum syllables for the dancing peacock and names the vel that destroyed the demons, the son of the mountain's daughter, and the lover of the hunter girl, at Senthil.","n":"புநிதமிலி மாந்தர் தங்கள் புகழ்பகர்தல் நீங்கி: the poet gives up praising patrons, as in 55. The lines on studying Sangam Tamil poems (தாந்த்ரி சங்கத் தமிழ் பனுவல் ஆய்ந்து) may describe the poet himself or the men he praised; the reference is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","pugazh","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:7"},"p:61":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":61,"t":["தண்டே னுண்டே வண்டார் வஞ்சேர்","தண்டார் மஞ்சுக் ...... குழல்மானார்","தம்பா லன்பார் நெஞ்சே கொண்டே","சம்பா வஞ்சொற் ...... றடிநாயேன்","மண்டோ யந்தீ மென்கால் விண்டோ ய்","வண்கா யம்பொய்க் ...... குடில்வேறாய்","வன்கா னம்போ யண்டா முன்பே","வந்தே நின்பொற் ...... கழல்தாராய்","கொண்டா டும்பேர் கொண்டா டுஞ்சூர்","கொன்றாய் வென்றிக் ...... குமரேசா","கொங்கார் வண்டார் பண்பா டுஞ்சீர்","குன்றா மன்றற் ...... கிரியோனே","கண்டா கும்பா லுண்டா யண்டார்","கண்டா கந்தப் ...... புயவேளே","கந்தா மைந்தா ரந்தோள் மைந்தா","கந்தா செந்திற் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet calls himself a dog whose heart is set on women with cloud-dark hair full of honey and bees, and asks Murugan to come and give his golden feet before this false hut of earth, water, fire, wind and sky falls apart and goes to the harsh burning ground. The praise names Kumaresa who killed famous Sura, the lord of the fragrant hill where bees sing, and Kandha the son at Senthil.","n":"மண் தோயம் தீ மென்கால் விண் தோய் வண் காயம் பொய்க் குடில்: the body is a false hut of the five elements, which come apart at death. Parts of the praise (கண்டா கும்பால் உண்டாய்) are unclear.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","nilaiyaamai","kaamam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:7"},"p:62":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":62,"t":["தண்டையணி வெண்டையங் கிண்கிணிச தங்கையுந்","தண்கழல்சி லம்புடன் ...... கொஞ்சவேநின்","தந்தையினை முன்பரிந் தின்பவுரி கொண்டுநன்","சந்தொடம ணைந்துநின் ...... றன்புபோலக்","கண்டுறக டம்புடன் சந்தமகு டங்களுங்","கஞ்சமலர் செங்கையுஞ் ...... சிந்துவேலும்","கண்களுமு கங்களுஞ் சந்திரநி றங்களுங்","கண்குளிர என்றன்முன் ...... சந்தியாவோ","புண்டரிகர் அண்டமுங் கொண்டபகி ரண்டமும்","பொங்கியெழ வெங்களங் ...... கொண்டபோது","பொன்கிரியெ னஞ்சிறந் தெங்கினும்வ ளர்ந்துமுன்","புண்டரிகர் தந்தையுஞ் ...... சிந்தைகூரக்","கொண்டநட னம்பதஞ் செந்திலிலும் என்றன்முன்","கொஞ்சிநட னங்கொளுங் ...... கந்தவேளே","கொங்கைகுற மங்கையின் சந்தமணம் உண்டிடுங்","கும்பமுநி கும்பிடுந் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether Murugan will not appear before him, so that his eyes are cooled, with anklets and bells ringing, as when he once danced with joy and embraced his father, with kadamba, crowns, lotus hands, vel, eyes and faces. The praise names the form that grew like a golden mountain to fill the worlds in battle until even Brahma's father (Vishnu) was amazed, the dance he dances at Senthil and before the poet himself, and the lord whom Agastya worships.","n":"There is no confession here, only longing for sight (கண்குளிர என்றன்முன் சந்தியாவோ). செந்திலிலும் என்றன் முன் கொஞ்சி நடனங் கொளும்: the poet says Murugan dances before him, a claim of vision. The cosmic form (பொன்கிரியெனச் சிறந்து எங்கினும் வளர்ந்து) is Murugan's, not Vishnu's.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paththi","viyaabagam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:7"},"p:63":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":63,"t":["தந்த பசிதனைய றிந்து முலையமுது","தந்து முதுகுதட ...... வியதாயார்","தம்பி பணிவிடைசெய் தொண்டர் பிரியமுள","தங்கை மருகருயி ...... ரெனவேசார்","மைந்தர் மனைவியர்க டும்பு கடனுதவு","மந்த வரிசைமொழி ...... பகர்கோடா","வந்து தலைநவிர விழ்ந்து தரைபுகம","யங்க வொருமகிட ...... மிசையேறி","அந்த கனுமெனைய டர்ந்து வருகையினி","லஞ்ச லெனவலிய ...... மயில்மேல்நீ","அந்த மறலியொடு கந்த மனிதனம","தன்ப னெனமொழிய ...... வருவாயே","சிந்தை மகிழமலை மங்கை நகிலிணைகள்","சிந்து பயமயிலு ...... மயில்வீரா","திங்க ளரவுநதி துன்று சடிலரருள்","செந்தி னகரிலுறை ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet lists the kin who gather at death, mothers who fed and stroked him, brothers, servants, sisters, nephews, sons and wives, with their claims, and asks that when Death himself comes pressing on a buffalo, Murugan come on the strong peacock saying 'fear not' and tell that Death, 'Kandha says this man is our devotee'. The praise names the warrior whose peacock delights Uma, and the lord at Senthil of Siva who wears the moon, the serpent and the river.","n":"அந்த மறலியொடு கந்த மனிதன் நமது அன்பன் என மொழிய: the plea is not only for Murugan to come but for him to speak to Death and claim the man as his own. The kin are pictured with the debts and claims they bring (கடனுதவு), so they offer no help.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","sutram","adiyaar","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:7"},"p:64":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":64,"t":["தரிக்குங்கலை நெகிழ்க்கும்பர","தவிக்குங்கொடி ...... மதனேவிற்","றகைக்குந்தனி திகைக்குஞ்சிறு","தமிழ்த்தென்றலி ...... னுடனேநின்","றெரிக்கும்பிறை யெனப்புண்படு","மெனப்புன்கவி ...... சிலபாடி","இருக்குஞ்சிலர் திருச்செந்திலை","யுரைத்துய்ந்திட ...... அறியாரே","அரிக்குஞ்சதுர் மறைக்கும்பிர","மனுக்குந்தெரி ...... வரிதான","அடிச்செஞ்சடை முடிக்கொண்டிடு","மரற்கும்புரி ...... தவபாரக்","கிரிக்கும்பநன் முநிக்குங்க்ருபை","வரிக்குங்குரு ...... பரவாழ்வே","கிளைக்குந்திற லரக்கன்கிளை","கெடக்கன்றிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet scorns some who sit singing a few poor poems about a girl whose garment slips, who pines struck by Kama's arrows and is bewildered by the little southern breeze and burnt by the crescent, and do not know to speak of Thiruchendur and be saved. The praise names Murugan the guru who gave grace both to Siva, whose feet and crown Vishnu, Brahma and the Vedas could not know, and to the sage Agastya, and the lord who destroyed the demon's kin.","n":"The love-sickness of the girl, with Kama's arrow, the south wind and the crescent, is the stock of akam verse; the complaint is against poems that spend these devices without naming the shrine. அரற்கும் ... கும்பநன் முநிக்கும் க்ருபை வரிக்கும் குருபர: Murugan as teacher both of Siva and of Agastya.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","pugazh","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:7"},"p:65":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":65,"t":["துன்பங்கொண் டங்க மெலிந்தற","நொந்தன்பும் பண்பு மறந்தொளி","துஞ்சும்பெண் சஞ்சல மென்பதி ...... லணுகாதே","இன்பந்தந் தும்பர் தொழும்பத","கஞ்சந்தந் தஞ்ச மெனும்படி","யென்றென்றுந் தொண்டு செயும்படி ...... யருள்வாயே","நின்பங்கொன் றுங்குற மின்சர","ணங்கண்டுந் தஞ்ச மெனும்படி","நின்றன்பின் றன்படி கும்பிடு ...... மிளையோனே","பைம்பொன்சிந் தின்றுறை தங்கிய","குன்றெங்குஞ் சங்கு வலம்புரி","பம்புந்தென் செந்திலில் வந்தருள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks not to go near the agitation of women, which leaves the body wasted and suffering and love and goodness forgotten, but to be given the lotus feet the gods worship, so he may cry 'refuge' and do service forever. The praise names the younger one who, seeing the feet of the hunter girl who shares his side, cried 'refuge', stood behind her and bowed, and the lord at Senthil where valampuri conches throng the golden shore.","n":"தஞ்சம் is said twice: the poet asks to cry 'refuge' at the Lord's feet, and the praise has the Lord crying 'refuge' at Valli's feet and bowing (கும்பிடும்). The devotee's surrender is mirrored in the god's surrender to his beloved.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thondu","thiruvadi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:7"},"p:66":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":66,"t":["தெருப்பு றத்துத் துவக்கியாய்","முலைக்கு வட்டைக் குலுக்கியாய்","சிரித்து ருக்கித் தருக்கியே ...... பண்டைகூள மெனவாழ்","சிறுக்கி ரட்சைக் கிதக்கியாய்","மனத்தை வைத்துக் கனத்தபேர்","தியக்க முற்றுத் தவிக்கவே ...... கண்டுபேசி யுடனே","இருப்ப கத்துத் தளத்துமேல்","விளக்கெ டுத்துப் படுத்துமே","லிருத்தி வைத்துப் பசப்பியே ...... கொண்டுகாசு தணியா","திதுக்க துக்குக் கடப்படா","மெனக்கை கக்கக் கழற்றியே","இளைக்க விட்டுத் துரத்துவார் ...... தங்கள்சேர்வை தவிராய்","பொருப்பை யொக்கப் பணைத்ததோ","ரிரட்டி பத்துப் புயத்தினால்","பொறுத்த பத்துச் சிரத்தினால் ...... மண்டுகோப முடனே","பொரப்பொ ருப்பிற் கதித்தபோ","ரரக்கர் பட்டுப் பதைக்கவே","புடைத்து முட்டத் துணித்தமா ...... லன்புகூரு மருகா","வரப்பை யெட்டி குதித்துமே","லிடத்தில் வட்டத் தளத்திலே","மதர்த்த முத்தைக் குவட்டியே ...... நின்றுசேலி னினம்வாழ்","வயற்பு றத்துப் புவிக்குள்நீள்","திருத்த ணிக்குட் சிறப்பில்வாழ்","வயத்த நித்தத் துவத்தனே ...... செந்தில்மேவு குகனே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes the women who lure men from the street, shaking their breasts and laughing, who set their minds on rich men, take them up to the upper floor, lie with them by lamplight and coax coins from them, demand more as owed, strip even the jewels from their hands, and drive them out exhausted; he asks to be kept from their company. The praise names Vishnu, who in anger struck down the demon of twenty mountain-like arms and ten heads, as the loving uncle, and calls Murugan the Guha of Senthil who also lives in glory at Thiruttani, where fish leap over the field ridges onto heaped pearls.","n":"The sequence is a small drama of the courtesan's house: the street, the upstairs room, the lamp, the coaxing (பசப்பி), the claim 'this is owed for that' (இதுக்கு அதுக்குக் கடப்படாம்), and the expulsion (இளைக்க விட்டுத் துரத்துவார்). A Senthil song names Thiruttani in its last lines (திருத்தணிக்குள் சிறப்பில் வாழ்) before ending at Senthil. The phrase பண்டைகூளமென வாழ் and the closing epithet வயத்த நித்தத் துவத்தனே are hard to divide.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:8"},"p:67":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":67,"t":["தொடரிய மன்போற் றுங்கப்","படையைவ ளைந்தோட் டுந்துட்","டரையிள குந்தோட் கொங்கைக் ...... கிடுமாயத்","துகில்விழ வுஞ்சேர்த் தங்கத்","துளைவிர குஞ்சூழ்த் தண்டித்","துயர்விளை யுஞ்சூட் டின்பத் ...... தொடுபாயற்","கிடைகொடு சென்றீட் டும்பொற்","பணியரை மென்றேற் றங்கற்","றனையென இன்றோட் டென்றற் ...... கிடுமாதர்க்","கினிமையி லொன்றாய்ச் சென்றுட்","படுமன முன்றாட் கன்புற்","றியலிசை கொண்டேத் தென்றுட் ...... டருவாயே","நெடிதுத வங்கூர்க் குஞ்சற்","புருடரும் நைந்தேக் கம்பெற்","றயர்வுற நின்றார்த் தங்கட் ...... கணையேவும்","நிகரில்ம தன்தேர்க் குன்றற்","றெரியில்வி ழுந்தேர்ப் பொன்றச்","சிறிதுநி னைந்தாட் டங்கற் ...... றிடுவார்முன்","திடமுறு அன்பாற் சிந்தைக்","கறிவிட முஞ்சேர்த் தும்பர்க்","கிடர்களை யும்போர்ச் செங்கைத் ...... திறல்வேலா","தினவரி வண்டார்த் தின்புற்","றிசைகொடு வந்தேத் திஞ்சித்","திருவளர் செந்தூர்க் கந்தப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of women who draw men to the bed of burning pleasure, gather the gold and ornaments they bring, and when a man is spent tell him to go today; he asks that his mind, which goes so sweetly to them, be given love for Murugan's feet and the power to praise him in poetry and song. The praise recalls Kama, whose arrows made even great ascetics wilt, falling into the fire of Siva who burned him with a slight thought, and the vel that removes the gods' distress, at Senthil where striped bees hum his praise.","n":"அற்றனை என இன்று ஓட்டு என்று: the woman's dismissal once the man has nothing left, the same charge as in 33 and 66. The plea is to redirect the same mind (இனிமையில் ஒன்றாய்ச் சென்று உட்படு மனம்) rather than to kill it. The dense rhythm makes the first eight lines and the phrase திடமுறு அன்பால் சிந்தைக்கு அறிவிடமும் சேர்த்து hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","selvam","pugazh","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:8"},"p:68":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":68,"t":["தொந்தி சரிய மயிரே வெளிறநிரை","தந்த மசைய முதுகே வளையஇதழ்","தொங்க வொருகை தடிமேல் வரமகளிர் ...... நகையாடி","தொண்டு கிழவ னிவனா ரெனஇருமல்","கிண்கி ணெனமு னுரையே குழறவிழி","துஞ்சு குருடு படவே செவிடுபடு ...... செவியாகி","வந்த பிணியு மதிலே மிடையுமொரு","பண்டி தனுமெ யுறுவே தனையுமிள","மைந்த ருடைமை கடனே தெனமுடுக ...... துயர்மேவி","மங்கை யழுது விழவே யமபடர்கள்","நின்று சருவ மலமே யொழுகவுயிர்","மங்கு பொழுது கடிதே மயிலின்மிசை ...... வரவேணும்","எந்தை வருக ரகுநா யகவருக","மைந்த வருக மகனே யினிவருக","என்கண் வருக எனதா ருயிர்வருக ...... அபிராம","இங்கு வருக அரசே வருகமுலை","யுண்க வருக மலர்சூ டிடவருக","என்று பரிவி னொடுகோ சலைபுகல ...... வருமாயன்","சிந்தை மகிழு மருகா குறவரிள","வஞ்சி மருவு மழகா அமரர்சிறை","சிந்த அசுரர் கிளைவே ரொடுமடிய ...... அடுதீரா","திங்க ளரவு நதிசூ டியபரமர்","தந்த குமர அலையே கரைபொருத","செந்தி னகரி லினிதே மருவிவளர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet pictures his old age, belly sagging, hair white, teeth loose, back bent, lips hanging, one hand on a stick, while women laugh 'who is this worn-out old man', his cough rattles, speech slurs, eyes go blind and ears deaf; a physician comes, his young sons ask about property and debts, his wife weeps and falls, and Yama's servants stand by as his filth flows: at that hour, he asks, come quickly on the peacock. The praise is a long lullaby of Kausalya calling the child Rama, 'come, my father, come, lord of the Raghus, come, my son, my eye, my life, come and drink at the breast, come and wear flowers', and calls Murugan the nephew who delights that Mayan's heart, Valli's lover and son of Siva who wears the moon, the serpent and the river, at Senthil.","n":"தொண்டு கிழவன் here means a decrepit old man, not a servant: the same word the book uses for service is turned into mockery in the women's mouths. The deathbed is social as well as bodily: the doctor, the sons asking உடைமை கடன் ஏது, the falling wife. The Kausalya passage, ten repetitions of வருக, mirrors the plea: the poet calls the Lord to come as Kausalya called her child.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","yaakkai","ilamai","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:8"},"p:69":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":69,"t":["தோலொடு மூடிய கூரையை நம்பிப்","பாவையர் தோதக லீலைநி ரம்பிச்","சூழ்பொருள் தேடிட வோடிவ ருந்திப் ...... புதிதான","தூதொடு நான்மணி மாலைப்ர பந்தக்","கோவையு லாமடல் கூறிய ழுந்தித்","தோமுறு காளையர் வாசல்தொ றும்புக் ...... கலமாருங்","காலனை வீணனை நீதிகெ டும்பொய்க்","கோளனை மானமி லாவழி நெஞ்சக்","காதக லோபவ்ரு தாவனை நிந்தைப் ...... புலையேனைக்","காரண காரிய லோகப்ர பஞ்சச்","சோகமெ லாமற வாழ்வுற நம்பிற்","காசறு வாரிமெய்ஞ் ஞான தவஞ்சற் ...... றருளாதோ","பாலன மீதும னான்முக செம்பொற்","பாலனை மோதப ராதன பண்டப்","பாரிய மாருதி தோள்மிசை கொண்டுற் ...... றமராடிப்","பாவியி ராவண னார்தலை சிந்திச்","சீரிய வீடணர் வாழ்வுற மன்றற்","பாவையர் தோள்புணர் மாதுலர் சிந்தைக் ...... கினியோனே","சீலமு லாவிய நாரதர் வந்துற்","றீதவள் வாழ்புன மாமென முந்தித்","தேமொழி பாளித கோமள இன்பக் ...... கிரிதோய்வாய்","சேலொடு வாளைவ ரால்கள் கிளம்பித்","தாறுகொள் பூகம ளாவிய இன்பச்","சீரலை வாய்நகர் மேவிய கந்தப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses that, trusting this hut thatched with skin and full of women's deceitful play, he ran after wealth composing new தூது, நான்மணிமாலை, கோவை, உலா and மடல் poems and entering the doors of worthless patrons, and calls himself idle, unjust, a liar and slanderer, shameless, cruel, miserly and reviled; he asks whether grace will not grant a little of the stainless sea of true knowledge and austerity, so that all the sorrow of the world of cause and effect ends. The praise tells of Rama, borne on Hanuman's shoulders, who cut off Ravana's heads and gave Vibhishana his kingdom, and of Narada coming to say 'this is the field where she lives', so that Murugan went to Valli, at Seeralaivay.","n":"The plea names the prabandha genres the poet used to win gifts (தூது, நான்மணிமாலைப் பிரபந்தக் கோவை, உலா, மடல்) and the doors of patrons (தோமுறு காளையர் வாசல்தொறும்): the confession is of poetry sold, not only of lust. தோலொடு மூடிய கூரை, a roof covered with skin, is the body. Narada's part in the Valli story is named outright (நாரதர் வந்துற்று ஈது அவள் வாழ் புனமாம்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","kutram","gnaanam","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:8"},"p:70":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":70,"t":["நாலு மைந்து வாசல் கீறு தூறு டம்பு கால்கை யாகி","நாரி யென்பி லாகு மாக ...... மதனு஡டே","நாத மொன்ற ஆதி வாயில் நாட கங்க ளான ஆடி","நாட றிந்தி டாம லேக ...... வளராமுன்","நூல நந்த கோடி தேடி மால்மி குந்து பாரு ளோரை","நூறு செஞ்சொல் கூறி மாறி ...... விளைதீமை","நோய்க லந்த வாழ்வு றாமல் நீக லந்து ளாகு ஞான","நூல டங்க வோத வாழ்வு ...... தருவாயே","காலன் வந்து பால னாவி காய வென்று பாசம் வீசு","காலம் வந்து வோல மோல ...... மெனுமாதி","காம னைந்து பாண மோடு வேமி னென்று காணு மோனர்","காள கண்ட ரோடு வேத ...... மொழிவோனே","ஆல மொன்று வேலை யாகி யானை யஞ்சல் தீரு மூல","ஆழி யங்கை ஆயன் மாயன் ...... மருகோனே","ஆர ணங்கள் தாளை நாட வார ணங்கை மேவு மாதி","யான செந்தில் வாழ்வ தான ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of the body of four and five gates, a bush of sinew and bone with arms and legs, in which sound joins at the first gate and its plays go on unnoticed, and asks that before it grows old in seeking endless books and speaking clever words to men he be given a life of reciting fully the books of knowledge in which Murugan is mingled. The praise names the primal one who cried 'refuge' when Death came casting his noose for the boy's life, the silent dark-throated one who looked at Kama and his five arrows and said 'burn', to whom Murugan spoke the Veda, and Vishnu with the discus who ended the elephant's fear, at Senthil where the Vedas seek the feet.","n":"நாலும் ஐந்து வாசல் is the body of nine openings. The plea sets two kinds of learning against each other: the endless books sought with delusion and the clever words that breed harm, and the ஞான நூல் in which the Lord is present. The boy whose life Death came for, with the cry ஓலம், points to the story of Markandeya; whose the cry is, the boy's or Siva's, depends on the division. Several phrases (நாரி என்பில் ஆகும், நாதம் ஒன்ற ஆதி வாயில்) are uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["yaakkai","gnaanam","kalvi","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:8"},"p:71":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":71,"t":["நிதிக்குப் பிங்கலன் பதத்துக் கிந்திரன்","நிறத்திற் கந்தனென் ...... றினைவொரை","நிலத்திற் றன்பெரும் பசிக்குத் தஞ்சமென்","றரற்றித் துன்பநெஞ் ...... சினில்நாளும்","புதுச்சொற் சங்கமொன் றிசைத்துச் சங்கடம்","புகட்டிக் கொண்டுடம் ......பழிமாயும்","புலத்திற் சஞ்சலங் குலைத்திட் டுன்பதம்","புணர்க்கைக் கன்புதந் ...... தருள்வாயே","மதித்துத் திண்புரஞ் சிரித்துக் கொன்றிடும்","மறத்திற் றந்தைமன் ...... றினிலாடி","மழுக்கைக் கொண்டசங் கரர்க்குச் சென்றுவண்","டமிழ்ச்சொற் சந்தமொன் ...... றருள்வோனே","குதித்துக் குன்றிடந் தலைத்துச் செம்பொனுங்","கொழித்துக் கொண்டசெந் ...... திலின்வாழ்வே","குறப்பொற் கொம்பைமுன் புனத்திற் செங்கரங்","குவித்துக் கும்பிடும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses that he compared men to Kubera for wealth, Indra for rank and Kandan for beauty, cried to them that they were his refuge in his great hunger, and sang new verses daily while pouring out his distress; he asks Murugan to break the agitation of the senses in which the body perishes and give him love that unites him to his feet. The praise names Siva, who laughed and burned the strong cities and dances in the hall with the axe, to whom Murugan gave a Tamil verse in rhythm, the splitter of the hill at Senthil, and Murugan who joined his red hands and bowed to the golden creeper of the hunters in the field.","n":"The begging poet even compares patrons to Kandan (நிறத்திற் கந்தன்), the god he should be praising: the flattery borrows Murugan's own name. In the praise Murugan bows to Valli (செங்கரம் குவித்துக் கும்பிடும்), the lover humbling himself. மழுக்கைக் கொண்ட சங்கரர்க்குச் சென்று வண்டமிழ்ச் சொல் சந்தம் ஒன்று அருள்வோனே: the teaching to Siva is given here as Tamil verse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","pasi","anbu","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:8"},"p:72":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":72,"t":["நிலையாப் பொருளை யுடலாக் கருதி","நெடுநாட் பொழுது ...... மவமேபோய்","நிறைபோய்ச் செவிடு குருடாய்ப் பிணிகள்","நிறைவாய்ப் பொறிகள் ...... தடுமாறி","மலநீர்ச் சயன மிசையாப் பெருகி","மடுவேற் குரிய ...... நெறியாக","மறைபோற் றரிய வொளியாய்ப் பரவு","மலர்தாட் கமல ...... மருள்வாயே","கொலைகாட் டவுணர் கெடமாச் சலதி","குளமாய்ச் சுவற ...... முதுசூதம்","குறிபோய்ப் பிளவு படமேற் கதுவு","கொதிவேற் படையை ...... விடுவோனே","அலைவாய்க் கரையின் மகிழ்சீர்க் குமர","அழியாப் புநித ...... வடிவாகும்","அரனார்க் கதித பொருள்காட் டதிப","அடியார்க் கெளிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses that, taking impermanent things for himself, he wasted long days until his strength went, he grew deaf and blind, full of disease, his senses faltering, swollen on a bed of filth and water, on the way to the burning ground, and asks for the lotus feet that spread as a light the Vedas cannot praise. The praise names the boiling vel that dried the sea like a pond and split the old mango tree of the demons, and calls Murugan the Kumara of Alaivay who showed the highest meaning to Siva of undying pure form, easy for his devotees to reach.","n":"நிலையாப் பொருளை உடலாக் கருதி: the error is taking the passing thing for oneself, and the decay that follows is its result. மறைபோற்ற அரிய ஒளியாய்ப் பரவு மலர்த்தாள் makes the feet a pervading light. The mango tree (சூதம்) is Sura's last form. அடியார்க்கு எளிய: the Lord is easy of access for devotees, the closing note. மடுவேற்கு உரிய நெறியாக is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","yaakkai","thiruvadi","oli"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:8"},"p:73":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":73,"t":["நிறுக்குஞ் சூதன மெய்த்தன முண்டைகள்","கருப்பஞ் சாறொட ரைத்துள வுண்டைகள்","நிழற்கண் காணவு ணக்கிம ணம்பல ...... தடவாமேல்","நெருக்கும் பாயலில் வெற்றிலை யின்புறம்","ஒளித்தன் பாகஅ ளித்தபி னிங்கெனை","நினைக்கின் றீரிலை மெச்சலி தஞ்சொலி ...... யெனவோதி","உறக்கண் டாசைவ லைக்குள ழுந்திட","விடுக்கும் பாவிகள் பொட்டிகள் சிந்தனை","யுருக்குந் தூவைகள் செட்டைகு ணந்தனி ...... லுழலாமே","உலப்பின் றாறெனு மக்கர முங்கமழ்","கடப்பந் தாருமு கப்ரபை யுந்தினம்","உளத்தின் பார்வையி டத்தினி னைந்திட ...... அருள்வாயே","கறுக்குந் தூயமி டற்றன ருஞ்சிலை","யெடுக்குந் தோளனி றத்தம ரெண்கரி","கடக்குந் தானவ னைக்கொல ரும்புயன் ...... மருகோனே","கனத்தஞ் சாபுரி சிக்கல்வ லஞ்சுழி","திருச்செங் கோடு இடைக்கழி தண்டலை","களர்ச்செங் காடு குறுக்கை புறம்பயம் ...... அமர்வோனே","சிறுக்கண் கூர்மத அத்தி சயிந்தவ","நடக்குந் தேரனி கப்படை கொண்டமர்","செலுத்தும் பாதகன் அக்ரமன் வஞ்சனை ...... யுருவானோன்","செருக்குஞ் சூரக லத்தை யிடந்துயிர்","குடிக்குங் கூரிய சத்திய மர்ந்தருள்","திருச்செந் தூர்நக ரிக்குள்வி ளங்கிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women who make love-pellets ground with sugarcane juice and feed them to men, rub on many scents, hide something inside the betel they give on the bed, then complain 'you never think of me', and so drag men into the net of desire; he asks not to wander in their ways but to be granted daily vision in his heart of the fragrant six letters, the kadamba garland and the radiance of Murugan's face. The praise names Siva of the dark throat and Rama of the bow as kin, lists the shrines where Murugan dwells (Sikkal, Valanchuzhi, Thiruchengodu, Idaikkazhi, Thandalai, Kalar, Chengadu, Kurukkai, Purambayam and others), and the vel that pierced the chest of the deceitful Sura and drank his life, at Thiruchendur.","n":"The deceit named here is the love-charm: கருப்பஞ் சாறொடு அரைத்துள உண்டைகள் and something hidden in the betel (வெற்றிலையின் புறம் ஒளித்து), the courtesans' magic rather than their price. ஆறெனும் அக்கரம், the six letters, is Murugan's six-syllable mantra, set against those potions. The list of shrines in the praise is unusual. The opening phrase நிறுக்கும் சூதன மெய்த்தன முண்டைகள் is hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","marundhu","mandhiram"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:8"},"p:74":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":74,"t":["பங்கமே வும்பிறப் பந்தகா ரந்தனிற்","பந்தபா சந்தனிற் ...... றடுமாறிப்","பஞ்சபா ணம்படப் புண்படா வஞ்சகப்","பண்பிலா டம்பரப் ...... பொதுமாதர்","தங்களா லிங்கனக் கொங்கையா கம்படச்","சங்கைமால் கொண்டிளைத் ...... தயராதே","தண்டைசூழ் கிண்கிணிப் புண்டாண கந்தனை","தந்துநீ யன்புவைத் ...... தருள்வாயே","அங்கைவேல் கொண்டரக் கன்ப்ரதா பங்கெடுத்","தண்டவே தண்டமுட் ...... படவேதான்","அஞ்சவே திண்டிறற் கொண்டலா கண்டலற்","கண்டலோ கங்கொடுத் ...... தருள்வோனே","திங்களார் கொன்றைமத் தந்துழாய் துன்றுபொற்","செஞ்சடா பஞ்சரத் ...... துறுதோகை","சிந்தையே தென்றிசைத் தென்றல்வீ சும்பொழிற்","செந்தில்வாழ் செந்தமிழ்ப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks that, stumbling in the darkness of defiled birth and its bonds, wounded by Kama's five arrows, he not waste away in doubt and delusion in the embraces of deceitful, showy common women, but be given Murugan's flower feet with anklets and bells, and his love. The praise names the vel that broke the demon's power and gave the world back to Indra, a picture of Siva's red matted hair with the moon, konrai, datura and tulasi, and Murugan of fine Tamil at Senthil, where the southern breeze blows through the groves.","n":"பிறப்பு அந்தகாரம், the darkness of birth, and பந்த பாசம, the binding bonds, set the women within the larger bondage of birth. Tulasi (துழாய்), Vishnu's plant, appears among the flowers in Siva's hair; what the peacock (தோகை) in that hair stands for is unclear to me.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","piravi","paasam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:8"},"p:75":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":75,"t":["பஞ்ச பாதக முறுபிறை யெயிறெரி","குஞ்சி கூர்விட மதர்விழி பிலவக","பங்க வாண்முக முடுகிய நெடுகிய ...... திரிசூலம்","பந்த பாசமு மருவிய கரதல","மிஞ்சி நீடிய கருமுகி லுருவொடு","பண்பி லாதொரு பகடது முதுகினில் ...... யமராஜன்","அஞ்ச வேவரு மவதர மதிலொரு","தஞ்ச மாகிய வழிவழி யருள்பெறும்","அன்பி னாலுன தடிபுக ழடிமையெ ...... னெதிரேநீ","அண்ட கோளகை வெடிபட இடிபட","எண்டி சாமுக மடமட நடமிடும்","அந்த மோகர மயிலினி லியலுடன் ...... வரவேணும்","மஞ்சு போல்வள ரளகமு மிளகிய","ரஞ்சி தாம்ருத வசனமு நிலவென","வந்த தூயவெண் முறுவலு மிருகுழை ...... யளவோடும்","மன்றல் வாரிச நயனமு மழகிய","குன்ற வாணர்த மடமகள் தடமுலை","மந்த ராசல மிசைதுயி லழகிய ...... மணவாளா","செஞ்சொல் மாதிசை வடதிசை குடதிசை","விஞ்சு கீழ்திசை சகலமு மிகல்செய்து","திங்கள் வேணியர் பலதளி தொழுதுயர் ...... மகமேரு","செண்டு மோதின ரரசரு ளதிபதி","தொண்ட ராதியும் வழிவழி நெறிபெறு","செந்தில் மாநக ரினிதுறை யமரர்கள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet draws Yama in full, with the five great sins, crescent fangs, fiery hair, poisoned eyes, trident and noose, black as a rain cloud on his graceless buffalo, and asks that at the hour of his coming Murugan come before him, his servant who praises the feet with a love received from generation to generation, on the peacock whose dance cracks the cosmic sphere and shakes the eight directions. The praise dwells on Valli's beauty, her cloud of hair, nectar speech and moonlit smile, with Murugan as the bridegroom asleep on her breast, and names him the king among kings who conquered every direction, worshipped at Siva's shrines and struck Mount Meru with a ball, at Senthil where devotees find the path from age to age.","n":"வழிவழி அருள்பெறும் அன்பினால் உனது அடிபுகழ் அடிமை: the claim on the Lord at death is hereditary service, the வழியடிமை of 34. மகமேரு செண்டு மோதின அரசருள் அதிபதி points to the story of the Pandya king who struck Meru with a ball, which is told of Murugan born as Ugra Pandya; the text gives the deed but not the name.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","thondu","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:8"},"p:76":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":76,"t":["படர்புவியின் மீது மீறி வஞ்சர்கள்","வியனினுரை பானு வாய்வி யந்துரை","பழுதில்பெரு சீல நூல்க ளுந்தெரி ...... சங்கபாடல்","பனுவல்கதை காவ்ய மாமெ ணெண்கலை","திருவளுவ தேவர் வாய்மை யென்கிற","பழமொழியை யோதி யேயு ணர்ந்துபல் ...... சந்தமாலை","மடல்பரணி கோவை யார்க லம்பக","முதலுளது கோடி கோள்ப்ர பந்தமும்","வகை வகையி லாசு சேர்பெ ருங்கவி ...... சண்டவாயு","மதுரகவி ராஜ னானென் வெண்குடை","விருதுகொடி தாள மேள தண்டிகை","வரிசையொடு லாவு மால கந்தைத ...... விர்ந்திடாதோ","அடல்பொருது பூச லேவி ளைந்திட","எதிர்பொரவொ ணாம லேக சங்கர","அரஹர சிவாம ஹாதெ வென்றுனி ...... அன்றுசேவித்","தவனிவெகு கால மாய்வ ணங்கியு","ளுருகிவெகு பாச கோச சம்ப்ரம","அதிபெல கடோ ர மாச லந்தர ...... னொந்துவீழ","உடல்தடியு மாழி தாவெ னம்புய","மலர்கள்தச நூறு தாளி டும்பக","லொருமலரி லாது கோவ ணிந்திடு ...... செங்கண்மாலுக்","குதவியம கேசர் பால இந்திரன்","மகளைமண மேவி வீறு செந்திலி","லுரியஅடி யேனை யாள வந்தருள் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet sets out his learning and fame as a poet: he has read the Sangam songs and books, stories and kavyas, the sixty-four arts, the truthful word of Thiruvalluvar and the proverbs, has made garlands of many rhythms, madal, parani, kovai and kalambakam, crores of prabandhas and extempore verse like a storm, and goes about as king of sweet poetry with white umbrella, titles, flags, drums and palanquin; he asks whether this pride will not be removed. The praise tells how Vishnu, unable to defeat the fierce Jalandhara, worshipped Siva with a thousand lotuses a day, and when one flower was missing offered his eye, and received the discus that cut Jalandhara's body; Murugan, son of that Mahesa, married Indra's daughter and came to rule the poet at Senthil.","n":"The song is a self-portrait of a court poet and a confession that the honours (வெண்குடை, விருது, கொடி, தண்டிகை) are pride (மால் அகந்தை). Among the learning listed is திருவள்ளுவ தேவர் வாய்மை, the Kural named as a classic. In the myth, Vishnu offering his eye to make up the missing lotus is my reading of கோ அணிந்திடு செங்கண்மால் and is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["agandhai","kalvi","thamizh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:8"},"p:77":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":77,"t":["பதும இருசரண் கும்பிட் டின்பக்","கலவி நலமிகுந் துங்கக் கொங்கைப்","பகடு புளகிதந் துன்றக் கன்றிக் ...... கயல்போலும்","பரிய கரியகண் செம்பொற் கம்பிக்","குழைகள் பொரமருண் டின்சொற் கொஞ்சிப்","பதற விதமுறுங் கந்துக் கொந்துக் ...... குழல்சாயப்","புதுமை நுதிநகம் பங்கத் தங்கத்","தினிது வரையவெண் சந்தத் திந்துப்","புருவ வெயர்வுடன் பொங்கக் கங்கைச் ...... சடைதாரி","பொடிசெய் தருள்மதன் தந்த்ரப் பந்திக்","கறிவை யிழவிடும் பண்புத் துன்பப்","பொருளின் மகளிர்தம் மன்புப் பண்பைத் ...... தவிரேனோ","திதிதி ததததந் திந்திந் தந்தட்","டிடிடி டடடடண் டிண்டிட் டண்டத்","தெனன தனதனந் தெந்தத் தந்தத் ...... தெனனானா","திகுர்தி தகிர்ததிந் திந்தித் திந்தித்","திரிரி தரரவென் றென்றொப் பின்றித்","திமிலை பறையறைந் தெண்டிக் கண்டச் ...... சுவர்சோரச்","சதியில் வருபெருஞ் சங்கத் தொங்கற்","புயவ சுரர்வெகுண் டஞ்சிக் குஞ்சித்","தலைகொ டடிபணிந் தெங்கட் குன்கட் ...... க்ருபைதாவென்","சமர குமரகஞ் சஞ்சுற் றுஞ்செய்ப்","பதியில் முருகமுன் பொங்கித் தங்கிச்","சலதி யலைபொருஞ் செந்திற் கந்தப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes lovemaking with women, bowing at their lotus feet, breasts thrilling, large dark eyes clashing with gold earrings, sweet lisping words, hair falling, new nail-marks and sweat on the brow, and asks whether he will not give up the 'love' of these women of wealth that makes men lose their sense in the snare of Kama, whom Siva burned to ash. The praise is a battle in drum syllables, with the demons bowing their heads to Murugan's feet and begging for his grace, and names him the Kumara of Seyppathi and Senthil by the sea.","n":"பொருளின் மகளிர்: women of wealth, the Kural's phrase for courtesans, and their அன்பு is the love the poet asks to give up. In the praise the defeated demons themselves ask for grace (எங்கட்கு உன் கட் க்ருபை தா), not only the gods. செய்ப்பதி, the field town, is probably Vayalur.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","varaivinmagalir","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:8"},"p:78":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":78,"t":["பரிமள களபசு கந்தச் சந்தத் ...... தனமானார்","படையம படையென அந்திக் குங்கட் ...... கடையாலே","வரியளி நிரைமுரல் கொங்குக் கங்குற் ...... குழலாலே","மறுகிடு மருளனை யின்புற் றன்புற் ...... றருள்வாயே","அரிதிரு மருகக டம்பத் தொங்கற் ...... றிருமார்பா","அலைகுமு குமுவென வெம்பக் கண்டித் ...... தெறிவேலா","திரிபுர தகனரும் வந்திக் குஞ்சற் ...... குருநாதா","ஜெயஜெய ஹரஹர செந்திற் கந்தப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A short song: the poet, bewildered by women of fragrant sandal-dressed breasts, by eye-corners they whet like weapons and by dark hair humming with bees, asks Murugan to grant him grace with joy and love. The praise names the nephew of Hari with the kadamba garland, the vel that made the sea boil, and the true guru whom the burner of the three cities worships, at Senthil.","n":"மறுகிடு மருளனை: the poet names himself the bewildered one; the remedy asked is grace given with joy (இன்புற்று அன்புற்று அருள்வாயே), as if the Lord's pleasure in giving were part of the gift. திரிபுர தகனரும் வந்திக்கும் சற்குருநாதா: Siva worships his son as teacher.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mayal","thiruvarul","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:8"},"p:79":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":79,"t":["பருத்தந்தத் தினைத்தந்திட்","டிருக்குங்கச் சடர்த்துந்திப்","பருக்கும்பொற் ப்ரபைக்குன்றத் ...... தனமானார்","பரிக்குந்துற் சரக்கொன்றத்","திளைத்தங்குற் பலப்பண்பைப்","பரக்குஞ்சக் கரத்தின்சத் ...... தியைநேரும்","துரைச்செங்கட் கடைக்கொன்றிப்","பெருத்தன்புற் றிளைத்தங்குத்","துணிக்கும்புத் தியைச்சங்கித் ...... தறியேனைத்","துணைச்செம்பொற் பதத்தின்புற்","றெனக்கென்றப் பொருட்டங்கத்","தொடுக்குஞ்சொற் றமிழ்த்தந்திப் ...... படியாள்வாய்","தருத்தங்கப் பொலத்தண்டத்","தினைக்கொண்டச் சுரர்க்கஞ்சத்","தடத்துன்பத் தினைத்தந்திட் ...... டெதிர்சூரன்","சமர்க்கெஞ்சிப் படித்துஞ்சக்","கதிர்த்துங்கத் தயிற்கொண்டத்","தலத்தும்பர்ப் பதிக்கன்புற் ...... றருள்வோனே","திருக்கஞ்சத் தனைக்கண்டித்","துறக்கங்குட் டிவிட்டுஞ்சற்","சிவக்கன்றப் பொருட்கொஞ்சிப் ...... பகர்வோனே","செயத்துங்கக் கொடைத்துங்கத்","திருத்தங்கித் தரிக்கும்பொற்","றிருச்செந்திற் பதிக்கந்தப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of women with breasts like a tusker's, whose red eye-corners hold a man until he grows weak, and calls himself one who never suspected the mind that cuts him; he asks Murugan to rule him by giving Tamil words that string together the meaning of his golden feet. The praise names Murugan who answered the gods' distress at Sura's hands and slew Sura with his vel for love of Indra, and who rebuked the lotus-born Brahma, struck him, and as Siva's true calf lisped that meaning to him, at Thiruchendur.","n":"எனக்கு என்று அப்பொருள் தங்கத் தொடுக்கும் சொல் தமிழ் தந்து: the plea is for the gift of song, as in 2 and 25. In the praise, திருக் கஞ்சத்தனைக் கண்டித்து ... குட்டி and சற்சிவக் கன்று அப்பொருள் கொஞ்சிப் பகர்வோனே give the Om story without naming Om. The dense rhythm makes much of the first half uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","thamizh","guru","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:8"},"p:80":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":80,"t":["பாத நூபுரம் பாடகஞ் சீர்கொள்நடை","யோதி மோகுலம் போலசம் போகமொடு","பாடி பாளிதங் காருகம் பாவையிடை ...... வஞ்சிபோலப்","பாகு பால்குடம் போலிரண் டானகுவ","டாட நீள்வடஞ் சேரலங் காரகுழல்","பாவ மேகபொன் சாபமிந் தேபொருவ ...... ரந்தமீதே","மாதர் கோகிலம் போல்கரும் பானமொழி","தோகை வாகர்கண் டாரைகொண் டாடிதகை","வாரும் வீடெயென் றோதிதம் பாயல்மிசை ...... யன்புளார்போல்","வாச பாசகஞ் சூதுபந் தாடஇழி","வேர்வை பாயசிந் தாகுகொஞ் சாரவிழி","வாகு தோள்கரஞ் சேர்வைதந் தாடுமவர் ...... சந்தமாமோ","தீத தோதகந் தீததிந் தோதிதிமி","டூடு டூடுடுண் டூடுடுண் டூடுடுடு","டீகு டீகுகம் போலவொண் பேரிமுர ...... சங்கள்வீறச்","சேடன் மேருவுஞ் சூரனுந் தாருகனும்","வீழ ஏழ்தடந் தூளிகொண் டாடமரர்","சேசெ சேசெயென் றாடநின் றாடிவிடு ...... மங்கிவேலா","தாதை காதிலங் கோதுசிங் காரமுக","மாறும் வாகுவுங் கூரசந் தானசுக","தாரி மார்பலங் காரியென் பாவைவளி ...... யெங்கள்மாதைத்","தாரு பாளிதஞ் சோரசிந் தாமணிக","ளாட வேபுணர்ந் தாடிவங் காரமொடு","தாழை வானுயர்ந் தாடுசெந் தூரிலுறை ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes the courtesans' charms from anklets to hair, their cuckoo voices welcoming whoever looks, 'come home', their bed where they seem loving, and their play with sweat and kisses, and asks whether union with them is fitting. The praise is a battle in drum syllables in which Sesha, Meru, Sura and Taraka fall and the gods dance, names the face that whispered into the father's ear, and ends with Murugan embracing Valli, 'my doll, our girl', at Senthil of tall screwpine.","n":"அன்புளார் போல், 'as if loving', and வாரும் வீடே, 'come home', are the two marks of the courtesan here. தாதை காதில் அங்கு ஓது is the teaching of Siva, whispered in his ear. என் பாவை வள்ளி எங்கள் மாதை sounds like the voice of Valli's own people, which suggests the praise briefly takes another speaker.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","punarchi","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:8"},"p:81":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":81,"t":["புகரப் புங்கப் பகரக் குன்றிற்","புயலிற் றங்கிப் ...... பொலிவோனும்","பொருவிற் றஞ்சச் சுருதிச் சங்கப்","பொருளைப் பண்பிற் ...... புகல்வோனும்","திகிரிச் செங்கட் செவியிற் றுஞ்சத்","திகிரிச் செங்கைத் ...... திருமாலும்","திரியப் பொங்கித் திரையற் றுண்டுட்","டெளிதற் கொன்றைத் ...... தரவேணும்","தகரத் தந்தச் சிகரத் தொன்றித்","தடநற் கஞ்சத் ...... துறைவோனே","தருணக் கொங்கைக் குறவிக் கின்பத்","தையளித் தன்புற் ...... றருள்வோனே","பகரப் பைம்பொற் சிகரக் குன்றைப்","படியிற் சிந்தத் ...... தொடும்வேலா","பவளத் துங்கப் புரிசைச் செந்திற்","பதியிற் கந்தப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks to be given the One that Indra on his spotted elephant amid the clouds, Brahma who expounds the Vedas, and Vishnu of the discus asleep on the serpent all wander in search of, so that, the waves stilled, he may taste it and grow clear within. The praise names Murugan who rides the ram and dwells in the lotus pool, who gave joy to the young hunter girl, and whose vel shattered the golden peak, at coral-walled Senthil.","n":"ஒன்றைத் தரவேணும்: what is asked for is named only as 'the one', the thing the three great gods fail to find. திரை அற்று, with the waves gone, and தெளிதல், becoming clear, make the goal a stilled and clarified mind. The identifications of the three gods rest on epithets (புகர், புயல், சுருதிச் சங்கப் பொருள், திகிரி) and the first lines are hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","upasaantham"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:9"},"p:82":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":82,"t":["பூரண வார கும்ப சீதப டீர கொங்கை","மாதர் விகார வஞ்ச லீலையி லேயு ழன்று","போதவ மேயி ழந்து போனது மான மென்ப ...... தறியாத","பூரிய னாகி நெஞ்சு காவல்ப டாத பஞ்ச","பாதக னாய றஞ்செ யாதடி யோடி றந்து","போனவர் வாழ்வு கண்டு மாசையி லேய ழுந்து ...... மயல்தீரக்","காரண காரி யங்க ளானதெ லாமொ ழிந்து","யானெனு மேதை விண்டு பாவக மாயி ருந்து","காலுட லுடி யங்கி நாசியின் மீதி ரண்டு ...... விழிபாயக்","காயமு நாவு நெஞ்சு மோர்வழி யாக அன்பு","காயம்வி டாம லுன்ற னீடிய தாள்நி னைந்து","காணுதல் கூர்த வஞ்செய் யோகிக ளாய்வி ளங்க ...... அருள்வாயே","ஆரண சார மந்த்ர வேதமெ லாம்வி ளங்க","ஆதிரை யானை நின்று தாழ்வனெ னாவ ணங்கு","மாதர வால்வி ளங்கு பூரண ஞான மிஞ்சு ...... முரவோனே","ஆர்கலி யூடெ ழுந்து மாவடி வாகி நின்ற","சூரனை மாள வென்று வானுல காளு மண்ட","ரானவர் கூர ரந்தை தீரமு னாள்ம கிழ்ந்த ...... முருகேசா","வாரண மூல மென்ற போதினி லாழி கொண்டு","வாவியின் மாடி டங்கர் பாழ்பட வேயெ றிந்த","மாமுகில் போலி ருண்ட மேனிய னாமு குந்தன் ...... மருகோனே","வாலுக மீது வண்ட லோடிய காலில் வந்து","சூல்நிறை வான சங்கு மாமணி யீன வுந்து","வாரிதி நீர்ப ரந்த சீரலை வாயு கந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses that, wandering in the deceitful play of women with breasts like full cool pots, he lost his wisdom and did not know shame, became base, left his heart unguarded, did no dharma and sank in desire even watching the lives of those who had died; to end that delusion he asks that cause and effect fall away, the self that says 'I' break, both eyes rest on the tip of the nose with breath and fire held, body, speech and heart go one way, and that, thinking of the feet, he shine as a yogi of seeing. The praise names Murugan, full of perfect knowledge, to whom Siva bowed saying 'I am lower', the slayer of Sura risen from the sea in a vast form, and the nephew of Mukunda who hurled the discus at the crocodile when the elephant cried 'Root!', at Seeralaivay where conches give birth to pearls on the sand.","n":"The remedy for infatuation is a yogic programme: யானெனும் மேதை விண்டு (the 'I' broken), நாசியின் மீது இரண்டு விழிபாய (the gaze on the nose tip), காயமும் நாவும் நெஞ்சும் ஓர் வழியாக (body, speech and mind made one). ஆதிரையான் is Siva, whose star is Thiruvathirai; his saying நின்று தாழ்வன் makes the father bow to his son as teacher. A garbled word in line 9 (காலுட லுடி) is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","mayal","thavam","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:9"},"p:83":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":83,"t":["பெருக்கச்சஞ் சலித்துக்கந்","தலுற்றுப்புந் தியற்றுப்பின்","பிழைப்பற்றுங் குறைப்புற்றும் ...... பொதுமாதர்","ப்ரியப்பட்டங் கழைத்துத்தங்","கலைக்குட்டங் கிடப்பட்சம்","பிணித்துத்தந் தனத்தைத்தந் ...... தணையாதே","புரக்கைக்குன் பதத்தைத்தந்","தெனக்குத்தொண் டுறப்பற்றும்","புலத்துக்கண் செழிக்கச்செந் ...... தமிழ்பாடும்","புலப்பட்டங் கொடுத்தற்கும்","கருத்திற்கண் படக்கிட்டும்","புகழ்ச்சிக்குங் க்ருபைச்சித்தம் ...... புரிவாயே","தருக்கிக்கண் களிக்கத்தெண்","டனிட்டுத்தண் புனத்திற்செங்","குறத்திக்கன் புறச்சித்தந் ...... தளர்வோனே","சலிப்புற்றங் குரத்திற்சம்","ப்ரமித்துக்கொண் டலைத்துத்தன்","சமர்த்திற்சங் கரிக்கத்தண் ...... டியசூரன்","சிரத்தைச்சென் றறுத்துப்பந்","தடித்துத்திண் குவட்டைக்கண்","டிடித்துச்செந் திலிற்புக்கங் ...... குறைவோனே","சிறக்கற்கஞ் செழுத்தத்தந்","திருச்சிற்றம் பலத்தத்தன்","செவிக்குப்பண் புறச்செப்பும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks that, agitated, grieved, without sense or livelihood, he not lie in the laps of common women who call him with pretended love and bind him; instead, he asks, give your feet to protect me, the title of a poet who sings fine Tamil so that service flourishes, and the renown that comes to the mind. The praise names Murugan who fell at the hunter girl's feet in the millet field, cut off Sura's head and struck the hill, and spoke into the ear of the father of Thiruchitrambalam, apparently the five letters.","n":"The plea asks for honours (புலப்பட்டம், புகழ்ச்சி) but for the sake of service: எனக்குத் தொண்டுறப் பற்றும் புலத்துக் கண் செழிக்கச் செந்தமிழ் பாடும். In 76 the poet's titles are pride; here they are asked for as tools of devotion. சிறக் கற்கும் அஞ்செழுத்து அத் தந்து, if so divided, makes Murugan teach Siva of Chidambaram his own five-letter mantra; the division is uncertain. As in 71, Murugan prostrates before Valli (தெண்டனிட்டு).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","thondu","thamizh","panchaakkaram"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:9"},"p:84":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":84,"t":["மங்கை சிறுவர் தங்கள் கிளைஞர்","வந்து கதற ...... வுடல்தீயின்","மண்டி யெரிய விண்டு புனலில்","வஞ்ச மொழிய ...... விழஆவி","வெங்கண் மறலி தன்கை மருவ","வெம்பி யிடறு ...... மொருபாச","விஞ்சை விளைவு மன்று னடிமை","வென்றி யடிகள் ...... தொழவாராய்","சிங்க முழுவை தங்கு மடவி","சென்று மறமி ...... னுடன்வாழ்வாய்","சிந்தை மகிழ அன்பர் புகழு","செந்தி லுறையு ...... முருகோனே","எங்கு மிலகு திங்கள் கமல","மென்று புகலு ...... முகமாதர்","இன்பம் விளைய அன்பி னணையு","மென்று மிளைய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet pictures his death, wife, children and kin wailing, the body burning in the fire, what is left cast into water, and the life in the hand of cruel-eyed Death, and asks Murugan to come so that he, his servant, may worship his victorious feet. The praise names Murugan who went into the forest of lions and tigers and lived with the hunter girl, the one at Senthil whom devotees praise, the ever-young lord whom women with faces like moon and lotus embrace.","n":"The scene runs past the moment of death to the cremation and the casting of the ashes into water (விண்டு புனலில் வஞ்சம் ஒழிய விழ), an unusual length for the plea. ஒரு பாச விஞ்சை விளைவும் அன்று is obscure; it may say that what happens is no work of magic or bond, or be divided otherwise.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kootru","saakkaadu","thondu","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:9"},"p:85":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":85,"t":["மஞ்செ னுங்குழ லும்பிறை யம்புரு","வங்க ளென்சிலை யுங்கணை யங்கயல்","வண்டு புண்டரி கங்களை யும்பழி ......சிந்துபார்வை","மண்ட லஞ்சுழ லுஞ்செவி யங்குழை","தங்க வண்டர ளம்பதி யும்பலு","மண்ட லந்திக ழுங்கமு கஞ்சிறு ...... கண்டமாதர்","கஞ்சு கங்குர லுங்கழை யம்புய","கொங்கை செங்கிரி யும்பவ ளம்பொறி","கந்த சந்தன மும்பொலி யுந்துகில் ...... வஞ்சிசேருங்","கஞ்ச மண்டுளி னின்றிர சம்புகு","கண்ப டர்ந்திட ரம்பையெ னுந்தொடை","கண்கை யஞ்சர ணஞ்செயல் வஞ்சரை ...... நம்புவேனோ","சஞ்ச சஞ்சக ணஞ்சக டுண்டுடு","டுண்டு டிண்டிமி டண்டம டுண்டுடு","தந்த னந்தன திந்திமி சங்குகள் ...... பொங்குதாரை","சம்பு வின்கும ரன்புல வன்பொரு","கந்த னென்றிடு துந்தமி யுந்துவ","சங்க ளங்கொளி ருங்குடை யுந்திசை ...... விஞ்சவேகண்","டஞ்ச வஞ்சசு ரன்திர ளுங்குவ","டன்ற டங்கலும் வெந்துபொ ரிந்திட","அண்ட ரிந்திர னுஞ்சர ணம்புக ...... வென்றவேளே","அம்பு யந்தண ரம்பைகு றிஞ்சியின்","மங்கை யங்குடில் மங்கையொ டன்புடன்","அண்ட ருந்தொழு செந்திலி லின்புறு ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet gives a head-to-foot catalogue of women, cloud hair, crescent brow, bow-like eyebrows, eyes that shame the carp, the bee and the lotus, earrings, pearl teeth, areca-like neck, breasts like hills, coral lips and thighs like plantain stems, and asks whether he will trust these deceivers in eye, hand, foot and act. The praise is a battle in drum syllables with conches and trumpets proclaiming the son of Sambhu, the poet, Kandan, as Sura's host and his hill burn and Indra takes refuge, and names Murugan who delights at Senthil with the girl of the Kurinji hut.","n":"The whole first half is the conventional blazon of beauty, each part matched to its comparison, and the question only comes at the end: செயல் வஞ்சரை நம்புவேனோ. Among Murugan's titles proclaimed by the drums is புலவன், the poet.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","nalam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:9"},"p:86":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":86,"t":["மனத்தின்பங் கெனத்தங்கைம்","புலத்தென்றன் குணத்தஞ்சிந்","த்ரியத்தம்பந் தனைச்சிந்தும் ...... படிகாலன்","மலர்ச்செங்கண் கனற்பொங்குந்","திறத்தின்தண் டெடுத்தண்டங்","கிழித்தின்றிங் குறத்தங்கும் ...... பலவோரும்","எனக்கென்றிங் குனக்கேன்றங்","கினத்தின்கண் கணக்கென்றென்","றிளைத்தன்புங் கெடுத்தங்கங் ...... கழிவாமுன்","இசைக்குஞ்செந் தமிழ்க்கொண்டங்","கிரக்கும்புன் றொழிற்பங்கங்","கெடத்துன்பங் கழித்தின்பந் ...... தருவாயே","கனைக்குந்தண் கடற்சங்கங்","கரத்தின்கண் தரித்தெங்குங்","கலக்கஞ்சிந் திடக்கண்துஞ் ...... சிடுமாலும்","கதித்தொண்பங் கயத்தன்பண்","பனைத்துங்குன் றிடச்சந்தங்","களிக்குஞ்சம் புவுக்குஞ்செம் ...... பொருளீவாய்","தினைக்குன்றந் தனிற்றங்குஞ்","சிறுப்பெண்குங் குமக்கும்பந்","திருச்செம்பொன் புயத்தென்றும் ...... புனைவோனே","செழிக்குங்குண் டகழ்ச்சங்கங்","கொழிக்குஞ்சந் தனத்தின்பைம்","பொழிற்றண்செந் திலிற்றங்கும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks that before Death, his flower-red eyes blazing, raises his club and tears him away, before the kin who live here wear out their love quarrelling 'this is mine, why yours?' over their shares, and before the body passes, Murugan end the sorrow of his base trade of begging with sung Tamil and give him joy. The praise names Murugan who gave the true meaning to Sambhu while Vishnu, conch in hand, slept and Brahma's wisdom failed, who wears the hunter girl's kumkum on his golden arms, at Senthil of moats and sandalwood groves.","n":"இசைக்கும் செந்தமிழ்க் கொண்டு அங்கு இரக்கும் புன்தொழில்: the poet's Tamil is named as the means of his begging, the confession of 69 and 71. The kin dividing the dying man's goods (எனக்கு என்று இங்கு உனக்கு ஏன்று) is a sharp detail. The opening lines on the senses are hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kootru","iravu","thamizh","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:9"},"p:87":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":87,"t":["மனைகனக மைந்தர் தமதழகு பெண்டிர்","வலிமைகுல நின்ற ...... நிலையூர்பேர்","வளரிளமை தஞ்ச முனைபுனைவ ளங்கள்","வரிசைதம ரென்று ...... வருமாயக்","கனவுநிலை யின்ப மதனையென தென்று","கருதிவிழி யின்ப ...... மடவார்தம்","கலவிமயல் கொண்டு பலவுடல்பு ணர்ந்து","கருவில்விழு கின்ற ...... தியல்போதான்","நினையுநின தன்பர் பழவினைக ளைந்து","நெடுவரைபி ளந்த ...... கதிர்வேலா","நிலமுதல் விளங்கு நலமருவு செந்தில்","நிலைபெறஇ ருந்த ...... முருகோனே","புனைமலர்பு னைந்த புனமறம டந்தை","புளகஇரு கொங்கை ...... புணர்மார்பா","பொருதுடனெ திர்ந்த நிருதர்மகு டங்கள்","பொடிபடந டந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet lists house, gold, sons, beautiful wife, strength, family standing, town and name, youth, ornaments, ranks and kin, and asks whether it is right to take the pleasure of this passing dream as his own, lie with women, join many bodies and fall again into the womb. The praise names Murugan who removes the old deeds of devotees who think of him, whose vel split the great mountain, who stays at Senthil, and who embraced the hunter maiden.","n":"மாயக் கனவுநிலை இன்பம் அதனை எனது என்று கருதி: the error is the claim 'mine' over a dream. The list moves from goods to people to rank, then to women, and ends at the womb (கருவில் விழுகின்றது): attachment's end is rebirth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","pattru","piravi","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:9"},"p:88":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":88,"t":["மாய வாடைதி மிர்ந்திடு கொங்கையில்","மூடு சீலைதி றந்தம ழுங்கிகள்","வாசல் தொறுந டந்துசி ணுங்கிகள் ...... பழையோர்மேல்","வால நேசநி னைந்தழு வம்பிகள்","ஆசை நோய்கொள்ம ருந்திடு சண்டிகள்","வாற பேர்பொருள் கண்டுவி ரும்பிக ...... ளெவரேனும்","நேய மேகவி கொண்டுசொல் மிண்டிகள்","காசி லாதவர் தங்களை யன்பற","நீதி போலநெ கிழ்ந்தப றம்பிக ...... ளவர்தாய்மார்","நீலி நாடக மும்பயில் மண்டைகள்","பாளை யூறுக ளுண்டிடு தொண்டிகள்","நீச ரோடுமி ணங்குக டம்பிக ...... ளுறவாமோ","பாயு மாமத தந்திமு கம்பெறு","மாதி பாரத மென்ற பெருங்கதை","பார மேருவி லன்று வரைந்தவ ...... னிளையோனே","பாவை யாள்குற மங்கை செழுந்தன","பார மீதில ணைந்து முயங்கிய","பாக மாகிய சந்தன குங்கும ...... மணிமார்பா","சீய மாயுரு வங்கொடு வந்தசு","ரேசன் மார்பையி டந்து பசுங்குடர்","சேர வாரிய ணிந்த நெடும்புயன் ...... மருகோனே","தேனு லாவுக டம்ப மணிந்தகி","஡ணட சேகர சங்கரர் தந்தருள்","தேவ நாயக செந்திலு கந்தருள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet heaps names on the courtesans: women who open the cloth over scented breasts, whimper at every door, feign a childhood love for old lovers, sell remedies for love-sickness, want whatever wealth comes, coldly drop men without money, have mothers who play the dark-goddess drama, drink palm toddy and mix with the low, and asks whether kinship with them is fitting. The praise names Murugan as the younger brother of the elephant-faced one who wrote the Bharata on Meru, Valli's lover, the nephew of Vishnu who came as a lion and tore open the demon king's chest and wore his entrails, and the lord of the gods given by Sankara, at Senthil.","n":"The catalogue of abuse (மழுங்கிகள், சிணுங்கிகள், வம்பிகள், சண்டிகள், மிண்டிகள், பறம்பிகள், தொண்டிகள், கடம்பிகள்) runs on one suffix, a set feature of the book. அவர் தாய்மார் நீலி நாடகமும் பயில் மண்டைகள: the mothers are part of the trade, as in Naladiyar's courtesan chapter. The praise names Ganesa writing the Mahabharata on Meru and the Narasimha story outright.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["varaivinmagalir","selvam","kal","vanjam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:9"},"p:89":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":89,"t":["மான்போற்கண் பார்வை பெற்றிடு","மூஞ்சாற்பண் பாடு மக்களை","வாய்ந்தாற்பொன் கோடு செப்பெனு ...... முலைமாதர்","வாங்காத்திண் டாடு சித்திர","நீங்காச்சங் கேத முக்கிய","வாஞ்சாற்செஞ் சாறு மெய்த்திடு ...... மொழியாலே","ஏன்காற்பங் காக நற்புறு","பூங்காற்கொங் காரு மெத்தையில்","ஏய்ந்தாற்பொன் சாரு பொற்பண ...... முதல்நீதா","ஈந்தாற்கன் றோர மிப்பென","ஆன்பாற்றென் போல செப்பிடும்","ஈண்டாச்சம் போக மட்டிக ...... ளுறவாமோ","கான்பாற்சந் தாடு பொற்கிரி","தூம்பாற்பைந் தோளி கட்கடை","காண்பாற்றுஞ் சாமல் நத்திடும் ...... அசுரேசன்","காம்பேய்ப்பந் தாட விக்ரம","வான்றோய்க்கெம் பீர விற்கணை","காண்டேர்க்கொண் டேவு மச்சுதன் ...... மருகோனே","தீம்பாற்கும் பாகு சர்க்கரை","காம்பாற்செந் தேற லொத்துரை","தீர்ந்தார்க்கங் காளி பெற்றருள் ...... புதல்வோனே","தீண்பார்க்குன் போத முற்றுற","மாண்டார்க்கொண் டோ து முக்கிய","தேன்போற்செந் தூரில் மொய்த்தருள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of women with doe eyes and breasts like golden peaks, whose speech drips like cane juice, who on the flowered bed say 'first give the gold and coins' and, once given, talk as sweet as milk and honey, and asks whether kinship with these fools of pleasure is fitting. The praise names Vishnu, Achyuta, who fought the demon king with bow and arrows, as uncle, Murugan as the son born to Kali for those who have left behind talk sweet as milk, syrup and sugar, and as honey at Senthil where devotees swarm.","n":"பொற்பணம் முதல் நீ தா, ஈந்தால் ... பால் தேன் போல செப்பிடும்: the payment comes first and the sweet words after, the sequence the Kural's courtesan chapter condemns. The rhythm, with long syllables at every foot, splits words heavily and much of the praise is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:9"},"p:90":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":90,"t":["முகிலாமெனு மளகங் காட்டி","மதிபோலுயர் நுதலுங் காட்டி","முகிழாகிய நகையுங் காட்டி ...... அமுதூறு","மொழியாகிய மதுரங் காட்டி","விழியாகிய கொடியுங் காட்டி","முகமாகிய கமலங் காட்டி ...... மலைபோலே","வகையாமிள முலையுங் காட்டி","யிடையாகிய கொடியுங் காட்டி","வளமானகை வளையுங் காட்டி ...... யிதமான","மணிசேர்கடி தடமுங் காட்டி","மிகவேதொழி லதிகங் காட்டு","மடமாதர்கள் மயலின் சேற்றி ...... லுழல்வேனே","நகையால்மத னுருவந் தீத்த","சிவனாரருள் சுதனென் றார்க்கு","நலநேயரு ளமர்செந் தூர்க்கு ...... ளுறைவோனே","நவமாமணி வடமும் பூத்த","தனமாதெனு மிபமின் சேர்க்கை","நழுவாவகை பிரியங் காட்டு ...... முருகோனே","அகமேவிய நிருதன் போர்க்கு","வரவேசமர் புரியுந் தோற்ற","மறியாமலு மபயங் காட்டி ...... முறைகூறி","அயிராவத முதுகின் தோற்றி","யடையாமென இனிதன் பேத்து","மமரேசனை முழுதுங் காத்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet lists what the young women show, cloud-like hair, moon-like brow, budding smile, nectar speech, creeper eyes, lotus face, breasts like hills, creeper waist, bangled hands and jewelled hips, and above all the practised arts, and confesses that he wallows in the mire of infatuation with them, without asking for anything. The praise names Murugan the son of Siva who burned Kama's form with a smile, who shows love so that his union with the elephant's maiden never slips, and who showed refuge to Indra on Airavata's back and protected him wholly.","n":"Each line repeats காட்டி, 'showing', so that the women's beauty is a display, and the confession ends without a request: மயலின் சேற்றில் உழல்வேனே. இபமின், the elephant's maiden, is Devayanai, raised by Airavata.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mayal","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:9"},"p:91":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":91,"t":["முந்துதமிழ் மாலை கோடிக் கோடி","சந்தமொடு நீடு பாடிப் பாடி","முஞ்சர்மனை வாசல் தேடித் தேடி ...... யுழலாதே","முந்தைவினை யேவ ராமற் போக","மங்கையர்கள் காதல் தூரத் தேக","முந்தடிமை யேனை யாளத் தானு ...... முனைமீதே","திந்திதிமி தோதி தீதித் தீதி","தந்ததன தான தானத் தான","செஞ்செணகு சேகு தாளத் தோடு ...... நடமாடுஞ்","செஞ்சிறிய கால்வி சாலத் தோகை","துங்கஅநு கூல பார்வைத் தீர","செம்பொன்மயில் மீதி லேயெப் போது ...... வருவாயே","அந்தண்மறை வேள்வி காவற் கார","செந்தமிழ்சொல் பாவின் மாலைக் கார","அண்டருப கார சேவற் கார ...... முடிமேலே","அஞ்சலிசெய் வோர்கள் நேயக் கார","குன்றுருவ ஏவும் வேலைக் கார","அந்தம்வெகு வான ரூபக் கார ...... எழிலான","சிந்துரமின் மேவு போகக் கார","விந்தைகுற மாது வேளைக் கார","செஞ்சொலடி யார்கள் வாரக் கார ...... எதிரான","செஞ்சமரை மாயு மாயக் கார","துங்கரண சூர சூறைக் கார","செந்தினகர் வாழு மாண்மைக் கார ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks that, no longer wandering to the doors of fools singing crores of rhythmic Tamil garlands, with past deeds gone and love of women far away, he, the old servant, be ruled by Murugan, who will come at the decisive hour on the golden peacock that dances to the drum. The praise is a long string of titles ending in காரன்: guardian of the Vedic sacrifice, master of Tamil verse, helper of the gods, lord of the cock, lover of those who fold hands over their heads, wielder of the vel that pierced the hill, lover of the elephant's maiden and of the hunter girl, friend of devotees of fine speech, and destroyer of Sura, at Senthil.","n":"முந்து தமிழ் மாலை கோடிக் கோடி ... முஞ்சர் மனை வாசல் தேடித் தேடி: the confession of begging with poetry again, now with the poet's verse called garlands. The praise answers it: Murugan is himself செந்தமிழ் சொல் பாவின் மாலைக் காரன், the one to whom such garlands belong.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","thondu","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:9"},"p:92":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":92,"t":["முலைமு கந்தி மிர்ந்த கலவை யுந்து லங்கு","முறுவ லுஞ்சி வந்த ...... கனிவாயும்","முருக விழ்ந்து திர்ந்த மலர்க ளுஞ்ச ரிந்த","முகிலு மின்ப சிங்கி ...... விழிவேலும்","சிலைமு கங்க லந்த திலத முங்கு ளிர்ந்த","திருமு கந்த தும்பு ...... குறுவேர்வும்","தெரிய வந்து நின்ற மகளிர் பின்சு ழன்று","செயல ழிந்து ழன்று ...... திரிவேனோ","மலைமு கஞ்சு மந்த புலவர் செஞ்சொல் கொண்டு","வழிதி றந்த செங்கை ...... வடிவேலா","வளர்பு னம்ப யின்ற குறம டந்தை கொங்கை","மணிவ டம்பு தைந்த ...... புயவேளே","அலைமு கந்த வழ்ந்து சினைமு திர்ந்த சங்க","மலறி வந்து கஞ்ச ...... மலர்மீதே","அளிக லந்தி ரங்க இசையு டன்து யின்ற","அரிய செந்தில் வந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet pictures women with sandal-dressed breasts, smiles, red lips, flowers falling from their hair, spear eyes, tilaka on the brow and beads of sweat on the face, and asks whether he will wander after them, his acts undone. The praise names the vel that opened a way out for the poets shut in the mountain at their fine words, the arms embraced by the hunter girl's breasts, and Senthil, where conches cry from the waves onto the lotus and bees sing.","n":"செயல் அழிந்து உழன்று: infatuation undoes one's own agency, where in 22 the undoing of one's agency was the goal. மலைமுகம் சுமந்த புலவர் செஞ்சொல் கொண்டு வழிதிறந்த செங்கை வடிவேலா points to the story of Nakkirar and the poets held in a mountain cave, freed by the vel when he sang; the text gives the details but not the name.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","mayal"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:9"},"p:93":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":93,"t":["மூப்புற் றுச்செவி கேட்பற் றுப்பெரு","மூச்சுற் றுச்செயல் ...... தடுமாறி","முர்க்கச் சொற்குரல் காட்டிக் கக்கிட","மூக்குக் குட்சளி ...... யிளையோடும்","கோப்புக் கட்டியி னாப்பிச் செற்றிடு","கூட்டிற் புக்குயி ...... ரலையாமுன்","கூற்றத் தத்துவ நீக்கிப் பொற்கழல்","கூட்டிச் சற்றருள் ...... புரிவாயே","காப்புப் பொற்கிரி கோட்டிப் பற்றலர்","காப்பைக் கட்டவர் ...... குருநாதா","காட்டுக் குட்குற வாட்டிக் குப்பல","காப்புக் குத்திர ...... மொழிவோனே","வாய்ப்புற் றத்தமிழ் மார்க்கத் திட்பொருள்","வாய்க்குச் சித்திர ...... முருகோனே","வார்த்தைச் சிற்பர தீர்த்தச் சுற்றலை","வாய்க்குட் பொற்பமர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet pictures old age, hearing gone, heavy breathing, faltering acts, harsh words, vomiting, phlegm in the nose, and asks that before his life wanders out of its cage, Murugan remove the reality of Death, join him to the golden feet and grant a little grace. The praise names Murugan as guru to Siva who bent the golden mountain against his foes, the one who spoke many sly words to the hunter girl in the forest, the Murugan in whose mouth lies the meaning of the Tamil way, at Senthil of the holy waters.","n":"கூற்றத் தத்துவம் நீக்கி: what is asked is not only rescue at death but the removal of Death's principle. வாய்ப்புற்ற தமிழ் மார்க்கத்து இட்பொருள் வாய்க்குச் சித்திர முருகோனே makes Murugan the master of Tamil's meaning.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","kootru","thiruvadi","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:9"},"p:94":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":94,"t":["மூளும்வினை சேர மேல்கொண்டி டாஐந்து","பூதவெகு வாய மாயங்கள் தானெஞ்சில்","முடிநெறி நீதி யேதுஞ்செ யாவஞ்சி ...... யதிபார","மோகநினை வான போகஞ்செய் வேனண்டர்","தேடஅரி தாய ஞேயங்க ளாய்நின்ற","முலபர யோக மேல்கொண் டிடாநின்ற ...... துளதாகி","நாளுமதி வேக கால்கொண்டு தீமண்ட","வாசியன லு஡டு போயொன்றி வானின்க","ணாமமதி மீதி லு஡றுங்க லாஇன்ப ...... அமுதூறல்","நாடியதன் மீது போய்நின்ற அநந்த","மேலைவெளி யேறி நீயின்றி நானின்றி","நாடியினும் வேறு தானின்றி வாழ்கின்ற ...... தொருநாளே","காளவிட மூணி மாதங்கி வேதஞ்சொல்","பேதைநெடு நீலி பாதங்க ளால்வந்த","காலன்விழ மோது சாமுண்டி பாரம்பொ ...... டனல்வாயு","காதிமுதிர் வான மேதங்கி வாழ்வஞ்சி","ஆடல்விடை யேறி பாகங்கு லாமங்கை","காளிநட மாடி நாளன்பர் தாம்வந்து ...... தொழுமாது","வாளமுழு தாளு மோர்தண்டு ழாய்தங்கு","சோதிமணி மார்ப மாலின்பி னாளின்சொல்","வாழுமுமை மாத ராள்மைந்த னேயெந்தை ...... யிளையோனே","மாசிலடி யார்கள் வாழ்கின்ற வூர்சென்று","தேடிவிளை யாடி யேயங்ங னேநின்று","வாழுமயில் வீர னேசெந்தில் வாழ்கின்ற ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet, who has done no just deed and enjoyed delusion amid the illusions of the five elements, asks when the one day will come when, taking up the root yoga that the gods cannot find, the breath driven fast through the fire, rising to the moon in the head where the nectar of bliss wells, going up through the nadi to the space beyond, he will live where there is neither you nor I nor even the nadi. The praise is given to the goddess, the drinker of poison, Matangi who speaks the Veda, the dark Nili, Chamundi who struck down Death with her feet, the one who dwells in fire, wind and sky, the dancing Kali, partner of the rider of the bull and Vishnu's younger sister, and names Murugan as her son, who went to the towns of his devotees and played there, the peacock hero of Senthil.","n":"The plea is a full account of yogic ascent: வாசி (breath) through அனல் (fire) to மதி (the moon), where the இன்ப அமுது ஊறல் flows, and then மேலைவெளி, the space above. The goal, நீ இன்றி நான் இன்றி, is beyond the duality of devotee and Lord. The praise, nearly all of it on Uma as Shakti, gives her the deed of kicking Death (காலன் விழ மோது சாமுண்டி).","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["yogam","praanan","amudham","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:9"},"p:95":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":95,"t":["வஞ்சங்கொண் டுந்திட ராவண","னும்பந்தென் திண்பரி தேர்கரி","மஞ்சின்பண் புஞ்சரி யாமென ...... வெகுசேனை","வந்தம்பும் பொங்கிய தாகஎ","திர்ந்துந்தன் சம்பிர தாயமும்","வம்புந்தும் பும்பல பேசியு ...... மெதிரேகை","மிஞ்சென்றுஞ் சண்டைசெய் போதுகு","ரங்குந்துஞ் சுங்கனல் போலவே","குண்டுங்குன் றுங்கர டார்மர ...... மதும்வீசி","மிண்டுந்துங் கங்களி னாலெத","கர்ந்தங்கங் கங்கர மார்பொடு","மின்சந்துஞ் சிந்தநி சாசரர் ...... வகைசேர","வுஞ்சண்டன் தென்றிசை நாடிவி","ழுந்தங்குஞ் சென்றெம தூதர்க","ளுந்துந்துந் தென்றிட வேதசை ...... நிண்முளை","உண்டுங்கண் டுஞ்சில கூளிகள்","டிண்டிண்டென் றுங்குதி போடவு","யர்ந்தம்புங் கொண்டுவெல் மாதவன் ...... மருகோனே","தஞ்சந்தஞ் சஞ்சிறி யேன்மதி","கொஞ்சங்கொஞ் சந்துரை யேயருள்","தந்தென்றின் பந்தரு வீடது ...... தருவாயே","சங்கங்கஞ் சங்கயல் சூழ்தட","மெங்கெங்கும் பொங்கம காபுநி","தந்தங்குஞ் செந்திலில் வாழ்வுயர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"Most of the song is a battle: Ravana comes with his army of horses, chariots and elephants, boasts and fights, the monkeys hurl rocks and trees, the demons are torn apart, Yama's messengers drive them south and ghouls feast on flesh and marrow, and Vishnu wins with his arrows. The poet then calls 'refuge, refuge', asks the Lord to give his small mind grace little by little and to grant the house of bliss, at Senthil of ponds full of conches, lotuses and fish.","n":"The plea comes only at the end and is brief: தஞ்சம் தஞ்சம் ... இன்பம் தரு வீடு அது தருவாயே. Uncommonly, the battle in the praise is Rama's against Ravana rather than Murugan's against Sura.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["veedu","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:9"},"p:96":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":96,"t":["வஞ்சத் துடனொரு நெஞ்சிற் பலநினை","வஞ்சிக் கொடியிடை ...... மடவாரும்","வந்திப் புதல்வரும் அந்திக் கிளைஞரு","மண்டிக் கதறிடு ...... வகைகூர","அஞ்சக் கலைபடு பஞ்சிப் புழுவுடல்","அங்கிக் கிரையென ...... வுடன்மேவ","அண்டிப் பயமுற வென்றிச் சமன்வரும்","அன்றைக் கடியிணை ...... தரவேணும்","கஞ்சப் பிரமனை அஞ்சத் துயர்செய்து","கன்றச் சிறையிடு ...... மயில்வீரா","கண்டொத் தனமொழி அண்டத் திருமயில்","கண்டத் தழகிய ...... திருமார்பா","செஞ்சொற் புலவர்கள் சங்கத் தமிழ்தெரி","செந்திற் பதிநக ...... ருறைவோனே","செம்பொற் குலவட குன்றைக் கடலிடை","சிந்தப் பொரவல ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet pictures his death: women who think many things in one deceitful heart, the sons and the kin gathered at evening crowd round wailing, the worm-like body wrapped in cloth goes to be food for the fire, and victorious Yama comes near to terrify; on that day, he asks, give your pair of feet. The praise names the peacock hero who made lotus-born Brahma suffer and imprisoned him, the chest that the sweet-voiced lady embraces, Senthil where poets of fine speech weigh the Tamil of the Sangam, and the one who fought so that the golden northern mountain scattered into the sea.","n":"The plea is the pilot's hour-of-death formula: அண்டிப் பயமுற வென்றிச் சமன் வரும் அன்றைக்கு அடியிணை தரவேணும். The wailing wife, sons and kin (மடவார், புதல்வர், கிளைஞர்) are the household that cannot help at death, and the body is பஞ்சிப் புழுவுடல் அங்கிக்கு இரை, a worm-body for the fire. Brahma's imprisonment appears without the Om teaching. The line on the chest (கண்டொத்த அனமொழி அண்டத் திருமயில் கண்டத்து அழகிய) is hard to split; 'a woman with speech like sugar candy' is the likely sense.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kootru","thiruvadi","sutram","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:10"},"p:97":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":97,"t":["வந்து வந்து முன்த வழ்ந்து","வெஞ்சு கந்த யங்க நின்று","மொஞ்சி மொஞ்சி யென்ற ழுங்கு ...... ழந்தையோடு","மண்ட லங்கு லுங்க அண்டர்","விண்ட லம்பி ளந்தெ ழுந்த","செம்பொன் மண்ட பங்க ளும்ப ...... யின்றவீடு","கொந்த ளைந்த குந்த ளந்த","ழைந்து குங்கு மந்த யங்கு","கொங்கை வஞ்சி தஞ்ச மென்று ...... மங்குகாலம்","கொங்க டம்பு கொங்கு பொங்கு","பைங்க டம்பு தண்டை கொஞ்சு","செஞ்ச தங்கை தங்கு பங்க ...... யங்கள்தாராய்","சந்த டர்ந்தெ ழுந்த ரும்பு","மந்த ரஞ்செ ழுங்க ரும்பு","கந்த ரம்பை செண்ப தங்கொள் ...... செந்தில்வாழ்வே","தண்க டங்க டந்து சென்று","பண்க டங்க டர்ந்த இன்சொல்","திண்பு னம்பு குந்து கண்டி ...... றைஞ்சுகோவே","அந்த கன்க லங்க வந்த","கந்த ரங்க லந்த சிந்து","ரஞ்சி றந்து வந்த லம்பு ...... ரிந்தமார்பா","அம்பு னம்பு குந்த நண்பர்","சம்பு நன்பு ரந்த ரன்த","ரம்ப லும்பர் கும்பர் நம்பு ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet names what a man holds to, the baby that crawls up again and again crying for him, the house of golden halls that seem to split the sky, the wife with curled hair and kumkum on her breasts taken as refuge, and asks for Murugan's lotus feet with their anklets and kadamba at the time when these fade. The praise names Senthil of sandal, mandara, sugarcane and plantain, Murugan who crossed the cool forest into the millet field and bowed to the girl with sweet words, and the lord whom Siva, Indra and the gods trust.","n":"மங்கு காலம், the time of fading, is the key: the child, the house and the wife are listed with delight before the turn. The song is set in very short rhythmic units, so many words are split; the last six lines (அந்தகன் கலங்க வந்த ... கும்பர் நம்பு தம்பிரானே) can be divided more than one way, and I have given only their outline.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","nilaiyaamai","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:10"},"p:98":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":98,"t":["வரியார் கருங்கண் ...... மடமாதர்","மகவா சைதொந்த ...... மதுவாகி","இருபோ துநைந்து ...... மெலியாதே","இருதா ளினன்பு ...... தருவாயே","பரிபா லனஞ்செய் ...... தருள்வோனே","பரமே சுரன்ற ...... னருள்பாலா","அரிகே சவன்றன் ...... மருகோனே","அலைவா யமர்ந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A short song: the poet asks that he not waste away morning and evening in the bond of desire for dark-eyed women and for children, and that Murugan give him love for his two feet. The praise names him the protector, the son of Parameswara's grace and the nephew of Hari Kesava, at Alaivay.","n":"மடமாதர் மக ஆசை தொந்தம்: the bond (தொந்தம்) joins desire for women and for children in one phrase. The plea is not for release but for love (இருதாளின் அன்பு).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","thiruvadi","paasam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:10"},"p:99":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":99,"t":["விதிபோலு முந்த விழியாலு மிந்து","நுதலாலு மொன்றி ...... யிளைஞோர்தம்","விரிவான சிந்தை யுருவாகி நொந்து","விறல்வேறு சிந்தை ...... வினையாலே","இதமாகி யின்ப மதுபோத வுண்டு","இனிதாளு மென்று ...... மொழிமாதர்","இருளாய துன்ப மருள்மாயை வந்து","எனையீர்வ தென்றும் ...... ஒழியாதோ","மதிசூடி யண்டர் பதிவாழ மண்டி","வருமால முண்டு ...... விடையேறி","மறவாத சிந்தை யடியார்கள் பங்கில்","வருதேவ சம்பு ...... தருபாலா","அதிமாய மொன்றி வருசூரர் பொன்ற","அயில்வேல்கொ டன்று ...... பொரும்வீரா","அழகான செம்பொன் மயில்மே லமர்ந்து","அலைவாயு கந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of women whose eyes press like fate and whose foreheads are like the moon, who join young men's minds, offer pleasure to drink its fill and say 'rule us sweetly', and asks whether the dark sorrow and delusion of maya that come to tear him will never end. The praise names Siva who wears the moon, drank the rising poison so the gods might live, rides the bull and comes to the side of devotees who never forget, and Murugan his son, who fought the Suras of great sorcery with the vel, on the peacock at Alaivay.","n":"விதிபோலும் உந்த விழி makes the women's eyes press on a man like fate. மருள் மாயை, delusion and maya, names the poet's condition in doctrinal terms, and the Suras who are defeated are themselves அதிமாயம், great maya, so the praise answers the plea.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","maayai","idumbai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:10"},"p:100":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":100,"t":["விந்ததி னு஡றி வந்தது காயம்","வெந்தது கோடி ...... யினிமேலோ","விண்டுவி டாம லுன்பத மேவு","விஞ்சையர் போல ...... அடியேனும்","வந்துவி நாச முன்கலி தீர","வண்சிவ ஞான ...... வடிவாகி","வன்பத மேறி யென்களை யாற","வந்தருள் பாத ...... மலர்தாராய்","எந்தனு ளேக செஞ்சுட ராகி","யென்கணி லாடு ...... தழல்வேணி","எந்தையர் தேடு மன்பர்ச காய","ரெங்கள் சுவாமி ...... யருள்பாலா","சுந்தர ஞான மென்குற மாது","தன்றிரு மார்பி ...... லணைவோனே","சுந்தர மான செந்திலில் மேவு","கந்தசு ரேசர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says his body came from seed and has burned a crore of times, and asks that from now on, like the knowing ones who never part from Murugan's feet, he too may have his ruin and old sin ended, take the form of Siva-knowledge, rise to the strong state and rest, and be given the flower feet. The praise names Siva, the one red flame within the poet, with fire in his eye and flame-coloured hair, helper of devotees, and Murugan his son, who embraces the beautiful hunter girl of wisdom, at Senthil.","n":"விந்ததின் ஊறி வந்தது காயம் வெந்தது கோடி: the body comes from seed and has been burned on the pyre countless times, so the plea is against rebirth itself. சிவஞான வடிவு and எந்தன் உளே ஏக செஞ்சுடர் give the song a Saiva Siddhanta cast. விஞ்சையர் may be the Vidyadharas or simply the wise; ஞானம் என் குறமாது makes Valli herself wisdom.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["piravi","vindhu","gnaanam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:10"},"p:101":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":101,"t":["விறல்மார னைந்து மலர்வாளி சிந்த","மிகவானி லிந்து ...... வெயில்காய","மிதவாடை வந்து தழல்போல வொன்ற","வினைமாதர் தந்தம் ...... வசைகூற","குறவாணர் குன்றி லுறைபேதை கொண்ட","கொடிதான துன்ப ...... மயல்தீர","குளிர்மாலை யின்க ணணிமாலை தந்து","குறைதீர வந்து ...... குறுகாயோ","மறிமா னுகந்த இறையோன் மகிழ்ந்து","வழிபாடு தந்த ...... மதியாளா","மலைமாவு சிந்த அலைவேலை யஞ்ச","வடிவே லெறிந்த ...... அதிதீரா","அறிவா லறிந்து னிருதா ளிறைஞ்சு","மடியா ரிடைஞ்சல் ...... களைவோனே","அழகான செம்பொன் மயில்மே லமர்ந்து","அலைவா யுகந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"Spoken for a girl of the hunters' hill who is sick with love: Kama's five flower arrows scatter, the moon burns like the sun, the soft breeze comes like fire and spiteful women speak blame, and the speaker asks Murugan to come near with his cool garland to end her cruel infatuation. The praise names him the wise one whom Siva, holder of the leaping deer, worshipped with joy, the hero who threw the vel so that the hill and the mango scattered and the sea feared, and the one who removes the troubles of devotees, at Alaivay.","n":"This is an akam song: குறவாணர் குன்றில் உறை பேதை is spoken of in the third person, by her mother or friend, and the cure asked is Murugan's garland, the lover's token. The moon, breeze and gossip (வினைமாதர் தம்தம் வசைகூற) are the stock torments of the separated woman. மறிமான் உகந்த இறையோன் ... வழிபாடு தந்த makes Siva the worshipper of his son.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","alar","mayal"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:10"},"p:102":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":102,"t":["வெங்கா ளம்பா ணஞ்சேல் கண்பால்","மென்பா கஞ்சொற் ...... குயில்மாலை","மென்கே சந்தா னென்றே கொண்டார்","மென்றோ ளொன்றப் ...... பொருள்தேடி","வங்கா ளஞ்சோ னஞ்சீ னம்போய்","வன்பே துன்பப் ...... படலாமோ","மைந்தா ருந்தோள் மைந்தா அந்தா","வந்தே யிந்தப் ...... பொழுதாள்வாய்","கொங்கார் பைந்தே னுண்டே வண்டார்","குன்றாள் கொங்கைக் ...... கினியோனே","குன்றோ டுஞ்சூ ழம்பே ழுஞ்சூ","ரும்போய் மங்கப் ...... பொருகோபா","கங்கா ளஞ்சேர் மொய்ம்பா ரன்பார்","கன்றே வும்பர்க் ...... கொருநாதா","கம்பூர் சிந்தார் தென்பால் வந்தாய்","கந்தா செந்திற் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says that taking women's eyes for cruel black arrows and fish, their speech for cuckoos' song and their hair as soft as it seems, he went seeking wealth as far as Bengal, Sonam and China to join their soft shoulders, and asks whether he must suffer such hard pain; he asks the strong-shouldered young lord to come and rule him at this hour. The praise names Murugan sweet to the hill girl, who fought in anger so that the hill, the seas and Sura were lost, and the son of Siva who bears the skeleton, lord of the gods, at Senthil.","n":"பொருள்தேடி வங்காளம் சோனம் சீனம் போய்: the price of women is paid with wealth earned by voyages to distant lands, a merchant's confession rather than a poet's. சோனம் is not certain (perhaps a land to the east). The song is set in heavy monosyllabic beats (வெங்காளம் பாணம் சேல் கண்), so the splits are not all sure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","selvam","idumbai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:10"},"p:103":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":103,"t":["வெஞ்ச ரோருக மோகடு நஞ்ச மோகய லோநெடு","வின்ப சாகர மோவடு ...... வகிரோமுன்","வெந்து போன புராதன சம்ப ராரி புராரியை","வென்ற சாயக மோகரு ...... விளையோகண்","தஞ்ச மோயம தூதுவர் நெஞ்ச மோவெனு மாமத","சங்க மாதர் பயோதர ...... மதில்மூழ்கு","சங்கை யோர்விரு கூதள கந்த மாலிகை தோய்தரு","தண்டை சேர்கழ லீவது ...... மொருநாளே","பஞ்ச பாதக தாருக தண்ட னீறெழ வானவர்","பண்டு போலம ராவதி ...... குடியேறப்","பங்க யாசனர் கேசவ ரஞ்ச லேயென மால்வரை","பங்க நீறெழ வேல்விடு ...... மிளையோனே","செஞ்ச டாடவி மீமிசை கங்கை மாதவி தாதகி","திங்கள் சூடிய நாயகர் ...... பெருவாழ்வே","செண்ப காடவி நீடிய துங்க மாமதிள் சுழ்தரு","செந்தில் மாநகர் மேவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks of a woman's eye whether it is a fierce lotus, harsh poison, a fish, a sea of pleasure, a slice of mango, the arrow of the burnt Kama that defeated Siva, a dark flower, a refuge or the heart of Yama's messengers, and, sunk in such women's breasts and in doubt, asks whether a day will come when Murugan gives his feet with their anklets and garlands. The praise names the vel that turned Taraka of the five great sins to ash, let the gods return to Amaravati as before and told Brahma and Vishnu not to fear, and Murugan as the treasure of Siva who wears Ganga, flowers and the moon in his matted hair, at Senthil.","n":"The opening is a chain of doubting comparisons (ஓ ... ஓ ...), ending with தஞ்சமோ யமதூதுவர் நெஞ்சமோ: is the eye a refuge or Death's servants' heart? The poet's own state is சங்கை, doubt. Kama the enemy of Sambara (சம்பராரி) is said to have defeated Siva before he was burnt.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","kaamam","kootru","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:10"},"p:104":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":104,"t":["அகல்வினை யுட்சார் சட்சம யிகளொடு வெட்கா தட்கிடு","மறிவிலி வித்தா ரத்தன ...... மவிகார","அகில்கமழ் கத்து ரித்தனி யணைமிசை கைக்கா சுக்கள","வருள்பவர் நட்பே கொட்புறு ...... மொருபோதன்","பகலிர விற்போ திற்பணி பணியற விட்டா ரெட்டிய","பரமம யச்சோ திச்சிவ ...... மயமாநின்","பழநித னிற்போ யுற்பவ வினைவிள கட்சேர் வெட்சிகு","ரவுபயில் நற்றாள் பற்றுவ ...... தொருநாளே","புகலிவ னப்பே றப்புகல் மதுரைமன் வெப்பா றத்திகழ்","பொடிகொடு புற்பாய் சுற்றிகள் ...... கழுவேறப்","பொருதச மர்த்தா குத்திர துரகமு கக்கோ தைக்கிடை","புலவரில் நக்கீ ரர்க்குத ...... வியவேளே","இகல்படு நெட்டூர் பொட்டெழ இளநகை யிட்டே சுட்டருள்","எழுபுவி துய்த்தார் மைத்துனர் ...... மதலாய்வென்","றிடரற முப்பால் செப்பிய கவிதையின் மிக்கா ரத்தினை","யெழுதிவ னத்தே யெற்றிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet calls himself a witless man who joins the followers of the six sects without shame and keeps friendship with women who give their favours for coins on beds scented with agil and musk, and asks when he will go to Palani and grasp Murugan's feet, worn with vetchi and kuravu flowers, which end the deeds of birth. The praise names him the one who, for the glory of Pugali, cured the fever of the Madurai king and sent the Jains with their grass mats to the stakes, who helped Nakkirar among poets against the horse-faced demoness, the son of Siva who burned the cities with a smile, and the one who wrote a poem and cast it on the water.","n":"The praise makes Murugan the Tamil saint of Sirkazhi (புகலி) who cured the Pandya's fever with holy ash (பொடி) and impaled the Jains, the story told of Sambandar; the closing lines (கவிதை ... எழுதி வனத்தே எற்றிய) seem to recall the palm leaf thrown into the river in the same contest. துரகமுகக் கோதை and நக்கீரர் point to the story of the poet Nakkirar rescued from a horse-faced demoness. முப்பால் செப்பிய may name the Kural, but the phrase's syntax is not clear to me.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","samayam","thamizh","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:11"},"p:105":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":105,"t":["அணிபட் டணுகித் திணிபட் டமனத்","தவர்விட் டவிழிக் ...... கணையாலும்","அரிசுற் றுகழைத் தவர்பெற் றவளத்","தவன்விட் டமலர்க் ...... கணையாலும்","பிணிபட் டுணர்வற் றவமுற் றியமற்","பெறுமக் குணமுற் ...... றுயிர்மாளும்","பிறவிக் கடல்விட் டுயர்நற் கதியைப்","பெறுதற் கருளைத் ...... தரவேணும்","கணிநற் சொருபத் தையெடுத் துமலைக்","கனியைக் கணியுற் ...... றிடுவோனே","கமலத் தயனைப் ப்ரணவத் துரையைக்","கருதிச் சிறைவைத் ...... திடுவோனே","பணியப் பணியப் பரமர்ப் பரவப்","பரிவுற் றொருசொற் ...... பகர்வோனே","பவளத் தவளக் கனகப் புரிசைப்","பழநிக் குமரப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says that by the arrows of women's eyes and the flower arrows of Kama, son of Vishnu, he is struck with disease, loses his senses, wastes away and, taking on what Yama takes, dies, and asks for grace to leave the sea of births and reach the high good state. The praise names Murugan who took a seer's form and won the fruit of the hill, who imprisoned lotus-born Brahma over the meaning of the Pranava, and who, when Siva bowed and bowed again and praised him, told him the one word lovingly, at Palani with walls of coral, white and gold.","n":"ப்ரணவத்து உரையைக் கருதிச் சிறைவைத்திடுவோனே and பணியப் பணியப் பரமர் பரவ ... ஒருசொல் பகர்வோனே tell the pilot's Om story in full: Brahma jailed for the meaning of Om, Siva bowing to receive one word. கணி நற் சொருபத்தை எடுத்து மலைக் கனியைக் கணியுற்றிடுவோனே is unclear; it may be Murugan taking a fortune-teller's form to win Valli.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","kaamam","piranavam","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:11"},"p:106":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":106,"t":["அதல விதலமுத லந்தத்த லங்களென","அவனி யெனஅமரர் அண்டத்த கண்டமென","அகில சலதியென எண்டிக்குள் விண்டுவென ...... அங்கிபாநு","அமுத கதிர்களென அந்தித்த மந்த்ரமென","அறையு மறைஅயனஅ ருந்தத்து வங்களென","அணுவி லணுவெனநி றைந்திட்டு நின்றதொரு ...... சம்ப்ரதாயம்","உதய மெழஇருள்வி டிந்தக்க ணந்தனிலி","ருதய கமலமுகி ழங்கட்ட விழ்ந்துணர்வி","லுணரு மநுபவம னம்பெற்றி டும்படியை ...... வந்துநீமுன்","உதவ இயலினியல் செஞ்சொற்ப்ர பந்தமென","மதுர கவிகளில்ம னம்பற்றி ருந்துபுகழ்","உரிய அடிமையுனை யன்றிப்ப்ர பஞ்சமதை ...... நம்புவேனோ","ததத ததததத தந்தத்த தந்ததத","திதிதி திதிதிதிதி திந்தித்தி திந்திதிதி","தகுகு தகுதகுகு தந்தத்த தந்தகுகு ...... திந்திதோதி","சகக சககெணக தந்தத்த குங்கெணக","டிடிடி டிடிடிடிடி டிண்டிட்டி டிண்டிடிடி","தகக தகதகக தந்தத்த தந்தகக ...... என்றுதாளம்","பதலை திமிலைதுடி தம்பட்ட மும்பெருக","அகில நிசிசரர்ந டுங்கக்கொ டுங்கழுகு பரிய குடர்பழுவெ லும்பைப்பி டுங்கரண ...... துங்ககாளி","பவுரி யிடநரிபு லம்பப்ப ருந்திறகு","கவரி யிடஇகலை வென்றுச்சி கண்டிதனில்","பழநி மலையின்மிசை வந்துற்ற இந்திரர்கள் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes Murugan as the one tradition that stands filling everything, the nether worlds, the earth, the gods' worlds, the cosmic egg, the seas, the eight directions, fire, sun and moon, the twilight mantras, the Veda, the tattvas and the atom within the atom, and says that Murugan came and first gave him the experience in which, as dark breaks at sunrise, the lotus of the heart opens; holding to sweet poems of crafted Tamil, will his rightful servant trust any world but him? The praise is a battle sung in drum syllables, with Kali dancing and foxes and vultures feeding, ending at Palani as lord of the gods.","n":"The plea is unusual: no confession, only a grateful refusal to trust the world (ப்ரபஞ்சம்) after an experience already given (அநுபவம்). அணுவில் அணு, the atom within the atom, and the long list of worlds and elements make Murugan the all-pervading Lord, as Tirumular says of Siva. உரிய அடிமை is the poet's title for himself, service and not bondage.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["viyaabagam","gnaanam","thondu","thamizh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:11"},"p:107":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":107,"t":["அபகார நிந்தைபட் ...... டுழலாதே","அறியாத வஞ்சரைக் ...... குறியாதே","உபதேச மந்திரப் ...... பொருளாலே","உனைநானி னைந்தருட் ...... பெறுவேனோ","இபமாமு கன்தனக் ...... கிளையோனே","இமவான்ம டந்தையுத் ...... தமிபாலா","ஜெபமாலை தந்தசற் ...... குருநாதா","திருவாவி னன்குடிப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A short song: the poet asks whether, no longer wandering in harm and slander and no longer seeking out ignorant deceivers, he will think of Murugan through the meaning of the mantra of instruction and gain grace. The praise names him the younger brother of the elephant-faced god, the son of Himavan's excellent daughter, the true guru who gave the rosary, at Thiruvavinankudi.","n":"உபதேச மந்திரப் பொருள் is the meaning of a mantra given by a guru, and ஜெபமாலை தந்த சற்குருநாதா makes Murugan the guru who gave the rosary; the song does not say to whom, but it reads like the poet's own initiation. Thiruvavinankudi is the shrine at the foot of the Palani hill.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","guru","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:11"},"p:108":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":108,"t":["அரிசன வாடைச் சேர்வை குளித்துப்","பலவித கோலச் சேலை யுடுத்திட்","டலர்குழ லோதிக் கோதி முடித்துச் ...... சுருளோடே","அமர்பொரு காதுக் கோலை திருத்தித்","திருநுதல் நீவிப் பாளித பொட்டிட்","டகில்புழு காரச் சேறு தனத்திட் ...... டலர்வேளின்","சுரத விநோதப் பார்வைமை யிட்டுத்","தருணக லாரத் தோடைத ரித்துத்","தொழிலிடு தோளுக் கேறவ ரித்திட் ...... டிளைஞோர்மார்","துறவினர் சோரச் சோரந கைத்துப்","பொருள்கவர் மாதர்க் காசைய ளித்தற்","றுயரற வேபொற் பாதமெ னக்குத் ...... தருவாயே","கிரியலை வாரிச் சூரரி ரத்தப்","புணரியின் மூழ்கிக் கூளிக ளிக்கக்","கிரணவை வேல்புத் தேளிர்பி ழைக்கத் ...... தொடுவோனே","கெருவித கோலப் பாரத னத்துக்","குறமகள் பாதச் சேகர சொர்க்கக்","கிளிதெய்வ யானைக் கேபுய வெற்பைத் ...... தருவோனே","பரிமள நீபத் தாரொடு வெட்சித்","தொடைபுனை சேவற் கேதன துத்திப்","பணியகல் பீடத் தோகைம யிற்பொற் ...... பரியோனே","பனிமல ரோடைச் சேலுக ளித்துக்","ககனம ளாவிப் போய்வரு வெற்றிப்","பழநியில் வாழ்பொற் கோமள சத்திப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes courtesans at their toilet, bathing in turmeric and scent, dressing in many saris, combing and knotting their hair, setting their earrings, putting a camphor dot on the forehead, smearing agil and musk on their breasts, darkening their eyes for love's glances and wearing lily ear-flowers, then laughing so that young men and even ascetics weaken; he asks for Murugan's golden feet to end the desire and sorrow he has given to these women who take wealth. The praise names the vel that churned the sea of the Suras' blood so the gods survived, Murugan who wears the hunter girl's feet on his head and gives his shoulders to Devayanai, the peacock rider with the cock banner, at Palani.","n":"The whole first half is the courtesan's preparation, step by step, and its end is பொருள்கவர் மாதர், women who take wealth. துறவினர் சோர claims that their laughter loosens even renouncers. குறமகள் பாதச் சேகர reverses rank as in 15: Murugan wears Valli's feet as a crown.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","thuravu","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:11"},"p:109":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":109,"t":["அருத்தி வாழ்வொடு தனகிய மனைவியு ...... முறவோரும்","அடுத்த பேர்களு மிதமுறு மகவோடு ...... வளநாடும்","தரித்த வூருமெ யெனமன நினைவது ...... நினையாதுன்","தனைப்ப ராவியும் வழிபடு தொழிலது ...... தருவாயே","எருத்தி லேறிய இறையவர் செவிபுக ...... வுபதேசம்","இசைத்த நாவின இதணுறு குறமக ...... ளிருபாதம்","பரித்த சேகர மகபதி தரவரு ...... தெய்வயானை","பதிக்கொ ளாறிரு புயபழ நியிலுறை ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks that instead of thinking real the wife who delights in a life of desire, the kin, those close to him, the children who please him, the rich land and the town he holds, he be given the work of praising and worshipping Murugan. The praise names him the tongue that spoke instruction into the ear of the lord who rides the bull, the head that bears the hunter girl's feet on her watch-platform, the twelve-shouldered one to whom Indra gave Devayanai, at Palani.","n":"மெய் என மனம் நினைவது நினையாது: the fault is taking household and possessions for real, and the cure is a task, வழிபடு தொழில். Siva receiving instruction in his ear (செவிபுக உபதேசம்) is the Om story without the word Om.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","sutram","vazhipaadu","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:11"},"p:110":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":110,"t":["அவனிதனி லேபி றந்து மதலையென வேத வழ்ந்து","அழகுபெற வேந டந்து ...... இளைஞோனாய்","அருமழலை யேமி குந்து குதலைமொழி யேபு கன்று","அதிவிதம தாய்வ ளர்ந்து ...... பதினாறாய்","சிவகலைக ளாக மங்கள் மிகவுமறை யோது மன்பர்","திருவடிக ளேநி னைந்து ...... துதியாமல்","தெரிவையர்க ளாசை மிஞ்சி வெகுகவலை யாயு ழன்று","திரியுமடி யேனை யுன்ற ...... னடிசேராய்","மவுனவுப தேச சம்பு மதியறுகு வேணி தும்பை","மணிமுடியின் மீத ணிந்த ...... மகதேவர்","மனமகிழ வேய ணைந்து ஒருபுறம தாக வந்த","மலைமகள்கு மார துங்க ...... வடிவேலா","பவனிவர வேயு கந்து மயிலின்மிசை யேதி கழ்ந்து","படியதிர வேந டந்த ...... கழல்வீரா","பரமபத மேசெ றிந்த முருகனென வேயு கந்து","பழநிமலை மேல மர்ந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet traces his life from birth through crawling, walking, lisping speech and growing to sixteen, saying that he never praised the feet of devotees who recite the Siva texts, Agamas and Vedas but wandered in many cares with growing desire for women, and asks Murugan to join him to his feet. The praise names Siva who taught in silence and wears the moon, arugu and thumbai on his crown, the mountain's daughter who came as his half, and Murugan her son, the vel who walked on the peacock so the earth shook, the Murugan who is the supreme state, at Palani.","n":"The omission confessed is not worship of the Lord but praise of his devotees' feet (அன்பர் திருவடிகளே நினைந்து). மவுன உபதேச சம்பு is Siva teaching in silence, the Dakshinamurti form.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilamai","kaamam","adiyaar","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:11"},"p:111":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":111,"t":["அறமிலா நிலைகற்று கொடியவேல் விழிவிட்டு","ளறிவுதா னறவைத்து ...... விலைபேசி","அமளிமீ தினில்வைத்து பவளவா யமுதத்தை","யதிகமா வுதவிக்கை ...... வளையாலே","உறவினா லுடலத்தை யிறுகவே தழுவிக்கொ","ளுலையிலே மெழுகொத்த ...... மடவாரோ","டுருகியே வருபெற்றி மதனா டகபித்து","ஒழியுமா றொருமுத்தி ...... தரவேணும்","மறவர்மா தொருரத்ந விமலகோ கநகத்தி","மயிலனாள் புணர்செச்சை ...... மணிமார்பா","மருள்நிசா சரன்வெற்பி லுருகிவீழ் வுறமிக்க","மயிலிலே றியவுக்ர ...... வடிவேலா","பறைகள்பே ணியருத்ரி கரியகா ரளகத்தி","பரமர்பா லுறைசத்தி ...... யெமதாயி","பழையபார் வதிகொற்றி பெரியநா யகிபெற்ற","பழநிமா மலையுற்ற ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women who learn a life without virtue, send cruel vel-like glances, put away a man's understanding, bargain a price, lay him on the bed, give the nectar of coral lips and hold his body tight with bangled arms, women like wax in the furnace, and asks for a release that ends the madness of love's play that melts him with them. The praise names Murugan whose chest the hunter girl embraced, whose fierce vel made the demon's hill melt and fall, and his mother, Rudri who loves drums, the dark-haired Shakti beside Siva, old Parvati, Kotri and Periyanayaki, at the great hill of Palani.","n":"விலைபேசி, bargaining a price, marks the women as courtesans. மதன நாடக பித்து, the madness of Kama's drama, calls the affair a performance. The praise gives as many lines to the goddess as to Murugan, ending with பெரியநாயகி, a name of the goddess at Palani.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","punarchi","veedu","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:11"},"p:112":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":112,"t":["ஆதா ளிகள்புரி கோலா கலவிழி","யாலே யமுதெனு ...... மொழியாலே","ஆழ்சீ ரிளநகை யாலே துடியிடை","யாலே மணமலி ...... குழலாலே","சூதா ரிளமுலை யாலே யழகிய","தோடா ரிருகுழை ...... யதனாலே","சோரா மயல்தரு மானா ருறவிடர்","சூழா வகையருள் ...... புரிவாயே","போதா ரிருகழல் சூழா ததுதொழில்","பூணா தெதிருற ...... மதியாதே","போரா டியஅதி சூரா பொறுபொறு","போகா தெனஅடு ...... திறலோனே","வேதா வுடனெடு மாலா னவனறி","யாதா ரருளிய ...... குமரேசா","வீரா புரிவரு கோவே பழநியுள்","வேலா இமையவர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet names the women's lively eyes, nectar speech, smile, drum-like waist, scented hair, deceitful young breasts and earrings, and asks for grace so that the trouble of ties with women who give an unfailing infatuation does not close round him. The praise names Murugan who killed Sura, who would not circle his feet or respect him and fought him, crying 'wait, wait, you will not escape', the son graced by the one whom Brahma and Vishnu could not know, the vel at Palani, lord of the gods.","n":"The plea is built as a list of the women's charms, each ending ஆலே, 'by', and the danger is called சோரா மயல், infatuation that does not slacken. The meaning of ஆதாளிகள் and of புரிவரு in the last lines is not clear to me.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mayal","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:11"},"p:113":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":113,"t":["ஆல காலமெ னக்கொலை முற்றிய","வேல தாமென மிக்கவி ழிக்கடை","யாலு மோகம்வி ளைத்துவி தத்துட ...... னிளைஞோரை","ஆர வாணைமெ யிட்டும றித்துவி","கார மோகமெ ழுப்பிய தற்குற","வான பேரைய கப்படு வித்ததி ...... விதமாகச்","சால மாலைய ளித்தவர் கைப்பொருள்","மாள வேசிலு கிட்டும ருட்டியெ","சாதி பேதம றத்தழு வித்திரி ...... மடமாதர்","தாக போகமொ ழித்துஉனக்கடி","யானென் வேள்விமு கத்தவ முற்றிரு","தாளை நாளும்வ ழுத்திநி னைத்திட ...... அருள்வாயே","வால மாமதி மத்தமெ ருக்கறு","காறு பூளைத ரித்தச டைத்திரு","வால வாயன ளித்தரு ளற்புத ...... முருகோனே","மாய மானொட ரக்கரை வெற்றிகொள்","வாலி மார்புதொ ளைத்திட விற்கொடு","வாளி யேவிய மற்புய னச்சுதன் ...... மருகோனே","நாலு வேதந விற்றுமு றைப்பயில்","வீணை நாதனு ரைத்தவ னத்திடை","நாடி யோடிகு றத்தித னைக்கொடு ...... வருவோனே","நாளி கேரம்வ ருக்கைப ழுத்துதிர்","சோலை சூழ்பழ நிப்பதி யிற்றிரு","ஞான பூரண சத்தித ரித்தருள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women whose eye-corners, keen as a killing vel and deadly as poison, rouse desire in young men, who swear oaths, hold them back, catch the men worth having, take all the wealth in their hands, then quarrel and bewilder them, and embrace without regard to caste; he asks for grace to leave their pleasures and, as Murugan's servant, praise and think of his feet daily. The praise names him the wonder given by the lord of Alavay who wears the moon, datura, erukku, arugu and pulai flowers, the nephew of Vishnu who shot the magic deer and pierced Vali's chest, the one who sought out the hunter girl in the forest Narada told of, and the lord holding the full-wisdom spear at Palani.","n":"கைப்பொருள் மாள and சாதி பேதம் அறத் தழுவி: the women spend a man's wealth and embrace any man regardless of caste, the charge Parimelazhagar also makes on Kural 913. உனக்கு அடியான் sets the poet's service against his bondage to them. ஞான பூரண சத்தி names the vel as Shakti, full of wisdom.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","selvam","thondu","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:11"},"p:114":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":114,"t":["ஆறுமுகம் ஆறுமுகம் ஆறுமுகம் ஆறுமுகம்","ஆறுமுகம் ஆறுமுகம் ...... என்றுபூதி","ஆகமணி மாதவர்கள் பாதமலர் சூடுமடி","யார்கள்பத மேதுணைய ...... தென்றுநாளும்","ஏறுமயில் வாகனகு காசரவ ணாஎனது","ஈசஎன மானமுன ...... தென்றுமோதும்","ஏழைகள் வியாகுலமி தேதெனவி னாவிலுனை","யேவர்புகழ் வார்மறையு ...... மென்சொலாதோ","நீறுபடு மாழைபொரு மேனியவ வேலஅணி","நீலமயில் வாகவுமை ...... தந்தவேளே","நீசர்கட மோடெனது தீவினையெ லாமடிய","நீடுதனி வேல்விடும ...... டங்கல்வேலா","சீறிவரு மாறவுண னாவியுணு மானைமுக","தேவர்துணை வாசிகரி ...... அண்டகூடஞ்","சேருமழ கார்பழநி வாழ்குமர னேபிரம","தேவர்வர தாமுருக ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of devotees who chant 'Arumugam' six times with holy ash, take as their aid the feet of those who wear the feet of great ascetics, and say daily 'Guha who rides the peacock, Saravana, my lord, my honour is yours', and asks: if you only ask what these poor ones' trouble is, who will praise you, and what will the Veda not say? The praise names Murugan with a body like gold under ash, son of Uma, the lion-like vel thrown to end the poet's evil deeds, the companion of the elephant-faced god who took the life of the raging asura, at Palani, boon-giver to Brahma.","n":"ஆறுமுகம் repeated six times opens the song with the chant itself. The question in the second quatrain seems to bind Murugan's fame to his care for devotees: if he neglects their distress, who will praise him? Its syntax (ஏழைகள் வியாகுலம் இது ஏது என வினாவில்) allows other readings.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["adiyaar","thiruneeru","thiruvarul","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:11"},"p:115":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":115,"t":["இத்தா ரணிக்குள்மநு வித்தாய் முளைத்தழுத","கேவிக் கிடந்துமடி மீதிற் றவழ்ந்தடிகள்","தத்தா தனத்ததன இட்டே தெருத்தலையில்","ஓடித் திரிந்துநவ கோடிப் ப்ரபந்தகலை","யிச்சீர் பயிற்றவய தெட்டொடு மெட்டுவர","வாலக் குணங்கள்பயில் கோலப் பெதும்பையர்க ...... ளுடனுறவாகி","இக்கார் சரத்துமத னுக்கே இளைத்துவெகு","வாகக் கலம்பவகை பாடிப் புகழ்ந்துபல","திக்கோடு திக்குவரை மட்டோ டி மிக்கபொருள்","தேடிச் சுகந்தஅணை மீதிற் றுயின்றுசுக","மிட்டா தரத்துருகி வட்டார் முலைக்குளிடை","முழ்கிக் கிடந்துமய லாகித் துளைந்துசில ...... பிணிய\u0004முடிச்","சத்தான புத்தியது கெட்டே கிடக்கநம","னோடித் தொடர்ந்துகயி றாடிக் கொளும்பொழுது","பெற்றோர்கள் சுற்றியழ வுற்றார்கள் மெத்தஅழ","ஊருக் கடங்கலிலர் காலற் கடங்கவுயிர்","தக்கா திவர்க்குமய னிட்டான் விதிப்படியி","னோலைப் பழம்படியி னாலிற் றிறந்ததென ...... எடுமெனவோடிச்","சட்டா நவப்பறைகள் கொட்டா வரிச்சுடலை","யேகிச் சடம்பெரிது வேகப் புடஞ்சமைய","இட்டே யனற்குளெரி பட்டா ரெனத்தழுவி","நீரிற் படிந்துவிடு பாசத் தகன்றுனது","சற்போ தகப்பதும முற்றே தமிழ்க்கவிதை","பேசிப் பணிந்துருகு நேசத்தை யின்றுதர ...... இனிவரவேணும்","தித்தா திரித்திகுட தத்தா தனத்தகுத","தாதத் தனந்ததன தானத் தனந்ததன","செச்சே செகுச்செகுகு தித்தா திமித்ததிகு","தாதத்த செந்திகுத தீதத்த செந்தரிக","தித்தா கிடக்கணக டக்கா குகுக்குகுகு","தோதக் கணங்கணக கூகுக் கிணங்கிணென ...... ஒருமயிலேறித்","திட்டே ரதத்தசுரர் பட்டே விழப்பொருது","வேலைத் தொளைந்துவரை யேழைப் பிளந்துவரு","சித்தா பரத்தமரர் கத்தா குறத்திமுலை","மீதிற் புணர்ந்துசுக லீலைக் கதம்பமணி","சுத்தா வுமைக்குமொரு முத்தாய் முளைத்தகுரு","நாதக் குழந்தையென வோடிக் கடம்பமலர் ...... அணிதிருமார்பா","மத்தா மதக்களிறு பிற்றா னுதித்தகுக","னேதத் திலங்கையினி லாதிக்க முண்டதொரு","முட்டா ளரக்கர்தலை யிற்றே விழக்கணைக","ளேதொட்ட கொண்டலுரு வாகிச் சுமந்ததிக","மட்டார் மலர்க்கமல முற்றா சனத்திருவை","மார்பிற் புணர்ந்தரகு ராமற்கு மன்புடைய ...... மருமகனாகி","வற்றா மதுக்கருணை யுற்றே மறைக்கலைக","ளோதித் தெரிந்துதமிழ் சோதித் தலங்கலணி","யத்தா பரத்தையறி வித்தாவி சுற்றுமொளி","யாகிப் ப்ரபந்தமணி வேல்தொட்ட மைந்தபுய","வர்க்கா மருப்புழுகு முட்டா திருப்பழநி","வாழவுக் குகந்தடிய ராவிக்குள் நின்றுலவி ...... வருபெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet tells a whole life: a human seed is born crying, crawls on the lap, toddles with dancing steps, runs in the streets, is taught the arts, at sixteen joins with young girls, weakens to Kama, sings poems in praise of patrons, runs to every quarter for wealth, melts in pleasure on scented beds and sinks in women's breasts until disease ruins his mind; then Yama chases him with the noose, parents and kin weep, they say Brahma's leaf has run out and carry him with drums to the burning ground, and those who embraced him bathe and leave the bond. Before that, he asks Murugan to come now and give the love that bows at his true lotus feet and melts singing Tamil poems. The praise, after drum syllables, names the vel that pierced the sea and split the seven hills, Murugan the guru-child of Uma, the nephew of Rama who felled Lanka's heads, who knew the Vedas and tested Tamil, at Palani, the lord who moves within his devotees' lives.","n":"The cremation is told in detail (நவப்பறைகள் கொட்டா, சுடலை, சடம் பெரிது வேக), and the last act of the mourners, நீரில் படிந்து விடு பாசத்து அகன்று, is that they wash and let go. The leaf by which Brahma set the span of life (ஓலைப் பழம்படி) is the summons the pilot calls சமனோலை. The plea, இனி வரவேணும், is to come before this ending.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","yaakkai","sutram","thamizh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:11"},"p:116":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":116,"t":["இரவியென வடவையென ஆலால விடமதென","உருவுகொடு ககனமிசை மீதேகி மதியும்வர","இரதிபதி கணைகளொரு நாலேவ விருதுகுயி ...... லதுகூவ","எழுகடலின் முரசினிசை வேயோசை விடையின்மணி","யிசைகுறுகி யிருசெவியி னாராச முறுவதென","இகல்புரிய மதனகுரு வோராத அனையர்கொடு ...... வசைபேச","அரஹரென வநிதைபடு பாடோ த அரிதரிது","அமுதமயி லதுகருதி யாரோடு மிகல்புரிவள்","அவசமுற அவசமுற ஆரோமல் தரவுமிக ...... மெலிவானாள்","அகுதியிவள் தலையில்விதி யானாலும் விலகரிது","அடிமைகொள வுனதுபரம் ஆறாத வொருதனிமை","யவளையணை தரஇனிதி னோகார பரியின்மிசை ...... வருவாயே","நிரைபரவி வரவரையு ளோர்சீத மருதினொடு","பொருசகடு வுதையதுசெய் தாமாய மழைசொரிதல்","நிலைகுலைய மலைகுடைய தாவேகொள் கரகமலன் ...... மருகோனே","நிருமலிய திரிநயனி வாள்வீச வருகுமரி","கவுரிபயி ரவியரவ பூணாரி திரிபுவனி","நிபுடமலை யரசனருள் வாழ்வான புரணவுமை ...... யருள்பாலா","பரவைகிரி யசுரர்திரள் மாசேனை தவிடுபொடி","படஅமரர் துயரகல வேலேவி யமர்பொருத","பதுமகர தலமுருக நால்வேத கரரணிக ...... மயில்வீரா","பளிதம்ருக மதகளப சேறார வளருமுலை","வநிதைகுற மகள்மகிழும் லீலாவி தரமதுர","பநுவல்தரு பழநிவரு கோலாக லவவமரர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"Spoken for a girl sick with love: the moon climbs the sky like the sun, the submarine fire and poison, Kama shoots his arrows and the cuckoo calls, the drum of the seas, the flute and the bull's bell pierce her ears like iron, and women blame her; she cries 'Hara, Hara', longs for the peacock's rider and quarrels with everyone, and has grown faint. Though it is fate written on her head, the speaker says, taking her into his service is his burden; let him come on the horse that is Om to embrace her in her loneliness. The praise names Murugan the nephew of Krishna who kicked the cart, felled the twin trees and held up the hill against the rain, the son of the goddess under many names, the peacock hero whose vel turned the demon army to dust, and the delight of the hunter girl, at Palani.","n":"The speaker calls her அகுதி இவள், this helpless one, so the voice is a mother's or friend's. அடிமைகொள உனது பரம், 'to take her as your servant is your charge', fuses the akam lover with the devotee. ஓகார பரி, the steed that is the syllable Om, is the peacock.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","alar","melivu","oozh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:11"},"p:117":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":117,"t":["இருகனக மாமேரு வோகளப துங்க","கடகடின பாடீர வாரமுத கும்ப","மிணைசொலிள நீரோக ராசலஇ ரண்டு ...... குவடேயோ","இலகுமல ரேவாளி யாகியஅ நங்க","னணிமகுட மோதானெ னாமிகவ ளர்ந்த","இளமுலைமி னார்மோக மாயையில்வி ழுந்து ...... தணியாமல்","பெருகியொரு காசேகொ டாதவரை யைந்து","தருவைநிக ரேயாக வேயெதிர்பு கழந்து","பெரியதமி ழேபாடி நாடொறுமி ரந்து ...... நிலைகாணாப்","பிணியினக மேயான பாழுடலை நம்பி","உயிரையவ மாய்நாடி யேபவநி ரம்பு","பிறவிதனி லேபோக மீளவுமு ழன்று ...... திரிவேனோ","கருணையுமை மாதேவி காரணிய நந்த","சயனகளி கூராரி சோதரிபு ரந்த","கடவுளுடன் வாதாடு காளிமலை மங்கை ...... யருள்பாலா","கருடனுடன் வீறான கேதானம்வி ளங்கு","மதிலினொடு மாமாட மேடைகள்து லங்கு","கலிசைவரு காவேரி சேவகனொ டன்பு ...... புரிவோனே","பரவையிடை யேபாத காசுரர்வி ழுந்து","கதறியிட வேபாக சாதனனு நெஞ்சு","பலிதமென வேயேக வேமயிலில் வந்த ...... குமரேசா","பலமலர்க ளேதூவி யாரணந வின்று","பரவியிமை யோர்சூழ நாடொறுமி சைந்து","பழநிமலை மீதோர்ப ராபரனி றைஞ்சு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether women's young breasts are two golden Merus, nectar pots, tender coconuts, elephant-hill peaks or the crown of Kama, says he fell into the maya of desire for them, praised as equal to the five heavenly trees men who never gave a coin, sang great Tamil and begged daily without finding a footing, and asks whether, trusting this diseased and ruined body and seeking life in vain, he will wander again in birth. The praise names his mother, Uma, sister of Vishnu and Kali who contended with Siva, the one who showed love to the Kaveri chieftain of Kalisai, the Kumaresa who came on the peacock so the asuras fell in the sea and Indra's heart was eased, and the lord whom the gods and Siva himself worship at Palani.","n":"The confession joins two of the book's subjects, desire for women and begging from patrons in Tamil (ஒரு காசே கொடாதவரை ஐந்து தருவை நிகரே யாக ... புகழ்ந்து), and the begging is plainly in order to pay for the first. கலிசைவரு காவேரி சேவகன் seems to be a patron or devotee named in the praise; I cannot identify him.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","iravu","yaakkai","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:11"},"p:118":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":118,"t":["இருசெப் பெனவெற் பெனவட் டமுமொத்","திளகிப் புளகித் ...... திடுமாதர்","இடையைச் சுமையைப் பெறுதற் குறவுற்","றிறுகக் குறுகிக் ...... குழல்சோரத்","தருமெய்ச் சுவையுற் றிதழைப் பருகித்","தழுவிக் கடிசுற் ...... றணைமீதே","சருவிச் சருவிக் குனகித் தனகித்","தவமற் றுழலக் ...... கடவேனோ","அரிபுத் திரசித் தஜனுக் கருமைக்","குரியத் திருமைத் ...... துனவேளே","அடல்குக் குடநற் கொடிபெற் றெதிருற்","றசுரக் கிளையைப் ...... பொருவோனே","பரிவுற் றரனுக் கருணற் பொருளைப்","பயனுற் றறியப் ...... பகர்வோனே","பவனப் புவனச் செறிவுற் றுயர்மெய்ப்","பழநிக் குமரப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women whose breasts are like caskets and hills, and the act with them, the hair loosened, the lips drunk, the embrace on the bed, the struggling and fondling, and asks whether he must wander so without any austerity. The praise names Murugan as the holy kinsman dear to Brahma, Vishnu's son, the one with the cock banner who fought the asura clan, and the one who lovingly told Siva the good meaning so he might know its fruit, at Palani.","n":"தவமற்று உழல, wandering without tapas, is the whole plea. அரனுக்கு அருள் நற்பொருளைப் பகர்வோனே is the Om story without naming Om. அரிபுத்திர சித்த அஜனுக்கு ... மைத்துன வேளே makes Murugan Brahma's brother-in-law, perhaps because his consorts are counted Vishnu's daughters; the reading is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","thavam","guru","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:11"},"p:119":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":119,"t":["இலகிய களபசு கந்த வாடையின்","ம்ருகமத மதனைம கிழ்ந்து பூசியெ","இலைசுருள் பிளவைய ருந்தி யேயதை ...... யிதமாகக்","கலவியி லவரவர் தங்கள் வாய்தனி","லிடுபவர் பலபல சிந்தை மாதர்கள்","கசனையை விடுவது மெந்த நாளது ...... பகர்வாயே","சிலைதரு குறவர்ம டந்தை நாயகி","தினைவன மதனிலு கந்த நாயகி","திரள்தன மதனில ணைந்த நாயக ...... சிவலோகா","கொலைபுரி யசுரர்கு லங்கள் மாளவெ","அயிலயி லதனையு கந்த நாயக","குருபர பழநியி லென்று மேவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women who smear themselves with sandal paste and musk, chew betel and in lovemaking pass it from their mouths into their lovers', women of many minds, and asks Murugan to tell him on what day he will let go of his attachment to them. The praise names him lord of the hunter girl of the millet field, whose breasts he embraced, the one who delighted in the vel that ended the murderous asura clans, and the guru who dwells always at Palani.","n":"பலபல சிந்தை மாதர்கள், women of many minds, is the charge of divided hearts. The word கசனை, which carries the plea, I take as attachment from context; I cannot confirm the word.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","punarchi","pattru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:12"},"p:120":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":120,"t":["இலகுகனி மிஞ்சு மொழியிரவு துஞ்சு","மிருவிழியெ னஞ்சு ...... முகமீதே","இசைமுரல்சு ரும்பு மிளமுலைய ரும்பு","மிலகியக ரும்பு ...... மயலாலே","நிலவிலுடல் வெந்து கரியஅல மந்து","நெகிழுமுயிர் நொந்து ...... மதவேளால்","நிலையழியு நெஞ்சி லவர்குடிபு குந்த","நினைவொடுமி றந்து ...... படலாமோ","புலவினைய ளைந்து படுமணிக லந்து","புதுமலர ணிந்த ...... கதிர்வேலா","புழுகெழம ணந்த குறமகள்கு ரும்பை","பொரமுகையு டைந்த ...... தொடைமார்பா","பலநிறமி டைந்த விழுசிறைய லர்ந்த","பருமயில டைந்த ...... குகவீரா","பணைபணிசி றந்த தரளமணி சிந்து","பழநிமலை வந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet, caught by infatuation with women's sleepless eyes, their humming-bee hair, budding breasts and Kama's sugarcane bow, says his body burns in the moonlight and his life loosens in pain, and asks whether he must die with the thought of these women living in his heart. The praise names the vel wet with flesh and hung with bells and fresh flowers, the chest whose garland was crushed by the hunter girl's breasts, and Guha on the many-coloured peacock, at Palani hill.","n":"அவர் குடிபுகுந்த நினைவொடும் இறந்துபடலாமோ: the fear is not death itself but dying with the women settled (குடிபுகுந்த) in the heart, where the Lord should be. The love-sickness uses the stock moonlight and Kama's bow (மதவேள்) of Tamil love poetry.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mayal","kaamanoy","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:12"},"p:121":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":121,"t":["உயிர்க்கூடு விடுமளவும் உமைக்கூடி மருவுதொழில்","ஒருக்காலு நெகிழ்வதிலை ...... யெனவேசூள்","உரைத்தேமுன் மருவினரை வெறுத்தேம திரவியம","துடைத்தாய்பின் வருகுமவ ...... ரெதிரேபோய்ப்","பயிற்பேசி யிரவுபகல் அவர்க்கான பதமைபல","படப்பேசி யுறுபொருள்கொள் ...... விலைமாதர்","படப்பார வலைபடுதல் தவிர்த்தாள மணிபொருவு","பதத்தாள மயிலின்மிசை ...... வரவேணும்","தயிர்ச்சோர னெனுமவுரை வசைக்கோவ வனிதையர்கள்","தரத்தாடல் புரியுமரி ...... மருகோனே","தமிழக்காழி மருதவன் மறைக்காடு திருமருகல்","தநுக்கோடி வருகுழகர் ...... தருவாழ்வே","செயிற்சேல்வி ணுடுவினொடு பொரப்போய்வி மமர்பொருது","செயித்தோடி வருபழநி ...... யமர்வோனே","தினைக்காவல் புரியவல குறப்பாவை முலைதழுவு","திருத்தோள அமரர்பணி ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes courtesans (விலைமாதர்) who swear that until life leaves the body they will never once slacken in their union with a man, then turn against those they joined before, strip them of their wealth, go out to meet the next ones and talk sweetly to them night and day; he asks Murugan to come on the peacock and free him from their net. The praise names Hari who danced with the cowherd women when mocked as a curd thief, the Lord of Kazhi, Marudur, Maraikkadu, Marugal and Dhanushkodi whose gift Murugan is, and Palani where carp leap up to fight the stars.","n":"The song turns on the false oath (சூள்): உயிர்க்கூடு விடுமளவும், until the body's nest is left, is the promise; திரவியம் அதுடைத்து, wiping out his wealth, is the outcome. The shrine list names Siva's Tamil temples (சீகாழி, மருதூர், மறைக்காடு, மருகல், தனுக்கோடி) as one Lord's.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:12"},"p:122":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":122,"t":["உலகபசு பாச தொந்த ...... மதுவான","உறவுகிளை தாயர் தந்தை ...... மனைபாலர்","மலசலசு வாச சஞ்ச ...... லமதாலென்","மதிநிலைகெ டாம லுன்ற ...... னருள்தாராய்","சலமறுகு பூளை தும்பை ...... யணிசேயே","சரவணப வாமு குந்தன் ...... மருகோனே","பலகலைசி வாக மங்கள் ...... பயில்வோனே","பழநிமலை வாழ வந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A short song: the poet names the bonds of the world, pasu and pasam, kin, mother, father, home and children, and the agitation of the body's filth and breath, and asks for grace so that his mind's balance is not destroyed by them. The praise names Murugan as the son adorned with Ganga, arugu grass, poolai and thumbai flowers, Saravanabhava, nephew of Mukunda, who studies the many arts and the Siva Agamas, at Palani.","n":"உலக பசு பாச தொந்தம் uses the Saiva Siddhanta terms pasu and pasam for the ordinary ties of family. The flowers of சலம் அறுகு பூளை தும்பை are Siva's; the line can make Murugan wear them or make him the son (சேய்) of the one who does. பலகலை சிவாகமங்கள் பயில்வோன் makes Murugan a student or master of the Agamas.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paasam","sutram","yaakkai","aagamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:12"},"p:123":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":123,"t":["ஒருபொழுது மிருசரண நேசத் தேவைத் ...... துணரேனே","உனதுபழ நிமலையெனு மூரைச் சேவித் ...... தறியேனே","பெருபுவி லுயர்வரிய வாழ்வைத் தீரக் ...... குறியேனே","பிறவியற நினைகுவனெ னாசைப் பாடைத் ...... தவிரேனோ","துரிதமிடு நிருதர்புர சூறைக் காரப் ...... பெருமாளே","தொழுதுவழி படுமடியர் காவற் காரப் ...... பெருமாளே","விருதுகவி விதரணவி நோதக் காரப் ...... பெருமாளே","விறன்மறவர் சிறுமிதிரு வேளைக் காரப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A short song: the poet confesses that he has never once known the god with love for his two feet, never worshipped at Palani, never set his aim on the rare life, and asks whether he will not give up the burden of desire and think of ending birth. The praise calls Murugan, in four rhyming lines, the plunderer of the demons' city, the guardian of devotees who worship him, the one who delights in giving and in honoured verse, and the bodyguard (வேளைக்காரன்) of the hunters' little girl.","n":"The four epithets end in காரப் பெருமாளே, and the last makes the Lord a servant: வேளைக்காரன் was a king's sworn bodyguard, here in Valli's service. ஆசைப் பாடை may be the burden or suffering (பாடு) of desire, or desire as a bier; the split is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvadi","vazhipaadu","avaa","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:12"},"p:124":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":124,"t":["ஒருவரை யொருவர் தேறி யறிகிலர் மதவி சாரர்","ஒருகுண வழியு றாத ...... பொறியாளர்","உடலது சதமெ னாடி களவுபொய் கொலைக ளாடி","உறநம னரகில் வீழ்வ ...... ரதுபோய்பின்","வருமொரு வடிவ மேவி யிருவினை கடலு ளாடி","மறைவரி னனைய கோல ...... மதுவாக","மருவிய பரம ஞான சிவகதி பெறுக நீறு","வடிவுற அருளி பாத ...... மருள்வாயே","திரிபுர மெரிய வேழ சிலைமத னெரிய மூரல்","திருவிழி யருள்மெய்ஞ் ஞான ...... குருநாதன்","திருசரஸ் வதிம யேசு வரியிவர் தலைவ ரோத","திருநட மருள நாத ...... னருள்பாலா","சுரர்பதி யயனு மாலு முறையிட அசுரர் கோடி","துகளேழ விடுமெய்ஞ் ஞான ...... அயிலோனே","சுககுற மகள்ம ணாள னெனமறை பலவு மோதி","தொழமுது பழநி மேவு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes sectarians who cannot recognise one another's truth, men of the senses on no single path, who think the body permanent, steal, lie and kill, fall into Death's hell and are born again to swim the sea of the two deeds; he asks Murugan to give him holy ash and his feet, so that he may gain the supreme knowledge and the path to Siva. The praise names the guru of true knowledge who burned the three cities and Kama with a smile and an eye, the Lord whose dance Saraswati and Maheswari praise, and Murugan's vel of true knowledge that powdered crores of asuras when Indra, Brahma and Vishnu cried out, at ancient Palani where the Vedas hail him as the hunter girl's husband.","n":"மதவிசாரர், the sect-disputers, open the song, and the remedy is not a better doctrine but a sign and a gift: நீறு வடிவுற அருளி, grant the ash to take form on me. The vel is called மெய்ஞ்ஞான அயில், the lance of true knowledge. The line மறைவரின் அனைய கோலம் is unclear to me.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["samayam","naragam","vinai","thiruneeru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:12"},"p:125":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":125,"t":["ஓடி யோடி யழைத்துவ ரச்சில","சேடி மார்கள் பசப்பஅ தற்குமு","னோதி கோதி முடித்தவி லைச்சுரு ...... ளதுகோதி","நீடு வாச நிறைத்தஅ கிற்புழு","கோட மீது திமிர்த்தத னத்தினில்","நேச மாகி யணைத்தசி றுக்கிக ...... ளுறவாமோ","நாடி வாயும் வயற்றலை யிற்புன","லோடை மீதி னிலத்ததி வட்கையி","னாத கீத மலர்த்துளி பெற்றளி ...... யிசைபாடுங்","கோடு லாவிய முத்துநி ரைத்தவை","காவுர் நாடத னிற்பழ நிப்பதி","கோதி லாதகு றத்திய ணைத்தருள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women whose maids run back and forth to call men and coax them, women who dress their hair, tuck in betel rolls and smear agil and civet on their breasts before embracing with a show of love, and asks whether a bond with such hussies is fitting. The praise names Palani in Vaikavur country, where bees drink from lotus pools in the fields and sing and the conches spill pearls, and Murugan who embraced the faultless hunter girl.","n":"சேடிமார்கள் பசப்ப, maids who coax, is the household of a courtesan, but the song does not name money. சிறுக்கிகள் உறவாமோ, is a bond with such hussies fit, makes the confession a question of the company he keeps.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","vanjam","inam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:12"},"p:126":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":126,"t":["கடலைச் சிறைவைத் துமலர்ப் பொழிலிற்","ப்ரமரத் தையுடற் பொறியிட் டுமடுக்","கமலத் தைமலர்த் திவிடத் தையிரப் ...... பவனு஡ணாக்","கருதிச் சருவிக் கயலைக் கயமுட்","படுவித் துழையைக் கவனத் தடைசிக்","கணையைக் கடைவித் துவடுத் தனையுப் ...... பினின்மேவி","அடலைச் செயல்சத் தியையக் கினியிற்","புகுவித் துயமப் ப்ரபுவைத் துகைவித்","தரிகட் கம்விதிர்த் துமுறித் துமதித் ...... தசகோரம்","அலறப் பணிரத் நமணிக் குழையைச்","சிலுகிட் டுமையிட் டொளிவிட் டுமருட்","டுதலுற் றபொறிச் சியர்கட் கடையிற் ...... படுவேனோ","சடிலத் தவனிட் டவிசிட் டகுலத்","தொருசெட் டியிடத் தினுதித் தருள்வித்","தகருத் ரஜன்மப் பெயர்செப் பியிடப் ...... பரிவாலே","சநகர்க் குமகஸ்த் யபுலஸ்த் யசநற்","குமரர்க் குமநுக் க்ரக மெய்ப் பலகைச்","சதுபத் துநவப் புலவர்க் கும்விபத் ...... தியில்ஞான","படலத் துறுலக் கணலக் யதமிழ்த்","த்ரயமத் திலகப் பொருள்வ்ருத் தியினைப்","பழுதற் றுணர்வித் தருள்வித் தகசற் ...... குருநாதா","பவளக் கொடிசுற் றியபொற் கமுகிற்","றலையிற் குலையிற் பலமுத் துதிர்செய்ப்","பழநிப் பதிவெற் பினில்நிற் குமரப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women's eyes as having defeated every standard of comparison, imprisoning the sea, branding the bee, leaving poison fit only for the beggar-god's food, sending the carp into the pond and the deer into the forest, putting the mango slice in salt, the spear in the fire, trampling Yama and breaking the sword, and asks whether he will fall to the eye-corners of these wily women. The praise names Murugan as the one born, by Siva's command, in a merchant family under the name Rudrajanma, who taught Sanaka, Agastya, Pulastya and Sanatkumara, and made the forty-nine poets of the plank understand without fault the sense of the three Tamils, grammar and literature; he is the true guru, at Palani.","n":"The first half is a single conceit: each thing a poet compares eyes to has been beaten and put away. The second names the story of the dumb child Uruttiracanman (ருத்ரஜன்மப் பெயர்), born to a merchant (செட்டி), who judged the commentaries of the Sangam's poets (சதுபத்துநவப் புலவர், the forty-nine on the plank, பலகை). Murugan is thus the judge of Tamil itself. Several lines of the first half are compressed, and my split of them is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["kan","thamizh","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:12"},"p:127":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":127,"t":["கடலை பொரியவரை பலகனி கழைநுகர்","கடின குடவுதர ...... விபாணத","கரட தடமுமத நளின சிறுநயன","கரிணி முகவரது ...... துணைவோனே","வடவ ரையின்முகடு அதிர வொருநொடியில்","வலம்வரு மரகத ...... மயில்வீரா","மகபதி தருசுதை குறமினொ டிருவரு","மருவு சரசவித ...... மணவாளா","அடல சுரர்கள்குல முழுது மடியவுய","ரமரர் சிறையைவிட ...... எழில்மீறும்","அருண கிரணவொளி யொளிரு மயிலைவிடு","மரகர சரவண ...... பவலோலா","படல வுடுபதியை யிதழி யணிசடில","பசுபதி வரநதி ...... அழகான","பழநி மலையருள்செய் மழலை மொழிமதலை","பழநி மலையில்வரு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A song of praise only: Murugan is the brother of the pot-bellied, small-eyed elephant-faced god who eats gram, puffed rice, beans, fruit and sugarcane; the hero whose emerald peacock circled the northern mountain in an instant, shaking its peak; the bridegroom of Indra's daughter and the hunter girl; and the lisping child given by the grace of Pasupati, who wears the moon, konrai and the river, who came to Palani.","n":"The song is a family portrait: the brother, the two wives, the father. The peacock circling the mountain in a moment may allude to the race round the world for the fruit that tradition ties to Palani, but the text names no fruit. மழலை மொழி மதலை, the lisping child, is Murugan as an infant.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:12"},"p:128":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":128,"t":["கதியை விலக்கு மாதர்கள் புதிய இரத்ன பூஷண","கனதன வெற்பு மேல்மிகு ...... மயலான","கவலை மனத்த னாகிலும் உனது ப்ரசித்த மாகிய","கனதன மொத்த மேனியு ...... முகமாறும்","அதிபல வஜ்ர வாகுவும் அயில்நுனை வெற்றி வேலதும்","அரவு பிடித்த தோகையு ...... முலகேழும்","அதிர வரற்று கோழியும் அடியர் வழுத்தி வாழ்வுறும்","அபிநவ பத்ம பாதமு ...... மறவேனே","இரவி குலத்தி ராசத மருவி யெதிர்த்து வீழ்கடு","ரணமுக சுத்த வீரிய ...... குணமான","இளையவ னுக்கு நீண்முடி அரசது பெற்று வாழ்வுற","இதமொ டளித்த ராகவன் ...... மருகோனே","பதினொரு ருத்தி ராதிகள் தபனம் விளக்கு மாளிகை","பரிவோடு நிற்கு மீசுர ...... சுரலோக","பரிமள கற்ப காடவி அரியளி சுற்று பூவுதிர்","பழநி மலைக்குள் மேவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says that though his mind is anxious and infatuated with the jewelled breasts of women who block the way to release, he will not forget Murugan's golden body, six faces, twelve mighty arms, the victorious vel, the peacock that holds the snake, the cock whose cry shakes the seven worlds, and the new lotus feet in which devotees thrive. The praise names Raghava, who gladly gave the long crown to the younger brother when the elder of the sun's line fell fighting, as Murugan's uncle, and Palani where the eleven Rudras keep the lamps and flowers fall from the kalpaka forest.","n":"The song is a vow, not a plea: மறவேனே, I will not forget, answers ஆகிலும், even though, so the remembered form is set against the infatuation. கதியை விலக்கும் மாதர்கள், women who turn one from release, names what is at stake. The brother given the crown is Sugriva, son of the Sun, by the details.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mayal","thiruvadi","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:12"},"p:129":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":129,"t":["கரிய பெரிய எருமை கடவு","கடிய கொடிய ...... திரிசூலன்","கறுவி யிறுகு கயிறொ டுயிர்கள்","கழிய முடுகி ...... யெழுகாலந்","திரியு நரியு மெரியு முரிமை","தெரிய வரவி ...... யணுகாதே","செறிவு மறிவு முறவு மனைய","திகழு மடிகள் ...... தரவேணும்","பரிய வரையி னரிவை மருவு","பரம ரருளு ...... முருகோனே","பழன முழவர் கொழுவி லெழுது","பழைய பழநி ...... யமர்வோனே","அரிய மயனும் வெருவ வுருவ","அரிய கிரியை ...... யெறிவோனே","அயிலு மயிலு மறமு நிறமும்","அழகு முடைய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks that the cruel trident-bearer who drives the great black buffalo, rushing in with his tightening noose when lives end, and the jackals and fire that claim the body, not come near him, and that Murugan give him his feet, which shine with closeness, knowledge and kinship. The praise names him the son born of the grace of the Lord joined to the mountain's daughter, dwelling at old Palani where the ploughmen's shares write in the fields, the one who pierced the hard mountain with the vel so that even Mayan feared, and the Lord with lance, peacock, valour, colour and beauty.","n":"Death here is திரிசூலன், the trident-bearer, with buffalo and noose; the jackal and fire (நரியும் எரியும் உரிமை) are the claims on the corpse, earth burial or burning. செறிவும் அறிவும் உறவும் lists what the feet give; the meaning of each word there is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kootru","yaakkai","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:12"},"p:130":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":130,"t":["கரிய மேகம தோஇரு ளோகுழல்","அரிய பூரண மாமதி யோமுகம்","கணைகொ லோஅயில் வேலது வோவிழி ...... யிதழ்பாகோ","கமுகு தானிக ரோவளை யோகளம்","அரிய மாமல ரோதுளி ரோகரம்","கனக மேரது வோகுட மோமுலை ...... மொழிதேனோ","கருணை மால்துயி லாலிலை யோவயி","றிடைய தீரொரு நூலது வோவென","கனக மாமயில் போல்மட வாருடன் ...... மிகநாடி","கசட னாய்வய தாயொரு நூறுசெல்","வதனின் மேலென தாவியை நீயிரு","கமல மீதினி லேவர வேயருள் ...... புரிவாயே","திரிபு ராதிகள் நீறெழ வேமிக","மதனை யேவிழி யால்விழ வேசெயும்","சிவசொ ரூபம கேசுர னீடிய ...... தனயோனே","சினம தாய்வரு சூரர்கள் வேரற","அமரர் வானவர் வாடிடு தேவர்கள்","சிறைகள் மீளவு மேவடி வேல்விடு ...... முருகோனே","பரிவு சேர்கம லாலய சீதன","மருவு வார்திரு மாலரி நாரணர்","பழைய மாயவர் மாதவ னார்திரு ...... மருகோனே","பனக மாமணி தேவிக்ரு பாகரி","குமர னேபதி னாலுல கோர்புகழ்","பழநி மாமலை மீதினி லேயுறை ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks a run of questions about women: is the hair a black cloud or darkness, the face the full moon, the eyes arrows or a vel, the breasts golden Meru or pots, the belly the banyan leaf on which Vishnu sleeps, the waist a thread; seeking such women like golden peacocks, he calls himself worthless and asks that after his hundred years pass his life may come to Murugan's two lotus feet. The praise names him son of Mahesvara who burned the three cities and Kama with his eye, the vel that freed the gods from Sura's prison, nephew of Narayana, and son of the merciful goddess, at Palani praised by the fourteen worlds.","n":"The comparisons are put as questions (மேகமதோ இருளோ), the infatuated man's own way of seeing. கசடன், the worthless one, is the self-judgement. வயதாய் ஒரு நூறு செல்வதனின் மேல் I read as asking for the feet when the full life is over; it could also mean before.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kutram","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:12"},"p:131":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":131,"t":["கரியிணை கோடெனத் தனமசைத் தாடிநற்","கயல்விழிப் பார்வையிற் ...... பொருள்பேசிக்","கலையிழுத் தேகுலுக் கெனநகைத் தேமயற்","கலதியிட் டேயழைத் ...... தணையூடே","செருமிவித் தாரசிற் றிடைதுடித் தாடமற்","றிறமளித் தேபொருட் ...... பறிமாதர்","செயலிழுக் காமலிக் கலியுகத் தேபுகழ்ச்","சிவபதபத் தேபதித் ...... தருள்வாயே","திரிபுரக் கோலவெற் பழல்கொளச் சீர்நகைச்","சிறிதருட் டேவருட் ...... புதல்வோனே","திரைகடற் கோவெனக் குவடுகட் டூள்படத்","திருடர்கெட் டோ டவிட் ...... டிடும்வேலா","பரிமளப் பாகலிற் கனிகளைப் பீறிநற்","படியினிட் டேகுரக் ...... கினமாடும்","பழநியிற் சீருறப் புகழ்குறப் பாவையைப்","பரிவுறச் சேர்மணப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes courtesans who sway their breasts, talk of money with carp-eyed glances, tug at clothes, laugh, cause infatuation and confusion and then embrace, women who snatch a man's wealth; he asks that, without falling into their ways in this Kali age, Murugan fix him in devotion to Siva's state. The praise names him son of the god who burned the three cities with a small smile, the vel that made the sea cry out and the thieves flee, and the bridegroom of the hunter girl at Palani, where monkeys tear open fragrant fruit and play.","n":"கயல்விழிப் பார்வையில் பொருள் பேசி, talking of money with the eyes, and பொருட்பறி மாதர், women who snatch wealth, name the trade. The plea places the poet in the Kali age (இக்கலியுகத்தே) and asks for சிவபதம், Siva's state, from Murugan. The split of சிவபதபத்தே is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","mayal","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:12"},"p:132":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":132,"t":["கருகி யகன்று வரிசெறி கண்கள்","கயல்நிக ரென்று ...... துதிபேசிக்","கலைசுரு ளொன்று மிடைபடு கின்ற","கடிவிட முண்டு ...... பலநாளும்","விரகுறு சண்ட வினையுடல் கொண்டு","விதிவழி நின்று ...... தளராதே","விரைகமழ் தொங்கல் மருவிய துங்க","விதபத மென்று ...... பெறுவேனோ","முருக கடம்ப குறமகள் பங்க","முறையென அண்டர் ...... முறைபேச","முதுதிரை யொன்ற வருதிறல் வஞ்ச","முரணசுர் வென்ற ...... வடிவேலா","பரிமள இன்ப மரகத துங்க","பகடித வென்றி ...... மயில்வீரா","பறிதலை குண்டர் கழுநிரை கண்டு","பழநிய மர்ந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says that, praising women's dark, wide eyes as carp and swallowing the poison of the waist where the garment gathers, he has passed many days in a body of fierce deeds driven by passion, and asks that he not falter on the way fate set but gain Murugan's high feet fragrant with garlands. The praise names Muruga of the kadamba with the hunter girl at his side, whose vel defeated the deceitful asura who came with the old sea when the gods cried for justice, and who settled at Palani after seeing the shaven-headed Jains on rows of stakes.","n":"விதிவழி நின்று, standing on fate's path, sets the confession inside fate and deed (வினை உடல், the body of deeds). பறிதலைக் குண்டர் கழுநிரை கண்டு, having seen the hair-plucked Jains in rows on stakes, is again the Madurai story told of Sambandar, now given to Murugan.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","oozh","vinai","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:12"},"p:133":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":133,"t":["கருப்புவிலில் மருப்பகழி தொடுத்துமதன் விடுத்தனைய","கடைக்கணொடு சிரித்தணுகு","கருத்தினால் விரகுசெய் ...... மடமாதர்","கதக்களிறு திடுக்கமுற மதர்த்துமிக வெதிர்த்துமலை","கனத்தவிரு தனத்தின்மிசை","கலக்குமோ கனமதில் ...... மருளாமே","ஒருப்படுதல் விருப்புடைமை மனத்தில்வர நினைத்தருளி","யுனைப்புகழு மெனைப்புவியில்","ஒருத்தனாம் வகைதிரு ...... அருளாலே","உருத்திரனும் விருத்திபெற அநுக்கிரகி யெனக்குறுகி","யுரைக்கமறை யடுத்து பொருள்","உணர்த்துநா ளடிமையு ...... முடையேனோ","பருப்பதமு முருப்பெரிய அரக்கர்களு மிரைக்குமெழு","படிக்கடலு மலைக்கவல","பருத்ததோ கையில்வரு ...... முருகோனே","பதித்தமர கதத்தினுட னிரத்னமணி நிரைத்தபல","பணிப்பனிரு புயச்சயில","பரக்கவே இயல்தெரி ...... வயலு஡ரா","திருப்புகழை யுரைப்பவர்கள் படிப்பவர்கள் மிடிப்பகைமை","செயித்தருளு மிசைப்பிரிய","திருத்தமா தவர்புகழ் ...... குருநாதா","சிலைக்குறவ ரிலைக்குடிலில் புகைக்களக முகிற்புடைசெல்","திருப்பழநி மலைக்குளுறை","திருக்கைவே லழகிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks not to be bewildered by the infatuation that women stir with sidelong eyes like the arrows Kama shoots from his sugarcane bow and with breasts that rival a rutting elephant, and asks whether he, a servant who praises Murugan, will have the day when Murugan, by grace, teaches him the meaning of the Veda, as he did when Rudra came near asking for grace. The praise names the Lord on the great peacock that shook mountains, giant demons and the seven seas, with twelve jewelled arms like hills, lord of Vayalur, the guru who defeats poverty and enmity for those who recite and read the Thiruppugazh, at Palani where hunters' smoke rises among the clouds.","n":"உருத்திரனும் விருத்திபெற ... மறை அடுத்து பொருள் உணர்த்து நாள்: the poet asks for the teaching Murugan gave Siva, and asks it as அடிமை, a servant. The song names its own book: திருப்புகழை உரைப்பவர்கள் படிப்பவர்கள் are promised victory over want (மிடி) and enmity. The syntax of the Rudra line is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["guru","gnaanam","thondu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:12"},"p:134":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":134,"t":["கருவினுரு வாகி வந்து வயதளவி லேவ ளர்ந்து","கலைகள்பல வேதெ ரிந்து ...... மதனாலே","கரியகுழல் மாதர் தங்க ளடிசுவடு மார்பு தைந்து","கவலைபெரி தாகி நொந்து ...... மிகவாடி","அரகரசி வாய வென்று தினமுநினை யாமல் நின்று","அறுசமய நீதி யொன்று ...... மறியாமல்","அசனமிடு வார்கள் தங்கள் மனைகள்தலை வாசல் நின்று","அநுதினமு நாண மின்றி ...... யழிவேனோ","உரகபட மேல்வ ளர்ந்த பெரியபெரு மாள ரங்கர்","உலகளவு மால்ம கிழ்ந்த ...... மருகோனே","உபயகுல தீப துங்க விருதுகவி ராஜ சிங்க","உறைபுகலி யூரி லன்று ...... வருவோனே","பரவைமனை மீதி லன்று ஒருபொழுது தூது சென்ற","பரமனரு ளால்வ ளர்ந்த ...... குமரேசா","பகையசுரர் சேனை கொன்று அமரர்சிறை மீள வென்று","பழநிமலை மீதில் நின்ற ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet traces his life from the womb through growing up and learning many arts, then being kicked on the chest by dark-haired women's feet and wasting in grief, never thinking daily 'Hara Hara Sivaya', knowing nothing of the six religions, and standing every day without shame at the gates of those who give food; he asks whether he will perish so. The praise names Murugan as nephew of Ranganatha on the serpent and of Vishnu who measured the world, the lamp of both lines and lion of honoured poets who came to Pukali that day, and Kumaresa raised by the grace of the Lord who went once as messenger to Paravai's house, at Palani.","n":"The life runs from கருவின் உரு to begging at food-givers' doors (அசனமிடுவார்கள் ... தலைவாசல்), with women in the middle. உபயகுல தீப ... விருதுகவி ராஜசிங்க ... புகலியூரில் அன்று வருவோனே identifies Murugan with Sambandar, born at Pukali (Sirkazhi). The messenger to Paravai is Siva sent by Sundarar to his wife, so the praise gathers the Tamil saints round Murugan.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karu","kaamam","iravu","thamizh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:13"},"p:135":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":135,"t":["கலக வாள்விழி வேலோ சேலோ","மதுர வாய்மொழி தேனோ பாலோ","கரிய வார்குழல் காரோ கானோ ...... துவரோவாய்","களமு நீள்கமு கோதோள் வேயோ","உதர மானது மாலேர் பாயோ","களப வார்முலை மேரோ கோடோ ...... இடைதானும்","இழைய தோமலர் வேதா வானோ","னெழுதி னானிலை யோவாய் பேசீ","ரிதென மோனமி னாரே பா஡ண ...... ரெனமாதர்","இருகண் மாயையி ஧ல்முழ் காதே","யுனது காவிய நூலா ராய்வே","னிடர்ப டாதருள் வாழ்வே நீயே ...... தரவேணும்","அலைவி லாதுயர் வானோ ரானோர்","நிலைமை யேகுறி வேலா சீலா","அடியர் பாலரு ளீவாய் நீபார் ...... மணிமார்பா","அழகு லாவுவி சாகா வாகா","ரிபமி னாள்மகிழ் கேள்வா தாழ்வா","ரயலு லாவிய சீலா கோலா ...... கலவீரா","வலபை கேள்வர்பி னானாய் கானார்","குறவர் மாதும னாளா நாளார்","வனச மேல்வரு தேவா மூவா ...... மயில்வாழ்வே","மதுர ஞானவி நோதா நாதா","பழநி மேவுகு மாரா தீரா","மயுர வாகன தேவா வானோர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet repeats the questions men ask about women, whether the eyes are vel or carp, the words honey or milk, the hair cloud or forest, the breasts Meru or tusks, the waist a thread, and whether the Creator did not draw them so; he asks not to drown in the maya of such women's eyes, and promises to study Murugan's poems if given a life free of trouble. The praise is a string of rhyming names: Visakha, joy of the elephant-reared girl, husband of the hunters' daughter, younger brother of Vallabha's husband, ageless god, lord of sweet knowledge, Kumara of Palani on the peacock.","n":"இருகண் மாயையில் மூழ்காதே: maya here is the enchantment of the eyes. The cure offered is reading: உனது காவிய நூல் ஆராய்வேன், I will study the poems about you. Two words are corrupt, and the women's or the man's voice in the questions is not clear to me.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["kan","mayal","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:13"},"p:136":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":136,"t":["கலகக் கயல்விழி போர்செய வேள்படை","நடுவிற் புடைவரு பாபிகள் கோபிகள்","கனியக் கனியவு மேமொழி பேசிய ...... விலைமாதர்","கலவித் தொழினல மேயினி தாமென","மனமிப் படிதின மேயுழ லாவகை","கருளைப் படியெனை யாளவு மேயருள் ...... தரவேணும்","இலவுக் கிளையெனும் வாய்வளி நாயகி","குழையத் தழுவிய மேன்முயி னாலுயர்","இசைபெற் றருளிய காமுக னாகிய ...... வடிவோனே","இதமிக் கருமறை வேதிய ரானவர்","புகலத் தயவுட னேயருள் மேன்மைகள்","இசையத் தருமநு கூலவ சீகர ...... முதல்வோனே","நிலவைச் சடைமிசை யேபுனை காரணர்","செவியிற் பிரணவ மோதிய தேசிக","நிருதர்க் கொருபகை யாளியு மாகிய ...... சுடர்வேலா","நிமலக் குருபர ஆறிரு பார்வையும்","அருளைத் தரஅடி யார்தமை நாடொறும்","நிகரற் றவரென வேமகிழ் கூர்தரு ...... முரியோனே","பலவிற் கனிபணை மீறிய மாமர","முருகிற் கனியுட னேநெடு வாளைகள்","பரவித் தனியுதிர் சோலைகள் மேவிய ...... வகையாலே","பழனத் துழவர்க ளேரிட வேவிளை","கழனிப் புரவுகள் போதவு மீறிய","பழநிச் சிவகிரி மீதினி லேவளர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of courtesans whose quarrelsome carp eyes make war as Kama's army, sinful, angry women who speak ever so sweetly, and asks for grace so that his mind will not wander daily thinking union with them is sweet. The praise names Murugan the lover who embraced Valli till she melted, the giver of favours to Vedic Brahmins, the teacher who spoke the Pranava into the ear of the moon-crowned Siva, the enemy of the demons, and the pure guru whose twelve eyes give grace and who delights in his devotees, at Palani Sivagiri among groves and fields.","n":"செவியிற் பிரணவம் ஓதிய தேசிக names the Om story outright. The praise calls Murugan himself காமுகன், the lover, for Valli, in the same song that confesses desire for courtesans; I read the contrast as deliberate, love given to the god against love sold.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","kaamam","guru","piranavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:13"},"p:137":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":137,"t":["கலவியி லிச்சித் திரங்கி நின்றிரு","கனதனம் விற்கச் சமைந்த மங்கையர்","கயல்கள் சிவப்பப் பரிந்து நண்பொடு ...... மின்ப்முறிக்","கனியித ழுற்றுற் றருந்தி யங்குறு","மவச மிகுத்துப் பொருந்தி யின்புறு","கலகம் விளைத்துக் கலந்து மண்டணை ...... யங்கமீதே","குலவிய நற்கைத் தலங்கொ டங்கணை","கொடியிடை மெத்தத் துவண்டு தண்புயல்","குழலள கக்கட் டவிழ்ந்து பண்டையி ...... லங்கம்வேறாய்க்","குறிதரு வட்டத் தடர்ந்த சிந்துர","முகதல முத்துப் பொலிந்தி லங்கிட","கொடிய மயற்செய்ப் பெருந்த டந்தனில் ...... மங்கலாமோ","இலகிய சித்ரப் புனந்த னிந்துறை","குறமகள் கச்சுக் கிடந்த கொங்கைமி","னினிதுறு பத்மப் பதம்ப ணிந்தருள் ...... கந்தவேளே","எழுகடல் வற்றப் பெருங்கொ டுங்கிரி","யிடிபட மிக்கப் ப்ரசண்டம் விண்டுறு","மிகலர்ப தைக்கத் தடிந்தி லங்கிய ...... செங்கைவேலா","பலவித நற்கற் படர்ந்த சுந்தரி","பயில்தரு வெற்புத் தருஞ்செ ழுங்கொடி","பணைமுலை மெத்தப் பொதிந்து பண்புறு ...... கின்றபாலைப்","பலதிசை மெச்சத் தெரிந்த செந்தமிழ்","பகரென இச்சித் துகந்து கொண்டருள்","பழநியில் வெற்பிற் றிகழ்ந்து நின்றருள் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes at length lovemaking with women ready to sell their breasts, drinking from their lips, hair loosening, the waist bending and the forehead mark smudged, and asks whether he must fade in the great pool of cruel infatuation. The praise names Kandavel who bowed at the lotus feet of the hunter girl, the vel that dried the seven seas and shattered the mountain, and Murugan who gladly accepted the milk of the mountain's daughter, bidden to sing Tamil praised in every direction, at Palani.","n":"கனதனம் விற்கச் சமைந்த, ready to sell their breasts, marks them as courtesans. The milk from the breast of the mountain's daughter given with செந்தமிழ் பகர் is the story told of Sambandar, fed by Parvati before he sang; the song gives it to Murugan. As in 15, Murugan bows before Valli (பதம் பணிந்தருள்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","varaivinmagalir","mayal","thamizh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:13"},"p:138":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":138,"t":["கலைகொடு பவுத்தர் காம கருமிகள் துருக்கர் மாய","கபிலர்பக ரக்க ணாதர் ...... உலகாயர்","கலகமிடு தர்க்கர் வாம பயிரவர் விருத்த ரோடு","கலகலென மிக்க நூல்க ...... ளதனாலே","சிலுகியெதிர் குத்தி வாது செயவுமொரு வர்க்கு நீதி","தெரிவரிய சித்தி யான ...... வுபதேசந்","தெரிதர விளக்கி ஞான தரிசந மளித்து வீறு","திருவடி யெனக்கு நேர்வ ...... தொருநாளே","கெலையுற எதிர்த்த கோர இபமுக அரக்க னோடு","குரகத முகத்தர் சீய ...... முகவீரர்","குறையுட லெடுத்து வீசி யலகையொடு பத்ர காளி","குலவியிட வெற்றி வேலை ...... விடுவோனே","பலமிகு புனத்து லாவு குறவநிதை சித்ர பார","பரிமள தனத்தில் மேவு ...... மணிமார்பா","படைபொருது மிக்க யூக மழைமுகிலை யொட்டி யேறு","பழநிமலை யுற்ற தேவர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet lists the schools that clamour with their many books, the Buddhists, the ritualists, the Turks, the followers of Kapila and Kanada, the materialists, the quarrelling logicians and the left-hand Bhairavas, and asks whether there will be a day when Murugan makes clear the teaching that is hard for anyone to discern, gives him the vision of knowledge and grants him his feet. The praise names the vel thrown at the elephant-faced demon and the horse-faced and lion-faced warriors while ghouls and Bhadrakali danced, the chest that rests on the hunter girl's breasts, and Palani, which rises to the rain clouds.","n":"The list names Islam (துருக்கர்) beside the Indian schools, as one more voice in the dispute. The plea is for உபதேசம் and ஞான தரிசனம்: not to win the debate but to be taught past it. காம கருமிகள் may be the ritualists of desire-rites; that identification is mine.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["samayam","gnaanam","guru","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:13"},"p:139":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":139,"t":["களபமுலை யைத்தி றந்து தளவ நகை யைக்கொ ணர்ந்து","கயலொடுப கைத்த கண்கள் ...... குழைதாவக்","கரியகுழ லைப்ப கிர்ந்து மலர்சொருகு கொப்ப விழந்து","கடியிருளு டுக்கு லங்க ...... ளெனவீழ","முழுமதி யெனச்சி றந்த நகைமுக மினுக்கி யின்ப","முருகிதழ்சி வப்ப நின்று ...... விலைகூறி","முதலுளது கைப்பு குந்து அழகுதுகி லைத்தி றந்து","முடுகுமவ ருக்கி ரங்கி ...... மெலிவேனோ","இளமதி கடுக்கை தும்பை அரவணி பவர்க்கி சைந்து","இனியபொரு ளைப்ப கர்ந்த ...... குருநாதா","இபமுகவ னுக்கு கந்த இளையவ மருக்க டம்ப","எனதுதலை யிற்ப தங்க ...... ளருள்வோனே","குழகென எடுத்து கந்த உமைமுலை பிடித்த ருந்து","குமரசிவ வெற்ப மர்ந்த ...... குகவேலா","குடிலொடு மிகச்செ றிந்த இதணுள புனத்தி ருந்த","குரவர்மக ளைப்பு ணர்ந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes courtesans who bare their sandal-smeared breasts, show a jasmine smile, let flowers fall from loosened hair like stars, stand and name a price, and once the money is in their hands open their garments and press close, and asks whether he will waste away for them. The praise names Murugan the guru who told the sweet meaning to Siva of the crescent, konrai and serpent, brother of the elephant-faced god, the one who grants his feet on the poet's head, the child who drank at Uma's breast when she lifted him, and the lover of the hunters' daughter at Sivagiri.","n":"விலைகூறி, naming the price, and முதல் உளது கைப்புகுந்து, once the capital is in hand, make the trade plain. எனது தலையில் பதங்கள் அருள்வோனே, who grants his feet on my head, is the poet speaking of a gift already given, the feet set on the devotee's head.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","guru","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:13"},"p:140":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":140,"t":["கறுத்த குழலணி மலரணி பொங்கப்","பதித்த சிலைநுத லணிதில தம்பொற்","கணைக்கு நிகர்விழி சுழலெழு கஞ்சச் ...... சிரமான","கழுத்தி லுறுமணி வளைகுழை மின்னக்","குவட்டு முலையசை படஇடை யண்பைக்","கமைத்த கலையிறு குறுதுவள் வஞ்சிக் ...... கொடிபோலச்","சிறுத்த களமிகு மதமொழு கின்சொற்","குயிற்க ளெனமட மயிலெகி னங்கட்","டிருக்கு நடைபழ கிகள்கள் பங்கச் ...... சுடைமாதர்","திகைத்த தனமொடு பொருள்பறி யொண்கட்","குவட்டி யவர்வலை யழலுறு பங்கத்","திடக்கு தலைபுலை யவர்வழி யின்பைத் ...... தவிர்வேனோ","பறித்த விழிதலை மழுவுழை செங்கைச்","செழித்த சிவபர னிதழிநல் தும்பைப்","படித்த மதியற லரவணி சம்புக் ...... குருநாதா","பருத்த அசுரர்க ளுடன்மலை துஞ்சக்","கொதித்த அலைகட லெரிபட செம்பொற்","படைக்கை மணியயில் விடுநட னங்கொட் ...... கதிர்வேலா","தெறித்து விழியர வுடல்நிமி ரம்பொற்","குவட்டொ டிகைகிரி பொடிபட சண்டச்","சிறப்பு மயில்மிசை பவரிகொ ளும்பொற் ...... றிருபாதா","சிறக்கு மழகிய திருமகள் வஞ்சிக்","குறத்தி மகளுமை மருமகள் கொங்கைச்","சிலைக்கு ளணைகுக சிவமலை கந்தப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women with flowered hair, brow-mark, arrow-eyes, conch necks, breasts and a creeper-thin waist, cuckoo voices and the gait of peahens and swans, women who seize a man's wealth and spread nets, and asks whether he will give up the base pleasure of following them in the burning mire. The praise names Murugan the guru of Sambhu, who holds axe and deer and wears konrai, thumbai, moon, river and snake; the vel that boiled the sea and laid the demons and mountains to sleep; the feet on the whirling peacock; and the husband of the hunter girl, daughter of Lakshmi and daughter-in-law of Uma, at Sivamalai.","n":"பொருள்பறி, seizing wealth, and புலையவர் வழி இன்பு, the pleasure of the base path, carry the judgement. திருமகள் ... குறத்தி மகள், making Valli Lakshmi's daughter, follows the tradition of her birth. The opening words of Siva's description (பறித்த விழி) I cannot place, and the long description of the women has compressed lines.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","kaamam","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:13"},"p:141":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":141,"t":["கனக கும்பமி ரண்டு நேர்மலை","யெனநெ ருங்குகு ரும்பை மாமணி","கதிர்சி றந்தவ டங்கு லாவிய ...... முந்துசூதம்","கடையில் நின்றுப ரந்து நாடொறு","மிளகி விஞ்சியெ ழுந்த கோமள","களப குங்கும கொங்கை யானையை ...... யின்பமாக","அனைவ ருங்கொளு மென்று மேவிலை","யிடும டந்தையர் தங்கள் தோதக","மதின்ம ருண்டு துவண்ட வாசையில் ...... நைந்துபாயல்","அவச மன்கொளு மின்ப சாகர","முழுகும் வஞ்சக நெஞ்சை யேயொழி","தருப தங்கதி யெம்பி ரானருள் ...... தந்திடாயோ","தனத னந்தன தந்த னாவென","டிகுகு டிங்குகு டிங்கு பேரிகை","தகுதி திந்திகு திந்த தோவென ...... வுந்துதாளந்","தமர சஞ்சலி சஞ்ச லாவென","முழவு டுண்டுடு டுண்டு டூவென","தருண கிண்கிணி கிண்கி ணாரமு ...... முந்தவோதும்","பணிப தங்கய மெண்டி சாமுக","கரிய டங்கலு மண்ட கோளகை","பதறி நின்றிட நின்று தோதக ...... என்றுதோகை","பவுரி கொண்டிட மண்டி யேவரு","நிசிச ரன்கிளை கொன்ற வேலவ","பழநி யங்கிரி யின்கண் மேவிய ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women who stand every day at the doorway with breasts like golden pots, setting a price so that all may have their pleasure, and confesses that, bewildered by their tricks, he has wasted in desire on the bed and drowned in a sea of pleasure; he asks Murugan to remove his deceitful heart and give him the refuge of his feet. The praise is a long passage of drum syllables, the peacock dancing so that the elephants of the eight directions and the cosmic sphere tremble, and the vel that killed the demon's kin, at Palani.","n":"அனைவரும் கொளும் என்று மேவு இலையிடும், setting a price so that all may take, is the common woman of Kural chapter 92. The deceit confessed is the poet's own (வஞ்சக நெஞ்சு) as well as the women's (தோதகம்). The drum syllables (தனதனந்தன, டிகுகு, டுண்டுடு) are rhythm.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:13"},"p:142":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":142,"t":["கனத்திறுகிப் பெருத்திளகிப்","பணைத்துமணத் திதத்துமுகக்","கறுப்புமிகுத் தடர்த்துநிகர்த் ...... தலமேராய்","கவட்டையுமெத் தடக்கிமதர்த்","தறக்கெருவித் திதத்திடுநற்","கலைச்சவுளித் தலைக்குலவிக் ...... களிகூருந்","தனத்தியர்கட் கிதத்துமிகுத்","தனற்குண்மெழுக் கெனப்புவியிற்","றவித்திழிசொற் பவக்கடலுற் ...... றயர்வாலே","சலித்தவெறித் துடக்குமனத்","திடக்கனெனச் சிரிக்கமயற்","சலத்தின்வசைக் கிணக்கமுறக் ...... கடவேனோ","புனத்தின்மலைக் குறத்தியுயர்த்","திருக்குதனக் குடத்தினறைப்","புயத்தவநற் கருத்தையுடைக் ...... குகவீரா","பொருப்பரசற் கிரக்கமொடுற்","றறற்சடிலத் தவச்சிவனிற்","புலச்சிதனக் கிதத்தைமிகுத் ...... திடுநாதா","சினத்தெதிர்துட் டரக்கர்தமைத்","திகைத்துவிழக் கணப்பொழுதிற்","சிதைத்திடுநற் கதிர்க்கைபடைத் ...... துடையோனே","செருக்கொடுநற் றவக்கமலத்","தயற்குமரிக் கருட்புரிசைத்","திருப்பழநிக் கிரிக்குமரப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes the breasts of women who delight in fine garments, and says that, melting like wax in fire for them and sunk in the sea of birth with debased words, he is like a fool to be laughed at; he asks whether he must be bound to the shame of this infatuation. The praise names Guha whose shoulders bear the scent of the hunter girl's breasts, the lord who gave delight to the mountain king's daughter, consort of the ascetic Siva with the river in his hair, the vel that destroyed the demons in an instant, and Kumara of Palani.","n":"அனற்குள் மெழுக்கென, like wax in fire, and பவக்கடல், the sea of births, set the infatuation within the round of births. The song's syllables are packed tightly into its rhythm, and several word-splits in both halves, especially the last two lines, are my guesses.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mayal","kaamam","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:13"},"p:143":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":143,"t":["கனமா யெழுந்துவெற் பெனவே யுயர்ந்துகற்","புரமா ரணந்துளுத் ...... திடுமானார்","கனிவா யுகந்துசிக் கெனவே யணைந்துகைப்","பொருளே யிழந்துவிட் ...... டயர்வாயே","மனமே தளர்ந்துவிக் கலுமே யெழுந்துமட்","டறவே யுலந்துசுக் ...... கதுபோலே","வசமே யழிந்துவுக் கிடுநோய் துறந்துவைப்","பெனவே நினைந்துனைப் ...... புகழ்வேனோ","புனவேடர் தந்தபொற் குறமாது இன்புறப்","புணர்காதல் கொண்டஅக் ...... கிழவோனே","புனலேழு மங்கவெற் பொடுசூர் சிரங்கள்பொட்","டெழவே லெறிந்தவுக் ...... கிரவீரா","தினமேவு குங்குமப் புயவாச கிண்கிணிச்","சிறுகீத செம்பதத் ...... தருளாளா","சிவலோக சங்கரிக் கிறைபால பைங்கயத்","திருவா வினன்குடிப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says that after embracing women with breasts rising like mountains and anointed with camphor, he lost the wealth in his hand and grew weary, his mind failing, hiccups rising, dried out like ginger, and asks whether, leaving this wasting disease, he will praise Murugan as his treasure. The praise names him the old man who loved the golden hunter girl given by the forest hunters, the fierce hero whose vel shattered the seas, the mountain and Sura's heads, the gracious one whose small feet ring with anklet bells, and the son of Sankari, at Thiru Avinankudi.","n":"கைப்பொருளே இழந்து, losing the wealth in the hand, links the women to the man's ruin. சுக்கதுபோலே, like dried ginger, is the body after. அக்கிழவோனே, that old man, is Murugan's disguise as an old man to win Valli.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","noy","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:13"},"p:144":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":144,"t":["கார ணிந்தவரைப் பார டர்ந்துவினைக்","காதல் நெஞ்சயரத் ...... தடுமாறிக்","கான ரம்புதிரத் தோல்வ ழும்புறுபொய்க்","காய மொன்றுபொறுத் ...... தடியேனும்","தாரி ணங்குகுழற் கூர ணிந்தவிழிச்","சாப மொன்றுநுதற் ...... கொடியார்தம்","தாள்ப ணிந்தவர்பொற் றோள்வி ரும்பிமிகத்","தாழ்வ டைந்துலையத் ...... தகுமோதான்","சூர னங்கம்விழத் தேவர் நின்றுதொழத்","தோய முஞ்சுவறப் ...... பொரும்வேலா","தூய்மை கொண்டகுறத் தோகை நின்றபுனச்","சூழ்பெ ருங்கிரியிற் ...... றிரிவோனே","ஆர ணன்கருடக் கேத னன்தொழமுற்","றால முண்டவருக் ...... குரியோனே","ஆலை யும்பழனச் சோலை யும்புடைசுற்","றாவி னன்குடியிற் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says that, his heart wavering with a love bound to deeds and bearing a false body of flesh, blood, skin and fat, he has bowed at the feet of women with flowered hair, sharp eyes and bow brows and desired their golden shoulders, and asks whether it is right for him to sink so low and perish. The praise names the vel that fought until Sura fell and the waters dried while the gods stood worshipping, the one who roams the hill field where the pure hunter peahen stood, the one dear to the Lord who drank the poison, whom Brahma and Garuda-bannered Vishnu worship, at Thiru Avinankudi among sugar mills and groves.","n":"தாள் பணிந்து, bowing at their feet, is worship given to the wrong feet, and தாழ்வடைந்து உலைய, sinking and perishing, is its result. பொய்க் காயம், the false body, is listed as flesh, blood, skin and fat.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","yaakkai","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:13"},"p:145":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":145,"t":["குரம்பை மலசலம் வழுவளு நிணமொடு","எலும்பு அணிசரி தசையிரல் குடல்நெதி","குலைந்த செயிர்மயிர் குருதியொ டிவைபல ...... கசுமாலக்","குடின்பு குதுமவ ரவர்கடு கொடுமையர்","இடும்ப ரொருவழி யிணையிலர் கசடர்கள்","குரங்க ரறிவிலர் நெறியிலர் மிருகணை ...... விறலான","சரம்ப ருறவனை நரகனை துரகனை","இரங்கு கலியனை பரிவுறு சடலனை","சவுந்த ரிகமுக சரவண பதமொடு ...... மயிலேறித்","தழைந்த சிவசுடர் தனையென மனதினில்","அழுந்த வுரைசெய வருமுக நகையொளி","தழைந்த நயனமு மிருமலர் சரணமு ...... மறவேனே","இரும்பை வகுளமொ டியைபல முகில்பொழி","லுறைந்த குயிலளி யொலிபர விடமயி","லிசைந்து நடமிடு மிணையிலி புலிநகர் ...... வளநாடா","இருண்ட குவடிடி பொடிபட வெகுமுக","டெரிந்து மகரமொ டிசைகரி குமுறுக","இரைந்த அசுரரொ டிபபரி யமபுரம் ...... விடும்வேளே","சிரம்பொ னயனொடு முநிவர்க ளமரர்கள்","அரம்பை மகளிரொ டரகர சிவசிவ","செயம்பு வெனநட மிடுபத மழகியர் ...... குருநாதா","செழும்ப வளவொளி நகைமுக மதிநகு","சிறந்த குறமக ளிணைமுலை புதைபட","செயங்கொ டணைகுக சிவமலை மருவிய பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet calls the body a hut of excrement, urine, fat, bone, flesh, guts, hair and blood, and those who live in it cruel, ignorant and pathless; calling himself hellish and wretched, he says he will not forget the smiling faces, shining eyes and two flower feet of Murugan, who came on the peacock so that he might hold in his mind 'Saravana' and the light of Siva. The praise names Murugan lord of the land of Puliyur, where cuckoos and bees sound in the groves and peacocks dance, the one who sent the asuras with their elephants and horses to Yama's city, guru of the Lord whose dancing feet Brahma, sages, gods and Rambha hail with 'Hara Hara Siva Siva', and the lover of the hunter girl at Sivamalai.","n":"குரம்பை, the hut, is the body, as in Saiva poetry generally. The string of names for himself (நரகனை, கலியனை, சடலனை) is the poet's self-abuse before the grace. புலிநகர் is Puliyur, Chidambaram, the dancer's city. The middle lines are hard to construe, and whether the cruel ones are all who live in bodies or the women is my reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","kutram","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:13"},"p:146":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":146,"t":["குருதி மலசல மொழுகு நரகுட","லரிய புழுவது நெளியு முடல்மத","குருபி நிணசதை விளையு முளைசளி ...... யுடலு஡டே","குடிக ளெனபல குடிகை வலிகொடி","குமர வலிதலை வயிறு வலியென","கொடுமை யெனபிணி கலக மிடுமிதை ...... யடல்பேணி","மருவி மதனனுள் கரிய புளகித","மணிய சலபல கவடி மலர்புனை","மதன கலைகொடு குவடு மலைதனில் ...... மயலாகா","மனது துயரற வினைகள் சிதறிட","மதன பிணியொடு கலைகள் சிதறிட","மனது பதமுற வெனது தலைபத ...... மருள்வாயே","நிருதர் பொடிபட அமரர் பதிபெற","நிசித அரவளை முடிகள் சிதறிட","நெரிய கிரிகட லெரிய வுருவிய ...... கதிர்வேலா","நிறைய மலர்பொழி யமரர் முநிவரும்","நிருப குருபர குமர சரணென","நெடிய முகிலுடல் கிழிய வருபரி ...... மயிலோனே","பருதி மதிகனல் விழிய சிவனிட","மருவு மொருமலை யரையர் திருமகள்","படிவ முகிலென அரியி னிளையவ ...... ளருள்பாலா","பரம கணபதி யயலின் மதகரி","வடிவு கொடுவர விரவு குறமக","ளபய மெனவணை பழநி மருவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes the body, oozing blood, filth and urine, with worms writhing, flesh, marrow and phlegm, as a house whose tenants are diseases, fits, colic, headache and stomach-ache, and confesses he has cherished it and been infatuated with women; he asks that his grief and deeds be scattered and that Murugan place his feet on his head. The praise names the vel that pierced the mountain and burned the sea, the peacock rider whom gods and sages hail as guru and refuge, the son of the mountain's daughter, sister of Hari, and the one whom the hunter girl embraced in fear when Ganapati came as an elephant, at Palani.","n":"குடிகள் எனப் பல ... பிணி: the diseases are the body's tenants. The plea is the same as 139's, the feet on the head (எனது தலை பதம் அருள்வாயே). The elephant that frightened Valli into Murugan's arms is told outright (கணபதி ... மதகரி வடிவு கொடு வர).","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["yaakkai","noy","mayal","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:13"},"p:147":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":147,"t":["குழல டவிமுகில் பொழில்வி ரவில்நுதல்","குமுத வதரமு ...... றுவலாரம்","குழைம கரம்வளை மொழிகு யிலமுது","குயமு ளரிமுகை ...... கிரிசூது","விழிக யலயில்ப கழிவ ருணிகரு","விளைகு வளைவிட ...... மெனநாயேன்","மிகவ ரிவையரை அவநெ றிகள்சொலி","வெறிது ளம்விதன ...... முறலாமோ","கழல்ப ணியவினை கழல்ப ணியையணி","கழல்ப ணியவருள் ...... மயில்வீரா","கமலை திருமரு கமலை நிருதரு","கமலை தொளைசெய்த ...... கதிர்வேலா","பழனி மலைவரு பழநி மலைதரு","பழநி மலைமுரு ...... கவிசாகா","பரவு பரவைகொல் பரவை வணஅரி","பரவு மிமையவர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet lists the things women's hair, brows, lips, teeth, ears, words, breasts and eyes are compared to, and asks whether he, a dog, should go on telling women false ways and be uselessly troubled. The praise is word play: Murugan the peacock hero by whose grace the deeds of those who bow at his anklets come loose, the nephew of Lakshmi's lord, the vel that pierced the mountain and the demons, Muruga Visakha of the Palani hill, lord of the gods whom the sea-coloured Hari praises.","n":"The second half is a yamakam: கழல், கமலை, பழநி மலை and பரவை are each repeated three times with different word-splits and senses (கழல் as anklet and as coming loose). பழநி may play on பழம் நீ, the fruit story, but the text gives only the repeated word. நாயேன், I the dog, is the self-abuse. Several of the splits are uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kutram","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:13"},"p:148":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":148,"t":["குழல்கள் சரிய மொழிகள் பதற விழிகளுலவ","கொலைகள் செயவெ ...... களவோடே","குலவு கிகிகி கிகிகி எனவு மிடறி லொலிகள்","குமுற வளையி ...... னொலிமீற","இளநி ரெனவு முலைக ளசைய உபய தொடையும்","இடையு மசைய ...... மயில்போல","இனிய அமுத ரசமும் வடிய உபரி புரிவர்","இடரில் மயலில் ...... உளர்வேனோ","மிளிரு மதுர கவிதை யொளிரும் அருண கிரிசொல்","விஜய கிரிசொல் ...... அணிவோனே","விமலி அமலி நிமலி குமரி கவுரி தருணி","விபின கெமனி ...... யருள்பாலா","பழைய மறையின் முடிவி லகர மகர உகர","படிவ வடிவ ...... முடையோனே","பழன வயல்கள் கமுகு கதலி பனசை யுலவ","பழநி மருவு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes lovemaking with women whose hair slips, words falter, eyes roll, throats make sounds, breasts and thighs sway and who take the upper place, and asks whether he will be troubled in that infatuation. The praise names Murugan as the one who wears the bright, sweet poetry spoken by Arunagiri, son of the pure virgin goddess Gauri who walks in the forest, and the one whose form is the A, U and M at the end of the ancient Veda, at Palani among fields, areca, plantain and jack.","n":"அருணகிரி சொல் ... அணிவோனே: the poet names himself, and his poem as Murugan's ornament. பழைய மறையின் முடிவில் அகர மகர உகர படிவ வடிவம் makes Murugan the form of Om, the end of the Veda. விஜயகிரி is perhaps another name of the poet or of Palani.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","mayal","piranavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:13"},"p:149":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":149,"t":["குறித்தமணிப் பணித்துகிலைத்","திருத்தியுடுத் திருட்குழலைக்","குலைத்துமுடித் திலைச்சுருளைப் ...... பிளவோடே","குதட்டியதுப் புதட்டைமடித்","தயிற்பயிலிட் டழைத்துமருட்","கொடுத்துணர்வைக் கெடுத்துநகக் ...... குறியாலே","பொறித்ததனத் தணைத்துமனச்","செருக்கினர்கைப் பொருட்கவரப்","புணர்ச்சிதனிற் பிணிப்படுவித் ...... திடுமாதர்","புலத்தலையிற் செலுத்துமனப்","ப்ரமத்தையறப் ப்ரசித்தமுறப்","புரித்தருளித் திருக்கழலைத் ...... தருவாயே","பறித்ததலைத் திருட்டமணக்","குருக்களசட் டுருக்களிடைப்","பழுக்களுகக் கழுக்கள்புகத் ...... திருநீறு","பரப்பியதத் திருப்பதிபுக்","கனற்புனலிற் கனத்தசொலைப்","பதித்தெழுதிப் புகட்டதிறற் ...... கவிராசா","செறித்தசடைச் சசித்தரியத்","தகப்பன்மதித் துகப்பனெனச்","சிறக்கவெழுத் தருட்கருணைப் ...... பெருவாழ்வே","திகழ்படுசெய்ப் பதிக்குளெனைத்","தடுத்தடிமைப் படுத்தஅருட்","டிருப்பழநிக் கிரிக்குமரப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women who arrange their clothes and hair, chew betel, call a man to bed, cloud his sense, embrace him with nail-marked breasts and take the wealth of proud men while binding them in lust, and asks Murugan to end the delusion that drives his mind to the senses and give him his holy anklets. The praise makes Murugan the poet-king who impaled the Jain teachers on heated stakes, spread holy ash, and set his weighty word into fire and water; whom the moon-crowned Siva honoured as 'father'; and who stopped the poet at Seypathi and made him his servant, at Palani.","n":"கைப்பொருட் கவர, taking the wealth in a man's hand, marks the women as courtesans; ப்ரமம், delusion, is the poet's word for his state. கனற்புனலிற் கனத்த சொலைப் பதித்தெழுதி points to the ordeals of fire and water by which, in tradition, Sambandar's written verses defeated the Jains of Madurai. தகப்பன் மதித்து உகப்பன் எனச் சிறக்க has Siva honour his son as a father, the teacher's rank. செய்ப்பதிக்குள் எனைத் தடுத்து அடிமைப் படுத்த is the poet's own story: he was stopped and taken into service at Vayalur.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","mayal","thamizh","thondu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:14"},"p:150":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":150,"t":["குன்றுங் குன்றுஞ் செண்டுங் கன்றும்","படிவளர் முலையினில் ம்ருகமத மெழுகியர்","இந்துஞ் சந்தந் தங்குந் தண்செங்","கமலமு மெனவொளிர் தருமுக வநிதையர்","கொஞ்சுங் கெஞ்சுஞ் செஞ்சும் வஞ்சஞ்","சமரச முறவொரு தொழில்வினை புரிபவர் ...... விரகாலும்","கும்பும் பம்புஞ் சொம்புந் தெம்புங்","குடியென வளர்தரு கொடியவர் கடியவர்","எங்கெங் கெம்பங் கென்றென் றென்றுந்","தனதுரி மையதென நலமுட னணைபவர்","கொஞ்சந் தங்கின் பந்தந் தெந்தந்","பொருளுள தெவைகளு நயமொடு கவர்பவர் ...... மயலாலும்","என்றென் றுங்கன் றுந்துன் புங்கொண்","டுனதிரு மலரடி பரவிட மனதினில்","நன்றென் றுங்கொண் டென்றுஞ் சென்றுந்","தொழுமகி மையினிலை யுணர்வினி னருள்பெற","இன்பும் பண்புந் தெம்புஞ் சம்பந்","தமுமிக வருள்பெற விடைதரு விதமுன ...... மருள்வாயே","எங்குங் கஞ்சன் வஞ்சன் கொஞ்சன்","அவன்விடு மதிசய வினையுறு மலகையை","வென்றுங் கொன்றுந் துண்டந் துண்டஞ்","செயுமரி யொருமுறை யிரணிய வலனுயிர்","நுங்குஞ் சிங்கம் வங்கந் தன்கண்","துயில்பவ னெகினனை யுதவிய கருமுகில் ...... மருகோனே","ஒன்றென் றென்றுந் துன்றுங் குன்றுந்","தொளைபட மதகரி முகனுடல் நெரிபட","டுண்டுண் டுண்டுண் டிண்டிண் டிண்டிண்","டிடியென விழுமெழு படிகளு மதிர்பட","ஒண்சங் கஞ்சஞ் சஞ்சஞ் சஞ்சென்","றொலிசெய மகபதி துதிசெய அசுரரை ...... யடுவோனே","உந்தன் தஞ்சந் தஞ்சந் தஞ்சஞ்","சிவனருள் குருபர வெனமுநி வரர்பணி","யுந்தொந் தந்தொந் தந்தொந் தந்தென்","றொலிபட நடமிடு பரனரு ளறுமுக","உண்கண் வண்டுங் கொண்டுந் தங்கும்","விரைபடு குரவல ரலர்தரு மெழில்புனை ...... புயவீரா","அன்றென் றொன்றுங் கொண்டன் பின்றங்","கடியவர் தமையிகழ் சமணர்கள் கழுவினில்","அங்கஞ் சிந்தும் பங்கந் துஞ்சும்","படியொரு தொகுதியி னுரைநதி யெதிர்பட","அன்பின் பண்பெங் குங்கண் டென்பின்","அரிவையை யெதிர்வர விடுகவி புகல்தரு ...... திறலோனே","அண்டங் கண்டும் பண்டுண் டும்பொங்","கமர்தனில் விஜயவ னிரதமை நடவிய","துங்கன் வஞ்சன் சங்கன் மைந்தன்","தருமகன் முநிதழல் வருதக ரிவர்வல","அங்கங் கஞ்சஞ் சங்கம் பொங்குங்","கயநிறை வளமுறு சிவகிரி மருவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women with musk-smeared breasts and moon-bright faces who coax, plead and deceive in one trade, claim a man as their right and charmingly take every possession, and asks that, through the sorrow and infatuation they cause, he may still come to praise Murugan's flower feet and gain grace, joy and kinship with him. The praise runs through Vishnu's deeds (the demoness Kamsa sent, the lion that devoured Hiranya, the one who drove Arjuna's chariot), a battle in drum syllables against an elephant-faced foe, the Jains impaled, the leaf that went against the river and the poem that brought a girl back from her bones, at Sivagiri.","n":"பொருளுள தெவைகளு நயமொடு கவர்பவர், those who charmingly take whatever wealth there is, is the courtesan charge. உரை நதி எதிர்பட and என்பின் அரிவையை எதிர்வர விடுகவி are the leaf floating upstream in the Vaigai and the poem that raised a girl from her bones, both told of Sambandar, here credited to Murugan. Many runs of the rhythm (the list of Vishnu's names before மருகோனே, துங்கன் வஞ்சன் சங்கன் மைந்தன் தருமகன்) split uncertainly.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","mayal","thiruvadi","thamizh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:14"},"p:151":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":151,"t":["கொந்துத் தருகுழ லிருளோ புயலோ","விந்தைத் தருநுதல் சிலையோ பிறையோ","கொஞ்சிப் பயில்மொழி அமுதோ கனியோ ...... விழிவேலோ","கொங்கைக் குடமிரு கரியோ கிரியோ","வஞ்சிக் கொடியிடை துடியோ பிடியோ","கொங்குற் றுயரல்கு லரவோ ரதமோ ...... எனுமாதர்","திந்தித் திமிதிமி திமிதா திமிதோ","தந்தித் திரிகட கிடதா எனவே","சிந்திப் படிபயில் நடமா டியபா ...... விகள்பாலே","சிந்தைத் தயவுகள் புரிவே னுனையே","வந்தித் தருள்தரு மிருசே வடியே","சிந்தித் திடமிகு மறையா கியசீ ...... ரருள்வாயே","வெந்திப் புடன்வரு மவுணே சனையே","துண்டித் திடுமொரு கதிர்வே லுடையாய்","வென்றிக் கொருமலை யெனவாழ் மலையே ...... தவவாழ்வே","விஞ்சைக் குடையவர் தொழவே வருவாய்","கஞ்சத் தயனுட னமரே சனுமே","விந்தைப் பணிவிடை புரிபோ தவர்மே ...... லருள்கூர்வாய்","தொந்திக் கணபதி மகிழ்சோ தரனே","செங்கட் கருமுகில் மருகா குகனே","சொந்தக் குறமகள் கணவா திறல்சேர் ...... கதிர்காமா","சொம்பிற் பலவள முதிர்சோ லைகள்சூழ்","இஞ்சித் திருமகள் புடைசூ ழருள்சேர்","துங்கப் பழநியில் முருகா இமையோர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet runs through a woman's beauty as a string of questions (is her hair darkness or cloud, her brow a bow or a crescent, her speech nectar or fruit, her breasts elephants or hills), says he gives his heart to such dancing sinful women, and asks instead for the glory of a scripture that thinks only of Murugan's two red feet. The praise names the vel that cut down the asura chief, the gods and sages who serve him, Ganapati's brother, the hunter girl's husband and Kathirkamam, at Palani.","n":"The doubting similes (இருளோ புயலோ, சிலையோ பிறையோ) are the stock catalogue of beauty, and the dance syllables (திந்தித் திமிதிமி) belong to the women's dance, not to battle. மறையாகிய சீர் may mean the glory of scripture or of a secret; the sense of the plea is not certain. Kathirkamam (கதிர்காமா) is named in a Palani song.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:14"},"p:152":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":152,"t":["கோல குங்கும கற்புர மெட்டொன்","றான சந்தன வித்துரு மத்தின்","கோவை செண்பக தட்பம கிழ்ச்செங் ...... கழுநீரின்","கோதை சங்கிலி யுற்றக ழுத்தும்","பூஷ ணம்பல வொப்பனை மெச்சுங்","கூறு கொண்டப ணைத்தனம் விற்கும் ...... பொதுமாதர்","பாலு டன்கனி சர்க்கரை சுத்தந்","தேனெ னும்படி மெத்தரு சிக்கும்","பாத கம்பகர் சொற்களி லிட்டம் ...... பயிலாமே","பாத பங்கய முற்றிட வுட்கொண்","டோ து கின்றதி ருப்புகழ் நித்தம்","பாடு மன்பது செய்ப்பதி யிற்றந் ...... தவனீயே","தால முன்புப டைத்தப்ர புச்சந்","தேக மின்றிம திக்கவ திர்க்குஞ்","சாக ரஞ்சுவ றக்கிரி யெட்டுந் ...... தலைசாயச்","சாடு குன்றது பொட்டெழ மற்றுஞ்","சூர னும்பொடி பட்டிட யுத்தஞ்","சாத கஞ்செய்தி ருக்கைவி திர்க்குந் ...... தனிவேலா","ஆல முண்டக ழுத்தினி லக்குந்","தேவ ரென்புநி ரைத்தெரி யிற்சென்","றாடு கின்றத கப்பனு கக்குங் ...... குருநாதா","ஆட கம்புனை பொற்குடம் வைக்குங்","கோபு ரங்களி னுச்சியு டுத்தங்","காவி னன்குடி வெற்பினை னிற்கும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes courtesans adorned with saffron, camphor, sandal, coral and garlands who sell their breasts for a price, and asks not to delight in their sinful words sweet as milk, sugar and honey, but to keep the love Murugan gave him at Seypathi of singing Thiruppugazh daily with his lotus feet held within. The praise names the vel that dried the sea, bowed the eight hills, burst Krauncha and crushed Sura, and the guru loved by the father who drank the poison and dances in the burning ground wearing the gods' bones, at Avinankudi with its golden-pot towers.","n":"பணைத்தனம் விற்கும் பொதுமாதர், women who sell their breasts, is the plainest courtesan charge of the book. The song names its own book: ஓதுகின்ற திருப்புகழ் நித்தம் பாடும் அன்பு, the love of singing Thiruppugazh daily, given at Seypathi (Vayalur), where by tradition the poet received his calling.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","pugazh","thiruvadi","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:14"},"p:153":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":153,"t":["கோல மதிவதனம் வேர்வு தரஅளக","பாரம் நெகிழவிழி வேல்கள் சுழலநுவல்","கோவை யிதழ்வெளிற வாய்மை பதறியிள ...... முகையான","கோக னகவுபய மேரு முலையசைய","நூலி னிடைதுவள வீறு பறவைவகை","கூற யினியகள மோல மிடவளைகள் ...... கரமீதே","காலி னணிகனக நூபு ரமுமொலிக","ளோல மிடஅதிக போக மதுமருவு","காலை வெகுசரச லீலை யளவுசெயு ...... மடமானார்","காதல் புரியுமநு போக நதியினிடை","வீழு கினுமடிமை மோச மறவுனது","காமர் கழலிணைக ளான தொருசிறிது ...... மறவேனே","ஞால முழுதுமம ரோர்கள் புரியுமிக","லாக வருமவுணர் சேர வுததியிடை","நாச முறஅமர்செய் வீர தரகுமர ...... முருகோனே","நாடி யொருகுறமின் மேவு தினைசெய்புன","மீதி லியலகல்கல் நீழ லிடைநிலவி","நாணம் வரவிரக மோது மொருசதுர ...... புரிவேலா","மேலை யமரர்தொழு மானை முகரரனை","யோடி வலம்வருமுன் மோது திரைமகர","வேலை யுலகைவல மாக வருதுரக ...... மயில்வீரா","வீறு கலிசைவரு சேவ கனதிதய","மேவு முதல்வவயல் வாவி புடைமருவு","வீரை வருபழநி ஞான மலையில்வளர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes in detail lovemaking with women, from the sweating face and loosened hair to the bird-cries of the throat and the sound of bangles and anklets, and says that even if he falls into that river of pleasure he, Murugan's servant, will never for a moment forget his feet. The praise names the war against the asuras in the sea, Murugan's courtship of the hunter girl in the millet field, and the peacock that circled the world while the elephant-faced one circled Siva, and the lord in the heart of the servant from Kalisai, at Veerai and Palani, the hill of knowledge.","n":"The plea is unusual: not to be freed from women but, even while falling (வீழுகினும்), not to forget the feet (மறவேனே). ஆனைமுகர் அரனை ஓடி வலம்வரு முன் ... உலகை வலமாக வரு மயில் is the race round the world for the fruit, told fully in 176. கலிசை வரு சேவகன், the servant from Kalisai, recurs in the Palani songs (158, 159) as a devotee in whose heart Murugan lives.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","thiruvadi","thondu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:14"},"p:154":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":154,"t":["சகடத்திற் குழையிட் டெற்றிக்","குழலுக்குச் சரம்வைத் தெற்றிப்","புளகித்துக் குவளைக் கட்பொற்","கணையொத்திட் டுழலச் சுத்தித்","தரளப்பற் பவளத் தொட்டக்","களபப்பொட் டுதலிட் டத்திக் ...... குவடான","தனதுத்திப் படிகப் பொற்பிற்","டசையப்பெட் பசளைத் துப்புக்","கொடியொத்திட் டிடையிற் பட்டைத்","தகையிற்றொட் டுகளப் பச்சைச்","சரணத்துக் கியலச் சுற்றிச்","சுழலிட்டுக் கடனைப் பற்றிக் ...... கொளுமாதர்","சுகமுற்றுக் கவலைப் பட்டுப்","பொருள்கெட்டுக் கடைகெட் டுச்சொற்","குளறிட்டுத் தடிதொட் டெற்றிப்","பிணியுற்றுக் கசதிப் பட்டுச்","சுகதுக்கத் திடர்கெட் டுற்றுத்","தளர்பட்டுக் கிடைபட் டுப்பிக் ...... கிடைநாளிற்","சுழலர்ச்சக் கிரியைச் சுற்றிட்","டிறுகக்கட் டுயிரைப் பற்றிக்","கொளுகப்பற் பலரைக் கட்டிக்","கரம்வைத்துத் தலையிற் குத்திச்","சுடுகட்டைச் சுடலைக் கட்டைக்","கிரையிட்டுப் பொடிபட் டுட்கிச் ...... சடமாமோ","திகுடத்திக் குகுடட் டுட்டுட்","டமடட்டட் டமடட் டிக்குட்","டிமிடிட்டிட் டிமிடிட் டிக்குத்","தொகுதொக்குத் தொகுதத் தொக்குச்","செகணக்கச் செகணச் செக்குத்","தகுடத்தத் தகுடத் தட்டுட் ...... டிடிபேரி","திமிலைக்கைத் துடிதட் டெக்கைப்","பகடிட்டுப் பறையொத் தக்கட்","டிகையெட்டுக் கடல்வற் றித்தித்","தரவுக்கக் கிரியெட் டுத்தைத்","தியருக்குச் சிரமிற் றுட்கச்","சுரர் பொற்புச் சொரியக் கைத்தொட் ...... டிடும்வேலா","பகலைப்பற் சொரியத் தக்கற்","பதிபுக்கட் டழலிட் டுத்திட்","புரமட்கிக் கழைவிற் புட்பச்","சரனைச்சுட் டயனைக் கொத்திப்","பவுரிக்கொட் பரமர்க் குச்சற்","குருவொத்துப் பொருளைக் கற்பித் ...... தருள்வோனே","பவளப்பொற் கிரிதுத் திப்பொற்","றனகொச்சைக் கிளிசொற் பற்றிப்","பரிவுற்றுக் கமலப் புட்பத்","திதழ்பற்றிப் புணர்ச்சித் ரப்பொற்","படிகத்துப் பவளப் பச்சைப்","பதமுத்துப் பழநிச் சொக்கப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes adorned women who clutch a man, then his life after them: worry, wealth gone, stammering speech, a staff, disease, exhaustion and the sickbed, until many bind the body, beat their heads, lay it on the cremation logs and burn it to ash, and asks whether he will become a mere corpse. The praise, after a battle in drum syllables, names the deeds of Siva who burned the cities and Kama and the punishment of Brahma, Murugan as the true guru who taught the meaning to the supreme dancer, and his union with the parrot-voiced girl, at Palani.","n":"The confession moves in one breath from the women's embrace (கொளுமாதர் சுகமுற்று) to ruin, old age and the pyre, so that lust leads straight to the burning ground; சடமாமோ, 'shall I become a corpse?', is the whole plea. பரமர்க்குச் சற்குருவொத்துப் பொருளைக் கற்பித்தருள்வோனே is Murugan teaching Siva. Who does the acts before it (knocking out the sun's teeth, burning the cities, burning Kama, striking Brahma) depends on how the run is parsed; most are Siva's deeds.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","saakkaadu","kaamam","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:14"},"p:155":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":155,"t":["சிந்துர கூரம ருப்புச் செஞ்சரி","செங்கைகு லாவந டித்துத் தென்புற","செண்பக் மாலைமு டித்துப் பண்புள ...... தெருவூடே","சிந்துகள் பாடிமு ழக்கிச் செங்கய","லம்புகள் போலவி ழித்துச் சிங்கியில்","செம்பவ ளாடைது லக்கிப் பொன்பறி ...... விலைமாதர்","வந்தவ ராரென ழைத்துக் கொங்கையை","யன்பூற மூடிநெ கிழ்த்திக் கண்பட","மஞ்சணி ராடி னுக்கிப் பஞ்சணை ...... தனிலேறி","மந்திர மோகமெ ழுப்பிக் கெஞ்சிட","முன்றலை வாயில டைத்துச் சிங்கிகொள்","மங்கைய ராசைவி லக்கிப் பொன்பத ...... மருள்வாயே","இந்திர நீலவ னத்திற் செம்புவி","யண்டக டாகம ளித்திட் டண்டர்க","ளெண்படு சூரைய ழித்துக் கொண்டரு ...... ளொருபேடி","இன்கன தேரைந டத்திச் செங்குரு","மண்டல நாடும ளித்துப் பஞ்சவ","ரின்புறு தோழ்மையு டைக்கத் தன்திரு ...... மருகோனே","சந்திர சூரியர் திக்கெட் டும்புக","ழந்தமில் வாழ்வது பெற்றுத் தங்கிய","சங்கர னார்செவி புக்கப் பண்பருள் ...... குருநாதா","சம்ப்ரப மானகு றத்திக் கின்புறு","கொங்கையின் மேவுச மர்த்தச் சுந்தர","தண்டமிழ் சேர்பழ நிக்குட் டங்கிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes priced women who sing in the street, call out 'who has come?', bathe, take a man to the cotton bed, rouse lust with charms and, when he begs, shut the front door, and asks Murugan to remove his desire for them and give his golden feet. The praise names Krishna, who had Arjuna drive the chariot as a eunuch and gave the Pandavas Kurukshetra through friendship, as uncle, and Murugan as the guru who spoke the quality into Siva's ear, at Palani of cool Tamil.","n":"பொன் பறி விலைமாதர், priced women who snatch gold, and முன்றலை வாயில் அடைத்து, shutting the door, mark the courtesan: the door closes when the money ends. ஒரு பேடி இன்கன தேரை நடத்தி recalls Arjuna as Brihannala. சங்கரனார் செவி புக்கப் பண்பு does not name Om, so `gnaanam` stands for what was taught.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","guru","gnaanam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:14"},"p:156":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":156,"t":["சிவனார் மனங்குளிர உபதேச மந்த்ரமிரு","செவிமீதி லும்பகர்செய் ...... குருநாதா","சிவகாம சுந்தரிதன் வரபால கந்தநின","செயலேவி ரும்பியுளம் ...... நினையாமல்","அவமாயை கொண்டுலகில் விருதாவ லைந்துழலு","மடியேனை அஞ்சலென ...... வரவேணும்","அறிவாக மும்பெருக இடரான துந்தொலைய","அருள்ஞான இன்பமது ...... புரிவாயே","நவநீத முந்திருடி உரலோடெ யொன்றுமரி","ரகுராமர் சிந்தைமகிழ் ...... மருகோனே","நவலோக முங்கைதொழு நிசதேவ லங்கிருத","நலமான விஞ்சைகரு ...... விளைகோவே","தெவயானை யங்குறமின் மணவாள சம்ப்ரமுறு","திறல்வீர மிஞ்சுகதிர் ...... வடிவேலா","திருவாவி னன்குடியில் வருவேள்ச வுந்தரிக","செகமேல்மெய் கண்டவிறல் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A short song: Murugan, the guru who spoke the instructing mantra into both of Siva's ears to cool his heart, the son of Sivakama Sundari, is asked to come and say 'do not fear' to one who, not loving his deeds, wanders the world in vain in base illusion, and to give the bliss of grace-knowledge so that understanding grows and trouble ends. The praise names Krishna who stole butter and was tied to the mortar, Rama as uncle, Devayanai and the hunter girl, at Thiruvavinankudi.","n":"The song opens with the teaching (உபதேச மந்த்ரம் இரு செவிமீதிலும்) and asks for the same gift for the poet: அருள்ஞான இன்பம். அவமாயை, base illusion, is the condition. செகமேல் மெய் கண்ட may mean 'who saw the truth in the world' or allude to Meykandar of the Siddhanta; I can't tell.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","mandhiram","gnaanam","maayai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:14"},"p:157":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":157,"t":["சிறுபறையு முரசுதுடி சத்தக் கணப்பறையு","மொகுமொகென அதிரவுட னெட்டிப் பிடித்துமுடி","சிறுகயிறு நெடிதுகொடு கட்டிட் டிழுக்கஇனி ...... யணுகாதே","சிலதமர்க ளுறவுகிளை கத்திப் பிதற்றியெடு","சுடலைதனி லிடுகனலை யிட்டுக் கொளுத்துபுனல்","திரைகடலில் முழுகெனவு ரைக்கப் படிக்குடிலை ...... யொழியாதே","மறைமுறையி னிறுதிநிலை முத்திக் கிசைத்தபடி","உடலுயிர்கள் கரணவெளி பட்டுக் குணத்திரயம்","வழிபடவும் நினதடிமை யிச்சைப் படுத்துவது ...... மொருநாளே","வருதுரக மயில்மணிகள் சத்திக்க நிர்த்தமிட","ஒருபதுட னிருபுயமு மட்டுத் தொடைக்கிசைய","மனமகிழ இனியமொழி செப்பிச் சிவத்தபத ...... மருள்வாயே","நறையிதழி யறுகுபல புட்பத் திரட்களொடு","சிறுபிறையு மரவுமெழி லப்புத் திருத்தலையி","னளினமுற அணிசடையர் மெச்சிப் ப்ரியப்படவு ...... மயிலேறி","நவநதிகள் குமுகுமென வெற்புத் திரட்சுழல","அகிலமுத லெழுபுவன மெத்தத் திடுக்கிடவும்","நவமணிகள் உரகனுடல் கக்கத் துரத்திவரு ...... முருகோனே","குறவர்முனை கெடமனது வெட்கப் படக்குடிலில்","மலையிலெழு தினையிதணில் வைத்துச் சிறுக்கியிரு","குவிமுலையு மணியிடையு மெச்சிப் புணர்ச்சிசெயு ...... மணவாளா","குறுமுநிவ னிருபொழுதும் அர்ச்சித்து முத்திபெற","அறிவுநெறி தவநிலைகள் செப்புத் தமிழ்க்கினிய","குருகுமர பழநிவளர் வெற்புத் தனிற்றிகழு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet pictures death: drums beat, Death's men seize him and drag him with a rope, kin wail, the body is burned and the ashes sent to the sea; before this hut is gone, he asks that, as the Veda describes the final state of release, body, life and faculties be passed beyond and the three gunas brought under, and that Murugan come dancing on the peacock and give him Siva's feet. The praise tells how Siva praised him, how the worlds shook as he chased the serpent, his union with the hunter girl in the millet hut, and how he taught Agastya the ways of knowledge and penance in Tamil, at Palani.","n":"The plea is for release while still in the body (படிக்குடிலை ஒழியாதே, before the earthly hut is gone), described in Vedantic terms: மறைமுறையின் இறுதிநிலை முத்தி, கரணவெளி, குணத்திரயம். குறுமுநிவன் is Agastya; Murugan as his Tamil teacher recurs through the book. The middle lines of the plea split uncertainly.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kootru","saakkaadu","veedu","thamizh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:14"},"p:158":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":158,"t":["சீயுதிர மெங்கு மேய்புழுநி ரம்பு","மாயமல பிண்ட நோயிடுகு ரம்பை","தீநரிகள் கங்கு காகமிவை தின்ப ...... தொழியாதே","தீதுளகு ணங்க ளேபெருகு தொந்த","மாயையில்வ ளர்ந்த தோல்தசையெ லும்பு","சேரிடந ரம்பு தானிவைபொ திந்து ...... நிலைகாணா","ஆயுதுந மன்கை போகவுயி ரந்த","நாழிகையில் விஞ்ச ஊசிடுமி டும்பை","யாகியவு டம்பு பேணிநிலை யென்று ...... மடவார்பால்","ஆசையைவி ரும்பி யேவிரக சிங்கி","தானுமிக வந்து மேவிடம யங்கு","மாழ்துயர்வி ழுந்து மாளுமெனை யன்பு ...... புரிவாயே","மாயைவல கஞ்ச னால்விடவெ குண்டு","பார்முழுது மண்ட கோளமுந டுங்க","வாய்பிளறி நின்று மேகநிகர் தன்கை ...... யதனாலே","வாரியுற அண்டி வீறொடுமு ழங்கு","நீரைநுகர் கின்ற கோபமொடெ திர்ந்த","வாரண இரண்டு கோடொடிய வென்ற ...... நெடியோனாம்","வேயினிசை கொண்டு கோநிரைபு ரந்து","மேயல்புரி செங்கண் மால்மருக துங்க","வேலகிர வுஞ்ச மால்வரையி டிந்து ...... பொடியாக","வேலைவிடு கந்த காவிரிவி ளங்கு","கார்கலிசை வந்த சேவகன்வ ணங்க","வீரைநகர் வந்து வாழ்பழநி யண்டர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes the body as an illusory mass of filth, pus, blood, worms, skin, flesh, bone and nerves, food for jackals, vultures and crows, that stinks the moment the life passes to Yama's hand, and confesses that, cherishing it as lasting, he desires women and is dying in that sorrow; he asks Murugan to love him. The praise names Krishna who broke the two tusks of the elephant sent by Kamsa and herded cows with his flute, the vel that broke Krauncha, and the servant from Kalisai who bows to Murugan, at Veerai and Palani.","n":"The catalogue of the body (சீ, உதிரம், புழு, தோல், தசை, எலும்பு, நரம்பு) is the Naladiyar's method, turned on the poet's own body and set beside his desire: he knows what the body is and still cherishes it (பேணி நிலையென்று). The elephant whose tusks Krishna broke (வாரண இரண்டு கோடொடிய) is Kuvalayapida, by its details.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","nilaiyaamai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:14"},"p:159":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":159,"t":["சீற லசடன்வினை காரன் முறைமையிலி","தீமை புரிகபடி ...... பவநோயே","தேடு பரிசிகன நீதி நெறிமுறைமை","சீர்மை சிறிதுமிலி ...... எவரோடுங்","கூறு மொழியதுபொய் யான கொடுமையுள","கோள னறிவிலியு ...... னடிபேணாக்","கூள னெனினுமெனை நீயு னடியரொடு","கூடும் வகைமையருள் ...... புரிவாயே","மாறு படுமவுணர் மாள அமர்பொருது","வாகை யுளமவுலி ...... புனைவோனே","மாக முகடதிர வீ சு சிறைமயிலை","வாசி யெனவுடைய ...... முருகோனே","வீறு கலிசைவரு சேவ கனதிதய","மேவு மொருபெருமை ...... யுடையோனே","வீரை யுறைகுமர தீர தரபழநி","வேல இமையவர்கள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet lists his own faults: an angry fool, a wrongdoer without order, a deceiver who seeks the disease of birth, without justice or worth, a liar to all, cruel, a talebearer, ignorant, rubbish who does not cherish Murugan's feet; and asks that even so he be joined to Murugan's devotees. The praise names the victory garland of the war, the peacock as horse, the servant from Kalisai in whose heart he lives, at Veerai and Palani.","n":"The whole confession is self-abuse without a story (சீறல் அசடன், கபடி, கோளன், கூளன்), and the plea is modest: not release but company, உனடியரொடு கூடும் வகைமை.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kutram","adiyaar","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:14"},"p:160":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":160,"t":["சுருதிமுடி மோனஞ்சொல் சிற்பரம ஞானசிவ","சமயவடி வாய்வந்த அத்துவித மானபர","சுடரொளிய தாய்நின்ற நிட்களசொ ரூபமுத ...... லொருவாழ்வே","துரியநிலை யேகண்ட முத்தரித யாகமல","மதனில்விளை யாநின்ற அற்புதசு போதசுக","சுயபடிக மாவின்ப பத்மபத மேஅடைய ...... உணராதே","கருவிலுரு வேதங்கு சுக்கிலநி தானவளி","பொருமஅதி லேகொண்ட முக்குணவி பாகநிலை","கருதவரி யாவஞ்ச கக்கபட மூடியுடல் ...... வினைதானே","கலகமிட வேபொங்கு குப்பைமல வாழ்வுநிஜ","மெனவுழலு மாயஞ்செ னித்தகுகை யேஉறுதி","கருதசுழ மாமிந்த மட்டைதனை யாளஉன ...... தருள்தாராய்","ஒருநியம மேவிண்ட சட்சமய வேதஅடி","முடிநடுவு மாயண்ட முட்டைவெளி யாகியுயி","ருடலுணர்வ தாயெங்கு முற்பனம தாகஅம ...... ருளவோனே","உததரிச மாமின்ப புத்தமிர்த போகசுக","முதவுமம லாநந்த சத்திகர மேவுணர","வுருபிரண வாமந்த்ர கர்த்தவிய மாகவரு ...... குருநாதா","பருதிகதி ரேகொஞ்சு நற்சரண நூபுரம","தசையநிறை பேரண்ட மொக்கநட மாடுகன","பதகெருவி தாதுங்க வெற்றிமயி லேறுமொரு ...... திறலோனே","பணியுமடி யார்சிந்தை மெய்ப்பொருள தாகநவில்","சரவணப வாவொன்று வற்கரமு மாகிவளர்","பழநிமலை மேனின்ற சுப்ரமணி யாவமரர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A song of doctrine: Murugan is addressed as the one life that is the silence at the crown of the Veda, Siva as knowledge, the non-dual supreme flame and the formless nature; the poet confesses that, not knowing how to reach the lotus feet of bliss known to the liberated who have seen turiya, he takes as real the body formed in the womb from seed and breath, divided by the three gunas and stirred by deeds, a cave of illusion, and asks for grace to rule this worthless thing. The praise names him as root, crown and middle of the six religions and the Veda, the cosmic egg and space, the guru who came as the pranava mantra, the dancer on the peacock and Saravanabhava, Subramanya on Palani hill.","n":"The vocabulary is Vedanta and Siddhanta together: சுருதிமுடி மோனம், அத்துவிதம், நிட்கள சொரூபம், துரியநிலை, முக்குண விபாகம். The body is born in the womb (கருவில் உரு, சுக்கில நிதானம், வளி பொரும) and is called refuse (குப்பைமல வாழ்வு) and a cave of maya. உருபிரணவாம் மந்த்ர ... குருநாதா makes Murugan the pranava in person. Several compounds split uncertainly.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vedaantham","karu","avaththai","piranavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:14"},"p:161":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":161,"t":["சுருளளக பார கொங்கை மகளிர்வச மாயி சைந்து","சுரதக்ரியை யால்வி ளங்கு ...... மதனு஡லே","சுருதியென வேநி னைந்து அறிவிலிக ளோடி ணங்கு","தொழிலுடைய யானு மிங்கு ...... னடியார்போல்","அருமறைக ளேநி னைந்து மநுநெறியி லேந டந்து","அறிவையறி வால றிந்து ...... நிறைவாகி","அகிலபுவ னாதி யெங்கும் வெளியுறமெய்ஞ் ஞான இன்ப","அமுதையொழி யாத ருந்த ...... அருள்வாயே","பருதிமகன் வாசல் மந்த்ரி அநுமனொடு நேர்ப ணிந்து","பரிதகழை யாமுன் வந்து ...... பரிவாலே","பரவியவி பீஷ ணன்பொன் மகுடமுடி சூட நின்ற","படைஞரொடி ராவ ணன்ற ...... னுறவோடே","எரிபுகுத மாறி லண்டர் குடிபுகுத மாறு கொண்ட","ரகுபதியி ராம சந்த்ரன் ...... மருகோனே","இளையகுற மாது பங்க பழநிமலை நாத கந்த","இமையவள்த னால்ம கிழ்ந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses that, enslaved to women, he took Kama's treatise of lovemaking as if it were scripture and mixed with fools, and asks that, like Murugan's devotees, he may meditate on the scriptures, walk Manu's path, know understanding by understanding, become full, and drink unceasingly the nectar of true knowledge and bliss. The praise tells the Rama story through Sugriva, Hanuman and Vibhishana's crowning and Ravana's burning with his kin, and names Murugan Rama's nephew, the hunter girl's lord, at Palani, the joy of the mountain's daughter.","n":"மதனூலே சுருதியென நினைந்து, taking the love-treatise for the Veda, is the poet's sharpest self-mockery: the wrong book taken as scripture, set against அருமறைகளே நினைந்து. அறிவை அறிவால் அறிந்து, knowing knowledge by knowledge, is the goal.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kaamam","vedham","gnaanam","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:14"},"p:162":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":162,"t":["ஞானங்கொள் பொறிகள் கூடி வானிந்து கதிரி லாத","நாடண்டி நமசி வாய ...... வரையேறி","நாவின்ப ரசம தான ஆநந்த அருவி பாய","நாதங்க ளொடுகு லாவி ...... விளையாடி","ஊனங்க ளுயிர்கள் மோக நானென்ப தறிவி லாம","லோமங்கி யுருவ மாகி ...... யிருவோரும்","ஓரந்த மருவி ஞான மாவிஞ்சை முதுகி னேறி","லோகங்கள் வலம தாட ...... அருள்தாராய்","தேனங்கொ ளிதழி தாகி தாரிந்து சலில வேணி","சீரங் னெனது தாதை ...... ஒருமாது","சேர்பஞ்ச வடிவி மோகி யோகங்கொள் மவுன ஜோதி","சேர்பங்கி னமல நாத ...... னருள்பாலா","கானங்கள் வரைகள் தீவு ஓதங்கள் பொடிய நீல","காடந்த மயிலி லேறு ...... முருகோனே","காமன்கை மலர்கள் நாண வேலம்பெ ணமளி சேர்வை","காணெங்கள் பழநி மேவு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A song of yoga: the poet asks that his senses gather and reach the land without moon or sun, climb the mountain of Namasivaya, let the bliss of the tongue fall like a waterfall, play among the nadas, forget body, souls, delusion and 'I', become one end with the Lord, and ride on great knowledge around the worlds. The praise names Siva with konrai, moon and river in his hair, 'my father', and his consort as the pure lord's half, Murugan on the peacock that shattered forests and seas, and Kama's arrows shamed, at 'our Palani'.","n":"The plea is a yogic ascent, not a confession: வான் இந்து கதிர் இலாத நாடு (where neither moon nor sun shines), நமசிவாய வரை, நாவின்ப ரசம், நாதங்களொடு குலாவி, நான் என்பது அறிவிலாமல். ஓமங்கி உருவமாகி and இருவோரும் ஓரந்த மருவி (the two joining in one end) are uncertain splits; who the two are is not said. The last lines of praise are hard to parse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","panchaakkaram","naadham","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:14"},"p:163":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":163,"t":["தகர நறுமலர் பொதுளிய குழலியர்","கலக கெருவித விழிவலை படவிதி","தலையி லெழுதியு மனைவயி னுறவிடு ...... வதனாலே","தனயர் அனைமதர் மனைவியர் சினெகிதர்","சுரபி விரவிய வகையென நினைவுறு","தவன சலதியின் முழுகியெ யிடர்படு ...... துயர்தீர","அகர முதலுள பொருளினை யருளிட","இருகை குவிசெய்து ளுருகிட வுருகியெ","அரக ரெனவல னிடமுற எழிலுன ...... திருபாதம்","அருள அருளுடன் மருளற இருளற","கிரண அயில்கொடு குருகணி கொடியொடு","அழகு பெறமர கதமயில் மிசைவர ...... இசைவாயே","சிகர குடையினி னிரைவர இசைதெரி","சதுரன் விதுரனில் வருபவ னளையது","திருடி யடிபடு சிறியவ னெடியவன் ...... மதுசூதன்","திகிரி வளைகதை வசிதநு வுடையவ","னெழிலி வடிவின னரவுபொன் முடிமிசை","திமித திமிதிமி யெனநட மிடுமரி ...... மருகோனே","பகர புகர்முக மதகரி யுழைதரு","வனிதை வெருவமுன் வரஅருள் புரிகுக","பரம குருபர இமகிரி தருமயில் ...... புதல்வோனே","பலவின் முதுபழம் விழைவுசெய் தொழுகிய","நறவு நிறைவயல் கமுகடர் பொழில்திகழ்","பழநி மலைவரு புரவல அமரர்கள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says that fate, written on his head, drove him into houses with fragrant-haired women whose eyes are nets, and that he sinks in a burning sea of attachment to sons, mother, father, wives, friends and cattle; he asks for the meaning that is first as A is first, to join his hands and melt crying 'Hara', and for Murugan's feet, coming on the emerald peacock with vel and cock banner. The praise runs through Krishna's deeds (the hill as umbrella, Vidura's house, the stolen butter, Vamana, the dance on the serpent) as uncle, and Murugan who brought the elephant to frighten the girl among the deer, guru, son of the mountain's daughter, at Palani.","n":"விதி தலையில் எழுதியும் places the poet's lust under fate. The bonds of kin (தனயர் அனை மதர் மனைவியர்) are a sea of fire (தவன சலதி). அகர முதல் உள பொருள் echoes the Kural's first line and points, as in 173, to the meaning of the first letter. வனிதை வெருவ முன் வர is the elephant that frightened Valli into Murugan's arms.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","sutram","oozh","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:14"},"p:164":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":164,"t":["தகைமைத் தனியிற் பகைகற் றுறுகைத்","தநுமுட் டவளைப் ...... பவனாலே","தரளத் திரளிற் புரளக் கரளத்","தமரத் திமிரக் ...... கடலாலே","உகைமுத் தமிகுத் ததெனப் பகல்புக்","கொளிமட் குமிகைப் ...... பொழுதாலே","உரையற் றுணர்வற் றுயிரெய்த் தகொடிக்","குனநற் பிணையற் ...... றரவேணும்","திகைபத் துமுகக் கமலத் தனைமுற்","சிறையிட் டபகைத் ...... திறல்வீரா","திகழ்கற் பகமிட் டவனக் கனகத்","திருவுக் குருகிக் ...... குழைமார்பா","பகலக் கிரணப் பரணச் சடிலப்","பரமற் கொருசொற் ...... பகர்வோனே","பவனப் புவனச் செறிவுற் றுயர்மெய்ப்","பழநிக் குமரப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A love-lament, apparently in a mother's or companion's voice: through Kama's bow, the dark roaring sea with its rolling pearls and the evening when the light fades, the creeper-like girl has lost speech and sense and her life grows faint, and Murugan is asked to give her his garland. The praise names him the hero who imprisoned the lotus-born Brahma, the one who melted for Devayanai, and the one who told a word to Siva of the radiant matted hair, at Palani.","n":"The speaker never says 'I': the girl is கொடி, the creeper, and the plea is for Murugan's garland (நற்பிணையல்) for her, the stock request of akam poems addressed to a god. The metre and machinery (Kama's arrows, the sea, the evening) match 24. The first lines split uncertainly.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","maalai","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:15"},"p:165":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":165,"t":["தமரு மமரு மனையு மினிய","தனமு மரசும் ...... அயலாகத்","தறுகண் மறலி முறுகு கயிறு","தலையை வளைய ...... எறியாதே","கமல விமல மரக தமணி","கனக மருவு ...... மிருபாதங்","கருத அருளி யெனது தனிமை","கழிய அறிவு ...... தரவேணும்","குமர சமர முருக பரம","குலவு பழநி ...... மலையோனே","கொடிய பகடு முடிய முடுகு","குறவர் சிறுமி ...... மணவாளா","அமர ரிடரு மவுண ருடலு","மழிய அமர்செய் ...... தருள்வோனே","அறமு நிறமு மயிலு மயிலு","மழகு முடைய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks that before fierce-eyed Death throws his twisted rope around his head, when kin, friends, house, wealth and power become strangers, Murugan grant him thought of his two feet, end his loneliness and give him knowledge. The praise names Kumara of war, the lord of Palani hill, the hunter girl's bridegroom, the destroyer of the asuras who troubled the gods, the owner of dharma, colour, the peacock and the vel.","n":"அயலாக, become strangers, is the point: at Death's coming everything owned turns foreign, and the poet names his state தனிமை, loneliness. மயிலும் அயிலும் is a play on peacock and vel.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","thiruvadi","nilaiyaamai","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:15"},"p:166":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":166,"t":["தலைவலி மருத்தீடு காமாலை சோகைசுரம்","விழிவலி வறட்சூலை காயாசு வாசம்வெகு","சலமிகு விஷப்பாக மாயாவி காரபிணி ...... யணுகாதே","தலமிசை யதற்கான பேரோடு கூறியிது","பரிகரி யெனக்காது கேளாது போலுமவர்","சரியும்வய துக்கேது தா஡ணர்சொ லீரெனவும் ...... விதியாதே","உலைவறவி ருப்பாக நீள்காவின் வாசமலர்","வகைவகை யெடுத்தேதொ டாமாலி காபரண","முனதடியி னிற்சூட வேநாடு மாதவர்க ...... ளிருபாதம்","உளமது தரித்தேவி னாவோடு பாடியருள்","வழிபட எனக்கேத யாவோடு தாளுதவ","உரகம தெடுத்தாடு மேகார மீதின்மிசை ...... வரவேணும்","அலைகட லடைத்தேம காகோர ராவணனை","மணிமுடி துணித்தாவி யேயான ஜானகியை","அடலுட னழைத்தேகொள் மாயோனை மாமனெனு ...... மருகோனே","அறுகினை முடித்தோனை யாதார மானவனை","மழுவுழை பிடித்தோனை மாகாளி நாணமுனம்","அவைதனில் நடித்தோனை மாதாதை யேஎனவும் ...... வருவோனே","பலகலை படித்தோது பாவாணர் நாவிலுறை","யிருசரண வித்தார வேலாயு தாவுயர்செய்","பரண்மிசை குறப்பாவை தோள்மேவ மோகமுறு ...... மணவாளா","பதுமவய லிற்பூக மீதேவ ரால்கள் துயில்","வருபுனல் பெருக்காறு காவேரி சூழவளர்","பழநிவரு கற்பூர கோலாக லாவமரர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet lists the diseases (headache, jaundice, anaemia, fever, eye pain, colic, asthma) and asks that they not come near, nor the indignity of physicians who name remedies, turn deaf, and demand payment for failing age; and asks that, like the sages who gather flowers for garlands to adorn his feet and hold them in their hearts, he may be given the feet, with Murugan coming on the peacock that lifts the snake. The praise names Rama who bridged the sea, cut Ravana's crowns and brought back Janaki, as uncle; Siva who danced in the hall to shame Kali, to whom Murugan came as a father; and the hunter girl on her platform, at Palani.","n":"The list of illnesses (தலைவலி, காமாலை, சோகை, சுரம், சூலை, காயாசுவாசம்) is unusually medical. The physicians' lines are hard to parse: who turns deaf and who is asked to give (தாரீர்) is not certain. மாதாதையே எனவும் வருவோனே again makes Murugan father to his father, the teacher's rank.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["noy","marundhu","vazhipaadu","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:15"},"p:167":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":167,"t":["திடமிலிசற் குணமிலிநற் றிறமிலியற் ...... புதமான","செயலிலிமெய்த் தவமிலிநற் செபமிலிசொர்க் ...... கமுமீதே","இடமிலிகைக் கொடையிலிசொற் கியல்பிலிநற் ...... றமிழ்பாட","இருபதமுற் றிருவினையற் றியல்கதியைப் ...... பெறவேணும்","கெடுமதியுற் றிடுமசுரக் கிளைமடியப் ...... பொரும்வேலா","கிரணகுறைப் பிறையறுகக் கிதழ்மலர்கொக் ...... கிறகோடே","படர்சடையிற் புனைநடனப் பரமர்தமக் ...... கொருபாலா","பலவயலிற் றரளநிறைப் பழநிமலைப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A short song of self-accusation: the poet says he has no firmness, no good quality, no skill, no wonderful deed, no true penance, no japa, no place in heaven, no giving hand and no fit speech, and asks that he may sing good Tamil, reach the two feet, be rid of both kinds of deeds and gain the destined state. The praise names the vel against the asuras and Murugan as son of the dancer whose matted hair wears the crescent, arugu, konrai and crane feather, at Palani.","n":"The confession is built of negatives in -இலி (திடமிலி, சற்குணமிலி, தவமிலி, கொடையிலி); the remedy is first to sing Tamil (நற்றமிழ் பாட) and then to be rid of இருவினை, good and bad deeds alike.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kutram","thamizh","vinai","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:15"},"p:168":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":168,"t":["திமிர வுததி யனைய நரக","செனன மதனில் ...... விடுவாயேல்","செவிடு குருடு வடிவு குறைவு","சிறிது மிடியு ...... மணுகாதே","அமரர் வடிவு மதிக குலமு","மறிவு நிறையும் ...... வரவேநின்","அருள தருளி யெனையு மனதோ","டடிமை கொளவும் ...... வரவேணும்","சமர முகவெ லசுரர் தமது","தலைக ளுருள ...... மிகவேநீள்","சலதி யலற நெடிய பதலை","தகர அயிலை ...... விடுவோனே","வெமர வணையி லினிது துயிலும்","விழிகள் நளினன் ...... மருகோனே","மிடறு கரியர் குமர பழநி","விரவு மமரர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks that if Murugan leaves him in birth, which is like a dark sea of hell, he not be deaf, blind, deformed or the least poor, but have a fine form, high family, knowledge and fullness, and that Murugan give his grace and come to take him, mind and all, into service. The praise names the vel that made the asuras' heads roll, the sea wail and the mountain break, Vishnu on the serpent bed as uncle and the dark-throated Siva as father, at Palani.","n":"The plea is conditional and practical: if rebirth must come (விடுவாயேல்), let it be a whole and fortunate one, then service. It does not ask for release outright.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","kudi","thondu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:15"},"p:169":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":169,"t":["தோகைமயி லேகமல மானேயு லாசமிகு","காமதுரை யானமத வேள்பூவை யேயினிமை","தோயுமநு போகசுக லீலாவி நோதமுழு ...... துணர்தேனே","சூதனைய சீதஇள நீரான பாரமுலை","மீதணைய வாருமிதழ் தா஡ணரெ னாணைமொழி","சோர்வதிலை யானடிமை யாவேனு மாணைமிக ...... மயலானேன்","ஆகமுற வேநகம தாலேவி டாதஅடை","யாளமிட வாருமென வேமாத ரார்களுட","னாசைசொலி யேயுழலு மாபாத னீதியிலி ...... யுனையோதேன்","ஆமுனது நேயஅடி யாரோடு கூடுகில","னீறுநுதல் மீதிடலி லர்முட னேதுமிலி","யாயினுமி யானடிமை யீடேற வேகழல்கள் ...... தருவாயே","மாகமுக டோ டகில பாதாள மேருவுட","னேசுழல வாரியது வேதாழி யாவமரர்","வாலிமுத லானவர்க ளேனோர்க ளாலமுது ...... கடைநாளில்","வாருமென வேயொருவர் நோகாம லாலவிட","மீசர்பெறு மாறுதவி யேதேவர் யாவர்களும்","வாழஅமு தேபகிரு மாமாய னாரினிய ...... மருகோனே","மேகநிக ரானகொடை மானாய காதிபதி","வாரிகலி மாருதக ரோபாரி மாமதன","வேள்கலிசை வாழவரு காவேரி சேவகன ...... துளமேவும்","வீரஅதி சூரர்கிளை வேர்மாள வேபொருத","தீரகும ராகுவளை சேரோடை சூழ்கழனி","வீரைநகர் வாழ்பழநி வேலாயு தாவமரர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet quotes his own wooing of women (peacock, lotus, deer, honey who knows every art of love, come to my breasts, give me your lips, I swear I will be your slave) and says he wandered so, a great sinner without justice, infatuated, and never chanted Murugan's name, joined his devotees or wore ash; yet he asks for the anklets so that the servant may be saved. The praise tells the churning of the ocean, Siva taking the poison and Vishnu as the enchantress sharing the nectar, as uncle, and the generous lord of Kalisai on the Kaveri, at Veerai and Palani.","n":"Unusually the poet's own pleading speech to women is quoted (என் ஆணை மொழி சோர்வதிலை, யான் அடிமையாவேன்): he offers them the servitude (அடிமை) he owes Murugan, and the song turns on that word. The omissions are worship's marks: chanting, devotees, ash on the forehead (நீறு நுதல்மீதிடலிலன்). The patron of Kalisai is praised as a cloud in giving.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["mayal","kaamam","kutram","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:15"},"p:170":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":170,"t":["நாத விந்துக லாதீ நமோநம","வேத மந்த்ரசொ ரூபா நமோநம","ஞான பண்டித ஸாமீ நமோநம ...... வெகுகோடி","நாம சம்புகு மாரா நமோநம","போக அந்தரி பாலா நமோநம","நாக பந்தம யூரா நமோநம ...... பரசூரர்","சேத தண்டவி நோதா நமோநம","கீத கிண்கிணி பாதா நமோநம","தீர சம்ப்ரம வீரா நமோநம ...... கிரிராஜ","தீப மங்கள ஜோதீ நமோநம","தூய அம்பல லீலா நமோநம","தேவ குஞ்சரி பாகா நமோநம ...... அருள்தாராய்","ஈத லும்பல கோலால பூஜையும்","ஓத லுங்குண ஆசார நீதியும்","ஈர முங்குரு சீர்பாத சேவையு ...... மறவாத","ஏழ்த லம்புகழ் காவேரி யால்விளை","சோழ மண்டல மீதே மநோகர","ராஜ கெம்பிர நாடாளு நாயக ...... வயலு஡ரா","ஆத ரம்பயி லர்ருரர் தோழமை","சேர்தல் கொண்டவ ரோடே முனாளினில்","ஆடல் வெம்பரி மீதேறி மாகயி ...... லையிலேகி","ஆதி யந்தவு லாவாசு பாடிய","சேரர் கொங்குவை காவூர் நனாடதில்","ஆவி னன்குடி வாழ்வான தேவர்கள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A litany of homage (நமோநம): Murugan beyond nada, bindu and kala, the form of Vedic mantra, the scholar of knowledge, son of Sambhu, lord of the serpent-bound peacock, the one who split the Suras, the lamp of auspicious light, Devayanai's lord; the poet asks never to forget giving, worship with many rites, recitation, good conduct, kindness and service at the guru's feet. The praise names him lord of Vayalur in the Chola land of the Kaveri and recalls the Chera king who rode with Sundarar to Kailas and sang the Ula, at Avinankudi in the Kongu land.","n":"நாத விந்து கலாதீ puts Murugan beyond the tattvas of Saiva cosmology. The plea is a short list of practice (ஈதல், பூஜை, ஓதல், குருசீர்பாத சேவை), not a confession. The last lines recall Cheraman Perumal's ride to Kailas with Sundarar (ஆரூரர் தோழமை, the horse, the Ula).","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["naadham","vindhu","eegai","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:15"},"p:171":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":171,"t":["நிகமமெனி லொன்று மற்று நாடொறு","நெருடுகவி கொண்டு வித்தை பேசிய","நிழலர்சிறு புன்சொல் கற்று வீறுள ...... பெயர்கூறா","நெளியமுது தண்டு சத்ர சாமர","நிபிடமிட வந்து கைக்கு மோதிர","நெடுகியதி குண்ட லப்ர தாபமு ...... முடையோராய்","முகமுமொரு சம்பு மிக்க நூல்களு","முதுமொழியும் வந்தி ருக்கு மோவெனில்","முடிவிலவை யொன்று மற்று வேறொரு ...... நிறமாகி","முறியுமவர் தங்கள் வித்தை தானிது","முடியவுனை நின்று பத்தி யால்மிக","மொழியும்வளர் செஞ்சொல் வர்க்க மேவர ...... அருள்வாயே","திகுதிகென மண்ட விட்ட தீயொரு","செழியனுடல் சென்று பற்றி யாருகர்","திகையினமண் வந்து விட்ட போதினு ...... மமையாது","சிறியகர பங்க யத்து நீறொரு","தினையளவு சென்று பட்ட போதினில்","தெளியஇனி வென்றி விட்ட மோழைகள் ...... கழுவேற","மகிதலம ணைந்த அத்த யோனியை","வரைவறம ணந்து நித்த நீடருள்","வகைதனைய கன்றி ருக்கு மூடனை ...... மல்ருபம்","வரவரம னந்தி கைத்த பாவியை","வழியடிமை கொண்டு மிக்க மாதவர்","வளர்பழநி வந்த கொற்ற வேலவ ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet attacks court poets who learn petty verses, call them Veda, boast daily, win titles, palanquins, umbrellas, rings and earrings, but whose learning breaks and changes colour when tested, and asks that he may stand before Murugan and speak fine words with full devotion. The praise tells how a pinch of ash from a small hand cured the Pandya of the fever the Jains had set on him and the Jains were impaled, and how Murugan took as his hereditary servant a fool who embraced women without limit and a sinner dazed by impurity, at Palani.","n":"The target is professional learning: titles and regalia (சத்ர சாமர, மோதிரம், குண்டலம்) without substance. The cure of செழியன் with ash from a child's hand (சிறிய கர பங்கயத்து நீறு) is Sambandar's story, credited to Murugan as in 149 and 150. The poet calls himself the one taken into வழியடிமை, inherited service. Some lines of the confession are unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","thamizh","thiruneeru","thondu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:15"},"p:172":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":172,"t":["நெற்றிவெ யர்த்துளி துளிக்க வேயிரு","குத்துமு லைக்குட மசைத்து வீதியி","னிற்பவர் மைப்படர் விழிக்க லாபியர் ...... மொழியாலே","நித்தம யக்கிகள் மணத்த பூமலர்","மெத்தையில் வைத்ததி விதத்தி லேயுட","னெட்டுவ ரத்தொழில் கொடுத்து மேவியு ...... முறவாடி","உற்றவ கைப்படி பொருட்கள் யாவையு","மெத்தவு நட்பொடு பறித்து நாடொறு","முற்பன வித்தைகள் தொடுக்கு மாதர்க ...... ளுறவாமே","உச்சித மெய்ப்புற அனைத்து யாவுடன்","மெய்ப்படு பத்தியி னிணக்க மேபெற","வுட்குளிர் புத்தியை யெனக்கு நீதர ...... வருவாயே","கற்றத மிழ்ப்புல வனுக்கு மேமகிழ்","வுற்றொரு பொற்கொடி களிக்க வேபொரு","கற்பனை நெற்பல அளித்த காரண ...... னருள்பாலா","கற்பந கர்க்களி றளித்த மாதணை","பொற்புய மைப்புயல் நிறத்த வானவர்","கட்கிறை யுட்கிட அருட்க்ரு பாகர ...... எனநாளும்","நற்றவ ரர்ச்சனை யிடத்த யாபர","வஸ்துவெ னப்புவி யிடத்தி லேவளர்","நத்தணி செக்கரன் மகிழ்ச்சி கூர்தரு ...... மருகோனே","நட்டுவர் மத்தள முழக்க மாமென","மைக்குல மெத்தவு முழக்க மேதரு","நற்பழ நிப்பதி செழிக்க மேவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes kohl-eyed women who stand in the street with sweating foreheads and swaying breasts, delude men daily on flower beds, take every possession under friendship and invent new tricks each day, and asks that, instead of their company, Murugan give him a cool mind of true devotion. The praise names Siva who gave paddy to a learned Tamil poet, Devayanai whom the elephant of heaven raised, Vishnu with the conch as uncle, and Palani where the drummers' mridangam sounds like thunder.","n":"மெத்தவும் நட்பொடு பறித்து, robbing under friendship, is the courtesan charge. மெய்ப்படு பத்தி, true devotion, and உட்குளிர் புத்தி, a cool mind, are what is asked. The story of Siva giving paddy to a poet (நெல் பல அளித்த) I cannot identify with certainty.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","paththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:15"},"p:173":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":173,"t":["பகர்தற்கரி தான செந்தமி ழிசையிற்சில பாட லன்பொடு","பயிலப்பல காவி யங்களை ......யுணராதே","பவளத்தினை வீழி யின்கனி யதனைப்பொரு வாய் மடந்தையர்","பசலைத்தன மேபெ றும்படி ...... விரகாலே","சகரக்கடல் சூழ மம்புவி மிசையிப்படி யேதி ரிந்துழல்","சருகொத்துள மேய யர்ந்துடல் ...... மெலியாமுன்","தகதித்திமி தாகி ணங்கிண எனவுற்றெழு தோகை யம்பரி","தனிலற்புத மாக வந்தருள் ...... புரிவாயே","நுகர்வித்தக மாகு மென்றுமை மொழியிற்பொழி பாலை யுண்டிடு","நுவல்மெய்ப்புள பால னென்றிடு ...... மிளையோனே","நுதிவைத்தக ராம லைந்திடு களிறுக்கரு ளேபு ரிந்திட","நொடியிற்பரி வாக வந்தவன் ...... மருகோனே","அகரப்பொரு ளாதி யொன்றிடு முதலக்கர மான தின்பொருள்","அரனுக்கினி தாமொ ழிந்திடு ...... குருநாதா","அமரர்க்கிறை யேவ ணங்கிய பழநித்திரு வாவி னன்குடி","அதனிற்குடி யாயி ருந்தருள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses that he has not learned a few songs of fine Tamil in music or known the epics, and that through women with coral lips he wanders the sea-girt earth like a dry leaf; before his body thins he asks Murugan to come on the dancing peacock. The praise names him as the child who drank the milk Uma poured, Vishnu who came in a moment to save the elephant from the crocodile as uncle, and the guru who told Hara the meaning of the first letter, at Thiruvavinankudi in Palani.","n":"The confession sets neglected Tamil learning beside lust. உமை மொழியிற் பொழி பாலை உண்டிடு பாலன் is Sambandar, fed by Uma, again identified with Murugan. அகரப் பொருள் ஆதி ஒன்றிடு முதல் அக்கரம் is the meaning of the first syllable, the pranava, told to Siva.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thamizh","kaamam","guru","piranavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:15"},"p:174":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":174,"t":["பஞ்ச பாதகன் பாவிமுழு மூடன்வெகு","வஞ்ச லோபியன் சூதுகொலை காரன்மதி","பண்கொ ளாதவன் பாவகட லு஡டுநுழை ...... பவுஷாசை","பங்கன் மோதியம் பாழ்நரகில் வீணின்விழ","பெண்டிர் வீடுபொன் தேடிநொடி மீதில்மறை","பஞ்ச மாமலம் பாசமொடு கூடிவெகு ...... சதிகாரர்","அஞ்சு பூதமுண் டாகடிய காரரிவர்","தங்கள் வாணிபங் காரியம லாமலரு","ளன்பர் பாலுடன் கூடியறி யாதபுக ...... ழடியேனை","அண்டர் மாலயன் தேடியறி யாதவொளி","சந்த்ர சேகரன் பாவைவிளை யாடுபடி","கந்த நாடுடன் கூடிவிளை யாடஅருள் ...... புரிவாயே","வஞ்ச மாசுரன் சேனைகட லோடுகுவ","டுங்க வேயினன் போலவொளிர் வேலைவிடு","வண்கை யாகடம் பேடுதொடை யாடுமுடி ...... முருகோனே","மங்கை மோகசிங் காரரகு ராமரிட","தங்கை சூலியங் காளியெமை யீணபுகழ்","மங்க ளாயிசந் தானசிவ காமியுமை ...... யருள்பாலா","கொஞ்சு மாசுகம் போலமொழி நீலகடை","பெண்கள் நாயகந் தோகைமயில் போலிரச","கொங்கை மால்குறம் பாவையவல் தீரவர ...... அணைவோனே","கொண்டல் சூழமஞ் சோலைமலர் வாவிகயல்","கந்து பாயநின் றாடுதுவர் பாகையுதிர்","கந்தி யோடகஞ் சேர்பழநி வாழ்குமர ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet lists his sins: guilty of the five great crimes, a total fool, deceitful, a miser, a gambler, a killer, seeking women, house and gold, held by the five malas and bonds and by the five elements, who never joined the devotees of grace; and asks to be granted to play in Kanda's world, where the consort of the moon-crowned Siva, the light Vishnu and Brahma could not find, plays. The praise names the vel against Sura's army, the sea and Krauncha, Murugan as son of Uma, Rama's sister, called Sivakami, Kali and Mangala, and the hunter girl, at Palani.","n":"பஞ்ச பாதகன் (guilty of the five great sins) and பஞ்ச மாமலம் (five impurities) set the confession in Siddhanta terms. The goal named is கந்த நாடு, Kanda's world, a heaven rather than union. Uma as Rama's sister (ரகுராமர் இட தங்கை) follows the tradition that makes Vishnu Uma's brother.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["kutram","malam","paasam","adiyaar"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:15"},"p:175":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":175,"t":["பாரி யானகொடைக் கொண்ட லேதிரு","வாழ்வி சாலதொடைத் திண்பு யாஎழு","பாரு மேறுபுகழ்க் கொண்ட நாயக ...... அபிராம","பாவ லோர்கள்கிளைக் கென்றும் வாழ்வருள்","சீல ஞாலவிளக் கின்ப சீவக","பாக சாதனவுத் துங்க மானத ...... எனவோதிச்","சீர தாகஎடுத் தொன்று மாகவி","பாடி னாலுமிரக் கஞ்செ யாதுரை","சீறு வார்கடையிற் சென்று தாமயர் ...... வுறவீணே","சேய பாவகையைக் கொண்டு போயறி","யாம லேகமரிற் சிந்து வார்சிலர்","சேய னார்மனதிற் சிந்தி யாரரு ...... குறலாமோ","ஆரு நீர்மைமடுக் கண்க ராநெடு","வாயி னேர்படவுற் றன்று மூலமெ","னார வாரமதத் தந்தி தானுய ...... அருள்மாயன்","ஆதி நாராணனற் சங்க பாணிய","னோது வார்களுளத் தன்பன் மாதவ","னான நான்முகனற் றந்தை சீதரன் ...... மருகோனே","வீர சேவகவுத் தண்ட தேவகு","மார ஆறிருபொற் செங்கை நாயக","வீசு தோகைமயிற் றுங்க வாகன ...... முடையோனே","வீறு காவிரியுட் கொண்ட சேகர","னான சேவகனற் சிந்தை மேவிய","வீரை வாழ்பழநித் துங்க வானவர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes singing great poems to patrons, praising them as Pari in giving, a cloud, mighty-shouldered, famed in seven worlds, the light of the world and Indra's equal, only to be met with anger and no pity and to wilt at their doors in vain, while others carry off the poem and waste it; he asks whether those who do not think of Murugan can come near him. The praise names Vishnu, who saved the elephant crying 'first cause' from the crocodile, as uncle, Murugan with twelve hands on the peacock, and the servant from Kalisai, at Veerai and Palani.","n":"The patrons are flattered with the stock comparisons (பாரியான கொடை, கொண்டலே), the very praise the poet now regrets (சீறுவார் கடையிற் சென்று). The closing question turns the complaint: men who do not think of Murugan are unworthy of praise. Vishnu saving the elephant (மூலமென் ஆரவாரம்) parallels 173.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iravu","pugazh","thamizh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:15"},"p:176":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":176,"t":["புடவிக் கணிதுகி லெனவள ரந்தக்","கடலெட் டையுமற குடிமுநி யெண்கட்","புநிதச் சததள நிலைகொள்ச யம்புச் ...... சதுர்வேதன்","புரமட் டெரியெழ விழிகனல் சிந்திக்","கடினத் தொடுசில சிறுநகை கொண்டற்","புதகர்த் தரகர பரசிவ னிந்தத் ...... தன஧முவ","ரிடசித் தமுநிறை தெளிவுற வும்பொற்","செவியுட் பிரணவ ரகசிய மன்புற்","றிடவுற் பனமொழி யுரைசெய் குழந்தைக் ...... குருநாதா","எதிருற் றசுரர்கள் படைகொடு சண்டைக்","கிடம்வைத் திடஅவர் குலமுழு தும்பட்","டிடவுக் கிரமொடு வெகுளிகள் பொங்கக் ...... கிரியாவும்","பொடிபட் டுதிரவும் விரிவுறு மண்டச்","சுவர்விட் டதிரவு முகடுகி ழிந்தப்","புறமப் பரவெளி கிடுகிடெ னுஞ்சத் ...... தமுமாகப்","பொருதுக் கையிலுள அயில்நிண முண்கக்","குருதிப் புனலெழு கடலினு மிஞ்சப்","புரவிக் கனமயில் நடவிடும் விந்தைக் ...... குமரேசா","படியிற் பெருமித தகவுயர் செம்பொற்","கிரியைத் தனிவலம் வரஅர னந்தப்","பலனைக் கரிமுகன் வசமரு ளும்பொற் ...... பதனாலே","பரன்வெட் கிடவுள மிகவும்வெ குண்டக்","கனியைத் தரவிலை யெனஅருள் செந்திற்","பழநிச் சிவகிரி தனிலுறை கந்தப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A song of praise alone: Siva, who burned the cities with a smile, is made clear in mind when the child guru speaks the secret of the pranava into his golden ear; Murugan fights the asuras until the mountains turn to dust and blood overflows the sea. When Siva gave the fruit to the elephant-faced one for circling his feet while Murugan circled the world, Murugan grew angry that it was not given to him and came to dwell at Palani, Sivagiri.","n":"This is the Palani legend: பொற்கிரியைத் தனி வலம் வர (circling the golden mountain, the world), அக்கனியைத் தரவிலை என வெகுண்டு (angry that the fruit was not given). The anger is told plainly and the shrine is its result. பொற்செவியுட் பிரணவ ரகசியம் ... உரைசெய் குழந்தைக் குருநாதா names the pranava. One line is corrupt.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["piranavam","guru","vekuli"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:15"},"p:177":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":177,"t":["புடைசெப் பெனமுத் தணிகச் சறவுட்","பொருமிக் கலசத் ...... திணையாய","புளகக் களபக் கெருவத் தனமெய்ப்","புணரத் தலையிட் ...... டமரேசெய்","அடைவிற் றினமுற் றவசப் படுமெற்","கறிவிற் பதடிக் ...... கவமான","அசடற் குயர்வொப் பதில்நற் க்ருபையுற்","றடிமைக் கொருசொற் ...... புகல்வாயே","குடமொத் தகடக் கரடக் கலுழிக்","குணமெய்க் களிறுக் ...... கிளையோனே","குடிபுக் கிடமிட் டசுரப் படையைக்","குறுகித் தகரப் ...... பொரும்வேலா","படலைச் செறிநற் கதலிக் குலையிற்","பழமுற் றொழுகப் ...... புனல்சேர்நீள்","பழனக் கரையிற் கழைமுத் துகுநற்","பழநிக் குமரப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses that, embracing women's adorned breasts daily, he has become helpless, chaff in understanding, a worthless fool, and asks Murugan, with incomparable grace, to speak one word to his servant. The praise names him the younger brother of the elephant with temples like pots and the vel that rushed on the asura army, at Palani with its dripping banana clusters and sugarcane fields.","n":"அறிவிற் பதடி, chaff in understanding, and அசடன் are the self-names. The plea for 'one word' (ஒருசொல் புகல்வாயே) echoes the word Murugan spoke to Siva, asked now for the poet.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","pedhaimai","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:15"},"p:178":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":178,"t":["பெரியதோர் கரியிரு கொம்பு போலவெ","வடிவமார் புளகித கும்ப மாமுலை","பெருகியே யொளிசெறி தங்க வாரமு ...... மணியான","பிறையதோ வெனுநுதல் துங்க மீறுவை","அயிலதோ வெனுமிரு கண்க ளாரவெ","பிறகெலாம் விழுகுழல் கங்கு லாரவெ ...... வருமானார்","உரியதோர் பொருள்கொடு வந்த பேர்களை","மனையிலே வினவியெ கொண்டு போகிய","யுளவிலே மருவிய வஞ்ச மாதர்கள் ...... மயலாலே","உருகியே யுடலற வெம்பி வாடியெ","வினையிலே மறுகியெ நொந்த பாதக","னுனதுதாள் தொழுதிட இன்ப ஞானம ...... தருள்வாயே","அரியதோ ரமரர்க ளண்ட மேறவெ","கொடியதோ ரசுரர்க ளங்க மாளவெ","அடலதோ டமரர்புரி கின்ற கூரிய ...... வடிவேலா","அரகரா வெனமிக அன்பர் சூழவெ","கடியதோர் மயில்மிசை யன்றை யேறியெ","அவனியோர் நொடிவரு கின்ற காரண ...... முருகோனே","பரியதோர் கயிறனை கொண்டு வீசவெ","உறியதோய் தயிர்தனை யுண்டு நாடியே","பசியதோ கெடவருள் கொண்ட மாயவன் ...... மருகோனே","பரமமா நதிபுடை கொண்ட ணாவவெ","வனசமா மலரினில் வண்டு லாவவெ","பழநிமா மலைதனி லென்று மேவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women with breasts like tusks, crescent brows, vel eyes and night-dark hair, who question those who come with property at their houses and carry it away, deceitful at heart, and says that, infatuated, he melted, wasted and whirled in deeds; he asks for the bliss of knowledge to worship Murugan's feet. The praise names the vel that restored the gods, Murugan circling the earth on the peacock in a moment with devotees crying 'Hara', and Krishna who was tied with a rope and ate curd from the hanging pot, at Palani by the great river.","n":"உரியதோர் பொருள்கொடு வந்த பேர்களை மனையிலே வினவியே கொண்டுபோகிய: the women take the property men bring, and the verse calls them வஞ்ச மாதர்கள். The poet's state is மயல், and the result is bodily wasting (உடலற வெம்பி).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","mayal","vanjam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:15"},"p:179":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":179,"t":["போத கந்தரு கோவே நமோநம","நீதி தங்கிய தேவா நமோநம","பூத லந்தனை யாள்வாய் நமோநம ...... பணியாவும்","பூணு கின்றபி ரானே நமோநம","வேடர் தங்கொடி மாலா நமோநம","போத வன்புகழ் ஸாமீ நமோநம ...... அரிதான","வேத மந்திர ரூபா நமோநம","ஞான பண்டித நாதா நமோநம","வீர கண்டைகொள் தாளா நமோநம ...... அழகான","மேனி தங்கிய வேளே நமோநம","வான பைந்தொடி வாழ்வே நமோநம","வீறு கொண்டவி சாகா நமோநம ...... அருள்தாராய்","பாத கஞ்செறி சூராதி மாளவெ","கூர்மை கொண்டயி லாலே பொராடியெ","பார அண்டர்கள் வானாடு சேர்தர ...... அருள்வோனே","பாதி சந்தர னேசூடும் வேணியர்","சூல சங்கர னார்கீத நாயகர்","பார திண்புய மேசேரு சோதியர் ...... கயிலாயர்","ஆதி சங்கர னார்பாக மாதுமை","கோல அம்பிகை மாதா மநோமணி","ஆயி சுந்தரி தாயான நாரணி ...... அபிராமி","ஆவல் கொண்டுவி றாலே சிராடவெ","கோம ளம்பல சூழ்கோ யில்மீறிய","ஆவி னன்குடி வாழ்வான தேவர்கள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A litany of praise ending each line with நமோநம: Murugan is hailed as the king who gives teaching, the god in whom justice dwells, the form of the rare Veda mantras, the master of jnana, the one in love with the hunters' creeper and the treasure of the heavenly girl, and is asked for grace. The second half tells how his sharp vel killed sinful Sura so the gods regained heaven, praises Siva of Kailash, and names Uma by many titles, Ambika, Manonmani, Sundari, Narayani, Abhirami, as the mother who fondles him, at the shrine of Avinankudi.","n":"There is no confession here; the song is a string of invocations, a form of worship by naming. போதகந்தரு கோவே and ஞான பண்டித நாதா make Murugan the giver of knowledge, and வேத மந்திர ரூபா makes him the mantra's form. The phrase ஆவல் கொண்டு விறாலே சிராடவெ, apparently the mother's fond play with her son, is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","gnaanam","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:16"},"p:180":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":180,"t":["மந்தரம தெனவே சிறந்த","கும்பமுலை தனிலே புனைந்த","மஞ்சள்மண மதுவே துலங்க ...... வகைபேசி","மன்றுகமழ் தெருவீ திவந்து","நின்றவரை விழியால் வளைந்து","வந்தவரை யருகே யணைந்து ...... தொழில்கூறி","எந்தளவு மினிதா கநம்பு","தந்துபொருள் தனையே பிடுங்கி","யின்பமருள் விலைமாதர் தங்கள் ...... மனைதேடி","எஞ்சிமன முழலாம லுன்றன்","அன்புடைமை மிகவே வழங்கி","என்றனையு மினிதாள இன்று ...... வரவேணும்","விந்தையெனு முமைமா துதந்த","கந்தகுரு பரதே வவங்க","மென்றவரை தனில்மேவு மெந்தை ...... புதல்வோனே","மிஞ்சுமழ கினிலே சிறந்த","மங்கைகுற மடமா துகொங்கை","மென்கிரியி லிதமா யணைந்த ...... முருகோனே","சிந்தைமகிழ் புலவோர்கள் வந்து","வந்தனைசெய் சரணார விந்த","செந்தமிழ் லுனையே வணங்கு ...... குருநாதர்","தென்றல்வரை முநிநாத ரன்று","கும்பிடந லருளே பொழிந்த","தென்பழநி மலைமே லுகந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women with turmeric-scented breasts who walk the fragrant streets, bend passers-by with their eyes, embrace those who come, state their trade, win full trust and then snatch their wealth, and asks that his mind not wander seeking the houses of these women who sell pleasure, but that Murugan come today, give him abundant love and rule him. The praise names him Uma's son and Kanda the guru, lover of the Kurava girl, whose lotus feet poets worship, and to whom the sage of the southern-breeze mountain bowed in fine Tamil and received grace, on southern Palani.","n":"தொழில்கூறி and விலைமாதர் name the trade plainly: these are women who sell. The plea asks for the Lord's love (அன்புடைமை) to replace theirs. தென்றல்வரை முநிநாதர் is the sage of Pothigai, Agastya, here worshipping Murugan as guru in Tamil, the claim the book repeats that Murugan taught Tamil to Agastya.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","thiruvarul","thamizh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:16"},"p:181":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":181,"t":["மருமலரி னன்து ரந்து விடவினைய ருந்த அந்தி","மதியொடுபி றந்து முன்பெய் ...... வதையாலே","வகைதனைம றந்தெ ழுந்து முலைதனைய ருந்தி யந்த","மதலையென வந்து குன்றின் ...... வடிவாகி","இருமயல்கொ டுந்து வண்டு பொதுவையர கம்பு குந்து","இரவுபகல் கொண்டொ டுங்கி ...... யசடாகும்","இருவினைபொ திந்த இந்த ஜனனமர ணந்து றந்து","னிணையடிவ ணங்க என்று ...... பெறுவேனோ","திருவொடுபெ யர்ந்தி ருண்ட வனமிசைந டந்தி லங்கை","திகழெரியி டுங்கு ரங்கை ...... நெகிழாத","திடமுளமு குந்தர் கஞ்சன் வரவிடுமெல் வஞ்ச கங்கள்","செறிவுடன் றிந்து வென்ற ...... பொறியாளர்","பரிவொடும கிழ்ந்தி றைஞ்சு மருதிடைத வழ்ந்து நின்ற","பரமபத நண்ப ரன்பின் ...... மருகோனே","பதுமமிசை வண்ட லம்பு சுனைபலவி ளங்கு துங்க","பழநிமலை வந்த மர்ந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet traces his life from Brahma's sending him out to eat his deeds, through birth, the breast and childhood, to a grown body that enters the houses of common women and spends night and day there until it turns foolish, and asks when, leaving this birth and death bound up with two kinds of deed, he will worship the Lord's paired feet. The praise is given to Vishnu as Murugan's uncle: Rama who walked the dark forest with Sita and whose monkey burned Lanka, and Krishna who saw through Kamsa's tricks and crawled between the marudha trees, and ends at Palani of lotus pools.","n":"The song is a small autobiography of the soul: மருமலரினன் துரந்துவிட, sent off by the one on the flower (Brahma), வினை அருந்த, to consume deeds. பொதுவையர் அகம் are the houses of women common to all. The opening lines (அந்தி மதியொடு பிறந்து முன் பெய்வதையாலே) are hard to divide, and செறிவுடன் றிந்து looks damaged.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","vinai","kaamam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:16"},"p:182":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":182,"t":["மனக்கவலை யேது மின்றி உனக்கடிமை யேபு ரிந்து","வகைக்குமநு நூல்வி தங்கள் ...... தவறாதே","வகைப்படிம னோர தங்கள் தொகைப்படியி னாலி லங்கி","மயக்கமற வேத முங்கொள் ...... பொருள்நாடி","வினைக்குரிய பாத கங்கள் துகைத்துவகை யால்நி னைந்து","மிகுத்தபொரு ளாக மங்கள் ...... முறையாலே","வெகுட்சிதனை யேது ரந்து களிப்பினுட னேந டந்து","மிகுக்குமுனை யேவ ணங்க ...... வரவேணும்","மனத்தில்வரு வோனெ என்று னடைக்கலம தாக வந்து","மலர்ப்பதம தேப ணிந்த ...... முநிவோர்கள்","வரர்க்குமிமை யோர்க ளென்பர் தமக்குமன மேயி ரங்கி","மருட்டிவரு சூரை வென்ற ...... முனைவேலா","தினைப்புனமு னேந டந்து குறக்கொடியை யேம ணந்து","செகத்தைமுழு தாள வந்த ...... பெரியோனே","செழித்தவள மேசி றந்த மலர்ப்பொழில்க ளேநி றைந்த","திருப்பழநி வாழ வந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"Unusually, the plea confesses nothing: the poet asks that, free of anxiety and serving Murugan, not straying from the rules of the Manu texts, seeking the meaning the Vedas hold and following the Agamas in order, trampling the sins deeds bring and driving out anger, he walk in joy and come to worship him. The praise names him the one who enters the mind, who pitied the sages and gods who took refuge at his flower feet and defeated Sura with the vel, and who married the Kurava creeper in the millet field, at fertile Palani.","n":"உனக்கடிமையே புரிந்து is service, and it is joined to an orthodox programme: மநுநூல், the Veda and the ஆகமங்கள் முறையாலே. The rhythm makes வகைப்படி மனோரதங்கள் தொகைப்படியினால் இலங்கி hard to construe; it seems to ask that his wishes be fulfilled in due order.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thondu","vedham","aagamam","vekuli"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:16"},"p:183":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":183,"t":["மலரணி கொண்டைச் சொருக்கி லேயவள்","சொலுமொழி யின்பச் செருக்கி லேகொடு","மையுமடர் நெஞ்சத் திருக்கி லேமுக ...... மதியாலே","மருவுநி தம்பத் தடத்தி லேநிறை","பரிமள கொங்கைக் குடத்தி லேமிக","வலியவும் வந்தொத் திடத்தி லேவிழி ...... வலையாலே","நிலவெறி யங்கக் குலுக்கி லேயெழில்","வளைபுனை செங்கைக் கிலுக்கி லேகன","நிதிபறி யந்தப் பிலுக்கி லேசெயு ...... மொயிலாலே","நிதமிய லுந்தர்க் குணத்தி லேபர","வசமுட னன்புற் றிணக்கி லேயொரு","நிமிஷமி ணங்கிக் கணத்தி லேவெகு ...... மதிகேடாய்","அலையநி னைந்துற் பநத்தி லேயநு","தினமிகு மென்சொப் பனத்தி லேவர","அறிவும ழிந்தற் பனத்தி லேநிதம் ...... உலைவேனோ","அசடனை வஞ்சச் சமர்த்த னாகிய","கசடனை யுன்சிற் கடைக்கணாடிய","மலர்கொடு நின்பொற் பதத்தை யேதொழ ...... அருள்தாராய்","பலபல பைம்பொற் பதக்க மாரமு","மடிமைசொ லுஞ்சொற் றமிழ்ப்ப னீரொடு","பரிமள மிஞ்சக் கடப்ப மாலையு ...... மணிவோனே","பதியினில் மங்கைக் கதித்த மாமலை","யொடுசில குன்றிற் றரித்து வாழ்வுயர்","பழநியி லன்புற் றிருக்கும் வானவர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"In a long chain of phrases each ending in இலே, the poet names what caught him: a woman's flowered hair, the pride of her sweet words, the twist of her cruel heart, her hips and breasts, her coming close uninvited, the net of her eyes, the jingle of her bangles and the pose that snatches great wealth, so that joined to her for a moment he lost his wits and wanders in thought and dream; he asks that, fool and deceiver as he is, he may worship the golden feet with flowers by Murugan's glance. The praise pictures Murugan wearing gold pendants and kadamba garlands and the Tamil of those who call themselves his servants, dwelling in love at Palani.","n":"The anaphora (கொண்டைச் சொருக்கிலே, செருக்கிலே, இருக்கிலே) makes the list itself the snare. கனநிதி பறி அந்தப் பிலுக்கிலே, the pose that plucks away great wealth, marks her as a courtesan. In the praise the devotee's Tamil is one of Murugan's ornaments, worn with rose-water and the kadamba garland. The lines on the mountain and hills before Palani are unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","kutram","thiruvadi","thamizh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:16"},"p:184":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":184,"t":["முகிலள கத்திற் கமழ்ந்த வண்பரி","மளஅலர் துற்றக் கலந்தி டந்தரு","முகிழ்நுதி தைத்துத் துயர்ந்த மங்கைய ...... ரங்கமீதே","முகம்வெயர் வுற்றுப் பரந்து செங்கயல்","விழியிணை செக்கச் சிவந்து குங்கும","ம்ருகமத மத்தத் தனங்க ளின்மிசை ...... யெங்குமேவி","உகவுயி ரொத்துப் புயங்க ளின்புற","வுறவினை யுற்றுத் திரண்டு கொங்கள","வுறுமணை யுற்றுத் திரங்கு மஞ்சமி ...... லொன்றிமேவி","ஒளிதிகழ் பத்மக் கரங்க ளின்புற","முறுவளை யொக்கக் கலின்க லென்கவு","முயர்மய லுற்றுற் றிரங்கு மன்பொத ...... ழிந்திடாதோ","செகமுழு தொக்கப் பயந்த சங்கரி","அடியவர் சித்தத் துறைந்த சம்ப்ரம","சிவனொரு பக்கத் துறைந்த மங்கைசு ...... மங்கைநீடு","திகழ்வன பச்சைப் பசங்கி யம்பண","கரதலி கச்சுற் றிலங்கு கொங்கையள்","திருவரு ணற்பொற் பரந்தி டும்பரை ...... யண்டமீதே","பகலிர வற்றிட் டுயர்ந்த அம்பிகை","திரிபுரை முற்றிட் டிரண்டொ டொன்றலர்","பரிவுற வொக்கச் செயும்ப ரம்ப்ரமி ...... யன்புகூரும்","பதிவ்ரதை மிக்கச் சிரந்தெ ரிந்தருள்","பகிரதி வெற்பிற் பிறந்த பெண்தரு","பழநியில் வெற்பிற் றிகழ்ந்து நின்றருள் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes lovemaking at length, flowers in the hair, nail-marks, sweating faces, reddened eyes, musk on breasts, bodies joined as one life on the couch, bangles ringing, and asks whether this love of rising infatuation will not end. The whole praise is given to the Goddess: Sankari who bore the world, dwelling in devotees' minds and in one half of Siva, the green one, Parai, Ambika beyond day and night, Tripura, Parabrahmi, the faithful wife, the girl born on the mountain, who gave Murugan, Lord at Palani.","n":"The plea is only a question (அன்பு ஒழிந்திடாதோ), asking for no gift. The praise is a hymn to Shakti in her cosmic titles, and Murugan appears only in the last word, தம்பிரானே, which here replaces பெருமாளே. Several compounds in the Goddess's titles (பசங்கியம் பண கரதலி, இரண்டொடு ஒன்று அலர்) are obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","mayal","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:16"},"p:185":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":185,"t":["முகைமுளரி ப்ரபைவீசு மெழில்கனக மலைபோலு","முதிர்விலிள தனபார ...... மடவார்தோள்","முழுகியமி ழநுபோக விழலனென வுலகோர்கள்","மொழியுமது மதியாமல் ...... தலைகீழ்வீழ்ந்","தகமகிழ விதமான நகையமுத மெனவூற","லசடரக மெழவாகி ...... மிகவேயுண்","டழியுமொரு தமியேனு மொழியுமுன திருதாளி","னமுதுபரு கிடஞான ...... மருளாயோ","மகரமெறி திரைமோது பகரகடல் தடவாரி","மறுகுபுனல் கெடவேலை ...... விடுவோனே","வரிசையவுண் மகசேனை யுகமுடிய மயிலேறி","வருபவனிரு கரதீர ...... முருகோனே","பகர்வரிய ரெனலாகு முமைகொழுந ருளமேவு","பரமகுரு வெனநாடு ...... மிளையோனே","பணிலமணி வெயில்வீசு மணிசிகர மதிசூடு","பழநிமலை தனில்மேவு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses sinking into women's arms and pleasure, heedless of the world calling him a worthless man, falling headlong and drinking the nectar of their smiles until he perishes, and asks Murugan for jnana, so that he may drink instead the nectar of the Lord's two feet. The praise names him the one whose vel churned the sea, who rode the peacock against the asura army, and the young one whom Siva, said to be beyond speech, holds in his heart as supreme guru, at Palani whose peaks touch the moon.","n":"விழலன் என உலகோர்கள் மொழியும் அது மதியாமல்: he heard the world's blame and ignored it. The two nectars are set against each other, the smile's (நகையமுதம்) and the feet's (இருதாளின் அமுது). உமைகொழுநர் உளமேவு பரமகுரு alludes to Murugan as Siva's teacher without telling the story.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","pazhi","gnaanam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:16"},"p:186":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":186,"t":["முதிர வுழையை வனத்தில் முடுகி வடுவை யழித்து","முதிய கயல்கள் கயத்தி ...... னிடையோடி","முரண வளரும் விழிக்குள் மதன விரகு பயிற்றி","முறைமை கெடவு மயக்கி ...... வருமாதர்","மதுர அமுத மொழிக்கு மகுட களப முலைக்கு","வலிய அடிமை புகுத்தி ...... விடுமாய","மனதை யுடைய அசட்டு மனிதன் முழுது புரட்டன்","மகிழ வுனது பதத்தை ...... யருள்வாயே","சதுரன் வரையை யெடுத்த நிருத னுடலை வதைத்து","சகடு மருத முதைத்த ...... தகவோடே","தழையு மரமு நிலத்தில் மடிய அமரை விளைத்த","தநுவை யுடைய சமர்த்தன் ...... மருகோனே","அதிர முடுகி யெதிர்த்த அசுர ருடலை வதைத்து","அமரர் சிறையை விடுத்து ...... வருவோனே","அரிய புகழை யமைத்த பெரிய பழநி மலைக்கு","ளழகு மயிலை நடத்து ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women whose eyes outdo the deer, the mango slice and the carp, teaching Kama's arts and destroying propriety, and whose sweet speech and sandal-pasted breasts force men into slavery, and asks that he, a foolish, false-minded cheat, be given Murugan's feet to rejoice in. The praise is for Vishnu as uncle, who killed the demon that lifted the mountain, kicked the cart and the marudha trees and drew the bow that felled trees, and for Murugan who freed the gods from prison and makes his peacock walk at Palani.","n":"வலிய அடிமை புகுத்திவிடும்: here அடிமை is bondage to the women, not service to the Lord, the sense the concept adimai names. The eyes are praised by the old conceit of defeating deer, mango and carp. The poet calls himself அசட்டு மனிதன் முழுது புரட்டன, a foolish man and a cheat through and through.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kan","adimai","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:16"},"p:187":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":187,"t":["முத்துக்குச் சிட்டுக் குப்பிமு","டித்துச்சுக் கைப்பிற் சுற்றியு","முற்பக்கத் திற்பொற் புற்றிட ...... நுதல்மீதே","முக்யப்பச் சைப்பொட் டிட்டணி","ரத்நச்சுட் டிப்பொற் பட்டிவை","முச்சட்டைச் சித்ரக் கட்டழ ...... கெழிலாடத்","தித்திக்கச் சொற்சொற் றுப்பிதழ்","நச்சுக்கட் கற்புச் சொக்கியர்","செப்புக்கொக் கக்கச் சுப்பெறு ...... தனமேருத்","திட்டத்தைப் பற்றிப் பற்பல","லச்சைக்குட் பட்டுத் தொட்டுயிர்","சிக்கிச்சொக் கிக்கெட் டிப்படி ...... யுழல்வேனோ","மெத்தத்துக் கத்தைத் தித்தியி","னிச்சித்தத் திற்பத் தத்தொடு","மெச்சிச்சொர்க் கத்திற் சிற்பர ...... மருள்வாயே","வித்தைக்குக் கர்த்ருத் தற்பர","முக்கட்சித் தர்க்குப் புத்திர","விச்சித்ரச் செச்சைக் கத்திகை ...... புனைவோனே","நித்யக்கற் பத்திற் சித்தர்க","ளெட்டுத்திக் குக்குட் பட்டவர்","நிஷ்டைக்கற் புற்றப் பத்தர்கள் ...... அமரோரும்","நெட்டுக்குப் புட்பத் தைக்கொடு","முற்றத்துற் றர்ச்சிக் கப்பழ","நிக்குட்பட் டத்துக் குற்றுறை ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"In a dense, doubled-consonant rhythm the poet lists a woman's adornments, the pearl tassel and coiled hair, the green mark and jewelled pendant on the forehead, the three-layered bodice, the sweet words, coral lips and poisonous eyes, and asks whether he must go on wandering, caught by such seductresses and their breasts, his life snared and ruined; he asks that his great sorrow be removed and that, with devotion in his mind, he be given the supreme consciousness. The praise names Murugan the master of knowledge, son of the three-eyed Siddha, wearing the red vetchi garland, worshipped with flowers by siddhas of the eight directions, devotees in nishtha and gods at Palani.","n":"The sound is the point: almost every word doubles a hard consonant (முத்துக்குச் சிட்டுக் குப்பி). பற்பல லச்சைக்குட்பட்டு, falling into many shames, names his own disgrace. சிற்பரம் is the supreme of consciousness, and பத்தத்தொடு seems to stand for bhakti; the splitting of many lines is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","paththi","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:16"},"p:188":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":188,"t":["மூலங்கிள ரோருரு வாய்நடு","நாலங்குல மேனடு வேரிடை","மூள்பிங்கலை நாடியொ டாடிய ...... முதல்வேர்கள்","மூணும்பிர காசம தாயொரு","சூலம்பெற வோடிய வாயுவை","மூலந்திகழ் தூண்வழி யேயள ...... விடவோடிப்","பாலங்கிள ராறுசி காரமொ","டாருஞ்சுட ராடுப ராபர","பாதம்பெற ஞானச தாசிவ ...... மதின்மேவிப்","பாடுந்தொனி நாதமு நூபுர","மாடுங்கழ லோசையி லேபரி","வாகும்படி யேயடி யேனையும் ...... அருள்வாயே","சூலங்கலை மான்மழு வோர்துடி","வேதன்தலை யோடும ராவிரி","தோடுங்குழை சேர்பர னார்தரு ...... முருகோனே","சூரன்கர மார்சிலை வாளணி","தோளுந்தலை தூள்பட வேஅவர்","சூளுங்கெட வேல்விடு சேவக ...... மயில்வீரா","காலின்கழ லோசையு நூபுர","வார்வெண்டைய வோசையு மேயுக","காலங்களி னோசைய தாநட ...... மிடுவோனே","கானங்கலை மான்மக ளார்தமை","நாணங்கெட வேயணை வேள்பிர","காசம்பழ னாபுரி மேவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A song of yoga: the poet asks that the breath, run through the channels from the root (ida, pingala and the middle way) up the pillar through the six centres, reach the feet of the Supreme in the radiance of Jnana Sadasiva, and that he become one with the nada and the ringing anklets of the dancing foot. The praise names Murugan the son of Siva who bears the trident, deer, axe, drum and Brahma's skull, the vel that reduced Sura to dust, the dancer whose anklets sound through the ages, and the lover of the forest deer-girl, at Palani.","n":"The vocabulary is technical yoga, as in Tirumular: மூலம், பிங்கலை நாடி, வாயு, மூலந்திகழ் தூண் (the spine as pillar), பால் அங்கு இளர் ஆறு (the six centres), ஞான சதாசிவம். The goal is heard, not only seen: the sound of the nada is the anklet of the Lord's dancing foot. The measurements (நாலங்குலம்) and the சிகாரம் of the sixth line are obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kundalini","aadhaaram","praanan","naadham"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:16"},"p:189":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":189,"t":["மூல மந்திர மோத லிங்கிலை","யீவ திங்கிலை நேய மிங்கிலை","மோன மிங்கிலை ஞான மிங்கிலை ...... மடவார்கள்","மோக முண்டதி தாக முண்டப","சார முண்டப ராத முண்டிடு","மூக னென்றொரு பேரு முண்டருள் ...... பயிலாத","கோல முங்குண வீன துன்பர்கள்","வார்மை யும்பல வாகி வெந்தெழு","கோர கும்பியி லேவி ழுந்திட ...... நினைவாகிக்","கூடு கொண்டுழல் வேனை யன்பொடு","ஞான நெஞ்சினர் பாலி ணங்கிடு","கூர்மை தந்தினி யாள வந்தருள் ...... புரிவாயே","பீலி வெந்துய ராலி வெந்தவ","சோகு வெந்தமண் மூகர் நெஞ்சிடை","பீதி கொண்டிட வாது கொண்டரு ...... ளெழுதேடு","பேணி யங்கெதி ராறு சென்றிட","மாற னும்பிணி தீர வஞ்சகர்","பீறு வெங்கழு வேற வேன்றிடு ...... முருகோனே","ஆல முண்டவர் சோதி யங்கணர்","பாக மொன்றிய வாலை யந்தரி","ஆதி யந்தமு மான சங்கரி ...... குமரேசா","ஆர ணம்பயில் ஞான புங்கவ","சேவ லங்கொடி யான பைங்கர","ஆவி னன்குடி வாழ்வு கொண்டருள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet lists what he lacks, the chanting of the root mantra, giving, love, silence and jnana, and what he has, lust for women, great thirst, transgression and offence and the name of a dumb fool, and confesses he wanders in this body while thinking of falling into the burning hell Kumbhi; he asks for the sharpness to keep company with those of jnana-filled heart. The praise tells of the Jains, their peacock-feather fans burnt, who argued, of the palm leaf that went against the river, the Pandya cured of illness and the deceivers sent to the stake, as Murugan's work, and names him Sankari's son, the jnana master of the Vedas, at Avinankudi.","n":"The opening is a catalogue of negations (ஓதல் இங்கு இலை, ஈவது இங்கு இலை, மோனம் இங்கு இலை, ஞானம் இங்கு இலை) followed by a catalogue of possessions, with மூகன் (dumb one) as his name. The remedy asked is fellowship: ஞான நெஞ்சினர் பால் இணங்கிடு கூர்மை. The praise identifies Murugan with Sambandar, by tradition the saint whose leaf floated against the Vaigai and who cured the Pandya (மாறன்) before the Jains were impaled.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kutram","naragam","adiyaar","mandhiram"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:16"},"p:190":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":190,"t":["முருகுசெறி குழலவிழ முலைபுளக மெழநிலவு","முறுவல்தர விரகமெழ ...... அநுராகம்","முதிரவச மறவிதரி யெழுவகைவளை கலகலென","முகநிலவு குறுவெயர்வு ...... துளிவீச","அருமதுர மொழிபதற இதழமுது பருகிமிக","அகமகிழ இருகயல்கள் ...... குழையேற","அமளிபடு மமளிமல ரணையின் மிசை துயிலுகினும்","அலர்கமல மலரடியை ...... மறவேனே","நிருதனொடு வருபுரியு மடுகரியும் ரதநிரையும்","நெறுநெறன முறியவிடும் ...... வடிவேலா","நிகழகள சகளகுரு நிருபகுரு பரகுமர","நெடியநெடு ககனமுக ...... டுறைவோனே","வருமருவி நவமணிகள் மலர்கமுகின் மிசைசிதற","மதுவினிரை பெருகுவளி ...... மலைமீதே","வளர்குறவர் சிறுமியிரு வளர்தனமு மிருபுயமு","மருவிமகிழ் பழநிவரு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes lovemaking in full detail, loosened hair, thrilling breasts, jingling bangles, sweat, faltering words, drinking the lips' nectar, and says that even lying on that flower-bed he will not forget Murugan's lotus feet. The praise names the vel that crushed Nirudan's chariots and elephants, the guru with form and without, the Kumara who dwells at the top of the sky, and the lover of the Kurava girl at Palani where waterfalls scatter gems over the areca palms.","n":"Unlike most such songs this one does not ask to leave the embrace: துயிலுகினும் அலர்கமல மலரடியை மறவேனே, 'even if I sleep there, I will not forget the feet'. It reads as a vow of remembrance inside desire rather than a plea for escape. நிகழ் அகள சகள குரு names the Lord as both formless and formed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","thiruvadi","paththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:16"},"p:191":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":191,"t":["முருகு செறிகுழல் முகிலென நகில்நறு","முளரி முகையென இயலென மயிலென","முறுவல் தளவென நடைமட வனமென ...... இருபார்வை","முளரி மடலென இடைதுடி யதுவென","அதர மிலவென அடியிணை மலரென","மொழியு மமுதென முகமெழில் மதியென ...... மடமாதர்","உருவ மினையன எனவரு முருவக","வுரைசெய் தவர்தரு கலவியி னிலவிய","வுலையின் மெழுகென வுருகிய கசடனை ...... யொழியாமல்","உவகை தருகலை பலவுணர் பிறவியி","னுவரி தனிலுறு மவலனை யசடனை","உனது பரிபுர கழலிணை பெறஅருள் ...... புரிவாயே","அரவ மலிகடல் விடமமு துடனெழ","அரிய யனுநரை யிபன்முத லனைவரும்","அபய மிகவென அதையயி லிமையவ ...... னருள்பாலா","அமர்செய் நிசிசர ருடலவை துணிபட","அவனி யிடிபட அலைகடல் பொடிபட","அமரர் சிறைவிட அடலயில் நொடியினில் ...... விடுவோனே","பரவு புனமிசை யுறைதரு குறமகள்","பணைகொ ளணிமுலை முழுகுப னிருபுய","பணில சரவணை தனில்முள ரியின்வரு ...... முருகோனே","பரம குருபர எனுமுரை பரசொடு","பரவி யடியவர் துதிசெய மதிதவழ்","பழநி மலைதனி லினிதுறை யமரர்கள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses that he described women in the poets' figures, hair as cloud, breasts as lotus buds, smile as jasmine, gait as a swan, waist as a drum, face as the moon, and then melted like wax in a furnace in the union they gave; he asks that he, a fool sunk in the sea of births, be given the Lord's anklet feet. The praise tells how, when poison rose with the nectar from the churned sea and Vishnu, Brahma and Indra cried for refuge, Siva who took it fathered Murugan, whose vel shattered the asuras, who embraced the Kurava girl and was born in the lotus of Saravana, worshipped as supreme guru at Palani.","n":"The confession is partly a poet's: உருவக உரைசெய்து, having made metaphors of women's forms, the very figures the song lists. The list is the stock catalogue of Tamil love poetry, so the poet indicts his own craft. உவகை தரு கலை பல உணர் பிறவியின் உவரி: the sea of births is the place where he learned the many arts of pleasure.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","azhagu","piravi","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:16"},"p:192":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":192,"t":["வசன மிக வேற்றி ...... மறவாதே","மனதுதுய ராற்றி ...... லுழலாதே","இசைபயில்ஷ டாஷ ...... ரமதாலே","இகபரசெள பாக்ய ...... மருள்வாயே","பசுபதிசி வாக்ய ...... முணர்வோனே","பழநிமலை வீற்ற ...... ருளும்வோலா","அசுரர்கிளை வாட்டி ...... மிகவாழ","அமரர்சிறை மீட்ட ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A short song asking that the poet, not forgetting to speak Murugan's praise and not wandering in the river of the mind's sorrow, gain fortune in this world and the next through the six-syllable mantra set to song. The praise names Murugan the one who knows Siva's own word, the vel-bearer enthroned on Palani, who withered the asuras and freed the gods.","n":"இசைபயில் ஷடாக்ஷரம், the six syllables practised in music, is the Murugan mantra (by tradition சரவணபவ). இகபர செளபாக்யம் asks for good in both worlds, not release. பசுபதி சிவாக்யம் உணர்வோனே may allude to Murugan knowing the word he taught Siva; the sense of வசனமிக ஏற்றி is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:16"},"p:193":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":193,"t":["வஞ்சனை மிஞ்சிய மாய வம்பிகள்","வந்தவர் தங்களை வாதை கண்டவர்","வங்கண முந்தெரி யாம லன்புகள் ...... பலபேசி","மஞ்சமி ருந்தநு ராக விந்தைகள்","தந்தக டம்பிக ளூற லுண்டிடு","மண்டைகள் கண்டித மாய்மொ ழிந்திடு ...... முரையாலே","சஞ்சல முந்தரு மோக லண்டிகள்","இன்சொல்பு ரிந்துரு காத தொண்டிகள்","சங்கம மென்பதை யேபு ரிந்தவ ...... னயராதே","தங்களில் நெஞ்சக மேம கிழ்ந்தவர்","கொஞ்சிந டம்பயில் வேசை முண்டைகள்","தந்தசு கந்தனை யேயு கந்துடல் ...... மெலிவேனோ","கஞ்சன்வி டுஞ்சக டாசு ரன்பட","வென்றுகு ருந்தினி லேறி மங்கையர்","கண்கள்சி வந்திட வேக லந்தரு ...... முறையாலே","கண்டும கிழ்ந்தழ காயி ருந்திசை","கொண்டுவி ளங்கிய நாளி லன்பொடு","கண்குளி ருந்திரு மால்ம கிழ்ந்தருள் ...... மருகோனே","குஞ்சர வஞ்சியு மான்ம டந்தையு","மின்பமி குந்திட வேய ணைந்தருள்","குன்றென வந்தருள் நீப முந்திய ...... மணிமார்பா","கொந்தவி ழுந்தட மேநி ரம்பிய","பண்புத ருந்திரு வாவி னன்குடி","குன்றுக ளெங்கினு மேவ ளர்ந்தருள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet heaps abuse on deceitful courtesans who torment those who come, talk of love without knowing friendship, show the wonders of passion on the couch, speak harshly, never melt despite sweet words and only act at union, and asks whether he must waste his body delighting in the pleasure they give. The praise tells of Krishna killing Sakatasura sent by Kamsa and climbing the kurundham tree before the cowherd women, as Vishnu, Murugan's uncle, and of Murugan embracing Devayanai and Valli, Lord of Avinankudi and of every hill.","n":"வஞ்சனை மிஞ்சிய மாய வம்பிகள், இன்சொல் புரிந்து உருகாத தொண்டிகள், சங்கமம் என்பதையே புரிந்தவர்: the deceit is that the sweet words and the union are performance. The abuse is coarse (வேசை முண்டைகள்). குன்றுகள் எங்கினுமே வளர்ந்தருள் makes Murugan lord of all hills. Some words (கடம்பிகள், மண்டைகள், லண்டிகள்) are abusive terms whose exact sense is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:16"},"p:194":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":194,"t":["வரதா மணிநீ ...... யெனவோரில்","வருகா தெதுதா ...... னதில்வாரா","திரதா திகளால் ...... நவலோக","மிடவே கரியா ...... மிதிலேது","சரதா மறையோ ...... தயன்மாலும்","சகலா கமநூ ...... லறியாத","பரதே வதையாள் ...... தருசேயே","பழனா புரிவாழ் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A short song: if one realises that Murugan, the giver of boons, is the gem, what will not come; it does not come by mercury and the like, for the nine metals put in turn to charcoal, so what use is that? The praise names him the son of the supreme Goddess whom the Vedas, Brahma, Vishnu and all the Agamas do not know, Lord of Palani.","n":"இரதாதிகளால் நவலோகம் இடவே கரியாம் seems to reject the alchemist's art: mercury (இரதம்) and the nine metals give only charcoal, while the true gem is Murugan himself. The reading depends on how the clipped short lines divide, so it is tentative.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["siththi","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:17"},"p:195":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":195,"t":["வனிதையுடல் காய நின்று வுதிரமதி லேயு ருண்டு","வயிறில்நெடு நாள லைந்து ...... புவிமீதே","மனிதருரு வாகி வந்து அநுதினமு மேவ ளர்ந்து","வயதுபதி னாறு சென்று ...... வடிவாகிக்","கனகமுலை மாதர் தங்கள் வலையில்மிக வேயு ழன்று","கனிவதுட னேய ணைந்து ...... பொருள்தேடிக்","கனபொருளெ லாமி ழந்து மயலில்மிக வேய லைந்த","கசடனெனை யாள வுன்ற ...... னருள்தாராய்","புனமதனில் வாழு கின்ற வநிதைரகு நாதர் தந்த","புதல்வியித ழூற லுண்ட ...... புலவோனே","பொருமதனை நீறு கண்ட அரியசிவ னாரு கந்த","புதியமயி லேறு கந்த ...... வடிவேலா","பனகமணி மாம தங்கி குமரிவெகு நீலி சண்டி","பரமகலி யாணி தந்த ...... பெருவாழ்வே","பகையசுரர் மாள வென்று அமரர் சிறை மீள வென்று","பழநிமலை மீதி னின்ற ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet traces his life: heated in a woman's body, rolling in blood, long in the womb, born in human form, growing daily to sixteen, then wandering in the nets of women, embracing them, seeking wealth and losing it all, lost in infatuation, and asks Murugan to rule him by grace. The praise names him the lover of the millet-field girl given by Vishnu (Raghunatha), son of Siva who burned Kama and of the Goddess called Matangi, Kumari, Nili and Chandi, who destroyed the asuras and freed the gods, at Palani.","n":"The life runs in a single sentence from the womb (வனிதை உடல் காய நின்று உதிரமதிலே உருண்டு) to ruin, with sixteen as the age when the body takes its shape and the trouble begins. பொருள்தேடி... கனபொருள் எலாம் இழந்து: the wealth he gathered was lost in the women's nets. புனமதனில் வாழுகின்ற வநிதை ரகுநாதர் தந்த புதல்வி makes Valli Vishnu's daughter.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karu","mayal","varaivinmagalir","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:17"},"p:196":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":196,"t":["வாதம் பித்தமி லாவயி றீளைகள்","சீதம் பற்சனி சூலைம கோதர","மாசங் கட்பெரு மூலவி யாதிகள் ...... குளிர்காசம்","மாறுங் கக்கலொ டேசில நோய்பிணி","யோடுந் தத்துவ காரர்தொ ணு஡றறு","வாருஞ் சுற்றினில் வாழ்சதி காரர்கள் ...... வெகுமோகர்","சூழ்துன் சித்ரக பாயைமு வாசைகொ","டேதுஞ் சற்றுண ராமலே மாயைசெய்","சோரம் பொய்க்குடி லேசுக மாமென ...... இதின்மேவித்","தூசின் பொற்சர மோடுக லாயுல","கேழும் பிற்பட வோடிடு மூடனை","தூவஞ் சுத்தடி யாரடி சேரநி ...... னருள்தாராய்","தீதந் தித்திமி தீதக தோதிமி","டுடுண் டுட்டுடு டூடுடு டூடுடு","சேசெஞ் செக்கெண தோதக தீகுட ...... வெனபேரி","சேடன் சொக்கிட வேலைக டாகமெ","லாமஞ் சுற்றிட வேயசு ரார்கிரி","தீவும் பொட்டெழ வேயனல் வேல்விடு ...... மயில்வீரா","வேதன் பொற்சிர மீதுக டாவிந","லீசன் சற்குரு வாயவர் காதினில்","மேவும் பற்றிலர் பேறரு ளோதிய ...... முருகோனே","வேஷங் கட்டிபி னேகிம காவளி","மாலின் பித்துற வாகிவி ணோர்பணி","வீரங் கொட்பழ னாபுரி மேவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet lists the body's diseases, wind, bile, phlegm, colic, dropsy, eye disease, piles, cough and vomiting, and the ninety-six tattvas and the cheats who live around a man, and confesses that, with the three cravings, he took this thieving, false hut for happiness and ran in fine cloth and gold chains as if to outrun the seven worlds; he asks to reach the feet of the Lord's pure devotees. The praise sounds the battle drums as the vel shakes Adisesha, the seas and the asura hill, tells how Murugan struck Brahma's golden head and as true guru spoke in Siva's ear the gain of the detached, and how he went in disguise after Valli, at Palani.","n":"பொய்க்குடில், the false hut, is the body, and the list of diseases is the evidence. தத்துவகாரர் தொண்ணூற்றறுவர் treats the ninety-six tattvas as agents who crowd the body. The teaching myth is told in full except for the word Om: வேதன் பொற்சிரம் மீது கடாவி, நல் ஈசன் சற்குருவாய் அவர் காதினில் மேவும் பற்றிலர் பேறு அருள் ஓதிய. The opening lines on the drums (தீதந் தித்திமி, டுடுண் டுட்டுடு) are rhythm, not words.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["noy","yaakkai","thaththuvam","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:17"},"p:197":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":197,"t":["வார ணந்தனை நேரான மாமுலை","மீத ணிந்திடு பூணார மாரொளி","வால சந்திர னேராக மாமுக ...... மெழில்கூர","வார ணங்கிடு சேலான நீள்விழி","யோலை தங்கிய வார்காது வாவிட","வான இன்சுதை மோலான வாயித ...... ழமுதூறத்","தோர ணஞ்செறி தார்வாழை யேய்தொடை","மீதில் நின்றிடை நூல்போலு லாவியெ","தோகை யென்றிட வாகாக வூரன ...... நடைமானார்","தோத கந்தனை மாமாயை யேவடி","வாக நின்றதெ னாஆய வோர்வது","தோணி டும்படி நாயேனுள் நீயருள் ...... தருவாயே","கார ணந்தனை யோராநி சாசரர்","தாம டங்கலு மீறாக வானவர்","காவ லிந்திர னாடாள வேயயில் ...... விடும்வீரா","கார்வி டந்தனை யூணாக வானவர்","வாழ்த ரும்படி மேனாளி லேமிசை","காள கண்டம காதேவ னார்தரு ...... முருகோனே","ஆர ணன்றனை வாதாடி யோருரை","ஓது கின்றென வாராதெ னாவவ","னாண வங்கெட வேகாவ லாமதி ...... விடும்வேலா","ஆத வன்கதி ரோவாது லாவிய","கோபு ரங்கிளர் மாமாது மேவிய","ஆவி னன்குடி யோனேசு ராதிபர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes a woman, breasts like an elephant's brow under necklaces, face like the young moon, long carp eyes, nectar lips, plantain thighs, thread-thin waist, the gait of a peacock and swan, and asks that the understanding arise in him that this deceit is great maya itself standing in form. The praise names the vel that destroyed the night-walkers so Indra could rule, the son of Siva who drank the poison for the gods, the one who argued with Brahma, asked him for one word he could not give, and broke his pride by confinement, at towered Avinankudi.","n":"தோதகந்தனை மாமாயையே வடிவாக நின்றது: the plea asks not to escape the woman but to see her charm as maya embodied, a doctrinal turn on the usual confession. The Brahma story (ஆரணன்றனை வாதாடி ஓர் உரை ஓதுகின்றென வாராது) is told without naming Om.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maayai","kaamam","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:17"},"p:198":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":198,"t":["விதமி சைந்தினி தாமலர் மாலைகள்","குழல ணிந்தநு ராகமு மேசொலி","விதர ணஞ்சொலி வீறுக ளேசொலி ...... யழகாக","விரிகு ரும்பைக ளாமென வீறிய","கனக சம்ப்ரம மேருவ தாமதி","விரக மொங்கிய மாமுலை யாலெதி ...... ரமர்நாடி","இதமி சைந்தன மாமென வேயின","நடைந டந்தனர் வீதியி லேவர","எவர்க ளுஞ்சித மால்கொளு மாதர்கண் ...... வலையாலே","எனது சிந்தையும் வாடிவி டாவகை","அருள்பு ரிந்தழ காகிய தாமரை","இருப தங்களி னாலெனை யாள்வது ...... மொருநாளே","மதமி சைந்தெதி ரேபொரு சூரனை","யுடலி ரண்டுகு றாய்விழ வேசின","வடிவு தங்கிய வேலினை யேவிய ...... அதிதீரா","மதுர இன்சொலி மாதுமை நாரணி","கவுரி யம்பிகை யாமளை பார்வதி","மவுந சுந்தரி காரணி யோகினி ...... சிறுவோனே","பதமி சைந்தெழு லோகமு மேவலம்","நொடியில் வந்திடு மாமயில் மீதொரு","பவனி வந்தக்ரு பாகர சேவக ...... விறல்வீரா","பருதி யின்ப்ரபை கோடிய தாமெனும்","வடிவு கொண்டருள் காசியின் மீறிய","பழநி யங்கிரி மீதினில் மேவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women with flower-garlanded hair who speak of love, generosity and their own pride, with golden, Meru-like breasts that seek battle, walking swan-like in the street and deluding all with the net of their eyes, and asks that his mind not wither but that one day Murugan rule him by his two lotus feet. The praise names the vel that split Sura in two, the son of Uma called Narani, Gauri, Syamala, Parvati, Mauna Sundari and Yogini, who circles the seven worlds in an instant on the peacock, at Palani, said to surpass Kasi, shining like ten million suns.","n":"எனை யாள்வதும் ஒருநாளே: the plea is put as a hope for one day. காசியின் மீறிய பழநி, Palani above Kasi, claims for the shrine more than the holiest pilgrim city.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kan","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:17"},"p:199":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":199,"t":["விரைமருவு மலர ணிந்த கரியபுரி குழல்ச ரிந்து","விழவதன மதிவி ளங்க ...... அதிமோக","விழிபுரள முலைகு லுங்க மொழிகுழற அணைபு குந்து","விரகமயல் புரியு மின்ப ...... மடவார்பால்","இரவுபக லணுகி நெஞ்ச மறிவழிய வுருகு மந்த","இருளகல வுனது தண்டை ...... யணிபாதம்","எனதுதலை மிசைய ணிந்து அழுதழுது னருள்வி ரும்பி","யினியபுகழ் தனைவி ளம்ப ...... அருள்தாராய்","அரவில்விழி துயில்மு குந்த னலர்கமல மலர்ம டந்தை","அழகினொடு தழுவு கொண்டல் ...... மருகோனே","அடலசுர ருடல்பி ளந்து நிணமதனில் முழுகி யண்ட","அமரர்சிறை விடுப்ர சண்ட ...... வடிவேலா","பரவைவரு விடம ருந்து மிடறுடைய கடவுள் கங்கை","படர்சடையர் விடைய ரன்ப ...... ருளமேவும்","பரமரரு ளியக டம்ப முருகஅறு முகவ கந்த","பழநிமலை தனில மர்ந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women in the bed of desire, hair falling, eyes rolling, words faltering, whom he sought night and day until his heart's knowledge was gone, and asks that, the darkness lifted, he wear Murugan's anklet feet on his head, weep and weep for his grace and proclaim his sweet praise. The praise names him nephew of Vishnu sleeping on the serpent beside Lakshmi, the vel that plunged into the asuras' fat and freed the gods, and son of Siva who drank the poison and bears the Ganga, at Palani.","n":"அழுதழுது உன் அருள் விரும்பி: the remedy is tears, devotion shown in the body that desire had seized. The feet are asked for on the head (எனது தலைமிசை அணிந்து), as a crown.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","thiruvadi","paththi","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:17"},"p:200":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":200,"t":["வேயி சைந்தெழு தோள்கள் தங்கிய","மாதர் கொங்கையி லேமு யங்கிட","வீணி லுஞ்சில பாத கஞ்செய ...... அவமேதான்","வீறு கொண்டுட னேவ ருந்தியு","மேயு லைந்தவ மேதி ரிந்துள","மேக வன்றறி வேக லங்கிட ...... வெகுதூரம்","போய லைந்துழ லாகி நொந்துபின்","வாடி நைந்தென தாவி வெம்பியெ","பூத லந்தனி லேம யங்கிய ...... மதிபோகப்","போது கங்கையி னீர்சொ ரிந்திரு","பாத பங்கய மேவ ணங்கியெ","பூசை யுஞ்சில வேபு ரிந்திட ...... அருள்வாயே","தீயி சைந்தெழ வேயி லங்கையில்","ராவ ணன்சிர மேய ரிந்தவர்","சேனை யுஞ்செல மாள வென்றவன் ...... மருகோனே","தேச மெங்கணு மேபு ரந்திடு","சூர்ம டிந்திட வேலின் வென்றவ","தேவர் தம்பதி யாள அன்புசெய் ...... திடுவோனே","ஆயி சுந்தரி நீலி பிங்கலை","போக அந்தரி சூலி குண்டலி","ஆதி யம்பிகை வேத தந்திரி ...... யிடமாகும்","ஆல முண்டர னாரி றைஞ்சவொர்","போத கந்தனை யேயு கந்தருள்","ஆவி னன்குடி மீதி லங்கிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses that he embraced women with bamboo-like shoulders, did sins in vain, wandered far, his mind scattered and his life withered, and asks that, his delusion gone, he pour Ganges water on Murugan's two lotus feet, bow to them and perform some puja. The praise names Rama who burned Lanka and cut off Ravana's heads as his uncle, the vel that killed Sura so the gods could rule, the Goddess as Sundari, Nili, Kundali and Veda Tantri on Siva's left, and Murugan who gladly gave a teaching when Siva, who drank the poison, bowed to him, at Avinankudi.","n":"The plea is modest and ritual: some puja (பூசையும் சிலவே), with Ganges water poured on the feet. ஆலமுண்ட அரனார் இறைஞ்ச ஒர் போதகந்தனையே உகந்து அருள் is the teaching of Siva, without naming Om.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","kaamam","guru","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:17"},"p:201":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":201,"t":["அவாமரு வினாவசு தைகாணும டவாரெனு","மவார்கனலில் வாழ்வென் ...... றுணராதே","அராநுக ரவாதையு றுதேரேக திநாடும","றிவாகியுள மால்கொண் டதனாலே","சிவாயவெ னுநாமமொ ருகாலுநி னையாததி","மிராகரனை வாவென் ...... றருள்வாயே","திரோதம லமாறும டியார்கள ருமாதவர்","தியானமுறு பாதந் ...... தருவாயே","உவாவினி யகானுவி னிலாவும யில்வாகன","முலாசமுட னேறுங் ...... கழலோனே","உலாவுத யபாநுச தகோடியு ருவானவொ","ளிவாகுமயில் வேலங் ...... கையிலோனே","துவாதச புயாசல ஷடாநந வராசிவ","சுதாஎயினர் மானன் ...... புடையோனே","சுராதிப திமாலய னுமாலொடு சலாமிடு","சுவாமிமலை வாழும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses that, not seeing that a life spent in the fire of craving for women is ruin, he grew deluded and never once thought of the name 'Sivaya', and asks Murugan to call him in his darkness and give him the feet that great ascetics meditate on, where the veiling impurity ends. The praise names the twelve-armed, six-faced son of Siva who rides the peacock with the vel in hand, the lover of the hunters' doe, and the lord of Swamimalai to whom Indra, Brahma and Vishnu bow.","n":"The first word, அவா (craving), sets the theme, and every line opens on a long syllable of the same pattern (அவா, அரா, சிவா, உவா, உலா, துவா, சுரா). திரோத மலம் is the veiling (tirodhana) counted among the impurities in Saiva doctrine. The opening words are hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaa","malam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:18"},"p:202":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":202,"t":["ஆனனமு கந்து தோளோடு தோளிணைக லந்து பாலன","ஆரமுது கண்டு தேனென ...... இத்ழுறல்","ஆதரவி னுண்டு வேல்விழி பூசலிட நன்று காணென","ஆனையுர மெங்கு மோதிட ...... அபிராம","மானனைய மங்கை மார்மடு நாபியில்வி ழுந்து கீடமில்","மாயுமனு வின்ப வாசைய ...... தறவேயுன்","வாரிஜப தங்கள் நாயடி யேன்முடிபு னைந்து போதக","வாசகம்வ ழங்கி யாள்வது ...... மொருநாளே","ஈனவதி பஞ்ச பாதக தானவர்ப்ர சண்ட சேனைகள்","ஈடழிய வென்று வானவர் ...... குலசேனை","ஏவல்கொளு மிந்த்ர லோகவ சீகரவ லங்க்ரு தாகர","ராசதம றிந்த கோமள ...... வடிவோனே","சோனைசொரி குன்ற வேடுவர் பேதைபயில் கின்ற ஆறிரு","தோளுடைய கந்த னேவய ...... லியில்வாழ்வே","சூளிகையு யர்ந்த கோபுர மாளிகைபொ னிஞ்சி சூழ்தரு","ஸ்வாமிமலை நின்று லாவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes embracing women and drinking their lips' nectar, and asks that his craving for the pleasure of sinking like a worm into their navel cease, and that one day Murugan set his lotus feet as a crown on this dog-like servant's head and rule him with a word of teaching. The praise names the conqueror of the demon armies who commands the gods' host, twelve-armed Kanda whom the hunter girl of the rain-wet hill loves, the life of Vayalur, and the lord of Swamimalai of towers and golden walls.","n":"கீடம்: the lover as a worm dying in a pit. The feet set on the head and a word of instruction (போதக வாசகம்) are the marks of a guru's initiation. Vayalur is named inside a Swamimalai song, as in 203.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","punarchi","thiruvadi","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:18"},"p:203":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":203,"t":["ஆனாத பிருதிவிப் பாச நிகளமும்","மாமாய விருளுமற் றேகி பவமென","வாகாச பரமசிற் சோதி பரையைய ...... டைந்துளாமே","ஆறாறி னதிகமக் க்ராய மநுதினம்","யோகீச ரெவருமெட் டாத பரதுரி","யாதீத மகளமெப் போது முதயம ...... நந்தமோகம்","வானாதி சகலவிஸ்த் தார விபவரம்","லோகாதி முடிவுமெய்ப் போத மலரயன்","மாலீச ரெனுமவற் கேது விபுலம ...... சங்கையால்நீள்","மாளாத தனிசமுற் றாய தரியநி","ராதார முலைவில்சற் சோதி நிருபமு","மாறாத சுகவெளத் தாணு வுடனினி ...... தென்றுசேர்வேன்","நானாவி தகருவிச் சேனை வகைவகை","சூழ்போது பிரபலச் சூரர் கொடுநெடு","நாவாய்செல் கடலடைத் தேறி நிலைமையி ...... லங்கைசாய","நாலாறு மணிமுடிப் பாவி தனையடு","சீராமன் மருகமைக் காவில் பரிமள","நாவீசு வயலியக் கீசர் குமரக ...... டம்பவேலா","கானாளு மெயனர்தற் சாதி வளர்குற","மானொடு மகிழ்கருத் தாகி மருடரு","காதாடு முனதுகட் பாண மெனதுடை ...... நெஞ்சுபாய்தல்","காணாது மமதைவிட் டாவி யுயவருள்","பாராயெ னுரைவெகுப் ப்஡ணதி யிளையவ","காவேரி வடகரைச் சாமி மலையுறை ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"A doctrinal plea: the poet asks when, the fetter of earth and the darkness of great maya gone, he will reach the supreme light of consciousness, vast as space, which lies beyond the thirty-six tattvas and beyond turiya and which no master of yoga attains, and join with joy the unchanging, supportless Sthanu of bliss. The praise names Rama who bridged the sea and killed the ten-headed sinner as uncle, the son of Vayalur's Agnisvara, and the lover of the hunter girl, and asks for grace that ends the sense of 'mine'; the shrine is Swamimalai on the Kaveri's north bank.","n":"ஆறாறின் அதிகம், beyond the thirty-six; பர துரியாதீதம், beyond turiya; பரை, the supreme Shakti; தாணு, Siva as the immovable. The whole plea is in Siddhanta's technical words, close to Tirumular's. The lines on the hunter girl and the eye-arrows that pierce the heart may speak of Murugan's love for Valli or of the poet's own heart; they are hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["thaththuvam","avaththai","maayai","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:18"},"p:204":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":204,"t":["இராவினிருள் போலும் பராவுகுழ லாலும்","இராமசர மாகும் ...... விழியாலும்","இராகமொழி யாலும் பொறாதமுலை யாலும்","இராதஇடை யாலும் ...... இளைஞோர்நெஞ்","சராவியிரு போதும் பராவிவிழ வேவந்","தடாதவிலை கூறும் ...... மடவாரன்","படாமலடி யேனுஞ் சுவாமியடி தேடும்","அநாதிமொழி ஞானந் ...... தருவாயே","குராவினிழல் மேவுங் குமாரனென நாளுங்","குலாவியினி தோதன் ...... பினர்வாழ்வே","குணாலமிடு சூரன் பணாமுடிக டோ ருங்","குடாவியிட வேலங் ...... கெறிவோனே","துராலுமிக தீமுன் பிராதவகை போலுந்","தொடாமல்வினை யோடும் ...... படிநூறுஞ்","சுபானமுறு ஞானந் தபோதனர்கள் சேருஞ்","சுவாமிமலை வாழும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet lists women whose dark hair, eyes like Rama's arrows, musical speech, heavy breasts and vanishing waists wear down young men's hearts morning and evening while they name a price no one can meet, and asks that he not suffer in their love but be given the beginningless word of jnana that seeks the Lord's feet. The praise names Murugan whom devotees hail daily as Kumara in the shade of the kura tree, the vel that split Sura's hooded heads, and Swamimalai, where ascetics gather before whose good jnana deeds flee like straw before fire.","n":"அடாத விலை கூறும்: naming an impossible price is what marks these women as courtesans. துரால் மிக தீ முன்: deeds burned like straw before fire, a common figure for knowledge destroying karma. அநாதி மொழி, 'the beginningless word', may mean the Veda or Om; the text does not say.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","gnaanam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:18"},"p:205":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":205,"t":["இருவினை புனைந்து ஞான விழிமுனை திறந்து நோயி","னிருவினை யிடைந்து போக ...... மல்முட","விருளற விளங்கி யாறு முகமொடு கலந்து பேத","மிலையென இரண்டு பேரு ...... மழகான","பரிமள சுகந்த வீத மயமென மகிழ்ந்து தேவர்","பணியவிண் மடந்தை பாத ...... மலர்தூவப்","பரிவுகொ டநந்த கோடி முநிவர்கள் புகழ்ந்து பாட","பருமயி லுடன்கு லாவி ...... வரவேணும்","அரியய னறிந்தி டாத அடியிணை சிவந்த பாதம்","அடியென விளங்கி யாடு ...... நடராஜன்","அழலுறு மிரும்பின் மேனி மகிழ்மர கதம்பெ ணாகம்","அயலணி சிவன்பு ராரி ...... யருள்சேயே","மருவலர் கள்திண்ப ணார முடியுடல் நடுங்க ஆவி","மறலியுண வென்ற வேலை ...... யுடையோனே","வளைகுல மலங்கு காவி ரியின்வட புறஞ்சு வாமி","மலைமிசை விளங்கு தேவர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks Murugan to come so that, the two deeds broken, the eye of jnana opened at its tip and the dark of impurity cleared, he and the six faces may mingle and the two become one without difference, while the gods bow, the women of heaven strew flowers and countless sages sing. The praise names Nataraja, whose red feet Vishnu and Brahma could not find, Siva of the three cities, whose body is like red-hot iron with the emerald woman at his side, and the vel that gave the enemies' lives to Death, at Swamimalai on the Kaveri's north bank.","n":"பேதம் இலை என இரண்டு பேரும்: the goal is union without difference with the Lord, not only escape. அழலுறும் இரும்பின் மேனி ... மரகதம் பெண் ஆகம் அயல்: red Siva with green Uma at his side, the half-woman form. The first lines are partly garbled.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","malam","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:18"},"p:207":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":207,"t":["ஒருவரையு மொருவரறி யாம லுந்திரிந்","திருவினையி னிடர்கலியொ டாடி நொந்துநொந்","துலையிலிடு மெழுகதென வாடி முன்செய்வஞ் ...... சனையாலே","ஒளிபெறவெ யெழுபுமர பாவை துன்றிடுங்","கயிறுவித மெனமருவி யாடி விண்பறிந்","தொளிருமின லுருவதென வோடி யங்கம்வெந் ...... திடுவேனைக்","கருதியொரு பரமபொரு ளீது என்றுஎன்","செவியிணையி னருளியுரு வாகி வந்தஎன்","கருவினையொ டருமலமு நீறு கண்டுதண் ...... டருமாமென்","கருணைபொழி கமலுமுக மாறு மிந்துளந்","தொடைமகுட முடியுமொளிர் நூபு ரஞ்சரண்","கலகலென மயிலின்மிசை யேறி வந்துகந் ...... தெனையாள்வாய்","திரிபுரமு மதனுடலு நீறு கண்டவன்","தருணமழ விடையனட ராஜ னெங்கணுந்","திகழருண கிரிசொருப னாதி யந்தமங் ...... கறியாத","சிவயநம நமசிவய கார ணன்சுரந்","தமுதமதை யருளியெமை யாளு மெந்தைதன்","திருவுருவின் மகிழெனது தாய்ப யந்திடும் ...... புதல்வோனே","குருகுகொடி யுடன்மயிலி லேறி மந்தரம்","புவனகிரி சுழலமறை யாயி ரங்களுங்","குமரகுரு வெனவலிய சேட னஞ்சவந் ...... திடுவோனே","குறமகளி னிடைதுவள பாத செஞ்சிலம்","பொலியவொரு சசிமகளொ டேக லந்துதிண்","குருமலையின் மருவுகுரு நாத வும்பர்தம் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says he roamed, no one knowing anyone, tormented in the troubles of his two deeds, melting like wax in a furnace, dancing like a puppet on strings and vanishing like lightning, and asks Murugan, who came in form, spoke in his ears 'this is the supreme thing' and burned his karma and impurity to ash, to come on the peacock with his six compassionate faces and ringing anklets and rule him. The praise names Siva who burned the three cities and Kama, the dancer on the young bull, Arunagiri in form, the cause in Sivayanama, and calls Murugan the son of Uma, the poet's mother, the Kumaraguru the thousand Vedas hail, who joined Valli and Devayanai on Gurumalai.","n":"என் செவியிணையின் அருளி உருவாகி வந்த: the song speaks of an initiation already given, the Lord coming in form and teaching in the poet's ears. Three images of a life driven by deeds: wax, a string-puppet, lightning. Siva is called அருணகிரி சொருபன், the form of Arunachala, which is also the poet's name.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["guru","vinai","malam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:18"},"p:208":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":208,"t":["கடாவினிடை வீரங் கெடாமலினி தேறுங்","கடாவினிக ராகுஞ் ...... சமனாருங்","கடாவிவிடு தூதன் கெடாதவழி போலுங்","கனாவில்விளை யாடுங் ...... கதைபோலும்","இடாதுபல தேடுங் கிராதர்பொருள் போலிங்","கிராமலுயிர் கோலிங் ...... கிதமாகும்","இதாமெனிரு போதுஞ் சதாவின்மொழி யாலின்","றியானுமுனை யோதும் ...... படிபாராய்","விடாதுநட நாளும் பிடாரியுட னாடும்","வியாகரண ஈசன் ...... பெருவாழ்வே","விகாரமுறு சூரன் பகாரமுயிர் வாழ்வும்","விநாசமுற வேலங் ...... கெறிவோனே","தொடாதுநெடு தூரந் தடாதுமிக வோடுஞ்","சுவாசமது தானைம் ...... புலனோடுஞ்","சுபானமுறு ஞானந் தபோதனர்கள் சேருஞ்","சுவாமிமலை வாழும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet sets out how uncertain life is: Yama on his buffalo, the messenger he sends whose path never fails, a story played in a dream, the wealth hunters hoard without giving; seeing that life does not stay, he asks Murugan to see that he too may chant him with sweet words morning and evening, today. The praise names Siva, lord of grammar, who dances daily with Pidari, the vel that ended Sura, and Swamimalai where ascetics in good jnana gather.","n":"கிராதர் பொருள், wealth hunters gather and never give, stands beside the dream as a figure for what will not stay with you. வியாகரண ஈசன்: Siva as lord of grammar. The closing lines on breath and the five senses may describe the ascetics' restraint; the song shares its last line with 204.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kootru","nilaiyaamai","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:18"},"p:209":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":209,"t":["கடிமா மலர்க்கு ளின்ப முளவேரி கக்கு நண்பு","தருமா கடப்ப மைந்த ...... தெடைமாலை","கனமேரு வொத்தி டும்ப னிருமாபு யத்த ணிந்த","கருணா கரப்ர சண்ட ...... கதிர்வேலா","வடிவார் குறத்தி தன்பொ னடிமீது நித்த முந்தண்","முடியான துற்று கந்து ...... பணிவோனே","வளவாய்மை சொற்ப்ர பந்த முளகீர னுக்கு கந்து","மலர்வாயி லக்க ணங்க ...... ளியல்போதி","அடிமோனை சொற்கி ணங்க வுலகாமு வப்ப என்று","னருளால ளிக்கு கந்த ...... பெரியோனே","அடியேனு ரைத்த புன்சொ லதுமீது நித்த முந்த","ணருளே தழைத்து கந்து ...... வரவேணும்","செடிநேரு டற்கு டம்பை தனின்மேவி யுற்றி டிந்த","படிதான லக்க ணிங்க ...... ணுறலாமோ","திறமாத வர்க்க னிந்து னிருபாத பத்ம முய்ந்த","திருவேர கத்த மர்ந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"Mostly praise: Murugan wears the kadamba garland on twelve arms like Meru, bows his crown daily at the hunter girl's feet, delighted in Keeran's poems of true speech and taught him grammar, and by grace gives verse whose alliteration fits; the poet asks that grace grow daily on the mean words he speaks, and asks whether he must suffer here, lodged in this body like a nest in a bush. The shrine is Tiruverakam.","n":"கீரன் is Nakkirar; the song makes Murugan his teacher of grammar (இலக்கணங்கள் இயல்பு ஓதி). அடிமோனை: the poet credits even his alliteration to grace. Murugan bowing his crown at Valli's feet reverses the usual order of worship.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thamizh","pugazh","thiruvarul","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:18"},"p:210":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":210,"t":["கதிரவனெ ழுந்து லாவு திசையளவு கண்டு மோது","கடலளவு கண்டு மாய ...... மருளாலே","கணபணபு யங்க ராஜன் முடியளவு கண்டு தாள்கள்","கவினறந டந்து தேயும் ...... வகையேபோய்","இதமிதமி தென்று நாளு மருகருகி ருந்து கூடு","மிடமிடமி தென்று சோர்வு ...... படையாதே","இசையொடுபு கழந்த போது நழுவியப்ர சண்டர் வாச","லிரவுபகல் சென்று வாடி ...... யுழல்வேனோ","மதுகரமி டைந்து வேரி தருநறவ முண்டு பூக","மலர்வளநி றைந்த பாளை ...... மல்ருடே","வகைவகையெ ழுந்த சாம வதிமறைவி யந்து பாட","மதிநிழலி டுஞ்சு வாமி ...... மலைவாழ்வே","அதிரவரு சண்ட வாயு வெனவருக ருங்க லாப","அணிமயில்வி ரும்பி யேறு ...... மிளையோனே","அடைவொடுல கங்கள் யாவு முதவிநிலை கண்ட பாவை","அருள்புதல்வ அண்ட ராஜர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether he must wander, deluded, to the edges of the directions where the sun rises, the shores of the sea and the crown of the serpent king, wearing out his feet, never tiring of saying 'this is the place to sit near', and, when the powerful men he praised with music slip away, go to their gates night and day and wilt. The praise pictures bees in the areca blossoms and Sama singers at Swamimalai where the moon casts its shade, Murugan riding the peacock like a storm wind, and the son of the Mother who set all worlds in place.","n":"இசையொடு புகழ்ந்த போது நழுவிய ப்ரசண்டர் வாசல்: the gates of patrons who slip away once they are praised in song, the same complaint as 220. The first lines may also describe wandering for wealth in general.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iravu","avaa"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:18"},"p:211":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":211,"t":["கறைபடுமு டம்பி ராதெனக்","கருதுதலொ ழிந்து வாயுவைக்","கருமவச னங்க ளால்மறித் ...... தனலு஡திக்","கவலைபடு கின்ற யோககற்","பனைமருவு சிந்தை போய்விடக்","கலகமிடு மஞ்சும் வேரறச் ...... செயல்மாளக்","குறைவறநி றைந்த மோனநிர்க்","குணமதுபொ ருந்தி வீடுறக்","குருமலைவி ளங்கு ஞானசற் ...... குருநாதா","குமரசர ணென்று கூதளப்","புதுமலர்சொ ரிந்து கோமளப்","பதயுகள புண்ட ஡ணகமுற் ...... றுணர்வேனோ","சிறைதளைவி ளங்கு பேர்முடிப்","புயலுடன டங்க வேபிழைத்","திமையவர்கள் தங்க ளூர்புகச் ...... சமராடித்","திமிரமிகு சிந்து வாய்விடச்","சிகரிகளும் வெந்து நீறெழத்","திகிரிகொள நந்த சூடிகைத் ...... திருமாலும்","பிறைமவுலி மைந்த கோவெனப்","பிரமனைமு னிந்து காவலிட்","டொருநொடியில் மண்டு சூரனைப் ...... பொருதேறிப்","பெருகுமத கும்ப லாளிதக்","கரியெனப்ர சண்ட வாரணப்","பிடிதனைம ணந்த சேவகப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks that the mind given to the anxious fancies of yoga go away, the yoga that refuses to think this stained body will not last, turns back the breath with ritual words and fans the inner fire; that the quarrelling five senses be uprooted and his own action end, so that he enters the full silence beyond qualities and reaches release; and he asks whether he will know, as the true guru of Gurumalai, the lotus feet on which he strews fresh flowers crying 'Kumara, refuge'. The praise tells of the war in which Murugan freed the gods to their city, burned the peaks and dried the sea, punished Brahma and kept him under guard, and married the elephant-raised Devayanai.","n":"The participles (கருதுதல் ஒழிந்து, வாயுவை மறித்து, அனல் ஊதி, கவலைபடுகின்ற) appear to describe the yoga the poet wants to be rid of: a yoga of breath and fire aimed at keeping the body, set against the silence (மோனம்) beyond the gunas. Brahma's punishment (பிரமனை முனிந்து காவலிட்டு) is told without naming Om. Two corrupt glyphs.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["yogam","praanan","mounam","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:18"},"p:212":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":212,"t":["காமியத் தழுந்தி ...... யிளையாதே","காலர்கைப் படிந்து ...... மடியாதே","ஓமெழுத்தி லன்பு ...... மிகவூறி","ஓவியத்தி லந்த ...... மருள்வாயே","தூமமேய்க் கணிந்த ...... சுகலீலா","சூரனைக் கடிந்த ...... கதிர்வேலா","ஏமவெற் புயர்ந்த ...... மயில்வீரா","ஏரகத் தமர்ந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A short song: the poet asks not to waste away in desires nor die in the hands of Death's men, but, with love for the letter Om welling up, to be given the end 'in the painting'. The praise names the vel that drove off Sura, the hero on a peacock high as the golden mountain, and the lord at Erakam.","n":"ஓவியத்தில் அந்தம் is obscure: it may be the stillness of a painted figure, an image for absorption. காமியம் may mean desire or rites done for desired ends. தூமமேய்க் கணிந்த in the praise is hard to read.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piranavam","kootru","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:18"},"p:213":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":213,"t":["குமரகுரு பரமுருக குகனே குறச்சிறுமி","கணவசர வணநிருதர் கலகா பிறைச்சடையர்","குருவெனந லுரையுதவு மயிலா எனத்தினமு ...... முருகாதே","குயில்மொழிநன் மடவியர்கள் விழியா லுருக்குபவர்","தெருவிலத வரதமன மெனவே நடப்பர்நகை","கொளுமவர்க ளுடைமைமன முடனே பறிப்பவர்க ...... ளனைவோரும்","தமதுவச முறவசிய முகமே மினுக்கியர்கள்","முலையிலுறு துகில்சரிய நடுவீதி நிற்பவர்கள்","தனமிலியர் மனமுறிய நழுவா வுழப்பியர்கண் ...... வலையாலே","சதிசெய்தவ ரவர்மகிழ அணைமீ துருக்கியர்கள்","வசமொழுகி யவரடிமை யெனமாத ரிட்டதொழில்","தனிலுழலு மசடனையு னடியே வழுத்தஅருள் ...... தருவாயே","சமரமொடு மசுரர்படை களமீ தெதிர்த்தபொழு","தொருநொடியி லவர்கள்படை கெடவே லெடுத்தவனி","தனில்நிருதர் சிரமுருள ரணதூள் படுத்திவிடு ...... செருமீதே","தவனமொடு மலகைநட மிடவீர பத்திரர்க","ளதிரநிண மொடுகுருதி குடிகாளி கொக்கரிசெய்","தசையுணவு தனின்மகிழ விடுபேய் நிரைத்திரள்கள் ...... பலகோடி","திமிதமிட நரிகொடிகள் கழுகா டரத்தவெறி","வயிரவர்கள் சுழலவொரு தனியா யுதத்தைவிடு","திமிரதிந கரஅமரர் பதிவாழ்வு பெற்றுலவு ...... முருகோனே","திருமருவு புயனயனொ டயிரா வதக்குரிசி","லடிபரவு பழநிமலை கதிர்காம முற்றுவளர்","சிவசமய அறுமுகவ திருவேர கத்திலுறை ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet laments that he did not melt daily chanting Murugan's names, but wandered after cuckoo-voiced women who melt men with their eyes, walk the street as if lost in thought, seize their lovers' belongings, stand mid-street with garments slipping, net the penniless with their eyes and, having taken all, made him their slave doing whatever they bid; he asks for grace to praise the feet. The praise is a long battle with Virabhadras, Kali drinking blood, ghosts, jackals and vultures, and names Murugan of the Saiva faith, of Palani and Kathirkamam, dwelling at Tiruverakam.","n":"அவர் அடிமை என மாதர் இட்ட தொழில் தனில் உழலும் அசடன்: the poet calls himself the women's slave, the bondage the other songs turn into service of the Lord. சிவசமய அறுமுக places Murugan within the Saiva faith. The battle is in drum syllables (திமிதமிட).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","adimai","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:18"},"p:214":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":214,"t":["குமர குருபர முருக சரவண","குகசண் முககரி ...... பிறகான","குழக சிவசுத சிவய நமவென","குரவ னருள்குரு ...... மணியேயென்","றமுத இமையவர் திமிர்த மிடுகட","லதென அநுதின ...... முனையோதும்","அமலை அடியவர் கொடிய வினைகொடு","மபய மிடுகுர ...... லறியாயோ","திமிர எழுகட லுலக முறிபட","திசைகள் பொடிபட ...... வருசூரர்","சிகர முடியுடல் புவியில் விழவுயிர்","திறைகொ டமர்பொரு ...... மயில்வீரா","நமனை யுயிர்கொளு மழலி னிணைகழல்","நதிகொள் சடையினர் ...... குருநாதா","நளின குருமலை மருவி யமர்தரு","நவிலு மறைபுகழ் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether Murugan does not hear the cry for refuge of his devotees, crushed by cruel deeds, who chant him daily, 'Kumara, Guruparan, Muruga, Saravana, Guha, Shanmukha, younger brother of the elephant one, son of Siva, jewel of a guru', as gods once cried out at the sea. The praise names the peacock hero who felled the Suras, the guru of Siva whose feet took the life of Death and whose hair holds the river, and the lord of Gurumalai praised by the Vedas.","n":"The plea is a litany of names, as if the song were itself the devotees' cry. நமனை உயிர்கொளும் இணைகழல்: Siva's feet that killed Yama, though the text names only the deed. The simile of the gods at the sea (அமுத இமையவர் திமிர்தம் இடு கடல்) is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["adiyaar","vinai","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:18"},"p:215":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":215,"t":["கோமள வெற்பினை யொத்தத னத்தியர்","காமனை யொப்பவர் சித்தமு ருக்கிகள்","கோவையி தழ்க்கனி நித்தமும் விற்பவர் ...... மயில்காடை","கோகில நற்புற வத்தொடு குக்குட","ஆரணி யப்புள்வ கைக்குரல் கற்றிகல்","கோலவி ழிக்கடை யிட்டும ருட்டிகள் ...... விரகாலே","தூமம லர்ப்பளி மெத்தைப டுப்பவர்","யாரையு மெத்திம னைக்குள ழைப்பவர்","சோலைவ னக்கிளி யொத்தமொ ழிச்சியர் ...... நெறிகூடா","தூசுநெ கிழ்த்தரை சுற்றியு டுப்பவர்","காசுப றிக்கம றித்துமு யக்கிகள்","தோதக வித்தைப டித்துந டிப்பவ ...... ருறவாமோ","மாமர மொத்துவ ரிக்குணெ ருக்கிய","சூரனை வெட்டிநி ணக்குட லைக்கொடி","வாரண மெச்சஅ ளித்தஅ யிற்குக ...... கதிர்காம","மாமலை யிற்பழ நிப்பதி யிற்றனி","மாகிரி யிற்றணி கைக்கிரி யிற்பர","மாகிரி யிற்றிரை சுற்றிவ ளைத்திடும் ...... அலைவாயில்","ஏமவெ யிற்பல வெற்பினி னற்பதி","னாலுல கத்தினி லுற்றுறு பத்தர்கள்","ஏதுநி னைத்தது மெத்தஅ ளித்தரு ...... ளிளையோனே","ஏரக வெற்பெனு மற்புத மிக்கசு","வாமிம லைப்பதி மெச்சிய சித்தஇ","ராஜத லக்ஷண லக்ஷூமி பெற்றருள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether he should keep company with courtesans who melt men's hearts, sell their lips daily, learn the calls of peacock, quail, cuckoo, dove and cock to bewitch with sidelong eyes, lie on flower beds, call anyone into their house, loosen their garments, embrace and snatch coins, and act out a learned art of deceit. The praise names the vel that cut down Sura, and lists the shrines where Murugan grants devotees all they wish, Kathirkamam, Palani, Tiruttani, Tiruchendur by the sea and Swamimalai called Erakam.","n":"தோதக வித்தை படித்து நடிப்பவர்: deceit as a learned art and a performance. காசு பறிக்க மறித்து முயக்கிகள்: the embrace is for the coin. The praise is a pilgrim's list of shrines.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:18"},"p:216":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":216,"t":["சரணகம லால யத்தை அரைநிமிஷ நேர மட்டில்","தவமுறைதி யானம் வைக்க ...... அறியாத","சடகசட மூட மட்டி பவவினையி லேச னித்த","தமியன்மிடி யால்ம யக்க ...... முறுவேனோ","கருணைபுரி யாதி ருப்ப தெனகுறையி வேளை செப்பு","கயிலைமலை நாதர் பெற்ற குமரோனே","கடகபுய மீதி ரத்ந மணியணிபொன் மாலே செச்சை","கமழுமண மார்க டப்ப ...... மணிவோனே","தருணமிதை யாமி குத்த கனமதுறு நீள்ச வுக்ய","சகலசெல்வ யோக மிக்க ...... பெருவாழ்வு","தகைமைசிவ ஞான முத்தி பரகதியு நீகொ டுத்து","தவிபுரிய வேணு நெய்த்த ...... வடிவேலா","அருணதள பாத பத்ம மதுநிதமு மேது திக்க","அரியதமிழ் தான ளித்த ...... மயில்வீரா","அதிசயம நேக முற்ற பழநிமலை மீது தித்த","அழகதிரு வேர கத்தின் ...... முருகோனே."],"p":"","r":"The poet calls himself a dull, foolish wretch, born of worldly deeds, who never knew how to fix meditation on the lotus feet even for half a moment, and asks whether he must stay confused by poverty and what fault keeps Murugan from compassion now; he asks for great wealth, yoga, a life of greatness, Siva-jnana, release and the supreme state together. The praise names the son of Kailasa's lord who gave rare Tamil so the poet could praise his feet daily, and Murugan of Palani and Tiruverakam.","n":"The plea sets worldly gifts beside final ones without embarrassment: wealth and yoga beside Siva-jnana and mukti. அரியதமிழ் தான் அளித்த: the gift of Tamil verse to praise the feet, as in 209. மிடி is poverty.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kutram","thiyaanam","inmai","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:18"},"p:217":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":217,"t":["சுத்தியந ரப்புடனெ லுப்புறுத சைக்குடலொ","டப்புடனி ணச்சளிவ லிப்புடனி ரத்தகுகை","சுக்கிலம் விளைப்புழுவொ டக்கையும ழுக்குமயிர் ...... சங்முளை","துக்கம்விளை வித்தபிணை யற்கறைமு னைப்பெருகு","குட்டமொடு விப்புருதி புற்றெழுதல் முட்டுவலி","துச்சிபிள வைப்பொருமல் பித்தமொடு றக்கமிக ...... வங்க்முடே","எத்தனைநி னைப்பையும்வி ளைப்பையும யக்கமுற","லெத்தனைச லிப்பொடுக லிப்பையுமி டற்பெருமை","எத்தனைக சத்தையும லத்தையும டைத்தகுடில் ...... பஞ்சபூதம்","எத்தனைகு லுக்கையுமி னுக்கையும னக்கவலை","யெத்தனைக வட்டையுந டக்கையுமு யிர்க்குழுமல்","எத்தனைபி றப்பையுமி றப்பையுமெ டுத்துலகில் ...... மங்குவேனோ","தத்தனத னத்தனத னத்தனவெ னத்திமிலை","யொத்தமுர சத்துடியி டக்கைமுழ வுப்பறைகள்","சத்தமறை யத்தொகுதி யொத்தசெனி ரத்தவெள ...... மண்டியோடச்","சக்கிரிநெ ளிப்பஅவு ணப்பிணமி தப்பமரர்","கைத்தலம்வி ரித்தரஹ ரச்சிவபி ழைத்தொமென","சக்கிரகி ரிச்சுவர்கள் அக்கணமே பக்குவிட ...... வென்றவேலா","சித்தமதி லெத்தனைசெ கத்தலம்வி தித்துடன","ழித்துகம லத்தனைம ணிக்குடுமி பற்றிமலர்","சித்திரக ரத்தலம்வ லிப்பபல குட்டிநட ...... னங்கொள்வேளே","செட்டிவடி வைக்கொடுதி னைப்புனம திற்சிறுகு","றப்பெணம ளிக்குள்மகிழ் செட்டிகுரு வெற்பிலுறை","சிற்பரம ருக்கொருகு ருக்களென முத்தர்புகழ் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet catalogues the body, nerves, bones, flesh, fat, phlegm, the cave of blood, semen, worms, filth and hair, and its diseases, leprosy, abscess, swelling, colic and bile, and asks whether he must fade in the world, taking up again and again this hut of five elements with all its thoughts, cravings, pride and deceits, birth after birth. The praise is a battle in drum syllables at the Krauncha hill, and tells how Murugan seized the lotus-born by his tuft and rapped his head; he is the merchant who came to Valli's millet field and the guru of Siva whom the freed souls praise.","n":"The fullest anatomy of disgust so far in the book. கமலத்தனை மணிக்குடுமி பற்றி ... பல குட்டி: Brahma punished by Murugan, though Om is not named here; செட்டி வடிவு: Murugan disguised as a merchant to woo Valli. Some disease names and two garbled words (சங்முளை, வங்க்முடே) are uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","noy","piravi","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:19"},"p:218":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":218,"t":["செகமாயை யுற்றெ னகவாழ்வில் வைத்த","திருமாது கெர்ப்ப ...... முடலு஡றித்","தெசமாத முற்றி வடிவாய்நி லத்தில்","திரமாய ளித்த ...... பொருளாகி","மகவாவி னுச்சி விழியாந நத்தில்","மலைநேர்பு யத்தி ...... லுறவாடி","மடிமீத டுத்து விளையாடி நித்த","மணிவாயின் முத்தி ...... தரவேணும்","முகமாய மிட்ட குறமாதி னுக்கு","முலைமேல ணைக்க ...... வருநீதா","முதுமாம றைக்கு ளொருமாபொ ருட்குள்","மொழியேயு ரைத்த ...... குருநாதா","தகையாதெ னக்கு னடிகாண வைத்த","தனியேர கத்தின் ...... முருகோனே","தருகாவி ரிக்கு வடபாரி சத்தில்","சமர்வேலெ டுத்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks Murugan to come into the household life that the world's maya has given him, as the child his wife carries ten months and brings forth, so that he may play with him on his head, at his eyes and face and on his shoulders, take him on his lap, and receive kisses from his jewelled mouth every day. The praise names the one who came to embrace the hunter girl, the guru who told the one great meaning within the ancient Veda, Murugan of Erakam who let the poet see his feet, and the lord with the battle-vel on the Kaveri's north bank.","n":"மணிவாயின் முத்தி: முத்தி is the child's kiss (முத்தம்) and also mukti, so the plea asks for both at once. The Lord is asked into household life, as the poet's son, rather than as an escape from it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["makkal","anbu","karu","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:19"},"p:219":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":219,"t":["சேலுமயி லுந்த ரித்த வாளையட ருங்க டைக்கண்","மாதரைவ சம்ப டைத்த ...... வசமாகிச்","சீலமறை யும்ப ணத்தி லாசையிலை யென்ற வத்தை","காலமுமு டன்கி டக்கு ...... மவர்போலே","காலுமயி ரும்பி டித்து மேவுசிலு கும்பி ணக்கு","நாளுமிக நின்ற லைத்த ...... விதமாய","காமகல கம்பி ணித்த தோதகமெ னுந்து வக்கி","லேயடிமை யுங்க லக்க ...... முறலாமோ","ஏலமில வங்க வர்க்க நாகம்வகு ளம்ப டப்பை","பூகமரு தந்த ழைத்த ...... கரவீரம்","யாவுமலை கொண்டு கைத்த காவிரிபு றம்பு சுற்றும்","ஏரகம மர்ந்த பச்சை ...... மயில்வீரா","சேலைமடல் கொண்டு சக்ர மால்வரைய ரிந்த வஜ்ர","பாணியர்தொ ழுந்தி ருக்கை ...... வடிவேலா","சூர்முதிர்க்ர வுஞ்ச வெற்பும் வேலைநில மும்ப கைத்த","சூரனுட லுந்து ணித்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women with eyes like fish and spears, who say they have no wish for money and cling like a wife, then seize a man's legs and hair and quarrel, and asks whether he, the Lord's servant, should be thrown into turmoil in this snare of deceitful lust. The praise pictures the Kaveri carrying cardamom, cloves and areca around Erakam, the vel that Indra, holder of the thunderbolt, worships, and the lord who split Krauncha, the sea and Sura.","n":"பணத்தில் ஆசை இலை என்ற: the protest that she does not want money belongs to the trick. The second and third lines are hard to divide, so the picture of the women is tentative.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:19"},"p:220":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":220,"t":["தருவரிவ ராகு மென்று பொருணசையி னாடி வண்டு","தனைவிடுசொல் தூது தண்ட ...... முதலான","சரசகவி மாலை சிந்து கலிதுறைக ளேச லின்ப","தருமுதல தான செஞ்சொல் ...... வகைபாடி","மருவுகையு மோதி நொந்து அடிகள்முடி யேதெ ரிந்து","வரினுமிவர் வீத மெங்க ...... ளிடமாக","வருமதுவொ போது மென்று வொருபணமு தாசி னஞ்சொல்","மடையரிட மேந டந்து ...... மனம்வேறாய்","உருகிமிக வாக வெந்து கவிதைசொலி யேதி ரிந்து","உழல்வதுவு மேத விர்ந்து ...... விடவேநல்","உபயபத மால்வி ளங்கி யிகபரமு மேவ இன்ப","முதவியெனை யாள அன்பு ...... தருவாயே","குருகினொடு நாரை யன்றில் இரைகளது நாடி டங்கள்","குதிகொளிள வாளை கண்டு ...... பயமாகக்","குரைகடல்க ளேய திர்ந்து வருவதென வேவி ளங்கு","குருமலையின் மேல மர்ந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes singing for patrons: thinking 'these men will give', he made bee-messages, envoy poems, garlands of verse, sindhu and kalitturai for love of wealth, praising their hands and heads, only to be told 'come another time' and tossed a coin with contempt by fools; he asks that this wandering end and that he be given the love that rules him with joy here and hereafter under Murugan's two feet. The praise pictures Gurumalai's waters, where storks, herons and anril birds startle at leaping vaalai fish as if the sea had roared in.","n":"A poet's complaint about patronage that names the genres he wasted on men (வண்டு விடு தூது, கவிமாலை, சிந்து, கலித்துறை). The remedy is not to give up song but to turn it to the Lord. A few lines (வருமதுவொ போதும்) are hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iravu","pugazh","thiruvadi","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:19"},"p:221":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":221,"t":["தெருவினில் நடவா மடவார்","திரண்டொ றுக்கும் ...... வசையாலே","தினகர னெனவே லையிலே","சிவந்து திக்கும் ...... மதியாலே","பெருசிலை வளையா இளையா","மதன்தொ டுக்குங் ...... கணையாலே","புளகித முலையா ளலையா","மனஞ்ச லித்தும் ...... விடலாமோ","ஒருமலை யிருகூ றெழவே","யுரம்பு குத்தும் ...... வடிவேலா","ஒளிவளர் திருவே ரகமே","யுகந்து நிற்கும் ...... முருகோனே","அருமறை தமிழ்நூ லடைவே","தெரிந்து ரைக்கும் ...... புலவோனே","அரியரி பிரமா தியர்கால்","விலங்க விழ்க்கும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"In the voice of someone close to a girl in love, the song asks whether she, her breasts thrilling, should be left wavering and worn in mind by the taunts of the women who gather in the street, by the moon rising red over the sea like the sun, and by the arrows of Kama. The praise names the vel that split a hill in two, Murugan who stands at Tiruverakam, the poet who tells the Vedas and Tamil works in order, and the lord who frees the fettered feet of Vishnu, Brahma and the gods.","n":"Akam conventions throughout: the street's gossip, the evening moon that burns like the sun, Kama's bow. The speaker is not named; it reads as a mother or friend, as in 24. அருமறை தமிழ்நூல் அடைவே தெரிந்துரைக்கும் புலவோனே: Murugan as poet of Veda and Tamil alike.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","alar","maalai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:19"},"p:222":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":222,"t":["நாசர்தங் கடையதனில் விரவிநான் மெத்த நொந்து ...... தடுமாறி","ஞானமுங் கெடஅடைய வழுவியா ழத்த ழுந்தி ...... மெலியாதே","மாசகந் தொழுமுனது புகழினோர் சொற்ப கர்ந்து ...... சுகமேவி","மாமணங் கமழுமிரு கமலபா தத்தை நின்று ...... பணிவேனோ","வாசகம் புகலவொரு பரமர்தா மெச்சு கின்ற ...... குருநாதா","வாசவன் தருதிருவை யொருதெய்வா னைக்கி ரங்கு ...... மணவாளா","கீசகஞ் சுரர்தருவு மகிழுமா வத்தி சந்து ...... புடைசூழுங்","கேசவன் பரவுகுரு மலையில்யோ கத்த மர்ந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks that he not stumble, worn out, mingling at the doors of the wicked, nor lose his knowledge and sink deep, but, speaking the words of those who praise Murugan, live in joy and stand worshipping the two fragrant lotus feet. The praise names the guru whom Siva praised for telling the word, the bridegroom moved by Devayanai whom Indra gave, and the lord in yoga on Gurumalai, worshipped by Kesava.","n":"நாசர் தம் கடை அதனில் விரவி: the doors of the wicked may be those of patrons, as in 220, or the company of bad men generally. The guru 'for the word' (வாசகம் புகல) is the Swamimalai teaching of Siva.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["inam","pugazh","thiruvadi","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:19"},"p:223":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":223,"t":["நாவேறு பாம ணத்த பாதார மேநி னைத்து","நாலாறு நாலு பற்று ...... வகையான","நாலாரு மாக மத்தி னுலாய ஞான முத்தி","நாடோ று நானு ரைத்த ...... நெறியாக","நீவேறெ னாதி ருக்க நான்வேறெ னாதி ருக்க","நேராக வாழ்வ தற்கு ...... னருள்கூர","நீடார்ஷ டாத ரத்தின் மீதேப ராப ரத்தை","நீகாணெ னாவ னைச்சொ ...... லருள்வாயே","சேவேறு மீசர் சுற்ற மாஞான போத புத்தி","சீராக வேயு ரைத்த ...... குருநாதா","தேரார்கள் நாடு சுட்ட சூரார்கள் மாள வெட்டு","தீராகு காகு றத்தி ...... மணவாளா","காவேரி நேர்வ டக்கி லேவாவி பூம ணத்த","காவார்சு வாமி வெற்பின் ...... முருகோனே","கார்போலு மேனி பெற்ற மாகாளி வாலை சத்தி","காமாரி வாமி பொற்ற ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks for the jnana-release that runs through the twenty-eight Agamas and their four ways, as he has spoken it daily in songs on the Lord's feet, so that, with neither the Lord saying 'you are other' nor he saying 'I am other', he may live in union, and that Murugan tell him 'see' the Supreme above the six supports. The praise names the guru who taught Siva and his circle the understanding of jnana, the bridegroom of the hunter girl, Murugan of Swamimalai by the Kaveri, and the son of Kali, Bala, Shakti, the left half of Kama's foe.","n":"நாலாறு நாலு: twenty-eight, the number of the Saiva Agamas; the 'four' may be the four paths. நீ வேறு எனாதிருக்க நான் வேறு எனாதிருக்க: non-difference asked from both sides. ஷடாதாரத்தின் மீதே பராபரம்: the Supreme above the six centres of yoga. The counting is the likeliest reading, not a certain one.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aagamam","aadhaaram","gnaanam","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:19"},"p:224":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":224,"t":["நிலவினி லேயி ருந்து வகைமல ரேதெ ரிந்து","நிறைகுழல் மீத ணிந்து ...... குழைதாவும்","நிகரறு வேலி னங்கள் வரிதர வாச கங்கள்","நினைவற வேமொ ழிந்து ...... மதனு஡லின்","கலபம னோக ரங்க ளளவற வேபு ரிந்து","கனியித ழேய ருந்தி ...... யநுராகக்","கலவியி லேமு யங்கி வனிதையர் பால்ம யங்கு","கபடனை யாள வுன்ற ...... னருள்கூராய்","உலகமொ ரேழு மண்ட ருலகமு மீசர் தங்கு","முயர்கயி லாய மும்பொன் ...... வரைதானும்","உயிரொடு பூத மைந்து மொருமுத லாகி நின்ற","உமையரு ளால்வ ளர்ந்த ...... குமரேசா","குலைபடு சூர னங்க மழிபட வேலெ றிந்த","குமரக டோ ர வெங்கண் ...... மயில்வாழ்வே","கொடுமுடி யாய்வ ளர்ந்து புயனிலை போலு யர்ந்த","குருமலை மீத மர்ந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women who sit in moonlight choosing flowers for their hair, whose spear-eyes reach their ear-rings, whose words drive out thought, and who practise without measure the arts of Kama's treatise, and calls himself the deceiver deluded in lovemaking with them; he asks for grace to rule him. The praise names Uma, who stood as the single source of the seven worlds, the gods' worlds, Kailasa, Meru, life and the five elements, as the one by whose grace Kumara grew, and the vel against Sura, at Gurumalai rising like a peak.","n":"மதன் நூல் (garbled in the source): Kama's treatise as the women's discipline, as மதனாகமம் in 230. கபடன், deceiver, is the poet's name for himself. The praise gives Uma the place of first cause.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kaamam","punarchi","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:19"},"p:225":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":225,"t":["நிறைமதி முகமெனு ...... மொளியாலே","நெறிவிழி கணையெனு ...... நிகராலே","உறவுகொள் மடவர்க ...... ளுறவாமோ","உனதிரு வடிவியினி ...... யருள்வாயே","மறைபயி லரிதிரு ...... மருகோனே","மருவல ரசுரர்கள் ...... குலகாலா","குறமகள் தனைமண ...... மருள்வோனே","குருமலை மருவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A short song: the poet asks whether the love of women whose faces shine like the full moon and whose eyes are arrows can be a true bond, and asks for Murugan's holy feet now. The praise names Vishnu's nephew, death to the demon clans, Valli's bridegroom, at Gurumalai.","n":"உனதிரு வடிவியினி is garbled; it most likely reads உன திருவடி இனி, 'your holy feet now', though திருவடிவு, 'holy form', is possible.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:19"},"p:226":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":226,"t":["பரவரி தாகிய வரையென நீடிய","பணைமுலை மீதினி ...... லுருவான","பணிகளு லாவிட இழையிடை சாய்தரு","பயிலிகள் வாள்விழி ...... அயிலாலே","நிரவரி யோடியல் குழல்களி னாண்மலர்","நிரைதரு மூரலி ...... னகைமீது","நிலவியல் சேர்முக மதிலுயர் மாமயல்","நிலையெழ வேயலை ...... வதுவாமோ","அரவணை யார்குழை பரசிவ ஆரண","அரனிட பாகம ...... துறைசோதி","அமையுமை டாகினி திரிபுரை நாரணி","அழகிய மாதருள் ...... புதல்வோனே","குரவணி பூஷண சரவண தேசிக","குககரு ணாநிதி ...... அமரேசா","குறமக ளானைமின் மருவிய பூரண","குருகிரி மேவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether he must wander as great infatuation rises for women whose breasts carry ornaments, whose eyes are swords, whose hair is full of flowers and whose smile is like moonlight. The praise centres on Uma, the light on the left side of Siva who wears the serpent, named Dakini, Tripurai and Narani, and names Murugan Saravana the teacher, Guha the treasure of compassion, at Gurumalai.","n":"மாமயல் நிலையெழவே அலைவது: infatuation as a state that rises and drives him wandering. As in 32, the praise dwells on the Mother more than on Murugan.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mayal","kaamam","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:19"},"p:227":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":227,"t":["பலகாதல் பெற்றிடவு மொருநாழி கைக்குளொரு","பலனேபெ றப்பரவு ...... கயவாலே","பலபேரை மெச்சிவரு தொழிலேசெ லுத்தியுடல்","பதறாமல் வெட்கமறு ...... வகைகூறி","விலகாத லச்சைதணி மலையாமு லைச்சியர்கள்","வினையேமி குத்தவர்கள் ...... தொழிலாலே","விடமேகொ டுத்துவெகு பொருளேப றித்தருளும்","விலைமாதர் பொய்க்கலவி ...... யினிதாமோ","மலையேயெ டுத்தருளு மொருவாள ரக்கனுடல்","வடமேரெ னத்தரையில் ...... விழவேதான்","வகையாவி டுத்தகணை யுடையான்ம கிழ்ச்சிபெறு","மருகாக டப்பமல ...... ரணிமார்பா","சிலகாவி யத்ததுறைக ளுணர்வோர்ப டித்ததமிழ்","செவியார வைத்தருளு ...... முருகோனே","சிவனார்த மக்குரிய வுபதேச வித்தையருள்","திருவேர கத்தில்வரு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether the false lovemaking of women who sell themselves can be sweet: they promise many loves for one hour's gain, praise many men, talk away shame, give poison and snatch great wealth. The praise names Rama, whose arrow felled the demon who lifted the mountain, as uncle, Murugan who hears with delight the Tamil of those who know the kavyas, and the lord at Tiruverakam who gave Siva the teaching due to him.","n":"விலைமாதர் பொய்க்கலவி: the courtesan's love called false, in the Kural's own terms. விடமே கொடுத்து may be a love-drug or poison. சிவனார் தமக்குரிய உபதேச வித்தை: the teaching to Siva, the Swamimalai myth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","selvam","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:19"},"p:228":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":228,"t":["பாதி மதிநதி போது மணிசடை","நாத ரருளிய ...... குமரேசா","பாகு கனிமொழி மாது குறமகள்","பாதம் வருடிய ...... மணவாளா","காது மொருவிழி காக முறஅருள்","மாய னரிதிரு ...... மருகோனே","கால னெனையணு காம லுனதிரு","காலில் வழிபட ...... அருள்வாயே","ஆதி யயனொடு தேவர் சுரருல","காளும் வகையுறு ...... சிறைமீளா","ஆடு மயிலினி லேறி யமரர்கள்","சூழ வரவரு ...... மிளையோனே","சூத மிகவளர் சோலை மருவுசு","வாமி மலைதனி ...... லுறைவோனே","சூர னுடலற வாரி சுவறிட","வேலை விடவல ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks that Death not come near him but that he worship at Murugan's holy feet. The praise names the son given by Siva of the crescent, river and flowers, the bridegroom who pressed the hunter girl's feet, the nephew of Vishnu who showed grace to the crow that lost one eye, the young one who freed the gods from prison and comes on the peacock, at Swamimalai, whose vel dried the sea.","n":"காதும் ஒருவிழி காகம் உற அருள் மாயன்: the crow that lost one eye to Rama's arrow and was spared, an act of grace. Murugan pressing Valli's feet (பாதம் வருடிய) matches 209.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","thiruvadi","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:19"},"p:229":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":229,"t":["மகர கேத னத்த னுருவி லானெ டுத்து","மதுர நாணி யிட்டு ...... நெறிசேர்வார்","மலைய வேவ ளைத்த சிலையி னு஡டோ ளித்த","வலிய சாய கக்கண் ...... மடமாதர்","இகழ வாச முற்ற தலையெ லாம்வெ ளுத்து","இளமை போயொ ளித்து ...... விடுமாறு","இடைவி டாதெ டுத்த பிறவி வேர றுத்து","னினிய தாள ளிப்ப ...... தொருநாளே","அகில மேழு மெட்டு வரையின் மீது முட்ட","அதிர வேந டத்து ...... மயில்வீரா","அசுரர் சேனை கெட்டு முறிய வான வர்க்கு","அடைய வாழ்வ ளிக்கு ...... மிளையோனே","மிகநி லாவெ றித்த அமுத வேணி நிற்க","விழைசு வாமி வெற்பி ...... லுறைவோனே","விரைய ஞான வித்தை யருள்செய் தாதை கற்க","வினவ வோது வித்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women whose eyes are arrows hidden in the bow of the bodiless Kama, and asks whether one day, before they scorn him as his scented hair whitens and youth slips away, Murugan will cut the root of the births he has taken without pause and give his sweet feet. The praise names the peacock that shakes the seven worlds and eight mountains, the young one who gave the gods back their life, Murugan of Swamimalai, and the lord who taught the jnana his father asked to learn.","n":"The argument against desire is the women's scorn when he ages, not their deceit (compare 34). தாதை கற்க வினவ ஓதுவித்த: the father asks and the son teaches.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["ilamai","piravi","thiruvadi","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:19"},"p:230":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":230,"t":["மருவே செறித்த குழலார் மயக்கி","மதனா கமத்தின் ...... விரகாலே","மயலே யெழுப்பி யிதழே யருத்த","மலைபோல் முலைக்கு ...... ளுறவாகிப்","பெருகாத லுற்ற தமியேனை நித்தல்","பிரியாது பட்ச ...... மறவாதே","பிழையே பொறுத்து னிருதாளி லுற்ற","பெருவாழ்வு பற்ற ...... அருள்வாயே","குருவா யரற்கு முபதேசம் வைத்த","குகனே குறத்தி ...... மணவாளா","குளிர்கா மிகுத்த வளர்பூக மெத்து","குடகா விரிக்கு ...... மிளையோனே","திருமால் தனக்கு மருகா வரக்கர்","சிரமே துணித்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses that he was deluded by women with fragrant hair through the arts of Kama's scripture, drank their lips and was bound to their breasts, and asks Murugan, not forgetting his favour to this lonely one, to bear with his faults and grant the great life that holds to his feet. The praise names Guha who gave the teaching to Siva as guru, the bridegroom of the hunter girl, the young one of the Kaveri from the western hills, and Vishnu's nephew who cut off the demons' heads.","n":"மதனாகமம்: Kama's Agama, a pointed phrase in a Saiva poet, as if love had its own scripture. பிழையே பொறுத்து: the plea is for forgiveness, not reward.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mayal","kaamam","thiruvadi","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:19"},"p:231":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":231,"t":["முறுகு காள விடம யின்ற இருகண் வேலி னுளம யங்கி","முளரி வேரி முகைய டர்ந்த ...... முலைமீதே","முழுகு காதல் தனைம றந்து பரம ஞான வொளிசி றந்து","முகமொ ராறு மிகவி ரும்பி ...... அயராதே","அறுகு தாளி நறைய விழ்ந்த குவளை வாச மலர்க ரந்தை","அடைய வாரி மிசைபொ ழிந்து ...... னடிபேணி","அவச மாகி யுருகு தொண்ட ருடன தாகி விளையு மன்பி","னடிமை யாகு முறைமை யொன்றை ...... அருள்வாயே","தறுகண் வீரர் தலைய ரிந்து பொருத சூர னுடல்பி ளந்து","தமர வேலை சுவற வென்ற ...... வடிவேலா","தரள மூர லுமைம டந்தை முலையி லார அமுத முண்டு","தரணி யேழும் வலம்வ ருந்திண் ...... மயில்வீரா","மறுவி லாத தினைவி ளைந்த புனம்வி டாம லிதணி ருந்து","வலிய காவல் புனைய ணங்கின் ...... மணவாளா","மருவு ஞாழ லணிசெ ருந்தி யடவி சூத வனநெ ருங்கி","வளர்சு வாமி மலைய மர்ந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks that, forgetting the love that drowned him in women's breasts, with the light of supreme jnana growing and longing for the six faces without tiring, he strew arugu, thali, kuvalai and karandai flowers on the feet, and be given the one way of servanthood that grows from love among devotees who melt beside themselves. The praise names the vel that dried the sea, the peacock hero who drank Uma's milk and circled the seven worlds, Valli's bridegroom, at Swamimalai of groves.","n":"அவசமாகி உருகு தொண்டருடன் அதாகி: the poet asks to become one with the melting devotees; அடிமையாகு முறைமை is devotional service here, the opposite of the bondage to women in 213.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thondu","adiyaar","anbu","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:19"},"p:232":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":232,"t":["வாதமொடு சூலை கண்ட மாலைகுலை நோவு சந்து","மாவலிவி யாதி குன்ம ...... மொடுகாசம்","வாயுவுட னேப ரந்த தாமரைகள் பீன சம்பின்","மாதர்தரு பூஷ ணங்க ...... ளெனவாகும்","பாதகவி யாதி புண்க ளானதுட னேதொ டர்ந்து","பாயலைவி டாது மங்க ...... இவையால்நின்","பாதமல ரான தின்க ணேயமற வேம றந்து","பாவமது பான முண்டு ...... வெற஧முடி","ஏதமுறு பாச பந்த மானவலை யோடு ழன்று","ஈனமிகு சாதி யின்க ...... ணதிலேயான்","ஈடழித லான தின்பின் மூடனென வோது முன்புன்","ஈரஅருள் கூர வந்து ...... எனையாள்வாய்","சூதமகிழ் பாலை கொன்றை தாதுவளர் சோலை துன்றி","சூழமதில் தாவி மஞ்சி ...... னளவாகத்","தோரணநன் மாட மெங்கு நீடுகொடி யேத ழைந்த","சுவாமிமலை வாழ வந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet lists the diseases that have come on him, wind, colic, scrofula, swellings, cough, ringworm and the sores he calls the ornaments women give, which keep him to his bed; through them he has forgotten the love of Murugan's flower feet and drunk sin like liquor. Caught in the net of bonds in a low birth, he asks Murugan to come with grace and rule him before he is ruined and called a fool. The praise is only of Swamimalai, its groves of mango and konrai, its walls reaching the clouds and its flags.","n":"மாதர்தரு பூஷணங்கள், 'ornaments women give', is bitter irony: the diseases caught from women are the jewels they return for the jewels given them. பாதகவியாதி, 'diseases of sin', ties illness to fault. The word in line 12 is corrupt and its sense uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["noy","paasam","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:20"},"p:233":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":233,"t":["வார முற்ற பண்பின் மாத முற்ற நண்பி","னீடு மெய்த்து யர்ந்து ...... வயதாகி","வாலை யிற்றி ரிந்து கோல மைக்கண் மங்கை","மார்க ளுக்கி சைந்து ...... பொருள்தேடி","ஆர மிக்க பொன்க ளால மைத்த மர்ந்த","மாப ணிக்கள் விந்தை ...... யதுவான","ஆட கொப்ப மைந்த வோலை முத்த முங்கொ","டாவி மெத்த நொந்து ...... திரிவேனோ","சூர னைத்து ரந்து வேர றப்பி ளந்து","சூழ்சு ரர்க்க ணன்பு ...... செயும்வீரா","சூக ரத்தொ டம்பு தானெ டுத்து வந்த","சூத னுக்கி சைந்த ...... மருகோனே","ஏரெ திர்த்து வந்து நீர்கள் கட்டி யன்று","தானி றைக்க வந்த ...... தொருசாலி","யேமி குத்து யர்ந்த மாவ யற்கள் மிஞ்சு","மேர கத்த மர்ந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet tells how he grew old in friendship with women, wandering from youth after kohl-eyed girls and seeking wealth to buy them gold ornaments, earrings and pearls, and asks whether he must go on wandering in such pain of spirit. The praise names the hero who drove off Sura and split him to the root, the nephew of the charioteer Vishnu, and Eragam among high paddy fields where farmers raise water.","n":"The confession is of spending: பொருள்தேடி, 'seeking wealth', for women's ornaments (மாபணிக்கள், ஓலை, முத்தம்). The epithet சூகரத்தொடு அம்பு தானெடுத்து வந்த சூதன் may join Vishnu's boar form to the charioteer Krishna; the reading of the opening lines and of this epithet is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","selvam","ilamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:20"},"p:234":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":234,"t":["வார்குழ லைச்சொரு கிக்கரு விற்குழை","காதொடி ணைத்தசை யக்கதிர் பற்கொடு","வாயிதழ் பொற்கம லர்க்குமி ழொத்துள ...... துண்டக்஡ணவ","வார்கமு கிற்புய நற்கழை பொற்குவ","டாடிள நிர்ச்சுரர் பொற்குட மொத்திணை","மார்பழ கிற்பொறி முத்தொளிர் சித்திர ...... ரம்பைமாதர்","காருறும் வித்திடை யிற்கத லித்தொடை","சேரல்குல் நற்பிர சத்தட முட்கொடு","கால்மறை யத்துவ ளச்செறி பொற்கலை ...... யொண்குலாவக்","கார்குயி லைக்குர லைக்கொடு நற்றெரு","மீதில்நெ ளித்துந கைத்துந டிப்பவர்","காமனு கப்பம ளிச்சுழல் குத்திரர் ...... சந்தமாமோ","சூரர்ப தைக்கர வுட்கிநெ ளித்துய","ராழியி ரைப்பநி ணக்குட லைக்கழு","சூழந ரிக்கெரு டக்கொடி பற்பல ...... சங்மாகச்","சூழ்கிரி யைக்கைத டித்தும லைத்திகை","யானையு ழற்றிந டுக்கிம தப்பொறி","சோரந கைத்தயி லைக்கொடு விட்டருள் ...... செங்கைவேலா","ஏரணி நற்குழ லைக்கக னச்சசி","மோகினி யைப்புணர் சித்தொரு அற்புத","வேடமு தச்சொரு பத்தகு றத்திம ...... ணங்கொள்வோனே","ஏரக வெற்பெனு மற்புத மிக்கசு","வாமிம லைப்பதி நிற்குமி லக்ஷண","ராஜத லக்ஷண லக்ஷுமி பெற்றருள் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes at length the beauty of dancing women like Rambha, from their hair, earrings, teeth and lips to their breasts, waist and thighs, who sing in cuckoo voices and twist, smile and dance in the street, and asks whether it is fit to keep company with these deceivers who serve Kama's bed. The praise is a battle in which the sea roars, jackals and vultures feed, the mountain is struck and the elephants of the directions shake as Murugan sends the vel with a smile; he weds Indra's daughter and the hunter girl and stands at Eragam.","n":"The whole charge sits in the last line of the confession: குத்திரர், deceivers, and சந்தமாமோ, 'is it fitting?'. Dancing in the street (நற்றெருமீதில் ... நடிப்பவர்) suggests temple or street dancers, but no payment is named. The final epithet, லக்ஷுமி பெற்றருள், is unclear to me.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["kaamam","vanjam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:20"},"p:235":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":235,"t":["வார்குழல் விரித்துத் தூக்கி வேல்விழி சுழற்றிப் பார்த்து","வாவென நகைத்துத் தோட்டு ...... குழையாட","வாசக முரைத்துச் சூத்ர பாவையெ னுறுப்பைக் காட்டி","வாசனை முலைக்கச் சாட்டி ...... யழகாகச்","சீர்கலை நெகிழ்த்துப் போர்த்து நூலிடை நெளித்துக் காட்டி","தீதெய நடித்துப் பாட்டு ...... குயில்போலச்","சேருற அழைத்துப் பார்த்து சார்வுற மருத்திட் டாட்டி","சீர்பொருள் பறிப்பொய்க் கூத்த ...... ருறவாமோ","சூரர்கள் பதைக்கத் தேர்க்க ளானைக ளழித்துத் தாக்கி","சூர்கிரி கொளுத்திக் கூற்று ...... ரிடும்வேலா","தூமொழி நகைத்துக் கூற்றை மாளிட வுதைத்துக் கோத்த","தோலுடை யெனப்பர்க் கேற்றி ...... திரிவோனே","ஏரணி சடைச்சிப் பாற்சொ லாரணி சிறக்கப் போற்று","மேரெழி னிறத்துக் கூர்த்த ...... மகவோனே","ஏடணி குழைச்சித் தூர்த்த வாடகி குறத்திக் கேற்ற","ஏரக பொருப்பிற் பூத்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes courtesans who loosen and toss their hair, roll their spear eyes, laugh 'come', show their bodies like puppets on strings, slip their garments, dance and sing like cuckoos, draw a man close, dose him with a drug and strip him of his wealth, and asks whether a bond with these false dancers is right. The praise names the vel that burned Sura's hill, the father who kicked Death, Uma as mother, and the hunter girl, at Eragam.","n":"மருத்திட்டு ஆட்டி, 'dosing him with medicine', and சீர்பொருள் பறி, 'snatching his wealth', make these women thieves as well as lures; பொய்க் கூத்தர், false dancers, joins the two. The lines on Siva who kicked Death (கூற்றை மாளிட உதைத்து) and wears a skin may say that Murugan taught him (எனப்பர்க்கு ஏற்றி), but the syntax is not clear to me.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:20"},"p:236":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":236,"t":["விடமும்வடி வேலு மதனச ரங்களும்","வடுவுநிக ரான மகரநெ டுங்குழை","விரவியுடன் மீளும் விழிகளு மென்புழு ...... கதுதோயும்","ம்ருகமதப டீர பரிமள குங்கும","மணியுமிள நீரும் வடகுல குன்றமும்","வெருவுவன பார புளகத னங்களும் ...... வெகுகாம","நடனபத நூபு ரமுமுகில் கெஞ்சிட","மலர்சொருகு கேச பரமுமி லங்கிய","நளினமலர் சோதி மதிமுக விம்பமும் ...... அனநேராம்","நடையுநளிர் மாதர் நிலவுதொ ழுந்தனு","முழுதுமபி ராம அரிவய கிண்கிணெ","னகையுமுள மாதர் கலவியி னைந்துரு ...... கிடலாமோ","வடிவுடைய மானு மிகல்கர னுந்திக","ழெழுவகைம ராம ரமுநிக ரொன்றுமில்","வலியதிறல் வாலி யுரமுநெ டுங்கட ...... லவையேழும்","மறநிருதர் சேனை முழுதுமி லங்கைமன்","வகையிரவி போலு மணியும லங்க்ருத","மணிமவுலி யான வொருபதும் விஞ்சிரு ...... பதுதோளும்","அடைவலமு மாள விடுசர அம்புடை","தசரதகு மார ரகுகுல புங்கவன்","அருள்புனைமு ராரி மருகவி ளங்கிய ...... மயிலேறி","அடையலர்கள் மாள வொருநிமி டந்தனி","லுலகைவல மாக நொடியினில் வந்துயர்","அழகியசு வாமி மலையில மர்ந்தருள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women's eyes, like poison, the vel and Kama's arrows, their musk-scented breasts like the northern mountain, their anklets, flowered hair, moon faces and swan gait, and asks whether he may melt and waste in union with them. The praise tells the whole Rama story, the golden deer, Kara, the seven sala trees, Vali's chest, the demon army and the ten crowned heads of Lanka's king, and names Murugan as that Rama's nephew, who mounted the peacock and circled the world in an instant, at Swamimalai.","n":"The plea is one question, கலவியில் நைந்துருகிடலாமோ, 'may I melt in union?', after nine lines of description. உலகை வலமாக நொடியினில் வந்து, circling the world in a moment, recalls the story of the fruit contest, but the text gives only the circling.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","punarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:20"},"p:237":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":237,"t":["விரித்த பைங்குழ லொளிர்மல ரளிதன","தனத்த னந்தன தனதன வெனவொலி","விரிப்ப வண்கயல் விழியுறை குழையொடு ...... மலைபாய","மிகுந்த வண்சிலை நுதல்மிசை திலதமொ","டசைத்த பொன்குழை யழகெழ முகவொளி","வெயிற்ப ரந்திட நகையிதழ் முருகலர் ...... வரிபோதத்","தரித்த தந்திரி மறிபுய மிசைபல","பணிக்கி லங்கிய பரிமள குவடிணை","தனக்கொ ழுந்துகள் ததைபட கொடியிடை ...... படுசேலை","தரித்து சுந்தர மெனஅடர் பரிபுர","பதச்சி லம்பொடு நடமிடு கணிகையர்","சழக்கர் விஞ்சையர் மயல்களின் முழுகுவ ...... தொழியாதோ","உரித்த வெங்கய மறியொடு புலிகலை","தரித்த சங்கரர் மதிநதி சடையினர்","ஒருத்தி பங்கின ரவர்பணி குருபர ...... முருகோனே","உவட்டி வந்திடு மவுணரொ டெழுகடல்","குவட்டை யும்பொடி படசத முடிவுற","வுழைத்த இந்தரர் பிரமனு மகிழ்வுற ...... விடும்வேலா","வரித்த ரந்துள வணிதிரு மருவிய","வுரத்த பங்கயர் மரகத மழகிய","வணத்த ரம்பர முறவிடு கணையினர் ...... மருகோனே","வனத்தில் வந்தொரு பழையவ னெனவொரு","குறத்தி மென்புன மருவிய கிளிதனை","மயக்கி மந்திர குருமலை தனிலமர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes the hair, carp eyes, earrings, brow, smile and breasts of courtesans who dance in anklets, and asks whether his drowning in infatuation with these deceivers and enchantresses will never end. The praise names Siva who wears the flayed elephant and tiger skin and shares his body with Uma, and Murugan as the guru Siva bows to; the vel that broke the seven seas; Vishnu as uncle; and Murugan who came to the millet field as an old man and beguiled the hunter girl, at the Mantra-guru hill.","n":"கணிகையர் சழக்கர் விஞ்சையர் piles three names on the women: courtesans, deceivers, sorceresses. அவர்பணி குருபர, 'the guru whom he serves', puts Siva at Murugan's feet. மந்திர குருமலை, the hill of the mantra-guru, names Swamimalai by that teaching.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mayal","kaamam","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:20"},"p:238":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":238,"t":["விழியால் மருட்டி நின்று முலைதூச கற்றி மண்டு","விரகான லத்த ழுந்த ...... நகையாடி","விலையாக மிக்க செம்பொன் வரவேப ரப்பி வஞ்ச","விளையாட லுக்கி சைந்து ...... சிலநாள்மேல்","மொழியாத சொற்கள் வந்து சிலுகாகி விட்ட தொந்த","முழுமாயை யிற்பி ணங்கள் ...... வசமாகி","முடியாது பொற்ச தங்கை தருகீத வெட்சி துன்று","முதிராத நற்ப தங்கள் ...... தருவாயே","பொழிகார்மு கிற்கி ணைந்த யமராஜ னுட்க அன்று","பொருதாளெ டுத்த தந்தை ...... மகிழ்வோனே","புருகூத னுட்கு ளிர்ந்த கனகாபு ரிப்ர சண்ட","புனிதாம்ரு கக்க ரும்பு ...... புணர்மார்பா","செழுவாரி சத்தி லொன்று முதுவேதன் வெட்க அன்று","திருவாய்மை செப்பி நின்ற ...... முருகோனே","திரளாம ணிக்கு லங்கள் அருணோத யத்தை வென்ற","திருவேர கத்த மர்ந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet tells how women bewitch with their eyes, slip their breast-cloths and drown a man in the fire of lust, agree to deceitful play once red gold is spread before them as the price, and after a few days quarrel with unheard-of words, and asks for Murugan's ever-fresh feet with golden anklets and vetchi flowers. The praise names Siva who raised his foot against Yama, Murugan's marriages, and the Murugan who once told the truth so that the old Veda-knower Brahma was ashamed, at Eragam.","n":"விலையாக மிக்க செம்பொன், gold 'as the price', and the quarrel that follows (சிலுகாகி) are the courtesan story in brief, and the bond is called முழுமாயை, wholly illusion. முதுவேதன் வெட்க ... திருவாய்மை செப்பி is the story of Brahma's ignorance, here without naming Om. The epithets for the two consorts (புருகூதன், ம்ருகக் கரும்பு) are uncertain to me.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","thiruvadi","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:20"},"p:239":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":239,"t":["அமைவுற் றடையப் பசியுற் றவருக்","கமுதைப் பகிர்தற் ...... கிசையாதே","அடையப் பொருள்கைக் கிளமைக் கெனவைத்","தருள்தப் பிமதத் ...... தயராதே","தமர்சுற் றியழப் பறைகொட் டியிடச்","சமனெட் டுயிரைக் ...... கொடுபோகுஞ்","சரிரத் தினைநிற் குமெனக் கருதித்","தளர்வுற் றொழியக் ...... கடவேனோ","இமயத் துமயிற் கொருபக் கமளித்","தவருக் கிசையப் ...... புகல்வோனே","இரணத் தினிலெற் றுவரைக் கழுகுக்","கிரையிட் டிடுவிக் ...... ரமவேலா","சமயச் சிலுகிட் டவரைத் தவறித்","தவமுற் றவருட் ...... புகநாடும்","சடுபத் மமுகக் குகபுக் ககனத்","தணியிற் குமரப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether he must fail and perish, having refused to share food with the hungry who came to him, kept his wealth for his hands and his youth, strayed from grace and suffered in pride, and taken this body to be lasting until kin surround it weeping, drums beat and Death takes the life. The praise names Murugan as the one who spoke agreeably to Siva, who gave half his body to Uma, who fed his foes to vultures, and who seeks out those who leave the quarrels of religions for austerity; the song ends at Thani.","n":"The confession is of not giving: பசியுற்றவருக்கு அமுதைப் பகிர்தற்கு இசையாதே, refusing to share food with the hungry, set against the funeral (தமர் சுற்றி யழப் பறைகொட்டியிட). சமயச் சிலுகிட்டவரைத் தவறி, leaving those who quarrel over religions, is unusual praise: Murugan seeks those who go beyond sect.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","yaakkai","kootru","samayam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:20"},"p:240":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":240,"t":["அரகர சிவனரி அயனிவர் பரவிமு","னறுமுக சரவண ...... பவனேயென்","றநுதின மொழிதர அசுரர்கள் கெடஅயில்","அநலென எழவிடு ...... மதிவீரா","பரிபுர கமலம தடியிணை யடியவர்","உளமதி லுறவருள் ...... முருகேசா","பகவதி வரைமகள் உமைதர வருகுக","பரமன திருசெவி ...... களிகூர","உரைசெயு மொருமொழி பிரணவ முடிவதை","உரைதரு குருபர ...... வுயர்வாய","உலகம னலகில வுயிர்களு மிமையவ","ரவர்களு முறுவர ...... முநிவோரும்","பரவிமு னநுதின மானமகிழ் வுறவணி","பணிதிகழ் தணிகையி ...... லுறைவோனே","பகர்தரு குறமகள் தருவமை வநிதையு","மிருபுடை யுறவரு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A song of praise: Siva, Vishnu and Brahma praise Murugan daily as six-faced Saravanabhava; he sent the vel like fire against the asuras, dwells by his anklet-ringed feet in devotees' hearts, was born of Uma, and as guru told Siva, to his delight, the meaning that ends the one word Pranava. All worlds, gods and sages praise him at Thanigai, where he comes with the hunter girl and the daughter of the heavenly tree at either side.","n":"பரமன திருசெவி களிகூர உரைசெயு மொருமொழி பிரணவ முடிவதை: the teaching is spoken into Siva's ear and is the end (meaning) of Om, not the syllable alone. The song opens with the three gods as worshippers.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piranavam","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:21"},"p:241":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":241,"t":["அருக்கிமெத் தெனச்சிரித் துருக்கியிட் டுளக்கருத்","தழித்தறக் கறுத்தகட் ...... பயிலாலே","அழைத்தகப் படுத்தியொட் டறப்பொருட் பறிப்பவர்க்","கடுத்தபத் தமுற்றுவித் ...... தகர்போலத்","தரிக்கும்வித் தரிக்குமிக் கதத்துவப் ப்ரசித்தியெத்","தலத்துமற் றிலைப்பிறர்க் ...... கெனஞானம்","சமைத்துரைத் திமைப்பினிற் சடக்கெனப் படுத்தெழச்","சறுக்குமிப் பிறப்புபெற் ...... றிடலாமோ","பொருக்கெழக் கடற்பரப் பரக்கர்கொத் திறப்புறப்","பொருப்பினிற் பெருக்கவுற் ...... றிடுமாயம்","புடைத்திடித் தடற்கரத் துறப்பிடித் தகற்பகப்","புரிக்கிரக் கம்வைத்தபொற் ...... கதிர்வேலா","திருத்தமுத் தமிழ்க்கவிக் கொருத்தமைக் குறத்தியைத்","தினைப்புனக் கிரித்தலத் ...... திடைதோயுஞ்","சிவத்தகுக் குடக்கொடிச் செருக்கவுற் பலச்சுனைச்","சிறப்புடைத் திருத்தணிப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of women who laugh softly, melt a man and wipe out his thought with dark eyes, draw him in and take all his wealth, and of men bound to them who boast of a tattva-knowledge found nowhere else; he asks whether he must slip again into this birth, lying down and rising in an instant. The praise names the vel that split the sea and the demons' mountain of illusion and took pity on Indra's city, the hunter girl, the cock banner, and the famous lily pool of Thiruthani.","n":"ஒட்டறப் பொருட் பறிப்பவர், those who strip wealth entirely, are courtesans. The middle lines (தத்துவப் ப்ரசித்தி ... ஞானம் சமைத்துரைத்து) seem to mock those who contrive and proclaim a knowledge of the tattvas, but how they connect to the women, and the sense of the birth line, depend on a word split I am not sure of. உற்பலச் சுனை is the blue-lily pool of Thani.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","piravi","vanjam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:21"},"p:242":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":242,"t":["இருப்பவல் திருப்புகழ் விருப்பொடு படிப்பவர்","இடுக்கினை யறுத்திடு ...... மெனவோதும்","இசைத்தமிழ் நடத்தமி ழெனத்துறை விருப்புட","னிலக்கண இலக்கிய ...... கவிநாலுந்","தரிப்பவ ருரைப்பவர் நினைப்பவர் மிகச்சக","தலத்தினில் நவிற்றுத ...... லறியாதே","தனத்தினில் முகத்தினில் மனத்தினி லுருக்கிடு","சமர்த்திகள் மயக்கினில் ...... விழலாமோ","கருப்புவில் வளைத்தணி மலர்க்கணை தொடுத்தியல்","களிப்புட னொளித்தெய்த ...... மதவேளைக்","கருத்தினில் நினைத்தவ னெருப்பெழ நுதற்படு","கனற்கணி லெரித்தவர் ...... கயிலாயப்","பொருப்பினி லிருப்பவர் பருப்பத வுமைக்கொரு","புறத்தினை யளித்தவர் ...... தருசேயே","புயற்பொழில் வயற்பதி நயப்படு திருத்தணி","பொருப்பினில் விருப்புறு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet recalls that those who read the Thiruppugazh with love are said to be freed of their troubles, and that men keep and recite the four kinds of poetry in music, dance, grammar and literature; without learning to recite it, he asks, must he fall into the delusion of clever women who melt men with breast, face and mind? The praise names Siva who burned Kama with the fire of his forehead eye, lives on Kailasa and gave half of himself to Uma, and Murugan as their son at Thiruthani.","n":"The song names its own book: திருப்புகழ் விருப்பொடு படிப்பவர் இடுக்கினை அறுத்திடும், reading the Thiruppugazh with love cuts affliction. The burning of Kama (மதவேள்) in the praise answers the women of the plea. The opening word இருப்பவல் is unclear to me.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thamizh","kaamam","mayal"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:21"},"p:243":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":243,"t":["இருமலு ரோக முயலகன் வாத","மெரிகுண நாசி ...... விடமேநீ","ரிழிவுவி டாத தலைவலி சோகை","யெழுகள மாலை ...... யிவையோடே","பெருவயி றீளை யெரிகுலை சூலை","பெருவலி வேறு ...... முளநோய்கள்","பிறவிகள் தோறு மெனைநலி யாத","படியுன தாள்கள் ...... அருள்வாயே","வருமொரு கோடி யசுரர்ப தாதி","மடியஅ நேக ...... இசைபாடி","வருமொரு கால வயிரவ ராட","வடிசுடர் வேலை ...... விடுவோனே","தருநிழல் மீதி லுறைமுகி லு஡ர்தி","தருதரு மாதின் ...... மணவாளா","சலமிடை பூவி னடுவினில் வீறு","தணிமலை மேவு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet lists diseases, cough, epilepsy, wind, burning fevers, poison, urinary flux, headache, anaemia, scrofula, dropsy, phlegm and colic, and asks for Murugan's feet so that they do not torment him in birth after birth. The praise names the vel thrown while a crore of asuras died and Bhairavas danced, the husband of the girl given by Indra, and Thani hill with its lotus pool.","n":"The plea is not to be cured now but to be spared the diseases in every birth (பிறவிகள் தோறும்), so the list stands for embodied life itself. முயலகன் is the epilepsy demon of the Siva story.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["noy","piravi","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:21"},"p:244":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":244,"t":["உடலி னு஡டு போய்மீளு முயிரி னு஡டு மாயாத","உணர்வி னு஡டு வானு஡டு ...... முதுதீயூ","டுலவை யூடு நநருடு புவியி னு஡டு வாதாடு","மொருவ ரோடு மேவாத ...... தனிஞானச்","சுடரி னு஡டு நால்வேத முடியி னு஡டு மூடாடு","துரிய வாகு லாதீத ...... சிவ்ருபம்","தொலைவி லாத போராசை துரிச றாத வோர்பேதை","தொடுமு பாய மேதோசொ ...... லருள்வாயே","மடல றாத வா஡ணச அடவி சாடி மாறான","வரிவ ரால்கு வால்சாய ...... அமராடி","மதகு தாவி மீதோடி யுழவ ரால டாதோடி","மடையை மோதி யாறு஡டு ...... தடமாகக்","கடல்பு காம காமீனை முடுகி வாளை தான்மேவு","கமல வாவி மேல்வீழு ...... மலர்வாவிக்","கடவுள் நீல மாறாத தணிகை காவ லாவீர","கருணை மேரு வேதேவர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of the light of lone knowledge that runs through the body, through the life that goes and returns, through awareness, sky, fire, wind, water and earth, through the crown of the four Vedas, the turiya state beyond, the form of Siva that does not join those who argue; and asks the Lord to tell a fool of endless desire and unremoved faults some means of touching it. The praise is a long scene of Thani's fields, where fish leap the sluices and flee the farmers into lotus pools, and names Murugan the guard of Thanigai, the mountain of compassion.","n":"The repeated ஊடு, 'through', builds the pervasion line by line, from body and breath to the five elements and the Veda's end (நால்வேத முடி, Vedanta) to துரிய ... அதீத. வாதாடும் ஒருவரோடும் மேவாத, not joined to any who dispute, sets the truth apart from debate. The plea asks only for an உபாயம், a means. The text has nine corrupt glyphs, but ஊடு is clear throughout.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["viyaabagam","gnaanam","sivam","avaa"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:21"},"p:245":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":245,"t":["உடையவர்க ளேவ ரெவர்களென நாடி","யுளமகிழ ஆசு ...... கவிபாடி","உமதுபுகழ் மேரு கிரியளவு மான","தெனவுரமு மான ...... மொழிபேசி","நடைபழகி மீள வறியவர்கள் நாளை","நடவுமென வாடி ...... முகம்வேறாய்","நலியுமுன மேயு னருணவொளி வீசு","நளினஇரு பாத ...... மருள்வாயே","விடைகொளுவு பாகர் விமலர்திரி சூலர்","விகிர் தர்பர யோகர் ...... நிலவோடே","விளவு சிறு பூளை நகுதலையொ டாறு","விடவரவு சூடு ...... மதிபாரச்","சடையிறைவர் காண உமைமகிழ ஞான","தளர் நடையி டாமுன் ...... வருவோனே","தவமலரு நீல மலர்கனைய நாதி","தணிமலையு லாவு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses that he searched out who the rich were, sang them extempore verses saying their fame was as great as Mount Meru, and walked back and forth until they said 'go, come tomorrow' and turned their faces; he asks for Murugan's two lotus feet before he is worn down. The praise names Siva with bull, trident, moon, vilva, skull, river and serpent in his matted hair, and Murugan who came in the toddling steps of wisdom while Siva watched and Uma rejoiced, at Thani hill of blue lilies.","n":"ஆசுகவி, the quick verse made to order, is the poet's trade, and நாளை நடவும், 'go, tomorrow', its reward. ஞான தளர்நடை, the toddling child of wisdom before Siva and Uma, may point to Sambandar, whom the book identifies with Murugan; the text does not name him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","thamizh","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:21"},"p:246":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":246,"t":["உய்யஞா னத்துநெறி கைவிடா தெப்பொழுது","முள்ளவே தத்துறைகொ ...... டுணர்வோதி","உள்ளமோ கத்திருளை விள்ளமோ கப்பொருளை","யுள்ளமோ கத்தருளி ...... யுறவாகி","வையமே ழுக்குநிலை செய்யுநீ திப்பழைய","வல்லமீ துற்பலச ...... யிலமேவும்","வள்ளியா நிற்புதிய வெள்ளில்தோய் முத்தமுறி","கிள்ளிவீ சுற்றுமலர் ...... பணிவேனோ","பையரா வைப்புனையு மையர்பா கத்தலைவி","துய்யவே ணிப்பகிர ...... திகுமாரா","பையமால் பற்றிவளர் சையமேல் வைக்குமுது","நெய்யனே சுற்றியகு ...... றவர்கோவே","செய்யுமால் வெற்புருவ வெய்யவேல் சுற்றிவிடு","கையமால் வைத்ததிரு ...... மருகோனே","தெய்வயா னைக்கிளைய வெள்ளையா னைத்தலைவ","தெய்வயா னைக்கினிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether, never leaving the path of saving knowledge and reciting through the Veda, so that the darkness of delusion in the heart breaks and grace becomes his kin, he will worship Murugan at the hill of lilies with fresh vilva sprouts and scattered flowers. The praise names Murugan as son of Siva's consort and of Ganga, lord of the hunters, nephew of Vishnu, and plays on Devayanai's name: younger than the divine elephant, lord of the white elephant, dear to Devayanai.","n":"The closing line puns three times on தெய்வ யானை: Ganapati the divine elephant, Indra's white elephant, and the goddess Devayanai. Much of the plea depends on how the rhythmic syllables split (மோகப்பொருளை, பழைய வல்ல), and I offer the reading only in outline.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","gnaanam","vedham"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:21"},"p:247":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":247,"t":["எத்தனைக லாதி சித்தங் கெத்தனைவி யாதி பித்தங்","கெத்தனைச ராச ரத்தின் ...... செடமான","எத்தனைவி டாவெ ருட்டங் கெத்தனைவ லாண்மை பற்றங்","கெத்தனைகொ லு஡னை நித்தம் ...... பசியாறல்","பித்தனைய னான கட்டுண் டிப்படிகெ டாமல் முத்தம்","பெற்றிடநி னாச னத்தின் ...... செயலான","பெற்றியுமொ ராது நிற்குந் த்ததகுரு தார நிற்கும்","பெத்தமுமொ ராது நிற்குங் ...... கழல்தாராய்","தத்தனத னாத னத்தந் தத்தனத னாத னத்தந்","தத்தனத னாத னத்தந் ...... தகுதீதோ","தக்குகுகு டூடு டுட்டுண் டிக்குகுகு டீகு தத்தந்","தத்தனத னான னுர்த்துஞ் ...... சதபேரி","சித்தர்கள்நி டாதர் வெற்பின் கொற்றவர்சு வாமி பத்தர்","திக்குகளொர் நாலி ரட்டின் ...... கிரிசூழச்","செக்கணரி மாக னைக்குஞ் சித்தணிகை வாழ்சி வப்பின்","செக்கர்நிற மாயி ருக்கும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet runs through his bondage: how many arts, diseases, madnesses, inert things moving and unmoving, terrors, powers and attachments, and the daily satisfying of hunger with the flesh of killing; and asks that a madman so bound not perish thus but gain release, for the feet that stand beyond bondage. The praise is drum syllables and Thanigai, ringed by eight mountains, home of siddhas and hunters, red as the evening sky.","n":"முத்தம் is release (mukti) and பெத்தம் bondage, Siddhanta terms set against each other. கொல் ஊனை நித்தம் பசியாறல, eating killed flesh daily, is the one concrete fault in the list. The lines on Murugan's seat (ஆசனத்தின் செயல்) and the garbled த்ததகுரு are beyond me.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["paasam","pulaal","veedu","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:21"},"p:248":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":248,"t":["எலுப்பு நாடிக ளப்பொடி ரத்தமொ","டழுக்கு மூளைகள் மச்சொடு கொட்புழு","விருக்கும் வீடதி லெத்தனை தத்துவ ...... சதிகாரர்","இறப்பர் சூதக வர்ச்சுத ரப்பதி","யுழப்பர் பூமித ரிப்பர்பி றப்புட","னிருப்பர் வீடுகள் கட்டிய லட்டுறு ...... சமுசாரம்","கெலிப்பர் மால்வலை பட்டுறு துட்டர்கள்","அழிப்பர் மாதவ முற்றுநி னைக்கிலர்","கெடுப்பர் யாரையு மித்திர குத்தரர் ...... கொலைகாரர்","கிருத்தர் கோளகர் பெற்றுதி ரிக்கள","வரிப்பர் சூடக ரெத்தனை வெப்பிணி","கெலிக்கும் வீடதை நத்தியெ டுத்திவ ...... ணுழல்வேனோ","ஒலிப்பல் பேரிகை யுக்ரவ மர்க்கள","மெதிர்த்த சூரரை வெட்டியி ருட்கிரி","யுடைத்து வானவர் சித்தர்து தித்திட ...... விடும்வேலா","உலுத்த ராவண னைச்சிர மிற்றிட","வதைத்து மாபலி யைச்சிறை வைத்தவன்","உலக்கை ராவிந டுக்கடல் விட்டவன் ...... மருகோனே","வலிக்க வேதனை குட்டிந டித்தொரு","செகத்தை யீனவள் பச்சைநி றத்தியை","மணத்த தாதைப ரப்ரம ருக்கருள் ...... குருநாதா","வனத்தில் வாழும யிற்குல மொத்திடு","குறத்தி யாரைம யக்கிய ணைத்துள","மகிழ்ச்சி யோடுதி ருத்தணி பற்றிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet calls the body a house of bones, nerves, blood, filth, brain, fat and worms, inhabited by many cheating tattvas, and describes the people who live in such houses, gamblers, householders in samsara, villains in the net of lust who never think of austerity, traitors to friends and killers, beset by fevers; he asks whether he must desire and carry this house and wander. The praise names the vel against Sura, Vishnu who slew Ravana, imprisoned Mahabali and cast the filed pestle into the sea, and Murugan as guru of his father after humbling Brahma, at Thiruthani.","n":"The body is a வீடு, a house, and the tattvas within it சதிகாரர், conspirators. உலக்கை ராவி நடுக்கடல் விட்டவன் recalls the pestle ground to powder and thrown in the sea in the Yadava story. வலிக்க வேதனை குட்டி, knocking Brahma on the head, joins the Om story to பரப்ரமருக்கு அருள் குருநாதா.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","thaththuvam","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:21"},"p:249":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":249,"t":["எனக்கென யாவும் படைத்திட நாளும்","இளைப்பொடு காலந் ...... தனிலோயா","எடுத்திடு காயந் தனைக்கொடு மாயும்","இலச்சையி லாதென் ...... பவமாற","உனைப்பல நாளுந் திருப்புக ழாலும்","உரைத்திடு வார்தங் ...... குளிமேவி","உணர்த்திய போதந் தனைப்பிரி யாதொண்","பொலச்சர ணானுந் ...... தொழுவேனோ","வினைத்திற மோடன் றெதிர்த்திடும் வீரன்","விழக்கொடு வேள்கொன் ...... றவனீயே","விளப்பென மேலென் றிடக்கய னாரும்","விருப்புற வேதம் ...... புகல்வோனே","சினத்தொடு சூரன் தனைக்கொடு வேலின்","சிரத்தினை மாறும் ...... முருகோனே","தினைப்புன மோவுங் குறக்கொடி யோடுந்","திருத்தணி மேவும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses that he has gathered everything for himself, wearied without rest, and that the body he took dies without shame; to end his births he asks to live among those who speak of Murugan daily through the Thiruppugazh and never to part from the knowledge they teach, so that he too may worship his feet. The praise names the slaying of an opponent, the Veda Murugan told that pleased Brahma, the vel that took Sura's head, and the hunter girl, at Thiruthani.","n":"திருப்புகழாலும் உரைத்திடுவார் தங்குழி மேவி asks for the company of Thiruppugazh singers, so the book is again named within itself as a means of grace. The first line of the praise (வேள் கொன்றவன்) and the Brahma line are uncertain to me.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["adiyaar","piravi","yaakkai","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:21"},"p:250":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":250,"t":["எனைய டைந்த குட்டம் வினைமி குந்த பித்த","மெரிவ ழங்கு வெப்பு ...... வலிபோசா","இகலி நின்ற லைக்கு முயல கன்கு லைப்பொ","டிரும லென்று ரைக்கு ...... மிவையோடே","மனைகள் பெண்டிர் மக்கள் தமைநி னைந்து சுத்த","மதிம யங்கி விட்டு ...... மடியாதே","மருவி யின்றே னக்கு மரக தஞ்சி றக்கு","மயிலில் வந்து முத்தி ...... தரவேணும்","நினைவ ணங்கு பத்த ரனைவ ருந்த ழைக்க","நெறியில் நின்ற வெற்றி ...... முனைவேலா","நிலைபெ றுந்தி ருத்த ணியில்வி ளங்கு சித்ர","நெடிய குன்றில் நிற்கு ...... முருகோனே","தினைவி ளங்க லுற்ற புனஇ ளங்கு றத்தி","செயல றிந்த ணைக்கு ...... மணிமார்பா","திசைமு கன்தி கைக்க அசுர ரன்ற டைத்த","சிறைதி றந்து விட்ட ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet names leprosy, bile, fever, pain, epilepsy and cough, and asks not to die with his mind confused by thoughts of house, wife and children, but that Murugan come today on the emerald peacock and give him release. The praise names the vel that stands on the path to protect devotees, the chest that embraces the hunter girl, and Murugan who opened the prison where the asuras held the gods, at Thiruthani.","n":"The disease list joins the family list: மனைகள் பெண்டிர் மக்கள் தமை நினைந்து, and both are what the mind should not be clouded by at the end. முத்தி தரவேணும் asks for release directly.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["noy","pattru","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:21"},"p:251":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":251,"t":["ஏது புத்திஐ யாஎ னக்கினி","யாரை நத்திடு வேன வத்தினி","லேயி றத்தல்கொ லோஎ னக்குனி ...... தந்தைதாயென்","றேயி ருக்கவு நானு மிப்படி","யேத வித்திட வோச கத்தவ","ரேச லிற்பட வோந கைத்தவர் ...... கண்கள்காணப்","பாதம் வைத்திடை யாதே ரித்தெனை","தாளில் வைக்கநி யேம றுத்திடில்","பார்ந கைக்குமை யாத கப்பன்முன் ...... மைந்தனோடிப்","பால்மொ ழிக்குர லோல மிட்டிடில்","யாரெ டுப்பதெ னாவெ றுத்தழ","பார்வி டுப்பர்க ளோஎ னக்கிது ...... சிந்தியாதோ","ஓத முற்றெழு பால்கொ தித்தது","போல எட்டிகை நீசமுட்டரை","யோட வெட்டிய பாநு சத்திகை ...... யெங்கள்கோவே","ஓத மொய்ச்சடை யாட வுற்றமர்","மான்ம ழுக்கர மாட பொற்கழ","லோசை பெற்றிட வேந டித்தவர் ...... தந்தவாழ்வே","மாதி னைப்புண மீதி ருக்குமை","வாள்வி ழிக்குற மாதி னைத்தரு","மார்ப ணைத்தம யூர அற்புத ...... கந்தவேளே","மாரன் வெற்றிகொள் பூமு டிக்குழ","லார்வி யப்புற நீடு மெய்த்தவர்","வாழ்தி ருத்தணி மாம லைப்பதி ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet argues with Murugan as a son: what wisdom has he, whom else can he seek, must he die in vain; Murugan is father and mother to him, and if he refuses to set his feet on him the world will laugh, for when a child runs crying before its father, who else picks it up, and would a father leave it to cry? The praise names the vel that drove off the villains of the eight directions like milk boiling over, Siva who danced with deer and axe, and Valli, at Thiruthani.","n":"The plea is a lawyer's argument from kinship: பார் நகைக்கும், the world will laugh, if a father abandons his crying son. The grace is claimed as a father's duty, not begged as a favour.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","thiruvadi","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:21"},"p:252":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":252,"t":["ஓலை யிட்டகு ழைச்சிகள் சித்திர","ரூப மொத்தநி றத்திகள் விற்கணை","யோடி ணைத்தவி ழிச்சிகள் சர்க்கரை ...... யமுதோடே","ஊறி யொத்தமொ ழிச்சிகள் புட்குர","லோடு வைத்துமி ழற்றுமி டற்றிகள்","ஓசை பெற்றது டிக்கொளி டைச்சிகள் ...... மணம்வீசும்","மாலை யிட்டக ழுத்திகள் முத்தணி","வார ழுத்துத னத்திகள் குத்திர","மால்வி ளைத்தும னத்தைய ழித்திடு ...... மடமாதர்","மார்ப சைத்தும ருட்டியி ருட்டறை","வாவெ னப்பொருள் பற்றிமு யக்கிடு","மாத ருக்குவ ருத்தமி ருப்பது ...... தணியாதோ","வேலை வற்றிட நற்கணை தொட்டலை","மீத டைத்துத னிப்படை விட்டுற","வீற ரக்கன்மு டித்தலை பத்தையு ...... மலைபோலே","மீத றுத்திநி லத்தில டித்துமெய்","வேத லக்ஷுமி யைச்சிறை விட்டருள்","வீர அச்சுத னுக்குந லற்புத ...... மருகோனே","நீலி நிட்களி நிர்க்குணி நித்தில","வாரி முத்துந கைக்கொடி சித்திர","நீல ரத்தின மிட்டஅ றக்கிளி ...... புதல்வோனே","நீற திட்டுநி னைப்பவர் புத்தியில்","நேச மெத்தஅ ளித்தருள் சற்குரு","நீல முற்றதி ருத்தணி வெற்புறை ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women with palm-leaf earrings, picture-like colour, arrow eyes, sugared speech, bird voices, drum waists, garlands and pearl-hung breasts, whose deceitful delusion destroys the mind, who call a man into a dark room and, seizing his wealth, embrace him, and asks whether his suffering over them will not ease. The praise tells how Rama dried the sea, cut down Ravana's ten heads and freed Lakshmi from prison, names Murugan as his nephew and as son of Uma the parrot of dharma, the true guru who gives love to those who wear the ash, at Thiruthani.","n":"இருட்டறை வாவெனப் பொருள் பற்றி முயக்கிடு, 'come into the dark room', seizing wealth and embracing, is the Kural's image of the courtesan's embrace almost word for word. நீறிட்டு நினைப்பவர், those who wear the ash and think of him, are the ones the true guru blesses.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","mayal","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:21"},"p:253":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":253,"t":["கச்சணி யிளமுலை முத்தணி பலவகை","கைச்சரி சொலிவர ...... மயல்கூறிக்","கைப்பொருள் கவர்தரு மைப்பயில் விழியினர்","கட்செவி நிகரல்குல் ...... மடமாதர்","இச்சையி னுருகிய கச்சைய னறிவிலி","யெச்சமி லொருபொரு ...... ளறியேனுக்","கிப்புவி மிசைகமழ் பொற்பத மலரிணை","யிப்பொழு தணுகவு ...... னருள்தாராய்","கொச்சையர் மனையிலி டைச்சியர் தயிர்தனை","நச்சியெ திருடிய ...... குறையால்வீழ்","குற்கிர வினியொடு நற்றிற வகையறி","கொற்றவு வணமிசை ...... வருகேசன்","அச்சுதை நிறைகடல் நச்சர வணைதுயில்","அச்சுதன் மகிழ்தரு ...... மருகோனே","அப்பணி சடையரன் மெச்சிய தணிமலை","யப்பனெ யழகிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women with bodiced breasts, pearls and jingling bangles who speak of infatuation and take the wealth in a man's hand, and calls himself a slave of desire, melted and ignorant, who does not know the one thing that has no remainder; he asks for Murugan's fragrant feet now. The praise names Krishna who stole curd from the cowherd women and came on Garuda, and Vishnu who sleeps on the serpent as uncle, at Thani hill praised by Siva.","n":"கைப்பொருள் கவர்தரு, taking the wealth in hand, marks the courtesans. எச்சமில் ஒருபொருள், the one thing without remainder, names what the poet does not know against what the women take. The line on the bird (குற்கிர வினியொடு) is unclear to me.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","pedhaimai","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:21"},"p:254":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":254,"t":["கடற்செகத் தடக்கிமற் றடுத்தவர்க் கிடுக்கணைக்","கடைக்கணிற் கொடுத்தழைத் ...... தியல்காமக்","கலைக்கதற் றுரைத்துபுட் குரற்கள்விட் டுளத்தினைக்","கரைத்துடுத் தபட்டவிழ்த் ...... தணைமீதே","சடக்கெனப் புகத்தனத் தணைத்திதழ்க் கொடுத்துமுத்","தமிட்டிருட் குழற்பிணித் ...... துகிரேகை","சளப்படப் புதைத்தடித் திலைக்குணக் கடித்தடத்","தலத்தில்வைப் பவர்க்கிதப் ...... படுவேனோ","இடக்கடக் குமெய்ப்பொருட் டிருப்புகழ்க் குயிர்ப்பளித்","தெழிற்றினைக் கிரிப்புறத் ...... துறைவேலா","இகற்செருக் கரக்கரைத் தகர்த்தொலித் துரத்தபச்","சிறைச்சியைப் பசித்திரைக் ...... கிசைகூவும்","பெடைத்திரட் களித்தகுக் குடக்கொடிக் கரத்தபொய்ப்","பிதற்றறப் படுத்துசற் ...... குருவாய்முன்","பிறப்பிலிக் குணர்த்துசித் தவுற்றநெற் பெருக்குவைப்","பெருக்குமெய்த் திருத்தணிப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women who trouble those who come near with a sidelong glance, call them, speak the arts of love, melt the heart with bird cries, loosen their silk, take a man to bed, kiss, bind him in their hair, mark him with nails and teeth and keep him in their laps, and asks whether he must delight in them. The praise names Murugan who gave life to the Thiruppugazh that tames adversity, the vel of the millet hill, the cock banner, and the true guru who taught the Unborn Siva and ended false babble, at Thiruthani of paddy heaps.","n":"மெய்ப்பொருள் திருப்புகழ்க்கு உயிர்ப்பளித்து makes Murugan the giver of the book's life, the third song of the chapter to name it. பிறப்பிலிக்கு உணர்த்து சற்குரு, the true guru who taught the Unborn, is the Siva-teaching story without naming Om.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","kaamam","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:21"},"p:255":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":255,"t":["கரிக்குழல் விரித்தும் புறக்கயல் விழித்துங்","கரிக்குவ டிணைக்குந் ...... தனபாரக்","கரத்திடு வளைச்சங் கிலிச்சர மொலித்துங்","கலைத்துகில் மினுக்யும் ...... பணிவாரைத்","தரித்துள மழிக்குங் கவட்டர்க ளிணக்கந்","தவிர்த்துனது சித்தங் ...... களிகூரத்","தவக்கடல் குளித்திங் குனக்கடி மையுற்றுன்","தலத்தினி லிருக்கும் ...... படிபாராய்","புரத்தையு மெரித்தங் கயத்தையு முரித்தொண்","பொடிப்பணி யெனப்பன் ...... குருநாதா","புயப்பணி கடப்பந் தொடைச்சிக ரமுற்றின்","புகழ்ச்சிய முதத்திண் ...... புலவோனே","திரட்பரி கரிக்கும் பொடிப்பட வுணர்க்குந்","தெறிப்புற விடுக்குங் ...... கதிர்வேலா","சிறப்பொடு குறப்பெண் களிக்கும்வி சயத்தென்","திருத்தணி யிருக்கும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women who loosen their dark hair, glance with fish eyes, jingle bangles and chains and shimmer their garments, and the deceitful ones (கவட்டர்) who bow only to destroy the heart; he asks Murugan to look on him so that, free of their company, he may bathe in the sea of austerity, become his servant and live in his shrine with a glad mind. The praise names Murugan the guru of his father Siva, who burnt the cities, flayed the elephant and wears ash, the poet of nectar-praise in a kadamba garland, and the vel that dusts the demons' horses and elephants, at Thiruttani where the hunter girl rejoices.","n":"உனக்கு அடிமையுற்று உன் தலத்தினில் இருக்கும்படி: the remedy asked is to live at the shrine itself as the Lord's servant, a concrete hope beside the inward bath in தவக்கடல். எனப்பன் குருநாதா calls Murugan 'guru of my father', the poet claiming Siva as his own father.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","vanjam","thondu","thavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:22"},"p:256":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":256,"t":["கலைமட வார்தஞ் சிலையத னாலுங்","கனவளை யாலுங் ...... கரைமேலே","கருகிய காளம் பெருகிய தோயங்","கருதலை யாலுஞ் ...... சிலையாலுங்","கொலைதரு காமன் பலகணை யாலுங்","கொடியிடை யாள்நின் ...... றழியாதே","குரவணி நீடும் புமணி நீபங்","குளிர்தொடை நீதந் ...... தருள்வாயே","சிலைமகள் நாயன் கலைமகள் நாயன்","திருமகள் நாயன் ...... தொழும்வேலா","தினைவன மானுங் கநவன மானுஞ்","செறிவுடன் மேவுந் ...... திருமார்பா","தலமகள் மீதெண் புலவரு லாவுந்","தணிகையில் வாழ்செங் ...... கதிர்வேலா","தனியவர் கூருந் தனிகெட நாளுந்","தனிமயி லேறும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"In the voice of one watching over a lovesick girl, the singer asks that the creeper-waisted girl not perish under the women's taunts and the noise of bangles, the dark cloud and swelling water on the shore, and the many killing arrows of Kama, and that Murugan graciously give her his cool garland of kura and kadamba flowers. The praise names the vel that the lords of Uma, Saraswati and Lakshmi worship, the chest where Valli of the millet field and the heavenly deer Devayanai dwell, and the lord who rides his peacock daily at Thanigai so that the loneliness of the lonely ends.","n":"The request for the god's garland is the akam convention of the girl asking for her lover's token. கலைமடவார் தஞ் சிலை may be the women's gossip (சிலை as sound) or their bow-like brows, so whether this is அலர் is uncertain. தனியவர் கூருந் தனிகெட: the god rides out to end the solitude of the lonely, which turns the praise back to the girl.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","alar","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:22"},"p:257":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":257,"t":["கவடுற்ற சித்தர்சட் சமயப்ர மத்தர்நற்","கடவுட்ப்ர திஷ்டைபற் ...... பலவாகக்","கருதிப்பெ யர்க்குறித் துருவர்க்க மிட்டிடர்க்","கருவிற்பு கப்பகுத் ...... துழல்வானேன்","சவடிக்கி லச்சினைக் கிருகைச்ச ரிக்குமிக்","கசரப்ப ளிக்கெனப் ...... பொருள்தேடி","சகலத்து மொற்றைபட் டயல்பட்டு நிற்குநிற்","சரணப்ர சித்திசற் ...... றுணராரோ","குவட்டெட்டு மட்டுநெட் டுவரிக்க ணத்தினைக்","குமுறக்க லக்கிவிக் ...... ரமசூரன்","குடலைப்பு யத்திலிட் டுடலைத்த றித்துருத்","துதிரத்தி னிற்குளித் ...... தெழும்வேலா","சுவடுற்ற அற்புதக் கவலைப்பு னத்தினிற்","றுவலைச்சி மிழ்த்துநிற் ...... பவள்நாணத்","தொழுதெத்து முத்தபொற் புரிசைச்செ ருத்தணிச்","சுருதித்த மிழ்க்கவிப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks why the deceitful siddhas and those confused by the six sects, who consecrate many gods, fix names and set up images, and seek wealth for necklaces, signet rings, bangles and chains, should wander dividing themselves only to enter the womb again; will they not understand a little the fame of the Lord's feet, which stand one with everything and yet apart? The praise is of the vel that churned the eight peaks and the sea, cut Surapadman and bathed in his blood, of Valli bowing in shame in the millet field, and of Thiruttani with its golden walls, lord of Sruti and Tamil poetry.","n":"சகலத்தும் ஒற்றைபட்டு அயல்பட்டு நிற்கும்: the Lord is at once one with all things and other than them, the doctrine of pervasion with transcendence, set against the sects' many installed gods. The jewels named (சவடி, இலச்சினை, சரி, சரப்பளி) make the sectarians' religion a trade. Whether the wanderer of the first lines is the poet or the sectarians depends on how உழல்வானேன் is taken.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["samayam","viyaabagam","piravi","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:22"},"p:258":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":258,"t":["கனத்தறப் பணைத்தபொற் கழைப்புயத் தனக்கிரிக்","கனத்தையொத் துமொய்த்தமைக் ...... குழலார்தங்","கறுத்தமைக் கயற்கணிற் கருத்துவைத் தொருத்தநிற்","கழற்பதத் தடுத்திடற் ...... கறியாதே","இனப்பிணிக் கணத்தினுக் கிருப்பெனத் துருத்தியொத்","திசைத்தசைத் தசுக்கிலத் ...... தசைதோலால்","எடுத்தபொய்க் கடத்தினைப் பொறுக்குமிப் பிறப்பறுத்","தெனக்குநித் தமுத்தியைத் ...... தரவேணும்","பனைக்கரச் சினத்திபத் தனைத்துரத் தரக்கனைப்","பயத்தினிற் பயப்படப் ...... பொரும்வேலா","பருப்பதச் செருக்கறத் துகைக்குமுட் பதத்தினைப்","படைத்தகுக் குடக்கொடிக் ...... குமரேசா","தினைப்புனப் பருப்பதத் தினிற்குடிக் குறத்தியைச்","செருக்குறத் திருப்புயத் ...... தணைவோனே","திருப்புரப் புறத்தியற் றிருத்தகுத் துநித்திலத்","திருத்திசைத் திருத்தணிப் பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"Not knowing how to draw near the anklet feet, his mind on women with heavy breasts and dark kohl eyes, the poet asks Murugan to cut off this birth that bears the false pot of flesh, skin and semen, a bellows that is home to the troop of diseases, and to give him release every day. The praise names the vel that fought the elephant-borne demon, the Kumaresa of the cock banner whose feet trample the pride of mountains, and the embrace of the hunter girl, at Thiruttani of pearls.","n":"இனப்பிணிக் கணத்தினுக்கு இருப்பு, துருத்தி, பொய்க் கடம்: three images of the body in one line, a lodging for diseases, a bellows of breath, a false pot. The plea asks for release daily (நித்தம் முத்தி), as if it must be renewed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","piravi","veedu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:22"},"p:259":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":259,"t":["கனைத்த திர்க்குமிப் பொங்கு கார்க்கட ...... லொன்றினாலே","கறுத்த றச்சிவத் தங்கி வாய்த்தெழு ...... திங்களாலே","தனிக்க ருப்புவிற் கொண்டு வீழ்த்தச ...... ரங்களாலே","தகைத்தொ ருத்தியெய்த் திங்கு யாக்கைச ...... ழங்கலாமோ","தினைப்பு னத்தினைப் பண்டு காத்தம ...... டந்தைகேள்வா","திருத்த ணிப்பதிக் குன்றின் மேற்றிகழ் ...... கந்தவேளே","பனைக்க ரக்கயத் தண்டர் போற்றிய ...... மங்கைபாகா","படைத்த ளித்தழிக் குந்த்ரி மூர்த்திகள் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"A short love lament: the singer asks whether, because of the roaring dark sea, the moon that rises red as fire, and the arrows Kama shoots from his sugarcane bow, a girl should be stopped here and her body grow slack. The praise names Murugan the husband of the girl who once guarded the millet, Kandavel shining on the hill of Thiruttani, and the lord of the three gods who create, protect and destroy.","n":"The torments are the classical night machinery, sea, moon and Kama's cane bow, as in 24; the moon is called hot (சிவத்து அங்கி வாய்த்து எழு திங்கள்), the cool moon turned to fire by longing. ஒருத்தி is in the third person, so the speaker is a mother or friend. பனைக்கரக் கயத்து அண்டர் போற்றிய மங்கைபாகா is hard to place: it may name Siva or Murugan.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","melivu","maalai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:22"},"p:260":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":260,"t":["கிரியு லாவிய முலைமிசை துகிலிடு","கபட நாடக விரகிக ளசடிகள்","கெடுவி யாதிக ளடைவுடை யுடலினர் ...... விரகாலே","க்ருபையி னாரொடு மணமிசை நழுவிகள்","முழுது நாறிக ளிதமொழி வசனிகள்","கிடையின் மேல்மன முருகிட தழுவிகள் ...... பொருளாலே","பரிவி லாமயல் கொடுசமர் புரிபவர்","அதிக மாவொரு பொருள்தரு பவரொடு","பழைய பேரென இதமுற அணைபவர் ...... விழியாலே","பகழி போல்விடு வினைகவர் திருடிகள்","தமையெ ணாவகை யுறுகதி பெரும்வகை","பகர மாமயில் மிசைவர நினைவது ...... மொருநாளே","அரிய ராதிபர் மலரய னிமையவர்","நிலைபெ றாதிடர் படவுடன் முடுகியெ","அசுரர் தூள்பட அயில்தொடு மறுமுக ...... இளையோனே","அரிய கானக முறைகுற மகளிட","கணவ னாகிய அறிவுள விதரண","அமரர் நாயக சரவண பவதிற ...... லுடையோனே","தரும நீதியர் மறையுளர் பொறையுளர்","சரிவு றாநிலை பெருதவ முடையவர்","தளர்வி லாமன முடையவ ரறிவினர் ...... பரராஜர்","சகல லோகமு முடையவர் நினைபவர்","பரவு தாமரை மலரடி யினிதுற","தணிகை மாமலை மணிமுடி யழகியல் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet lists the courtesans: actresses of deceit who drop their garments, bodies full of disease, sweet talkers who embrace on the bed until the heart melts for money, who fight with loveless infatuation, embrace whoever gives more as if he were an old friend, and steal like arrows with their eyes; he asks whether a day will come when, not thinking of them, he thinks of Murugan coming on the peacock to tell him the way to the state of release. The praise names the vel that dusted the demons who harried Vishnu, Brahma and the gods, the husband of the hunter girl, and the lord at Thanigai whose lotus feet are reached by those of dharma, Veda, patience, unwavering austerity and wisdom.","n":"அதிகமா ஒரு பொருள் தருபவரொடு பழைய பேரென இதமுற அணைபவர்: the courtesan treats the highest payer as an old friend, the stock charge of money for love. The praise ends with a long list of who reach the feet, the devotees' virtues answering the women's vices.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","selvam","adiyaar"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:22"},"p:261":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":261,"t":["கிறிமொழிக் கிருதரைப் பொறிவழிச் செறிஞரைக்","கெடுபிறப் பறவிழிக் ...... கிறபார்வைக்","கெடுமடக் குருடரைத் திருடரைச் சமயதர்க்","கிகள்தமைச் செறிதலுற் ...... றறிவேதும்","அறிதலற் றயர்தலுற் றவிழ்தலற் றருகலுற்","றறவுநெக் கழிகருக் ...... கடலு஡டே","அமிழ்தலற் றெழுதலுற் றுணர்நலத் துயர்தலுற்","றடியிணைக் கணுகிடப் ...... பெறுவேனோ","பொறியுடைச் செழியன்வெப் பொழிதரப் பறிதலைப்","பொறியிலச் சமணரத் ...... தனைபேரும்","பொடிபடச் சிவமணப் பொடிபரப் பியதிருப்","புகலியிற் கவுணியப் ...... புலவோனே","தறிவளைத் துறநகைப் பொறியெழப் புரமெரித்","தவர்திருப் புதல்வநற் ...... சுனைமேவுந்","தனிமணக் குவளைநித் தமுமலர்த் தருசெருத்","தணியினிற் சரவணப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether, having fallen in with liars, followers of the senses, blind fools whose eyes do not look to end birth, thieves and sectarian disputants, knowing nothing, weary and sinking in the sea of the womb, he will rise in understanding and reach the paired feet. The praise identifies Murugan with Sambandar, the poet of the Kaundinya line at Pugali who spread holy ash to cure the Pandyan's fever and crushed the Jains, and names him the son of Siva who burnt the cities with a laugh, lord at Seruttani where the kuvalai blooms daily in the spring.","n":"The plea turns on company: the bad are named in a string of types (கிருதர், பொறிவழிச் செறிஞர், குருடர், திருடர், சமயதர்க்கிகள்), and to keep company with them is already to sink. The praise is Sambandar's story told of Murugan: the Pandyan (செழியன்) freed of fever by holy ash at Madurai and the shaven-headed Jains (பறிதலைப் சமணர்) defeated.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["inam","samayam","piravi","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:22"},"p:262":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":262,"t":["குயிலொன் றுமொழிக் குயினின் றலையக்","கொலையின் பமலர்க் ...... கணையாலே","குளிருந் தவளக் குலசந்த் ரவொளிக்","கொடிகொங் கையின்முத் ...... தனலாலே","புயல்வந் தெறியக் கடனின் றலறப்","பொருமங் கையருக் ...... கலராலே","புயமொன் றமிகத் தளர்கின் றதனிப்","புயம்வந் தணையக் ...... கிடையாதோ","சயிலங் குலையத் தடமுந் தகரச்","சமனின் றலையப் ...... பொரும்வீரா","தருமங் கைவனக் குறமங் கையர்மெய்த்","தனமொன் றுமணித் ...... திருமார்பா","பயிலுங் ககனப் பிறைதண் பொழிலிற்","பணியுந் தணிகைப் ...... பதிவாழ்வே","பரமன் பணியப் பொருளன் றருளிற்","பகர்செங் கழநிப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A love lament: the cuckoo torments the cuckoo-voiced girl, Kama's flower arrows kill, the cool white moon and the fire of her breasts burn, the wind and the roaring sea and the women's gossip assail her, and her arm grows weak; the singer asks whether she may not be given Murugan's own arm to embrace. The praise names the hero who fought until the mountain shook, the chest that joins the hunter girl's breast, Thanigai where the crescent bows in the cool groves, and Murugan of Sengazhani who told the meaning to the Supreme when he bowed.","n":"பொருமங்கையர் உக்கு அலர்: the women's gossip is one of the torments, alongside cuckoo, moon and sea. பரமன் பணியப் பொருள் அன்று அருளிற் பகர்: Siva bowed and Murugan told him the meaning, the teaching of 6 and 24, here without naming Om. The speaker may be the girl or one who watches her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","alar","melivu","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:22"},"p:263":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":263,"t":["குருவி யெனப்பல கழுகு நரித்திரள்","அரிய வனத்திடை மிருக மெனப்புழு","குறவை யெனக்கரி மரமு மெனத்திரி ...... யுறவாகா","குமரி கலித்துறை முழுகி மனத்துயர்","கொடுமை யெனப்பிணி கலக மிடத்திரி","குலைய னெனப்புலை கலிய னெனப்பலர் ...... நகையாமல்","மருவு புயத்திடை பணிக ளணப்பல","கரிபரி சுற்றிட கலைகள் தரித்தொரு","மதன சரக்கென கனக பலக்குட ...... னதுதேடேன்","வரிய பதத்தினி னருவி யிருப்பிடம்","அமையு மெனக்கிட முனது பதச்சரண்","மருவு திருப்புக ழருள எனக்கினி ...... யருள்வாயே","விருது தனத்தன தனன தனத்தன","விதமி திமித்திமி திமித திமித்திமி","விகிர்த டடுட்டுடு ரிரிரி யெனக்குகு ...... வெகுதாளம்","வெருவ முகிழ்த்திசை யுரகன் முடித்தலை","நெறுநெ றெனத்திசை யதிர அடைத்திட","மிகுதி கெடப்பொரு அசுரர் தெறித்திட ...... விடும்வேலா","அரிய திரிப்புர மெரிய விழித்தவன்","அயனை முடித்தலை யரியு மழுக்கையன்","அகில மனைத்தையு முயிரு மளித்தவ ...... னருள்சேயே","அமண ருடற்கெட வசியி லழுத்திவி","ணமரர் கொடுத்திடு மரிவை குறத்தியொ","டழகு திருத்தணி மலையில் நடித்தருள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks not to wander born as sparrow, kite, jackal, forest beast, worm, fish, elephant and tree, nor to bathe at Kumari with a sorrowing mind and be mocked by many as a diseased wretch; he will not seek ornaments, elephants, horses, silks and golden vessels, and asks instead that, as Murugan's feet suffice for his dwelling, he be given the Thiruppugazh that dwells in them. The praise, after drum syllables of battle, names Siva who burnt the cities with his eye and cut Brahma's head, and Murugan who impaled the Jains and dances at Thiruttani with Valli and Devayanai.","n":"The list of creatures reads as the births the poet fears (திரி உறவாகா), though it may be a list of insults the crowd would throw. உனது பதச்சரண் மருவு திருப்புகழ்: the poet asks for his own book's name, praise that rests in the feet. அமணர் உடற்கெட வசியில் அழுத்தி is again the impaling of the Jains, Sambandar's story told of Murugan, as in 261. The lines on the feet and the waterfall (வரிய பதத்தினில் நருவி) are hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","selvam","pugazh","theerththam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:22"},"p:264":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":264,"t":["குலைத்து மயிர்க் கலைத்து வளைக்","கழுத்து மணித் தனப்பு ரளக்","குவித்த விழிக் கயற்சு ழலப் ...... பிறைபோலக்","குனித்த நுதற் புரட்டி நகைத்","துருக்கி மயற் கொளுத்தி யிணைக்","குழைச்செ வியிற் றழைப்ப பொறித் ...... தனபாரப்","பொலித்து மதத் தரித்த கரிக்","குவட்டு முலைப் பளப்ப ளெனப்","புனைத்த துகிற் பிடித்த இடைப் ...... பொதுமாதர்","புயத்தில் வளைப் பிலுக்கில் நடைக்","குலுக்கி லறப் பசப்பி மயற்","புகட்டி தவத் தழிப்ப வருக் ...... குறவாமோ","தலத்த நுவைக் குனித்தொ ருமுப்","புரத்தை விழக் கொளுத்தி மழுத்","தரித்து புலிக் கரித்து கிலைப் ...... பரமாகத்","தரித்து தவச் சுரர்க்கண் முதற்","பிழைக்க மிடற் றடக்கு விடச்","சடைக்க டவுட் சிறக்க பொருட் ...... பகர்வோனே","சிலுத்த சுரர்க் கெலித்து மிகக்","கொளுத்தி மறைத் துதிக்க அதிற்","செழிக்க அருட் கொடுத்த மணிக் ...... கதிர்வேலா","தினைப்பு னமிற் குறத்தி மகட்","டனத்தின் மயற் குளித்து மகிழ்த்","திருத்த ணியிற் றரித்த புகழ்ப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether it is fit to be kin to the common women who loosen their hair, roll their gemmed breasts, whirl their fish eyes, arch their crescent brows, laugh, kindle infatuation, sway as they walk and, feeding his desire, destroy his austerity. The praise names Siva who bent the bow and burnt the three cities, wore the axe and the tiger and elephant skins, and held the poison in his throat so the gods survived, and calls Murugan the one who told the meaning to that matted-haired god, and the hero bathed in infatuation for the hunter girl at Thiruttani.","n":"பொதுமாதர்: common women, but money is not named, so the charge is desire, not trade. தவத்து அழிப்பவர்: what they destroy is austerity. சடைக் கடவுட் சிறக்கப் பொருட் பகர்வோனே is the teaching of Siva, without naming Om.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","mayal","thavam","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:22"},"p:265":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":265,"t":["குவளைக் கணைதொட் டவனுக் குமுடிக்","குடையிட் டகுறைப் ...... பிறையாலே","குறுகுற் றஅலர்த் தெரிவைக் குமொழிக்","குயிலுக் குமினித் ...... தளராதே","இவளைத் துவளக் கலவிக் குநயக்","திறுகத் தழுவிப் ...... புயமீதெ","இணையற் றழகிற் புனையக் கருணைக்","கினிமைத் தொடையைத் ...... தரவேணும்","கவளக் கரடக் கரியெட் டலறக்","கனகக் கிரியைப் ...... பொரும்வேலா","கருதிச் செயலைப் புயனுக் குருகிக்","கலவிக் கணயத் ...... தெழுமார்பா","பவளத் தரளத் திரளக் குவைவெற்","பவையொப் புவயற் ...... புறமீதே","பணிலத் திரள்மொய்த் ததிருத் தணிகைப்","பதியிற் குமரப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A love lament: the singer asks that this girl not weaken under the crescent moon, the umbrella-crown of Kama who shot the kuvalai arrow, and under the cuckoo, and that Murugan, who has drawn her close and embraced her shoulders in love, give her his sweet garland of compassion to wear in peerless beauty. The praise names the vel that fought the golden mountain until the eight elephants cried, the chest that rises to embrace the girl, and Thiruttani where heaps of coral and pearls lie like hills by the fields and conches crowd.","n":"இவளை: 'this girl', so the voice is a mother or friend pleading for her. The garland is called கருணைக்கு இனிமைத் தொடை, a garland of compassion: the love token is also grace. The crescent as Kama's royal umbrella (குடை) is a striking image.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","melivu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:22"},"p:266":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":266,"t":["கூந்தல விழ்த்துமு டித்துமி னுக்கிகள்","பாய்ந்தவி ழிக்குமை யிட்டுமி ரட்டிகள்","கோம்புப டைத்தமொ ழிச்சொல்ப ரத்தையர் ...... புயமீதே","கோங்குப டைத்தத னத்தைய ழுத்திகள்","வாஞ்சையு றத்தழு விச்சிலு கிட்டவர்","கூன்பிறை யொத்தந கக்குறி வைப்பவர் ...... பலநாளும்","ஈந்தபொ ருட்பெற இச்சையு ரைப்பவ","ராந்துணை யற்றழு கைக்குர லிட்டவ","஡ணங்கிசை யுற்றவ லக்குண மட்டைகள் ...... பொருள்தீரில்","ஏங்கியி டக்கடை யிற்றளி வைப்பவர்","பாங்கக லக்கரு ணைக்கழல் பெற்றிட","ஈந்திலை யெப்படி நற்கதி புக்கிட ...... லருள்வாயே","காந்தள்ம லர்த்தொடை யிட்டெதிர் விட்டொரு","வேந்துகு ரக்கர ணத்தொடு மட்டிடு","காண்டிப அச்சுத னுத்தம சற்குணன் ...... மருகோனே","காங்கிசை மிக்கம றக்கொடி வெற்றியில்","வாங்கிய முக்கனி சர்க்கரை மொக்கிய","கான்கனி முற்கியல் கற்பக மைக்கரி ...... யிளையோனே","தேந்தினை வித்தின ருற்றிட வெற்றிலை","வேங்கைம ரத்தெழி லைக்கொடு நிற்பவ","தேன்சொலி யைப்புண ரப்புன முற்றுறை ...... குவைவானந்","தீண்டுக ழைத்திர ளுற்றது துற்றிடு","வேங்கைத னிற்குவ ளைச்சுனை சுற்றலர்","சேர்ந்ததி ருத்தணி கைப்பதி வெற்புறை ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes courtesans who loosen and retie their hair, paint their eyes and deal double, press their breasts on a man's shoulders, leave crescent nail-marks, speak desire for many days to get the money given, cry as if forsaken, and when the money ends leave him groaning at the door; he asks how, without the compassionate feet that would take him from their side, he can enter the good state. The praise names Murugan nephew of Achyuta of the Gandiva and of Rama with the monkey army, younger brother of the elephant-faced god who ate fruits and sugar, and the one who stood as the vengai tree to unite with honey-voiced Valli, at the Thiruttani hill of sugarcane and kuvalai springs.","n":"பொருள்தீரில் ஏங்கியிடக் கடையில் தளிவைப்பவர்: the man left groaning at the door once his money ends, the formula of 33. ஈந்திலை: 'you have not given', a reproach to the Lord, not only a plea. In the praise Vishnu is named as Krishna with Arjuna's bow (காண்டிப அச்சுதன்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["varaivinmagalir","selvam","vanjam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:22"},"p:267":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":267,"t":["கூர்வேல் பழித்தவிழி யாலே மருட்டிமுலை","கோடா லழைத்துமல ...... ரணைமீதே","கோபா விதழ்ப்பருக மார்போ டணைத்துகணை","கோல்போல் சுழற்றியிடை ...... யுடைநாணக்","கார்போல் குழற்சரிய வேவா யதட்டியிரு","காதோ லையிற்றுவிழ ...... விளையாடுங்","காமா மயர்க்கியர்க ளூடே களித்துநம","கானு஡ ருறைக்கலக ...... மொழியாதோ","வீராணம் வெற்றிமுர சோடே தவிற்றிமிலை","வேதா கமத்தொலிகள் ...... கடல்போல","வீறாய் முழக்கவரு சூரா ரிறக்கவிடும்","வேலா திருத்தணியி ...... லுறைவோனே","மாரோ னிறக்கநகை தாதா திருச்செவியில்","மாபோ தகத்தையருள் ...... குருநாதா","மாலோ னளித்தவளி யார்மால் களிப்பவெகு","மாலோ டனைத்துமகிழ் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women who bewilder with eyes sharper than spears, call with their breasts, draw a man to the flower bed, drink from his lips, loosen their hair and scold while their ear ornaments fall in play, and asks whether the strife of dwelling rejoicing among these infatuating women, as in Yama's forest-town, will not end. The praise names the vel that killed Sura amid drums and the sound of Vedas and Agamas, the guru who gave the great teaching in the holy ear of Siva who burnt Kama with a laugh, and the lord who embraces Valli, given by Vishnu, at Thiruttani.","n":"நம கானூர் உறைக் கலகம் is uncertain: it may be the turmoil of dwelling in Yama's town (நமன் கானூர்) or be split otherwise. தாதா திருச்செவியில் மாபோதகத்தை அருள் குருநாதா: the teaching whispered in Siva's ear, the Om story, though Om is not named. மாலோன் அளித்த வளி makes Valli Vishnu's daughter.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["kaamam","punarchi","mayal","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:22"},"p:268":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":268,"t":["கொந்து வார்குர வடியினு மடியவர்","சிந்தை வாரிஜ நடுவினு நெறிபல","கொண்ட வேதநன் முடியினு மருவியா ...... குருநாதா","கொங்கி லேர்தரு பழநியி லறுமுக","செந்தில் காவல தணிகையி லிணையிலி","கொந்து காவென மொழிதர வருசம ...... யவிரோத","தந்த்ர வாதிகள் பெறவரி யதுபிறர்","சந்தி யாதது தனதென வருமொரு","சம்ப்ர தாயமு மிதுவென வுரைசெய்து ...... விரைநீபச்","சஞ்ச ஡ணகரி கரமுரல் தமனிய","கிண்கி ணீமுக விதபத யுகமலர்","தந்த பேரருள் கனவிலு நனவிலு ...... மறவேனே","சிந்து வாரமு மிதழியு மிளநவ","சந்த்ர ரேகையு மரவமு மணிதரு","செஞ்ச டாதரர் திருமக வெனவரு ...... முருகோனே","செண்ப காடவி யினுமித ணினுமுயர்","சந்த னாடவி யினுமுறை குறமகள்","செம்பொ னு஡புற கமலமும் வளையணி ...... புதுவேயும்","இந்து வாண்முக வனசமு ம்ருகமத","குங்கு மாசல யுகளமு மதுரித","இந்த ளாம்ருத வசனமு முறுவலு ...... மபிராம","இந்த்ர கோபமு மரகத வடிவமு","மிந்த்ர சாபமு மிருகுழை யொடுபொரு","மிந்த்ர நீலமு மடலிடை யெழுதிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet thanks the guru who dwells at the foot of the kura tree, in the lotus of devotees' hearts and at the crown of the Vedas, who told him the way that sectarian disputants cannot reach and others never meet, 'this is the tradition', and gave the great grace of his anklet feet; he will not forget it in dream or waking. The praise names Murugan son of Siva of the matted hair, and the lord who painted on the madal Valli's lotus feet, bamboo arms, moon face, musk-dark breasts, honey speech and smile, red as the cochineal insect and green as emerald.","n":"A song of thanksgiving rather than plea: the poet speaks of a teaching already received, தனது என வரும் ஒரு சம்ப்ரதாயம், a tradition that comes as one's own, beyond the reach of சமய விரோத தந்த்ர வாதிகள். குரவடி may recall Siva teaching under the kura tree. The ending makes Murugan the lover who rides the madal, painting the beloved's form on it, the akam convention turned on the god. கொந்து காவென is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["guru","thiruvadi","vedaantham","madal"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:22"},"p:269":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":269,"t":["சினத்தவர் முடிக்கும் பகைத்தவர் குடிக்குஞ்","செகுத்தவர் ருயிர்க்குஞ் ...... சினமாகச்","சிரிப்பவர் தமக்கும் பழிப்பவர் தமக்கும்","திருப்புகழ் நெருப்பென் ...... றறிவோம்யாம்","நினைத்தது மளிக்கும் மனத்தையு முருக்கும்","நிசிக்கரு வறுக்கும் ...... பிறவாமல்","நெருப்பையு மெரிக்கும் பொருப்பையு மிடிக்கும்","நிறைப்புக ழுரைக்குஞ் ...... செயல்தாராய்","தனத்தன தனத்தந் திமித்திமி திமித்திந்","தகுத்தகு தகுத்தந் ...... தனபேரி","தடுட்டுடு டுடுட்டுண் டெனத்துடி முழக்குந்","தளத்துட னடக்குங் ...... கொடுசூரர்","சினத்தையு முடற்சங் கரித்தம லைமுற்றுஞ்","சிரித்தெரி கொளுத்துங் ...... கதிர்வேலா","தினைக்கிரி குறப்பெண் தனத்தினில் சுகித்தெண்","திருத்தணி யிருக்கும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet declares that the Thiruppugazh is fire to the heads of the angry, the clans of enemies, the lives of destroyers, and to those who laugh and those who blame; it grants what one thinks, melts the mind, cuts the womb of darkness so that one is not born, burns fire itself and breaks mountains, and he asks for the act of speaking that full praise. The praise, in drum syllables, shows the vel that destroyed the Suras and burnt the mountain with a laugh, and Murugan delighting in the hunter girl at Thiruttani.","n":"The book praises its own name: திருப்புகழ் is given the powers of a mantra, to burn enemies, fulfil desire and end birth. நெருப்பையும் எரிக்கும்: it burns even fire. The plea is not for the praise itself but for the act of speaking it (உரைக்கும் செயல்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","piravi","mandhiram"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:22"},"p:270":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":270,"t":["சினத்தி லத்தினை சிறுமண லளவுடல்","செறித்த தெத்தனை சிலைகட லினிலுயிர்","செனித்த தெத்தனை திரள்கய லெனபல ...... வதுபோதா","செமித்த தெத்தனை மலைசுனை யுலகிடை","செழித்த தெத்தனை சிறுதன மயல்கொடு","செடத்தி லெத்தனை நமனுயிர் பறிகொள்வ ...... தளவேதோ","மனத்தி லெத்தனை நினைகவ டுகள்குடி","கெடுத்த தெத்தனை மிருகம தெனவுயிர்","வதைத்த தெத்தனை யளவிலை விதிகர ...... மொழியாமல்","வகுத்த தெத்தனை மசகனை முருடனை","மடைக்கு லத்தனை மதியழி விரகனை","மலர்ப்ப தத்தினி லுருகவு மினியருள் ...... புரிவாயே","தனத்த னத்தன தனதன தனதன","திமித்தி மித்திமி திமிதிமி திமிதிமி","தகுத்த குத்தகு தகுதகு தகுதகு ...... தகுதீதோ","தரித்த ரித்தரி தரிரிரி ரிரிரிரி","தடுட்டு டுட்டுடு டடுடுடு டுடுடுடு","தமித்த மத்தள தமருக விருதொலி ...... கடல்போலச்","சினத்த மர்க்கள செருதிகழ் குருதிய","திமிழ்த்தி டக்கரி யசுரர்கள் பரிசிலை","தெறித்தி டக்கழு நரிதின நிணமிசை ...... பொரும்வேலா","செழிக்கு முத்தம சிவசர ணர்கள்தவ","முநிக்க ணத்தவர் மதுமலர் கொடுபணி","திருத்த ணிப்பதி மருவிய குறமகள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks how many bodies, countless as grains of sand, he has filled, how many lives born in the sea like shoals of fish, on hills and in springs, how many times infatuation for women held him and Death snatched his life, how many deceits he thought, families he ruined and lives he killed like a beast; and he asks Murugan to grace this fool, rough and wretched, to melt at the flower feet. The praise, in a long run of drum syllables, shows the vel in the battlefield where kites and jackals feed, and Thiruttani where Siva's devotees and sages worship with honeyed flowers.","n":"The plea is a catalogue built on எத்தனை, 'how many', closing with அளவேதோ and அளவிலை, 'what measure? there is none': the poet counts his births and his sins alike. The first words (சினத்திலத்தினை) are hard to divide, and விதிகர may be the deeds fate ordains.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","vinai","kootru","kutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:23"},"p:271":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":271,"t":["சொரியு முகிலைப் பதும நிதியைச்","சுரபி தருவைச் ...... சமமாகச்","சொலியு மனமெட் டனையு நெகிழ்விற்","சுமட ரருகுற் ...... றியல்வாணர்","தெரியு மருமைப் பழைய மொழியைத்","திருடி நெருடிக் ...... கவிபாடித்","திரியு மருள்விட் டுனது குவளைச்","சிகரி பகரப் ...... பெறுவேனோ","கரிய புருவச் சிலையும் வளையக்","கடையில் விடமெத் ...... தியநீலக்","கடிய கணைபட் டுருவ வெருவிக்","கலைகள் பலபட் ...... டனகானிற்","குரிய குமரிக் கபய மெனநெக்","குபய சரணத் ...... தினில்வீழா","உழையின் மகளைத் தழுவ மயலுற்","றுருக முருகப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks to leave the delusion of the poets who praise base patrons as equal to the rain cloud, the treasure of Kubera, the wish-cow and the wish-tree, melting their own minds before them, and who steal and twist old verses of rare meaning to make poems, so that he may sing instead of Murugan's kuvalai hill. The praise shows Murugan himself, wounded by the poison-tipped blue arrows of Valli's eyes under her dark bow-brows, falling at the feet of the doe's daughter crying 'refuge' and melting in infatuation to embrace her.","n":"A satire of court poetry: the patron is likened to cloud, padma-nidhi, Kamadhenu and kalpaka tree, the stock comparisons for a giver, and the poems are plagiarised (பழைய மொழியைத் திருடி நெருடி). The praise reverses the plea: the god falls at a girl's feet crying அபயம், as the poet will not fall at a patron's. உழையின் மகள் is Valli as a doe's daughter.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","iravu","kalavu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:23"},"p:272":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":272,"t":["தாக்கம ருக்கொரு சாரையை வேறொரு","சாக்ஷிய றப்பசி யாறியை நீறிடு","சாஸ்த்ர வழிக்கதி தூரனை வேர்விழு ...... தவ்முழ்குந்","தாற்பர்ய மற்றுழல் பாவியை நாவலர்","போற்பரி வுற்றுனை யேகரு தாதிகல்","சாற்றுத மிழ்க்குரை ஞாளியை நாள்வரை ...... தடுமாறிப்","போக்கிட மற்றவ்ரு தாவனை ஞானிகள்","போற்றுத லற்றது ரோகியை மாமருள்","பூத்தம லத்ரய பூரியை நேரிய ...... புலையேனைப்","போக்கிவி டக்கட னோஅடி யாரொடு","போய்ப்பெறு கைக்கிலை யோகதி யானது","போர்ச்சுடர் வஜ்ரவை வேல்மயி லாவருள் ...... புரிவாயே","மூக்கறை மட்டைம காபல காரணி","சூர்ப்பந கைப்படு மூளியு தாசனி","மூர்க்க குலத்திவி பீஷணர் சோதரி ...... முழுமோடி","மூத்தவ ரக்கனி ராவண னோடியல்","பேற்றிவி டக்கம லாலய சீதையை","மோட்டன் வளைத்தொரு தேர்மிசை யேகொடு ...... முகிலேபோய்","மாக்கன சித்திர கோபுர நீள்படை","வீட்டிலி ருத்திய நாளவன் வேரற","மார்க்கமு டித்தவி லாளிகள் நாயகன் ...... மருகோனே","வாச்சிய மத்தள பேரிகை போல்மறை","வாழ்த்தம லர்க்கழு நீர்தரு நீள்சுனை","வாய்த்ததி ருத்தணி மாமலை மேவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet heaps names on himself: a rat-snake for quarrels, one who eats without witness, far from the shastra of holy ash, a sinner without the meaning of austerity, a dog barking Tamil who does not think of Murugan with love as poets do, a traitor who does not honour the wise, a wretch flowering with the three malas; he asks whether it is Murugan's duty to cast him off, and whether there is no state to be gained by going with the devotees. The praise tells at length of Surpanakha's nose cut off and Ravana carrying Sita away, and names Murugan nephew of Rama the archer who destroyed him, at Thiruttani with its long spring of kazhuneer.","n":"போக்கிவிடக் கடனோ: 'is it your duty to throw me away?', a challenge as much as a plea. அடியாரொடு போய்ப் பெறுகைக்கு இலையோ கதி: the hope is to be saved in the company of devotees, not alone. Several of the epithets (சாரை, சாக்ஷியறப் பசியாறி, வ்ருதாவன்) are obscure, and the source seems damaged in தவ்முழ்குந்.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kutram","malam","adiyaar","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:23"},"p:273":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":273,"t":["திருட்டு நாரிகள் பப்பர மட்டைகள்","வறட்டு மோடியி னித்தந டிப்பவர்","சிறக்க மேனியு லுக்கிம டக்குகண் ...... வலையாலே","திகைத்து ளாவிக ரைத்தும னத்தினில்","இதத்தை யோடவி டுத்தும யக்கிடு","சிமிட்டு காமவி தத்திலு முட்பட ...... அலைவேனோ","தரித்து நீறுபி தற்றிடு பித்தனு","மிதத்து மாகுடி லைப்பொருள் சொற்றிடு","சமர்த்த பாலஎ னப்புகழ் பெற்றிடு ...... முருகோனே","சமப்ர வீணம தித்திடு புத்தியில்","இரக்க மாய்வரு தற்பர சிற்பர","சகத்ர யோகவி தக்ஷண தெக்ஷிண ...... குருநாதா","வெருட்டு சூரனை வெட்டிர ணப்பெலி","களத்தி லேகழு துக்கிரை யிட்டிடர்","விடுத்த கூளிகள் தித்திகு தித்தென ...... விளையாட","விதித்த வீரச மர்க்கள ரத்தமு","மிரற்றி யோடவெ குப்ரள யத்தினில்","விலக்கி வேல்செரு கிட்டுயிர் மொக்கிய ...... மறவோனே","பெருக்க மோடுச ரித்திடு மச்சமு","முளத்தின் மாமகிழ் பெற்றிட வுற்றிடு","பிளப்பு வாயிடை முப்பொழு தத்துமொர் ...... கழுநீரின்","பிணித்த போதுவெ டித்துர சத்துளி","கொடுக்கு மோடைமி குத்ததி ருத்தணி","பிறக்க மேவுற அத்தல முற்றுறை ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether he will wander caught in the winking ways of lust, bewildered by thieving, hollow, proud women who dance daily, sway their bodies and cast the net of their eyes, letting what is good run out of his mind. The praise names Murugan the able child who told the meaning of the great Om (மாகுடிலை) to the ash-smeared, babbling madman Siva, the supreme who comes in compassion to the wise, the southern guru skilled in a thousand yogas, the slayer of Sura in a field of ghouls, and the lord of Thiruttani, whose spring holds a lily that blooms three times a day.","n":"குடிலை is a name for the pranava, so the Om teaching is explicit here. தெக்ஷிண குருநாதா, the southern guru, gives Murugan the title of Dakshinamurti, Siva's own teaching form. Siva is called பித்தன், the madman, a devotional epithet.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","mayal","guru","piranavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:23"},"p:274":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":274,"t":["துப்பா ரப்பா டற்றீ மொய்க்கால்","சொற்பா வெளிமுக் ...... குணமோகம்","துற்றா யப்பீ றற்றோ லிட்டே","சுற்றா மதனப் ...... பிணிதோயும்","இப்பா வக்கா யத்தா சைப்பா","டெற்றே யுலகிற் ...... பிறவாதே","எத்தார் வித்தா ரத்தே கிட்டா","எட்டா அருளைத் ...... தரவேணும்","தப்பா மற்பா டிச்சே விப்பார்","தத்தாம் வினையைக் ...... களைவோனே","தற்கா ழிச்சூர் செற்றாய் மெய்ப்போ","தத்தாய் தணிகைத் ...... தனிவேலா","அப்பா கைப்பா லைப்போல் சொற்கா","வற்பா வைதனத் ...... தணைவோனே","அத்தா நித்தா முத்தா சித்தா","அப்பா குமரப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks not to be born again in the world through desire for this sinful body, steeped in the delusion of the three gunas, wrapped in skin and soaked in Kama's sickness, and to be given the grace that neither cheats nor the proud can reach. The praise names Murugan who removes the deeds of those who sing and serve without fail, who destroyed Sura, the true wisdom of Thanigai, the one who embraces the girl of candied-milk speech, and calls him father, eternal, free and knowing.","n":"The song is in clipped syllables (துப்பா ரப்பா டற்றீ), so the first lines are hard to divide and the reading of the body's description is tentative. அத்தா நித்தா முத்தா சித்தா: four titles in a rhyme, father, eternal, freed, knower. எத்தார் வித்தாரத்தே கிட்டா அருள்: grace is beyond both deceivers and the learned show-offs.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","mukkunam","piravi","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:23"},"p:275":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":275,"t":["தொக்கறாக் குடில சுத்தமேற் றசுக","துக்கமாற் கடமு ...... மலமாயை","துற்றகாற் பதலை சொற்படாக் குதலை","துப்பிலாப் பலச ...... மயநூலைக்","கைக் கொளாக் கதறு கைக்கொளாக் கையவ","லப்புலாற் றசைகு ...... ருதியாலே","கட்டுகூட் டருவ ருப்புவேட் டுழல","சட்டவாக் கழிவ ...... தொருநாளே","அக்கராப் பொடியின் மெய்க்கிடாக் குரவர்","அர்ச்சியாத் தொழுமு ...... நிவனாய","அப்பபோர்ப் பனிரு வெற்பபூத் தணியல்","வெற்பபார்ப் பதிந ...... திகுமாரா","இக்கணோக் குறில்நி ருத்தநோக் குறுத","வத்தினோர்க் குதவு ...... மிளையோனே","எத்திடார்க் கரிய முத்தபாத் தமிழ்கொ","டெத்தினார்க் கெளிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether a day will come when this body falls: a hut of skin, the pot of pleasure and pain, of mala and maya, of the humours, of babbling speech, unable to grasp the many sect books, bound with flesh and blood, loathsome, in which he wanders seeking. The praise names Murugan worshipped by gurus who wear rudraksha and ash, lord of the twelve hills and Thanigai, son of Parvati and the river, who helps the ascetics, easy to reach for those who praise him in Tamil verse and hard for those who do not.","n":"சட்டவாக்கு அழிவது ஒருநாளே: the plea is for the body's end, not its health. The praise closes on a paired rule, எத்திடார்க்கு அரிய, முத்த பாத் தமிழ் கொடு எத்தினார்க்கு எளிய: the Lord is hard for those who do not praise and easy for those who praise in Tamil. The crowded first lines (காற் பதலை, கைக்கொளாக் கதறு) are hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","malam","maayai","thamizh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:23"},"p:276":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":276,"t":["தொடத்து ளக்கிகள் அபகட நினைவிகள்","குருட்டு மட்டைகள் குமரிகள் கமரிகள்","சுதைச்சி றுக்கிகள் குசலிக ளிசலிகள் ...... முழுமோசந்","துறுத்த மட்டைகள் அசடிகள் கசடிகள்","முழுப்பு ரட்டிகள் நழுவிகள் மழுவிகள்","துமித்த மித்திரர் விலைமுலை யினவலை ...... புகுதாமல்","அடைத்த வர்க்கியல் சரசிகள் விரசிகள்","தரித்த வித்ரும நிறமென வரவுட","னழைத்து சக்கிர கிரிவளை படிகொடு ...... விளையாடி","அவத்தை தத்துவ மழிபட இருளறை","விலக்கு வித்தொரு சுடரொளி பரவந","லருட்பு கட்டியு னடியிணை யருளுவ ...... தொருநாளே","படைத்த னைததையும் வினையுற நடனொடு","துடைத்த பத்தினி மரகத சொருபியொர்","பரத்தி னுச்சியி னடநவி லுமையரு ...... ளிளையோனே","பகைத்த ரக்கர்கள் யமனுல குறஅமர்","தொடுத்த சக்கிர வளைகர மழகியர்","படிக்க டத்தையும் வயிறடை நெடியவர் ...... மருகோனே","திடுக்கி டக்கட லசுரர்கள் முறிபட","கொளுத்தி சைக்கிரி பொடிபட சுடரயில்","திருத்தி விட்டொரு நொடியினில் வலம்வரு ...... மயில்வீரா","தினைப்பு னத்திரு தனகிரி குமரிநல்","குறத்தி முத்தொடு சசிமக ளொடுபுகழ்","திருத்த ணிப்பதி மலைமிசை நிலைபெறு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet heaps names on the women who sell their breasts for a price, shaking, scheming, blind, deceitful, slippery, and asks not to enter their net, nor to play with those who call men coral-red and bangled; he asks whether a day will come when the states of consciousness and the tattvas perish, the darkness is cleared, a single light spreads, grace is fed to him and the paired feet given. The praise names Uma the emerald-formed, who creates all and with the dancer removes it, as Murugan's mother, Vishnu with discus and conch who swallowed the earth as his uncle, and the peacock that circles the world in a moment, at Thiruttani with Valli and Devayanai.","n":"விலைமுலை: breasts sold for a price, which makes these courtesans in the trade sense. The plea is in the language of Saiva doctrine: அவத்தை தத்துவம் அழிபட, இருள் அறை விலக்கு, ஒரு சுடரொளி பரவ. The praise gives Uma the dance at the summit of the Supreme (பரத்தின் உச்சியில் நடம் நவிலும் உமை).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","avaththai","thaththuvam","oli"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:23"},"p:277":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":277,"t":["நிலையாத சமுத்திர மான சமுசார துறைக்கணின் மூழ்கி","நிசமான தெனப்பல பேசி ...... யதனு஡டே","நெடுநாளு முழைப்புள தாகி பெரியோர்க ளிடைக்கர வாகி","நினைவால்நி னடித்தொழில் பேணி ...... துதியாமல்","தலையான வுடற்பிணி யூறி பவநோயி னலைப்பல வேகி","சலமான பயித்திய மாகி ...... தடுமாறித்","தவியாமல் பிறப்பையு நாடி யதுவேரை யறுத்துனை யோதி","தலைமீதில் பிழைத்திட வேநி ...... னருள்தாராய்","கலியாண சுபுத்திர னாக குறமாது தனக்குவி நோத","கவினாரு புயத்திலு லாவி ...... விளையாடிக்","களிகூரு முனைத்துணை தேடு மடியேனை சுகப்பட வேவை","கடனாகு மிதுக்கன மாகு ...... முருகோனே","பலகாலு முனைத்தொழு வோர்கள் மறவாமல் திருப்புகழ் கூறி","படிமீது துதித்துடன் வாழ ...... அருள்வாயே","பதியான திருத்தணி மேவு சிவலோக மெனப்பரி வேறு","பவரோக வயித்திய நாத ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks for grace so that, instead of plunging into the unstable sea of samsara and calling it real, labouring long, hiding from the great, failing to praise the feet, steeped in bodily disease and the sickness of birth and maddened, he may seek out and cut the root of birth, chant Murugan's name and be saved; he asks Murugan to keep him, a servant who seeks him as companion, in joy, since that is his duty, and to let those who worship him live praising him with the Thiruppugazh. The praise names Murugan Valli's bridegroom, and the lord of Thiruttani, which is like Sivaloka, the physician of the disease of birth.","n":"பவரோக வயித்திய நாத: Murugan as Vaidyanatha, physician of the disease of becoming, which matches the plea's பவநோய். கடனாகும் இது: as in 272, the poet tells the Lord that saving him is his duty. The song names its own book (திருப்புகழ் கூறி).","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["nilaiyaamai","piravi","marundhu","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:23"},"p:278":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":278,"t":["நினைத்த தெத்தனையிற் ...... றவறாமல்","மிலைத்த புத்திதனைப் ...... பிரியாமற்","கனத்த தத்துவமுற் ...... றழியாமற்","கதித்த நித்தியசித் ...... தருள்வாயே","மனித்தர் பத்தர்தமக் ...... கெளியோனே","மதித் முத்தமிழிற் ...... பெரியோனே","செனித்த புத்திரரிற் ...... சிறியோனே","திருத்த ணிப்பதியிற் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A short prayer: the poet asks that whatever he thinks not fail, that he not be parted from the steadied mind, that he not perish in the great tattvas, and that he be given the eternal consciousness that rises. The praise names Murugan easy for men and devotees, great in the three Tamils, the youngest of the sons born to Siva, at Thiruttani.","n":"நித்திய சித்து: eternal consciousness, the soul's goal named in a single phrase. மிலைத்த புத்தி may mean the mind that has been crowned or fixed; the sense of the first two lines is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","thaththuvam","paththi","thamizh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:23"},"p:279":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":279,"t":["பகலி ராவினுங் கருவி யாலனம்","பருகி யாவிகொண் ...... டுடல்பேணிப்","பழைய வேதமும் புதிய நூல்களும்","பலபு ராணமுஞ் ...... சிலவோதி","அகல நீளமென் றளவு கூறரும்","பொருளி லேயமைந் ...... தடைவோரை","அசடர் மூகரென் றவல மேமொழிந்","தறிவி லேனழிந் ...... திடலாமோ","சகல லோகமும் புகல நாடொறுஞ்","சறுகி லாதசெங் ...... கழுநீருந்","தளவு நீபமும் புனையு மார்பதென்","தணிகை மேவுசெங் ...... கதிர்வேலா","சிகர பூதரந் தகர நான்முகன்","சிறுகு வாசவன் ...... சிறைமீளத்","திமிர சாகரங் கதற மாமரஞ்","சிதற வேல்விடும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet, who feeds his body day and night through the senses, has read some old Vedas, new books and many Puranas, and calls those settled in the reality whose breadth and length cannot be measured 'fools and dumb', asks whether he must perish without knowledge. The praise shows the vel thrown so that the mountain broke, Brahma was humbled, Indra freed from prison, the dark sea cried out and the great mango tree scattered, and names the chest adorned with kazhuneer, jasmine and kadamba at Thanigai.","n":"The confession is of learning that breeds contempt: reading some Vedas and Puranas, the poet mocked the silent knowers as அசடர் மூகர், fools and mutes, when their silence was the knowledge. அகல நீளம் என்று அளவு கூறரும் பொருள்: the Reality whose breadth and length cannot be told.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pullarivu","kalvi","nindhai","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:23"},"p:280":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":280,"t":["பருத்தபற் சிரத்தினைக் குருத்திறற் கரத்தினைப்","பரித்தவப் பதத்தினைப் ...... பரிவோடே","படைத்தபொய்க் குடத்தினைப் பழிப்பவத் திடத்தினைப்","பசிக்குடற் கடத்தினைப் ...... பயமேவும்","பெருத்தபித் துருத்தனைக் கிருத்திமத் துருத்தியைப்","பிணித்தமுக் குறத்தொடைப் ...... புலனாலும்","பிணித்தவிப் பிணிப்பையைப் பொறுத்தமிழ்ப் பிறப்பறக்","குறிக்கருத் தெனக்களித் ...... தருள்வாயே","கருத்திலுற் றுரைத்தபத் தரைத்தொறுத் திருக்கரைக்","கழித்தமெய்ப் பதத்தில்வைத் ...... திடுவீரா","கதித்தநற் றினைப்புனக் கதித்தநற் குறத்தியைக்","கதித்தநற் றிருப்புயத் ...... தணைவோனே","செருத்தெறுத் தெதிர்த்தமுப் புரத்துரத் தரக்கரைச்","சிரித்தெரித் தநித்தர்பொற் ...... குமரேசா","சிறப்புறப் பிரித்தறத் திறத்தமிழ்க் குயர்த்திசைச்","சிறப்புடைத் திருத்தணிப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet takes apart his body: a head of big teeth, hands and feet that bear it, a false pot, a place of blame, a belly-pot for hunger, an artificial bellows full of bile, bound by the three humours and the five senses, a bag of disease; he asks Murugan to give him the purpose that would cut off its birth. The praise names the hero who sets on his true feet those devotees who speak from the heart, the embracer of the hunter girl, the golden son of Siva who burnt the three cities with a laugh, at Thiruttani, renowned for the Tamil of dharma.","n":"பசிக்குடற் கடம், கிருத்திமத் துருத்தி, இப்பிணிப்பை: pot, bellows and bag, the same images as 258. முக்குற்றம் is probably the three humours of medicine (wind, bile, phlegm), which would make பித்து bile rather than madness. The plea asks not for health but for the end of the birth that makes such a body.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","noy","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:23"},"p:281":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":281,"t":["பழமை செப்பிய ழைத்தித மித்துடன்","முறைம சக்கிய ணைத்துந கக்குறி","படஅ ழுத்திமு கத்தைமு கத்துற ...... வுறவாடிப்","பதறி யெச்சிலை யிட்டும ருத்திடு","விரவு குத்திர வித்தைவி ளைப்பவர்","பலவி தத்திலு மற்பரெ னச்சொலு ...... மடமாதர்","அழிதொ ழிற்குவி ருப்பொடு நத்திய","அசட னைப்பழி யுற்றஅ வத்தனை","அடைவு கெட்டபு ரட்டனை முட்டனை ...... அடியேனை","அகில சத்தியு மெட்டுறு சித்தியு","மெளிதெ னப்பெரு வெட்டவெ ளிப்படு","மருண பொற்பத முற்றிட வைப்பது ...... மொருநாளே","குழவி ழிப்பெரு நெட்டல கைத்திரள்","கரண மிட்டுந டித்தமி தப்படு","குலிலி யிட்டக ளத்திலெ திர்த்திடு ...... மொருசூரன்","குருதி கக்கிய திர்த்துவி ழப்பொரு","நிசிச ரப்படை பொட்டெழ விக்ரம","குலிச சத்தியை விட்டருள் கெர்ச்சித ...... மயில்வீரா","தழையு டுத்தகு றத்திப தத்துணை","வருடி வட்டமு கத்தில தக்குறி","தடவி வெற்றிக தித்தமு லைக்குவ ...... டதன்மீதே","தரள பொற்பணி கச்சுவி சித்திரு","குழைதி ருத்திய ருத்திமி குத்திடு","தணிம லைச்சிக ரத்திடை யுற்றருள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women who recall old intimacy, call sweetly, embrace, leave nail-marks, press face to face, feed a man with their spittle and work base magic, women who say they are many men's; he asks whether a day will come when Murugan sets him, a fool who longed for their ruinous work, disgraced and lost, at his red-gold feet, which make all powers and the eight siddhis easy and plain. The praise shows ghouls dancing in the battlefield and Sura vomiting blood, and Murugan stroking the feet of Valli in her leaf skirt, touching the tilaka on her round face and fastening a golden bodice over her breasts on the Thani hill.","n":"குத்திர வித்தை: base arts, probably love-charms or drugs, the deceit of the women. The praise inverts the plea: the poet asks to be set at the Lord's feet, and the Lord himself strokes Valli's feet (பதத்துணை வருடி). The feet that make the siddhis easy suggest the siddhis are a by-product, not the goal.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","vanjam","siththi","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:23"},"p:282":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":282,"t":["புருவ நெறித்துக் குறுவெயர் வுற்றுப்","புளகித வட்டத் ...... தனமானார்","பொருவிழி யிற்பட் டவரொடு கட்டிப்","புரளு மசட்டுப் ...... புலையேனைக்","கருவிழி யுற்றுக் குருமொழி யற்றுக்","கதிதனை விட்டிட் ...... டிடுதீயக்","கயவனை வெற்றிப் புகழ்திகழ் பத்மக்","கழல்கள் துதிக்கக் ...... கருதாதோ","செருவசு ரப்பொய்க் குலமது கெட்டுத்","திரைகட லுட்கப் ...... பொரும்வேலா","தினைவன முற்றுக் குறவர் மடப்பைக்","கொடிதன வெற்பைப் ...... புணர்மார்பா","பெருகிய நித்தச் சிறுபறை கொட்டிப்","பெரிகை முழக்கப் ...... புவிமீதே","ப்ரபலமுள் சுத்தத் தணிமலை யுற்றுப்","ப்ரியமிகு சொக்கப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet calls himself the foolish wretch who rolls embracing women with knitted brows, sweating and thrilled, caught in their eyes, the evil base man who has lost the guru's word and abandoned the way of release, and asks whether Murugan will not think to let him praise the victorious lotus feet. The praise names the vel that ended the false race of demons, the chest joined to Valli, and the beautiful lord (சொக்கப் பெருமாள்) on the pure Thani hill where drums sound daily.","n":"குருமொழி அற்று: what the poet has lost is the teacher's word, so desire is cast as a failure of instruction. The question turns to the Lord: கருதாதோ, will you not think of it, rather than asking what the poet should do.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kutram","guru","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:23"},"p:283":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":283,"t":["பூசலிட் டுச்ச ரத்தை நேர்கழித் துப்பெ ருத்த","போர்விடத் தைக்கெ டுத்து ...... வடிகூர்வாள்","போலமுட் டிக்கு ழைக்கு ளோடிவெட் டித்தொ ளைத்து","போகமிக் கப்ப ரிக்கும் ...... விழியார்மேல்","ஆசைவைத் துக்க லக்க மோகமுற் றுத்து யர்க்கு","ளாகிமெத் தக்க ளைத்து ...... ளழியாமே","ஆரணத் துக்க ணத்து னாண்மலர்ப் பொற்ப தத்தை","யான்வழுத் திச்சு கிக்க ...... அருள்வாயே","வாசமுற் றுத்த ழைத்த தாளிணைப் பத்த ரத்த","மாதர்கட் கட்சி றைக்கு ...... ளழியாமே","வாழ்வுறப் புக்கி ரத்ன ரேகையொக் கச்சி றக்கு","மாமயிற் பொற்க ழுத்தில் ...... வரும்வீரா","வீசுமுத் துத்தெ றிக்க வோலைபுக் குற்றி ருக்கும்","வீறுடைப் பொற்கு றத்தி ...... கணவோனே","வேலெடுத் துக்க ரத்தி னீலவெற் பிற்ற ழைத்த","வேளெனச் சொற்க ருத்தர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of women whose eyes, like a sharpened sword, rush to the ears, cut and pierce and steal pleasure, and asks that he not perish exhausted in desire, confusion and sorrow over them, but be granted to praise the fresh-flowered golden feet of the Vedas and be happy. The praise names Murugan who comes on the gem-necked peacock so that the devotees of his fragrant feet do not perish in the prison of women's eyes, the husband of the golden hunter girl, and the lord at the blue hill.","n":"The whole song is about the eyes: in the plea they are a sword that cuts to the ears, in the praise a prison (மாதர் கண் சிறை) from which the Lord comes to free his devotees. சொற்கருத்தர் at the close may mean the lord of those who hold his word in mind.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kan","mayal","thiruvadi","adiyaar"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:23"},"p:284":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":284,"t":["பெருக்கவு பாயங் கருத்துடை யோர்தம்","ப்ரபுத்தன பாரங் ...... களிலேசம்","ப்ரமத்துட னாளும் ப்ரமித்திருள் கூரும்","ப்ரியக்கட லு஡டுந் ...... தணியாத","கருக்கட லு஡டுங் கதற்றும நேகங்","கலைக்கட லு஡டுஞ் ...... சுழலாதே","கடப்பலர் சேர்கிண் கிணிப்ரபை வீசும்","கழற்புணை நீதந் ...... தருள்வாயே","தருக்கிய வேதன் சிறைப்பட நாளுஞ்","சதுர்த்தச லோகங் ...... களும்வாழச்","சமுத்திர மேழுங் குலக்கிரி யேழுஞ்","சளப்பட மாவுந் ...... தனிவீழத்","திருக்கையில் வேலொன் றெடுத்தம ராடுஞ்","செருக்கு மயூரந் ...... தனில்வாழ்வே","சிறப்பொடு ஞானந் தமிழ்த்ரய நீடுந்","திருத்தணி மேவும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks not to whirl in the sea of desire for the breasts of scheming women, in the unending sea of the womb, or in the sea of the many arts, but to be given the raft of Murugan's kadamba-garlanded, anklet-bright feet. The praise shows Brahma imprisoned, the fourteen worlds living, the seven seas and seven mountains shaken, and the vel taken up in war on the proud peacock, at Thiruttani of wisdom and the three Tamils.","n":"Three seas, of desire (ப்ரியக்கடல்), birth (கருக்கடல்) and learning (கலைக்கடல்), and one raft, the feet (கழற்புணை). Learning is placed beside desire and birth as something to drown in.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["thiruvadi","piravi","kaamam","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:23"},"p:285":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":285,"t":["பொரியப் பொரியப் பொலிமுத் துவடத்","துகளிற் புதையத் ...... தனமீதே","புரளப் புரளக் கறுவித் தறுகட்","பொருவிற் சுறவக் ...... கொடிவேள்தோள்","தெரிவைக் கரிவைப் பரவைக் குருகிச்","செயலற் றனள்கற் ...... பழியாதே","செறிவுற் றணையிற் றுயிலுற் றருமைத்","தெரிவைக் குணர்வைத் ...... தரவேணும்","சொரிகற் பகநற் பதியைத் தொழுகைச்","சுரருக் குரிமைப் ...... புரிவோனே","சுடர்பொற் கயிலைக் கடவுட் கிசையச்","சுருதிப் பொருளைப் ...... பகர்வோனே","தரிகெட் டசுரப் படைகெட் டொழியத்","தனிநெட் டயிலைத் ...... தொடும்வீரா","தவளப் பணிலத் தரளப் பழனத்","தணிகைக் குமரப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"In the voice of one who watches over a girl, the singer says that with her pearl strings scorched and buried in the dust on her breasts, and Kama of the fish banner fierce against her, she has melted and lost all power to act; she asks that, without her chastity perishing, the dear girl be given back her senses so that she may sleep on her bed. The praise names Murugan who gives the gods of the kalpaka city their rights, who told the meaning of the Veda to the god of golden Kailasa, and whose single long vel destroyed the asura armies, at Thanigai of white conches and pearl-strewn fields.","n":"This is akam poetry for a girl in love with the god, not the poet's own confession. கற்பழியாதே, that her chastity not perish, sets the girl's steadfastness against the love that is wasting her. The praise repeats Murugan the teacher of Siva: சுடர்பொற் கயிலைக் கடவுட்கு இசையச் சுருதிப் பொருளைப் பகர்வோனே. The opening lines (தெரிவைக் கரிவைப் பரவைக் குருகி) are hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","karpu","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:24"},"p:286":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":286,"t":["பொருவிக் கந்தொடடர்ச் செருவிக் கன்றொடுமிப்","புதுமைப் புண்டரிகக் ...... கணையாலே","புளகக் கொங்கையிடத் திளகக் கொங்கையனற்","பொழியத் தென்றல்துரக் ...... குதலாலே","தெருவிற் பெண்கள்மிகக் கறுவிச் சண்டையிடத்","திரியத் திங்களுதிப் ...... பதனானே","செயலற் றிங்கணையிற் றுயிலற் றஞ்சியயர்த்","தெரிவைக் குன்குரவைத் ...... தரவேணும்","அருவிக் குன்றடையப் பரவிச் செந்தினைவித்","தருமைக் குன்றவருக் ...... கெளியோனே","அசுரர்க் கங்கயல்பட் டமரர்க் கண்டமளித்","தயில்கைக் கொண்டதிறற் ...... குமரேசா","தருவைக் கும்பதியிற் றிருவைச் சென்றணுகித்","தழுவிக் கொண்டபுயத் ...... திருமார்பா","தரளச் சங்குவயற் றிரளிற் றங்குதிருத்","தணிகைச் செங்கழநிப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"Again for a lovesick girl, the singer tells how Kama's new lotus arrow, the south wind that makes her breasts pour fire, the women in the street who quarrel angrily over her, and the rising moon have left her helpless, sleepless and afraid on her bed, and asks Murugan to give her his kuravam garland. The praise names him easy of reach for the hill folk who sow millet, the Kumaresa who took up the vel and gave the gods their realm, and the one who went to the city of the gods' tree and embraced its Lady, at Thanigai of the red lilies.","n":"உன் குரவைத் தரவேணும்: the girl asks for the god's garland, a classical token of the lover. தெருவில் பெண்கள் மிகக் கறுவிச் சண்டையிட is the town's gossip (அலர்) at its harshest, women in the street quarrelling over her. The திரு embraced in the city of the tree is probably Devayanai, Indra's daughter.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","alar","maalai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:24"},"p:287":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":287,"t":["பொற்குட மொத்தகு யத்தைய சைப்பவர்","கைப்பொருள் புக்கிட ...... வேதான்","புட்குரல் விச்சைபி தற்றுமொ ழிச்சியர்","பொட்டணி நெற்றிய ...... ரானோர்","அற்பவி டைக்கலை சுற்றிநெ கிழ்ப்பவர்","அற்பர மட்டைகள் ...... பால்சென்","றக்கண்வ லைக்குள கப்படு புத்தியை","அற்றிட வைத்தருள் ...... வாயே","கொக்கரை சச்சரி மத்தளி யொத்துவி","டக்கைமு ழக்கொலி ...... யாலக்","கொக்கிற கக்கர மத்தம ணிக்கருள்","குத்தத ணிக்கும ...... ரேசா","சர்க்கரை முப்பழ மொத்தமொ ழிச்சிகு","றத்தித னக்கிரி ...... மேலே","தைக்கும னத்தச மர்த்தஅ ரக்கர்த","லைக்குலை கொத்திய ...... வேளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of women who sway breasts like golden pots, babble bird-call arts so that the money in a man's hand passes to them, wear the pottu on the forehead and loosen the cloth about their small waists, and calls them base and worthless; he asks that the mind caught in the net of their eyes be made to cease. The praise names Murugan of Thanigai, Kumaresa gracious to Siva who wears crane feathers and datura, to the sound of drums, and the lord who rests his heart on the breasts of the hunter girl whose speech is like sugar and the three fruits, and who smashed the heads of the rakshasas.","n":"கைப்பொருள் புக்கிடவே தான் புட்குரல் விச்சை பிதற்றும்: their sweet talk, likened to the cries of birds, is a skill for getting the money out of a man's hand. அக்கண் வலைக்குள் அகப்படு புத்தி: the eyes are a net. The song ends with வேளே, not பெருமாளே.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","kan","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:24"},"p:288":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":288,"t":["பொற்ப தத்தி னைத்து தித்து நற்ப தத்தி லுற்ற பத்தர்","பொற்பு ரைத்து நெக்கு ருக்க ...... அறியாதே","புத்த கப்பி தற்றை விட்டு வித்த கத்து னைத்து திக்க","புத்தி யிற்க லக்க மற்று ...... நினையாதே","முற்ப டத்த லத்து தித்து பிற்ப டைத்த கிர்த்ய முற்றி","முற்க டைத்த வித்து நித்த ...... முழல்வேனை","முட்ட விக்க டைப்பி றப்பி னுட்கி டப்ப தைத்த விர்த்து","முத்தி சற்றெ னக்க ளிப்ப ...... தொருநாளே","வெற்ப ளித்த தற்ப ரைக்கி டப்பு றத்தை யுற்ற ளித்த","வித்த கத்ததர் பெற்ற கொற்ற ...... மயில்வீரா","வித்தை தத்வ முத்த மிழ்ச்சொ லத்த சத்தம் வித்த ரிக்கு","மெய்த்தி ருத்த ணிப்பொ ருப்பி ...... லுறைவோனே","கற்ப கப்பு னக்கு றத்தி கச்ச டர்த்த சித்ர முற்ற","கற்பு ரத்தி ருத்த னத்தி ...... லணைவோனே","கைத்த ரக்கர் கொத்து கச்சி னத்து வஜ்ர னுக்க மைத்த","கைத்தொ ழுத்த றித்து விட்ட ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses that he has not learned to melt in praise of devotees who praise the golden feet, has not left the babble of books to praise Murugan with skill and an untroubled mind, and wanders always, born and acting out deeds whose seed was sown before; he asks whether a day will come when Murugan ends his lying in this base birth and gives him a little release. The praise names Murugan the peacock hero, son of the wise one who gave his left side to the daughter of the mountain, who dwells on Thanigai hill where knowledge, tattva and the three Tamils in word, meaning and sound are unfolded, and who embraces the hunter girl.","n":"புத்தகப் பிதற்றை விட்டு: book-learning is the babble that stands in the way of praise, a confession of the learned rather than of the lustful. முத்தி சற்று எனக்கு அளிப்பது: he asks only for 'a little' release. The last lines of praise (வஜ்ரனுக்கு அமைத்த கைத்தொழுத்து அறித்துவிட்ட) seem to tell of Indra's hand, but the allusion is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["veedu","piravi","adiyaar","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:24"},"p:289":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":289,"t":["மருக்குல மேவுங் குழற்கனி வாய்வெண்","மதிப்பிள வாகும் ...... நுதலார்தம்","மயக்கினி லேநண் புறப்படு வேனுன்","மலர்க்கழல் பாடுந் ...... திறநாடாத்","தருக்கனு தாரந் துணுக்கிலி லோபன்","சமத்தறி யாவன் ...... பில஧முகன்","தலத்தினி லேவந் துறப்பணி யாதன்","தனக்கினி யார்தஞ் ...... சபைதாராய்","குருக்குல ராஜன் தனக்கொரு தூதன்","குறட்பெல மாயன் ...... நவநீதங்","குறித்தயில் நேயன் திருப்பயில் மார்பன்","குணத்ரய நாதன் ...... மருகோனே","திருக்குள நாளும் பலத்திசை மூசும்","சிறப்பது றாஎண் ...... டிசையோடும்","திரைக்கடல் சூழும் புவிக்குயி ராகுந்","திருத்தணி மேவும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says he befriends the delusion of women with fragrant hair, fruit-red mouths and foreheads like the half moon, without seeking the skill to sing Murugan's flower feet, and calls himself proud, without a shred of generosity, a miser, one who never comes to the shrine to bow; he asks to be given the company of those dear to Murugan. The praise names Vishnu, envoy for the king of the Kuru line, the dwarf, the lover of butter with Lakshmi on his chest and lord of the three gunas, as his uncle, and Murugan at Thanigai, life of the sea-girt earth.","n":"The plea is for a place in the assembly of devotees (இனியார் தம் சபை), not for the feet alone. Among the self-reproaches, the miser who gives nothing (உதாரம் துணுக்கிலி லோபன்) stands out. One word (வன்பில஧முகன்) is corrupt.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["mayal","kutram","ivaral","adiyaar"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:24"},"p:290":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":290,"t":["மலைமு லைச்சியர் கயல்வி ழிச்சியர்","மதிமு கத்திய ...... ரழகான","மயில்ந டைச்சியர் குயில்மொ ழிச்சியர்","மனது ருக்கிக ...... ளணைமீதே","கலைநெ கிழ்த்தியே உறவ ணைத்திடு","கலவி யிற்றுவள் ...... பிணிதீராக்","கசட னைக்குண அசட னைப்புகல்","கதியில் வைப்பது ...... மொருநாளே","குலகி ரிக்குல முருவ விட்டமர்","குலவு சித்திர ...... முனைவேலா","குறவர் பெற்றிடு சிறுமி யைப்புணர்","குமர சற்குண ...... மயில்வீரா","தலம திற்புக லமர ருற்றிடர்","தனைய கற்றிய ...... அருளாளா","தருநி ரைத்தெழு பொழில்மி குத்திடு","தணிம லைக்குயர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women with mountain breasts, carp eyes, moon faces, peacock gait and cuckoo speech who melt the heart and loosen their clothes on the bed, and calls himself dross and a fool drooping in an incurable sickness of union; he asks whether a day will come when Murugan sets him in the state of refuge. The praise names the vel that pierced the Krauncha, the Kumara who joined the hunters' little girl, the gracious one who removed the troubles of the gods who took refuge, at Thanigai hill of groves.","n":"கலவியில் துவள் பிணி தீரா: union itself is an incurable disease. கசடனைக் குண அசடனை: dross, and a fool in character, the stock self-abuse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kutram","noy"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:24"},"p:291":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":291,"t":["முகத்தைமி னுக்கிக ளசடிகள் கபடிகள்","விழித்தும ருட்டிகள் கெருவிகள் திருடிகள்","மொழிக்குள்ம யக்கிகள் வகைதனில் நகைதனில் ...... விதமாக","முழித்தும யற்கொளு மறிவிலி நெறியிலி","புழுக்குட லைப்பொரு ளெனமிக எணியவர்","முயக்கம டுத்துழி தருமடி யவனிடர் ...... ஒழிவாக","மிகுத்தழ கைப்பெறு மறுமுக சரவண","புயத்திள கிக்கமழ் நறைமலர் தொடைமிக","விசைக்கொடு மைப்பெறு மரகத கலபியும் ...... வடிவேலும்","வெளிப்படெ னக்கினி யிரவொடு பகலற","திருப்பதி யப்புக ழமுதியல் கவிசொலி","விதித்தனெ ழுத்தினை தரவரு மொருபொரு ...... ளருளாயோ","புகைத்தழ லைக்கொடு திரிபுர மெரிபட","நகைத்தவ ருக்கிட முறைபவள் வலைமகள்","பொருப்பிலி மக்கிரி பதிபெறு மிமையவ ...... ளபிராமி","பொதுற்றுதி மித்திமி நடமிடு பகிரதி","எழுத்தறி ருத்திரி பகவதி கவுரிகை","பொருட்பய னுக்குரை யடுகிய சமைபவள் ...... அமுதாகச்","செகத்தைய கத்திடு நெடியவர் கடையவள்","அறத்தைவ ளர்த்திடு பரசிவை குலவதி","திறத்தமி ழைத்தரு பழையவ ளருளிய ...... சிறியோனே","செருக்கும ரக்கர்கள் பொடிபட வடிவுள","கரத்தில யிற்கொடு பொருதிமை யவர்பணி","திருத்தணி பொற்பதி தனில்மயில் நடவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet heaps names on women who gloss their faces, deceive, frighten with their eyes, steal and beguile in speech, and calls himself the witless, pathless servant who stared and was deluded and took the worm-filled body for a thing of worth; he asks that the six-faced Saravana appear with his peacock and vel, and that, singing poems in padigams night and day, he be given the one thing that comes to erase what Brahma wrote. Most of the praise is for Parvati, who dwells in the side of Siva who laughed and burned the triple cities, the fisher girl, the daughter of the Himalaya, Abhirami, Gauri, the one who raises dharma and gives Tamil, whose little son Murugan dances on the peacock at Thanigai.","n":"விதித்தன் எழுத்தினை: Brahma's writing on the forehead, fate, which the 'one thing' (ஒருபொருள்) is asked to erase. புழுக் குடலைப் பொருளென மிக எணி: the confession is of valuing a worm-filled body. The long list of Parvati's names (வலைமகள், அபிராமி, பகவதி, கவுரி, அறத்தை வளர்த்திடு பரசிவை) is the heart of the praise; several of its compounds are hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","yaakkai","oozh","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:24"},"p:292":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":292,"t":["முகிலு மிரவியு முழுகதிர் தரளமு","முடுகு சிலைகொடு கணைவிடு மதனனு","முடிய வொருபொரு ளுதவிய புதல்வனு ...... மெனநாடி","முதிய கனனென தெய்வதரு நிகரென","முதலை மடுவினி லதவிய புயலென","முகமு மறுமுக முடையவ னிவனென ...... வறியோரைச்","சகல பதவியு முடையவ ரிவரென","தனிய தநுவல விஜயவ னிவனென","தபனன் வலம்வரு கிரிதனை நிகரென ...... இசைபாடிச்","சயில பகலவ ரிடைதொறு நடைசெயு","மிரவு தவிரவெ யிருபத மடையவெ","சவித அடியவர் தவமதில் வரவருள் ...... புரிவாயே","அகில புவனமு மடைவினி லுதவிய","இமய கிரிமயில் குலவரை தநுவென","அதிகை வருபுர நொடியினி லெரிசெய்த ...... அபிராமி","அமரு மிடனன லெனுமொரு வடிவுடை","யவனி லுரையவன் முதுதமி ழுடையவ","னரியொ டயனுல கரியவ னடநவில் ...... சிவன்வாழ்வே","திகிரி நிசிசரர் தடமுடி பொடிபட","திரைக ளெறிகடல் சுவறிட களமிசை","திரடு குறடுகள் புரள்வெகு குருதிகள் ...... பெருகாறாச்","சிகர கிரிநெரி படபடை பொருதருள்","திமிர தினகர குருபர இளமயில்","சிவணி வருமொரு தணிகையில் நிலைதிகழ் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses that he has sung praises of poor men, likening them to the cloud, the sun, Kama, the bountiful son, Kubera, the kalpaka tree, Vishnu who saved the elephant in the crocodile pool, the six-faced Murugan himself, Arjuna and Meru, and walking from door to door; he asks that this begging end, that he reach the two feet, and that devotees come into austerity. The praise names Parvati of the Himalaya who with the mountain as bow burned the cities at Athigai in an instant, and Siva of the old Tamil who dances beyond Vishnu and Brahma, whose wealth Murugan is, and a battle in which the asuras' crowns were crushed, at Thanigai.","n":"The list of comparisons is the patron-poem itself, quoted to be renounced; it even includes comparing the patron to Murugan (முகமும் அறுமுகம் உடையவன் இவன்), the god the poet should have praised. இரவு தவிர: the plea names begging. Several comparisons (முதிய கனன், சயில பகலவர்) are uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iravu","thiruvadi","adiyaar"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:24"},"p:293":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":293,"t":["முடித்த குழலினர் வடித்த மொழியினர்","முகத்தி லிலகிய ...... விழியாலும்","முலைக்கி ரிகள்மிசை யசைத்த துகிலினும்","இளைத்த இடையினு ...... மயலாகிப்","படுத்த அணைதனி லணைத்த அவரொடு","படிக்கு ளநுதின ...... முழலாதே","பருத்த மயில்மிசை நினைத்த பொழுதுன","பதத்து மலரிணை ...... யருள்வாயே","துடித்து தசமுகன் முடித்த லைகள்விழ","தொடுத்த சரம்விடு ...... ரகுராமன்","துகைத்தி வுலகையொ ரடிக்கு ளளவிடு","துலக்க அரிதிரு ...... மருகோனே","தடத்து ளுறைகயல் வயற்கு ளெதிர்படு","தழைத்த கதலிக ...... ளவைசாயத்","தருக்கு மெழிலுறு திருத்த ணிகையினில்","தழைத்த சரவண ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of women with bound hair, refined speech and bright eyes, whose cloth sways over their breasts and whose waists are thin, and asks that, infatuated, he not wander daily on earth with those he embraced in bed, but that Murugan give him the paired lotus feet whenever he thinks of him on the stout peacock. The praise names Raghu-Rama, whose arrow felled the ten-headed one's crowned heads, and Vishnu who measured the world in a step, as his uncle, at Thanigai of fish-filled tanks and bending plantains.","n":"நினைத்த பொழுது உன பதத்து மலரிணை அருள்வாயே: the feet are asked for at whatever moment he thinks of the Lord, a simple and complete plea.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mayal","kaamam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:24"},"p:294":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":294,"t":["முத்துத்தெ றிக்கவள ரிக்குச்சி லைக்கைமதன்","முட்டத்தொ டுத்த ...... மலராலே","முத்தத்தி ருச்சலதி முற்றத்து தித்தியென","முற்பட்டெ றிக்கு ......நிலவாலே","எத்தத்தை யர்க்குமித மிக்குப்பெ ருக்கமணி","இப்பொற்கொ டிச்சி ...... தளராதே","எத்திக்கு முற்றபுகழ் வெற்றித்தி ருத்தணியில்","இற்றைத்தி னத்தில் ...... வரவேணும்","மெத்தச்சி னத்துவட திக்குக்கு லச்சிகர","வெற்பைத்தொ ளைத்த ...... கதிர்வோலா","மெச்சிக்கு றத்திதன மிச்சித் தணைத்துருகி","மிக்குப்ப ணைத்த ...... மணிமார்பா","மத்தப்ர மத்தரணி மத்தச்ச டைப்பரமர்","சித்தத்தில் வைத்த ...... கழலோனே","வட்டத்தி ரைக்கடலில் மட்டித்தெ திர்த்தவரை","வெட்டித்து ணித்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"For a lovesick girl, the singer says that through the flowers shot by Kama with his sugarcane bow and the moon that rises from the sea scattering pearls, this golden creeper is wasting, and asks Murugan to come today to Thanigai, famed in every direction, so that she does not droop. The praise names the bright vel that pierced the northern mountain, the jewelled chest that melted embracing the hunter girl, and the feet kept in the heart of Siva who wears datura and the river, and the lord who cut down the one who opposed him in the sea.","n":"இற்றைத் தினத்தில் வரவேணும்: the plea is that the god come today, as a lover is begged to come. The line எத்தத்தையர்க்கும் இதம் மிக்குப் பெருக்க is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:24"},"p:295":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":295,"t":["முலைபுளக மெழஅங்கை மருவுசரி வளைகொஞ்ச","முகிலளக மகில்பொங்க ...... அமுதான","மொழிபதற வருமந்த விழிகுவிய மதிகொண்ட","முகம்வெயர்வு பெறமன்ற ...... லணையூடே","கலைநெகிழ வளர்வஞ்சி யிடைதுவள வுடலொன்று","படவுருகி யிதயங்கள் ...... ப்ரியமேகூர்","கலவிகரை யழியின்ப அலையிலலை படுகின்ற","கவலைகெட நினதன்பு ...... பெறுவேனோ","அலையெறியு மெழில்சண்ட உததிவயி றழல்மண்ட","அதிரவெடி படஅண்ட ...... மிமையோர்கள்","அபயமென நடுகின்ற அசுரர்பட அடியுண்டு","அவர்கள்முனை கெடநின்று ...... பொரும்வேலா","தலைமதிய நதிதும்பை யிளவறுகு கமழ்கொன்றை","சடைமுடியி லணிகின்ற ...... பெருமானார்","தருகுமர விடவைந்து தலையரவு தொழுகின்ற","தணிமலையி லுறைகின்ற ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes lovemaking on the bridal bed, breasts thrilling, bangles chiming, hair loosened, speech faltering, eyes closing, faces sweating, clothes slipping and bodies made one as hearts melt, and asks whether he will gain Murugan's love so that the care of being tossed on the shoreless waves of union's pleasure ends. The praise names the vel that fought when the sea burned and the gods cried 'refuge', Siva who wears the moon, the river, thumbai, arugu and konrai in his matted hair as father, and Murugan at Thanigai hill where the five-headed serpent worships.","n":"கலவி கரை அழி இன்ப அலை: the pleasure of union is a sea without shore, and what he asks for against it is love (நினது அன்பு), not escape. ஐந்து தலை அரவு தொழுகின்ற தணிமலை: a serpent's worship at Thanigai, probably the local legend of Vasuki.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","anbu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:24"},"p:296":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":296,"t":["மொகுமொகென நறைகொண்மலர் வற்கத்தி லற்புடைய","முளரிமயி லனையவர்கள் நெய்த்துக்க றுத்துமழை","முகிலனைய குழல்சரிய வொக்கக்க னத்துவள ...... ரதிபார","முலைபுளக மெழவளைகள் சத்திக்க முத்தமணி","முறுவலிள நிலவுதர மெத்தத்த வித்தசில","மொழிபதற விடைதுவள வட்டச்சி லைப்புருவ ...... இணைகோட","அகில்மிருக மதசலிலம் விட்டுப்ப ணித்தமல","ரமளிபட வொளிவிரவு ரத்நப்ர பைக்குழையொ","டமர்பொருத நெடியவிழி செக்கச்சி வக்கமர ...... மதநீதி","அடல்வடிவு நலமிதனில் மட்கச்செ ருக்கியுள","முருகநரை பெருகவுட லொக்கப்ப ழுத்துவிழு","மளவிலொரு பரமவொளி யிற்புக்கி ருக்கவெனை ...... நினையாதோ","செகுதகெண கெணசெகுத செக்குச்செ குச்செகுத","கிருதசெய செயகிருத தொக்குத்தொ குத்தொகுத","டிமிடடிமி டிமிடிமிட டிட்டிட்டி டிட்டிமிட ...... டிடிதீதோ","திரிகடக கடகதிரி தித்திக்ர தித்ரிகட","திமிர்ததிமி திமிர்ததிமி தித்தித்தி தித்திதிதி","செணுசெணுத தணசெணுத தத்தித்தி குத்ரிகுட ...... ததிதீதோ","தகுடதிகு திகுடதிமி தத்தத்த தித்திகுட","குகுகுகுகு குகுகுகுகு குக்குக்கு குக்குகுத","தரரரர ரிரிரிரிரி றிற்றித்த றிற்றிரிரி ...... யெனவேநீள்","சதிமுழவு பலவுமிரு பக்கத்தி சைப்பமுது","சமையபயி ரவியிதய முட்கிப்ர மிக்கவுயர்","தணிகைமலை தனின்மயிலி னிர்த்தத்தி னிற்கவல ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet gives a long and elaborate scene of lovemaking with women like lotus-peacocks on a bed sprinkled with agil, musk and rose water, according to the law of love, and asks whether, when strength, form and beauty have rotted in that pride, his hair has greyed and his body ripened and fallen, Murugan will not think of him and let him enter and stay in the one supreme light. The praise is a battle sung in drum syllables, at which the ancient Bhairavi's heart trembled in wonder, and Murugan dancing on the peacock at Thanigai.","n":"உடல் ஒக்கப் பழுத்து விழும் அளவில்: the hour of death is pictured as fruit ripening and falling, and the goal as entering a light (பரம ஒளியில் புக்கிருக்க), not as reaching the feet. The praise is almost all rhythm syllables.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","yaakkai","nilaiyaamai","oli"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:24"},"p:297":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":297,"t":["வங்கம் பெறுகட லெங்கும் பொருதிரை","வந்துந் தியதிரு ...... மதனாலே","வஞ்சம் பெறுதிட நெஞ்சன் தழலுற","வஞ்சம் பதும்விடு ...... மதனாலே","பங்கம் படுமென் தங்கந் தனிலுதி","பண்பொன் றியவொரு ...... கொடியான","பஞ்சொன் றியமயில் நெஞ்சொன் றியெயழல்","பொன்றுந் தனிமையை ...... நினையாயோ","தெங்கந் திரளுட னெங்குங் கதலிகள்","சென்றொன் றியபொழி ...... லதனு஡டே","தெந்தெந் தெனதென என்றண் டுறஅளி","நின்றுந் திகழ்வொடு ...... மயிலாடப்","பொங்குஞ் சுனைகளி லெங்குங் குவளைகள்","என்றும் புகழ்பெற ...... மலாணனும்","பொன்றென் றணிகையில் நின்றங் கெழுபுவி","யென்றுஞ் செயவல ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"For a lovesick girl, the singer says that by the waves beating on the sea and by Kama, firm in deceit, loosing his five arrows, this one creeper, soft as cotton and like a peacock, has her golden body marred and her heart on fire, and asks Murugan whether he will not think of her loneliness that is wasting her. The praise is a picture of Thanigai, its coconut and plantain groves where bees hum and peacocks dance, its springs full of blue lilies, where Murugan stands.","n":"பொன்றும் தனிமையை நினையாயோ: the girl's loneliness, the pain of separation, is what she asks the god to think of. The praise is almost all landscape. The closing lines are garbled (லதனு஡டே, மலாணனும்) and their sense is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["kaamanoy","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:24"},"p:298":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":298,"t":["வட்டவாட் டனம னைச்சிபாற் குதலை","மக்கள்தாய்க் கிழவி ...... பதிநாடு","வைத்ததோட் டமனை யத்தமீட் டுபொருள்","மற்றகூட் டமறி ...... வயலாக","முட்டவோட் டிமிக வெட்டுமோட் டெருமை","முட்டர்பூட் டியெனை ...... யழையாமுன்","முத்திவீட் டணுக முத்தராக் கசுரு","திக்குராக் கொளிரு ...... கழல்தாராய்","பட்டநாற் பெரும ருப்பினாற் கரஇ","பத்தின்வாட் பிடியின் ...... மணவாளா","பச்சைவேய்ப் பணவை கொச்சைவேட் டுவர்ப","திச்சிதோட் புணர்த ...... ணியில்வேளே","எட்டுநாற் கரவொ ருத்தல்மாத் திகிரி","யெட்டுமாக் குலைய ...... எறிவேலா","எத்திடார்க் கரிய முத்தபாத் தமிழ்கொ","டெத்தினார்க் கெளிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks that, before the fools who drive the goring buffalo bind him and call him away, his wife, his babbling children at the breast, his old mother, his town and land, the garden he kept, his house, gold, earned wealth and all the rest become alien to his mind, and that he be given the two feet that make one free so that he may draw near release. The praise names Murugan the bridegroom of the daughter of the four-tusked white elephant, the one who joined the hunter girl at Thanigai, whose vel shook the eight elephants and eight mountains of the directions, hard to reach for those who do not praise and easy for those who praise in pure Tamil verse.","n":"The list of what must become alien (மனைச்சி, குதலை மக்கள், தாய்க் கிழவி, பதி, நாடு, தோட்டம், மனை, அத்தம்) is the whole household, given in order of nearness; the plea is detachment before death rather than rescue at death. The bride of the elephant (இபத்தின் பிடி) is Devayanai, raised by Airavata. One word (அசுருதிக்கு) is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kootru","pattru","veedu","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:24"},"p:299":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":299,"t":["வரிக்கலையி னிகரான விழிக்கடையி லிளைஞோரை","மயக்கியிடு மடவார்கள் ...... மயலாலே","மதிக்குளறி யுளகாசு மவர்க்குதவி மிடியாகி","வயிற்றிலெரி மிக்முள ...... அதனாலே","ஒருத்தருட னுறவாகி ஒருத்தரொடு பகையாகி","ஒருத்தர்தமை மிகநாடி ...... யவரோடே","உணக்கையிடு படுபாவி எனக்குனது கழல்பாட","உயர்ச்சிபெறு குணசீல ...... மருள்வாயே","விரித்தருண கிரிநாத னுரைத்ததமி ழெனுமாலை","மிகுத்தபல முடனோத ...... மகிழ்வோனே","வெடித்தமணர் கழுவேற ஒருத்திகண வனுமீள","விளைத்ததொரு தமிழ்பாடு ...... புலவோனே","செருக்கியிடு பொருசூரர் குலத்தையடி யறமோது","திருக்கையினில் வடிவேலை ...... யுடையோனே","திருக்குலவு மொருநீல மலர்ச்சுனையி லழகான","திருத்தணிகை மலைமேவு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses that, infatuated with women whose glances bewitch young men, he lost his wits, gave them all his money and became destitute with hunger burning in his belly, befriending one, quarrelling with another, chasing a third, and calls himself a wretched sinner; he asks for the noble character to sing Murugan's feet. The praise names Murugan who delights when the garland of Tamil spoken by Arunagirinathan is recited, the poet whose Tamil sent the Jains to the stake and brought back a woman's husband, and the vel that destroyed Sura's clan, at Thanigai with its spring of the one blue lily.","n":"விரித்து அருணகிரிநாதன் உரைத்த தமிழ்: the poet names himself, and his songs as a garland Murugan enjoys. வெடித்த அமணர் கழுவேற ஒருத்தி கணவனும் மீள: Murugan is the Tamil poet who impaled the Jains and revived a woman's husband, i.e. Sambandar. The confession runs from infatuation to money to hunger, a sequence of ruin.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","inmai","thamizh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:24"},"p:300":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":300,"t":["வாருற்றெழு பூண்முலை வஞ்சியர்","காருற்றெழு நீள்குழல் மஞ்சியர்","வாலக்குயில் போல்மொழி கொஞ்சியர் ...... தெருமீதே","மாணுற்றெதிர் மோகன விஞ்சையர்","சேலுற்றெழு நேர்விழி விஞ்சியர்","வாகக்குழை யாமப ரஞ்சியர் ...... மயலாலே","சீருற்றெழு ஞானமு டன்கல்வி","நேரற்றவர் மால்கொடு மங்கியெ","சேருற்றறி வானத ழிந்துயி ...... ரிழவாமுன்","சேவற்கொடி யோடுசி கண்டியின்","மீதுற்றறி ஞோர்புகழ் பொங்கிய","தேசுக்கதிர் கோடியெ னும்பத ...... மருள்வாயே","போருற்றிடு சூரர்சி ரங்களை","வீரத்தொடு பாரில ரிந்தெழு","பூதக்கொடி சோரிய ருந்திட ...... விடும்வேலா","பூகக்குலை யேவிழ மென்கயல்","தாவக்குலை வாழைக ளுஞ்செறி","போகச்செநெ லேயுதி ருஞ்செய்க ...... ளவைகோடி","சாரற்கிரி தோறுமெ ழும்பொழில்","தூரத்தொழு வார்வினை சிந்திடு","தாதுற்றெழு கோபுர மண்டப ...... மவைசூழுந்","தார்மெத்திய தோரண மென்தெரு","தேர்சுற்றிய வார்பதி அண்டர்கள்","தாமெச்சிய நீள்தணி யம்பதி ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes the women on the street with bound breasts, long dark hair, speech like a young cuckoo, carp eyes and swinging earrings, seducers skilled in enchantment, and asks that, before he, lacking true knowledge and learning, fades in infatuation, loses his understanding and dies, Murugan grant him the feet, bright as a crore of suns, that the wise praise, coming on the peacock with the rooster banner. The praise names the vel that cut down Sura's warriors for the ghosts to drink their blood, and Thanigai with its areca, plantain and paddy, its groves on the slopes, its towers and halls whose golden dust scatters the deeds of those who worship from afar, and its festooned streets circled by the chariot.","n":"தூரத் தொழுவார் வினை சிந்திடு ... கோபுர மண்டபம்: the shrine's towers remove the deeds even of those who bow from a distance, a claim for the place itself. மோகன விஞ்சையர்: the women are 'enchanters', practisers of a spell.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mayal","gnaanam","thiruvadi","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:25"},"p:301":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":301,"t":["வினைக்கின மாகுத் தனத்தினர் வேளம்","பினுக்கெதி ராகும் ...... விழிமாதர்","மிகப்பல மானந் தனிற்புகு தாவெஞ்","சமத்திடை போய்வந் ...... துயர்மூழ்கிக்","கனத்தவி சாரம் பிறப்படி தோயுங்","கருக்குழி தோறுங் ...... கவிழாதே","கலைப்புல வோர்பண் படைத்திட வோதுங்","கழற்புக ழோதுங் ...... கலைதாராய்","புனத்திடை போய்வெஞ் சிலைக்குற வோர்வஞ்","சியைப்புணர் வாகம் ...... புயவேளே","பொருப்பிரு கூறும் படக்கடல் தானும்","பொருக்கெழ வானும் ...... புநக்முளச்","சினத்தொடு சூரன் கனத்திணி மார்பந்","திறக்கம ராடுந் ...... திறல்வேலா","திருப்புக ழோதுங் கருத்தினர் சேருந்","திருத்தணி மேவும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of women whose breasts are kin to evil deeds and whose eyes rival Kama's arrows, and asks that, after going among them and sinking in sorrow, he not fall face down into every womb-pit where heavy care and birth sink, but be given the art of chanting the praise of the feet that learned poets set to music. The praise names Murugan who went to the millet field and embraced Valli of the bow-bearing hunters, whose vel split the mountain, dried the sea and opened Sura's chest, at Thanigai where those who are minded to chant Thiruppugazh gather.","n":"கருக்குழி தோறும் கவிழாதே: each birth is a fall headlong into a womb-pit. திருப்புகழ் ஓதும் கருத்தினர் சேரும் திருத்தணி: the song names the book's own title as the chant of Thanigai's devotees. The battle line has an unflagged garble (புநக்முள).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","karu","pugazh","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:25"},"p:302":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":302,"t":["வெற்றிசெய வுற்றகழை விற்குதைவ ளைத்துமதன்","விட்டகணை பட்ட ...... விசையாலே","வெட்டவெளி யிற்றெருவில் வட்டபணை யிற்கனல்வி","ரித்தொளிப ரப்பு ...... மதியாலே","பற்றிவசை கற்றபல தத்தையர்த மக்குமிசை","பட்டதிகி ரிக்கு ...... மழியாதே","பத்தியையெ னக்கருளி முத்தியைய ளித்துவளர்","பச்சைமயி லுற்று ...... வரவேணும்","நெற்றிவிழி பட்டெரிய நட்டமிடு முத்தமர்நி","னைக்குமன மொத்த ...... கழல்வீரா","நெய்கமல மொக்குமுலை மெய்க்குறவி யிச்சையுற","நித்தமிறு கத்த ...... ழுவுமார்பா","எற்றியதி ருச்சலதி சுற்றியதி ருத்தணியில்","எப்பொழுது நிற்கு ...... முருகோனே","எட்டசல மெட்டநில முட்டமுடி நெட்டசுரர்","இட்டசிறை விட்ட ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks that he not perish under the arrows of Kama's victorious cane bow, the moon spreading fiery light over the open street, the many parrot-tongued women skilled in slander, and the sounding wheel, but that Murugan, giving him devotion and granting release, come on the green peacock. The praise names the hero whose anklets are held in the heart of the dancer whose forehead eye burned, the chest that daily embraces the hunter girl, Murugan who always stands at Thiruthanigai, and the lord who freed the gods from the asuras' prison.","n":"The poet borrows the lovesick girl's afflictions (Kama's arrows, the moon, the women's slander) for his own voice, and asks for பத்தி and முத்தி as the lover's cure. இசைபட்ட திகிரி is unclear; it may be the sounding conch or the moon's wheel. The shrine named is Thanigai, though the heading is Kundruthoradal.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","alar","paththi","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:26"},"p:303":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":303,"t":["அதிருங் கழல்ப ணிந்து ...... னடியேனுன்","அபயம் புகுவ தென்று ...... நிலைகாண","இதயந் தனிலி ருந்து ...... க்ருபையாகி","இடர்சங் கைகள்க லங்க ...... அருள்வாயே","எதிரங் கொருவ ரின்றி ...... நடமாடும்","இறைவன் தனது பங்கி ...... லுமைபாலா","பதியங் கிலுமி ருந்து ...... விளையாடிப்","பலகுன் றிலும மர்ந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A short song: the poet asks when he will see the state in which he, bowing at Murugan's ringing anklets, takes refuge in him, and asks Murugan to dwell in his heart with compassion so that his troubles and doubts dissolve. The praise names him the son of Uma, who is part of the Lord who dances with none to rival him, and the one who plays in every town and dwells on many hills.","n":"இடர் சங்கைகள் கலங்க: doubts as well as troubles are what grace should scatter. பலகுன்றிலும் அமர்ந்த: the song's close names the heading's sense, Murugan on every hill.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvadi","thiruvarul","thondu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:26"},"p:304":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":304,"t":["எழுதிகழ் புவன நொடியள வதனி","லியல்பெற மயிலில் ...... வருவோனே","இமையவர் பரவி யடிதொழ அவுணர்","மடிவுற விடுவ ...... தொருவேலா","வழுதியர் தமிழி னொருபொரு ளதனை","வழிபட மொழியு ...... முருகேசா","மலரடி பணியு மடமகள் பசலை","மயல்கொடு தளர்வ ...... தழகோதான்","முழுகிய புனலி லினமணி தரள","முறுகிடு பவள ...... மிகவாரி","முறையொடு குறவர் மடமகள் சொரியு","முதுமலை யழக ...... குருநாதா","பழகிய வினைகள் பொடிபட அருளில்","படிபவ ரிதய ...... முறுகோவே","பருவரை துணிய வொருகணை தெரிவ","பலமலை யுடைய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"Mostly praise: Murugan circled the seven worlds on his peacock in an instant, threw the vel for the gods, and spoke the one meaning of the Pandyas' Tamil so that it was honoured; within it the singer asks whether it is fitting that the girl who bows to his flower feet should droop with pallor and infatuation. The praise goes on to the old hill where the hunter girl pours out gems, pearls and coral from the stream, Murugan the guru who dwells in the hearts of those immersed in grace, and the lord of many hills.","n":"வழுதியர் தமிழின் ஒருபொருள் அதனை வழிபட மொழியும்: Murugan declares the one meaning of the Pandyas' Tamil, perhaps the story of his judging the true commentary at the Madurai assembly, though the text names no more than this. The love plea is a single line set in the middle of the praise.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","kaamanoy","thamizh","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:26"},"p:305":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":305,"t":["தறையின் மானுட ராசையி னால்மட","லெழுது மாலருள் மாதர்கள் தோதக","சரசர் மாமல ரோதியி னாலிரு ...... கொங்கையாலுந்","தளர்மி னேரிடை யாலுடை யால்நடை","யழகி னால்மொழி யால்விழி யால்மருள்","சவலை நாயடி யேன்மிக வாடிம ...... யங்கலாமோ","பறவை யானமெய்ஞ் ஞானிகள் மோனிக","ளணுகொ ணாவகை நீடுமி ராசிய","பவன பூரக வேகிக மாகிய ...... விந்துநாதம்","பகரொ ணாதது சேரவொ ணாதது","நினையொ ணாதது வானத யாபர","பதிய தானச மாதிம னோலயம் ...... வந்துதாராய்","சிறைவி டாதநி சாசரர் சேனைகள்","மடிய நீலக லாபம தேறிய","திறல்வி நோதச மேளத யாபர ...... அம்புராசித்","திரைகள் போலலை மோதிய சீதள","குடக காவிரி நீளலை சூடிய","திரிசி ராமலை மேலுறை வீரகு ...... றிஞ்சிவாழும்","மறவர் நாயக ஆதிவி நாயக","ரிளைய நாயக காவிரி நாயக","வடிவி னாயக ஆனைத னாயக ......எங்கள்மானின்","மகிழு நாயக தேவர்கள் நாயக","கவுரி நாயக னார்குரு நாயக","வடிவ தாமலை யாவையு மேவிய ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of deceitful flirting women for whom men on earth write the madal, and asks whether he, a weak dog of a servant, should wilt and be deluded by their hair, breasts, waist, gait, speech and eyes; he asks Murugan to come and give him the samadhi and dissolution of mind, the secret bindu and nada reached by the swift filling of the breath, which true jnanis and silent sages cannot approach and which cannot be spoken, reached or thought. The praise names Murugan the hero on Thirisiramalai by the cool Kaveri from Kudagu, lord of the kurinji hunters, younger brother of the first Vinayaka, lord of the gods, guru of Gauri's lord, the master who dwells on all hills.","n":"The plea is in the language of yoga: பவன பூரகம் (filling the breath), விந்து நாதம், சமாதி, மனோலயம். Yet the song says even jnanis and munis cannot reach it and asks it as a gift. மடல் எழுதும்: the women are those for whom men write the madal, the desperate lover's public plea. The praise is a litany of நாயக.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["samaadhi","vindhu","praanan","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:26"},"p:306":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":306,"t":["வஞ்சக லோப மூடர் தம்பொரு ளூர்கள் தேடி","மஞ்சரி கோவை தூது ...... பலபாவின்","வண்புகழ் பாரி காரி யென்றிசை வாது கூறி","வந்தியர் போல வீணி ...... லழியாதே","செஞ்சர ணாத கீத கிண்கிணி நீப மாலை","திண்டிறல் வேல்ம யூர ...... முகமாறும்","செந்தமிழ் நாளு மோதி உய்ந்திட ஞான மூறு","செங்கனி வாயி லோர்சொ ...... லருள்வாயே","பஞ்சவ னீடு கூனு மொன்றிடு தாப மோடு","பஞ்சற வாது கூறு ...... சமண்மூகர்","பண்பறு பீலி யோடு வெங்கழு வேற வோது","பண்டித ஞான நீறு ...... தருவோனே","குஞ்சரம் யாளி மேவு பைம்புன மீது லாவு","குன்றவர் சாதி கூடி ...... வெறியாடிக்","கும்பிட நாடி வாழ்வு தந்தவ ரோடு வீறு","குன்றுதோ றாடல் மேவு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks that he not perish in vain like the court bards who seek out the wealth and towns of deceitful, miserly fools, praising them in manjari, kovai, thoothu and many verse forms and arguing they are as generous as Pari and Kari, but that, chanting daily in Tamil Murugan's feet, anklets, kadamba garland, vel, peacock and six faces, he be given one word from the red mouth where wisdom springs. The praise names Murugan the learned one who gave the holy ash of wisdom that cured the Pandya's hunch and fever and sent the mute Jains to the stake with their peacock feathers, and the lord of Kundruthoradal whom the hill tribes worship with frenzied dance.","n":"பாரி காரி என்று இசை வாது கூறி: the poet mocks the bard's stock claim that a miser is Pari or Kari, the legendary patrons. ஓர் சொல் அருள்வாயே: he asks for a single word, the guru's teaching. The praise identifies Murugan with Sambandar at Madurai: the Pandya's hunch (கூன்) and fever (தாபம்) cured by the ash, and the Jains (சமண்) impaled.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","thamizh","guru","thiruneeru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:26"},"p:307":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":307,"t":["அலைகடல் நிகராகிய விழிகொடு வலைவீசிகள்","அபகட மகபாவிகள் ...... விரகாலே","அதிவித மதராயத நிதமொழி பலகூறிகள்","அசடரொ டுறவாடிகள் ...... அநியாயக்","கலைபகர் விலைமாதர்கள் இளைஞர்கள் குடிகேடிகள்","கருதிடு கொடியாருட ...... னினிதாகக்","கனதன முலைமேல்விழு கபடனை நி முடனை","கழலிணை பெறவேயினி ...... யருள்வாயே","அலைபுனல் தலைசூடிய பசுபதி மகனாகிய","அறுமுக வடிவே அருள் ...... குருநாதா","அசுரர்கள் குடியேகெட அமரர்கள் பதியேபெற","அதிரிடும் வடிவேல்விடு ...... மதிசூரா","தலையய னறியாவொரு சிவகுரு பரனேயென","தரணியி லடியார்கண ...... நினைவாகா","சகலமு முதலாகிய அறுபதி நிலைமேவிய","தடமயில் தனிலேறிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of courtesans who cast nets with eyes like the wave-tossed sea, deceitful great sinners full of tricks, who flirt with fools, sell their arts for a price and ruin the families of young men, and calls himself the cheat and utter fool who falls on their breasts; he asks to be granted now the paired feet. The praise names Murugan son of Pasupati who bears the river, the six-faced form of grace and guru, the hero whose vel ruined the asuras and gave the gods back their city, the Sivaguru whom Brahma did not know, whom devotees on earth hold in mind, and the lord on the peacock abiding in the six shrines that are the source of all.","n":"விலைமாதர்கள் ... இளைஞர்கள் குடிகேடிகள்: the courtesans sell and ruin households, so the charge is money and family, not desire alone. தலை அயன் அறியா ஒரு சிவகுருபரன்: Murugan as the teacher whom Brahma did not know recalls the Om story without naming it. சகலமும் முதலாகிய அறுபதி: the six shrines are themselves the first of all things.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","kutram","thiruvadi","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:27"},"p:308":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":308,"t":["ஈனமிகுத் துளபிறவி ...... யணுகாதே","யானுமுனக் கடிமையென ...... வகையாக","ஞானஅருட் டனையருளி ...... வினைதீர","நாணமகற் றியகருணை ...... புரிவாயே","தானதவத் தினின்மிகுதி ...... பெறுவோனே","சாரதியுத் தமிதுணைவ ...... முருகோனே","ஆனதிருப் பதிகமரு ...... ளிளையோனே","ஆறுதிருப் பதியில்வளர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A short song: the poet asks that base birth not come near him, that he become Murugan's servant, and that he be given the grace of jnana so that his deeds end, with a compassion that takes away his shame. The praise names Murugan who excels in gift and penance, the companion of an excellent consort, the young one who gave the sacred padhigams, and the lord who grows in the six holy shrines.","n":"ஆன திருப்பதிகம் அருள் இளையோனே: 'the young one who gave the holy padhigams' identifies Murugan with Sambandar, the child poet of the Thevaram padhigams. சாரதி உத்தமி துணைவ is unclear: சாரதி may name the Goddess or a consort, and the line may be divided otherwise. நாணம் அகற்றிய கருணை could also be the compassion that ended Valli's shyness.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thondu","gnaanam","piravi","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:28"},"p:309":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":309,"t":["அதிமதங் கக்கப் பக்கமு கக்குஞ்","சரிதனந் தைக்கச் சிக்கென நெக்கங்","கணைதருஞ் செச்சைப் பொற்புய னத்தன் ...... குறவாணார்","அடவியந் தத்தைக் கெய்த்துரு கிச்சென்","றடிபணிந் திட்டப் பட்டும யற்கொண்","டயர்பவன் சத்திக் கைத்தல னித்தன் ...... குமரேசன்","துதிசெயும் சுத்தப் பத்தியர் துக்கங்","களைபவன் பச்சைப் பக்ஷிந டத்துந்","துணைவனென் றர்ச்சித் திச்சைத ணித்துன் ...... புகழ்பாடிச்","சுருதியின் கொத்துப் பத்தியு முற்றுந்","துரியமுந் தப்பித் தத்வம னைத்துந்","தொலையுமந் தத்துக் கப்புற நிற்கும் ...... படிபாராய்","கதிபொருந் தக்கற் பித்துந டத்துங்","கனல்தலம் புக்குச் சக்ரமெ டுக்குங்","கடவுளும் பத்மத் தச்சனு முட்கும் ...... படிமோதிக்","கதிரவன் பற்குற் றிக்குயி லைத்திண்","சிறகரிந் தெட்டுத் திக்கர்வ குக்குங்","கடகமுந் தட்டுப் பட்டொழி யக்கொன் ...... றபிராமி","பதிவ்ரதம் பற்றப் பெற்றம கப்பெண்","பரிவொழிந் தக்கிக் குட்படு தக்கன்","பரிபவம் பட்டுக் கெட்டொழி யத்தன் ...... செவிபோயப்","பணவிபங் கப்பட் டப்படி வெட்கும்","படிமுனிந் தற்றைக் கொற்றம்வி ளைக்கும்","பரமர்வந் திக்கக் கச்சியில் நிற்கும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet praises Murugan as the one who, for the parrot of the hunters' forest, melted, went and bowed at her feet and swooned in infatuation, and the one who ends the sorrow of pure devotees, and asks that, worshipping him so with desire quenched and singing his fame, he be looked on and made to stand beyond the end where the Vedas' rows of devotion, the turiya state and all the tattvas vanish. The praise tells of Daksha's sacrifice, where the god of the discus and Brahma were struck in fear, the sun's teeth knocked out, the bird's wings cut and the guardians' army scattered, after Daksha's daughter, keeping her vow, entered the fire; Siva, who raged that day, worships Murugan at Kachi.","n":"The plea reverses the usual roles: the first half gives Murugan's own infatuation (மயற்கொண்டு அயர்பவன்) for Valli, and asks for the poet's desire to be quenched (இச்சை தணித்து). துரியமும் தப்பித் தத்வம் அனைத்தும் தொலையும் அந்தத்துக்கு அப்புறம்: the goal is beyond turiya and the tattvas, as in 203. The sacrifice is named by its details: Daksha (தக்கன்), his daughter in the fire, the sun's teeth, the wings cut off the bird (குயில், here perhaps Agni in bird form).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","avaththai","mayal","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:28"},"p:310":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":310,"t":["கனகதம் பத்தைச் செச்சையை மெச்சுங்","கடகசங் கத்துப் பொற்புய வெற்பன்","கடலுள்வஞ் சித்துப் புக்கதொர் கொக்கும் ...... பொடியாக","கறுவுசெஞ் சத்திப் பத்மக ரத்தன்","குமரனென் றர்ச்சித் தப்படி செப்புங்","கவிமொழிந் தத்தைக் கற்றற வுற்றும் ...... புவியோர்போய்","குனகியுங் கைக்குக் கற்பக மொப்பென்","றனகனென் றிச்சைப் பட்டத ளிக்குங்","குமணனென றொப்பிட் டித்தனை பட்டிங் ...... கிரவான","குருடுகொண் டத்தச் சத்தம னைத்துந்","திருடியுஞ் சொற்குத் தக்கதொ டுத்துங்","குலவியுங் கத்தப் பட்டக லக்கந் ...... தெளியாதோ","சனகனன் புற்றுப் பெற்றம டப்பெண்","தனிப்பெருங் கற்புச் சக்ரந டத்துந்","தகையிலங் கைச்சுற் றத்தைமு ழுத்துஞ் ...... சுடவேவெஞ்","சமரசண் டக்கொற் றத்தவ ரக்கன்","கதிர்விடும் பத்துக் கொத்துமு டிக்குந்","தனியொரம் பைத்தொட் டுச்சுரர் விக்னங் ...... களைவோனும்","தினகரன் சொர்க்கத் துக்கிறை சுக்ரன்","சசிதரன் திக்குக் கத்தர கத்யன்","திசைமுகன் செப்பப் பட்டவ சிட்டன் ...... திரள்வேதஞ்","செகதலஞ் சுத்தப் பத்தியர் சித்தம்","செயலொழிந் தற்றுப் பெற்றவர் மற்றும்","சிவனும்வந் திக்கக் கச்சியில் நிற்கும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses that, though he learned the poetry that worships Murugan of the golden arms and the red vel that reduced the mango in the sea to dust, he went to the people of the world, fawned on them, called a man a kalpaka tree for his giving, the sinless one, a Kumanan who gives whatever is wished, and, blind with begging, stole all the words of meaning and strung them to suit; he asks whether this confusion will not clear. The praise names Rama, who for Janaka's daughter of matchless chastity burned the whole kin of Lanka and cut the ten crowned heads with a single arrow, and names the sun, Indra, Sukra, the moon, the guardians, Agastya, Brahma, Vasishtha, the Vedas, the pure devotees whose action has ceased, and Siva himself as worshipping Murugan at Kachi.","n":"The confession is the poet's trade again: the patron is flattered as the wishing tree and as குமணன், the Sangam chieftain famed for giving. இரவான குருடு, 'the blindness that is begging', and சத்தம் அனைத்தும் திருடி, stealing every word, make the flattery a theft from the poetry owed to the Lord. The long list of worshippers at Kachi ends with those whose action has ceased (செயலொழிந்து).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","pugazh","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:28"},"p:311":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":311,"t":["செடியுடம் பத்தித் தெற்றியி ரத்தஞ்","செறிநரம் பிட்டுக் கட்டிய சட்டஞ்","சிறைதிரண் டொக்கத் தொக்கவி னைப்பந் ...... தவிகாரம்","திமிரதுங் கத்தத் துத்திரை யெற்றுஞ்","செனனபங் கத்துத் துக்கக டற்கண்","திருகுரும் பைப்பட் டுச்சுழல் தெப்பங் ...... கரணாதி","குடிபுகும் பொக்கப் புக்கிலி றப்பின்","குடிகலம்வெந் தொக்குக் கொட்டில்ம லத்தின்","குசைசுமந் தெட்டுத் திக்கிலு முற்றுந் ...... தடுமாறுங்","குவலயங் கற்றுக் கத்தியி ளைக்குஞ்","சமயசங் கத்தைத் தப்பியி ருக்குங்","குணமடைந் துட்பட் டொக்கஇ ருக்கும் ...... படிபாராய்","படிதருங் கற்புக் கற்பக முக்கண்","கொடிபசுஞ் சித்ரக் குத்தர முத்தம்","பணிநிதம் பத்துச் சத்தியு கக்குங் ...... குமரேசா","பரவசங் கெட்டெட் டக்கர நித்தம்","பரவுமன் பர்க்குச் சித்திய ளிக்கும்","பரமர்வந் திக்கத் தக்கப தத்தன் ...... குருநாதா","தொடியிடும் பத்மக் கைக்குமி டைக்குஞ்","சுருள்படும் பத்திப் பட்டகு ழற்குந்","துகிர்கடைந் தொப்பித் திட்டஇ தழ்க்குங் ...... குறமானின்","சுடர்படுங் கச்சுக் கட்டுமு லைக்குந்","துவளுநெஞ் சத்தச் சுத்தஇ ருக்கும்","சுரரும்வந் திக்கக் கச்சியில் நிற்கும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet calls the body a stinking frame of bones and blood bound with nerves, a prison of karmic bonds, a raft whirling in the dark-waved sea of birth's sorrow, a hollow dwelling of the instruments that carries the filth of death and stumbles in all eight directions, and asks to escape the crowd of sects who learn the world and shout themselves weary and to reach the quality of staying within, at one. The praise names Murugan whom the three-eyed Goddess, wishing tree of chastity, delights in, the guru whose feet Siva worships, he who gives siddhi to devotees reciting the syllables daily, and the one whose heart melts for Valli's hands, waist, hair, lips and breasts, worshipped by the gods at Kachi.","n":"The body images pile up as in 30: frame (சட்டம்), prison (சிறை), raft in the sea of birth (செனன ... துக்கக்கடல் ... தெப்பம்), hollow house (பொக்கப் புக்கில்). The remedy is not only escape from the body but from the sects that 'learn and shout and tire' (கற்றுக் கத்தி இளைக்கும் சமய சங்கம்). எட்டக்கரம் may be eight syllables or a misdivision; it is left unexplained.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","piravi","samayam","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:28"},"p:312":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":312,"t":["கனக்ரவுஞ் சத்திற் சத்தியை விட்டன்","றசுரர்தண் டத்தைச் செற்றவி தழ்ப்பங்","கயனைமுன் குட்டிக் கைத்தளை யிட்டும் ...... பரையாளுங்","கடவுளன் புற்றுக் கற்றவர் சுற்றும்","பெரியதும் பிக்கைக் கற்பக முற்றங்","கரதலம் பற்றப் பெற்றவொ ருத்தன் ...... ஜகதாதை","புனவிளந் தத்தைக் கிச்சையு ரைக்கும்","புரவலன் பத்தர்க் குத்துணை நிற்கும்","புதியவன் செச்சைப் புட்பம ணக்கும் ...... பலபாரப்","புயனெனுஞ் சொற்கற் றுப்பிற கற்கும்","பசையொழிந் தத்தத் திக்கென நிற்கும்","பொருடொறும் பொத்தப் பட்டதொ ரத்தம் ...... பெறுவேனோ","அனல்விடுஞ் செக்கட் டுக்கய மெட்டும்","பொரவரிந் திட்டெட் டிற்பகு திக்கொம்","பணிதருஞ் சித்ரத் தொற்றையு ரத்தன் ...... திடமாக","அடியொடும் பற்றிப் பொற்கயி லைக்குன்","றதுபிடுங் கப்புக் கப்பொழு தக்குன்","றணிபுயம் பத்துப் பத்துநெ ரிப்புண் ...... டவனீடுந்","தனதொரங் குட்டத் தெட்பல டுக்குஞ்","சரியலன் கொற்றத் துக்ரவ ரக்கன்","தசமுகன் கைக்குக் கட்கம ளிக்கும் ...... பெரியோனுந்","தலைவியும் பக்கத் தொக்கவி ருக்குஞ்","சயிலமுந் தெற்குச் சற்குரு வெற்புந்","தணியலும் பெற்றுக் கச்சியில் நிற்கும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet strings Murugan's names, the one who threw the sakti at the golden Krauncha, knocked Brahma on the head and fettered his hands, the god whom Siva loves, the one whose hand Ganesa held, the patron who spoke his desire to the young parrot of the millet field, and asks whether, having learned these names, his clinging will go and he will gain the one meaning hidden in every thing. The praise tells of Ravana, who fought the eight elephants of the quarters and pulled up golden Kailasa by its roots, then had his twenty arms crushed under one toe, and of Siva who afterwards gave him a sword; Murugan stands at Kachi, having that god and his consort, Kailasa and the southern hill of the true guru.","n":"பசை ஒழிந்து ... பொருள் தொறும் பொத்தப்பட்டது ஒர் அத்தம்: the plea is for the meaning enclosed in every object once attachment has gone, a pervasion read through the names. Brahma knocked and fettered (குட்டிக் கைத்தளையிட்டு) is the Om story, though Om is not named here. தெற்குச் சற்குரு வெற்பு, the southern hill of the true guru, is Swamimalai.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","pattru","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:28"},"p:313":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":313,"t":["தெரியலஞ் செச்சைக் கொத்து முடிக்கும்","பரிதிகந் தத்தைச் சுற்றந டத்துஞ்","சிறைவிடுஞ் சொர்க்கத் துச்சுர ரைக்கங் ...... கையில்வாழுஞ்","சிறுவனென் றிச்சைப் பட்டுப ஜிக்கும்","படிபெரும் பத்திச் சித்ரக வித்வஞ்","சிறிதுமின் றிச்சித் தப்பரி சுத்தம் ...... பிறவாதே","பரிகரஞ் சுத்தத் தக்கப்ர புத்வம்","பதறியங் கட்டப் பட்டனர் தத்வம்","பலவையுங் கற்றுத் தர்க்கம தத்வம் ...... பழியாதே","பரபதம் பற்றப் பெற்றஎ வர்க்கும்","பரவசம் பற்றிப் பற்றற நிற்கும்","பரவ்ரதம் பற்றப் பெற்றிலன் மற்றென் ...... துயர்போமோ","சரியுடன் துத்திப் பத்திமு டிச்செம்","பணதரங் கைக்குக் கட்டிய நெட்டன்","தனிசிவன் பக்கத் தற்புதை பற்பந் ...... திரிசூலந்","தரிகரும் பொக்கத் தக்கமொ ழிச்சுந்","தரியரும் பிக்கப் பித்தத னத்தந்","தரிசுரும் பிக்குப் பத்ரையெ வர்க்குந் ...... தெரியாத","பெரியபண் டத்தைச் சத்திய பித்தன்","பிரிதியுண் கற்புப் பச்சையெ றிக்கும்","ப்ரபையள்தண் டிற்கைப் பத்மம டப்பெண் ...... கொடிவாழ்வே","பிரமரண் டத்தைத் தொட்டதொர் வெற்பும்","பிளவிடுஞ் சத்திக் கைத்தல நித்தம்","பெருமிதம் பெற்றுக் கச்சியில் நிற்கும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses that he has no great poetry of devotion to worship Murugan as the child of Ganga who freed the imprisoned gods, that his mind was never purified, that he learned many tattvas only to fall into argument, and that he has not gained the high vow by which those who reach the supreme state stand rapt and free of all attachment; he asks how else his sorrow can go. The praise is a long litany of the Goddess, Siva's consort with the trident and cane bow, Bhadra, the great thing unknown to anyone, the lotus-handed creeper, whose child Murugan is, and of the sakti that split the mountain touching Brahma's egg, at Kachi.","n":"பரவசம் பற்றிப் பற்று அற நிற்கும் பரவ்ரதம்: the devotee's rapture is what frees from attachment, not learning; தத்வம் பலவையும் கற்றுத் தர்க்க ம(த)த்துவம் sets learned argument against it. Much of the litany of the Goddess is hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["paththi","pattru","thaththuvam","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:28"},"p:314":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":314,"t":["புனமடந் தைக்குத் தக்கபு யத்தன்","குமரனென் றெத்திப் பத்தர்து திக்கும்","பொருளைநெஞ் சத்துக் கற்பனை முற்றும் ...... பிறிதேதும்","புகலுமெண் பத்தெட் டெட்டியல் தத்வம்","சகலமும் பற்றிப் பற்றற நிற்கும்","பொதுவையென் றொக்கத் தக்கதொ ரத்தந் ...... தனைநாளும்","சினமுடன் தர்க்கித் துச்சிலு கிக்கொண்","டறுவருங் கைக்குத் திட்டொரு வர்க்குந்","தெரிவரும் சத்யத் தைத்தெரி சித்துன் ...... செயல்பாடித்","திசைதொறுங் கற்பிக் கைக்கினி யற்பந்","திருவுளம் பற்றிச் செச்சைம ணக்குஞ்","சிறுசதங் கைப்பொற் பத்மமெ னக்கென் ...... றருள்வாயே","கனபெருந் தொப்பைக் கெட்பொரி யப்பம்","கனிகிழங் கிக்குச் சர்க்கரை முக்கண்","கடலைகண் டப்பிப் பிட்டொடு மொக்கும் ...... திருவாயன்","கவளதுங் கக்கைக் கற்பக முக்கண்","திகழுநங் கொற்றத் தொற்றைம ருப்பன்","கரிமுகன் சித்ரப் பொற்புகர் வெற்பன் ...... றனையீனும்","பனவியொன் றெட்டுச் சக்ரத லப்பெண்","கவுரிசெம் பொற்பட் டுத்தரி யப்பெண்","பழயஅண் டத்தைப் பெற்றம டப்பெண் ...... பணிவாரைப்","பவதரங் கத்தைத் தப்பநி றுத்தும்","பவதிகம் பர்க்குப் புக்கவள் பக்கம்","பயில்வரம் பெற்றுக் கச்சியில் நிற்கும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks to see the meaning the devotees praise as Murugan, the common ground that holds all the ninety-six tattvas yet stands free of them, the truth hard to know for the six sects who argue and quarrel in anger every day, and, having seen it, to sing his deeds and teach it in every direction; he asks for the small anklets and golden lotus feet. The praise lists Ganesa's offerings, sesame, puffed rice, appam, fruit, roots, cane, sugar, gram and pittu, names him the elephant-faced brother, and names their mother Gauri, who bore the old universe and rescues worshippers from the waves of birth, at the side of the naked Siva, at Kachi.","n":"எண்பத்தெட்டு எட்டு இயல் தத்வம்: eighty-eight and eight, the ninety-six tattvas of the Saiva count. சகலமும் பற்றிப் பற்று அற நிற்கும் பொது: the Lord holds all and clings to none, the formula used again in 320; பொது, 'the common', makes him what all the quarrelling sects share. The plea ends in teaching (திசைதொறும் கற்பிக்கை), a rare missionary note.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","samayam","viyaabagam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:28"},"p:315":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":315,"t":["கறையிலங் குக்ரச் சத்தித ரிக்குஞ்","சரவணன் சித்தத் துக்குளொ ளிக்குங்","கரவடன் கொற்றக் குக்குட வத்தன் ...... தனிவீரக்","கழலிடும் பத்மக் கட்செவி வெற்பன்","பழநிமன் கச்சிக் கொற்றவன் மற்றுங்","கடகவஞ் சிக்குக் கர்த்தனெ னச்செந் ...... தமிழ்பாடிக்","குறையிலன் புற்றுக் குற்றம றுக்கும்","பொறைகள்நந் தற்பப் புத்தியை விட்டென்","குணமடங் கக்கெட் டுக்குண மற்றொன் ...... றிலதான","குணமடைந் தெப்பற் றுக்களு மற்றுங்","குறியொடுஞ் சுத்தப் பத்தரி ருக்குங்","குருபதஞ் சித்திக் கைக்கருள் சற்றுங் ...... கிடையாதோ","பிறைகரந் தைக்கொத் துப்பணி மத்தந்","தலையெலும் பப்புக் கொக்கிற கக்கம்","பிரமனன் றெட்டற் கற்றதி ருக்கொன் ...... றையும்வேணிப்","பிறவுநின் றொக்கத் தொக்கும ணக்குஞ்","சரணியம் பத்மக் கைக்கொடி முக்கண்","பெறுகரும் பத்தக் கத்தருள் நற்பங் ...... கயவாவி","திறைகொளுஞ் சித்ரக் குத்துமு லைக்கொம்","பறியுமந் தத்தைக் கைக்கக மொய்க்குந்","த்ரிபுரைசெம் பட்டுக் கட்டுநு சுப்பின் ...... திருவான","தெரிவையந் துர்க்கிச் சத்தியெ வர்க்குந்","தெரிவருஞ் சுத்தப் பச்சைநி றப்பெண்","சிறுவதொண் டர்க்குச் சித்திய ளிக்கும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet names Murugan Saravana of the fierce sakti, the thief who hides in the mind, the lord of the cock, the lord of the serpent hill, of Palani, of Kachi and of Vanji, and asks whether, singing him so in Tamil with flawless love, forbearance growing, pride left, his own qualities ended and having reached the quality that has no other, free of all attachment, he will not get even a little grace to attain the guru's feet where pure devotees abide. The praise is of the Goddess, worn at Siva's side with his crescent, karandai, serpent, datura, skull and konrai, Tripura, Durga, Sakti, the pure green-hued woman, whose son gives siddhi to his servants.","n":"சித்தத்துக்குள் ஒளிக்கும் கரவடன், 'the thief who hides in the mind', is a bold epithet for the indwelling Lord. குணம் அடங்கக் கெட்டு குணம் அற்று ஒன்று இலதான குணம் அடைந்து: the goal is a quality beyond the qualities. குருபதம் is both the guru's feet and the guru's state.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["guru","pattru","porai","thamizh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:28"},"p:316":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":316,"t":["செறிதரும் செப்பத் துற்பல வெற்பும்","பிறிதுமங் கத்தைக் குற்றவி ருப்புஞ்","சிகரிதுண் டிக்கக் கற்றத னிச்செஞ் ...... சுடர்வேலும்","திரள்புயங் கொத்துப் பட்டவ னைத்துந்","தெளியநெஞ் சத்துப் புற்றும யக்கம்","திகழ்ப்ரபஞ் சத்தைப் புற்புத மொக்கும் ...... படிநாடும்","அறிவறிந் தத்தற் கற்றது செப்புங்","கடவுளன் பத்தர்க கச்சம றுத்தன்","பருள்பவன் பொற்புக் கச்சியுள் நிற்கும் ...... பெருமானென்","றவிழுமன் புற்றுக் கற்றும னத்தின்","செயலொழிந் தெட்டப் பட்டத னைச்சென்","றடைதரும் பக்வத் தைத்தமி யெற்கென் ...... றருள்வாயே","குறியவன் செப்பப் பட்டஎ வர்க்கும்","பெரியவன் கற்பிக் கப்படு சுக்ரன்","குலைகுலைந் துட்கக் சத்யமி ழற்றுஞ் ...... சிறுபாலன்","குதலையின் சொற்குத் தர்க்கமு ரைக்குங்","கனகனங் கத்திற் குத்திநி ணச்செங்","குடர்பிடுங் கித்திக் குற்றமு கச்சிங் ...... கமுராரி","பொறிவிடுந் துத்திக் கட்செவி யிற்கண்","துயில்கொளுஞ் சக்ரக் கைக்கிரி சுத்தம்","புயலெனும் பொற்புப் பெற்றநி றத்தன் ...... ஜகதாதை","புனிதசங் கத்துக் கைத்தல நிர்த்தன்","பழையசந் தத்தைப் பெற்றம டப்பெண்","புகலுகொண் டற்குச் சித்திய ளிக்கும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks for the ripeness to reach the state attained when the mind's action ceases, through a love that opens as he learns of Murugan's hills, his vel that split the peak and his arms, and through the clear knowledge that sees the world, where delusion grows like an anthill, as a bubble; he names Murugan the god who told his father what he lacked, who cuts the fear of devotees, at Kachi. The praise tells of Vishnu, the dwarf who became greater than all while Sukra trembled, and the man-lion who tore out the entrails of Hiranya when the little boy Prahlada spoke the truth, the Murari who sleeps on the serpent with the discus, father of the world, whom Murugan grants siddhi.","n":"ப்ரபஞ்சத்தைப் புற்புதம் ஒக்கும்படி நாடும் அறிவு: the world seen as a bubble. பக்வம், ripeness, is the soul's readiness for grace. அத்தற்கு அற்றது செப்பும் கடவுள, 'the god who told his father what he lacked', points to the teaching of Siva. The Vamana and Narasimha stories are named by their details (குறியவன், சுக்ரன், சிறுபாலன் குதலை, கனகன்). The last lines on who receives siddhi are unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","gnaanam","guru","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:28"},"p:317":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":317,"t":["அரியயன் புட்பிக் கக்குழு மிக்கொண்","டமரர்வந் திக்கத் தட்டுரு வச்சென்","றவுணரங் கத்தைக் குத்திமு றித்தங் ...... கொருகோடி","அலகைநின் றொத்தித் தித்திய றுத்தும்","பலவியங் கொட்டச் சக்கடி கற்றந்","தரியுடன் பற்றிக் குச்சரி மெச்சும் ...... படிபாடிப்","பரிமுகங் கக்கச் செக்கண் விழித்தும்","பவுரிகொண் டெட்டுத் திக்கையு டைத்தும்","படுகளம் புக்குத் தொக்குந டிக்கும் ...... படிமோதிப்","படைபொருஞ் சத்திப் பத்மநி னைத்துஞ்","சரவணன் கச்சிப் பொற்பனெ னப்பின்","பரவியுஞ் சித்தத் துக்குவ ரத்தொண் ...... டடைவேனோ","பெரியதண் செச்சைக் கச்சணி வெற்பும்","சிறியவஞ் சிக்கொத் தெய்த்தநு சுப்பும்","ப்ரிதியொழிந் தொக்கக் கைக்கிளை துத்தங் ...... குரலாதி","பிரிவில்கண் டிக்கப் பட்டவு ருட்டும்","கமுகமுஞ் சிற்பச் சித்ரமு ருக்கும்","பிரதியண் டத்தைப் பெற்றருள் சிற்றுந் ...... தியும்நீலக்","கரியகொண் டற்கொப் பித்தக துப்புந்","திலதமுஞ் செப்பொற் பட்டமு முத்தின்","கனவடங் கட்டப் பட்டக ழுத்துந் ...... திருவான","கருணையுஞ் சுத்தப் பச்சைவ னப்புங்","கருதுமன் பர்க்குச் சித்திய ளிக்குங்","கவுரியம் பைக்குப் புத்ரஎ வர்க்கும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether, thinking of the lotus of the sakti that fought, while Vishnu and Brahma showered flowers, the gods worshipped and a crore of ghosts danced to the drums on the battlefield where the Asuras were pierced, and praising Saravana the beautiful one of Kachi, he will reach the boon of service in his mind. The praise is a long picture of the Goddess's body, her sandal-dressed breasts, slender waist, a voice holding the notes kaikkilai, tuttam and kural, the navel that bore the worlds, cloud-dark hair, tilak and pearl-hung neck, and her compassion that gives siddhi to devotees: Murugan is the son of Gauri Amba and lord of all.","n":"The first half is almost all battle, sung in drum syllables, with the plea folded into its last line (சித்தத்துக்கு வரத் தொண்டு அடைவேனோ). The Goddess's voice is described with the names of the Tamil musical notes (கைக்கிளை, துத்தம், குரல்).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thondu","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:28"},"p:318":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":318,"t":["கனிதருங் கொக்குக் கட்செவி வெற்பும்","பழநியுந் தெற்குச் சற்குரு வெற்புங்","கதிரையுஞ் சொற்குட் பட்டதி ருச்செந் ...... திலும்வேலும்","கனவிலுஞ் செப்பத் தப்புமெ னைச்சங்","கடவுடம் புக்குத் தக்கவ னைத்துங்","களவுகொண் டிட்டுக் கற்பனை யிற்கண் ...... சுழல்வேனைப்","புனிதனம் பைக்குக் கைத்தல ரத்நம்","பழையகங் கைக்குற் றப்புது முத்தம்","புவியிலன் றைக்கற் றெய்ப்பவர் வைப்பென் ...... றுருகாஎப்","பொழுதும் வந்திக்கைக் கற்றஎ னைப்பின்","பிழையுடன் பட்டுப் பத்தருள் வைக்கும்","பொறையையென் செப்பிச் செப்புவ தொப்பொன் ...... றுளதோதான்","அனனியம் பெற்றற் றற்றொரு பற்றுந்","தெளிதருஞ் சித்தர்க் குத்தெளி சிற்கொந்","தமலைதென் கச்சிப் பிச்சிம லர்க்கொந் ...... தளபாரை","அறவிநுண் பச்சைப் பொற்கொடி கற்கண்","டமதினுந் தித்திக் கப்படு சொற்கொம்","பகிலஅண் டத்துற் பத்திசெய் முத்தின் ...... பொலமேருத்","தனிவடம் பொற்புப் பெற்றமு லைக்குன்","றிணைசுமந் தெய்க்கப் பட்டநு சுப்பின்","தருணிசங் குற்றுத் தத்துதி ரைக்கம் ...... பையினு஡டே","தவமுயன் றப்பொற் றப்படி கைக்கொண்","டறமிரண் டெட்டெட் டெட்டும் வளர்க்கும்","தலைவிபங் கர்க்குச் சத்யமு ரைக்கும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"Not a plea but thanks: the poet calls himself one who never spoke even in dream of Murugan's mango of Kachi, the serpent hill, Palani, the southern hill of the true guru, Kathirai and Thiruchendur, who stole all that suits this body and whirled in fancies, who never melted and worshipped him as the jewel in Amba's hand, the new pearl of old Ganga and the treasure of the destitute, and asks what words could compare with the forbearance by which Murugan bore his faults and placed him among devotees. The praise names the Goddess of southern Kachi, sweeter than candy, mother of all worlds, the young woman who did penance in the waters of the Kampa, took the measures of grain and nurtures the thirty-two charities, and Murugan who told the truth to her consort.","n":"பிழையுடன் பட்டுப் பத்தருள் வைக்கும் பொறை: the forbearance here is the Lord's, who bears with the poet's faults. The Kanchi legend of the Goddess is told by its details: penance in the Kampa (கம்பையினூடே தவமுயன்று), the measure of grain (படி) and the thirty-two charities (அறம் இரண்டு எட்டு எட்டு எட்டு, read as a count). சத்யம் உரைக்கும், telling the truth to Siva, is the teaching of 6 and 12.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["porai","thiruvarul","adiyaar","kutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:28"},"p:319":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":319,"t":["தசைதுறுந் தொக்குக் கட்டளை சட்டஞ்","சரியவெண் கொக்குக் கொக்கந ரைத்தந்","தலையுடம் பெய்த்தெற் புத்தளை நெக்கிந் ...... த்ரியமாறித்","தடிகொடுந் திக்குத் தப்பந டக்கும்","தளர்வுறுஞ் சுத்தப் பித்தவி ருத்தன்","தகைபெறும் பற்கொத் துக்கள னைத்துங் ...... கழலாநின்","றசலருஞ் செச்செச் செச்செயெ னச்சந்","ததிகளும் சிச்சிச் சிச்சியெ னத்தங்","கரிவையும் துத்துத் துத்துவெ னக்கண் ...... டுமியாமற்","றவருநிந் திக்கத் தக்கபி றப்பிங்","கலமலஞ் செச்சைச் சித்ரம ணித்தண்","டையரவிந் தத்திற் புக்கடை தற்கென் ...... றருள்வாயே","குசைமுடிந் தொக்கப் பக்கரை யிட்டெண்","டிசையினுந் தத்தப் புத்தியை நத்துங்","குரகதங் கட்டிக் கிட்டிந டத்துங் ...... கதிர்நேமிக்","குலரதம் புக்கொற் றைக்கணை யிட்டெண்","டிரிபுரஞ் சுட்டுக் கொட்டைப ரப்புங்","குரிசில்வந் திக்கக் கச்சியில் நிற்குங் ...... கதிர்வேலா","திசைமுகன் தட்டுப் பட்டெழ வற்குஞ்","சிகரியுங் குத்துப் பட்டுவி ழத்தெண்","டிரையலங் கத்துப் புக்குல விச்சென் ...... றெதிரேறிச்","சிரமதுங் கப்பொற் கட்டிகை யிட்டன்","றவுணர்நெஞ் சிற்குத் திக்கறை கட்கஞ்","சிதறிநின் றொட்டிப் பொட்டெழ வெட்டும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet pictures himself old: the skin hanging loose on its frame, hair white as a crane, head and body worn, bones and joints slack, the senses changed, walking with a stick and staggering off course, a frail, half-mad old man whose teeth all fall, while others say 'chee chee', his descendants 'chi chi' and his own wife spits 'thu thu'; before that despised birth, he asks to reach the lotus feet with anklets. The praise tells of Siva, who yoked the Veda horses with the reins, mounted the chariot with the sun for its wheel and burned the three cities with one arrow, worshipping Murugan at Kachi, and of the vel that struck Brahma, pierced the mountain and the sea and cut down the Asuras.","n":"The scorn is given in sounds (செச்செச்செ, சிச்சிச்சி, துத்துத்து) set to the beat, the household's disgust heard rather than described. The poet asks for the feet before old age, not at the hour of death as in 28 or 68.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilamai","yaakkai","nilaiyaamai","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:28"},"p:320":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":320,"t":["புரைபடுஞ் செற்றக் குற்றம னத்தன்","தவமிலன் சுத்தச் சத்யஅ சத்யன்","புகலிலன் சுற்றச் செத்தையுள் நிற்குந் ...... துரிசாளன்","பொறையிலன் கொத்துத் தத்வவி கற்பஞ்","சகலமும் பற்றிப் பற்றற நிற்கும்","பொருளுடன் பற்றுச் சற்றுமில் வெற்றன் ...... கொடியேனின்","கரையறுஞ் சித்ரச் சொற்புகழ் கற்குங்","கலையிலன் கட்டைப் புத்தியன் மட்டன்","கதியிலன் செச்சைப் பொற்புய வெற்புங் ...... கதிர்வேலுங்","கதிரையுஞ் சக்ரப் பொற்றையு மற்றும்","பதிகளும் பொற்புக் கச்சியு முற்றுங்","கனவிலுஞ் சித்தத் திற்கரு திக்கொண் ...... டடைவேனோ","குரைதருஞ் சுற்றுச் சத்தச முத்ரங்","கதறிவெந் துட்கக் கட்புர துட்டன்","குலமடங் கக்கெட் டொட்டொழி யச்சென் ...... றொருநேமிக்","குவடொதுங் கச்சொர்க் கத்தரி டுக்கங்","கெடநடுங் கத்திக் கிற்கிரி வர்க்கங்","குலிசதுங் கக்கைக் கொற்றவ னத்தங் ...... குடியேறத்","தரைவிசும் பைச்சிட் டித்தஇ ருக்கன்","சதுர்முகன் சிட்சைப் பட்டொழி யச்சந்","ததமும்வந் திக்கப் பெற்றவர் தத்தம் ...... பகையோடத்","தகையதண் டைப்பொற் சித்ரவி சித்ரந்","தருசதங் கைக்கொத் தொத்துமு ழக்குஞ்","சரணகஞ் சத்திற் பொற்கழல் கட்டும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet heaps names on himself: a mind of hatred and fault, without penance, wholly false, without refuge, a sinner standing in the rubbish of kin, without forbearance, empty of any bond to the one who holds all the tattvas and stands free of them, cruel, without the art of learning Murugan's boundless praise, block-witted, base, without a way; he asks whether he will reach Murugan by thinking even in dream of his arms, his vel, Kathirai, the golden hill and the other shrines and all of Kachi. The praise tells of the vel that made the roaring seas cry out, ended Surapadman's clan, eased the gods' distress so that Indra returned to his city, and saw Brahma punished, and of the feet with golden anklets.","n":"The self-abuse (துரிசாளன், வெற்றன், கட்டைப் புத்தியன், மட்டன்) is a set piece, as in 29. Inside it is the formula of 314, the Lord who holds all and stands free (சகலமும் பற்றிப் பற்று அற நிற்கும் பொருள்): the poet's lack is that he has no bond to him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kutram","pedhaimai","pattru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:28"},"p:321":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":321,"t":["சலமலம் விட்டத் தடம்பெ ருங்குடில்","சகலவி னைக்கொத் திருந்தி டும்படி","சதிரவு றுப்புச் சமைந்து வந்தொரு ...... தந்தைதாயும்","தரவரு பொய்க்குட் கிடந்த கந்தலி","லுறையுமு யிர்ப்பைச் சமன்து ரந்தொரு","தனியிலி ழுக்கப் படுந்த ரங்கமும் ...... வந்திடாமுன்","பலவுரு வத்தைப் பொருந்தி யன்றுயர்","படியுநெ ளிக்கப் படர்ந்த வன்கண","படமயில் புக்குத் துரந்து கொண்டிகல் ...... வென்றிவேலா","பரிமள மிக்கச் சிவந்த நின்கழல்","பழுதற நற்சொற் றெரிந்து அன்பொடு","பகர்வதி னிச்சற் றுகந்து தந்திட ...... வந்திடாயோ","சிலையுமெ னப்பொற் சிலம்பை முன்கொடு","சிவமய மற்றுத் திடங்கு லைந்தவர்","திரிபுர மத்தைச் சுடுந்தி னந்திரி ...... திண்கையாளி","திருமகள் கச்சுப் பொருந்தி டுந்தன","தெரிவையி ரக்கத் துடன்பி றந்தவள்","திசைகளி லொக்கப் படர்ந்தி டம்பொரு ...... கின்றஞானக்","கலைகள ணைக்கொத் தடர்ந்து வம்பலர்","நதிகொள கத்திற் பயந்து கம்பர்மெய்","கருகஇ டத்திற் கலந்தி ருந்தவள் ...... கஞ்சபாதங்","கருணைமி குத்துக் கசிந்து ளங்கொடு","கருதும வர்க்குப் பதங்கள் தந்தருள்","கவுரிதி ருக்கொட் டமர்ந்த இநதிரர் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"Before Death drives out and drags away alone the breath dwelling in this rag of a false body, a great hut that lets out urine and filth, built with limbs to hold all the deeds and given by a father and mother, the poet asks Murugan to come and gladly grant him the power to speak with love, in good and flawless words, of his fragrant red feet. The praise is of the Goddess: Siva who burned the three cities, and she who, frightened by the flooding river, embraced Kampar so that his body was marked, and who gives her feet to those who think of her lotus feet; Murugan is Gauri's son, lord of Indras at Thirukkottam.","n":"சல மலம் விட்ட ... குடில் and தரவரு பொய்க்குள் கிடந்த கந்தல்: the body is a latrine-hut and a rag given by parents. The Kanchi legend of the Goddess embracing Ekambaresvara (கம்பர்) in the flood is named by its details. திருக்கொட்டம் is the Kamakottam of Kanchi. The battle lines on Sura taking many forms and the peacock are hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kootru","yaakkai","pugazh","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:28"},"p:322":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":322,"t":["தலைவலை யத்துத் தரம்பெ றும்பல","புலவர் மதிக்கச் சிகண்டி குன்றெறி","தருமயில் செச்சைப் புயங்க யங்குற ...... வஞ்சியோடு","தமனிய முத்துச் சதங்கை கிண்கிணி","தழுவிய செக்கச் சிவந்த பங்கய","சரணமும் வைத்துப் பெரும்ப்ர பந்தம்வி ...... னம்புகாளப்","புலவனெ னத்தத் துவந்த ரந்தெரி","தலைவனெ னத்தக் கறஞ்செ யுங்குண","புருஷனெ னப்பொற் பதந்த ருஞ்சன ...... னம்பெறாதோ","பொறையனெ னப்பொய்ப் ப்ரபஞ்ச மஞ்சிய","துறவனெ னத்திக் கியம்பு கின்றது","புதுமைய லச்சிற் பரம்பொ ருந்துகை ...... தந்திடாதோ","குலசயி லத்துப் பிறந்த பெண்கொடி","யுலகடை யப்பெற் றவுந்தி யந்தணி","குறைவற முப்பத் திரண்ட றம்புரி ...... கின்றபேதை","குணதரி சக்ரப் ப்ரசண்ட சங்கரி","கணபண ரத்நப் புயங்க கங்கணி","குவடுகு னித்துப் புரஞ்சு டுஞ்சின ...... வஞ்சிநீலி","கலபவி சித்ரச் சிகண்டி சுந்தரி","கடியவி டத்தைப் பொதிந்த கந்தரி","கருணைவி ழிக்கற் பகந்தி கம்பரி ...... யெங்களாயி","கருதிய பத்தர்க் கிரங்கு மம்பிகை","சுருதிது திக்கப் படுந்த்ரி யம்பகி","கவுரிதி ருக்கொட் டமர்ந்த இந்திரர் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks for a birth in which, having sung great poems on the peacock, Murugan's arms, Valli and his red lotus feet with anklets, he will be honoured by many poets as a true poet, a master who knows the tattvas, a virtuous man of good qualities, and gain the golden feet; and whether the world's calling him forbearing and a renunciate who fears the false world will not bring him to the supreme consciousness. The praise is wholly of the Goddess: daughter of the mountain, whose womb bore the world, the Brahmin lady who keeps the thirty-two charities without lack, Sankari, Neeli, the peacock beauty who holds the poison in her throat, Digambari, 'our mother', Ambika, Tryambaki, Gauri: Murugan is lord of Indras at Thirukkottam.","n":"An unusual plea: for fame as a poet and a name for virtue (புலவன் எனத் ... தலைவன் எனத் ... குண புருஷன் என), joined to release. புதுமை அல(ச்) சிற்பரம் பொருந்துகை may mean 'it is no wonder' or a 'new union with the supreme'. The thirty-two charities (முப்பத்திரண்டு அறம்) are here named outright, as in 318. Shares its closing line with 321.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","thuravu","porai","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:28"},"p:323":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":323,"t":["இதத்துப்பற் றிதழ்த்துப்பற் றிருட்பொக்கக் கருத்திட்டத்","தியக்கத்திற் றியக்குற்றுச் ...... சுழலாதே","எலுப்புச்சுக் கிலக்கத்தத் தடித்தொக்குக் கடத்தைப்பெற்","றெடுத்துப்பற் றடுத்தற்பத் ...... துழலாதே","சுதத்தத்தச் சதத்தத்தப் பதத்தர்க்குற் றவற்றைச்சொற்","றுவக்கிற்பட் டவத்தைப்பட் ...... டயராதே","துணைச்செப்பத் தலர்கொத்துற் பலச்செச்சைத் தொடைப்பத்திக்","கடப்பப்பொற் கழற்செப்பித் ...... தொழுவேனோ","கொதித்துக்குத் திரக்கொக்கைச் சதித்துப்பற் றிகைக்குட்பொற்","குலத்தைத்குத் திரத்தைக்குத் ...... தியவேலா","குறத்தத்தைத் கறத்தத்திக் குமுத்தத்தத் தமொக்கிக்குக்","குலத்துக்குக் குடக்கொற்றக் ...... கொடியோனே","கதச்சுத்தச் சுதைச்சித்ரக் களிற்றுக்கொற் றவற்குக்கற்","பகச்சொர்க்கப் புரப்பொற்பைப் ...... புரிவோனே","கடுக்கைக்கட் செவிக்கற்றைச் சடைப்பக்கக் கொடிக்கற்புக்","கடற்கச்சிப் பதிச்சொக்கப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks not to whirl in the confusion of desire for women's coral lips and dark hair, not to wander tending this pot of bone, semen, blood, fat and skin, and not to languish in suffering, but to praise and worship the golden feet with their garland of lotus and kadamba. The praise names the vel that pierced the Krauncha and the golden mountain, the lord of the cock banner, who gave Indra his golden heavenly city, at Kachi, where Siva of the konrai and matted hair has the Goddess of chastity at his side.","n":"எலுப்பு, சுக்கிலம், அத்தம், தடி, தொக்கு: the body is listed by its parts (bone, semen, blood, fat, skin) and called a pot (கடம்). The third line (சுதத்தத்தச் சதத்தத்தப் பதத்தர்க்கு ...) is too dense to divide with confidence.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","yaakkai","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:29"},"p:324":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":324,"t":["எனக்குச்சற் றுனக்குச்சற் றெனக்கத்தத் தவர்க்கிச்சைப்","பொருட்பொற்றட் டிடிக்கைக்குக் ...... குடில்மாயம்","எனக்கட்டைக் கிடைப்பட்டிட் டனற்சுட்டிட் டடக்கைக்குப்","பிறக்கைக்குத் தலத்திற்புக் ...... கிடியாமுன்","தினைக்குட்சித் திரக்கொச்சைக் குறத்தத்தைத் தனத்தைப்பொற்","பெறச்செச்சைப் புயத்தொப்பித் ...... தணிவோனே","செருக்கிச்சற் றுறுக்கிச்சொற் பிரட்டத்துட் டரைத்தப்பித்","திரட்டப்பிக் கழற்செப்பத் ...... திறல்தாராய்","பனைக்கைக்கொக் கனைத்தட்டுப் படக்குத்திப் படச்சற்பப்","பணத்துட்கக் கடற்றுட்கப் ...... பொரும்வேலா","பரப்பற்றுச் சுருக்கற்றுப் பதைப்பற்றுத் திகைப்பற்றுப்","பலிப்பப்பத் தருக்கொப்பித் ...... தருள்வாழ்வே","கனிக்குத்திக் கனைத்துச்சுற் றிடப்பச்சைக் கனப்பக்ஷிக்","கிடைப்புக்குக் களிப்புக்குத் ...... திரிவோனே","கலிக்கொப்பிற் சலிப்பற்றுக் கதிக்கொத்திட் டெழிற்சத்திக்","கடற்கச்சிப் பதிச்சொக்கப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"Before the kin cry 'a little for me, a little for you' over the wealth and the gold plate, call the body-hut an illusion, lay it on the logs, burn it, bury it, and the poet falls to earth to be born again, he asks for the strength to escape the arrogant, threatening, word-twisting wicked and to sing Murugan's feet. The praise names the one who embraced Valli of the millet field, the vel that struck down the mango so that the serpent's hoods and the sea trembled, the life who gives fruit to devotees free of spreading, shrinking, trembling and bewilderment, and the one who circled the world on the green peacock for the fruit, at Kachi.","n":"எனக்குச் சற்று உனக்குச் சற்று: the heirs' squabble at the deathbed is quoted, and the same words return in 325 as the poet's own craving. The plea is unusual in asking to escape wicked men (துட்டர்) rather than women. கனிக்குத் திக்கனைத்துச் சுற்றிட is the circling of the world for the fruit, as in 3 and 30.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saakkaadu","selvam","kayamai","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:29"},"p:325":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":325,"t":["இறைச்சிப்பற் றிரத்தத்திட் டிசைக்கொக்கப் பரப்பப்பட்","டெலுப்புக்கட் டளைச்சுற்றிச் ...... சுவர்கோலி","எடுத்துச்செப் பெனக்கட்டிப் புதுக்குப்புத் தகத்திற்புக்","கெனக்குச்சற் றுனக்குச்சற் ...... றெனுமாசைக்","சிறைக்கொத்திப் பிறப்பிற்பட் டுறக்கச்சொப் பனத்துற்றுத்","திகைக்கப்பட் டவத்தைப்பட் ...... டுழலாதுன்","திருப்பத்மத் திறத்தைப்பற் றுகைக்குச்சித் திரத்தைச்சொற்","றிதக்கொற்றப் புகழ்ச்செப்பித் ...... திரிவேனோ","பிறைச்செக்கர்ப் புரைக்கொத்துச் சடைப்பச்சைக் கொடிக்கிச்சைப்","பிறக்குற்றத் திருப்பக்கச் ...... சிவநாதர்","பெருக்கப்புத் தடக்கைக்கற் பகத்தொப்பைக் கணத்துக்குப்","பிரசித்தக் கொடிக்குக்டக் ...... கொடியோனே","பறைக்கொட்டிக் களைச்சுற்றக் குறட்செக்கட் கணத்திற்குப்","பலிக்குப்பச் சுடற்குத்திப் ...... பகிர்வேலா","பணப்பத்திக் கணத்துத்திப் படுக்கைக்கச் சபத்திச்சைப்","படுக்கச்சிப் பதிச்சொக்கப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet calls the body a house walled with flesh and blood and framed with bones, and himself caught in the prison of the craving that says 'a little for me, a little for you', fallen into birth, bewildered in sleep and dream and suffering the states of the soul; he asks whether he will rather wander singing the power and fame of Murugan's lotus feet. The praise names Siva of the red crescent and matted hair, who loves the green creeper at his side, the brother of the big-bellied wishing tree Ganesa, the lord of the cock banner, the vel that gives raw flesh to the drum-beating ghosts, at Kachi.","n":"உறக்கச் சொப்பனத்து உற்றுத் திகைக்கப்பட்டு அவத்தைப்பட்டு: the states of sleep and dream are named as part of the soul's suffering. The last line (படுக்கைக் கச்சபத்து இச்சைப்படு கச்சி) may name the tortoise (கச்சபம) of the Kachchapesvara shrine, linked with Vishnu, but the division is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","avaa","avaththai","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:29"},"p:326":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":326,"t":["கடத்தைப்பற் றெனப்பற்றிக் கருத்துற்றுக் களித்திட்டுக்","கயற்கட்பொற் பிணைச்சித்ரத் ...... தனமாதர்","கலைக்குட்பட் டறக்கத்திச் சலித்துக்கட் டளைச்சொற்பொய்த்","திரைக்குட்பட் டறச்செத்திட் ...... டுயிர்போனால்","எடுத்துக்கொட் டிடக்கட்டைப் படத்தெட்டத் தணற்றட்டக்","கொளுத்திச்சுற் றவர்ப்பற்றற் ...... றவர்போமுன்","இணக்கிப்பத் திமைச்செச்சைப் பதத்தைப்பற் றுகைக்குச்சொற்","றமிழ்க்கொற்றப் புகழ்செப்பித் ...... திரிவேனோ","அடைத்திட்டுப் புடைத்துப்பொற் பதச்சொர்க்கத் தனைச்சுற்றிட்","டலைப்புப்பற் றெனச்சொற்றிட் ...... டறுசூரை","அடித்துச்செற் றிடித்துப்பொட் டெழப்பொர்ப்புப் படக்குத்திட்","டலைத்துச்சுற் றலைத்தெற்றுக் ...... கடல்மாயப்","புடைத்திட்டுப் படிக்குட்செற் றடப்புக்குக் கதத்துக்கக்","கயிற்கொக்கைப் படக்குத்திப் ...... பொருவோனே","புனத்திற்பொற் குறத்திக்குப் புணர்க்கொத்தப் பசப்பெத்திப்","பூணர்க்கச்சிப் பதிச்சொக்கப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses that he took the body as his own, delighted in women with fish-eyes and painted breasts, fell into their arts, cried out and grew weary in the waves of their false words; before, when he dies, they carry him to the drums, lay him on the logs, set the fire and the kin give him up and go, he asks whether he will join in devotion, hold the kadamba-adorned feet and wander singing their fame in Tamil. The praise tells of Surapadman driven into the sea and struck down, the mango pierced, and Valli of the millet field embraced, at Kachi.","n":"கடத்தைப் பற்று எனப் பற்றி: the first error is clinging to the pot of the body as one's own. The funeral is told quickly (எடுத்துக் கொட்டிடக் கட்டைப்படத் ... அனல் ... கொளுத்தி), and the kin let go (பற்றற்றவர் போமுன்) before the poet does.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","saakkaadu","nilaiyaamai","thamizh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:29"},"p:327":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":327,"t":["கருப்பற்றிப் பருத்தொக்கத் தரைக்குற்றிட் டுருப்பெற்றுக்","கருத்திற்கட் பொருட்பட்டுப் ...... பயில்காலங்","கணக்கிட்டுப் பிணக்கிட்டுக் கதித்திட்டுக் கொதித்திட்டுக்","கயிற்றிட்டுப் பிடித்திட்டுச் ...... சமனாவி","பெருக்கப்புத் தியிற்பட்டுப் புடைத்துக்கக் கிளைப்பிற்பொய்ப்","பிணத்தைச்சுட் டகத்திற்புக் ...... கனைவோரும்","பிறத்தற்சுற் றமுற்றுற்றிட் டழைத்துத்தொக் கறக்கத்துப்","பிறப்புப்பற் றறச்செச்சைக் ...... கழல்தாராய்","பொருப்புக்கர்ப் புரக்கச்சுத் தனப்பொற்புத் தினைப்பச்சைப்","புனக்கொச்சைக் குறத்தத்தைக் ...... கினியோனே","புரத்தைச்சுட் டெரித்துப்பற் றலர்க்குப்பொற் பதத்துய்ப்பைப்","புணர்த்தப்பித் தனைக்கற்பித் ...... தருள்வோனே","செருக்கக்குக் கரைக்குத்திச் செருப்புக்குப் பிடித்தெற்றிச்","சினத்திட்டுச் சிதைத்திட்டுப் ...... பொரும்வீரா","திருத்தத்திற் புகற்சுத்தத் தமிழ்ச்செப்புத் த்ரயச்சித்ரத்","திருக்கச்சிப் பதிச்சொக்கப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet traces a life from the womb, growing, falling to earth and taking form, caught by things seen, until Yama counts its days, quarrels, rushes in, rages, throws the noose and seizes the breath, and the kin burn the false corpse and go home; he asks that birth and its clinging end and he be given the kadamba-adorned feet. The praise names Murugan dear to Valli of the millet field, the one who taught the Madman who burned the cities and gave his foes his golden feet, the hero who fought the mango and the boar-like demons, at Kachi where pure Tamil of the three kinds is spoken.","n":"Yama's coming is given as a string of verbs (கணக்கிட்டு, பிணக்கிட்டு, கதித்திட்டு, கொதித்திட்டு, கயிற்றிட்டு, பிடித்திட்டு), counting, quarrelling, rushing, raging, noosing, seizing. பித்தனைக் கற்பித்தருள்வோனே names the teaching of Siva (the பித்தன்) without naming Om. த்ரயச் சித்ரத் தமிழ் is the threefold Tamil of verse, music and drama.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","kootru","saakkaadu","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:29"},"p:328":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":328,"t":["கறுக்கப்பற் றுவர்ப்பிட்டுச் சிரித்துச்சற் றுறுக்கிக்கட்","பிறக்கிட்டுப் படக்கற்பித் ...... திளைஞோர்தங்","கழுத்தைச்சிக் கெனக்கட்டித் தனச்செப்புப் படக்குத்திட்","டுருக்கிக்கற் பழிக்கப்பொற் ...... பெழுகாதல்","புறப்பட்டுக் களிக்கக்கற் புதர்தைப்பிட் டரக்கிப்பொற்","பணிக்கட்டிற் புறத்துற்றுப் ...... புணர்மாதர்","பொருத்தத்தைத் தவிர்த்துச்சற் றிரக்ஷித்துப் புரப்பப்பொற்","பதத்தைப்பெற் றிருக்கைக்குப் ...... பெருவேனோ","திறற்கொக்கைப் படக்குத்திச் செருக்கிக்கொக் கரித்துச்சக்","கரிக்குப்புத் திரற்குற்றுத் ...... தளைபூணச்","சினத்துப்பொற் பொருப்பைப்பொட் டெழுத்தித்திக் கரித்துப்புத்","திரத்தத்திற் சிரித்துற்றுப் ...... பலபேய்கள்","பறிக்கப்பச் சிறைச்சிக்கட் கறிக்குப்பைச் சிரச்சிக்குப்","பரப்பொய்க்கட் டறப்புக்குப் ...... பொருதோனே","பணிச்செச்சைத் தொடைச்சித்ரப் புயத்துக்ரப் படைச்சத்திப்","படைக்கச்சிப் பதிச்சொக்கப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women who darken their teeth with betel, laugh, frown and turn their eyes, bind young men by the neck, press their breasts on them, melt them and spoil their virtue, and unite on gold-adorned beds, and asks whether, avoiding such unions, he will gain the golden feet to guard him. The praise names the vel that pierced the mango and the golden mountain, Brahma, son of the discus-bearer, fettered, and the ghosts tearing raw flesh on the battlefield, and Murugan of the kadamba garland and the fierce sakti, at Kachi.","n":"சக்கரிக்குப் புத்திரற்கு உற்றுத் தளைபூண: Brahma, son of Vishnu, put in fetters, the Om story told only by its outcome. The courtesans' arts are listed but money is not named.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","punarchi","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:29"},"p:329":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":329,"t":["அற்றைக் கிரைதேடி அத்தத் ...... திலுமாசை","பற்றித் தவியாத பற்றைப் ...... பெறுவேனோ","வெற்றிக் கதிர்வேலா வெற்பைத் ...... தொளைசீலா","கற்றுற் றுணர்போதா கச்சிப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A four-line song: the poet asks whether he will gain the attachment that does not suffer, seeking each day's food and clinging to wealth. The praise names the victorious vel, the one who pierced the mountain, the wisdom known by learning and experience, at Kachi.","n":"தவியாத பற்று: the cure for the clinging that suffers is another clinging, to the Lord; the line can also be divided to ask for freedom from the clinging that seeks food and wealth. கற்று உற்று உணர் போதா: Murugan as the wisdom reached by learning and by experience.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pattru","avaa","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:29"},"p:330":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":330,"t":["முட்டுப் பட்டுக் ...... கதிதோறும்","முற்றச் சுற்றிப் ...... பலநாளும்","தட்டுப் பட்டுச் ...... சுழல்வேனைச்","சற்றுப் பற்றக் ...... கருதாதோ","வட்டப் புட்பத் ...... தலமீதே","வைக்கத் தக்கத் ...... திருபாதா","கட்டத் தற்றத் ...... தருள்வோனே","கச்சிச் சொக்கப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A short song: the poet, who wanders stumbling through every birth for many days, colliding and whirling, asks whether Murugan will not think to take hold of him a little. The praise names the holy feet fit to be placed on the round flower, the one who gives grace to those whose bonds are cut, at Kachi.","n":"கதிதோறும் ... சுழல்வேனை: கதி here is each birth or state the soul passes through. வட்டப் புட்பத் தலம் may be the lotus of the heart where the feet are set.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","thiruvarul","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:29"},"p:331":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":331,"t":["அற்றைக் கற்றைக் கொப்பித் தொப்பித்","தத்தத் தத்தத் ...... தருவோர்தாள்","அர்ச்சித் திச்சித் தக்கத் தக்கத்","தொக்குத் திக்குக் ...... குடில்பேணிச்","செற்றைப் புற்சொற் கற்றுக் கற்றுச்","செத்துச் செத்துப் ...... பிறவாதே","செப்பச் செப்பப் பச்சைப் பச்சைச்","செச்சைச் செச்சைக் ...... கழல்தாராய்","துற்றுப் பிற்புக் குற்றக் கொக்கைத்","துட்கத் திட்கப் ...... பொரும்வேலா","சுத்தப் பத்திச் சித்ரச் சொர்க்கச்","சொர்க்கத் தத்தைக் ...... கினியோனே","கற்றைப் பொற்றைப் பற்றக் குத்திக்","கத்தக் கத்தக் ...... களைவோனே","கற்புச் சத்திப் பொற்புச் சத்திக்","கச்சிச் சொக்கப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks not to worship the feet of those who give wealth, cherishing the body-hut, learning worthless words and dying and dying to be born again, but to be given Murugan's green kadamba-adorned feet to praise. The praise names the vel that struck the mango, the one sweet to Valli, the one who pierced the golden mountain, at Kachi.","n":"அத்தத் தத்தத் தருவோர் தாள் அர்ச்சித்து: worshipping the feet of patrons who give gold is set against worshipping the Lord's feet, the confession of 310 in brief. செத்துச் செத்துப் பிறவாதே: repeated death and birth. The doubled syllables of the rhythm make the division of the first lines uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iravu","piravi","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:29"},"p:332":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":332,"t":["சுத்தச் சித்தத் தொற்பத் தர்க்குச்","சுத்தப் பட்டிட் ...... டமுறாதே","தொக்கப் பொக்கச் சிற்கட் சிக்குட்","சொற்குற் றத்துத் ...... துறைநாடி","பித்தத் தைப்பற் றித்தைத் தற்றுற்","றொத்துக் கித்திப் ...... பிணிமாதர்","பெட்டிற் கட்டுத் தட்டுப் பட்டுப்","பிற்பட் டிட்டுத் ...... தளர்வேனோ","அத்தத் தத்திக் கத்தற் கெய்த்தத்","தத்திக் கத்துப் ...... பலமீவாய்","அர்ச்சித் துப்பொற் செக்கொச் சைத்தத்","தைக்குச் செச்சைத் ...... தொடைசூழ்வாய்","கத்தத் தித்தத் தத்திற் கொக்கைக்","கைத்தச் சத்திப் ...... படையேவுங்","கற்புச் சத்திப் பொற்புச் சத்திக்","கச்சிச் சொக்கப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether, not joining the pure devotees of pure mind, seeking crooked words, caught in the madness of women who bring sickness, he will be trapped in their snare, fall behind and languish. The praise names Murugan adorning Valli with the kadamba garland, sending the sakti against the mango, at Kachi of the chaste and beautiful Sakti.","n":"பிணிமாதர் பெட்டிற் கட்டுத் தட்டுப்பட்டு: the women are a sickness and a box that traps. Several lines, especially the praise of the fruit given (பலம் ஈவாய்), are too dense to read confidently.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","adiyaar"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:29"},"p:333":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":333,"t":["கொக்குக் கொக்கத் தலையிற் பற்றுச் சிக்கத் தளகக்","கொத்துற் றுக்குப் பிணியுற் ...... றவனாகிக்","குக்கிக் கக்கிக் கடையிற் பற்றத் துற்றுக் கழலக்","கொத்தைச் சொற்கற் றுலகிற் ...... பலபாஷை","திக்கித் திக்கிக் குளறிச் செப்பித் தப்பிக் கெடுபொய்ச்","செற்றைச் சட்டைக் குடிலைச் ...... சுமைபேணும்","சிக்கற் றுட்குக் கருணைச் சுத்தச் சித்தித் தமிழைத்","திட்டத் துக்குப் புகலப் ...... பெறுவேனோ","அக்கிட் டிக்கிட் டமருக் கொட்டிக் கிட்டிட் டெதிரிட்","டத்ரத் தெற்றிக் கடுகப் ...... பொருசூரன்","அச்சுக் கெட்டுப் படைவிட் டச்சப் பட்டுக் கடலுட்","புக்குப் பட்டுத் துருமத் ...... தடைவாகத்","தக்குத் திக்குத் தறுகட் டொக்குத் தொக்குற் றதுகட்","கைக்கொட் டிட்டிட் டுடல்சிற் ...... கணமாடிச்","சத்திக் குத்தித் துடியிற் சத்திக் கக்கைச் சமர்செய்ச்","சத்திக் கச்சிக் குமரப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet pictures himself old, hair white as a crane, its tufts falling, diseased, coughing and vomiting, learning crooked speech and stammering in many languages, carrying the false, rubbish shirt of a body-hut; he asks whether, free of that tangle, he will gain grace to speak with conviction the pure Tamil of siddhi. The praise, in drum syllables, tells of Surapadman who lost his strength, threw down his arms in fear and entered the sea as a tree, the ghosts clapping and dancing, and the sakti, at Kachi.","n":"The cure for the old man's stammer (திக்கித் திக்கிக் குளறி) is pure Tamil spoken firmly (சுத்தச் சித்தித் தமிழைத் திட்டத்துக்குப் புகல). செற்றைச் சட்டைக் குடில்: the body is rubbish, a shirt and a hut at once. Sura in the sea as a tree (துருமம்) is the mango.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ilamai","yaakkai","thamizh","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:29"},"p:334":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":334,"t":["தத்தித் தத்திச் சட்டப் பட்டுச்","சத்தப் படுமைக் ...... கடலாலே","சர்ப்பத் தத்திற் பட்டுக் கெட்டுத்","தட்டுப் படுமப் ...... பிறையாலே","சித்தத் துக்குப் பித்துற் றுச்சச்","சித்ரக் கொடியுற் ...... றழியாதே","செப்பக் கொற்றச் சிற்பப் பத்திச்","செச்சைத் தொடையைத் ...... தரவேணும்","கொத்துத் திக்குப் பத்துட் புக்குக்","குத்திக் கிரியைப் ...... பொரும்வேலா","கொச்சைப் பொச்சைப் பொற்பிற் பச்சைக்","கொச்சைக் குறவிக் ...... கினியோனே","சுத்தப் பத்தத் தர்க்குச் சித்தத்","துக்கத் தையொழித் ...... திடும்வீரா","சொர்க்கத் துக்கொப் புற்றக் கச்சிச்","சொக்கப் பதியிற் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"Spoken for a girl sick with love for Murugan: the speaker asks that the painted creeper not be maddened and perish because of the roaring dark sea and the crescent caught by the serpent, and that Murugan give his kadamba garland. The praise names the vel that pierced the mountain and fought in the ten directions, the one sweet to the hunter girl Valli, the hero who ends the sorrow of pure devotees, at Kachi.","n":"This is akam poetry in another's voice, a mother's or friend's plea for a girl, not the poet's confession. The sea's roar and the crescent are the classical torments of the lovesick; சர்ப்பத்து அத்திற் பட்டு, the moon seized by the serpent, is the eclipse. The garland asked for is the lover's token.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:29"},"p:335":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":335,"t":["பொக்குப்பைக் கத்தத் தொக்குப்பைக் குத்துப்","பொய்த்தெத்துத் தத்துக் ...... குடில்பேணிப்","பொச்சைப்பிச் சற்பக் கொச்சைச்சொற் கற்றுப்","பொற்சித்ரக் கச்சுக் ...... கிரியார்தோய்","துக்கத்துக் கத்திற் சிக்குப்பட் டிட்டுத்","துக்கித்துக் கெய்த்துச் ...... சுழலாதே","சுத்தச்சித் தத்துப் பத்திப்பத் தர்க்கொத்","துச்சற்றர்ச் சிக்கப் ...... பெறுவேனோ","திக்குத்திக் கற்றுப் பைத்தத்தத் திக்குச்","செற்பத்ரக் கொக்கைப் ...... பொரும்வேலா","செப்பச்சொர்க் கத்துச் செப்பொற்றத் தைக்குச்","செச்சைக்கொத் தொப்பித் ...... தணிவோனே","கக்கக்கைத் தக்கக் கக்கட்கக் கக்கிக்","கட்கத்தத் தர்க்குப் ...... பெரியோனே","கற்றைப்பொற் றெத்தப் பெற்றப்பொற் சிற்பக்","கச்சிக்குட் சொக்கப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks not to whirl in sorrow, tending this hollow sack of skin, a false and lying hut, learning crude and obscene speech and caught in the grief of women with hill-like breasts in golden bodices, but to join the pure devotees of pure mind and worship a little. The praise names the vel that fought the mango, Murugan who adorns Valli with the kadamba cluster, the great one, at Kachi.","n":"பொக்குப் பை ... தொக்குப் பை: the body is a hollow bag of skin. The plea asks only to worship 'a little' (சற்று அர்ச்சிக்க), among the devotees. The line beginning கக்கக் கைத்தக்க is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","kaamam","adiyaar"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:29"},"p:336":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":336,"t":["அயிலப்புக் கயலப்புத் தலைமெச்சுற் பலநச்சுக்","கணுரத்தைக் கனவெற்புத் ...... தனமேகம்","அளகக்கொத் தெனவொப்பிப் புளுகிச்சொற் பலகற்பித்","திளகிக்கற் புளநெக்குத் ...... தடுமாறித்","துயில்விட்டுச் செயல்விட்டுத் துயர்வுற்றுக் கயர்வுற்றுத்","தொடியர்க்கிப் படியெய்த்துச் ...... சுழலாதே","சுருதிப்பொற் பொருள்செக்கர்க் குரவிட்டுத் தமர்பற்றித்","தொழுசெச்சைக் கழல்பற்றிப் ...... பணிவேனோ","புயலத்தைக் குயில்தத்தைக் கிளைபுக்குத் தொளைபச்சைப்","புனமுத்தைப் புணர்சித்ரப் ...... புயவீரா","புரவிக்கொட் பிரதற்றத் திருள்திக்கிப் படிமட்கப்","புகல்பொற்குக் குடவெற்றிக் ...... கொடியோனே","கயிலச்சுத் தரதத்துச் சயிலத்துத் தரநிற்கக்","கரணிச்சித் தருள்கச்சிப் ...... பதியோனே","கடலிற்கொக் கடல்கெட்டுக் கரமுட்கத் தரமுட்கப்","பொருசத்திக் கரசொக்கப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women whose eyes are vel, fish and poison, breasts like mountains, hair like dark clouds, who lie with many words and melt a man's heart, so that he staggers, loses sleep and action, grieves and tires, and asks whether, not whirling so for bangled women, he will hold and worship the kadamba-adorned feet that are the golden meaning of the Vedas. The praise names the hero of the arms that embraced Valli, the pearl of the millet field, the lord of the cock banner, the one at Kachi, and the vel that destroyed the mango in the sea.","n":"துயில்விட்டுச் செயல்விட்டுத் துயர்வுற்றுக் கயர்வுற்று: infatuation as sleeplessness, inaction, grief and exhaustion. சுருதிப் பொற்பொருள்: the feet are the Vedas' golden meaning. The line on Kailasa (கயிலச் சுத்த ரதத்துச் சயிலத்து) is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","mayal","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:29"},"p:337":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":337,"t":["கச்சிட் டணிமுலை தைச்சிட் டுருவிய","மச்சக் கொடிமதன் ...... மலராலுங்","கச்சைக் கலைமதி நச்சுக் கடலிடை","அச்சப் படவெழு ...... மதனாலும்","பிச்சுற் றிவளுள மெய்ச்சுத் தளர்வது","சொச்சத் தரமல ...... இனிதான","பிச்சிப் புதுமலர் வைச்சுச் சொருகிய","செச்சைத் தொடையது ...... தரவேணும்","பச்சைத் திருவுமை யிச்சித் தருளிய","கச்சிப் பதிதனி ...... லுறைவோனே","பற்றிப் பணிபவர் குற்றப் பகைகெட","உற்றுப் பொரவல ...... கதிர்வேலா","இச்சித் தழகிய கொச்சைக் குறமகள்","மெச்சித் தழுவிய ...... திருமார்பா","எட்டுக் குலகிரி முட்டப் பொடிபட","வெட்டித் துணிசெய்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"Spoken for a girl sick with love: wounded by the flowers of Kama with his fish banner and by the moon that rose to fright from the poison sea, her heart is maddened and she grows weak, which is no small thing; the speaker asks Murugan to give his kadamba garland tucked with fresh jasmine. The praise names Murugan dwelling at Kachi, which green Uma desired, the vel that fights to end the faults and enemies of those who cling to him, the chest Valli embraced, and the one who cut the eight great mountains to dust.","n":"Akam in another's voice, as in 334: இவள் உளம், 'her heart', is spoken by a mother or friend. Kama's arrows and the moon born with the poison of the churned sea are the torments; the cure is the god's garland.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:29"},"p:338":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":338,"t":["கமலரு சோகம்பர முடிநடு வேய்பூங்கணை","கலகமர் வாய்தோய்ந்தம ...... ளியின்மீதே","களையற மீதூர்ந்தெழ மதனவி டாய்போம்படி","கனவிய வாரேந்தின ...... இளநீர்தோய்ந்","தெமதுயிர் நீலாஞ்சன மதர்விழி யால்வாங்கிய","இவளுடன் மால்கூர்ந்திடு ...... மநுபோகம்","இனிவிட வேதாந்தப ரமசுக வீடாம்பொருள்","இதவிய பாதாம்புய ...... மருள்வாயே","அமகர ஆசாம்பர அதுகர ஏகாம்பர","அதுலன நீலாம்பர ...... மறியாத","அநகர நாளாங்கிதர் தமையுமை யாள்சேர்ந்தருள்","அறமுறு சீகாஞ்சியி ...... லுறைவோனே","விமலகி ராதாங்கனை தனகிரி தோய்காங்கெய","வெடிபடு தேவேந்திர ...... னகர்வாழ","விரிகடல் தநமுண்டிட நிசிசரர் வேர்மாண்டிட","வினையற வேல்வாங்கிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"Struck by Kama's flower arrows and taken by a woman whose kohl-dark, intoxicated eyes drew out his life, the poet asks to give up the experience of infatuation with her and be granted the lotus feet that are the substance of the house of supreme bliss taught in Vedanta. The praise names Siva Ekambara clothed in the directions, and Uma who joined him at Kanchi of the dharmas, and Murugan who embraced the huntress, let Indra's city live, and drew the vel that dried the sea and uprooted the night-walkers.","n":"வேதாந்த பரமசுக வீடாம் பொருள் இதவிய பாதாம்புயம்: the feet are themselves the Vedantic goal, the bliss of release, set against the அநுபோகம் of the woman. ஏகாம்பர names Siva of the Kanchi temple. The epithets of Siva in lines 12 to 14 (அமகர, அதுலன நீலாம்பரம் அறியாத, அநகர நாளாங்கிதர்) are hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mayal","kaamam","vedaantham","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:30"},"p:339":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":339,"t":["கரும மானபி றப்பற வொருகதி","காணா தெய்த்துத் ...... தடுமாறுங்","கலக காரண துற்குண சமயிகள்","நானா வர்க்கக் ...... கலைநூலின்","வரும நேகவி கற்பவி பரிதம","னோபா வத்துக் ...... கரிதாய","மவுன பூரித சத்திய வடிவினை","மாயா மற்குப் ...... புகல்வாயே","தரும வீம அருச்சுன நகுலச","காதே வர்க்குப் ...... புகலாகிச்","சமர பூமியில் விக்ரம வளைகொடு","நாளோர் பத்தெட் ...... டினிலாளுங்","குரும கீதல முட்பட வுளமது","கோடா மற்க்ஷத் ...... ரியர்மாளக்","குலவு தோர்கட வச்சுதன் மருககு","மாரா கச்சிப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet sets aside the quarrelsome, ill-natured followers of the sects, who find no single way to end karma-born birth and stagger among the doubts and contradictions of their many texts, and asks Murugan to tell him, so that he does not perish, the true form filled with silence that the mind cannot reach. The praise is of Krishna, refuge of the five Pandavas, who drove the chariot through the eighteen days on the field of the Kurus until the kshatriyas died, and calls Murugan his nephew at Kachi.","n":"கலக காரண துற்குண சமயிகள்: the sects are not merely wrong but causes of quarrel, and their many-class texts breed விகற்பம் and விபரீதம். Against them the goal is மவுன பூரித சத்திய வடிவு, a truth filled with silence. நாளோர் பத்தெட்டு, eighteen days, and தேர்கடவு அச்சுதன, Achyuta driving the chariot, name the Kurukshetra war, told at unusual length for a praise section.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["samayam","mounam","gnaanam","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:30"},"p:340":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":340,"t":["கலகலெ னப்பொற் சேந்த நூபுர","பரிபுர மொத்தித் தாந்த னாமென","கரமல ரச்சிற் றாந்தொ மாடிய ...... பொறியார்பைங்","கடிதட முற்றுக் காந்த ளாமென","இடைபிடி பட்டுச் சேர்ந்த ஆலிலை","கனதன பொற்பிட் டோ ங்கு மார்பொடு ...... வடமாடச்","சலசல சச்சச் சேங்கை பூண்வளை","பரிமள பச்சைச் சேர்ந்து லாவிய","சலசமு கத்துச் சார்ந்த வாள்விழி ...... சுழலாடத்","தரளந கைப்பித் தாம்ப லாரிதழ்","குலமுகி லொத்திட் டாய்ந்த வோதியர்","சரசமு ரைத்துச் சேர்ந்த தூவைய ...... ருறவாமோ","திலதமு கப்பொற் காந்தி மாதுமை","யெனையருள் வைத்திட் டாண்ட நாயகி","சிவனுரு வத்திற் சேர்ந்த பார்வதி ...... சிவகாமி","திரிபுவ னத்தைக் காண்ட நாடகி","குமரிசு கத்தைப் பூண்ட காரணி","சிவைசுடர் சத்திச் சாம்ப வீஅமை ...... யருள்பாலா","அலகையி ரத்தத் தோங்கி மூழ்கிட","நரிகழு குப்பிச் சீர்ந்து வாயிட","அசுரர்கு லத்தைக் காய்ந்த வேல்கர ...... முடையோனே","அமரர்ம கட்குப் போந்த மால்கொளும்","விபுதகு றத்திக் காண்ட வாதின","மழகுசி றக்கக் காஞ்சி மேவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"After a long picture of women with ringing anklets, gem-hung breasts, rolling sword-eyes, pearl smiles and cloud-dark hair who speak flirtatious words, the poet asks whether kinship with them is fitting. The praise is mostly of Uma, the golden lady who kept her grace on him and ruled him, Parvati joined in Siva's form, Sivakami, the dancer who saw the three worlds, Sambhavi, and of Murugan her son, whose vel burned the asura clans and who loves Devayanai and the wise huntress, at Kanchi.","n":"எனையருள் வைத்திட்டு ஆண்ட நாயகி: the poet claims the goddess's grace on himself, not only on Murugan. The many names of Uma (சிவை, சுடர், சத்தி, சாம்பவி, அமை) make the praise a hymn to the Shakti of Kanchi, as in 32. The plea itself is only a rhetorical question; தூவையர் is unclear.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:30"},"p:341":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":341,"t":["கொத்தார் பற்கா லற்றே கப்பாழ்","குப்பா யத்திற் ...... செயல்மாறிக்","கொக்கா கிக்கூ னிக்கோல் தொட்டே","கொட்டா விக்குப் ...... புறவாசித்","தித்தா நிற்றார் செத்தார் கெட்டேன்","அஆ உஉ...... எனவேகேள்","செற்றே சுட்டே விட்டே றிப்போ","மப்பே துத்துக் ...... கமறாதோ","நித்தா வித்தா ரத்தோ கைக்கே","நிற்பாய் கச்சிக் ...... குமரேசா","நிட்டூ ரச்சூர் கெட்டோ டப்போர்","நெட்டோ தத்திற் ...... பொருதோனே","முத்தா ரத்தோ ளிற்கோ டற்பூ","முட்டா திட்டத் ...... தணிவோனே","முற்றா நித்தா அத்தா சுத்தா","முத்தா முத்திப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet pictures old age and death: teeth fall like clusters, the ruined coat of the body stops working, the man turns white as a crane, bent over a stick, yawning, his breath going out, and then the kin cry 'he is dead, I am ruined, ah, oo', burn him, leave and go; he asks whether that bewilderment will not end. The praise calls Murugan the eternal Kumaresa of Kachi who stands for the peacock, who fought Sura in the long sea, and the unending, pure, released lord of release.","n":"The song is set in hard, clipped syllables, and the kin's wailing is given as sound (அஆ உஉ). குப்பாயம், a coat, is the body worn and thrown off. The last line runs epithets of release (முற்றா நித்தா அத்தா சுத்தா முத்தா முத்தி). The close of the plea (அப்பேதுத்துக்கு அமறாதோ) is unclear in division.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","ilamai","saakkaadu","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:30"},"p:342":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":342,"t":["கோவைச் சுத்தத் துப்பத ரத்துக் ...... கொடியார்தங்","கோலக் கச்சுக் கட்டிய முத்தத் ...... தனமேவிப்","பாவத் துக்குத் தக்கவை பற்றித் ...... திரியாதே","பாடப் பத்திச் சித்த மெனக்குத் ...... தரவேணும்","மாவைக் குத்திக் கைத்தற எற்றிப் ...... பொரும்வேலா","மாணிக் கச்சொர்க் கத்தொரு தத்தைக் ...... கினியோனே","சேவற் பொற்கைக் கொற்றவ கச்சிப் ...... பதியோனே","தேவச் சொர்க்கச் சக்கிர வர்த்திப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A short song: the poet asks not to wander clinging to what suits sin, the women with coral lips and bodice-bound breasts, but to be given a heart of devotion so that he may sing. The praise names the vel that pierced the mango tree, Murugan sweet to the parrot of the ruby heaven, with the cock in his golden hand, lord of Kachi and emperor of the gods' heaven.","n":"பாடப் பத்திச் சித்தம்: devotion is asked for as the condition of song. The mango (மா) is Sura in his tree form.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","paththi","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:30"},"p:343":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":343,"t":["சீசி முப்புரக் காடு நீறெழச்","சாடி நித்திரைக் கோசம் வேரறச்","சீவன் முத்தியிற் கூட வேகளித் ...... தநுபூதி","சேர அற்புதக் கோல மாமெனச்","சூரி யப்புவிக் கேறி யாடுகச்","சீலம் வைத்தருட் டேறி யேயிருக் ...... கறியாமற்","பாசம் விட்டுவிட் டோ டி போனதுப்","போது மிப்படிக் காகி லேனினிப்","பாழ்வ ழிக்கடைக் காம லேபிடித் ...... தடியேனைப்","பார டைக்கலக் கோல மாமெனத்","தாப ரித்துநித் தார மீதெனப்","பாத பத்மநற் போதை யேதரித் ...... தருள்வாயே","தேசில் துட்டநிட் டூர கோதுடைச்","சூரை வெட்டியெட் டாசை யேழ்புவித்","தேவர் முத்தர்கட் கேத மேதவிர்த் ...... தருள்வோனே","சீர்ப டைத்தழற் சூல மான்மழுப்","பாணி வித்துருப் பாத னோர்புறச்","சீர்தி கழ்ப்புகழ்ப் பாவை யீனபொற் ...... குருநாதா","காசி முத்தமிழ்க் கூட லேழ்மலைக்","கோவ லத்தியிற் கான நான்மறைக்","காடு பொற்கிரிக் காழி யாருர்பொற் ...... புலி஧வ்ளுர்","காள அத்தியப் பால்சி ராமலைத்","தேச முற்றுமுப் பூசை மேவிநற்","காம கச்சியிற் சால மேவுபொற் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet seems to rebuke himself ('fie!') for having tasted jivanmukti and experience, burning the three cities to ash and uprooting the sheath of sleep, and yet, not knowing how to stay settled in grace, having run off and let go; he asks Murugan to hold him so he does not end on the ruined road and to grant the lotus feet. The praise names Siva with trident, deer and axe and Uma at his side, whose golden guru Murugan is, and lists the shrines where Murugan receives worship three times a day: Kasi, Madurai, the seven hills, Kovalur, Vedaranyam, Kazhi, Arur, Puliyur, Kalatti, Chiramalai and Kanchi.","n":"சீவன் முத்தியிற் கூடவே களித்து அநுபூதி சேர: the plea speaks of liberation in life and அநுபூதி, the direct experience of the Lord, as something reached and then lost. The three cities (முப்புரம்) and the sheath of sleep (நித்திரைக் கோசம்) seem to be inner, not mythic. The list of shrines in the praise is a small map of Tamil and northern holy places.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["veedu","thiruvarul","thiruvadi","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:30"},"p:344":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":344,"t":["நச்சரவ மென்று நச்சரவ மென்று","நச்சுமிழ்க ளங்க ...... மதியாலும்","நத்தொடுமு ழங்க னத்தொடுமு ழங்கு","நத்திரைவ ழங்கு ...... கடலாலும்","இச்சையுணர் வின்றி யிச்சையென வந்த","இச்சிறுமி நொந்து ...... மெலியாதே","எத்தனையி நெஞ்சில் எத்தனமு யங்கி","இத்தனையி லஞ்ச ...... லெனவேணும்","பச்சைமயில் கொண்டு பச்சைமற மங்கை","பச்சைமலை யெங்கு ...... முறைவோனே","பத்தியுட னின்று பத்திசெயு மன்பர்","பத்திரம ணிந்த ...... கழலோனே","கச்சிவர் குரும்பை கச்சவர்வி ரும்பு","கச்சியில மர்ந்த ...... கதிர்வேலா","கற்பக வனங்கொள் கற்பகவி சும்பர்","கைத்தளைக ளைந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"In the voice of one who watches over a lovesick girl, the singer asks that this child, who came to desire without yet knowing desire, not waste away under the stained moon the serpent seeks and the sea roaring with its conches and waves, and that Murugan come and say 'do not fear'. The praise names him with the green peacock, the green hunter maid and the green hills, the one whose anklets his devotees serve, the Kachi lord of the shining vel who freed the bound hands of the gods of the kalpaka grove.","n":"The whole song is built on doubled words with different senses (நச்சரவம், பச்சை, பத்தி, கச்சி, கற்பக): a யமகம். இச்சையுணர்வின்றி இச்சையென வந்த இச்சிறுமி: the girl is too young to know what she feels. This is akam, not the poet's confession.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","melivu","maalai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:30"},"p:345":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":345,"t":["படிறொ ழுக்கமு மடம னத்துள","படிப ரித்துட ...... னொடிபேசும்","பகடி கட்குள மகழ மெய்ப்பொருள்","பலகொ டுத்தற ...... உயிர்வாடா","மிடியெ னப்பெரு வடவை சுட்டிய","விதன முற்றிட ...... மிகவாழும்","விரகு கெட்டரு நரகு விட்டிரு","வினைய றப்பத ...... மருள்வாயே","கொடியி டைக்குற வடிவி யைப்புணர்","குமர கச்சியி ...... லமர்வோனே","குரவு செச்சைவெண் முளரி புத்தலர்","குவளை முற்றணி ...... திருமார்பா","பொடிப டப்பட நெடிய விற்கொடு","புரமெ ரித்தவர் ...... குருநாதா","பொருதி ரைக்கடல் நிருத ரைப்படை","பொருது ழக்கிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses that with deceitful conduct and a foolish heart he gave much real wealth to the quick-talking jesting women to please them until his life withered, and now lives in poverty that burns like the undersea fire; he asks that, his cunning gone, hell left behind and the two deeds cut, he be given the feet. The praise names Murugan who embraced the huntress at Kachi, his chest hung with kuravu, vetchi, lotus and kuvalai flowers, guru to Siva who burned the cities with his long bow, and the one who crushed the nirudas' army by the sea.","n":"மிடியெனப் பெரு வடவை சுட்டிய விதனம்: poverty is the வடவை fire under the sea, the result of what he gave the women, so the courtesan's charge is told from its end. மெய்ப்பொருள் may be wealth or 'true substance', a pun on what he threw away.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","inmai","vinai","naragam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:30"},"p:346":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":346,"t":["மகுடக்கொப் பாடக் காதினில்","நுதலிற்பொட் டூரக் கோதிய","மயிரிற்சுற் றோலைப் பூவோடு ...... வண்டுபாட","வகைமுத்துச் சோரச் சேர்நகை","யிதழிற்சொற் சாதிப் பாரியல்","மதனச்சொற் பாடுக் கோகில ...... ரம்பைமாதர்","பகடிச்சொற் கூறிப் போர்மயல்","முகவிச்சைப் பேசிச் சீரிடை","பவளப்பட் டாடைத் தோளிரு ...... கொங்கைமேலாப்","பணமெத்தப் பேசித் தூதிடு","மிதயச்சுத் தீனச் சோலிகள்","பலரெச்சிற் காசைக் காரிகள் ...... சந்தமாமோ","தகுடத்தத் தானத் தானன","திகுடத்தித் தீதித் தோதிமி","தடுடுட்டுட் டாடப் பேரிகை ...... சங்குவீணை","தடமிட்டுப் பாவக் கார்கிரி","பொடிபட்டுப் போகச் சூரர்கள்","தலையிற்றிட் டாடப் போர்புரி ...... கின்றவேலா","திகிரிப்பொற் பாணிப் பாலனை","மறைகற்புத் தேளப் பூமனை","சினமுற்றுச் சேடிற் சாடிய ...... கந்தவேளே","தினையுற்றுக் காவற் காரியை","மணமுற்றுத் தேவப் பூவொடு","திகழ்கச்சித் தேவக் கோன்மகிழ் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes ornamented women with cuckoo voices who speak Kama's words and jesting talk, then talk money and send go-betweens, impure of heart, greedy for many men's leavings, and asks whether such company befits him. The praise, after a drum passage of battle, names Kandavel who angrily struck on the head the Veda-learned god of the lotus, child of the one with the discus, and who married the guardian of the millet field, at Kanchi.","n":"பணமெத்தப் பேசித் தூதிடும் ... பலர் எச்சிற்கு ஆசைக் காரிகள்: money and many men, the two stock charges. The praise alludes to the punishment of Brahma (மறைகற்ற புத்தேள், the lotus-dweller born of Vishnu) but does not say here that it was for not knowing Om.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","mayal","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:30"},"p:347":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":347,"t":["மக்கட்குக் கூறரி தானது","கற்றெட்டத் தான்முடி யாதது","மற்றொப்புக் கியாதுமொ வாதது ...... மனதாலே","மட்டிட்டுத் தேடவோ ணாதது","தத்வத்திற் கோவைப டாதது","மத்தப்பொற் போதுப கீரதி ...... மதிசூடும்","முக்கட்பொற் பாளரு சாவிய","அர்த்தக்குப் போதக மானது","முத்திக்குக் காரண மானது ...... பெறலாகா","முட்டர்க்கெட் டாதது நான்மறை","யெட்டிற்றெட் டாதென வேவரு","முற்பட்டப் பாலையி லாவது ...... புரிவாயே","செக்கட்சக் ராயுத மாதுலன்","மெச்சப்புற் போதுப டாவிய","திக்குப்பொற் பூதர மேமுதல் ...... வெருபம்","சிட்டித்துப் பூதப சாசுகள்","கைக்கொட்டிட் டாடம கோததி","செற்றுக்ரச் சூரனை மார்பக ...... முதுசோரி","கக்கக்கைத் தாமரை வேல்விடு","செச்சைக்கர்ப் பூரபு யாசல","கச்சுற்றப் பாரப யோதர ...... முலையாள்முன்","கற்புத்தப் பாதுல கேழையு","மொக்கப்பெற் றாள்விளை யாடிய","கச்சிக்கச் சாலையில் மேவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks for That which is hard to tell people, cannot be reached by learning, has no likeness, cannot be measured by the mind or caught in the chain of tattvas, which was the meaning taught to the three-eyed Siva, the cause of release, out of fools' reach, and which the four Vedas say they cannot reach even at their height. The praise names the vel that pierced the great Sura's chest, Vishnu's admiration, and Murugan who played before the mother whose chastity holds although she bore all seven worlds, at the Kachchalai of Kachi.","n":"The plea is built of negations (முடியாதது, ஒவாதது, படாதது, எட்டாதது): a way of speaking of the Absolute rather than the poet's own state. முக்கண் ... அருசாவிய அர்த்தக்குப் போதகம்: the meaning Siva asked to hear is the teaching of Siva, without Om named. கற்பு தப்பாது உலகேழையும் ஒக்கப் பெற்றாள்: the virgin mother of all worlds. One word in the praise (வெருபம்) may be garbled.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","guru","thaththuvam","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:30"},"p:348":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":348,"t":["மயலோது மந்த நிலையாலும் வஞ்ச","வசைபேசு கின்ற ...... மொழியாலும்","மறிபோலு கின்ற விழிசேரு மந்தி","மதிநேரு கின்ற ...... நுதலாலும்","அயிலேநி கர்ந்த விழியாலும் அஞ்ச","நடையாலும் அங்கை ...... வளையாலும்","அறிவே யழிந்து அயர்வாகி நைந்து","அடியேன் மயங்கி ...... விடலாமோ","மயிலேறி யன்று நொடி போதி லண்டம்","வலமாக வந்த ...... குமரேசா","மறிதாவு செங்கை அரனா ரிடங்கொள்","மலைமாது தந்த ...... முருகேசா","நயவா னுயர்ந்த மணிமாட மும்பர்","நடுவே நிறைந்த ...... மதிசூழ","நறைவீசு கும்ப குடமேவு கம்பை","நகர்மீத மர்ந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether he, Murugan's servant, should swoon, his mind destroyed and wasted, by a woman's dull infatuation, her deceitful words, deer and spear eyes, moon forehead, swan gait and bangled hands. The praise names Kumaresa who once circled the world on the peacock in an instant, the Murugesa given by the mountain daughter who shares Siva's side, at Kampai city of tall jewelled mansions.","n":"அறிவே அழிந்து அயர்வாகி நைந்து: the harm named is to understanding, not to wealth. The circling of the world is the contest for the fruit; கம்பை is Kanchi by the Kampa river.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mayal","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:30"},"p:349":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":349,"t":["முத்து ரத்ந சூத்ர மொத்த சித்ர மார்க்கர்","முற்செ மத்து மூர்க்கர் ...... வெகுபாவர்","முத்து திர்த்த வார்த்தை யொத்த பத்ர வாட்கண்","முச்சர் மெத்த சூட்சர் ...... நகையாலே","எத்தர் குத்தி ரார்த்தர் துட்ட முட்ட காக்கர்","இட்ட முற்ற கூட்டர் ...... விலைமாதர்","எக்கர் துக்கர் வாழ்க்கை யுற்ற சித்த நோய்ப்புண்","இப்ப டிக்கு மார்க்கம் ...... உழல்வேனோ","தித்தி மித்தி மீத்த னத்த னத்த மூட்டு","சிற்று டுக்கை சேட்டை ...... தவில்பேரி","திக்கு மக்க ளாக்கை துக்க வெற்பு மீக்கொள்","செக்க டற்கு ளாழ்த்து ...... விடும்வேலா","கற்பு ரத்தை வீட்டி நட்ட மிட்ட நீற்றர்","கத்தர் பித்தர் கூத்தர் ...... குருநாதா","கற்கு றிச்சி வாழ்ப்பெ ணொக்க வெற்றி வேற்கொள்","கச்சி நத்தி நாட்கொள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet lists the courtesans, priced women decked in pearl and gem strings, sinful, tricky, cheating, with leaf-like sword eyes and smiles, whose way of life brings sickness of mind and sores, and asks whether he must wander on that path. The praise, after drum syllables, names the vel that sank the enemies and their mountain of sorrow in the red sea, Murugan as guru to Siva the dancer smeared with ash, and the husband of the girl of the hill village, at Kachi.","n":"விலைமாதர், women with a price, is named outright, and சித்த நோய்ப் புண் joins the mind's sickness to the body's sores. கற்புரத்தை வீட்டி is unclear: perhaps Siva destroying the stone (or triple) cities.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","noy","kutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:30"},"p:350":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":350,"t":["வம்ப றாச்சில கன்ன மிடுஞ்சம","யத்துக் கத்துத் ...... திரையாளர்","வன்க லாத்திரள் தன்னை யகன்றும","னத்திற் பற்றற் ...... றருளாலே","தம்ப ராக்கற நின்னை யுணர்ந்துரு","கிப்பொற் பத்மக் ...... கழல்சேர்வார்","தங்கு ழாத்தினி லென்னையு மன்பொடு","வைக்கச் சற்றுக் ...... கருதாதோ","வெம்ப ராக்ரம மின்னயில் கொண்டொரு","வெற்புப் பொட்டுப் ...... படமாசூர்","வென்ற பார்த்திப பன்னிரு திண்புய","வெட்சிச் சித்ரத் ...... திருமார்பா","கம்ப ராய்ப்பணி மன்னு புயம்பெறு","கைக்குக் கற்புத் ...... தவறாதே","கம்பை யாற்றினி லன்னை தவம்புரி","கச்சிச் சொக்கப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet praises those who leave behind the mischievous, wave-loud sects and their hard mass of learning, who with minds free of attachment, by grace, without distraction, know Murugan, melt and reach his golden lotus feet, and asks whether he will not think of placing him too with love in their company. The praise names the twelve-armed hero whose vel broke a mountain and Sura, and Murugan of Kachi by the Kampa river, where the Mother did penance so that her chastity toward Siva's arms would not fail.","n":"தங்குழாத்தினில் என்னையும் வைக்க: the request is not for release directly but for a place in the gathering of devotees. கம்பையாற்றினில் அன்னை தவம்புரி: the Kanchi legend of Uma's penance by the Kampa river is named. கன்னமிடும் (housebreaking) in the first line may charge the sects with robbing minds; the division is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["adiyaar","samayam","pattru","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:30"},"p:351":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":351,"t":["வாய்ந்தப்பிடை நீடு குலாவிய","நீந்திப்பது மாதியை மீதினி","லு஡ர்ந்துற்பல வோடையில் நீடிய ...... உகள்சேலை","வார்ந்துப்பக ழீயெதி ராகிமை","கூர்ந்துப்பரி யாவரி சேரவை","சேர்ந்துக்குழை யோடுச லாடிய ...... விழியாலே","சாய்ந்துப்பனை யூணவ ரானபொ","லாய்ந்துப்பாணி னாரிரு தாளினில்","வீழ்ந்திப்படி போகினு மோர்மொழி ...... மறவேனே","சார்ந்தப்பெரு நீர்வெள மாகவெ","பாய்ந்தப்பொழு தாருமி லாமலெ","காந்தப்பெரு நாதனு மாகிய ...... மதராலே","தாந்தக்கிட தாகிட தாகிட","தோந்திக்கிட தோதிமி தோதிமி","சேஞ்செக்கண சேகெண சேகெண ...... வெனதாளம்","காந்தப்பத மாறியு லாவுய","ராந்தற்குரு நாதனு மாகியெ","போந்தப்பெரு மான்முரு காவொரு ...... பெரியோனே","காந்தக்கலு மூசியு மேயென","ஆய்ந்துத்தமி ழோதிய சீர்பெறு","காஞ்சிப்பதி மாநகர் மேவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"After describing women's carp-like eyes that dart, rival arrows and reach the ears, the poet says that even if he falls at their feet and goes on in this way, he will not forget the one word. The praise, through drum syllables, names Murugan as guru to the dancing Siva at the time of the great flood, the great one, and the lord of famed Kanchi who sang in Tamil that the bond is like magnet and needle.","n":"ஓர் மொழி மறவேனே: the plea is turned into a vow; the one word may be the Lord's name or a mantra. காந்தக் கல்லும் ஊசியுமே என ஆய்ந்து தமிழ் ஓதிய: the magnet and the needle is the Saiva image of the Lord drawing the soul. The flood (பெருநீர் வெள்ளம்) may be the Kampa flood of the Kanchi legend. Several lines of the praise are hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["mayal","kan","mandhiram"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:30"},"p:352":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":352,"t":["அறிவிலாப் பித்த ருன்ற னடிதொழாக் கெட்ட வஞ்சர்","அசடர்பேய்க் கத்தர் நன்றி ...... யறியாத","அவலர்மேற் சொற்கள் கொண்டு கவிகளாக் கிப்பு கழ்ந்து","அவரைவாழ்த் தித்தி ரிந்து ...... பொருள்தேடிச்","சிறிதுகூட் டிக்கொ ணர்ந்து தெருவுலாத் தித்தி ரிந்து","தெரிவைமார்க் குச்சொ ரிந்து ...... அவமேயான்","திரியுமார்க் கத்து நிந்தை யதனைமாற் றிப்ப ரிந்து","தெளியமோ க்ஷத்தை யென்று ...... அருள்வாயே","இறைவர்மாற் றற்ற செம்பொன் வடிவம்வேற் றுப்பி ரிந்து","இடபமேற் கச்சி வந்த ...... உமையாள்தன்","இருளைநீக் கத்த வஞ்செய் தருளநோக் கிக்கு ழைந்த","இறைவர்கேட் கத்த குஞ்சொ ...... லுடையோனே","குறவர்கூட் டத்தில் வந்து கிழவனாய்ப் புக்கு நின்று","குருவியோட் டித்தி ரிந்த ...... தவமானைக்","குணமதாக் கிச்சி றந்த வடிவுகாட் டிப்பு ணர்ந்த","குமரகோட் டத்த மர்ந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses that he made poems praising senseless, ungrateful, deceitful men who never bow to Murugan's feet, wandered blessing them in search of wealth, gathered a little, and poured it out on women while roaming the streets; he asks that this disgrace be removed and that release be given clearly. The praise tells how Uma came on the bull to Kachi to do penance and remove her darkness and the Lord melted toward her, and how Murugan, whose words Siva hears, entered among the hunters as an old man and embraced the girl who chased the birds, at Kumarakottam.","n":"The confession is of the poet's own trade: praising unworthy patrons (அவலர் மேல் சொற்கள் கொண்டு கவிகள் ஆக்கி) and spending the gain on women. இருளை நீக்கத் தவஞ்செய்து அருள நோக்கிக் குழைந்த: the Kanchi legend of Uma's penance and Siva's melting toward her. குமரகோட்டம் is the Murugan shrine at Kanchi.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","selvam","kutram","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:30"},"p:353":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":353,"t":["அஞ்சன வேல்விழி யிட்டழைக்கவு","மிங்கித மாகந கைத்துருக்கவு","மம்புயல் நேர்குழ லைக்குலைக்கவும் ...... நகரேகை","அங்கையின் மூலம்வெ ளிப்படுத்தவு","மந்தர மாமுலை சற்றசைக்கவு","மம்பரம் வீணில விழ்த்துடுக்கவு ...... மிளைஞோர்கள்","நெஞ்சினி லாசைநெ ருப்பெழுப்பவும்","வம்புரை கூறிவ ளைத்திணக்கவு","மன்றிடை யாடிம ருட்கொடுக்கவு ...... மெவரேனும்","நிந்தைசெ யாதுபொ ருட்பறிக்கவு","மிங்குவ லார்கள்கை யிற்பிணிப்பற","நின்பத சேவைய நுக்ரகிப்பது ...... மொருநாளே","குஞ்சர மாமுக விக்கிநப்ரபு","அங்குச பாசக ரப்ரசித்தனொர்","கொம்பன்ம கோதரன் முக்கண்விக்ரம ...... கணராஜன்","கும்பிடு வார்வினை பற்றறுப்பவன்","எங்கள் விநாயக னக்கர்பெற்றருள்","குன்றைய ரூபக கற்பகப்பிளை ...... யிளையோனே","துஞ்சலி லாதச டக்ஷரப்பிர","பந்த சடானன துஷ்டநிக்ரக","தும்பிகள் சூழவை யிற்றமிழ்த்ரய ...... பரிபாலா","துங்க கஜாரணி யத்திலுத்தம","சம்பு தடாகம டுத்ததக்ஷிண","சுந்தர மாறன்ம திட்புறத்துறை ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet lists the arts of women who call with kohl-dark eyes, smile to melt, loosen their hair, show nail-marks on their palms, kindle the fire of desire in young men, bind them with idle talk, bewilder them by dancing in the hall, and take their wealth without blame, and asks whether a day will come when, the bond at these clever hands cut, Murugan grants service at his feet. The praise is first a hymn to Ganesa, elephant-faced, with goad and noose, one tusk and great belly, who cuts the deeds of those who bow, and then names Murugan his younger brother, the six-faced lord of the six syllables who cherished the three Tamils, at the elephant forest by the Jambu tank.","n":"எவரேனும் நிந்தை செய்யாது பொருட்பறிக்கவும்: the courtesans rob without anyone blaming them. The remedy is சேவை, service at the feet. The long praise of Ganesa (விக்கிநப்ரபு, கணராஜன், எங்கள் விநாயகன்) is unusual. கஜாரணியம் and சம்பு தடாகம் name Thiruvanaikka; சுந்தர மாறன் மதில் appears to be a wall built by a Pandya king.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","thondu","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:31"},"p:354":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":354,"t":["அம்புலி நீரைச் சூடிய செஞ்சடை மீதிற் றாவிய","ஐந்தலை நாகப் பூஷண ...... ரருள்பாலா","அன்புட னாவிற் பாவது சந்தத மோதிப் பாதமு","மங்கையி னானிற் பூசையு ...... மணியாமல்","வம்பணி பாரப் பூண்முலை வஞ்சியர் மாயச் சாயலில்","வண்டுழ லோதித் தாழலி ...... லிருகாதில்","மண்டிய நீலப் பார்வையில் வெண்துகி லாடைச்சேர்வையில்","மங்கியெ யேழைப் பாவியெ ...... னழிவேனோ","கொம்பனை நீலக் கோமளை அம்புய மாலைப் பூஷணி","குண்டலி யாலப் போசனி ...... யபிராமி","கொஞ்சிய வானச் சானவி சங்கரி வேதப் பார்வதி","குன்றது வார்பொற் காரிகை ...... யருள்பாலா","செம்பவ ளாயக் கூரிதழ் மின்குற மானைப் பூண்முலை","திண்புய மாரப் பூரண ...... மருள்வோனே","செந்தமிழ் பாணப் பாவலர் சங்கித யாழைப் பாடிய","தென்திரு வானைக் காவுறை ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether, without singing on his tongue with love and without worshipping the feet with flowers in his hand, he, a wretched sinner, must perish, dimmed in the deceptive charm of women with heavy breasts, bee-hovering hair and blue looks that reach both ears. The praise is a string of names of Uma, the blue tender one, Kundali, Abhirami, Sankari, Parvati of the Veda, and names Murugan her son who embraced the hunter girl, at Thiruvanaikka where Tamil poets and panars sing to the yazh.","n":"The plea defines the loss by what is left undone: பாதமும் அங்கையின் நானில் பூசையும் அணியாமல், the worship of the feet with flowers. ஆலப்போசனி, she who ate the poison, gives Uma Siva's deed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","vazhipaadu","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:31"},"p:355":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":355,"t":["அனித்த மான வூனாளு மிருப்ப தாக வேநாசி","யடைதலந வாயு வோடாத ...... வகைசாதித்","தவத்தி லேகு வால்மூலி புசித்து வாடு மாயாச","அசட்டு யோகி யாகாமல் ...... மலமாயை","செனித்த காரி யோபாதி யொழித்து ஞான ஆசார","சிரத்தை யாகி யான்வேறெ ...... னுடல்வேறு","செகத்தி யாவும் வேறாக நிகழ்ச்சி யாம நோதீத","சிவச்சொ ரூபமாயோகி ...... யெனஆள்வாய்","தொனித்த நாத வேயூது சகஸ்ர நாம கோபால","சுதற்கு நேச மாறாத ...... மருகோனே","சுவர்க்க லோக மீகாம சமஸ்த லோக பூபால","தொடுத்த நீப வேல்வீர ...... வயலுரா","மனித்த ராதி சோணாடு தழைக்க மேவு காவேரி","மகப்ர வாக பானீய ...... மலைமோதும்","மணத்த சோலை சூழ்காவை அனைத்து லோக மாள்வாரு","மதித்த சாமி யேதேவர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks not to become a foolish yogi of desire and illusion who, thinking this impermanent flesh will last, holds the breath so it does not leave the nostrils and withers in austerity eating herbs; he asks instead that, with malam, maya and their adjuncts removed, devoted to the conduct of knowledge, knowing 'I am other, my body is other, the whole world is other', he be ruled as a yogi of Siva's own form beyond the mind. The praise names Gopala of the thousand names who plays the flute as uncle, Murugan of Vayalur, and the grove-circled Kavai watered by the Kaveri's flood, which all world-rulers revere.","n":"அனித்தமான ஊன் நாளும் இருப்பதாகவே ... அசட்டு யோகி: the target is breath-holding and herbs used to keep the body, not yoga itself, since the plea ends asking to be a சிவச்சொரூப மாயோகி. யான் வேறு என் உடல் வேறு செகத்து யாவும் வேறாக: release is described as disidentification from body and world.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yogam","praanan","malam","upaadhi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:31"},"p:356":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":356,"t":["ஆரமணி வாரைப் பீறியற மேலிட்","டாடவர்கள் வாடத் ...... துறவோரை","ஆசைமட லு஡ர்வித் தாளுமதி பாரப்","பாளித படீரத் ...... தனமானார்","காரளக நீழற் காதளவு மோடிக்","காதுமபி ராமக் ...... கயல்போலக்","காலனுடல் போடத் தேடிவரு நாளிற்","காலைமற வாமற் ...... புகல்வேனோ","பாரடைய வாழ்வித் தாரபதி பாசச்","சாமளக லாபப் ...... பரியேறிப்","பாய்மதக போலத் தானொடிக லாமுற்","பாடிவரு மேழைச் ...... சிறியோனே","சூரர்புர சூறைக் காரசுரர் காவற்","காரஇள வேனற் ...... புனமேவுந்","தோகைதிரு வேளைக் காரதமிழ் வேதச்","சோதிவளர் காவைப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of women whose breasts, smeared with camphor and sandal, burst their bands and make men wither and even ascetics ride the palmyra horse of desire, with carp-like eyes reaching to the ears, and asks whether, on the day Death comes seeking to throw down his body, he will call on Murugan without forgetting his feet. The praise names Murugan on the dark-plumed peacock, the little one younger than the elephant with flowing musth, plunderer of Sura's city, guardian of the gods, servant of the peacock-girl of the millet field, the light of the Tamil Veda, at Kavai.","n":"துறவோரை ஆசை மடல் ஊர்வித்து: the classical madal, the spurned lover's ride on the palmyra horse, is forced even on renouncers. காலைமறவாமல்: the feet are what must be remembered at Death's hour. Some lines of the praise (பாய்மத கபோலத்தானொடு இகலாம் முற்பாடி) are hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["kootru","madal","thiruvadi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:31"},"p:357":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":357,"t":["ஆலம் வைத்தவி ழிச்சிகள் சித்தச","னாக மக்கலை கற்றச மர்த்திக","ளார்ம னத்தையு மெத்திவ ளைப்பவர் ...... தெருவூடே","ஆர வட்டமு லைக்குவி லைப்பண","மாயி ரக்கல மொட்டிய ளப்பினு","மாசை யப்பொரு ளொக்கந டிப்பவ ...... ருடன்மாலாய்","மேலி ளைப்புமு சிப்பும வத்தையு","மாயெ டுத்தகு லைப்பொடு பித்தமு","மேல்கொ ளத்தலை யிட்டவி திப்படி ...... யதனாலே","மேதி னிக்குள பத்தனெ னப்பல","பாடு பட்டுபு ழுக்கொள்ம லக்குகை","வீடு கட்டியி ருக்குமெ னக்குநி ...... னருள்தாராய்","பீலி மிக்கம யிற்றுர கத்தினி","லேறி முட்டவ ளைத்துவ குத்துடல்","பீற லுற்றவு யுத்தக ளத்திடை ...... மடியாத","பேர ரக்கரெ திர்த்தவ ரத்தனை","பேரை யுக்ரக ளப்பலி யிட்டுயர்","பேய்கை கொட்டிந டிப்பம ணிக்கழு ...... குடனாட","ஏலம் வைத்தபு யத்தி லணைத்தருள்","வேலெ டுத்தச மர்த்தையு ரைப்பவர்","ஏவ ருக்கும னத்தில்நி னைப்பவை ...... யருள்வோனே","ஏழி சைத்தமி ழிற்பய னுற்றவெ","ணாவ லுற்றடி யிற்பயி லுத்தம","ஈசன் முக்கணி ருத்தன ளித்தருள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says that, infatuated with women of poison eyes skilled in Kama's arts, who act out desire exactly as far as the money goes even if a thousand measures of gold are paid for their breasts, he suffered weakness, trembling and madness as fate wrote on his head, and asks grace for himself who has built a house that is a worm-filled cave of filth. The praise shows the battle with the giants, ghouls clapping and vultures dancing, says that Murugan gives whatever those who tell of his vel desire, and names him the son of the three-eyed Siva of the white jambu where seven-note Tamil found its fruit.","n":"ஆசை அப்பொருள் ஒக்க நடிப்பவர்: the desire is acted in proportion to the payment. தலையிட்ட விதிப்படி: the poet lays his ruin on the fate written on his head, a rare appeal to fate in these confessions. புழுக்கொள் மலக் குகை வீடு: the body as a house of filth. வெண்ணாவல், the white jambu, is the tree of Thiruvanaikka.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","yaakkai","oozh","noy"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:31"},"p:358":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":358,"t":["உரைக்கா ரிகைப்பா லெனக்கே முதற்பே","ருனக்கோ மடற்கோவை ...... யொன்றுபாட","உழப்பா திபக்கோ டெழுத்தா ணியைத்தே","டுனைப்பாரி லொப்பார்கள் ...... கண்டிலேன்யான்","குரைக்கான வித்யா கவிப்பூ பருக்கே","குடிக்காண் முடிப்போடு ...... கொண்டுவாபொன்","குலப்பூ ணிரத்நா திபொற்று஡ செடுப்பா","யெனக்கூ றிடர்ப்பாடின் ...... மங்குவேனோ","அரைக்காடை சுற்றார் தமிழ்க்கூட லிற்போய்","அனற்கே புனற்கேவ ...... ரைந்தஏடிட்","டறத்தா யெனப்பேர் படைத்தாய் புனற்சே","லறப்பாய் வயற்கீழ ...... மர்ந்தவேளே","திரைக்கா விரிக்கே கரைக்கா னகத்தே","சிவத்யான முற்றோர்சி ...... லந்திநூல்செய்","திருக்கா வணத்தே யிருப்பா ரருட்கூர்","திருச்சால கச்சோதி ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet voices the begging poet's speech to a patron, 'I am first in the rules of prosody; shall I sing you a madal or a kovai? Fetch palm leaf and stylus; I have seen no one your equal on earth; bring this king of poets gold in a bundle, jewels and gems', and asks whether he must wither in the trouble of such talk. The praise names Murugan who at Tamil Madurai, against those who wear no waist-cloth, put the palm leaf into fire and water and won the name of father of dharma, and the light of the shrine on the Kaveri bank where the spider meditating on Siva wove a canopy.","n":"The confession is of the court poet's begging flattery, quoted in his own voice, including the genres he offers (மடல், கோவை). அரைக்காடை சுற்றார் are the naked Jains, and the leaf put into fire and water (அனற்கே புனற்கே வரைந்த ஏடு) is Sambandar's contest at Madurai: Murugan is identified with Sambandar. சிலந்தி நூல்செய் திருக்காவணம் is the Thiruvanaikka story of the spider that sheltered the linga.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["iravu","pugazh","thamizh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:31"},"p:359":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":359,"t":["ஓல மறைக ளறைகின்ற வொன்றது","மேலை வெளியி லொளிரும் பரஞ்சுடர்","ஓது சரியை க்ரியையும் புணர்ந்தவ ...... ரெவராலும்","ஓத வரிய துரியங் கடந்தது","போத அருவ சுருபம் ப்ரபஞ்சமும்","ஊனு முயிரு முழுதுங் கலந்தது ...... சிவஞானம்","சால வுடைய தவர்கண்டு கொண்டது","மூல நிறைவு குறைவின்றி நின்றது","சாதி குலமு மிலதன்றி யன்பர்சொ ...... னவியோமஞ்","சாரு மநுப வரமைந்த மைந்தமெய்","வீடு பரம சுகசிந்து இந்த்ரிய","தாப சபல மறவந்து நின்கழல் ...... பெறுவேனோ","வால குமர குககந்த குன்றெறி","வேல மயில எனவந்து கும்பிடு","வான விபுதர் பதியிந்த்ரன் வெந்துயர் ...... களைவோனே","வாச களப வரதுங்க மங்கல","வீர கடக புயசிங்க சுந்தர","வாகை புனையும் ரணரங்க புங்கவ ...... வயலு஡ரா","ஞால முதல்வி யிமயம் பயந்தமின்","நீலி கவுரி பரைமங்கை குண்டலி","நாளு மினிய கனியெங்க ளம்பிகை ...... த்ரிபுராயி","நாத வடிவி யகிலம் பரந்தவ","ளாலி னுதர முளபைங் கரும்புவெ","ணாவ லரசு மனைவஞ்சி தந்தருள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes the One the Vedas proclaim, the supreme light in the upper space, beyond those who practise chariyai and kiriyai, beyond turiya, formless knowledge mingled with world, flesh and life, seen by those with Siva-knowledge, full without lack, without caste or clan, the space the devotees speak of, the true house and sea of bliss, and asks whether, the senses' torment ended, he will gain the feet. The praise names Murugan as the child Guha who removed Indra's sorrow, and the son of the Mother, first of the world, daughter of the Himalaya, Gauri, Kundali, the form of nada, spouse of the lord of the white jambu.","n":"சரியை க்ரியையும் புணர்ந்தவர் எவராலும் ஓத அரிய: the rites of the first two stages cannot reach it, and துரியம் கடந்தது puts it beyond the fourth state. சாதி குலமும் இலது: it has no caste or clan, a pointed phrase. The goddess's names close the song at Thiruvanaikka, where she is Akhilandeswari.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["gnaanam","sariyai","avaththai","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:31"},"p:360":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":360,"t":["கருமுகில்திர ளாகக் கூடிய","இருளெனமரு ளேறித் தேறிய","கடிகமழள காயக் காரிகள் ...... புவிமீதே","கனவியவிலை யோலைக் காதிகள்","முழுமதிவத னேரப் பாவைகள்","களவியமுழு மோசக் காரிகள் ...... மயலாலே","பரநெறியுண ராவக் காமுகர்","உயிர்பலிகொளு மோகக் காரிகள்","பகழியைவிழி யாகத் தேடிகள் ...... முகமாயப்","பகடிகள்பொரு ளாசைப் பாடிக","ளுருவியதன பாரக் கோடுகள்","படவுளமழி வேனுக் கோரருள் ...... புரிவாயே","மரகதவித நேர்முத் தார்நகை","குறமகளதி பாரப் பூண்முலை","மருவியமண வாளக் கோலமு ...... முடையோனே","வளைதருபெரு ஞாலத் தாழ்கடல்","முறையிடநடு வாகப் போயிரு","வரைதொளைபட வேல்விட் டேவிய ...... அதிதீரா","அரவணைதனி லேறிச் சீருடன்","விழிதுயில்திரு மால்சக் ராயுதன்","அடியிணைமுடி தேடிக் காணவும் ...... அரிதாய","அலைபுனல்சடை யார்மெச் சாண்மையும்","உடையதொர்மயில் வாசிச் சேவக","அழகியதிரு வானைக் காவுறை ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet calls the women of cloud-dark hair and moon faces thieves of love, total cheats, who take the lives of lustful men ignorant of the higher path, who make arrows their eyes and sing for greed of wealth, and asks grace for himself who perishes by them. The praise names Murugan the bridegroom of the hunter girl, the vel that pierced the two hills while the sea cried out, and the peacock rider whose valour Siva, whose head and feet Vishnu on the serpent bed could not find, admires, at Thiruvanaikka.","n":"பரநெறி உணரா அக்காமுகர் உயிர்பலி கொளும்: the women take the lustful as a sacrifice, and their victims are those who do not know the higher path. The search for Siva's head and feet is given to Vishnu alone here.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:31"},"p:361":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":361,"t":["காவிப் பூவை யேவை யிகல்கவன","நீலத் தால கால நிகர்வன","காதிப் போக மோக மருள்வன ...... இருதோடார்","காதிற் காதி மோதி யுழல்கண","மாயத் தார்கள் தேக பரிசன","காமக் ரோத லோப மப மிவை ...... சிதையாத","பாவிக் காயு வாயு வலம்வர","லாலிப் பார்கள் போத கருமவு","பாயத் தான ஞான நெறிதனை ...... யினிமேலன்","பாலெக் காக யோக ஜெபதப","நேசித் தார வார பரிபுர","பாதத் தாளு மாறு திருவுள ...... நினையாதோ","கூவிக் கோழி வாழி யெனமயி","லாலித் தால கால மெனவுயர்","கூளிச் சேனை வான மிசைதனில் ...... விளையாடக்","கோரத் தீர சூர னுடைவினை","பாறச் சீற லேன பதிதனை","கோலக் கால மாக அமர்செய்த ...... வடிவேலா","ஆவிச் சேல்கள் பூக மடலிள","பாளைத் தாறு கூறு படவுய","ராலைச் சோலை மேலை வயலியி ...... லுறைவோனே","ஆசைத் தோகை மார்க ளிசையுட","னாடிப் பாடி நாடி வருதிரு","ஆனைக் காவில் மேவி யருளிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of women's poison-blue eyes and illusory bodies, and of lust, anger, greed and pride that are not destroyed in him, and asks whether Murugan will not think to rule him at his anklet feet, so that from now on he may love the path of knowledge that the masters of breath praise, and yoga, japa and tapas. The praise has the cock crying 'long live', the peacock dancing and ghoul armies playing in the sky, the vel that ended Sura, Murugan of Vayalur, and Thiruvanaikka where women sing and dance.","n":"காமக் ரோத லோப ... இவை சிதையாத பாவி: the inner enemies are named, though the fourth word is garbled. Unlike 355, here breath practice (வாயு வலம் வரல்) and yoga are part of what the poet hopes for. The division of lines 9 to 11 is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","vekuli","yogam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:31"},"p:362":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":362,"t":["குருதிபு லாலென்பு தொன ரம்புகள்","கிருமிகள் மாலம்பி சீத மண்டிய","குடர்நிணம் ரோமங்கள் மூளை யென்பன ...... பொதிகாயக்","குடிலிடை யோந்து னேரைம்புல","அடவியி லோடுந்து ராசை வஞ்சகர்","கொடியவர் மாபஞ்ச பாத கஞ்செய ...... அதனாலே","சுருதிபு ராணங்க ளாக மம்பகர்","சரியைக்ரி யாவண்டர் பூசை வந்தனை","துதியொடு நாடுந்தி யான மொன்றையு ...... முயலாதே","சுமடம தாய்வம்பு மால்கொ ளுந்திய","திமிரரொ டேபந்த மாய்வ ருந்திய","துரிசற ஆநந்த வீடு கண்டிட ...... அருள்வாயே","ஒருதனி வேல்கொண்டு நீள்க்ர வுஞ்சமும்","நிருதரு மாவுங்க லோல சிந்துவும்","உடைபட மோதுங்கு மார பங்கய ...... கரவீரா","உயர்தவர் மாவும்ப ரான அண்டர்கள்","அடிதொழு தேமன்ப ராவு தொண்டர்கள்","உளமதில் நாளுங்க லாவி யின்புற ...... வுறைவோனே","கருதிய ஆறங்க வேள்வி யந்தணர்","அரிகரி கோவிந்த கேச வென்றிரு","கழல்தொழு சீரங்க ராச னண்புறு ...... மருகோனே","கமலனு மாகண்ட லாதி யண்டரு","மெமது பிரானென்று தாள்வ ணங்கிய","கரிவனம் வாழ்சம்பு நாதர் தந்தருள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes the body as a hut packed with blood, flesh, bone, sinew, worms, bile, phlegm, gut, fat, hair and brain, in which the five senses run into the forest and drive him to the five great sins, so that he never tries the Vedas, Puranas, Agamas, chariyai, kiriyai, worship or meditation; he asks that this fault be removed so he may see the house of bliss. The praise names the single vel that broke Krauncha, the giants, the mango and the sea, Murugan the nephew of Ranganatha of Srirangam whom Brahmins of the six limbs praise as Hari and Govinda, and the son of Sambhunatha of the elephant forest.","n":"The inventory of the body (குருதி புலால் என்பு தோல் நரம்புகள் கிருமிகள் ...) is the same device as the Naladiyar's, but here it explains why the poet's senses ran wild, not why a woman should not be desired. சீரங்கராசன் names Srirangam, across the river from Thiruvanaikka.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","kutram","sariyai","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:31"},"p:363":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":363,"t":["நாடித் தேடித் ...... தொழுவார்பால்","நானத் தாகத் ...... திரிவேனோ","மாடக் கூடற் ...... பதிஞான","வாழ்வைச் சேரத் ...... தருவாயே","பாடற் காதற் ...... புரிவோனே","பாலைத் தேனொத் ...... தருள்வோனே","ஆடற் றோகைக் ...... கினியோனே","ஆனைக் காவிற் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A very short song: the poet asks whether he must wander apart from those who seek and worship Murugan, and asks to be given the wise life of Madurai of mansions. The praise names him the lover of songs, gracious as milk and honey, sweet to the dancing peacock, at Thiruvanaikka.","n":"மாடக் கூடற் பதி ஞான வாழ்வு may point to the Sambandar episode at Madurai, as in 358, or simply mean wisdom at that city. நானத்தாக is hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["adiyaar","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:31"},"p:364":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":364,"t":["நிறைந்த துப்பிதழ் தேனு஡றல் நேரென","மறந்த ரித்தக ணாலால நேரென","நெடுஞ்சு ருட்குழல் ஜநமுத நேரென ...... நெஞ்சின்மேலே","நெருங்கு பொற்றன மாமேரு நேரென","மருங்கு நிட்கள ஆகாச நேரென","நிதம்ப முக்கணர் பூணார நேரென ...... நைந்துசீவன்","குறைந்தி தப்பட வாய்பாடி யாதர","வழிந்த ழைத்தணை மேல்வீழு மாலொடு","குமண்டை யிட்டுடை சோராவி டாயில ...... மைந்துநாபி","குடைந்தி ளைப்புறு மாமாய வாழ்வருள்","மடந்தை யர்க்கொரு கோமாள மாகிய","குரங்கை யொத்துழல் வேனோம னோலய ...... மென்றுசேர்வேன்","மறந்த சுக்ரிப மாநீசன் வாசலி","லிருந்து லுத்தநி யோராத தேதுசொல்","மானங்க ளித்திட லாமோது ரோகித ...... முன்புவாலி","வதஞ்செய் விக்ரம சீராம னானில","மறிந்த திச்சர மோகோ கெடாதினி","வரும்ப டிக்குரை யாய்பார் பலாகவ ...... மென்றுபேசி","அறந்த ழைத்துநு மானோடு மாகடல்","வரம்ப டைத்ததின் மேலேறி ராவண","னரண்கு லைத்தெதிர் போராடு நாரணன் ...... மைந்தனான","அநங்கன் மைத்துன வேளே கலாபியின்","விளங்கு செய்ப்பதி வேலா யுதாவிய","னலங்க யப்பதி வாழ்வான தேவர்கள் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet compares a woman's lips to honey, her eyes to poison, her breasts to Meru, her waist to empty space, and describes lovemaking in which his life wanes and his clothes slip, then asks whether he must roam like a clown's monkey for women who give an illusory life, and when he will reach the stilling of mind. The praise retells the Ramayana at length: Rama's message to the forgetful Sugriva that the arrow that killed Vali is not spent, the bridge across the sea and Ravana's fortress broken, and names Murugan the brother-in-law of Kama, Narayana's son.","n":"குரங்கை ஒத்து உழல்வேனோ: the poet sees himself as a performing monkey; the image leads, perhaps by design, into the monkey-king of the praise. மனோலயம் என்று சேர்வேன்: the goal named is the dissolution of mind. The speech to Sugriva is hard to divide, and ஜநமுத in line 3 may stand for ஜலமுத, cloud.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["kaamam","punarchi","mayal"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:31"},"p:365":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":365,"t":["பரிமள மிகவுள சாந்து மாமத","முருகவிழ் வகைமலர் சேர்ந்து கூடிய","பலவரி யளிதுயில் கூர்ந்து வானுறு ...... முகில்போலே","பரவிய இருள்செறி கூந்தல் மாதர்கள்","பரிபுர மலரடி வேண்டி யேவிய","பணிவிடை களிலிறு மாந்த கூளனை ...... நெறிபேணா","விரகனை யசடனை வீம்பு பேசிய","விழலனை யுறுகலை யாய்ந்தி டாமுழு","வெகுளியை யறிவது போங்க பாடனை ...... மலமாறா","வினையனை யுரைமொழி சோர்ந்த பாவியை","விளிவுறு நரகிடை வீழ்ந்த மோடனை","வினவிமு னருள்செய்து பாங்கி னாள்வது ...... மொருநாளே","கருதலர் திரிபுர மாண்டு நீறெழ","மலைசிலை யொருகையில் வாங்கு நாரணி","கழலணி மலைமகள் காஞ்சி மாநக ...... ருறைபேதை","களிமயில் சிவனுடன் வாழ்ந்த மோகினி","கடலுடை யுலகினை யீன்ற தாயுமை","கரிவன முறையகி லாண்ட நாயகி ...... யருள்பாலா","முரணிய சமரினில் மூண்ட ராவண","னிடியென அலறிமு னேங்கி வாய்விட","முடிபல திருகிய நீண்ட மாயவன் ...... மருகோனே","முதலொரு குறமகள் நேர்ந்த நூலிடை","யிருதன கிரிமிசை தோய்ந்த காமுக","முதுபழ மறைமொழி யாய்ந்த தேவர்கள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet heaps names on himself: the wretch proud of running errands at fragrant-haired women's feet, trickster, fool, boaster, worthless, unlearned, all anger, stupid, a sinner of unchanging deeds whose speech fails, fallen into hell, and asks whether a day will come when Murugan asks after him and rules him kindly. The praise is of Uma as Narayani who held the mountain bow to burn the cities, the girl of Kanchi, mother of the sea-girt world, Akhilanda Nayaki of the elephant forest, and names Murugan the nephew of Vishnu who twisted off Ravana's heads.","n":"வினவி முன் அருள்செய்து பாங்கின் ஆள்வது: the hope is that the Lord will take the first step and inquire. The praise gives the burning of the three cities to Uma (மலைசிலை ஒருகையில் வாங்கு நாரணி), and names her as the goddess of both Kanchi and Thiruvanaikka (அகிலாண்ட நாயகி).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kutram","pedhaimai","saththi","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:31"},"p:366":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":366,"t":["வேலைப் போல்விழி யிட்டும ருட்டிகள்","காமக் ரோதம்வி ளைத்திடு துட்டிகள்","வீதிக் கேதிரி பப்பர மட்டைகள் ...... முலையானை","மேலிட் டேபொர விட்டபொ றிச்சிகள்","மார்பைத் தோளைய சைத்துந டப்பிகள்","வேளுக் காண்மைசெ லுத்துச மர்த்திகள் ...... களிகூருஞ்","சோலைக் கோகில மொத்தமொ ழிச்சிகள்","காசற் றாரையி தத்திலொ ழிச்சிகள்","தோலைப் பூசிமி னுக்கியு ருக்கிகள் ...... எவரேனும்","தோயப் பாயல ழைக்கும வத்திகள்","மோகப் போகமு யக்கிம யக்கிகள்","சூறைக் காரிகள் துக்கவ லைப்பட ...... லொழிவேனோ","காலைக் கேமுழு கிக்குண திக்கினில்","ஆதித் யாயஎ னப்பகர் தர்ப்பண","காயத் ஡ணசெப மர்ச்சனை யைச்செயு ...... முநிவோர்கள்","கானத் தாசிர மத்தினி லுத்தம","வேள்விச் சாலைய ளித்தல்பொ ருட்டெதிர்","காதத் தாடகை யைக்கொல்க்ரு பைக்கடல் ...... மருகோனே","ஆலைச் சாறுகொ தித்துவ யற்றலை","பாயச் சாலித ழைத்திர தித்தமு","தாகத் தேவர்கள் மெச்சிய செய்ப்பதி ...... யுறைவேலா","ஆழித் தேர்மறு கிற்பயில் மெய்த்திரு","நீறிட் டான்மதிள் சுற்றிய பொற்றிரு","ஆனைக் காவினி லப்பர்ப்ரி யப்படு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet lists courtesan epithets, women who bewilder with spear eyes, breed lust and anger, roam the streets, send their breasts into battle, speak like cuckoos, drive out men without money, paint their skin and call anyone to bed, plunderers, and asks whether he will escape the net of sorrow. The praise names Rama, the sea of compassion who killed Tataka to guard the sacrifice of sages who bathe at dawn and offer to the sun and recite the Gayatri, as uncle, Murugan of the cane and paddy fields of Vayalur, and the beloved of the Father at Thiruvanaikka.","n":"காசற்றாரை இதத்தில் ஒழிச்சிகள்: the women gently put out those without money. காம க்ரோதம் விளைத்திடு: they breed anger as well as lust. The praise sets Vedic morning rites (தர்ப்பணம், காயத்ரீ செபம்) beside the confession.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vekuli"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:31"},"p:367":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":367,"t":["குமர குருபர குணதர நிசிசர","திமிர தினகர சரவண பவகிரி","குமரி சுதபகி ரதிசுத சுரபதி ...... குலமானுங்","குறவர் சிறுமியு மருவிய திரள்புய","முருக சரணென வுருகுதல் சிறிதுமில்","கொடிய வினையனை யவலனை யசடனை ...... யதிமோகக்","கமரில் விழவிடு மழகுடை யரிவையர்","களவி னொடுபொரு ளளவள வருளிய","கலவி யளறிடை துவளுறும் வெளிறனை ...... யினிதாளக்","கருணை யடியரொ டருணையி லொருவிசை","சுருதி புடைதர வருமிரு பரிபுர","கமல மலரடி கனவிலு நனவிலு ...... மறவேனே","தமர மிகுதிரை யெறிவளை கடல்குடல்","மறுகி யலைபட விடநதி யுமிழ்வன","சமுக முககண பணபணி பதிநெடு ...... வடமாகச்","சகல வுலகமு நிலைபெற நிறுவிய","கனக கிரிதிரி தரவெகு கரமலர்","தளர வினியதொ ரமுதினை யொருதனி ...... கடையாநின்","றமரர் பசிகெட வுதவிய க்ருபைமுகில்","அகில புவனமு மளவிடு குறியவன்","அளவு நெடியவ னளவிட அரியவன் ...... மருகோனே","அரவு புனைதரு புநிதரும் வழிபட","மழலை மொழிகொடு தெளிதர வொளிதிகழ்","அறிவை யறிவது பொருளென அருளிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet calls himself cruel, wretched and foolish, one who never melts crying 'Kumara, Guru, Saravanabhava, Muruga, refuge', who falls into the pit of beautiful women who give embraces in measure with wealth, and vows that he will not forget, waking or dreaming, the lotus feet with anklets which once at Arunai came with the compassionate devotees and the Vedas at their side to rule him. The praise names Vishnu who churned the sea with Vasuki and Meru so the gods' hunger ended, the dwarf who measured the worlds, as uncle, and Murugan who, in prattling speech, taught Siva that the meaning is to know the knower.","n":"அருணையில் ஒருவிசை ... வரும் இருபரிபுர கமல மலரடி: the feet came once at Arunai (Tiruvannamalai), apparently the poet's own experience of grace, so the vow rests on memory. அறிவை அறிவது பொருளென அருளிய: the teaching to Siva is given as 'know that which knows', without Om. The courtesans who give union by the measure of wealth (பொருளளவளவு) appear again.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvadi","thiruvarul","guru","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:31"},"p:368":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":368,"t":["அருவ மிடையென வருபவர் துவரிதழ்","அமுது பருகியு முருகியு ம்ருகமத","அளக மலையவு மணிதுகி லகலவு ...... மதிபார","அசல முலைபுள கிதமெழ அமளியில்","அமளி படஅந வரதமு மவசமொ","டணையு மழகிய கலவியு மலமல ...... முலகோரைத்","தருவை நிகரிடு புலமையு மலமல","முருவு மிளமையு மலமலம் விபரித","சமய கலைகளு மலமல மலமரும் ...... வினைவாழ்வுஞ்","சலில லிபியன சனனமு மலமல","மினியு னடியரொ டொருவழி படஇரு","தமர பரிபுர சரணமு மவுனமு ...... மருள்வாயே","உருவு கரியதொர் கணைகொடு பணிபதி","யிருகு தையுமுடி தமனிய தநுவுட","னுருளை யிருசுடர் வலவனு மயனென ...... மறைபூணும்","உறுதி படுசுர ரதமிசை யடியிட","நெறுநெ றெனமுறி தலுநிலை பெறுதவம்","உடைய வொருவரு மிருவரு மருள்பெற ...... வொருகோடி","தெருவு நகரியு நிசிசரர் முடியொடு","சடச டெனவெடி படுவன புகைவன","திகுதி கெனஎரி வனஅனல் நகைகொடு ......முனிவார்தஞ்","சிறுவ வனசரர் சிறுமியொ டுருகிய","பெரும அருணையி லெழுநிலை திகழ்வன","சிகரி மிசையொரு கலபியி லுலவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says 'enough, enough' (அலம் அலம்) to lovemaking with women, to the learning that likens men of the world to the wish-tree, to form and youth, to the arts of perverse sects, to the bewildering life of deeds and to births like writing on water, and asks now for his feet and for silence, to walk one way with his devotees. The praise tells at length of Siva's burning of the three cities, the chariot breaking under his foot and the cities blazing at his laugh, and names Murugan his son, who melted with the hunter girl and roams on a peacock over the peak of Arunai with its seven-tiered towers.","n":"The refrain அலமலம் sweeps away not only women but the poet's own trade: உலகோரைத் தருவை நிகரிடு புலமை is the art of praising patrons as the kalpa tree. சலில லிபியன சனனம், births like letters written on water, is a striking image of impermanence. The Tripura story is told in its full details (Meru as bow, the serpent king as string, sun and moon as wheels, Brahma as charioteer, the Vedas as horses), and the three spared devotees appear as ஒருவரும் இருவரும் அருள்பெற.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","samayam","piravi","mounam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:32"},"p:369":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":369,"t":["கருணை சிறிதுமில் பறிதலை நிசிசரர்","பிசித அசனம றவரிவர் முதலிய","கலக விபரித வெகுபர சமயிகள் ...... பலர்கூடிக்","கலக லெனநெறி கெடமுறை முறைமுறை","கதறி வதறிய குதறிய கலைகொடு","கருத அரியதை விழிபுனல் வரமொழி ...... குழறாவன்","புருகி யுனதருள் பரவுகை வரில்விர","கொழியி லுலகியல் பிணைவிடி லுரைசெய","லுணர்வு கெடிலுயிர் புணரிரு வினையள ...... றதுபோக","உதறி லெனதெனு மலமறி லறிவினி","லெளிது பெறலென மறைபறை யறைவதொ","ருதய மரணமில் பொருளினை யருளுவ ...... தொருநாளே","தருண சததள பரிமள பரிபுர","சரணி தமனிய தநுதரி திரிபுர","தகனி கவுரிப வதிபக வதிபயி ...... ரவிசூலி","சடில தரியநு பவையுமை திரிபுரை","சகல புவனமு முதவிய பதிவ்ருதை","சமய முதல்வித னயபகி ரதிசுத ...... சதகோடி","அருண ரவியினு மழகிய ப்ரபைவிடு","கருணை வருணித தனுபர குருபர","அருணை நகருறை சரவண குரவணி ...... புயவேளே","அடவி சரர்குல மரகத வனிதையு","மமரர் குமரியு மனவர தமுமரு","கழகு பெறநிலை பெறவர மருளிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet sets against the pitiless, hair-plucked night-wanderers, flesh-eaters and many quarrelsome sectarians who wail broken scriptures till the path is lost, the reality without rising or death which the Vedas drum is easily gained in knowledge when the eyes stream, the words falter, the heart melts, craving, worldly bonds, speech, act and feeling end, the two deeds fall away and 'mine' is shaken off; he asks when that day of grace will come. The praise is a long litany of the Goddess, Gauri, Bhairavi, Tripurai, the faithful wife who bore all worlds, and names Murugan her son and Ganga's, the guru brighter than countless suns, who gave the forest girl and the gods' daughter their place beside him at Arunai.","n":"பறிதலை நிசிசரர், the hair-plucked night-wanderers, likely means Jain monks, who pluck their hair; பிசித அசனர், flesh-eaters, may be another group. The conditions are stacked as a chain of 'if' clauses (விடில், கெடில், அறில்) ending in எனது எனும் மலம் அறில், 'if the impurity called mine ends'. The Goddess's names in the praise are partly Sanskrit compounds whose divisions are uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["samayam","pattru","gnaanam","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:32"},"p:370":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":370,"t":["துகிலு ம்ருகமத பரிமள அளகமு","நெகிழ இருதன கிரியசை தரஇடை","துவள மனிதரு மமரரு முநிவரு ...... முடனோடித்","தொடர வனமணி மகரமி லகுகுழை","யடரு வனவிட மிளிர்வன ரதிபதி","சுருதி மொழிவன கயல்விழி புரள்தர ...... நடுவாக","வகிரு மதிபுரை தநுநுதல் பனிவர","வனச பதயுக பரிபுர மொலிபட","மறுகு தொறுமுல வியினிய கலவியை ...... விலைகூறும்","வரைவி லரிவையர் தருசுக சலதியி","லலையு மெனதுயி ரநுதின நெறிதரு","மவுன சிவசுக சலதியில் முழுகுவ ...... தொருநாளே","முகிலு மதியமும் ரவியெழு புரவியு","நெடிய குலைமிட றிடறமு துககன","முகடு கிழிபட வளர்வன கமுகின ...... மிசைவாளை","முடுகு கயலுகள் வயல்களு முருகவிழ்","தடமு முளரிய அகழியு மதிள்களு","முழுது முடையதொ ரருணையி லுறைதரு ...... மிளையோனே","அகிலு மருதமு முகுளித வகுளமு","மமுத கதலியும் அருணமும் வருடையு","மபரி மிதமத கரிகளு மரிகளு ...... முடனேகொண்","டருவி யிழிதரு மருவரை தனிலொரு","சவர வனிதையை முநிதரு புனிதையை","அவச முடன்மல ரடிதொழு துருகிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet pictures the women without bounds who sell their sweet lovemaking at a price in every street, their hair and garments loosening, breasts swaying, eyes speaking Kama's scripture, so that men, gods and sages run after them, and asks when his life, tossing in the sea of pleasure they give, will plunge instead into the sea of silent Siva-bliss. The praise paints Arunai, its areca palms tearing the sky so that clouds, moon and the sun's horses stumble, its fields, tanks, moat and walls, and the hill stream carrying agil, flowers, gems, goats, elephants and lions, where Murugan bowed melting at the feet of the hunter girl born of a sage.","n":"The song is built on two seas: the சுக சலதி the women give and the மவுன சிவசுக சலதி the poet longs for. வரைவில் அரிவையர் and விலைகூறும் use the very terms of the Kural's chapter on women who draw no line. முநி தரு புனிதை, the pure one born of a sage, points to the story of Valli's birth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","aanandham","mounam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:32"},"p:371":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":371,"t":["மகர மெறிகடல் விழியினு மொழியினு","மதுப முரல்குழல் வகையினு நகையினும்","வளமை யினுமுக நிலவினு மிலவினு ...... நிற்முசும்","மதுர இதழினு மிடையினு நடையினு","மகளிர் முகுளித முலையினு நிலையினும்","வனச பரிபுர மலரினு முலரினு ...... மவர்நாமம்","பகரு கினுமவர் பணிவிடை திரிகினு","முருகி நெறிமுறை தவறினு மவரொடு","பகடி யிடுகினு மமளியி லவர்தரு ...... மநுராகப்","பரவை படியினும் வசமழி யினுமுத","லருணை நகர்மிசை கருணையொ டருளிய","பரம வொருவச னமுமிரு சரணமு ...... மறவேனே","ககன சுரபதி வழிபட எழுகிரி","கடக கிரியொடு மிதிபட வடகுல","கனக கனகுவ டடியொடு முறிபட ...... முதுசூதங்","கதறு சுழிகட லிடைகிழி படமிகு","கலக நிசிசரர் பொடிபட நடவிய","கலப மதகத துரகத ந்ருபகிரி ...... மயில்வாழ்வே","தகன கரதல சிவசுத கணபதி","சகச சரவண பரிமள சததள","சயன வனசரர் கதிபெற முனிபெறு ...... புனமானின்","தரள முகபட நெறிபட நிமிர்வன","தருண புளகித ம்ருகமத தனகிரி","தழுவ மயல்கொடு தனிமட லெழுதிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says that even if he melts at women's eyes, hair, smile, lips, waist, gait and feet, speaks their names, runs their errands, strays from the path, jests with them and loses himself in the sea of passion on their bed, he will not forget the one supreme word and the two feet that Murugan gave him with compassion at Arunai. The praise shows the vel trampling the seven hills, breaking Meru's peak and tearing the old mango tree in the sea, names Murugan Siva's son and Ganapati's brother, and has him, for the hunters' salvation, write a lone madal out of longing for the doe of the millet field.","n":"அருணை நகர்மிசை கருணையொடு அருளிய பரம ஒரு வசனமும் இரு சரணமும் மறவேனே: the poet speaks of a word and the feet given to him at Arunai, apparently his own initiation; the vow is not to be pure but not to forget, even in the midst of desire. தனி மடல் எழுதிய makes Murugan the lover who takes the desperate madal for Valli. முதுசூதம், the old mango, is Sura in tree form.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvadi","guru","kaamam","madal"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:32"},"p:372":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":372,"t":["முகிலை யிகல்பொரு முழுவிருள் குழலென","முதிய மதியது முகமென நுதலிணை","முரணர் வரிசிலை முடுகிடு கணைவிழி ...... யென்முவா","முளரி தனின்முகு ளிதமலர் முலையென","முறுவல் தனையிரு குழைதனை மொழிதனை","மொழிய வரியதொர் தெரிவையர் வினையென ...... மொழிகூறிப்","பகலு மிரவினு மிகமன மருள்கொடு","பதியி லவர்வடி வுளதழ கெனவொரு","பழுது மறஅவர் பரிவுற இதமது ...... பகராதே","பகைகொ டெதிர்பொரு மசுரர்கள் துகைபட","விகட முடனடை பயில்மயில் மிசைவரு","பவனி தனையநு தினநினை யென அருள் ...... பகர்வாயே","புகல வரியது பொருளிது எனவொரு","புதுமை யிடஅரி யதுமுத லெனுமொரு","பொதுவை யிதுவென தவமுடை முநிவர்கள் ...... புடைசூழப்","புரமு மெரியெழ நகையது புரிபவர்","புனலும் வளர்மதி புனைசடை யினரவர்","புடவி வழிபட புதை பொருள் விரகொடு ...... புகல்வோனே","அகில கலைகளு மறநெறி முறைமையு","மகில மொழிதரு புலவரு முலகினி","லறிஞர் தவமுயல் பவர்களு மியலிசை ...... யதனாலே","அறுவர் முலையுணு மறுமுக னிவனென","அரிய நடமிடு மடியவ ரடிதொழ","அருணை நகர்தனி லழகுடன் மருவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks not to spend day and night praising women in set comparisons, hair as darkness that fights the clouds, face as the moon, eyes as arrows, breasts as lotus buds, nor to flatter them that their form is beauty, but to be told by grace to think daily of Murugan's procession on the peacock that crushes the asuras. The praise has Murugan, amid sages, tell the hidden meaning called hard to speak to Siva who burned the cities with a laugh, and has poets, scholars and ascetics declare him the six-faced one who drank from the six mothers, while devotees dance and worship at Arunai.","n":"The fault confessed is poetic: the set similes of love poetry spent on women, against which the poet sets a new subject, the peacock procession (பவனி). புகல அரியது பொருள் இது ... புதை பொருள் விரகொடு புகல்வோனே is the teaching of Siva, though Om is not named. Two glyphs in the first lines are garbled.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","guru","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:32"},"p:373":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":373,"t":["முருகு செறிகுழல் சொருகிய விரகிகள்","முலைக ளளவிடு முகபட பகடிகள்","முதலு முயிர்களு மளவிடு களவியர் ...... முழுநீல","முழுகு புழுககில் குழைவடி வழகியர்","முதிர வளர்கனி யதுகவ ரிதழியர்","முனைகொ ளயிலென விழியெறி கடைசிய ...... ரநுராகம்","மருவி யமளியி னலமிடு கலவியர்","மனது திரவிய மளவள வளவியர்","வசன மொருநொடி நிலைமையில் கபடியர் ...... வழியேநான்","மருளு மறிவின னடிமுடி யறிகிலன்","அருணை நகர்மிசை கருணையொ டருளிய","மவுன வசனமு மிருபெரு சரணமு ...... மறவேனே","கருதி யிருபது கரமுடி யொருபது","கனக மவுலிகொள் புரிசைசெய் பழையது","கடிய வியனகர் புகவரு கனபதி ...... கனல்மூழ்கக்","கவச அநுமனொ டெழுபது கவிவிழ","அணையி லலையெறி யெதிரமர் பொருதிடு","களரி தனிலொரு கணைவிடு மடலரி ...... மருகோனே","சருவு மவுணர்கள் தளமொடு பெருவலி","யகல நிலைபெறு சயிலமு மிடிசெய்து","தரும னவர்பதி குடிவிடு பதனிசை ...... மயில்வீரா","தருண மணியவை பலபல செருகிய","தலையள் துகிலிடை யழகிய குறமகள்","தனது தனமது பரிவொடு தழுவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet lists the courtesans who weigh a man's capital and life, scent themselves with civet and agil, throw spear-like glances, measure the heart by the money and keep their word an instant, and says that he, deluded on their path and knowing neither the foot nor the crown, will not forget the silent word and the two great feet given with compassion at Arunai. The praise tells of Rama against Ravana's walled city, Hanuman's fire and the monkeys' causeway, names Murugan his nephew, and the vel that broke the asuras and restored the gods, and his embrace of the hunter girl.","n":"மனது திரவியம் அளவளவு அளவியர், women who measure their heart exactly to the money, is the stock charge against courtesans. அடிமுடி அறிகிலன், 'I who know neither foot nor crown', may glance at Arunai's own story of the Lord whose foot and crown Vishnu and Brahma could not find, though the song does not tell it. The refrain repeats 371 with மவுன வசனம், the word of silence.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","mounam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:32"},"p:374":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":374,"t":["விடமு மமுதமு மிளிர்வன இணைவிழி","வனச மலதழல் முழுகிய சரமென","விரைசெய் ம்ருகமத அளகமு முகிலல ...... வொருஞான","விழியின் வழிகெட இருள்வதொ ரிருளென","மொழியு மமுதல வுயிர்கவர் வலையென","விழையு மிளநகை தளவல களவென ...... வியனாபித்","தடமு மடுவல படுகுழி யெனஇடை","துடியு மலமத னுருவென வனமுலை","சயில மலகொலை யமனென முலைமிசை ...... புரள்கோவை","தரள மணியல யமன்விடு கயிறென","மகளிர் மகளிரு மலபல வினைகொடு","சமையு முருவென வுணர்வொடு புணர்வது ...... மொருநாளே","அடவி வனிதையர் தனதிரு பரிபுர","சரண மலரடி மலர்கொடு வழிபட","அசல மிசைவிளை புனமதி லினிதுறை ...... தனிமானும்","அமர ரரிவையு மிருபுடை யினும்வர","முகர முகபட கவளத வளகர","அசல மிசைவரு மபிநவ கலவியும் ...... விளையாடுங்","கடக புளகித புயகிரி சமுகவி","கடக கசரத துரகத நிசிசரர்","கடக பயிரவ கயிரவ மலர்களும் ...... எரிதீயுங்","கருக வொளிவிடு தனுபர கவுதம","புநித முநிதொழ அருணையி லறம்வளர்","கருணை யுமைதரு சரவண சுரபதி ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet takes each charm of women and names what it truly is: the eyes not lotuses but fire-dipped arrows, the hair not a cloud but a darkness that blinds the eye of jnana, the speech not nectar but a net for life, the smile not jasmine but theft, the navel not a pool but a pit, the breasts not hills but Yama the killer, the pearls on them Yama's rope; women are not women but forms made of many deeds, and he asks when he will join with that awareness. The praise shows Valli and Devayanai at his sides, the asura armies burned, and Murugan son of compassionate Uma who fosters dharma, at Arunai where the pure sage Gautama worships.","n":"Each line turns on அல, 'not': the poet undoes the love poet's similes (as 372 renounces them) and replaces each with a danger. மகளிர் மகளிரும் அல பல வினைகொடு சமையும் உருவு: the conclusion is metaphysical, that the woman is a body built of karma. கவுதம புநித முநி தொழ and அறம் வளர் கருணை உமை may point to a local story of Uma's penance at Arunai under the sage Gautama, which the song does not tell.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kootru","vinai","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:32"},"p:375":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":375,"t":["கமரி மலர்குழல் சரிய புளகித","கனக தனகிரி யசைய பொருவிழி","கணைக ளெனநுதல் புரள துகிலதை ...... நெகிழ்மாதர்","கரிய மணிபுர ளரிய கதிரொளி","பரவ இணைகுழை யசைய நகைகதிர்","கனக வளைகல நடைகள் பழகிகள் ...... மயில்போலத்","திமிரு மதபுழு கொழுக தெருவினி","லலைய விலைமுலை தெரிய மயல்கொடு","திலத மணிமுக அழகு சுழலிக ...... ளித்ழுறல்","திரையி லமதென கழைகள் பலசுளை","யெனவு மவர்மயல் தழுவு மசடனை","திருகு புலைகொலை கலிகள் சிதறிட ...... அருள்தாராய்","குமர குருபர குமர குருபர","குமர குருபர குமர குருபர","குமர குருபர குமர குருபர ...... எனதாளங்","குரைசெய் முரசமொ டரிய விருதொலி","டமட டமடம டமட டமவென","குமுற திமிலைச லரிகி னரிமுத ...... லிவைபாட","அமரர் முநிவரு மயனு மனைவரு","மதுகை மலர்கொடு தொழுது பதமுற","அசுரர் பரிகரி யிரத முடைபட ...... விடும்வேலா","அகில புவனமொ டடைய வொளிபெற","அழகு சரண்மயில் புறம தருளியொ","ரருண கிரிகுற மகளை மருவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet pictures women who walk the street like peacocks, smeared with musk, showing their breasts for sale, their hair falling and garments loose, and calls himself the fool who embraces their delusion, tasting their lips as cane and fruit; he asks for grace that scatters his base and murderous sins. The praise makes the drums sound 'Kumara Gurupara' six times over as their beat while gods, sages and Brahma worship, and names the vel that broke the asuras' horses, elephants and chariots and Murugan who embraced the hunter girl of the red hill.","n":"விலைமுலை, breasts for sale, names the trade. குமர குருபர repeated six times is given as the drums' own rhythm (எனதாளம் குரைசெய் முரசம்), so the name of the Lord becomes the beat. ஒரு அருண கிரி குறமகள் plays on Arunagiri, the red hill, which is also the poet's name. One word (இத்ழுறல், probably இதழூறல்) is garbled.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","kutram","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:32"},"p:376":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":376,"t":["கயல்விழித் தேனெனைச் செயலழித் தாயெனக்","கணவகெட் டேனெனப் ...... பெறுமாது","கருதுபுத் ராஎனப் புதல்வரப் பாஎனக்","கதறிடப் பாடையிற் ...... றலைமீதே","பயில்குலத் தாரழப் பழையநட் பாரழப்","பறைகள்கொட் டாவரச் ...... சமனாரும்","பரியகைப் பாசம்விட் டெறியுமப் போதெனைப்","பரிகரித் தாவியைத் ...... தரவேணும்","அயிலறச் சேவல்கைக் கினிதரத் தோகையுற்","றருணையிற் கோபுரத் ...... துறைவோனே","அமரரத் தாசிறுக் குமரிமுத் தாசிவத்","தரியசொற் பாவலர்க் ...... கெளியோனே","புயலிளைப் பாறுபொற் சயிலமொய்ச் சாரலிற்","புனமறப் பாவையைப் ...... புணர்வோனே","பொடிபடப் பூதரத் தொடுகடற் சூரனைப்","பொருமுழுச் சேவகப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet pictures his death: his wife wailing 'you have undone me, husband, I am ruined', his sons crying 'father', kin and old friends weeping round the bier, drums beating, and Yama casting his thick noose; at that hour he asks Murugan to protect him and give back his life. The praise names Murugan with vel, cock and peacock dwelling in the tower of Arunai, lord of the gods, easy for poets of rare Saiva song, lover of the hunter girl on the golden hill, and the warrior who crushed Sura with mountain and sea.","n":"The wailing is given in direct speech (கயல்விழித் தேன் எனைச் செயல் அழித்தாய், கணவ கெட்டேன், புதல்வர் அப்பா). பரிகரித்து ஆவியைத் தரவேணும் asks not for release but for the life itself to be saved at the noose. அருணையிற் கோபுரத்து உறைவோனே, the Murugan of the tower, recurs in 377 and 378.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","saakkaadu","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:32"},"p:377":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":377,"t":["கறுவுமிக் காவியைக் கலகுமக் காலனொத்","திலகுகட் சேல்களிப் ...... புடனாடக்","கருதிமுற் பாடுகட் டளையுடற் பேசியுட்","களவினிற் காசினுக் ...... குறவாலுற்","றுறுமலர்ப் பாயலிற் றுயர்விளைத் தூடலுற்","றுயர் பொருட் கோதியுட் ...... படுமாதர்","ஒறுவினைக் கேயுளத் தறிவுகெட் டேனுயிர்ப்","புணையிணைத் தாள்தனைத் ...... தொழுவேனோ","மறையெடுத் தோதிவச் சிரமெடுத் தானுமைச்","செறிதிருக் கோலமுற் ...... றணைவானும்","மறைகள்புக் காரெனக் குவடுநெட் டாழிவற்","றிடஅடற் சூரனைப் ...... பொரும்வேலா","அறிவுடைத் தாருமற் றுடனுனைப் பாடலுற்","றருணையிற் கோபுரத் ...... துறைவோனே","அடவியிற் றோகைபொற் றடமுலைக் காசையுற்","றயருமச் சேவகப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of women whose carp-eyes dance like Death who stirs the life, who settle the body's terms in advance, make relations in secret for coin, cause grief and sulk on the flower bed and give themselves for a high price, and says that for this painful deed he has lost his mind's sense; he asks whether he will worship the two feet that are the raft for life. The praise names Brahma who recites the Vedas, Indra with the vajra and Siva who comes with Uma, the vel that fought Sura so that the peaks and the sea dried, Murugan of the tower at Arunai sung by the wise, and the warrior who longed for the forest girl.","n":"காசினுக்கு உறவு, relation for coin, and உயர் பொருட்கு, for a high price, name the trade. உயிர்ப் புணை இணைத் தாள், the feet as a raft for the life, is an image of crossing. The opening lines (கறுவுமிக் காவியைக் கலகும் அக் காலன் ஒத்து) and முற்பாடு கட்டளை are hard to divide, and the praise's மறைகள் புக்கார் is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","selvam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:32"},"p:378":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":378,"t":["பரியகைப் பாசம்விட் டெறியுமக் காலனுட்","பயனுயிர்ப் போயகப் ...... படமோகப்","படியிலுற் றாரெனப் பலர்கள்பற் றாவடற்","படரெரிக் கூடுவிட் ...... டலைநீரிற்","பிரியுமிப் பாதகப் பிறவியுற் றேமிகப்","பிணிகளுக் கேயிளைத் ...... துழல்நாயேன்","பிழைபொறுத் தாயெனப் பழுதறுத் தாளெனப்","பிரியமுற் றோதிடப் ...... பெறுவேனோ","கரியமெய்க் கோலமுற் றரியினற் றாமரைக்","கமைவபற் றாசையக் ...... கழலோர்முன்","கலைவகுத் தோதிவெற் பதுதொளைத் தோனியற்","கடவுள்செச் சேவல்கைக் ...... கொடியோனென்","றரியநற் பாடலைத் தெரியுமுற் றோற்கிளைக்","கருணையிற் கோபுரத் ...... துறைவோனே","அடவியிற் றோகைபொற் றடமுலைக் காசையுற்","றயருமச் சேவகப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet imagines Death casting his thick noose, the life going out, the kin of passion no longer holding on, and the body left to the fire and its remains to the water; he, a dog born in this sinful birth and wasted by disease, asks whether he will hear Murugan say with love 'I have forgiven your fault, I have cut away your flaw'. The praise is obscure; it seems to name Murugan the god of the red cock banner who pierced the hill and expounded the arts, and closes as 377 does, at the tower of Arunai.","n":"பிழை பொறுத்தாய் எனப் பழுது அறுத்தாள் என: the grace asked for is forgiveness spoken aloud. The first line repeats 376's பரியகைப் பாசம் விட்டு எறியும் காலன. The praise lines 9 to 13 (கரியமெய்க் கோலமுற்ற அரியின் ...) are hard to divide, and the last two lines are identical to 377's.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kootru","piravi","noy","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:32"},"p:379":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":379,"t":["தருணமணி வானி லத்தி லருணமணி யால விட்ட","தழலமளி மீதெ றிக்கு ...... நிலவாலே","தலைமைதவி ராம னத்தி னிலைமையறி யாதெ திர்த்த","தறுகண்மத வேள்தொ டுத்த ...... கணையாலே","வருணமட மாதர் கற்ற வசையின்மிகை பேச முற்று","மருவுமென தாவி சற்று ...... மழியாதே","மகுடமணி வாரி சைக்கும் விகடமது லாவு சித்ர","மயிலின்மிசை யேறி நித்தம் ...... வரவேணும்","கருணையக லாவி ழிச்சி களபமழி யாமு லைச்சி","கலவிதொலை யாம றத்தி ...... மணவாளா","கடுவுடைய ராநி ரைத்த சடிலமுடி மீது வைத்த","கடியமல ராத ரித்த ...... கழல்வீரா","அருணமணி யால மைத்த கிரணமணி சூழும் வெற்றி","அருணைநகர் கோபு ரத்தி ...... லுறைவோனே","அசுரர்குலம் வேர றுத்து வடவனலை மீதெ ழுப்பி","அமரர்சிறை மீள விட்ட ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"In the voice of a lovesick woman: by the moonlight that strikes her bed of fire, by the arrows of the fierce god of love who attacked without knowing her state, and by the blame the women of the town speak, her life is all but gone, and she asks Murugan to come daily on his peacock so that it does not perish. The praise names him the bridegroom of the hunter girl, the hero whose feet Siva cherished like flowers on his serpent-wreathed hair, dwelling in the tower of Arunai, who uprooted the asuras and freed the gods.","n":"This is akam poetry in the woman's own voice (எனது ஆவி), the poet in the role of the girl in love with the god. The classical torments are here, the moon and Kama's arrows, with the town's gossip (வருண மட மாதர் கற்ற வசை). The line on Siva placing flowers on his hair (சடிலமுடி மீது வைத்த கடிய மலர் ஆதரித்த கழல்) may mean that Siva wears Murugan's feet; the division is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","alar","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:32"},"p:380":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":380,"t":["முழுகிவட வாமு கத்தி னெழுகனலி லேபி றக்கு","முழுமதிநி லாவி னுக்கும் ...... வசையாலும்","மொழியுமட மாத ருக்கு மினியதனி வேயி சைக்கு","முதியமத ராஜ னுக்கு ...... மழியாதே","புழுகுதிகழ் நீப மத்தி லழகியகு ராநி ரைத்த","புதுமையினி லாறி ரட்டி ...... புயமீதே","புணரும்வகை தானி னைத்த துணரும்வகை நீல சித்ர","பொருமயிலி லேறி நித்தம் ...... வரவேணும்","எழுமகர வாவி சுற்று பொழிலருணை மாந கர்க்கு","ளெழுதரிய கோபு ரத்தி ...... லுறைவோனே","இடைதுவள வேடு வச்சி படமசைய வேக னத்த","இளமுலைவி டாத சித்ர ...... மணிமார்பா","செழுமகுட நாக மொய்த்த ஒழுகுபுனல் வேணி வைத்த","சிவனைமுத லோது வித்த ...... குருநாதா","திசைமுகன்மு ராரி மற்று மரியபல தேவ ருற்ற","சிறையடைய மீள விட்ட ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"Again in the lovesick woman's voice: so that she does not perish from the full moon born of the submarine fire, from the blame of foolish women, from the sweet lone flute and from old King Kama, she asks Murugan to come daily on his blue peacock, so she may know how to embrace his twelve shoulders garlanded with kadamba and kura. The praise names Arunai's tower and tanks, the chest that never leaves the hunter girl, the guru who taught Siva from the first, and the deliverer of Brahma, Vishnu and the gods from prison.","n":"The torments now include the flute (வேயிசை), a classical herald of evening longing. The moon born of the வடவாமுகம், the submarine mare-fire, explains why its light burns. சிவனை முதல் ஓதுவித்த குருநாதா: முதல் may be the first syllable, Om.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","alar","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:32"},"p:381":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":381,"t":["வடவையன லு஡டு புக்கு முழுகியெழு மாம திக்கு","மதுரமொழி யாழி சைக்கு ...... மிருநாலு","வரைதிசைவி டாது சுற்றி யலறுதிரை வாரி திக்கு","மடியருவ வேள்க ணைக்கு ...... மறவாடி","நெடுகனக மேரு வொத்த புளகமுலை மாத ருக்கு","நிறையுமிகு காத லுற்ற ...... மயல்தீர","நினைவினொடு பீலி வெற்றி மரகத லாப சித்ர","நிலவுமயி லேறி யுற்று ...... வரவேணும்","மடலவிழு மாலை சுற்று புயமிருப தோடு பத்து","மவுலியற வாளி தொட்ட ...... அரிராமன்","மருகபல வான வர்க்கு மரியசிவ னார்ப டிக்க","மவுனமறை யோது வித்த ...... குருநாதா","இடையரிவு லாவு முக்ர அருணகிரி மாந கர்க்கு","ளினியகுண கோபு ரத்தி ...... லுறைவோனே","எழுபுவிய ளாவு வெற்பு முடலிநெடு நாக மெட்டு","மிடையுருவ வேலை விட்ட ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"Told of a girl rather than by her: so that the full infatuation of the woman with breasts like Meru may end, harried by the moon that rises after plunging through the submarine fire, by the yazh sweet as speech, by the sea wailing round the eight directions, and by the formless Kama's arrows, Murugan is asked to come on his emerald peacock. The praise names Rama, who cut Ravana's twenty arms and ten crowns, as his uncle, and Murugan the guru who made Siva study the silent Veda, dwelling in the tower of fierce Arunagiri, whose vel pierced the hill and the eight serpents.","n":"The speaker is now a third person (a mother or friend), as in 24. மவுன மறை ஓதுவித்த, taught the silent Veda, links the teaching of Siva with the silence asked for in 368 and 370. One word (வடவையன லு஡டு) has a corrupt glyph.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kaamanoy","mayal","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:32"},"p:382":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":382,"t":["ஆலவிழி நீலத் தாலதர பானத்","தாலளக பாரக் ...... கொண்டலாலே","ஆரநகை யால்விற் போர்நுதலி னால்வித்","தாரநடை யால்நற் ...... கொங்கையாலே","சாலமய லாகிக் காலதிரி சூலத்","தாலிறுகு பாசத் ...... துன்ப்முழ்கித்","தாழ்விலுயிர் வீழ்பட் டூழ்வினைவி டாமற்","சாவதன்மு னேவற் ...... கொண்டிடாயோ","சோலைதரு கானிற் கோலமற மானைத்","தோளிலுற வாகக் ...... கொண்டவாழ்வே","சோதிமுரு காநித் தாபழய ஞானச்","சோணகிரி வீதிக் ...... கந்தவேளே","பாலகக லாபக் கோமளம யூரப்","பாகவுமை பாகத் ...... தன்குமாரா","பாதமலர் மீதிற் போதமலர் தூவிப்","பாடுமவர் தோழத் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet, infatuated by eyes like poison, lips, hair, smile, brow, gait and breasts, sunk in the misery of Death's trident and tightening noose, asks whether, before he dies with his old deeds unshed, Murugan will not take him into his service. The praise names him the light, Muruga, eternal, who took the hunter girl on his shoulder, Kandavel of the street of the ancient jnana-hill Sonagiri, son of the one who shares Uma, and the comrade-lord of those who sing scattering flowers of knowledge on his feet.","n":"ஏவல் கொண்டிடாயோ, 'will you not take my service', asks to become a servant before death. பழய ஞானச் சோணகிரி calls the red hill itself a hill of ancient knowledge. பாடுமவர் தோழத் தம்பிரானே, lord who is comrade to his singers, may echo the Saiva title of Sundarar, the Lord's comrade, from the other side. The last two lines are identical to 383's.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mayal","kootru","vinai","thondu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:32"},"p:383":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":383,"t":["பேதகவி ரோதத் தோதகவி நோதப்","பேதையர்கு லாவைக் ...... கண்டுமாலின்","பேதைமையு றாமற் றேதமக லாமற்","பேதவுடல் பேணித் ...... தென்படாதே","சாதகவி காரச் சாதலவை போகத்","தாழ்விலுயி ராகச் ...... சிந்தையாலுன்","தாரைவடி வேலைச் சேவல்தனை யேனற்","சாரல்மற மானைச் ...... சிந்தியேனோ","போதகம யூரப் போதகக டாமற்","போதருணை வீதிக் ...... கந்தவேளே","போதகக லாபக் கோதைமுது வானிற்","போனசிறை மீளச் ...... சென்றவேலா","பாதகப தாதிச் சூரன்முதல் வீழப்","பாருலகு வாழக் ...... கண்டகோவே","பாதமலர் மீதிற் போதமலர் தூவிப்","பாடுமவர் தோழத் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks that, seeing the throngs of deceitful, quarrelsome, tricky women, he not fall into the folly of delusion nor tend this divided body, but that birth, change and death leave him and he think in his mind of Murugan's sharp vel, his cock and the hunter girl of the millet slope. The praise names Kandavel of Arunai's street, the vel that went to free the gods' prisoners in the old sky, the lord who saw the world live when Sura and his foot soldiers fell, and the comrade-lord of those who sing scattering flowers of knowledge on his feet.","n":"சாதக விகாரச் சாதல் may be birth (ஜாதகம்), change and death, the bodily cycle. The remedy is contemplation of the Lord's emblems (vel, cock, Valli) rather than his feet. The opening (பேதக விரோதத் தோதக விநோத) and the line ending தென்படாதே are hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","pedhaimai","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:33"},"p:384":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":384,"t":["அமுத மூறுசோ லாகிய தோகையர்","பொருளு ளாரையெ னாணையு னாணையெ","னருகு வீடிது தானதில் வாருமெ ...... னுரைகூறும்","அசடு மாதர்கு வாதுசொல் கேடிகள்","தெருவின் மீதுகு லாவியு லாவிகள்","அவர்கள் மாயைப டாமல்கெ டாமல்நி ...... னருள்தாராய்","குமரி காளிவ ராகிம கேசுரி","கவுரி மோடிசு ராரிநி ராபரி","கொடிய சூலிசு டாரணி யாமளி ...... மகமாயி","குறளு ரூபமு ராரிச கோதரி","யுலக தாரிஉதாரிப ராபரி","குருப ராரிவி காரிந மோகரி ...... அபிராமி","சமர நீலிபு ராரித னாயகி","மலைகு மாரிக பாலிந னாரணி","சலில மாரிசி வாயம னோகரி ...... பரையோகி","சவுரி வீரிமு நீர்விட போஜனி","திகிரி மேவுகை யாளிசெ யாளொரு","சகல வேதமு மாயின தாயுமை ...... யருள்பாலா","திமித மாடுசு ராரிநி சாசரர்","முடிக டோ றுக டாவியி டேயொரு","சிலப சாசுகு ணாலிநி ணாமுண ...... விடும்வேலா","திருவு லாவுசொ ணேசர ணாமலை","முகிலு லாவுவி மானந வோநிலை","சிகர மீதுகு லாவியு லாவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks for grace not to fall into the maya of the sweet-tongued women who say to men of wealth 'by my oath, by your oath, my house is near, come there', foolish, quarrelsome and wicked, sauntering in the streets. The praise is a long litany of the Goddess, Kumari, Kali, Varahi, Maheswari, Gauri, Mahamayi, Abhirami, Narayani, sister of Vishnu, she who drank the poison of the sea, mother Uma who became all the Vedas, and names Murugan her son, whose vel fed the ghouls on the asuras' heads, roaming on the cloud-touched tower of Sonesar's Annamalai.","n":"பொருளுளாரை, men of wealth, names whom the women call: the invitation 'my house is near' is the courtesan's speech of 21. The praise is chiefly of the Goddess, gathering the names of fierce and gentle forms (Kali, Varahi, Kapali, Abhirami) into the one mother Uma. Some names in the litany (மோடி, நிராபரி, சுடாரணி) are uncertain to me.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:33"},"p:385":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":385,"t":["உருகு மாமெழு காகவு மேமயல்","பெருகு மாசையு ளாகிய பேர்வரி","லுரிய மேடையில் வார்குழல் நீவிய ...... வொளிமானார்","உடைகொள் மேகலை யால்முலை மூடியும்","நெகிழ நாடிய தோதக மாடியு","முவமை மாமயில் போல்நிற மேனிய ...... ருரையாடுங்","கரவ தாமன மாதர்கள் நீள்வலை","கலக வாரியில் வீழடி யேநெறி","கருதொ ணாவதி பாதக னேசம ...... தறியாத","கசட மூடனை யாளவு மேயருள்","கருணை வாரிதி யேயிரு நாயகி","கணவ னேயுன தாளிணை மாமலர் ...... தருவாயே","சுருதி மாமொழி வேதியன் வானவர்","பரவு கேசனை யாயுத பாணிநல்","துளப மாலையை மார்பணி மாயவன் ...... மருகோனே","தொலைவி லாவசு ரேசர்க ளானவர்","துகள தாகவு மேயெதி ராடிடு","சுடரின் வேலவ னேயுல கேழ்வலம் ...... வருவோனே","அருணர் கோடியி னாரொளி வீசிய","தருண வாண்முக மேனிய னேயர","னணையு நாயகி பாலக னேநிறை ...... கலையோனே","அணிபொன் மேருயர் கோபுர மாமதி","ளதிரு மாரண வாரண வீதியு","ளருணை மாநகர் மேவியு லாவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of women who melt men like wax, comb their long hair on the street platforms, cover and loosen their breasts with their garments, play tricks and talk with deceitful hearts, and calls himself the great sinner and dunce who has fallen into their long net in the sea of strife and does not know love; he asks the sea of compassion, husband of two consorts, to give him his feet and rule him. The praise names Brahma and the gods who praise him, Vishnu with the tulasi garland as uncle, the bright vel, the one who circles the seven worlds, son of the Lady whom Hara embraces, at Arunai with its golden tower, walls and streets.","n":"நேசமது அறியாத கசட மூடன்: the poet's fault is that he does not know love, set against the women's false love. The praise's உலகேழ் வலம் வருவோனே recalls the circling of the world in the contest for the fruit.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kutram","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:33"},"p:386":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":386,"t":["கரியுரி அரவ மணிந்த மேனியர்","கலைமதி சலமு நிறைந்த வேணியர்","கனல்மழு வுழையு மமர்ந்த பாணியர் ...... கஞ்சமாதின்","கனமுலை பருகி வளர்ந்த காமனை","முனிபவர் கயிலை யமர்ந்த காரணர்","கதிர்விரி மணிபொ னிறைந்த தோளினர் ...... கண்டகாள","விரிவென வுனது ளுகந்த வேலென","மிகவிரு குழையு டர்ந்து வேளினை","யனையவ ருயிரை விழுங்கி மேலும்வெ ...... குண்டுநாடும்","வினைவிழி மகளிர் தனங்கள் மார்புற","விதமிகு கலவி பொருந்தி மேனியு","மெழில்கெட நினைவு மழிந்து மாய்வதொ ...... ழிந்திடாதோ","எரிசொரி விழியு மிரண்டு வாளெயி","றிருபிறை சயில மிரண்டு தோள்முகி","லெனவரு மசுரர் சிரங்கள் மேருஇ ...... டிந்துவீழ்வ","தெனவிழ முதுகு பிளந்து காளிக","ளிடுபலி யெனவு நடந்து தாள்தொழ","எதிர்பொரு துதிர முகந்த வேகமு ...... கைந்தவேலா","அரிகரி யுழுவை யடர்ந்த வாண்மலை","அருணையி லறவு முயர்ந்த கோபுர","மதினுறை குமர அநந்த வேதமொ ...... ழிந்துவாழும்","அறுமுக வடிவை யொழிந்து வேடர்கள்","அடவியி லரிவை குயங்கள் தோய்புய","அரியர பிரம புரந்த ராதியர் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet likens women's eyes to the dark poison of Siva's throat and to Murugan's own vel, Siva being described in full with hide, serpent, crescent, Ganga, fire, axe and deer, the burner of Kama; these eyes press against the earrings and swallow the lives of men like Kama, and he asks whether his dying, body and mind ruined in their embrace, will not end. The praise pictures asura heads falling like broken Meru while ghouls walk as at an offering, Kumara in Arunai's tall tower, and the one who left the six-faced form the Vedas praise to go among hunters and embrace the forest girl, lord of Hari, Hara, Brahma and Indra.","n":"The simile is bold: the eyes that kill are compared to the poison Siva holds in his throat (கண்டகாளம்) and to the vel Murugan loves (உனது உள் உகந்த வேல்), so the deadly look is matched with the Lord's own weapons. அறுமுக வடிவை ஒழிந்து: Murugan put aside his six-faced form for Valli, a theme repeated in 387.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kan","kaamam","yaakkai","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:33"},"p:387":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":387,"t":["கனைகடல் வயிறுகு ழம்பி வாய்விட","வடதம னியகிரி கம்ப மாய்நட","கணபண விபரித கந்த காளபு ...... யங்கராஜன்","கயிறென அமரர நந்த கோடியு","முறைமுறை யமுதுக டைந்த நாளொரு","கதியற வுலகைவி ழுங்கு மேகவொ ...... ழுங்குபோல","வினைமத கரிகளு மெண்டி சாமுக","கிரிகளு முறுகிட அண்ட கோளகை","வெடிபட எவரையும் விஞ்சி வேலிடு ...... நஞ்சுபோல","விடுகுழை யளவும ளந்து காமுக","ருயிர்பலி கவர்வுறு பஞ்ச பாதக","விழிவலை மகளிரொ டன்பு கூர்வதொ ...... ழிந்திடாதோ","முனைபெற வளையஅ ணைந்த மோகர","நிசிசரர் கடகமு றிந்து தூளெழ","முகிலென வுருவமி ருண்ட தாருக ...... னஞ்சமீன","முழுகிய திமிரத ரங்க சாகர","முறையிட இமையவர் தங்க ளூர்புக","முதுகிரி யுருவமு னிந்த சேவக ...... செம்பொன்மேரு","அனையன கனவித சண்ட கோபுர","அருணையி லுறையும ருந்து ணாமுலை","அபிநவ வனிதைத ருங்கு மாரநெ ...... ருங்குமால்கொண்","டடவியில் வடிவுக ரந்து போயொரு","குறமகள் பிறகுதி ரிந்த காமுக","அரியர பிரமபு ரந்த ராதியர் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet compares women's eyes to the poison that rose when the gods churned the sea with Meru as post and the serpent king as rope, a poison that spread like massed clouds, shook the elephants and mountains of the eight directions and cracked the cosmic egg; these eye-nets, guilty of the five great sins, take lovers' lives as sacrifice, and he asks whether love for such women will not end. The praise shows Taraka destroyed and the gods restored, Murugan son of Unnamulai, the ever-new goddess of Arunai, who in great infatuation hid his form and followed a hunter girl through the forest.","n":"The churning of the sea is told in full only to explain a simile: the women's eyes are that poison. அருந்துணாமுலை (உண்ணாமுலை) is the name of the Goddess at Thiruvannamalai; the song names her as Murugan's mother. அடவியில் வடிவு கரந்து போய் ஒரு குறமகள் பிறகு திரிந்த காமுக: the god himself is called the lover (காமுக) who trailed the hunter girl, a playful answer to the poet's own desire.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","kaamam","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:33"},"p:388":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":388,"t":["இரவியு மதியுந் தெரிவுற எழுமம்","புவிதனி லினமொன் ...... றிடுமாதும்","எழில்புதல் வருநின் றழுதுள முருகும்","மிடர்கொடு நடலம் ...... பலகூறக்","கருகிய வுருவங் கொடுகனல் விழிகொண்","டுயிரினை நமனுங் ...... கருதாமுன்","கலைகொடு பலதுன் பமுமக லிடநின்","கழலிணை கருதும் ...... படிபாராய்","திருமரு வியதிண் புயனயன் விரியெண்","டிசைகிடு கிடவந் ...... திடுசூரன்","திணிபுய மதுசிந் திடஅலை கடலஞ்","சிடவலி யொடுகன் ...... றிடும்வேலா","அருமறை யவரந் தரமுறை பவரன்","புடையவ ருயஅன் ...... றறமேவும்","அரிவையு மொருபங் கிடமுடை யவர்தங்","கருணையி லுறையும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks that, before Yama with charred form and fiery eyes comes for his life while his wife and fine sons stand weeping and telling their griefs, Murugan look on him so that through knowledge his troubles leave and he can think of the two feet. The praise names the vel that cut the shoulders of Sura, at whose coming Vishnu, Brahma and the eight directions trembled, and Arunai of Siva, who for the Vedic priests, the gods and his devotees gave a share of himself to the Lady who practised dharma.","n":"அறம் மேவும் அரிவையும் ஒருபங்கு இடமுடையவர்: Siva sharing his body with the Lady who practised dharma, perhaps pointing to Arunai's story that Uma there gained half of Siva; the song does not tell it. The meaning of the opening (இரவியும் மதியும் தெரிவுற எழும் அம்புவி தனில் இனம் ஒன்றிடு மாது) is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kootru","sutram","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:33"},"p:389":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":389,"t":["விரகொடு வளைசங் கடமது தருவெம்","பிணிகொடு விழிவெங் ...... கனல்போல","வெறிகொடு சமனின் றுயிர்கொளு நெறியின்","றெனவிதி வழிவந் ...... திடுபோதிற்","கரவட மதுபொங் கிடுமன மொடுமங்","கையருற வினர்கண் ...... புனல்பாயுங்","கலகமும் வருமுன் குலவினை களையுங்","கழல்தொழு மியல்தந் ...... தருள்வாயே","பரவிடு மவர்சிந் தையர்விட முமிழும்","படவர வணைகண் ...... டுயில்மாலம்","பழமறை மொழிபங் கயனிமை யவர்தம்","பயமற விடமுண் ...... டெருதேறி","அரவொடு மதியம் பொதிசடை மிசைகங்","கையுமுற அனலங் ...... கையில்மேவ","அரிவையு மொருபங் கிடமுடை யவர்தங்","கருணையில் மருவும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet pictures Yama coming by fate's way with cunning, crushing trouble and cruel disease, eyes like hot fire, raging that today is the day to take the life, while women and kin, their hearts surging, pour tears; before that tumult comes, he asks for the nature to worship the feet that remove the deeds of the line. The praise names Vishnu on the serpent and Brahma of the old Vedas, and Siva who drank the poison to end the gods' fear, rides the bull, wears the snake, moon and Ganga in his hair and fire in his hand, and shares himself with the Lady, at whose Arunai Murugan dwells.","n":"விதி வழி வந்திடு போதில்: Death comes along fate's path. குல வினை களையும் கழல், feet that clear the deeds of the line, may mean inherited karma or the deeds that make up birth. The praise is entirely of Siva; Murugan appears only in the closing பெருமாளே.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","oozh","vinai","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:33"},"p:390":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":390,"t":["இடமடு சுறவை முடுகிய மகர","மெறிகட லிடையெழு ...... திங்களாலே","இருவினை மகளிர் மருவிய தெருவி","லெரியென வருசிறு ...... தென்றலாலே","தடநடு வுடைய கடிபடு கொடிய","சரம்விடு தறுகண ...... நங்கனாலே","சரிவளை கழல மயல்கொளு மரிவை","தனிமல ரணையின ...... லங்கலாமோ","வடகுல சயில நெடுவுட லசுரர்","மணிமுடி சிதறஎ ...... றிந்தவேலா","மறமக ளமுத புளகித களப","வளரிள முலையைம ...... ணந்தமார்பா","அடலணி விகட மரகத மயிலி","லழகுட னருணையி ...... னின்றகோவே","அருமறை விததி முறைமுறை பகரு","மரியர பிரமர்கள் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"A lament for a lovesick girl, in the voice of one who watches her: should the girl, her bangles slipping in her infatuation, suffer on her lone flower bed, harried by the moon rising from the sea of sharks and makaras, by the small south breeze that comes like fire through the street where two-faced women live, and by the arrows of the bodiless god? The praise names the vel that scattered the asuras' crowns, the chest wedded to the hunter girl, the lord on the peacock at Arunai, and the master of Hari, Hara and Brahma whom the Vedas declare.","n":"சரிவளை கழல, bangles slipping, is the classical sign of wasting in love. இருவினை மகளிர் மருவிய தெருவு, the street where women of double dealing live, seems to be the gossips of the town; the phrase could instead name courtesans.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","melivu","alar"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:33"},"p:391":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":391,"t":["கெஜநடை மடவார் வசமதி லுருகா","கிலெசம துறுபாழ் ...... வினையாலே","கெதிபெற நினையா துதிதனை யறியா","கெடுசுக மதிலாழ் ...... மதியாலே","தசையது மருவீ வசையுட லுடனே","தரணியில் மிகவே ...... யுலைவேனோ","சததள மலர்வார் புணைநின கழலார்","தருநிழல் புகவே ...... தருவாயே","திசைமுக வனைநீள் சிறையுற விடுவாய்","திருநெடு கருமால் ...... மருகோனே","திரிபுர தகனா ரிடமதில் மகிழ்வார்","திரிபுரை யருள்சீர் ...... முருகோனே","நிசிசர ருறைமா கிரியிரு பிளவா","நிறையயில் முடுகா ...... விடுவோனே","நிலமிசை புகழார் தலமெனு மருணா","நெடுமதில் வடசார் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether, melting under women of elephant gait, by ruinous deeds that bring distress, never thinking of salvation, not knowing praise and sunk in ruinous pleasure, he must wear himself out in the world with this body of flesh and blame; he asks to enter the shade of the tree that is Murugan's feet, a raft of lotus. The praise names him the one who imprisoned Brahma, nephew of Vishnu, Murugan of Tripurai, in whom the burner of the cities delights, the vel that split the demons' hill, at Arunai, famous shrine, by the north side of its long wall.","n":"சததள மலர்வார் புணை நின கழல் ஆர் தருநிழல்: the feet are both a raft and the shade of a tree, two images of refuge in one line (as in 377). வடசார், the north side of the wall, may place this song at a shrine within the temple.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","yaakkai","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:33"},"p:392":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":392,"t":["அருக்கார் நலத்தைத் திரிப்பார் மனத்துக்","கடுத்தாசை பற்றித் ...... தளராதே","அடற்கா லனுக்குக் கடைக்கால் மிதித்திட்","டறப்பே தகப்பட் ...... டழியாதே","கருக்காரர் நட்பைப் பெருக்கா சரித்துக்","கலிச்சா கரத்திற் ...... பிறவாதே","கருத்தா லெனக்குத் திருத்தா ளளித்துக்","கலைப்போ தகத்தைப் ...... புகல்வாயே","ஒருக்கால் நினைத்திட் டிருக்கால் மிகுத்திட்","டுரைப்பார்கள் சித்தத் ...... துறைவோனே","உரத்தோ ளிடத்திற் குறத்தேனை வைத்திட்","டொளித்தோடும் வெற்றிக் ...... குமரேசா","செருக்கா தருக்கிச் சுரச்சூர் நெருக்கச்","செருச்சூர் மரிக்கப் ...... பொரும்வேலா","திறப்பூ தலத்திற் றிரட்சோண வெற்பிற்","றிருக்கோ புரத்திற் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks not to droop clinging in desire to those who twist the good, not to perish crushed beneath mighty Death's foot, and not to be born into the sea of misery by cultivating friendship with the wicked, but to be given the holy feet and told the teaching of the arts. The praise names Murugan who dwells in the minds of those who think of him once and say his name twice, the victor who hid the hunter girl's honey on his chest and ran off, and the vel that fought proud Sura, at the holy tower on the red hill.","n":"கருக்காரர் நட்பை பெருக்கு ஆசரித்து, cultivating the friendship of wicked men, adds bad company to women and death. ஒருக்கால் நினைத்திட்டு இருக்கால் மிகுத்திட்டு உரைப்பார்: devotion asks little, one thought and two utterances. ஒளித்தோடும், running away in hiding with Valli, is the eloping lover. The opening phrase அருக்கு ஆர் நலத்தைத் திரிப்பார் is hard to construe.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaa","kootru","inam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:33"},"p:393":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":393,"t":["அருமா மதனைப் பிரியா தசரக்","கயலார் நயனக் ...... கொடியார்தம்","அழகார் புளகப் புழுகார் சயிலத்","தணையா வலிகெட் ...... டுடல்தாழ","இருமா நடைபுக் குரைபோ யுணர்வற்","றிளையா வுளமுக் ...... குயிர்சோர","எரிவாய் நரகிற் புகுதா தபடிக்","கிருபா தமெனக் ...... கருள்வாயே","ஒருமால் வரையைச் சிறுதூள் படவிட்","டுரமோ டெறிபொற் ...... கதிர்வேலா","உறைமா னடவிக் குறமா மகளுக்","குருகா றிருபொற் ...... புயவீரா","திருமால் கமலப் பிரமா விழியிற்","றெரியா வரனுக் ...... கரியோனே","செழுநீர் வயல்சுற் றருணா புரியிற்","றிருவீ தியினிற் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet traces a life spent leaning on the breasts of carp-eyed women never parted from Kama, until strength fails, the body bends, he walks coughing, speech goes, feeling ends and the life grows faint, and asks for Murugan's two feet so that he does not enter fire-mouthed hell. The praise names the vel that ground the great hill to dust, the twelve golden shoulders that melted for the hunter girl, the one dear to Hara whom Vishnu and Brahma could not discern with their eyes, at the holy street of Arunapuri among rich fields.","n":"The plea runs as one life, lust then decay (இருமா நடைபுக்கு, walking with a cough) then hell. திருமால் கமலப் பிரமா விழியின் தெரியா அரன், Hara whom Vishnu and lotus Brahma could not see, is the story of the column of fire that Thiruvannamalai claims as its own.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","yaakkai","naragam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:33"},"p:394":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":394,"t":["அழுதுமா வாவெனத் தொழுத்மு டூடுநெக்","கவசமா யாதரக் ...... கடலு஡டுற்","றமைவில்கோ லாகலச் சமயமா பாதகர்க்","கறியொணா மோனமுத் ...... திரைநாடிப்","பிழைபடா ஞானமெய்ப் பொருள்பெறா தேவினைப்","பெரியஆ தேசபுற் ...... புதமாய","பிறவிவா ராகரச் சுழியிலே போய்விழப்","பெறுவதோ நானினிப் ...... புகல்வாயே","பழையபா கீரதிப் படுகைமேல் வாழ்வெனப்","படியுமா றாயினத் ...... தனசாரம்","பருகுமா றானனச் சிறுவசோ ணாசலப்","பரமமா யூரவித் ...... தகவேளே","பொழுதுசூழ் போதுவெற் பிடிபடா பார்முதற்","பொடிபடா வோடமுத் ...... தெறிமீனப்","புணரிகோ கோவெனச் சுருதிகோ கோவெனப்","பொருதவே லாயுதப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet, crying 'come, come', worshipping, melting and losing himself in the sea of love, seeks the seal of silence that the great sinners of the noisy sects cannot know, and asks whether, without gaining the flawless true reality of jnana, he must fall into the whirlpool of the sea of birth, a mere bubble. The praise names Murugan the child of the six who drank their breasts on the bank of the old Ganga, the skilled one on the peacock at the red hill, whose vel made the fish-filled sea and the Vedas alike cry 'ko ko'.","n":"மோன முத்திரை, the seal of silence, is set against the கோலாகலச் சமயம், the uproar of the sects, as in 369. புற்புதமாய பிறவி வாராகரச் சுழி: birth is a bubble in a whirlpool of the sea. The opening line has a garbled word (தொழுத்மு டூடு).","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["mounam","samayam","gnaanam","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:33"},"p:395":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":395,"t":["ஆனைவரிக் கோடிளநிர்ப் பாரமுலைச் சாரசைபட்","டாடைமறைத் தாடுமலர்க் ...... குழலார்கள்","ஆரவடத் தோடலையப் பேசிநகைத் தாசைபொருட்","டாரையுமெத் தாகமயக் ...... கிடுமோகர்","சோனைமழைப் பாரவிழித் தோகைமயிற் சாதியர்கைத்","தூதுவிடுத் தேபொருளைப் ...... பறிமாதர்","தோதகமுற் றேழ்நரகிற் சேருமழற் காயனையுட்","சோதியொளிப் பாதமளித் ...... தருள்வாயே","தானதனத் தீதிமிலைப் பேரிகைகொட் டாசமலைச்","சாயகடற் சூரைவதைத் ...... திடுவோனே","தாளவியற் சோதிநிறக் காலினெழக் கோலியெடுத்","தாபரம்வைத் தாடுபவர்க் ...... கொருசேயே","தேனிரசக் கோவையிதழ்ப் பூவைகுறப் பாவைதனத்","தேயுருகிச் சேருமணிக் ...... கதிர்வேலா","சீரருணைக் கோபுரமுற் றானபுனத் தோகையுமெய்த்","தேவமகட் கோர்கருணைப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of women with heavy breasts and flowered hair who hide in their garments, talk and laugh, bewitch anyone for the love of wealth, send messengers by hand and snatch a man's money, and asks for Murugan's feet of light for himself, who burns on the way to the seven hells through their tricks. The praise sounds the drums of the battle in which Sura was slain, names Murugan son of the one who dances with a raised foot, the vel that melted at the hunter girl's breasts, and the lord gracious to both the peacock of the field and the gods' true daughter at Arunai's tower.","n":"ஆசை பொருட்டு, for the desire of wealth, and கைத் தூதுவிடுத்தே பொருளைப் பறி, sending go-betweens and snatching money, name the trade. சோதி ஒளிப் பாதம், feet of light, may glance at the hill of fire. The opening simile (ஆனை வரிக் கோடு) is unclear.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","naragam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:33"},"p:396":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":396,"t":["இடருக் கிடராகிய கொடுமைக் கணைமேல்வரு","மிறுதிச் சிறுகால்வரு ...... மதனாலே","இயலைத் தருகானக முயலைத் தருமேனியி","லெரியைத் தருமாமதி ...... நிலவாலே","தொடரக் கொடுவாதையி லடையக் கரைமேலலை","தொலையத் தனிவீசிய ...... கடலாலே","துணையற் றணிபூமல ரணையிற் றனியேனுயிர்","துவளத் தகுமோதுயர் ...... தொலையாதோ","வடபொற் குலமேருவின் முடுகிப் பொருசூரனை","மடியச் சுடஏவிய ...... வடிவேலா","மறவக் குலமாமொரு குறமெய்த் திருமாமகள்","மகிழப் புனமேவிய ...... மயில்வீரா","அடரப் படர்கேதகை மடலிற் றழைசேர்வய","லருணைத் திருவீதியி ...... லுறைவோனே","அவனித் திருமாதொடு சிவனுக் கிமையாவிழி","அமரர்க் கரசாகிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"In the voice of a lovesick woman alone on her flower bed: tormented by Kama's cruel arrows and the small breeze of the end, by the great moon that bears the hare and sets her body on fire, and by the sea that sends its waves to the shore, she asks whether it is fitting that her life droop, and whether her grief will not end. The praise names the vel thrown at Sura on golden Meru, the hero who came to the millet field to delight the hunter girl, dwelling in Arunai's street among screwpine-hedged fields, king of the unblinking gods.","n":"The torments are the classical three of akam night, the wind, the moon and the sea, each given its own line ending in -ஆலே. இறுதிச் சிறுகால், the small wind of the end, makes the breeze a herald of death. The closing அவனித் திருமாதொடு சிவனுக்கு is hard to construe.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:33"},"p:397":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":397,"t":["இமராஜனி லாவதெ றிக்குங் ...... கனலாலே","இளவாடையு மூருமொ றுக்கும் ...... படியாலே","சமராகிய மாரனெ டுக்குங் ...... கணையாலே","தனிமானுயிர் சோரும தற்கொன் ...... றருள்வாயே","குமராமுரு காசடி லத்தன் ...... குருநாதா","குறமாமக ளாசைத ணிக்குந் ...... திருமார்பா","அமராவதி வாழ்வம ரர்க்கன் ...... றருள்வோனே","அருணாபுரி வீதியி னிற்கும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A short lament for a lovesick girl, spoken for her: by the moon, the king of cold, that strikes fire, by the young north wind and the town that reproaches her, and by the arrows Kama takes up for war, the lone doe's life faints; grant her a word of grace. The praise names Kumara, Muruga, guru of the matted-haired Siva, the chest that quenches the hunter girl's desire, the giver of Amaravati back to the gods, standing in the street of Arunapuri.","n":"இளவாடையும் ஊரும் ஒறுக்கும்: the north wind and the town punish her together, gossip set beside the weather. தனிமான், the lone doe, is the girl spoken of in third person, so the voice is a mother or friend.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","alar","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:33"},"p:398":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":398,"t":["இரத சுரதமுலை களுமார்பு குத்த நுதல்வேர் வரும்ப","அமுத நிலையில்விர லுகிரேகை தைக்க மணிபோல் விளங்க","இசலி யிசலியுப ரிதலீலை யுற்று இடைநூல் நுடங்க","..... வுளமகிழ்ச் சியினோடே","இருவ ருடலுமொரு வுருவாய் நயக்க முகமே லழுந்த","அளக மவிழவளை களுமேக லிக்க நயனார விந்த","லகரி பெருகஅத ரமுமே யருத்தி முறையே யருந்த","..... உரையெழப் பரிவாலே","புருவ நிமிரஇரு கணவாள் நிமைக்க வுபசார மிஞ்ச","அவச கவசமள வியலே தரிக்க அதிலே யநந்த","புதுமை விளையஅது பரமா பரிக்க இணைதோளு மொன்றி",".... அதிசுகக் கலையாலே","புளக முதிரவிர கமென்வாரி தத்த வரைநாண் மழுங்க","மனமு மனமுமுரு கியெயா தரிக்க வுயிர்போ லுகந்து","பொருள தளவுமரு வுறுமாய வித்தை விலைமாதர் சிங்கி","..... விடஅருட் புரிவாயே","பரவு மகரமுக ரமுமேவ லுற்ற சகரால் விளைந்த","தமர திமிரபிர பலமோக ரத்ந சலராசி கொண்ட","படியை முழுதுமொரு நொடியே மதித்து வலமாக வந்து","..... சிவனிடத் தமர்சேயே","பழநி மிசையிலிசை யிசையே ரகத்தில் திருவா வினன்கு","டியினில் பிரமபுர மதில்வாழ் திருத்த ணிகையூடு மண்டர்","பதிய முதியகதி யதுநா யெனுக்கு முறவாகி நின்று","..... கவிதையைப் புனைவோனே","அரியு மயனுமம ரருமாய சிட்ட பரிபால னன்ப","ரடையு மிடரைமுடு கியெநூற துட்ட கொலைகார ரென்ற","அசுரர் படையையடை யவும்வே ரறுத்த அபிராம செந்தி","..... லுரகவெற் புடையோனே","அருண கிரணகரு ணையபூர ணச்ச ரணமே லெழுந்த","இரண கரணமுர ணுறுசூர னுட்க மயிலேறு கந்த","அருணை யிறையவர்பெ ரியகோபு ரத்தில் வடபா லமர்ந்த","..... ஆறுமுகப் பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes at length the act of love with women who sell themselves, two bodies becoming one form, hair loosened, bangles jingling, lips drunk, until mind melts into mind, and names them women of magic arts who cling only as far as the money goes; he asks for grace to be let go from them. The praise tells how Murugan circled the whole sea-girt earth in an instant and came to sit at Siva's side, names Palani, Swamimalai (ஏரகம்), Thiruvavinankudi, Brahmapuram and Thiruttani, calls him the one who became kin to the poet, a dog, and made poems, and ends at the north side of Arunai's great tower.","n":"பொருளது அளவும் மருவுறு மாய வித்தை விலைமாதர்: the embrace lasts as far as the money does, which is why the long love scene ends in a plea for release. படியை முழுதும் ஒரு நொடியே மதித்து வலமாக வந்து சிவனிடத்து அமர் சேயே: circling the earth in a moment and sitting by Siva has the details of the contest for the fruit, though no fruit is named. நாயெனுக்கும் உறவாகி நின்று கவிதையைப் புனைவோனே may refer to the poet's own call to sing; the split of these words is unsure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","punarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:34"},"p:399":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":399,"t":["இரவுபகற் பலகாலும் இயலிசைமுத் ...... தமிழ்கூறித்","திரமதனைத் தெளிவாகத் திருவருளைத் ...... தருவாயே","பரகருணைப் பெருவாழ்வே பரசிவதத் ...... துவஞானா","அரனருள்சற் புதல்வோனே அருணகிரிப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet, who speaks the threefold Tamil of verse and song night and day, asks Murugan to give him clearly a settled firmness and his holy grace. The praise names him the great life of supreme compassion, the knower of the truth of Parasiva, and the good son born of Hara's grace, at Arunagiri.","n":"திரம் அதனைத் தெளிவாக: what is asked is steadiness, not release or vision; the poet already sings, and wants the singing to stand firm.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","thamizh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:34"},"p:400":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":400,"t":["இருவர் மயலோ அமளி விதமோ","எனென செயலோ ...... அணுகாத","இருடி அயன்மா லமர ரடியா","ரிசையு மொலிதா ...... னிவைகேளா","தொருவ னடியே னலறு மொழிதா","னொருவர் பரிவாய் ...... மொழிவாரோ","உனது பததூள் புவன கிரிதா","னுனது கிருபா ...... கரமேதோ","பரம குருவா யணுவி லசைவாய்","பவன முதலா ...... கியபூதப்","படையு முடையாய் சகல வடிவாய்","பழைய வடிவா ...... கியவேலா","அரியு மயனோ டபய மெனவே","அயிலை யிருள்மேல் ...... விடுவோனே","அடிமை கொடுநோய் பொடிகள் படவே","அருண கிரிவாழ் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet seems to say that amid the praises of sages, Brahma, Vishnu, the gods and the devotees, his lone servant's cry goes unheard, and asks whether anyone will speak kindly for him; he wonders what the dust of Murugan's feet and his store of grace are. The praise names him the supreme guru who moves in the atom, who has the elements from wind onward for his army, who is all forms and the ancient form, who threw the vel on darkness when Vishnu and Brahma cried for refuge, and the one at Arunagiri who powders his servant's cruel disease.","n":"அணுவில் அசைவாய், சகல வடிவாய், பழைய வடிவாகிய: Murugan as the mover within the atom and as every form is the doctrine of pervasion, unusual in so short a song. The opening (இருவர் மயலோ அமளி விதமோ) may instead describe love's confusion; how its words split is unsure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["viyaabagam","guru","noy"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:34"},"p:401":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":401,"t":["இருவினை யஞ்ச மலவகை மங்க","இருள்பிணி மங்க ...... மயிலேறி","இனவரு ளன்பு மொழியக டம்பு","வினதக முங்கொ ...... டளிபாடக்","கரிமுக னெம்பி முருகனெ னண்டர்","களிமலர் சிந்த ...... அடியேன்முன்","கருணைபொ ழிந்து முகமும லர்ந்து","கடுகிந டங்கொ ...... டருள்வாயே","திரிபுர மங்க மதனுடல் மங்க","திகழ்நகை கொண்ட ...... விடையேறிச்","சிவம்வெளி யங்க ணருள்குடி கொண்டு","திகழந டஞ்செய் ...... தெமையீண","அரசியி டங்கொள் மழுவுடை யெந்தை","அமலன்ம கிழ்ந்த ...... குருநாதா","அருணைவி லங்கல் மகிழ்குர மங்கை","அமளிந லங்கொள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks Murugan to come quickly before him on the peacock, face blossoming and pouring grace, dancing, while the two deeds take fright, the kinds of impurity and the dark disease fade, bees hum at the kadamba and the gods shower flowers calling him the elephant-faced one's younger brother. The praise names Siva, who burned the three cities and Kama's body with a smile, rides the bull and dances in the open space of Sivam with the queen at his side, as the one whom Murugan delighted as guru, and ends with the bed of the hunter girl at Arunai.","n":"The plea is for the sight of Murugan arriving, and the release from deeds and malas is the effect of that sight, not something earned first. சிவம் வெளி அங்கண் ... நடம் செய்து: Siva's dance is placed in the space that is Sivam.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","malam","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:34"},"p:402":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":402,"t":["இருவினை யூண்ப சும்பை கருவிளை கூன்கு டம்பை","யிடரடை பாழ்ம்பொ தும்ப ...... கிதவாரி","இடைதிரி சோங்கு கந்த மதுவது தேங்கு கும்ப","மிரவிடை தூங்கு கின்ற ...... பிணநோவுக்","குருவியல் பாண்ட மஞ்சு மருவிய கூண்டு நெஞ்சொ","டுயிர்குடி போங்கு ரம்பை ...... யழியாதென்","றுலகுட னேன்று கொண்ட கருமபி ராந்தொ ழிந்து","னுபயப தாம்பு யங்க ...... ளடைவேனோ","அருணையி லோங்கு துங்க சிகரக ராம்பு யங்க","ளமரர் குழாங்கு விந்து ...... தொழவாழும்","அடியவர் பாங்க பண்டு புகலகி லாண்ட முண்ட","அபிநவ சார்ங்க கண்டன் ...... மருகோனே","கருணைம்ரு கேந்த்ர அன்ப ருடனுர கேந்த்ரர் கண்ட","கடவுள்ந டேந்த்ரர் மைந்த ...... வரைசாடுங்","கலபக கேந்த்ர தந்த்ர அரசநி சேந்த்ர கந்த","கரகுலி சேந்த்ரர் தங்கள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet names the body a fresh bag fed by the two deeds, a bent nest grown in the womb, a ruined hollow of trouble, a pot where stench and liquor pool, a corpse that sleeps at night, a vessel shaped for pain and a hut that heart and life move out of; and asks whether, giving up the delusion he shared with the world that it will not perish, he will reach Murugan's two lotus feet. The praise names Arunai, where the gods crowd to worship, Vishnu of the Sarnga bow who swallowed the worlds as uncle, Murugan as son of the lord of dance whom the serpent king and another devotee beheld, and the lord of Indra with the vajra.","n":"The confession is a chain of names for the body, each a container (பை, குடம்பை, பொதும்பு, கும்பம், பாண்டம், கூண்டு, குரம்பை), so the point is that what the world takes as lasting is a vessel to be left. உரகேந்த்ரர் கண்ட கடவுள் நடேந்த்ரர் probably means Nataraja as seen by Patanjali, the serpent king; the other devotee (ம்ருகேந்த்ர அன்பர்) may be Vyaghrapada, but the epithets are hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","nilaiyaamai","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:34"},"p:403":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":403,"t":["இருளளக மவிழமதி போத முத்த ரும்ப","இலகுகயல் புரளஇரு பார பொற்ற னங்கள்","இளகஇடை துவளவளை பூச லிட்டி ரங்க ...... எவராலும்","எழுதரிய கலைநெகிழ ஆசை மெத்த வுந்தி","யினியசுழி மடுவினிடை மூழ்கி நட்பொ டந்த","இதழமுது பருகியுயிர் தேக மொத்தி ருந்து ...... முனிவாறி","மருகுகமழ் மலரமளி மீதி னிற்பு குந்து","முகவனச மலர்குவிய மோக முற்ற ழிந்து","மொழிபதற வசமழிய ஆசை யிற்க விழ்ந்து ...... விடுபோதும்","முழுதுணர வுடையமுது மாத வத்து யர்ந்த","பழுதில்மறை பயிலுவஎ னாத ரித்து நின்று","முநிவர்சுரர் தொழுதுருகு பாத பத்ம மென்று ...... மறவேனே","ஒருசிறுவன் மணமதுசெய் போதி லெய்த்து வந்து","கிழவடிவு கொடுமுடுகி வாச லிற்பு குந்து","உலகறிய இவனடிமை யாமெ னக்கொ ணர்ந்து ...... சபையூடே","ஒருபழைய சருகுமடி ஆவ ணத்தை யன்று","உரமொடவ னதுவலிய வேகி ழிக்க நின்று","உதறிமுறை யிடுபழைய வேத வித்தர் தந்த ...... சிறியோனே","அரியவுடு பதிகடவி யாட கச்சி லம்பொ","டழகுவட மணிமுடிவி யாள மிட்ட ழுந்த","அமரரொடு பலர்முடுகி ஆழி யைக்க டைந்து ...... அமுதாக","அருளுமரி திருமருக வார ணத்தை யன்று","அறிவினுட னொருகொடியி லேத ரித்து கந்த","அருணகிரி நகரிலெழு கோபு ரத்த மர்ந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes the act of love, hair loosened, sweat like pearls on the face, eyes rolling, garments slipping, lips drunk, until speech fails and the self is lost in desire, and says that even then he will never forget the lotus feet that sages and gods worship, melting. The praise names Murugan the child given by the old knower of the Veda who came in an old man's form to a young man's wedding, pushed in at the gate, claimed him before the assembly as his slave, and stood shaking and protesting when the youth tore the old palm-leaf deed; it also names Vishnu who churned the sea for nectar, the cock on the banner, and the tower of Arunagiri.","n":"The plea is not to escape the embrace but to keep the feet in mind within it (விடுபோதும் ... பாதபத்மம் என்றும் மறவேனே), a different stance from the songs that ask for release from women. The story in the praise has the unmistakable details of Siva claiming Sundarar as his bondsman at his wedding, with the slave deed (ஆவணம்) torn before the assembly; that the god of the story is the giver of Murugan makes Siva himself the claimant of a servant.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","thiruvadi","thondu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:34"},"p:404":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":404,"t":["இறுகு மணிமுலை மருவு தரளமு","மெரியு முமிழ்மதி ...... நிலவாலே","இரவி யெனதுயிர் கவர வருகுழ","லிசையி லுறுகட ...... லலையாலே","தறுகண் ரதிபதி மதனன் விடுகொடு","சரமி லெளியெனு ...... மழியாதே","தருண மணிபொழி லருணை நகருறை","சயில மிசையினில் ...... வரவேணும்","முறுகு திரிபுர மறுகு கனலெழ","முறுவ லுடையவர் ...... குருநாதா","முடிய கொடுமுடி யசுரர் பொடிபட","முடுகு மரகத ...... மயில்வீரா","குறவர் மடமக ளமுத கனதன","குவடு படுமொரு ...... திருமார்பா","கொடிய சுடரிலை தனையு மெழுகடல்","குறுக விடவல ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"In a girl's own voice: the pearls on her breasts and the moon that spits fire, the flute's music that comes like the sun to take her life, the sea's waves and Kama's cruel arrows torment her, and she asks Murugan to come in time to the hill at Arunai with its gem-strewn groves so that she does not perish. The praise names him the guru of Siva who burned the three cities with a smile, the hero on the emerald peacock who powdered the asuras, the chest marked by the hunter girl's breasts, and the one who sent the vel to the seven seas.","n":"Akam love poetry in the first person (எனது உயிர், எளியெனும்), not the poet's confession. The torments are the classical ones of evening: moon, flute and sea.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","maalai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:34"},"p:405":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":405,"t":["உலையிலன லொத்தா வுடலினனல் பற்றி","யுடுபதியை முட்டி ...... யமுதூற","லுருகிவர விட்ட பரமசுக முற்று","வுனதடியை நத்தி ...... நினையாமற்","சிலைநுதலி லிட்ட திலதமவிர் பொட்டு","திகழ்முகவர் முத்து ...... நகையாலே","சிலுகுவலை யிட்ட மயல்கவலை பட்டுத்","திருடனென வெட்கி ...... யலைவேனோ","கலைகனக வட்ட திமிலைபறை கொட்ட","கனகமயில் விட்ட ...... கதிர்வேலா","கருதலரின் முட்டி கருகிவரு துட்ட","கதவமண ருற்ற ...... குலகாலா","அலைகடலு டுத்த தலமதனில் வெற்றி","அருணைவளர் வெற்பி ...... லுறைவோனே","அசுரர்களை வெட்டி யமரர்சிறை விட்டு","அரசுநிலை யிட்ட ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether, instead of raising the body's fire like a forge to strike the moon so that nectar melts and flows and he reaches the supreme bliss longing for Murugan's feet, he will wander ashamed like a thief, caught in the net of infatuation with women of bow brows and pearl smiles. The praise names the vel let fly from the golden peacock to drums, the one who was death to the race of the wicked Jains, the one dwelling on the hill of Arunai in the sea-girt land, and the one who cut down the asuras, freed the gods and set up their rule.","n":"உலையில் அனல் ஒத்து ... உடுபதியை முட்டி அமுது ஊறல் உருகி வர: the inner fire raised to the moon so that its nectar flows is the yoga of the Tirumanthiram, set here against the women. கதவு அமணர் உற்ற குலகாலா again makes Murugan the Sambandar who destroyed the Jains.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yogam","amudham","mayal"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:34"},"p:406":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":406,"t":["கடல்பரவு தரங்க மீதெழு ...... திங்களாலே","கருதிமிக மலந்தை மார்சொல்வ ...... தந்தியாலே","வடவனலை முனிந்து வீசிய ...... தென்றலாலே","வயலருணையில் வஞ்சி போதந ...... லங்கலாமோ","இடமுமையை மணந்த நாதரி ...... றைஞ்சும்வீரா","எழுகிரிகள் பிளந்து வீழஎ ...... றிந்தவேலா","அடலசுரர் கலங்கி யோடமு ...... னிந்தகோவே","அரிபிரம புரந்த ராதியர் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"A voice, perhaps the girl's mother or friend, asks whether the vanji-creeper girl of field-rich Arunai should suffer from the moon rising over the sea's waves, the evening and the talk of the gossiping women, and the south wind that scorns even the undersea fire. The praise names Murugan the hero whom Siva, who married Uma at his left, worships, the vel that split the seven hills, the lord who routed the asuras, and the master of Vishnu, Brahma and Indra.","n":"The speaker is not named; வஞ்சி போதநலங்கலாமோ speaks of the girl in the third person. மலந்தைமார் sounds like the blaming women of akam poetry; the word is unusual.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","alar","maalai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:34"},"p:407":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":407,"t":["கமலமுகப் பிறைநுதல்பொற் சிலையெனவச் சிரகணைநற்","கயலெனபொற் சுழலும்விழிக் ...... குழல்கார்போல்","கதிர்தரளொப் பியதசனக் கமுகுகளப் புயகழைபொற்","கரகமலத் துகிர்விரலிற் ...... கிளிசேருங்","குமரிதனத் திதமலைக் கிசலியிணைக் கலசமெனக்","குவிமுலைசற் றசையமணிக் ...... கலனாடக்","கொடியிடைபட் டுடைநடைபொற் சரணமயிற் கெமனமெனக்","குனகிபொருட் பறிபவருக் ...... குறவாமோ","திமிலையுடுக் குடன்முரசுப் பறைதிமிதித் திமிதிமெனட்","டிமிடிமிடிட் டிகுர்திமிதித் ...... தொலிதாளம்","செககணசெக் கணகதறத் திடுதிடெனக் கொடுமுடியெட்","டிகைசிலைபட் டுவரிபடச் ...... சிலைகோடித்","துமிலவுடற் றசுரர்முடிப் பொடிபடரத் தமுள்பெருகத்","தொகுதசைதொட் டலகையுணத் ...... தொடும்வேலா","துவனிதினைப் புனமருவிக் குறமகளைக் களவுமயற்","சுகமொடணைத் தருணகிரிப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet paints a woman from lotus face, crescent brow and carp eyes to pearl teeth, areca neck, breasts like pots, creeper waist and peacock gait, and asks whether he should be kin to those who coax a man and snatch his wealth. The praise is a battle in drum syllables, the vel that feeds the demons on the asuras' flesh, and Murugan who went to the millet field and embraced the hunter girl in stealthy love, at Arunagiri.","n":"குனகி பொருள் பறிபவர்: the whole catalogue of beauty ends in two words, coaxing and robbing, so the portrait is a lure. The praise closes on Murugan's own களவு with Valli, stealth in a good sense against the women's theft.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:34"},"p:408":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":408,"t":["கமல மொட்டைக் கட்ட ழித்துக் குமிழியை","நிலகு லைத்துப் பொற்கு டத்தைத் தமனிய","கலச வர்க்கத் தைத்த கர்த்துக் குலையற ...... இளநீரைக்","கறுவி வட்டைப் பிற்று ரத்திப் பொருதப","சயம்வி ளைத்துச் செப்ப டித்துக் குலவிய","கரிம ருப்பைப் புக்கொ டித்துத் திறல்மத ...... னபிஷேகம்","அமலர் நெற்றிக் கட்ட ழற்குட் பொடிசெய்து","அதிக சக்ரப் புட்ப றக்கக் கொடுமையி","னடல்ப டைத்தச் சப்ப டுத்திச் சபதமொ ...... டிருதாளம்","அறைதல் கற்பித் துப்பொ ருப்பைப் பரவிய","சிறக றுப்பித் துக்க திர்த்துப் புடைபடு","மபிந வச்சித் ரத்த னத்துத் திருடிக ...... ளுறவாமோ","தமர மிக்குத் திக்க திர்க்கப் பலபறை","தொகுதொ குக்குத் தொத்தொ குக்குத் தொகுதொகு","தரிகி டத்தத் தத்த ரிக்கத் தரிகிட ...... எனவோதிச்","சவடு றப்பக் கப்ப ழொத்திப் புகையெழ","விழிக ளுட்செக் கச்சி வத்துக் குறளிகள்","தசைகள் பட்சித் துக்க ளித்துக் கழுதொடு ...... கழுகாட","அமலை யுற்றுக் கொக்க ரித்துப் படுகள","அசுர ரத்தத் திற்கு ளித்துத் திமியென","அடிந டித்திட் டிட்டி டித்துப் பொருதிடு ...... மயிலோனே","அழகு மிக்கச் சித்ர பச்சைப் புரவியி","னுலவு மெய்ப்ரத் யக்ஷ நற்சற் குருபர","அருணை யிற்சித் தித்தெ னக்குத் தெளிவருள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet heaps conceits on women's breasts, which defeat the lotus bud, the bubble, the golden pot, the tender coconut, the elephant's tusk and the chakravaka bird, and asks whether he should be kin to these thieving women with painted breasts. The praise is a battle in drum syllables with ghouls feasting and the peacock dancing in asura blood, and names Murugan the true guru made manifest on the green steed, who appeared at Arunai and gave the poet clarity.","n":"திருடிகள், thieves, carries the charge. In the praise, மெய்ப்ரத்யக்ஷ நற்சற்குருபர ... அருணையில் சித்தித்து எனக்குத் தெளிவருள் makes Arunai the place where the guru appeared to the poet himself. The comparisons in the first half are dense and some, such as the burning of Kama's crown, are hard to place.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","vanjam","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:34"},"p:409":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":409,"t":["கரிமுகக் கடகளிற் றதிககற் பகமதக்","கஜமுகத் தவுணனைக் ...... கடியானை","கடலையெட் பயறுநற் கதலியிற் கனிபலக்","கனிவயிற் றினிலடக் ...... கியவேழம்","அரிமுகத் தினனெதிர்த் திடுகளத் தினின்மிகுத்","தமர்புரிக் கணபதிக் ...... கிளையோனே","அயிலெடுத் தசுரர்வெற் பலைவுறப் பொருதுவெற்","றியைமிகுத் தறுமுகக் ...... குமரேசா","நரிமிகுக் கிளைகளைப் பரியெனக் கடிவளக்","கையில்பிடித் தெதிர்நடத் ...... திடுமீசன்","நடனமிப் படியிடத் தினுமிசைத் தரையினிற்","கரியுரித் தணிபவற் ...... கொருசேயே","துரிபெறச் சரிபொழிற் கனவயற் கழகுளத்","துரியமெய்த் தரளமொய்த் ...... திடவீறிச்","சுரர்துதித் திடமிகுத் தியல்தழைத் தருணையிற்","சுடரயிற் சரவணப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A song of praise without a plea: it names Ganapati, the elephant-faced one who destroyed the elephant-faced asura and holds gram, sesame, lentils, plantains and many fruits in his belly, and Murugan his younger brother who fought the asuras and their hill with the vel. It names Siva who held the reins and led jackals as horses, who dances everywhere and wears the flayed elephant's hide, and ends with Saravana of the bright vel at Arunai of rich fields and pools.","n":"நரிமிகுக் கிளைகளைப் பரியெனக் கடிவளக் கையில் பிடித்து எதிர் நடத்திடும் ஈசன்: the jackals turned into horses are the story of Siva acting for Manikkavasagar, a devotee's story in the praise of Murugan's father. The list of foods follows Ganapati's offerings in the opening songs.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adiyaar","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:34"},"p:410":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":410,"t":["கருநி றஞ்சிறந் தகல்வன புகல்வன","மதன தந்திரங் கடியன கொடியன","கனக குண்டலம் பொருவன வருவன ...... பரிதாவும்","கடலு டன்படர்ந் தடர்வன தொடர்வன","விளையு நஞ்சளைந் தொளிர்வன பிளிர்வன","கணையை நின்றுநின் றெதிர்வன முதிர்வன ...... இளையோர்முன்","செருவை முண்டகஞ் சிறுவன வுறுவன","களவு வஞ்சகஞ் சுழல்வன வுழல்வன","தெனன தெந்தனந் தெனதென தெனதென ...... எனநாதம்","தெரிசு ரும்பைவென் றிடுவன அடுவன","மருள்செய் கண்கள்கொண் டணைவர்த முயிரது","திருகு கின்றமங் கையர்வச மழிதலை ...... யொழிவேனோ","மருவு தண்டைகிண் கிணிபரி புரமிவை","கலக லன்கலின் கலினென இருசரண்","மலர்கள் நொந்துநொந் தடியிட வடிவமு ...... மிகவேறாய்","வலிய சிங்கமுங் கரடியு முழுவையு","முறைசெ ழும்புனந் தினைவிளை யிதண்மிசை","மறவர் தங்கள்பெண் கொடிதனை யொருதிரு ...... வுளநாடி","அருகு சென்றடைந் தவள்சிறு பதயுக","சதத ளம்பணிந் ததிவித கலவியு","ளறம ருண்டுநெஞ் சவளுடன் மகிழ்வுட ...... னணைவோனே","அமரர் சங்கமுங் குடிபுக நொடியினில்","நிருதர் சங்கமும் பொடிபட அமர்செய்து","அருணை வந்துதென் திசைதனி லுறைதரு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet piles up what women's eyes do: they widen and speak, work Kama's tricks, rival golden earrings, spread like the sea, glow with poison, meet arrows, wage war on the young, wander in stealth and deceit and defeat the humming bee, and he asks whether he will escape being ruined under women who embrace with bewildering eyes and wring out a man's life. The praise shows Murugan walking, his anklets ringing and his flower feet aching, in a changed form among lions, bears and tigers to the millet field, bowing at the hunter girl's small feet and embracing her, then destroying the asuras and dwelling at Arunai on the south side.","n":"Each pair of verbs ends -வன (அகல்வன புகல்வன, கடியன கொடியன), a list of what the eyes do that sounds like a list of beasts. The praise reverses it: the god himself goes in disguise and bows at a woman's feet (சிறு பதயுக சததளம் பணிந்து).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","mayal","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:34"},"p:411":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":411,"t":["காணாத தூர நீணாத வாரி","காதார வாரம ...... தன்பினாலே","காலாளும் வேளும் ஆலால நாதர்","காலால் நிலாவுமு ...... னிந்துபூமேல்","நாணான தோகை நூலாடை சோர","நாடோ ர்க ளேசஅ ...... ழிந்துதானே","நானாப வாத மேலாக ஆக","நாடோ றும் வாடிம ...... யங்கலாமோ","சோணாச லேச பூணார நீடு","தோளாறு மாறும்வி ...... ளங்குநாதா","தோலாத வீர வேலால டாத","சூராளன் மாளவெ ...... குண்டகோவே","சேணாடர் லோகம் வாழ்மாதி யானை","தீராத காதல்சி ...... றந்தமார்பா","தேவாதி கூடு மூவாதி மூவர்","தேவாதி தேவர்கள் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"A voice laments that a girl, tormented by the distant sea's roar in her ears, by Kama and by the moon, her garment slipping on her bed of flowers, reviled by the townsfolk and the subject of many slanders, should wilt and faint day after day. The praise names Murugan the lord of Sonachala with twelve shining shoulders, whose unfailing vel destroyed Sura, the chest dear to the daughter of the heavenly world, and the master of the gods.","n":"நாடோர்கள் ஏச ... நானா பவாதம்: gossip and slander are part of the girl's suffering, as in akam poetry. The second line (காலாளும் வேளும் ஆலால நாதர் காலால் நிலாவும் முனிந்து) is hard to divide, and who is speaking is not said.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","alar"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:34"},"p:412":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":412,"t":["காரா டக்குழ லாலா லக்கணை","கண்கள் சுழன்றிட வேமு கங்களி","னாலா பச்சிலை யாலே மெற்புசி","மஞ்சள் கலந்தணி வாளி கொந்தள","காதா டக்கலன் மேலா டக்குடி","யின்ப ரசங்குட மார்ப ளிங்கொளி ...... கொங்கைமாதர்","காசா சைச்செய லாலே சொக்கிடு","விஞ்சையர் கொஞ்சிடு வாரி ளங்குயில்","போலே நற்றெரு வூடா டித்துயல்","தொங்கல் நெகிழ்ந்திடை யேது வண்டிட","கால்தா விச்சதி யோடே சித்திர","மென்ப நடம்புரி வாரு டன்செயல் ...... மிஞ்சலாகிச்","சீரா டிச்சில நாள்போய் மெய்த்திரை","வந்து கலந்துயி ரோட வங்கமொ","டூடா டிப்பல நோயோ டுத்தடி","கொண்டு குரங்கென வேந டந்துசொல்","சீயோ டிக்கிடை பாயோ டுக்கிய","டங்கி யழிந்துயி ரோடு ளைஞ்சொளி ...... யுங்கண்மாறிச்","சேரா மற்பொறி கேளா மற்செவி","துன்பமொ டின்பமு மேம றந்துபின்","ஊரார் சுற்றமு மாதோர் மக்களு","மண்டியு மண்டையு டேகு விந்திது","சீசீ சிச்சிசி போகா நற்சனி","யன்கட வென்றிட வேகி டந்துடல் ...... மங்குவேனோ","மாரோன் முப்புர நிறா யுற்றிட","அங்கி யுமிழ்ந்திடு வோரி பம்புலி","தோல்சீ யத்தொடெ யேகா சர்ச்சடை","கங்கை யிளம்பிறை யார ணிந்தவர்","மாடே றிக்கட லாலா லத்தையு","முண்டவ ரெந்தைசி வாநு பங்குறை ...... யென்றன்மாதா","மாலோ னுக்கிளை யாள்மா பத்தினி","யம்பிகை சங்கரி மோக சுந்தரி","வேதா மக்கலை ரூபாள் முக்கணி","ரம்பிய கொங்கையி னாள்ப யந்தருள்","மாஞா னக்கும ராதோ கைப்பரி","யின்பத வண்குரு வேயெ னஞ்சுரர் ...... தொண்டுபாடச்","சூரார் மக்கிட மாமே ருக்கிட","அங்கட லெண்கிரி யோடி பங்கொடு","தீபே ழற்றிட பாதா ளத்துறை","நஞ்சர வின்பண மாயி ரங்கெட","சூழ்வா ளக்கிரி தூளா கிப்பொடி","விண்கணி றைந்திட வேந டம்புரி ...... கின்றவேலா","சோர்வே தத்தலை மேலா டிச்சுக","பங்கய செங்கர மோட கம்பெற","வாகா னக்குற மாதோ டற்புத","மங்குல ணங்குட னேம கிழ்ந்துநல்","தூணோ டிச்சுட ராகா சத்தைய","ணைந்துவி ளங்கரு ணாச லந்திகழ் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes courtesans who dance in the street and charm for love of coin, wasting some days with them until wrinkles come, then the body's ruin: disease, a staff, walking like a monkey, phlegm, lying on a mat, eyes changed, ears deaf, pain and pleasure forgotten, while townsfolk, kin, wife and children crowd round saying 'chi, this curse won't go'; he asks whether he will fade like that. The praise names Siva who burned Kama and the three cities and drank the poison, and Uma, Vishnu's sister and the poet's mother, who bore the great Kumara of wisdom; the vel dances as Sura, Meru, the seas, the mountains and the serpent's hoods are shaken; Murugan dances on the head of the Veda, and Arunachalam is the pillar of light that ran up and touched the sky.","n":"காசாசைச் செயலாலே: the women's charm is for coin. The kin's verdict (சீசீ சிச்சிசி போகா நற்சனியன்) is the bitter end of the courtesan's life, and the song gives it in their own words. தூணோடிச் சுடர் ஆகாசத்தை அணைந்து: the hill as the column of fire that rose to the sky recalls the Annamalai story of Siva's endless form.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","yaakkai","nilaiyaamai","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:34"},"p:413":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":413,"t":["காருமரு வும்பெருகு சோலைமரு வுங்கொடிய","காகளம டங்கவுமு ...... ழங்கு மதனாலே","காலடர வம்பமளி மேலடர வந்துபொரு","காமன்விடு விஞ்சுகணை ...... அஞ்சு மலராலே","ஊருமுல கும்பழைய பேருகம்வி ளைந்ததென","ஓரிரவு வந்தெனது ...... சிந்தை யழியாதே","ஊடியிரு கொங்கைமிசை கூடிவரி வண்டினமு","லாவியக டம்பமலர் ...... தந்த ருளுவாயே","ஆருமர வும்பிறையு நீருமணி யுஞ்சடைய","ராதிபர வும்படிநி ...... னைந்த குருநாதா","ஆறுமுக முங்குரவு மேறுமயி லுங்குறவி","யாளுமுர முந்திருவும் ...... அன்பு முடையோனே","மேருமலை யும்பெரிய சூருமலை யுங்கரிய","வேலையலை யும்பகையும் ...... அஞ்ச விடும்வேலா","மேதினியி றைஞ்சுமரு ணாபுரிவி ளங்குதிரு","வீதியிலெ ழுந்தருளி ...... நின்ற பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"In a girl's voice: in the groves the cruel horn of Kama sounds, the wind presses, his five flower arrows strike her on her bed, and one night seems as long as an age; she asks Murugan, lest her mind be ruined, to come and give her his kadamba garland where the bees roam. The praise names him the guru whom Siva with serpent, crescent, river and gems in his hair thought on, the one with six faces, kura flowers, the peacock, the hunter girl and Lakshmi on his chest, whose vel made Meru, Sura and the sea afraid, standing in the holy street of Arunapuri.","n":"ஊரும் உலகும் பழைய பேருகம் விளைந்ததென ஓரிரவு: a single night of separation feels like an aeon, the lover's sense of time. The plea for the god's garland is the conventional request of the love-sick girl.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","pirivu","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:35"},"p:414":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":414,"t":["கீத விநோத மெச்சு ...... குரலாலே","கீறு மையார் முடித்த ...... குழலாலே","நீதி யிலாத ழித்து ...... முழலாதே","நீமயி லேறி யுற்று ...... வரவேணும்","சூதமர் சூர ருட்க ...... பொருசூரா","சோண கி஡ணயி லுற்ற ...... குமரேசா","ஆதியர் காதொ ருச்சொ ...... லருள்வோனே","ஆனை முகார்க னிட்ட ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks Murugan to come on the peacock so that he does not wander without virtue, ruined by women's voices skilled in song and their dark tied hair. The praise names him the warrior whom the deceitful asuras feared, Kumaresa at Sonagiri, the one who spoke a single word into the ear of the First One, and the younger brother of the elephant-faced god.","n":"ஆதியர் காது ஒருச்சொல் அருள்வோனே: the teaching whispered into Siva's ear, here 'one word' without naming Om.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kaamam","guru","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:35"},"p:415":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":415,"t":["குரவநறு மளககுழல் கோதிக் காட்டியெ","குலவுமிரு கயல்கள்விழி மோதித் தாக்கியெ","குமுதமல ரொளிபவள வாயைக் காட்டியெ ...... குழையாத","குணமுறுக இனிதுபயில் கூறிக் காட்டியெ","குலையஇரு கலைநெகிழ வீசிக் காட்டியெ","குடவியிடு மரிவையர்க ளாசைப் பாட்டிலெ ...... கொடியேன்யான்","பொருளிளமை கலைமனமு மேகப் போக்கிய","புலையனிவ னெனவுலக மேசப் போக்கென","பொறிவழியி லறிவழிய பூதச் சேட்டைகள் ...... பெருகாதே","புதுமலர்கள் மருவுமிரு பாதத் தாற்றியெ","பொதுவகையி லருணைநிலை நீள்கர்த்தாவென","புகழடிமை தனையுனது பார்வைக் காத்திட ...... நினையாதோ","அரவமுட னறுகுமதி யார்மத் தாக்கமு","மணியுமொரு சடைமவுலி நாதர்க் கேற்கவெ","அறிவரிய வொருபொருளை போதத் தேற்றிய ...... அறிவோனே","அழகுசெறி குழலியர்கள் வானத் தாட்டியர்","தருமமது சரவணையில் வாவித் தேக்கியெ","அறுசிறுவ ரொருவுடல மாகித் தோற்றிய ...... இளையோனே","சுரருலவ அசுரர்கள் மாளத் தூட்பட","துயவுமுட லயிலைவிடு மாவுக் ராக்ரம","சுவறியெழு கடலுமுறை யாகக் கூப்பிட ...... முனிவோனே","துடிமுழவு மறவரிட சேவற் காட்டினில்","துணைமலரி னணுகிதினை காவற் காத்தனை","சுரியகுழல் குறமகளை வேளைக் காத்தணை ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet tells how women show off combed hair, darting eyes and coral mouths, speak sweetly and loosen their garments, and how he, cruel one, spent his wealth, youth, learning and mind on them until the world mocked him as an outcast and his understanding was ruined by the senses; he asks whether Murugan, master of the common stance at Arunai, will not think to keep his servant under his gaze. The praise names him the one who taught Siva the one hard-to-know thing, the young one born as six children in the Saravana pool where the heavenly women nursed him, the vel that made the seas cry out, and the one who guarded the millet and embraced the hunter girl.","n":"பொருள் இளமை கலை மனமும் ஏகப் போக்கிய புலையன் இவன்: the loss is itemised, wealth, youth, learning and mind, and the world's verdict 'outcast' is quoted. அறுசிறுவர் ஒருவுடலமாகித் தோற்றிய: the six infants of the Saravana pool who became one body.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kutram","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:35"},"p:416":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":416,"t":["குழவியு மாய்மோக மோகித குமரனு மாய்வீடு காதலி","குலவனு மாய்நாடு காடொடு ...... தடுமாறிக்","குனிகொடு கூனீடு மாகிடு கிழவனு மாயாவி போய்விட","விறகுட னேதூளி யாவது ...... மறியாதாய்ப்","பழயச டாதார மெனிகழ் கழியுடல் காணாநி ராதர","பரிவிலி வானாலை நாடொறு ...... மடைமாறிப்","பலபல வாம்யோக சாதக வுடல்கொடு மாயாத போதக","பதியழி யாவீடு போயினி ...... யடைவேனோ","எழுகடல் தநமுள மேருவு மிடிபட வேதாவும் வேதமு","மிரவியும் வாய்பாறி யோடிட ...... முதுசேடன்","இருளறு பாதாள லோகமு மிமையமு நீறாக வாள்கிரி","யிருபிள வாய்வீழ மாதிர ...... மலைசாய","அழகிய மாபாக சாதன னமரரு மூர்பூத மாறுசெய்","அவுணர்த மாசேனை தூளெழ ...... விளையாடி","அமரினை மேவாத சூரரை அமர்செயும் வேலாயு தாவுயர்","அருணையில் வாழ்வாக மேவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet runs through a life, infant, youth in passion, householder loving his home, wanderer, bent old man, the life gone and the body dust with the firewood, and says he never knew this; he asks whether, beyond the six supports of the body and with a body trained in many yogas, he will now reach the undying home of the Lord of deathless knowledge. The praise is Murugan's battle: the seven seas, Meru, Brahma and the Veda and the sun put to flight, the old serpent and the underworld shaken, the mountain split in two, and Indra and the gods given back their city, at Arunai.","n":"The first lines are a life in one breath, as in 9 and 110. பழய சடாதாரம் ... நிராதர: the six adharas of the yogic body and the supportless state beyond them; the plea moves from impermanence to yoga, but the words of the third and fourth lines are hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","aadhaaram","yogam","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:35"},"p:417":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":417,"t":["கேதகைய பூமுடித்த மாதர்தம யாலிலுற்று","கேவலம தானஅற்ப ...... நினைவாலே","கேள்வியதி லாதிருக்கு மூழ்வினையி னால்மிகுத்த","கேடுறுக வேநினைக்கும் ...... வினையாலே","வேதனையி லேமிகுத்த பாதகனு மாயவத்தில்","மேதினியெ லாமுழற்று ...... மடியேனை","வீடுதவி யாளவெற்றி வேல்கரம தேயெடுத்து","வீறுமயில் மீதிலுற்று ...... வருவாயே","நீதிநெறி யேயழித்த தாருகனை வேரறுத்து","நீடுபுகழ் தேவரிற்கள் ...... குடியேற","நீடருளி னால்விடுத்த பாலகும ராசெழித்த","நீலநிற மால்தனக்கு ...... மருகோனே","சோதியன லாவுதித்த சோணகிரி மாமலைக்குள்","சோபைவட கோபுரத்தி ...... லுறைவோனே","சோனைமழை போலெதிர்த்த தானவர்கள் மாளவெற்றி","தோளின்மிசை வாளெடுத்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses that, sunk in infatuation with women wearing ketaki flowers, with petty thoughts, no learning by listening, and deeds of fate bent on his ruin, he has become a great sinner wandering the earth in vain; he asks Murugan to come on the peacock with the vel to rule him and grant release. The praise names him the child who uprooted Taraka, destroyer of justice, so the gods returned to their homes, Vishnu's nephew, the one who dwells in the northern tower of Sonagiri, the hill that rose as fire and light, and the one who took up the sword against the asuras.","n":"கேள்வியதிலாது, ஊழ்வினையினால்: lack of learning and fate are named together as causes, but the plea does not excuse him; he still calls himself பாதகன். சோதி அனலா உதித்த சோணகிரி: the hill as the light that rose as fire.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","vinai","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:35"},"p:418":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":418,"t":["கோடான மடவார்கள் ...... முலைமீதே","கூர்வேலை யிணையான ...... விழியூடே","ஊடாடி யவரோடு ...... முழலாதே","ஊராக திகழ்பாத ...... மருள்வாயே","நீடாழி சுழல்தேசம் ...... வலமாக","நீடோ டி மயில்மீது ...... வருவோனே","சூடான தொருசோதி ...... மலைமேவு","சோணாடு புகழ்தேவர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks not to wander with women on whose breasts and spear-like eyes he dwells, and to be given the feet that shine as his home. The praise names Murugan the one who ran round the sea-girt land on the peacock and the lord praised by the Chola land on the hill of hot light.","n":"ஊராக திகழ் பாதம்: the feet as a town to live in. நீடாழி சுழல் தேசம் வலமாக நீடோடி: circling the world on the peacock, as in 398.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:35"},"p:419":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":419,"t":["கோடுசெறி மத்த கத்தை வீசுபலை தத்த வொத்தி","கூறுசெய்த ழித்து ரித்து ...... நடைமாணார்","கோளுலவு முப்பு ரத்தை வாளெரிகொ ளுத்தி விட்ட","கோபநுத லத்த ரத்தர் ...... குருநாதா","நீடுகன கத்த லத்தை யூடுருவி மற்ற வெற்பு","நீறெழமி தித்த நித்த ...... மனதாலே","நீபமலர் பத்தி மெத்த வோதுமவர் சித்த மெத்த","நீலமயில் தத்த விட்டு ...... வரவேணும்","ஆடலணி பொற்சி லைக்கை வேடுவர்பு னக்கு றத்தி","ஆரமது மெத்து சித்ர ...... முலைமீதே","ஆதரவு பற்றி மெத்த மாமணிநி றைத்த வெற்றி","ஆறிருதி ருப்பு யத்தில் ...... அணைவீரா","தேடிமையொர் புத்தி மெத்தி நீடுறநி னைத்த பத்தி","சீருறவு ளத்தெ ரித்த ...... சிவவேளே","தேறருணை யிற்ற ரித்த சேண்முகடி டத்த டர்த்த","தேவர்சிறை வெட்டி விட்ட ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The praise names Murugan the guru of Siva, who flayed the elephant and burned the three cities, and asks him, who pierced the golden mountain and trampled the other hill to ash, to come daily on the blue peacock to the minds of those who sing the kadamba with devotion. It names him the hero who embraces the hunter girl on his twelve shoulders, the Sivavel known in the minds of devotees, and the one who broke the gods' prison at Arunai.","n":"The plea is not for the poet himself but for all who sing with devotion (நீபமலர் பத்தி மெத்த ஓதுமவர் சித்தம்): Murugan is asked to come into their minds.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paththi","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:35"},"p:420":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":420,"t":["சிலைநுதல் வைத்துச் சிறந்த குங்கும","தலதமு மிட்டுக் குளிர்ந்த பங்கய","திருமுக வட்டத் தமர்ந்த மென்குமிழ் ...... தனிலேறிச்","செழுமணி ரத்நத் திலங்கு பைங்குழை","தனைமுனி வுற்றுச் சிவந்து நஞ்சணி","செயலினை யொத்துத் தயங்கு வஞ்சக ...... விழிசீறிப்","புலவிமி குத்திட் டிருந்த வஞ்சியர்","பதமல ருக்குட் பணிந்த ணிந்தணி","புரிவளை கைக்குட் கலின்க லென்றிட ...... அநுராகம்","புகழ்நல மெத்தப் புரிந்து கொங்கையி","லுருகிய ணைத்துப் பெரும்ப்ரி யங்கொடு","புணரினும் நிற்பொற் பதங்கள் நெஞ்சினுள் ...... மறவேனோ","கலைமதி வைத்துப் புனைந்து செஞ்சடை","மலைமகள் பக்கத் தமர்ந்தி ருந்திட","கணகண கட்கட் கணின்க ணென்றிட ...... நடமாடுங்","கருணைய னுற்றத் த்ரியம்ப கன்தரு","முருகபு னத்திற் றிரிந்த மென்கொடி","கனதன வெற்பிற் கலந்த ணைந்தருள் ...... புயவீரா","அலைகடல் புக்குப் பொரும்பெ ரும்படை","யவுணரை வெட்டிக் களைந்து வென்றுயர்","அமரர்தொ ழப்பொற் சதங்கை கொஞ்சிட ...... வருவோனே","அடியவ ரச்சத் தழுங்கி டுந்துயர்","தனையொழி வித்துப் ப்ரியங்கள் தந்திடும்","அருணகி ரிக்குட் சிறந்த மர்ந்தருள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes a sulking woman with kumkum on her bow brow and angry eyes, at whose flower feet he bows until her bangles jingle and love returns, and their union, and asks whether even in that union he would forget Murugan's golden feet in his heart. The praise names Siva, three-eyed and compassionate, who dances beside the mountain's daughter, as the giver of Murugan, the hero who embraced the hunter girl, the one who fought the asuras in the sea, and the lord at Arunagiri who ends the fears of devotees.","n":"புணரினும் நிற் பொற்பதங்கள் நெஞ்சினுள் மறவேனோ: the question means he will not forget; the feet are held in the very act the other songs flee. The lovers' quarrel (புலவி) and the bowing at her feet are classical akam, told as the poet's own.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","punarchi","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:35"},"p:421":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":421,"t":["சிவமா துடனே அநுபோ கமதாய்","சிவஞா னமுதே ...... பசியாறித்","திகழ்வோ டிருவோ ரொ ரு பமதாய்","திகைலோ கமெலா ...... மநுபோகி","இவனே யெனமா லயனோ டமரோ","ரிளையோ னெனவே ...... மறையோத","இறையோ னிடமாய் விளையா டுகவே","யியல்வே லுடன்மா ...... அருள்வாயே","தவலோ கமெலா முறையோ வெனவே","தழல்வேல் கொடுபோ ...... யசுராரைத்","தலைதூள் படஏழ் கடல்தூள் படமா","தவம்வாழ் வுறவே ...... விடுவோனே","கவர்பூ வடிவாள் குறமா துடன்மால்","கடனா மெனவே ...... அணைமார்பா","கடையேன் மிடிதூள் படநோய் விடவே","கனல்மால் வரைசேர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks that, enjoying union with Siva's consort and with his hunger stilled by the nectar of Siva-knowledge, the two becoming one form and all the worlds enjoyed, he may play at the Lord's side while Vishnu, Brahma and the gods say 'this is the one' and the Vedas chant, and that Murugan grant this with his vel and peacock. The praise names him the one who threw the fiery vel so that the asuras' heads and the seven seas were dust, the chest that embraced the hunter girl, and the lord of the great fire hill, asked to end the poet's poverty and disease.","n":"சிவஞான அமுதே பசியாறி, இருவோர் ஒரு ரூபமதாய்: hunger stilled by knowledge and two becoming one form describe union with Siva, an unusually high plea. The ending asks for the end of poverty (மிடி) as well. A letter is lost in line 3, and who the 'two' are is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","gnaanam","saayujjiyam","amudham"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:35"},"p:422":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":422,"t":["சினமுடுவல் நரிகழுகு டன்பருந் தின்கணங்","கொடிகெருடன் அலகைபுழு வுண்டுகண் டின்புறுஞ்","செடமளறு மலசலமொ டென்புதுன் றுங்கலந் ...... துன்பமேவு","செனனவலை மரணவலை ரண்டுமுன் பின்தொடர்ந்","தணுகுமுட லநெகவடி விங்கடைந் தம்பரஞ்","சிறுமணலை யளவிடினு மங்குயர்ந் திங்குலந் ...... தொன்றுநாயேன்","கனகபுவி நிழல்மருவி யன்புறுந் தொண்டர்பங்","குறுகஇனி யருள்கிருபை வந்துதந் தென்றுமுன்","கடனெனது உடலுயிரு முன்பரந் தொண்டுகொண் ...... டன்பரோடே","கலவிநல மருவிவடி வஞ்சிறந் துன்பதம்","புணர்கரண மயில்புறமொ டின்புகொண் டண்டருங்","கனகமலர் பொழியஉன தன்புகந் தின்றுமுன் ...... சிந்தியாதோ","தனனதன தனனதன தந்தனந் தந்தனந்","தகுகுகுகு குகுகுகுகு டங்குடங் குந்தடந்","தவில்முரசு பறைதிமிலை டிங்குடிங் குந்தடர்ந் ...... தண்டர்பேரி","தடுடுடுடு டுடுடுடுடு டுண்டுடுண் டுண்டுடுண்","டிமிடிமிட டகுர்திகுகு சங்குவெண் கொம்புதிண்","கடையுகமொ டொலியகட லஞ்சவஞ் சன்குலஞ் ...... சிந்திமாளச்","சினமுடுகி அயிலருளி யும்பரந் தம்பரந்","திகையுரகர் புவியுளது மந்தரம் பங்கயன்","செகமுழுது மகிழஅரி அம்புயன் தொண்டுகொண் ...... டஞ்சல்பாடத்","திருமுறுவ லருளியென தெந்தையின் பங்குறுங்","கவுரிமன முருகவொரு கங்கைகண் டன்புறுந்","திருவருண கிரிமருவு சங்கரன் கும்பிடுந் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet sees his body as what dogs, jackals, vultures, kites, demons and worms feed on gladly, a mire of filth and bones caught between the nets of birth and death, taking more forms than the sand of the sky could count; he asks that, calling his body and life Murugan's due, he be taken into service and joined to the devotees, and asks whether Murugan will not think of him today. The praise is a battle in drum syllables, the vel that sets the worlds rejoicing while Vishnu and Brahma serve and sing, and Murugan as the lord whom the Sankara of Arunagiri, with Gauri at his side and Ganga, worships.","n":"செனனவலை மரணவலை ரண்டும் முன்பின் தொடர்ந்து: birth and death as two nets, one before and one behind. The remedy is not solitary but the company of devotees (அன்பரோடே கலவி நலம் மருவி), and கடன் எனது உடலுயிரும் makes body and life a debt owed to the Lord.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","piravi","thondu","adiyaar"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:35"},"p:423":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":423,"t":["சுக்கி லச்சுரொணி தத்தி லுற்றநளி","னத்தி லப்புவென ரத்த முற்றிசுக","சுக்கி லக்குளிகை யொத்து கெர்ப்பகுகை ...... வந்துகோலத்","தொப்பை யிட்டவயி றிற்பெ ருத்துமிக","வட்ட மிட்டுடல வெப்ப முற்றுமதி","சொற்ற பத்தின் மறியக்ஷ ரத்தினுடை ...... விஞ்சையாலே","கக்க நற்புவியி லுற்ற ரற்றிமுலை","யைக்கொ டுக்கவமுர் தைப்பு சித்துவளர்","கைக்க சத்தியொடு ழைத்து தத்துநடை ...... அந்தமேவிக்","கற்று வெற்றறிவு பெற்று தொக்கைமயி","லொத்த மக்கள்மய லிற்கு ளித்துநெறி","கட்டி யிப்படிபி றப்பி லுற்றுடல ...... மங்குவேனோ","தெற்க ரக்கர்பவி ஷைக்கு லைத்துவிட","ணற்கு நத்தரச ளித்து முத்திகொடு","சித்தி ரத்திருவு ரத்த சக்கிரிதன் ...... மருகோனே","செக்க ரத்தின்மலை முப்பு ரத்திலெரி","யிட்ட சத்திசிவ னுற்று நத்தமிகு","சித்த னைத்தையும்வி ழித்த சத்தியுமை ...... தந்தபாலா","தர்க்க மிட்டசுர ரைக்கெ லித்துமலை","யுக்கெ ழுக்கடல்கொ ளுத்தி அட்ட திசை","தட்ட முட்டையடை யக்கொ டிப்புகையின் ...... மண்டும்வேலா","தத்தை வித்ருமநி றத்தி முத்தணிகு","றத்தி கற்பகவ னத்தி சித்தமவை","தக்கு நத்தஅரு ணைக்கி ரிக்குள்மகிழ் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet traces his making from seed and blood in the womb, growing like a pill, the belly swelling and the body's heat rising until birth in the tenth month, then crying, nursing, crawling, toddling, learning only empty knowledge and sinking into infatuation with peacock-like women, and asks whether he will waste away in birth like this. The praise names Vishnu who destroyed the southern rakshasas and gave a kingdom to the one who came to him, the guru Siva who burned the three cities, Uma who woke all the worlds as his mother, the vel that set the seas on fire, and the coral-hued hunter girl, at Arunai.","n":"கற்று வெற்றறிவு பெற்று: learning itself is listed among the stages that lead nowhere. The giving of a kingdom to a southern suppliant after the rakshasas' fall points to Vibhishana, though the name is garbled.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karu","piravi","mayal"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:35"},"p:424":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":424,"t":["செஞ்சொற் பண்பெற் றிடுகுட மாமுலை","கும்பத் தந்திக் குவடென வாலிய","தெந்தப் பந்தித் தரளம தாமென ...... விடராவி","சிந்திக் கந்தித் திடுகளை யாமுன","தங்கத் தம்பொற் பெதுவென வோதுவ","திண்டுப் புந்தித் திடுகனி தானுமு ...... னிதழாமோ","மஞ்சொக் குங்கொத் தளகமெ னாமிடை","கஞ்சத் தின்புற் றிடுதிரு வேயிள","வஞ்சிக் கொம்பொப் பெனுமயி லேயென ...... முறையேய","வந்தித் திந்தப் படிமட வாரொடு","கொஞ்சிக் கெஞ்சித் தினமவர் தாடொழு","மந்தப் புந்திக் கசடனெ நாளுன ...... தடிசேர்வேன்","நஞ்சைக் கண்டத் திடுபவ ராரொடு","திங்கட் பிஞ்சக் கரவணி வேணியர்","நம்பர்ச் செம்பொற் பெயரசு ரேசனை ...... யுகிராலே","நந்தக் கொந்திச் சொரிகுடல் சோர்வர","நந்திக் கம்பத் தெழுநர கேசரி","நஞ்சக் குண்டைக் கொருவழி யேதென ...... மிகநாடி","வெஞ்சச் சிம்புட் சொருபம தானவர்","பங்கிற் பெண்கற் புடையபெ ணாயகி","விந்தைச் செங்கைப் பொலிசுத வேடுவர் ...... புனமீதே","வெண்டித் தங்கித் திரிகிழ வாவதி","துங்கத் துங்கக் கிரியரு ணாபுரி","வெங்கட் சிங்கத் தடிமயி லேறிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet quotes his own flattery of women, breasts like pots and an elephant's brow, teeth like pearls, lips sweeter than fruit, hair like clouds, Lakshmi of the lotus, young vanji twig, peacock, and calls himself the dull-witted fool who coaxes, pleads and daily worships their feet; he asks when he will reach Murugan's feet. The praise names Siva who drank the poison, who took the form of the fierce Sarabha bird to stop the man-lion that tore Hiranya with its claws, and Uma his chaste consort as Murugan's mother, and Murugan as the old man who roamed the hunters' millet field, on the peacock at Arunapuri.","n":"The flattery is given as direct speech, so the poet's folly is shown, not described: he worships the women's feet (தினமவர் தாடொழு) where he should worship Murugan's. வெஞ்சச் சிம்புள் சொருபமதானவர்: Siva as the Sarabha subduing Narasimha. திரிகிழவா: Murugan's disguise as an old man to court Valli.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","pedhaimai","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:35"},"p:425":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":425,"t":["செயசெய அருணாத் திரிசிவ யநமச்","செயசெய அருணாத் திரிமசி வயநச்","செயசெய அருணாத் திரிநம சிவயத் ...... தி முலா","செயசெய அருணாத் திரியந மசிவச்","செயசெய அருணாத் திரிவய நமசிச்","செயசெய அருணாத் திரிசிவ யநமஸ்த் ...... தெனமாறி","செயசெய அருணாத் திரிதனின் விழிவைத்","தரகர சரணாத் திரியென உருகிச்","செயசெய குருபாக் கியமென மருவிச் ...... சுடர்தாளைச்","சிவசிவ சரணாத் திரிசெய செயனச்","சரண்மிசை தொழுதேத் தியசுவை பெருகத்","திருவடி சிவவாக் கியகட லமுதைக் ...... குடியேனோ","செயசெய சரணாத் திரியென முநிவர்க்","கணமிது வினைகாத் திடுமென மருவச்","செடமுடி மலைபோற் றவுணர்க ளவியச் ...... சுடும்வேலா","திருமுடி யடிபார்த் திடுமென இருவர்க்","கடிதலை தெரியாப் படிநிண அருணச்","சிவசுடர் சிகிநாட் டவனிரு செவியிற் ...... புகல்வோனே","செயசெய சரணாத் திரியெனு மடியெற்","கிருவினை பொடியாக் கியசுடர் வெளியிற்","றிருநட மிதுபார்த் திடுமென மகிழ்பொற் ...... குருநாதா","திகழ்கிளி மொழிபாற் சுவையித ழமுதக்","குறமகள் முலைமேற் புதுமண மருவிச்","சிவகிரி அருணாத் திரிதல மகிழ்பொற் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The song chants 'Jaya jaya Arunadri' with the five syllables of Siva in each of their rotations, then asks whether, fixing his eyes on Arunadri and melting at 'Hara Hara Saranadri', the poet will drink the sea-nectar of the Siva-word at the holy feet. The praise names Murugan the vel that burned the asuras, the one who spoke into the two ears of the red Siva-light whose head and feet the two seekers could not find, the golden guru who showed the dance in the space of light that powders the two deeds, and the lord who delights in Arunadri, the hill of Siva.","n":"The opening rotates the five letters in order, சிவயநம, மசிவயந, நமசிவய, யநமசிவ, வயநமசி, each joined to அருணாத்திரி, so the mantra and the hill are one chant. திருமுடி அடி ... இருவர்க்கு அடிதலை தெரியாப்படி: Brahma and Vishnu failing to find the ends of the column of light is the story of Arunachala, and Murugan is the one who taught that light.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panchaakkaram","mandhiram","guru","oli"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:35"},"p:426":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":426,"t":["தமர குரங்களுங் காரி ருட்பி ழம்பு","மெழுகிய அங்கமும் பார்வை யிற்கொ ளுந்து","தழலுமிழ் கண்களுங் காள மொத்த கொம்பு","...... முளகதக் கடமாமேல்","தனிவரு மந்தகன் பாசம் விட்டெ றிந்து","அடவரு மென்றுசிந் தாகு லத்தி ருந்து","தமரழ மைந்தருஞ் சோக முற்றி ரங்க",".... மரணபக் குவமாநாள்","கமல முகங்களுங் கோம ளத்தி லங்கு","நகையு நெடுங்கணுங் காதி னிற்று லங்கு","கனக குதம்பையுந் தோடும் வஜ்ர அங்க","..... தமுமடற் சுடர்வேலுங்","கடிதுல கெங்கணுந் தாடி யிட்டு வந்த","மயிலுமி லங்கலங் கார பொற்ச தங்கை","கழலொலி தண்டையங் காலு மொக்க வந்து","...... வரமெனக் கருள்கூர்வாய்","இமகிரி வந்தபொன் பாவை பச்சை வஞ்சி","அகில தலம்பெறும் பூவை சத்தி யம்பை","யிளமுலை யின்செழும் பால்கு டுத்தி லங்கு","...... மியல்நிமிர்த் திடுவோனே","இறைவ ரிறைஞ்சநின் றாக மப்ர சங்க","முரைசெய் திடும்ப்ரசண் டாவி சித்து நின்ற","ரணமுக துங்கவெஞ் சூரு டற்பி ளந்த","...... அயிலுடைக் கதிர்வேலா","அமண ரடங்கலுங் கூட லிற்றி ரண்டு","கழுவி லுதைந்துதைந் தேற விட்டு நின்ற","அபிநவ துங்ககங் காந திக்கு மைந்த","..... அடியவர்க் கெளியோனே","அமரர் வணங்குகந் தாகு றத்தி கொங்கை","தனில்முழு குங்கடம் பாமி குத்த செஞ்சொ","லருணை நெடுந்தடங் கோபு ரத்த மர்ந்த","..... அறுமுகப் பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet pictures the lone Death on his buffalo, dark as a mass of night with fiery eyes and horns like black serpents, throwing the noose while he sits in anxious thought and his kin and sons weep at the hour ripe for death; he asks Murugan to come then with his lotus faces, smile, long eyes, earrings, vel and dancing peacock, and grant him a boon. The praise names him the one who drank the milk of the mountain's daughter, who taught the Agama to the bowing lords, who split Sura, who set the Jains gathered at Koodal kicking on the stakes, son of Ganga, easy to reach for devotees, at the great tower of Arunai.","n":"The plea is the familiar one of 8 and 46, but the picture of Murugan coming is unusually full, face, smile, ornaments and anklets, as if the sight itself were the rescue. இளமுலையின் செழும்பால் குடுத்து and அமணர் ... கூடலில் ... கழுவில்: the milk of Uma and the impaled Jains of Madurai are Sambandar's story, here given to Murugan.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","aagamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:35"},"p:427":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":427,"t":["தமிழோதிய குயிலோமயி லாண்டலை யாம்புறவங்","கிளிகாடையி னணிலேரளி யாங்குரல் வாய்ந்ததிசெந்","தகுமாமிட றொலியாரித ழாஞ்சுளை தேன்கனியின் ...... சுவைசேருந்","தனபாரமு மலையாமென வோங்கிட மாம்பொறிசிந்","திடவேல்விழி நுதலோசிலை வான்பிறை மாந்துளிரின்","சரிரார்குழ லிருளாநகை யோங்கிய வான்கதிரின் ...... சுடர்பாயக்","குமிழ்நாசியின் முகமோமதி யாங்குளிர் சேங்கமலஞ்","சரிதோடிணை செவியாடுச லாங்கள பூங்கமுகங்","கொடிநூலிடை யுடையாரன மாம்ப்ரியர் மாண்புரிமின் ...... கொடிமாதர்","குணமோடம ளியினாடினு மோங்கிய பூங்கமலஞ்","சரணுபுர குரலோசையு மேந்திடு மாண்டலையின்","கொடியோடெழு தரிதாம்வடி வோங்கிய பாங்கையுமன் ...... தகையேனே","திமிதோதிமி திமிதோதிமி தாங்கண தீங்கணதொந்","தகுதோதகு தகுதோதகு டாங்குட தீங்கடதொந்","திகுடோ டிமி டிமிடோ டிமி டாங்குட டிந்தகமென் ...... றியல்பேரி","திநச்முடுக கடலேழ்பொடி யாம்படி யோங்கியவெங்","கரிதேர்பரி யசுரார்கள மாண்டிட நீண்டரவின்","சிரமீள்பட குவடோ துகள் வான்பெற வாங்கியவண் ...... கதிர்வேலா","கமழ்மாவிதழ் சடையாரடி யேன்துயர் தீர்ந்திடவெண்","தழல்மாபொடி யருள்வோரடல் மான்துடி தாங்கியவண்","கரர்மாடரு ளுமையாளெமை யீன்றவ ளீன்றருள்மென் ...... குரவோனே","கடையேனிரு வினைநோய்மல மாண்டிட தீண்டியவொண்","சுகமோகினி வளிநாயகி பாங்கனெ னாம்பகர்மின்","கலைநூலுடை முருகாவழ லோங்கிய வோங்கலின்வண் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet lists women's charms, voices like cuckoo, peacock, dove, parrot and bee, mountainous breasts, spear eyes, crescent brow, dark hair, bright teeth, moon face and swan gait, and seems to say that even if he sports with such women in bed he will not let go of Murugan's anklet-sounding lotus feet, the cock banner and his form beyond painting. The praise is a battle in drum syllables, the vel that made the seven seas dust, Siva who gives the white ash to end the poet's sorrow, Uma who bore 'us' and bore Murugan, and Valli's lover who ends the poet's two deeds and malas, at the hill of fire.","n":"If அன்தகையேனே is read 'I will not be held back from', the song makes the same vow as 420, keeping the feet in mind in the embrace. அடியேன் துயர் தீர்ந்திட வெண்தழல் மாபொடி அருள்வோர்: Siva's gift of holy ash for the poet's sorrow. Line 16 is corrupt.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","thiruvadi","thiruneeru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:35"},"p:428":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":428,"t":["தலையை மழித்துச் சிவந்த துணியை யரைக்குப் புனைந்து","சடையை வளர்த்துப் புரிந்து ...... புலியாடை","சதிரொடு வப்பப் புனைந்து விரகொடு கற்கப் புகுந்து","தவமொரு சத்தத் தறிந்து ...... திருநீறு","கலையை மிகுத்திட் டணிந்து கரண வலைக்குட் புகுந்து","கதறு நிலைக்கைக் கமர்ந்த ...... எழிலோடே","கனக மியற்றித் திரிந்து துவளு மெனைச்சற் றறிந்து","கவலை யொழித்தற் கிரங்கி ...... யருள்வாயே","அலைகட லிற்கொக் கரிந்து மருவரை யைப்பொட் டெறிந்து","மமரு லகத்திற் புகுந்து ...... முயரானை","அருளொடு கைப்பற்றி வந்து மருண கிரிப்புக் கிருந்து","மறிவு ளபத்தர்க் கிரங்கு ...... மிளையோனே","மலையை வளைத்துப் பறந்து மருவு புரத்தைச் சிவந்து","வறிது நகைத்திட் டிருந்த ...... சிவனார்தம்","மதலை புனத்திற் புகுந்து நரவடி வுற்றுத் திரிந்து","மறம யிலைச்சுற் றிவந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses a counterfeit renunciation: head shaved, ochre cloth at the waist, matted hair grown and twisted, a tiger skin worn with flourish, tricks learned, penance known only as a word, ash smeared thick, while he stays caught in the net of the senses and wanders making gold; he asks Murugan to take a little notice of him as he wilts and in pity end his care. The praise names Murugan who cut down the tree in the sea, shattered the hill, entered the gods' world and took the hand of the elephant-reared bride, who came to Arunagiri and pities devotees who know, the son of Siva who bent the mountain and burned the cities with an idle laugh, and who entered the millet field in human form.","n":"The plea is a confession, not a satire of others: the participles all lead to துவளும் எனை, 'me, who wilt'. He has worn both the shaved head (தலையை மழித்து) and the matted hair (சடையை வளர்த்து), every mark of the ascetic, and தவம் ஒரு சத்தத்து அறிந்து says he knew penance only as a sound. கனகம் இயற்றித் திரிந்து names the motive, gold. கொக்கு in the praise is taken as the mango tree that Sura became in the sea. சதிரொடு வப்ப and கதறு நிலைக்கைக்கு அமர்ந்த are obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vesham","koodaozhukkam","thavam","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:36"},"p:429":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":429,"t":["திருட்டு வாணிப விக்ரம துட்டிகள்","மதத்த ரூபிகள் துர்ச்சன பொட்டிகள்","செகத்து நீலிகள் கெட்டப ரத்தைகள் ...... மிகநாணார்","சிலைக்கு நேர்புரு வப்பெரு நெற்றிக","ளெடுப்பு மார்பிக ளெச்சிலு தட்டிகள்","சிரித்து மாநுடர் சித்தமு ருக்கிகள் ...... விழியாலே","வெருட்டி மேல்விழு பப்பர மட்டைகள்","மிகுத்த பாவிகள் வட்டமு கத்தினை","மினுக்கி யோலைகள் பித்தளை யிற்பணி ...... மிகநீறால்","விளக்கி யேகுழை யிட்டபு ரட்டிகள்","தமக்கு மால்கொடு நிற்கும ருட்டனை","விடுத்து நானொரு மித்திரு பொற்கழல் ...... பணிவேனே","தரித்த தோகண தக்கண செக்கண","குகுக்கு கூகுகு குக்குகு குக்குகு","தகுத்த தீதிகு தக்குகு திக்குகு ...... எனதாளந்","தடக்கை தாளமு மிட்டியல் மத்தள","மிடக்கை தாளமு மொக்கந டித்தொளி","தரித்த கூளிகள் தத்திமி தித்தென ...... கணபூதம்","அருக்க னாரொளி யிற்ப்ரபை யுற்றிடு","மிரந்த மாமுடி யைக்கொடு கக்கழ","லடக்கை யாடிநி ணத்தையெ டுத்துண ...... அறவேதான்","அரக்கர் சேனைகள் பட்டுவி ழச்செறி","திருக்கை வேல்தனை விட்டரு ளிப்பொரும்","அருட்கு காவரு ணைப்பதி யுற்றருள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet heaps abuse on courtesans: thieves who trade in their bodies, wicked and shameless, with bow-like brows and lifted breasts, who melt men's minds with laughter, frighten them with their eyes and fall on them, and who polish brass ornaments with ash to pass them off as gold; he asks to leave behind his delusion for them and bow single-minded at Murugan's golden feet. The praise is a battle scene in drum syllables, ghouls dancing with hands and drums and eating flesh as the demon armies fall to the vel, and ends with Guha who abides at Arunai.","n":"திருட்டு வாணிப, 'thieving trade', names the commerce at once. பித்தளையில் பணி மிக நீறால் விளக்கியே குழையிட்ட புரட்டிகள்: the cheats wear brass ear-ornaments scoured with ash to shine like gold, so even their jewels deceive. ஒருமித்து, single-minded, is set against the divided heart the women produce.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:36"},"p:430":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":430,"t":["தேதென வாச முற்ற கீதவி நோத மெச்சு","தேனளி சூழ மொய்த்த ...... மலராலே","சீறும ராவெ யிற்றி லு஡றிய காளம் விட்ட","சீதநி லாவெ றிக்கு ...... மனலாலே","போதனை நீதி யற்ற வேதனை வாளி தொட்ட","போர்மத ராஜ னுக்கு ...... மழியாதே","போகமெ லாநி றைத்து மோகவி டாய்மி குத்த","பூவையை நீய ணைக்க ...... வரவேணும்","மாதினை வேணி வைத்த நாதனு மோது பச்சை","மாயனு மாத ரிக்கு ...... மயில்வீரா","வானவர் சேனை முற்றும் வாழம ராப திக்குள்","வாரண மான தத்தை ...... மணவாளா","மேதினி யோர்த ழைக்க வேயரு ணாச லத்து","வீதியின் மேவி நிற்கு ...... முருகோனே","மேருவை நீறெ ழுப்பி நான்முக னார்ப தத்தில்","வேலடை யாள மிட்ட ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A love-lament for a girl: the speaker names what torments her, the flowers swarming with bees, the cool moonlight that burns like poison from a serpent's fang, and the arrows of Kama the warrior, and asks Murugan to come and embrace this girl filled with longing. The praise names Murugan the peacock hero honoured by Siva who bears a woman in his hair and by Vishnu, the bridegroom of the parrot raised by the elephant in Indra's city, who stands in the streets of Arunachala for the world to see, and who reduced Meru to dust and set the vel's mark on Brahma.","n":"பூவையை நீ அணைக்க வரவேணும: the speaker asks the god to embrace the girl, so this is akam poetry in the voice of a mother or companion, not the poet's confession. The moon that burns is a classical torment of the lovesick. வாரணமான தத்தை is Devayanai, raised by Indra's elephant. The last line (நான்முகனார் பதத்தில் வேல் அடையாளம் இட்ட) is unclear: the vel's mark may be set on Brahma's feet or on his station.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["kaamanoy","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:36"},"p:431":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":431,"t":["தோத கப்பெ ரும்ப யோத ரத்தி யங்கு","தோகை யர்க்கு நெஞ்ச ...... மழியாதே","சூலை வெப்ப டர்ந்த வாத பித்த மென்று","சூழ்பி ணிக்க ணங்க ...... ளணுகாதே","பாத கச்ச மன்தன் மேதி யிற்பு குந்து","பாசம் விட்டெ றிந்து ...... பிடியாதே","பாவ லற்கி ரங்கி நாவ லர்க்கி சைந்த","பாடல் மிக்க செஞ்சொல் ...... தரவேணும்","வேத மிக்க விந்து நாத மெய்க்க டம்ப","வீர பத்ர கந்த ...... முருகோனே","மேரு வைப்பி ளந்து சூர னைக்க டிந்து","வேலை யிற்றொ ளைந்த ...... கதிர்வேலா","கோதை பொற்கு றிஞ்சி மாது கச்ச ணிந்த","கோம ளக்கு ரும்பை ...... புணர்வோனே","கோல முற்றி லங்கு சோண வெற்பு யர்ந்த","கோபு ரத்த மர்ந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks that his heart not be lost to women with great breasts, that colic, fever, wind and bile and the other diseases not come near him, and that Death, the sinner, not come on his buffalo and throw the noose to seize him; he asks Murugan to pity this poet and give him songs of fine words fit for the learned. The praise names Murugan the Veda, bindu and nada, the true kadamba-wearer called Virabhadra and Kanda, the vel that split Meru, cut down Sura and pierced the sea, the lover of the golden girl of the hills, who sits in the beautiful tall tower of Sonagiri.","n":"The three things the poet fears come in order, women, disease, Death, and the remedy is the gift of song (பாடல் மிக்க செஞ்சொல்), the poet asking to be made a poet. Murugan is given Siva's fierce name வீரபத்ர. கோபுரத்து அமர்ந்த places him at the temple tower of Arunai.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","noy","kaamam","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:36"},"p:432":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":432,"t":["பாண மலரது தைக்கும் ...... படியாலே","பாவி யிளமதி கக்குங் ...... கனலாலே","நாண மழிய வுரைக்குங் ...... குயிலாலே","நானு மயலி லிளைக்குந் ...... தரமோதான்","சேணி லரிவை யணைக்குந் ...... திருமார்பா","தேவர் மகுட மணக்குங் ...... கழல்வீரா","காண அருணையில் நிற்குங் ...... கதிர்வேலா","காலன் முதுகை விரிக்கும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A short song in a lovesick woman's voice: struck by Kama's flower arrows, by the cruel young moon spitting fire and by the cuckoo whose call destroys her modesty, she asks whether it is right that she waste away in longing. The praise names the chest that embraces the maiden of heaven, the hero whose anklets are fragrant with the gods' crowns, the bright vel standing at Arunai to be seen, and the Lord who breaks the back of Death.","n":"நானும் மயலில் இளைக்கும் தரமோ: the woman speaks herself. நாணம் அழிய உரைக்கும் குயில்: the cuckoo's call makes her lose her shame. The last line, காலன் முதுகை விரிக்கும், gives Murugan a victory over Death even in a love song.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","mayal","naanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:36"},"p:433":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":433,"t":["பாலாய் நூலாய் தேனாய் நீளாய்","பாகாய் வாய்சொற் ...... கொடியார்தாம்","பாடா வாடா வேடா வாலே","பாடா யீடற் ...... றிடைபீறுந்","தோலா லேகா லாலே யூனா","லேசூழ் பாசக் ...... குடில்மாசு","தோயா மாயா வோயா நோயால்","சோர்வாய் மாளக் ...... கடவேனோ","ஞாலா மேலா வேதா போதா","நாதா சோதிக் ...... கிரியோனே","ஞானா சாரா வானாள் கோனே","நானா வேதப் ...... பொருளோனே","வேலா பாலா சீலா காரா","வேளே வேடக் ...... கொடிகோவே","வீரா தாரா ஆறா தாரா","வீரா வீரப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"In a rhythm of long vowels, the poet speaks of women whose speech is milk, honey and syrup, and of the body, a hut bound by the noose, made of skin, wind and flesh and stained, and asks whether he must tire and die of the unending disease of maya. The praise is a string of vocatives: Lord of the world, the high one, Veda, master, lord of the hill of light, way of jnana, king of the heavens, meaning of the many Vedas, vel-bearer, child, lord of the hunter girl, the support of the six centres, the hero.","n":"The song is built of paired long syllables (ஞாலா மேலா வேதா போதா), so meaning bends to sound and several splits are guesses. மாயா ஓயா நோய், the never-ceasing disease of maya, makes maya itself the illness. சோதிக் கிரி is Arunachala, the hill of fire, and ஆறாதாரா names Murugan the six adharas of yoga.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","maayai","kaamam","aadhaaram"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:36"},"p:434":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":434,"t":["பூணர்முலை மடந்தை மாதர் வலையினி லுழன்ற நேக","பொறியுட லிறந்து போன ...... தளவேதுன்","புகழ்மறை யறிந்து கூறு மினியென தகம்பொ னாவி","பொருளென நினைந்து நாயெ ...... னிடர்தீர","மணமுணர் மடந்தை மாரொ டொளிர்திரு முகங்க ளாறு","மணிகிரி யிடங்கொள் பாநு ...... வெயிலாசை","வரிபர வநந்த கோடி முநிவர்கள் புகழ்ந்து போத","மயில்மிசை மகிழ்ந்து நாடி ...... வரவேணும்","பணைமுலை யரம்பை மார்கள் குயில்கிளி யினங்கள் போல","பரிவுகொ டுகந்து வேத ...... மதுகூறப்","பறைமுர சநந்த பேரி முறைமுறை ததும்ப நீசர்","படைகட லிறந்து போக ...... விடும்வேலா","அணிசுக நரம்பு வீணை குயில்புற வினங்கள் போல","அமளியில் களங்க ளோசை ...... வளர்மாது","அரிமகள் மணங்கொ டேகி யெனதிட ரெரிந்து போக","அருணையின் விலங்கல் மேவு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says that much of his life has gone wandering in the net of women, and that now, knowing the praise the Vedas give Murugan, he will count his heart, gold, life and goods as Murugan's; to end this dog's sorrow he asks Murugan to come gladly on the peacock with his consorts, his six faces shining like the sun on a gem hill, praised by countless sages. The praise shows heavenly women chanting the Veda like cuckoos and parrots, drums sounding as the vel destroys the demons' army in the sea, and Murugan who married Hari's daughter and burns away the poet's trouble on the hill of Arunai.","n":"அகம் பொன் ஆவி பொருள் என நினைந்து: the offering of heart, gold, life and goods is the gift of the whole self, the servant's surrender. அரிமகள், Hari's daughter, is Valli. The opening lines (பொறியுடல் இறந்து போனது அளவு ஏது) are hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","thondu","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:36"},"p:435":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":435,"t":["புலைய னான மாவீனன் வினையி லேகு மாபாதன்","பொறையி லாத கோபீகன் ...... முமுடன்","புகழி லாத தாமீகன் அறிவி லாத காபோதி","பொறிக ளோடி போய்வீழு ...... மதிசூதன்","நிலையி லாத கோமாளி கொடையி லாத ஊதாரி","நெறியி லாத வேமாளி ...... குலபாதன்","நினது தாளை நாடோ று மனதி லாசை வீடாமல்","நினையு மாறு நீமேவி ...... யருள்வாயே","சிலையில் வாளி தானேவி யெதிரி ராவ ணார்தோள்கள்","சிதையு மாறு போராடி ...... யொருசீதை","சிறையி லாம லேகூடி புவனி மீதி லேவீறு","திறமி யான மாமாயன் ...... மருகோனே","அலைய மேரு மாசூரர் பொடிய தாக வேலேவி","அமர தாடி யேதோகை ...... மயிலேறி","அதிக தேவ ரேசூழ உலக மீதி லேகூறும்","அருணை மீதி லேமேவு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet heaps names on himself: an outcast, futile, a doer of great sins, angry without patience, a fool, without renown, dark-natured, without understanding, blind, his senses running off, an unsteady clown, a spendthrift who gives nothing, a ruin of his line; he asks Murugan to come and grant that he think of the feet every day with a longing that never ends. The praise names Rama, who fought until Ravana's arms were broken and took back Sita, as his uncle, and Murugan who sent the vel to powder Meru and Sura, rode the peacock among the gods and dwells at Arunai.","n":"கொடையிலாத ஊதாரி, a spendthrift who never gives, is a pointed paradox. The plea does not ask that desire end but that it turn: ஆசை வீடாமல், thinking of the feet with a longing that is not let go.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kutram","pedhaimai","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:36"},"p:436":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":436,"t":["போககற் பக்கடவுட் பூருகத் தைப்புயலைப்","பாரியைப் பொற்குவையுச் ...... சிப்பொ ழுதிலீயும்","போதுடைப் புத்திரரைப் போலவொப் பிட்டுலகத்","தோரைமெச் சிப்பிரியப் ...... பட்டு மிடிபோகத்","த்யாகமெத் தத்தருதற் காசுநற் சித்திரவித்","தாரமுட் பட்டதிருட் ...... டுக்க விகள்பாடித்","தேடியிட் டப்படுபொற் பாவையர்க் கிட்டவர்கட்","சேல்வலைப் பட்டடிமைப் ...... பட்டு விடலாமோ","ஆகமப் பத்தருமற் றாரணச் சுத்தருமுற்","றாதரிக் கைக்கருணைத் ...... துப்பு மதில்சூழும்","ஆடகச் சித்ரமணிக் கோபுரத் துத்தரதிக்","காகவெற் றிக்கலபக் ...... கற்கி யமர்வோனே","தோகையைப் பெற்றஇடப் பாகரொற் றைப்பகழித்","தூணிமுட் டச்சுவறத் ...... திக்கி லெழுபாரச்","சோதிவெற் பெட்டுமுதிர்த் தூளிதப் பட்டமிழச்","சூரனைப் பட்டுருவத் ...... தொட்ட பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes poets who, to end their poverty, liken rich men to the wish-granting tree, the rain cloud and Pari, praise them as fit to give heaps of gold at once, make 'thieving poems' of ornate figures to get money, and then spend what they earn on golden women and fall slaves in the net of their fish eyes; he asks whether one should come to that. The praise names Murugan sitting on the peacock at the northern side of the golden, jewelled tower of Arunai where Agama devotees and pure Vedic men gather, and the son of Siva who shares his left side with the peacock-like Uma, whose vel pierced Sura.","n":"திருட்டுக் கவிகள்: the poet calls praise-poems for patrons theft, an unusual confession about his own craft; the patron comparisons (கற்பகம், புயல், பாரி) are the stock ones of court poetry. The money won by false praise ends with the courtesans, so the two trades are joined. The praise lines on the tower (உத்தர திக்கு, கலபக் கற்கி) are hard to construe.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","iravu","varaivinmagalir","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:36"},"p:437":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":437,"t":["மானை விடத்தைத் தடத்தி னிற்கயல்","மீனை நிரப்பிக் குனித்து விட்டணை","வாளி யைவட்டச் சமுத்தி ரத்தினை ...... வடிவேலை","வாளை வனத்துற் பலத்தி னைச்செல","மீனை விழிக்கொப் பெனப்பி டித்தவர்","மாய வலைப்பட் டிலைத்து டக்குழல் ...... மணநாறும்","ஊன விடத்தைச் சடக்கெ னக்கொழு","வூறு முபத்தக் கருத்த டத்தினை","யூது பிணத்தைக் குணத்ர யத்தொடு ...... தடுமாறும்","ஊசலை நித்தத் த்வமற்ற செத்தையு","பாதியை யொப்பித் துனிப்ப வத்தற","வோகை செலுத்திப் ப்ரமிக்கு மிப்ரமை ...... தெளியாதோ","சானகி கற்புத் தனைச்சு டத்தன","சோக வனத்திற் சிறைப்ப டுத்திய","தானை யரக்கற் குலத்த ரத்தனை ...... வருமாளச்","சாலை மரத்துப் புறத்தொ ளித்தடல்","வாலி யுரத்திற் சரத்தை விட்டொரு","தாரை தனைச்சுக் ரிவற்க ளித்தவன் ...... மருகோனே","சோனை மிகுத்துத் திரட்பு னத்தினி","லானை மதத்துக் கிடக்கு மற்புத","சோண கிரிச்சுத் தர்பெற்ற கொற்றவ ...... மணிநீபத்","தோள்கொடு சக்ரப் பொருப்பி னைப்பொடி","யாகநெ ருக்கிச் செருக்க ளத்தெதிர்","சூரனை வெட்டித் துணித்த டக்கிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet tells how he was caught in the net of women whose eyes are likened to the deer, poison, the carp, Kama's arrow, the sea, the vel, the sword and the blue lily, and names the body as a poison of flesh, an oozing filth, a bloated corpse swinging with the three gunas, impermanent rubbish; he asks whether this delusion will ever clear. The praise names Rama, who struck Vali from behind a sal tree and gave Tara to Sugriva after Ravana had imprisoned Janaki in the grove of sorrow, as his uncle, and Murugan, son of the pure Lord of Sonagiri, whose kadamba-garlanded shoulders crushed the hill and who cut down Sura.","n":"குணத்ரயத்தொடு தடுமாறும் ஊசல்: the body is a swing tossed by the three gunas. நித்தத்துவம் அற்ற செத்தை, 'rubbish without permanence'. The line உபாதியை ஒப்பித்துத் துனிப்பவத்து அற is obscure; உபாதி may be the limiting adjunct of philosophy or simply affliction.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","yaakkai","mukkunam","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:36"},"p:438":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":438,"t":["முகத்து லக்கிக ளாசா ரவினிகள்","விலைச்சி றுக்கிகள் நேரா வசடிகள்","முழுச்ச மர்த்திகள் காமா விரகிகள் ...... முந்துசூது","மொழிப்ப ரத்தைகள் காசா சையில்முலை","பலர்க்கும் விற்பவர் நானா வநுபவ","முயற்று பொட்டிகள் மோகா வலமுறு ...... கின்ற்முடர்","செகத்தி லெத்திகள் சார்வாய் மயகிகள்","திருட்டு மட்டைகள் மாயா சொருபிகள்","சிரித்து ருக்கிகள் ஆகா வெனநகை ...... சிந்தைமாயத்","திரட்பொ றிச்சிகள் மாபா விகளப","கடத்த சட்டைகள் மூதே விகளொடு","திளைத்த லற்றிரு சீர்பா தமுமினி ...... யென்றுசேர்வேன்","தொகுத்தொ குத்தொகு தோதோ தொகுதொகு","செகுச்செ குச்செகு சேசே செககண","தொகுத்தொ குத்தொகு தோதோ தொகுதொகு ...... தொந்ததீதோ","துடுட்டு டுட்டுடு டூடூ டுடுடுடு","திகுத்தி குத்திகு தீதோ எனவொரு","துவக்க நிர்த்தன மாடா வுறைபவர் ...... தொணடர்பேணும்","அகத்தி யப்பனு மால்வே தனும்அறம்","வளர்த்த கற்பக மாஞா லியுமகி","ழவுற்ற நித்தபி ரானே அருணையில் ...... நின்றகோவே","அமர்க்க ளத்தொரு சூரே சனைவிழ","முறித்து ழக்கிய வானோர் குடிபுக","அமர்த்தி விட்டசு வாமீ அடியவர் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet lists the courtesans' kinds: those who flatter to the face, the scheming, the low-priced, the cunning, the deceitful in speech, those who for love of coin sell their breasts to many, who melt men with laughter and 'aha', thieves and forms of illusion; he asks when, leaving his revelling with them, he will reach the holy feet. The praise shows Siva dancing to drum syllables, worshipped by his devotees, Agastya, Vishnu, Brahma and the Goddess who nurtured dharma rejoicing in Murugan the eternal Lord who stands at Arunai, who smashed Sura and resettled the gods, the Lord of devotees.","n":"காசாசையில் முலை பலர்க்கும் விற்பவர்: the song names the sale outright. அறம் வளர்த்த கற்பகம் is the Goddess as nurturer of dharma, rejoicing with the others in Murugan.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:36"},"p:439":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":439,"t":["மேக மொத்தகுழ லார்சி லைப்புருவ","வாளி யொத்தவிழி யார்மு கக்கமல","மீது பொட்டிடழ கார்க ளத்திலணி ...... வடமாட","மேரு வொத்தமுலை யார்ப ளப்பளென","மார்பு துத்திபுய வார்வ ளைக்கடகம்","வீறி டத்துவளு நூலொ டொத்தஇடை ...... யுடைமாதர்","தோகை பக்ஷிநடை யார்ப தத்திலிடு","நூபு ரக்குரல்கள் பாட கத்துகில்கள்","சோர நற்றெருவு டேந டித்துமுலை ...... விலைகூறிச்","சூத கச்சரச மோடெ யெத்திவரு","வோரை நத்திவிழி யால்ம ருட்டுமயல்","தூள்ம ருத்திடுயி ரேப றிப்பவர்க ...... ளுறவாமோ","சேக ணச்செகண தோதி மித்திகுட","டாடு டுட்டமட டீகு தத்தொகுர்தி","தீத கத்திமித தோவு டுக்கைமணி ...... முரசோதை","தேச முட்கவர ஆயி ரச்சிரமு","மூளி பட்டுமக மேரு வுக்கவுணர்","தீவு கெட்டுமுறை யோவெ னக்கதற ...... விடும்வேலா","ஆதி மத்திபல கார ணத்தியெனை","யீண சத்திஅரி ஆச னத்திசிவ","னாக முற்றசிவ காமி பத்தினியின் ...... முருகோனே","ஆர ணற்குமறை தேடி யிட்டதிரு","மால்ம கட்சிறுமி மோக சித்ரவளி","ஆசை பற்றிஅரு ணாச லத்தின்மகிழ் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women with cloud-dark hair, bow brows, arrow eyes and breasts like Meru, who walk dancing down the good street crying the price of their breasts, lure those who come with deceitful play, and snatch their life with a powder of infatuation; he asks whether such women can be kin. The praise has drums sounding as the vel leaves Sura's thousand heads bare, shakes Meru and wrecks the demons' island; it names Murugan the son of Sivakami, the Shakti who is first, middle and cause of many things, and the one who rejoices at Arunachala in his love for Valli, daughter of Vishnu.","n":"முலை விலைகூறி: the women cry their price in the street. மயல் தூள் மருத்திடு suggests a love-powder or drug given to the men. The string of Shakti's names (ஆதி மத்தி பலகாரணத்தி ... ஈசசத்தி) is hard to divide, and the phrase on Vishnu searching out the Veda for Brahma is tentative.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","mayal","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:36"},"p:440":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":440,"t":["மொழிய நிறங்கறுத்து மகரவி னங்கலக்கி","முடியவ ளைந்தரற்று ...... கடலாலும்","முதிரவி டம்பரப்பி வடவைமு கந்தழற்குள்","முழுகியெ ழுந்திருக்கு ...... நிலவாலும்","மழையள கந்தரித்த கொடியிடை வஞ்சியுற்ற","மயல்தணி யும்படிக்கு ...... நினைவாயே","மரகத துங்கவெற்றி விகடந டங்கொள்சித்ர","மயிலினில் வந்துமுத்தி ...... தரவேணும்","அழகிய மென்குறத்தி புளகித சந்தனத்தி","னமுதத னம்படைத்த ...... திருமார்பா","அமரர்பு ரந்தனக்கு மழகிய செந்திலுக்கு","மருணைவ ளம்பதிக்கு ...... மிறையோனே","எழுபுவ னம்பிழைக்க அசுரர்சி ரந்தெறிக்க","எழுசயி லந்தொளைத்த ...... சுடர்வேலா","இரவிக ளந்தரத்தர் அரியர பங்கயத்த","ரிவர்கள்ப யந்தவிர்த்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A love-lament: the speaker names the roaring sea, darkened, its fishes stirred, and the moon that rises full of poison out of the mare-faced fire beneath the sea, and asks Murugan to think how to ease the infatuation of the girl with rain-dark hair and vine waist, and to come on the emerald peacock and give release. The praise names the chest that holds Valli's sandal-smeared breasts, the Lord of Indra's city, of Senthil and of Arunai, the vel that pierced the seven hills, and the one who ended the fear of the suns, Hari, Hara and Brahma.","n":"The torments are the classical sea and moon, with the moon rising from வடவை, the submarine fire. முத்தி தரவேணும: the girl's longing is to be ended by mukti, so the akam frame opens onto the plea for release.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","mayal","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:36"},"p:441":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":441,"t":["வலிவாத பித்தமொடு களமாலை விப்புருதி","வறல்சூலை குட்டமொடு ...... குளிர்தாகம்","மலிநீரி ழிச்சல்பெரு வயிறீளை கக்குகளை","வருநீர டைப்பினுடன் ...... வெகுகோடி","சிலைநோய டைத்தவுடல் புவிமீதெ டுத்துழல்கை","தெளியாவெ னக்குமினி ...... முடியாதே","சிவமார்தி ருப்புகழை எனுநாவி னிற்புகழ","சிவஞான சித்திதனை ...... யருள்வாயே","தொலையாத பத்தியுள திருமால்க ளிக்கவொரு","சுடர்வீசு சக்ரமதை ...... யருள்ஞான","துவர்வேணி யப்பன்மிகு சிவகாமி கர்த்தன்மிகு","சுகவாரி சித்தனருள் ...... முருகோனே","அலைசூரன் வெற்புமரி முகனானை வத்திரனொ","டசுராரி றக்கவிடு ...... மழல்வேலா","அமுதாச னத்திகுற மடவாள்க ரிப்பெணொடும்","அருணா சலத்திலுறை ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet lists the diseases of his body, wind, bile, scrofula, abscess, dry colic, leprosy, chills, thirst, diabetes, the great belly, phlegm, vomiting, retention of urine and countless more, says he can no longer bear to carry it about the earth, and asks for the attainment of Siva-knowledge so that his tongue may praise the Thiruppugazh, full of Siva. The praise names Murugan the son of the coral-haired Siva who gave the blazing discus to Vishnu for his unfailing devotion, the vel that killed Sura, the hill, the lion-faced and the elephant-faced demons, and the Lord at Arunachala with the hunter girl and the elephant's daughter.","n":"சிவமார் திருப்புகழை எனுநாவினில் புகழ: the book names itself, and the cure asked for is not health but the power to sing it. சிவஞான சித்தி joins knowledge and its attainment. The discus given to Vishnu for his devotion is told only as தொலையாத பத்தி.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["noy","yaakkai","pugazh","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:36"},"p:442":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":442,"t":["விடுமத வேள்வாளியின் விசைபெறு மாலாகல","விழிகொடு வாபோவென ...... வுரையாடும்","விரகுட னு஡றாயிர மனமுடை மாபாவிகள்","ம்ருகமத கோலாகல ...... முலைதோய","அடையவு மாசாபர வசமுறு கோமாளியை","அவனியு மாகாசமும் ...... வசைபேசும்","அசடஅ நாசாரனை அவலனை ஆபாசனை","அடியவ ரோடாள்வது ...... மொருநாளே","வடகுல கோபாலர்த மொருபதி னாறாயிரம்","வனிதையர் தோள்தோய்தரு ...... மபிராம","மரகத நாராயணன் மருமக சோணாசல","மகிபச தாகாலமு ...... மிளையோனே","உடுபதி சாயாபதி சுரபதி மாயாதுற","உலகுய வாரார்கலி ...... வறிதாக","உயரிய மாநாகமு நிருதரு நீறாய்விழ","ஒருதனி வேலேவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of women who, with eyes swift as Kama's arrows, chatter 'come, go' with the cunning of a hundred thousand minds, and of himself as a clown wholly in thrall to their musk-scented breasts, reviled by earth and sky, a fool without conduct, wretched and vile; he asks whether there will be a day when Murugan rules him among his devotees. The praise names the emerald Narayana, who embraced the sixteen thousand women of the northern cowherds, as his uncle, the ever-young king of Sonachala, and the lone vel thrown so the world might live, the sea dry and the demons fall as ash.","n":"நூறாயிர மனமுடை, 'with a hundred thousand minds', is the women's fickleness. The poet asks to be ruled together with the devotees (அடியவரோடு), not alone. Krishna's sixteen thousand women stand in the praise just after the poet's own confession, without comment.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kaamam","vanjam","kutram","adiyaar"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:36"},"p:443":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":443,"t":["விதிய தாகவெ பருவ மாதரார்","விரகி லேமனந் ...... தடுமாறி","விவர மானதொ ரறிவு மாறியே","வினையி லேஅலைந் ...... தி\u0002முடன்","முதிய மாதமி ழிசைய தாகவே","மொழிசெய் தேநினைந் ...... திடுமாறு","முறைமை யாகநி னடிகள் மேவவே","முனிவு தீரவந் ...... தருள்வாயே","சதிய தாகிய அசுரர் மாமுடீ","தரணி மீதுகுஞ் ...... சமராடிச்","சகல லோகமும் வலம தாகியே","தழைய வேவருங் ...... குமரேசா","அதிக வானவர் கவரி வீசவே","அரிய கோபுரந் ...... தனில்மேவி","அருணை மீதிலே மயிலி லேறியே","அழக தாய்வரும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says that, as fate had it, his mind wavered in the craft of young women, his understanding turned and he wandered in deeds as a fool; he asks Murugan to come so that this displeasure ends and he may reach the feet in due order, thinking of them through the ancient great Tamil set to music. The praise names Kumaresa who fought the deceitful demons' crowns to the ground and whom all the worlds circle, fanned by the gods with chowries, who comes beautifully on the peacock to the tower of Arunai.","n":"விதியதாகவே opens the confession by laying it to fate. முதிய மா தமிழ் இசையதாகவே மொழிசெய்து: the way to the feet is song in old Tamil. முனிவு தீர may mean the Lord's displeasure or the poet's own vexation.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oozh","kaamam","thamizh","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:37"},"p:444":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":444,"t":["விந்துப் புளகித இன்புற் றுருகிட","சிந்திக் கருவினி லுண்பச் சிறுதுளி","விரித்த கமலமெல் தரித்து ளொருசுழி","யிரத்த குளிகையொ டுதித்து வளர்மதி","விண்டுற் றருள்பதி கண்டுற் றருள்கொடு","மிண்டிச் செயலினி ரம்பித் துருவொடு","மெழுக்கி லுருவென வலித்து எழுமதி","கழித்து வயிர்குட முகுப்ப வொருபதில்","விஞ்சைச் செயல்கொடு கஞ்சச் சலவழி","வந்துப் புவிமிசை பண்டைச் செயல்கொடு","விழுப்பொ டுடல்தலை அழுக்கு மலமொடு","கவிழ்த்து விழுதழு துகுப்ப அனைவரு ...... மருள்கூர","மென்பற் றுருகிமு கந்திட் டனைமுலை","யுண்டித் தரகொடு வுண்கிச் சொலிவளர்","வளத்தொ டளைமல சலத்தொ டுழைகிடை","துடித்து தவழ்நடை வளர்த்தி யெனதகு","வெண்டைப் பரிபுர தண்டைச் சரவட","முங்கட் டியல்முடி பண்பித் தியல்கொடு","விதித்த முறைபடி படித்து மயல்கொள","தெருக்க ளினில்வரு வியப்ப இளமுலை","விந்தைக் கயல்விழி கொண்டற் குழல்மதி","துண்டக் கரவளை கொஞ்சக் குயில்மொழி","விடுப்ப துதைகலை நெகிழ்த்தி மயிலென","நடித்த வர்கள்மயல் பிடித்தி டவர்வரு ...... வழியேபோய்ச்","சத்தித் துறவொடு பஞ்சிட் டணைமிசை","கொஞ்சிப் பலபல விஞ்சைச் சரசமொ","டணைத்து மலரிதழ் கடித்து இருகர","மடர்த்த குவிமுலை யழுத்தி யுரமிடர்","சங்குத் தொனியொடு பொங்கக் குழல்மலர்","சிந்தக் கொடியிடை தங்கிச் சுழலிட","சரத்தொ டிகள்வெயி லெறிப்ப மதிநுதல்","வியர்ப்ப பரிபுர மொலிப்ப எழுமத","சம்பத் திதுசெய லின்பத் திருள்கொடு","வம்பிற் பொருள்கள்வ ழங்கிற் றிதுபினை","சலித்து வெகுதுய ரிளைப்பொ டுடல்பிணி","பிடித்தி டனைவரும் நகைப்ப கருமயிர் ...... நரைமேவித்","தன்கைத் தடிகொடு குந்திக் கவியென","உந்திக் கசனம றந்திட் டுளமிக","சலித்து வுடல்சல மிகுத்து மதிசெவி","விழிப்பு மறைபட கிடத்தி மனையவள்","சம்பத் துறைமுறை யண்டைக் கொளுகையில்","சண்டக் கருநம னண்டிக் கொளுகயி","றெடுத்து விசைகொடு பிடித்து வுயிர்தனை","பதைப்ப தனிவழி யடித்து கொடுசெல","சந்தித் தவரவர் பங்குக் கழுதுஇ","ரங்கப் பிணமெடு மென்றிட் டறைபறை","தடிப்ப சுடலையி லிறக்கி விறகொடு","கொளுத்தி யொருபிடி பொடிக்கு மிலையெனு ...... முடலாமோ","திந்தித் திமிதிமி திந்தித் திமிதிமி","திந்தித் திமிதிமி திந்தித் திமிதிமி","திமித்தி திமிதிமி திமித்தி திமிதிமி","திமித்தி திமிதிமி திமித்தி திமிதிமி","என்பத் துடிகள்த வுண்டைக் கிடுபிடி","பம்பைச் சலிகைகள் சங்கப் பறைவளை","திகுர்த்த திகுதிகு டுடுட்டு டுடுடுடு","டிடிக்கு நிகரென வுடுக்கை முரசொடு","செம்பொற் குடமுழ வுந்தப் புடன்மணி","பொங்கச் சுரர்மலர் சிந்தப் பதமிசை","செழித்த மறைசிலர் துதிப்ப முநிவர்கள்","களித்து வகைமனி முழக்க அசுரர்கள் ...... களமீதே","சிந்திக் குருதிக ளண்டச் சுவரகம்","ரம்பக் கிரியொடு பொங்கிப் பெருகியெ","சிவப்ப அதில்கரி மதர்த்த புரவிகள்","சிரத்தொ டிரதமு மிதப்ப நிணமொடு","செம்புட் கழுகுக ளுண்பத் தலைகள்த","தும்பக் கருடன டங்கொட் டிடகொடி","மறைப்ப நரிகண மிகுப்ப குறளிகள்","நடிக்க இருள்மலை கொளுத்தி யலைகடல்","செம்பொற் பவளமு டங்கிக் கமர்விட","வெந்திட் டிகமலை விண்டுத் துகள்பட","சிமக்கு முரகனு முழக்கி விடபட","மடைத்த சதமுடி நடுக்கி யலைபட ...... விடும்வேலா","தொந்தத் தொகுகுட என்பக் கழலொலி","பொங்கப் பரிபுர செம்பொற் பதமணி","சுழற்றி நடமிடு நிருத்த ரயன்முடி","கரத்த ரரிகரி யுரித்த கடவுள்மெய்","தொண்டர்க் கருள்பவர் வெந்தத் துகளணி","கங்கைப் பணிமதி கொன்றைச் சடையினர்","தொடுத்த மதனுரு பொடித்த விழியினர்","மிகுத்த புரமதை யெரித்த நகையினர்","தும்பைத் தொடையினர் கண்டக் கறையினர்","தொந்திக் கடவுளை தந்திட் டவரிட","சுகத்தி மழுவுழை கரத்தி மரகத","நிறத்தி முயலக பதத்தி அருளிய ...... முருகோனே","துண்டச் சசிநுதல் சம்பைக் கொடியிடை","ரம்பைக் கரசியெ னும்பற் றருமகள்","சுகிப்ப மணவறை களிக்க அணையறு","முகத்தொ டுறமயல் செழித்த திருபுய","செம்பொற் கரகம லம்பத் திருதல","மம்பொற் சசியெழ சந்தப் பலபடை","செறித்த கதிர்முடி கடப்ப மலர்தொடை","சிறப்பொ டொருகுடில் மருத்து வனமகள்","தொந்தப் புணர்செயல் கண்டுற் றடியெனி","டைஞ்சற் பொடிபட முன்புற் றருளயில்","தொடுத்து மிளநகை பரப்பி மயில்மிசை","நடித்து அழல்கிரி பதிக்குள் மருவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet traces a life from conception to the pyre: the drop in the womb, the whorl and the clot of blood, the growth month by month until birth in the tenth, the infant crying in its filth, at the breast and crawling, the decked child sent to learn, the youth following dancing women into lovemaking and spending his wealth, then disease and mockery, grey hair, the stick and a body bent like a monkey's, the wife taking charge of the goods, Death's noose dragging the life away, kin wailing each for their share, the drum, and the burning; he asks whether he should be this body, not worth even a handful of ash. The praise is a long battle in drum syllables, a catalogue of Siva's emblems and of Uma who bore Murugan, and his union with Devayanai and Valli, ending at the hill of fire, Arunai.","n":"The plea runs as one sentence through every stage of life, each image piled on the last, until ஒருபிடி பொடிக்கும் இலை எனும் உடல், a body not worth a handful of ash. சந்தித்து அவரவர் பங்குக்கு அழுது: the kin weep, each for their share, and the wife has already taken the property (சம்பத்து ... அண்டைக் கொளுகையில்). In the praise Uma is given Siva's own emblems, the axe and deer in her hand and the dwarf Muyalakan under her foot (மழு உழை கரத்தி ... முயலக பதத்தி). Many lines of the embryo passage and of the battle are hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["karu","yaakkai","kootru","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:37"},"p:445":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":445,"t":["வீறுபுழு கானபனி நீர்கள்மல தோயல்விடு","மேருகிரி யானகொடு ...... தனபார","மீதுபுர ளாபரண சோதிவித மானநகை","மேகமனு காடுகட ...... லிருள்மேவி","நாறுமலர் வாசமயிர் நூலிடைய தேதுவள","நாணமழி வார்களுட ...... னுறவாடி","நாடியது வேகதியெ னாசுழலு மோடனைநின்","ஞானசிவ மானபத ...... மருள்வாயே","கூறுமடி யார்கள்வினை நீறுபட வேஅரிய","கோலமயி லானபத ...... மருள்வோனே","கூடஅர னோடுநட மாடரிய காளியருள்","கூருசிவ காமியுமை ...... யருள்பாலா","ஆறுமுக மானநதி பாலகுற மாதுதன","மாரவிளை யாடிமண ...... மருள்வோனே","ஆதிரகு ராமஜய மாலின்மரு காபெரிய","ஆதியரு ணாபுரியில் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women anointed with civet, rosewater and musk, with heavy breasts, scented cloud-dark hair and thread-thin waists, who lose all shame, and calls himself the fool who mixed with them and wandered, thinking what he sought was the goal; he asks for the feet that are Jnana-Siva. The praise names the feet on the peacock that turn devotees' deeds to ash, the son of Sivakami Uma who granted Kali her dance with Hara, the six-faced child of the river, who sported with Valli and married her, the nephew of victorious Raghurama, at ancient Arunapuri.","n":"நாடியதுவே கதி எனா சுழலும் மோடன்: the error is taking the object of desire for the goal. ஞானசிவமான பதம்: the feet are not a means to knowledge but are knowledge and Siva. The line on Kali dancing with Hara appears to allude to their dance contest; how Uma's grace enters it is unclear.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","gnaanam","sivam","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:37"},"p:446":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":446,"t":["சரக்கே றித்தப் பதிவாழ் தொந்தப்","பரிக்கா யத்திற் பரிவோ டைந்துச்","சதிக்கா ரர்ப்புக் குலைமே விந்தச் ...... செயல்மேவிச்","சலித்தே மெத்தச் சமுசா ரம்பொற்","சுகித்தே சுற்றத் தவரோ டின்பத்","தழைத்தே மெச்சத் தயவோ டிந்தக் ...... குடிபேணிக்","குரக்கோ ணத்திற் கழுநா யுண்பக்","குழிக்கே வைத்துச் சவமாய் நந்திக்","குடிற்கே நத்திப் பழுதாய் மங்கப் ...... படுவேனைக்","குறித்தே முத்திக் குமறா வின்பத்","தடத்தே பற்றிச் சகமா யம்பொய்க்","குலக்கால் வற்றச் சிவஞா னம்பொற் ...... கழல்தாராய்","புரக்கா டற்றுப் பொடியாய் மங்கக்","கழைச்சா பத்தைச் சடலா னுங்கப்","புகைத்தீ பற்றப் புகலோ ரன்புற் ...... றருள்வோனே","புடைத்தே யெட்டுத் திசையோ ரஞ்சத்","தனிக்கோ லத்துப் புகுசூர் மங்கப்","புகழ்ப்போர் சத்திக் கிரையா நந்தத் ...... தருள்வோனே","திருக்கா னத்திற் பரிவோ டந்தக்","குறக்கோ லத்துச் செயலா ளஞ்சத்","திகழ்ச்சீ ரத்திக் கழல்வா வென்பப் ...... புணர்வோனே","சிவப்பே றுக்குக் கடையேன் வந்துட்","புகச்சீர் வைத்துக் கொளுஞா னம்பொற்","றிருக்கா ளத்திப் பதிவாழ் கந்தப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes himself carried along in this body with the five cheats, wearied by household life, delighting in kin, cherishing his family, until he is laid in a pit as a corpse for vultures and dogs to eat and the body-hut decays; he asks Murugan to mark him, hold him on the shore of release's unfailing bliss, dry up the false roots of the world's maya, and give him Siva-knowledge and the golden feet. The praise names Murugan the son of Siva who burned the cities to dust and burned Kama of the cane bow, who struck down Sura so the eight directions feared, who embraced the girl in hunter's guise in the holy forest, and who takes in even the lowest who come for Siva's attainment at Kalahasti.","n":"The fault confessed is not lust but the ordinary householder's life (சமுசாரம், சுற்றம், குடிபேணி), followed straight into the grave pit where vultures and dogs feed. சிவஞானம் பொற்கழல் joins knowledge and the feet as one gift. The opening lines (சரக்கேறித் தப்பதி, பரிக்காயம்) and குரக்கோணம் are obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","yaakkai","gnaanam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:38"},"p:447":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":447,"t":["சிரத்தா னத்திற் ...... பணியாதே","செகத்தோர் பற்றைக் ...... குறியாதே","வருத்தா மற்றொப் ...... பிலதான","மலர்த்தாள் வைத்தெத் ...... தனையாள்வாய்","நிருத்தா கர்த்தத் ...... துவநேசா","நினைத்தார் சித்தத் ...... துறைவோனே","திருத்தாள் முத்தர்க் ...... கருள்வோனே","திருக்கா ளத்திப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A short song: the poet asks that, so he neither bows his head to others nor aims at the attachments of worldly men, Murugan, without making him suffer, set his peerless flower feet on him and rule him. The praise names Murugan the dancer, lover of the true principle, who dwells in the minds of those who think of him and gives his holy feet to the freed, the Lord of Kalahasti.","n":"சிரத்தானத்திற் பணியாதே may mean 'without my bowing my head' to men, or ask for the feet to be set on the head (சிரத்தானம்); the reading depends on the split. கர்த்தத்துவ நேசா is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvadi","pattru","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:38"},"p:448":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":448,"t":["பங்கய னார்பெற் றிடுஞ்ச ராசர","அண்டம தாயுற் றிருந்த பார்மிசை","பஞ்சவர் கூடித் திரண்ட தோர்நர ...... உருவாயே","பந்தம தாகப் பிணிந்த ஆசையில்","இங்கித மாகத் திரிந்து மாதர்கள்","பண்பொழி சூதைக் கடந்தி டாதுழல் ...... படிறாயே","சங்கட னாகித் தளர்ந்து நோய்வினை","வந்துடல் மூடக் கலங்கி டாமதி","தந்தடி யேனைப் புரந்தி டாயுன ...... தருளாலே","சங்கரர் வாமத் திருந்த நூபுர","சுந்தரி யாதித் தருஞ்சு தாபத","தண்டைய னேகுக் குடம்ப தாகையின் ...... முருகோனே","திங்களு லாவப் பணிந்த வேணியர்","பொங்கர வாடப் புனைந்த மார்பினர்","திண்சிலை சூலத் தழுந்து பாணியர் ...... நெடிதாழ்வார்","சிந்துவி லேயுற் றெழுந்து காளவி","டங்கள மீதிற் சிறந்த சோதியர்","திண்புய மீதிற் றவழ்ந்து வீறிய ...... குருநாதா","சிங்கம தாகத் திரிந்த மால்கெரு","வம்பொடி யாகப் பறந்து சீறிய","சிம்புள தாகச் சிறந்த காவென ...... வருகோமுன்","செங்கதி ரோனைக் கடிந்த தீவினை","துஞ்சிட வேநற் றவஞ்செய் தேறிய","தென்கயி லாயத் தமர்ந்து வாழ்வருள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of the human form assembled by the five in the world of moving and unmoving things that Brahma creates, of wandering in the attachments that bind and failing to cross women's deceit, and of being troubled as disease and deeds cover the body; he asks Murugan to protect him by grace and give him a mind that does not waver. The praise names Murugan, son of the anklet-wearing beauty at Siva's left, with the cock banner, the guru who crawled on the shoulders of Siva of the crescent, serpent, bow and trident, whose throat shines with the poison of the sea; it tells of the Sarabha that flew raging to crush the pride of Vishnu as the lion, and ends at the southern Kailasa, Kalahasti, where one did penance so the sin of punishing the sun would end.","n":"பஞ்சவர் கூடித் திரண்டது ஓர் நர உரு: the body is made by 'the five', the elements, gathered together. கலங்கிடா மதி, an unshaken mind, is the whole request. The Sarabha story (சிங்கமதாகத் திரிந்த மால், சிம்புள்) and the closing lines on the sun (செங்கதிரோனைக் கடிந்த தீவினை) are told obliquely, and who did penance at Kalahasti is not clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","pattru","noy","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:38"},"p:449":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":449,"t":["கனகசபை மேவு மெனதுகுரு நாத","கருணைமுரு கேசப் ...... பெருமாள்காண்","கனகநிற வேத னபயமிட மோது","கரகமல சோதிப் ...... பெருமாள்காண்","வினவுமடி யாரை மருவிவிளை யாடு","விரகுரச மோகப் ...... பெருமாள்காண்","விதிமுநிவர் தேவ ரருணகிரி நாதர்","விமலசர சோதிப் ...... பெருமாள்காண்","சனகிமண வாளன் மருகனென வேத","சதமகிழ்கு மாரப் ...... பெருமாள்காண்","சரணசிவ காமி யிரணகுல காரி","தருமுருக நாமப் ...... பெருமாள்காண்","இனிதுவன மேவு மமிர்தகுற மாதொ","டியல்பரவு காதற் ...... பெருமாள்காண்","இணையிலிப தோகை மதியின்மக ளோடு","மியல்புலியுர் வாழ்பொற் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A song of praise only, each line ending 'behold the Lord': Murugan, the poet's own guru who dwells in the golden hall, the lotus-handed light, the Lord who plays among the devotees who seek him, the pure light worshipped by Brahma, sages, gods and Arunagirinathar, the nephew of Janaki's husband, son of Sivakami, bearer of the name Muruga, the lover of the nectar-sweet hunter girl in the forest, who lives with his consorts at Puliyur.","n":"அருணகிரிநாதர் appears among the worshippers, a signature of the poet inside his own praise, and எனது குருநாத makes Murugan his personal guru. Puliyur is Chidambaram. The line on golden-hued Brahma (கனகநிற வேதன் அபயமிட) is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","adiyaar","oli"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:39"},"p:450":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":450,"t":["கைத்தருண சோதி யத்திமுக வேத","கற்பகச கோத்ரப் ...... பெருமாள்காண்","கற்புசிவ காமி நித்யகலி யாணி","கத்தர்குரு நாதப் ...... பெருமாள்காண்","வித்துருப ராம ருக்குமரு கான","வெற்றி யயில் பாணிப் ...... பெருமாள்காண்","வெற்புளக டாக முட்குதிர வீசு","வெற்றிமயில் வாகப் ...... பெருமாள்காண்","சித்ரமுக மாறு முத்துமணி மார்பு","திக்கினினி லாதப் ...... பெருமாள்காண்","தித்திமிதி தீதே னொத்திவிளை யாடு","சித்திரகு மாரப் ...... பெருமாள்காண்","சுத்தவிர சூரர் பட்டுவிழ வேலை","தொட்டகவி ராஜப் ...... பெருமாள்காண்","துப்புவளி யோடு மப்புலியுர் மேவு","சுத்தசிவ ஞானப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A second litany ending 'behold the Lord': Murugan the brother of the elephant-faced one, the guru of the Lord of chaste Sivakami, the nephew of Rama, the bearer of the victorious vel, the rider of the peacock whose wings shake the mountains, the six-faced one with pearls on his chest, the child dancing to the drum, the king of poets who threw the vel so the Suras fell, and the Lord of pure Siva-knowledge who lives at Puliyur with Valli.","n":"கத்தர் குருநாத: Murugan as guru to Siva, the Lord of Sivakami, without the story told. கவிராஜப் பெருமாள் makes him king of poets, and சுத்த சிவஞானப் பெருமாள் makes him Siva-knowledge itself. வித்துருப ராமருக்கு is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:39"},"p:451":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":451,"t":["இருவினையின் மதிம யங்கித் ...... திரியாதே","எழுநரகி லுழலு நெஞ்சுற் ...... றலையாதே","பரமகுரு அருள்நி னைந்திட் ...... டுணர்வாலே","பரவுதரி சனையை யென்றெற் ...... கருள்வாயே","தெரிதமிழை யுதவு சங்கப் ...... புலவோனே","சிவனருளு முருக செம்பொற் ...... கழலோனே","கருணைநெறி புரியு மன்பர்க் ...... கெளியோனே","கனகசபை மருவு கந்தப் பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A short song: the poet asks not to wander with his mind confused by the two kinds of deed, nor to roam with his heart in the seven hells, but, through understanding that thinks on the grace of the supreme guru, to be given Murugan's vision. The praise names Murugan the Sangam poet who gave Tamil that discerns, the son of Siva with golden anklets, easy to reach for those who walk the path of compassion, the Lord in the golden hall.","n":"தெரிதமிழை உதவு சங்கப் புலவோனே: Murugan is himself a poet of the Sangam who gives Tamil. கருணைநெறி புரியும் அன்பர்க்கு எளியோன்: he is easy for those who practise compassion, not only for those who worship.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","naragam","guru","thamizh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:39"},"p:452":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":452,"t":["குகனெ குருபர னேயென நெஞ்சிற்","புகழ அருள்கொடு நாவினி லின்பக்","குமுளி சிவவமு தூறுக வுந்திப் ...... பசியாறிக்","கொடிய இருவினை மூலமும் வஞ்சக்","கலிகள் பிணியிவை வேரொடு சிந்திக்","குலைய நமசிவ யோமென கொஞ்சிக் ...... களிகூரப்","பகலு மிரவுமி லாவெளி யின்புக்","குறுகி யிணையிலி நாடக செம்பொற்","பரம கதியிது வாமென சிந்தித் ...... தழகாகப்","பவள மனதிரு மேனியு டன்பொற்","சரண அடியவ ரார்மன வம்பொற்","றருண சரண்மயி லேறியு னம்பொற் ...... கழல்தாராய்","தகுட தகுதகு தாதக தந்தத்","திகுட திகுதிகு தீதக தொந்தத்","தடுடு டுடுடுடு டாடக டிங்குட் ...... டியல்தாளம்","தபலை திமிலைகள் பூரிகை பம்பைக்","கரடி தமருகம் வீணைகள் பொங்கத்","தடிய ழனவுக மாருத சண்டச் ...... சமரேறிக்","ககன மறைபட ஆடிய செம்புட்","பசிகள் தணிவுற சூரர்கள் மங்கக்","கடல்க ளெறிபட நாகமு மஞ்சத் ...... தொடும்வேலா","கயிலை மலைதனி லாடிய தந்தைக்","குருக மனமுன நாடியெ கொஞ்சிக்","கனக சபைதனில் மேவிய கந்தப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks that, praising 'Guha, supreme guru' in his heart, Siva's nectar well up on his tongue and end his hunger, that the root of the two kinds of deed, the deceits and the diseases be torn out, that he chant 'namasivaya om' with joy, and that, knowing the space with neither day nor night to be the supreme state, he be given the golden feet as Murugan comes on the peacock. The praise is a battle in drum syllables, the vel scattering Sura's forces and frightening the serpent, and Murugan who sweetly taught his father, the dancer of Kailasa, until his heart melted, the Lord in the golden hall.","n":"நமசிவய ஓம்: the five syllables joined with Om. பகலும் இரவும் இலா வெளி, the space beyond day and night, names the state beyond waking and sleep. The teaching of Siva is told without naming Om (தந்தைக்கு உருக ... கொஞ்சி).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panchaakkaram","amudham","aakaasam","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:39"},"p:453":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":453,"t":["வண்டையொத் துக்கயல் கண்சுழற் றுப்புரு","வஞ்சிலைக் குத்தொடு அம்பையொத் துத்தொடை","வண்டுசுற் றுக்குழல் கொண்டலொத் துக்கமு ...... கென்பக்஡ணவம்","மந்தரத் தைக்கட பொங்கிபத் துப்பணை","கொம்பையொத் துத்தன முந்துகுப் பத்தெரு","வந்துஎத் திப்பொரு மங்கையர்க் கைப்பொரு ...... ளன்பினாலே","கொண்டழைத் துத்தழு வுங்கைதட் டிற்பொருள்","கொண்டுதெட் டிச்சர சம்புகழ்க் குக்குன","குங்குழற் கிப்படி நொந்துகெட் டுக்குடில் ...... மங்குறாமல்","கொண்டுசத் திக்கட லுண்டுகுப் பத்துனி","னன்பருக் குச்செயல் தொண்டுபட் டுக்கமழ்","குங்குமத் திற்சர ணம்பிடித் துக்கரை ...... யென்றுசேர்வேன்","அண்டமிட் டிக்குட டிண்டிமிட் டிக்குகு","டந்தகொட் டத்தகு டிங்குதொக் கத்தம","டஞ்சகட் டைக்குண கொம்புடக் கைக்கிட ...... லென்பதாளம்","அண்டமெட் டுத்திசை யும்பல்சர்ப் பத்திரள்","கொண்டல்பட் டுக்கிரி யும்பொடித் துப்புல","னஞ்சவித் துத்திர ளண்டமுட் டத்துகள் ...... வந்தசூரர்","கண்டமற் றுக்குட லென்புநெக் குத்தச","னங்கடித் துக்குடி லஞ்சிவப் பச்செநிர்","கண்தெறிக் கத்தலை பந்தடித் துக்கையி ...... லங்குவேலால்","கண்களிக் கக்கக னந்துளுக் கப்புக","ழிந்திரற் குப்பதம் வந்தளித் துக்கன","கம்பலத் திற்குற மங்கைபக் கத்துறை ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women with eyes like bees and carp, bow brows, cloud-dark hair, areca necks and breasts like an elephant's temples, who come down the street to cheat, call men with feigned love, embrace them and take their money; he asks that he not grieve and let his body-hut decay this way, but serve Murugan's devotees, hold the kumkum-scented feet and reach the shore. The praise is a battle in drum syllables in which the vel breaks the Suras, who had crushed the eight directions, the serpents and the hills, gives Indra back his place, and Murugan sits beside the hunter girl in the golden hall.","n":"உன் அன்பருக்குச் செயல் தொண்டுபட்டு: service to the devotees is the remedy for service to the women. The line on drinking a sea (சத்திக் கடல் உண்டு) is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["varaivinmagalir","thondu","adiyaar","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:39"},"p:454":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":454,"t":["கங்கு லின்குழற் கார்மு கஞ்சசி","மஞ்ச ளின்புயத் தார்ச ரம்பெறு","கண்கள் கொந்தளக் காது கொஞ்சுக ...... செம்பொனாரம்","கந்த ரந்தரித் தாடு கொங்கைக","ளும்ப லின்குவட் டாமெ னுங்கிரி","கந்த முஞ்சிறுத் தேம லும்பட ...... சம்பைபோல","அங்க மைந்திடைப் பாளி தங்கொடு","குந்தி யின்குறைக் கால்ம றைந்திட","அண்சி லம்பொலிப் பாட கஞ்சரி ...... கொஞ்சமேவும்","அஞ்சு கங்குயிற் பூவை யின்குரல்","அங்கை பொன்பறிக் கார பெண்களோ","டண்டி மண்டையர்க் கூழி யஞ்செய்வ ...... தென்றுபோமோ","சங்கு பொன்தவிற் காள முந்துரி","யங்கள் துந்துமிக் காட திர்ந்திட","சந்த செந்தமிழ்ப் பாணர் கொஞ்சிட ...... அண்டகோசம்","சந்தி ரன்பதத் தோர்வ ணங்கிட","இந்தி ரன்குலத் தார்பொ ழிந்திட","தந்தி ரம்புயத் தார்பு கழ்ந்திட ...... வந்தசூரைச்","செங்கை யுஞ்சிரத் தோடு பங்கெழ","அந்த கன்புரத் தேற வஞ்சகர்","செஞ்ச ரந்தொடுத் தேந டம்புரி ...... கந்தவேளே","திங்க ளொண்முகக் காமர் கொண்டவன்","கொங்கை மென்குறப் பாவை யுங்கொடு","செம்பொ னம்பலத் தேசி றந்தருள் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women with night-dark hair, moon faces, arrow eyes, breasts like an elephant's temples and anklets ringing, who speak like parrots and cuckoos and snatch the gold from a man's palm, and asks when his servitude to these cheats will end. The praise has conches, drums and Tamil bards sounding as the vel cuts off Sura's arms and head and sends him to Death's city, and names Kandavel who lives with the moon-faced Devayanai and the hunter girl in the golden hall.","n":"அங்கை பொன் பறிக்கார பெண்கள்: women who snatch the gold from the hand. ஊழியம் செய்வது என்று போமோ: the poet names his state as servitude, the bondage that the service of the feet replaces. அண்டி மண்டையர் is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","adimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:39"},"p:455":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":455,"t":["கொந்த ளம்புழு கெந்த வண்பனி","ரம்ப சம்ப்ரம ணிந்த மந்தர","கொங்கை வெண்கரி கொம்பி ணங்கிய ...... மடமாதர்","கொந்த ணங்குழ லின்ப மஞ்சள","ணிந்து சண்பக வஞ்சி ளங்கொடி","கொஞ்சு பைங்கிளி யன்பெ னுங்குயில் ...... மயில்போலே","வந்து பஞ்சணை யின்ப முங்கொடு","கொங்கை யும்புய முந்த ழும்புற","மஞ்சு வொண்கலை யுங்கு லைந்தவ ...... மயல்மேலாய்","வஞ்சி னங்கள்தி ரண்டு கண்செவி","யுஞ்சு கங்கள்தி ரும்பி முன்செய்த","வஞ்சி னங்களு டன்கி டந்துட ...... லழிவேனோ","தந்த னந்தன தந்த னந்தன","திந்தி மிந்திமி திந்தி மிந்திமி","சங்கு வெண்கல கொம்பு துந்துமி ...... பலபேரி","சஞ்ச லஞ்சல கொஞ்சு கிண்கிணி","தங்கு டுண்டுடு டுண்டு டன்பல","சந்தி ரம்பறை பொங்கு வஞ்சகர் ...... களமீதே","சிந்த வெண்கழு கொங்கு பொங்கெழு","செம்பு ளங்கரு டன்ப ருந்துகள்","செங்க ளந்திகை யெங்கு மண்டிட ...... விடும்வேலா","திங்க ளிந்திர னும்ப ரந்தர","ரும்பு கழ்ந்துரு கும்ப ரன்சபை","செம்பொ னம்பல மங்கொ ளன்பர்கள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women with perfumed hair and breasts like white elephant tusks, who come to the bed like parrot, cuckoo and peacock, leave scars on breasts and arms and loosen their garments; he asks whether, in that infatuation, with his eyes and ears failing, he must lie with the wrongs he did before and see his body perish. The praise is a battle in drum syllables, vultures, kites and eagles crowding the field where the vel struck the deceivers, and the Lord of the devotees in the golden hall where the moon, Indra and the gods melt in praise.","n":"முன்செய்த வஞ்சினங்களுடன் கிடந்து உடல் அழிவேனோ: the body is pictured dying in bed with its old deeds, the same bed as the embrace. வஞ்சினம் may mean wrongs or vows of hatred.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","mayal","vinai","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:39"},"p:456":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":456,"t":["மந்தர மென்குவ டார்த னங்களி","லார மழுந்திட வேம ணம்பெறு","சந்தன குங்கும சேறு டன்பனி","நீர்கள் கலந்திடு வார்மு கஞ்சசி","மஞ்சுறை யுங்குழ லார்ச ரங்கயல்","வாள்விழி செங்கழு நீர்த தும்பிய ...... கொந்தளோலை","வண்சுழ லுஞ்செவி யார்நு டங்கிடை","வாடந டம்புரி வார்ம ருந்திடு","விஞ்சையர் கொஞ்சிடு வாரி ளங்குயில்","மோகன வஞ்சியர் போல கம்பெற","வந்தவ ரெந்தவுர் நீர றிந்தவர்","போல இருந்ததெ னாம யங்கிட ...... இன்சொல்கூறிச்","சுந்தர வங்கண மாய்நெ ருங்கிநிர்","வாருமெ னும்படி யால கங்கொடு","பண்சர சங்கொள வேணு மென்றவர்","சேம வளந்துறு தேன ருந்திட","துன்றுபொ னங்கையின் மீது கண்டவ","ரோடு விழைந்துமெ கூடி யின்புறு ...... மங்கையோரால்","துன்பமு டங்கழி நோய்சி ரங்கொடு","சீபுழு வுஞ்சல மோடி றங்கிய","புண்குட வன்கடி யோடி ளஞ்சனி","சூலைமி குந்திட வேப றந்துடல்","துஞ்சிய மன்பதி யேபு குந்துய","ராழி விடும்படி சீர்ப தம்பெறு ...... விஞ்சைதாராய்","அந்தர துந்துமி யோடு டன்கண","நாதர் புகழ்ந்திட வேத விஞ்சைய","ரிந்திர சந்திரர் சூரி யன்கவி","வாணர்த வம்புலி யோர்ப தஞ்சலி","அம்புய னந்திரு மாலொ டிந்திரை","வாணிய ணங்கவ ளோட ருந்தவர் ...... தங்கள்மாதர்","அம்பர ரம்பைய ரோடு டன்திகழ்","மாவுர கன்புவி யோர்கள் மங்கையர்","அம்புவி மங்கைய ரோட ருந்ததி","மாதர் புகழ்ந்திட வேந டம்புரி","அம்புய செம்பதர் மாட கஞ்சிவ","காம சவுந்தரி யாள்ப யந்தருள் ...... கந்தவேளே","திந்திமி திந்திமி தோதி மிந்திமி","தீததி திந்தித தீதி திந்திமி","தந்தன தந்தன னாத னந்தன","தான தனந்தன னாவெ னும்பறை","செந்தவில் சங்குட னேம ழங்கசு","ரார்கள் சிரம்பொடி யாய்வி டுஞ்செயல் ...... கண்டவேலா","செந்தினை யின்புன மேர்கு றிஞ்சியில்","வாழுமி ளங்கொடி யாள்ப தங்களில்","வந்துவ ணங்கிநி ணேமு கம்பெறு","தாளழ கங்கையின் வேலு டன்புவி","செம்பொனி னம்பல மேல கம்பிர","காரச மந்திர மீத மர்ந்தருள் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women smeared with sandal and kumkum, moon-faced and fish-eyed, who speak sweetly as though they knew a man already ('which town are you from?'), say 'come', and taste union with whoever shows gold in his hand; through them come suffering, sores with pus and worms, and colic, until the body dies and enters Death's city, and he asks for the knowledge to cross that sea and gain the feet. The praise shows gods, sages, Brahma, Vishnu, Lakshmi, Sarasvati, Arundhati and the women of every world praising the dance of Sivakami Sundari, mother of Kandavel, the vel that powders the demons' heads, and Murugan who bowed at Valli's feet in the millet field and sits in the golden hall.","n":"நீர் எந்த ஊர் அறிந்தவர் போல: the courtesan's opening, feigning old acquaintance. துன்று பொன் அங்கையின் மீது கண்டவரோடு: union for whoever has gold in hand. The praise centres on Sivakami's dance, praised by the women of every world.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","noy","kootru","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:39"},"p:457":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":457,"t":["வந்து வந்துவித் தூறி யென்றனுடல்","வெந்து வெந்துவிட் டோ ட நொந்துயிரும்","வஞ்சி னங்களிற் காடு கொண்டவடி ...... வங்களாலே","மங்கி மங்கிவிட் டேனை யுன்றனது","சிந்தை சந்தோஷித் தாளு கொண்டருள","வந்து சிந்துரத் தேறி யண்டரொடு ...... தொண்டர்சூழ","எந்தன் வஞ்சனைக் காடு சிந்திவிழ","சந்த ரண்டிசைத் தேவ ரம்பையர்க","னிந்து பந்தடித் தாடல் கொண்டுவர ...... மந்திமேவும்","எண்க டம்பணித் தோளு மம்பொன்முடி","சுந்த ரந்திருப் பாத பங்கயமும்","என்றன் முந்துறத் தோணி யுன்றனது ...... சிந்தைதாராய்","அந்த ரந்திகைத் தோட விஞ்சையர்கள்","சிந்தை மந்திரத் தோட கெந்தருவ","ரம்பு யன்சலித் தோட எண்டிசையை ...... யுண்டமாயோன்","அஞ்சி யுன்பதச் சேவை தந்திடென","வந்த வெஞ்சினர்க் காடெ ரிந்துவிழ","அங்கி யின்குணக் கோலை யுந்திவிடு ...... செங்கைவேலா","சிந்து ரம்பணைக் கோடு கொங்கைகுற","மங்கை யின்புறத் தோள ணைந்துருக","சிந்து ரந்தனைச் சீர்ம ணம்புணர்நல் ...... கந்தவேளே","சிந்தி முன்புரக் காடு மங்கநகை","கொண்ட செந்தழற் கோல ரண்டர்புகழ்","செம்பொ னம்பலத் தாடு மம்பலவர் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says he has come again and again, sprouting from seed, his body burning and his life worn away in the forest of his deceits, until he faded; he asks Murugan, pleased in heart, to come on the elephant with gods and devotees around him, scatter that forest, and appear before him with kadamba-garlanded shoulders, golden crown and lotus feet. The praise names the vel thrown at the angry demons when Vishnu, who swallowed the eight directions, fled in fear and begged for service at Murugan's feet, the lover of the hunter girl and of Devayanai, and the Lord of the dancer of the golden hall who burned the cities with a smile.","n":"எந்தன் வஞ்சனைக் காடு: the forest of deceit is the poet's own, not the women's. வந்து வந்து வித்தூறி: births come one after another like seeds sprouting. The lines on the gods fleeing are hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","vanjam","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:39"},"p:458":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":458,"t":["கதித்துப்பொங் கலுக்கொத்துப்","பணைத்துக்கொம் பெனத்தெற்றிக்","கவித்துச்செம் பொனைத்துற்றுக் ...... குழலார்பின்","கழுத்தைப்பண் புறக்கட்டிச்","சிரித்துத்தொங் கலைப்பற்றிக்","கலைத்துச்செங் குணத்திற்பித் ...... திடுமாதர்","பதித்துத்தந் தனத்தொக்கப்","பிணித்துப்பண் புறக்கட்டிப்","பசப்பிப்பொன் தரப்பற்றிப் ...... பொருள்மாளப்","பறித்துப்பின் துரத்துச்சொற்","கபட்டுப்பெண் களுக்கிச்சைப்","பலித்துப்பின் கசுத்திப்பட் ...... டுழல்வேனோ","கதித்துக்கொண் டெதிர்த்துப்பிற்","கொதித்துச்சங் கரித்துப்பற்","கடித்துச்சென் றுழக்கித்துக் ...... கசுரோரைக்","கழித்துப்பண் டமர்க்குச்செப்","பதத்தைத்தந் தளித்துக்கைக்","கணிக்குச்சந் தரத்தைச்சுத் ...... தொளிர்வேலா","சிதைத்திட்டம் புரத்தைச்சொற்","கயத்தைச்சென் றுரித்துத்தற்","சினத்தக்கன் சிரத்தைத்தட் ...... சிவனார்தஞ்","செவிக்குச்செம் பொருட்கற்கப்","புகட்டிச்செம் பரத்திற்செய்த்","திருச்சிற்றம் பலச்சொக்கப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women with plaited hair, adorned necks and laughter, who grab a man's garland, madden him, bind him with coaxing, take gold until his wealth is gone and then drive him out with deceitful words, and asks whether he must wander, his desire for them satisfied and then turned bitter. The praise names the vel that crushed the demons and gave the gods back their place, Siva who burned the cities, flayed the elephant and struck off Daksha's head, and Murugan who poured the true meaning into Siva's ear, the beautiful Lord of Tiruchitrambalam.","n":"பசப்பிப் பொன் தரப் பற்றிப் பொருள் மாளப் பறித்துப் பின் துரத்து: the courtesan's whole course in a line. சிவனார்தம் செவிக்குச் செம்பொருள் கற்கப் புகட்டி: Murugan teaches Siva, though Om is not named.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","selvam","guru","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:39"},"p:459":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":459,"t":["சிரித்துச் சங்கொளி யாமின லாமென","வுருக்கிக் கொங்கையி னாலுற மேல்விழு","செணத்திற் சம்பள மேபறி காரிகள் ...... சிலபேரைச்","சிமிட்டிக் கண்களி னாலுற வேமயல்","புகட்டிச் செந்துகி லால்வெளி யாயிடை","திருத்திப் பண்குழ லேய்முகி லோவிய ...... மயில்போலே","அருக்கிப் பண்புற வேகலை யால்முலை","மறைத்துச் செந்துவர் வாயமு தூறல்க","ளளித்துப் பொன்குயி லாமென வேகுரல் ...... மிடறோதை","அசைத்துக் கொந்தள வோலைக ளார்பணி","மினுக்கிச் சந்தன வாசனை சேறுட","னமைத்துப் பஞ்சணை மீதணை மாதர்க ...... ளுறவாமோ","இரைத்துப் பண்டம ராவதி வானவ","ரொளித்துக் கந்தசு வாமிப ராபர","மெனப்பட் டெண்கிரி ஏழ்கடல் தூள்பட ...... அசுரார்கள்","இறக்கச் சிங்கம தேர்பரி யானையொ","டுறுப்பிற் செங்கழு கோரிகள் கூளியொ","டிரத்தச் சங்கம தாடிட வேல்விடு ...... மயில்வீரா","சிரித்திட் டம்புர மேமத னாருட","லெரித்துக் கண்டக பாலியர் பாலுறை","திகழ்ப்பொற் சுந்தரி யாள்சிவ காமிநல் ...... கியசேயே","திருச்சித் தந்தனி லேகுற மானதை","யிருத்திக் கண்களி கூர்திக ழாடக","திருச்சிற் றம்பல மேவியு லாவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women who laugh with teeth bright as lightning, melt men and fall on them, snatch their wages in a moment, wink, wear fine cloth to show their waists, hide their breasts, give their lips' nectar, speak like cuckoos and embrace men on soft beds, and asks whether such women can be kin. The praise shows the gods hiding and crying 'Kanda, supreme', the vel slaying the demons with lions, chariots, horses and elephants while vultures and ghouls dance in blood, the son of Sivakami beside Siva who burned Kama and bears the skull, and Murugan who keeps the hunter girl in his heart and walks in Tiruchitrambalam.","n":"செணத்தில் சம்பளமே பறிகாரிகள்: the women snatch a man's wages in an instant. திருச்சித்தந்தனிலே குறமானதை இருத்தி: Murugan holds Valli in his heart as the poet asks to hold the feet.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:39"},"p:460":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":460,"t":["தத்தையென் றொப்பிடுங் தோகைநட் டங்கொளுவர்","பத்திரங் கட்கயங் காரியொப் புங்குழல்கள்","சச்சையங் கெச்சையுந் தாளவொத் தும்பதுமை ...... யென்பநீலச்","சக்கரம் பொற்குடம் பாலிருக் குந்தனமொ","டொற்றிநன் சித்திரம் போலஎத் தும்பறியர்","சக்களஞ் சக்கடஞ் சாதிதுக் கங்கொலையர் ...... சங்கமாதர்","சுத்திடும் பித்திடும் சூதுகற் குஞ்சதியர்","முற்பணங் கைக்கொடுந் தாருமிட் டங்கொளுவர்","சொக்கிடும் புக்கடன் சேருமட் டுந்தனகும் ...... விஞ்சையோர்பால்","தொக்கிடுங் கக்கலுஞ் சூலைபக் கம்பிளவை","விக்கலுந் துக்கமுஞ் சீதபித் தங்கள்கொடு","துப்படங் கிப்படுஞ் சோரனுக் கும்பதவி ...... யெந்தநாளோ","குத்திரங் கற்றசண் டாளர்சத் தங்குவடு","பொட்டெழுந் திட்டுநின் றாடஎட் டந்திகையர்","கொற்றமுங் கட்டியம் பாடநிர்த் தம்பவுரி ...... கொண்டவேலா","கொற்றர்பங் குற்றசிந் தாமணிச் செங்குமரி","பத்தரன் புற்றஎந் தாயெழிற் கொஞ்சுகிளி","கொட்புரந் தொக்கவெந் தாடவிட் டங்கிவிழி ...... மங்கைபாலா","சித்திரம் பொற்குறம் பாவைபக் கம்புணர","செட்டியென் றெத்திவந் தாடிநிர்த் தங்கள்புரி","சிற்சிதம் பொற்புயஞ் சேரமுற் றும்புணரு ...... மெங்கள்கோவே","சிற்பரன் தற்பரன் சீர்திகழ்த் தென்புலியுர்","ருத்திரன் பத்திரஞ் சூலகர்த் தன்சபையில்","தித்தியென் றொத்திநின் றாடுசிற் றம்பலவர் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes courtesans with parrot speech and peacock gait who cheat and snatch, take money in advance, cling until a man's debts cover him, and leave him with vomiting, colic, boils, hiccups, sorrow, cold and bile, and asks when this thief will gain a place in grace. The praise names the vel dancing while the hills burst and the guardians of the directions sing, the son of Uma, Siva's half, the wish-granting gem, the mother dear to devotees, the son who came as a merchant to deceive and win the hunter girl Valli, and the Lord of the hall where Siva dances.","n":"முற்பணம் கைக்கொடும் தாரும்: 'give money in hand in advance', the courtesans' terms. In the praise Murugan comes as a merchant (செட்டியென்று எத்தி) to win Valli: the verb எத்தி, 'cheating', is the one used of the women (எத்தும் பறியர்), so by one reading the poet lets the god play the trick he condemns. Some of Uma's epithets are unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","noy","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:39"},"p:461":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":461,"t":["தனத்திற்குங் குமத்தைச்சந்","தனத்தைக்கொண் டணைத்துச்சங்","கிலிக்கொத்தும் பிலுக்குப்பொன்","தனிற்கொத்துந் தரித்துச்சுந்","தரத்திற்பண் பழித்துக்கண்","சுழற்றிச்சண் பகப்புட்பங் ...... குழல்மேவித்","தரத்தைக்கொண் டசைத்துப்பொன்","தகைப்பட்டுந் தரித்துப்பின்","சிரித்துக்கொண் டழைத்துக்கொந்","தளத்தைத்தண் குலுக்கிச்சங்","கலப்புத்தன் கரத்துக்கொண்","டணைத்துச்சம் ப்ரமித்துக்கொண் ...... டுறவாடிப்","புனித்தப்பஞ் சணைக்கட்டிண்","படுத்துச்சந் தனப்பொட்டுங்","குலைத்துப்பின் புயத்தைக்கொண்","டணைத்துப்பின் சுகித்திட்டின்","புகட்டிப்பொன் சரக்கொத்துஞ்","சிதைப்பப்பொன் தரப்பற்றும் ...... பொதுமாதர்","புணர்ப்பித்தும் பிடித்துப்பொன்","கொடுத்துப்பின் பிதிர்ச்சித்தன்","திணிக்கட்டுஞ் சிதைத்துக்கண்","சிறுப்பப்புண் பிடித்தப்புண்","புடைத்துக்கண் பழுத்துக்கண்","டவர்க்குக்கண் புதைப்பச்சென் ...... றுழல்வேனோ","சினத்துக்கண் சிவப்பச்சங்","கொலிப்பத்திண் கவட்டுச்செங்","குவட்டைச்சென் றிடித்துச்செண்","டரைத்துக்கம் பிடிக்கப்பண்","சிரத்தைப்பந் தடித்துக்கொண்","டிறைத்துத்தெண் கடற்றிட்டுங் ...... கொளைபோகச்","செழித்துப்பொன் சுரர்ச்சுற்றங்","களித்துக்கொண் டளிப்புட்பஞ்","சிறக்கப்பண் சிரத்திற்கொண்","டிறைத்துச்செம் பதத்திற்கண்","திளைப்பத்தந் தலைத்தழ்த்தம்","புகழ்ச்செப்புஞ் சயத்துத்திண் ...... புயவேளே","பனித்துட்கங் கசற்குக்கண்","பரப்பித்தன் சினத்திற்றிண்","புரத்தைக்கண் டெரித்துப்பண்","கயத்தைப்பண் டுரித்துப்பன்","பகைத்தக்கன் தவத்தைச்சென்","றழித்துக்கொன் றடற்பித்தன் ...... தருவாழ்வே","படைத்துப்பொன் றுடைத்திட்பன்","தனைக்குட்டும் படுத்திப்பண்","கடிப்புட்பங் கலைச்சுற்றும்","பதத்தப்பண் புறச்சிற்றம்","பலத்திற்கண் களித்தப்பைம்","புனத்திற்செங் குறத்திப்பெண் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes the common women (பொதுமாதர்) who adorn and perfume themselves, laugh and call, embrace a man on the bed and take his gold, and the man who, after paying, wanders with sores that swell and eyes that fester, so that those who see him cover their eyes; he asks whether he must wander so. The praise names the vel that broke the hill and struck the demons' heads like balls into the sea while the gods rejoiced and showered flowers, the son of the Mad One who burned Kama and the cities, flayed the elephant and ruined Daksha's sacrifice, Murugan who rapped Brahma the creator on the head, and the Lord of the Chitrambalam with the hunter girl.","n":"கண்டவர்க்குக் கண் புதைப்ப: the disease ends in the disgust of onlookers, the public shame that answers the private pleasure. படைத்துப் பொன்றுடைத் திட்பன் தனைக் குட்டும் படுத்தி: Brahma rapped on the head, from the story of Om, though Om itself is not named.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","punarchi","noy"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:39"},"p:462":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":462,"t":["திருடிக ளிணக்கிச் சம்ப ளம்பறி","நடுவிகள் மயக்கிச் சங்க முண்கிகள்","சிதடிகள் முலைக்கச் சும்பல் கண்டிகள் ...... சதிகாரர்","செவிடிகள் மதப்பட் டுங்கு குண்டிகள்","அசடிகள் பிணக்கிட் டும்பு றம்பிகள்","செழுமிக ளழைத்திச் சங்கொ ளுஞ்செயர் ...... வெகுமோகக்","குருடிகள் நகைத்திட் டம்பு லம்புக","ளுதடிகள் கணக்கிட் டும்பி ணங்கிகள்","குசலிகள் மருத்திட் டுங்கொ டுங்குணர் ...... விழியாலே","கொளுவிகள் மினுக்குச் சங்கி ரங்கிகள்","நடனமு நடித்திட் டொங்கு சண்டிகள்","குணமதில் முழுச்சுத் தசங்க்ய சங்கிக ...... ளுறவாமோ","இருடிய ரினத்துற் றும்ப தங்கொளு","மறையவ னிலத்தொக் குஞ்சு கம்பெறு","மிமையவ ரினக்கட் டுங்கு லைந்திட ...... வருசூரர்","இபமொடு வெதித்தச் சிங்க மும்பல","இரதமொ டெதத்திக் கும்பி ளந்திட","இவுளியி ரதத்துற் றங்க மங்கிட ...... விடும்வேலா","அரிகரி யுரித்திட் டங்க சன்புர","மெரிதர நகைத்துப் பங்க யன்சிர","மளவொடு மறுத்துப் பண்ட ணிந்தவ ...... ரருள்கோனே","அமரர்த மகட்கிட் டம்பு ரிந்துநல்","குறவர்த மகட்பக் கஞ்சி றந்துற","அழகிய திருச்சிற் றம்ப லம்புகு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet heaps names on courtesans: thieves who snatch wages, deceivers who intoxicate, the deaf, the blind, the stupid and the quarrelsome, who count and wrangle, ensnare with their eyes, preen and dance, and asks whether such women can be kin. The praise names the vel that broke the elephants, lions, chariots and horses of Sura when he came to ruin the sages, Brahmins and gods, the son of Siva who flayed the elephant, burned the cities with a laugh and cut off Brahma's head, and Murugan who married the daughter of the gods and the daughter of the hunters and entered the beautiful Tiruchitrambalam.","n":"The plea is almost entirely a list of epithets in -இகள், each a separate insult, as in 429 and 438. Several (சிதடிகள், உதடிகள், சங்க்ய சங்கிகள்) are uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:39"},"p:463":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":463,"t":["கொந்த ரங்குழ லிந்து வண்புரு","வங்கள் கண்கய லுஞ்ச ரங்கணை","கொண்ட ரம்பைய ரந்த முஞ்சசி ...... துண்டமாதர்","கொந்த ளங்கதி ரின்கு லங்களி","னுஞ்சு ழன்றிர சம்ப லங்கனி","கொண்ட நண்பித ழின்சு கங்குயி ...... லின்சொல்மேவுந்","தந்த வந்தர ளஞ்சி றந்தெழு","கந்த ரங்கமு கென்ப பைங்கழை","தண்பு யந்தளி ரின்கு டங்கைய ...... ரம்பொனாரந்","தந்தி யின்குவ டின்த னங்களி","ரண்டை யுங்குலை கொண்டு விண்டவர்","தங்க டம்படி யுங்க வண்டிய ...... சிந்தையாமோ","மந்த ரங்கட லுஞ்சு ழன்றமிர்","தங்க டைந்தவ னஞ்சு மங்குலி","மந்தி ரஞ்செல்வ முஞ்சு கம்பெற ...... எந்தவாழ்வும்","வந்த ரம்பையெ ணும்ப கிர்ந்துந","டங்கோ ளுந்திரு மங்கை பங்கினன்","வண்டர் லங்கையு ளன்சி ரம்பொடி ...... கண்டமாயோன்","உந்தி யின்புவ னங்க ளெங்கும","டங்க வுண்டகு டங்கை யன்புக","ழொண்பு ரம்பொடி கண்ட எந்தையர் ...... பங்கின்மேவும்","உம்ப லின்கலை மங்கை சங்கரி","மைந்த னென்றய னும்பு கழ்ந்திட","வொண்ப ரந்திரு வம்ப லந்திகழ் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet lists a woman's beauties, hair, brows, carp eyes, moon forehead, lips sweet as fruit, cuckoo voice, pearl teeth, areca neck, bamboo arms, golden necklaces and breasts like an elephant's temples, and asks whether his mind should be one that clutches at those who take even the last coin of their lovers. The praise names Vishnu who churned the sea for nectar, who shares himself with Lakshmi, who powdered the head of Lanka's king and holds the worlds in his belly, and Siva who burned the cities, and calls Murugan the son of Sankari, praised by Brahma, the Lord of the shining Ambalam.","n":"The plea is almost all description, the catalogue of beauty that the closing question turns against. விண்டவர் தங்கள் தம்படியும் கவண்டிய: 'grabbing even the copper coin of those who part', but the split and subject of this line are uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","varaivinmagalir","azhagu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:39"},"p:464":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":464,"t":["தியங்குஞ்சஞ் சலந்துன்பங்","கடந்தொந்தஞ் செறிந்தைந்திந்","த்ரியம்பந்தந் தருந்துன்பம் ...... படுமேழை","திதம்பண்பொன் றிலன்பண்டன்","தலன்குண்டன் சலன்கண்டன்","தெளிந்துன்றன் பழந்தொண்டென் ...... றுயர்வாகப்","புயங்கந்திங் களின்துண்டங்","குருந்தின்கொந் தயன்றன்கம்","பொருந்துங்கங் கலந்தஞ்செஞ் ...... சடைசூடி","புகழ்ந்துங்கண் டுகந்துங்கும்","பிடுஞ்செம்பொன் சிலம்பென்றும்","புலம்பும்பங் கயந்தந்தென் ...... குறைதீராய்","இயம்புஞ்சம் புகந்துன்றுஞ்","சுணங்கன்செம் பருந்தங்கங்","கிணங்குஞ்செந் தடங்கண்டுங் ...... களிகூர","இடும்பைங்கண் சிரங்கண்டம்","பதந்தந்தங் கரஞ்சந்தொன்","றெலும்புஞ்சிந் திடும்பங்கஞ் ...... செயும்வேலா","தயங்கும்பைஞ் சுரும்பெங்குந்","தனந்தந்தந் தனந்தந்தந்","தடந்தண்பங் கயங்கொஞ்சுஞ் ...... சிறுகூரா","தவங்கொண்டுஞ் செபங்கொண்டுஞ்","சிவங்கொண்டும் ப்ரியங்கொண்டுந்","தலந்துன்றம் பலந்தங்கும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet calls himself a wretch who suffers the bewilderment, wavering and grief the bond of the five senses brings, with no steadiness or good quality, a fool and fickle, and asks that Murugan, recognising him as his old servant, give the lotus feet with golden anklets that Siva, wearing the serpent, the crescent, kurundu flowers, Brahma's skull and Ganga in his red hair, praises, sees with joy and worships. The praise shows the battlefield where jackals, dogs and red kites feast on heads, necks, tusks and bones, and the hall at Chidambaram where people come with austerity, japa and love.","n":"The whole song runs in nasal-heavy syllables (தியங்குஞ்சஞ் சலந்துன்பங்), and the word divisions are often a guess. உன்றன் பழந்தொண்டு: the poet claims to be Murugan's servant of old. The feet asked for are those Siva himself worships (கும்பிடும் செம்பொன் சிலம்பு), so the son's feet are above the father's. The meaning of சிறுகூரா near the end is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kutram","thondu","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:40"},"p:465":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":465,"t":["பருவம் பணைத்தி ரண்டு கரிகொம் பெனத்தி ரண்டு","பவளம் பதித்த செம்பொ ...... னிறமார்பிற்","படருங் கனத்த கொங்கை மினல்கொந் தளித்து சிந்த","பலவிஞ் சையைப்பு லம்பி ...... யழகான","புருவஞ் சுழற்றி யிந்த்ர தநுவந் துதித்த தென்று","புளகஞ் செலுத்தி ரண்டு ...... கயல்மேவும்","பொறிகண் சுழற்றி ரம்ப பரிசம் பயிற்றி மந்த்ர","பொடிகொண் டழிக்கும் வஞ்ச ...... ருறவாமோ","உருவந் தரித்து கந்து கரமும் பிடித்து வந்து","உறவும் பிடித்த ணங்கை ...... வனமீதே","ஒளிர்கொம் பினைச்ச வுந்த ரியவும் பலைக்கொ ணர்ந்து","ஒளிர்வஞ் சியைப்பு ணர்ந்த ...... மணிமார்பா","செருவெங் களத்தில் வந்த அவுணன் தெறித்து மங்க","சிவமஞ் செழுத்தை முந்த ...... விடுவோனே","தினமுங் களித்து செம்பொ னுலகந் துதித்தி றைஞ்சு","திருவம் பலத்த மர்ந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women with breasts like elephant tusks, hair flashing like lightning, brows whirled like Indra's bow, carp eyes and practised touch, who destroy men with magic powder, and asks whether union with such deceivers is fitting. The praise shows Murugan taking a disguise in the forest to win and embrace Valli, sending the five syllables of Siva ahead in battle so the demon is struck down, and dwelling in the holy hall that the golden world praises daily.","n":"மந்த்ர பொடிகொண்டு அழிக்கும் வஞ்சர்: the women's weapon is a charmed powder, a love-magic, which the praise answers with a holier spell: சிவம் அஞ்செழுத்தை முந்த விடுவோனே, the five syllables of Siva sent first against the asura. The line on Valli (ஒளிர்கொம்பினைச் சவுந்தரியவும் பலைக்கொணர்ந்து) does not divide clearly.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","panchaakkaram"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:40"},"p:466":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":466,"t":["மதவெங் கரிக்கி ரண்டு வலுகொம் பெனத்தி ரண்டு","வளரும் தனத்த ணிந்த ...... மணியாரம்","வளைசெங் கையிற்சி றந்த வொளிகண் டுநித்தி லங்கு","வரருந் திகைத்தி ரங்க ...... வருமானார்","விதவிங் கிதப்ரி யங்கள் நகைகொஞ் சுதற்கு ணங்கள்","மிகைகண் டுறக்க லங்கி ...... மருளாதே","விடுசங் கையற்று ணர்ந்து வலம்வந் துனைப்பு கழ்ந்து","மிகவிஞ் சுபொற்ப தங்கள் ...... தருவாயே","நதியுந் திருக்க ரந்தை மதியுஞ் சடைக்க ணிந்த","நடநம் பருற்றி ருந்த ...... கயிலாய","நகமங் கையிற்பி டுங்கு மசுரன் சிரத்தொ டங்கம்","நவதுங் கரத்ந முந்து ...... திரடோ ளுஞ்","சிதையும் படிக்கொ ரம்பு தனைமுன் தொடுத்த கொண்டல்","திறல்செங் கணச்சு தன்றன் ...... மருகோனே","தினமுங் கருத்து ணர்ந்து சுரர்வந் துறப்ப ணிந்த","திருவம் பலத்த மர்ந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women with breasts like a rutting elephant's tusks under gem necklaces, with bangled hands, whose varied pleasing ways, smiles and coaxing he sees in excess, and asks not to be confused and deluded but, with doubt gone, to circle Murugan, praise him and receive the golden feet. The praise names Siva who wears the river, basil and moon and dances at Kailasa, and Vishnu who shot the arrow that shattered the heads and jewelled arms of the asura who tried to uproot that mountain, as Murugan's uncle, at the holy hall the gods worship daily.","n":"The song is a twin of 465 in metre and opening (the breasts as an elephant's two tusks). The praise links two Ravana stories: the one who tried to pluck Kailasa in his hands (நகம் அங்கையில் பிடுங்கும் அசுரன்) is the one Rama's arrow destroyed. வலம்வந்து: the plea names the temple rite of circling.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mayal","vazhipaadu","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:40"},"p:467":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":467,"t":["முகசந்திர புருவஞ்சிலை விழியுங்கயல் நீல","முகிலங்குழ லொளிர்தொங்கலொ டிசைவண்டுகள் பாட","மொழியுங்கிளி யிதழ்பங்கய நகைசங்கொளி காதிற் ...... குழையாட","முழவங்கர கமுகம்பரி மளகுங்கும வாச","முலையின்பர சகுடங்குவ டிணைகொண்டுநல் மார்பில்","முரணுஞ்சிறு பவளந்தர ளவடந்தொடை யாடக் ...... கொடிபோலத்","துகிரின்கொடி யொடியும்படி நடனந்தொடை வாழை","மறையும்படி துயல்சுந்தர சுகமங்கைய ரோடு","துதைபஞ்சணை மிசையங்கசன் ரதியின்பம தாகச் ...... செயல்மேவித்","தொடைசிந்திட மொழிகொஞ்சிட அளகஞ்சுழ லாட","விழிதுஞ்சிட இடைதொய்ஞ்சிட மயல்கொண் டணைகீனும்","சுகசந்திர முகமும்பத அழகுந்தமி யேனுக் ...... கருள்வாயே","அகரந்திரு உயிர்பண்புற அரியென்பது மாகி","உறையுஞ்சுட ரொளியென்கணில் வளருஞ்சிவ காமி","அமுதம்பொழி பரையந்தரி உமைபங்கர னாருக் ...... கொருசேயே","அசுரன்சிர மிரதம்பரி சிலையுங்கெட கோடு","சரமும்பல படையும்பொடி கடலுங்கிரி சாய","அமர்கொண்டயில் விடுசெங்கர வொளிசெங்கதிர் போலத் ...... திகழ்வோனே","மகரங்கொடி நிலவின்குடை மதனன்திரு தாதை","மருகென்றணி விருதும்பல முரசங்கலை யோத","மறையன்றலை யுடையும்படி நடனங்கொளு மாழைக் ...... கதிர்வேலா","வடிவிந்திரன் மகள்சுந்தர மணமுங்கொடு மோக","சரசங்குற மகள்பங்கொடு வளர்தென்புலி யூரில்","மகிழும்புகழ் திருவம்பல மருவுங்கும ரேசப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women with moon faces, bow brows, carp eyes, cloud-dark hair, parrot speech and conch-white smiles, and lovemaking with them on the bed as Kama and Rati's pleasure, hair loosened, words faltering and eyes closing; he asks that even then Murugan grant him, alone as he is, the blissful moon faces and the beauty of the feet. The praise calls Sivakami the letter A, the life of all, who is also Hari and grows as a light in the poet's eye, and Murugan her son, whose vel destroyed the asura's army and before whom Brahma's head broke, the lord of Valli and Devayanai at the hall in southern Puliyur.","n":"The plea seems not to ask to leave the bed but to be given Murugan's faces and feet there (மயல்கொண்டு அணைகீனும் ... அருள்வாயே), as 468 says outright. அகரம் திரு உயிர் பண்புற அரி என்பதுமாகி: Sivakami is the letter A and the life of letters, and also Hari, the Saiva teaching that Vishnu is a form of the Goddess. மறையன் தலை உடையும்படி may allude to Brahma's punishment.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","saththi","thiyaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:40"},"p:468":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":468,"t":["சந்திர வோலைகு லாவ கொங்கைகள்","மந்தர மாலந னீர்த தும்பநல்","சண்பக மாலைகு லாவி ளங்குழல் ...... மஞ்சுபோலத்","தண்கயல் வாளிக ணாரி ளம்பிறை","விண்புரு வாரிதழ் கோவை யின்கனி","தன்செய லார்நகை சோதி யின்கதிர் ...... சங்குமேவுங்","கந்தரர் தேமலு மார்ப ரம்பநல்","சந்தன சேறுட னார்க வின்பெறு","கஞ்சுக மாமிட றோதை கொஞ்சிய ...... ரம்பையாரைக்","கண்களி கூரவெ காசை கொண்டவர்","பஞ்சணை மீதுகு லாவி னுந்திரு","கண்களி ராறுமி ராறு திண்புய ...... முங்கொள்வேனே","இந்திர லோகமு ளாரி தம்பெற","சந்திர சூரியர் தேர்ந டந்திட","எண்கிரி சூரர்கு ழாமி றந்திட ...... கண்டவேலா","இந்திரை கேள்வர்பி தாம கன்கதி","ரிந்துச டாதரன் வாச வன்தொழு","தின்புற வேமனு நூல்வி ளம்பிய ...... கந்தவேளே","சிந்துர மால்குவ டார்த னஞ்சிறு","பெண்கள்சி காமணி மோக வஞ்சியர்","செந்தினை வாழ்வளி நாய கொண்குக ...... அன்பரோது","செந்தமிழ் ஞானத டாக மென்சிவ","கங்கைய ளாவு மகாசி தம்பர","திண்சபை மேவும னாச வுந்தர ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women with moon-like earrings, Mandara-like breasts, champak-wreathed hair like cloud, carp eyes, crescent brows, kovai lips and conch necks, coaxing like heavenly dancers, and says that even if he sports with them on the bed in single desire, he will hold Murugan's twelve holy eyes and twelve strong arms. The praise shows Indra's world restored, sun and moon moving again and the Sura hosts dying, and Murugan teaching the mantra to Vishnu, Brahma, the sun, moon, Siva and Indra, with Valli, at the great hall of Chidambaram beside the Sivaganga tank that devotees call a pool of Tamil wisdom.","n":"பஞ்சணை மீது குலாவினும் ... கொள்வேனே: unlike most songs, the plea does not renounce the women but vows to keep the Lord's form even in their embrace. மனு நூல் விளம்பிய: மனு is probably mantra, so this is Murugan as teacher of the gods. செந்தமிழ் ஞான தடாகம் ... சிவகங்கை: the temple tank is praised as a pool of Tamil wisdom.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","thiyaanam","mandhiram","theerththam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:40"},"p:469":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":469,"t":["காய மாய வீடு மீறிய கூடு நந்து","புற்பு தந்த னிற்கு ரம்பை ...... கொண்டுநாளுங்","காசி லாசை தேடி வாழ்வினை நாடி யிந்த்ரி","யப்ர மந்த டித்த லைந்து ...... சிந்தைவேறாய்","வேயி லாய தோள மாமட வார்கள் பங்க","யத்து கொங்கை யுற்றி ணங்கி ...... நொந்திடாதே","வேத கீத போத மோனமெய் ஞான நந்த","முற்றி டின்ப முத்தி யொன்று ...... தந்திடாயோ","மாய வீர தீர சூரர்கள் பாற நின்ற","விக்ர மங்கொள் வெற்பி டந்த ...... செங்கைவேலா","வாகை வேடர் பேதை காதல வேழ மங்கை","யைப்பு ணர்ந்த வெற்ப கந்த ...... செந்தில்வேளே","ஆயும் வேத கீத மேழிசை பாட வஞ்சே","ழுத்த ழங்க முட்ட நின்று ...... துன்றுசோதீ","ஆதி நாத ராடு நாடக சாலை யம்ப","லச்சி தம்ப ரத்த மர்ந்த ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet calls the body a house of illusion, a nest that perishes like a bubble, a hut in which he daily seeks money and livelihood with his senses whirling and his mind astray, and asks not to suffer clinging to bamboo-shouldered women but to be given the single release that is complete in Vedic song, knowledge, silence, true wisdom and bliss. The praise names the vel that split the mountain, Valli's lover, the Vel of Senthil, the light where the Vedas, the seven notes and the five syllables sound, and the lord at the dance hall of the First Lord at Chidambaram.","n":"காயம் மாய வீடு ... நந்து புற்புதம்: the body is a house of maya and a perishing bubble, an image the Naladiyar also uses. The goal is a single release (இன்ப முத்தி ஒன்று) in which Veda, knowledge, silence and bliss are all fulfilled. The song names both Senthil and Chidambaram.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","nilaiyaamai","veedu","mounam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:40"},"p:470":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":470,"t":["அவகுண விரகனை வேதாள ரூபனை","அசடனை மசடனை ஆசார ஈனனை","அகதியை மறவனை ஆதாளி வாயனை ...... அஞ்சுபூதம்","அடைசிய சவடனை மோடாதி மோடனை","அழிகரு வழிவரு வீணாதி வீணனை","அழுகலை யவிசலை ஆறான வூணனை ...... அன்பிலாத","கவடனை விகடனை நானாவி காரனை","வெகுளியை வெகுவித மூதேவி மூடிய","கலியனை அலியனை ஆதேச வாழ்வனை ...... வெம்பிவீழுங்","களியனை யறிவுரை பேணாத மாநுட","கசனியை யசனியை மாபாத னாகிய","கதியிலி தனையடி நாயேனை யாளுவ ...... தெந்தநாளோ","மவுலியி லழகிய பாதாள லோகனு","மரகத முழுகிய காகோத ராஜனு","மநுநெறி யுடன்வளர் சோணாடர் கோனுட ...... னும்பர்சேரும்","மகபதி புகழ்புலி யூர்வாழு நாயகர்","மடமயில் மகிழ்வுற வானாடர் கோவென","மலைமக ளுமைதரு வாழ்வேம னோகர ...... மன்றுளாடும்","சிவசிவ ஹரஹர தேவா நமோநம","தெரிசன பரகதி யானாய் நமோநம","திசையினு மிசையினும் வாழ்வே நமோநம ...... செஞ்சொல்சேருந்","திருதரு கலவி மணாளா நமோநம","திரிபுர மெரிசெய்த கோவே நமோநம","ஜெயஜெய ஹரஹர தேவா சுராதிபர் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet heaps names on himself, man of vile qualities, ghoul-formed, dull and stupid, low in conduct, helpless, cruel, braggart, bound to the five elements, a waste born of a ruined womb, rotten, angry, shrouded by ill fortune, sinner, eunuch, drunkard who heeds no advice, and asks when Murugan will rule this dog of a servant. The praise names the serpent king of the netherworld, the Chola king of Manu's way and Indra praising the lord of Puliyur, and ends in a litany of 'namo nama' to the one who dances in the hall, gives the highest state by his sight, and burned the three cities.","n":"The self-abuse is the longest yet, a run of rhymed names in -னை. The closing litany (சிவசிவ ஹரஹர தேவா நமோநம ... திரிபுரம் எரிசெய்த கோவே நமோநம) gives Murugan the acts of Siva, the dancer and burner of the three cities, or else turns to hail Siva himself; the text does not separate them. The serpent king (பாதாள லோகன்) is probably Patanjali of the Chidambaram legend; several of the abusive names are obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kutram","pedhaimai","thondu","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:40"},"p:471":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":471,"t":["கட்டி முண்டகர பாலி யங்கிதனை","முட்டி யண்டமொடு தாவி விந்துவொலி","கத்த மந்திரவ தான வெண்புரவி ...... மிசையேறிக்","கற்ப கந்தெருவில் வீதி கொண்டுசுடர்","பட்டி மண்டபமு டாடி யிந்துவொடு","கட்டி விந்துபிச காமல் வெண்பொடிகொ ...... டசையாமற்","கட்டு வெம்புரநி றாக விஞ்சைகொடு","தத்து வங்கள்விழ சாடி யெண்குணவர்","சொர்க்கம் வந்துகையு ளாக எந்தைபத ...... முறமேவித்","துக்கம் வெந்துவிழ ஞான முண்டுகுடில்","வச்சி ரங்களென மேனி தங்கமுற","சுத்த கம்புகுத வேத விந்தையொடு ...... புகழ்வேனோ","எட்டி ரண்டுமறி யாத என்செவியி","லெட்டி ரண்டுமிது வாமி லிங்கமென","எட்டி ரண்டும்வெளி யாமொ ழிந்தகுரு ...... முருகோனே","எட்டி ரண்டுதிசை யோட செங்குருதி","யெட்டி ரண்டுமுரு வாகி வஞ்சகர்மெ","லெட்டி ரண்டுதிசை யோர்கள் பொன்றஅயில் ...... விடுவோனே","செட்டி யென்றுசிவ காமி தன்பதியில்","கட்டு செங்கைவளை கூறு மெந்தையிட","சித்த முங்குளிர நாதி வண்பொருளை ...... நவில்வோனே","செட்டி யென்றுவன மேவி யின்பரச","சத்தி யின்செயலி னாளை யன்புருக","தெட்டி வந்துபுலி யூரின் மன்றுள்வளர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A yogic song: the poet asks whether, binding the fire at the root, driving it up to the crown, with bindu and sound crying out, mounting the white horse of mantra and attention, passing through the radiant hall, binding the bindu with the moon so it does not slip, burning the three cities to ash and striking down the tattvas, he will reach his Father's feet with sorrow burnt, eat wisdom, his body hard as diamond and golden, and praise Murugan. The praise calls Murugan the guru who spoke into the poet's ear the 'eight and two' he did not know, saying 'this is the lingam'; who, as a merchant, told the meaning to Siva who once sold bangles as a merchant in Sivakami's town; and who went to the forest as a merchant to win Valli, at the hall in Puliyur.","n":"The plea is a map of the yogic ascent: fire (அங்கி) at the base, the cosmos above, bindu and nada, the moon, the burning of the three cities taken inwardly as the bonds, the tattvas falling, the body turned to diamond (குடில் வச்சிரங்களென). எட்டிரண்டு, 'eight and two', recurs six times; in Tamil numerals அ is eight and உ two, so the guru's teaching may be the letters of Om or Siva and Shakti, while elsewhere the phrase is the ten directions. செட்டி: Siva's bangle-selling and Murugan's courting of Valli are both played as merchant disguises.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kundalini","vindhu","thaththuvam","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:40"},"p:472":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":472,"t":["நஞ்சினைப் போலுமன வஞ்சகக் கோளர்களை","நம்புதற் றீதெனநி ...... னைந்துநாயேன்","நண்புகப் பாதமதி லன்புறத் தேடியுனை","நங்களப் பாசரண ...... மென்றுகூறல்","உன்செவிக் கேறலைகொல் பெண்கள்மெற் பார்வையைகொல்","உன்சொலைத் தாழ்வுசெய்து ...... மிஞ்சுவாரார்","உன்றனக் கேபரமும் என்றனக் கார்துணைவர்","உம்பருக் காவதினின் ...... வந்துதோணாய்","கஞ்சனைத் தாவிமுடி முன்புகுட் டேயமிகு","கண்களிப் பாகவிடு ...... செங்கையோனே","கண்கயற் பாவைகுற மங்கைபொற் றோடழுவு","கஞ்சுகப் பான்மைபுனை ...... பொன்செய்தோளாய்","அஞ்சவெற் பேழுகடல் மங்கநிட் டூரர்குலம்","அந்தரத் தேறவிடு ...... கந்தவேளே","அண்டமுற் பார்புகழு மெந்தைபொற் பூர்புலிசை","அம்பலத் தாடுமவர் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says that, judging it evil to trust deceitful talebearers with poisoned minds, he sought Murugan's feet with love and cried 'our father, refuge', and asks whether this has not reached his ear, or whether his eyes are on his women; no one can defy his word, the burden is his, and the poet has no other helper, so let him come and appear as he did for the gods. The praise shows Murugan knocking Brahma on the head, embracing Valli and shaking the mountains and seas, the lord of Siva who dances in the hall at Puliyur.","n":"உன்செவிக் கேறலைகொல் பெண்கள்மெற் பார்வையைகொல்: the poet chides the Lord, asking whether he is deaf or too busy looking at his wives, an intimacy of complaint. உன்றனக்கே பரமும்: the burden of saving him belongs to the Lord. The knock on Brahma's head (கஞ்சனைத் தாவி முடி முன்பு குட்டே) recalls the Om story without naming Om.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","thunai","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:40"},"p:473":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":473,"t":["செங்கலச ...... முலையார்பால்","சிந்தைபல ...... தடுமாறி","அங்கமிக ...... மெலியாதே","அன்புருக ...... அருள்வாயே","செங்கைபிடி ...... கொடியோனே","செஞ்சொல்தெரி ...... புலவோனே","மங்கையுமை ...... தருசேயே","மன்றுள்வளர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A short song: the poet asks not to waver in mind over women with breasts like red pots until his body wastes, but to be given grace so that he melts in love. The praise names Murugan who holds the banner in his red hand, the poet who knows fine words, the son of Uma, growing in the hall.","n":"செஞ்சொல்தெரி புலவோனே: Murugan is himself the poet who discerns fine speech.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:40"},"p:474":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":474,"t":["கரிய மேக மெனுங்குழ லார்பிறை","சிலைகொள் வாகு வெனும்புரு வார்விழி","கயல்கள் வாளி யெனுஞ்செய லார்மதி ...... துண்டமாதர்","கமுக க்஡ணவர் புயங்கழை யார்தன","மலைக ளாஇணை யுங்குவ டார்கர","கமல வாழை மனுந்தொடை யார்சர ...... சுங்கமாடை","வரிய பாளித முந்துடை யாரிடை","துடிகள் நூலிய லுங்கவி னாரல்குல்","மணமு லாவிய ரம்பையி னார்பொருள் ...... சங்கமாதர்","மயில்கள் போல நடம்புரி வாரியல்","குணமி லாத வியன்செய லார்வலை","மசகி நாயெ னழிந்திட வோவுன ...... தன்புதாராய்","சரியி லாத சயம்பவி யார்முகி","லளக பார பொனின்சடை யாள்சிவை","சருவ லோக சவுந்தரி யாளருள் ...... கந்தவேளே","சதப ணாம குடம்பொடி யாய்விட","அவுணர் சேனை மடிந்திட வேயொரு","தழல்கொள் வேலை யெறிந்திடு சேவக ...... செம்பொன்வாகா","அரிய மேனி யிலங்கை யிராவணன்","முடிகள் வீழ சரந்தொடு மாயவன்","அகில மீரெழு முண்டவன் மாமரு ...... கண்டரோதும்","அழகு சோபித அங்கொளு மானன","விபுதை மோகி குறிஞ்சியின் வாழ்வளி","அருள்கொ டாடி சிதம்பர மேவிய ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet runs through a woman's beauty, cloud hair, bow brows, carp-arrow eyes, moon forehead, areca neck, bamboo arms, mountain breasts, lotus hands, plantain thighs, drum waist and fine silk, and calls such women, dancing like peacocks, without good qualities; he asks whether he must be caught in their net and perish, and asks for Murugan's love. The praise names Sivai, the self-born, the beauty of all worlds, as his mother, the fiery vel thrown at the asura army, Vishnu who killed Ravana and holds the fourteen worlds as his uncle, and Devayanai and Valli, at Chidambaram.","n":"The beauty is catalogued only to be set against இயல் குணமிலாத வியன் செயலார், women of display with no good nature. பொருள் சங்க மாதர் may mean women joined for money, but the division is unsure.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kaamam","azhagu","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:40"},"p:475":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":475,"t":["கூந்த லாழவி ரிந்து சரிந்திட","காந்து மாலைகு லைந்து பளிங்கிட","கூர்ந்த வாள்விழி கெண்டை கலங்கிட ...... கொங்கைதானுங்","கூண்க ளாமென பொங்கந லம்பெறு","காந்தள் மேனிம ருங்குது வண்டிட","கூர்ந்த ஆடைகு லைந்துபு ரண்டிர ...... சங்கள்பாயச்","சாந்து வேர்வின ழிந்து மணந்தப","வோங்க வாகில்க லந்து முகங்கொடு","தான்ப லாசுளை யின்சுவை கண்டித ...... ழுண்டுமோகந்","தாம்பு றாமயி லின்குரல் கொஞ்சிட","வாஞ்சை மாதரு டன்புள கங்கொடு","சார்ந்து நாயென ழிந்துவி ழுந்துடல் ...... மங்குவேனோ","தீந்த தோதக தந்தன திந்திமி","ஆண்ட பேரிகை துந்துமி சங்கொடு","சேர்ந்த பூரிகை பம்பை தவண்டைகள் ...... பொங்குசூரைச்","சேண்சு லாமகு டம்பொடி தம்பட","வோங்க வேழ்கட லுஞ்சுவ றங்கையில்","சேந்த வேலது கொண்டு நடம்பயில் ...... கந்தவேளே","மாந்த ணாருவ னங்குயில் கொஞ்சிட","தேங்கு வாழைக ரும்புகள் விஞ்சிடு","வான்கு லாவுசி தம்பரம் வந்தமர் ...... செங்கைவேலா","மாண்ப்ர காசத னங்கிரி சுந்தர","மேய்ந்த நாயகி சம்பைம ருங்குபொன்","வார்ந்த ரூபிகு றம்பெண் வணங்கிய ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes lovemaking in detail, hair loosening, garlands crushed, eyes troubled, sandal paste washed by sweat, lips tasted like jackfruit segments and voices cooing like doves and peacocks, and asks whether he, a dog, must be ruined and fall with his body wasting. The praise shows the battle in drum syllables, Sura's crown smashed and the seven seas dried by the vel, Murugan dancing, and Chidambaram of mango groves, cuckoos, plantain and cane, where Sivakami and the hunter girl bow to him.","n":"The plea is only a question at the end of the scene (நாயென அழிந்து விழுந்து உடல் மங்குவேனோ): the ruin is the body's fading.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","kaamam","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:40"},"p:476":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":476,"t":["அத்த னன்னை யில்லம் வைத்த சொன்னம் வெள்ளி","அத்தை நண்ணு செல்வ ...... ருடனாகி","அத்து பண்ணு கல்வி சுற்ற மென்னு மல்ல","லற்று நின்னை வல்ல ...... படிபாடி","முத்த னென்ன வல்லை யத்த னென்ன வள்ளி","முத்த னென்ன வுள்ள ...... முணராதே","முட்ட வெண்மை யுள்ள பட்ட னெண்மை கொள்ளு","முட்ட னிங்ங னைவ ...... தொழியாதோ","தித்தி மன்னு தில்லை நிர்த்தர் கண்ணி னுள்ளு","தித்து மன்னு பிள்ளை ...... முருகோனே","சித்தி மன்னு செய்ய சத்தி துன்னு கைய","சித்ர வண்ண வல்லி ...... யலர்சூடும்","பத்த ருண்மை சொல்லு ளுற்ற செம்மல் வெள்ளி","பத்தர் கன்னி புல்லு ...... மணிமார்பா","பச்சை வன்னி யல்லி செச்சை சென்னி யுள்ள","பச்சை மஞ்ஞை வல்ல ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks that, free of the trouble called father, mother, house, stored gold and silver, the rich who seek them, learning and kin, he sing Murugan as best he can, and asks whether this complete fool's withering will not end while his heart fails to know him as the freed one, the swift one, the Lord, Valli's beloved. The praise names Murugan the child born from the eye of the dancer of Thillai, holding the red vel of power, worn by devotees who speak the truth, and embracing Devayanai, with green vanni, lilies and vetchi on his head and the green peacock.","n":"கல்வி stands in the list of bonds with gold, silver and kin: learning too is a trouble (அல்லல்) to be shed before one can sing. Each line pair turns on repeated syllables (அத்தன், அத்தை, அத்து; முத்தன், முத்தன், முட்டன்), so several words (அத்து, பட்டன், முத்தன்) can be read more than one way.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pattru","pedhaimai","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:40"},"p:477":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":477,"t":["இருள்காட்டு செவ்வி ததிகாட்டி வில்லி","னுதல்காட்டி வெல்லு ...... மிருபாண","இயல்காட்டு கொல்கு வளைகாட்டி முல்லை","நகைகாட்டு வல்லி ...... யிடைமாதர்","மருள்காட்டி நல்கு ரவுகாட்டு மில்ல","இடுகாட்டி னெல்லை ...... நடவாத","வழிகாட்டி நல்ல றிவுகாட்டி மெல்ல","வினை வாட்டி யல்லல் ...... செயலாமோ","தெருள்காட்டு தொல்லை மறைகாட்டு மல்லல்","மொழிகாட்டு தில்லை ...... யிளையோனே","தினைகாட்டு கொல்லை வழிகாட்ட வல்ல","குறவாட்டி புல்லு ...... மணிமார்பா","அருள்காட்டு கல்வி நெறிகாட்டு செல்வ","அடல்காட்டு வல்ல ...... சுரர்கோபா","அடிபோற்றி யல்லி முடிசூட்ட வல்ல","அடியார்க்கு நல்ல ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of women who show dark hair, bow brows, eyes like killing lilies, jasmine smiles and creeper waists, who show delusion and then show poverty, and asks whether Murugan, who can show the path that does not end at the burning ground, show good understanding and slowly wither karma, should let him suffer. The praise names Murugan the young lord of Thillai, which shows clarity, the ancient Veda and rich speech, Valli of the millet field, the one who shows grace and the path of learning, and the good lord of devotees who praise his feet.","n":"The verb காட்டு, to show, runs through every line: the women show their charms and then poverty (நல்குரவு காட்டும்), the Lord shows the path past the cremation ground (இடுகாட்டின் எல்லை நடவாத வழி), and Thillai shows wisdom. The play joins காட்டு with இடுகாடு, the burning ground.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","arivu","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:40"},"p:478":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":478,"t":["முல்லைமலர் போலுமுத் தாயுதிர்ந் தானநகை","வள்ளைகொடி போலுநற் காதிலங் காடுகுழை","முல்லைமலர் மாலைசுற் றாடுகொந் தாருகுழ ...... லலைபோதம்","மொள்குசிலை வாணுதற் பார்வையம் பானகயல்","கிள்ளைகுர லாரிதழ்ப் பூவெனும் போதுமுக","முன்னல்கமு கார்களத் தோய்சுணங் காயமுலை ...... மலையானை","வல்லகுவ டாலிலைப் போலுசந் தானவயி","றுள் ளதுகில் நூலிடைக் காமபண் டார அல் குல்","வழ்ழைதொடை யார்மலர்க் காலணிந் தாடுபரி ...... புரவோசை","மல்லிசலி யாடபட் டாடைகொண் டாடமயல்","தள்ளுநடை யோடுசற் றேமொழிந் தாசைகொடு","வல்லவர்கள் போலபொற் சூறைகொண் டார்கள்மய ...... லுறவாமோ","அல்லல்வினை போகசத் தாதிவிண் டோ டநய","வுள்ளமுற வாகவைத் தாளுமெந் தாதைமகி","ழள்ளமைய ஞானவித் தோதுகந் தாகுமர ...... முருகோனே","அன்னநடை யாள்குறப் பாவைபந் தாடுவிரல்","என்னுடைய தாய்வெண்முத் தார்கடம் பாடுகுழல்","அன்னைவலி சேர்தனக் கோடிரண் டானவளி ...... மணவாளா","செல்லுமுக ஏழ்கடற் பாழிவிண் டோ டதிர","வல்லசுரர் சேனைபட் டேமடிந் தேகுருதி","செல்லதிசை யோடுவிட் டாடுசிங் காரமுக ...... வடிவேலா","தெள்ளுதமிழ் பாடியிட் டாசைகொண் டாடசசி","வல்லியொடு கூடிதிக் கோர்கள்கொண் டாடஇயல்","தில்லைநகர் கோபுரத் தேமகிழ்ந் தேகுலவு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet runs through a woman's beauty from smile to anklets, her walk swaying in silk, and calls such women plunderers of gold like skilled thieves, and asks whether infatuation with them is fitting. The praise names Murugan who, so that troubling deeds and the gunas flee, holds the poet's heart as his own and rules it, who taught the seed of wisdom to delight his father, Valli whom the poet calls his own mother, the vel, and the lord who rejoices at the tower of Thillai with Devayanai, sung in clear Tamil.","n":"பொற் சூறை கொண்டார்கள்: the women plunder gold like raiders. எந்தாதை மகிழ ... ஞானவித்து ஓது: Murugan teaching his father the seed of wisdom, the Om story without the word. என்னுடைய தாய்: Valli is called the poet's mother.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","mayal","guru","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:40"},"p:479":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":479,"t":["அடப்பக் கம்பிடித்துத் தோளொடு தோள்பொர","வளைத்துச் செங்கரத்திற் சீரொடு பாவொடு","அணுக்கிச் செந்துணுக்கிற் கோவித ழுறல்க ...... ளதுகோதி","அணிப்பொற் பங்கயத்துப் பூண்முலை மேகலை","நெகிழ்த்துப் பஞ்சரித்துத் தாபண மேயென","அருட்டிக் கண்சிமிட்டிப் பேசிய மாதர்க ...... ளுறவோடே","படிச்சித் தங்களித்துத் தான்மிக மாயைகள்","படித்துப் பண்பயிற்றிக் காதல்கள் மேல்கொள","பசப்பிப் பின்பிணக்கைக் கூறிய வீணிக ...... ளவமாயப்","பரத்தைக் குண்டுணர்த்துத் தோதக பேதைகள்","பழிக்குட் சஞ்சரித்துப் போடிடு மூடனை","பரத்துற் றண்பதத்துப் போதக் மீதென ...... அருள்தாராய்","தடக்கைத் தண்டெடுத்துச் சூரரை வீரரை","நொறுக்கிப் பொன்றவிட்டுத் தூளெழ நீறெழ","தகர்த்துப் பந்தடித்துச் சூடிய தோரண ...... கலைவீரா","தகட்டுப் பொன்சுவட்டுப் பூவணை மேடையில்","சமைப்பித் தங்கொருத்திக் கோதில மாமயில்","தனிப்பொற் பைம்புனத்திற் கோகில மாவளி ...... மணவாளா","திடத்திற் றிண்பொருப்பைத் தோள்கொடு சாடிய","அரக்கத் திண்குலத்தைச் சூறைகொள் வீரிய","திருப்பொற் பங்கயத்துக் கேசவர் மாயவர் ...... அறியாமல்","திமித்தத் திந்திமித்தத் தோவென ஆடிய","சமர்த்தர்ப் பொன்புவிக்குட் டேவர்க ணாயக","திருச்சிற் றம்பலத்துட் கோபுர மேவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women who grab a man's side, press shoulder to shoulder, bite his lips, loosen their ornaments, say 'give money', wink and talk, coax with feigned love and then pick quarrels, and calls himself the fool who wanders in the blame of these tricksters; he asks for grace to reach the supreme and Murugan's feet. The praise shows Murugan crushing the Suras with a club, Devayanai on a golden couch and Valli of the millet field, and Siva who danced to the drum's beat beyond the knowing of Brahma and Vishnu, at the tower of the Tiruchitrambalam.","n":"தாபணமே என: the women's demand for money is quoted in their own words. பசப்பிப் பின் பிணக்கைக் கூறிய: feigned love followed by the made-up quarrel is the courtesan's way of ending a spent man's welcome. The plea's last words (பரத்துற் றண்பதத்துப் போதக் மீதென) are garbled.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:41"},"p:480":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":480,"t":["அக்குப் பீளைமு ளாவிளை மூளையொ","டுப்புக் காய்பனி நிர்மயிர் தோல்குடி","லப்புச் சீபுழு வோடடை யார்தசை ...... யுறமேவி","அத்திப் பால்பல நாடிகு ழாயள்வ","ழுப்புச் சார்வல மேவிளை யூளைகொ","ளச்சுத் தோல்குடி லாமதி லேபொறி ...... விரகாளர்","சுக்கத் தாழ்கட லேசுக மாமென","புக்கிட் டாசைபெ ணாசைம ணாசைகள்","தொக்குத் தீவினை யூழ்வினை காலமொ ...... டதனாலே","துக்கத் தேபர வாமல்ச தாசிவ","முத்திக் கேசுக மாகப ராபர","சொர்க்கப் பூமியி லேறிட வேபத ...... மருள்வாயே","தக்கத் தோகிட தாகிட தீகிட","செக்கச் சேகண தாகண தோகண","தத்தத் தானன டீகுட டாடுடு ...... வெனதாளந்","தத்திச் சூரர்கு ழாமொடு தேர்பரி","கெட்டுக் கேவல மாய்கடல் மூழ்கிட","சத்திக் கேயிரை யாமென வேவிடு ...... கதிர்வேலா","திக்கத் தோகண தாவென வேபொரு","சொச்சத் தாதையர் தாமென வேதிரு","செக்கர்ப் பாதம தேபதி யாசுதி ...... யவைபாடச்","செப்பொற் பீலியு லாமயில் மாமிசை","பக்கத் தேகுற மாதொடு சீர்பெறு","தெற்குக் கோபுர வாசலில் மேவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet calls the body a hut of bone, rheum, marrow, brain, phlegm, water, hair and skin, crammed with pus, worms and flesh, its nerves like tubes, where the tricksters of the senses dwell; he says the desires for women and for land, with evil and fated deeds and time, have gathered in it, and asks not to spread in sorrow but to be given the feet that rise to the release of Sadasiva and the supreme world. The praise shows the battle in drum syllables, the Suras drowned with their chariots and horses as food for the vel, Siva dancing, and Murugan on the peacock with Valli at the southern tower.","n":"ஆசை பெணாசை மணாசைகள்: the familiar triad of cravings (woman, land, gold) appears with gold folded into ஆசை. தீவினை ஊழ்வினை காலமொடு: deed, fated deed and time are named together as the forces that keep him in sorrow.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","avaa","vinai","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:41"},"p:481":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":481,"t":["ஆரத் தோடணி மார்பிணை யானைகள்","போருக் காமென மாமுலை யேகொடு","ஆயத் தூசினை மேவிய நூலிடை ...... மடமாதர்","ஆலைக் கோதினி லீரமி லாமன","நேசத் தோடுற வானவர் போலுவர்","ஆருக் கேபொரு ளாமென வேநினை ...... வதனாலே","காருக் கேநிக ராகிய வோதிய","மாழைத் தோடணி காதொடு மோதிய","காலத் தூதர்கை வேலெனு நீள்விழி ...... வலையாலே","காதற் சாகர மூழ்கிய காமுகர்","மேலிட் டேயெறி கீலிகள் நீலிகள்","காமத் தோடுற வாகையி லாவருள் ...... புரிவாயே","சூரர்க் கேயொரு கோளரி யாமென","நீலத் தோகைம யூரம தேறிய","தூளிக் கேகடல் தூரநி சாசரர் ...... களமீதே","சோரிக் கேவெகு ரூபம தாவடு","தானத் தானன தானன தானன","சூழிட் டேபல சோகுக ளாடவெ ...... பொரும்வேலா","வீரத் தால்வல ராவண னார்முடி","போகத் தானொரு வாளியை யேவிய","மேகத் தேநிக ராகிய மேனியன் ...... மருகோனே","வேதத் தோன்முத லாகிய தேவர்கள்","பூசித் தேதொழ வாழ்புலி யூரினில்","மேலைக் கோபுர வாசலில் மேவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women whose breasts are like elephants ready for war, with hearts as dry as the cane waste from the mill, who seem like lovers while thinking only of whose wealth they can take, and whose long eyes are a net like the spears of Death's messengers; he asks for grace not to be joined in lust with these tricksters who cast down the lustful drowned in love's sea. The praise shows Murugan as a lion against the Suras on the blue peacock, Vishnu who shot Ravana's crowns as his uncle, and Brahma and the gods worshipping at the western tower in Puliyur.","n":"ஆலைக் கோதினில் ஈரமிலா மனம்: their hearts have no more moisture than the crushed cane thrown from the mill. ஆருக்கே பொருளாம் என நினைவதனாலே: their thought is only whose money it will be. The eyes are காலத் தூதர் கை வேல், Death's messengers' spears, so the courtesan and Death are drawn together.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","selvam","vanjam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:41"},"p:482":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":482,"t":["காதைக் காதி மெத்த மோதிக் கேள்வி யற்ற","காமப் பூச லிட்டு ...... மதியாதே","காரொத் தேய்நி றத்த வோதிக் காவனத்தி","னீழற் கேத ருக்கி ...... விளையாடிச்","சேதித் தேக ருத்தை நேருற்றேபெ ருத்த","சேலொத் தேவ ருத்தும் ...... விழிமானார்","தேமற் பார வெற்பில் மூழ்கித் தாப மிக்க","தீமைக் காவி தப்ப ...... நெறிதாராய்","மாதைக் காத லித்து வேடக் கான கத்து","வாசத் தாள்சி வப்ப ...... வருவோனே","வாரிக் கேயொ ளித்த மாயச் சூரை வெட்டி","மாளப் போர்தொ லைத்த ...... வடிவேலா","வீதித் தேர்ந டத்து தூளத் தால ருக்கன்","வீரத் தேர்ம றைத்த ...... புலியூர்வாழ்","மேலைக் கோபு ரத்து மேவிக் கேள்வி மிக்க","வேதத் தோர்து தித்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women whose eyes reach to the ears and wound like carp, with cloud-dark hair, who play proudly in the shade of groves and stir lust that heeds no counsel, and asks to be given the path that escapes the burning evil of drowning in their breasts. The praise shows Murugan walking through the hunters' forest for love of Valli until his fragrant feet reddened, cutting down Sura who hid in the sea, and Puliyur where the dust of chariots in the streets hides the sun's chariot, at the western tower praised by learned Vedic priests.","n":"வாசத் தாள் சிவப்ப வருவோனே: the god's feet redden from walking to Valli, a tender picture against the battle praise. கேள்வி அற்ற காமப் பூசல்: lust is a quarrel deaf to instruction.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kan","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:41"},"p:483":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":483,"t":["கொள்ளை யாசைக் காரிகள் பாதக","வல்ல மாயக் காரிகள் சூறைகள்","கொள்ளும் ஆயக் காரிகள் வீணிகள் ...... விழியாலே","கொல்லும் லீலைக் காரிகள் யாரையும்","வெல்லு மோகக் காரிகள் சூதுசொல்","கொவ்வை வாய்நிட் டூரிகள் மேல்விழு ...... மவர்போலே","உள்ள நோவைத் தேயுற வாடியர்","அல்லை நேரோப் பாமன தோஷிகள்","உள்வி ரோதக் காரிகள் மாயையி ...... லுழல்நாயேன்","உய்ய வேபொற் றோள்களும் ஆறிரு","கையு நீபத் தார்முக மாறுமுன்","உள்ள ஞானப் போதமு நீதர ...... வருவாயே","கள்ள மாயத் தாருகன் மாமுடி","துள்ள நீலத் தோகையின் மீதொரு","கையின் வேல்தொட் டேவிய சேவக ...... முருகோனே","கல்லி லேபொற் றாள்பட வேயது","நல்ல ரூபத் தேவர கானிடை","கெளவை தீரப் போகுமி ராகவன் ...... மருகோனே","தெள்ளி யேமுற் றீரமு னோதிய","சொல்வ ழாமற் றானொரு வானுறு","செல்வி மார்பிற் பூஷண மாயணை ...... மணவாளா","தெள்ளு மேனற் சூழ்புன மேவிய","வள்ளி வேளைக் காரம னோகர","தில்லை மேலைக் கோபுர மேவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet lists women: greedy plunderers, skilled deceivers, collectors who take a toll, killers with their eyes, gamblers in speech, coral-lipped and cruel, who pain the heart while seeming close and hide enmity within, and asks that he, a dog wandering in maya, be saved by Murugan coming with golden shoulders, twelve hands, the kadamba garland and six faces, and giving inner wisdom. The praise shows the vel thrown at Taraka, Rama whose golden foot turned a stone into a fair form as he went to the forest, Devayanai joined as promised, and Valli, at the western tower of Thillai.","n":"சூறைகள் கொள்ளும் ஆயக்காரிகள்: the women are toll collectors who plunder, a picture of love as a tax. கல்லிலே பொற்றாள் படவே அது நல்ல ரூபத்தே வர: the stone that became a woman at Rama's touch is Ahalya, named only by the deed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:41"},"p:484":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":484,"t":["தாது மாமலர் முடியா லேபத","றாத நூபுர அடியா லேகர","தாள மாகிய நொடியா லேமடி ...... பிடியாலே","சாடை பேசிய வகையா லேமிகு","வாடை பூசிய நகையா லேபல","தாறு மாறுசொல் மிகையா லேயன ...... நடையாலே","மோதி மீறிய முலையா லேமுலை","மீதி லேறிய கலையா லேவெகு","மோடி நாணய விலையா லேமயல் ...... தருமானார்","மோக வாரிதி தனிலே நாடொறு","மூழ்கு வேனுன தடியா ராகிய","மோன ஞானிக ளுடனே சேரவு ...... மருள்வாயே","காத லாயருள் புரிவாய் நான்மறை","மூல மேயென வுடனே மாகரி","காண நேர்வரு திருமால் நாரணன் ...... மருகோனே","காதல் மாதவர் வலமே சூழ்சபை","நாத னார்தம திடமே வாழ்சிவ","காம நாயகி தருபா லாபுலி ...... சையில்வாழ்வே","வேத நூன்முறை வழுவா மேதினம்","வேள்வி யாலெழில் புநன்மு வாயிர","மேன்மை வேதியர் மிகவே பூசனை ...... புரிகோவே","வீறு சேர்வரை யரசாய் மேவிய","மேரு மால்வரை யெனநீள் கோபுர","மேலை வாயிலின் மயில்மீ தேறிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet lists the arts by which women give infatuation, flowered hair, anklet feet, rhythm of hands, sly talk, scented smiles, contrary speech, swan gait, breasts and their cloth, and their haughty price in coin, and says he sinks daily in their sea of lust; he asks for grace to join Murugan's devotees, the silent jnanis. The praise names Vishnu who came when the elephant cried 'root of the four Vedas' as his uncle, Sivakami as his mother, the three thousand Vedic priests of Puliyur who worship daily by sacrifice, and Murugan on the peacock at the western tower, tall as Meru.","n":"The plea's remedy is company: மோன ஞானிகளுடனே சேரவும், to join the silent knowers. மேன்மை மூவாயிர வேதியர்: the three thousand Brahmins of Thillai, the temple's hereditary priests. The elephant's cry is the Gajendra story, as in 25.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","mayal","adiyaar","mounam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:41"},"p:485":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":485,"t":["எலுப்புத் தோல்மயிர் நாடிகு ழாமிடை","இறுக்குச் சீபுழு வோடடை மூளைகள்","இரத்தச் சாகர நீர்மல மேவிய ...... கும்பியோடை","இளைப்புச் சோகைகள் வாதம் விலாவலி","உளைப்புச் சூலையொ டேவலு வாகிய","இரைப்புக் கேவல மூலவி யாதியொ ...... டண்டவாதங்","குலைப்புக் காய்கனல் நீரிழி வீளையொ","டளைப்புக் காதடை கூனல்வி சூசிகை","குருட்டுக் கால்முட மூமையு ளூடறு ...... கண்டமாலை","குடிப்புக் கூனமி தேசத மாமென","எடுத்துப் பாழ்வினை யாலுழல் நாயெனு","னிடத்துத் தாள்பெற ஞானச தாசிவ ...... அன்புதாராய்","கெலிக்கப் போர்பொரு சூரர்கு ழாமுமி","ழிரத்தச் சேறெழ தேர்பரி யாளிகள்","கெடுத்திட் டேகடல் சூர்கிரி தூள்பட ...... கண்டவேலா","கிளர்ப்பொற் றோளிச ராசர மேவியெ","யசைத்துப் பூசைகொள் ஆயிப ராபரி","கிழப்பொற் காளைமெ லேறுமெ நாயகி ...... பங்கின்மேவும்","வலித்துத் தோள்மலை ராவண னானவன்","எடுத்தப் போதுடல் கீழ்விழ வேசெய்து","மகிழ்ப்பொற் பாதசி வாயந மோஅர ...... சம்புபாலா","மலைக்கொப் பாமுலை யாள்குற மாதினை","அணைத்துச் சீர்புலி யூர்பர மாகிய","வடக்குக் கோபுர வாசலில் மேவிய ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet catalogues the body, bone, skin, hair, nerves, pus, worms, brain, a sea of blood, a pot of filth, and its diseases, fatigue, anaemia, wind, rib pain, colic, asthma, hernia, fever, diabetes, phlegm, deafness, hunchback, cholera, blindness, lameness, dumbness and scrofula, and confesses that, taking this flawed dwelling as permanent, he wanders by ruinous deeds; he asks for the feet, wisdom and love. The praise names the Goddess who rides the old golden bull, Siva who pressed Ravana down under his foot when he lifted the mountain, and Valli, at the northern tower in Puliyur.","n":"குடிப்புக்கு ஊனம் இதே சதமாம் என எடுத்து: the fault is not having a diseased body but taking it for a lasting home. Siva's name is given in the five syllables (சிவாயநமோ).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","noy","vinai","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:41"},"p:486":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":486,"t":["நீல மாமுகில் போலும் வார்குழ","லார்கள் மாலைகு லாவ வேல்கணை","நீள வாள்விழி பார்வை காதிரு ...... குழையாட","நீடு மார்பணி யாட வோடிய","கோடு போலிணை யாட நூலிடை","நேச பாளித சோலை மாமயி ...... லெனவேகிக்","காலி னு஡புர வோசை கோவென","ஆடி மால்கொடு நாணி யேவியர்","காய மோடணு பாகு பால்மொழி ...... விலைமாதர்","காத லாயவ ரோடு பாழ்வினை","மூழ்கி யேழ்நர காழு மூடனை","காரிர் பாருமை யாசி வாபத ...... மருள்வாயே","கோல மாமயி லேறி வார்குழை","யாட வேல்கொடு வீர வார் கழல்","கோடி கோடிடி யோசை போல்மிக ...... மெருதூளாய்க்","கோடு கோவென ஆழி பாடுகள்","தீவு தாடசு ரார்கு ழாமொடு","கூள மாகவி ணோர்கள் வாழ்வுற ...... விடும்வேலா","நாலு வேதமு டாடு வேதனை","யீண கேசவ னார்ச கோதரி","நாதர் பாகம்வி டாள்சி காமணி ...... உமைபாலா","ஞான பூமிய தான பேர்புலி","யூரில் வாழ்தெய்வ யானை மானொடு","நாலு கோபுர வாசல் மேவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women with dark hair, swinging earrings, dancing ornaments and ringing anklets, who walk like peacocks and speak words sweet as milk, women for a price, and calls himself the fool who, loving them, drowns in ruinous deeds and sinks into the seven hells; he asks for Siva's son's feet. The praise shows Murugan on the peacock with the vel, the seas and islands shaken and the asuras reduced to rubbish, Uma, Vishnu's sister who never leaves Siva's side, and Murugan with Devayanai at the four towers of Puliyur, land of wisdom.","n":"விலைமாதர்: the women are named by their price. ஞான பூமியதான பேர்புலியூர்: Chidambaram is called the land of wisdom. The plea's last line (காரிர் பாருமை யாசி வாபத) is garbled.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["varaivinmagalir","naragam","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:41"},"p:487":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":487,"t":["வாத பித்தமொடு சூலை விப்புருதி","யேறு கற்படுவ னீளை பொக்கிருமல்","மாலை புற்றெழுத லு஡சல் பற்சனியொ ...... டந்திமாலை","மாச டைக்குருடு காத டைப்பு செவி","டூமை கெட்டவலி மூல முற்றுதரு","மாலை யுற்றதொணு றாறு தத்துவர்க ...... ளுண்டகாயம்","வேத வித்துபரி கோல முற்றுவிளை","யாடு வித்தகட லோட மொய்த்தபல","வேட மிட்டுபொரு ளாசை பற்றியுழல் ...... சிங்கியாலே","வீடு கட்டிமய லாசை பட்டுவிழ","வோசை கெட்டுமடி யாமல் முத்திபெற","வீட ளித்துமயி லாடு சுத்தவெளி ...... சிந்தியாதோ","ஓத அத்திமுகி லோடு சர்ப்பமுடி","நீறு பட்டலற சூர வெற்பவுண","ரோடு பட்டுவிழ வேலை விட்டபுக ...... ழங்கிவேலா","ஓந மச்சிவய சாமி சுத்தஅடி","யார்க ளுக்குமுப காரி பச்சையுமை","ஓர்பு றத்தருள்சி காம ணிக்கடவுள் ...... தந்தசேயே","ஆதி கற்பகவி நாய கர்க்குபிற","கான பொற்சரவ ணாப ரப்பிரம","னாதி யுற்றபொருள் ஓது வித்தமைய ...... றிந்தகோவே","ஆசை பெற்றகுற மாதை நித்தவன","மேவி சுத்தமண மாடி நற்புலியு","ராட கப்படிக கோபு ரத்தின்மகிழ் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet lists the body's diseases, wind, bile, colic, abscess, stone, phlegm, cough, scrofula, blindness, deafness, dumbness, piles and dropsy, calls it a body eaten by the ninety-six tattvas, and confesses wandering in many guises, gripped by the poison of desire for wealth; he asks whether, instead of building houses, falling into infatuation and dying with his voice gone, his mind will not think of the pure space where the peacock dances, which gives release. The praise names Siva of the five syllables, helper of pure devotees, with green Uma, as Murugan's father, Murugan as the younger brother of Vinayaka who taught the supreme one the primal meaning, and the lord of Valli at the golden crystal tower of Puliyur.","n":"தொண்ணூற்றாறு தத்துவர்கள் உண்ட காயம்: the ninety-six tattvas are pictured as eaters of the body. மயிலாடு சுத்தவெளி: release is a pure space where the peacock dances, the Lord's vehicle become the image of the goal. பரப்பிரமன் ஆதி உற்ற பொருள் ஓதுவித்த: the Om story, with Siva as the supreme one taught, though the phrase may be divided otherwise.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["noy","thaththuvam","veedu","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:41"},"p:488":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":488,"t":["சுரும்புற்ற பொழில்தோறும் விரும்புற்ற குயில்கூவ","துரந்துற்ற குளிர்வாடை ...... யதனாலுந்","துலங்குற்ற மருவாளி விரைந்துற்ற படியால","தொடர்ந்துற்று வருமாதர் ...... வசையாலும்","அரும்புற்ற மலர்மேவு செழுங்கொற்ற அணையாலு","மடைந்திட்ட விடைமேவு ...... மணியாலும்","அழிந்துற்ற மடமானை யறிந்தற்ற மதுபேணி","அசைந்துற்ற மதுமாலை ...... தரவேணும்","கருங்கொற்ற மதவேழ முனிந்துற்ற கலைமேவி","கரந்துற்ற மடமானி ...... னுடனேசார்","கரும்புற்ற வயல்சூழ பெரும்பற்ற புலியூரில்","களம்பற்றி நடமாடு ...... மரன்வாழ்வே","இருந்துற்று மலர்பேணி யிடும்பத்தர் துயர்தீர","இதம்பெற்ற மயிலேறி ...... வருகொவே","இனந்துற்ற வருசூர னுருண்டிட்டு விழவேல்கொ","டெறிந்திட்டு விளையாடு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"In the voice of a lovesick girl's companion or mother, the singer says the girl, an innocent doe, is ruined by the cuckoos calling in every grove, the cold north wind, Kama's fragrant arrows, the gossip of women who follow her, the flower bed and the bell of the returning bull, and asks Murugan, knowing her plight, to give her his honeyed garland. The praise shows the elephant that frightened Valli into Murugan's arms, Siva who dances in the hall at Perumpatrappuliyur among cane fields, Murugan who comes on the peacock to end devotees' sorrow, and the vel that rolled Sura over.","n":"This is akam poetry with no confession of the poet's own: the signs of evening (the bull's bell, the cold wind) and the women's gossip (மாதர் வசை) are classical. The garland asked for (மது மாலை) is the lover's token that would make the love public.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","maalai","alar"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:41"},"p:489":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":489,"t":["இணங்கித் தட்பொடு பால்மொழி பேசிகள்","மணந்திட் டுச்சுக மாய்விளை யாடிகள்","இளஞ்சொற் செப்பிகள் சாதனை வீணிகள் ...... கடிதாகும்","இடும்பைப் பற்றிய தாமென மேயினர்","பெருஞ்சொற் பித்தளை தானும்வை யாதவர்","இரும்பிற் பற்றிய கூர்விழி மாதர்கள் ...... எவரேனும்","பணஞ்சுற் றிக்கொளு பாயவு தாரிகள்","மணங்கட் டுக்குழல் வாசனை வீசிகள்","பலஞ்செப் பித்தர மீளழை யாதவர் ...... அவரோடே","பதந்துய்த் துக்கொடு தீமைய மாநர","கடைந்திட் டுச்சவ மாகிவி டாதுன","பதம்பற் றிப்புக ழானது கூறிட ...... அருள்வாயே","வணங்கச் சித்தமி லாதஇ ராவணன்","சிரம்பத் துக்கெட வாளிக டாவியெ","மலங்கப் பொக்கரை யீடழி மாதவன் ...... மருகோனே","மதம்பட் டுப்பொரு சூரபன் மாதியர்","குலங்கொட் டத்திகல் கூறிய மோடரை","வளைந்திட் டுக்கள மீதினி லேகொல ...... விடும்வேலா","பிணம்பற் றிக்கழு கோடுபல் கூளிகள்","பிடுங்கிக் கொத்திட வேயம ராடியெ","பிளந்திட் டுப்பல மாமயி லேறிய ...... முருகோனே","பிரிந்திட் டுப்பரி வாகிய ஞானிகள்","சிலம்பத் தக்கழல் சேரவெ நாடிடு","பெரும்பற் றப்புலி யூர்தனில் மேவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women who talk with milk-sweet words, play at pleasure, are skilled in schemes and wind in money with their tricks, and will not call a man back unless he pays what they name, and asks that, rather than enjoying them, falling into evil and great hell and becoming a corpse, he grasp Murugan's feet and speak his praise. The praise names Vishnu who cut Ravana's ten heads as his uncle, the vel that killed Surapadman's clan, the battlefield where vultures and ghouls tear corpses, and Murugan on the peacock at Perumpatrappuliyur, where jnanis seek his anklet feet.","n":"பலஞ் செப்பித் தர மீள அழையாதவர்: they call a man back only if he pays the sum they name. The plea sets two ends side by side: becoming a corpse in hell (நரகடைந்திட்டுச் சவமாகி) or holding the feet and speaking praise.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","selvam","naragam","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:41"},"p:490":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":490,"t":["விடுங்கைக் கொத்தக டாவுடை யானிட","மடங்கிக் கைச்சிறை யானஅ நேகமும்","விழுங்கப் பட்டற வேயற லோதியர் ...... விழியாலே","விரும்பத் தக்கன போகமு மோகமும்","விளம்பத் தக்கன ஞானமு மானமும்","வெறுஞ்சுத் தச்சல மாய்வெளி யாயுயிர் ...... விடுநாளில்","இடுங்கட் டைக்கிரை யாயடி யேனுடல்","கிடந்திட் டுத்தம ரானவர் கோவென","இடங்கட் டிச்சுடு காடுபு காமுன ...... மனதாலே","இறந்திட் டுப்பெற வேகதி யாயினும்","இருந்திட் டுப்பெற வேமதி யாயினும்","இரண்டிற் றக்கதொ ரூதியம் நீதர ...... இசைவாயே","கொடுங்கைப் பட்டம ராமர மேழுடன்","நடுங்கச் சுக்ரிவ னோடம ராடிய","குரங்கைச் செற்றும கோததி தூளெழ ...... நிருதேசன்","குலங்கட் பட்டநி சாசரர் கோவென","இலங்கைக் குட்டழ லோனெழ நீடிய","குமண்டைக் குத்திர ராவண னார்முடி ...... அடியோடே","பிடுங்கத் தொட்டச ராதிப னாரதி","ப்ரியங் கொட் டக்கநன் மாமரு காஇயல்","ப்ரபஞ்சத் துக்கொரு பாவல னாரென ...... விருதூதும்","ப்ரசண்டச் சொற்சிவ வேதசி காமணி","ப்ரபந்தத் துக்கொரு நாதச தாசிவ","பெரும்பற் றப்புலி யூர்தனில் மேவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet imagines the day his life departs: held captive by the buffalo-rider Death, the pleasures desired through women's eyes and the wisdom and honour he spoke of all become mere water and empty space, his body lies as food for the pyre, kin wail and carry it to the burning ground; he asks that before then Murugan grant whichever of two gains is fitting, the goal won by dying in the mind, or understanding won by living on. The praise tells of Rama, who shook the seven sal trees, killed Vali, saw Lanka burned and cut down Ravana's crowns, as Murugan's uncle, and hails Murugan as the one poet for the world, the crest-jewel of the Saiva Veda and lord of its hymns, at Perumpatrappuliyur.","n":"மனதாலே இறந்திட்டுப் பெறவே கதியாயினும் இருந்திட்டுப் பெறவே மதியாயினும்: the poet leaves to the Lord the choice between dying to the world in mind and living on to gain understanding. ப்ரபஞ்சத்துக்கொரு பாவலனார் ... சிவ வேத சிகாமணி ப்ரபந்தத்துக்கொரு நாத: the titles fit Sambandar, the Thevaram poet, so Murugan seems identified with him.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kootru","saakkaadu","gnaanam","thamizh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:41"},"p:491":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":491,"t":["கொந்தள வோலைக ளாடப்பண்","சங்கொளி போல்நகை வீசித்தண்","கொங்கைகள் மார்பினி லாடக்கொண் ...... டையென்மேகம்","கொங்கெழு தோள்வளை யாடக்கண்","செங்கயல் வாளிகள் போலப்பண்","கொஞ்சிய கோகில மாகப்பொன் ...... பறிகாரர்","தந்திர மாமென வேகிப்பொன்","தொங்கலொ டாரமு மாடச்செந்","தம்பல வாயொடு பேசிக்கொண் ...... டுறவாடிச்","சம்பள மீதென வோதிப்பின்","பஞ்சணை மேல்மய லாடச்சஞ்","சங்கையில் மூளியர் பால்வைக்குஞ் ...... செயல்தீராய்","அந்தக னாருயிர் போகப்பொன்","திண்புர மோடெரி பாயப்பண்","டங்கச னாருடல் வேகக்கண் ...... டழல்மேவி","அண்டர்க ளோடட லார்தக்கன்","சந்திர சூரியர் வீழச்சென்","றம்பல மீதினி லாடத்தன் ...... குருநாதா","சிந்துர மோடரி தேர்வர்க்கம்","பொங்கமொ டேழ்கடல் சூர்பத்மன்","சிந்திட வேல்விடு வாகைத்திண் ...... புயவேளே","செங்குற மாதுமி னாளைக்கண்","டிங்கித மாயுற வாடிப்பண்","செந்தமிழ் மால்புலி யூர்நத்தும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women with swinging earrings, conch-white smiles, cloud hair, carp-arrow eyes and cuckoo voices, snatchers of gold who come with tricks, talk with betel-red mouths, name their wage and then sport on the bed, and asks Murugan to end his act of handing his goods to them. The praise names Siva who took Death's life, burned the three cities and Kama's body, felled Daksha, the sun and moon, and dances in the hall, and Murugan as that Siva's guru, the vel against Surapadman, and Valli, at Puliyur.","n":"சம்பளம் ஈது என ஓதி: the women state their wage, love priced as hired work. தன் குருநாதா: Murugan is the guru of the very Siva whose deeds the praise lists. மூளியர் may mean women stripped of ornaments, or be abuse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","selvam","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:41"},"p:492":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":492,"t":["நகையா லெத்திகள் வாயிற்றம்","பலமோ டெத்திகள் நாணற்றின்","நயனா லெத்திகள் நாறற்புண் ...... தொடைமாதர்","நடையா லெத்திக ளாரக்கொங்","கையினா லெத்திகள் மோகத்தின்","நவிலா லெத்திகள் தோகைப்பைங் ...... குழல்மேகச்","சிகையா லெத்திக ளாசைச்சங்","கடியா லெத்திகள் பாடிப்பண்","திறனா லெத்திகள் பாரத்திண் ...... தெருவூடே","சிலர்கூ டிக்கொடு ஆடிக்கொண்","டுழல்வா ருக்குழல் நாயெற்குன்","செயலா லற்புத ஞானத்தின் ...... கழல்தாராய்","பகையா ருட்கிட வேலைக்கொண்","டுவரா ழிக்கிரி நாகத்தின்","படமோ டிற்றிட சூரைச்சங் ...... கரிசூரா","பணநா கத்திடை சேர்முத்தின்","சிவகா மிககொரு பாகத்தன்","பரிவால் சத்துப தேசிக்குங் ...... குரவோனே","சுகஞா னக்கடல் மூழ்கத்தந்","தடியே னுக்கருள் பாலிக்குஞ்","சுடர்பா தக்குக னேமுத்தின் ...... கழல்வீரா","சுகரே சத்தன பாரச்செங்","குறமா தைக்கள வால்நித்தஞ்","சுக்முழ் கிப்புலி யூர்நத்தும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet names women as cheats in every way, by smile, betel mouth, shamelessness, eyes, sore-ridden thighs, gait, breasts, speech, hair and song, who gather and dance through the streets, and asks that Murugan, by his own act, give the anklet of wondrous wisdom to him, a dog who wanders with them. The praise shows the vel that broke the hill and the serpent's hoods, Murugan as the teacher who lovingly taught the truth to Siva, partner of Sivakami, giving his servant the sea of the bliss of wisdom, and his secret love for Valli, at Puliyur.","n":"எத்திகள், cheats, ends every phrase of the first half, a rhetorical list like 470's. சத்துப தேசிக்குங் குரவோனே: Murugan as Siva's teacher of the Real. உன் செயலால்: the wisdom is to come by the Lord's act, not the poet's effort.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","guru","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:41"},"p:493":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":493,"t":["எழுகடல் மணலை அளவிடி னதிக","மெனதிடர் பிறவி ...... அவதாரம்","இனியுன தபய மெனதுயி ருடலு","மினியுடல் விடுக ...... முடியாது","கழுகொடு நரியு மெரிபுவி மறலி","கமலனு மிகவு ...... மயர்வானார்","கடனுன தபய மடிமையு னடிமை","கடுகியு னடிகள் ...... தருவாயே","விழுதிக ழழகி மரகத வடிவி","விமலிமு னருளு ...... முருகோனே","விரிதல மெரிய குலகிரி நெரிய","விசைபெறு மயிலில் ...... வருவோனே","எழுகடல் குமுற அவுணர்க ளுயிரை","யிரைகொளும் அயிலை ...... யுடையோனே","இமையவர் முநிவர் பரவிய புலியு","ரினில்நட மருவு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says his births are more than the sands of the seven seas if counted, that now his life and body are Murugan's charge and he can no longer leave a body, that vultures, jackals, fire, earth, Yama and Brahma are all worn out with him, and that protection is Murugan's duty and this servant is his servant, so let him quickly give his feet. The praise names Uma, emerald-formed and pure, as Murugan's mother, the peacock that shakes the mountains, the vel that feeds on asura lives, and the dance at Puliyur praised by gods and sages.","n":"கழுகொடு நரியும் எரி புவி மறலி கமலனும் மிகவும் அயர்வானார்: those who receive each body (vultures and jackals, fire and earth), the god who takes it (Yama) and the god who makes the next (Brahma) are all exhausted by his countless births, a wry turn. கடன் உனது அபயம்: refuge is the Lord's debt. இனியுடல் விடுக முடியாது may also mean he cannot bear to give up a body again.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","thondu","thiruvadi","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:41"},"p:494":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":494,"t":["தறுகணன் மறலி முறுகிய கயிறு","தலைகொடு விசிறீக் ...... கொடுபோகுஞ்","சளமது தவிர அளவிடு சுருதி","தலைகொடு பலசாத் ...... திரமோதி","அறுவகை சமய முறைமுறை சருவி","யலைபடு தநல்முச் ...... சினையாகும்","அருவரு வொழிய வடிவுள பொருளை","அலம்வர அடியேற் ...... கருள்வாயே","நறுமல ரிறைவி யரிதிரு மருக","நகமுத வியபார்ப் ...... பதிவாழ்வே","நதிமதி யிதழி பணியணி கடவுள்","நடமிடு புலியூர்க் ...... குமரேசா","கறுவிய நிருதர் எறிதிரை பரவு","கடலிடை பொடியாப் ...... பொருதோனே","கழலிணை பணியு மவருடன் முனிவு","கனவிலு மறியாப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks to be spared the trouble of being carried off by merciless Death with his twisted rope swung at the head, and the turmoil of reciting Vedas and many shastras while the six sects quarrel one after another; he asks for the reality that has form, beyond the formless and the formed. The praise names Murugan nephew of Hari, the treasure of Parvati, Kumaresa of Puliyur where the god who wears river, moon, konrai and serpent dances, who fought the Nirudar to powder in the sea, and who never even in dreams knows anger toward those who bow at his feet.","n":"அறுவகை சமயம் முறைமுறை சருவி, the six sects quarrelling in turn, sits beside Death's rope as a second torment: the plea is for one true object (வடிவுள பொருள்) beyond அருவுரு. The line அலைபடு தநல்முச் சினையாகும் does not split cleanly and may be corrupt.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kootru","samayam","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:42"},"p:495":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":495,"t":["இரசபா கொத்தமொழி யமுர்தமா ணிக்கநகை","யிணையிலா சத்திவிழி யார்பசும் பொனிரர்","எழிலிநே ரொத்தஇரு ளளகபா ரச்செயல்க","ளெழுதொணா தப்பிறையி னாரரும் புருவர்","எழுதுதோ டிட்டசெவி பவளநீ லக்கொடிக","ளிகலியா டப்படிக மோடடும் பொனுரு ...... திங்கள்மேவும்","இலவுதா வித்தஇதழ் குமிழைநே ரொத்தஎழி","லிலகுநா சிக்கமுகு மாலசங் கினொளி","யிணைசொல்க்஡ண வத்தரள வினவொள்தா லப்பனையி","னியல்கலா புத்தகமொ டேர்சிறந் தவடி","யிணையிலா னைக்குவடெ னொளிநிலா துத்திபட","ரிகலியா ரத்தொடையு மாருமின் பரச ...... தங்கமார்பின்","வரிகள்தா பித்தமுலை யிசையஆ லிற்றளிரின்","வயிறுநா பிக்கமல மாமெனுஞ் சுழிய","மடுவுரோ மக்கொடியென் அளிகள்சூழ் வுற்றநிரை","மருவுநூ லொத்தஇடை யாரசம் பையல்குல்","மணமெலா முற்றநறை கமலபோ துத்தொடையென்","வளமையார் புக்கதலி சேருசெம் பொனுடை ...... ரம்பைமாதர்","மயலதா லிற்றடியெ னவர்கள்பா லுற்றுவெகு","மதனபா ணத்தினுடன் மேவிமஞ் சமிசை","வதனம்வேர் வுற்றவிர முலைகள்பூ ரிக்கமிடர்","மயில்புறா தத்தைகுயில் போலிலங் கமளி","வசனமாய் பொத்தியிடை துவளமோ கத்துளமிழ்","வசமெலாம் விட்டுமற வேறுசிந் தனையை ...... தந்துஆள்வாய்","முரசுபே ரித்திமிலை துடிகள்பூ ரித்தவில்கள்","முருடுகா ளப்பறைகள் தாரைகொம் புவளை","முகடுபேர் வுற்றவொலி யிடிகள்போ லொத்தமறை","முதுவர்பா டிக்குமுற வேயிறந் தசுரர்","முடிகளோ டெற்றியரி யிரதமா னைப்பிணமொ","டிவுளிவே லைக்குருதி நீர்மிதந் துதிசை ...... யெங்குமோட","முடுகிவேல் விட்டுவட குவடு வாய் விட்டமரர்","முநிவரா டிப்புகழ வேதவிஞ் சையர்கள்","முழவுவீ ணைக்கினரி யமுர்தகீ தத்தொனிகள்","முறையதா கப்பறைய வோதிரம் பையர்கள்","முலைகள்பா ரிக்கவுட னடனமா டிற்றுவர","முடிபதா கைப்பொலிய வேந டங்குலவு ...... கந்தவேளே","அரசுமா கற்பகமொ டகில்பலா இர்ப்பைமகி","ழழகுவே யத்திகமு கோடரம் பையுடன்","அளவிமே கத்திலொளிர் வனமொடா டக்குயில்க","ளளிகள்தோ கைக்கிளிகள் கோவெனம் பெரிய","அமுர்தவா விக்கழனி வயலில்வா ளைக்கயல்க","ளடையுமே ரக்கனக நாடெனும் புலியுர் ...... சந்தவேலா","அழகுமோ கக்குமரி விபுதையே னற்புனவி","யளிகுலா வுற்றகுழல் சேர்கடம் புதொடை","அரசிவே தச்சொருபி கமலபா தக்கரவி","யரியவே டச்சிறுமி யாளணைந் தபுகழ்","அருண்ரு பப்பதமொ டிவுளிதோ கைச்செயல்கொ","டணைதெய்வா னைத்தனமு மேமகிழ்ந் துபுணர் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes a courtesan from lips, smile, eyes, hair and brows down to breasts, navel, waist, thighs and feet, then frankly the lovemaking on the couch with sweating faces and bird-like cries, and asks Murugan to give him another thought and rule him, since he has lost all self-control. The praise is a long battle with drums and rivers of blood, the vel thrown at the northern mountain, the gods and Vidyadharas singing and apsaras dancing, Puliyur as a golden land of groves, tanks and birds, and Murugan's union with Valli and Devayanai.","n":"The plea is one phrase at the end of the confession: வசமெலாம் விட்டு மறவே, having let go of all control, வேறு சிந்தனையைத் தந்து ஆள்வாய், give me a different thought. The head-to-foot catalogue is the stock form of these songs, here carried to its greatest length. One word is corrupt (see source_issues).","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kaamam","punarchi","mayal"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:42"},"p:496":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":496,"t":["இருளு மோர்கதி ரணுகொ ணாதபொ","னிடம தேறியெ ...... னிருநோயும்","எரிய வேமல மொழிய வேசுட","ரிலகு மூலக ...... வொளிமேவி","அருவி பாயஇ னமுத மூறவுன்","அருளெ லாமென ...... தளவாக","அருளி யேசிவ மகிழ வேபெற","அருளி யேயிணை ...... யடிதாராய்","பரம தேசிகர் குருவி லாதவர்","பரவை வான்மதி ...... தவழ்வேணிப்","பவள மேனியர் எனது தாதையர்","பரம ராசியர் ...... அருள்பாலா","மருவி நாயெனை யடிமை யாமென","மகிழ்மெய் ஞானமு ...... மருள்வோனே","மறைகு லாவிய புலியுர் வாழ்குற","மகள்மெ லாசைகொள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A yogic plea: the poet asks to reach the golden place that neither darkness nor the sun's ray can touch, where his two ills burn, impurity leaves, the light at the root shines and nectar flows like a stream, so that Murugan's grace becomes his whole measure and Siva rejoices; and he asks for the feet. The praise names Siva as the supreme teacher who has no teacher, with moon and coral body, and Murugan as his son, who said of the poet 'this dog is my servant' and gave him true knowledge, at Puliyur, lover of the hunter girl.","n":"The ascent is told in the language of Tirumular's yoga: மூலக ஒளி, the light at the base, and அமுதம் welling as an அருவி. இருநோய், the two diseases, is most likely the two kinds of deed (இருவினை); that is a guess. மருவி நாயெனை அடிமையாம் என is the book's formula of being taken into service.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","amudham","malam","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:42"},"p:497":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":497,"t":["காவி யுடுத்துந் தாழ்சடை வைத்துங்","காடுகள் புக்குந் ...... தடுமாறிக்","காயகனி துய்த்துங் காயமொ றுத்துங்","காசினி முற்றுந் ...... திரியாதே","சீவ னொடுக்கம் பூத வொடுக்கம்","தேற வுதிக்கும் ...... பரஞான","தீப விளக்கங் காண எனக்குன்","சீதள பத்மந் ...... தருவாயே","பாவ நிறத்தின் தாருக வர்க்கம்","பாழ்பட வுக்ரந் ...... தருவீரா","பாணிகள் கொட்டும் பேய்கள் பிதற்றும்","பாடலை மெச்சுங் ...... கதிர்வேலா","தூவிகள் நிற்குஞ் சாலி வளைக்குஞ்","சோலை சிறக்கும் ...... புலியூரா","சூரர் மிகக்கொண் டாட நடிக்குந்","தோகை நடத்தும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks not to wander the whole earth wearing ochre, growing matted hair, entering forests, living on raw fruit and punishing the body, but to be given Murugan's cool lotus feet so that he may see the lamp of supreme knowledge that dawns when the self and the elements subside. The praise names the fierce hero who ruined the sinful Daruka clan, the vel whose ghouls clap and sing, and the peacock dancing, at Puliyur among groves and bending paddy.","n":"சீவ னொடுக்கம் பூத வொடுக்கம், the stilling of the self and of the elements, is what the outward marks of the ascetic fail to bring. The song does not condemn ascetics; it says this is not the path the poet asks for.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vesham","thavam","gnaanam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:42"},"p:498":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":498,"t":["கோதிக் கோதிக் கூந்தலி லேமலர்","பாவித் தாகச் சாந்தணி வார்முலை","கோடுத் தானைத் தேன்துவர் வாய்மொழி ...... குயில்போலக்","கூவிக் கூவிக் காண்டிசை போலவெ","நாணிக் கூனிப் பாய்ந்திடு வார்சிலர்","கூடித் தேறிச் சூழ்ந்திடு வார்பொருள் ...... வருமோவென்","றோதித் தோளிற் பூந்துகி லால்முலை","மூடிச் சூதிற் று஡ங்கமி லார்தெரு","வோடித் தேடிச் சோம்பிடு வார்சில ...... விலைமாதர்","ஓருச் சேரச் சேர்ந்திடு வார்கலி","சூளைக் காரச் சாங்கமி லார்சில","வோரைச் சாகத் தீம்பிடு வார்செய ...... லுறவாமோ","வேதத் தோனைக் காந்தள்கை யால்தலை","மேல்குட் டாடிப் பாந்தள் சதாமுடி","வீரிட் டாடக் காய்ந்தசு ரார்கள்மெல் ...... விடும்வேலா","வேளைச் சீறித் தூங்கலொ டேவய","மாவைத் தோலைச் சேர்ந்தணி வாரிட","மீதுற் றாள்பொற் சாம்பவி மாதுமை ...... தருசேயே","நாதத் தோசைக் காண்டுணை யேசுடர்","மூலத் தோனைத் தூண்டிட வேயுயிர்","நாடிக் காலிற் சேர்ந்திட வேயருள் ...... சுரமானை","ஞானப் பால்முத் தேன்சுரு பாள்வளி","மாதைக் கானிற் சேர்ந்தணை வாய்சிவ","ஞானப் பூமித் தேன்புலி யூர்மகிழ் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes courtesans who comb flowers into their hair, sing like cuckoos, act coy, gather asking 'will money come?', run through the streets in deceit, and join with many at once to some men's ruin, and asks whether such company is fitting. The praise names the vel that struck the Suras, Murugan who knocked the Veda-maker on the head with his kanthal hand, the son of Uma who sits beside the one who burned Kama and wears the tiger skin, and Valli as the deer whose grace kindles the yogic fire at the root and the nada, at Puliyur, the land of Siva-knowledge.","n":"பொருள் வருமோ, 'will money come?', and விலைமாதர் mark these as courtesans. The praise gives Valli a yogic role unusual in the book: she grants grace so that the light at the root (சுடர் மூலத்தோன்) is kindled and the life joins the breath in the nadi; the syntax is not certain. One word is corrupt.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["varaivinmagalir","selvam","vanjam","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:42"},"p:499":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":499,"t":["சகசம்பக் குடைசூழ் சிவிகைமெல்","மதவின்பத் துடனே பலபணி","தனிதம்பட் டுடையோ டிகல்முர ...... சொலிவீணை","தவளந்தப் புடனே கிடுகிடு","நடைதம்பட் டமிடோ ல் பலவொலி","சதளம்பொற் றடிகா ரருமிவை ...... புடைசூழ","வெகுகும்பத் துடனே பலபடை","கரகஞ்சுற் றிடவே வரஇசை","வெகுசம்பத் துடனே யழகுட ...... னிதமேவும்","விருமஞ்சித் திரமமா மிதுநொடி","மறையும் பொய்ப் பவுஷோ டுழல்வது","விடவும்பர்க் கரிதா மிணையடி ...... தருவாயே","திகுதந்தித் திகுதோ திகுதிகு","திகுதந்தித் திகுதோ திகுதிகு","திகுர் தஞ்செச் செகசே செககண ...... எனபேரிக்","திமிர்தங்கற் குவடோ டெழுகட","லொலிகொண்டற் றுருவோ டலறிட","திரள்சண்டத் தவுணோர் பொடிபட ...... விடும்வேலா","அகரம்பச் சுருவோ டொளியுறை","படிகம்பொற் செயலா ளரனரி","அயனண்டர்க் கரியா ளுமையருள் ...... முருகோனே","அமுர்தம்பொற் குவடோ டிணைமுலை","மதிதுண்டப் புகழ்மான் மகளொடும்","அருள் செம்பொற் புலியூர் மருவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes the pomp of wealth: a palanquin under champak umbrellas, servants, silk, drums, veena and conch, armies and music, and says that this false show vanishes in a moment; instead of wandering in it he asks for the feet that are hard even for the gods to reach. The praise turns the Asura host to dust with the vel amid drum syllables and roaring seas, names Murugan son of Uma, whom Hara, Hari and Brahma cannot reach, and his union with the deer's daughter, at Puliyur.","n":"இது நொடி மறையும் பொய்ப் பவுஷு, this false pomp that vanishes in a moment, is the whole argument. The song is unusual in confessing not women but status. The words விருமஞ்சித் திரமமா do not split clearly.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["selvam","nilaiyaamai","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:42"},"p:500":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":500,"t":["சகுடமுந்துங் கடலடைந்துங்","குளமகிழ்ந்துந் தோய்சங்கங்","கமுகடைந்தண் டமுதகண்டந்","தரளகந்தந் தேர்கஞ்சஞ்","சரமெனுங்கண் குமிழதுண்டம்","புருவெனுஞ்செஞ் சாபம்பொன் ...... திகழ்மாதர்","சலசகெந்தம் புழுகுடன்சண்","பகமணங்கொண் டேய்ரண்டந்","தனகனம்பொன் கிரிவணங்கும்","பொறிபடுஞ்செம் பேர்வந்தண்","சலனசம்பொன் றிடைபணங்கின்","கடிதடங்கொண் டாரம்பொன் ...... தொடர்பாவை","புகலல்கண்டஞ் சரிகரம்பொன்","சரணபந்தந் தோதிந்தம்","புரமுடன்கிண் கிணிசிலம்பும்","பொலியலம்புந் தாள்ரங்கம்","புணர்வணைந்தண் டுவரொடுந்தொண்","டிடர்கிடந்துண் டேர்கொஞ்சுங் ...... கடைநாயேன்","புகழடைந்துன் கழல்பணிந்தொண்","பொடியணிந் தங்காநந்தம்","புனல்படிந்துண் டவசமிஞ்சுந்","தவசர்சந்தம் போலுந்திண்","புவனிகண்டின் றடிவணங்குஞ்","செயல்கொளஞ்செஞ் சீர்செம்பொன் ...... கழல்தாராய்","திகுடதிந்திந் தகுடதந்தந்","திகுடதிந்திந் தோதிந்தம்","டகுடடண்டண் டிகுடடிண்டிண்","டகுடடண்டண் டோ டிண்டிண்","டிமுடடிண்டிண் டுமுடடுண்டுண்","டிமுடடிண்டென் றேசங்கம் ...... பலபேரி","செககணஞ்சஞ் சலிகைபஞ்சம்","பறைமுழங்கும் போரண்டஞ்","சிலையிடிந்துங் கடல்வடிந்தும்","பொடிபறந்துண் டோ ர்சங்கஞ்","சிரமுடைந்தண் டவுணரங்கம்","பிணமலைந்தன் றாடுஞ்செங் ...... கதிர்வேலா","அகிலஅண்டஞ் சுழலஎங்கும்","பவுரிகொண்டங் காடுங்கொன்","புகழ்விளங்குங் கவுரிபங்கன்","குருவெனுஞ்சிங் காரங்கொண்","டறுமுகம்பொன் சதிதுலங்குந்","திருபதங்கந் தாஎன்றென் ...... றமரோர்பால்","அலர்பொழிந்தங் கரமுகிழ்ந்தொண்","சரணமுங் கொண் டோ தந்தம்","புனைகுறம்பெண் சிறுமியங்கம்","புணர்செயங்கொண் டேயம்பொன்","அமைவிளங்கும் புலிசரம்பொன்","திருநடங்கொண் டார்கந்தம் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet, calling himself the lowest dog, describes a woman's neck, eyes, brows, perfumes, breasts, waist and anklets and his union with her, and asks instead to win praise by bowing at Murugan's anklets, wearing holy ash and bathing in the waters of bliss like ascetics, worshipping his feet today. The praise is a battle in drum syllables, Murugan as guru to the consort of Gauri who dances, the gods showering flowers crying 'Kanda', and his union with the gypsy girl, at Puliyur where the golden dance is held.","n":"The plea sets one bodily practice against another: ஒண்பொடி அணிந்து, wearing the bright ash, and ஆநந்தம் புனல் படிந்து, bathing in bliss, against the perfumed body of the confession. கவுரிபங்கன் குருவெனும் makes Murugan Siva's teacher. The rhythm packs the words tightly and several splits are uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","punarchi","thiruneeru","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:42"},"p:501":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":501,"t":["சாந்துட னேபுழுகு தோய்ந்தழ கார்குழலை","மோந்துப யோதரம ...... தணையாகச்","சாய்ந்துப்ர தாபமுடன் வாழ்ந்த நுராகசுக","காந்தமொ டூசியென ...... மடவார்பால்","கூர்ந்தக்ரு பாமனது போந்துன தாள்குறுகி","ஓர்ந்துண ராவுணர்வி ...... லடிநாயேன்","கூம்பவிழ் கோகநக பூம்பத கோதிலிணை","பூண்டுற வாடுதின ...... முளதோதான்","பாந்தளின் மீதினிதி னோங்குக ணேதுயில்கொள்","நீண்டிடு மாலொடய ...... னறியாது","பாம்புரு வானமுநி வாம்புலி யானபதன்","ஏய்ந்தெதிர் காணநட ...... மிடுபாதர்","பூந்துணர் பாதிமதி வேய்ந்தச டாமகுட","மாங்கன காபுரியி ...... லமர்வாழ்வே","பூங்கமு கார்வுசெறி யூங்கந காபுரிசை","சூழ்ம்புலி யூரிலுறை ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet tells how he smelled women's perfumed hair, lay on their breasts and lived proudly in pleasure, drawn to them like a needle to a magnet, and asks whether a day will come when grace fills him and, knowing without reasoning, he wears Murugan's faultless lotus feet. The praise names Siva, whom Vishnu and Brahma could not know, dancing before the serpent sage and the tiger-footed sage, wearing the half moon in his matted hair in the golden city, and Murugan as his treasure, at Puliyur with its golden walls.","n":"காந்தமொடு ஊசி, needle and lodestone, is the image of compulsion. பாம்புருவான முநி and புலியான பதன் are Patanjali and Vyaghrapada, the two sages for whom Siva danced at Chidambaram; the song names them by their marks, not their names.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","mayal","thiruvadi","thirukkoothu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:42"},"p:502":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":502,"t":["சுடரனைய திருமேனி யுடையழகு முதுஞான","சொருபகிரி யிடமேவு ...... முகமாறும்","சுரர்தெரிய லளிபாட மழலைகதி நறைபாய","துகிரிதழின் மொழிவேத ...... மணம்வீச","அடர்பவள வொளிபாய அரியபரி புரமாட","அயில்தரமொ டெழில்தோகை ...... மயிலேறி","அடியனிரு வினைநீறு படஅமர ரிதுபூரை","அதிசயமெ னருள்பாட ...... வரவேணும்","விடைபரவி அயன்மாலொ டமரர்முநி கணமோட","மிடறடைய விடம்வாரி ...... யருள்நாதன்","மினலனைய இடைமாது இடமருவு குருநாதன்","மிகமகிழ அநுபூதி ...... யருள்வோனே","இடர்கலிகள் பிணியோட எனையுமருள் குறமாதி","னிணையிளநிர் முலைமார்பி ...... னணைமார்பா","இனியமுது புலிபாத னுடனரவு சதகோடி","யிருடியர்கள் புகழ்ஞான ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet calls Murugan to come with his six faces shining from the mountain of ancient knowledge, his lips speaking the Veda, his anklets ringing, mounted on the peacock with the vel, so that the poet's two deeds turn to ash and the gods sing of the wonder. The praise names Siva who swallowed the poison when the gods fled, Murugan as the guru who gave him direct experience (அநுபூதி), the embracer of the hunter girl who drives off troubles and disease, praised by the tiger-footed sage, the serpent sage and countless rishis.","n":"அடியன் இருவினை நீறுபட, that the servant's two deeds become ash, is the only plea; the rest is a vision of Murugan's coming. குருநாதன் மிகமகிழ அநுபூதி அருள்வோனே again makes Murugan the teacher of Siva.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","guru","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:42"},"p:503":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":503,"t":["தத்தைமயில் போலுமியல் பேசிபல மோகநகை","யிட்டுமுட னாணிமுலை மீதுதுகில் மூடியவர்","சற்றவிடம் வீடுமினி வாருமென வோடிமடி ...... பிடிபோல","தைச்சரச மோடுறவெ யாடியக மேகொடுபொ","யெத்தியணை மீதிலிது காலமெனிர் போவதென","தட்டுபுழு கோடுபனி நீர்பலச வாதையவ ...... ருடல்பூசி","வைத்துமுக மோடிரச வாயிதழி னு஡றல்பெரு","கக்குழல ளாவசுழல் வாள்விழிக ளேபதற","வட்டமுலை மார்புதைய வேர்வைதர தோளிறுகி ...... யுடைசோர","மச்சவிழி பூசலிட வாய்புலியு லாசமுட","னொப்பியிரு வோருமயல் மூழ்கியபின் ஆபரணம்","வைத்தடகு தேடுபொருள் சூறைகொளு வார்கலவி ...... செயலாமோ","சத்திசர சோதிதிரு மாதுவெகு ரூபிசுக","நித்தியகல் யாணியெனை யீணமலை மாதுசிவை","தற்பரனொ டாடுமபி ராமிசிவ காமியுமை ...... யருள்பாலா","சக்ரகிரி மூரிமக மேருகடல் தூளிபட","ரத்நமயி லேறிவிளை யாடியசு ராரைவிழ","சத்தியினை யேவிஅம ரோர்கள்சிறை மீளநட ...... மிடுவோனே","துத்திதன பாரவெகு மோகசுக வாரிமிகு","சித்ரமுக ரூபியென தாயிவளி நாயகியை","சுத்தஅணை யூடுவட மாமுலைவி டாதகர ...... மணிமார்பா","சுத்தவம காதவசி காமணியெ னோதுமவர்","சித்தமதி லேகுடிய தாவுறையும் ஆறுமுக","சுப்ரமணி யாபுலியுர் மேவியுறை தேவர்புகழ் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes courtesans who talk like parrots, invite a man with 'the house is free now, come', lead him to the bed, perfume him, and make love to him frankly described; when both have drowned in infatuation they pawn his ornaments and plunder his wealth, and he asks whether such lovemaking is right. The praise is a long litany of the Goddess as Shakti, Kalyani, Abhirami, Sivakami and Uma who dances with the Supreme, and of Murugan who rode the peacock, threw the Shakti and freed the gods, Valli's husband, who dwells in the minds of those who chant his name, Subramanya at Puliyur.","n":"ஆபரணம் வைத்து அடகு தேடு பொருள் சூறைகொளுவார்: the women pawn his jewels and loot his goods once infatuation has taken him, the courtesan's stock charge. The Goddess is called எனை ஈண மலைமாது, the mountain daughter who bore me. One word is corrupt.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["varaivinmagalir","punarchi","selvam","mayal"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:42"},"p:504":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":504,"t":["துத்தி பொற்றன மேரு வாமென","வொத்தி பத்திரள் வாகு வாயவிர்","துப்பு முத்தொடு மார்பினாடிட ...... மயில்போலே","சுக்கை மைக்குழ லாட நூலிடை","பட்டு விட்டவிர் காம னாரல்குல்","சுற்று வித்துறு வாழை சேர்தொடை ...... விலைமாதர்","தத்தை பட்குர லோசை நூபுர","மொத்த நட்டமொ டாடி மார்முலை","சற்ற சைத்துகு லாவும் வேசிய ...... ரவரோடே","தர்க்க மிட்டுற வாடி யீளைநொய்","கக்கல் விக்கல்கொ ளூளை நாயென","சிச்சி சிச்சியெ னால்வர் கூறிட ...... வுழல்வேனோ","தித்தி மித்திமி தீத தோதக","தத்த னத்தன தான தீதிமி","திக்கு முக்கிட மூரி பேரிகை ...... தவில்போடச்","சித்ர வித்தைய ராட வானவர்","பொற்பு விட்டிடு சேசெ சேயென","செக்கு விட்டசு ரோர்கள் தூள்பட ...... விடும்வேலா","செத்தி டச்சம னார்க டாபட","அற்று தைத்தசு வாமி யாரிட","சித்தி ரச்சிவ காமி யாரருள் ...... முருகோனே","தெற்க ரக்கர்கள் தீவு நீறிட","விட்ட அச்சுத ஡ணன மானொடு","சித்தி ரப்புலி யூரில் மேவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes saleswomen who dance like peacocks with swinging breasts and parrot voices, and asks whether he must wander, quarrelling and making love with such harlots, until he coughs, vomits and hiccups like a mangy dog while people say 'chi chi'. The praise sets the vel against the Asuras amid drum syllables, names Murugan son of Sivakami and of the Lord who kicked Death, and his union with the deer born of Achyuta, who burned the southern demons' island, at Puliyur.","n":"The confession runs straight from the women to disease and disgust (சிச்சி சிச்சி என நால்வர் கூறிட), with no interval. The phrase about Achyuta is corrupt; the sense that Valli is Vishnu's daughter is a guess from tradition.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["varaivinmagalir","noy","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:42"},"p:505":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":505,"t":["நாடா பிறப்புமுடி யாதோ வெனக்கருதி","நாயே னரற்றுமொழி ...... வினையாயின்","நாதா திருச்சபையி னேறாது சித்தமென","நாலா வகைக்குமுன ...... தருள்பேசி","வாடா மலர்ப்பதவி தாதா எனக்குழறி","வாய்பாறி நிற்குமெனை ...... அருள்கூர","வாராய் மனக்கவலை தீராய் நினைத்தொழுது","வாரே னெனக்கெதிர் முன் ...... வரவேணும்","சூடா மணிப்பிரபை ரூபா கனத்தவரி","தோலா சனத்தியுமை ...... யருள்பாலா","தூயா துதித்தவர்கள் நேயா வெமக்கமிர்த","தோழா கடப்பமல ...... ரணிவோனே","ஏடார் குழற்சுருபி ஞானா தனத்திமிகு","மேராள் குறத்திதிரு ...... மணவாளா","ஈசா தனிப்புலிசை வாழ்வே சுரர்த்திரளை","ஈடேற வைத்தபுகழ் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet, a dog lamenting 'will birth never end?', asks that if it is his deeds that keep his mind from reaching the holy hall, Murugan come in grace as he stands crying with a dry mouth, end the anxiety of his mind, and appear before him as he comes worshipping. The praise names Uma who sits on the skin seat, Murugan as the nectar friend of those who praise him, wearer of kadamba, husband of Valli the form of knowledge, the treasure of Puliyur, who raised up the gods.","n":"திருச்சபை is the hall of the dance at Chidambaram: the poet's goal is to reach it in mind. வாய்பாறி நிற்கும், standing with mouth dried from crying, is the posture of the plea.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","vinai","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:42"},"p:506":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":506,"t":["நாலுசது ரத்த பஞ்சறை மூலகம லத்தி லங்கியை","நாடியின டத்தி மந்திர ...... பந்தியாலே","நாரண புரத்தி லிந்துவி னு஡டுற இணக்கி நன்சுடர்","நாறிசை நடத்தி மண்டல ...... சந்தியாறிற்","கோலமு முதிப்ப கண்டுள நாலினை மறித்தி தம்பெறு","கோவென முழக்கு சங்கொலி ...... விந்துநாதங்","கூடிய முகப்பி லிந்திர வானவமு தத்தை யுண்டொரு","கோடிநட னப்ப தஞ்சபை ...... யென்றுசேர்வேன்","ஆலமல ருற்ற சம்பவி வேரிலி குலக்கொ ழுந்திலி","ஆரணர் தலைக்க லங்கொளி ...... செம்பொன்வாசி","ஆணவ மயக்க முங்கலி காமிய மகற்றி யென்றனை","ஆளுமை பரத்தி சுந்தரி ...... தந்தசேயே","வேலதை யெடுத்து மிந்திரர் மால்விதி பிழைக்க வஞ்சகர்","வீடெரி கொளுத்தி யெண்கட ...... லுண்டவேலா","வேசது ரத்தர் தென்புலி யூருறை யொருத்தி பங்கினர்","வீறுநட னர்க்கி சைந்தருள் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"A yogic plea: the poet asks when, from the fire in the four-cornered root lotus, through the nadis and mantras, past the moon and the six junctions, with the conch-like sound of bindu and nada at the front, he will drink the gods' nectar and reach the hall of the countless dances. The praise names the Goddess who removed his delusion of anava, kali and desire and ruled him, Murugan as her son, the vel that burned the deceivers' homes and drank the eight seas, and Siva who dances at south Puliyur.","n":"நாலுசதுரத்த ... மூலகமலம் is the four-sided base lotus (muladhara) of the yogic body, and விந்துநாதம் the bindu and nada of Tirumular's teaching; the goal is the dance hall (சபை), set inside the body. ஆணவ மயக்கம் names the root impurity. The ascent's stages are not clear to me, and one word is corrupt.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["yogam","aadhaaram","amudham","naadham"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:42"},"p:507":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":507,"t":["நீலக்குழ லார்முத்தணி வாய்சர்க்கரை யார்தைப்பிறை","நீளச்சசி யார்பொட்டணி ...... நுதல்மாதர்","நீலக்கய லார்பத்திர வேலொப்பிடு வார்நற்கணி","நேமித்தெழு தாசித்திர ...... வடிவார்தோள்","ஆலைக்கழை யார்துத்திகொ ளாரக்குவ டார்கட்டளை","யாகத்தமி யேனித்தமு ...... முழல்வேனோ","ஆசைப்பத மேல்புத்திமெய் ஞானத்துட னேபத்திர","மாகக்கொள வேமுத்தியை ...... யருள்வாயே","மாலைக்குழ லாளற்புத வேதச்சொரு பாளக்கினி","மார்பிற்பிர காசக்கிரி ...... தனபார","வாசக்குயி லாள்நற்சிவ காமச்செய லாள்பத்தினி","மாணிக்கமி னாள்நிஷ்கள ...... உமைபாகர்","சூலக்கையி னாரக்கினி மேனிப்பர னாருக்கொரு","சோதிப்பொருள் கேள்விக்கிடு ...... முருகோனே","சோதிப்பிர காசச்செய லாள்முத்தமிழ் மானைப்புணர்","சோதிப்புலி யூர்நத்திய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women with dark hair, pearl-adorned lips, moon foreheads and carp eyes and asks whether he must wander daily at their command; he asks for release, so that his mind may hold to Murugan's feet with true knowledge. The praise names Uma at length and Murugan as the one who told the meaning of the light to Siva with the trident and fiery body, and his union with the deer of the three Tamils, at Puliyur.","n":"சோதிப்பொருள் கேள்விக்கிடு முருகோனே, Murugan who gave Siva the meaning of the light to hear, is the story of the teaching of Siva; Om is not named. கட்டளையாக, at their command, makes the women masters and the poet a servant.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","veedu","guru","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:42"},"p:508":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":508,"t":["பனிபோ லத்துளி சலவா யுட்கரு","பதின்மா தத்திடை ...... தலைகீழாய்ப்","படிமே விட்டுடல் தவழ்வார் தத்தடி","பயில்வா ருத்தியில் ...... சிலநாள்போய்த்","தனமா தர்க்குழி விழுவார் தத்துவர்","சதிகா ரச்சமன் ...... வருநாளிற்","றறியா ரிற்சடம் விடுவா ரிப்படி","தளர்மா யத்துய ...... ரொழியாதோ","வினைமா யக்கிரி பொடியா கக்கடல்","விகடா ருக்கிட ...... விடும்வேலா","விதியோ னைச்சது முடிநால் பொட்டெழ","மிகவே குட்டிய ...... குருநாதா","நினைவோர் சித்தமொ டகலா மற்புகு","நிழலாள் பத்தினி ...... மணவாளா","நிதியா மிப்புவி புலியூ ருக்கொரு","நிறைவே பத்தர்கள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet traces life from a drop like dew, ten months upside down in the womb, birth, crawling and toddling, to falling into the pit of women and leaving the body unawares when cunning Death comes, and asks whether this weakening sorrow of illusion will never end. The praise names the vel that powdered the mountain of illusion and dried the sea, Murugan as the guru who knocked Brahma's four heads, and husband of the lady who enters the minds of those who think of him, the fullness of Puliyur.","n":"The whole life is told in eight short lines, and the only adult act is falling into women (மாதர்க் குழி விழுவார்) before Death arrives. மாயத்துயர், the sorrow of illusion, names the whole cycle.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karu","kootru","maayai","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:42"},"p:509":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":509,"t":["மகரமொ டுறுகுழை யோலை காட்டியு","மழைதவழ் வனைகுழல் மாலை காட்டியும்","வரவர வரஇத ழூற லு஡ட்டியும் ...... வலைவீசும்","மகரவி ழிமகளிர் பாடல் வார்த்தையில்","வழிவழி யொழுகுமு பாய வாழ்க்கையில்","வளமையி லிளமையில் மாடை வேட்கையில் ...... மறுகாதே","இகலிய பிரமக பால பாத்திர","மெழில்பட இடுதிரு நீறு சேர்த்திற","மிதழியை யழகிய வேணி யார்த்ததும் ...... விருதாக","எழில்பட மழுவுடன் மானு மேற்றது","மிசைபட இசைதரு ஆதி தோற்றமு","மிவையிவை யெனவுப தேச மேற்றுவ ...... தொருநாளே","ஜகதல மதிலருள் ஞான வாட்கொடு","தலைபறி யமணர்ச மூக மாற்றிய","தவமுனி சகமுளர் பாடு பாட்டென ...... மறைபாடி","தரிகிட தரிகிட தாகு டாத்திரி","கிடதரி கிடதரி தாவெ னாச்சில","சபதமொ டெழுவன தாள வாச்சிய ...... முடனேநீள்","அகுகுகு குகுவென ஆளி வாய்ப்பல","அலகைக ளடைவுட னாடு மாட்டமு","மரனவ னுடனெழு காளி கூட்டமு ...... மகலாதே","அரிதுயில் சயனவி யாள மூர்த்தனு","மணிதிகழ் மிகுபுலி யூர்வி யாக்ரனு","மரிதென முறைமுறை யாடல் காட்டிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks not to be confused by courtesans who cast nets with their earrings, garlanded hair and lip-nectar, nor by their songs, their scheming life, youth and lust for gold; he asks for the day when he will be taught what Siva's emblems mean: the skull-bowl, the holy ash, the konrai in the matted hair, the axe and the deer, and the primal origin. The praise identifies Murugan with the sage who with the sword of knowledge defeated the hair-plucking Jains and sang the Veda as songs, and names the dance shown in turn to the serpent-form sage and the tiger sage of Puliyur, amid drum syllables, ghouls and Kali's host.","n":"The plea is unusual: not for the feet but for உபதேசம், teaching on what Siva's ornaments signify (இவையிவை என). தலைபறி அமணர், the Jains who pluck their hair, and the sage who changed their community points to Sambandar, with whom the book identifies Murugan. அரிதுயில் சயன வியாளமூர்த்தன், the serpent on which Hari sleeps, is Patanjali as Adisesha. One word is corrupt.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kaamam","guru","sivam","thamizh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:43"},"p:510":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":510,"t":["மச்ச மெச்சு சூத்ரம் ரத்த பித்த மூத்ரம்","வைச்சி றைச்ச பாத்ர ...... மநுபோகம்","மட்க விட்ட சேக்கை உட்பு ழுத்த வாழ்க்கை","மட்டு லப்ப தார்த்த ...... மிடிபாறை","எய்ச்சி ளைச்ச பேய்க்கு மெய்ச்சி ளைச்ச நாய்க்கு","மெய்ச்சி ளைச்ச ஈக்கு ...... மிரையாகும்","இக்க டத்தை நீக்கி அக்க டத்து ளாக்கி","இப்படிக்கு மோக்ஷ ...... மருள்வாயே","பொய்ச்சி னத்தை மாற்றி மெய்ச்சி னத்தை யேற்றி","பொற்ப தத்து ளாக்கு ...... புலியூரா","பொக்க ணத்து நீற்றை யிட்டொ ருத்த னார்க்கு","புத்தி மெத்த காட்டு ...... புனவேடன்","பச்சி லைக்கும் வாய்க்கு ளெச்சி லுக்கும் வீக்கு","பைச்சி லைக்கு மாட்கொ ...... ளரன்வாழ்வே","பத்தி சித்தி காட்டி அத்தர் சித்த மீட்ட","பத்த ருக்கு வாய்த்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet calls his body a vessel of flesh, blood, bile and urine bound together, a rotting bed of pleasures, a worm-eaten life that becomes food for the tired ghoul, the dog and the fly, and asks Murugan to remove this pot and place him in that other one, granting release. The praise names Murugan at Puliyur who turns false anger to true anger and places devotees at his golden feet, and the son of Hara who took into service the hunter of the field, who offered green leaves and water from his mouth.","n":"இக்கடத்தை நீக்கி அக்கடத்துள் ஆக்கி, remove this pot and make me within that pot, plays on கடம், pot or body: the second pot is the state of release. The leaves and the water carried in the mouth (பச்சிலை, வாய்க்குள் எச்சில்) are the marks of the hunter Kannappar's worship, which Hara accepted. Who the பொக்கணத்து நீற்றை இட்டு ... புனவேடன் is, Kannappar or Murugan as hunter, is not clear to me.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","veedu","paththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:43"},"p:511":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":511,"t":["மதிய மண்குண மஞ்சு நால்முக","நகர முன்கலை கங்கை நால்குண","மகர முன்சிக ரங்கி மூணிடை ...... தங்குகோண","மதன முன்தரி சண்ட மாருத","மிருகு ணம்பெறி லஞ்செ லோர்தெரு","வகர மிஞ்சிய கன்ப டாகமொ ...... ரென்றுசேறுங்","கதிர டங்கிய அண்ட கோளகை","யகர நின்றிடும் ரண்டு கால்மிசை","ககன மின்சுழி ரண்டு கால்பரி ...... கந்துபாயுங்","கருணை யிந்திரி யங்கள் சோதிய","அருண சந்திர மண்ட லீகரர்","கதிகொள் யந்திர விந்து நாதமொ ...... டென்றுசேர்வேன்","அதிர பம்பைகள் டங்கு டாடிக","முதிர அண்டமொ டைந்து பேரிகை","டகுட டண்டட தொந்த தோதக ...... என்றுதாளம்","அதிக விஞ்சையர் தும்ப்ரு நார்தரோ","டிதவி தம்பெறு சிந்து பாடிட","அமரர் துந்துமி சங்கு தாரைகள் ...... பொங்கவூடு","உதிர மண்டல மெங்கு மாயொளி","யெழகு மண்டியெ ழுந்து சூரரை","உயர்ந ரம்பொடெ லும்பு மாமுடி ...... சிந்திவீழ","உறுசி னங்கொடெ திர்ந்த சேவக","மழைபு குந்துய ரண்டம் வாழ்வுற","வுரகு னும்புலி கண்ட வூர்மகிழ் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"A yogic plea: setting out the elements, the letters na, ma, si, va and ya at their places in the body, the cosmic sphere where the rays are stilled, the breath in its two channels, the purified senses, the sun and moon circles, the yantra, bindu and nada, the poet asks when he will reach that union. The praise is a battle in drum syllables with Vidyadharas, Tumburu and Narada singing and the Suras' bones and crowns scattered, and names the town that the serpent and the tiger saw.","n":"நகர, மகர, சிகர, வகர, யகர are the five letters of நமசிவாய, set beside the elements (மண், கங்கை, மாருதம், ககனம்) as Tirumular sets them. The sequence is dense and I cannot follow every stage. உரகு உனும் புலி கண்ட ஊர் is Chidambaram as the place where Patanjali and Vyaghrapada saw the dance.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","panchaakkaram","vindhu","naadham"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:43"},"p:512":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":512,"t":["மருவு கடல்முகி லனைய குழல்மதி","வதன நுதல்சிலை பிறைய தெணும்விழி","மச்சப் பொற்கணை முக்குப் பொற்குமி","ழொப்பக் கத்தரி யொத்திட் டச்செவி","குமுத மலரித ழமுத மொழிநிரை","தரள மெனுநகை மிடறு கமுகென","வைத்துப் பொற்புய பச்சைத் தட்டையொ","டொப்பிட் டுக்கம லக்கைப் பொற்றுகிர்","வகைய விரலொடு கிளிகள் முகநக","மெனவு மிகலிய குவடு மிணையென","வட்டத் துத்திமு கிழ்ப்பச் சக்கிரம்","வைத்தப் பொற்குட மொத்திட் டுத்திகழ் ...... முலைமேவும்","வடமு நிரைநிரை தரள பவளமொ","டசைய பழுமர இலைவ யிறுமயி","ரற்பத் திக்கிணை பொற்புத் தொப்புளும்","அப்புக் குட்சுழி யொத்துப் பொற்கொடி","மதன னுருதுடி யிடையு மினலென","அரிய கடிதட மமிர்த கழைரச","மட்டுப் பொற்கம லத்திற் சக்கிரி","துத்திப் பைக்கொரு மித்துப் பட்டுடை","மருவு தொடையிணை கதலி பரடுகொள்","கணையு முழவென கமட மெழுதிய","வட்டப் புத்தக மொத்துப் பொற்சர","ணத்திற் பிற்புற மெத்துத் தத்தைகள் ...... மயில்போலே","தெருவில் முலைவிலை யுரைசெய் தவரவர்","மயல்கொ டணைவர மருள்செய் தொழில்கொடு","தெட்டிப் பற்பல சொக்கிட் டுப்பொருள்","பற்றிக் கட்டில ணைக்கொப் பிப்புணர்","திலத மழிபட விழிகள் சுழலிட","மலர்க ளணைகுழ லிடைகொள் துகில்பட","தித்தித் துப்பிதழ் வைத்துக் கைக்கொடு","கட்டிக் குத்துமு லைக்குட் கைப்பட","திரையி லமுதென கழையில் ரசமென","பலவில் சுளையென வுருக வுயர்மயல்","சிக்குப் பட்டுடல் கெட்டுச் சித்தமும்","வெட்கித் துக்கமு முற்றுக் கொக்கென ...... நரைமேவிச்","செவியொ டொளிர்விழி மறைய மலசல","மொழுக பலவுரை குழற தடிகொடு","தெத்திப் பித்தமு முற்றித் தற்செய","லற்றுச் சிச்சியெ னத்துக் கப்பட","சிலர்கள் முதுவுடல் வினவு பொழுதினி","லுவரி நிறமுடை நமனு முயிர்கொள","செப்பற் றுப்பிண மொப்பித் துப்பெய","ரிட்டுப் பொற்பறை கொட்டச் செப்பிடு","செனன மிதுவென அழுது முகமிசை","அறைய அணைபவ ரெடென சுடலையில்","சிற்றிக் குக்கிரை யிட்டிட் டிப்படி","நித்தத் துக்கமெ டுத்திட் டுச்சட ...... முழல்வேனோ","குருவி னுருவென அருள்செய் துறையினில்","குதிரை கொளவரு நிறைத வசிதலை","கொற்றப் பொற்பதம் வைத்திட் டற்புத","மெற்றிப் பொற்பொரு ளிட்டுக் கைக்கொளு","முதல்வ ரிளகலை மதிய மடைசடை","அருண வுழைமழு மருவு திருபுயர்","கொட்டத் துப்புரர் கெட்டுப் பொட்டெழ","விட்டத் திக்கணை நக்கர்க் கற்புத","குமர னெனவிரு தொலியு முரசொடு","வளையு மெழுகட லதிர முழவொடு","கொட்டத் துட்டரை வெட்டித் தட்கட","லொப்பத் திக்கும டுத்துத் தத்திட ...... அமர்மேவிக்","குருகு கொடிசிலை குடைகள் மிடைபட","மலைகள் பொடிபட வுடுக ளுதிரிட","கொத்திச் சக்கிரி பற்றப் பொற்பரி","எட்டுத் திக்குமெ டுத்திட் டுக்குரல்","குமர குருபர குமர குருபர","குமர குருபர எனவோ தமரர்கள்","கொட்பப் புட்பமி றைத்துப் பொற்சர","ணத்திற் கைச்சிரம் வைத்துக் குப்பிட","குலவு நரிசிறை கழுகு கொடிபல","கருட னடமிட குருதி பருகிட","கொற்றப் பத்திர மிட்டுப் பொற்கக","னத்தைச் சித்தமி ரக்ஷித் துக்கொளு ...... மயில்வீரா","சிரமொ டிரணிய னுடல்கி ழியவொரு","பொழுதி னுகிர்கொடு அரியெ னடமிடு","சிற்பர்த் திட்பதம் வைத்துச் சக்கிர","வர்த்திக் குச்சிறை யிட்டுச் சுக்கிரன்","அரிய விழிகெட இருப தமுமுல","கடைய நெடியவர் திருவு மழகியர்","தெற்குத் திக்கில ரக்கர்க் குச்சின","முற்றுப் பொற்றசர் தற்குப் புத்திர","செயமு மனவலி சிலைகை கொடுகர","மிருப துடைகிரி சிரமொர் பதும்விழ","திக்கெட் டைக்கக னத்தர்க் குக்கொடு","பச்சைப் பொற்புய லுக்குச் சித்திர ...... மருகோனே","திலத மதிமுக அழகி மரகத","வடிவி பரிபுர நடனி மலர்பத","சித்தர்க் குக்குறி வைத்திட் டத்தன்","முத்தப் பொற்கிரி யொத்தச் சித்திர","சிவைகொள் திருசர சுவதி வெகுவித","சொருபி முதுவிய கிழவி யியல்கொடு","செட்டிக் குச்சுக முற்றத் தத்துவ","சித்திற் சிற்பதம் வைத்தக் கற்புறு","திரையி லமுதென மொழிசெய் கவுரியி","னரிய மகனென புகழ்பு லிநகரில்","செப்புப் பொற்றன முற்றப் பொற்குற","தத்தைக் குப்புள கித்திட் டொப்பிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes courtesans from hair to feet, who sell their breasts in the street, cheat and take a man's goods, and make love to him; caught in that infatuation he ruins his body, turns grey like a crane, loses sight and hearing, leaks filth, totters on a stick while people say 'chi chi', until Death takes his life, the corpse is dressed and named, drums are beaten, kin cry and beat their faces, and it is fed to the fire in the burning ground; he asks whether he must take up such sorrow daily. The praise names Siva who came as guru at the ford to take the horses and set his foot on the ascetic's head, the gods crying 'Kumara guru para', Vishnu's deeds as Narasimha, Vamana and Rama, and Murugan's embrace of the gypsy girl, at Puliyur.","n":"The longest confession in these chapters, it runs without pause from the courtesans' trade (முலைவிலை உரைசெய்து, பொருள் பற்றி) to old age and the funeral, so that lust is shown leading straight to the burning ground. குருவின் உருவென ... குதிரை கொளவரு ... தவசி தலை பொற்பதம் வைத்து recalls the story of Manikkavasagar, for whom Siva came as guru and brought horses; the text gives its details, not the name.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","yaakkai","kootru","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:43"},"p:513":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":513,"t":["மனமே உனக்குறுதி புகல்வே னெனக்கருகில்","வருவா யுரைத்தமொழி ...... தவறாதே","மயில்வாக னக்கடவுள் அடியார் தமக்கரசு","மனமாயை யற்றசுக ...... மதிபாலன்","நினைவே துனக்கமரர் சிவலோக மிட்டுமல","நிலைவே ரறுக்கவல ...... பிரகாசன்","நிதிகா நமக்குறுதி அவரே பரப்பிரம","நிழலாளி யைத்தொழுது ...... வருவாயே","இனமோ தொருத்திருபி நலமேர் மறைக்கரிய","இளையோ ளொரொப்புமிலி ...... நிருவாணி","எனையீ ணெடுத்தபுகழ் கலியாணி பக்கமுறை","யிதழ்வேணி யப்பனுடை ...... குருநாதா","முனவோர் துதித்து மலர் மழைபோ லிறைத்துவர","முதுசூ ரரைத்தலை கொள் ...... முருகோனே","மொழிபாகு முத்துநகை மயிலாள் தனக்குருகு","முருகா தமிழ்ப்புலியுர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks to his mind: 'Mind, come near and keep my word; I tell you what is good for you: worship the god of the peacock, king of his devotees, who gives the gods Siva's world and cuts the root of impurity, the supreme Brahman, our treasure and refuge.' The praise names Murugan as guru of Siva who sits with Kalyani, who took the Suras' heads while the ancients showered flowers, and who melts for the peacock-like Valli, at Tamil Puliyur.","n":"The whole plea is addressed to மனமே, the mind as a separate party, as the lover speaks to his heart in love poetry; here the mind is urged toward worship rather than a beloved. மலநிலை வேர் அறுக்கவல, able to cut the root of the state of impurity, is Siddhanta language.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","malam","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:43"},"p:514":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":514,"t":["முத்த மோகன தத்தையி னார்குர","லொத்த வாயித சர்க்கரை யார்நகை","முத்து வாரணி பொற்குவ டார்முலை ...... விலைமாதர்","மொக்கை போகசெ குத்திடு வார்பொருள்","பற்றி வேறும ழைத்திடு வார்சிலர்","முச்ச லீலிகை சொக்கிடு வாரிடர் ...... கலிசூழச்","சித்தி லாடஅ ழைத்திடு வார்கவ","டுற்ற மாதர்வ லைப்புகு நாயெனை","சித்தி ஞானம்வெ ளிப்பட வேசுடர் ...... மடமீதே","சித்தெ லாமொரு மித்துன தாறினம்","வைத்து நாயென ருட்பெற வேபொருள்","செப்பி யாறும கப்பரி வோடுணர் ...... வருள்வாயே","தத்த னானத னத்தன தானெனு","டுக்கை பேரிமு ழக்கிட வேகடல்","சத்த தீவுத யித்தியர் மாளிட ...... விடும்வேலா","சத்தி லோகப ரப்பர மேசுர","நிர்த்த மாடுக ழற்கரு ணாகர","தற்ப ராபர நித்தனொர் பாலுறை ...... யுமைபாலா","துத்தி மார்முலை முத்தணி மோகன","பொற்ப்ர காசமு ளக்குற மான்மகள்","துப்பு வாயிதழ் வைத்தணை சோதிபொன் ...... மணிமார்பா","சுட்டி நீலஇ ரத்தின மாமயி","லுற்று மேவிய ருட்புலி யூர்வளர்","சுத்த னேசசி பெற்றபெ ணாயகி ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of courtesans with parrot voices and pearl-hung breasts who seize a man's wealth and call in others, and asks that he, a dog caught in the net of deceitful women, be given knowledge and attainment on the seat of light, his whole mind gathered to Murugan's six, and be told the meaning with love. The praise names the vel that killed the Daityas of the seven islands, Murugan as son of Uma who is half of the dancing Lord, his embrace of the gypsy girl, and the husband of Indrani's daughter, at Puliyur.","n":"பொருள் பற்றி வேறு அழைத்திடுவார், they take the wealth and call another, is the courtesan's trade. உனது ஆறினம் வைத்து may mean the six faces or the six-syllable mantra; the sense is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:43"},"p:515":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":515,"t":["பரமகுரு நாத கருணையுப தேச","பதவிதரு ஞானப் ...... பெருமாள்காண்","பகலிரவி லாத ஒளிவெளியில் மேன்மை","பகருமதி காரப் ...... பெருமாள்காண்","திருவளரு நீதி தினமனொக ராதி","செகபதியை யாளப் ...... பெருமாள்காண்","செகதலமும் வானு மருவையவை பூத","தெரிசனைசி வாயப் ...... பெருமாள்காண்","ஒருபொருள தாகி அருவிடையை யூரு","முமைதன்மண வாளப் ...... பெருமாள்காண்","உகமுடிவு கால மிறுதிகளி லாத","உறுதியநு பூதிப் ...... பெருமாள்காண்","கருவுதனி லு஡று மருவினைகள் மாய","கலவிபுகு தாமெய்ப் ...... பெருமாள்காண்","கனகசபை மேவி அனவரத மாடு","கடவுள்செக சோதிப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A litany with no plea, each line ending 'see, the Lord': the supreme guru who gives the state of teaching, the one exalted in the space of light without day or night, who rules the worlds, seen as Sivaya in earth, sky and elements, Uma's husband who rides the bull, the experience that has no end of ages, who ends the deeds and illusion that enter in the womb, the god of world-light who dances without ceasing in the golden hall.","n":"Every epithet here belongs to Siva (உமைதன் மணவாளன், the dancer of கனகசபை), so the song either praises Siva or makes Murugan one with him; it does not say which. The refrain பெருமாள் காண் is unusual in the book. One word is corrupt.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["sivam","guru","thirukkoothu","oli"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:43"},"p:516":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":516,"t":["வஞ்சமே கோடி கோடிகள் நெஞ்சமே சேர மேவிய","வன்கணா ரார வாரமு ...... மருள்வோராய்","வம்பிலே வாது கூறிகள் கொஞ்சியே காம லீலைகள்","வந்தியா ஆசை யேதரு ...... விலைமாதர்","பஞ்சமா பாவ மேதரு கொங்கைமேல் நேச மாய்வெகு","பஞ்சியே பேசி நாடொறு ...... மெலியாதே","பந்தியாய் வானு ளோர்தொழ நின்றசீ ரேகு லாவிய","பண்புசேர் பாத தாமரை ...... யருள்வாயே","அஞ்சவே சூர னானவ னுய்ஞ்சுபோ காம லேயயில்","அன்றுதா னேவி வானவர் ...... சிறைமீள","அன்பினோ டேம னோரத மிஞ்சமே லான வாழ்வருள்","அண்டர்கோ வேப ராபர ...... முதல்வோனே","கொஞ்சவே காலின் மேவுச தங்கைதா னாட ஆடிய","கொன்றையா னாளு மேமகிழ் ...... புதல்வோனே","கொந்துசேர் சோலை மேவிய குன்றுசூழ் வாக வேவரு","குன்றுதோ றாடல் மேவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of courtesans with crores of deceit in their hearts, cooing and quarrelling, who bring desire, and asks not to waste away daily talking of love to breasts that bring the five great sins, but to be given the lotus feet the gods worship. The praise names the vel thrown at Sura to free the gods, Murugan as son of the konrai-wearer who danced with tinkling anklets, the supreme first, who dances on every hill.","n":"பஞ்சமா பாவம் ஏதரு கொங்கை, breasts that bring the five great sins, puts lust among the gravest faults. The song ends at குன்றுதோறாடல், not at Chidambaram (see source_issues).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:43"},"p:517":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":517,"t":["திருநில மருவிக் காலி னிருவழி யடைபட் டோ டி","சிவவழி யுடனுற் றேக ...... பரமீதே","சிவசுட ரதனைப் பாவை மணமென மருவிக் கோல","திரிபுர மெரியத் தீயி ...... னகைமேவி","இருவினை பொரியக் கோல திருவரு ளுருவத் தேகி","யிருள்கதி ரிலிபொற் பூமி ...... தவசூடே","இருவரு முருகிக் காய நிலையென மருவித் தேவ","ரிளையவ னெனவித் தார ...... மருள்வாயே","பரிபுர கழலெட் டாசை செவிடுகள் படமுத் தேவர்","பழமறை பணியச் சூல ...... மழுமானும்","பரிவொடு சுழலச் சேடன் முடிநெறு நெறெனக் கோவு","பரியினை மலர்விட் டாடி ...... அடியொர்கள்","அரஹர வுருகிச் சேசெ யெனதிரு நடனக் கோல","மருள்செயு முமையிற் பாக ...... ரருள்பாலா","அலரணி குழல்பொற் பாவை திருமக ளமளிப் போரொ","டடியவர் கயிலைக் கான ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A yogic plea: the poet asks that, with the breath's two paths closed, he go by the path of Siva to the one beyond, join Siva's light as in a marriage, his two deeds roasted, and enter the golden land beyond darkness and sun, where the body is made steady and both melt into one. The praise is Siva's dance, with anklets deafening the eight directions, the three gods and the Vedas bowing, trident, axe and deer whirling, Adisesha's crowns crackling and devotees crying 'Hara Hara', and Murugan as Uma's son, at Kailasa of the devotees.","n":"காலின் இருவழி அடைபட்டு, the two ways of the breath closed, and சிவசுடர் ... மணமென மருவி, joining Siva's light as in marriage, are the stages of the ascent. The second half of the plea does not split cleanly, and what is asked at its end (இளையவன் என வித்தாரம்) is not clear to me.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","praanan","vinai","thirukkoothu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:44"},"p:518":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":518,"t":["தேனுந்து முக்கனிகள் பால்செங் கருப்பிளநிர்","சீரும் பழ஧த்தசிவ ...... ம ளுறத்","தீதும் பிடித்தவினை யேதும் பொடித்துவிழ","சீவன் சிவச்சொருப ...... மெனதேறி","நானென்ப தற்றுயிரொ டூனென்ப தற்றுவெளி","நாதம் பரப்பிரம ...... வொளிமீதே","ஞானஞ் சுரப்பமகி ழாநந்த சித்தியொடெ","நாளுங் களிக்கபத ...... மருள்வாயே","வானந் தழைக்கஅடி யேனுஞ் செழிக்கஅயன்","மாலும் பிழைக்கஅலை ...... விடமாள","வாருங் கரத்தனெமை யாளுந் தகப்பன்மழு","மானின் கரத்தனருள் ...... முருகோனே","தானந் தனத்ததன னாவண்டு சுற்றிமது","தானுண் கடப்பமல ...... ரணிமார்பா","தானங் குறித்துஎமை யாளுந் திருக்கயிலை","சாலுங் குறத்திமகிழ் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks that his bound deeds be powdered, his self become Siva's form, the 'I' and the flesh fall away, and that in the light of nada and Brahman knowledge well up so that he rejoices daily in bliss and attainment; he asks for the feet. The praise names Siva, our father, whose hand took the ocean's poison so that the sky, the poet, Brahma and Vishnu might live, Murugan with the bee-hummed kadamba garland, and Kailasa, where Valli delights.","n":"சீவன் சிவச்சொருபம் என தேறி and நானென்பது அற்று, the self found to be Siva's form and the 'I' gone, are the language of union. The opening likens the hoped-for grace to honey, the three fruits, milk, cane and tender coconut, but its last words are corrupt.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["aanandham","sivam","naadham","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:44"},"p:519":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":519,"t":["நகைத்து வுருக்கி விழித்து மிரட்டி","நடித்து விதத்தி ...... லதிமோகம்","நடத்து சமத்தி முகத்தை மினுக்கி","நலத்தி லணைத்து ...... மொழியாலுந்","திகைத்த வரத்தி லடுத்த பொருட்கை","திரட்டி யெடுத்து ...... வரவேசெய்","திருட்டு முலைப்பெண் மருட்டு வலைக்குள்","தெவிட்டு கலைக்குள் ...... விழுவேனோ","பகைத்த அரக்கர் சிரத்தை யறுத்து","படர்ச்சி கறுத்த ...... மயிலேறிப்","பணைத்த கரத்த குணத்த மணத்த","பதத்த கனத்த ...... தனமாதை","மிகைத்த புனத்தி லிருத்தி யணைத்து","வெளுத்த பொருப்பி ...... லுறைநாதா","விரித்த சடைக்கு ளொருத்தி யிருக்க","ம்ருகத்தை யெடுத்தொர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of women who laugh, stare, threaten, act and polish their faces to stir infatuation, and gather the wealth that comes, and asks whether he must fall into the net of these thieving women. The praise names Murugan who cut off the demons' heads, rode the dark peacock and embraced the girl of the millet field, the Lord of the white mountain, who keeps a woman in his spread matted hair and holds the deer.","n":"திருட்டு முலைப்பெண் மருட்டு வலை, the deluding net of thieving women, names deceit and theft together. The closing marks, Ganga in the matted hair and the deer in the hand, are Siva's, here given to Murugan.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:44"},"p:520":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":520,"t":["பனியின் விந்துளி போலவே கருவினுறு","மளவி லங்கொரு சூசமாய் மிளகுதுவர்","பனைதெ னங்கனி போலவே பலகனியின் ...... வயறாகிப்","பருவ முந்தலை கீழதாய் நழுவிநில","மருவி யொன்பது வாசல்சே ருருவமுள","பதுமை யின்செயல் போலவே வளிகயிறி ...... னுடனாடி","மனவி தந்தெரி யாமலே மலசலமொ","டுடல்ந கர்ந்தழு தாறியே அனைமுலையின்","மயம யின்றொரு பாலனா யிகமுடைய ...... செயல்மேவி","வடிவ முன்செய்த தீமையா லெயுமுனையும்","அறம றந்தக மீதுபோய் தினதினமு","மனம ழிந்துடல் நாறினே னினியுனது ...... கழல்தாராய்","தனன தந்தன தானனா தனதனன","தினன திந்தன தீததோ திகுததிகு","தகுத குந்ததி தாகுதோ வெனமுழவு ...... வளைபேரி","தவில்க ணம்பறை காளமோ டிமிலைதொனி","யினமு ழங்கெழு வேலைபோ லதிரபொரு","சமர்மு கங்களின் மேவியே விருதுசொலு ...... மவுணோர்கள்","சினம ழிந்திட தேர்கள் தோலரிபரிகள்","குருதி யெண்டிசை மூடவே அலகைநரி","சிறையி னங்களி கூரவே நகையருளி ...... விடும்வேலா","சிவன்ம கிழ்ந்திரு ளானைமா முகன்மருவி","மனம கிழ்ந்தருள் கூரவோர் கயிலைமகிழ்","திகழ்கு றிஞ்சியின் மாதுமால் மருவுபுகழ் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet traces his life from a drop like dew in the womb, growing small as a seed and then as large as palm and coconut fruit, slipping head down to earth as a doll of nine gates moved by the rope of breath, crawling and crying at his mother's breast, and then, through past wrongs, forgetting virtue until his mind was ruined and his body stank; he asks now for the anklets. The praise is a battle in drum syllables in which the vel is thrown with a smile, and names Siva, Ganapati and Murugan rejoicing at Kailasa, and his union with the kurinji girl.","n":"ஒன்பது வாசல் சேர் உருவம் ... வளிகயிறினுடன் ஆடி, a figure of nine gates dancing on the rope of breath, makes the body a puppet. இனி, now, is the turn from the life told to the plea.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karu","yaakkai","vinai","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:44"},"p:521":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":521,"t":["புமியதனிற் ......ப்ரபுவான","புகலியில்வித் ...... தகர்போல","அமர்தகவித் ...... தொடைபாட","அடிமைதனக் ...... கருள்வாயே","சமரிலெதிர்த் ...... தசுர்மாளத்","தனியயில்விட் ...... டருள்வோனே","நமசிவயப் ...... பொருளானே","ரசதகிரிப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A short song: the poet asks to be given, as Murugan's servant, the gift of singing garlands of immortal poems like the wise one of Pugali, lord on earth. The praise names the lone vel thrown against the Asuras and Murugan as the meaning of Namasivaya, Lord of the silver mountain.","n":"புகலியில் வித்தகர் is Sambandar of Sirkazhi (Pugali), the model of the poet-saint; the plea is to be a poet as he was. நமசிவயப் பொருளானே makes Murugan the meaning of Siva's five syllables.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thamizh","panchaakkaram","thondu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:44"},"p:522":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":522,"t":["முகத்தைப் பிலுக்கி மெத்த மினுக்கித் தொடைத்து ரத்ந","முலைக்கச் சவிழ்த்த சைத்து ...... முசியாதே","முழுக்கக் கழப்பி யெத்தி மழுப்பிப் பொருட்ப றித்து","மொழிக்குட் படுத்த ழைத்த ...... மளிமீதே","நகைத்திட் டழுத்தி முத்த மளித்துக் களித்து மெத்த","நயத்திற் கழுத்தி றுக்கி ...... யணைவார்பால்","நடுக்குற் றவர்க்கு மெத்த மனத்தைப் பெருக்க வைத்து","நயத்துக் தியக்கி நித்த ...... மழிவேனோ","செகக்கச் செகக்க செக்க தரிக்கத் தரிக்க தக்க","திமித்தித் திமித்தி தித்தி ...... யெனஆடும்","செகத்துக் கொருத்தர் புத்ர நினைத்துத் துதித்த பத்த","ஜெனத்துக் கினித்த சித்தி ...... யருள்வோனே","மிசைத்துத் திடத்தொ டுற்று அசைத்துப் பொறுத்த ரக்கன்","மிகுத்துப் பெயர்த்தெ டுத்த ...... கயிலாய","மிசைக்குற் றடுத்து மற்ற பொருப்பைப் பொடித்தி டித்து","மிதித்துத் துகைத்து விட்ட ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of courtesans who polish their faces, loosen their breast-bands, deceive and snatch a man's goods, invite him to bed, laugh, kiss and grip his neck, and asks whether he must perish daily trembling for them. The praise names Murugan as son of the one who dances for the world in drum syllables, who gives attainment to devotees who think of him, and the Lord at Kailasa, which the demon heaved up, who powdered and trampled the other mountain.","n":"பொருட்பறித்து, snatching wealth, marks the courtesan. The end tells of the demon (Ravana, by his deed) who lifted Kailasa; whether மற்ற பொருப்பைப் பொடித்து, powdering the other mountain, means Krauncha is my guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:44"},"p:523":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":523,"t":["ஒருபது மிருபது மறுபது முடனறு","முணர்வுற இருபத ...... முளநாடி","உருகிட முழுமதி தழலென வொளிதிகழ்","வெளியொடு வொளிபெற ...... விரவாதே","தெருவினில் மரமென எவரொடு முரைசெய்து","திரிதொழி லவமது ...... புரியாதே","திருமகள் மருவிய திரள்புய அறுமுக","தெரிசனை பெறஅருள் ...... புரிவாயே","பரிவுட னழகிய பழமொடு கடலைகள்","பயறொடு சிலவகை ...... பணியாரம்","பருகிடு பெருவயி றுடையவர் பழமொழி","எழுதிய கணபதி ...... யிளையோனே","பெருமலை யுருவிட அடியவ ருருகிட","பிணிகெட அருள்தரு ...... குமரேசா","பிடியொடு களிறுகள் நடையிட கலைதிரள்","பிணையமர் திருமலை ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks that, knowing the ten, twenty, sixty and six, he seek the two feet within, melt, and shine with the light of the full moon and fire in inner space, and that he not wander in the streets talking with everyone like a block of wood, but be granted the sight of the six-faced Lord. The praise names Ganapati of the great belly, who eats fruit, gram, pulses and sweets and wrote the ancient tale, as Murugan's elder brother, and Tirumalai where elephants and deer roam.","n":"ஒருபது இருபது அறுபது உடன் அறு adds to ninety-six, the number of tattvas in the Tamil Siddha count; that reading is mine, and the song does not name them. தெருவினில் மரமென, like a tree in the street, sets idle talk against inward seeing. பழமொழி எழுதிய கணபதி is Ganapati as scribe of the Bharata.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","yogam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:45"},"p:524":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":524,"t":["கறுத்ததலை வெளிறு மிகுந்து","மதர்த்த இணை விழிகள் குழிந்து","கதுப்பிலுறு தசைகள் வறண்டு ...... செவிதோலாய்க்","கழுத்தடியு மடைய வளைந்து","கனத்தநெடு முதுகு குனிந்து","கதுப்புறுப லடைய விழுந்து ...... தடுநீர்சோர்","உறக்கம்வரு மளவி லெலும்பு","குலுக்கிவிடு மிருமல் தொடங்கி","உரத்தகன குரலு நெரிந்து ...... தடிகாலாய்","உரத்தநடை தளரு முடம்பு","பழுத்திடுமுன் மிகவும் விரும்பி","உனக்கடிமை படுமவர் தொண்டு ...... புரிவேனோ","சிறுத்தசெலு வதன ளிருந்து","பெருத்ததிரை யுததி கரந்து","செறித்தமறை கொணர நிவந்த ...... ஜெயமாலே","செறித்தவளை கடலில் வரம்பு","புதுக்கியிளை யவனோ டறிந்து","செயிர்த்தஅநு மனையு முகந்து ...... படையோடி","மறப்புரிசை வளையு மிலங்கை","யரக்கனொரு பதுமுடி சிந்த","வளைத்தசிலை விஜய முகுந்தன் ...... முருகோனே","மலர்க்கமல வடிவுள செங்கை","அயிற்குமர குகைவழி வந்த","மலைச்சிகர வடமலை நின்ற ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes old age: black hair whitened, eyes sunken, cheeks dried, ears turned to skin, neck and back bent, teeth fallen, drool, a cough that shakes the bones at night, a cracked voice and a stick for a leg; before his body ripens so, he asks whether he will eagerly serve those who are Murugan's servants. The praise tells of Vishnu who brought back the Vedas from the sea, bridged the ocean, took Hanuman to his side and cut Ravana's ten heads with his bow, and names Murugan, with the vel in his lotus hand, his nephew, at the northern mountain.","n":"உடம்பு பழுத்திடு முன், before the body ripens, puts the plea before old age, not at death. The service asked is to the Lord's servants (உனக்கடிமை படுமவர் தொண்டு), a step below serving the Lord himself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","ilamai","thondu","adiyaar"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:46"},"p:525":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":525,"t":["சரவண பவநிதி யறுமக குருபர","சரவண பவநிதி யறுமக குருபர","சரவண பவநிதி யறுமக குருபர ...... எனவோதித்","தமிழினி லுருகிய வடியவ ரிடமுறு","சனனம ரணமதை யொழிவுற சிவமுற","தருபிணி துளவர மெமதுயிர் சுகமுற ...... வருள்வாயே","கருணைய விழிபொழி யொருதனி முதலென","வருகரி திருமுகர் துணைகொளு மிளையவ","கவிதைய முதமொழி தருபவ ருயிர்பெற ...... வருள்நேயா","கடலுல கினில்வரு முயிர்படு மவதிகள்","கலகமி னையதுள கழியவும் நிலைபெற","கதியுமு னதுதிரு வடிநிழல் தருவது ...... மொருநாளே","திரிபுர மெரிசெயு மிறையவ ரருளிய","குமரச மரபுரி தணிகையு மிகுமுயர்","சிவகிரி யிலும்வட மலையிலு முலவிய ...... வடிவேலா","தினமுமு னதுதுதி பரவிய அடியவர்","மனதுகு டியுமிரு பொருளிலு மிலகுவ","திமிரம லமொழிய தினகர னெனவரு ...... பெருவாழ்வே","அரவணை மிசைதுயில் நரகரி நெடியவர்","மருகனெ னவெவரு மதிசய முடையவ","அமலிவி மலிபரை உமையவ ளருளிய ...... முருகோனே","அதலவி தலமுதல் கிடுகிடு கிடுவென","வருமயி லினிதொளிர் ஷடுமையில் நடுவுற","அழகினு லனமரு மரகர சிவசிவ ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks that, chanting 'Saravanabhava, treasure, six-faced one, supreme guru' and melting in Tamil like the devotees, his birth and death end, he reach Sivam, his disease end and his life be well, and that one day he be given the shade of Murugan's feet so that the sufferings of creatures on the sea-girt earth may cease. The praise names Murugan as Ganapati's younger brother, who gives life to poets, who walks at Tanigai, Sivagiri and the northern mountain, who dispels the darkness of impurity from devotees' minds like the sun, nephew of Vishnu and Narasimha, son of Uma, on the peacock that shakes the nether worlds.","n":"The opening repeats the formula சரவணபவ நிதி அறுமுக குருபர three times, so the song enacts the chanting it describes. The plea widens beyond the poet to all creatures (கடலுலகினில் வரும் உயிர்படும் அவதிகள்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mandhiram","piravi","thamizh","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:46"},"p:526":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":526,"t":["நெச்சுப் பிச்சிப் புட்பத் தட்பக்","கச்சிக் கச்சுற் றறன்மேவி","நெறித்து வெறித்து இருட்டை வெருட்டிய","நிரைதரு மருமலர் செருகிடு பரிமள","நிறையுறை மதுகர ...... நெடிதாடி","நிச்சிக் கச்சப் பட்டுச் சிக்கற்","றொப்புக் கொப்புக் குயர்வாகி","நெளித்த சுளித்த விழைக்கு ளழைத்துமை","நிகரென வகருவு முகுபுகை தொகுமிகு","நிகழ்புழு கொழுகிய ...... குழன்மேலும்","வச்ரப் பச்சைப் பொட்டிட் டப்பொட்","டுக்குட் செக்கர்ப் ப்ரபைபோல","வளைத்த தழைத்த பிறைக்கு முறைக்குமன்","மதசிலை யதுவென மகபதி தனுவென","மதிதில தமும்வதி ...... நுதன்மேலும்","மச்சச் செச்சைச் சித்ரச் சத்ரப்","பொற்பக் கத்திச் சையனாகி","மனத்தி னனைத்து மணைத்த துணைப்பத","மலரல திலைநிலை யெனமொழி தழியமெய்","வழிபட லொழிவனை ...... யருள்வாயே","நச்சுத் துச்சொப் பிச்சுக் குட்டத்","துட்டக் கட்டத் தசிகாண","நடத்தி விடத்தை யுடைத்த படத்தினில்","நடநவில் கடலிடை யடுபடை தொடுமுகில்","நகைமுக திருவுறை ...... மணிமார்பன்","நத்தத் தைச்சக் ரத்தைப் பத்மத்","தைக்கைப் பற்றிப் பொருமாய","னரிக்கு மரிக்கு மெரிக்கும் விருப்புற","நசிதரு நிசிசர ருடகுட லிடல்செய்த","நரகரி யொருதிரு ...... மருகோனே","கச்சுத் தச்சுப் பொற்கட் டிட்டுப்","பட்டுக் குட்பட் டமுதாலுங்","கருப்பி ரசத்து முருச்செய் துவைச்சிடு","கனதன பரிமள முழுகுப னிருபுய","கனகதி வியமணி ...... யணிமார்பா","கைச்சத் திக்குக் கெற்சித் தொக்கப்","பட்சிக் கக்கொட் டசுராதி","கறுத்த நிறத்த அரக்கர் குலத்தொடு","கறுவிய சிறியவ கடவைகள் புடைபடு","கடவட மலையுறை ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes a woman's hair dense with flowers, bees and incense smoke and her forehead with its mark like Kama's or Indra's bow, and asks for grace for one who, drawn by these, gave up the true worship that says there is no refuge but Murugan's flower feet. The praise names Vishnu who danced on the serpent's hood, holds conch, discus and lotus and tore the night-demon, Murugan as his nephew with twelve shoulders, who fought the dark demon clans, at the northern mountain.","n":"The plea turns on மெய் வழிபடல் ஒழிவனை, one who left off true worship, and its object, the feet as the only support (துணைப் பதமலர் அலது இலை). The rhythm compresses the words heavily and the split of the plea is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","vazhipaadu","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:46"},"p:527":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":527,"t":["கோங்கிள நீரிளக வீங்குப யோதரமும்","வாங்கிய வேல்விழியும் ...... இருள்கூருங்","கூந்தலு நீள்வளைகொள் காந்தளு நூலிடையும்","மாந்தளிர் போல்வடிவும் ...... மிகநாடிப்","பூங்கொடி யார்கலவி நீங்கரி தாகிமிகு","தீங்குட னேயுழலும் ...... உயிர்வாழ்வு","பூண்டடி யேனெறியில் மாண்டிங னேநரகில்","வீழ்ந்தலை யாமலருள் ...... புரிவாயே","பாங்கியும் வேடுவரும் ஏங்கிட மாமுநியும்","வேங்கையு மாய்மறமி ...... னுடன்வாழ்வாய்","பாண்டவர் தேர்கடவும் நீண்டபி ரான்மருக","பாண்டிய னீறணிய ...... மொழிவோனே","வேங்கையும் வாரணமும் வேங்யு மானும்வளர்","வேங்கட மாமலையி ...... லுறைவோனே","வேண்டிய போதடியர் வேண்டிய போகமது","வேண்டவெ றாதுதவு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of desiring women's breasts, spear eyes, dark hair, bangled hands and mango-leaf bodies, unable to leave their union and wandering in great wrong, and asks not to die that way and fall into hell. The praise names Murugan who became a sage and a vengai tree to win the hunter girl, nephew of the one who drove the Pandavas' chariot, who told the Pandya king to wear holy ash, Lord of Venkatam where tigers, elephants and deer live, who gives his devotees whatever they ask.","n":"நரகில் வீழ்ந்து அலையாமல், not to fall and toss in hell, is a rarer fear than Death in the book. பாண்டியன் நீறணிய மொழிவோனே again identifies Murugan with Sambandar, who brought the Pandya king to wear the ash.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","naragam","thiruneeru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:46"},"p:528":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":528,"t":["சாந்தமில் மோகவெரி காந்திய வாவனில","மூண்டவி யாதசம ...... யவிரோத","சாங்கலை வாரிதியை நீந்தவொ ணாதுலகர்","தாந்துணை யாவரென ...... மடவார்மேல்","ஏந்திள வார்முளரி சாந்தணி மார்பினொடு","தோயந்துரு காவறிவு ...... தடுமாறி","ஏங்கிட ஆருயிரை வாங்கிய காலன்வசம்","யான்தனி போய்விடுவ ...... தியல்போதான்","காந்தளி னானகர மான்தரு கானமயில்","காந்தவி சாகசர ...... வணவேளே","காண்டகு தேவர்பதி யாண்டவ னேசுருதி","யாண்டகை யேயிபமின் ...... மணவாளா","வேந்தகு மாரகுக சேந்தம யூரவட","வேங்கட மாமலையி ...... லுறைவோனே","வேண்டிய போதடியர் வேண்டிய போகமது","வேண்டவெ றாதுதவு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet tells how he could not swim the sea of quarrelling sects or quench the fire of desire, trusted worldly people as his help, and melted over women until his understanding failed, and asks whether it is right that he go alone into the power of Death, who takes the life as one wails. The praise names Valli of the forest, Murugan as Visakha and Saravana, Lord of the gods and of the Veda, husband of Devayanai, Guha and Senthan, at the Venkatam hill, who gives his devotees whatever they ask.","n":"யான் தனி போய்விடுவது இயல்போதான், is it right that I go alone, is the plea: at death the friends trusted (உலகர் தாம் துணையாவர்) do not come. சமய விரோத சாங்கலை வாரி, the ocean of hostile sects, stands beside desire as the second thing the poet cannot cross.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","samayam","avaa","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:46"},"p:529":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":529,"t":["வரிசேர்ந்திடு சேல்கய லோவெனு","முழைவார்ந்திடு வேலையு நீலமும்","வடுவாங்கிடு வாள்விழி மாதர்கள் ...... வலையாலே","வளர்கோங்கிள மாமுகை யாகிய","தனவாஞ்சையி லேமுக மாயையில்","வளமாந்தளிர் போல்நிற மாகிய ...... வடிவாலே","இருள்போன்றிடு வார்குழல் நீழலில்","மயல்சேர்ந்திடு பாயலின் மீதுற","இனிதாங்கனி வாயமு தூறல்கள் ...... பருகாமே","எனதாந்தன தானவை போயற","மலமாங்கடு மோகவி காரமு","மிவைநீங்கிட வேயிரு தாளினை ...... யருள்வாயே","கரிவாம்பரி தேர்திரள் சேனையு","முடனாந்துரி யோதன னாதிகள்","களமாண்டிட வேயொரு பாரத ...... மதிலேகிக்","கனபாண்டவர் தேர்தனி லேயெழு","பரிதூண்டிய சாரதி யாகிய","கதிரோங்கிய நேமிய னாமரி ...... ரகுராமன்","திரைநீண்டிரை வாரியும் வாலியும்","நெடிதோங்கும ராமர மேழொடு","தெசமாஞ்சிர ராவண னார்முடி ...... பொடியாகச்","சிலைவாங்கிய நாரண னார்மரு","மகனாங்குக னேபொழில் சூழ்தரு","திருவேங்கட மாமலை மேவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks to be spared drinking lip-nectar on the bed under women's fish-like eyes and dark hair, caught in their net and the illusion of their faces, and to be given the two feet so that 'mine' and 'theirs' fall away and the harsh frenzy of impurity and delusion ends. The praise names Krishna as the charioteer of the Pandavas against Duryodhana, the discus-bearer, and Rama who pierced Vali and the seven sal trees and shattered Ravana's ten heads, and Murugan as Guha, their nephew, at Tiruvenkatam.","n":"எனதாம் தனதானவை போய் அற, that what is mine and what is theirs go, names attachment as the root beneath lust; மலமாம் கடு மோக விகாரம் makes the delusion itself an impurity.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","pattru","malam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:46"},"p:530":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":530,"t":["அல்லிவிழி யாலு முல்லைநகை யாலு","மல்லல்பட ஆசைக் ...... கடலீயும்","அள்ளவினி தாகி நள்ளிரவு போலு","முள்ளவினை யாரத் ...... தனமாரும்","இல்லுமிளை யோரு மெல்ல அயலாக","வல்லெருமை மாயச் ...... சமனாரும்","எள்ளியென தாவி கொள்ளைகொளு நாளில்","உய்யவொரு நீபொற் ...... கழல்தாராய்","தொல்லைமறை தேடி யில்லையெனு நாதர்","சொல்லுமுப தேசக் ...... குருநாதா","துள்ளிவிளை யாடு புள்ளியுழை நாண","வெள்ளிவன மீதுற் ...... றுறைவோனே","வல்லசுரர் மாள நல்லசுரர் வாழ","வல்லைவடி வேலைத் ...... தொடுவோனே","வள்ளிபடர் சாரல் வள்ளிமலை மேவு","வள்ளிமண வாளப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet tells of women whose lily eyes and jasmine smiles raise a sea of desire, and of the day when home and young kin slowly turn strangers and Yama on his strong buffalo mocks him and plunders his life; he asks for Murugan's golden anklets on that day so that he may be saved. The praise names the guru who teaches the Lord whom the ancient Vedas sought and could not find, the one who dwells in the forest that shamed the leaping spotted deer, and whose vel killed the asuras so that the gods might live, Valli's bridegroom at Vallimalai.","n":"இல்லும் இளையோரும் மெல்ல அயலாக, home and the young ones slowly becoming strangers, is the dying man's isolation before Death's arrival (வல்லெருமை மாயச் சமனாரும் எள்ளி). தொல்லைமறை தேடி இல்லையெனு நாதர் is Siva, whom the Vedas sought and could not find, and Murugan is his teacher. The spotted deer shamed (புள்ளியுழை நாண) may allude to Valli's birth from a doe; that is my guess. The line அள்ளவினி தாகி நள்ளிரவு போலும் உள்ளவினை யாரத் தனமாரும் is hard to construe.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kootru","kaamam","thiruvadi","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:47"},"p:531":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":531,"t":["ஐயுமுறு நோயு மையலும வாவி","னைவருமு பாயப் ...... பலநூலின்","அள்ளல்கட வாது துள்ளியதில் மாயு","முள்ளமுமில் வாழ்வைக் ...... கருதாசைப்","பொய்யுமக லாத மெய்யைவள ராவி","உய்யும்வகை யோகத் ...... தணுகாதே","புல்லறிவு பேசி யல்லல் படு வேனை","நல்லஇரு தாளிற் ...... புணர்வாயே","மெய்யபொழில் நீடு தையலைமு நாலு","செய்யபுய மீதுற் ...... றணைவோனே","வெள்ளையிப மேறு வள்ளல்கிளை வாழ","வெள்ளமுது மாவைப் ...... பொருதோனே","வையமுழு தாளு மையமயில் வீர","வல்லமுரு காமுத் ...... தமிழ்வேளே","வள்ளிபடர் சாரல் வள்ளிமலை மேவு","வள்ளிமண வாளப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet lists what holds him: phlegm and disease, infatuation, the five senses of desire, the mire of many books of stratagem he cannot cross, a heart that leaps and dies in them, the false wish for household life, a body that never turns to the yoga by which the soul is saved; he, who talks petty knowledge and suffers, asks to be joined to the two good feet. The praise names the one whose twelve red shoulders embrace the woman of the true grove, who fought Sura as the mango in the sea so that the kin of the rider of the white elephant might live, the lord of the peacock and of the three Tamils, Valli's bridegroom at Vallimalai.","n":"உபாயப் பலநூலின் அள்ளல், the swamp of many books of means, sets bookish learning beside phlegm and lust as one more bondage; புல்லறிவு பேசி, 'speaking petty knowledge', is the poet's verdict on himself. The remedy named is yoga (ஆவி உய்யும்வகை யோகம்) and then the feet. வெள்ளமுது மாவை, Sura as the mango tree in the sea, is the usual battle myth; வெள்ளையிபம் ஏறு வள்ளல் is Indra on Airavata.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pullarivu","yogam","avaa","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:47"},"p:532":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":532,"t":["கையொத்து வாழு மிந்த மெய்யொத்த வாழ்வி கந்து","பொய்யொத்த வாழ்வு கண்டு ...... மயலாகிக்","கல்லுக்கு நேரும் வஞ்ச வுள்ளத்தர் மேல்வி ழுந்து","கள்ளப்ப யோத ரங்க ...... ளுடன்மேவி","உய்யப்ப டாமல் நின்று கையர்க்கு பாய மொன்று","பொய்யர்க்கு மேய யர்ந்து ...... ளுடைநாயேன்","உள்ளப்பெ றாக நின்று தொய்யப்ப டாம லென்று","முள்ளத்தின் மாய்வ தொன்றை ...... மொழியாயோ","ஐயப்ப டாத ஐந்து பொய்யற்ற சோலை தங்கு","தெய்வத்தெய் வானை கொங்கை ...... புணர்வோனே","அல்லைப்பொ றாமு ழங்கு சொல்லுக்ர சேவ லொன்று","வெல்லப்ப தாகை கொண்ட ...... திறல்வேலா","வையத்தை யோடி யைந்து கையற்கு வீசு தந்தை","மெய்யொத்த நீதி கண்ட ...... பெரியோனே","வள்ளிக்கு ழாம டர்ந்த வள்ளிக்கல் மீது சென்று","வள்ளிக்கு வேடை கொண்ட ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses that he left the true life for a false one, became infatuated, fell on women with hearts like stone and thieving breasts, and grew weary serving the tricks of the low and the liars; he asks Murugan to tell him the one thing by which the mind dies within and never droops again. The praise names Devayanai of the five trees of heaven, the fierce cock on his banner that crows against the night, the one who ran round the world while the father gave to the five-handed one, and the lord who climbed Valli's hill thick with creepers in longing for Valli.","n":"உள்ளத்தின் மாய்வது ஒன்றை மொழியாயோ asks for a teaching that makes the mind itself die, not only for rescue; how the poet means it is my inference. வையத்தை ஓடி ஐந்து கையற்கு வீசு தந்தை points to the story of the fruit: Murugan circled the world while Siva gave the prize to Ganapati; the following words (மெய்யொத்த நீதி கண்ட) and much of the first half are hard to split.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mayal","vanjam","kaamam","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:47"},"p:533":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":533,"t":["முல்லைக்கு மார னங்கை வில்லுக்கு மாதர் தங்கள்","பல்லுக்கும் வாடி யின்ப ...... முயலாநீள்","முள்ளுற்ற கால்ம டிந்து கொள்ளிக்குள் மூழ்கி வெந்து","பள்ளத்தில் வீழ்வ தன்றி ...... யொருஞான","எல்லைக்கு மார ணங்கள் சொல்லித்தொ ழாவ ணங்கு","மெல்லைக்கும் வாவி நின்ற ...... னருள்நாமம்","எள்ளற்கு மால யர்ந்து வுள்ளத்தி லாவ என்று","முள்ளப்பெ றாரி ணங்கை ...... யொழிவேனோ","அல்லைக்க வானை தந்த வல்லிக்கு மார்பி லங்க","அல்லிக்கொள் மார்ப லங்கல் ...... புனைவோனே","அள்ளற்ப டாத கங்கை வெள்ளத்து வாவி தங்கி","மெள்ளச்ச ரோரு கங்கள் ...... பயில்நாதா","வல்லைக்கு மார கந்த தில்லைப்பு ராரி மைந்த","மல்லுப்பொ ராறி ரண்டு ...... புயவீரா","வள்ளிக்கு ழாம டர்ந்த வள்ளிக்கல் மீது சென்று","வள்ளிக்கு வேடை கொண்ட ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet, wilting before jasmine, Kama's bow and women's teeth and striving for pleasure, sees the end ahead, the body folding, burned in the firebrands and dropped into the pit, and asks whether he will leave the company of those who never think of Murugan's name of grace. The praise names the one who wears a garland for the creeper-like woman given by the elephant, the lord born among lotuses in the pool fed by the Ganga, the son of the destroyer of the cities at Thillai with twelve wrestling shoulders, who went up the creeper-thick hill in longing for Valli.","n":"கொள்ளிக்குள் மூழ்கி வெந்து பள்ளத்தில் வீழ்வது, sinking into the pyre's brands, burning and falling into the pit, gives the end of the pleasure-seeker in one line. The middle (ஞான எல்லைக்கு மாரணங்கள் ... இணங்கை ஒழிவேனோ) is very hard to split and my reading of it is a guess. அல்லைக்கவானை தந்த வல்லி is probably Devayanai, raised by the elephant Airavata.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","nilaiyaamai","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:47"},"p:534":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":534,"t":["கள்ளக்கு வாற்பை தொள்ளைப்பு லாற்பை","துள்ளிக்க னார்க்க ...... யவுகோப","கள்வைத்த தோற்பை பொள்ளுற்ற காற்பை","கொள்ளைத்து ராற்பை ...... பசுபாச","அள்ளற்பை மாற்பை ஞெள்ளற்பை சீப்பை","வெள்ளிட்ட சாப்பி ...... சிதமீரல்","அள்ளச்சு வாக்கள் சள்ளிட்டி ழாப்பல்","கொள்ளப்ப டாக்கை ...... தவிர்வேனோ","தெள்ளத்தி சேர்ப்ப வெள்ளத்தி மாற்கும்","வெள்ளுத்தி மாற்கு ...... மருகோனே","சிள்ளிட்ட காட்டி லுள்ளக்கி ரார்க்கொல்","புள்ளத்த மார்க்கம் ...... வருவோனே","வள்ளிச்சன் மார்க்கம் விள்ளைக்கு நோக்க","வல்லைக்கு ளேற்று ...... மிளையோனே","வள்ளிக்கு ழாத்து வள்ளிக்கல் காத்த","வள்ளிக்கு வாய்த்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet names his body as a string of bags: a heap-bag of deceit, a holed bag of flesh, a skin bag holding the toddy of anger, a leaking bag of wind, a bag of rubbish, a bag of the mire of soul and bond, of delusion, of pus, and a carcass for dogs to drag and tear, a body no one would take; he asks whether he will be rid of it. The praise names Murugan as nephew of Vishnu on the flood, as the one who came by the forest path loud with crickets, and as the lord destined for Valli who guarded her creeper-thick hill.","n":"Every unit of the first half ends in பை, bag: குவாற்பை, புலாற்பை, தோற்பை, காற்பை, அள்ளற்பை, சீப்பை. பசுபாச அள்ளற்பை makes the body the swamp in which the soul's bond is held. Several of the bag-words (ஞெள்ளற்பை, துராற்பை) and most of the praise (வெள்ளுத்தி மால், சிள்ளிட்ட காட்டில் உள்ளக் கிரார்) are obscure to me.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","paasam","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:47"},"p:535":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":535,"t":["வெல்லிக்கு வீக்கு முல்லைக்கை வீக்கு","வில்லிக்க தாக்க ...... ருதும்வேளால்","வில்லற் றவாக்கொள் சொல்லற்று காப்பொய்","யில்லத் துறாக்க ...... வலைமேவு","பல்லத்தி வாய்க்க அல்லற்ப டாக்கை","நல்லிற் பொறாச்ச ...... மயமாறின்","பல்லத்த மார்க்க வல்லர்க்கர் மூர்க்கர்","கல்விக் கலாத்த ...... லையலாமோ","அல்லைக்கொல் வார்த்தை சொல்லிக்கி தோத்து","சொல்குக்கு டார்த்த ...... இளையோனே","அல்லுக்கு மாற்றி னெல்லுக்க மேற்புல்","கெல்லைப்ப டாக்க ...... ருணைவேளே","வல்லைக்கு மேற்றர் தில்லைக்கு மேற்றர்","வல்லிக்கு மேற்ற ...... ரருள்வோனே","வள்ளிக்கு ழாத்து வள்ளிக்கல் காத்த","வள்ளிக்கு வாய்த்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet, struck by Kama with his jasmine arrows, caught in a house of false talk and care, asks whether he must be tossed among the learned quarrels of the stubborn logicians of the six sects and their many paths of meaning. The praise names the younger brother whose cock crows away the night, the god of boundless compassion, the lord who grants grace and is welcomed at Vallai, Thillai and by Valli, the one destined for Valli who guarded her hill.","n":"சமயமாறின் பல்லத்த மார்க்க வல்லர்க்கர் மூர்க்கர் கல்விக் கலாத்து அலையலாமோ, 'must I wander in the learned wrangling of the stubborn logicians of the six sects', is the clearest line; the rest of the song is dense rhythm and its splitting is mostly my guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["samayam","kaamam","pullarivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:47"},"p:536":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":536,"t":["ககனமு மநிலமு மனல்புனல் நிலமமை","கள்ளப் புலாற்கி ...... ருமிவீடு","கனலெழ மொழிக்தரு சினமென மதமிகு","கள்வைத்த தோற்பை ...... சுமவாதே","யுகஇறு திகளனலு மிறுதியி லொருபொருள்","உள்ளக்க ணோக்கு ...... மறிவூறி","ஒளிதிக ழருவுரு வெனுமறை யிறுதியி","லுள்ளத்தை நோக்க ...... அருள்வாயே","ம்ருகமத பரிமள விகசித நளினநள்","வெள்ளைப்பி ராட்டி ...... இறைகாணா","விடதர குடிலச டிலமிசை வெகுமுக","வெள்ளத்தை யேற்ற ...... பதிவாழ்வே","வகுளமு முகுளித வழைகளு மலிபுன","வள்ளிக்கு லாத்தி ...... கிரிவாழும்","வனசரர் மரபினில் வருமொரு மரகத","வள்ளிக்கு வாய்த்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks not to carry the body, a thieving worm-house of flesh made of sky, wind, fire, water and earth, a skin bag holding the toddy of anger and pride; he asks to be given the inner eye that sees the one thing that remains at the end of the ages, the shining formless form the end of the Vedas speaks of. The praise names Murugan as the treasure of Siva, whom Brahma, lord of the white lady in the lotus, could not see, who bears the poison and took the many-mouthed flood on his matted hair, and as husband of the emerald Valli of the hunters' clan on the hill.","n":"The plea moves from the five elements of the body (ககனம், அநிலம், அனல், புனல், நிலம்) to the one thing beyond the end of the ages (யுக இறுதிகளின் இறுதியில் ஒரு பொருள்) seen with the inner eye. அருவுரு, formless form, and மறை இறுதி, the Veda's end, are Vedantic terms. வெள்ளைப் பிராட்டி இறை is Brahma as Saraswati's lord.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","vedaantham","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:47"},"p:537":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":537,"t":["அல்லசல டைந்த வில்லடல நங்கன்","அல்லிமல ரம்பு ...... தனையேவ","அள்ளியெரி சிந்த பிள்ளைமதி தென்ற","லையமது கிண்ட ...... அணையூடே","சொல்லுமர விந்த வல்லிதனி நின்று","தொல்லைவினை யென்று ...... முனியாதே","துய்யவரி வண்டு செய்யுமது வுண்டு","துள்ளியக டம்பு ...... தரவேணும்","கல்லசல மங்கை யெல்லையில் விரிந்த","கல்விகரை கண்ட ...... புலவோனே","கள்ளொழுகு கொன்றை வள்ளல்தொழ அன்று","கல்லறை வொன்றை ...... யருள்வோனே","வல்லசுர ரஞ்ச நல்லசுரர் விஞ்ச","வல்லமைதெ ரிந்த ...... மயில்வீரா","வள்ளிபடர் கின்ற வள்ளிமலை சென்று","வள்ளியை மணந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A song for a lovesick girl: bodiless Kama shoots his lily arrows, the young moon scatters fire, the south wind stirs her doubt on the bed, and the speaker asks that Murugan, without being angry at the lotus-like girl standing alone or calling it her old karma, give her his kadamba garland where the striped bees drink honey. The praise names him the poet who reached the shore of all learning, who taught the one word when Siva wearing honeyed konrai worshipped him, and the peacock hero who married Valli on Vallimalai.","n":"The girl (அரவிந்த வல்லி) is spoken of, not speaking, so the voice is a mother's or friend's, and the cure asked is the god's garland, the lover's token. கொன்றை வள்ளல்தொழ அன்று கல்லறைவு ஒன்றை அருள்வோனே seems to mean teaching Siva the one word to learn, the Om story without the word Om; the split is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:47"},"p:538":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":538,"t":["குடிவாழ்க்கை யன்னை மனையாட்டி பிள்ளை","குயில்போற்ப்ர சன்ன ...... மொழியார்கள்","குலம்வாய்த்த நல்ல தனம்வாய்த்த தென்ன","குருவார்த்தை தன்னை ...... யுணராதே","இடநாட்கள் வெய்ய நமனீட்டி தொய்ய","இடர்கூட்ட இன்னல் ...... கொடுபோகி","இடுகாட்டி லென்னை எரியூட்டு முன்னு","னிருதாட்கள் தம்மை ...... யுணர்வேனோ","வடநாட்டில் வெள்ளி மலைகாத்து புள்ளி","மயில்மேற்றி கழ்ந்த ...... குமரேசா","வடிவாட்டி வள்ளி அடிபோற்றி வள்ளி","மலைகாத்த நல்ல ...... மணவாளா","அடிநாட்கள் செய்த பிழைநீக்கி யென்னை","யருள்போற்றும் வண்மை ...... தரும்வாழ்வே","அடிபோற்றி யல்லி முடிசூட்ட வல்ல","அடியார்க்கு நல்ல ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses that he took household life, mother, wife, children, women with cuckoo voices, good family and wealth as all he needed and did not grasp the guru's word; he asks whether, before cruel Yama ends his days and he is carried off in grief and burned at the cremation ground, he will come to know Murugan's two feet. The praise names the Kumara on the spotted peacock who guards the silver mountain in the north, the good bridegroom who praised beautiful Valli's feet and guarded Vallimalai, who removes the faults of earlier days, and who is good to devotees who crown his feet with lilies.","n":"The household, not the courtesan, is the snare here: குடிவாழ்க்கை அன்னை மனையாட்டி பிள்ளை, all taken as a gain (வாய்த்தது) while the குருவார்த்தை went unheard. வள்ளி அடிபோற்றி, praising Valli's feet, reverses the usual direction of worship, and 539 does the same.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","kootru","guru","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:47"},"p:539":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":539,"t":["சிரமங்க மங்கைகண் செவிவஞ்ச நெஞ்சுசெஞ்","சலமென்பு திண்பொருந் ...... திடுமாயம்","சிலதுன்ப மின்பமொன் றிறவந்து பின்புசெந்","தழலிண்கண் வெந்துசிந் ...... திடஆவி","விரைவின்க ணந்தகன் பொரவந்த தென்றுவெந்","துயர்கொண்ட லைந்துலைந் ...... தழியாமுன்","வினையொன்று மின்றிநன் றியலொன்றி நின்பதம்","வினவென்று அன்புதந் ...... தருள்வாயே","அரவின்கண் முன்துயின் றருள்கொண்ட லண்டர்கண்","டமரஞ்ச மண்டிவந் ...... திடுசூரன்","அகலம் பிளந்தணைந் தகிலம் பரந்திரங்","கிடஅன் றுடன்றுகொன் ...... றிடும்வேலா","மரைவெங் கயம்பொருந் திடவண் டினங்குவிந்","திசையொன்ற மந்திசந் ...... துடனாடும்","வரையின்கண் வந்துவண் குறமங்கை பங்கயம்","வரநின்று கும்பிடும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes the body as an illusion held together of head, limbs, eyes, ears, a deceitful heart, blood and bone, which has a few sorrows and joys and then burns in red fire, while the soul, crying that Death has come quickly to fight, wanders and perishes; before that he asks for a love free of karma that seeks Murugan's feet. The praise names Vishnu who sleeps on the serpent, the vel that split Sura's chest when the gods fled, and the lord who, on the hill where deer, elephants, bees and monkeys play, stood and worshipped the lotus feet of the hunter girl.","n":"திண்பொருந்திடு மாயம, an illusion firmly joined, is the body; அந்தகன் பொரவந்தது, 'Death has come to fight', is the soul's cry at the end. The closing line (குறமங்கை பங்கயம் வரநின்று கும்பிடும்) has the god worship the girl's feet, the same reversal as 538.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","kootru","anbu","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:47"},"p:540":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":540,"t":["வரைவில்பொய் மங்கையர் தங்க ளஞ்சன","விழியையு கந்துமு கந்து கொண்டடி","வருடிநி தம்பம ளைந்து தெந்தென ...... அளிகாடை","மயில்குயி லன்றிலெ னும்பு ளின்பல","குரல்செய்தி ருந்துபி னுந்தி யென்கிற","மடுவில்வி ழுந்துகி டந்து செந்தழல் ...... மெழுகாகி","உருகியு கந்திதழ் தின்று மென்றுகை","யடியின கங்கள்வ ரைந்து குங்கும","உபய தனங்கள்த தும்ப அன்புட ...... னணையாமஞ்","சுலவிய கொண்டைகு லைந்த லைந்தெழ","அமளியில் மின்சொல்ம ருங்கி லங்கிட","உணர்வழி யின்பம றந்து நின்றனை ...... நினைவேனோ","விரவி நெருங்குகு ரங்கி னங்கொடு","மொகுமொகெ னுங்கட லுங்க டந்துறு","விசைகொடி லங்கைபு குந்த ருந்தவர் ...... களிகூர","வெயில்நில வும்பரு மிம்ப ரும்படி","ஜெயஜெய வென்றுவி டுங்கொ டுங்கணை","விறல்நிரு தன்தலை சிந்தி னன்திரு ...... மருகோனே","அருகர் கணங்கள்பி ணங்கி டும்படி","மதுரையில் வெண்பொடி யும்ம ரந்திட","அரகர சங்கர வென்று வென்றருள் ...... புகழ்வேலா","அறம்வளர் சுந்தரி மைந்த தண்டலை","வயல்கள் பொருந்திய சந்த வண்கரை","யரிவை விலங்கலில் வந்து கந்தருள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes lovemaking with false women, from delighting in their kohl eyes and stroking their feet to the cries of birds, falling into the pool of the navel, melting like wax in fire, biting lips, nail marks, the scattered hair and the bed, and asks whether he will ever forget that pleasure, which wipes out awareness, and think of Murugan. The praise names Rama, who crossed the sea with the monkeys, entered Lanka and cut off the demon's heads, as his uncle, and Murugan as the vel who at Madurai scattered the Jains and spread the white ash crying 'Hara Hara Sankara', son of Aram Valar Sundari, who came gladly to the woman's hill.","n":"வரைவில் பொய் மங்கையர், 'false women without bounds', comes close to the classical word for courtesans (வரைவின் மகளிர்), but no money is named. மதுரையில் வெண்பொடியும் மரந்திட அரகர சங்கர என்று வென்றருள் is Sambandar's victory over the Jains, given to Murugan. அறம்வளர் சுந்தரி is the goddess Dharmasamvardhini; அரிவை விலங்கல், 'the woman's hill', is Vallimalai.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","kaamam","samayam","thamizh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:47"},"p:541":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":541,"t":["அகத்தி னைக்கொண் டிப்புவி மேல்சில","தினத்து மற்றொன் றுற்றறி யாதுபின்","அவத்துள் வைக்குஞ் சித்தச னாரடு ...... கணையாலே","அசுத்த மைக்கண் கொட்புறு பாவையர்","நகைத்து ரைக்கும் பொய்க்கடல் மூழ்கியெ","அலக்க ணிற்சென் றுத்தடு மாறியெ ...... சிலநாள்போய்","இகத்தை மெய்க்கொண் டிப்புவி பாலர்பொன்","மயக்கி லுற்றம் பற்றைவி டாதுட","லிளைப்பி ரைப்பும் பித்தமு மாய்நரை ...... முதிர்வாயே","எமக்க யிற்றின் சிக்கினி லாமுனுன்","மலர்ப்ப தத்தின் பத்திவி டாமன","திருக்கு நற்றொண் டர்க்கிணை யாகவு ...... னருள்தாராய்","புகழ்ச்சி லைக்கந் தர்ப்பனு மேபொடி","படச்சி ரித்தண் முப்புர நீறுசெய்","புகைக்க னற்கண் பெற்றவர் காதலி ...... யருள்பாலா","புவிக்குள் யுத்தம் புத்திரர் சேயர","சனைத்து முற்றுஞ் செற்றிட வேபகை","புகட்டி வைக்குஞ் சக்கிர பாணிதன் ...... மருகோனே","திகழ்க்க டப்பம் புட்பம தார்புய","மறைத்து ருக்கொண் டற்புத மாகிய","தினைப்பு னத்தின் புற்றுறை பாவையை ...... யணைசீலா","செகத்தி லுச்சம் பெற்றம ராவதி","யதற்கு மொப்பென் றுற்றழ கேசெறி","திருக்க ழுக்குன் றத்தினில் மேவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet tells how, struck by Kama's arrows that waste a life, he sank in the sea of lies of laughing kohl-eyed women and fell into sorrow, then took this world as real, clung to children and gold, and grew weak, breathless, bilious and grey; he asks that before he is caught in Yama's rope Murugan grant him grace to be like the good devotees whose minds never leave devotion to his flower feet. The praise names Siva who burned Kama and the three cities with a laugh and a smoking eye, Vishnu the discus-bearer who set enmity among men so that sons and grandsons perished in war, and Murugan who hid his kadamba-garlanded shoulders in a strange disguise to embrace the girl of the millet field, at Thirukkazhukundram, equal to the city of the gods.","n":"The life moves in stages: Kama's arrows and the women, then இகத்தை மெய்க்கொண்டு, taking this world as true, with children (பாலர்) and gold, then old age, then எமக்கயிறு, Yama's rope. The goal is not release but equality with the devotees (நற்றொண்டர்க்கு இணையாக). The discus-bearer who set kin against kin so that all their sons died (புத்திரர் சேயர் அசனைத்தும் செற்றிட) points to Krishna and the Bharata war; the text does not name it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pattru","kaamam","kootru","thondu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:48"},"p:542":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":542,"t":["எழுகுநிறை நாபி அரிபிரமர் சோதி","யிலகுமரன் மூவர் ...... முதலானோர்","இறைவியெனு மாதி பரைமுலையி னு஡றி","யெழுமமிர்த நாறு ...... கனிவாயா","புழுகொழுகு காழி கவுணியரில் ஞான","புநிதனென ஏடு ...... தமிழாலே","புனலிலெதி ரேற சமணர்கழு வேற","பொருதகவி வீர ...... குருநாதா","மழுவுழைக பால டமரகத்ரி சூல","மணிகரவி நோத ...... ரருள்பாலா","மலரயனை நீடு சிறைசெய்தவன் வேலை","வளமைபெற வேசெய் ...... முருகோனே","கழுகுதொழு வேத கிரிசிகரி வீறு","கதிருலவு வாசல் ...... நிறைவானோர்","கடலொலிய தான மறைதமிழ்க ளோது","கதலிவன மேவு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A song of praise only: Murugan is the one whose mouth is fragrant with the nectar that welled from the breast of the first Goddess, whom Vishnu, Brahma and Rudra call their Lady; the pure one of wisdom among the Kauniyas of Kazhi, the hero-poet and guru who made the palm leaf of Tamil swim against the stream and sent the Jains to the stake; the son of Siva who plays with axe, deer, skull, drum, trident and bell; the one who imprisoned Brahma; and the lord of the banana grove at Vedagiri, where eagles worship and the Vedas and Tamil are recited like the sea's roar.","n":"Every detail of the second quatrain is Sambandar's: the Goddess's milk, the Kauniya clan of Kazhi (Sirkazhi), the leaf floated upstream and the impaled Jains. Here they are Murugan's own deeds. கழுகுதொழு வேதகிரி names the eagles of Thirukkazhukundram, and மறைதமிழ்கள் ஓது sets the Vedas and Tamil side by side. One glyph is corrupt (முலையினு஡றி for முலையினூறி).","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["thamizh","guru","samayam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:48"},"p:543":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":543,"t":["ஓல மிட்ட சுரும்பு தனா தனாவென","வேசி ரத்தில் விழுங்கை பளீர் பளீரென","வோசை பெற்ற சிலம்பு கலீர் கலீரென ...... விரகலீலை","ஓர்மி டற்றி லெழும்புள் குகூ குகூவென","வேர்வை மெத்த வெழுந்து சலா சலாவென","ரோம குச்சு நிறைந்து சிலீர் சிலீரென ...... அமுதமாரன்","ஆல யத்து ளிருந்து குபீர் குபீரென","வேகு திக்க வுடம்பு வி஡ணர் வி஡ணரென","ஆர முத்த மணிந்து அளா வளாவென ...... மருவுமாதர்","ஆசை யிற்கை கலந்து சுமா சுமாபவ","சாக ரத்தி லழுந்தி எழா எழாதுளம்","ஆறெ ழுத்தை நினைந்து குகா குகாவென ...... வகைவராதோ","மாலை யிட்ட சிரங்கள் செவேல் செவேலென","மேலெ ழுச்சி தரும்பல் வெளேல் வெளேலென","வாகை பெற்ற புயங்கள் கறேல் கறேலென ...... எதிர்கொள்சூரன்","மார்பு மொக்க நெரிந்து காணல் காணலென","பேய்கு திக்க நிணங்கள்ழு ழுவென","வாய்பு தைத்து விழுந்து ஐயோ ஐயோவென ...... உதிரமாறாய்","வேலை வற்றி வறண்டு சுறீல் சுறீலென","மாலை வெற்பு மிடிந்து திடீல் திடீலென","மேன்மை பெற்ற ஜனங்கள் ஐயா ஐயாவென ...... விசைகள்கூற","வேலெ டுத்து நடந்த திவா கராசல","வேடு வப்பெண் மணந்த புயா சலாதமிழ்","வேத வெற்பி லமர்ந்த க்ருபா கராசிவ ...... குமரவேளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes lovemaking in a run of doubled sound-words, bees humming, hands clapping on the head, anklets tinkling, birds crying in the throat, sweat running, hair bristling, and asks whether, sunk in the sea of birth and unable to rise, his heart will ever come to think of the six syllables and cry 'Guha, Guha'. The praise, in the same doubled words, shows Sura's garlanded heads red, his teeth white and arms black, his chest crushed, ghouls leaping, the sea dried up and the people crying out, as Murugan took up the vel; it ends at the Tamil Veda hill, Vedagiri, with the son of Siva who married the hunter girl.","n":"The sound-doubling (சலா சலா, சிலீர் சிலீர்) runs through both halves, so that the cure, குகா குகா, comes in the same form as the passion. ஆறெழுத்து, the six syllables, is Murugan's mantra. பவசாகரம், the sea of birth, is where the scene ends. Two corrupt words (வி஡ணர் வி஡ணரென, probably விரீர் விரீரென) and a gap in நிணங்கள்ழு ழுவென leave the doubled words of two lines uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["punarchi","mandhiram","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:48"},"p:544":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":544,"t":["வேத வெற்பி லேபு னத்தில் மேவி நிற்கு ...... மபிராம","வேடு வச்சி பாத பத்ம மீது செச்சை ...... முடிதோய","ஆத ரித்து வேளை புக்க ஆறி ரட்டி ...... புயநேய","ஆத ரத்தோ டாத ரிக்க ஆன புத்தி ...... புகல்வாயே","காது முக்ர வீர பத்ர காளி வெட்க ...... மகுடாமா","காச முட்ட வீசி விட்ட காலர் பத்தி ...... யிமையோரை","ஓது வித்த நாதர் கற்க வோது வித்த ...... முநிநாண","ஓரெ ழுத்தி லாறெ ழுத்தை யோது வித்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A short song: the poet asks the twelve-shouldered god, who at the right moment bowed his vetchi-crowned head to the lotus feet of the lovely hunter girl in the millet field on Vedagiri, to tell him the wisdom by which he may love him with love. The praise names him as the one who, to the shame of the sage, taught the six syllables within the one syllable to the lord who had taught the gods, so that he too learned.","n":"ஓரெழுத்தில் ஆறெழுத்தை ஓதுவித்த, teaching the six syllables within the one, joins Om to Murugan's six-syllable mantra. The taught one (இமையோரை ஓதுவித்த நாதர்) is Siva, and the shamed sage (முநி நாண) is probably Brahma. The line காதுமுக்ர வீரபத்ர காளி வெட்க is unclear to me.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piranavam","guru","mandhiram","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:48"},"p:545":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":545,"t":["நீலமயில் சேரு மந்தி மாலை நிக ராகி யந்த","காரமிக வேநி றைந்த ...... குழலாலும்","நீடுமதி ரேக இன்ப மாகியச லாப சந்த்ர","னேர்தருமு கார விந்த ...... மதனாலும்","ஆலினிக ரான வுந்தி யாலுமட வார்கள் தங்கள்","ஆசைவலை வீசு கெண்டை ...... விழியாலும்","ஆடியக டாமி சைந்த வார்முலைக ளாலு மந்த","னாகிமயல் நானு ழன்று ...... திரிவேனோ","கோலவுரு வாயெ ழுந்து பாரதனை யேயி டந்து","கூவிடு முராரி விண்டு ...... திருமார்பன்","கூடமுறை நீடு செம்பொன் மாமதலை யூடெ ழுந்த","கோபவரி நார சிங்கன் ...... மருகோனே","பீலிமயில் மீது றைந்து சூரர்தமை யேசெ யங்கொள்","பேர்பெரிய வேல்கொள் செங்கை ...... முருகோனே","பேடைமட ஓதி மங்கள் கூடிவிளை யாடு கின்ற","பேறைநகர் வாழ வந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet lists women's hair dark as an evening sky, faces like the full moon, navels like the banyan leaf, carp eyes that cast the net of desire and swaying breasts, and asks whether he must wander, blind and infatuated, among them. The praise names Vishnu, who rose as the Boar and lifted the earth and who sprang in anger as the Man-Lion from the golden pillar, as Murugan's uncle, and Murugan as the one of the peacock and the great vel who won against the Suras, come to dwell in Peraiyur, where young swans play.","n":"The confession is a plain catalogue of the woman's body, part by part, each with its stock comparison, and the poet's state is அந்தனாகி மயல், blinded by infatuation. The two avatars are named by their details: the boar lifting the earth (பார்தனையே இடந்து) and the lion from the golden pillar (செம்பொன் மாமதலை ஊடெழுந்த கோபவரி நாரசிங்கன்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mayal","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:49"},"p:546":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":546,"t":["கொலைகொண்ட போர்விழி கோலோ வாளோ","விடமிஞ்சு பாதக வேலோ சேலோ","குழைகொண்டு லாவிய மீனோ மானோ ...... எனுமானார்","குயில்தங்கு மாமொழி யாலே நேரே","யிழைதங்கு நூலிடை யாலே மீதூர்","குளிர்கொங்கை மேருவி னாலே நானா ...... விதமாகி","உலைகொண்ட மாமெழு காயே மோகா","யலையம்பு ராசியி னு஡டே மூழ்கா","வுடல்பஞ்ச பாதக மாயா நோயா ...... லழிவேனோ","உறுதண்ட பாசமொ டாரா வாரா","எனையண்டி யேநம னார்தூ தானோர்","உயிர்கொண்டு போய்விடு நாள்நீ மீதா ...... ளருள்வாயே","அலைகொண்ட வாரிதி கோகோ கோகோ","எனநின்று வாய்விட வேநீள் மாசூ","ரணியஞ்ச ராசனம் வேறாய் நீறா ...... யிடவேதான்","அவிர்கின்ற சோதிய வாரார் நீள்சீ","ரனலங்கை வேல்விடும் வீரா தீரா","அருமந்த ரூபக ஏகா வேறோர் ...... வடிவாகி","மலைகொண்ட வேடுவர் கானு஡ டேபோய்","குறமங்கை யாளுட னேமா லாயே","மயல்கொண்டு லாயவள் தாள்மீ தேவீழ் ...... குமரேசா","மதிமிஞ்சு போதக வேலா ஆளா","மகிழ்சம்பு வேதொழு பாதா நாதா","மயிலந்தண் மாமலை வாழ்வே வானோர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether women's eyes are arrow or sword, poisoned spear or carp, fish or deer, and whether, overcome by their cuckoo speech, thread-thin waists and mountainous breasts, melting like wax in the forge and drowning in the sea of passion, he must perish by the disease of the body's five great sins; he asks that on the day Yama's messengers come shouting with club and noose to take his life, Murugan grant him his feet. The praise names the vel that dried the roaring sea and turned Sura's array to ash, the one who took another form, went into the hunters' forest, fell in love with the hunter girl and fell at her feet, the lord whose feet Sambhu worships, at the cool hill of Mayilam.","n":"The opening is a riddle of comparisons (கோலோ வாளோ ... மீனோ மானோ), the familiar question of what the woman's eye is. The same word, மயல், describes Murugan's love for Valli (மயல்கொண்டு உலாய் அவள் தாள்மீதே வீழ்): the god's infatuation is praised in the song that confesses the poet's. உறுதண்ட பாசமொடு ஆரா வாரா is Death's men coming with club and noose, shouting.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kaamam","kootru","mayal","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:50"},"p:547":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":547,"t":["அங்கை நீட்டிய ழைத்துப் பாரிய","கொங்கை காட்டிம றைத்துச் சீரிய","அன்பு போற்பொய்ந டித்துக் காசள ...... வுறவாடி","அம்பு தோற்றக ணிட்டுத் தோதக","இன்ப சாஸ்த்ரமு ரைத்துக் கோகிலம்","அன்றில் போற்குர லிட்டுக் கூரிய ...... நகரேகை","பங்க மாக்கிய லைத்துத் தாடனை","கொண்டு வேட்கையெ ழுப்பிக் காமுகர்","பண்பில் வாய்க்கம யக்கிக் கூடுத ...... லியல்பாகப்","பண்டி ராப்பகல் கற்றுச் சூளைகள்","தங்கள் மேற்ப்ரமை விட்டுப் பார்வதி","பங்கர் போற்றிய பத்மத் தாள்தொழ ...... அருள்வாயே","எங்கு மாய்க்குறை வற்றுச் சேதன","அங் மாய்ப்பரி சுத்தத் தோர்பெறும்","இன்ப மாய்ப்புகழ் முப்பத் தாறினின் ...... முடிவேறாய்","இந்த்ர கோட்டிம யக்கத் தான்மிக","மந்த்ர மூர்த்தமெ டுத்துத் தாமத","மின்றி வாழ்த்திய சொர்க்கக் காவல ...... வயலு஡ரா","செங்கை வேற்கொடு துட்டச் சூரனை","வென்று தோற்பறை கொட்டக் கூளிகள்","தின்று கூத்துந டிக்கத் தோகையில் ...... வரும்வீரா","செம்பொ னாற்றிகழ் சித்ரக் கோபுர","மஞ்சி ராப்பகல் மெத்தச் சூழ்தரு","தென்சி ராப்பள்ளி வெற்பிற் றேவர்கள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes courtesans who beckon with an outstretched hand, show and hide their breasts, act out a false love and deal with a man as far as his coins go, recite the deceitful science of pleasure, cry like cuckoo and anril, rake him with nails and strike him to rouse his lust, having studied day and night how to confuse lustful men; he asks to leave his delusion over these whores and worship the lotus feet praised by Siva. The praise names Murugan as the one present everywhere, lacking nothing, the conscious form, the bliss the pure attain, distinct from the end of the thirty-six tattvas, the guardian of heaven whom Indra praised, the hero of Vayalur whose vel beat Sura while ghouls ate and danced, the lord of the gods on the hill of south Sirappalli with its golden towers.","n":"காசளவு உறவாடி, 'relating to the measure of the coins', and சூளைகள, whores, make the trade explicit. The women's arts are a learned discipline (இன்ப சாஸ்த்ரம், பண்டு இராப்பகல் கற்று). The praise borrows Saiva Siddhanta: முப்பத்தாறினின் முடிவு வேறாய், beyond the end of the thirty-six tattvas. The line அங் மாய்ப்பரி சுத்தத் has lost a letter (probably அங்கமாய்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","thaththuvam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:51"},"p:548":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":548,"t":["அந்தோமன மேநம தாக்கையை","நம்பாதெயி தாகித சூத்திர","மம்போருக னாடிய பூட்டிது ...... இனிமேல்நாம்","அஞ்சாதமை யாகிரி யாக்கையை","பஞ்சாடிய வேலவ னார்க்கிய","லங்காகுவம் வாஇனி தாக்கையை ...... ஒழியாமல்","வந்தோமிது வேகதி யாட்சியு","மிந்தாமயில் வாகனர் சீட்டிது","வந்தாளுவம் நாமென வீக்கிய ...... சிவநீறும்","வந்தேவெகு வாநமை யாட்கொளு","வந்தார்மத மேதினி மேற்கொள","மைந்தாகும ராவெனு மார்ப்புய ...... மறவாதே","திந்தோதிமி தீதத மாத்துடி","தந்தாதன னாதன தாத்தன","செம்பூரிகை பேரிகை யார்த்தெழ ...... மறையோதச்","செங்காடென வேவரு மூர்க்கரை","சங்காரசி காமணி வேற்கொடு","செண்டாடிம காமயில் மேற்கொளு ...... முருகோனே","இந்தோடிதழ் நாகம காக்கடல்","கங்காளமி னார்சடை சூட்டிய","என்தாதைச தாசிவ கோத்திர ...... னருள்பாலா","எண்கூடரு ளால்நெளவி நோக்கியை","நன்பூமண மேவிசி ராப்பளி","யென்பார்மன மேதினி நோக்கிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks to his own mind: alas, do not trust this body, a contraption of strings, a lock Brahma made; from now on, without fear, let us belong to the vel-bearer who turned the mountain to cotton; this is our refuge, here is the peacock-rider's deed of possession, 'we come to rule you', and the holy ash he has bound on us; do not forget the cry 'Son, Kumara'. The praise names the drums and Vedas of the battle where Murugan played ball with the vel among the slain on his peacock, the child of grace of 'my father Sadasiva' who wears the moon, the serpent, the Ganga and the skeleton, and the lord who married the doe-eyed one and looks into the minds of those who say 'Sirappalli'.","n":"The song is a speech to the mind (அந்தோ மனமே), not to the god. மயில்வாகனர் சீட்டு இது, 'this is the peacock-rider's deed', with வந்தாளுவம் நாம், 'we come to rule you', reads like a deed of possession, the claim of a master on his servant; that it alludes to a particular story is my guess. என்தாதை சதாசிவ, 'my father Sadasiva', makes the poet Siva's son as well. Many splits in the middle lines are uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","manam","thondu","thiruneeru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:51"},"p:549":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":549,"t":["அரிவையர் நெஞ்சுரு காப்புணர்","தருவிர கங்களி னாற்பெரி","தவசம்வி ளைந்துவி டாய்த்தடர் ...... முலைமேல்வீழ்ந்","தகிலொடு சந்தன சேற்றினில்","முழுகியெ ழுந்தெதிர் கூப்புகை","யடியின கம்பிறை போற்பட ...... விளையாடிப்","பரிமளம் விஞ்சிய பூக்குழல்","சரியம ருங்குடை போய்ச்சில","பறவைக ளின்குர லாய்க்கயல் ...... விழிசோரப்","பனிமுக முங்குறு வேர்ப்பெழ","இதழமு துண்டிர வாய்ப்பகல்","பகடியி டும்படி தூர்த்தனை ...... விடலாமோ","சரியையு டன்க்ரியை போற்றிய","பரமப தம்பெறு வார்க்கருள்","தருகணன் ரங்கபு ரோச்சிதன் ...... மருகோனே","சயிலமெ றிந்தகை வேற்கொடு","மயிலினில் வந்தெனை யாட்கொளல்","சகமறி யும்படி காட்டிய ...... குருநாதா","திரிபுவ னந்தொழு பார்த்திபன்","மருவிய மண்டப கோட்டிகள்","தெருவில்வி ளங்குசி ராப்பளி ...... மலைமீதே","தெரியஇ ருந்தப ராக்ரம","உருவளர் குன்றுடை யார்க்கொரு","திலதமெ னும்படி தோற்றிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes lovemaking with women whose hearts melt, falling on their breasts, bathing in agil and sandal paste, nail marks like crescents, hair falling, bird cries, drooping carp eyes and sweat on the face, drinking their lips night and day, and asks whether Murugan should abandon him, a lecher, to be mocked. The praise names Vishnu of Srirangam, who grants grace to those who reach the supreme state through charya and kriya, as his uncle, and Murugan as the guru who came on the peacock with the vel that broke the mountain and took the poet as his own so that the world knew it, the one who appeared as a forehead mark to the Lord of the hill at Sirappalli.","n":"மயிலினில் வந்தெனை ஆட்கொளல் சகமறியும்படி காட்டிய குருநாதா, 'the guru who showed the world that he came on the peacock and took me', is the poet's own claim to a public grace. The praise of Vishnu uses the Saiva terms சரியை and க்ரியை for the way to his heaven (பரமபதம்). உருவளர் குன்றுடையார்க்கு ஒரு திலதம் makes Murugan the ornament of Siva of the rock.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","guru","sariyai","kiriyai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:51"},"p:550":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":550,"t":["அழுதழு தாசார நேசமு முடையவர் போலேபொய் சூழ்வுறும்","அசடிகள் மாலான காமுகர் ...... பொன்கொடாநாள்","அவருடன் வாய்பேசி டாமையு முனிதலு மாறாத தோஷிகள்","அறுதியில் காசாசை வேசைகள் ...... நஞசுதோயும்","விழிகளி னால்மாட வீதியில் முலைகளை யோராம லாரொடும்","விலையிடு மாமாய ரூபிகள் ...... பண்பிலாத","விரகிகள் வேதாள மோவென முறையிடு கோமாள மூளிகள்","வினைசெய லாலேயெ னாவியு ...... யங்கலாமோ","வழியினில் வாழ்ஞான போதக பரமசு வாமீவ ரோதய","வயலியில் வேலாயு தாவரை ...... யெங்குமானாய்","மதுரையின் மீதால வாயினில் எதிரம ணாரோ ரெணாயிரர்","மறிகழு மீதேற நீறுப ...... ரந்துலாவச்","செழியனு மாளாக வாதுசெய் கவிமத சீகாழி மாமுனி","சிவசிவ மாதேவ காவென ...... வந்துபாடும்","திருவுடை யாய்தீதி லாதவர் உமையொரு பாலான மேனியர்","சிரகிரி வாழ்வான தேவர்கள் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes whores who weep and weep as if loving, entrap fools, will not speak to the lustful on a day they give no gold, are always angry, whose greed for coin has no end, whose eyes are steeped in poison, who price their breasts to anyone in the mansion streets, and asks whether his life can be saved from their works. The praise names Murugan as the supreme guide to wisdom, the vel of Vayalur who is everywhere, and as the poet-sage of Sirkazhi who at Madurai made the eight thousand Jains mount the stakes, spread the ash, made the Pandiya his servant and sang 'Siva Siva Mahadeva', the lord of the gods at Sira hill.","n":"பொன்கொடா நாள் அவருடன் வாய்பேசிடாமை, not speaking on a day no gold is given, and அறுதியில் காசாசை, an endless greed for coin, put money at the centre. The Sambandar story is told in full detail (ஆலவாய், எண்ணாயிரர், கழு, செழியன், சீகாழி மாமுனி) and given to Murugan (வந்துபாடும் திருவுடையாய்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","samayam","thamizh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:51"},"p:551":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":551,"t":["இளையவர் நெஞ்சத் தளையமெ னுஞ்சிற்","றிடைகொடு வஞ்சிக் ...... கொடிபோல்வார்","இணையடி கும்பிட் டணியல்குல் பம்பித்","திதழமு துந்துய்த் ...... தணியாரக்","களபசு கந்தப் புளகித இன்பக்","கனதன கும்பத் ...... திநட்முழ்குங்","கலவியை நிந்தித் திலகிய நின்பொற்","கழல்தொழு மன்பைத் ...... தருவாயே","தளர்வறு மன்பர்க் குளமெனு மன்றிற்","சதுமறை சந்தத் ...... தொடுபாடத்","தரிகிட தந்தத் திரிகிட திந்தித்","தகுர்தியெ னுங்கொட் ...... டுடனாடித்","தெளிவுற வந்துற் றொளிர்சிவ னன்பிற்","சிறுவஅ லங்கற் ...... றிருமார்பா","செழுமறை யஞ்சொற் பரிபுர சண்டத்","திரிசிர குன்றப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of creeper-like women whose small waists are a trap for young men's hearts, and of bowing at their feet and drinking their lips, and asks for the love that scorns such union and worships Murugan's shining golden anklets. The praise names him as the son, in Siva's love, of the Lord who dances to the drum in the hall that is the heart of unfailing devotees while the four Vedas sing in rhythm, the garlanded one of the Trisira hill.","n":"கலவியை நிந்தித்து, 'scorning union', is unusually explicit: the plea is for a love that condemns the pleasure, not only escapes it. அன்பர்க்கு உளமெனும் மன்றில், 'in the hall that is the devotees' heart', moves Siva's dance from Chidambaram into the heart. The word திநட்முழ்குங் is corrupt.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","anbu","thirukkoothu","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:51"},"p:552":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":552,"t":["பகலவ னொக்குங் கனவிய ரத்னம்","பவளவெண் முத்தந் ...... திரமாகப்","பயிலமு லைக்குன் றுடையவர் சுற்றம்","பரிவென வைக்கும் ...... பணவாசை","அகமகிழ் துட்டன் பகிடிம ருட்கொண்","டழியும வத்தன் ...... குணவீனன்","அறிவிலி சற்றும் பொறையிலி பெற்றுண்","டலைதலொ ழித்தென் ...... றருள்வாயே","சகலரு மெச்சும் பரிமள பத்மந்","தருணப தத்திண் ...... சுரலோகத்","தலைவர்ம கட்குங் குறவர்ம கட்குந்","தழுவஅ ணைக்குந் ...... திருமார்பா","செகதல மெச்சும் புகழ்வய லிக்குந்","திகுதிகெ னெப்பொங் ...... கியவோசை","திமிலைத விற்றுந் துமிகள்மு ழக்குஞ்","சிரகிரி யிற்கும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of the greed for money that the kin of women with mountainous breasts present as affection, demanding gems bright as the sun, coral and pearls, and calls himself a rogue glad at heart, mocked and deluded, futile, base, witless and without patience, and asks when Murugan will end his wandering after getting and eating. The praise names the twelve-armed one whose chest is embraced by the daughter of the lord of heaven and the daughter of the hunters, at Vayalur and at Sira hill where drums sound.","n":"The demand comes from the woman's family (சுற்றம் பரிவென வைக்கும் பணவாசை): their greed wears the face of affection. The self-abuse is a list (துட்டன், அவத்தன், குணவீனன், அறிவிலி, பொறையிலி).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","kutram","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:51"},"p:553":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":553,"t":["ஒருவரொடு கண்கள் ஒருவரொடு கொங்கை","ஒருவரொடு செங்கை ...... யுறவாடி","ஒருவரொடு சிந்தை ஒருவரொடு நிந்தை","ஒருவரொடி ரண்டு ...... முரையாரை","மருவமிக அன்பு பெருகவுள தென்று","மனநினையு மிந்த ...... மருள்தீர","வனசமென வண்டு தனதனன வென்று","மருவுசர ணங்க ...... ளருளாயோ","அரவமெதிர் கண்டு நடுநடுந டுங்க","அடலிடுப்ர சண்ட ...... மயில்வீரா","அமரர்முத லன்பர் முநிவர்கள்வ ணங்கி","அடிதொழவி ளங்கு ...... வயலு஡ரா","திருவையொரு பங்கர் கமலமலர் வந்த","திசைமுகன்ம கிழ்ந்த ...... பெருமானார்","திகுதகுதி யென்று நடமிட முழங்கு","த்ரிசிரகிரி வந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women who play with one man with their eyes, another with their breasts and a third with their hands, give their thoughts to one, their scorn to another and both their words to a third, and asks Murugan to end his delusion that such women have great love for him and to grant the feet that bees circle as if they were lotuses. The praise names the peacock that makes the serpent tremble, the hero of Vayalur whom the gods, devotees and sages worship, and the lord of Trisira hill, where Siva with the Goddess at his side, whom Brahma delights in, dances.","n":"The six lines of ஒருவரொடு carry the charge: the deceit is a division of the body and mind among many men. The delusion confessed is the belief (மனநினையும் இந்த மருள்) that the love is real. வயலு஡ரா has the corrupt glyph ஡ for ஊ.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:51"},"p:554":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":554,"t":["குமுத வாய்க்கனி யமுத வாக்கினர்","கோலே வேலே சேலே போலே ...... அழகான","குழைகள் தாக்கிய விழிக ளாற்களி","கூரா வீறா தீரா மாலா ...... யவரோடே","உமது தோட்களி லெமது வேட்கையை","ஓ஡ணர் பா஡ணர் வா஡ணர் சோணர் ...... எனவேநின்","றுடைதொ டாப்பண மிடைபொ றாத்தன","மூடே வீழ்வே னீடே றாதே ...... யுழல்வேனோ","தமர வாக்கிய அமரர் வாழ்த்திய","தாதா வேமா ஞாதா வேதோ ...... கையிலேறீ","சயில நாட்டிறை வயலி நாட்டிறை","சாவா மூவா மேவா நீவா ...... இளையோனே","திமிர ராக்கதர் சமர வேற்கர","தீரா வீரா நேரா தோரா ...... உமைபாலா","திரிசி ராப்பளி மலையின் மேற்றிகழ்","தேவே கோவே வேளே வானோர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women with lily mouths and nectar speech whose earring-touching eyes are arrow, spear and carp, who stand saying 'learn, look, come, join our desire for your shoulders', and money before a man so much as touches their cloth, and asks whether he must keep falling on their breasts and wander without rising. The praise strings epithets: the giver and great knower praised by the gods, rider of the peacock, lord of Vayalur, the deathless, ageless young one, the hero whose vel fought the demons of darkness, Uma's son, the god and king of the gods on the Trisira hill.","n":"உடைதொடாப் பணம், money before the garment is touched, names the price. The women's invitation is corrupt in the source (ஓ஡ணர் பா஡ணர் வா஡ணர் சோணர்); with ஡ண read as ரீ it is ஓரீர் பாரீர் வாரீர், and சோணர் may stand for சேரீர். The praise is a chant of short epithets ending in ஏ (தேவே கோவே வேளே).","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["varaivinmagalir","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:51"},"p:555":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":555,"t":["குவளை பூசல்வி ளைத்திடு மங்கயல்","கடுவ தாமெனு மைக்கண் மடந்தையர்","குமுத வாயமு தத்தை நுகர்ந்திசை ...... பொருகாடை","குயில்பு றாமயில் குக்கில் சுரும்பினம்","வனப தாயுத மொக்கு மெனும்படி","குரல்வி டாஇரு பொற்குட மும்புள ...... கிதமாகப்","பவள ரேகைப டைத்தத ரங்குறி","யுறவி யாளப டத்தை யணைந்துகை","பரிச தாடன மெய்க்கர ணங்களின் ...... மதனு஡லின்","படியி லேசெய்து ருக்கிமு யங்கியெ","அவச மாய்வட பத்ர நெடுஞ்சுழி","படியு மோகச முத்ரம ழுந்துத ...... லொழிவேனோ","தவள ரூபச ரச்சுதி யிந்திரை","ரதிபு லோமசை க்ருத்திகை ரம்பையர்","சமுக சேவித துர்க்கை பயங்கரி ...... புவநேசை","சகல காரணி சத்தி பரம்பரி","யிமய பார்வதி ருத்ரி நிரஞ்சனி","சமய நாயகி நிஷ்களி குண்டலி ...... யெமதாயி","சிவைம நோமணி சிற்சுக சுந்தரி","கவுரி வேதவி தக்ஷணி யம்பிகை","த்ரிபுரை யாமளை யற்பொடு தந்தருள் ...... முருகோனே","சிகர கோபுர சித்திர மண்டப","மகர தோரண ரத்ந அலங்க்ருத","திரிசி ராமலை அப்பர் வணங்கிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes lovemaking with kohl-eyed women at length, drinking their lips, bird cries, the coral lips, embraces and blows done by the book of Kama, and asks whether he will stop sinking in the sea of passion. The praise is a litany of the Goddess, attended by Saraswati, Lakshmi, Rati, Sachi, the Krittikas and Rambha: Durga, the terrible one, Lady of the worlds, cause of all, Shakti, Parvati of the Himalaya, Rudri, the stainless, Lady of the religions, the partless, Kundali, Sivai, Gauri, Ambika, Tripura, Yamalai, who bore Murugan in love; and it ends at the Trisira hill of towered halls, where the Father of the hill worshipped him.","n":"மதனூலின் படியிலே செய்து, 'doing it by the book of Kama', makes the lovemaking a learned art, as in 547. The second half is nearly all the mother's names, Shakta and Saiva titles together (குண்டலி, சமய நாயகி, நிஷ்களி). Whether திரிசிராமலை அப்பர் is Siva of the hill or the saint Appar is not certain; I read the former. மதனு஡லின் has the corrupt glyph ஡ for ஊ.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["punarchi","saththi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:51"},"p:556":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":556,"t":["சத்தி பாணீ நமோநம முத்தி ஞானீ நமோநம","தத்வ வாதீ நமோநம ...... விந்துநாத","சத்து ரூபா நமோநம ரத்ந தீபா நமோநம","தற்ப்ர தாபா நமோநம ...... என்றுபாடும்","பத்தி பூணா மலேயுல கத்தின் மானார் சவாதகில்","பச்சை பாடீர பூஷித ...... கொங்கைமேல்வீழ்","பட்டி மாடான நானுனை விட்டிரா மேயு லோகித","பத்ம சீர்பாத நீயினி ...... வந்துதாராய்","அத்ர தேவா யுதாசுர ருக்ர சேனா பதீசுசி","யர்க்ய சோமாசி யாகுற ...... சம்ப்ரதாயா","அர்ச்ச னாவாக னாவய லிக்குள் வாழ்நாய காபுய","அக்ஷ மாலா தராகுற ...... மங்கைகோவே","சித்ர கோலா கலாவிர லஷ்மி சாதா ரதாபல","திக்கு பாலா சிவாகம ...... தந்தரபோதா","சிட்ட நாதா சிராமலை யப்பர் ஸ்வாமீ மகாவ்ருத","தெர்ப்பை யாசார வேதியர் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses that he never took up the devotion that sings 'Hail, hail' to Murugan as bearer of the spear, knower of release, teacher of the tattvas, the true form of bindu and nada, the jewelled lamp, but fell like a stray bull on breasts smeared with civet, agil and camphor; he asks Murugan to come now and give his red lotus feet so that he does not leave him. The praise, mostly in Sanskrit epithets, names the fierce commander of the gods, the one of the tradition of offerings and soma sacrifice, the lord of Vayalur who holds the rosary, the teacher of the Saiva Agamas and tantras, the swami of the Father of the Sira hill, the lord of Vedic brahmins who keep great vows with darbha grass.","n":"The opening namo-namas are quoted as the song the poet failed to sing (என்றுபாடும் பத்தி பூணாமலே), so the litany is both praise and confession. பட்டிமாடு, the stray bull that grazes anywhere, is his image for himself. The praise ties Murugan to Vedic and Agamic ritual (சோமாசி, சிவாகம தந்த்ர போதா, தெர்ப்பை).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paththi","kaamam","thiruvadi","aagamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:51"},"p:557":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":557,"t":["பகலிரவினிற் ...... றடுமாறா","பதிகுருவெனத் ...... தெளிபோத","ரகசியமுரைத் ...... தநுபூதி","ரதநிலைதனைத் ...... தருவாயே","இகபரமதற் ...... கிறையோனே","இயலிசையின்முத் ...... தமிழோனே","சகசிரகிப் ...... பதிவேளே","சரவணபவப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A short song: the poet asks Murugan to tell him the secret of the knowledge that makes clear that the Lord is the guru, so that he does not waver day and night, and to grant him the settled state of experience. The praise names him lord of this world and the next, of the three Tamils of verse and music, Saravanabhava, the god of the hill.","n":"பதி குருவெனத் தெளி போத ரகசியம், the secret by which one knows that the Lord is the guru, is the doctrine that God comes as the teacher; அநுபூதி is direct experience. The shrine word சகசிரகிப் has probably lost a syllable (சக சிரகிரிப் பதி?).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","pathi","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:51"},"p:558":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":558,"t":["புவனத் தொருபொற் றொடிசிற் றுதரக்","கருவிற் பவமுற் றுவிதிப் படியிற்","புணர்துக் கசுகப் பயில்வுற் றுமரித் ...... திடிலாவி","புரியட் டகமிட் டதுகட் டியிறுக்","கடிகுத் தெனஅச் சம்விளைத் தலறப்","புரள்வித் துவருத் திமணற் சொரிவித் ...... தனலு஡டே","தவனப் படவிட் டுயிர்செக் கிலரைத்","தணிபற் களுதிர்த் தெரிசெப் புருவைத்","தழுவப் பணிமுட் களில்கட் டியிசித் ...... திடவாய்கண்","சலனப் படஎற் றியிறைச் சியறுத்","தயில்வித் துமுரித் துநெரித் துளையத்","தளையிட் டுவருத் தும்யமப் ரகரத் ...... துயர்தீராய்","பவனத் தையொடுக் குமனக் கவலைப்","ப்ரமையற் றைவகைப் புலனிற் கடிதிற்","படரிச் சையொழித் ததவச் சரியைக் ...... க்ரியையோகர்","பரிபக் குவர்நிட் டைநிவிர்த் தியினிற்","பரிசுத் தர்விரத் தர்கருத் ததனிற்","பரவப் படுசெய்ப் பதியிற் பரமக் ...... குருநாதா","சிவனுத் தமனித் தவுருத் திரன்முக்","கணனக் கன்மழுக் கரனுக் ரரணத்","த்ரிபுரத் தையெரித் தருள்சிற் குணனிற் ...... குணணாதி","செகவித் தனிசப் பொருள்சிற் பரனற்","புதனொப் பிலியுற் பவபத் மதடத்","த்ரிசிரப் புரவெற் புறைசற் குமரப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet traces a life from the womb through birth, pleasure and pain to death, and then the torments of Yama's servants: they bind the subtle body tight, beat and terrify it, roll it, pour sand and drop it in fire, grind it in an oil press, knock out the teeth, make it embrace a red-hot copper figure, tie it on thorns, poke the eyes, cut and feed its flesh, break and crush it and bind it in chains; he asks Murugan to end the suffering of Yama's blows. The praise names him the supreme guru at Seyppathi, praised by those who still the breath, shed the mind's cares and the senses' desires and walk charya, kriya and yoga, and the son of Siva, Rudra of three eyes and the axe who burned the three cities, quality and beyond quality, at Trisira hill.","n":"The tortures follow the hell lists of Saiva and Puranic texts: செக்கிலரைத்து, ground in the press, எரிசெப்புருவைத் தழுவ, embracing the burning copper image. புரியட்டகம் is the eightfold subtle body that the messengers bind. Nothing is confessed here; the fear itself is the plea. அனலு஡டே has the corrupt glyph ஡ for ஊ.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["naragam","kootru","piravi","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:51"},"p:559":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":559,"t":["பொருளின் மேற்ப்ரிய காமா காரிகள்","பரிவு போற்புணர் க்஡ணடா பீடிகள்","புருஷர் கோட்டியில் நாணா மோடிகள் ...... கொங்கைமேலே","புடைவை போட்டிடு மாயா ரூபிகள்","மிடிய ராக்குபொ லர்மு தேவிகள்","புலையர் மாட்டும றாதே கூடிகள் ...... நெஞ்சமாயம்","கருதொ ணாப்பல கோடா கோடிகள்","விரகி னாற்பலர் மேல்வீழ் வீணிகள்","கலவி சாத்திர நூலே யோதிகள் ...... தங்களாசைக்","கவிகள் கூப்பிடு மோயா மாரிகள்","அவச மாக்கிடு பேய்நீ ரூணிகள்","கருணை நோக்கமி லாமா பாவிக ...... ளின்பமாமோ","குருக டாக்ஷக லாவே தாகம","பரம வாக்கிய ஞானா சாரிய","குறைவு தீர்த்தருள் ஸ்வாமி கார்முக ...... வன்பரான","கொடிய வேட்டுவர் கோகோ கோவென","மடிய நீட்டிய கூர்வே லாயுத","குருகி க்ஷேத்ரபு ரேசா வாசுகி ...... அஞ்சமாறும்","செருப ராக்ரம கேகே வாகன","சரவ ணோற்பவ மாலா லாளித","திரள்பு யாத்திரி யீரா றாகிய ...... கந்தவேளே","சிகர தீர்க்கம காசீ கோபுர","முகச டாக்கர சேணா டாக்ருத","திரிசி ராப்பளி வாழ்வே தேவர்கள் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet heaps names on courtesans: lustful for wealth, seats of play, shameless among men, women of illusion who throw their cloth over their breasts, goddesses of misfortune who make men poor, who couple even with outcastes, with crores of tricks, who fall on many, read only the books of union, who drink the demon's water, sinners without compassion, and asks whether pleasure with them is pleasure. The praise names Murugan as the teacher of the Vedas and Agamas by the guru's glance, the vel that killed the hunters crying 'ko ko', the lord of the peacock, born in the reed pond, with twelve shoulders, at the Trisira hill with its towers.","n":"பொருளின் மேல் ப்ரிய காமாகாரிகள், 'women whose desire is for wealth', and மிடியர் ஆக்கு மூதேவிகள், 'misfortune-goddesses who make men poor', name money. பேய்நீர் ஊணிகள் probably means they drink liquor. க்஡ணடா பீடிகள் is corrupt; with ஡ண read as ரீ it is க்ரீடா பீடிகள், seats of sport.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","kal"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:51"},"p:560":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":560,"t":["பொருள்கவர் சிந்தை அரிவையர் தங்கள்","புழுககில் சந்து ...... பனிநீர்தோய்","புளகித கொங்கை யிளகவ டங்கள்","புரளம ருங்கி ...... லுடைசோர","இருள்வளர் கொண்டை சரியஇ சைந்து","இணைதரு பங்க ...... அநுராகத்","திரிதலொ ழிந்து மனதுக சிந்து","னிணையடி யென்று ...... புகழ்வேனோ","மருள்கொடு சென்று பரிவுட னன்று","மலையில்வி ளைந்த ...... தினைகாவல்","மயிலை மணந்த அயிலவ எங்கள்","வயலியில் வந்த ...... முருகோனே","தெருளுறு மன்பர் பரவ விளங்கு","திரிசிர குன்றில் ...... முதனாளில்","தெரிய இருந்த பெரியவர் தந்த","சிறியவ அண்டர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of women whose minds are set on seizing wealth, their musk-scented breasts, slipping cloth and loosened hair, and asks whether he will stop wandering in passion and, with a melting heart, praise Murugan's two feet. The praise names the bearer of the vel who went lovingly to the hill and married the peacock-like girl who guarded the millet, who came to Vayalur, and the little one given by the great Lord who appeared on the first day at the Trisira hill.","n":"பொருள்கவர் சிந்தை அரிவையர், women with minds bent on seizing wealth, is the whole charge; the rest is the stock catalogue. முதனாளில் தெரிய இருந்த பெரியவர் may refer to a shrine legend of Siva's first appearance on the rock; I cannot identify it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","anbu","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:51"},"p:561":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":561,"t":["வாசித்துக் காணொ ணாதது பூசித்துக் கூடொ ணாதது","வாய்விட்டுப் போசொ ணாதது ...... நெஞ்சினாலே","மாசர்க்குத் தோணொ ணாதது நேசர்க்குப் பேரொ ணாதது","மாயைக்குச் சூழொ ணாதது ...... விந்துநாத","ஓசைக்குத் தூர மானது மாகத்துக் கீற தானது","லோகத்துக் காதி யானது ...... கண்டுநாயேன்","யோகத்தைச் சேரு மாறுமெய்ஞ் ஞானத்தைப் போதி யாயினி","யூனத்தைப் போடி டாதும ...... யங்கலாமோ","ஆசைப்பட் டேனல் காவல்செய் வேடிச்சிக் காக மாமய","லாகிப்பொற் பாத மேபணி ...... கந்தவேள","ஆலித்துச் சேல்கள் பாய்வய லு஡ரத்திற் காள மோடட","ராரத்தைப் பூண்ம யூரது ...... ரங்கவீரா","நாசிக்குட் ப்ராண வாயுவை ரேசித்தெட் டாத யோகிகள்","நாடிற்றுக் காணொ ணாதென ...... நின்றநாதா","நாகத்துச் சாகை போயுயர் மேகத்தைச் சேர்சி ராமலை","நாதர்க்குச் சாமி யேசுரர் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes the Reality that cannot be seen by reading, reached by worship or spoken aloud, that cannot appear to the impure or be named even by those who love it, that maya cannot surround, far beyond the sound of bindu and nada, beyond the sky, the origin of the world; having seen it, he asks Murugan to teach him the true knowledge by which he may join yoga, and asks whether he must stay bewildered without casting off the flesh. The praise names the Kanda who, longing for the girl who guarded the millet, worshipped her golden feet, the peacock hero of Vayalur wearing the serpent, the Lord whom yogis who expel the breath through the nose say cannot be found, the swami of the Lord of Sira hill.","n":"The first half is a negative description (வாசித்துக் காணொணாதது, பூசித்துக் கூடொணாதது, வாய்விட்டுப் பேசொணாதது) of the kind Saiva mystics use for the Lord. The tension within the song is worth noticing: the poet asks for yoga (யோகத்தைச் சேருமாறு) while the praise says breath-controlling yogis cannot find the Lord (நாசிக்குட் ப்ராண வாயுவை ரேசித்து ... காணொணாது); the second may mean only yoga without grace. வயலு஡ரத்தில் has the corrupt glyph ஡ for ஊ.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["gnaanam","yogam","mounam","maayai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:51"},"p:562":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":562,"t":["வெருட்டி யாட்கொளும் விடமிகள் புடைவையை","நெகிழ்த்த ணாப்பிகள் படிறிகள் சடுதியில்","விருப்ப மாக்கிகள் விரவிய திரவிய ...... மிலரானால்","வெறுத்து நோக்கிகள் கபடிகள் நடமிடு","பதத்தர் தூர்த்திகள் ம்ருகமத பரிமள","விசித்ர மேற்படு முலையினு நிலையினு ...... மெவரோடும்","மருட்டி வேட்கைசொல் மொழியினும் விழியினும்","அவிழ்த்த பூக்கமழ் குழலினு நிழலினு","மதிக்கொ ணாத்தள ரிடையினு நடையினு ...... மவமேயான்","மயக்க மாய்ப்பொருள் வரும்வகை க்ருஷிபணு","தடத்து மோக்ஷம தருளிய பலமலர்","மணத்த வார்க்கழல் கனவிலு நனவிலு ...... மறவேனே","இருட்டி லாச்சுர ருலகினி லிலகிய","சகஸ்ர நேத்திர முடையவன் மிடியற","இரக்ஷை வாய்த்தருள் முருகப னிருகர ...... குகவீரா","இலக்ஷு மிச்சுர பசுபதி குருபர","சமஸ்த ராச்சிய ந்ருபபுகழ் வயமியல்","இலக்க ரேய்ப்படை முகடெழு ககபதி ...... களிகூரத்","திருட்டு ராக்ஷதர் பொடிபட வெடிபட","எடுத்த வேற்கொடு கடுகிய முடுகிய","செருக்கு வேட்டுவர் திறையிட முறையிட ...... மயிலேறும்","செருப்ப ராக்ரம நிதிசர வணபவ","சிவத்த பாற்கர னிமகரன் வலம்வரு","திருச்சி ராப்பளி மலைமிசை நிலைபெறு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes poisonous women who frighten and ensnare, loosen their clothes, deceive, are eager in a moment and look with contempt on a man who has no money left, dancers and lechers with musky breasts who beguile everyone with words, eyes, hair, waist and gait; he says that, though he has been deluded in vain and toiled to make money, he will not forget in dream or waking the long-anklet feet fragrant with many flowers that gave release. The praise names the twelve-handed Guha who protected the thousand-eyed Indra and ended his poverty, the guru of Pasupati, whose vel powdered the thieving demons while the hunters paid tribute and pleaded, Saravanabhava at Thiruchirappalli hill, round which the sun and moon circle.","n":"விரவிய திரவியம் இலரானால் வெறுத்து நோக்கிகள், women who look with disgust once the money is gone, is the courtesan's mark. Unlike most songs, the turn is a vow rather than a plea: கனவிலும் நனவிலும் மறவேனே, 'I will not forget, in dream or waking'. மோக்ஷமது அருளிய ... கழல் makes the feet the giver of release. செருக்கு வேட்டுவர் திறையிட முறையிட, the proud hunters paying tribute and pleading, is from the Valli story. The words க்ருஷிபணு தடத்து are hard to split.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","thiruvadi","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:52"},"p:563":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":563,"t":["குடத்தைத் தகர்த்துக் களிற்றைத் துரத்திக்","குவட்டைச் செறுத்துக் ...... ககசாலக்","குலத்தைக் குமைத்துப் பகட்டிச் செருக்கிக்","குருத்தத் துவத்துத் ...... தவர்சோரப்","புடைத்துப் பணைத்துப் பெருக்கக் கதித்துப்","புறப்பட்ட கச்சுத் ...... தனமாதர்","புணர்ச்சிச் சமுத்ரத் திளைப்பற் றிருக்கப்","புரித்துப் பதத்தைத் ...... தருவாயே","கடத்துப் புனத்துக் குறத்திக்கு மெத்தக்","கருத்திச்சை யுற்றுப் ...... பரிவாகக்","கனக்கப்ரி யப்பட் டகப்பட்டு மைக்கட்","கடைப்பட்டு நிற்கைக் ...... குரியோனே","தடத்துற் பவித்துச் சுவர்க்கத் தலத்தைத்","தழைப்பித்த கொற்றத் ...... தனிவேலா","தமிழ்க்குக் கவிக்குப் புகழ்ச்செய்ப் பதிக்குத்","தருக்கற் குடிக்குப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women's breasts as conquerors that shatter the pot, chase off the elephant, defeat the mountain peak, crush the flocks of birds and swell so proudly that ascetics of the great principle faint, and asks for Murugan's feet so that he may be free of weariness in the sea of union. The praise names the god who, deeply desiring the hunter girl in the forest millet field, was caught at the corner of her kohl-dark eye, who was born in the pond and revived the heavenly world with his matchless vel, the lord of Thirukkarkudi, famed for Tamil and for poetry.","n":"The breasts are described with the verbs of a conqueror (தகர்த்து, துரத்தி, செறுத்து, குமைத்து), each beating one of the stock comparisons: pot, elephant's temple, hill, the chakravaka birds. குருத் தத்துவத்துத் தவர் சோர, even the ascetics fainting, is the measure of their power. In the praise Murugan is the one caught (மைக்கட் கடைப்பட்டு நிற்கைக்கு உரியோனே).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","kaamam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:53"},"p:564":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":564,"t":["நெறித்துப் பொருப்புக் கொத்த","முலைக்குத் தனத்தைக் கொட்டி","நிறைத்துச் சுகித்துச் சிக்கி ...... வெகுநாளாய்","நினைத்துக் கொடத்துக் கத்தை","யவத்தைக் கடுக்கைப் பெற்று","நிசத்திற் சுழுத்திப் பட்ட ...... அடியேனை","இறுக்கிப் பிடித்துக் கட்டி","யுகைத்துத் துடிக்கப் பற்றி","யிழுத்துத் துவைத்துச் சுற்றி ...... யமதூதர்","எனக்குக் கணக்குக் கட்டு","விரித்துத் தொகைக்குட் பட்ட","இலக்கப் படிக்குத் தக்க ...... படியேதான்","முறுக்கித் திருப்பிச் சுட்டு","மலத்திற் புகட்டித் திட்டி","முழுக்கக் கலக்கப் பட்டு ...... அலையாமல்","மொழிக்குத் தரத்துக் குற்ற","தமிழ்க்குச் சரித்துச் சித்தி","முகத்திற் களிப்புப் பெற்று ...... மயிலேறி","உறுக்கிச் சினத்துச் சத்தி","யயிற்குத் தரத்தைக் கைக்குள்","உதிக்கப் பணித்துப் பக்கல் ...... வருவாயே","உனைச்சொற் றுதிக்கத் தக்க","கருத்தைக் கொடுப்பைச் சித்தி","யுடைக்கற் குடிக்குட் பத்தர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses that he poured out wealth on mountainous breasts and was caught in their pleasure for many days, and fell into the stupor of deep sleep; he asks that when Yama's messengers seize, bind, kick, drag, trample, open his bundle of accounts and, according to the total, twist, turn, burn, push him into filth and abuse him, he not be left to wander so tormented, but that Murugan, pleased with his Tamil, come in anger on the peacock with the vel's command in hand and stand at his side. The praise asks Murugan to give him the thought fit to praise him in words, the lord of the devotees at Karkudi, the place of attainment.","n":"கணக்குக் கட்டு விரித்துத் தொகைக்குட் பட்ட இலக்கப்படிக்குத் தக்கபடியே, opening the bundle of accounts and punishing 'according to the figure in the total', makes Yama a clerk with a ledger; the torture is measured by the sum. சுழுத்தி is the state of deep sleep, here the soul's stupor. The poet's claim on Murugan is his Tamil (தமிழ்க்குச் சரித்து). Splits in the first lines (கொடத்துக் கத்தை யவத்தைக் கடுக்கை) are uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kootru","naragam","varaivinmagalir","thamizh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:53"},"p:565":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":565,"t":["கயலைச் சருவிப் பிணையொத் தலர்பொற்","கமலத் தியல்மைக் ...... கணினாலே","கடிமொய்ப் புயலைக் கருதிக் கறுவிக்","கதிர்விட் டெழுமைக் ...... குழலாலே","நயபொற் கலசத் தினைவெற் பினைமிக்","குளநற் பெருசெப் ...... பிணையாலே","நலமற் றறிவற் றுணர்வற் றனனற்","கதியெப் படிபெற் ...... றிடுவேனோ","புயலுற் றியல்மைக் கடலிற் புகுகொக்","கறமுற் சரமுய்த் ...... தமிழ்வோடும்","பொருதிட் டமரர்க் குறுதுக் கமும்விட்","டொழியப் புகழ்பெற் ...... றிடுவோனே","செயசித் திரமுத் தமிழுற் பவநற்","செபமுற் பொருளுற் ...... றருள்வாழ்வே","சிவதைப் பதிரத் தினவெற் பதனிற்","றிகழ்மெய்க் குமரப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says that through the kohl-dark eyes like carp that rival the doe, the dark hair like a fragrant swarming cloud and the breasts like golden pots, hills and caskets, he has lost goodness, knowledge and feeling, and asks how he will ever gain the good path. The praise names Murugan who struck down the mango-tree form in the dark sea and ended the gods' grief, the source of the three Tamils and of the meaning of japa, the true Kumara shining on Ratnagiri.","n":"நலமற்று அறிவற்று உணர்வற்றனன்: the confession is a triple loss, goodness, knowledge and feeling, in one breath. கடலில் புகு கொக்கு: கொக்கு is the mango tree, and the line points to Sura hiding in the sea in that form, though the splitting of the line is not certain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","mayal","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:54"},"p:566":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":566,"t":["சுற்றகப டோ டுபல சூதுவினை யானபல","கற்றகள வோடுபழி காரர் கொலை காரர்சலி","சுற்றவிழ லானபவி ஷோடுகடல் மூழ்கிவரு ...... துயர்மேவித்","துக்கசமு சாரவலை மீனதென கூழில்விழு","செத்தையென மூளுமொரு தீயில்மெழு கானவுடல்","சுத்தமறி யாதபறி காயமதில் மேவிவரு ...... பொறியாலே","சற்றுமதி யாதகலி காலன்வரு நேரமதில்","தத்துஅறி யாமலொடி யாடிவரு சூதரைவர்","சத்தபரி சானமண ரூபரச மானபொய்மை ...... விளையாடித்","தக்கமட வார்மனையை நாடியவ ரோடுபல","சித்துவிளை யாடுவினை சீசியிது நாறவுடல்","தத்திமுடி வாகிவிடு வேனொமுடி யாதபத ...... மருள்வாயே","தித்திமித தீதிமித தீதிமித தீமிதத","தத்ததன தானதன தானனன தானனன","திக்குடுடு டூடமட டாடமட டூடுடுடு ...... எனதாளம்","திக்குமுகி லாடஅரி யாடஅய னாடசிவ","னொத்துவிளை யாடபரை யாடவர ராடபல","திக்கசுரர் வாடசுரர் பாடமறை பாடஎதிர் ...... களமீதே","எத்திசையு நாடியம னார்நிணமொ டாடபெல","மிக்கநரி யாடகழு தாடகொடி யாடசமர்","எற்றிவரு பூதகண மாடவொளி யாடவிடு ...... வடிவேலா","எத்தியொரு மானைதினை காவல்வல பூவைதனை","சித்தமலை காமுககு காநமசி வாயனெடு","ரத்நகிரி வாழ்முருக னேயிளைய வாவமரர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes himself caught in a sorrowful household like a fish in a net, like straw fallen into gruel, his body like wax in a fire, a basket of impurity driven by the senses, while the five cheats of sound, touch, smell, form and taste play their falsehood as heedless Death draws near; he asks whether, seeking women's houses, he must stumble to his end in a body that stinks, and asks for the state that does not end. The praise is a battle dance in drum syllables in which Hari, Brahma, Siva and the goddess dance, Yama, foxes and ghouls feast as the vel is thrown, and names Murugan, lover of the doe-girl guarding the millet, at Ratnagiri.","n":"சூதர் ஐவர் சத்த பரிசான மண ரூப ரசம்: the five senses are named as five gamblers or cheats, the five sense-objects listed in order. The similes for the trapped self (மீன் in the net, செத்தை in the gruel, மெழுகு in the fire) are piled one on another. சீசி இது நாற is the poet's own disgust at his body.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","kootru","kaamam","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:54"},"p:567":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":567,"t":["பத்தியால் யானுனைப் ...... பலகாலும்","பற்றியே மாதிருப் ...... புகழ்பாடி","முத்தனா மாறெனைப் ...... பெருவாழ்வின்","முத்தியே சேர்வதற் ...... கருள்வாயே","உத்தமா தானசற் ...... குணர்நேயா","ஒப்பிலா மாமணிக் ...... கிரிவாசா","வித்தகா ஞானசத் ...... திநிபாதா","வெற்றிவே லாயுதப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A short song: the poet asks that, holding to Murugan with devotion and singing his great Thiruppugazh again and again, he become one freed and reach liberation. The praise names Murugan the best one, friend of the good who give, dweller on the peerless gem hill, the wise one who is the descent of the power of knowledge, the lord of the victorious vel.","n":"The song names the book's own title (மா திருப்புகழ் பாடி) and makes singing it the way to mukti. ஞானசத்தி நிபாதா: சத்திநிபாதம், the descent of grace on a ripe soul, is a term of Saiva Siddhanta, here made a name of Murugan.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paththi","pugazh","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:54"},"p:568":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":568,"t":["சீரான கோல கால நவமணி","மாலாபி ஷேக பார வெகுவித","தேவாதி தேவர் சேவை செயுமுக ...... மலராலும்","சீராடு வீர மாது மருவிய","ஈராறு தோளு நீளும் வரியளி","சீராக மோது நீப பரிமள ...... இருதாளும்","ஆராத காதல் வேடர் மடமகள்","ஜநமுத மூர்வ லாரி மடமகள்","ஆதார பூத மாக வலமிட ...... முறைவாழ்வும்","ஆராயு நீதி வேலு மயிலுமெய்ஞ்","ஞானாபி ராம தாப வடிவமும்","ஆபாத னேனு நாளு நினைவது ...... பெறவேணும்","ஏராரு மாட கூட மதுரையில்","மீதேறி மாறி யாடு மிறையவர்","ஏழேழு பேர்கள் கூற வருபொரு ...... ளதிகாரம்","ஈடாய வூமர் போல வணிகரி","லு஡டாடி யால வாயில் விதிசெய்த","லீலாவி சார தீர வரதர ...... குருநாதா","கூராழி யால்முன் வீய நினைபவ","னீடேறு மாறு பாநு மறைவுசெய்","கோபால ராய னேய முளதிரு ...... மருகோனே","கோடாம லார வார அலையெறி","காவேரி யாறு பாயும் வயலியில்","கோனாடு சூழ்வி ராலி மலையுறை ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks that, lowly as he is, he may every day hold in thought Murugan's flower faces crowned with gem garlands, his twelve arms, his two fragrant feet, Valli and Indra's daughter on either side, the vel, the peacock and his beautiful form of true knowledge. The praise tells how at Madurai, when the forty-nine poets disputed the meaning of a work, Murugan came as a mute merchant's child at Alavai and settled it, and names him nephew of Krishna who hid the sun with his discus, at Viralimalai by the Kaveri.","n":"The plea is a meditation list (தியானம்), the god's form part by part, rather than a confession. ஏழேழு பேர்கள் கூற வரு பொருள் அதிகாரம் ... ஊமர் போல வணிகரில் ஊடாடி: the Madurai story of the mute child who judged the commentaries on Iraiyanar's Akapporul, here told of Murugan, so he is identified with a figure of the Tamil Sangam. பாநு மறைவுசெய் is Krishna covering the sun in the Bharata war.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["thiyaanam","thamizh","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:55"},"p:569":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":569,"t":["பாதாள மாதி லோக நிகிலமு","மாதார மான மேரு வெனவளர்","பாடீர பார மான முலையினை ...... விலைகூறிப்","பாலோடு பாகு தேனெ னினியசொ","லாலேய நேக மோக மிடுபவர்","பாதாதி கேச மாக வகைவகை ...... கவிபாடும்","வேதாள ஞான கீனன் விதரண","நாதானி லாத பாவி யநிஜவன்","வீணாள்ப டாத போத தவமிலி ...... பசுபாச","வ்யாபார மூடன் யானு முனதிரு","சீர்பாத தூளி யாகி நரகிடை","வீழாம லேசு வாமி திருவருள் ...... புரிவாயே","தூதாள ரோடு காலன் வெருவிட","வேதாமு ராரி யோட அடுபடை","சோராவ லாரி சேனை பொடிபட ...... மறைவேள்விச்","சோமாசி மார்சி வாய நமவென","மாமாய வீர கோர முடனிகல்","சூர்மாள வேலை யேவும் வயலியி ...... லிளையோனே","கூதாள நீப நாக மலர்மிசை","சாதாரி தேசி நாம க்ரியைமுதல்","கோலால நாத கீத மதுகர ...... மடர்சோலை","கூராரல் தேரு நாரை மருவிய","கானாறு பாயு மேரி வயல்பயில்","கோனாடு சூழ்வி ராலி மலையுறை ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet calls himself the one who sang poems of every kind, from feet to hair, on women who put a price on breasts like Meru and stir desire with words of milk, syrup and honey; a goblin-witted, ungenerous, untrue sinner without penance, a fool busy with bondage, he asks to become the dust of Murugan's feet and not fall into hell. The praise shows Death and his messengers afraid, Brahma and Vishnu fleeing, Vedic priests chanting Sivaya nama as Sura dies, and the groves of Viralimalai where bees hum ragas.","n":"பாதாதி கேசமாக வகைவகை கவிபாடும்: the poet confesses not only desire but misuse of his art, poems describing women from foot to head, a recognised genre. பசுபாச வ்யாபார மூடன் uses the Siddhanta terms for the bound soul and its bond. முலையினை விலைகூறி names the sale.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","kutram","naragam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:55"},"p:570":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":570,"t":["இலாபமில் பொலாவுரை சொலாமன தபோதன","ரியாவரு மிராவுபக ...... லடியேனை","இராகமும் விநோதமு முலொபமு டன்மோகமு","மிலானிவ னுமாபுருஷ ...... னெனஏய","சலாபவ மலாகர சசீதர விதாரண","சதாசிவ மயேசுரச ...... கலலோக","சராசர வியாபக பராபர மநோலய","சமாதிய நுபூதிபெற ...... நினைவாயே","நிலாவிரி நிலாமதி நிலாதவ நிலாசன","நியாயப ரிபாலஅர ...... நதிசூடி","நிசார குலாதிப திராவண புயாரிட","நிராமய சரோருகர ...... னருள்பாலா","விலாசுகம் வலாரெனு முலாசவி தவாகவ","வியாதர்கள் விநோதமகள் ...... மணவாளா","விராவுவ யலார்புரி சிராமலை பிரான்மலை","விராலிம லைமீதிலுறை ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks Murugan to think of him so that ascetics who speak no idle word may say of him day and night that he has no passion, no diversion, no greed and no infatuation, and so that he gains the experience of samadhi in which the mind dissolves into the transcendent that pervades all moving and unmoving worlds. The praise names Siva who wears the moon and the river and crushed Ravana's arms, and Murugan the bridegroom of the hunters' daughter, at Siramalai, Piranmalai and Viralimalai.","n":"மநோலய சமாதி அநுபூதி: samadhi as the dissolution of mind, a yogic term. The long run of Sanskrit epithets (சதாசிவ மயேசுர சகலலோக சராசர வியாபக பராபர) may describe the state sought or the god addressed, and உமாபுருஷன் என may compare the poet to Siva or be divided otherwise.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["samaadhi","avaa","viyaabagam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:55"},"p:571":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":571,"t":["நிராமய புராதன பராபர வராம்ருத","நிராகுல சிராதிகப் ...... ப்ரபையாகி","நிராசசி வராஜத வராஜர்கள் பராவிய","நிராயுத புராரியச் ...... சுதன்வேதா","சுராலய தராதல சராசர பராணிகள்","செர்ருபமி வராதியைக் ...... குறியாமே","துரால்புகழ் பராதின கராவுள பராமுக","துரோகரை தராசையுற் ...... றடைவேனோ","இராகவ இராமன்முன் இராவண இராவண","இராவண இராஜனுட் ...... குடன்மாய்வென்","றிராகன்ம லராணிஜ புராணர்கு மராகலை","யிராஜசொ லவாரணர்க் ...... கிளையோனே","விராகவ சுராதிப பொராதுத விராதடு","விராயண பராயணச் ...... செருவூரா","விராவிய குராவகில் பராரைமு திராவளர்","விராலிம லைராஜதப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether, not fixing his mind on the stainless, ancient, transcendent one whom Siva, Vishnu and Brahma praise and who is the form of heaven, earth and all beings, he will go with longing for gifts to faithless, indifferent men of empty fame. The praise plays on Ravana's name in telling of Rama, names Murugan the younger brother of the elephant-faced lord of arts, and ends at Viralimalai.","n":"The second line of the plea (துரால்புகழ் பராதின கராவுள பராமுக துரோகரை தராசையுற்று அடைவேனோ) seems to name patrons, men of chaff-like fame who turn their faces away, as in 579 and 589. The song is a dense chain of Sanskrit compounds and its division is doubtful throughout.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iravu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:55"},"p:572":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":572,"t":["இதமுறு விரைபுனல் முழுகி யகில்மண","முதவிய புகையினி லளவி வகைவகை","கொத்தலர்க ளின்தொடையல் வைத்துவளர் கொண்டலென","அறலென இசையளி யெனந ளிருளென","நிறமது கருகிநெ டுகிநெ றிவுபட","நெய்த்துமுசு வின்திரிகை யொத்தசுருள் குந்தளமும்","இலகிய பிறையென எயினர் சிலையென","விலகிய திலதநு தலும திமுகமும்","உற்பலமும் வண்டுவடு விற்கணைய மன்படரு ...... முனைவாளும்","இடர்படு கவுநடு வனும்வ லடல்பொரு","கடுவது மெனநெடி தடுவ கொடியன","இக்குசிலை கொண்டமதன் மெய்த்தவநி றைந்தவிழி","தளவன முறுவலு மமுத குமுதமும்","விளைநற வினியமொ ழியுமி னையதென","ஒப்பறுந கங்கள்விரல் துப்பெனவு றைந்துகமு","கிடியொடி படவினை செயும்வின் மதகலை","நெடியக வுடியிசை முரலு சுரிமுக","நத்தனைய கண்டமும்வெண் முத்துவிளை விண்டனைய ...... எழில்தோளும்","விதரண மனவித னமதை யருள்வன","சததள மறைமுகி ழதனை நிகர்வன","புத்தமிர்து கந்தகுடம் வெற்பெனநி ரம்புவன","இமசல ம்ருகமத களப பரிமள","தமனிய ப்ரபைமிகு தருண புளகித","சித்ரவர மங்கலவி சித்ரவிரு துங்ககன","விகலித மிருதுள ம்ருதுள நவமணி","முகபட விகடின தனமு முயர்வட","பத்திரமி ருந்தகடி லொத்தசுழி யுந்தியுள ...... மதியாத","விபரித முடையிடை யிளைஞர் களைபட","அபகட மதுபுரி யரவ சுடிகைய","ரத்நபண மென்பவழ குற்றவரை யும்புதிய","நுணியத ளிரெனவு லவிய பரிபுர","அணிநட னபதமு முடைய வடிவினர்","பொற்கலவி யின்பமதி துக்கமென லன்றியவர்","விரகினி லெனதுறு மனம துருகிய","பிரமையு மறவுன தருள்கை வரவுயர்","பத்திவழி யும்பரம முத்திநெறி யுந்தெரிவ ...... தொருநாளே","தததத தததத ததத தததத","திதிதிதி திதிதிதி திதிதி திதிதிதி","தத்ததத தந்ததத தித்திதிதி திந்திதிதி","டகுடகு டிகுடிகு டகுகு டிகுடிகு","டிகுடிகு டகுடகு டிகுகு டகுடகு","தத்ததிமி டங்குகுகு தித்திதிமி டிங்குகுகு","தமிதமி தமிதக தமித திமிதக","திமிதிமி செககண திமித திகதிக","தத்திமித தந்திமித தித்திமிதி திந்திமிதி ...... யெனவேதான்","தபலை டமுழவு திமிலை படகம","தபுதச லிகைதவில் முரசு கரடிகை","மத்தளித வண்டையற வைத்தகுணி துந்துமிகள்","மொகுமொகு மொகுவென அலற விருதுகள்","திகுதிகு திகுவென அலகை குறளிகள்","விக்கிடநி ணம்பருக பக்கியுவ ணங்கழுகு","சதிர்பெற அதிர்தர உததி சுவறிட","எதிர்பொரு நிருதர்கள் குருதி பெருகிட","வப்புவின்மி தந்தெழுப தற்புதக வந்தமெழ ...... வெகுகோடி","மதகஜ துரகர தமுமு டையபுவி","யதலமு தல்முடிய இடிய நெடியதொர்","மிக்கொலிமு ழங்கஇரு ளக்கணம்வி டிந்துவிட","இரவியு மதியமு நிலைமை பெறஅடி","பரவிய அமரர்கள் தலைமை பெறஇயல்","அத்திறல ணங்குசெய சத்திவிடு கந்ததிரு","வயலியி லடிமைய குடிமை யினலற","மயலொடு மலமற அரிய பெரியதி","ருப்புகழ்வி ளம்புவென்மு னற்புதமெ ழுந்தருள்கு ...... கவிராலி","மலையுறை குரவந லிறைவ வருகலை","பலதெரி விதரண முருக சரவண","உற்பவக்ர வுஞ்சகிரி நிக்ரகஅ கண்டமய","நிருபவி மலசுக சொருப பரசிவ","குருபர வெளிமுக டுருவ வுயர்தரு","சக்ரகிரி யுங்குலைய விக்ரமந டம்புரியு","மரகத கலபமெ ரிவிடு மயில்மிசை","மருவியெ யருமைய இளமை யுருவொடு","சொர்க்கதல மும்புலவர் வர்க்கமும்வி ளங்கவரு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"In a long rhythm the poet describes a woman from her bathed and scented hair to her anklets, and asks whether a day will come when, no longer melting in such women's wiles, the delusion ends, grace comes into his hands, and he sees the high way of devotion and the path to supreme liberation. The praise is a long battle in drum syllables, with sun and moon restored and the gods regaining their rule, and recalls that at Vayalur Murugan appeared before the poet and told him to sing the rare great Thiruppugazh so that his delusion and impurity might end; it ends at Viralimalai with Murugan on the peacock.","n":"வயலியில் ... மயலொடு மலமற அரிய பெரிய திருப்புகழ் விளம்பு என் முன் அற்புதம் எழுந்தருள் குக: an autobiographical line, the poet's own call at Vayalur to sing the book, set inside the praise. The woman's description is a full head-to-foot catalogue of the kind 569 confesses to having composed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mayal","paththi","veedu","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:55"},"p:573":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":573,"t":["உருவேற வேஜெ பித்து வொருகோடி யோம சித்தி","யுடனாக ஆக மத்து ...... கந்துபேணி","உணர்வாசை யாரி டத்து மருவாது வோரெ ழுத்தை","யொழியாது வூதை விட்டி ...... ருந்துநாளும்","தரியாத போத கத்தர் குருவாவ ரோரொ ருத்தர்","தருவார்கள் ஞான வித்தை ...... தஞ்சமாமோ","தழலாடி வீதி வட்ட மொளிபோத ஞான சித்தி","தருமாகி லாகு மத்தை ...... கண்டிலேனே","குருநாடி ராச ரிக்கர் துரியோத னாதி வர்க்க","குடிமாள மாய விட்டு ...... குந்திபாலர்","குலையாமல் நீதி கட்டி யெழுபாரை யாள விட்ட","குறளாக னு஡றில் நெட்டை ...... கொண்டஆதி","மருகா புராரி சித்தன் மகனே விராலி சித்ர","மலைமே லுலாவு சித்த ...... அங்கைவேலா","மதுரா புரேசர் மெய்க்க அரசாளு மாறன் வெப்பு","வளைகூனை யேநி மிர்த்த ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether the knowledge handed on by gurus, one after another, who teach japa until the mantra takes form, a crore of fire-offerings, devotion to the Agamas, holding the one syllable and restraining the breath, can be a refuge; if Siva the fire-dancer himself gave the light and the attainment of knowledge, that would be it, and he has not found it. The praise tells of the Kauravas destroyed and Kunti's sons ruling by justice, Vishnu the dwarf who grew tall, and names Murugan Siva's son with the vel at Viralimalai, the master who cured the Pandya king's fever and straightened his hump at Madurai.","n":"The plea weighs ritual and yogic discipline taught by human gurus (ஜெபித்து, ஓம சித்தி, ஆகமத்து, ஊதை) against knowledge given by Siva and asks whether the first is a refuge (தஞ்சமாமோ). மாறன் வெப்பு வளைகூனையே நிமிர்த்த: Sambandar's cure of the Pandya, so Murugan is again Sambandar. The split of தரியாத போதகத்தர் is uncertain, and so whether these gurus are praised or doubted.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["guru","gnaanam","mandhiram","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:55"},"p:574":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":574,"t":["எதிரெதிர்கண் டோ டி யாட்கள் களவதறிந் தாசை பூட்டி","இடறிவிழும் பாழி காட்டு ...... மடமாதர்","இறைவைகொளுங் கூவல் மூத்த கறையொழுகுந் தாரை பார்க்கி","லிளமைகொடுங் காத லாற்றில் ...... நிலையாத","அதிவிகடம் பீழ லாற்ற அழுகிவிழும் பீற லு஡த்தை","அடையுமிடஞ் சீலை தீற்று ...... கருவாயில்","அருவிசலம் பாயு மோட்டை அடைவுகெடுந் தூரை பாழ்த்த","அளறிலழுந் தாம லாட்கொ ...... டருள்வாயே","விதுரனெடுந் த்ரோண மேற்று எதிர்பொருமம் பாதி யேற்றி","விரகினெழுந் தோய நூற்று ...... வருமாள","விரவுஜெயன் காளி காட்டில் வருதருமன் தூத னீற்ற","விஜயனெடும் பாக தீர்த்தன் ...... மருகோனே","மதியணையுஞ் சோலை யார்த்து மதிவளசந் தான கோட்டின்","வழியருளின் பேறு காட்டி ...... யவிராலி","மலைமருவும் பாதி யேற்றி கடிகமழ்சந் தான கோட்டில்","வழியருளின் பேறு காட்டு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women who bind men with desire and show them a pit to stumble into, and in deliberately foul images, a well drawn by bucket, a channel of old stains, a rotting hole stopped with cloth where streams flow, calls their sex a ruined mire, and asks Murugan to take him as his own so that he does not sink in it. The praise tells of the Bharata war, Vidura, Drona, Jayadratha and Arjuna, Krishna as Dharma's envoy and Arjuna's charioteer as Murugan's uncle, at Viralimalai with Uma at Siva's half.","n":"அளறில் அழுந்தாமல்: அளறு is mire and also hell, the word the Kural uses for the courtesan. The method is that of the Naladiyar's body chapters, desire countered by naming the body's filth. The images are crude and partly obscure (இறைவைகொளுங் கூவல், சீலை தீற்று), and the last two lines of the praise nearly repeat each other.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["kaamam","yaakkai","naragam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:55"},"p:575":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":575,"t":["ஐந்து பூதமு மாறு சமயமு","மந்த்ர வேதபு ராண கலைகளும்","ஐம்ப தோர்வித மான லிபிகளும் ...... வெருப","அண்ட ராதிச ராச ரமுமுயர்","புண்ட ஡ணகனு மேக நிறவனும்","அந்தி போலுரு வானு நிலவொடு ...... வெயில்காலும்","சந்த்ர சூரியர் தாமு மசபையும்","விந்து நாதமு மேக வடிவம","தன்சொ ரூபம தாக வுறைவது ...... சிவயோகம்","தங்க ளாணவ மாயை கருமம","லங்கள் போயுப தேச குருபர","சம்ப்ர தாயமொ டேயு நெறியது ...... பெறுவேனோ","வந்த தானவர் சேனை கெடிபுக","இந்த்ர லோகம்வி பூதர் குடிபுக","மண்டு பூதப சாசு பசிகெட ...... மயிடாரி","வன்கண் வீரிபி டாரி ஹரஹர","சங்க ராஎன மேரு கிரிதலை","மண்டு தூளழ வேலை யுருவிய ...... வயலு஡ரா","வெந்த நீறணி வேணி யிருடிகள்","பந்த பாசவி கார பரவச","வென்றி யானச மாதி முறுகுகல் ...... முழைகூடும்","விண்டு மேல்மயி லாட இனியக","ளுண்டு காரளி பாட இதழிபொன்","விஞ்ச வீசுவி ராலி மலையுறை ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet defines Sivayoga as the abiding of everything, the five elements, six religions, mantras, Vedas, Puranas and arts, the fifty-one letters, all beings, Brahma, Vishnu, Rudra, sun and moon, the ajapa, bindu and nada, as one form, its own nature, and asks whether, with the malas of anava, maya and karma gone, he will gain that path in the line of the teaching guru. The praise shows the demons routed, the gods back in Indra's world, the goddess crying 'Hara Hara Sankara' as the vel pierces Meru, and ash-smeared sages in samadhi in the caves of Viralimalai where peacocks dance.","n":"ஏக வடிவம் அதன் சொரூபமதாக உறைவது சிவயோகம்: Sivayoga as seeing all things abiding as one form, which is pervasion turned into a practice. The three malas are named exactly (ஆணவ மாயை கருமம்), and the path is to be had in a guru's tradition (உபதேச குருபர சம்ப்ரதாயமொடு).","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["yogam","viyaabagam","malam","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:55"},"p:576":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":576,"t":["கரதல முங்குறி கொண்ட கண்டமும்","விரவியெ ழுந்துசு ருண்டு வண்டடர்","கனவிய கொண்டைகு லைந்த லைந்திட ...... அதிபாரக்","களபசு கந்தமி குந்த கொங்கைக","ளிளகமு யங்கிம யங்கி யன்புசெய்","கனியித ழுண்டுது வண்டு பஞ்சணை ...... மிசைவீழா","இரதம ருந்தியு றுங்க ருங்கயல்","பொருதுசி வந்துகு விந்தி டும்படி","யிதவிய வுந்தியெ னுந்த டந்தனி ...... லுற்முழ்கி","இனியதொ ரின்பம்வி ளைந்த ளைந்துபொய்","வனிதையர் தங்கள்ம ருங்கி ணங்கிய","இளமை கிழம்படு முன்ப தம்பெற ...... வுணர்வேனோ","பரத சிலம்புபு லம்பு மம்பத","வரிமுக எண்கினு டன்கு ரங்கணி","பணிவிடை சென்றுமு யன்ற குன்றணி ...... யிடையேபோய்ப்","பகடியி லங்கை கலங்க அம்பொனின்","மகுடசி ரந்தச முந்து ணிந்தெழு","படியுந டுங்கவி ழும்ப னம்பழ ...... மெனவாகும்","மருதமு தைந்தமு குந்த னன்புறு","மருககு விந்தும லர்ந்த பங்கய","வயலியில் வம்பவிழ் சண்ப கம்பெரி ...... யவிராலி","மலையில் விளங்கிய கந்த என்றுனை","மகிழ்வொடு வந்திசெய் மைந்த னென்றனை","வழிவழி யன்புசெய் தொண்டு கொண்டருள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes lovemaking in detail and asks whether, before his youth turns to age, he will come to know and gain Murugan's feet. The praise tells of Rama's army of bears and monkeys, Lanka shaken and Ravana's ten heads falling like palm fruit, Krishna who kicked down the marudu trees, and asks Murugan at Viralimalai to take the poet, the son who worships him gladly as Kanda, into hereditary loving service.","n":"இளமை கிழம்படு முன்: the urgency is time, to reach the feet before youth becomes age. வழிவழி அன்புசெய் தொண்டு: servitude by succession, the வழியடிமை of other songs.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","ilamai","thondu","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:55"},"p:577":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":577,"t":["கரிபு ராரி காமாரி திரிபு ராரி தீயாடி","கயிலை யாளி காபாலி ...... கழையோனி","கரவு தாச னாசாரி பரசு பாணி பானாளி","கணமொ டாடி காயோகி ...... சிவயோகி","பரம யோகி மாயோகி பரிய ராஜ டாசூடி","பகரொ ணாத மாஞானி ...... பசுவேறி","பரத மாடி கானாடி பரவ யோதி காதீத","பரம ஞான வூர்பூத ...... அருளாயோ","சுருதி யாடி தாதாவி வெருவி யோட மூதேவி","துரக கோப மீதோடி ...... வடமேரு","சுழல வேலை தநமுள அழுத ளாவி வாய்பாறி","சுரதி னோடு சூர்மாள ...... வுலகேழும்","திகிரி மாதி ராவார திகிரி சாய வேதாள","திரளி னோடு பாறோடு ...... கழுகாடச்","செருவி னாடு வானீப கருணை மேரு வேபார","திருவி ராலி யூர்மேவு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The first half is a chain of Siva's names, the elephant's foe, Tripura's foe, Kama's foe, the dancer in fire, lord of Kailasa, skull-bearer, great yogi, wearer of matted hair, the knower beyond words, rider of the bull, dancer in the forest, ending with a plea for grace and the city of supreme knowledge. The praise shows Brahma fleeing, Meru whirling, Sura dying, the world-rim hill tilting and vultures and ghouls dancing on the battlefield, and names Murugan the Meru of compassion at Viraliyur.","n":"The plea is almost wholly epithets of Siva (கரிபுராரி காமாரி திரிபுராரி தீயாடி ... பசுவேறி), as if Murugan were addressed as Siva himself, or Siva first praised and then asked. Which is meant, and what பரம ஞான வூர்பூத asks for, is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:55"},"p:578":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":578,"t":["காமாத்திர மாகி யிளைஞர்கள்","வாழ்நாட்கொடு போகி யழகிய","காதாட்டிய பார இருகுழை ...... யளவோடிக்","கார்போற்றவ ழோதி நிழல்தனி","லார்வாட்கடை யீடு கனகொடு","காலேற்றுவை வேலின் முனைகடை ...... யமதூதர்","ஏமாப்பற மோக வியல்செய்து","நீலோற்பல ஆசில் மலருட","னேராட்டவி நோத மிடும்விழி ...... மடவார்பால்","ஏகாப்பழி பூணு மருளற","நீதோற்றிமு னாளு மடிமையை","யீடேற்றுத லாலுன் வலிமையை ...... மறவேனே","சீமாட்டியு மாய திரிபுரை","காலாக்கினி கோப பயிரவி","சீலோத்தமி நீலி சுரதிரி ...... புவநேசை","சீகார்த்திகை யாய அறுவகை","மாதாக்கள்கு மார னெனவெகு","சீராட்டொடு பேண வடதிசை ...... கயிலாசக்","கோமாற்குப தேச முபநிட","வேதார்த்தமெய்ஞ் ஞான நெறியருள்","கோதாட்டி ஸ்வாமி யெனவரு ...... மிளையோனே","கோடாச்சிவ பூஜை பவுருஷ","மாறாக்கொடை நாளு மருவிய","கோனாட்டுவி ராலி மலையுறை ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women's eyes as Kama's weapons that carry off young men's lives, running to the earrings, sharp as sword and spear at the corners, Yama's messengers, rivals of the blue lily, and says that because Murugan once appeared and saved his servant from the delusion that wears endless blame, he will never forget his power. The praise names the goddess in her many forms and the six Krittika mothers who reared Kumara, and Murugan who taught the true meaning of the Upanishads and Vedas to Siva of Kailasa, at Viralimalai where Siva worship and giving never fail.","n":"Unusually the song gives thanks rather than asking: நீ தோற்றி முன் நாளும் அடிமையை ஈடேற்றுதலால் உன் வலிமையை மறவேனே, a past rescue remembered, perhaps the same as the Vayalur call in 572. வேலின் முனை கடை யமதூதர்: the eye is Death's servant, a classical figure. The teaching of Siva is of Upanishad and Veda meaning, not explicitly Om.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","kootru","thiruvarul","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:55"},"p:579":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":579,"t":["கொடாதவனை யேபு கழ்ந்து குபேரனென வேமொ ழிந்து","குலாவியவ மேதி ரிந்து ...... புவிமீதே","எடாதசுமை யேசு மந்து எணாதகலி யால்மெ லிந்து","எலாவறுமை தீர அன்று ...... னருள்பேணேன்","சுடாததன மான கொங்கை களாலிதய மேம யங்கி","சுகாதரம தாயொ ழுங்கி ...... லொழுகாமல்","கெடாததவ மேம றைந்து கிலேசமது வேமி குந்து","கிலாதவுட லாவி நொந்து ...... மடியாமுன்","தொடாய்மறலி யேநி யென்ற சொலாகியது னாவ ருங்கொல்","சொலேழுலக மீனு மம்பை ...... யருள்பாலா","நடாதசுழி மூல விந்து நளாவிவிளை ஞான நம்ப","நபோமணி சமான துங்க ...... வடிவேலா","படாதகுளிர் சோலை யண்ட மளாவியுயர் வாய்வ ளர்ந்து","பசேலெனவு மேத ழைந்து ...... தினமேதான்","விடாதுமழை மாரி சிந்த அநேகமலர் வாவி பொங்கு","விராலிமலை மீது கந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses praising the man who never gives as if he were Kubera, fawning and wandering in vain, bearing burdens, wasted by poverty, without seeking Murugan's grace to end it, and his heart deluded by women's breasts; he asks whether, before his worthless body and life ache and die, Murugan's tongue will say 'Death, do not touch him'. The praise names Murugan son of the mother who bore the seven worlds, the lord of knowledge born where the bindu meets the root whorl, at Viralimalai of rain-fed groves and flowering pools.","n":"கொடாதவனையே புகழ்ந்து குபேரனெனவே மொழிந்து: the poet's begging songs to misers, a confession of the court poet's trade. தொடாய் மறலியே நீ: the plea at death is for a single spoken word from Murugan to Death. நடாதசுழி மூல விந்து names the kundalini centre in the praise.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","inmai","kootru","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:55"},"p:580":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":580,"t":["மாயா செர்ருப முழுச்ச மத்திகள்","ஓயா வுபாய மனப்ப சப்பிகள்","வாணாளை யீரும் விழிக்க டைச்சிகள் ...... முநிவோரும்","மாலாகி வாட நகைத்து ருக்கிகள்","ஏகாச மீது தனத்தி றப்பிகள்","வா஡ண ரி஡ணரென் முழுப்பு ரட்டிகள் ...... வெகுமோகம்","ஆயாத வாசை யெழுப்பு மெத்திகள்","ஈயாத போதி லறப்பி ணக்கிகள்","ஆவேச நீருண் மதப்பொ றிச்சிகள் ...... பழிபாவம்","ஆமா றெணாத திருட்டு மட்டைகள்","கோமாள மான குறிக்க ழுத்திகள்","ஆசார வீன விலைத்த னத்திய ...... ருறவாமோ","காயாத பால்நெய் தயிர்க்கு டத்தினை","ஏயா வெணாம லெடுத்தி டைச்சிகள்","காணாத வாறு குடிக்கு மப்பொழு ...... துரலோடே","கார்போலு மேனி தனைப்பி ணித்தொரு","போர்போ லசோதை பிடித்த டித்திட","காதோடு காது கையிற்பி டித்தழு ...... தினிதூதும்","வேயா லநேக விதப்ப சுத்திரள்","சாயாமல் மீள அழைக்கு மச்சுதன்","வீறான மாம னெனப்ப டைத்தருள் ...... வயலு஡ரா","வீணாள் கொடாத படைச்செ ருக்கினில்","சூர்மாள வேலை விடுக்கும் அற்புத","வேலா விராலி மலைத்த லத்துறை ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet lists the courtesans' arts in a string of names: wholly of maya, endless schemers, eyes that saw through lives, who melt even sages with laughter, who cry 'come, come', rouse desire, quarrel when not paid, drink themselves wild, and steal without counting sin, and asks whether a bond with these women of low conduct who sell their breasts is fitting. The praise tells of the child Krishna drinking the cowherd women's milk, curd and ghee, tied to the mortar and beaten by Yasoda until he cried, whose flute calls back the herds, as uncle of Murugan of Vayalur, at Viralimalai.","n":"ஈயாத போதில் அறப் பிணக்கிகள் and விலைத் தனத்தியர்: money is named twice. The list form (each name ending -கள், -கிகள்) is a set piece of abuse. The Krishna story is told with unusual tenderness, his ears held as he weeps.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","kal"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:55"},"p:581":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":581,"t":["மாலாசை கோப மோயாதெ நாளு","மாயா விகார ...... வழியேசெல்","மாபாவி காளி தானேனு நாத","மாதா பிதாவு ...... மினிநீயே","நாலான வேத நூலாக மாதி","நானோதி னேனு ...... மிலைவீணே","நாள்போய் விடாம லாறாறு மீதில்","ஞானோப தேச ...... மருள்வாயே","பாலா கலார ஆமோத லேப","பாடீர வாக ...... அணிமீதே","பாதாள பூமி யாதார மீன","பானீய மேலை ...... வயலு஡ரா","வேலா விராலி வாழ்வே ச்முக","வேதாள பூத ...... பதிசேயே","வீரா கடோ ர சூராரி யேசெ","வேளே சுரேசர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says that though he is a great sinner walking maya's twisted way, with infatuation, desire and anger unceasing, Murugan is now his mother and father; he has not studied the four Vedas or the Agamas, so that his days not pass in vain he asks for the teaching of knowledge beyond the thirty-six. The praise names the child adorned with fragrant sandal, Murugan of Vayalur and Viralimalai, the six-faced son of the lord of ghouls and goblins, foe of Sura, lord of the gods.","n":"ஆறாறு மீதில் ஞானோபதேசம்: teaching beyond the six times six, the thirty-six tattvas of Saiva doctrine. The poet's lack of learning (நான் ஓதினேனும் இலை) is made the ground of the plea, not a bar to it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["gnaanam","thaththuvam","kutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:55"},"p:582":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":582,"t":["மேக மெனுங்குழல் சாய்த்திரு கோக னகங்கொடு கோத்தணை","மேல்விழு கின்ற பராக்கினி ...... லுடைசோர","மேகலை யுந்தனி போய்த்தனி யேகர ணங்களு மாய்க்கயல்","வேல்விழி யுங்குவி யாக்குரல் ...... மயில்காடை","கோகில மென்றெழ போய்க்கனி வாயமு துண்டுரு காக்களி","கூரவு டன்பிரி யாக்கல ...... வியின்மூழ்கிக்","கூடிமுயங்கி விடாய்த்திரு பார தனங்களின் மேற்றுயில்","கூரினு மம்புய தாட்டுணை ...... மறவேனே","மோகர துந்துமி யார்ப்ப விராலி விலங்கலின் வீட்டதில்","மூவுல குந்தொழு தேத்திட ...... வுறைவோனே","மூதிசை முன்பொரு காற்றட மேருவை யம்பினில் வீழ்த்திய","மோகன சங்கரி வாழ்த்திட ...... மதியாமல்","ஆக மடிந்திட வேற்கொடு சூரனை வென்றடல் போய்த்தணி","யாமையின் வென்றவ னாற்பிற ...... கிடுதேவர்","ஆதி யிளந்தலை காத்தர சாள அவன்சிறை மீட்டவ","னாளு லகங்குடி யேற்றிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes lovemaking, hair loosened, girdle slipping, eyes closing, cries like peacock, quail and cuckoo, and says that even if he sleeps exhausted on the breasts after union he will not forget Murugan's lotus feet. The praise names Murugan dwelling on the hill of Viralimalai with drums sounding, worshipped by the three worlds and praised by the goddess who once felled Meru with an arrow, who killed Sura with the vel, freed the gods from prison and resettled the worlds.","n":"துயில் கூரினும் அம்புயதாள் துணை மறவேனே: the song does not ask to leave the embrace but vows remembrance of the feet within it. It may be read as hyperbole of devotion or as a different stance from songs that ask to be freed from women.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","thiruvadi","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:55"},"p:583":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":583,"t":["மோதி யிறுகிவட மேரு வெனவளரு","மோக முலையசைய ...... வந்துகாயம்","மோச மிடுமவர்கள் மாயை தனில்முழுகி","மூட மெனஅறிவு ...... கொண்டதாலே","காதி வருமியம தூதர் கயிறுகொடு","காலி லிறுகஎனை ...... வந்திழாதே","காவ லெனவிரைய வோடி யுனதடிமை","காண வருவதினி ...... யெந்தநாளோ","ஆதி மறையவனு மாலு முயர்சுடலை","யாடு மரனுமிவ ...... ரொன்றதான","ஆயி யமலைதிரி சூலி குமரிமக","மாயி கவுரியுமை ...... தந்தவாழ்வே","சோதி நிலவுகதிர் வீசு மதியின்மிசை","தோய வளர்கிரியி ...... னுந்திநீடு","சோலை செறிவுளவி ராலி நகரில்வளர்","தோகை மயிலுலவு ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says that, sinking in the maya of women whose breasts sway like Meru and who deceive the body, he has a fool's understanding, and asks on what day Murugan will come running as his guard to see his servant, so that Yama's messengers do not come and drag him with a rope bound tight on his legs. The praise names Uma, in whom Brahma, Vishnu and Siva the dancer of the burning ground are one, the trident-bearing virgin, great mother and Gauri, who gave Murugan, and ends at the grove-filled town of Viralimalai where the peacock roams.","n":"காலில் இறுக ... இழாதே: Death's men bind the legs, not the neck, and drag. காவல் என விரைய ஓடி: Murugan is asked to run, the urgency of the plea at the hour of death. ஆதி மறையவனும் மாலும் ... அரனும் இவர் ஒன்றதான ஆயி: the praise makes Uma the one in whom the three gods are united.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","kaamam","maayai","thondu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:56"},"p:584":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":584,"t":["சரவண ஜாதா நமோநம கருணைய தீதா நமோநம","சததள பாதா நமோநம ...... அபிராம","தருணக தீரா நமோநம நிருபமர் வீரா நமோநம","சமதள வூரா நமோநம ...... ஜகதீச","பரம செர்ருபா நமோநம சுரர்பதி பூபா நமோநம","பரிமள நீபா நமோநம ...... உமைகாளி","பகவதி பாலா நமோநம இகபர மூலா நமோநம","பவுருஷ சீலா நமோநம ...... அருள்தாராய்","இரவியு மாகாச பூமியும் விரவிய தூளேற வானவ","ரெவர்களு மீடேற ஏழ்கடல் ...... முறையோவென்","றிடர்பட மாமேரு பூதர மிடிபட வேதா னிசாசர","ரிகல்கெட மாவேக நீடயில் ...... விடுவோனே","மரகத ஆகார ஆயனு மிரணிய ஆகார வேதனும்","வசுவெனு மாகார ஈசனு ...... மடிபேண","மயிலுறை வாழ்வே விநாயக மலையுறை வோலா மகீதர","வனசர ராதார மாகிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A song of salutations: the poet bows again and again (நமோநம) to Murugan as born in Saravana, lotus-footed, young and valiant, lord of the world, supreme form, king of the gods, wearer of fragrant kadamba, child of Uma, Kali and Bhagavati, root of this world and the next, and asks for grace. The praise shows the vel thrown so that dust rose to the sun, the seven seas cried out, Meru was shattered and the demons fell, and Vishnu, Brahma and Isa worshipping his feet, the lord on the peacock dwelling on Vinayakamalai, support of the forest people.","n":"The plea is a litany in the form of a mantra of praise (நமோநம repeated twelve times), with no confession. இகபர மூலா: root of this world and the next. சமதளவூரா and வசுவெனும் ஆகார ஈசன் are not clear to me.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:57"},"p:585":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":585,"t":["அன்பாக வந்து உன்றாள் பணிந்து","ஐம்பூத மொன்ற ...... நினையாமல்","அன்பால் மிகுந்து நஞ்சாரு கண்க","ளம்போரு கங்கள் ...... முலைதானும்","கொந்தே மிகுந்து வண்டாடி நின்று","கொண்டாடு கின்ற ...... குழலாரைக்","கொண்டே நினைந்து மன்பேது மண்டி","குன்றா மலைந்து ...... அலைவேனோ","மன்றாடி தந்த மைந்தா மிகுந்த","வம்பார் கடம்பை ...... யணிவோனே","வந்தே பணிந்து நின்றார் பவங்கள்","வம்பே தொலைந்த ...... வடிவேலா","சென்றே யிடங்கள் கந்தா எனும்பொ","செஞ்சேவல் கொண்டு ...... வரவேணும்","செஞ்சாலி கஞ்ச மொன்றாய் வளர்ந்த","செங்கோ டமர்ந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether, instead of coming with love to bow at Murugan's feet, he will keep thinking of women whose eyes are poison-filled lotuses and whose hair is full of bees, and wander deluded and fighting without end. The praise names Murugan son of the dancer of the hall, wearer of kadamba, whose vel ends the births of those who come and bow, and asks him to come with his red cock wherever the poet goes calling 'Kanda', at Tiruchengodu of red paddy and lotus.","n":"சென்றே இடங்கள் கந்தா எனும்பொ(து) ... வரவேணும்: a plea for the god's company in daily wandering, not only at death. ஐம்பூதம் ஒன்ற நினையாமல் is unclear: perhaps 'not thinking of the five elements becoming one'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","mayal","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:58"},"p:586":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":586,"t":["பந்தாடி யங்கை நொந்தார் பரிந்து","பைந்தார் புனைந்த ...... குழல்மீதே","பண்பார் சுரும்பு பண்பாடு கின்ற","பங்கே ருகங்கொள் ...... முகமீதே","மந்தார மன்றல் சந்தார மொன்றி","வன்பாத கஞ்செய் ...... தனமீதே","மண்டாசை கொண்டு விண்டாவி நைந்து","மங்காம லுன்ற ...... னருள்தாராய்","கந்தா அரன்றன் மைந்தா விளங்கு","கன்றா முகுந்தன் ...... மருகோனே","கன்றா விலங்க லொன்றாறு கண்ட","கண்டா வரம்பை ...... மணவாளா","செந்தா தடர்ந்த கொந்தார் கடம்பு","திண்டோ ள் நிரம்ப ...... அணிவோனே","திண்கோ டரங்க ளெண்கோ டுறங்கு","செங்கோட மர்ந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks that he not wither away, his life bursting with crowded desire, over women whose palms ache from playing ball, whose garlanded hair and lotus faces draw singing bees and whose breasts do harm, but that Murugan give his grace. The praise names Kanda, son of Hara and nephew of Mukunda, the one who split the hill, bridegroom of the celestial maiden, wearing a kadamba garland on his strong shoulders, at Tiruchengodu.","n":"The song is short and conventional; the women are pictured at play (பந்தாடி அங்கை நொந்தார்), an image from love poetry. கன்றா விலங்கல் ஒன்றாறு கண்ட and the eight elephants sleeping at Tiruchengodu are hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:58"},"p:587":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":587,"t":["வண்டார் மதங்க ளுண்டே மயங்கி","வந்தூரு கொண்ட ...... லதனோடும்","வண்காம னம்பு தன்கால் மடங்க","வன்போர் மலைந்த ...... விழிவேலும்","கொண்டே வளைந்து கண்டார் தியங்க","நின்றார் குரும்பை ...... முலைமேவிக்","கொந்தா ரரும்பு நின்தாள் மறந்து","குன்றாம லுன்ற ...... னருள்தாராய்","பண்டாழி சங்கு கொண்டாழி தங்கு","பண்போ னுகந்த ...... மருகோனே","பண்சார நைந்து நண்போது மன்பர்","பங்காகி நின்ற ...... குமரேசா","செண்டாடி யண்டர் கொண்டாட மன்றில்","நின்றாடி சிந்தை ...... மகிழ்வாழ்வே","செஞ்சாலி மிஞ்சி மஞ்சாடு கின்ற","செங்கோ டமர்ந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks for grace so that he does not decline, forgetting Murugan's flower-decked feet, among women whose spear-like eyes defeat Kama's arrows and bewilder all who see them. The praise names Murugan nephew of Vishnu of the discus and conch, Kumaresa who stands as part of the devotees who melt in song and true friendship, joy of Siva who dances in the hall, at Tiruchengodu of red paddy.","n":"நண்போதும் அன்பர் பங்காகி நின்ற குமரேசா: Murugan stands as a share of his devotees, close to them rather than above them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","thiruvadi","adiyaar"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:58"},"p:588":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":588,"t":["கரையற வுருகுதல் தருகயல் விழியினர்","கண்டான செஞ்சொல் ...... மடமாதர்","கலவியில் முழுகிய நெறியினி லறிவுக","லங்காம யங்கும் ...... வினையேனும்","உரையையு மறிவையும் உயிரையு முணர்வையும்","உன்பாத கஞ்ச ...... மலர்மீதே","உரவாடு புனைதர நினைதரு மடியரொ","டொன்றாக என்று ...... பெறுவேனோ","வரையிரு துணிபட வளைபடு சுரர்குடி","வந்தேற இந்த்ர ...... புரிவாழ","மதவித கஜரத துரகத பததியின்","வன்சேனை மங்க ...... முதுமீன","திரைமலி சலநிதி முறையிட நிசிசரர்","திண்டாட வென்ற ...... கதிர்வேலா","ஜெகதல மிடிகெட விளைவன வயலணி","செங்கோ டமர்ந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet, whose mind is confused and deluded in the way of union with women with carp eyes and sweet words, asks when he will be one with the devotees who think to lay their speech, knowledge, life and feeling on Murugan's lotus feet as a garland. The praise shows the hill split, the gods resettled and Indra's city flourishing, the demon armies of elephants, chariots, horses and foot ruined and the sea crying out, at Tiruchengodu of rich fields.","n":"உரையையும் அறிவையும் உயிரையும் உணர்வையும் ... உரவாடு புனைதர: the whole self, speech, mind, life and feeling, is offered as a garland at the feet, and the goal is the company of devotees (அடியரொடு ஒன்றாக).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adiyaar","thiruvadi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:58"},"p:589":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":589,"t":["இடம்பார்த் திடம்பார்த் திதங்கேட் டிரந்தேற்","றிணங்காப் பசிப்பொங் ...... கனல்மூழ்கி","இறுங்காற் கிறுங்கார்க் கிரும்பார்க் குநெஞ்சார்க்","கிரங்கார்க் கியற்றண் ...... டமிழ்நூலின்","உடம்பாட் டுடன்பாட் டியம்பாத் தயங்காத்","துளங்காத் திடப்புன் ...... கவிபாடி","ஒதுங்காப் பொதுங்காப் பதுங்காப் புகன்றேத்","துறும்பாற் குணக்கன் ...... புறலாமோ","கடந்தோற் கடந்தோற் றறிந்தாட் கருந்தாட்","கணைந்தாட் கணித்திண் ...... புயமீவாய்","கரும்போற் கரும்போர்க் குளங்காட் டிகண்டேத்","துசெங்கோட் டில்நிற்குங் ...... கதிர்வேலா","அடைந்தோர்க் குணந்தோர்க் களிந்தோர்க் கமைந்தோர்க்","கவிழ்ந்தோர்க் குணற்கொன் ...... றிலதாகி","அலைந்தோர்க் குலைந்தோர்க் கினைந்தோர்க் கலந்தோர்க்","கறிந்தோர்க் களிக்கும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses going from place to place, begging and accepting, sinking in the fire of hunger, singing paltry poems shaped to the rules of Tamil grammar to iron-hearted men who show no pity, faltering, trembling and cringing in praise, and asks whether such a one can gain love for Murugan. The praise, in heavy wordplay, names Murugan who gave his arms to the hunter girl, the vel at Tiruchengodu, who grants joy to those who reach, feel, melt, bow and know him.","n":"இயல் தண் தமிழ் நூலின் உடம்பாட்டுடன் பாட்டு இயம்பா: the poet's skill in correct Tamil is spent on patrons who do not pity. The rhyme runs in clusters (இறுங்கார்க் கிரும்பார்க் குநெஞ்சார்க் கிரங்கார்க்கு), which makes the splitting of the words partly uncertain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","pasi","thamizh","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:58"},"p:590":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":590,"t":["கலக்குங் கோதற வடிக்குஞ் சீரிய","கருப்பஞ் சாறெனு ...... மொழியாலே","கருத்தும் பார்வையு முருக்கும் பாவிகளள்","கடைக்கண் பார்வையி ...... லழியாதே","விலக்கும் போதக மெனக்கென் றேபெற","விருப்பஞ் சாலவு ...... முடையேனான்","வினைக்கொண் டேமன நினைக்குந் தீமையை","விடற்கஞ் சேலென ...... அருள்வாயே","அலைக்குந் தானவர் குலத்தின் சேனையை","அறுக்குங் கூரிய ...... வடிவேலா","அழைத்துன் சீரிய கழற்செந் தாமரை","யடுக்கும் போதக ...... முடையோராம்","சிலர்க்கன் றேகதி பலிக்குந் தேசிக","திருச்செங் கோபுர ...... வயலு஡ரா","திதிக்கும் பார்வயின் மதிப்புண் டாகிய","திருச்செங் கோடுறை ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks not to perish in the sidelong glances of sinful women who melt thought and sight with words sweet as strained cane juice; he says he longs to receive the teaching that turns him away, and asks Murugan to say 'do not fear' so that he gives up the evil his mind plans by its deeds. The praise names the vel that cuts down the demon armies and the teacher through whom liberation bears fruit at once for those few who know how to call on his feet, at the towered Tiruchengodu.","n":"விலக்கும் போதகம்: the teaching asked for is one that turns him back from women. அஞ்சேல் என: the plea is for the assuring word of the Lord, a common gesture (the hand of fearlessness) put into speech.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kaamam","guru","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:58"},"p:591":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":591,"t":["துஞ்சு கோட்டிச் சுழற்கண் காட்டிக்","கொங்கை நோக்கப் பலர்க்குங் காட்டிக்","கொண்ட ணாப்பித் துலக்கஞ் சீர்த்துத் ...... திரிமானார்","தொண்டை வாய்ப்பொற் கருப்பஞ் சாற்றைத்","தந்து சேர்த்துக் கலக்குந் தூர்த்தத்","துன்ப வாழ்க்கைத் தொழிற்பண் டாட்டத் ...... துழலாதே","கஞ்சம் வாய்த்திட் டவர்க்குங் கூட்டிக்","கன்று மேய்த்திட் டவர்க்குங் கூற்றைக்","கன்ற மாய்த்திட் டவர்க்குந் தோற்றக் ...... கிடையாநீ","கண்டு வேட்டுப் பொருட் கொண்டாட்டத்","தின்ப வாக்யத் தெனக்குங் கேட்கத்","தந்து காத்துத் திருக்கண் சாத்தப் ...... பெறுவேனோ","வஞ்ச மாய்ப்புக் கொளிக்குஞ் சூற்கைத்","துன்று சூர்ப்பொட் டெழச்சென் றோட்டிப்","பண்டு வாட்குட் களிக்குந் தோட்கொத் ...... துடையோனே","வண்டு பாட்டுற் றிசைக்குந் தோட்டத்","தண்கு ராப்பொற் புரக்கும் பேற்றித்","தொண்டர் கூட்டத் திருக்குந் தோற்றத் ...... திளையோனே","கொஞ்சு வார்த்தைக் கிளித்தண் சேற்கட்","குன்ற வேட்டிச் சியைக்கண் காட்டிக்","கொண்டு வேட்டுப் புனப்பைங் காட்டிற் ...... புணர்வோனே","கொங்கு லாத்தித் தழைக்குங் காப்பொற்","கொண்ட லார்த்துச் சிறக்குங் காட்சிக்","கொங்கு நாட்டுத் திருச்செங் கோட்டுப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks not to wander in the lewd, sorrowful trade of women who show rolling eyes and their breasts to many, deceive, and give the cane-sweet juice of their lips, and asks whether Murugan, whom Brahma, Vishnu and Siva the slayer of Death could not see, will protect him, let him hear the sweet words of the meaning, and look on him with grace. The praise names Murugan who drove out Sura hiding in deceit, who stands in the company of devotees in the kura groves, who wooed the hunter girl in the millet field, at Tiruchengodu in the Kongu country.","n":"தொழில் பண்டாட்டம்: the courtesan's life is called a trade, a bargaining. பொருள் கொண்டாட்டத்து இன்ப வாக்யம்: perhaps the teaching sentence (vakya) of the true meaning, asked as a gift to the ear; the phrase is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","thiruvarul","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:58"},"p:592":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":592,"t":["நீலமஞ் சானகுழல் மாலைவண் டோ டுகதி","நீடுபந் தாடுவிழி யார்பளிங் கானநகை","நீலபொன் சாபநுத லாசையின் தோடசையு","நீள்முகந் தாமரையி னார்மொழிந் தாரமொழி","நேர்சுகம் போலமு கானகந் தாரர்புய","நேர்சுணங் காவிகிளை யேர்சிறந் தார்மலையி ...... ரண்டுபோல","நீளிபங் கோடிளநிர் தேனிருந் தாரமுலை","நீடலங் காரசர மோடடைந் தார்மருவி","நீள்மணஞ் சாறுபொழி யாவளம் போதிவையி","னீலவண் டேவியநல் காமனங் காரநிறை","நேசசந் தானஅல்குல் காமபண் டாரமுதை","நேருசம் போகரிடை நூலொளிர்ந் தாசையுயிர் ...... சம்பையாரஞ்","சாலுபொன் தோகையமை பாளிதஞ் சூழ்சரண","தாள்சிலம் போலமிட வேநடந் தானநடை","சாதிசந் தானெகின மார்பரந் தோகையென","தானெழுங் கோலவிலை மாதரின் பார்கலவி","தாவுகொண் டேகலிய நோய்கள்கொண் டேபிறவி","தானடைந் தாழுமடி யேனிடஞ் சாலும்வினை ...... யஞ்சியோடத்","தார்கடம் பாடுகழல் பாதசெந் தாமரைகள்","தாழ்பெரும் பாதைவழி யேபடிந் தேவருகு","தாபம்விண் டேயமுத வாரியுண் டேபசிகள்","தாபமுந் தீரதுகிர் போனிறங் காழ்கொளுரு","சாரவுஞ் சோதிமுரு காவெனுங் காதல்கொடு","தானிருந் தோதஇரு வோரகம் பேறுறுக ...... விஞ்சைதாராய்","சூலியெந் தாய்கவுரி மோகசங் காரிகுழை","தோடுகொண் டாடுசிவ காமசுந் தாரிநல","தூளணைந் தாளிநிரு வாணியங் காளிகலை","தோகைசெந் தாமரையின் மாதுநின் றேதுதிசெய்","தூயஅம் பாகழைகொள் தோளிபங் காளக்ருபை","தோய்பரன் சேயெனவு மேபெரும் பார்புகழும் ...... விந்தையோனே","சூரசங் காரசுரர் லோகபங் காவறுவர்","தோகைமைந் தாகுமர வேள்கடம் பாரதொடை","தோளகண் டாபரம தேசிகந் தாவமரர்","தோகைபங் காஎனவே தாகமஞ் சூழ்சுருதி","தோதகம் பாடமலை யேழுதுண் டாயெழுவர்","சோரிகொண் டாறுவர வேலெறிந் தேநடன ...... முங்கொள்வேலா","மாலியன் பாறவொரு ஆடகன் சாகமிகு","வாலியும் பாழிமர மோடுகும் பாகனனு","மாழியுங் கோரவலி ராவணன் பாறவிடு","மாசுகன் கோலமுகி லோனுகந் தோதிடையர்","மாதுடன் கூடிவிளை யாடுசம் போகதிரு","மார்பகன் காணமுடி யோனணங் கானமதி ...... யொன்றுமானை","மார்புடன் கோடுதன பாரமுஞ் சேரஇடை","வார்துவண் டாடமுக மோடுகந் தீரரச","வாயிதங் கோதிமணி நூபுரம் பாடமண","வாசைகொண் டாடுமயி லாளிதுங் காகுறவி","மாதுபங்கா மறைகு லாவுசெங் கோடைநகர்","வாழவந் தாய்கரிய மாலயன் தேவர்புகழ் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"After a long head-to-foot portrait of courtesans who sell themselves, the poet says that, leaping into union with them, he has caught diseases and sunk in birth, and asks for the knowledge by which his deeds flee, so that coming the great way of bowing to the kadamba-garlanded feet his burning ends, he drinks the sea of nectar, approaches the coral-red form and chants 'radiant Muruga' with love. The praise names the goddess in many forms, Murugan the teacher and destroyer of Sura, Vishnu's deeds against Hiranya, Vali, Kumbhakarna and Ravana and his sport with the cowherd girls, Valli's beauty, and Tiruchengodu.","n":"கோல விலை மாதர் and கலியநோய்கள் கொண்டே: the courtesans are named by their price and the poet names disease caught from them, a rare literal consequence in these songs. அமுத வாரி here is the nectar of grace, not Tirumular's yogic fluid.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","noy","piravi","amudham"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:58"},"p:593":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":593,"t":["பொன்ற லைப்பொய்க் கும்பி றப்பைத்","தும்ப றுத்திட் டின்று நிற்கப்","புந்தி யிற்சற் றுங்கு றிக்கைக் ...... கறியாமே","பொங்கி முக்கிச் சங்கை பற்றிச்","சிங்கி யொத்தச் சங்க டத்துப்","புண்ப டைத்துக் கஞ்ச மைக்கட் ...... கொடியார்மேல்","துன்று மிச்சைப் பண்ட னுக்குப்","பண்ப ளித்துச் சம்ப்ர மித்துத்","தும்பி பட்சிக் கும்ப்ர சச்செய்ப் ...... பதிமீதே","தொண்டு பட்டுத் தெண்ட னிட்டுக்","கண்டு பற்றத் தண்டை வர்க்கத்","துங்க ரத்தப் பங்க யத்தைத் ...... தருவாயே","குன்றெ டுத்துப் பந்த டித்துக்","கண்சி வத்துச் சங்க ரித்துக்","கொண்ட லொத்திட் டிந்த்ர னுக்கிச் ...... சுரலோகா","கொம்பு குத்திச் சம்ப ழுத்தித்","திண்ட லத்திற் றண்டு வெற்பைக்","கொண்ட முக்கிச் சண்டை யிட்டுப் ...... பொரும்வேழம்","சென்று ரித்துச் சுந்த ரிக்கச்","சந்த விர்த்துக் கண்சு கித்துச்","சிந்தை யுட்பற் றின்றி நித்தக் ...... களிகூருஞ்","செண்ப கத்துச் சம்பு வுக்குத்","தொம்ப தத்துப் பண்பு ரைத்துச்","செங்கு வட்டிற் றங்கு சொக்கப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says he never once thought to cut the rope of false birth, and, wounded, doubting and troubled, became a vessel of desire for cruel lotus-eyed women; he asks for good nature and the anklet-bearing red lotus feet, so that he may serve and prostrate at Tiruchengodu. The praise tells of Siva flaying the elephant to delight Uma, and of Murugan who explained the sense of the word 'thou' (தொம்பதம்) to Sambhu, the beautiful lord on the red peak.","n":"சம்புவுக்குத் தொம்பதத்துப் பண்பு உரைத்து: தொம்பதம் is the 'tvam' of the Vedantic sentence tat tvam asi, so the familiar story of Murugan teaching Siva is here told in Vedantic terms rather than as the meaning of Om. The opening lines (பொங்கி முக்கிச் சங்கை பற்றிச் சிங்கி ஒத்த) are uncertain in sense.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","kaamam","thondu","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:58"},"p:594":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":594,"t":["மந்தக் கடைக்கண் காட்டுவர் கந்தக் குழற்பின் காட்டுவர்","மஞ்சட் பிணிப்பொன் காட்டுவ ...... ரநுராக","வஞ்சத் திரக்கங் காட்டுவர் நெஞ்சிற் பொருத்தங் காட்டுவர்","வண்பற் றிருப்புங் காட்டுவர் ...... தனபாரச்","சந்தப் பொருப்புங் காட்டுவர் உந்திச் சுழிப்புங் காட்டுவர்","சங்கக் கழுத்துங் காட்டுவர் ...... விரகாலே","சண்டைப் பிணக்குங் காட்டுவர் பண்டிட் டொடுக்கங் காட்டுவர்","தங்கட் கிரங்கங் காட்டுவ ...... தொழிவேனோ","பந்தித் தெருக்கந் தோட்டினை யிந்துச் சடைக்கண் சூட்டுமை","பங்கிற் றகப்பன் தாட்டொழு ...... குருநாதா","பைம்பொற் பதக்கம் பூட்டிய அன்பற் கெதிர்க்குங் கூட்டலர்","பங்கப் படச்சென் றோட்டிய ...... வயலு஡ரா","கொந்திற் புனத்தின் பாட்டிய லந்தக் குறப்பெண் டாட்டொடு","கும்பிட் டிடக்கொண் டாட்டமொ ...... டணைவோனே","குன்றிற் கடப்பந் தோட்டலர் மன்றற் ப்ரசித்தங் கோட்டிய","கொங்கிற் றிருச்செங் கோட்டுறை ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"In a refrain of 'they show' (காட்டுவர்), the poet lists the courtesans' displays, the slow sidelong look, the back of the scented hair, turmeric-gold skin, false pity, agreement of heart, firm attachment, breasts, navel, conch-like neck, quarrels and pretended modesty, and asks whether he will stop showing longing for them. The praise names Murugan the guru whom his father worships, who drove off the enemies of a devotee who had adorned him with a golden pendant, who embraced the hunter girl, at Tiruchengodu in Kongu.","n":"The refrain makes the whole courtship a show (காட்டுவர்), so that the poet's own feeling (இரங்கம் காட்டுவது) is the last display in the list. The devotee with the golden pendant (பைம்பொன் பதக்கம் பூட்டிய அன்பன்) is an allusion I cannot identify.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kaamam","vanjam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:58"},"p:595":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":595,"t":["மெய்ச்சார் வற்றே பொய்ச்சார் வுற்றே","நிச்சார் துற்பப் ...... பவவேலை","விட்டே றிப்போ கொட்டா மற்றே","மட்டே யத்தத் ...... தையர்மேலே","பிச்சா யுச்சா கிப்போ ரெய்த்தார்","பத்தார் விற்பொற் ...... கழல்பேணிப்","பிற்பால் பட்டே நற்பால் பெற்றார்","முற்பா லைக்கற் ...... பகமேதான்","செச்சா லிச்சா லத்தே றிச்சே","லுற்றா ணித்துப் ...... பொழிலேறுஞ்","செக்கோ டைக்கோ டுக்கே நிற்பாய்","நித்தா செக்கர்க் ...... கதிரேனல்","முச்சா லிச்சா லித்தாள் வெற்பாள்","முத்தார் வெட்சிப் ...... புயவேளே","முத்தா முத்தீ யத்தா சுத்தா","முத்தா முத்திப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"In packed, clipped syllables the poet seems to say that, leaving true support for false, he cannot climb out of the sea of births of constant sorrow, maddened for honey-sweet women, while those who cherished Murugan's golden anklets came later and gained the good portion. The praise names Murugan of Tiruchengodu, lord of the millet-hill girl Valli, wearer of vetchi flowers, the pure one, the pearl, the lord of liberation.","n":"மெய்ச்சார்வு அற்றே பொய்ச்சார்வு உற்றே: losing the support of truth for the support of the false is the clearest phrase. The song is a feat of doubled consonants (செச்சா லிச்சா லத்தே றிச்சே) and most of it can be divided several ways.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","kaamam","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:58"},"p:596":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":596,"t":["வருத்தங் காண நாடிய குணத்தன் பான மாதரு","மயக்கம் பூண மோதிய ...... துரமீதே","மலக்கங் கூடி யேயின வுயிர்க்குஞ் சேத மாகிய","மரிக்கும் பேர்க ளோடுற ...... வணியாதே","பெருத்தும் பாவ நீடிய மலத்தின் தீமை கூடிய","பிறப்புந் தீர வேயுன ...... திருதாளே","பெறத்தந் தாள வேயுயர் சுவர்க்கஞ் சேர வேயருள்","பெலத்தின் கூர்மை யானது ...... மொழிவாயே","இரத்தம் பாய மேனிக ளுரத்துஞ் சாடி வேல்கொடு","எதிர்த்துஞ் சூரர் மாளவெ ...... பொரும்வேலா","இசைக்குந் தாள மேளமெ தனத்தந் தான தானன்","எனத்திண் கூளி கோடிகள் ...... புடைசூழத்","திருத்தன் பாக வேயொரு மயிற்கொண் டாடி யேபுகழ்","செழித்தன் பாக வீறிய ...... பெருவாழ்வே","திரட்சங் கோடை வாவிகள் மிகுத்துங் காவி சூழ்தரு","திருச்செங் கோடு மேவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks not to bind himself to women whose nature seeks to cause pain and to men who die harming others, and that, with birth full of sin and the evil of malam ended, Murugan give his two feet, rule him and tell him the sharp strength of grace so he reaches the high heaven. The praise shows the vel slaying the demons with bodies bathed in blood, the ghost hosts beating time with drums, and Murugan rejoicing on the peacock at Tiruchengodu of pools and lilies.","n":"சுவர்க்கம் சேர: heaven is named as the goal, less common than release in these songs. The first lines (மலக்கங் கூடியே இன உயிர்க்கும் சேதமாகிய மரிக்கும் பேர்கள்) are uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","malam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:58"},"p:597":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":597,"t":["ஆல காலப டப்பைம டப்பியர்","ஈர வாளற வெற்றும்வி ழிச்சியர்","யாவ ராயினு நத்திய ழைப்பவர் ...... தெருவூடே","ஆடி யாடிந டப்பதொர் பிச்சியர்","பேசி யாசை கொடுத்தும ருட்டிகள்","ஆசை வீசிய ணைக்குமு லைச்சியர் ...... பல்ருடே","மாலை யோதிவி ரித்துமு டிப்பவர்","சேலை தாழநெ கிழ்த்தரை சுற்றிகள்","வாசம் வீசும ணத்தில்மி னுக்கிகள் ...... உறவாலே","மாயை யூடுவி ழுத்திய ழுத்திகள்","காம போகவி னைக்குளு னைப்பணி","வாழ்வி லாமல்ம லச்சன னத்தினி ...... லுழல்வேனோ","மேலை வானொரு ரைத்தச ரற்கொரு","பால னாகியு தித்தொர்மு நிக்கொரு","வேள்வி காவல்ந டத்திய கற்குரு ...... அடியாலே","மேவி யேமிதி லைச்சிலை செற்றுமின்","மாது தோள்தழு விப்பதி புக்கிட","வேறு தாயட விக்குள் விடுத்தபி ...... னவனோடே","ஞால மாதொடு புக்கவ னத்தினில்","வாழும் வாலிப டக்கணை தொட்டவ","னாடி ராவண னைச்செகு வித்தவன் ...... மருகோனே","ஞான தேசிக சற்குரு உத்தம","வேல வாநெரு வைப்பதி வித்தக","நாக மாமலை சொற்பெற நிற்பதொர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet lists courtesans who are venomous as cobras, whose eyes cut like swords, who call to anyone at all, dance along the streets, rouse hopes, loosen hair and sari and glitter with scent, and drown men in maya, and asks whether, caught in lust, he must wander in births of malam without the life of serving Murugan. The praise tells the Ramayana in order, Dasaratha's son guarding the sage's sacrifice, the stone made woman by his foot, the bow broken at Mithila, the exile, Vali and Ravana slain, and names Murugan the teacher of knowledge on the great Naga hill.","n":"மலச் சனனம்: births are named as the work of malam. The Ramayana sequence in the praise is unusually complete. நெருவைப்பதி is a place name I cannot identify.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","maayai","piravi","thondu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:58"},"p:598":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":598,"t":["காலனிடத் ...... தணுகாதே","காசினியிற் ...... பிறவாதே","சீலஅகத் ...... தியஞான","தேனமுதைத் ...... தருவாயே","மாலயனுக் ...... கரியானே","மாதவரைப் ...... பிரியானே","நாலுமறைப் ...... பொருளானே","நாககிரிப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A short song: the poet asks not to draw near Death and not to be born again on earth, and to be given the honey-nectar of the wisdom of the virtuous Agastya. The praise names Murugan hard for Vishnu and Brahma to reach, never parted from the great ascetics, the meaning of the four Vedas, the lord of Nagagiri.","n":"சீல அகத்திய ஞான: probably the knowledge of Agastya, whom Murugan taught (see 28); it could also be read as 'the knowledge within' (அகத்து இய). The song asks for knowledge as the escape from death and birth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","piravi","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:58"},"p:599":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":599,"t":["தாமா தாமா லாபா லோகா","தாரா தாரத் ...... தரணீசா","தானா சாரோ பாவா பாவோ","நாசா பாசத் ...... தபராத","யாமா யாமா தேசா ரூடா","யாரா யாபத் ...... தெனதாவி","யாமா காவாய் தீயே னீர்வா","யாதே யீமத் ...... துகலாமோ","காமா காமா தீனா நீணா","காவாய் காளக் ...... கிரியாய்கங்","காளா லீலா பாலா நீபா","காமா மோதக் ...... கனமானின்","தேமார் தேமா காமீ பாகீ","தேசா தேசத் ...... தவரோதுஞ்","சேயே வேளே பூவே கோவே","தேவே தேவப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A song made almost wholly of long syllables: through strings of vocatives the poet seems to ask the lord of the earth to protect his life in danger, lest he, a sinner, fall into the burning ground. The praise is a chain of names, Kama-like in beauty, the playful child, wearer of kadamba, the son, the Vel, the flower, the king, the god, lord of the gods.","n":"யாபத்து எனது ஆவி ... காவாய் ... ஈமத்து உகலாமோ: 'protect my life in danger; should I fall into the pyre?' is the only clear plea. The song is a metrical feat (தாமா தாமா லாபா லோகா) in which nearly every word can be split another way.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saakkaadu","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:58"},"p:600":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":600,"t":["அத்து கிரினலது ரத்து அலனவள","கத்து வளர்செய்புள ...... கிதபூத","ரத்தி ருகமலக ரத்தி தயமுருகி","யத்தி யிடனுறையு ...... நெடுமாம","ரத்து மலர்கனிய லைத்து வருமிடைத","லத்து ரகசிகரி ...... பகராதே","யத்தி மலவுடல்ந டத்தி யெரிகொள்நிரை","யத்தி னிடையடிமை ...... விழலாமோ","தத்து கவனவரி ணத்து வுபநிடவி","தத்து முநியுதவு ...... மொழியாறுத்","தத்தை நறவையமு தத்தை நிகர்குறவர்","தத்தை தழுவியப ...... னிருதோளா","தத்து ததிதுரக தத்து மிகுதிதிசர்","தத்து மலையவுணர் ...... குலநாகந்","தத்த மிசைமரக தத்த மனியமயில்","தத்த விடுமமரர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether he, a servant, should drive this impure body about and fall into the fiery hell; the opening lines, which seem to describe a mountain, a great tree heavy with flowers and fruit and a place he should not speak of, are too broken by the rhythm for a sure reading. The praise names the leaping deer and the sage of the Upanishads (Valli's birth), Murugan's twelve shoulders that embraced the hunters' parrot-like girl sweet as honey and nectar, and the emerald and gold peacock that carried him against the demon clans.","n":"மலவுடல் நடத்தி எரிகொள் நிரயத்தின் இடை அடிமை விழலாமோ is the one clear plea: the body is called impure (மல) and the fear is of hell, not of rebirth. The praise's தத்து கவன வரிணத்து உபநிட விதத்து முநி seems to allude to Valli's birth from a deer to a sage. The first eight lines run syllables together (அத்து கிரினலது ரத்து அலனவள கத்து ...) and I cannot divide them with confidence.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naragam","yaakkai","thondu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:59"},"p:601":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":601,"t":["அத்த வேட்கைப் பற்றி நோக்கத்","தத்தை மார்க்குத் ...... தமராயன்","பற்ற கூட்டத் திற்ப ராக்குற்","றச்சு தோட்பற் ...... றியவோடும்","சித்த மீட்டுப் பொய்த்த வாழ்க்கைச்","சிக்கை நீக்கித் ...... திணிதாய","சித்ர வாக்குப் பெற்று வாழ்த்திச்","செச்சை சாத்தப் ...... பெறுவேனோ","கொத்து நூற்றுப் பத்து நாட்டக்","கொற்ற வேத்துக் ...... கரசாய","குக்கு டாத்தச் சர்ப்ப கோத்ரப்","பொற்ப வேற்கைக் ...... குமரேசா","தத்வ நாற்பத் தெட்டு நாற்பத்","தெட்டு மேற்றுத் ...... திடமேவும்","தர்க்க சாத்ரத் தக்க மார்க்கச்","சத்ய வாக்யப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses that, gripped by desire for wealth and looking on, he became kin to parrot-like women and was distracted in a loveless company, his mind running to clasp their shoulders; he asks whether he will call his mind back, cut the tangle of a false life, gain firm and beautiful speech, praise Murugan and adorn him with vetchi flowers. The praise names Murugan king over kings, holder of the cock and the vel at the serpent hill, and the true word of the right path of reasoning that establishes the tattvas, forty-eight and forty-eight.","n":"அத்த வேட்கை, desire for wealth (அத்தம்), stands first, before the women. தத்வ நாற்பத்தெட்டு நாற்பத்தெட்டு makes ninety-six, the larger Saiva count of the tattvas; Murugan is the true teaching (சத்ய வாக்யம்) of the logic-shastra that sets them out. கொத்து நூற்றுப் பத்து நாட்ட is unclear to me.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["avaa","kaamam","vazhipaadu","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:59"},"p:602":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":602,"t":["பத்தர்க ணப்ரிய நிர்த்தந டித்திடு","பட்சிந டத்திய ...... குகபூர்வ","பச்சிம தட்சிண வுத்தர திக்குள","பத்தர்க ளற்புத ...... மெனவோதுஞ்","சித்ரக வித்துவ சத்தமி குத்ததி","ருப்புக ழைச்சிறி ...... தடியேனுஞ்","செப்பென வைத்துல கிற்பர வத்தெரி","சித்தவ நுக்ரக ...... மறவேனே","கத்திய தத்தைக ளைத்துவி ழத்திரி","கற்கவ ணிட்டெறி ...... தினைகாவல்","கற்றகு றத்திநி றத்தக ழுத்தடி","கட்டிய ணைத்தப ...... னிருதோளா","சத்தியை யொக்கஇ டத்தினில் வைத்தத","கப்பனு மெச்சிட ...... மறைநூலின்","தத்துவ தற்பர முற்றுமு ணர்த்திய","சர்ப்பகி ரிச்சுரர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says he will never forget the grace by which Murugan appeared to him and set even him, a small servant, to sing the Thiruppugazh, rich in the sound of beautiful poetry, which devotees in the east, west, south and north recite as a wonder, so that it spread through the world. The praise names the hunter girl who guarded the millet slinging stones at the screaming parrots and whom Murugan embraced, and Murugan as the one who taught the whole of the Vedas' truth and the Supreme to his father, who keeps Shakti on his left side, at the serpent hill.","n":"This is the book naming itself again: திருப்புகழைச் சிறிது அடியேனும் செப்பென வைத்து, the grace of being set to sing it, and தெரிசித்த, that Murugan showed himself. Here it is already sung in the four directions. மறைநூலின் தத்துவ தற்பரம் முற்றும் உணர்த்திய is the teaching of Siva without naming Om.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","pugazh","guru","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:59"},"p:603":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":603,"t":["புற்புதமெ னாம அற்பநிலை யாத","பொய்க்குடில்கு லாவு ...... மனையாளும்","புத்திரரும் வீடு மித்திரரு மான","புத்திசலி யாத ...... பெருவாழ்வு","நிற்பதொரு கோடி கற்பமென மாய","நிட்டையுடன் வாழு ...... மடியேன்யான்","நித்தநின தாளில் வைத்ததொரு காதல்","நிற்கும்வகை யோத ...... நினைவாயே","சற்பகிரி நாத முத்தமிழ்வி நோத","சக்ரகதை பாணி ...... மருகோனே","தர்க்சமண் மூகர் மிக்ககழு வேற","வைத்தவொரு காழி ...... மறையோன","கற்புவழு வாது வெற்படியின் மேவு","கற்றைமற வாணர் ...... கொடிகோவே","கைத்தஅசு ரேசர் மொய்த்தகுல கால","கற்பதரு நாடர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says that he lives in the illusion that this life, impermanent as a bubble, in a false hut of a body, with wife, sons, house and friends, is an unshaken greatness that will stand for a crore of aeons, and asks Murugan to think of a way for the love he has set daily on his feet to stay. The praise names Murugan lord of the serpent hill, delight of the three Tamils, nephew of the one with discus and mace, the Brahmin of Kazhi who impaled the dumb arguing Jains, and lord of the hunters' daughter whose chastity never failed.","n":"புற்புதம், the bubble, and ஒரு கோடி கற்பம், a crore of aeons, set the real span of life against the imagined one; நிட்டை, a steady state, is used ironically of the illusion. தர்க்க சமண் மூகர் மிக்க கழு ஏற வைத்த ஒரு காழி மறையோன் is Sambandar's story told of Murugan.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","pattru","yaakkai","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:59"},"p:604":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":604,"t":["பொற்சித்ரப் பச்சைப் பட்டுக்","கச்சிட்டுக் கட்டிப் பத்மப்","புட்பத்துக் கொப்பக் கற்பித் ...... திளைஞோர்கள்","புட்பட்டுச் செப்பத் துப்பற்","கொத்தப்பொற் றித்தத் திட்பப்","பொற்பிற்பெற் றுக்ரச் சக்ரத் ...... தனமானார்","கற்சித்தச் சுத்தப் பொய்ப்பித்","தத்திற்புக் கிட்டப் பட்டுக்","கைக்குத்திட் டிட்டுச் சுற்றித் ...... திரியாமல்","கற்றுற்றுச் சித்திக் கைக்குச்","சித்திப்பப் பக்ஷத் திற்சொற்","கற்பித்தொப் பித்துக் கொற்றக் ...... கழல்தாராய்","குற்சித்துக் கொட்டுக் கொட்டுத்","துக்கச்சத் துக்குக் குக்குக்","குக்குக்குக் குக்குக் குக்குக் ...... கெனமாறா","குட்சிக்குப் பக்ஷிக் கைக்குக்","கக்ஷத்திற் பட்சத் தத்தக்","கொட்டிச்சுட் டிக்கொக் ரிக்குக் ...... குடதாரி","சற்சித்துத் தொற்புத் திப்பட்","சத்தர்க்கொப் பித்தட் சத்துச்","சத்தத்தைச் சத்திக் கொச்சைப் ...... பதிவாழ்வே","தக்ஷப்பற் றுக்கெர்ப் பத்திற்","செற்பற்றைச் செற்றிட் டுச்சச்","சற்பப்பொற் றைக்குட் சொக்கப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"In a clipped, hammering rhythm the poet describes women whose breasts are bound in green silk bodices worked with gold and likened to lotus buds, praised by young men, and asks not to wander in their stony-hearted, false madness, loving them and being struck; he asks Murugan to teach him with love the word by which he may learn and attain, and to give his victorious anklets. The praise hears the cock crow (குக்குக்குக்), names Murugan the bearer of the cock banner and the life of Kochai, and ends at the high serpent hill.","n":"பொய்ப் பித்தம், false madness, is the poet's word for the infatuation. கொச்சைப் பதி is a name of Sirkazhi, Sambandar's town, so the praise may again make Murugan Sambandar. The song is built almost entirely of doubled consonants (கற்சித்தச் சுத்தப் பொய்ப்பித்தத்திற் புக்கு), and many divisions, especially in the closing lines on Daksha (தக்ஷப் பற்றுக்கு), are guesses.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","mayal","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:59"},"p:605":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":605,"t":["கொடிய மறலியு மவனது கடகமு","மடிய வொருதின மிருபதம் வழிபடு","குதலை யடியவ னினதருள் கொடுபொரு ...... மமர்காண","குறவர் மகள்புணர் புயகிரி சமுகமு","மறுமு கமும்வெகு நயனமும் ரவியுமிழ்","கொடியு மகிலமும் வெளிபட இருதிசை ...... யிருநாலும்","படியு நெடியன எழுபுண ரியுமுது","திகிரி திகிரியும் வருகென வருதகு","பவுரி வருமொரு மரகத துரகத ...... மிசையேறிப்","பழய அடியவ ருடனிமை யவர்கண","மிருபு டையுமிகு தமிழ்கொடு மறைகொடு","பரவ வருமதி லருணையி லொருவிசை ...... வரவேணும்","சடில தரவிட தரபணி தரதர","பரசு தரசசி தரசுசி தரவித","தமரு கமிருக தரவனி தரசிர ...... தரபாரத்","தரணி தரதநு தரவெகு முககுல","தடினி தரசிவ சுதகுண தரபணி","சயில விதரண தருபுர சசிதரு ...... மயில்வாழ்வே","நெடிய வுடலரு இருளெழ நிலவெழ","எயிறு சுழல்விழி தழலெழ எழுகிரி","நெரிய அதிர்குரல் புகையெழ இடியெழ ...... நெடுவானும்","நிலனும் வெருவர வருநிசி சரர்தள","நிகில சகலமு மடியவொர் படைதொடு","நிருப குருபர சுரபதி பரவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks that, on the day when cruel Death and his army fall as Murugan's stammering servant, who worships his two feet, fights with his grace, Murugan come once to Arunai to see that battle, mounted on the emerald horse with his six faces, many eyes, his shoulders that embraced the hunter girl and his sun-bright banner, while old devotees and the gods praise him on both sides with Tamil and the Vedas. The praise lists Siva's emblems, matted hair, poison, serpents, axe, moon, fire, drum, deer, and the many-mouthed river, names Murugan his son, and ends with the battle against the night-roaming demons.","n":"The plea reverses the usual fear: the servant himself fights Death (கொடிய மறலியும் அவனது கடகமும் மடிய) with the Lord's grace, and asks Murugan only to come and watch (அமர் காண). அருணையில் ஒருவிசை வரவேணும் names Thiruvannamalai inside a Thiruchengode song; பணி சயில, the serpent hill, is the local name in the praise.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","thiruvarul","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:59"},"p:606":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":606,"t":["கட்ட மன்னு மள்ளல் கொட்டி பண்ணு மைவர்","கட்கு மன்னு மில்ல ...... மிதுபேணி","கற்ற விஞ்ஞை சொல்லி யுற்ற வெண்மை யுள்ளு","கக்க எண்ணி முல்லை ...... நகைமாதர்","இட்ட மெங்ங னல்ல கொட்டி யங்ங னல்கி","யிட்டு பொன்னை யில்லை ...... யெனஏகி","எத்து பொய்ம்மை யுள்ள லுற்று மின்மை யுள்ளி","யெற்று மிங்ங னைவ ...... தியல்போதான்","முட்ட வுண்மை சொல்லு செட்டி திண்மை கொள்ள","முட்ட நன்மை விள்ள ...... வருவோனே","முத்து வண்ண வல்லி சித்ர வண்ண வல்லி","முத்தி விண்ண வல்லி ...... மணவாளா","பட்ட மன்ன வல்லி மட்ட மன்ன வல்லி","பட்ட துன்னு கொல்லி ...... மலைநாடா","பச்சை வன்னி யல்லி செச்சை சென்னி யுள்ள","பச்சை மஞ்சை வல்ல ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says that, tending this house of the body where the five senses dwell, he poured out whatever jasmine-smiling women wanted and gave them gold, and they said there was nothing and went; thinking of their deceit and of his own poverty, he asks whether it is right that he should waste away like this. The praise names Murugan as the merchant who came speaking the whole truth, the bridegroom of Valli of pearl colour, of painted colour, of release and heaven, the lord of the Kolli hills, wearing green vanni, lilies and vetchi, rider of the green peacock.","n":"பொன்னை இல்லை என ஏகி: the gold is given and the women deny it and leave; the poet then dwells on his own lack (இன்மை உள்ளி). ஐவர்கட்கு மன்னும் இல்லம், the house where the five live, is the body. செட்டி, the merchant, is a disguise of Murugan in the Valli story; the wordplay on வல்லி runs through the praise. The opening lines (கட்ட மன்னும் அள்ளல் கொட்டி பண்ணும்) and கற்ற விஞ்ஞை சொல்லி are hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","inmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:60"},"p:607":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":607,"t":["தொல்லைமுதல் தானொன்று மெல்லியிரு பேதங்கள்","சொல்லுகுண மூவந்த ...... மெனவாகி","துய்யசதுர் வேதங்கள் வெய்யபுல னோரைந்து","தொய்யுபொரு ளாறங்க ...... மெனமேவும்","பல்லபல நாதங்கள் அல்கபசு பாசங்கள்","பல்குதமிழ் தானொன்றி ...... யிசையாகிப்","பல்லுயிரு மாயந்த மில்லசொரு பாநந்த","பெளவமுற வேநின்ற ...... தருள்வாயே","கல்லுருக வேயின்கண் அல்லல்படு கோவம்பு","கல்லுருக வேநின்ற ...... குழலு஡துங்","கையன் மிசை யேறும்பன் நொய்யசடை யோனெந்தை","கைதொழமெய்ஞ் ஞானஞ்சொல் ...... கதிர்வேலா","கொல்லைமிசை வாழ்கின்ற வள்ளிபுன மேசென்று","கொள்ளைகொளு மாரன்கை ...... யலராலே","கொய்துதழை யேகொண்டு செல்லுமழ வாகந்த","கொல்லிமலை மேனின்ற ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks for the state that stands as the shoreless ocean of one's own nature and bliss, which is the ancient first, itself one, then the two, the three gunas, the pure four Vedas, the fierce five senses, the six limbs of the Veda, the many sounds, the bonds of soul and pasa, abundant Tamil become music, and all the many lives. The praise names Krishna whose flute melts stones and calls the cattle in distress, Siva who worshipped Murugan as he told the true knowledge, and Murugan who went to Valli's millet field, plundered by Kama's flower arrows, carrying leaf garments, at the Kolli hills.","n":"The plea is a counting ladder (ஒன்று, இரு பேதங்கள், குண மூ, சதுர் வேதங்கள், புலன் ஓர் ஐந்து, ஆறு அங்கம்) whose end is சொருபாநந்த பெளவம், the sea of the bliss of one's own form, which is all of them and beyond them. பசு பாசங்கள் names two of the three Siddhanta categories. In the praise கைதொழ மெய்ஞ்ஞானஞ்சொல் is the Siva-teaching story; கையன் மிசை யேறும்பன் is unclear to me.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["viyaabagam","aanandham","guru","pasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:60"},"p:608":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":608,"t":["மாகசஞ் சாரமுகில் தோற்ற குழல்கொடு","போகஇந்த் ராதிசிலை தோற்ற நுதல்கொடு","மானவண் டேறுகணை தோற்ற விழிகொடு ...... கண்டுபோல","மாலர்கொண் டாடுகனி தோற்ற இதழ்கொடு","சோலைசென் று஡துகுயில் தோற்ற இசைகொடு","வார்பொரும் பாரமலை தோற்ற முலைகொடு ...... மன்றுளாடி","சீகரம் பேணுதுடி தோற்ற இடைகொடு","போகபண் டாரபணி தோற்ற அரைகொடு","தேனுகுஞ் சீர்கதலி தோற்ற தொடைகொடு ...... வந்துகாசு","தேடுகின் றாரொடுமெய் தூர்த்த னெனவுற","வாடுகின் றேனைமல நீக்கி யொளிதரு","சீவனொன் றானபர மார்த்த தெரிசனை ...... வந்துதாராய்","வேகமுண் டாகியுமை சாற்று மளவினில்","மாமகங் கூருமது தீர்க்க வடிவுடை","வீரனென் பானொருப ராக்ர னெனவர ...... அன்றுசோமன்","மேனியுந் தேயகதிர் தோற்ற எயிறுக","ஆனுகுந் தீகையற சேட்ட விதிதலை","வீழநன் பாரதியு மூக்கு நழுவிட ...... வந்தமாயன்","ஏகநின் றாகியமர் தோற்று வதறிட","வேகவுங் காரமொடு ஆர்க்க அலகைகள்","ஏறிவென் றாடுகள நீக்கி முநிவரர் ...... வந்துசேயென்","றீசநண் பானபுரு ஷார்த்த தெரிசனை","தாவெனுங் கேள்விநெறி கீர்த்தி மருவிய","ராசகெம் பீரவள நாட்டு மலைவளர் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet catalogues women whose hair beats the cloud, brow Indra's bow, eyes the arrow, lips fruit, voice the cuckoo, breasts the mountain, waist the drum, hips the serpent's hood and thighs the plantain, who dance in public and come seeking coins, and says he wastes away with them, called a rogue; he asks Murugan to remove his impurity and give the vision of the supreme truth, light-giving, in which the soul becomes one. The praise tells how, when Uma spoke in haste, a hero came to end the great sacrifice: the moon's body was worn away, the sun's teeth knocked out, Agni's hand cut off, Brahma's head fell, Saraswati's nose slipped and Mayan was defeated, while ghouls danced; then the sages called Murugan son and asked for the vision.","n":"Every line of the confession is built on தோற்ற, 'defeated': each limb outdoes its standard comparison, and the list ends in காசு தேடுகின்றார், women seeking coin. The plea's சீவன் ஒன்றான பரமார்த்த தெரிசனை is union, the soul become one. The sacrifice story (மாமகம், உமை, the punished gods) is Daksha's, with Veerabhadra as the hero (வீரன்); its last lines (முநிவரர் வந்து சேயென்று ஈசநண்பான புருஷார்த்த தெரிசனை தா) are hard to construe.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["varaivinmagalir","malam","saayujjiyam","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:61"},"p:609":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":609,"t":["சூதுகொலை கார ராசைபண மாதர்","தூவையர்கள் சோகை ...... முகநீலர்","சூலைவலி வாத மோடளைவர் பாவர்","தூமையர்கள் கோளர் ...... தெருவூடே","சாதனைகள் பேசி வாருமென நாழி","தாழிவிலை கூறி ...... தெனவோதி","சாயவெகு மாய தூளியுற வாக","தாடியிடு வோர்க ...... ளுறவாமோ","வேதமுநி வோர்கள் பாலகர்கள் மாதர்","வேதியர்கள் பூச ...... லெனஏகி","வீறசுரர் பாறி வீழஅலை யேழு","வேலையள றாக ...... விடும்வேலா","நாதரிட மேவு மாதுசிவ காமி","நாரியபி ராமி ...... யருள்பாலா","நாரண சுவாமி யீனுமக ளோடு","ஞானமலை மேவு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet lists gamblers and killers and women greedy for money, filthy, pallid and blue-faced, aching with colic and rheumatism, sinful and slanderous, who boast in the street, call 'come', name their price by the measure and the pot and jut their chins in pride, and asks whether relationship with them can be right. The praise names the vel that dried the seven seas to mire while Vedic sages, children, women and Brahmins cried out, the son of Sivakami and Abhirami, and Murugan at Gnanamalai with the daughter of Narayana.","n":"சூது கொலைகாரர் ஆசைபண மாதர் sets gamblers, killers and money-loving women in one list, and the women's diseases (சோகை, சூலை, வாதம்) are named as warning. நாழி தாழி விலை கூறி: they price themselves like grain or a pot of goods. நாரண சுவாமி ஈனும் மகள் is the tradition that Murugan's consorts are Vishnu's daughters.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","soodhu","noy"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:62"},"p:610":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":610,"t":["மனையவள் நகைக்க வூரி னனைவரு நகைக்க லோக","மகளிரு நகைக்க தாதை ...... தமரோடும்","மனமது சலிப்ப நாய னுளமது சலிப்ப யாரும்","வசைமொழி பிதற்றி நாளு ...... மடியேனை","அனைவரு மிழிப்ப நாடு மனவிருள் மிகுத்து நாடி","னகமதை யெடுத்த சேம ...... மிதுவோவென்","றடியனு நினைத்து நாளு முடலுயிர் விடுத்த போது","மணுகிமு னளித்த பாத ...... மருள்வாயே","தனதன தனத்த தான எனமுர சொலிப்ப வீணை","தமருக மறைக்கு ழாமு ...... மலைமோதத்","தடிநிக ரயிற்க டாவி யசுரர்க ளிறக்கு மாறு","சமரிடை விடுத்த சோதி ...... முருகோனே","எனைமன முருக்கி யோக அநுபுதி யளித்த பாத","எழுதரிய பச்சை மேனி ...... யுமைபாலா","இமையவர் துதிப்ப ஞான மலையுறை குறத்தி பாக","இலகிய சசிப்பெண் மேவு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes how his wife, the town and the world's women laugh at him, his father and kin are troubled, and everyone babbles abuse, and how his mind's darkness grows until he asks 'is this the welfare I gained by taking a body?'; he asks that when his life leaves the body Murugan come near and give the feet he gave before. The praise names the vel that blazed in battle against the demons with drums, veena and Vedas sounding, the feet that melted his mind and gave the experience of yoga, Uma's son, and Murugan at Gnanamalai with the hunter girl and Indra's daughter.","n":"The shame is told as an ever-widening circle: wife, town, women, father, kin, everyone (அனைவரும் இழிப்ப). முன் அளித்த பாதம் and எனை மனம் உருக்கி யோக அநுபுதி அளித்த பாத point back to a gift of the feet already received, which tradition places at Gnanamalai. நாயன் உளமது சலிப்ப, 'the lord's heart troubled', is unclear: the lord may be the poet's master or the Lord himself.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvadi","pazhi","saakkaadu","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:62"},"p:611":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":611,"t":["ஆதிமக மாயி யம்பை தேவிசிவ னார்ம கிழ்ந்த","ஆவுடைய மாது தந்த ...... குமரேசா","ஆதரவ தாய்வ ருந்தி யாதியரு ணேச ரென்று","ஆளுமுனை யேவ ணங்க ...... அருள்வாயே","பூதமது வான வைந்து பேதமிட வேய லைந்து","பூரணசி வாக மங்க ...... ளறியாதே","பூணுமுலை மாதர் தங்கள் ஆசைவகை யேநி னைந்து","போகமுற வேவி ரும்பி ...... மடியேனை","நீதயவ தாயி ரங்கி நேசவரு ளேபு ரிந்து","நீதிநெறி யேவி ளங்க ...... வுபதேச","நேர்மைசிவ னார்தி கழ்ந்த காதிலுரை வேத மந்த்ர","நீலமயி லேறி வந்த ...... வடிவேலா","ஓதுமறை யாக மஞ்சொல் யோகமது வேபு ரிந்து","ஊழியுணர் வார்கள் தங்கள் ...... வினைதீர","ஊனுமுயி ராய்வ ளர்ந்து ஓசையுடன் வாழ்வு தந்த","ஊதிமலை மீது கந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks to be able to worship Murugan, the son given by the primal Mahamayi Ambai who delighted Siva, as the Lord of grace; he confesses that he has wandered through the five elements without knowing the full Siva Agamas, thinking only of the desires of women and wanting pleasure, and asks Murugan to pity him so that the path of right may shine. The praise names Murugan as the one who spoke the Vedic mantra into Siva's ear and came on the blue peacock, and who, to end the karma of those who practise the yoga taught by Veda and Agama, grew as their flesh and life and gave them life with sound, at Oothimalai.","n":"The fault named is ignorance of the Agamas (பூரண சிவாகமங்கள் அறியாதே) as much as lust. சிவனார் திகழ்ந்த காதில் உரை வேத மந்த்ர is the Siva-teaching story as a whisper in the ear. ஊனும் உயிராய் வளர்ந்து ஓசையுடன் வாழ்வு தந்த may play on the shrine's name (ஊதி, 'blowing'): the Lord as the body, life and sound of the yogi; the sense is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aagamam","kaamam","guru","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:63"},"p:612":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":612,"t":["கோதி முடித்துக் கனத்த கொண்டையர்","சூது விதத்துக் கிதத்து மங்கையர்","கூடிய அற்பச் சுகத்தை நெஞ்சினில் ...... நினையாதே","கோழை மனத்தைக் கெடுத்து வன்புல","ஞான குணத்தைக் கொடுத்து நின்செயல்","கூறு மிடத்துக் கிதத்து நின்றருள் ...... புரிவாயே","நாத நிலைக்குட் கருத்து கந்தருள்","போதக மற்றெச் சகத்தை யுந்தரு","நான்முக னுக்குக் கிளத்து தந்தையின் ...... மருகோனே","நாடு மகத்தெற் கிடுக்கண் வந்தது","தீரிடு தற்குப் பதத்தை யுந்தரு","நாயகர் புத்ரக் குருக்க ளென்றருள் ...... வடிவேலா","தோதிமி தித்தித் திமித்த டிங்குகு","டீகுகு டிக்குட் டிகுக்கு டிண்டிமி","தோதிமி தித்தித் தனத்த தந்தவெ ...... னிசையோடே","சூழ நடித்துச் சடத்தில் நின்றுயி","ரான துறத்தற் கிரக்க முஞ்சுப","சோபன முய்க்கக் கருத்தும் வந்தருள் ...... புரிவோனே","ஓத வெழுத்துக் கடக்க முஞ்சிவ","காரண பத்தர்க் கிரக்க முந்தகு","ஓமெ னெழுத்துக் குயிர்ப்பு மென்சுட ...... ரொளியோனே","ஓதி யிணர்த்திக் குகைக்கி டுங்கன","காபர ணத்திற் பொருட் பயன்றரு","ஊதி கிரிக்குட் கருத்து கந்தருள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks not to think in his heart of the petty pleasure of women with combed, heavy hair buns, deceitful and charming, but for Murugan to destroy his weak mind, give firm knowledge and stand gracious where he tells Murugan's deeds. The praise names Murugan nephew of Vishnu, the father of four-faced Brahma; the guru son of the Lord; the dancer to drum syllables who gives mercy to the soul that leaves the inert body; and the bright light who is the restraint of the recited letters, the mercy to devotees and the breath of the letter Om, at Oothi hill.","n":"கோழை மனத்தைக் கெடுத்து வன்புல ஞான குணத்தைக் கொடுத்து: a weak mind exchanged for a firm knowledge. ஓம் எனும் எழுத்துக்கு உயிர்ப்பும் என் சுடர் ஒளியோனே makes Murugan the breath of Om, and நாத நிலைக்குள் the state of nada. The lines on the trouble at the sacrifice (நாடு மகத்து எற்கு இடுக்கண்) and on the soul leaving the body (சடத்தில் நின்று உயிரான துறத்தற்கு) are hard to construe.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","gnaanam","piranavam","naadham"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:63"},"p:613":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":613,"t":["கருடன் மிசைவரு கரிய புயலென","கமல மணியென ...... வுலகோரைக்","கதறி யவர்பெயர் செருகி மனமது","கருதி முதுமொழி ...... களைநாடித்","திருடி யொருபடி நெருடி யறிவிலர்","செவியில் நுழைவன ...... கவிபாடித்","திரியு மவர்சில புலவர் மொழிவது","சிறிது முணர்வகை ...... யறியேனே","வருடை யினமது முருடு படுமகில்","மரமு மருதமு ...... மடிசாய","மதுர மெனுநதி பெருகி யிருகரை","வழிய வகைவகை ...... குதிபாயுங்","குருடி மலையுறை முருக குலவட","குவடு தவிடெழ ...... மயிலேறுங்","குமர குருபர திமிர தினகர","குறைவி லிமையவர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes poets who cry out praising worldly men as the dark cloud riding on Garuda or a lotus jewel, slip their names into their verses, hunt through old sayings, steal and knead a measure of them, and sing poems that enter only the ears of the ignorant, and says he does not know how to grasp even a little of what such poets say. The praise pictures the river called Madhuram flooding both banks and toppling agil and marutham trees where wild goats graze, and names Murugan of Kurudimalai who rides the peacock to grind the northern peak to chaff, Kumara the guru, the sun to darkness.","n":"The target is the patron-poet's trade: praising a man as Vishnu (கருடன் மிசைவரு கரிய புயல்), inserting his name (அவர்பெயர் செருகி), and stealing from older poems (திருடி ஒருபடி நெருடி). Whether 'I do not know their ways' is irony or confession is not said; the song has no explicit plea, which is unusual.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iravu","kalavu","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:64"},"p:614":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":614,"t":["எங்கேனு மொருவர்வர அங்கேக ணினிதுகொடு","இங்கேவ ருனதுமயல் ...... தரியாரென்","றிந்தாவெ னினியஇதழ் தந்தேனை யுறமருவ","என்றாசை குழையவிழி ...... யிணையாடித்","தங்காம லவருடைய வுண்டான பொருளுயிர்கள்","சந்தேக மறவெபறி ...... கொளுமானார்","சங்கீத கலவிநல மென்றோது முததிவிட","தண்பாரு முனதருளை ...... யருள்வாயே","சங்கோடு திகிரியது கொண்டேயு நிரைபிறகு","சந்தாரும் வெதிருகுழ ...... லதுவூதித்","தன்காதல் தனையுகள என்றேழு மடவியர்கள்","தங்கூறை கொடுமரமி ...... லதுவேறுஞ்","சிங்கார அரிமருக பங்கேரு கனுமருள","சென்றேயும் அமரருடை ...... சிறைமீளச்","செண்டாடி அசுரர்களை ஒன்றாக அடியர்தொழு","தென்சேரி கிரியில்வரு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet gives the women's own words, offered to whoever comes, 'here, the one who cannot bear your passion', 'here, take my sweet lips', with eyes dancing and desire melting, and says they take every possession and life without hesitation; he asks for the cool grace that frees him from the sea they call the good of music and lovemaking. The praise names Krishna who took conch and discus, followed the cattle playing the bamboo flute and climbed the tree with the clothes of the women, Brahma bewildered, the gods freed from prison and the demons beaten, at Thenserigiri.","n":"As in 21, the courtesans' speech is quoted (இங்கே வருக, இந்தா), and the charge is that they strip a man of wealth and life alike (பொருள் உயிர்கள் ... பறிகொளும்). சங்கீத கலவி நலம் என்று ஓதும் உததி: the ocean they call the delight of song and love.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:65"},"p:615":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":615,"t":["கொண்டாடிக் கொஞ்சு மொழிகோடு","கண்டாரைச் சிந்து விழிகொடு","கொந்தாரச் சென்ற குழல்கொடு ...... வடமேருக்","குன்றோடொப் பென்ற முலைகொடு","நின்றோலக் கஞ்செய் நிலைகொடு","கொம்பாயெய்ப் புண்ட விடைகொடு ...... பலரோடும்","பண்டாடச் சிங்கி யிடுமவர்","விண்டாலிக் கின்ற மயிலன","பண்பாலிட் டஞ்செல் மருளது ...... விடுமாறு","பண்டேசொற் றந்த பழமறை","கொண்டேதர்க் கங்க ளறவுமை","பங்காளர்க் கன்று பகர்பொருள் ...... அருள்வாயே","வண்டாடத் தென்றல் தடமிசை","தண்டாதப் புண்ட ரிகமலர்","மங்காமற் சென்று மதுவைசெய் ...... வயலு஡ரா","வன்காளக் கொண்டல் வடிவொரு","சங்க்ராமக் கஞ்சன் விழவுதை","மன்றாடிக் கன்பு தருதிரு ...... மருகோனே","திண்டாடச் சிந்து நிசிசரர்","தொண்டாடக் கண்ட வமர்பொரு","செஞ்சேவற் செங்கை யுடையசண் ...... முகதேவே","சிங்காரச் செம்பொன் மதிளத","லங்காரச் சந்த்ர கலைதவழ்","தென்சேரிக் குன்றி லினிதுறை ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet lists women's cajoling speech, eyes, hair heavy with flowers, breasts like Meru, the posture of their display and slender waists, as they dance with many, like peacocks, and asks Murugan, to free him from the delusion of desire for them, to grant the meaning he told Siva of old, from the ancient Veda, ending all arguments. The praise names the lord of Vayalur's fields, nephew of the cloud-dark one who kicked Kamsa, the six-faced god holding the red cock, at Thenseri hill with its golden walls touched by the moon.","n":"மருள், delusion, is what the meaning cures, and the meaning is the one Murugan told உமை பங்காளர், Siva, the Siva-teaching story. தர்க்கங்கள் அற: the knowledge ends disputation. மன்றாடிக்கு அன்பு தரு may mean the one dear to the dancer in the hall, Siva, and is unsure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["kaamam","mayal","guru","vedham"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:65"},"p:616":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":616,"t":["ஐங்கரனை யொத்தமன மைம்புலம கற்றிவள","ரந்திபக லற்றநினை ...... வருள்வாயே","அம்புவித னக்குள்வளர் செந்தமிழ்வ ழுத்தியுனை","அன்பொடுது திக்கமன ...... மருள்வாயே","தங்கியத வத்துணர்வு தந்தடிமை முத்திபெற","சந்திரவெ ளிக்குவழி ...... யருள்வாயே","தண்டிகைக னப்பவுசு எண்டிசைம திக்கவளர்","சம்ப்ரமவி தத்துடனெ ...... யருள்வாயே","மங்கையர்சு கத்தைவெகு இங்கிதமெ னுற்றமன","முன்றனைநி னைத்தமைய ...... அருள்வாயே","மண்டலிக ரப்பகலும் வந்தசுப ரட்சைபுரி","வந்தணைய புத்தியினை ...... யருள்வாயே","கொங்கிலுயிர் பெற்றுவளர் தென்கரையி லப்பரருள்","கொண்டுஉட லுற்றபொரு ...... ளருள்வாயே","குஞ்சரமு கற்கிளைய கந்தனென வெற்றிபெறு","கொங்கணகி ரிக்குள்வளர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The song is a string of prayers, each ending 'grant': a mind like Ganapati's that has driven off the five senses and a thought beyond night and day; a mind to praise Murugan with love in the Tamil that grows on earth; the knowledge of austerity, so that as a servant he gains release and a way to the moon-space; a grand palanquin and splendour esteemed in the eight directions; a mind that turns from the pleasure of women to think of Murugan; and the meaning that a father gained by grace on the southern bank in Kongu. It ends with Murugan, younger brother of the elephant-faced one, at Konganagiri.","n":"A rare song with no confession and no myth, only petitions (அருள்வாயே eight times). It asks for both release (அடிமை முத்திபெற, சந்திர வெளிக்கு வழி, the moon-space of the yogi) and a palanquin with worldly honour (தண்டிகை கனப் பவுசு), without seeing a conflict. கொங்கில் உயிர் பெற்று வளர் தென்கரையில் அப்பர் அருள் may allude to a Kongu miracle of restoring life; I cannot identify it, and the மண்டலிகர் line is also unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","thamizh","thavam","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:66"},"p:617":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":617,"t":["பாட்டி லுருகிலை கேட்டு முருகிலை","கூற்று வருவழி பார்த்து முருகிலை","பாட்டை யநுதின மேற்று மறிகிலை ...... தினமானம்","யாப்ப ணியனருள் வீட்டை விழைகிலை","நாக்கி னுனிகொடு ஏத்த அறிகிலை","பாழ்த்த பிறவியி லேற்ற மனதுநல் ...... வழிபோக","மாட்ட மெனுகிறை கூட்டை விடுகிலை","யேட்டின் விதிவழி யோட்ட மறிகிலை","பார்த்து மினியொரு வார்த்தை அறைகுவ ...... னிதுகேளாய்","வாக்கு முனதுள நோக்கு மருளுவ","னேத்த புகழடி யார்க்கு மெளியனை","வாழ்த்த இருவினை நீக்கு முருகனை ...... மருவாயோ","ஆட்டி வடவரை வாட்டி யரவொடு","பூட்டி திரிபுர மூட்டி மறலியி","னாட்ட மறசர ணீட்டி மதனுடல் ...... திருநீறாய்","ஆக்கி மகமதை வீட்டி யொருவனை","யாட்டின் முகமதை நாட்டி மறைமக","ளார்க்கும் வடுவுற வாட்டு முமையவ ...... னருள்பாலா","சீட்டை யெழுதிவை யாற்றி லெதிருற","ஓட்டி யழல்பசை காட்டி சமணரை","சீற்ற மொடுகழு வேற்ற அருளிய ...... குருநாதா","தீர்த்த எனதக மேட்டை யுடனினை","ஏத்த அருளுட னோக்கி அருளுதி","தீர்த்த மலைநகர் காத்த சசிமகள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"Addressing his own mind, the poet says it does not melt at song, does not melt on hearing, does not melt watching the road by which Death comes, does not desire the release that is the Lord's grace, cannot praise with the tip of the tongue, and will not leave the cage of the body that refuses the good path or see the course of fate written on the leaf; then he says, 'Hear one word: will you not cling to Murugan, who is easy for devotees and removes the two karmas of those who praise him?' The praise names Siva who bent the northern mountain with the serpent for a string, burned the three cities, kicked Death, burned Kama to ash, ruined the sacrifice and set a goat's head on its lord, and Murugan as the guru who sent the palm leaf against the river, showed it unburnt in fire and impaled the angry Jains, at Theerthamalai.","n":"The refrain of negatives (உருகிலை, அறிகிலை, விழைகிலை, விடுகிலை) is spoken to the mind as a separate party, the நெஞ்சு of love poetry turned to devotion. ஏட்டின் விதி, fate written on the leaf, is set beside Death's road. The praise names Sambandar's ordeals at Madurai: சீட்டை எழுதி வை யாற்றில் எதிருற ஓட்டி, அழல் பசை காட்டி, the leaf that swam upstream and did not burn.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","kootru","vinai","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:67"},"p:618":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":618,"t":["அரிவையர்கள் தொடரு மின்பத்","துலகுநெறி மிகம ருண்டிட்","டசடனென மனது நொந்திட் ...... டயராமல்","அநுதினமு முவகை மிஞ்சிச்","சுகநெறியை விழைவு கொண்டிட்","டவநெறியின் விழையு மொன்றைத் ...... தவிர்வேனோ","பரிதிமதி நிறைய நின்றஃ","தெனவொளிரு முனது துங்கப்","படிவமுக மவைகள் கண்டுற் ...... றகமேவும்","படர்கள்முழு வதும கன்றுட்","பரிவினொடு துதிபு கன்றெற்","பதயுகள மிசைவ ணங்கற் ...... கருள்வாயே","செருவிலகு மசுரர் மங்கக்","குலகிரிகள் நடுந டுங்கச்","சிலுசிலென வலைகு லுங்கத் ...... திடமான","செயமுதவு மலர்பொ ருங்கைத்","தலமிலகு மயில்கொ ளுஞ்சத்","தியைவிடுதல் புரியு முன்பிற் ...... குழகோனே","கருணைபொழி கிருபை முந்தப்","பரிவினொடு கவுரி கொஞ்சக்","கலகலென வருக டம்பத் ...... திருமார்பா","கரிமுகவர் தமைய னென்றுற்","றிடுமிளைய குமர பண்பிற்","கநககிரி யிலகு கந்தப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether he will give up the one thing he longs for on the wrong path, the world's pleasure that follows women and bewilders him until his mind aches as a fool's, and asks that, seeing Murugan's exalted faces shining as if sun and moon stood full together, all his inner sorrows go and he praise with love and bow at the pair of feet. The praise names the Shakti vel that made the demons fade, the clan mountains shake and the seas tremble, Murugan coming with jingling ornaments while Gauri fondles him, the kadamba-chested younger brother of the elephant-faced god, at Kanagagiri.","n":"The cure asked for is a sight: பரிதி மதி நிறைய நின்றஃதென ஒளிரும் உனது படிவ முகம், the faces bright as sun and moon together. கருணை பொழி கிருபை முந்தப் பரிவினொடு கவுரி கொஞ்ச: the praise includes a tender scene of the mother fondling the child.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","thiruvadi","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:68"},"p:619":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":619,"t":["மருவுமலர் வாச முறுகுழலி னாலும்","வரிவிழியி னாலு ...... மதியாலும்","மலையினிக ரான இளமுலைக ளாலு","மயல்கள்தரு மாதர் ...... வகையாலும்","கருதுபொரு ளாலு மனைவிமக வான","கடலலையில் மூழ்கி ...... அலைவேனோ","கமலபத வாழ்வு தரமயிலின் மீது","கருணையுட னேமுன் ...... வரவேணும்","அருமறைக ளோது பிரமன்முதல் மாலும்","அமரர்முநி ராசர் ...... தொழுவோனே","அகிலதல மோது நதிமருவு சோலை","அழகுபெறு போக ...... வளநாடா","பொருதவரு சூரர் கிரியுருவ வாரி","புனல்சுவற வேலை ...... யெறிவோனே","புகலரிய தான தமிழ்முநிவ ரோது","புகழிமலை மேவு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether, through women's fragrant hair, lined eyes, faces and young breasts, through the possessions he thinks of, and through wife and children, he will sink and wander in the waves of the sea; he asks Murugan to come before him on the peacock with compassion and give the life of the lotus feet. The praise names Murugan worshipped by Brahma, Vishnu, gods and sages, lord of the Bhoga land with its rivers and groves, who threw the vel that pierced Sura's mountain and dried the sea, at Pugazhimalai, praised by the Tamil sage.","n":"The list moves from women to wealth (கருது பொருள்) to wife and children (மனைவி மகவு) as one sea, so that household life and courtesans are the same entanglement. தமிழ் முநிவர், the Tamil sage, is probably Agastya.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pattru","kaamam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:69"},"p:620":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":620,"t":["மாந்தளிர்கள் போல வேய்ந்தவுடல் மாதர்","வாந்தவிய மாக ...... முறைபேசி","வாஞ்சைபெரு மோக சாந்திதர நாடி","வாழ்ந்தமனை தேடி ...... உறவாடி","ஏந்துமுலை மீது சாந்துபல பூசி","ஏங்குமிடை வாட ...... விளையாடி","ஈங்கிசைகள் மேவ லாஞ்சனையி லாமல்","ஏய்ந்தவிலை மாதர் ...... உறவாமோ","பாந்தண்முடி மீது தாந்ததிமி தோதி","தாஞ்செகண சேசெ ...... எனவோசை","பாங்குபெறு தாள மேங்கநட மாடு","பாண்டவர்ச காயன் ...... மருகோனே","பூந்தளிர்கள் வீறு வேங்கைகள்ப லாசு","பூங்கதலி கோடி ...... திகழ்சோலை","பூந்தடமு லாவு கோம்பைகள்கு லாவு","பூம்பறையின் மேவு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women with bodies soft as mango shoots who talk of kinship, look for houses where men live well, make friends, smear sandal on their breasts and play until their waists ache, and asks whether a shameless relationship with such price-women can be right. The praise names Krishna who danced to rhythm on the serpent's heads and helped the Pandavas, Murugan his nephew, and the groves of vengai, palasa and plantain and the flowering tanks of Poomparai.","n":"விலைமாதர், price-women, is the plainest name; முறை பேசி, talking of kinship, is their false familiarity, and வாழ்ந்த மனை தேடி, seeking out prosperous houses, their motive. பாந்தள் முடிமீது ... நடமாடு is Krishna's dance on Kaliya.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:70"},"p:621":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":621,"t":["அனங்க னம்பொன் றஞ்சுந் தங்குங் ...... கண்களாலே","அடர்ந்தெ ழும்பொன் குன்றங் கும்பங் ...... கொங்கையாலே","முனிந்து மன்றங் கண்டுந் தண்டும் ...... பெண்களாலே","முடங்கு மென்றன் தொண்டுங் கண்டின் ...... றின்புறாதோ","தெனந்தெ னந்தெந் தெந்தெந் தெந்தெந் ...... தெந்தெனானா","செறிந்த டர்ந்துஞ் சென்றும் பண்பின் ...... தும்பிபாடக்","குனிந்தி லங்குங் கொம்புங் கொந்துந் ...... துன்றுசோலை","கொழுங்கொ டுந்திண் குன்றந் தங்குந் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says that through eyes where Kama's five arrows dwell, through breasts like golden hills and pots, and through women who grow angry and exact payment in public, his service is crippled, and asks whether Murugan will not see it and be glad today. The praise hears the bees humming in the dense groves and names the Lord who dwells on the rich, steep hill of Kodunkundram.","n":"முடங்கும் என்றன் தொண்டு: service is not abandoned but bent and crippled, and the plea is only that the Lord look at it. மன்றம் கண்டும் தண்டும் பெண்கள் may mean women who take a man to the public hall and exact money; the reading is unsure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","thondu","varaivinmagalir"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:71"},"p:622":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":622,"t":["எதிர்பொருது கவிகடின கச்சுக்க ளும்பொருது","குத்தித் திறந்துமலை","யிவைகளென வதிம்ருகம தப்பட்டு நின்றொழுகி","முத்துச் செறிந்தவட","மெனுநிகள மவையறவு தைத்திட்ட ணைந்துகிரி","னிற்கொத்து மங்குசநெ ...... ருங்குபாகர்","எதிர்பரவ உரமிசைது கைத்துக்கி டந்துடல்ப","தைக்கக்க டிந்துமிக","இரதிபதி மணிமவுலி யெற்றித்ரி யம்பகனு","முட்கத்தி ரண்டிளகி","யிளைஞருயிர் கவளமென மட்டித்த சைந்தெதிர்பு","டைத்துச்சி னந்துபொரு ...... கொங்கையானை","பொதுவில்விலை யிடுமகளிர் பத்மக்க ரந்தழுவி","யொக்கத்து வண்டமளி","புகஇணைய வரிபரவு நச்சுக்க ருங்கயல்கள்","செக்கச்சி வந்தமுது","பொதியுமொழி பதறஅள கக்கற்றை யுங்குலைய","முத்தத்து டன்கருணை ...... தந்துமேல்வீழ்","புதுமைதரு கலவிவலை யிற்பட்ட ழுந்தியுயிர்","தட்டுப்ப டுந்திமிர","புணரியுத தியில்மறுகி மட்டற்ற இந்திரிய","சட்டைக்கு ரம்பையழி","பொழுதினிலும் அருள்முருக சுத்தக்கொ டுங்கிரியி","னிர்த்தச் சரண்களைம ...... றந்திடேனே","திதிதிதிதி திதிதிதிதி தித்தித்தி திந்திதிதி","தத்தத்த தந்ததத","தெதததெத தெதததெத தெத்தெத்த தெந்ததெத","திக்கட்டி கண்டிகட","ஜெகணகெண கெணஜெகுத தெத்தித்ரி யந்திரித","தக்கத்த குந்தகுர்த ...... திந்திதீதோ","திகுடதிகு தொகுடதொகு திக்கட்டி கண்டிகட","டக்கட்ட கண்டகட","டிடிடுடுடு டிடிடுடுடு டிக்கட்டி கண்டிகட","டுட்டுட்டு டுண்டுடுடு","திகுகுதிகு திகுகுகுகு திக்குத்தி குந்திகுகு","குக்குக்கு குங்குகுகு ...... என்றுதாளம்","முதிர்திமிலை கரடிகையி டக்கைக்கொ டுந்துடியு","டுக்கைப்பெ ரும்பதலை","முழவுபல மொகுமொகென வொத்திக்கொ டும்பிரம","கத்திக்க ளும்பரவ","முகடுபுகு வெகுகொடிகள் பக்கத்தெ ழுந்தலைய","மிக்கக்க வந்தநிரை ...... தங்கியாட","முதுகழுகு கொடிகருட னொக்கத்தி ரண்டுவர","வுக்ரப்பெ ருங்குருதி","முழுகியெழு பயிரவர்ந டித்திட்ட கண்டமும்வே","டிக்கத்து ணிந்ததிர","முடுகிவரு நிசிசரரை முட்டிச்சி ரந்திருகி","வெட்டிக்க ளம்பொருத ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes breasts as rutting war elephants that burst their bodices, run with musk, break the pearl chains that bind them, crush their keepers, shake even Kama and three-eyed Siva and swallow young men's lives; then the women who set a price in public, their embraces, poisonous carp eyes and kisses, and the net of lovemaking in which he sinks into a dark sea of senses. He vows that even when this hut of the body, a shirt for the senses, perishes, he will not forget Murugan's dancing feet at Kodunkiri. The praise is almost all drum syllables and a battlefield of vultures, Bhairavas bathing in blood and demons beheaded.","n":"கொங்கை யானை, breast-elephant, is sustained for many lines, with chains (நிகளம்), goad (அங்குசம்) and keepers (பாகர்). பொதுவில் விலையிடு மகளிர் are women who set their price publicly. இந்திரிய சட்டைக் குரம்பை, the body as a hut that is the senses' shirt, is the image of what perishes; the plea is not to escape but not to forget (மறந்திடேனே).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","punarchi","yaakkai","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:71"},"p:623":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":623,"t":["அழகெ றிந்த சந்த்ர முகவ டங்க லந்த","அமுத புஞ்ச இன்சொல் ...... மொழியாலே","அடிது வண்ட தண்டை கலிலெ னுஞ்சி லம்பொ","டணிச தங்கை கொஞ்சு ...... நடையாலே","சுழியெ றிந்து நெஞ்சு சுழல நஞ்ச ணைந்து","தொடுமி ரண்டு கண்க ...... ளதனாலே","துணைநெ ருங்கு கொங்கை மருவு கின்ற பெண்கள்","துயரை யென்றொ ழிந்து ...... விடுவேனோ","எழுது கும்ப கன்பி னிளைய தம்பி நம்பி","யெதிர டைந்தி றைஞ்சல் ...... புரிபோதே","இதம கிழ்ந்தி லங்கை யசுர ரந்த தங்க","மொழிய வென்ற கொண்டல் ...... மருகோனே","மழுவு கந்த செங்கை அரனு கந்தி றைஞ்ச","மநுவி யம்பி நின்ற ...... குருநாதா","வளமி குந்த குன்ற நகர்பு ரந்து துங்க","மலைவி ளங்க வந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks when he will leave the sorrow of women whose moon faces, nectar speech, gait with anklets and bells, and two poison eyes that whirl the heart, draw him. The praise names Rama, the cloud-dark one who rejoiced when Kumbhakarna's younger brother came and bowed, and won Lanka's golden city, Murugan as the guru who stood and spoke the mantra while Siva, axe in hand, bowed to him, at the rich town of Kundram.","n":"மழு உகந்த செங்கை அரன் உகந்து இறைஞ்ச மநு இயம்பி நின்ற குருநாதா: the Siva-teaching story with Siva bowing and the teaching called மநு, the mantra. எழுது கும்பகன் பின் இளைய தம்பி is Vibhishana coming over to Rama.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","guru","mandhiram"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:72"},"p:624":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":624,"t":["ககுபநிலை குலையவிகல் மிகுபகடின் வலியுடைய","தந்தத்தி னைத்தடிவ தொந்தத் திரத்தையுள","அகிலமறை புகழ்பரமர் ஞெகிழிகல கலகலெனும்","அம்பொற்ப தத்தர்தநு வம்பொற்பொ ருப்படர்வ","களபபரி மளமெழுகும் எழிலில்முழு குவமுளரி","யஞ்சப் புடைத்தெழுவ வஞ்சக் கருத்துமத ...... னபிஷேகங்","கடிவபடு கொலையிடுவ கொடியமுக படமணிவ","இன்பச் சுடர்க்கனக கும்பத் தரச்செருவ","பிருதில்புள கிதசுகமு மிருதுளமும் வளரிளைஞர்","புந்திக் கிடர்த்தருவ பந்தித் தகச்சடர்வ","கயல்மகர நிகரமிக வியன்மருவு நதியில்முதிர்","சங்கிப்பி முத்தணிவ பொங்கிக் கனத்தொளிர்வ ...... முலைமாதர்","வகுளமலர் குவளையிதழ் தருமணமு மிருகமத","மொன்றிக்க றுத்துமுகில் வென்றிட்டு நெய்த்தகுழல்","அசையருசி யமுர்தக்ருத வசியமொழி மயில்குயிலெ","னும்புட் குரற்பகர வம்புற்ற மற்புரிய","வருமறலி யரணமொடு முடுகுசமர் விழியிணைகள்","கன்றிச் சிவக்கமகிழ் நன்றிச் சமத்துநக ...... நுதிரேகை","வகைவகைமெ யுறவளைகள் கழலவிடை துவளவிதழ்","உண்டுட்ப்ர மிக்கநசை கொண்டுற் றணைத்தவதி","செறிகலவி வலையிலென தறிவுடைய கலைபடுதல்","உந்திப் பிறப்பறநி னைந்திட்ட முற்றுனடி","வயலிநகர் முருகசெரு முயல்பனிரு கரகுமர","துன்றட்ட சிட்டகுண குன்றக் குடிக்கதிப ...... அருளாதோ","தகுகுதகு தகுதகுகு திகுகுதிகு திகுதிகுகு","தங்குத் தகுத்தககு திங்குத் திகுத்திகிகு","சகணசக சகசகண செகணசெக செகசெகெண","சங்கச் சகச்சகண செங்கச்செ கச்செகண","தனனதன தனதனன தெனனதென தெனதெனன","தந்தத்த னத்தனன தெந்தத்தெ னத்தெனன ...... தனனானா","தகுததகு தகுதகுதி திகுதிதிகு திகுதிகுதி","தங்குத் தகுத்தகுகு திங்குத் திகுத்திகுகு","டணணடண டணடணண டிணிணிடிணி டிணிடிணிணி","டண்டட்ட டட்டடண டிண்டிட்டி டிட்டிடிணி","தரரதர தரதரர திரிரிதிரி திரிதிரிரி","தன்றத் தரத்தரர தின்றித் திரித்திரிரி ...... யெனதாளந்","தொகுதிவெகு முரசுகர டிகைடமரு முழவுதவில்","தம்பட்ட மத்தளமி னம்பட் டடக்கைபறை","பதலைபல திமிலைமுத லதிரவுதிர் பெரியதலை","மண்டைத் திரட்பருகு சண்டைத் திரட்கழுகு","துடர்நிபிட கருடனடர் தரகரட மொகுமொகென","வந்துற் றிடக்குடர்நி ணந்துற் றிசைத்ததிர ...... முதுபேய்கள்","சுனகனரி நெறுநெறென வினிதினிது தினவினைசெய்","வெங்குக் குடத்தகொடி துங்குக் குகுக்குகென","வடனமிடு திசைபரவி நடனமிட வடலிரவி","திங்கட்ப்ர பைக்கதிர்கள் மங்கப்ர சித்தகுல","துரககஜ ரதகடக முரணரண நிருதர்விறல்","மிண்டைக் குலைத்தமர்செய் தண்டர்க் குரத்தையருள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"At great length the poet describes women's breasts with elephant tusks, golden pots and pearl-bearing rivers, their dark fragrant hair, their voices of peacock and cuckoo, their eyes like Death's fortress, nail marks, slipping bangles and bitten lips, and says that in the net of lovemaking his knowing mind was wasted; he asks whether Murugan of Vayalur and lord of Kundrakudi will not grant that he think of his feet so that birth ends. The praise is a long run of drum syllables and a battlefield of vultures, ghouls and jackals, where Murugan broke the demons' armies and gave the gods protection.","n":"வருமறலி அரணமொடு முடுகு சமர் விழியிணைகள: the eyes are Death's fortress pressing into battle, a link the poet often draws between lust and Death. என தறிவுடைய கலைபடுதல் is the waste of knowledge in lust; the cure is உந்திப் பிறப்பற நினைந்திட உன் அடி. The image-chains in the first half are hard to divide in places.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","kaamam","piravi","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:72"},"p:625":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":625,"t":["கடினதட கும்ப நேரென வளருமிரு கொங்கை மேல்விழு","கலவிதரு கின்ற மாதரொ ...... டுறவாடிக்","கனவளக பந்தி யாகிய நிழல்தனிலி ருந்து தேனுமிழ்","கனியிதழை மென்று தாடனை ...... செயலாலே","துடியிடைநு டங்க வாள்விழி குழைபொரநி ரம்ப மூடிய","துகில்நெகிழ வண்டு கோகில ...... மயில்காடை","தொனியெழவி ழைந்து கூரிய கொடுநகமி சைந்து தோள்மிசை","துயிலவச இன்ப மேவுத ...... லொழிவேனோ","இடிமுரச றைந்து பூசல்செய் அசுரர்கள்மு றிந்து தூளெழ","எழுகடல்ப யந்து கோவென ...... அதிகோப","எமபடரு மென்செய் வோமென நடுநடுந டுங்க வேல்விடு","இரணமுக சண்ட மாருத ...... மயிலோனே","வடிவுடைய அம்பி காபதி கணபதிசி றந்து வாழ்தட","வயலிநகர் குன்ற மாநக ...... ருறைவோனே","வகைவகைபு கழ்ந்து வாசவன் அரிபிரமர் சந்த்ர சூரியர்","வழிபடுதல் கண்டு வாழ்வருள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether he will give up the pleasure of women whose breasts are like great pots, their lips, their dark hair, their sounds like bees, cuckoos, peacocks and quails, the nail marks and the swoon of union on the shoulder. The praise names Murugan whose vel made the demons break, the seven seas cry out and Yama's servants tremble asking 'what shall we do?', who rides the storm-wind peacock, dwells at Vayalur and Kundram where Ambikapati and Ganapati live, and is worshipped by Indra, Hari, Brahma, sun and moon.","n":"எமபடரும் என் செய்வோம் என நடுநடுநடுங்க: Death's servants, whom the confessions fear, are themselves made to tremble by the vel, the answer to the death-hour plea told as praise.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","kaamam","kootru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:72"},"p:626":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":626,"t":["நேசா சாரா டம்பர மட்டைகள்","பேசா தேயே சுங்கள மட்டைகள்","நீசா ளோடே யும்பழ கிக்கவர் ...... பொருளாலே","நீயே நானே யென்றொரு சத்தியம்","வாய்கூ சாதோ துங்க படத்திகள்","நேரா லேதா னின்றுபி லுக்கிகள் ...... எவர்மேலும்","ஆசா பாசா தொந்தரை யிட்டவர்","மேல்வீழ் வார்பால் சண்டிகள் கட்டழ","காயே மீதோ லெங்கு மினுக்கிகள் ...... வெகுமோகம்","ஆகா தாவே சந்தரு திப்பொழு","தோகோ வாவா வென்று பகட்டிக","ளாகா மோகா வம்பிகள் கிட்டிலு ...... முறவாமோ","பேசா தேபோய் நின்றுமி யிற்றுயிர்","வாயா வாவா வென்று குடித்தருள்","பேரா லேநீள் கஞ்சன் விடுத்தெதிர் ...... வருதூது","பேழ்வாய் வேதா ளம்பக டைப்பகு","வாய்நீள் மானா ளுஞ்சர ளத்தொடு","பேயானாள் போர் வென்றெதி ரிட்டவன் ...... மருகோனே","மாசூ டாடா டும்பகை யைப்பகை","சூரா ளோடே வன்செரு வைச்செறு","மாசூ ராபா ரெங்கும ருட்பொலி ...... முருகோனே","வானா டேழ்நா டும்புகழ் பெற்றிடு","தேனா றேசூழ் துங்க மலைப்பதி","மாயூ ராவாழ் குன்றை தழைத்தருள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet pours out abuse of courtesans: frauds of pretended love and display, foul-mouthed, keeping company with base men for their money, swearing without shame 'you are me and I am you', liars who stand and show off, who torment whoever they cling to, painted beauties who flash their skin and cry 'oho, come, come', and asks whether a relationship with them is fit even if they come near. The praise names Krishna who silently drank Putana's life and defeated the demons Kamsa sent, Murugan who destroyed Sura's host, and Mayura hill, Kundrakudi, ringed by the river Thenaru.","n":"The confession is a torrent of epithets ending in -கள் (மட்டைகள், படத்திகள், பிலுக்கிகள், மினுக்கிகள், பகட்டிகள், வம்பிகள்). நீயே நானே என்றொரு சத்தியம் வாய் கூசாது ஓதும்: the oath of oneness spoken without shame is the centre of the charge. பேயானாள் is Putana.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:72"},"p:627":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":627,"t":["பிறர்புக ழின்சொற் பயிலுமி ளந்தைப்","பருவம தன்கைச் ...... சிலையாலே","பிறவித ருஞ்சிக் கதுபெரு கும்பொய்ப்","பெருவழி செனறக் ...... குணமேவிச்","சிறுமைபொ ருந்திப் பெருமைமு டங்கிச்","செயலும ழிந்தற் ...... பமதான","தெரிவையர் தங்கட் கயலைவி ரும்பிச்","சிலசில பங்கப் ...... படலாமோ","கெறுவித வஞ்சக் கபடமொ டெண்டிக்","கிலுமெதிர் சண்டைக் ...... கெழுசூரன்","கிளையுடன் மங்கத் தலைமுடி சிந்தக்","கிழிபட துன்றிப் ...... பொருதோனே","குறுமுநி யின்பப் பொருள்பெற அன்றுற்","பனமநு வுஞ்சொற் ...... குருநாதா","குலகிரி துங்கக் கிரியுயர் குன்றக்","குடிவளர் கந்தப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether, struck by Kama's bow through young women of sweet flattering speech, he should go the great false way that multiplies the tangle of birth, take on such a nature, shrink, let his greatness bend and his actions fail, and suffer disgraces through desire for the carp eyes of petty women. The praise names Murugan who fought Sura with all his kin until their crowns were scattered, the guru who once spoke the mantra so that the short sage gained the meaning of bliss, at Kundrakudi.","n":"சிறுமை பொருந்திப் பெருமை முடங்கி: lust is told in the Kural's terms, smallness gained and greatness bent. குறுமுநி இன்பப் பொருள் பெற ... மநு உஞ்சொல் குருநாதா is Murugan as Agastya's teacher.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","sirumai","piravi","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:72"},"p:628":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":628,"t":["தவள மதிய மெறிக்குந் ...... தணலாலே","சரச மதனன் விடுக்குங் ...... கணையாலே","கவன மிகவு முரைக்குங் ...... குயிலாலே","கருதி மிகவு மயக்கம் ...... படவோநான்","பவள நிகரு மிதழ்ப்பைங் ...... குறமானின்","பரிய வரையை நிகர்க்குந் ...... தனமேவுந்","திவளு மணிகள் கிடக்குந் ...... திருமார்பா","திகழு மயிலின் மலைக்கண் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A short song: the speaker asks whether he must be so bewildered, thinking ceaselessly, by the fire of the white moon, the arrows of playful Kama and the cuckoo's calling. The praise names the beautiful chest on which lie the breasts of the coral-lipped young deer of the hunters, at the peacock hill.","n":"The love-sickness is told with the stock trio of moon, Kama and cuckoo (cf. 14). Nothing marks the speaker's sex or names women as the cause, and the praise ends on Valli on Murugan's chest, so this may be a lovesick girl's lament for Murugan rather than the poet's confession; the text does not settle it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","mayal"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:72"},"p:629":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":629,"t":["நாமேவு குயிலாலு மாமார னயிலாலு","நாடோ று மதிகாயும் ...... வெயிலாலும்","நார்மாதர் வசையாலும் வேயூது மிசையாலு","நாடாசை தருமோக ...... வலையூடே","ஏமாறி முழுநாளு மாலாகி விருதாவி","லேவாரும் விழிமாதர் ...... துய்ருடே","ஏகாம லழியாத மேலான பதமீதி","லேகீயு னுடன்மேவ ...... அருள்தாராய்","தாமோக முடனு஡று பால்தேடி யுரலு஡டு","தானேறி விளையாடு ...... மொருபோதில்","தாயாக வருசோதை காணாது களவாடு","தாமோத ரன்முராரி ...... மருகோனே","மாமாது வனமாது கார்மேவு சிலைமாது","மாலாகி விளையாடு ...... புயவீரா","வானாடு புகழ்நாடு தேனாறு புடைசூழ","மாயூர கிரிமேவு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says that through the cuckoo, Kama's spear, the moon that burns daily like sunlight, the gossip of women and the flute's music, he has been deceived in the net of desire and is maddened all day, and asks not to go into the sorrow of women with arrow eyes but to be given grace to join Murugan in the high, undying state. The praise names Krishna, who climbed the mortar to steal milk and play while Yasoda did not see, Damodara and Murari, Murugan's shoulders that delight the great lady, the forest lady and the lady of the cloudy hill, at Mayuragiri ringed by the Thenaru.","n":"The love-poetry machinery (குயில், மதி, வேயூது இசை, மாதர் வசை) is the poet's own state here, unlike 628: he names விழிமாதர் and asks for அழியாத மேலான பதம், release. தாமோதரன், 'rope-bellied', is the child Krishna tied to the mortar. The three ladies in the praise are not all identifiable.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["kaamanoy","alar","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:72"},"p:630":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":630,"t":["மைக்க ணிக்கன் வாளி போல வுட்க ளத்தை மாறி நாடி","மட்டு முற்ற கோதை போத ...... முடிசூடி","மத்த கத்தி னீடு கோடு வைத்த தொத்தின் மார்பி னு஡டு","வட்ட மிட்ட வாரு லாவு ...... முலைமீதே","இக்கு வைக்கு மாடை வீழ வெட்கி யக்க மான பேரை","யெத்தி முத்த மாடும் வாயி ...... னிசைபேசி","எட்டு துட்ட மாதர் பாய லிச்சை யுற்றெ னாக மாவி","யெய்த்து நித்த மான வீன ...... முறலாமோ","துர்க்கை பக்க சூல காளி செக்கை புக்க தாள வோசை","தொக்க திக்க தோத தீத ...... வெனவோதச்","சுற்றி வெற்றி யோடு தாள்கள் சுத்த நிர்த்த மாடு மாதி","சொற்கு நிற்கு மாறு தார ...... மொழிவோனே","திக்கு மிக்க வானி னு஡டு புக்க விக்க மூடு சூரர்","திக்க முட்டி யாடு தீர ...... வடிவேலா","செச்சை பிச்சி மாலை மார்ப விச்சை கொச்சை மாதி னோடு","செப்பு வெற்பில் சேய தான ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women whose kohled eyes, like Kama's arrows, change the heart, who wear honeyed garlands, whose breasts move under their bands, who cheat bashful men, kiss and speak sweetly, and asks whether, with desire for the bed of these wicked women, body and life wearing out daily, he should become debased. The praise names the dance of the First One, with Durga beside him and Kali with trident beating time, and Murugan who told the sacred syllable so that it stood by the word, the vel that struck Sura's host, and the son at the mountain with vetchi and jasmine garlands and the hunter girl of rustic speech.","n":"தாரம் is a name of the syllable Om; சொற்கு நிற்குமாறு தாரம் மொழிவோனே seems to make Murugan teach it to the dancing Siva, though the line's syntax is unsure. விச்சை கொச்சை மாது: Valli, whose speech is rustic. செப்பு வெற்பு, if it names the shrine, is Pothigai.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["kaamam","guru","piranavam","thirukkoothu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:73"},"p:631":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":631,"t":["வெடித்த வார்குழல் விரித்து மேல்விழி","விழித்து மேகலை பதித்து வார்தொடு","மிகுத்த மாமுலை யசைத்து நூலின்ம ...... ருங்கினாடை","மினுக்கி யோலைகள் பிலுக்கி யேவளை","துலக்கி யேவிள நகைத்து கீழ்விழி","மிரட்டி யாரையு மழைத்து மால்கொடு ...... தந்தவாய்நீர்","குடித்து நாயென முடக்கு மேல்பிணி","யடுத்த பாதிகள் படுத்த தாய்தமர்","குலத்தர் யாவரு நகைக்க வேயுடல் ...... மங்குவேனைக்","குறித்து நீயரு கழைத்து மாதவர்","கணத்தின் மேவென அளித்து வேல்மயில்","கொடுத்து வேதமு மொருத்த னாமென ...... சிந்தைகூராய்","உடுட்டு டூடுடு டுடுட்டொ டோ வென","திகுத்த தீதிகு திகுர்த்த தாவென","உடுக்கை பேரிகை தவிற்கு ழாமுமி ...... ரங்குபோரில்","உலுத்த நீசர்கள் பதைப்ப மாகரி","துடிப்ப நீள்கட லெரித்து சூர்மலை","யுடைத்து நீதிகள் பரப்பி யேயவ ...... ரும்பராரை","அடைத்த மாசிறை விடுத்து வானுல","களிக்கு மாயிர திருக்க ணானர","சளித்து நாளுமெ னுளத்தி லேமகி ...... ழுங்குமாரா","அளித்த தாதையு மிகுத்த மாமனும்","அனைத்து ளோர்களு மதிக்க வேமகிழ்","அகத்ய மாமுநி பொருப்பின் மேவிய ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women who loosen their hair, stare, shake their girdles and breasts, flash their ear-leaves and bangles, smile and frighten with sidelong eyes, call anyone, give their mouth's water to drink and make a man cringe like a dog; then disease comes, he is half-dead, and mother, kin and clan laugh as his body wastes. He asks Murugan to mark him, call him near, set him among the great ascetics, give him the vel and peacock and make his mind know that the Veda speaks of one. The praise names the battle in which Murugan burned the sea and broke Sura's mountain, freed the gods from prison and made Indra rejoice, esteemed by his father and uncle, at Agastya's mountain.","n":"மாதவர் கணத்தின் மேவென: the plea is for a place among the company of great ascetics, not only for the feet. வேதமும் ஒருத்தன் ஆமென சிந்தை கூராய்: the Vedas speak of one. நாயென முடக்கு, crouching like a dog, is the poet's harshest picture of himself under women.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","noy","adiyaar","pazhi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:73"},"p:632":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":632,"t":["குதலை மொழியினார் நிதிக்கொள் வாரணி","முலையை விலைசெய்வார் தமக்கு மாமயல்","கொடிது கொடிததால் வருத்த மாயுறு ...... துயராலே","மதலை மறுகிவா லிபத்தி லேவெகு","பதகர் கொடியவா ளிடத்தி லேமிக","வறுமை புகல்வதே யெனக்கு மோஇனி ...... முடியாதே","முதல வரிவிலோ டெதிர்த்த சூருடல்","மடிய அயிலையே விடுத்த வாகரு","முகிலை யனையதா நிறத்த மால்திரு ...... மருகோனே","கதலி கமுகுசூழ் வயற்கு ளேயளி","யிசையை முரலமா வறத்தில் மீறிய","கழுகு மலைமகா நகர்க்குள் மேவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says that the infatuation with women of lisping speech who take wealth and sell their breasts has been bitter beyond bitter, and that he, bewildered as a child, can no longer, in his youth, plead his poverty before many base and cruel men. The praise names the cloud-dark nephew of Vishnu who threw the vel so that Sura, who opposed Rama's bow, fell, and Murugan at the great town of Kazhugumalai, rich in virtue, among plantain and areca and fields where bees hum.","n":"The two humiliations are set side by side: money lost to women (நிதிக் கொள்வார், முலையை விலை செய்வார்) and then poverty pleaded to the wicked (பதகர் ... இடத்திலே மிக வறுமை புகல்வதே). இனி முடியாதே, 'no longer can I', is the plea. The praise line on Sura and the bow is hard to construe.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","iravu","inmai","mayal"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:74"},"p:633":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":633,"t":["முலையை மறைத்துத் திறப்ப ராடையை","நெகிழ வுடுத்துப் படுப்பர் வாயிதழ்","முறைமுறை முத்திக் கொடுப்பர் பூமல ...... ரணைமீதே","அலைகுலை யக்கொட் டணைப்ப ராடவர்","மனவலி யைத்தட் டழிப்பர் மால்பெரி","தவர்பொரு ளைக்கைப் பறிப்பர் வேசைக ...... ளுறவாமோ","தலைமுடி பத்துத் தெறித்து ராவண","னுடல்தொளை பட்டுத் துடிக்க வேயொரு","தநுவை வளைத்துத் தொடுத்த வாளியன் ...... மருகோனே","கலைமதி யப்புத் தரித்த வேணிய","ருதவிய வெற்றித் திருக்கை வேலவ","கழுகு மலைக்குட் சிறக்க மேவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes whores who hide and bare their breasts, loosen their clothes and lie down, kiss again and again on beds of flowers, embrace until a man is shaken, destroy men's strength of mind and snatch their wealth from their hands while their passion is great, and asks whether a relationship with them can be right. The praise names Rama who bent the bow so that Ravana's ten crowns flew and his body was pierced, Murugan nephew of that archer, the victorious vel given by Siva who bears the moon and Ganga, at Kazhugumalai.","n":"வேசைகள் is the bluntest name; the sequence runs from seduction (முத்திக் கொடுப்பர்) to the destruction of the man's will (மனவலியைத் தட்டழிப்பர்) to the theft (பொருளைக் கைப் பறிப்பர்). மால் பெரிது: they take it while the man's infatuation is at its height.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","punarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:74"},"p:634":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":634,"t":["கோங்க முநகுயு மெலிய வீங்கு புளக களப","மேந்து குவடு குழையும் ...... படிகாதல்","கூர்ந்து குழையை அமளி தோய்ந்து குலவு மினிய","தேங்கு கலவி யமுதுண் ...... டியல்மாதர்","வாங்கு பகழி விழியை மோந்து பகலு மிரவும்","வாய்ந்த துயிலை மிகவுந் ...... தணியாத","வாஞ்சை யுடைய அடிமை நீண்ட பிறவி யலையை","நீந்தி அமல அடிவந் ...... தடைவேனோ","ஓங்க லனைய பெரிய சோங்கு தகர மகர","மோங்கு ததியின் முழுகும் ...... பொருசூரும்","ஓய்ந்து பிரமன் வெருவ வாய்ந்த குருகு மலையில்","ஊர்ந்து மயில துலவுந் ...... தனிவேலா","வேங்கை யடவி மறவர் ஏங்க வனிதை யுருக","வேங்கை வடிவு மருவுங் ...... குமரேசா","வேண்டு மடியர் புலவர் வேண்ட அரிய பொருளை","வேண்டு மளவி லுதவும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet, a servant with an unending longing for women whose eyes are bent arrows and whose lovemaking is sweet as nectar, sleepless day and night, asks whether he will swim the long wave of birth and reach the pure feet. The praise names Murugan's vel against Sura hiding in the sea of ships and sharks, Brahma afraid, the lone vel-bearer riding the peacock on the heron hill, the Kumara who took the form of a vengai tree to the hunters' grief so that the girl melted, and the Lord who gives devotees and poets the rare thing they ask, as much as they ask.","n":"பிறவி அலையை நீந்தி அமல அடி வந்து அடைவேனோ recalls the Kural's sea of births crossed by the feet. வேங்கை வடிவு மருவும் குமரேசா: in the Valli story Murugan hid as a vengai tree when her kin came; the text names it. குருகு மலை, heron hill, stands for the shrine's own name, the vulture hill.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","piravi","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:74"},"p:635":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":635,"t":["அல்லில் நேருமி ...... னதுதானும்","அல்ல தாகிய ...... உடல்மாயை","கல்லி னேரஅ ...... வழிதோறுங்","கையு நானுமு ...... லையலாமோ","சொல்லி நேர்படு ...... முதுசூரர்","தொய்ய வூர்கெட ...... விடும்வேலா","வல்லி மாரிரு ...... புறமாக","வள்ளி யூருறை ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet calls the body an illusion, like the lightning at night and not even that, and asks whether he and his hand must suffer along every stony road. The praise names the vel that wore down the old Suras and ruined their town, and Murugan dwelling at Valliyur with his two consorts on either side.","n":"அல்லில் நேரும் மின் அதுதானும் அல்லதாகிய உடல் மாயை: the body is less lasting than a flash of lightning at night. The second quatrain (கல்லின் நேர அவ்வழி தோறும் கையும் நானும் உலையலாமோ) may speak of wandering on hard roads or of begging with the hand; I cannot settle it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","maayai","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:75"},"p:636":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":636,"t":["திருமக ளுலாவு மிருபுய முராரி","திருமருக நாமப் ...... பெருமாள்காண்","செகதலமும் வானு மிகுதிபெறு பாடல்","தெரிதரு குமாரப் ...... பெருமாள்காண்","மருவுமடி யார்கள் மனதில்விளை யாடு","மரகதம யூரப் ...... பெருமாள்காண்","மணிதரளம் வீசி யணியருவி சூழ","மருவுகதிர் காமப் ...... பெருமாள்காண்","அருவரைகள் நீறு படஅசுரர் மாள","அமர்பொருத வீரப் ...... பெருமாள்காண்","அரவுபிறை வாரி விரவுசடை வேணி","அமலர்குரு நாதப் ...... பெருமாள்காண்","இருவினையி லாத தருவினைவி டாத","இமையவர்கு லேசப் ...... பெருமாள்காண்","இலகுசிலை வேடர் கொடியினதி பார","இருதனவி நோதப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A song of praise only, each line closing 'behold the Lord' (பெருமாள்காண்): Murugan is the nephew of Murari with Lakshmi on his chest, the Kumara whose songs fill earth and sky, the emerald-peacock lord who plays in the minds of devotees, the lord of Kathirkamam where waterfalls fling gems and pearls, the hero who ground mountains to dust as the demons died, and the guru of the pure Siva with serpent, crescent and Ganga in his hair. It ends with the lord of the gods and the lover of the hunter girl.","n":"The refrain காண் ('see!') makes the song a pointing-out, as if the poet showed the god to the listener line by line. மருவும் அடியார்கள் மனதில் விளையாடு: the devotees' minds are named as one of his playgrounds, beside the hill and the field. இருவினை இலாத ... இமையவர் are the gods free of the two deeds.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["guru","adiyaar","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:76"},"p:637":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":637,"t":["அலகின் மாறு மாறாத கலதி பூத வேதாளி","அடைவில் ஞாளி கோமாளி ...... அறமீயா","அழிவு கோளி நாணாது புழுகு பூசி வாழ்மாதர்","அருளி லாத தோடாய ...... மருளாகிப்","பலக லாக ராமேரு மலைக ராச லாவீசு","பருவ மேக மேதாரு ...... வெனயாதும்","பரிவு றாத மாபாதர் வரிசை பாடி யோயாத","பரிசில் தேடி மாயாத ...... படிபாராய்","இலகு வேலை நீழ்வாடை யெரிகொள் வேலை மாசூரி","லெறியும் வேலை மாறாத ...... திறல்வீரா","இமய மாது பாகீர திநதி பால காசார","லிறைவி கான மால்வேடர் ...... சுதைபாகா","கலக வாரி போல்மோதி வடவை யாறு சூழ்சீத","கதிர காம மூதூரி ...... லிளையோனே","கனக நாடு வீடாய கடவுள் யானை வாழ்வான","கருணை மேரு வேதேவர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet heaps abuse on the shameless, civet-scented women without grace who deluded him, and confesses that he sang the praises of great sinners who show no kindness, calling them masters of the arts, Meru, the rain cloud and the wish-tree, tirelessly seeking gifts; he asks Murugan to look on him so that he does not die so. The praise names the vel thrown into the sea at Sura, Murugan as son of the Himalaya's daughter and of the Ganga and the lake, husband of the hunters' daughter and Devayanai, the young one of the old town Kathirkamam.","n":"The patron's praises are given in his own flattering terms (மேரு, பருவமேகம், தரு), the stock comparisons of court poetry, so the confession is also a poet's shame at his craft misused. The opening string of abuse (கலதி, பூத வேதாளி, ஞாளி, கோமாளி) is hard to divide; நீழ்வாடை in the praise is probably நீள்வாடை.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iravu","pugazh","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:76"},"p:638":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":638,"t":["உடுக்கத் துகில்வேணு நீள்பசி","யவிக்கக் கனபானம் வேணுநல்","ஒளிக்குப் புனலாடை வேணுமெய் ...... யுறுநோயை","ஒழிக்கப் பரிகாரம் வேணுமுள்","இருக்கச் சிறுநாரி வேணுமொர்","படுக்கத் தனிவீடு வேணுமிவ் ...... வகையாவுங்","கிடைத்துக் க்ருஹவாசி யாகிய","மயக்கக் கடலாடி நீடிய","கிளைக்குப் பரிபால னாயுயி ...... ரவமேபோம்","க்ருபைச்சித் தமுஞான போதமு","மழைத்துத் தரவேணு மூழ்பவ","கிரிக்குட் சுழல்வேனை யாளுவ ...... தொருநாளே","குடக்குச் சிலதூதர் தேடுக","வடக்குச் சிலதூதர் நாடுக","குணக்குச் சிலதூதர் தேடுக ...... வெனமேவிக்","குறிப்பிற் குறிகாணு மாருதி","யினித்தெற் கொருதூது போவது","குறிப்பிற் குறிபோன போதிலும் ...... வரலாமோ","அடிக்குத் திரகார ராகிய","அரக்கர்க் கிளையாத தீரனு","மலைக்கப் புறமேவி மாதுறு ...... வனமேசென்","றருட்பொற் றிருவாழி மோதிர","மளித்துற் றவர்மேல் மனோகர","மளித்துக் கதிர்காம மேவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet counts what a householder needs: cloth to wear, rich food for hunger, water and clothes, remedies for the body's sickness, a young woman within and a house to sleep in; getting all this, he bathes in the sea of delusion as protector of a long line of kin and his life goes in vain, and he asks for the day when Murugan calls him, gives him a mind of grace and the awareness of jnana, and rules him as he whirls in the mountain of births. The praise tells at length how the monkeys were sent searching west, north and east, and Maruti, crossing south, found the lady in the grove and gave Rama's golden ring.","n":"The plea is built of a repeated வேணும் ('one needs'), so the household appears as a list of wants that never ends; the verdict is உயிர் அவமே போம, the life simply wasted. The Ramayana in the praise follows Hanuman rather than Rama, and how it connects to Murugan at Kathirkamam is not clear from the text; the line குறிப்பிற் குறிபோன போதிலும் வரலாமோ is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","pattru","piravi","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:76"},"p:639":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":639,"t":["எதிரி லாத பத்தி ...... தனைமேவி","இனிய தாள்நி னைப்பை ...... யிருபோதும்","இதய வாரி திக்கு ...... ளுறவாகி","எனது ளேசி றக்க ...... அருள்வாயே","கதிர காம வெற்பி ...... லுறைவோனே","கனக மேரு வொத்த ...... புயவீரா","மதுர வாணி யுற்ற ...... கழலோனே","வழுதி கூனி மிர்த்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A short song: the poet asks for matchless bhakti, the thought of the sweet feet morning and evening, and for Murugan to dwell close in the ocean of his heart and shine within him. The praise names the dweller on Kathirkamam hill with arms like golden Meru, whose anklets carry sweet speech, and the Lord who straightened the hump of the Pandya.","n":"வழுதி கூன் நிமிர்த்த: straightening the Pandya king's hump is Sambandar's miracle at Madurai, so Murugan is identified with Sambandar, as in 641, 653 and 666.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paththi","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:76"},"p:640":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":640,"t":["கடகட கருவிகள் தபவகி ரதிர்கதிர்","காமத் தரங்கம் ...... மலைவீரா","கனகத நககுலி புணரித குணகுக","காமத் தனஞ்சம் ...... புயனோட","வடசிக ரகிரித விடுபட நடமிடு","மாவிற் புகுங்கந் ...... தவழாது","வழிவழி தமரென வழிபடு கிலனென","வாவிக் கினம்பொன் ...... றிடுமோதான்","அடவியி ருடியபி நவகும ரியடிமை","யாயப் புனஞ்சென் ...... றயர்வோனே","அயிலவ சமுடன ததிதிரி தருகவி","யாளப் புயங்கொண் ...... டருள்வோனே","இடமொரு மரகத மயில்மிசை வடிவுள","ஏழைக் கிடங்கண் ...... டவர்வாழ்வே","இதமொழி பகரினு மதமொழி பகரினு","மேழைக் கிரங்கும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"Much of this song is hard to divide. It seems to praise the hero of Kathirkamam hill, who rides the peacock dancing until the northern peak is powdered, and to confess that the poet does not worship as one of the hereditary kin; it then names Murugan who went to the millet field as the servant of the new maiden of the forest and wearied there, the life of those who make room for the poor, seated on an emerald peacock, and ends: whether one speaks kind words or proud words, the Lord pities the poor.","n":"The close is the clearest line: இதமொழி பகரினும் மதமொழி பகரினும் ஏழைக்கு இரங்கும், grace that does not wait on the right words. அபிநவ குமரி அடிமையாய் புனம் சென்று reverses the usual servitude: the god becomes Valli's servant. The opening lines (கனகத நககுலி புணரித ...) resist division.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","thondu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:76"},"p:641":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":641,"t":["சமரமுக வேலொத்த விழிபுரள வாரிட்ட","தனமசைய வீதிக்குள் ...... மயில்போ லுலாவியே","சரியைக்ரியை யோகத்தின் வழிவருக்கு பாசுத்தர்","தமையுணர ராகத்தின் ...... வசமாக மேவியே","உமதடிய னாருக்கு மனுமரண மாயைக்கு","முரியவர் மகாதத்தை ...... யெனுமாய மாதரார்","ஒளிரமளி பீடத்தி லமடுபடு வேனுக்கு","முனதருள்க்ரு பாசித்த ...... மருள்கூர வேணுமே","இமகிரிகு மாரத்தி யநுபவைப ராசத்தி","யெழுதரிய காயத்ரி ...... யுமையாள் குமாரனே","எயினர்மட மானுக்கு மடலெழுதி மோகித்து","இதணருகு சேவிக்கு ...... முருகா விசாகனே","அமரர்சிறை மீள்விக்க அமர்செய்துப்ர தாபிக்கு","மதிகவித சாமர்த்ய ...... கவிராஜ ராஜனே","அழுதுலகை வாழ்வித்த கவுணியகு லாதித்த","அரியகதிர் காமத்தி ...... லுரியாபி ராமனே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women who stroll the streets like peacocks with eyes like spears, who bring under their bodies' power even those on the paths of chariyai, kiriyai and yoga, deceitful women whom he calls the great snare of death and maya, and asks that Murugan's grace come to him, sunk on their shining bed. The praise names Murugan son of Uma, the Gayatri and Parasakti, who wrote a madal and served by the watch-platform for the hunter girl, the king of poets who freed the gods, and the sun of the Kauniya clan who wept and saved the world, at Kathirkamam.","n":"The women undo even those on the ordered Saiva paths (சரியை க்ரியை யோகத்தின் வழிவருக்கு), so the danger is set above ascetic discipline. அழுது உலகை வாழ்வித்த கவுணிய குலாதித்த is Sambandar, of the Kauniya gotra, who wept as a child and was fed by Uma: Murugan is identified with him. மடலெழுதி: Murugan himself in the lover's extremity, as in 656 and 664. The phrase மகாதத்தை in the plea is uncertain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","sariyai","yogam","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:76"},"p:642":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":642,"t":["சரத்தே யுதித்தா யுரத்தே குதித்தே","சமர்த்தா யெதிர்த்தே ...... வருசூரைச்","சரிப்போன மட்டே விடுத்தா யடுத்தாய்","தகர்த்தா யுடற்றா ...... னிருகூறாச்","சிரத்தோ டுரத்தோ டறுத்தே குவித்தாய்","செகுத்தாய் பலத்தார் ...... விருதாகச்","சிறைச்சேவல் பெற்றாய் வலக்கார முற்றாய்","திருத்தா மரைத்தா ...... ளருள்வாயே","புரத்தார் வரத்தார் சரச்சே கரத்தார்","பொரத்தா னெதிர்தே ...... வருபோது","பொறுத்தார் பரித்தார் சிரித்தா ரெரித்தார்","பொரித்தார் நுதற்பார் ...... வையிலேபின்","கரித்தோ லுரித்தார் விரித்தார் தரித்தார்","கருத்தார் மருத்தூர் ...... மதனாரைக்","கரிக்கோல மிட்டார் கணுக்கான முத்தே","கதிர்க்காம முற்றார் ...... முருகோனே"],"p":"","r":"A song of praise in two parts: first Murugan, born in the reeds, who leapt at Sura, followed him as far as he fled, split his body in two, heaped his heads and chests and won the cock as his banner, with a plea for the lotus feet; then Siva, who laughed and burned the cities when their lords came to fight, flayed and wore the elephant's hide, and burned Madana to charcoal with the look of his brow. It ends with Murugan the pearl of Kathirkamam.","n":"The rhythm is a chain of short past-tense verbs (பொறுத்தார் பரித்தார் சிரித்தார் எரித்தார் ...), each act of Siva one beat. The song closes on முருகோனே, not பெருமாளே.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:76"},"p:643":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":643,"t":["சரியையா ளர்க்குமக் கிரியையா ளர்க்குநற்","சகலயோ கர்க்குமெட் ...... டரிதாய","சமயபே தத்தினுக் கணுகொணா மெய்ப்பொருட்","டருபரா சத்தியிற் ...... பரமான","துரியமே லற்புதப் பரமஞா னத்தனிச்","சுடர்வியா பித்தநற் ...... பதிநீடு","துகளில்சா யுச்சியக் கதியையீ றற்றசொற்","சுகசெர்ரு பத்தையுற் ...... றடைவேனோ","புரிசைசூழ் செய்ப்பதிக் குரியசா மர்த்யசற்","புருஷவீ ரத்துவிக் ...... ரமசூரன்","புரளவேல் தொட்டகைக் குமரமேன் மைத்திருப்","புகழையோ தற்கெனக் ...... கருள்வோனே","கரியயூ கத்திரட் பலவின்மீ திற்சுளைக்","கனிகள்பீ றிப்புசித் ...... தமராடிக்","கதலிசூ தத்தினிற் பயிலுமீ ழத்தினிற்","கதிரகா மக்கிரிப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether he will reach the true reality that is beyond the reach of those on the paths of chariyai, kiriyai and yoga, that the divisions of sects cannot approach, beyond Parasakti and beyond turiya, the single flame of supreme jnana pervading all, the stainless state of sayujya and the bliss-form beyond words. The praise names Murugan, who gave the poet grace to sing the glorious Thiruppugazh of the Kumara whose vel rolled Sura over, and Kathirkamam hill in Ilam, where black monkeys tear open jackfruits and play among plantains and mangoes.","n":"The goal lies past every path the Saiva system names: சரியை, கிரியை and யோகம் fall short, சமயபேதம் cannot come near, and the state is above துரியம். திருப்புகழை ஓதற்கு எனக்கு அருள்வோனே: the book names itself as Murugan's gift. ஈழம் places Kathirkamam in Lanka. சுகசெர்ரு பத்தை is a garble, probably சுகசொரூபத்தை.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saayujjiyam","samayam","sariyai","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:76"},"p:644":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":644,"t":["பாரவித முத்தப்ப டீரபுள கப்பொற்ப","யோதர நெருக்குற்ற ...... இடையாலே","பாகளவு தித்தித்த கீதமொழி யிற்புட்ப","பாணவிழி யிற்பொத்தி ...... விடுமாதர்","காரணி குழற்கற்றை மேல்மகர மொப்பித்த","காதில்முக வட்டத்தி ...... லதிமோக","காமுக னகப்பட்ட வாசையைம றப்பித்த","கால்களைம றக்கைக்கும் ...... வருமோதான்","தேரிரவி யுட்கிப்பு காமுதுபு ரத்திற்றெ","சாசிரனை மர்த்தித்த ...... அரிமாயன்","சீர்மருக அத்யுக்ர யானைபடும் ரத்நத்ரி","கோணசயி லத்துக்ர ...... கதிர்காம","வீரபுன வெற்பிற்க லாபியெயி னச்சிக்கு","மேகலை யிடைக்கொத்தி ...... னிருதாளின்","வேரிமழை யிற்பச்சை வேயிலரு ணக்கற்றை","வேல்களில கப்பட்ட ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women with heavy pearl-hung breasts, honey-sweet song-like speech, flower-arrow eyes, dark hair and makara earrings, and asks whether he, the lustful man caught in them, will come to forget even the feet that made him forget desire. The praise names Vishnu who crushed the ten-headed one in the city the sun feared to cross, and Murugan at fierce Kathirkamam on the triangular hill of gems, caught in Valli's feet, waist and spear-like eyes on the millet hill.","n":"The song is built on a mirror: the poet is caught (அகப்பட்ட) by women's eyes, and Murugan ends caught (அகப்பட்ட) by Valli's spear-eyes. ஆசையை மறப்பித்த கால்களை மறக்கைக்கும் வருமோ may instead ask whether he will ever forget his desire; the division is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","mayal","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:76"},"p:645":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":645,"t":["மருவறா வெற்றி மலர்தொடா விற்கை","வலிசெயா நிற்கு ...... மதனாலும்","மதில்கள்தா வுற்ற கலைபடா வட்ட","மதிசுடா நிற்கு ...... மதனாலும்","இருகணால் முத்த முதிரயா மத்தி","னிரவினால் நித்த ...... மெலியாதே","இடருறா மெத்த மயல்கொளா நிற்கு","மிவளைவாழ் விக்க ...... வரவேணும்","கரிகள்சேர் வெற்பி லரியவே டிச்சி","கலவிகூர் சித்ர ...... மணிமார்பா","கனகமா ணிக்க வடிவனே மிக்க","கதிரகா மத்தி ...... லுறைவோனே","முருகனே பத்த ரருகனே முத்தி","முதல்வனே பச்சை ...... மயில்வீரா","முடுகிமே லிட்ட கொடியசூர் கெட்டு","முறியவேல் தொட்ட ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"Spoken for a girl sick with love: wounded by Madana's flower bow and burned by the full moon above the walls, weeping pearls from both eyes through the night, she is wasting away in deep infatuation, and the speaker asks Murugan to come and give her life. The praise names his jewelled chest that delighted in the hunter girl, his form of gold and ruby, Murugan near to his devotees, the first of release, the hero on the green peacock whose vel broke Sura.","n":"An akam lament in another's voice (இவளை வாழ்விக்க வரவேணும்), not the poet's confession. The torments are the classical ones, Kama's bow and the moon at night, with the tears as pearls (முத்தம்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","melivu","mayal"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:76"},"p:646":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":646,"t":["மாதர்வச மாயுற் ...... றுழல்வாரும்","மாதவமெ ணாமற் ...... றிரிவாரும்","தீதகல வோதிப் ...... பணியாரும்","தீநரக மீதிற் ...... றிகழ்வாரே","நாதவொளி யேநற் ...... குணசீலா","நாரியிரு வோரைப் ...... புணர்வேலா","சோதிசிவ ஞானக் ...... குமரேசா","தோமில் கதிர்காமப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A short song that judges rather than confesses: those who wander under women's power, who roam without thinking of great austerity, and who do not recite and bow so that evil leaves them will shine in the fire of hell. The praise names Murugan the light of nada, of good qualities, the vel who embraces two women, the Kumara of Sivajnana, the flawless lord of Kathirkamam.","n":"தீநரக மீதில் திகழ்வாரே: 'they will shine in hell's fire' is ironic, the verb of glory used for the damned. The song speaks of others in the third person, unusual in a book of first-person pleas.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naragam","kaamam","thavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:76"},"p:647":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":647,"t":["முதிரு மார வார நட்பொ டிலகு மார வார மெற்றி","முனியு மார வார முற்ற ...... கடலாலே","முடிவி லாத தோர் வடக்கி லெரியு மால மார்பி டத்து","முழுகி யேறி மேலெ றிக்கு ...... நிலவாலே","வெதிரி லாயர் வாயில் வைத்து மதுர ராக நீடி சைக்கும்","வினைவி டாத தாய ருக்கு ...... மழியாதே","விளையு மோக போக முற்றி அளவி லாத காதல் பெற்ற","விகட மாதை நீ யணைக்க ...... வரவேணும்","கதிர காம மாந கர்க்கு ளெதிரி லாத வேல்த ரித்த","கடவு ளேக லாப சித்ர ...... மயில்வீரா","கயலு லாம்வி லோச னத்தி களப மார்ப யோத ரத்தி","ககன மேவு வாளொ ருத்தி ...... மணவாளா","அதிர வீசி யாடும் வெற்றி விடையி லேறு மீசர் கற்க","அரிய ஞான வாச கத்தை ...... யருள்வோனே","அகில லோக மீது சுற்றி யசுரர் லோக நீறெ ழுப்பி","அமரர் லோகம் வாழ வைத்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"Spoken for a girl sick with love: tormented by the roaring sea, by the moon that rises burning from the poison of the northern fire, by the sweet raga of the cowherds' bamboo flute and by her mothers' unending scolding, she has come to boundless love, and the speaker asks Murugan to come and embrace her. The praise names the god of Kathirkamam with the matchless vel and the bright peacock, the husband of the one of the sky (Devayanai), who gave the bull-riding Isa the hard word of jnana, and who scattered the demons' world to ash so the gods' world might live.","n":"The sea, moon and flute are the evening torments of separated lovers in classical akam; the cowherd's flute is the Kural's image (1228). அரிய ஞான வாசகத்தை அருள்வோனே is the teaching of Siva, without Om being named. விகட மாதை, 'the bold girl', may describe her daring in love.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","maalai","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:76"},"p:648":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":648,"t":["வருபவர்க ளோலை கொண்டு நமனுடைய தூத ரென்று","மடிபிடிய தாக நின்று ...... தொடர்போது","மயலதுபொ லாத வம்பன் விரகுடைய னாகு மென்று","வசைகளுட னேதொ டர்ந்து ...... அடைவார்கள்","கருவியத னாலெ றிந்து சதைகள்தனை யேய ரிந்து","கரியபுன லேசொ ரிந்து ...... விடவேதான்","கழுமுனையி லேயி ரென்று விடுமெனும வேளை கண்டு","கடுகிவர வேணு மெந்தன் ...... முனமேதான்","பரகிரியு லாவு செந்தி மனையினுட னேயி டும்பன்","பழநிதனி லேயி ருந்த ...... குமரேசா","பதிகள் பல வாயி ரங்கள் மலைகள்வெகு கோடி நின்ற","பதமடியர் காண வந்த ...... கதிர்காமா","அரவுபிறை பூளை தும்பை விலுவமொடு தூர்வை கொன்றை","யணிவர் சடை யாளர் தந்த ...... முருகோனே","அரகரசி வாய சம்புலத குருபரகு மார நம்பு","மடியர்தமை யாள வந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet pictures Yama's messengers coming with the palm leaf, seizing him by the waist-cloth, following him with abuse ('a wicked rogue, a cheat in his infatuation'), striking him with weapons, cutting his flesh and pouring out his dark blood until they say 'lay him on the stake', and asks Murugan to come quickly before him at that hour. The praise names the Kumaresa of Senthil, of Idumban and of Palani, who came to Kathirkamam for devotees to see after standing in thousands of shrines and countless hills, the son of the matted-haired Siva who wears serpent, crescent, vilvam and konrai, the guru who came to rule those who trust him.","n":"Death's men here are torturers rather than a summons: the abuse, the cutting and the stake (கழுமுனை) belong more to pictures of hell than to the deathbed songs. The Kathirkamam shrine is presented as the place Murugan came to after all his other shrines.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","adiyaar","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:76"},"p:649":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":649,"t":["தொடுத்த வாளென விழித்து மார்முலை","யசைத்து மேகலை மறைத்து மூடிகள்","துடித்து நேர்கலை நெகிழ்த்து மாவியல் ...... கொளுமாதர்","சுகித்த ஹாவென நகைத்து மேல்விழ","முடித்த வார்குழல் விரித்து மேவிதழ்","துவர்த்த வாய்சுரு ளடக்கி மால்கொடு ...... வழியேபோய்ப்","படுத்த பாயலி லணைத்து மாமுலை","பிடித்து மார்பொடு மழுத்தி வாயிதழ்","கடித்து நாணம தழித்த பாவிகள் ...... வலையாலே","பலித்து நோய்பிணி கிடத்து பாய்மிசை","வெளுத்து வாய்களு மலத்தி னாயென","பசித்து தாகமு மெடுத்தி டாவுயி ...... ருழல்வேனோ","வெடுத்த தாடகை சினத்தை யோர்கணை","விடுத்து யாகமும் நடத்தி யேயொரு","மிகுத்த வார்சிலை முறித்த மாயவன் ...... மருகோனே","விதித்து ஞாலம தளித்த வேதனை","யதிர்த்து வோர்முடி கரத்து லாயனல்","விழித்து காமனை யெரித்த தாதையர் ...... குருநாதா","அடுத்த ஆயிர விடப்ப ணாமுடி","நடுக்க மாமலை பிளக்க வேகவ","டரக்கர் மாமுடி பதைக்க வேபொரு ...... மயில்வீரா","அறத்தில் வாழுமை சிறக்க வேயறு","முகத்தி னோடணி குறத்தி யானையொ","டருக்கொ ணாமலை தருக்கு லாவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet lists the arts of women who glare with sword-like eyes, sway their breasts, loosen their clothes, laugh, let down their hair, embrace on the bed and bite the lips until shame is gone, and asks whether, caught in the net of these sinners, he must lie diseased on a mat, pale, hungry and thirsty like a dog. The praise names Rama, who killed Tataka with one arrow, guarded the sacrifice and broke the great bow, as uncle; Murugan as guru of the father who plucked Brahma's head and burned Kama; the hero on the peacock that shook the serpent's thousand heads and split the mountain; and the lord with Uma who lives in dharma.","n":"The consequence of lust is disease and degradation in this life (நோய் பிணி கிடத்து பாய்மிசை வெளுத்து), not hell. The closing அருக்கொணா மலை is not Kathirkamam, and may be Thirukkonamalai (see 650).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","punarchi","noy"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:76"},"p:650":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":650,"t":["விலைக்கு மேனியி லணிக்கோவை மேகலை","தரித்த வாடையு மணிப்பூணு மாகவெ","மினுக்கு மாதர்க ளிடக்காம மூழ்கியெ ...... மயலு஡றி","மிகுத்த காமிய னெனப்பாரு ளொரெதிர்","நகைக்க வேயுட லெடுத்தேவி யாகுல","வெறுப்ப தாகியெ யுழைத்தேவி டாய்படு ...... கொடியேனைக்","கலக்க மாகவெ மலக்கூடி லேமிகு","பிணிக்கு ளாகியெ தவிக்காம லேயுனை","கவிக்கு ளாய்சொலி கடைத்தேற வேசெயு ...... மொருவாழ்வே","கதிக்கு நாதனி யுனைத்தேடி யேபுகு","ழுரைக்கு நாயெனை யருட்பார்வை யாகவெ","கழற்கு ளாகவெ சிறப்பான தாயருள் ...... தரவேணும்","மலைக்கு நாயக சிவக்காமி நாயகர்","திருக்கு மாரனெ முகத்தாறு தேசிக","வடிப்ப மாதொரு குறப்பாவை யாள்மகிழ் ...... தருவேளே","வசிட்டர் காசிபர் தவத்தான யோகியர்","அகத்ய மாமுநி யிடைக்காடர் கீரனும்","வகுத்த பாவினில் பொருட்கோல மாய்வரு ...... முருகோனே","நிலைக்கு நான்மறை மகத்தான பூசுரர்","திருக்கொ ணாமலை தலத்தாரு கோபுர","நிலைக்குள் வாயினில் கிளிப்பாடு பூதியில் ...... வருவோனே","நிகழ்த்து மேழ்பவ கடற்சூறை யாகவெ","யெடுத்த வேல்கொடு பொடித்தூள தாஎறி","நினைத்த காரிய மநுக்கூல மேபுரி ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses that, drowning in desire for women who glitter with girdles and jewels on bodies put up for sale, he became the great lustful man the world laughs at, toiling and exhausted, and asks Murugan, the one life who lets him cross over by telling him in verse, not to let him suffer in disease in this cage of filth, but to look with grace on the dog who seeks and praises him and give him the grace of his feet. The praise names the son of Sivakami's lord, the six-faced teacher, the one who comes as the meaning in the verses of Vasishtha, Kashyapa, the yogis, Agastya, Idaikkadar and Keeran, who comes to the gopuram gateway at Thirukkonamalai, and whose vel powdered the sea of seven births.","n":"விலைக்கு மேனி: the women's bodies are 'for a price', so the courtesan's trade is named. உனைக் கவிக்குள்ளாய்ச் சொலிக் கடைத்தேறவே: the way out is to put the god into verse. வகுத்த பாவினில் பொருட்கோலமாய் வரு: Murugan is the meaning-form that comes in the poems of the sages and of Tamil poets such as Idaikkadar and Nakkirar (கீரன்). The words about parrots and பூதி at the gopuram gate are obscure.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["varaivinmagalir","yaakkai","thamizh","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:77"},"p:651":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":651,"t":["தார ணிக்கதி பாவி யாய்வெகு","சூது மெத்திய மூட னாய்மன","சாத னைக்கள வாணி யாயுறு ...... மதிமோக","தாப மிக்குள வீண னாய்பொரு","வேல்வி ழிச்சிய ராகு மாதர்கள்","தாமு யச்செயு மேது தேடிய ...... நினைவாகிப்","பூர ணச்சிவ ஞான காவிய","மோது தற்புணர் வான நேயர்கள்","பூசு மெய்த்திரு நீறி டாஇரு ...... வினையேனைப்","பூசி மெய்ப்பத மான சேவடி","காண வைத்தருள் ஞான மாகிய","போத கத்தினை யேயு மாறருள் ...... புரிவாயே","வார ணத்தினை யேக ராவுமு","னேவ ளைத்திடு போதுமேவிய","மாய வற்கித மாக வீறிய ...... மருகோனே","வாழு முப்புர வீற தானது","நீறெ ழப்புகை யாக வேசெய்த","மாம திப்பிறை வேணி யாரருள் ...... புதல்வோனே","கார ணக்குறி யான நீதிய","ரான வர்க்குமு னாக வேநெறி","காவி யச்சிவ நூலை யோதிய ...... கதிர்வேலா","கான கக்குற மாதை மேவிய","ஞான சொற்கும ராப ராபர","காசி யிற்பிர தாப மாயுறை ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses that he is a sinner on earth, a fool steeped in gambling, a thief of the mind's discipline, a vain man burning with desire who thought only of ways to keep spear-eyed women, a man of two deeds who never wore the true holy ash that the lovers of the full Sivajnana scriptures wear, and asks Murugan to smear it on him, let him see the red feet and grant the awakening of jnana. The praise names Vishnu who came when the crocodile seized the elephant, Siva who burned the three cities to ash, and Murugan who recited the Saiva scripture before the righteous, the jnana-speaking Kumara of the forest girl, at Kasi.","n":"பூசு மெய்த்திரு நீறிடா இருவினையேனைப் பூசி: the verb is repeated, 'me who did not smear it, smear', so the poet asks the god to put the ash on him himself. காரணக்குறியான நீதியரானவர்க்கு முன்னாகவே நெறி காவியச் சிவநூலை ஓதிய may refer to a particular telling of a Saiva text before an assembly; the allusion is not clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruneeru","soodhu","gnaanam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:78"},"p:652":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":652,"t":["மங்கைக் கணவனும் வாழ்சிவ ணாமயல்","பங்கப் படமிசை யேபனி போல்மதம்","வந்துட் பெருகிட வேவிதி யானவ ...... னருள்மேவி","வண்டுத் தடிகைபொ லாகியெ நாள்பல","பந்துப் பனைபழ மோடிள நீர்குட","மண்டிப் பலபல வாய்வினை கோலும ...... வழியாலே","திங்கட் பதுசெல வேதலை கீழுற","வந்துப் புவிதனி லேமத லாயென","சிந்தைக் குழவியெ னாவனை தாதையு ...... மருள்கூரச்","செம்பொற் றடமுலை பால்குடி நாள்பல","பண்புத் தவழ்நடை போய்வித மாய்பல","சிங்கிப் பெருவிழி யாரவ மாயதி ...... லழிவேனோ","அங்கைத் தரியென வேயொரு பாலக","னின்பக் கிருபைய தாயொரு தூண்மிசை","அம்பற் கொடுவரி யாயிரண் யாசுர ...... னுடல்பீறி","அண்டர்க் கருள்பெரு மான் முதி ராவணி","சங்குத் திகிரிக ரோனரி நாரவ","ரங்கத் திருவணை மேல்துயில் நாரணன் ...... மருகோனே","கங்கைச் சடைமுடி யோனிட மேவிய","தங்கப் பவளொளி பால்மதி போல்முக","கங்குற் றரிகுழ லாள்பர மேசுரி ...... யருள்பாலா","கந்துப் பரிமயில் வாகன மீதிரு","கொங்கைக் குறமக ளாசையொ டேமகிழ்","கங்கைப் பதிநதி காசியில் மேவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet traces his making: the union of a man and a woman, the seed growing within by Brahma's will, the embryo taking shape like a grub, a ball, a palm fruit, a tender coconut and a pot by the way of deeds, ten months passing until it comes head down into the world, the parents calling it their child, the milk, the crawling and the toddling; then he asks whether he must perish through big-eyed women. The praise tells of Narasimha bursting from a pillar to tear Hiranya's body for the boy who said Hari was there, names Narayana of the conch and discus asleep on the serpent at Srirangam as uncle, and Parameswari at Siva's side as mother, at Kasi on the Ganga.","n":"The growth of the embryo by stages of size (பந்து, பனைபழம், இளநீர், குடம்) and the tenth month head down (திங்கட் பதுசெல வேதலை கீழுற) is a formula the book repeats (see 520). The whole of the plea is the life story; the fault is named only in its last words, the women.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["karu","piravi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:78"},"p:653":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":653,"t":["வேழ முண்ட விளாகனி ...... யதுபோல","மேனி கொண்டு வியாபக ...... மயலு஡றி","நாளு மிண்டர்கள் போல்மிக ...... அயர்வாகி","நானு நைந்து விடாதருள் ...... புரிவாயே","மாள அன்றம ணீசர்கள் ...... கழுவேற","வாதில் வென்ற சிகாமணி ...... மயில்வீரா","காள கண்ட னுமாபதி ...... தருபாலா","காசி கங்கையில் மேவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A short song: the poet asks that, with his body become like a wood-apple eaten by an elephant and pervaded by infatuation, wearied daily like the boorish, he not be let to wither, but be given grace. The praise names Murugan the crest-jewel who won the debate when the Jain lords went to the stake, the hero on the peacock, son of Umapati of the dark throat, at Kasi by the Ganga.","n":"வேழம் உண்ட விளாங்கனி: the proverbial wood-apple that the elephant leaves whole outside and empty within, an image of a body hollowed while it looks intact. அமணீசர்கள் கழுவேற வாதில் வென்ற is Sambandar's victory at Madurai, so Murugan is again identified with Sambandar (639, 641, 666).","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["yaakkai","mayal","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:78"},"p:654":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":654,"t":["சிகர மருந்த வாழ்வது ...... சிவஞானம்","சிதறி யலைந்து போவது ...... செயலாசை","மகர நெருங்க வீழ்வது ...... மகமாய","மருவி நினைந்தி டாவருள் ...... புரிவாயே","அகர நெருங்கி னாமய ...... முறவாகி","அவச மொடுங்கை யாறொடு ...... முனமேகிக்","ககன மிசைந்த சூரியர் ...... புகமாயை","கருணை பொழிந்து மேவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A short, obscure song. It seems to say that living by tasting the letter சி is Sivajnana, that scattering and wandering is the desire for action, and that falling as the letter ம draws near is great maya, and asks for grace not to cling to it in thought. The praise, also hard to divide, names a Lord who poured compassion so that the suns in the sky might enter, with the letter அ again in its first line; no shrine is named.","n":"சிகரம், அகரம், மகரம் can be read as 'the letter சி', 'the letter அ', 'the letter ம'. In the doctrine of the five letters (சிவாயநம) சி stands for Siva and ம for malam, which would fit சிகரம் with சிவஞானம் and மகரம் with மாயை; but சிகரம் can also mean a summit, and the reading is a guess. The shrine's name Mayapuri may be played on in மாயை.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panchaakkaram","maayai","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:79"},"p:655":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":655,"t":["அருவரை யெடுத்த வீர னெரிபட விரற்க ளூணு","மரனிட மிருக்கு மாயி ...... யருள்வோனே","அலைகட லடைத்த ராமன் மிகமன மகிழ்ச்சி கூரு","மணிமயில நடத்து மாசை ...... மருகோனே","பருதியி னொளிக்கண் வீறும் அறுமுக நிரைத்த தோள்ப","னிருகர மிகுத்த பார ...... முருகாநின்","பதமல ருளத்தி னாளு நினைவுறு கருத்தர் தாள்கள்","பணியவு மெனக்கு ஞானம் ...... அருள்வாயே","சுருதிக ளுரைத்த வேத னுரைமொழி தனக் குளாதி","சொலுவென வுரைத்த ஞான ...... குருநாதா","சுரர்பதி தழைத்து வாழ அமர்சிறை யனைத்து மீள","துணிபட அரக்கர் மாள ...... விடும்வேலா","மருமலர் மணக்கும் வாச நிறைதரு தருக்கள் சூழும்","வயல்புடை கிடக்கு நீல ...... மலர்வாவி","வளமுறு தடத்தி னோடு சரஸ்வதி நதிக்கண் வீறு","வயிரவி வனத்தில் மேவு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks Murugan to grant him the jnana to bow at the feet of those who think daily in their hearts of his flower feet. The praise names him son of the Mother who dwells with Hara, whose toes crushed the hero who lifted the mountain, nephew of Rama who bridged the sea, six-faced and twelve-armed in the sun's light, the jnana guru who told Brahma, the speaker of the Vedas, to say the first word within his own speech, the vel that freed the gods, at Vayiravivanam on the Saraswati amid fragrant groves and blue-lily pools.","n":"The plea is twice removed: not for the feet, but to bow at the feet of those who hold his feet in mind (கருத்தர் தாள்கள் பணிய), service of devotees as the form of jnana. சுருதிகள் உரைத்த வேதன் உரைமொழி தனக்குள் ஆதி சொலு: the challenge to Brahma to speak the first word is the Om episode, but Om is not named.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adiyaar","gnaanam","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:80"},"p:656":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":656,"t":["அடலரி மகவு விதிவழி யொழுகு","மைவ ருமொய்க்கு ரம்பை ...... யுடனாளும்","அலைகட லுலகி லலம்வரு கலக","வைவர் தமக்கு டைந்து ...... தடுமாறி","இடர்படு மடிமை யுளமுரை யுடலொ","டெல்லை விடப்ர பஞ்ச ...... மயல்தீர","எனதற நினது கழல்பெற மவுன","வெல்லை குறிப்ப தொன்று ...... புகல்வாயே","வடமணி முலையு மழகிய முகமும்","வள்ளை யெனத்த யங்கு ...... மிருகாதும்","மரகத வடிவு மடலிடை யெழுதி","வள்ளி புனத்தில் நின்ற ...... மயில்வீரா","விடதர திகுணர் சசிதரர் நிமலர்","வெள்ளி மலைச்ச யம்பு ...... குருநாதா","விகசித கமல பரிபுர முளரி","வெள்ளி கரத்த மர்ந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet tells how, in this body swarmed by the five that follow Brahma's ordaining, in the wave-girt world, battered by the five quarrelsome ones and stumbling, a servant in distress, he asks that heart, word and body reach their end, the world's infatuation leave him, and, with himself gone, to gain Murugan's feet, Murugan tell him the one thing that marks the bound of silence. The praise pictures Valli's breasts, face and ears like vallai leaves, names Murugan who wrote his emerald form on the madal and stood in Valli's field, the guru of self-born Siva of the silver mountain, at Vellikaram of the blooming lotus.","n":"எனது அற நினது கழல் பெற: the feet are gained when 'mine' ends. மவுன எல்லை குறிப்பது ஒன்று புகல்வாயே asks to be told the one word that points to silence, a paradox the book returns to. The ஐவர் are the five senses, pictured as quarrelsome masters. The opening (அடல் அரி மகவு விதி) is hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mounam","mayal","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:81"},"p:657":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":657,"t":["சிகரிக ளிடிய நடநவில் கலவி","செவ்வி மலர்க்க டம்பு ...... சிறுவாள்வேல்","திருமுக சமுக சததள முளரி","திவ்ய கரத்தி ணங்கு ...... பொருசேவல்","அகிலடி பறிய எறிதிரை யருவி","ஐவன வெற்பில் வஞ்சி ...... கணவாஎன்","றகிலமு முணர மொழிதரு மொழியி","னல்லது பொற்பதங்கள் ...... பெறலாமோ","நிகரிட அரிய சிவசுத பரம","நிர்வச னப்ர சங்க ...... குருநாதா","நிரைதிகழ் பொதுவர் நெறிபடு பழைய","நெல்லி மரத்த மர்ந்த ...... அபிராம","வெகுமுக ககன நதிமதி யிதழி","வில்வ முடித்த நம்பர் ...... பெருவாழ்வே","விகசித கமல பரிமள கமல","வெள்ளி கரத்த மர்ந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether the golden feet can be gained except by words that make all the world know Murugan's emblems: the dancing peacock that broke the peaks, the fresh kadamba, the small sword-like vel, the lotus faces, the fighting cock in his hand, and his name as Valli's husband of the Aivana hill. The praise names him the incomparable son of Siva, the guru of the discourse beyond words, the beautiful one under the old nelli tree where the cowherds pass with their herds, the great life of Siva who wears Ganga, the moon, konrai and vilvam, at Vellikaram.","n":"The plea makes proclaiming the god the only road to his feet (மொழியின் அல்லது ... பெறலாமோ), while the praise calls him the teacher of a discourse beyond words (நிர்வசன ப்ரசங்க குருநாதா): speech is the poet's way, wordlessness the god's teaching.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","thiruvadi","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:81"},"p:658":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":658,"t":["குவலய மல்கு தவலிகள் முல்லை","குளிர்நகை சொல்லு ...... முதுபாகு","குழையிள வள்ளை யிடைசிறு வல்லி","குயமுலை கொள்ளை ...... விழைமேவிக்","கவலைசெய் வல்ல தவலரு முள்ள","கலவியில் தெள்ளு ...... கவிமாலை","கடிமல ரைய அணிவன செய்ய","கழலிணை பைய ...... அருள்வாயே","தவநெறி யுள்ளு சிவமுனி துள்ளு","தனியுழை புள்ளி ...... யுடனாடித்","தருபுன வள்ளி மலைமற வள்ளி","தருதினை மெள்ள ...... நுகர்வோனே","அவநெறி சொல்லு மவரவை கொல்லு","மழகிய வெள்ளி ...... நகர்வாழ்வே","அடையலர் செல்வ மளறிடை செல்ல","அமர்செய வல்ல ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet tells of desiring women whose jasmine-white teeth and sweet speech, ear-rings, slender waists and breasts draw him into a union that brings worry, and asks Murugan instead to give gently his red feet, adorned with a garland of clear verse and fragrant flowers. The praise names Valli, born of the lone spotted doe with which the ascetic sage played, whose millet Murugan slowly ate in the field on Valli's hill, and Murugan of the beautiful silver town who destroys the assemblies of those who preach false paths and makes enemies' wealth sink in the mire.","n":"The plea offers a garland of verse (கவிமாலை) for the feet, the poet's song set against the union. அவநெறி சொல்லும் அவரவை கொல்லும் may again point to the defeat of the Jains, though no sect is named. The doubled ல்ல and ள்ள rhythm makes several words hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","thiruvadi","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:81"},"p:659":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":659,"t":["பொருவன கள்ள இருகயல் வள்ளை","புரிகுழை தள்ளி ...... விளையாடும்","புளகித வல்லி யிளகித வல்லி","புரியிள முல்லை ...... நகைமீதே","உருகிட வுள்ள விரகுடை யுள்ள","முலகுயி ருள்ள ...... பொழுதேநின்","றுமைதரு செல்வ னெனமிகு கல்வி","யுணர்வொடு சொல்ல ...... வுணராதோ","மருமலர் வள்ளி புரமுள வள்ளி","மலைமற வள்ளி ...... மணவாளா","வளர்புவி யெல்லை யளவிடு தொல்லை","மரகத நல்ல ...... மயில்வீரா","அருவரை விள்ள அயில்விடு மள்ள","அணிவயல் வெள்ளி ...... நகர்வாழ்வே","அடையலர் செல்வ மளறிடை செல்ல","அமர்செய வல்ல ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether his heart, cunning and melting at women's eyes that push at their ear-rings and their jasmine smiles, will not learn, while life remains in the world, to say with learning and understanding that Murugan is the treasure Uma gave. The praise names Valli's bridegroom, the hero of the old emerald peacock that measured the world's bounds, the warrior who threw the vel to split the hill, the life of the silver town amid fields.","n":"உலகு உயிர் உள்ள பொழுதே: the time to learn to praise is now, while life lasts. The heart that melts (உருகிட) for women is the same heart the poet would have melt in praise.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","pugazh","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:81"},"p:660":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":660,"t":["கள்ள முள்ள வல்ல வல்லி","கையி லள்ளி ...... பொருளீயக்","கல்லு நெல்லு வெள்ளி தெள்ளு","கல்வி செல்வர் ...... கிளைமாய","அள்ளல் துள்ளி ஐவர் செல்லு","மல்லல் சொல்ல ...... முடியாதே","ஐய ரைய மெய்யர் மெய்ய","ஐய செய்ய ...... கழல்தாராய்","வள்ளல் புள்ளி நவ்வி நல்கு","வள்ளி கிள்ளை ...... மொழியாலே","மைய லெய்து மைய செய்யில்","வையில் வெள்வ ...... ளைகளேற","மெள்ள மள்ளர் கொய்யு நெல்லின்","வெள்ள வெள்ளி ...... நகர்வாழ்வே","வெய்ய சைய வில்லி சொல்லை","வெல்ல வல்ல ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet tells how, giving his wealth into the hands of a deceitful, clever woman, his gems, grain, silver, learning and wealthy kin all vanished, and of the indescribable misery of the mire to which the five senses lead, and asks the true Lord to give his red feet. The praise names Murugan infatuated by the parrot speech of Valli, daughter of the generous spotted doe, the life of the silver town where white conches climb in the dark fields as the farmers slowly reap the paddy, and the Lord able to overcome the word of the fierce bowman of the mountain.","n":"கையில் அள்ளி பொருள் ஈய: the wealth goes by the handful into the woman's hand, so the courtesan's trade is named, and with it go not only goods but learning and kin. The refrain வெய்ய சைய வில்லி சொல்லை வெல்ல வல்ல probably names Siva, whose bow was the mountain, and may refer to Murugan's teaching of Siva; the allusion is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","selvam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:81"},"p:661":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":661,"t":["தொய்யில் செய்யில் நொய்யர் கையர்","தொய்யு மைய ...... இடையாலுந்","துள்ளி வள்ளை தள்ளி யுள்ளல்","சொல்லு கள்ள ...... விழியாலும்","மைய செவ்வி மவ்வல் முல்லை","மல்கு நல்ல ...... குழலாலும்","மையல் கொள்ள எள்ளல் செய்யும்","வல்லி சொல்லை ...... மகிழ்வேனோ","செய்ய துய்ய புள்ளி நவ்வி","செல்வி கல்வ ...... ரையிலேனல்","தெய்வ வள்ளி மையல் கொள்ளு","செல்வ பிள்ளை ...... முருகோனே","மெய்யர் மெய்ய பொய்யர் பொய்ய","வெள்ளை வெள்ளி ...... நகர்வாழ்வே","வெய்ய சைய வில்லி சொல்லை","வெல்ல வல்ல ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether he will take delight in the words of a creeper-like woman who mocks him while making him infatuated with her painted breasts, slender waist, deceitful eyes that push at her ear-rings, and dark hair full of jasmine. The praise names Murugan the beloved son infatuated with divine Valli, daughter of the spotted doe on the stony hill of millet, true to the true and false to the false, the life of the white silver town.","n":"மெய்யர் மெய்ய பொய்யர் பொய்ய: Murugan is real to those who are real and unreal to the false, an epithet that answers the woman's deceit (கள்ள விழி) in the plea. எள்ளல் செய்யும்: the woman mocks the man she infatuates.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","mayal"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:81"},"p:662":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":662,"t":["இல்லையென நாணி யுள்ளதின் மறாம","லெள்ளினள வேனும் ...... பகிராரை","எவ்வமென நாடி யுய்வகையி லேனை","யெவ்வகையு நாமங் ...... கவியாகச்","சொல்லவறி யேனை யெல்லைதெரி யாத","தொல்லைமுத லேதென் ...... றுணரேனைத்","தொய்யுமுடல் பேணு பொய்யனைவி டாது","துய்யகழ லாளுந் ...... திறமேதோ","வல்லசுரர் மாள நல்லசுரர் வாழ","மையவரை பாகம் ...... படமோது","மையுலவு சோலை செய்யகுளிர் சாரல்","வள்ளிமலை வாழுங் ...... கொடிகோவே","வெல்லுமயி லேறு வல்லகும ரேச","வெள்ளிலுட னீபம் ...... புனைவோனே","வெள்ளிமணி மாட மல்குதிரு வீதி","வெள்ளிநகர் மேவும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet calls himself one without a way to salvation, who in his need went to people who will not share even a sesame seed's worth, unlike those ashamed to say 'there is none' and who never refuse from what they have; who cannot put Murugan's names into verse, does not understand the boundless first cause, and is a liar who tends a sagging body; and asks by what power the pure feet will rule him without letting him go. The praise names the vel that struck the dark mountain so the demons died and the gods lived, the lord of the creeper of Valli's hill, the Kumaresa on the peacock wearing vellil and kadamba, at the silver town of jewelled mansions.","n":"இல்லையென நாணி உள்ளதின் மறாமல் describes the true giver, ashamed to say 'none', who does not refuse from what he has, the Kural's ideal (223). Whether the poet confesses begging from misers (பகிராரை ... நாடி) or his own failure to share depends on how the line is divided.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","iravu","yaakkai","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:81"},"p:663":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":663,"t":["பையரவு போலு நொய்யஇடை மாதர்","பையவரு கோலந் ...... தனைநாடிப்","பையலென வோடி மையல்மிகு மோக","பவ்வமிசை வீழுந் ...... தனிநாயேன்","உய்யவொரு கால மையவுப தேச","முள்ளுருக நாடும் ...... படிபேசி","உள்ளதுமி லாது மல்லதவி ரோத","உல்லசவி நோதந் ...... தருவாயே","வையமுழு தாளு மையகும ரேச","வள்ளிபடர் கானம் ...... புடைசூழும்","வள்ளிமலை வாழும் வள்ளிமண வாள","மையுததி யேழுங் ...... கனல்மூள","வெய்யநிரு தேசர் சையமுடன் வீழ","வெல்லயில்வி நோதம் ...... புரிவோனே","வெள்ளிமணி மாட மல்குதிரு வீதி","வெள்ளிநகர் மேவும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet, a lone dog who ran like a boy after the slow beauty of thin-waisted women and fell into the sea of infatuation, asks that, to be saved, Murugan once speak to him the instruction that makes the heart melt and seek, and give him the joyous play that is neither being nor non-being, beyond delusion. The praise names the lord who rules the whole earth, Valli's bridegroom on Valli's hill, whose vel set the seven seas aflame and felled the demon lords with their mountain, at the silver town.","n":"உள்ளதும் இலாது அல்லது: the bliss asked for is beyond both 'is' and 'is not', the language of the Siddhanta and Vedanta for the Lord. உபதேசம் is the guru's instruction, asked for directly. The division of மல்லதவிரோத is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mayal","guru","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:81"},"p:664":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":664,"t":["வதன சரோருக நயன சிலீமுக வள்ளி புனத்தில் நின்று","வாராய்பதி காதங் காதரை ...... யொன்\u000eமுரும்","வயலு மொரேவிடை யெனவொரு காவிடை வல்லப","மற்றழிந்து மாலாய்மட லேறுங் காமுக ...... எம்பிரானே","இதவிய காணிவை ததையென வேடுவ னெய்திடு மெச்சில் தின்று","லீலாசல மாடுந் தூயவன் ...... மைந்தநாளும்","இளையவ மூதுரை மலைகிழ வோனென வெள்ள மெனக் கலந்து","நூறாயிர பேதஞ் சாதமொ ...... ழிந்தவாதான்","கதைகன சாபதி கிரிவளை வாளொடு கைவசி வித்தநந்த","கோபாலம கீபன் தேவிம ...... கிழ்ந்துவாழக்","கயிறொ டுலு஡கல முருள வுலாவிய கள்வ னறப் பயந்து","ஆகாய பாலம் பீறநி ...... மிர்ந்துநீள","விதரண மாவலி வெருவ மகாவ்ருத வெள்ள வெளுக்க நின்ற","நாராயண மாமன் சேயைம ...... னிந்தகோவே","விளைவய லு஡டிடை வளைவிளை யாடிய வெள்ளிநகர்க் கமர்ந்த","வேலாயுத மேவுந் தேவர்கள் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"A song of praise in long, intricate lines: it pictures Murugan in Valli's millet field, her lotus face and arrow eyes, the god who lost his strength and, infatuated, mounted the palmyra horse, the lustful one who is our Lord; names him son of the pure Siva who ate the meat the hunter tasted first and offered with 'see, these are sweet'; and tells of his uncle as Krishna, delighting Yashoda, rolling the mortar he was tied to, growing until he pierced the sky as Mahabali feared, at the silver town where conches play in the fields.","n":"மாலாய் மடலேறும் காமுக எம்பிரானே: Murugan himself takes the lover's last resort, riding the madal, and is called 'the lustful one' and 'our Lord' in one breath, as in 641 and 656. The hunter whose spittle-tasted meat Siva ate is Kannappar, the type of love that ignores rule. Several phrases (வாராய்பதி காதங் காதரை ஒன்முரும், நூறாயிர பேதஞ் சாதமொழிந்த) are obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["madal","kaamam","paththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:81"},"p:665":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":665,"t":["நசையொடு தோலுந் தசைதுறு நீரும்","நடுநடு வேயென் ...... புறுகீலும்","நலமுறு வேயொன் றிடஇரு கால்நன்","றுறநடை யாருங் ...... குடிலு஡டே","விசையுறு காலம் புலனெறி யேவெங்","கனலுயிர் வேழந் ...... திரியாதே","விழுமடி யார்முன் பழுதற வேள்கந்","தனுமென வோதும் ...... விறல்தாராய்","இசையுற வேயன் றசைவற வூதும்","எழிலரி வேழம் ...... எனையாளென்","றிடர்கொடு மூலந் தொடர்வுட னோதும்","இடமிமை யாமுன் ...... வருமாயன்","திசைமுக னாருந் திசைபுவி வானுந்","திரிதர வாழுஞ் ...... சிவன்மூதூர்","தெரிவையர் தாம்வந் தருநட மாடுந்","திருவல மேவும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet pictures the body as a hut of desire and skin, flesh and fluids, with bones driven in as pegs, walking on two legs, and asks that, lest the life, like a fierce elephant, run loose along the paths of the senses when its time of speed comes, Murugan give him the strength to recite 'Vel, Kanda' faultlessly before the devotees who fall at his feet. The praise names Vishnu who played the flute and who came before an eyelid could blink when the elephant, in distress, cried 'Moolam, rule me', and Thiruvallam, Siva's ancient town round which Brahma, the directions, earth and sky revolve, where women come to dance.","n":"The body is a hut (குடில்) built of skin, flesh, water and bone pegs (என்புறு கீல்), a builder's inventory of the body. The life as an elephant on the paths of the senses is answered in the praise by Gajendra, the elephant saved when it called. The plea is for speech, the strength (விறல்) to praise aloud among devotees.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["yaakkai","pugazh","adiyaar"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:82"},"p:666":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":666,"t":["அதிக ராய்ப்பொரு ளீவார் நேர்படில்","ரசனை காட்டிக ளீயார் கூடினும்","அகல வோட்டிகள் மாயா ரூபிகள் ...... நண்புபோலே","அசட ராக்கிகள் மார்மே லேபடு","முலைகள் காட்டிகள் கூசா தேவிழும்","அழகு காட்டிக ளாரோ டாகிலு ...... மன்புபோலே","சதிர தாய்த்திரி வோயா வேசிகள்","கருணை நோக்கமி லாமா பாவிகள்","தருமு பேட்சைசெய் தோஷா தோஷிகள் ...... நம்பொணாத","சரச வார்த்தையி னாலே வாதுசெய்","விரக மாக்கி விடர்மு தேவிகள்","தகைமை நீத்துன தாளே சேர்வதும் ...... எந்தநாளோ","மதுரை நாட்டினி லேவாழ் வாகிய","அருகர் வாக்கினி லேசார் வாகிய","வழுதி மேற்றிரு நீறே பூசிநி .....மிர்ந்துகூனும்","மருவு மாற்றெதிர் வீறே டேறிட","அழகி போற்றிய மாறா லாகிய","மகிமையாற் சமண் வேரோ டேகெட ...... வென்றகோவே","புதிய மாக்கனி வீழ்தே னு஡றல்கள்","பகலி ராத்திரி யோயா ஆலைகள்","புரள மேற்செல வூரூர் பாயஅ ...... ணைந்துபோதும்","புகழி னாற்கடல் சூழ்பார் மீதினி","லளகை போற்பல வாழ்வால் வீறிய","புலவர் போற்றிய வேலு஡ர் மேவிய ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet lists the courtesans who show delight when men who give much wealth come and drive away those who do not give even after union, illusions who feign friendship and make men fools, show their breasts and their beauty to anyone as if in love, wandering prostitutes without compassion, and asks on what day he will leave their ways and join Murugan's feet. The praise tells how, in the Pandya land of Madurai, Murugan smeared holy ash on the king who leaned to the Jains, straightened his hump, sent the palm leaf up against the river's current, and, praised by the queen, destroyed the Jains to the root; and names Vellore, where mangoes fall, honey flows and sugar presses run day and night.","n":"அதிகராய்ப் பொருள் ஈவார் நேர்படில் ரசனை காட்டிகள், ஈயார் கூடினும் அகல ஓட்டிகள்: love measured to the gift. The praise tells the Madurai episode of Sambandar in full (the ash, the hump, the leaf against the stream, the queen, அழகி), all as Murugan's deed. Two words have corrupt glyphs.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["varaivinmagalir","selvam","thiruneeru","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:83"},"p:667":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":667,"t":["சேலால மொன்று செங்கண் வேலாலும் வென்று மைந்தர்","சீர்வாழ்வு சிந்தை பொன்ற ...... முதல்நாடித்","தேன்மேவு செஞ்சொ லின்சொல் தானோதி வந்த ணைந்து","தீராத துன்ப இன்ப ...... முறுமாதர்","கோலாக லங்கள் கண்டு மாலாகி நின்ற னன்பு","கூராமல் மங்கி யங்க ...... மழியாதே","கோள்கோடி பொன்ற வென்று நாடோ று நின்றி யங்கு","கூர்வாய்மை கொண்டி றைஞ்ச ...... அருள்தாராய்","மாலாலு ழன்ற ணங்கை யார்மாம தன்க ரும்பின்","வாகோட ழிந்தொ டுங்க ...... முதல்நாடி","வாழ்வான கந்த முந்த மாறாகி வந்த டர்ந்த","மாசூரர் குன்ற வென்றி ...... மயிலேறீ","மேலாகு மொன்ற மைந்த மேனாடர் நின்றி ரங்க","வேலாலெ றிந்து குன்றை ...... மலைவோனே","வேய்போல வுந்தி ரண்ட தோள்மாதர் வந்தி றைஞ்சு","வேலு஡ர்வி ளங்க வந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet tells of women whose eyes defeat the fish and the spear, who set out first to ruin young men's prosperity and minds, come with honeyed words and embrace them, and bring a sorrowful pleasure that never ends, and asks that, infatuated by their show, his love not wane and his limbs not waste, but that he be given grace to worship daily with keen truthfulness, having overcome countless evils. The praise names Murugan who rode the peacock and defeated the great Sura, who threw the vel at the hill while the gods stood in pity, and Vellore, where women with bamboo-like shoulders come to worship.","n":"தீராத துன்ப இன்பம்: the pleasure the women give is itself a sorrow. கூர்வாய்மை கொண்டு இறைஞ்ச: worship asked for is worship with truthfulness. கோள்கோடி பொன்ற வென்று may mean overcoming the planets, or slanders; the opening lines of the praise on Kama's cane bow are hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["kaamam","mayal","vaaimai","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:83"},"p:668":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":668,"t":["ஒருவரைச் சிறுமனைச் சயனமெத் தையினில்வைத்","தொருவரைத் தமதலைக் கடையினிற் சுழலவிட்","டொருவரைப் பரபரப் பொடுதெருத் திரியவிட் ...... டதனாலே","ஒருவருக் கொருவர்சக் களமையிற் சருவவிட்","டுருவுபத் திரமெடுத் தறையின்மற் புரியவிட்","டுயிர்பிழைப் பதுகருத் தளவிலுச் சிதமெனச் ...... செயுமானார்","தருமயற் ப்ரமைதனிற் றவநெறிக் கயலெனச்","சரியையிற் கிரியையிற் றவமுமற் றெனதுகைத்","தனமவத் தினிலிறைத் தெவருமுற் றிகழ்வுறத் ...... திரிவேனைச்","சகலதுக் கமுமறச் சகலசற் குணம்வரத்","தரணியிற் புகழ்பெறத் தகைமைபெற் றுனதுபொற்","சரணமெப் பொழுதுநட் பொடுநினைத் திடஅருட் ...... டருவாயே","குருமொழித் தவமுடைப் புலவரைச் சிறையில்வைத்","தறவுமுக் கிரம்விளைத் திடுமரக் கரைமுழுக்","கொடியதுர்க் குணஅவத் தரைமுதற் றுரிசறுத் ...... திடும்வேலா","குயில்மொழிக் கயல்விழித் துகிரிதழ்ச் சிலைநுதற்","சசிமுகத் திளநகைக் கனகுழற் றனகிரிக்","கொடியிடைப் பிடிநடைக் குறமகட் டிருவினைப் ...... புணர்வோனே","கருதுசட் சமயிகட் கமைவுறக் கிறியுடைப்","பறிதலைச் சமணரைக் குலமுதற் பொடிபடக்","கலகமிட் டுடலுயிர்க் கழுவினுச் சியினில்வைத் ...... திடுவோனே","கமுகினிற் குலையறக் கதலியிற் கனியுகக்","கழையின்முத் தமுதிரக் கயல்குதித் துலவுநற்","கனவயற் றிகழ்திருக் கரபுரத் தறுமுகப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women who keep one man on the bed in a small house, set another circling at their door, send a third rushing about the street, set lovers against each other until they draw knives and fight in the room, and think it all fitting so long as they make a living; deluded by them he abandoned the path of austerity, chariyai, kiriyai and tapas, threw away his wealth and wandered scorned, and asks grace that his sorrows end, good qualities come and he think always with love of the golden feet. The praise names the vel that ended the cruel demons who imprisoned the ascetic sages (புலவர்), Valli of cuckoo speech, and Murugan who impaled the shaven-headed Jains, at Karapuram of areca, plantain and cane.","n":"The opening is a sharp comic sketch of a courtesan managing four men at once (ஒருவரை ... ஒருவரை ... ஒருவரை), her motive given as உயிர்பிழைப்பது, earning a living. The loss is named in Saiva terms: சரியையில் கிரியையில் தவமும் அற்று. கழுவின் உச்சியில் வைத்திடுவோனே identifies Murugan with Sambandar at Madurai. Who the imprisoned புலவர் are (gods or poets) is not clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","sariyai","kiriyai","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:84"},"p:669":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":669,"t":["குலையமயி ரோதி குவியவிழி வீறு","குருகினிசை பாடி ...... முகமீதே","குறுவியர்வு லாவ அமுதினினி தான","குதலையுமொ ராறு ...... படவேதான்","பலவிதவி நோத முடனுபய பாத","பரிபுரமு மாட ...... அணைமீதே","பரிவுதரு மாசை விடமனமொ வாத","பதகனையு மாள ...... நினைவாயே","சிலைமலைய தான பரமர்தரு பால","சிகிபரிய தான ...... குமரேசா","திருமதுரை மேவு மமணர்குல மான","திருடர்கழு வேற ...... வருவோனே","கலின்வடிவ மான அகலிகைபெ ணான","கமலபத மாயன் ...... மருகோனே","கழனிநெடு வாளை கமுகொடிய மோது","கரபுரியில் வீறு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet pictures lovemaking on the bed, the hair loosened, eyes closing, cooing voice, sweat on the face, prattle and anklets on both feet, and asks Murugan to think of ruling even the sinner whose mind will not let go of that desire. The praise names Murugan the son of Siva whose bow is a mountain, the lord riding the peacock, who came to Madurai so the Jain thieves went to the stake, the nephew of Vishnu whose lotus foot made the stone Ahalya a woman again, at Karapuri where long valai fish leap at the areca palms.","n":"பதகனையும் ஆள நினைவாயே: the plea asks only to be thought of, the 'even' (உம்) marking the poet as least worthy. The Madurai impalement again gives Murugan Sambandar's deed. Ahalya's release by Rama's foot is told as Vishnu's.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","avaa","kutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:84"},"p:670":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":670,"t":["நிகரில் பஞ்ச பூதமு நினையு நெஞ்சு மாவியு","நெகிழ வந்து நேர்படு ...... மவிரோதம்","நிகழ்த ரும்ப்ர பாகர நிரவ யம்ப ராபர","நிருப அங்கு மாரவெ ...... ளெனவேதம்","சகர சங்க சாகர மெனமு ழங்கு வாதிகள்","சமய பஞ்ச பாதக ...... ரறியாத","தனிமை கண்ட தானகிண் கிணிய தண்டை சூழ்வன","சரண புண்ட ஡ணகம ...... தருள்வாயே","மகர விம்ப சீகர முகர வங்க வாரிதி","மறுகி வெந்து வாய்விட ...... நெடுவான","வழிதி றந்து சேனையு மெதிர்ம லைந்த சூரனு","மடிய இந்தி ராதியர் ...... குடியேறச்","சிகர துங்க மால்வரை தகர வென்றி வேல்விடு","சிறுவ சந்த்ர சேகரர் ...... பெருவாழ்வே","திசைதொ றும்ப்ர பூபதி திசைமு கன்ப ராவிய","திருவி ரிஞ்சை மேவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks for the lotus feet with tinkling anklets that are seen in solitude, beyond the five elements, the thinking heart and the life, which the Veda names as radiant, partless and beyond, and which the disputants of the five sects, roaring like the sea, do not know. The praise tells of the sea boiling and crying out, the way opened in the sky, Sura and his army killed so Indra and the gods could return home, and the vel that shattered the high peak, and names Murugan the son of Chandrasekhara at Thiruvirinchai, worshipped by the kings of every quarter and by four-faced Brahma.","n":"சமய பஞ்ச பாதகர் அறியாத: the feet are what the sect-disputants cannot know; the knowing is தனிமை கண்ட, in solitude. The run நிகழ்தரும் ப்ரபாகர நிரவயம் பராபர நிருப reads as Sanskrit names of the Absolute but its division is uncertain. திசைமுகன் பராவிய திருவிரிஞ்சை: the town's name is tied to Brahma (விரிஞ்சன்).","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["thiruvadi","samayam","vedham"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:84"},"p:671":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":671,"t":["பரவி யுனது பொற் கரமு முகமுமுத்","தணியு முரமுமெய்ப் ப்ரபையு மருமலர்ப்","பதமும் விரவுகுக் குடமு மயிலுமுட் ...... பரிவாலே","படிய மனதில்வைத் துறுதி சிவமிகுத்","தெவரு மகிழ்வுறத் தரும நெறியின்மெய்ப்","பசியில் வருமவர்க் கசன மொருபிடிப் ...... படையாதே","சருவி யினியநட் புறவு சொலிமுதற்","பழகு மவரெனப் பதறி யருகினிற்","சரச விதமளித் துரிய பொருள்பறித் ...... திடுமானார்","தமது மருகமதக் களப புளகிதச்","சயில நிகர்தனத் திணையின் மகிழ்வுறத்","தழுவி யவசமுற் றுருகி மருளெனத் ...... திரிவேனோ","கரிய நிறமுடைக் கொடிய அசுரரைக்","கெருவ மதமொழித் துடல்கள் துணிபடக்","கழுகு பசிகெடக் கடுகி அயில்விடுத் ...... திடுதீரா","கமல அயனுமச் சுதனும் வருணனக்","கினியு நமனுக் கரியு லுறையுமெய்க்","கணனு மமரரத் தனையு நிலைபெறப் ...... புரிவோனே","இரையு முததியிற் கடுவை மிடறமைத்","துழுவை யதளுடுத் தரவு பணிதரித்","திலகு பெறநடிப் பவர்மு னருளுமுத் ...... தமவேளே","இசையு மருமறைப் பொருள்கள் தினமுரைத்","தவனி தனி லெழிற் கரும முனிவருக்","கினிய கரபுரப் பதியி லறுமுகப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether, instead of holding in his mind with love Murugan's golden hands, faces, pearl-hung chest, radiance, flower feet, cock and peacock, and instead of giving in dharma even a handful of food to those who come in real hunger, he must wander deluded, embracing women who speak sweet friendship as if old acquaintances, give amorous play and snatch his wealth. The praise names the vel that cut down the dark demons until the vultures were full, Murugan who made Brahma, Vishnu, Varuna, Agni, Yama and Indra stand firm, the noble one who gave grace before Siva, the dancer with the poisoned throat, tiger skin and serpents, and the lord at Karapuram who daily tells the meaning of the Vedas to the sages.","n":"The neglect confessed is twofold: contemplation of the Lord's form and feeding the hungry (பசியில் வருமவர்க்கு அசனம் ஒருபிடி படையாதே). உரிய பொருள் பறித்திடுமானார: the women take what rightly belongs to him. உத்தமவேள் may mean Murugan taught before Siva the dancer, as elsewhere in the book.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","eegai","pasi","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:84"},"p:672":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":672,"t":["மருவு மஞ்சு பூத முரிமை வந்தி டாது","மலமி தென்று போட ...... அறியாது","மயல்கொ ளிந்த வாழ்வு அமையு மெந்த நாளும்","வகையில் வந்தி ராத ...... அடியேனும்","உ ருகி யன்பி னோடு உ னைநி னைந்து நாளும்","உ லக மென்று பேச ......அறியாத","உ ருவ மொன்றி லாத பருவம் வந்து சேர","உ பய துங்க பாத ...... மருள்வாயே","அரிவி ரிஞ்சர் தேட அரிய தம்பி ரானும்","அடிப ணிந்து பேசி ...... கடையூடே","அருளு கென்ற போது பொருளி தென்று காண","அருளு மைந்த ஆதி ...... குருநாதா","திரியு மும்பர் நீடு கிரிபி ளந்து சூரர்","செருவ டங்க வேலை ...... விடுவோனே","செயல மைந்த வேத தொனிமு ழங்கு வீதி","திருவி ரிஞ்சை மேவு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses that he does not know how to cast off the five elements as filth not his own, lives in delusion, and has not come by the proper way; he asks that, melting with love and thinking of Murugan daily, he reach the stage that has no form and does not even speak of 'the world', and be given the paired high feet. The praise names Murugan the first guru, whom Siva, the Lord whom Vishnu and Brahma could not find, bowed to and asked 'grant it', and to whom Murugan showed 'this is the meaning'; the vel that split the mountain; and Thiruvirinchai, whose streets resound with the Veda.","n":"உலகம் என்று பேச அறியாத உருவம் ஒன்றிலாத பருவம்: the goal is a state without form in which the world is not even named. The myth of Murugan teaching Siva is told with Siva bowing at his feet (அடிபணிந்து பேசி), though Om is not named. மலம் இது என்று போட: the elements of the body are to be thrown off as impurity.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["guru","gnaanam","anbu","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:84"},"p:673":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":673,"t":["கனவாலங் கூர்விழி மாதர்கள்","மனசாலஞ் சால்பழி காரிகள்","கனபோகம் போருக மாமிணை ...... முலைமீதே","கசிவாருங் கீறுகி ளாலுறு","வசைகாணுங் காளிம வீணிகள்","களிகூரும் பேயமு தூணிடு ...... கசுமாலர்","மனவேலங் கீலக லாவிகள்","மயமாயங் கீதவி நோதிகள்","மருளாருங் காதலர் மேல்விழு ...... மகளீர்வில்","மதிமாடம் வானிகழ் வார்மிசை","மகிழ்கூரும் பாழ்மன மாமுன","மலர்பேணுந் தாளுன வேயரு ...... ளருளாயோ","தனதானந் தானன தானன","எனவேதங் கூறுசொல் மீறளி","ததைசேர்தண் பூமண மாலிகை ...... யணிமார்பா","தகரேறங் காரச மேவிய","குகவீரம் பாகும ராமிகு","தகைசாலன் பாரடி யார்மகிழ் ...... பெருவாழ்வே","தினமாமன் பாபுன மேவிய","தனிமானின் தோளுட னாடிய","தினைமாவின் பாவுயர் தேவர்கள் ...... தலைவாமா","திகழ்வேடங் காளியொ டாடிய","ஜெகதீசங் கேசந டேசுரர்","திருவாலங் காடினில் வீறிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet heaps abuse on women with poison-sharp eyes and scheming minds, makers of blame, whose breasts show nail-scratches, who feed men a ghoulish food, who are wanton singers and charmers falling on deluded lovers, and asks that before his ruined mind delights in them on moonlit terraces he be granted the feet that flowers honour. The praise names Murugan's chest garlanded with flowers where bees hum 'thanathana' like the Veda, the hero Guha who rides the ram, who played with Valli's shoulders in the millet field, at Thiruvalangadu where Nataraja, the Lord of the world, danced with Kali.","n":"பேய் அமுது ஊணிடு may refer to the charge that courtesans feed men a love-philtre; the reading is a guess. தகர் ஏறு, riding the ram, is a rarer mount than the peacock. காளியொடு ஆடிய: the shrine's dance contest of Siva and Kali. Several compound epithets in the first half (கீலகலாவிகள், காளிமவீணிகள்) are hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","vanjam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:85"},"p:674":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":674,"t":["பொன்றாமன் றாக்கும் புதல்வரும்","நன்றாமன் றார்க்கின் றுறுதுணை","பொன்றானென் றாட்டம் பெருகிய ...... புவியூடே","பொங்காவெங் கூற்றம் பொதிதரு","சிங்காரஞ் சேர்த்திங் குயரிய","புன்கூடொன் றாய்க்கொண் டுறைதரு ...... முயிர்கோல","நின்றானின் றேத்தும் படிநினை","வுந்தானும் போச்சென் றுயர்வற","நிந்தாகும் பேச்சென் பதுபட ...... நிகழாமுன்","நெஞ்சாலஞ் சாற்பொங் கியவினை","விஞ்சாதென் பாற்சென் றகலிட","நின்தாள்தந் தாட்கொண் டருள்தர ...... நினைவாயே","குன்றால்விண் டாழ்க்குங் குடைகொடு","கன்றாமுன் காத்துங் குவலய","முண்டார்கொண் டாட்டம் பெருகிய ...... மருகோனே","கொந்தார்பைந் தார்த்திண் குயகுற","மின்தாள்சிந் தாச்சிந் தையில்மயல்","கொண்டேசென் றாட்கொண் டருளென ...... மொழிவோனே","அன்றாலங் காட்டண் டருமுய","நின்றாடுங் கூத்தன் திருவருள்","அங்காகும் பாட்டின் பயனினை ...... யருள்வாழ்வே","அன்பால்நின் தாட்கும் பிடுபவர்","தம்பாவந் தீர்த்தம் புவியிடை","அஞ்சாநெஞ் சாக்கந் தரவல ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"In a world where people celebrate sons and gold as their firm support, the poet asks that before the life that lives in this mean nest of a body, packed with fierce Death, is taken, and before people say his thought of praising Murugan has gone and speak scorn, the deeds swelling in his heart leave him and Murugan give his feet and rule him. The praise names Vishnu, who held up the hill as an umbrella, guarded the calves and swallowed the world, as uncle, Murugan who went infatuated to Valli and said 'rule me', and the grace that gives a fearless heart to those who bow with love, linked to the dancer who danced at Alangadu for the gods' life.","n":"புதல்வரும் ... பொன்தான் என்று: the false supports named are sons and gold. புன்கூடு, the mean nest, is the body; கூற்றம் பொதிதரு, Death is already packed inside it. The line on the Alangadu dancer and 'the fruit of the song' (பாட்டின் பயன்) is obscure; it may allude to a poet-saint's songs at the shrine.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","kootru","vinai","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:85"},"p:675":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":675,"t":["புவிபுனல் காலுங் காட்டி சிகியொடு வானுஞ் சேர்த்தி","புதுமன மானும் பூட்டி ...... யிடையூடே","பொறிபுல னீரைந் தாக்கி கருவிகள் நாலுங் காட்டி","புகல்வழி நாலைந் தாக்கி ...... வருகாயம்","பவவினை நூறுங் காட்டி சுவமதி தானுஞ் சூட்டி","பசுபதி பாசங் காட்டி ...... புலமாயப்","படிமிசை போவென் றோட்டி அடிமையை நீவந் தேத்தி","பரகதி தானுங் காட்டி ...... யருள்வாயே","சிவமய ஞானங் கேட்க தவமுநி வோரும் பார்க்க","திருநட மாடுங் கூத்தர் ...... மருகோனே","திருவளர் மார்பன் போற்ற திசைமுக னாளும் போற்ற","ஜெகமொடு வானங் காக்க ...... மயிலேறிக்","குவடொடு சூரன் தோற்க எழுகடல் சூதந் தாக்கி","குதர்வடி வேலங் கோட்டு ...... குமரேசா","குவலயம் யாவும் போற்ற பழனையி லாலங் காட்டில்","குறமகள் பாதம் போற்று ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet lays out how the body is made: earth, water, wind, fire and sky joined, the new mind yoked, the ten senses and organs, the four inner instruments, and the paths, until the body comes, bearing a hundred deeds of becoming and its own understanding, with Pati and the bond (pasa) shown; he asks Murugan to drive him off this world of illusion, come and raise his servant, and show the supreme state. The praise names the dancer who dances so that sages may hear and see Siva-knowledge, Lakshmi's lord and Brahma praising, the peacock ride that saved earth and sky, the vel that defeated Sura with his mountain and struck the seven seas, and Murugan who honours the hunter girl's feet at Palayanur Alangadu.","n":"The first half is a short tattva doctrine: the five elements, the ten பொறி-புலன், the four கருவிகள் (the inner instruments), and பசுபதி பாசம், the Lord and the bond, before the plea for பரகதி. சுவமதி and புகல்வழி நாலைந்து (perhaps twenty paths) are not clear. பழனையில் ஆலங்காடு names the shrine's village Palayanur.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","yaakkai","paasam","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:85"},"p:676":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":676,"t":["வடிவது நீலங் காட்டி முடிவுள காலன் கூட்டி","வரவிடு தூதன் கோட்டி ...... விடுபாசம்","மகனொடு மாமன் பாட்டி முதலுற வோருங் கேட்டு","மதிகெட மாயந் தீட்டி ...... யுயிர்போமுன்","படிமிசை தாளுங் காட்டி யுடலுறு நோய்பண் டேற்ற","பழவினை பாவந் தீர்த்து ...... னடியேனைப்","பரிவொடு நாளுங் காத்து விரிதமி ழாலங் கூர்த்த","பரபுகழ் பாடென் றாட்கொ ...... டருள்வாயே","முடிமிசை சோமன் சூட்டி வடிவுள ஆலங் காட்டில்","முதிர்நட மாடுங் கூத்தர் ...... புதல்வோனே","முருகவிழ் தாருஞ் சூட்டி யொருதனி வேழங் கூட்டி","முதல்மற மானின் சேர்க்கை ...... மயல்கூர்வாய்","இடியென வேகங் காட்டி நெடிதரு சூலந் தீட்டி","யெதிர்பொரு சூரன் தாக்க ...... வரஏகி","இலகிய வேல்கொண் டார்த்து உ டலிரு கூறன் றாக்கி","யிமையவ ரேதந் தீர்த்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks that before the dark-bodied messenger sent by Death throws his noose, and son, uncle, grandmother and kin hear of it, and life departs with the mind confused, Murugan show his feet on earth, remove the old deeds and sins that brought the body's disease, guard him daily, and rule him with the command 'sing the supreme praise in expansive Tamil'. The praise names Murugan the son of the dancer with the moon on his head at Alangadu, who brought the elephant to win the hunter girl he longed for, and whose vel split Sura in two and ended the gods' distress.","n":"விரிதமிழால் அம் கூர்த்த பரபுகழ் பாடு என்று ஆட்கொள்: the poet asks to be ruled by a command to sing, the song itself as the form of grace. நோய் பண்டு ஏற்ற பழவினை: disease is traced to old deeds. ஒருதனி வேழம் கூட்டி: the elephant that frightened Valli into Murugan's arms.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","vinai","pugazh","thamizh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:85"},"p:677":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":677,"t":["தவர்வாட் டோமர சூலந் தரியாக் காதிய சூருந்","தணியாச் சாகர மேழுங் ...... கிரியேழுஞ்","சருகாக் காய்கதிர் வேலும் பொருகாற் சேவலு நீலந்","தரிகூத் தாடிய மாவுந் ...... தினைகாவல்","துவர்வாய்க் கானவர் மானுஞ் சுரநாட் டாளொரு தேனுந்","துணையாத் தாழ்வற வாழும் ...... பெரியோனே","துணையாய்க் காவல்செய் வாயென் றுணராப் பாவிகள் பாலுந்","தொலையாப் பாடலை யானும் ...... புகல்வேனோ","பவமாய்த் தாணது வாகும் பனைகாய்த் தேமண நாறும்","பழமாய்ப் பார்மிசை வீழும் ...... படிவேதம்","படியாப் பாதகர் பாயன் றியுடாப் பேதைகள் கேசம்","பறிகோப் பாளிகள் யாருங் ...... கழுவேறச்","சிவமாய்த் தேனமுதூறுந் திருவாக் காலொளி சேர்வெண்","டிருநீற் றாலம ராடுஞ் ...... சிறியோனே","செழுநீர்ச் சேய்நதி யாரங் கொழியாக் கோமளம் வீசுந்","திருவோத் தூர்தனில் மேவும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The first half praises Murugan as the great one who lives without lack with his companions, the vel that burned dry Sura, the seven seas and seven hills, the fighting cock, the peacock, Valli of the hunters and Devayanai of heaven, and the poet asks whether he too must sing endless songs to sinners who do not know that Murugan guards as a companion. The praise names the little one who, with the holy word flowing with honeyed nectar and with the white holy ash, made the male palmyra bear fragrant fruit that fell to the ground and sent to the stake the Jains who read no Veda, wear only mats and pluck their hair, at Thiruvothur by the red river.","n":"The plea is about the poet's trade: பாவிகள் பாலும் தொலையாப் பாடலை யானும் புகல்வேனோ, singing to unworthy patrons. The praise gives Murugan two of Sambandar's deeds: the male palmyra made to fruit at Thiruvothur (பனை காய்த்து ... பழமாய்ப் பார்மிசை வீழும்படி) and the impalement of the Jains; திருநீற்றால் அமராடும் may refer to his curing the Pandya with ash.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iravu","pugazh","thiruneeru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:85"},"p:678":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":678,"t":["கார்க்கொத்த மேனிகடல் போற்சுற்ற மானவழி","காய்த்தொட்டொ ணாதவுரு ...... ஒருகோடி","காக்கைக்கு நாய்கழுகு பேய்க்கக்க மானவுடல்","காட்டத்தி னீளெரியி ...... லுறவானிற்","கூர்ப்பித்த சூலனத னாற்குத்தி யாவிகொடு","போத்துக்க மானகுறை ...... யுடையேனைக்","கூப்பிட்டு சாவருளி வாக்கிட்டு நாமமொழி","கோக்கைக்கு நூலறிவு ...... தருவாயே","போர்க்கெய்த்தி டாமறலி போற்குத்தி மேவசுரர்","போய்த்திக்கெ லாமடிய ...... வடிவேலாற்","பூச்சித்தர் தேவர்மழை போற்றுர்க்க வேபொருது","போற்றிச்செய் வார்சிறையை ...... விடுவோனே","பார்க்கொற்ற நீறுபுனை வார்க்கொக்க ஞானபர","னாய்ப்பத்தி கூர்மொழிகள் ...... பகர்வாழ்வே","பாக்கொத்தி னாலியலர் நோக்கைக்கு வேல்கொடுயர்","பாக்கத்தில் மேவவல ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of his body, dark as a cloud, with kin as many as the sea, food for crows, dogs, vultures and ghouls in the long fire of the burning ground, and of Death with his sharpened trident stabbing and carrying off the life, and asks Murugan to call him, grant him grace and give him the knowledge of texts to string his names into words. The praise names the vel that sent the demons fleeing to every quarter and freed the gods from prison, and Murugan, the jnana-master who spoke words full of devotion for those who wear the holy ash, at Pakkam where poets look on him with clusters of verse.","n":"காக்கைக்கு நாய் கழுகு பேய்க்கு அக்கமான உடல்: the body is already apportioned to scavengers. The plea is for skill in verse (நாமமொழி கோக்கைக்கு நூலறிவு), again the gift of song. சாவருளி is unclear: it may be 'grant (escape from) death' or a different division. நீறுபுனைவார்க்கு ஒக்க ஞானபரனாய்ப் பத்தி கூர் மொழிகள் பகர் may point to Sambandar's hymns.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","kootru","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:86"},"p:679":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":679,"t":["பாற்றுக் கணங்கள் தின்று தேக்கிட் டிடுங்கு ரம்பை","நோக்கிச் சுமந்து கொண்டு ...... பதிதோறும்","பார்த்துத் திரிந்து ழன்று ஆக்கத் தையுந்தெ ரிந்து","ஏக்கற்று நின்று நின்று ...... தளராதே","வேற்றுப் புலன்க ளைந்து மோட்டிப் புகழ்ந்து கொண்டு","வீட்டிற் புகுந்தி ருந்து ...... மகிழ்வேனோ","மாற்றற்ற பொன்து லங்கு வாட்சக்கி ரந்தெ ரிந்து","வாய்ப்புற்ற மைந்த சங்கு ...... தடிசாப","மாற்பொற்க லந்து லங்க நாட்டச்சு தன்ப ணிந்து","வார்க்கைத்த லங்க ளென்று ...... திரைமோதும்","பாற்சொற்ற டம்பு குந்து வேற்கட்சி னம்பொ ருந்து","பாய்க்குட் டுயின்ற வன்றன் ...... மருகோனே","பாக்குக் கரும்பை கெண்டை தாக்கித் தடம் படிந்த","பாக்கத் தமர்ந்தி ருந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether, rather than carrying this hut of a body that flocks of vultures will feed on, wandering from town to town, eyeing others' prosperity and standing in longing until he wilts, he will drive out the five senses, praise Murugan, and enter the house and rejoice. The praise names Vishnu, who bears the shining disc, sword, conch, mace and bow, whose hands the sea-waves wash, and who sleeps on the serpent bed in the milk sea, as uncle, at Pakkam where fish strike the areca and the cane.","n":"வீட்டில் புகுந்திருந்து மகிழ்வேனோ: வீடு is both home and release, so the end of wandering from town to town is the house of liberation. ஆக்கத்தையும் தெரிந்து ஏக்கற்று: craving is aroused by seeing others' wealth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","avaa","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:86"},"p:680":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":680,"t":["ஆலம்போ லெழுநீல மேலங்காய் வரிகோல","மாளம்போர் செயுமாய ...... விழியாலே","ஆரம்பால் தொடைசால ஆலுங்கோ புரவார","ஆடம்பார் குவிநேய ...... முலையாலே","சாலந்தாழ் வுறுமால ஏலங்கோர் பிடியாய","வேளங்கார் துடிநீப ...... இடையாலே","சாரஞ்சார் விலனாய நேகங்கா யமன்மீறு","காலந்தா னொழிவேது ...... உ ரையாயோ","பாலம்பால் மணநாறு காலங்கே யிறிலாத","மாதம்பா தருசேய ...... வயலு¡ரா","பாடம்பார் திரிசூல நீடந்தா கரவீர","பாசந்தா திருமாலின் ...... மருகோனே","வேலம்பார் குறமாது மேலும்பார் தருமாதும்","வீறங்கே யிருபாலு ...... முறவீறு","வேதந்தா வபிராம நாதந்தா வருள்பாவு","வேலங்கா டுறைசீல ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"Through eyes dark as poison that wage war, breasts and a slender waist, the poet, without support, takes body after body, and asks Murugan to say when that time will end. The praise names Murugan the child of the milk-fragrant mother, the lord of Vayalur, nephew of Vishnu, with the hunter girl and the heavenly maiden on either side, giver of the Veda and of nada, at Velangadu.","n":"அநேகம் காயம் ... காலம் தான் ஒழிவது ஏது: desire is tied directly to rebirth, the many bodies. The second half of the plea (சாலந்தாழ்வுறு மால ஏலங்கோர் பிடியாய) is hard to divide. வேலங்காடு is Verkadu.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["kaamam","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:87"},"p:681":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":681,"t":["கார்ச்சார்குழ லார்விழி யாரயி","லார்ப்பால்மொழி யாரிடை நூலெழு","வார்ச்சாரிள நீர்முலை மாதர்கள் ...... மயலாலே","காழ்க்காதல தாமன மேமிக","வார்க்காமுக னாயுறு சாதக","மாப்பாதக னாமடி யேனைநி ...... னருளாலே","பார்ப்பாயலை யோவடி யாரொடு","சேர்ப்பாயலை யோவுன தாரருள்","கூர்ப்பாயலை யோவுமை யாள்தரு ...... குமரேசா","பார்ப்பாவல ரோதுசொ லால்முது","நீர்ப்பாரினில் மீறிய கீரரை","யார்ப்பாயுன தாமரு ளாலொர்சொ ...... லருள்வாயே","வார்ப்பேரரு ளேபொழி காரண","நேர்ப்பாவச காரண மாமத","ஏற்பாடிக ளேயழி வேயுற ...... அறைகோப","வாக்காசிவ மாமத மேமிக","வூக்காதிப யோகம தேயுறு","மாத்தாசிவ பாலகு காவடி ...... யர்கள்வாழ்வே","வேர்காடவல் வேடர்கள் மாமக","ளார்க்கார்வநன் மாமகி ணாதிரு","வேற்காடுறை வேதபு ¡£சுரர் ...... தருசேயே","வேட்டார்மக வான்மக ளானவ","ளேட்டார்திரு மாமண வாபொனி","னாட்டார்பெரு வாழ்வென வேவரு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"Infatuated with women of dark hair, spear eyes, milk-sweet speech, thread waists and tender-coconut breasts, the poet calls himself a lustful great sinner and asks: will Murugan not look on him by grace, not join him to his devotees, not sharpen his grace; and as he once rescued Keeran, who excelled on the old sea-girt earth through the poets' words, will he not give him one word. The praise names Murugan as the one whose angry word destroyed the sects built on false causes, the Siva-faith and the yoga of the great, the life of devotees, the son of Vedapurisvara at Thiruverkadu, and the bridegroom of Indra's daughter.","n":"The plea is a run of questions ending in அலையோ (பார்ப்பாய் அலையோ, சேர்ப்பாய் அலையோ, கூர்ப்பாய் அலையோ). கீரரை ஆர்ப்பாய்: Nakkirar, whom tradition says Murugan freed from captivity, is the precedent for the poet's request for one word (ஒர் சொல்). The praise lines on sects (மத ஏற்பாடிகளே அழிவே உற) are uncertain in division.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["kaamam","adiyaar","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:87"},"p:682":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":682,"t":["அணிசெவ்வி யார்திரை சூழ்புவி","தனநிவ்வி யேகரை யேறிட","அறிவில்லி யாமடி யேனிட ...... ரதுதீர","அருள்வல்லை யோநெடு நாளின","மிருளில்லி லேயிடு மோவுன","தருளில்லை யோஇன மானவை ...... யறியேனே","குணவில்ல தாமக மேரினை","யணிசெல்வி யாயரு ணாசல","குருவல்ல மாதவ மேபெறு ...... குணசாத","குடிலில்ல மேதரு நாளெது","மொழிநல்ல யோகவ ரேபணி","குணவல் வாசிவ னேசிவ ...... குருநாதா","பணிகொள்ளி மாகண பூதமொ","டமர்கள்ளி கானக நாடக","பரமெல்லி யார்பர மேசுரி ...... தருகோவே","படரல்லி மாமலர் பாணம","துடைவில்லி மாமத னாரனை","பரிசெல்ரவி யார்மரு காசுர ...... முருகேசா","மணமொல்லை யாகி நகாகன","தனவல்லி மோகன மோடமடர்","மகிழ்தில்லை மாநட மாடின ...... ரருள்பாலா","மருமல்லி மாவன நீடிய","பொழில் மெல்லி காவன மாடமை","வடமுல்லை வாயிலின் மேவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet, calling himself without understanding, asks whether Murugan has the grace to remove his distress so he may reach the shore of this sea-girt earth, whether he will leave him long in the house of darkness, whether there is no grace for him, for he does not know what is fitting, and when the day will come that ends the hut of the body. The praise is a tangle of names: the Goddess at Arunachala, Murugan as Siva-guru, the son of Parameswari who plays with ghosts in the cactus forest of the burning ground, the nephew of Vishnu, the son of the one who danced at Thillai, at Vadamullaivayil of jasmine groves.","n":"The plea is a series of anxious questions (அருள்வல்லையோ, இடுமோ, அருளில்லையோ). குடில் இல்லமே தரு நாள் எது may ask when the body-hut will cease to be given, that is, when birth ends; the reading is tentative. Many words of the praise (குணவல்வா, பரிசெல்ரவி, மோடமடர்) look garbled.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","pedhaimai","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:88"},"p:683":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":683,"t":["சோதி மாமதி போல்முக முங்கிளர்","மேரு லாவிய மாமுலை யுங்கொடு","தூர வேவரு மாடவர் தங்கள்மு ...... னெதிராயே","சோலி பேசிமு னாளிலி ணங்கிய","மாதர் போலிரு தோளில்வி ழுந்தொரு","சூதி னால்வர வேமனை கொண்டவ ...... ருடன்மேவி","மோதி யேகனி வாயத ரந்தரு","நாளி லேபொருள் சூறைகள் கொண்டுபின்","மோன மாயவ மேசில சண்டைக ...... ளுடனேசி","மோச மேதரு தோதக வம்பியர்","மீதி லேமய லாகிம னந்தளர்","மோட னாகிய பாதக னுங்கதி ...... பெறுவேனோ","ஆதி யேயெனும் வானவர் தம்பகை","யான சூரனை மோதிய ரும்பொடி","யாக வேமயி லேறிமு னிந்திடு ...... நெடுவேலா","ஆயர் வாழ்பதி தோறுமு கந்துர","லேறி யேயுறி மீதளை யுங்கள","வாக வேகொடு போதநு கர்ந்தவன் ...... மருகோனே","வாதி னால்வரு காளியை வென்றிடு","மாதி நாயகர் வீறுத யங்குகை","வாரி ராசனு மேபணி யுந்திரு ...... நடபாதர்","வாச மாமல ரோனொடு செந்திரு","மார்பில் வீறிய மாயவ னும்பணி","மாசி லாமணி யிசர்ம கிழ்ந்தருள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women with moonlike faces who face men coming from afar, flirt, fall on their shoulders as if old lovers, take them home by a trick, give their lips while they plunder the wealth, then fall silent and pick quarrels, and asks whether he, a fool and sinner whose mind has failed in delusion for these deceitful wanton women, will gain salvation. The praise names the vel thrown from the peacock at Sura, the nephew of Krishna who climbed the mortar to steal butter from the hanging pots in the herders' villages, and Siva the Masilamani, the first Lord who defeated Kali in contest and whose dancing feet the sea king, Brahma and Vishnu worship.","n":"The courtesan's cycle is told in order: approach, false intimacy (முன்னாளில் இணங்கிய மாதர் போல்), a trick to bring him home, plunder while the lips are given, then silence and quarrels. மாசிலாமணி is the name of the Siva of Thirumullaivayil.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","kutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:88"},"p:684":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":684,"t":["மின்னிடைக லாப தொங்கலொ","டன்னமயில் நாண விஞ்சிய","மெல்லியர்கு ழாமி சைந்தொரு ...... தெருமீதே","மெள்ளவுமு லாவி யிங்கித","சொல்குயில்கு லாவி நண்பொடு","வில்லியல்பு ரூர கண்கணை ...... தொடுமோக","கன்னியர்கள் போலி தம்பெறு","மின்னணிக லார கொங்கையர்","கண்ணியில்வி ழாம லன்பொடு ...... பதஞான","கண்ணியிலு ளாக சுந்தர","பொன்னியல்ப தார முங்கொடு","கண்ணுறுவ ராம லின்பமொ ...... டெனையாள்வாய்","சென்னியிலு டாடி ளம்பிறை","வன்னியும ராவு கொன்றையர்","செம்மணிகு லாவு மெந்தையர் ...... குருநாதா","செம்முகஇ ராவ ணன்தலை","விண்ணுறவில் வாளி யுந்தொடு","தெய்விகபொ னாழி வண்கையன் ...... மருகோனே","துன்னியெதிர் சூரர் மங்கிட","சண்முகம தாகி வன்கிரி","துள்ளிடவெ லாயு தந்தனை ...... விடுவோனே","சொல்லுமுனி வோர்த வம்புரி","முல்லைவட வாயில் வந்தருள்","துல்யபர ஞான வும்பர்கள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks that he not fall into the snare of women who walk the street outdoing swans and peacocks, speak like cuckoos and shoot eye-arrows like Kama, false maidens with jewelled breasts, but that Murugan rule him in joy through the snare of loving knowledge of his feet, without the evil eye. The praise names Murugan the guru of the father who wears the crescent, vanni, serpent and konrai, the nephew of Rama whose arrow sent Ravana's heads to the sky, the vel that made the mountain leap, and the lord of supreme knowledge at northern Mullaivayil where sages do penance.","n":"கண்ணி is played twice: the women's snare (கண்ணியில் விழாமல்) and a 'snare' of knowledge of the feet (பதஞான கண்ணியில்), so the remedy is a better binding. கண்ணுறு வராமல் may mean free of the evil eye. சொல்லுமுனிவோர் தவம்புரி names sages doing penance at the shrine.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","gnaanam","thiruvadi","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:88"},"p:685":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":685,"t":["மருமல்லி யார்குழலின் ...... மடமாதர்","மருளுள்ளி நாயடிய ...... னலையாமல்","இருநல்ல வாகுமுன ...... தடிபேண","இனவல்ல மானமன ...... தருளாயோ","கருநெல்லி மேனியரி ...... மருகோனே","கனவள்ளி யார்கணவ ...... முருகேசா","திருவல்லி தாயமதி ...... லுறைவோனே","திகழ்வல்ல மாதவர்கள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A short song: the poet, a dog of a servant, asks not to wander thinking of the delusion of young women with fragrant jasmine hair, but to be given a mind able to honour the two good feet. The praise names Murugan the nephew of Vishnu, the husband of Valli, who dwells at Thiruvalithayam, lord of the great sages.","n":"இனவல்ல மானமனது அருளாயோ: the request is for a mind, not for release, a mind able (வல்ல) to honour the feet. நாயடியன், the dog-servant, is the poet's self-abasement.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","manam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:89"},"p:686":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":686,"t":["கரியமுகில் போலு மிருளளக பார","கயல்பொருத வேலின் ...... விழிமாதர்","கலவிகளில் மூழ்கி ம்ருகமத படீர","களனமுலை தோய ...... அணையூடே","விரகமது வான மதனகலை யோது","வெறியனென நாளு ...... முலகோர்கள்","விதரணம தான வகைநகைகள் கூறி","விடுவதன்முன் ஞான ...... அருள்தாராய்","அரிபிரமர் தேவர் முனிவர்சிவ யோகர்","அவர்கள்புக ழோத ...... புவிமீதே","அதிகநட ராஜர் பரவுகுரு ராஜ","அமரர்குல நேச ...... குமரேசா","சிரகர கபாலர் அரிவையொரு பாகர்","திகழ் கநக மேனி ...... யுடையாளர்","திருவளரு மாதி புரியதனில் மேவு","ஜெயமுருக தேவர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet, plunged in lovemaking with women of dark hair and carp eyes, their breasts scented with musk and sandal, asks for the grace of knowledge before the world laughs at him in every way as a madman reciting Kama's arts. The praise names Murugan the guru-king whom Nataraja praises and whom Brahma, Vishnu, gods, sages and Siva-yogis extol, the son of Siva who holds the skull, shares his body with a woman and is golden-bodied, at Adhipuri.","n":"The fear named is public mockery (உலகோர்கள் ... நகைகள் கூறி), not Death or hell. நடராஜர் பரவு குருராஜ: Siva the dancer praises Murugan as guru. ஆதிபுரி is Thiruvotriyur.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","pazhi","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:90"},"p:687":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":687,"t":["சொருபப்பிர காசவிசு வருபப்பிர மாகநிச","சுகவிப்பிர தேசரச ...... சுபமாயா","துலியப்பிர காசமத சொலியற்றர சாசவித","தொகைவிக்ரம மாதர்வயி ...... றிடையூறு","கருவிற்பிற வாதபடி யுருவிற்பிர மோதஅடி","களையெத்திடி ராகவகை ...... யதின்மீறிக்","கருணைப்பிர காசவுன தருளுற்றிட ஆசில்சிவ","கதிபெற்றிட ரானவையை ...... யொழிவேனோ","குருகுக்குட வாரகொடி செருவுக்கிர ஆதபயில்","பிடிகைத்தல ஆதியரி ...... மருகோனே","குமரப்பிர தாபகுக சிவசுப்பிர மாமணிய","குணமுட்டர வாவசுரர் ...... குலகாலா","திருவொற்றியு றாமருவு நகரொற்றியுர் வாரிதிரை","யருகுற்றிடு மாதிசிவ ...... னருள்பாலா","திகழுற்றிடு யோகதவ மிகுமுக்கிய மாதவர்க","ளிதயத்திட மேமருவு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"In a dense run of Sanskrit names of the Lord as self-luminous form, cosmic form and true bliss, the poet asks whether, not born again in the womb of women, and gaining the grace of the light of compassion, he will reach the blameless Siva-state and be rid of all troubles. The praise names Murugan with the cock banner, nephew of Vishnu with the discus, Kumara, Guha, Siva-Subrahmanya, death to the demons, the son of the first Siva at Thiruvotriyur by the sea, who dwells in the hearts of the great tapasvins of yoga.","n":"மாதர் வயிறு இடையூறு கருவிற் பிறவாதபடி: release is asked as never again entering a woman's womb. The opening compounds (சொருபப்பிரகாச விசுவரூப ப்ரமாக நிசசுக) read as Sanskrit epithets, but their divisions and the sense of துலியப்பிரகாச are uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","karu","thiruvarul","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:90"},"p:688":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":688,"t":["அமரு மமரரினி லதிக னயனுமரி","யவரும் வெருவவரு ...... மதிகாளம்","அதனை யதகரண விதன பரிபுரண","மமைய னவர்கரண ...... அகிலேச","நிமிர வருள்சரண நிபிட மதெனவுன","நிமிர சமிரமய ...... நியமாய","நிமிட மதனிலுண வலசி வசுதவர","நினது பதவிதர ...... வருவாயே","சமர சமரசுர அசுர விதரபர","சரத விரதஅயில் ...... விடுவோனே","தகுர்த தகுர்ததிகு திகுர்த திகுர்ததிகு","தரர ரரரரிரி ...... தகுர்தாத","எமர நடனவித மயிலின் முதுகில்வரு","மிமைய மகள்குமர ...... எமதீச","இயலி னியல்மயிலை நகரி லினிதுறையு","மெமது பரகுரவ ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The song opens with the great poison that came terrifying Brahma, Vishnu and the gods, and names the Lord of all whose instruments it fitted; the poet asks Murugan, son of Siva, to come in an instant to give him his own state. The praise names the vel thrown in the war of gods and demons, sounds the battle in drum syllables, and names Murugan, son of the Himalaya's daughter, who comes dancing on the peacock, our supreme guru dwelling at Mylai.","n":"The first eight lines are a run of Sanskrit-derived words (அதகரண விதன பரிபுரண ... நிமிர சமிரமய) whose division is uncertain; they seem to speak of Siva holding the poison and of the Lord's pervasion. நினது பதவி தர வருவாயே: the plea is for Murugan's own state. Lines 11 to 12 are drum syllables.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:91"},"p:689":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":689,"t":["அயிலொத் தெழுமிரு ...... விழியாலே","அமுதொத் திடுமரு ...... மொழியாலே","சயிலத் தெழுதுணை ...... முலையாலே","தடையுற் றடியனு ...... மடிவேனோ","கயிலைப் பதியரன் ...... முருகோனே","கடலக் கரைதிரை ...... யருகேசூழ்","மயிலைப் பதிதனி ...... லுறைவோனே","மகிமைக் கடியவர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A short song: the poet asks whether he too must perish, stopped by women's spear-like eyes, nectar-like speech and mountain breasts. The praise names Murugan the son of Siva of Kailas, who dwells at Mylai by the waves of the sea, the lord of his devotees.","n":"The three charms come in three short lines (விழியாலே, மொழியாலே, முலையாலே), and the plea is only a question, with no request.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kan"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:91"},"p:690":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":690,"t":["அறமி லாவதி பாதக வஞ்சத் ...... தொழிலாலே","அடிய னேன்மெலி வாகிம னஞ்சற் ...... றிளையாதே","திறகு லாவிய சேவடி வந்தித் ...... தருள்கூடத்","தினமு மேமிக வாழ்வுறு மின்பைத் ...... தருவாயே","விறல் சாசரர் சேனைக ளஞ்சப் ...... பொரும்வேலா","விமல மாதபி ராமித ருஞ்செய்ப் ...... புதல்வோனே","மறவர் வாணுதல் வேடைகொ ளும்பொற் ...... புயவீரா","மயிலை மாநகர் மேவிய கந்தப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks that he not grow weak and his mind waste by the deceitful deeds of a great sinner without dharma, but be given the joy of living each day, worshipping the red feet and joined to grace. The praise names the vel that frightened the demons' armies, the son of the pure Abhirami, the hero whose shoulders the hunter girl longed for, and Kanda at the great city of Mylai.","n":"அறமிலா அதிபாதக வஞ்சத் தொழில்: the confession is of conduct, not of women. The plea asks for a daily life (தினமுமே மிக வாழ்வுறும் இன்பம்), not release.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kutram","thiruvadi","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:91"},"p:691":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":691,"t":["இகல வருதிரை பெருகிய சலநிதி","நிலவு முலகினி லிகமுறு பிறவியி","னினிமை பெறவரு மிடருறு மிருவினை ...... யதுதீர","இசையு முனதிரு பதமலர் தனைமன","மிசைய நினைகிலி யிதமுற வுனதரு","ளிவர வுருகிலி அயர்கிலி தொழுகிலி ...... உ மைபாகர்","மகிழு மகவென அறைகிலி நிறைகிலி","மடமை குறைகிலி மதியுணர் வறிகிலி","வசன மறவுறு மவுனமொ டுறைகிலி ...... மடமாதர்","மயம தடரிட இடருறு மடியனு","மினிமை தருமுன தடியவ ருடனுற","மருவ அருள்தரு கிருபையின் மலிகுவ ...... தொருநாளே","சிகர தனகிரி குறமக ளினிதுற","சிலத நலமுறு சிலபல வசனமு","திறைய அறைபயி லறுமுக நிறைதரு ...... மருணீத","சிரண புரணவி தரணவி சிரவண","சரணு சரவண பவகுக சயனொளி","திரவ பரவதி சிரமறை முடிவுறு ...... பொருணீத","அகர உ கரதி மகரதி சிகரதி","யகர அருளதி தெருளதி வலவல","அரண முரணுறு மசுரர்கள் கெடஅயில் ...... விடுவோனே","அழகு மிலகிய புலமையு மகிமையும்","வளமு முறைதிரு மயிலையி லநுதின","மமரு மரகர சிவசுத அடியவர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"In a long run of negatives the poet confesses that he does not think of the feet that end the two deeds that bring sorrow in birth, does not melt in grace, does not tire, does not worship, does not proclaim Murugan as the child Uma's lord delights in, is not filled, does not lessen his folly, does not know true insight and does not dwell in the silence where speech ends; afflicted by women, he asks whether a day will come when grace joins him to the devotees. The praise names Murugan who spoke sweet words to the hunter girl, Saravanabhava, the meaning at the end of the Vedas, the one of A, U and M and the syllables of the mantra, whose vel destroyed the demons, at Mylai of beauty and learning.","n":"The confession is built from a dozen verbs ending in -கிலி (நினைகிலி, உருகிலி, அயர்கிலி, தொழுகிலி ...), a list of what devotion does by what the poet fails to do. வசனம் அறவுறு மவுனம்: silence beyond speech is among the things lacked. அகர உகர ... மகர names the parts of Om; the following சிகர ... யகர may be the syllables of the five-letter mantra, and the line is hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","mounam","adiyaar","piranavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:91"},"p:692":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":692,"t":["இணையதில தாமி ரண்டு கயல்களென வேபு ரண்டு","இருகுழையின் மீத டர்ந்து ...... அமராடி","இலகுசிலை வேள்து ரந்த கணையதிலு மேசி றந்த","இருநயனர் வாரி ணங்கு ...... மதபாரப்","பணைமுலையின் மீத ணிந்த தரளமணி யார்து லங்கு","பருவரதி போல வந்த ...... விலைமானார்","பயிலுநடை யாலு ழன்று அவர்களிட மோக மென்ற","படுகுழியி லேம யங்கி ...... விழலாமோ","கணகணென வீர தண்டை சரணமதி லேவி ளங்க","கலபமயில் மேலு கந்த ...... குமரேசா","கறுவிவரு சூர னங்க மிருபிளவ தாக விண்டு","கதறிவிழ வேலெ றிந்த ...... முருகோனே","மணிமகுட வேணி கொன்றை அறுகுமதி யாற ணிந்த","மலையவிலி னாய கன்றன் ...... ஓருபாக","மலையரையன் மாது தந்த சிறுவனென வேவ ளர்ந்து","மயிலைநகர் வாழ வந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes the price-women, eyes like paired carp rolling to their earrings to wage war and sharper than Kama's arrows, breasts hung with pearls, coming like Rati, and asks whether he should wander after their gait and fall into the pit called desire. The praise names Murugan who rides the peacock with anklets ringing, whose vel split Sura in two, and who grew as the child of the mountain king's daughter, half of Siva, and came to live at Mylai.","n":"விலைமானார், women with a price, names the trade, and மோகம் என்ற படுகுழி makes desire the pit the poet walks into following their gait.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:91"},"p:693":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":693,"t":["களபமணி யார முற்ற வனசமுலை மீது கொற்ற","கலகமத வேள்தொ டுத்த ...... கணையாலுங்","கனிமொழிமி னார்கள் முற்று மிசைவசைகள் பேச வுற்ற","கனலெனவு லாவு வட்ட ...... மதியாலும்","வளமையணி நீடு புஷ்ப சயனஅணை மீது ருக்கி","வனிதை மடல் நாடி நித்த ...... நலியாதே","வரியளியு லாவு துற்ற இருபுயம ளாவி வெற்றி","மலரணையில் நீய ணைக்க ...... வரவேணும்","துளபமணி மார்ப சக்ர தரனரிமு ராரி சர்ப்ப","துயிலதர னாத ரித்த ...... மருகோனே","சுருதிமறை வேள்வி மிக்க மயிலைநகர் மேவு முக்ர","துரகதக லாப பச்சை ...... மயில்வீரா","அளகைவணி கோர்கு லத்தில் வனிதையுயிர் மீள ழைப்ப","அருள்பரவு பாடல் சொற்ற ...... குமரேசா","அருவரையை நீறெ ழுப்பி நிருதர்தமை வேர றுத்து","அமரர்பதி வாழ வைத்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"In the voice of a woman in love with Murugan, the singer asks not to waste away each night on the flower bed, seeking to ride the madal, through the arrows Kama shoots at her sandalled breasts, the slander that the women speak, and the moon circling like fire, but that Murugan come and embrace her with his shoulders on the bed. The praise names Vishnu who holds the discus and sleeps on the serpent as uncle, the hero of the green peacock at Mylai of Vedic sacrifice, and the Kumara who sang the song of grace that called back to life the girl of the merchant clan.","n":"This is akam poetry, not the poet's confession: the classical torments of the lovesick girl, the gossip of women (இசைவசைகள்), the moon as fire, and the madal. அளகை வணிகோர் குலத்தில் வனிதை உயிர் மீள அழைப்ப அருள் பரவு பாடல் சொற்ற: Sambandar's raising of the merchant's daughter at Mylapore, given to Murugan, sung at the very shrine where the tradition places it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","alar","madal"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:91"},"p:694":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":694,"t":["கடிய வேக மாறாத விரத சூத ராபாதர்","கலக மேசெய் பாழ்மூடர் ...... வினைவேடர்","கபட வீன ராகாத இயல்பு நாடி யேநீடு","கனவி கார மேபேசி ...... நெறி பேணாக்","கொடிய னேது மோராது விரக சால §ம்முடு","குடிலின் மேவி யேநாளு ...... மடியாதே","குலவு தோகை மீதாறு முகமும் வேலு மீராறு","குவளை வாகும் நேர்காண ...... வருவாயே","படியி னோடு மாமேரு அதிர வீசி யேசேட","பணமு மாட வேநீடு ...... வரைசாடிப்","பரவை யாழி நீர்மோத நிருதர் மாள வானாடு","பதிய தாக வேலேவு ...... மயில்வீரா","வடிவு லாவி யாகாச மிளிர்ப லாவி னீள்சோலை","வனச வாவி பூவோடை ...... வயலோடே","மணிசெய் மாட மாமேடை சிகர மோடு வாகான","மயிலை மேவி வாழ்தேவர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet calls himself a cruel man who, not seeking a nature unlike that of wicked gamblers who break their vows, fools who make strife, hunters of deeds and deceivers, speaks great perversity and does not keep the path, and asks that, rather than dying daily in this hut of the body, he see Murugan come on the peacock with six faces, the vel and twelve shoulders. The praise tells how Murugan shook the earth and Meru, set Sesha's hoods dancing, struck the mountain and the sea, and killed the demons so the gods regained their land, at Mylai of jack groves, lotus ponds, fields and jewelled mansions.","n":"The list of vile people (சூதர், பாழ்மூடர், வினைவேடர், கபட ஈனர்) is the company the poet has resembled. The plea asks for a vision of the Lord's form (நேர்காண வருவாயே). One corrupt character in line 5 obscures a word.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["inam","kutram","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:91"},"p:695":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":695,"t":["திரைவார் கடல்சூழ்புவி தனிலே யுலகோரொடு","திரிவே னுனையோதுதல் ...... திகழாமே","தினநா ளுமுனேதுதி மனதா ரபினேசிவ","சுதனே திரிதேவர்கள் ...... தலைவாமால்","வரைமா துமையாள் தரு மணியே குகனேயென","அறையா வடியேனுமு ...... னடியாராய்","வழிபா டுறுவாரொடு அருளா தரமாயிடு","மகநா ளுளதோசொல ...... அருள்வாயே","இறைவா ரணதேவனு மிமையோ ரவரேவரு","மிழிவா கிமுனேயிய ...... லிலராகி","இருளா மனதேயுற அசுரே சர்களேமிக","இடரே செயவேயவ ...... ரிடர்தீர","மறமா வயிலேகொடு வுடலே யிருகூறெழ","மதமா மிகுசூரனை ...... மடிவாக","வதையே செயுமாவலி யுடையா யழகாகிய","மயிலா புரிமேவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether, instead of wandering with worldly people on this sea-girt earth without praising Murugan, he will have a great day when, crying daily with full heart 'Son of Siva, lord of the three gods, gem of Uma, Guha', he is joined by grace to the devotees who worship. The praise tells how Indra and all the gods were humbled and the demon lords oppressed them, and how Murugan's vel split the proud Sura in two, at Mayilapuri.","n":"The contrast is between two companies: உலகோரொடு திரிவேன், wandering with the worldly, and உன் அடியாராய் வழிபாடுறுவாரொடு, the worshipping devotees. The names to be cried out are given in the plea itself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adiyaar","vazhipaadu","inam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:91"},"p:696":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":696,"t":["நிரைதரு மணியணி யார்ந்த பூரித","ம்ருகமத களபகில் சாந்து சேரிய","இளமுலை யுரமிசை தோய்ந்து மாமல ...... ரணைமீதே","நெகிழ்தர அரைதுகில் வீழ்ந்து மாமதி","முகம்வெயர் வெழவிழி பாய்ந்து வார்குழை","யொடுபொர இருகர மேந்து நீள்வளை ...... யொலிகூர","விரைமலர் செறிகுழல் சாய்ந்து நூபுர","மிசைதர இலவிதழ் மோந்து வாயமு","தியல்பொடு பருகிய வாஞ்சை யேதக ...... வியனாடும்","வினையனை யிருவினை யீண்டு மாழ்கட","லிடர்படு சுழியிடை தாழ்ந்து போமதி","யிருகதி பெறஅருள் சேர்ந்து வாழ்வது ...... மொருநாளே","பரையபி நவைசிவை சாம்ப வீயுமை","யகிலமு மருளரு ளேய்ந்த கோமளி","பயிரவி திரிபுரை யாய்ந்த நூல்மறை ...... சதகோடி","பகவதி யிருசுட ரேந்து காரணி","மலைமகள் கவுரிவி தார்ந்த மோகினி","படர்சடை யவனிட நீங்கு றாதவள் ...... தருகோவே","குரைகடல் மறுகிட மூண்ட சூரர்க","ளணிகெட நெடுவரை சாய்ந்து தூளெழ","முடுகிய மயில்மிசை யூர்ந்து வேல்விடு ...... முருகோனே","குலநறை மலரளி சூழ்ந்து லாவிய","மயிலையி லுறைதரு சேந்த சேவக","குகசர வணபவ வாய்ந்த தேவர்கள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes lovemaking at length, the breasts scented with musk and sandal, the garment slipping, the sweating face, the earrings, the bangles, the loosened hair, the anklets, the drinking of the lips, and asks whether a day will come when he, a man of deeds sunk in the whirlpool of the deep sea of the two deeds, gains grace and lives. The praise is a long litany of the Goddess, Parai, Abhinavai, Sivai, Sambhavi, Uma, Bhairavi, Tripurai, Bhagavati who holds sun and moon, the mountain's daughter, Gauri, Mohini, who never leaves Siva's side, and names Murugan her son who rides the peacock and throws the vel, at Mylai.","n":"இருவினை ஈண்டும் ஆழ்கடல் இடர்படு சுழி: the two deeds are a deep sea with a whirlpool. இருகதி பெற may mean the two goals or a great goal; the sense is uncertain. The litany of the Goddess (பரை அபிநவை சிவை சாம்பவி) is longer than the praise of Murugan.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","vinai","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:91"},"p:697":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":697,"t":["வருமயி லொத்தவ ¡£வார் மாமுக","மதியென வைத்தவர் தாவா காமிகள்","வரிசையின் முற்றிய வாகா ராமியல் ...... மடமாதர்","மயலினி லுற்றவர் மோகா வாரிதி","யதனிடை புக்கவ ராளாய் நீணிதி","தருவிய லுத்தர்கள் மாடா மாமதி ...... மிக்முழ்கி","தருபர வுத்தம வேளே சீருறை","அறுமுக நற்றவ லீலா கூருடை","அயிலுறை கைத்தல சீலா பூரண ...... பரயோக","சரவண வெற்றிவி நோதா மாமணி","தருமர வைக்கடி நீதா வாமணி","மயிலுறை வித்தவு னாதா ராமணி ...... பெறுவேனோ","திரிரிரி தித்திதி தீதீ தீதிதி","தொகுதொகு தொத்தொகு தோதோ தோதிகு","திமிதிமி தித்திமி ஜேஜே தீதிமி ...... தொதிதீதோ","தெனவரி மத்தள மீதார் தேமுழ","திடுவென மிக்கியல் வேதா வேதொழு","திருநட மிட்டவர் காதே மூடிய ...... குருபோதம்","உ ரை செயு முத்தம வீரா நாரணி","உ மையவ ளுத்தர பூர்வா காரணி","உ றுஜக ரக்ஷணி நீரா வாரணி ...... தருசேயே","உ யர்வர முற்றிய கோவே யாரண","மறைமுடி வித்தக தேவே காரண","ஒருமயி லைப்பதி வாழ்வே தேவர்கள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses that, deluded by peacock-like women with moonlike faces and unfading lust, he became the servant of those sunk in the sea of desire and of misers holding great wealth, his mind sinking like an ox, and asks, with many epithets of Murugan, whether he will gain the jewel of his grace. The praise sounds the drum in syllables, names Murugan the hero who, while Siva danced, spoke the guru's teaching with the ears covered, the son of Narayani Uma who protects the world, the master of the end of the Vedas, and the life of Mylai.","n":"நீள்நிதி தரு வியலுத்தர்கள்: the poet's servitude is to misers as well as women, perhaps as a poet begging patrons. காதே மூடிய குருபோதம் உரைசெயும்: the teaching given to Siva is told as spoken with ears covered, the secret of the ear-whispered instruction, though Om is not named. The lines asking for the jewel (மாமணி ... பெறுவேனோ) are hard to divide, and two characters are corrupt.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["mayal","iravu","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:91"},"p:698":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":698,"t":["குசமாகி யாருமலை மரைமாநு ணூலினிடை","குடிலான ஆல்வயிறு ...... குழையூடே","குறிபோகு மீனவிழி மதிமாமு காருமலர்","குழல்கார தானகுண ...... மிலிமாதர்","புசவாசை யால்மனது உ னைநாடி டாதபடி","புலையேனு லாவிமிகு ...... புணர்வாகிப்","புகழான பூமிமிசை மடிவாயி றாதவகை","பொலிவான பாதமல ...... ரருள்வாயே","நிசநார ணாதிதிரு மருகாவு லாசமிகு","நிகழ்பொத மானபர ...... முருகோனே","நிதிஞான போதமர னிருகாதி லேயுதவு","நிபுணாநி சாசரர்கள் ...... குலகாலா","திசைமாமு காழியரி மகவான்மு னோர்கள்பணி","சிவநாத ராலமயில் ...... அமுதேசர்","திகழ்பால மாகமுற மணிமாளி மாடமுயர்","திருவான்மி யூர்மருவு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women with mountain breasts, thread-thin waists, carp eyes and moon faces who have no virtue, and confesses that, a base man, through desire for their arms he wandered in union without seeking Murugan, and asks for the shining lotus feet so that he does not perish on this famed earth. The praise names Murugan the nephew of Narayana, the expert who gave the treasure of knowledge into Siva's two ears, death to the demons, and the son of Siva worshipped by Brahma, Vishnu and Indra, at Thiruvanmiyur of jewelled mansions.","n":"அரன் இருகாதிலே உதவு நிபுணா: the teaching of Siva is placed in his ears, without naming Om. மடிவாய் இறாத வகை may ask that he not die before reaching the feet; the phrase is hard to divide. ஆலமயில் அமுதேசர் may name the shrine's Siva.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kutram","guru","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:92"},"p:699":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":699,"t":["ஆதவித பாரமுலை மாதரிடை நூல்வயிற","தாலிலையெ னாமதன ...... கலைலீலை","யாவும்விளை வானகுழி யானதிரி கோணமதி","லாசைமிக வாயடிய ...... னலையாமல்","நாதசத கோடிமறை யோலமிடு நூபுரமு","னானபத மாமலரை ...... நலமாக","நானநுதி னாதினமு மேநினைய வேகிருபை","நாடியரு ளேயருள ...... வருவாயே","சீதமதி யாடரவு வேரறுகு மாஇறகு","சீதசல மாசடில ...... பரமேசர்","சீர்மைபெற வேயுதவு கூர்மைதரு வேலசிவ","சீறிவரு மாவசுரர் ...... குலகாலா","கோதைகுற மாதுகுண தேவமட மாதுமிரு","பாலுமுற வீறிவரு ...... குமரேசா","கோசைநகர் வாழவரு மீசடியர் நேசசரு","வேசமுரு காவமரர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks that he not wander with great desire for the women's bodies where all of Kama's arts and play are born, but that grace come so that he thinks every day of the great lotus feet whose anklets cry out with a hundred crore Vedas. The praise names Siva, who wears the cool moon, the serpent, arugu grass, the feather and Ganga in his matted hair, the sharp vel given for Siva's glory, the death of the demons, Valli and Devayanai on either side, and Murugan beloved of devotees at Kosainagar.","n":"நாத சதகோடி மறை ஓலமிடு நூபுரம்: the anklets of the feet sound the Vedas, so the feet are where scripture itself cries out. The opening names the woman's body plainly (திரிகோணம்) as the place where Kama's arts arise.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","thiruvadi","vedham"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:93"},"p:700":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":700,"t":["தலங்களில் வருங்கன இலங்கொடு மடந்தையர்","தழைந்தவு தரந்திகழ் ...... தசமாதஞ்","சமைந்தனர் பிறந்தனர் கிடந்தன ரிருந்தனர்","தவழ்ந்தனர் நடந்தனர் ...... சிலகாலந்","துலங்கு நலபெண்களை முயங்கினர் மயங்கினர்","தொடுந்தொழி லுடன்தம ...... க்ருகபாரஞ்","சுமந்தன ரமைந்தனர் குறைந்தன ரிறந்தனர்","சுடும்பினை யெனும்பவ ...... மொழியேனோ","இலங்கையி லிலங்கிய இலங்களு ளிலங்கரு","ளிலெங்கணு மிலங்கென ...... முறையோதி","இடுங்கனல் குரங்கொடு நெடுங்கடல் நடுங்கிட","எழுந்தருள் முகுந்தனன் ...... மருகோனே","பெலங்கொடு விலங்கலு நலங்கஅ யில்கொண்டெறி","ப்ரசண்டக ரதண்டமிழ் ...... வயலு¡ரா","பெரும்பொழில் கரும்புக ளரம்பைகள் நிரம்பிய","பெருங்குடி மருங்குறை ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet tells a life as people live it: mothers bore them ten months in the womb; they were born, lay, sat, crawled and walked; for a while they embraced fine women and were deluded, carried the burden of the household with their work, settled, declined, died and were burned; he asks whether he will not end this birth that ends in burning. The praise names Mukunda, who in Lanka, with the monkey and his fire and the long sea trembling, came forth, as uncle, the lord of Tamil Vayalur whose fierce hand threw the vel that shook the mountain, at Perungudi of groves, cane and plantain.","n":"The first half is a list of plural past verbs (சமைந்தனர் பிறந்தனர் கிடந்தனர் இருந்தனர் தவழ்ந்தனர் நடந்தனர் ... இறந்தனர்), so the life is everyone's and passes in a breath; only the last line turns to the poet. The household burden (க்ருகபாரம் சுமந்தனர்) is part of the round, not its remedy. The line on Lanka (இலங்கையில் இலங்கிய இலங்களுள் இலங்கு அருள்) plays on the sound இலங்கு and is hard to read.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["piravi","nilaiyaamai","karu","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:94"},"p:701":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":701,"t":["தோடு றுங் குழை யாலே கோல்வளை","சூடு செங்கைக ளாலே யாழ்தரு","கீத மென்குர லாலே தூமணி ...... நகையாலே","தூம மென்குழ லாலே யூறிய","தேனி லங்கித ழாலே யாலவி","லோச னங்களி னாலே சோபித ...... அழகாலே","பாட கம்புனை தாளா லேமிக","வீசு தண்பனி நீரா லேவளர்","பார கொங்கைக ளாலே கோலிய ...... விலைமாதர்","பாவ கங்களி னாலே யான்மயல்","மூழ்கி நின்றய ராதே நூபுர","பாத பங்கய மீதே யாள்வது ...... கருதாயோ","நாட ருஞ்சுடர் தானா வோதுசி","வாக மங்களி னானா பேதவ","நாத தந்த்ரக லாமா போதக ...... வடிவாகி","நால்வி தந்தரு வேதா வேதமு","நாடி நின்றதொர் மாயா தீதம","னோல யந்தரு நாதா ஆறிரு ...... புயவேளே","வாட யங்கியவேலா லேபொரு","சூர்த டிந்தருள் வீரா மாமயி","லேறு கந்தவி நோதா கூறென ...... அரனார்முன்","வாச கம்பிற வாதோர் ஞானசு","கோத யம்புகல் வாசா தேசிக","மாடை யம்பதி வாழ்வே தேவர்கள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet lists the charms of the price-women one by one, earrings, bangled hands, voice like the yazh, smile, scented hair, honeyed lips, wide eyes, anklets, rose water and breasts, and asks Murugan whether he will not think to rule him at the lotus feet with anklets, so that he does not tire, sunk in infatuation through their wiles. The praise names Murugan as the light hard to reach that the Sivagamas proclaim, the form of nada, tantra and kala, the one sought by the four Vedas, beyond maya, who brings the stilling of the mind, the hero whose vel cut down Sura, and the teacher who, when Siva said 'tell it', spoke the dawn of the bliss of knowledge, at Madaiyampathi.","n":"The first half repeats ஆலே, 'by', after each charm, a catalogue that ends in விலைமாதர் பாவகங்கள், the price-women's feigned feelings. The praise is unusually doctrinal: சிவாகமங்கள், நாத தந்த்ர கலா, மாயாதீத, மனோலயம். கூறென அரனார் முன் ... ஞானசுகோதயம் புகல் தேசிக: Murugan teaching Siva, without naming Om.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","mayal","aagamam","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:95"},"p:702":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":702,"t":["விலைய றுக்கவு முலைம றைக்கவு","மணந்துன் றுஞ்செ ழுந்தார்","புனைமு கிற்குழல் தனைய விழ்க்கவும்","விடங்கஞ் சஞ்ச ரஞ்சேர்","விழிவெ ருட்டவு மொழிபு ரட்டவு","நிணந்துன் றுஞ்ச லம்பா ...... யுதிரநீ ருடனே","வெளியி னிற்கவும் வலிய முட்டரை","யெதிர்ந்தும் பின்தொ டர்ந்தே","யிலைசு ணப்பொடி பிளவெ டுத்திடை","திரும்பும் பண்ப ரன்றே","யெனவு ரைத்தவர் தமைவ ரப்பணி","யுடன்கொண் டன்பு டன்போய் ...... சயனபா யலின்மேல்","கலைநெ கிழ்க்கவு மயல்வி ளைக்கவு","நயங்கொண் டங்கி ருந்தே","குணுகி யிட்டுள பொருள்ப றித்தற","முனிந்தங் கொன்று கண்டே","கலக மிட்டவ ரகல டித்தபின்","வரும்பங் கங்கு ணங்கோர் ...... புதியபே ருடனே","கதைகள் செப்பவும் வலச மர்த்திகள்","குணங்கண் டுந்து ளங்கா","மனித னிற்சிறு பொழுது முற்றுற","நினைந்துங் கண்டு கந்தே","கடிம லர்ப்பத மணுகு தற்கறி","விலன் பொங்கும் பெரும்பா ...... தகனையா ளுவையோ","சிலைத னைக்கொடு மிகஅ டித்திட","மனந்தந் தந்தண ந்தா","மரைம லர்ப்பிர மனைந டுத்தலை","யரிந்துங் கொண்டி ரந்தே","திரிபு ரத்தெரி புகந கைத்தருள்","சிவன்பங் கங்கி ருந்தா ...... ளருளுமா முருகா","செருவி டத்தல கைகள் தெனத்தென","தெனந்தெந் தெந்தெ னந்தா","எனஇ டக்கைகள் மணிக ணப்பறை","டிகுண்டிங் குண்டி குண்டா","டிகுகு டிக்குகு டிகுகு டிக்குகு","டிகுண்டிங் குண்டி குண்டீ ...... யெனஇரா வணனீள்","மலையெ னத்திகழ் முடிகள் பத்தையு","மிரண்டஞ் சொன்ப தொன்றேய்","பணைபு யத்தையு மொருவ கைப்பட","வெகுண்டம் பொன்றெ றிந்தோன்","மதலை மைத்துன அசுர ரைக்குடல்","திறந்தங் கம்பி ளந்தே ...... மயிலின்மேல் வருவாய்","வயல்க ளிற்கய லினமி குத்தெழு","வரம்பின் கண்பு ரண்டே","பெருக யற்கொடு சொரியு நித்தில","நிறைந்தெங் குஞ்சி றந்தே","வரிசை பெற்றுயர் தமனி யப்பதி","யிடங்கொண் டின்பு றுஞ்சீர் ...... இளைய நாயகனே."],"p":"","r":"The poet tells the courtesans' whole routine: setting the price, hiding the breasts, loosening the hair, darting eyes, twisting speech, standing out in the open, accosting rough men and following them, saying 'isn't it wrong to turn back after taking the betel?', taking them to bed, then snatching all their wealth, quarrelling and beating them away, and taking up a new man with new stories; he asks whether Murugan will rule him, a great sinner unshaken even after seeing their ways, who lacks the knowledge to approach the fragrant feet even for a moment. The praise names Siva, struck with a bow, who cut off Brahma's middle head and begged with it and burned the triple city with a smile, with Uma at his side, sounds the battle in drum syllables, names Rama who cut Ravana's ten heads and twenty arms with one arrow, and Murugan who comes on the peacock, the young lord of a golden town amid pearl-strewn fields.","n":"The routine runs from விலையறுக்கவும், fixing the price, to புதிய பேருடனே கதைகள் செப்பவும், the next customer, a full cycle of the trade. குணங்கண்டும் துளங்கா: the poet's fault is that seeing it all does not shake him. The song ends with இளைய நாயகனே rather than பெருமாளே and names the town only as தமனியப்பதி, the golden town.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","selvam","vanjam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:95"},"p:703":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":703,"t":["ஆதிமுத னாளி லென்றன் தாயுடலி யி ருந்து","ஆகமல மாகி நின்று ...... புவிமீதில்","ஆசையுட னேபி றந்து நேசமுட னேவ ளர்ந்து","ஆளழக னாகி நின்று ...... விளையாடிப்","பூதலமெ லாம லைந்து மாதருட னேக லந்து","பூமிதனில் வேணு மென்று ...... பொருள்தேடிப்","போகமதி லேயு ழன்று பாழவெளநேரகெய் தாம லுன்றன்","பூவடிகள் சேர அன்பு ...... தருவாயே","சீதைகொடு போகு மந்த ராவணனை மாள வென்ற","தீரனரி நார ணன்றன் ...... மருகோனே.","தேவர்முநி வோர்கள் கொண்டல் மாலரிபிர் மாவு நின்று","தேடஅரி தான வன்றன் ...... முருகோனே","கோதைமலை வாழு கின்ற நாதரிட பாக நின்ற","கோமளிய நாதி தந்த ...... குமரேசா","கூடிவரு சூரர் தங்கள் மார்பையிரு கூறு கண்ட","கோடைநகர் வாழ வந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet tells his life from the first day as an embryo in his mother's body, born with desire, raised with love, grown into a handsome man who played, roamed the earth, mixed with women and sought wealth thinking it needful, and asks to be given love to reach the flower feet before he falls into ruinous hell. The praise names Murugan nephew of Narayana who killed Ravana for carrying off Sita, son of the one whom gods, sages, Vishnu and Brahma could not find in their search, and the lord who came to dwell at Kodainagar.","n":"The plea is a life in stages (ஆகமலமாகி நின்று, பிறந்து, வளர்ந்து, ஆளழகனாகி, மாதருடனே கலந்து, பொருள்தேடி), each a step toward the hell it ends in. What it asks for is love (அன்பு) that reaches the feet, not release as such. கோதைமலை in the praise is unclear to me.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karu","naragam","anbu","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:96"},"p:704":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":704,"t":["சாலநெடு நாள்ம டந்தை காயமதி லேய லைந்து","சாமளவ தாக வந்து ...... புவிமீதே","சாதகமு மான பின்பு சீறியழு தேகி டந்து","தாரணியி லேத வழ்ந்து ...... விளையாடிப்","பாலனென வேமொ ழிந்து பாகுமொழி மாதர் தங்கள்","பாரதன மீத ணைந்து ...... பொருள்தேடிப்","பார்மிசையி லேயு ழன்று பாழ்நரகெய் தாம லொன்று","பாதமலர் சேர அன்பு ...... தருவாயே","ஆலமமு தாக வுண்ட ஆறுசடை நாதர் திங்கள்","ஆடரவு பூணர் தந்த ...... முருகோனே","ஆனைமடு வாயி லன்று மூலமென வோல மென்ற","ஆதிமுதல் நார ணன்றன் ...... மருகோனே","கோலமலர் வாவி யெங்கு மேவுபுனம் வாழ்ம டந்தை","கோவையமு தூற லுண்ட ...... குமரேசா","கூடிவரு சூர டங்க மாளவடி வேலெறிந்த","கோடைநகர் வாழ வந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A twin of 703: the poet tells of lying long in the woman's body, coming to earth in pains near death, the birth rites, crying, crawling and playing, being called a child, then lying on the heavy breasts of sweet-voiced women, seeking wealth and wandering, and asks for love to reach the one flower foot before he falls into hell. The praise names Siva who drank the poison as nectar, Narayana who answered the elephant's cry of 'Root' in the pond, Valli whose lips' nectar Murugan drank, and the vel that felled Sura, at Kodainagar.","n":"சாதகமுமான பின்பு: the birth rite (jatakarma) is one stage among the others. The elephant crying மூலமே is the story of Gajendra, as in 25.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karu","naragam","anbu","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:96"},"p:705":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":705,"t":["ஏறா னாலே நீறாய் மாயா","வேளே வாசக் ...... கணையாலே","ஏயா வேயா மாயா வேயா","லாமே ழோசைத் ...... தொளையாலே","மாறா யூறா யீறாய் மாலாய்","வாடா மானைக் ...... கழியாதே","வாராய் பாராய் சேரா யானால்","வாடா நீபத் ...... தொடைதாராய்","சீறா வீறா ஈரேழ் பார்சூழ்","சீரார் தோகைக் ...... குமரேசா","தேவா சாவா மூவா நாதா","தீரா கோடைப் ...... பதியோனே","வேறாய் மாறா யாறா மாசூர்","வேர்போய் வீழப் ...... பொருதோனே","வேதா போதா வேலா பாலா","வீரா வீரப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A short akam song in the voice of someone pleading for a lovesick girl: Kama, who did not die though the rider of the bull burned him to ash, wounds her with his fragrant arrows, and the flute with its seven-note holes torments her; it asks Murugan not to let this deer-like girl, wasting into enmity, hurt, end and longing, perish, but to come, look on her and join her, or else give his unfading kadamba garland. The praise names the Kumara of the peacock who circles the fourteen worlds, the deathless, ageless lord of Kodai, who fought until great Sura fell uprooted, the hero of the vel.","n":"The song is a chain of short rhyming words (வாராய் பாராய் சேராய்; தேவா சாவா மூவா நாதா), so the splitting is often a guess. Asking for the god's garland when he will not come is a standing akam motif of the Thiruppugazh. I read the voice as a mother's or companion's; it is not said.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:96"},"p:706":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":706,"t":["ஞால மெங்கும் வளைத்த ரற்று ...... கடலாலே","நாளும் வஞ்சி யருற்று ரைக்கும் ...... வசையாலே","ஆலமுந்து மதித்த ழற்கும் ...... அழியாதே","ஆறி ரண்டு புயத்த ணைக்க ...... வருவாயே","கோல மொன்று குறத்தி யைத்த ...... ழுவுமார்பா","கோடை யம்பதி யுற்று நிற்கு ...... மயில்வீரா","கால னஞ்ச வரைத்தொ ளைத்த ...... முதல்வானோர்","கால்வி லங்கு களைத்த றித்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A short akam song: a lovesick girl, or one speaking for her, asks Murugan to come and embrace her with his twelve arms so that she does not perish from the sea that roars around the world, the slander the women speak each day, and the moon that burns like poison. The praise names the chest that embraced the hunter girl, the peacock hero who stands at Kodai, and the lord who pierced the mountain so that Death took fright and loosed the fetters of the gods.","n":"The torments are the classical ones: the sea's roar, the town's gossip (வஞ்சியர் உற்று உரைக்கும் வசை) and the moon that burns like the poison (ஆலம்). The speaker is not named.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","alar"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:96"},"p:707":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":707,"t":["தோழமை கொண்டுச லஞ்செய் குண்டர்கள்","ஓதிய நன்றிம றந்த குண்டர்கள்","சூழ்விர தங்கள்க டிந்த குண்டர்கள் ...... பெரியோரைத்","தூஷண நிந்தைப கர்ந்த குண்டர்கள்","ஈவது கண்டுத கைந்த குண்டர்கள்","சூளுற வென்பதொ ழிந்த குண்டர்கள் ...... தொலையாமல்","வாழநி னைந்துவ ருந்து குண்டர்கள்","நீதிய றங்கள்சி தைந்த குண்டர்கள்","மானவ கந்தைமி குந்த குண்டர்கள் ...... வலையாலே","மாயையில் நின்றுவ ருந்து குண்டர்கள்","தேவர்கள் சொங்கள்க வர்ந்த குண்டர்கள்","வாதைந மன்றன்வ ருந்தி டுங்குழி ...... விழுவாரே","ஏழு மரங்களும் வன்கு ரங்கெனும்","வாலியு மம்பர மும்ப ரம்பரை","ராவண னுஞ்சது ரங்க லங்கையு ...... மடைவேமுன்","ஈடழி யும்படி சந்த்ர னுஞ்சிவ","சூரிய னுஞ்சுர ரும்ப தம்பெற","ராம சரந்தொடு புங்க வன்திரு ...... மருகோனே","கோழி சிலம்பந லம்ப யின்றக","லாப நடஞ்செய மஞ்சு தங்கிய","கோபுர மெங்கும்வி ளங்கு மங்கல ...... வயலு¡ரா","கோமள அண்டர்கள் தொண்டர் மண்டலர்","வேல னெனும்பெய ரன்பு டன்புகழ்","கோடை யெனும்பதி வந்த இந்திரர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The song lists the brutes (குண்டர்கள்) who fall into the pit where Yama torments: those who make friends and then betray, who forget the good done them, who break their vows, who revile the great, who obstruct giving when they see it, who abandon their oaths, who long to live forever, who wreck justice and dharma, who swell with pride, who suffer in maya, and who seize the property of the gods. The praise names Rama, whose arrow pierced the seven trees and Vali and brought down Ravana's Lanka, as uncle, Murugan of Vayalur with its towers and peacocks, and the lord of Kodai praised by gods and devotees.","n":"There is no plea and no confession: this is a moral catalogue in the manner of the didactic books, and Yama's pit is its only sanction. ஈவது கண்டு தகைந்த, blocking another's giving, and ஓதிய நன்றி மறந்த, forgetting a good done, are Kural sins. வயலூரா in the praise names another shrine.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["naragam","nanri","nindhai","agandhai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:96"},"p:708":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":708,"t":["தோடப் பாமற் றோய்தப் பாணிச்","சூழ்துற் றார்துற் ...... றழுவாருந்","தூரப் போகக் கோரப் பாரச்","சூலப் பாசச் ...... சமனாரும்","பாடைக் கூடத் தீயிற் றேறிப்","பாழ்பட் டேபட் ...... டழியாதே","பாசத் தேனைத் தேசுற் றார்பொற்","பாதத் தேவைத் ...... தருள்வாயே","ஆடற் சூர்கெட் டோடத் தோயத்","தாரச் சீறிப் ...... பொரும்வேலா","ஆனைச் சேனைக் கானிற் றேனுக்","காரத் தாரைத் ...... தரும்வீரா","கூடற் பாடிக் கோவைப் பாவைக்","கூடப் பாடித் ...... திரிவோனே","கோலச் சாலிச் சோலைச் சீலக்","கோடைத் தேவப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet pictures the kin who gather weeping, Yama come with his terrible heavy trident and noose, the bier and the fire, and asks that he not perish thus in ruin but that Murugan, in grace, set him, a man in bonds, at the golden feet. The praise names the vel that fought until Sura fled into the sea, the hero who gave a garland to the honey-girl in the forest, the one who goes singing at Koodal, and the lord of Kodai with its rice fields and groves.","n":"பாசத்தேனை: the poet calls himself the one in bonds, so Yama's noose (பாசம்) and his own bondage share a word. கூடற் பாடிக் கோவைப் பாவைக் கூடப் பாடித் திரிவோனே may refer to Murugan singing at Madurai, or to a kovai poem; I can't identify it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kootru","paasam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:96"},"p:709":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":709,"t":["வாசித்த நூல்ம தங்கள் பேசிக்கொ டாத விந்து","வாய்மைப்ர காச மென்று ...... நிலையாக","மாசிக்க பால மன்றில் நாசிக்கு ளோடு கின்ற","வாயுப்பி ராண னொன்று ...... மடைமாறி","யோசித்த யாரு டம்பை நேசித்து றாத லைந்து","ரோமத்து வார மெங்கு ...... முயிர்போக","யோகச்ச மாதி கொண்டு மோகப்ப சாசு மண்டு","லோகத்தில் மாய்வ தென்று ...... மொழியாதோ","வீசப்ப யோதி துஞ்ச வேதக்கு லால னஞ்ச","மேலிட்ட சூர்த டிந்த ...... கதிர்வேலா","வீரப்ர தாப பஞ்ச பாணத்தி னால்ம யங்கி","வேடிச்சி காலி லன்று ...... விழுவோனே","கூசிப்பு காவொ துங்க மாமற்றி காத ரிந்த","கூளப்பு ராரி தந்த ...... சிறியோனே","கோழிப்ப தாகை கொண்ட கோலக்கு மார கண்ட","கோடைக்குள் வாழ வந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether, with the bindu that read books and argued creeds cannot give made steady as the light of truth, and the breath that runs in the nose turned into the hall of the skull, he will not by yogic samadhi end this dying again and again in a world crowded by the demon of delusion, the life leaving through every pore. The praise names the vel that dried the sea and frightened Brahma, the potter of the Vedas, the Murugan who, dazed by Kama's five arrows, fell at the hunter girl's feet, the son of the destroyer of the cities, and the lord of the cock banner who came to dwell at Kodai.","n":"A rare yogic plea: knowledge from books and sects (வாசித்த நூல் மதங்கள்) is set against the bindu-light, and the breath is to be turned (மடைமாறி) into the skull (கபால மன்றில்). The middle lines (யோசித்த யாருடம்பை நேசித்துறாது அலைந்து) are hard to divide, and whether the dying through the pores is ordinary death or the yogi's departure is not clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","praanan","samaadhi","vindhu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:96"},"p:710":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":710,"t":["அனுத்தே னேர்மொழி யாலே மாமய","லுடைத்தார் போலவு மோர்நா ளானதி","லடுத்தே தூதுகள் நூறா றானதும் ...... விடுவார்கள்","அழைத்தே வீடினி லேதா னேகுவர்","நகைத்தே மோடிக ளாவார் காதலொ","டடுத்தே மாமுலை மீதே மார்புற ...... அணைவார்பின்","குனித்தே பாகிலை யீவார் பாதியில்","கடிப்பார் வாயிதழ் வாய்நீ ரானது","குடிப்பார் தேனென நானா லீலைகள் ...... புரிவார்கள்","குறித்தே மாமய லாலே நீள்பொருள்","பறிப்பா ராசுகள் சூழ்மா பாதக","குணத்தார் மாதர்கள் மேலா சாவிட ...... அருள்வாயே","வனத்தே வேடுவர் மாதா மோர்மினை","யெடுத்தே தான்வர வேதான் யாவரும்","வளைத்தே சூழவு மோர்வா ளால்வெலும் ...... விறல்வீரா","மலர்த்தே னோடையி லோர்மா வானதை","பிடித்தே நீள்கர வாதா டாழியை","மனத்தா லேவிய மாமா லானவர் ...... மருகோனே","சினத்தே சூரர்கள் போராய் மாளவு","மெடுத்தோர் வேல்விடு தீரா தாரணி","திருத்தோ ளாஇரு பாதா தாமரை ...... முருகோனே","திருத்தேர் சூழ்மதி ளேரார் தூபிக","ளடுக்கார் மாளிகை யேநீ ளேருள","திருப்போ ரூருறை தேவா தேவர்கள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet tells how women with honeyed speech feign great infatuation, send hundreds of messengers in a day, call a man home, laugh and flirt, embrace him, bend to give betel and bite it halfway, drink the saliva of his lips as honey and play many games, all meaning to snatch his wealth through his infatuation, and asks for grace so that his desire for these sinful women leaves him. The praise names the hero who, carrying off the hunter girl, won with one sword against the hunters who surrounded him, the nephew of Vishnu who sent his discus by thought to the elephant seized in the lotus pond, and the lord of Tiruporur with its chariots, walls and towers.","n":"நீள்பொருள் பறிப்பார்: the whole performance, messengers and betel included, is aimed at wealth, which is what makes these courtesans in the Kural's sense. The sword fight with the hunters is a less common detail of the Valli story.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:97"},"p:711":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":711,"t":["உ ருக்கார் வாளி கண்கள் பொருப்பார் வார்த னங்கள்","உ கப்பார் வால சந்த்ர ...... னுதனூலாம்","உ ருச்சேர் நீண்ம ருங்குல் பணைத்தோ ளோதி கொண்ட","லுவப்பா மேல்வி ழுந்து ...... திரிவோர்கள்","அருக்கா மாதர் தங்கள் வரைக்கே யோடி யின்ப","வலைக்கே பூணு நெஞ்ச ...... னதிபாவி","அசட்டால் மூடு கின்ற மசக்கால் மாயு மிந்த","அவத்தா லீன மின்றி ...... யருள்வாயே","எருக்கார் தாளி தும்பை மருச்சேர் போது கங்கை","யினைச்சூ டாதி நம்பர் ...... புதல்வோனே","இருக்கா லேநி னைந்து துதிப்பார் நாவி னெஞ்சி","லிருப்பா யானை தங்கு ...... மணிமார்பா","செருக்கா லேமி குந்த கடற்சூர் மாள வென்ற","திறற்சேர் வேல்கை கொண்ட ...... முருகோனே","தினைக்கோர் காவல் கொண்ட குறத்தேன் மாது பங்க","திருப்போ ரூர மர்ந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet calls himself a great sinner whose heart runs to the women with arrow-eyes, mountain breasts, crescent foreheads and cloud-dark hair, and wears their net of pleasure, and asks for grace so that he is spared the disgrace of dying in the dullness and folly that cover him. The praise names Murugan son of the lord who wears erukku, thali and thumbai flowers and the Ganga, who dwells in the tongues and hearts of those who praise him in Vedic verse, whose vel destroyed Sura in the sea, and Valli's partner at Tiruporur.","n":"அசட்டால் மூடுகின்ற மசக்கால் மாயும் இந்த அவத்தால்: what the poet fears is a pointless death in stupor, more than the women themselves. இருக்காலே நினைந்து may mean 'by the Rig Veda' or 'with the verses'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","mayal","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:97"},"p:712":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":712,"t":["சீரு லாவிய வோதிம மான மாநடை மாமயில்","சேய சாயல்க லாமதி ...... முகமானார்","தேனு லாவிய மாமொழி மேரு நேரிள மாமுலை","சேலு லாவிய கூர்விழி ...... குமிழ்நாசி","தாரு லாவிய நீள்குழல் வேய ளாவிய தோளியர்","சார்பி லேதிரி வேனைநி ...... னருளாலே","சாம வேதியர் வானவ ரோதி நாண்மலர் தூவிய","தாளில் வீழ வினாமிக ...... அருள்வாயே","காரு லாவிய நீள்புன வேடர் மால்வரை மீதுறை","காவல் மாதினொ டாவல்செய் ...... தணைவோனே","காண ஆகம வேதபு ராண நூல்பல வோதிய","கார ணாகரு ணாகர ...... முருகோனே","போரு லாவிய சூரனை வாரி சேறெழ வேல்விடு","பூப சேவக மாமயில் ...... மிசையோனே","போதன் மாதவன் மாதுமை பாதி யாதியு மேதொழு","போரி மாநகர் மேவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes the women he roams beside, swan-gaited and peacock-graceful, moon-faced, honey-tongued, with breasts like Meru, carp eyes and bamboo shoulders, and asks that by Murugan's grace he fall at the feet on which Sama Vedic priests and gods strew fresh flowers. The praise names Murugan who embraced the girl guarding the field on the hunters' hill, the cause who spoke the Agamas, Vedas and Puranas, the compassionate one whose vel churned the sea to mud against Sura, worshipped by Brahma, Vishnu, Uma and Siva, at Porur.","n":"The description of the women is a catalogue of conventional likenesses, part for part, and takes half the song. வினாமிக is obscure to me.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:97"},"p:713":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":713,"t":["திமிர மாமன மாமட மடமை யேனிட ராணவ","திமிர மேயரி சூரிய ...... திரிலோக","தினக ராசிவ காரண பனக பூஷண ஆரண","சிவசு தாவரி நாரணன் ...... மருகோனே","குமரி சாமளை மாதுமை அமலி யாமளை பூரணி","குணக லாநிதி நாரணி ...... தருகோவே","குருகு காகும ரேசுர சரவ ணாசக ளேசுர","குறவர் மாமக ளாசைகொள் ...... மணியேசம்","பமர பாரப்ர பாருண படல தாரக மாசுக","பசுர பாடன பாளித ...... பகளேச","பசித பாரண வாரண துவச ஏடக மாவயில்","பரவு பாணித பாவல ...... பரயோக","சமப ராமத சாதல சமய மாறிரு தேவத","சமய நாயக மாமயில் ...... முதுவீர","சகல லோகமு மாசறு சகல வேதமு மேதொழு","சமர மாபுரி மேவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A song of praise alone: Murugan is the sun that destroys the darkness of ego in the poet's dark and foolish mind, the sun of the three worlds, the son of Siva and nephew of Narayana, the child given by Uma under her many names (Kumari, Syamala, Amali, Yamala, Purani, Narayani), Guha, Saravana, the jewel desired by the hunters' daughter, the lord of the six sects and their gods, worshipped by all worlds and all Vedas at Samarapuri.","n":"ஆணவ திமிரமே அரி சூரிய: the one doctrinal phrase makes Murugan the sun that removes anava, the first of the bonds. சமய மாறிரு தேவத சமய நாயக reads as lord of the six sects. Many of the compound epithets in the middle (பமர பாரப்ர பாருண, பசுர பாடன பாளித) are Sanskrit I can't divide with confidence. சமர மாபுரி translates the shrine's name, போர் being battle.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["malam","saththi","samayam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:97"},"p:714":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":714,"t":["சுருதி மறைக ளிருநாலு திசையி லதிபர் முநிவேர்கள்","துகரி லிருடி யெழுபேர்கள் ...... சுடர்மூவர்","சொலவில் முடிவில் முகியாத பகுதி புருடர் நவநாதர்","தொலைவி லுடுவி னுலகோர்கள் ...... மறையோர்கள்","அரிய சமய மொருகோடி அமாரர் சரணர் சதகோடி","அரிய மயனு மொருகொடி ...... யிவர்கூடி","அறிய அறிய அறியாத அடிக ளறிய அடியேனும்","அறிவு ளறியு மறிவூர ...... அருள்வாயே","வரைகள் தவிடு பொடியாக நிருதர் பதியு மழிவாக","மகர சலதி அளறாக ...... முதுசூரும்","மடிய அலகை நடமாட விஜய வனிதை மகிழ்வாக","மவுலி சிதறி இரைதேடி ...... வருநாய்கள்","நரிகள் கொடிகள் பசியாற உ திர நதிக ளலைமோத","நமனும் வெருவி யடிபேண ...... மயிலேறி","நளின வுபய கரவேலை முடுகு முருக வடமேரு","நகரி யுறையு மிமையோர்கள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet lists those who seek and cannot know the Lord's feet, the Vedas, the guardians of the eight directions, the seven rishis, the three lights, the nine Naths, the star-worlds, the priests, a crore of sects and hundreds of crores of gods, and asks that he, a servant, be given the knowledge that knows within knowledge, so that he may know them. The praise is a battle: mountains turn to powder, the sea to mud, Sura dies, ghouls dance, dogs, jackals and crows feed on rivers of blood, and even Yama bows, as Murugan rides the peacock, lord of the gods at Vada Meru.","n":"அறிய அறிய அறியாத அடிகள் ... அறிவுள் அறியும் அறிவு: the feet that all knowing fails to know are to be known by a knowing within knowing, given by grace. The long list of seekers is there to set off the poet's 'even I' (அடியேனும்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","arivu","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:98"},"p:715":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":715,"t":["தோலெ லும்பு சீந ரம்பு பீளை துன்று கோழை பொங்கு","சொரி பிண்ட மாயு ருண்டு ...... வடிவான","தூல பங்க காயம் வம்பி லேசு மந்து நான்மெ லிந்து","சோரு மிந்த நோய கன்று ...... துயராற","ஆல முண்ட கோன கண்ட லோக முண்ட மால்வி ரிஞ்ச","னார ணங்க ளாக மங்கள் ...... புகழ்தாளும்","ஆன னங்கள் மூவி ரண்டு மாறி ரண்டு தோளு மங்கை","யாடல் வென்றி வேலு மென்று ...... நினைவேனோ","வால சந்த்ர சூடி சந்த வேத மந்த்ர ரூபி யம்பை","வாணி பஞ்ச பாணி தந்த ...... முருகோனே","மாயை யைந்து வேக மைந்து பூத மைந்து நாத மைந்து","வாழ்பெ ருஞ்ச ராச ரங்க ...... ளுறைவோனே","வேலையன்பு கூர வந்த ஏக தந்த யானை கண்டு","வேடர் மங்கை யோடி யஞ்ச ...... அணைவோனே","வீர மங்கை வாரி மங்கை பாரின் மங்கை மேவு கின்ற","மேரு மங்கை யாள வந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of the gross body built of skin, bone, pus, nerves, rheum and phlegm, rolled into a lump, which he carries uselessly and wastes in, and asks whether, his sickness and sorrow gone, he will think of the feet praised by Siva, Vishnu, Brahma, the Vedas and Agamas, and of the six faces, twelve shoulders and victorious vel. The praise names Murugan son of the goddess who wears the crescent and is the form of Vedic mantra, the one who dwells in the five mayas, five elements and five nadas and all that moves and stays, who embraced the hunter girl when the elephant frightened her, at Meru Mangai.","n":"தூல பங்க காயம்: the 'gross' body, a technical word that implies the subtle body beside it. The elephant that frightens Valli into Murugan's arms is Ganesha helping his brother.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","thiruvadi","viyaabagam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:98"},"p:716":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":716,"t":["நீள்புயற் குழல்மாதர் பேரினிற் க்ருபையாகி","நேசமுற் றடியேனு ...... நெறிகெடாய்","நேமியிற் பொருள்தேடி யோடியெய்த் துளம்வாடி","நீதியிற் சிவவாழ்வை ...... நினையாதே","பாழினுக் கிரையாய நாமம்வைத் தொருகோடி","பாடலுற் றிடவேசெய் ...... திடுமோச","பாவியெப் படிவாழ்வ னேயர்கட் குளதான","பார்வைசற் றருளோடு ...... பணியாயோ","ஆழியிற் றுயில்வோனு மாமலர்ப் பிரமாவு","மாகமப் பொருளோரு ...... மனைவோரும்","ஆனைமத் தகவோனும் ஞானமுற் றியல்வோரு","மாயிரத் திருநூறு ...... மறையோரும்","வாழுமுத் தரமேருர் மேவியற் புதமாக","வாகுசித் திரதோகை ...... மயிலேறி","மாறெனப் பொருசூர னீறெழப் பொரும்வேல","மான்மகட் குளனான ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses that he fell in love with long-haired women and lost his way, ran over the earth seeking wealth until his heart withered, never thought justly of the life in Siva, and, a deceiving sinner, made a crore of songs giving names to what only feeds ruin; he asks how he will live, and whether Murugan will not look on him with the gaze he has for his devotees. The praise names Uttaramerur, where Vishnu, Brahma, those who know the Agamas, Ganesha, jnanis and the twelve hundred Vedic priests dwell, and Murugan who came there on the peacock, whose vel burned Sura to ash, the lord of the deer's daughter.","n":"பாழினுக்கு இரையாய நாமம் வைத்து ஒருகோடி பாடல்: I read this as the poet's regret at having sung praise of perishable men or things, a confession about his own art; the line could be divided otherwise. ஆயிரத்திருநூறு மறையோர் is a local detail of the shrine.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","selvam","pugazh","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:98"},"p:717":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":717,"t":["மாதர் கொங்கையில் வித்தா ரத்திரு","மார்பி லங்கியல் முத்தா ரத்தினில்","வாச மென்குழ லிற்சே லைப்பொரும் ...... விழிவேலில்","மாமை யொன்றும லர்த்தாள் வைப்பினில்","வாகு வஞ்சியில் மெய்த்தா மத்தினில்","வானி ளம்பிறை யைப்போல் நெற்றியில் ...... மயலாகி","ஆத ரங்கொடு கெட்டே யிப்படி","ஆசை யின்கட லுக்கே மெத்தவும்","ஆகி நின்றுத வித்தே நித்தலும் ...... அலைவேனோ","ஆறி ரண்டுப ணைத்தோ ளற்புத","ஆயி ரங்கலை கத்தா மத்திப","னாயு ழன்றலை கிற்பே னுக்கருள் ...... புரிவாயே","சாத னங்கொடு தத்தா மெத்தென","வேந டந்துபொய் பித்தா வுத்தர","மேதெ னும்படி தற்காய் நிற்பவர் ...... சபையூடே","தாழ்வில் சுந்தர னைத்தா னொற்றிகொள்","நீதி தந்திர நற்சார் புற்றருள்","சால நின்றுச மர்த்தா வெற்றிகொ ...... ளரன்வாழ்வே","வேத முங்கரி யைச்சூழ் நித்தமும்","வேள்வி யும்புவி யிற்றா பித்தருள்","வேர்வி ழும்படி செய்த்தேர் மெய்த்தமிழ் ...... மறையோர்வாழ்","மேரு மங்கையி லத்தா வித்தக","வேலொ டும்படை குத்தா வொற்றிய","வேடர் மங்கைகொள் சித்தா பத்தர்கள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet tells how, infatuated by women's breasts and pearl strings, fragrant hair, carp-like eyes, feet, waist and crescent foreheads, he is ruined and wanders daily in the sea of desire, and asks the twelve-shouldered master of a thousand arts to grant grace to him who wanders as a man of the middle sort. The praise names Murugan the treasure of Siva, who came with a deed into the assembly, called a madman and asked for his answer, and by that just stratagem claimed faultless Sundarar for his own, and the lord of Meru Mangai, home of priests of true Tamil, with its Vedas and sacrifices.","n":"சாதனங்கொடு ... பித்தா ... சுந்தரனைத் தான் ஒற்றிகொள்: the story of Siva appearing with a bond to claim Sundarar as his hereditary slave, in which Sundarar called him பித்தா. It is the book's model of service by claim (thondu). மத்திபன் may mean a middling or mediocre man, and the lines on the priests who establish Vedas and sacrifices are hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mayal","kaamam","thondu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:98"},"p:718":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":718,"t":["குதிபாய்ந்தி ரத்தம் வடிதொ ளைத்தொக்","கிந்த்ரி யக்கு ரம்பை ...... வினைகூர்தூர்","குணபாண்ட முற்ற கிலமெ னக்கைக்","கொண்டி ளைத்த யர்ந்து ...... சுழலாதே","உ திதாம்ப ரத்தை யுயிர்கெ டப்பொற்","கிண்கி ணிச்ச தங்கை ...... விதகீத","உ பயாம்பு யப்பு ணையையி னிப்பற்","றுங்க ருத்தை யென்று ...... தருவாயே","கதைசார்ங்க கட்கம் வளைய டற்சக்","ரந்த ரித்த கொண்டல் ...... மருகோனே","கருணாஞ்ச னக்க மலவி ழிப்பொற்","பைம்பு னக்க ரும்பின் ...... மணவாளா","மதனாந்த கர்க்கு மகவெ னப்பத்","மந்த னிற்பி றந்த ...... குமரேசா","மதுராந்த கத்து வடதி ருச்சிற்","றம்ப லத்த மர்ந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks that he not tire and whirl taking as everything this hut of the senses, skin and holes from which blood spurts and drains, packed with deeds, a vessel of filth, but that he be given the thought to hold the raft of the twin lotus feet with golden bells and anklets. The praise names Murugan nephew of the cloud-dark one who bears mace, Sarnga bow, sword, conch and discus, bridegroom of the cane-sweet girl of the millet field, born in the lotus as the son of the destroyer of Kama, lord of the northern Chirrambalam at Madurantakam.","n":"உபய அம்புயப் புணை: the feet as a raft across the sea of births, an image the Kural also uses (kural:10). குணபாண்டம் is perhaps 'corpse-vessel' (குணபம், corpse). The line உதித அம்பரத்தை உயிர் கெட is obscure to me.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:99"},"p:719":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":719,"t":["சயிலாங்க னைக்கு ருகியி டப்பக்","கங்கொ டுத்த கம்பரி¨ன் ...... வெகுசாரி","சதிதாண்ட வத்தர் சடையி டத்துக்","கங்கை வைத்த நம்பர் ...... உ ரைமாளச்","செயல்மாண்டு சித்த மவிழ நித்தத்","த்வம்பெ றப்ப கர்ந்த ...... வுபதேசஞ்","சிறியேன்த னக்கு முரைசெ யிற்சற்","றுங்கு ருத்து வங்கு ...... றையுமோதான்","அயில்வாங்கி யெற்றி யுததி யிற்கொக்","கன்ற னைப்பி ளந்து ...... சுரர்வாழ","அகிலாண்ட முற்று நொடியி னிற்சுற்","றுந்தி றற்ப்ர சண்ட ...... முழுநீல","மயில்தாண்ட விட்டு முதுகு லப்பொற்","குன்றி டித்த சங்க்ர ...... மவிநோதா","மதுராந்த கத்து வடதி ருச்சிற்","றம்ப லத்த மர்ந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether Murugan's guruship would be diminished in the least if he spoke to him, small as he is, the instruction he gave Siva, who gave the mountain-daughter his left side and keeps the Ganga in his hair, so that Siva's speech ended, his action died, his mind loosened and he attained the eternal. The praise names the vel that split the mango in the sea so the gods might live, the blue peacock that circled the whole universe in an instant, and the one who broke the ancient golden hill, at Madurantakam.","n":"The teaching of Siva is described by its effect, not named as Om: உரைமாள, செயல் மாண்டு, சித்தம் அவிழ, நித்தத்துவம் பெற. The plea is witty: சற்றும் குருத்துவம் குறையுமோ, would it cost the guru anything to teach the poet too? கொக்கு is the mango tree that Sura became.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["guru","gnaanam","mounam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:99"},"p:720":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":720,"t":["மனைமாண்சுத ரான சுணங்கரு","மனம்வேந்திணை யான தனங்களு","மடிவேன்றனை யீண அணங்குறு ...... வம்பராதி","மயமாம்பல வான கணங்குல","மெனப்ராந்தியும் யானென தென்றுறு","வணவாம்பிர மாத குணங்குறி ...... யின்பசார","இனவாம்பரி தான்ய தனம்பதி","விடஏன்றெனை மோன தடம்பர","மிகுதாம்பதி காண கணங்கன ...... வும்பரேசா","இடவார்ந்தன சானு நயம்பெறு","கடகாங்கர சோண வியம்பர","இடாமங்கன தாளரு ளும்படி ...... யென்றுதானோ","தனதாந்தன தான தனந்தன","தெனதோங்கிட தோன துனங்கிட","தனவாம்பர மான நடம்பயில் ...... எம்பிரானார்","தமதாஞ்சுத தாபர சங்கம","மெனவோம்புறு தாவன வம்படர்","தகுதாம்பிர சேவித ரஞ்சித ...... வும்பர்வாழ்வே","முனவாம்பத மூடிக வந்தன","முயல்வான்பிடி மாடிமை யைங்கரர்","முகதாம்பின மேவுறு சம்ப்ரம ...... சங்கணாறு","முககாம்பிர மோடமர் சம்பன","மதுராந்தக மாநக ரந்திகழ்","முருகாந்திர மோடம ரும்பர்கள் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet seems to ask when, leaving house, sons, kin, horses, grain, wealth and town and the delusion of 'I and mine', he will be taken into the vast silence and given Murugan's feet. The praise, much of it in drum syllables, names Siva who dances in space, whose child Murugan is, the elephant-faced brother with the mouse, the six-faced lord of Madurantakam honoured by the gods.","n":"யானெனதென்று: the plea names the 'I and mine' that binds, and மோன தடம்பர the expanse of silence that replaces it. The song is set to a dense rhythm, and most compounds in both halves (வணவாம்பிர மாத குணங்குறி, இடவார்ந்தன சானு) can be split several ways, so this reading is only the outline.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pattru","mounam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:99"},"p:721":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":721,"t":["முகிலாமெனும் வார்குழ லார்சிலை","புருவார்கயல் வேல்விழி யார்சசி","முகவார்தர ளாமென வேநகை ...... புரிமாதர்","முலைமாலிணை கோபுர மாமென","வடமாடிட வேகொடி நூலிடை","முதுபாளித சேலைகு லாவிய ...... மயில்போல்வார்","அகிசேரல்கு லார்தொடை வாழையின்","அழகார்கழ லார்தர வேய்தரு","அழகார்கன நூபுர மாடிட ...... நடைமேவி","அனமாமென யாரையு மால்கொள","விழியால்சுழ லாவிடு பாவையர்","அவர்பாயலி லேயடி யேனுட ...... லழிவேனோ","ககனார்பதி யோர்முறை கோவென","இருள்காரசு ரார்படை தூள்பட","கடலேழ்கிரி நாகமு நூறிட ...... விடும்வேலா","கமலாலய நாயகி வானவர்","தொழுமீசுர னாரிட மேவிய","கருணாகர ஞானப ராபரை ...... யருள்பாலா","மகிழ்மாலதி நாவல்ப லாகமு","குடனாடநி லாமயில் கோகில","மகிழ்நாடுறை மால்வளி நாயகி ...... மணவாளா","மதிமாமுக வாவடி யேனிரு","வினைதூள் பட வேயயி லேவிய","வளவாபுரி வாழ்மயில் வாகன ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes the women with cloud-dark hair, bow brows, carp eyes, pearl smiles, breasts like towers, thread waists and peacock grace, who bewitch everyone with whirling glances, and asks whether he must perish in their beds. The praise names the vel thrown so that the gods' city cried out and the dark demons' army, the seven seas and the mountains were crushed, the son of the gracious Lady of the lotus and of the Lord the gods worship, Valli's bridegroom in a land of magizh, malati, rose-apple and jackfruit groves, and the lord of Valavapuri who threw the vel to turn his servant's two deeds to dust.","n":"அடியேன் இருவினை தூள்பட அயில் ஏவிய: the vel that destroyed the demons is also said to have shattered the poet's own deeds, so the battle becomes his release. The song names the shrine வளவாபுரி, not Seyur.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:100"},"p:722":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":722,"t":["கலகலெ னச்சில கலைகள் பிதற்றுவ","தொழிவ துனைச்சிறி ...... துரையாதே","கருவழிதத்திய மடுவ தனிற்புகு","கடுநர குக்கிடை ...... யிடைவீழா","உ லகு தனிற்பல பிறவி தரித்தற","வுழல்வது விட்டினி ...... யடிநாயேன்","உ னதடி மைத்திரள் அதனினு முட்பட","வுபய மலர்ப்பத ...... மருள்வாயே","குலகிரி பொட்டெழ அலைகடல் வற்றிட","நிசிசர னைப்பொரு ...... மயில்வீரா","குணதர வித்தக குமர புனத்திடை","குறமக ளைப்புணர் ...... மணிமார்பா","அலைபுன லிற்றவழ் வளைநில வைத்தரு","மணிதிரு வக்கரை ...... யுறைவோனே","அடியவ ரிச்சையி லெவையெவை யுற்றன","அவைதரு வித்தருள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks that he stop babbling some scriptures noisily without saying even a little about Murugan, not fall again and again into the cruel hell the path of the womb leads to, and stop wandering through many births in the world, and that he, a dog of a servant, be taken into the throng of Murugan's servants and given the two flower feet. The praise names the peacock hero who fought the night-roamer until the great mountains cracked and the sea dried, the hunter girl's lover, the lord of Tiruvakkarai where conches crawl in the waves and give up pearls, who grants devotees whatever they wish.","n":"கலைகள் பிதற்றுவது: learning recited without the Lord is the first thing the poet wants to give up, before hell and rebirth. உனதடிமைத் திரள் அதனினும் உட்பட: the plea is to belong to the company of servants, not only to reach the feet alone.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thondu","adiyaar","piravi","naragam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:101"},"p:723":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":723,"t":["பச்சிலை யிட்டுமு கத்தைமி னுக்கிகள்","குத்திர வித்தைமி குத்தச மர்த்திகள்","பப்பர மட்டைகள் கைப்பொருள் பற்றிட ...... நினைவோர்கள்","பத்திநி ரைத்தவ ளத்தர ளத்தினை","யொத்தந கைப்பில்வி ழிப்பில்ம யக்கிகள்","பக்ஷமி குத்திட முக்கனி சர்க்கரை ...... யித்ழுறல்","எச்சி லளிப்பவர் கச்சணி மெத்தையில்","இச்சக மெத்தவு ரைத்துந யத்தொடு","மெத்திய ழைத்துஅ ணைத்தும யக்கிடு ...... மடமாதர்","இச்சையி லிப்படி நித்தம னத்துயர்","பெற்றுல கத்தவர் சிச்சியெ னத்திரி","இத்தொழி லிக்குணம் விட்டிட நற்பத ...... மருள்வாயே","நச்சர விற்றுயில் பச்சைமு கிற்கரு","ணைக்கடல் பத்மம லர்த்திரு வைப்புணர்","நத்துதரித்தக ரத்தர்தி ருத்துள ...... வணிமார்பர்","நட்டந டுக்கட லிற்பெரு வெற்பினை","நட்டர வப்பணி சுற்றிம தித்துள","நத்தமு தத்தையெ ழுப்பிய ளித்தவர் ...... மருகோனே","கொச்சைமொ ழிச்சிக றுத்தவி ழிச்சிசி","றுத்தஇ டைச்சிபெ ருத்தத னத்திகு","றத்தித னக்கும னப்ரிய முற்றிடு ...... குமரேசா","கொத்தவிழ் பத்மம லர்ப்பழ னத்தொடு","குற்றம றக்கடி கைப்புனல் சுற்றிய","கொட்புள நற்றிரு வக்கரை யுற்றுறை ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes the women who rub green herbs on their faces to make them shine, cunning in crafty arts, who think only of seizing the money in a man's hand, who bewitch with pearl smiles and glances, give the spittle of their lips as if fruit and sugar, flatter on the cushioned bed and draw him into their embrace; he asks for the good feet so that he may give up this trade and this habit, which leave him wretched every day while the world says 'chi, chi'. The praise names Vishnu who sleeps on the serpent, Lakshmi's lord with the conch and tulasi, who set the mountain in the sea and churned out nectar, as uncle, Murugan dear to the hunter girl of rustic speech, and the lord of Tiruvakkarai of lotus fields and moat.","n":"உலகத்தவர் சிச்சியெனத் திரி: public contempt is part of the poet's suffering. இத்தொழில் இக்குணம்: he calls his own habit a trade, as if visiting courtesans were his occupation.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","pazhi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:101"},"p:724":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":724,"t":["அண்டர்பதி குடியேற மண்டசுரர் உ ருமாற","அண்டர்மன மகிழ்மீற ...... வருளாலே","அந்தரியொ டுடனாடு சங்கரனு மகிழ்கூர","ஐங்கரனு முமையாளு ...... மகிழ்வாக","மண்டலமு முநிவோரு மெண்டிசையி லுளபேரு","மஞ்சினனு மயனாரு ...... மெதிர்காண","மங்கையுட னரிதானு மின்பமுற மகிழ்கூற","மைந்துமயி லுடனாடி ...... வரவேணும்","புண்டரிக விழியாள அண்டர்மகள் மணவாளா","புந்திநிறை யறிவாள ...... வுயர்தோளா","பொங்குகட லுடனாகம் விண்டுவரை யிகல்சாடு","பொன்பரவு கதிர்வீசு ...... வடிவேலா","தண்டரள மணிமார்ப செம்பொனெழில் செற§ருப","தண்டமிழின் மிகுநேய ...... முருகேசா","சந்தமு மடியார்கள் சிந்தையது குடியான","தண்சிறுவை தனில்மேவு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks Murugan to come dancing with his peacock so that Indra is resettled in his city, the demons are changed, the gods rejoice, Siva who dances with the goddess, Ganesha and Uma are glad, and the world, the sages, all in the eight directions, Brahma and Vishnu with Lakshmi see him before them. The praise names the lotus-eyed bridegroom of the gods' daughter, full of wisdom, whose vel split the sea and the mountain, with a pearl-hung chest, dear to cool Tamil, at Siruvai where he dwells in his devotees' hearts.","n":"The plea asks for nothing for the poet: it asks for an epiphany, Murugan coming in sight of everyone (எதிர்காண). தண்டமிழின் மிகுநேய, the lover of cool Tamil, and அடியார்கள் சிந்தையது குடியான, who lives in his devotees' minds, frame the shrine.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["thiruvarul","thamizh","adiyaar"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:102"},"p:725":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":725,"t":["சீதள வாரிஜ பாதாந மோநம","நாரத கீதவி நோதாந மோநம","சேவல மாமயில் ப்¡£தாந மோநம ...... மறைதேடுஞ்","சேகர மானப்ர தாபாந மோநம","ஆகம சாரசொ ரூபாந மோநம","தேவர்கள் சேனைம கீபாந மோநம ...... கதிதோயப்","பாதக நீவுகு டாராந மோநம","மாவசு ரேசக டோராந மோநம","பாரினி லேஜய வீராந மோநம ...... மலைமாது","பார்வதி யாள்தரு பாலாந மோநம","நாவல ஞானம னோலாந மோநம","பாலகு மாரசு வாமீந மோநம ...... அருள்தாராய்","போதக மாமுக னேரான சோதர","நீறணி வேணியர் நேயாப்ர பாகர","பூமக ளார்மரு கேசாம கோததி ...... யிகல்சூரா","போதக மாமறை ஞானாத யாகர","தேனவிழ் நீபந றாவாரு மார்பக","பூரண மாமதி போலாறு மாமுக ...... முருகேசா","மாதவர் தேவர்க ளோடேமு ராரியு","மாமலர் மீதுறை வேதாவு மேபுகழ்","மாநில மேழினு மோலான நாயக ...... வடிவேலா","வானவ ரூரினும் வீறாகி வீறள","காபுரி வாழ்வினு மேலாக வேதிரு","வாழ்சிறு வாபுரி வாழ்வேசு ராதிபர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A litany of salutations (நமோ நம) to Murugan: to the cool lotus feet, to the one who delights in Narada's songs, to the lover of the cock and peacock, the crest the Vedas seek, the essence of the Agamas, the commander of the gods' army, the axe that cuts sins so that souls reach their goal, the terror of the asura king, the child of Parvati, ending in a plea for grace. The praise names the brother of the elephant-faced one, the one dear to Siva, the nephew of Lakshmi's lord, the six faces like full moons, praised by Vishnu and Brahma, and Siruvapuri, richer than the gods' city and Alaka.","n":"The whole first half is salutation, not plea, until the closing அருள்தாராய். பாதக நீவு குடாரா, the axe that cuts away sin so the soul reaches its goal (கதி தோய), is the one line about the poet's need.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pugazh","thiruvarul","aagamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:102"},"p:726":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":726,"t":["பிறவி யான சடமி றங்கி வழியி லாத துறைசெ றிந்து","பிணிக ளான துயரு ழன்று ...... தடுமாறிப்","பெருகு தீய வினையி னொந்து கதிக டோறு மலைபொ ருந்தி","பிடிப டாத ஜனன நம்பி ...... யழியாதே","நறைவி ழாத மலர்மு கந்த வரிய மோன வழிதி றந்த","நளின பாத மெனது சிந்தை ...... யகலாதே","நரர்சு ராதி பரும்வ ணங்கு மினிய சேவை தனைவி ரும்பி","நலன தாக அடிய னென்று ...... பெறுவேனோ","பொறிவ ழாத முநிவர் தங்கள் நெறிவ ழாத பிலனு ழன்று","பொருநி சாச ரனைநி னைந்து ...... வினைநாடிப்","பொருவி லாம லருள்பு ரிந்து மயிலி னேறி நொடியில் வந்து","புளக மேவ தமிழ்பு னைந்த ...... முருகோனே","சிறுவ ராகி யிருவ ரந்த கரிப தாதி கொடுபொ ருஞ்சொல்","சிலையி ராம னுடனெ திர்ந்து ...... சமராடிச்","செயம தான நகர மர்ந்த அளகை போல வளமி குந்த","சிறுவை மேவி வரமி குந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks that he not perish trusting in birth, which has no hold: coming down into the inert body, crowding into pathless places, wandering in sickness and sorrow, grieving under growing evil deeds and roaming through every state; that the lotus feet which opened the rare way of silence never leave his mind; and whether, desiring the sweet service that men and gods offer, he will be counted Murugan's servant. The praise seems to tell how Murugan, mindful of a demon in a cave who troubled the sages, came in an instant on the peacock and, delighted, accepted Tamil, and names Siruvai, rich as Alaka, the town where two boys fought Rama with his elephants and foot soldiers and won.","n":"மோன வழி திறந்த நளின பாதம்: the feet themselves open the way of silence. The cave (பிலன்), the demon, the sudden coming on the peacock and the Tamil (தமிழ் புனைந்த) may point to Nakkirar freed from a demon's cave by his Murugan poem, but the text does not name him. The boys who fought Rama are the shrine's own legend, presumably Lava and Kusa.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","mounam","thiruvadi","thondu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:102"},"p:727":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":727,"t":["வேலி ரண்டெனு நீள்விழி மாதர்கள்","காத லின்பொருள் மேவின பாதகர்","வீணில் விண்டுள நாடிய ரூமைகள் ...... விலைகூறி","வேளை யென்பதி லாவசை பேசியர்","வேசி யென்பவ ராமிசை மோகிகள்","மீது நெஞ்சழி யாசையி லேயுழல் ...... சிறியேனும்","மால யன்பர னாரிமை யோர்முனி","வோர் புரந்தர னாதிய ரேதொழ","மாத வம்பெறு தாளிணை யேதின ...... மறவாதே","வாழ்த ருஞ்சிவ போகந னூனெறி","யேவி ரும்பி வினாவுட னேதொழ","வாழ்வ ரந்தரு வாயடி யேனிடர் ...... களைவாயே","நீல சுந்தரி கோமளி யாமளி","நாட கம்பயில் நாரணி பூரணி","நீடு பஞ்சவி சூலினி மாலினி ...... யுமைகாளி","நேயர் பங்கெழு மாதவி யாள்சிவ","காம சுந்தரி யேதரு பாலக","நீர்பொ ருஞ்சடை யாரருள் தேசிக ...... முருகேச","ஆலில் நின்றுல கோர்நிலை யேபெற","மாநி லங்களெ லாநிலை யேதரு","ஆய னந்திரு வூரக மால்திரு ...... மருகோனே","ஆட கம்பயில் கோபுர மாமதி","லால யம்பல வீதியு மேநிறை","வான தென்சிறு வாபுரி மேவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of women with spear eyes who sell love for money, who abuse a man without regard for the hour and are called whores, and of himself, a small man wandering in desire for them, and asks that he never forget the feet worshipped by Vishnu, Brahma, Siva, the gods, the sages and Indra, that he worship desiring the good path to Siva's bliss, and that his troubles be removed. The praise names Murugan as the child given by the goddess under a string of names (Nila Sundari, Komali, Yamali, Narani who dances, Purani, Sulini, Malini, Uma, Kali, Sivakama Sundari), the teacher by Siva's grace, nephew of Vishnu who lies on the banyan leaf to keep the world steady, at Siruvapuri with its golden towers.","n":"வேசி என்பவர், காதலின் பொருள் மேவின பாதகர்: here the women are named outright as harlots who trade love for money. The praise is mostly a list of the goddess's names, so it centres on Shakti more than on Murugan. சிவபோக நல் நூல் நெறி, the good path to Siva's bliss, is set against the women's போகம்.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","thiruvadi","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:102"},"p:728":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":728,"t":["அடல்வடி வேல்கள் வாளிக ளவைவிட வோடல் நேர்படு","மயில்விழி யாலு மாலெனு ...... மதவேழத்","தளவிய கோடு போல்வினை யளவள வான கூர்முலை","யதின்முக மூடு மாடையி ...... னழகாலுந்","துடியிடை யாலும் வாலர்கள் துயர்வுற மாய மாயொரு","துணிவுட னூடு மாதர்கள் ...... துணையாகத்","தொழுதவர் பாத மோதியுன் வழிவழி யானெ னாவுயர்","துலையலை மாறு போலுயிர் ...... சுழல்வேனோ","அடவியி னூடு வேடர்க ளரிவையொ டாசை பேசியு","மடிதொழு தாடு மாண்மையு ...... முடையோனே","அழகிய தோளி ராறுடை அறுமுக வேளெ னாவுனை","அறிவுட னோது மாதவர் ...... பெருவாழ்வே","விடையெறு மீசர் நேசமு மிகநினை வார்கள் தீவினை","யுகநெடி தோட மேலணை ...... பவர்மூதூர்","விரைசெறி தோகை மாதர்கள் விரகுட னாடு மாதையில்","விறல்மயில் மீது மேவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of women whose eyes strike like spears and arrows, whose breasts are like the tusks of a rutting elephant, whose drum-like waists torment young men, and who deceive them boldly, and asks whether, having worshipped and said 'I am your hereditary servant', he must still have his life swing up and down like the beam of a balance in their company. The praise names Murugan who spoke longingly with the hunter girl in the forest and bowed at her feet, the treasure of the ascetics who chant him knowingly as the six-faced lord, at Mathai, the ancient town of those who think of Siva on the bull, where evil deeds run far away.","n":"துலையலை மாறு போல் உயிர் சுழல்வேனோ: the image of a balance beam tipping between his vow of service (வழிவழியான் எனா) and the women is the song's own. அடிதொழுது ஆடும் ஆண்மை: Murugan's bowing at Valli's feet is called manliness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","thondu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:103"},"p:729":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":729,"t":["கண்க யற்பிணை மானொடுற வுண்டெ னக்கழை தோளானது","நன்க மைக்கின மாமமென ...... முகையான","கஞ்ச மொத்தெழு கூர்மாமுலை குஞ்ச ரத்திரு கோடோடுற","விஞ்சு மைப்பொரு கார்கோதைகொ ...... டுயர்காலன்","பெண்ட னக்குள கோலாகல மின்றெ டுத்திளை யோராவிகள்","மன்பி டிப்பது போல்நீள்வடி ...... வுடைமாதர்","பின்பொ ழித்திடு மாமாயையி லன்பு வைத்தழி யாதேயுறு","கிஞ்சி லத்தனை தாள்பேணிட ...... அருள்தாராய்","விண்ட னக்குற வானோனுடல் கண்ப டைத்தவன் வேதாவொடு","விண்டு வித்தகன் வீழ்தாளினர் ...... விடையேறி","வெந்த னத்துமை யாள்மேவிய சந்த னப்புய மாதீசுரர்","வெங்க யத்துரி யார்போர்வையர் ...... மிகுவாழ்வே","தண்பு டைப்பொழில் சூழ்மாதையில் நண்பு வைத்தருள் தாராதல","முங்கி ளைத்திட வானீள்திசை ...... யொடுதாவித்","தண்டரக் கர்கள் கோகோவென விண்டி டத்தட மாமீமிசை","சண்ட விக்ரம வேலேவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women with carp and doe eyes, bamboo shoulders, breasts like lotus buds and elephant tusks and hair darker than collyrium, as if Death had put on a woman's form to seize young men's lives, and asks that he not perish setting his love on the great maya they lay, but be given grace to cherish Murugan's feet even a little. The praise names Murugan the treasure of Siva on the bull, at whose feet Indra, Brahma and Vishnu fall, with Uma on his side and the elephant hide as his cloak, the lord who loves Mathai and whose vel made the demons cry out.","n":"உயர் காலன் பெண் தனக்கு உள கோலாகலம் இன்று எடுத்து: the women are Death himself in a woman's festive dress, come for young men's lives. The plea asks only for கிஞ்சில் அத்தனை, the least amount, of love for the feet. விண்டனக்கு உறவானோன் உடல் கண்படைத்தவன் seems to be Indra, whose body was covered with eyes.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["anangu","maayai","kootru","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:103"},"p:730":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":730,"t":["கருமுகில் போல்மட் டாகிய அளகிகள் தேனிற் பாகொடு","கனியமு தூறித் தேறிய ...... மொழிமாதர்","கலவிகள் நேரொப் பாகிகள் மதனிகள் காமக் க்ரோதிகள்","கனதன பாரக் காரிகள் ...... செயலோடே","பொருகயல் வாளைத் தாவிய விழியினர் சூறைக் காரிகள்","பொருளள வாசைப் பாடிகள் ...... புவிமீதே","பொதுவிகள் போகப் பாவிகள் வசமழி வேனுக் கோரருள்","புரிவது தானெப் போதது ...... புகல்வாயே","தருவடு தீரச் சூரர்கள் அவர்கிளை மாளத் தூளெழ","சமனிலை யேறப் பாறொடு ...... கொடிவீழத்","தனதன தானத் தானன எனஇசை பாடிப் பேய்பல","தசையுண வேல்விட் டேவிய ...... தனிவீரா","அரிதிரு மால்சக் ராயுத னவனிளை யாள் முத் தார்நகை","அழகுடை யாள்மெய்ப் பாலுமை ...... யருள்பாலா","அரவொடு பூளைத் தார்மதி அறுகொடு வேணிச் சூடிய","அழகர்தென் மாதைக் கேயுறை ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of women with dark cloud hair and speech like honey, syrup and fruit, lustful and quick to anger, heavy-breasted, with carp-like eyes, plunderers who sing their love to the measure of the money, common to all, sinners of pleasure, and asks when Murugan will grant grace to him who perishes in their power. The praise is a battle, with drum syllables and ghouls feeding on flesh, and names Murugan son of Uma, the younger sister of Vishnu who bears the discus, and of the lord who wears the serpent, poolai flowers, the moon and arugu grass, at southern Mathai.","n":"பொருள் அளவு ஆசைப்பாடிகள், பொதுவிகள்: love sung to the measure of the coin, and women belonging to all, are the Kural's two charges against the courtesan. Uma as Vishnu's younger sister explains why Vishnu is always Murugan's uncle.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","selvam","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:103"},"p:731":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":731,"t":["கால முகிலென நினைவுகொ டுருவிலி","காதி யமர்பொரு கணையென வடுவகிர்","காணு மிதுவென இளைஞர்கள் விதவிடு ...... கயலாலுங்","கான மமர்குழ லரிவையர் சிலுகொடு","காசி னளவொரு தலையணு மனதினர்","காம மிவர்சில கபடிகள் படிறுசொல் ...... கலையாலுஞ்","சால மயல்கொடு புளகித கனதன","பார முறவண முருகவிழ் மலரணை","சாயல் தனின்மிகு கலவியி லழிவுறும் ...... அடியேனைச்","சாதி குலமுறு படியினின் முழுகிய","தாழ்வ தறஇடை தருவன வெளியுயர்","தாள தடைவது தவமிக நினைவது ...... தருவாயே","வேலை தனில்விழி துயில்பவ னரவணை","வேயி னிசையது நிரைதனி லருள்பவன்","வீர துரகத நரபதி வனிதையர் ...... கரமீதே","வேறு வடிவுகொ டுறிவெணெய் தயிரது","வேடை கெடவமு தருளிய பொழுதினில்","வீசு கயிறுட னடிபடு சிறியவ ...... னதிகோப","வாலி யுடனெழு மரமற நிசிசரன்","வாகு முடியொரு பதுகர மிருபது","மாள வொருசரம் விடுமொரு கரியவன் ...... மருகோனே","வாச முறுமலர் விசிறிய பரிமள","மாதை நகர்தனி லுறையுமொ ரறுமுக","வானி லடியவ ரிடர்கெட அருளிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of women whose eyes are the arrows of the bodiless Kama, who quarrel and whose minds are set on the coin, frauds with lying speech, in whose flower beds he perishes, and asks to be given the end of the lowness of being sunk in caste and clan, the attainment of the feet high in open space, and much thought of austerity. The praise tells of Krishna, who sleeps on the serpent, plays the flute to the herds, stole butter and curd from the hanging pots and was beaten with the rope, and of Rama who felled the seven trees and Vali and with one arrow the ten heads and twenty arms of Ravana, as uncle, and the six-faced lord of fragrant Mathai.","n":"சாதி குலம் உறு படியினில் முழுகிய தாழ்வு அற: the poet names caste and clan as something he is sunk in and wants freed from, an unusual plea set beside the courtesans. காசின் அளவு ஒருதலை அணு மனதினர் repeats the charge of love measured by the coin. The Krishna scenes (butter, rope) are told unusually fully.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","kudi","thavam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:103"},"p:732":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":732,"t":["அச்சா யிறுக்காணி காட்டிக் கடைந்த","செப்பார் முலைக்கோடு நீட்டிச் சரங்க","ளைப்போல் விழிக்கூர்மை நோக்கிக் குழைந்து ...... உ றவாடி","அத்தா னெனக்காசை கூட்டித் தயங்க","வைத்தா யெனப்பேசி மூக்கைச் சொறிந்து","அக்கா லொருக்கால மேக்கற் றிருந்தி ...... ரிலையாசை","வைச்சா யெடுப்பான பேச்சுக் கிடங்க","ளொப்பா ருனக்கீடு பார்க்கிற் கடம்பன்","மட்டோ எனப்பாரின் மூர்க்கத் தனங்க ...... ளதனாலே","மைப்பா கெனக்கூறி வீட்டிற் கொணர்ந்து","புற்பா யலிற்காலம் வீற்றுக் கலந்து","வைப்பார் தமக்காசை யாற்பித் தளைந்து ...... திரிவேனோ","எச்சாய் மருட்பாடு மேற்பட் டிருந்த","பிச்சா சருக்கோதி கோட்டைக் கிலங்க","மிக்கா நினைப்போர்கள் வீக்கிற் பொருந்தி ...... நிலையாயே","எட்டா மெழுத்தேழை யேற்குப் பகர்ந்த","முத்தா வலுப்பான போர்க்குட் டொடங்கி","யெக்காலு மக்காத சூர்க்கொத் தரிந்த ...... சினவேலா","தச்சா மயிற்சேவ லாக்கிப் பிளந்த","சித்தா குறப்பாவை தாட்குட் படிந்து","சக்காகி யப்பேடை யாட்குப் புகுந்து ...... மணமாகித்","தப்பாம லிப்பூர்வ மேற்குத் தரங்கள்","தெற்காகு மிப்பாரில் கீர்த்திக் கிசைந்த","தச்சூர் வடக்காகு மார்க்கத் தமர்ந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet gives the courtesan's wheedling: she shows her breasts, aims her arrow-eyes, calls him 'cousin', says he made her long and then held back, that he stayed away that time and has no love for her, and that if one looks for his equal, even Kadamban falls short; with such talk she calls him her sugar, brings him home and lies with him on a grass mat, and the poet asks whether he will wander mad with desire for such women. The praise names the pearl who told 'the eighth letter' to the poor poet, the vel that cut down Sura's ranks, the adept who split him and made him peacock and cock, who fell at the hunter girl's feet and married her, and the lord of Tachur.","n":"உனக்கு ஈடு பார்க்கில் கடம்பன் மட்டோ: the courtesan's flattery makes the client the equal of Murugan, an irony aimed at the poet who sings Murugan's praise. எட்டாம் எழுத்து ஏழையேற்குப் பகர்ந்த may refer to the poet's own initiation; which letter is meant (perhaps அ, which is also the Tamil numeral for eight) is not said. The opening of the praise (எச்சாய் மருட்பாடு ... பிச்சாசருக்கு) and the geography of the last lines are obscure to me.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:104"},"p:733":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":733,"t":["பாவ நாரிகள் மாமட மாதர் வீணிக ளாணவ","பாவை யாரிள நீரன ...... முலையாலும்","பார்வை யாமிகு கூரயி லாலு மாமணி யார்குழை","பார காரன வார்குழ ...... லதனாலுஞ்","சாவ தார விதாரமு தார்த ராவித ழாலித","சாத மூரலி தாமதி ...... முகமாலுஞ்","சார்வ தாவடி யேனிடர் வீற மாலறி வேமிகு","சார மாயதி லேயுற ...... லொழிவேனோ","ஆவ ஆர்வன நான்மறை யாதி மூல பராவரி","யாதி காணரி தாகிய ...... பரமேச","ஆதி யாரருள் மாமுரு கேச மால்மரு கேசுர","னாதி தேவர்க ளியாவர்கள் ...... பணிபாத","கோவ தாமறை யோர்மறை யோது மோதம் விழாவொலி","கோடி யாகம மாவொலி ...... மிகவீறும்","கோவை மாநகர் மேவிய வீர வேலயி லாயுத","கோதை யானையி னோடமர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of sinful, foolish women puffed with pride, their breasts like tender coconuts, eyes sharper than a spear, hair weighed with jewels, lips sweet as nectar and moon faces, and asks whether, so that his trouble ends, he will give up being drawn into that essence of infatuation. The praise names the supreme lord whom the four Vedas, Vishnu and Brahma cannot see, Murugan by the grace of the First, whose feet the gods all worship, the hero of the vel at Kovai, where the Vedas are chanted and festival and Agamic sounds roar, dwelling with the maid reared by the elephant.","n":"The plea is short and set inside a long catalogue of the women's parts. கோதை யானையினோடு is Devayanai, named by her elephant nurture. கோவை is the shrine's name shortened.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","mayal"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:105"},"p:734":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":734,"t":["ஆறு மாறு மஞ்சு மஞ்சும் ஆறு மாறு மஞ்சு மஞ்சும்","ஆறு மாறு மஞ்சு மஞ்சும் ...... அறுநாலும்","ஆறு மாய சஞ்ச லங்கள் வேற தாவி ளங்கு கின்ற","ஆரணாக மங்க டந்த ...... கலையான","ஈறு கூற ரும்பெ ருஞ்சு வாமி யாயி ருந்த நன்றி","யேது வேறி யம்ப லின்றி ...... யொருதானாய்","யாவு மாய்ம னங்க டந்த மோன வீட டைந்தொ ருங்கி","யான வாவ டங்க என்று ...... பெறுவேனோ","மாறு கூறி வந்தெ திர்ந்த சூரர் சேனை மங்க வங்க","வாரி மேல்வெ குண்ட சண்ட ...... விததாரை","வாகை வேல கொன்றை தும்பை மாலை கூவி ளங்கொ ழுந்து","வால சோம னஞ்சு பொங்கு ...... பகுவாய","சீறு மாசு ணங்க ரந்தை ஆறு வேணி கொண்ட நம்பர்","தேசி காக டம்ப லங்கல் ...... புனைவோனே","தேவர் யாவ ருந்தி ரண்டு பாரின் மீது வந்தி றைஞ்சு","தேவ னூர்வி ளங்க வந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet counts off sixes and fives and fours, the constituents of the world, and asks when, beyond these restless things and beyond the art of the Vedas and Agamas, in the great lord whose end cannot be told, without anything else to say, alone and yet all things, he will reach the house of silence beyond the mind, gathered in, with his craving stilled. The praise names the vel that drove back Sura's army over the sea, and Murugan teacher of the lord with matted hair who wears konrai, thumbai, vilva shoots, the young moon, the hissing serpent, karanthai and the river, and the lord who came to shine at Devanur, where all the gods gather.","n":"ஆறும் ஆறும் அஞ்சும் அஞ்சும் ... அறுநாலும்: the counting points to the tattvas, but how the numbers add up is not said and I don't reconstruct it. மனங்கடந்த மோன வீடு and யான் அவா அடங்க: release is a house of silence beyond the mind, reached when the 'I' and its craving are stilled. தேசிகா: Murugan is again Siva's teacher.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thaththuvam","mounam","veedu","avaa"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:106"},"p:735":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":735,"t":["தார காசு ரன்ச ரிந்து வீழ வேரு டன்ப றிந்து","சாதி பூத ரங்கு லுங்க ...... முதுமீனச்","சாக ரோதை யங்கு ழம்பி நீடு தீகொ ளுந்த அன்று","தாரை வேல்தொ டுங்க டம்ப ...... மததாரை","ஆர வார வும்பர் கும்ப வார ணாச லம்பொ ருந்து","மானை யாளு நின்ற குன்ற ...... மறமானும்","ஆசை கூரு நண்ப என்று மாம யூர கந்த என்றும்","ஆவல தீர என்று நின்று ...... புகழ்வேனோ","பார மார்த ழும்பர் செம்பொன் மேனி யாளர் கங்கை வெண்க","பால மாலை கொன்றை தும்பை ...... சிறுதாளி","பார மாசு ணங்கள் சிந்து வார வார மென்ப டம்பு","பானல் கூவி ளங்க ரந்தை ...... அறுகோடே","சேர வேம ணந்த நம்ப ¡£ச னாரி டஞ்சி றந்த","சீத ளார விந்த வஞ்சி ...... பெருவாழ்வே","தேவர் யாவ ருந்தி ரண்டு பாரின் மீது வந்தி றைஞ்சு","தேவ னூர்வி ளங்க வந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether he will stand praising Murugan as the friend whom the gods' elephant-reared maid and the hunter girl of the hill desire, as Kanda of the great peacock, until his longing is satisfied. The praise names the vel that uprooted Tarakasura, shook the mountains and set the sea on fire, and Murugan the treasure of the cool lotus-like Lady on the left of the lord with the scars, golden body, Ganga, white skull garland, konrai, thumbai, serpents and arugu, at Devanur.","n":"The plea asks for nothing but the chance to praise: ஆவல் தீர என்று நின்று புகழ்வேனோ. Taraka, Sura's brother, is named here rather than Sura. தழும்பர் is Siva marked with scars, possibly those of Uma's embrace; the text does not say.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pugazh","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:106"},"p:736":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":736,"t":["காணொ ணாதது உ ருவோ டருவது","பேசொ ணாதது உ ரையே தரவது","காணு நான்மறை முடிவாய் நிறைவது ...... பஞ்சபூதக்","காய பாசம தனிலே யுறைவது","மாய மாயுட லறியா வகையது","காய மானவ ரெதிரே யவரென ...... வந்துபேசிப்","பேணொ ணாதது வெளியே யொளியது","மாய னாரய னறியா வகையது","பேத பேதமொ டுலகாய் வளர்வது ...... விந்துநாதப்","பேரு மாய்கலை யறிவாய் துரியவ","தீத மானது வினையேன் முடிதவ","பேறு மாயருள் நிறைவாய் விளைவது ...... ஒன்றுநீயே","வீணொ ணாதென அமையா தசுரரை","நூறி யேயுயிர் நமனீ கொளுவென","வேல்க டாவிய கரனே யுமைமுலை ...... யுண்டகோவே","வேத நான்முக மறையோ னொடும்விளை","யாடி யேகுடு மியிலே கரமொடு","வீற மோதின மறவா குறவர்கு ...... றிஞ்சியூடே","சேணொ ணாயிடு மிதண்மே லரிவையை","மேவி யேமயல் கொளலீ லைகள்செய்து","சேர நாடிய திருடா வருடரு ...... கந்தவேளே","சேரொ ணாவகை வெளியே திரியுமெய்ஞ்","ஞான யோகிக ளுளமே யுறைதரு","தேவ னூர்வரு குமரா வமரர்கள் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"A song of praise with no plea: the one that cannot be seen, both form and formless, that cannot be spoken yet gives speech, the fullness at the end of the four Vedas, dwelling in the bond of the five-element body, unknown to the illusory body, coming before the embodied and speaking as them, the light in space, unknown to Vishnu and Brahma, growing as the world in difference and non-difference, as bindu and nada, as the knowledge of the kalas, as turiya and beyond it, the fruit of the poet's austerity and the fullness of grace: that one is Murugan. The praise names the vel that crushed the demons and gave their lives to Yama, the child who drank Uma's milk, who struck Brahma on the tuft of his head, the thief who sought the girl on the millet platform, and the lord of Devanur who dwells in the hearts of true jnana-yogis.","n":"The first half is a definition of the Absolute in negatives and pairs (காணொணாதது உருவோடு அருவது, பேசொணாதது உரையே தரவது, பேதாபேதமொடு உலகாய் வளர்வது), ending ஒன்று நீயே, 'the one is you'. Striking Brahma's tuft (குடுமியிலே கரமொடு மோதின) is the punishment for not knowing Om, though Om is not named. காயமானவர் எதிரே அவரென வந்து பேசி may mean the Lord appearing as a human guru.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["viyaabagam","avaththai","naadham","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:106"},"p:737":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":737,"t":["பரவுவரிக் கயல்குவியக் குயில்கிளியொத் துரைபதறப்","பவளநிறத் ததரம்விளைத் ...... தமுதூறல்","பருகிநிறத் தரளமணிக் களபமுலைக் குவடசையப்","படைமதனக் கலையடவிப் ...... பொதுமாதர்","சொருகுமலர்க் குழல்சரியத் தளர்வுறுசிற் றிடைதுவளத்","துகிலகலக் க்ருபைவிளைவித் ...... துருகாமுன்","சொரிமலர்மட் டலரணைபுக் கிதமதுரக் கலவிதனிற்","சுழலுமனக் கவலையொழித் ...... தருள்வாயே","கருகுநிறத் தசுரன்முடித் தலையொருபத் தறமுடுகிக்","கணைதொடுமச் சுதன்மருகக் ...... குமரேசா","கயிலைமலைக் கிழவனிடக் குமரிவிருப் பொடுகருதக்","கவிநிறையப் பெறும்வரிசைப் ...... புலவோனே","திரள்கமுகிற் றலையிடறிப் பலகதலிக் குலைசிதறிச்","செறியும்வயற் கதிரலையத் ...... திரைமோதித்","திமிதிமெனப் பறையறையப் பெருகுபுனற் கெடிலநதித்","திருவதிகைப் பதிமுருகப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes common women whose eyes close like carp, whose voices falter like cuckoos and parrots, who give the nectar of coral lips, whose breasts sway, whose hair slips and waists sag as their garments fall in lovemaking on the flower bed, and asks Murugan to remove the anxiety of his mind, whirling in that sweet union, before he melts away. The praise names the nephew of Achyuta, whose arrow cut the ten heads of the dark asura, the poet of honour who received his poems in full as the Lady on the left of the lord of Kailasa wished, and the lord of Tiruvathigai, where the Kedilam floods past areca and plantain and the drums beat.","n":"கயிலைமலைக் கிழவன் இடக் குமரி விருப்பொடு கருதக் கவி நிறையப் பெறும் வரிசைப் புலவோனே: Murugan as the poet favoured by Uma may point to Sambandar, whom she fed with milk and who then sang, and whom the book often makes Murugan; the text does not name him. பொதுமாதர் are women common to all, but money is not mentioned.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","punarchi","thamizh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:107"},"p:738":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":738,"t":["விடமும் வேலன மலரன விழிகளு","மிரத மேதரு மமுதெனு மொழிகளும்","விரகி னாலெழு மிருதன வகைகளு ...... மிதமாடி","மிகவு மாண்மையு மெழினல முடையவர்","வினையு மாவியு முடனிரு வலையிடை","வெளியி லேபட விசிறிய விஷமிக ...... ளுடன்மேவா","இடரு றாதுனை நினைபவர் துணைகொள","இனிமை போலெழு பிறவியெ னுவரியி","னிடைகெ டாதினி யிருவினை யிழிவினி ...... லிழியாதே","இசையி னாடொறு மிமையவர் முநிவர்கள்","ககன பூபதி யிடர்கெட அருளிய","இறைநி னாறிரு புயமென வுரைசெய ...... அருள்வாயே","படரு மார்பினி லிருபது புயமதொ","டரிய மாமணி முடியொளி ரொருபது","படியி லேவிழ வொருகணை தொடுபவ ...... ரிடமாராய்","பரவை யூடெரி பகழியை விடுபவர்","பரவு வார்வினை கெடஅரு ளுதவியெ","பரவு பால்கட லரவணை துயில்பவர் ...... மருகோனே","அடர வேவரு மசுரர்கள் குருதியை","அரக ராவென அலகைகள் பலியுண","அலையும் வேலையும் அலறிட எதிர்பொரு ...... மயில்வீரா","அமர ராதிய ரிடர்பட அடர்தரு","கொடிய தானவர் திரிபுர மெரிசெய்த","அதிகை மாநகர் மருவிய சசிமகள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of women with eyes of poison and spears, nectar words and artful breasts, poisonous ones who cast the deeds and the lives of even manly and handsome men into their double net, and asks that he not join them or suffer, but in the company of those who think of Murugan never be lost in the sea of seven births nor sink in the baseness of the two deeds, and be given to say each day, 'Lord, your twelve arms', which freed the gods, sages and Indra from their trouble. The praise names Rama who brought down Ravana's twenty arms and ten crowned heads with one arrow and shot fire into the sea, Vishnu asleep on the serpent in the milk sea, as uncle, the peacock hero of the battle, and the lord of Devayanai at Adhigai, where the cruel demons' three cities were burned.","n":"உனை நினைபவர் துணைகொள: the company of devotees is the remedy, as in 722. இருவலை, the double net, catches both deeds and life. The shrine's own myth, the burning of the three cities (திரிபுரம் எரிசெய்த அதிகை), closes the song.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","adiyaar","piravi","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:107"},"p:739":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":739,"t":["சீத மதிய மெறிக்குந் ...... தழலாலே","சீறி மதனன் வளைக்குஞ் ...... சிலையாலே","ஓத மருவி யலைக்குங் ...... கடலாலே","ஊழி யிரவு தொலைக்கும் ...... படியொதான்","மாது புகழை வளர்க்குந் ...... திருவர்முர்","வாழு மயிலி லிருக்குங் ...... குமரேசா","காத லடியர் கருத்தின் ...... பெருவாழ்வே","காலன் முதுகை விரிக்கும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A short love song: the speaker asks whether the night as long as an aeon must be spent this way, burned by the fire of the cool moon, by the bow Kama bends in anger and by the sea whose waves beat on. The song names Murugan as Kumaresa on the peacock at Thiruvamur, the great life of his loving devotees, who makes Death show his back.","n":"The four torments (moon, Kama's bow, the sea, the endless night) are the stock signs of a woman's love-sickness in akam poetry; the song has no confession of sin, so the speaker is the lover longing for Murugan, though the text does not name her. காலன் முதுகை விரிக்கும், making Death turn his back in flight, sets the Lord against Death even in a love song. படியொதான் is probably படியோதான். மாது புகழை வளர்க்கும் may allude to a woman of the shrine's story, which I cannot identify.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","kootru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:108"},"p:740":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":740,"t":["அரியய னறியா தவரெரி புர்மு","ணதுபுக நகையே ...... வியநாதர்","அவிர்சடை மிசையோர் வனிதையர் பதிசீ","றழலையு மழுநேர் ...... பிடிநாதர்","வரைமக ளொருகூ றுடையவர் மதனா","கமும்விழ விழியே ...... வியநாதர்","மனமகிழ் குமரா எனவுன திருதாள்","மலரடி தொழுமா ...... றருள்வாயே","அருவரை யிருகூ றிடவொரு மயில்மேல்","அவனியை வலமாய் ...... வருவோனே","அமரர்க ளிகல்நீ டசுரர்கள் சிரமேல்","அயில்தனை விசையாய் ...... விடுவோனே","வரிசையொ டொருமா தினைதரு வனமே","மருவியொர் குறமா ...... தணைவேடா","மலைகளில் மகிழ்வாய் மருவிநல் வடுகூர்","வருதவ முநிவோர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The first half praises Siva: the lord whom Hari and Brahma could not find, who burned the cities with a smile, who bears a woman on his matted hair and holds fire and axe, who has the mountain's daughter as his half and felled Kama's body with his eye; the poet asks for grace to worship Murugan's two flower feet, calling him the Kumara who gladdens that lord's heart. The praise names Murugan who circled the earth on a peacock and split the mountain, who threw the vel at the asuras' heads, and who went into the millet forest to embrace the hunter girl, at Vadugur of the sages.","n":"The plea is a single line of worship (இருதாள் மலரடி தொழுமாறு அருள்வாயே), framed by Siva's deeds: Murugan is approached as the joy of Siva's mind. அவனியை வலமாய் வருவோனே, who goes round the earth on the peacock, points to the story of the contest for the fruit, though the fruit is not named.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvadi","vazhipaadu","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:109"},"p:741":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":741,"t":["ஆரத்தன பாரத்துகில் மூடிப்பலர் காணக்கையில்","யாழ்வைத்திசை கூரக்குழ ...... லுடைசோர","ஆகப்பனி நீரப்புழு கோடக்குழை யாடப்பிரை","யாசப்படு வார்பொட்டணி ...... சசிநேர்வாள்","கூரக்கணை வேல்கட்கயல் போலச்சுழல் வார்சர்க்கரை","கோவைக்கனி வாய்பற்கதி ...... ரொளிசேருங்","கோலக்குயி லார்பட்டுடை நூலொத்திடை யார்சித்திர","கோபச்செய லார்பித்தர்க ...... ளுறவாமோ","பூரித்தன பாரச்சடை வேதக்குழ லாள்பத்தர்கள்","பூசைக்கியல் வாள்பத்தினி ...... சிவகாமி","பூமிக்கடல் மூவர்க்குமு னாள்பத்திர காளிப்புணர்","போகர்க்குப தேசித்தருள் ...... குருநாதா","சூரக்குவ டாழித்தவி டாய்முட்டசு ராருக்கிட","சோர்விற்கதிர் வேல்விட்டருள் ...... விறல்வீரா","தோகைச்செய லாள்பொற்பிர காசக்குற மான்முத்தொடு","சோதித்துறை யூர்நத்திய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women who hold a yaazh and sing while their cloth and hair slip, scented with rosewater and civet, with earrings swaying, eyes like spears and carp, lips like sugar and kovai fruit, voices like cuckoos and waists like thread, and asks whether he should keep company with these maddening women of fickle anger. The praise names Sivakami, the goddess the devotees worship, and Murugan as the guru who taught one joined to Bhadrakali, as the hero whose vel struck Sura's mountain and sea, and as the lord of the shining hunter girl at Thuraiyur.","n":"பித்தர்கள் உறவாமோ closes the description with a question rather than a plea. The line பூமிக்கடல் மூவர்க்கு முன்னாள் பத்திரகாளிப் புணர் போகர்க்கு உபதேசித்தருள் குருநாதா makes Murugan a teacher, but whom he taught is unclear: போகர் may be Siva as Bhadrakali's consort or the siddha Bhogar, and the first words do not split with certainty.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:110"},"p:742":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":742,"t":["வெகுமாய விதத்துரு வாகிய","திறமேப ழகப்படு சாதக","விதமேழ்க டலிற்பெரி தாமதில் ...... சுழலாகி","வினையான கருக்குழி யாமெனு","மடையாள முளத்தினின் மேவினும்","விதியாரும் விலக்கவொ ணாதெனு ...... முதியோர்சொல்","தகவாம தெனைப்பிடி யாமிடை","கயிறாலு மிறுக்கிம காகட","சலதாரை வெளிக்கிடை யேசெல ...... வுருவாகிச்","சதிகாரர் விடக்கதி லேதிரள்","புழுவாக நெளித்தெரி யேபெறு","மெழுகாக வுருக்குமு பாதிகள் ...... தவிர்வேனோ","உ ககால நெருப்பதி லேபுகை","யெழவேகு முறைப்படு பாவனை","யுறவேகு கையிற்புட மாய்விட ...... வெளியாகி","உ லவாநர குக்கிரை யாமவர்","பலவோர்கள் தலைக்கடை போயெதிர்","உ ளமாழ்கி மிகக்குழை வாகவு ...... முறவாடித்","தொகலாவ தெனக்கினி தானற","வளமாக அருட்பத மாமலர்","துணையேப ணியத்தரு வாய்பரி ...... மயில்வேலா","துதிமாத வர்சித்தர்ம கேசுரர்","அரிமால்பி ரமர்க்கருள் கூர்தரு","துறையூர்நக ரிற்குடி யாய்வரு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says that through many forms and births, in a womb-pit made of deeds, he has learned the elders' word that fate cannot be turned aside; he fears the hour when he is seized and bound with rope, the body becomes a heap of worms, is melted like wax and burned with rising smoke, becomes food for hell, while his kin stand at the door weeping, and asks to be freed from these and given the flower feet of grace to bow to. The praise names Murugan of the vel on the galloping peacock, who gives grace at Thuraiyur to sages, siddhas, Maheswaras, Hari and Brahma.","n":"விதியாரும் விலக்கவொணாது எனும் முதியோர் சொல்: the poet cites, and accepts, the proverb that no one can avert fate, and the plea is to escape it by grace. உபாதிகள், afflictions, is the word for the whole chain from rope to pyre. Several lines (சாதக விதம், மகாகட சலதாரை, சதிகாரர் விடக்கு) are hard to split, so the order of events is a reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oozh","karu","naragam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:110"},"p:743":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":743,"t":["கோல மறை யொத்த மாலைதனி லுற்ற","கோரமதன் விட்ட ...... கணையாலே","கோதிலத ருக்கள் மேவுபொழி லுற்ற","கோகிலமி குத்த ...... குரலாலே","ஆலமென விட்டு வீசுகலை பற்றி","ஆரழலி றைக்கு ...... நிலவாலே","ஆவிதளர் வுற்று வாடுமெனை நித்த","மாசைகொட ணைக்க ...... வரவேணும்","நாலுமறை கற்ற நான்முகனு தித்த","நாரணனு மெச்சு ...... மருகோனே","நாவலர்ம திக்க வேல்தனையெ டுத்து","நாகமற விட்ட ...... மயில்வீரா","சேலெனும் விழிச்சி வேடுவர் சிறுக்கி","சீரணி தனத்தி ...... லணைவோனே","சீதவயல் சுற்று நாவல்தனி லுற்ற","தேவர்சிறை விட்ட ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The speaker says that by the evening, the arrows of cruel Kama, the loud cuckoo in the grove and the moon that pours fire like poison, life itself grows faint, and asks Murugan to come every day with longing to embrace. The praise names him as nephew of Narayana, whom four-faced Brahma also praises, the peacock hero who took up the vel so poets honour him and destroyed the mountain, the lover of the hunter girl, at Naval where he freed the gods from prison.","n":"The torments of evening, cuckoo and moon are the stock signs of a heroine's longing; the plea to be embraced (ஆசைகொடு அணைக்க வரவேணும்) makes the speaker a woman in love with Murugan, though the voice is not named. நாகம் may be the mountain (Krauncha) or a serpent; I take it as the mountain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","maalai","punarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:111"},"p:744":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":744,"t":["பலபல தத்துவ மதனை யெரித்திருள்","பரையர ணப்படர் வடவன லுக்கிரை","படநட நச்சுடர் பெருவெளி யிற்கொள ...... விடமேவிப்","பவன மொழித்திரு வழியை யடைத்தொரு","பருதி வழிப்பட விடல்கக னத்தொடு","பவுரி கொளச்சிவ மயமென முற்றிய ...... பர்முடே","கலகலெ னக்கழல் பரிபுர பொற்பத","வொலிமலி யத்திரு நடன மியற்றிய","கனக சபைக்குளி லுருகி நிறைக்கட ...... லதில்மூழ்கிக்","கவுரி மினற்சடை யரனொடு நித்தமொ","டனக சகத்துவம் வருதலு மிப்படி","கழிய நலக்கினி நிறமென விற்றுட ...... லருள்வாயே","புலையர் பொடித்தளும் அமண ருடற்களை","நிரையில் கழுக்களி லுறவிடு சித்திர","புலவனெ னச்சில விருது படைத்திடு ...... மிளையோனே","புனமலை யிற்குற மகளய லுற்றொரு","கிழவ னெனச்சுனை தனில வளைப்புய","புளகித முற்றிபம் வரவணை யப்புணர் ...... மணிமார்பா","மலைசிலை பற்றிய கடவு ளிடத்துறை","கிழவி யறச்சுக குமரி தகப்பனை","மழுகொடு வெட்டிய நிமலிகை பெற்றருள் ...... முருகோனே","மகிழ்பெணை யிற்கரை பொழில்முகில் சுற்றிய","திருவெணெய் நற்பதி புகழ்பெற அற்புத","மயிலின் மிசைக்கொடு திருநட மிட்டுறை ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes a yogic ascent: burning the many tattvas, the darkness consumed by fire, stopping the breath and closing the one path so that it runs by the sun's path into the sky until all is Siva, then melting in the golden hall where the dance is done to ringing anklets and sinking in the full sea, ever with Gauri and Hara; he asks for grace that this may come. The praise names Murugan as the young one who won fame as the poet who set the base Jains on stakes in rows, who came to the hunter girl as an old man by the spring and embraced her when the elephant came, and as son of the pure goddess, at Thiruvennainallur by the Pennai where he dances on the peacock.","n":"பவனம் ஒழித்து, stopping the breath, and பருதி வழி, the sun's path, are terms of breath yoga, and சிவமயம் names its end; the song then moves to the dance in கனக சபை, Chidambaram's golden hall. அமணர் உடல்களை நிரையில் கழுக்களில் உறவிடு சித்திர புலவன் identifies Murugan with Sambandar. The goddess lines (குமரி தகப்பனை மழுகொடு வெட்டிய) seem to recall Daksha's punishment, and the last line of the plea (கழிய நலக்கினி நிறமென விற்றுடல்) does not split with certainty.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","praanan","thaththuvam","thirukkoothu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:112"},"p:745":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":745,"t":["நிணமொடு குருதி நரம்பு மாறிய","தசைகுடல் மிடையு மெலும்பு தோலிவை","நிரைநிரை செறியு முடம்பு நோய்படு ...... முதுகாயம்","நிலைநிலை யுருவ மலங்க ளாவது","நவதொளை யுடைய குரம்பை யாமிதில்","நிகழ்தரு பொழுதில் முயன்று மாதவ ...... முயவோரும்","உ ணர்விலி செபமுத லொன்று தானிலி","நிறையிலி முறையிலி யன்பு தானிலி","உ யர்விலி யெனினுமெ னெஞ்சு தானினை ...... வழியாமுன்","ஒருதிரு மரகத துங்க மாமிசை","யறுமுக மொளிவிட வந்து நான்மறை","யுபநிட மதனை விளங்க நீயருள் ...... புரிவாயே","புணரியில் விரவி யெழுந்த ஞாயிறு","விலகிய புரிசை யிலங்கை வாழ்பதி","பொலமணி மகுட சிரங்கள் தாமொரு ...... பதுமாறிப்","புவியிடை யுருள முனிந்து கூர்கணை","யுறுசிலை வளைய வலிந்து நாடிய","புயலதி விறலரி விண்டு மால்திரு ...... மருகோனே","அணிதரு கயிலை நடுங்க வோரெழு","குலகிரி யடைய இடிந்து தூளெழ","அலையெறி யுததி குழம்ப வேல்விடு ...... முருகோனே","அமலைமு னரிய தவஞ்செய் பாடல","வளநகர் மருவி யமர்ந்த தேசிக","அறுமுக குறமக ளன்ப மாதவர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes the body as fat, blood, nerves, flesh, gut, bone and skin packed row on row, an old frame struck by disease and impurities, a hut with nine holes, and confesses that while living in it he has done no austerity and has no awareness, no japa, no restraint, no order, no love; yet before his heart's thought perishes he asks Murugan to come on the emerald peacock with his six faces shining and make clear to him the Upanishad of the four Vedas. The praise names Vishnu who as Rama bent his bow so the ten crowned heads of Lanka's king rolled on the earth, the vel that shook Kailas, crumbled the seven mountains and churned the sea, and Murugan as the teacher at Patalai, where the goddess did penance.","n":"நவதொளை உடைய குரம்பை, the hut with nine holes, is the old image of the body's nine openings. The list of lacks (உணர்விலி, செபமுதல் ஒன்றுதானிலி, அன்புதானிலி) is the poet's self-indictment, and the plea is for teaching, not rescue: நான்மறை உபநிடம் அதனை விளங்க. தேசிக, teacher, names Murugan as guru. அமலை முன் அரிய தவஞ்செய் பாடல வளநகர is the Pathiripuliyur story of the goddess's penance.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","kutram","vedaantham","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:113"},"p:746":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":746,"t":["மதிக்கு நேரெனும் வாண்மூகம் வான்மக","நதிக்கு மேல்வரு சேலேனு நேர்விழி","மணத்த வார்குழல் மாமாத ராரிரு ...... கொங்¨க்முழ்கி","மதித்த பூதர மாமாம னோலயர்","செருக்கி மேல்விழ நாடோறு மேமிக","வடித்த தேன்மொழி வாயூற லேநுகர் ...... பண்டநாயேன்","பதித்த நூபுர சீர்பாத மாமலர்","படைக்குள் மேவிய சீராவொ டேகலை","பணைத்த தோள்களொ டீராறு தோடுகள் ...... தங்குகாதும்","பணக்க லாபமும் வேலொடு சேவலும்","வடிக்கொள் சூலமும் வாள்வீசு நீள்சிலை","படைத்த வாகையு நாடாது பாழில்ம ...... யங்கலாமோ","கதித்து மேல்வரு மாசூரர் சூழ்படை","நொறுக்கி மாவுயர் தேரோடு மேகரி","கலக்கி யூர்பதி த¨முள வேவிடும் ...... வஞ்சவேலா","களித்த பேய்கண மாகாளி கூளிகள்","திரட்பி ரேதமெ லேமேவி மூளைகள்","கடித்த பூதமொ டேபாடி யாடுதல் ...... கண்டவீரா","குதித்து வானர மேலேறு தாறுகள்","குலைத்து நீள்கமு கூடாடி வாழைகொள்","குலைக்கு மேல்விழ வேரேறு போகமும் ...... வஞ்சிதோயுங்","குளத்தி லு¡றிய தேனூறல் மாதுகள்","குடித்து லாவியெ சேலோடு மாணிகொள்","குழிக்குள் மேவிய வானோர்க ளேதொழு ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women with faces like the moon, eyes like carp, fragrant long hair and breasts in which he drowns, who fall upon men daily while he sips the honey of their lips, and calls himself a dog; he asks whether it is right to languish in ruin without seeking Murugan's anklet-ringed feet, his twelve shoulders and earrings, the peacock, the vel, the cock, the trident and the bow. The praise names the vel that crushed the Sura armies, chariots and elephants and burned their city while ghouls and Kali danced on the corpses, and the shrine at Manikuzhi where monkeys leap in areca and plantain groves and honey flows in the ponds, worshipped by the gods.","n":"The plea is a catalogue of what the poet has failed to seek: the feet first, then the twelve shoulders, the weapons and emblems one by one, as if to contemplate them were the cure (நாடாது பாழில் மயங்கலாமோ). பண்டநாயேன், I the dog, is the self-abuse of the book.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kaamam","kutram","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:114"},"p:747":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":747,"t":["சதுரத்தரை நோக்கிய பூவொடு","கதிரொத்திட ஆக்கிய கோளகை","தழையச்சிவ பாக்கிய நாடக ...... அநுபூதி","சரணக்கழல் காட்டியெ னாணவ","மலமற்றிட வாட்டிய ஆறிரு","சயிலக்குல மீட்டிய தோளொடு ...... முகமாறுங்","கதிர்சுற்றுக நோக்கிய பாதமு","மயிலிற்புற நோக்கிய னாமென","கருணைக்கடல் காட்டிய கோலமும் ...... அடியேனைக்","கனகத்தினு நோக்கினி தாயடி","யவர்முத்தமி ழாற்புக வேபர","கதிபெற்றிட நோக்கிய பார்வையு ...... மறவேனே","சிதறத்தரை நாற்றிசை பூதர","நெரியப்பறை மூர்க்கர்கள் மாமுடி","சிதறக்கட லார்ப்புற வேயயில் ...... விடுவோனே","சிவபத்தினி கூற்றினை மோதிய","பதசத்தினி மூத்தவி நாயகி","செகமிப்படி தோற்றிய பார்வதி ...... யருள்பாலா","விதுரற்கும ராக்கொடி யானையும்","விகடத்துற வாக்கிய மாதவன்","விசையற்குயர் தேர்ப்பரி யூர்பவன் ...... மருகோனே","வெளியெட்டிசை சூர்ப்பொரு தாடிய","கொடிகைக்கொடு கீர்த்தியு லாவிய","விறல்மெய்த்திரு வேட்கள மேவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet vows he will not forget what Murugan showed him: the experience of Siva's grace, the anklet feet that dried up his anava impurity, the twelve shoulders and six faces, the form on the peacock that showed the sea of compassion, and the look that gently brought him among the devotees of three Tamils so that he might reach the supreme state. The praise names the vel that scattered the mountains and the Suras' crowns, the goddess who is the power of the foot that struck Death, and Vishnu who set Vidura against Duryodhana and drove Arjuna's chariot, at Thiruvetkalam.","n":"Each line ends in a thing seen (பாதம், கோலம், பார்வை) and the whole song hangs on மறவேனே, 'I will not forget': it is thanksgiving, not plea. ஆணவ மலம் அற்றிட is Saiva Siddhanta language for the root impurity. சிவபத்தினி கூற்றினை மோதிய பதசத்தினி makes the goddess the power in the foot that kicked Death, the left foot being hers. The opening lines (சதுரத்தரை நோக்கிய பூவொடு கதிரொத்திட ஆக்கிய கோளகை) are obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","malam","thiruvadi","adiyaar"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:115"},"p:748":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":748,"t":["மாத்திரை யாகிலு நாத்தவ றாளுடன்","வாழ்க்கையை நீடென ...... மதியாமல்","மாக்களை யாரையு மேற்றிடு சீலிகள்","மாப்பரி வேயெய்தி ...... அநுபோக","பாத்திர மீதென மூட்டிடு மாசைகள்","பாற்படு ஆடக ...... மதுதேடப்","பார்க்கள மீதினில் மூர்க்கரை யேகவி","பாற்கட லானென ...... வுழல்வேனோ","சாத்திர மாறையு நீத்தம னோலய","சாத்தியர் மேவிய ...... பதவேளே","தாத்தரி தாகிட சேக்கெனு மாநட","தாட்பர னார்தரு...... குமரேசா","வேத்திர சாலம தேற்றிடு வேடுவர்","மீக்கமு தாமயில் ...... மணவாளா","வேத்தம தாமறை யார்த்திடு சீர்திரு","வேட்கள மேவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses that, not reckoning that life may slip in a moment, he took this life as lasting, and for the desires roused by women who receive any man, he went seeking gold, praising fools in the world as if each were Vishnu of the milk sea, and asks whether he must wander so. The praise names Murugan as the lord of the state reached by those who leave the six systems and still the mind, the son of the dancer to the beat, the bridegroom of the hunters' peacock girl, at Thiruvetkalam where the Vedas sound.","n":"பார்க்களம் மீதினில் மூர்க்கரையே கவி பாற்கடலான் என: the poet's fault is flattering patrons as gods for gold; the gold is wanted for women (பாற்படு ஆடகம்). சாத்திரம் ஆறையும் நீத்த மனோலய சாத்தியர் sets the goal beyond all six philosophical systems.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","iravu","varaivinmagalir"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:115"},"p:749":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":749,"t":["அறிவி லாதவ ¡£னர்பேச் சிரண்டு","பகரு நாவினர் லோபர்தீக் குணங்க","ளதிக பாதகர் மாதர்மேற் கலன்கள் ...... புனையாதர்","அசடர் பூமிசை வீணராய்ப் பிறந்து","திரியு மானுடர் பேதைமார்க் கிரங்கி","யழியு மாலினர் நீதிநூற் பயன்கள் ...... தெரியாத","நெறியி லாதவர் சூதினாற் கவர்ந்து","பொருள்செய் பூரியர் மோகமாய்ப் ப்ரபஞ்ச","நிலையில் வீழ்தரு மூடர்பாற் சிறந்த ...... தமிழ்கூறி","நினைவு பாழ்பட வாடிநோக் கிழந்து","வறுமை யாகிய தீயின்மேற் கிடந்து","நெளியு நீள்புழு வாயினேற் கிரங்கி ...... யருள்வாயே","நறிய வார்குழல் வானநாட் டரம்பை","மகளிர் காதலர் தோள்கள்வேட் டிணங்கி","நகைகொ டேழிசை பாடிமேற் பொலிந்து ...... களிகூர","நடுவி லாதகு ரோதமாய்த் தடிந்த","தகுவர் மாதர்ம ணாளர்தோட் பிரிந்து","நசைபொ றாதழு தாகமாய்த் தழுங்கி ...... யிடர்கூர","மறியு மாழ்கட லு¡டுபோய்க் கரந்து","கவடு கோடியின் மேலுமாய்ப் பரந்து","வளரு மாவிரு கூறதாய்த் தடிந்த ...... வடிவேலா","மரவு காளமு கீல்கள்கூட் டெழுந்து","மதியு லாவிய மாடமேற் படிந்த","வயல்கள் மேவுநெல் வாயில்வீற் றிருந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet lists the men he has praised: the witless and base, the double-tongued, misers, sinners, fools who adorn women, men who perish melting for women, who do not know the fruit of the books of justice, who take wealth by gambling, and fools fallen into the world's delusion; to them he sang fine Tamil until his thought was ruined and his eyes failed, and now, lying in the fire of poverty like a long worm writhing, he asks Murugan to pity him. The praise sets the heavenly dancers who rejoice and sing embracing their lovers against the wives of the slain demons who weep for their husbands, and names the vel that cut in two the tree that hid in the sea and spread a crore of branches, at Nelvayil with its fields and moon-touched mansions.","n":"சிறந்த தமிழ் கூறி: the confession is not of lust but of misusing Tamil on unworthy patrons, and the list of patrons runs to a dozen kinds. The praise is a diptych of joy and grief, the gods' women and the demons' widows (மணாளர் தோள் பிரிந்து). The tree in the sea (கவடு கோடியின் மேலுமாய்ப் பரந்து வளரும் மா) is Sura's last form, split in two by the vel.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["iravu","inmai","thamizh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:116"},"p:750":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":750,"t":["குடத்தாமரை யாமென வேயிரு","தனத்தார்மதி வாணுத லாரிருள்","குழற்காடின மாமுகில் போல்மது ...... கலைமோதக்","குலக்கார்மயி லாமென வேகயல்","விழித்தார்கர மேல்கொடு மாமுலை","குடத்தியாழ்கிளி யாமென வேகுயில் ...... குரலோசை","படித்தார்மயி லாமென வேநடை","நெளித்தார்பல காமுகர் வார்கலை","பழிப்பாரவ ராசையை மேல்கொடு ...... விலைமாதர்","படிக்கார்மின லாமென வேநகை","புரித்தார்பலர் வாயிதழ் சேர்பொருள்","பறிப்பார்பழி காரிகள் நாரிக ...... ளுறவாமோ","அடைத்தார்கட லோர்வலி ராவண","குலத்தோடரி யோர்சர னார்சின","மழித்தார்முகி லேய்நிற ராகவர் ...... மருகோனே","அறுத்தாரய னார்தலை யேபுர","மெரித்தாரதி லேபுல னாருயி","ரளித்தாருடல் பாதியி லேயுமை ...... அருள்பாலா","விடத்தாரசு ரார்பதி வேரற","அடித்தாய்கதிர் வேல்கொடு சேவகம்","விளைத்தாய்குடி வாழம ரோர்சிறை ...... மிடிதீர","விழித்தாமரை போலழ காகுற","மகட்கானவ ணாஎன தாயுறை","விருத்தாசலம் வாழ்மயில் வாகன ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women with breasts like lotus pots, moon brows, hair like cloud, carp eyes, voices like the yaazh, parrot and cuckoo, and the gait of peacocks, who mock the many lustful men, smile like lightning and strip of wealth those who kiss them, women who sell themselves, and asks whether their friendship befits him. The praise names Rama who bridged the sea and destroyed Ravana's clan as uncle, Siva who cut Brahma's head and burned the cities, Uma in Siva's half, the vel that uprooted the poisonous asuras and freed the gods, Murugan the beautiful one of the hunter girl, at Viruthachalam where 'my mother' dwells.","n":"விலைமாதர், women with a price, and பொருள் பறிப்பார், who snatch wealth, make the charge explicit. என தாய் உறை விருத்தாசலம names the shrine's goddess as the poet's own mother.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","selvam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:117"},"p:751":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":751,"t":["திருமொழி யுரைபெற அரனுன துழிபணி","செயமுன மருளிய ...... குளவோனே","திறலுயர் மதுரையி லமணரை யுயிர்கழு","தெறிபட மறுகிட ...... விடுவோனே","ஒருவரு முனதருள் பரிவில ரவர்களி","னுறுபட ருறுமெனை ...... யருள்வாயோ","உ லகினி லனைவர்கள் புகழ்வுற அருணையில்","ஒருநொடி தனில்வரு ...... மயில்வீரா","கருவரி யுறுபொரு கணைவிழி குறமகள்","கணினெதிர் தருவென ...... முனமானாய்","கருமுகில் பொருநிற அரிதிரு மருமக","கருணையில் மொழிதரு ...... முதல்வோனே","முருகலர் தருவுறை யமரர்கள் சிறைவிட","முரணுறு மசுரனை ...... முனிவோனே","முடிபவர் வடிவறு சுசிகர முறைதமிழ்","முதுகிரி வலம்வரு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"Mostly praise: Murugan, born in the pond, once gave Siva the holy word as Siva served at his side; at Madurai he sent the Jains to the stake; he came to Arunai in an instant so the whole world praised him; he became a tree before the hunter girl's eyes; he is Hari's nephew and the first one who speaks in compassion. In the middle the poet, suffering among those who have no love for Murugan's grace, asks whether he will be given grace, at Mudhukiri, the old hill.","n":"அரன் உனது உழி பணி செய முன் அருளிய: Siva serves at Murugan's side to receive the word, the teaching of Om in the book's usual story, here without naming Om. அருணையில் ஒருநொடி தனில் வரு மயில்வீரா refers to an appearance at Tiruvannamalai, which tradition ties to the poet's own life; the text says only that he came in a moment. The last line (முடிபவர் வடிவறு சுசிகர முறைதமிழ்) is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","thamizh","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:117"},"p:752":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":752,"t":["பசையற்ற வுடல்வற்ற வினைமுற்றி நடைநெட்டி","பறியக்கை சொறியப்பல் ...... வெளியாகிப்","படலைக்கு விழிகெட்ட குருடுற்று மிகநெக்க","பழமுற்று நரைகொக்கி ...... னிறமாகி","விசைபெற்று வருபித்தம் வளியைக்க ணிலைகெட்டு","மெலிவுற்று விரல்பற்று ...... தடியோடே","வெளிநிற்கும் விதமுற்ற இடர்பெற்ற ஜனனத்தை","விடுவித்து னருள்வைப்ப ...... தொருநாளே","அசைவற்ற நிருதர்க்கு மடிவுற்ற பிரியத்தி","னடல்வஜ்ர கரன்மற்று ...... முளவானோர்","அளவற்ற மலர்விட்டு நிலமுற்று மறையச்செய்","அதுலச்ச மரவெற்றி ...... யுடையோனே","வசையற்று முடிவற்று வளர்பற்றி னளவற்ற","வடிவுற்ற முகில்கிட்ணன் ...... மருகோனே","மதுரச்செ மொழிசெப்பி யருள்பெற்ற சிவபத்தர்","வளர் விர்த்த கிரியுற்ற ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes old age: the body dried and sapless as deeds ripen, the gait tottering, the hands scratching, the teeth bare, eyes blinded by cataract, skin wrinkled like fruit, hair white as a crane, bile and wind rising, weak, gripping a staff and left standing outside; he asks whether there will be a day when Murugan frees him from this birth of suffering and sets grace on him. The praise names the gods led by Indra who covered the earth with flowers after Murugan's victory over the asuras, Krishna as his uncle, and the Siva devotees who sang sweet words and won grace at Viruddhagiri.","n":"The portrait of age is built of small physical signs, and its last image, வெளிநிற்கும் விதம், standing outside the house, adds the social exclusion of the old. The plea is not against age itself but against the birth (ஜனனம்) that leads to it. The Siva devotees who won grace with sweet words may be the Tevaram poets; the text does not name them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","noy","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:117"},"p:753":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":753,"t":["குரைகட லுலகினி லுயிர்கொடு போந்து","கூத்தாடு கின்ற ...... குடில்பேணிக்","குகையிட மருவிய கருவிழி மாந்தர்","கோட்டாலை யின்றி ...... யவிரோதம்","வரஇரு வினையற உ ணர்வொடு தூங்கு","வார்க்கே விளங்கு ...... மநுபூதி","வடிவினை யுனதழ கியதிரு வார்ந்த","வாக்கால்மொ ழிந்த ...... ருளவேணும்","திரள்வரை பகமிகு குருகுல வேந்து","தேர்ப்பாகன் மைந்தன் ...... மறையோடு","தெருமர நிசிசரர் மனைவியர் சேர்ந்து","தீப்பாய இந்த்ர ...... புரிவாழ","விரிதிரை யெரியெழ முதலுற வாங்கு","வேற்கார கந்த ...... புவியேழும்","மிடிகெட விளைவன வளவயல் சூழ்ந்த","வேப்பூர மர்ந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks that, rather than tending the hut that carries life and dances in this sea-girt world and the dark-eyed people with their quarrels, he be told by Murugan's own holy word the form of the experience (அநுபூதி) that shines only to those who, both kinds of deeds ended, sleep in awareness. The praise names an episode of the Kuru clan, the wives of the night-roaming demons leaping into fire while Indra's city prospers, the vel that set the sea ablaze to its depths, and Veppur, whose fertile fields end want in the seven worlds.","n":"இருவினையற உணர்வொடு தூங்குவார்க்கே விளங்கும் அநுபூதி: the state is open only to those in whom good and bad deeds have ceased and who sleep yet are aware, a known description of the knower's waking sleep. The plea is for teaching by word (வாக்கால் மொழிந்தருள). குருகுல வேந்து தேர்ப்பாகன் மைந்தன் probably alludes to Krishna as Arjuna's charioteer, but how the phrase splits and whose son is meant is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","vinai","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:118"},"p:754":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":754,"t":["அஞ்சுவித பூத முங்கரண நாலு","மந்திபகல் யாது ...... மறியாத","அந்தநடு வாதி யொன்றுமில தான","அந்தவொரு வீடு ...... பெறுமாறு","மஞ்சுதவழ் சார லஞ்சயில வேடர்","மங்கைதனை நாடி ...... வனமீது","வந்தசர ணார விந்தமது பாட","வண்டமிழ்வி நோத ...... மருள்வாயே","குஞ்சரக லாப வஞ்சியபி ராம","குங்குமப டீர ...... வதிரேகக்","கும்பதன மீது சென்றணையு மார்ப","குன்றுதடு மாற ...... இகல்கோப","வெஞ்சமர சூர னெஞ்சுபக வீர","வென்றிவடி வேலை ...... விடுவோனே","விம்பமதில் சூழு நிம்பபுர வாண","விண்டலம கீபர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks to gain that one release which knows neither the five elements, the four inner faculties, night nor day, and has no end, middle or beginning, and for the delight of fine Tamil to sing the lotus feet that came into the forest seeking the hunter maiden of the misty slopes. The praise names Murugan's chest that embraces Devayanai's kumkum and sandal breasts, the vel thrown so Sura's heart split and the mountain shook, and the lord of Nimbapuram with its walls, the king of heaven and earth.","n":"The release is defined by negation: அஞ்சுவித பூதமும் கரண நாலும் are tattvas, and அந்தி பகல் யாதும் அறியாத is the state beyond waking and sleep. The way to it is song: the feet to be sung are those that walked to Valli, so the love story becomes the subject of the release-giving Tamil. குஞ்சர கலாப வஞ்சி is Devayanai, raised by Indra's elephant.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["veedu","thiruvadi","thamizh","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:119"},"p:755":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":755,"t":["நாட்டந் தங்கிக் கொங்கைக் குவடிற் ...... படியாதே","நாட்டுந் தொண்டர்க் கண்டக் கமலப் ...... பதமீவாய்","வாட்டங் கண்டுற் றண்டத் தமரப் ...... படைமீதே","மாற்றந் தந்துப் பந்திச் சமருக் ...... கெதிரானோர்","கூட்டங் கந்திச் சிந்திச் சிதறப் ...... பொருவோனே","கூற்றன் பந்திச் சிந்தைக் குணமொத் ...... தொளிர்வேலா","வேட்டந் தொந்தித் தந்திப் பரனுக் ...... கிளையோனே","வேப்பஞ் சந்திக் கந்தக் குமரப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A short song: the poet asks that his gaze not settle and sink on the hills of women's breasts, and names Murugan as the one who gives his lotus feet to the devotees who hold firm. The praise names the battle in which he scattered the hosts who came against the gods, the vel that shines with the mind of Death, and Murugan as younger brother of the pot-bellied elephant god, at Veppanchandhi.","n":"The song is in clipped beats, and the plea is one line: நாட்டம் தங்கிக் கொங்கைக் குவடில் படியாதே, the eye is the point of danger. கூற்றன் பந்திச் சிந்தைக் குணமொத்து ஒளிர்வேலா, a vel like Death in temper, is my reading of a hard split.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","thondu","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:120"},"p:756":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":756,"t":["வாட்டியெனைச் சூழ்ந்தவினை யாசையமு வாசையனல்","மூட்டியுலைக் காய்ந்தமழு வாமெனவி காசமொடு","மாட்டியெனைப் பாய்ந்துகட வோடடமொ டாடிவிடு ...... விஞ்சையாலே","வாய்த்தமலர்ச் சாந்துபுழு கானபனி நீர்களொடு","காற்றுவரத் தாங்குவன மார்பிலணி யாரமொடு","வாய்க்குமெனப் பூண்டழக தாகபவி சோடுமகிழ் ...... வன்புகூரத்","தீட்டுவிழிக் காந்திமட வார்களுட னாடிவலை","பூட்டிவிடப் போந்துபிணி யோடுவலி வாதமென","சேர்த்துவிடப் பேர்ந்துவினை மூடியடி யேனுமுன ...... தன்பிலாமல்","தேட்டமுறத் தேர்ந்துமமிர் தாமெனவெ யேகிநம","னோட்டிவிடக் காய்ந்துவரி வேதனடை யாளமருள்","சீட்டுவரக் காண்டுநலி காலனணு காநினரு ...... ளன்புதாராய்","வேட்டுவரைக் காய்ந்துகுற மாதையுற வாடியிருள்","நாட்டவரைச் சேந்தகதிர் வேல்கொடம ராடிசிறை","மீட்டமரர்க் காண்டவனை வாழ்கநிலை யாகவைகும் ...... விஞ்சையோனே","வேற்றுருவிற் போந்துமது ராபுரியி லாடிவைகை","யாற்றின்மணற் றாங்குமழு வாளியென தாதைபுர","மேட்டையெரித் தாண்டசிவ லோகன்விடை யேறியிட ...... முங்கொளாயி","கோட்டுமுலைத் தாங்குமிழை யானஇடை கோடிமதி","தோற்றமெனப் போந்தஅழ கானசிவ காமிவிறல்","கூற்றுவனைக் காய்ந்தஅபி ராமிமன தாரஅருள் ...... கந்தவேளே","கூட்டுநதித் தேங்கியவெ ளாறுதர ளாறுதிகழ்","நாட்டிலுறைச் சேந்தமயி லாவளிதெய் வானையொடெ","கூற்றுவிழத் தாண்டியென தாகமதில் வாழ்குமர ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says that his deeds surrounded him, desire and the three desires lit a fire like an axe heated in the forge, and that, adorned with flowers, sandal, civet and rosewater, he sported with women of painted eyes until caught in their net, then disease, pain and wind came on him, and he had no love for Murugan; he asks that when Yama's men drive him and the summons marked by Brahma arrives, cruel Kalan not come near, and that Murugan give him his grace and love. The praise names Murugan who freed the gods with the vel and loved the hunter girl, Siva who came to Madurai in another form and bore sand on the Vaigai's bank, burned the cities and rides the bull, the goddess who burned Death, and Murugan who leapt so that Death fell and who lives in the poet's body, at the meeting of the Vellar and Manimuthar.","n":"மூவாசை are the three desires, for land, gold and women by tradition. சீட்டு is Death's written summons, which Brahma has marked (வேதன் அடையாளம்). The praise turns the same motif round: கூற்றுவனைக் காய்ந்த அபிராமி and கூற்று விழத் தாண்டி name the goddess and Murugan as conquerors of Death. மதுராபுரியில் ஆடி வைகை ஆற்றின் மணல் தாங்கு is Siva's carrying earth at Madurai, and எனது ஆகமதில் வாழ் places Murugan within the poet.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","avaa","kaamam","noy"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:121"},"p:757":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":757,"t":["வாருமிங் கேவீடி தோபணம் பாஷாண","மால்கடந் தேபோமே ...... னியலு¡டே","வாடிபெண் காள்பாயை போடுமென் றாசார","வாசகம் போல்கூறி ...... யணைமீதே","சேருமுன் காசாடை வாவியும் போதாமை","தீமைகொண் டேபோமெ ...... னடமாதர்","சேரிடம் போகாம லாசுவந் தேறாமல்","சீதளம் பாதார ...... மருள்வாயே","நாரணன் சீராம கேசவன் கூராழி","நாயகன் பூவாயன் ...... மருகோனே","நாரதும் பூர்கீத மோதநின் றேயாடு","நாடகஞ் சேய்தாள ...... ரருள்பாலா","சூரணங் கோடாழி போய்கிடந் தேவாட","சூரியன் தேரோட ...... அயிலேவீ","தூநறுங் காவேரி சேருமொண் சீறாறு","சூழ்கடம் பூர்தேவர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes dancing women who say 'come here, this is the house', tell their maids to spread the mat, speak like courteous hosts and take a man to bed, then before joining him say the cash and the cloth are not enough and go off in spite; he asks not to go where they are and to be given Murugan's cool feet. The praise names Narayana, Rama, Kesava with the discus as his uncle, Siva who dances while Narada and Tumburu sing as his father, and the vel that sent Sura's sea to ruin so the sun's chariot could run, at Kadambur where the Kaveri and Seeraru meet.","n":"The women's own speech is quoted (வாரும் இங்கே வீடிதோ, வாடி பெண்காள் பாயைப் போடும்), as in Kural and Naladiyar warnings; ஆசார வாசகம் போல், like words of courtesy, names the pretence. காசு ஆடை போதாமை is the turn: love lasts until the price falls short. Some words of the opening (பணம் பாஷாணம், மால் கடந்தே போம் மேனி) are hard to construe.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["varaivinmagalir","selvam","vanjam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:122"},"p:758":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":758,"t":["கறுவி மைக்கணிட் டினித ழைத்தியற்","கவிசொ லிச்சிரித் ...... துறவாடிக்","களவு வித்தையட் டுளமு ருக்கிமுற்","கருதி வைத்தவைப் ...... பவைசேரத்","தறுக ணிற்பறித் திருக ழுத்துறத்","தழுவி நெக்குநெக் ...... குயிர்சோரச்","சயன மெத்தையிற் செயல ழிக்குமித்","தருணி கட்ககப் ...... படலாமோ","பிறவி யைத்தணித் தருளு நிட்களப்","பிரம சிற்சுகக் ...... கடல்மூழ்கும்","பெருமு னித்திரட் பரவு செய்ப்பதிப்","ப்ரபல கொச்சையிற் ...... சதுர்வேதச்","சிறுவ நிற்கருட் கவிகை நித்திலச்","சிவிகை யைக்கொடுத் ...... தருளீசன்","செகத லத்தினிற் புகழ்ப டைத்தமெய்த்","திருவ ரத்துறைப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women who, angry or kind, put on kohl, call sweetly, recite poems, laugh and sport, melt the heart with arts of stealth, cruelly snatch away what a man had saved, embrace his neck and leave him faint on the bed, and asks whether he should be caught by them. The praise names Murugan as the one who stills birth, the partless Brahman in whose sea of conscious bliss sages bathe, and as the four-Veda child of Kochai to whom the Lord gave the pearl umbrella and palanquin, at Thiruvarathurai.","n":"களவு வித்தை and முன் கருதி வைத்த வைப்பவை சேர தறுகணில் பறித்து: theft is named twice, of the heart and of the hoard. கொச்சை is Sirkazhi, and the pearl palanquin at Arathurai belongs to Sambandar's story; the song addresses that child as Murugan (சதுர்வேதச் சிறுவ), the book's identification of Murugan with Sambandar.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:123"},"p:759":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":759,"t":["பூத்தார் சூடுங் கொத்தலர் குழலியர்","பார்த்தால் வேலுங் கட்கமு மதன்விடு","போர்க்கார் நீடுங் கட்சர மொடுநமன் ...... விடுதூதும்","போற்றாய் நாளுங் கைப்பொரு ளுடையவர்","மேற்றா ளார்தம் பற்றிடு ப்ரமையது","பூட்டா மாயங் கற்றமை விழியின ...... ரமுதூறல்","வாய்த்தார் பேதஞ் செப்புபொய் விரகியர்","நூற்றேய் நூலின் சிற்றிடை யிடர்பட","வாட்டாய் வீசுங் கர்ப்புர ம்ருகமத ...... மகிலாரம்","மாப்பூ ணாரங் கச்சணி முலையினர்","வேட்பூ ணாகங் கெட்டெனை யுனதுமெய்","வாக்கால் ஞானம் பெற்றினி வழிபட ...... அருளாயோ","ஆத்தாள் மால்தங் கைச்சிக னிகையுமை","கூத்தா டாநந் தச்சிவை திரிபுரை","யாட்பேய் பூதஞ் சுற்றிய பயிரவி ...... புவநேசை","ஆக்கா யாவும் பற்றியெ திரிபுற","நோக்கா ஏதுஞ் செற்றவள் திருவிளை","யாட்டா லீசன் பக்கம் துறைபவள் ...... பெறுசேயே","ஏத்தா நாளுந் தர்ப்பண செபமொடு","நீத்தார் ஞானம் பற்றிய குருபர","யாப்பா ராயுஞ் சொற்றமி ழருள்தரு ...... முருகோனே","ஏற்போர் தாம்வந் திச்சையின் மகிழ்வொடு","வாய்ப்பாய் வீசும் பொற்ப்ரபை நெடுமதிள்","யாழ்ப்பா ணாயன் பட்டின மருவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes flower-haired women whose glance is spear and sword, Kama's war arrows and Death's own messenger, who daily bind those with money in hand in delusion, skilled in illusion, with nectar lips and lying words, thread waists and perfumed, jewelled breasts, and asks Murugan to destroy his desire for their bodies and grant that by his true word he gain knowledge and worship him. The praise is a litany of the mother: Vishnu's sister, the maiden Uma, Sivai who dances in bliss, Tripurai, Bhairavi among ghouls, lady of the worlds who makes, holds and destroys and dwells at Siva's side in play; her son is the guru of the renouncers' knowledge and the giver of the Tamil prosodists weigh, at Yazhppanayanpattinam with its golden walls.","n":"நமன் விடு தூது: the women's eyes are Death's messengers, joining the book's two themes in one image. கைப்பொருள் உடையவர் marks them as women who want money. The goddess litany (திரிபுரை, பயிரவி, புவநேசை) uses Sakta names. யாழ்ப்பாணாயன் பட்டினம் is Jaffna, one of the book's shrines in Lanka.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","gnaanam","saththi","thamizh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:124"},"p:760":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":760,"t":["கழைமுத்து மாலை புயல்முத்து மாலை","கரிமுத்து மாலை ...... மலைமேவுங்","கடிமுத்து மாலை வளைமுத்து மாலை","கடல்முத்து மாலை ...... யரவீனும்","அழல்முத்து மாலை யிவைமுற்று மார்பி","னடைவொத்து லாவ ...... அடியேன்முன்","அடர்பச்சை மாவி லருளிற்பெ ணோடு","மடிமைக்கு ழாமொ ...... டருள்வாயே","மழையொத்த சோதி குயில்தத்தை போலு","மழலைச்சொ லாயி ...... யெமையீனு","மதமத்த நீல களநித்த நாதர்","மகிழ்சத்தி யீனு ...... முருகோனே","செழுமுத்து மார்பி னமுதத்தெய் வானை","திருமுத்தி மாதின் ...... மணவாளா","சிறையிட்ட சூரர் தளைவெட்டி ஞான","திருமுட்ட மேவு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks Murugan to come before him on the green peacock with his consort and the throng of his servants, wearing on his chest garlands of pearls from bamboo, cloud, elephant, mountain, conch, sea, serpent and fire. The praise names the mother who bore us, bright as rain-cloud and lisping like cuckoo and parrot, the dark-throated Siva with datura, Murugan their son, the bridegroom of Devayanai and of the lady of release, who cut the bonds of the gods Sura had imprisoned, at Thirumuttam.","n":"The pearl list follows the traditional sources of pearls (bamboo, cloud, elephant tusk, conch, sea, cobra), each as a separate garland; the plea is simply to see. அடிமைக் குழாமொடு: Murugan is asked to come with the company of his servants, not alone. திருமுத்தி மாதின் மணவாளா, bridegroom of the lady of release, is either a name of Valli or a figure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["adiyaar","thondu","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:125"},"p:761":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":761,"t":["சரம்வெற் றிக்கய லாமெனும் வேல்விழி","சிலைவட் டப்புரு வார்குழல் கார்முகில்","தனமுத் துக்கிரி யாமெனு நூலிடை ...... மடவார்கள்","சனுமெத் தப்பரி வாகிய மாமய","லிடுமுத் தித்திகழ் மால்கொடு பாவையர்","தகுதத் தக்கிட தோதகு தீதென ...... விளையாடும்","விரகத் துர்க்குண வேசைய ராசையர்","பணமெத் தப்பறி காரிகள் மாறிகள்","விதமெத் தக்கொடு மேவிகள் பாவிகள் ...... அதிபோக","மெலிவுற் றுக்குறி நாறிகள் பீறிகள்","கலகத் தைச்செயு மோடிகள் பீடிகள்","விருதிட் டுக்குடி கேடிகள் சேடிகள் ...... உ றவாமோ","பொருவெற் றிக்கழை வார்சிலை யானுட","லெரிபட் டுச்சரு காய்விழ வேநகை","புகுவித் தப்பிறை வாழ்சடை யானிட ...... மொருமாது","புகழ்சத் திச்சிலு காவண மீதுறை","சிவபத் திப்பர மேஸ்வரி யாள்திரி","புவனத் தைப்பரி வாய்முத லீனுமை ...... யருள்பாலா","திரையிற் பொற்கிரி யாடவும் வாசுகி","புனைவித் துத்தலை நாளமு தார்சுவை","சிவபத் தர்க்கிது வாமென வேபகி ...... ரரிராமர்","திருவுற் றுப்பணி யாதிவ ராகர்த","மகளைப் பொற்றன வாசையொ டாடிய","திருமுட் டப்பதி வாழ்முரு காசுரர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women with arrow-eyes and bow brows who stir deep infatuation and dance to the beat, and calls them greedy prostitutes who snatch money, deceivers, sinners, worn by excess, quarrel-makers, ruiners of families, and asks whether their friendship befits him. The praise names Siva who burned Kama with laughter, the goddess at his side who bore the three worlds, Rama who churned the sea with Vasuki and gave the first nectar, and Murugan who sported with the daughter of Adi Varaha at Thirumuttam.","n":"The second half of the confession is a run of abusive nouns in -கள் (பறிகாரிகள், மாறிகள், கேடிகள்) set to the rhythm; பணம் எத்தப் பறிகாரிகள் and குடிகேடிகள் carry the charge. ஆதிவராகர் தம் மகள் makes Valli the daughter of Vishnu as the Boar, whose temple is at Srimushnam.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","selvam","vanjam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:125"},"p:762":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":762,"t":["மூல முண்டகனு பூதி மந்திரப","ராப ரஞ்சுடர்கள் மூணு மண்டலஅ","தார சந்திமுக மாறு மிந்த்ரதரு ...... வுந்தளாமேல்","மூது ரம்பலவர் பீட மந்தமுமி","லாத பந்தவொளி யாயி ரங்கிரண","மூணு மிந்துவொளிர் சோதி விண்படிக ...... விந்துநாதம்","ஓல மென்றுபல தாள சந்தமிடு","சேவை கண்டமுதை வாரி யுண்டுலகி","ரேழு கண்டுவிளை யாடி யிந்துகதி ...... ரங்கிசூலம்","ஓடு மந்தகலி காலொ டுங்கநடு","தூணில் தங்கவரி ஞான வண்கயிறு","மீத ணைந்துசத கோடி சந்த்ரவொளி ...... சந்தியாதோ","சூலி யந்தரிக பாலி சங்கரிபு","ராரி யம்பரிகு மாரி யெண்குணசு","வாமி பங்கிசிவ காம சுந்தரியு ...... கந்தசேயே","சூர சங்கரகு மார இந்திரச","காய அன்பருப கார சுந்தரகு","காஎ னுஞ்சுருதி யோல மொன்றநட ...... னங்கொள்வேலா","சீல வெண்பொடியி டாத வெஞ்சமணர்","மாள வெங்கழுவி லேறு மென்றுபொடி","நீறி டுங்கமல பாணி சந்த்ரமுக ...... கந்தவேளே","தேவ ரம்பையமு தீண மங்கைதரு","மான ணைந்தபுய தீர சங்கரதி","யாகர் வந்துறைந லு¡ர மர்ந்துவளர் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes an inner ascent: the root centre and the mantra of experience, the supreme lights of the three mandalas, the six centres, the boundless light of a thousand rays, bindu and nada, the sound of 'Olam' kept to many rhythms, the nectar drunk, the course of moon, sun and fire stilled, and the climb up the middle pillar on the rope of knowledge, and asks whether he will not meet the light of a hundred crore moons. The praise names the goddess by many names, Murugan as destroyer of Sura and helper of Indra, who dances to the sound of the Veda, the lotus-handed one who put sacred ash on so that the Jains who refused it died on the stake, the lover of Devayanai and the doe, at Nallur where Siva as Thiyagar dwells.","n":"The plea is a single question (சத கோடி சந்த்ர ஒளி சந்தியாதோ) at the top of a ladder of yogic terms: மூலம், மண்டலம் மூணு, விந்து நாதம், நடு தூண் (the central channel), ஞான வண் கயிறு. சீல வெண்பொடி இடாத வெஞ்சமணர் மாள ... நீறிடும் is the Sambandar story again. Many compounds in the first half split uncertainly.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["yogam","aadhaaram","naadham","amudham"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:126"},"p:763":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":763,"t":["வண்டணி யுங்கமழ் கூந்த லார்விழி","அம்பிய லுஞ்சிலை போந்த வாணுதல்","வண்டர ளந்திக ழாய்ந்த வார்நகை ...... குயில்போல","வண்பயி லுங்குவ டாண்ட மார்முலை","யின்பொறி யங்குமி ழாம்பல் தோள்கர","வஞ்சியெ னுங்கொடி சேர்ந்த நூலிடை ...... மடவார்பொன்","கண்டவு டன்களி கூர்ந்து பேசிகள்","குண்டுணி யுங்குரல் சாங்க மோதிகள்","கண்சுழ லும்படி தாண்டி யாடிகள் ...... சதிகாரர்","கஞ்சுளி யுந்தடி யீந்து போவென","நஞ்சையி டுங்கவ டார்ந்த பாவிகள்","கம்பையி லுஞ்சட மாய்ந்து நாயனு ...... முழல்வேனோ","அண்டரு டன்தவ சேந்து மாதவர்","புண்டரி கன்திரு பாங்கர் கோவென","அஞ்சலெ னும்படி போந்து வீரமொ ...... டசுராரை","அங்கமொ டுங்கிட மாண்டொ டாழிக","ளென்கிரி யும்பொடி சாம்பர் நூறிட","அந்தக னுங்கயி றாங்கை வீசிட ...... விடும்வேலா","செண்டணி யுஞ்சடை பாந்தள் நீர்மதி","யென்பணி யன்கன சாம்பல் பூசிய","செஞ்சட லன்சுத சேந்த வேலவ ...... முருகொனே","திங்கள்மு கந்தன சாந்து மார்பின","ளென்றனு ளம்புகு பாங்கி மானொடு","சிந்தைம கிழ்ந்தும யேந்த்ர மேவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women with bee-haunted hair, arrow eyes, pearl teeth, cuckoo voices and creeper waists who rejoice and chatter when they see gold, gossip, flatter and dance making eyes whirl, cheats who, when the gold is gone, hand a man the begging bowl and staff and say 'go', and even give poison, and asks whether he, a dog, must wander and perish for them. The praise tells how, when the gods and sages and Brahma cried out, Murugan came saying 'fear not' and threw the vel that crushed the asuras, turned mountains to dust while Death swung his noose, names him son of Siva with the ash and serpent and moon in his hair, and lover of the moon-faced girl who has entered the poet's heart, at Mayendram.","n":"பொன் கண்டவுடன் களிகூர்ந்து and கஞ்சுளியும் தடியும் ஈந்து போ என: the women's welcome begins at gold and ends by sending the man away as a beggar. அந்தகனும் கயிறு ஆங்கை வீசிட turns Death's noose against the asuras. என்றன் உளம் புகு பாங்கி மானொடு is uncertain: the doe may be Valli, and பாங்கி may be her companion.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","selvam","vanjam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:127"},"p:764":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":764,"t":["அலைகடல் சிலைமதன் அந்தி யூதையும்","அரிவையர் வசையுட னங்கி போல்வர","அசைவன விடைமணி யன்றில் கோகிலம் ...... அஞ்சிநானும்","அழலிடு மெழுகென வெம்பி வேர்வெழ","அகிலொடு ம்ருகமத நஞ்சு போலுற","அணிபணி மணிபல வெந்து நீறெழ ...... அங்கம்வேறாய்","முலைகனல் சொரிவர முன்பு போல்நினை","வழிவச மறஅற நின்று சோர்வுற","முழுதுகொள் விரகனல் மொண்டு வீசிட ...... மங்கிடாதே","முருகவிழ் திரள்புய முந்து வேலணி","முளரியொ டழகிய தொங்கல் தாரினை","முனிவற நினதருள் தந்தென் மாலைமு ...... னிந்திடாதோ","சிலைநுதல் கயல்விழி செஞ்சொல் வானவி","திரிபுரை பயிரவி திங்கள் சூடிய","திகழ்சடை நெடியவள் செம்பொன் மேனியள் ...... சிங்கமேறி","திரள்படை யலகைகள் பொங்கு கோடுகள்","திமிலையொ டறைபறை நின்று மோதிட","சிவனுட னடம்வரு மங்கை மாதுமை ...... தந்தவேளே","மலைதனி லொருமுநி தந்த மாதுதன்","மாலரடி வருடியெ நின்று நாடொறு","மயில்பயில் குயில்கிளி வம்பி லேகடி ...... தொண்டினோனே","மழைமுகில் தவழ்தரு மண்டு கோபுர","மதிள்வயல் புடையுற விஞ்சு காழியில்","வருமொரு கவுணியர் மைந்த தேவர்கள் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"In the voice of a lovesick girl, the singer says the sea, Kama's bow, the evening wind, the blame of women, the swinging bull-bells, the anril and the cuckoo come at her like fire; she melts like wax, sweats, finds musk and agil like poison, her ornaments burn to ash and her breasts pour heat, and she asks whether, his anger ended, he will not give her his honeyed, vel-adorned garland of lotus and end her longing. The praise names Uma as Bhairavi and Tripura, riding the lion and dancing with Siva among ghosts and drums, Valli who served at his feet, and Murugan the Kaundinya son of Kazhi.","n":"அரிவையர் வசை, the blame of women, is the gossip (அலர்) of classical akam, set beside the anril, the cuckoo and the bull's bell. என் மாலை is probably மால், her infatuation, playing on the garland (தார்) she asks for. கவுணியர் மைந்த, son of the Kaundinyas, is Sambandar's clan name given to Murugan. The lines on Valli serving his feet among peacocks, cuckoos and parrots are hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","maalai","alar"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:128"},"p:765":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":765,"t":["இரத மான தேனூற லதர மான மாமாத","ரெதிரி லாத பூணார ...... முலைமீதே","இனது போடு மேகாச உ டையி னாலு மாலால","விழியி னாலு மாலாகி ...... யநுராக","விரக மாகி யேபாய லிடைவி டாமல் நாடோறு","ம்ருகம தாதி சேரோதி ...... நிழல்மூழ்கி","விளையு மோக மாமாயை கழலு மாறு நாயேனும்","விழல னாய்வி டாதேநி ...... னருள்தாராய்","அரக ராஎ னர்முடர் திருவெ ணீறி டர்முடர்","அடிகள் பூசி யர்முடர் ...... கரையேற","அறிவு நூல்க லர்முடர் நெறியி லேநி லர்முடர்","அறம்வி சாரி யர்முடர் ...... நரகேழிற்","புரள வீழ்வ ¡£ராறு கரவி நோத சேய்சோதி","புரண பூர ணாகார ...... முருகோனே","புயலு லாவு சேணாடு பரவி நாளு மீடேறு","புகலி மேவி வாழ்தேவர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses that, maddened by the honeyed lips, breasts, slipping silk and poisonous eyes of women, he lay day after day in passion, sunk in the shade of their musk-scented hair, and asks that, worthless as he is, the great delusion this breeds come loose and grace be given. The praise speaks of the fools who do not say Hara, do not wear the holy ash, do not anoint the Lord's feet, do not learn the books of knowledge, keep to no path and weigh no dharma, rolling in the seven hells, and calls Murugan the light and full form who lives at Pugali.","n":"The repeated மூடர் (written முடர் here) marks a list of the irreligious: those who do not cry அரகரா, wear திருவெணீறு or learn அறிவு நூல். கரையேற, 'to reach the shore', sits in the list in a way that leaves unclear whether these fools are the ones Murugan lifts out or the ones who fall; the reading takes the seven hells as their end. விழலன், a useless reed, is the poet's name for himself.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["mayal","naragam","thiruneeru","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:128"},"p:766":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":766,"t":["ஊனத்தசை தோல்கள் சுமந்த காயப்பொதி மாய மிகுந்த","ஊசற்சுடு நாறு குரம்பை ...... மறைநாலும்","ஓதப்படு நாலு முகன்ற னாலுற்றிடு கோல மெழுந்து","ஓடித்தடு மாறி யுழன்று ...... தளர்வாகிக்","கூனித்தடி யோடு நடந்து ஈனப்படு கோழை மிகுந்த","கூளச்சட மீதை யுகந்து ...... புவிமீதே","கூசப்பிர மாண ப்ரபஞ்ச மாயக்கொடு நோய்க ளகன்று","கோலக்கழ லேபெற இன்று ...... அருள்வாயே","சேனக்குரு கூடலி லன்று ஞானத்தமிழ் நூல்கள் பகர்ந்து","சேனைச்சம ணோர்கழு வின்கண் ...... மிசையேறத்","தீரத்திரு நீறு புரிந்து மீனக்கொடி யோனுடல் துன்று","தீமைப்பிணி தீர வுவந்த ...... குருநாதா","கானச்சிறு மானை நினைந்து ஏனற்புன மீது நடந்து","காதற்கிளி யோடு மொழிந்து ...... சிலைவேடர்","காணக்கணி யாக வளர்ந்து ஞானக்குற மானை மணந்து","காழிப்பதி மேவி யுகந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet calls the body a bundle of flesh and skin, a stinking, pus-hot hut shaped by the four-faced Brahma who recites the Vedas, which runs, stumbles, tires, walks bent on a stick and fills with phlegm, and says he has loved this rubbish-body; he asks that the cruel diseases of the illusory world leave him and that today he be given the beautiful feet. The praise makes Murugan the teacher who at Kudal spoke Tamil books of wisdom, sent the Jain host up the stakes and with holy ash cured the fish-bannered king's fever, and who wooed Valli in the millet field and lives at Kazhi.","n":"ப்ரபஞ்ச மாயக் கொடுநோய்கள்: the diseases are of the world as maya, not of the body only. The second half tells Sambandar's Madurai story in detail (ஞானத்தமிழ் நூல்கள் பகர்ந்து, சமணோர் கழுவின்கண் மிசையேற, திருநீறு, மீனக்கொடியோன்) and calls its hero குருநாதா, so the saint is Murugan himself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","maayai","thamizh","thiruneeru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:128"},"p:767":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":767,"t":["ஒய்யா ரச்சிலை யாமென வாசனை","மெய்யா ரப்பணி பூஷண மாலைக","ளுய்யா நற்கலை யேகொடு மாமத ...... விதமாகி","ஒவ்வா ரிப்படி யோரென வேயிரு","கையா ரக்கணை மோதிர மேய்பல","வுள்ளார் செப்பிட ஏமுற நாளிலு ...... முடல்பேணிச்","செய்வா ரிப்படி யேபல வாணிப","மிய்யா ரிற்பண மேயொரு காசிடை","செய்யார் சற்பனை காரர்பி சாசரு ...... னடிபேணாச்","செய்வா ரிற்படு நானொரு பாதகன்","மெய்யா எப்படி யோர்கரை சேர்வது","செய்யா யற்புத மேபெற வோர்பொரு ...... ளருள்வாயே","மையா ரக்கிரி யேபொடி யாய்விட","பொய்சூ ரப்பதி யேகெட வானவர்","வையாய் பொற்சர ணாஎன வேதொழ ...... விடும்வேலா","வையா ளிப்பரி வாகன மாகொளு","துவ்வா ழிக்கட லேழ்மலை தூளிசெய்","மைபோ லக்கதி ரேய்நிற மாகிய ...... மயில்வாழ்வே","தெய்வா னைக்கர சேகுற மான்மகிழ்","செய்யா முத்தமி ழாகர னேபுகழ்","தெய்வீ கப்பர மாகுரு வேயென ...... விருதூதத்","திய்யா ரக்கழு வேறிட நீறிடு","கையா அற்புத னேபிர மாபுர","செய்கா ழிப்பதி வாழ்முரு காசுரர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women graceful as a bow, scented, jewelled and finely clothed, praised as matchless by ring-handed men, who tend their bodies, run many trades and will not give a man a moment without money, deceivers who do not worship the Lord's feet; fallen among them, a sinner, he asks how he can reach the shore and begs for one gift by a wonder. The praise shows the vel that turned the dark hill to dust and ruined Sura's city, the peacock, Devayanai and Valli, heralds blowing 'divine supreme guru', and the hand that gave ash and sent the Jains to the stakes at Brahmapuram, Kazhi.","n":"பணமே ஒரு காசு and சற்பனைகாரர் (deceivers) mark the women as courtesans. பாதகன், sinner, and எப்படி ஓர் கரை சேர்வது set the plea as a crossing. நீறிடு கையா ... கழுவேறிட again gives Sambandar's deeds to Murugan; பிரமாபுரம் is one of Sirkazhi's twelve names. The opening lines are hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","kutram","thamizh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:128"},"p:768":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":768,"t":["கட்கா மக்ரோ தத்தே கட்சீ","மிழ்த்தோர் கட்குக் ...... கவிபாடிக்","கச்சா பிச்சா கத்தா வித்தா","ரத்தே யக்கொட் ...... களைநீளக்","கொட்கா லக்கோ லக்ஆகா ணத்தே","யிட்டா சைப்பட் ...... டிடவேவை","கொட்டா னக்கூ னுக்கா எய்த்தே","னித்தீ தத்தைக் ...... களைவாயே","வெட்கா மற்பாய் சுற்று¡ மர்ச்சேர்","விக்கா னத்தைத் ...... தரிமாறன்","வெப்பா றப்பா டிக்கா ழிக்கே","புக்காய் வெற்பிற் ...... குறமானை","முட்கா னிற்கால் வைத்தோ டிப்போய்","முற்சார் செச்சைப் ...... புயவீரா","முத்தா முத்தீ யத்தா சுத்தா","முத்தா முத்திப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"In tight, clipped rhythm the poet says he sang verses for men blind with drink, lust and anger, in rambling, padded words, and wore himself out for a bent and wretched gain, and asks Murugan to remove this evil. The praise tells how he sang away the fever of the Pandya Maran, against the shameless mat-wrapped Jains, and came to Kazhi, chased Valli through the thorny forest, and calls him pearl, the three fires, father, pure one, ancient one, lord of release.","n":"The confession is of the patron-poet's trade, singing praise to the unworthy (கவிபாடிக் கச்சா பிச்சா), as in 245 and 773. மாறன் வெப்பு ஆறப் பாடி is Sambandar curing the Pandya's fever; பாய் சுற்று அமணர் are the Jains wrapped in mats. The compressed syllables make most of the plea uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["iravu","thamizh","kutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:128"},"p:769":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":769,"t":["கொங்கு லாவிய குழலினு நிழலினு","நஞ்ச ளாவிய விழியினு மிரணிய","குன்று போல்வளர் முலையினு நிலையினு ...... மடமாதர்","கொம்பு சேர்வன இடையினு நடையினு","மன்பு கூர்வன மொழியினு மெழில்குடி","கொண்ட சேயித ழமுதினு நகையினு ...... மனதாய","சங்கை யாளியை அணுவிடை பிளவள","வின்சொல் வாசக மொழிவன இவையில","சம்ப்ர தாயனை அவலனை ஒளிதிக ...... ழிசைகூருந்","தண்டை நூபுர மணுகிய இருகழல்","கண்டு நாளவ மிகையற விழியருள்","தந்த பேரருள் கனவிலு நனவிலு ...... மறவேனே","வங்க வாரிதி முறையிட நிசிசரர்","துங்க மாமுடி பொடிபட வடவனல்","மங்கி நீறெழ அலகைகள் நடமிட ...... மயிலேறி","வஞ்ச வேல்கொடு முனிபவ அழகிய","சண்பை மாநக ருறையுமொ ரறுமுக","வந்த வானவர் மனதினி லிடர்கெட ...... நினைவோனே","பங்க வீரியர் பறிதலை விரகினர்","மிஞ்சு பாதக ரறநெறி பயனிலர்","பந்த மேவிய பகடிகள் கபடிகள் ...... நிலைகேடர்","பண்பி லாதவர் கொலைசெயு மனதின","ரிங்கெ ணாயிர ருயரிய கழுமிசை","பஞ்ச பாதகர் முனைகெட அருளிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says he was drawn by women's hair, poisoned eyes, breasts, waists, gait, words, lips and smiles, a doubter without tradition and a wretch, yet the Lord gave him by grace the sight of the two anklet-bearing feet that ended his wasted days, and this he will not forget in dream or waking. The praise shows the sea crying out and the demons' crowns broken by the vel, Murugan of Sanbai who thinks of the gods' troubles, and ends with a long abuse of the Jains as sinners without dharma and the eight thousand sent to the stakes.","n":"Unusually the plea is a thanksgiving: grace has already been given (விழி அருள் தந்த பேரருள்), and the vow is கனவிலும் நனவிலும் மறவேனே. The abuse of the Jains (பறிதலை, அறநெறி பயனிலர், கழுமிசை) takes up the whole close; எணாயிரர், the eight thousand, is the number the Sambandar story gives.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","thiruvadi","kaamam","samayam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:128"},"p:770":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":770,"t":["சந்த னம்பரி மளபுழு கொடுபுனை","கொங்கை வஞ்சியர் சரியொடு கொடுவளை","தங்கு செங்கையர் அனமென வருநடை ...... மடமாதர்","சந்த தம்பொலி வழகுள வடிவினர்","வஞ்ச கம்பொதி மனதின ரணுகினர்","தங்கள் நெஞ்சக மகிழ்வுற நிதிதர ...... அவர்மீதே","சிந்தை வஞ்சக நயமொடு பொருள்கவர்","தந்த்ர மந்த்ரிகள் தரணியி லணைபவர்","செம்பொ னிங்கினி யிலையெனில் மிகுதியு ...... முனிவாகித்","திங்க ளொன்றினில் நெனல்பொரு ளுதவில","னென்று சண்டைகள் புரிதரு மயலியர்","சிங்கி யுங்கொடு மிடிமையு மகலநி ...... னருள்கூர்வாய்","மந்த ரங்குடை யெனநிரை யுறுதுயர்","சிந்த அன்றடர் மழைதனி லுதவிய","மஞ்செ னும்படி வடிவுறு மரிபுகழ் ...... மருகோனே","மங்கை யம்பிகை மகிழ்சர வணபவ","துங்க வெங்கய முகன்மகிழ் துணைவநல்","வஞ்சி தண்குற மகள்பத மலர்பணி ...... மணவாளா","தந்த னந்தன தனதன தனவென","வண்டு விண்டிசை முரல்தரு மணமலர்","தங்கு சண்பக முகிலள வுயர்தரு ...... பொழில்மீதே","சங்கு நன்குமிழ் தரளமு மெழில்பெறு","துங்க வொண்பணி மணிகளும் வெயில்விடு","சண்பை யம்பதி மருவிய அமரர்கள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes sandal-anointed, bangled women of swan gait and deceitful minds who come close to those who give wealth, steal it with charm and spells, flare up if no gold is given now and quarrel that a month has passed without a gift, and asks for grace so that this poison and cruel poverty leave him. The praise names Murugan the nephew of the cloud-hued one who held the mountain as an umbrella for the herds in the rain, the Saravanabhava of Ambika, brother of the elephant-faced god and husband of Valli, at Sanbai where bees hum the rhythm in champaka groves.","n":"நிதிதர, பொருள்கவர், செம்பொன் இங்கு இனி இலையெனில் and the month without a gift (திங்கள் ஒன்றினில் ... பொருள் உதவிலன்) make the money plain. தந்த்ர மந்த்ரிகள் accuses the women of spells. The poverty asked to be removed (கொடு மிடிமை) is the result of the courtesans. மந்தரம் குடையென is the story of Krishna lifting the hill, with the hill named Mandara.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","inmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:128"},"p:771":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":771,"t":["சருவி யிகழ்ந்து மருண்டு வெகுண்டுறு","சமயமு மொன்றிலை யென்ற வரும்பறி","தலையரு நின்று கலங்க விரும்பிய ...... தமிழ்கூறுஞ்","சலிகையு நன்றியும் வென்றியு மங்கள","பெருமைக ளுங்கன முங்குண மும்பயில்","சரவண மும்பொறை யும்புக ழுந்திகழ் ...... தனிவேலும்","விருது துலங்க சிகண்டியி லண்டரு","முருகி வணங்க வரும்பத மும்பல","விதரண முந்திற முந்தர முந்தினை ...... புனமானின்","ம்ருகமத குங்கும கொங்கையில் நொந்தடி","வருடிம ணந்துபு ணர்ந்தது வும்பல","விஜயமு மன்பின்மொ ழிந்துமொ ழிந்தியல் ...... மறவேனே","கருதியி லங்கை யழிந்துவி டும்படி","அவுணர டங்கம டிந்துவி ழும்படி","கதிரவ னிந்து விளங்கி வரும்படி ...... விடுமாயன்","கடகரி யஞ்சி நடுங்கி வருந்திடு","மடுவினில் வந்துத வும்புய லிந்திரை","கணவன ரங்க முகுந்தன் வருஞ்சக ...... டறமோதி","மருது குலுங்கி நலங்க முனிந்திடு","வரதன லங்கல் புனைந்தரு ளுங்குறள்","வடிவனெ டுங்கடல் மங்கவொ ரம்புகை ...... தொடுமீளி","மருகபு ரந்தர னுந்தவ மொன்றிய","பிரமபு ரந்தனி லுங்குக னென்பவர்","மனதினி லும்பரி வொன்றிய மர்ந்தருள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet vows never to forget, and to speak with love, Murugan's glories: the Tamil that confounded the Jains and those who said there is no faith, his grace, victory and renown, Saravana, the vel, the peacock, the feet the gods melt to worship, his gifts, and his stroking of Valli's feet and marrying her. The praise is long on Vishnu, who burnt Lanka, saved the elephant in the pool, broke the cart and the marutha trees, became the dwarf and shot at the sea, as Murugan's uncle, and ends at Brahmapuram, where Indra did penance and Guha dwells in hearts.","n":"There is no confession: the plea is the vow மறவேனே, so the song is a list of what to remember. சமயமும் ஒன்றிலை என்றவரும் பறிதலையரும் sets those who deny any faith beside the Jains, both silenced by Tamil. The Vishnu catalogue gathers Rama and Krishna stories (சகடு, மருது, குறள்) into one uncle.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","thamizh","paththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:128"},"p:772":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":772,"t":["சிந்துற்றெழு மாமதி அங்கித் ...... திரளாலே","தென்றற்றரு வாசமி குந்துற் ...... றெழலாலே","அந்திப்பொழு தாகிய கங்குற் ...... றிரளாலே","அன்புற்றெழு பேதைம யங்கித் ...... தனியானாள்","நந்துற்றிடு வாரியை மங்கத் ...... திகழாயே","நஞ்சொத்தொளிர் வேலினை யுந்திப் ...... பொருவேளே","சந்தக்கவி நூலினர் தஞ்சொற் ...... கினியோனே","சண்பைப்பதி மேவிய கந்தப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"In a short song in another's voice, the singer says that the moon rising from the sea like fire, the fragrant south wind and the night coming at evening have made the simple girl in love faint and stand alone, and asks Murugan to shine so that the conch-filled sea grows dim. He is the one who fights with the poison-bright vel, sweet to the words of rhythmic poets, at Sanbai.","n":"The voice is a mother's or companion's (அன்புற்றெழு பேதை மயங்கித் தனியானாள்), and the classical torments of evening are the moon, the south wind and the dark. சந்தக்கவி நூலினர் may glance at the poet's own rhythmic verse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","maalai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:128"},"p:773":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":773,"t":["செக்கர்வா னப்பிறைக் கிக்குமா ரற்கலத்","தெற்கிலு¡ தைக்கனற் ...... றணியாத","சித்ரவீ ணைக்கலர்ப் பெற்றதா யர்க்கவச்","சித்தம்வா டிக்கனக் ...... கவிபாடிக்","கைக்கபோ லக்கிரிப் பொற்கொள்ரா சிக்கொடைக்","கற்பதா ருச்செகத் ...... த்ரயபாநு","கற்றபேர் வைப்பெனச் செத்தையோ கத்தினர்க்","கைக்குணான் வெட்கிநிற் ...... பதுபாராய்","சக்ரபா ணிக்குமப் பத்மயோ னிக்குநித்","தப்ரதா பர்க்குமெட் ...... டரிதாய","தத்வவே தத்தனுற் பத்திபோ தித்தஅத்","தத்வ்ரு பக்கிரிப் ...... புரைசாடிக்","கொக்கிலே புக்கொளித் திட்டசூர் பொட்டெழக்","குத்துரா வுத்தபொற் ...... குமரோனே","கொற்றவா வுற்பலச் செச்சைமா லைப்புயக்","கொச்சைவாழ் முத்தமிழ்ப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks Murugan to see how he stands ashamed, having wilted in mind and sung rich verses to rubbish men, calling them elephants, mountains of gold, wish-trees of giving, suns of the three worlds and treasuries of the learned; the opening tormentors, the crescent in the red sky, Kama of the cane bow, the southern wind, the vina and the gossip of mothers, are a lover's, and how they bear on the plea is unclear. The praise makes Murugan the one who taught the principle of the Veda beyond Vishnu and Brahma, split the hill and struck Sura hiding in the mango, the Muttamizh lord of Kochchai.","n":"செத்தை யோகத்தினர், men of rubbish worth, and the flattering titles (கற்பதாரு, செகத்த்ரய பாநு) show the patron-poet's shame again, as in 768. The teaching of the தத்வ வேதம் to those Vishnu and Brahma could not reach is the guru-myth without Om named. The first four lines may describe the poet as a lover, or a girl's torments; the split is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["iravu","kutram","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:128"},"p:774":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":774,"t":["தினமணி சார்ங்க பாணி யெனமதிள் நீண்டு சால","தினகர னேய்ந்த மாளி ...... கையிலாரஞ்","செழுமணி சேர்ந்த பீடி கையிலிசை வாய்ந்த பாடல்","வயிரியர் சேர்ந்து பாட ...... இருபாலும்","இனவளை பூண்கை யார்க வரியிட வேய்ந்து மாலை","புழுககில் சாந்து பூசி ...... யரசாகி","இனிதிரு மாந்து வாழு மிருவினை நீண்ட காய","மொருபிடி சாம்ப லாகி ...... விடலாமோ","வனசர ரேங்க வான முகடுற வோங்கி ஆசை","மயிலொடு பாங்கி மார்க ...... ளருகாக","மயிலொடு மான்கள் சூழ வளவரி வேங்கை யாகி","மலைமிசை தோன்று மாய ...... வடிவோனே","கனசமண் மூங்கர் கோடி கழுமிசை தூங்க நீறு","கருணைகொள் பாண்டி நாடு ...... பெறவேதக்","கவிதரு காந்த பால கழுமல பூந்த ராய","கவுணியர் வேந்த தேவர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet pictures a life of royal comfort: walls and mansions reaching the sun, a jewelled seat, singers on both sides, bangled women waving chowries, garlands, musk, agil and sandal paste, ruling as a king, and asks whether this body of two deeds, living in pride, should simply become a handful of ash. The praise shows Murugan taking the magic form of a vengai tree on the hill among the hunters with Valli and her friends, sending the Jains to the stakes and giving the Pandya land the ash in grace, the Kaundinya king at Kazhumalam.","n":"ஒருபிடி சாம்பல், a handful of ash, is the whole of the rhetorical question; the splendour is described only to be weighed against it. வேங்கையாகி மலைமிசை தோன்றும் மாய வடிவோனே: Murugan's disguise as a tree in the Valli story. கனசமண் ... கழுமிசை தூங்க நீறு கருணைகொள் பாண்டி நாடு is Sambandar's story again.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","yaakkai","selvam","thamizh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:128"},"p:775":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":775,"t":["பூமாதுர மேயணி மான்மறை","வாய்நாலுடை யோன்மலி வானவர்","கோமான்முநி வோர்முதல் யாருமி ...... யம்புவேதம்","பூராயம தாய்மொழி நூல்களும்","ஆராய்வதி லாதட லாசுரர்","போரால்மறை வாயுறு பீதியின் ...... வந்துகூடி","நீமாறரு ளாயென ஈசனை","பாமாலைக ளால்தொழு தேதிரு","நீறார்தரு மேனிய தேனியல் ...... கொன்றையோடு","நீரேர்தரு சானவி மாமதி","காகோதர மாதுளை கூவிளை","நேரோடம் விளாமுத லார்சடை ...... யெம்பிரானே","போமாறினி வேறெது வோதென","வேயாரரு ளாலவ ¡£தரு","போர்வேலவ நீலக லாவியி ...... வர்ந்துநீடு","பூலோகமொ டேயறு லோகமு","நேரோர் நொடி யேவரு வோய்சுர","சேனாபதி யாயவ னேயுனை ...... யன்பினோடுங்","காமாவறு சோம ஸமானன","தாமாமண மார்தரு நீபசு","தாமாவென வேதுதி யாதுழல் ...... வஞ்சனேனைக்","காவாயடி நாளசு ரேசரை","யேசாடிய கூர்வடி வேலவ","காரார்தரு காழியின் மேவிய ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The praise tells how Vishnu with Lakshmi on his breast, four-faced Brahma, Indra and the sages, not having searched the Vedas, fled in fear from the warring asuras and praised Siva with garlands of song, Siva of the ash, konrai, Ganga, moon and serpent, and how by his grace the war-vel and Murugan on the blue peacock came, crossing the earth and six worlds in a moment as general of the gods. The plea asks Murugan to protect the deceiver who wanders without praising him with love as moon-faced, Kama-lovely and kadamba-garlanded.","n":"The song is nearly all praise, built as a narrative of Murugan's birth for the war with the asuras; the confession is only வஞ்சனேனை, the poet's failure to praise. The lines naming the adornments of Siva's matted hair (மாதுளை, கூவிளை) and the line with corrupt characters (ஆலவ ... தரு) are hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["kutram","pugazh","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:128"},"p:776":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":776,"t":["மதனச்சொற் காரக் காரிகள் பவளக்கொப் பாடச் சீறிகள்","மருளப்பட் டாடைக் காரிக ...... ளழகாக","மவுனச்சுட் டாடிச் சோலிகள் இசலிப்பித் தாசைக் காரிகள்","வகைமுத்துச் சாரச் சூடிகள் ...... விலைமாதர்","குதலைச்சொற் சாரப் பேசிகள் நரகச்சிற் சாடிப் பீடிகள்","குசலைக்கொட் சூலைக் காலிகள் ...... மயல்மேலாய்க்","கொளுவிக்கட் டாசைப் பாசனை பவதுக்கக் காரச் சூதனை","குமுதப்பொற் பாதச் சேவையி ...... லருள்வாயே","கதறக்கற் சூரைக் கார்கட லெரியத்திக் கூறிற் பாழ்பட","ககனக்கட் டாரிக் காயிரை ...... யிடும்வேலா","கதிர்சுற்றிட் டாசைப் பால்கிரி யுறைபச்சைப் பாசக் கோகில","கவுரிப்பொற் சேர்வைச் சேகர ...... முருகோனே","திதலைப்பொற் பாணிக் கார்குயி லழகிற்பொற் றோகைப் பாவையை","தினமுற்றுச் சாரத் தோள்மிசை ...... யணைவோனே","திலதப்பொட் டாசைச் சேர்முக மயிலுற்றிட் டேறிக் காழியில்","சிவன்மெச்சக் காதுக் கோதிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet heaps up names for the women who sell themselves: women of love-words and coral earrings, scolders in silk, women of silent hints and quarrels, lisping talkers, pits of hell, and asks that he, bound by desire, the trickster caught in birth's sorrow, be given service at Murugan's golden lotus feet. The praise shows the vel that made the sea roar and burn and fed the sky's kites, Gauri's son, Valli embraced daily, and Murugan who rides the peacock at Kazhi and spoke into Siva's ear what Siva praised.","n":"விலைமாதர், women for a price, names the trade, and the long string of -காரிகள், -சோலிகள் forms an insult catalogue in the rhythm. சிவன் மெச்சக் காதுக்கு ஓதிய: the teaching in Siva's ear, with Om not named. சேவையில் அருள்வாயே asks for service (சேவை) at the feet, not only for refuge.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","mayal","guru","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:128"},"p:777":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":777,"t":["விடமெனமி குத்தவட வனலென வுயர்த்துரவி","விரிகதி ரெனப்பரவு ...... நிலவாலே","விதனமிக வுற்றுவரு ரதிபதி கடுத்துவிடு","விரைதரு விதட்கமல ...... கணையாலே","அடலமரி யற்றுதிசை யினில்மருவி மிக்கவனல்","அழலொடு கொதித்துவரு ...... கடைநாளில்","அணுகிநம னெற்றமயல் கொளுமநிலை சித்தமுற","அவசமொ டணைத்தருள ...... வரவேணும்","அடவிதனில் மிக்கபரு வரையவ ரளித்ததிரு","அனையமயில் முத்தமணி ...... சுரயானை","அழகிய மணிக்கலச முலைகளில் மயக்கமுறு","மதிவிரக சித்ரமணி ...... மயில்வீரா","கடதடக ளிற்றுமுக ரிளையவ கிரிக்குமரி","கருணையொ டளித்ததிற ...... முருகோனே","கமலமல ரொத்தவிழி யரிமருக பத்தர்பணி","கழுமலந கர்க்குமர ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says the moonlight burns like poison and the undersea fire and spreads like the sun, Kama's flower arrows wound, and on the last day, when fire boils in every direction and Death comes near to strike, he asks Murugan to come with grace and embrace him in that helpless, bewildered state. The praise shows the peacock-like Valli given by the hill people and Devayanai of the elephant, Murugan as younger brother of the elephant-faced god, son given by the mountain's daughter, Vishnu's nephew and the lord of Kazhumalam.","n":"The torments of a lover (the moon, Kama's arrows) run straight into the day of death (கடைநாளில் அணுகி நமன் எற்ற), so that the lover's longing and the dying man's need meet in one request: அவசமொடு அணைத்து அருள வரவேணும். It is unclear whether the first half is the poet's own or a borrowed lover's voice.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kootru","kaamanoy","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:128"},"p:778":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":778,"t":["அளிசுழ லளகக் காடு காட்டவும்","விழிகொடு கலவித் தீயை மூட்டவும்","அமளியில் முடியப் போது போக்கவும் ...... இளைஞோர்கள்","அவர்வச மொழுகிக் காசு கேட்கவும்","அழகிய மயிலிற் சாயல் காட்டவும்","அளவிய தெருவிற் போயு லாத்தவும் ...... அதிபார","இளமுலை மிசையிற் று¡சு நீக்கவும்","முகமொடு முகம்வைத் தாசை யாக்கவும்","இருநிதி யிலரைத் தூர நீக்கவும் ...... இனிதாக","எவரையு மளவிப் போய ணாப்பவும்","நினைபவ ரளவிற் காதல் நீக்கியென்","இடரது தொலையத் தாள்கள் காட்டிநின் ...... அருள்தாராய்","நெளிபடு களமுற் றாறு போற்சுழல்","குருதியில் முழுகிப் பேய்கள் கூப்பிட","நிணமது பருகிப் பாறு காக்கைகள் ...... கழுகாட","நிரைநிரை யணியிட் டோரி யார்த்திட","அதிர்தரு சமரிற் சேனை கூட்டிய","நிசிசரர் மடியச் சாடு வேற்கொடு ...... பொரும்வீரா","களிமயில் தனில்புக் கேறு தாட்டிக","அழகிய கனகத் தாம மார்த்தொளிர்","கனகிரி புயமுத் தார மேற்றருள் ...... திருமார்பா","கரியவ னகரிற் றேவ பார்ப்பதி","யருள்சுத குறநற் பாவை தாட்பணி","கருணைய தமிழிற் பாடல் கேட்டருள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet lists what the women do: show their bee-circled hair, kindle the fire of union with their eyes, spend the time in bed, give themselves to young men and ask for coins, show a peacock's grace, parade the streets, brush dust from their breasts, put face to face to rouse desire, send far away those without wealth and measure up anyone to embrace, and asks that love for them be removed, his trouble ended, and grace given by showing the feet. The praise shows the battlefield of blood, ghosts and vultures and the vel that killed the night-roamers, Murugan on the peacock with a golden garland, son of Parvati, worshipped by Valli, who in grace hears songs in Tamil at Kariyavanagar.","n":"காசு கேட்கவும் and இருநிதி இலரைத் தூர நீக்கவும் (sending away those without wealth) make these courtesans. The confession is built as a string of infinitives (-வும்), each a habit of the women. கருணைய தமிழிற் பாடல் கேட்டருள் may point to the poet's own songs.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["varaivinmagalir","thiruvadi","thamizh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:129"},"p:779":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":779,"t":["உ ரத்துறை போதத் ...... தனியான","உ னைச்சிறி தோதத் ...... தெரியாது","மரத்துறை போலுற் ...... றடியேனும்","மலத்திருள் மூடிக் ...... கெடலாமோ","பரத்துறை சீலத் ...... தவர்வாழ்வே","பணித்தடி வாழ்வுற் ...... றருள்வோனே","வரத்துறை நீதர்க் ...... கொருசேயே","வயித்திய நாதப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says he cannot speak even a little of Murugan, the unique wisdom of the heart, and asks whether, like a block of wood, he should perish covered in the darkness of mala. The praise names Murugan the life of those of high virtue, who grants life at his feet, son of the just giver of boons, Vaidyanatha the lord.","n":"மரத்துறை போல், like a thing of wood, is the image of the unaware soul, and மலத்திருள் the darkness of impurity of Siddhanta. வயித்திய நாதப் பெருமாளே: the shrine's name for Siva, the physician, is given to Murugan.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["malam","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:130"},"p:780":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":780,"t":["எத்தனை கோடி கோடி விட்டுட லோடி யாடி","யெத்தனை கோடி போன ...... தளவேதோ","இப்படி மோக போக மிப்படி யாகி யாகி","யிப்படி யாவ தேது ...... இனிமேலோ","சித்திடில் சீசி சீசி குத்திர மாய மாயை","சிக்கினி லாயு மாயு ...... மடியேனைச்","சித்தினி லாட லோடு முத்தமிழ் வாண ரோது","சித்திர ஞான பாத ...... மருள்வாயே","நித்தமு மோது வார்கள் சித்தமெ வீட தாக","நிர்த்தம தாடு மாறு ...... முகவோனே","நிட்கள ரூபர் பாதி பச்சுரு வான மூணு","நெட்டிலை சூல பாணி ...... யருள்பாலா","பைத்தலை நீடு மாயி ரத்தலை மீது பீறு","பத்திர பாத நீல ...... மயில்வீரா","பச்சிள பூக பாளை செய்க்கயல் தாவு §வ்ளுர்","பற்றிய மூவர் தேவர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks how many crores of bodies he has left after running and dancing in them, how many have gone, how often he has fallen into such pleasure and what will come hereafter; caught in the tangle of mean maya, which on thought is only 'chi, chi', he asks for the wondrous feet of wisdom sung by the masters of the three Tamils. The praise shows Murugan dancing in the hearts of those who chant him daily as his house, son of the formless Siva who is half green and bears the trident, on the peacock that tears the thousand-headed serpent, at Velur of the areca and the leaping fish.","n":"எத்தனை கோடி கோடி விட்டுடல்: the count of bodies left behind opens the song, as in 493. சீசீ, the cry of disgust, is set inside the plea. நிட்கள ரூபர் பாதி பச்சுரு: Siva the partless with the green half that is Uma.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["piravi","maayai","gnaanam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:130"},"p:781":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":781,"t":["பாட கச்சிலம் போடு செச்சைமணி","கோவெ னக்கலந் தாடு பொற்சரணர்","பாவை சித்திரம் போல்வர் பட்டுடையி ...... னிடைநூலார்","பார பொற்றனங் கோபு ரச்சிகர","மாமெ னப்படர்ந் தேம லிப்பரித","மாகு நற்கரும் போடு சர்க்கரையின் ...... மொழிமாதர்","ஏட கக்குலஞ் சேரு மைக்குழலொ","டாட ளிக்குலம் பாட நற்றெருவி","லேகி புட்குலம் போல பற்பலசொ ...... லிசைபாடி","ஏறி யிச்சகம் பேசி யெத்தியிதம்","வாரு முற்பணந் தாரு மிட்டமென","ஏணி வைத்துவந் தேற விட்டிடுவர் ...... செயலாமோ","சேட னுக்கசண் டாள ரக்கர்குல","மாள அட்டகுன் றேழ லைக்கடல்கள்","சேர வற்றநின் றாட யிற்கரமி ...... ரறுதோள்மேல்","சேணி லத்தர்பொன் பூவை விட்டிருடி","யோர்கள் கட்டியம் பாட எட்டரசர்","சேசெ யொத்தசெந் தாம ரைக்கிழவி ...... புகழ்வேலா","நாட கப்புனங் காவ லுற்றசுக","மோக னத்திமென் தோளி சித்ரவளி","நாய கிக்கிதம் பாடி நித்தமணி ...... புனைவோனே","ஞான வெற்புகந் தாடு மத்தர்தையல்","நாய கிக்குநன் பாக ரக்கணியும்","நாதர் மெச்சவந் தாடு முத்தமருள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women with anklets and silk, like painted dolls, breasts like tower peaks and speech like cane and sugar, who walk the streets with bees around their hair singing like flocks of birds, flatter and trick, saying 'bring money first, then what you like', and set up a ladder for a man to climb, and asks whether this is to be his lot. The praise shows the demons destroyed, hills and seas dried by the vel, gods raining flowers, sages as heralds and Lakshmi praising, Murugan singing to Valli in the millet field, and the child who comes dancing to give kisses at the praise of Siva of the hill of wisdom and his consort.","n":"வாரும் முற்பணம் தாரும் இட்டமென, 'come, give the money first, then as you please', is the courtesans' own speech. ஏணி வைத்து வந்து ஏற விட்டிடுவர் may mean they lift a man up only to drop him; the image is not clear. நாதர் மெச்ச வந்து ஆடு முத்தம் அருள்: Murugan as the child giving kisses to his parents.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:130"},"p:782":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":782,"t":["மாலி னாலெ டுத்த கந்தல் சோறி னால்வ ளர்த்த பொந்தி","மாறி யாடெ டுத்தசி ந்தை ...... யநியாய","மாயை யாலெ டுத்து மங்கி னேனை யாஎ னக்கி ரங்கி","வாரை யாயி னிப்பி றந்து ...... இறவாமல்","வேலி னால்வி னைக்க ணங்கள் தூள தாஎ ரித்து உ ன்றன்","வீடு தாப ரித்த அன்பர் ...... கண்முடே","மேவி யானு னைப்பொல் சிந்தை யாக வேக ளித்து கந்த","வேளெ யாமெ னப்ப ரிந்து ...... அருள்வாயே","காலி னாலெ னப்ப ரந்த சூரர் மாள வெற்றி கொண்ட","கால பாநு சத்தி யங்கை ...... முருகோனே","காம பாண மட்ட நந்த கோடி மாத ரைப்பு ணர்ந்த","காளை யேறு கர்த்த னெந்தை ...... யருள்பாலா","சேலை நேர்வி ழிக்கு றம்பெ ணாசை தோளு றப்பு ணர்ந்து","சீரை யோது பத்த ரன்பி ...... லுறைவோனே","தேவர் மாதர் சித்தர் தொண்டர் ஏக வேளு ருக்கு கந்த","சேவல் கேது சுற்று கந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet calls himself a belly raised on rags and rice, a mind that takes changing forms, wasted by unjust maya, and asks Murugan to pity him so that he is not born and does not die again: to burn the hosts of deeds to dust with the vel, set him among the devotees who keep his house of release, make his mind like Murugan's own, and let him say 'Kanda Vel is mine'. The praise names the vel that killed the Suras spread like wind, Siva of the bull as father, Valli embraced, and Murugan who dwells in the love of devotees, worshipped by gods, siddhas and servants at Velur with the cock banner.","n":"The plea climbs from release from birth to burning deeds (வினைக்கணங்கள் தூளதா எரித்து), to the company of devotees (அன்பர்கண் மேவி), to likeness of mind (உனைப்போல் சிந்தையாக). The line on Siva who burnt Kama's arrows and 'embraced endless crores of women' (காம பாணம் அட்ட நந்த கோடி மாதரைப் புணர்ந்த) is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","vinai","adiyaar","maayai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:130"},"p:783":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":783,"t":["முலா தாரமொ டேற்றி யங்கியை","ஆறா தாரமொ டோட்டி யந்திர","மூலா வாயுவை யேற்று நன்சுழி ...... முனையூடே","மூதா தாரம ரூப்பி லந்தர","நாதா கீதம தார்த்தி டும்பர","மூடே பாலொளி ஆத்து மந்தனை ...... விலகாமல்","மாலா டூனொடு சேர்த்தி தம்பெற","நானா வேதம சாத்தி ரஞ்சொலும்","வாழ்ஞா னாபுரி யேற்றி மந்திர ...... தவிசூடே","மாதா நாதனும் வீற்றி ருந்திடும்","வீடே மூணொளி காட்டி சந்திர","வாகார் தேனமு தூட்டி யென்றனை ...... யுடனாள்வாய்","சூலாள் மாதுமை தூர்த்த சம்பவி","மாதா ராபகல் காத்த மைந்தனை","சூடோ டீர்வினை வாட்டி மைந்தரெ ...... னெமையாளுந்","தூயாள் மூவரை நாட்டு மெந்தையர்","§வ்ளுர் வாழ்வினை தீர்த்த சங்கரர்","தோய்சா ரூபரொ டேற்றி ருந்தவ ...... ளருள்பாலா","வேலா ஏழ்கடல் வீட்டி வஞ்சக","மூடார் சூரரை வாட்டி யந்தகன்","வீடு டேவிய காத்தி ரம்பரி ...... மயில்வாழ்வே","வேதா நால்தலை சீக்கொ ளும்படி","கோலா காலம தாட்டு மந்திர","வேலா மால்மக ளார்க்கி ரங்கிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes the yogic ascent: raising the fire from the root centre through the six supports, driving the breath up the middle channel, hearing the music of nada, the milk-white light and the self held fast, then mounting the seat of mantra in the city of wisdom where mother and father sit, and asks Murugan to show him the three lights in that house, feed him the honeyed nectar of the moon and rule him. The praise names Uma who bears the trident and guards day and night, Siva who cured disease and deed at Velur, and Murugan's vel that dried the seven seas, the peacock and his pity for Vishnu's daughter.","n":"The plea is technical yoga: மூலாதாரம், ஆறாதாரம், சுழிமுனை, நாதம், and the moon's nectar (சந்திர ... தேனமுது). வீடே மூணொளி காட்டி: in the house of release, show the three lights (perhaps sun, moon and fire). சூடோடு ஈர்வினை வாட்டி ... வாழ்வினை தீர்த்த சங்கரர்: the healing at Vaitheeswaran is named in passing. Several syllable runs in the first half are hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["kundalini","aadhaaram","amudham","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:130"},"p:784":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":784,"t":["மேக வார்குழல தாடதன பாரமிசை","யார மாடகுழை யாடவிழி யாடபொறி","மேனி வாசனைகள் வீச அல்குல் மோதிபரி ...... மளமேற","மீனு லாடையிடை யாடமயில் போலநடை","யோல மோலமென பாதமணி நூபுரமு","மேல்வில் வீசபணி கீரகுயில் போலகுரல் ...... முழவோசை","ஆக வேயவைகள் கூடிடுவர் வீதிவரு","வோரை வாருமென வேசரச மோடுருகி","ஆசை போலமனை யேகொடணை வார்கள்குவ ...... டதிபார","ஆணி மாமுலையின் மூழ்கிசுக வாரிகொடு","வேர்வை பாயஅணை யூடமளி யாடியிட","ரான சூலைபல நோய்கள்கட லாடியுட ...... லுழல்வேனோ","நாக லோகர்மதி லோகர்பக லோகர்விதி","நாடு ளோர்களம ரோர்கள்கண நாதர்விடை","நாதர் வேதியர்கள் ஆதிசர சோதிதிகழ் ...... முநிவோர்கள்","நாத ரேநரர்ம னாரணர்பு ராணவகை","வேத கீதவொலி பூரையிது பூரையென","நாச மாயசுரர் மேவுகிரி தூளிபட ...... விடும்வேலா","தோகை மாதுகுற மாதமுத மாதுவினல்","தோழி மாதுவளி நாயகிமி னாளைசுக","சோக மோடிறுகி மார்முலைவி டாமலணை ...... புணர்வோனே","தோளி ராறுமுக மாறுமயில் வேலழகு","மீதெய் வானவடி வாதொழுதெ ணாவயனர்","சூழு காவிரியும் §வ்ளுர்முரு காவமரர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women with swaying hair, breasts, earrings and eyes, scent rising from them, gait like a peacock, anklets ringing and voices like cuckoos, who gather in the streets, call passers-by 'come', melt with play, take them home and embrace them, and asks whether, having sunk in their breasts and sweated on the bed, he must wander in a sea of colic and many diseases. The praise gathers the serpent world, moon and sun worlds, Brahma's world, the gods, ganas, Siva, Brahmins and sages, Narayana, Puranas and Vedic song around the vel thrown at the hill, Valli embraced, and Murugan at Velur on the Kaveri.","n":"வீதிவருவோரை வாருமெனவே, calling passers-by in the street, and மனையே கொடு அணைவார்கள், taking them home, mark the courtesans. The end of the confession is disease (சூலை பல நோய்கள் கடலாடி), the cost of the bed. The song ends with Velur, but the Kaveri lies some way off.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["varaivinmagalir","punarchi","noy"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:130"},"p:785":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":785,"t":["ஏட்டின் விதிப்படி யேகொடு மாபுர","வீட்டி லடைத்திசை வேகசை மூணதி","லேற்றி யடித்திட வேகட லோடம ...... தெனவேகி","ஏற்கு மெனப்பொரு ளாசைபெ ணாசைகொ","ளாத்து வெனத்திரி யாபரி யாதவ","மேற்றி யிருப்பிட மேயறி யாமலு ...... முடல்பேணிப்","பூட்டு சரப்பளி யேமத னாமென","ஆட்டி யசைத்திய லேதிரி நாளையில்","பூத்த மலக்குகை யோபொதி சோறென ...... கழுகாகம்","போற்றி நமக்கிரை யாமென வேகொள","நாட்டி லொடுக்கென வேவிழு போதினில்","பூட்டு பணிப்பத மாமயி லாவருள் ...... புரிவாயே","வீட்டி லடைத்தெரி யேயிடு பாதக","னாட்டை விடுத்திட வேபல சூதினில","வீழ்த்த விதிப்படி யேகுரு காவலர் ...... வனமேபோய்","வேற்றுமை யுற்றுரு வோடியல் நாளது","பார்த்து முடித்திட வேயொரு பாரத","மேற்புனை வித்தம காவிர மாயவன் ...... மருகோனே","கோட்டை யழித்தசு ரார்பதி கோவென","மூட்டி யெரித்தப ராபர சேகர","கோத்த மணிக்கதி ரேநிக ராகிய ...... வடிவேலா","கூற்று மரித்திட வேயுதை பார்வதி","யார்க்கு மினித்தபெ ணாகிய மான்மகள்","கோட்டு முலைக்கதி பாகட வூருறை ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says that, as written in fate's leaf, the soul is shut in the cruel house of the body, whipped by three lashes and driven like a boat on the sea, gripped by desire for wealth and for women, not knowing where it truly dwells, tending the body and adorning it as if it were Kama; on the day this cave of filth falls to the ground as a packed meal that vultures and crows hail as their food, he asks Murugan to come on the peacock with his anklet-bound feet. The praise retells the Mahabharata, the house set on fire, the dice, the forest and the war, with Krishna as uncle, the vel bright as the sun, and Uma whom Siva joined when he kicked Death, at Kadavur.","n":"ஏட்டின் விதிப்படி, by the writing on the leaf, puts fate at the start; கசை மூணு, three whips, may be the three humours or the three desires. பொதி சோறு, a packed meal for the road, makes the corpse provisions for the birds. கூற்று மரித்திடவே உதை is the Kadavur story of Siva kicking Death.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oozh","yaakkai","avaa","kootru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:131"},"p:786":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":786,"t":["சூலமென வோடு சர்ப்ப வாயுவைவி டாத டக்கி","தூயவொளி காண முத்தி ...... விதமாகச்","சூழுமிருள் பாவ கத்தை வீழ அழ லு¡டெ ரித்து","சோதிமணி பீட மிட்ட ...... மடமேவி","மேலைவெளி யாயி ரத்து நாலிருப ராப ரத்தின்","மேவியரு ணாச லத்தி ...... னுடன்மூழ்கி","வேலுமயில் வாக னப்ர காசமதி லேத ரித்து","வீடுமது வேசி றக்க ...... அருள்தாராய்","ஓலசுர ராழி யெட்டு வாளகிரி மாய வெற்பு","மூடுருவ வேல்தொ டுத்த ...... மயில்வீரா","ஓதுகுற மான்வ னத்தில் மேவியவள் கால்பி டித்து","ளோமெனுப தேச வித்தொ ...... டணைவோனே","காலனொடு மேதி மட்க வூழிபுவி மேல்கி டத்து","காலனிட மேவு சத்தி ...... யருள்பாலா","காலமுதல் வாழ்பு விக்க தாரநகர் கோபு ரத்துள்","கானமயில் மேல்த ரித்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks to hold the serpent-like breath without letting it go, see the pure light, burn the surrounding darkness of sin in fire, enter the matam where a jewelled seat of light is set, plunge into the space above, the thousand, and the light of Arunachala, and there see the vel and the peacock shining, and be given release. The praise shows the vel that pierced the eight hills and the magic mountain, Murugan who held Valli's feet in the forest and embraced her with the teaching of Om, son of the Shakti on the left of Siva who crushed Death and his buffalo, at the tower of Kadavur.","n":"சர்ப்ப வாயுவை விடாது அடக்கி: the breath held like a coiled serpent, then the light, the fire and the space above (மேலைவெளி, ஆயிரம்), a compressed yoga ladder. ஓமெனும் உபதேச வித்தொடு அணைவோனே gives the teaching of Om to Valli, not to Siva. காலனிடம் மேவு சத்தி: Uma on the left side, the side with which Siva kicked Death at Kadavur.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["yogam","praanan","oli","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:131"},"p:787":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":787,"t":["அருக்கி மெத்தெனச் சிரித்து மைக்கணிட்","டழைத்தி தப்தடச் ...... சிலகூறி","அரைப்ப ணத்தைவிற் றுடுத்த பட்டவிழ்த்","தணைத்தி தழ்க்கொடுத் ...... தநுராகத்","துருக்கி மட்டறப் பொருட்ப றிப்பவர்க்","குளக்க ருத்தினற் ...... ப்ரமைகூரா","துரைத்து செய்ப்பதித் தலத்தி னைத்துதித்","துனைத்தி ருப்புகழ்ப் ...... பகர்வேனோ","தருக்கு மற்கடப் படைப்ப லத்தினிற்","றடப்பொ ருப்பெடுத் ...... தணையாகச்","சமுத்திர த்தினைக் குறுக்க டைத்ததிற்","றரித்த ரக்கர்பொட் ...... டெழவேபோர்","செருக்கு விக்ரமச் சரத்தை விட்டுறச்","செயித்த வுத்தமத் ...... திருமாமன்","திருத்த கப்பன்மெச் சொருத்த முத்தமிழ்த்","திருப்ப டிக்கரைப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women who come close, smile softly, line their eyes, call, say sweet things, loosen the silk bought by selling a half-panam, embrace, give their lips and melt a man with love until they have taken his wealth without limit, and asks whether, without delusion for them in his heart, he will praise the shrine of Seyppathi and sing Murugan's Thiruppugazh. The praise tells of Rama with the army of monkeys, the dammed sea and the arrow that shattered the demons, as uncle, and names Murugan the one in the three Tamils whom his father praises, at Thiruppadikkarai.","n":"மட்டறப் பொருட் பறிப்பவர், those who pluck wealth without measure, names the courtesans. உனைத் திருப்புகழ்ப் பகர்வேனோ: the poet names his own work as the alternative to the women, as in 263. செய்ப்பதித் தலம் is probably the shrine itself, or Vayalur, whose name is the same; the reading leaves it open.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","pugazh","mayal"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:132"},"p:788":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":788,"t":["அமுதினை மெத்தச் சொரிந்து மாவின","தினியப ழத்தைப் பிழிந்து பானற","வதனொடு தித்தித் தகண்ட ளாவிய ...... விதழாராய்","அழகிய பொற்றட்டி னொண்டு வேடையின்","வருபசி யர்க்குற் றவன்பி னாலுண","வருள்பவ ரொத்துத் தளர்ந்த காமுகர் ...... மயல்தீரக்","குமுதம் விளர்க்கத் தடங்கு லாவிய","நிலவெழு முத்தைப் புனைந்த பாரிய","குலவிய சித்ரப் ப்ரசண்ட பூரண ...... தனபாரக்","குவடிள கக்கட் டியுந்தி மேல்விழு","மவர்மய லிற்புக் கழிந்த பாவியை","குரைகழல் பற்றிப் புகழ்ந்து வாழ்வுற ...... அருள்வாயே","வமிசமி குத்துப் ப்ரபஞ்சம் யாவையு","மறுகிட வுக்ரக் கொடும்பை யானபுன்","மதிகொ டழித்திட் டிடும்பை ராவணன் ...... மதியாமே","மறுவறு கற்பிற் சிறந்த சீதையை","விதனம் விளைக்கக் குரங்கி னாலவன்","வமிச மறுத்திட் டிலங்கு மாயவன் ...... மருகோனே","எமதும லத்தைக் களைந்து பாடென","அருள அதற்குப் புகழ்ந்து பாடிய","இயல்கவி மெச்சிட் டுயர்ந்த பேறருள் ...... முருகோனே","எழில்வளை மிக்கத் தவழ்ந்து லாவிய","பொனிநதி தெற்கிற் றிகழ்ந்து மேவிய","இணையிலி ரத்னச் சிகண்டி யூருறை ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women whose lips are nectar, pressed mango, milk and sugar, who serve them like hosts lovingly feeding the hungry in summer heat, with pearl-hung breasts that sway as they fall on a man, and calls himself the sinner ruined in their spell; he asks to hold the sounding feet, praise them and live. The praise tells of Ravana, who took Sita of spotless chastity, and of Rama who destroyed his line with the monkeys as uncle, of Murugan who removed 'our mala' and bade 'sing', then praised the verse sung and gave the high reward, at Sikandiyur south of the Ponni.","n":"எமது மலத்தைக் களைந்து பாடென அருள அதற்குப் புகழ்ந்து பாடிய இயல்கவி மெச்சிட்டு: an autobiographical line, the poet's call to sing told as Murugan's grace, the removal of mala coming before the command. The women's lips given like food to the hungry (வருபசியர்க்கு ... அருள்பவர் ஒத்து) is an ironic image of hospitality.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mayal","malam","pugazh","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:133"},"p:789":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":789,"t":["ஆடல் மாமத ராஜன் பூசல் வாளியி லேநொந்","தாகம் வேர்வுற மால்கொண் ...... டயராதே","ஆர வாணகை யார்செஞ் சேலி னேவலி லேசென்","றாயு வேதனை யேயென் ...... றுலையாதே","சேடன் மாமுடி மேவும் பாரு ளோர்களுள் நீடுந்","த்யாக மீபவர் யாரென் ...... றலையாதே","தேடி நான்மறை நாடுங் காடு மோடிய தாளுந்","தேவ நாயக நானின் ...... றடைவேனோ","பாடு நான்மறை யோனுந் தாதை யாகிய மாலும்","பாவை பாகனு நாளும் ...... தவறாதே","பாக நாண்மலர் சூடுஞ் சேக ராமதில் சூழ்தென்","பாகை மாநக ராளுங் ...... குமரேசா","கூட லான்முது கூனன் றோட வாதுயர் வேதங்","கூறு நாவல மேவுந் ...... தமிழ்வீரா","கோடி தானவர் தோளுந் தாளும் வீழவு லாவுங்","கோல மாமயி லேறும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks not to faint, sweating and infatuated, under the arrows of Kama, not to go at the bidding of women's fish-like eyes and wither, and not to wander asking who on this serpent-borne earth gives generously, and asks whether he will reach today the feet that ran into the forest the four Vedas seek. The praise names Brahma, Vishnu and Siva who daily wear flowers, Murugan the ruler of walled southern Pagai, the Tamil hero whose tongue spoke the Veda in debate so that the old hunch of the king of Kudal ran away, riding the peacock.","n":"த்யாகம் ஈபவர் யாரென்று அலையாதே: the poet's wandering in search of patrons is set beside desire. நான்மறை நாடும் காடும் ஓடிய தாள் are Murugan's feet that ran in the forest after Valli, which the Vedas search for. கூடலான் முதுகூன் அன்று ஓட is the Pandya Koon (the hunchback) straightened by Sambandar, again Murugan.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","iravu","thiruvadi","thamizh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:134"},"p:790":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":790,"t":["ஈளை சுரங்குளிர் வாத மெனும்பல","நோய்கள் வளைந்தற ...... இளையாதே","ஈடு படுஞ்சிறு கூடு புகுந்திடு","காடு பயின்றுயி ...... ரிழவாதே","மூளை யெலும்புகள் நாடி நரம்புகள்","வேறு படுந்தழல் ...... முழுகாதே","மூல மெனுஞ்சிவ யோக பதந்தனில்","வாழ்வு பெறும்படி ...... மொழிவாயே","வாளை நெருங்கிய வாவியி லுங்கயல்","சேல்கள் மறிந்திட ...... வலைபீறா","வாகை துதைந்தணி கேதகை மங்கிட","மோதி வெகுண்டிள ...... மதிதோயும்","பாளை நறுங்கமழ் பூக வனந்தலை","சாடி நெடுங்கடல் ...... கழிபாயும்","பாகை வளம்பதி மேவி வளஞ்செறி","தோகை விரும்பிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks not to waste away with phlegm, fever, chills and wind, not to lose his life in the little nest of the body and go to the burning ground, and not to sink in the fire with brain, bones, nerves and sinews falling apart, and asks Murugan to speak to him so that he may live in the state of Sivayoga called the root. The praise describes Pagai, where fish leap and tear nets in the ponds, ketaki flowers are crushed, areca groves are beaten by the sea and backwaters, and Murugan who loves the peacock.","n":"மூலம் எனும் சிவயோக பதம்: the alternative to the burning body is a state, Sivayoga, not escape. The diseases (ஈளை, சுரம், குளிர், வாதம்) are named first, then the cremation fire that takes the parts of the body. The praise is all landscape.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["noy","yaakkai","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:134"},"p:791":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":791,"t":["குவளை பொருதிரு குழையை முடுகிய","கோல வேல்விழி ...... மடவார்தங்","கொடிய ம்ருகமத புளக தனகிரி","கூடி நாடொறு ...... மயலாகித்","துவள வுருகிய சரச விதமது","சோர வாரிதி ...... யலையூடே","சுழலு மெனதுயிர் மவுன பரமசு","கோம கோததி ...... படியாதோ","கவள கரதல கரட விகடக","போல பூதர ...... முகமான","கடவுள் கணபதி பிறகு வருமொரு","கார ணாகதிர் ...... வடிவேலா","பவள மரகத கநக வயிரக","பாட கோபுர ...... அரிதேரின்","பரியு மிடறிய புரிசை தழுவிய","பாகை மேவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses that, drawn to women whose spear-like eyes rush at their earrings and to their musk-scented breasts, he grew infatuated day by day and melted in play, and asks whether his life, whirling amid the waves of the sea, will not sink into the ocean of silent supreme bliss. The praise names Murugan the one who came after the elephant-faced Ganapati with cheeks and trunk, bearer of the bright vel, at Pagai whose towers of coral, emerald, gold and diamond are brushed by the horses of the sun's chariot.","n":"மவுன பரம சுக ... உததி: the ocean of silent supreme joy is set against the sea of waves in which the life whirls, two seas in one plea. The word between (சுகோம கோததி) is hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","mounam","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:134"},"p:792":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":792,"t":["அனலப் பரிபுக் ககுணத் ரயம்வைத்","தடர்பொய்க் குருதிக் ...... குடில்பேணா","அவலக் கவலைச் சவலைக் கலைகற்","றதனிற் பொருள்சற் ...... றறியாதே","குணகித் தனகிக் கனலொத் துருகிக்","குலவிக் கலவிக் ...... கொடியார்தங்","கொடுமைக் கடுமைக் குவளைக் கடையிற்","குலைபட் டலையக் ...... கடவேனோ","தினைவித் தினநற் புனமுற் றகுறத்","திருவைப் புணர்பொற் ...... புயவீரா","தெளியத் தெளியப் பவளச் சடிலச்","சிவனுக் கொருசொற் ...... பகர்வோனே","கனகச் சிகரக் குலவெற் புருவக்","கறுவிப் பொருகைக் ...... கதிர்வேலா","கழியைக் கிழியக் கயல்தத் துமிடைக்","கழியிற் குமரப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether, not cherishing the hut of blood with fire and three gunas, and not learning sorrowful, anxious, useless arts without knowing their meaning, he must still be crushed in the corners of the eyes of cruel women who cajole and melt and join in union. The praise names Valli of the millet field, Murugan who told Siva with coral matted hair the one word to make him clear, the bright vel that split the golden-peaked hill, at Idaikkazhi where fish leap in the backwater.","n":"சவலைக் கலை கற்று அதனிற் பொருள் சற்று அறியாதே: learning without knowing its meaning is joined to the body and to women as one wasted life. சிவனுக்கு ஒருசொல் பகர்வோனே is the guru-myth with Om not named. கடவேனோ, 'must I?', frames the whole first half as a question.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kalvi","yaakkai","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:135"},"p:793":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":793,"t":["இரக்குமவர்க் கிரக்கமிகுத் தளிப்பனசொப் பனத்திலுமற்","றெனக்கியலுக் கிசைக்கெதிரெப் ...... புலவோரென்","றெடுத்துமுடித் தடக்கைமுடித் திரட்டையுடுத் திலச்சினையிட்","டடைப்பையிடப் ப்ரபுத்துவமுற் ...... றியல்மாதர்","குரக்குமுகத் தினைக்குழலைப் பனிப்பிறையொப் பெனப்புயலொப்","பெனக்குறுகிக் கலைக்குள்மறைத் ...... திடுமானின்","குளப்படியிற் சளப்படுமிப் பவக்கடலைக் கடக்கஇனிக்","குறித்திருபொற் கழற்புணையைத் ...... தருவாயே","அரக்கரடற் கடக்கஅமர்க் களத்தடையப் புடைத்துலகுக்","கலக்கணறக் குலக்கிரிபொட் ...... டெழவாரி","அனைத்தும்வறப் புறச்சுரர்கற் பகப்புரியிற் புகக்கமலத்","தனைச்சிறையிட் டிடைக்கழியிற் ...... பயில்வோனே","கரக்கரடக் களிற்றுமருப் புலக்கையினிற் கொழித்தமணிக்","கழைத்தரளத் தினைத்தினையிற் ...... குறுவாளைக்","கணிக்குறவக் குறிச்சியினிற் சிலைக்குறவர்க் கிலச்சைவரக்","கயத்தொடுகைப் பிடித்தமணப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet tells of the vanity of those who say 'I give generously to those who beg, even in dreams; which poet matches me in verse and music?', tie on turbans and rings and keep betel bags in lordly style, and of women who hide in their clothes faces, hair and brows likened to moon and cloud; to cross the ocean of birth that is mired in this hoof-print puddle, he asks for the raft of the golden feet. The praise shows the battle, the hills and seas dried, the gods returned to their city and Brahma imprisoned, Murugan at Idaikkazhi, and Valli in the millet field of pearls from tusks and bamboo, whom he took by the hand to the hunters' shame.","n":"குளப்படியிற் சளப்படும் இப்பவக் கடல்: the ocean of birth is mocked as a hoof-print puddle, and yet the feet are needed as a raft (கழற் புணை) to cross it. The boasting in the first lines may be the poet's own or that of patrons; the reading leaves it open.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","thiruvadi","agandhai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:135"},"p:794":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":794,"t":["பகரு முத்தமிழ்ப் பொருளு மெய்த்தவப்","பயனு மெப்படிப் ...... பலவாழ்வும்","பழைய முத்தியிற் பதமு நட்புறப்","பரவு கற்பகத் ...... தருவாழ்வும்","புகரில் புத்தியுற் றரசு பெற்றுறப்","பொலியும் அற்புதப் ......பெருவாழ்வும்","புலன கற்றிடப் பலவி தத்தினைப்","புகழ்ப லத்தினைத் ...... தரவேணும்","தகரி லற்றகைத் தலம்வி டப்பிணைச்","சரவ ணத்தினிற் ...... பயில்வோனே","தனிவ னத்தினிற் புனம றத்தியைத்","தழுவு பொற்புயத் ...... திருமார்பா","சிகர வெற்பினைப் பகிரும் வித்தகத்","திறல யிற்சுடர்க் ...... குமரேசா","செழும லர்ப்பொழிற் குரவ முற்றபொற்","றிருவி டைக்கழிப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks for a list of gifts: the meaning of the three Tamils, the fruit of true austerity, many kinds of life, the ancient state of release, the life of the wish-tree, a faultless mind, kingship and a wondrous great life, and with the senses gone, the strength of praise. The praise names Murugan of the Saravana pool, who embraced the hunter girl in the lone forest and split the peaked hill, at Idaikkazhi of the kura groves.","n":"The plea is all asking, with no confession; worldly gifts (அரசு, கற்பகத்தரு வாழ்வு) sit beside release (முத்தியிற் பதம்) and end in புகழ் பலம், the power of praise, the poet's own gift. புலன் அகற்றிட: the senses removed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thamizh","thavam","veedu","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:135"},"p:795":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":795,"t":["படிபுனல்நெ ருப்படற் பவனம்வெளி பொய்கருப்","பவமுறைய வத்தைமுக் ...... குணநீடு","பயில்பிணிகள் மச்சைசுக் கிலமுதிர மத்திமெய்ப்","பசிபடுநி ணச்சடக் ...... குடில்பேணும்","உ டலது பொ றுத்தறக் கடைபெறுபி றப்பினுக்","குணர்வுடைய சித்தமற் ...... றடிநாயேன்","உ ழலுமது கற்பலக் கழலிணையெ னக்களித்","துனதுதம ரொக்கவைத் ...... தருள்வாயே","கொடியவொரு குக்குடக் கொடியவடி விற்புனக்","கொடிபடர்பு யக்கிரிக் ...... கதிர்வேலா","குமரசம ரச்சினக் குமரவணி யத்தன்மெய்க்","குமரமகிழ் முத்தமிழ்ப் ...... புலவோனே","தடவிகட மத்தகத் தடவரைய ரத்தரத்","தடலனுச வித்தகத் ...... துறையோனே","தருமருவு மெத்தலத் தருமருவ முத்தியைத்","தருதிருவி டைக்கழிப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes the body of earth, water, fire, wind and space, the false embryo, the states, the three gunas, diseases, marrow, semen, blood, bone and fat, a hungry hut, and confesses that, bearing it in a birth low in virtue without an aware mind, he wanders; he asks for the pair of feet and a place among Murugan's own people. The praise names the cock banner, Valli's arms, Murugan the son of the true father, poet of the three Tamils, at Idaikkazhi which grants release.","n":"The catalogue mixes the elements, the states of consciousness (அவத்தை) and the three gunas with the body's fluids, a doctrinal and a physical body in one list. உனது தமர் ஒக்க வைத்து: be set among your people. The praise lines on the battle and the elephant (தடவிகட மத்தகத் தடவரை) are hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","avaththai","adiyaar","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:135"},"p:796":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":796,"t":["பழியுறுசட் டகமான குடிலையெடுத் திழிவான","பகரும்வினைச் செயல்மாதர் ...... தருமாயப்","படுகுழிபுக் கினிதேறும் வழிதடவித் தெரியாது","பழமைபிதற் றிடுலொக ...... மு முடர்","உ ழலும்விருப் புடனோது பலசவலைக் கலைதேடி","யொருபயனைத் தெளியாது ...... விளியாமுன்","உ னகமலப் பதநாடி யுருகியுளத் தமுதூற","உ னதுதிருப் புழோத ...... அருள்வாயே","தெழியுவரிச் சலராசி மொகுமொகெனப் பெருமேரு","திடுதிடெனப் பலபூதர் ...... விதமாகத்","திமிதிமெனப் பொருசூர னெறுநெறெனப் பலதேவர்","ஜெயஜெயெனக் கொதிவேலை ...... விடுவோனே","அழகுதரித் திடுநீப சரவணவுற் பவவேல","அடல்தருகெற் சிதநீல ...... மயில்வீரா","அருணைதிருத் தணிநாக மலைபழநிப் பதிகோடை","அதிபஇடைக் கழிமேவு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses that, carrying the blamed hut of the body, he fell into the pit of illusion women offer and gropes for the way out, that the world's fools babble of old things, and that he sought many useless arts without grasping the one meaning; before he dies he asks to seek the lotus feet, melt with nectar welling in his heart and sing Murugan's holy praise. The praise is a battle in drum syllables, the sea churning, Meru shaking and the gods crying victory, the kadamba-wearing Saravana on the peacock, lord of Arunai, Tiruttani, Nagamalai, Palani, Kodai and Idaikkazhi.","n":"ஒருபயனைத் தெளியாது: learning without the one aim is the fault, as in 792. உனது திருப்புகழ் ஓத (written திருப்புழோத) asks for the poet's book by name. The list of shrines at the close makes Idaikkazhi one of Murugan's many seats.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","kalvi","pugazh","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:135"},"p:797":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":797,"t":["பெருக்க மாகிய நிதியினர் வரின்மிக","நகைத்து வாமென அமளிய ருகுவிரல்","பிடித்து போயவர் தொடையொடு தொடைபட ...... வுறவாடிப்","பிதற்றி யேயள விடுபண மதுதம","திடத்தி லேவரு மளவுந லுரைகொடு","பிலுக்கி யேவெகு சரசமொ டணைகுவர் ...... கனமாலாய்","முருக்கி னேரித ழமுதுப ருகுமென","வுரைத்து லீலைக ளதிவித மொடுமலை","முலைக்கு ளேதுயில் கொளமயல் புரிகுவர் ...... பொருள்தீரின்","முறுக்கி யேயுதை கொடுவசை யுரைதரு","மனத்து ரோகிக ளிடுதொழில் வினையற","முடுக்கி யேயுன திருகழல் மலர்தொழ ...... அருள்தாராய்","நெருக்கி யேவரு மவுணர்கள் குலமற","வுறுக்கி யேமயில் முதுகினில் விசைகொடு","நிலத்தி லேசமர் பொருதவ ருயிர்பலி ...... கொளும்வேலா","நெகத்தி லேஅயன் முடிபறி யிறைதிரி","புரத்தி லேநகை புரிபர னடியவர்","நினைப்பி லேயருள் தருசிவ னுதவிய ...... புதல்வோனே","செருக்கு வேடுவர் தருமொரு சிறுமியை","மருக்கு லாவிய மலரணை மிசைபுணர்","திருக்கை வேல்வடி வழகிய குருபர ...... முருகோனே","சிறக்கு மாதவ முனிவரர் மகபதி","யிருக்கு வேதனு மிமையவர் பரவிய","திருக்கு ராவடி நிழல்தனி லுலவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women who laugh and say 'come' when a wealthy man arrives, take his finger to the bed, play thigh to thigh, babble and talk sweetly until the agreed money reaches their place, and lull him to sleep on their breasts; when his wealth ends they twist round, kick and abuse him, and he asks that the work of these traitors end and he be driven to worship the two flower feet. The praise shows the vel that killed the asuras from the peacock's back, Murugan the son of Siva, who plucked Brahma's head with a nail, smiled at the triple city and gives grace in his devotees' thought, embracing the hunter girl on a fragrant bed, under the kura tree praised by sages, Indra and Brahma.","n":"பொருள் தீரின் முறுக்கியே உதை கொடு வசை உரைதரு: the expulsion when the money runs out is shown as a physical kick, the courtesan story told at its harshest. திருக்குராவடி நிழல், the shade of the holy kura tree, is the sthala tree of Idaikkazhi, named again in 798.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:135"},"p:798":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":798,"t":["மருக்கு லாவிய மலரணை ...... கொதியாதே","வளர்த்த தாய்தமர் வசையது ...... மொழியாதே","கருக்கு லாவிய அயலவர் ...... பழியாதே","கடப்ப மாலையை யினிவர ...... விடவேணும்","தருக்கு லாவிய கொடியிடை ...... மணவாளா","சமர்த்த னேமணி மரகத ...... மயில்வீரா","திருக்கு ராவடி நிழல்தனி ...... லுறைவோனே","திருக்கை வேல்வடி வழகிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"In another's voice, the singer asks that the fragrant flower bed not burn the girl, that the mother who raised her and her kin not speak blame, and that the neighbours not slander her, and asks Murugan to send his kadamba garland now. The praise names the husband of the creeper-waisted girl, the hero on the emerald peacock, who dwells in the shade of the holy kura tree, with the beautiful vel in hand.","n":"The three dangers are the bed (கொதியாதே), the family's blame (வசை) and the neighbours' gossip (அயலவர் பழி), so the garland is asked to stop talk as well as longing. The eight lines take their praise words from 797 (மருக்குலாவிய மலரணை, திருக்குராவடி நிழல், திருக்கை வேல் வடிவழகிய).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","alar"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:135"},"p:799":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":799,"t":["முலைகு லுக்கிகள் கபடிகள் வடிபு ழுக்கைக ளசடிகள்","முறைம சக்கிகள் திருடிகள் ...... மதவேணூல்","மொழிப சப்பிகள் விகடிகள் அழும னத்திகள் தகுநகை","முகமி னுக்கிகள் கசடிகள் ...... இடையேசூழ்","கலைநெ கிழ்த்திக ளிளைஞர்கள் பொருள்ப றித்தம","கனியி தழ்ச்சுருள் பிளவிலை ...... யொருபாதி","கலவி யிற்றரும் வசவிகள் விழிம யக்கினில் வசமழி","கவலை யற்றிட நினதருள் ...... புரிவாயே","அலைநெ ருப்பெழ வடவரை பொடிப டச்சம ணர்கள்குலம்","அணிக ழுப்பெற நடவிய ...... மயில்வீரா","அரன ரிப்பிர மர்கள்முதல் வழிப டப்பிரி யமும்வர","அவர வர்க்கொரு பொருள்புகல் ...... பெரியோனே","சிலைமொ ளுக்கென முறிபட மிதிலை யிற்சந கமனருள்","திருவி னைப்புண ரரிதிரு ...... மருகோனே","திரள்வ ருக்கைகள் கமுகுகள் சொரிம துக்கத லிகள்வளர்","திருவி டைக்கழி மருவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet heaps names on the women: breast-shakers, deceivers, dull ones, thieves, coaxers, mockers, fakers who weep, women who loosen their clothes and take young men's wealth, give half a betel roll in union and then abuse; he asks for grace so that the worry of losing himself in their bewitching eyes may end. The praise shows the hill turned to dust and the Jains impaled by the peacock hero, Murugan who told Siva, Vishnu and Brahma each a meaning, nephew of Rama who broke the bow at Mithila and won Sita, at Idaikkazhi of jackfruit, areca and plantain.","n":"இளைஞர்கள் பொருள் பறித்த and ஒருபாதி வெற்றிலைச் சுருள் (half a betel roll) mark these as courtesans who take much and give little. அவரவர்க்கு ஒரு பொருள் புகல் பெரியோனே widens the guru-myth to all three gods. சமணர்கள் குலம் அணி கழுப்பெற gives the impaling of the Jains to the peacock hero.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","mayal"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:135"},"p:800":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":800,"t":["சூழ்ந்தேன்ற துக்கவினை செர்ந்தூன்று மப்பில்வளர்","தூண்போன்ற இக்குடிலு ...... முலகூடே","சோர்ந் தூய்ந்து மக்கினியில் நூண்சாம்பல் பட்டுவிடு","தோம்பாங்கை யுட்பெரிது ...... முணராமே","வீழ்ந்தீண்டி நற்கலைகள் தான்தோண்டி மிக்கபொருள்","வேண்டீங்கை யிட்டுவர ...... குழுவார்போல்","வேம்பாங்கு மற்றுவினை யாம்பாங்கு மற்றுவிளை","வாம்பாங்கில் நற்கழல்கள் ...... தொழஆளாய்","வாழ்ந்தான்ற கற்புடைமை வாய்ந்தாய்ந்த நற்றவர்கள்","வான்தோன்று மற்றவரு ...... மடிபேண","மான்போன்ற பொற்றொடிகள் தாந்தோய்ந்த நற்புயமும்","வான்தீண்ட வுற்றமயில் ...... மிசையேறித்","தாழ்ந்தாழ்ந்த மிக்கடல் வீழ்ந்தீண்டு வெற்பசுரர்","சாய்ந்தேங்க வுற்றமர்செய் ...... வடிவேலா","தானதோன்றி யப்பர்குடி வாழ்ந்தீன்ற நற்புதல்வ","தான்தோன்றி நிற்கவல ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of this hut of a body, a pillar rooted in sorrowful deeds, which tires, wears out, decays and becomes fine ash in the fire, and of not grasping that flaw; like those who dig through learning seeking great wealth and come with outstretched hands, he has gone astray, and asks to be taken as a servant so that, with burning and deeds gone, he worships the good feet in the way that bears fruit. The praise shows ascetics and gods tending his feet, Murugan on the sky-high peacock with arms embraced by his golden-bangled consorts, the vel that fought the Suras of the sunken hill, and the son of the Father of Thanthondri, able to stand self-arisen (தான்தோன்றி).","n":"The song is built of long-vowel clusters ending in -ந்த, -ன்ற (சூழ்ந்தேன்ற, வீழ்ந்தீண்டி, வாழ்ந்தான்ற), so word division is often a guess. The last line puns on the shrine: தான்தோன்றி is both the place and the Lord who appears of himself. வேம் பாங்கும் அற்று வினையாம் பாங்கும் அற்று may mean freedom from burning (birth and death) and from deeds; the simile of the learned beggars is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","nilaiyaamai","vinai","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:136"},"p:801":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":801,"t":["எந்தன்சட லங்கம்பல பங்கம்படு தொந்தங்களை","யென்றுந்துயர் பொன்றும்படி ...... யொருநாளே","இன்பந்தரு செம்பொன்கழ லுந்துங்கழல் தந்தும்பினை","யென்றும்படி பந்தங்கெட ...... மயிலேறி","வந்தும்பிர சண்டம்பகி ரண்டம்புவி யெங்குந்திசை","மண்டும்படி நின்றுஞ்சுட ...... ரொளிபோலும்","வஞ்சங்குடி கொண்டுந்திரி நெஞ்சன்துக ளென்றுங்கொளும்","வண்டன்தமி யன்றன்பவம் ...... ஒழியாதோ","தந்தந்தன திந்திந்திமி யென்றும்பல சஞ்சங்கொடு","தஞ்சம்புரி கொஞ்சுஞ்சிறு ...... மணியாரம்","சந்தந்தொனி கண்டும்புய லங்கன்சிவ னம்பன்பதி","சம்புந்தொழ நின்றுந்தினம் ...... விளையாடும்","கந்தன்குக னென்றன்குரு வென்றுந்தொழு மன்பன்கவி","கண்டுய்ந்திட அன்றன்பொடு ...... வருவோனே","கண்டின்கனி சிந்துஞ்சுவை பொங்கும்புனல் தங்குஞ்சுனை","கந்தன்குடி யின்தங்கிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks that one day Murugan come on the peacock, blazing like a light that fills the heavens, the cosmos, the earth and every direction, and give his red-gold feet that bring joy, so that the ties of his trembling, defective body and his sorrow end; he asks whether the births of this lonely rogue, whose heart houses deceit and always gathers faults, will not cease. The praise shows the little bells of the child's necklace sounding as he plays daily while Siva, the Sambhu, worships, and Murugan coming that day with love so that the devotee who praises him as 'Kandan, Guha, my guru' might see and be saved, at Kandankudi of pools sweet with spilled fruit.","n":"The song chimes on nasal clusters (எந்தன், பங்கம், தொந்தங்களை, வஞ்சம்), and the shrine's name is echoed in the devotee's own formula கந்தன் குகன் என்றன் குரு. நெஞ்சன் துகள் என்றும் கொளும் வண்டன் தமியன்: the self-abuse is of a heart that keeps choosing fault. The devotee saved by Murugan's coming is not named.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","paasam","vanjam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:137"},"p:802":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":802,"t":["இறையத் தனையோ ...... அதுதானும்","இலையிட் டுணலேய் ...... தருகாலம்","அறையிற் பெரிதா ...... மலமாயை","அலையப் படுமா ...... றினியாமொ","மறையத் தனைமா ...... சிறைசாலை","வழியுய்த் துயர்வா ...... னுறுதேவர்","சிறையைத் தவிரா ...... விடும்வேலா","திலதைப் பதிவாழ் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"In a short song the poet asks whether he must be tossed about by impurity and maya, too great to tell, through a life that is a mere moment and even that a time of eating from a leaf. The praise names the vel that ended the imprisonment of the gods kept in the great jail, at Thilathaippathi.","n":"இறை அத்தனையோ ... இலையிட்டு உணல் ஏய்தரு காலம்: the life span may be called 'only a moment' and taken up with eating; the opening lines are compressed and the sense is a guess. அறையில் பெரிதாம் மலமாயை names impurity and maya together, a Siddhanta pairing.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["malam","maayai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:138"},"p:803":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":803,"t":["பனகப் படமி சைந்த முழையிற் றரள நின்று","பாடர்பொற் பணிபு னைந்த ...... முலைமீதிற்","பரிவற் றெரியு நெஞ்சில் முகிலிற் களிய கொண்டை","படுபுட் பவன முன்றி ...... லியலாரும்","அனமொத் திடுசி றந்த நடையிற் கிளியி னின்சொல்","அழகிற் றனித ளர்ந்து ...... மதிமோக","மளவிப் புளக கொங்கை குழையத் தழுவி யின்ப","அலையிற் றிரிவ னென்று ...... மறிவோனே","தனனத் தனன தந்த தனனத் தனன தந்த","தனனத் தனன தந்த ...... தனதானா","தகிடத் தகிட தந்த திமிதத் திமித வென்று","தனிமத் தளமு ழங்க ...... வருவோனே","செநெனற் கழனி பொங்கி திமிலக் கமல மண்டி","செறிநற் கழைதி ரண்டு ...... வளமேவித்","திருநற் சிகரி துங்க வரையைப் பெருவு கின்ற","திலதைப் பதிய மர்ந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes the women whose breasts, like a serpent's hood, bear pearl strings and gold, with cloud-dark hair full of flowers, swan's gait and a parrot's sweet speech, and says that, his heart burning without pity, his mind deluded, he embraces them and wanders in the waves of pleasure; he says this to the Lord 'who knows it always' and makes no further plea. The praise has Murugan come to the sound of the drum, given in the metre's own syllables (தனனத் தனன தந்த, தகிடத் தகிட), at Thilathaippathi of red paddy, lotus, sugarcane and a tall peak.","n":"The plea is replaced by a statement: திரிவன் என்று அறிவோனே, 'you who know that I wander so'. Lines 9 and 10 set out the song's metrical pattern (தனனத் தனன தந்த ... தனதானா) as the drum's sound, and the next line continues it as the beat of the drum.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","mayal","punarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:138"},"p:804":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":804,"t":["மகரக் குழைக்கு ளுந்து நயனக் கடைக்கி லங்கு","வசியச் சரத்தி யைந்த ...... குறியாலே","வடவெற் பதைத்து ரந்து களபக் குடத்தை வென்று","மதர்விற் பணைத்தெ ழுந்த ...... முலைமீதே","உ கமெய்ப் பதைத்து நெஞ்சும் விரகக் கடற்பொ திந்த","வுலைபட் டலர்ச்ச ரங்கள் ...... நலியாமல்","உ லகப் புகழ்ப்பு லம்பு கலியற் றுணர்ச்சி கொண்டு","னுரிமைப் புகழ்ப்ப கர்ந்து ...... திரிவேனோ","புகர்கைக் கரிப்பொ திந்த முளரிக் குளத்தி ழிந்த","பொழுதிற் கரத்தொ டர்ந்து ...... பிடிநாளிற்","பொருமித் திகைத்து நின்று வரதற் கடைக்க லங்கள்","புகுதக் கணத்து வந்து ...... கையிலாருந்","திகிரிப் படைத்து ரந்த வரதற் குடற்பி றந்த","சிவைதற் பரைக்கி சைந்த ...... புதல்வோனே","சிவபத் தர்முத்த ரும்பர் தவசித் தர்சித்த மொன்று","திலதைப் பதிக்கு கந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether, freed from the eyes that dart toward the makara earrings with enchanting arrows, from breasts that outdo Meru and the sandal pot, and from Kama's flower arrows that torment a trembling body and a heart in the furnace of longing, and rid of the babble of praising the world, he will wander speaking the praise that is rightfully Murugan's. The praise tells how the elephant entered the lotus pool, was seized by the crocodile and stood struggling and bewildered, and how Vishnu, the boon-giver, came at once as its refuge and threw the discus; Murugan is the son of Siva's consort, born of the same body as that Vishnu, at Thilathaippathi where Siva's devotees, the freed, gods, ascetics and siddhas gather with one mind.","n":"உலகப் புகழ் புலம்பு கலி அற்று ... உன் உரிமைப் புகழ் பகர்ந்து: the poet sets the praise of worldly men against the praise that belongs to Murugan, a turn his songs at the court shrines make often. The praise tells the story of Gajendra in its unmistakable details (the pool, the crocodile, the discus). Uma is called Vishnu's sister (வரதற்கு உடற்பிறந்த சிவை).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kaamanoy","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:138"},"p:805":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":805,"t":["சோதி மந்திரம் போத கம்பரவு","ஞான கம்பரந் தேயி ருந்தவெளி","தோட லர்ந்தபொன் பூவி ருந்தஇட ...... முங்கொளாமல்","சூது பந்தயம் பேசி யஞ்சுவகை","சாதி விண்பறிந் தோடு கண்டர்மிகு","தோத கம்பரிந் தாடு சிந்துபரி ...... கந்துபாயும்","வீதி மண்டலம் பூண மர்ந்துகழி","கோல மண்டிநின் றாடி யின்பவகை","வேணு மென்றுகண் சோர ஐம்புலனொ ...... டுங்குபோதில்","வேதி யன்புரிந் தேடு கண்டளவி","லோடி வெஞ்சுடுங் காட ணைந்துசுட","வீழ்கி வெந்துகுந் தீடு மிந்தஇட ...... ரென்றுபோமோ","ஆதி மண்டலஞ் சேர வும்பரம","சோம மண்டலங் கூட வும்பதும","வாளன் மண்டலஞ் சார வுஞ்சுழிப ...... டர்ந்ததோகை","ஆழி மண்டலந் தாவி யண்டமுத","லான மண்டலந் தேடி யொன்றதொழு","கான மண்டலஞ் சேட னங்கணயில் ...... கொண்டுலாவிச்","சூதர் மண்டலந் தூளெ ழுந்துபொடி","யாகி விண்பறந் தோட மண்டியொரு","சூரி யன்திரண் டோட கண்டுநகை ...... கொண்டவேலா","சோடை கொண்டுளங் கான மங்கைமய","லாடி இந்திரன் தேவர் வந்துதொழ","சோழ மண்டலஞ் சாரு மம்பர்வளர் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says that, without taking hold of the light, the mantra, the teaching and the open space where the golden flower blooms, he talked of gambling and wagers, of five breeds of horses leaping in the street circuits, dressed up and danced for pleasure until his eyes grew dim and his five senses shrank, and that when the term written on the leaf by Brahma is reached, men run to the burning ground, fall and are burnt; he asks when this trouble will end. The praise has the peacock leap over the circles of sun, moon and sea and search the cosmos, the dust of the deceivers' realm fly to the sky while Murugan smiles, and Murugan infatuated with the forest girl, lord of Ambar in the Chola land where Indra and the gods worship.","n":"சோதி மந்திரம் போதகம் ... வெளி தோடு அலர்ந்த பொன் பூ இருந்த இடம்: the first lines seem to name the yogic goals, the inner light, the mantra, the open space and the golden lotus, which the poet failed to reach. The second half is built on மண்டலம் (circles of sun and moon, of the sea, of the cosmos), perhaps also the yogic circles. The lines on gambling and horses (அஞ்சுவகை சாதி, சிந்துபரி) are hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["soodhu","nilaiyaamai","oli","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:139"},"p:806":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":806,"t":["காதோடு தோடிகலி யாடவிழி வாள்சுழல","கோலாக லாரமுலை மார்புதைய பூணகல","காரோடு கூடளக பாரமல ரோடலைய ...... அணைமீதே","காலோடு காலிகலி யாடபரி நூபுரமொ","டேகாச மானவுடை வீசியிடை நூல்துவள","காவீர மானஇத ழூறல்தர நேசமென ...... மிடறோதை","நாதான கீதகுயில் போலஅல்குல் மால்புரள","மார்போடு தோள்கரமொ டாடிமிக நாணழிய","நானாவி நோதமுற மாதரொடு கூடிமயல் ...... படுவேனை","நானாரு நீயெவனெ னாமலென தாவிகவர்","சீர்பாத மேகவலை யாயுமுன வேநிதமு","நாதாகு மாரமுரு காஎனவு மோதஅருள் ...... புரிவாயே","பாதாள சேடனுட லாயிரப ணாமகுட","மாமேரொ டேழுகட லோதமலை சூரருடல்","பாழாக தூளிவிணி லேறபுவி வாழவிடு ...... சுடர்வேலா","பாலாழி மீதரவின் மேல்திருவொ டேயமளி","சேர்நீல ரூபன்வலி ராவணகு ழாமிரிய","பாரேவை யேவியமு ராரியைவர் தோழனரி ...... மருகோனே","மாதாபு ராரிசுக வாரிபரை நாரியுமை","ஆகாச ரூபியபி ராமிவல மேவுசிவன்","மாடேறி யாடுமொரு நாதன்மகிழ் போதமருள் ...... குருநாதா","வானோர்க ளீசன்மயி லோடுகுற மாதுமண","வாளாகு காகுமர மாமயிலின் மீதுதிரு","மாகாள மாநகரில் மாலொடடி யார்பரவு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes lovemaking on the bed: earrings swinging, eyes wheeling like swords, ornaments slipping, hair and flowers loosened, legs entwined, anklets and garments, the nectar of the lips and a cuckoo's voice in the throat, and calls himself infatuated with such women; he asks for grace so that, without asking 'who am I, who are you', he may think anxiously on the feet that seized his life and chant daily 'Lord, Kumara, Muruga'. The praise names the vel that sent Sesha's thousand hoods, Meru, the seven seas and the Suras' bodies to dust so the earth could live, Vishnu on the serpent in the milk sea who routed Ravana's host and was friend of the five Pandavas as uncle, and Murugan the guru who gave the joy of understanding to Siva the dancer on the bull with Uma at his side, at Thirumakalam where Vishnu and devotees worship.","n":"நான் ஆரு நீ எவன் எனாமல்: perhaps a plea to hold the feet without the questioning that keeps self and Lord apart, or simply without hesitation; the phrase is open. எனது ஆவி கவர் சீர்பாதம்: the feet are said to have already taken the poet's life, so the turn is to what has claimed him. Siva is called மாடேறி ஆடும் ஒரு நாதன், and Murugan the guru who gave him போதம்.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","mayal","thiruvadi","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:140"},"p:807":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":807,"t":["குங்குமகற் பூர நாவி யிமசல","சந்தனகத் தூரி லேப பரிமள","கொங்கைதனைக் கோலி நீடு முகபட ...... நகரேகை","கொண்டைதனைக் கோதி வாரி வகைவகை","துங்கமுடித் தால கால மெனவடல்","கொண்டவிடப் பார்வை காதி னெதிர்பொரு ...... மமுதேயாம்","அங்குளநிட் டூர மாய விழிகொடு","வஞ்சமனத் தாசை கூறி யெவரையு","மன்புடைமெய்க் கோல ராக விரகினி ...... லுறவாடி","அன்றளவுக் கான காசு பொருள்கவர்","மங்கையர்பொய்க் காதல் மோக வலைவிழ","லன்றியுனைப் பாடி வீடு புகுவது ...... மொருநாளே","சங்கதசக் ¡£வ னோடு சொலவள","மிண்டுசெயப் போன வாயு சுதனொடு","சம்பவசுக் ¡£வ னாதி யெழுபது ...... வெளமாகச்","சண்டகவிச் சேனை யால்மு னலைகடல்","குன்றிலடைத் தேறி மோச நிசிசரர்","தங்கிளைகெட் டோட ஏவு சரபதி ...... மருகோனே","எங்குநினைப் போர்கள் நேச சரவண","சிந்துரகர்ப் பூர ஆறு முககுக","எந்தனுடைச் சாமி நாத வயலியி ...... லுறைவேலா","இன்புறுபொற் கூட மாட நவமணி","மண்டபவித் தார வீதி புடைவளர்","இஞ்சிகுடிப் பார்வ தீச ரருளிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes the women with breasts perfumed with kumkum, camphor, musk and sandal under a breast-cloth, combed hair, poison-like eyes that fight the ears, who with cruel deceitful eyes and deceitful hearts speak desire, play the lover with everyone, and take the coins and goods the day is worth; he asks whether, instead of falling into the net of their false love, one day he will sing Murugan and enter release. The praise tells of Hanuman, son of the wind, who went to the ten-necked Ravana with a message, Sugriva and the seventy vellam of monkeys, the sea dammed with hills and the night-roamers' clan scattered by Rama, Murugan's uncle; it names Murugan the six-faced Guha and 'my Swaminatha', the vel of Vayalur, and the lord at Inchikudi blessed by Siva Parvatheesar.","n":"அன்றளவுக்கான காசு பொருள் கவர்: the courtesans take exactly what the day yields, the money-measured love of 33. அன்புடை மெய்க் கோலராக: they put on a 'loving true form' for everyone. The Rama story is told with Hanuman and Sugriva named. Murugan is also called the vel of Vayalur, a shrine dear to the poet.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:141"},"p:808":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":808,"t":["பச்சை யொண்கிரி போலிரு மாதன","முற்றி தம்பொறி சேர்குழல் வாளயில்","பற்று புண்டரி காமென ஏய்கயல் ...... விழிஞான","பத்தி வெண்டர ளாமெனும் வாணகை","வித்ரு மஞ்சிலை போல்நுத லாரிதழ்","பத்ம செண்பக மாமநு பூதியி ...... னழகாளென்","றிச்சை யந்தரி பார்வதி மோகினி","தத்தை பொன்கவி னாலிலை போல்வயி","றிற்ப சுங்கிளி யானமி னூலிடை ...... யபிராமி","எக்கு லங்குடி லோடுல கியாவையு","மிற்ப திந்திரு நாழிநெ லாலற","மெப்பொ தும்பகிர் வாள்கும ராஎன ...... வுருகேனோ","கச்சை யுந்திரு வாளுமி ராறுடை","பொற்பு யங்களும் வேலுமி ராறுள","கட்சி வங்கம லாமுக மாறுள ...... முருகோனே","கற்ப கந்திரு நாடுயர் வாழ்வுற","சித்தர் விஞ்சையர் மாகர்ச பாசென","கட்ட வெங்கொடு சூர்கிளை வேரற ...... விடும்வேலா","நச்சு வெண்பட மீதணை வார்முகில்","பச்சை வண்புய னார்கரு டாசனர்","நற்க ரந்தநு கோல்வளை நேமியர் ...... மருகோனே","நற்பு னந்தனில் வாழ்வளி நாயகி","யிச்சை கொண்டொரு வாரண மாதொடு","நத்தி வந்துந ளாறுறை தேவர்கள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet lists the beauties of Parvati, breasts like green mountains, fish-like eyes, pearl teeth, coral lips, a bow-like brow, a banyan-leaf belly and a waist thin as a thread, calls her Abhirami, Mohini and the parrot, the beauty of inner experience, and the one who, settling every clan, hut and world in her house, shares out charity at all times with two measures of paddy; he asks whether he will melt calling Murugan 'Kumara' as her son. The praise names his belt and sword, twelve arms, twelve eyes and six faces, the vel that cut out the root of Sura's kin so the kalpaka heaven could live while siddhas and vidyadharas cried 'well done', Vishnu on the white serpent with Garuda, bow, conch and discus as uncle, and Murugan coming with Valli of the millet field and the elephant's daughter to Nallaru.","n":"The opening uses the form the poet usually gives to courtesans, the catalogue of breasts, eyes, lips and waist, but the woman is the Goddess, and it ends in devotion, not confession. இரு நாழி நெல்லால் அறம் எப்பொதும் பகிர்வாள: the Goddess who keeps the charities with two measures of paddy, as at Kanchi. அநுபூதியின் அழகாள் may mean 'she whose beauty is inner experience'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saththi","aram","paththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:142"},"p:809":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":809,"t":["தருவூரிசை யாரமு தார்நிகர்","குயிலார்மொழி தோதக மாதர்கள்","தணியாமய லாழியி லாழவு ...... மமிழாதே","தழலேபொழி கோரவி லோசன","மெறிபாசம காமுனை சூலமுள்","சமனார்முகில் மேனிக டாவினி ...... லணுகாதே","கருவூறிய நாளுமு நூறெழு","மலதேகமு மாவலு மாசைக","படமாகிய பாதக தீதற ...... மிடிதீரக்","கனிவீறிய போதமெய் ஞானமு","மியலார்சிவ நேசமு மேவர","கழல்சேரணி நூபுர தாளிணை ...... நிழல்தாராய்","புருகூதன்மி னாளொரு பாலுற","சிலைவேடுவர் மானொரு பாலுற","புதுமாமயில் மீதணை யாவரு ...... மழகோனே","புழுகார்பனிர் மூசிய வாசனை","யுரகாலணி கோலமென் மாலைய","புரிநூலுமு லாவுது வாதச ...... புயவீரா","மருவூர்குளிர் வாவிகள் சோலைகள்","செழிசாலிகு லாவிய கார்வயல்","மகதாபத சீலமு மேபுனை ...... வள்முதூர்","மகதேவர்பு ராரிச தாசிவர்","சுதராகிய தேவசி காமணி","வழுவூரில்நி லாவிய வாழ்வருள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks not to sink in the sea of unending infatuation with cuckoo-voiced deceitful women, nor to have Death, fiery-eyed, with noose and trident, cloud-black on his buffalo, come near; and that, with the sins of this impure body since the womb, its sorrow, desire and deceit, and his poverty ended, true knowledge, ripened awareness and love of Siva come, and the shade of the anklet-bearing feet be given. The praise shows Murugan on the new peacock with Indra's daughter on one side and the hunters' deer on the other, the twelve-armed hero with civet and rosewater garlands and the sacred thread, son of Siva the Mahadeva and Sadasiva, at the old town of Vazhuvur of cool tanks, groves and paddy, known for its great penance.","n":"The plea moves from two dangers to avoid (women, Death) to a list of gifts: போதம், மெய்ஞானம், சிவநேசம். கருவூறிய நாளும் ... மல தேகம்: the body is impure from conception. மிடி (poverty) is named among the ills to end, beside sin and desire.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mayal","kootru","gnaanam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:143"},"p:810":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":810,"t":["தலைநா ளிற்பத மேத்தி யன்புற","வுபதே சப்பொரு ளூட்டி மந்திர","தவஞா னக்கட லாட்டி யென்றனை ...... யருளாலுன்","சதுரா கத்தொடு கூட்டி யண்டர்க","ளறியா முத்தமி ழூட்டி முண்டக","தளிர்வே தத்துறை காட்டி மண்டலம் ...... வலமேவும்","கலைசோ திக்கதிர் காட்டி நன்சுட","ரொளிநா தப்பர மேற்றி முன்சுழி","கமழ்வா சற்படி நாட்ட முங்கொள ...... விதிதாவிக்","கமலா லைப்பதி சேர்த்து முன்பதி","வெளியா கப்புக ஏற்றி யன்பொடு","கதிர்தோ கைப்பரி மேற்கொ ளுஞ்செயல் ...... மறவேனே","சிலைவீ ழக்கடல் கூட்ட முங்கெட","அவுணோ ரைத்தலை வாட்டி யம்பர","சிரமா லைப்புக வேற்ற வுந்தொடு ...... கதிர்வேலா","சிவகா மிக்கொரு தூர்த்த ரெந்தையர்","வரிநா கத்தொடை யார்க்கு கந்தொரு","சிவஞா னப்பொரு ளூட்டு முண்டக ...... அழகோனே","மலைமே வித்தினை காக்கு மொண்கிளி","யமுதா கத்தன வாட்டி யிந்துள","மலர்மா லைக்குழ லாட்ட ணங்கிதன் ...... மணவாளா","வரிகோ ழிக்கொடி மீக்கொ ளும்படி","நடமா டிச்சுரர் போற்று தண்பொழில்","வழுவூர் நற்பதி வீற்றி ருந்தருள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet recalls what the Lord did: on the first day made him praise the feet with love, fed him the meaning of the teaching, bathed him in the sea of mantra, penance and knowledge, joined him to his company, fed him the three Tamils the gods do not know, showed the Veda's path, showed the light of the kala circling the mandala, raised him to the nada beyond the light, set him at the doorstep of the fragrant whorl, leapt over fate, brought him to Kamalalaya and the open space, and then came lovingly mounted on the radiant peacock; he will not forget it. The praise names the vel that felled the hill and the demons' heads, Murugan who fed Siva, Sivakami's rogue lover with serpent garlands, the meaning of Siva-knowledge, Valli's husband, and the lord of Vazhuvur where the gods praise his dance.","n":"A rare autobiographical song: the plea is replaced by remembrance (செயல் மறவேனே), and the stages read as an inner ascent: teaching (உபதேசப் பொருள்), Tamil (முத்தமிழ்), the light of kala (கலை சோதிக் கதிர்), nada (நாதப் பரம்), the whorl (சுழி), the space (வெளி). கமலாலைப் பதி may be Tiruvarur, whose tank is Kamalalayam, or the lotus within; I cannot settle it. சிவகாமிக்கு ஒரு தூர்த்தர் is a playful epithet of Siva.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","oli","naadham","thamizh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:143"},"p:811":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":811,"t":["அன்னமிசைச் செந்நளிநச் சென்மிகணக் கந்நியமத்","தன்னமயப் புலால்யாக்கை ...... துஞ்சிடாதென்","றந்நினைவுற் றன்னினைவுற் றன்னியரிற் றன்னெறிபுக்","கன்னியசற் றுலர்முச்ச ...... டங்கயோகம்","என்னுமருட் கின்னமுடைப் பன்னவைகற் றின்னவைவிட்","டின்னணமெய்த் தடாமார்க்க ...... மின்புறாதென்","றின்னதெனக் கென்னுமதப் புன்மைகெடுத் தின்னல்விடுத்","தின்னதெனப் படாவாழ்க்கை ...... தந்திடாதோ","கன்னல்மொழிப் பின்னளகத் தன்னநடைப் பன்னவுடைக்","கண்ணவிரச் சுறாவீட்டு ...... கெண்டையாளைக்","கன்னமிடப் பின்னிரவிற் றுன்னுபுரைக் கன்முழையிற்","கன்னிலையிற் புகாவேர்த்து ...... நின்றவாழ்வே","பொன்னசலப் பின்னசலச் சென்னியினற் கன்னபுரப்","பொன்னிநதிக் கராநீர்ப்பு ...... யங்கனாதா","பொன்மலையிற் பொன்னினகர்ப் புண்ணியர்பொற் பொன்மவுலிப்","பொன்னுலகத் திராசாக்கள் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of thinking that this flesh body made of food, under the reckoning of Brahma born on the lotus, will not die, of entering others' paths and learning the many books of the delusion called yoga, and of the pettiness of sect that says 'this is for me'; he asks whether Murugan will not destroy that pettiness, free him from trouble and give a life that cannot be named 'this'. The praise shows Murugan, for the girl with cane-sweet speech, braided hair and swan's gait, standing sweating late at night at the mouth of a cave in the hill like a burglar, unable to enter; it names the Lord of Kannapuram on the Ponni with the serpent bracelet and Murugan lord of the kings of the golden world.","n":"The song alliterates on அன்ன/இன்ன/கன்ன throughout, so word division is uncertain. இன்னது எனக்கு என்னும் மதப் புன்மை: the fault named is sectarian pride; முச்சடங்க யோகம் may refer to a threefold practice. இன்னது எனப்படா வாழ்க்கை: the life asked for cannot be named or defined. கன்னமிட in the praise compares Murugan at Valli's cave to a housebreaker, a striking image.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["samayam","yaakkai","yogam","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:144"},"p:812":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":812,"t":["இபமாந்தர் சக்ர பதிசெறி","படையாண்டு சக்ர வரிசைக","ளிடவாழ்ந்து திக்கு விசயம ...... ணரசாகி","இறுமாந்து வட்ட வணைமிசை","விரிசார்ந்து வெற்றி மலர்தொடை","யெழிலார்ந்த பட்டி வகைபரி ...... மளலேபந்","தபனாங்க ரத்ந வணிகல","னிவைசேர்ந்த விச்சு வடிவது","தமர்சூழ்ந்து மிக்க வுயிர்நழு ...... வியபோது","தழல்தாங்கொ ளுத்தி யிடவொரு","பிடிசாம்பல் பட்ட தறிகிலர்","னவாஞ்சை மிக்கு னடிதொழ ...... நினையாரே","உ பசாந்த சித்த குருகுல","பவபாண்ட வர்க்கு வரதன்மை","யுருவோன்ப்ர சித்த நெடியவன் ...... ரிஷிகேசன்","உ லகீன்ற பச்சை யுமையணன்","வடவேங்க டத்தி லுறைபவ","னுயர்சார்ங்க சக்ர கரதலன் ...... மருகோனே","த்ரிபுராந்த கற்கு வரசுத","ரதிகாந்தன் மைத்து னமுருக","திறல்பூண்ட சுப்ர மணியஷண் ......முகவேலா","திசைபாய்ந்த பத்ம தடவய","லியில்வேந்த முத்தி யருள்தரு","திருவாஞ்சி யத்தி லமரர்கள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes kings: lords of elephants and the wheel of rule, commanding armies, honoured, conquering the directions, swelling with pride on round cushions, with victory garlands, silks, perfumes and jewels, whose body, when the life slips away among the kin, is lit with fire and becomes a handful of ash; they do not know it, and with longing do not think to worship Murugan's feet. The praise names Vishnu, boon-giver to the Pandavas of the Kuru line, Hrishikesa, Uma's brother, dweller on northern Venkatam with bow and discus, as uncle, and Murugan as son of the destroyer of the triple city and kin of Kama, Subrahmanya of the six faces, at Thiruvanchiyam where the king grants release.","n":"The confession is not the poet's own but a portrait of rulers, an argument from impermanence: ஒரு பிடி சாம்பல் பட்டது அறிகிலர். உன் அவாஞ்சை மிக்கு அடிதொழ நினையாரே may pun on the shrine's name (வாஞ்சியம், வாஞ்சை 'longing'). Murugan is called மைத்துனன் of Kama, both being in the poet's scheme Vishnu's kin.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","arasu","selvam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:145"},"p:813":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":813,"t":["வங்கார மார்பிலணி தாரொடுயர் கோடசைய","கொந்தார மாலைகுழ லாரமொடு தோள்புரள","வண்காதி லோலைகதிர் போலவொளி வீசஇதழ் ...... மலர்போல","மஞ்சாடு சாபநுதல் வாளனைய வேல்விழிகள்","கொஞ்சார மோககிளி யாகநகை பேசியுற","வந்தாரை வாருமிரு நீருறவெ னாசைமய ...... லிடுமாதர்","சங்காளர் சூதுகொலை காரர்குடி கேடர்சுழல்","சிங்கார தோளர்பண ஆசையுளர் சாதியிலர்","சண்டாளர் சீசியவர் மாயவலை யோடடியெ ...... னுழலாமற்","சங்கோதை நாதமொடு கூடிவெகு மாயையிருள்","வெந்தோட மூலஅழல் வீசவுப தேசமது","தண்காதி லோதியிரு பாதமலர் சேரஅருள் ...... புரிவாயே","சிங்கார ரூபமயில் வாகனந மோநமென","கந்தாகு மாரசிவ தேசிகந மோநமென","சிந்தூர பார்வதிசு தாகரந மோநமென ...... விருதோதை","சிந்தான சோதிகதிர் வேலவந மோநமென","கங்காள வேணிகுரு வானவந மோநமனெ","திண்சூர ராழிமலை தூள்படவை வேலைவிடு ...... முருகோனே","இங்கீத வேதபிர மாவைவிழ மோதியொரு","பெண்காத லோடுவன மேவிவளி நாயகியை","யின்பான தேனிரச மார்முலைவி டாதகர ...... மணிமார்பா","எண்டோளர் காதல்கொடு காதல்கறி யேபருகு","செங்காடு மேவிபிர காசமயில் மேலழகொ","டென்காதல் மாலைமுடி ஆறுமுக வாவமரர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women with jewelled chests, garlanded hair, earrings bright as rays, bow-like brows and spear-like eyes, who speak like parrots and invite all comers with 'come, be close', and calls them cheats, gamblers, killers, ruiners of families, lovers of money, outcasts and wretches; he asks that, rather than wander in their net of maya, the dense darkness of maya burn away with the conch-like nada sounding and the root fire rising, and that the teaching be spoken into his cool ear so he reaches the two flower feet. The praise is a chain of 'namo nama' to the peacock rider, Kumara, the teacher of Siva, Parvati's son and the bright vel, names the vel that turned Sura, the sea and the hill to dust, Brahma struck down, Murugan gone to the forest for love of Valli, and Chengadu, where the eight-armed Siva with love drank the curry of love.","n":"சங்கோதை நாதமொடு கூடி ... மூல அழல் வீச உபதேசமது தண் காதில் ஓதி: the remedy is yogic and given by a guru, the inner sound (nada) and the root fire (மூலாதார) rising, with the teaching whispered in the ear. எண்டோளர் காதல் கொடு காதல் கறியே பருகு செங்காடு names the shrine's story, Siva eating the curry offered by Siruthondar; the text names only the curry and the place. The courtesans' abuse list (சூது, கொலைகாரர், குடிகேடர், பண ஆசையுளர்) is harsher than usual.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","naadham","kundalini","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:146"},"p:814":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":814,"t":["சித்தி ரத்திலுமி குத்த பொற்பவள","மொத்த மெத்தஅழ குற்ற குத்துமுலை","சிற்ப சிற்பமயி ரொத்த சிற்றிடைய ...... வஞ்சிமாதர்","சித்த மத்தனையு முற்ற ளப்பகடல்","மொய்த்த சிற்றுமண லுக்கு மெட்டியது","சிக்கு மைக்குழல்கள் கஸ்து ரிப்பரிம ...... ளங்கள்வீசப்","பத்தி ரத்திலுமி குத்த கட்கயல்கள்","வித்து ருத்துநுவ ளைத்த நெற்றிவனை","பற்க ளைப்பளிரெ னச்சி ரித்துமயல் ...... விஞ்சைபேசிப்","பச்சை ரத்நமயி லைப்பொ லத்தெருவி","லத்தி யொத்தமத மொத்து நிற்பர்வலை","பட்டு ழைத்துகுழி யுற்ற அத்தியென ...... மங்குவேனா","தத்த னத்தனத னத்த னத்தனன","தித்தி மித்திமிதி மித்தி மித்திமித","தக்கு டுக்குடுடு டுக்கு டுக்குடென ...... சங்குபேரி","சத்த முற்றுகடல் திக்கு லக்கிரிகள்","நெக்கு விட்டுமுகி லுக்கு சர்ப்பமுடி","சக்கு முக்கிவிட கட்க துட்டசுர ...... ரங்கமாள","வெற்றி யுற்றகதிர் பத்தி ரத்தையரு","ளிச்சு ரர்க்கதிப திப்ப தத்தையரு","வித்த ளித்தமதி பெற்ற தத்தைமண ...... முண்டவேலா","வெட்கி டப்பிரம னைப்பி டித்துமுடி","யைக்கு லைத்துசிறை வைத்து முத்தர்புகழ்","விற்கு டிப்பதியி லிச்சை யுற்றுமகிழ் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women with breasts lovelier than in a painting, waists thin as a hair, who measure every mind as one would count the sea's sand, with musky hair, fish-like eyes and bright laughter, speaking spells of infatuation and standing in the golden street like a green-gem peacock or a rutting elephant, and asks whether, caught in their net, he will fade like an elephant that has fallen into a pit. The praise is a battle in drum syllables, conch and drums sounding, the sea, the mountains of the directions and the serpent's hoods shaken as the wicked Suras die; Murugan gave Indra back his place and married the parrot-like Devayanai, and seized Brahma, beat his head and imprisoned him to his shame; he is lord at Virkudi praised by the freed.","n":"சித்தம் அத்தனையும் முற்று அளப்ப கடல் மொய்த்த சிற்றுமணலுக்கும் எட்டியது: the women can measure a whole mind as if counting the sea's grains, so their skill is knowledge of men. The poet becomes the elephant trapped in a pit (குழி உற்ற அத்தி), after the women were compared to a rutting elephant (அத்தி ஒத்த மதம்). Brahma's imprisonment is given without its usual cause.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","mayal","vanjam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:147"},"p:815":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":815,"t":["குடல்நிண மென்புபு லால்கமழ் குருதிந ரம்பிவை தோலிடை","குளுகுளெ னும்படி மூடிய ...... மலமாசு","குதிகொளு மொன்பது வாசலை யுடையகு ரம்பையை நீரெழு","குமிழியி னுங்கடி தாகியெ ...... யழிமாய","அடலையு டம்பைய வாவியெ அநவர தஞ்சில சாரமி","லவுடத மும்பல யோகமு ...... முயலாநின்","றலமரு சிந்தையி னாகுல மலமல மென்றினி யானுநி","னழகிய தண்டைவி டாமல ...... ரடைவேனோ","இடமற மண்டு நிசாசர ரடைய மடிந்தெழு பூதர","மிடிபட இன்பம கோததி ...... வறிதாக","இமையவ ருஞ்சிறை போயவர் பதியு ளிலங்க விடாதர","எழில்பட மொன்று மொராயிர ...... முகமான","விடதர கஞ்சுகி மேருவில் வளைவதன் முன்புர நீறெழ","வெயில்நகை தந்த புராரிம ...... தனகோபர்","விழியினில் வந்து பகீரதி மிசைவள ருஞ்சிறு வாவட","விஜயபு ரந்தனில் மேவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes the body as gut, fat, bone, flesh, blood and nerves, wrapped in skin so the filth sloshes within, a hut with nine gates of impurity, perishing faster than a bubble on water; desiring this ashen body, he kept trying worthless medicines and many yogas until his anguished mind cried 'enough, enough', and he asks whether he will now reach the beautiful flower feet with anklets. The praise names the night-roamers dead, the mountain crushed, the great sea dried and the gods freed from prison, Siva who bent Meru with the thousand-hooded serpent and burned the triple city with a smile and glared at Kama, and Murugan who came from his eye and grew on the Ganga, at Vijayapuram.","n":"குடல் நிணம் என்பு புலால் ... ஒன்பது வாசலை உடைய குரம்பை: the body is the familiar hut with nine openings. The plea rejects the means of preserving it: சாரமில் அவுடதமும் பல யோகமும் முயலா நின்று அலமரு சிந்தையின் ஆகுலம் அலமலம், medicines and yogas tried on the body are a source of anguish, not rescue.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","nilaiyaamai","marundhu","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:148"},"p:816":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":816,"t":["கூசா தேபா ரேசா தேமால்","கூறா நூல்கற் ...... றுளம்வேறு","கோடா தேவேல் பாடா தேமால்","கூர்கூ தாளத் ...... தொடைதோளில்","வீசா தேபேர் பேசா தேசீர்","வேதா தீதக் ...... கழல்மீதே","வீழா தேபோய் நாயேன் வாணாள்","வீணே போகத் ...... தகுமோதான்","நேசா வானோ ¡£சா வாமா","நீபா கானப் ...... புனமானை","நேர்வா யார்வாய் சூர்வாய் சார்வாய்","நீள்கார் சூழ்கற் ...... பகசாலத்","தேசா தீனா தீனா ¡£சா","சீரா ரூரிற் ...... பெருவாழ்வே","சேயே வேளே பூவே கோவே","தேவே தேவப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether it is right that his days go in vain, a dog's life, without his singing the vel, putting the koothalam garland Murugan loves on his shoulders, speaking his name and falling at the feet beyond the Veda, the mind turning nowhere else. The praise calls Murugan friend, lord of the gods, the kadamba-wearer who went to the millet field for the deer-like Valli, lord of the land of kalpaka trees, lord of the lowly, the great life of glorious Arur, child, Vel, flower, king and god of gods.","n":"The song is made almost entirely of long syllables in two-syllable words ending in -ஆதே (கூசாதே, பாடாதே, பேசாதே, வீழாதே), a list of what the poet has failed to do. The first line (கூசாதே பார் ஏசாதே மால் கூறா நூல் கற்று) is hard to divide; it may describe learning, unashamed, books that tell no delusion.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["pugazh","thiruvadi","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:149"},"p:817":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":817,"t":["கூர்வாய் நாராய் வாராய் போனார்","கூடா ரேசற் ...... றலஆவி","கோதா னேன்மா தாமா றானாள்","கோளே கேள்மற் ...... றிளவாடை","ஈர்வாள் போலே மேலே வீசா","ஏறா வேறிட் ...... டதுதீயின்","ஈயா வாழ்வோர் பேரே பாடா","ஈடே றா¡¢ற் ...... கெடலாமோ","சூர்வா ழாதே மாறா தேவாழ்","சூழ்வா னோர்கட் ...... கருள்கூருந்","தோலா வேலா வீறா ரூர்வாழ்","சோதீ பாகத் ...... துமையூடே","சேர்வாய் நீதீ வானோர் வீரா","சேரா ரூரைச் ...... சுடுவார்தஞ்","சேயே வேளே பூவே கோவே","தேவே தேவப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"In the voice of a girl in love, the singer calls to the sharp-beaked crane: the one who left has not come back, her life has withered to a husk, her mother has turned against her, the town's slander is heard, and the young north wind cuts like a saw and burns like fire; she asks whether she must be ruined like those who sing the names of misers and never prosper. The praise names the unbeaten vel that let no Sura live and gave grace to the gods, the light of Arur with Uma in his half, and Murugan as son of Siva who burned the enemies' towns.","n":"This is akam poetry: the crane as messenger (கூர்வாய் நாராய்), the mother's anger and the north wind (இளவாடை) are classical, and the poet's own confession is absent. ஈயா வாழ்வோர் பேரே பாடா ஈடேறாரின் கெடலாமோ: the girl compares her fate to poets who sing for misers, the poet's own recurring complaint (827, 828). Word division in the long syllables is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["kaamanoy","pirivu","iravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:149"},"p:818":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":818,"t":["பாலோ தேனோ பாகோ வானோர்","பாரா வாரத் ...... தமுதேயோ","பாரோர் சீரோ வேளேர் வாழ்வோ","பானோ வான்முத் ...... தெனநீளத்","தாலோ தாலே லோபா டாதே","தாயமார் நேசத் ...... துனுசாரந்","தாரா தேபே ¡£யா தேபே","சாதே யேசத் ...... தகுமோதான்","ஆலோல் கேளா மேலோர் நாண்மா","லானா தேனற் ...... புனமேபோய்","ஆயாள் தாள்மேல் வீழா வாழா","ஆளா வேளைப் ...... புகுவோனே","சேலோ டேசே ராரால் சாலார்","சீரா ரூரிற் ...... பெருவாழ்வே","சேயே வேளே பூவே கோவே","தேவே தேவப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The first half strings the endearments of a lullaby, 'are you milk, honey, syrup, the nectar of the gods' sea, the glory of the world, the sun, the pearl of the sky', and asks whether it is fit to be reproached without having sung that lullaby, talo talelo, with a mother's love. The praise shows Murugan, hearing Valli's bird-scaring cry, going in love to the millet field and falling at her feet, and names him the great life of Arur with its fish-filled tanks.","n":"பாலோ தேனோ பாகோ ... தாலோ தாலேலோ: the opening is a lullaby's string of questions, the form of pillaitamil, and 822 opens with the same words for a woman's speech. Who is reproached and what is asked in lines 5 to 8 is unclear; the line with பே¡£யாதே is damaged. ஆயாள் தாள்மேல் வீழா: Murugan falling at Valli's feet is a recurring image of his humility in love.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["anbu","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:149"},"p:819":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":819,"t":["நீதானெத் தனையாலும் ந¨டுழிக் ...... க்ருபையாகி","மாதானத் தனமாக மாஞானக் ...... கழல்தாராய்","வேதாமைத் துனவேளே வீராசற் ...... குணசீலா","ஆதாரத் தொளியானே எருரிற் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"In four lines the poet asks Murugan to be gracious for long ages in every way and to give him, as a great gift and treasure, the feet of great knowledge. The praise names him Brahma's kin, the hero of true virtue, the light in the adharas, lord at Arur.","n":"மாஞானக் கழல்: the feet are named as knowledge itself. ஆதாரத்து ஒளியானே: the Lord as the light in the yogic centres. வேதா மைத்துன: Brahma, as Vishnu's son, is Murugan's cousin in the poet's scheme.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["thiruvadi","gnaanam","thiruvarul","aadhaaram"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:149"},"p:820":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":820,"t":["மகரம துகெட இருகுமி ழடைசி","வாரார்ச ரங்க ...... ளெனநீளும்","மதர்விழி வலைகொ டுலகினில் மனிதர்","வாணாள டங்க ...... வருவார்தம்","பகர்தரு மொழியில் ம்ருகமத களப","பாடீர கும்ப ...... மிசைவாவிப்","படிமன துனது பரிபுர சரண","பாதார விந்த ...... நினையாதோ","நகமுக சமுக நிருதரு மடிய","நானாவி லங்கல் ...... பொடியாக","நதிபதி கதற வொருகணை தெரியு","நாராய ணன்றன் ...... மருகோனே","அகனக கனக சிவதல முழுது","மாராம பந்தி ...... யவைதோறும்","அரியளி விததி முறைமுறை கருது","மர்ருர மர்ந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women whose long eyes, like arrows, are nets that end men's lifetimes, and whose speech, musk and sandal on their breasts draw his mind, and asks whether that mind will not think of Murugan's anklet-bearing lotus feet. The praise names the Nirutas' host dead and the hills crushed, Rama who made the lord of rivers cry out with a single arrow as Narayana, Murugan's uncle, and Arur, where in every grove of the Siva shrine the bees hum in order.","n":"மனிதர் வாணாள் அடங்க வருவார்: the women are said to come to end men's lives, the courtesan as a form of Death. நதிபதி கதற ஒரு கணை: Rama threatening the ocean with one arrow before the bridge.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","mayal","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:149"},"p:821":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":821,"t":["கரமு முளரியின் மலர்முக மதிகுழல்","கனம தெணுமொழி கனிகதிர் முலைநகை","கலக மிடுவிழி கடலென விடமென ...... மனதூடே","கருதி யனநடை கொடியிடை யியல்மயில்","கமழு மகிலுட னிளகிய ம்ருகமத","களப புளகித கிரியினு மயல்கொடு ...... திரிவேனும்","இரவு பகலற இகலற மலமற","இயலு மயலற விழியினி ரிழிவர","இதய முருகியெ யொருகுள பதமுற ...... மடலு¡டே","யெழுத அரியவள் குறமக ளிருதன","கிரியில் முழுகின இளையவ னெனு முரை","யினிமை பெறுவது மிருபத மடைவது ...... மொருநாளே","சுரபி மகவினை யெழு¦பொருள் வினவிட","மனுவி னெறிமணி யசைவுற விசைமிகு","துயரில் செவியினி லடிபட வினவுமி ...... னதிதீது","துணிவி லிதுபிழை பெரிதென வருமநு","உ ருகி யரகர சிவசிவ பெறுமதொர்","சுரபி யலமர விழிபுனல் பெருகிட ...... நடுவாகப்","பரவி யதனது துயர்கொடு நடவிய","பழுதின் மதலையை யுடலிரு பிளவொடு","படிய ரதமதை நடவிட மொழிபவ ...... னருளர்ருர்ப்","படியு லறுமுக சிவசுத கணபதி","யிளைய குமரநி ருபபதி சரவண","பரவை முறையிட அயில்கொடு நடவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet, who wanders infatuated with women whose hands are lotuses, faces moons and breasts hills, asks whether a day will come when, with night and day, enmity, impurity and infatuation gone, tears flowing and heart melted, he gains the sweetness of the word 'the young one who sank into the breasts of the hunter girl, too beautiful to draw on the madal' and reaches the two feet. The praise tells how a cow, whose calf had been killed, rang the bell of Manu's justice; the king heard of the wrong, melted crying 'Hara Siva', and ordered the chariot driven over his own guilty son, splitting his body, while the cow wept; Murugan is the six-faced son of Siva, Ganapati's younger brother, lord at that Arur of grace, whose vel made the sea cry out.","n":"குறமகள் ... மடலூடே எழுத அரியவள்: Valli is the girl too lovely to draw on the madal, the banner of the lover's palmyra horse, so the love theme is carried into the praise. The praise tells, unusually at length, the Tiruvarur story of the Chola king of Manu's justice, who executed his own son for a calf; the king is not named beyond மனுவின் நெறி.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["mayal","malam","sengol","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:149"},"p:822":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":822,"t":["பாலோ தேனோ பலவுறு சுளையது","தானோ வானோர் அமுதுகொல் கழைரச","பாகோ வூனோ டுருகிய மகனுண ...... வருண்ஞானப்","பாலோ வேறோ மொழியென அடுகொடு","வேலோ கோலோ விழியென முகமது","பானோ வானூர் நிலவுகொ லெனமகண் ...... மகிழ்வேனை","நாலாம் ரூபா கமலக்ஷண் முகவொளி","யேதோ மாதோம் எனதகம் வளரொளி","நானோ நீயோ படிகமொ டொளிரிட ...... மதுசோதி","நாடோ வீடோ நடுமொழி யெனநடு","தூணேர் தோளா சுரமுக கனசபை","நாதா தாதா எனவுரு கிடஅருள் ...... புரிவாயே","மாலாய் வானோர் மலர்மழை பொழியவ","தாரா சூரா எனமுநி வர்கள்புகழ்","மாயா ரூபா அரகர சிவசிவ ...... எனவோதா","வாதா டூரோ டவுணரொ டலைகடல்","கோகோ கோகோ எனமலை வெடிபட","வாளால் வேலால் மடிவுசெய் தருளிய ...... முருகோனே","சூலாள் மாலாள் மலர்மகள் கலைமகள்","ஓதார் சீராள் கதிர்மதி குலவிய","தோடாள் கோடா ரிணைமுலை குமரிமுன் ...... அருள்பாலா","தூயா ராயார் இதுசுக சிவபத","வாழ்வா மீனே வதிவமெ னுணர்வொடு","சூழ்சீ ரர்ருர் மருவிய இமையவர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet, delighting in a woman and asking whether her speech is milk, honey, jackfruit, the gods' nectar, cane syrup or the milk of knowledge given to the child who melted, whether her eyes are vel or arrow and her face sun or moon, asks for grace to melt instead asking: what is the light of the six faces, is the light growing within me I or you, is the place that shines like crystal a land or release, tell me the middle word, Lord of the golden hall. The praise has the gods rain flowers, sages hail 'Hara Siva', the demons and the roaring sea cry out and the hill split, and names Murugan the son born to the maiden who is Uma, Lakshmi and Saraswati, at Arur, where the pure say 'this is the bliss of Siva's state, let us stay here'.","n":"The song uses one form for two loves: the lover's questions (மொழி பாலோ தேனோ, விழி வேலோ கோலோ) are matched by the seeker's (நானோ நீயோ, நாடோ வீடோ). ஊனோடு உருகிய மகன் உண அருள் ஞானப் பால் refers to the milk of knowledge given to the child Sambandar. எனது அகம் வளர் ஒளி நானோ நீயோ: whether the inner light is self or Lord is put as a question, not answered.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oli","veedu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:149"},"p:823":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":823,"t":["கலகவிழி மாமகளிர் கைக்குளே யாய்ப்பொய்","களவுமத னூல்பலப டித்தவா வேட்கை","கனதனமு மார்புமுற லிச்சையா லார்த்து ...... கழுநீரார்","கமழ்நறைச வாதுபுழு கைத்துழாய் வார்த்து","நிலவரசு நாடறிய கட்டில்போட் டார்ச்செய்","கருமமறி யாதுசிறு புத்தியால் வாழ்க்கை ...... கருதாதே","தலமடைசு சாளரமு கப்பிலே காத்து","நிறைபவுசு வாழ்வரசு சத்யமே வாய்த்த","தெனவுருகி யோடியொரு சற்றுளே வார்த்தை ...... தடுமாறித்","தழுவியது ராகமும்வி ளைத்துமா யாக்கை","தனையுமரு நாளையும வத்திலே போக்கு","தலையறிவி லேனைநெறி நிற்கநீ தீ¨க்ஷ ...... தரவேணும்","அலகில்தமி ழாலுயர்ச மர்த்தனே போற்றி","அருணைநகர் கோபுரவி ருப்பனே போற்றி","அடல்மயில்ந டாவியப்ரி யத்தனே போற்றி ...... அவதான","அறுமுகசு வாமியெனும் அத்தனே போற்றி","அகிலதல மோடிவரு நிர்த்தனே போற்றி","அருணகிரி நாதஎனும் அப்பனே போற்றி ...... அசுரேசர்","பெலமடிய வேல்விடுக ரத்தனே போற்றி","கரதலக பாலிகுரு வித்தனே போற்றி","பெரியகுற மாதணைபு யத்தனே போற்றி ......பெருவாழ்வாம்","பிரமனறி யாவிரத தக்ஷ¢ணா மூர்த்தி","பரசமய கோளிரித வத்தினால் வாய்த்த","பெரியமட மேவியசு கத்தனே யோக்யர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses that in the hands of quarrel-eyed women he learned the false, thieving books of love, bathed and perfumed himself with civet, musk and tulasi for desire of their breasts, made a bed known to the whole land, waited at the window, thought all wealth and rule had come true, ran melting and stammered in embrace, and so wasted this illusory body and precious days; he asks that Murugan give initiation to one without sense, so he may stand on the path. The praise is a chain of 'hail': to the master exalted by boundless Tamil, to the one fond of the tower of Arunai, to the rider of the peacock, the six-faced lord called the father, the dancer who runs through all shrines, the father called Arunagirinathar, the vel against the Asuras, the guru of Siva who holds the skull, Valli's embrace, and the lord of the joy of Periyamadam, gained by the penance of a Dakshinamurthy unknown to Brahma, a lion to other sects.","n":"நெறி நிற்க நீ தீக்ஷை தரவேணும்: the plea is for formal initiation (தீக்ஷை), fitting a monastery shrine. அருணை நகர் கோபுர விருப்பனே and அருணகிரி நாத எனும் அப்பனே: Murugan is called by the name of the Lord of Arunai, which is also the poet's own name, and placed at Tiruvannamalai's tower. Whether the Dakshinamurthy of the last lines is Siva as the silent teacher or the ascetic who founded the madam, a 'lion to other sects' (பரசமய கோளரி), the text does not settle.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["kaamam","guru","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:150"},"p:824":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":824,"t":["ஒருவழிப டாது மாயை யிருவினைவி டாது நாளு","முழலுமநு ராக மோக ...... அநுபோகம்","உ டலுமுயிர் தானு மாயு னுணர்விலொரு காலி ராத","வுளமுநெகிழ் வாகு மாறு ...... அடியேனுக்","கிரவுபகல் போன ஞான பரமசிவ யோக தீர","மெனமொழியும் வீசு பாச ...... கனகோப","எமபடரை மோது மோன வுரையிலுப தேச வாளை","யெனதுபகை தீர நீயும் ...... அருள்வாயே","அரிவையொரு பாக மான அருணகிரி நாதர் பூசை","அடைவுதவ றாது பேணும் ...... அறிவாளன்","அமணர்குல கால னாகும் அரியதவ ராஜ ராஜன்","அவனிபுகழ் சோமநாதன் ...... மடமேவும்","முருகபொரு சூரர் சேனை முறியவட மேரு வீழ","முகரசல ராசி வேக ...... முனிவோனே","மொழியுமடி யார்கள் கோடி குறைகருதி னாலும் வேறு","முனியஅறி யாத தேவர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of the passion, infatuation and enjoyment that wander daily and never go one way, with maya and the two deeds never leaving him, and asks that, so his body, life and a heart that never stays a moment in awareness of the Lord may melt, Murugan give him the sword of teaching in the silent word, called the steadfast yoga of Siva-knowledge where night and day are gone, which strikes Yama's servants who fling the noose in anger, and so end his enemies. The praise names the monastery of Somanathan, praised on earth, a wise man who never fails in the worship of the Lord of Arunai who is half woman, death to the Jains' clan, a king among ascetics; Murugan who broke the Sura army and Meru; and the god who never knows anger though countless devotees state their wants.","n":"மோன உரையில் உபதேச வாள்: the teaching given in silence is a sword against Death's servants, joining the two common pleas (the guru's word, rescue at death). The praise honours a human founder, சோமநாதன், as அமணர் குல காலன், the same title the poet gives Sambandar. The last line, முனிய அறியாத தேவர், praises the Lord's patience with petitioners.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mounam","guru","kootru","maayai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:151"},"p:825":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":825,"t":["உ ரையொ ழிந்துநின் றவர்பொரு ளெளிதென","வுணர்வு கண்டுபின் திரவிய இகலரு","ளொருவர் நண்படைந் துளதிரள் கவர்கொடு ...... பொருள்தேடி","உ ளம கிழ்ந்துவந் துரிமையில் நினைவுறு","சகல இந்த்ரதந்த் ரமும்வல விலைமக","ளுபய கொங்கையும் புளகித மெழமிக ...... வுறவாயே","விரக வன்புடன் பரிமள மிகவுள","முழுகி நன்றியொன் றிடமல ரமளியில்","வெகுவி தம்புரிந் தமர்பொரு சமயம ...... துறுநாளே","விளைத னங்கவர்ந் திடுபல மனதிய","ரயல்த னங்களுந் தனதென நினைபவர்","வெகுளி யின்கணின் றிழிதொழி லதுவற ...... அருள்வாயே","செருதி னைந்திடுஞ் சினவலி யசுரர்க","ளுகமு டிந்திடும் படியெழு பொழுதிடை","செகம டங்கலும் பயமற மயில்மிசை ...... தனிலேறித்","திகுதி குந்திகுந் திகுதிகு திகுதிகு","தெனதெ னந்தெனந் தெனதென தெனதென","திமிதி மிந்திமிந் திமிதிமி திமியென ...... வருபூதங்","கரையி றந்திடுங் கடலென மருவிய","வுதிர மொண்டுமுண் டிடஅமர் புரிபவ","கலவி யன்புடன் குறமகள் தழுவிய ...... முருகோனே","கனமு றுந்த்ரியம் பகபுர மருவிய","கவுரி தந்தகந் தறுமுக எனஇரு","கழல்ப ணிந்துநின் றமரர்கள் தொழவல ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes the women who see that men struck speechless are easy prey, befriend one and seize all his wealth, skilled in every art, the price-women whose breasts thrill and who make love in every way on the flower bed; they take what a man has earned and think others' wealth their own, and he asks to be freed from the base work of standing in their anger. The praise has Murugan mount the peacock to end the Asuras' age and free the world from fear, the drums sounding in syllables, ghosts drinking blood from a shoreless sea of it, the hunter girl embraced, and the gods bowing at Thiriyambakapuram saying 'Kanda, six-faced, given by Gauri'.","n":"விலைமகள் is named outright. அயல் தனங்களும் தனது என நினைபவர்: the courtesans think others' wealth their own. வெகுளியின்கண் நின்று இழிதொழில் is obscure; it may mean the base life of serving their tempers.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","selvam","punarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:152"},"p:826":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":826,"t":["கன்ன லொத்த மொழிச்சொல் வேசியர்","வன்ம னத்தை யுருக்கு லீலையர்","கண்வெ ருட்டி விழித்த பார்வையர் ...... இதமாகக்","கையி லுற்ற பொருட்கள் யாவையும்","வையெ னக்கை விரிக்கும் வீணியர்","கைகள் பற்றி யிழுத்து மார்முலை ...... தனில்வீழப்","பின்னி விட்ட சடைக்கு ளேமலர்","தன்னை வைத்து முடிப்பை நீயவி","ழென்னு மற்ப குணத்த ராசையி ...... லுழலாமற்","பெய்யு முத்தமி ழிற்ற யாபர","என்ன முத்தர் துதிக்க வேமகிழ்","பிஞ்ஞ கர்க்குரை செப்பு நாயக ...... அருள்தாராய்","வன்னி யொத்த படைக்க லாதிய","துன்னு கைக்கொ ளரக்கர் மாமுடி","மண்ணி லற்று விழச்செய் மாதவன் ...... மருகோனே","மன்னு பைப்பணி யுற்ற நீள்விட","மென்ன விட்டு முடுக்கு சூரனை","மல்லு டற்று முருட்டு மார்பற ...... அடைவாகச்","சென்னி பற்றி யறுத்த கூரிய","மின்னி ழைத்த திறத்த வேலவ","செய்ய பொற்புன வெற்பு மானணை ...... மணிமார்பா","செம்ம னத்தர் மிகுத்த மாதவர்","நன்மை பெற்ற வுளத்தி லேமலர்","செல்வ சிக்கல் நகர்க்குள் மேவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes courtesans with cane-sweet speech who melt hard hearts, startled glances, who spread their hands saying 'put down everything in your hand', pull a man's hands to their breasts and say 'untie my braided hair where I put the flowers', and asks not to wander in desire for such petty women but to be given the grace of the lord who spoke the word to Siva, delighted by the freed who praised him as the merciful one who pours out the three Tamils. The praise names Vishnu who cut the demons' crowns as uncle, the sharp vel that took Sura's head as he came spreading like a serpent's poison, Valli's breast, and the lord of Sikkal, where great ascetics of upright heart dwell.","n":"கையில் உற்ற பொருட்கள் யாவையும் வை என கை விரிக்கும்: the courtesans' demand for everything in the hand is given as speech. பிஞ்ஞகர்க்கு உரை செப்பு நாயக: the teaching given to Siva, here without Om named; முத்தமிழில் தயாபர is how the freed praise him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","guru","thamizh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:153"},"p:827":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":827,"t":["புலவரை ரக்ஷ¢க் குந்தாரு வேமது","ரிதகுண வெற்பொக் கும்பூவை மார்முலை","பொருபுய திக்கெட் டும்போயு லாவிய ...... புகழாளா","பொருவரு நட்புப் பண்பான வாய்மையி","லுலகிலு னக்கொப் புண்டோவெ னாநல","பொருள்கள் நிரைத்துச் செம்பாக மாகிய ...... கவிபாடி","விலையில்த மிழ்ச்சொற் குன்போலு தாரிகள்","எவரென மெத்தக் கொண்டாடி வாழ்வெனும்","வெறிகொளு லுத்தர்க் கென்பாடு கூறிடு ...... மிடிதீர","மிகவரு மைப்பட் டுன்பாத தாமரை","சரணமெ னப்பற் றும்பேதை யேன்மிசை","விழியருள் வைத்துக் குன்றாத வாழ்வையு ...... மருள்வாயே","இலகிய வெட்சிச் செந்தாம மார்புய","சிலைநுதல் மைக்கட் சிந்தூர வாணுதல்","இமயம கட்குச் சந்தான மாகிய ...... முருகோனே","இளையகொ டிச்சிக் கும்பாக சாதன","னுதவுமொ ருத்திக் குஞ்சீல நாயக","எழிலியெ ழிற்பற் றுங்காய மாயவன் ...... மருகோனே","அலர்தரு புட்பத் துண்டாகும் வாசனை","திசைதொறு முப்பத் தெண்காதம் வீசிய","அணிபொழி லுக்குச் சஞ்சார மாமளி ...... யிசையாலே","அழகிய சிக்கற் சிங்கார வேலவ","சமரிடை மெத்தப் பொங்கார மாய்வரும்","அசுரரை வெட்டிச் சங்கார மாடிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses singing to misers drunk on their wealth: 'kalpaka tree that protects poets, mountain of sweet virtue, whose arms press women's breasts, whose fame has gone to the eight directions, is there anyone in the world equal to you in friendship and truth, who is as generous to priceless Tamil words?', telling them his plight to end his poverty; a fool in great hardship, he now clings to Murugan's lotus feet as refuge and asks to be looked on with grace and given unfailing life. The praise names Murugan with the vetchi garland, the child of Himalaya's daughter, lord of the young forest girl and of the one Indra gave, Vishnu's nephew, and Singaravelan of lovely Sikkal, whose groves' scent carries thirty-eight kathams, who cut down the Asuras in battle.","n":"The first half quotes the poet's own flattery, so the confession is in the genre of patron praise (செம்பாகமாகிய கவி), not of desire. வெறிகொள் உலுத்தர்க்கு என் பாடு கூறிடும் மிடி: it is poverty that drives the poet to beg from misers. சிங்கார வேலவ names Sikkal's god.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["iravu","inmai","pugazh","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:153"},"p:828":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":828,"t":["ஓல மிட்டி ரைத்தெ ழுந்த வேலை வட்ட மிட்ட இந்த","ஊர்மு கிற்ற ருக்க ளொன்று ...... மவராரென்","று¡ம ரைப்ர சித்த ரென்று மூட ரைச்ச மர்த்த ரென்றும்","ஊன ரைப்ர புக்க ளென்று ...... மறியாமற்","கோல முத்த மிழ்ப்ர பந்த மால ருக்க ரைத்த நந்த","கோடி யிச்சை செப்பி வம்பி ...... லுழல்நாயேன்","கோப மற்று மற்று மந்த மோக மற்று னைப்ப ணிந்து","கூடு தற்கு முத்தி யென்று ...... தருவாயே","வாலை துர்க்கை சக்தி யம்பி லோக கத்தர் பித்தர் பங்கில்","மாது பெற்றெ டுத்து கந்த ...... சிறியோனே","வாரி பொட்டெ ழக்ர வுஞ்சம் வீழ நெட்ட யிற்று ரந்த","வாகை மற்பு யப்ர சண்ட ...... மயில்வீரா","ஞால வட்ட முற்ற வுண்டு நாக மெத்தை யிற்று யின்ற","நார ணற்க ருட்சு ரந்த ...... மருகோனே","நாலு திக்கும் வெற்றி கொண்ட சூர பத்ம னைக்க ளைந்த","நாக பட்டி னத்த மர்ந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses that, asking who in this sea-girt world equals the clouds and the kalpaka trees, he called the dumb famous, fools capable and base men lords, composed fine Tamil prabandhas for them, spoke countless wants and wandered in vain like a dog; he asks when, with anger and delusion gone, Murugan will give him liberation, to worship him and join him. The praise names the Mother, Durga, Shakti, Ambika, who is in the half of the mad Lord and bore him, the vel thrown at Krauncha, Narayana who swallowed the world and sleeps on the serpent as uncle, and the slayer of Surapadma at Nagapattinam.","n":"ஊமரைப் பிரசித்தர் என்றும் மூடரைச் சமர்த்தர் என்றும் ஊனரைப் பிரபுக்கள் என்றும்: the inversions of patron poetry are listed plainly. முகில் தருக்கள் ஒன்றும் அவர் ஆர்: the patron is flattered as cloud and kalpaka tree, the same flattery quoted in 827.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["iravu","pugazh","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:154"},"p:829":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":829,"t":["மார்பு ரம்பினளி னங்கிரியெ னுந்தனமொ","டார மும்படித ரம்பொறியு டன்பணிகள்","மாலை யொண்பவள மும்பரிம ளங்கலவை ...... தொங்கலாட","வாள்ச ரங்கணிய லுங்குழைத ளம்பளக","பார தொங்கலணி பெண்கள்வத னங்கள்மதி","வாகை யென்பஇத ழுஞ்சலச மென்பகள ...... சங்குமோக","சார மஞ்சள்புய முங்கிளிமு கங்களுகிர்","பாளி தம்புனைது வண்டிடையொ டின்பரச","தாழி யென்பஅல்கு லுந்துளிர ரம்பைதொடை ...... ரம்பைமாதர்","தாள்ச தங்கைகொலு சுங்குலசி லம்புமணி","யாடல் கொண்டமட மங்கையரு டன்கலவி","தாக முண்டுழல்கி னுங்கழலு றுங்கழல்ம ...... றந்திடேனே","வீர வெண்டையமு ழங்கவரி சங்குமுர","சோடு பொன்பறைத தும்பவிதி யுஞ்சுரரும்","வேத விஞ்சையரு டன்குமுற வெந்துகவ ...... டர்ந்தசூரன்","வீற டங்கமுகி லுங்கமற நஞ்சுடைய","ஆயி ரம்பகடு கொண்டவுர கன்குவடு","மேகொ ளுந்தபல்சி ரந்தனையெ றிந்துநட ...... னங்கொள்வேலா","நார சிங்கவடி வங்கொடுப்ர சண்டிரணி","யோன டுங்கநட னஞ்செய்துஇ லங்கைவலி","ராவ ணன்குலம டங்கசிலை கொண்டகரர் ...... தந்த்முல","ஞான மங்கையமு தஞ்சொருபி யென்றனொரு","தாய ணங்குகுற மங்கையைம ணந்தபுய","நாகை யம்பதிய மர்ந்துவளர் நம்பர்புகழ் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet lists, part by part, the bodies of dancing young women: breasts like hills with necklaces and garlands, sword-like eyes, swinging earrings and heavy hair, moon faces and lotus lips, conch throats, turmeric arms, nails like parrots' beaks, a thin waist, the 'vessel of pleasure', banana thighs, anklets and bells, and says that even if he wanders thirsting for union with them he will not forget the feet where the anklets ring. The praise has drums and conches sound as Sura's pride is ended and the many-hooded serpent's peak struck while the vel dances, names Vishnu who as Narasimha made Hiranya tremble and with the bow ended Ravana's clan, the hunter girl, the nectar-formed maiden of knowledge, 'my one mother', and the lord at Nagai praised by devotees.","n":"The plea is concessive: கலவி தாகம் உண்டு உழல்கினும் கழல் உறும் கழல் மறந்திடேனே, even while wandering in desire, the poet will not forget the feet. Valli is called என்றன் ஒரு தாய், 'my one mother'. Line 21 ends in a garble (தந்த்முல), which leaves the lines on Valli uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:154"},"p:830":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":830,"t":["விழுதா தெனவே கருதா துடலை","வினைசேர் வதுவே ...... புரிதாக","விருதா வினிலே யுலகா யதமே","லிடவே மடவார் ...... மயலாலே","அழுதா கெடவே அவமா கிடநா","ளடைவே கழியா ...... துனையோதி","அலர்தா ளடியே னுறவாய் மருவோ","ரழியா வரமே ...... தருவாயே","தொழுதார் வினைவே ரடியோ டறவே","துகள்தீர் பரமே ...... தருதேவா","சுரர்பூபதியே கருணா லயனே","சுகிர்தா வடியார் ...... பெருவாழ்வே","எழுதா மறைமா முடிவே வடிவே","லிறைவா எனையா ...... ளுடையோனே","இறைவா எதுதா வதுதா தனையே","இணைநா கையில்வாழ் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks that, rather than let his days pass in waste, not thinking the body will fall, doing only what binds deeds, with Lokayata in the ascendant and weeping and ruined by infatuation with women, he be given an undying boon: to praise Murugan and be joined as kin to his flower feet. The praise names the god who uproots the deeds of those who worship and gives the stainless supreme, king of the gods, abode of compassion, life of devotees, the end of the unwritten Veda, the Lord who rules the poet, and asks him to give whatever is to be given, even himself, at Nagai.","n":"உலகாயதம் மேலிடவே: the poet names Lokayata, the materialist school that denies anything beyond the body, as part of his wasted life. எழுதா மறை, 'the unwritten Veda', is the oral scripture. இறைவா எது தாவது அது தா தனையே is open; it may ask the Lord to give himself.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["samayam","mayal","vinai","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:154"},"p:831":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":831,"t":["உ ரமுற் றிருசெப் பெனவட் டமுமொத்","திளகிப் புளகித் ...... திடமாயே","உ டைசுற் றுமிடைச் சுமையொக் கஅடுத்","தமிதக் கெறுவத் ...... துடன்வீறு","தரமொத் துபயக் களபத் தளமிக்","கவனத் தருணத் ...... தனமீதே","சருவிச் சருவித் தழுவித் தழுவித்","தவமற் கவிடுத் ...... துழல்வேனோ","அரிபுத் திரசித் தசஅக் கடவுட்","கருமைத் திருமைத் ...... துனவேளே","அடல்குக் குடநற் கொடிகட் டியனர்த்","தசுரப் படையைப் ...... பொருவோனே","பரிவுற் றவருக் கருள்வைத் தருள்வித்","தகமுத் தமிழைப் ...... பகர்வோனே","பழனத் தொளிர்முத் தணியெட் டிகுடிப்","பதியிற் குமரப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women with breasts like twin caskets, round, soft and thrilling, burdening the waist, smeared with sandal, and asks whether, abandoning penance, he will wander, quarrelling and embracing them again and again. The praise names Murugan the dear kinsman of Kama, son of Vishnu, who raised the cock banner and fought the Asura army, who in grace speaks the three Tamils to those who love him, and the lord at Ettikudi where pearls shine in the fields.","n":"சருவிச் சருவித் தழுவித் தழுவித் தவம் அற்க விடுத்து: the doubled verbs enact the repetition of desire, and penance is what it gives up. அரி புத்திர சித்தச: Kama as Vishnu's son and Murugan's cousin.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","punarchi","thavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:155"},"p:832":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":832,"t":["ஓங்கு மைம்புல னோட நினைத்தின் ...... பயர்வேனை","ஓம்பை றும்ப்ரண வாதி யுரைத்தெந் ...... தனையாள்வாய்","வாங்கி வெங்கணை சூரர் குலக்கொம் ...... புகடாவி","வாங்கி நின்றன ஏவி லுகைக்குங் ...... குமரேசா","மூங்கி லம்புய வாச மனக்குஞ் ...... சரிமானு","மூண்ட பைங்குற மாது மணக்குந் ...... திருமார்பா","கங்கை யங்கறு பாசில் மனத்தன் ...... பர்கள்வாழ்வே","காஞ்சி ரங்குடி ஆறு முகத்தெம் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"In a short song the poet, who grieves while thinking of pleasure as his five senses run, asks Murugan to rule him by speaking Om and what follows from it. The praise names Kumaresa, whose drawn arrows struck the Suras' clan, husband of the elephant's daughter and the hunter girl, life of devotees with minds free of attachment, the six-faced lord at Kanjirangudi.","n":"ப்ரணவாதி உரைத்து எந்தனை ஆள்வாய்: the teaching of Om is asked for directly. ஓம்பை றும் before it looks damaged. The song names Kanjirangudi (காஞ்சிரங்குடி), not Ettikudi, the heading it stands under.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piranavam","guru","avaa"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:155"},"p:833":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":833,"t":["கடலொத்த விடமொத்த கணையொத்த பிணையொத்த","கயலொத்த மலரொத்த ...... விழிமானார்","கனசெப்பு நளினத்து முகைவெற்பை நிகர்செப்பு","கதிர்முத்து முலைதைக்க ...... அகலாதே","மிடலுற்ற கலவிக்கு ளுளநச்சி வளமற்று","மிடிபட்டு மடிபட்டு ...... மனமாழ்கி","மெலிவுற்ற தமியற்கு னிருபத்ம சரணத்தை","மிகநட்பொ டருள்தற்கு ...... வருவாயே","தடையற்ற கணைவிட்டு மணிவஜ்ர முடிபெற்ற","தலைபத்து டையதுட்ட ...... னுயிர்போகச்","சலசத்து மயிலுற்ற சிறைவிட்டு வருவெற்றி","தருசக்ர தரனுக்கு ...... மருகோனே","திடமுற்ற கனகப்பொ துவில்நட்பு டனடித்த","சிவனுக்கு விழியொத்த ...... புதல்வோனே","செழுநத்து மிழுமுத்து வயலுக்குள் நிறைபெற்ற","திகழெட்டி குடியுற்ற ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women with eyes like the sea, poison, arrows, does, fish and flowers, and breasts like lotus buds, caskets and hills that pierce him, and says that, unable to leave them and craving their union, he lost his wealth, fell into poverty and ruin and sank in mind; he asks Murugan to come to him, alone and weak, and give his lotus feet in friendship. The praise names Rama, whose unstoppable arrow took the life of the wicked ten-headed king and freed the lotus-born Sita from prison, as uncle, Murugan as the eye-like son of Siva who danced in the golden hall, and Ettikudi where shells spit pearls in the fields.","n":"மிடலுற்ற கலவிக்குள் உளம் நச்சி வளமற்று மிடிபட்டு மடிபட்டு: here desire leads to poverty by itself, without the courtesans' theft. கனகப் பொதுவில் நட்புடன் அடித்த சிவன்: the dance in the golden hall is that of Chidambaram.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","inmai","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:155"},"p:834":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":834,"t":["மைக்குழ லொத்தவை நீலோ மாலோ","அக்கணி ணைக்கிணை சேலோ வேலோ","மற்றவர் சொற்றெளி பாலோ பாகோ ...... வடிதேனோ","வத்திர மெய்ச்சசி தானோ நாணா","குத்துமு லைக்கிள நீரோ மேரோ","வைப்பதி டைக்கிணை நூலோ மேலோ ...... வெனமாதர்","தக்கவு றுப்பினுள் மாலே மேலாய்","லச்சைய றப்புணர் வாதே காதே","சைச்சையெ னத்திரி நாயே னோயா ...... தலையாதே","தற்பொறி வைத்தருள் பாராய் தாராய்","தற்சமை யத்தக லாவே னாதா","தத்தும யிற்பரி மீதே நீதான் ...... வருவாயே","முக்கணர் மெச்சிய பாலா சீலா","சித்தசன் மைத்துன வேளே தோளார்","மொய்த்தம ணத்தது ழாயோன் மாயோன் ...... மருகோனே","முத்தமிழ் வித்வவி நோதா கீதா","மற்றவ ரொப்பில ரூபா தீபா","முத்திகொ டுத்தடி யார்மேல் மாமால் ...... முருகோனே","இக்குநி ரைத்தவி ராலு¡ர் சேலு¡ர்","செய்ப்பழ நிப்பதி யூரா வர்ருர்","மிக்கவி டைக்கழி §வ்ளுர் தர்ருர் ...... வயலு¡ரா","எச்சுரு திக்குளு நீயே தாயே","சுத்தவி றற்றிறல் வீரா தீரா","எட்டிகு டிப்பதி வேலா மேலோர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks of women: is the dark hair sapphire or cloud, are the eyes fish or vel, is their speech milk, syrup or honey, the face the moon, the breasts tender coconuts or Meru, the waist a thread; and as a dog who wanders crying 'chee chee' while craving their limbs and uniting shamelessly, he asks not to suffer and wander, but to have Murugan set his mark on him, look on him with grace and come on the peacock. The praise names the child whom the three-eyed Siva praised, Kama's kinsman, Vishnu's nephew, master of the three Tamils, the lamp and incomparable form, Muruga who gives liberation to devotees, lord of Viralur, Selur, Palani, Arur, Vidaikkazhi and Vayalur, the mother within every scripture, the vel of Ettikudi.","n":"The catalogue is put as questions (நீலோ மாலோ, சேலோ வேலோ), as in 822. தற்பொறி வைத்து அருள்: the Lord's mark set on the poet, like the brand on a servant. எச்சுருதிக்குளும் நீயே தாயே calls Murugan the mother in all scripture. Several shrine names in lines 19 to 21 are damaged.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["kaamam","punarchi","thondu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:155"},"p:835":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":835,"t":["சந்த னந்தி மிர்ந்த ணைந்து குங்கு மங்க டம்பி லங்கு","சண்ப கஞ்செ றிந்தி லங்கு ...... திரடோளுந்","தண்டை யஞ்சி லம்ப லம்ப வெண்டை யஞ்ச லன்ச லென்று","சஞ்சி தஞ்ச தங்கை கொஞ்ச ...... மயிலேறித்","திந்தி மிந்தி மிந்தி மிந்தி தந்த னந்த னந்த னென்று","சென்ற சைந்து கந்து வந்து ...... க்ருபையோடே","சிந்தை யங்கு லம்பு குந்து சந்த தம்பு கழ்ந்து ணர்ந்து","செம்ப தம்ப ணிந்தி ரென்று ...... மொழிவாயே","அந்த மந்தி கொண்டி லங்கை வெந்த ழிந்தி டும்ப கண்டன்","அங்க முங்கு லைந்த ரங்கொள் ...... பொடியாக","அம்ப கும்ப னுங்க லங்க வெஞ்சி னம்பு ரிந்து நின்று","அம்பு கொண்டு வென்ற கொண்டல் ...... மருகோனே","இந்து வுங்க ரந்தை தும்பை கொன்றை யுஞ்ச லம்பு னைந்தி","டும்ப ரன்ற னன்பில் வந்த ...... குமரேசா","இந்தி ரன்ப தம்பெ றண்டர் தம்ப யங்க டிந்த பின்பு","எண்க ணங்க மர்ந்தி ருந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks Murugan, his shoulders smeared with sandal and kumkum and wreathed with kadamba and champak, his anklets and bells ringing, to mount the peacock and come dancing to the drum's syllables, rejoicing and gracious, enter his mind and tell him: 'praise always, understand, and bow to the red feet'. The praise names Rama, whose monkey burned Lanka, who shattered the proud one's body to dust, shook Kumbhakarna and won with arrows, as uncle, Siva who wears the moon, karanthai, thumbai, konrai and the Ganga as father, and Murugan who settled at Enkan after freeing the gods from fear so Indra could regain his place.","n":"The plea asks not for rescue but for an instruction: செம்பதம் பணிந்திர் என்று மொழிவாயே, that Murugan himself tell the poet to praise and bow. The song chimes on nasal clusters (சந்தனம், திமிர்ந்து, அணிந்து), set to the drum's திந்திமி தந்தன.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvadi","guru","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:156"},"p:836":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":836,"t":["அயிலார் மைக்கடு விழியார் மட்டைகள்","அயலார் நத்திடு ...... விலைமாதர்","அணைமீ திற்றுயில் பொழுதே தெட்டிக","ளவரே வற்செய்து ...... தமியேனும்","மயலா கித்திரி வதுதா னற்றிட","மலமா யைக்குண ...... மதுமாற","மறையால் மிக்கருள் பெறவே யற்புத","மதுமா லைப்பத ...... மருள்வாயே","கயிலா யப்பதி யுடையா ருக்கொரு","பொருளே கட்டளை ...... யிடுவோனே","கடலோ டிப்புகு முதுசூர் பொட்டெழ","கதிர் வேல் விட்டிடு ...... திறலோனே","குயிலா லித்திடு பொழிலே சுற்றிய","குடவா யிற்பதி ...... யுறைவோனே","குறமா தைப்புணர் சதுரா வித்தக","குறையா மெய்த்தவர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of the price-women with spear-like kohl eyes whom strangers court, deceivers even as they lie on the bed, and asks that his wandering infatuation end, that mala, maya and the gunas change, and that by the Veda he gain grace and the wondrous feet sweet as a honey garland. The praise names Murugan who gave the meaning as a command to the lord of Kailasa, who threw the bright vel at old Sura hiding in the sea, the clever lover of the Kurava girl, at Kudavayil among groves where cuckoos sing.","n":"கயிலாயப்பதி உடையாருக்கு ஒரு பொருளே கட்டளை இடுவோனே: Murugan does not merely explain the meaning to Siva but lays it down as a command, the guru over the father. மறையால் மிக்கருள் பெறவே, grace through the Veda, is an unusual route for this poet, who more often asks grace through song or devotees; the phrase may also be read otherwise.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","mayal","malam","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:157"},"p:837":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":837,"t":["சுருதி யாயிய லாயியல் நீடிய","தொகுதி யாய்வெகு வாய்வெகு பாஷைகொள்","தொடர்பு மாயடி யாய்நடு வாய்மிகு ...... துணையாய்மேல்","துறவு மாயற மாய்நெறி யாய்மிகு","விரிவு மாய்விளை வாயருள் ஞானிகள்","சுகமு மாய்முகி லாய்மழை யாயெழு ...... சுடர்வீசும்","பருதி யாய்மதி யாய்நிறை தாரகை","பலவு மாய்வெளி யாயொளி யாயெழு","பகலி ராவிலை யாய்நிலை யாய்மிகு ...... பரமாகும்","பரம மாயையி னேர்மையை யாவரு","மறியொ ணாததை நீகுரு வாயிது","பகரு மாறுசெய் தாய்முதல் நாளுறு ...... பயனோதான்","கருது மாறிரு தோள்மயில் வேலிவை","கருதொ ணாவகை யோரர சாய்வரு","கவுணி யோர்குல வேதிய னாயுமை ...... கனபாரக்","களப புண்முலை யூறிய பாலுணு","மதலை யாய்மிகு பாடலின் மீறிய","கவிஞ னாய்விளை யாடிடம் வாதிகள் ...... கழுவேறக்","குருதி யாறெழ வீதியெ லாமலர்","நிறைவ தாய்விட நீறிட வேசெய்து","கொடிய மாறன்மெய் கூனிமி ராமுனை ...... குலையாவான்","குடிபு கீரென மாமது ராபுரி","யியலை யாரண வூரென நேர்செய்து","குடசை மாநகர் வாழ்வுற மேவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet marvels that Murugan, who is the Veda, grammar and the many languages, beginning and middle, renunciation, dharma and the path, the joy of the wise, cloud and rain, sun, moon and stars, space and light, beyond day and night, the supreme maya none can know, became his guru and made him speak of it, and wonders whether this is the fruit of some first day. The praise tells how Murugan came as the Kauniya brahmin child who drank Uma's milk, a poet beyond all singers, sent the disputants to the stake, spread holy ash, straightened the cruel Maran's hunch and made Madurai flourish, and dwells at Kudasai.","n":"The second half is the story of Sambandar told as Murugan's own: கவுணியர் குல வேதியன், the child who drank உமை's milk, the வாதிகள் impaled, the Pandya கூன் straightened, ash applied (நீறிட), all unmistakable details. நீ குருவாய் இது பகருமாறு செய்தாய் suggests the poet's own song is the guru's gift, a thanksgiving rather than a plea. The long list of what Murugan becomes is the pervasion the Tirumanthiram calls வியாபகம்.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["viyaabagam","guru","thiruneeru","thamizh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:157"},"p:838":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":838,"t":["தொடுத்த நாள்முதல் மருவிய இளைஞனும்","இருக்க வேறொரு பெயர்தம திடமது","துவட்சி யேபெறி லவருடன் மருவிடு ...... பொதுமாதர்","துவக்கி லேயடி படநறு மலரயன்","வதித்த தோதக வினையுறு தகவது","துறக்க நீறிட அரகர வேனவுள ...... மமையாதே","அடுத்த பேர்மனை துணைவியர் தமர்பொருள்","பெருத்த வாழ்விது சதமென மகிழ்வுறு","மசட்ட னாதுலன் அவமது தவிரநி ...... னடியாரோ","டமர்த்தி மாமலர் கொடுவழி படஎனை","யிருத்தி யேபர கதிபெற மயில்மிசை","யரத்த மாமணி யணிகழ லிணைதொழ ...... அருள்தாராய்","எடுத்த வேல்பிழை புகலரி தெனஎதிர்","விடுத்து ராவணன் மணிமுடி துணிபட","எதிர்த்து மோர்கணை விடல்தெரி கரதலன் ...... மருகோனே","எருக்கு மாலிகை குவளையி னறுமலர்","கடுக்கை மாலிகை பகிரதி சிறுபிறை","யெலுப்பு மாலிகை புனைசடி லவனருள் ...... புதல்வோனே","வடுத்த மாவென நிலைபெறு நிருதனை","அடக்க ஏழ்கட லெழுவரை துகளெழ","வடித்த வேல்விடு கரதல ம்ருகமத ...... புயவேளே","வனத்தில் வாழ்குற மகள்முலை முழுகிய","கடப்ப மாலிகை யணிபுய அமரர்கள்","மதித்த சேவக வலிவல நகருறை ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of common women who, though the young man who joined them from the first day is still there, go to another if they find more wealth with him, and of his own failure, caught in their snare and in deeds Brahma wrote, to wear ash and say 'Hara Hara'; he calls himself a poor fool who rejoices that house, wife, kin and wealth are permanent, and asks to be placed with the devotees offering flowers, to reach the higher state and worship the red-jewelled anklets of the feet on the peacock. The praise names Rama, who cut Ravana's crowns with one arrow, Siva of erukku, konrai, Ganga, crescent and bone garland, the vel that crushed Nirudan who stood as a mango tree, and the kadamba garland wet from Valli's breasts, at Valivalam.","n":"அடுத்த பேர் மனை துணைவியர் தமர் பொருள் பெருத்த வாழ்விது சதமென மகிழ்வுறும் அசட்டன்: the confession turns from the women to household attachment, taking house, wife, kin and wealth for permanent (சதம்). The opening lines on the woman and the young man (தொடுத்த நாள் முதல் மருவிய இளைஞனும் இருக்க) are hard to divide, and the reading of them is tentative.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","pattru","adiyaar","thiruneeru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:158"},"p:839":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":839,"t":["சூழும்வி னைக்கட் டுன்பநெ டும்பிணி ...... கழிகாமஞ்","சோரமி தற்குச் சிந்தைநி னைந்துறு ...... துணையாதே","ஏழையெ னித்துக் கங்களு டன்தின ...... முழல்வேனோ","ஏதம கற்றிச் செம்பத சிந்தனை ...... தருவாயே","ஆழிய டைத்துத் தங்கையி லங்கையை ...... யெழுநாளே","ஆண்மைசெ லுத்திக் கொண்டக ரும்புயல் ...... மருகோனே","வேழமு கற்கு தம்பியெ னுந்திரு ...... முருகோனே","வேதவ னத்தற் சங்கரர் தந்தருள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether he, a poor man who has not thought of the Lord as a companion in his karmic bond, his sorrow, long disease and wasting lust, must wander daily in griefs, and asks Murugan to remove his faults and give him thought of the red feet. The praise names the dark-cloud uncle who bridged the sea and conquered Lanka, the younger brother of the elephant-faced one, and the son given by Sankara of Vedavanam.","n":"சோரம் இதற்குச் சிந்தை நினைந்துறு துணையாதே: the failure confessed is not to have taken the Lord as companion (துணை) in distress. The line on Rama's seven days at Lanka is hard to read.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","idumbai","noy","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:159"},"p:840":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":840,"t":["சேலை யுடுத்துந டந்து மாலை யவிழ்த்துமு டிந்து","சீத வரிக்குழல் கிண்டி ...... யள§முசத்","தேனி லினிக்கமொ ழிந்து காமு கரைச்சிறை கொண்டு","தேச மனைத்தையும் வென்ற ...... விழிமானார்","மாலை மயக்கில்வி ழுந்து காம கலைக்குளு ளைந்து","மாலி லகப்பட நொந்து ...... திரிவேனோ","வால ரவிக்கிர ணங்க ளாமென வுற்றப தங்கள்","மாயை தொலைத்திட வுன்ற ...... னருள்தாராய்","பாலை வனத்தில்ந டந்து நீல அரக்கியை வென்று","பார மலைக்குள கன்று ...... கணையாலேழ்","பார மரத்திரள் மங்க வாலி யுரத்தையி டந்து","பால்வ ருணத்தலை வன்சொல் ...... வழியாலே","வேலை யடைத்துவ ரங்கள் சாடி யரக்கரி லங்கை","வீட ணருக்கருள் கொண்டல் ...... மருகோனே","மேவு திருத்தணி செந்தில் நீள்பழ நிக்குளு கந்து","வேத வனத்தில மர்ந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women who walk in fine sarees, loosen and tie their garlands, comb their hair, speak sweeter than honey, imprison lustful men and conquer every land with their eyes, and asks whether he must fall into their evening bewilderment, sink into the arts of love and wander in pain; he asks for the grace of feet like the young sun's rays that end maya. The praise tells Rama's story: the walk through the wilderness, the dark rakshasi defeated, seven trees pierced with one arrow, Vali's chest torn, the sea bridged at Varuna's word, Lanka destroyed and grace given to Vibhishana, Murugan's uncle, and names Tiruttani, Senthil, Palani and Vedavanam.","n":"மாலை மயக்கு can mean the bewilderment of the garland or of evening; both suit a street scene at dusk. The feet are likened to the rays of the young sun (வால ரவிக் கிரணங்கள்) that dispel maya, light against darkness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kaamam","mayal","maayai","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:159"},"p:841":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":841,"t":["நூலினை யொத்த மருங்குல் தேரினை யொத்த நிதம்பம்","நூபுர மொய்த்த பதங்கள் ...... இவையாலும்","நூறிசை பெற்ற பதங்கொள் மேருவை யொத்த தனங்கள்","நூல்வல்ம லர்ப்பொரு துண்டம் ...... அவையாலும்","சேலினை யொத்திடு கண்க ளாலும ழைத்திடு பெண்கள்","தேனிதழ் பற்றுமொ ரின்ப ...... வ¨ல்முழ்கிச்","சீலம னைத்து மொழிந்து காமவி தத்தி லழுந்தி","தேறுத வத்தை யிழந்து ...... திரிவேனோ","வாலஇ ளப்பிறை தும்பை யாறுக டுக்கை கரந்தை","வாசுகி யைப்புனை நம்பர் ...... தருசேயே","மாவலி யைச்சிறை மண்ட ஓரடி யொட்டிய ளந்து","வாளி பரப்பியி லங்கை ...... யரசானோன்","மேல்முடி பத்தும ரிந்து தோளிரு பத்தும ரிந்து","வீரமி குத்தமு குந்தன் ...... மருகோனே","மேவுதி ருத்தணி செந்தில் நீள்பழ நிக்குளு கந்து","வேதவ னத்தில மர்ந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet lists the women who lure with thread-thin waists, anklets, Meru-like breasts and fish eyes, and asks whether, plunging into the sea of pleasure on their honey lips, giving up all virtue and sunk in lust, he must wander having lost the tapas that brings clarity. The praise names Murugan the son of Siva who wears the crescent, thumbai, the river, konrai, karanthai and Vasuki, and the nephew of Mukunda, who measured Mahabali's world with a step and cut the ten heads and twenty arms of Lanka's king.","n":"சீலம் அனைத்தும் ஒழிந்து ... தேறு தவத்தை இழந்து: the loss named is of conduct and of tapas, not only of wealth or health. The closing two lines repeat 840's word for word.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kaamam","ozhukkam","thavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:159"},"p:842":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":842,"t":["நீலமுகி லானகுழ லானமட வார்கள்தன","நேயமதி லேதினமு ...... முழலாமல்","நீடுபுவி யாசைபொரு ளாசைமரு ளாகியலை","நீரிலுழல் மீனதென ...... முயலாமற்","காலனது நாவரவ வாயிலிடு தேரையென","காயமரு வாவிவிழ ...... அணுகாமுன்","காதலுட னோதுமுடி யார்களுட னாடியொரு","கால்முருக வேளெனவு ...... மருள்தாராய்","சோலைபரண் மீதுநிழ லாகதினை காவல்புரி","தோகைகுற மாதினுட ...... னுறவாடிச்","சோரனென நாடிவரு வார்கள்வன வேடர்விழ","சோதிகதிர் வேலுருவு ...... மயில்வீரா","கோல வழல் நீறுபுனை யாதிசரு வேசரொடு","கூடிவிளை யாடுமுமை ...... தருசேயே","கோடுமுக வானைபிற கானதுணை வாகுழகர்","கோடிநகர் மேவிவளர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks that he not wander daily in love of women with cloud-dark hair, nor strive like a fish in the waves out of greed for land and wealth, and that before his body falls into Death's tongue like a frog into a snake's mouth he be granted to join those who chant with love and say once 'Muruga Vel'. The praise tells how Murugan sported with the peacock-like Kurava girl guarding millet from her platform and drove the bright vel into the hunters who came searching for a thief, names him son of Uma who plays with the ash-smeared Lord and younger brother of the elephant-faced one, at Kodi of Kuzhagar.","n":"காலனது நா அரவ வாயில் இடு தேரையென: Death's tongue is a snake's mouth and the body a frog already in it, an image of death as already begun. புவியாசை பொருளாசை, the greed for land and for wealth, is named beside the women.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avaa","kootru","adiyaar"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:160"},"p:843":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":843,"t":["இரத்த முஞ்சியு மூளையெ லும்புட்","டசைப்ப சுங்குடல் நாடிபு னைந்திட்","டிருக்கு மண்சல வீடுபு குந்திட் ...... டதில்மேவி","இதத்து டன்புகல் சூதுமி குந்திட்","டகைத்தி டும்பொரு ளாசையெ னும்புட்","டெருட்ட வுந்தெளி யாதுப றந்திட் ...... டிடமாயா","பிரத்தம் வந்தடு வாதசு ரம்பித்","துளைப்பு டன்பல வாயுவு மிஞ்சிப்","பெலத்தை யுஞ்சில நாளுளொ டுங்கித் ...... தடிமேலாய்ப்","பிடித்தி டும்பல நாள்கொடு மந்திக்","குலத்தெ னும்படி கூனிய டங்கிப்","பிசக்கு வந்திடு போதுபி னஞ்சிச் ...... சடமாமோ","தரித்த னந்தன தானன தந்தத்","திமித்தி மிந்திமி தீதக் திந்தத்","தடுட்டு டுண்டுடு டூடுடி மிண்டிட் ...... டியல்தாளம்","தனத்த குந்தகு தானன தந்தக்","கொதித்து வந்திடு சூருடல் சிந்தச்","சலத்து டன்கிரி தூள்படெ றிந்திட் ...... டிடும்வேலா","சிரத்து டன்கர மேடுபொ ழிந்திட்","டிரைத்து வந்தம ரோர்கள் படிந்துச்","சிரத்தி னுங்கமழ் மாலைம ணம்பொற் ...... சரணோனே","செகத்தி னின்குரு வாகிய தந்தைக்","களித்தி டுங்குரு ஞானப்ர சங்கத்","திருப்பெ ருந்துறை மேவிய கந்தப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet pictures the body as a house of earth and water built of blood, fat, marrow, bone, flesh, guts and nerves, which the soul enters and lives in, flown off with deceit and the greed for wealth like a bird, then struck by fever, bile and winds, its strength shrinking until it goes on a stick, bent like the monkey clan, and asks whether, afraid when it finally slips, he will simply become a corpse. The praise, in drum syllables, shows the vel that scattered Sura and the hill, the gods bowing until their head-garlands scent the golden feet, and names Murugan the guru who gave the discourse of wisdom to his father, guru of the world, at Tirupperunthurai.","n":"மண்சல வீடு, a house of earth and water, makes the body a hut built of the elements; பொருளாசை எனும் புள் makes greed a bird that carries the soul off. சடமாமோ, 'shall I become a corpse?', ends the plea as a question rather than a prayer. செகத்தினின் குருவாகிய தந்தைக்கு அளித்திடும் குரு ஞானப் ப்ரசங்க: Murugan as guru to Siva, the world's guru.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","avaa","nilaiyaamai","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:161"},"p:844":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":844,"t":["வரித்தகுங் குமமணி முலைக்குரும் பையர்மன","மகிழ்ச்சிகொண் டிடஅதி ...... விதமான","வளைக்கரங் களினொடு வளைத்திதம் படவுடன்","மயக்கவந் ததிலறி ...... வழியாத","கருத்தழிந் திடஇரு கயற்கணும் புரள்தர","களிப்புடன் களிதரு ...... மடமாதர்","கருப்பெருங் கடலது கடக்கவுன் திருவடி","களைத்தருந் திருவுள ...... மினியாமோ","பொருப்பகம் பொடிபட அரக்கர்தம் பதியொடு","புகைப்பரந் தெரியெழ ...... விடும்வேலா","புகழ்ப்பெருங் கடவுளர் களித்திடும் படிபுவி","பொறுத்தமந் தரகிரி ...... கடலு¡டே","திரித்தகொண் டலுமொரு மறுப்பெறுஞ் சதுமுக","திருட்டியெண் கணன்முத ...... லடிபேணத்","திருக்குருந் தடியமர் குருத்வசங் கரரொடு","திருப்பெருந் துறையுறை ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of women with kumkum-painted breasts whose bangled arms embrace and whose fish eyes roll in delight until understanding is lost, and asks whether Murugan will now be willing to give his holy feet so that he may cross the great sea of birth. The praise names the vel that burned the hill and the demons' city, Vishnu who held Mandara in the sea, and the four-faced Brahma who worship the feet, and Sankara who sits as guru under the holy kurunda tree, with whom Murugan dwells at Tirupperunthurai.","n":"திருக்குருந்தடி அமர் குருத்வ சங்கரர்: Siva seated as guru under the kurunda tree at Perunthurai, the scene of Siva's teaching of Manikkavasagar there, though the song does not name the saint. Here the guru is Siva and Murugan dwells with him, a reversal of the songs where Murugan teaches Siva.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kaamam","piravi","thiruvadi","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:161"},"p:845":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":845,"t":["முகர வண்டெழுங் கருமுகி லலையவு","முதிய நஞ்சுமிழ்ந் தயில்விழி குவியவு","முகிள சந்திரன் பொருநுதல் வெயரவு ...... மமுதூறும்","முருகு தங்குசெந் துகிரிதழ் தெரியவு","மருவு சங்கநின் றொலிகொடு பதறவு","முழுது மன்புதந் தமளியி னுதவிய ...... அநுராகச்","சிகர கும்பகுங் குமபுள கிததன","மிருபு யம்புதைந் திடநடு விடைவெளி","தெரிய லின்றியொன் றிடவுயி ருயிருட ...... னுறமேவித்","திமிர கங்குலின் புதவிடு மவசர","நினைவு நெஞ்சினின் றறவவர் முகமது","தெரிச னஞ்செயும் பரிவற இனியருள் ...... புரிவாயே","மகர நின்றதெண் டிரைபொரு கனைகடல்","மறுகி யஞ்சிவந் தடிதொழு திடவொரு","வடிகொள் செஞ்சரந் தொடுபவ னிருபது ...... புயவீரன்","மடிய வங்குசென் றவனொரு பதுமுடி","முடிய முன்புமண் டமர்பொரு தமர்நிழல்","மதிலி லங்கையும் பொடிபட அருளரி ...... மருகோனே","நிகரி லண்டமெண் டிசைகளு மகிழ்வுற","விரகு கொண்டுநின் றழகுறு மயில்மிசை","நினைவி னுந்தியம் புவிதனை வலம்வரு ...... மிளையோனே","நிலவ ரும்புதண் டரளமு மிளிரொளிர்","பவள மும்பொரும் பழனமு மழகுற","நிழல்கு ருந்தமுஞ் செறிதுறை வளர்வுறு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes a night of lovemaking in detail, dark hair, poisoned eyes, sweating brow, coral lips, conch bangles crying out, breasts pressed until no space is left between them and life joins life, and asks for grace now so that the memory of that moment and the longing to see those faces leave his heart. The praise tells of Rama, before whom the sea came trembling to worship, who killed the twenty-armed hero and broke Lanka, names Murugan the young one who circles the earth on his peacock faster than thought, at Perunthurai among pearl-bright fields and kurundam shade.","n":"The plea asks not for escape from the women but for the end of the memory (நினைவு நெஞ்சினின்று அற) and of the longing to see their faces (முகமது தெரிசனம் செயும் பரிவற): the trouble is what the mind keeps. உயிர் உயிருடன் உற, life with life, is the poet's own phrase for the union.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","ninaivu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:161"},"p:846":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":846,"t":["மலைக் கனத்தென மார்பினி லேயிரு","முலைக் கனத்துற வேயிடை நூலென","வளைத்து குப்பமை யார்குழல் தோளொடும் ...... அலைமோத","மயிற் குலத்தவ ராமென நீள்கலை","நெகிழ்த்து வித்திரு வார்விழி வேல்கொடு","மயக்கி நத்தினர் மேல்மறு பாடும ...... விழியேவி","விலைக் கெனத்தன மாயிர மாயிர","முலைக்க ளப்பினு மாசைபொ தாதென","வெறுப்பர் குத்திர காரியர் வேசையர் ...... மயல்மேலாய்","வெடுக் கெடுத்தும காபிணி மேலிட","முடக்கி வெட்கு மதாமத வீணனை","மினற் பொலிப்பத மோடுற வேயருள் ...... புரிவாயே","அலைக் கடுத்தசு ரார்பதி கோவென","விடப் பணச்சிர மாயிர சேடனும்","அதிர்த்தி டக்கதிர் வேல்விடு சேவக ...... மயில்வீரா","அடைக் கலப்பொரு ளாமென நாயெனை","அழைத்து முத்திய தாமநு பூதியெ","னருட்டி ருப்புக ழோதுக வேல்மயி ...... லருள்வோனே","சிலைக்கை முப்புர நீறெழ வேதிரு","வுளத்தி லற்பமெ னாநினை தேசிகர்","சிறக்க முத்தமி ழாலொரு பாவக ...... மருள்பாலா","திருக் கடப்பலர் சூடிய வார்குழல்","குறத்தி கற்புட னேவிளை யாடியொர்","திருத் துருத்தியில் வாழ்முரு காசுரர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes the courtesans, heavy-breasted and thread-waisted, who loosen their garments and bewitch with spear eyes, then turn their glance against the men who trusted them, say that even thousands upon thousands of gold coins poured on their breasts are not enough, and cast men off; he calls himself a vain man whom a great disease has crippled and shamed and asks for the lightning-bright feet. The praise names Murugan who called him, a dog, as one taking refuge, gave him the experience of release and bade him recite the Thiruppugazh, the child who gave a poem in triple Tamil to the teacher who burned the three cities, and Valli's lover at Tirutturutti.","n":"அழைத்து முத்தியதாம் அநுபூதி என் அருள் திருப்புகழ் ஓதுக வேல் மயில் அருள்வோனே: the poet's own call, in which Murugan names the Thiruppugazh, appears here in the praise, as in 2. The line on the teacher (தேசிகர்) of the three cities and the poem in triple Tamil may refer to Siva or to a saint, and the reading is tentative. மகாபிணி after the courtesans is a disease caught from them.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","noy","pugazh","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:162"},"p:847":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":847,"t":["எருவாய் கருவாய் தனிலே யுருவா","யிதுவே பயிராய் ...... விளைவாகி","இவர்போ யவரா யவர்போ யிவரா","யிதுவே தொடர்பாய் ...... வெறிபோல","ஓருதா யிருதாய் பலகோ டியதா","யுடனே யவமா ...... யழியாதே","ஓருகால் முருகா பராம குமரா","உ யிர்கா வெனவோ ...... தருள்தாராய்","முருகா வெனவோர் தரமோ தடியார்","முடிமே லிணைதா ...... ளருள்வோனே","முநிவோ ரமரோர் முறையோ வெனவே","முதுசூ ருரமேல் ...... விடும்வேலா","திருமால் பிரமா வறியா தவர்சீர்","சிறுவா திருமால் ...... மருகோனே","செழுமா மதில்சே ரழகார் பொழில்சூழ்","திருவீ ழியில்வாழ் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet traces the round of birth, a form taking shape in the womb as from manure, growing as a crop to harvest, this one going and becoming that one and that one becoming this, a chain like madness, with one mother, two mothers and many crores of them, and asks not to perish in vain but to be given the grace to say once 'Muruga, supreme Kumara, save my life'. The praise names Murugan who sets his feet on the heads of devotees who say 'Muruga' once, who threw the vel at old Sura when the sages and gods cried for justice, the son of the one Vishnu and Brahma could not find, and nephew of Vishnu, at Thiruveezhi.","n":"ஒருதாய் இருதாய் பலகோடியதாய்: the mothers multiply with the births, a compact image of samsara. The plea and the praise turn on one word: the poet asks to say 'Muruga' once (ஒருகால்), and the praise says Murugan gives his feet to those who say it once (ஓர் தரம்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","nilaiyaamai","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:163"},"p:848":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":848,"t":["சொற்பிழைவ ராம லுனைக்கனக் கத்துதித்து","நிற்பதுவ ராத பவக்கடத் திற்சுழற்றி","சுக்கிலவ தார வழிக்கிணக் கிக்களித்து ...... விலைமாதர்","துப்பிறைய தான இதழ்க்கனிக் குக்கருத்தை","வைத்துமய லாகி மனத்தைவிட் டுக்கடுத்த","துற்சனமகாத கரைப்புவிக் குட்டழைத்த ...... நிதிமேவு","கற்பகஇ ராச னெனப்படைக் குப்பெருத்த","அர்ச்சுனந ராதி யெனக்கவிக் குட்பதித்து","கற்றறிவி னாவை யெடுத்தடுத் துப்படித்து ...... மிகையாகக்","கத்திடுமெ யாக வலிக்கலிப் பைத்தொலைத்து","கைப்பொருளி லாமை யெனைக்கலக் கப்படுத்து","கற்பனைவி டாம லலைத்திருக் கச்சலிக்க ...... விடலாமோ","எற்பணிய ராவை மிதுத்துவெட் டித்துவைத்து","பற்றியக ராவை யிழுத்துரக் கக்கிழித்து","எட்கரிப டாம லிதத்தபுத் திக்கதிக்கு ...... நிலையோதி","எத்தியப சாசின் முலைக்குடத் தைக்குடித்து","முற்றுயிரி லாம லடக்கிவிட் டுச்சிரித்த","யிற்கணையி ராமர் சுகித்திருக் கச்சினத்த ...... திறல்வீரா","வெற்பெனம தாணி நிறுத்துருக் கிச்சமைத்து","வர்க்கமணி யாக வடித்திருத் தித்தகட்டின்","மெய்க்குலம தாக மலைக்கமுத் தைப்பதித்து ...... வெகுகோடி","விட்கதிர தாக நிகர்த்தொளிக் கச்சிவத்த","ரத்தினப டாக மயிற்பரிக் குத்தரித்து","மிக்கதிரு வாவ டுநற்றுறைக் குட்செழித்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses that, failing to praise Murugan without a slip of speech, he whirled in the pot of birth, delighted in the way of the seed and set his mind on the coral lips of price-women, then went to evil, cruel men of the earth, set them in verse as a kalpaka king of wealth and an Arjuna in battle, and recited his learning loudly to end his poverty; he asks whether Murugan will let him be worn out by this fancy, empty-handed and confused. The praise tells of Krishna who trampled the hooded serpent, tore the crocodile that seized the elephant and drank the breast of the deceiving demoness until she died, and of Rama, and describes the peacock adorned with gold and gems, at Thiruvavaduthurai.","n":"The confession is double: the courtesans, and the court poet's trade, praising துற்சனர் (the wicked) as கற்பக ராசன் and அர்ச்சுனன் for money. The proper object of praise (உனைக் கனக்கத் துதித்து) is set against the false one. The praise of the peacock's jewelled trappings is hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["inmai","iravu","kaamam","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:164"},"p:849":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":849,"t":["கருத்தி தப்படு காமா லீலைகள்","விதத்தை நத்திய வீணா வீணிகள்","கவட்டு விற்பன மாயா வாதிகள் ...... பலகாலுங்","கரைத்து ரைத்திடு மோகா மோகிகள்","அளிக்கு லப்பதி கார்போ லோதிகள்","கடைக்க ணிற்சுழ லாயே பாழ்படு ...... வினையேனை","உ ரைத்த புத்திகள் கேளா நீசனை","யவத்த மெத்திய ஆசா பாசனை","யுளத்தில் மெயப்பொரு ளோரா மூடனை ...... யருளாகி","உ யர்ச்சி பெற்றிடு மேலா மூதுரை","யளிக்கு நற்பொரு ளாயே மாதவ","வுணர்ச்சி பெற்றிட வேநீ தாளிணை ...... யருள்வாயே","செருக்கி வெட்டிய தீயோ ராமெனு","மதத்த துட்டர்கள் மாசூ ராதிய","சினத்தர் பட்டிட வேவே லேவிய ...... முருகோனே","சிவத்தை யுற்றிடு தூயா தூயவர்","கதித்த முத்தமிழ் மாலா யோதிய","செழிப்பை நத்திய சீலா வீறிய ...... மயில்வீரா","வரைத்த வர்க்கரர் சூலா பாணிய","ரதிக்கு ணத்தரர் தீரா தீரர்த","மனத்தி யற்படு ஞானா தேசிக ...... வடிவேலா","வருக்கை யிற்கனி சாறாய் மேலிடு","தழைத்த செய்த்தலை யூடே பாய்தரு","மருத்து வக்குடி வாழ்வே தேவர்கள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of vain women who pursue the arts of lust, deceitful tricksters and sweet-talkers with cloud-dark hair, in whose sidelong glance he turned and was ruined, and calls himself a low man who will not hear wise counsel, full of craving and bonds, a fool who does not grasp the true thing; he asks for the feet so that he may gain the great realization that gives the good meaning of the lofty old sayings. The praise names the vel against Sura and the arrogant demons, Murugan who loves the triple-Tamil garland sung by the pure who reached Siva, the jnana guru in the mind of Siva the trident-bearer, at Maruthuvakkudi where jackfruit juice floods the fields.","n":"உரைத்த புத்திகள் கேளா நீசன் ... உளத்தில் மெய்ப்பொருள் ஓரா மூடன்: the poet blames himself for not heeding counsel, and asks for the feet so that the sense of the மூதுரை, the old sayings, may become his own experience (உணர்ச்சி). அரர் மனத்து இயல்படு ஞான தேசிக: Murugan as the jnana teacher in Siva's mind.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","pedhaimai","thiruvadi","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:165"},"p:850":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":850,"t":["இதசந்தன புழுகுஞ்சில மணமுந்தக வீசி","யணையுந்தன கிரிகொண்டிணை யழகும்பொறி சோர","இருளுங்குழல் மழையென்பந வரசங்கொளு மோகக் ...... குயில்பொலே","இடையுங்கொடி மதனன்தளை யிடுகுந்தள பார","இலையுஞ்சுழி தொடைரம்பையு மமுதந்தட மான","இயலங்கடி தடமும்பொழி மதவிஞ்சைகள் பேசித் ...... தெருமீதே","பதபங்கய மணையும்பரி புரமங்கொலி வீச","நடைகொண்டிடு மயிலென்பன கலையுஞ்சுழ லாட","பரிசும்பல மொழியுஞ்சில கிளிகொஞ்சுகை போலப் ...... பரிவாகிப்","பணமுண்டென தவலம்படு நினைவுண்டிடை சோர","இதுகண்டவர் மயல்கொண்டிட மனமுஞ்செயல் மாற","பகலுஞ்சில இரவுந்துயில் சிலவஞ்சகர் மாயைத் ...... துயர்தீராய்","திதிதிந்திமி தனதந்தன டுடுடுண்டுடு பேரி","டகுடங்குகு டிகுடிங்குகு படகந்துடி வீணை","செகணஞ்செக வெனவும்பறை திசையெங்கினு மோதக் ...... கொடுசூரர்","சிரமுங்கர வுடலும்பரி யிரதங்கரி யாளி","நிணமுங் குடல் தசையுங்கட லெனசெம்புன லோட","சிலசெம்புள்கள் கழுகுஞ் சிறு நரியுங்கொடி யாடப் ...... பொரும்வேலா","மதவெங்கய முரிகொண்டவர் மழுவுங்கலை பாணி","யிடமன்பொடு வளருஞ்சிவை புகழ்சுந்தரி யாதி","வளருந்தழ லொளிர்சம்பவி பரைவிண்டிள தோகைத் ...... தருசேயே","வதனஞ்சசி யமுதம்பொழி முலைநன்குற மாதொ","டிசையுஞ்சுரர் தருமங்கையொ டிதயங்களி கூர","வருபந்தணை நகர்வந்துறை விமலன்குரு நாதப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes the courtesans of the street, scented with sandal and civet, cloud-haired, cuckoo-voiced, walking like peacocks with anklets ringing and lisping like parrots, whose first thought is 'is there money?', who change the minds and deeds of those who see them, and asks Murugan to end the sorrow of the maya of these deceivers who sleep by day and some nights. The praise, in drum syllables, shows the battle with Sura's army and the birds and jackals feeding, names the goddess Sivai, Sundari, Sambhavi and Parai, Siva's left half, as his mother, and Murugan with Valli and Devayanai as the pure guru of Pandanai.","n":"பணம் உண்டென தவலம்படு நினைவு: the women's thought turns at once to money, the mark of the price-woman. The goddess's names (சிவை, சுந்தரி, சம்பவி, பரை) are a litany of Sakti; the song gives them to Murugan's mother.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","maayai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:166"},"p:851":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":851,"t":["இருவினை யஞ்ச வருவினை கெஞ்ச","இருள்பிணி துஞ்ச ...... மலமாய","எனதிடர் மங்க வுனதருள் பொங்க","இசைகொடு துங்க ...... புகழ்கூறித்","திருமுக சந்த்ர முருகக டம்ப","சிவசுத கந்த ...... குகவேல","சிவசிவ என்று தெளிவுறு நெஞ்சு","திகழந டஞ்செய் ...... கழல்தாராய்","மருதொடு கஞ்ச னுயிர்பலி கொண்டு","மகிழரி விண்டு ...... மருகோனே","வதைபுரி கின்ற நிசிசரர் குன்ற","வலம்வரு செம்பொன் ...... மயில்வீரா","அருகுறு மங்கை யொடுவிடை யுந்து","மமலனு கந்த ...... முருகோனே","அருள்செறி பந்த ணையிலிரு மங்கை","அமளிந லங்கொள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks that his two deeds fear, coming deeds plead, the dark disease sleep and mala and maya fade, that Murugan's grace swell, and that, singing his lofty praise and calling 'moon-faced Muruga, Kadamba, son of Siva, Kandha, Guha, Vela' and 'Siva Siva' with a clear heart, he be given the dancing anklets. The praise names the nephew of Vishnu who took the life of the marutha trees and Kamsa, the hero on the golden peacock who circles the demons' hill, the son loved by the pure Lord who rides the bull with his consort, and the lord of Pandanai with his two consorts.","n":"The plea is a string of names, a chant within the song; சிவசிவ என்று தெளிவுறு நெஞ்சு makes the clarity of heart come through repeating Siva's name. The first lines personify deeds and disease: past deeds fear, coming deeds beg.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","malam","pugazh","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:166"},"p:852":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":852,"t":["எகினி னம்பழி நாடக மாடிகள்","மயிலெ னுஞ்செய லாரகி நேரல்குல்","இசையி டுங்குர லார்கட னாளிகள் ...... வெகுமோகம்","எனவி ழுந்திடு வார்முலை மேல்துகில்","அலைய வுந்திரி வாரெவ ராயினும்","இளகு கண்சுழல் வார்விலை வேசியர் ...... வலைவீசும்","அதிக வஞ்சக பாவனை யால்மயல்","கொடுவி ழுந்திட ராகமு நோய்பிணி","யதிக முங்கொடு நாயடி யேனினி ...... யுழலாமல்","அமுத மந்திர ஞானொப தேசமும்","அருளி யன்புற வேமுரு காவென","அருள்பு குந்திட வேகழ லார்கழல் ...... அருள்வாயே","ககன விஞ்சையர் கோவென வேகுவ","டவுணர் சிந்திட வேகடல் தீவுகள்","கமற வெந்தழல் வேல்விடு சேவக ...... முருகோனே","கரிநெ டும்புலி தோலுடை யாரெனை","யடிமை கொண்டசு வாமிச தாசிவ","கடவு ளெந்தையர் பாகம்வி டாவுமை ...... யருள்பாலா","செகமு மண்டமு மோருரு வாய்நிறை","நெடிய அம்புயல் மேனிய னாரரி","தீருவு றைந்துள மார்பக னார்திரு ...... மருகோனே","தினைவ னந்தனில் வாழ்வளி நாயகி","வளர்த னம்புதை மார்பழ காமிகு","திலக பந்தணை மாநகர் மேவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes the price-women who shame swans in gait, play dramas, sing, run up debts, fall for anyone with great show of passion and cast the net of deceit, and asks that he, a dog of a servant who fell into infatuation and gained passion, disease and illness, wander no more but be given the nectar of mantra and jnana teaching and the anklets, so that grace enters when he calls 'Muruga' with love. The praise names the vel that burned seas and islands, Siva clothed in elephant and tiger skin who took the poet as his servant, Sadasiva whose side Uma never leaves, Vishnu who bears Lakshmi on his chest, and Valli's lord at Pandanai.","n":"அமுத மந்திர ஞானோபதேசமும் அருளி: the plea is for initiation, a mantra given as teaching. கரி நெடும் புலி தோல் உடையார் எனை அடிமை கொண்ட சுவாமி: the poet calls Siva the one who made him his servant, a rare first-person claim on the father rather than the son.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","noy","guru","mandhiram"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:166"},"p:853":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":853,"t":["கும்பமு நிகர்த்த கொங்கையை வளர்த்த","கொஞ்சுகிளி யொத்த ...... மொழிமானார்","குங்கும பணிக்குள் வண்புழுகு விட்ட","கொந்தளகம் வைத்த ...... மடவார்பால்","வம்புகள் விளைத்து நண்புகள் கொடுத்து","மங்கிநர கத்தில் ...... மெலியாமல்","வண்கயிலை சுற்றி வந்திடு பதத்தை","வந்தனைசெய் புத்தி ...... தருவாயே","பம்புநதி யுற்ற பங்கொருச மர்த்தி","பண்டுள தவத்தி ...... லருள்சேயே","பைம்புய லுடுத்த தண்டலை மிகுத்த","பந்தணை நகர்க்கு ...... ளுறைவோனே","சம்புநிழ லுக்குள் வந்தவ தரித்த","சங்கரர்த மக்கு ...... மிறையோனே","சங்கணி கரத்த ரும்பர்பய முற்ற","சஞ்சல மறுத்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of women with pot-like breasts and parrot speech, musk in their hair, with whom he stirs up mischief and gives friendship, and asks not to wither in hell but to be given the mind to worship the feet that came round Kailasa. The praise names Murugan son of the goddess who shares half of the river-bearer, born of her old tapas, dweller at cloud-wrapped Pandanai, lord even to Sankara who came down under the jambu tree's shade, who ended the fear of the gods.","n":"சம்பு நிழலுக்குள் வந்து அவதரித்த சங்கரர் points to Siva under the jambu tree, the form worshipped at Thiruvanaikka, and Murugan is called lord even to him. வண்கயிலை சுற்றி வந்திடு பதம may recall Murugan circling the world or Kailasa, but the allusion is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","naragam","thiruvadi","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:166"},"p:854":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":854,"t":["கெண்டைகள்பொ ருங்கண்மங் கையர்மலர்க்","கொண்டைகள்கு லுங்கநின் றருகினிற்","கெஞ்சுபலு டன்குழைந் தமளியிற் ...... கொடுபோய்வண்","கெந்தபொடி யும்புனைந் துறவணைத்","தின்பவச னந்தருந் தொழிலடுக்","கின்றமய லின்படுந் துயரறப் ...... ப்ரபைவீசுந்","தண்டைகள் கலின்கலின் கலினெனக்","கிண்கிணி கிணின்கிணின் கிணினெனத்","தண்கொலு சுடன்சிலம் பசையவுட் ...... பரிவாகிச்","சந்ததமும் வந்திரும் பரிமளப்","பங்கய பதங்களென் கொடுவினைச்","சஞ்சல மலங்கெடும் படியருட் ...... புரிவாயே","தொண்டர்கள் சரண்சரண் சரணெனக்","கொம்புகள் குகுங்குகுங் குகுமெனத்","துந்துமி திமிந்திமிந் திமினெனக் ...... குறுமோசை","சுந்தரி மணஞ்செயுஞ் சவுரியக்","கந்தகுற வஞ்சிதங் கருவனத்","துங்கமலை யும்புரந் தமரருக் ...... கிடர்கூரும்","பண்டர்கள்பு யங்களும் பொடிபடக்","கண்டவப்ர சண்டகுஞ் சரியெழிற்","பைந்தருவ னம்புரந் தகழெயிற் ...... புடைசூழும்","பந்திவரு மந்திசெண் பகமகிற்","சந்துசெறி கொன்றைதுன் றியவனப்","பந்தணையில் வந்திடுஞ் சரவணப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet tells how fish-eyed women with flowered hair coax him to bed, scent him with powder and embrace him with sweet words, and asks that the sorrow of that infatuation end through the fragrant lotus feet, with thandai, kinkini and anklets ringing, coming always to him so that his cruel deeds, anxiety and mala perish. The praise names Murugan to whom devotees cry 'refuge', who wed Devayanai and the Kurava girl, whose vel crushed the arms of the gods' enemies, and Saravana at Pandanai among monkeys, champak, agil, sandal and konrai.","n":"The ringing of the anklets is sung in sound (கலின் கலின், கிணின் கிணின்), the feet arriving audibly; the plea is that they come to him (வந்திரும்), not that he go to them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mayal","thiruvadi","malam","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:166"},"p:855":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":855,"t":["தேனி ருந்தஇத ழார்ப ளிங்குநகை","யார்கு ளிர்ந்தமொழி யார்ச ரங்கள்விழி","சீர்சி றந்தமுக வாரி ளம்பிறைய ...... தென்புரூவர்","தேன மர்ந்தகுழ லார்க ளங்கமுகி","னார்பு யங்கழையி னார்த னங்குவடு","சேர்சி வந்தவடி வார்து வண்டஇடை ...... புண்டா£கம்","சூனி யங்கொள்செய லார ரம்பைதொடை","யார்ச ரண்கமல நேரி ளம்பருவ","தோகை சந்தமணி வாரு டன்கலவி ...... யின்ப்முடே","சோக முண்டுவிளை யாடி னுங்கமல","பாத மும்புயமி ராறு மிந்துளபல்","தோட லங்கலணி மார்ப மும்பரிவு ...... ளங்கொள்வேனே","ஓந மந்தசிவ ரூபி யஞ்சுமுக","நீலி கண்டிகலி யாணி விந்துவொளி","யோசை தங்குமபி ராமி யம்பிகைப ...... யந்தவேளே","ஓல மொன்றவுணர் சேனை மங்கையர்கள்","சேறு டன்குருதி யோட எண்டிசையும்","ஓது கெந்தருவர் பாட நின்றுநட ...... னங்கொள்வேலா","ஏனல் மங்கைசுசி ஞான ரம்பையென","தாயி சந்த்ரமுக பாவை வஞ்சிகுற","மானொ டும்பர்தரு மான ணைந்தழகி ...... லங்குமார்பா","ஏர்க ரந்தையறு கோடு கொன்றைமதி","யாற ணிந்தசடை யார்வி ளங்குமெழில்","ஈறில் பந்தணைந லு¡ர மர்ந்துவளர் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet lists the beauty of women from honey lips and crystal smile to lotus feet, and says that even if he plays in sorrow in the pleasure of union with them, he will lovingly hold in his heart Murugan's lotus feet, his twelve arms and his chest with its garland. The praise names his mother as the Siva-form of 'Om Namah', five-faced, Neeli, Kalyani, the light of bindu and the dwelling of sound, Abhirami Ambika; Murugan whose vel made the demons' blood run while gandharvas sang, the lover of Valli and Devayanai, the lord of Pandanai where Siva wears karanthai, konrai, the moon and the river.","n":"The song does not ask to leave the women: விளையாடினும் ... பரிவுளங்கொள்வேனே, 'even if I play, I will hold you in mind', makes remembrance the vow, as in 153. The mother's names ஓநமந்த சிவரூபி ... விந்து ஒளி ஓசை தங்கும் make the goddess bindu and nada, the Tirumanthiram's terms.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["thiruvadi","kaamam","saththi","vindhu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:166"},"p:856":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":856,"t":["மதியஞ் சத்திரு நிறைந்த மாமுக","மயிலஞ் சக்கிளி யினங்க ளாமென","மதுரஞ் செப்பிய மடந்தை மேனகை ...... ரதிபோல","மருவும் பொற்குட மெழுந்த மாமுலை","வளர்வஞ் சிக்கொடி நடந்த வாறென","வருதுங் கக்கட லணங்கு போல்பவர் ...... தெருவூடே","நிதமிந் தப்படி யிருந்து வாறவர்","பொருள்தங் கப்பணி கலந்து போய்வர","நெறிதந் திட்டவர் வசங்க ளாமென ...... வுழலாதே","நிதிபொங் கப்பல தவங்க ளாலுனை","மொழியும் புத்திகள் தெரிந்து நானுனை","நிகர்சந் தத்தமிழ் சொரிந்து பாடவு ...... மருள்தாராய்","நதிமிஞ் சச்சடை விரிந்த நாயக","னுமையன் பிற்செயு மிகுந்த பூசனை","நலமென் றுட்குளிர் சிவன்ப ராபர ...... னருள்பாலா","நவகங் கைக்கிணை பகர்ந்த மாமணி","நதிபங் கிற்குலவு கந்து காபுரி","நகர்பொங் கித்த¨ழைய வந்து வாழ்வுறு ...... முருகோனே","கெதிதங் கத்தகு கணங்கள் வானவர்","அரிகஞ் சத்தவர் முகுந்தர் நாவலர்","கிளைபொங்கக் க்ருபை புரிந்து வாழ்கென ...... அருள்நாதா","கெருவம் பற்றிகல் விளைந்த சூரொடு","தளமஞ் சப்பொரு தெழுந்து தீயுகள்","கிரவுஞ் சக்கிரி வகிர்ந்த வேலுள ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of women with moon faces and parrot voices, like Menaka and Rati, like sea-goddesses, who stay daily in the street while men bring wealth and gold jewels and go, and asks not to wander under their sway but, with wealth abundant through many tapas, to know the wisdom of speaking of Murugan and to pour out rhythmic Tamil fit to sing him. The praise names Siva of the spreading matted hair who rejoiced in Uma's worship, the city that flourishes on a river equal to the nine Gangas, Murugan who blessed the ganas, gods, Hari, Brahma, Mukunda and the poets, and the vel that split Krauncha.","n":"நிகர் சந்தத் தமிழ் சொரிந்து பாட: the gift asked is the rhythmic Tamil of the Thiruppugazh itself. நதிபங்கில் ... கந்துகாபுரி, the name of the city by the river, is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["varaivinmagalir","pugazh","thamizh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:166"},"p:857":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":857,"t":["கொந்தார் மைக்குழ லிந்தார் சர்க்கரை","யென்றே செப்பிய ...... மொழிமாதர்","கொங்கார் முத்துவ டந்தா னிட்டத","னந்தா னித்தரை ...... மலைபோலே","வந்தே சுற்றிவ ளைந்தா லற்பம","னந்தா னிப்படி ...... யுழலாமல்","மங்கா நற்பொரு ளிந்தா அற்புதம்","என்றே யிப்படி ...... அருள்வாயே","இந்தோ டக்கதிர் கண்டோ டக்கட","மண்டா நற்றவர் ...... குடியோட","எங்கே யக்கிரி யெங்கே யிக்கிரி","யென்றே திக்கென ...... வருசூரைப்","பந்தா டித்தலை விண்டோ டக்களம்","வந்தோ ரைச்சில ...... ரணகாளிப்","பங்கா கத்தரு கந்தா மிக்கப","னந்தா ளுற்றருள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of women with clustered dark hair whose speech is called sugar, whose breasts under pearl strings rise like mountains, and asks that his petty mind not wander as they surround him, but that Murugan say to him 'here is the unfading good thing, the wonder'. The praise shows the moon and sun fleeing and the ascetics' homes emptied before Sura, who came asking 'where is that hill, where is this one', his head played like a ball by the vel, the battlefield given to the Kali of war, and Kandha of Panandal.","n":"மங்கா நற்பொருள் இந்தா அற்புதம் என்றே இப்படி அருள்வாயே: the plea imagines the Lord handing over the truth with a word, 'here, take it'. The praise gives a share of the field to ரணகாளி, the battle-goddess.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:167"},"p:858":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":858,"t":["அறுகுநுனி பனியனைய சிறியதுளி பெரியதொரு","ஆக மாகியோர் பால ரூபமாய்","அருமதலை குதலைமொழி தனிலுருகி யவருடைய","ஆயி தாதையார் மாய மோகமாய்","அருமையினி லருமையிட மொளுமொளென வுடல்வளர","ஆளு மேளமாய் வால ரூபமாய் ...... அவரொரு பெரியோராய்","அழகுபெறு நடையடைய கிறுதுபடு மொழிபழகி","ஆவி யாயவோர் தேவி மாருமாய்","விழுசுவரை யரிவவையர்கள் படுகுழியை நிலைமையென","வீடு வாசலாய் மாட கூடமாய்","அணுவளவு தவிடுமிக பிதிரவிட மனமிறுகி","ஆசை யாளராய் ஊசி வாசியாய் ...... அவியுறு சுடர்போலே","வெறுமிடிய னொருதவசி யமுதுபடை யெனுமளவில்","மேலை வீடுகேள் கீழை வீடுகேள்","திடுதிடென நுழைவதன்முன் எதிர்முடுகி யவர்களொடு","சீறி ஞாளிபோல் ஏறி வீழ்வதாய்","விரகினொடு வருபொருள்கள் சுவறியிட மொழியுமொரு","வீணி யார்சொலே மேல தாயிடா ...... விதிதனை நினையாதே","மினுகுமினு கெனுமுடல மறமுறுகி நெகிழ்வுறவும்","வீணர் சேவையே பூணு பாவியாய்","மறுமையுள தெனும வரை விடும்விழலை யதனின்வரு","வார்கள் போகுவார் காணு மோஎனா","விடுதுறவு பெரியவரை மறையவரை வெடுவெடென","மேள மேசொலா யாளி வாயராய் ...... மிடையுற வருநாளில்","வறுமைகளு முடுகிவர வுறுபொருளு நழுவசில","வாத மூதுகா மாலை சோகைநோய்","பெருவயிறு வயிறுவலி படுவன்வர இருவிழிகள்","பீளை சாறிடா ஈளை மேலிடா","வழவழென உ மிழுமது கொழகொழென ஒழுகிவிழ","வாடி யூனெலாம் நாடி பேதமாய் ...... மனையவள் மனம்வேறாய்","மறுகமனை யுறுமவர்கள் நணுகுநணு கெனுமளவில்","மாதர் சீயெனா வாலர் சீயெனா","கனவுதனி லிரதமொடு குதிரைவர நெடியசுடு","காடு வாவெனா வீடு போவெனா","வலதழிய விரகழிய வுரைகுழறி விழிசொருகி","வாயு மேலிடா ஆவி போகுநாள் ...... மனதர்கள் பலபேச","இறுதியதொ டறுதியென உ றவின்முறை கதறியழ","ஏழை மாதராள் மோதி மேல்விழா","எனதுடைமை யெனதடிமை யெனுமறிவு சிறிதுமற","ஈமொ லேலெனா வாயை ஆவெனா","இடுகுபறை சிறுபறைகள் திமிலையொடு தவிலறைய","ஈம தேசமே பேய்கள் சூழ்வதாய் ...... எரிதனி லிடும்வாழ்வே","இணையடிகள் பரவுமுன தடியவர்கள் பெறுவதுவும்","ஏசி டார்களோ பாச நாசனே","இருவினைமு மலமுமற இறவியொடு பிறவியற","ஏக போகமாய் நீயு நானுமாய்","இறுகும்வகை பரமசுக மதனையரு ளிடைமருதில்","ஏக நாயகா லோக நாயகா ...... இமையவர் பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet traces a life: a drop small as dew on a grass tip becoming a body and a child whose prattle melts its parents, growing into a man with wives, house and halls, a miser who would not let a crumb of husk fall, who sends a poor ascetic asking for food to the upper house and the lower and leaps at him like a dog, follows a vain woman's word, serves vain men, mocks those who say there is a next world and scolds renunciants and brahmins; then poverty and diseases come, his eyes run, phlegm rises, his wife's mind changes and women and children say 'chee', he dreams of chariot and horse and the burning ground, his speech fails and breath leaves while kin wail and drums beat at the cremation ground among ghosts, and he is put into the fire. Against this he asks Murugan, destroyer of bonds, to grant the supreme bliss in which the two deeds, the three malas, death and birth end and 'you and I' become one enjoyment, at Idaimarudur, lord of the world and the gods.","n":"The miser's sin is refusal, sending the begging ascetic from door to door (மேலை வீடு கேள் கீழை வீடு கேள்), and the denial of the next world (மறுமை உளதெனுமவரை ... வருவார்கள் போகுவார் காணுமோ எனா). The end is not escape to the feet but union: நீயும் நானுமாய் ஏக போகமாய், 'you and I as one enjoyment'. The line on what the devotees of the feet obtain (பெறுவதுவும் ஏசிடார்களோ) is hard to read.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","eegai","marumai","saayujjiyam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:168"},"p:859":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":859,"t":["இலகு குழைகிழிய வூடு போயுலவி","யடர வருமதன னூல ளாவியெதி","ரிளைஞ ருயிர்கவர ஆசை நேர்வலைபொ ...... திந்தநீலம்","இனிமை கரைபுரள வாகு லாவுசரி","நெறிவு கலகலென வாசம் வீசுகுழ","லிருளின் முகநிலவு கூர மாணுடைய ...... கன்றுபோக","மலையு மிதழ்பருகி வேடை தீரவுட","லிறுக இறுகியநு ராக போகமிக","வளரு மிளகுதன பார மீதினில்மு ...... யங்குவேனை","மதுர கவியடைவு பாடி வீடறிவு","முதிர அரியதமி ழோசை யாகவொளி","வசன முடையவழி பாடு சேருமருள் ...... தந்திடாதோ","கலக அசுரர்கிளை மாள மேருகிரி","தவிடு படவுதிர வோல வாரியலை","கதற வரியரவம் வாய்வி டாபசித ...... ணிந்தபோகக்","கலப மயிலின்மிசை யேறி வேதநெறி","பரவு மமரர்குடி யேற நாளும்விளை","கடிய கொடியவினை வீழ வேலைவிட ...... வந்தவாழ்வே","அலகை யுடனடம தாடு தாதைசெவி","நிறைய மவுனவுரை யாடு நீபஎழில்","அடவி தனிலுறையும் வேடர் பேதையைம ...... ணந்தகோவே","அமணர் கழுவில்விளை யாட வாதுபடை","கருது குமரகுரு நாத நீதியுள","தருளு மிடைமருதில் மேவு மாமுனிவர் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women whose eyes reach their earrings as nets for young men's lives, whose lips he drinks and whose breasts he embraces in growing passion, and asks whether Murugan will not grant him the grace to sing sweet poems in order, with knowledge of release ripening, in rare Tamil sound, joined to a worship of bright speech. The praise tells how the vel shattered Meru and the sea, how Murugan rode the peacock to restore the gods and destroy cruel deeds, spoke silent teaching into the ear of his father who dances with ghosts, married the hunter girl, and as Kumara guru sought debate so that the Jains played on the stakes, lord of the great sages of Idaimarudur.","n":"தாதை செவி நிறைய மவுன உரையாடு: Murugan fills his father's ear with a 'silent' teaching, the Om story without naming Om. அமணர் கழுவில் விளையாட வாது படை கருது குமரகுருநாத: the impaling of the Jains, the Sambandar story, given to Murugan as Kumaraguru.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","pugazh","thamizh","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:168"},"p:860":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":860,"t":["படியையள விடுநெடிய கொண்டலுஞ் சண்டனும்","தமரசது மறையமரர் சங்கமுஞ் சம்புவும்","பரவரிய நிருபன்விர கன்சுடுஞ் சம்பனன் ...... செம்பொன்மேனிப்","பரமனெழில் புனையுமர வங்களுங் கங்கையுந்","திருவளரு முளரியொடு திங்களுங் கொன்றையும்","பரியகுமி ழறுகுகன தும்பையுஞ் செம்பையுந் ...... துன்\u000eமுலச்","சடைமுடியி லணியுநல சங்கரன் கும்பிடுங்","குமரனறு முகவன்மது ரந்தருஞ் செஞ்சொலன்","சரவணையில் வருமுதலி கொந்தகன் கந்தனென் ...... றுய்ந்துபாடி","தணியவொலி புகலும்வித மொன்றிலுஞ் சென்றிலன்","பகிரவொரு தினையளவு பண்புகொண் டண்டிலன்","தவநெறியி லொழுகிவழி பண்படுங் கங்கணஞ் ...... சிந்தியாதோ","கடுகுபொடி தவிடுபட மந்திரந் தந்திரம்","பயிலவரு நிருதருட லம்பிளந் தம்பரங்","கதறிவெகு குருதிநதி பொங்கிடுஞ் சம்ப்ரமங் ...... கண்டுசேரக்","கழுகுநரி கொடிகருட னங்கெழுந் தெங்குநின்","றலகைபல திமிலைகொடு தந்தனந் தந்தனங்","கருதியிசை பொசியுநசை கண்டுகண் டின்புறுந் ...... துங்கவேலா","அடல்புனையு மிடைமருதில் வந்திணங் குங்குணம்","பெரியகுரு பரமகுமர சிந்துரஞ் சென்றடங்","கடவிதனி லுறைகுமரி சந்திலங் குந்தனந் ...... தங்குமார்பா","அருணமணி வெயிலிலகு தண்டையம் பங்கயங்","கருணைபொழி வனகழலி லந்தமுந் தம்பமென்","றழகுபெற நெறிவருடி யண்டருந் தொண்டுறுந் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses that he never once went to sing calmly 'Kumara, six-faced one of sweet red speech, the first who came in Saravana, Kandha', the one whom Sankara himself worships, Sankara whom Vishnu, Brahma, the Vedas and the gods cannot praise and who wears serpents, Ganga, moon, konrai and thumbai in his hair; that he never came near with the grace to share even a millet grain; and asks whether he will not think of the vow of walking in the way of tapas. The praise shows the vel splitting the demons skilled in mantra and tantra while vultures, foxes and ghosts feast and dance, names Murugan the great guru of Idaimarudur, lover of the forest maiden, whose lotus feet with ruby anklets, raining compassion, the gods stroke in service as their final refuge.","n":"The confession is of three omissions: not singing, not sharing (பகிர ஒரு தினையளவு), not keeping to tapas, so the remedy is the same as in 23, sharing even a millet grain. சங்கரன் கும்பிடும் குமரன்: Siva worships Murugan. The opening list of who cannot praise Siva is long and its division tentative.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","eegai","thavam","thondu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:168"},"p:861":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":861,"t":["புழுகொடுபனி நீர்ச வாதுட னிருகரமிகு மார்பி லேபன","புளகிதஅபி ராம பூஷித ...... கொங்கையானை","பொதுவினில்விலை கூறு மாதர்கள் மணியணிகுழை மீது தாவடி","பொருவனகணை போல்வி லோசன ...... வந்தியாலே","மெழுகெனவுரு காவ னார்தம திதயகலக மோடு மோகன","வெகுவிதபரி தாப வாதனை ...... கொண்டுநாயேன்","மிடைபடுமல மாயை யால்மிக கலவிய அறி வேக சாமிநின்","விதரணசிவ ஞான போதகம் ...... வந்துதாராய்","எழுகிரிநிலை யோட வாரிதி மொகுமொகுவென வீச மேதினி","யிடர்கெடஅசு ரேசர் சேனைமு ...... றிந்துபோக","இமையவர்சிறை மீள நாய்நரி கழுகுகள்கக ராசன் மேலிட","ரணமுககண பூத சேனைகள் ...... நின்றுலாவச்","செழுமதகரி நீல கோமள அபிநவமயி லேறு சேவக","செயசெயமுரு காகு காவளர் ...... கந்தவேளே","திரைபொருகரை மோது காவிரி வருபுனல்வயல் வாவி சூழ்தரு","திருவிடைமரு தூரில் மேவிய ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of the women who sell themselves in public with civet- and rose-water-scented breasts and arrow eyes, says that he, a dog who does not melt like wax, suffers confusion, delusion and anguish and is entangled in mala and maya, and asks Murugan to come and give his bountiful teaching of Siva-jnana. The praise shows the seven hills fleeing, the sea roaring, the demon army broken and the gods freed while dogs, jackals and vultures feast, names the hero on the elephant and the young peacock, 'victory, Muruga, Guha, Kandavel', and the lord of Idaimarudur where the Kaveri beats its banks.","n":"பொதுவினில் விலைகூறு மாதர்: women who name their price in public. மெழுகென உருகாவனார் turns the usual image round: he does not melt like wax for the Lord. சிவஞான போதகம், the teaching asked for, is also the name of Meykandar's Saiva Siddhanta text; whether the poet means the book is uncertain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","malam","gnaanam","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:168"},"p:862":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":862,"t":["தனுநுதல் வெயர்வெழ விழிகுழி தரவளை","சத்திக் கச்சில தித்திக் கப்படும் ...... அன்புபேசித்","தழுவிய மகளிர்த முகிழ்முலை யுரமிசை","தைக்கச் சர்க்கரை கைக்கப் பட்டன ...... தொண்டையூறல்","கனவிலு நுகர்தரு கலவியின் வலையிடை","கட்டுப் பட்டுயிர் தட்டுப் பட்டழி ...... கின்றதோதான்","கதிபெற விதியிலி மதியிலி யுனதிரு","கச்சுற் றச்சிறு செச்சைப் பத்மப ...... தம்பெறேனோ","முனைமலி குலிசைதன் ம்ருகமத புளகித","முத்தச் சித்ரத னத்துக் கிச்சித ...... அம்புராசி","முறையிட முதுநிசி சரர்திரள் முதுகிட","முட்டப் பொட்டெழ வெட்டிக் குத்தும ...... டங்கல்வீரா","அனுபவ மளிதரு நிகழ்தரு மொருபொருள்","அப்பர்க் கப்படி யொப்பித் தர்ச்சனை ...... கொண்டநாதா","அகிலமு மழியினு நிலைபெறு திரிபுவ","னத்துப் பொற்புறு சித்திச் சித்தர்கள் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes the women whose brows sweat and eyes sink as they speak sweet love and embrace, the lips' moisture sweeter than sugar, and says his life is caught and perishing in the net of a union he enjoys even in dreams; a man without fate or wit, he asks whether he will not gain the small red lotus feet that bring release. The praise names Devayanai, daughter of the thunderbolt-bearer, the sea crying out and the demons turning tail before the lion-like hero, Murugan who offered the one thing that gives experience to his father and received his worship, lord of the siddhas of Tribhuvanam who remain even when all the world perishes.","n":"கனவிலும் நுகர்தரு கலவி: the union is enjoyed even in dreams, desire outlasting waking. அனுபவம் அளிதரும் ஒரு பொருள் அப்பர்க்கு அப்படி ஒப்பித்து அர்ச்சனை கொண்ட நாதா: the teaching of Siva again, here as 'the one thing that gives experience', after which the father worships the son.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","thiruvadi","guru","siththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:169"},"p:863":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":863,"t":["இந்துகதிர்ச் சேரருணப் பந்திநடுத் தூணொளிபட்","டின்பரசப் பாலமுதச் ...... சுவைமேவு","எண்குணமுற் றோனடனச் சந்த்ரவொளிப் பீடகமுற்","றெந்தைநடித் தாடுமணிச் ...... சபையூடே","கந்தமெழுத் தோடுறுசிற் கெந்தமணப் பூவிதழைக்","கண்டுகளித் தேயமுதக் ...... கடல்மூழ்கிக்","கந்தமதித் தாயிரவெட் டண்டமதைக் கோல்புவனக்","கண்டமதைக் காணஎனக் ...... கருள்வாயே","திந்ததிமித் தீதகுடட் டுண்டுமிடட் டாடுடுடிட்","டிந்தமெனக் காளமணித் ...... தவிலோசை","சிந்தைதிகைத் தேழுகடற் பொங்கவரிச் சூர்மகுடச்","செண்டுகுலைத் தாடுமணிக் ...... கதிர்வேலா","குந்தியரித் தாழ்துளபச் செந்திருவைச் சேர்களபக்","கொண்டல்நிறத் தோன்மகளைத் ...... தரைமீதே","கும்பிடகைத் தாளமெடுத் தம்பொனுருப் பாவைபுகழ்க்","கும்பகொணத் தாறுமுகப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks for the grace to see, where the light of moon and sun strikes the central pillar and the sweet taste of nectar-milk flows, the Lord of eight qualities dancing on the moon-bright seat in the jewelled hall, to see and rejoice in the fragrant petal of the letters, bathe in the sea of nectar, and behold the thousand and eight worlds. The praise, in drum syllables, shows the vel scattering Sura's crown as the seven seas surge, and names Murugan whom the daughter of the cloud-hued Vishnu, the golden doll, worships with hand-cymbals and praises, the six-faced lord of Kumbakonam.","n":"The plea is a yogic vision in the Tirumanthiram's terms: moon and sun (இந்து, கதிர்), the central pillar (நடுத்தூண்), likely the central channel, the nectar, and the dance in the inner hall (மணிச்சபை). எண்குணமுற்றோன் is the Kural's எண்குணத்தான். கந்த எழுத்து, the fragrant letter, may mean the mystic syllable on a lotus petal, but the line is hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","amudham","thirukkoothu","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:170"},"p:864":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":864,"t":["தும்பிமுகத் தானைபணைக் கொம்பதெனத் தாவிமயற்","றொந்தமெனப் பாயுமுலைக் ...... கனமாதர்","தும்பிமலர்ச் சோலைமுகிற் கங்குலிருட் காரினிறத்","தொங்கல்மயிற் சாயலெனக் ...... குழல்மேவிச்","செம்பொனுருக் கானமொழிச் சங்கினொளிக் காமநகைச்","செங்கயலைப் போலும்விழிக் ...... கணையாலே","சிந்தைதகர்த் தாளுமிதச் சந்தரமுகப் பாவையர்தித்","திந்திதமனுற் றாடுமவர்க் ...... குழல்வேனோ","தம்பிவரச் சாதிதிருக் கொம்புவரக் கூடவனச்","சந்தமயிற் சாய்விலகிச் ...... சிறைபோகச்","சண்டர்முடித் தூள்கள்படச் சிந்தியரக் கோர்கள்விழத்","தங்கநிறத் தாள்சிறையைத் ...... தவிர்மாயோன்","கொம்புகுறிக் காளமடுத் திந்தமெனுற் றாடிநிரைக்","கொண்டுவளைத் தேமகிழச் ...... சுதனீண","கொஞ்சுசுகப் பாவையிணைக் கொங்கைதனிற் றாவிமகிழ்க்","கும்பகொணத் தாறுமுகப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether he must wander for the women whose breasts leap like the tusks of an elephant, whose hair is like a cloud-dark night and a peacock's plumes, whose speech is molten gold and whose fish eyes shoot arrows that shatter the mind. The praise tells of Vishnu: as Rama, with his brother and Sita, who was drawn off by the forest deer and imprisoned until he broke the demons' crowns and freed her; as Krishna, blowing the horn and dancing as he gathered the herds; and names Murugan who delights in the breasts of the parrot-like girl, six-faced lord of Kumbakonam.","n":"The plea is a single question; most of the song is the description and the praise of Vishnu's two avatars. The lines on Rama and the deer are hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","mayal"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:170"},"p:865":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":865,"t":["கெண்டைநெ ரொத்தவிழி மங்கைமோ கக்கலவை","கெந்தவா சப்புழுகு ...... மணநாறுங்","கிம்பு¡£ சக்களப கொங்கையா னைச்சிறிது","கிஞ்சுகா ணப்பெருகி ...... யடியேனும்","மண்டிமோ சக்கலவி கொண்டுகா மித்துருகி","வண்டனா கப்புவியி ...... லுழலாமல்","வந்துஞா னப்பொருளி லொன்றுபோ தித்துனது","மஞ்சுதா ளைத்தினமு ...... மருள்வாயே","அண்டர்வா ழப்பிரபை சண்டமே ருக்கிரியி","ளைந்துவீ ழப்பொருத ...... கதிர்வேலா","அஞ்சுவா யிற்பரனை நெஞ்சிலு¡ றித்தவசி","னன்புளா ரைச்சிறையி ...... டசுரோரைக்","கொண்டுபோய் வைத்தகழு நெஞ்சிலே றக்கழுகு","கொந்தியா டத்தலையை ...... யரிவோனே","கொண்டல்சூ ழக்கழனி சங்குலா விப்பரவு","கும்பகோ ணத்திலுறை ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet tells how at a glimpse of a woman's scented breasts he swells with desire, sinks into deceptive union and melts as a rogue, and asks not to wander thus but for Murugan to come, teach him the one thing in the meaning of jnana and give his fair feet daily. The praise names the vel that shattered Meru so the gods might live, and Murugan who cut the heads of those who imprisoned the loving ascetics of the Lord of the five gates, sending them to the stake while vultures pecked, lord of Kumbakonam among fields where conches crawl.","n":"The praise seems to tell of enemies who harmed devotees of Siva being impaled, which recalls the Jains of the Sambandar story, though here they are called அசுரோர்; அஞ்சுவாயில் பரன், the Lord of the five gates, may mean the Lord reached through the five senses or the five letters. The reading is tentative.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["kaamam","punarchi","gnaanam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:170"},"p:866":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":866,"t":["பஞ்சுசேர் நிர்த்தப் ...... பதமாதர்","பங்கமார் தொக்கிற் ...... படியாமற்","செஞ்சொல்சேர் சித்ரத் ...... தமிழாலுன்","செம்பொனார் வத்தைப் ...... பெறுவேனோ","பஞ்சபா ணத்தற் ...... பொருதேவர்","பங்கில்வாழ் சத்திக் ...... குமரேசா","குஞ்சா£ வெற்புத் ...... தனநேயா","கும்பகோ ணத்திற் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A short song: the poet asks whether, instead of sinking into the foul skin of dancing women with cotton-soft feet, he will gain Murugan's golden love through colourful Tamil of fine words. The praise names Murugan son of the Sakti who lives at the side of the god who fought Kama of the five arrows, lover of Devayanai's breasts, lord of Kumbakonam.","n":"பங்கமார் தொக்கு, the soiled skin, reduces the women to their surface; the remedy is செஞ்சொல் சேர் சித்ரத் தமிழ், the poet's own art.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kaamam","thamizh","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:170"},"p:867":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":867,"t":["மாலைதனில் வந்து வீதிதனில் நின்று","வாசமலர் சிந்து ...... குழல்கோதி","வாரிருத னங்கள் பூணொடுகு லுங்க","மால்பெருகி நின்ற ...... மடவாரைச்","சாலைவழி வந்து போமவர்க ணின்று","தாழ்குழல்கள் கண்டு ...... தடுமாறித்","தாகமயல் கொண்டு மாலிருள ழுந்தி","சாலமிக நொந்து ...... தவியாமற்","காலையிலெ ழுந்து னாமமெமொ ழுந்தி","காதலுமை மைந்த ...... எனவோதிக்","காலமுமு ணர்ந்து ஞானவெளி கண்கள்","காண அரு ளென்று ...... பெறுவேனோ","கோலமுட னன்று சூர்படையின் முன்பு","கோபமுட னின்ற ...... குமரேசா","கோதையிரு பங்கின் மேவவளர் கும்ப","கோணநகர் வந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women who stand in the street at evening combing their scented hair, breasts and ornaments swaying, so that those passing on the road stagger at the sight, and asks that instead of suffering in thirst and dark delusion he may rise at dawn, recite Murugan's names, calling him son of loving Uma, know the right times and gain the grace for his eyes to see the space of knowledge. The praise names Kumaresa who stood in anger before Sura's army and the lord of Kumbakonam with a consort on either side.","n":"The evening street and the morning worship are set against each other: மாலைதனில் ... காலையில் எழுந்து. ஞானவெளி கண்கள் காண, to see with the eyes the open space of knowledge, is the goal of the daily discipline.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mayal","vazhipaadu","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:170"},"p:868":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":868,"t":["கறுத்த குஞ்சியும் வெளிறி யெழுங்கொத்","துருத்த வெண்பலு மடைய விழுந்துட்","கருத்து டன்திகழ் மதியு மருண்டுச் ...... சுருள்நோயாற்","கலக்க முண்டல மலமுற வெண்டிப்","பழுத்தெ ழும்பிய முதுகு முடங்கக்","கழுத்தில் வந்திளை யிரும லொதுங்கக் ...... கொழுமேனி","அறத்தி ரங்கியொர் தடிகை நடுங்கப்","பிடித்தி டும்புறு மனைவியு நிந்தித்","தடுத்த மைந்தரும் வசைகள் விளம்பச் ...... சடமாகி","அழுக்க டைந்திடர் படுமுடல் பங்கப்","பிறப்பெ னுங்கட லழிய லொழிந்திட்","டடுத்தி ருந்திரு வடிதனை யென்றுற் ...... றிடுவேனோ","புறத்த லம்பொடி படமிக வுங்கட்","டறப்பெ ருங்கடல் வயிறு குழம்பப்","புகட்ட ரங்கிய விரக துரங்கத் ...... திறல்வீரா","பொருப்பு ரம்படர் கிழிபட வென்றட்","டரக்கர் வன்றலை நெரிய நெருங்கிப்","புதைக்கு றுந்தசை குருதிகள் பொங்கப் ...... பொரும்வேலா","சிறுத்த தண்டைய மதலையொ ரஞ்சச்","சினத்து மிஞ்சரி திரிதரு குன்றத்","தினைப்பு னந்திகழ் குறமகள் கொங்கைக் ...... கிரிமேவிச்","செருக்கு நெஞ்சுடை முருக சிகண்டிப்","பரிச்சு மந்திடு குமர கடம்பத்","திருக்கு டந்தையி லுறைதரு கந்தப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet pictures old age, black hair whitening, teeth falling, the mind clouded, dizziness and disease, the back bent, cough in the throat, the body shrunk and a stick shaking in the hand, the wife reviling him and the sons abusing him until he is a corpse, and asks when, the sea of birth of this filthy suffering body ended, he will reach the holy feet. The praise names the hero of the peacock-steed who churned the great sea, the vel that crushed the demons, Murugan proud on the breasts of the Kurava girl of the millet field, rider of the peacock, Kandha of Kudanthai.","n":"The ageing is told through the household's scorn as much as the body: மனைவியும் நிந்தித்து அடுத்த மைந்தரும் வசைகள் விளம்ப. The praise line on the peacock as a steed (விரக துரங்கம்) is tentative.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","ilamai","piravi","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:170"},"p:869":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":869,"t":["செனித்தி டுஞ்சல சாழலு மூழலும்","விளைத்தி டுங்குடல் பீறியு மீறிய","செருக்கொ டுஞ்சதை பீளையு மீளையு ...... முடலு¡டே","தெளித்தி டும்பல சாதியும் வாதியும்","இரைத்தி டுங்குல மேசில கால்படர்","சினத்தி டும்பவ நோயென வேயிதை ...... யனைவோருங்","கனைத்தி டுங்கலி காலமி தோவென","வெடுத்தி டுஞ்சுடு காடுபு காவென","கவிழ்த்தி டுஞ்சட மோபொடி யாய்விடு ...... முடல்பேணிக்","கடுக்க னுஞ்சில பூடண மாடைகள்","இருக்கி டுங்கலை யேபல வாசைகள்","கழித்தி டுஞ்சிவ யோகமு ஞானமு ...... மருள்வாயே","தனத்த னந்தன தானன தானன","திமித்தி திந்திமி தீதக தோதக","தகுத்து துந்துமி தாரைவி ராணமொ ...... டடல்பேரி","சமர்த்த மொன்றிய தானவர் சேனையை","வளைத்து வெஞ்சின வேல்விடு சேவக","சமத்து ணர்ந்திடு மாதவர் பாலருள் ...... புரிவோனே","தினைப்பு னந்தனி லேமய லாலொரு","மயிற்ப தந்தனி லேசர ணானென","திருப்பு யந்தரு மோகன மானினை ...... யணைவோனே","சிவக்கொ ழுஞ்சுட ரேபர னாகிய","தவத்தில் வந்தருள் பாலக்ரு பாகர","திருக்கு டந்தையில் வாழ்முரு காசுரர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of the body born of water and phlegm, guts, flesh, rheum and cough, over which castes and disputants clamour about lineage, which all say is a disease of birth, of which they cry 'this is the Kali age' and 'take it to the burning ground', and which falls to dust, and asks that, instead of cherishing it with earrings, ornaments, clothes, arts and many desires, he be given the Sivayoga and jnana that remove them. The praise, in drum syllables, shows the vel against the Danava army, names Murugan who graces the great ascetics who know equanimity, who took refuge at the feet of the peacock-like girl in the millet field, the child who came through the tapas of Siva's bright flame, at Kudanthai.","n":"பல சாதியும் வாதியும் இரைத்திடும் குலமே may say that the castes and sects quarrel over a body that is only filth, a rare social note, but the line is hard to read. சமத்து உணர்ந்திடு மாதவர், ascetics who know equanimity, are the model held up.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["yaakkai","nilaiyaamai","yogam","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:170"},"p:870":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":870,"t":["கரியகுழல் சரியமுகம் வேர்வாட வாசமுறு","களபமுலை புளகமெழ நேரான வேல்விழிகள்","கயல்பொருது செயலதென நீள்பூச லாடநல ...... கனிவாயின்","கமழ்குமுத அதரவிதழ் தேனூறல் பாயமிகு","கடலமுத முதவியிரு தோள்மாலை தாழவளை","கலகலென மொழிபதற மாமோக காதலது ...... கரைகாணா","தெரியணுகு மெழுகுபத மாய்மேவி மேவியிணை","யிருவருட லொருவரென நாணாது பாயல்மிசை","யிளமகளிர் கலவிதனி §ல்முழ்கி யாழுகினு ...... மிமையாதே","இரவினிடை துயிலுகினும் யாரோடு பேசுகினும்","இளமையுமு னழகுபுனை யீராறு தோள்நிரையும்","இருபதமு மறுமுகமும் யானோத ஞானமதை ...... யருள்வாயே","உ ரியதவ நெறியில்நம நாராய ணாயவென","ஒருமதலை மொழியளவி லோராத கோபமுட","னுனதிறைவ னெதனிலுள னோதாய டாவெனுமு ...... னுறுதூணில்","உ ரமுடைய அரிவடிவ தாய்மோதி வீழவிரல்","உ கிர்புதைய இரணியனை மார்பீறி வாகைபுனை","உ வணபதி நெடியவனும் வேதாவும் நான்மறையு ...... முயர்வாக","வரியளிக ளிசைமுரல வாகான தோகையிள","மயிலிடையில் நடனமிட ஆகாச மூடுருவ","வளர்கமுகின் விரிகுலைகள் பூணார மாகியிட ...... மதில்சூழும்","மருதரசர் படைவிடுதி வீடாக நாடிமிக","மழவிடையின் மிசையிவரு சோமீசர் கோயில்தனில்","மகிழ்வுபெற வுறைமுருக னேபேணு வானவர்கள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes lovemaking in detail, hair loosened, face sweating, breasts thrilling, lips like nectar, bangles ringing, two bodies made one without shame, and asks that even while he sinks in union with young women, without a blink, while he sleeps at night or talks with anyone, he be given the knowledge to recite Murugan's youth, beauty, twelve arms, two feet and six faces. The praise tells how, when a child chanted 'Nama Narayanaya' and his father raged 'where is your god?', Vishnu burst from the pillar as a lion and tore Hiranya's chest with his claws, and names Murugan who dwells gladly in the Somesar temple among dancing peacocks and areca groves, by the camp of the Marutha kings.","n":"As in 855, the plea is not to stop but to remember in every state: in union, in sleep, in speech (கலவிதனில் மூழ்கி ஆழுகினும் இமையாதே இரவினிடை துயிலுகினும் யாரோடு பேசுகினும்). Prahlada is unnamed but the story is unmistakable: the child's நமநாராயணாய, the father's question, the pillar and the claws. மருதரசர் படைவிடுதி, the camp of the Marutha kings, is an allusion not identified here.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["punarchi","thiruvadi","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:171"},"p:871":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":871,"t":["பட்டுமணிக் கச்சிருகக் கட்டியவிழ்த் துத்தரியப்","பத்தியின்முத் துச்செறிவெற் ...... பிணையாமென்","பற்பமுகைக் குத்துமுலைத் தத்தையர்கைப் புக்குவசப்","பட்டுருகிக் கெட்டவினைத் ...... தொழிலாலே","துட்டனெனக் கட்டனெனப் பித்தனெனப் ப்ரட்டனெனச்","சுற்றுமறச் சித்தனெனத் ...... திரிவேனைத்","துக்கமறுத் துக்கமலப் பொற்பதம்வைத் துப்பதவிச்","சுத்தியணைப் பத்தரில்வைத் ...... தருள்வாயே","சுட்டபொருட் கட்டியின்மெய்ச் செக்கமலப் பொற்கொடியைத்","துக்கமுறச் சொர்க்கமுறக் ...... கொடியாழார்","சுத்தரதத் திற்கொடுபுக் குக்கடுகித் தெற்கடைசிச்","சுற்றுவனத் திற்சிறைவைத் ...... திடுதீரன்","கொட்டமறப் புற்றரவச் செற்றமறச் சத்தமறக்","குற்றமறச் சுற்றமறப் ...... பலதோளின்","கொற்றமறப் பத்துமுடிக் கொத்துமறுத் திட்டதிறற்","கொற்றர்பணிக் கொட்டைநகர்ப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet tells how, falling into the hands of parrot-like women whose silk bodices loosen over pearl strings, he melted under their sway and by ruined deeds wanders called wicked, troublesome, mad, fallen and cruel-minded, and asks Murugan to cut his sorrow, set his golden lotus feet on him and place him among the devotees of the purified state. The praise tells how Ravana carried the golden creeper of the lotus off in his chariot to a grove in the far south and imprisoned her, and how the victor cut his ten crowned heads and many arms, ending his pride, and names the lord of Kottaiyur whom the victorious worship.","n":"துட்டனெனக் கட்டனெனப் பித்தனெனப் ப்ரட்டனென: the names are what others call him, a public shame. The praise piles up அற ('cut off, ended') through the line on Ravana, a sound-play. பதவிச் சுத்தி அணைப் பத்தர் is hard to divide.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kutram","adiyaar","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:172"},"p:872":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":872,"t":["மனமெ னும்பொருள் வானறை கால்கனல்","புனலு டன்புவி கூடிய தோருடல்","வடிவு கொண்டதி லேபதி மூணெழு ...... வகையாலே","வருசு கந்துய ராசையி லேயுழல்","மதியை வென்றுப ராபர ஞானநல்","வழிபெ றும்படி நாயடி யேனைநி ...... னருள்சேராய்","செனனி சங்கரி ஆரணி நாரணி","விமலி யெண்குண பூரணி காரணி","சிவைப ரம்பரை யாகிய பார்வதி ...... அருள்பாலா","சிறைபு குஞ்சுரர் மாதவர் மேல்பெற","அசுரர் தங்கிளை யானது வேரற","சிவனு கந்தருள் கூர்தரு வேல்விடு ...... முருகோனே","கனக னங்கையி னாலறை தூணிடை","மனித சிங்கம் தாய்வரை பார்திசை","கடல்க லங்கிட வேபொரு தேயுகிர் ...... முனையாலே","கதற வென்றுடல் கீணவ னாருயி","ருதிர முஞ்சித றாதமு தாயுணு","கமல வுந்திய னாகிய மால்திரு ...... மருகோனே","தினக ரன்சிலை வேளருள் மாதவர்","சுரர்க ளிந்திர னாருர காதிபர்","திசைமு கன்செழு மாமறை யோர்புக ...... ழழகோனே","திரும டந்தையர் நாலிரு வோர்நிறை","அகமொ டம்பொனி னாலய நீடிய","சிவபு ரந்தனில் வாழ்குரு நாயக ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of the one body made of mind and the five elements, in which he wanders in pleasure, sorrow and desire, and asks Murugan to conquer that mind and join him, a dog of a servant, to grace so that he gains the good path of supreme knowledge. The praise calls Murugan the son of Parvati, named as Janani, Sankari, Narani, the spotless one full of the eight qualities, the cause, Siva's consort; the vel that freed the imprisoned gods and uprooted the asuras; the nephew of Vishnu who came as the Man-Lion from the pillar Hiranya struck and drank his blood; and the lord praised by gods and sages at Sivapuram, whose houses are filled with gold by the eight Lakshmis.","n":"மனமெனும் பொருள் ... கூடியதோர் உடல்: mind is counted with sky, wind, fire, water and earth as what makes up the body, and the plea is to conquer the mind (மதியை வென்று), not the body. பதி மூணெழு வகையாலே is obscure; it may count kinds of birth or constituents. The Narasimha story is told in its detail (the pillar, the claws, the blood drunk like nectar), more fully than usual. திருமடந்தையர் நாலிருவோர் are the eight Lakshmis.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["manam","gnaanam","thiruvarul","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:173"},"p:873":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":873,"t":["ஆசார வீனக் குதர்க்க துட்டர்கள்","மாதாபி தாவைப் பழித்த துட்டர்கள்","ஆமாவி னூனைச் செகுத்த துட்டர்கள் ...... பரதாரம்","ஆகாதே னாமற் பொசித்த துட்டர்கள்","நானாவு பாயச் சரித்ர துட்டர்கள்","ஆவேச நீரைக் குடித்த துட்டர்கள் ...... தமியோர்சொங்","கூசாது சேரப் பறித்த துட்டர்கள்","ஊரார்க ளாசைப் பிதற்று துட்டர்கள்","கோலால வாள்விற் செருக்கு துட்டர்கள் ...... குருசேவை","கூடாத பாவத் தவத்த துட்டர்கள்","ஈயாது தேடிப் புதைத்த துட்டர்கள்","கோமாள நாயிற் கடைப்பி றப்பினி ...... லுழல்வாரே","வீசாவி சாலப் பொருப்பெ டுத்தெறி","பேரார வாரச் சமுத்தி ரத்தினில்","மீளாம லோடித் துரத்தி யுட்குறு ...... மொருமாவை","வேரோடு வீழத் தறித்த டுக்கிய","போராடு சாமர்த் தியத்தி ருக்கையில்","வேலாயு தாமெய்த் திருப்பு கழ்ப்பெறு ...... வயலு¡ரா","நாசாதி ப்ராரத் ததுக்க மிக்கவர்","மாயாவி காரத் தியக்க றுத்தருள்","ஞானோப தேசப் ப்ரசித்த சற்குரு ...... வடிவான","நாதாவெ னாமுற் றுதித்தி டப்புவி","யாதார மாய்கைக் குமுட்ட முற்றருள்","நாகேச நாமத் தகப்பன் மெச்சிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"Without any plea of his own, the poet lists the wicked who will wander in the lowest births like dogs: those who have no conduct and argue perversely, revile mother and father, kill the cow for its flesh, enjoy other men's wives without a qualm, live by every trick, drink intoxicating liquor, snatch the goods of the helpless without shame, babble their desire for what belongs to others, swagger with club, sword and bow, do sinful austerities without serving a guru, and hoard and bury wealth without giving. The praise names the vel that felled the one mango in the roaring sea, Murugan of Vayalur who received the true Thiruppugazh, the famed true guru whose teaching cuts the delusion of maya for those suffering from past deeds, praised by his father named Nagesa.","n":"The song is a list of துட்டர்கள் rather than a confession, close to a moral code: the faults named are those the Kural also names (other men's wives, liquor, theft from the helpless, hoarding), with refusal of guru-service (குருசேவை கூடாத) among them. கோமாள நாயிற் கடைப்பிறப்பு: the punishment is birth as the lowest of dogs. மெய்த் திருப்புகழ் பெறு வயலூரா recalls Vayalur as the place of the Thiruppugazh itself. நாகேச நாமத் தகப்பன் is Siva of this shrine. The lines on ப்ராரத்த துக்கம் and புவி ஆதாரமாய்கைக்கு are hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["piravi","piranil","kal","ivaral"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:174"},"p:874":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":874,"t":["தரையினில் வெகுவழி சார்ந்த மூடனை","வெறியனை நிறைபொறை வேண்டி டாமத","சடலனை மகிமைகள் தாழ்ந்த வீணணை ...... மீகுகேள்வி","தவநெறி தனைவிடு தாண்டு காலியை","யவமதி யதனில்பொ லாங்கு தீமைசெய்","சமடனை வலியஅ சாங்க மாகிய ...... தமியேனை","விரைசெறி குழலியர் வீம்பு நாரியர்","மதிமுக வனிதையர் வாஞ்சை மோகியர்","விழிவலை மகளிரொ டாங்கு கூடிய ...... வினையேனை","வெகுமல ரதுகொடு வேண்டி யாகிலு","மொருமல ரிலைகொடு மோர்ந்து யானுனை","விதமுறு பரிவொடு வீழ்ந்து தாடொழ ...... அருள்வாயே","ஒருபது சிரமிசை போந்த ராவண","னிருபது புயமுட னேந்து மேதியு","மொருகணை தனிலற வாங்கு மாயவன் ...... மருகோனே","உ னதடி யவர்புக ழாய்ந்த நூலின","ரமரர்கள் முனிவர்க ளீந்த பாலகர்","உ யர்கதி பெறஅரு ளோங்கு மாமயி ...... லுறைவோனே","குரைகழல் பணிவொடு கூம்பி டார்பொரு","களமிசை யறமது தீர்ந்த சூரர்கள்","குலமுழு தனைவரு மாய்ந்து தூளெழ ...... முனிவோனே","கொடுவிட மதுதனை வாங்கி யேதிரு","மிடறினி லிருவென ஏந்து மீசுரர்","குருபர னெனவரு கூந்த லு¡ருறை ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet calls himself a fool who has gone many ways on earth, a madman, a proud lump of a body without patience, an empty one fallen from greatness, a vagabond who left the path of penance, a sinner joined to scented-haired, moon-faced, net-eyed women, and asks for grace that, if not with many flowers then with one flower or one leaf, he may fall with love and worship Murugan's feet. The praise names Vishnu who with one arrow cut Ravana's ten heads and twenty arms as uncle, Murugan who dwells on the great peacock and gives the high state to devotees, poets, gods, sages and children, who raged until the whole clan of Suras who would not bow fell to dust, and whom Siva, holding the poison in his throat, calls guru, at Koonthalur.","n":"வெகுமலர் அது கொடு வேண்டியாகிலும் ஒரு மலர் இலை கொடும்: the plea scales worship down to one flower or leaf offered with love, so the measure is love, not the offering. குருபரன் என வரு: Siva himself names Murugan guru. One word in the self-abuse (மீகுகேள்வி) is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["kutram","kaamam","vazhipaadu","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:175"},"p:875":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":875,"t":["கடகரிம ருப்பிற்க திர்த்துப்ர மிக்கமிக","வுரமிடநெ ருக்கிப்பி டித்துப்பு டைத்துவளர்","கனககுட மொத்துக்க னத்துப்பெ ருத்தமணி ...... யணியாலே","கதிர்திகழு செப்பைக்க திக்கப்ப தித்துமகிழ்","கமலமுகை பட்சத்தி ருத்திப்பொ ருத்துமுலை","கமழ்விரைகொள் செச்சைக்க லப்பைப்பொ தித்ததனை ...... விலகாது","கடுவைவடு வைப்பற்றி விற்சிக்க வைத்தசெய","லெனநிறமி யற்றிக்கு யிற்றிப்பு ரட்டிவரு","கயல்விழிவெ டுட்டித்து ரத்திச்செ விக்குழையின் ...... மிசைதாவுங்","களமதன னுக்குச்ச யத்தைப்ப டைத்துலவு","கடுமொழிப யிற்றக்க ளைத்துக்கொ டிச்சியர்கள்","கணியினில் கப்பட்ட ழுத்தத்து யர்ப்படுவ ...... தொழியேனோ","அடலைபுனை முக்கட்ப ரற்குப்பொ ருட்சொலரு","மறைதனையு ணர்த்திச்செ கத்தைப்பெ ருத்தமயில்","அதனைமுன டத்திக்க ணத்திற்றி ரித்துவரு ...... மழகோனே","அபகடமு ரைத்தத்த மெத்தப்ப டைத்துலகி","லெளியரைம ருட்டிச்செ கத்திற்பி ழைக்கவெணு","மசடர்தம னத்தைக்க லக்கித்து ணித்தடரு ...... மதிசூரா","விடவரவ ணைக்குட்டு யிற்கொட்க்ரு பைக்கடவுள்","உ லவுமலை செப்பைச்செ விக்கட்செ றித்துமிக","விரைவிலுவ ணத்திற்சி றக்கப்ரி யத்தில்வரு ...... மொருமாயோன்","விழைமருக கொக்கிற்ச முத்ரத்தி லுற்றவனை","நெறுநெறென வெட்டுக்ர சத்தித்த னிப்படைய","விடையவர்தி ருச்சத்தி முத்தத்தி னிற்குலவு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women's breasts, compared to elephant tusks, golden pots and lotus buds and smeared with fragrant paste, and their kayal-fish eyes leaping to the earrings and their hard words, and asks whether he will not cease to suffer, pressed in the grip of these women. The praise names Murugan who told the meaning of the rare Veda to three-eyed Siva and drove the peacock round the world in an instant, the hero who confounds and cuts down the fools who tell lies, heap up wealth and frighten simple people to make their living, the nephew of Vishnu who sleeps on the serpent and came swiftly on Garuda, and the lord who cut the one in the sea with his shakti, at Thiruchaththimuththam.","n":"அபகடம் உரைத்து அத்தம் மெத்தப் படைத்து உலகில் எளியரை மருட்டி ... அசடர்: an unusual target for Murugan's wrath, not demons but men who grow rich by deceiving the simple. அடலை புனை முக்கண் பரற்குப் பொருள் சொல் அருமறை: the teaching of Siva, here of the Veda's meaning without naming Om. The second half of the plea (கணியினில் அகப்பட்டு) is hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","guru","vanjam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:176"},"p:876":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":876,"t":["மகர குண்டல மீதே மோதுவ","வருண பங்கய மோபூ வோடையில்","மருவு செங்கழு நீரோநீவிடு ...... வடிவேலோ","மதன்வி டுங்கணை யோவா ளோசில","கயல்கள் கெண்டைக ளோசே லோகொலை","மறலி யென்பவ னோமா னோமது ...... நுகர்கீத","முகர வண்டின மோவான் மேலெழு","நிலவ ருந்துபு ளோமா தேவருண்","முதிய வெங்கடு வோதே மாவடு ...... வகிரோபார்","முடிவெ னுங்கட லோயா தோவென","வுலவுகண்கொடு நேரே சூறைகொள்","முறைய றிந்தப சாசே போல்பவ ...... ருறவாமோ","நிகரில் வஞ்சக மா¡£ சாதிகள்","தசமு கன்படை கோடா கோடிய","நிருத ரும்பட வோரே வேவியெ ...... யடுபோர்செய்","நெடிய னங்கனு மானோ டேயெழு","பதுவெ ளங்கவி சேனா சேவித","நிருப னம்பரர் கோமான் ராகவன் ...... மருகோனே","சிகர வும்பர்கள் பாகீ ராதிகள்","பிரபை யொன்றுபி ராசா தாதிகள்","சிவச டங்கமொ டீசா னாதிகள் ...... சிவமோனார்","தெளியு மந்த்ரக லாபா யோகிகள்","அயல்வி ளங்குசு வாமீ காமரு","திருவ லஞ்சுழி வாழ்வே தேவர்கள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks what the women's eyes are: spears striking the makara earrings, red lotus or lily, Murugan's own vel, Kama's arrows, swords, kayal and kendai fish, Death the killer, deer, humming bees, the bird that drinks moonlight, the old poison Siva ate, split tender mango, the sea that ends the world; and asks whether kinship is possible with women who roam with such eyes and plunder openly, like demons who know the way. The praise names Rama, who with Hanuman and the monkey armies destroyed Maricha and the countless demons of the ten-faced one, as uncle, and the lord at Thiruvalanjuzhi among gods, Bhagiratha and others, the Saiva rites of the Isana mantras, the silent ones of Siva and yogis who know the mantras.","n":"The figure is the doubting comparison (ஐய உவமம்): each guess names one power of the look, to wound, to charm, to kill. மறலி என்பவனோ ... மானோ puts Death and the deer side by side as the Kural does. The list of Siva's worshippers in the praise (பிராசாதாதிகள், ஈசானாதிகள், மந்த்ரகலாப யோகிகள்) is partly obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["kan","anangu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:177"},"p:877":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":877,"t":["தோடுற்றுக் காதள வோடிய","வேலுக்குத் தானிக ராயெழு","சூதத்திற் காமனி ராசத ...... விழியாலே","சோடுற்றத் தாமரை மாமுகை","போலக்கற் பூரம ளாவிய","தோல்முத்துக் கோடென வீறிய ...... முலைமானார்","கூடச்சிக் காயவ ரூழிய","மேபற்றிக் காதலி னோடிய","கூளச்சித் தாளனை மூளனை ...... வினையேனைக்","கோபித்துத் தாயென நீயொரு","போதத்தைப் பேசவ தாலருள்","கோடித்துத் தானடி யேனடி ...... பெறவேணும்","வேடிக்கைக் காரவு தாரகு","ணபத்மத் தாரணி காரண","வீரச்சுத் தாமகு டாசமர் ...... அடுதீரா","வேலைக்கட் டாணிம காரத","சூரர்க்குச் சூரனை வேல்விடு","வேழத்திற் சீரரு ளூறிய ...... இளையோனே","ஆடத்தக் காருமை பாதியர்","வேதப்பொற் கோவண வாடையர்","ஆலித்துத் தானரு ளூறிய ...... முருகோனே","ஆடப்பொற் கோபுர மேவிய","ஆடிக்கொப் பாமதிள் சூழ்பழை","யாறைப்பொற் கோயிலின் மேவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women whose eyes run to the ears like spears and whose camphor-scented breasts are like lotus buds, and calls himself a rubbish-minded slave and sinner who, caught by them, took up their service and ran after them with love; he asks Murugan to be angry with him like a mother and speak one word of teaching, so that by that grace he gains the feet. The praise names the playful, generous hero who sent the vel at Sura in the sea, the younger brother of the elephant-faced one, the son of Siva who dances with Uma as his half and wears the Vedas as his loincloth, at the golden temple of Pazhaiyarai with its towers and walls like mirrors.","n":"கோபித்துத் தாயென நீ ஒரு போதத்தைப் பேச: the plea asks for a mother's scolding and a single teaching (போதம்), grace that corrects. ஊழியமே பற்றி: the poet's service has gone to women instead of to the Lord. வேதப் பொற் கோவண ஆடையர் gives Siva the Vedas as his loincloth. A few words (கூளச் சித்தாளன், மூளன்) are uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","guru","thiruvarul","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:178"},"p:878":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":878,"t":["திட்டெ னப்பல செப்பைய டிப்பன","பொற்கு டத்தையு டைப்பன வுத்தர","திக்கி னிற்பெரு வெற்பைவி டுப்பன ...... வதின்மேலே","செப்ப வத்திம ருப்பையொ டிப்பன","புற்பு தத்தையி மைப்பில ழிப்பன","செய்த்த லைக்கம லத்தைய லைப்பன ...... திறமேய","புட்ட னைக்கக னத்தில்வி டுப்பன","சித்த முற்பொர விட்டுமு றிப்பன","புட்ப விக்கன்மு டிக்குறி யுய்ப்பன ...... இளநீரைப்","புக்கு டைப்பன முத்திரை யிட்டத","னத்தை விற்பவர் பொய்க்கல விக்குழல்","புத்தி யுற்றமை யற்றிட எப்பொழு ...... தருள்வாயே","துட்ட நிக்ரக சத்தித ரப்ரப","லப்ர சித்தச மர்த்தத மிழ்த்ரய","துட்க ரக்கவி தைப்புக லிக்கர ...... செனுநாமச்","சொற்க நிற்கசொ லட்சண தட்சண","குத்த ரத்தில கத்திய னுக்கருள்","சொற்கு ருத்வம கத்துவ சத்வஷண் ...... முகநாத","தட்ட றச்சமை யத்தைவ ளர்ப்பவ","ளத்தன் முற்புகழ் செப்பவ நுக்ரக","சத்து வத்தைய ளித்திடு செய்ப்பதி ...... மயிலேறி","சட்ப தத்திரள் மொய்த்தம ணப்பொழில்","மிக்க ரத்நம திற்புடை சுற்றிய","சக்கி ரப்பளி முக்கணர் பெற்றருள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet lists what the women's breasts do, outshine vessels, break golden pots, drive away the northern mountain, snap elephants' tusks, put bubbles to shame in a blink, rock lotuses in the field, send the paired birds into the sky, split tender coconuts, and asks when Murugan will grant that his mind's attachment to the false union and the hair of those who seal and sell those breasts will end. The praise, mostly in Sanskrit compounds, names Murugan the destroyer of the wicked who bears the shakti, master of the three Tamils, known by the name of the king of Pugali, the teacher who gave Agastya grammar in the south, the six-faced lord, who graced Parvati, she who grows the faiths, and rides the peacock at Seyppathi and dwells at Chakkarappalli, son of the three-eyed one.","n":"முத்திரையிட்ட தனத்தை விற்பவர்: the breasts are sealed goods sold for money, which makes these courtesans. துட்கரக் கவிதைப் புகலிக் கரசு எனும் நாமம்: Murugan bears the name of the king of Pugali (Sirkazhi) of hard poems, that is Sambandar. சொல்லட்சண ... அகத்தியனுக்கருள் சொற்குரு: the teacher of grammar to Agastya. Some compounds in the praise are hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","kaamam","thamizh","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:179"},"p:879":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":879,"t":["அலங்கார முடிக்கிரணத் திரண்டாறு முகத்தழகிற்","கசைந்தாடு குழைக்கவசத் ...... திரடோளும்","அலந்தாம மணித்திரளை புரண்டாட நிரைத்ததகரத்","தணிந்தாழி வனைக்கடகச் ...... சுடர்வேலுஞ்","சிலம்போடு மணிச்சுருதிச் சலங்கோசை மிகுத்ததிரச்","சிவந்தேறி மணத்தமலர்ப் ...... புனைபாதந்","திமிந்தோதி திமித்திமிதித் தனந்தான தனத்தனனத்","தினந்தோறு நடிப்பதுமற் ...... புகல்வேனோ","இலங்கேசர் வனத்துள்வனக் குரங்கேவி யழற்புகையிட்","டிளந்தாது மலர்த்திருவைச் ...... சிறைமீளும்","இளங்காள முகிற் கடுமைச் சரங்கோடு கரத்திலெடுத்","திருங்கான நடக்குமவற் ...... கினியோனே","குலங்கோடு படைத்தசுரப் பெருஞ்சேனை யழிக்கமுனைக்","கொடுந்தாரை வெயிற்கயிலைத் ...... தொடும்வீரா","கொழுங்காவின் மலர்ப்பொழிலிற் கரும்பாலை புணர்க்குமிசைக்","குரங்காடு துறைக்குமரப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether he will tell of anything but the beauty of Murugan's crowned six faces, his earringed shoulders, the garlands and gems, the flaming vel, the red flower feet with anklets, and his dance each day to drum syllables. The praise names Rama, who sent the forest monkey into Ravana's grove to set fire and freed Sita, and who walked the great forest with bow and arrows, as the one to whom Murugan is dear, the hero whose vel ended the asuras' army, and the lord at Kurangaduthurai where cane mills hum in flowering groves.","n":"The first half has no confession: it is a description of Murugan's form and dance (திமிந்தோதி திமித்திமி ... தினந்தோறு நடிப்பது), and the question புகல்வேனோ may mean 'shall I tell of it?' or 'shall I tell of anything else?'. The drum syllables are rhythm. கொடுந்தாரை வெயில் கயிலைத் தொடும் is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","pugazh","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:180"},"p:880":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":880,"t":["குறித்தநெஞ் சாசை விரகிகள்","நவிற்றுசங் கீத மிடறிகள்","குதித்தரங் கேறு நடனிகள் ...... எவரோடுங்","குறைப்படுங் காதல் குனகிகள்","அரைப்பணங் கூறு விலையினர்","கொலைக்கொடும் பார்வை நயனிகள் ...... நகரேகை","பொறித்தசிங் கார முலையினர்","வடுப்படுங் கோவை யிதழிகள்","பொருட்டினந் தேடு கபடிகள் ...... தவர்சோரப்","புரித்திடும் பாவ சொருபிகள்","உ ருக்குசம் போக சரசிகள்","புணர்ச்சிகொண் டாடு மருளது ...... தவிர்வேனோ","நெறித்திருண் டாறு பதமலர்","மணத்தபைங் கோதை வகைவகை","நெகிழ்க்குமஞ் சோதி வனசரி ...... மணவாளா","நெருக்குமிந்த் ராதி யமரர்கள்","வளப்பெருஞ் சேனை யுடையவர்","நினைக்குமென் போலு மடியவர் ...... பெருவாழ்வே","செறித்தமந் தாரை மகிழ்புனை","மிகுத்ததண் சோலை வகைவகை","தியக்கியம் பேறு நதியது ...... பலவாறுந்","திரைக்கரங் கோலி நவமணி","கொழித்திடுஞ் சாரல் வயலணி","திருக்குரங் காடு துறையுறை ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet lists the women of scheming desire: singers, dancers who leap onto the stage, coaxers whose love falls short with anyone, who name a price of half a coin, with killing glances, nail-marked breasts and bitten lips, cheats who seek money every day, forms of sin who make ascetics falter, who melt men in pleasure, and asks whether he will give up the delusion of celebrating union with them. The praise names Murugan the bridegroom of the forest girl with dark scented hair, the great life of devotees like himself whom Indra and the gods remember, at Kurangaduthurai where the river's waves cast up the nine gems on the fields.","n":"அரைப்பணம் கூறு விலையினர் and பொருட்டினம் தேடு கபடிகள் name the price and the greed. தவர் சோரப் புரித்திடும் பாவ சொருபிகள்: their power reaches even ascetics. நினைக்கும் என் போலும் அடியவர்: the poet counts himself among the devotees despite the confession.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","punarchi","mayal","thavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:180"},"p:881":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":881,"t":["குடங்கள் நிரைத்தேறு தடங்கள் குறித்தார","வடங்கள் அசைத்தார ...... செயநீலங்","குதம்பை யிடத்தேறு வடிந்த குழைக்காது","குளிர்ந்த முகப்பார்வை ...... வலையாலே","உ டம்பு மறக்கூனி நடந்து மிகச்சாறி","யுலந்து மிகக்கோலு ...... மகலாதே","உ றங்கி விழிப்பாய பிறந்து பிறப்பேனு","முரங்கொள பொற்பாத ...... மருள்வாயே","விடங்கள் கதுப்பேறு படங்க ணடித்தாட","விதங்கொள் முதற்பாய ...... லுறைமாயன்","விலங்கை முறித்தோடி யிடங்கள் வளைத்தேறு","விளங்கு முகிற்கான ...... மருகோனே","தடங்கொள் வரைச்சாரல் நளுங்கு மயிற்பேடை","தழங்கு மியற்பாடி ...... யளிசூழத்","தயங்கு வயற்சாரல் குரங்கு குதித்தாடு","தலங்க ளிசைப்பான ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of women with pot-like breasts and garlands, caught in the net of their cool-faced glance, and then of the body bent and stooping, walking unsteadily, withered, never without its staff, sleeping and waking, being born and born again; even so, he asks, give the golden feet to wear on his chest. The praise names Vishnu who danced on the poisonous hoods, sleeps on the first serpent-bed, and broke his shackles and ran, the dark cloud, as uncle, and the lord of the shrine where peahens cry on the slopes, bees swarm, and monkeys leap in the fields.","n":"பிறந்து பிறப்பேனும் ... பொற்பாதம் அருள்வாயே: the plea does not ask to end birth but for the feet even if he is born again. The move from the glance to the stooping body with its staff (கூனி, கோலும் அகலாதே) makes old age the sequel of desire. The Vishnu lines point to Krishna on Kaliya's hoods and his chains breaking at birth, but the reading of விலங்கை முறித்தோடி is mine.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","yaakkai","piravi","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:180"},"p:882":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":882,"t":["மானை நேர்விழி யொத்தம டந்தையர்","பாலை நேர்மொழி யொத்துவி ளம்பியர்","வாச மாமலர் கட்டும ரம்பைய ...... ரிருதோளும்","மார்பு மீதினு முத்துவ டம்புரள்","காம பூரண பொற்கட கம்பொர","வாரி நீலவ ளைக்கைபு லம்பிட ...... அநுராகம்","ஆன நேரில்வி தத்திர யங்களும்","நாண மாறம யக்கியி யம்பவும்","ஆடை சோரநெ கிழ்த்தியி ரங்கவும் ...... உ றவாடி","ஆர வாரந யத்தகு ணங்களில்","வேளி னூல்களை கற்றவி ளம்பவும்","ஆகு மோகவி பத்துமொ ழிந்துனை ...... யடைவேனோ","சான கீதுய ரத்தில ருஞ்சிறை","போன போதுதொ குத்தசி னங்களில்","தாப சோபமொ ழிப்பஇ லங்கையு ...... மழிவாகத்","தாரை மானொரு சுக்கிரி பன்பெற","வாலி வாகுத லத்தில்வி ழுந்திட","சாத வாளிதொ டுத்தமு குந்தனன் ...... மருகோனே","கான வேடர்சி றுக்குடி லம்புன","மீதில் வாழித ணத்திலு றைந்திடு","காவல் கூருகு றத்திபு ணர்ந்திடு ...... மணிமார்பா","காவு லாவிய பொற்கமு கின்திரள்","பாளை வீசம லர்த்தட முஞ்செறி","காவ ளூர்தனில் முத்தமி ழுந்தெரி ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes deer-eyed women with milk-sweet speech and pearl-strung breasts, bangles crying as they make love, shame dropped and garments loosened, reciting the texts of Kama, and asks whether, escaping the ruin of that delusion, he will reach Murugan. The praise names Rama who, in anger at Janaki's captivity, destroyed Lanka, gave Sugriva the kingdom and Tara and felled Vali with an arrow, as uncle, the lover of the kura girl who kept watch on the platform in the hunters' millet field, and the lord at Kavalur of areca groves where the three Tamils are known.","n":"வேளின் நூல்களைக் கற்ற விளம்பவும்: the women recite Kama's texts, love as a learned art. ஆகும் மோக விபத்து: the delusion is called a calamity. The headword is printed காவ்ளுர் and the last line reads காவளூர்.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","punarchi","mayal"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:181"},"p:883":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":883,"t":["அஞ்சன வேல்விழி ...... மடமாதர்","அங்கவர் மாயையி ...... லலைவேனோ","விஞ்சுறு மாவுன ...... தடிசேர","விம்பம தாயரு ...... ளருளாதோ","நஞ்சமு தாவுணு ...... மரனார்தம்","நன்கும ராவுமை ...... யருள்பாலா","தஞ்சென வாமடி ...... யவர்வாழத்","தஞ்சையில் மேவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A short song: the poet asks whether he will wander in the maya of women with kohl-dark spear eyes, and whether grace will not come in visible form so that he may reach Murugan's feet. The praise names him the son of Hara who ate poison as nectar and of Uma, dwelling at Thanjai so that devotees who take refuge may live.","n":"தஞ்சென ... தஞ்சையில்: a pun on the place name and தஞ்சம், refuge. விம்பமதாய் அருள் may mean grace taking an image or form; the sense is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maayai","kaamam","thiruvarul","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:182"},"p:884":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":884,"t":["அம்பு ராசியிற் கெண்டை செலொளித்","தஞ்ச வேமணிக் ...... குழைவீசும்","அங்க ணாரிடத் தின்ப சாகரத்","தங்கி மூழ்குமிச் ...... சையினாலே","எம்பி ரானுனைச் சிந்தி யாதொழித்","திந்த்ர சாலஇப் ...... ப்ரமைதீர","எங்கு வாவெனப் பண்பி னாலழைத்","தெங்கு மானமெய்ப் ...... பொருள்தாராய்","கொம்பு போலிடைத் தொண்டை போலிதழ்க்","கொண்டல் போல்குழற் ...... கனமேருக்","குன்று போல்முலைப் பைங்கி ராதியைக்","கொண்ட கோலசற் ...... குணவேலா","சம்ப ராரியைக் கொன்ற தீவிழிச்","சம்பு போதகக் ...... குருநாதா","சண்ட கோபுரச் செம்பொன் மாளிகைத்","தஞ்சை மாநகர்ப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses that in the wish to sink in the sea of pleasure with women whose eyes are like kendai fish hiding in the sea, he left off thinking of Murugan, and asks that, to end this conjuror's illusion, Murugan call him kindly 'come here' and give the true substance that is everywhere. The praise names Valli with her twig-waist and cloud-dark hair, Murugan the teacher of Sambhu whose fire-eye killed Kama, and the lord of great Thanjai with its towering gopuram and golden mansions.","n":"இந்த்ரசால இப்ப்ரமை: the world of desire is a magic show (indrajala) and a delusion. எங்குமான மெய்ப்பொருள் தாராய்: the remedy is the pervading reality, given by the call 'come here' (எங்கு வா). சம்பராரியைக் கொன்ற தீவிழிச் சம்பு போதகக் குருநாதா: Murugan as guru to Siva who burned Kama.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mayal","maayai","viyaabagam","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:182"},"p:885":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":885,"t":["கந்த வார்குழல் கோதி மாலை யைப்பு னைந்து","மஞ்ச ளாலழ காக மேனி யிற்றி மிர்ந்து","கண்ட மாலைக ளான ஆணி முத்த ணிந்து ...... தெருவூடே","கண்ட பேரையெ லாம வாவி னிற்கொ ணர்ந்து","வண்ப யோதர பார மேரு வைத்தி றந்து","கண்க ளாகிய கூர வேலை விட்டெ றிந்து ...... விலைகூறி","வந்த பேர்களை யேகை யாலே டுத்த ணைந்து","கொண்டு தேனித ழூறு வாயை வைத்த ருந்தி","மந்த மாருதம் வீசு பாய லிற்பு ணர்ந்து ...... மயல்பூணு","மங்கை மாரநு போக தீவி னைப்ப வங்கள்","மங்கி யேகிடு மாறு ஞான வித்தை தந்து","வண்டு லாவிய நீப மாலை சற்றி லங்க ...... வருவாயே","இந்த்ர தாருவை ஞால மீதி னிற்கொ ணர்ந்த","சங்க பாணிய னாதி கேச வப்ர சங்க","னென்று வாழ்மணி மார்பன் வீர விக்ர மன்றன் ...... மருகோனே","எண்டி சாமுக வேலை ஞால முற்று மண்டு","கந்த தாருக சேனை நீறு பட்டொ துங்க","வென்று பேரொளி சேர்ப்ர காசம் விட்டி லங்கு ...... கதிர்வேலா","சந்த்ர சேகரி நாக பூஷ ணத்தி யண்ட","முண்ட நாரணி யால போஜ னத்தி யம்பை","தந்த பூரண ஞான வேள்கு றத்தி துஞ்சு ...... மணிமார்பா","சண்ட நீலக லாப வாசி யிற்றி கழ்ந்து","கஞ்சன் வாசவன் மேவி வாழ்ப திக்கு யர்ந்த","தஞ்சை மாநகர் ராஜ கோபு ரத்த மர்ந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes courtesans who comb their hair, smear turmeric, wear pearls, draw in everyone they see on the street, bare their breasts, throw the spears of their eyes, name a price and take those who come to bed, and asks that the evil deeds and births of pleasure with them fade away, that Murugan give the knowledge of jnana and come with his kadamba garland. The praise names Vishnu who brought Indra's tree to earth, the conch-bearer Kesava, as uncle, the vel that dusted Taraka's armies in the eight directions, Parvati, moon-crowned, serpent-adorned, who swallowed the poison, as the mother who gave Murugan, and the lord on the peacock at the royal gopuram of great Thanjai.","n":"விலைகூறி: the price is named. அநுபோக தீவினைப் பவங்கள்: the pleasures are counted as evil deeds that make births, and the cure asked is ஞான வித்தை. இந்த்ர தாருவை ஞாலமீதினிற் கொணர்ந்த is Krishna bringing the parijata tree. தஞ்சைமாநகர் ராஜகோபுரம்: the great gopuram of Thanjavur.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vinai","piravi","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:182"},"p:886":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":886,"t":["மருவு லாவிடு மோதி குலைப்பவர்","சமர வேலெனு நீடு விழிச்சியர்","மனதி லேகப டூரு பரத்தைய ...... ரதிகேள்வர்","மதன னோடுறழ் பூச லிடைச்சியர்","இளைஞ ராருயிர் வாழு முலைச்சியர்","மதுர மாமொழி பேசு குணத்தியர் ...... தெருமீதே","சருவி யாரையும் வாவெ னழைப்பவர்","பொருளி லேவெகு ஆசை பரப்பிகள்","சகல தோதக மாயை படிப்பரை ...... யணுகாதே","சலச மேவிய பாத நி¨த்துமுன்","அருணை நாடதி லோது திருப்புகழ்","தணிய வோகையி லோத எனக்கருள் ...... புரிவாயே","அரிய கானக மேவு குறத்திதன்","இதணி லேசில நாளு மனத்துடன்","அடவி தோறுமெ வாழியல் பத்தினி ...... மணவாளா","அசுரர் வீடுகள் நூறு பொடிப்பட","உ ழவர் சாகர மோடி யொளித்திட","அமரர் நாடுபொன் மாரி மிகுத்திட ...... நினைவோனே","திருவின் மாமர மார்பழ னப்பதி","அயிலு சோறவை யாளுது றைப்பதி","திசையி னான்மறை தேடிய முற்குடி ...... விதியாதிச்","சிரமு மாநிலம் வீழ்தரு மெய்ப்பதி","பதும நாயகன் வாழ்பதி நெய்ப்பதி","திருவை யாறுட னேழுதி ருப்பதி ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes courtesans with deceit in their hearts, spear eyes and sweet words, who call everyone on the street, spread their desire for money and read every trick of maya, and asks not to approach them but, thinking on the lotus feet, to sing in calm joy the Thiruppugazh he sang before at Arunai. The praise names Murugan the bridegroom of Valli, with whom he stayed on her platform and wandered every forest, at whose thought the asuras' houses fell to dust and the gods' land rained gold, and the lord of the seven shrines of Thiruvaiyaru: Pazhanam, Chotruthurai, Vedikudi, Kandiyur where Brahma's head fell, Poonthuruthi, Neyththanam and Thiruvaiyaru itself.","n":"முன் அருணை நாடதில் ஓது திருப்புகழ்: the poet refers to his own earlier songs at Tiruvannamalai, where the book's tradition places the start of the Thiruppugazh. The seven shrines are named by allusion: அயிலு சோறு for Chotruthurai, மறை தேடிய ... குடி for Vedikudi, விதி ஆதிச் சிரமும் மாநிலம் வீழ்தரு for Kandiyur, நெய்ப்பதி for Neyththanam. The asura line (உழவர் சாகரமோடி ஒளித்திட) is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","pugazh","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:183"},"p:887":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":887,"t":["சொரியு மாமுகி லோஇரு ளோகுழல்","சுடர்கொள் வாளிணை யோபிணை யோவிழி","சுரர்த மாரமு தோகுயி லோமொழி ...... யிதழ்கோவை","துவர தோஇல வோதெரி யாஇடை","துகளி லாவன மோபிடி யோநடை","துணைகொள் மாமலை யோமுலை தானென ...... உ ரையாடிப்","பரிவி னாலெனை யாளுக நானொரு","பழுதி லானென வாணுத லாரொடு","பகடி யேபடி யாவொழி யாஇடர் ...... படுமாயப்","பரவை மீதழி யாவகை ஞானிகள்","பரவு நீள்புக ழேயது வாமிகு","பரம வீடது சேர்வது மாவது ...... மொருநாளே","கரிய மேனிய னானிரை யாள்பவன்","அரிய ராவணை மேல்வளர் மாமுகில்","கனகன் மார்பது பீறிய வாளரி ...... கனமாயக்","கபடன் மாமுடி யாறுட னாலுமொர்","கணையி னால்நில மீதுற நூறிய","கருணை மால்கவி கோபக்ரு பாகரன் ...... மருகோனே","திரிபு ராதிகள் தூளெழ வானவர்","திகழ வேமுனி யாவருள் கூர்பவர்","தெரிவை பாதியர் சாதியி லாதவர் ...... தருசேயே","சிகர பூதர நீறுசெய் வேலவ","திமிர மோகர வீரதி வாகர","திருவை யாறுறை தேவக்ரு பாகர ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet gives the lover's questions, is her hair a raining cloud or darkness, are her eyes swords or does, is her speech the gods' nectar or the cuckoo, her gait a swan's or a she-elephant's, her breasts a mountain, and confesses that he talks thus to bright-browed women, saying 'rule me in love, I am without fault', and suffers; he asks whether one day, not perishing in that sea of illusion, he will reach the long praise that jnanis sing, which is the supreme release. The praise names Vishnu the cowherd on the serpent-bed, the Man-Lion who tore Hiranya's chest and Rama who felled Ravana's ten heads with one arrow, as uncle, and Murugan the son of Siva who burned the three cities, has a woman as his half and has no caste, the vel that turned the peak to ash, at Thiruvaiyaru.","n":"ஞானிகள் பரவு நீள்புகழே அது ஆம் மிகு பரம வீடு: the praise itself is equated with release. சாதியிலாதவர்: Siva is named as one without caste or birth. The doubting comparisons of the first half are the stock of akam praise of a woman.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","maayai","pugazh","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:184"},"p:888":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":888,"t":["வீங்கு பச்சிள நீர்போல் மாமுலை","சேர்ந்த ணைத்தெதிர் மார்பூ டேபொர","வேண்டு சர்க்கரை பால்தே னேரிதழ் ...... உ ண்டுதோயா","வேண்டு ரைத்துகில் வேறாய் மோகன","வாஞ்சை யிற்களி கூரா வாள்விழி","மேம்ப டக்குழை மீதே மோதிட ...... வண்டிராசி","ஓங்கு மைக்குழல் சாதா வீறென","வீந்து புட்குரல் கூவா வேள்கலை","யோர்ந்தி டப்பல க்¡£டா பேதமு ...... யங்குமாகா","ஊண்பு ணர்ச்சியு மாயா வாதனை","தீர்ந்து னக்கெளி தாயே மாதவ","மூன்று தற்குமெய்ஞ் ஞானா சாரம்வ ...... ழங்குவாயே","தாங்கு நிற்சரர் சேனா நீதரு","னாங்கு ருத்ரகு மாரா கோஷண","தாண்ட வற்கருள் கேகீ வாகன ...... துங்கவீரா","சாங்கி பற்சுகர் சீநா தீசுர","ரேந்த்ரன் மெச்சிய வேலா போதக","சாந்த வித்தக ஸ்வாமீ நீபவ ...... லங்கன்மார்பா","பூங்கு ளத்திடை தாரா வோடன","மேய்ந்த செய்ப்பதி நாதா மாமலை","போன்ற விக்ரக சூரா ¡£பகி ...... ரண்ட்ருபா","போந்த பத்தர்பொ லாநோய் போயிட","வேண்ட நுக்ரக போதா மேவிய","பூந்து ருத்தியில் வாழ்வே தேவர்கள் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes the embrace, breasts like tender coconuts, lips sweet as sugar, milk and honey, garments parted, eyes striking the earrings, love-cries and Kama's many kinds of play, and asks that, with the cravings of food, union and maya ended, Murugan give him the conduct of true knowledge that makes him easy to reach. The praise calls Murugan commander of the gods' army, Rudra's son, rider of the peacock, praised by sages and Indra, the calm wise teacher wearing the kadamba garland, lord of Seyppathi, the hero with a mountain-like form, who grants grace so that his devotees' diseases leave them, at Poonthuruthi.","n":"ஊண் புணர்ச்சியுமாயா வாதனை: hunger, union and maya are named together as the vasanas (traces) to end. மெய்ஞ்ஞான ஆசாரம்: knowledge is asked as a conduct, not only a vision. போந்த பத்தர் பொலா நோய் போயிட: Murugan as healer. Several epithets in the praise are corrupt or obscure (சாங்கிபர், சீநாதீசுரர், ¡£பகிரண்ட்ருபா).","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["punarchi","kaamam","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:185"},"p:889":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":889,"t":["முகிலைக் காரைச் சருவிய குழலது","சரியத் தாமத் தொடைவகை நெகிழ்தர","முளரிப் பூவைப் பனிமதி தனைநிகர் ...... முகம்வேர்வ","முனையிற் காதிப் பொருகணை யினையிள","வடுவைப் பானற் பரிமள நறையிதழ்","முகையைப் போலச் சமர்செயு மிருவிழி ...... குழைமோதத்","துகிரைக் கோவைக் கனிதனை நிகரிதழ்","பருகிக் காதற் றுயரற வளநிறை","துணைபொற் றோளிற் குழைவுற மனமது ...... களிகூரச்","சுடர்முத் தாரப் பணியணி ம்ருகமத","நிறைபொற் பாரத் திளகிய முகிழ்முலை","துவளக் கூடித் துயில்கினு முனதடி ...... மறவேனே","குகுகுக் கூகுக் குகுகுகு குகுவென","திமிதித் தீதித் திமிதியென் முரசொடு","குழுமிச் சீறிச் சமர்செயு மசுரர்கள் ...... களமீதே","குழறிக் கூளித் திரளெழ வயிரவர்","குவியக் கூடிக் கொடுவர அலகைகள்","குணலிட் டாடிப் பசிகெட அயில்வெடு ...... குமரேசா","செகசெச் சேசெச் செகவென முரசொலி","திகழச் சூழத் திருநட மிடுபவர்","செறிகட் காளப் பணியணி யிறையவர் ...... தருசேயே","சிகரப் பாரக் கிரியுறை குறமகள்","கலசத் தாமத் தனகிரி தழுவிய","திருநெய்த் தானத் துறைபவ சுரபதி ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes the lovemaking, cloud-dark hair loosening, garlands slipping, the lotus face sweating, the eyes striking the earrings, coral lips drunk, the musk-scented breasts pressed, and says that even sleeping so in embrace he will not forget Murugan's feet. The praise shows the battle in drum syllables, ghouls rising, Bhairavas and demons dancing with their hunger ended by the vel, names Murugan the son of the Lord who dances to the drum wearing serpents, who embraced the kura girl of the mountain, at Thiruneyththanam, lord of the gods.","n":"துயில்கினும் உனதடி மறவேனே: the song does not renounce the embrace but vows remembrance in the middle of it, an unusual turn for the book. The drum syllables (குகுகுக் கூகுக், செகசெச்) are rhythm.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","thiruvadi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:186"},"p:890":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":890,"t":["விகட சங்கட வார்த்தை பேசிகள்","அவல மங்கைய ரூத்தை நாறிகள்","விரிவ டங்கிட மாற்றும் வாறென ...... வருவார்தம்","விதம்வி தங்களை நோக்கி யாசையி","லுபரி தங்களை மூட்டி யேதம","இடும ருந்தொடு சோற்றை யேயிடு ...... விலைமாதர்","சகல மஞ்சன மாட்டி யேமுலை","படவ ளைந்திசை மூட்டி யேவரு","சரச இங்கித நேத்தி யாகிய ...... சுழலாலே","சதிமு ழங்கிட வாய்ப்ப ணானது","மலர வுந்தியை வாட்டி யேயிடை","தளர வுங்கணை யாட்டும் வேசிய ...... ருறவாமோ","திகிரி கொண்டிரு ளாக்கி யேயிரு","தமையர் தம்பியர் மூத்த தாதையர்","திலக மைந்தரை யேற்ற சூரரை ...... வெகுவான","செனம டங்கலு மாற்றி யேயுடல்","தகர அங்கவர் கூட்டை யேநரி","திருகி யுண்டிட ஆர்த்த கூளிக ...... ளடர்பூமி","அகடு துஞ்சிட மூட்டு பாரத","முடிய அன்பர்க ளேத்த வேயரி","யருளு மைந்தர்கள் வாழ்த்து மாயவன் ...... மருகோனே","அமர ரந்தணர் போற்ற வேகிரி","கடல திர்ந்திட நோக்கு மாமயில்","அழகொ டும்பழு வூர்க்குள் மேவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet lists the price-women who speak crooked troubling words, whose mouths stink, who size up men, kindle their desire, and serve rice mixed with a drug, who bathe in unguents, dance with anklets, and whirl men with seductive eyes and swaying waists, and asks whether kinship with such prostitutes can be. The praise names Krishna, who darkened the sun with his discus and finished the Bharata war, killing brothers, uncles, elders and sons of the Kauravas until foxes and ghouls fed on the field, praised by the devotees and the Pandavas, as uncle, and Murugan with his peacock that shakes mountain and sea, at Pazhuvur.","n":"இடும் மருந்தொடு சோற்றையே இடு விலைமாதர்: the women feed men rice laced with a drug, a charge of love-medicine (வசிய மருந்து) beyond the usual deceit. திகிரி கொண்டு இருளாக்கி: Krishna hiding the sun with his discus, from the Mahabharata war. The Pandava lines (அரி அருளும் மைந்தர்கள்) are my reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","marundhu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:187"},"p:891":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":891,"t":["சதங்கைமணி வீரச் சிலம்பினிசை பாடச்","சரங்களொளி வீசப் ...... புயமீதே","தனங்கள்குவ டாடப் படர்ந்தபொறி மால்பொற்","சரங்கண்மறி காதிற் ...... குழையாட","இதங்கொள்மயி லேரொத் துகந்தநகை பேசுற்","றிரம்பையழ கார்மைக் ...... குழலாரோ","டிழைந்தமளி யோடுற் றழுந்துமெனை நீசற்","றிரங்கியிரு தாளைத் ...... தருவாயே","சிதம்பரகு மாரக் கடம்புதொடை யாடச்","சிறந்தமயில் மேலுற் ...... றிடுவோனே","சிவந்தகழு காடப் பிணங்கள்மலை சாயச்","சினந்தசுரர் வேரைக் ...... களைவோனே","பெதும்பையெழு கோலச் செயங்கொள்சிவ காமிப்","ப்ரசண்டஅபி ராமிக் ...... கொருபாலா","பெரும்புனம தேகிக் குறம்பெணொடு கூடிப்","பெரும்புலியுர் வாழ்பொற் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women with anklets and jingling ornaments, breasts heaving, earrings swinging, peacock-like and laughing, and asks Murugan, who sees him sunk with them on the bed, to pity him a little and give his two feet. The praise names Murugan the son of Chidambaram on the peacock with his kadamba garland, the one at whose anger the asuras were uprooted and vultures fed on heaped corpses, the son of Sivakami, the radiant Abhirami, who went to the millet field and joined the kura girl, at Perumpuliyur.","n":"சிதம்பரகுமார and சிவகாமி: the praise names Murugan through Chidambaram's dancer and his consort, near Perumpuliyur (புலியூர் is also Chidambaram's old name). பெதும்பை எழு கோலம் may refer to the seven stages of a woman's life.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","punarchi","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:188"},"p:892":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":892,"t":["பஞ்சபுல னும்பழைய ரண்டுவினை யும்பிணிகள்","பஞ்செனஎ ரிந்துபொடி ...... யங்கமாகிப்","பண்டறவுடன்பழைய தொண்டர்களு டன்பழகி","பஞ்சவர்வி யன்பதியு ...... டன்குலாவக்","குஞ்சரமு கன்குணமொ டந்தவனம் வந்துலவ","கொஞ்சியசி லம்புகழல் ...... விந்துநாதங்","கொஞ்சமயி லினற்புறமெல் வந்தருளி யென்கவலை","கொன்றருள்நி றைந்தகழ ...... லின்றுதாராய்","எஞ்சியிடை யுஞ்சுழல அம்புவிழி யுஞ்சுழல","இன்பரச கொங்கைகர ...... முங்கொளாமல்","எந்தவுடை சிந்தபெல மிஞ்சியமு தம்புரள","இந்துநுத லும்புரள ...... கங்குல்மேகம்","அஞ்சுமள கம்புரள மென்குழைக ளும்புரள","அம்பொனுரு மங்கைமண ...... முண்டபாலா","அன்பர்குல வுந்திருநெ டுங்களவ ளம்பதியில்","அண்டரய னும்பரவு ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks that the five senses, the two old deeds and diseases burn to ash like cotton, that he live among the old servants of the Lord, and that Murugan, with the elephant-faced one, come on the peacock with anklets sounding bindu and nada, kill his care and give the full feet today. The praise describes the union of Murugan with his golden consort, waist and eyes whirling, garments slipping, the moon-brow and dark hair tumbling, and names him the lord of Nedungalam, where devotees gather and Brahma and the gods praise him.","n":"The frank love scene here belongs to the god, not to the poet's confession: மங்கை மணமுண்ட பாலா. பழைய தொண்டர்களுடன் பழகி asks for the company of the old devotees. சிலம்பு கழல் விந்து நாதம்: the anklets sound as bindu and nada. பஞ்சவர் வியன் பதியுடன் குலாவ is obscure; it may name the five (the senses, or Siva's five faces).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","adiyaar","thiruvadi","punarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:189"},"p:893":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":893,"t":["கூரிய கடைக்க ணாலு மேருநி கரொப்ப தான","கோடதனில் மெத்த வீறு ...... முலையாலுங்","கோபவ தரத்தி னாலு மேவிடு விதத்து ளால","கோலவு தரத்தி னாலு ...... மொழியாலும்","சீரிய வளைக்கை யாலு மேகலை நெகிழ்ச்சி யேசெய்","சீருறு நுசுப்பி னாலும் ...... விலைமாதர்","சேறுத னினித்த மூழ்கி நாளவ மிறைத்து மாயை","சேர்தரு முளத்த னாகி ...... யுழல்வேனோ","தாரணி தனக்குள் வீறி யேசம ரதுட்டனான","ராவணன் மிகுத்த தானை ...... பொடியாகச்","சாடுமு வணப்ப தாகை நீடுமு கிலொத்த மேனி","தாதுறை புயத்து மாயன் ...... மருகோனே","வாரண முரித்து மாதர் மேகலை வளைக்கை நாண","மாபலி முதற்கொ ணாதன் ...... முருகோனே","வாருறு தனத்தி னார்கள் சேரும திளுப்ப ¡£கை","வாகுள குறட்டி மேவு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet lists the sharp eye-corners, mountain breasts, red lips, belly, speech, bangled hands and loosened girdles of the price-women, and asks whether he will wander with a mind joined to maya, sinking daily in their mud and wasting his days. The praise names Vishnu of the Garuda banner who destroyed Ravana's army, as uncle, and Murugan the son of the Lord who flayed the elephant and took alms while women's girdles and bangles slipped in shame, at Kurattai with its walls and balconies.","n":"சேறு தனில் நித்தம் மூழ்கி நாள் அவம் இறைத்து: the women are mud and the days are poured away. மாதர் மேகலை வளைக்கை நாண மாபலி முதற்கொள் நாதன் appears to name Siva as the beggar (Bhikshatana) whose beauty made the sages' wives lose their girdles and bangles.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["varaivinmagalir","maayai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:190"},"p:894":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":894,"t":["நீரிழிவு குட்ட மீளை வாதமொடு பித்த மூல","நீள்குளிர் வெதுப்பு வேறு ...... முளநோய்கள்","நேருறு புழுக்கள் கூடு நான்முக னெடுத்த வீடு","நீடிய விரத்த மூளை ...... தசைதோல்சீ","பாரிய நவத்து வார நாறுமு மலத்தி லாறு","பாய்பிணி யியற்று பாவை ...... நரிநாய்பேய்","பாறோடு கழுக்கள் கூகை தாமிவை புசிப்ப தான","பாழுட லெடுத்து வீணி ...... லுழல்வேனோ","நாரணி யறத்தி னாரி ஆறுச மயத்தி பூத","நாயக ரிடத்து காமி ...... மகமாயி","நாடக நடத்தி கோல நீலவ ருணத்தி வேத","நாயகி யுமைச்சி நீலி ...... திரிசூலி","வாரணி முலைச்சி ஞான பூரணி கலைச்சி நாக","வாணுத லளித்த வீர ...... மயிலோனே","மாடம தில்முத்து மேடை கோபுர மணத்த சோலை","வாகுள குறட்டி மேவு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet lists the diseases, diabetes, leprosy, phlegm, wind, bile, piles, fever, and the worms of the house Brahma built of blood, brain, flesh, skin and pus, stinking through nine gates, a puppet of disease that foxes, dogs, ghouls, vultures and owls will eat, and asks whether he will wander in vain carrying this ruined body. The praise is a litany of Parvati as Narani, the woman of dharma, lady of the six faiths, Mahamayi, the dancer, the blue one, queen of the Vedas, Uma, Neeli, bearer of the trident, full of jnana, who gave birth to the hero on the peacock, at Kurattai of mansions, towers and groves.","n":"நான்முகன் எடுத்த வீடு: the body is a house Brahma built, and the inventory runs from disease through its stuff to its scavengers. The praise is given almost wholly to the Mother (ஆறு சமயத்தி, ஞானபூரணி).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","noy","nilaiyaamai","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:190"},"p:895":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":895,"t":["கருகியறி வகலவுயிர் விட்டுக் கிக்கிளைஞர்","கதறியழ விரவுபறை முட்டக் கொட்டியிட","கனகமணி சிவிகையில மர்த்திக் கட்டையினி ...... லிடைபோடாக்","கரமலர்கொ டரிசியினை யிட்டுச் சித்ரமிகு","கலையைபுரி செய்துமறைகள் பற்றப் பற்றுகனல்","கணகணென எரியவுடல் சுட்டுக் கக்ஷ¢யவர் ...... வழியேபோய்","மருவுபுனல் முழுகிமனை புக்குத் துக்கமறு","மனிதர்தமை யுறவுநிலை சுட்டுச் சுட்டியுற","மகிழ்வுசெய்து அழுதுபட வைத்தத் துட்டன்மதன் ...... மலராலே","மயல்விளைய அரியையர்கள் கைப்பட் டெய்த்துமிக","மனமழியு மடிமையைநி னைத்துச் சொர்க்கபதி","வழியையிது வழியெனவு ரைத்துப் பொற்கழல்கள் ...... தருவாயே","பொருவின்மலை யரையனருள் பச்சைச் சித்ரமயில்","புரமெரிய இரணியத னுக்கைப் பற்றியியல்","புதியமுடு கரியதவ முற்றுக் கச்சியினி ...... லுறமேவும்","புகழ்வனிதை தருபுதல்வ பத்துக் கொத்துமுடி","புயமிருப தறவுமெய்த சக்ரக் கைக்கடவுள்","பொறியரவின் மிசைதுயிலு சுத்தப் பச்சைமுகில் ...... மருகோனே","அரியமர கதமயிலி லுற்றுக் கத்துகட","லதுசுவற அசுரர்கிளை கெட்டுக் கட்டையற","அமரர்பதி யினியகுடி வைத்தற் குற்றமிகு ...... மிளையோனே","அருணமணி வெயில்பரவு பத்துத் திக்குமிகு","மழகுபொதி மதர்மகுட தத்தித் தத்திவளர்","அணியகய லுகளும்வயல் அத்திப் பட்டிலுறை ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet pictures death: sense gone, the life leaves, kin cry, drums beat, the body is set on a golden bier, laid on the pyre with rice from flower hands and cloth, the Vedas recited, and burned in crackling fire; the mourners bathe, go home and forget their grief, and wicked Kama with his flowers makes them laugh and weep again. Thinking of his servant, worn out in women's hands, the poet asks Murugan to tell him 'this is the way to heaven' and give the golden feet. The praise names Murugan the son of the mountain-king's daughter, the green peacock who did her hard penance at Kanchi, the nephew of Vishnu who shot Ravana's ten heads and twenty arms, the one on the emerald peacock who dried the sea and resettled the gods, at Aththippattu of fields where kayal fish leap.","n":"The funeral is told rite by rite (அரிசி இட்டு, கலை, மறைகள், புனல் முழுகி மனை புக்கு), and the turn is that the mourners' grief ends at the bath and Kama takes them again. கச்சியினில் அரிய தவம்: Parvati's penance at Kanchi. The line on the mourners (உறவுநிலை சுட்டுச் சுட்டி) is hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["saakkaadu","nilaiyaamai","mayal","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:191"},"p:896":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":896,"t":["தொக்கைக்கழு விப்பொற் றகுமுடை","சுற்றிக்கல னிட்டுக் கடிதரு","சொக்குப் புலியப்பிப் புகழுறு ...... களியாலே","சுத்தத்தைய கற்றிப் பெரியவர்","சொற்றப்பிய கத்தைப் புரிபுல","சுற்றத்துட னுற்றிப் புவியிடை ...... யலையாமல்","முக்குற்றம கற்றிப் பலகலை","கற்றுப்பிழை யற்றுத் தனையுணர்","முத்தர்க்கடி மைப்பட் டிலகிய ...... அறிவாலே","முத்தித்தவ சுற்றுக் கதியுறு","சத்தைத்தெரி சித்துக் கரையகல்","முத்திப்புண ரிக்குட் புகவர ...... மருள்வாயே","திக்கெட்டும டக்கிக் கடவுள","ருக்குப்பணி கற்பித் தருளறு","சித்தத்தொட டுத்துப் படைகொடு ...... பொருசூரர்","செச்சைப்புய மற்றுப் புகவொரு","சத்திப்படை விட்டுச் சுரர்பதி","சித்தத்துயர் கெட்டுப் பதிபெற ...... அருள்வோனே","அக்கைப்புனை கொச்சைக் குறமகள்","அச்சத்தையொ ழித்துக் கரிவரும்","அத்தத்தி லழைத்துப் பரிவுட ...... னணைவோனே","அப்பைப்பிறை யைக்கட் டியசடை","அத்தர்க்கரு மைப்புத் திரவிரி","அத்திக்கரை யிச்சித் துறைதரு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks not to wander on earth washing and decking the skin with gold and scent, proud, leaving purity and doing what the elders' words forbid among mean kin, but to remove the three faults, learn many arts without error, and, by the knowledge gained in serving the freed ones who know themselves, see the Real and enter the shoreless sea of release. The praise names the one shakti that pierced the Suras who had made the eight directions and the gods serve them, so that Indra's grief ended and he regained his city, Murugan who called the frightened kura girl when the elephant came and embraced her, and the dear son of Siva with water and the crescent in his hair, at Aththikkarai.","n":"முக்குற்றம் அகற்றி: the three faults are traditionally desire, anger and delusion. தனையுணர் முத்தர்க்கு அடிமைப்பட்டு இலகிய அறிவாலே: knowledge comes through service to the freed, not by study alone. கரையகல் முத்திப்புணரி: release as a sea without a shore. கரி வரும் அத்தத்தில் அழைத்து: the elephant that frightened Valli into Murugan's arms.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["veedu","adiyaar","kalvi","kutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:192"},"p:897":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":897,"t":["விந்துபே தித்தவடி வங்களா யெத்திசையு","மின்சரா சர்க்குலமும் ...... வந்துலாவி","விண்டுபோய் விட்டவுடல் சிந்தைதா னுற்றறியு","மிஞ்சநீ விட்டவடி ...... வங்களாலே","வந்துநா யிற்கடைய னொந்துஞா னப்பதவி","வந்துதா இக்கணமெ ...... யென்றுகூற","மைந்தர்தா விப்புகழ தந்தைதா யுற்றுருகி","வந்துசே யைத்தழுவல் ...... சிந்தியாதோ","அந்தகா ரத்திலிடி யென்பவாய் விட்டுவரு","மங்கிபார் வைப்பறையர் ...... மங்கிமாள","அங்கைவேல் விட்டருளி யிந்த்ரலோ கத்தின்மகிழ்","அண்டரே றக்கிருபை ...... கொண்டபாலா","எந்தனா விக்குதவு சந்த்ரசேர் வைச்சடையர்","எந்தைபா கத்துறையு ...... மந்தமாது","எங்குமாய் நிற்குமொரு கந்தனூர் சத்திபுகழ்","எந்தைபூ சித்துமகிழ் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of the forms divided from bindu, moving and unmoving lives in every direction, through which he has come and wandered, and of the body that falls away; when he, lower than a dog, cries in pain 'come and give me the state of jnana this instant', he asks whether Murugan will not think of him as a father and mother melt and embrace the child who leaps toward them. The praise names the vel that destroyed the fire-eyed ones who come roaring like thunder in the dark, so the gods returned to Indra's world, and Murugan the son of Siva with the moon in his hair, who helps the poet's life, with the Mother in his half who stands everywhere, at Kandanur.","n":"மைந்தர் தாவிப் புகழ தந்தை தாய் உற்றுருகி வந்து சேயைத் தழுவல்: grace pictured as parents running to the child who leaps toward them, a tender image rare in the book. விந்து பேதித்த வடிவங்கள்: births as forms differentiated from bindu. The first quatrain's syntax (மிஞ்ச நீ விட்ட வடிவங்களாலே) is uncertain, and the fire-eyed ones may be asuras or Death's servants.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","vindhu","gnaanam","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:193"},"p:898":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":898,"t":["ஈயெ றும்புநரி நாய்க ணங்கழுகு","காக முண்பவுட லேசு மந்துஇது","ஏல்வ தென்றுமத மேமொ ழிந்துமத ...... வும்பல்போலே","ஏது மென்றனிட §ர்¡லெ னும்பரிவு","மேவி நம்பியிது போது மென்கசில","ரேய்த னங்கள்தனி வாகு சிந்தைவச ...... னங்கள்பேசிச்","சீத தொங்கலழ காவ ணிந்துமணம்","வீச மங்கையர்க ளாட வெண்கவரி","சீற கொம்புகுழ லு¡த தண்டிகையி ...... லந்தமாகச்","சேர்க னம்பெரிய வாழ்வு கொண்டுழலு","மாசை வெந்திடவு னாசை மிஞ்சிசிவ","சேவை கண்டுனது பாத தொண்டனென ...... அன்புதாராய்","சூதி ருந்தவிடர் மேயி ருண்டகிரி","சூரர் வெந்துபொடி யாகி மங்கிவிழ","சூரி யன்புரவி தேர்ந டந்துநடு ...... பங்கினோடச்","சோதி யந்தபிர மாபு ரந்தரனு","மாதி யந்தமுதல் தேவ ருந்தொழுது","சூழ மன்றில்நட மாடு மெந்தைமுத ...... லன்புகூர","வாது கொண்டவுணர் மாள செங்கையயி","லேவி யண்டர்குடி யேற விஞ்சையர்கள்","மாதர் சிந்தைகளி கூர நின்றுநட ...... னங்கொள்வோனே","வாச கும்பதன மானை வந்துதினை","காவல் கொண்டமுரு காஎ ணும்பெரிய","வாலி கொண்டபுர மர்ந்துவளர் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes those who carry a body that flies, ants, foxes, dogs, ghouls, vultures and crows will eat, yet call it fitting and speak arrogantly like a rutting elephant, boasting of their wealth, arms and minds, wearing cool garlands among dancing women, white chowries, horns and flutes, riding in palanquins to the end; he asks that the desire for that great life burn away, that desire for Murugan grow, and that, seeing the service of Siva, he be given love as a servant of the feet. The praise names Murugan who burned the Suras of the dark mountain of deceit, while Siva dances in the hall with Brahma, Indra and the gods worshipping, who danced in victory as the vidyadhara women rejoiced, and who came to guard the millet with Valli, at Valikondapuram.","n":"The scavenger list opens the song, so the pomp that follows (வெண்கவரி, கொம்பு குழல், தண்டிகை) is seen as carried by a corpse to be. உனது பாத தொண்டனென அன்பு தாராய்: the plea asks for love as service. Two corrupt words in line 4 (§ர்¡லெ) leave a phrase unread.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["selvam","agandhai","yaakkai","thondu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:194"},"p:899":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":899,"t":["ஆங்குடல் வளைந்து நீங்குபல் நெகிழ்ந்து","ஆய்ஞ்சுதளர் சிந்தை ...... தடுமாறி","ஆர்ந்துள கடன்கள் வாங்கவு மறிந்து","ஆண்டுபல சென்று ...... கிடையோடே","ஊங்கிருமல் வந்து வீங்குகுடல் நொந்து","ஓய்ந்துணர் வழிந்து ...... உ யிர்போமுன்","ஓங்குமயில் வந்து சேண்பெறஇ சைந்து","ஊன்றிய பதங்கள் ...... தருவாயே","வேங்கையு முயர்ந்த தீம்புன மிருந்த","வேந்திழையி னின்ப ...... மணவாளா","வேண்டுமவர் தங்கள் பூண்டபத மிஞ்ச","வேண்டிய பதங்கள் ...... புரிவோனே","மாங்கனி யுடைந்து தேங்கவயல் வந்து","மாண்புநெல் விளைந்த ...... வளநாடா","மாந்தர்வ ரும்பர் கோன்பரவி நின்ற","மாந்துறை யமர்ந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet pictures the body bent, the teeth loose, the mind faltering, years passing on the bed, a cough, a swollen aching belly and sense fading, and asks that before life goes Murugan come on the high peacock and give his firmly planted feet. The praise names Murugan the bridegroom of Valli in the sweet millet field with its vengai trees, who grants those who seek him states beyond what they hold, and the lord of the fertile land where ripe mangoes burst and flow into the paddy fields, at Manthurai where men and the king of the gods praise him.","n":"The decline is told in a short run of verbs (வளைந்து, நெகிழ்ந்து, தடுமாறி, நொந்து, ஓய்ந்து, அழிந்து). ஆர்ந்துள கடன்கள் வாங்கவும் அறிந்து may mean knowing how to collect what is owed, the old man's mind still on debts; the reading is uncertain. மாங்கனி உடைந்து தேங்க: a play on the shrine's name, mango ford.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","ilamai","saakkaadu","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:195"},"p:900":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":900,"t":["அரிமரு கோனே நமோவென் றறுதியி லானே நமோவென்","றறுமுக வேளே நமோவென் ...... றுனபாதம்","அரகர சேயே நமோவென் றிமையவர் வாழ்வே நமோவென்","றருண செர்ருபா நமோவென் ...... றுளதாசை","பரிபுர பாதா சுரேசன் றருமக ணாதா வராவின்","பகைமயில் வேலா யுதாடம் ...... பரநாளும்","பகர்தலி லாதாளை யேதுஞ் சிலதறி யாவேழை நானுன்","பதிபசு பாசோப தேசம் ...... பெறவேணும்","கரதல சூலாயு தாமுன் சலபதி போலார வாரங்","கடினசு ராபான சாமுண் ...... டியுமாடக்","கரிபரி மேலேறு வானுஞ் செயசெய சேனா பதீயென்","களமிசை தானேறி யேயஞ் ...... சியசூரன்","குரல்விட நாய்பேய்கள் பூதங் கழுகுகள் கோமாயு காகங்","குடல்கொள வேபூச லாடும் ...... பலதோளா","குடதிசை வாராழி போலும் படர்நதி காவேரி சூழுங்","குளிர்வய லு¡ரார மேவும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet sets out a litany of homage (நமோ), to Hari's nephew, the one without end, the six-faced Vel, Hara's son, the life of the gods, and says his desire is for the feet; he calls himself a poor man who knows nothing and asks to receive Murugan's teaching of pati, pasu and pasam. The praise shows Chamundi drunk and roaring like the sea as she dances, the rider of elephant and horse hailing Murugan 'victory, victory, commander', and dogs, ghouls, vultures, jackals and crows feasting on the field where Sura fell, at Vayalur by the Kaveri.","n":"பதி பசு பாச உபதேசம் is the Saiva Siddhanta triad named outright as the content of the teaching asked for. ஆடம்பர நாளும் பகர்தல் இலாது may contrast showy daily recitation with the poor man's plea, but the division of that run of syllables is uncertain. The rider of elephant and horse who hails the commander is probably Indra.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["pathi","paasam","guru","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:196"},"p:901":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":901,"t":["ஆரமுலை காட்டி மாரநிலை காட்டி","யாடையணி காட்டி ...... அநுராக","ஆலவிழி காட்டி ஓசைமொழி காட்டி","ஆதரவு காட்டி ...... எவரோடும்","ஈரநகை காட்டி நேரமிகை காட்டி","யேவினைகள் காட்டி ...... யுறவாடி","ஏதமயல் காட்டு மாதர்வலை காட்டி","யீடழிதல் காட்ட ...... லமையாதோ","வீரவப ராட்டு சூரர்படை காட்டில்","வீழனலை யூட்டி ...... மயிலு¡ர்தி","வேலையுறை நீட்டி வேலைதனி லோட்டு","வேலைவிளை யாட்டு ...... வயலு¡ரா","சேரமலை நாட்டில் வாரமுடன் வேட்ட","சீலிகுற வாட்டி ...... மணவாளா","தேசுபுகழ் தீட்டி யாசைவரு கோட்டி","தேவர்சிறை மீட்ட ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"With the refrain காட்டி ('showing'), the poet lists what women show: breasts, Kama's state, clothes and jewels, alluring eyes, sweet voice, feigned affection to anyone, wet smiles and their arts, until they show the net of their snare; he asks whether that showing of ruin is not enough. The praise plays on வேலை (vel, sea, sport): Murugan fed fire to the forest of Sura's armies, rides the peacock, sent the vel into the sea, wed the virtuous hunter girl of the hill country, and freed the gods, at Vayalur.","n":"The song is built on one verb: the women 'show' everything, and the plea turns the verb on the poet's own ruin (ஈடழிதல் காட்டல் அமையாதோ), which may mean 'is it not enough to have shown me my ruin?' or 'will this showing of ruin not stop?'. சேர மலை நாட்டில் may place Valli in the Chera hill country or simply mean the hill land where they gather.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["kaamam","mayal","vanjam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:196"},"p:902":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":902,"t":["இகல்கடின முகபடவி சித்ரத்து திக்கைமத","மத்தக்க ளிற்றையெதிர்","புளகதன மிளகஇனி தெட்டிக்க ழுத்தொடுகை","கட்டிப்பி ணித்திறுகி","யிதழ்பொதியி னமுதுமுறை மெத்தப்பு சித்துருகி","முத்தத்தை யிட்டுநக ...... தந்தமான","இடுகுறியும் வரையையுற நெற்றித்த லத்திடையில்","எற்றிக்க லக்கமுற","இடைதுவள வுடைகழல இட்டத்த ரைப்பையது","தொட்டுத்தி ரித்துமிக","இரணமிடு முரணர்விழி யொக்கக்க றுத்தவிழி","செக்கச்சி வக்கவளை ...... செங்கைசோர","அகருவிடு ம்ருகமதம ணத்துக்க னத்தபல","கொத்துக்கு ழற்குலைய","மயில்புறவு குயில்ஞிமிறு குக்கிற்கு ரற்பகர","நெக்குக்க ருத்தழிய","அமளிபெரி தமளிபட வக்கிட்டு மெய்க்கரண","வர்க்கத்தி னிற்புணரு ...... மின்பவேலை","அலையின்விழி மணியின்வலை யிட்டுப்பொ ருட்கவர","கட்டுப்பொ றிச்சியர்கள்","மதனகலை விதனமறு வித்துத்தி ருப்புகழை","யுற்றுத்து திக்கும்வகை","அபரிமித சிவஅறிவு சிக்குற்று ணர்ச்சியினில்","ரக்ஷ¢த்த ளித்தருள்வ ...... தெந்தநாளோ","திகுடதிகு தகுடதகு திக்குத்தி குத்திகுட","தத்தித்த ரித்தகுட","செகணசெக சகணசக செக்கச் செகச்செகண","சத்தச்ச கச்சகண","திகுதிகுர்தி தகுதகுர்த திக்குத்தி குத்திகுர்தி","தக்குத்த குத்தகுர்த ...... திங்குதீதோ","திரிரிதிரி தரிரிதரி தித்தித்தி ரித்திரிரி","தத்தித்த ரித்தரிரி","டிகுடடகு டகுடடிகு டிட்டிட்டி குட்டிகுடி","டட்டட்ட குட்டகுட","தெனதிமிர்த தவில்மிருக டக்கைத்தி ரட்சலிகை","பக்கக்க ணப்பறைத ...... வண்டைபேரி","வகைவகையின் மிகவதிர வுக்ரத்த ரக்கர்படை","பக்கத்தி னிற்சரிய","எழுதுதுகில் முழுதுலவி பட்டப்ப கற்பருதி","விட்டத்த மித்ததென","வருகுறளி பெருகுகுரு திக்குட்கு ளித்துழுது","தொக்குக்கு னிப்புவிட ...... வென்றவேலா","வயலிநகர் பயில்குமர பத்தர்க்க நுக்ரகவி","சித்ரப்ர சித்தமுறு","அரிமருக அறுமுகவ முக்கட்க ணத்தர்துதி","த்தவத்தி றச்சிகர","வடகுவடில் நடனமிடு மப்பர்க்கு முத்திநெறி","தப்பற்று ரைக்கவல ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"In a long, dense rhythm the poet describes lovemaking in detail, nails, kisses, loosened hair and bird-cries on the bed, and the women who cast the net of their eyes to seize wealth; he asks when Murugan will cut away the grief of Kama's arts and protect him, held in boundless Siva-knowledge, so that he praises in Thiruppugazh. After a battle in drum syllables, the praise names the Kumara of Vayali, Hari's nephew, the six-faced one, and the lord who could tell without error the path of release to the father who dances on the northern peak.","n":"பொருட் கவர கட்டுப் பொறிச்சியர்கள், 'trappers who bind to seize wealth', marks the women as courtesans. The remedy is the book itself: திருப்புகழை உற்றுத் துதிக்கும் வகை. வடகுவடில் நடனமிடும் அப்பர்க்கு முத்திநெறி தப்பற்று உரைக்க is Murugan as Siva's teacher, here of the path to mukti rather than of Om.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["varaivinmagalir","punarchi","pugazh","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:196"},"p:903":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":903,"t":["இலகு முலைவிலை யசடிகள் கசடிகள்","கலைகள் பலவறி தெருளிகள் மருளிகள்","எயிறு கடிபடு முதடிகள் பதடிகள் ...... எவரோடும்","இனிய நயமொழி பழகிக ளழகிகள்","மடையர் பொருள்பெற மருவிகள் சருவிகள்","யமனு மிகையென வழிதரு முழிதரும் ...... விழிவாளால்","உ லக மிடர்செயு நடலிகள் மடலிகள்","சிலுகு சிலரொடு புகலிக ளிகலிகள்","உ றவு சொலவல துரகிகள் விரகிகள் ...... பிறைபோலே","உ கிர்கை குறியிடு கமுகிகள் சமுகிகள்","பகடி யிடவல கபடிகள் முகடிகள்","உ ணர்வு கெடும்வகை பருவிக ளுருவிக ...... ளுறவாமோ","அலகை புடைபட வருவன பொருவன","கலக கணநிரை நகுவன தகவன","அசுரர் தசைவழி நிமிர்வன திமிர்வன ...... பொடியாடி","அலரி குடதிசை யடைவன குடைவன","தரும வநிதையு மகிழ்வன புகழ்வன","அகில புவனமு மரகர கரவென ...... அமர்வேள்வி","திலக நுதலுமை பணிவரு செயமகள்","கலையி னடமிட வெரிவிரி முடியினர்","திரள்ப லுயிருடல் குவடுக ளெனநட ...... மயிலேறிச்","சிறிது பொழுதினி லயில்விடு குருபர","அறிவு நெறியுள அறுமுக இறையவ","த்ரிசிரி கிரியயல் வயலியி லினிதுறை ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet heaps up epithets for courtesans in rhyming -igal endings: they price their breasts, know many arts, bite lips, join fools for their money, carry eyes like swords that even Yama would find excessive, torment the world, speak of kinship and deceive, and he asks whether they are fit for kinship. The praise is a battlefield of ghouls and demons where Uma bows and the goddess of victory dances, and names Murugan the guru who threw the vel in a moment, lord of the way of knowledge, at Vayali near Trisirapuram.","n":"மடையர் பொருள் பெற மருவிகள், those who join fools to get their money, and உறவு சொலவல துரகிகள் விரகிகள், skilled at talk of kinship and deceit, are the stock charges. The rhyme-chain (அசடிகள் கசடிகள் ... கபடிகள் முகடிகள்) is itself the rhetoric: abuse by sound. த்ரிசிரி கிரி is Trichy's rock, which places Vayali.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:196"},"p:904":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":904,"t":["என்னால் பிறக்கவும் என்னா லிறக்கவும்","என்னால் துதிக்கவும் ...... கண்களாலே","என்னா லழைக்கவும் என்னால் நடக்கவும்","என்னா லிருக்கவும் ...... பெண்டிர்வீடு","என்னால் சுகிக்கவும் என்னால் முசிக்கவும்","என்னால் சலிக்கவும் ...... தொந்தநோயை","என்னா லெரிக்கவும் என்னால் நினைக்கவும்","என்னால் தரிக்கவும் ...... இங்குநானார்","கன்னா ருரித்தஎன் மன்னா எனக்குநல்","கர்ணா மிர்தப்பதம் ...... தந்தகோவே","கல்லார் மனத்துட னில்லா மனத்தவ","கண்ணா டியிற்றடம் ...... கண்டவேலா","மன்னான தக்கனை முன்னாள்மு டித்தலை","வன்வாளி யிற்கொளும் ...... தங்க்ருபன்","மன்னா குறத்தியின் மன்னா வயற்பதி","மன்னா முவர்க்கொரு ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether it is by him that he is born and dies, praises, calls, walks, sits, enjoys women's houses, tires, burns the disease of attachment, thinks and bears, and ends: who am I here? The praise names Murugan the king who drew fibre from stone, the lord who gave him the word that is nectar to the ear, the vel seen as in a mirror, not dwelling in the minds of the unlearned, son of the gracious Siva who destroyed Daksha, king of the hunter girl and of Vayal town, the one lord over the three.","n":"The repeated என்னால் ('by me') ends in இங்கு நான் ஆர், 'who am I here?': agency is taken from the self, and even the visits to women are listed among acts not the poet's own. கல் நார் உரித்த, drawing fibre from stone, is the softening of a stony heart. கர்ணாமிர்தப் பதம் தந்த கோவே points to the word given the poet at Vayalur. Whether the question is surrender or bewilderment is not said.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","agandhai","guru","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:196"},"p:905":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":905,"t":["கடல்போற் கணைவிழி சிலைபோற் பிறைநுதல்","கனிபோற் றுகிரிதழ் ...... எழிலாகும்","கரிபோற் கிரிமுலை கொடிபோற் றுடியிடை","கடிபோற் பணியரை ...... யெனவாகும்","உ டல்காட் டினிமையி லெழில்பாத் திரமிவ","ளுடையாற் கெறுவித ...... நடையாலும்","ஒருநாட் பிரிவது மரிதாய்ச் சுழல்படும்","ஒழியாத் துயரது ...... தவிரேனோ","குடலீர்த் தசுரர்க ளுடல்காக் கைகள்நரி","கொளிவாய்ப் பலஅல ...... கைகள்பேய்கள்","கொலைபோர்க் களமிசை தினமேற் றமரர்கள்","குடியேற் றியகுக ...... வுயர்தாழை","மடல்கீற் றினிலெழு விரைபூப் பொழில்செறி","வயலு¡ர்ப் பதிதனி ...... லுறைவோனே","மலைமேற் குடியுறை கொடுவேட் டுவருடை","மகள்மேற் ப்ரியமுள ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet compares a woman's parts, eyes to the sea and arrows, brow to bow and crescent, lips to fruit and coral, breasts to elephant and hill, waist to creeper and drum, hip to a serpent's hood, and says that by her body, dress and proud walk he whirls in a sorrow where even one day apart is hard; he asks whether he will not escape it. The praise has crows, jackals and ghouls feeding on the demons whose guts were torn out, the gods resettled, and Murugan who loves the daughter of the fierce hill hunters, at Vayalur among fragrant screwpine groves.","n":"The plea names a lover's grief rather than a courtesan's fraud: ஒருநாள் பிரிவது அரிதாய்ச் சுழல்படும் ஒழியாத் துயர், the unending sorrow of one who cannot bear a day's parting. எழில் பாத்திரம் இவள், 'she is a vessel of beauty', gives the woman one direct line.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kaamam","azhagu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:196"},"p:906":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":906,"t":["கமலத் தேகு லாவு மரிவையை","நிகர்பொற் கோல மாதர் மருள்தரு","கலகக் காம நூலை முழுதுண ...... ரிளைஞோர்கள்","கலவிக் காசை கூர வளர்பரி","மளகற் பூர தூம கனதன","கலகத் தாலும் வானி னழையுமி ...... னிடையாலும்","விமலச் சோதி ரூப இமகர","வதனத் தாலு நாத முதலிய","விரவுற் றாறு கால்கள் சுழலிருள் ...... குழலாலும்","வெயிலெப் போதும் வீசு மணிவளை","அணிபொற் றோள்க ளாலும் வடுவகிர்","விழியிற் பார்வை யாலு மினியிடர் ...... படுவேனோ","சமரிற் பூதம் யாளி பரிபிணி","கனகத் தேர்கள் யானை யவுணர்கள்","தகரக் கூர்கொள் வேலை விடுதிற ...... லு§வோனே","சமுகப் பேய்கள் வாழி யெனஎதிர்","புகழக் கானி லாடு பரிபுர","சரணத் தேக வீர அமைமன ...... மகிழ்வீரா","அமரர்க் கீச னான சசிபதி","மகள்மெய்த் தோயு நாத குறமகள்","அணையச் சூழ நீத கரமிசை ...... யுறுவேலா","அருளிற் சீர்பொ யாத கணபதி","திருவக் கீசன் வாழும் வயலியின்","அழகுக் கோயில் மீதில மருவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes golden women like Lakshmi in the lotus, whose camphor-scented breasts, lightning waists, moon faces, dark hair circled by humming bees, bangled shoulders and mango-slice eyes bewilder young men versed in the whole of Kama's quarrelsome lore, and asks whether he must suffer again through them. The praise names the vel that shattered demons, chariots and elephants, the hero praised by ghouls in the forest, the lord of Indra's daughter and the hunter girl, at the beautiful temple of Vayali where the Ganapati of unfailing grace and the holy Lord also dwell.","n":"கலகக் காம நூலை முழுதுணர் இளைஞோர்: the young men are learned in the manual of love, so learning itself is part of the trap. அருளில் சீர் பொய்யாத கணபதி and திருவக்கீசன் point to the shrine's own deities: Vayalur has a Ganapati called Poyyā Ganapati and a Siva called Agnisvara, which is my identification from the names.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kaamam","azhagu","mayal"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:196"},"p:907":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":907,"t":["கமையற்ற சீர்கேடர் வெகுதர்க்க கோலாலர்","களையுற்று மாயாது ...... மந்த்ரவாதக்","கடைகெட்ட ஆபாத முறுசித்ர கோமாளர்","கருமத்தின் மாயாது ...... கொண்டுபூணுஞ்","சமயத்த ராசார நியமத்தின் மாயாது","சகளத்து ளேநாளு ...... நண்புளோர்செய்","சரியைக்ரி யாயோக நியமத்தின் மாயாது","சலனப்ப டாஞானம் ...... வந்துதாராய்","அமரிற்சு ராபான திதிபுத்ர ராலோக","மதுதுக்க மேயாக ...... மிஞ்சிடாமல்","அடமிட்ட வேல்வீர திருவொற்றி யுர்நாதர்","அருணச்சி காநீல ...... கண்டபார","மமபட்ச மாதேவ ரருமைச்சு வாமீநி","மலநிட்க ளாமாயை ...... விந்துநாதம்","வரசத்தி மேலான பரவத்து வேமேலை","வயலிக்குள் வாழ்தேவர் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks not to perish among impatient men of endless argument, among those who deal in mantra-magic, in the ritual works and rules of conduct that sectarians take up, or even in the chariyai, kiriyai and yoga practised by those devoted to the embodied Lord, but to be given jnana that does not waver. The praise names Murugan as the vel that ended the Diti-born demons' torment of the world, the precious lord of Siva of Thiruvotriyur with the red flame and blue throat, and the supreme beyond maya, bindu, nada and shakti, the god of Mélai Vayali.","n":"The list descends from quarrelling logicians to sectarians and then to the Saiva paths themselves: சரியை க்ரியா யோக நியமத்தின் மாயாது. The first three paths, usually steps toward jnana, become places one can be lost in. சலனப்படா ஞானம், knowledge that does not waver, is set against all of them. மாயை விந்து நாதம் வரசத்தி மேலான பரவத்து puts Murugan above the tattvas of the Siddhanta scheme.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","samayam","sariyai","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:196"},"p:908":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":908,"t":["குருதி கிருமிகள் சலமல மயிர்தசை","மருவு முருவமு மலமல மழகொடு","குலவு பலபணி பரிமள மறசுவை ...... மடைபாயல்","குளிரி லறையக மிவைகளு மலமல","மனைவி மகவனை யநுசர்கள் முறைமுறை","குனகு கிளைஞர்க ளிவர்களு மலமல ...... மொருநாலு","சுருதி வழிமொழி சிவகலை யலதினி","யுலக கலைகளு மலமல மிலகிய","தொலைவி லுனைநினை பவருற வலதினி ...... யயலார்பால்","சுழல்வ தினிதென வசமுடன் வழிபடு","முறவு மலமல மருளலை கடல்கழி","துறைசெ லறிவினை யெனதுள மகிழ்வுற ...... அருள்வாயே","விருது முரசுகள் மொகுமொகு மொகுவென","முகுற ககபதி முகில்திகழ் முகடதில்","விகட இறகுகள் பறையிட அலகைகள் ...... நடமாட","விபுத ரரகர சிவசிவ சரணென","விரவு கதிர்முதி ரிமகரன் வலம்வர","வினைகொள் நிசிசரர் பொடிபட அடல்செயும் ...... வடிவேலா","மருது நெறுநெறு நெறுவென முறிபட","வுருளு முரலொடு தவழரி மருகசெ","வனச மலர்சுனை புலிநுழை முழையுடை ...... யவிராலி","மலையி லுறைகிற அறுமுக குருபர","கயலு மயிலையு மகரமு முகள்செநெல்","வயலி நகரியி லிறையவ அருள்தரு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"With the refrain அலமல ('enough, enough'), the poet renounces the body of blood, worms, water, filth, hair and flesh, its ornaments, perfumes, six-flavoured food and bed, wife, children, mother and kin, all worldly learning but the Siva-lore of the four Vedas, and every tie but with those who think of the Lord; he asks for the knowledge that reaches the shore of the sea of grace. The praise has drums, ghouls and gods crying 'Hara, Siva', names Murugan the nephew of the child who crawled with the mortar and broke the marutha trees, and places him at Viralimalai with its tiger caves and at Vayali where carp leap in the paddy.","n":"The renunciation is ordered from the body outward to food and bed, kin and learning, and keeps only one tie, உனை நினைபவர் உறவு, kinship with those who think of the Lord. The infant Krishna dragging the mortar between the marutha trees (மருது முறிபட உருளும் உரலொடு தவழ்) is told in its unmistakable details.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pattru","yaakkai","thuravu","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:196"},"p:909":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":909,"t":["குயிலோமொழி அயிலோவிழி கொடியோஇடை பிடியோநடை","குறியீர்தனி செறியீரினி ...... யென்றுபாடிக்","குனகாவடி பிடியாவிதழ் கடியாநகம் வகிராவுடை","குலையாவல்கு லளையாவிரு ...... கொங்கைமீதிற்","பயிலாமன மகிழ்மோகித சுகசாகர மடமாதர்கள்","பகையேயென நினையாதுற ...... நண்புகூரும்","பசுபாசமு மகிலாதிக பரிபூரண புரணாகர","பதிநேருநி னருளால் மெயு ...... ணர்ந்திடேனோ","வெயில்வீசிய கதிராயிர வருணோதய விருணாசன","விசையேழ்பரி ரவிசேயெனு ...... மங்கராசன்","விசிகாகவ மயல்பேடிகை படுபோதுசன் னிதியானவன்","விதிதேடிய திருவாளிய ...... ரன்குமாரா","அயலு¡ருறை மயிலாபல கலைமானுழை புலிதோல்களை","யகிலாரம தெறிகாவிரி ...... வண்டல்மேவும்","அதிமோகர வயலு¡ர்மிசை திரிசேவக முருகேசுர","அமராபதி யதில்வாழ்பவர் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet gives the questions of courtship, 'is that a cuckoo's voice, a spear for eyes, a creeper waist?', and the lovemaking with women in their sea of pleasure whom he befriends instead of thinking them enemies, and asks whether, by the grace of Murugan who equals the Pati above pasu and pasam, he will not know the truth. The praise is obscure: it seems to recall Karna, king of Anga and son of the sun, facing the one in a eunuch's guise, and names Murugan the son of Hara and the peacock at Vayalur where the Kaveri carries akil and sandal, lord of the gods of Amaravati.","n":"பகையே என நினையாது உற நண்பு கூரும்: the error is taking enemies for friends. The plea asks for மெய் உணர்தல, knowing the real, through the Pati of the Siddhanta triad. இரவிசேய் எனும் அங்கராசன் is Karna; மயல் பேடிகை may be Arjuna disguised as Brihannala, but how the lines join is not clear to me.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["kaamam","punarchi","meyyunarvu","pathi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:196"},"p:910":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":910,"t":["கோவை வாயி தழுக்குந் தாக போக மளிக்குங்","கோதை மாதர் முலைக்குங் ...... குறியாலும்","கோல மாலை வளைக்குந் தோளி னாலு மணத்தங்","கோதி வாரி முடிக்குங் ...... குழலாலும்","ஆவி கோடி யவிக்குஞ் சேலி னாலு மயக்குண்","டாசை யாயி னுநித்தந் ...... தளராதே","ஆசி லாத மறைக்குந் தேடொ ணாதொரு வர்க்கொன்","றாடல் தாள்க ளெனக்கின் ...... றருள்வாயே","சேவி லேறு நிருத்தன் தோகை பாக னளிக்குந்","த்யாக சீல குணத்தன் ...... திருமாலும்","தேடொ ணாத பதத்தன் தீதி லாத மனத்தன்","தேயு வான நிறத்தன் ...... புதல்வோனே","காவி டாத திருச்செங் கோடு நாடு தனக்குங்","காவி சூழ் வயலிக்கும் ...... ப்ரியமானாய்","காதி மோதி யெதிர்க்குஞ் சூர தீரர் ப்ரமிக்குங்","கால னாடல் தவிர்க்கும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of women's kovai-fruit lips, breasts, garland-bearing shoulders, combed and scented hair and carp-like eyes that wither a crore of lives, and asks that, deluded, he not wear himself out daily but be given today the dancing feet that even the faultless Veda cannot find. The praise names Siva, dancer on the bull, consort of the peacock-like Uma, whose feet Vishnu could not find, of fire's colour, as Murugan's father, says Murugan loves Thiruchengodu and Vayali among the lilies, and calls him the one who stops Death's play.","n":"The feet asked for are ஆடல் தாள்கள், dancing feet, and the Veda cannot reach them (ஆசிலாத மறைக்கும் தேடொணாது), as Vishnu could not find Siva's feet in the praise: the plea and the praise share one motif.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:196"},"p:911":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":911,"t":["தாமரையின் மட்டு வாசமல ரொத்த","தாளிணை நினைப்பி ...... லடியேனைத்","தாதவிழ் கடுக்கை நாகமகிழ் கற்ப","தாருவென மெத்தி ...... யவிராலி","மாமலையி னிற்ப நீகருதி யுற்று","வாவென அழைத்தென் ...... மனதாசை","மாசினை யறுத்து ஞானமு தளித்த","வாரமினி நித்த ...... மறவேனே","காமனை யெரித்த தீநயன நெற்றி","காதிய சுவர்க்க ...... நதிவேணி","கானிலுறை புற்றி லாடுபணி யிட்ட","காதுடைய அப்பர் ...... குருநாதா","சோமனொ டருக்கன் மீனுலவு மிக்க","சோலைபுடை சுற்று ...... வயலு¡ரா","சூடிய தடக்கை வேல்கொடு விடுத்து","சூர்தலை துணித்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet recalls how Murugan, intending him for his lotus-like feet, came to him on the great hill of Viralimalai crowded with konrai and magizh trees, called 'come', cut away the stain of his mind's desire and gave him the nectar of jnana, and vows never to forget that love. The praise names Murugan the guru of Siva, who burned Kama with his forehead eye, bears the heavenly river in his hair and wears a forest snake as an earring, and the lord of Vayalur, whose vel cut off Sura's head.","n":"This is autobiography, not confession: வா என அழைத்து என் மனது ஆசை மாசினை அறுத்து ஞானமுது அளித்த வாரம் இனி நித்தம் மறவேனே. The call is placed at Viralimalai (அவிராலி மாமலை), though the song is sung at Vayalur, which tradition makes the place of the first call. The pledge is gratitude, not a request.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["thiruvarul","gnaanam","nanri","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:196"},"p:912":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":912,"t":["திருவு ரூப நேராக அழக தான மாமாய","திமிர மோக மானார்கள் ...... க¨ல்முடுஞ்","சிகரி யூடு தேமாலை யடவி யூடு போயாவி","செருகு மால னாசார ...... வினையேனைக்","கருவி ழாது சீரோதி யடிமை பூண லாமாறு","கனவி லாள்சு வாமீநின் ...... மயில்வாழ்வுங்","கருணை வாரி கூரேக முகமும் வீர மாறாத","கழலு நீப வேல்வாகு ...... மறவேனே","சருவ தேவ தேவாதி நமசி வாய நாமாதி","சயில நாரி பாகாதி ...... புதல்வோனே","சதம கீவல் போர்மேவு குலிச பாணி மால்யானை","சகச மான சா¡£செ ...... யிளையோனே","மருவு லோக மீரேழு மளவிடாவொ ணாவான","வரையில் வீசு தாள்மாயன் ...... மருகோனே","மநு நியாய சோணாடு தலைமை யாக வேமேலை","வயலி மீது வாழ்தேவர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet calls himself a sinner of bad conduct, his life pierced by women of beautiful form and deep delusion, and thanks Murugan who ruled him in a dream so that he would not fall into the womb but sing his glory and take up service; he vows never to forget the peacock, the sea of compassion, the faces, the hero's anklets, the kadamba and the vel. The praise names Siva, god of gods, of the name Namasivaya, half woman with the mountain's daughter, as father, Vishnu whose foot measured the fourteen worlds as uncle, and the god of Mélai Vayali in the Chola land ruled by Manu's justice.","n":"கனவில் ஆள் சுவாமீ: the Lord took the poet as his servant in a dream, a second piece of the poet's own story beside 911. கருவிழாது சீரோதி அடிமை பூணலாமாறு gives the purpose: not to fall back into the womb, to sing and to serve. The line on Indra's thunderbolt and elephant is partly corrupt, and whom it names (Devayanai, raised by Indra's elephant?) is not clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["thondu","kanavu","karu","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:196"},"p:913":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":913,"t":["நெய்த்த சுரிகுழ லறலோ முகிலோ","பத்ம நறுநுதல் சிலையோ பிறையோ","நெட்டை யிணைவிழி கணையோ பிணையோ ...... இனிதூறும்","நெக்க அமுதிதழ் கனியோ துவரோ","சுத்த மிடறது வளையோ கமுகோ","நிற்கு மிளமுலை குடமோ மலையோ ...... அறவேதேய்ந்","தெய்த்த இடையது கொடியோ துடியோ","மிக்க திருவரை அரவோ ரதமோ","இப்பொ னடியிணை மலரோ தளிரோ ...... எனமாலாய்","இச்சை விரகுடன் மடவா ருடனே","செப்ப மருளுட னவமே திரிவேன்","ரத்ந பரிபுர இருகா லொருகால் ...... மறவேனே","புத்த ரமணர்கள் மிகவே கெடவே","தெற்கு நரபதி திருநீ றிடவே","புக்க அனல்வய மிகஏ டுயவே ...... உ மையாள்தன்","புத்ர னென இசை பகர்நூல் மறைநூல்","கற்ற தவமுனி பிரமா புரம்வாழ்","பொற்ப கவுணியர் பெருமானுருவாய் ...... வருவோனே","சத்த முடையஷண் முகனே குகனே","வெற்பி லெறிசுட ரயிலா மயிலா","சத்தி கணபதி யிளையா யுளையா ...... யொளிகூருஞ்","சக்ர தரஅரி மருகா முருகா","உ க்ர இறையவர் புதல்வா முதல்வா","தட்ப முளதட வயலு¡ ரியலு¡ர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"In a run of either-or questions, the poet asks whether a woman's hair is black sand or cloud, her brow a bow or the crescent, her eyes arrows or does, her lips fruit or coral, her breasts pots or hills, her waist a creeper or a drum, her feet flowers or shoots, and confesses wandering vainly in that infatuation; he vows not to forget even once the jewelled-anklet feet. The praise makes Murugan the one who came in the form of the Kaundinya lord of Brahmapuram, learned in Tamil and Veda and praised as Uma's son, when the Buddhists and Jains were ruined, the southern king took the ash and the palm leaf survived the fire, and names him Hari's nephew and Shakti-Ganapati's younger brother, at Vayalur.","n":"The questions (அறலோ முகிலோ, சிலையோ பிறையோ) are the conventional comparisons put as a lover's doubt, so the confession is made from inside the poetry it renounces. பிரமாபுரம் வாழ் கவுணியர் பெருமான் is Sambandar of Sirkazhi of the Kaundinya clan; the ordeal by fire (அனல் ... ஏடு உயவே) and the Pandya's ash are his story, credited to Murugan.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kaamam","mayal","thamizh","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:196"},"p:914":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":914,"t":["முலைம றைக்கவும் வாசலி லேதலை","மறைய நிற்கவும் ஆசையு ளோரென","முகிழ்ந கைச்சிறு தூதினை யேவவு ...... முகமோடே","முகம ழுத்தவும் ஆசைகள் கூறவு","நகம ழுத்தவும் லீலையி லேயுற","முறைம சக்கவும் வாசமு லாமல ...... ரணைமீதே","கலைநெ கிழ்க்கவும் வாலிப ரானவர்","உ டல்ச ளப்பட நாள்வழி நாள்வழி","கறைய ழிக்கவு நானென வேயணி ...... விலையீதே","கடிய சத்திய மாமென வேசொலி","யவர்கொ டப்பண மாறிட வீறொடு","கடுக டுத்திடு வாரொடு கூடிய ...... தமையாதோ","மலையை மத்தென வாசுகி யேகடை","கயிறெ னத்திரு மாலொரு பாதியு","மருவு மற்றது வாலியு மேலிட ...... அலையாழி","வலய முட்டவொ ரோசைய தாயொலி","திமிதி மித்திமெ னாவெழ வேயலை","மறுகி டக்கடை யாவெழ மேலெழு ...... மமுதோடே","துலைவ ருத்திரு மாமயில் வாழ்வுள","வயலை யற்புத னேவினை யானவை","தொடர றுத்திடு மாரிய கேவலி ...... மணவாளா","துவள்க டிச்சிலை வேள்பகை வாதிரு","மறுவொ ரெட்டுட னாயிர மேலொரு","துகள றுத்தணி யாரழ காசுரர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes courtesans who half-hide at the door, send small messengers to men of desire, press faces and nails, loosen their garments on the flower bed and wear young men down day after day, then swear 'this is the price, this is the truth' and turn harsh when the money given runs out, and asks whether having joined them is not enough. The praise tells of the churning of the sea with the mountain as churn and Vasuki as rope, Vishnu at one end and Vali at the other, and Lakshmi rising with the nectar, and names Murugan the bridegroom of the noble one who cuts the chain of deeds, at Vayalur.","n":"விலை ஈதே கடிய சத்தியமாம் எனவே சொலி: the woman names her price and swears to it, the trade and the oath in one line. அவர் கொடப் பணம் மாறிட கடுகடுத்திடுவார், growing harsh when the money given changes, is the stock end. Vali among the churners is an unusual detail. The last lines (மறு ஒர் எட்டுடன் ஆயிரம்) are obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","selvam","vanjam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:196"},"p:915":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":915,"t":["மேகலை நெகிழ்த்துக் காட்டி வார்குழல் விரித்துக் காட்டி","வேல்விழி புரட்டிக் காட்டி ...... யழகாக","மேனியை மினுக்கிக் காட்டி நாடக நடித்துக் காட்டி","வீடுக ளழைத்துக் காட்டி ...... மதராசன்","ஆகம முரைத்துக் காட்டி வாரணி தனத்தைக் காட்டி","யாரொடு நகைத்துக் காட்டி ...... விரகாலே","ஆதர மனத்தைக் காட்டி வேசைகள் மயக்கைக் காட்ட","ஆசையை யவர்க்குக் காட்டி ...... யழிவேனோ","மோகன விருப்பைக் காட்டி ஞானமு மெடுத்துக் காட்டி","மூதமிழ் முனிக்குக் கூட்டு ...... குருநாதா","மூவுல களித்துக் காட்டி சேவலை யுயர்த்திக் காட்டு","மூரிவில் மதற்குக் காட்டு ...... வயலு¡ரா","வாகையை முடித்துக் காட்டி கானவர் சமர்த்தைக் காட்டி","வாழ்மயில் நடத்திக் காட்டு ...... மிளையோனே","மாமலை வெதுப்பிக் காட்டி தானவர் திறத்தைக் காட்டி","வானவர் சிரத்தைக் காத்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"With the refrain காட்டி ('showing'), the poet lists what courtesans show, the loosened girdle, spread hair, rolling spear eyes, shining body, staged drama, invitations to their houses, Kama's rules recited, breasts, laughter with anyone and pretended feeling, and asks whether he will perish by the desire they show. The praise uses the same verb for Murugan: he showed love, drew out and showed jnana as the guru who taught the old Tamil sage, gave the three worlds, raised the cock banner, made the peacock dance, heated the great mountain, and guarded the gods' heads, at Vayalur.","n":"The one verb carries both halves: the women 'show' to deceive (விரகாலே, வேசைகள் மயக்கைக் காட்ட), and the Lord 'shows' jnana (ஞானமும் எடுத்துக் காட்டி). மூதமிழ் முனிக்குக் கூட்டு குருநாதா is Murugan as Agastya's teacher of Tamil.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:197"},"p:916":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":916,"t":["வாளின் முனையினு நஞ்சினும் வெஞ்சம","ராஜ நடையினு மம்பதி னும்பெரு","வாதை வகைசெய்க ருங்கணு மெங்கணு ...... மரிதான","வாரி யமுதுபொ சிந்துக சிந்தசெ","வாயு நகைமுக வெண்பலு நண்புடன்","வாரு மிருமெனு மின்சொலு மிஞ்சிய ...... பனிநீருந்","தூளி படுநவ குங்கும முங்குளி","ரார மகில்புழு கும்புனை சம்ப்ரம","சோதி வளர்வன கொங்கையு மங்கையு ...... மெவரேனுந்","தோயு மளறெனி தம்பமு முந்தியு","மாயை குடிகொள்கு டம்பையுள் மன்பயில்","சூளை யரையெதிர் கண்டும ருண்டிட ...... லொழிவேனோ","காளி திரிபுரை யந்தரி சுந்தரி","நீலி கவுரிப யங்கரி சங்கரி","காரு ணியசிவை குண்டலி சண்டிகை ...... த்ரிபுராரி","காதல் மனைவிப ரம்பரை யம்பிகை","ஆதி மலைமகள் மங்கலை பிங்கலை","கான நடனமு கந்தவள் செந்திரு ...... அயன்மாது","வேளி னிரதிய ருந்ததி யிந்திர","தேவி முதல்வர்வ ணங்குத்ரி யம்பகி","மேக வடிவர்பின் வந்தவள் தந்தரு ...... ளிளையோனே","வேலு மயிலுநி னைந்தவர் தந்துயர்","தீர வருள்தரு கந்தநி ரந்தர","மேலை வயலையு கந்துள நின்றருள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says women's eyes are crueller than a sword's edge, poison, Yama's tread and arrows, and lists their nectar mouths, white smiles, sweet 'come, sit', rosewater, saffron, sandal and musk, breasts, hips and navel, the hut where maya dwells, and asks whether he will cease to be bewildered on seeing such harlots. The praise is a litany of the Goddess, Kali, Tripurai, Sundari, Nili, Gauri, Sankari, Sivai, Kundali, Chandika, Ambika, Tryambaki whom Lakshmi, Saraswati, Rati, Arundhati and Indra's queen worship, Vishnu's sister, and names Murugan her son, who removes the sorrow of those who think of the vel and peacock, at Mélai Vayalur.","n":"மாயை குடிகொள் குடம்பை, the nest where maya lives, calls the woman's body the dwelling of illusion. The praise gives most of its length to the Goddess's names, so the song is as much hers as her son's, as in 32.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","saththi","maayai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:197"},"p:917":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":917,"t":["விகட பரிமள ம்ருகமத இமசல","வகிர படிரமு மளவிய களபமு","மட்டித்தி தழ்த்தொடைமு டித்துத்தெ ருத்தலையில்","உ லவி யிளைஞர்கள் பொருளுட னுயிர்கவர்","கலவி விதவிய னரிவையர் மருள்வலை","யிட்டுத்து வக்கியிடர் பட்டுத் தியக்கியவர்","விரவு நவமணி முகபட எதிர்பொரு","பரண புளகித இளமுலை யுரமிசை","தைக்கக் கழுத்தொடுகை யொக்கப்பி ணித்திறுகி ...... யன்புகூர","விபுத ரமுதென மதுவென அறுசுவை","அபரி மிதமென இலவிதழ் முறைமுறை","துய்த்துக்க ளித்துநகம் வைத்துப்ப லிற்குறியின்","வரையு முறைசெய்து முனிவரு மனவலி","கரையு மரிசன பரிசன ப்ரியவுடை","தொட்டுக்கு லைத்துநுதல் பொட்டுப்ப டுத்திமதர்","விழிகள் குழைபொர மதிமுகம் வெயர்வெழ","மொழிகள் பதறிட ரதிபதி கலைவழி","கற்றிட்ட புட்குரல்மி டற்றிற்ப யிற்றிமடு ...... வுந்த§முழ்கிப்","புகடு வெகுவித கரணமு மருவிய","வகையின் முகிலென இருளென வனமென","ஒப்பித்த நெய்த்தபல புட்பக்கு ழற்சரிய","அமுத நிலைமல ரடிமுதல் முடிகடை","குமுத பதிகலை குறைகலை நிறைகலை","சித்தத்த ழுத்தியநு வர்க்கத்து ருக்கியொரு","பொழுதும் விடலரி தெனுமநு பவமவை","முழுது மொழிவற மருவிய கலவியி","தத்துப்ரி யப்படந டித்துத்து வட்சியினில் ...... நைந்துசோரப்","புணரு மிதுசிறு சுகமென இகபரம்","உ ணரு மறிவிலி ப்ரைமைதரு திரிமலம்","அற்றுக்க ருத்தொருமை யுற்றுப்பு லத்தலையில்","மறுகு பொறிகழல் நிறுவியெ சிறிதுமெய்","உ ணர்வு முணர்வுற வழுவற வொருஜக","வித்தைக்கு ணத்ரயமும் நிர்த்தத்து வைத்துமறை","புகலு மநுபவ வடிவினை யளவறு","அகில வெளியையு மொளியையு மறிசிவ","தத்வப்ர சித்திதனை முத்திச்சி வக்கடலை ...... யென்றுசேர்வேன்","திகுட திகுகுட திகுகுட திகுகுட","தகுட தகுகுட தகுகுட தகுகுட","திக்குத்தி குத்திகுட தக்குத்த குத்தகுட","டுமிட டுமிமிட டுமிமிட டுமிமிட","டமட டமமட டமமட டமமட","டுட்டுட்டு டுட்டுமிட டட்டட்ட டட்டமட","திகுர்தி திகுதிகு திகுகுர்தி திகுகுர்தி","தகுர்தி தகுதகு தகுகுர்தி தகுகுர்தி","திக்குத்தி குத்திகுர்தி தக்குத்த குத்தகுர்தி ...... என்றுபேரி","திமிலை கரடிகை பதலைச லரிதவில்","தமர முரசுகள் குடமுழ வொடுதுடி","சத்தக்க ணப்பறைகள் மெத்தத்தொ னித்ததிர","அசுரர் குலஅரி அமரர்கள் ஜயபதி","குசல பசுபதி குருவென விருதுகள்","ஒத்தத்தி ரட்பலவு முற்றிக்க லிக்கஎழு","சிகர கொடுமுடி கிடுகிடு கிடுவென","மகர சலநிதி மொகுமொகு மொகுவென","எட்டுத்தி சைக்களிறு மட்டற்ற றப்பிளிற ...... நின்றசேடன்","மகுட சிரதலம் நெறுநெறு நெறுவென","அகில புவனமும் ஹரஹர ஹரவென","நக்ஷத்ர முக்கிவிழ வக்கிட்ட துட்டகுண","நிருதர் தலையற வடிவெனு மலைசொரி","குருதி யருவியின் முழுகிய கழுகுகள்","பக்கப்ப ழுத்தவுடல் செக்கச்சி வத்துவிட","வயிறு சரிகுடல் நரிதின நிணமவை","எயிறு அலைகள் நெடுகிய குறளிகள்","பக்ஷ¢த்து நிர்த்தமிட ரக்ஷ¢த்த லைப்பரவி ...... யும்பர்வாழ","மடிய அவுணர்கள் குரகத கஜரத","கடக முடைபட வெடிபட எழுகிரி","அற்றுப்ப றக்கவெகு திக்குப்ப டித்துநவ","நதிகள் குழைதர இபபதி மகிழ்வுற","அமர்செய் தயில்கையில் வெயிலெழ மயில்மிசை","அக்குக்கு டக்கொடிசெ ருக்கப்பெ ருக்கமுடன்","வயலி நகருறை சரவண பவகுக","இயலு மிசைகளு நடனமும் வகைவகை","சத்யப்ப டிக்கினித கஸ்த்யர்க்கு ணர்த்தியருள் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"Over many lines the poet describes perfumed women who roam the streets and take young men's wealth with their lives, and a long scene of lovemaking, lips tasted like nectar and honey, marks of nail and teeth, the bird-cries learned from Kama's art, and the lovers' points of the moon from foot to crown, an experience 'one cannot leave even for a moment'; knowing this union a small pleasure, he asks when, the three impurities cut and the mind made one, he will reach the boundless space and light, the manifest Siva-tattva and the sea of release. The praise, after drum syllables, shakes peaks, seas, the eight elephants and the serpent Sesha, fills the field with blood, vultures and jackals, and names the Saravanabhava of Vayali who taught Agastya the Tamil of verse, music and drama.","n":"புணரும் இது சிறு சுகம்: the poet judges union a small pleasure, after describing it at length as one that 'cannot be left for a moment' (ஒரு பொழுதும் விடலரிது). The lunar kalas waxing and waning on the body (குமுத பதி கலை குறைகலை நிறைகலை) come from the love manuals. The plea runs through Siddhanta terms: திரிமலம் அற்று, கருத்து ஒருமை உற்று, சிவ தத்வ ப்ரசித்தி, முத்திச் சிவக் கடல். The division of several lines in the plea is unsure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["punarchi","malam","sivam","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:197"},"p:918":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":918,"t":["காரணி யுங்குழ லைக்கு வித்திடு","கோகன கங்கொடு மெத்தெ னப்பிறர்","காணவ ருந்திமு டித்தி டக்கடு ...... விரகாலே","காதள வுங்கய லைப்பு ரட்டிம","னாதிகள் வஞ்சமி குத்தி டப்படி","காமுக ரன்புகு வித்த கைப்பொரு ...... ளுறவாகிப்","பூரண கும்பமெ னப்பு டைத்தெழு","சீதள குங்கும மொத்த சித்திர","பூஷித கொங்கையி லுற்று முத்தணி ...... பிறையான","போருவை யொன்றுநெ கிழ்த்து ருக்கிமெய்","யாரையும் நெஞ்சைவி லைப்ப டுத்திடு","பூவையர் தங்கள்ம யக்கை விட்டிட ...... அருள்வாயே","வீரபு யங்கிரி யுக்ர விக்ரம","பூதக ணம்பல நிர்த்த மிட்டிட","வேகமு டன்பறை கொட்டி டக்கழு ...... கினமாட","வீசிய பம்பர மொப்பெ னக்களி","வீசந டஞ்செய் விடைத்த னித்துசர்","வேதப ரம்பரை யுட்க ளித்திட ...... வரும்வீரா","சீரணி யுந்திரை தத்து முத்தெறி","காவிரி யின்கரை மொத்து மெத்திய","சீர்புனை கின்றதி ருத்த வத்துறை ...... வரும்வாழ்வே","சீறியெ திர்ந்தவ ரக்க ரைக்கெட","மோதிய டர்ந்தருள் பட்ச முற்றிய","தேவர்கள் தஞ்சிறை வெட்டி விட்டருள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes courtesans who gather and tie up their dark hair for others to see, roll eyes that reach their ears, grow deceit in their minds, make the wealth in lustful men's hands their kin, bare breasts like full pots and put a price on anyone's heart, and asks for grace to leave their delusion. The praise shows ghouls dancing to drums and vultures circling, Siva on the bull dancing like a spun top, and names Murugan the hero of Thiruthavathurai on the Kaveri, who crushed the demons and cut the gods free.","n":"காமுகர் அன்பு புகுவித்த கைப்பொருள் உறவாகி: the women's kinship is with the money, not the man, and யாரையும் நெஞ்சை விலைப்படுத்திடு, pricing anyone's heart, says the trade reaches the heart itself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:198"},"p:919":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":919,"t":["நிரைத்த நித்தில நீள்மணி மாலைகள்","பொருத்த வெற்பிணை மார்முலை மேலணி","நெறித்த நெய்க்குழல் வாள்விழி மாமதி ...... முகமானார்","நெளித்த சிற்றிடை மேல்கலை ய¨டையை","யுடுத்தி யத்தமு ளோர்தமை யேமயல்","நிரப்பி நித்தமும் வீதியில் நேருறு ...... நெறியாலே","கரைத்தி தக்குயில் போல்மொழி மாதர்கள்","வலைக்கு ளிற்சுழ லாவகை யேயுன","கழற்று தித்திடு வாழ்வது தான்மன ...... துறமேவிக்","கதித்த பத்திமை சாலடி யார்சபை","மிகுத்தி ழிக்குண பாதக னேனுயர்","கதிக்க டுத்துயர் வாகவு மேயரு ...... ளுரையாதோ","வரைத்த நுக்கரர் மாதவ மேவின","ரகத்தி டத்தினில் வாழ்சிவ னார்திரு","மணிச்செ விக்குள்மெய்ஞ் ஞானம தோதிய ...... வடிவேலா","மதித்த முத்தமி ழாய்வினர் மேலவ","ருரைத்து ளத்திரு வாசக மானது","மனத்து ளெத்தழ கார்புகழ் வீசிய ...... மணிமாடத்","திரைக்க டற்பொரு காவிரி மாநதி","பெருக்கெ டுத்துமெ பாய்வள நீர்பொலி","செழித்த நெற்செநெல் வாரிக ளேகுவை ...... குவையாகச்","செருக்கு செய்ப்பதி வாழ்முரு காஅறம்","வளர்த்த நித்யகல் யாணிக்ரு பாகரி","திருத்த வத்துறை மாநகர் தானுறை ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of women in pearl garlands with bent waists and fine garments who fill men of wealth with infatuation and stand daily in the streets with cuckoo voices, and asks whether, so that he does not whirl in their net but lives praising the anklets, Murugan will not speak the word that raises him, a sinner of low quality, to the high state in the assembly of devotees. The praise names the vel that spoke true jnana into the ear of Siva who dwells in the hearts of great ascetics, mansions where the fame of the Thiruvasagam spreads, the Kaveri's floods and heaped paddy, Seyppathi, and the goddess of eternal auspiciousness who fosters dharma, at Thiruthavathurai.","n":"அத்தமுளோர் தமையே மயல் நிரப்பி: the women choose men of wealth. The plea is for a place in the சபை of devotees, a social salvation. The line on the Thiruvasagam spoken by the great one among Tamil scholars seems to refer to Manikkavasagar's work, but its syntax is unclear to me. அறம் வளர்த்த நித்ய கல்யாணி names the goddess as the one who fosters dharma.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["varaivinmagalir","adiyaar","guru","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:198"},"p:920":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":920,"t":["காலன் வேற்கணை யீர்வா ளாலமு","நேர்க ணாற்கொலை சூழ்மா பாவிகள்","காம சாத்திர வாய்ப்பா டேணிக ...... ளெவரேனுங்","காத லார்க்கும்வி னாவாய் கூறிகள்","போக பாத்திர மர்மு தேவிகள்","காசு கேட்டிடு மாயா ரூபிக ...... ளதிமோக","மாலை மூட்டிகள் வானூ டேநிமிர்","ஆனை போற்பொர நேரே போர்முலை","மார்பு காட்டிகள் நானா பேதக ...... மெனமாயா","மாப ராக்கிக ளோடே சீரிய","போது போக்குத லாமோ நீயினி","வாவெ னாப்பரி வாலே யாள்வது ...... மொருநாளே","பால றாத்திரு வாயா லோதிய","ஏடு நீர்க்கெதிர் போயே வாதுசெய்","பாடல் தோற்றிரு நாலா மாயிர ...... சமண்மூடர்","பாரின் மேற்கழு மீதே யேறிட","நீறி டாத்தமிழ் நாடீ டேறிட","பாது காத்தரு ளாலே கூனிமி ...... ரிறையோனும்","ஞால மேத்திய தோர்மா தேவியும்","ஆல வாய்ப்பதி வாழ்வா மாறெணு","ஞான பாக்கிய பாலா வேலவ ...... மயில்வீரா","ஞான தீக்ஷ¢த சேயே காவிரி","யாறு தேக்கிய கால்வாய் மாமழ","நாடு போற்றிய பூவா ளுருறை ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet lists courtesans whose eyes are Death's arrows, saw-blades and poison, murderous sinners who recite the Kama-shastra, question any lover, are vessels of pleasure, ask for coins, are shapes of illusion, and charge with breasts like elephants, and asks whether he should waste time with them; he asks for the day Murugan will say 'come' and rule him with love. The praise is Sambandar's story: the mouth still wet with the Goddess's milk sang, the palm leaf went against the current, eight thousand Jains lost the debate and mounted the stakes, the Tamil land that had abandoned the ash was saved, and the king whose hunch was straightened and his queen were made to live at Alavai; Murugan is that child of jnana, at Poovalur.","n":"காசு கேட்டிடும் மாயா ரூபிகள, those who ask for coins, mark the courtesans. In the praise every detail is Sambandar's: பால் அறாத் திருவாய் (the infant fed by the Goddess), ஏடு நீர்க்கு எதிர் போய் (the leaf in the Vaigai), இரு நாலாம் ஆயிர சமண் (eight thousand Jains), கூன் நிமிர் இறையோன் (the Pandya cured of his hunch). The queen is not named; tradition calls her Mangaiyarkkarasi. நீறிடாத் தமிழ்நாடு, the Tamil land without the ash, is the poet's word for the Jain period.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["varaivinmagalir","thamizh","thiruneeru","samayam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:199"},"p:921":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":921,"t":["வாசனை மங்கையர் போற்று சிற்றடி","பூஷண கிண்கிணி யார்ப்ப ரித்திட","மாமலை ரண்டென நாட்டு மத்தக ...... முலையானை","வாடைம யங்கிட நூற்ற சிற்றிழை","நூலிடை நன்கலை தேக்க இக்குவில்","மாரன்வி டுங்கணை போற்சி வத்திடு ...... விழியார்கள்","நேசிகள் வம்பிக ளாட்ட மிட்டவர்","தீயர்வி ரும்புவர் போற்சு ழற்றியெ","நீசனெ னும்படி யாக்கி விட்டொரு ...... பிணியான","நீரின்மி குந்துழ லாக்கை யிற்றிட","யோகமி குந்திட நீக்கி யிப்படி","நீயக லந்தனில் வீற்றி ருப்பது ...... மொருநாளே","தேசம டங்கலு மேத்து மைப்புய","லாயநெ டுந்தகை வாழ்த்த வச்சிர","தேகமி லங்கிய தீர்க்க புத்திர ...... முதல்வோனே","தீரனெ னும்படி சாற்று விக்ரம","சூரன டுங்கிட வாய்த்த வெற்புடல்","தேயந டந்திடு கீர்த்தி பெற்றிடு ...... கதிர்வேலா","மூசளி பம்பிய நூற்றி தழ்க்கம","லாசனன் வந்துல காக்கி வைத்திடு","வேதன கந்தையை மாற்றி முக்கண ...... ¡றிவாக","மூதறி வுந்திய தீ¨க்ஷ செப்பிய","ஞானம்வி ளங்கிய மூர்த்தி யற்புத","மூவரி லங்குப ராய்த்து றைப்பதி ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes fragrant women whose small feet ring with anklets, whose breasts stand like two hills and whose eyes redden like Kama's arrows, lovers and deceivers who spin him about until he is called vile, and asks whether there will be a day when, with yoga growing and the body that whirls in the water of disease removed, Murugan sits within his breast. The praise names the vel that wore down the mountain and made Sura tremble, and the wondrous one who changed the pride of Brahma, maker of the world, and spoke the initiation of ancient knowledge, at Paraithurai.","n":"நீசன் எனும்படியாக்கி: the women's harm is a loss of name as well as of self. நீ அகலம் தனில் வீற்றிருப்பது asks for the Lord enthroned in the breast. வேதன் அகந்தையை மாற்றி ... தீக்ஷை செப்பிய is the binding of Brahma and the teaching that followed, though Om is not named. The opening of the praise (தீர்க்க புத்திர முதல்வோனே, praised by the cloud-dark Vishnu, with a body of diamond) is obscure to me.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["kaamam","yaakkai","yogam","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:200"},"p:922":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":922,"t":["புணரியும னங்கனம் புஞ்சுரும் புங்கருங்","கயலினொடு கெண்டையுஞ் சண்டனுங் கஞ்சமும்","புதுநிலவ ருந்தியுந் துஞ்சுநஞ் சும்பொருப் ...... பெறிவேலும்","பொருவெனஇ கன்றகன் றங்குமிங் குஞ்சுழன்","றிடைகடைசி வந்துவஞ் சம்பொதிந் திங்கிதம்","புவியிளைஞர் முன்பயின் றம்பொனின் கம்பிதக் ...... குழைமோதிக்","குணலையொடு மிந்த்ரியஞ் சஞ்சலங் கண்டிடும்","படியமர்பு ரிந்தருஞ் சங்கடஞ் சந்ததங்","கொடுமைசெய்து சங்கொடுஞ் சிங்கிதங் குங்கடைக் ...... கணினார்பால்","குலவுபல செந்தனந் தந்துதந் தின்புறுந்","த்ரிவிதகர ணங்களுங் கந்தநின் செம்பதங்","குறுகும்வகை யந்தியுஞ் சந்தியுந் தொந்தமற் ...... றமைவேனோ","துணர்விரிக டம்பமென் தொங்கலும் பம்புறும்","புழுகுமச லம்பசுஞ் சந்தனங் குங்குமந்","தொகுகளப முந்துதைந் தென்றுநன் கொன்றுபத் ...... திருதோளுந்","தொலைவில்சண்மு கங்களுந் தந்த்ரமந் த்ரங்களும்","பழநிமலை யும்பரங் குன்றமுஞ் செந்திலுந்","துதிசெயுமெ யன்பர்தஞ் சிந்தையுஞ் சென்றுசெய்ப் ...... பதிவாழ்வாய்","கணபணபு யங்கமுங் கங்கையுந் திங்களுங்","குரவுமறு குங்குறுந் தும்பையுங் கொன்றையுங்","கமழ்சடில சம்புவுங் கும்பிடும் பண்புடைக் ...... குருநாதா","கனகுடகில் நின்றகுன் றந்தருஞ் சங்கரன்","குறுமுனிக மண்டலங் கொண்டுமுன் கண்டிடுங்","கதிசெய்நதி வந்துறுந் தென்கடம் பந்துறைப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet sets women's eyes against the sea, Kama, the bee, the carp and kendai fish, Yama, the lotus, poison and the vel that pierced the hill, eyes that roam here and there, redden at the corners and are packed with deceit, and asks whether, having given much red gold to such women, he will settle at dawn and dusk, free of ties, so that mind, speech and body draw near Murugan's red feet. The praise lists the kadamba garland, the twelve shoulders, the six faces, mantra and tantra, Palani, Parangundram, Senthil and the hearts of true devotees, Siva with serpent, Ganga and moon who bows to him as guru, and the shrine on the river that came from the sage's water-pot on the Kudagu hill.","n":"செந்தனம் தந்து, giving red gold, makes the women courtesans. த்ரிவித கரணங்களும் ... குறுகும்வகை: the three instruments of mind, speech and body are to approach the feet together. குடகு, the sage's கமண்டலம் and the river point to the Kaveri born from Agastya's water-pot; the part played by சங்கரன் in that line is not clear to me.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","kan","vanjam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:201"},"p:923":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":923,"t":["மதியால்வித் தகனாகி மனதாலுத் ...... தமனாகிப்","பதிவாகிச் சிவஞான பரயோகத் ...... தருள்வாயே","நிதியேநித் தியமேயென் நினைவேநற் ...... பொருளாயோய்","கதியேசொற் பரவேளே கருவூரிற் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A short song: the poet asks to be made wise in understanding and excellent in mind, settled, and given the supreme yoga of Siva-jnana. The praise calls Murugan treasure, the eternal, his own thought, the good meaning, the refuge, the lord beyond words, at Karuvur.","n":"No women and no death: the whole plea is positive, for மதி, மனம் and சிவஞான பரயோகம். சொற்பர வேளே, the lord beyond words, sets the goal past speech.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","yogam","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:202"},"p:924":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":924,"t":["இளநிர்க் குவட்டுமுலை யமுதத் தடத்தைகனி","யிரதக் குடத்தையெணு ...... மரபோடே","இருகைக் கடைத்துஇடை துவளக் குழற்சரிய","இதழ்சர்க்க ரைப்பழமொ ...... டுற்ழுறல்","முளரிப்பு வொத்தமுக முகம்வைத் தருத்திநல","முதிரத்து வற்பஅல்குல் ...... மி¨ச்முழ்கி","மொழிதத்தை யொப்பகடை விழிகட் சிவப்பமளி","முழுகிச்சு கிக்கும்வினை ...... யறஆளாய்","நளினப் பதக்கழலு மொளிர்செச்சை பொற்புயமெ","னயனத்தி லுற்றுநட ...... மிடும்வேலா","நரனுக் கமைத்தகொடி யிரதச்சு தக்களவ","னறைபுட்ப நற்றுளவன் ...... மருகோனே","களபத் தனத்திசுக சரசக் குறத்திமுக","கமலப் புயத்துவளி ...... மணவாளா","கடலைக் குவட்டவுணை யிரணப் படுத்தியுயர்","கருவைப் பதிக்குளுறை ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes lovemaking: breasts like tender coconut, a pool of nectar and a pot of juice, held in both hands, the waist bending, hair slipping, lips sweet as sugar, face pressed to face, sinking into the bed, and asks to be ruled so that this deed ends. The praise names the anklets and golden shoulders with vetchi flowers that dance in the poet's eyes, Vishnu who set up the flagged chariot for Arjuna as uncle, the hunter girl's bridegroom, and the one who wounded the demon of the sea-hill, at Karuvai.","n":"The plea is one phrase, சுகிக்கும் வினை அற ஆளாய், 'rule me so that the deed of enjoying ends', after a long description. நரனுக்கு அமைத்த கொடி இரதம் is Krishna as Arjuna's charioteer.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["punarchi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:202"},"p:925":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":925,"t":["தசையா கியற் றையினால் முடியத்","தலைகா லளவொப் ...... பனையாயே","தடுமா றுதல்சற் றொருநா ளுலகிற்","றவிரா வுடலத் ...... தினைநாயேன்","பசுபா சமும்விட் டறிவா லறியப்","படுபூ ரணநிட் ...... களமான","பதிபா வனையுற் றநுபூ தியிலப்","படியே யடைவித் ...... தருள்வாயே","அசலே சுரர்புத் திரனே குணதிக்","கருணோ தயமுத் ...... தமிழோனே","அகிலா கமவித் தகனை துகளற்","றவர்வாழ் வயலித் ...... திருநாடா","கசிவா ரிதயத் தமிர்தே மதுபக்","கமலா லயன்மைத் ...... துனவேளே","கருணா கரசற் குருவே குடகிற்","கருவூ ரழகப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet calls himself a dog who stumbles daily in the world with this body of flesh adorned from head to foot, and asks to be brought, leaving pasu and pasam, into the contemplation of the Pati, the full and partless one known by knowledge, and into that experience. The praise names Murugan son of the lord of the mountain, the rising sun of the east, master of the three Tamils and all the agamas, nectar of melting hearts, brother-in-law of Brahma, the compassionate true guru, beautiful at Karuvur in the west and at Vayali.","n":"பசு பாசமும் விட்டு அறிவால் அறியப்படு பூரண நிட்கள பதி: the Siddhanta triad with its standard terms (பூரணம், நிட்களம்), ending in அநுபூதி, direct experience. கமலாலயன் மைத்துன is Brahma's brother-in-law, since Murugan's consorts are Vishnu's daughters and Brahma Vishnu's son; that reasoning is mine.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pathi","paasam","yaakkai","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:202"},"p:926":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":926,"t":["நித்தப் பிணிகொடு மேவிய காயமி","தப்புப் பிருதிவி வாயுவு தேயுவு","நிற்பொற் ககனமொ டாமிவை பூதக ...... லவைமேவி","நிற்கப் படுமுல காளவு மாகரி","டத்தைக் கொளவுமெ நாடிடு மோடிடு","நெட்டுப் பணிகலை பூணிடு நானெனு ...... மடவாண்மை","எத்தித் திரியுமி தேதுபொ யாதென","வுற்றுத் தெளிவுண ராதுமெய் ஞானமொ","டிச்சைப் படஅறி யாதுபொய் மாயையி ...... லுழல்வேனை","எத்திற் கொடுநின தாரடி யாரொடு","முய்த்திட் டுனதரு ளாலுயர் ஞானமு","திட்டுத் திருவடி யாமுயர் வாழ்வுற ...... இனிதாள்வாய்","தத்தத் தனதன தானன தானன","தித்தித் திமிதிமி தீதக தோதக","டத்தக் குடகுகு தாகுட தீகுட ...... வெனபேரிச்","சத்தத் தொலிதிகை தாவிட வானவர்","திக்குக் கெடவரு சூரர்கள் தூள்பட","சர்ப்பச் சதமுடி நாணிட வேலதை ...... யெறிவோனே","வெற்றிப் பொடியணி மேனியர் கோகுல","சத்திக் கிடமருள் தாதகி வேணியர்","வெற்புப் புரமது நீறெழ காணிய ...... ரருள்பாலா","வெற்புத் தடமலை யாள்வளி நாயகி","சித்தத் தமர்கும ராஎமை யாள்கொள","வெற்றிப் புகழ்கரு வூர்தனில் மேவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says this body of daily disease is made of earth, water, air, fire and space, yet it seeks to rule the world and win a great crown, runs about wearing ornaments and the foolish manhood that says 'I', and never sees that it is false or knows to desire true jnana; he asks to be joined by any means to Murugan's devotees and given high jnana and the life of the holy feet. The praise has drums sounding to the directions, Sura's host scattered and the serpent's hundred heads bowed by the vel, Siva who gave half his body to Shakti and burned the cities as father, and Valli's beloved, at Karuvur.","n":"The body is analysed into the five elements (பிருதிவி, அப்பு, வாயு, தேயு, ககனம்) before its ambitions are listed, so the pride of rule and of 'I' (நானெனும் மடவாண்மை) rests on a heap of elements. எத்திற் கொடு நின் அடியாரொடும் உய்த்திட்டு: the means of rescue is the company of devotees. கோகுல சத்தி may call the Goddess the one of Gokula; I am unsure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","agandhai","maayai","adiyaar"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:202"},"p:927":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":927,"t":["முட்டம ருட்டி யிருகுழை தொட்டக டைக்க ணியலென","மொட்பைவி ளைத்து முறையளித் ...... திடுமாதர்","முத்தமி ரத்ந மரகதம் வைத்தவி சித்ர முகபட","மொச்சிய பச்சை யகில்மணத் ...... தனபாரம்","கட்டிய ணைத்து நகநுதி பட்டக ழுத்தி லிறுகிய","கைத்தல மெய்த்து வசனமற் ...... றுயிர்சோருங்","கட்டமு யக்கி னநுபவம் விட்டவி டற்கு நியமித","கற்பனை பக்ஷ முடனளித் ...... தருளாதோ","வெட்டிய கட்க முனைகொடு வட்டகு ணத்து ரணமுக","விக்ரம வுக்ர வெகுவிதப் ...... படைவீரா","வெற்றியை யுற்ற குறவர்கள் பெற்றகொ டிக்கு மிகமகிழ்","வித்தக சித்த வயலியிற் ...... குமரேசா","கிட்டிய பற்கொ டசுரர்கள் மட்டற வுட்க வடலொடு","கித்திந டக்கு மலகைசுற் ...... றியவேலா","கெட்டவ ருற்ற துணையென நட்டருள் சிட்ட பசுபதி","கெர்ப்பபு ரத்தி லறுமுகப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women whose eyes reach their earrings, breasts covered with jewelled cloth and scented with akil, embraces with nail-marks on the neck in which words stop and life faints, and asks whether, having left that experience, he will not be granted with love a disciplined contemplation. The praise names the warrior of many weapons who delights the hunters' daughter, the Kumaresa of Vayali, the vel ringed by dancing ghouls, and the six-faced lord of Garbhapuram, son of Pasupati who stands as refuge for the ruined.","n":"நியமித கற்பனை is my best reading of what is asked, a regulated or ordained contemplation, but the line may divide otherwise, and விட்ட may mean the poet has left the embrace or wants to. கெர்ப்பபுரம் is Karuvur in Sanskrit, கரு = கர்ப்பம்.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","kaamam","thiyaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:202"},"p:928":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":928,"t":["சஞ்சல சரித பரநாட்டர்கள்","மந்திரி குமரர் படையாட்சிகள்","சங்கட மகிபர் தொழஆக்கினை ...... முடிசூடித்","தண்டிகை களிறு பரிமேற்றனி","வெண்குடை நிழலி லுலவாக்கன","சம்ப்ரம விபவ சவுபாக்கிய ...... முடையோராய்க்","குஞ்சமும் விசிற இறுமாப்பொடு","பஞ்சணை மிசையி லிசையாத்திரள்","கொம்புகள் குழல்கள் வெகுவாத்திய ...... மியல்கீதங்","கொங்கணி மகளிர் பெருநாட்டிய","நன்றென மனது மகிழ்பார்த்திபர்","கொண்டய னெழுதும் யமகோட்டியை ...... யுணராரே","பஞ்சவர் கொடிய வினைநூற்றுவர்","வென்றிட சகுனி கவறாற்பொருள்","பங்குடை யவனி பதிதோற்றிட ...... அயலேபோய்ப்","பண்டையில் விதியை நினையாப்பனி","ரண்டுடை வருக்ஷ முறையாப்பல","பண்புடன் மறைவின் முறையாற்றிரு ...... வருளாலே","வஞ்சனை நழுவி நிரைமீட்சியில்","முந்துத முடைய மனைவாழ்க்கையின்","வந்தபி னுரிமை யதுகேட்டிட ...... இசையாநாள்","மண்கொள விசையன் விடுதேர்ப்பரி","யுந்தினன் மருக வயலு¡ர்க்குக","வஞ்சியி லமரர் சிறைமீட்டருள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet pictures kings in their pomp, bowed to by ministers, princes, commanders and lesser kings, crowned with command, riding palanquin, elephant and horse under the white umbrella, fanned with whisks, proud on cotton beds, delighting in horns, flutes, songs and the dances of Konkan women, and says they do not know the death that Brahma writes. The praise tells the Mahabharata: the Pandavas lose to Sakuni's dice and go into exile, twelve years and a year in hiding, recover the cattle, are refused their share, and win the land when Krishna drives Arjuna's horses; Murugan is his nephew, Guha of Vayalur, who freed the gods, at Vanji.","n":"There is no first-person plea: the whole first half describes others, and the judgment comes in one line, அயன் எழுதும் யம கோட்டியை உணராரே. The praise is apt to it, a story of a kingdom lost and won. யம கோட்டி is unclear: it may mean Yama's decree or the death written by Brahma.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["arasu","selvam","kootru","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:202"},"p:929":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":929,"t":["முகிலள கஞ்சரி யாக்குழை யிகல்வன கண்சிவ வாச்சிவ","முறுவல்மு கங்குறு வேர்ப்பெழ ...... வநுபோக","முளைபுள கஞ்செய வார்த்தையு நிலையழி யும்படி கூப்பிட","முகுளித பங்கய மாக்கர ...... நுதல்சேரத்","துயரொழு குஞ்செல பாத்திர மெலியமி குந்துத ராக்கினி","துவளமு யங்கிவி டாய்த்தரி ...... வையர்தோளின்","துவயலி நின்றன வ்யாத்தமும் வயலியல் வஞ்சியில் மேற்பயில்","சொருபமு நெஞ்சிலி ராப்பகல் ...... மறவேனே","சகலம யம்பர மேச்சுரன் மகபதி யுய்ந்திட வாய்த்தருள்","சரவண சம்பவ தீர்க்கஷண் ...... முகமாகிச்","சருவுக்ர வுஞ்சசி லோச்சய முருவவெ றிந்தகை வேற்கொடு","சமர முகந்தனில் நாட்டிய ...... மயிலேறி","அகிலமு மஞ்சிய வாக்ரம விகடப யங்கர ராக்கத","அசுரர கங்கெட வார்த்திடு ...... கொடிகூவ","அமரர டங்கலு மாட்கொள அமரர்த லங்குடி யேற்றிட","அமரரை யுஞ்சிறை மீட்டருள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes lovemaking, hair slipping, eyes reddened, smiles, sweat, cries and lotus hands raised to the brow, and the body, a vessel of flowing sorrow, wasting in the women's embrace, and vows not to forget night and day the form in which Murugan stands at Vayali and the form at Vanji. The praise names the all-pervading lord born in Saravana for Indra's sake with six faces, the vel that pierced Krauncha, the peacock, the cock crying out against the demons, and the one who restored the gods' cities and freed them.","n":"The plea does not ask to escape; it vows memory of two shrine forms, Vayali and Vanji (Karuvur), held in the heart night and day. The opening of that line (துவயலி நின்றன வ்யாத்தமும்) is garbled.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","kaamam","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:202"},"p:930":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":930,"t":["குருவு மடியவ ரடியவ ரடிமையு","மருண மணியணி கணபண விதகர","குடில செடிலினு நிகரென வழிபடு ...... குணசீலர்","குழுவி லொழுகுதல் தொழுகுதல் விழுகுதல்","அழுகு தலுமிலி நலமிலி பொறையிலி","குசல கலையிலி தலையிலி நிலையிலி ...... விலைமாதர்","மருவு முலையெனு மலையினி லிடறியும்","அளக மெனவள ரடவியில் மறுகியு","மகர மெறியிரு கடலினில் முழுகியு ...... முழலாமே","வயலி நகரியி லருள்பெற மயில்மிசை","யுதவு பரிமள மதுகர வெகுவித","வனச மலரடி கனவிலு நனவிலு ...... மறவேனே","உ ருவு பெருகயல் கரியதொர் முகிலெனு","மருது நெறிபட முறைபட வரைதனில்","உ ரலி னொடுதவழ் விரகுள இளமையு ...... மிகமாரி","உ மிழ நிரைகளி னிடர்கெட வடர்கிரி","கவிகை யிடவல மதுகையு நிலைகெட","வுலவில் நிலவறை யுருவிய வருமையு ...... மொருநூறு","நிருப ரணமுக வரசர்கள் வலிதப","விசயன் ரதமுதல் நடவிய வெளிமையு","நிகில செகதல முரைசெயு மரிதிரு ...... மருகோனே","நிலவு சொரிவளை வயல்களு நெடுகிய","குடக தமனியு நளினமு மருவிய","நெருவை நகருறை திருவுரு வழகிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet sets himself against the virtuous company who worship the guru, the devotees and the devotees' servants as equal to the Lord, and calls himself one without their conduct, worship, falling at the feet or weeping, without goodness, patience, skill, sense or steadiness; he asks not to stumble on the hill of priced women's breasts, wander in the forest of their hair and drown in the sea of their eyes, and vows not to forget in dream or waking the lotus feet given him at Vayali. The praise is Krishna's: the dark child who crawled with the mortar and broke the marutha trees, lifted the hill as an umbrella against the rain for the cattle, came through the trap of the underground chamber, and humbly drove Arjuna's chariot to break the hundred kings, and calls Murugan his nephew, at Neruvai among paddy and lotuses.","n":"அடியவர் அடியவர் அடிமையும் ... நிகரென வழிபடு: the devotees honour even the servants of servants as the Lord, and the poet's list of what he lacks (நலமிலி பொறையிலி ... நிலையிலி) is the standard self-abuse set against them. In the praise எளிமை, the humility of Krishna as charioteer, is named as a virtue. The opening run about the snake-bearing devotees is hard to divide, and நிலவறை, the underground chamber, is my reading of an allusion I cannot place exactly.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["adiyaar","kutram","varaivinmagalir","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:203"},"p:931":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":931,"t":["வண்டுபோற் சாரத் ...... தருள்தேடி","மந்திபோற்ள காலப் ...... பிணிசாடிச்","செண்டுபோற் பாசத் ...... துடனாடிச்","சிந்தைமாய்த் தேசித் ...... தருள்வாயே","தொண்டராற் காணப் ...... பெறுவோனே","துங்கவேற் கானத் ...... துறைவோனே","மிண்டராற் காணக் ...... கிடையானே","வெஞ்சமாக் கூடற் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A short song: the poet asks to seek grace like a bee seeking nectar, to strike down the disease of time like a monkey, to play with the bond like a ball, and, his mind ended, to be taught and given grace. The praise names Murugan the one his servants see, the lord of the high vel dwelling in the forest shrine, not to be found by the stubborn, at Venjamakkoodal.","n":"சிந்தை மாய்த்து: the mind is to be ended, not calmed. The three similes are compact and their sense is uncertain: the bee is clear, but what the monkey and the ball stand for (காலப் பிணி சாடி, பாசத்துடன் ஆடி) is my guess. தொண்டரால் காணப் பெறுவோனே against மிண்டரால் காணக் கிடையானே: devotion sees what stubborn pride cannot.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","manam","adiyaar"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:204"},"p:932":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":932,"t":["இருவினைப் பிறவிக் ...... கடல்மூழ்கி","இடர்கள்பட் டலையப் ...... புகுதாதே","திருவருட் கருணைப் ...... ப்ரபையாலே","திரமெனக் கதியைப் ...... பெறுவேனோ","அரியயற் கறிதற் ...... கரியானே","அடியவர்க் கெளியற் ...... புதநேயா","குருவெனச் சிவனுக் ...... கருள்போதா","கொடுமுடிக் குமரப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A short song: the poet asks whether, instead of drowning in the sea of births made by the two kinds of deeds and tossing in suffering, he will by the radiance of grace's compassion firmly reach the goal. The praise calls Murugan hard for Hari and Brahma to know, easy for his devotees, the wondrous lover, the teacher who gave grace to Siva as guru, the Kumara of Kodumudi.","n":"அரியயற்கு அறிதற்கு அரியானே, அடியவர்க்கு எளிய: hard for the gods, easy for devotees, a pairing of the hymns. குருவெனச் சிவனுக்கு அருள் போதா keeps the teaching of Siva in a single phrase.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","vinai","thiruvarul","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:205"},"p:933":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":933,"t":["காந்தட் கரவளை சேந்துற் றிடமத","காண்டத் தரிவைய ...... ருடனூசி","காந்தத் துறவென வீழ்ந்தப் படிகுறி","காண்டற் கநுபவ ...... விதமேவிச்","சாந்தைத் தடவிய கூந்தற் கருமுகில்","சாய்ந்திட் டயில்விழி ...... குழைமீதே","தாண்டிப் பொரவுடை தீண்டித் தனகிரி","தாங்கித் தழுவுத ...... லொழியேனோ","மாந்தர்க் கமரர்கள் வேந்தற் கவரவர்","வாஞ்சைப் படியருள் ...... வயலு¡ரா","வான்கிட் டியபெரு மூங்கிற் புனமிசை","மான்சிற் றடிதொழு ...... மதிகாமி","பாந்தட் சடைமுடி யேந்திக் குலவிய","பாண்டிக் கொடுமுடி ...... யுடையாரும்","பாங்கிற் பரகுரு வாங்கற் பனையொடு","பாண்சொற் பரவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes falling to women with glory-lily hands and bangles as a needle falls to a magnet, their sandal-scented hair like dark cloud, spear eyes reaching their earrings, and the touching of garments and holding of breasts, and asks whether he will not give up those embraces. The praise names Murugan of Vayalur, who grants men and the king of gods what each desires, the lover who bowed to the small feet of the deer-like girl in the bamboo-fringed millet field, and the lord praised in bards' words to whom the lord of Pandikkodumudi with serpent-bound hair is drawn as to a supreme guru.","n":"ஊசி காந்தத்து உறவென வீழ்ந்து: the needle drawn to the magnet makes desire a force the poet does not choose. மான் சிற்றடி தொழு மதிகாமி: Murugan bowing to Valli's small feet reverses the usual direction of worship. The last lines on Siva of Kodumudi are hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["kaamam","punarchi","mayal"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:205"},"p:934":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":934,"t":["பரிவுறு நாரற் றழல்மதி வீசச்","சிலைபொரு காலுற் ...... றதனாலே","பனிபடு சோலைக் குயிலது கூவக்","குழல்தனி யோசைத் ...... தரலாலே","மருவியல் மாதுக் கிருகயல் சோரத்","தனிமிக வாடித் ...... தளராதே","மனமுற வாழத் திருமணி மார்பத்","தருள்முரு காவுற் ...... றணைவாயே","கிரிதனில் வேல்விட் டிருதொளை யாகத்","தொடுகும ராமுத் ...... தமிழோனே","கிளரொளி நாதர்க் கொருமக னாகித்","திருவளர் சேலத் ...... தமர்வோனே","பொருகிரி சூரக் கிளையது மாளத்","தனிமயி லேறித் ...... திரிவோனே","புகர்முக வேழக் கணபதி யாருக்","கிளையவி நோதப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"In another's voice, the singer says that because the moon pitilessly throws fire, the wind strikes, the cuckoo calls in the cool grove and a flute sounds alone, the girl's carp-like eyes droop, and asks Murugan not to let her fade and fail but to come and hold her to his jewelled chest so that she may live content. The praise names the vel that pierced the mountain through, the Kumara of the three Tamils, son of the radiant lord, who rode the peacock to destroy Sura's kin, the younger brother of the elephant-faced Ganapati, at Salem.","n":"No confession: the speaker is the girl's mother or friend, and the girl's love is for the god. The machinery of akam longing is all here, the moon as fire, the wind, the cuckoo and the lone flute, as in 24; சிலைபொரு கால், the wind, may compare it to a bow or bring it from a hill. மாதுக்கு இரு கயல் சோர: the drooping eyes are the one physical sign given.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","melivu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:206"},"p:935":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":935,"t":["சங்கு வார்முடி பொற்கழல் பொங்கு சாமரை கத்திகை","தண்டு மாகரி பெற்றவன் ...... வெகுகோடிச்","சந்த பாஷைகள் கற்றவன் மந்த்ர வாதிச துர்க்கவி","சண்ட மாருத மற்றுள ...... கவிராஜப்","பங்கி பாலச ரச்வதி சங்க நூல்கள்வி தித்தப்ர","பந்த போதமு ரைத்திடு ...... புலவோன்யான்","பண்டை மூவெழு வர்க்கெதிர் கண்ட நீயுமெ னச்சில","பஞ்ச பாதக ரைப்புகழ் ...... செயலாமோ","வெங்கை யானை வனத்திடை துங்க மாமுத லைக்குவெ","ருண்டு மூலமெ னக்கரு ...... டனிலேறி","விண்ப ராவஅ டுக்கிய மண்ப ராவஅ தற்குவி","தம்ப ராவஅ டுப்பவன் ...... மருகோனே","கொங்க ணாதித ரப்பெறு கொங்கி னூடுசு கித்திடு","கொங்கின் வீரக ணப்ரிய ...... குமராபொற்","கொங்கு லாவுகு றக்கொடி கொங்கை யேதழு விச்செறி","கொங்கு ராஜபு ரத்துறை ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet sets out his credentials, conch and crown, golden anklet, whisks and umbrella, palanquin and elephant, many crores of rhythmic languages, mastery of mantra, the four kinds of verse, the title of king of poets, a child Saraswati, the expounder of prabandhas laid down by the Sangam books, and asks whether it is fitting for such a poet to praise some sinners of the five great sins as 'you who equal the ancient one-and-twenty patrons'. The praise tells how the elephant, terrified by the crocodile in the forest pool, cried 'Root!' and Vishnu came on Garuda, names Murugan his nephew, the hero of the Kongu land and lover of the hunter girl, at Rajapuram.","n":"The boast is the argument: the honours (சத்ர, சாமரை, கவிராஜ) are the regalia a court poet earns, and the poet lists them to say they are too good to be spent on பஞ்ச பாதகர். பண்டை மூவெழுவர் are the three sets of seven patrons of Tamil tradition. Whether the poet speaks of himself or in a typical bard's voice is not said.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iravu","kalvi","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:207"},"p:936":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":936,"t":["கலக சம்ப்ரமத் தாலேவி லோசன","மலர்சி வந்திடப் பூணார மானவை","கழல வண்டெனச் சா¡£ரம் வாய்விட ...... அபிராமக்","கனத னங்களிற் கோமாள மாகியெ","பலந கம்படச் சீரோடு பேதக","கரண முஞ்செய்துட் பாலு¡று தேனித ...... ழமுதூறல்","செலுவி மென்பணைத் தோளோடு தோள்பொர","நிலைகு லைந்திளைத் தேராகு மாருயிர்","செருகு முந்தியிற் போய்வீழு மாலுட ...... னநுராகந்","தெரிகு மண்டையிட் டாராத சேர்வையி","லுருகி மங்கையர்க் காளாகி யேவல்செய்","திடினு நின்கழற் சீர்பாத நானினி ...... மறவேனே","உ லக கண்டமிட் டாகாச மேல்விரி","சலதி கண்டிடச் சேராய மாமவ","ருடன்ம டிந்திடக் கோபாலர் சேரியில் ...... மகவாயும்","உ ணர்சி றந்தசக் ராதார நாரணன்","மருக மந்திரக் காபாலி யாகிய","உ ரக கங்கணப் பூதேசர் பாலக ...... வயலு¡ரா","விலைத ருங்கொலைப் போர்வேடர் கோவென","இனையு மங்குறப் பாவாய்வி யாகுலம்","விடுவி டென்றுகைக் கூர்வேலை யேவிய ...... இளையோனே","விறல்சு ரும்புநற் க்¡£தேசி பாடிய","விரைசெய் பங்கயப் பூவோடை மேவிய","விஜய மங்கலத் தேவாதி தேவர்கள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes lovemaking with a woman whose eyes redden in love's quarrel, whose garlands slip, whose voice hums like a bee, whose breasts bear nail marks and whose lips flow with honeyed nectar, until his life sinks into her navel; he says that even if he melts and becomes the servant of women doing their bidding, he will not forget Murugan's feet from now on. The praise names him nephew of the discus-bearing Narayana who lived as a child in the cowherds' village, son of the skull-bearing lord of ghosts with serpent bracelets, the lord of Vayalur who threw his vel when the hunters cried out and told the Kura girl to stop grieving, and the lord of Vijayamangalam, where bees sing the desi melody over lotus pools.","n":"The turn is unusual: மங்கையர்க்கு ஆளாகி ஏவல் செய்திடினும் நின் கழல் சீர்பாதம் நான் இனி மறவேனே makes no request to be freed from women, only a vow that service to them will not erase the feet. The lines on Vishnu (உலக கண்டமிட்டு ஆகாச மேல் விரி சலதி கண்டிட சேராய மாமவருடன் மடிந்திட) are hard to divide, and the hunters' cry and Valli's grief seem to point to the battle at Valli's hill without naming it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["kaamam","punarchi","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:208"},"p:937":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":937,"t":["சஞ்சரியு கந்து நின்றுமுரல் கின்ற","தண்குவளை யுந்து ...... குழலாலுந்","தண்டரள தங்க மங்கமணி கின்ற","சண்டவித கும்ப ...... கிரியாலும்","நஞ்சவினை யொன்றி தஞ்சமென வந்து","நம்பிவிட நங்கை ...... யுடனாசை","நண்புறெனை யின்று நன்றில்வினை கொன்று","நன்றுமயில் துன்றி ...... வரவேணும்","கஞ்சமலர் கொன்றை தும்பைமகிழ் விஞ்சி","கந்திகமழ் கின்ற ...... கதிர்வேலா","கன்றிடுபி ணங்கள் தின்றிடுக ணங்கள்","கண்டுபொரு கின்ற ...... கதிர்வேலா","செஞ்சொல்புனை கின்ற எங்கள்குற மங்கை","திண்குயம ணைந்த ...... திருமார்பா","செண்பகமி லங்கு மின்பொழில்சி றந்த","சிங்கையில மர்ந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says that, drawn by women's hair where bees hum among cool lilies and by their pearl-hung breasts like hills, he came trusting as refuge a poisonous deed and a desire for a woman; he asks Murugan today to destroy his evil deeds and come on the peacock. The praise names the vel fragrant with lotus, konrai, thumbai and magizh flowers, the vel that fought while goblins feasted on corpses, the chest that embraced the Kura girl of fine speech, and the lord at Singai of shining champak groves.","n":"தஞ்சமென வந்து நம்பி: he sought the woman as a refuge (தஞ்சம்), the word devotion reserves for the Lord. The line ending கதிர்வேலா is used twice in the praise, which may be a slip in the source.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:209"},"p:938":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":938,"t":["சந்திதொறு நாண மின்றியகம் வாடி","உ ந்திபொரு ளாக ...... அலைவேனோ","சங்கைபெற நாளு மங்கமுள மாதர்","தங்கள்வச மாகி ...... அலையாமற்","சுந்தரம தாக எந்தன்வினை யேக","சிந்தைகளி கூர ...... அருள்வாயே","தொங்குசடை மீது திங்களணி நாதர்","மங்கைரண காளி ...... தலைசாயத்","தொந்திதிமி தோதி தந்திதிமி தாதி","என்றுநட மாடு ...... மவர்பாலா","துங்கமுள வேடர் தங்கள்குல மாதை","மங்களம தாக ...... அணைவோனே","கந்தமுரு கேச மிண்டசுரர் மாள","அந்தமுனை வேல்கொ ...... டெறிவோனே","கம்பர்கயி லாசர் மைந்தவடி வேல","சிங்கைநகர் மேவு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether he must wander shamelessly at every crossroads, heart withered, with the belly as his only aim, and asks for grace so that, no longer roaming under the sway of women, his deeds may leave him and his mind be glad. The praise names Murugan son of Siva with the crescent on his hanging matted hair, who danced to the drum syllables until Kali, the woman of battle, bowed her head, the one who married the hunters' daughter, threw the vel at the asuras, and is son of Kampar and of the lord of Kailasa, at Singai.","n":"சந்திதொறு நாணமின்றி: shame lost at every crossroads is the confession; உந்தி பொருளாக may mean living for the belly or for the women's navel. மங்கை ரணகாளி தலைசாய points to Siva's dance contest with Kali, in which she yielded; கம்பர் names Siva of Kanchi.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","naanam","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:209"},"p:939":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":939,"t":["இருகுழை யிடறிக் காது மோதுவ","பரிமள நளினத் தோடு சீறுவ","இணையறு வினையைத் தாவி மீளுவ ...... வதிசூர","எமபடர் படைகெட் டோட நாடுவ","அமுதுடன் விடமொத் தாளை யீருவ","ரதிபதி கலைதப் பாது சூழுவ ...... முநிவோரும்","உ ருகிட விரகிற் பார்வை மேவுவ","பொருளது திருடற் காசை கூறுவ","யுகமுடி விதெனப் பூச லாடுவ ...... வடிவேல்போல்","உ யிர்வதை நயனக் காதல் மாதர்கள்","மயல்தரு கமரிற் போய்வி ழாவகை","உ னதடி நிழலிற் சேர வாழ்வது ...... மொருநாளே","முருகவிழ் தொடையைச் சூடி நாடிய","மரகத கிரணப் பீலி மாமயில்","முதுரவி கிரணச் சோதி போல்வய ...... லியில்வாழ்வே","முரண்முடி யிரணச் சூலி மாலினி","சரணெனு மவர்பற் றான சாதகி","முடுகிய கடினத் தாளி வாகினி ...... மதுபானம்","பருகினர் பரமப் போக மோகினி","அரகர வெனும்வித் தாரி யாமளி","பரிபுர சரணக் காளி கூளிகள் ...... நடமாடும்","பறையறை சுடலைக் கோயில் நாயகி","இறையோடு மிடமிட் டாடு காரணி","பயிரவி யருள்பட் டாலி யூர்வரு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet lists what women's eyes do: they strike the earrings, outrage the lotus, put even Yama's men to flight, cut a man as nectar mixed with poison, practise Kama's arts, melt even sages with cunning glances, speak of a desire to steal wealth and quarrel as if the age were ending; he asks whether a day will come when, instead of falling into the pit of infatuation these eyes give, he lives in the shade of Murugan's feet. The praise calls him the life of Vayalur on the emerald-feathered peacock, and then names the goddess at length, the trident-bearing Malini, the refuge, the lion-rider, the Mohini of those who drink liquor, the Kali whose goblins dance at the drum-beaten cremation ground, the Bhairavi of Pattaliyur.","n":"Every line of the first half ends in a verb of the eyes (மோதுவ, சீறுவ, மீளுவ, நாடுவ), so the whole plea is built as one sentence about them, ending in உயிர்வதை நயனம், eyes that torture life. The praise gives more space to the goddess, a cremation-ground Kali and Bhairavi worshipped with liquor (மதுபானம் பருகினர்), than to Murugan; several of her epithets (சாதகி, யாமளி, வித்தாரி) are obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kan","mayal","thiruvadi","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:210"},"p:940":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":940,"t":["கத்தூரி யகரு ம்ருகமத வித்தார படிர இமசல","கற்பூர களப மணிவன ...... மணிசேரக்","கட்டார வடமு மடர்வன நிட்டூர கலக மிடுவன","கச்சோடு பொருது நிமிர்வன ...... தனமாதர்","கொத்தூரு நறவ மெனவத ரத்தூறல் பருகி யவரொடு","கொற்சேரி யுலையில் மெழுகென ...... வுருகாமே","கொக்காக நரைகள் வருமன மிக்காய விளமை யுடன்முயல்","குற்றேவல் அடிமை செயும்வகை ...... யருளாதோ","அத்தூர புவன தரிசன நித்தார கனக நெடுமதி","லச்சான வயலி நகரியி ...... லுறைவேலா","அச்சோவெ னவச வுவகையி லுட்சோர்த லுடைய பரவையொ","டக்காகி விரக பரிபவ ...... மறவேபார்","பத்தூரர் பரவ விரைவுசெல் மெய்த்தூதர் விரவ வருடரு","பற்றாய பரம பவுருஷ ...... குருநாதா","பச்சோலை குலவு பனைவளர் மைச்சோலை மயில்கள் நடமிடு","பட்டாலி மருவு மமரர்கள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women whose breasts, rubbed with musk, agil, sandal and camphor and hung with pearl chains, strain against the bodice, and asks that he not drink their lips as honey and melt like wax in a smith's furnace; while youth is strong, before his hair turns white as a crane, he asks to be shown the way of humble service at Murugan's feet. The praise names Vayalur within golden walls, recalls the story of Paravai in the pain of separation and the true messenger who hurried to her, calls Murugan the supreme guru, and ends at Pattali where peacocks dance in groves of green palmyra.","n":"கொக்காக நரைகள் வரும் முன்: the plea sets a deadline, service while the body is still young. குற்றேவல் அடிமை is humble service, the book's word for devotion. The lines on பரவை and the மெய்த்தூதர் recall Siva going as messenger to Paravai for Sundarar; how Murugan is tied to that story (as the one who grants it, or as Siva's own form) is not clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","ilamai","thondu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:210"},"p:941":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":941,"t":["சங்கைக்கத் தோடு சிலுகிடு சங்கிச்சட் கோல சமயிகள்","சங்கற்பித் தோதும் வெகுவித ...... கலைஞானச்","சண்டைக்குட் கேள்வி யலமல மண்டற்குப் பூசை யிடுமவர்","சம்பத்துக் கேள்வி யலமல ...... மிமவானின்","மங்கைக்குப் பாக னிருடிக ளெங்கட்குச் சாமி யெனவடி","வந்திக்கப் பேசி யருளிய ...... சிவநூலின்","மந்த்ரப்ரஸ்த் தார தரிசன யந்த்ரத்துக் கேள்வி யலமலம்","வம்பிற்சுற் றாது பரகதி ...... யருள்வாயே","வெங்கைச்சுக் ¡£பர் படையையி லங்கைக்குப் போக விடவல","வென்றிச்சக் ரேசன் மிகமகிழ் ...... மருகோனே","வெண்பட்டுப் பூணல் வனகமு கெண்பட்டுப் பாளை விரிபொழில்","விஞ்சிட்டுச் சூழ வெயில்மறை ...... வயலு¡ரா","கொங்கைக்கொப் பாகும் வடகிரி செங்கைக்கொப் பாகு நறுமலர்","கொண்டைக்கொப் பாகு முகிலென ...... வனமாதைக்","கும்பிட்டுக் காதல் குனகிய இன்பச்சொற் பாடு மிளையவ","கொங்கிற்பட் டாலி நகருறை ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says 'enough, enough' to hearing the clamour of the six faiths arguing their many arts of knowledge, to hearing of the wealth of those who make worship for gain, and even to hearing the mantra, its arrangements, the darshanas and the yantras of the Saiva texts that the consort of Himavan's daughter taught the sages; he asks that, no longer wandering in vain, he be given the highest state. The praise names Murugan the nephew of Rama, who sent Sugriva's army to Lanka, the lord of Vayalur with its areca groves, and the young one at Pattali in Kongu who bowed to the forest girl, likened her breasts, hands and hair to the northern mountain, flowers and cloud, and spoke sweet words of love.","n":"The refusal reaches past the sectarians to the Saiva scriptures themselves: சிவநூலின் மந்த்ரப்ரஸ்தார தரிசன யந்த்ரத்துக் கேள்வி அலமலம். The highest state (பரகதி) is set above hearing even Siva's own mantra lore. The praise ends with Murugan himself humbled in love, bowing to Valli (கும்பிட்டுக் காதல் குனகிய).","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["samayam","mandhiram","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:210"},"p:942":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":942,"t":["அவசியமுன் வேண்டிப் ...... பலகாலும்","அறிவினுணர்ந் தாண்டுக் ...... கொருநாளில்","தவசெபமுந் தீண்டிக் ...... கனிவாகிச்","சரணமதும் புண்டற் ...... கருள்வாயே","சவதமொடுந் தாண்டித் ...... தக்ருர்வாய்","சடுசமயங் காண்டற் ...... கரியானே","சிவகுமரன் பீண்டிற் ...... பெயரானே","திருமுருகன் பூண்டிப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks that, having pleaded many times for what is needful and come to know it, he ripen by touching penance and prayer and be granted Murugan's feet. The praise calls him hard for the six faiths to see, Siva's son, and the lord of Thirumuruganpoondi.","n":"தவசெபமும் தீண்டிக் கனிவாகி: penance and japa are what ripen the soul for the feet. The line சவதமொடுந் தாண்டித் தக்ருர்வாய் is garbled and unread, and சிவகுமரன் பீண்டிற் பெயரானே may say that the town bears his name.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thavam","thiruvadi","samayam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:211"},"p:943":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":943,"t":["இறவாமற் பிறவாமல் எனையாள்சற் ...... குருவாகிப்","பிறவாகித் திரமான பெருவாழ்வைத் ...... தருவாயே","குறமாதைப் புணர்வோனே குகனேசொற் ...... குமரேசா","அறநாலைப் புகல்வோனே அவிநாசிப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A short song: the poet asks Murugan to become his true guru and rule him so that he neither dies nor is born again, and to give him the steadfast great life. The praise calls him the Kura girl's husband, Guha, Kumaresa of the Word, the one who declares the four, at Avinasi.","n":"இறவாமல் பிறவாமல்: release is named as the end of both death and birth. அறநாலைப் புகல்வோனே may mean he who speaks the four Vedas or the four aims of life; the text does not say which.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","piravi","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:212"},"p:944":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":944,"t":["பந்தப்பொற் பாரப யோதர","முந்திற் றாடகை மேகலை","பண்புற்றுத் தாளொடு மேவிய ...... துகிலோடே","பண்டெச்சிற் சேரியில் வீதியில்","கண்டிச்சிச் சாரொடு மேவியெ","பங்குக்கைக் காசுகொள் வேசியர் ...... பனிநீர்தோய்","கொந்துச்சிப் பூவணி கோதையர்","சந்தச்செத் தாமரை வாயினர்","கும்பிட்டுப் பாணியர் வீணிய ...... ரநுராகங்","கொண்டுற்றுப் பாயலின் மூழ்கினு","மண்டிச்செச் சேயென வானவர்","கொஞ்சுற்றுத் தாழ்பத தாமரை ...... மறவேனே","அந்தத் தொக் காதியு மாதியும்","வந்திக்கத் தானவர் வாழ்வுறும்","அண்டத்துப் பாலுற மாமணி ...... யொளிவீசி","அங்கத்தைப் பாவைசெய் தாமென","சங்கத்துற் றார்தமி ழோதவு","வந்துக்கிட் டார்கழு வேறிட ...... வொருகோடிச்","சந்தச்செக் காளநி சாசரர்","வெந்துக்கத் தூளிப டாமெழ","சண்டைக்கெய்த் தாரம ராபதி ...... குடியேறத்","தங்கச்செக் கோலசை சேவக","கொங்கிற்றொக் காரவி நாசியில்","தண்டைச்சிக் காரயில் வேல்விடு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes courtesans of the street who take coins as their share, rosewater-scented, flower-crowned, lotus-mouthed, who greet with folded hands and are idle, and says that even if he sinks in love into their beds he will not forget the lotus feet that the gods bow to. The praise seems to identify Murugan with Sambandar, who made a girl from bones and sang Tamil at the Sangam so that the Jains were impaled, and names the vel that burned the demons and restored Indra to Amaravati, at Avinasi in Kongu.","n":"பங்குக் கைக் காசுகொள் வேசியர்: the coin taken as their share marks these as courtesans. As in 936, the plea is a vow not to forget the feet even in their beds. அங்கத்தைப் பாவை செய்தாம் ... கழு ஏறிட points to Sambandar's raising of Poompavai from her bones and to the impaling of the Jains at Madurai; the opening lines of the praise are obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","thiruvadi","thamizh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:212"},"p:945":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":945,"t":["மனத்தி ரைந்தெழு மீளையு மேலிட","கறுத்த குஞ்சியு மேநரை யாயிட","மலர்க்க ணண்டிரு ளாகியு மேநடை ...... தடுமாறி","வருத்த முந்தர தாய்மனை யாள்மக","வெறுத்தி டங்கிளை யோருடன் யாவரும்","வசைக்கு றுஞ்சொலி னால்மிக வேதின ...... நகையாட","எனைக்க டந்திடு பாசமு மேகொடு","சினத்து வந்தெதிர் சூலமு மேகையி","லெடுத்தெ றிந்தழல் வாய்விட வேபய ...... முறவேதான்","இழுக்க வந்திடு தூதர்க ளானவர்","பிடிக்கு முன்புன தாள்மல ராகிய","இணைப்ப தந்தர வேமயில் மீதினில் ...... வரவேணும்","கனத்த செந்தமி ழால்நினை யேதின","நினைக்க வுந்தரு வாயுன தாரருள்","கருத்தி ருந்துறை வாயென தாருயிர் ...... துணையாகக்","கடற்ச லந்தனி லேயொளி சூரனை","யுடற்ப குந்திரு கூறென வேயது","கதித்தெ ழுந்தொரு சேவலு மாமயில் ...... விடும்வேலா","அனத்த னுங்கம லாலய மீதுறை","திருக்க லந்திடு மாலடி நேடிய","அரற்க ரும்பொருள் தானுரை கூறிய ...... குமரேசா","அறத்தை யுந்தரு வோர்கன பூசுரர்","நீனைத்தி னந்தொழு வாரம ராய்புரி","யருட்செ றிந்தவி நாசியுள் மேவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet pictures old age, phlegm rising, black hair grey, eyes dimmed, gait faltering, mother, wife and children disgusted and kin mocking with abuse, and asks that before Death's messengers come in anger with noose and trident to drag him away, Murugan come on the peacock and give his paired feet; he asks also to think of him daily in rich Tamil and that Murugan dwell in his mind as his life's companion. The praise names the vel that split Sura in the sea into the cock and the peacock, Kumaresa who told the hidden meaning to Hara, whose feet Brahma and Vishnu searched for, and Avinasi, where Brahmins who give dharma worship daily.","n":"The plea moves from the body's decay to the household's contempt (வசைக்குறும் சொலினால் மிகவே தினம் நகையாட) to the messengers, then asks for the feet before they seize him (பிடிக்கு முன்). அரற்கு அரும்பொருள் தான் உரை கூறிய is the teaching of Siva without naming Om.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","yaakkai","thiruvadi","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:212"},"p:946":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":946,"t":["பக்குவ வாசார லட்சண சாகாதி","பட்சண மாமோன ...... சிவயோகர்","பத்தியி லாறாறு தத்துவ மேல்வீடு","பற்றுநி ராதார ...... நிலையாக","அக்கண மேமாய துர்க்குணம் வேறாக","அப்படை யேஞான ...... வுபதேசம்","அக்கற வாய்பேசு சற்குரு நாதாவு","னற்புத சீர்பாத ...... மறவேனே","உ க்கிர வீராறு மெய்ப்புய னேநீல","வுற்பல வீராசி ...... மணநாற","ஒத்தநி லாவீசு நித்தில நீராவி","யுற்பல ராசீவ ...... வயலு¡ரா","பொக்கமி லாவீர விக்ரம மாமேனி","பொற்ப்ரபை யாகார ...... அவிநாசிப்","பொய்க்கலி போமாறு மெய்க்கருள் சீரான","புக்கொளி யூர்மேவு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says he will not forget the wondrous feet of Murugan, the true guru who, to ripe Siva-yogis of disciplined conduct and silence who eat only greens and with devotion pass beyond the thirty-six tattvas to the supportless state of release, gave in that very moment the teaching of jnana, so that their deluding faults fell away. The praise names him the fierce hero with twelve arms, the lord of Vayalur where blue lilies and pearl-bright pools spread fragrance, the golden-bodied lord of Avinasi, who gives grace to truth so that the false Kali age departs, at Pukkoliyur.","n":"ஆறாறு தத்துவ மேல் வீடு பற்று நிராதார நிலை: the thirty-six tattvas of Saiva doctrine are passed, and release is held as a state without support. The teaching acts instantly (அக்கணமே). The song asks nothing for the poet beyond not forgetting the feet.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["guru","gnaanam","thaththuvam","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:213"},"p:947":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":947,"t":["மதப்பட்டவி சாலக போலமு","முகப்பிற்சன வாடையு மோடையு","மருக்கற்புர லேபல லாடமு ...... மஞ்சையாரி","வயிற்றுக்கிடு சீகர பாணியு","மிதற்செக்கர்வி லோசன வேகமு","மணிச்சத்தக டோரபு ரோசமு ...... மொன்றுகோல","விதப்பட்டவெ ளானையி லேறியு","நிறைக்கற்பக நீழலி லாறியும்","விஷத்துர்க்கன சூளிகை மாளிகை ...... யிந்த்ரலோகம்","விளக்கச்சுரர் சூழ்தர வாழ்தரு","பிரப்புத்வகு மாரசொ ரூபக","வெளிப்பட்டெனை யாள்வய லு¡ரிலி ...... ருந்துவாழ்வே","இதப்பட்டிட வேகம லாலய","வொருத்திக்கிசை வானபொ னாயிர","மியற்றப்பதி தோறுமு லாவிய ...... தொண்டர்தாள","இசைக்கொக்கவி ராசத பாவனை","யுளப்பெற்றொடு பாடிட வேடையி","லிளைப்புக்கிட வார்மறை யோனென ...... வந்துகானிற்","றிதப்பட்டெதி ரேபொதி சோறினை","யவிழ்த்திட்டவி நாசியி லேவரு","திசைக்குற்றச காயனு மாகிம ...... றைந்துபோமுன்","செறிப்பித்த கராவதின் வாய்மக","வழைப்பித்தபு ராணக்ரு பாகர","திருப்புக்கொளி யூருடை யார்புகழ் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The first half pictures Indra's white elephant and Indra living in the shade of the kalpaka tree in his splendid city with the gods around him, and asks the Kumara of lordly form to appear and rule the poet, the life of Vayalur. The second half recalls Siva's acts for his devotee Sundarar: the thousand gold coins sought from shrine to shrine for the woman of Kamalalayam, the Lord coming as a Brahmin in the forest to untie a packet of rice for the tired singer, and at Avinasi the child called back from the crocodile's mouth; Murugan is the Lord whom that lord of Pukkoliyur praises.","n":"The song is almost wholly praise and its hero is Sundarar (தொண்டர்), served by Siva as friend and helper (சகாயன்). செறிப்பித்த கராவதின் வாய் மக அழைப்பித்த is the boy swallowed by a crocodile at Avinasi and restored at Sundarar's song. The first half is hard to construe, and its link to the plea எனை ஆள் is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["adiyaar","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:213"},"p:948":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":948,"t":["வனப்புற்றெழு கேத மேவு கோகிலம்","அழைக்கப்பொரு மார னேவ தாமலர்","மருத்துப்பயில் தேரி லேறி மாமதி ...... தொங்கலாக","மறுத்துக்கடல் பேரி மோத வேயிசை","பெருக்கப்படை கூடி மேலெ ழாவணி","வகுத்துக்கொடு சேம மாக மாலையில் ...... வந்துகாதிக்","கனக்கப்பறை தாய ளாவ நீள்கன","கருப்புச்சிலை காம ரோவில் வாளிகள்","களித்துப்பொர வாசம் வீசு வார்குழல் ...... மங்கைமார்கள்","கலைக்குட்படு பேத மாகி மாயும","துனக்குப்ரிய மோக்ரு பாக ராஇது","கடக்கப்படு நாம மான ஞானம ...... தென்றுசேர்வேன்","புனத்திற்றினை காவ லான காரிகை","தனப்பொற்குவ டேயு மோக சாதக","குனித்தப்பிறை சூடும் வேணி நாயகர் ...... நன்குமாரா","பொறைக்குப்புவி போலு நீதி மாதவர்","சிறக்கத்தொகு பாசி சோலை மாலைகள்","புயத்துற்றணி பாவ சூர னாருயிர் ...... கொண்டவேலா","சினத்துக்கடி வீசி மோது மாகட","லடைத்துப்பிசி தாச னாதி மாமுடி","தெறிக்கக்கணை யேவு வீர மாமனும் ...... உ ந்திமீதே","செனித்துச்சதுர் வேத மோது நாமனு","மதித்துப்புகழ் சேவ காவி ழாமலி","திருப்புக்கொளி மேவு தேவர்கள் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet pictures evening as a battle: Kama, called by the cuckoo, rides the chariot of the breeze with flower arrows and the moon as his hanging garland, the sea as his war drum, and draws his sugarcane bow; caught in the arts of fragrant-haired women he is dying, and asks whether this pleases the compassionate one and when he will reach the jnana that is the Name by which it is crossed. The praise names Murugan the lover of the millet-field girl, son of the lord with the crescent in his hair, the vel that took Sura's life, and nephew of the heroic Rama who dammed the sea and made Ravana's crowns fly, at the festival town of Pukkoliyur.","n":"The akam convention of evening as the hour when love's pain grows, usually given to a woman, is here the poet's own, armed as Kama's march (படை கூடி மேலெழா அணி வகுத்து). கடக்கப்படு நாமமான ஞானம்: the knowledge that crosses it is a Name, presumably Murugan's.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maalai","kaamam","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:213"},"p:949":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":949,"t":["தீராப் பிணிதீர சீவாத் ...... துமஞான","ஊராட் சியதான ஓர்வாக் ...... கருள்வாயே","பாரோர்க் கிறைசேயே பாலாக் ...... கிரிராசே","போராற் பெரியோனே §ப்ருர்ப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A short song: the poet asks for the one word whose rule is the knowledge of the soul, to cure the disease that does not end. The praise calls Murugan the son of the lord of the world's people, the child, the king of hills, great in battle, at Perur.","n":"தீராப் பிணி, the disease that will not go, is probably birth itself; ஓர் வாக்கு is a teaching word, an upadesa. சீவாத்தும ஞான ஊராட்சி, the 'town-rule' of the knowledge of the soul, is an odd figure and its sense is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["gnaanam","noy","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:214"},"p:950":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":950,"t":["மைச்ச ரோருக நச்சு வாள்விழி","மானா ரோடே நானார் நீயா ...... ரெனுமாறு","வைத்த போதக சித்த யோகியர்","வாணாள் கோணாள் வீணாள் காணா ...... ரதுபோலே","நிச்ச மாகவு மிச்சை யானவை","நேரே தீரா யூரே பேரே ...... பிறவேயென்","நிட்க ராதிகண் முற்பு காதினி","நீயே தாயாய் நாயேன் மாயா ...... தருள்வாயே","மிச்ச ரோருக வச்ர பாணியன்","வேதா வாழ்வே நாதா தீதா ...... வயலு¡ரா","வெற்பை யூடுரு வப்ப டாவரு","வேலா சீலா பாலா காலா ...... யுதமாளி","பச்சை மாமயில் மெச்ச வேறிய","பாகா சூரா வாகா போகா ...... தெனும்வீரா","பட்டி யாள்பவர் கொட்டி யாடினர்","பர்ரு ராசூழ் §ப்ரு ராள்வார் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks that, as the yogis who have been taught see no span of life, no ill-omened day and no wasted day, his desires too should truly end, together with town, name and all else, so that he can say to women with poisoned sword-eyes 'who am I, who are you'; he asks Murugan to be his mother and give grace to him, a dog. The praise names him the life of Indra and Brahma, beyond all, the lord of Vayalur, the vel that pierced the mountain, the bearer of the cock-banner riding the green peacock, and the lord at Perur, ruled by the Lord who danced there.","n":"நானார் நீயார் எனுமாறு: the end of desire is pictured as no longer recognising the women at all. வாணாள் கோணாள் வீணாள் காணார், yogis who see neither lifetime nor bad days nor wasted days, sets them outside time. பட்டி ஆள்பவர் கொட்டியாடினர் is Siva of Perur, Patteeswarar, and his dance. Much of the plea's syntax is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["pattru","yogam","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:214"},"p:951":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":951,"t":["கலைஞரெணுங் கற்புக் கலியுகபந் தத்துக்","கடனபயம் பட்டுக் ...... கசடாகுங்","கருமசடங் கச்சட் சமயிகள்பங் கிட்டுக்","கலகலெனுங் கொட்புற் ...... றுடன்மோதும்","அலகில்பெருந் தர்க்கப் பலகலையின் பற்றற்","றரவியிடந் தப்பிக் ...... குறியாத","அறிவையறிந் தப்பற் றதனினொடுஞ் சற்றுற்","றருள்வசனங் கிட்டப் ...... பெறலாமோ","கொலைஞரெனுங் கொச்சைக் குறவரிளம் பச்சைக்","கொடிமருவுஞ் செச்சை ...... புயமார்பா","கொடியநெடுங் கொக்குக் குறுகவுணன் பட்டுக்","குரைகடல்செம் பச்சக் ...... கரவாளச்","சிலைபகஎண் டிக்குத் திகிரிகளும் பத்துத்","திசைகளினுந் தத்தச் ...... செகமேழுந்","திருகுசிகண் டிப்பொற் குதிரைவிடுஞ் செட்டித்","திறல கொடும் பைக்குட் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether, leaving attachment to the countless arts of argument, the rites and quarrels of the six sects who divide and clamour, and the learning of scholars bound to this Kali age, he can know the knowledge that cannot be pointed to, abide a little in it, and receive the word of grace. The praise names Murugan the chest embraced by the young green creeper of the Kuravar called killers, the one before whom the cruel Krauncha and the demon perished while the sea, the encircling mountains and the ten directions shook, and the merchant (செட்டி) who rides the golden horse that is the peacock, at Kodumbai.","n":"குறியாத அறிவை அறிந்து: the knowledge sought is one without a mark or sign, set against தர்க்கப் பலகலை, the arts of disputation. The epithet செட்டி, the merchant, recalls the story of Murugan coming as a trader; the text names only the title and the peacock as his horse. அரவியிடம் தப்பி is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["samayam","gnaanam","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:215"},"p:952":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":952,"t":["ஈர மோடுசி ரித்துவ ருத்தவும்","நாத கீதந டிப்பிலு ருக்கவும்","ஏவ ராயினு மெத்திய ழைக்கவு ...... மதராஜன்","ஏவின் மோதுக ணிட்டும ருட்டவும்","வீதி மீதுத லைக்கடை நிற்கவும்","ஏறு மாறும னத்தினி னைக்கவும் ...... விலைகூறி","ஆர பாரத னத்தைய சைக்கவு","மாலை யோதிகு லைத்துமு டிக்கவும்","ஆடை சோரஅ விழ்த்தரை சுற்றவும் ...... அதிமோக","ஆசை போல்மன இஷ்டமு ரைக்கவு","மேல்வி ழாவெகு துக்கம்வி ளைக்கவும்","ஆன தோதக வித்தைகள் கற்பவ ...... ருறவாமோ","பார மேருப ருப்பத மத்தென","நேரி தாகஎ டுத்துட னட்டுமை","பாக ராரப டப்பணி சுற்றிடு ...... கயிறாகப்","பாதி வாலிபி டித்திட மற்றொரு","பாதி தேவர்பி டித்திட லக்ஷ¤மி","பாரி சாதமு தற்பல சித்திகள் ...... வருமாறு","கீர வாரிதி யைக்கடை வித்ததி","காரி யாயமு தத்தைய ளித்தக்ரு","பாளு வாகிய பச்சுரு வச்சுதன் ...... மருகோனே","கேடி லாவள கைப்பதி யிற்பல","மாட கூடம லர்ப்பொழில் சுற்றிய","கீர னுருறை சத்தித ரித்தருள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet lists what courtesans do: laugh with charm, melt men with song and dance, call anyone at all, wound with Kama's eyes, stand at the doorway in the street, name a price, sway their breasts, loosen and tie their hair, let their garments slip, speak desire, fall on a man and leave him in great sorrow; he asks whether kinship with women who learn these arts of deceit is fit for him. The praise tells the churning of the milk sea, Meru as the churning stick and the serpent as rope, Vali holding half and the gods the other half, until Lakshmi, the Parijata and many powers rose, and names Murugan nephew of the green Achyuta who gave out the nectar, the bearer of the vel at Keeranur, a town of mansions and groves like Kubera's Alaka.","n":"விலைகூறி: naming a price is the plainest courtesan charge, and தோதக வித்தைகள், arts of deceit, are something learned. In the churning, பாதி வாலி பிடித்திட gives Vali the place usually held by the demons.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:216"},"p:953":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":953,"t":["தரங்க வார்குழற் றநுநுதல் ...... விழியாலம்","தகைந்த மாமுலைத் துடியிடை ...... மடமாதர்","பரந்த மாலிருட் படுகுழி ...... வசமாகிப்","பயந்து காலனுக் குயிர்கொடு ...... தவியாமல்","வரந்த ராவிடிற் பிறரெவர் ...... தருவாரே","மகிழ்ந்து தோகையிற் புவிவலம் ...... வருவோனே","குரும்பை மாமுலைக் குறமகண் ...... மணவாளா","குளந்தை மாநகர்த் தளியுறை ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks that, falling under the sway of women with wave-like hair, bow-brows, poison eyes and drum-like waists into the wide pit of delusion, he not give up his life to Death in fear and suffering; if Murugan does not grant the boon, who else will? The praise names him the one who circled the earth on the peacock, the groom of the Kura girl, and the lord of the temple at Kulandai.","n":"வரம் தராவிடில் பிறர் எவர் தருவாரே: the plea rests on there being no other giver. The circling of the earth recalls the contest for the fruit.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kootru","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:217"},"p:954":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":954,"t":["இலைச்சுருட் கொடுத்தணைத் தலத்திருத் திமட்டைகட்","கிதத்தபுட் குரற்கள்விட் ...... டநுராகம்","எழுப்பிமைக் கயற்கணைக் கழுத்தைமுத் தமிட்டணைத்","தெடுத்திதழ்க் கடித்துரத் ...... திடைதாவி","அலைச்சலுற் றிலச்சையற் றரைப்பைதொட் டுழைத்துழைத்","தலக்கணுற் றுயிர்க்களைத் ...... திடவேதான்","அறத்தவித் திளைத்துறத் தனத்தினிற் புணர்ச்சிபட்","டயர்க்குமிப் பிறப்பினித் ...... தவிராதோ","கொலைச்செருக் கரக்கரைக் கலக்குமிக் ககுக்குடக்","கொடித்திருக் கரத்தபொற் ...... பதிபாடுங்","குறித்தநற் றிருப்புகழ்ப் ப்ரபுத்துவக் கவித்துவக்","குருத்துவத் தெனைப்பணித் ...... தருள்வோனே","தலைச்சுமைச் சடைச்சிவற் கிலக்கணத் திலக்கியத்","தமிழ்த்ரயத் தகத்தியற் ...... கறிவோதுஞ்","சமர்த்தரிற் சமர்த்தபச் சிமத்திசைக் குளுத்தமத்","தனிச்சயத் தினிற்பிளைப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes lovemaking frankly, giving betel, embracing, making bird-cries, kissing the neck, biting the lips and toiling without shame until his life is exhausted, and asks whether this birth of union, in which virtue is lost, will not end. The praise names Murugan who commanded him to sing the good Thiruppugazh with its lordship, poetic gift and authority of a guru, the one with the cock-banner, the most able of the able who teaches Siva and Agastya the grammar and literature of the three Tamils, at Thanichayam in the west.","n":"The book names itself: குறித்த நற் றிருப்புகழ் ... எனைப் பணித்து அருள்வோனே, Murugan as the one who set the poet to sing. அறத் தவித்து: the union is the loss of dharma, and the plea is that the birth itself, not only the habit, end.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","piravi","pugazh","thamizh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:218"},"p:955":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":955,"t":["உ ரைத்த சம்ப்ரம வடிவு திரங்கிக்","கறுத்த குஞ்சியும் வெளிறிய பஞ்சொத்","தொலித்தி டுஞ்செவி செவிடுற வொண்கட் ...... குருடாகி","உ ரத்த வெண்பலு நழுவிம தங்கெட்","டிரைத்து கிண்கிணெ னிருமலெ ழுந்திட்","டுளைப்பு டன்தலை கிறுகிறெ னும்பித் ...... தமுமேல்கொண்","டரத்த மின்றிய புழுவினும் விஞ்சிப்","பழுத்து ளஞ்செயல் வசனம் வரம்பற்","றடுத்த பெண்டிரு மெதிர்வர நிந்தித் ...... தனைவோரும்","அசுத்த னென்றிட வுணர்வத குன்றித்","துடிப்ப துஞ்சிறி துளதில தென்கைக்","கவத்தை வந்துயி ரலமரு மன்றைக் ...... கருள்வாயே","திரித்தி ரிந்திரி ரிரிரிரி ரின்றிட்","டுடுட்டு டுண்டுடு டுடுடுடு டுண்டுட்","டிகுட்டி குண்டிகு டிகுடிகு டிண்டிட் ...... டிகுதீதோ","திமித்தி மிந்திமி திமிதிமி யென்றிட்","டிடக்கை துந்துமி முரசு முழங்கச்","செருக்க ளந்தனில் நிருதர் தயங்கச் ...... சிலபேய்கள்","தரித்து மண்டையி லுதிர மருந்தத்","திரட்ப ருந்துகள் குடர்கள் பிடுங்கத்","தருக்கு சம்புகள் நிணமது சிந்தப் ...... பொரும்வேலா","தடச்சி கண்டியில் வயலியி லன்பைப்","படைத்த நெஞ்சினி லியல்செறி கொங்கிற்","றனிச்ச யந்தனி லினிதுறை கந்தப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet pictures the body's ruin in old age: its once-proud form shrivels, black hair whitens like cotton, the ears go deaf, the eyes blind, the teeth loosen, cough and phlegm rise, the head reels with bile, the body rots worse than a bloodless worm, mind, deeds and speech run out of bounds, the wife who came reviles him, all call him unclean, sense fails and the pulse is barely there; on that day of the soul's distress he asks for grace. The praise is a battle in drum syllables where ghouls drink blood from skulls and kites pull entrails, and names Murugan the lord of Vayalur and of Thanichayam in Kongu.","n":"The decay is listed from the outside in, ending in துடிப்பதும் சிறிது உளது இலது, a pulse barely there or gone. அவத்தை here is the soul's distress at death. தடச்சிகண்டியில் in the last lines is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","nilaiyaamai","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:218"},"p:956":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":956,"t":["அலகில வுணரைக் கொன்ற தோளென","மலைதொளை யுருவச் சென்ற வேலென","அழகிய கனகத் தண்டை சூழ்வன ...... புண்டா£க","அடியென முடியிற் கொண்ட கூதள","மெனவன சரியைக் கொண்ட மார்பென","அறுமுக மெனநெக் கென்பெ லாமுரு ...... கன்புறாதோ","கலகல கலெனக் கண்ட பேரொடு","சிலுகிடு சமயப் பங்க வாதிகள்","கதறிய வெகுசொற் பங்க மாகிய ...... பொங்களாவுங்","கலைகளு மொழியப் பஞ்ச பூதமு","மொழியுற மொழியிற் றுஞ்சு றாதன","கரணமு மொழியத் தந்த ஞானமி ...... ருந்தவாறென்","இலகுக டலைகற் கண்டு தேனொடு","மிரதமு றுதினைப் பிண்டி பாகுடன்","இனிமையி னுகருற் றெம்பி ரானொரு ...... கொம்பினாலே","எழுதென மொழியப் பண்டு பாரதம்","வடகன சிகரச் செம்பொன் மேருவில்","எழுதிய பவளக் குன்று தாதையை ...... யன்றுசூழ்","வலம்வரு மளவிற் சண்ட மாருத","விசையினும் விசையுற் றெண்டி சாமுக","மகிதல மடையக் கண்டு மாசுண ...... முண்டுலாவு","மரகத கலபச் செம்புள் வாகன","மிசைவரு முருகச் சிம்பு ளேயென","மதுரையில் வழிபட் டும்ப ரார்தொழு ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether his bones will not melt in love as he names Murugan's shoulder that killed the demons, the vel that pierced the hill, the golden-anklet lotus feet, the kuthalam flower on his crown, the chest that took the forest girl and the six faces, and marvels at the jnana Murugan gave, in which the clamour of the sects, the arts, the five elements and the restless instruments of mind all fell silent. The praise tells how Ganapati, who loves chickpeas, candy, honey and millet dough, wrote the Bharata with one tusk on Meru when asked, and how Murugan circled the world on the peacock faster than the wind while Ganapati circled his father, and names him worshipped by the gods at Madurai.","n":"The song is thanks rather than plea: தந்த ஞானம் இருந்தவாறு என், how wonderful the knowledge given. என்பெலாம் உருகு அன்பு, love that melts the bones, is a phrase of Saiva devotional poetry. The praise gives both sides of the fruit contest.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["anbu","gnaanam","samayam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:219"},"p:957":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":957,"t":["ஆனைமுக வற்கு நேரிளைய பத்த","ஆறுமுக வித்த ...... கமரேசா","ஆதியர னுக்கும் வேதமுதல் வற்கும்","ஆரணமு ரைத்த ...... குருநாதா","தானவர் குலத்தை வாள்கொடு துணித்த","சால்சதுர் மிகுத்த ...... திறல்வீரா","தாளிணைக ளுற்று மேவியப தத்தில்","வாழ்வொடு சிறக்க ...... அருள்வாயே","வானெழு புவிக்கு மாலுமய னுக்கும்","யாவரொரு வர்க்கு ...... மறியாத","மாமதுரை சொக்கர் மாதுமை களிக்க","மாமயில் நடத்து ...... முருகோனே","தேனெழு புனத்தில் மான்விழி குறத்தி","சேரமரு வுற்ற ...... திரள்தோளா","தேவர்கள் கருத்தில் மேவிய பயத்தை","வேல்கொடு தணித்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks for grace to live and flourish in the state of reaching Murugan's paired feet. The praise calls him the younger brother of the elephant-faced one, the six-faced one, the guru who taught the Veda to Hara and to Brahma, the hero who cut down the demons, and the one who made his peacock dance to delight Uma and Chokkar of Madurai, whom none in heaven or earth, not Vishnu or Brahma, can know, and who calmed the gods' fear with the vel.","n":"ஆதி அரனுக்கும் வேத முதல்வற்கும் ஆரணம் உரைத்த: here Murugan teaches both Siva and Brahma, not only binding Brahma for his ignorance.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["guru","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:219"},"p:958":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":958,"t":["பரவு நெடுங்கதி ருலகில் விரும்பிய","பவனி வரும்படி ...... யதனாலே","பகர வளங்களு நிகர விளங்கிய","இருளை விடிந்தது ...... நிலவாலே","வரையினி லெங்கணு முலவி நிறைந்தது","வரிசை தரும்பத ...... மதுபாடி","வளமொடு செந்தமி ழுரைசெய அன்பரு","மகிழ வரங்களு ...... மருள்வாயே","அரஹர சுந்தர அறுமுக என்றுனி","அடியர் பணிந்திட ...... மகிழ்வோனே","அசலநெ டுங்கொடி அமையுமை தன்சுத","குறமக ளிங்கித ...... மணவாளா","கருதரு திண்புய சரவண குங்கும","களபம ணிந்திடு ...... மணிமார்பா","கனக மிகும்பதி மதுரை வளம்பதி","யதனில் வளர்ந்தருள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet seems to compare Murugan's coming to the sun's procession that lights the world and the moonlight that ends the dark, and asks that, singing the feet that grant honour, he may speak rich Tamil so that devotees rejoice, and be given boons. The praise names Murugan who delights when devotees bow saying 'Arahara, Sundara, Arumuga', son of Uma the creeper of the mountain, the beloved of the Kura girl, Saravana with kumkum on his chest, who grew up in golden Madurai.","n":"The opening images (பரவு நெடுங்கதிர் உலகில் விரும்பிய பவனி, இருளை விடிந்தது நிலவாலே) have no clear subject and the reading of the first half is a guess. செந்தமிழ் உரைசெய அன்பரும் மகிழ: the gift sought is song that gladdens devotees.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","thamizh","adiyaar"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:219"},"p:959":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":959,"t":["பழிப்பர் வாழ்த்துவர் சிலசில பெயர்தமை","ஒருத்தர் வாய்ச்சுரு ளொருவர்கை யுதவுவர்","பணத்தை நோக்குவர் பிணமது தழுவுவர் ...... அளவளப் பதனாலே","படுக்கை வீட்டுனு ளவுஷத முதவுவர்","அணைப்பர் கார்த்திகை வருதென வுறுபொருள்","பறிப்பர் மாத்தையி லொருவிசை வருகென ...... அவரவர்க் குறவாயே","அழைப்ப ராஸ்திகள் கருதுவ ரொருவரை","முடிக்கி யோட்டுவ ரழிகுடி யரிவையர்","அலட்டி னாற்பிணை யெருதென மயலெனு ...... நரகினிற் சுழல்வேனோ","அவத்த மாய்ச்சில படுகுழி தனில்விழும்","விபத்தை நீக்கியு னடியவ ரருடனெனை","அமர்த்தி யாட்கொள மனதினி லருள்செய்து ...... கதிதனைத் தருவாயே","தழைத்த சாத்திர மறைபொரு ளறிவுள","குருக்கள் போற்சிவ நெறிதனை யடைவொடு","தகப்ப னார்க்கொரு செவிதனி லுரைசெய்த ...... முருகவித் தகவேளே","சமத்தி னாற்புகழ் சனகியை நலிவுசெய்","திருட்டு ராக்கத னுடலது துணிசெய்து","சயத்த யோத்தியில் வருபவ னரிதிரு ...... மருமகப் பரிவோனே","செழித்த வேற்றனை யசுரர்க ளுடலது","பிளக்க வோச்சிய பிறகம ரர்கள்பதி","செலுத்தி யீட்டிய சுரபதி மகள்தனை ...... மணமதுற் றிடுவோனே","திறத்தி னாற்பல சமணரை யெதிரெதிர்","கழுக்க ளேற்றிய புதுமையை யினிதொடு","திருத்த மாய்ப்புகழ் மதுரையி லுறைதரும் ...... அறுமுகப் பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes courtesans who abuse some men and praise others, give one the betel from their mouths and another a helping hand, look only at money and embrace even a corpse in measure with it, give potions in the bedroom, embrace saying 'Karthigai is coming' and take a man's goods, say 'come once in Masi', call men while thinking of their property, finish with one and drive him off; he asks whether he must whirl like a yoked threshing bull in the hell called infatuation, and asks to be lifted from these pits, set among Murugan's devotees, ruled and given the goal. The praise names him the one who, like a guru learned in shastra and Veda, told the Saiva path into his father's ear, nephew of Rama who killed the demon who stole Janaki, husband of Indra's daughter, and the one who impaled the Jains, at Madurai.","n":"பணத்தை நோக்குவர் பிணமது தழுவுவர்: they embrace even a corpse if the money is right, close to the Kural's image of the courtesan's embrace. The festival calls (கார்த்திகை வருது, மாத்தையில்) show the gifts expected at feasts. The remedy is company: உனடியவர் உடன் எனை அமர்த்தி. பல சமணரைக் கழுக்கள் ஏற்றிய identifies Murugan with Sambandar at Madurai.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","mayal","adiyaar"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:219"},"p:960":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":960,"t":["கீத வாசனைம லர்க்குழல்பி லுக்கிமுக","மாய வேல்விழிபு ரட்டிநகை முத்தமெழ","தேமல் மார்பினிள பொற்கிரிப ளப்பளென ...... தொங்கலாரஞ்","சேரு மோவியமெ னச்சடமி னுக்கிவெகு","வாசை நேசமும்வி ளைத்துஇடை யுற்றவரி","சேலை காலில்விழ விட்டுநடை யிட்டுமயி ...... லின்கலாபச்","சாதி யாமெனவெ ருட்டிநட மிட்டுவலை","யான பேர்தமையி ரக்கவகை யிட்டுகொடி","சாக நோய்பிணிகொ டுத்திடர்ப டுத்துவர்கள் ...... பங்கினூடே","தாவி மூழ்கிமதி கெட்டவல முற்றவனை","பாவ மானபிற விக்கடலு ழப்பவனை","தாரு லாவுபத பத்தியிலி ருத்துவது ...... மெந்தநாளோ","வாத வூரனைம தித்தொருகு ருக்களென","ஞான பாதம்வெளி யிட்டுநரி யிற்குழுவை","வாசி யாமெனந டத்துவகை யுற்றரச ...... னன்புகாண","மாடை யாடைதர பற்றிமுன கைத்துவைகை","யாறின் மீதுநட மிட்டுமணெ டுத்துமகிழ்","மாது வாணிதரு பிட்டுநுகர் பித்தனருள் ...... கந்தவேளே","வேத லோகர்பொனி லத்தர்தவ சித்தரதி","பார சீலமுனி வர்க்கமுறை யிட்டலற","வேலை யேவியவு ணக்குலமி றக்கநகை ...... கொண்டசீலா","வேத மீணகம லக்கணர்மெய் பச்சைரகு","ராம ¡£ணமயி லொக்கமது ரைப்பதியின்","மேவி வாழமரர் முத்தர்சிவ பத்தர்பணி ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes a courtesan's display, fragrant hair, rolling spear-eyes, pearl smile, swinging chain, glittering body, the sari let fall to her feet, a walk like a peacock's dance, the net cast for men whom she makes beg and then gives disease and suffering, and asks when Murugan will seat in devotion to his garlanded feet one who leapt into their share, lost his mind and churns in the sea of sinful birth. The praise recounts Siva's Madurai sports: honouring Vathavurar as guru and revealing the jnana feet, turning jackals into horses for the king, dancing on the Vaigai bund and eating the woman's puttu, and calls Murugan the son of that madman; it also names the vel that ended the asuras and the green peacock like Rama.","n":"நோய் பிணி கொடுத்து: the women give disease, a rare explicit charge. The praise is Madurai's own legend: வாதவூரனை, நரியின் குழுவை வாசியாம் என, வைகையாறின் மீது மண் எடுத்து, மாது தரு பிட்டு, Manikkavachakar's story. Several words in the last lines are corrupt.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["kaamam","noy","paththi","adiyaar"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:219"},"p:961":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":961,"t":["புருவச் செஞ்சிலை கொண்டிரு கணைவிழி","யெறியக் கொங்கையி ரண்டெனு மதகரி","பொரமுத் தந்தரு மிங்கித நயவித ...... மதனாலே","புகலச் சங்கிசை கண்டம தனிலெழ","உ ருவச் செந்துவர் தந்தத ரமுமருள்","புதுமைத் தம்பல முஞ்சில தரவரு ...... மனதாலே","பருகித் தின்றிட லஞ்சுக மெனமன","துருகிக் குங்கும சந்தன மதிவியர்","படியச் சம்ப்ரம ரஞ்சித மருள்கல ...... வியினாலே","பலருக் குங்கடை யென்றனை யிகழவு","மயலைத் தந்தரு மங்கையர் தமைவெகு","பலமிற் கொண்டிடு வண்டனு முனதடி ...... பணிவேனோ","திருவைக் கொண்டொரு தண்டக வனமிசை","வரவச் சங்கொடு வந்திடு முழையுடல்","சிதறக் கண்டக வெங்கர னொடுதிரி ...... சிரனோடு","திரமிற் றங்கிய கும்பக னொருபது","தலைபெற் றும்பரை வென்றிடு மவனொடு","சிலையிற் கொன்றமு குந்தன லகமகிழ் ...... மருகோனே","மருவைத் துன்றிய பைங்குழ லுமையவள்","சிவனுக் கன்பரு ளம்பிகை கவுரிகை","மலையத் தன்தரு சங்கரி கருணைசெய் ...... முருகோனே","வடவெற் பங்கய லன்றணி குசசர","வணையிற் றங்கிய பங்கய முகதமிழ்","மதுரைச் சங்கிலி மண்டப இமையவர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women whose brows are bows, eyes arrows and breasts rutting elephants, whose coral lips and betel he thought pleasure to drink and eat, melting in their embrace with kumkum and sandal soaked in sweat, and asks whether even he, the rogue despised by all who took these women as his strength, will bow to Murugan's feet. The praise tells of Rama with Sita in the Dandaka forest killing Khara and Trisiras, Kumbhakarna and the ten-headed one with his bow, names Murugan the nephew of that Mukunda and the son of Uma, Gauri, Shankari, and the lotus-faced one born on the reed bed of the north, at Tamil Madurai.","n":"பலருக்கும் கடை என்றனை இகழவும் ... வண்டனும் உனதடி பணிவேனோ: the self-abuse sets up the question whether even such a one can still bow. முழையுடல் சிதற and சங்கிலி மண்டப are obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","punarchi","kutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:219"},"p:962":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":962,"t":["முகமெ லாநெய் பூசித் தயங்கு","நுதலின் மீதி லேபொட் டணிந்து","முருகு மாலை யோதிக் கணிந்த ...... மடமாதர்","முதிரு மார பாரத் தனங்கள்","மிசையி லாவி யாய்நெக் கழிந்து","முடிய மாலி லேபட் டலைந்து ...... பொருள்தேடிச்","செகமெ லாமு லாவிக் கரந்து","திருட னாகி யேசற் றுழன்று","திமிர னாகி யோடிப் பறந்து ...... திரியாமல்","தெளியு ஞான மோதிக் கரைந்து","சிவபு ராண நூலிற் பயின்று","செறியு மாறு தாளைப் பரிந்து ...... தரவேணும்","அகர மாதி யாமக்ஷ ரங்க","ளவனி கால்வி ணாரப் பொடங்கி","அடைய வேக ரூபத்தி லொன்றி ...... முதலாகி","அமரர் காண வேயத்த மன்றில்","அரிவை பாட ஆடிக் கலந்த","அமல நாத னார்முற் பயந்த ...... முருகோனே","சகல வேத சாமுத்ரி யங்கள்","சமய மாறு லோகத்ர யங்கள்","தரும நீதி சேர்தத் துவங்கள் ...... தவயோகம்","தவறி லாம லாளப் பிறந்த","தமிழ்செய் மாறர் கூன்வெப் பொடன்று","தவிர ஆல வாயிற் சிறந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks that, instead of dying on the breasts of oiled and garlanded women, wandering in delusion, roaming the world for wealth, hiding and becoming a thief and running in darkness, he chant and melt in clear jnana, study the Siva Purana books, and be given Murugan's feet. The praise names Siva, who from the letter A and the elements became the one form and the first, and danced in the hall before the gods while his consort sang, and calls Murugan his son, born as the maker of Tamil who, so that the Vedas, the six faiths, the three worlds, dharma, the tattvas and yoga might be kept, cured the Pandya Maran's hunchback and fever at Alavai.","n":"திருடனாகி: the search for money to spend on women ends in theft. The praise says outright that Murugan was born as Sambandar (தமிழ்செய் மாறர் கூன் வெப்பொடு அன்று தவிர), who cured the king Koon Pandiyan of his hunch and fever.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","selvam","gnaanam","thamizh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:219"},"p:963":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":963,"t":["ஏலப்பனி நீரணி மாதர்கள்","கானத்தினு மேயுற வாடிடு","மீரத்தினு மேவளை சேர்கர ...... மதனாலும்","ஏமக்கிரி மீதினி லேகரு","நீலக்கய மேறிய னேரென","ஏதுற்றிடு மாதன மீதினு ...... மயலாகிச்","சோலைக்குயில் போல்மொழி யாலுமெ","தூசுற்றிடு நூலிடை யாலுமெ","தோமிற்கத லீநிக ராகிய ...... தொடையாலும்","சோமப்ரபை வீசிய மாமுக","சாலத்திலு மாகடு வேல்விழி","சூதத்தினு நானவ மேதின ...... முழல்வேனோ","ஆலப்பணி மீதினில் மாசறு","மாழிக்கிடை யேதுயில் மாதவ","னானைக்கினி தாயுத வீயருள் ...... நெடுமாயன்","ஆதித்திரு நேமியன் வாமன","னீலப்புயல் நேர்தரு மேனியன்","ஆரத்துள வார்திரு மார்பினன் ...... மருகோனே","கோலக்கய மாவுரி போர்வையர்","ஆலக்கடு வார்கள் நாயகர்","கோவிற்பொறி யால்வரு மாசுத ...... குமரேசா","கூர்முத்தமிழ் வாணர்கள் வீறிய","சீரற்புத மாநக ராகிய","கூடற்பதி மீதினில் மேவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether he must wander every day, infatuated by women's songs and bangled hands, breasts like elephants on a golden hill, cuckoo speech, thread-thin waists, plantain thighs, moon faces and the trickery of spear-eyes. The praise names Murugan nephew of Vishnu who sleeps on the serpent in the sea, helped the elephant, became Vamana and wears the tulasi garland, the son born from the sparks of the eye of Siva clad in elephant hide with poison in his throat, at Koodal, the wonder-city of poets of the three Tamils.","n":"கோவிற் பொறியால் வரு: Murugan born from the sparks of Siva's eye, stated plainly. The plea is a list of the woman's parts, each one a reason for wandering (உழல்வேனோ).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","mayal"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:219"},"p:964":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":964,"t":["கலைமேவு ஞானப் பிரகாசக் கடலாடி ஆசைக் ...... கடலேறிப்","பலமாய வாதிற் பிறழாதே பதிஞான வாழ்வைத் ...... தருவாயே","மலைமேவு மாயக் குறமாதின் மனைமேவு வாலக் ...... குமரேசா","சிலைவேட சேவற் கொடியோனே திருவாணி கூடற் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A short song: the poet asks that, having bathed in the sea of the light of jnana found in the arts and crossed the sea of desire, he not stray in the many deluding disputes, and be given the life of knowledge of the Lord. The praise names Murugan who dwelt in the house of the Kura girl of the mountain, the hunter with the bow, bearer of the cock-banner, at Koodal.","n":"பதிஞான வாழ்வு: the life sought is knowledge of Pati, the Lord. Two seas are set against each other, the sea of jnana to bathe in and the sea of desire to cross. திருவாணி கூடல் is unexplained.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","avaa","pathi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:219"},"p:965":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":965,"t":["நீதத் துவமாகி - நேமத் ...... துணையாகிப்","பூதத் தயவான - போதைத் ...... தருவாயே","நாதத் தொனியோனே - ஞானக் ...... கடலோனே","கோதற் றமுதானே - கூடற் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A very short song: the poet asks to be given the understanding of kindness to living beings, with truth and justice as its nature and discipline as its support. The praise calls Murugan the one of the sound of nada, the sea of jnana and the flawless nectar, at Koodal.","n":"பூதத் தயவான போதம்: compassion to all creatures is asked for as a kind of understanding. The opening நீதத்துவம் can be split as நீதி தத்துவம் or otherwise, so the first line is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["arul","naadham","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:219"},"p:966":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":966,"t":["மனநினை சுத்தஞ் சூது காரிகள்","அமளிவி ளைக்குங் கூளி மூளிகள்","மதபல நித்தம் பாரி நாரிக ...... ளழகாக","வளைகுழை முத்தும் பூணும் வீணிகள்","விழலிகள் மெச்சுண் டாடி பாடிகள்","வரமிகு வெட்கம் போல வோடிகள் ...... தெருவூடே","குனகிகள் பக்ஷம் போல பேசிகள்","தனகிக ளிச்சம் பேசி கூசிகள்","குசலிகள் வர்க்கஞ் சூறை காரிகள் ...... பொருளாசைக்","கொளுவிக ளிஷ்டம் பாறி வீழ்பட","அருளமு தத்தின் சேரு மோர்வழி","குறிதனி லுய்த்துன் பாத மேறிட ...... அருள்தாராய்","தனதன தத்தந் தான தானன","டுடுடுடு டுட்டுண் டூடு டூடுடு","தகுதிகு தத்தந் தீத தோதக ...... எனபேரி","தவில்முர சத்தந் தாரை பூரிகை","வளைதுடி பொற்கொம் பார சூரரை","சமர்தனில் முற்றும் பாறி நூறிட ...... விடும்வேலா","தினைவன நித்தங் காவ லாளியள்","நகைமுறை முத்தின் பாவை மான்மகள்","திகழ்பெற நித்தங் கூடி யாடிய ...... முருகோனே","திரிபுர நக்கன் பாதி மாதுறை","யழகிய சொக்கன் காதி லோர்பொருள்","செலவரு ளித்தென் கூடல்மேவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet lists courtesans as schemers, bed-quarrellers, goblins, women with mutilated noses, proud and fat, idle women who flatter, eat, dance and sing, who run down the street feigning modesty, coax and speak as if on a man's side, plunder and hook men for desire of wealth, and asks that their hold scatter and that Murugan place him on the one way that joins the nectar of grace and grant that he climb to his feet. The praise is a battle in drum syllables in which the vel destroyed the Suras, names Murugan who daily played with the millet-guarding daughter of the deer, and who whispered a meaning into the ear of Chokkan of south Koodal, the burner of the three cities with the woman as his half.","n":"The plea is a list of feminine epithets ending in -கள் (காரிகள், மூளிகள், வீணிகள், பேசிகள்), a set form of the book. குனகிகள் பக்ஷம் போல பேசிகள், those who talk as if on your side, is the courtesan's double dealing. சொக்கன் காதில் ஓர் பொருள் places the teaching of Siva in Madurai.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","thiruvadi","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:219"},"p:967":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":967,"t":["முத்துநவ ரத்நமணி பத்திநிறை சத்தியிட","மொய்த்தகிரி முத்திதரு ...... வெனவோதும்","முக்கணிறை வாக்குமருள் வைத்தமுரு கக்கடவுள்","முப்பதுமு வர்க்கசுர ...... ரடிபேணி","பத்துமுடி தத்தும்வகை யுற்றகணை விட்டஅரி","பற்குனனை வெற்றிபெற ...... ரத்முரும்","பச்சைநிற முற்றபுய லச்சமற வைத்தபொருள்","பத்தர்மன துற்றசிவம் ...... அருள்வாயே","தித்திமிதி தித்திமிதி திக்குகுகு திக்குகுகு","தெய்த்ததென தெய்தததென ...... தெனனான","திக்குவென மத்தளமி டக்கைதுடி தத்ததகு","செச்சரிகை செச்சரிகை ...... யெனஆடும்","அத்தனுட னொத்தநட நித்ரிபுவ னத்திநவ","சித்தியருள் சத்தியருள் ...... புரிபாலா","அற்பவிடை தற்பமது முற்றுநிலை பெற்றுவள","ரற்கனக பத்மபுரி ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks Murugan, who gave grace to the word of the three-eyed Lord who chants him as the shakti, the hill and the seed of release, and whose feet the thirty-three classes of gods worship, to grant him the Sivam that dwells in devotees' hearts, the treasure held by the green-cloud Vishnu who loosed the arrow at the ten heads and drove the chariot to Arjuna's victory. The praise is a dance in drum syllables in which Murugan, equal to his dancing father, grants the nine siddhis, at the City of the Golden Lotus.","n":"The song rebuilds the book's opening song 6 phrase by phrase: முத்து ... சத்தி ... முத்தி, the three-eyed one who chants, the thirty-three gods, the arrow at the ten heads, the chariot driven for a devotee, the green cloud. Here the treasure asked for is named as சிவம் in devotees' hearts. கனக பத்மபுரி is Madurai of the golden lotus tank. The phrase division of the first half is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sivam","paththi","siththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:219"},"p:968":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":968,"t":["ஆடல்மத னம்பின் மங்கைய","ராலவிழி யின்பி றங்கொளி","யாரமத லமபு கொங்கையின் ...... மயலாகி","ஆதிகுரு வின்ப தங்களை","நீதியுட னன்பு டன்பணி","யாமல்மன நைந்து நொந்துட ...... லழியாதே","வேடரென நின்ற ஐம்புல","ணாலுகர ணங்க ளின்தொழில்","வேறுபட நின்று ணர்ந்தருள் ...... பெறுமாறென்","வேடைகெட வந்து சிந்தனை","மாயையற வென்று துன்றிய","வேதமுடி வின்ப ரம்பொரு ...... ளருள்வாயே","தாடகையு ரங்க டிந்தொளிர்","மாமுனிம கஞ்சி றந்தொரு","தாழ்வறந டந்து திண்சிலை ...... முறியாவொண்","ஜாநகித னங்க லந்தபின்","ஊரில்மகு டங்க டந்தொரு","தாயர்வ சனஞ்சி றந்தவன் ...... மருகோனே","சேடன்முடி யுங்க லங்கிட","வாடைமுழு தும்ப ரந்தெழ","தேவர்கள்ம கிழ்ந்து பொங்கிட ...... நடமாடுஞ்","சீர்மயில மஞ்சு துஞ்சிய","சோலைவளர் செம்பொ னுந்திய","ஸ்ரீபுருட மங்கை தங்கிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks that he not, infatuated by the breasts of women whose eyes are Kama's arrows, fail to bow with justice and love to the feet of the First Guru and so waste in mind and body; that he stand apart from the work of the five senses, which stand like hunters, and the four inner instruments, and understand and receive grace; and that Murugan come and end his craving, defeat the maya of thought and grant the supreme reality at the end of the Veda. The praise tells Rama's story, Tataka's death, the guarding of Vishvamitra's sacrifice, the breaking of the bow, marriage to Janaki and giving up the crown for a mother's word, names Murugan his nephew, and pictures his peacock dancing so that Adisesha's heads shake, at Sri Purushamangai.","n":"வேடர் என நின்ற ஐம்புலன் நாலு கரணங்கள்: the senses are hunters and the inner instruments (mind, intellect, ego, thought) are named as four; release is standing apart from their work (தொழில் வேறுபட நின்று). வேதமுடிவின் பரம்பொருள் is the supreme of Vedanta.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maayai","vedaantham","guru","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:220"},"p:969":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":969,"t":["கார்குழல்கு லைந்த லைந்து வார்குழையி சைந்த சைந்து","காதலுறு சிந்தை யுந்து ...... மடமானார்","காமுகர கங்க லங்க போர்மருவ முந்தி வந்த","காழ்கடிய கும்ப தம்ப ...... இருகோடார்","பேர்மருவு மந்தி தந்தி வாரணஅ னங்க னங்க","பேதையர்கள் தங்கள் கண்கள் ...... வலையாலே","பேரறிவு குந்து நொந்து காதலில லைந்த சிந்தை","பீடையற வந்து நின்ற ...... னருள்தாராய்","ஏர்மருவு தண்டை கொண்ட தாளசைய வந்த கந்த","ஏகமயி லங்க துங்க ...... வடிவேலா","ஏமனுமை மைந்த சந்தி சேவலணி கொண்டு அண்டர்","ஈடேறஇ ருந்த செந்தில் ...... நகர்வாழ்வே","தேருகள்மி குந்த சந்தி வீதிகள ணிந்த கெந்த","சீரலர்கு ளுந்து யர்ந்த ...... பொழிலோடே","சேரவெ யிலங்கு துங்க வாவிக ளிசைந்தி ருந்த","ஸ்ரீ புருட மங்கை தங்கு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of women with loosened hair and swinging earrings who stir desire, whose breasts stand firm against lovers, and whose eyes are a net in which his great understanding was lost; with a heart tossed in love, he asks Murugan to come, stand before him and give grace to end his trouble. The praise names Kanda on the one peacock with the vel and anklets, son of Uma, holder of the cock-banner, the life of Senthil, and the lord of Sri Purushamangai with its chariot streets, fragrant groves and high pools.","n":"கண்கள் வலையாலே பேரறிவு குந்து: the eyes are a net that takes away understanding. மந்தி தந்தி வாரண அனங்கன் is a string of comparisons whose sense is unclear. The praise names Senthil though the song is placed at Purushamangai.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kan","kaamam","mayal"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:220"},"p:970":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":970,"t":["வேனின்மத னைந்து பாணம்விட நொந்து","வீதிதொறு நின்ற ...... மடவார்பால்","வேளையென வந்து தாளினில் விழுந்து","வேடைகெட நண்பு ...... பலபேசித்","தேனினு மணந்த வாயமுத முண்டு","சீதள தனங்க ...... ளினின்மூழ்கித்","தேடிய தனங்கள் பாழ்பட முயன்று","சேர்கதிய தின்றி ...... யுழல்வேனோ","ஆனிரை துரந்து மாநில மளந்தொ","ராலிலையி லன்று ...... துயில்மாயன்","ஆயர்மனை சென்று பால்தயி ரளைந்த","ஆரண முகுந்தன் ...... மருகோனே","வானவர் புகழ்ந்த கானவர் பயந்த","மானொடு விளங்கு ...... மணிமார்பா","மாமறை முழங்கு ஸ்ரீபுருட மங்கை","மாநக ரமர்ந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says that, pained by the five arrows of Kama in spring, he went to women standing in every street, came at the right hour, fell at their feet, talked much friendship, drank the nectar of their honeyed mouths and sank into their cool breasts, and worked until the wealth he had sought ran to waste; he asks whether he must wander without any goal. The praise names Murugan nephew of Krishna, who drove the cattle, measured the earth, slept on a banyan leaf and stirred the milk and curd in cowherd homes, the one whose chest shines with the deer-girl of the hunters, at Sri Purushamangai, where the Vedas sound.","n":"தேடிய தனங்கள் பாழ்பட: money earned and wasted on the women of the street is what leaves him with no goal (சேர்கதி அதின்றி).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","selvam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:220"},"p:971":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":971,"t":["கரங்க மலமின தரம்ப வளம்வளை","களம்ப கழிவிழி ...... மொழிபாகு","கரும்ப முதுமலை குரும்பை குருகுப","கரும்பி டியினடை ...... யெயின்மாதோ","டரங்க நககன தனங்கு தலையிசை","யலங்க நியமுற ...... மயில்மீதே","அமர்ந்து பவவினை களைந்து வருகொடி","யவந்த கனகல ...... வருவாயே","தரங்க முதியம கரம்பொ ருததிரை","சலந்தி நதிகும ...... ரெனவான","தலம்ப ரவ மறை புலம்ப வருசிறு","சதங்கை யடிதொழு ...... பவராழி","இரங்கு தொலைதிரு வரங்கர் மருகப","னிரண்டு புயமலை ...... கிழவோனே","இலங்கு தரதமிழ் விளங்க வருதிரு","இலஞ்சி மருவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The first half paints the hunter girl, her eyes, sweet speech, young breasts and elephant-like gait, and asks Murugan to come seated on the peacock with her, clearing the karma of birth, when cruel Death arrives. The praise tells of the Veda lamenting as the little anklets on his feet are worshipped, names him nephew of Vishnu of Srirangam and twelve-armed lord of the hills, and ends at Ilanji where Tamil shines.","n":"The plea itself is only the last line of the first half: பவவினை களைந்து வரு கொடிய அந்தகன் அலவருவாயே, come when Death comes; everything before it describes Valli (எயின்மாது), so the song asks for the divine couple together at death. The opening run of syllables (கரங்க மலமின தரம்ப ...) is hard to divide, and the river with crocodiles and waves that hails him 'Kumara' is not clearly identified.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kootru","piravi","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:221"},"p:972":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":972,"t":["கொந்தள வோலை குலுங்கிட வாளிச்","சங்குட னாழி கழன்றிட மேகக்","கொண்டைகள் மாலை சரிந்திட வாசப் ...... பனிநீர்சேர்","கொங்கைகள் மார்பு குழைந்திட வாளிக்","கண்கயல் மேனி சிவந்திட கோவைக்","கொஞ்சிய வாயி ரசங்கொடு மோகக் ...... கடலு¡டே","சந்திர ஆர மழிந்திட நூலிற்","பங்கிடை யாடை துவண்டிட நேசத்","தந்திட மாலு ததும்பியு மூழ்குற் ...... றிடுபோதுன்","சந்திர மேனி முகங்களு நீலச்","சந்த்ரகி மேல்கொ டமர்ந்திடு பாதச்","சந்திர வாகு சதங்கையு மோசற் ...... றருள்வாயே","சுந்தரர் பாட லுகந்திரு தாளைக்","கொண்டுநல் தூது நடந்தவ ராகத்","தொந்தமொ டாடி யிருந்தவள் ஞானச் ...... சிவகாமி","தொண்டர்க ளாக மமர்ந்தவள் நீலச்","சங்கரி மோக சவுந்தரி கோலச்","சுந்தரி காளி பயந்தரு ளானைக் ...... கிளியோனே","இந்திர வேதர் பயங்கெட சூரைச்","சிந்திட வேல்கொ டெறிந்துநல் தோகைக்","கின்புற மேவி யிருந்திடு வேதப் ...... பொருளோனே","எண்புன மேவி யிருந்தவள் மோகப்","பெண்திரு வாளை மணந்திய லார்சொற்","கிஞ்சியளாவு மிலஞ்சி விசாகப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes lovemaking in detail, earrings shaking, bangles slipping, hair falling loose, sandal paste rubbed away and the waist-cloth slackening as he drowns in the sea of passion, and asks that at that very moment Murugan grant him the vision of his moon-like faces, seated on the blue peacock, and his anklet-bearing feet. The praise names the goddess Sivakami, who dances united with Siva, the one who walked as messenger for Sundarar's song, and calls Murugan the younger brother of the elephant-faced god, the vel against Sura, the meaning of the Veda, and Visakha of Ilanji wedded to the girl of the millet field.","n":"The plea does not ask to escape the embrace but to see Murugan in the middle of it (மூழ்குற்றிடு போது ... அருள்வாயே). சுந்தரர் பாடல் உகந்து இரு தாளைக் கொண்டு நல் தூது நடந்தவர் is Siva going on foot as Sundarar's envoy, named here through the goddess who shares his body.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["punarchi","kaamam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:221"},"p:973":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":973,"t":["சுரும்பணி கொண்டல் நெடுங்குழல் கண்டு","துரந்தெறி கின்ற ...... விழிவேலால்","சுழன்று சுழன்று துவண்டு துவண்டு","சுருண்டு மயங்கி ...... மடவார்தோள்","விரும்பி வரம்பு கடந்து நடந்து","மெலிந்து தளர்ந்து ...... மடியாதே","விளங்கு கடம்பு விழைந்தணி தண்டை","விதங்கொள் சதங்கை ...... யடிதாராய்","பொருந்த லமைந்து சிதம்பெற நின்ற","பொலங்கிரி யொன்றை ...... யெறிவோனே","புகழ்ந்து மகிழ்ந்து வணங்கு குணங்கொள்","புரந்தரன் வஞ்சி ...... மணவாளா","இரும்புன மங்கை பெரும்புள கஞ்செய்","குரும்பை மணந்த ...... மணிமார்பா","இலஞ்சியில் வந்த இலஞ்சிய மென்று","இலஞ்சி யமர்ந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks that, whirling, wilting and swooning at the spear of women's eyes, he not desire their shoulders, cross the bounds and so waste away and die, and asks for the feet adorned with kadamba, thandai and anklets. The praise names the vel that struck the golden hill, the bridegroom of Indra's daughter, the chest that wedded the girl of the millet field, and the lord who came in the pool as a treasure and dwells at Ilanji.","n":"The plea is built of doubled verbs (சுழன்று சுழன்று துவண்டு துவண்டு), the body acting out its own ruin. The last line plays on இலஞ்சி three times: the pool (the Saravana pond where he was born), இலஞ்சியம (a treasure or refuge), and the shrine.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","mayal","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:221"},"p:974":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":974,"t":["மாலையில் வந்து மாலை வழங்கு","மாலை யநங்கன் ...... மலராலும்","வாடை யெழுந்து வாடை செறிந்து","வாடை யெறிந்த ...... அனலாலுங்","கோல மழிந்து சால மெலிந்து","கோமள வஞ்சி ...... தளராமுன்","கூடிய கொங்கை நீடிய அன்பு","கூரவு மின்று ...... வரவேணும்","கால னடுங்க வேலது கொண்டு","கானில் நடந்த ...... முருகோனே","கான மடந்தை நாண மொழிந்து","காத லிரங்கு ...... குமரேசா","சோலை வளைந்து சாலி விளைந்து","சூழ மிலஞ்சி ...... மகிழ்வோனே","சூரிய னஞ்ச வாரியில் வந்த","சூரனை வென்ற ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"Speaking for a lovesick girl, the singer tells how the evening, the garland, Kama's flower arrows and the burning north wind have stripped her beauty and worn her thin, and asks Murugan to come today, before the tender creeper gives way, so that the love of the breasts once joined may grow. The praise names Murugan who made Death tremble with the vel, walked in the forest and, shame cast aside, pleaded his love to the forest girl, the lord of Ilanji amid groves and paddy, who conquered Sura when even the sun feared him.","n":"This is an akam song, not the poet's own confession: the girl is spoken of in the third person (கோமள வஞ்சி தளராமுன்). Each image is a pun repeated three times, மாலை (evening and garland) and வாடை (north wind, fading, the wind's blast). The praise answers the girl's longing with Murugan's own: he too pleaded with the forest girl (காதல் இரங்கு குமரேசா).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","kaamanoy","melivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:221"},"p:975":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":975,"t":["ஏடுக்கொத் தாரலர் வார்குழ","லாடப்பட் டாடைநி லாவிய","ஏதப்பொற் றோள்மிசை மூடிய ...... கரமாதர்","ஏதத்தைப் பேசுப ணாளிகள்","வீசத்துக் காசைகொ டாடிகள்","ஏறிட்டிட் டேணியை வீழ்விடு ...... முழுமாயர்","ம¨டொக்கக் கூடிய காமுகர்","மூழ்குற்றுக் காயமொ டேவரு","வாயுப்புற் சூலைவி யாதிக ...... ளிவைமேலாய்","மாசுற்றுப் பாசம் விடாசம","னூர்புக்குப் பாழ்நர கேவிழு","மாயத்தைச் சீவியு னாதர ...... வருள்வாயே","தாடுட்டுட் டூடுடு டீடிமி","டூடுட்டுட் டூடுடு டாடமி","தானத்தத் தானத னாவென ...... வெகுபேரி","தானொத்தப் பூதப சாசுகள்","வாய்விட்டுச் சூரர்கள் சேனைகள்","சாகப்பொற் றோகையி லேறிய ...... சதிரோனே","கூடற்கச் சாலைசி ராமலை","காவைப்பொற் காழிவெ ளூர்திகழ்","கோடைக்கச் சூர்கரு வூரிலு ...... முயர்வான","கோதிற்பத் தாரொடு மாதவ","சீலச்சித் தாதியர் சூழ்தரு","கோலக்குற் றாலமு லாவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet names the courtesans who speak harm, dance for the coins thrown to them, lift a man up and then pull the ladder away, and the lustful men who sink with them, till wind diseases, colic and other ailments take the body and Yama, who never lets go of his noose, drags it to his town and a ruined hell; he asks Murugan to cut away this illusion and give his support. The praise sounds the battle in drum syllables, with ghosts gaping as Sura's armies die before the rider of the golden peacock, and sets Kutralam, thronged with faultless devotees, ascetics and siddhas, above Koodal, Siramalai, Kazhi, Karuvur and other shrines.","n":"ஏறிட்டிட்டு ஏணியை வீழ்விடு முழுமாயர்: the women raise a man up and kick away the ladder, an image of the bargain's end. The song carries the courtesan's harm through to disease, Yama's noose and hell, one chain of consequence. The list of shrines in the praise ranks Kutralam above Madurai and the other famed centres.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["varaivinmagalir","noy","kootru","naragam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:222"},"p:976":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":976,"t":["வேதத்திற் கேள்வி யிலாதது","போதத்திற் காண வொணாதது","வீசத்திற் று¡ர மிலாதது ...... கதியாளர்","வீதித்துத் தேடரி தானது","ஆதித்தற் காய வோணாதது","வேகெத்துத் தீயில் வெகாதது ...... சுடர்கானம்","வாதத்துக் கேயவி யாதது","காதத்திற் பூவிய லானது","வாசத்திற் பேரொளி யானது ...... மத்முறு","மாயத்திற் காய மதாசல","தீதர்க்குத் தூரம தாகிய","வாழ்வைச்சற் காரம தாஇனி ...... யருள்வாயே","கதத்திற் காயம தாகும","தீதித்தித் தீதிது தீதென","காதற்பட் டோதியு மேவிடு ...... கதிகாணார்","காணப்பட் டேகொடு நோய்கொடு","வாதைப்பட் டேமதி தீதக","லாமற்கெட் டேதடு மாறிட ...... அடுவோனே","கோதைப்பித் தாயொரு வேடுவ","ரூபைப்பெற் றேவன வேடுவர்","கூடத்துக் கேகுடி யாய்வரு ...... முருகோனே","கோதிற்பத் தாரொடு மாதவ","சீலச்சித் தாதியர் சூழ்தரு","கோலக்குற் றாலமு லாவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks to be given, as an honour, the life that is not learned in the Veda, not seen by understanding, has no distance in space, is hard for seekers of salvation to find, is not burned by fire or shaken by wind, the great light within fragrance, and far from those drunk with maya. The praise speaks obscurely of those who recite 'this is evil' yet never see the path, and names Murugan who, mad for the girl, took a hunter's form and came to live among the hunters, the lord of Kutralam among devotees, ascetics and siddhas.","n":"The whole plea is a string of negations ending in -அது (கேள்வியிலாதது, காணவொணாதது, வேகாதது), the way the Upanishads speak of the Self as what fire does not burn nor wind dry; what is asked for is that state itself (வாழ்வை ... அருள்வாயே), not escape from women or death. The opening of the second half is hard to divide and the reading of it is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["veedu","oli","meyyunarvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:222"},"p:977":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":977,"t":["முத்தோலை தனைக்கி ழித்தயி","லைப்போரிக லிச்சி வத்துமு","கத்தாமரை யிற்செ ருக்கிடும் ...... விழிமானார்","முற்றாதிள கிப்ப ணைத்தணி","கச்சார மறுத்த நித்தில","முத்தார மழுத்து கிர்க்குறி ...... யதனாலே","வித்தார கவித்தி றத்தினர்","பட்டோலை நிகர்த்தி ணைத்தெழு","வெற்பான தனத்தில் நித்தலு ...... முழல்வேனோ","மெய்த்தேவர் துதித்தி டத்தரு","பொற்பர்கம லப்ப தத்தினை","மெய்ப்பாக வழுத்தி டக்ருபை ...... புரிவாயே","பத்தான முடித்த லைக்குவ","டிற்றாட வரக்க ருக்கிறை","பட்டாவி விடச்செ யித்தவன் ...... மருகோனே","பற்பாசன்மி கைச்சி ரத்தைய","றுத்தாதவ னைச்சி னத்துறு","பற்போகவு டைத்த தற்பரன் ...... மகிழ்வோனே","கொத்தார்கத லிப்ப ழக்குலை","வித்தாரவ ருக்கை யிற்சுளை","கொத்தோடுதி ரக்கு தித்தெழு ...... கயலாரங்","கொட்டாசுழி யிற்கொ ழித்தெறி","சிற்றாறுத னிற்க ளித்திடு","குற்றாலரி டத்தி லுற்றருள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether he must wander daily over breasts that bear nail-marks like palm leaves scored by poets of elaborate verse, captive to women whose eyes tear at their pearl ear-ornaments and fight like spears, and asks for grace to praise truly the golden lotus feet that the gods praise. The praise names Rama, who made the ten-headed king of demons give up his breath, and Siva, who cut off Brahma's extra head and broke the sun's teeth in anger, and ends at Kutralam where jackfruit, plantain and leaping fish fill the little river.","n":"வித்தார கவித்திறத்தினர் பட்டோலை நிகர்த்து: the poet compares the scored breasts to a poet's written palm leaf, a craftsman's self-aware simile. பற்பாசன் மிகைச் சிரத்தை அறுத்து ஆதவனைச் சினத்துறு பல் போக உடைத்த: Siva cutting Brahma's fifth head and breaking the sun's teeth, the latter a detail of the destruction of Daksha's sacrifice.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","punarchi","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:222"},"p:978":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":978,"t":["வாட்படச் சேனைபட வோட்டியொட் டாரையிறு","மாப்புடைத் தாளரசர் ...... பெருவாழ்வும்","மாத்திரைப் போதிலிடு காட்டினிற் போமெனஇல்","வாழ்க்கைவிட் டேறுமடி ...... யவர்போலக்","கோட்படப் பாதமலர் பார்த்திளைப் பாறவினை","கோத்தமெய்க் கோலமுடன் ...... வெருபக்","கோப்புடைத் தாகியல மாப்பினிற் பாரிவரு","கூத்தினிப் பூரையிட ...... அமையாதோ","தாட்படக் கோபவிஷ பாப்பினிற் பாலன்மிசை","சாய்த்தொடுப் பாரவுநிள் ...... கழல்தாவிச்","சாற்றுமக் கோரவுரு கூற்றுதை தார்மவுலி","தாழ்க்கவஜ் ராயுதனு ...... மிமையோரும்","ஆட்படச் சாமபர மேட்டியைக் காவலிடு","மாய்க்குடிக் காவலவு ...... ததிமீதே","ஆர்க்குமத் தானவரை வேற் கரத் தால்வரையை","ஆர்ப்பெழச் சாடவல ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"Seeing that the great life of kings whose swords put armies to flight goes in a moment to the burning ground, the poet asks whether he may not, like the devotees who left household life and rose, look on the flower feet, rest from his weariness, and end the dance of coming into the world in a karma-strung body. The praise tells of Siva leaping with his anklet to kick the fierce form of Death when its noose struck at a boy, names Indra and the gods bowing, and calls Murugan the guardian of Aykudi who imprisoned Brahma and shattered the demons and the mountain by the sea with his vel.","n":"The plea draws its lesson from the fall of kings, not from courtesans: மாத்திரைப் போதில் இடுகாட்டினில் போம், gone in an instant to the cremation ground, and its model is the renunciant (இல்வாழ்க்கை விட்டு ஏறும் அடியவர்). The boy and the noose are the story of Markandeya; the kick is Siva's, not Murugan's. The plea's last lines (கோப்புடைத்தாகி அலமாப்பினில் ...) are hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","thuravu","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:223"},"p:979":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":979,"t":["கருப்புச் சாப னனைய இளைஞர்கள்","ப்ரமிக்கக் காத லுலவு நெடுகிய","கடைக்கட் பார்வை யினிய வனிதையர் ...... தனபாரங்","களிற்றுக் கோடு கலச மலிநவ","மணிச்செப் போடை வனச நறுமலர்","கனத்துப் பாளை முறிய வருநிக ...... ரிளநீர்போற்","பொருப்பைச் சாடும் வலியை யுடையன","அறச்சற் றான இடையை நலிவன","புதுக்கச் சார வடமொ டடர்வன ...... எனநாளும்","புகழ்ச்சிப் பாட லடிமை யவரவர்","ப்ரியப்பட் டாள வுரைசெ யிழிதொழில்","பொகட்டெப் போது சரியை கிரியைசெய் ...... துயிர்வாழ்வேன்","இருட்டுப் பாரில் மறலி தனதுடல்","பதைக்கக் கால்கொ டுதைசெய் தவன்விழ","எயிற்றுப் போவி யமர ருடலவர் ...... தலைமாலை","எலுப்புக் கோவை யணியு மவர்மிக","அதிர்த்துக் காளி வெருவ நொடியினில்","எதிர்த்திட் டாடும் வயிர பயிரவர் ...... நவநீத","திருட்டுப் பாணி யிடப முதுகிடை","சமுக்கிட் டேறி யதிர வருபவர்","செலுத்துப் பூத மலகை யிலகிய ...... படையாளி","செடைக்குட் பூளை மதிய மிதழிவெ","ளெருக்குச் சூடி குமர வயலியல்","திருப்புத் தூரில் மருவி யுறைதரு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet recalls women whose glances bewilder young men and whose breasts he likened to elephant tusks, pots, gem caskets, lotus buds and tender coconuts, and asks when he will cast off the base trade of making praise-songs, as a servant, to please each patron, and live doing chariyai and kiriyai. The praise is all of Siva: the one who kicked Death, Bhairava garlanded with gods' skulls and bones who dances against Kali, the rider on the back of the bull who is the butter-thief, leader of the ghost army, wearing moon, konrai and white erukku, and Murugan his son at Thiruppuththur among the fields.","n":"புகழ்ச்சிப் பாடல் அடிமை அவரவர் ப்ரியப்பட்டு ஆள உரைசெய் இழிதொழில்: the confession is of the poet's craft, praise poetry made to please patrons, with its stock comparisons of women's breasts, which the song itself first rehearses. The remedy is ritual service (சரியை கிரியை). நவநீதத் திருட்டுப் பாணி இடபம் makes Krishna, the butter-thief, Siva's bull, a Saiva claim of precedence.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sariyai","kiriyai","pugazh","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:224"},"p:980":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":980,"t":["வேலை தோற்க விழித்துக் காதினில்","ஓலை காட்டி நகைத்துப் போதொரு","வீடுகாட்டி யுடுத்தப் போர்வையை ...... நெகிழ்வாகி","மேனி காட்டி வளைத்துப் போர்முலை","யானை காட்டி மறைத்தத் தோதக","வீறு காட்டி யெதிர்த்துப் போரெதிர் ...... வருவார்மேல்","கால மேற்க வுழப்பிக் கூறிய","காசு கேட்டது கைப்பற் றாஇடை","காதி யோட்டி வருத்தப் பாடுடன் ...... வருவார்போல்","காதல் போற்று மலர்ப்பொற் பாயலின்","மீத ணாப்பு மசட்டுச் சூளைகள்","காம நோய்ப்படு சித்தத் தீவினை ...... யொழியேனோ","ஆல கோட்டு மிடற்றுச் சோதிக","பாலி பார்ப்பதி பக்ஷத் தால்நட","மாடி தாத்திரி பட்சித் தாவென ...... வுமிழ்வாளி","ஆடல் கோத்த சிலைக்கைச் சேவக","னோடை பூத்த தளக்கட் சானவி","யாறு தேக்கிய கற்றைச் சேகர ...... சடதாரி","சீல மாப்பதி மத்தப் பாரிட","சேனை போற்றிடு மப்பர்க் கோதிய","சேத னார்த்தப்ர சித்திக் கேவரு ...... முருகோனே","சேல றாக்கயல் தத்தச் சூழ்வய","லு¡ர வேற்கர விப்ரர்க் காதர","தீர தீர்த்த திருப்புத் தூருறை ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes the courtesans who mock with their eyes, show a house, slip their cloaks and display their breasts, then harass the men who come at the agreed hour, demand the coins named, and drive them out midway when the money is not in hand; he asks whether he will not end the evil of a mind sick with love for these cheats on their golden beds. The praise names Siva of the poison throat, the skull-bearer who danced for Parvati, the archer whose arrow was the earth-swallower, who bears the Ganga in his hair and is praised by ghost armies, and Murugan who came to teach him meaning, at Thiruppuththur of holy waters.","n":"காசு கேட்டது கைப்பற்றா இடை காதி ஓட்டி: the money is the point, so this is the trade, not desire alone. தாத்திரி பட்சித்தா என உமிழ் வாளி: the arrow that 'ate the earth' is Vishnu, who became the arrow when Siva burned the triple city. சேதனார்த்த ப்ரசித்திக்கே வரு முருகோனே: Murugan teaching Siva, without naming Om.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","kaamanoy","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:224"},"p:981":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":981,"t":["ஊனாரு முட்பிணியு மானாக வித்தவுதட","லு¡தாரி பட்டொழிய ...... வுயிர்போனால்","ஊரார் குவித்துவர ஆவா வெனக்குறுகி","ஓயா முழக்கமெழ ...... அழுதோய","நானா விதச்சிவிகை மேலே கிடத்தியது","நாறா தெடுத்தடவி ...... யெரியூடே","நாணாமல் வைத்துவிட நீறாமெ னிப்பிறவி","நாடா தெனக்குனருள் ...... புரிவாயே","மானாக துத்திமுடி மீதே நிருத்தமிடு","மாயோனு மட்டொழுகு ...... மலர்மீதே","வாழ்வா யிருக்குமொரு வேதாவு மெட்டிசையும்","வானோரு மட்டகுல ...... கிரியாவும்","ஆனா வரக்கருடன் வானார் பிழைக்கவரு","மாலால முற்றவமு ...... தயில்வோன்முன்","ஆசார பத்தியுடன் ஞானாக மத்தையருள்","ஆடானை நித்தமுறை ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet follows the flesh-filled, sickly body to its end: when life goes the townsfolk gather and wail until they tire, lay it on a bier, carry it off before it stinks, and set it in the fire without shame until it is ash; he asks for grace so that he will not seek this birth again. The praise names Vishnu who dances on the serpent's hood, Brahma on the flower, the gods and the eight mountains, saved when Siva ate the halahala as nectar, and Murugan who, before that Siva, gave the Jnana Agama with right conduct and devotion, at Adanai.","n":"நாணாமல் வைத்துவிட: the kin burn the body 'without shame', with no more regard than for refuse. The remedy is not the body's improvement but an end of birth (இப்பிறவி நாடாது). ஆசார பத்தியுடன் ஞானாகமத்தை அருள் makes Murugan the teacher of the Agama to Siva, the Om story in another dress.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["yaakkai","piravi","nilaiyaamai","aagamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:225"},"p:982":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":982,"t":["கற்பக ஞானக் கடவுண்மு னண்டத்","திற்புத சேனைக் கதிபதி யின்பக்","கட்கழை பாகப் பமமுது வெண்சர்க் ...... கரைபால்தேன்","கட்டிள நீர்முக் கனிபய றம்பொற்","றொப்பையி னேறிட் டருளிய தந்திக்","கட்டிளை யாய்பொற் பதமதி றைஞ்சிப் ...... பரியாய","பொற்சிகி யாய்கொத் துருண்மணி தண்டைப்","பொற்சரி நாதப் பரிபுர என்றுப்","பொற்புற வோதிக் கசிவொடு சிந்தித் ...... தினிதேயான்","பொற்புகழ் பாடிச் சிவபத மும்பெற்","றுப்பொருள் ஞானப் பெருவெளி யும்பெற்","றுப்புக லாகத் தமுதையு முண்டிட் ...... டிடுவேனோ","தெற்பமு ளாகத் திரள்பரி யும்பற்","குப்பைக ளாகத் தசுரர்பி ணந்திக்","கெட்டையு மூடிக் குருதிகள் மங்குற் ...... செவையாகித்","திக்கய மாடச் சிலசில பம்பைத்","தத்தன தானத் தடுடுடு வென்கச்","செப்பறை தாளத் தகுதொகு வென்கச் ...... சிலபேரி","உ ற்பன மாகத் தடிபடு சம்பத்","தற்புத மாகத் தமரர்பு ரம்பெற்","றுட்செல்வ மேவிக் கனமலர் சிந்தத் ...... தொடுவேலா","உ ட்பொருள் ஞானக் குறமக ளும்பற்","சித்திரை நீடப் பரிமயில் முன்பெற்","றுத்தர கோசத் தலமுறை கந்தப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet hails Murugan as younger brother of the elephant god of wish-granting knowledge, lord of the bhuta host, who ate sugarcane, jaggery, sugar, milk, honey, tender coconut, the three fruits and lentils into his belly, and asks whether, bowing at the golden feet and naming the peacock and the jingling anklets with melting heart, he will sing that golden praise and gain Siva's state, the great open space of knowledge, and the nectar of refuge. The praise is a battle scene in drum syllables, horses and elephants heaped up, demons' corpses covering the directions and blood like red cloud, ending with the gods regaining their city and Valli, the hunter girl of inner meaning and knowledge, at Uttarakosamangai.","n":"The opening is a full offering-list to Ganapati, a rare frame, and Murugan is approached as his brother (தந்திக்கு இளையாய்). The goal is laid out in three steps: சிவபதம், ஞானப் பெருவெளி, and அமுது. உட்பொருள் ஞானக் குறமகள் gives Valli an allegorical title, the hunter girl who is the inner meaning of knowledge.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","sivam","aakaasam","amudham"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:226"},"p:983":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":983,"t":["வாலவயதாகி தாகியழ காகிமத னாகிபணி","வாணிபமொ டாடிமரு ளாடிவிளை யாடிவிழல்","வாழ்வுசத மாகிவலு வாகிமட கூடமொடு ...... பொருள்தேடி","வாசபுழு கேடுமல ரோடுமன மாகிமகிழ்","வாசனைக ளாதியிட லாகிமய லாகிவிலை","மாதர்களை மேவியவ ராசைதனி லேசுழல ...... சிலநாள்போய்த்","தோல்திரைக ளாகிநரை யாகிகுரு டாகியிரு","கால்கள்தடு மாறிசெவி மாறிபசு பாசபதி","சூழ்கதிகள் மாறிசுக மாறிதடி யோடுதிரி ...... யுறுநாளிற்","சூலைசொறி யீளைவலி வாதமொடு நீரிழிவு","சோகைகள மாலைசுர மோடுபிணி தூறிருமல்","சூழலுற மூலகசு மாலமென நாறியுட ...... லழிவேனோ","நாலுமுக னாதியரி யோமெனஅ தாரமுரை","யாதபிர மாவைவிழ மோதிபொரு ளோதுகென","நாலுசிர மோடுசிகை தூளிபட தாளமிடு ...... மிளையோனே","நாறிதழி வேணிசிவ ரூபகலி யாணிமுத","லீணமக வானைமகிழ் தோழவன மீதுசெறி","ஞானகுற மாதைதின காவில்மண மேவுபுகழ் ...... மயில்வீரா","ஓலமிடு தாடகைசு வாகுவள ரேழுமரம்","வாலியொடு நீலிபக னோடொருவி ராதனெழு","மோதகட லோடுவிறல் ராவணகு ழாமமரில் ...... பொடியாக","ஓகைதழல் வாளிவிடு மூரிதநு நேமிவளை","பாணிதிரு மார்பனரி கோசன்மரு காஎனவெ","யோதமறை ராமெசுர மேவுகும ராவமரர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet runs through a life: youth, beauty, play, trade, a worthless life thought to last a hundred years, building mansions and seeking wealth, perfumes and flowers, and whirling for some days in desire for price-women; then wrinkled skin, grey hair, blindness, faltering legs, deafness, a stick, and colic, itch, phlegm, wind, diabetes, anaemia, scrofula, fever and cough, until the body stinks like filth, and he asks whether he must perish so. The praise tells how Murugan struck the four-faced Brahma who could not explain the ground of Om, beating his four heads, names him son of the goddess and bridegroom of the hunter girl of knowledge, and lists Rama's deeds against Tataka, Subahu, the seven trees, Vali, Viradha, the sea and Ravana before ending at Rameswaram where the Vedas are chanted.","n":"The song is a life in two quatrains, built on chains of -ஆகி and -மாறி (ஆகி ... ஆகி, then மாறி ... மாறி), so that each stage becomes the next and each faculty changes away. ஓம் என அதாரம் உரையாத பிரமாவை விழ மோதி பொருள் ஓதுக என: the Om myth is explicit. பசு பாச பதி சூழ் கதிகள் மாறி may mean that even the soul's paths among bondage and the Lord fail in old age.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilamai","yaakkai","noy","piranavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:227"},"p:984":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":984,"t":["வானோர் வழுத்துனது பாதார பத்மமலர்","மீதே பணிக்கும்வகை ...... யறியாதே","மானார் வலைக்கணதி லேதூளி மெத்தையினி","லு¡டே யணைத்துதவு ...... மதனாலே","தேனோ கருப்பிலெழு பாகோ விதற்கிணைக","ளேதோ வெனக்கலவி ...... பலகோடி","தீரா மயக்கினொடு நாகா படத்திலெழு","சேறாடல் பெற்றதுய ...... ரொழியேனோ","மேனாடு பெற்றுவளர் சூராதி பற்கெதிரி","னூடேகி நிற்குமிரு ...... கழலோனே","மேகார வுக்ரபரி தானேறி வெற்றிபுனை","வீரா குறச்சிறுமி ...... மணவாளா","ஞானா பரற்கினிய வேதாக மப்பொருளை","நாணா துரைக்குமொரு ...... பெரியோனே","நாரா யணற்குமரு காவீறு பெற்றிலகு","ராமே சுரத்திலுறை ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"Not knowing how to bow at the lotus feet the gods praise, the poet tells of embracing women within the net of their eyes on soft beds, asking 'is it honey, is it cane syrup, what equals it?', and asks whether he will not end the sorrow of countless unions and endless delusion. The praise names the anklets that went against Sura, the fierce peacock, Valli's bridegroom, the great one who told Siva the meaning of Veda and Agama without shyness, and Vishnu's nephew at Rameswaram.","n":"The poet's question, தேனோ கருப்பில் எழு பாகோ இதற்கிணைகள் ஏதோ, is the lover's own praise of pleasure quoted against himself. ஞானபரற்கு இனிய வேதாகமப் பொருளை நாணாது உரைக்கும் பெரியோனே: the child teaching the father 'without shame', Murugan as guru to Siva.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["punarchi","kaamam","thiruvadi","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:227"},"p:985":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":985,"t":["அமல கமலவுரு சங்கந் தொனித்தமறை","அரிய பரமவெளி யெங்கும் பொலித்தசெய","லளவு மசலமது கண்டங் கொருத்தருள ...... வறியாத","தகர முதலுருகொ ளைம்பந் தொரக்ஷரமொ","டகில புவனநதி யண்டங் களுக்குமுத","லருண கிரணவொளி யெங்கெங் குமுற்றுமுதல் ...... நடுவான","கமல துரியமயி லிந்துங் கதிர்ப்பரவு","கனக நிறமுடைய பண்பம் படிக்கதவ","ககன சுழிமுனையி லஞ்சுங் களித்தமுத ...... சிவயோகம்","கருணை யுடனறிவி தங்கொண் டிடக்கவுரி","குமர குமரகுரு வென்றென் றுரைப்பமுது","கனிவு வரஇளமை தந்துன் பதத்திலெனை ...... யருள்வாயே","திமிலை பலமுருடு திந்திந் திமித்திமித","டுமுட டுமுடுமுட டுண்டுண் டுமுட்டுமுட","திகுட திகுடதிகு திந்திந் திகுர்த்திகுர்த ...... திகுதீதோ","செகண செகணசெக செஞ்செஞ் செகக்கணென","அகில முரகன்முடி யண்டம் பிளக்கவெகு","திமிர்த குலவிருது சங்கந் தொனித்தசுரர் ...... களமீதே","அமரர் முழுமிமலர் கொண்டங் கிறைத் தருள","அரிய குருகுகொடி யெங்குந் தழைத்தருள","அரியொ டயன்முனிவ ரண்டம் பிழைத்தருள ...... விடும்வேலா","அரியின் மகள்தனமொ டங்கம் புதைக்க முக","அழகு புயமொடணை யினபங் களித்துமகிழ்","அரிய மயிலயில்கொ டிந்தம் பலத்தின்மகிழ் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes a reality no one can know: the stainless lotus form, the sounding conch and Veda, the supreme space shining everywhere, the fifty-one letters from which the worlds, rivers and cosmic eggs begin, the red light everywhere, beginning and middle, the lotus and turiya where moon and sun spread their rays, and the Sivayoga of nectar enjoyed by the five in the sushumna of the sky; he asks that, knowing it with compassion and calling 'son of Gauri, Kumara, Kumaraguru', he be given youth and grace at the feet. The praise is a battle sounded in drum syllables, the vel sent so that gods, Hari, Brahma and the sages survive, and Murugan embracing Valli, daughter of Hari, at Inthampalam.","n":"The vocabulary is that of Tirumular's yoga: ககன சுழிமுனை (the sushumna in the sky of the head), துரியம், அமுத சிவயோகம், and ஐம்பந்தொரு அக்ஷரம் (the fifty-one letters). அரியின் மகள் makes Valli the daughter of Vishnu, a story the text names only in these two words. Much of the first half is a list whose syntax is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","naadi","amudham","oli"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:228"},"p:986":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":986,"t":["விரகற நோக்கியு முருகியும் வாழ்த்தியும்","விழிபுனல் தேக்கிட ...... அன்புமேன்மேல்","மிகவுமி ராப்பகல் பிறிதுப ராக்கற","விழைவுகு ராப்புனை ...... யுங்குமார","முருகஷ டாக்ஷர சரவண கார்த்திகை","முலைநுகர் பார்த்திப ...... என்றுபாடி","மொழிகுழ றாத்தொழு தழுதழு தாட்பட","முழுதும லாப்பொருள் தந்திடாயோ","பரகதி காட்டிய விரகசி லோச்சய","பரமப ராக்ரம ...... சம்பராரி","படவிழி யாற்பொரு பசுபதி போற்றிய","பகவதி பார்ப்பதி ...... தந்தவாழ்வே","இரைகடல் தீப்பட நிசிசரர் கூப்பிட","எழுகிரி யார்ப்பெழ ...... வென்றவேலா","இமையவர் நாட்டினில் நிறைகுடி யேற்றிய","எழுகரை நாட்டவர் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks that, gazing without guile, melting and praising, eyes full of tears and love growing ever greater night and day with no other distraction, singing 'Kumara, Muruga, six-lettered one, Saravana, who drank the milk of the Krittikas' with faltering speech, worshipping and weeping again and again, he become Murugan's servant and be given the wholly stainless meaning. The praise names Murugan who showed the supreme path, the son of Parvati and of Pasupati who fought Kama with his eye, the vel that set the sea afire and the seven hills roaring, and the lord who settled the gods in their land and rules the people of Ezhukarai Nadu.","n":"The plea asks for the signs of bhakti themselves, tears, melting, faltering speech (மொழி குழறா), weeping (அழுதழுது), rather than for release from women or death, and only then for the meaning (மலாப் பொருள்). ஷடாக்ஷர names the six-syllable mantra of Murugan.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["paththi","anbu","thondu","mandhiram"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:229"},"p:987":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":987,"t":["வழக்குச் சொற்பயில் வாற்ச ளப்படு","மருத்துப் பச்சிலை தீற்று மட்டைகள்","வளைத்துச் சித்தச சாத்தி ரக்கள ...... வதனாலே","மனத்துக் கற்களை நீற்று ருக்கிகள்","சுகித்துத் தெட்டிக ளூர்த்து திப்பரை","மருட்டிக் குத்திர வார்த்தை செப்பிகள் ...... மதியாதே","கழுத்தைக் கட்டிய ணாப்பி நட்பொடு","சிரித்துப் பற்கறை காட்டி கைப்பொருள்","கழற்றிக் கற்புகர் மாற்று ரைப்பது ...... கரிசாணி","கணக்கிட் டுப்பொழு தேற்றி வைத்தொரு","பிணக்கிட் டுச்சிலு காக்கு பட்டிகள்","கலைக்குட் புக்கிடு பாழ்த்த புத்தியை ...... யொழியேனோ","அழற்கட் டப்பறை மோட்ட ரக்கரை","நெருக்கிப் பொட்டெழ நூக்கி யக்கணம்","அழித்திட் டுக்குற வாட்டி பொற்றன ...... கிரிதோய்வாய்","அகப்பட் டுத்தமிழ் தேர்த்த வித்தகர்","சமத்துக் கட்டியி லாத்த முற்றவன்","அலைக்குட் கட்செவி மேற்ப டுக்கையி ...... லுறைமாயன்","உ ழைக்கட் பொற்கொடி மாக்கு லக்குயில்","விருப்புற் றுப்புணர் தோட்க்ரு பைக்கடல்","உ றிக்குட் கைத்தல நீட்டு மச்சுதன் ...... மருகோனே","உ ரைக்கச் செட்டிய னாய்ப்பன் முத்தமிழ்","மதித்திட் டுச்செறி நாற்க விப்பணர்","ஒடுக்கத் துச்செறி வாய்த்த லத்துறை ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes courtesans versed in deceitful talk and the arts of Kama, who feed men herbal potions, melt and cheat hard-hearted fools, speak base words, hug a man's neck, laugh showing stained teeth, take the wealth from his hand and assay its gold, count the hours and pick a quarrel to drive him off, and asks whether he will not rid himself of the ruined mind that enters their arts. The praise names Murugan who crushed the demons and wedded the hunter girl, Vishnu who lies on the serpent in the sea and stretched his hand into the butter-pot, and Murugan as the one who came as a merchant to honour Tamil, at Odukkaththu Cherivay.","n":"மருத்துப் பச்சிலை தீற்று: the women dose their men with herbal love-potions, a rarer charge than flattery. கற்புகர் மாற்று உரைப்பது: they test the touchstone value of the gold they take, the bargain made exact. உரைக்கச் செட்டியனாய்ப் பன் முத்தமிழ் மதித்திட்டு: Murugan coming as a merchant to esteem Tamil points to a local story the text does not tell, and the lines on the Tamil-learned one (தமிழ் தேர்த்த வித்தகர்) are hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","marundhu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:230"},"p:988":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":988,"t":["ஆகத் தேதப் பாமற் சேரிக்","கார்கைத் தேறற் ...... கணையாலே","ஆலப் பாலைப் போலக் கோலத்","தாயக் காயப் ...... பிறையாலே","போகத் தேசற் றேதற் பாயற்","பூவிற் றீயிற் ...... கருகாதே","போதக் காதற் போகத் தாளைப்","பூரித் தாரப் ...... பூணராயே","தோகைக் கேயுற் றேறித் தோயச்","சூர்கெட் டோடப் ...... பொரும்வேலா","சோதிக் காலைப் போதக் கூவத்","தூவற் சேவற் ...... கொடியோனே","பாகொத் தேசொற் பாகத் தாளைப்","பாரித் தார்நற் ...... குமரேசா","பாரிற் காமத் தூரிற் சீலப்","பாலத் தேவப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"Speaking for a girl in love, the singer asks that she not be scorched on her bed of flowers by Kama's honeyed arrows and by the crescent moon that burns like poison, and that Murugan come wearing his garland to fill her with joy. The praise names the vel that sent Sura running, the rooster banner that crows to call the dawn, and Murugan the bridegroom of the girl whose speech is like syrup, the lord of Kamaththur.","n":"The song is in short tight syllables (ஆகத் தேதப் பாமற் சேரிக் ...) and much of it is hard to divide; the reading of the plea as an akam lament for a girl, like 974, rests on the moon, Kama's arrows and the burning flower-bed. ஆலப் பாலைப் போல: the moon is like the halahala poison, though white as milk.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:231"},"p:989":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":989,"t":["மின்னார் பயந்த மைந்தர் தன்னா டினங்கு விந்து","வெவ்வே றுழன்று றன்று ...... மொழிகூற","விண்மேல் நமன்க ரந்து மண்மே லுடம்பொ ருங்க","மென்னா ளறிந்த டைந்து ...... உ யிர்போமுன்","பொன்னார் சதங்கை தண்டை முந்நூல் கடம்ப ணிந்து","பொய்யார் மனங்கள் தங்கு ...... மதுபோலப்","பொல்லே னிறைஞ்சி ரந்த சொன்னீ தெரிந்த ழுங்கு","புன்னா யுளுங்க வின்று ...... புகுவாயே","பன்னா ளிறைஞ்சு மன்பர் பொன்னா டுறங்கை தந்து","பன்னா கணைந்து சங்க ...... முறவாயிற்","பன்னூல் முழங்க லென்று விண்ணோர் மயங்க நின்று","பண்ணாது கின்ற கொண்டல் ...... மருகோனே","முன்னாய் மதன்க ரும்பு வின்னேர் தடந்தெ ரிந்து","முன்னோர் பொருங்கை யென்று ...... முனையாட","மொய்வார் நிமிர்ந்த கொங்கை மெய்ம்மாதர் வந்தி றைஞ்சு","முள்வாய் விளங்க நின்ற ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks that before his life goes, while the sons born of women quarrel among their own and speak against each other, Death hides in the sky, and the body wastes on earth knowing its day has come, Murugan, wearing golden anklets, thandai, the sacred thread and kadamba, enter his brief life as honey dwells in unfeigning hearts. The praise names Vishnu, the cloud who gives the heavenly world to devotees and lies on the serpent, and ends at Mulvai, where women whose bodies are bent like Kama's cane bow come to worship.","n":"The plea sets the quarrel of heirs beside the approach of Death (விண்மேல் நமன் கரந்து), the family already dividing what the dying man leaves. பொய்யார் மனங்கள் தங்கும் மதுபோல: Murugan is asked to enter as he stays in true hearts. The last quatrain and the lines on Vishnu are hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kootru","sutram","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:232"},"p:990":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":990,"t":["ஆலை யான மொழிக்கு மாளை யூடு கிழிக்கு","மால கால விழிக்கு ...... முறுகாதல்","ஆசை மாத ரழைக்கு மோசை யான தொனிக்கு","மார பார முலைக்கு ...... மழகான","ஓலை மேவு குழைக்கு மோடை யானை நடைக்கு","மோரை சாயு மிடைக்கு ...... மயல்மேவி","ஊறு பாவ வுறுப்பி லு¡றல் தேறு கரிப்பி","லு¡ர வோடு விருப்பி ...... லுழல்வேனோ","வேலை யாக வளைக்கை வேடர் பாவை தனக்கு","மீறு காத லளிக்கு ...... முகமாய","மேவு வேடை யளித்து நீடு கோல மளித்து","மீள வாய்மை தெளித்து ...... மிதண்மீது","மாலை யோதி முடித்து மாது தாள்கள் பிடித்து","வாயி லு¡றல் குடித்து ...... மயல்தீர","வாகு தோளி லணைத்து மாக மார்பொழி லுற்ற","வாகை மாநகர் பற்று ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet lists the women's speech sweet as cane juice, eyes deadly as Death's poison, calling voices, heavy breasts, ear-ornaments, elephant gait and slender waist, and asks whether, in infatuation, he will wander in the bitterness and the moisture of the sinful body. The praise tells how Murugan gave the hunter girl surpassing love, took on the hunter's guise, then on the platform in the field combed her hair, held her feet, drank the nectar of her mouth and embraced her to end her longing, at Vagaimanagar of groves.","n":"The two halves use the same words: the poet wanders in மயல் and in the ஊறல் of the body, while Murugan drinks the ஊறல் of Valli's mouth so that her மயல் ends. The god's love is told as tenderly and physically as the poet's sin, and the song leaves the contrast unstated.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["mayal","kaamam","punarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:233"},"p:991":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":991,"t":["திருகு செறிந்த குழலை வகிர்ந்து","முடிமலர் கொண்டொ ...... ரழகாகச்","செயவரு துங்க முகமும் விளங்க","முலைகள் குலுங்க ...... வருமோக","அரிவையர் தங்கள் வலையில் விழுந்து","அறிவு மெலிந்து ...... தளராதே","அமரர் மகிழ்ந்து தொழுது வணங்கு","னடியிணை யன்பொ ...... டருள்வாயே","வரையை முனிந்து விழவெ கடிந்து","வடிவெ லெறிந்த ...... திறலோனே","மதுரித செஞ்சொல் குறமட மங்கை","நகிலது பொங்க ...... வரும்வேலா","விரைசெறி கொன்றை யறுக புனைந்த","விடையரர் தந்த ...... முருகோனே","விரைமிகு சந்து பொழில்கள் துலங்கு","விசுவை விளங்கு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks not to fall into the net of women who part and deck their curling hair and come with their breasts swaying, losing his understanding and growing weak, and asks for the twin feet that the gods worship, given with love. The praise names the vel that struck the mountain, the hunter girl of sweet speech, Siva of the konrai and the bull as his father, and Visuvai amid fragrant sandal groves.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","thiruvadi","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:234"},"p:992":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":992,"t":["போத நிர்க்குண போதா நமோநம","நாத நிஷ்கள நாதா நமோநம","பூர ணக்கலை சாரா நமோநம ...... பஞ்சபாண","பூபன் மைத்துன பூபா நமோநம","நீப புஷ்பக தாளா நமோநம","போக சொர்க்கபு பாலா நமோநம ...... சங்கமேறும்","மாத மிழத்ரய சேயே நமோநம","வேத னத்ரய வேளே நமோநம","வாழ்ஜ கத்ரய வாழ்வே நமோநம ...... என்றுபாத","வாரி ஜத்தில்வி ழாதே மகோததி","யேழ்பி றப்பினில் மூழ்கா மனோபவ","மாயை யிற்சுழி யூடே விடாதுக ...... லங்கலாமோ","கீத நிர்த்தவெ தாளா டவீநட","நாத புத்திர பாகீ ரதீகிரு","பாச முத்திர ஜ¨முத வாகனர் ...... தந்திபாகா","கேக யப்பிர தாபா முலாதிப","மாலி கைக்கும ரேசா விசாகக்ரு","பாலு வித்ரும காரா ஷடானன ...... புண்டா£கா","வேத வித்தக வேதா விநோதகி","ராத லஷ்மிகி ¡£டா மகாசல","வீர விக்ரம பாரா வதானவ ...... கண்டசூரா","வீர நிட்டுர வீராதி காரண","தீர நிர்ப்பய தீராபி ராமவி","நாய கப்ரிய வேலாயு தாசுரர் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet hails Murugan in a litany of 'namo namah': knowledge beyond the gunas, lord of nada, the partless, the fullness of the kalas, cousin of Kama, kadamba-footed, son of the three Tamils and life of the three worlds, and asks whether, without falling at the lotus feet with that praise, he must drown in the ocean of seven births and whirl in Kama's maya. The praise piles up names: son of Siva who dances with vetalas in the forest, son of the Ganga, master of the elephant-raised maid, lord of the peacock, Visakha, six-faced, sporting with the hunter Lakshmi, slayer of Sura, and Vinayaka's dear brother.","n":"The plea is short and sits inside the litany: the 'namo namah' is itself what the poet fears he will fail to say (என்று பாத வாரிஜத்தில் விழாதே). பஞ்சபாண பூபன் மைத்துன: Murugan as cousin of Kama, since Kama is Vishnu's son and Vishnu is Murugan's uncle.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["piravi","maayai","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:235"},"p:993":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":993,"t":["ஓது முத்தமிழ் தேராவ்ரு தாவனை","வேத னைப்படு காமாவி காரனை","ஊன முற்றுழல் ஆசாப ஈனனை ...... அந்தர்யாமி","யோக மற்றுழல் ஆசாப சாசனை","மோக முற்றிய மோடாதி மோடனை","ஊதி யத்தவம் நாடாத கேடனை ...... அன்றிலாதி","பாத கக்கொலை யேசூழ்க பாடனை","நீதி சற்றுமி லாகீத நாடனை","பாவி யர்க்குளெ லாமாது ரோகனை ...... மண்ணின்மீதில்","பாடு பட்டலை மாகோப லோபனை","வீடு பட்டழி கோமாள வீணனை","பாச சிக்கினில் வாழ்வேனை யாளுவ ...... தெந்தநாளோ","ஆதி சற்குண சீலா நமோநம","ஆட கத்திரி சூலா நமோநம","ஆத ரித்தருள் பாலா நமோநம ...... உ ந்தியாமை","ஆன வர்க்கினி யானே நமோநம","ஞான முத்தமிழ் தேனே நமோநம","ஆர ணற்கரி யானே நமோநம ...... மன்றுளாடும்","தோதி தித்திமி தீதா நமோநம","வேத சித்திர ரூபா நமோநம","சோப மற்றவர் சாமீ நமோநம ...... தன்மராச","தூத னைத்துகை பாதா நமோநம","நாத சற்குரு நாதா நமோநம","ஜோதி யிற்ஜக ஜோதீ மஹாதெவர் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet condemns himself at length as one who never studied the three Tamils, useless, lustful, a demon of desire without the yoga of the indweller, fool of fools, a ruin who never sought the fruit of penance, a plotter of sin and killing, without justice, the greatest traitor among sinners, angry and greedy, a ruined jester living in the tangle of bonds, and asks when Murugan will take him. The praise is a litany of 'namo namah': the virtuous one with the golden trident, honey of the three Tamils, hard for Brahma to reach, dancer in the hall, the feet that trampled Death's messenger, the true guru of nada, light within the light, lord of the great gods.","n":"The self-condemnation is formally a string of accusatives ending in -அனை (வ்ருதாவனை, காமாவிகாரனை, மோடாதி மோடனை), each a charge the poet lays on himself, closed by ஆளுவது எந்நாளோ. The second half answers it in the same rhythm with 'namo namah'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kutram","pedhaimai","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:235"},"p:994":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":994,"t":["வேத வித்தகா சாமீ நமோநம","வேல்மி குத்தமா சூரா நமோநம","வீம சக்ரயூ காளா நமோநம ...... விந்துநாத","வீர பத்மசீர் பாதா நமோநம","நீல மிக்ககூ தாளா நமோநம","மேக மொத்தமா யூரா நமோநம ...... விண்டிடாத","போத மொத்தபேர் போதா நமோநம","பூத மற்றுமே யானாய் நமோநம","பூர ணத்துளே வாழ்வாய் நமோநம ...... துங்கமேவும்","பூத ரத்தெலாம் வாழ்வாய் நமோநம","ஆறி ரட்டிநீள் தோளா நமோநம","பூஷ ணத்துமா மார்பா நமோநம ...... புண்டா£க","மீதி ருக்குநா மாதோடு சேயிதழ்","மீதி ருக்குமே ரார்மாபு லோமசை","வீர மிக்கஏழ் பேர்மாதர் நீடினம் ...... நின்றுநாளும்","வேத வித்தகீ வீமா விராகிணி","வீறு மிக்கமா வீணா கரேமக","மேரு வுற்றுவாழ் சீரெ சிவாதரெ ...... யங்கராகீ","ஆதி சத்திசா மாதேவி பார்வதி","நீலி துத்தியார் நீணாக பூஷணி","ஆயி நித்தியே கோடீர மாதவி ...... யென்றுதாழும்","ஆர்யை பெற்றசீ ராளா நமோநம","சூரை யட்டுநீள் பேரா நமோநம","ஆர ணத்தினார் வாழ்வே நமோநம ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"A litany without a plea: 'namo namah' to Murugan, knower of the Veda, hero of the vel, the lotus feet of bindu and nada, the peacock like a cloud, undivided knowledge, the one who became the elements and lives in fullness, twelve-armed. Then it names the goddess whom Lakshmi, Sarasvati, Indrani and the seven mothers serve, the vina-bearer, Siva's support, Adishakti, Parvati, Neeli, wearer of serpents, eternal mother, and hails Murugan as her son, slayer of Sura and life of the Veda-knowers.","n":"பூதம் மற்றுமே ஆனாய், பூரணத்துளே வாழ்வாய்: Murugan as the elements and as the fullness within them. Nearly half the song is the goddess's names, and Murugan's praise is that he is the son of that Aryai.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["saththi","viyaabagam","naadham"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:235"},"p:995":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":995,"t":["ஆவி காப்பது மேற்பத மாத லாற்புரு டார்த்தமி","தாமெ னாப்பர மார்த்தம ...... துணராதே","ஆனை மேற்பரி மேற்பல சேனை போற்றிட வீட்டொட","நேக நாட்டொடு காட்டொடு ...... தடுமாறிப்","பூவை மார்க்குரு காப்புதி தான கூத்தொடு பாட்டொடு","பூவி னாற்றம றாத்தன ...... கிரிதோயும்","பொக போக்யக லாத்தொடு வாழ்ப ராக்கொடி ராப்பகல்","போது போக்கியே னாக்கையை ...... விடலாமோ","தேவி பார்ப்பதி சேர்ப்பர பாவ னார்க்கொரு சாக்ரஅ","தீத தீ¨க்ஷப ¡£¨க்ஷக ...... ளறவோதுந்","தேவ பாற்கர நாற்கவி பாடு லாக்ஷண மோக்ஷதி","யாக ராத்திகழ் கார்த்திகை ...... பெறுவாழ்வே","மேவி னார்க்கருள் தேக்குது வாத சாக்ஷ ஷடாக்ஷர","மேரு வீழ்த்தப ராக்ரம ...... வடிவேலா","வீர ராக்கத ரார்ப்பெழ வேத தாக்ஷக னாக்கெட","வேலை கூப்பிட வீக்கிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether he, who never grasped the supreme truth that guarding the soul is the highest state and the true aim of life, but wandered on elephants and horses with armies, houses, lands and forests, and spent night and day with women's dance, song and scented breasts, should lose this body so. The praise names Murugan who taught Siva, Parvati's lord, the initiation beyond waking, the poet of the four kinds of verse, son of the Krittikas, the six-lettered and twelve-eyed one, and the vel that felled Meru and made the demons roar and the sea cry out.","n":"ஆவி காப்பதுமேற் பதமாதலாற் புருடார்த்தம் இதாம் எனாப் பரமார்த்தம்: the plea is philosophical, the soul's keeping as the true புருஷார்த்தம், against the pomp of rule and pleasure. சாக்ர அதீத தீக்ஷை: the initiation Murugan gives Siva is named as beyond the waking state. Several Sanskrit words in the praise are corrupt.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["meyyunarvu","selvam","kaamam","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:235"},"p:996":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":996,"t":["ஏக மாய்ப்பல வாய்ச்சிவ போக மாய்த்தெளி வாய்ச்சிவ","மீதெ னாக்குரு வார்த்தையை ...... யுணராதே","ஏழு பார்க்கும்வி யாக்கிரன் யானெ னாப்பரி தேர்க்கரி","யேறு மாப்பிறு மாப்புட ...... னரசாகி","தோகை மார்க்கொரு காற்றொலை யாத வேட்கையி னாற்கெடு","சோர்வி னாற்கொடி தாக்கையை ...... யிழவாமுன்","சோதி காட்டவ ராச்சுத நாத னார்க்கருள் போற்றிய","தூரி தாப்பர மார்த்தம ...... தருள்வாயே","நாக மேற்றுயில் வார்க்கய னான பேர்க்கரி யார்க்கொரு","ஞான வார்த்தையி னாற்குரு ...... பரனாய","நாத நாட்டமு றாப்பல காலும் வேட்கையி னாற்புகல்","நாவ லோர்க்கரு ளாற்பத ...... மருள்வாழ்வே","வேக மேற்கொ ளராப்புடை தோகை மேற்கொடு வேற்கொடு","வீர மாக்குலை யாக்குல ...... வரைசாய","மோல நாட்டவர் பூக்கொடு வேல போற்றியெ னாத்தொழ","வேலை கூப்பிட வீக்கிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks, before he loses his cruel body through craving for women and the weariness of boasting as a king, 'I am the tiger of the seven worlds', on horse, chariot and elephant, for the supreme truth he never understood in the guru's word that Siva is one and many, Siva-bliss and clarity. The praise names Murugan who became guru by one word of knowledge, hard for Vishnu on the serpent and for Brahma, who gives his state to poets who sing him with longing, and the vel that brought down the mountain of the demons while the sea cried out.","n":"ஏகமாய்ப் பலவாய்ச் சிவபோகமாய்த் தெளிவாய்ச் சிவமீது எனா: the guru's word quoted is a compact Saiva creed, Siva as one and many. Like 995, which shares its refrain (வேலை கூப்பிட வீக்கிய பெருமாளே), it sets royal pride and desire against the truth not grasped. சோதி காட்ட அராச்சுத நாதனார்க்கு is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","guru","sivam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:235"},"p:997":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":997,"t":["தோடு மென்குழை யூடே போரிடு","வாணெ டுங்கயல் போலே யாருயிர்","சூறை கொண்டிடு வேல்போ லேதொடர் ...... விழிமானார்","சூத கந்தனி லேமா லாயவர்","ஓது மன்றறி யாதே யூழ்வினை","சூழும் வெந்துய ராலே தானுயிர் ...... சுழலாதே","ஆடு வெம்பண காகோ தாசன","மூறு கண்டிட மேல்வீழ் தோகையி","லாரும் வண்கும ரேசா ஆறிரு ...... புயவேளே","ஆரு நின்றரு ளாலே தாடொழ","ஆண்மை தந்தருள் வாழ்வே தாழ்வற","ஆதி தந்தவ நாயேன் வாழ்வுற ...... அருள்வாயே","ஓடு வெங்கதி ரோடே சோமனு","மூழி யண்டமும் லோகா லோகமு","மூரு மந்தர நானா தேவரு ...... மடிபேண","ஊழி டம்புயன் வேலா வாலய","மூடு தங்கிய மாலா ராதர","வோத வெண்டிரை சூர்மார் பூடுற ...... விடும்வேலா","வேடு கொண்டுள வேடா வேடைய","வேழ வெம்புலி போலே வேடர்கள்","மேவு திண்புன மீதே மாதொடு ...... மிகமாலாய்","மேக மென்குழ லாய்நீ கேளினி","வேறு தஞ்சமு நீயே யாமென","வேளை கொண்டபி ரானே வானவர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks that his life not whirl in the fierce sorrow of fate, deluded by women whose kayal-fish eyes fight through their ear-rings and plunder life like spears, and asks for the manhood to worship the feet by Murugan's grace, so that this dog may live. The praise names the sun, moon, worlds and gods who bow, the vel that pierced Sura's chest, and Murugan who in a hunter's guise went to the millet field and said to the cloud-haired girl, 'hear me, you alone are my refuge'.","n":"ஆண்மை தந்தருள்: the strength asked for is to worship, not to conquer. In the praise the god himself speaks Valli's refuge-formula to her (வேறு தஞ்சமும் நீயே), inverting the devotee's words.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","oozh","aanmai","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:235"},"p:998":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":998,"t":["நாலி ரண்டித ழாலே கோலிய","ஞால முண்டக மேலே தானிள","ஞாயி றென்றுறு கோலா காலனு ...... மதின்மேலே","ஞால முண்டபி ராணா தாரனும்","யோக மந்திர மூலா தாரனு","நாடி நின்றப்ர பாவா காரனு ...... நடுவாக","மேலி ருந்தகி ரீடா பீடமு","நூல றிந்தம ணீமா மாடமு","மேத கும்ப்ரபை கோடா கோடியு ...... மிடமாக","வீசி நின்றுள தூபா தீபவி","சால மண்டப மீதே யேறிய","வீர பண்டித வீரா சாரிய ...... வினைதீராய்","ஆல கந்தரி மோடா மோடிகு","மாரி பிங்கலை நானா தேசிய","மோகி மங்கலை லோகா லோகியெ ...... வுயிர்பாலும்","ஆன சம்ப்ரமி மாதா மாதவி","ஆதி யம்பகை ஞாதா வானவ","ராட மன்றினி லாடா நாடிய ...... அபிராமி","கால சங்கரி சீலா சீலித்ரி","சூலி மந்த்ரசு பாஷா பாஷணி","காள கண்டிக பாலீ மாலினி ...... கலியாணி","காம தந்திர லீலா லோகினி","வாம தந்திர நூலாய் வாள்சிவ","காம சுந்தரி வாழ்வே தேவர்கள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes the eight-petalled lotus of the world, with the sun on it, the deities set in their places, the yogic mantra of the mooladhara, the crowned seat above, the jewelled mansion known by the texts and countless lights, incense and lamps in a wide hall, and asks Murugan, the hero, pandit and acharya who ascended it, to remove his karma. Then a long list of the goddess's names, Kumari, Pingalai, Mangalai, Abhirami who danced in the hall, Kala-sankari, trident-bearer, dark-throated, skull-bearer, Kalyani, the text of the Vama tantra, Sivakamasundari, ends by hailing Murugan as her son.","n":"The first half seems to describe the body's lotuses as a temple, with மூலாதாரம், the gods stationed in it and the crown above as the mandapa, but the syntax is a long list and the reading is tentative. The goddess's names include tantric ones (பிங்கலை, காம தந்திர, வாம தந்திர நூல்).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aadhaaram","yogam","saththi","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:235"},"p:999":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":999,"t":["போதிலி ருந்துவி டாச்சதுர் வேதமொ ழிந்தவ னாற்புளி","னாகமு கந்தவ னாற்றெரி ...... வரிதான","போதுயர் செந்தழ லாப்பெரு வானநி றைந்த விடாப்புக","ழாளன ருஞ்சிவ கீர்த்திய ...... னெறிகாண","ஆதர வின்பருள் மாக்குரு நாதனெ னும்படி போற்றிட","ஆனப தங்களை நாக்கரு ...... திடவேயென்","ஆசையெ ணும்படி மேற்கவி பாடுமி தம்பல பார்த்தடி","யேனும றிந்துனை யேத்துவ ...... தொருநாளே","காதட ரும்படி போய்ப்பல பூசலி டுங்கய லாற்கனி","வாயித ழின்சுவை யாற்பயில் ...... குறமாதின்","காரட ருங்குழ லாற்கிரி யானத னங்களி னாற்கலை","மேவும ருங்கத னாற்செறி ...... குழையோலை","சாதன மென்றுரை யாப்பரி தாபமெ னும்படி வாய்த்திடு","மாறிம னந்தள ராத்தனி ...... திரிவோனே","சாகர மன்றெரி யாக்கொடு சூரரு கும்படி யாத்திணி","வேலையு ரம்பெற வோட்டிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether a day will come when he too, knowing it, praises Murugan, singing many verses: the one whom Brahma who spoke the Vedas and Vishnu of the bird could not find, the fire filling the sky, Siva of great fame, praised Murugan with love as great guru so that he might think on the feet. The praise lingers on Valli's eyes, lips, hair, breasts, waist and ear-leaf, and names Murugan who, saying 'this is the way', wandered alone and steadfast in her service, and the vel that struck the sea and Sura.","n":"The first lines tell the column of fire that Brahma and Vishnu could not measure, and then make that Siva praise Murugan as guru. The plea is for the gift of song and knowing, as in 2 and 25. The praise's word சாதனம் is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","guru","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:235"},"p:1000":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1000,"t":["வேடர்செ ழுந்தினை காத்திதண் மீதிலி ருந்தபி ராட்டிவி","லோசன அம்புக ளாற்செயல் ...... தடுமாறி","மேனித ளர்ந்துரு காப்பரி தாபமு டன்புன மேற்றிரு","வேளைபு குந்தப ராக்ரம ...... மதுபாடி","நாடறி யும்படி கூப்பிடு நாவலர் தங்களை யார்ப்பதி","னாலுல கங்களு மேத்திய ...... இருதாளில்","நாறுக டம்பணி யாப்பரி வோடுபு ரந்தப ராக்ரம","நாடஅ ருந்தவம் வாய்ப்பது ...... மொருநாளே","ஆடக மந்தர நீர்க்கசை யாமலு ரம்பெற நாட்டியொ","ராயிர வெம்பகு வாய்ப்பணி ...... கயிறாக","ஆழிக டைந்தமு தாக்கிய நேகர்பெ ரும்பசி தீர்த்தரு","ளாயனு மன்றெயில் தீப்பட ...... அதிபார","வாடைநெ டுங்கிரி கோட்டிய வீரனு மெம்பர மாற்றிய","வாழ்வென வஞ்சக ராக்ஷதர் ...... குலமாள","வாசவன் வன்சிறை மீட்டவ னூரும டங்கலு மீட்டவன்","வானுல குங்குடி யேற்றிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet tells how Valli, guarding the millet on her platform, was stirred by love, and Murugan entered the field, and asks whether a day will come when, singing that valour and calling to the poets so that all the land knows, he gains the rare penance to seek the two feet praised by the fourteen worlds. The praise names Vishnu who churned the sea with Mandara and the serpent for rope and fed the hungry gods, Siva who burned the forts and bent the great mountain as bow, the gods' cry 'you bore our burden', the demons destroyed, Indra freed from prison, and Murugan who resettled heaven.","n":"அருந்தவம் வாய்ப்பதும் ஒருநாளே: the penance hoped for is to sing Murugan's valour in the Valli story, praise itself as the tapas. The division of the first half's syntax is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","thavam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:235"},"p:1001":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1001,"t":["இலகி யிருகுழை கிழிகயல் விழியினு","மிசையி னசைதரு மொழியினு மருவமர்","இருள்செய் குழலினு மிடையினு நடையினு ...... மநுராக","இனிமை தருமொரு இதழினு நகையினு","மிளைய ம்ருகமத தனகுவ டழகினு","மியலு மயல்கொடு துணிவது பணிவது ...... தணியாதே","குலவி விரகெனு மளறிடை முழுகிய","கொடிய நடலைய னடமிட வருபிணி","குறுகி யிடஎம னிறுதியி லுயிரது ...... கொடுபோநாள்","குனகி யழுபவர் அயர்பவர் முயல்பவர்","குதறு முதுபிண மெடுமென வொருபறை","குணலை யிடஅடு சுடலையில் நடவுத ...... லினிதோதான்","மலையில் நிகரில தொருமலை தனையுடல்","மறுகி யலமர அறவுர முடுகிய","வலிய பெலமிக வுடையவ னடையவு ...... மதிகாய","மவுலி யொருபது மிருபது கரமுடன்","மடிய வொருசரம் விடுபவபன் மதகரி","மடுவில் முறையிட வுதவிய க்ருபைமுகில் ...... மதியாதே","அலகை யுயிர்முலை யமுதுசெய் தருளிய","அதுல னிருபத மதுதனி லெழுபுவி","யடைய அளவிட நெடுகிய அரிதிரு ...... மருகோனே","அவுண ருடலம தலமர அலைகட","லறவு மறுகிட வடகுவ டனகிரி","யடைய இடிபொடி படஅயில் விடவல ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses the infatuation for women's eyes, voice, hair, waist, gait, lips, smile and musk-scented breasts that never abates, and pictures the end: disease, Yama taking the life, mourners fondling, weeping and fainting, someone calling 'take away the old corpse', a drum beating, and the walk to the burning ground, and asks whether that is sweet. The praise names Rama, who cut off ten heads and twenty arms with one arrow, Vishnu who answered the elephant in the pond and drank out the demoness's life with her milk, and who measured the worlds with his feet, and the vel that shattered the northern peak.","n":"The question இனிதோ தான், 'is that sweet?', turns the lover's word back on him at the funeral. குதறு முதுபிணம் எடும் என: the mourners themselves call the body an old corpse to be taken away.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kootru","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:235"},"p:1002":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1002,"t":["கடலை பயறொடு துவரையெ ளவல்பொரி","சுகியன் வடைகனல் கதலியி னமுதொடு","கனியு முதுபல கனிவகை நலமிவை ...... யினிதாகக்","கடல்கொள் புவிமுதல் துளிர்வொடு வளமுற","அமுது துதிகையில் மனமது களிபெற","கருணை யுடனளி திருவருள் மகிழ்வுற ...... நெடிதான","குடகு வயிறினி லடைவிடு மதகரி","பிறகு வருமொரு முருகசண் முகவென","குவிய இருகர மலர்விழி புனலொடு ...... பணியாமற்","கொடிய நெடியன அதிவினை துயர்கொடு","வறுமை சிறுமையி னலைவுட னரிவையர்","குழியில் முழுகியு மழுகியு முழல்வகை ...... யொழியாதோ","நெடிய கடலினில் முடுகியெ வரமுறு","மறலி வெருவுற ரவிமதி பயமுற","நிலமு நெறுநெறு நெறுவென வருமொரு ...... கொடிதான","நிசிசர் கொடுமுடி சடசட சடவென","பகர கிரிமுடி கிடுகிடு கிடுவென","நிகரிலயில்வெயி லெழுபசு மையநிற ...... முளதான","நடன மிடுபரிதுரகத மயிலது","முடுகி கடுமையி லுலகதை வலம்வரு","நளின பதவர நதிகுமு குமுவென ...... முநிவோரும்","நறிய மலர்கொடு ஹரஹர ஹரவென","அமரர் சிறைகெட நறைகமழ் மலர்மிசை","நணியெ சரவண மதில்வள ரழகிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet lists the offerings to Ganapati, chickpeas, lentils, flattened and puffed rice, sweets, vadai, plantain and many fruits, set before the great-bellied elephant god with gladness, and confesses that he has not bowed with folded hands and streaming eyes to the one who comes after him, Muruga, Shanmukha; he asks whether his wandering in cruel karma, poverty, smallness and the pit of women will not end. The praise shows Death and sun and moon afraid as the vel, the peacock that circles the world and the cry 'Hara Hara' of sages and gods, and ends with Murugan grown in the Saravana pool.","n":"The worship of Ganapati is laid out in full and Murugan is named as the one who comes after (பிறகு வரும் ஒரு முருக), the order of temple worship; the fault confessed is to have stopped short of bowing to him. Poverty (வறுமை) is named among the sufferings, unusual beside the women.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","inmai","kaamam","idumbai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:235"},"p:1003":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1003,"t":["கமல குமிளித முலைமிசை துகிலிடு","விகட கெருவிக ளசடிகள் கபடிகள்","கலக மிடுவிழி வலைகொடு தழுவிக ...... ளிளைஞோர்கள்","கனலி லிடுமெழு கெனநகை யருளிகள்","அநெக விதமொடு தனியென நடவிகள்","கமரில் விழுகிடு கெடுவிகள் திருடிகள் ...... தமைநாடி","அமுத மொழிகொடு தவநிலை யருளிய","பெரிய குணதர ருரைசெய்த மொழிவகை","அடைவு நடைபடி பயிலவு முயலவு ...... மறியாத","அசட னறிவிலி யிழிகுல னிவனென","இனமு மனிதரு ளனைவரு முரைசெய","அடிய னிதுபட அரிதினி யொருபொரு ...... ளருள்வாயே","திமித திமிதிமி டமடம டமவென","சிகர கரதல டமருக மடிபட","தெனன தெனதென தெனவென நடைபட ...... முநிவோர்கள்","சிவமி லுருகியு மரகர வெனவதி","பரத பரிபுர மலரடி தொழஅநு","தினமு நடமிடு பவரிட முறைபவள் ...... தருசேயே","குமர சரவண பவதிற லுதவிய","தரும நிகரொடு புலமையு மழகிய","குழக குருபர னெனவொரு மயில்மிசை ...... வருவோனே","குறவ ரிடுதினை வனமிசை யிதணிடை","மலையு மரையொடு பசலைகொள் வளர்முலை","குலவு குறமக ளழகொடு தழுவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes the courtesans, proud, foolish, deceitful thieves who draw young men into the net of their quarrelsome eyes and melt them like wax in fire, and confesses that, seeking them, he never even tried to follow the words of the great virtuous ones who teach the ascetic state, so that his kin and all people call him a fool and low-born; he asks, since this servant suffers so, to be given the one rare meaning. The praise shows Siva dancing to the damaru while sages melt crying 'Hara', names Murugan as son of the goddess who dwells with him, Kumara, Saravanabhava, guru on the peacock, who embraced Valli.","n":"The poet reports what others say of him (அசடன் அறிவிலி இழிகுலன் இவன் என இனமும் மனிதருள் அனைவரும் உரைசெய), a public shame rather than a private confession. The cure asked for is a teaching (ஒரு பொருள்), in keeping with the neglect of the sages' words.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","pedhaimai","thirukkoothu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:235"},"p:1004":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1004,"t":["தசையு முதிரமு நிணமொடு செருமிய","கரும கிருமிக ளொழுகிய பழகிய","சடல வுடல்கடை சுடலையி லிடுசிறு ...... குடில்பேணுஞ்","சகல கருமிகள் சருவிய சமயிகள்","சரியை கிரியைகள் தவமெனு மவர்சிலர்","சவலை யறிவினர் நெறியினை விடஇனி ...... யடியேனுக்","கிசைய இதுபொரு ளெனஅறி வுறவொரு","வசன முறஇரு வினையற மலமற","இரவு பகலற எனதற நினதற ...... அநுபூதி","இனிமை தருமொரு தனிமையை மறைகளின்","இறுதி யறுதியி டவரிய பெறுதியை","இருமை யொருமையில் பெருமையை வெளிபட ...... மொழிவாயே","அசல குலபதி தருமொரு திருமகள்","அமலை விமலைக ளெழுவரும் வழிபட","அருளி அருணையி லுறைதரு மிறையவ ...... ளபிராமி","அநகை அநுபவை அநுதயை அபிநவை","அதல முதலெழு தலமிவை முறைமுறை","அடைய அருளிய பழையவ ளருளிய ...... சிறியோனே","வசுவ பசுபதி மகிழ்தர வொருமொழி","மவுன மருளிய மகிமையு மிமையவர்","மரபில் வனிதையும் வனசரர் புதல்வியும் ...... வடிவேலும்","மயிலு மியலறி புலமையு முபநிட","மதுர கவிதையும் விதரண கருணையும்","வடிவு மிளமையும் வளமையு மழகிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks Murugan to leave behind the path of those who tend the little hut of a body of flesh, blood and worms bound for the cremation ground: the ritualists, the quarrelling sectarians, and those of half-grown knowledge who call chariyai and kiriyai penance, and to tell him plainly 'this is the thing', so that the two deeds, the malas, night and day, 'mine' and 'yours' all cease, and the experience of that sweet solitude, beyond what the Vedas' end can fix, the greatness of two in one, is revealed. The praise names the goddess, daughter of the mountain king, worshipped by the seven mothers, Abhirami of Arunai who gave the seven nether worlds, and Murugan her son, who gave Pasupati the one word of silence, with his consorts, vel, peacock, poetry, the sweet verse of the Upanishads, generosity and beauty.","n":"இரவு பகலற எனதற நினதற அநுபூதி: the state asked for is named by what ceases, and the word அநுபூதி (direct experience) is the goal. Chariyai and kiriyai are here among the paths to be left, though in 979 the poet longs for them. மவுனம் அருளிய: the teaching to Siva is given as silence, not as Om.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["samayam","malam","mounam","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:235"},"p:1005":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1005,"t":["நெடிய வடகுவ டிடியவு மெழுகிரி","நெறுநெ றெனநெரியவுமுது பணிபதி","நிபிட முடிகிழி யவுநில மதிரவும் ...... விளையாடும்","நிகரில் கலபியும் ரவியுமிழ் துவசமும்","நினது கருணையு முறைதரு பெருமையும்","நிறமு மிளமையும் வளமையு மிருசர ...... ணமும்நீப","முடியு மபிநவ வனசரர் கொடியிடை","தளர வளர்வன ம்ருகமத பரிமள","முகுள புளகித தனகிரிதழுவிய ...... திரடோ ளும்","மொகுமொ கெனமது கரமுரல் குரவணி","முருக னறுமுக னெனவரு வனபெயர்","முழுது மியல்கொடு பழுதற மொழிவது ...... மொருநாளே","கொடிய படுகொலை நிசிசர ருரமொடு","குமுகு மெனவிசை யுடனிசை பெறமிகு","குருதி நதிவித சதியொடு குதிகொள ...... விதியோடக்","குமுறு கடல்குடல் கிழிபட வடுமர","மொளுமொ ளெனஅடி யொடலறி விழவுயர்","குருகு பெயரிய வரைதொளை படவிடு ...... சுடர்வேலா","இடியு முனைமலி குலிசமு மிலகிடு","கவள தவளவி கடதட கனகட","இபமு மிரணிய தரணியு முடையதொர் ...... தனியானைக்","கிறைவ குருபர சரவண வெகுமுக","ககன புனிதையும் வனிதைய ரறுவரும்","எனது மகவென வுமைதரு மிமையவர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether a day will come when he can speak without fault all the names that come as 'Muruga, the six-faced', telling the peacock that crushed the northern peak, the seven hills and the serpent king's hoods, the rooster banner, Murugan's compassion, greatness, colour, youth, the two feet, the kadamba crown and the shoulders that embraced Valli. The praise sounds the vel against the demons, the blood river, the sea torn and the mango tree felled, and the hill named for a bird pierced, and names Murugan guru, Saravana, whom the Ganga, the six women and Uma each call 'my child'.","n":"The plea is the wish to praise rightly (பழுதற மொழிவது), and the list of what is to be praised is the song. குருகு பெயரிய வரை is the Krauncha hill, named for the heron; the mango (அடுமரம்) is Sura.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:235"},"p:1006":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1006,"t":["பகிர நினைவொரு தினையள விலுமிலி","கருணை யிலியுன தருணையொ டுதணியல்","பழநி மலைகுரு மலைபணி மலைபல ...... மலைபாடிப்","பரவு மிடறிலி படிறுகொ டிடறுசொல்","பழகி யழகிலி குலமிலி நலமிலி","பதிமை யிலிபவு ஷதுமிலி மகிமையி ...... லிகுலாலன்","திகிரிவருமொரு செலவிநி லெழுபது","செலவு வருமன பவுரிகொ டலமரு","திருக னுருகுத லழுகுதல் தொழுகுதல் ...... நினையாத","திமிர னியல்பிலி யருளிலி பொருளிலி","திருடன் மதியிலி கதியிலி விதியிலி","செயலி லுணர்விலி சிவபத மடைவது ...... மொருநாளே","மகர சலநிதி முறையிட நிசிசரன்","மகுட மொருபது மிருபது திரள்புய","வரையு மறவொரு கணைதெரிபுயல்குரு ...... ந்ருபதூதன்","மடுவில் மதகரிமுதலென வுதவிய","வரத னிருதிறல் மருதொடு பொருதவன்","மதலை குதலையின் மறைமொழி யிகழிர ...... ணியனாகம்","உ கிரினுதிகொடு வகிருமொ ரடலரி","திகிரிதரமர கதகிரியெரியுமிழ்","உ ரக சுடிகையில் நடநவி லரிதிரு ...... மருகோனே","உ ருகு மடியவ ரிருவினை யிருள்பொரு","முதய தினகர இமகரன் வலம்வரும்","உ லக முழுதொரு நொடியினில் வலம்வரு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet condemns himself at length: without a millet-grain's thought of sharing, without compassion, who never sang Arunai, Thanigai, Palani, Swamimalai and the many hills, deceitful of speech, without beauty, caste, goodness or the medicine for birth, a mind that whirls seventy times in one turn of the potter's wheel, who never thought to melt, weep and worship, darkened, graceless, without wealth, a thief, without mind, path or rule, and asks whether a day will come for him to reach Siva's state. The praise names Vishnu at length, Rama who cut Ravana's heads and arms with one arrow, the royal envoy, the giver to the elephant in the pond, the one who fought the twin marutha trees, the man-lion who tore Hiranya when his child prattled the Veda, the dancer on the serpent's hood, and Murugan the sun of devotees who circled the world in an instant.","n":"The list is built of words in -இலி (one without): கருணையிலி, அழகிலி, குலமிலி, அருளிலி, விதியிலி, and the first lack named is sharing (பகிர நினைவு ஒரு தினையளவிலும் இலி). திகிரி வரும் ஒரு செலவினில் எழுபது செலவு வரு மன பவுரி: the mind spins seventy times while the potter's wheel turns once.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kutram","eegai","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:235"},"p:1007":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1007,"t":["முருகு செறிகுழ லவிழ்தர முகமதி","முடிய வெயர்வர முதுதிரை யமுதன","மொழிகள் பதறிட வளைகல கலவென ...... அணைபோக","முலையின் மிசையிடு வடமுடி யறஇடை","முறியு மெனஇரு பரிபுர மலறிட","முகுள அலரிள நிலவெழ இலவிதழ் ...... பருகாநின்","றுருகி யுளமுட லுடலொடு செருகிட","வுயிரு மெனதுயி ரெனமிக வுறவுசெய்","துதவு மடமக ளிர்களொடு மமளியி ...... லநுராக","உ ததி யதனிடை விழுகினு மெழுகினும்","உ ழலு கினுமுன தடியிணை எனதுயி","ருதவி யெனவுனை நினைவது மொழிவது ...... மறவேனே","எருவை யொடுகொடி கெருடனும் வெளிசிறி","திடமு மிலையென வுலவிட அலகையின்","இனமும் நிணமுண எழுகுறள் களுமிய ...... லிசைபாட","இகலி முதுகள மினமிசை யொடுதனி","யிரண பயிரவி பதயுக மிகுநட","மிடவு மிகவெதி ரெதிரெதி ரொருதனு ...... விருகாலும்","வரிசை யதனுடன் வளைதர வொருபது","மகுட மிருபது புயமுடன் மடிபட","வலியி னொருகணை விடுகர முதலரி...... நெடுமாயன்","மருக குருபர சரவண மதில்வரு","மகிப சுரபதி பதிபெற அவுணர்கள்","மடிய இயல்கொளு மயில்மிசை வரவல ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"After a frank picture of lovemaking with women who swear 'you are my very life', the poet does not ask to be pulled out: he vows that whether he sinks, rises or tosses in that ocean of passion he will never forget to think and say that Murugan's two feet are his life's help. The praise shows the battlefield of vultures, ghouls and dancing Bhairavi, Rama's one arrow felling the ten crowns and twenty arms, and Murugan, nephew of that Vishnu, the guru and Saravana who rides the peacock so that Indra regains his city.","n":"அநுராக உததியதனிடை விழுகினும் எழுகினும் உழலுகினும் உனது அடியிணை எனது உயிர் உதவி என ... மறவேனே: the song ends its plea with a vow (மறவேனே) rather than a request, setting remembrance of the feet inside the passion instead of after escape from it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","thiruvadi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:236"},"p:1008":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1008,"t":["இலகு வேலெனு மிருவினை விழிகளும்","எழுதொ ணாதெனு மிருதன கிரிகளும்","இசையி னால்வசை பொசிதரு மொழிகளு ...... மெதிர்வேகொண்","டெதிரிலாவதி பலமுடை யிளைஞரெ","னினிய மாவினை யிருளெனும் வலைகொடு","இடைவி டாதெறு நடுவனு மெனவளை ...... மடவார்தம்","கலவி மால்கொடு கலைகளு மறிவொடு","கருதொ ணாதென முனிவுற மருள்கொடு","கரையி லாவிதி யெனுமொரு கடலிடை ...... கவிழாதே","கருணை வானவர் தொழுதெழு மயிலுறை","குமர கானவர் சிறுமியொ டுருகிய","கமல தாளிணை கனவிலு நினைவுற ...... அருள்தாராய்","பலகை யோடொரு பதுசிர மறஎறி","பகழி யானர வணைமிசை துயில்தரு","பரமன் மால்படி யளவிடு மரிதிரு ...... மருகோனே","பழுதி லாமன முடையவர் மலர்கொடு","பரவ மால்விடை மிசையுறை பவரொடு","பரம ஞானமு மிதுவென வுரைசெய்த ...... பெரியோனே","அலகை காளிகள் நடமிட அலைகட","லதனில் நீள்குடல் நிணமலை பிணமலை","அசுரர் மார்பக மளறது படவிடு ...... மயில்வேலா","அரிய பாவல ருரைசெய அருள்புரி","முருக ஆறிரு புயஇய லிசையுடன்","அழகு மாண்மையு மிலகிய சரவண ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women whose eyes are spears and whose music oozes blame, who strike young men without pause like Death himself with his net of karma's darkness, and asks not to capsize in the shoreless sea of fate through infatuation with them, but to have the lotus feet of the Kumara who melted for the hunter girl in mind even in dreams. The praise names Vishnu who sleeps on the serpent and measured the earth, Murugan the great one who told Siva on the bull 'this is the supreme knowledge', and the vel that turned the asuras' chests to mire.","n":"இடைவிடாது எறு நடுவனும் என: the women are likened to நடுவன், Yama, the impartial killer, so desire and death are one figure. கரையிலா விதி எனும் ஒரு கடல்: fate is a sea without shore. The line about learning and knowledge that sages despise (கலைகளும் அறிவொடு கருதொணாதென முனிவுற) is hard to construe.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","oozh","thiruvadi","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:236"},"p:1009":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1009,"t":["முருகு லாவிய குழலினு நிழலினும்","அருவ மாகிய இடையினு நடையினு","முளரிபோலுநல் விழியினு மொழியினு ...... மடமாதர்","முனிவி லாநகை வலையினு நிலையினும்","இறுக வாரிடு மலையெனு முலையினு","முடிவி லாததொர் கொடுவிட மடுவித ...... மயலாகி","நரகி லேவிழு மவலனை யசடனை","வழிப டாதவொர் திருடனை மருடனை","நலமி லாவக கபடனை விகடனை ...... வினையேனை","நடுவி லாதன படிறுகொ ளிடறுசொ","லதனில் மூழ்கிய மறவனை யிறவனை","நளின மார்பத மதுபெற ஒருவழி ...... யருள்வாயே","வரிய ராவினின் முடிமிசை நடமிடு","பரத மாயவ னெழுபுவி யளவிடு","வரதன் மாதவ னிரணிய னுடலிரு ...... பிளவாக","வகிரு மாலரிதிகிரிய னலையெறி","தமர வாரிதி முறையிட நிசிசரன்","மகுட மானவை யொருபதும் விழவொரு ...... கணையேவுங்","கரிய மேனியன் மருதொடு பொருதவன்","இனிய பாவல னுரையினி லொழுகிய","கடவுள் வேயிசை கொடுநிரை பரவிடு ...... மபிராமன்","கருணை நாரண னரபதி சுரபதி","மருக கானக மதனிடை யுறைதரு","கரிய வேடுவர் சிறுமியொ டுருகிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"Infatuated by women's hair, waist, gait, eyes, speech and breasts as by a deadly poison, the poet calls himself a fool falling into hell, a thief who never worshipped, a deceiver and a sinner sunk in lying talk, and asks to be shown one way to reach the lotus feet. The praise is almost wholly of Vishnu: dancer on the serpent's head, measurer of the worlds, splitter of Hiranya, Rama of the single arrow, Krishna who fought the marutha trees and played the flute to the cattle; Murugan is his nephew and the lover of the dark hunters' girl.","n":"The self-abuse is a pileup of rhymed nouns (அவலனை, அசடனை, திருடனை, மருடனை, கபடனை, விகடனை), its rhythm part of the confession. The praise spends nearly all its length on Vishnu's avatars before naming Murugan in the last line.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mayal","kutram","naragam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:236"},"p:1010":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1010,"t":["அரிசன பரிசஅ லங்க்ரு தாம்ருத","கலசமு மதனுய ரம்பொன் மாமுடி","யதுமென இளைஞர்கள் நெஞ்சு மாவியு ...... மொருகோடி","அடைபடு குடயுக ளங்க ளாமென","ம்ருகமத களபம ணிந்த சீதள","அபிநவ கனதன மங்கை மாருடன் ...... விளையாடி","இரவொடு பகலொழி வின்றி மால்தரு","மலைகட லளறுப டிந்து வாயமு","தினிதென அருளஅ ருந்தி யார்வமொ ...... டிதமாகி","இருவரு மருவிய ணைந்து பாழ்படு","மருவினை யறவும றந்து னீள்தரு","மிணைமல ரடிகள்நி னைந்து வாழ்வது ...... மொருநாளே","சுரர்குல பதிவிதி விண்டு தோலுரி","யுடைபுனை யிருடிக ளண்ட ரானவர்","துதிசெய எதிர்பொர வந்த தானவ ...... ரடிமாள","தொலைவறு மலகையி னங்க ளானவை","நடமிட நிணமலை துன்ற வேயதில்","துவரிது புளியிது தொய்ந்த தீதிது ...... இதுவீணால்","பருகுத லரியது கந்த தீதிது","உ ளதென குறளிகள் தின்று மெதகு","பசிகெட வொருதனி வென்ற சேவக ...... மயில்வீரா","பகிரதி சிறுவலி லங்க லு஡டுறு","குறமகள் கொழுநப டர்ந்து மேலெழு","பருவரை யுருவஎ றிந்த வேல்வல ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses playing day and night with women whose breasts imprison the hearts of a crore of young men, drinking their lips as nectar and sinking in a sea of infatuation, and asks whether there will be a day when he forgets that ruinous karma and lives thinking of the long flower feet. The praise shows the gods and sages hailing Murugan as the demons die, and the ghouls on mountains of fat tasting the flesh, 'this is astringent, this sour, this rotten, this good', and eating until their hunger ends; he is Ganga's son, the hunter girl's husband, the vel that pierced the mountain.","n":"The ghouls' tasting talk (துவரிது புளியிது தொய்ந்ததீதிது ... உளதென) is a comic set piece inside the battle scene, not stock drum syllables. பகிரதி சிறுவ names Murugan as Ganga's child.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["punarchi","kaamam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:236"},"p:1011":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1011,"t":["உ ரைதரு பரசம யங்க ளோதுவ","துருவென அருவென வொன்றி லாததொ","ரொளியென வெளியென வும்ப ராமென ...... இம்பராநின்","றுலகுகள் நிலைபெறு தம்ப மாமென","வுரைசெய அதுபொருள் கண்டு மோனமொ","டுணர்வுற வுணர்வொடி ருந்ந நாளும ...... ழிந்திடாதே","பரகதி பெறுவதொ ழிந்தி டார்வன","பரிசன தெரிசன கந்த வோசைகள்","பலநல விதமுள துன்ப மாகிம ...... யங்கிடாதே","பரிபுர பதமுள வஞ்ச மாதர்கள்","பலபல விதமுள துன்ப சாகர","படுகுழி யிடைவிழு பஞ்ச பாதக ...... னென்றுதீர்வேன்","அரகர சிவசுத கந்த னேநின","தபயம பயமென நின்று வானவர்","அலறிட வொழிகினி யஞ்சி டாதென ...... அஞ்சல்கூறி","அடல்தரு நிருதர நந்த வாகினி","யமபுர மடையஅ டர்ந்து போர்புரி","அசுரன தகலமி டந்து போகவ ...... கிர்ந்தவேகம்","விரிகடல் துகளெழ வென்ற வேலவ","மரகத கலபசி கண்டி வாகன","விரகுள சரவண முந்தை நான்மறை ...... யந்தமோதும்","விரைதரு மலரிலி ருந்த வேதனும்","விடவர வமளிது யின்ற மாயனும்","விமலைகொள் சடையர னும்ப ராவிய ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks when, hearing what the rival sects call the ultimate (form, the formless, a light that is neither, space, heaven, the pillar holding the worlds) and seeing the reality itself, he will rest in awareness and silence, strive for the highest state, not be bewildered by the pleasures of touch, sight, smell and sound, and be freed from the pit of deceitful women into which he, guilty of the five great sins, has fallen. The praise shows the gods crying 'Hara Hara, Siva's son Skanda, refuge!' and Murugan saying 'fear not', the vel that tore the asura's chest, the emerald peacock, and Brahma, Vishnu and Siva praising him.","n":"உரைதரு பரசமயங்கள் ஓதுவது ... அது பொருள் கண்டு மோனமொடு உணர்வுற: the definitions of the other sects are listed, then passed beyond by seeing the thing itself in silence. Whether the definitions are rejected or taken as partial pointers depends on the syntax, which is unclear. பஞ்ச பாதகன்: the five great sins.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["samayam","mounam","veedu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:236"},"p:1012":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1012,"t":["இமகிரிமத்திற் புயங்க வெம்பணி","கயிறது சுற்றித் தரங்க வொண்கடல்","இமையவர் பற்றிக் கடைந்த அன்றெழு ...... நஞ்சுபோலே","இருகுழை தத்திப் புரண்டு வந்தொரு","குமிழையு மெற்றிக் கரும்பெ னுஞ்சிலை","ரதிபதி வெற்றிச் சரங்க ளஞ்சையும் ...... விஞ்சிநீடு","சமரமி குத்துப் பரந்த செங்கயல்","விழியினில் மெத்தத் ததும்பி விஞ்சிய","தமனிய வெற்புக் கிசைந்த வம்பணி ...... கொங்கைமீதே","தனிமனம் வைத்துத் தளர்ந்து வண்டமர்","குழலியர் பொய்க்குட் கலங்க லின்றியெ","சததளம் வைத்துச் சிவந்த நின்கழல் ...... தந்திடாயோ","அமரர்து திக்கப் புரந்த ரன்தொழ","எழுபது வர்க்கக் குரங்கு கொண்டெறி","யலையைய டைத்துக் கடந்து சென்றெதிர் ...... முந்துபோரில்","அசுரர்மு தற்கொற் றவன்பெ ருந்திறல்","இருபது கொற்றப் புயங்கள் சிந்திட","அழகிய கொத்துச் சிரங்க ளொன்பது ...... மொன்றுமாளக்","கமலம லர்க்கைச் சரந்து ரந்தவர்","மருமக மட்டுக் கொன்றை யந்தொடை","கறையற வொப்பற்ற தும்பை யம்புலி ...... கங்கைசூடுங்","கடவுளர் பக்கத் தணங்கு தந்தருள்","குமரகு றத்தத் தைபின்தி ரிந்தவள்","கடினத னத்திற் கலந்தி லங்கிய ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet likens women's eyes to the poison that rose when the gods churned the sea with the mountain and the serpent rope, eyes that outdo Kama's five arrows, and asks whether, his mind no longer fixed and faltering on their breasts and troubled in their lies, he will not be given Murugan's red feet like a hundred-petalled lotus. The praise tells of Rama crossing the bridged sea with the monkey army and felling the ten heads and twenty arms, Siva wearing konrai, thumbai, moon and Ganga with the goddess at his side, and Murugan following the parrot-like hunter girl.","n":"இமையவர் பற்றிக் கடைந்த அன்றெழு நஞ்சுபோலே: the eyes are the halahala poison of the churning, an image that makes the woman's glance as world-threatening as the poison Siva had to swallow.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","kaamam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:236"},"p:1013":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1013,"t":["முகமுமி னுக்கிப் பெருங்க ருங்குழல்","முகிலைய விழ்த்துச் செருந்தி சண்பக","முடியநி றைத்துத் ததும்பி வந்தடி ...... முன்பினாக","முலையைய சைத்துத் திருந்த முன்தரி","கலையைநெ கிழ்த்துப் புனைந்து வஞ்சக","முறுவல்வி ளைத்துத் துணிந்து தந்தெரு ...... முன்றிலு஡டே","மகளிர்வ ரப்பிற் சிறந்த பந்தியின்","மதனனு நிற்கக் கொளுந்து வெண்பிறை","வடவையெ றிக்கத் திரண்டு பண்டனை ...... வண்டுபாட","மலயநி லத்துப் பிறந்த தென்றலு","நிலைகுலை யத்தொட் டுடம்பு புண்செய","மயலைய ளிக்கக் குழைந்து சிந்தைம ...... லங்கலாமோ","பகலவன் மட்கப் புகுந்து கந்தர","ககனமு கட்டைப் பிளந்து மந்தர","பருவரை யொக்கச் சுழன்று பின்புப ...... றந்துபோகப்","பணமணி பட்சத் துரங்க முந்தனி","முடுகின டத்திக் கிழிந்து விந்தெழு","பரவைய ரற்றப் ப்ரபஞ்ச நின்றுப ...... யந்துவாடக்","குகனென முக்கட் சயம்பு வும்ப்ரிய","மிகவசு ரர்க்குக் குரம்பை வந்தரு","குறவமர் குத்திப் பொருங்கொ டும்படை ...... வென்றவேளே","குழைசயை யொப்பற் றிருந்த சங்கரி","கவுரியெ டுத்துப் பரிந்து கொங்கையில்","குணவமு துய்க்கத் தெளிந்து கொண்டருள் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"Women brighten their faces, loose and refill their hair with flowers, adjust their dress and come out through the street courtyards in a line with knowing smiles; then Kama stands, the white crescent burns like the undersea fire, the bees hum and the Malaya breeze wounds the body, and the song asks whether the mind should melt and be troubled by this infatuation. The praise shows the peacock's flight splitting the sky and whirling Mandara, Murugan dear to the three-eyed Self-born, victor of the hunters' war, and the child whom Sankari Gauri took up and fed from her breast.","n":"The plea has no first person. The women dressing and coming into the street, then afflicted by Kama, the moon, bees and the south wind, look like the ula convention of women who fall in love watching the god's procession; if so the song is in their voice or about them, not the poet's confession. This is an inference from the imagery.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["kaamanoy","mayal","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:236"},"p:1014":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1014,"t":["படிதனி லுறவெனு மனைவர்கள் பரிவொடு","பக்கத் திற்பல கத்திட் டுத்துயர் ...... கொண்டுபாவப்","பணைமர விறகுடை யழலிடை யுடலது","பற்றக் கொட்டுகள் தட்டிச் சுட்டலை ...... யொன்றியேகக்","கடிசம னுயிர்தனை யிருவிழி யனலது","கக்கச் சிக்கென முட்டிக் கட்டியு ...... டன்றுபோமுன்","கதிதரு முருகனு மெனநினை நினைபவர்","கற்பிற் புக்கறி வொக்கக் கற்பது ...... தந்திடாயோ","வடகிரிதொளைபட அலைகடல் சுவறிட","மற்றுத் திக்கெனு மெட்டுத் திக்கிலும் ......வென்றிவாய","வலியுட னெதிர்பொரு மசுரர்கள் பொடிபட","மட்டித் திட்டுயர் கொக்கைக் குத்திம ...... லைந்தவீரா","அடர்சடை மிசைமதி யலைஜல மதுபுனை","அத்தர்க் குப்பொருள் கற்பித் துப்புகழ் ...... கொண்டவாழ்வே","அடியுக முடியினும் வடிவுட னெழுமவு","னத்திற் பற்றுறு நித்தச் சுத்தர்கள் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet pictures kin wailing at his side, the body on the palm-wood pyre with drums beating, and Death with fire-spitting eyes tying the life tight to take it, and asks that before then he be given to learn, entering their discipline, with the understanding of those who think of Murugan as the giver of the goal. The praise names the vel that pierced the northern mountain, dried the sea and struck down the mango, the teacher of the meaning to Siva of the moon and Ganga, and the Lord of the eternal pure ones who hold fast to the silence that endures when the ages end.","n":"கதிதரு முருகனும் என நினைபவர் கற்பிற் புக்கு அறிவு ஒக்கக் கற்பது: the remedy for death is to learn in the company and discipline of those who know Murugan as the goal; கற்பு here seems to mean their way or learning, not chastity, but the line is compressed. மவுனத்திற் பற்றுறு நித்தச் சுத்தர்கள்: silence as the refuge of the pure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kootru","saakkaadu","guru","mounam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:236"},"p:1015":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1015,"t":["விடமென அயிலென அடுவன நடுவன மிளிர்வன சுழல்விழி","வித்தைத் குப்பக ரொப்புச் சற்றிலை ...... யென்றுபேசும்","விரகுடை வனிதைய ரணைமிசை யுருகிய வெகுமுக கலவியில்","இச்சைப் பட்டுயிர் தட்டுப் பட்டுவு ...... ழன்றுவாடும்","நடலையில் வழிமிக அழிபடு தமியனை நமன்விடு திரளது","கட்டிச் சிக்கென வொத்திக் கைக்கொடு ...... கொண்டுபோயே","நரகதில் விடுமெனு மளவினி லிலகிய நறைகமழ் திருவடி","முத்திக் குட்படு நித்யத் தத்துவம் ...... வந்திடாதோ","இடியென அதிர்குரல் நிசிசரர் குலபதி யிருபது திரள்புய","மற்றுப் பொற்றலை தத்தக் கொத்தொடு ...... நஞ்சுவாளி","எரியெழ முடுகிய சிலையின ரழகொழு கியல்சிறு வினைமகள்","பச்சைப் பட்சித னைக்கைப் பற்றிடு ...... மிந்த்ரலோகா","வடவரை யிடிபட அலைகடல் சுவறிட மகவரை பொடிபட","மைக்கட் பெற்றிடு முக்ரக் கட்செவி ...... யஞ்சசூரன்","மணிமுடி சிதறிட அலகைகள் பலவுடன் வயிரவர் நடமிட","முட்டிப் பொட்டெழ வெட்டிக் குத்திய ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet, ruined alone by passion for women who flatter a man's skill and melt in many-sided union, asks whether, when Death's host binds him tight and drags him off to hell, the fragrant holy feet, the eternal reality that is release, will not come. The praise tells of Rama's arrow scattering Ravana's twenty arms and golden heads, Murugan who takes Valli with her green parrot, and the vel that shattered Sura's crown while the Bhairavas danced.","n":"திருவடி முத்திக்குட்படு நித்யத் தத்துவம்: the feet are named as the eternal tattva within release itself. இயல் சிறு வினைமகள் and the vocative இந்த்ரலோகா are unclear: the first seems to be Valli, the second may make Murugan lord of Indra's heaven.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","kootru","naragam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:236"},"p:1016":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1016,"t":["குகையில்நவ நாத ருஞ்சி றந்த","முகைவனச சாத னுந்த யங்கு","குணமுமசு ரேச ருந்த ரங்க ...... முரல்வேதக்","குரகதபு ராரியும்ப்ர சண்ட","மரகதமு ராரியுஞ்செ யங்கொள்","குலிசகைவ லாரியுங்கொ டுங்க ...... ணறநூலும்","அகலியபு ராண மும்ப்ர பஞ்ச","சகலகலை நூல்க ளும்ப ரந்த","அருமறைய நேக முங்கு விந்தும் ...... அறியாத","அறிவுமறி யாமை யுங்க டந்த","அறிவுதிரு மேனி யென்று ணர்ந்துன்","அருணசர ணார விந்த மென்று ...... அடைவேனோ","பகைகொள்துரியோத னன்பி றந்து","படைபொருத பார தந்தெ ரிந்து","பரியதொரு கோடு கொண்டு சண்ட ...... வரைமீதே","பழுதறவி யாச னன்றி யம்ப","எழுதியவி நாய கன்சி வந்த","பவளமத யானை பின்பு வந்த ...... முருகோனே","மிகுதமர சாக ரங்க லங்க","எழுசிகர பூத ரங்கு லுங்க","விபரிதநி சாச ரன்தி யங்க ...... அமராடி","விபுதர்குல வேழ மங்கை துங்க","பரிமளப டீர கும்ப விம்ப","ம்ருகமதப யோத ரம்பு ணர்ந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet lists who and what cannot know the Lord: the nine siddhas in their caves, Brahma, the asura lords, Siva who burned the cities, Vishnu, Indra with the thunderbolt, the codes of dharma, the Puranas, all the arts and the many Vedas; and asks when, knowing that the Lord's holy form is the knowledge beyond both knowing and not knowing, he will reach the red lotus feet. The praise tells of Vinayaka who, as Vyasa dictated, wrote the Bharata war on the mountain with his broken tusk, the coral elephant after whom Murugan came, and his union with Devayanai.","n":"அறிவும் அறியாமையும் கடந்த அறிவு திருமேனி: the form of the Lord is itself a knowledge beyond the pair of knowing and ignorance. The myth of Ganesha writing the Mahabharata with his tusk at Vyasa's dictation is named outright (பழுதற வியாசன் அன்று இயம்ப எழுதிய விநாயகன்), with Murugan as his younger brother.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","thiruvadi","vedham"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:236"},"p:1017":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1017,"t":["மழையளக பார முங்கு லைந்து","வரிபரவு நீல முஞ்சி வந்து","மதிமுகமும் வேர்வு வந்த ரும்ப ...... அணைமீதே","மகுடதன பார முங்கு லுங்க","மணிகலைக ளேற வுந்தி ரைந்து","வசமழிய வேபு ணர்ந்த ணைந்து ...... மகிழ்வாகிக்","குழையஇத ழூற லுண்ட ழுந்தி","குருகுமொழி வாய்ம லர்ந்து கொஞ்ச","குமுதபதி போக பொங்கு கங்கை ...... குதிபாயக்","குழியிலிழி யாவி தங்க ளொங்கு","மதனகலை யாக மங்கள் விஞ்சி","குமரியர்க ளோடு ழன்று நைந்து ...... விடலாமோ","எழுபடைகள் சூர வஞ்ச ரஞ்ச","இரணகள மாக அன்று சென்று","எழுசிகர மாநி லங்கு லுங்க ...... விசையூடே","எழுகடலு மேரு வுங்க லங்க","விழிபடர்வு தோகை கொண்ட துங்க","இயல்மயிலின் மாறு கொண்ட மர்ந்த ...... வடிவேலா","பொழுதளவு நீடு குன்று சென்று","குறவர்மகள் காலி னும்ப ணிந்து","புளிஞரறி யாம லுந்தி ரிந்து ...... புனமீதே","புதியமட லேற வுந்து ணிந்த","அரியபரிதாப முந்த ணிந்து","புளகிதப யோத ரம்பு ணர்ந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes lovemaking in detail, hair loosened, face sweating, breasts shaking, the self lost in union, and the love-arts learned from the books of passion, and asks whether he may waste away wandering with young women. The praise shows Murugan going to Sura's battlefield and taking the peacock from his enemy, then going to the hill, bowing even at the hunter girl's feet, wandering unseen by the hunters in the millet field, resolving even to mount the palmyra-stem horse, and at last uniting with her.","n":"மதனகலை ஆகமங்கள்: the poet calls the manuals of love 'agamas', a mocking echo of scripture. In the praise the god himself takes on the lover's humiliations of Tamil akam poetry: bowing at the girl's feet and resolving to ride the madal (புதிய மடல் ஏறவும் துணிந்த அரிய பரிதாபம்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","kaamam","madal"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:236"},"p:1018":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1018,"t":["கற்பார்மெய்ப் பாட்டைத் தவறிய","சொற்பாகைக் காட்டிப் புழுகொடு","கஸ்தூரிச் சேற்றைத் தடவிய ...... இளநீரைக்","கட்சேலைக் காட்டிக் குழலழ","கைத்தோளைக் காட்டித் தரகொடு","கைக்காசைக் கேட்டுத் தெருவினில் ...... மயில்போலே","நிற்பாருக் காட்பட் டுயரிய","வித்தாரப் பூக்கட் டிலின்மிசை","நெட்டூரக் கூட்டத் தநவர ...... தமுமாயும்","நெட்டாசைப் பாட்டைத் துரிசற","விட்டேறிப் போய்ப்பத் தியருடன்","நெக்கோதிப் போற்றிக் கழலிணை ...... பணிவேனோ","வெற்பால்மத் தாக்கிக் கடல்கடை","மைச்சாவிக் காக்கைக் கடவுளை","விட்டார்முக் கோட்டைக் கொருகிரி...... யிருகாலும்","விற்போலக் கோட்டிப் பிறகொரு","சற்றேபற் காட்டித் தழலெழு","வித்தார்தத் வார்த்தக் குருபர ...... னெனவோதும்","பொற்பாபற் றாக்கைப் புதுமலர்","பெட்டேயப் பாற்பட் டுயரிய","பொற்றோளிற் சேர்த்துக் கருணைசெ ...... யெனமாலாய்ப்","புட்கானத் தோச்சிக் கிரிமிசை","பச்சேனற் காத்துத் திரிதரு","பொற்பூவைப் பேச்சுக் குருகிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses becoming the slave of women who stray from the learned path, show sweet talk, musk-smeared breasts, eyes, hair and arms, ask for coins through a broker and stand in the street like peacocks, and asks whether he will cast off that long desire and, going with the devotees, melt and praise and bow at the feet. The praise recalls the churning, Siva who bent the mountain into a bow and smiled the cities into flame and called Murugan the guru of the true meaning, and Murugan, who melted at the speech of the golden myna guarding the millet on the hill.","n":"தரகொடு கைக்காசைக் கேட்டு: a broker (தரகு) negotiates the price, an unusually concrete detail of the trade. தத்வார்த்தக் குருபரன் என ஓதும்: Siva himself names Murugan guru. The opening lines of the praise (மைச்சாவிக் காக்கைக் கடவுளை ...) are obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","adiyaar","thiruvadi","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:236"},"p:1019":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1019,"t":["சிற்றாயக் கூட்டத் தெரிவையர்","வித்தாரச் சூழ்ச்சிக் கயல்விழி","சற்றேறப் பார்த்துச் சிலபணி ...... விடையேவிச்","சிற்றாபத் தாக்கைப் பொருள்கொடு","பித்தேறிக் கூப்பிட் டவர்பரி","செட்டாமற் று஡ர்த்தத் தலைபடு ...... சிறுகாலை","உ ற்றார்பெற் றார்க்குப் பெரிதொரு","பற்றாயப் பூட்டுக் கயிறுகொ","டுச்சாயத் தாக்கைத் தொழிலொடு ...... தடுமாறி","உ க்காரித் தேக்கற் றுயிர்நழு","விக்காயத் தீப்பட் டெரியுட","லுக்கேன்மெய்க் காட்டைத் தவிர்வது ...... மொருநாளே","வற்றாமுற் றாப்பச் சிளமுலை","யிற்பால்கைப் பார்த்துத் தருமொரு","மைக்காமக் கோட்டக் குலமயில் ...... தருபாலா","மத்தோசைப் போக்கிற் றயிருறி","நெய்பாலுக் காயச்சிக் கிருபதம்","வைத்தாடிக் காட்டிப் பருகரி...... மருகோனே","கற்றாவிற் காட்டிக் கரைதுறை","நற்றாயிற் காட்டிப் புகழ்கலை","கற்றார் சொற் கேட்கத் தனிவழி ...... வருவோனே","கைச்சூலக் கூற்றைக் கணைமத","னைத்தூள்பட் டார்ப்பக் கனல்பொழி","கர்த்தாவுக் கேற்கப் பொருளருள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet tells of a life spent: sent on small errands by sly-eyed women and robbed by them in youth, then bound by the rope of kin and parents and stumbling in bodily toil, then coughing, spitting and losing breath, the body burned in fire, and asks whether there will be a day he escapes this forest of a body. The praise names Murugan the son of the peacock of Kamakottam who gives her breast milk, nephew of Krishna who danced for the cowherd women's curds and milk, the one who came alone on the road so the learned could hear, and the giver of the meaning to Siva who burned Kama.","n":"The plea moves through three stages of life (சிறுகாலை, the rope of kin, the cough and the pyre), a compressed biography. மெய்க்காடு: the body as a wilderness. காமக் கோட்டக் குலமயில் points to the goddess of Kamakottam at Kanchi. கற்றாவிற் காட்டி ... நற்றாயிற் காட்டி is hard to construe.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["varaivinmagalir","yaakkai","sutram","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:236"},"p:1020":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1020,"t":["இருட்குழலைக் குலைத்துமுடித்","தெழிற்கலையைத் திருத்தியுடுத்","திணைக்கயலைப் புரட்டிவிழித் ...... ததிபார","இழைக்களபப் பொருப்பணிகச்","செடுத்துமறைத் தழைத்துவளைத்","திருத்தியகப் படுத்திநகைத் ...... துறவாடி","பொருட்குமிகத் துதித்திளகிப்","புலப்படுசித் திரக்கரணப்","புணர்ச்சிவிளைத் துருக்குபரத் ...... தையர்மோகப்","புழுத்தொளையிற் றிளைத்ததனைப்","பொறுத்தருளிச் சடக்கெனஅப்","புறத்திலழைத் திருத்தியளித் ...... திடுவாயே","உ ருத்திரரைப் பழித்துலகுக்","குகக்கடையப் பெனக்ககனத்","துடுத்தகரப் படுத்துகிரித் ...... தலமேழும்","உ டுத்தபொலப் பொருப்புவெடித்","தொலிப்பமருத் திளைப்பநெருப்","பொளிக்கஇருப் பிடத்தைவிடச் ...... சுரரோடித்","திரைக்கடலுட் படச்சுழலச்","செகத்ரையமிப் படிக்கலையச்","சிரித்தெதிர்கொக் கரித்துமலைத் ...... திடுபாவி","செருக்கழியத் தெழித்துதிரத்","திரைக்கடலிற் சுழித்தலையிற்","றிளைத்தஅயிற் கரக்குமரப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women who dress their hair, roll their eyes, half-hide their breasts, call, catch and smile, praise a man for money and make love in elaborate postures, and asks Murugan to bear with him who wallowed in that worm-hole of infatuation, call him out at once and protect him. The praise shows the sinner Sura who mocked the Rudras, burst the mountains, sent the gods fleeing and shook the three worlds, and the Kumara whose spear revelled in the sea of his blood.","n":"பொருட்கு மிகத் துதித்து: the praise is for money. மோகப் புழுத்தொளை, the worm-hole of passion, is among the harshest images for the courtesan. பொறுத்தருளி ... அப்புறத்தில் அழைத்து: the plea is for forbearance and to be called out, not for knowledge.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","punarchi","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:236"},"p:1021":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1021,"t":["வினைத்திரளுக் கிருப்பெனவித்","தகப்படவிற் சலப்பிலமிட்","டிசைக்குமிடற் குடிற்கிடைபுக் ...... கிடுமாய","விளைப்பகுதிப் பயப்பளவுற்","றமைத்ததெனக் கருத்தமைவிற்","சகப்பொருள்மெய்க் குறப்பருகக் ...... கருதாதே","எனக்கெதிரொப் பிசைப்பவரெத்","தளத்துளரெச் சமர்த்தரெனப்","புறத்துரையிட் டிகழ்ச்சியினுற் ...... றிளையாதுன்","எழிற்கமலத் திணைக்கழலைத்","தமிழ்ச்சுவையிட் டிறப்பறஎய்த்","திடக்கருணைத் திறத்தெனைவைத் ...... தருள்வாயே","சினத்தைமிகுத் தனைத்துலகத்","திசைக்கருதிக் கடற்பரவித்","திடத்தொடதிர்த் தெதிர்த்திடலுற் ...... றிடுசூரன்","சிரத்துடன்மற் புயத்தகலத்","தினிற்குருதிக் கடற்பெருகச்","சிறப்புமிகத் திறத்தொடுகைத் ...... திடும்வேலா","கனத்தமருப் பினக்கரிநற்","கலைத்திரள்கற் புடைக்கிளியுட்","கருத்துருகத் தினைக்குளிசைத் ...... திசைபாடி","கனிக்குதலைச் சிறுக்குயிலைக்","கதித்தமறக் குலப்பதியிற்","களிப்பொடுகைப் பிடித்தமணப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks Murugan not to leave him in this body, a hut that houses his heap of karma and the fruit of maya, unable to seek the truth, nor wearied by men who boast 'who can match me, who is so able?' and slight him, but in his grace to set him where he may reach the lotus feet with the taste of Tamil and end death. The praise shows the vel opening a sea of blood from Sura's head, arms and chest, and Murugan joyfully marrying the prattling cuckoo Valli in the hunters' town where she sang in the millet field.","n":"தமிழ்ச்சுவையிட்டு இறப்பற எய்த்திட: Tamil song is the means of reaching the feet. The first quatrain (இசைக்கும் இடற் குடில், விளைப்பகுதிப் பயப்பளவுற்று) is very compressed and its split into words is a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","agandhai","thamizh","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:236"},"p:1022":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1022,"t":["முத்து மணிபணிக ளாரத் தாலு","மொய்த்த மலைமுலைகொ டேவித் தார","முற்று மிளைஞருயிர் மோகித் தேகப் ...... பொருமாதர்","முற்று மதிமுகமும் வானிற் காரு","மொத்த குழல்விழியும் வேய்நற் றோளு","முத்தி தகுமெனும்வி னாவிற் பாயற் ...... கிநட்முழ்கிப்","புத்தி கரவடமு லாவிச் சால","மெத்த மிகஅறிவி லாரைத் தேறி","பொற்கை புகழ்பெரிய ராகப் பாடிப் ...... புவியூடே","பொய்க்கு ளொழுகியய ராமற் போது","மொய்த்த கமலஇரு தாளைப் பூண","பொற்பு மியல்புதுமை யாகப் பாடப் ...... புகல்வாயே","பத்து முடியுமத னோடத் தோளிர்","பத்து மிறையவொரு வாளிக் கேசெய்","பச்சை முகில்சதுர வேதத் தோடுற் ...... றயனாரும்","பற்ற வரியநட மாடத் தாளில்","பத்தி மிகவினிய ஞானப் பாடல்","பற்று மரபுநிலை யாகப் பாடித் ...... திரிவோனே","மெத்த அலைகடலும் வாய்விட் டோ ட","வெற்றி மயில்மிசைகொ டேகிச் சூரர்","மெய்க்கு ளுறஇலகு வேலைப் போகைக் ...... கெறிவோனே","வெற்றி மிகுசிலையி னால்மிக் கோர்தம்","வித்து விளைபுனமும் வேய்முத் தீனும்","வெற்பு முறையுமயில் வேளைக் காரப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses being entranced by bejewelled women, and also wandering with a cunning mind, trusting men without understanding and singing them as golden-handed great patrons, and asks to be told how to sing the lotus feet in fresh and fitting verse instead of living in lies. The praise names the green-cloud Vishnu who as Rama felled ten heads and twenty arms with one arrow, one who wandered singing sweet songs of wisdom in devotion at the feet of the dancer whom Vishnu and Brahma cannot grasp, the vel thrown at Sura, and the Velaikkaran of the hill of millet and bamboo pearls.","n":"அறிவிலாரைத் தேறிப் பொற்கை புகழ் பெரியராகப் பாடி: the poet confesses the court poet's sin of praising undeserving patrons as generous, and asks instead for song for the Lord. The figure who wanders singing ஞானப் பாடல் at the dancer's feet may be Murugan as Sambandar, but the text does not name him.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","pugazh","iravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:237"},"p:1023":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1023,"t":["விட்ட புழுகுபனி நீர்கத் தூரி","மொய்த்த பரிமளப டீரச் சேறு","மிக்க முலையைவிலை கூறிக் காசுக் ...... களவேதான்","மெத்த விரியுமலர் சேர்கற் பூர","மெத்தை மிசைகலவி யாசைப் பாடு","விற்கு மகளிர்சுரு ளோலைக் கோலக் ...... குழையோடே","முட்டி யிலகுகுமிழ் தாவிக் காமன்","விட்ட பகழிதனை யோடிச் சாடி","மொய்க்கு மளியதனை வேலைச் சேலைக் ...... கயல்மீனை","முக்கி யமனையட மீறிச் சீறு","மைக்கண் விழிவலையி லேபட் டோ டி","முட்ட வினையன்மரு ளாகிப் போகக் ...... கடவேனோ","செட்டி யெனுமொர்திரு நாமக் கார","வெற்றி யயில்தொடுப்ர தாபக் கார","திக்கை யுலகைவல மாகப் போகிக் ...... கணமீளுஞ்","சித்ர குலகலப வாசிக் கார","தத்து மகரசல கோபக் கார","செச்சை புனையுமண வாளக் கோலத் ...... திருமார்பா","துட்ட நிருதர்பதி சூறைக் கார","செப்பு மமரர்பதி காவற் கார","துப்பு முகபடக போலத் தானக் ...... களிறு஡ரும்","சொர்க்க கனதளவி நோதக் கார","முத்தி விதரணவு தாரக் கார","சுத்த மறவர்மகள் வேளைக் காரப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women who price their musk-smeared breasts for coins and sell love on camphor-scented beds, and asks whether, caught in the net of their kohl eyes that outdo Kama's arrows and Yama himself, he must go deluded to ruin. The praise is a chain of epithets ending in -kara: the one with the holy name Chetti, the glorious thrower of the vel, who circled the world in a moment on the peacock, the bridegroom with red flowers on his chest, plunderer of the asura lord, guardian of the gods' king, rider of the elephant, giver of release, and the Velaikkaran of the pure hunters' daughter.","n":"முலையை விலை கூறிக் காசுக்கு அளவே: the price is stated outright. செட்டி எனும் ஒர் திருநாமக்கார: Murugan bears the name Chetti, the merchant, presumably from a guise taken to approach Valli; the song names the title, not the story. The -kara chain is a rhythmic device.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["varaivinmagalir","kan","mayal"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:237"},"p:1024":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1024,"t":["ஏடுமல ருற்ற ஆடல்மத னுய்க்கு","மேவதுப ழிக்கும் ...... விழியாலே","ஏதையும ழிக்கு மாதர்தம யக்கி","லேமருவி மெத்த ...... மருளாகி","நாடுநகர் மிக்க வீடுதன மக்கள்","நாரியர்கள் சுற்ற ...... மிவைபேணா","ஞானவுணர் வற்று நானெழுபி றப்பும்","நாடிநர கத்தில் ...... விழலாமோ","ஆடுமர வத்தை யோடியுடல் கொத்தி","யாடுமொரு பச்சை ...... மயில்வீரா","ஆரணமு ரைக்கு மோனகவி டத்தில்","ஆருமுய நிற்கு ...... முருகோனே","வேடுவர்பு னத்தில் நீடுமித ணத்தில்","மேவியகு றத்தி ...... மணவாளா","மேலசுர ரிட்ட தேவர்சிறை வெட்டி","மீளவிடு வித்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether, deluded by women whose eyes scorn Kama's arrows, caring for neither land, town, house, wealth, children, wife nor kin, and without true knowledge, he must search through seven births and fall into hell. The praise names the green peacock that pecks the dancing serpent, Murugan who stands so all may be saved where the Veda speaks silence, Valli's bridegroom in the hunters' field, and the one who cut the prison where the asuras held the gods and set them free.","n":"நாடு நகர் ... சுற்றம் இவை பேணா: the poet's fault includes neglecting worldly duties for women, not only neglecting God. ஆரணம் உரைக்கும் மோன கவிடத்தில் is unclear; it may mean the place of silence the Veda points to.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","gnaanam","piravi","naragam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:237"},"p:1025":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1025,"t":["சீதமலம் வெப்பு வாதமிகு பித்த","மானபிணி சுற்றி ...... யுடலு஡டே","சேருமுயிர் தப்பி யேகும்வண மிக்க","தீதுவிளை விக்க ...... வருபோதில்","தாதையொடு மக்கள் நீதியொடு துக்க","சாகரம தற்கு ...... ளழியாமுன்","தாரணி தனக்கு ளாரண முரைத்த","தாள்தர நினைத்து ...... வரவேணும்","மாதர்மய லுற்று வாடவடி வுற்று","மாமயிலில் நித்தம் ...... வருவோனே","மாலுமய னொப்பி லாதபடி பற்றி","மாலுழலு மற்ற ...... மறையோர்முன்","வேதமொழி வித்தை யோதியறி வித்த","நாதவிறல் மிக்க ...... இகல்வேலா","மேலசுர ரிட்ட தேவர்சிறை வெட்டி","மீளவிடு வித்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks that when disease of cold, phlegm, fever, wind and bile surrounds the body and brings the life to slip away, before he perishes in a sea of grief with father and children around him, Murugan think to come and give the feet that taught the Veda on earth. The praise names Murugan who comes daily on the peacock to those who pine in love, who taught the Veda to the deluded brahmins before Vishnu and Brahma, and who freed the gods.","n":"The diseases are named by the humoral scheme (வாதம், பித்தம், with சீதம் and மலம்). மாதர் மயலுற்று வாட ... வருவோனே: here the pining is of women in love with Murugan, a reversal of the poet's usual infatuation.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["noy","saakkaadu","thiruvadi","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:237"},"p:1026":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1026,"t":["தோடுபொரு மைக்க ணாடவடி வுற்ற","தோர்தனம சைத்து ...... இளைஞோர்தம்","தோள்வலி மனத்து வாள்வலி யுழக்கு","தோகையர் மயக்கி ...... லுழலாதே","பாடலிசை மிக்க ஆடல்கொடு பத்தி","யோடுநினை பத்தர் ...... பெருவாழ்வே","பாவவினை யற்று னாமநினை புத்தி","பாரிலருள் கைக்கு ...... வரவேணும்","ஆடலழ கொக்க ஆடுமயி லெற்றி","ஆண்மையுட னிற்கு ...... முருகோனே","ஆதியர னுக்கு வேதமொழி முற்றி","யார்வம்விளை வித்த ...... அறிவோனே","வேடைமய லுற்று வேடர்மக ளுக்கு","வேளையென நிற்கும் ...... விறல்வீரா","மேலசுர ரிட்ட தேவர்சிறை வெட்டி","மீளவிடு வித்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks not to wander in the delusion of peacock-like women whose eyes and breasts crush young men's strength, and that a mind which thinks on Murugan's name, with sin gone, come into his hands: Murugan is the great life of devotees who think of him with song, music, dance and devotion. The praise names the dancing peacock, the teacher of the Veda's word to Siva, and the hero who stood as the hunter girl's servant.","n":"பாடல் இசை மிக்க ஆடல்கொடு பத்தியோடு நினை பத்தர்: devotion is pictured as performance, song, music and dance, the setting the poet asks to join.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","paththi","adiyaar","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:237"},"p:1027":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1027,"t":["தோதகமி குத்த பூதமருள் பக்க","சூலைவலி வெப்பு ...... மதநீர்தோய்","சூழ்பெருவ யிற்று நோயிருமல் குற்று","சோகைபல குட்ட ...... மவைதீரா","வாதமொடு பித்த மூலமுடன் மற்று","மாயபணி சற்று ...... மணுகாதே","வாடுமெனை முத்தி நீடியப தத்தில்","வாழமிக வைத்து ...... அருள்வாயே","காதல்மிக வுற்று மாதினைவி ளைத்த","கானககு றத்தி ...... மணவாளா","காசினிய னைத்து மோடியள விட்ட","கால்நெடிய பச்சை ...... மயில்வீரா","வேதமொழி மெத்த வோதிவரு பத்தர்","வேதனைத விர்க்கு ...... முருகோனே","மேலசுர ரிட்ட தேவர்சிறை வெட்டி","மீளவிடு வித்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet lists the diseases (spirit possession, colic, fever, swelling, the great belly, cough, anaemia, leprosy, wind, bile and piles) and asks that none of them come near him as he wastes, and that he be set to live in the high state of release. The praise names Valli's husband, the peacock that measured the whole earth in its running, and Murugan who removes the sufferings of devotees who chant the Veda.","n":"The plea is a medical catalogue; the cure asked for is release (முத்தி நீடிய பதம்) rather than health alone.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["noy","yaakkai","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:237"},"p:1028":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1028,"t":["காதி மோதி வாதாடு நூல்கற் ...... றிடுவோருங்","காசு தேடி யீயாமல் வாழப் ...... பெறுவோரும்","மாதுபாகர் வாழ்வே யெனாநெக் ...... குருகாரும்","மாறி லாத மாகால னூர்புக் ...... கலைவாரே","நாத ரூப மாநாத ராகத் ...... துறைவோனே","நாக லோக மீரேழு பாருக் ...... குரியோனே","தீதி லாத வேல்வீர சேவற் ...... கொடியோனே","தேவ தேவ தேவாதி தேவப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"Those who learn books to quarrel and win arguments, those who seek money and live without giving, and those who never melt saying 'life of the Lord who shares his body with the woman' all go to the town of great Death and wander there. The praise names Murugan the Lord of the form of nada who dwells in the heart, ruler of the serpent world and the fourteen worlds, bearer of the vel and the cock banner, God of the gods.","n":"The song has no plea: it is a judgement on three kinds of men, setting disputatious learning and ungiving wealth beside lovelessness. மாதுபாகர் வாழ்வே, the life of Siva the half-woman, is the address Murugan is given by those who do melt.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","ivaral","kalvi","paththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:237"},"p:1029":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1029,"t":["கூறு மார வேளார வாரக் ...... கடலாலே","கோப மீது மாறாத கானக் ...... குயிலாலே","மாறு போலு மாதாவின் வார்மைப் ...... பகையாலே","மாது போத மாலாகி வாடத் ...... தகுமோதான்","ஏறு தோகை மீதேறி யாலித் ...... திடும்வீரா","ஏழு லோகம் வாழ்வான சேவற் ...... கொடியோனே","சீறு சூரர் நீறாக மோதிப் ...... பொரும்வேலா","தேவ தேவ தேவாதி தேவப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The song asks whether it is fitting that the girl, deluded by love, should waste away under Kama's arrows, the roaring sea, the cuckoo whose anger never ends and her own mother's harsh enmity. The praise names Murugan who rides the peacock in joy, the cock banner by which the seven worlds live, and the vel that fought Sura to ashes.","n":"An akam love-lament, not the poet's confession: the speaker (a companion, the mother or the girl herself; the text does not say) presents the classical afflictors of the lovesick girl, adding the mother's scolding (மாதாவின் வார்மைப் பகை).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:237"},"p:1030":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1030,"t":["பேர வாவ றாவாய்மை பேசற் ...... கறியாமே","பேதை மாத ராரோடு கூடிப் ...... பிணிமேவா","ஆர வார மாறாத நூல்கற் ...... றடிநாயேன்","ஆவி சாவி யாகாமல் நீசற் ...... றருள்வாயே","சூர சூர சூராதி சூரர்க் ...... கெளிவாயா","தோகை யாகு மாரா கிராதக் ...... கொடிகேள்வா","தீர தீர தீராதி தீரப் ...... பெரியோனே","தேவ தேவ தேவாதி தேவப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet, who cannot speak truth while great craving remains, who suffered by joining ignorant women and learned only noisy books, a dog of a servant, asks Murugan to show him a little grace so that his life does not become empty chaff. The praise names him easy to reach for the bravest of heroes, the Kumara of the peacock, husband of the hunter creeper, and the steadfast God of gods.","n":"ஆவி சாவி யாகாமல்: சாவி is grain without a kernel, so the life is pictured as a husk. ஆரவாரம் மாறாத நூல்: learning that is only noise, as in 1028.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avaa","kaamam","kalvi","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:237"},"p:1031":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1031,"t":["காதி லோலை கிழிக்குங் காம பாண விழிக்குங்","கான யாழின் மொழிக்கும் ...... பொதுமாதர்","காணொ ணாத இடைக்கும் பூணு லாவு முலைக்குங்","காதில் நீடு குழைக்கும் ...... புதிதாய","கோதி லாத கருப்பஞ் சாறு போல ருசிக்குங்","கோவை வாயமு துக்குந் ...... தணியாமல்","கூருவே னொரு வர்க்குந் தேடொ ணாததொ ரர்த்தங்","கூடு மாறொரு சற்றுங் ...... கருதாயோ","பூதி பூஷணர் கற்பின் பேதை பாகர் துதிக்கும்","போத தேசிக சக்ரந் ...... தவறாதே","போக பூமி புரக்குந் த்யாக மோக குறப்பெண்","போத ஆதர வைக்கும் ...... புயவீரா","சோதி வேலை யெடுத்தன் றோத வேலையில் நிற்குஞ்","சூத தாருவும் வெற்பும் ...... பொருகோவே","சூரர் சேனை யனைத்துந் தூளி யாக நடிக்குந்","தோகை வாசி நடத்தும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet, never sated by women's spear-eyes that reach their ears, their yazh-like speech, unseen waists, breasts and lips sweet as fresh cane juice, asks whether Murugan will not think even a little of letting him reach the meaning that no one can find by searching. The praise names Murugan the teacher of wisdom praised by Siva of the ash and his consort, the arms that embrace Valli, the vel that fought the mango tree and the hill in the sea, and the rider who makes the peacock dance as Sura's armies turn to dust.","n":"ஒருவர்க்கும் தேடொணாததொர் அர்த்தம்: the goal is a meaning beyond anyone's search, given only by grace. போத தேசிகன், the teacher of knowledge, is a title of Murugan here.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","gnaanam","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:237"},"p:1032":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1032,"t":["காரு லாவு குழற்குங் கூரிதான விழிக்குங்","காதல் பேணு நுதற்குங் ...... கதிர்போலுங்","காவி சேர்பவ ளத்தின் கோவை வாயித ழுக்குங்","காசு பூணு முலைக்குங் ...... கதிசேரா","நேரிதான இடைக்குஞ் சீத வார நகைக்கும்","நேரிலாத தொடைக்குஞ் ...... சதிபாடும்","நீத மான அடிக்கும் மாலு றாத படிக்குன்","னேய மோடு துதிக்கும் ...... படிபாராய்","பார மேரு வளைக்கும் பாணி யார்சடை யிற்செம்","பாதி சோம னெருக்கும் ...... புனைவார்தம்","பால காஎன நித்தம் பாடு நாவலர் துக்கம்","பாவ நாச மறுத்தின் ...... பதமீவாய்","சோரிவாரியிடச்சென் றேறி யோடி யழற்கண்","சூல காளி நடிக்கும் ...... படிவேலாற்","சூரர் சேனை தனைக்கொன் றார வார மிகுத்தெண்","தோகை வாசி நடத்தும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks Murugan to look on him so that, not infatuated with women's hair, eyes, lips, breasts and dancing feet, he may praise him with love. The praise names Murugan the child whom poets daily sing as 'Bala' and whose sorrow and sin he cuts away, giving the state of joy, son of Siva who bent Meru and wears the half moon, and the rider of the peacock while Kali dances in the blood sea of Sura's army.","n":"பாலகா என நித்தம் பாடு நாவலர்: the reward the praise names goes to poets who sing the god as a child, which is what the plea asks to become.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","mayal","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:237"},"p:1033":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1033,"t":["தோடுற்ற காதொக்க நீடுற்ற போருற்ற","தோய்மைக்க ணால்மிக்க ...... நுதலாலே","தோள்வெற்பி னால்விற்கை வேளுக்கு மேன்மக்கள்","சோர்கைக்கு மால்விற்கு ...... மடவார்தம்","ஊடற்கு ளேபுக்கு வாடிக்க லாமிக்க","ஓசைக்கு நேசித்து ...... உ ழலாதே","ஊர்பெற்ற தாய்சுற்ற மாயுற்ற தாள்பற்றி","யோதற்கு நீசற்று ...... முணர்வாயே","வேடர்க்கு நீள்சொர்க்கம் வாழ்விக்க வோர்வெற்பின்","மீதுற்ற பேதைக்கொர் ...... மணவாளா","வேழத்தி னாபத்தை மீள்வித்த மாலொக்க","வேதத்தி லேநிற்கு ...... மயனாருந்","தேடற்கொ ணாநிற்கும் வேடத்தர் தாம்வைத்த","சேமத்தி னாமத்தை ...... மொழிவோனே","தீதற்ற நீதிக்கு ளேய்பத்தி கூர்பத்தர்","சேவிக்க வாழ்வித்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks not to wander in love with the sulking and noise of women whose eyes, brows and arms sell infatuation and make good men faint, and asks Murugan to understand him a little so that he may hold the feet that are his town, his mother and his kin. The praise names him the bridegroom of the girl on the hill who gave the hunters heaven, the one who speaks the name of safety kept by Siva, whom Vishnu and Brahma could not find, and the one who gives life to devotees of blameless justice.","n":"ஊர் பெற்ற தாய் சுற்றமாய் உற்ற தாள்: the feet take the place of home, mother and kin. ஊடற்குள்ளே புக்கு: the courtesan's sulk (ஊடல்) is a trap. Which name is meant in சேமத்தின் நாமத்தை மொழிவோனே is not said.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","oodal","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:237"},"p:1034":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1034,"t":["தோலத்தி யாலப்பி னாலொப்பி லாதுற்ற","தோளுக்கை காலுற்ற ...... குடிலு஡டே","சொர்வற்று வாழ்வுற்ற கால்பற்றி யேகைக்கு","வேதித்த சூலத்த ...... னணுகாமுன்","கோலத்தை வேலைக்கு ளேவிட்ட சூர்கொத்தொ","டேபட்டு வீழ்வித்த ...... கொலைவேலா","கோதற்ற பாதத்தி லேபத்தி கூர்புத்தி","கூர்கைக்கு நீகொற்ற ...... அருள்தாராய்","ஆலத்தை ஞாலத்து ளோர்திக்கு வானத்த","ராவிக்கள் மாள்வித்து ...... மடியாதே","ஆலித்து மூலத்தொ டேயுட்கொ ளாதிக்கு","மாம்வித்தை யாமத்தை ...... யருள்வோனே","சேலொத்த வேலொத்த நீலத்து மேலிட்ட","தோதக்கண் மானுக்கு ...... மணவாளா","தீதற்ற நீதிக்கு ளேய்பத்தி கூர்பத்தர்","சேவிக்க வாழ்வித்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks that before the trident-bearer comes to seize the life from this hut of skin, bone and flesh, Murugan give him a mind keen in devotion to the flawless feet. The praise names the vel that struck down Sura who hid in the sea, Murugan who gave knowledge to the Primal one who swallowed the poison that would have killed the world and the gods, and the bridegroom of the doe-eyed girl.","n":"சூலத்தன்: here Death is the bearer of the trident, a weapon usually Siva's. The lines on the poison (ஆலத்தை ... ஆம் வித்தையாமத்தை அருள்வோனே) are compressed and their split into words is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["yaakkai","kootru","paththi","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:237"},"p:1035":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1035,"t":["ஊனுந் தசையுடல் தானொன் பதுவழி","யூருங் கருவழி ...... யொருகோடி","ஓதும் பலகலை கீதஞ் சகலமு","மோரும் படியுன ...... தருள்பாடி","நானுன் திருவடி பேணும் படியிரு","போதுங் கருணையில் ...... மறவாதுன்","நாமம் புகழ்பவர் பாதந் தொழஇனி","நாடும் படியருள் ...... புரிவாயே","கானுந் திகழ்கதி ரோனுஞ் சசியொடு","காலங் களுநடை ...... யுடையோனுங்","காருங் கடல்வரை நீருந் தருகயி","லாயன் கழல்தொழு ...... மிமையோரும்","வானிந் திரனெடு மாலும் பிரமனும்","வாழும் படிவிடும் ...... வடிவேலா","மாயம் பலபுரிசூரன் பொடிபட","வாள்கொண் டமர்செய்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet, in a body of flesh with nine openings and a crore of womb-paths behind it, asks to sing Murugan's grace so as to grasp all learning and music, to seek his feet morning and evening without forgetting, and to worship the feet of those who praise his name. The praise names the vel by which the sun, moon, time, wind, cloud, sea, mountain and water, the gods who worship Siva's feet, Indra, Vishnu and Brahma live, and the sword-fight with Sura of many magics.","n":"நாமம் புகழ்பவர் பாதம் தொழ: the plea reaches beyond the Lord's feet to the devotees' feet. ஒன்பது வழி: the body's nine gates.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","piravi","adiyaar","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:237"},"p:1036":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1036,"t":["தீயும் பவனமு நீருந் தரணியும்","வானுஞ் செறிதரு ...... பசுபாசத்","தேகந் தனைநிலை யேயொன் றிருவினை","தீருந் திறல்வினை ...... யறியாதே","ஓயும் படியறு நூறும் பதினுறழ்","நூறும் பதினிரு ...... பதுநூறும்","ஓடுஞ் சிறுவுயிர் மீளும் படிநல","யோகம் புரிவது ...... கிடையாதோ","வேயுங் கணியும்வி ளாவும் படுபுன","மேவுஞ் சிறுமிதன் ...... மணவாளா","மீனம் படுகட லேழுந் தழல்பட","வேதங் கதறிய ...... வொருநாலு","வாயுங் குலகிரிபாலுந் தளைபட","மாகந் தரமதில் ...... மறைசூரன்","மார்புந் துணையுறு தோளுந் துணிபட","வாள்கொண் டமர்செய்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet, not knowing the strong act that ends the two karmas of this body of five elements bound as pasu and pasam, asks whether he will not attain the good yoga by which the little life that runs out in hundreds and thousands of breaths may be turned back. The praise names Valli of the millet field, the seas set on fire, the Vedas crying out, and the sword that cut Sura's chest and arms.","n":"அறுநூறும் பதினுறழ் நூறும் பதினிருபது நூறும் ஓடும் சிறுவுயிர்: the numbers seem to count the breaths a life spends, and the yoga asked for would make the breath return (மீளும்படி). Whether they add up to the traditional daily count of breaths is a guess. The plea is in the language of Saiva Siddhanta (பசுபாசத் தேகம், இருவினை).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["praanan","yogam","paasam","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:237"},"p:1037":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1037,"t":["வாதந் தலைவலி சூலம் பெருவயி","றாகும் பிணியிவை ...... யணுகாதே","மாயம் பொதிதரு காயந் தனின்மிசை","வாழுங் கருவழி ...... மருவாதே","ஓதம் பெறுகடல் மோதுந் திரையது","போலும் பிறவியி ...... லுழலாதே","ஓதும் பலஅடி யாருங் கதிபெற","யானுன் கழலிணை ...... பெறுவேனோ","கீதம் புகழிசை நாதங் கனிவொடு","வேதங் கிளர்தர ...... மொழிவார்தம்","கேடின் பெருவலி மாளும் படியவ","ரோடுங் கெழுமுத ...... லுடையோனே","வேதந் தொழுதிரு மாலும் பிரமனு","மேவும் பதமுடை ...... விறல்வீரா","மேல்வந் தெதிர்பொரு சூரன் பொடிபட","வேல்கொண் டமர்செய்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks that wind-disease, headache, colic and swollen belly not come near, that he not enter the womb-path again in this body packed with illusion, nor whirl in births like waves beating on the sea, and asks whether he will gain Murugan's paired anklets so that the many devotees who recite may also reach the goal. The praise calls Murugan kin to those who sing the Vedas with music so that their ruin perishes, the hero whose feet Vishnu and Brahma seek, and the Lord whose vel powdered Sura.","n":"The plea names diseases one by one (வாதம், தலைவலி, சூலம், பெருவயிறு), the body's own failings before birth's cycle. ஓதும் பல அடியாரும் கதி பெற யான் உன் கழலிணை பெறுவேனோ seems to ask the feet not only for himself but so that other devotees reach the goal, though the clause could also mean 'as many devotees have'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["noy","piravi","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:238"},"p:1038":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1038,"t":["ஊனே தானா யோயா நோயா","லு஡சா டூசற் ...... குடில்பேணா","ஓதா மோதா வாதா காதே","லோகா சாரத் ...... துளம்வேறாய்","நானே நீயாய் நீயே நானாய்","நானா வேதப் ...... பொருளாலும்","நாடா வீடா யீடே றாதே","நாயேன் மாயக் ...... கடவேனோ","வானே காலே தீயே நீரே","பாரே பாருக் ...... குரியோனே","மாயா மானே கோனே மானார்","வாழ்வே கோழிக் ...... கொடியோனே","தேனே தேனீள் கானா றாய்வீழ்","தேசார் சாரற் ...... கிரியோனே","சேயே வேளே பூவே கோவே","தேவே தேவப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether he, a dog, must die without being saved, not tending the swinging hut of flesh with its ceaseless disease, his mind apart from worldly custom, unreached by the liberation in which 'I am you and you are I' that the many Vedas' meanings cannot search out. The praise calls Murugan sky, wind, fire, water and earth and their owner, life of the doe-like Valli, bearer of the cock banner, the honey of the hill where honey falls in long forest streams.","n":"நானே நீயாய் நீயே நானாய் is the non-dual goal stated in four words; the Vedas' meanings cannot seek it (நாடா வீடு). The praise reuses the five-element formula of Murugan as everything and its owner. The rhythm of long syllables makes several splits uncertain, and ஡ in the second line stands for ஊ (ஊசாடு ஊசல் குடில).","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["veedu","yaakkai","viyaabagam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:238"},"p:1039":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1039,"t":["சாவா மூவா வேளே போல்வாய்","தாளா வேனுக் ...... கருள்கூருந்","தாதா வேஞா தாவே கோவே","சார்பா னார்கட் ...... குயிர்போல்வாய்","ஏவால் மாலே போல்வாய் காரே","போல்வா யீதற் ...... கெனையாள்கொண்","டேயா பாடா வாழ்வோர் பாலே","யான்வீ ணேகத் ...... திடலாமோ","பாவா நாவாய் வாணீ சார்வார்","பாரா வாரத் ...... துரகேசப்","பாய்மீ தேசாய் வார்கா ணாதே","பாதா ளாழத் ...... துறுபாதச்","சேவா மாவூர் கோமான் வாழ்வே","சீமா னேசெச் ...... சையமார்பா","சேயே வேளே பூவே கோவே","தேவே தேவப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet seems to recite the flattery poets give patrons: 'you are like the deathless, ageless Kama, a giver who pours grace, a knower and king, life to those who depend on you, like Mal with his arrow, like the rain cloud', and asks whether he must go in vain to such people who neither fit the praise nor sing. The praise names Siva, lord who rides the bull, whose feet in the depth of the netherworld Vishnu lying on the serpent in the ocean could not find, and calls Murugan his treasure, wearing the vetchi garland.","n":"The opening string of comparisons (வேளே போல்வாய், தாதாவே, மாலே போல்வாய், காரே போல்வாய்) is the stock language of praise-poems to patrons, and யான் வீணே ஏகத்திடலாமோ regrets carrying it to unworthy men; the same complaint returns in 1062. The rhythm hides word breaks, so whether the comparisons are the poet's flattery or addressed to Murugan is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iravu","pugazh","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:238"},"p:1040":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1040,"t":["நாரா லேதோல் நீரா லேயாம்","நானா வாசற் ...... குடிலு஡டே","ஞாதா வாயே வாழ்கா லேகாய்","நாய்பேய் சூழ்கைக் ...... கிடமாமுன்","தாரா ரார்தோ ளீரா றானே","சார்வா னோர்நற் ...... பெருவாழ்வே","தாழா தேநா யேனா வாலே","தாள்பா டாண்மைத் ...... திறல்தாராய்","பாரே ழோர்தா ளாலே யாள்வோர்","பாவார்வேதத் ...... தயனாரும்","பர்ழு டேவா னூடே பர்ரு","டேயூர் பாதத் ...... தினைநாடாச்","சீரார் மாதோ டேவாழ் வார்நீள்","சேவூர் வார்பொற் ...... சடையீசர்","சேயே வேளே பூவே கோவே","தேவே தேவப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"Before the hut of many doors made of sinew, skin and water becomes a place for dogs and ghouls to crowd round, the poet asks Murugan of the twelve garlanded shoulders, life of the gods who take refuge, to give him, a dog, without delay the strength to sing his feet with his tongue. The praise names Vishnu who rules the seven worlds with one step and Brahma of the Vedas, who could not reach the feet through earth and sky, and Siva of the golden hair who rides the bull and lives with his lady.","n":"நானா வாசல் குடில், the hut of many doors, is the body with its openings, food for dogs and ghouls once dead. Two lines of the praise (பர்ழு டேவா னூடே பர்ரு டேயூர்) are garbled; the sense of Vishnu and Brahma searching the feet through earth and sky is a guess from what survives.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["yaakkai","thiruvadi","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:238"},"p:1041":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1041,"t":["மாதா வோடே மாமா னானோர்","மாதோ டேமைத் ...... துனமாரும்","மாறா னார்போ னீள்தீ யூடே","மாயா மோகக் ...... குடில்போடாப்","போதா நீரு டேபோய் மூழ்கா","வீழ்கா வேதைக் ...... குயிர்போமுன்","போதா காரா பாராய் சீரார்","போதார் பாதத் ...... தருள்தாராய்","வேதா வோடே மாலா னார்மேல்","வானோர் மேனிப் ...... பயமீள","வேதா னோர்மே லாகா தேயோர்","வேலால் வேதித் ...... திடும்வீரா","தீதார் தீயார் தீயு ஧ட்முள்","சேரா சேதித் ...... திடுவோர்தஞ்","சேயே வேளே பூவே கோவே","தேவே தேவப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet pictures mother, uncles, women and cousins turned to strangers throwing this hut of illusion and infatuation into the long fire, then going down to bathe in the water, and asks Murugan, whose form is knowledge, to look on him and give grace at his flower feet before life leaves in that anguish. The praise names the vel that relieved the gods' fear and Siva who destroyed the wicked, whose son Murugan is.","n":"மாறானார் போல் neatly says the kin act as if they were strangers once the body is ash. போதாகாரா, 'whose form is knowledge', is the address of the plea. A line of the praise (தீயு ஧ட்முள்) is corrupt.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["nilaiyaamai","sutram","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:238"},"p:1042":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1042,"t":["வாராய் பேதாய் கேளாய் நீதாய்","மானார் மோகத் ...... துடனாசை","மாசூ டாடா தூடே பாராய்","மாறா ஞானச் ...... சுடர்தானின்","றாரா யாதே யாராய் பேறாம்","ஆனா வேதப் ...... பொருள்காணென்","றாள்வாய் நீதா னாதா பார்மீ","தார்வே றாள்கைக் ...... குரியார்தாம்","தோரா வானோர் சேனா தாரா","சூரா சாரற் ...... புனமாது","தோள்தோய் தோளீ ராறா மாசூர்","தூளாய் வீழச் ...... சிறுதாரைச்","சீரா வாலே வாளா லேவே","லாலே சேதித் ...... திடும்வீரா","சேயே வேளே பூவே கோவே","தேவே தேவப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks Murugan to rule him by saying, as a teacher: 'Come, fool, listen: do not play in the filth of desire and infatuation for women; look within; the unchanging flame of wisdom stands, find it without searching; see the endless meaning of the Vedas that is the true gain', for who else on earth is fit to rule him. The praise calls Murugan the commander of the gods' army, whose twelve shoulders embrace the millet-field girl, and whose arrow, sword and vel cut Sura to dust.","n":"The plea is cast as the guru's own words the poet wants to hear, beginning வாராய் பேதாய் கேளாய். ஆராயாதே ஆராய், 'investigate without investigating', points to knowledge not won by effort. The word splits are often doubtful in this rhythm.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","gnaanam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:238"},"p:1043":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1043,"t":["அகல நீளம் யாதாலு மொருவ ராலு மாராய","அரிய மோன மேகோயி ...... லெனமேவி","அசைய வேக்ரியாபீட மிசைபு காம காஞான","அறிவி னாத ராமோத ...... மலர்தூவிச்","சகல வேத னாதீத சகல வாச காதீத","சகல மாக்ரியாதீத ...... சிவ்ருப","சகல சாத காதீத சகல வாச னாதீத","தனுவை நாடி மாபூசை ...... புரிவேனே","விகட தார சூதான நிகள பாத போதூள","விரக ராக போதார ...... சுரர்கால","விபுத மாலி காநீல முகப டாக மாயூர","விமல வ்யாப காசீல ...... கவிநோத","ககன கூட பாடீர தவள சோபி தாளான","கவன பூத ரர்ருட ...... சதகோடி","களப காம வீர்வீசு கரமு கார வேல்வீர","கருணை மேரு வேதேவர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether, taking as his temple the silence that no one can measure in breadth or length, strewing flowers of the joy of great knowledge on the seat of ritual, he will perform the great worship of the Siva-form beyond all Vedas, all words, all action, all disciplines and all residual impressions. The praise is a run of Sanskrit epithets for Murugan of the peacock and the vel, pure and all-pervading, delight of poets, ending in the Meru of compassion.","n":"அரிய மோனமே கோயில்: the temple is silence, and the worship is inward, offered to what is beyond Vedas, words, actions and vasanas (சகல வேதனாதீத, சகல வாசகாதீத, சகல வாசனாதீத). The praise packs Sanskrit compounds whose splits are hard to fix.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mounam","vazhipaadu","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:238"},"p:1044":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1044,"t":["அடைப டாது நாடோ றும் இடைவிடாது போம்வாயு","அடைய மீளில் வீடாகு ...... மெனநாடி","அருள்பெ றாவ னாசார கரும யோகி யாகாமல்","அவனி மீதி லோயாது ...... தடுமாறும்","உ டலம் வேறு யான்வேறு கரணம் வேறு வேறாக","உ தறி வாச காதீத ...... அடியூடே","உ ருகி ஆரியாசார பரம யோகி யாமாறுன்","உ பய பாத ராசீக ...... மருள்வாயே","வடப ராரை மாமேரு கிரியெ டாந டாமோது","மகர வாரியோரேழு ...... மமுதாக","மகுட வாள ராநோவ மதிய நோவ வா஡ணச","வனிதை மேவு தோளாயி ...... ரமுநோவக்","கடையு மாதி கோபாலன் மருக சூலி காபாலி","புதல்வ கான வேல்வேடர் ...... கொடிகோவே","கனக லோக பூபால சகல லோக ஆதார","கருணை மேரு வேதேவர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks not to become a graceless karma-yogi of improper conduct who, thinking that the breath that leaves daily without pause will give liberation if turned back, stumbles endlessly on earth, but to be given Murugan's lotus feet so that, shaking off body, self and the instruments as separate things and melting into the feet beyond words, he may become a supreme yogi of noble conduct. The praise tells of the churning of the ocean with Meru, the serpent and the moon pained, by Gopala, whose nephew Murugan is, and of Siva with trident and skull, whose son he is.","n":"The song distinguishes two yogis: one who relies on stopping the breath (வாயு அடைய மீளில் வீடாகும் என நாடி) but lacks grace, and the one who melts into the feet. உடலம் வேறு யான் வேறு கரணம் வேறு: body, self and faculties are seen apart and shaken off. In the praise வா஡ணச is corrupt, perhaps for வாரீச, the sea-born Lakshmi.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["yogam","praanan","thiruvarul","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:238"},"p:1045":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1045,"t":["அமல வாயு வோடாத கமல நாபி மேல்மூல","அமுத பான ஧ம்முல ...... அனல்மூள","அசைவு றாது பேராத விதமு மேவி யோவாது","அரிச தான சோபான ...... மதனாலே","எமனை மோதி யாகாச கமன மாம னோபாவ","மெளிது சால மேலாக ...... வுரையாடும்","எனதி யானும் வேறாகி எவரும் யாதும் யானாகும்","இதய பாவ னாதீத ...... மருள்வாயே","விமலை தோடி மீதொடு யமுனை போல வோரேழு","விபுத மேக மேபோல ...... வுலகேழும்","விரிவு காணு மாமாயன் முடிய நீளு மாபோல","வெகுவி தாமு காகாய ...... பதமோடிக்","கமல யோனி வீடான ககன கோள மீதோடு","கலப நீல மாயூர ...... இளையோனே","கருணை மேக மேதூய கருணை வாரியேயீறில்","கருணை மேரு வேதேவர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks for the state beyond the heart's imagining in which 'mine' and 'I' fall away and everyone and everything becomes himself, reached as the pure breath stops, the fire is kindled at the root above the lotus navel and the nectar drunk, step by step without wavering, striking Yama and gaining the power to travel the sky. The praise compares Murugan's peacock racing over the spheres to Vishnu stretching over the seven worlds, and calls him cloud, ocean and Meru of compassion.","n":"The yogic steps (breath held, fire at மூலம், nectar drunk, ascent by சோபானம், a ladder) and their powers (எமனை மோதி, ஆகாச கமனம்) lead to the stated goal: எனது யானும் வேறாகி எவரும் யாதும் யானாகும், loss of ego and identity with all. A line near the start is corrupt (பான ஧ம்முல), and the river names in the praise are unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["kundalini","yogam","siththi","viyaabagam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:238"},"p:1046":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1046,"t":["அயிலின் வாளி வேல்வாளி அளவு கூரிதாயீச","ரமுத ளாவு மாவேச ...... மதுபோல","அறவு நீளி தாய்மீள அகலி தாய வார்காதி","னளவு மோடி நீடோ தி ...... நிழலாறித்","துயில்கொ ளாத வானோரு மயல்கொ ளாத ஆவேத","துறவ ரான பேர்யாரு ...... மடலேறத்","துணியு மாறு லாநீல நயன மாத ராரோடு","துவளு வேனை யீடேறு ...... நெறிபாராய்","பயிலு மேக நீகார சயில ராசன் வாழ்வான","பவதி யாம ளாவாமை ...... அபிராமி","பரிபு ரார பாதார சரணி சாம ளாகார","பரம யோகி னீமோகி ...... மகமாயி","கயிலை யாள ரோர்பாதி கடவு ளாளி லோகாயி","கனத னாச லாபார ...... அமுதூறல்","கமழு மார ணாகீத கவிதை வாண வேல்வீர","கருணை மேரு வேதேவர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women whose blue eyes, sharper than arrow and vel, maddening like intoxicating drink, long and reaching to the ears, make even sleepless gods and Vedic ascetics free of infatuation resolve to ride the palm-stem horse, and asks Murugan to show a path by which he, drooping with such women, may be saved. The praise is a hymn to Uma, daughter of the Himalaya, Abhirami, Syamala, supreme yogini, Mahamayi, half of the lord of Kailasa, and calls Murugan the poet of Vedic songs fragrant with the nectar of her breast.","n":"மடலேற, mounting the palm-stem horse, is the akam lover's public declaration of desperate love; here even ascetics are driven to it by the women's eyes. கனதனாசல அமுதூறல் கமழும் ஆரணா கீத கவிதை வாண points to Sambandar, fed by Uma's milk and singing the hymns, identified with Murugan.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","madal","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:238"},"p:1047":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1047,"t":["இரத மான வாயூறல் பருகி டாவி டாய்போக","இனிய போக வாராழி ...... யதில்மூழ்கி","இதயம் வேறு போகாம லுருகி யேக மாய்நாளு","மினிய மாதர் தோள்கூடி ...... விளையாடுஞ்","சரச மோக மாவேத சரியை யோக்ரியாஞான","சமுக மோத ராபூத ...... முதலான","சகள மோச டாதார முகுள மோநி ராதார","தரணி யோநி ராகார ...... வடிவேயோ","பரத நீல மாயூர வரத நாக கேயூர","பரம யோகி மாதேசி ...... மிகுஞான","பரமர் தேசி காவேட பதிவ்ரு தாசு சீபாத","பதும சேக ராவேலை ...... மறவாத","கரத லாவி சாகாச கலக லாத ராபோத","கமுக மூஷி கர்ருட ...... மததாரைக்","கடவுள் தாதை சூழ்போதில் உ லக மேழு சூழ்போது","கருணை மேரு வேதேவர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"Having described drinking a woman's mouth until thirst goes, sinking in the sea of pleasure with a heart that melts and does not stray, the poet asks what Murugan's form is: the sum of Veda, chariyai, yoga, kriyai and gnanam, the world with form beginning with the elements, the bud of the six supports, the support-less, or the formless. The praise calls Murugan guru to Siva, who wears on his head the lotus feet of the chaste hunter girl, and who circled the seven worlds when the elephant-faced god on his mouse circled their father.","n":"The two halves of the plea are joined by the word வடிவேயோ, and it is unclear whether the poet asks what Murugan's form is or contrasts the lovers' oneness with it. சடாதார முகுளம், the bud of the six centres, and நிராதாரம், the support-less, name yogic levels. The last line alludes to the contest in which Ganapati circled his parents and Murugan circled the world.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","sariyai","aadhaaram","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:238"},"p:1048":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1048,"t":["குருதி தோலி னால்மேவு குடிலி லேத மாமாவி","குலைய ஏம னாலேவி ...... விடுகாலன்","கொடிய பாச மோர்சூல படையி னோடு கூசாத","கொடுமை நோய்கொ டேகோலி ...... யெதிராமுன்","பருதி சோமன் வானாடர் படியு ளோர்கள் பாலாழி","பயமு றாமல் வேலேவு ...... மிளையோனே","பழுது றா பாவாண ரெழுதொ ணாத தோள்வீர","பரிவி னோடு தாள்பாட ...... அருள்தாராய்","மருது நீற தாய்வீழ வலிசெய் மாயன் வேயூதி","மடுவி லானை தான்மூல ...... மெனவோடி","வருமு ராரிகோபாலர் மகளிர் கேள்வன் மாதாவின்","வசன மோம றாகேசன் ...... மருகோனே","கருதொ ணாத ஞானாதி எருதி லேறு காபாலி","கடிய பேயி னோடாடி ...... கருதார்வெங்","கனலில் மூழ்க வேநாடி புதல்வ கார ணாதீத","கருணை மேரு வேதேவர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"Before Death, sent by Yama to break the life in the hut of blood and skin, comes with his cruel noose and trident and his diseases, the poet asks Murugan, whose vel freed sun, moon, gods and the milk ocean from fear, to grant him grace to sing his feet with love. The praise names Krishna who felled the marutha trees and played the flute, who ran when the elephant cried 'Root!', husband of the cowherd women and Rama obedient to his mother's word, whose nephew Murugan is, and Siva the skull-bearer who dances with ghosts, whose son he is.","n":"காலன் is here Yama's servant (ஏமனால் ஏவி விடு காலன்), coming with பாசம், சூலம் and diseases as weapons. பழுது றா பாவாணர் எழுதொணாத தோள்: the shoulders flawless poets cannot write, the praise admitting its limit.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","thiruvadi","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:238"},"p:1049":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1049,"t":["சுருதி யூடு கேளாது சரியை யாளர் காணாது","துரிய மீது சாராது ...... எவராலுந்","தொடரொ ணாது மாமாயை யிடைபு காது ஆனாத","சுகம கோத தீயாகி ...... யொழியாது","பருதி காயில் வாடாது வடவை மூளில் வேகாது","பவனம் வீசில் வீழாது ...... சலியாது","பரவை சூழி லாழாது படைகள் மோதில் மாயாது","பரம ஞான வீடேது ...... புகல்வாயே","நிருதர் பூமி பாழாக மகர பூமி தநமுள","நிபிட தாரு காபூமி ...... குடியேற","நிகர பார நீகார சிகர மீது வேலேவு","நிருப வேத ஆசாரி...... யனுமாலும்","கருது மாக மாசாரிகனக கார்மு காசாரி","ககன சாரிபூசாரி...... வெகுசாரி","கயிலை நாட காசாரிசகல சாரிவாழ்வான","கருணை மேரு வேதேவர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks Murugan to tell him where the abode of supreme wisdom is: unheard through scripture, unseen by those of ritual conduct, not reached above turiya, followed by no one, untouched by great maya, unending bliss that does not die in fire, wither in the sun, burn in the undersea fire, fall in the wind, sink in the ocean or perish when weapons strike. The praise calls Murugan the vel-thrower on the snowy peak, teacher whom Brahma and Vishnu esteem, and treasure of Siva of the golden bow who moves in sky and earth and dances in Kailasa.","n":"The list of negations (fire, sun, wind, ocean, weapons) recalls the Gita's description of the self that weapons do not cut nor fire burn, though the text does not name it; here it describes the abode of wisdom. துரிய மீது சாராது: even turiya, the fourth state, does not reach it. The question பரம ஞான வீடு ஏது புகல்வாயே recurs in 1054.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["veedu","gnaanam","avaththai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:238"},"p:1050":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1050,"t":["தொடஅ டாது நேராக வடிவு காண வாராது","சுருதி கூறு வாராலு ...... மெதிர்கூறத்**","துறையி லாத தோராசை யிறைவ னாகி யோரேக","துரிய மாகி வேறாகி ...... யறிவாகி","நெடிய கால்கை யோடாடு முடலின் மேவி நீநானு","மெனவு நேர்மை நூல்கூறி ...... நிறைமாயம்","நிகரில் கால னாரேவ முகரியான தூதாளி","நினைவொ டேகு மோர்நீதி ...... மொழியாதோ","அடல்கெ டாத சூர்கோடி மடிய வாகை வேலேவி","யமர்செய் வீர ஈராறு ...... புயவேளே","அழகி னோடு மானீனு மரிவை காவ லாவேதன்","அரியும் வாழ வானாளு ...... மதிரேகா","கடுவி டாக ளர்ருப நடவி நோத தாடாளர்","கருதி டார்கள் தநமுள ...... முதல்நாடுங்","கடவு ளேறு மீதேறி புதல்வ கார ணாவேத","கருணை மேரு வேதேவர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether Murugan, who cannot be touched or seen directly or answered by those who recite scripture, who is lord of the directions, the one turiya, other than all and knowledge itself, and who entered the body of long arms and legs saying 'you and I', will not tell him the one truth by which he may go before the fierce messenger sent by Death arrives. The praise names the vel that killed crores of Suras, the guardian of the girl born of a doe, and Siva the poison-throated dancer riding the bull.","n":"நீ நானும் என நேர்மை நூல் கூறி: the Lord entering the body and teaching 'you and I' may point to the non-dual teaching of 1038. மான் ஈனும் அரிவை is Valli, born of a doe. The plea's last clause is hard to split, and the line has a stray ** in the source.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","kootru","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:238"},"p:1051":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1051,"t":["நிலவில் மார னேறு஡தை யசைய வீசு மாராம","நிழலில் மாட மாமாளி ...... கையின்மேலாம்","நிலையில் வாச மாறாத அணையில் மாத ராரோடு","நியதி யாக வாயார ...... வயிறார","இலவி லு஡று தேனூறல் பருகி யார வாமீறி","யிளகி யேறு பாடீர ...... தனபாரம்","எனது மார்பி ஧ல்முழ்க இறுக மேவி மால்கூரு","கினுமு னீப சீர்பாத ...... மறவேனே","குலவி யோம பாகீர திமிலை நாதர் மாதேவர்","குழைய மாலி காநாக ...... மொடுதாவிக்","குடில கோம ளாகார சடில மோலி மீதேறு","குமர வேட மாதோடு ...... பிரியாது","கலவி கூரு மீராறு கனக வாகு வேசூரர்","கடக வாரிதூளாக ...... அமராடுங்","கடக போல மால்யானை வனிதை பாக வேல்வீர","கருணை மேரு வேதேவர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes lovemaking in moonlight as Kama's wind blows, in garden shade and on the mansion's upper floor, on a fragrant bed, drinking the honey of lips like silk-cotton flowers and pressing breasts to his chest, and vows that even as infatuation grows he will not forget Murugan's kadamba-garlanded feet. The praise shows the child Kumara climbing onto Siva's matted crown with Ganga and the serpent, never parting from the hunter girl, and the vel that turned Sura's army to dust, consort of the elephant's girl.","n":"Unusually, the plea does not ask to be freed from women: மால் கூரினும் உன் நீப சீர்பாதம் மறவேனே, even in growing infatuation he will not forget the feet. The child climbing the father's matted crown (சடில மோலி மீது ஏறு குமர) is a tender image. ஡ stands for ஊ (ஊதை, ஊறு), and பி஧ல்முழ்க is corrupt for பில் மூழ்க.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["punarchi","thiruvadi","mayal"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:238"},"p:1052":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1052,"t":["மனக பாட பாடீர தனத ராத ரர்ருப","மதன ராச ராசீப ...... சரகோப","வருண பாத காலோக தருண சோபி தாகார","மகளி ரோடு சீராடி ...... யிதமாடிக்","குனகு வேனை நாணாது தனகு வேனை வீணான","குறைய னேனை நாயேனை ...... வினையேனைக்","கொடிய னேனை யொதாத குதலை யேனை நாடாத","குருட னேனை நீயாள்வ ...... தொருநாளே","அநக வாம னாகார முநி ராக மால்தேட","அரிய தாதை தானேவ ...... மதுரேசன்","அரிய சார தாபீட மதனி லேறி யீடேற","அகில நாலு மாராயு ...... மிளையோனே","கனக பாவ னாகார பவள கோம ளாகார","கலப சாம ளாகார ...... மயிலேறுங்","கடவு ளேக்ரு பாகார கமல வேத னாகார","கருணை மேரு வேதேவர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"After playing with women of sandal-smeared breasts and Kama's arrows, the poet heaps names on himself: one who fawns shamelessly, dallies, is worthless, a dog, sinful, cruel, a babbler without learning, blind and seeking nothing, and asks whether there will be a day when Murugan rules him. The praise says that, sent by the father whom Vishnu could not find, Murugan as Madurai's lord mounted the seat of learning and examined all four, and calls him Meru of compassion, rider of the peacock.","n":"The run of self-names (குனகுவேனை, குறையனேனை, நாயேனை, வினையேனை, கொடியனேனை, குதலையேனை, குருடனேனை) makes the confession. மதுரேசன் அரிய சாரதா பீடம் அதனில் ஏறி அகில நாலும் ஆராயும் may allude to Murugan judging the poems of the Madurai academy, but which story and what 'all four' means is not clear from the text.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kutram","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:239"},"p:1053":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1053,"t":["அதல சேட னாராட அகில மேரு மீதாட","அபின காளி தானாட ...... அவளோடன்","றதிர வீசி வாதாடும் விடையி லேறு வாராட","அருகு பூத வேதாள ...... மவையாட","மதுர வாணி தானாட மலரில் வேத னாராட","மருவு வானு ளோராட ...... மதியாட","வனச மாமி யாராட நெடிய மாம னாராட","மயிலு மாடி நீயாடி ...... வரவேணும்","கதைவி டாத தோள்வீம னெதிர்கொள் வாளி யால்நீடு","கருத லார்கள் மாசேனை ...... பொடியாகக்","கதறு காலி போய்மீள விஜய னேறு தேர்மீது","கனக வேத கேர்டுதி ...... அலைமோதும்","உ ததி மீதி லேசாயு முலக மூடு சீர்பாத","உ வண மூர்தி மாமாயன் ...... மருகோனே","உ தய தாம மார்பான ப்ரபுட தேவ மாராஜ","னுளமு மாட வாழ்தேவர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks Murugan to come dancing on his peacock while all dance: the serpent Adisesha below, Meru, Kali and Siva on his bull who danced against her, ghosts and vetalas, Sarasvati and Brahma, the gods, the moon, and Lakshmi and tall Vishnu as Murugan's in-laws. The praise recalls Bhima with his mace and Arjuna's arrows reducing the enemy armies, the bellowing cattle brought back, and Vishnu who reclines on the ocean and rides Garuda, Murugan's uncle, and names him Lord who dwells in the heart of the great king Prabhuda Deva.","n":"The whole plea is a cosmic dance with every figure joining (ஆட repeated), ending மயிலும் ஆடி நீ ஆடி வரவேணும். வனச மாமியார், நெடிய மாமனார: Lakshmi and Vishnu as mother- and father-in-law, since Murugan's wives are counted Vishnu's daughters. ப்ரபுட தேவ மாராஜன் names a king, the only patron named in this chapter. கேர்டுதி is corrupt.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thirukkoothu","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:239"},"p:1054":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1054,"t":["குருதி மூளை யூனாறு மலம றாத தோல்மூடு","குடிசை கோழை மாசூறு ...... குழிநீர்மேற்","குமிழி போல நேராகி அழியு மாயை யாதார","குறடு பாறு நாய்கூளி ...... பலகாகம்","பருகு காய மேபேணி அறிவி லாம லேவீணில்","படியின் மூழ்கி யேபோது ...... தளிர்வீசிப்","பரவு நாட காசார கிரியை யாளர் காணாத","பரம ஞான வீடேது ...... புகல்வாயே","எரியின் மேனி நீறாடு பரமர் பாலில் வாழ்வான","இமய மாது மாசூலி ...... தருபாலா","எழுமை யீறு காணாதர் முநிவ ரோடு வானாடர்","இசைக ளோடு பாராட ...... மகிழ்வோனே","அரவி னோடு மாமேரு மகர வாரிபூலோக","மதிர நாக மோரேழு ...... பொடியாக","அலகை பூத மாகாளி சமர பூமி மீதாட","அசுரர் மாள வேலேவு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses cherishing without sense the body, a hut covered with skin over blood, brain, flesh, excrement and phlegm, a bubble on pit water that bursts, food for vultures, dogs, ghouls and crows, sinking in the world in vain, and asks Murugan to tell him the abode of supreme wisdom that ritualists who strew flowers and shoots in showy rites cannot see. The praise names him child of Uma, wife of the ash-smeared Siva, delighting as sages and gods praise him in song, whose vel powdered serpent, Meru and seas while ghouls and Kali danced on the battlefield.","n":"குழிநீர் மேல் குமிழி போல: the body as a bubble on standing water. The ritualists are dismissed as நாடக ஆசார கிரியையாளர், rites like a stage play. The closing question repeats 1049's formula word for word.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","veedu","kiriyai","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:239"},"p:1055":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1055,"t":["சரியு மவல யாக்கையு ளெரியு முரிய தீப்பசி","தணிகை பொருடி ராப்பகல் ...... தடுமாறுஞ்","சகல சமய தார்க்கிகர் கலக மொழிய நாக்கொடு","சரண கமல மேத்திய ...... வழிபாடுற்","றரிய துரிய மேற்படு கருவி கரண நீத்ததொ","ரறிவின் வடிவ மாய்ப்புள ...... கிதமாகி","அவச கவச மூச்சற அமரு மமலர் மேற்சில","ரதிப திவிடு பூக்கணை ...... படுமோதான்","விரியு முதய பாஸ்கர கிரண மறைய வார்ப்பெழ","மிடையு மலகில் தேர்ப்படை ...... யொடுசூழும்","விகட மகுட பார்த்திப ரனைவ ருடனு நூற்றுவர்","விசைய னொருவ னாற்பட ...... வொருதூது","திரியு மொருப ராக்ரம அரியின் மருக பார்ப்பதி","சிறுவ தறுகண் வேட்டுவர் ...... கொடிகோவே","திமிர யுததி கூப்பிட அவுணர் மடிய வேற்கொடு","சிகரிதகர வீக்கிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet, staggering day and night to quench the hunger-fire burning in his sagging body, asks: when the quarrels of logicians of every creed are ended and one worships the lotus feet with the tongue, and becomes a form of knowledge beyond turiya and beyond all instruments, thrilled, the breath stilled, will Kama's flower arrows strike those pure ones? The praise recalls the Kurukshetra war in which the hundred fell by Arjuna, and Krishna who went once as envoy, Murugan's uncle, and the vel that made the ocean cry out and shattered the hill.","n":"சகல சமய தார்க்கிகர் கலகம் ஒழிய: the quarrel of the creeds' debaters must end before worship. The question is rhetorical: Kama's arrows (ரதிபதி விடு பூக்கணை) cannot touch one who has passed beyond the instruments, so the plea is for that state. ஒரு தூது திரியும் அரி is Krishna's embassy to the Kauravas.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["samayam","gnaanam","pasi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:239"},"p:1056":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1056,"t":["மகளு மனைவி தாய்க்குல மணையு மனைவர் வாக்கினில்","மறுகி புறமு மார்த்திட ...... வுடலு஡டே","மருவு முயிரை நோக்கமு மெரியை யுமிழ ஆர்ப்பவ","ருடனு மியமன் மாட்டிட ...... அணுகாமுன்","உ கமு முடிவு மாச்செலு முதய மதியி னோட்டமு","முளது மிலது மாச்சென ...... வுறைவோரும்","உ ருகு முரிமை காட்டிய முருக னெனவு நாக்கொடு","உ னது கழல்கள் போற்றிட ...... அருள்தாராய்","புகல வரிய போர்ச்சிலை விரக விசைய னாற்புக","ழுடைய திருத ராட்டிர ...... புதல்வோர்தம்","புரவி கரிகள் தேர்ப்படை மடிய அரசை மாய்த்துயர்","புவியின் விதன மாற்றினர் ...... மருகோனே","மிகவு மலையு மாக்கடல் முழுது மடிய வேற்றுரு","வெனவு மருவி வேற்கொடு ...... பொருசூரன்","விரைசெய் நெடிய தோட்கன அடலுமுருவ வேற்படை","விசைய முறவும் வீக்கிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"Before Yama binds him, while daughter, wife, mother's kin and all who come wail and cry out inside and outside the house and Death's men with fire-spitting eyes shout, the poet asks to praise Murugan's anklets with his tongue, calling him the Murugan who showed his claim on those who dwell as the ages and their end, the moon's course, being and non-being. The praise recalls Arjuna whose bow destroyed the horses, elephants and chariots of Dhritarashtra's sons and Krishna who relieved the earth's burden, and the vel that pierced Sura when he took the form of mountain and sea.","n":"The deathbed is crowded with named kin (மகள், மனைவி, தாய்க்குலம்) whose wailing is useless against Yama's men. The second clause of the plea is hard to split; its sense of Murugan as one whom even beings beyond time melt toward is a guess. ஡ stands for ஊ in உடலூடே.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["kootru","sutram","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:239"},"p:1057":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1057,"t":["குடரு நீர்க்கொழு மலமு மீத்தொரு","குறைவி லாப்பல ...... என்பினாலுங்","கொடிய நோய்க்கிட மெனவு நாட்டிய","குடிலி லேற்றுயி ...... ரென்றுகூறும்","வடிவி லாப்புல மதனை நாட்டிடு","மறலி யாட்பொர ...... வந்திடாமுன்","மதியு மூத்துன தடிக ளேத்திட","மறுவிலாப் பொருள் ...... தந்திடாதோ","கடிய காட்டக முறையும் வேட்டுவர்","கருதொ ணாக்கணி ...... வெங்கையாகிக்","கழைசெய் தோட்குற மயிலை வேட்டுயர்","களவி னாற்புணர் ...... கந்தவேளே","முடுகி மேற்பொரு மசுர ரார்ப்பெழ","முடிய வேற்கொடு ...... வென்றவீரா","முடிய லாத்திரு வடிவை நோக்கிய","முதிய மூர்த்திகள் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The body is a hut of guts, water, fat and excrement on many bones, set up as a home for cruel disease, with life said to be in it; before Death's men come to fight for it, the poet asks whether Murugan will not give him the flawless truth so that his mind may ripen and praise his feet. The praise names Kanda who, becoming a vengai tree in the forest of the hunters, loved and secretly united with the Kuratti, and the Lord of the ancient gods who gazed at his endless form.","n":"மதியும் மூத்து: the mind ripening, as if wisdom were a matter of maturing, not escape. The vengai tree and களவு belong to the Valli story. வடிவிலாப் புலமதனை நாட்டிடு is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","kootru","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:239"},"p:1058":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1058,"t":["பொதுவ தாய்த்தனி முதல தாய்ப்பகல்","இரவு போய்ப்புகல் ...... கின்றவேதப்","பொருள தாய்ப்பொருள் முடிவ தாய்ப்பெரு","வெளிய தாய்ப்புதை ...... வின்றியீறில்","கதிய தாய்க்கரு தரிய தாய்ப்பரு","கமுத மாய்ப்புல ...... னைந்துமாயக்","கரண மாய்த்தெனை மரண மாற்றிய","கருணை வார்த்தையி ...... ருந்தவாறென்","உ ததி கூப்பிட நிருத ரார்ப்பெழ","உ லகு போற்றிட ...... வெங்கலாப","ஒருப ராக்ரம துரக மோட்டிய","வுரவ கோக்கிரி...... நண்பவானோர்","முதல்வ பார்ப்பதி புதல்வ கார்த்திகை","முலைகள் தேக்கிட ...... வுண்டவாழ்வே","முளரிபாற்கடல் சயில மேற்பயில்","முதிய மூர்த்திகள் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet marvels at the word of grace Murugan spoke that killed his five senses and instruments and turned death away from him: a word common to all and the one first source, the meaning of the Vedas beyond day and night, the end of all meaning, great open space, the endless goal, inconceivable, nectar to drink. The praise names the vel that made the ocean cry out, the mighty peacock, the son of Parvati who drank at the breasts of the Krittika women, and the Lord of the ancient gods of lotus, milk sea and mountain.","n":"Unusually the plea is in the past tense: கருணை வார்த்தை இருந்தவாறு என், 'how was that word of grace!', marvelling at a teaching already received. மரணம் மாற்றிய: the word has turned death away. பெருவெளியதாய் names the teaching as vast space.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["guru","gnaanam","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:239"},"p:1059":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1059,"t":["கவடு கோத்தெழு முவரிமாத்திறல்","காய்வேல் பாடே னாடேன் வீடா ...... னதுகூட","கருணை கூர்ப்பன கழல்க ளார்ப்பன","கால்மேல் வீழேன் வீழ்வார் கால்மீ ...... தினும்வீழேன்","தவிடி னார்ப்பத மெனினு மேற்பவர்","தாழா தீயேன் வாழா தேசா ...... வதுசாலத்","தரமு மோக்ஷமு மினியெ னாக்கைச","தாவா மாறே நீதா னாதா ...... புரிவாயே","சுவடு பார்த்தட வருக ராத்தலை","தூளா மாறே தானா நாரா ...... யணனேநற்","றுணைவ பாற்கடல் வனிதை சேர்ப்பது","ழாய்மார் பாகோ பாலா காவா ...... யெனவேகைக்","குவடு கூப்பிட வுவண மேற்கன","கேர்டு தாவா னேபோ தாள்வான் ...... மருகோனே","குலிச பார்த்திப னுலகு காத்தருள்","கோவே தேவே வேளே வானோர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses that he does not sing or seek the vel that withered the great mango tree rising from the salt sea, to reach liberation, does not fall at the ringing feet full of compassion, nor even at the feet of those who fall at them, does not give without delay even a handful of bran to those who ask, and so dies without having lived; he asks Murugan to grant him worth and liberation forever. The praise tells how the elephant gripped by the crocodile cried 'Narayana, Gopala, save!' and Vishnu came on Garuda and crushed its head, and calls Murugan his nephew and protector of Indra's world.","n":"The confession is a list of omissions, each worse than the last: not singing, not falling at the feet, not falling even at the devotees' feet (வீழ்வார் கால் மீதினும் வீழேன்), not giving even bran (தவிடின் ஆர்ப் பதம்). வாழாதே சாவது: to die without having lived.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","kutram","adiyaar","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:239"},"p:1060":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1060,"t":["பருதி யாய்ப்பனி மதிய மாய்ப்படர்","பாராய் வானாய் நீர்தீ காலா ...... யுடுசாலம்","பலவு மாய்ப்பல கிழமை யாய்ப் பதி","னாலா றேழா மேனா ளாயே ...... ழுலகாகிச்","சுருதி யாய்ச்சுரு திகளின் மேற்சுட","ராய்வே தாவாய் மாலாய் மேலே ...... சிவமான","தொலைவி லாப்பொரு ளிருள்பு காக்கழல்","சூடா நாடா ஈடே றாதே ...... சுழல்வேனோ","திருத ராட்டிர னுதவு நூற்றுவர்","சேணா டாள்வா னாளோர் மூவா ...... றினில்வீழத்","திலக பார்த்தனு முலகு காத்தருள்","சீரா மாறே ஧த்ருர் கோமான் ...... மருகோனே","குருதி வேற்கர நிருத ராஷத","கோபா நீபா குதா ளாமா ...... மயில்வீரா","குலிச பார்த்திப னுலகு காத்தருள்","கோவே தேவே வேளே வானோர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether he must go round without being saved, not wearing or seeking the feet that darkness cannot enter of the boundless reality that is sun, moon, earth, sky, water, fire, wind, the stars, the days of the week, the twenty-seven asterisms, the seven worlds, the Veda and the light beyond it, Brahma, Vishnu and, above them, Sivam. The praise recalls the eighteen days in which the hundred sons of Dhritarashtra fell while Partha was protected by Krishna, Murugan's uncle, and names Murugan of the vel and kadamba on his peacock.","n":"The list climbs from the elements and time (கிழமை, பதினாலு ஆறு ஏழாம் நாள், the 27 stars) through the gods to மேலே சிவமான தொலைவிலாப் பொருள். இருள் புகாக் கழல்: feet no darkness enters. The line naming Krishna is corrupt (஧த்ருர் கோமான்).","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["viyaabagam","sivam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:239"},"p:1061":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1061,"t":["முதலி யாக்கையு மிளமை நீத்தற","மூவா தாரா காவா தாரா ...... எனஞாலம்","முறையி டாப்படு பறைக ளார்த்தெழ","மூடா வீடு டேகேள் கோகோ ...... எனநோவ","மதலை கூப்பிட மனைவி கூப்பிட","மாதா மோதா வீழா வாழ்வே ...... யெனமாய","மறலி யூர்ப்புகு மரண யாத்திரை","வாரா வானாள் போநாம் நீமீ ...... ளெனவேணும்","புதல றாப்புன எயினர் கூக்குரல்","போகா நாடார் பாரா வாரா ...... ரசுரோடப்","பொருது தாக்கிய வயப ராக்ரம","பூபா லாநீ பாபா லாதா ...... தையுமோதுங்","குதலை வாய்க்குரு பரச டாக்ஷர","கோடா ரூபா ரூபா பா஡ண ...... சதவேள்விக்","குலிச பார்த்திப னுலகு காத்தருள்","கோவே தேவே வேளே வானோர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"As youth passes and the body goes, drums sound, the house rings with 'ko ko', the child cries, the wife cries, the mother falls crying 'my life', and the funeral journey enters Death's town, the poet asks Murugan to come on that day and say to Death 'go' and to him 'return'. The praise names the hero who routed the asuras amid the hunters' cries in the millet field, the babbling child-guru who taught even his father, the six-syllabled one, with form and without.","n":"தாதையும் ஓதும் குதலை வாய்க் குருபர: the child who with a babbling mouth taught even his father, the teaching of Siva. சடாக்ஷர is the six-syllable mantra of Murugan. The plea's last words (வாரா வானாள் போநாம் நீ மீள்) are hard to split, and பா஡ண is corrupt, perhaps பூரண.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["kootru","nilaiyaamai","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:239"},"p:1062":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1062,"t":["வருக வீட்டெனும் விரகர் நேத்திரம்","வாளோ வேலோ சேலோ மானோ ...... எனுமாதர்","மனது போற்கரு கினகு வாற்குழல்","வானோ கானோ மாயா மாயோன் ...... வடிவேயோ","பருகு பாற்கடல் முருகு தேக்கிய","பாலோ தேனோ பாகோ வானோ ...... ரமுதேயோ","பவள வாய்ப்பனி மொழியெ னாக்கவி","பாடா நாயே னீடே றாதே ...... யொழிவேனோ","அருகு பார்ப்பதி யுருகி நோக்கவொ","ரால்கீழ் வாழ்வார் வாழ்வே கோகோ ...... வெனஏகி","அவுணர் கூப்பிட வுததி தீப்பட","ஆகா சூரா போகா தேமீ ...... ளெனவோடிக்","குருகு பேர்க்கிரியுருவ வோச்சிய","கூர்வே லாலே யோர்வா ளாலே ...... அமராடிக்","குலிச பார்த்திப னுலகு காத்தருள்","கோவே தேவே வேளே வானோர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet, who sang verses on women who say 'come to the house', asking whether their eyes are sword, vel, carp or deer, their dark curling hair cloud, forest or Mayon's form, their coral mouths milk, honey, syrup or the gods' nectar, asks whether he, a dog, must perish without being saved. The praise shows Murugan running as Parvati watches melting, crying to Sura 'don't go, turn back' as the asuras wail and the ocean burns, the vel that pierced the Krauncha hill, and calls him treasure of the one under the banyan.","n":"The confession is about the poet's own craft: the string of 'is it this or that?' comparisons is the love poetry he wasted on courtesans (கவி பாடா நாயேன்), as in 1039 he wasted praise on patrons. ஆல் கீழ் வாழ்வார் is Siva seated under the banyan.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","mayal","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:239"},"p:1063":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1063,"t":["மறலி போற்சில நயன வேற்கொடு","மாயா தோயா வேயார் தோளார் ...... மறையோதும்","வகையு மார்க்கமு மறமு மாய்த்திட","வாறா ராயா தேபோ மாறா ...... திடதீர","விறலு மேற்பொலி அறிவு மாக்கமும்","வேறாய் நீரே றாதோர் மேடாய் ...... வினையூடே","விழுவி னாற்களை யெழும தாற்பெரு","வீரா பாராய் வீணே மேவா ...... தெனையாளாய்","மறலி சாய்த்தவ ரிறைப ராக்ரம","மால்கா ணாதே மாதோ டேவாழ் ...... பவர்சேயே","மறுவி லாத்திரு வடிக ணாட்டொறும்","வாயார் நாவால் மாறா தேயோ ...... தினர்வாழ்வே","குறவர் காற்புன அரிவை தோட்கன","கோடார் மார்பா கூர்வே லாலே ...... அசுரேசர்","குலைய மாக்கட லதனி லோட்டிய","கோவே தேவே வேளே வானோர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says women's eyes like Death's vels have killed the way, path and dharma of Vedic recitation, his courage, shining knowledge and fortune have left, and he has become a mound water cannot climb, where weeds spring up in what falls; he asks the hero to look and rule him so that he does not go in vain. The praise calls Murugan son of the one who felled Death, whom Vishnu could not see, treasure of those who daily recite his flawless feet with full mouths, and the vel that drove the asura chiefs into the sea.","n":"நீர் ஏறாதோர் மேடாய்: a raised patch the water never reaches, where only weeds grow, the poet's barren life. மறலி சாய்த்தவர், the one who felled Death, is Siva. Several splits in the plea are uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kedu","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:239"},"p:1064":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1064,"t":["குருதி யொழுகி யழுகு மவல","குடிலை யினிது ...... புகலாலே","குலவு மினிய கலவி மகளிர்","கொடிய கடிய ...... விழியாலே","கருது மெனது விரக முழுது","கலக மறலி ...... அழியாமுன்","கனக மயிலி னழகு பொழிய","கருணை மருவி ...... வரவேணும்","பரிதி சுழல மருவு கிரியை","பகிர எறிசெய் ...... பணிவேலா","பணில வுததி யதனி லசுரர்","பதியை முடுக ...... வரும்வீரா","இரதி பதியை யெரிசெய் தருளு","மிறைவர் குமர ...... முருகோனே","இலகு கமல முகமு மழகு","மெழுத வரிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"Before quarrelsome Death destroys all the lust that, through women's cruel eyes in lovemaking, drew him with delight into this rotting hut that runs with blood, the poet asks Murugan to come in compassion on the golden peacock, its beauty pouring. The praise names the vel that split the hill the sun circles, the hero who chased the asura chief across the conch-filled sea, the son of the Lord who burned Rati's husband, and the lotus face too beautiful to draw.","n":"இரதி பதியை எரி செய்தருளும் இறைவர் குமர: Murugan as son of the Lord who burned Kama, set against the lust of the plea. எழுத அரிய, the beauty that cannot be drawn.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","kaamam","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:239"},"p:1065":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1065,"t":["துயர மறுநின் வறுமை தொலையு","மொழியு மமிர்த ...... சுரபானம்","சுரபி குளிகை யெளிது பெறுக","துவளு மெமது ...... பசிதீரத்","தயிரு மமுது மமையு மிடுக","சவடி கடக ...... நெளிகாறை","தருக தகடொ டுருக எனுமி","விரகு தவிர்வ ...... தொருநாளே","உ யரு நிகரில் சிகரிமிடறு","முடலு மவுணர் ...... நெடுமார்பும்","உ ருவ மகர முகர திமிர","வுததி யுதர ...... மதுபீற","அயரு மமரர் சரண நிகள","முறிய எறியு ...... மயில்வீரா","அறிவு முரமு மறமு நிறமு","மழகு முடைய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks when there will be a day free of the trickery of those who say 'your sorrow will end and your poverty go; you will easily get the nectar drink, the wish-cow and the magic pill; to end our wasting hunger give us curd and rice, anklets, bracelets and necklaces and gold plates to melt'. The praise names the vel that pierced the high hill and the asuras' chests and split the ocean's belly, the hero on the peacock who broke the gods' fetters, possessed of knowledge, strength, virtue, splendour and beauty.","n":"The speakers are not named. They promise wonders (அமிர்த சுரபானம், சுரபி, குளிகை, the alchemical pill) and ask for food and gold, which suggests false wonder-workers rather than courtesans. இவ்விரகு தவிர்வது ஒருநாளே asks for release from this deceit.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","siththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:239"},"p:1066":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1066,"t":["பணிகள் பணமு மணிகொள் துகில்கள்","பழைய அடிமை ...... யொடுமாதும்","பகரிலொருவர் வருக அரிய","பயண மதனி ...... லுயிர்போகக்","குணமு மனமு முடைய கிளைஞர்","குறுகி விறகி ...... லுடல்போடாக்","கொடுமை யிடுமு னடிமை யடிகள்","குளிர மொழிவ ...... தருள்வாயே","இணையி லருணை பழநி கிழவ","இளைய இறைவ ...... முருகோனே","எயினர் வயினின் முயலு மயிலை","யிருகை தொழுது ...... புணர்மார்பா","அணியொ டமரர் பணிய அசுரர்","அடைய மடிய ...... விடும்வேலா","அறிவு முரமு மறமு நிறமு","மழகு முடைய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"Since ornaments, wealth, jewelled clothes, old servants and wife will not come with him on the hard journey when life leaves, and kin of good heart will gather only to lay the body on firewood, the poet asks Murugan, before that cruelty, to grant that he, his servant, speak of his feet so that they are pleased. The praise calls him the matchless one of Arunai and Palani, the old one and the young lord, who embraced the hunters' peacock-like girl after worshipping her with both hands.","n":"பகரில் ஒருவர் வருக அரிய பயணம்: no one comes on that journey. கிழவ இளைய pairs 'old' and 'young' as epithets. இருகை தொழுது புணர்: Murugan worships Valli with folded hands before union. அடிமை அடிகள் குளிர மொழிவது may also mean that the feet speak coolly to the servant.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","sutram","thondu","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:239"},"p:1067":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1067,"t":["மைந்த ரினிய தந்தை மனைவி","மண்டி யலறி ...... மதிமாய","வஞ்ச விழிகள் விஞ்சு மறலி","வன்கை யதனி ...... லுறுபாசந்","தந்து வளைய புந்தி யறிவு","தங்கை குலைய ...... உ யிர்போமுன்","தம்ப முனது செம்பொ னடிகள்","தந்து கருணை ...... புரிவாயே","மந்தி குதிகொ ளந்தண் வரையில்","மங்கை மருவு ...... மணவாளா","மண்டு மசுரர் தண்ட முடைய","அண்டர் பரவ ...... மலைவோனே","இந்து நுதலு மந்த முகமு","மென்று மினிய ...... மடவார்தம்","இன்பம் விளைய அன்பி னணையு","மென்று மிளைய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"Before his children, father and wife crowd round wailing, before Death with his cruel eyes casts the noose from his strong hand to bind him, the mind fails and life goes, the poet asks Murugan to give his red-gold feet as a pillar to lean on and show grace. The praise names him bridegroom of the girl of the cool hill where monkeys leap, the warrior who fights the crowding asuras as the gods praise, and the ever-young lord who embraces with love women with moon-like foreheads.","n":"தம்பம் உனது செம்பொன் அடிகள்: the feet as a pillar (ஸ்தம்பம்) to hold when the body is shaken. The closing epithet, the ever-young lord who embraces women for their delight, turns the language of the poet's own confession into praise of the god's love.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kootru","thiruvadi","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:240"},"p:1068":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1068,"t":["ஒழுகூ னிரத்த மொடுதோ லுடுத்தி","உ யர்கால் கரத்தி ...... னுருவாகி","ஒருதாய் வயிற்றி னிடையே யுதித்து","உ ழல்மாய மிக்கு ...... வருகாயம்","பழசா யிரைப்பொ டிளையா விருத்த","பரிதாப முற்று ...... மடியாமுன்","பரிவா லுளத்தில் முருகா எனச்சொல்","பகர்வாழ் வெனக்கு ...... மருள்வாயே","எழுவா னகத்தி லிருநாலு திக்கில்","இமையோர் தமக்கு ...... மரசாகி","எதிரேறு மத்த மதவார ணத்தில்","இனிதேறு கொற்ற ...... முடன்வாழுஞ்","செழுமா மணிப்பொ னகர்பாழ் படுத்து","செழுதீ விளைத்து ...... மதிள்கோலித்","திடமோ டரக்கர் கொடுபோ யடைத்த","சிறைமீள விட்ட ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet traces the body from its birth in one mother's womb, a shape of legs and arms clothed in skin over oozing flesh and blood, wandering full of illusion, to the wheezing and pitiable old age that ends in death, and asks that before then he be given the life of saying 'Muruga' with love in his heart. The praise tells how the demons laid waste the gem-rich golden city of the king of the gods who rides the rutting elephant, set it on fire, and carried the gods off to prison, and how Murugan set them free.","n":"The whole remedy is a name: முருகா எனச் சொல் பகர் வாழ்வு, a life of saying 'Muruga'. The city is Indra's Amaravati and the elephant his Airavata, though neither is named here; 1069 names them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","karu","ilamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:240"},"p:1069":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1069,"t":["கருவாய் வயிற்றி லுருவா யுதித்து","முருகாய் மனக்க ...... வலையோஆட","கலைநூல் பிதற்றி நடுவே கறுத்த","தலைபோய் வெளுத்து ...... மரியாதே","இருபோது மற்றை யொருபோது மிட்ட","கனல்மூழ்கி மிக்க ...... புனல்மூழ்கி","இறவாத சுத்த மறையோர் துதிக்கு","மியல்போத கத்தை ...... மொழிவாயே","அருமாத பத்தஅமரா பதிக்கு","வழ஧முடி விட்ட ...... தனைமீள","அயிரா வதத்து விழியா யிரத்த","னுடனே பிடித்து ...... முடியாதே","திருவான கற்ப தருநா டழித்து","விபுதேசர் சுற்ற ...... மவைகோலித்","திடமோ டரக்கர் கொடுபோ யடைத்த","சிறைமீள விட்ட ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet, born in a womb, beautiful in youth, babbling the learned books while his black hair turns white without understanding, asks to be taught the true knowledge that the pure, undying Vedic brahmins praise, who plunge into the fire rite twice a day and once more and bathe in water. The praise, sharing its close with 1068, tells how the demons took the crown of Amaravati, seized Indra of the thousand eyes with his elephant Airavata, ruined the land of the wish-granting tree and imprisoned the gods, whom Murugan freed.","n":"கலைநூல் பிதற்றி: book learning is mere prattle while the hair greys. The knowledge sought (இயல் போதகம்) is the one the fire-tending brahmins praise, so the song sets learning against teaching rather than rite against devotion. A corrupt glyph sits in the line about the crown, and how முருகாய் மனக்கவலையோஆட divides is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["gnaanam","kalvi","andhanar","aaguthi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:240"},"p:1070":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1070,"t":["புரக்க வந்தநங் குறக்க ரும்பைமென்","புனத்தி லன்றுசென் ...... றுறவாடிப்","புடைத்த லங்க்ருதம் படைத்தெ ழுந்ததிண்","புதுக்கு ரும்பைமென் ...... புயமீதே","செருக்க நெஞ்சகங் களிக்க அன்புடன்","திளைக்கு நின்திறம் ...... புகலாதிந்","த்ரியக்க டஞ்சுமந் தலக்கண் மண்டிடுந்","தியக்க மென்றொழிந் ...... திடுவேனோ","குரக்கி னங்கொணர்ந் தரக்கர் தண்டமுங்","குவட்டி லங்கையுந் ...... துகளாகக்","கொதித்த கொண்டலுந் த்ரியக்ஷ ருங்கடங்","கொதித்து மண்டுவெம் ...... பகையோடத்","துரக்கும் விம்பகிம் புரிப்ர சண்டசிந்","துரத்த னும்பிறந் ...... திறவாத","சுகத்தி லன்பருஞ் செகத்ர யங்களுந்","துதிக்கு மும்பர்தம் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether he will end the bewilderment of carrying this pot of the senses and swarming in distress without telling, with love, of how Murugan once went to the millet field of the hunter girl who guarded it and rejoiced upon her young breasts. The praise recalls Rama, who brought the monkey hordes and ground Lanka and its demons to dust, and names Murugan lord of the gods, praised by devotees in the bliss beyond birth and death and by the three worlds.","n":"The plea is not to escape Valli's story but to sing it: telling of Murugan's love for her (நின் திறம் புகலாது) is the cure for the confusion of the senses (இந்த்ரிய கடம், the body as a pot). The middle of the praise, with the angry cloud-hued one, the three-eyed one and a fierce elephant, is hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","yaakkai","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:240"},"p:1071":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1071,"t":["பெருக்க நெஞ்சுவந் துருக்கு மன்பிலன்","ப்ரபுத்த னங்கள்பண் ...... பெணுநாணும்","பிழைக்க வொன்றிலன் சிலைக்கை மிண்டர்குன்","றமைத்த பெண்தனந் ...... தனையாரத்","திருக்கை கொண்டணைந் திடச்செல் கின்றநின்","திறத்தை யன்புடன் ...... தெளியாதே","சினத்தில் மண்டிமிண் டுரைக்கும் வம்பனென்","திருக்கு மென்றொழிந் ...... திடுவேனோ","தருக்கி யன்றுசென் றருட்க ணொன்றரன்","தரித்த குன்றநின் ...... றடியோடுந்","தடக்கை கொண்டுவந் தெடுத்த வன்சிரந்","தறித்த கண்டனெண் ...... டிசையோருஞ்","சுருக்க மின்றிநின் றருக்க னிந்திரன்","துணைச்செய் கின்றநின் ...... பதமேவும்","சுகத்தி லன்பருஞ் செகத்ர யங்களுந்","துதிக்கு மும்பர்தம் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet calls himself one with no love that melts the heart, no nobility, character or shame, nothing to be saved by, and asks whether he will end as a quarrelsome scoundrel who speaks insolently in anger, without clearly knowing with love how Murugan went to embrace the breasts of the girl of the bowmen's hill. The praise recalls the one who arrogantly lifted the hill where Siva stays and had his heads cut off, and names Murugan, whose feet the gods of the eight directions, the sun and Indra serve, praised by the three worlds.","n":"The self-abuse is specific: not lust but anger and insolent speech (சினத்தில் மண்டி மிண்டு உரைக்கும் வம்பன்). The one who lifted Siva's hill is Ravana; who cut his heads (Rama, as in 1070) and how that clause joins the praise of Murugan is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kutram","vekuli","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:240"},"p:1072":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1072,"t":["இருந்த வீடுங் கொஞ்சிய சிறுவரு ...... முறுகேளும்","இசைந்த வூரும் பெண்டிரு மிளமையும் ...... வளமேவும்","விரிந்த நாடுங் குன்றமு நிலையென ...... மகிழாதே","விளங்கு தீபங் கொண்டுனை வழிபட ...... அருள்வாயே","குருந்தி லேருங் கொண்டலின் வடிவினன் ...... மருகோனே","குரங்கு லாவுங் குன்றுறை குறமகள் ...... மணவாளா","திருந்த வேதந் தண்டமிழ் தெரிதரு ...... புலவோனெ","சிவந்த காலுந் தண்டையு மழகிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks not to rejoice as if they were permanent in the house he lives in, his prattling children, kin, home town, wives, youth, wealth, wide lands and hills, but to be granted to worship Murugan with a shining lamp. The praise names him nephew of the cloud-hued one who climbed the kurundu tree, bridegroom of the hunter girl of the monkey-haunted hill, the scholar who knows the Veda and cool Tamil, and the lord of red feet and anklets.","n":"The remedy for clinging is a concrete rite, worship with a lamp (விளங்கு தீபம் கொண்டு உனை வழிபட). Murugan as the scholar of both Veda and Tamil (வேதம் தண்டமிழ் தெரிதரு புலவோன்) sets the two side by side.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","pattru","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:240"},"p:1073":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1073,"t":["கலந்த மாதுங் கண்களி யுறவரு ...... புதல்வோருங்","கலங்கி டாரென் றின்பமு றுலகிடை ...... கலிமேவி","உ லந்த காயங் கொண்டுள முறுதுய ...... ருடன்மேவா","உ கந்த பாதந் தந்துனை யுரைசெய ...... அருள்வாயே","மலர்ந்த பூவின் மங்கையை மருவரி...... மருகோனே","மறஞ்செய் வார்தம் வஞ்சியை மருவிய ...... மணவாளா","சிலம்பி னோடுங் கிண்கிணி திசைதொறும் ...... ஒலிவீசச்","சிவந்த காலுந் தண்டையு மழகிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"Trusting that the wife he joined and the sons who delight his eyes would never fail him, the poet took his pleasure in the world and fell into misery, with a wasting body and grief in his heart; he asks instead for Murugan's beloved feet and the grace to speak of him. The praise names him nephew of Hari who joined the lady of the blossoming flower, bridegroom of the hunters' creeper-like girl, and lord of red feet whose anklets and bells ring in every direction.","n":"Companion to 1072: the same closing line, and the same turn from family to the feet, here as the disappointment of trust (கலங்கிடார் என்று, 'they will not falter') rather than a list of things that pass.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pattru","ilvaazhkkai","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:240"},"p:1074":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1074,"t":["இசைந்த ஏறுங் கரியுரிபோர்வையும் ...... எழில்நீறும்","இலங்கு நூலும் புலியத ளாடையு ...... மழுமானும்","அசைந்த தோடுஞ் சிரமணி மாலையு ...... முடிமீதே","அணிந்த ஈசன் பரிவுடன் மேவிய ...... குருநாதா","உ சந்த சூரன் கிளையுடன் வேரற ...... முனிவோனே","உ கந்த பாசங் கயிறொடு தூதுவர் ...... நலியாதே","அசந்த போதென் துயர்கெட மாமயில் ...... வரவேணும்","அமைந்த வேலும் புயமிசை மேவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"Most of the song is praise of Siva, who wears the bull, the elephant-skin cloak, ash, the sacred thread, tiger skin, axe and deer, swaying ear-ring and garland of skulls on his crown, and of Murugan as the guru whom that Lord lovingly approached, who in anger uprooted Sura with his kin. The plea asks that when the poet is spent, so that Death's messengers with their noose and rope do not torment him, Murugan come on the great peacock to end his sorrow.","n":"ஈசன் பரிவுடன் மேவிய குருநாதா: the long list of Siva's emblems builds up to Murugan as Siva's own teacher, the teaching of Om though the syllable is not named here. The plea is only two lines, for the hour of death.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","guru","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:240"},"p:1075":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1075,"t":["திரிபுர மதனை யொருநொடி யதனி","லெரிசெய்த ருளிய ...... சிவன்வாழ்வே","சினமுடை யசுரர் மனமது வெருவ","மயிலது முடுகி ...... விடுவோனே","பருவரை யதனை யுருவிட எறியு","மறுமுக முடைய ...... வடிவேலா","பசலையொ டணையு மிளமுலை மகளை","மதன்விடு பகழி ...... தொடலாமோ","கரிதிரு முகமு மிடமுடை வயிறு","முடையவர் பிறகு ...... வருவொனே","கனதன முடைய குறவர்த மகளை","கருணையொ டணையு ...... மணிமார்பா","அரவணை துயிலு மரிதிரு மருக","அவனியு முழுது ...... முடையோனே","அடியவர் வினையு மமரர்கள் துயரு","மறஅரு ளுதவு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"Praising Murugan as the life of Siva who burned the three cities in an instant, the rider who drives the peacock to terrify the asuras, the six-faced lord whose vel pierced the great mountain, and the younger one who comes after the elephant-faced, big-bellied god, the singer asks whether Kama's arrows may strike the young girl who lies pale with longing. The praise ends with Murugan who embraced the hunters' daughter, nephew of Hari asleep on the serpent, lord of the whole earth who ends the deeds of devotees and the sorrows of the gods.","n":"An akam plea in the voice of someone who speaks for a lovesick girl, a mother or a friend, set inside a frame of praise; the poet's own confession is absent. பசலையொடு அணையும் is the classical pallor of love-sickness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","pasalai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:240"},"p:1076":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1076,"t":["புழுககில் களப மொளிவிடு தரள","மணிபல செறிய ...... வடமேருப்","பொருமிரு கலச முலையினை யரிவை","புனையிடு பொதுவின் ...... மடமாதர்","அழகிய குவளை விழியினு மமுத","மொழியினு மவச ...... வநுராக","அமளியின் மிசையி லவர்வச முருகி","அழியுநி னடிமை ...... தனையாள்வாய்","குழலிசை யதுகொ டறவெருள் சுரபி","குறுநிரை தருளி ...... யலைமோதுங்","குரைசெறி யுததி வரைதனில் விறுசு","குமுகுமு குமென ...... வுலகோடு","முழுமதி சுழல வரைநெறு நெறென","முடுகிய முகிலின் ...... மருகோனே","மொகுமொகு மொகென ஞிமிறிசை பரவு","முளரியின் முதல்வர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes the common women whose Meru-like breasts are adorned with civet, agil, sandal, pearls and gem chains, with lovely blue-lily eyes and nectar speech, in whose beds he melts under their sway in helpless passion, and asks Murugan to rule him, his slave who is perishing. The praise names Murugan nephew of the cloud-hued one who gathered the frightened cows with his flute and who drove the churning of the roaring sea while the mountain whirled, and lord of the one on the bee-haunted lotus.","n":"பொதுவின் மடமாதர்: women common to all, but neither money nor deceit is named, so the song stays with passion. The praise joins Krishna's flute and the churning of the ocean as feats of Vishnu, sung in drum syllables (குமுகுமு, நெறுநெறு, மொகுமொகு). The last epithet makes Murugan lord of Brahma.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","mayal","thondu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:240"},"p:1077":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1077,"t":["முழுமதி யனைய முகமிரு குழையில்","முனிவழி முனைகள் ...... கொண்\u0002முவா","முதலறி வதனை வளைபவர் கலவி","முழுகிய வினையை ...... மொண்டுநாயேன்","வழிவழி யடிமை யெனுமறி வகல","மனமுறு துயர்கள் ...... வெந்துவாட","மதிதரு மதிக கதிபெறு மடிகள்","மகிழ்வொடு புகழு ...... மன்புதாராய்","எழுதிட அரிய எழில்மற மகளின்","இருதன கிரிகள் ...... தங்குமார்பா","எதிர்பொரு மசுரர் பொடிபட முடுகி","இமையவர் சிறையை ...... யன்றுமீள்வாய்","அழகிய குமர எழுதல மகிழ","அறுவர்கள் முலையை ...... யுண்டவாழ்வே","அமருல கிறைவ உ மைதரு புதல்வ","அரியர பிரமர் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of women with moon faces and eyes that rival their ear-rings, who ensnare the first understanding, and of the karma of plunging into union with them that he, a dog, has drawn on himself; he asks for the love that praises with joy the feet that give wisdom and the higher state, so that the sorrows of his mind wither. The praise names Murugan the chest where the hunter girl's breasts rest, the one who crushed the asuras and freed the gods, the beautiful Kumara who drank the milk of the six mothers, son of Uma, and lord of Hari, Hara and Brahma.","n":"வழிவழி அடிமை, hereditary service, appears in the plea, but whether the poet asks for that knowledge to widen or is complaining that it has been driven out is unclear from the split. அரி அர பிரமர் தம்பிரானே puts Murugan above the three gods.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","thondu","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:240"},"p:1078":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1078,"t":["கொடியன பிணிகொடு விக்கிக் கக்கிக்","கூன்போந் ...... தசடாகுங்","குடிலுற வருமொரு மிக்கச் சித்ரக்","கோண்பூண் ...... டமையாதே","பொடிவன பரசம யத்துத் தப்பிப்","போந்தேன் ...... தலைமேலே","பொருளது பெறஅடி நட்புச் சற்றுப்","பூண்டாண் ...... டருள்வாயே","துடிபட அலகைகள் கைக்கொட் டிட்டுச்","சூழ்ந்தாங் ...... குடனாடத்","தொகுதொகு திகுதிகு தொக்குத் திக்குத்","தோந்தாந் ...... தரிதாளம்","படிதரு பதிவ்ரதை யொத்தச் சுத்தப்","பாழ்ங்கான் ...... தனிலாடும்","பழயவர் குமரகு றத்தத் தைக்குப்","பாங்காம் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"Not wishing to stay in this hut of a body that comes with cruel diseases, hiccups and vomiting, bent and dulled, the poet says he has escaped from the other faiths that will crumble and come to Murugan, and asks him to set his feet on his head in a little friendship and rule him, so that he gains the true thing. The praise shows Siva, the ancient one, dancing in the bare burning ground with demons clapping and dancing round to the drum's beat and his chaste consort matching him, and names Murugan his Kumara, companion of the hunters' parrot.","n":"பரசமயத்துத் தப்பிப் போந்தேன்: the poet speaks as a convert from other sects. அடி ... தலைமேலே ... பூண்டு: the feet set on the head, the Saiva image of the guru's initiation. பதிவ்ரதை ஒத்த names Uma as the chaste wife who keeps time with Siva's dance.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["samayam","thiruvadi","yaakkai","thirukkoothu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:240"},"p:1079":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1079,"t":["சுடரொளி கதிரவ னுற்றுப் பற்றிச்","சூழ்ந்தோங் ...... கிடுபாரிற்","றுயரிரு வினைபல சுற்றப் பட்டுச்","சோர்ந்தோய்ந் ...... திடநாறுங்","கடுகென எடுமெனு டற்பற் றற்றுக்","கான்போந் ...... துறவோருங்","கனலிடை விதியிடு தத்துக் கத்தைக்","காய்ந்தாண் ...... டருளாயோ","தடமுடை வயிரவர் தற்கித் தொக்கத்","தாந்தோய்ந் ...... திருபாலும்","தமருக வொலிசவு தத்திற் றத்தத்","தாழ்ந்தூர்ந் ...... திடநாகம்","படிநெடி யவர்கர மொத்தக் கெத்துப்","பாய்ந்தாய்ந் ...... துயர்கானம்","பயில்பவர் புதல்வகு றத்தத் தைக்குப்","பாங்காம் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"In this world circled by the sun, wound round by the two kinds of deeds and worn out, the poet sees how the stinking body is treated: kin say 'take it quickly', drop their attachment, go to the burning ground and lay it in the fire by rule; he asks whether Murugan will not burn away this bundle of peril and rule him. The praise, paired with 1078, shows the Bhairavas, the damaru's sound and the serpent as Siva dances in the high burning ground while the tall one keeps time with his hands, and names Murugan his son, companion of the hunters' parrot.","n":"The plea turns the cremation fire into a figure for grace: kin burn the body by rule, and the poet asks Murugan to burn (காய்ந்து) the bundle of his troubles. Whether உறவோரும் means kin or is a variant of துறவோரும் (renouncers) changes the scene, and தத்துக் கத்தை is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","nilaiyaamai","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:240"},"p:1080":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1080,"t":["குடமென வொத்த கொங்கை குயில்மொழி யொத்த இன்சொல்","குறமகள் வைத்த நண்பை ...... நினைவோனே","வடவரை யுற்று றைந்த மகதெவர் பெற்ற கந்த","மதசல முற்ற தந்தி ...... யிளையோனே","இடமுடன் வைத்த சிந்தை யினைவற முத்தி தந்து","இசையறி வித்து வந்து ...... எனையாள்வாய்","தடவரை வெற்பி னின்று சரவண முற்றெ ழுந்து","சமர்கள வெற்றி கொண்ட ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks Murugan to come and rule him, removing the sorrow of his mind with release and making him know song. The praise names him the one who keeps in mind the love of the hunter girl with pot-like breasts and cuckoo speech, younger brother of the elephant born of the great god of the northern mountain, and the victor in battle who rose from the Saravana pool.","n":"A short song where praise outweighs plea. இசை அறிவித்து may mean 'teach me music' or 'make known (your) glory'; how இடமுடன் வைத்த சிந்தை divides is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["veedu","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:240"},"p:1081":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1081,"t":["மடவிய ரெச்சி லுண்டு கையில்முத லைக்க ளைந்து","மறுமைத னிற்சு ழன்று ...... வடிவான","சடமிக வற்றி நொந்து கலவிசெ யத்து ணிந்து","தளர்வுறு தற்கு முந்தி ...... யெனையாள்வாய்","படவர விற்சி றந்த இடமிதெ னத்து யின்ற","பசுமுகி லுக்கு கந்த ...... மருகோனே","குடமுனி கற்க வன்று தமிழ்செவி யிற்ப கர்ந்த","குமரகு றத்தி நம்பு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"Before he weakens, having eaten the spittle of women, thrown away the capital in his hand, whirled toward another birth, and still dared to make love when his body was withered and aching, the poet asks Murugan to rule him. The praise names him nephew of the green cloud who chose the hooded serpent as his bed, the one who spoke Tamil into the ear of the pot-born sage, and Kumara trusted by the hunter girl.","n":"கையில் முதலைக் களைந்து: the money lost is the capital itself, not just income. குடமுனி கற்க அன்று தமிழ் செவியிற் பகர்ந்த: Murugan as the teacher of Tamil to Agastya.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","selvam","thamizh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:240"},"p:1082":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1082,"t":["கருமய லேறிப் பெருகிய காமக்","கடலினில் மூழ்கித் ...... துயராலே","கயல்விழி யாரைப் பொருளென நாடிக்","கழியும நாளிற் ...... கடைநாளே","எருமையி லேறித் தருமனும் வாவுற்","றிறுகிய பாசக் ...... கயிறாலே","எனைவளை யாமற் றுணைநினை வேனுக்","கியலிசை பாடத் ...... தரவேணும்","திருமயில் சேர்பொற் புயனென வாழத்","தெரியல னோடப் ...... பொரும்வீரா","செகதல மீதிற் பகர்தமிழ் பாடற்","செழுமறை சேர்பொற் ...... புயநாதா","பொருமயி லேறிக் கிரிபொடி யாகப்","புவியது சூழத் ...... திரிவோனே","புனமக ளாரைக் கனதன மார்பிற்","புணரும்வி நோதப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"Sunk in the sea of lust that swelled from dark infatuation, seeking fish-eyed women as if they were wealth, the poet fears the last day when Dharma comes on his buffalo with the tightened noose, and asks that before he is bound, he who thinks of Murugan as his help be given songs of verse and music. The praise names the hero who fights until enemies flee, the lord whose golden shoulders bear the Tamil songs and Vedas sung on earth, who rode the peacock round the world making the hill dust, and who joins the millet-field girl in sport.","n":"Yama is called தருமன், Dharma, the judge, rather than கூற்று. The plea is for இயல் இசை, verse and music: the gift of song as the poet's defence at death. பகர் தமிழ் பாடல் செழுமறை சேர் பொற்புயம: songs of Tamil and Veda as garlands on Murugan's shoulders.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","kaamam","thamizh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:241"},"p:1083":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1083,"t":["குடலிடை தீதுற் றிடையிடை பீறிக்","குலவிய தோலத் ...... தியினூடே","குருதியி லேசுக் கிலமது கூடிக்","குவலயம் வானப் ...... பொருகாலாய்","உ டலெழு மாயப் பிறவியி லாவித்","துறுபிணி நோயுற் ...... றுழலாதே","உ ரையடி யேனுக் கொளிமிகு நீபத்","துனதிரு தாளைத் ...... தரவேணும்","கடலிடை சூரப் படைபொடி யாகக்","கருதல ரோடப் ...... பொரும்வேலா","கதிர்விடு வேலைக் கதிரினில் மேவிக்","கலைபல தேர்முத் ...... தமிழ்நாடா","சடையினர் நாடப் படர்மலை யோடித்","தனிவிளை யாடித் ...... திரிவோனே","தனமட மானைப் பரிவுட னாரத்","தழும்வி நோதப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet sees the body formed of earth, sky, wind and fire, where blood and semen join in the bag of skin with its foul gut, and asks that he not wander in this illusory birth suffering disease, but be given Murugan's holy feet bright with kadamba. The praise names the vel that ground Sura's army in the sea to dust, the lord of the three Tamils who chose many arts, the one who ran alone in play over the hill that the matted-haired one sought, and the one who embraces the doe-like girl.","n":"குருதியிலே சுக்கிலமது கூடி: the embryology of blood and semen, with the elements, makes birth itself the illusion (மாயப் பிறவி). The line on the 'bright sea' (கதிர்விடு வேலை) may name a shrine; the episode of the solitary play on the hill is not clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["karu","yaakkai","piravi","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:241"},"p:1084":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1084,"t":["கருதியே மெத்த விடமெலாம் வைத்த","கலகவா ளொத்த ...... விழிமானார்","கடினபோ கத்த புளகவா ருற்ற","களபமார் செப்பு ...... முலைமீதே","உ ருகியான் மெத்த அவசமே வுற்ற","வுரைகளே செப்பி ...... யழியாதுன்","உ பயபா தத்தி னருளையே செப்பு","முதயஞா னத்தை ...... அருள்வாயே","பருவரா லுற்று மடுவின்மீ துற்ற","பகடுவாய் விட்ட ...... மொழியாலே","பரிவினோ டுற்ற திகிரியே விட்ட","பழயமா யற்கு ...... மருகோனே","முருகுலா வுற்ற குழலிவே டிச்சி","முலையின்மே வுற்ற ...... க்ருபைவேளே","முருகனே பத்த ரருகனே வெற்பு","முரியவேல் தொட்ட ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses melting over the sandal-smeared, bodiced breasts of doe-eyed women whose eyes are like strife-making swords with poison set in them, and speaking words of helpless rapture; rather than perish so, he asks for the rising wisdom that speaks only of the grace of Murugan's two feet. The praise names him nephew of the old Mayan who sent the discus at the cry of the elephant seized by the crocodile in the pool, the grace of the huntress's breasts, and Murugan near to devotees whose vel broke the mountain.","n":"The cry of the elephant (பகடு வாய் விட்ட மொழி) is Gajendra's, as in 25. உதய ஞானம்: wisdom that rises like the dawn, and whose content is the feet's grace.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","gnaanam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:241"},"p:1085":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1085,"t":["கொலையிலே மெத்த விரகிலே கற்ற","குவளையேர் மைக்கண் ...... விழிமானார்","குழையிலே யெய்த்த நடையிலே நெய்த்த","குழலிலே பற்கள் ...... தனிலேமா","முலையிலே யற்ப இடையிலே பத்ம","முகநிலா வட்ட ...... மதின்மீதே","முதுகிலே பொட்டு நுதலிலே தத்தை","மொழியிலே சித்தம் ...... விடலாமோ","கலையனே உ க்ர முருகனே துட்டர்","கலகனே மெத்த ...... இளையோனே","கனகனே பித்தர் புதல்வனே மெச்சு","கடவுளே பச்சை ...... மயிலோனே","உ லகனே முத்தி முதல்வனே சித்தி","உ டையனே விஷ்ணு ...... மருகோனே","ஒருவனே செச்சை மருவுநேர் சித்ர","வுருவனே மிக்க ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"Listing the courtesans' eyes skilled in killing, their ear-rings, gait, oiled hair, teeth, breasts, slight waist, lotus face, back, forehead mark and parrot speech, the poet asks whether he should give his mind to these. The praise is a chain of names: master of arts, fierce Murugan, scourge of the wicked, the young, the golden, son of the mad one, praised god of the green peacock, the world, first of release, lord of siddhi, nephew of Vishnu, the One, the bright form garlanded with vetchi.","n":"Both halves are lists: the plea strings body parts with -இலே, the praise strings names with -னே, so the song sets the catalogue of women against the catalogue of the god. பித்தர் புதல்வனே, son of the madman, is Siva as பித்தன்.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","mayal"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:241"},"p:1086":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1086,"t":["அகிலநறுஞ் சேறு ம்ருகமதமுந் தோயு","மசலமிரண் டாலு ...... மிடைபோமென்","றடியில்விழுந் தாடு பரிபுரசெஞ் சீர","தபயமிடுங் கீத ...... மமையாதே","நகமிசைசென் றாடி வனசரர்சந் தான","நவையறநின் றேனல் ...... விளைவாள்தன்","லளிதவிர்சிங் கார தனமுறுசிந் தூர","நமசரணென் றோத ...... அருள்வாயே","பகலிரவுண் டான இருவரும்வண் டாடு","பரிமளபங் கேரு ...... கனுமாலும்","படிகநெடும் பா கடதடகெம் பீர","பணைமுகசெம் பால ...... மணிமாலை","முகபடசிந் தூர கரியில்வருந் தேவு","முடியஅரன் தேவி ...... யுடனாட","முழுதுலகுந் தாவி எழுகடல்மண் டூழி","முடிவினுமஞ் சாத ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"Not content with the song of the anklets that fall at women's feet crying for refuge, as if the waist would break under breasts soaked in agil paste and musk, the poet asks to be granted to chant 'namah, refuge' to Murugan, who loves the girl of the forest dwellers who guards the millet on the hill. The praise names the lord who does not fear even at the end of the aeon, when the seven seas flood all the worlds, Brahma of the lotus and Mal, and the god on the great vermilion-faced elephant, perish and Hara dances with his consort.","n":"The cosmic dissolution (ஊழி முடிவு) is the setting of the praise: only Murugan stands unafraid when the gods go under. நம சரண் is a Sanskrit formula of surrender. Many compounds (பகலிரவு உண்டான இருவரும், லளிதவிர்) are hard to divide, and the identity of 'the two who made day and night' is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","vazhipaadu","andam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:241"},"p:1087":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1087,"t":["கலகமதன் காதுங் கனமலரம் பாலுங்","களிமதுவண் டூதும் ...... பயிலாலும்","கடலலையங் காலுங் கனஇரையொன் றாலும்","கலைமதியங் காயும் ...... வெயிலாலும்","இலகியசங் காளும் இனியவளன் பீனும்","எனதருமின் தானின் ...... றிளையாதே","இருள்கெடமுன் தானின் றினமணிசெந் தார்தங்","கிருதனமுந் தோள்கொண் ...... டணைவாயே","உ லகைவளைந் தோடுந் கதிரவன்விண் பால்நின்","றுனதபயங் காவென் ...... றுனைநாட","உ ரவியவெஞ் சூரன் சிரமுடன்வன் தோளும்","உ ருவியுடன் போதும் ...... ஒளிவேலா","அலகையுடன் பூதம் பலகவிதம் பாடும்","அடைவுடனின் றாடும் ...... பெரியோர்முன்","அறமுமறந் தோயும் அறிவுநிரம் போதென்","றழகுடனன் றோதும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"In a mother's voice, the singer asks that before her precious lightning-like girl wastes away, tormented by quarrelsome Kama's heavy flower arrows, the humming of bees drunk on honey, the sea's waves and roar, the moon that burns like the sun, and slipping conch bangles, Murugan dispel the darkness and embrace her with his red-garlanded shoulders. The praise shows the sun circling the world crying 'protect' and seeking Murugan, the vel that tore through Sura's head and shoulders, and the lord who once explained to the great one dancing with demons and ghosts.","n":"Akam convention throughout: the moon that burns (கலைமதி அங்காயும் வெயில்), the sea's roar and slipping conch bangles (சங்கு ஆளும்) are the classical tormentors of a girl in love. What Murugan taught Siva in the last line (அறமும் மறம் தோயும் அறிவு நிரம்பு) is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","melivu","maalai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:241"},"p:1088":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1088,"t":["குருதிசலந் தோலுங் குடலுடனென் பாலுங்","குலவியெழுங் கோலந் ...... தனில்மாயக்","கொடுமையுடன் கோபங் கடுவிரகஞ் சேருங்","குணவுயிர்கொண் டேகும் ...... படிகாலன்","கருதிநெடும் பாசங் கொடுவரநின் றாயுங்","கதறமறந் தேனென் ...... றகலாமுன்","கமலமலர்ந் தேறுங் குகனெனவும் போதுன்","கருணைமகிழ்ந் தோதுங் ...... கலைதாராய்","நிருதர்தளஞ் சூழும் பெரியநெடுஞ் சூரன்","நினைவுமழிந் தோடும் ...... படிவேலால்","நிகரிலதம் பாரொன் றிமையவர்நெஞ் சால்நின்","நிலைதொழநின் றேமுன் ...... பொருவீரா","பருதியுடன் சோமன் படியையிடந் தானும்","பரவவிடந் தானுண் ...... டெழுபாரும்","பயமறநின் றாடும் பரமருளங் கூரும்","பழமறையன் றோதும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet sees the body as blood, water, skin, gut and bone, holding a life full of illusion, cruelty, anger and harsh lust, and asks that before Death comes with his long noose to take it and his mother cries out, he be given the art of joyfully chanting Murugan's grace, calling him Guha who rose from the lotus. The praise names the hero whose vel left great Sura's mind ruined and fleeing, and the one who once recited the ancient Veda that gladdened the Supreme who drank the poison and dances, praised by sun, moon and the one who dug up the earth.","n":"பழமறை அன்று ஓதும்: Murugan reciting the ancient Veda to Siva, the teaching story, without Om being named. The one who dug the earth (படியை இடந்தான்) is Vishnu as the boar.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","yaakkai","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:241"},"p:1089":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1089,"t":["இருவினைக ளீட்டு மிழவுபடு கூட்டை","யெடுமெடென வீட்டி ...... லனைவோரும்","இறுதியிடு காட்டி லழுதுதலை மாட்டில்","எரியஎரிமூட்டி ...... யிடுமாறு","கரியஇரு கோட்டு முரணெருமை மோட்டர்","கயிறிறுக மாட்டி ...... யழையாமுன்","கனகமணி வாட்டு மருவுகழல் பூட்டு","கழலிணைகள் காட்டி ...... யருள்வாயே","பருவமலை நாட்டு மருவுகிளி யோட்டு","பழையகுற வாட்டி ...... மணவாளா","பகைஞர்படை வீட்டில் முதியகன லு஡ட்டு","பகருநுதல் நாட்ட ...... குமரேசா","அருமறைகள் கூட்டி யுரைசெய்தமிழ் பாட்டை","அடைவடைவு கேட்ட ...... முருகோனே","அலைகடலி லீட்ட அவுணர்தமை யோட்டி","அமரர்சிறை மீட்ட ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"Before everyone at home says 'take it, take it' of the body that heaps up both kinds of deeds and is ruined, weeps at the burning ground and lights the fire at its head, and before the riders of the dark, twin-horned buffalo tie their rope tight and call him, the poet asks Murugan to show him his pair of anklet-bound feet. The praise names him bridegroom of the hunter girl who drove off parrots, Kumara of the forehead eye that set old fire to the enemies' homes, the Murugan who listened in order to the Tamil song that set forth the Vedas, and the one who freed the gods.","n":"அருமறைகள் கூட்டி உரைசெய் தமிழ்ப் பாட்டை அடைவடைவு கேட்ட: Murugan as the listener to Tamil song that carries the Vedas, perhaps the hymns of the Saiva saints, though none is named. The forehead eye burning the enemies' homes is Siva's burning of the three cities; பகருநுதல் நாட்ட makes Murugan its son.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["kootru","thiruvadi","thamizh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:241"},"p:1090":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1090,"t":["உ றவின்முறை யோர்க்கு முறுதுயரம் வாய்த்து","உ ளமுருகு தீர்த்தி ...... வுடலு஡டே","உ டலை முடிவாக்கு நெடியதொரு காட்டில்","உ யர்கனலை மூட்டி ...... விடஆவி","மறலிமற மார்த்த கயிறுதனை வீக்கி","வலிவினொடு தாக்கி ...... வளையாமுன்","மனமுமுனி வேட்கை மிகவு முன தாட்கள்","மகிழ்வியல்கொ டேத்த ...... மதிதாராய்","பிறைநுதலி சேற்க ணமையரிவை வேட்பு","வரையில்மற வோ஡க்கு ...... மகவாகப்","பிறிதுருவில் வாய்த்து நிறைதினைகள் காத்த","பிடியினடி போற்று ...... மணவாளா","அறுகுபிறை யாத்தி அலைசலமு மார்த்த","அடர்சடையி னார்க்கு ...... மறிவீவாய்","அடரவரு போர்க்கை அசுரர்கிளை மாய்த்து","அமரர்சிறை மீட்ட ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"Before his kin are struck with grief, the body is laid on a high fire in the long forest of the dead, and Death binds his rope and strikes to bind the life, the poet asks for the mind to praise Murugan's feet with joy and eager longing. The praise names him the bridegroom who honours the feet of the girl with crescent brow and carp eyes, born in another form as child to the hunters to guard the millet, the one who gave knowledge to Siva of the matted hair with arugu, crescent, aththi and the Ganges, and the one who freed the gods.","n":"பிடியின் அடி போற்று மணவாளா: Murugan himself praises the feet of Valli, the she-elephant, reversing the poet's plea for Murugan's feet. பிறிது உருவில் வாய்த்து: Valli as a goddess born in another form among the hunters. அறிவீவாய்: the teaching of Siva.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kootru","thiruvadi","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:241"},"p:1091":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1091,"t":["அளகநிரை குலையவிழி குவியவளை கலகலென","அமுதமொழி பதறியெழ ...... அணியாரம்","அழகொழுகு புளகமுலை குழையஇடை துவளமிக","அமுதநிலை யதுபரவ ...... அதிமோகம்","உ ளமுருக வருகலவி தருமகளிர் கொடுமையெனு","முறுகபட மதனில்மதி ...... யழியாதே","உ லகடைய மயிலின்மிசை நொடியளவில் வலம்வருமு","னுபயநறு மலரடியை ...... அருள்வாயே","வளையமலை கடல்சுவற விடுபகழி வரதனிரு","மருதினொடு பொருதருளு ...... மபிராமன்","வரியரவின் மிசைதுயிலும் வரதஜய மகள்கொழுநன்","மருகஅமர் முடுகிவரு ...... நிருதேசர்","தளமுறிய வரைதகர அசுரர்பதி தலைசிதற","தகனமெழ முடுகவிடு ...... வடிவேலா","தரளமணி வடமிலகு குறவர்திரு மகள்கணவ","சகலகலை முழுதும்வல ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"Describing the union that women give, hair scattered, eyes closing, bangles jingling, sweet speech trembling and breasts pressed, the poet asks not to lose his mind in the cruel deceit of such women but to be given Murugan's two fragrant feet, the god who circled the world on his peacock in an instant. The praise names Rama whose arrow dried the sea, Krishna who fought the twin marutha trees, Hari who sleeps on the serpent with Lakshmi, Murugan's vel that scattered the demons, and the husband of the hunter girl, master of all arts.","n":"கலவி தரு மகளிர் கொடுமை எனும் உறு கபடம்: the scene ends in a judgment of deceit. The world-circling ride on the peacock is the race for the fruit, alluded to in passing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","varaivinmagalir","vanjam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:241"},"p:1092":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1092,"t":["அனகனென அதிகனென அமலனென அசலனென","அபயனென அதுலனென ...... அநபாயன்","அடல்மதன னெனவிசைய னெனமுருக னெனநெருடி","யவர்பெயரு மிடைசெருகி ...... யிசைபாடி","வனசமணி பணிலமழை சுரபிசுரர் தருநிகர்கை","மகிபஎன தினையளவு ...... ளவுமீயா","மனிதர்கடை தொறுமுழலு மிடியொழிய மொழியொழிய","மனமொழிய வொருபொருளை ...... அருள்வாயே","இனனிலவு தலைமலைய அடியினுகி ரிலைகளென","இருசதுர திசையிலுர ...... கமும்வீழ","இரணியச யிலம்ரசித சயிலமர கதசயில","மெனவிமலை யமுனை யென ...... நிழல்வீசிக்","ககனமழை யுகைகடவு ளுடலமென முதியவிழி","கதுவியெழில் பொதியமிசை ...... படர்கோலக்","கலபகக மயில்கடவு நிருதர்கஜ ரததுரக","கடகமுட னமர்பொருத ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses singing to patrons, stuffing their names among titles like sinless, peerless, pure, steadfast, refuge-giver, Kama, Vijaya and Murugan, and telling each that his hand rivals the lotus, gem, conch, rain cloud, wish-cow and wish-tree, while they would not give a millet's worth; he asks for an end to the poverty of wandering from door to door, and for the one Thing where speech and mind cease. The praise describes the peacock, shining like gold, silver and emerald mountains, its shadow cast like the Vimala and Yamuna, spreading over Pothigai, and Murugan riding it against the demons' elephants, chariots, horses and armies.","n":"The poet's confession is of a court poet: the names of the god (முருகன் among them) wasted on men. மொழி ஒழிய மனம் ஒழிய ஒரு பொருள்: the remedy for begging is a silence where speech and mind end. The peacock lines are hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iravu","inmai","mounam","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:241"},"p:1093":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1093,"t":["குடருமல சலமுமிடை யிடைதடியு முடையளவு","கொழுவுமுதி ரமும்வெளிற ...... ளறுமாகக்","கொளகொளென அளவில்புழு நெளுநெளென விளைகுருதி","குமுகுமென இடைவழியில் ...... வரநாறும்","உ டலில்மண மலிபுழுகு தடவியணி கலமிலக","வுலகமரு ளுறவரும ...... ரிவையாரன்","பொழியவினை யொழியமன மொழியஇரு ளொழியஎன","தொழிவிலக லறிவையருள் ...... புரிவாயே","வடகனக சயிலமுத லியசயில மெனநெடிய","வடிவுகொளு நெடியவிறல் ...... மருவாரை","வகிருமொரு திகிரியென மதிமுதிய பணிலமென","மகரசல நிதிமுழுகி ...... விளையாடிக்","கடலுலகை யளவுசெய வளருமுகி லெனஅகில","ககனமுக டுறநிமிரு ...... முழுநீலக்","கலபகக மயில்கடவி நிருதர்கஜ ரததுரக","கடகமுட னமர்பொருத ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes the body of gut, faeces and urine, flesh, fat, blood and pale mire, with countless worms writhing and blood oozing until it stinks, which women perfume with civet and adorn with shining jewels as they come to delude the world, and asks for the understanding in which love pours, deeds end, mind ends and darkness ends. The praise likens the peacock to a mountain, to the discus that rends enemies, to the conch, and to the cloud that grew to measure the sea-girt world, and closes with Murugan riding it against the demons' armies.","n":"The peacock is described with the emblems of Vishnu, discus, conch and the cloud that grows to measure the world (Trivikrama), so the mount takes on the uncle's form. The plea is a chain of endings (வினை ஒழிய மனம் ஒழிய இருள் ஒழிய).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","kaamam","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:241"},"p:1094":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1094,"t":["குதறுமுனை யறிவுகொடு பதறியெதிர் கதறிமிகு","குமுதமிடு பரசமய ...... மொருகோடி","குருடர்தெரிவரியதொரு பொருள்தெரிய நிகழ்மனது","கொடியஇரு வினையெனும ...... ளறுபோக","உ தறிவித றியகரண மரணமற விரணமற","வுருகியுரை பருகியநு ...... தினஞான","உ ணர்வுவிழி பெறவுனது மிருகமத நளினபத","யுகளமினி யுணரஅருள் ...... புரிவாயே","சிதறவெளி முழுதுமொளி திகழுமுடு படலமவை","சிறுபொறிக ளெனவுரக ...... பிலமேழுஞ்","செகதலமு நிகர்சிகரிபலவுநல கெசபுயக","திசையுமுட னுருகவரு ...... கடைநாளிற்","கதறுமெழு கடல்பருகி வடவைவிடு கரியபுகை","யெனமுடிவில் ககனமுக ...... டதிலோடுங்","கலபகக மயில்கடவி நிருதர்கஜ ரததுரக","கடகமுட னமர்பொருத ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet sets against a crore of rival faiths, clamouring with sharp arguments, blind to the one Thing, his plea that the mire of the two deeds go, the instruments of the mind be shaken off, death and wound end, and melting and drinking the word he gain daily the sight of wisdom to know Murugan's musk-fragrant lotus feet. The praise shows the peacock at the end of time, when the stars scatter like sparks and the seven nether worlds, the mountains and the elephants of the quarters melt, running to the sky's summit like the black smoke of the submarine fire that drinks the seas.","n":"பரசமயம் ஒரு கோடி குருடர் தெரிவரியது ஒரு பொருள்: the sects are blind to the One, as in 1078, where the poet has left them. The praise sets the peacock in the cosmic dissolution, as 1086 does for Murugan.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["samayam","gnaanam","vinai","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:241"},"p:1095":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1095,"t":["வதைபழக மறலிவிறல் மதனன்வழி படுதுமென","வயிரமர கதமகர ...... மளவாக","வரிசிதறி விடமளவி வளருமிரு கலகவிழி","வளையிளைஞ ருயிர்கவர ...... வருமாய","இதையமள விடஅரிய அரிவையர்கள் நெறியொழுகி","எழுபிறவி நெறியொழிய ...... வழிகாணா","இடர்கள்படு குருடனெனை அடிமைகொள மகிழ்வொடுன","திருநயன கருணைசிறி ...... தருள்வாயே","பதயுகள மலர்தொழுது பழுதில்பொரிஅவல்துவரை","பயறுபெரு வயிறுநிறை ...... யவிடாமுப்","பழமுமினி துதவிமுனி பகரவட சிகரிமிசை","பரியதனி யெயிறுகொடு ...... குருநாடார்","கதைமுழுது மெழுதுமொரு களிறுபிளி றிடநெடிய","கடலுலகு நொடியில்வரு ...... மதிவேகக்","கலபகக மயில்கடவி நிருதர்கஜ ரததுரக","கடகமுட னமர்பொருத ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet tells of women whose diamond and emerald-adorned eyes, as if Kama and Death had joined to worship slaughter, carry poison to take young men's lives, and of how following their unfathomable ways he found no path out of the seven births; he asks Murugan to grant a little of his eyes' grace to take this suffering blind man as his servant. The praise tells of Ganesha, whose belly is filled with puffed rice, aval, lentils and the three fruits offered at his feet, and who wrote the whole story of the Kurus with his one tusk on the northern peak as the sage spoke, and of Murugan's peacock that circled the sea-girt world in a moment.","n":"The praise turns to Ganesha as the scribe of the Mahabharata (குருநாடர் கதை முழுதும் எழுதும் ஒரு களிறு) before naming Murugan's swift peacock, perhaps the race for the fruit between the brothers. மறலி விறல் மதனன்: Death and Kama joined in the women's eyes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","piravi","thondu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:241"},"p:1096":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1096,"t":["விடமளவி யரிபரவு விழிகுவிய மொழிபதற","விதறிவளை கலகலென ...... அநுராகம்","விளையம்ருக மதமகுள முலைபுளக மெழநுதலில்","வியர்வுவர அணிசிதற ...... மதுமாலை","அடரளக மவிழஅணி துகிலகல அமுதுபொதி","யிதழ்பருகி யுருகியரி...... வையரோடே","அமளிமிசை யமளிபட விரகசல தியில்முழுகி","யவசமுறு கினுமடிகள் ...... மறவேனே","உ டலுமுய லகன்முதுகு நெறுநெறென எழுதிமிர","வுரகர்பில முடியவொரு ...... பதமோடி","உ ருவமுது ககனமுக டிடியமதி முடிபெயர","வுயரவகி லபுவனம ...... திரவீசிக்","கடககர தலமிலக நடனமிடு மிறைவர்மகிழ்","கருதரிய விதமொடழ ...... குடனாடுங்","கலபகக மயில்கடவி நிருதர்கஜ ரததுரக","கடகமுட னமர்பொருத ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes lovemaking in full, the women's eyes closing, speech trembling, bangles jingling, sweat on the brow, the garland scattered and hair loosened, drinking their lips and plunging into the sea of passion on the bed, and vows that even then he will not forget Murugan's feet. The praise shows Siva's dance, the back of Muyalakan cracking, the nether worlds struck as one foot rises, the moon shaken from his crown and all worlds reeling, and Murugan dancing beautifully in ways that delight that Lord, before the shared close of the peacock against the demons.","n":"Unusually, the plea is not to be saved from women but a vow: அவசமுறுகினும் அடிகள் மறவேனே, 'even when I am lost in passion I will not forget your feet'. Whether the dancer delighting Siva at the end is Murugan or the peacock is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","thiruvadi","thirukkoothu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:241"},"p:1097":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1097,"t":["எழுபிறவி நீர்நிலத்தி லிருவினைகள் வேர்பிடித்து","இடர்முளைக ளேமுளைத்து ...... வளர்மாயை","எனுமுலவை யேயணைத்து விரககுழை யேகுழைத்து","இருளிலைக ளேதழைத்து ...... மிகநீளும்","இழவுநனை யேபிடித்து மரணபழ மேபழுத்து","இடியுமுடல் மாமரத்தி ...... னருநீழல்","இசையில்விழ ஆதபத்தி யழியுமுன மேயெனக்கு","இனியதொரு போதகத்தை ...... யருள்வாயே","வழுவுநெறி பேசுதக்க னிசையுமக சாலையுற்ற","மதியிரவி தேவர்வஜ்ர ...... படையாளி","மலர்கமல யோனிசக்ர வளைமருவு பாணிவிக்ர","மறையஎதிர் வீரவுக்ரர் ...... புதல்வோனே","அழகியக லாபகற்றை விகடமயி லேறியெட்டு","அசலமிசை வாகையிட்டு ...... வரும்வேலா","அடலசுரர் சேனைகெட்டு முறியமிக மோதிவெட்டி","அமரர்சிறை மீளவிட்ட ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The body is pictured as a great tree: rooted by the two kinds of deed in the soil and water of seven births, sprouting troubles, spreading maya as branches, desire as tender shoots and darkness as leaves, budding with loss and ripening the fruit of death; before its shade falls and it perishes in the heat, the poet asks Murugan for sweet teaching. The praise names him son of the fierce hero who struck down the moon, sun, gods, Indra, Brahma and Vishnu gathered at Daksha's sacrifice, rider of the peacock over the eight mountains who freed the gods from prison.","n":"The tree is worked out stage by stage (வேர், முளை, உலவை, குழை, இலை, நனை, பழம்), so that each phase of growth is a phase of ruin: the buds are loss (இழவு) and the ripe fruit is death (மரணம்). போதகம், teaching, is what the poet asks for here and in 1099. The Daksha lines name the gods at the sacrifice and an எதிர் வீர உக்ரர் who struck them, Siva in his fierce form.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","vinai","piravi","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:242"},"p:1098":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1098,"t":["நடையுடையி லேயருக்கி நெடியதெரு வீதியிற்குள்","நயனமத னால்மருட்டி ...... வருவாரை","நணுகிமய லேவிளைத்து முலையைவிலை கூறிவிற்று","லளிதமுட னேபசப்பி ...... யுறவாடி","வடிவதிக வீடுபுக்கு மலரணையின் மீதிருத்தி","மதனனுடை யாகமத்தி ...... னடைவாக","மருவியுள மேயுருக்கி நிதியமுள தேபறிக்கும்","வனிதையர்க ளாசைபற்றி ...... யுழல்வேனோ","இடையர்மனை தோறுநித்த முறிதயிர்நெய் பால்குடிக்க","இருகையுற வேபிடித்து ...... உ ரலோடே","இறுகிடஅ சோதைகட்ட அழுதிடுகொ பாலக்ருஷ்ண","னியல்மருக னேகுறத்தி ...... மணவாளா","அடலெழுது மேடுமெத்த வருபுனலி லேறவிட்டு","அரியதமிழ் வாதுவெற்றி ...... கொளும்வேலா","அவுணர்குலம் வேரறுத்து அபயமென வோலமிட்ட","அமரர்சிறை மீளவிட்ட ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women who dress up and preen on the long street, beguile passers-by with their eyes, name a price for their breasts, coax them into their fine houses and onto flowered beds, and after love by Kama's rules melt the heart and take whatever wealth there is, and asks whether he must wander bound by desire for them. The praise names Murugan nephew of Krishna, who drank curd and ghee in the cowherds' homes and cried when Yasoda bound him to the mortar, bridegroom of the hunter girl, and the vel who won the Tamil debate by setting the palm leaf afloat against the flood.","n":"முலையை விலை கூறி விற்று and நிதியம் உளதே பறிக்கும் name the sale and the taking of money outright. மதனனுடை ஆகமம், 'Kama's scripture', makes the art of love a rival rule. The palm leaf sent up the river (ஏடு ... வருபுனலில் ஏறவிட்டு) in a Tamil debate is the story of Sambandar against the Jains at Madurai, and the song credits it to Murugan.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","mayal","thamizh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:242"},"p:1099":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1099,"t":["மடலவிழ்ச ரோருகத்து முகிழ்நகையி லேவளைத்து","மதசுகப்ர தாபசித்ர ...... முலையாலே","மலரமளி மீதணைத்து விளையுமமு தாதரத்தை","மனமகிழ் வேயளித்து ...... மறவாதே","உ டலுயிர தாயிருக்க உ னதெனதெ னாமறிக்கை","ஒருபொழுதொ ணாதுசற்று ...... மெனவேதான்","உ ரைசெய்மட வாரளித்த கலவிதரு தோதகத்தை","யொழியவொரு போதகத்தை ...... யருள்வாயே","தடமகுட நாகரத்ந படநெளிய ஆடுபத்ம","சரணயுக மாயனுக்கு ...... மருகோனே","சரவணமி லேயுதித்த குமரமுரு கேசக்ர","சயிலம்வல மாய்நடத்து ...... மயில்வீரா","அடல்மருவு வேல்கரத்தி லழகுபெற வேயிருத்தும்","அறுமுகவ ஞானதத்வ ...... நெறிவாழ்வே","அசுரர்குல வேரைவெட்டி அபயமென வோலமிட்ட","அமரர்சிறை மீளவிட்ட ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet tells how women with lotus faces and budding smiles embrace a man on the bed, give him their lips and say 'don't forget me; while body and life are one there is no yours and mine, I cannot bear a moment apart', and asks for teaching that will end the deceit of that union. The praise names Murugan nephew of the Mayan whose feet danced on the serpent's crowned, jewelled hoods, born in the Saravana pool, the peacock hero who circles the world-mountain, the six-faced one who is the life of the path of knowledge.","n":"The deceit is in the women's words of total union (உடல் உயிரதாய் இருக்க உனது எனது எனாமறிக்கை), which the poet quotes; தோதகம், fraud, is set against போதகம், teaching, in a rhyme. The dance on the serpent's hoods is Krishna's on Kaliya, told without naming him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","vanjam","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:242"},"p:1100":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1100,"t":["அங்கதன் கண்டகன் பங்கிலன் பொங்குநெஞ்","சன்பிலன் துன்பவன் ...... புகழ்வாரா","அஞ்சொடுங் கும்பொதும் பொன்றையென் றுஞ்சுமந்","தங்குமிங் குந்திரிந் ...... திரைதேடுஞ்","சங்கடங் கொண்டவெஞ் சண்டிபண் டன்பெருஞ்","சஞ்சலன் கிஞ்சுகந் ...... தருவாயார்","தந்தொழும் பன்தழும் பன்பணிந் தென்றுநின்","தண்டையம் பங்கயம் ...... புகழ்வேனோ","கங்கையும் பொங்குநஞ் சம்பொருந் தும்புயங்","கங்களுங் திங்களுங் ...... கழுநீருங்","கஞ்சமுந் தும்பையுங் கொன்றையுஞ் சந்ததங்","கந்தமுந் துன்றுசெஞ் ...... சடையாளர்","பங்குதங் கும்பசுங் கொம்புதந் தின்புறும்","பந்தவெங் குண்டர்தங் ...... குலகாலா","பண்டிதன் கந்தனென் றண்டரண் டந்தொழும்","பண்புநண் பும்பெறும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet calls himself a wicked thorn without merit, with a seething, loveless heart, who never comes to praise, carries the hollow where the five senses lurk, wanders seeking food, a stubborn, troubled wretch who is servant to women with kinsuka-red mouths, and asks when he will bow and praise Murugan's lotus feet with their anklets. The praise lists the Ganga, snakes, moon, lotus, thumbai and konrai in Siva's red matted hair, the green creeper at his side who bore Murugan, and names him the death of the clan of the cruel Jains, whom the worlds worship as the learned Kanda.","n":"The first half is almost all self-abuse, a run of nouns ending in -அன் (கண்டகன், பங்கிலன், அன்பிலன், சஞ்சலன், தொழும்பன், தழும்பன்). குண்டர் is the usual word for the Jains, so குலகாலா again points to Sambandar's victory credited to Murugan. Several words (அங்கதன், பண்டன், பொதும்பு) depend on how the syllables split.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kutram","kaamam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:242"},"p:1101":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1101,"t":["தந்தமுந் துன்பவெஞ் சிந்தைகொண் டந்தகன்","தண்டவொன் றன்றொடுங் ...... கிடுமாவி","தஞ்சமென் றும்பரிந் தின்சொல்வஞ் சந்தெரிந்","தன்றுமென் றுந்தனந் ...... தனைநாடி","நின்தனன் பென்பதொன் றின்றிநன் றென்றுநெஞ்","சின்கணன் பொன்றில்மங் ...... கையர்நேசம்","நின்றளந் துஞ்சளங் கொண்டிடும் புன்கணந்","தின்பமொன் றின்றியிங் ...... குழல்வேனோ","சுந்தரன் பந்தமுஞ் சிந்தவந் தன்புடன்","தொண்டனென் றன்றுகொண் ...... டிடுமாதி","தும்பைசெம் பொன்சொரிந் துந்தருங் கொன்றைதுன்","பங்கடிந் தென்பொடுந் ...... தொலையாநீர்","அந்தமுந் திந்துவுங் கெந்தமிஞ் சுங்கொழுந்","தன்றுமின் றும்புனைந் ...... திடும்வேணி","அன்பர்நெஞ் சின்புறுஞ் செஞ்சொலன் கந்தனென்","றண்டரண் டந்தொழும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of the life that shrinks on the day Death punishes, and of women who, seeking only money, offer refuge with sweet words while knowing their own deceit, measure their love and deal falsely, and asks whether he must wander joyless, with no love for Murugan, in desire for them. The praise names Siva, who came with love to break Sundarar's bonds and claim him as his servant, who wears thumbai, konrai, bones, the Ganga and the moon in his hair, and Murugan the Kanda of fitting words who delights devotees' hearts.","n":"தனந்தனை நாடி and இன்சொல் வஞ்சம் make the women's motive money and their means sweet speech. சுந்தரன் பந்தமுஞ் சிந்த வந்து ... தொண்டன் என்று அன்று கொண்டிடும் is the story of Siva claiming Sundarar as his servant at his wedding, the model of being taken into service. The run of syllables in the first lines is hard to split.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","thondu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:242"},"p:1102":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1102,"t":["உ ம்பரா ரமுதெனுந் தொண்டைவா யமுதமுண்","டுண்டுமே கலைகழன் ...... றயலாக","உ ந்திவா வியில்விழுந் தின்பமா முழுகியன்","பொன்றிலா ரொடுதுவண் ...... டணைமீதே","செம்பொனார் குடமெனுங் கொங்கையா பரணமுஞ்","சிந்தவாள் விழிசிவந் ...... தமராடத்","திங்கள்வேர் வுறவணைந் தின்பவா ரியில்விழுஞ்","சிந்தையே னெனவிதங் ...... கரைசேர்வேன்","கொம்புநா லுடையவெண் கம்பமால் கிரிவருங்","கொண்டல்ப்லோ மசையள்சங்க் ...... ரமபாரக்","கும்பமால் வரைபொருந் திந்த்ரபூ பதிதருங்","கொண்டலா னையைமணஞ் ...... செயும்வீரா","அம்புரா சியுநெடுங் குன்றுமா மரமுமன்","றஞ்சவா னவருறுஞ் ...... சிறைமீள","அங்கநான் மறைசொலும் பங்கயா சனமிருந்","தங்கைவே லுறவிடும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes lovemaking at length: drinking lips sweeter than the gods' nectar, the girdle loosed, falling into the pool of the navel, struggling on the bed with loveless women, breasts like golden pots, ornaments scattering, sword eyes reddening, sweat under the moon, and asks how a mind sunk in that sea of pleasure will ever reach the shore. The praise names Murugan the hero who married Devayanai, daughter given by Indra of the four-tusked white elephant and Puloma's daughter, and who sent the vel from his hand so that the sea, the mountain and the mango tree trembled and the gods were freed.","n":"The plea is a single question, எனவிதங் கரைசேர்வேன், 'how will I reach the shore', turning the sea of pleasure (இன்ப வாரி) into the sea of birth. அன்பொன்றிலாரொடு, 'with women who have no love', is the only judgement passed on the scene. The lines on the lotus-seated reciter of the Vedas are not clear to me.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:242"},"p:1103":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1103,"t":["வண்டுதான் மிகவிடங் கொண்டகா ரளகமென்","பந்திமா மலர்சொரிந் ...... துடைசோர","வம்புசேர் கனிபொருந் தின்பவா யமுதருந்","தந்தமா மதனலம் ...... விதமாக","விண்டுமே னிகள்துவண் டன்றில்போ லுளமிரண்","டொன்றுமா யுறவழிந் ...... தநுபோகம்","விஞ்சவே தருமிளங் கொங்கையார் வினைகடந்","துன்றன்மே லுருகஎன் ...... றருள்வாயே","பண்டுபா ரினையளந் துண்டமால் மருகசெம்","பைம்பொன்மா நகரிலிந் ...... திரன்வாழ்வு","பண்பெலா மிகுதிபொங் கின்பயா னையைமணந்","தன்பினோ ரகமமர்ந் ...... திடுவோனே","அண்டர்தா மதிபயங் கொண்டுவா டிடநெடுந்","தண்டுவாள் கொடுநடந் ...... திடுசூரன்","அங்கமா னதுபிளந் தெங்கும்வீ ரிடவெகுண்","டங்கைவே லுறவிடும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes union with young women, flowers falling from their bee-haunted hair, cloth slipping, lips drunk, bodies entwined like anril birds until two hearts become one, and asks when Murugan will let him pass beyond the deeds those women bind him in and melt for him instead. The praise names him nephew of Vishnu who measured and swallowed the earth, bridegroom of Devayanai of Indra's golden city, who dwells in the hearts of those who love him and split Sura's body with the vel.","n":"The plea asks not for the melting to stop but for it to change object: உன்றன் மேல் உருக, 'to melt for you', answers the lovers' melting (அழிந்து) in the first half. The anril pair, a stock image of inseparable lovers, here describes the union the poet would leave.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","anbu","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:242"},"p:1104":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1104,"t":["காதல்மோ கந்தருங் கோதைமார் கொங்கைசிங்","காரநா கஞ்செழுங் ...... கனிவாய்கண்","காளகூ டங்கொடுங் கால்ரு பம்பொருங்","காமபா ணஞ்சுரும் ...... பினம்வாழும்","ஓதிகார் செஞ்சொல்மென் பாகுதே னென்றயர்ந்","தோநமோ கந்தஎன் ...... றுரையாதே","ஊசலா டும்புலன் தாரியே சென்றுநின்","றோயுமா றொன்றையுங் ...... கருதாதோ","தாதகீ சண்பகம் பூகமார் கந்தமந்","தாரம்வா சந்திசந் ...... தனநீடு","சாமவே தண்டவெங் கோபகோ தண்டசந்","தானமா தெங்கள்பைம் ...... புனமேவும்","தீதிலா வஞ்சியஞ் சீதபா தம்படுஞ்","சேகரா தண்டையங் ...... கழல்பேணித்","தேவிபா கம்பொருந் தாதிநா தன்தொழுந்","தேசிகா வும்பர்தம் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet tells how he is undone by women whose breasts, red mouths, poison-dark eyes like Kama's arrows, dark hair and honeyed speech overwhelm him, and asks whether his senses, swinging to and fro, will never think of any way to go and rest without saying 'Om namo Kanda'. The praise names the fragrant trees of Valli's millet field, Murugan whose crown touched the cool feet of that faultless girl, and the teacher whom Siva with the goddess at his side worships.","n":"ஓநமோ கந்த என்று உரையாதே makes the failure a failure to say the mantra; ஊசலாடும் புலன், the swinging senses, is the image of the state. சீத பாதம் படுஞ் சேகரா has Murugan bowing his head to Valli's feet, the lover's humility, and தேவி பாகம் பொருந்து ஆதிநாதன் தொழுந் தேசிகா makes him Siva's preceptor. The list of trees in the praise is partly obscure to me.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","mayal","mandhiram","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:242"},"p:1105":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1105,"t":["கோலகா லத்தைவிட் டாசுபா டக்கொடிக்","கோவைபா டக்கொடிக் ...... கொடிவாதிற்","கோடிகூ ளக்கவிச் சேனைசா டக்கெடிக்","கூறுகா ளக்கவிப் ...... புலவோன்யான்","சீலகா லப்புயற் பாரிசா தத்தருத்","த்யாகமே ருப்பொருப் ...... பெனவோதுஞ்","சீதரா சித்ரவித் தாரமே செப்பிடக்","கேளெனா நிற்பதைத் ...... தவிர்வேனோ","ஆலகா லப்பணிப் பாயல்நீ ளப்படுத்","தாரவா ரக்கடற் ...... கிடைசாயும்","ஆழிமா லுக்குநற் சாமவே தற்குமெட்","டாத்ரு பத்தினிற் ...... சுடராய","காலகா லப்ரபுச் சாலுமா லுற்றுமைக்","காகவே ளைப்புகக் ...... கழுநீராற்","காதும்வே ழச்சிலைப் பாரமீ னக்கொடிக்","காமவேள் மைத்துனப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet boasts that he is a poet who sings swift verse and kovai, crushes a crore of trash poets in debate, and asks whether he will ever give up standing before patrons, praising them as the rain cloud, the parijata tree and the Meru of giving, and saying 'listen' while he recites ornate and expansive verse. The praise names Vishnu on the serpent bed in the sea and Brahma, for whom Siva was a light beyond reach; Siva the death of Death; and Kama with his cane bow and fish flag who shot at Siva for Uma, whose cousin Murugan is.","n":"The poet names the kinds of verse he trades in (ஆசு, கோவை, சித்ர, வித்தாரம்), so his skill is the very thing he would give up. The patrons' praises (புயல், பாரிசாதத் தரு, த்யாக மேரு) are the stock hyperboles 1125 calls false. காமவேள் மைத்துனன: Kama is Vishnu's son and Murugan Vishnu's nephew.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","pugazh","agandhai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:242"},"p:1106":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1106,"t":["ஞாலமோ டொப்பமக் காளெனா நற்சொலைத்","தீதெனா நற்றவத் ...... தணைவோர்தம்","நாதமோ டுட்கருத் தோடவே தர்க்கமிட்","டோ யுநா யொப்பவர்க் ...... கிளையாதே","நீலமே னிக்குலத் தோகைமே லுற்றுநிட்","டூரசூர் கெட்டுகப் ...... பொரும்வேலா","நேசமாய் நித்தநிற் றாளைநீ ளச்சமற்","றோதநீ திப்பொருட் ...... டரவேணும்","கோலவா ரிக்கிடைக் கோபரா விற்படுத்","தானும்வே தக்குலத் ...... தயனாருங்","கூறும்வா னப்புவிக் கூறுதீ ரக்குறிப்","போதுறா நிற்பஅக் ...... கொடிதான","காலன்மார் புற்றுதைத் தானுமோர் கற்புடைக்","கோதைகா மக்கடற் ...... கிடைமுழ்கக்","காவிசேர் கொத்தலர்ப் பாணமேய் வித்தகக்","காமவேள் மைத்துனப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks not to be worn down by dog-like disputants who call good words evil and argue the Vedas against those who come to real austerity, and asks Murugan, who rode the blue peacock and fought cruel Sura, to give him the true meaning so that he may lovingly chant his feet every day without fear. The praise names Vishnu on the serpent in the sea and Brahma, Siva who kicked Death's chest, and Kama, Murugan's cousin, whose flower arrows sank the chaste goddess in a sea of love.","n":"வேதர்க்கமிட்டு ஓயும் நாய் ஒப்பவர் compares sectarian disputants to dogs. நீதிப் பொருள், the righteous meaning, is set against their argument, and அச்சமற்று makes freedom from fear the fruit. The first lines' syntax is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["samayam","thiruvadi","acham"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:242"},"p:1107":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1107,"t":["கரவுசேர் மகளிர்குங் குமபயோ தரதனங்","களினறா துயில்வதுஞ் ...... சரிபேசுங்","கரசரோ ருகநகம் படவிடாய் தணிவதுங்","கமலநா பியின்முயங் ...... கியவாழ்வும்","அரவுபோ லிடைபடிந் திரவெலா முழுகுமின்","பநல்மகோ ததிநலம் ...... பெறுமாறும்","அதரபா னமுதமுந் தவிரவே மவுனபஞ்","சரமனோ லயசுகந் ...... தருவாயே","பரவுமா யிரமுகங் கொடுதிசா முகதலம்","படர்பகீ ரதிவிதம் ...... பெறஆடல்","பயில்பணா வனமுகந் தகுணமா சுணகணம்","பனிநிலா வுமிழுமம் ...... புலிதாளி","குரவுகூ விளமரும் பிதழிதா தகிநெடுங்","குடிலவே ணியிலணிந் ...... தவராகங்","குழையஆ தரவுடன் தழுவுநா யகிதருங்","குமரனே யமரர்தம் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet lists the pleasures of concealing women, sleeping on their breasts, nail marks, the navel, the night's plunge into the great sea of delight and the nectar of their lips, and asks Murugan to end them all and give him the bliss of a mind dissolved in silence. The praise pictures Siva with the thousand-mouthed Ganga, hooded snakes, the moon, kura, vilva, konrai and other flowers in his long matted hair, and Murugan as the son given by the goddess who embraces him.","n":"மவுன ... மனோலய சுகம், the bliss of the mind's dissolution in silence, is a yogic goal set against the sea of sexual bliss (இன்ப ... மகோததி): one absorption is to replace the other. பஞ்சர between them is unclear to me (a cage, or Kama's five arrows).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","mounam","samaadhi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:242"},"p:1108":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1108,"t":["வடிவவேல் தனைவெகுண் டிளைஞரா வியைவளைந்","தமர்செய்வாள் விழியர்நெஞ் ...... சினில்மாயம்","வளரமால் தனைமிகுந் தவர்கள்போ லளவிவந்","தணுகுமா நிதிகவர்ந் ...... திடுமாதர்","துடியைநே ரிடைதனந் துவளவே துயில்பொருந்","தமளிதோய் பவர்வசஞ் ...... சுழலாதே","தொலைவிலா இயல்தெரிந் தவலமா னதுகடந்","துனதுதாள் தொழமனந் ...... தருவாயே","படியெலா முடியநின் றருளுமா லுதவுபங்","கயனுநான் மறையுமும் ...... பரும்வாழப்","பரவையூ டெழுவிடம் பருகிநீள் பவுரிகொண்","டலகையோ டெரிபயின் ...... றெருதேறிக்","கொடியவா ளரவிளம் பிறையினோ டலைசலங்","குவளைசேர் சடையர்தந் ...... திருமேனி","குழையஆ தரவுடன் தழுவுநா யகிதருங்","குமரனே யமரர்தம் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of women whose sword eyes, sharper than the vel, war on young men's lives, who pretend love with deceit in their hearts and take great wealth, and asks not to whirl in their power but, knowing the nature that has no end and passing beyond misery, to have a mind to worship Murugan's feet. The praise names Siva, who drank the poison from the sea so Vishnu, Brahma, the Vedas and the gods might live, danced with ghouls in the fire, rode the bull and wears the snake, crescent and river, and Murugan the son of the goddess who embraces him.","n":"அளவி வந்து அணுகு மாநிதி கவர்ந்திடு மாதர: love is a pretence to take money. The last two lines repeat 1107's, so the two songs close as a pair.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:242"},"p:1109":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1109,"t":["கட்டமுறு நோய்தீமை யிட்டகுடில் மாமாய","கட்டுவிடு மோர்கால ...... மளவாவே","கத்தவுற வோர்பாலர் தத்தைசெறி வார்வாழ்வு","கற்புநெறி தான்மாய ...... வுயர்காலன்","இட்டவொரு தூதாளு முட்டவினை யால்மூடி","யிட்டவிதி யோயாவி ...... யிழவாமுன்","எத்தியுனை நாடோ று முத்தமிழி னாலோத","இட்டமினி தோடார ...... நினைவாயே","துட்டரென ஏழ்பாரு முட்டவினை யாள்சூரர்","தொக்கில்நெடு மாமார்பு ...... தொளையாகத்","தொட்டவடி வேல்வீர நட்டமிடு வார்பால","சுத்ததமி ழார்ஞான ...... முருகோனே","மட்டுமரை நால்வேத னிட்டமலர் போல்மேவ","மத்தமயில் மீதேறி ...... வருநாளை","வைத்தநிதி போல்நாடி நித்தமடி யார்வாழ","வைத்தபடி மாறாத ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet pictures the hut of the body full of disease and evil, children crying, wife and household, until Death's messenger comes and fate closes over his deeds, and asks that before his life is lost Murugan think of him with love so that he may praise him every day in the three Tamils. The praise names the vel that pierced the chests of the wicked Suras, the child of the dancer, Muruga of pure Tamil knowledge, coming on the peacock, who keeps his devotees like stored treasure and never changes.","n":"முத்தமிழினால் ஓத makes the praise he wants to sing Tamil praise, and the epithet சுத்த தமிழார் ஞான முருகோனே joins Tamil and knowledge in Murugan. வைத்த நிதி போல், 'like a hoarded treasure', is the image of how he keeps devotees, shared with 1110. Some lines (on the household, and the flower Brahma placed) are hard to construe.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kootru","yaakkai","thamizh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:242"},"p:1110":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1110,"t":["பக்கமுற நேரான மக்களுட னேமாதர்","பத்தியுடன் மேல்மூடி ...... யினிதான","பட்டினுட னேமாலை யிட்டுநெடி தோர்பாடை","பற்றியணை வோர்கூவி ...... யலைநீரிற்","புக்குமுழு காநீடு துக்கமது போய்வேறு","பொற்றியிட வேயாவி ...... பிரியாமுன்","பொற்கழலை நாடோ று முட்பரிவி னாலோது","புத்திநெடி தாம்வாழ்வு ...... புரிவாயே","இக்கனுக வேநாடு முக்கணர்ம காதேவர்","எப்பொருளு மாமீசர் ...... பெருவாழ்வே","எட்டவரிதோர்வேலை வற்றமுது சூர்மாள","எட்டியெதி ரேயேறு ...... மிகல்வேலா","மக்களொடு வானாடர் திக்கில்முனி வோர்சூழ","மத்தமயில் மீதேறி ...... வருவோனே","வைத்தநிதி போல்நாடி நித்தமடி யார்வாழ","வைத்தபடி மாறாத ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet pictures his funeral: kin and children close by, women devoutly covering the body with silk and garlands, the bier lifted, the mourners calling and then plunging into the water to wash off their grief, the body burned; and asks that before his life departs Murugan give him a life of praising his golden feet every day with inner love. The praise names Murugan son of the three-eyed Mahadeva who is all things, the vel that dried the sea and killed Sura, who rides the peacock among men, gods and sages and keeps his devotees like stored treasure.","n":"The funeral is told from outside, as a sequence the dead man cannot see: silk, garland, bier, the plunge into water, and grief gone. The plea's timing, ஆவி பிரியாமுன், 'before life parts', is the book's common hour-of-death plea, here asking for a whole life of praise rather than rescue at the end.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saakkaadu","nilaiyaamai","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:242"},"p:1111":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1111,"t":["நீருநில மண்டாத தாமரைப டர்ந்தோடி","நீளமக லஞ்சோதி ...... வடிவான","நேசமல ரும்பூவை மாதின்மண மும்போல","நேர்மருவி யுண்காத ...... லுடன்மேவிச்","சூரியனு டன்சோம னீழலிவை யண்டாத","சோதிமரு வும்பூமி ...... யவையூடே","தோகைமயி லின்பாக னாமெனம கிழ்ந்தாட","சோதிஅயி லுந்தாரு ...... மருள்வாயே","வாரியகி லங்கூச ஆயிரப ணஞ்சேடன்","வாய்விடவொ டெண்பாலு ...... முடுபோல","வார்மணியு திர்ந்தோட வேகவினி றைந்தாட","மாமயில்வி டுஞ்சேவல் ...... கொடியோனே","ஆரியன வன்தாதை தேடியின மும்பாடு","மாடலரு ணஞ்சோதி ...... யருள்பாலா","ஆனைமுக வன்தேடி யோடியெய ணங்காத","லாசைமரு வுஞ்சோதி ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks for an inner vision: a lotus that neither water nor earth touches, spreading in length and breadth as a form of light, joined with love like a flower and its fragrance, in a ground of light that sun and moon do not reach, where he may dance for joy that Murugan is the peacock's rider; he asks for the shining vel and the garland. The praise names the peacock whose dance shakes the thousand-hooded serpent and scatters gems like stars, the cock banner, and Murugan the child of the light of Arunai that Vishnu and Brahma searched for and could not find.","n":"சூரியனுடன் சோமன் நீழல் இவை அண்டாத சோதி, a light that sun and moon do not touch, is the language of yogic inner sight (as in the Tirumanthiram), here joined to bhakti: the vision ends in dancing. அருணஞ்சோதி points to the fire-column at Annamalai; who searched in the last line (ஆனைமுகவன், the elephant-faced one?) is unclear, and the whole song's syntax is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oli","yogam","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:242"},"p:1112":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1112,"t":["சுட்டதுபோ லாசை விட்டுலகா சார","துக்கமிலா ஞான ...... சுகமேவிச்","சொற்கரணா தீத நிற்குண்மு டாடு","சுத்தநிரா தார ...... வெளிகாண","மொட்டலர்வா ஡ணச சக்ரசடா தார","முட்டவுமீ தேறி ...... மதிமீதாய்","முப்பதுமா றாறு முப்பதும்வே றான","முத்திரையா மோன ...... மடைவேனோ","எட்டவொணா வேத னத்தொடுகோ கோவெ","னப்பிரமா வோட ...... வரைசாய","எற்றியஏ ழாழி வற்றிடமா றாய","எத்தனையோ கோடி ...... யசுரேசர்","பட்டொருசூர் மாள விக்ரமவே லேவு","பத்திருதோள் வீர ...... தினைகாவல்","பத்தினிதோள் தோயு முத்தமம றாது","பத்திசெய்வா னாடர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether, dropping desire as if it were burnt, he will reach the bliss of knowledge free of the world's customs and sorrows, see the pure, supportless space beyond words and the inner instruments, climb through the blossoming lotus-wheels of the six centres to the moon above, and attain the sealed silence that is other than the thirty, the thirty-six and the thirty. The praise tells how Brahma ran crying, mountains toppled, the seven seas dried and crores of asura lords and Sura himself died by the vel, and names the twelve-shouldered hero who embraces the shoulders of the wife who guarded the millet.","n":"The plea is a full yogic programme in Tirumular's vocabulary: கரணாதீதம் (beyond the instruments), நிராதார வெளி (the supportless space), சடாதாரம் (the six centres), மதி (the moon at the crown), and முத்திரையா மோனம் (silence as the seal). முப்பதும் ஆறாறும் முப்பதும் counts the tattvas as the Tirumanthiram does (thirty, thirty and thirty-six) and places the goal beyond them. The corrupt word in line 5 is most likely வாரிச, lotus.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["aadhaaram","mounam","thaththuvam","muththirai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:243"},"p:1113":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1113,"t":["மைச்சுனமார் மாம னைச்சியுமா தாவு","மக்களுமா றாத ...... துயர்கூர","மட்டிலதோர் தீயி லிக்குடில்தான் வேவ","வைத்தவர்தா மேக ...... மதிமாய","நிச்சயமாய் நாளு மிட்டொருதூ தேவு","நெட்டளவாம் வாதை ...... யணுகாமுன்","நெக்குருகா ஞான முற்றுனதா ளோதி","நித்தலும்வாழ் மாறு ...... தருவாயே","நச்சணைமேல் வாழு மச்சுதனால் வேத","னற்றவர்தா நாட ...... விடையேறி","நற்புதல்வா சூரர் பட்டிடவே லேவு","நற்றுணைவா ஞால ...... மிகவாழப்","பச்செனுநீள் தோகை மெய்ப்பரியூர் பாக","பத்தியதா மாறு ...... முகநாளும்","பக்ஷமுமே லாய்ஷ டக்ஷரசூழ் பாத","பத்திசெய்வா னாடர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet pictures his kin, brother-in-law, uncle's daughter, mother and children, grieving without end as his body burns in the fire and those who set it there leave, and asks that before the fixed day comes and Death's messenger brings his torment, his heart may melt and he may gain knowledge and live chanting Murugan's feet. The praise names the gods Vishnu and Brahma who sought him, Siva on the bull, and Murugan with the long-plumed peacock for his horse, the six-faced one whose feet are ringed by the six-syllable mantra.","n":"நாளும் இட்டு ஒரு தூது ஏவு: Death fixes the day and sends a single messenger. பக்ஷமுமேலாய் ஷடக்ஷர சூழ் பாத, feet surrounded on both sides by the six syllables, points to the mantra சரவணபவ, though the line is not named.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","saakkaadu","thiruvadi","mandhiram"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:243"},"p:1114":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1114,"t":["தத்துவத் துச்செயலொ டொட்டில்பட் டக்குருகு","சத்துவிட் டப்படிபொ ...... லடியேனுஞ்","சச்சிலுற் றுப்படியில் விட்டுவிட் டுக்குளறி","சத்துவத் தைப்பிரிய ...... விடும்வேளை","சுத்தமத் தப்பதவி பெற்றநற் பத்தரொடு","தொக்குசற் றுக்கடையன் ...... மிடிதீரத்","துப்புமுத் துச்சரண பச்சைவெற் றிப்புரவி","சுற்றவிட் டுக்கடுகி ...... வரவேணும்","வித்தகத் திப்பவள தொப்பையப் பற்கிளைய","வெற்றிசத் திக்கரக ...... முருகோனே","வெற்புமெட் டுத்திசையும் வட்டமிட் டுச்சுழல","விட்டபச் சைச்சரண ...... மயில்வீரா","கத்தர்நெட் டுச்சடையர் முக்கணக் கக்கடவுள்","கச்சியப் பர்க்கருள்செய் ...... குருநாதா","கற்பதத் தைக்குருகி யுற்பதத் துக்குறவர்","கற்பினுக் குற்றுபுணர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet pictures himself, like a bird caught in the snare of the tattvas' workings, stammering as he falls to the ground and parts with life, and asks that at that time Murugan come quickly on the green victorious horse with his devotees who have reached that pure state, so that this lowly one's poverty may end. The praise names the younger brother of the wise elephant god with the coral belly, the peacock that circles the eight mountains and directions, the guru who gave grace to the three-eyed, matted-haired lord of Kanchi, and the bridegroom of the chaste hunter girl.","n":"The plea asks Murugan to come at death with his devotees (பத்தரொடு தொக்கு), so that the dying man joins their company. கச்சியப்பர்க்கு அருள்செய் குருநாதா seems to name Siva of Kanchi as the one taught; it might instead recall the Kandapuranam's poet Kachiyappar. The bird image (ஒட்டில்பட்ட குருகு) is uncertain in its first word.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["uyir","adiyaar","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:243"},"p:1115":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1115,"t":["மக்களொக் கற்றெரிவை பக்கமிக் கத்துணைவர்","மற்றுமுற் றக்குரவ ...... ரனைவோரும்","வைத்தசெப் பிற்பணமும் ரத்நமுத் திற்பணியு","மட்டுமற் றுப்பெருகு ...... மடியாரும்","புக்குதுக் தித்தெரிகள் தத்தவைக் கப்புகுது","பொய்க்குமெய்க் குச்செயலு ...... முருகாதே","புஷ்பமிட் டுக்கருணை நற்பதத் தைப்பரவு","புத்திமெத் தத்தருவ ...... தொருநாளே","செக்கர்கற் றைச்சடையில் மிக்ககொக் கிற்சிறகு","செக்கமுற் றச்சலமு ...... மதிசூடி","சித்தமுற் றுத்தெளிய மெத்தமெத் தத்திகழு","சித்தமுத் திச்சிவமு ...... மருள்வோனே","கொக்குறுப் புக்கொடுமை நிற்கும்வட் டத்தசுரை","கொத்தினொக் கக்கொலைசெய் ...... வடிவேலா","கொற்றவெற் றிப்பரிசை யொட்டியெட் டிச்சிறிது","குத்திவெட் டிப்பொருத ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet lists children, wife, brothers, elders, money in boxes, gems, pearls, ornaments and servants, all of whom grieve and leave at the pyre, and asks for the one day when, no longer melting for what is false, he may have the mind to strew flowers and praise Murugan's gracious feet. The praise names Siva who wears the crane's feather, the river and the moon in his red matted hair, and Murugan who gives clear siddhi, release and Sivam, and whose vel killed the asura of the mango tree.","n":"The list of kin and possessions is the Tirumanthiram's argument that none of them follows the dead. சித்த முத்திச் சிவமும் அருள்வோனே puts siddhi, mukti and Sivam among Murugan's gifts. The lines on what they do at the pyre are hard to construe.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","sutram","selvam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:243"},"p:1116":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1116,"t":["உ ற்பா தப்பூ தக்கா யத்தே","யொத்தோ டித்தத் ...... தியல்காலை","உ ட்பூ ரித்தே சற்றே சற்றே","யுக்கா ரித்தற் ...... புதனேரும்","அற்பா யிற்றாய் நிற்பா ரைப்போ","லப்பா வித்துத் ...... திரிவேனுக்","கப்பா சத்தா லெட்டா அப்பா","லைப்போ தத்தைப் ...... புரிவாயே","பொற்பார் பொற்பார் புத்தே ளிர்க்கா","கப்போய் முட்டிக் ...... கிரிசாடிப்","புக்கா ழிச்சூழ் கிட்டா கிச்சூர்","பொட்டா கக்குத் ...... தியவேலா","முற்பா டப்பா டற்றா ருக்கோர்","முட்கா டற்கப் ...... பொருளீவாய்","முத்தா முத்தீ யத்தா சுத்தா","முத்தா முத்திப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes the body of the five elements running its course, filling and belching, and himself wandering while pretending, like those who make a show of devotion, and asks for the knowledge beyond the reach of the bond. The praise names the vel that went for the gods, split the mountain and pierced Sura in the sea, and Murugan who gives the meaning to those without earlier merit, the pure, freed one, lord of release.","n":"பாசத்தால் எட்டா அப்பாலைப் போதம், the knowledge beyond what the bond can reach, is the plea in Siddhanta terms. அப்பாவித்துத் திரிவேன் seems to confess a pretended devotion. The clipped metre leaves most word splits open, and the reading is a sketch.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","paasam","koodaozhukkam","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:243"},"p:1117":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1117,"t":["எற்றா வற்றா மட்டா கத்தீ","யிற்காய் செக்கட் ...... பிறைவாளே","யிற்றார் கைப்பா சத்தே கட்டா","டிக்கோ பித்துக் ...... கொடுபோமுன்","உ ற்றார் பெற்றார் சுற்றா நிற்பா","ரொட்டோ ம் விட்டுக் ...... கழியீரென்","றுற்றோ துற்றே பற்றா நிற்பா","ரக்கா லத்துக் ...... குறவார்தான்","பற்றார் மற்றா டைக்கே குத்தா","பற்றா னப்பிற் ...... களைவோனே","பச்சே னற்கா னத்தே நிற்பாள்","பொற்பா தத்திற் ...... பணிவோனே","முற்றா வற்றா மெய்ப்போ தத்தே","யுற்றார் சித்தத் ...... துறைவோனே","முத்தா முத்தீ யத்தா சுத்தா","முத்தா முத்திப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet pictures Yama's men with red, fiery eyes and crescent swords binding him with the noose and dragging him off in anger, while kin and parents stand round saying 'we won't keep you, go', and asks who will be his kin at that time. The praise names Murugan who removes clinging attachments, who bowed at the golden feet of the girl in the green millet field, and who dwells in the minds of those who hold to true knowledge.","n":"அக்காலத்துக்கு உறவு ஆர்தான், 'who then is kin?', is the whole plea; the praise answers it by naming Murugan as the remover of attachments (பற்று ... களைவோனே). The kin's words, ஒட்டோம் விட்டுக் கழியீர், as I read them, turn the dead out of the house.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","sutram","pattru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:243"},"p:1118":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1118,"t":["செட்டா கத்தே னைப்போ லச்சீ","ரைத்தே டித்திட் ...... பமதாகத்","திக்கா மற்பா டுற்றா ரிற்சீ","ருற்றா ருக்குச் ...... சிலபாடல்","பெட்டா கக்கூ றிப்போ தத்தா","ரைப்போல் வப்புற் ...... றுழலாதே","பெற்றா ரிற்சார் வுற்றாய் நற்றாள்","சற்றோ தப்பெற் ...... றிடுவேனோ","எட்டா நெட்டா கத்தோ கைக்கே","புக்கோ லத்திட் ...... டிமையொர்வா","னிற்பா ரிற்சூ ழச்சூ ரைத்தா","னெட்டா வெட்டிப் ...... பொரும்வேலா","முட்டா மற்றா ளைச்சே விப்பார்","முற்பா வத்தைக் ...... களைவோனே","முத்தா முத்தீ யத்தா சுத்தா","முத்தா முத்திப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses seeking wealth as a merchant seeks honey and singing a few songs to those who had come into fortune, like men of petty knowledge, and asks whether, leaning instead on those who have attained, he will be allowed to praise Murugan's good feet a little. The praise names the vel that fought and cut down Sura while the gods stood round, and Murugan who removes the past sins of those who serve his feet without fail.","n":"The contrast is between the patrons (திக்காமல் பாடுற்றார்) to whom he sings for pay and the devotees (பெற்றார்) he would lean on instead; the same confession as 1105 and 1123. Most word splits are uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iravu","adiyaar","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:243"},"p:1119":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1119,"t":["பட்டா டைக்கே பச்சோ லைக்கா","துக்கே பத்தித் ...... தனமாகும்","பக்கே நிட்டூ ரப்பார் வைக்கே","பட்டா சைப்பட் ...... டுறவாடி","ஒட்டார் நட்டார் வட்டா ரத்தே","சுற்றே முற்றத் ...... தடுமாறும்","ஒட்டா ரப்பா விக்கே மிக்கா","முற்றாள் கிட்டத் ...... தகுமோதான்","கட்டா விப்போ துட்டா விப்பூ","கக்கா விற்புக் ...... களிபாடுங்","கற்பூர் நற்சா ரக்கா ழித்தோய்","கத்தா சத்தித் ...... தகவோடே","முட்டா கக்கூ ரிட்டே னற்றாள்","முற்றா மற்கொட் ...... குமரேசா","முத்தா முத்தீ யத்தா சுத்தா","முத்தா முத்திப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of women whose devotion is for silk and green palm-leaf ear ornaments, who look with cruel glances, and of his consorting with them while friends and strangers in the neighbourhood mock him, and asks whether a sinner like him may reach Murugan's feet. The praise appears to name the lord of Kaazhi and the Sakti's grace, and Kumaresa who guarded the millet field.","n":"The women's devotion (பத்தி) is for silk and ornaments, gifts the man must pay for. காழி, Sirkazhi, with சத்தி may allude to Sambandar's birthplace and the milk the goddess gave him; the praise is too clipped to be sure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","kaamam","kutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:243"},"p:1120":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1120,"t":["பத்தே ழெட்டீ ரெட்டேழ் ரட்டால்","வைத்தே பத்திப் ...... படவேயும்","பைப்பீ றற்கூ ரைப்பா சத்தா","சற்கா ரத்துக் ...... கிரைதேடி","எத்தே சத்தோ டித்தே சத்தோ","டொத்தேய் சப்தத் ...... திலுமோடி","எய்த்தே நத்தா பற்றா மற்றா","திற்றே முக்கக் ...... கடவேனோ","சத்தே முற்றா யத்தா னைச்சூர்","கற்சா டிக்கற் ...... பணிதேசா","சட்சோ திப்பூ திப்பா லத்தா","அக்கோ டற்செச் ...... சையமார்பா","முத்தா பத்தா ரெட்டா வைப்பா","வித்தா முத்தர்க் ...... கிறையோனே","முத்தா முத்தீ யத்தா சுத்தா","முத்தா முத்திப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet pictures the body as a torn, leaking hut put together from numbered parts, and himself running to this land and that for its food until worn out, and asks whether he must be stuck in that. The praise names Murugan who crushed Sura, the child of the six lights and the holy ash, with red flowers on his chest, the lord of the freed.","n":"பத்தே ழெட்டீ ரெட்டேழ் ரட்டால் counts the body's parts as numbers, like the Tirumanthiram's numbered body (159); what the numbers stand for is not given. பைப்பீறல் கூரை, a torn-bag roof, is the hut of the body. The reading is a sketch.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","undi","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:243"},"p:1121":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1121,"t":["பொற்கோ வைக்கே பற்கோ வைக்கே","பொய்ப்போ கத்தைப் ...... பகர்வார்தம்","பொய்க்கே மெய்க்கே பித்தா கிப்போ","கித்தே கைக்குப் ...... பொருள்தேடித்","தெற்கோ டிக்கா சிக்கோ டிக்கீழ்","திக்கோ டிப்பச் ...... சிமமான","திக்கோ டிப்பா ணிக்கோ டித்தீ","வுக்கோ டிக்கெட் ...... டிடலாமோ","தற்கோ லிப்பா விப்பார் நற்சீ","ரைச்சா ரத்தற் ...... பரமானாய்","தப்பா முப்பா லைத்தே டித்தே","சத்தோர் நிற்கத் ...... தகையோடே","முற்கா னப்பே தைக்கா கப்போய்","முற்பால் வெற்பிற் ...... கணியானாய்","முத்தா முத்தீ யத்தா சுத்தா","முத்தா முத்திப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses that for women with coral lips and false talk of pleasure, mad with taking their lies as truth, he seeks money for their hands, running south, to Kasi, east, west, to every direction and to the islands, and asks whether he must be ruined so. The praise names Murugan who became supreme for those who discipline themselves and meditate on him, and who went to the simple forest girl and became a vengai tree on the hill.","n":"The distances (தெற்கு, காசி, கீழ், பச்சிமம், தீவு) measure how far money for the women drives him. முற்பால் வெற்பில் கணியானாய் recalls the story in which Murugan stood as a vengai tree before Valli. தப்பா முப்பாலை may name the Kural; the line is not clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","avaa"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:243"},"p:1122":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1122,"t":["பொற்பூ வைச்சீ ரைப்போ லப்போ","தப்பே சிப்பொற் ...... கனிவாயின்","பொய்க்கா மத்தே மெய்க்கா மப்பூ","ணைப்பூண் வெற்பிற் ...... றுகில்சாயக்","கற்பா லெக்கா வுட்கோ லிக்கா","சுக்கே கைக்குத் ...... திடுமாதர்","கட்கே பட்டே நெட்டா சைப்பா","டுற்றே கட்டப் ...... படுவேனோ","சொற்கோ லத்தே நற்கா லைச்சே","விப்பார் சித்தத் ...... துறைவோனே","தொக்கே கொக்கா கிச்சூ ழச்சூர்","விக்கா முக்கத் ...... தொடும்வேலா","முற்கா லத்தே வெற்பேய் வுற்றார்","முத்தாள் முத்தச் ...... சிறியோனே","முத்தா முத்தீ யத்தா சுத்தா","முத்தா முத்திப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of women who talk like golden mynahs, pass off false love as true through fruit-red mouths, let their cloth slip from mountain breasts and press for money in hand with hard hearts, and asks whether he must be caught by their eyes and bound by long desire. The praise names Murugan who dwells in the hearts of those who serve his feet with words, whose vel struck Sura as the mango tree, and the child kissed by the mountain's daughter.","n":"பொய்க்காமத்தே மெய்க்காம, false love as true love, is the deceit; காசுக்கே கைக்குத்திடு makes the payment explicit. கட்கே பட்டு, struck by the eyes, makes the eyes the snare.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","kan","vanjam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:243"},"p:1123":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1123,"t":["மெய்க்கூ ணைத்தே டிப்பூ மிக்கே","வித்தா ரத்திற் ...... பலகாலும்","வேட்கா மற்சே ரிச்சோ ரர்க்கே","வித்தா சைச்சொற் ...... களையோதிக்","கைக்கா ணிக்கோ ணற்போ தத்தா","ரைப்போ லக்கற் ...... பழியாதுன்","கற்பூ டுற்றே நற்றா ளைப்பா","டற்கே நற்சொற் ...... றருவாயே","பொய்க்கோ ணத்தாழ் மெய்க்கோ ணிப்போய்","முற்பால் வெற்பிற் ...... புனமானைப்","பொற்றோ ளிற்சேர் கைக்கா கப்பா","தத்தாள் பற்றிப் ...... புகல்வோனே","முக்கோ ணத்தா னத்தா ளைப்பால்","வைத்தார் முத்தச் ...... சிறியோனே","முத்தா முத்தீ யத்தா சுத்தா","முத்தா முத்திப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses that he has sought food for the body all over the earth, praised unkind and thieving men with words of desire, like men of crooked knowledge, and asks for good words to sing Murugan's feet. The praise names Murugan who went to the deer-like girl in the millet field on the hill, held her feet and begged refuge for her golden shoulders, and the child kissed by the goddess whom Siva keeps at his side.","n":"The plea asks for words (நற்சொல்) to replace the words already spent on patrons. Murugan holding Valli's feet as a suppliant (பாதத்தாள் பற்றிப் புகல்வோனே) mirrors the poet's own begging, turned to love. முக்கோணத்தானத்தாள, the lady of the triangle, names the goddess by the yantra.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iravu","sol","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:243"},"p:1124":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1124,"t":["அகரமுத லெனவுரைசெய் ஐம்பந்தொ ரக்ஷரமும்","அகிலகலை களும்வெகுவி தங்கொண்ட தத்துவமும்","அபரிமித சுருதியும டங்குந்த னிப்பொருளை எப்பொருளு ...... மாய","அறிவையறி பவரறியும் இன்பந்த னைத்துரிய","முடிவைஅடி நடுமுடிவில் துங்கந்த னைச்சிறிய","அணுவையணு வினின்மலமு நெஞ்சுங்கு ணத்ரயமு மற்றதொரு ...... காலம்","நிகழும்வடி வினைமுடிவி லொன்றென்றி ருப்பதனை","நிறைவுகுறை வொழிவறநி றைந்தெங்கு நிற்பதனை","நிகர்பகர அரியதைவி சும்பின்பு ரத்ரயமெ ரித்தபெரு ...... மானும்","நிருபகுரு பரகுமர என்றென்று பத்திகொடு","பரவஅரு ளியமவுன மந்த்ரந்த னைப்பழைய","நினதுவழி யடிமையும்வி ளங்கும்ப டிக்கினிது ணர்த்தியருள் ...... வாயே","தகுதகுகு தகுதகுகு தந்தந்த குத்தகுகு","டிகுடிகுகு டிகுடிகுகு டிண்டிண்டி குக்குடிகு","தகுதகெண கெணசெகுத தந்தந்த ரித்தகுத தத்ததகு ...... தீதோ","தனதனன தனதனன தந்தந்த னத்ததன","டுடுடுடுடு டுடுடுடுடு டுண்டுண்டு டுட்டுடுடு","தரரரர ரிரிரிரிரியென்றென்றி டக்கையுமு டுக்கையுமி ...... யாவும்","மொகுமொகென அதிரமுதி ரண்டம்பி ளக்கநிமிர்","அலகைகர ணமிடவுல கெங்கும்ப்ர மிக்கநட","முடுகுபயி ரவர்பவுரிகொண்டின்பு றப்படுக ளத்திலொரு ...... கோடி","முதுகழுகு கொடிகருட னங்கம்பொ ரக்குருதி","நதிபெருக வெகுமுகக வந்தங்கள் நிர்த்தமிட","முரசதிர நிசிசரரை வென்றிந்தி ரற்கரச ளித்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks Murugan to teach him sweetly the silent mantra that he gave to Siva, who burned the three cities, when Siva praised him with devotion as king, guru and Kumara: the one entity in which the fifty-one letters from a, all the arts, the many tattvas and the endless Vedas are contained, the bliss known by those who know, the end of the fourth state, the atom within the atom, beyond impurity, mind, the three qualities and time, filling all things, beyond comparison; and to make his old servitude shine. The praise is a battle in drum syllables, ghouls and Bhairavas dancing, vultures and rivers of blood, in which Murugan conquered the night-walkers and gave Indra back his kingship.","n":"The guru story is told as Siva's own act of devotion (நிருப குரு பர குமர என்று என்று பத்திகொடு பரவ), and what Murugan gave is called a மவுன மந்திரம், a silent mantra; Om is not named. The long description of the absolute (துரிய முடிவு, அணுவை அணுவினின், நிறைவு குறைவு ஒழிவற) is Vedanta and Siddhanta vocabulary, and நினது வழியடிமை asks for hereditary service. The battle lines are rhythm.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["guru","mounam","mandhiram","thondu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:243"},"p:1125":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1125,"t":["அரிய வஞ்சக ரறவே கொடியவர்","அவலர் வன்கண ரினியா ரவகுணர்","அசட ரன்பில ரவமே திரிபவர் ...... அதிமோக","அலையில் மண்டிய வழியே யொழுகியர்","வினைநி ரம்பிடு பவமே செறிபவர்","அருள்து றந்தவ ரிடம்வாழ் சவலைகள் ...... நரகேற","உ ரிய சஞ்சல மதியா னதுபெறு","மனஇ டும்பர்க ளிடமே தெனஅவர்","உ பய அங்கமு நிலையா கிடவொரு ...... கவியாலே","உ லக முண்டவர் மதனா ரிமையவர்","தருவெ னும்படி மொழியா வவர்தர","உ ளது கொண்டுயி ரவமே விடுவது ...... தவிராதோ","கரிய கொந்தள மலையா ளிருதன","அமுது ணுங்குரு பரனே திரைபடு","கடல டும்படி கணையே வியஅரி...... மருகோனே","கருணை கொண்டொரு குறமா மகளிடை","கலவி தங்கிய குமரா மயில்மிசை","கடுகி யெண்டிசை நொடியே வலம்வரு ...... மிளையோனே","திரிபு ரங்கனல் நகையா லெரிசெய்து","பொதுந டம்புரியரனா ரிடமுறை","சிவைச வுந்தரியுமையா ளருளிய ...... புதல்வோனே","சிகர வெண்கரிஅயிரா வதமிசை","வருபு ரந்தர னமரா பதியவர்","சிறைவி டும்படி வடிவேல் விடவல ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet lists the men he goes to, deceitful, cruel, hard-eyed, ignorant, loveless, sunk in delusion and births, cut off from grace and bound for hell, and asks whether his habit of praising them in verse as Vishnu who swallowed the worlds, as Kama or as the tree of the gods, and wasting his life on what they give, will ever end. The praise names Murugan who drinks at Valli's breasts, nephew of Rama who shot the arrow at the sea, who circles the eight directions on the peacock in a moment, son of Uma and of Siva who burned the three cities with a smile and dances in the hall, and who freed Indra's city.","n":"The patrons are condemned in a long list of plural nouns before the confession comes; the poet's fault is the false praise (உலகம் உண்டவர் மதனார் இமையவர் தரு எனும்படி மொழியா), which compares the base to gods. உயிர் அவமே விடுவது, wasting life, is the cost.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","kayamai","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:243"},"p:1126":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1126,"t":["ஆராத காத லாகி மாதர்த","மாபாத சூட மீதி லேவிழி","யாலோல னாய்வி கார மாகியி ...... லஞ்சியாலே","ஆசாப சாசு மூடி மேலிட","ஆசார வீன னாகி யேமிக","ஆபாச னாகி யோடி நாளும ...... ழிந்திடாதே","ஈராறு தோளு மாறு மாமுக","மோடாரு நீப வாச மாலையு","மேறான தோகை நீல வாசியு ...... மன்பினாலே","ஏனோரு மோது மாறு தீதற","நானாசு பாடி யாடி நாடொறு","மீடேறு மாறு ஞான போதக ...... மன்புறாதோ","வாராகி நீள்க பாலி மாலினி","மாமாயி யாயி தேவி யாமளை","வாசாம கோச ராப ராபரை ...... யிங்குளாயி","வாதாடி மோடி காடு காளுமை","மாஞால லீலி யால போசனி","மாகாளி சூலி வாலை யோகினி ...... யம்பவானி","சூராரி மாபு ராரிகோமளை","தூளாய பூதி பூசு நாரணி","சோணாச லாதி லோக நாயகி ...... தந்தவாழ்வே","தோளாலும் வாளி னாலு மாறிடு","தோலாத வான நாடு சூறைகொள்","சூராரியேவி சாக னேசுரர் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses unending desire, eyes roving over women from foot to crown, the demon of desire covering him, without conduct and vile, running every day, and asks whether, instead of perishing so, the teaching of knowledge will come with love so that he may sing and dance daily of Murugan's twelve shoulders, six faces, kadamba garland and blue peacock. The praise is a litany of the goddess's names, Varahi, Kapali, Malini, Mahamayi, Yamalai, Kali, Uma, Bhavani, Narayani, mother of the worlds at Sonachalam and more, whose son is Visakha, who sent the vel against Sura.","n":"ஆசா பசாசு, desire as a possessing demon, and ஆசார ஈனன், one without conduct, are the confession's key words. The cure is ecstatic devotion (பாடி ஆடி). The goddess's names mix Saiva, Sakta and Vaishnava titles (வாராகி, காளி, நாரணி, அம்பவானி) and place her at சோணாசலம், Annamalai.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avaa","saththi","paththi","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:243"},"p:1127":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1127,"t":["ஆராதன ராடம் பரத்து மாறாதுச வாலம் பனத்து","மாவாகன மாமந் திரத்து ...... மடலாலும்","ஆறார்தெச மாமண் டபத்தும் வேதாகம மோதுந் தலத்து","மாமாறெரிதாமிந் தனத்து ...... மருளாதே","நீராளக நீர்மஞ் சனத்த நீடாரக வேதண்ட மத்த","நீநானற வேறின்றி நிற்க ...... நியமாக","நீவாவென நீயிங் கழைத்து பாராவர வாநந்த சித்தி","நேரேபர மாநந்த முத்தி ...... தரவேணும்","வீராகர சாமுண்டி சக்ர பாராகண பூதங் களிக்க","வேதாளச மூகம் பிழைக்க ...... அமராடி","வேதாமுறை யோவென் றரற்ற ஆகாசக பாலம் பிளக்க","வேர்மாமர மூலந் தறித்து ...... வடவாலும்","வாராகர மேழுங் குடித்து மாசூரொடு போரம் பறுத்து","வாணாசன மேலுந் துணித்த ...... கதிர்வேலா","வானாடர சாளும் படிக்கு வாவாவென வாவென் றழைத்து","வானோர்பரிதாபந் தவிர்த்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks not to be bewildered by the pomp of worship, invocations and mantras, palm-leaf texts, pillared halls, places where Veda and Agama are chanted and fuel burned in sacrifice, but to be called by Murugan himself, 'come', and given, regularly and without difference, the state where 'you' and 'I' cease, the siddhi of bliss and release into supreme bliss. The praise is a battle where Chamundi and the ghosts rejoice, Brahma cries out, the sky cracks, the mango tree is cut at the root and the seven seas are drunk, and Murugan calls the gods back to rule their heaven.","n":"The opening list (ஆராதனை ஆடம்பரம், ஆவாகன மாமந்திரம், வேதாகமம் ஓதும் தலம், எரிதாம் இந்தனம்) is ritual the poet does not want to be lost in (மருளாதே); against it stands நீ நான் அற வேறின்றி நிற்க, a non-dual union. Several compounds in the first half (சவாலம்பனத்து, வேதண்டமத்த) do not split securely, so the exact list is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","veedu","aanandham","siththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:244"},"p:1128":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1128,"t":["ஆலா லத்தைய ழுத்திய வேல்போல் நற்குழை யைப்பொரு","தாகா ரைத்தொடர் கைக்கெணும் ...... விழியாலே","ஆளா மற்றவர் சுற்றிட மீளா மற்றலை யிட்டறி","வார்போ கச்செயல் விச்சைகள் ...... விலைகூறிக்","கோலா லக்கண மிட்டுவ ராதார் நெக்குரு கப்பொருள்","கூறா கப்பெறில் நிற்கவு ...... மிலதானார்","கூடா நட்புமு ரைத்திடு கேடா விட்டகல் மட்டைகள்","கோமா ளத்துய ருட்பய ...... முறலாமோ","பாலா மக்கட லிற்றுயில் மாலோ ரெட்டுத லைக்கிரி","பால்பார் வைக்கள விட்டுமை ...... யுறுபோதிற்","பார்மே லிக்கனு டற்பொறி யாய்வீ ழச்சுடும் வித்தகர்","பாலா பத்தரிடத்தியல் ...... பயில்வோனே","மேலா யத்தொடு திக்கடை மேவார் வெற்பொட ரக்கரை","வேர்மா ளப்பொரு திட்டொளி ...... விடும்வேலா","மேனா டர்ச்சிறை விட்டருள் மீளா விக்கிர மத்தொடு","வேதா வைச்சிறை யிட்டருள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes courtesans whose eyes are like a vel steeped in poison, who surround men and will not let them go, name a price for their arts of pleasure, and stay only if they get a share of wealth, offering false friendship; he asks whether he must suffer the fear and misery of such clowning. The praise names Siva who burned Kama when Uma came near, Murugan dwelling among devotees, the vel that struck the mountain and the demons, and the lord who freed the gods from prison and imprisoned Brahma.","n":"விலைகூறி and பொருள் கூறாகப் பெறில் நிற்கவும் name the bargain outright: the women stay only for a share of the money. கூடா நட்பு, 'friendship that does not join', is a Kural phrase for false friendship; the women are also மட்டைகள், worthless husks.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","kan"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:244"},"p:1129":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1129,"t":["ஆனாத ஞான புத்தி யைக்கொ டுத்ததும்","ஆராயு நூல்க ளிற்க ருத்த ளித்ததும்","ஆதேச வாழ்வி னிற்ப்ர மித்தி ளைத்துயி ...... ரழியாதே","ஆசாப யோதி யைக்க டக்க விட்டதும்","வாசாம கோச ரத்தி ருத்து வித்ததும்","ஆபாத னேன்மி கப்ர சித்தி பெற்றினி ...... துலகேழும்","யானாக நாம அற்பு தத்தி ருப்புகழ்","தேனூற வோதி யெத்தி சைப்பு றத்தினும்","ஏடேவு ராஜ தத்தி னைப்ப ணித்ததும் ...... இடராழி","ஏறாத மாம லத்ர யக்கு ணத்ரய","நானாவி கார புற்பு தப்பி றப்பற","ஏதேம மாயெ னக்க நுக்ர கித்ததும் ...... மறவேனே","மாநாக நாண்வ லுப்பு றத்து வக்கியொர்","மாமேரு பூத ரத்த னுப்பி டித்தொரு","மாலாய வாளி யைத்தொ டுத்த ரக்கரி...... லொ முவர்","மாளாது பாத கப்பு ரத்ர யத்தவர்","தூளாக வேமு தற்சி ரித்த வித்தகர்","வாழ்வேவ லாரிபெற்றெ டுத்த கற்பக ...... வனமேவும்","தேநாய காஎ னத்து தித்த வுத்தம","வானாடர் வாழ விக்ர மத்தி ருக்கழல்","சேராத சூர னைத்து ணித்த டக்கிய ...... வரைமோதிச்","சேறாய சோரிபுக்க ளக்கர் திட்டெழ","மாறாநி சாச ரக்கு லத்தை யிப்படி","சீராவி னால றுத்த றுத்தொ துக்கிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A song of thanks: the poet says he will never forget that Murugan gave him unfailing wisdom and understanding of the books he studied, kept him from perishing in a passing life, carried him across the sea of desire, set him in the state beyond speech and mind, and made him, a base man, famous for singing the wondrous Thiruppugazh with honey in every direction, freeing him from the three impurities, the three qualities and bubble-like births. The praise names Siva who drew Meru as a bow with the serpent for string and Vishnu as arrow and burned the three cities, Devayanai daughter of Indra, and Murugan's sword that cut down Sura and his clan.","n":"The song names its own book: அற்புதத் திருப்புகழ் தேனூற ஓதி. The plea of other songs is here a list of graces already given (கொடுத்ததும், அளித்ததும் ... மறவேனே). வாசாமகோசரம், beyond word and mind, and மலத்ரயம், குணத்ரயம் are Saiva doctrinal terms. ஏடு ஏவு ராஜதத்தினைப் பணித்ததும் may refer to some royal command or honour; its sense is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","gnaanam","malam","mukkunam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:244"},"p:1130":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1130,"t":["இடமருவுஞ் சீற்ற வேலெ டுத்து","விடமுழுதுந் தேக்கி யேநி றைத்து","இருகுழையுந் தாக்கி மீள்க யற்கண் ...... வலையாலே","இனிமையுடன் பார்த்து ளேய ழைத்து","முகபடமுஞ் சேர்த்து வார ழுத்தும்","இருவரையுங் காட்டி மாலெ ழுப்பி ...... விலைபேசி","மடலவிழும் பூக்க ளால்நி றைத்த","சுருளளகந் தூற்றி யேமு டித்து","மறுகிடைநின் றார்க்க வேந கைத்து ...... நிலையாக","வருபொருள்கண் டேற்க வேப றிக்கும்","அரிவையர்தம் பேச்சி லேமு ழுக்க","மனமுருகுந் தூர்த்த னாயி ளைத்து ...... விடலாமோ","படிமுழுதுங் கூர்த்த மாகு லத்தி","முதுமறையின் பேச்சி நூலி டைச்சி","பகிர்மதியம் பூத்த தாழ்ச டைச்சி ...... யிருநாழி","படிகொடறங் காத்த மாப ரைச்சி","மணிவயிரங் கோத்த தோள்வ ளைச்சி","பலதிசையும் போய்க்கு லாவி ருப்பி ...... நெடுநீலி","அடுபுலியின் தோற்ப டாமு டைச்சி","சமரமுகங் காட்டு மால்வி டைச்சி","அகிலமுமுண் டார்க்கு நேரிளைச்சி ...... பெருவாழ்வே","அரியயனின் றேத்த வேமி குத்த","விபுதர்குலம் பேர்க்க வாளெ டுத்த","அசுரர்குலம் பாழ்க்க வேலெ டுத்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes courtesans with poisoned eyes who call a man in with sweet looks, show their breasts, rouse desire and bargain over the price, laugh from the street and snatch whatever wealth comes, and asks whether he, a rogue whose mind melts at their talk, must waste away. The praise is almost all to the goddess: speaker of the Veda, with crescent-moon locks, who kept dharma with two measures of grain, wearer of the tiger skin, rider of the bull, younger sister of Vishnu, whose great treasure Murugan is.","n":"விலைபேசி and வருபொருள் கண்டு ஏற்கவே பறிக்கும் name the trade. The goddess's epithets run in a string ending in -ச்சி (குலத்தி, பேச்சி, இடைச்சி, சடைச்சி ...). இருநாழி படிகொடு அறம் காத்த points to the story, told of Kanchi, of the goddess who kept the charities with two measures of grain; I offer it as a likely allusion.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","kaamam","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:244"},"p:1131":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1131,"t":["இடர்மொய்த்துத் தொடரிற்பொய்க் குடிலக்கிக் கிடையிட்டிட்","டினிமைச்சுற் றமுமற்றைப் ...... புதல்வோரும்","இனமொப்பித் திசையச்சொற் பலகத்திட் டிழியப்பிற்","கிடையத்துக் கமும்விட்டிட் ...... டவரேக","விடமெத்தச் சொரிசெக்கட் சமன்வெட்டத் தனமுற்றிட்","டுயிர்வித்துத் தனையெற்றிக் ...... கொடுபோமுன்","வினைபற்றற் றறநித்தப் புதுமைச்சொற் கொடுவெட்சிப்","புயவெற்றிப் புகழ்செப்பப் ...... பெறுவேனோ","அடர்செக்கர்ச் சடையிற்பொற் பிறையப்புப் புனையப்பர்க்","கறிவொக்கப் பொருள்கற்பித் ...... திடுவோனே","அலகைக்குட் பசிதித்தப் பலகைக்கொத் ததுபட்டிட்","டலறக்குத் துறமுட்டிப் ...... பொரும்வேலா","கடலுக்குட் படுசர்ப்பத் தினில்மெச்சத் துயில்பச்சைக்","கிரிகைக்குட் டிகிரிக்கொற் ...... றவன்மாயன்","கமலத்திற் பயில்நெட்டைக் குயவற்கெட் டிசையர்க்குக்","கடவுட்சக் கிரவர்த்திப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet pictures death: troubles crowd round, the false hut of the body is laid out, kin and sons gather and lament with many words and then leave, and red-eyed Yama strikes and drags the life away; before that, he asks, may he be freed of his deeds and praise in ever-new words the victory of Murugan's vetchi-garlanded shoulders? The praise names Murugan as the one who taught his father, with crescent and Ganga in his red matted hair, the meaning of knowledge, and as god-emperor of Vishnu with his discus and Brahma the tall potter.","n":"The kin weep and then go (அவர் ஏக), a single beat that empties the house before Yama (சமன்) arrives. The plea is not for rescue but to praise (புகழ் செப்பப் பெறுவேனோ) before the end. அப்பர்க்கு அறிவொக்கப் பொருள் கற்பித்திடுவோனே is the story of Murugan teaching Siva, here without naming Om. குயவன், potter, for Brahma the maker is unusual.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","sutram","pugazh","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:244"},"p:1132":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1132,"t":["இரவினிடை வேள்தொ டுத்து டன்று","முறுகுமலர் வாளி யைப்பி ணங்கி","யிருகுழையு மோதி யப்ப டங்கு ...... கடலோடே","எதிர்பொருது மானி னைத்து ரந்து","சலதிகிழி வேல்த னைப்பொ ருந்தி","யினியமுத ஆல முற்ற கண்கள் ...... வலையாலே","முரணிளைஞ ராவி யைத்தொ டர்ந்து","விசிறிவளை மாத ரைக்க லந்து","மொழியதர கோவை யிக்க ருந்தி ...... யமுதாகு","முகிழ்முகுளி தார வெற்ப ணைந்து","சுழிமிதுன வாவி யிற்பு குந்து","முழுகியழி யாம னற்ப தங்கள் ...... தரவேணும்","திரையுலகு சாக ரத்தி லங்கை","நகரிலுறை ராவ ணற்கி யைந்த","தெசமுடியு மீரு பத்தொ ழுங்கு ...... திணிதோளுஞ்","சிதையவொரு வாளி யைத்து ரந்த","அரிமருக தீத றக்க டந்து","தெளிமருவு கார ணத்த மர்ந்த ...... முருகோனே","அரணமதிள் சூழ்பு ரத்தி ருந்து","கருதுமொரு மூவ ருக்கி ரங்கி","யருளுமொரு நாய கற்ப ணிந்த ...... குருநாதா","அகல்முடிவை யாதி யைத்தெ ளிந்து","இரவுபகலாக நெக்க விழ்ந்த","அடியவர்கள் பாட லுக்கி சைந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet tells how at night Kama shoots his flower arrows and women's eyes, carp-like, deer-defeating, nectar and poison together, net young men's lives, and how, drinking their lips and embracing them, he sinks into the whirlpool of union; he asks for the good undying feet so that he does not drown. The praise names Vishnu who shot the arrow through Ravana's ten heads and twenty shoulders, Murugan as guru to Siva who pitied three in the walled cities, and the lord pleased by the songs of devotees who melt day and night.","n":"சுழி மிதுன வாவி, the eddying pool of coupling, makes union a drowning, so the remedy is feet that do not perish (அழியா மன் நல் பதங்கள்). தெளிமருவு காரணத்து அமர்ந்த may name a place or a cause; I cannot tell which.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kan","punarchi","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:244"},"p:1133":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1133,"t":["இரவொ டும்பக லேமா றாதே","அநுதி னந்துய ரோயா தேயே","யெரியு முந்தியி னாலே மாலே ...... பெரிதாகி","இரைகொ ளும்படி யூடே பாடே","மிகுதி கொண்டொழி யாதே வாதே","யிடைக ளின்சில நாளே போயே ...... வயதாகி","நரைக ளும்பெரிதாயே போயே","கிழவ னென்றொரு பேரே சார்வே","நடைக ளும்பல தாறே மாறே ...... விழலாகி","நயன முந்தெரியாதே போனால்","விடிவ தென்றடி யேனே தானே","நடன் குஞ்சித வீடே கூடா ...... தழிவேனோ","திருந லம்புரிதாளீ தூளீ","மகர குண்டலி மா஡ண சூரி","திரிபு ரந்தழ லேவீ சார்வீ ...... யபிராமி","சிவனி டந்தரிநீலீ சூலீ","கவுரிபஞ்சவி யாயீ மாயீ","சிவைபெ ணம்பிகை வாலா சீலா ...... அருள்பாலா","அரவ கிங்கிணி வீரா தீரா","கிரிபு ரந்தொளிர் நாதா பாதா","அழகி ளங்குற மானார் தேனார் ...... மணவாளா","அரிய ரன்பிர மாவோ ஧ட்மு","வகைய ரிந்திர கோமா னீள்வா","னமரர் கந்தரு வானோ ரேனோர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet traces a life of unceasing sorrow driven by the burning belly, toil for food, the years passing, hair greying, the name 'old man', faltering steps and failing eyes, and asks whether he will perish without reaching the abode of the Dancer's raised foot. The praise is a litany of the goddess's names (Abhirami, Nili, Suli, Gauri, Mayi, Sivai, Ambikai, who burned the three cities and shares Siva's side), whose son Murugan is, Valli's bridegroom, lord praised by Hari, Hara, Brahma, Indra and the gods.","n":"நடன் குஞ்சித வீடு is release imagined as the lifted foot of Siva's dance; the song asks for it from the son, not the father. விடிவது என்று, 'when will it dawn', is the old man's question. Two lines are corrupt (see source issues).","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["ilamai","yaakkai","veedu","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:244"},"p:1134":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1134,"t":["இருகுழை மீதோடி மீளவும்","கயல்களு மாலால காலமும்","ரதிபதி கோலாடு பூசலு ...... மெனவேநின்","றிலகிய கூர்வேல் விலோசன","ம்ருகமத பாடிர பூஷித","இளமுலை மாமாத ரார்வச ...... முருகாதே","முருகவிழ் கூதாள மாலிகை","தழுவிய சீர்பாத தூளியின்","முழுகிவி டாய்போ மனோலயம் ...... வரவோது","முழுமதி மாயாவி காரமு","மொழிவது வாசாம கோசர","முகுளித ஞானோப தேசமு ...... மருள்வேணும்","அருமறை நூலோதும் வேதியன்","இரணிய ரூபாந மோவென","அரிகரிநாராய ணாவென ...... ஒருபாலன்","அவனெவ னாதார மேதென","இதனுள னோவோது நீயென","அகிலமும் வாழ்வான நாயக ...... னெனவேகி","ஒருகணை தூணோடு மோதிட","விசைகொடு தோள்போறு வாளரி","யுகிர்கொடு வாரா நிசாசர ...... னுடல்பீறும்","உ லகொரு தாளான மாமனும்","உ மையொரு கூறான தாதையும்","உ ரைதரு தேவாசு ராதிபர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks not to melt under the sway of young women whose sharp eyes are like Kama's battle, but to sink into the dust of Murugan's feet with their koothalam garland until the mind dissolves, and to receive the teaching of a knowledge beyond word and mind. The praise tells the story of Prahlada at length, the boy who answered his Brahmin teacher with Narayana's name and the man-lion who tore his father from the pillar, and names Vishnu as uncle and Siva as father.","n":"மனோலயம், the dissolving of mind, comes from immersion in the dust of the feet (பாததூளியின் முழுகி), and the plea ends in வாசாமகோசர ... ஞானோபதேசம். The Prahlada story is told in Prahlada's own words (இரணிய ரூபா நமோ, அரி கரி நாராயணா).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","thiruvadi","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:244"},"p:1135":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1135,"t":["இருமுலை மலையென ஒப்பிட் டேயவர்","இருவிழி யதனில் கப்பட் டேமன","மிசைபட வசனமு ரைத்திட் டேபல ...... மினிதொடே","இடையது துவளகு லுக்கிக் காலணி","பரிபுர வொலிகள் தொனிக்கப் பூதர","இளமுலை குழைய அணைத்துக் கேயுர ...... மணியோடே","மதகத பவள மழுத்திப் பூஷண","மணிபல சிதறி நெறித்துத் தானுக","மருமலர் புனுகு தரித்துப் பூவணை ...... மதராஜன்","மருவிய கலவி தனக்கொப் பாமென","மகிழ்வொடு ரசிது மிகுத்துக் கோதையை","மருவியு முருகி களைத்துப் பூமியி ...... லுழல்வேனோ","திரிபுர மெரிய நகைத்துக் காலனை","யுதைபட மதனை யழித்துச் சாகர","திரைவரு கடுவை மிடற்றிற் றானணி ...... சிவனார்தந்","திருவருள் முருக பெருத்துப் பாரினில்","சியொதனன் மடிய மிகுத்துப் பாரத","செயமுறு மரிதன் மனத்துக் காகிய ...... மருகோனே","நரிகழு வதுகள் களிக்கச் சோரிகள்","ரணகள முழுதுமி குத்துக் கூளிகள்","நடமிட அசுரர் குலத்துக் காலனை ...... நிகராகி","நனிகடல் கதற பொருப்புத் தூளெழ","நணுகிய இமையவ ருக்குச் சீருற","நணுகலர் மடியதொ லைத்துப் பேர்பேறு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes lovemaking in frank detail, the swaying waist, ringing anklets, scattered jewels, scented flowers on the bed, and asks whether, after melting in a union said to equal Kama's own, he must wander the earth exhausted. The praise names Siva who burned the cities with a laugh, kicked Death, destroyed Kama and wore the sea's poison, and Vishnu who brought Duryodhana down in the Bharata war, with a battle in which jackals feast.","n":"மதராஜன் மருவிய கலவி தனக்கு ஒப்பாம் is the boast inside the confession: the pleasure is called equal to Kama's, and its end is weariness (களைத்து).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:244"},"p:1136":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1136,"t":["இலகிய வேலோ சேலோ ஒளிவிடு வாளோ போதோ","எமன்விடு தூதோ மானோ ...... விடமீதோ","எனவிழி கூறா வாரா அரிவையர் தேர்ளு டாடா","இறுதியில் வேறாய் மாறா ...... நினைவாலே","பலபல கோளாய் மாலா யுழலும தானால் வீணே","படிறுசொ லாகா லோகா ...... யதனாகிப்","பரிவுட னாடாய் வீடா யடிமையு மீடே றாதே","பணிதியில் மூழ்கா மாயா ...... விடுவேனோ","அலைகடல் கோகோ கோகோ எனவுரை கூறா வோடா","அவுணரை வாடா போடா ...... எனலாகி","அழகிய வேலால் வாளால் நிலவிய சீரா வாலே","யவருடல் வாணா ளீரா ...... எதிராகி","மலைமிகு தோளா போதா அழகிய வாலா பாலா","மகபதி வாழ்வே சேயே ...... மயில்வீரா","மறைதொழு கோவே தேவே நறை செறி பூவே நீரே","வளவிய வேளே மேலோர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet tells how he praised women's eyes as vel, carp, sword, flower, Death's messenger, deer and poison, wandered deluded to the end, spoke deceit and became a materialist, and asks whether he will die without his service being fulfilled. The praise is a battle in which the sea cries out and Murugan drives off the demons with vel and sword, the lord of the peacock and son of Indra's fortune, worshipped by the Veda.","n":"லோகாயதனாகி: the poet names the materialist school as one of his falls, beside false speech (படிறு சொல்). The list of eye-comparisons (வேலோ சேலோ ... எமன்விடு தூதோ) is the opening's whole substance. The plea's lines (நாடாய் வீடாய் அடிமையும் ஈடேறாதே பணிதியில் மூழ்கா) do not split securely, and I read them only roughly.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kan","kaamam","samayam","thondu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:244"},"p:1137":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1137,"t":["உ மையெனு மயில்பெற்ற மயில்வா கனனே","வனிதைய ரறுவர்க்கு மொருபா லகனே","உ ளமுரு கியபத்த ருறவே மறவே ...... னெனவோதி","உ ருகுத லொருசற்று மறியேன் வறியேன்","இ ருவினை யிடையிட்ட கொடியே னடியேன்","உ ணர்விலி பெறமுத்தி தருவாய் துகிர்வாய் ...... மடமாதர்","அமையென வளர்சித்ர இருதோள் தழுவா","அமுதென மதுரித்த கனிவா யணுகா","அமளியி லணைவுற்ற அநுரா கமகோ ...... தத஧முழ்கி","அநவர தமுமுற்ற மணிமா முலைதோய்","கலவியி னலமற்ப சுகமா கினுமா","அநுபவ மிதுசற்றும் விடவோ இயலா ...... தியலாதே","தமனிய குலசக்ர கிரியோ கடலோ","விடமென முடிவைத்த முதுபே ரிருளோ","தனுவென முனையிட்ட கொநல்மு விலைவேல் ...... கொடுபார்வை","தழலெழ வருமுக்ர எமபா தகனோ","யுகஇறு தியில்மிக்க வடவா னலமோ","தனியிவ னெனமிக்க பிசிதா சனபூ ...... பதியாகி","இமையவ ரனைவர்க்கும் அறையோ அறையோ","அரியயன் முழுதுக்கும் அறையோ அறையோ","எழுபுவி யுலகுக்கும் அறையோ அறையோ ...... பொரவாரும்","எனவரு மொருதுட்டன் முறையோ முறையோ","வடகுல கிரியெட்டும் அபிதா அபிதா","எனவொரு அயில்தொட்ட அரசே யிமையோர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet opens by naming Murugan as the peacock-rider born of peacock-like Uma and the child of six mothers, confesses that he never melted in devotion, is caught between his two deeds and is without understanding, and admits that though the pleasure of union with women is slight, he cannot, cannot give it up; he asks for release. The praise pictures Sura as a golden mountain, a sea, primeval darkness, Yama with trident and fiery eyes, the fire at the end of the age, a flesh-eating king challenging gods, Hari, Brahma and the seven worlds, and names Murugan as the king who threw the vel at him.","n":"சுகம் அற்பமாகினும் ... விடவோ இயலாது இயலாதே is a rare admission: the poet grants the pleasure is small and still cannot leave it. The demon's challenge is in his own voice (அறையோ அறையோ), and the cries of the worlds answer (முறையோ முறையோ). Two words are corrupt (see source issues).","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kaamam","punarchi","vinai","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:244"},"p:1138":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1138,"t":["உ ரைத்த பற்றுட னடிகள்ப ணிந்திட்","டிருத்தி மெத்தென இளநகை யுஞ்சற்","றுமிழ்த்த டைக்கல மெனஎதிர் கும்பிட் ...... டணைமேல்வீழ்ந்","துடுத்த பொற்றுகி லகலல்கு லுந்தொட்","டெடுத்த ணைத்திதழ் பெருகமு தந்துய்த்","துனக்கெ னக்கென வுருகிமு யங்கிட் ...... டுளும்வேறாய்","அருக்கி யத்தனை யெனுமவ சம்பட்","டறுத்தொ துக்கிய நகநுதி யுந்தைத்","தறப்பி தற்றிட அமளிக லங்கித் ...... தடுமாறி","அளைத்து ழைத்திரு விழிகள்சி வந்திட்","டயர்த்தி தத்தொடு மொழிபவ ருந்திக்","கடுத்த கப்படு கலவியில் நொந்தெய்த் ...... திடலாமோ","தரைக்க டற்புகு நிருதர்த யங்கச்","சளப்ப டத்தட முடிகள்பி டுங்தித்","தகர்த்தொ லித்தெழு மலையொடு துண்டப் ...... பிறைசூடி","தனுக்கி ரித்திரிதரஎதி ருங்கொக்","கினைப்ப தைத்துட லலறிட வஞ்சத்","தருக்க டக்கிய சமர்பொரு துங்கத் ...... தனிவேலா","பருப்ப தப்ரிய குறுமுனி வந்தித்","திருக்கு முத்தம நிருதர்க லங்கப்","படைப்பெ லத்தொடு பழயக்ர வுஞ்சக் ...... கிரிசாடிப்","படர்ப்ப றைக்குரு குடலுதி ரங்குக்","குடக்கொ டிக்கிடு குமரகொ டுங்கற்","பதத்தி றுத்துகு பசியசி கண்டிப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes in detail a night with a woman, from bowing at her feet and her offered smile and betel to the bed, the loosened cloth, the nail marks, the babbling and the reddened eyes, and asks whether he must wear himself out in such union. The praise is a battle in which the demons of the sea lose their crowns, the mango tree is cut, the Krauncha hill is smashed, and the vel is worshipped by the short sage who loves the mountain, ending with the green peacock.","n":"The scene is told as a sequence (பணிந்திட்டு, இருத்தி, அணைமேல் வீழ்ந்து, உடுத்த பொற்றுகில் அகல ...) and the plea is one line at the end (நொந்தெய்த்திடலாமோ). பருப்பதப்ரிய குறுமுனி is Agastya, here a worshipper, not named as Murugan's pupil.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:244"},"p:1139":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1139,"t":["உ லகத்தினில் மாதரு மைந்தரும்","உ றுசுற்றமும் வாழ்வொடு றுங்கிளை","உ யர்துக்கமு மோடுற வென்றுற ...... வருகாலன்","உ திரத்துட னேசல மென்பொடு","உ றுதிப்பட வேவள ருங்குடில்","உ திரக்கனல் மீதுற என்றனை ...... யொழியாமுன்","கலகக்கலை நூல்பல கொண்டெதிர்","கதறிப்பத றாவுரை வென்றுயர்","கயவர்க்குள னாய்வினை நெஞ்சொடு ...... களிகூருங்","கவலைப்புல மோடுற என்துயர்","கழிவித்துன தாளினை யன்பொடு","கருதித்தொழும் வாழ்வது தந்திட ...... நினைவாயே","இலகப்பதி னாலுல கங்களும்","இருளைக்கடி வானெழு மம்புலி","யெழில்மிக்கிட வேணியில் வந்துற ...... எருதேறி","இருகைத்தல மான்மழு வும்புனை","யிறையப்பதி யாகிய இன்சொலன்","இசையப்பரிவோடினி தன்றரு ...... ளிளையோனே","மலைபட்டிரு கூறெழ வன்கடல்","நிலைகெட்டபி தாவென அஞ்சகர்","வலியற்றசு ரேசரு மங்கிட ...... வடிவேலால்","மலைவித்தக வானவ ரிந்திரர்","மலர்கைக்கொடு மாதவ ருந்தொழ","வடிவுற்றொரு தோகையில் வந்தருள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks that before Death comes saying women, sons, kin, life and clan are ended and his body of blood, water and bone is put on the fire, Murugan remove the grief of his anxious senses and his place among base men who rise by shouting down rivals with quarrelsome books, and give him a life of worshipping his feet with love. The praise names Siva who rides the bull with moon in his locks and deer and axe in his hands as the father who graced his younger son, and Murugan who split the mountain and came on the peacock.","n":"கலகக் கலை நூல் பல கொண்டு எதிர் கதறி ... வென்று உயர் கயவர் is a sharp picture of learning used to win debates, which the poet counts as baseness. Death is காலன், and the body is the hut grown from blood, water and bone (குடில்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","yaakkai","thiruvadi","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:244"},"p:1140":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1140,"t":["உ றவின்முறை கதறியழ ஊராரு மாசையற","பறைதிமிலை முழவினிசை யாகாச மீதுமுற","உ லகிலுள பலரரிசி வாய்மீதி லேசொரியு ...... மந்தநாளில்","உ னதுமுக கருணைமல ரோராறு மாறிருகை","திரள்புயமு மெழில்பணிகொள் வார்காது நீள்விழியும்","உ பயபத மிசைகுலவு சீரேறு நூபுரமும் ...... அந்தமார்பும்","மறையறைய அமரர்தரு பூமாரியேசொரிய","மதுவொழுகு தரவில்மணி மீதேமு நூலொளிர","மயிலின்மிசை யழகுபொலி யாளாய்மு னாரடியர் ...... வந்துகூட","மறலிபடை யமபுரமு மீதோட வேபொருது","விருதுபல முறைமுறையி லேயூதி வாதுசெய்து","மதலையொரு குதலையடி நாயேனை யாளஇஙன் ...... வந்திடாயோ","பிறையெயிறு முரணசுரர் பேராது பாரில்விழ","அதிரஎழு புவியுலக மீரேழு மோலமிட","பிடிகளிறி னடல்நிரைகள் பாழாக வேதிசையில் ...... நின்றநாகம்","பிரியநெடு மலையிடிய மாவாரிதூளியெழ","பெரியதொரு வயிறுடைய மாகாளி கூளியொடு","பிணநிணமு முணவுசெய்து பேயோடு மாடல்செய ...... வென்றதீரா","குறமறவர் கொடியடிகள் கூசாது போய்வருட","கரடிபுலி திரிகடிய வாரான கானில்மிகு","குளிர்கணியி னிளமரம தேயாகி நீடியுயர் ...... குன்றுலாவி","கொடியதொரு முயலகனின் மீதாடு வாருடைய","வொருபுறம துறவளரு மாதாபெ றாவருள்செய்","குமரகுரு பரஅமரர் வானாடர் பேணஅருள் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet pictures his funeral day, kin wailing, the town giving up hope, drums rising to the sky and many dropping rice into the dead man's mouth, and asks Murugan to come then with his six faces and twelve arms on the peacock, with Vedas sounding, gods raining flowers and his old devotees gathered, to rout Death's army and take him, a prattling dog-like servant. The praise names the victory where Kali and the ghosts feasted, Murugan's wandering in Valli's forest where he became a young tree, and his birth from the mother on Siva's side who bore him without bearing.","n":"The funeral is told with its rites (பறை, திமிலை, முழவு, அரிசி வாய்மீது), and the plea answers it with a procession of its own, Murugan's arrival. மறலி படை யமபுரமும் மீது ஓட வே பொருது makes the rescue a battle against Death. மாதா பெறா, the mother who bore without bearing, is Uma, since Murugan came from Siva's eyes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","saakkaadu","sutram","adiyaar"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:244"},"p:1141":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1141,"t":["உ றவு சிங்கிகள் காமா காரிகள்","முறைம சங்கிக ளாசா வேசிகள்","உ தடு கன்றிகள் நாணா வீணிகள் ...... நகரேகை","உ டைய கொங்கையின் மீதே தூசிகள்","பிணமே னும்படி பேய்நீ ராகிய","உ ணவை யுண்டுடை சோர்கோ மாளிகள் ...... கடல்ஞாலத்","தறவு நெஞ்சுபொ லாமா பாவிகள்","வறுமை தந்திடு பாழ்மூ தேவிகள்","அணிநெ ருங்கிக ளாசா பாஷண ...... மடமாதர்","அழகு யர்ந்தபொய் மாயா ரூபிகள்","கலவி யின்பமெ னாவே சோருதல்","அலம லந்தடு மாறா தோர்கதி ...... யருள்வாயே","பறவை யென்கிற கூடார் மூவரண்","முறையி டுந்தமர் வானோர் தேரரி","பகழி குன்றவி லாலே நீறெழ ...... வொ முவர்","பதநி னைந்துவி டாதே தாள் பெற","அருள்பு ரிந்தபி ரானார் மாபதி","பரவு கந்தசு வாமீ கானக ...... மதின்மேவுங்","குறவர் தங்கள்பி ரானே மாமரம்","நெறுநெ றென்றடி வேரோ டேநிலை","குலைய வென்றிகொள் வேலே யேவிய ...... புயவீரா","குயில்க ளன்றில்கள் கூகூ கூவென","மலர்கள் பொங்கிய தேன்வீழ் காமிசை","குறவர் சுந்தரியோடே கூடிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet heaps abuse on courtesans as lustful, deceitful, shameless, drunk on liquor till their clothes slip, wicked sinners, goddesses of misfortune who bring poverty and false shapes of illusion, and asks for a state in which he is not tossed about in the pleasure of union. The praise names Siva who burned the flying cities with Vishnu as arrow and granted his feet to three who kept him in mind, and Murugan who cut the mango tree and joined the hunter girl in a grove where cuckoos and anril birds call.","n":"வறுமை தந்திடு பாழ் மூதேவிகள், 'ruinous Misfortune-goddesses who bring poverty', ties the women to loss of wealth, and பேய் நீராகிய உணவு to drink. The abuse is a list of plural nouns ending in -கள் with no verb until the plea.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","kal","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:244"},"p:1142":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1142,"t":["ஊனோடு வாதுயிர் தரித்து மட்டற","வூசாடு பாழ்குடி லெடுத்த திற்படி","ஓயாத மாமய லுழற்றி னிற்படு ...... வம்பனேனை","ஊதாரியாய்விடு சமத்தில் நிற்பது","மாராத காதலை மனத்தில் வைப்பது","மூரோடு போயெதிர் பிணக்கி னிற்பது ...... முந்திடாதே","தேனூறு வாய்மொழி பரத்தை யர்க்கொரு","நாய்போல வேயவர் வசத்தில் நிற்பது","சீர்கேட தாய்விடு சிறுப்பி ளைத்தன ...... மென்றுநீபச்","சீதாள மாமலர் தொடுத்த பத்தர்கள்","சீராடி நாண்மல ரெனப்ரியப்படு","சீர்பாத போதக மநுக்ர கிப்பது ...... மெந்தநாளோ","மானாக பாயலில் படுக்கை யிட்டவர்","மாமேரு வாரியில் திரித்து விட்டவர்","மாடோ டு போய்வரு மிடைக்குலத்தவ ...... ரன்றுவாவி","வாய்நாக மோலிட பிடித்த சக்கிர","வாளேவி யேகர வினைத்த றித்தவர்","மாமாய னாயுல களித்த வித்தகர் ...... தங்கைவாழ்வே","கானாரு மாமலை தினைப்பு னத்தினில்","கால்மேல்வி ழாவொரு குறச்சி றுக்கியை","காணாது போயியல் புணர்ச்சி யிட்டருள் ...... கந்தவேளே","காரேழு மாமலை யிடித்து ருக்கெட","காராழி யேழவை கலக்கி விட்டுயர்","காவான நாடர்கள் பகைச்ச வட்டிய ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses that in this ruined, swaying hut of flesh he has been a wastrel driven by infatuation, nursing endless desire, quarrelling through the town, and standing like a dog in the power of honey-tongued courtesans, and asks when the day will come that Murugan grants the feet that devotees adorn with kadamba flowers. The praise names Vishnu who sleeps on the serpent, churned the sea with Meru, herded cattle and hurled the discus to cut down the crocodile when the elephant cried out, Murugan as his sister's treasure, who fell at the feet of the hunter girl in the millet field, and the lord who shattered the seven mountains and stirred the seven seas.","n":"பரத்தையர்க்கு ஒரு நாய்போலவே அவர் வசத்தில் நிற்பது: the courtesans are named by the Tamil word, and the poet's image for himself is a dog. சிறுப்பிள்ளைத்தனம், childishness, sums up the life. The elephant and crocodile are the Gajendra story (வாய் நாகம் ஓலமிட ... கர வினைத் தறித்தவர்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","mayal","yaakkai","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:245"},"p:1143":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1143,"t":["எட்டுட னொருதொளை வாயா யதுபசு","மட்கல மிருவினை தோயா மிகுபிணி","யிட்டிடை செயவொரு போதா கிலுமுயிர் ...... நிலையாக","எப்படி யுயர்கதி நாமே றுவதென","எட்பகி ரினுமிது வோரார் தமதம","திச்சையி னிடருறு பேரா சைகொள்கட ...... லதிலேவீழ்","முட்டர்க ணெறியினில் வீழா தடலொடு","முப்பதி னறுபதின் மேலா மறுவரு","முற்றுத லறிவரு ஞானோ தயவொளி ...... வெளியாக","முக்குண மதுகெட நானா வெனவரு","முத்திரை யழிதர ஆரா வமுதன","முத்தமிழ் தெரிகனி வாயா லருளுவ ...... தொருநாளே","திட்டென எதிர்வரு மாகா ளியினொடு","திக்கிட தரிகிட தீதோ மெனவொரு","சித்திர வெகுவித வாதா டியபத ...... மலராளன்","செப்புக வெனமுன மோதா துணர்வது","சிற்சுக பரவெளி யீதே யெனஅவர்","தெக்ஷண செவிதனி லேபோ தனையருள் ...... குருநாதா","மட்டற அமர்பொரு சூரா திபனுடல்","பொட்டெழ முடுகிவை வேலா லெறிதரு","மற்புய மரகத மாதோ கையில்நட ...... மிடுவோனே","வச்சிர கரதல வானோ ரதிபதி","பொற்புறு கரிபரிதேரோ டழகுற","வைத்திடு மருமக னேவா ழமரர்கள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet calls the body a raw clay pot with nine openings, soaked in two kinds of deed and disease, and asks that, not falling into the path of fools who never think how to reach the high state but sink in the sea of craving, he be taught one day from Murugan's Tamil-knowing mouth the dawning light of knowledge beyond the thirty-six, in which the three qualities and the seal of 'I' are destroyed. The praise names Siva who danced against Kali and asked to be told, and Murugan who whispered into his right ear that this was the supreme space of the bliss of consciousness, and Indra's son-in-law who killed Sura with the vel.","n":"முப்பதின் அறுபதின் மேலாம் may count the thirty-six tattvas and what lies beyond them, and நான் ஆ என வரு முத்திரை is the stamp of the ego; both readings depend on how the syllables split. The guru story has a new detail: the teaching is given into Siva's right ear (தெக்ஷண செவி), and what is taught is சிற்சுக பரவெளி, the space of conscious bliss, not Om by name.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","gnaanam","guru","thaththuvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:245"},"p:1144":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1144,"t":["எத்தி யிருகுழையை மோதி மீனமதின்","முட்டி யிடறியம தூதர் போல முகி","லெட்டி வயவர்கர வாளை வேல்முனையை ...... யெதிர்சீறி","எத்தி சையினுமொரு காம ராஜன்மிக","வெற்றி யரசுதனை யாள வீசியட","லெற்றி யிளைஞருயிர் கோலு நீலவிழி ...... மடமாதர்","வித்தை தனிலுருகி யாசை யாகியவர்","கைக்குள் மருவுபொரு ளான ஆகும்வரை","மெத்தை தனிலுருகி மோக மாகிவிட ...... அதன்மேலே","வெட்க மிலைநடவு மேகு மேகுமினி","மற்ற வரையழையு மாத ரேயெனமுன்","விட்ட படிறிகள்தம் நேச ஆசைகெட ...... அருள்வாயே","ஒத்த வரிகமுகு வாளை தாவுபுனல்","அத்தி நகரமர சான வாள்நிருபன்","ஒக்கு நினைவுமுனி லாமல் வாகுபெல ...... நிலைகூற","உ ற்ற தருமனடல் வீமன் வேல்விசையன்","வெற்றி நகுலசக தேவர் தேர்தனிலும்","ஒத்து முடுகிவிடு பாகன் வாளமரி...... லசுரேசன்","பத்து முடிகள்துக ளாக வாகுஇரு","பத்து மொருகணையில் வீழ நேரவுணர்","பட்டு மடியஅமர் மோது காளமுகில் ...... மருகோனே","பச்சை மயிலில்வரு வீர வேல்முருக","துட்ட நிருதர்குல கால வானவர்கள்","பத்தி யுடனடியில் வீழ வாழ்வுதவு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes courtesans whose dark eyes, like Death's messengers, warriors' swords and the vel, take young men's lives for Kama's kingdom, how he melted in their arts until the wealth in his hand was gone, and how they then said 'no shame? go, go now, mother, call the others'; he asks that his love for these deceivers end. The praise names Krishna, charioteer of Dharma, Bhima, Arjuna, Nakula and Sahadeva against the king of Hastinapura, and Rama who felled Ravana's ten heads and twenty arms with one arrow, as Murugan's uncle.","n":"The dismissal is given in the courtesans' own words (வெட்கமிலை நட ஏகும் ஏகும் இனி மற்றவரை அழையும் மாதரே), so the turn comes exactly when the money is gone (பொருளான ஆகும் வரை). படிறிகள், deceivers, is the poet's last word for them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","kan","vanjam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:245"},"p:1145":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1145,"t":["ஒக்க வண்டெழு கொண்டைகு லைந்திட","வெற்பெ னுங்கன கொங்கைகு ழைந்திட","உ ற்ப லங்கள்சி வந்துக விந்திட ....... ; ந்த்ரகோபம்","ஒத்த தொண்டைது வண்டமு தந்தர","மெச்சு தும்பிக ருங்குயில் மென்புற","வொக்க மென்தொனி வந்துபி றந்திட ...... அன்புகூர","மிக்க சந்திர னொன்றுநி லங்களில்","விக்ர மஞ்செய்தி லங்குந கம்பட","மெத்த மென்பொரு ளன்பள வுந்துவ ...... ளின்பமாதர்","வித்த கந்தரு விந்துத புங்குழி","பட்ட ழிந்துந லங்குகு ரம்பையை","விட்ட கன்றுநி னம்புய மென்பத ...... மென்றுசேர்வேன்","மைக்க ருங்கட லன்றெரிமண்டிட","மெய்க்ர வுஞ்சசி லம்புடல் வெம்பிட","மற்று நன்பதி குன்றிய ழிந்திட ...... வும்பர்நாடன்","வச்சி ரங்கைய ணிந்துப தம்பெற","மெச்சு குஞ்சரிகொங்கைபு யம்பெற","மத்த வெஞ்சின வஞ்சகர் தங்களை ...... நுங்கும்வேலா","குக்கு டங்கொடி கொண்டப ரம்பர","சக்ர மண்டல மெண்டிசை யம்புகழ்","கொட்க கொன்றைய ணிந்தசி ரஞ்சர ...... ணங்கிகாரா","கொத்த விழ்ந்தக டம்பலர் தங்கிய","மிக்க வங்கண கங்கண திண்புய","கொற்ற வங்குற மங்கைவி ரும்பிய ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes union, loosened hair, pressed breasts, reddened eyes, lips like the red indragopa insect, coos like the cuckoo and the dove, nail marks, and asks when he will leave this hut of a body, worn out in that pit, and reach Murugan's lotus feet. The praise tells how the sea burned and the Krauncha hill was split, how Indra regained his place and Devayanai her bridegroom, and names Murugan with the rooster banner, wearing kadamba and loved by the hunter girl.","n":"குரம்பை, a hut, for the body, is the word that turns the scene: the poet asks to leave it (விட்டு அகன்று) for the feet. One line is garbled (see source issues).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","yaakkai","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:245"},"p:1146":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1146,"t":["ஓது வித்தவர் கூலிகொ டாதவர்","மாத வர்க்கதி பாதக மானவர்","ஊச லிற்கன லாயெரிகாளையர் ...... மறையோர்கள்","ஊர்த னக்கிட ரேசெயு மேழைகள்","ஆர்த னக்குமு தாசின தாரிகள்","ஓடி யுத்தம ரூதிய நாடின ...... ரிரவோருக்","கேது மித்தனை தானமி டாதவர்","பூத லத்தினி லோரம தானவர்","ஈசர் விஷ்ணுவை சேவைசெய் வோர்தமை ...... யிகழ்வோர்கள்","ஏக சித்ததி யானமி லாதவர்","மோக முற்றிடு போகித மூறினர்","ஈன ரித்தனை பேர்களு மேழ்நர ...... குழல்வாரே","தாத தத்தத தாதத தாதத","தூது துத்துது தூதுது தூதுது","சாச சச்சச சாசச சாசச ...... சசசாச","தாட டட்டட டாடட டாடட","டூடு டுட்டுடு டூடுடு டூடுடு","தாடி டிட்டிடி டீடிடி டீடிடி ...... டிடிடீடீ","தீதி தித்திதி தீதிதி தீதிதி","தோதி குத்திகு தோதிகு தோதிகு","சேகு செக்குகு சேகுகு சேகுகு ...... செகுசேகு","சேயெ னப்பல ராடிட மாகலை","ஆயு முத்தமர் கூறிடும் வாசக","சேகு சித்திர மாக நிணாடிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The song lists sinners: those who do not pay the teacher who taught them, who wrong great ascetics, Brahmins who harm their own town, those who give nothing to the beggar, who are partial, who mock those who serve Siva and Vishnu, who have no single-minded meditation, who are steeped in lust; all of them, it says, will suffer in the seven hells. The praise is almost wholly drum syllables (தாதத, தூதுது, சாசச, டுட்டுடு ...) for Murugan's dance, praised by the learned.","n":"There is no first-person plea: the first half is a moral catalogue ending in ஏழ் நரகு உழல்வாரே. ஈசர் விஷ்ணுவை சேவை செய்வோர் protects the devotees of both Siva and Vishnu. Several items in the list (ஊசலிற் கனலாய் எரி காளையர், ஆர் தனக்கும் உதாசின தாரிகள்) are hard to construe, so the list is only roughly read.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naragam","eegai","nindhai","thiyaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:245"},"p:1147":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1147,"t":["ஓலைத ரித்தகு ழைக்கு மப்புற","மோடிநி றத்தும தர்த்து நெய்த்தற","லோதிநி ழற்குள ளிக்கு லத்துட ...... னொன்றிஞானம்","ஓதிமி குத்தத வத்த வர்க்கிட","ரோகைசெ லுத்திவ டுப்ப டுத்தகி","யூடுவி டத்தையி ருத்தி வைத்தக ...... ணம்பினாலே","மாலைம யக்கைவி ளைத்து நற்பொருள்","வாசமு லைக்குள கப்ப டுத்தியில்","வாவென முற்றிந டத்தி யுட்புகு ...... மந்தமாதர்","மாயம யக்கையொ ழித்து மெத்தென","வானவ ருக்கரு ளுற்ற அக்ஷர","வாய்மையெ னக்குமி னித்த ளித்தருள் ...... தந்திடாதோ","வேலைய டைக்க அரிக்கு லத்தொடு","வேணுமெ னச்சொலு மக்க ணத்தினில்","வேகமொ டப்பும லைக்கு லத்தைந ...... ளன்கைமேலே","வீசஅ வற்றினை யொப்ப மிட்டணை","மேவிய ரக்கர்ப திக்குள் முற்பட","வீடண னுக்கருள் வைத்த வற்றமை ...... யன்கள்மாளக்","காலயி லக்கணை தொட்ட ருட்கன","மாலமை திக்கரை யிற்ற ரித்துல","காளஅ ளித்தப்ர புத்வ ருட்கடல் ...... தந்தகாமன்","காயமொ ழித்தவர் பெற்ற கொற்றவ","நானில வித்ததி னைப்பு னத்தொரு","காதல்மி குத்துமி கப்ர மித்தருள் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes courtesans whose eyes reach their palm-leaf earrings, who trouble even ascetics learned in wisdom with the poison set in their eye-arrows, catch good wealth at their fragrant breasts and lead a man in with 'come', and asks that their illusion be ended and he be given the true akshara that was given to the gods. The praise tells how Nala threw mountains into the sea for Rama's bridge, Vibhishana received grace and the demons died, names Siva who destroyed Kama's body, and Murugan who fell in love in the millet field.","n":"ஞானம் ஓதி மிகுத்த தவத்தவர்க்கு இடர் ஓகை செலுத்தி: the women's eyes threaten even ascetics, a point the confession uses against itself. வானவருக்கு அருளுற்ற அக்ஷர வாய்மை may mean an imperishable truth or a sacred syllable; I cannot tell which.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","kan","thavam","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:245"},"p:1148":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1148,"t":["கடைசி வந்தகன் றுரைபு கன்றிரு","குழையை யுந்துரந் தரிப ரந்தொளிர்","கரிய கண்துறந் தவர்நி றந்தொளை ...... படவோடக்","கலைநெ கிழ்ந்திருங் குழல்ச ரிந்திட","முலைசு மந்தசைந் திடையொ சிந்துயிர்","கவர இங்கிதங் கெறுவி தம்பெற ...... விளையாடும்","படைம தன்பெருங் கிளைதி ருந்திய","அதர கிஞ்சுகந் தனையு ணர்ந்தணி","பணிநி தம்பஇன் பசுக முந்தர ...... முதிர்காம","பரவ சந்தணிந் துனையு ணர்ந்தொரு","மவுன பஞ்சரம் பயில்த ருஞ்சுக","பதம டைந்திருந் தருள்பொ ருந்தும ...... தொருநாளே","வடநெ டுஞ்சிலம் புகள்பு லம்பிட","மகித லம்ப்ரியங் கொடும கிழ்ந்திட","வருபு ரந்தரன் தனபு ரம்பெற ...... முதுகோப","மகர வெங்கருங் கடலொ டுங்கிட","நிசிச ரன்பெருங் குலமொ ருங்கிற","வனச னின்றழும் படிநெ ருங்கிய ...... வொருசூதம்","அடியொ டும்பிடுங் கியத டங்கர","வடிவ அஞ்சுரும் புறவி ரும்பிய","அடவி யுந்தொழும் பொடுதொ ழும்படி ...... யநுராக","அவச மும்புனைந் தறமு னைந்தெழு","பருவ தஞ்சிறந் தகன தந்தியின்","அமுத மென்குயங் களின்மு யங்கிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women whose eyes reach their earrings, their loosened cloth and hair, their lips and play, and asks whether one day the frenzy of ripe lust will subside and, knowing Murugan, he will reach the blissful state practised in the cage of silence and abide in grace. The praise tells how the northern mountains wailed, the earth rejoiced, Indra regained his city, the sea shrank and Sura's mango tree was torn up by the root, and names Murugan who embraced the daughter raised by the elephant.","n":"மவுன பஞ்சரம், the cage of silence, turns the image of a caged bird into the goal: the silence where knowing Murugan (உனை உணர்ந்து) brings bliss (சுக பதம்). The plea grows directly out of lust subsiding (காம பரவசம் தணிந்து).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","mounam","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:245"},"p:1149":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1149,"t":["கதறிய கலைகொடு சுட்டாத் தீர்பொருள்","பதறிய சமயிக ளெட்டாப் பேரொளி","கருவற இருவினை கெட்டாற் காண்வரு ...... மென்றஏகங்","கருகிய வினைமன துட்டாக் காதது","சுருதிக ளுருகியொர் வட்டாய்த் தோய்வது","கசடற முழுதையும் விட்டாற் சேர்வது ...... ணர்ந்திடாதே","விதமது கரமுரல் மொட்டாற் சாடிய","ரதிபதி யெனவரு துட்டாத் மாவுடன்","வினைபுரிபவரிடு முற்றாச் சாலிரு ...... புண்டாணக","ம்ருகமத முகுளித மொட்டாற் கார்முக","நுதலெழு தியசிறு பொட்டாற் சாயக","விரகுடை விழிவலை பட்டாற் றாதுந ...... லங்கலாமோ","பதமலர் மிசைகழல் கட்டாப் பாலக","சுருதிக ளடிதொழ எட்டாத் தேசிக","பருகென வனமுலை கிட்டாத் தாரகை ...... தந்துநாளும்","பரிவுற வெகுமுக நெட்டாற் று஡டொரு","படுகையி னிடைபுழு வெட்டாப் பாசடை","படர்வன பரிமள முட்டாட் டாமரை ...... தங்கிவாழுஞ்","சததள அமளியை விட்டாற் றேறிய","சலநிதி குறுகிட வொட்டாச் சூரொடு","தமனிய குலகிரிபொட்டாய்த் தூளெழ ...... வென்றகோவே","தழைதரு குழைதரு பட்டாட் சாலவு","மழகிய கலவிதெ விட்டாக் காதலி","தலைமக நிலமடி தட்டாத் தேவர்கள் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of the One that the clamouring arts cannot point to and the warring sects cannot reach, the great light seen when birth and the two deeds end, which the Vedas melt toward and which is reached by letting go of everything, and confesses that, not knowing it, he is caught in the net of women's eyes, musk and brow-marks; he asks whether he must suffer. The praise names Murugan as the child with anklets whose feet the Vedas worship, nursed by the stars, born on the hundred-petalled lotus bed in the pond, who crushed Sura and the golden mountain, lord of the gods.","n":"The opening is a negative theology: ஏகம் is what the பதறிய சமயிகள் cannot reach and is found only by leaving all (முழுதையும் விட்டால் சேர்வது). The praise's pond and lotus (பாசடை, டாமரை, சததள அமளி) seem to describe the birth in the Saravana pool, though the pool is not named; one word is corrupt.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["samayam","oli","thuravu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:245"},"p:1150":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1150,"t":["கலவியி னலமுரை யாமட வார்சந்","தனத் தனங்களில் வசம தாகி யவரவர்","பாதாதி கேச மளவும் பாடுங்","கவிஞ னாய்த்திரிவேனைக் காமக் ரோதத் ...... தூர்த்தனை யபராதக்","கபடனை வெகுபரிதாபனை நாளும்","ப்ரமிக்கு நெஞ்சனை உ ருவ மாறி முறை முறை","ஆசார வீன சமயந் தோறுங்","களவு சாத்திர மோதிச் சாதித் தேனைச் ...... சாத்திர நெறிபோயைம்","புலன்வழி யொழுகிய மோகனை மூகந்","தனிற் பிறந் தொரு நொடியின் மீள அழிதரு","மாதேச வாழ்வை நிலையென் றேயம்","புவியின் மேற்பசு பாசத் தேபட் டேனைப் ...... பூக்கழ லிணைசேரப்","பொறியிலி தனையதி பாவியை நீடுங்","குணத் ரயங்களும் வரும நேக வினைகளு","மாயா விகார முழுதுஞ் சாடும்","பொருளின் மேற்சிறி தாசைப் பாடற் றேனைக் ...... காப்பது மொருநாளே","குலகிரிதருமபி ராம மயூரம்","ப்ரியப் படும்படி குவளை வாச மலர்கொடு","வாரா வுலாவி யுணரும் யோகங்","குலைய வீக்கிய வேளைக் கோபித் தேறப் ...... பார்த்தரு ளியபார்வைக்","குரிசிலு மொருசுரர் பூசுர னோமென்","றதற் கநந்தர மிரணி யாய நமவென","நாராய ணாய நமவென் றோதுங்","குதலை வாய்ச்சிறி யோனுக் காகத் தூணிற் ...... றோற்றிய வசபாணிப்","பலநக நுதியி னிசாசர னாகங்","கிழித் தளைந்தணி துளசி யோடு சிறுகுடல்","தோண்மாலை யாக அணியுங் கோவும்","பரவி வாழ்த்திட வேகற்றாரச் சோதிப் ...... பாற்பணி யிறைவாகைப்","படமுக வடலயி ராபத மேறும்","ப்ரபுப் பயங்கெட வடப ராரை வரைகெட","வேலேவி வாவி மகரஞ் சீறும்","பரவை கூப்பிட மோதிச் சூர்கெட் டோ டத் ...... தாக்கிய பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet accuses himself at length: he was a poet who sang women's bodies from foot to head, a lustful, angry rogue, a deceiver, unsettled, who argued for shastras in every sect without discipline, followed the five senses, took this passing life as lasting and was caught in the bonds of the bound soul; he asks to be guarded and brought to the flowered feet, having at least a little desire for the reality that destroys the qualities, deeds and maya. The praise names Siva who burned Kama with a look when he broke his yoga, Narasimha who came from the pillar for the child who said 'namo Narayana', and Murugan who freed Indra from fear and drove off Sura.","n":"பாதாதிகேசம் அளவும் பாடும் கவிஞனாய்த் திரிவேனை: the poet confesses the very genre of praising women limb by limb, which his own confessions resemble. ஆசாரவீன சமயம் தோறும் களவு சாத்திரம் ஓதி may mean he argued false doctrines in every sect; the phrase is uncertain, as is மூகம் தனிற் பிறந்து.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kutram","samayam","paasam","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:245"},"p:1151":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1151,"t":["கறுத்து நீவிடு கூர்வேலி னுங்கடை","சிவத்து நீடிய வாய்மீன வொண்குழை","கடக்க வோடிய ஆலால நஞ்சன ...... வஞ்சநீடு","கயற்க ணார்கனி வாயூற லுண்டணி","கழுத்து மாகமு மேகீப வங்கொடு","கலக்க மார்பக பாடீர குங்கும ...... கொங்கைமீதே","உ றுத்து மாரமு மோகாவ டங்களு","மருத்து நேரிய கூர்வாள்ந கம்பட","உ டுத்த ஆடையும் வேறாயு ழன்றுக ...... ழன்றுவீழ","உ ருக்கு நாபியின் மூழ்காம ருங்கிடை","செருக்கு மோகன வாராத ரங்களை","யொழிக்க வோர்வகை காணேனு றுந்துணை ...... யொன்றுகாணேன்","நிறத்த நூபுர பாதார விந்தமு","முடுத்த பீலியும் வாரார்த னங்களும்","நிறத்தி லேபடு வேலான கண்களும் ...... வண்டுபாட","நெறித்த வோதியு மாயான்ம னம்பர","தவிக்க மால்தர லாமோ கலந்திட","நினைக்க லாமென வேல்வேடர் கொம்புட ...... னண்புகூர்வாய்","மறித்த வாரிதி கோகோவெ னும்படி","வெறுத்த ராவணன் வாணாளை யம்பினில்","வதைத்த மாமனு மேவார்பு ரங்கனல் ...... மண்டமேரு","வளைத்த தாதையு மாறான குன்றமு","மனைத்து லோகமும் வேதாக மங்களும்","மதித்த சேவக வானாளு மும்பர்கள் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women with eyes darker than a vel, drinking their lips, embracing them, nail marks and slipping cloth, and confesses that he sees no way to end it and no companion to help. The praise turns the same gaze on Valli, her anklets, peacock-feather dress, breasts and spear-like eyes that drove Murugan to longing until he joined her, and names Rama who killed Ravana, Siva who bent Meru as a bow, and Murugan as the servant-hero honoured by both.","n":"The second half describes Valli's body in the same terms as the courtesans' (கண்கள், தனங்கள், ஓதி), and has the Lord's mind tremble (மனம் பரதவிக்க), so the desire the poet cannot end is shown sanctified in Murugan's love. ஒன்றுகாணேன் twice, 'I see none', is the plea's whole force. Who is மாயான் whose mind is troubled (line 12) is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:245"},"p:1152":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1152,"t":["குறிப்பரிய குழற்குமதி நுதற்புருவ விலுக்குமிரு","குழைக்கும்வடு விழிக்குமெழு ...... குமிழாலுங்","கொடிப்பவள இதழ்க்குமிகு சுடர்த்தரள நகைக்குமமு","தினுக்குமிக வுறத்தழுவு ...... குறியாலும்","அறப்பெரிய தனக்குமன நடைக்குமினி னிடைக்குமல","ரடிக்குமிள நகைக்குமுள ...... மயராதே","அகத்தியனொ டுரைத்தபொரு ளளித்தருளி அரிப்பிரமர்","அளப்பரிய பதக்கமல ...... மருள்வாயே","கறுத்தடரு மரக்கரணி கருக்குலைய நெருக்கியொரு","கணத்திலவர் நிணத்தகுடல் ...... கதிர்வேலாற்","கறுத்தருளி யலக்கணுறு சுரர்க்கவர்கள் பதிக்குரிமை","யளித்திடரை யறுத்தருளு ...... மயில்வீரா","செறுத்துவரு கரித்திரள்கள் திடுக்கிடவல் மருப்பையரி","சினத்தினொடு பறித்தமர்செய் ...... பெருகானிற்","செலக்கருதி யறக்கொடிய சிலைக்குறவர் கொடித்தனது","சிமிழ்த்தனமு னுறத்தழுவு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet runs through a woman's hair, brow, ears, eyes, nose, lips, teeth, breasts, gait, waist and feet, and asks that his heart not weaken at them, and that Murugan grant the meaning he told Agastya and the lotus feet that Hari and Brahma could not measure. The praise names the vel that killed the demons and restored the gods to their cities, and Murugan who embraced the hunter girl in the forest where the hunters wrest the tusks from elephant herds.","n":"அகத்தியனொடு உரைத்த பொருள் makes Murugan Agastya's teacher; the text gives the 'meaning', not Tamil by name. The catalogue of the woman's parts in -க்கும் is answered by the feet that cannot be measured (அளப்பரிய பதக்கமலம்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","guru","gnaanam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:245"},"p:1153":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1153,"t":["குனகியொரு மயில்போல வாராம னோலீலை","விளையவினை நினையாம லேயேகி மீளாத","கொடியமன தநியாய மாபாத காபோதி ...... யெனஆசைக்","கொளுவ அதில் மயலாகி வீறொடு போய்நீள","மலரமளி தனிலேறி யாமாறு போமாறு","குலவிநல மொழிகூறி வாரேறு பூணார ...... முநல்முழ்கி","மனமுருக மதராஜ கோலாடு மாபூசல்","விளையவிழி சுழலாடி மேலோதி போய்மீள","மதிவதன மொளிவீச நீராள மாய்மேவி ...... யநுராக","வகைவகையி லதிமோக வாராழி யூடான","பொருளளவ தளவாக யாரோடு மாலான","வனிதையர்கள் வசமாய நாயேனு மீடேற ...... அருள்வாயே","எனதுமொழி வழுவாமல் நீயேகு கான்மீதி","லெனவிரகு குலையாத மாதாவு நேரோத","இசையுமொழி தவறாம லேயேகி மாமாது ...... மிளையோனும்","இனிமையொடு வருமாய மா஡ணச மானாவி","குலையவரு கரதூஷ ணாவீரர் போர்மாள","இறுகிநெடு மரமேழு தூளாக வேவாலி ...... யுயிர்சீறி","அநுமனொடு கவிகூட வாராக நீராழி","யடைசெய்தணை தனிலேறி மாபாவி யூர்மேவி","அவுணர்கிளை கெடநூறி யாலால மாகோப ...... நிருதேசன்","அருணமணி திகழ்பார வீராக ராமோலி","யொருபதுமொர் கணைவீழ வேமோது போராளி","அடல்மருக குமரேச மேலாய வானோர்கள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet tells how, caught by peacock-like women's games in an unrighteous mind, he climbed on their flowered bed and drowned in a sea of passion, and asks that even he, a dog in their power, be raised. The praise tells the Ramayana: Rama going to the forest at his mother's word with Sita and Lakshmana, the golden deer Maricha, Khara and Dushana, the seven trees and Vali, Hanuman and the bridge, and the single arrow that felled Ravana's ten heads, with Murugan as his nephew.","n":"The poet calls himself கொடிய மனது அநியாய மாபாதகா போதி, a great sinner of crooked mind. The Ramayana is told in order and at unusual length, but as stock praise of the uncle. The word for the deer is corrupt (see source issues).","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kaamam","mayal","punarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:245"},"p:1154":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1154,"t":["கொலைவிழி சுழலச் சுழலச் சிலைநுதல் குவியக் குவியக்","கொடியிடை துவளத் துவளத் ...... தனபாரக்","குறியணி சிதறச் சிதறக் கரவளை கதறக் கதறக்","குயில்மொழி பதறப் பதறப் ...... ப்ரியமோகக்","கலவியி லொருமித் தொருமித் திலவிதழ் பருகிப் பருகிக்","கரமொடு தழுவித் தழுவிச் ...... சிலநாளிற்","கையிலுள பொருள்கெட் டருள்கெட் டனைவரும் விடுசிச் சியெனக்","கடியொரு செயலுற் றுலகிற் ...... றிரிவேனோ","சலநிதி சுவறச் சவறத் திசைநிலை பெயரப் பெயரத்","தடவரை பிதிரப் பிதிரத் ...... திடமேருத்","தமனிய நெடுவெற் பதிரப் பணிமணி சிரம்விட் டகலச்","சமனுடல் கிழியக் கிழியப் ...... பொருசூரன்","பெலமது குறையக் குறையக் கருவிகள் பறையப் பறையப்","பிறநரிதொடரத் தொடரத் ...... திரள்கூகை","பெடையொடு குழறக் குழறச் சுரபதி பரவப் பரவப்","ப்ரபையயில் தொடுநற் குமரப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes union in doubled words, eyes rolling, waist bending, bangles crying out, lips drunk again and again, and how after a few days, his wealth and good name gone, everyone drove him off with 'chi, chi', and asks whether he must wander the world. The praise is a battle in doubled words, the sea drying, mountains shattering, Sura's strength failing and jackals and owls following, ending with the vel.","n":"The doubling (சுழலச் சுழல, குவியக் குவிய ...) runs through both halves, so the union and the war share one rhythm. கையிலுள பொருள்கெட்டு அருள்கெட்டு is the turn: money and grace go together.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","punarchi","kedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:245"},"p:1155":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1155,"t":["கோழையா ணவமி குத்த வீரமே புகல்வர் அற்பர்","கோதுசே ரிழிகு லத்தர் ...... குலமேன்மை","கூறியே நடுவி ருப்பர் சோறிடார் தரும புத்ர","கோவுநா னென இசைப்பர் ...... மிடியூடே","ஆழுவார் நிதியு டைக்கு பேரனா மெனஇ சைப்பர்","ஆசுசேர் கலியு கத்தி ...... னெறியீதே","ஆயுநூ லறிவு கெட்ட நானும் வேறல அதற்கு","ளாகையா லவைய டக்க ...... வுரையீதே","ஏழைவா னவர ழைக்க ஆனைவா சவனு ருத்ர","ஈசன்மேல் வெயிலெ றிக்க ...... மதிவேணி","ஈசனார் தமதி டுக்க மாறியே கயிலை வெற்பில்","ஏறியே யினிதி ருக்க ...... வருவோனே","வேழமீ துறையும் வஜ்ர தேவர்கோ சிறைவி டுத்து","வேதனா ரையும் விடுத்து ...... முடிசூடி","வீரசூ ரவன் முடிக்கு ளேறியே கழுகு கொத்த","வீறுசேர் சிலை யெடுத்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet mocks the boasts of the age: cowards proclaim their valour, men of low birth claim high lineage and sit in the middle, those who feed no one call themselves Dharmaputra, the poor call themselves Kubera; this, he says, is the way of the Kali age, and he, without real knowledge, is no different, so this song is his apology to the assembly. The praise names Murugan who came to Kailas to end the gods' distress, freed Indra from prison and released Brahma, and took up the bow while vultures pecked at Sura's crown.","n":"அவையடக்க உரையீதே: the song names itself as the convention by which a poet abases himself before the learned. The satire turns on the poet in one line (நானும் வேறல அதற்குள் ஆகையால்). The opening of the praise (ஆனை வாசவன் உருத்ர ஈசன் மேல் வெயில் எறிக்க) I cannot construe with confidence.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["agandhai","kudi","panivu","avai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:245"},"p:1156":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1156,"t":["சந்தனங் கலந்த குங்கு மம்புனைந் தணிந்த கொங்கை","சந்திரந் ததும்ப சைந்து ...... தெருவூடே","சங்கினங் குலங்க செங்கை யெங்கிலும் பணிந்து டம்பு","சந்தனந் துவண்ட சைந்து ...... வருமாபோல்","கொந்தளங் குலுங்க வண்சி லம்புபொங்க இன்சு கங்கள்","கொஞ்சிபொன் தொடர்ந்தி டும்பொன் ...... மடவார்தோள்","கொங்கைபைங் கரம் புணர்ந்த ழிந்துணங் கலுந்த விர்ந்து","கொஞ்சுநின் சரண்க ளண்ட ...... அருள்தாராய்","தந்தனந் தசெஞ்சி லம்பு கிண்கிணின் குலங்கள் கொஞ்ச","தண்டையம்ப தம்பு லம்ப ...... வருவோனே","சந்தனம் புனைந்த கொங்கை கண்களுஞ் சிவந்து பொங்க","சண்பகம் புனங்கு றம்பொன் ...... அணைமார்பா","வந்தநஞ் சுகந் தமைந்த கந்தரன் புணர்ந்த வஞ்சி","மந்தரம் பொதிந்த கொங்கை ...... யுமையீனும்","மைந்தனென் றுகந்து விஞ்சு மன்பணிந் தசிந்தை யன்பர்","மங்கலின் றுளம்பு குந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes adorned women swaying through the street, their bangles, hair and anklets, and asks to be freed from their embraces and given Murugan's feet. The praise names the anklets on Murugan's own feet as he comes, the hunter girl of the champak field whose breasts he embraced, the son of Uma who shares Siva's body, and the lord who enters the hearts of loving devotees.","n":"The women's anklets (வண்சிலம்பு பொங்க) are answered by Murugan's (செஞ்சிலம்பு கிண்கிணி ... தண்டை), the same sound turned to the Lord's coming.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","thiruvadi","adiyaar"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:245"},"p:1157":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1157,"t":["சுருதி வெகுமுகபு ராண கோடிகள்","சரியை கிரியைமக யோக மோகிகள்","துரித பரசமய பேத வாதிகள் ...... என்றுமோடித்","தொடர வுணரஅரிதாய தூரிய","பொருளை யணுகியநு போக மானவை","தொலைய இனியவொரு ஸ்வாமி யாகிய ...... நின்ப்ரகாசங்","கருதி யுருகியவி ரோதி யாயருள்","பெருகு பரமசுக மாம கோததி","கருணை யடியரொடு கூடியாடிம ...... கிழ்ந்துநீபக்","கனக மணிவயிர நூபு ராரிய","கிரண சரண அபி ராம கோமள","கமல யுகளமற வாது பாடநி ...... னைந்திடாதோ","மருது நெறுநெறென மோதி வேரோடு","கருது மலகைமுலை கோதி வீதியில்","மதுகை யொடுதறுக ணானை வீரிட ...... வென்றுதாளால்","வலிய சகடிலறி மாய மாய்மடி","படிய நடைபழகி யாயர் பாடியில்","வளரு முகில்மருக வேல்வி நோதசி ...... கண்டிவீரா","விருதர் நிருதர்குல சேனை சாடிய","விஜய கடதடக போல வாரண","விபுதை புளகதன பார பூஷண ...... அங்கிராத","விமலை நகிலருண வாகு பூதர","விபுத கடககிரிமேரு பூதர","விகட சமரசத கோடி வானவர் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says the reality that the Vedas, the crores of Puranas, those infatuated with chariyai, kiriyai, sacrifice and yoga, and the debaters of rival sects chase and cannot know, is far off; he asks whether his mind will not one day melt in the Lord's light, join the compassionate devotees in the ocean of bliss, dance and rejoice, and sing without forgetting the jewelled lotus feet. The praise names Murugan as nephew of the cloud-dark Krishna who felled the marutha trees, sucked the demoness's breast, made the elephant scream and kicked the cart, and as the hero of the peacock, lord of Devayanai and the hunter girl and of crores of gods.","n":"The opening list sets the four Saiva paths (சரியை கிரியை ... யோக) beside the Vedas and the quarrelling sects (பரசமய பேத வாதிகள்) as things that run after the truth without reaching it; what is asked for instead is melting (உருகி), the company of devotees and song. மகோததி, great ocean, recurs in 1175 for the same bliss. The Krishna deeds (மருது, அலகை முலை, சகடு) are stock praise of the uncle.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["samayam","aanandham","adiyaar","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:246"},"p:1158":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1158,"t":["சுற்றத் தவர்களு மக்களு மிதமுள","சொற்குற் றரிவையும் விட்டது சலமிது","சுத்தச் சலமினி சற்றிது கிடைபடு ...... மெனமாழ்கித்","துக்கத் தொடுகொடி தொட்டியெ யழுதழல்","சுட்டக் குடமொடு சுட்டெரிகனலொடு","தொக்குத் தொகுதொகு தொக்கென இடுபறை ...... பிண்முடச்","சற்றொப் புளதொரு சச்சையு மெழுமுடல்","சட்டப் படவுயிர் சற்றுடன் விசியது","தப்பிற் றவறுறு மத்திப நடையென ...... உ ரையாடிச்","சத்திப் பொடுகரம் வைத்திடர் தலைமிசை","தப்பிற் றிதுபிழை யெப்படி யெனுமொழி","தத்தச் சடம்விடு மப்பொழு திருசர ...... ணருள்வாயே","சிற்றிற் கிரிமகள் கொத்தலர் புரிகுழல்","சித்ரப் ப்ரபைபுனை பொற்பின ளிளமயில்","செற்கட் சிவகதி யுத்தமி களிதர ...... முதுபேய்கள்","திக்குச் செககெண தித்தரிதிகுதிகு","செச்செச் செணக்ருத டொட்டரிசெணக்ருத","டெட்டெட் டுடுடுடு தத்தரிதரியென ...... நடமாடுங்","கொற்றப் புலியதள் சுற்றிய அரனருள்","குட்டிக் கரிமுக னிக்கவ லமுதுசெய்","கொச்சைக் கணபதி முக்கண னிளையவ ...... களமீதே","குப்புற் றுடனெழு சச்சரிமுழவியல்","கொட்டச் சுரர்பதி மெய்த்திட நிசிசரர்","கொத்துக் கிளையுடல் பட்டுக அமர்செய்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet pictures his own funeral: kin, children and his sweet-spoken wife say it is over and he is cold, wail and beat their heads asking how this mistake happened, the fire-pot is carried, the drum sounds and the corpse is covered; he asks that at the hour his body is left Murugan give his two feet. The praise names the mountain daughter who rejoices while old ghouls dance to the drum, Siva in the tiger skin, the elephant-faced Ganapati who eats sugarcane and flattened rice as Murugan's elder brother, and the battle in which the night-walkers' clans fell.","n":"The funeral is told through the mourners' talk, which is hard to split in places (சலமிது சுத்தச் சலம், 'it is cold, quite cold'; மத்திப நடை): the reading of those lines is a guess. The plea is narrow: only at that moment (அப்பொழுது இருசரண் அருள்வாயே). The drum phrase தொக்குத் தொகுதொகு is rhythm, not words.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["saakkaadu","sutram","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:246"},"p:1159":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1159,"t":["செங்கை னற்புகை யோமாதிகள் குண்ட மிட்டெழு சோமாசிகள்","தெண்டெ னத்துணை தாள்மேல்விழ ...... அமராடிச்","சிந்த னைப்படி மோகாதியி லிந்த்ரியத்தினி லோடாசில","திண்டி றற்றவ வாள்வீரரொ ...... டிகலாநின்","றங்கம் வெட்டிய கூர்வாள்விழி மங்கை யர்க்கற மாலாய்மன","மந்தி பட்டிருள் மூடாவகை ...... யவிரோத","அந்த நிற்குண ஞானோதய சுந்த ரச்சுட ராராயந","லன்பு வைத்தரு ளாமோர்கழ ...... லருளாதோ","கொங்க டுத்தகு ராமாலிகை தண்க டுக்கைது ழாய்தாதகி","கும்பி டத்தகு பாகீரதி ...... மதிமீது","கொண்ட சித்ரக லாசூடிகை யிண்டெ ருக்கணி காகோதர","குண்ட லத்தர்பி னாகாயுத ...... ருடனேயச்","சங்கு சக்ரக தாபாணியு மெங்க ளுக்கொரு வாழ்வேசுரர்","தங்க ளைச்சிறை மீளாயென ...... அசுரேசன்","தஞ்ச மற்றிட வேதாகர னஞ்ச வெற்புக வீராகர","சண்ட விக்ரம வேலேவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says that while fire-priests and Soma-sacrificers fall prostrate at the pair of feet, he struggles with delusion and the senses, and his monkey mind is caught by women's sword-sharp eyes; he asks that darkness not cover it and that, in the dawn of attributeless knowledge, Murugan give him with love one of his feet. The praise has Siva, crowned with konrai, tulasi, datura, Ganga and the moon, and Vishnu with conch, discus and mace, beg Murugan to free the gods from prison, and names the vel he sent at the mountain.","n":"நிற்குண ஞானோதயம், the rising of knowledge without attributes, is the gift asked. The contrast between the ritualists (ஓமாதிகள், சோமாசிகள்) who already bow at the feet and the poet distracted by women is my reading of an opening whose syntax is not clear, especially திண்டிறல் தவ வாள்வீரரொடு இகலா.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","manam","kaamam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:246"},"p:1160":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1160,"t":["சேலையடர்த் தாலமிகுத் தேயுழையைச் சீறுவிதித்","தூறுசிவப் பேறுவிழிக் ...... கணையாலே","தேனிரதத் தேமுழுகிப் பாகுநிகர்த் தாரமுதத்","தேறலெனக் கூறுமொழிச் ...... செயலாலே","ஆலிலையைப் போலும்வயிற் றாலளகத் தாலதரத்","தாலுமிதத் தாலும்வளைப் ...... பிடுவோர்மேல்","ஆசையினைத் தூரவிடுத் தேபுகழ்வுற் றேப்ரியநற்","றாளிணையைச் சேரஎனக் ...... கருள்வாயே","காலனைமெய்ப் பாதமெடுத் தேயுதையிட் டேமதனைக்","காயஎரித் தேவிதியிற் ...... றலையூடே","காசியினிற் காணஇரப் போர்மதியைச் சூடியெருத்","தேறிவகித் தூருதிரைக் ...... கடல்மீதில்","ஆலமிடற் றானையுரித் தோலையுடுத் தீமமதுற்","றாடியிடத் தேயுமைபெற் ...... றருள்வாழ்வே","ஆழியினைச் சூரனைவெற் பேழினையுற் றேயயில்விட்","டாதுலருக் காறுமுகப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet lists the arrows of women's eyes, sharper than carp, deadlier than poison, angrier than a deer, their speech of honey and syrup, their banyan-leaf bellies, hair, lips and smiles, and asks that he put desire for them far away and reach Murugan's feet with praise. The praise is of Siva, who kicked Death, burned Kama, begged at Kasi in Brahma's skull, rode the bull, held the poison, wore the elephant's hide and danced in the cremation fire with Uma at his side, and of Murugan his child, who sent the vel at the sea, Sura and the seven hills.","n":"The eye that wounds (விழிக் கணை) and speech like nectar are the usual weapons; the plea is simply to send desire far off (ஆசையினைத் தூரவிடுத்து). ஆதுலருக்கு at the end means the helpless or destitute: Murugan as lord of those without support.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kan","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:246"},"p:1161":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1161,"t":["சொக்குப்பொட் டெத்திக் கைப்பொரு","ளைக்கெத்திற் பற்றிச் சிக்கொடு","சுற்றுப்பட் டெற்றித் தெட்டிகள் ...... முலைமீதே","சுற்றுப்பொற் பட்டுக் கச்சினர்","முற்றிக்குத் தத்தைக் கொப்பென","சொற்பித்துக் கற்பிற் செப்பிய ...... துயராலே","சிக்குப்பட் டுட்கிப் பற்கொடு","வெற்றிக்கைக் குத்துப் பட்டிதழ்","தித்திப்பிற் கொத்துப் பித்துயர் ...... கொடுநாயேன்","திக்குக்கெட் டொட்டுச் சிட்டென","பட்டத்துற புத்திக் கட்டற","செப்பத்துற் பற்றற் கற்புத ...... மருள்வாயே","தக்குத்தக் குக்குக் குக்குட","தட்டுட்டுட் டுட்டுட் டுட்டென","தக்குத்திக் கெட்டுப் பொட்டெழ ...... விருதோதை","தத்தித்தித் தித்தித் தித்தென","தெற்றுத்துட் டக்கட் டர்ப்படை","சத்திக்கொற் றத்திற் குத்திய ...... முருகோனே","துக்கித்திட் டத்தித் துக்கக","நெக்குப்பட டெக்கித் துட்டறு","சுத்தப்பொற் பத்தர்க் குப்பொரு ...... ளருள்வேலா","துற்றப்பொற் பச்சைக் கட்கல","பச்சித்ரப் பக்ஷிக் கொற்றவ","சொக்கர்க்கர்த் தத்தைச் சுட்டிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet tells of cheating women who use love-powder, take the wealth in a man's hand by trickery, deck their breasts in silk and talk like parrots, and of his own madness in their bites and kisses; he asks for the wonder that cuts his attachment. The praise names the vel that pierced the demon army to battle drums, the vel that gives wealth to pure devotees who melt in grief, the lord of the green peacock, and Murugan who pointed out the meaning to Chokkar, Siva of Madurai.","n":"சொக்குப்பொடி is a love-drug and கைப்பொருளைக் கெத்தில் பற்றி is wealth seized by cunning, so these are the paid women. சொக்கர்க்கு அர்த்தத்தைச் சுட்டிய is the teaching of Siva again, with Siva named by his Madurai name. Lines 101 to 103 (திக்குக்கெட்டு ஒட்டுச் சிட்டென பட்டத்துற) are obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","pattru","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:246"},"p:1162":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1162,"t":["ஞானாவி பூஷணி காரணி காரணி","காமாவி மோகினி வாகினி யாமளை","மாமாயி பார்வதி தேவிகு ணாதரி...... உ மையாள்தன்","நாதாக்ரு பாகர தேசிகர் தேசிக","வேதாக மேயருள் தேவர்கள் தேவந","லீசாச டாபர மேசர்சர் வேசுரி...... முருகோனே","தேனார்மொ ழீவளி நாயகி நாயக","வானாடு ளோர்தொழு மாமயில் வாகன","சேணாளு மானின்ம னோகர மாகிய ...... மணவாளா","சீர்பாத சேகர னாகவு நாயினன்","மோகாவி காரவி டாய்கெட ஓடவெ","சீராக வேகலை யாலுனை ஓதவும் ...... அருள்வாயே","பேணார்கள் நீறதி டாஅம ணோர்களை","சூராடி யேகழு மீதினி லேறிட","கூனான மீனனி டேறிட கூடலில் ...... வருவோனே","பேராண்மை யாளனி சாசரர் கோனிரு","கூறாக வாளிதொ டூரகு நாயகன்","பூவாய னாரணன் மாயனி ராகவன் ...... மருகோனே","வாணாள்ப டாவரு சூரர்கள் மாளவெ","சேணாடு ளோரவர் வீடதி டேறிட","கோனாக வேவரு நாதகு ரூபர ...... குமரேசா","வாசாம கோசர மாகிய வாசக","தேசாதி யோரவர் பாதம தேதொழ","பாசாவி நாசக னாகவு மேவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The song opens with a string of names for Uma (giver of knowledge, cause of causes, the great Maya, Parvati) and names Murugan her son, guru of gurus, Valli's lord and the bridegroom of the heavenly deer-girl; the poet, calling himself a dog, asks to wear Murugan's feet on his head, to be rid of the fever of delusion, and to praise him rightly through learning. The praise names him as the one who came to Koodal and had the Jains who would not wear ash impaled so that the hunchbacked Pandya rose, as nephew of Rama who split Ravana, and as the lord beyond word and mind who destroys bonds.","n":"நீறு இடா அமணோர் ... கழுமீதினில் ஏறிட, கூனான மீனன் இடு ஏறிட, கூடலில் வருவோனே is the story of Sambandar at Madurai, here told of Murugan himself, with holy ash as the mark that divides the two faiths. சீர்பாத சேகரன், one who wears the feet as a crown, is what the poet asks to become. பாசவிநாசகன் names Murugan as the destroyer of the bonds.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvadi","mayal","thiruneeru","paasam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:246"},"p:1163":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1163,"t":["தரணிமிசை அனையினிட வுந்தியின் வந்துகுந்","துளிபயறு கழலினிய அண்டமுங் கொண்டதின்","தசையுதிர நிணநிறைய அங்கமுந் தங்கவொன் ...... பதுவாயுந்","தருகரமொ டினியபத முங்கொடங் கொன்பதும்","பெருகியொரு பதினவனி வந்துகண் டன்புடன்","தநயனென நடைபழகி மங்கைதன் சிங்கியின் ...... வசமாகித்","திரிகியுடல் வளையநடை தண்டுடன் சென்றுபின்","கிடையெனவு மருவிமனை முந்திவந் தந்தகன்","சிதறுவுயிர் பிணமெனவெ மைந்தரும் பந்துவும் ...... அயர்வாகிச்","செடமிதனை யெடுமெடுமி னென்றுகொண் டன்புடன்","சுடலைமிசை யெரியினிட வெந்துபின் சிந்திடுஞ்","செனனமிது தவிரஇரு தண்டையுங் கொண்டபைங் ...... கழல்தாராய்","செருவெதிரு மசுரர்கிளை மங்கஎங் கெங்கணுங்","கழுகருட னயனமிது கண்டுகொண் டம்பரந்","திரியமிகு அலகையுடன் வெங்கணந் தங்களின் ...... மகிழ்வாகிச்","சினவசுர ருடலமது தின்றுதின் றின்புடன்","டுமுடுமுட டுமுடுமுட டுண்டுடுண் டுண்டுடுண்","டிமிலைபறை முழவுதுடி பம்பையுஞ் சங்கமுந் ...... தவமோதச்","சரவரிசை விடுகுமர அண்டர்தம் பண்டுறுஞ்","சிறைவை஢ட வருமுருக என்றுவந் திந்திரன்","சதுமுகனு மடிபரவ மண்டுவெஞ் சம்பொருங் ...... கதிர்வேலா","சகமுழுது மடையஅமு துண்டிடுங் கொண்டலுந்","தெரிவரிய முடியினர வங்ளுந் திங்களுஞ்","சலமிதழி யணியுமொரு சங்கரன் தந்திடும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet traces a life from the drop placed in the mother, growing like a pea into flesh, blood, limbs and nine openings, through ten months, birth and first steps, to falling under a woman's spell, walking bent with a stick, lying bedridden, Death coming first, the corpse lifted by weary sons and kin and burned on the pyre; he asks for the anklet feet so that this birth may end. The praise is a battle in which vultures and ghouls feast to drums, Indra and Brahma praise the vel that freed the gods, and Murugan is the son of Sankara who wears the moon, Ganga, konrai and serpents.","n":"The life runs without a pause from womb to woman to stick to pyre, so that the plea is against birth itself (செனனமிது தவிர), not only death. அந்தகன் is Death. சிங்கி, the woman's power over him, is literally a poison.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["karu","yaakkai","piravi","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:246"},"p:1164":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1164,"t":["தனஞ்சற்றுக் குலுங்கப்பொற் கலன்கட்பட் டிலங்கப்பொற்","சதங்கைக்கற் சிலம்பொத்திக் ...... கையில்வீணை","ததும்பக்கைக் குழந்தைச்சொற் பரிந்தற்புக் கிதங்கப்பொற்","சரஞ்சுற்றிட் டிணங்கக்கட் ...... சரவேலால்","தினம்பித்திட் டிணங்கிச்சொற் கரங்கட்டிப் புணர்ந்திட்டுத்","தினந்தெட்டிக் கடன்பற்றிக் ...... கொளுமாதர்","சிலம்பத்திற் றிரிந்துற்றிட் டவம்புக்கக் குணஞ்செற்றுச்","சிவம்பெற்றுத் தவம்பற்றக் ...... கழல்தாராய்","தனந்தத்தத் தனந்தத்தத் தடுண்டுட்டுட் டிடிண்டிட்டிட்","டடண்டட்டட் டிமிண்டுட்டுட் ...... டியல்தாளந்","தகுந்தொத்தித் திமிந்தித்தித் தவண்டைக்குட் கயர்ந்துக்கத்","தகண்டத்தர்க் குடன்பட்டுற் ...... றசுராரைச்","சினந்தத்திக் கொளுந்தக்கைச் சரந்தொட்டுச் சதம்பொர்ப்பைச்","சிரந்தத்தப் பிளந்துட்கக் ...... கிரிதூளாச்","செகந்திக்குச் சுபம்பெற்றுத் துலங்கப்பொர்க் களம்புக்குச்","செயம்பற்றிக் கொளுஞ்சொக்கப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women with shaking breasts, golden ornaments, anklets and a vina in hand, child-like speech and arrow eyes, who daily make love to a man and daily cheat him and seize his goods as debts; he asks for the feet so that, turning from their tricks and his own worthless ways, he may gain Sivam and hold to austerity. The praise is a battle sung in drum syllables in which the vel splits the demons and grinds the mountain to dust, and names Murugan the beautiful lord.","n":"கடன்பற்றிக் கொளும் மாதர், women who seize things as if collecting a debt, makes these the paid women. The goal is named in Saiva terms, சிவம் பெற்றுத் தவம் பற்ற. Most of the second half (தனந்தத்தத் தனந்தத்தத் ...) is rhythm.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","kaamam","sivam","thavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:246"},"p:1165":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1165,"t":["நகரமிரு பாத மாகி மகரவயி றாகி மார்பு","நடுசிகர மாகி வாய்வ ...... கரமாகி","நதிமுடிய சார மாகி உ தயதிரு மேனி யாகி","நவசிவய மாமை யாகி ...... எழுதான","அகரவுக ரேத ரோம சகர வுணர் வான சூரன்","அறிவிலறி வான பூர ...... ணமுமாகும்","அதனைஅடி யேனும் ஓதி இதயகம லாலை யாகி","மருவுமவ தான போதம் ...... அருள்வாயே","குகனுமரு ளாண்மை கூர மகரமெனு சாப தாரி","குறையகல வேலை மீது ...... தனியூருங்","குழவிவடி வாக வேநம் பரதர்தவ மாக மீறு","குலவுதிரை சேரு மாது ...... தனைநாடி","அகிலவுல கோர்கள் காண அதிசயம தாக மேவி","அரியமண மேசெய் தேக ...... வலைதேடி","அறுமுகவன் மீக ரான பிறவியம ராசை வீசும்","அசபைசெகர் சோதி நாத ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes the Lord's form as made of the five letters, na the feet, ma the belly, si the chest, va the mouth, ya the crown, and of the syllables a and u of Om, the fullness that is knowledge within knowledge, and asks that he too recite it, make the lotus of his heart a temple and receive attentive knowledge. The praise appears to tell how Siva went to sea as a fisherman to seek and marry the fisher-girl, with Guha and the one cursed to be a makara in the story, and ends at the lord of light of the ajapa.","n":"நகரம் இரு பாதமாகி ... வாய் வகரமாகி lays the letters of நமசிவய on the body, and அகர உகர ... ஓம் adds the pranava; இதய கமல ஆலயம் makes the heart the shrine. The fishing story is my identification from the net (வலை), the fisher folk (பரதர்), the girl of the waves and the makara curse, as in the Tiruvilaiyadal tale; the syntax of the second half and the word அசபை (ajapa, the unspoken mantra) are uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panchaakkaram","piranavam","mandhiram","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:246"},"p:1166":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1166,"t":["நரையொடு பற்க ழன்று தோல்வற்றி","நடையற மெத்த நொந்து காலெய்த்து","நயனமி ருட்டி நின்று கோலுற்று ...... நடைதோயா","நழுவும்வி டக்கை யொன்று போல்வைத்து","நமதென மெத்த வந்த வாழ்வுற்று","நடலைப டுத்து மிந்த மாயத்தை ...... நகையாதே","விரையொடு பற்றி வண்டு பாடுற்ற","ம்ருகமத மப்பி வந்த வோதிக்கு","மிளிருமை யைச்செ றிந்த வேல்கட்கும் ...... வினையோடு","மிகுகவி னிட்டு நின்ற மாதர்க்கு","மிடைபடு சித்த மொன்று வேனுற்றுன்","விழுமிய பொற்ப தங்கள் பாடற்கு ...... வினவாதோ","உ ரையொடு சொற்றெ ரிந்த மூவர்க்கு","மொளிபெற நற்ப தங்கள் போதித்து","மொருபுடை பச்சை நங்கை யோடுற்று ...... முலகூடே","உ றுபலி பிச்சை கொண்டு போயுற்று","முவரிவி டத்தை யுண்டு சாதித்து","முலவிய முப்பு ரங்கள் வேவித்து ...... முறநாகம்","அரையொடு கட்டி யந்த மாய்வைத்து","மவிர்சடை வைத்த கங்கை யோடொக்க","அழகுதி ருத்தி யிந்து மேல்வைத்து ...... மரவோடே","அறுகொடு நொச்சி தும்பை மேல்வைத்த","அரியய னித்தம் வந்து பூசிக்கும்","அரநிம லர்க்கு நன்றி போதித்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet pictures old age, grey hair, fallen teeth, dried skin, failing legs, dim eyes and the staff, the body a slipping lump of flesh, and the illusion of a life called 'ours' that only brings grief; he asks whether his mind, still bound to women's musk-scented hair and kohl-dark eyes, will not turn to singing Murugan's golden feet. The praise is a long portrait of Siva, who taught the three poets, keeps the green lady at his side, begged alms, swallowed the sea's poison, burned the three cities and wears snake, Ganga, moon and herbs, and whom Vishnu and Brahma worship daily; Murugan taught the good to that Hara.","n":"The force of the song is that the old body and the lust come in the same breath: the mind is still tied to women (மிடைபடு சித்தம்) when the teeth are gone. உரையொடு சொல் தெரிந்த மூவர்க்கும் ... போதித்து may point to the three Tevaram poets, and நகையாதே may mean 'without seeing through (laughing at) this illusion'; both are uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","nilaiyaamai","kaamam","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:246"},"p:1167":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1167,"t":["நிமிர்ந்த முதுகுங் குனிந்து சிறந்த முகமுந் திரங்கி","நிறைந்த வயிறுஞ் சரிந்து ...... தடியூணி","நெகிழ்ந்து சடலந் தளர்ந்து விளங்கு விழியங் கிருண்டு","நினைந்த மதியுங் கலங்கி ...... மனையாள்கண்","டுமிழ்ந்து பலருங் கடிந்து சிறந்த வியலும் பெயர்ந்து","உ றைந்த உ யிருங் கழன்று ...... விடுநாள்முன்","உ கந்து மனமுங் குளிர்ந்து பயன்கொள் தருமம் புரிந்து","ஒடுங்கி நினையும் பணிந்து ...... மகிழ்வேனோ","திமிந்தி யெனவெங் கணங்கள் குணங்கர் பலவுங் குழும்பி","திரண்ட சதியும் புரிந்து ...... முதுசூரன்","சிரங்கை முழுதுங் குடைந்து நிணங்கொள் குடலுந் தொளைந்து","சினங்க ழுகொடும் பெருங்கு ...... ருத஧முழ்க","அமிழ்ந்தி மிகவும் பிணங்கள் அயின்று மகிழ்கொண்டு மண்ட","அடர்ந்த அயில்முன் துரந்து ...... பொருவேளே","அலங்க லெனவெண் கடம்பு புனைந்து புணருங் குறிஞ்சி","அணங்கை மணமுன் புணர்ந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet pictures the straight back bent, the face wrinkled, the belly sagging, the stick, the dim eyes and wandering mind, the wife spitting at the sight, others scolding and life slipping away, and asks whether before that day he will do dharma that bears fruit with a glad, cool heart, humbly bow to Murugan and rejoice. The praise is the battle with old Sura, the vultures and ghouls in the blood, and Murugan who wore white kadamba and married the goddess of the kurinji hills.","n":"Unlike most death songs, the plea is not for Murugan to come at the hour but for the poet to act before it: பயன்கொள் தருமம் புரிந்து, doing fruitful dharma, beside worship. The wife who spits (மனையாள் கண்டு உமிழ்ந்து) is the harshest touch in the picture.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["yaakkai","aram","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:246"},"p:1168":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1168,"t":["நிருத ரார்க்கொரு காலா ஜேஜெய","சுரர்க ளேத்திடு வேலா ஜேஜெய","நிமல னார்க்கொரு பாலா ஜேஜெய ...... விறலான","நெடிய வேற்படை யானே ஜேஜெய","எனஇ ராப்பகல் தானே நான்மிக","நினது தாட்டொழு மாறே தானினி ...... யுடனேதான்","தரையி னாழ்த்திரை யேழே போலெழு","பிறவி மாக்கட லு஡டே நானுறு","சவலை தீர்த்துன தாளே சூடியு ...... னடியார்வாழ்","சபையி னேற்றியின் ஞானா போதமு","மருளி யாட்கொளு மாறே தானது","தமிய னேற்குமு னேநீ மேவுவ ...... தொருநாளே","தருவி னாட்டர சாள்வான் வேணுவி","னுருவ மாய்ப்பல நாளே தானுறு","தவசி னாற்சிவ னீபோய் வானவர் ...... சிறைதீரச்","சகல லோக்கிய மேதா னாளுறு","மசுர பார்த்திப னோடே சேயவர்","தமரை வேற்கொடு நீறா யேபட ...... விழமோதென்","றருள ஏற்றம ரோடே போயவ","ருறையு மாக்கிரியோடே தானையு","மழிய வீழ்த்தெதிர் சூரோ டேயம ...... ரடலாகி","அமரில் வீட்டியும் வானோர் தானுறு","சிறையை மீட்டர னார்பால் மேவிய","அதிப ராக்ரம வீரா வானவர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet cries jaya to the Death of the Nirutar, the vel praised by the gods and the son of the pure one, worshipping the feet night and day, and asks that Murugan end his weariness in the sea of births that rises like seven seas, set him in the assembly of his devotees, give him knowledge and take him as his own: when will that day come? The praise tells how, after Indra's long penance in the form of a bamboo, Siva sent Murugan to free the gods, and how he went with his host, overthrew the mountain and Sura, freed the gods and returned to Hara.","n":"உனது அடியார் வாழ் சபையின் ஏற்றி, lifting him into the assembly where the devotees live, is the goal beside the ending of births. வேணுவின் உருவமாய் ... தவசினால் may allude to Indra hiding as a bamboo at Venupuram (Sirkazhi); I offer that identification as a guess.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["piravi","adiyaar","gnaanam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:246"},"p:1169":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1169,"t":["ஆர வார மாயி ருந்து ஏம தூத ரோடி வந்து","ஆழி வேலை போன்மு ழங்கி ...... யடர்வார்கள்","ஆக மீதி லேசி வந்து ஊசி தானு மேநு ழைந்து","ஆலைமீதி லேக ரும்பு ...... எனவேதான்","வீர மான சூரிகொண்டு நேரை நேரை யேபி ளந்து","வீசு வார்கள் கூகு வென்று ...... அழுபோது","வீடு வாச லான பெண்டிர் ஆசை யான மாதர் வந்து","மேலை வீழ்வ ஡ணது கண்டு ...... வருவாயே","நாரிவீரிசூரியம்பை வேத வேத மேபு கழ்ந்த","நாதர் பாலி லேயி ருந்த ...... மகமாயி","நாடி யோடி வாற அன்பர் காண வேண தேபு கழ்ந்து","நாளு நாளு மேபு கன்ற ...... வரைமாது","நீரின் மீதி லேயி ருந்த நீலி சூலி வாழ்வு மைந்த","நீப மாலை யேபு னைந்த ...... குமரேசா","நீல னாக வோடி வந்த சூரை வேறு வேறு கண்ட","நீத னான தோர்கு ழந்தை ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet pictures Death's messengers coming with a roar like the sea, piercing the body, crushing it like sugarcane in the mill and cutting it with daggers while people cry out, and the women of the house and the women he loved falling on him weeping; he asks Murugan to see this and come. The praise names the Goddess by many names, Nari, Suli, Ambai, Mahamayi, Neeli, the mountain daughter beside the Lord the Vedas praise, and Murugan her son, wearing kadamba, the child who cut Sura apart.","n":"ஏமதூதர், Yama's messengers, here torture the body (ஆலைமீதிலே கரும்பு எனவே) rather than simply bind it, and the plea is the familiar one, கண்டு வருவாயே. Most of the praise is of the Goddess, not of Murugan.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kootru","saakkaadu","sutram","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:246"},"p:1170":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1170,"t":["நீரு மென்பு தோலி னாலு மாவ தென்கை கால்க ளோடு","நீளு மங்க மாகி மாய ...... வுய஧ருறி","நேச மொன்று தாதை தாய ராசை கொண்ட போதில் மேவி","நீதி யொன்று பால னாகி ...... யழிவாய்வந்","தூரு மின்ப வாழ்வு மாகி யூன மொன்றி லாது மாத","ரோடு சிந்தை வேடை கூர ...... உ றவாகி","ஊழி யைந்த கால மேதி யோனும் வந்து பாசம் வீச","ஊனு டம்பு மாயு மாய ...... மொழியாதோ","சூர னண்ட லோக மேன்மை சூறை கொண்டு போய் விடாது","தோகை யின்கண் மேவி வேலை ...... விடும்வீரா","தோளி லென்பு மாலை வேணி மீது கங்கை சூடி யாடு","தோகை பங்க ரோடு சூது ...... மொழிவோனே","பாரை யுண்ட மாயன் வேயை யூதி பண்டு பாவ லோர்கள்","பாடல் கண்டு ஏகு மாலின் ...... மருகோனே","பாத கங்கள் வேறி நூறி நீதி யின்சொல் வேத வாய்மை","பாடு மன்பர் வாழ்வ தான ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says the body of water, bone and skin takes life when father and mother desire, grows as a child into the joys of youth and lust for women, and at the end the buffalo-rider comes and throws his noose; he asks whether the illusion by which this flesh perishes will not end. The praise names the vel against Sura, Siva wearing bones and Ganga with his peacock-like consort, Vishnu who swallowed the earth, played the flute and walked after the poets' song, and Murugan as the life of devotees who sing the truth of the Veda.","n":"மேதியோன், the buffalo-rider, is Yama, and பாசம் வீச is his noose. பாவலோர்கள் பாடல் கண்டு ஏகும் மால is the story of Vishnu following a poet, as in 14. சூது மொழிவோனே, 'who speaks சூது' to Siva and his consort, I cannot place: it may mean subtle or secret words.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["yaakkai","kootru","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:246"},"p:1171":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1171,"t":["பகல்மட்கச் செக்கர்ப் ப்ரபைவிடு","நவரத்நப் பத்தித் தொடைநக","நுதிபட்டிட் டற்றுச் சிதறிட ....... ; த்ழுறல்","பருகித்தித் திக்கப் படுமொழி","பதறக்கைப் பத்மத் தொளிவளை","வதறிச்சத் திக்கப் புளகித ...... தனபாரம்","அகலத்திற் றைக்கப் பரிமள","அமளிக்குட் கிக்கிச் சிறுகென","இறுகக்கைப் பற்றித் தழுவிய ...... அநுராக","அவசத்திற் சித்தத் தறிவையு","மிகவைத்துப் பொற்றித் தெரிவையர்","வசம்விட்டர்ச் சிக்கைக் கொருபொழு ...... துணர்வேனோ","இகல்வெற்றிச் சத்திக் கிரணமு","முரணிர்த்தப் பச்சைப் புரவியு","மிரவிக்கைக் குக்டத் துவசமு ...... மறமாதும்","இடைவைத்துச் சித்ரத் தமிழ்கொடு","கவிமெத்தச் செப்பிப் பழுதற","எழுதிக்கற் பித்துத் திரிபவர் ...... பெருவாழ்வே","புகலிற்றர்க் கிட்டுப் ப்ரமையுறு","கலகச்செற் றச்சட் சமயிகள்","புகலற்குப் பற்றற் கரியதொ ...... ருபதேசப்","பொருளைப்புட் பித்துக் குருபர","னெனமுக்கட் செக்கர்ச் சடைமதி","புனையப்பர்க் கொப்பித் தருளிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes lovemaking in detail, the gem string snapped by nails, the drinking of lips, faltering speech, sounding bangles, the perfumed bed and the tight embrace, in which he gives his mind and knowledge over to women, and asks whether he will ever give one moment to worship. The praise names the vel, the peacock, the cock banner and the hunter girl as what the poets who compose flawless Tamil set at the centre of their verse, and Murugan as the guru who made bloom for the three-eyed father the meaning of the teaching that the quarrelling six sects cannot reach.","n":"சட்சமயிகள், the six sects, deluded and quarrelling, cannot grasp the உபதேசப் பொருள் that Murugan gave Siva: the teaching story is set against sectarian argument. The Tamil poets who 'write and teach' (எழுதிக் கற்பித்துத் திரிபவர்) of the vel and the peacock are a self-portrait of the poet's own craft.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","guru","samayam","thamizh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:246"},"p:1172":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1172,"t":["பத்தித்தர ளக்கொத் தொளிர்வரி","பட்டப்புள கச்செப் பிளமுலை","பட்டிட்டெதிர் கட்டுப் பரதவ ...... ருயர்தாளப்","பத்மத்திய ரற்புக் கடுகடு","கட்சத்தியர் மெத்தத் திரவிய","பட்சத்திய ரிக்குச் சிலையுரு ...... விலிசேருஞ்","சித்தத்தரு ணர்க்குக் கனியத","ரப்புத்தமு தத்தைத் தருமவர்","சித்ரக்கிர ணப்பொட் டிடுபிறை ...... நுதலார்தந்","தெட்டிற்படு கட்டக் கனவிய","பட்சத்தரு ளற்றுற் றுனதடி","சிக்கிட்டிடை புக்கிட் டலைவது ...... தவிராதோ","மத்தப்பிர மத்தக் கயமுக","னைக்குத்திமி தித்துக் கழுதுகள்","மட்டிட்டஇ ரத்தக் குருதியில் ...... விளையாட","மற்றைப் பதினெட்டுக் கணவகை","சத்திக்கந டிக்கப் பலபல","வர்க்கத்தலை தத்தப் பொருபடை ...... யுடையோனே","முத்திப்பர மத்தைக் கருதிய","சித்தத்தினில் முற்றத் தவமுனி","முற்பட்டுழை பெற்றுத் தருகுற ...... மகள்மேல்மால்","முற்றித்திரிவெற்றிக் குருபர","முற்பட்டமு ரட்டுப் புலவனை","முட்டைப்பெயர் செப்பிக் கவிபெறு ...... பெறுமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes dancing women with pearl strings on their breasts, harsh eyes and a great appetite for wealth, who give their lips' nectar to young men struck by Kama's sugarcane bow, and asks whether his wandering, caught in their cheating and lost to grace, will not end so that he may cling to Murugan's feet. The praise names the war on the mad elephant-faced demon where ghouls play in blood and the eighteen hosts dance, Murugan's love for the kuravar girl born to a sage absorbed in tapas, and the guru who named a stubborn poet 'egg' and won a poem from him.","n":"மெத்தத் திரவிய பட்சத்தியர், women very fond of money, makes them the paid women. முற்பட்ட முரட்டுப் புலவனை முட்டைப் பெயர் செப்பிக் கவிபெறு refers to a story of Murugan mocking a proud poet that I cannot identify with confidence; the deer (உழை) and the sage point to Valli's birth.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:247"},"p:1173":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1173,"t":["பரதவித புண்டரிக பாதத் தாட்டிகள்","அமுதுபொழி யுங்குமுத கீதப் பாட்டிகள்","பலர்பொருள்க வர்ந்திடைக லாமிட் டோ ட்டிகள் ...... கொடிதாய","பழுதொழிய அன்புமுடை யாரைப் போற்சிறி","தழுதழுது கண்பிசையு மாசைக் கூற்றிகள்","பகழியென வந்துபடு பார்வைக் கூற்றினர் ...... ஒருகாம","விரகம்விளை கின்றகழு நீரைச் சேர்த்தகில்","ம்ருகமதமி குந்தபனி நீரைத் தேக்கியெ","விபுதர்பதி யங்கதல மேவிச் சாற்றிய ...... தமிழ்நூலின்","விததிகமழ் தென்றல்வர வீசிக் கோட்டிகள்","முலைகளில்வி ழுந்துபரிதாபத் தாற்றினில்","விடியளவு நைந்துருகு வேனைக் காப்பது ...... மொருநாளே","உ ரகபணை பந்தியபி ஷேகத் தாற்றிய","சகலவுல குந்தரும மோகப் பார்ப்பதி","யுடனுருவு பங்குடைய நாகக் காப்பனும் ...... உ றிதாவும்","ஒருகளவு கண்டுதனி கோபத் தாய்க்குல","மகளிர்சிறு தும்புகொடு மோதிச் சேர்ந்திடும்","உ ரலொடுத வழ்ந்தநவ நீதக் கூற்றனு ...... மதிகோபக்","கரவிகட வெங்கடக போலப் போர்க்கிரி","கடவியபு ரந்தரனும் வேளைப் போற்றுகை","கருமமென வந்துதொழ வேதப் பாற்பதி ...... பிறியாத","கடவுளைமு னிந்தமர ரூரைக் காத்துயர்","கரவடக்ர வுஞ்சகிரிசாயத் தோற்றெழு","கடலெனவு டைந்தவுண ரோடத் தாக்கிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women who dance and sing, rob many men and then pick quarrels to drive them out, weep and rub their eyes as if in love, and are Death in the shape of desire with glances like arrows; perfumed with lilies, musk and rosewater in the south wind, he falls on their breasts and melts till dawn, and asks whether a day will come when Murugan protects him. The praise names Siva with Parvati at his side, Krishna bound to the mortar by the cowherd women for stealing butter, Indra on his elephant who came to worship, and Murugan who was angry with Brahma, guarded the gods' city, toppled Krauncha and routed the demons.","n":"பலர் பொருள் கவர்ந்து இடை கலாமிட்டு ஓட்டிகள, they take many men's wealth and drive them out with a quarrel, is the Naladiyar's picture of the courtesan. ஆசைக் கூற்றிகள் and பார்வைக் கூற்றினர் call the women Death. விபுதர்பதி அங்கதலம் மேவிச் சாற்றிய தமிழ்நூல் seems to tie the south wind to a hill where Tamil was first told, perhaps Pothigai; that is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:247"},"p:1174":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1174,"t":["பழுதற வோதிக் கடந்து பகைவினை தீரத் துறந்து","பலபல யோகத் திருந்து ...... மதராசன்","பரிமள பாணத் தயர்ந்து பனைமட லு஡ர்தற் கிசைந்து","பரிதவி யாமெத்த நொந்து ...... மயல்கூர","அழுதழு தாசைப் படுங்க ணபிநய மாதர்க் கிரங்கி","யவர்விழி பாணத்து நெஞ்ச ...... மறைபோய்நின்","றழிவது யான்முற் பயந்த விதிவச மோமற்றையுன்ற","னருள்வச மோஇப்ர மந்தெ ...... ரிகிலேனே","எழுதரு வேதத்து மன்றி முழுதினு மாய்நிற்கு மெந்தை","யெனவொரு ஞானக் குருந்த ...... ருளமேவும்","இருவுரு வாகித் துலங்கி யொருகன தூணிற் பிறந்து","இரணியன் மார்பைப் பிளந்த ...... தனியாண்மை","பொழுதிசை யாவிக்ர மன்தன் மருகபு ராரிக்கு மைந்த","புளகப டீரக் குரும்பை ...... யுடன்மேவும்","புயல்கரிவாழச் சிலம்பின் வனசர மானுக் குகந்து","புனமிசை யோடிப் புகுந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says that though he studied without fault, renounced to end hostile karma and sat in many yogas, Kama's fragrant arrows wore him down until he was ready to ride the palmyra horse, and he melted for women weeping with acting eyes; he asks whether this ruin is the fate of his former deeds or the will of Murugan's grace, for in his confusion he cannot tell. The praise names the father present in the Veda and in all things, Vishnu who came from the pillar in two forms and split Hiranya's chest, Murugan as his nephew and as son of Siva, and the lord who ran into the millet field for the forest deer-girl.","n":"The song's question is explicit: முற்பயந்த விதிவசமோ, மற்றை உன்றன் அருள்வசமோ, fate of past deeds or your grace's will? It is left unanswered. That the renouncer and yogi is the one who agrees to ride the madal (பனைமடல் ஊர்தற்கு இசைந்து) turns the akam lover's last resort into an ascetic's fall. ஞானக் குருந்து may recall the kuruntham tree of 7.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["madal","oozh","thiruvarul","thuravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:247"},"p:1175":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1175,"t":["பாணிக்குட் படாது சாதகர் காணச்சற் றொணாது வாதிகள்","பாஷிக்கத் தகாது பாதக ...... பஞ்சபூத","பாசத்திற்படாது வேறொரு பாயத்திற் புகாது பாவனை","பாவிக்கப் பெறாது வாதனை ...... நெஞ்சமான","ஏணிக்கெட் லொணாது மீதுயர் சேணுக்குச் சமான நூல்வழி","யேறிபபற் றொணாது நாடினர் ...... தங்களாலும்","ஏதுச்செப் பொணாத தோர்பொருள் சேரத்துக் கமாம கோததி","யேறச்செச் சைநாறு தாளைவ ...... ணங்குவேனோ","ஆணிப்பொற் ப்ரதாப மேருவை வேலிட்டுக் கடாவி வாசவன்","ஆபத்தைக் கெடாநி சாசரர் ...... தம்ப்ரகாசம்","ஆழிச்சத் ரசாயை நீழலி லாதித்தப் ப்ரகாச நேர்தர","ஆழிச்சக் ரவாள மாள்தரும் ...... எம்பிரானே","மாணிக்க ப்ரவாள நீலம தாணிப்பொற் கிராதைநூபுர","வாசப்பத் மபாத சேகர ...... சம்புவேதா","வாசிக்கப் படாத வாசகம் ஈசர்க்குச் சுவாமி யாய்முதல்","வாசிப்பித் ததேசி காசுரர் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes a reality that cannot be held, cannot be glimpsed by practitioners or spoken by debaters, is not caught in the five elements, is not reached by other means, by imagined meditation, by the ladder of the desiring mind or by the path of books, nor stated by reasons, a great ocean of bliss, and asks whether he will bow to Murugan's sandal-scented feet. The praise names the vel driven into golden Meru to end Indra's danger, the rule of the world's circle under the shade of a single umbrella, Murugan who wears on his head the jewelled anklet feet of the hunter girl, and the teacher who first made Siva read the word that cannot be read.","n":"The plea is built from negatives (படாது, ஒணாது, தகாது, பெறாது), a way of naming the One by what cannot reach it. வாசிக்கப் படாத வாசகம், the unread word, taught to Siva is the Om story, though Om is not named. கிராதை நூபுர ... பாதசேகர, he who wears Valli's feet as a crown, reverses the usual devotee's posture.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","aanandham","guru","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:247"},"p:1176":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1176,"t":["பால்மொழி படித்துக் காட்டி ஆடையை நெகிழ்த்துக் காட்டி","பாயலி லிருத்திக் காட்டி ...... யநுராகம்","பாகிதழ் கொடுத்துக்காட்டி நூல்களை விரித்துக்காட்டி","பார்வைகள் புரட்டிக் காட்டி ...... யுறவாகி","மேல்நக மழுத்திக் காட்டி தோதக விதத்தைக் காட்டி","மேல்விழு நலத்தைக் காட்டு ...... மடவார்பால்","மேவிடு மயக்கைத் தீர்த்து சீர்பத நினைப்பைக் கூட்டு","மேன்மையை யெனக்குக் காட்டி ...... யருள்வாயே","காலனை யுதைத்துக் காட்டி யாவியை வதைத்துக் காட்டி","காரணம் விளைத்துக்காட்டி ...... யொருங்காலங்","கானினில் நடித்துக் காட்டி யாலமு மிடற்றிற் காட்டி","காமனை யெரித்துக் காட்டி ...... தருபாலா","மாலுற நிறத்தைக் காட்டி வேடுவர் புனத்திற் காட்டில்","வாலிப மிளைத்துக்காட்டி ...... அயர்வாகி","மான்மகள் தனத்தைச் சூட்டி ஏனென அழைத்துக் கேட்டு","வாழ்வுறு சமத்தைக்காட்டு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet lists what women show a man, reading milky speech, loosening their clothes, seating him on the bed, giving their lips, opening the books of love, rolling their eyes, pressing their nails and showing their tricks, and asks that Murugan end his delusion for them and show him the greatness of thinking of his feet. The praise uses the same refrain of showing for Siva, who kicked Death, danced in the forest at the end of time, held the poison and burned Kama, and for Murugan, who in the hunters' millet field showed himself worn with love, embraced the deer-girl and showed the skill of a happy life.","n":"The verb காட்டி ends almost every phrase on both sides of the song: the women display their arts, Siva displays his deeds, and the poet asks Murugan to display (காட்டி) the eminence of the feet. தோதகம் is deceit. Some phrases of the Valli part (வாலிபம் இளைத்து) are hard to construe.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","mayal","vanjam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:247"},"p:1177":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1177,"t":["புகரில் சேவல தந்துர சங்க்ரம","நிருதர் கோபக்ர வுஞ்சநெ டுங்கிரி","பொருத சேவக குன்றவர் பெண்கொடி ...... மணவாளா","புனித பூசுர ருஞ்சுர ரும்பணி","புயச பூதர என்றிரு கண்புனல்","பொழிய மீமிசை யன்புது ளும்பிய ...... மனனாகி","அகில பூதவு டம்புமு டம்பினில்","மருவு மாருயி ருங்கர ணங்களு","மவிழ யானுமி ழந்தஇ டந்தனி ...... லுணர்வாலே","அகில வாதிக ளுஞ்சம யங்களும்","அடைய ஆமென அன்றென நின்றதை","யறிவி லேனறி யும்படி யின்றருள் ...... புரிவாயே","மகர கேதன முந்திகழ் செந்தமிழ்","மலய மாருத மும்பல வெம்பரி","மளசி லீமுக மும்பல மஞ்சரி...... வெறியாடும்","மதுக ராரம்வி குஞ்சணி யுங்கர","மதுர கார்முக மும்பொர வந்தெழு","மதன ராஜனை வெந்துவி ழும்படி ...... முனிபால","முகிழ்வி லோசன ரஞ்சிறு திங்களு","முதுப கீரதி யும்புனை யுஞ்சடை","முடியர் வேதமு நின்றும ணங்கமழ் ...... அபிராமி","முகர நூபுர பங்கய சங்கரி","கிரிகு மாரித்ரியம்பகி தந்தருள்","முருக னேசுர குஞ்சரிரஞ்சித ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"Calling on the lord of the cock banner who fought the Nirutar and Krauncha and wed the kuravar girl, the poet asks that with tears pouring from both eyes and a mind brimming with love, as the body of elements, the life within it and the inner faculties fall away and he himself is lost, he may know by awareness what the debaters and sects call 'it is' and 'it is not'. The praise names Kama with his makara banner, Tamil breeze from the Malaya hills, flower arrows and sugarcane bow, burned by the eye of Siva, and Murugan as the son given by Abhirami, Sankari, the mountain daughter, delight of the gods' elephant-girl.","n":"யானும் இழந்த இடந்தனில் உணர்வாலே, by awareness in the place where I too am lost, puts knowledge after the loss of body, breath, faculties and self, not after argument. ஆமென அன்றென நின்றதை, that which stands as both 'yes' and 'no', is what the sects dispute.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","gnaanam","samayam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:247"},"p:1178":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1178,"t":["புருவத்தை நெறித்து விழிக்கயல்","பயிலிட்டு வெருட்டி மதித்திரு","புதுவட்டை மினுக்கி யளிக்குல ...... மிசைபாடும்","புயல்சற்று விரித்து நிரைத்தொளி","வளையிட்ட கரத்தை யசைத்தகில்","புனைமெத்தை படுத்த பளிக்கறை ...... தனிலேறிச்","சரசத்தை விளைத்து முலைக்கிரி","புளகிக்க அணைத்து நகக்குறி","தனைவைத்து முகத்தை முகத்துட ...... னுறமேவித்","தணிவித்தி ரதத்த தரத்துமி","ழமுதத்தை யளித்து வுருக்கிகள்","தருபித்தை யகற்றி யுனைத்தொழ ...... முயல்வேனோ","பரதத்தை யடக்கி நடிப்பவர்","த்ரிபுரத்தை யெரிக்க நகைப்பவர்","பரவைக்குள் விடத்தை மிடற்றிடு ...... பவர்தேர்கப்","பரையுற்ற கரத்தர் மிகப்பகி","ரதியுற்ற சிரத்தர் நிறத்துயர்","பரவத்தர் பொருப்பி லிருப்பவ ...... ருமையாளர்","சுரர் சுத்தர் மனத்துறை வித்தகர்","பணிபத்தர் பவத்தை யறுப்பவர்","சுடலைப்பொடி யைப்பரிசிப்பவர் ...... விடையேறுந்","துணையொத்த பதத்த ரெதிர்த்திடு","மதனைக்கடி முத்தர் கருத்தமர்","தொலைவற்ற க்ருபைக்கு ளுதித்தருள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women who knit their brows, flash carp eyes, spread their hair, wave bangled hands, lead a man to an aloe-scented bed in a crystal chamber, embrace him, mark him with nails and give the nectar of their lips, and asks whether, rid of the madness these melting women give, he will strive to worship Murugan. The praise is a long portrait of Siva, the dancer, burner of Tripura, holder of the poison, Ganga on his head, Uma's lord on the mountain, who cuts birth, smears cremation ash, rides the bull and burned Madana, and names Murugan as born of his boundless grace.","n":"உருக்கிகள், the women who melt a man, and தருபித்து, the madness they give, frame the plea, which is only to try (முயல்வேனோ) to worship. Murugan is called the one who arose within Siva's compassion (க்ருபைக்குள் உதித்தருள்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","kaamam","mayal"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:247"},"p:1179":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1179,"t":["புவிக்குன் பாத மதைநினை பவர்க்குங் கால தரிசனை","புலக்கண் கூடு மதுதனை ...... அறியாதே","புரட்டும் பாத சமயிகள் நெறிக்கண் பூது படிறரை","புழுக்கண் பாவ மதுகொளல் ...... பிழையாதே","கவிக்கொண் டாடு புகழினை படிக்கும் பாடு திறமிலி","களைக்கும் பாவ சுழல்படு ...... மடிநாயேன்","கலக்குண் டாகு புவிதனி லெனக்குண் டாகு பணிவிடை","கணக்குண் டாதல் திருவுள ...... மறியாதோ","சிவத்தின் சாமி மயில்மிசை நடிக்குஞ் சாமி யெமதுளெ","சிறக்குஞ் சாமி சொருபமி ...... தொளிகாணச்","செழிக்குஞ் சாமி பிறவியை யொழிக்குஞ் சாமி பவமதை","தெறிக்குஞ் சாமி முனிவர்க ...... ளிடமேவுந்","தவத்தின் சாமி புரிபிழை பொறுக்குஞ் சாமி குடிநிலை","தரிக்குஞ் சாமி யசுரர்கள் ...... பொடியாகச்","சதைக்குஞ் சாமி யெமைபணி விதிக்குஞ் சாமி சரவண","தகப்பன் சாமி யெனவரு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses that he does not know the sight that those who think of Murugan's feet attain, falls in with the deceiving sectarians who twist the path, has no skill to sing his praise and whirls in sin like a lazy dog, and asks whether Murugan does not know the account of service due to him in this troubled world. The praise is a litany of சாமி: the lord of Sivam, who dances on the peacock, shines within us, ends birth, forgives faults, grinds the demons to dust, ordains our service, and the one of Saravana who is called 'the father's Swami'.","n":"தகப்பன் சாமி, his father's lord, is the title from the story of Murugan teaching Siva. எனக்கு உண்டாகு பணிவிடை கணக்கு, the reckoning of service that falls to me, asks to be given work rather than release. The first lines (காலதரிசனை, புலக்கண், புழுக்கண் பாவம்) are hard to construe.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thondu","samayam","piravi","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:247"},"p:1180":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1180,"t":["பூசல்த ருங்கய லும்பொ ருந்திய","வாசந றுங்குழ லுந்து லங்கிய","பூரண கும்பமெ னுந்த னங்களு ...... மடமாதர்","போகம டங்கலை யும்பு ணர்ந்தநு","போயக லும்படி யொன்றை யன்புற ...... நினையாதே","ஆசையெ னும்படி யுந்த னங்களு","மோகைந டந்திட வுந்தி னங்களும்","ஆருட னும்பகை கொண்டு நின்றுற ...... நடமாடி","ஆடிய பம்பர முன்சு ழன்றெதி","ரோடிவி ழும்படி கண்ட தொன்றுற","ஆவிய கன்றுவி டும்ப யங்கெட ...... அருள்வாயே","வாசவ னன்புவி ளங்க நின்றசு","ரேசர்கு லங்கள டங்க லுங்கெட","வானவர் நின்றுதி யங்கு கின்றதொர் ...... குறைதீர","வாரிய திர்ந்துப யந்து நின்றிட","மேருஅ டங்கஇ டிந்து சென்றிட","வாகைபு னைந்தொரு வென்றி கொண்டரு ...... ளிளையோனே","வீசிய தென்றலொ டந்தி யும்பகை","யாகமு யங்கஅ நங்க னும்பொர","வேடையெ னும்படி சிந்தை நொந்திட ...... அடைவாக","வேடர்செ ழும்புன வஞ்சி யஞ்சன","வேலினு ளங்கள்க லங்கி யின்புற","வேளையெ னும்படி சென்றி றைஞ்சிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says he gives no thought to what would end the pleasure he takes in women's carp eyes, fragrant hair and pot-like breasts, but quarrels with all, spends his days and whirls like a spun top until he falls; he asks Murugan to end his fear of the hour life leaves. The praise names the destruction of the Sura clans so that Indra and the gods were freed, the sea trembling and Meru crumbling, and Murugan who, pained by the south wind, evening and Kama's attack, went to the hunters' millet field and bowed to the dark-eyed Valli.","n":"The same south wind and Kama that drive the poet to women make Murugan himself love-sick (வேடை எனும்படி சிந்தை நொந்திட) for Valli: the song puts the god in the lover's place. The top (பம்பரம்) spinning and falling is the poet's life.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","saakkaadu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:247"},"p:1181":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1181,"t":["பூசல் வந்திரு தோடார் காதொடு","மோதி டுங்கயல் மானார் மானமில்","போக மங்கையர் கோடா கோடிய ...... மனதானார்","பூர குங்கும தூளா மோதப","டீர சண்பக மாலா லாளித","பூத ரங்களின் மீதே மூழ்கிய ...... அநுராக","ஆசை யென்கிற பாரா வாரமு","மேறு கின்றில னானா பேதஅ","நேக தந்த்ரக்ரியாவே தாகம ...... கலையாய","ஆழி யுங்கரை காணே னூபுர","பாத பங்கய மோதே னேசில","னாயி னுங்குரு நாதா நீயருள் ...... புரிவாயே","வாச வன்பதி பாழா காமல்நி","சாச ரன்குலம் வாழா தேயடி","மாள வன்கிரிகூறாய் நீறெழ ...... நெடுநேமி","மாத வன்தரு வேதா வோடலை","மோது தெண்கடல் கோகோ கோவென","மாமு றிந்திட நீள்வே லேவிய ....... ; ளையோனே","வீசு தென்றலும் வேள்பூ வாளியு","மீறு கின்றமை யாமோ காமவி","டாய்கெ டும்படி காவா யாவியை ...... யெனஏனல்","மீது சென்றுற வாடா வேடுவர்","பேதை கொங்கையின் மீதே மால்கொடு","வேடை கொண்டபி ரானே வானவர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says he cannot cross the ocean of desire for proud pleasure-women with carp eyes and ear-studs, or the ocean of tantra, ritual, Veda and Agama whose shore he never sees; he has not sung the anklet feet and has no love, yet asks the guru for grace. The praise names the saving of Indra's city, the end of the night-walkers, the mountain split and the sea roaring as the vel broke the mango, and Murugan in the millet field begging the hunter's girl to save his life from the south wind and Kama's flower arrows.","n":"The two oceans are set side by side: desire (ஆசை என்கிற பாராவாரம்) and the endless scriptures (தந்த்ர க்ரியா வேதாகம கலையாய ஆழி); neither can be crossed, and the poet admits even love is lacking (நேசிலன்). Murugan's own plea to Valli, காமவிடாய் கெடும்படி காவாய் ஆவியை, echoes the poet's.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kaamanoy","aagamam","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:247"},"p:1182":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1182,"t":["பொங்குங் கொடிய கூற்றனு நஞ்சும் பொதுவில் நோக்கிய","பெங்கும் புதிய நேத்திர ...... வலைவீசிப்","பொன்கண் டிளகு கூத்திகள் பூன்கண் கலவி வேட்டுயிர்","பூண்கொண் டுருகி யாட்படு ...... மயல்தீரக்","கொங்கின் புசக கோத்திரிபங்கங் களையு மாய்க்குடி","கொங்கின் குவளை பூக்கிற ...... கிரிசோண","குன்றங் கதிரை பூப்பர முன்துன் றமரர் போற்றிய","குன்றம் பிறவும் வாழ்த்துவ ...... தொருநாளே","எங்கும் பகர மாய்க்கெடி விஞ்சும் பகழி வீக்கிய","வெஞ்சண் டதனு வேட்டுவர் ...... சரணார","விந்தம் பணிய வாய்த்தரு ளந்தண் புவன நோற்பவை","மென்குங் குமகு யாத்திரி...... பிரியாதே","எங்குங் கலுமி யார்த்தெழ எங்குஞ் சுருதி கூப்பிட","எங்குங் குருவி யோச்சிய ...... திருமானை","என்றென் றவச மாய்த்தொழு தென்றும் புதிய கூட்டமொ","டென்றும் பொழுது போக்கிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of dancing women who cast nets of eyes that look like Death and poison and melt only at the sight of gold, and of his enslavement in their embrace, and asks whether, his infatuation ended, there will be a day when he praises the sacred hills, among them Sonagiri, Kathirai and the Kundram. The praise names the hunters with their bows who bow at the lotus feet, and the deer-girl who scared the birds from the millet field, with whom Murugan spends his days with ever-new companions.","n":"பொன்கண்டு இளகு கூத்திகள், dancing girls who soften at the sight of gold, makes them the paid women. The list of hills in lines 576 to 579 is too garbled to read with confidence; சோண (Sonagiri, Tiruvannamalai) and கதிரை (Kathirkamam) are the clearest names.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","mayal","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:247"},"p:1183":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1183,"t":["பொருத கயல்விழி புரட்டிக் காட்டுவர்","புளக தனவட மசைத்துக் காட்டுவர்","புயலி னளகமும் விரித்துக் காட்டுவர் ...... பொதுமாதர்","புனித விதழ்மது நகைத்துக் காட்டுவர்","பொலிவி னிடைதுகில் குலைத்துக் காட்டுவர்","புதிய பரிபுர நடித்துக் காட்டுவ ...... ரிளைஞோரை","உ ருக அணைதனி லணைத்துக் காட்டுவர்","உ டைமை யடையவெ பறித்துத் தாழ்க்கவெ","உ ததி யமுதென நிகழ்த்திக் கேட்பவர் ...... பொடிமாயம்","உ தர மெரிதர மருத்திட் டாட்டிகள்","உ யிரினிலைகளை விரித்துச் சேர்ப்பவர்","உ றவு கலவியை விடுத்திட் டாட்கொள ...... நினையாதோ","மருது பொடிபட வுதைத்திட் டாய்ச்செரி","மகளி ருறிகளை யுடைத்துப் போட்டவர்","மறுக வொருகயி றடித்திட் டார்ப்புற ...... அழுதூறும்","வளரு நெடுமுகி லெதிர்த்துக் காட்டென","அசட னிரணிய னுரத்தைப் பேர்த்தவன்","மழையி னிரைமலை யெடுத்துக் காத்தவன் ...... மருகோனே","விருது பலபல பிடித்துச் சூர்க்கிளை","விகட தடமுடி பறித்துத் தோட்களை","விழவு முறியவு மடித்துத் தாக்கிய ...... அயில்வீரா","வெகுதி சலதியை யெரித்துத் தூட்பட","வினைசெ யசுரர்கள் பதிக்குட் பாய்ச்சிய","விபுத மலரடி விரித்துப் போற்றினர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet lists what common women show young men, rolled eyes, swaying breast-chains, loosened hair, smiles, slipping clothes, dancing feet, and how they embrace them till they melt, strip them of all they own, give drugs that burn the belly and draw out their very life; he asks whether Murugan will not free him from that union and take him. The praise names Krishna who kicked the marutha trees, broke the cowherd women's pots, was beaten with a rope and wept, split Hiranya's chest and held up the hill against the rain, and Murugan his nephew whose vel tore Sura's kin and whose lotus feet are praised.","n":"பொதுமாதர், women common to all, and உடைமை அடையவே பறித்து, stripping a man of everything he owns, are explicit; பொடிமாயம் and மருத்திட்டு ஆட்டிகள் add the love-drug, as in 1161. காட்டுவர் closes every phrase of the first half, as காட்டி does in 1176.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:247"},"p:1184":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1184,"t":["மங்காதிங் காக்குஞ் சிறுவரு","முண்டேயிங் காற்றுந் துணைவியும்","வம்பாருந் தேக்குண் டிடவறி ...... தெணும்வாதை","வந்தேபொன் தேட்டங் கொடுமன","நொந்தேயிங் காட்டம் பெரிதெழ","வண்போதன் தீட்டுந் தொடரது ...... படியேமன்","சங்காரம் போர்ச்சங் கையிலுடல்","வெங்கானம் போய்த்தங் குயிர்கொள","சந்தேகந் தீர்க்குந் தனுவுட ...... னணுகாமுன்","சந்தாரஞ் சாத்தும் புயவியல்","கந்தாஎன் றேத்தும் படியென","சந்தாபந் தீர்த்தென் றடியிணை ...... தருவாயே","கங்காளன் பார்த்தன் கையிலடி","யுண்டேதிண் டாட்டங் கொளுநெடு","கன்சாபஞ் சார்த்துங் கரதல ...... னெருதேறி","கந்தாவஞ் சேர்த்தண் புதுமல","ரம்பான்வெந் தார்ப்பொன் றிடவிழி","கண்டான்வெங் காட்டங் கனலுற ...... நடமாடி","அங்காலங் கோத்தெண் டிசைபுவி","மங்காதுண் டாற்கொன் றதிபதி","அந்தாபந் தீர்த்தம் பொருளினை ...... யருள்வோனே","அன்பாலந் தாட்கும் பிடுமவர்","தம்பாவந் தீர்த்தம் புவியிடை","அஞ்சாநெஞ் சாக்கந் தரவல ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of the children he must support and the wife who must be fed, the toil of seeking gold and the mind's great agitation, all as the chain Brahma writes decrees, and asks that before Death comes to the burning ground to take his life, he may praise 'Kanda of the sandal shoulders' and Murugan end his torment and give his feet. The praise names Siva the skull-bearer who took blows from Arjuna, rode the bull, burned Kama with his eye, danced in the fire and swallowed the poison, and Murugan who gave him the meaning, and who gives his loving devotees release from sin and a fearless heart.","n":"வண்போதன் தீட்டும் தொடரது படியே, according to the chain the lotus-born writes, makes the householder's cares Brahma's script for him. பார்த்தன் கையில் அடி உண்டு is the story of Siva as the hunter struck by Arjuna. The first half is hard to construe in places (வம்பாருந் தேக்குண் டிட).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["sutram","oozh","kootru","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:247"},"p:1185":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1185,"t":["மதன தனுநிக ரிடைக்கே மன","முருக வருபிடி நடைக்கே யிரு","வனச பரிபுர மலர்க்கே மது ...... கரம்வாழும்","வகுள ம்ருகமத மழைக்கே மணி","மகர மணிவன குழைக்கே மட","மகளிர் முகுளித முலைக்கே கட ...... லமுதூறும்","அதர மதுரித மொழிக்கே குழை","யளவு மளவிய விழிக்கே தள","வனைய தொருசிறு நகைக்கே பனி ...... மதிபோலும்","அழகு திகழ்தரு நுதற்கே யந","வரத மவயவ மனைத்தூ டினு","மவச முறுமயல் தவிர்த்தாள் வது ...... மொருநாளே","உ ததி புதைபட அடைத்தா தவன்","நிகரிலிரதமும் விடுக்கா நகர்","ஒருநொ டியில்வெயி லெழச்சா நகி ...... துயர்தீர","உ பய வொருபது வரைத்தோள் களு","நிசிச ரர்கள்பதி தசக்஡ண வமு","முருள ஒருகணை தெரித்தா னும ...... வுனஞான","திதமி லவுணர்த மிருப்பா கிய","புரமு மெரியெழ முதற்பூ தர","திலத குலகிரிவளைத்தா னும ...... கிழவானோர்","திருவ நகர்குடி புகச்சீ கர","மகர சலமுறை யிடச்சூ ரொடு","சிகர கிரிபொடி படச்சா டிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet runs through a woman's charms, the waist like Kama's bow, the elephant gait, the lotus feet, the musk-scented hair, the makara earrings, the bud-like breasts, the honey lips, the ear-long eyes, the jasmine smile and the moon forehead, and asks whether a day will come when Murugan ends the swooning infatuation he feels for every limb and takes him. The praise names Rama who bridged the sea, burned the city, ended Sita's grief and rolled the ten heads of Ravana with one arrow, Siva who burned the three cities with Meru for a bow, and Murugan who restored the gods to their city and smashed Sura and the mountain.","n":"The catalogue, each item ending in -க்கே (for the waist, for the gait ...), is itself the infatuation: அவயவம் அனைத்தூடினும் அவசமுறு மயல், a swoon at every limb. Rama and Siva are praised as Murugan's kin in one sweep.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["mayal","kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:247"},"p:1186":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1186,"t":["மதனேவிய கணையாலிரு வினையால்புவி கடல்சாரமும்","வடிவாயுடல் நடமாடுக ...... முடியாதேன்","மனமாயையொ டிருகாழ்வினை யற்முதுடை மலம்வேரற","மகிழ்ஞானக அநுபூதியி ...... னருள்மேவிப்","பதமேவுமு னடியாருடன் விளையாடுக அடியேன்முனெ","பரிபூரண கிருபாகர ...... முடன்ஞான","பரிமேலழ குடனேறிவி ணவர்பூமழை யடிமேல்விட","பலகோடிவெண் மதிபோலவெ ...... வருவாயே","சதகோடிவெண் மடவார்கட லெனசாமரை யசையாமுழு","சசிசூரியர் சுடராமென ...... வொருகோடிச்","சடைமாமுடி முநிவோர்சர ணெனவேதியர் மறையோதுக","சதிநாடக மருள்வேணிய ...... னருள்பாலா","விதியானவ னிளையாளென துளமேவிய வளிநாயகி","வெகுமாலுற தனமேலணை ...... முருகோனே","வெளியாசையொ டடைபூவணர் மருகாமணி முதிராடக","வெயில்வீசிய அழகாதமிழ் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says that by Kama's arrows and the two kinds of deeds his body of earth and sea dances on, and asks that the maya of the mind and the hard deeds be cut away with the old impurity at its root, that he gain the joy of knowledge in experience, and play with the devotees at the feet; he asks Murugan to come before him on his steed, full of compassion and knowledge, with the gods raining flowers, bright as crores of white moons. The praise names the countless women waving fans, sun and moon as lamps, the matted sages and Vedic chanters around Siva, and Murugan his son, lord of Valli, Vishnu's nephew, the lord of Tamil.","n":"மனமாயை, இருகாழ்வினை, மலம் வேரற names the three bonds of Saiva Siddhanta (maya, karma, anava) and asks for them to be cut at the root, followed by ஞான அநுபூதி, knowledge as lived experience. The garbled யற்முதுடை (line 671) leaves one word unread.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["malam","vinai","gnaanam","adiyaar"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:247"},"p:1187":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1187,"t":["மாடமதிட் சுற்று மொக்க வைத்திட","வீடுகனக் கத்த னத்தி லச்சுறு","மாலிபமொத் துப்ர புத்த னத்தினி ...... லடைவாக","மாதர்பெருக் கத்த ருக்க மற்றவர்","சூழவிருக் கத்த ரிக்க இப்படி","வாழ்க்கையில்மத் தப்ர மத்த சித்திகொள் ...... கடைநாளிற்","பாடையினிற் கட்டி விட்டு நட்டவர்","கூடஅரற் றிப்பு டைத்து றுப்புள","பாவையெடுத் துத்த ழற்கி ரைப்பட ...... விடலாய","பாடுதொலைத் துக்க ழிக்க அக்ருபை","தேடுமெனைத் தற்பு ரக்க வுற்றிரு","பாதுகையைப் பற்றி நிற்க வைத்தெனை ...... யருளாதோ","ஆடகவெற் பைப்பெ ருத்த மத்தென","நாகவடத் தைப்பி ணித்து ரத்தம","ரார்கள்பிடித் துத்தி ரித்தி டப்புகை ...... யனலாக","ஆழிகொதித் துக்க தற்றி விட்டிமை","யோர்களொளிக் கக்க ளித்த உ க்கிர","ஆலவிடத் தைத்த ரித்த அற்புதர் ...... குமரேசா","வேடர்சிறுக் கிக்கி லச்சை யற்றெழு","பாரும்வெறுத் துச்சி ரிப்ப நட்பொடு","வேளையெனப் புக்கு நிற்கும் வித்தக ....... ; ளையோனே","வேகமிகுத் துக்க திக்கும் விக்ரம","சூரர்சிரத் தைத்து ணித்த டக்குதல்","வீரமெனத் தத்து வத்து மெச்சிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet pictures a life of walled mansions, gold in the house and many women around him, lived drunk and deluded, until on the last day the body is tied to the bier, friends wail and beat their chests and the limbed doll is given to the fire; he asks whether grace will not end this misery and set him holding Murugan's two sandals. The praise tells the churning of the sea with the golden mountain and the serpent rope, the poison that sent the gods into hiding, and Siva who held it, Murugan's father; it ends with his wooing of the hunter girl while the seven worlds laughed, and the heads of Sura cut off.","n":"இருபாதுகையைப் பற்றி நிற்க: the poet asks to hold Murugan's sandals (பாதுகை), not his feet, a humbler image of refuge. பாவை, 'doll', for the corpse makes the body a puppet once its life is gone. The line on the hunter girl and the laughing worlds is hard to divide, and its end is corrupt.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","yaakkai","selvam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:248"},"p:1188":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1188,"t":["மாண்டாரெ லும்பணி யுஞ்சடை","யாண்டாரிறைஞ்ச மொழிந்ததை","வான்பூத லம்பவ னங்கனல் ...... புனலான","வான்பூத முங்கர ணங்களு","நான்போயொ டுங்கஅ டங்கலு","மாய்ந்தால்வி ளங்கும தொன்றினை ...... யருளாயேல்","வேண்டாமை யொன்றைய டைந்துள","மீண்டாறி நின்சர ணங்களில்","வீழந்தாவல் கொண்டுரு கன்பினை ...... யுடையேனாய்","வேந்தாக டம்புபு னைந்தருள்","சேந்தாச ரண்சர ணென்பது","வீண்போம தொன்றல என்பதை ...... யுணராதோ","ஆண்டார்த லங்கள ளந்திட","நீண்டார்மு குந்தர்த டந்தனில்","ஆண்டாவி துஞ்சிய தென்றுமு ...... தலைவாயுற்","றாங்கோர்சி லம்புபு லம்பிட","ஞான்று஡து துங்கச லஞ்சலம்","ஆம்பூமு ழங்கிய டங்கும ...... ளவில்நேசம்","பூண்டாழி கொண்டுவ னங்களி","லேய்ந்தாள வென்றுவெ றுந்தனி","போந்தோல மென்றுத வும்புயல் ...... மருகோனே","பூம்பாளை யெங்கும ணங்கமழ்","தேங்காவில் நின்றதொர் குன்றவர்","பூந்தோகை கொங்கைவி ரும்பிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks for the One that Murugan spoke when Siva, who wears the bones of the dead, bowed to him, the thing that shines when the five elements, the inner instruments and the 'I' all subside; having reached desirelessness and fallen melting at the feet, he asks whether Murugan will not know that the cry 'Sentha, refuge, refuge' is never in vain. The praise tells of Vishnu who measured the worlds coming alone with the discus when the elephant in the pond cried 'Lord, my life is going', and ends with the peacock-like girl of the hills in the fragrant grove.","n":"நான்போய் ஒடுங்க அடங்கலும் மாய்ந்தால் விளங்கும் அது ஒன்றினை: the plea is for what appears only when the elements, the கரணங்கள் and the self are gone, a doctrinal request rather than a confession. வேண்டாமை, desirelessness, is named as already reached. The elephant's cry is Gajendra's.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["guru","gnaanam","paththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:248"},"p:1189":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1189,"t":["மாறுபொரு கால னொக்கும் வானிலெழு மாம திக்கும்","வாரிதுயி லாவ தற்கும் ...... வசையேசொல்","மாயமட வார்த மக்கும் ஆயர்குழ லு஡தி சைக்கும்","வாயுமிள வாடை யிற்கு ...... மதனாலே","வேறுபடு பாய லுக்கு மேயெனது பேதை யெய்த்து","வேறுபடு மேனி சற்று ...... மழியாதே","வேடர்குல மாதி னுக்கு வேடைகெட வேந டித்து","மேவுமிரு பாத முற்று ...... வரவேணும்","ஆறுமிடை வாள ரக்கர் நீறுபட வேலெ டுத்த","ஆறுமுக னேகு றத்தி ...... மணவாளா","ஆழியுல கேழ டக்கி வாசுகியை வாய டக்கி","ஆலுமயி லேறி நிற்கு ...... மிளையோனே","சீறுபட மேரு வெற்பை நீறுபட வேசி னத்த","சேவலவ நீப மொய்த்த ...... திரள்தோளா","சேருமட லால்மி குத்த சூரர்அகாடு போய டைத்த","தேவர்சிறை மீள விட்ட ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"In a mother's voice, the singer says her innocent girl is worn down by love: by the moon that rises like Death, the sleepless sea, the deceitful women who speak blame, the cowherd's flute, the cold north wind, Kama, and the bed that has turned strange; she asks Murugan, who once played the hunter to end the hunter girl's longing, to come on his two feet before her changed body wastes away. The praise names the six-faced lord of the vel, the peacock that holds Vasuki in its beak, and the release of the gods from Sura's prison.","n":"This is akam, the classical pangs of the lovesick girl spoken by her mother, as in 24: the moon, the sea, the wind and the herdsman's flute all wound her, and வசையே சொல் மாய மடவார், the gossiping women, are the town's blame (alar). The daughter's lover is the god himself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kaamanoy","alar","maalai","melivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:248"},"p:1190":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1190,"t":["மின்னினில்ந டுக்க முற்ற நுண்ணியநு சுப்பில் முத்த","வெண்ணகையில் வட்ட மொத்து ...... அழகார","விம்மியிள கிக்க தித்த கொம்மைமுலை யிற்கு னித்த","வின்னுதலி லிட்ட பொட்டில் ...... விலைமாதர்","கன்னல்மொழி யிற்சி றக்கு மன்னநடை யிற்க றுத்த","கண்ணினிணை யிற்சி வத்த ...... கனிவாயிற்","கண்ணழிவு வைத்த புத்தி ஷண்முகநி னைக்க வைத்த","கன்மவச மெப்ப டிக்கு ...... மறவேனே","அன்னநடை யைப்ப ழித்த மஞ்ஞைமலை யிற்கு றத்தி","யம்மையட விப்பு னத்தில் ...... விளையாடும்","அன்னையிறு கப்பி ணித்த பன்னிருதி ருப்பு யத்தில்","அன்னியஅ ரக்க ரத்த ...... னையுமாளப்","பொன்னுலகி னைப்பு ரக்கு மன்னநல்வ்ர தத்தை விட்ட","புன்மையர்பு ரத்ர யத்தர் ...... பொடியாகப்","பொன்மலைவ ளைத்தெ ரித்த கண்ணுதலி டத்தி லுற்ற","புண்ணியவொ ருத்தி பெற்ற ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet lists the charms of women who sell themselves, the trembling waist, pearl smile, curved brow, sugared speech, swan's walk, dark eyes and red mouth, and says that the karma which, while his mind lay degraded in them, made him think of the six-faced one is something he will never forget. The praise has Valli playing in the millet field, binding Murugan's twelve arms, and the golden mountain bent as a bow to burn the three cities of those who abandoned their vow.","n":"கண்ணழிவு வைத்த புத்தி ஷண்முக நினைக்க வைத்த கன்மவசம்: the poet appears to thank karma itself for turning his fallen mind toward Murugan, an unusual twist where the song ends in a vow (மறவேனே) rather than a request. The Tripura dwellers are called those who left the good vow (நல்வ்ரதத்தை விட்ட புன்மையர்).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:248"},"p:1191":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1191,"t":["முத்தமு லாவு தனத்தியர் சித்தச னாணை செலுத்திகள்","முத்தமி டாம னுருக்கிக ...... ளிளைஞோர்பால்","முட்டவி லாவி மருட்டிகள் நெட்டிலை வேலின் விழிச்சியர்","முப்பது கோடி மனத்திய ...... ரநுராகத்","தத்தைக ளாசை விதத்தியர் கற்புர தோளின் மினுக்கிகள்","தப்புறு மாறக மெத்திக ...... ளளவேநான்","தட்டழி யாது திருப்புகழ் கற்கவு மோதவு முத்தமிழ்","தத்துவ ஞான மெனக்கருள் ...... புரிவாயே","மத்தக யானை யுரித்தவர் பெற்றகு மார இலட்சுமி","மைத்துன னாகிய விக்ரமன் ...... மருகோனே","வற்றிட வாரிதி முற்றிய வெற்றிகொள் சூரர் பதைப்புற","வற்புறு வேலைவி டுத்தரு ...... ளிளையோனே","சித்திர மான குறத்தியை யுற்றொரு போது புனத்திடை","சிக்கென வேதழு விப்புணர் ...... மணவாளா","செச்சையு லாவு பதத்தின மெய்த்தவர் வாழ்வு பெறத்தரு","சித்தவி சாக வியற்சுரர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women who enforce Kama's command, melt young men, have spear eyes and 'thirty crore minds', and asks that, not ruined by them, he be granted to learn and recite Thiruppugazh and the knowledge of the tattvas in threefold Tamil. The praise names Siva who flayed the elephant, Vishnu as uncle, the vel that dried the sea against Sura, Valli embraced in the field, and Murugan who gives the true ascetics their life.","n":"திருப்புகழ் கற்கவும் ஓதவும்: the song asks for the book's own name, the praise of the Lord as a thing to learn and recite. முப்பது கோடி மனத்தியர், women of thirty crore minds, is the poet's figure for their fickleness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","pugazh","thamizh","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:248"},"p:1192":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1192,"t":["முருகு லாவிய மைப்பாவு வார்குழல்","முளரிவாய்நெகிழ் வித்தார வேல்விழி","முடுகு வோர்குலை வித்தான கோடேனு ...... முலையாலே","முறைமை சேர்கெட மைத்தார்வு வார்கடல்","முடுகு வோரென எய்த்தோடி யாகமு","மொழியும் வேறிடு பித்தேறி னாரெனு ...... முயல்வேகொண்","டுருகு வார்சிக சிற்றாம னோலய","முயிரு மாகமு மொத்தாசை யோடுள","முருகி தீமெழு கிட்டான தோவென ...... வுரையாநண்","புலக வாவொழி வித்தார் மனோலய","முணர்வு நீடிய பொற்பாத சேவடி","யுலவு நீயெனை வைத்தாள வேயருள் ...... தருவாயே","குருகு லாவிய நற்றாழி சூழ்நகர்","குமர னேமுனை வெற்பார் பராபரை","குழக பூசுரர் மெய்க்காணும் வீரர்தம் ...... வடிவேலா","குறவர் சீர்மக ளைத்தேடி வாடிய","குழையு நீள்கர வைத்தோடி யேயவர்","குடியி லேமயி லைக்கொடு சோதிய ...... வுரவோனே","மருகு மாமது ரைக்கூடல் மால்வரை","வளைவு ளாகிய நக்கீர ரோதிய","வளகை சேர்தமி ழுக்காக நீடிய ...... கரவோனே","மதிய மேவிய சுற்றாத வேணியர்","மகிழ நீநொடி யற்றான போதினில்","மயிலை நீடுல கைச்சூழ வேவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women whose hair, lips and spear eyes undo men, who drive them like the sea and make them seem mad, and whose embrace melts body and life like wax in fire, and asks that, with craving for the world removed, Murugan keep him and rule him at the golden feet where the mind is dissolved. The praise recalls the vel that stretched out for the Tamil of Nakkirar at the Madurai Koodal hill, Murugan's search for the hunter girl, and the moment when, to please moon-crowned Siva, he sent his peacock round the world.","n":"மனோலயம், dissolution of the mind, occurs twice: first in the women's embrace, then at the feet the poet asks for, as if the same melting were sought in its true place. நக்கீரர் ஓதிய தமிழுக்காக நீடிய கரவோனே: the hand stretched out for Nakkirar's Tamil, the poet of Murugan's own praise. The peacock sent round the world in an instant points to the contest for the fruit, though the fruit is not named. Several runs in the first half are hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","thiruvadi","thamizh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:248"},"p:1193":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1193,"t":["முலைமேலிற் கலிங்க மொன்றிட","முதல்வானிற் பிறந்த மின்பிறை","நுதல்மேல்முத் தரும்ப புந்தியி ...... லிதமார","முகநேசித் திலங்க வும்பல","விநன்முசிப் புரண்ட வண்கடல்","முரணோசைக் கமைந்த வன்சர ...... மென்முவா","மலர்போலச் சிவந்த செங்கணில்","மருள்கூர்கைக் கிருண்ட அஞ்சனம்","வழுவாமற் புனைந்து திண்கய ...... மெனநாடி","வருமாதர்க் கிரங்கி நெஞ்சமு","மயலாகிப் பரந்து நின்செயல்","மருவாமற் கலங்கும் வஞ்சக ...... மொழியாதோ","தொலையாநற் றவங்க ணின்றுனை","நிலையாகப் புகழ்ந்து கொண்டுள","அடியாருட் டுலங்கி நின்றருள் ...... துணைவேளே","துடிநேரொத் திலங்கு மென்கொடி","யிடைதோகைக் கிசைந்த வொண்டொடி","சுரர்வாழப் பிறந்த சுந்தரி...... மணவாளா","மலைமாளப் பிளந்த செங்கையில்","வடிவேலைக் கொடந்த வஞ்சக","வடிவாகக் கரந்து வந்தமர் ...... பொருசூரன்","வலிமாளத் துரந்த வன்திறல்","முருகாமற் பொருந்து திண்புய","வடிவாமற் றநந்த மிந்திரர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women with cloth over their breasts, crescent brows and eyes blacked with collyrium, whom he sought out until his heart, infatuated and scattered, could not join Murugan's work, and asks whether this deceit will never leave him. The praise names Murugan as companion of the devotees who praise him steadily through tapas, bridegroom of Devayanai born for the gods' welfare, and the vel that broke the mountain and defeated Sura who came hidden in a deceiving form.","n":"நின்செயல் மருவாமல் கலங்கும் வஞ்சகம்: the deceit is the poet's own, a heart that will not join the Lord's work. Lines 5 and 6 (விநன்முசிப், வன்சர மென்முவா) appear garbled.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mayal","vanjam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:248"},"p:1194":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1194,"t":["முனைய ழிந்தது மேட்டிகு லைந்தது","வயது சென்றது வாய்ப்ப லுதிர்ந்தது","முதுகு வெஞ்சிலை காட்டிவ ளைந்தது ...... ப்ரபையான","முகமி ழிந்தது நோக்குமி ருண்டது","இருமல் வந்தது தூக்கமொ ழிந்தது","மொழித ளர்ந்தது நாக்குவி ழுந்தது ...... அறிவேபோய்","நினைவ யர்ந்தது நீட்டல் முடங்கலு","மவச மும்பல ஏக்கமு முந்தின","நெறிம றந்தது மூப்பு முதிர்ந்தது ...... பலநோயும்","நிலுவை கொண்டது பாய்க்கிடை கண்டது","சலம லங்களி னாற்றமெ ழுந்தது","நிமிஷ மிங்கினி யாச்சுதென் முன்பினி ...... தருள்வாயே","இனைய இந்திர னேற்றமு மண்டர்கள்","தலமு மங்கிட வோட்டியி ருஞ்சிறை","யிடுமி டும்புள ராக்கதர் தங்களில் ...... வெகுகோடி","எதிர்பொ ரும்படி போர்க்குளெ திர்ந்தவர்","தசைசி ரங்களு நாற்றிசை சிந்திட","இடிமு ழங்கிய வேற்படை யொன்றனை ...... யெறிவோனே","தினைவ னங்கிளி காத்தச வுந்தரி","அருகு சென்றடி போற்றிம ணஞ்செய்து","செகம றிந்திட வாழ்க்கைபு ரிந்திடு ...... மிளையோனே","திரிபு ரம்பொடி யாக்கிய சங்கரர்","குமர கந்தப ராக்ரம செந்தமிழ்","தெளிவு கொண்டடி யார்க்குவி ளம்பிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet sets out old age clause by clause: strength gone, pride broken, years spent, teeth fallen, back bent like a bow, face dimmed, sight darkened, cough come, sleep gone, speech failing, tongue fallen, mind fading, limbs that will not stretch, diseases lodged, the mat reached and the stench of filth risen, and says 'now it is a matter of a moment, come before me in grace'. The praise has the vel thrown against the demons who imprisoned Indra, Valli wooed in the millet field and married in the sight of the world, and Murugan who expounded clear Sentamil to his devotees.","n":"Each clause ends in அது (ஒழிந்தது, உதிர்ந்தது, வளைந்தது), so the decay is told as a running inventory, one verb at a time. நிமிஷம் இங்கு இனி ஆச்சுது: the plea is for grace at the very last minute, with no confession of women.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","nilaiyaamai","noy"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:248"},"p:1195":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1195,"t":["மைக்குக்கைப் புக்கக் கயல்விழி","யெற்றிக்கொட் டிட்டுச் சிலைமதன்","வர்க்கத்தைக் கற்பித் திடுதிற ...... மொழியாலே","மட்டிட்டுத் துட்டக் கெருவித","மிட்டிட்டுச் சுற்றிப் பரிமள","மச்சப்பொற் கட்டிற் செறிமல ...... ரணைமீதே","புக்குக்கைக் கொக்கப் புகுமொரு","அற்பச்சிற் றிற்பத் தெரிவையர்","பொய்க்குற்றுச் சுற்றித் திரிகிற ...... புலையேனைப்","பொற்பித்துக் கற்பித் துனதடி","அர்ச்சிக்கச் சற்றுக் க்ருபைசெய","புத்திக்குச் சித்தித் தருளுவ ...... தொருநாளே","திக்குக்குத் திக்குத் திகுதிகு","டுட்டுட்டுட் டுட்டுட் டுடுடுடு","தித்தித்தித் தித்தித் திதியென ...... நடமாடுஞ்","சித்தர்க்குச் சுத்தப் பரமநல்","முத்தர்க்குச் சித்தக் க்ருபையுள","சித்தர்க்குப் பத்தர்க் கருளிய ...... குருநாதா","ஒக்கத்தக் கிட்டுத் திரியசுர்","முட்டக்கொட் டற்றுத் திரிபுர","மொக்கக்கெட் டிட்டுத் திகுதிகு ...... வெனவேக","உ ற்பித்துக் கற்பித் தமரரை","முற்பட்டக் கட்டச் சிறைவிடு","மொட்குக்டக் கொற்றக் கொடியுள ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses wandering round the lies of petty women who teach Kama's arts with their carp eyes and draw men to perfumed golden beds, calls himself base, and asks for the one day when Murugan will make him fit, teach him to worship his feet and grant his mind grace. The praise, in drum syllables, shows Siva's dance and calls Murugan the guru who gave grace to siddhas, the pure freed ones and devotees, the destroyer of Tripura's demons who freed the gods, with the cock banner.","n":"உனதடி அர்ச்சிக்க: what the poet asks to be taught is ritual worship of the feet. The battle lines (திக்குக்குத் திகுதிகு, டுட்டுட்டு) are rhythm, not words.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kutram","vazhipaadu","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:248"},"p:1196":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1196,"t":["மோது மறலியொரு கோடி வேற்படை","கூடி முடுகியெம தாவி பாழ்த்திட","மோக முடையவெகு மாதர் கூட்டமு ...... மயலாரும்","மூளு மளவில்விசை மேல்வி ழாப்பரி","தாப முடனும்விழி நீர்கொ ளாக்கொடு","மோக வினையில்நெடு நாளின் மூத்தவ ...... ரிளையோர்கள்","ஏது கருமமிவர் சாவெ னாச்சிலர்","கூடி நடவுமிடு காடெ னாக்கடி","தேழு நரகினிடை வீழ்மெ னாப்பொறி ...... யறுபாவி","ஏழு புவனமிகு வான நாட்டவர்","சூழ முநிவர்கிளை தாமு மேத்திட","ஈச னருள்குமர வேத மார்த்தெழ ...... வருவாயே","சூது பொருதரும னாடு தோற்றிரு","வாறு வருஷம்வன வாச மேற்றியல்","தோகை யுடனுமெவி ராட ராச்சிய ...... முறைநாளிற்","சூறை நிரைகொடவ ரேக மீட்டெதி","ராளு முரிமைதரு மாறு கேட்டொரு","தூது செலஅடுவ லாண்மை தாக்குவ ...... னெனமீள","வாது சமர்திருத ரான ராட்டிர","ராஜ குமரர்துரியோத னாற்பிறர்","மாள நிருபரொடு சேனை தூட்பட ...... வரிசாப","வாகை விஜயனடல் வாசி பூட்டிய","தேரை முடுகுநெடு மால்ப ராக்ரம","மாயன் மருகஅமர் நாடர் பார்த்திப ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet pictures Death with a crore of spears pressing to waste his life while the women he loved still cling, and the elders and young saying 'what's left to do, is he dead?', carrying him to the burning ground and saying he will fall into the seven hells, a wretch without fortune; he asks Murugan to come with the Vedas sounding while the worlds, the gods and the sages praise him. The praise retells the Mahabharata: Dharma losing the land at dice, twelve years in the forest, the stay at Virata's court and the cattle raid, the embassy, Duryodhana's death in the war, and Vishnu who drove Arjuna's chariot, Murugan's uncle.","n":"ஏது கருமம் இவர் சாவெனா: the kin's words over the dying man are quoted, coldly practical. இருவாறு வருஷம், two sixes of years, is the Pandavas' twelve-year exile; the praise gives the epic in sequence rather than as single epithets.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","naragam","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:248"},"p:1197":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1197,"t":["வடிகட்டிய தேனென வாயினி","லுறுதுப்பன வூறலை யார்தர","வரைவிற்றிக ழூடலி லேதரு ...... மடவார்பால்","அடிபட்டலை பாவநிர் மூடனை","முகடித்தொழி லாமுன நீயுன","தடிமைத்தொழி லாகாஎ நாளினி ...... லருள்வாயோ","பொடிபட்டிட ராவணன் மாமுடி","சிதறச்சிலை வாளிக ளேகொடு","பொருகைக்கள மேவிய மாயவன் ...... மருகோனே","கொடுமைத்தொழி லாகிய கானவர்","மகிமைக்கொள வேயவர் வாழ்சிறு","குடிலிற்குற மானொடு மேவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet, beaten about by women whose mouths drip honey and whose sulks are their gift, calls himself a sinful utter fool and asks on what day, before he becomes the work of misfortune, Murugan will grant him the work of his service. The praise names Vishnu whose arrows scattered Ravana's crowns, Murugan's uncle, and the lord who lived with the hunter girl in the small hut of the forest people.","n":"முகடித் தொழில் against அடிமைத் தொழில்: the work of Mudevi, goddess of ill fortune, set against the work of service to the Lord, if முகடி is read so. ஆகாஎ நாளினில் is probably a garble of ஆக எந்நாளினில்.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thondu","kutram","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:248"},"p:1198":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1198,"t":["வட்ட முலைக்கச் சவிழ்த்து வைத்துள","முத்து வடத்தைக் கழுத்தி லிட்டிரு","மைக்கு வளைக்கட் குறிப்ப ழுத்திய ...... பொதுமாதர்","மட்டம ளிக்குட் டிருத்தி முத்தணி","மெத்தை தனக்குட் செருக்கி வெற்றிலை","வைத்த பழுப்பச் சிலைச்சு ருட்கடி ...... யிதழ்கோதிக்","கட்டி யணைத்திட் டெடுத்து டுத்திடு","பட்டை யவிழ்த்துக் கருத்தி தத்தொடு","கற்ற கலைச்சொற் களிற்ப யிற்றுள ...... முயல்போதுங்","கைக்கு ளிசைத்துப் பிடித்த கட்கமும்","வெட்சி மலர்ப்பொற் பதத்தி ரட்சணை","கட்டு மணிச்சித் திரத்தி றத்தையு ...... மறவேனே","கொட்ட மிகுத்திட் டரக்கர் பட்டணம்","இட்டு நெருப்புக் கொளுத்தி யத்தலை","கொட்டை பரப்பச் செருக்க ளத்திடை ...... யசுரோரைக்","குத்தி முறித்துக் குடிப்ப ரத்தமும்","வெட்டு யழித்துக் கனக்க ளிப்பொடு","கொக்கரியிட்டுத் தெரித்த டுப்பன ...... வொருகோடிப்","பட்ட பிணத்தைப் பிடித்தி ழுப்பன","சச்சரிகொட்டிட் டடுக்கெ டுப்பன","பற்கள் விரித்துச் சிரித்தி ருப்பன ...... வெகுபூதம்","பட்சி பறக்கத் திசைக்குள் மத்தளம்","வெற்றி முழக்கிக் கொடிப்பி டித்தயில்","பட்டற விட்டுத் துரத்தி வெட்டிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes plainly his nights with common women, the necklace, the betel-reddened lips, the loosened silk, and says that even while his mind practises the arts of love he will not forget the sword in Murugan's hand, the protection of his golden feet adorned with vetchi flowers, and his jewelled anklets. The praise is a battlefield: demon cities burned, asuras pierced, and countless ghouls dragging corpses, drumming and laughing while birds fly.","n":"மறவேனே: as in 1007 the song ends with a vow of remembrance inside the passion, not a plea for escape from it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:248"},"p:1199":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1199,"t":["வளைகர மாட்டி வேட்டி னிடைதுயில் வாட்டி யீட்டி","வரிவிழி தீட்டி யேட்டின் ...... மணம்வீசும்","மழைகுழல் காட்டி வேட்கை வளர்முலை காட்டி நோக்கின்","மயில்நடை காட்டி மூட்டி ...... மயலாகப்","புளகித வார்த்தை யேற்றி வரிகலை வாழ்த்தி யீழ்த்து","புணர்முலை சேர்த்து வீக்கி ...... விளையாடும்","பொதுமட வார்க்கு ஏற்ற வழியுறு வாழ்க்கை வேட்கை","புலைகுண மோட்டி மாற்றி ...... யருள்வாயே","தொளையொழு கேற்ற நோக்கி பலவகை வாச்சி தூர்த்து","சுடரடி நீத்த லேத்து ...... மடியார்கள்","துணைவன்மை நோக்கி னிடைமுறை யாய்ச்சி மார்ச் சொல்","சொலியமு தூட்டி யாட்டு ...... முருகோனே","இளநகை யோட்டி மூட்டர் குலம்விழ வாட்டி யேட்டை","யிமையவர் பாட்டை மீட்ட ...... குருநாதா","இயல்புவி வாழ்த்தி யேத்த எனதிடர் நோக்கி நோக்க","மிருவினை காட்டி மீட்ட ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes the common women who show their hair, breasts and peacock walk and kindle infatuation with thrilling words, and asks Murugan to drive out and change the base nature that craves a life fit for them. The praise names the devotees who praise his radiant feet with instruments, the women who fed and played with the child Murugan, the god who scattered the fools' clan with a smile and restored the gods' song, and the guru who saw the poet's trouble and rescued him by showing him his two deeds.","n":"இருவினை காட்டி மீட்ட: rescue comes by being shown one's own two kinds of deed. ஆய்ச்சிமார், cowherd women, who feed and bathe the child are hard to identify; they may be the foster-mothers of Murugan's birth or a borrowing from Krishna's story.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kutram","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:248"},"p:1200":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1200,"t":["வாடையில் மதனை யழைத்துற்று","வாள்வளை கலக லெனக்கற்றை","வார்குழல் சரிய முடித்திட்டு ...... துகிலாரும்","மால்கொள நெகிழ வுடுத்திட்டு","நூபுர மிணையடி யைப்பற்றி","வாய்விட நுதல்மிசை பொட்டிட்டு ...... வருமாய","நாடக மகளிர் நடிப்புற்ற","தோதக வலையில கப்பட்டு","ஞாலமு முழுது மிகப்பித்த ...... னெனுமாறு","நாணமு மரபு மொழுக்கற்று","நீதியு மறிவும றக்கெட்டு","நாயடி மையுமடி மைப்பட்டு ...... விடலாமோ","ஆடிய மயிலினை யொப்புற்று","பீலியு மிலையு முடுத்திட்டு","ஆரினு மழகு மிகப்பெற்று ...... யவனாளும்","ஆகிய விதண்மிசை யுற்றிட்டு","மானின மருள விழித்திட்டு","ஆயுத கவணொரு கைச்சுற்றி ...... விளையாடும்","வேடுவர் சிறுமி யொருத்திக்கு","யான்வழி யடிமை யெனச்செப்பி","வீறுள அடியிணை யைப்பற்றி ...... பலகாலும்","வேதமு மமரரு மெய்ச்சக்ர","வாளமு மறிய விலைப்பட்டு","மேருவில் மிகவு மெழுத்திட்ட ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether he should be caught in the deceiving net of play-acting dancing women, called a madman by the whole world, losing shame, custom, conduct, justice and sense, and become the slave of the slaves of dogs. The praise answers with Murugan's own servitude: to the hunter girl dressed in peacock feathers and leaves on the platform with her sling, he said 'I am your hereditary slave', clasped her feet again and again, and with the Vedas, the gods and the world's rim as witness sold himself and had it written on Meru.","n":"The two halves mirror each other: the poet fears becoming a slave (நாயடிமையும் அடிமைப்பட்டு) to women of deceit, while the god freely declares himself Valli's வழியடிமை, sold (விலைப்பட்டு) by a deed inscribed on Meru. Bondage to desire and bondage in love are set side by side.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","naanam","thondu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:248"},"p:1201":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1201,"t":["விரைசொரியு ம்ருகமதமு மலரும் வாய்த்திலகு","விரிகுழலு மவிழநறு மெழுகு கோட்டுமுலை","மிசையில்வரு பகலொளியை வெருவ வோட்டுமணி ...... வகையாரம்","விடுதொடைகள் நகநுதியி லறவும் வாய்த்தொளிர","விழிசெருக மொழிபதற அமுது தேக்கியகை","விதறிவளை கலகலென அழகு மேற்பொழிய ...... அலர்மேவும்","இருசரண பரிபுரசு ருதிக ளார்க்கவச","மிலகுகடல் கரைபுரள இனிமை கூட்டியுள","மிதம்விளைய இருவரெனு மளவு காட்டரிய ...... அநுராகத்","திடைமுழுகி யெனதுமன தழியு நாட்களினு","மிருசரண இயலும்வினை யெறியும் வேற்கரமு","மெழுதரிய திருமுகமு மருளு மேத்தும்வகை ...... தரவேணும்","அரிபிரம ரடிவருட வுததி கோத்தலற","அடல்வடவை யனலுமிழ அலகை கூட்டமிட","அணிநிணமு மலைபெருக அறையும் வாச்சியமு ...... மகலாது","அடல்கழுகு கொடிகெருட னிடைவி டாக்கணமு","மறுகுறளு மெறிகுருதி நதியின் மேற்பரவ","அருணரண முகவயிர வர்களு மார்ப்பரவ ...... மிடநாளும்","பரவுநிசி சரர்முடிகள் படியின் மேற்குவிய","பவுரிகொடு திரியவரை பலவும் வேர்ப்பறிய","பகர்வரிய ககனமுக டிடிய வேட்டைவரு ...... மயில்வீரா","படருநெறி சடையுடைய இறைவர் கேட்குரிய","பழயமறை தருமவுன வழியை யார்க்குமொரு","பரமகுரு பரனெனவு மறிவு காட்டவல ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"After a long and frank picture of lovemaking until the two seem one, the poet asks that even in the days when his mind is lost in that passion he be given ways to praise Murugan's two feet, the vel hand that casts out karma, his holy face beyond depicting, and his grace. The praise has Vishnu and Brahma pressing his feet, a battlefield of ghouls, vultures and Bhairavas, and calls Murugan the supreme guru who showed the Lord of the matted hair the silent way that the ancient Veda gives.","n":"எனது மனது அழியும் நாட்களினும் ... ஏத்தும் வகை தரவேணும்: praise is asked for within the passion, not after it. பழய மறை தரு மவுன வழி: what Murugan teaches Siva is named here as the silent way of the Veda, without Om.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","thiruvadi","guru","mounam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:248"},"p:1202":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1202,"t":["வேலொத்து வென்றி யங்கை வேளுக்கு வெஞ்ச ரங்க","ளாமிக்க கண்க ளென்று ...... மிருதோளை","வேயொக்குமென்று கொங்கை மேல்வெற்ப தென்றுகொண்டை","மேகத்தை வென்ற தென்று ...... மெழில்மாதர்","கோலத்தை விஞ்ச வெஞ்சொல் கோடித்து வஞ்ச நெஞ்சர்","கூடத்தில் நின்று நின்று ...... குறியாதே","கோதற்ற நின்ப தங்கள் நேர்பற்றி யின்ப மன்பு","கூர்கைக்கு வந்து சிந்தை ...... குறுகாதோ","ஞாலத்தை யன்ற ளந்து வேலைக்கு ளுந்து யின்று","நாடத்தி முன்பு வந்த ...... திருமாலும்","நாடத்த டஞ்சி லம்பை மாவைப்பி ளந்த டர்ந்து","நாகத்த லங்கு லுங்க ...... விடும்வேலா","ஆலித்தெ ழுந்த டர்ந்த ஆலத்தை யுண்ட கண்ட","ராகத்தில் மங்கை பங்கர் ...... நடமாடும்","ஆதிக்கு மைந்த னென்று நீதிக்குள் நின்ற அன்பர்","ஆபத்தி லஞ்ச லென்ற ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women whose eyes are Kama's arrows, arms bamboo, breasts hills and hair darker than clouds, and asks that he no longer stand again and again in the houses of the deceitful-hearted, but that his mind come near Murugan's flawless feet with joy and love. The praise has Vishnu who measured the earth, the vel that split the hill and the mango and shook the serpents' world, Siva who drank the poison and dances with his consort, and Murugan who says 'fear not' in danger to the devotees who stand in justice.","n":"வஞ்ச நெஞ்சர் கூடத்தில் நின்று நின்று: the repeated நின்று gives the poet's loitering at the women's doors.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","vanjam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:249"},"p:1203":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1203,"t":["அடியார்ம னஞ்சலிக்க எவராகி லும்ப ழிக்க","அபராதம் வந்து கெட்ட ...... பிண஧முடி","அனைவோரும் வந்து சிச்சி யெனநால்வ ருஞ்சி ரிக்க","அனலோட ழன்று செத்து ...... விடுமாபோற்","கடையேன்ம லங்கள் முற்று மிருநோயு டன்பி டித்த","கலியோடி றந்து சுத்த ...... வெளியாகிக்","களிகூர என்ற னுக்கு மயிலேறி வந்து முத்தி","கதியேற அன்பு வைத்து ...... னருள்தாராய்","சடைமீது கங்கை வைத்து விடையேறு மெந்தை சுத்த","தழல்மேனி யன்சி ரித்தொர் ...... புர்முணும்","தவிடாக வந்தெ திர்த்த மதனாக முஞ்சி தைத்த","தழல்பார்வை யன்ற ளித்த ...... குருநாதா","மிடிதீர அண்ட ருக்கு மயிலேறி வஞ்சர் கொட்டம்","வெளியாக வந்து நிர்த்த ...... மருள்வோனே","மினநூல்ம ருங்குல் பொற்பு முலைமாதி ளங்கு றத்தி","மிகுமாலொ டன்பு வைத்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet takes the corpse of one ruined by offence, whom all mock with 'chi, chi' while even the four bearers laugh and which burns to nothing in the fire, and asks that in the same way his impurities, with the two diseases and the Kali age, die, so that he becomes pure space and Murugan comes on the peacock to give release. The praise names Siva of the Ganga and the bull who laughed the three cities to husk and burned Kama with his eye, whose guru Murugan is, and his dance on the peacock for the gods.","n":"அடியார் மனம் சலிக்க ... அபராதம் வந்து கெட்ட பிணம்: the image seems to be the corpse of one who grieved the devotees, made the model for the death the poet wants for his own malas. சுத்த வெளியாகி, becoming pure space, names the goal. The first line has a corrupt glyph and the division of the opening clauses is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["malam","veedu","adiyaar"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:249"},"p:1204":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1204,"t":["அடியில்வி டாப்பிண மடையவி டாச்சிறி","தழியுமுன் வீட்டுமு ...... னுயர்பாடை","அழகொடு கூட்டூமி னழையுமின் வார்ப்பறை","யழுகையை மாற்றுமி ...... னொதியாமுன்","எடுமினி யாக்கையை யெனஇடு காட்டெரி","யிடைகொடு போய்த்தமர் ...... சுடுநாளில்","எயினர்கு லோத்தமை யுடன்மயில் மேற்கடி","தெனதுயிர் காத்திட ...... வரவேணும்","மடுவிடை போய்ப்பரு முதலையின் வாய்ப்படு","மதகரிகூப்பிட ...... வளையூதி","மழைமுகில் போற்கக பதிமிசை தோற்றிய","மகிபதி போற்றிடு ...... மருகோனே","படர்சடை யாத்திகர் பரிவுற ராட்சதர்","பரவையி லார்ப்பெழ ...... விடும்வேலாற்","படமுனி யாப்பணி தமனிய நாட்டவர்","பதிகுடி யேற்றிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The first half is the talk of mourners over a body: don't leave the corpse at the threshold, raise a tall bier before it rots, deck it, call the drummers, stop the weeping, lift it before it stinks; and the poet asks that on the day his kin carry him to the burning ground, Murugan come quickly on the peacock with the hunters' daughter to guard his life. The praise tells of the elephant seized by the crocodile in the pond and Vishnu who came on Garuda blowing the conch, the vel thrown against the demons, and the gods resettled in their golden land.","n":"As in 21 the confession is made through quoted speech: the kin's instructions over the body (எடுமின் யாக்கையை) show how quickly the person becomes a thing to be disposed of.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","nilaiyaamai","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:249"},"p:1205":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1205,"t":["அப்படி யேழு மேழும்வ குத்துவ ழாது போதினி","னக்ரம்வி யோம கோளகை ...... மிசைவாழும்","அக்ஷர தேவி கோவின்வி திப்படி மாறி மாறிய","னைத்துரு வாய காயம ...... தடைவேகொண்","டிப்படி யோனி வாய்தொறு முற்பவி யாவி ழாவுல","கிற்றடு மாறி யேதிரி...... தருகாலம்","எத்தனை யூழி காலமெ னத்தெரியாது வாழியி","னிப்பிற வாது நீயருள் ...... புரிவாயே","கற்பக வேழ மேய்வன பச்சிள ஏனல் மீதுறை","கற்புடை மாது தோய்தரு ...... மபிராம","கற்புர தூளி லேபன மற்புய பாக சாதன","கற்பக லோக தாரண ...... கிரிசால","விப்ரச மூக வேதன பச்சிம பூமி காவல","வெட்சியு நீப மாலையு ...... மணிவோனே","மெத்திய ஆழி சேறெழ வெற்பொடு சூர னீறெழ","விக்ரம வேலை யேவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says that, by the decree of Brahma, consort of the goddess of letters, he has taken every form of body again and again, born at the mouth of every womb and stumbling through the world for aeons he cannot count, and asks Murugan to grant that he not be born again. The praise names the hunter girl of the green millet field where the elephant came, Indra's world upheld, the guardian of the western land, and the vel hurled so that the sea turned to mud and Sura and the mountain to ash.","n":"அக்ஷர தேவி கோவின் விதிப்படி: birth is assigned by Brahma's writ, the lord of Sarasvati. The opening (அப்படி ஏழும் ஏழும் வகுத்து, அக்ரம் வியோம கோளகை) and the Sanskrit run of epithets in the praise are hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","oozh","karu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:249"},"p:1206":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1206,"t":["அயில்வி லோசனங் குவிய வாசகம்","பதற ஆனனங் ...... குறுவெர்வுற்","றளக பாரமுங் குலைய மேல்விழுந்","ததர பானமுண் ...... டியல்மாதர்","சயில பாரகுங் குமப யோதரந்","தழுவு மாதரந் ...... தமியேனால்","தவிரொ ணாதுநின் கருணை கூர்தருந்","தருண பாதமுந் ...... தரவேணும்","கயிலை யாளியுங் குலிச பாணியுங்","கமல யோனியும் ...... புயகேசன்","கணப ணாமுகங் கிழிய மோதுவெங்","கருட வாகனந் ...... தனிலேறும்","புயலி லேகரும் பரவ வானிலும்","புணரிமீதுனுங் ...... கிரிமீதும்","பொருநி சாசரன் தனது மார்பினும்","புதைய வேல்விடும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes lovemaking with women whose eyes close and speech falters, and admits that alone he cannot give up the embrace, so asks for Murugan's young feet rich in compassion. The praise has Siva of Kailas, Indra of the thunderbolt, Brahma and Vishnu on Garuda who tears the serpent's hoods, and the vel buried in clouds, sky, sea, mountain and the demon's chest.","n":"தமியேனால் தவிரொணாது: the poet confesses he cannot leave it by his own strength, so the feet must be given.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:249"},"p:1207":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1207,"t":["அருக்கி மெத்ததோள் திருத்தி யுற்றுமார்","பசைத்து வக்குமா ...... லிளைஞோரை","அழைத்து மிக்ககா சிழைத்து மெத்தைமீ","தணைத்து மெத்தமா ...... லதுகூர","உ ருக்கி யுட்கொள்மா தருக்கு ளெய்த்துநா","வுலற்றி யுட்குநா ...... ணுடன்மேவி","உ ழைக்கு மத்தைநீ யொழித்து முத்திபா","லுறக்கு ணத்ததா ...... ளருள்வாயே","சுருக்க முற்றமால் தனக்கு மெட்டிடா","தொருத்தர் மிக்கமா ...... நடமாடுஞ்","சுகத்தி லத்தர்தா மிகுத்த பத்திகூர்","சுரக்க வித்தைதா ...... னருள்வோனே","பெருக்க வெற்றிகூர் திருக்கை கொற்றவேல்","பிடித்து குற்றமா ...... ரொருசூரன்","பெலத்தை முட்டிமார் தொளைத்து நட்டுளோர்","பிழைக்க விட்டவோர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women who call young men, draw out a great deal of money, embrace them on beds and melt them, and says he is drained, his tongue dry, living in fear and shame; he asks Murugan to end this labour and give the feet that lead to release. The praise names Siva, whom even Vishnu could not reach, dancing the great dance, to whom Murugan gave the teaching that made his devotion overflow, and the vel that pierced Sura's chest and let the friendly survive.","n":"மிக்க காசு இழைத்து: money is named, the courtesan's trade. சுகத்தில் அத்தர் தாம் மிகுத்த பத்தி கூர் சுரக்க வித்தை தான் அருள்வோனே: Siva, the father, receives the teaching and overflows with devotion.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","veedu","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:249"},"p:1208":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1208,"t":["அரும்பி னாற்றனிக் கரும்பி னாற்றொடுத்","தடர்ந்து மேற்றெறித் ...... தமராடும்","அநங்க னார்க்கிளைத் தயர்ந்த ணாப்பியெத்","தரம்பை மார்க்கடைக் ...... கலமாகிக்","குரும்பை போற்பணைத் தரும்பு றாக்கொதித்","தெழுந்து கூற்றெனக் ...... கொலைசூழுங்","குயங்கள் வேட்டறத் தியங்கு தூர்த்தனைக்","குணங்க ளாக்கிநற் ...... கழல்சேராய்","பொருந்தி டார்ப்பரத் திலங்கை தீப்படக்","குரங்கி னாற்படைத் ...... தொருதேரிற்","புகுந்து நூற்றுவர்க் கொழிந்து பார்த்தனுக்","கிரங்கி யாற்புறத் ...... தலைமேவிப்","பெருங்கு றோட்டைவிட் டுறங்கு காற்றெனப்","பிறங்க வேத்தியக் ...... குறுமாசூர்","பிறங்க லார்ப்பெழச் சலங்கள் கூப்பிடப்","பிளந்த வேற்கரப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet, worn out by Kama who shoots the bud arrows from his sugarcane bow, calls himself a vessel at the door of women like Rambha and a wandering lecher who seeks breasts that plot death like Death himself, and asks Murugan to give him virtues and join him to the good anklets. The praise names Rama who burned Lanka with his monkey army and Krishna who in one chariot sided with Arjuna against the hundred, and the vel that split the hill of Sura.","n":"கூற்றெனக் கொலைசூழும் குயங்கள்: desire is again Death. Several runs in the praise (பெருங்குறோட்டைவிட்டு உறங்கு காற்றென) are obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kutram","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:249"},"p:1209":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1209,"t":["அலமல மிப்புலாற் புலையுடல் கட்டனேற்","கறுமுக நித்தர்போற் ...... றியநாதா","அறிவிலி யிட்டுணாப் பொறியிலி சித்தமாய்த்","தணிதரு முத்திவீட் ...... டணுகாதே","பலபல புத்தியாய்க் கலவியி லெய்த்திடாப்","பரிவொ தத்தைமார்க் ...... கிதமாடும்","பகடிது டுக்கன்வாய்க் கறையனெ னத்தராப்","படியில்ம னித்தர்தூற் ...... றிடலாமோ","குலகிரிபொற்றலாய்க் குரைகடல் வற்றலாய்க்","கொடியஅ ரக்கரார்ப் ...... பெழவேதக்","குயவனை நெற்றியேற் றவனெதிர் குட்டினாற்","குடுமியை நெட்டைபோக் ...... கியவீரா","கலைதலை கெட்டபாய்ச் சமணரை நட்டகூர்க்","கழுநிரை முட்டஏற் ...... றியதாளக்","கவிதையும் வெற்றிவேற் கரமுடன் வற்றிடாக்","கருணையு மொப்பிலாப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"Calling his body a bundle of flesh and filth, the poet asks whether, failing to reach release and wearing himself out in union with women, he should be slandered by men on earth as a buffoon, a rogue and a loudmouth. The praise names the Lord who knocked Brahma, the potter of the Veda, on the forehead, and the Tamil verses with the tala beat that sent the Jains in their mats onto rows of sharp stakes, with the victorious vel and unfailing compassion.","n":"கலைதலை கெட்ட பாய்ச் சமணரை நட்ட கூர்க் கழுநிரை ... ஏற்றிய தாளக் கவிதை: Murugan is identified with Sambandar, whose Tamil hymns sent the Jains to the stake. குயவனை ... குட்டினால்: Brahma struck as a potter, the punishment in the story of Om, though Om is not named here.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","pazhi","thamizh","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:249"},"p:1210":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1210,"t":["அளக பார முங்கு லைந்து அரிய பார்வை யுஞ்சி வந்து","அணுகி யாக மும்மு யங்கி ...... யமுதூறல்","அதர பான மும்நு கர்ந்து அறிவு சோர வும்மொ ழிந்து","அவச மாக வும்பு ணர்ந்து ...... மடவாரைப்","பளக னாவி யுந்த ளர்ந்து பதறு மாக மும்ப யந்து","பகலி ராவை யும்ம றந்து ...... திரியாமற்","பரம ஞான முந்தெ ளிந்து பரிவு நேச முங்கி ளர்ந்து","பகரு மாறு செம்ப தங்கள் ...... தரவேணும்","துளப மாய னுஞ்சி றந்த கமல வேத னும்பு கழ்ந்து","தொழுது தேட ரும்ப்ர சண்ட ...... னருள்பாலா","சுரர்கள் நாய கன்ப யந்த திருவை மாம ணம்பு ணர்ந்து","சுடரு மோக னம்மி குந்த ...... மயில்பாகா","களப மார்பு டன்த யங்கு குறவர் மாது டன்செ றிந்து","கலவி நாட கம்பொ ருந்தி ...... மகிழ்வோனே","கடிய பாத கந்த விர்ந்து கழலை நாடொ றுங்கி ளர்ந்து","கருது வார்ம னம்பு குந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes the embrace of women plainly, until reason fails and day and night are forgotten, and asks to be given Murugan's red feet so that, with supreme knowledge made clear and love rising, he may speak his praise. The praise names Siva whom Vishnu and Brahma could not find, Devayanai the daughter of the gods' king, Valli in love's play, and Murugan who enters the minds of those who daily think on his anklets and leave their sins.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","gnaanam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:249"},"p:1211":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1211,"t":["ஆசார வீன னறிவிலி கோபாப ராதி யவகுண","னாகாத நீச னநுசிதன் ...... விபாணதன்","ஆசாவி சார வெகுவித மோகாச ஡ணத பரவச","னாகாச நீர்ம ணனல்வளி ...... யுருமாறி","மாசான நாலெண் வகைதனை நீநானெ னாத அறிவுளம்","வாயாத பாவி யிவனென ...... நினையாமல்","மாதாபி தாவி னருணல மாறாம காரிலெனையினி","மாஞான போத மருள்செய ...... நினைவாயே","வீசால வேலை சுவறிட மாசூரர் மார்பு தொளைபட","வேதாள ராசி பசிகெட ...... அறைகூறி","மேகார வார மெனஅதிர் போர்யாது தான ரெமபுர","மீதேற வேல்கொ டமர்செயு ...... மிளையோனே","கூசாது வேட னுமிழ்தரு நீராடி யூனு ணெனுமுரை","கூறாம னீய அவனுகர் ...... தருசேடங்","கோதாமெ னாம லமுதுசெய் வேதாக மாதி முதல்தரு","கோலோக நாத குறமகள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet heaps names on himself, without conduct, witless, angry, an offender, base, unfit, carried away by desire, a sinner whose mind never gained the knowledge that says neither 'you' nor 'I', and asks Murugan not to think so of him but, with a mother's and father's grace, to grant great knowledge. The praise has the vel that dried the sea, fed the ghouls and sent the demons to Yama's city, and the Lord who, not shrinking when the hunter bathed him with water from his mouth and said 'eat this flesh', ate what he had tasted as nectar.","n":"நீ நான் எனாத அறிவு: the knowledge the poet lacks is non-dual. The hunter's mouth-water bath and tasted flesh are Kannappar's offering, usually told of Siva at Kalahasti; here the praise seems to give it to the Lord the song addresses. The first lines have a corrupt glyph and obscure words (விபாணதன், மாறாம காரிலெனையினி).","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["kutram","gnaanam","thiruvarul","paththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:249"},"p:1212":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1212,"t":["ஆசைகூர் பத்த னேன்மனோ பத்ம","மானபூ வைத்து ...... நடுவேயன்","பானநூ லிட்டு நாவிலே சித்ர","மாகவே கட்டி ...... யொருஞான","வாசம்வீ சிப்ர காசியா நிற்ப","மாசிலோர் புத்தி ...... யளிபாட","மாத்ருகா புஷ்ப மாலைகோ லப்ர","வாளபா தத்தி ...... லணிவேனோ","மூசுகா னத்து மீதுவாழ் முத்த","மூரல்வே டிச்சி ...... தனபார","மூழ்குநீ பப்ர தாபமார் பத்த","மூரிவே ழத்தன் ...... மயில்வாழ்வே","வீசுமீ னப்ப யோதிவாய் விட்டு","வேகவே தித்து ...... வருமாசூர்","வீழமோ திப்ப ராரைநா கத்து","வீரவேல் தொட்ட ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet, a devotee full of longing, asks whether he will set his heart's lotus as the flower, thread love through it, tie it on his tongue with art so that it spreads the fragrance of knowledge, with a pure mind as the singing bee, and so adorn Murugan's radiant feet with a garland of the flowers of the letters. The praise names the hunter girl of the forest, Devayanai of the great elephant, and the vel that brought down Sura, who came out of the fish-filled sea.","n":"மாத்ருகா புஷ்ப மாலை: the garland is made of the மாத்ருகா, the letters of the alphabet held to be mother-powers, so the song itself is the offering; heart, love, tongue and mind each become part of the garland.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","paththi","vazhipaadu","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:249"},"p:1213":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1213,"t":["ஆசைக் கொளுத்திவெகு வாகப் பசப்பிவரு","மாடைப் பணத்தையெடெ ...... னுறவாடி","ஆரக் கழுத்துமுலை மார்பைக் குலுக்கிவிழி","யாடக் குலத்துமயில் ...... கிளிபோலப்","பேசித் சிரித்துமயிர் கோதிக் குலைத்துமுடி","பேதைப் படுத்திமய ...... லிடுமாதர்","பீறற் சலத்துவழி நாறப் படுத்தியெனை","பீடைப் படுத்துமய ...... லொழியாதோ","தேசத் தடைத்துபிர காசித் தொலித்துவரி","சேடற் பிடுத்துதறு ...... மயில்வீரா","தேடித் துதித்தஅடி யார்சித்த முற்றருளு","சீர்பொற் பதத்தஅரி...... மருகோனே","நேசப் படுத்தியிமை யோரைக் கெடுத்தமுழு","நீசற் கனத்தமுற ...... விடும்வேலா","நேசக் குறத்திமய லோடுற் பவித்தபொனி","நீர்பொற் புவிக்குள்மகிழ் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women who kindle desire, ask for cloth and money, sway their breasts and mimic peacocks and parrots, and lead a man by their foul passage, and asks whether the infatuation that torments him will not end. The praise names the peacock that seizes and shakes the serpent, Hari's nephew whose feet fill devotees' minds, the vel thrown at Sura who harmed the gods, and the lord who rejoices with the hunter girl in the golden land of the Ponni river.","n":"ஆடைப் பணத்தை எடு: the women's demand is quoted. பொனி நீர் பொற்புவி: the Ponni, the Kaveri, is named, a pointer to a Chola-country shrine though the song is filed among the general songs.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","mayal","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:249"},"p:1214":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1214,"t":["ஆசை நேச மயக்கிகள் காசு தேடு மனத்திகள்","ஆவி சோர வுருக்கிகள் ...... தெருமீதே","யாவ ரோடு நகைப்பவர் வேறு கூறு விளைப்பவர்","ஆல கால விழிச்சிகள் ...... மலைபோலு","மாசி லாத தனத்தியர் ஆடை சோர நடப்பவர்","வாரியோதி முடிப்பவர் ...... ஒழியாமல்","வாயி லு஡ற லளிப்பவர் நாளு நாளு மினுக்கிகள்","வாசல் தேடி நடப்பது ...... தவிர்வேனோ","ஓசை யான திரைக்கடல் ஏழு ஞால முமுற்றருள்","ஈச ரோடுற வுற்றவள் ...... உ மையாயி","யோகி ஞானி பரப்ரமி நீலி நாரணி யுத்தமி","ஓல மான மறைச்சிசொல் ...... அபிராமி","ஏசி லாத மலைக்கொடி தாய்ம னோமணி சற்குணி","ஈறி லாதம லைக்கொடி ...... அருள்பாலா","ஏறு மேனி யொருத்தனும் வேத னான சமர்த்தனும்","ஈச னோடு ப்ரியப்படு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women who bewitch with desire, whose minds seek money, who melt men's life, laugh with anyone in the street and make quarrels, with poison eyes, and asks whether he will stop walking to their doors. The praise is a litany of the Goddess, Uma, mother of the seas and seven worlds: yogi, jnani, supreme Brahmi, Neeli, Narani, the excellent one, Abhirami of the Veda, mountain creeper, Manonmani; Murugan is her son, dear to Siva and Brahma.","n":"காசு தேடு மனத்திகள்: money is named. The praise gives almost all its space to the Goddess's names, so Murugan is praised as அருள்பாலா, child of her grace.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["varaivinmagalir","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:249"},"p:1215":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1215,"t":["ஆல மேற்ற விழியினர் சால நீட்டி யழுதழு","தாக மாய்க்க முறைமுறை ...... பறைமோதி","ஆடல் பார்க்க நிலையெழு பாடை கூட்டி விரையம","யான மேற்றி யுறவின ...... ரயலாகக்","கால மாச்சு வருகென ஓலை காட்டி யமபடர்","காவ லாக்கி யுயிரது ...... கொடுபோமுன்","காம வாழ்க்கை பொடிபட ஞானம் வாய்த்த கழலிணை","காத லாற்க ருதுமுணர் ...... தருவாயே","வேல கீர்த்தி விதரண சீலர் வாழ்த்து சரவண","வியாழ கோத்ர மருவிய ...... முருகோனே","வேடர் நாட்டில் விளைபுன ஏனல் காத்த சிறுமியை","வேட மாற்றி வழிபடு ...... மிளையோனே","ஞால மேத்தி வழிபடு மாறு பேர்க்கு மகவென","நாணல் பூத்த படுகையில் ...... வருவோனே","நாத போற்றி யெனமுது தாதை கேட்க அநுபவ","ஞான வார்த்தை யருளிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"When eyes of poison weep, drums beat, the bier is raised, kin stand apart and Yama's servants show the palm leaf saying 'the time has come, come' and carry off the life, the poet asks first to be given the awareness to think with love on the anklets that crush the life of lust and give knowledge. The praise names Saravana, Valli's devotee in disguise, the child who came in the reed-bed as son to the six, and the one who spoke the word of lived knowledge when his old father asked, saying 'Lord, praise'.","n":"நாத போற்றி என முது தாதை கேட்க அநுபவ ஞான வார்த்தை அருளிய: Siva as the old father asks and Murugan teaches, Om not named. காலமாச்சு வருக என ஓலை காட்டி: the summons is quoted. வியாழ கோத்ர is obscure.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","thiruvadi","guru","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:249"},"p:1216":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1216,"t":["ஆலுமயில் போலுற்ற தோகையர்க ளெமெத்த","ஆரவட மேலிட்ட ...... முலைமீதே","ஆனதுகி லேயிட்டு வீதிதனி லேநிற்க","ஆமவரை யேசற்று ...... முரையாதே","வேலுமழ கார்கொற்ற நீலமயில் மேலுற்று","வீறுமுன தார்பத்ம ...... முகமாறு","மேவியிரு பாகத்தும் வாழுமனை மார்தக்க","மேதகவு நானித்த ...... முரையேனோ","நாலுமுக வேதற்கு மாலிலையில் மாலுக்கு","நாடவரியார்பெற்ற ...... வொருபாலா","நாணமுடை யாள்வெற்றி வேடர்குல மீதொக்க","நாடுகுயில் பார்மிக்க ...... எழில்மாது","வேலைவிழி வேடச்சி யார்கணவ னேமத்த","வேழமுக வோனுக்கு ...... மிளையோனே","வீரமுட னேயுற்ற சூரனணி மார்பத்து","வேலைமிக வேவிட்ட ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women standing in the street with garlands and thin cloth on their breasts, and asks that, not speaking even a little with them, he speak daily of Murugan's vel, his blue peacock, his six lotus faces and the two consorts on either side. The praise names him son of the Lord whom Brahma and Vishnu could not find, husband of the hunter girl, younger brother of the elephant-faced one, and the vel thrown at Sura's chest.","n":"உரையாதே ... உரையேனோ: the poet turns his speech from the women to the Lord; the plea is about what the tongue does.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:249"},"p:1217":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1217,"t":["இடையித்தனை யுளதத்தைய ரிதழ்துய்த்தவ ...... ரநுபோகம்","இளகிக்கரை புரளப்புள கிதகற்புர ...... தனபாரம்","உ டன்மற்கடை படுதுற்குண மறநிற்குண ...... வுணர்வாலே","ஒருநிஷ்கள வடிவிற்புக வொருசற்றருள் ...... புரிவாயே","திடமற்றொளிர் நளினப்ரம சிறைபுக்கன ...... னெனவேகுந்","தெதிபட்சண க்ருதபட்சண செகபட்சண ...... னெனவோதும்","விடபட்சணர் திருமைத்துனன் வெருவச்சுரர் ...... பகைமேல்வேல்","விடுவிக்ரம கிரியெட்டையும் விழவெட்டிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses his enjoyment of slender-waisted women, their lips and camphor-smeared breasts, and asks for even a little grace so that, his base qualities gone through awareness of the attributeless, he may enter the one partless form. The praise tells of Brahma thrown into prison, names Vishnu as eater of curd, ghee and the world and brother-in-law of Siva who ate the poison, and calls Murugan the victor whose vel struck the gods' foes and felled the eight mountains.","n":"நிற்குண உணர்வாலே ஒரு நிஷ்கள வடிவில் புக: the goal is put in Sanskrit doctrinal terms, entry into the nirguna, nishkala form, against the துற்குணம் of the first lines. Brahma's imprisonment is named without its cause. The run of lines 5 to 7 (who fears whom) is hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","mukkunam","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:250"},"p:1218":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1218,"t":["இருகுழை மீது தாவடி யிடுவன வோதி நீழலி","னிடமது லாவி மீள்வன ...... நுதல்தாவி","இழிவன காம வேதமு மொழிவன தாரை வேலென","எறிவன காள கூடமு ...... மமுதாகக்","கருகிய நீல லோசன அபிநய மாத ரார்தரு","கலவியில் மூழ்கி வாடிய ...... தமியேனுங்","கதிபெற ஈட றாதன பதிபசு பாச மானவை","கசடற வேறு வேறுசெய் ...... தருள்வாயே","ஒருபது பார மோலியு மிருபது வாகு மேருவு","முததியில் வீழ வானர ...... முடனேசென்","றொருகணை யேவு ராகவன் மருகவி பூதி பூஷணர்","உ ணருப தேச தேசிக ...... வரையேனற்","பரவிய கான வேடுவர் தருமபி ராம நாயகி","பரிபுர பாத சேகர ...... சுரராஜன்","பதிகுடி யேற வேல்தொடு முருகம யூர வாகன","பரவச ஞான யோகிகள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women's dark eyes that leap to the earrings, roam in the hair's shade, speak the Veda of love and strike like spears and poison, and asks that he, who withered in their embrace, be set on the way by having Pati, pasu and pasam separated each from each without stain. The praise names Rama who went with the monkeys and felled the ten crowns with one arrow, calls Murugan the teacher whose instruction ash-adorned Siva understood, Valli's lord, the one who resettled Indra, and lord of yogis rapt in jnana.","n":"பதி பசு பாசம் ஆனவை கசடற வேறு வேறு செய்தருள்வாயே: the plea is in the terms of Saiva Siddhanta, asking for the discrimination of Lord, soul and bond. The praise has விபூதி பூஷணர் உணர் உபதேச தேசிக, Siva learning from Murugan, without naming Om.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","pathi","paasam","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:250"},"p:1219":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1219,"t":["இருநோய்ம லத்தைசிவ வொளியால்மி ரட்டியெனை","யினிதாவ ழைத்தெனது ...... முடிமேலே","இணைதாள ளித்துனது மயில்மேலி ருத்தியொளி","ரியல்வேல ளித்துமகி ...... ழிருவோரும்","ஒருவாகெ னக்கயிலை யிறையோன ளித்தருளு","மொளிர்வேத கற்பகந ...... லிளையோனே","ஒளிர்மாம றைத்தொகுதி சுரர்பார்து தித்தருள","உ பதேசி கப்பதமு ...... மருள்வாயே","கருநோய றுத்தெனது மிடிதூள்ப டுத்திவிடு","கரிமாமு கக்கடவு ...... ளடியார்கள்","கருதாவ கைக்குவர மருள்ஞான தொப்பைமகிழ்","கருணாக டப்பமல ...... ரணிவோனே","திருமால ளித்தருளு மொருஞான பத்தினியை","திகழ்மார்பு றத்தழுவு ...... மயில்வேலா","சிலைதூளே ழுப்பிகவ டவுணோரை வெட்டிசுரர்","சிறைமீள விட்டபுகழ் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks Murugan to drive off his twofold disease of impurity with Siva's light, call him kindly, set the paired feet on his head, seat him on the peacock, give him the vel, and grant him the place of a teacher whom the Vedas, gods and earth would praise. The praise calls Murugan younger brother of the elephant-faced god who cuts the disease of birth and poverty, wearer of kadamba, embracer of the wise wife Vishnu gave, and the one who powdered the hill, cut down the asuras and freed the gods.","n":"The plea is unusually bold: not only the feet on the head (முடிமேலே இணைதாள்) but the peacock, the vel and உபதேசிகப் பதம், the rank of a teacher. இருவோரும் ஒருவாக, 'the two as one', given by the lord of Kailasa, is obscure; it may mean Siva and Uma joined. Ganapati is praised at length in the second half.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["malam","thiruvadi","guru","oli"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:250"},"p:1220":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1220,"t":["இனமறைவி தங்கள்கொஞ் சியசிறுச தங்கைகிண்","கிணியிலகு தண்டையம் ...... புண்டாணகம்","எனதுமன பங்கயங் குவளைகுர வம்புனைந்","திரவுபகல் சந்ததஞ் ...... சிந்தியாதோ","உ னதருளை யன்றியிங் கொருதுணையு மின்றிநின்","றுளையுமொரு வஞ்சகன் ...... பஞ்சபூத","உ டலதுசு மந்தலைந் துலகுதொறும் வந்துவந்","துழலுமது துன்புகண் ...... டன்புறாதோ","கனநிவத தந்தசங் க்ரமகவள துங்கவெங்","கடவிகட குஞ்சரந் ...... தங்கும்யானை","கடகசயி லம்பெறும் படியவுணர் துஞ்சமுன்","கனககிரிசம்பெழுந் ...... தம்புராசி","அனலெழமு னிந்தசங் க்ரமமதலை கந்தனென்","றரனுமுமை யும்புகழ்ந் ...... தன்புகூர","அகிலபுவ னங்களுஞ் சுரரொடுதி ரண்டுநின்","றரிபிரமர் கும்பிடுந் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether his mind will not think day and night on the feet with little anklet bells that prattle the Vedas, adorned with the lotus of his heart and with flowers, and whether Murugan will not take pity on a deceiver with no help but his grace, who carries a body of five elements from world to world. The praise is of battle, an elephant and the golden hill, the sea set on fire, and Siva and Uma praising the child Kandan while all worlds, gods, Vishnu and Brahma bow.","n":"பஞ்சபூத உடலது சுமந்து அலைந்து உலகுதொறும் வந்துவந்து: rebirth is told as carrying the five-element body from world to world. The poet calls himself வஞ்சகன், a deceiver. The battle lines (கனநிவத... கடகசயிலம்) are hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvadi","piravi","yaakkai","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:250"},"p:1221":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1221,"t":["ஊனே றெலும்பு சீசீ மலங்க","ளோடே நரம்பு ...... கசுமாலம்","ஊழ்நோ யடைந்து மாசான மண்டு","மூனோ டுழன்ற ...... கடைநாயேன்","நானா ரொடுங்க நானார் வணங்க","நானார் மகிழ்ந்து ...... உ னையோத","நானா ரிரங்க நானா ருணங்க","நானார் நடந்து ...... விழநானார்","தானே புணர்ந்து தானே யறிந்து","தானே மகிழ்ந்து ...... அ ளுறித்","தாய்போல் பரிந்த தேனோ டுகந்து","தானே தழைந்து ...... சிவமாகித்","தானே வளர்ந்து தானே யிருந்த","தார்வேணி யெந்தை ...... யருள்பாலா","சாலோக தொண்டர் சாமீப தொண்டர்","சர்ருப தொண்டர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet calls his body flesh, bone, filth and nerves, eaten by fated disease, and himself the lowest dog, and asks who he is to subside, to bow, to sing Murugan in joy, to melt, to wither, to walk and fall. The praise answers with Siva, who himself united, knew, rejoiced, pitied like a mother, became Siva, grew and remained by himself, and calls Murugan his son and lord of the devotees of salokam, samipam and sarupam.","n":"The repeated நானார் ('who am I') is set against the repeated தானே ('he himself'): the poet disowns every act of devotion as his own and gives all agency to the Lord who does everything by himself. The three devotee classes in the refrain are the graded releases short of union; sayujyam is not named. The word after மகிழ்ந்து in line 10 is garbled.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","panivu","sivam","padhamukthi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:250"},"p:1222":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1222,"t":["எதிரொருவ ரிலையுலகி லெனஅலகு சிலுகுவிரு","திட்டுக்ரியைக்கேயெ ...... ழுந்துபாரின்","இடையுழல்வ சுழலுவன சமயவித சகலகலை","யெட்டெட்டு மெட்டாத ...... மந்த்ரவாளால்","விதிவழியி னுயிர்கவர வருகொடிய யமபடரை","வெட்டித் துணித்தாண்மை ...... கொண்டுநீபம்","விளவினிள இலைதளவு குவளைகமழ் பவளநிற","வெட்சித் திருத்தாள்வ ...... ணங்குவேனோ","திதிபுல்வ ரொடுபொருது குருதிநதி முழுகியொளிர்","செக்கச் செவத்தேறு ...... செங்கைவேலா","சிகரகிரிதகரவிடு முருவமர கதகலப","சித்ரக் ககத்தேறு ...... மெம்பிரானே","முதியபதி னொருவிடையர் முடுகுவன பரிககன","முட்டச் செலுத்தாறி ...... ரண்டுதேரர்","மொழியுமிரு அசுவினிக ளிருசதுவி தவசுவெனு","முப்பத்து முத்தேவர் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether he will worship the red feet adorned with kadamba, jasmine, kuvalai and vetchi, having won manhood by cutting down with the sword of mantra, which the sects, rites and all sixty-four arts cannot reach, the cruel servants of Yama who come by fate's path to take his life, and which those who wander raising banners that say none is their equal never find. The praise calls Murugan the red-handed vel that fought Diti's sons and bathed in a river of blood, rider of the bright peacock, and lord of the thirty-three gods, which it counts out.","n":"மந்த்ர வாளால் ... யமபடரை வெட்டித் துணித்து: the mantra is a sword against Death's servants, and it lies beyond the boasting sects and arts. The refrain lists the thirty-three: eleven on bulls (the Rudras), twelve in chariots (the Adityas), two Ashvins and eight Vasus. The opening lines' split is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kootru","mandhiram","samayam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:250"},"p:1223":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1223,"t":["எழுந்திடுங் கப்புச் செழுங்குரும் பைக்கொத்","திரண்டுகண் பட்டிட் ...... டிளையோர்நெஞ்","சிசைந்திசைந் தெட்டிக் கசிந்தசைந் திட்டிட்","டிணங்குபொன் செப்புத் ...... தனமாதர்","அழுங்கலங் கத்துக் குழைந்துமன் பற்றுற்","றணைந்துபின் பற்றற் ...... றகல்மாயத்","தழுங்குநெஞ் சுற்றுப் புழுங்குபுண் பட்டிட்","டலைந்தலைந் தெய்த்திட் ...... டுழல்வேனோ","பழம்பெருந் தித்திப் புறுங்கரும் பப்பத்","துடன்பெருங் கைக்குட் ...... படவாரிப்","பரந்தெழுந் தொப்பைக் கருந்திமுன் பத்தர்க்","கிதஞ்செய்தொன் றத்திக் ...... கிளையோனே","தழைந்தெழுந் தொத்துத் தடங்கைகொண் டப்பிச்","சலம்பிளந் தெற்றிப் ...... பொருசூரத்","தடம்பெருங் கொக்கைத் தொடர்ந்திடம் புக்குத்","தடிந்திடுஞ் சொக்கப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether he must wander, worn out with a heart sore and burning, after women whose young-coconut breasts draw young men's eyes, who cling with love and then leave without attachment by deceit. The praise calls Murugan younger brother of the pot-bellied elephant god who gathers fruit, sugarcane and cakes in his trunk and is kind to devotees, and the beautiful one whose vel split the sea and pursued and cut down the great mango.","n":"அணைந்து பின் பற்றற்று அகல் மாயம்: the deceit is the women's leaving once attached, not a price named. கொக்கு is the mango, the form Sura took, a detail the song names by its word only.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","vanjam","mayal"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:250"},"p:1224":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1224,"t":["ஏட்டி லேவரை பாட்டி லேசில","நீட்டி லேயினி ...... தென்றுதேடி","ஈட்டு மாபொருள் பாத்து ணாதிக","லேற்ற மானகு ...... லங்கள்பேசிக்","காட்டி லேயியல் நாட்டி லேபயில்","வீட்டி லேஉ ல ...... கங்களேசக்","காக்கை நாய்நரிபேய்க்கு ழாமுண","யாக்கை மாய்வதொ ...... ழிந்திடாதோ","கோட்டு மாயிர நாட்ட னாடுறை","கோட்டு வாலிப ...... மங்கைகோவே","கோத்த வேலையி லார்த்த சூர்பொரு","வேற்சி காவள ...... கொங்கில்வேளே","பூட்டு வார்சிலை கோட்டு வேடுவர்","பூட்கை சேர்குற ...... மங்கைபாகா","பூத்த மாமலர் சாத்தி யேகழல்","போற்று தேவர்கள் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether his dying will not end: a body for crows, dogs, jackals and ghosts, whether in forest, country or house, after seeking what is sweet in writings and songs, piling great wealth and eating it without sharing, and boasting of high lineage. The praise names Murugan lord of the daughter of thousand-eyed Indra, the peacock-rider whose vel fought Sura, the Vel of Kongu, partner of the hunters' girl, and lord of the gods who offer flowers at his feet.","n":"பாத்து உணாது: eating without dividing it with others, the Kural's own phrase for sharing, here named as the poet's fault. கொங்கில் வேளே places the song in Kongu without naming a shrine. The opening search in ஏட்டிலே ... பாட்டிலே ... நீட்டிலே may be of a poet's trade; its sense is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","eegai","selvam","kudi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:250"},"p:1225":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1225,"t":["கச்சுப் பூட்டு கைச்சக் கோட்ட","கத்திற் கோட்டு ...... கிரியாலங்","கக்கித் தேக்கு செக்கர்ப் போர்க்க","யற்கட் கூற்றில் ...... மயலாகி","அச்சக் கூச்ச மற்றுக் கேட்ட","வர்க்குத் தூர்த்த ...... னெனநாளும்","அத்தப் பேற்றி லிச்சிப் பார்க்க","றப்பித் தாய்த்தி ...... ரியலாமோ","பச்சைக் கூத்தர் மெச்சிச் சேத்த","பத்மக் கூட்டி ...... லுறைவோரி","பத்திற் சேர்ப்பல் சக்கிற் கூட்டர்","பத்தக் கூட்ட ...... ரியல்வானம்","மெச்சிப் போற்ற வெற்புத் தோற்று","வெட்கத் கோத்த ...... கடல்மீதே","மெத்தக் காய்த்த கொக்குக் கோட்டை","வெட்டிச் சாய்த்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether, infatuated by bodiced breasts and red-veined carp eyes that spit poison like Death, he should go about without fear or shame, called a rake by all who hear, mad after wealth. The praise is a dense string of epithets naming the green dancer, the one who dwells in the lotus, the bearer of the discus and conch and the gods of the sky, and calls Murugan the one who cut down the great mango in the sea.","n":"அச்சக் கூச்சமற்று ... தூர்த்தன் என: the confession is of public shame, being named a rake (தூர்த்தன்). The praise's clipped syllables are very hard to divide and only its last line is clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kan","mayal","naanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:250"},"p:1226":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1226,"t":["கடலினும் பெரியவிழி மலையினும் பெரியமுலை","கவரினுந் துவரதர ...... மிருதோள்பைங்","கழையினுங் குழையுமென மொழிபழங் கிளவிபல","களவுகொண் டொருவர்மிசை ...... கவிபாடி","அடலசஞ் சலனதுல னநுபமன் குணதரன்மெய்","அருள்பரங் குரனபய ...... னெனஆசித்","தலமரும் பிறவியினி யலமலம் பிறவியற","அருணபங் கயசரண ...... மருள்வாயே","வடநெடுங் குலரசத கிரியினின் றிருகலுழி","மகிதலம் புகவழியு ...... மதுபோல","மதசலஞ் சலசலென முதுசலஞ் சலதிநதி","வழிவிடும் படிபெருகு ...... முதுபாகை","உ டையசங் க்ரமகவள தவளசிந் துரதிலக","னுலகுமிந் திரனுநிலை ...... பெறவேல்கொண்","டுததிவெந் தபயமிட மலையொடுங் கொலையவுண","ருடனுடன் றமர்பொருத ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses stealing the old poets' phrases (eyes bigger than the sea, breasts bigger than mountains, lips redder than coral, shoulders softer than bamboo) and singing them on a man, calling him strong, peerless, gracious and fearless in hope of gain, and says 'enough, enough' of this restless birth, asking for the red lotus feet. The praise likens the must of the white elephant to two rivers falling from the silver mountain, and calls Murugan the one who took the vel so Indra and the worlds might stand, set the sea crying for refuge and fought the asuras with the hill.","n":"பழங் கிளவி பல களவுகொண்டு: the poet calls his borrowed stock comparisons theft, and the praise of a patron (அடல் அசஞ்சலன் அதுலன் அநுபமன்) begging. அலமலம், 'enough, enough', turns from the patron to the feet.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","iravu","piravi","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:250"},"p:1227":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1227,"t":["கட்டக் கணப்பறைகள் கொட்டக் குலத்திளைஞர்","கட்டிப் புறத்தி ...... லணைமீதே","கச்சுக் கிழித்ததுணி சுற்றிக் கிடத்தியெரி","கத்திக் கொளுத்தி ...... யனைவோரும்","சுட்டுக் குளித்துமனை புக்கிட் டிருப்பரிது","சுத்தப் பொயொப்ப ...... துயிர்வாழ்வு","துக்கப் பிறப்பகல மிக்கச் சிவத்ததொரு","சொர்க்கப் பதத்தை ...... யருள்வாயே","எட்டுக் குலச்சயில முட்டத் தொளைத்தமரர்","எய்ப்புத் தணித்த ...... கதிர்வேலா","எத்திக் குறத்தியிரு முத்தத் தனக்கிரியை","யெற்பொற் புயத்தி ...... லணைவோனே","வட்டக் கடப்பமலர் மட்டுற்ற செச்சைமலர்","வைத்துப் பணைத்த ...... மணிமார்பா","வட்டத் திரைக்கடலில் மட்டித் தெதிர்த்தவரை","வெட்டித் துணித்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes the funeral: drums beat, young men of the clan bind the body, wrap it in a torn cloth on the bier, burn it, bathe and go home to sit, and says life is pure falsehood; he asks for the high state of Siva so that sorrowful birth may go. The praise calls Murugan the vel that bored through the eight mountains and eased the gods, the one who beguiled the hill girl and embraced her, wearer of kadamba and vetchi, and the one who cut down his foe in the sea.","n":"சுத்தப் பொய் ஒப்பது உயிர்வாழ்வு: the funeral is told without comment until this judgement. மிக்கச் சிவத்ததொரு சொர்க்கப் பதம் may mean 'a heavenly state that is wholly Siva' or 'very red', a pun on சிவம்.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","yaakkai","sutram","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:250"},"p:1228":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1228,"t":["கண்டு போல்மொழி வண்டு சேர்குழல்","கண்கள் சேல்மதி ...... முகம்வேய்தோள்","கண்டு பாவனை கொண்டு தோள்களி","லொண்டு காதலி ...... லிருகோடு","மண்டி மார்பினில் விண்ட தாமென","வந்த கூர்முலை ...... மடவார்தம்","வஞ்ச மாலதில் நெஞ்சு போய்மடி","கின்ற மாயம ...... தொழியாதோ","கொண்ட லார்குழல் கெண்டை போல்விழி","கொண்டு கோகில ...... மொழிகூறுங்","கொங்கை யாள்குற மங்கை வாழ்தரு","குன்றில் மால்கொடு ...... செலும்வேலா","வெண்டி மாமன மண்டு சூர்கடல்","வெம்ப மேதினி ...... தனில்மீளா","வென்று யாயையு மன்றி வேளையும்","வென்று மேவிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether the illusion in which his heart goes and dies in the deceitful love of women, sweet-spoken, bee-haired, carp-eyed and moon-faced, will not end. The praise calls Murugan the one who went with love to the hill where the cloud-haired, cuckoo-voiced hill girl lives, who made Sura's sea boil, and who conquered both maya and Kama.","n":"வென்று மாயையும் அன்றி வேளையும் வென்று: in the refrain Murugan conquers maya and Vel (Kama), so the praise answers the plea's மாயம். The source has யாயை, read as மாயை; the reading is tentative.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","vanjam","maayai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:250"},"p:1229":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1229,"t":["கப்பரை கைக்கொள வைப்பவர் மைப்பயில்","கட்பயி லிட்டிள ...... வளவோரைக்","கைக்குள்வ சப்பட பற்கறை யிட்டுமு","கத்தைமி னுக்கிவ ...... ருமுபாயப்","பப்பர மட்டைகள் பொட்டிடு நெற்றியர்","பற்றென வுற்றவொர் ...... தமியேனைப்","பத்மப தத்தினில் வைத்தருள் துய்த்திரை","பட்டதெ னக்கினி ...... யமையாதோ","குப்பர வப்படு பட்சமி குத்துள","முத்தரை யர்க்கொரு ...... மகவாகிக்","குத்திர மற்றுரை பற்றுணர் வற்றவொர்","குற்றம றுத்திடு ...... முதல்வோனே","விப்ரமு னிக்குழை பெற்றகொ டிச்சிவி","சித்ரத னக்கிரி...... மிசைதோயும்","விக்ரம மற்புய வெற்பினை யிட்டெழு","வெற்பைநெ ருக்கிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women who leave men with a begging bowl, catch young men with kohl-eyes, stained teeth and painted faces, and asks whether it is not enough, now that he who clung to them has suffered, to be set at the lotus feet. The praise calls Murugan one who was born as a child to a line it names and removed a fault, the one who meets the breasts of the creeper girl born of the deer to the Brahmin sage, and who crushed the seven hills with his arms.","n":"கப்பரை கைக்கொள வைப்பவர்: the women reduce men to the beggar's bowl, so the loss of wealth is named in the first words. The lines on முத்தரையர்க்கு ஒரு மகவாகி point to a birth story I cannot identify; they may allude to Murugan as a saint, but the text does not say.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:250"},"p:1230":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1230,"t":["கலைகோட்டு வல்லி விலைகாட்டு வில்ல","ரிவைமார்க்கு மெய்யி ...... லவநூலின்","கலைகாட்டு பொய்ய மலைமாக்கள் சொல்ல","கடுகாட்டி வெய்ய ...... அதிபாரக்","கொலைகோட்டு கள்ளி டறிவோர்க்கு முள்ள","முகையாக்கை நையு ...... முயிர்வாழக்","கொடிகோட்டு மல்லி குரவார்க்கொள் தொல்லை","மறைவாழ்த்து செய்ய ...... கழல்தாராய்","சிலைகோட்டு மள்ளர் தினைகாத்த கிள்ளை","முலைவேட்ட பிள்ளை ...... முருகோனே","திணிகோட்டு வெள்ளி பவனாட்டி லுள்ள","சிறைமீட்ட தில்ல ...... மயில்வீரா","அலைகோட்டு வெள்ள மலைமாக்கள் விள்ள","மலைவீழ்த்த வல்ல ...... அயில்மோகா","அடிபோற்றி யல்லி முடிசூட்ட வல்ல","அடியார்க்கு நல்ல ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks for the red feet, praised by the ancient Veda and adorned with jasmine and kuravam, while life lasts in a body that wilts among women who show their price and men of false learning and liquor. The praise calls Murugan the child who longed for the breasts of the parrot girl guarding millet, the peacock hero who freed the prisoners, the vel that felled the mountain, and the good lord of devotees who crown his feet with lilies.","n":"விலை காட்டு ... அரிவைமார்: the women show a price. Most of the first four lines, in a clipped rhyme on -ல்ல, are hard to divide, so the company the poet lists is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","yaakkai","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:250"},"p:1231":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1231,"t":["களவு கொண்டு கைக்காசி னளவ றிந்து கர்ப்பூர","களப துங்க வித்தார ...... முலைமீதே","கலவி யின்பம் விற்பார்க ளவய வங்க ளைப்பாடு","கவிதெ ரிந்து கற்பார்கள் ...... சிலர்தாமே","உ ளநெ கிழ்ந்த சத்தான வுரைம றந்து சத்தான","உ னையு ணர்ந்து கத்தூரி...... மணநாறும்","உ பய பங்க யத்தாளி லபய மென்று னைப்பாடி","யுருகி நெஞ்சு சற்றோதி ...... லிழிவாமோ","அளவில் வன்க விச்சேனை பரவ வந்த சுக்஡ணவ","அரசு டன்க டற்று஡ளி ...... யெழவேபோய்","அடலி லங்கை சுட்டாடி நிசிச ரன்த சக்஡ணவ","மறவொ ரம்பு தொட்டார்த ...... மருகோனே","வளரு மந்த ரச்சோலை மிசைசெ றிந்த முற்பாலை","வனசர் கொம்பி னைத்தேடி ...... யொருவேட","வடிவு கொண்டு பித்தாகி யுருகி வெந்த றக்கானில்","மறவர் குன்றி னிற்போன ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet says some sell the pleasure of love after reckoning the coins in a man's hand, and some learn and make poems on these women's limbs; he asks whether it would be shameful if he forgot such speech, knew Murugan as the true, sang 'refuge' to his musk-scented feet and melted. The praise names Rama, who with Sugriva's army raised dust from the sea, burned Lanka and shot the one arrow at ten-headed Ravana, as Murugan's uncle, and tells of Murugan taking a hunter's form, mad with love, to find Valli in the hills.","n":"கைக்காசின் அளவறிந்து: the price is measured to the coin. The poet sets two trades side by side, the women who sell love and the poets who sing their bodies, and offers his song to the feet instead.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["varaivinmagalir","pugazh","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:250"},"p:1232":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1232,"t":["கள்ளமீ னச்சுறவு கொள்ளுமீ னற்பெரிய","கல்விவீ றக்கரிய ...... மனமாகுங்","கல்விடா துற்றதிசை சொல்விசா ரத்திசைய","மெய்கள்தோ ணிப்பிறவி ...... யலைவேலை","மெள்ள ஏறிக்குரவு வெள்ளிலார் வெட்சிதண","முல்லைவே ருற்பலமு ...... ளரிநீபம்","வில்லநீள் பொற்கனக வல்லிமே லிட்டுனது","சொல்லையோ திப்பணிவ ...... தொருநாளே","துள்ளுமா னித்தமுனி புள்ளிமான் வெற்புதவு","வள்ளிமா னுக்குமயல் ...... மொழிவோனே","தொல்வியா ளத்துவளர் செல்வர்யா கத்தரையன்","எல்லைகா ணற்கரியர் ...... குருநாதா","தெள்ளுநா தச்சுருதி வள்ளல்மோ லிப்புடைகொள்","செல்வனே முத்தமிணர் ...... பெருவாழ்வே","தெய்வயா னைக்கிளைய வெள்ளையா னைத்தலைவ","தெய்வயா னைக்கினிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether a day will come when, crossing the sea of birth with its deceitful fish and sharks in the boat of true speech, he will lay many flowers (kuravu, vetchi, mullai, utpalam, kadamba, vilvam and others) on Murugan and recite his words. The praise calls Murugan the one who spoke love to Valli born of the deer, guru to the lord whose limit Brahma and Vishnu could not see, and, with wordplay on தெய்வயானை, younger brother of the divine elephant, lord of the white elephant and dear to Devayanai.","n":"The plea is for flower worship and recitation (உனது சொல்லை ஓதிப் பணிவது), an outward worship rare in these songs. The refrain plays on தெய்வ யானை: Ganapati, Airavata and Devayanai. The first lines (கல்விடாது ... மெய்கள் தோணி) are hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","vazhipaadu","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:251"},"p:1233":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1233,"t":["கன்னியர் கடுவிட மன்னிய கயலன","கண்ணிலு மிருகன ...... தனமீதுங்","கன்மைகள் மருவிய மன்மத னுருவிலி","மென்மைகொ ளுருவிலு ...... மயலாகி","இன்னல்செய் குடிலுட னின்னமு முலகினி","லிந்நிலை பெறவிங ...... னுதியாதே","யெண்ணுமு னடியவர் நண்ணிய பதமிசை","யென்னையும் வழிபட ...... விடவேணும்","பொன்னவ மணிபயில் மன்னவ புனமற","மின்முலை தழுவிய ...... புயவீரா","புண்ணிய முளபல விண்ணவர் தொழுமுதல்","எண்மலை யொடுபொரு ...... கதிர்வேலா","தன்னிறை சடையிறை யென்முனி பரவரு","இன்னிசை யுறுதமிழ் ...... தெரிவோனே","தண்ணளி தருமொரு பன்னிரு விழிபயில்","சண்முக மழகிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet, infatuated by young women's poison carp eyes and breasts and by Kama's bodiless form, asks not to be born again into this world in the hut of suffering, and to be let worship at the feet that the devotees reach. The praise names Murugan the hero who embraced the hill girl, the vel that fought the eight mountains, the one who knows sweet Tamil set to music that the lord with matted hair praises, and the beautiful six-faced one with twelve eyes.","n":"இன்னிசை உறு தமிழ் தெரிவோனே: Murugan is the knower of Tamil with music, perhaps alluding to his teaching Siva, but the line (தன் நிறை சடை இறை என் முனி பரவரு) is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","piravi","adiyaar","thamizh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:251"},"p:1234":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1234,"t":["கிஞ்சுகமெ னச்சி வத்த தொண்டையள்மி கக்க றுத்த","கெண்டையள்பு னக்கொ டிச்சி ...... யதிபாரக்","கிம்புரிம ருப்பை யொத்த குங்குமமு லைக்கு றத்தி","கிங்கரனெ னப்ப டைத்த ...... பெயர்பேசா","நெஞ்சுருகி நெக்கு நெக்கு நின்றுதொழு நிர்க்கு ணத்தர்","நிந்தனையில் பத்தர் வெட்சி ...... மலர்தூவும்","நின்பதயு கப்ர சித்தி யென்பனவ குத்து ரைக்க","நின்பணித மிழ்த்ர யத்தை ...... யருள்வாயே","கஞ்சன்வர விட்ட துட்ட குஞ்சரம ருப்பொ சித்த","கங்கனும தித்தி கைக்க ...... மதம்வீசுங்","கந்தெறிக ளிற்று ரித்து வென்றுதிரு நட்ட மிட்ட","கம்பனும திக்க வுக்ர ...... வடிவேல்கொண்","டஞ்சியஜ கத்ர யத்தை யஞ்சலென விக்ர மித்து","அன்பர்புக ழப்பொ ருப்பொ ...... டமராடி","அன்றவுண ரைக்க ளத்தில் வென்றுததி யைக்க லக்கி","அண்டர்சிறை வெட்டி விட்ட ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks for the threefold Tamil in Murugan's service, so that he may tell in parts the fame of the two feet that melting devotees beyond the gunas and faultless bhaktas strew with vetchi, and speak the name Murugan took as servant of the hill girl with coral lips and dark eyes. The praise names Krishna, who broke the tusks of the elephant Kamsa sent, and Siva who flayed the elephant and danced, and calls Murugan the one whose fierce vel calmed the three worlds, fought the asuras, churned the sea and freed the gods.","n":"த்ரயத் தமிழ், the three Tamils of prose, music and drama, is what the poet asks for. குறத்தி கிங்கரன் எனப் படைத்த பெயர் is Murugan's own title as Valli's servant. Whether பேசா means 'so as to speak' or 'not speaking' is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thamizh","pugazh","paththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:251"},"p:1235":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1235,"t":["குடிமைமனை யாட்டியும் அடிமையொடு கூட்டமும்","குலமுமிறு மாப்புமி ...... குதியான","கொடியபெரு வாழ்க்கையி லினியபொரு ளீட்டியெ","குருடுபடு மோட்டென ...... வுடல்வீழில்","அடைவுடைவி டாச்சிறு பழையதுணி போர்த்தியெ","அரிடசுடு காட்டிடை ...... யிடுகாயம்","அழியுமள வாட்டிலுன் அமலமலர் மாப்பத","அருணசர ணாஸ்பதம் ...... அருள்வாயே","அடியினொடு மாத்தரு மொளமொளமொ ளாச்சென","அலறிவிழ வேர்க்குல ...... மொடுசாய","அவுணர்படை தோற்பெழ அருவரைக ளார்ப்பெழ","அயிலலகு சேப்பெழ ...... மறைநாலும்","உ டையமுனி யாட்பட முடுகவுணர் கீழ்ப்பட","உ யரமரர் மேற்பட ...... வடியாத","உ ததிகம ராப்பிள முதுகுலிச பார்த்திபன்","உ லகுகுடி யேற்றிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes a life of wife, servants, clan, pride and gathered wealth ending when the body falls like a broken pot, is wrapped in a small old rag and left at the burning ground, and asks to be given the red feet before the body is destroyed. The praise is a battle in drum syllables: trees fall roaring, the asuras' army flees, the mountains roar, the sage of four Vedas is made servant, the gods rise, and Indra of the thunderbolt is resettled in his world.","n":"சிறு பழைய துணி போர்த்தி: the rich man of the first lines is covered with a small old rag at the end, the reversal the song turns on. மறைநாலும் உடைய முனி ஆட்பட may be Brahma brought low, but he is not named. மொளமொளமொளாச் is drum syllable.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","yaakkai","selvam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:251"},"p:1236":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1236,"t":["குறைவ தின்றி மிக்க சலமெ லும்பு துற்ற","குடிலி லொன்றி நிற்கு ...... முயிர்மாயம்","குலைகு லைந்து தெர்ப்பை யிடைநி னைந்து நிற்ப","கொடிய கொண்ட லொத்த ...... வுருவாகி","மறலி வந்து துட்ட வினைகள் கொண்ட லைத்து","மரண மென்ற துக்க ...... மணுகாமுன்","மனமி டைஞ்ச லற்று னடிநி னைந்து நிற்க","மயிலில் வந்து முத்தி ...... தரவேணும்","அறுக மிந்து மத்த மலையெ றிந்த அப்பு","மளிசி றந்த புட்ப ...... மதுசூடி","அருந டஞ்செ யப்ப ரருளி ரங்கு கைக்கு","அரிய இன்சொல் செப்பு ...... முருகோனே","சிறுகு லந்த னக்கு ளறிவு வந்து தித்த","சிறுமி தன்த னத்தை ...... யணைமார்பா","திசைமு கன்தி கைக்க அசுர ரன்ற டைத்த","சிறைதி றந்து விட்ட ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks that, before Death comes like a dark cloud, harrows him with his evil deeds and the sorrow of death draws near while he lies on darbha grass, he may stand with untroubled mind thinking of Murugan's feet, and that Murugan come on the peacock and give release. The praise calls Murugan the one who spoke a rare sweet word to the father who dances wearing arugu grass, moon, datura and river, who embraced the girl of a small clan in whom wisdom dawned, and who opened the asuras' prison while Brahma stood bewildered.","n":"தெர்ப்பை இடை: the dying man laid on darbha grass. மறலி ... துட்ட வினைகள் கொண்டு அலைத்து: Death torments with one's own deeds. சிறுகுலம் தனக்குள் அறிவு வந்து உதித்த சிறுமி praises Valli's wisdom, not only her beauty.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","thiruvadi","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:251"},"p:1237":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1237,"t":["கோக னகமு கிழ்த்த போக புளகி தத்த","கோடு தலைகு லைத்த ...... முலையாலே","கூட வரவ ழைக்கு மாடு குழைய டர்த்த","நீடி யகுவ ளைக்கண் ...... மடமானார்","ஆக முறவ ணைத்து காசை யபக ரித்து","மீள விதழ்க டிப்ப ...... தறியாதே","ஆசை யதுகொ ளுத்து மால மதுகு டித்த","சேலில் பரித விப்ப ...... தினியேனோ","மாக நதிம திப்ர தாப மவுலி யர்க்கு","சாவி யதுவோ ரர்த்த ...... மொழிவோனே","வாகு வலைய சித்ர ஆறி ருபுய வெற்பில்","வாழ்வு பெருகு றத்தி ...... மணவாளா","வேக வுரக ரத்ந நாக சயன சக்ர","மேவி மரக தத்தின் ...... மருகோனே","வீசு திரைய லைத்த வேலை சுவற வெற்றி","வேலை யுருவ விட்ட ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women with lotus-bud breasts who call men to come, embrace them, take their money and then bite their lips again, and asks whether he must still suffer, drunk on the poison of desire. The praise calls Murugan the one who told a meaning to Siva of the crowned river and moon, the twelve-armed bridegroom of the hill girl, nephew of the emerald Vishnu lying on the serpent with the discus, and the one whose vel dried the sea.","n":"காசை அபகரித்து மீள இதழ் கடிப்பது: the theft of money comes between the embraces, which is the deceit. The word after மவுலியர்க்கு is unclear (சாவியது), but ஓர் அர்த்தம் மொழிவோனே gives Murugan telling Siva 'a meaning'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","punarchi","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:251"},"p:1238":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1238,"t":["சந்தம் புனைந்து சந்தஞ் சிறந்த","தண்கொங் கைவஞ்சி ...... மனையாளுந்","தஞ்சம் பயின்று கொஞ்சுஞ் சதங்கை","தங்கும் பதங்க ...... ளிளைஞோரும்","எந்தன் தனங்க ளென்றென்று நெஞ்சி","லென்றும் புகழ்ந்து ...... மிகவாழும்","இன்பங் களைந்து துன்பங்கள் மங்க","இன்றுன் பதங்கள் ...... தரவேணும்","கொந்தின் கடம்பு செந்தண் புயங்கள்","கொண்டங் குறிஞ்சி ...... யுறைவோனே","கொங்கின் புனஞ்செய் மின்கண்ட கந்த","குன்றம் பிளந்த ...... கதிர்வேலா","ஐந்திந் த்ரியங்கள் வென்றொன்று மன்பர்","அங்கம் பொருந்து ...... மழகோனே","அண்டந் தலங்க ளெங்குங் கலங்க","அன்றஞ் சலென்ற ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks Murugan to clear away the pleasure of living proud of a sandal-adorned wife and children with anklets, called 'my wealth', so that sorrows fade, and to give his feet today. The praise calls Murugan the dweller of Kurinji with kadamba shoulders, the vel that split the hill, the beautiful one who dwells in the bodies of devotees who conquered the five senses, and the one who said 'fear not' when the worlds shook.","n":"எந்தன் தனங்கள் என்றென்று: wife and children are named as 'my wealth', so the plea is against possessiveness more than the household itself. ஐந்திந்த்ரியங்கள் வென்று ஒன்றும் அன்பர் அங்கம் பொருந்தும்: Murugan's dwelling is the body of the self-controlled devotee.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pattru","ilvaazhkkai","thiruvadi","adakkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:251"},"p:1239":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1239,"t":["சலமல மசுத்த மிக்க தசைகுரு தியத்தி மொய்த்த","தடியுடல் தனக்கு ளுற்று ...... மிகுமாயம்","சகலமு மியற்றி மத்த மிகுமிரு தடக்கை யத்தி","தனிலுரு மிகுத்து மக்க ...... ளொடுதாரம்","கலனணி துகிற்கள் கற்பி னொடுகுல மனைத்து முற்றி","கருவழி யவத்தி லுற்று ...... மகிழ்வாகிக்","கலைபல பிடித்து நித்த மலைபடு மநர்த்த முற்ற","கடுவினை தனக்குள் நிற்ப ...... தொழியாதோ","மலைமக ளிடத்து வைத்து மதிபுனல் சடைக்குள் வைத்து","மழுவனல் கரத்துள் வைத்து ...... மருவார்கள்","மடிவுற நினைத்து வெற்பை வரிசிலை யிடக்கை வைத்து","மறைதொழ நகைத்த அத்தர் ...... பெருவாழ்வே","பலதிசை நடுக்க முற்று நிலைகெட அடற்கை யுற்ற","படையது பொருப்பில் விட்ட ...... முருகோனே","பழுதறு தவத்தி லுற்று வழிமொழி யுரைத்த பத்தர்","பலருய அருட்கண் வைத்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether his harsh karma will not end: in a body of water, filth, flesh, blood and bone, he does everything for maya, gathers elephants, children, wife, jewels, clothes and clan, rejoices in the womb's way, and studies arts, suffering daily without meaning. The praise calls Murugan the great life of the father who keeps the mountain's daughter at his side, the moon and river in his hair, axe and fire in his hand, the mountain as a bow, and who laughed down his foes, and the one who looks with grace on devotees in faultless tapas.","n":"கலைபல பிடித்து நித்தம் அலைபடும் அநர்த்தம்: learning is listed among the attachments, not set against them. மறைதொழ நகைத்த: Siva destroying his foes (Tripura) by a laugh, told in the text's own details.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","pattru","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:251"},"p:1240":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1240,"t":["சாங்கரிபாடியிட வோங்கிய ஞானசுக","தாண்டவ மாடியவர் ...... வடிவான","சாந்தம தீதமுணர் கூந்தம சாதியவர்","தாங்களு ஞானமுற ...... வடியேனுந்","தூங்கிய பார்வையொடு தாங்கிய வாயுவொடு","தோன்றிய சோதியொடு ...... சிவயோகந்","தூண்டிய சீவனோடு வேண்டிய காலமொடு","சோம்பினில் வாழும்வகை ...... அருளாதோ","வாங்குகை யானையென வீன்குலை வாழைவளர்","வான்பொழில் சூழும்வய ...... லயலேறி","மாங்கனி தேனொழுக வேங்கையில் மேலரிகள்","மாந்திய வாரணிய ...... மலைமீதிற்","பூங்கொடி போலுமிடை யேங்கிட வாரமணி","பூண்பன பாரியன ...... தனபாரப்","பூங்குற மாதினுட வாடியிருள்","பூம்பொழில் மேவிவளர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether Murugan will not grant him, as those who know peace beyond have gained wisdom, a way to live in stillness with gaze half-closed, breath held, the light appearing, and Sivayoga stirring the soul, in the dance-form of the one who danced the dance of jnana and bliss while Uma sang. The praise pictures the hill of plantains like elephant trunks, mango honey and bees on vengai, and Murugan dwelling in the grove with the hill girl.","n":"தூங்கிய பார்வை ... தாங்கிய வாயு ... தோன்றிய சோதி ... சிவயோகம்: the plea is a compact list of yoga's stages, and சோம்பினில் வாழும் வகை, living in 'idleness', means the stillness of the yogi. Line 15 seems short of syllables.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","praanan","oli","thirukkoothu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:251"},"p:1241":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1241,"t":["சிவஞான புண்டரிக மலர்மாது டன்கலவி","சிவபோக மன்பருக ...... அறியாமற்","செகமீது ழன்றுமல வடிவாயி ருந்துபொது","திகழ்மாதர் பின்செருமி ...... யழிவேனோ","தவமாத வங்கள்பயில் அடியார்க ணங்களொடு","தயவாய்ம கிழ்ந்துதினம் ...... விளையாடத்","தமியேன்ம லங்களிரு வினைநோயி டிந்தலற","ததிநாளும் வந்ததென்முன் ...... வரவேணும்","உ வகாரியன்பர்பணி கலியாணி யெந்தையிட","முறைநாய கங்கவுரி...... சிவகாமி","ஒளிரானை யின்கரமில் மகிழ்மாது ளங்கனியை","யொருநாள்ப கிர்ந்தவுமை ...... யருள்பாலா","அவமேபி றந்தஎனை யிறவாம லன்பர்புகு","மமுதால யம்பதவி ...... யருள்வோனே","அழகாந கம்பொலியு மயிலாகு றிஞ்சிமகிழ்","அயிலாபு கழ்ந்தவர்கள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether he must perish, not knowing how to drink the Siva-bliss of union with the lady of the lotus of Siva-knowledge, wandering as a form of impurity and panting after common women; he asks Murugan to come before him daily so he may play with the hosts of devotees and his impurities and twin deeds break. The praise names Uma, the bride, Gauri and Sivakami at Siva's side, who one day shared a pomegranate in the elephant's hand, and asks Murugan, her son, to grant the deathless abode of nectar to one born in vain.","n":"சிவஞான புண்டரிக மலர்மாது: Siva-knowledge is personified as a lady to be embraced, set against the common women (பொது மாதர்). The pomegranate shared by Uma is named but its story is not told.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","malam","adiyaar","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:251"},"p:1242":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1242,"t":["சீறிட்டு லாவு கண்கள் மாதர்க்கு நாள்ம ருண்டு","சேவித்து மாசை கொண்டு ...... முழல்வேனைச்","சீரிட்ட மாக நின்ற காசைக்கொ டாத பின்பு","சீரற்று வாழு மின்பம் ...... நலியாதே","ஆறெட்டு மாய்வி ரிந்து மாறெட்டு மாகி நின்று","மாருக்கு மேவி ளம்ப ...... அறியாதே","ஆகத்து ளேம கிழ்ந்த ஜோதிப்ர காச இன்பம்","ஆவிக்கு ளேது லங்கி ...... அருளாதோ","மாறிட்டு வான டுங்க மேலிட்டு மேல கண்டம்","வாய்விட்டு மாதி ரங்கள் ...... பிளவாக","வாள்தொட்டு நேர்ந டந்த சூர்வஜ்ர மார்பு நெஞ்சும்","வான்முட்ட வீறு செம்பொன் ...... வரையோடு","கூறிட்ட வேல பங்க வீரர்க்கு வீர கந்த","கோதற்ற வேடர் தங்கள் ...... புனம்வாழுங்","கோலப்பெண் வாகு கண்டு மாலுற்று வேளை கொண்டு","கூடிக்கு லாவு மண்டர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks that the wretchedness of being cast off, after he served women of darting eyes and stopped giving the money they wanted, not harm him, and asks whether the light shining as bliss in the body and in the soul will not appear, the one spread out as six and eight that no one can describe. The praise is of the vel that split Sura's diamond chest and the golden hill, and of Murugan, hero of heroes, who saw the hunters' beautiful girl and joined her in love.","n":"காசைக் கொடாத பின்பு சீரற்று: when the money stops the man is ruined, the courtesan's logic. ஆறெட்டுமாய் விரிந்தும் may count the tattvas, but the number is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","oli","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:251"},"p:1243":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1243,"t":["சூதினுண வாசைதனி லேசுழலு மீனதென","தூசுவழ கானவடி ...... வதனாலே","சூதமுட னேருமென மாதர்நசை தேடுபொரு","ளாசைதமி லேசுழல ...... வருகாலன்","ஆதிவிதி யோடுபிற ழாதவகை தேடியென","தாவிதனை யேகுருகி ...... வருபோது","ஆதிமுரு காதிமுரு காதிமுரு காஎனவு","மாதிமுரு காநினைவு ...... தருவாயே","ஓதமுகி லாடுகிரியேறுபட வாழசுரர்","ஓலமிட வேயயில்கொ ...... டமராடீ","ஓநமசி வாயகுரு பாதமதி லேபணியும்","யோகமயி லாஅமலை ...... மகிழ்பாலா","நாதரகு ராமஅரிமாயன்மரு காபுவன","நாடுமடி யார்கள்மன ...... துறைவோனே","ஞானசுர வானைகண வாமுருக னேயமரர்","நாடுபெற வாழவருள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet, caught by women seeking wealth like a fish on the hook, asks that when Death comes by fate to seek his life he be given the thought to call 'Adi Muruga, Adi Muruga'. The praise calls Murugan the one who fought with the vel while the asuras cried out, the yoga peacock who bows at the feet of the guru of Om Namasivaya, Uma's son, nephew of Rama and Hari, dweller in devotees' hearts, and Devayanai's husband.","n":"ஆதிமுருகா ஆதிமுருகா ஆதிமுருகா என: the plea at the hour of death is simply for the name, repeated. ஓநமசிவாய குரு பாதம் makes Siva's mantra itself the guru at whose feet Murugan bows.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","varaivinmagalir","panchaakkaram"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:251"},"p:1244":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1244,"t":["செழுந்தாது பார்மாது மரும்பாதி ரூபோடு","சிறந்தியாதி லு஡மாசை ...... யொழியாத","திறம்பூத வேதாள னரும்பாவ மேகோடி","செயுங்காய நோயாள ...... னரகேழில்","விழுந்தாழ ஧வ்முழ்க இடுங்காலன் மேயாவி","விடுங்கால மேநாயென் ...... வினைபாவம்","விரைந்தேக வேவாசி துரந்தோடி யேஞான","விளம்போசை யேபேசி ...... வரவேணும்","அழுங்கோடி தேவார்க ளமர்ந்தார வானீடி","அழன்றேகி மாசீத ...... நெடுவேலை","அதிர்ந்தோட வேகாலன் விழுந்தோட வேகூர","அலங்கார வேலேவு ...... முருகோனே","கொழுங்கானி லேமாதர் செழுஞ்சேலை யேகோடு","குருந்தேறு மால்மாயன் ...... மருகோனே","குறம்பாடு வார்சேரிபுகுந்தாசை மாதோடு","குணங்கூடி யேவாழு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet, a ghoul for gold, land and women, a diseased doer of crores of sins, asks that when Death comes to drop him into the seven hells Murugan come fast on his steed, speaking the word of wisdom, so his sins go. The praise calls Murugan the vel that made the sea quake and Death fall and run, nephew of Krishna who climbed the kurundha tree with the women's saris, and the one who lives with the hill girl.","n":"செழுந்தாது பார் மாது: gold, earth and woman, the triple desire. விழுந்தோடவே காலன் in the praise answers the plea: Death himself falls. Several words are corrupt.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["kootru","naragam","avaa"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:251"},"p:1245":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1245,"t":["தத்த னமுமடிமை சுற்ற மொடுபுதல்வர்","தக்க மனையினமு ...... மனைவாழ்வுந்","தப்பு நிலைமையணு கைக்கு வரவிரகு","தைக்கு மயல்நினைவு ...... குறுகாமுன்","பத்தி யுடனுருகி நித்த முனதடிகள்","பற்று மருள்நினைவு ...... தருவாயே","பத்து முடியுருளு வித்த பகழியினர்","பச்சை நிறமுகிலின் ...... மருகோனே","அத்தி முகவனழ குற்ற பெழைவயிற","னப்ப மவரைபொரி...... அவல்தேனும்","அப்பி யமுதுசெயு மொய்ப்ப னுதவஅட","விக்குள் மறமகளை ...... யணைவோனே","முத்தி தருமுதல்வர் முக்க ணிறைவரொடு","முற்று மறைமொழியை ...... மொழிவோனே","முட்ட வசுரர்கிளை கெட்டு முறியமுதல்","வெட்டி யமர்பொருத ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks to be given, before the failing time comes for wealth, servants, kin, sons and house, the thought of grace to melt in devotion and hold Murugan's feet daily. The praise names Rama who rolled the ten heads, calls Murugan the one whom the pot-bellied elephant god, eating cakes, puffed rice and honey, helped to embrace the hunter girl in the forest, and the one who told the Veda's word to the three-eyed lord who gives release.","n":"மொய்ப்பன் உதவ அடவிக்குள் மறமகளை அணைவோனே: Ganapati's help in the Valli story is named outright. முத்தி தரு முதல்வர் ... மறைமொழியை மொழிவோனே: Murugan teaching Siva, without naming Om.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pattru","paththi","thiruvadi","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:251"},"p:1246":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1246,"t":["தலைவலய போகமுஞ் சலனமிகு மோகமுந்","தவறுதரு காமமுங் ...... கனல்போலுந்","தணிவரிய கோபமுந் துணிவரிய லோபமுஞ்","சமயவெகு ரூபமும் ...... பிறிதேதும்","அலமலமெ னாஎழுந் தவர்களநு பூதிகொண்","டறியுமொரு காரணந் ...... தனைநாடா","ததிமதபு ராணமுஞ் சுருதிகளு மாகிநின்","றபரிமித மாய்விளம் ...... புவதோதான்","கலகஇரு பாணமுந் திலகவொரு சாபமுங்","களபமொழி யாதகொங் ...... கையுமாகிக்","கவருமவ தாரமுங் கொடியபரிதாபமுங்","கருதியிது வேளையென் ...... றுகிராத","குலதிலக மானுடன் கலவிபுரிவாய்பொருங்","குலிசகர வாசவன் ...... திருநாடு","குடிபுகநி சாசரன் பொடிபடம கீதரன்","குலையநெடு வேல்விடும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether one should speak without end as the Puranas and Srutis, without seeking the one cause known by experience by those who rose saying 'enough' to pleasures, delusion, lust, fire-like anger, greed and the many forms of the sects. The praise tells of Murugan who, taking a form and feigning distress, judged the time and joined the hunter girl, armed with Kama's two arrows and bow, and whose vel resettled Indra and powdered the demon.","n":"அநுபூதி கொண்டு அறியும் ஒரு காரணம்: experience, not scripture or sect, knows the one cause. The question (விளம்புவதோதான்) may be the poet's self-rebuke or a jab at scholars; the sentence's subject is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["meyyunarvu","samayam","vekuli","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:251"},"p:1247":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1247,"t":["தவநெறி தவறிய குருடுகள் தலைபறி கதறிய ...... பரபாதத்","தருமிகள் கருமிகள் வெகுவித சமயிக ளவரொடு ...... சருவாநின்","றவனிவ னுவனுட னவளிவ ளுவளது இதுவுது ...... வெனுமாறற்","றருவுரு வொழிதரு வுருவுடை யதுபதி தமியனு ...... முணர்வேனோ","குவலய முழுவதும் மதிர்பட வடகுவ டிடிபட ...... வுரகேசன்","கொடுமுடி பலநெரிதரநெடு முதுகுரை கடல்புனல் ...... வறிதாகத்","துவல்கொடு முறையிடு சுரர்பதி துயரது கெடநிசி ...... சரர்சேனை","துகளெழ நடநவில் மரகத துரகதம் வரவல ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether he, a lonely one, will come to know the Lord whose form passes beyond both form and the formless and beyond all talk of he, she and it, without being drawn into dispute with the blind who left the path of austerity, the hair-plucking ascetics, ritualists and sectarians of many kinds. The praise shows the earth shaken, the northern peak shattered, the serpent king's crests crushed and the ancient sea dried as Murugan answers the appeal of the gods' king and turns the demon army to dust, riding the dancing emerald steed.","n":"அவன் இவன் உவன் உடன் அவள் இவள் உவள் அது இது உது எனும் ஆறு அற்று: the Lord is beyond the threefold grammar of person and distance, a formula that also opens Saiva and Vaishnava doctrinal works. தலைபறி, those who pluck out their hair, are Jain ascetics; பரபாதத் தருமிகள் is less clear. The plea is not to defeat the sects but to stop quarrelling (சருவா நின்று) and know.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["samayam","pathi","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:252"},"p:1248":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1248,"t":["திதலையு லாத்துபொற் களபம்வி டாப்புதுத்","த்ரிவிதக டாக்களிற் ...... றுரகோடு","சிகரம காப்ரபைக் குவடென வாய்த்துநற்","சுரர்குடி யேற்றிவிட் ...... டிளநீரை","மதனவி டாய்த்தனத் திளைஞரை வாட்செப்","பிணமுலை மாத்தவக் ...... கொடிபோல்வார்","வலையிலி ராப்பகற் பொழுதுகள் போக்குமற்","றெனையுனை வாழ்த்தவைத் ...... தருள்வாயே","சததள பார்த்திபற் கரிபுரு ஷோத்தமற்","கெரிகன லேற்றவற் ...... குணராதோர்","சகலச மார்த்தசத் தியவன சூக்ஷமுக்","கியபர மார்த்தமுற் ...... புகல்வோனே","கதிர்மணி நீர்க்கடற் சுழிபுகு ராக்ஷதக்","கலகப ராக்ரமக் ...... கதிர்வேலா","கருதிய பாட்டினிற் றலைதெரிமாக்ஷணக்","கவிஞரு சாத்துணைப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes the breasts of women, likened to the tusks of rutting elephants, to radiant peaks and to tender coconuts, and the creeper-like women who exhaust young men, and asks that the nights and days he spends in their net end and he be set to praise Murugan. The praise names him the one who first told the subtle, supreme truth to Brahma, Vishnu and Siva who did not know it, the vel that struck the rakshasa hiding in the sea, and the companion of great poets who find the best word in an instant.","n":"சததள பார்த்திபற்கு அரி புருஷோத்தமற்கு எரிகனல் ஏற்றவற்கு: Brahma of the hundred-petalled lotus, Vishnu and Siva who holds fire all receive the teaching; Om is not named. கவிஞர் உசாத்துணை: Murugan as the poets' confidant, a role the poet claims for his own songs. The Sanskrit compounds of the praise are hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","guru","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:252"},"p:1249":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1249,"t":["திரைவஞ்ச இருவினைகள் நரையங் கமலமழிய","சிவகங்கை தனில்முழுகி ...... விளையாடிச்","சிவம்வந்து குதிகொளக வடிவுன்றன் வடிவமென","திகழண்டர் முநிவர்கண ...... மயன்மாலும்","அரன்மைந்த னெனகளிறு முகனெம்பி யெனமகிழ","அடியென்க ணளிபரவ ...... மயிலேறி","அயில்கொண்டு திருநடன மெனதந்தை யுடன்மருவி","அருமந்த பொருளையினி ...... யருள்வாயே","பரியென்ப நரிகள்தமை நடனங்கொ டொருவழுதி","பரிதுஞ்ச வருமதுரை ...... நடராஜன்","பழியஞ்சி யெனதருகி லுறைபுண்ட ரிகவடிவ","பவளஞ்சொ லுமைகொழுந ...... னருள்பாலா","இருள்வஞ்ச கிரியவுண ருடனெங்க ளிருவினையு","மெரியுண்டு பொடியஅயில் ...... விடுவோனே","எனதன்பி லுறைசயில மகிழ்வஞ்சி குறமகளொ","டெணுபஞ்ச ணையின்மருவு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks that, with wrinkles, grey hair, the body's suffering and the two deceitful deeds destroyed, he bathe and play in the Sivaganga tank, that Sivam leap into him so that his inner form becomes Murugan's form, and that, with gods, sages, Brahma and Vishnu calling him Hara's son and Ganapati 'my younger brother', Murugan come on the peacock with the vel, join his father in the holy dance, and give the precious meaning. The praise names Siva, the dancer of Madurai who made foxes into horses for a Pandya king, and Murugan the vel that burns to powder the dark hill, the asuras and our two deeds, who dwells in the poet's love with Valli.","n":"The plea is a vision, not a confession: bath, possession by Sivam, transformation into the god's form (அக வடிவு உன்றன் வடிவம் என), and the dance. பரி என்ப நரிகள் தமை and மதுரை நடராஜன் name the foxes turned to horses, a Madurai sacred game of Siva. பழியஞ்சி may be the Madurai Siva's name 'who feared blame', and புண்டரிக வடிவ may point to Chidambaram; both are unclear. The same vel burns the asuras and 'our two deeds'.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["theerththam","thirukkoothu","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:252"},"p:1250":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1250,"t":["தீயுதை தாத்ரிபானீய மேற்ற","வானீதி யாற்றி ...... கழுமாசைச்","சேறு஡று தோற்பை யானாக நோக்கு","மாமாயை தீர்க்க ...... அறியாதே","பேய்பூத மூத்த பாறோரிகாக்கை","பீறாஇ ழாத்தி ...... னுடல்பேணிப்","பேயோன டாத்து கோமாளி வாழ்க்கை","போமாறு பேர்த்து ...... னடிதாராய்","வேயூறு சீர்க்கை வேல்வேடர் காட்டி","லேய்வாளை வேட்க ...... வுருமாறி","மீளாது வேட்கை மீதூர வாய்த்த","வேலோடு வேய்த்த ....... ; ளையோனே","மாயூர வேற்றின் மீதே புகாப்பொன்","மாமேரு வேர்ப்ப ...... றியமோதி","மாறான மாக்கள் நீறாக வோட்டி","வானாடு காத்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses that, not knowing how to end the great maya that takes as 'I' a leather bag of the five elements oozing the mire of desire, he tends a body that ghosts, vultures, jackals and crows will tear and drag, and asks Murugan to uproot him from this clown's life driven as if by a madman and give his feet. The praise tells of Murugan changing form to court the hunter girl in the bamboo forest, riding the peacock, striking golden Meru to its roots and burning hostile beings to ash to guard heaven.","n":"தோற்பை யான் ஆக நோக்கும் மா மாயை: the delusion named is taking the body for oneself, not only lust. தீ, உதை, தாத்ரி, பானீயம், வான்: the five elements in mixed Tamil and Sanskrit. பேயோன் நடாத்து கோமாளி வாழ்க்கை, a buffoon's life run by a madman, is the song's sharpest image.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["yaakkai","maayai","agandhai","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:252"},"p:1251":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1251,"t":["துடித்தெதிர் வடித்தெழு குதர்க்கச மயத்தவர்","சுழற்கொரு கொடிக்கொடி ...... யெதிர்கூறித்","துகைப்பன கிதத்தலை யறுப்பன யில்விட்டுடல்","துணிப்பன கணித்தலை ...... மிசைபார","முடித்தலை விழுப்பன முழுக்கஅ டிமைப்பட","முறைப்படு மறைத்திர ...... ளறியாத","முதற்பொருள் புலப்பட வுணர்த்துவ னெனக்கொரு","மொழிப்பொருள் பழிப்பற ...... அருள்வாயே","குடிப்பன முகப்பன நெடிப்பன நடிப்பன","கொழுத்தகு ருதிக்கட ...... லிடையூடே","குதிப்பன மதிப்பன குளிப்பன களிப்பன","குவட்டினை யிடிப்பன ...... சிலபாடல்","படிப்பன திருப்புக ழெடுப்பன முடிப்பன","பயிற்றிய லகைக்குலம் ...... விளையாடப்","பகைத்தெழு மரக்கரை யிமைப்பொழு தினிற்பொடி","படப்பொரு துழக்கிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"Against sophist sectarians who rise quivering to argue and plant flag against flag, the poet asks to be wholly Murugan's servant and given, without fault, the meaning of the one word that makes plain the first principle unknown to the Vedas. The praise shows a trained troupe of ghosts drinking, dancing and bathing in a sea of blood, smashing hills, and reciting, beginning and ending Thiruppugazh songs, as Murugan crushes the demons in a blink.","n":"படிப்பன திருப்புகழ் எடுப்பன முடிப்பன: the book names itself, sung by the ghosts of the battlefield. ஒரு மொழிப்பொருள், the meaning of one word, likely points to Om but does not name it. The first half's chain of -ன verbs (துகைப்பன, அறுப்பன, துணிப்பன, விழுப்பன), trampling and cutting heads, is hard to assign: it may describe the poet's victory over the debaters in argument or the vel's work.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["samayam","thondu","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:252"},"p:1252":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1252,"t":["துத்தி நச்ச ராவிளம் பிச்சி நொச்சி கூவிளஞ்","சுக்கி லக்க லாமிர்தப் ...... பிறைசூதம்","சுத்த ரத்த பாடலம் பொற்க டுக்கை யேடலஞ்","சுத்த சொற்ப கீரதித் ...... திரைநீலம்","புத்தெ ருக்கு பாழிகங் கொத்தெ டுத்த தாளிதண்","பொற்பு மத்து வேணியர்க் ...... கருள்கூரும்","புத்தி சித்தி வாய்கனஞ் சுத்த சத்ய வாசகம்","புற்பு தப்பி ராணனுக் ...... கருள்வாயே","பத்தி யுற்ற தோகையம் பச்சை வெற்றி வாகனம்","பக்க மிட்டு லாவியச் ...... சுரர்மாளப்","பக்க விட்டு வாய்நிணங் கக்க வெட்டி வாய்தரும்","பத்ம சிட்ட னோடமுத் ...... தெறிமீனக்","கைத்த லைப்ர வாகையுந் தத்த ளிக்க மாமுறிந்","துட்க முத்து வாரணச் ...... சதகோடி","கைக்க ளிற்று வாரணம் புக்கொ ளிக்க வாரணங்","கைப்பி டித்த சேவகப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet lists the adornments of Siva's matted hair (the hooded serpent, jasmine, nochi, vilva, the crescent of nectar, the red patali, golden konrai, the waves of the Ganga, the blue lily, erukku, dhatura) and asks Murugan to give him, whose life is a bubble, the pure true word of weighty wisdom he gave in grace to that Siva. The praise shows Murugan on the peacock, killing the asuras, the sea heaving, Sura as the mango split, Indra's elephant fleeing to hide, and the cock held in his hand.","n":"புற்புதப் பிராணன், a life like a bubble (budbuda), is the plea's whole self-description, set against the long catalogue of Siva's flowers. வாரணம் returns in the last lines as sea, elephant and cock, a play the rhythm hides. Several flower names and the praise's compounds are uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","nilaiyaamai","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:252"},"p:1253":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1253,"t":["தெரிவை மக்கள் செல்வ முரிமை மிக்க வுண்மை","தெரிவ தற்கு உ ள்ள ...... முணராமுன்","சினமி குத்த திண்ணர் தனிவ ளைத்து வெய்ய","சிலுகு தைத்து வன்மை ...... சிதையாமுன்","பரவை புக்கு தொய்யு மரவ ணைக்குள் வைகு","பரம னுக்கு நல்ல ...... மருகோனே","பழுதில் நிற்சொல் சொல்லி யெழுதி நித்த முண்மை","பகர்வ தற்கு நன்மை ...... தருவாயே","இருகி ரிக்க ளுள்ள வரைத டிக்கு மின்னு","மிடியு மொய்த்த தென்ன ...... எழுசூரை","எழுக டற்கு ளுள்ளு முழுகு வித்து விண்ணு","ளிமைய வர்க்கு வன்மை ...... தருவோனே","அரிவை பக்க முய்ய வுருகி வைக்கு மைய","ரறிய மிக்க வுண்மை ...... யருள்வோனே","அறிவி னுக்கு ளென்னை நெறியில் வைக்க வல்ல","அடிய வர்க்கு நல்ல ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"Before his heart can grasp the truth about wife, children, wealth and kin, and before fierce, strong ones surround him, kick him with cruel troubles and break his strength, the poet asks to be given the good of speaking and writing faultless words of Murugan and telling the truth daily. The praise names him Vishnu's nephew, the one who drowned Sura in the seven seas and gave the gods strength, who gave the great truth to Siva, and who is good to the devotees able to keep the poet on the path within knowledge.","n":"சினமிகுத்த திண்ணர் are likely Death's servants, though the song does not name Yama. நிற்சொல் சொல்லி எழுதி: the plea is for speaking and writing praise, a poet's petition. The last line credits devotees (அடியவர்) with keeping the poet on the path.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","kootru","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:252"},"p:1254":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1254,"t":["தென்றலு மன்றி யின்றலை பொங்கு","திண்கட லொன்று ...... மிகமோதச்","செந்தழ லென்று வெந்தழல் சிந்து","திங்களும் வந்து ...... துணையேய","அன்றிலு மன்றி துன்றுச ரங்க","ளைந்துமெ னெஞ்ச ...... மழியாதே","அந்தியி லென்றன் வெந்துய ரஞ்ச","அன்பொட லங்கல் ...... தரவேணும்","வென்றிவி ளங்கு குன்றவர் வஞ்சி","விஞ்சிய கொங்கை ...... புணர்மார்பா","வெண்டர ளங்கள் தண்டைச தங்கை","மின்கொடி லங்கு ...... கழலோனே","கொன்றைய ணிந்த சங்கர ரன்று","கும்பிட வந்த ...... குமரேசா","குன்றிட அண்ட ரன்றுய வென்று","குன்றமெ றிந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"In the voice of a love-sick girl, the singer says that the south wind, the pounding sea, the moon scattering fire, the anril bird and the five arrows close in at dusk, and asks the lover to give his garland lovingly so that her heart does not perish. The praise names Murugan whose chest embraces Valli, whose anklets are hung with pearls and bells, whom konrai-wearing Siva once came to worship, and who struck the hill.","n":"This is akam poetry, not the poet's confession: the request for the hero's garland (அலங்கல்) is the heroine's classical plea. The machinery is that of 24: wind, sea, moon, anril and Kama's arrows at evening.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","maalai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:252"},"p:1255":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1255,"t":["தோரண கனக வாசலில் முழவு","தோல்முர சதிர ...... முதிராத","தோகையர் கவரிவீசவ யிரியர்","தோள்வலி புகழ ...... மதகோப","வாரண ரதப தாகினி துரக","மாதிர நிறைய ...... அரசாகி","வாழினும் வறுமை கூரினு நினது","வார்கழ லொழிய ...... மொழியேனே","பூரண புவன காரண சவரி","பூதர புளக ...... தனபார","பூஷண நிருதர் தூஷண விபுதர்","பூபதி நகரி...... குடியேற","ஆரண வனச ஈரிரு குடுமி","ஆரியன் வெருவ ...... மயிலேறு","மாரிய பரம ஞானமு மழகு","மாண்மையு முடைய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet vows that whether he lives as a king, with drums at a festooned golden gate, young women waving chowries, bards praising his arms and elephants, chariots, banners and horses filling the land, or whether poverty grows, he will speak of nothing but Murugan's feet. The praise names him the cause of the worlds, adorned by the hill-like breasts of the hunter girl, scourge of the asuras who resettled the gods' king in his city, who frightened Brahma and rides the peacock, with supreme wisdom, beauty and manliness.","n":"There is no confession and no request: வாழினும் வறுமை கூரினும் ... ஒழிய மொழியேனே is a vow of constancy in either fortune. The royal pomp is described at length only to be set aside.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thiruvadi","selvam","inmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:252"},"p:1256":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1256,"t":["நச்சுவாள் விழிக்கொ டெற்றியே தனத்தை","நத்துவார் சுகத்தில் ...... நலமாக","நட்டமா மனத்தை யிட்டமே கொடுத்து","நத்துவாழ் கடற்கு ...... ளணைபோலே","கச்சமே செலுத்தி யச்சமே படுத்து","கட்டஏழ் பிறப்பு ...... விடவேதான்","கற்றநூ லுகக்க வெட்கமே செறித்த","கட்டனே னினைப்ப ...... தொருநாளே","இச்சையே செலுத்தி யுச்சிதாள் பலிக்கு","மிட்டமா லவற்கு ...... மருகோனே","எற்றுவா ரிதிக்குள் முற்றிநீள் பொருப்பை","யெக்கிநேர் மடித்த ....... ; ளையோனே","மெச்சவே புடைத்த முத்தமார் தனத்தி","மிக்கவாள் படைத்த ...... விழியாலே","வெட்டுமா மறத்தி யொக்கவே யிருக்க","வெற்றிவே லெடுத்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses giving his mind willingly to women who strike with poison-sword eyes and covet wealth, a dam built in the conch-filled sea, and asks whether a day will come when he, a wretch packed with shame, will think of Murugan so that the seven troubled births leave and the books he learned rejoice. The praise names him Vishnu's nephew, the young one who crushed the long hill in the sea, and the one who took up the vel to stand beside the hunter woman with pearl-hung breasts and cutting eyes.","n":"தனத்தை நத்துவார் may mean 'who covet wealth' or refer to breasts; the reading of courtesans rests on that word. கற்ற நூல் உகக்க: the poet's learning is shamed by his life and would be fulfilled by thinking of the Lord. The marker before ளையோனே has lost a syllable.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","piravi","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:252"},"p:1257":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1257,"t":["நற்குணமு ளார்த மைப்பொல் மைக்குழலி லேசி றக்க","நற்பரிம ளாதி துற்ற ...... மலர்சூடி","நச்சுவிழி யால்ம யக்கி யிச்சைபல பேசி யுற்று","நற்பொருள வாம னத்தர் ...... வசமாகி","வெற்பனைய மாத னத்தை பொற்புறவு றாவ ணைத்து","மெத்தமய லாகி நித்த ...... மெலியாதே","வெட்சிகமழ் நீப புஷ்ப வெற்றிசிறு பாத பத்ம","மெய்க்கிருபை நீய ளிப்ப ...... தொருநாளே","ரத்தினப ணாதி ருத்தன் மெய்ச்சுதனு நாடு மிக்க","லக்ஷணகு மார சுப்ர ...... மணியோனே","நற்றிசையு மேறி யிட்ட பொய்ச்சமணை வேர றுத்து","நற்றிருநி றேப தப்பி ...... விளையாடும்","சற்சனகு மார வ்ருத்தி அற்புதசி வாய னுக்கொர்","சற்குருவி நோத சித்ர ...... மயில்வீரா","சக்ரதரன் மார்ப கத்தி லுக்ரமுட னேத ரித்த","சத்தியடை யாள மிட்ட ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women with flower-decked, fragrant hair who enchant with poison eyes and speak desires, their minds set on wealth, and asks whether, instead of wasting daily in infatuation with them, a day will come when Murugan gives the true grace of his small lotus feet fragrant with vetchi and kadamba. The praise names him the son of Rudra, the Kumara who uprooted the false Jains spread in all directions and spread the holy ash, the true guru of Siva, and the one who set the mark of his spear on the chest of discus-bearing Vishnu.","n":"பொய்ச் சமணை வேர் அறுத்து நல் திருநீறே பரப்பி: Murugan identified with Sambandar, who drove out the Jains and spread the holy ash. சக்ரதரன் மார்பகத்தில் சத்தி அடையாளம் இட்ட, the vel's mark on Vishnu's chest, is an allusion I cannot identify.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","mayal","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:252"},"p:1258":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1258,"t":["நாகாங்க ரோமங் காட்டி வாரேந்து நாகங் காட்டி","நாமேந்து பாலங் காட்டி ...... யபிராம","நானாங்க ராகங் காட்டி நாகேந்த்ர நீலங் காட்டி","நாயேன்ப்ர காசங் காட்டி ...... மடலு஡ர","மேகாங்க கேசங் காட்டி வாயாம்பல் வாசங் காட்டி","மீதூர்ந்த போகங் காட்டி ...... யுயி஡ணர்வார்","மேல்வீழ்ந்து தோயுந் தூர்த்தன் மோகாந்த காரந் தீர்க்க","வேதாந்த தீபங் காட்டி ...... யருள்வாயே","ஏகாந்த வீரம் போற்றி நீலாங்க யானம் போற்றி","யேடார்ந்த நீபம் போற்றி ...... முகில்தாவி","ஏறோங்க லேழுஞ் சாய்த்த நான்மூன்று தோளும் போற்றி","யார்வேண்டி னாலுங் கேட்ட ...... பொருளீயும்","த்யாகாங்க சீலம் போற்றி வாயோந்தி டாதன் றார்த்து","தேசாங்க சூரன் தோற்க ...... மயிலேறிச்","சேவேந்தி தேசம் பார்க்க வேலேந்தி மீனம் பூத்த","தேவேந்த்ர லோகங் காத்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet lists what women show, their hair-line like serpents, bodiced breasts, marked foreheads, cosmetics, dark eyes, cloud-black hair and lily-scented mouths, until he would ride the madal and falls upon those who drag out his life, and asks Murugan to show the lamp of Vedanta to end this rogue's darkness of delusion. The praise is a chain of 'hail': to his lone valour, his blue steed, his kadamba garland, his twelve arms that toppled the seven hills, his generosity that gives what anyone asks; he defeated Sura and guarded Indra's starry world.","n":"The refrain காட்டி, 'showing', runs through both halves: the women show their bodies, and the poet asks the Lord to show (காட்டி) the lamp of Vedanta instead. மடல் ஊர, to ride the palmyra horse, is the akam lover's public last resort, here turned on the poet himself. போற்றி is the praise's refrain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kaamam","mayal","vedaantham","madal"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:252"},"p:1259":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1259,"t":["பரிமள மலரடுத் தகில்மண முழுகிமைப்","பரவிய ம்ருகமதக் ...... குழல்மானார்","பருமணி வயிரமுத் திலகிய குழையினிற்","படைபொரு வனவிழிக் ...... கயலாலே","எரியுறு மெழுகெனத் தனிமன மடையநெக்","கினமையோ டுருகவிட் ...... டவமேயான்","இருவினை நலியமெய்த் திறலுட னறிவுகெட்","டிடர்படு வதுகெடுத் ...... தருள்வாயே","சொரிமத அருவிவிட் டொழுகிய புகர்முகத்","தொளைபடு கரமலைக் ...... கிளையோனே","துடியிடை யொருகுறக் குலமயில் புளகிதத்","துணைமுலை தழுவுபொற் ...... புயவீரா","அரியன பலவிதத் தொடுதிமி லையுமுடுக்","கையுமொகு மொகுவெனச் ...... சதகோடி","அலகையு முடனடித் திடவடி யயிலெடுத்","தமர்செயு மறுமுகப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"Melting like wax in fire before women with musk-scented, flower-decked hair and carp-eyes that fight like weapons by their earrings, the poet asks Murugan to end the suffering in which the two deeds torment him and he loses strength and sense. The praise names him younger brother of the elephant-faced one, the hero whose golden arms embrace Valli, and the six-faced one who fought with the vel while drums sounded and countless ghosts danced.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kan","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:252"},"p:1260":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1260,"t":["பற்றநெட் டைப்படைத் துட்டிருட் டைத்தயிர்ப்","பத்தைமுட் டிப்படுத் ...... தயில்மாதர்","பக்கமிட் டுப்பொருட் கொட்குமிட் டப்பரப்","பற்றுகெட் டுப்பயிர்க் ...... களைபோலுங்","கற்றகட் டுக்கவிக் கொட்டமொட் டிக்கனைத்","திட்டுகத் தத்தினுற் ...... றகமாயுங்","கட்டமற் றுக்கழற் பற்றிமுத் திக்கருத்","தொக்கநொக் குக்கணித் ...... தருள்வாயே","வற்றவட் டக்கடற் கிட்டிவட் டித்துரத்","திட்டுமட் டுப்படப் ...... பொருமாயன்","மற்றுமொப் புத்தரித்தெட்டஎட் டப்புறத்","துற்றஅத் தர்க்கருட் ...... பெருவாழ்வே","செற்றமுற் றச்சினத் திட்டுநெட் டைப்பொருப்","பெட்டைமுட் டிச்செருச் ...... செயும்வேலா","சித்தர்சித் தத்துறப் பற்றிமெத் தப்புகழ்ச்","செப்புமுத் தித்தமிழ்ப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet seems to confess going to vel-eyed women for money, losing his settled hold like a weed in a crop, and the pride of learned verse in which the inner self perishes, and asks Murugan to end that trouble, let him hold the feet and grant him the thought of release. The praise names Vishnu who dried the round sea to fight, Siva beyond reach whose great life Murugan is, the vel that struck the long hill in rage, and the Tamil of release that siddhas hold in their minds and praise.","n":"The song is set in doubled hard consonants (பற்றநெட் டைப்படைத் துட்டிருட் டை...), and most of the first half resists division into words; the reading is a guess at its outline. கவிக் கொட்டம், the arrogance of poets, would make this a confession of the poet's own pride. முத்தித் தமிழ், the Tamil of release, closes the song.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","agandhai","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:252"},"p:1261":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1261,"t":["பாதக மான யாக்கை வாதுசெய் பாவி கோத்த","பாணமும் வாளு மேற்ற ....... ; ருபார்வை","பாரப டிர மாப்ப யோதர மாதர் வாய்த்த","பாயலின் மீத ணாப்பி ...... யிதமாடுந்","தோதக மாய வார்த்தை போதக மாக நோக்கு","தூய்மையில் நாயி னேற்கும் ...... வினைதீரச்","சூழும னாதி நீத்த யானொடு தானி லாச்சு","கோதய ஞான வார்த்தை ...... யருள்வாயே","சாதன வேத நூற்பு ராதன பூண நூற்ப்ர","ஜாபதி யாண்மை தோற்க ...... வரைசாடிச்","சாகர சூர வேட்டை யாடிய வீர வேற்ப்ர","தாபம கீப போற்றி ...... யெனநேமி","மாதவன் மாது பூத்த பாகர னேக நாட்ட","வாசவ னோதி மீட்க ...... மறைநீப","மாமலர் தூவி வாழ்த்த யானையை மாலை சூட்டி","வானவர் சேனை காத்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet calls himself an impure dog who takes as instruction the deceitful words of women who cheat on their beds, with eyes sharper than Kama's arrows and swords, and asks for the word of wisdom, rising bliss without 'I' and 'mine', that will end his deeds and the beginningless bond that surrounds him. The praise shows Brahma of the Vedas and sacred thread humbled, the hill struck, Sura hunted in the sea, Indra praising and restored, and Murugan garlanding Devayanai and guarding the gods' army.","n":"தோதகமாய வார்த்தை போதகமாக நோக்கு: the poet takes the women's deceit (தோதகம்) as teaching (போதகம்), a rhyme that is the song's point. யானொடு தானிலா, without I and self, and சூழும் அநாதி, the beginningless that surrounds, point to the Saiva idea of beginningless bondage. A syllable before ருபார்வை is lost.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","agandhai","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:252"},"p:1262":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1262,"t":["பார நறுங்குழல் சோர நெகிழ்ந்துப","டீர தனம்புள ...... கிதமாகப்","பாவைய ருந்தியல் மூழ்கி நெடும்பரி","தாப முடன்பரி...... மளவாயின்","ஆரமு துண்டணை மீதி லிருந்தநு","ஆக நகம்பட ஆர முயங்கிய","ஆசை மறந்துனை ...... யுணர்வேனோ","நாரத னன்றுச காய மொழிந்திட","நாயகி பைம்புன ...... மதுதேடி","நாண மழிந்துரு மாறிய வஞ்சக","நாடியெ பங்கய ...... பதநோவ","மார சரம்பட மோக முடன்குற","வாணர் குறிஞ்சியின் ...... மிசையேபோய்","மாமுநி வன்புணர் மானுத வுந்தனி","மானை மணஞ்செய்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes lovemaking with women, hair loosening, breasts thrilled, the drinking of mouths and the nail-marks of passion on the bed, and asks whether he will forget that desire and know Murugan. The praise tells the Valli story at length: at Narada's word Murugan went seeking his lady in the millet field, lost his shame, changed his form in deceit, walked till his lotus feet ached, struck by Kama's arrows, up the hunters' hill, and married the girl born of the deer the great sage joined.","n":"The praise matches the confession: நாணம் அழிந்து, மார சரம்பட மோகமுடன், the god himself is shamed and struck by Kama for Valli. Line 274 lacks its beat marker and may have lost a word.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:253"},"p:1263":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1263,"t":["பிரமனும் விரகொடு பிணிவினை யிடர்கொடு","பேணிக் கரங்கொண் ...... டிருகாலும்","பெறநிமிர் குடிலென வுறவுயிர் புகமதி","பேதித் தளந்தம் ...... புவியூடே","வரவிட வருமுட லெரியிடை புகுதரு","வாதைத் தரங்கம் ...... பிறவாமுன்","மரகத மயில்மிசை வருமுரு கனுமென","வாழ்க்கைக் கொரன்புந் ...... தருவாயே","அருவரை தொளைபட அலைகடல் சுவறிட","ஆலிப் புடன்சென் ...... றசுரேசர்","அனைவரு மடிவுற அமர்பொரு தழகுட","னாண்மைத் தனங்கொண் ...... டெழும்வேலா","இருவினை யகலிட எழிலுமை யிடமுடை","யீசர்க் கிடுஞ்செந் ...... தமிழ்வாயா","இயல்பல கலைகொடு இசைமொழி பவரினும்","ஏழைக் கிரங்கும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"Before the waves of suffering of this body arise, the body that Brahma skilfully fashions with disease, deeds and troubles, a hut with hands and two legs into which life enters and which is sent into the world and ends in fire, the poet asks for a love for life that says 'Murugan who comes on the emerald peacock'. The praise names the vel that pierced the hill and dried the sea, the Tamil-mouthed one who gave sweet Tamil to Siva so the two deeds would leave, and the Lord who pities the poor more than those who sing with many arts.","n":"பிரமனும் விரகொடு பிணி வினை இடர் கொடு: Brahma builds the body out of disease and deeds themselves. இசை மொழிபவரினும் ஏழைக்கு இரங்கும்: the Lord prefers the helpless to the accomplished singers, a striking claim from a poet.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","karu","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:253"},"p:1264":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1264,"t":["பூதக லாதிகள் கொண்டு யோகமு மாகம கிழ்ந்து","பூசைகள் யாதுநி கழ்ந்து ...... பிழைகோடி","போம்வழி யேதுதெ ரிந்து ஆதிய நாதியி ரண்டு","பூரணி காரணி விந்து ...... வெளியான","நாதப ராபர மென்ற யோகியு லாசம றிந்து","ஞானசு வாச முணர்ந்து ...... வொளிகாண","நாடியொ ராயிரம் வந்த தாமரை மீதில மர்ந்த","நாயகர் பாதமி ரண்டு ...... மடைவேனோ","மாதுசர் வேஸ்வரிவஞ்சி காளிபி டாரிவி பஞ்சி","வாணிவ ராகிம டந்தை ...... யபிராமி","வாழ்சிவ காம சவுந்த்ரியாலமெ லாமுக பஞ்ச","வாலைபு ராரியி டந்த ...... குமையாயி","வேதபு ராணம்வி ளம்பி நீலமு ராரியர் தங்கை","மேலொடு கீழுல கங்கள் ...... தருபேதை","வேடமெ லாமுக சங்க பாடலொ டாடல்ப யின்ற","வேணியர் நாயகி தந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether, knowing the elements and kalas, rejoicing in yoga and worship and the way faults go, knowing the yogi's delight called Nada-Paraparam that came out of Bindu and the full and causal power, feeling the breath of wisdom and seeing the light, he will reach the two feet of the Lord seated on the lotus where a thousand nadis meet. The praise is a litany of the Goddess, Sarvesvari, Kali, Pidari, Vani, Varahi, Abhirami, Sivakami, Uma, sister of Vishnu, mother of the worlds, consort of the matted-haired dancer, who bore Murugan.","n":"நாடி ஒராயிரம் வந்த தாமரை is the thousand-petalled lotus at the crown, where the Lord's feet are to be reached. The sequence பூரணி காரணி விந்து வெளியான நாத பராபரம் sets Shakti, Bindu and Nada in the yogic order, and the praise turns entirely to the Goddess. How the opening (பூதகலாதிகள்) divides is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yogam","naadham","vindhu","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:253"},"p:1265":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1265,"t":["பெருங்கா ரியம்போல் வருங்கே டுடம்பால்","ப்ரியங்கூர வந்து ...... கருவூறிப்","பிறந்தார் கிடந்தா ரிருந்தார் தவழ்ந்தார்","நடந்தார் தளர்ந்து ...... பிணமானார்","அருங்கான் மருங்கே யெடுங்கோள் சுடுங்கோள்","அலங்கார நன்றி ...... தேன்முழ்கி","அகன்றா சையும்போய் விழும்பா ழுடம்பால்","அலந்தேனை யஞ்ச ...... லெனவேணும்","இருங்கா னகம்போ யிளங்கா ளைபின்போ","கவெங்கே மடந்தை ...... யெனவேகி","எழுந்தே குரங்கா லிலங்கா புரந்தீ","யிடுங்கா வலன்றன் ...... மருகோனே","பொருங்கார் முகம்பா ணிகொண்டே யிறைஞ்சார்","புறஞ்சாய அம்பு ...... தொடும்வேடர்","புனங்கா வலங்கோ தைபங்கா வபங்கா","புகழ்ந்தோது மண்டர் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet traces the body: they came in love, formed in the womb, were born, lay, sat, crawled, walked, weakened and became corpses, carried to the burning forest and burned; and he, worn out by this ruined body that falls when desire goes, asks Murugan to say 'fear not'. The praise tells of Rama going into the forest with his younger brother, crying 'where is my lady?', and of the monkey that burned Lanka, names Murugan his nephew, and Valli's partner whom the gods praise.","n":"பிறந்தார் கிடந்தார் இருந்தார் தவழ்ந்தார் நடந்தார் தளர்ந்து பிணமானார்: a life told as a list of verbs, in the plural of what others did. The plea asks only for reassurance (அஞ்சல்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","yaakkai","arul"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:253"},"p:1266":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1266,"t":["மக்கட் பிறப்புக்கு ளொக்கப் பிறப்புற்ற","மட்டுற்ற சுற்றத்தர் ...... மனையாளும்","மத்யத் தலத்துற்று நித்தப் பிணக்கிட்டு","வைத்துப் பொருட்பற்று ...... மிகநாட","நிக்ரித் திடுத்துட்டன் மட்டித் துயிர்பற்ற","நெட்டைக் கயிற்றிட்டு ...... வளையாமுன்","நெக்குக் குருப்பத்தி மிக்குக் கழற்செப்ப","நிற்றத் துவச்சொற்க ...... ளருள்வாயே","திக்கப் புறத்துக்குள் நிற்கப் புகழ்ப்பித்த","சித்ரத் தமிழ்க்கொற்ற ...... முடையோனே","சிப்பக் குடிற்கட்டு மற்பக் குறத்திச்சொல்","தித்திப்பை யிச்சிக்கு ...... மணவாளா","முக்கட் சடைச்சித்த ருட்புக் கிருக்கைக்கு","முத்தித் துவக்குற்று ...... மொழிவோனே","முட்டச் சினத்திட்டு முற்பட் டிணர்க்கொக்கை","முட்டித் தொளைத்திட்ட ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"Before Death, the wicked punisher, throws his long rope and seizes his life while brothers born with him, kin and wife gather in the house quarrelling daily and his attachment to wealth grows, the poet asks to melt with devotion to the guru and be given words of Murugan's truth to speak of his feet. The praise names him the one whose victory in beautiful Tamil is praised beyond the directions, Valli's bridegroom who loves the sweetness of her words in the hut, the teacher who gave the three-eyed Siva the beginning of release, and the piercer of Krauncha.","n":"The household is pictured as a daily quarrel over property (நித்தப் பிணக்கிட்டு ... பொருட்பற்று). நிற் தத்துவச் சொற்கள்: the plea is for doctrine as speech. குருப் பத்தி may be devotion to Murugan as guru or to a human teacher.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","sutram","pattru","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:253"},"p:1267":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1267,"t":["மக்கள்தா யர்க்குமரு கர்க்குமா மர்க்குமனை","விக்கும்வாழ் நர்க்குமிக ...... மனதூடே","மைத்தவே லைக்குநெடி துற்றமா யத்துயரம்","வைத்துவா டச்சமனு ...... முறமேவித்","திக்குநா டிக்கரிய மெய்க்கடா விற்றிருகி","திக்கஆ விக்களவு ...... தெரியாமுன்","சித்தமோ வித்துயிலு மற்றுவா ழச்சிறிது","சித்ரபா தக்கமல ...... மருள்வாயே","இக்குவே ளைக்கருக முக்கணா டிக்கனலை","யிட்டுயோ கத்தமரு ...... மிறையோர்முன்","எச்சரா திக்குமுற நிற்குமா யற்குமுத","லெட்டொணா வித்தைதனை ...... யினிதீவாய்","பக்கஆர் வத்துடனுள் நெக்குநா டிப்பரவு","பத்தர்பா டற்குருகு ...... முருகோனே","பக்கம் யானைத்திருவொ டொக்கவா ழக்குறவர்","பச்சைமா னுக்கினிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"Before Yama comes on his black buffalo seeking him and twists out his life, while the great illusory grief, deeper than the sea, for children, mothers, in-laws, wife and dependents withers him, the poet asks for a little of Murugan's lotus feet so that he may live with his mind stilled. The praise recalls Siva burning Kama with his third eye before the gods in yoga, and names Murugan the one who gave unattainable knowledge to Vishnu, who melts at the songs of devotees and is sweet to Devayanai and Valli.","n":"மாயத் துயரம்: the grief for kin is called illusory, and the list of relatives is the burden. சித்தம் ஓவித் துயிலும் அற்று: the stilling of mind and end of sleep is the life asked for. Who receives the 'knowledge beyond reach' (எட்டொணா வித்தை) is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kootru","sutram","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:253"},"p:1268":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1268,"t":["மதனிக் கதுகொடு பதுமப் புதுமலர்","மலையப் படவிடு ...... வலியாலே","வனமுற் றினவளை யினநித் திலமலை","வலையத் துகள்வளை ...... கடலாலே","விதனப் படுமதி வதனக் கொடியற","வெருவிப் பரிமள ...... அணைமீதே","மெலியக் கலைதலை குலையத் தகுமினி","விரையக் குரவலர் ...... தரவேணும்","புதனைச் சதுமுக விதியச் சுதனெதிர்","புனைவித் தவர்தொழு ...... கழல்வீரா","பொருகைச் சரிவரிபெருகச் செறிவுறு","புனமெய்க் குறமகள் ...... மணவாளா","முதுநற் சரவண மதனிற் சததள","முளரிப் பதிதனி ...... லுறைவோனே","முதுமைக் கடலட ரசுரப் படைகெட","முடுகிப் பொரவல ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"In the voice of someone watching a love-sick girl, the singer says that the moon-faced creeper, wounded by Kama's fresh lotus arrow and the pearl-and-shell sea, frightened on her fragrant bed, her clothes and hair in disorder, is wasting away, and asks the lover to send his kura blossoms quickly. The praise names Murugan whose feet are worshipped by Brahma and Vishnu, Valli's bridegroom, dweller in the lotus of Saravana pool, who fought the asura armies.","n":"This is akam poetry, not the poet's confession; the speaker is likely the mother or friend. The request for the hero's flowers (குரவு அலர்) matches the garland asked for in 1254. புதனை in the praise is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","melivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:253"},"p:1269":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1269,"t":["மதிதனை யிலாத பாவி குருநெற யிலாத கோபி","மனநிலை நிலாத பேயன் ...... அவமாயை","வகையது விடாத பேடி தவநினை விலாத மோடி","வரும்வகை யிதேது காய ...... மெனநாடும்","விதியிலி பொலாத லோபி சபைதனில் வராத கோழை","வினையிகல் விடாத கூள ...... னெனைநீயும்","மிகுபர மதான ஞான நெறிதனை விசார மாக","மிகுமுன \u0004ருப தான ...... மருள்வாயே","எதிர்வரு முதார சூர னிருபிள வதாக வேலை","யியலொடு கடாவு தீர ...... குமரேசா","இனியசொல் மறாத சீலர் கருவழி வராமல் நாளும்","இளமையது தானு மாக ...... நினைவோனே","நதியுட னராவு பூணு பரமர்குரு நாத னான","நடைபெறு க்டுர மான ...... மயில்வீரா","நகைமுக விநோத ஞான குறமினுட னேகு லாவு","நவமணி யுலாவு மார்ப ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet calls himself a chain of names, senseless sinner, angry man without a guru's path, ghost whose mind won't settle, coward who will not leave maya, idler who never thinks of penance, miser, one who shuns the assembly, wretch who will not leave the quarrel of deeds, and asks to be given the path of supreme wisdom to reflect on. The praise names the Kumara whose vel split Sura in two, who keeps sweet-spoken virtuous people from the womb and ever young, the guru of Siva who wears the river and serpent, and Valli's lover.","n":"The self-abuse is the song's whole first half: each epithet ends in a noun of contempt (பாவி, கோபி, பேயன், பேடி, மோடி, லோபி, கோழை, கூளன்). இனிய சொல் மறாத சீலர் கருவழி வராமல்: those who keep sweet speech are spared rebirth. மிகுமுனருபதானம் and நடைபெறு க்டுர are garbled.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kutram","gnaanam","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:253"},"p:1270":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1270,"t":["மலந்தோற் சலந்தேற் றெலும்பாற் கலந்தீட்","டிடுங்கூட் டினிற்றங் ...... கிடுமாய","மயங்காத் தியங்காப் பயங்கோட் டிடுங்காற்","றுடன்போக் குறத்தந் ...... தையுமாதும்","குலந்தாய்க் குடம்பாற் பிறந்தேற் றிடுங்கோத்","தடங்கூப் பிடத்தம் ...... புவியாவும்","குலைந்தார்ப் பெழுங்காட் டிலந்தாட் களன்பாற்","குணங்காத் துனைக்கும் ...... பிடஆளாய்","தலந்தாட் டொடண்டாத் தளைந்தார்க் கிளங்காத்","தடந்தாட் புடைத்தன் ...... பினர்வாழத்","தருங்கூத் தரும்பார்த் துகந்தேத் திடஞ்சாத்","திரஞ்சாற் றிநிற்கும் ...... பெருவாழ்வே","அலைந்தாற் றெழுங்கோச் சலந்தீக் கலந்தாட்","டரம்போச் செனக்கன் ...... றிடும்வேலா","அறங்காத் துறங்காத் திறம்பார்த் திருந்தோர்க்","கயர்ந்தோர்க் களிக்கும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"Before the breath leaves this cage made of filth, skin, water and bone and father, wife, mother and kin cry out at the burning ground, the poet asks to become Murugan's servant who worships him. The praise names Murugan the great life who proclaims the sastras while Siva the dancer watches and praises, the vel that struck Sura in the sea, and the Lord who gives to those who guard dharma without sleeping.","n":"The song is set in doubled hard syllables (மலந்தோற் சலந்தேற் றெலும்பாற்), and the body as கூடு of filth and bone is clear while much else is a guess. Murugan teaching sastra with Siva as delighted listener repeats the teacher-of-Siva theme.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","nilaiyaamai","thondu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:253"},"p:1271":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1271,"t":["மனநூறு கோடி துன்ப நொடிமீதி லேநி னைந்து","மதனூட லேமு யங்கி ...... யதீருப","மடமாத ராசை கொண்டு புவிமீதி லேம யங்கி","மதிசீரெ லாம ழிந்து ...... கொடிதான","விநன்முடி யேதி ரிந்து புவிமீதி லேயு ழன்று","விரகான்மெ யேத ளர்ந்து ...... விடுநாளில்","விசையான தோகை துங்க மயிலேறி யோடி வந்து","வெளிஞான வீடு தந்து ...... அருள்வாயே","தினைவேடர் காவல் தங்கு மலைகாடெ லாமு ழன்று","சிறுபேதை கால்ப ணிந்த ...... குமரேசா","திரையாழி சேது கண்டு பொருராவ ணேசை வென்ற","திருமால்மு ராரிதங்கை ...... யருள்பாலா","முனிவோர்கள் தேவ ரும்பர் சிறையாக வேவ ளைந்த","மதுசூரர் தானை தங்கள் ...... கிளையோடு","முடிகோடி தூளெ ழுந்து கழுகோடு பாற ருந்த","முனைவேலி னாலெ றிந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses a mind that thinks a hundred crore sorrows in an instant, embracing in Kama's sulks, desire for women that deluded him and wasted his sense, and a body weakened by lust, and asks that on the day it falls Murugan come running on his peacock and give the house of open wisdom. The praise names him the Kumara who wandered the hunters' hills and bowed at the girl's feet, nephew of Vishnu who as Rama built the bridge and defeated Ravana, and the vel that crushed the demons who imprisoned sages and gods, leaving them to vultures.","n":"வெளி ஞான வீடு, the house of open (spacious) wisdom, is the release asked for. சிறுபேதை கால் பணிந்த: Murugan bowing at Valli's feet. விநன்முடி in line 3 is garbled.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","mayal","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:253"},"p:1272":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1272,"t":["மாதர்மயல் தனிற்கலந்து காமபனி யெனப்புகுந்து","மாடவிய லெனச்சுழன்று ...... கருவூறி","மாறிபல வெனச்சுமந்து தேனுகுட மெனத்திரண்டு","மாதமிது வெனத்தளர்ந்து ...... வெளியாகி","வேதபுவி தனிற்கழன்று ஏனமென வெனத்தவழ்ந்து","வீறுமணி களைப்புனைந்து ...... நடைமேலாய்","வேணவித மெனத்திரிந்து நாறுபுழு குடற்றிமிர்ந்து","வேசிவலை தனிற்கலந்து ...... மடிவேனோ","ஆதிசர ணெனக்கயங்கு லாவமுத லையைக்கிடங்கி","லாரவுடல் தனைப்பிளந்த ...... அரிநேமி","ஆமைகய லெனச்செயங்கொள் கோலகுற ளரித்தடங்கை","யானஅர வணைச்சயந்தன் ...... மருகோனே","சோதியுரு வெனத்திரண்டு கோலஅரு ணையிற்கலந்த","சோமனணி குடிற்சிலம்ப ...... னருள்பாலா","தோகைமயி லெனச்சிறந்த ரூபிகுற மகட்கிரங்கி","தோள்களிறு கிடப்புணர்ந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet traces his life from conception in passion through the womb, the months and birth, to crawling, wearing jewels, walking, wandering as he pleased, smearing on stinking civet and mixing in the harlot's net, and asks whether he must die so. The praise tells of Vishnu splitting the crocodile when the elephant cried 'First one, refuge!', names his tortoise, fish, boar, dwarf and man-lion forms, and Siva as the column of light at Arunai, and ends with Murugan embracing Valli.","n":"The life comes full circle: it begins in மாதர் மயல் and ends in வேசி வலை. The images of gestation (தேனுகுடம் எனத் திரண்டு, a gathering like a honey pot) are hard to read exactly. The Gajendra story and the avatars are named outright.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["karu","piravi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:253"},"p:1273":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1273,"t":["முத்துமணி யாரமொய்த் திட்டஇரு கோடுமுற்","பட்டகரிபோலுமத் ...... தனமாதர்","முற்றுமதி யார்முகத் துற்றமுனை வேலுறப்","பட்டுமுகில் போல்மனத் ...... திருள்மூடிச்","சுத்தமதி போய்வினைத் துட்டனவ னாய்மனத்","துக்கமுற வேமிகச் ...... சுழலாதே","சொற்கள்பல நாவினிற் றொட்டுனிரு தாடொழச்","சொற்கமல வாழ்வுசற் ...... றருள்வாயே","கொத்துமுடி யானபத் தற்றுவிழ வேகுறிப்","புற்றஅதி கோபனச் ...... சுதன்மாயன்","கொற்றமரு காகுறக் கொச்சைமற மாதினுக்","கிச்சைமொழி கூறுநற் ...... குமரேசா","பத்தியுட னேநினைத் தெத்துமடி யார்வினைப்","பற்றுவிடு மாமறைப் ...... பொருளானாய்","பத்திவர ஞானசொற் கற்றவர்கள் பாடுநற்","பக்ஷபத தேவர்மெய்ப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women whose pearl-hung breasts are like an elephant's tusks and whose spear eyes wound, so that his mind is clouded, his pure sense gone and he whirls in grief, and asks instead to worship the two feet with many words on his tongue and be given a little of the life of the lotus. The praise names Vishnu who in wrath felled the ten heads, Murugan's courtship of Valli, the meaning of the Veda that frees devotees from deeds, and the Lord praised by those learned in devotion and wisdom.","n":"சொற்கமல வாழ்வு may mean the life of the lotus feet won by words, or a 'lotus of speech'; the plea is again for praise as the way.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kan","thiruvadi","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:253"},"p:1274":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1274,"t":["முருகம யூரச் சேவக சரவண ஏனற் பூதரி","முகுளப டீரக் கோமள ...... முலைமீதே","முழுகிய காதற் காமுக பதிபசு பாசத் தீர்வினை","முதியபு ராரிக் கோதிய ...... குருவேயென்","றுருகியு மாடிப் பாடியு மிருகழல் நாடிச் சூடியு","முணர்வினோ டூடிக் கூடியும் ...... வழிபாடுற்","றுலகினொ ராசைப் பாடற நிலைபெறு ஞானத் தாலினி","யுனதடி யாரைச் சேர்வது ...... மொருநாளே","மருகனெ னாமற் சூழ்கொலை கருதிய மாமப் பாதகன்","வரவிடு மாயப் பேய்முலை ...... பருகாமேல்","வருமத யானைக் கோடவை திருகிவி ளாவிற் காய்கனி","மதுகையில் வீழச் சாடிய ...... சதமாபுட்","பொருதிரு கோரப் பாரிய மருதிடை போயப் போதொரு","சகடுதை யாமற் போர்செய்து ...... விளையாடிப்","பொதுவியர் சேரிக் கேவளர் புயல்மரு காவஜ் ராயுத","புரமதில் மாபுத் தேளிர்கள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"Calling Murugan peacock warrior, Saravana, lover immersed in Valli's breasts, and guru who taught old Siva the end of the bonds of Lord, soul and bond, melting, dancing, singing, seeking and wearing his feet, the poet asks whether a day will come when, with worldly desire gone, he joins Murugan's devotees through steady wisdom. The praise is a Krishna catalogue: the demoness's breast drunk, the elephant's tusks wrenched, the wood-apple demon struck, the bird, the twin marutha trees and the cart, the child reared in the cowherds' settlement, and names Murugan his nephew.","n":"பதி பசு பாசத் தீர்வினை: the Saiva Siddhanta triad of pati, pasu and pasa is the teaching Murugan gives Siva here. உணர்வினோடு ஊடிக் கூடியும், sulking and reuniting, brings the lover's quarrel into worship. The goal is the company of devotees, not release alone.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adiyaar","guru","paasam","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:253"},"p:1275":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1275,"t":["மூலா நிலமதின் மேலே மனதுறு","மோகா டவிசுடர் ...... தனைநாடி","மோனா நிலைதனை நானா வகையிலு","மோதா நெறிமுறை ...... முதல்கூறும்","லீலா விதமுன தாலே கதிபெற","நேமா ரகசிய ...... வுபதேசம்","நநடு ழிதனிலை வாடா மணியொளி","நீதா பலமது ...... தருவாயே","நாலா ருசியமு தாலே திருமறை","நாலா யதுசெப ...... மணிமாலை","நாடாய் தவரிடர் கேடா வரிகரி","நாரா யணர்திரு ...... மருகோனே","சூலா திபர்சிவ ஞானார் யமனுதை","காலார் தரவரு ...... குருநாதா","தோதீ திகுதிகு தீதீ செகசெக","சோதீ நடமிடு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks for the light sought above the root centre in the forest of the mind's delusion, the state of silence, the secret instruction by which Murugan's play teaches a way not given in the many paths, and the steady, unfading gem-light in the middle channel. The praise names him Vishnu's nephew, guru to Siva the lord of the trident whose foot kicked Yama, and the light dancing to drum syllables.","n":"மூலா நிலமதின் மேலே and நடு வழி point to the muladhara and the central channel of yoga; வாடா மணி ஒளி is the light at its end. ரகசிய உபதேசம், secret instruction, makes Murugan the yogi's guru. நநடு doubles a syllable, and நாலா ருசி அமுது is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aadhaaram","yogam","mounam","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:253"},"p:1276":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1276,"t":["வரிபரந் திரண்டு நயனமுஞ் சிவந்து","வதனமண் டலங்கள் ...... குறுவேர்வாய்","மணிசிலம் பலம்ப அளகமுங் குலைந்து","வசமழிந் திழிந்து ...... மயல்கூர","இருதனங் குலுங்க இடைதுவண் டனுங்க","இனியதொண் டையுண்டு ...... மடவார்தோள்","இதமுடன் புணர்ந்து மதிமயங் கினும்பொ","னிலகுநின் பதங்கள் ...... மறவேனே","விரிபரந் தியங்கு முததியுங் கலங்க","விடமினும் பிறந்த ...... தெனவானோர்","வெருவிநெஞ் சமஞ்சி யுரனொடுந் தயங்கி","விரைபதம் பணிந்து ...... முறையோவென்","றுரைமறந் துணங்க அயில்தொடும் ப்ரசண்ட","உ யர்தலங் குலுங்க ...... வருதோகை","ஒருபெருஞ் சிகண்டி மயிலமர்ந் திலங்கி","உ லகமும் புரந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes lovemaking frankly, reddened eyes, sweat, ringing anklets, loosened hair, shaking breasts and bending waist, and says that even if he embraces women and his mind is deluded, he will not forget Murugan's shining feet. The praise recalls the churning of the sea when poison rose and the gods begged in fear, and Murugan on the great peacock that shook the heights and protected the world.","n":"மதிமயங்கினும் ... நின் பதங்கள் மறவேனே: unlike most songs, this one does not ask to be freed from women but vows remembrance in the midst of them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","thiruvadi","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:253"},"p:1277":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1277,"t":["வரிவிழி பூச லாட இருகுழை யூச லாட","வளர்முலை தானு மாட ...... வளையாட","மணிவட மாலை யாட முருகவி ழோதி யாட","மதுரமு தூறி வீழ ...... அநுராகம்","இருவரு மேக போக மொருவர்த மாக மாக","இதமொடு கூடி மாயை ...... படுபோதும்","இருகர மாறு மாறு மறுமுக நீப மார்பு","மிருகழல் தானு நானு ...... மறவேனே","திருநட மாடு காளி பயிரவி மோடி சூலி","திரிபுர நீற தாக ...... அனல்மோதுஞ்","சிவைகயி லாச வாசி மலைமகள் நாரிபாரி","திருமுலை யாயி தாயி ...... யருள்பாலா","குருபர நாத னாகி யரனொரு காதி லோது","குணநிதி யாசை நேச ...... முருகோனே","குறமக ளார பார முகிழ்முலை மீது தாது","குலவிய மாலை மேவு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes lovemaking in full motion, eyes battling, earrings, breasts, bangles and garlands swaying, honey dripping from the flowered hair, two becoming one body in maya, and vows that even then he will not forget Murugan's twelve arms, six faces, kadamba-garlanded chest and two feet. The praise calls him the child of grace of the dancing Kali, Bhairavi and trident-bearer who burned Tripura, Sivai of Kailasa, daughter of the mountain, and the guru who spoke into Siva's ear, whose garland rests on the Kuratti's breasts.","n":"Unusually the plea does not ask to escape the embrace: மாயை படுபோதும் ... மறவேனே, 'even when I fall into maya I will not forget', makes remembrance of the Lord hold within the act itself. அரன் ஒரு காதில் ஓது: Murugan teaching Siva in the ear, Om not named. The goddess is given a run of fierce names (காளி, பயிரவி, மோடி, சூலி) before the gentle ones.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","thiruvadi","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:254"},"p:1278":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1278,"t":["விழையுமனி தரையுமுநி வரையுமவ ருயிர் துணிய","வெட்டிப் பிளந்துளம் பிட்டுப் பறிந்திடுஞ் ...... செங்கண்வேலும்","விரையளக முகிலுமிள நகையும்ருக மதகனவி","சித்ரத் தனங்களுந் தித்தித்த தொண்டையும் ...... புண்டாணகச்","சுழிமடுவு மிடையுமழ கியமகளிர் தருகலவி","சுட்டித் திரிந்திஙன் தட்டுப் படுங்கொடும் ...... பங்கவாழ்வுந்","தொலைவில்பிற வியுமகல வொருமவுன பரமசுக","சுத்தப் பெரும்பதஞ் சித்திக்க அன்புடன் ...... சிந்தியாதோ","எழுதரிய அறுமுகமு மணிநுதலும் வயிரமிடை","யிட்டுச் சமைந்தசெஞ் சுட்டிக் கலன்களுந் ...... துங்கநீள்பன்","னிருகருணை விழிமலரு மிலகுபதி னிருகுழையும்","ரத்நக் குதம்பையும் பத்மக் கரங்களுஞ் ...... செம்பொனூலும்","மொழிபுகழு முடைமணியு மரைவடமு மடியிணையு","முத்தச் சதங்கையுஞ் சித்ரச் சிகண்டியுஞ் ...... செங்கைவேலும்","முழுதுமழ கியகுமர கிரிகுமரியுடனுருகு","முக்கட் சிவன்பெருஞ் சற்புத்ர வும்பர்தந் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The poet lists women's red spear-eyes that cut the lives of men and sages, their fragrant hair, smiles, musk-painted breasts, sweet lips, navel and waist, and asks whether Murugan will not lovingly think to grant, beyond that union, the cruel miry life and endless births, the great pure state of silent supreme bliss. The praise is a head-to-foot picture of Murugan: six faces, jewelled forehead ornament, twelve eyes of compassion and twelve earrings, lotus hands, sacred thread, waist-bells, anklets, peacock and vel, the beautiful Kumara, son of three-eyed Siva who melts with the mountain's daughter.","n":"The song sets two catalogues against each other: the woman's body part by part in the plea, and Murugan's form part by part in the praise, so that beauty is redirected rather than renounced. ஒரு மவுன பரமசுக சுத்தப் பெரும்பதம் names the goal as silence and bliss. புண்டாணகச் சுழிமடு is hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","mounam","piravi","azhagu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:254"},"p:1279":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1279,"t":["வீணையிசை கோட்டி யாலமிட று஡ட்டு","வீரமுனை யீட்டி ...... விழியார்தம்","வேதனையில் நாட்ட மாகியிடர் பாட்டில்","வீழுமயல் தீட்டி ...... யுழலாதே","ஆணியுள வீட்டை மேவியுள மாட்டை","யாவலுட னீட்டி ...... யழியாதே","ஆவியுறை கூட்டில் ஞானமறை யூட்டி","யானநிலை காட்டி ...... யருள்வாயே","கேணியுற வேட்ட ஞானநெறி வேட்டர்","கேள்சுறுதி நாட்டி ...... லுறைவோனே","கீதவிசை கூட்டி வேதமொழி சூட்டு","கீரரியல் கேட்ட ...... க்ருபைவேளே","சேணினுயர் காட்டில் வாழுமற வாட்டி","சீதவிரு கோட்டி ...... லணைவோனே","சீறவுணர் நாட்டி லாரவழல் மூட்டி","தேவர்சிறை மீட்ட ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks not to wander sharpening infatuation, caught in the torment of women whose voices are like the vina and whose eyes are poison and spear-points, nor to perish greedily piling up house and wealth, but that Murugan feed the scripture of knowledge into the nest where life dwells and show him the true state. The praise calls him the one who dwells in the land of the Veda, the gracious Vel who heard Nakkirar's composition set to song, who embraces the hunter girl of the high forest, and who set fire to the demons' land and freed the gods from prison.","n":"ஆவியுறை கூட்டில் ஞானமறை ஊட்டி: the body is the nest of the life, to be fed with knowledge rather than with women and wealth. கீரர் இயல் கேட்ட names Nakkirar, the poet of the Tirumurugatruppadai, as one whose poem Murugan received. The line on hunters of the knowledge path who sought a tank (கேணியுற வேட்ட ஞானநெறி வேட்டர்) is obscure, and ஆணியுள வீட்டை is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["mayal","pattru","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:254"},"p:1280":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1280,"t":["வேலைவா ளைக்கொடிய ஆலகா லத்தைமதன்","வீசுபா ணத்தைநிக ...... ரெனலாகும்","வேதைசா தித்தவிழி மாதரா பத்தில்விளை","யாடிமோ கித்திரியும் ...... வெருப","கோலகா லத்தைவிட லாகிமா றக்குணவி","காரமோ டத்தெளிய ...... அரிதான","கூறொணா தற்பரம ஞான்ரு பத்தின்வழி","கூடலா கப்பெருமை ...... தருவாயே","வாலிமார் பைத்துணிய ஏழ்மரா இற்றுவிழ","வாளிபோ டக்கருது ...... மநுராமன்","வானுலோ கத்திலம ரேசனோ லிக்கவளை","யூதிமோ கித்துவிழ ...... அருள்கூரும்","நீலமே னிக்குமரு காவுதா ரத்துவரு","நீசர்வாழ் வைக்களையு ...... மிளையோனே","நேசமா கக்குறவர் தோகைமா னைப்புணரு","நீபதோ ளொப்பரிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet, wandering infatuated in the danger of women whose tormenting eyes equal the sea, the sword, the halahala poison and Kama's arrows, asks to leave that tumult, have the distortion of the gunas run off and grow clear, and be given the greatness of joining the path of the unspeakable supreme form of knowledge. The praise names Rama who loosed the arrow that split Vali's chest and the seven sal trees, the blue-bodied Vishnu who blew the conch, Murugan as his nephew who uproots the base, and the lord who unites with the hunters' peacock-like girl.","n":"குணவிகாரம் ஓட: the disturbance of the qualities must run away before clarity (தெளிய) comes, and the goal is கூறொணா தற்பரம ஞானரூபம், the supreme knowledge-form that cannot be spoken. The conch episode (அமரேசன் ஒலிக்க வளை ஊதி மோகித்து விழ) is not clear to me, nor வெருப at the end of line 2.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kan","mayal","mukkunam","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:254"},"p:1281":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1281,"t":["இத்தரணி மீதிற் ...... பிறவாதே","எத்தரொடு கூடிக் ...... கலவாதே","முத்தமிழை யோதித் ...... தளராதே","முத்தியடி யேனுக் ...... கருள்வாயே","தத்துவமெய்ஞ் ஞானக் ...... குருநாதா","சத்தசொரு பாபுத் ...... தமுதோனே","நித்தியக்ரு தாநற் ...... பெருவாழ்வே","நிர்த்தஜெக ஜோதிப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks not to be born on this earth, not to mix with cheats, not to grow weary reciting the three Tamils, and to be given release. The praise calls Murugan the guru of true knowledge of the tattvas, the one whose form is sound, the eternal good life, and the light of the world who dances.","n":"முத்தமிழை ஓதித் தளராதே seems to set even the recitation of Tamil among the things that wear one out, perhaps poems recited for patrons as in 1284; the plain sense is uncertain. சத்த சொரூபா: Murugan as sound itself.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["piravi","veedu","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:254"},"p:1282":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1282,"t":["என்பந்த வினைத்தொடர் போக்கிவி ...... சையமாகி","இன்பந்தனை யுற்றும காப்ரிய ...... மதுவாகி","அன்புந்திய பொற்கிணி பாற்கட ...... லமுதான","அந்தந்தனி லிச்சைகொ ளாற்பத ...... மருள்வாயே","முன்புந்தி நினைத்துரு வாற்சிறு ...... வடிவாகி","முன்திந்தி யெனப்பர தாத்துட ...... னடமாடித்","தம்பந்த மறத்தவ நோற்பவர் ...... குறைதீரச்","சம்பந்த னெனத்தமிழ் தேக்கிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks that the chain of his binding deeds be removed and that he be given the state that, triumphant and in bliss, overflowing with love like the nectar of the milk ocean, desires that end. The praise says Murugan, having resolved it in mind, took a small form and danced the bharata to the beat, and as Sambandar filled the land with Tamil so that those who do penance to end their bonds had their want removed.","n":"சம்பந்தன் எனத் தமிழ் தேக்கிய: Murugan identified with the child-saint Sambandar, whose small form (சிறுவடிவு) and dancing are those of the boy. The second half of the plea (அந்தந்தனில் இச்சைகொள் ஆற்பதம்) is hard to construe.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vinai","aanandham","thamizh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:254"},"p:1283":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1283,"t":["கருப்பற் று஡றிப் ...... பிறவாதே","கனக்கப் பாடுற் ...... றுழலாதே","திருப்பொற் பாதத் ...... தநுபூதி","சிறக்கப் பாலித் ...... தருள்வாயே","பரப்பற் றாருக் ...... குரியோனே","பரத்தப் பாலுக் ...... கணியோனே","திருக்கைச் சேவற் ...... கொடியோனே","செகத்திற் சோதிப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks not to be born again oozing out of a womb, not to wander in heavy suffering, and that Murugan protect him so that the experience of his holy golden feet may flourish. The praise calls him the one who belongs to those free of attachment, the ornament of the beyond, holder of the cock banner, the light of the world.","n":"அநுபூதி, direct experience of the feet, is the goal, a word that recurs in 1293 and 1294. பரப்பற்றாருக்கு உரியோனே: he belongs to those who have cut their clinging. The glyph ஡ in line 1 stands for ஊ (கருப்பற்றூறி).","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["piravi","thiruvadi","pattru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:254"},"p:1284":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1284,"t":["கருப்பை யிற்சுக் கிலத் துலைத்துற் பவித்து ...... மறுகாதே","கபட்ட சட்டர்க் கிதத்த சித்ரத் தமிழ்க்க ...... ளுரையாதே","விருப்ப முற்றுத் துதித்தெ னைப்பற் றெனக்க ...... ருதுநீயே","வெளிப்ப டப்பற் றிடப்ப டுத்தத் தருக்கி ...... மகிழ்வோனே","பருப்ப தத்தைத் தொளைத்த சத்திப் படைச்ச ...... மரவேளே","பணிக்கு லத்தைக் கவர்ப்ப தத்துக் களித்த ...... மயிலோனே","செருப்பு றத்துச் சினத்தை முற்றப் பரப்பு ...... மிசையோனே","தினைப்பு னத்துக் குறத்தி யைக்கைப் பிடித்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks not to be churned about, born from seed in the womb, and not to recite colourful Tamil poems that please deceitful scoundrels; he says Murugan regards him as one who praised him longing to be held. The praise names the Vel whose Sakti weapon pierced the mountain, the peacock delighting in seizing serpents, the one whose fame spreads in battle, and the lord who took the Kuratti's hand in the millet field.","n":"கபட்ட சட்டர்க்கு இதத்த சித்ரத் தமிழ்கள் உரையாதே: the refusal to spend Tamil on flattering false patrons, so that praise is kept for the Lord. The line வெளிப்படப் பற்றிடப்படுத்தத் தருக்கி மகிழ்வோனே is obscure and my reading of the plea's second half is tentative.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["karu","pugazh","thamizh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:254"},"p:1285":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1285,"t":["கொடியமத வேள்கைக் ...... கணையாலே","குரைகணெடு நீலக் ...... கடலாலே","நெடியபுகழ் சோலைக் ...... குயிலாலே","நிலைமைகெடு மானைத் ...... தழுவாயே","கடியரவு பூணர்க் ...... கினியோனே","கலைகள்தெரிமாமெய்ப் ...... புலவோனே","அடியவர்கள் நேசத் ...... துறைவேலா","அறுமுகவி நோதப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"An akam plea in the voice of the girl's mother or friend: tormented by the arrows of cruel Kama, the roaring blue sea and the cuckoo of the grove, the deer-like girl has lost her composure, so let Murugan embrace her. The praise calls him dear to Siva who wears the serpent, the learned poet who knows the arts, Vela, harbour of his devotees' love, the six-faced lord.","n":"நிலைமை கெடு மானைத் தழுவாயே: the plea is not for release but for the god's embrace of the lovesick girl. The sea's roar and the cuckoo's call are the classical tormentors of separation. Nearly the same song stands at 1291.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:254"},"p:1286":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1286,"t":["கோடான மேருமலைத் ...... தனமானார்","கோமாள மாவலைக் ...... குழலாதே","நாடோ று மேன்மைபடைத் ...... திடவேதான்","நாயேனை யாளநினைத் ...... திடொணாதோ","ஈடேற ஞானமுரைத் ...... தருள்வோனே","ஈராறு தோள்கள்படைத் ...... திடுவோனே","மாடேறு மீசர்தமக் ...... கினியோனே","மாதானை யாறுமுகப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks not to wander in the great net of women with Meru-like breasts, and whether Murugan cannot think to rule this dog so that he may rise each day. The praise names the one who taught knowledge so that souls may be saved, who bears twelve shoulders, who is dear to Siva who rides the bull, and the six-faced lord of the elephant-maiden.","n":"நாயேனை ஆள: the plea is to be taken as servant. மாதானை may be மாது ஆனை, Devayanai, or a great army; I take the first.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","thondu","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:254"},"p:1287":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1287,"t":["சமய பத்தி வ்ருதாத்தனை ...... நினையாதே","சரண பத்ம சிவார்ச்சனை ...... தனைநாடி","அமைய சற்குரு சாத்திர ...... மொழிநூலால்","அருளெ னக்கினி மேற்றுனை ...... தருவாயே","உ மைமுலைத்தரு பாற்கொடு ...... அருள்கூறி","உ ரிய மெய்த்தவ மாக்கிந ...... லுபதேசத்","தமிழ்த னைக்கரை காட்டிய ...... திறலோனே","சமண ரைக்கழு வேற்றிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks to leave the vain devotion of the sects and, seeking the worship of Siva at the lotus feet, to be given grace through the true guru's scriptures and teaching. The praise names Murugan as the one who drank the milk of Uma's breast and spoke grace, made true penance his own, showed the farther shore of Tamil teaching, and impaled the Jains, that is, as Sambandar.","n":"சமய பத்தி வ்ருதா: devotion within the sects is vain, but Siva-worship (சிவார்ச்சனை) is not counted a sect. உமைமுலைத் தரு பால், சமணரைக் கழுவேற்றிய: the unmistakable details of Sambandar, given here to Murugan.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["samayam","guru","thamizh","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:254"},"p:1288":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1288,"t":["சருவிய சாத்திரத் ...... திரளான","சடுதிக ழாஸ்பதத் ...... தமையாத","அருமறை யாற்பெறற் ...... கரிதாய","அனிதய வார்த்தையைப் ...... பெறுவேனோ","நிருதரை மூக்கறுத் ...... தெழுபார","நெடுதிரை யார்ப்பெழப் ...... பொருதோனே","பொருளடி யாற்பெறக் ...... கவிபாடும்","புலவரு சாத்துணைப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether he will gain the word of the heart that is not settled in the mass of quarrelling scriptures nor in the six-fold seat, and that is hard to gain even by the rare Vedas. The praise calls Murugan the one who fought the demons as the seven seas roared, and the close companion of poets who sing to gain wealth from his feet.","n":"சருவிய சாத்திரத் திரள்: the scriptures that quarrel, as in 1287's vain sects. சடுதிகழ் ஆஸ்பதம் may be the six systems, and அனிதய வார்த்தை, perhaps a word from the heart or beyond it, is obscure. பொருள் அடியால் பெறக் கவிபாடும் புலவர் can also mean poets who seek the meaning through the feet.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["samayam","vedham","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:254"},"p:1289":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1289,"t":["சினத்துச் சீறிய ...... வழிகாணச்","சிரித்துப் பேசியு ...... மயல்பூண","கனத்துப் போர்செயு ...... முலைதோணக்","கலைக்குட் பாதியு ...... மறைவாக","மனத்துக் காறுதல் ...... வருமாறு","மலைப்பப் பேணியு ...... மிகவாய","தனத்தைச் சூறைகொள் ...... மடவார்தம்","சதிக்குப் போம்வழி ...... தவிர்வேனோ","தெனத்தத் தாதென ...... எனவேபண்","திருத்தத் தோடளி ...... யிசைபாடும்","புனத்துக் காவல்கொள் ...... குறமாதின்","புணர்ச்சிக் கேயொரு ...... வழிதேடி","இனத்துக் காவல ...... ரறியாமல்","இணக்கித் தோகையை ...... மகிழ்வோயென்","றெனக்குத் தாளிணை ...... யருள்வாய்சூர்","இறக்கப் போர்செய்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks whether he will escape the road to the deceit of women who rage and hiss, laugh and talk to bind him in infatuation, flaunt their breasts and half hide them in cloth, tend him until his mind is soothed, and then plunder his wealth. The praise recalls Murugan seeking a way to unite with the Kuratti guarding the millet field while bees sang, coaxing her unseen by her clansmen, and asks him for his feet as the one who fought until Sura died.","n":"தனத்தைச் சூறைகொள் மடவார்: the wealth taken is named, and சதி (deceit) closes the list. The praise sets Murugan's own secret courtship of Valli, hidden from her kin, against the women's false intimacy.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","mayal"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:254"},"p:1290":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1290,"t":["தீதுற் றேயெழு ...... திங்களாலே","தீயைத் தூவிய ...... தென்றலாலே","போதுற் றாடும ...... நங்கனாலே","போதப் பேதைந ...... லங்கலாமோ","வேதத் தோனைமு ...... னிந்தகோவே","வேடப் பாவைவி ...... ரும்புமார்பா","ஓதச் சூதமெ ...... றிந்தவேலா","ஊமைத் தேவர்கள் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"An akam plea: should the innocent girl suffer because of the moon that rises to harm her, the south wind that scatters fire, and Kama who dances with his flowers? The praise names the king who punished Brahma of the Veda, the chest the hunter's doll desires, the Vel that struck the mango in the sea, the lord of the speechless gods.","n":"The tormentors are the stock of the evening of separation: moon, south wind, Kama (அநங்கன்). ஓதச் சூதம் எறிந்த வேலா: Sura as the mango tree in the sea. ஊமைத் தேவர்கள் may be the gods struck dumb, perhaps for not knowing Om; I cannot place it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","maalai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:254"},"p:1291":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1291,"t":["துள்ளுமத வேள்கைக் ...... கணையாலே","தொல்லைநெடு நீலக் ...... கடலாலே","மெள்ளவரு சோலைக் ...... குயிலாலே","மெய்யுருகு மானைத் ...... தழுவாயே","தெள்ளுதமிழ் பாடத் ...... தெளிவோனே","செய்யகும ரேசத் ...... திறலோனே","வள்ளல்தொழு ஞானக் ...... கழலோனே","வள்ளிமண வாளப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A variant of 1285: by Kama's leaping arrows, the long blue sea and the cuckoo coming softly from the grove, the girl's body melts, and Murugan is asked to embrace her. The praise calls him the one clear in singing lucid Tamil, the valiant Kumaresa, whose anklets of knowledge Valli worships, Valli's bridegroom.","n":"Line for line the same frame as 1285 with different adjectives (துள்ளும், தொல்லை, மெள்ள, மெய்யுருகு), as if two drafts of one song survive. வள்ளல் தொழு may refer to Valli or to a generous devotee.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:254"},"p:1292":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1292,"t":["தேனியல்சொற் ...... கணிமாதர்","சேவைதனைக் ...... கருதாதே","யானெனதற் ...... றிடுபோதம்","யானறிதற் ...... கருள்வாயே","வானவருக் ...... கரசான","வாசவனுக் ...... கினியோனே","ஆனைமுகற் ...... கிளையோனே","ஆறுமுகப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks not to think of serving women whose words are sweet as honey, and that he be given to know the understanding in which 'I' and 'mine' are cut away. The praise calls Murugan dear to Indra, king of the gods, younger brother of the elephant-faced one, the six-faced lord.","n":"யான் எனது அற்றிடு போதம்: the same pair of 'I' and 'mine' that the Kural names as the root of bondage; here its cutting is asked as a gift of knowledge, not achieved by effort.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["agandhai","pattru","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:255"},"p:1293":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1293,"t":["நாரியர்க ளாசையைக் ...... கருதாதே","நானுனிரு பாதபத் ...... மமுநாட","ஆரமுத மானசர்க் ...... கரைதேனே","ஆனஅநு பூதியைத் ...... தருவாயே","காரணம தானவுத் ...... தமசீலா","கானகுற மாதினைப் ...... புணர்வோனே","சூரர்கிளை தூளெழப் ...... பொரும்வேலா","தோகைமயில் வாகனப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks not to think of desire for women but to seek Murugan's two lotus feet, and to be given the experience that is nectar, sugar and honey. The praise names him the cause of all, of excellent nature, who unites with the forest Kuratti, whose vel fought Sura's kin to dust, rider of the peacock.","n":"The sweetness the poet turns from (women) returns as the sweetness of அநுபூதி, nectar, sugar and honey (ஆரமுதமான சர்க்கரை தேனே). காரணமதான: Murugan as the cause.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","thiruvadi","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:255"},"p:1294":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1294,"t":["நாளு மிகுத்த ...... கசிவாகி","ஞான நிருத்த ...... மதைநாடும்","ஏழை தனக்கு ...... மநுபூதி","ராசி தழைக்க ...... அருள்வாயே","பூளை யெருக்கு ...... மதிநாக","பூண ரளித்த ...... சிறியோனே","வேளை தனக்கு ...... சிதமாக","வேழ மழைத்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks that to him, a poor one who seeks the dance of knowledge with a melting that grows every day, the wealth of experience may flourish. The praise calls Murugan the child given by Siva who wears poolai and erukku flowers, the moon and the snake, and the lord who called the elephant at the right moment.","n":"நாளும் மிகுத்த கசிவு: devotion as a melting that grows daily. ஞான நிருத்தம், the dance of knowledge, is the object sought. வேளை தனக்கு உசிதமாக வேழம் அழைத்த likely alludes to Murugan calling the elephant (Ganesa) to frighten Valli into his arms, but the text gives only the elephant and the timing.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["paththi","thirukkoothu","aanandham"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:255"},"p:1295":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1295,"t":["நித்தமுற் றுனைநினைத்து ...... மிகநாடி","நிட்டைபெற் றியல்கருத்தர் ...... துணையாக","நத்தியு தமதவத்தி ...... னெறியாலே","லக்யலக் கணநிருத்த ...... மருள்வாயே","வெற்றிவிக் ரமவரக்கர் ...... கிளைமாள","விட்டநத் துகரனுக்கு ...... மருகோனே","குற்றமற் றவருளத்தி ...... லுறைவோனே","குக்குடக் கொடிதரித்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet, thinking of and seeking Murugan daily, asks that with those who have attained nishtha as his companions, and by the path of their high austerity, he be given the dance of the aim and its marks. The praise calls him nephew of the conch-bearer Vishnu who sent destruction on the kin of the victorious rakshasas, dweller in the hearts of the faultless, bearer of the cock banner.","n":"நிட்டை பெற்று இயல் கருத்தர் துணையாக: the company of those established in contemplation is the means. லக்ய லக்கண நிருத்தம் may mean the dance that is both the goal and its sign; நத்தியு தமதவத்தின் is hard to split.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["samaadhi","adiyaar","thavam","thirukkoothu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:255"},"p:1296":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1296,"t":["நீலங்கொள் மேகத்தின் ...... மயில்மீதே","நீவந்த வாழ்வைக்கண் ...... டதனாலே","மால்கொண்ட பேதைக்குன் ...... மணநாறும்","மார்தங்கு தாரைத்தந் ...... தருள்வாயே","வேல்கொண்டு வேலைப்பண் ...... டெறிவோனே","வீரங்கொள் சூரர்க்குங் ...... குலகாலா","நாலந்த வேதத்தின் ...... பொருளோனே","நானென்று மார்தட்டும் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"An akam plea, in the voice of the girl's mother or friend: having seen Murugan come in glory on his peacock like a dark blue cloud, the girl is infatuated, so let him give her the fragrant garland on his chest. The praise names the Vel who once hurled the vel at the sea, death to the clan of valiant Suras, meaning of the four Vedas, the lord who strikes his chest saying 'It is I'.","n":"மால்கொண்ட பேதை: the girl's infatuation began with a sight of the god's procession, and what is asked is his garland (தார்), the classical token of the hero's acceptance. நான் என்று மார் தட்டும் gives Murugan a warrior's boast.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:255"},"p:1297":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1297,"t":["பட்டு படாத ...... மதனாலும்","பக்கத்து மாதர் ...... வசையாலும்","சுட்டுச் சுடாத ...... நிலவாலும்","துக்கத்தி லாழ்வ ...... தியல்போதான்","தட்டுப் படாத ...... திறல்வீரா","தர்க்கித்த சூரர் ...... குலகாலா","மட்டுப் படாத ...... மயிலோனே","மற்றொப்பி லாத ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"An akam plea: is it right that the girl sink in sorrow because of Kama, who died yet did not die, the reproach of the women nearby, and moonlight that burns without burning? The praise calls Murugan the hero of unchecked strength, death to the arguing Sura clan, rider of the unrestrained peacock, the peerless lord.","n":"The plea is built on paradoxes: பட்டுப் படாத மதன், Kama burned by Siva yet alive, and சுட்டுச் சுடாத நிலவு, moonlight that scorches without fire. பக்கத்து மாதர் வசை is the town's gossip of akam poetry.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","alar","maalai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:255"},"p:1298":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1298,"t":["பரவைக் கெத்தனை ...... விசைதூது","பகரற் குற்றவ ...... ரெனமாணுன்","மரபுக் குச்சித ...... ப்ரபுவாக","வரமெத் தத்தர ...... வருவாயே","கரடக் கற்பக ...... னிளையோனே","கலைவிற் கட்குற ...... மகள்கேள்வா","அரனுக் குற்றது ...... புகல்வோனே","அயனைக் குட்டிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks Murugan to come and grant boons in plenty, so that he may become a master fit for Murugan's line, as his father was found fit to carry a message to Paravai again and again. The praise names him younger brother of the karpaka-like elephant god, husband of the Kuratti whose eyes are bows, the one who told Siva what was fitting, and who knocked Brahma on the head.","n":"பரவைக்கு எத்தனை விசை தூது: Paravai is the wife of the saint Sundarar, to whom Siva went as messenger at his devotee's bidding, and the plea asks for grace on that scale. அரனுக்கு உற்றது புகல்வோனே, அயனைக் குட்டிய: the teaching of Siva and the punishing of Brahma, the story elsewhere told of Om, which is not named here. The construction of the plea's first two lines is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","guru","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:255"},"p:1299":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1299,"t":["பிறவியலை யாற்றினிற் ...... புகுதாதே","பிரகிருதி மார்க்கமுற் ...... றலையாதே","உ றுதிகுரு வாக்கியப் ...... பொருளாலே","உ னதுபத காட்சியைத் ...... தருவாயே","அறுசமய சாத்திரப் ...... பொருளோனே","அறிவுளறி வார்க்குணக் ...... கடலோனே","குறுமுனிவ னேத்துமுத் ...... தமிழோனே","குமரகுரு கார்த்திகைப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks not to enter the river of births with its waves nor wander on the path of nature, but by the meaning of the guru's sure word to be given the vision of Murugan's feet. The praise calls him the meaning of the scriptures of the six creeds, an ocean of virtue to those who know the knowledge within knowledge, the lord of the three Tamils whom the short sage Agastya praises, Kumaraguru of the Krittikas.","n":"பிரகிருதி மார்க்கம்: the path of nature, of the body and its tattvas, set against the guru's word (குரு வாக்கியப் பொருள்). அறுசமய சாத்திரப் பொருளோனே: here Murugan is the meaning the six creeds share, where 1287 and 1288 call sectarian devotion vain. குறுமுனிவன் ஏத்தும் முத்தமிழோனே: Murugan as Tamil, praised by Agastya.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","guru","thiruvadi","samayam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:255"},"p:1300":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1300,"t":["புத்தகத் தேட்டிற் றீட்டி ...... முடியாது","பொற்புறக் கூட்டிக் காட்டி ...... யருள்ஞான","வித்தகப் பேற்றைத் தேற்றி ...... யருளாலே","மெத்தெனக் கூட்டிக் காக்க ...... நினைவாயே","தத்தைபுக் கோட்டிக் காட்டி ...... லுறைவாளைச்","சற்கரித் தேத்திக் கீர்த்தி ...... பெறுவோனே","கைத்தலத் தீக்குப் பார்த்து ...... நுழையாத","கற்பகத் தோப்புக் காத்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks Murugan to think of revealing in grace the wise attainment of knowledge that cannot be completed by writing on the leaves of books, and of gently gathering him in and guarding him. The praise names him the one who won fame by honouring and praising the girl who dwells in the forest where parrots play, and who guarded the karpaka grove.","n":"புத்தகத்து ஏட்டில் தீட்டி முடியாது: the knowledge sought is beyond what can be written in a book, so it must be given by grace (அருளாலே). The line கைத்தலத் தீக்குப் பார்த்து நுழையாத is obscure; it may say the grove was one that (the demons) feared to enter.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","kalvi","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:255"},"p:1301":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1301,"t":["பொன்னை விரும்பிய ......பொதுமாதர்","புன்மை விரும்பியெ ...... தடுமாறும்","என்னை விரும்பிநி ...... யொருகால்நின்","எண்ணி விரும்பவு ...... மருள்வாயே","மின்னை விரும்பிய ...... சடையாளர்","மெய்யின் விரும்பிய ...... குருநாதா","அன்னை விரும்பிய ...... குறமானை","அண்மி விரும்பிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet, who wavers desiring the meanness of public women who desire gold, asks that Murugan, desiring him, grant him once to desire to think of Murugan. The praise calls him the guru desired truly by Siva of the lightning-bright matted hair, who drew near and desired the Kuratti whom the mother desired.","n":"Every line turns on விரும்பு, desire: the women desire gold, the poet desires their meanness, Murugan is asked to desire the poet so that the poet may desire him, and Siva and Murugan desire in turn. The wordplay moves desire from gold to grace. பொதுமாதர் and பொன்னை விரும்பிய name the courtesans' trade.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","anbu","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:255"},"p:1302":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1302,"t":["மனைமக்கள் சுற்ற ...... மெனுமாயா","வலையைக்க டக்க ...... அறியாதே","வினையிற்செ ருக்கி ...... யடிநாயேன்","விழலுக்கி றைத்து ...... விடலாமோ","சுனையைக்க லக்கி ...... விளையாடு","சொருபக்கு றத்தி ...... மணவாளா","தினநற்ச ரித்ர ...... முளதேவர்","சிறைவெட்டிவிட்ட ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"Not knowing how to cross the net of maya called house, children and kin, swollen with his deeds, the poet, a dog of a servant, asks whether he must pour water on weeds. The praise calls Murugan bridegroom of the Kuratti who plays stirring up the spring, and the lord who cut the gods free from prison.","n":"மனை மக்கள் சுற்றம் எனும் மாயா வலை: the household itself is the net. விழலுக்கு இறைத்தல், watering useless grass, is a proverb for wasted effort, here the poet's whole life.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maayai","pattru","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:255"},"p:1303":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1303,"t":["வாரிமீ தேயெழு ...... திங்களாலே","மாரவே ளேவிய ...... அம்பினாலே","பாரெலா மேசிய ...... பண்பினாலே","பாவியே னாவிம ...... யங்கலாமோ","சூரனீள் மார்புதொ ...... ளைந்தவேலா","சோதியே தோகைய ...... மர்ந்தகோவே","மூரிமால் யானைம ...... ணந்தமார்பா","மூவர்தே வாதிகள் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"The girl speaks for herself: must this sinner's life faint because of the moon rising over the sea, the arrows Kama shot, and the reproach that has spread over the whole earth? The praise names the Vel that pierced Sura's broad chest, the light, the king seated on the peacock, the chest that wedded the elephant-maiden, the lord of the three gods and all the gods.","n":"பாவியேன் ஆவி மயங்கலாமோ is the girl's own voice, where 1290 speaks of her. பாரெலாம் ஏசிய: the gossip has spread over the whole world. மூரி மால் யானை may be Devayanai.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","alar","maalai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:255"},"p:1304":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1304,"t":["வானப் புக்குப் பற்றும ருத்துக் ...... கனல்மேவு","மாயத் தெற்றிப் பொய்க்குடி லொக்கப் ...... பிறவாதே","ஞானச் சித்திச் சித்திர நித்தத் ...... தமிழாலுன்","நாமத் தைக்கற் றுப்புகழ் கைக்குப் ...... புரிவாயே","கானக் கொச்சைச் சொற்குற விக்குக் ...... கடவோனே","காதிக் கொற்றப் பொற்குல வெற்பைப் ...... பொரும்வேலா","தேனைத் தத்தச் சுற்றிய செச்சைத் ...... தொடையோனே","தேவச் சொர்க்கச் சக்கிர வர்த்திப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet asks not to be born into the deceptive false hut that sky, earth, wind, water and fire inhabit, but to learn Murugan's name with the Tamil of knowledge and attainment and to be given praise in his hand. The praise calls him the one bound to the forest Kuratti of rustic speech, the Vel that fought the golden mountain, wearer of the fragrant vetchi garland, emperor of the gods' heaven.","n":"மாயத் தெற்றிப் பொய்க்குடில்: the body as a false hut of the five elements. புகழ் கைக்குப் புரிவாயே may be read as asking for the gift of praise-songs, புகழ், the name of the book itself. The split of the first line (வானப் புக்குப் பற்றும் மருத்து) is uncertain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","piravi","thamizh","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:255"},"p:1305":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1305,"t":["குருபர சரவண பவசண் முககுக","ஒருபர வயமியல் எயினர் மகள்சுக","மண்டத் தனங்கள்புணர் சண்டத் திரண்டபுஜ","உ ழுவைகள் கரடிகள் கிடிகள் பகடுகள்","இளைகளை நெறுநெறு நெறென உ லவுவி","லங்கற்குறிஞ்சியுறைதொங்கற்கடம்ப ...... அருள் தருவாயே","... அடிபடு முரசு தவில்பட","கந்தக்கை துந்துமித டந்தப்பு டன் சலிகை","... கரடிகை யறைபறை திமிலை .. அபிநவ","சங்கொற்றை கொம்புகுழல் வங்கக் கருங்கடல் கொள்","பிரளய மிதுவென அதிர உ லகர்கள்","அரகர சிவசிவ அபய மபயமெ","னுஞ்சத்த மெங்குமெழ வெஞ்சத்தி கொண்டுபடை ...... புகவானோர்","... வனச மலர்நிகர், செம்பொற் சதங்கையடி யன்பர்க்கு வந்துதவு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The surviving lines invoke Murugan as guru, Saravanabhava, Shanmukha and Guha, delight of the hunter's daughter, lord of the kurinji hills where tigers, bears, boars, buffaloes and deer roam, and ask for his grace; then they describe drums, conches, horns and trumpets thundering as at the world's end while the world cries 'Hara Hara, Siva Siva, refuge', and end on the anklet-bearing feet that come to help devotees.","n":"The text is incomplete, so no plea survives beyond அருள் தருவாயே; the rest is a battle scene in instrument names and the closing image of the feet that come to the devotees (அன்பர்க்கு வந்து உதவு).","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thiruvarul","thiruvadi","adiyaar"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:255"},"p:1306":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1306,"t":["கும்ப கோணமொ டர்ருர் சிதம்பரம்","உ ம்பர் வாழ்வுறு சீகாழி நின்றிடு","கொன்றை வேணியர் மாயூர மம்பெறு ...... சிவகாசி","கொந்து லாவிய ராமே சுரந்தனி","வந்து பூஜைசெய் நால்வேத தந்திரர்","கும்பு கூடிய ஧வ்ளுர் பரங்கிரி...... தனில்வாழ்வே","செம்பு கேசுர மாடானை யின்புறு","செந்தி லேடகம் வாழ்சோலை யங்கிரி","தென்றன் மாகிரிநாடாள வந்தவ ...... செகநாதஞ்","செங்சோ லேரக மாவா வினன்குடி","குன்று தோறுடன் மூதூர் விரிஞ்சைநல்","செம்பொன் மேனிய சோணாடு வஞ்சியில் ...... வருதேவே","கம்பை மாவடி மீதேய சுந்தர","கம்பு லாவிய காவேரிசங்கமு","கஞ்சி ராமலை வாழ்தேவ தந்திர ...... வயலு஡ரா","கந்த மேவிய பேர்ருர் நடம்புரி","தென்சி வாயமு மேயா யகம்படு","கண்டி யூர்வரு சாமீக டம்பணி ...... மணிமார்பா","எம்பி ரானொடு வாதாடு மங்கையர்","உ ம்பர் வாணிபொ னீள்மால் சவுந்தரி","எந்த நாள்தொறு மேர்பாக நின்றுறு ...... துதியோதும்","இந்தி ராணிதன் மாதோடு நன்குற","மங்கை மானையு மாலாய்ம ணந்துல","கெங்கு மேவிய தேவால யந்தொறு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The song is a garland of shrine names: Kumbakonam, Arur, Chidambaram, Sirkazhi, Mayuram, Sivakasi, Rameswaram where the Veda-knowing one came alone to worship, Evvulur, Parangiri, Thiruvadanai, Senthil, Edakam, Solaimalai, Eragam, Avinankudi, the hills one by one, Virinjai, Kanchi, Vayalur, Perur, Kandiyur and others, each a place where Murugan lives. It closes with the women who contend with Siva, Vani, Lakshmi and the beautiful one, praising him every day, and with Murugan who married Indrani's daughter and the hunter girl, the lord in every temple throughout the world.","n":"The song has no confession: its devotion is the naming of places, and the last line (உலகெங்கு மேவிய தேவாலயந்தொறு பெருமாளே) turns the list into a claim that the Lord is in every temple, not only these. குன்று தோறுடன் echoes the 'every hill' (குன்றுதோறாடல்) of Murugan's worship. Several names are hard to identify because the rhythm splits them (செம்பு கேசுரம், செங்சோ லேரகம், கம்பை மாவடி, தென்சி வாயமு), and the reading of these is tentative.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["thalam","viyaabagam","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:256"},"p:1307":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1307,"t":["அகரமு மாகி யதிபனு மாகி யதிகமு மாகி ...... அகமாகி","அயனென வாகி அரியென வாகி அரனென வாகி ...... அவர்மேலாய்","இகரமு மாகி யெவைகளு மாகி யினிமையு மாகி ...... வருவோனே","இருநில மீதி லெளியனும் வாழ எனதுமு னோடி ...... வரவேணும்","மகபதி யாகி மருவும் வலாரிமகிழ்களி கூரும் ...... வடிவோனே","வனமுறை வேட னருளிய பூஜை மகிழ்கதிர் காம ...... முடையோனே","செககண சேகு தகுதிமி தோதி திமியென ஆடு ...... மயிலோனே","திருமலி வான பழமுதிர் சோலை மலைமிசை மேவு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"Murugan is hymned as the letter A, the lord, the excess and the within; as Brahma, Vishnu and Hara and the one above them; as the letter I, as all things and as sweetness; and the poet asks him to run ahead of him so that even a lowly one may live on this wide earth. The praise names the form that delights Indra, the lord who rejoices in the worship the forest hunter offered at Kathirkamam, and the peacock that dances to the drum syllables, at Pazhamudircholai.","n":"அகரமுமாகி ... இகரமுமாகி: Murugan as the first letters, the image the Kural opens with; here he is also அவர்மேலாய், above the three gods he becomes. The plea is small and tender: எனது முன் ஓடி வரவேணும், run ahead of me. The praise names கதிர்காமம், the shrine in Lanka, and the hunter's worship there.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["viyaabagam","iraivan","thiruvarul"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:257"},"p:1308":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1308,"t":["இலவிதழ் கோதி நேதி மதகலை யார வார இளநகை யாட ஆடி ...... மிகவாதுற்","றெதிர்பொரு கோர பார ம்ருகமத கோல கால இணைமுலை மார்பி லேற ...... மதராஜன்","கலவியி லோடி நீடு வெகுவித தாக போக கரணப்ர தாப லீலை ...... மடமாதர்","கலவியின் மூழ்கி யாழு மிழிதொழி லேனு மீது கருதிய ஞான போத ...... மடைவேனோ","கொலைபுரிகாளி சூலி வயரவி நீலி மோடி குலிச குடாரியாயி ...... மகமாயி","குமரிவ ராகி மோகி பகவதி யாதி சோதி குணவதி யால வூணி ...... யபிராமி","பலிகொள்க பாலி யோகி பரமகல் யாணி லோக பதிவ்ரதை வேத ஞானி ...... புதல்வோனே","படையொடு சூரன் மாள முடுகிய சூர தீர பழமுதிர் சோலை மேவு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes the musk-smeared breasts, laughing play and many kinds of pleasure of women in love-union, and asks whether he, sunk in that union and given to low deeds, will ever reach the wisdom of jnana he has thought of. The praise is a long litany of the Goddess, Kali, the trident-bearer, Bhairavi, Nili, Varahi, Bhagavati, the poison-eater, Abhirami, Kapali who takes offerings, the yogini, the supremely auspicious, the chaste wife of the world, the knower of the Veda, whose son Murugan is, the hero who rushed at Sura and his army, at Pazhamudircholai.","n":"The praise is almost wholly the Goddess's names (கொலைபுரி காளி சூலி ... வேத ஞானி), fierce and gentle together, before புதல்வோனே makes Murugan her son. மதராஜன் கலவி: the union is called Kama's own.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","gnaanam","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:257"},"p:1309":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1309,"t":["காரணம தாக வந்து ...... புவிமீதே","காலனணு காதி சைந்து ...... கதிகாண","நாரணனும் வேதன் முன்பு ...... தெரியாத","ஞானநட மேபு ரிந்து ...... வருவாயே","ஆரமுத மான தந்தி ...... மணவாளா","ஆறுமுக மாறி ரண்டு ...... விழியோனே","சூரர்கிளை மாள வென்ற ...... கதிர்வேலா","சோலைமலை மேவி நின்ற ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"A short song: the poet asks Murugan to come on earth for a purpose, so that Death does not come near and he may see the way, and to come performing the dance of knowledge that Narayana and Brahma never knew. The praise names him the bridegroom of the elephant-raised girl, the six-faced one with twelve eyes, the vel that destroyed Sura's kin, at Solaimalai.","n":"நாரணனும் வேதன் முன்பு தெரியாத ஞான நடம்: the dance that Vishnu and Brahma could not see is the idiom of Siva's unseen form, here given to Murugan's dance. ஆரமுதமான தந்தி மணவாளா: Devayanai, raised by the elephant.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","gnaanam","thirukkoothu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:257"},"p:1310":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1310,"t":["சீலமுள தாயர் தந்தை மாதுமனை யான மைந்தர்","சேருபொரு ளாசை நெஞ்சு ...... தடுமாறித்","தீமையுறு மாயை கொண்டு வாழ்வுசத மாமி தென்று","தேடினது போக என்று ...... தெருவூடே","வாலவய தான கொங்கை மேருநுத லான திங்கள்","மாதர்மய லோடு சிந்தை ...... மெலியாமல்","வாழுமயில் மீது வந்து தாளிணைகள் தாழு மென்றன்","மாயவினை தீர அன்பு ...... புரிவாயே","சேலவள நாட னங்கள் ஆரவயல் சூழ மிஞ்சி","சேணிலவு தாவ செம்பொன் ...... மணிமேடை","சேருமம ரேசர் தங்க ளூரிதென வாழ்வு கந்த","தீரமிகு சூரை வென்ற ...... திறல்வீரா","ஆலவிட மேவு கண்டர் கோலமுட னீடு மன்று","ளாடல்புரியீசர் தந்தை ...... களிகூர","ஆனமொழி யேப கர்ந்து சோலைமலை மேவு கந்த","ஆதிமுத லாக வந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses that his heart wavered in desire for mother, father, wife, house, children and wealth, that through evil maya he took this life for permanent, and that, his earnings gone, he wandered the streets wasting away for women; he asks Murugan to come on the living peacock so that he may bow at the feet, and to show love that ends his deluding karma. The praise names the hero who defeated Sura, honoured in a land of fields and golden terraces like the gods' own town, who spoke the fitting word so that his father, the poison-throated dancer of the hall, rejoiced, at Solaimalai.","n":"The attachments come in order, kin and household and wealth before women: மாதுமனை யான மைந்தர் சேருபொருள் ஆசை. வாழ்வு சதமாம் இது: the error is to take life as eternal. ஆனமொழியே பகர்ந்து, the word that delighted Siva, points to the teaching of Siva without naming Om.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pattru","maayai","mayal","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:257"},"p:1311":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1311,"t":["வீரமத னூல்வி ளம்பு போகமட மாதர் தங்கள்","வேல்விழியி னான்ம யங்கி ...... புவிமீதே","வீசுகையி னாலி தங்கள் பேசுமவர் வாயி தஞ்சொல்","வேலைசெய்து மால்மி குந்து ...... விரகாகிப்","பாரவச மான வங்க ணீடுபொருள் போன பின்பு","பாதகனு மாகி நின்று ...... பதையாமல்","பாகம்வர சேர அன்பு நீபமலர் சூடு தண்டை","பாதமலர் நாடி யென்று ...... பணிவேனோ","புரணம தான திங்கள் சூடுமர னாரிடங்கொள்","பூவையரு ளால்வ ளர்ந்த ...... முருகோனே","பூவுல கெலாம டங்க வோரடியி னால ளந்த","பூவைவடி வானு கந்த ...... மருகோனே","சூரர்கிளை யேத டிந்து பாரமுடி யேய ரிந்து","தூள்கள்பட நீறு கண்ட ...... வடிவேலா","சோலைதனி லேப றந்து லாவுமயி லேறி வந்து","சோலைமலை மேல மர்ந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses he was deluded by the spear eyes of women versed in the love-texts, did their bidding at their sweet words, grew mad with love, and when his great wealth had gone to them stood a sinner; he asks when he will seek and bow at the flower feet with anklets and kadamba. The praise names Murugan raised by the grace of Uma who holds the left of Siva, the nephew of the dark one who measured the earth with one step, and the vel that ground Sura's kin to dust, on the peacock that flies over the groves to Solaimalai.","n":"நீடு பொருள் போன பின்பு பாதகனுமாகி: the wealth spent on the women leaves him not only poor but a sinner. வீரமதனூல் விளம்பு போக மடமாதர்: the women are cast as teachers of Kama's treatise. Vishnu measuring the world (ஓரடியினால் அளந்த) is the Trivikrama story.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","selvam","kutram","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:257"},"p:1312":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1312,"t":["வாரண முகங்கி ழிந்து வீழவு மரும்ப லர்ந்து","மால்வரை யசைந்த நங்கன் ...... முடிசாய","வாளகிரியண்ட ரண்ட கோளமுற நின்றெ ழுந்த","மாதவ மறந்து றந்து ...... நிலைபேரப்","பூரண குடங்க டிந்து சிதகள பம்பு னைந்து","பூசலை விரும்பு கொங்கை ...... மடவார்தம்","போக சயனந் தவிர்ந்து னாடக பதம்ப ணிந்து","பூசனைசெய் தொண்ட னென்ப ...... தொருநாளே","ஆரண முழங்கு கின்ற ஆயிர மடந்த வங்கள்","ஆகுதி யிடங்கள் பொங்கு ...... நிறைவீதி","ஆயிர முகங்கள் கொண்ட நூபுர மிரங்கு கங்கை","யாரமர வந்த லம்பு ...... துறைசேரத்","தோரண மலங்கு துங்க கோபுர நெருங்கு கின்ற","சூழ்மணிபொன் மண்ட பங்கள் ...... ரவிபோலச்","சோதியின் மிகுந்த செம்பொன் மாளிகை விளங்குகின்ற","சோலைமலை வந்து கந்த ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes breasts that outdo an elephant's temples, shake the great mountain, bow Kama's head and make ascetics forget their penance, and asks whether a day will come when, leaving those women's beds, he bows at the golden feet and is called a servant who performs worship. The praise is a picture of Solaimalai: streets ringing with the Vedas and full of sacrificial fires, the thousand-faced stream of the anklet (நூபுர) Ganga at its bathing ghats, towers hung with festoons, gem-set halls and golden mansions bright as the sun.","n":"பூசனைசெய் தொண்டன் என்பது ஒருநாளே: the wish is to be named a servant who worships, a public identity rather than an inner state. The praise is a rare town-portrait; நூபுரம் இரங்கு கங்கை names the hill's stream as the Ganga of the anklet. The first lines of the plea are hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","thondu","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:257"},"p:1313":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1313,"t":["ஆசை நாலுசது ரக்கமல முற்றினொளி","வீசி யோடியிரு பக்கமொடு றச்செல்வளி","ஆவல் கூரமண்மு தற்சலச பொற்சபையு ...... மிந்துவாகை","ஆர மூணுபதி யிற்கொளநி றுத்திவெளி","யாரு சோதிநுறு பத்தினுட னெட்டுஇத","ழாகி யேழுமள விட்டருண விற்பதியின் ...... விந்துநாத","ஓசை சாலுமொரு சத்தமதி கப்படிக","மோடு கூடியொரு மித்தமுத சித்தியொடு","மோது வேதசர சத்தியடி யுற்றதிரு ...... நந்தியூடே","ஊமை யேனையொளிர் வித்துனது முத்திபெற","மூல வாசல்வெளி விட்டுனது ரத்திலொளிர்","யோக பேதவகை யெட்டுமிதி லொட்டும்வகை ...... யின்றுதாராய்","வாசி வாணிகனெ னக்குதிரை விற்றுமகிழ்","வாத வூரனடி மைக்கொளுக்ரு பைக்கடவுள்","மாழை ரூபன்முக மத்திகைவி தத்தருண ...... செங்கையாளி","வாகு பாதியுறை சத்திகவு ரிக்குதலை","வாயின் மாதுதுகிர் பச்சைவடி விச்சிவையென்","மாசு சேரழுபி றப்பையும றுத்தவுமை ...... தந்தவாழ்வே","காசி ராமெசுரம் ரத்நகிரிசர்ப்பகிரி","எருர் வேலுர் தெவுர் கச்சிமது ரைப்பறியல்","காவை மூதுரரு ணக்கிரிதி ருத்தணியல் ...... செந்தில்நாகை","காழி ஧வ்ளுர்பழ நிக்கிரிகுறுக்கைதிரு","நாவ லு஡ர்திருவெ ணெய்ப்பதியின் மிக்கதிகழ்","காதல் சோலைவளர் வெற்பிலுறை முத்தர்புகழ் ...... தம்பிரானே."],"p":"","r":"In the language of yoga the poet traces an ascent: from the four-petalled lotus with the breath running on both sides, through the centres of earth and the others, the three regions, the light of a hundred and eight petals, bindu and nada and the sound beyond, through the sacred Nandi; he asks Murugan to make him, a mute one, shine, to send him out by the root gate into the light on Murugan's breast, and give him today the way the eight kinds of yoga join in this. The praise names Siva who sold horses as a horse-merchant and took Vadavurar as his servant, Gauri on his left who cut the seven births, and a long list of shrines, Kasi, Rameswaram, Ratnagiri, Kanchi, Madurai, Thiruthani, Senthil, Nagai, Kazhi, Palani and Navalur, with the hill of Solaimalai.","n":"The terms are those of the yogic body: நாலு சதுரக் கமலம் (the four-petalled root centre), இரு பக்கம் செல் வளி (the two breaths), விந்து நாத, மூல வாசல், யோக பேதவகை எட்டு (the eight limbs). The splitting of the numbers (ஆர மூணு பதி, நுறு பத்தினுடன் எட்டு இதழ்) is unsure. வாசி வாணிகன் எனக் குதிரை விற்று ... வாதவூரன்: Siva as horse-dealer for Manikkavasagar, named by his town. The shrine list repeats the device of the catalogue song 1306.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["kundalini","aadhaaram","yogam","thalam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:257"},"p:1314":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1314,"t":["கருவாகியெ தாயுத ரத்தினி","லுருவாகவெ கால்கையு றுப்பொடு","கனிவாய்விழி நாசியு டற்செவி ...... நரைமாதர்","கையிலேவிழ வேகிய ணைத்துயி","லெனவேமிக மீதுது யிற்றிய","கருதாய்முலை யாரமு தத்தினி ...... லினிதாகித்","தருதாரமு மாகிய சுற்றமு","நலவாழ்வுநி லாதபொ ருட்பதி","சதமாமிது தானென வுற்றுனை ...... நினையாத","சதுராயுன தாளிணை யைத்தொழ","அறியாதநிர் மூடனை நிற்புகழ்","தனையோதிமெய்ஞ் ஞானமு றச்செய்வ ...... தொருநாளே","செருவாயெதி ராமசு ரத்திரள்","தநல்முளைக ளோடுநி ணத்தசை","திமிர்தாதுள பூதக ணத்தொடு ...... வருபேய்கள்","திகுதாவுண வாயுதி ரத்தினை","பலவாய்நரியோடுகு டித்திட","சிலகூகைகள் தாமுந டித்திட ...... அடுதீரா","அருமாமறை யோர்கள்து தித்திடு","புகர்வாரண மாதுத னைத்திகழ்","அளிசேர்குழல் மேவுகு றத்தியை ...... அணைவோனே","அழகானபொன் மேடையு யர்த்திடு","முகில்தாவிய சோலைவி யப்புறு","அலையாமலை மேவிய பத்தர்கள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet traces his life: formed in the mother's womb with limbs, mouth, eyes, nose and ears, falling into the hands of grey-haired women, laid to sleep and fed on the mother's breast, then taking wife, kin, a life that does not last and wealth as permanent, never thinking of Murugan; he asks whether a day will come when this utter fool, who never learned to worship the feet, is made to sing Murugan's praise and reach true knowledge. The praise shows the battlefield where ghosts with jackals drink the demons' blood and owls dance, and names Murugan who embraces the girl raised by the elephant and the hunter girl, lord of the devotees at the hill of golden terraces and cloud-touched groves.","n":"The life is told from conception (கருவாகியெ தாய் உதரத்தினில் உருவாகவெ) through birth and nursing to the household, so that the whole course becomes the delusion: சதமாம் இது தானென. நரைமாதர் are the old women, midwives, whose hands first receive the child. The hill is called அலையாமலை.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karu","nilaiyaamai","pugazh","gnaanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:257"},"p:1315":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1315,"t":["சீர்சிறக்கு மேனி பசேல் பசே லென","நூபுரத்தி னோசை கலீர் கலீ ரென","சேரவிட்ட தாள்கள் சிவேல் சிவே லென ...... வருமானார்","சேகரத்தின் வாலை சிலோர் சிலோர் களு","நூறுலக்ஷ கோடி மயால் மயால் கொடு","தேடியொக்க வாடி யையோ வையோ வென ...... மடமாதர்","மார்படைத்த கோடு பளீர் பளீ ரென","ஏமலித்தெ னாவி பகீர் பகீ ரென","மாமசக்கி லாசை யுளோ முளோ மென ...... நினைவோடி","வாடை பற்று வேளை யடா வடா வென","நீமயக்க மேது சொலாய் சொலாயென","வாரம்வைத்த பாத மிதோ இதோ என ...... அருள்வாயே","பாரதத்தை மேரு வெளீ வெளீ திகழ்","கோடொ டித்த நாளில் வரைஇ வரைஇ பவர்","பானி றக்க ணேசர் குவா குவா கனர் ....... ; ளையோனே","பாடல்முக்ய மாது தமீழ் தமீ ழிறை","மாமுநிக்கு காதி லுணார் வுணார் விடு","பாசமற்ற வேத ரு ரு பர ...... குமரோசா","போர்மிகுத்த சூரன் விடோ ம் விடோ மென","நேரெதிர்க்க வேலை படீர் படீ ரென","போயறுத்த போது குபீர் குபீ ரென ...... வெகுசோரி","பூமியுக்க வீசு குகா குகா திகழ்","சோலைவெற்பின் மேவு தெய்வா தெய்வா னைதொள்","பூணியிச்சை யாறு புயா புயா றுள ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"Built on doubled cries, the song describes women coming with anklets jingling, youths seeking them in endless infatuation, breasts gleaming and the poet's heart lurching, and asks that, at the hour when this seizes him, Murugan say 'Hey, hey! what delusion is this, tell me', and give grace saying 'here, here are the feet you love'. The praise names Murugan younger brother of Ganesha who broke his tusk to write the Bharata on Meru, the guru who taught Tamil in the ear of the great sage, and the Guha whose vel cut Sura as he cried 'we will not yield', with blood gushing, at Solaimalai, with Devayanai's shoulders and twelve arms.","n":"The whole song is made of doubled exclamations (கலீர் கலீர், பகீர் பகீர், அடா அடா, இதோ இதோ, குபீர் குபீர்), so the grace is imagined as a scolding and a showing in the god's own voice. பாரதத்தை மேரு ... கோடொடித்த: Ganesha's broken tusk used to write the Mahabharata. மாமுநிக்கு காதில் உணர்வு: Tamil taught to Agastya. வாடை பற்று வேளை may be the hour of love's wind or of death; the plea is unsure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mayal","thiruvadi","thiruvarul","thamizh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:257"},"p:1316":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1316,"t":["துடிகொ ணோய்க ளோடு வற்றி","தருண மேனி கோழை துற்ற","இரும லீளை வாத பித்த ...... மணுகாமல்","துறைக ளோடு வாழ்வு விட்டு","உ லக நூல்கள் வாதை யற்று","சுகமு ளாநு பூதி பெற்று ...... மகிழாமே","உ டல்செய் கோர பாழ்வ யிற்றை","நிதமு மூணி னாலு யர்த்தி","யுயிரினீடு யோக சித்தி ...... பெறலாமே","உ ருவி லாத பாழில் வெட்ட","வெளியி லாடு நாத நிர்த்த","உ னது ஞான பாத பத்ம ...... முறுவேனோ","கடிது லாவு வாயு பெற்ற","மகனும் வாலி சேயு மிக்க","மலைகள் போட ஆழி கட்டி ...... யிகலு஡ர்போய்க்","களமு றானை தேர்நு றுக்கி","தலைக ளாறு நாலு பெற்ற","அவனை வாளி யால டத்தன் ...... மருகோனே","முடுகு வீர சூர பத்மர்","தலையின் மூளை நீறு பட்டு","முடிவ தாக ஆடு நிர்த்த ...... மயில்வீரா","முநிவர் தேவர் ஞான முற்ற","புநித சோலை மாமலைக்குள்","முருக வேல த்யாகர் பெற்ற ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet speaks of the body wasted by fevers, phlegm, cough, wheezing and the humours, and asks whether, fattening the dreadful wretched belly with food every day, one can gain the yogic power that prolongs life, or whether, freed from the torment of worldly books and given blissful experience, he will reach Murugan's lotus feet of knowledge that dance the dance of nada in the formless void. The praise names Rama, for whom the son of the wind and Vali's son threw hills to bridge the sea, and who killed the ten-headed one with an arrow, as Murugan's uncle, the peacock that danced on Sura's crushed brains, and Murugan son of Tyagar at Solaimalai where sages and gods gained knowledge.","n":"உருவிலாத பாழில் வெட்டவெளியில் ஆடு நாத நிர்த்த ... ஞான பாதம்: the feet dance in the formless void, the open space of the yogis. The question on yoga siddhi gained by feeding the body seems ironic. How the halves of the plea join (what is before the diseases come, and what is asked) is unclear. தலைகள் ஆறு நாலு: six and four, ten heads.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["yaakkai","noy","siththi","paazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:257"},"p:1317":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1317,"t":["பாசத் தால்விலை கட்டிய பொட்டிகள்","நேசித் தாரவர் சித்தம ருட்டிகள்","பாரப் பூதர மொத்தத னத்திகள் ...... மிகவேதான்","பாவத் தால்மெயெ டுத்திடு பட்டிகள்","சீவிக் கோதிமு டித்தள கத்திகள்","பார்வைக் கேமயலைத்தரு துட்டிக ...... ளொழியாத","மாசுற் றேறிய பித்தளை யிற்பணி","நீறிட் டேயொளி பற்றவி ளக்கிகள்","மார்பிற் காதினி லிட்ட பிலுக்கிகள் ...... அதிமோக","வாய்வித் தாரமு ரைக்கும பத்திகள்","நேசித் தாரையு மெத்திவ டிப்பவர்","மாயைக் கேமனம் வைத்தத னுட்டின ...... மலைவேனோ","தேசிக் கானக முற்றதி னைப்புன","மேவிக் காவல்க வட்கல்சு ழற்றுவள்","சீதப் பாதகு றப்பெண்ம கிழ்ச்சிகொள் ...... மணவாளா","தேடிப் பாடிய சொற்புல வர்க்கித","மாகத் தூதுசெ லத்தரில் கற்பக","தேவர்க் காதிதி ருப்புக லிப்பதி ...... வருவோனே","ஆசித் தார்மன திற்புகு முத்தம","கூடற் கேவைகை யிற்கரை கட்டிட","ஆளொப் பாயுதிர் பிட்டமு துக்கடி ...... படுவோனோ","டாரத் தோடகி லுற்றத ருக்குல","மேகத் தோடொரு மித்துநெ ருக்கிய","ஆதிச் சோலை மலைப்பதி யிற்றிகழ் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet lists the courtesans who set a price on their affection, unsettle the minds of those who love them, take bodies through sin, comb and knot their hair, cast infatuation with a look, polish tarnished brass ornaments with ash to make them shine, flaunt jewels at breast and ear, speak lavish lies and cheat and drain even those who love them, and asks whether he will set his mind on their maya and suffer daily. The praise names Valli guarding the millet field with her sling, Siva who went as messenger for the poet who sang him and who was beaten for pittu as a labourer at the Vaigai bank in Koodal, and Murugan who came at the holy town of Pugali, at the ancient Solaimalai thick with sandal and agil trees.","n":"பாசத்தால் விலை கட்டிய பொட்டிகள் and பித்தளையிற் பணி நீறிட்டே ஒளி பற்ற விளக்கிகள்: love with a price on it, and brass passed off as gold, the fraud put in one picture. திருப்புகலிப் பதி வருவோனே: Murugan who came at Pugali (Sirkazhi), the identification with Sambandar. The Siva stories are Sundarar's messenger and the Madurai pittu labourer. How அடிபடுவோனோ டாரத்தோடு divides is unsure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","maayai"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:257"},"p:1318":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1318,"t":["வாதினை யடர்ந்த வேல்விழியர் தங்கள்","மாயமதொ ழிந்து ...... தெளியேனே","மாமலர்கள் கொண்டு மாலைகள் புனைந்து","மாபதம ணிந்து ...... பணியேனே","ஆதியொடு மந்த மாகிய நலங்கள்","ஆறுமுக மென்று ...... தெரியேனே","ஆனதனி மந்த்ர ரூபநிலை கொண்ட","தாடுமயி லென்ப ...... தறியேனே","நாதமொடு விந்து வானவுடல் கொண்டு","நானிலம லைந்து ...... திரிவேனே","நாகமணி கின்ற நாதநிலை கண்டு","நாடியதில் நின்று ...... தொழுகேனே","சோதியுணர் கின்ற வாழ்வுசிவ மென்ற","சோகமது தந்து ...... எனையாள்வாய்","சூரர்குலம் வென்று வாகையொடு சென்று","சோலைமலை நின்ற ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"In a run of confessions the poet says he has not seen through the illusion of spear-eyed women, has not worshipped with garlands, has not known that the goods that are beginning and end are the six faces, nor that the dancing peacock is the form of the one mantra, that he wanders the world in a body made of nada and bindu and has not stood worshipping in the state of nada; he asks Murugan to rule him by giving the 'I am He' that the life which knows the light is Siva. The praise names Murugan who won over Sura's clan and stands at Solaimalai.","n":"The plea is all negations (தெளியேனே, பணியேனே, தெரியேனே, அறியேனே). சோதி உணர்கின்ற வாழ்வு சிவம் என்ற சோகம் அது தந்து: சோகம் is likely सोऽहम् 'I am He', the meditation of identity with Siva, though it can also read as grief. The peacock as மந்த்ர ரூபம் makes Murugan's mount a mantra.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["gnaanam","naadham","vindhu","sivam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:257"},"p:1319":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1319,"t":["வார்குழையை யெட்டி வேளினைம ருட்டி","மாயநம னுக்கு ...... முறவாகி","மாதவம ழித்து லீலைகள் மிகுத்து","மாவடுவை யொத்த ...... விழிமாதர்","சீருட னழைத்து வாய்கனிவு வைத்து","தேனித ழளித்து ...... அநுபோக","சேர்வைதனை யுற்று மோசம்விளை வித்து","சீர்மைகெட வைப்ப ...... ருறவாமோ","வாரினை யறுத்து மேருவை மறித்து","மாகனக மொத்த ...... குடமாகி","வாரவணை வைத்து மாலளித முற்று","மாலைகளு மொய்த்த ...... தனமாது","தோரணி புயத்தி யோகினி சமர்த்தி","தோகையுமை பெற்ற ...... புதல்வோனே","சூர்கிளை மடித்து வேல்கர மெடுத்து","சோலைமலை யுற்ற ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes women whose earrings reach their eyes, who bewilder Kama and are kin even to Death, who destroy great penance, with eyes like mango slices, who call sweetly, give their honey lips and pleasure, then work betrayal and ruin a man's good name, and asks whether their kinship is fit. The praise describes Uma with breasts like golden pots that burst their bodice, the yogini skilled in all things, the peacock-like Uma who bore Murugan, and Murugan who took up the vel and cut down Sura's kin, at Solaimalai.","n":"மாய நமனுக்கும் உறவாகி: the women are kin to Death himself, desire and death in one person. The praise answers the plea with the same body: Uma's breasts described in the plea's terms, now as the mother's.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","vanjam","saththi"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:257"},"p:1320":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1320,"t":["அழகு தவழ்குழல் விரித்துக் காட்டி","விழிகள் கடையிணை புரட்டிக் காட்டி","அணிபொ னணிகுழை புரித்துக் காட்டி ...... யநுராக","அவச இதமொழி படித்துக் காட்டி","அதர மழிதுவர் வெளுப்பைக் காட்டி","அமர்செய் நகநுதி யழுத்தைக் காட்டி ...... யணியாரம்","ஒழுகு மிருதன மசைத்துக் காட்டி","எழுத வரியிடை வளைத்துக் காட்டி","உ லவு முடைதனை நெகிழ்த்திக் காட்டி ...... யுறவாடி","உ ருகு கடிதட மொளித்துக் காட்டி","உ பய பரிபுர பதத்தைக் காட்டி","உ யிரை விலைகொளு மவர்க்குத் தேட்ட ...... மொழிவேனோ","முழுகு மருமறை முகத்துப் பாட்டி","கொழுநர் குடுமியை யறுத்துப் போட்ட","முதல்வ குகைபடு திருப்பொற் கோட்டு ...... முனிநாடா","முடுகு முதலையை வரித்துக் கோட்டி","அடியர் தொழமக வழைத்துக் கூட்டி","முறைசெய் தமிழினை விரித்துக் கேட்ட ...... முதுநீதர்","பழைய கடதட முகத்துக் கோட்டு","வழுவை யுரியணி மறைச்சொற் கூட்டு","பரமர் பகிரதி சடைக்குட் சூட்டு ...... பரமேசர்","பணிய அருள்சிவ மயத்தைக் காட்டு","குமர குலமலை யுயர்த்திக் காட்டு","பரிவொ டணிமயில் நடத்திக் காட்டு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"With the refrain 'showing', the poet lists the courtesans' displays, loosened hair, rolling eye corners, earrings, love-talk, pale lips, nail marks, swaying breasts, bent waist, loosened clothes and anklet feet, and asks whether he will stop earning for those who buy a man's life for a price. The praise names Murugan who cut off the tuft of Brahma, husband of Saraswati of the Veda face, the Siva who made the crocodile give back the swallowed boy and heard Tamil expounded, the matted-haired lord with the elephant skin and Ganga who bowed to receive grace, and the one who showed him the nature of Siva and made the peacock dance.","n":"உயிரை விலைகொளும் அவர்க்குத் தேட்டம்: not wealth alone but life is the price. The praise refrain takes the same verb, காட்டி turning to காட்டு: the women show their bodies, Murugan shows Siva his own nature (சிவமயத்தைக் காட்டு). The crocodile story is Sundarar's at Avinashi. The phrases முதல்வ குகைபடு திருப்பொற் கோட்டு முனிநாடா and குலமலை are obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","selvam","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:257"},"p:1321":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1321,"t":["தலைமயிர் கொக்குக் கொக்கந ரைத்துக்","கலகலெ னப்பற் கட்டது விட்டுத்","தளர்நடை பட்டுத் தத்தடி யிட்டுத் ...... தடுமாறித்","தடிகொடு தத்திக் கக்கல்பெ ருத்திட்","டசனமும் விக்கிச் சத்தியெ டுத்துச்","சளியுமி குத்துப் பித்தமு முற்றிப் ...... பலகாலும்","திலதயி லத்திட் டொக்கவெ ரிக்கத்","திரிபலை சுக்குத் திப்பிலி யிட்டுத்","தெளியவ டித்துற் றுய்த்துடல் செத்திட் ......டுயிர்போமுன்","திகழ்புகழ் கற்றுச் சொற்கள்ப யிற்றித்","திருவடி யைப்பற் றித்தொழு துற்றுச்","செனனம றுக்கைக் குப்பர முத்திக் ...... கருள்தாராய்","கலணைவி சித்துப் பக்கரை யிட்டுப்","புரவிசெ லுத்திக் கைக்கொடு வெற்பைக்","கடகுந டத்தித் திட்டென எட்டிப் ...... பொருசூரன்","கனபடை கெட்டுத் தட்டற விட்டுத்","திரைகட லுக்குட் புக்கிட எற்றிக்","களிமயி லைச்சித் ரத்தில்ந டத்திப் ...... பொருகோவே","குலிசன்ம கட்குத் தப்பியு மற்றக்","குறவர்ம கட்குச் சித்தமும் வைத்துக்","குளிர்தினை மெத்தத் தத்துபு னத்திற் ...... றிரிவோனே","கொடியபொ ருப்பைக் குத்திமு றித்துச்","சமரம்வி ளைத்துத் தற்பர முற்றுக்","குலகிரியிற்புக் குற்றுரை யுக்ரப் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet pictures old age: hair grey as a crane, teeth rattling loose, faltering steps with a stick, coughing and hiccupping, vomiting phlegm and bile, then sesame oil boiled with triphala, dry ginger and long pepper, strained and taken, until the body dies; before life goes, he asks to learn Murugan's praise, hold and worship his feet, and be given the supreme release that cuts birth. The praise shows Murugan saddling his mount to ride against Sura's hosts, making the peacock dance in battle, escaping Indra's daughter to give his heart to the hunters' daughter in the millet field, and piercing the cruel hill, the fierce lord who dwells on Kulagiri.","n":"The remedies (திலதயிலம், திரிபலை, சுக்கு, திப்பிலி) are listed with a physician's care, only to end in உடல் செத்திட்டு: medicine prolongs nothing. The cure asked for is song and the feet. குலிசன் மகட்குத் தப்பியும்: a playful turn, Murugan slipping away from Devayanai to Valli.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","marundhu","piravi","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:257"},"p:1322":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1322,"t":["மலரணை ததும்ப மேக குழல்முடி சரிந்து வீழ","மணபரிமளங்கள் வேர்வை ...... யதனோடே","வழிபட இடங்க ணாட பிறைநுதல் புரண்டு மாழ்க","வனைகலை நெகிழ்ந்து போக ....... ; ளநீரின்","முலையிணை ததும்ப நூலின் வகிரிடை சுழன்று வாட","முகமுகமொ டொன்ற பாய ...... லதுனூடே","முதுமயல் கலந்து மூழ்கி மகிழ்கினும் அலங்க லாடு","முடிவடிவொ டங்கை வேலு ...... மறவேனே","சிலைநுத லிளம்பெண் மோகி சடையழகி யெந்தை பாதி","திகழ்மர கதம்பொன் மேனி ...... யுமைபாலா","சிறுநகை புரிந்து சூரர் கிரிகட லெரிந்து போக","திகழயி லெறிந்த ஞான ......முருகோனே","கொலைமிக பயின்ற வேடர் மகள்வளி மணந்த தோள","குணவலர் கடம்ப மாலை ...... யணிமார்பா","கொடிமின லடைந்த சோதி மழகதிர் தவழ்ந்த ஞான","குலகிரிமகிழ்ந்து மேவு ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes lovemaking on a flower bed, cloud-dark hair loosened, scent and sweat mingling, crescent brows turning, garments slipping, breasts heaving and faces pressed together, and says that even while he is sunk and rejoicing in that old infatuation he will not forget Murugan's crown swaying with garlands, his form and the vel in his hand. The praise names him son of Uma of the bow brows, half of Siva, green-gold of body, the Murugan of knowledge who with a small smile hurled the vel so that Sura's hill and sea burned, Valli's bridegroom with the kadamba garland, at Kulagiri.","n":"Unusually there is no plea to be freed from desire: மகிழ்கினும் ... மறவேனே, 'even while I rejoice, I will not forget'. The remembrance of the god is held inside the confession rather than set against it. The vel is called ஞான, knowledge, as it is elsewhere.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","mayal","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:258"},"p:1323":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1323,"t":["கருவெனு மாயை உ ருவினில் மூழ்கி","வயதள வாக ...... நிலமீதில்","கலைதெரிவாணர் கலைபல நூல்கள்","வெகுவித மாக ...... கவிபாடித்","தெருவழி போகி பொருளெனு மாசை","திரவியம் நாடி ...... நெடிதோடிச்","சிலைநுதல் மாதர் மயலினில் மூழ்கி","சிறுவித மாக ...... திரிவேனோ","அருளநு போக குருபர னேஉ ன்","அடியவர் வாழ ...... அருள்வோனே","அரனிரு காதில் அருள்பர ஞாந","அடைவினை ஓதி ...... அருள்பாலா","வெருவிடு சூரர் குலஅடி வேரை","விழவிடு சாசு ...... வதிபாலா","மிடலுட லாளர் அடரசுர் மாள","விடுமயில் வேல ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet confesses that, sunk in the maya called the womb and grown up on earth, he learned many arts and books from scholars, sang many kinds of poems, went along the streets running far after wealth, and sank in infatuation with bow-browed women, and asks whether he must wander in such petty ways. The praise names Murugan the guru of gracious experience who keeps his devotees alive, the son who recited supreme knowledge in both of Hara's ears, the son of the eternal Goddess who felled the roots of Sura's clan, the lord of the peacock and vel.","n":"The confession is of a learned poet: the arts and the poems themselves served the hunt for wealth (பொருளெனும் ஆசை). அரன் இரு காதில் அருள் பரஞான அடைவினை ஓதி: the teaching of Siva, given here as knowledge whispered into his ears; Om is not named.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","selvam","mayal","guru"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:259"},"p:1324":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1324,"t":["தங்க மிகுந்த முலைக்க டாமலை","பொங்க விரும்பிய முத்து மாலைகள்","தங்க அணிந்து முறுக்கும் வேசியர் ...... மொழியாலே","சஞ்ச லமிஞ்சி மயக்கி யேஒரு","மஞ்ச மிருந்து சுகிக்க வேவளர்","சந்து சுகந்த முடித்து நூலிடை ...... கிடையாடக்","கொங்கை குலுங்க வளைத்து வாயத","ரங்க ளருந்தி ருசிக்க வேமத","குங்கு மமிஞ்சு கழுத்தி லேகுயி ...... லெனஓசை","கொண்ட வரிந்த விதத்தி னாடர","சங்கி லிகொண்டு பிணித்து மாமயில்","கொஞ்சி மகிழ்ந்த வறட்டு வீணியர் ...... உ றவாமோ","திங்கள் அரும்பு சலத்தி லேவிடம்","வந்த துகண்டு பயப்ப டாதவர்","சிந்தை நடுங்கி இருக்க வேமயில் ...... மிசையேறிச்","சிங்க முகன்தலை வெட்டி மாமுகன்","அங்க மறுந்து கிடக்க வேவரு","சிம்பு ளெனும்படி விட்ட வேலுள ...... குருநாதா","மங்கை மடந்தை கதிக்கு நாயகி","சங்கரிசுந்தரிஅத்தி யானனை","மைந்த னெனும்படி பெற்ற ஈசுரி...... தருபாலா","மந்திர தந்திர முத்த யோகியர்","அஞ்சலி செங்கை முடிக்க வேஅருள்","வந்து தரும்படி நித்த மாடிய ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet describes courtesans with gold-laden breasts and pearl strings who twist men with their words, delude them on the couch, and with swaying waists, kissing lips and cuckoo cries bind them as with chains, and asks whether the kinship of such barren, useless women is fit. The praise shows Murugan mounting the peacock while even the one unafraid of the sea's poison trembled, cutting off Singamukha's head and leaving the great-faced Sura limbless with a vel sent like the Sarabha bird, the son of Sankari who bore the elephant-faced one, who dances daily so that yogis of mantra and tantra join their palms.","n":"The women are named வேசியர், courtesans, but the song speaks of their arts, not of money, and calls them வறட்டு வீணியர், barren and useless. சிம்புள் is the Sarabha, the bird-beast that subdued the man-lion, apt for a vel sent against the lion-faced Singamukha. The line on the poison (திங்கள் அரும்பு சலத்திலே விடம் வந்தது கண்டு பயப்படாதவர் சிந்தை நடுங்கி) is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","punarchi","yogam"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:259"},"p:1325":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1325,"t":["உ ரையுஞ் சென்றது நாவும் உ லர்ந்தது","விழியும் பஞ்சுபொ லானது கண்டயல்","உ ழலுஞ் சிந்துறு பால்கடை நின்றது ...... கடைவாயால்","ஒழுகுஞ் சஞ்சல மேனிகு ளிர்ந்தது","முறிமுன் கண்டுகை கால்கள்நி மிர்ந்தது","உ டலுந் தொந்தியும் ஓடிவ டிந்தது ...... பரிகாரி","வரவொன் றும்பலி யாதினி என்றன்பின்","உ றவும் பெண்டிரு மோதிவி ழுந்தழ","மறல்வந் திங்கென தாவிகொ ளுந்தினம் ....... ; யல்தோகை","மயிலுஞ் செங்கைக ளாறிரு திண்புய","வரைதுன் றுங்கடி மாலையும் இங்கித","வனமின் குஞ்சரிமாருடன் என்றன்முன் ...... வருவாயே","அரிமைந் தன்புகழ் மாருதி என்றுள","கவியின் சங்கமி ராகவ புங்கவன்","அறிவுங் கண்டருள் வாயென அன்பொடு ...... தரவேறுன்","அருளுங் கண்டத ராபதி வன்புறு","விஜயங் கொண்டெழு போதுபு லம்பிய","அகமும் பைந்தொடி சீதமை றைந்திட ...... வழிதோறும்","மருவுங் குண்டலம் ஆழிசி லம்புகள்","கடகந் தண்டைபொன் நூபுர மஞ்சரி","மணியின் பந்தெறி வாயிது பந்தென ...... முதலான","மலையுஞ் சங்கிலி போலம ருங்குவிண்","முழுதுங் கண்டந ராயணன் அன்புறு","மருகன் தென்புன வாயில மர்ந்தருள் ...... பெருமாளே."],"p":"","r":"The poet pictures his own death: speech gone, tongue dry, eyes like cotton, the milk given him standing and dribbling from the corner of the mouth, the body chilled, hands and legs stiff, flesh and belly shrunk, the physician saying no remedy will work now, kin and wives beating themselves and falling in tears; he asks that on the day Death comes to take his life Murugan appear before him with the peacock, the twelve strong arms, the garlands and his two consorts. The praise tells how Hanuman, son of the wind, brought tokens to Rama, and how Rama, seeing along the path Sita's earrings, ring, anklets, bracelets and the ball she had thrown, grieved; Murugan is Narayana's nephew at southern Punavayil.","n":"The death is told sign by sign from inside the dying body, then from the bedside: the physician's verdict (பரிகாரி வரவொன்றும் பலியாது இனி) and the women's wailing. The plea asks not to escape death but for a vision at the hour. In the praise Sita's ornaments scattered along the road are listed as fully as the dying body's signs; the reading of these lines is tentative.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kootru","saakkaadu","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:260"},"p:1326":{"book":"thiruppugazh","num":1326,"t":["ஓருரு வாகிய தாரகப் பிரமத்","தொருவகைத் தோற்றத் திருமர பெய்தி","ஒன்றா யொன்றி யிருவரிற் றோன்றி மூவா தாயினை","இருபிறப் பாளரினொருவ னாயினை","ஓராச் செய்கையி னிருமையின் முன்னாள்","நான்முகன் குடிமி இமைப்பினிற் பெயர்த்து","மூவரும் போந்து இருதாள் வேண்ட","ஒருசிறை விடுத்தனை","ஒருநொடி யதனில் இருசிறை மயிலின்","முந்நீ ருடுத்த நானிலம் அஞ்ச நீவலஞ் செய்தனை","நால்வகை மருப்பின் மும்மதத் திருசெவி","ஒருகைப் பொருப்பன் மகளை வேட்டனை","ஒருவகை வடிவினி லிருவகைத் தாகிய","மும்மதன் தனக்கு மூத்தோ னாகி","நால்வாய் முகத்தோன் ஐந்துகைக் கடவுள்","அறுகு சூடிக் கிளையோ னாயினை","ஐந்தெழுத் ததனில் நான்மறை யுணர்த்து","முக்கட் சுடரினை இருவினை முருந்துக்","கொருகுரு வாயினை","ஒருநாள் உ மையிரு முலைப்பா லருந்தி","முத்தமிழ் விரகன் நாற்கவி ராஜன்","ஐம்புலக் கிழவன் அறுமுக னிவனென","எழில்தரு மழகுடன் கழுமலத் துதித்தனை","அறுமீன் பயந்தனை ஐந்தரு வேந்தன்","நான்மறைத் தோற்றத்து முத்தலைச் செஞ்சூட்","டன்றி லங்கிரியிருபிள வாக ஒருவேல் விடுத்தனை","காவிரிவடகரை மேவிய குருகிரிஇருந்த","ஆறெழுத் தந்தணர் அடியிணை போற்ற","ஏரகத் திறைவ னென இருந்தனையே."],"p":"","r":"All praise, counted out in numbers: Murugan, the one form of the Taraka Brahman, born in the one line from the two and never ageing, once lifted Brahma's tuft in a blink and freed him from prison when the three gods begged at his feet, circled the four-fold sea-girt land in an instant on the two-winged peacock, married the daughter of the one-trunked, four-tusked elephant, became younger brother of the five-armed, arugu-wearing Ganesha, and guru to the three-eyed Siva who teaches the Vedas in the five letters. He was born at Kazhumalam, drinking Uma's milk, as the master of three Tamils and king of four kinds of poetry, born of six stars, split the Krauncha hill with one vel, and dwells as lord of Eragam, the hill on the Kaveri's north bank where Brahmins of the six letters praise his feet.","n":"The numbers carry the form, not the sense: the device is the classical ezhukutrirukkai, a chariot-shaped number poem. தாரகப் பிரமம், the 'saving Brahman', is the pranava, and Murugan's guruship over Siva (முக்கட் சுடரினை ... ஒருகுரு வாயினை) is set inside it. கழுமலத்து உதித்தனை ... முத்தமிழ் விரகன்: Murugan identified with Sambandar, born at Sirkazhi and fed by Uma. ஆறெழுத்து அந்தணர்: the six-letter mantra of Murugan. Some number-phrases (இருபிறப்பாளரின் ஒருவன், ஒருவகை வடிவினில் இருவகைத்தாகிய மும்மதன்) are obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["guru","piranavam","thamizh","mandhiram"],"pos":[],"ch":"p:261"}},"links":[{"a":"k:1","b":"k:1062","type":"tension","note":"The opening verse sets the world under a First One without reservation. In 1062, Valluvar curses whoever ordered the world so that people must beg to live. Read together, they show the book does not treat the world's source as beyond moral judgement.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:2","b":"k:400","type":"tension","note":"Here learning has no fruit without bowing at the feet. In 400, learning itself is the one imperishable wealth, with no such condition.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:6","b":"k:25","type":"same_image","note":"The same word, ஐந்தவித்தான், describes the Lord here and a human renunciant in 25. Sharing the epithet suggests that the divine model and the human achievement are not wholly separate.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:10","b":"k:38","type":"tension","note":"Here only those at the Lord's feet cross the sea of births. In 38, on the commentators' reading, a daily good deed blocks the road of births, and God is not mentioned.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:14","b":"k:1031","type":"qualifies","note":"In 1031, the whole world follows the plough. This verse adds what the praise of agriculture leaves out: the plough itself follows the rain.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:19","b":"k:34","type":"tension","note":"Here giving and austerity cannot last without rain. In 34, virtue is simply a spotless mind and everything else is noise. If virtue is only inward, the drought in 19 shouldn't threaten it.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:13","b":"k:225","type":"develops","note":"Hunger is here the world's worst consequence of drought, a natural evil. In 225, relieving hunger outranks the ascetic's power to endure it, which turns this natural fact into a moral priority.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:21","b":"k:49","type":"tension","note":"Here the texts' settled view exalts renouncers above all. In 49, on Parimelazhagar's reading, the thing called virtue is household life, and renunciation is good only if it is blameless. The book states both verdicts without reconciling them.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:26","b":"k:975","type":"agrees","note":"Both define greatness by rare, difficult acts. Chapter 3 says this of renouncers, while 975 in the chapter on greatness says it of the great in general, so the test is not limited to ascetics.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:29","b":"k:301","type":"tension","note":"Here the sage's unstoppable anger shows his stature. In 301, real restraint means holding anger back exactly where it can hurt. By the standard of 301, the anger praised in 29 is what should be held back.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:30","b":"k:134","type":"agrees","note":"Both make conduct the test of priestly status: 30 defines அந்தணர் by compassion, and 134 says a பார்ப்பான் is lost if conduct fails, even though forgotten scripture can be relearned.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:34","b":"k:280","type":"develops","note":"Here outward virtue is noise if the mind is stained. In 280, a shaven head and matted hair are unnecessary if one avoids what the world condemns. Note that 280 keeps the world's blame as the test, so the inward standard of 34 isn't all that matters.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:36","b":"k:335","type":"agrees","note":"Both press virtue into the present against the delay of 'later'. 36 promises a companion, and 335 frightens with the hiccup that stops the tongue. Each uses a different motive for the same urgency.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:37","b":"k:972","type":"tension","note":"On the commentators' reading, 37 treats the difference between rider and bearer as virtue made visible. In 972, birth is the same for all beings, and distinction comes from what one does, which still leaves open whether the palanquin bearer's lot is deserved.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:23","b":"k:46","type":"tension","note":"In 23 those who weigh both paths and take up virtue here stand highest in the world. In 46 the householder has nothing to gain by leaving. Parimelazhagar resolves this by limiting 46 to the forest stage, but the verse doesn't state that limit.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:44","b":"k:322","type":"develops","note":"Sharing food is a household duty here, and in 322 it becomes the chief of all that the authors have listed. What began as one household rule becomes the book's summary of virtue.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:50","b":"k:234","type":"develops","note":"Here a well-lived earthly life earns a place among the gods. In 234 earthly renown goes further, so that the gods' world ceases to praise its own sages. Both measure human achievement against heaven and rate it highly.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:9","b":"k:55","type":"tension","note":"Verse 9 calls a head that does not bow to the Lord's feet useless. Here a woman who does not worship a god is the one whom the rain obeys. Parimelazhagar's added 'other gods' softens the contrast, but the Tamil does not contain it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:18","b":"k:55","type":"develops","note":"In 18, even the gods' worship depends on rain. Here rain answers to a woman who bypasses the gods. Together the two verses invert the expected order of gods, rain and humans.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:51","b":"k:478","type":"parallel_domain","note":"The wife lives within her husband's income, and 478 applies the same rule to the king's treasury: a small inflow is no ruin if outflow doesn't widen. The Kural's household economics and statecraft share one rule.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:57","b":"k:1251","type":"tension","note":"Here நிறை is the guard that outdoes all confinement. In 1251, speaking as a woman in love, desire's axe breaks the door of நிறை barred with shame. The book's ethics section treats inner guard as sufficient, and its love section knows it isn't.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:11","b":"k:64","type":"same_image","note":"Rain is the world's amrita in 11, and in 64 a child's messy gruel beats amrita. The same word is the top of two different scales: what sustains all life, and what is sweet to one parent.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:63","b":"k:377","type":"tension","note":"Parimelazhagar's two comments pull apart. On 63 a son's good deeds reach his parents. On 377 he says the fruit of one soul's deed never goes to another (ch038). The verses themselves don't settle how merit is shared.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:67","b":"k:70","type":"develops","note":"The father's debt and the son's debt form an exchange: he gives the learning that seats the son at the front, and the son gives back a sentence of praise. Parimelazhagar's gloss on 70 makes the link explicit by calling the father 'the one who made him learned'.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:9","b":"k:80","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Both say that a living body can be dead in effect, and Manakkudavar on 9 also says such bodies lack life. The criterion is worship in chapter 1 and love in chapter 8, so the two chapters compete for what makes a person alive.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:71","b":"k:1251","type":"same_image","note":"Both picture a bolted door. Love has no bolt here, and in 1251 desire breaks the door of நிறை that shame has barred. The image passes from the ethics of love to its erotic form, with the same result: it won't stay shut in.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:76","b":"k:773","type":"develops","note":"This strengthens Kalaignar's reading of 76. In 773, the highest courage is fierce, and its sharp edge is kindness to those in need, so the book elsewhere does join courage to kindness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:64","b":"k:82","type":"same_image","note":"Amrita is beaten twice in these household chapters: a child's stirred gruel is sweeter than it, and even it isn't worth having while a guest waits. In both, food's worth is decided by who else is at the meal.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:81","b":"k:212","type":"agrees","note":"Both end in the same phrase, வேளாண்மை செய்தற் பொருட்டு: 81 says the home exists for this, and 212 says wealth earned by effort does. Two different goods are given one purpose.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:89","b":"k:1005","type":"develops","note":"Here refusal of guests is poverty amid plenty. In 1005, someone with crores is destitute if he neither gives nor enjoys. The later verse widens the charge by also treating failure to enjoy one's wealth as a failure.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:90","b":"k:1111","type":"same_image","note":"The anicham measures the guest here and the beloved in 1111, and both are more delicate than it. The book's most tender image for a lover was first used for a stranger at the door.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:84","b":"k:92","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the pair அகனமர்ந்து and முகனமர்ந்து. In 84 the goddess's glad heart answers the host's glad face. In 92 the glad face outranks even the giver's glad heart, so the same pair yields a different ranking.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:91","b":"k:292","type":"tension","note":"Sweet words here must be free of deceit. In 292, even a falsehood counts as truth if it yields blameless good. What 91 excludes by definition, 292 may allow when the outcome justifies it.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:92","b":"k:225","type":"tension","note":"Here a pleasant word beats a gladly given gift. In 225, relieving hunger is a power above the ascetic's. A hungry person's view of 92 might differ, which suggests the ranking in 92 assumes someone who isn't in need.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:95","b":"k:125","type":"qualifies","note":"Parimelazhagar limits humility in 95 to those who deserve deference. In 125, bowing is good for all and most of all for the rich, which speaks to who should bow rather than to whom, and doesn't contain his limit.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"k:97","b":"k:200","type":"agrees","note":"97 requires speech to be useful and gentle, and 200 says to speak only what is useful. The later chapter keeps the usefulness test and drops the sweetness, which shows the two tests are independent.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:99","b":"k:316","type":"parallel_domain","note":"On Parimelazhagar's reading, 99 argues from how sweet words feel to oneself. 316 makes the same move in general: don't do to others what you know to be painful. The same reciprocity works for speech and for deeds.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:62","b":"k:107","type":"same_image","note":"Both use seven births. In 62 a child's character shields the parent, and in 107 memory of a helper persists. Parimelazhagar on 107 refers back to his explanation at 62, so he treats the phrase as one idea.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:87","b":"k:105","type":"agrees","note":"On Kalaignar's reading of 105, both make the recipient the measure of the gift: the guest's worth in 87 and the helped person's character in 105. Parimelazhagar's different reading of 105 separates them.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:103","b":"k:221","type":"agrees","note":"Help without weighing the return is vaster than the sea, and in 221 giving outside the destitute is a kind of expected repayment. The two define real generosity by lack of calculation, from the receiver's and the giver's sides.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:104","b":"k:433","type":"same_image","note":"The same millet and palm. Here others' small kindnesses are enlarged, and in 433 one's own small faults are. The same mental act, turned in opposite directions, gives gratitude and conscience.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:112","b":"k:117","type":"qualifies","note":"112 promises that a fair person's wealth will last, and 117 allows that such a person may fall into low estate. The chapter keeps both. The second stops the first from being a simple reward promise.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:113","b":"k:657","type":"agrees","note":"Gain got by leaving what is fair should be given up, and in 657 even extreme poverty is better for the worthy than wealth got through blame. The later verse states the cost openly.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:115","b":"k:379","type":"agrees","note":"On Parimelazhagar's reading, both rest on past deeds, and each draws a response. 379 asks why one grieves at the bad after accepting the good, and 115 asks that one's heart not bend. The first is about feeling and the second about justice.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:116","b":"k:282","type":"agrees","note":"Parimelazhagar takes 116 to mean that thinking equals doing, and 282 says outright that even thinking of stealing another's goods is wrong. This suggests that the Kural's standard really does reach into thought.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:24","b":"k:126","type":"same_image","note":"The five senses are imagined as animals in both cases. In 24 they're elephants controlled by a goad, and in 126 they're a tortoise's limbs drawn in. One is control by force and the other is retreat to safety.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"k:25","b":"k:124","type":"tension","note":"The renouncer quenches the five in 25. In 124, on Parimelazhagar's reading, the householder is restrained while enjoying the senses and is also called greater than a mountain. They are two different pictures of what restraint is.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:32","b":"k:122","type":"tension","note":"Both say that no gain surpasses this, in the same formula (ஊங்கு), but 32 says it of virtue and 122 of restraint. The superlatives compete, as also in 61 on children, which suggests that the formula praises and doesn't actually rank.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:77","b":"k:130","type":"same_image","note":"In both அறம் acts like a person, and Parimelazhagar calls it a deity in both. In 77 it burns the loveless like the sun, and here it waits for the restrained like a petitioner. It's the same power with opposite manners.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:100","b":"k:129","type":"develops","note":"Harsh speech in 100 is merely a stupid choice of raw fruit. In 129 it leaves a scar that never heals. The first chapter on speech treats the speaker's loss, and this one treats the listener's lasting injury.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:109","b":"k:128","type":"tension","note":"In 109 one good act erases even murderous harm. In 128 one bad word turns all good to nothing. The ledgers are asymmetric, though the verses keep different books: the first is the recipient's memory, and the second, in Parimelazhagar's reading, is the speaker's merit.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:2","b":"k:140","type":"tension","note":"The two ask the same question, what learning is worth, and answer differently. For 2, learning lacks fruit without worship, and for 140 it isn't understanding without fitting conduct in the world. One looks up and the other looks out.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:131","b":"k:327","type":"develops","note":"131 places conduct above life in general. 327 gives a specific case: don't take another's life, even at the cost of your own. It shows what 'guarding it above life' can literally mean.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:133","b":"k:973","type":"agrees","note":"Both separate social rank from worth. 133 says this of conduct and birth, and 973 goes further with the high who aren't high and the low who aren't low. Read together, they show a repeated Kural challenge to status by birth.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:140","b":"k:426","type":"agrees","note":"Both tie understanding to how the world lives. Verse 426 calls it அறிவு to live as the world lives, which is a striking statement beside Parimelazhagar's narrowing of 'world' to the noble in 140.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:25","b":"k:144","type":"same_image","note":"Parimelazhagar uses Indra to illustrate both verses: 25 for the failure that shows the ascetic's power, and 144 for greatness lost through another's wife. This is one story serving two chapters in the commentary, though neither verse tells it.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:80","b":"k:143","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Both call a living person effectively dead. In 80 it's the lack of love, and in 143, as both early commentators say, it's the loss of all three aims. A person can stop being alive in more than one way.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:127","b":"k:150","type":"parallel_domain","note":"These are the two 'at least this' verses: however much else is left unguarded, guard the tongue; however wrong the rest, don't desire another's wife. They show what the book treats as the last line of moral defence.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:148","b":"k:773","type":"qualifies","note":"Both define பேராண்மை, the same word. In 773 it is ferocity in battle whose edge is kindness, and here it is not looking at another's wife. The book redefines the warrior's virtue as self-mastery.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:48","b":"k:160","type":"agrees","note":"Both set a kind of endurance above the ascetic's. 48 says the householder endures more because he also bears others' pain, and 160 says that bearing harsh words comes first. Each reassigns the word நோன்பு or நோன்மை to a life in society.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:57","b":"k:154","type":"parallel_domain","note":"நிறை appears in 57 as a woman's inner guard, and here it's what anyone stands to lose without forbearance. The same inner discipline is asked of women as chastity and of people in general as character.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:89","b":"k:153","type":"same_image","note":"Both call refusal of a guest a kind of poverty. 89 says so directly, and 153 uses it as an analogy for something else, which shows that this was a settled saying that could be drawn on.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:108","b":"k:152","type":"develops","note":"Both say that forgetting is what's best. On Parimelazhagar's reading of 108 it's narrowly about one who has also done good. Verse 152 removes that limit, and gives forgetting a rank above forbearance.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:157","b":"k:314","type":"develops","note":"157 asks for not retaliating, and 314 goes further: punish the wrongdoer by doing him good so that he's ashamed. What starts as restraint becomes a strategy.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:160","b":"k:225","type":"agrees","note":"Two verses, for different reasons, place the ascetic's hunger second. In 160 it's after bearing insults, and in 225 it's after relieving others' hunger. The book keeps finding what's greater than fasting.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:84","b":"k:167","type":"same_image","note":"The same goddess. She settles gladly in the home of the glad-faced host in 84, and here she hands the envious to her sister. Fortune is given a personality that responds to moral character.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:135","b":"k:166","type":"develops","note":"This is Parimelazhagar's own cross-reference. He cites 166 to read 135's simile: as the envious man's kin suffer, so do a man's kin when he lacks conduct. The commentary reads one verse by way of the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:165","b":"k:305","type":"parallel_domain","note":"The same logic is used in two domains: the passion is its own executioner. Envy needs no enemies, and in 305 anger left unguarded kills its owner. The book treats inner states as weapons pointed inward.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:169","b":"k:170","type":"tension","note":"169 says that the crooked prosper and the upright fall, and that this should be pondered. Then 170 says that it never happens. The two stand side by side, which suggests that 170 is a moral claim and that 169 knows what experience shows.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:169","b":"k:375","type":"agrees","note":"Both admit that outcomes don't track merit. 375 says so within the chapter on fate, and 169 only asks for thought, though Parimelazhagar explains it through past deeds. The tension in chapter 38 is here in an earlier and quieter form.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:39","b":"k:173","type":"develops","note":"In 39, joy comes only from virtue. Here that claim is put to work: the 'small joy' of taking others' goods is set against a greater one, and on Parimelazhagar's reading, the greater is the same joy of virtue as 39.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:46","b":"k:176","type":"tension","note":"In 46 there's nothing to gain by leaving household life. Parimelazhagar on 176 calls household life a path toward renunciation, so leaving is the natural end. This commentator's way of ordering the stages conflicts with the plain force of 46.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:112","b":"k:178","type":"agrees","note":"Both promise that wealth lasts on a moral condition: fairness in 112 and not coveting in 178. The second is the first's inward side, since 171 treats coveting as itself a lapse from fairness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:130","b":"k:179","type":"same_image","note":"Virtue in 130 and fortune in 179 both seek out the right person, and they look for the right moment to do so. Parimelazhagar uses செவ்வி in both notes. Moral power is pictured as a courtier waiting for an opening.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:180","b":"k:363","type":"agrees","note":"Not wanting yields victory here, and in 363 it's the choicest wealth in this world and the next. Chapter 18 is about not wanting others' goods, and 363 extends this to not wanting at all.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:34","b":"k:185","type":"agrees","note":"34 says that a clean mind is all of virtue and the rest is noise. 185 supplies the test: if you can't tell whether the mind behind fine moral talk is clean, listen to how the person speaks of others.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:100","b":"k:184","type":"qualifies","note":"100 calls harsh speech a foolish choice when sweet words are available. 184 ranks it above backbiting. The two together suggest a scale of speech: sweet, then harsh and open, then hidden.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:150","b":"k:181","type":"same_image","note":"These are built on the same frame: though one does no virtue and does wrong, at least this one thing. The verses name different things, chastity toward others' wives and not backbiting, so the 'last virtue' isn't a single item.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:151","b":"k:189","type":"same_image","note":"The earth is the model of forbearance in 151, holding up those who dig it. Here it has to talk itself into bearing the backbiter. This suggests that the backbiter does what digging doesn't: he tests even the earth's patience.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:182","b":"k:824","type":"agrees","note":"Both pick out the false smile. In 182 it's the worst part of backbiting, and in 824 the sweet-smiling face over a bitter heart is to be feared. The moral judgment in chapter 19 becomes a practical warning in the book on friendship.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:190","b":"k:436","type":"parallel_domain","note":"These share the word காண்கிற்பின் and the same question about faults. In 190 any person who sees his own faults is safe from harm. In 436 a king who has removed his own and then looks at others' has no fault left. The same self-scrutiny serves an ordinary person and a ruler.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:197","b":"k:100","type":"tension","note":"100 calls harsh speech a raw fruit taken over a ripe one, yet on Kalaignar's reading 197 tolerates unsweet speech before useless speech. Sweetness is not the highest test of a word.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies"},{"a":"k:200","b":"k:291","type":"tension","note":"291 defines truthfulness as speech without harm; 200 demands use. Harmless idle talk passes the one test and fails the other, so the two chapters set different standards for speech.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:200","b":"k:645","type":"develops","note":"645 echoes the same imperative and raises the standard: useful is not enough if some other word could defeat it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:196","b":"k:1071","type":"agrees","note":"Both deny full humanity by appearance alone: chaff has the look of grain, and the base look like people. For the Kural, being human is something a person can fail at.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:199","b":"k:204","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Both use 'even by forgetting', 199 for the speech of the clear-sighted and 204 for plotting harm. The first describes an achieved nature, and the second commands what such a nature would be.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:201","b":"k:1075","type":"tension","note":"201 makes fear the mark of the excellent; 1075 makes fear the conduct of the base. They can be reconciled only by asking what is feared, which suggests that fear of the deed and fear of the penalty are morally different things.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:203","b":"k:314","type":"develops","note":"203 only asks one to refrain from harming an enemy. 314 goes further and makes kindness to the wrongdoer the punishment itself. Prudent restraint becomes active shaming.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:205","b":"k:657","type":"qualifies","note":"205 argues that wrongdoing won't cure poverty. 657 removes that premise: even when wealth does come through blame, the poverty of the worthy is better. Moral refusal doesn't depend on the prediction in 205.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:206","b":"k:316","type":"qualifies","note":"They give the same rule different grounds. 206 rests on what will come back to you, 316 on knowing from your own experience what hurts. One argues from consequence and the other from fellow-feeling.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:207","b":"k:380","type":"same_image","note":"Two pursuers that can't be evaded: the deed follows from behind (பின் சென்று) and fate arrives first (முந்துறும்). Together they close off escape in both directions.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:209","b":"k:305","type":"agrees","note":"Both turn on தன்னைத்தான், making care for the self the argument for restraint: from evil deeds here, from anger in 305.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:210","b":"k:169","type":"tension","note":"210 says the upright man's ruin is absent or rare. 169 admits that this ruin does occur and is something to ponder. The Kural keeps the moral promise and the troubling fact side by side.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:211","b":"k:226","type":"tension","note":"211 insists that obligation looks for no return. 226 commends feeding the destitute as the place to store one's wealth, and Parimelazhagar says it comes back to help the giver. One motive disclaims reward and the other promises it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:216","b":"k:1008","type":"same_image","note":"An exact inversion: the same tree in mid-town, same ripening. In 216 it bears useful fruit; in 1008 it is a poison tree. The chapters on benevolence and on useless wealth share one picture, and only the owner's character changes it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:214","b":"k:80","type":"agrees","note":"Both move the definition of being alive from body to relationship: 80 to love, 214 to knowing what is due. The body without these is bone and skin, or a corpse.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:219","b":"k:230","type":"develops","note":"219 says the good man's poverty is being unable to give. 230 pushes this further: being unable to give makes even death sweet.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:220","b":"k:235","type":"develops","note":"220 only says that ruin from generosity is worth buying. 235 takes up the same word and explains how there can be a ruin that is a gain, and says that only the skilled achieve it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:221","b":"k:212","type":"tension","note":"On Manakkudavar's reading of 212, wealth is for helping those who deserve it. 221 says that true giving goes to those who lack. The worthy recipient and the needy recipient are different tests, and these chapters leave both standing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:222","b":"k:1051","type":"tension","note":"222 calls taking bad even if it were a path to heaven. 1051 permits begging from the right people and puts any blame on the one who refuses. The chapters on giving and begging judge the same act from different standpoints.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:229","b":"k:1066","type":"tension","note":"229 names something worse than begging; 1066 says that nothing disgraces the tongue more than begging, even for a cow's water. Their standards differ: the misery of a life against the shame of an act.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:225","b":"k:261","type":"qualifies","note":"225 places enduring hunger below removing it. 261 defines austerity as endurance plus not harming living things, so the austerity that 225 ranks low is not the fuller ideal of chapter 27.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:228","b":"k:1001","type":"develops","note":"228 asks whether hoarders know the joy they forfeit. 1001 gives the outcome: he dies without eating what he amassed, and nothing remains to be done with it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:233","b":"k:331","type":"tension","note":"233 names renown as the one thing in the world that doesn't die. 331 calls taking what doesn't last for what lasts the lowest kind of knowledge. Chapter 34 never excepts fame, so fame's permanence is exactly what the later chapter puts in doubt.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:231","b":"k:222","type":"tension","note":"222 calls giving good even with no reward in any world. 231 calls giving, with renown, the sole profit of a life. The same act is valued for itself in one and as a gain in the other.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:238","b":"k:114","type":"same_image","note":"Both rest on எச்சம், what is left behind. 114 reads a person's worth from what remains after them, and 238 makes renown the remainder that everyone should leave.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:239","b":"k:1040","type":"same_image","note":"The earth is morally responsive in both: it yields less under a body without fame, and laughs at those who sit idle pleading poverty. Land is more than a backdrop to human conduct in the Kural.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:240","b":"k:214","type":"agrees","note":"The same reclassification of the living as dead, with different tests: renown in 240, knowing what is due in 214. The chapters on benevolence and fame are linked as cause and result.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:241","b":"k:411","type":"tension","note":"The same formula, செல்வத்துட் செல்வம், crowns different things: அருள் here and hearing in 411. Both can't be 'the' wealth of wealths unless the phrase is a rhetorical form awarded in context, a caution against reading any single chapter as the Kural's ultimate ranking.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:247","b":"k:751","type":"develops","note":"247 mentions as an aside that this world is not for the penniless. 751 makes that its full subject: nothing but wealth makes nobodies into somebodies. The section on virtue quietly grants the premise of the section on wealth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:248","b":"k:250","type":"tension","note":"248 says those without அருள் are finished and can't become anything. 250, which Parimelazhagar calls the means of arousing அருள், assumes that the person going against the weak can still acquire it. The verdict is absolute, and the very next step offers a way out.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:249","b":"k:34","type":"agrees","note":"34 says stainlessness of mind is the whole of virtue and all else is show. 249 makes the same point about compassion: an outwardly right act without it does not count.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:250","b":"k:318","type":"agrees","note":"Both derive compassion from one's own experience: 250 remembers fear before the strong, and 318 asks how one who knows pain in his own life could inflict it. The Kural's ethics of harm consistently starts from self-knowledge and not from rules.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:253","b":"k:774","type":"tension","note":"253 uses the weapon-holder as the model of a mind closed to good. 774 celebrates a warrior who plucks a spear from his own body and laughs. The Kural honours in the section on polity the same figure that it distrusts in the section on virtue.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:256","b":"k:329","type":"develops","note":"329 condemns killers as base. 256 extends the guilt to the customer who keeps them in business, so the customer can't look down on the butcher.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:259","b":"k:88","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Both move the worth of sacrifice elsewhere: 88 calls hospitality the வேள்வி that misers miss, and 259 ranks not killing above a thousand sacrifices. The Kural repeatedly tests ritual by how others are treated.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:260","b":"k:268","type":"same_image","note":"Nearly the same ending: all living beings worship. In 260 the object is one who doesn't kill and doesn't eat flesh; in 268 it is one who has mastered, or on another reading lost, his own self. One route looks outward and the other inward.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:263","b":"k:225","type":"tension","note":"225 puts the strength of enduring hunger behind that of removing it, which favours the giver. Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar draw from 263 that தவம் exceeds giving. Whatever the verses themselves imply, the commentary tradition faces a real conflict here.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:264","b":"k:203","type":"tension","note":"203 calls refraining from harm even to enemies the height of understanding, and 261 defines austerity by harmlessness. Yet 264 advertises the power to crush foes. Parimelazhagar's 'enemies of virtue' and his comment that ascetics don't think of it are attempts to close this gap.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:266","b":"k:46","type":"tension","note":"46 asks what a virtuous householder could gain by leaving for the other path. 266 says that those not doing தவம் are doing futile things. The chapters on householding and austerity each claim the highest place.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:265","b":"k:370","type":"tension","note":"265 recommends austerity because it gets whatever one wants. 370 says the unchanging state comes from removing desire. The means is sold by appeal to the very thing the end abolishes.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:268","b":"k:346","type":"agrees","note":"On Manakkudavar's reading of 268, its subject has lost the ego, which is exactly the cutting of 'I' and 'mine' in 346. Parimelazhagar's 'possessing oneself' would make the link weaker.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:269","b":"k:326","type":"agrees","note":"Two different conquests of death: by the power of austerity in 269, and by the vow of not killing in 326, where death's advance doesn't reach. The Kural offers an ethical route alongside the ascetic one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:270","b":"k:377","type":"tension","note":"270 explains poverty as too little austerity, which is something people could change. 377 says enjoyment is only as the apportioner apportioned. The same fact gets a moral cause and a fatal one.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:272","b":"k:293","type":"agrees","note":"Both make one's own heart the witness: 272 says that an outward show can't help when the heart knows the fault, and 293 says the heart will burn the one who lies against what it knows. Self-knowledge is the Kural's court of last resort.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:278","b":"k:34","type":"develops","note":"278's மனத்தது மாசாக is the exact opposite of 34's மனத்துக்கண் மாசிலன். 34 states the principle that all else is show, and 278 gives the social picture of the show in practice.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:279","b":"k:827","type":"same_image","note":"827 warns that a bowing enemy's words are like a bow's bend, which signals harm. 279 says the lute's curve is straight and the arrow's straightness is cruel. So the curve is a warning in one verse and no evidence at all in the other, which supports 279's point that shape can't be read directly.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:275","b":"k:88","type":"same_image","note":"The same utterance appears in both: in 88, the miser who fed no guests later says பற்றற்றேம் as a lament for lost support, and in 275 the hypocrite says it as a boast of non-attachment. Both are words that the speaker's life makes hollow.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:282","b":"k:204","type":"agrees","note":"Both move the offence to the planning mind: 204 says that merely plotting ruin sets virtue plotting in return, and 282 says that merely thinking of theft is wrong. Parimelazhagar reads the standard in 282 as one for renunciants, while 204 has no such limit.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:283","b":"k:479","type":"same_image","note":"Both describe prosperity that seems to exist and then vanishes: stolen gain 'as if growing' in 283, and in 479 the life of one who doesn't live by measure, which seems to exist and is gone. Both also turn on அளவு, so here the moral and the economic meanings of 'measure' meet.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:283","b":"k:659","type":"agrees","note":"659 says that what is taken with tears goes with tears. Together they make the same bet about ill-gotten gain, but 283 stresses its illusion of growth and 659 stresses its justice.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:300","b":"k:323","type":"tension","note":"300 finds nothing better than truthfulness, while 323 puts not killing first and truthfulness right behind it. The Kural declares its rankings chapter by chapter, and here it comes close to correcting itself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:299","b":"k:753","type":"tension","note":"Almost the same phrase is applied to opposite things: பொய்யா விளக்கு is truthfulness here, and 753 calls wealth the பொய்யா விளக்கம் that cuts through darkness. The section on wealth deliberately takes over the language of the section on virtue.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:292","b":"k:297","type":"tension","note":"297 prizes unbroken not-lying, while 292 admits falsehood under a condition. They are consistent only given 291's redefinition of truth by effect, which shows how much that definition is carrying.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:295","b":"k:263","type":"develops","note":"225 favours giving over austerity, the commentators on 263 favour austerity over giving, and 295 now sets truth above both. The chapters rank a shifting set of virtues rather than a fixed ladder.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:298","b":"k:278","type":"agrees","note":"278 shows the bathers with impure minds, and 298 states the principle that this water only reaches the body. Read together, the chapter on hypocrisy provides the case for this general claim.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:301","b":"k:250","type":"develops","note":"Both are about the moment of having power over someone weaker. 250 offers a memory that stops you: your own fear before the strong. 301 says that only at this moment does restraint count as virtue at all.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:305","b":"k:264","type":"tension","note":"264 credits austerity with the power to crush foes. On Parimelazhagar's reading of 305, the ascetic who uses this power in a curse loses his austerity and returns to the misery of births. The power is real, but using it destroys its source.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:304","b":"k:786","type":"same_image","note":"Both distinguish the laughter of the face from that of the heart. 786 says that friendship that smiles only on the face isn't friendship, and 304 says anger kills both, so a smile alone isn't what is at stake.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:308","b":"k:157","type":"agrees","note":"Both ask for restraint under extreme provocation. 157 goes further and grounds it in pity for the wrongdoer's coming fall, whereas 308 offers no reason, only the admission that it is hard.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:309","b":"k:265","type":"agrees","note":"The same promise, that what one wants arrives as one wants it, is made for austerity in 265 and for non-anger in 309. On this comparison, dropping anger can do the work of austerity.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:314","b":"k:550","type":"tension","note":"Both use ஒறுத்தல், punish. 314 says to punish wrongdoers by shaming them with kindness; 550 says that the king punishing the worst of them with death is simply weeding a crop. The word keeps its form while the ethics of person and state divide.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:315","b":"k:203","type":"agrees","note":"Both test அறிவு by conduct toward others: 203 calls not harming enemies its peak, and 315 says it comes to nothing without guarding others from pain. Understanding here is measured morally rather than by what one knows.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:319","b":"k:207","type":"develops","note":"207 says that the enmity of deeds can't be escaped. 319 adds speed: by the same afternoon. One closes off every direction and the other closes off time.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:330","b":"k:1062","type":"tension","note":"330 reports that the diseased and destitute are paying for their killings. 1062 rages that if people must beg to live, the world's maker should perish. One explains misery as deserved, and the other refuses to accept it at all.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:330","b":"k:320","type":"develops","note":"320 says that all pain rests on those who caused pain, looking forward. 330 turns this around and reads a past killing from present suffering.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:327","b":"k:550","type":"tension","note":"327 forbids taking life even to save one's own. 550 likens the king's execution of murderers to weeding a crop. Here the Kural's most absolute rule meets its statecraft, and it does not reconcile them.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:328","b":"k:259","type":"qualifies","note":"259 explicitly mentions sacrifice but only of oblations, and sets not killing above it. 328 doesn't mention sacrifice, but the commentators find animal sacrifice there. That shows how a commentary tradition can place a controversy in the text.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"k:329","b":"k:972","type":"agrees","note":"972 says that birth is alike for all and distinction comes from the work done. 329 applies this to killing: base by occupation. The commentators' emphasis on 'by work' follows the same principle.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:333","b":"k:753","type":"tension","note":"333 says wealth is by nature unstaying; 753 calls it the unfailing lamp that drives out darkness wherever one intends. The chapter on impermanence and the chapter on acquiring wealth describe the same thing in opposite terms.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:333","b":"k:226","type":"agrees","note":"Both tell the owner of unstable wealth where to put it. 226 names the place: the hunger of the destitute. 333 explains why it must be done immediately.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:334","b":"k:257","type":"same_image","note":"Both end உணர்வார்ப் பெறின், 'if those who perceive it could be found'. Flesh as a wound, and a day as a blade: in each, the ordinary hides violence, and the verse doubts that many will see it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:335","b":"k:36","type":"agrees","note":"36 says not to put off virtue until later, since it is a companion that doesn't die when you die. 335 supplies the concrete deadline: before the hiccup stops the tongue.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:340","b":"k:345","type":"develops","note":"340 asks gently whether the lodger has no home of its own. 345 draws the conclusion for one who wants to leave for good: even the lodging is too much, let alone other ties.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:341","b":"k:344","type":"tension","note":"341 says each thing given up removes that thing's pain, a piecemeal gain. 344 says keeping even one thing brings it all back. Parimelazhagar accepts both, one as a gain and the other as a risk, but the practical advice differs.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies"},{"a":"k:343","b":"k:1101","type":"tension","note":"343 demands that the domain of the five senses be crushed. 1101 finds all five senses' delights gathered in the beloved. The third book takes what renunciation would destroy and fulfils it in one person.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:344","b":"k:1041","type":"tension","note":"இன்மை, having nothing, is natural and right for austerity in 344. In 1041, poverty, the same word, is what nothing else can match for misery. The word changes value completely with who has chosen it.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:348","b":"k:50","type":"qualifies","note":"50 places the good householder among the gods, and 346 and 348 put full renouncers beyond them and everyone else in the net. Parimelazhagar's ladder (fame and heaven for householders, release for renouncers) is how the commentary fits these together.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:350","b":"k:7","type":"develops","note":"7 says that only those who join the feet of the incomparable one can be rid of grief. 350 repeats the structure with attachment. Parimelazhagar himself notes that the generic word is kept to match the invocation chapter.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:355","b":"k:423","type":"same_image","note":"Their second lines are identical. 423 applies it to what we are told: whoever says it, find the truth in it. 355 applies it to what things are: whatever their appearance, find the truth in them. The same habit of mind is both critical thinking and metaphysics.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:354","b":"k:343","type":"qualifies","note":"343 demands crushing the domain of the five senses. 354, on Kalaignar's reading, says that this gets you nothing without true perception. The disciplined renouncer of chapter 35 isn't the goal in itself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:353","b":"k:242","type":"qualifies","note":"For Parimelazhagar, the schools conflict on everything in 242 and yet converge on அருள். On 353, he says that their conflict leaves doubt only experience can settle. Together they give his view of religious pluralism: agreement in ethics, and no resolution by argument in metaphysics.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:356","b":"k:373","type":"tension","note":"356 makes learning (கற்று) the route to seeing the true thing. 373 says however much one studies, one's own understanding prevails. Chapter 38 thus undercuts the very means this one recommends.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:360","b":"k:35","type":"develops","note":"35 defines virtue as conduct free of envy, craving, anger and harsh words. 360 lists desire, anger and confusion, whose ending ends pain. Anger and desire appear in both lists, and the shift to confusion in 360 marks the shift from conduct to knowledge.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:199","b":"k:352","type":"same_image","note":"Both describe those of மாசறு காட்சி who are free of மருள். 352 says what it does for them; 199 says what it looks like from outside: they don't utter a meaningless word even by accident.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:362","b":"k:350","type":"same_image","note":"Both solve the same problem by the same paradox: you can't leave desire by force, so desire is used against itself. 350 holds to the one without holding, and 362 wants to be without wanting.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:363","b":"k:400","type":"tension","note":"விழுச்செல்வம் names not wanting here and learning in 400. Taken with 241 and 411, the Kural has awarded the title of true wealth to at least four things, and that suggests the phrase is a rhetorical form and not a doctrine.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:364","b":"k:298","type":"develops","note":"298 says inner purity comes through truth. 364 gives the missing link: purity is the absence of craving, and wanting truth brings it. On Parimelazhagar's view, the meaning of truth has shifted from speech to the Supreme.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:361","b":"k:351","type":"qualifies","note":"351 traces birth to confusion and 361 traces it to craving, and both claim to be the root. Parimelazhagar makes them a sequence, with ignorance first and craving following, but the verses each say it singly.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:366","b":"k:201","type":"agrees","note":"Virtue as a kind of fear: in 201 the excellent fear evil deeds, and in 366 what is to be feared is craving, which is behind those deeds. It is the same moral fear moved one step closer to its source.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:368","b":"k:320","type":"tension","note":"320 says all pain rests on those who caused pain, and 368 says sorrow comes from craving and doesn't come to those without it. Two single-source explanations of suffering, one ethical and one psychological, sit a few chapters apart.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:370","b":"k:380","type":"tension","note":"Just ten verses apart, this one ends the section on renunciation by promising a nature that can't be shifted, once craving goes. 380 asks what is stronger than fate, which gets there first. The Kural moves from the highest claim for human release straight to the strongest claim against human power.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:365","b":"k:226","type":"same_image","note":"அற்றார் are the destitute in 226, to be fed, and the freed in 365, to be revered. The same word for lacking marks both the most pitiable person and the most complete one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:371","b":"k:620","type":"tension","note":"Here effort is fate's product. In 620, effort puts fate behind you. The tension is sharper than one between effort and fate as rivals, because 371 removes effort as an independent force.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:373","b":"k:396","type":"tension","note":"Here one's own understanding prevails over study. In 396, knowledge flows in proportion to study.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:374","b":"k:400","type":"qualifies","note":"374 separates wealth and wisdom as different fates, and 400 goes further by calling learning the only true wealth. Read together, they redefine wealth.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:379","b":"k:38","type":"develops","note":"In Parimelazhagar's reading both rest on deeds and births: 379 accepts the fruit of past deeds, and 38 stops future births.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:382","b":"k:428","type":"qualifies","note":"The king must never lack அஞ்சாமை, yet 428 calls not fearing what should be feared folly. Read together, kingly fearlessness can't mean fearing nothing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:383","b":"k:672","type":"qualifies","note":"தூங்காமை is a permanent royal quality here. 672 uses the same word to divide tasks: some should be slept on and some should not. The trait becomes a judgment about each case.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:383","b":"k:467","type":"develops","note":"467 fixes the order, deliberate and then resolve, which supports Manakkudavar's reading of துணிவு here and sits uneasily with Parimelazhagar's action without regard to anything.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:386","b":"k:565","type":"agrees","note":"The negative of the same pair. Being hard to see and harsh of look turns great wealth into what a demon has found: 386 gives the praise, 565 the horror.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:388","b":"k:50","type":"parallel_domain","note":"The same verb, வைக்கப்படும், raises a just ruler to இறை and a rightly living householder among the gods. Both are placed there by how they live, one in public and one at home.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:389","b":"k:448","type":"develops","note":"389 asks the king to bear bitter words; 448 says what follows if no one speaks them: he falls with no enemy needed. Parimelazhagar's gloss on 389 uses the same idea of இடிக்கும் துணை.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:391","b":"k:140","type":"develops","note":"391 demands standing by what is learned; 140 gives the failure's verdict: those who have learned much but can't act in accord with the world are unlearned and ignorant. Conduct can cancel the very category of learned.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:393","b":"k:575","type":"same_image","note":"Both turn eyes into sores, but by different tests: lack of learning here and lack of கண்ணோட்டம், kindly regard, in 575. One verse grounds sight in knowledge and the other in compassion.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:398","b":"k:409","type":"develops","note":"In Parimelazhagar's reading, the two make one argument across chapters: learning travels with the soul through births, while caste rank ends with the body. That is why 409 can rank learning over birth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:396","b":"k:416","type":"tension","note":"396 makes understanding strictly proportional to study. In Parimelazhagar's reading of 416, even a small hearing gives full greatness. Reading gains by degree and listening at once.","grade":"wrong","better":null},{"a":"k:404","b":"k:373","type":"tension","note":"404 denies that native brightness counts without learning; 373 says a person's own understanding prevails over any amount of study. On one reading, the Kural both dismisses innate understanding and calls it unbeatable.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:408","b":"k:1000","type":"agrees","note":"Both treat wealth as worse for being held by the wrong person, but 1000 faults character and 408 faults learning. 1000's spoilt milk also shows the damage to the good thing, not only to others.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:409","b":"k:972","type":"tension","note":"409 uses high and low birth as a working scale, even to overturn it. 972 denies the scale at the root: birth is the same for all beings, and only deeds differ. The two arguments against birth rank are different in kind.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:410","b":"k:414","type":"qualifies","note":"After a chapter that places the unlearned among beasts, 414 opens a door: though you haven't studied, listen. The next chapter quietly withdraws the finality of this one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:411","b":"k:241","type":"tension","note":"The same formula, செல்வத்துள் செல்வம், is given to the wealth of the ear here and to the wealth of grace in 241. 400 adds learning. The Kural's chapters each crown their own wealth, which raises the question of how they rank.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:415","b":"k:414","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the leaning staff, but 414 has listening as the staff and 415 the speaker's conduct. Read together, the staff only holds if the one speaking has conduct.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:419","b":"k:978","type":"develops","note":"978 says greatness bows while smallness vaunts. 419 gives that a source: the bowing mouth comes from listening, so 978's humility is partly a product of hearing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:423","b":"k:355","type":"develops","note":"Nearly the same second line. 355 tests a thing regardless of how it appears; 423 tests it regardless of who says it. Together they strip away both of the surfaces that mislead: appearance and authority.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:423","b":"k:415","type":"tension","note":"423 sets aside the speaker when judging what is said. 415 rests the value of words on the speaker's conduct, and Parimelazhagar says those lacking it shouldn't be heard. Who says it either doesn't matter or matters most.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:426","b":"k:423","type":"tension","note":"In the same chapter, 423 demands testing every claim for yourself and 426 says live as the world lives. The tension is softened if உலகம் means the worthy, as Manakkudavar has it, but the verse doesn't say so.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:429","b":"k:380","type":"tension","note":"380 says fate arrives first even when one devises another way; 429 says foresight spares one from being shaken. Manakkudavar faces this directly, allowing that early guarding softens even what comes from past deeds.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:430","b":"k:374","type":"tension","note":"374 separates being wealthy from being wise. In Parimelazhagar's gloss on 430, all wealth is made and kept by understanding, while his gloss on 374 draws the conclusion that wealth doesn't need wisdom as its cause.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:421","b":"k:434","type":"develops","note":"Both turn on அற்றம். Understanding is the inner fort against it, and one's own fault is the enemy that brings it. So the attacker and the defence are both within.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:433","b":"k:104","type":"same_image","note":"Millet and palmyra both times: 104 inflates a small favour received, and 433 a small fault committed. The same discipline of scale runs in both directions, gratitude for others and severity with oneself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:436","b":"k:190","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Same shape and the same காண்கிற்பின். 190 asks anyone to see their own faults as they see others', and 436 makes the order a condition for a ruler who judges.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:432","b":"k:384","type":"qualifies","note":"384 requires மானம் of a king; 432 forbids மானம் that has passed beyond dignity. The same word marks a virtue and a fault, and Parimelazhagar explicitly separates them in both places.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:440","b":"k:431","type":"tension","note":"431 praises those without desire, and 440 tells one how to enjoy desire safely. Parimelazhagar resolves this by making the fault excess in 431, but the verses themselves pull apart. Removing and hiding are different programmes.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:444","b":"k:471","type":"qualifies","note":"444 ranks support as the chief of strengths. 471 lists it as one of four to weigh before acting, alongside the task and the opponent. The superlative here gets absorbed into a calculation there.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:448","b":"k:434","type":"develops","note":"434 calls one's own fault the only enemy that brings ruin. 448 shows the mechanism: without those who rebuke, no outside enemy is needed. Self-correction turns out to be something a person needs other people for.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:454","b":"k:373","type":"tension","note":"373 says a person's own understanding prevails over all study; 454 says understanding only seems to be one's own and lies in one's company. These are two different accounts of where knowledge comes from, within or among others, and they don't fit together easily.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:455","b":"k:34","type":"tension","note":"34 makes a spotless mind the whole of virtue. 455 makes that purity lean on purity of company. If so, the root of virtue in 34 has a root of its own outside the person.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:456","b":"k:114","type":"agrees","note":"114 says worthiness is seen by one's எச்சம், and 456 says purity of mind produces a good one. The same disputed word closes the loop between what is inward and what is left behind.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:451","b":"k:428","type":"develops","note":"428 says the knowing fear what should be feared. 451 names one such object, a surprising one: not a danger, but a social circle. It only makes sense given 452's claim that company changes understanding.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:463","b":"k:449","type":"same_image","note":"Both use முதல், capital, from the merchant's world. 449 applies it to allies and 463 to risk: a king's ledger has two different kinds of principal that he can't afford to lose.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:470","b":"k:656","type":"agrees","note":"656 forbids what the worthy condemn even if one's mother is starving, the most extreme form of Manakkudavar's reading here, that being able to succeed doesn't excuse scorned means.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:469","b":"k:464","type":"develops","note":"464 says to avoid beginning what isn't clear. 469 adds that even with the deed and intent clear, the person is another thing to know. Clarity about the act is not clarity about the recipient.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:480","b":"k:220","type":"tension","note":"220 says if helpfulness brings ruin, it is worth buying even by selling oneself. 480 says helpfulness that ignores one's means quickly destroys wealth. Aram and Porul give opposite counsel on the same act, and 480 uses the same word, ஒப்புரவு.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:473","b":"k:595","type":"tension","note":"595 says a person's height rises with their spirit, as a lotus stem with the water. 473 says many have been broken by exactly that spirit, ஊக்கம், when it wasn't measured. One counts spirit as the measure and the other demands a measure for spirit.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies"},{"a":"k:479","b":"k:283","type":"same_image","note":"Prosperity that perishes while appearing to exist or grow. 283 blames fraud and 479 blames not knowing the measure, so honest wealth and stolen wealth can fail in the same deceptive way.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:477","b":"k:385","type":"develops","note":"385 ends the royal wealth cycle in distribution. 477 sets the limit on that distribution. Parimelazhagar explicitly connects them, sending the reader back to 385 for the way of giving.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:482","b":"k:376","type":"tension","note":"376 says what isn't yours won't stay however you guard it. 482 offers timing as the rope that keeps fortune from leaving. One denies that wealth can be held by care, the other names the means of holding it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:487","b":"k:303","type":"tension","note":"303 says to forget anger toward anyone, because evil is born of it. 487 keeps anger alive in secret until it can be used. The Aram book treats anger as something to give up, while the Porul book treats it as something to store.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:488","b":"k:828","type":"develops","note":"488 advises bowing to an enemy until he can be struck. 828 warns that a weapon is hidden in an enemy's folded hands. The Kural gives the technique and the defence against it, from either side.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:485","b":"k:471","type":"qualifies","note":"471 says act after weighing strengths. Parimelazhagar's 485 says that even with greater strength, wait for the time. So a favourable reckoning from 471 still isn't enough.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:495","b":"k:481","type":"parallel_domain","note":"The crow beats the owl by day and the crocodile wins in water. Time and place are two axes of the same argument: strength isn't an attribute of a creature but of a creature in a circumstance.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:493","b":"k:471","type":"tension","note":"In Parimelazhagar's reading, 471 says to act only if your side is stronger. 493 says the weak can win by choosing the ground. If so, the calculation of 471 must already have place in it, or 471's rule is too strict.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies"},{"a":"k:497","b":"k:444","type":"tension","note":"444 calls greater people as support the chief of all strengths; 497 says no support is needed but fearlessness, if the thinking and place are right. Five chapters later, the book seems to qualify its own superlative.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:500","b":"k:599","type":"same_image","note":"A great elephant is undone in both: by a tiger's spirit in 599 and by a jackal in the mud here. One verse credits the attacker's ஊக்கம் and the other only the ground, so size loses for two quite different reasons.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:502","b":"k:409","type":"tension","note":"409 ranks the learned low-born over the unlearned high-born. 502 makes good birth the first condition for trust. The two can be squared, but the book's position on birth clearly depends on what is being asked.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:506","b":"k:349","type":"tension","note":"349 has rebirth cut only when பற்று is gone. On Parimelazhagar's reading, 506 won't trust anyone without பற்று. The same absence is liberation in Aram and a security risk in Porul.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:504","b":"k:433","type":"develops","note":"433 treats one's own millet-sized fault as a palm. 504 takes others on balance, by what predominates. Read together, the Kural sets a different standard for judging oneself and judging others.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:505","b":"k:972","type":"agrees","note":"972 says standing differs because of deeds, and 505 makes deeds the touchstone. Together they offer a test of worth that doesn't refer to birth, and this is at odds with 502, three verses earlier.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:510","b":"k:791","type":"parallel_domain","note":"791 says of friendship what 510 says of ministers: examine first, because afterwards there is no way out. Both make trust something with a fixed before and after.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:520","b":"k:510","type":"tension","note":"510 says doubting one you have trusted brings endless trouble. 520 says inspect him every day. Parimelazhagar sees this and calls for private inspection, but the problem of watching without doubting remains.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:514","b":"k:501","type":"qualifies","note":"501 builds an elaborate test and, in Parimelazhagar's words, passing shows the person won't change. 514 says that many change anyway, through the work itself. The most careful selection can't test for what office will do.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:512","b":"k:385","type":"parallel_domain","note":"The king creates, gathers, guards and apportions in 385; the officer enlarges, increases and watches in 512. Parimelazhagar connects them. The servant's job is a copy of the master's, without the giving.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:513","b":"k:507","type":"qualifies","note":"507 condemns trusting on fondness alone. 513 puts love first among four qualities, so the fault in 507 lies in the 'alone', not in the love.","grade":"wrong","better":null},{"a":"k:523","b":"k:215","type":"same_image","note":"Both show wealth as a full tank. In 215 it is a public pond serving all, and in 523 it has no banks. The first image is about access, the second about containment: wealth has to be held to be shared.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:524","b":"k:212","type":"tension","note":"212 says all wealth earned exists to serve the worthy. 524 says its fruit is being surrounded by kin. Both state the purpose of wealth, with different beneficiaries, and neither mentions the other.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:521","b":"k:796","type":"qualifies","note":"521 declares that kin stay in adversity. 796 calls adversity a measuring rod for kin, which assumes some won't stay. A generalisation about kin in 53 becomes a test of them in 80.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:531","b":"k:305","type":"tension","note":"305 says anger kills the one who has it. In Parimelazhagar's argument for 531, anger at least sometimes reaches the enemy, while elated forgetting only kills oneself. The Aram book's worst self-destroyer gets demoted.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:532","b":"k:408","type":"tension","note":"In Parimelazhagar's reasoning on 408, poverty doesn't ruin the good. 532 makes chronic poverty the very image of what destroys understanding. Either understanding survives poverty, or it doesn't.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:534","b":"k:421","type":"develops","note":"421 builds an inner fort of understanding. 534 shows an outer fort falling to inner fear. Together they say that a fortress is finally a state of mind, which is unusual in a Porul section that counts forts as a limb of the state.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:537","b":"k:483","type":"develops","note":"'Nothing is hard' appears with different conditions: 462 counsel, 472 knowing strength, 483 means and time, and here attention. The refrain's repetition suggests that no single one suffices, and each chapter has claimed the last word.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:540","b":"k:666","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Same structure: what one has in mind is attained, if. 666's condition is firmness and 540's is continued remembering. They might be one quality seen from the will and from attention.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:550","b":"k:327","type":"tension","note":"327 says don't take another's life even if it costs your own. 550 says the king's killing of the cruel is like weeding. The book's sharpest split between Aram and Porul: an absolute rule meets the role of king. Parimelazhagar resolves it through caste duty, which the verses don't mention.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:541","b":"k:578","type":"tension","note":"541 makes justice not showing கண்ணோட்டம் to anyone. 578 gives the world to those able to show it without harming the work. So the king both must and must not, and 578's condition, the work unharmed, is the hinge.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies"},{"a":"k:541","b":"k:528","type":"tension","note":"528 tells a king not to look at all alike but by rank; 541 says to show no favour to anyone. Household management and the court of justice have different requirements, though they are only two chapters apart.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:542","b":"k:11","type":"parallel_domain","note":"The chapter on rain calls it the world's nectar because it keeps the world going. 542 gives the sceptre that same place for human society, which is why the failure of one can later cause the other in 559.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:543","b":"k:30","type":"develops","note":"30 defines அந்தணர் by compassion toward all life, and not by birth. If that is carried here, 543's அந்தணர் நூல் need not be the Veda, and this gives Kalaignar's reading a basis inside the Kural.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:546","b":"k:761","type":"tension","note":"761 calls a fearless army the chief of a king's assets. 546 says it isn't the spear but the sceptre that gives victory. Porul itself has two answers to what wins wars, one in its justice chapter and one in its army chapter.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:552","b":"k:546","type":"same_image","note":"546 says it isn't the spear but the sceptre that wins. 552 collapses the difference: a sceptre that asks is a spear. The same pair, வேல் and கோல், marks the line between justice and its corruption.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:551","b":"k:550","type":"develops","note":"550 has a king kill the cruel as weeds. The very next verse calls an oppressive king crueller than killers. My reading: read together, the two raise the question 550 leaves open, of who weeds the weeder, and the chapter's answer is tears, rain and time.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:555","b":"k:659","type":"agrees","note":"659 says what was taken with tears departs with tears. 555 makes the same moral arithmetic political. 659 is a personal warning about money, and here the crying of a whole people is the weapon against a throne.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:558","b":"k:1041","type":"tension","note":"1041 says nothing is more painful than poverty except poverty itself. 558 names something more painful: wealth under a tyrant. The later chapter's superlative doesn't hold in this one's political conditions.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:559","b":"k:545","type":"agrees","note":"A mirror: just rule brings rain and harvest in 545, and bent rule stops the rain in 559. Kalaignar rationalises both, a comparison in one and reservoirs in the other, because the older reading asks a modern reader to believe in moral weather.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:560","b":"k:543","type":"develops","note":"543 makes the sceptre the origin of books and virtue, and 560 shows the effect of its absence: the books are forgotten. Parimelazhagar's chain then connects these to rain through sacrifice, which ties 11, 543 and 559 into one circle.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:549","b":"k:567","type":"qualifies","note":"549 clears the king: punishing crime is no blemish but his work. 567 marks where that clearance ends, since punishment past the limit becomes a file on his own strength.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:565","b":"k:386","type":"develops","note":"386 promises praise to the king who is easy to see and not harsh of speech. 565 and 566 cost out the opposite, turning two virtues into two ways of losing wealth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:569","b":"k:382","type":"qualifies","note":"382 lists fearlessness as a king's nature. 569 suggests that it also has a material condition: without prepared defences, even a king comes to fear.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:570","b":"k:151","type":"same_image","note":"The earth in 151 patiently bears even those who dig it, the model of forbearance. In 570 that same earth finds one burden too heavy, which gives the hyperbole its force.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:578","b":"k:541","type":"tension","note":"541 defines justice partly as not extending கண்ணோட்டம் (ஓர்ந்துகண் ணோடாது). This chapter says the world depends on it. 578 is the hinge, and Parimelazhagar cites 541 there: the same grace is the condition of the world and a corruption of justice, depending on where it falls.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies"},{"a":"k:578","b":"k:580","type":"tension","note":"578 forbids kindness that damages the work, and 580 praises kindness that swallows poison. Parimelazhagar resolves this by confining forbearance to wrongs against oneself, but the verses themselves don't state that limit.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:572","b":"k:570","type":"same_image","note":"Adjacent chapters use the same phrase, நிலக்குப் பொறை, for two different faults: the alliance of harshness with ignorance, and the mere lack of kindly regard. The second is far milder and yet receives the same verdict.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:575","b":"k:393","type":"same_image","note":"Both call failed eyes wounds. In 393 the eye is failed by lack of learning, and in 575 by lack of kindly regard. Taken together, an eye must both know and regard, and neither alone saves it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:576","b":"k:1020","type":"same_image","note":"If Manakkudavar's reading of 576 is right, both verses use the wooden doll for a person hollow inside: 1020 lacks shame and 576 lacks regard. On Parimelazhagar's reading the image is a rooted tree, and this parallel fades.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:580","b":"k:807","type":"agrees","note":"807 says those of long affection don't give it up even when ruined by friends. 580 gives the same stance its most extreme image. If Parimelazhagar is right that the poisoner is an associate, the poison verse belongs to the same ethic as the chapter on old friendship.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:581","b":"k:392","type":"same_image","note":"Both name two things as a person's eyes. 392 gives every living person numbers and letters. 581 gives a ruler spies and the treatise. Each makes sight depend on something outside the body.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:581","b":"k:577","type":"tension","note":"In adjacent chapters a ruler's eye is first the grace that spares and then the instrument that watches everyone, kin included (584). The Kural does not reconcile the two eyes, and 578 is the nearest it comes to a rule between them.","grade":"forced","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:586","b":"k:274","type":"tension","note":"274 condemns hiding behind ascetic practice as a hunter hides in a bush to trap birds. 586 prescribes exactly that kind of cover for the state's spy, on Manakkudavar's reading at least. The Virtue book's hypocrisy becomes the Wealth book's technique.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:589","b":"k:423","type":"parallel_domain","note":"423 tells the individual to find the truth whoever is speaking. 589 turns the same refusal to trust a source into a state procedure, with independence and counting as its method.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:594","b":"k:371","type":"tension","note":"Both turn on the same word. In 594 wealth seeks out அசைவிலா energy. In 371 அசைவின்மை is itself produced by a fate of gain. The causal arrow reverses, so the question becomes whether steadfastness is one's own at all.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:597","b":"k:599","type":"same_image","note":"The elephant carries opposite morals three verses apart: under arrows it models unbreakable resolve, and before a tiger it is bulk without resolve. Parimelazhagar accepts this by locating the difference in the tiger's energy.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:598","b":"k:613","type":"develops","note":"Both speak of the pride of giving (செருக்கு) and both root it in exertion. 613 names the vocabulary of labour and liberality that 598 only implies, so generosity is shown to have an economic foundation.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:592","b":"k:400","type":"tension","note":"The Kural keeps naming the one true wealth: learning in 400, what is heard in 411, grace in 241, and energy here. Each dismisses ordinary wealth, but they cannot all be the sole true possession. Each is best read as a chapter's rhetorical peak.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:600","b":"k:576","type":"same_image","note":"Tree-people appear for two different lacks: kindly regard in 576 and energy in 600, and again for human quality in 997. The tree image stands for whatever a chapter takes to be essential to being human.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:609","b":"k:371","type":"tension","note":"371 says sloth (மடி) arises from a fate of loss. This chapter treats the same மடி as a thing one can depose at will. The tension is direct, and neither chapter acknowledges the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:602","b":"k:1023","type":"develops","note":"The same goal, raising one's family, and the same word used differently. 602 asks one to drive out மடி, and in 1023 the deity tightens its மடி (waist-cloth) and comes ahead of the one who resolves on this. That verse's தான்முந்துறும் also echoes fate's in 380.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:610","b":"k:1103","type":"tension","note":"Both invoke Vishnu's realm as the highest measure. 610 offers it to the sleepless ruler, and 605 has just listed sleep as a boat to ruin. 1103 asks whether that realm is even as sweet as sleep on a beloved's arms. The Porul and Kamam books rank sleep and sovereignty oppositely.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:607","b":"k:784","type":"develops","note":"784 says friendship exists to rebuke when one strays, and uses the same word இடி. If Parimelazhagar is right that the scolding in 607 comes from friends, the verse shows what happens when that duty is carried out and fails.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:620","b":"k:380","type":"tension","note":"380 asks what could be stronger than fate, which arrives ahead of every stratagem. 620 answers that those who keep toiling see its back. Parimelazhagar narrows the gap by saying fate blocks only a few attempts, which the two verses themselves don't say.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:619","b":"k:377","type":"tension","note":"377 limits what even hoarders of millions can enjoy to what has been apportioned. 619 guarantees effort a wage whatever divine order decides. One sets a ceiling on enjoyment and the other a floor on reward, and they may coexist only just.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:614","b":"k:727","type":"same_image","note":"The same sword in the same hand. In 614 it stands for generosity without labour and in 727 for learning without courage in an assembly. Both say an asset is void without the disposition to use it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:622","b":"k:595","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the phrase வெள்ளத் தனைய, 'as much as the flood'. In 595 the flood lifts a stalk, in a figure for the rise of energy. In 622 the flood is suffering itself, dissolved by a thought. In both the mind is what settles the scale.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:626","b":"k:593","type":"develops","note":"Both refuse the cry over lost wealth. 593 says energy is the real asset, so the loss is small. 626 says the one who never clutched has no such cry. One rests on what you still have, and the other on how you first held it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:628","b":"k:615","type":"same_image","note":"Both begin இன்பம் விழையான், 'not desiring pleasure'. In 615 this produces a pillar for kin through labour, and in 628 it produces a person pain can't reach. The same renunciation of pleasure serves worldly work and inner calm alike, without any talk of leaving the world.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:629","b":"k:379","type":"agrees","note":"Both ask for one response to good and bad, but they give different grounds: 379 reasons from fate that it's inconsistent to accept one and grieve the other, and 629 reasons from the training of desire, without mentioning fate.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:636","b":"k:373","type":"qualifies","note":"373 sets one's own understanding above study, and 636 asks for both at once: native acuity with treatises. Taken together, the opposition in 373 is not the Kural's last word on learning and nature.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:637","b":"k:140","type":"agrees","note":"140 calls the learned ignorant if they can't move in step with the world; 637 turns this into an instruction for statecraft. The Virtue book's moral rule becomes a professional one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:639","b":"k:889","type":"develops","note":"889 says an inner enemy holds ruin even when small as a sesame fragment; 639 gives the same inner enemy a face at the king's side and a scale, seventy crore outer foes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:644","b":"k:32","type":"tension","note":"32 says nothing brings more gain than virtue, and 644 uses the same ஊங்கு formula to put apt speech above both virtue and gain. This is either high rhetoric or a real rearrangement. Parimelazhagar makes apt speech a cause of virtue to avoid the clash.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:642","b":"k:127","type":"agrees","note":"127, in the Virtue book, says guard the tongue or suffer from its errors, and the fear is personal shame. 642 makes the same demand for a public reason, since gain and ruin follow. The same discipline serves different goals.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:646","b":"k:424","type":"agrees","note":"424 defines wisdom by the same two-part structure: making your speech enter easily and finding the fine meaning in others' words. 646 assigns it to excellence in office, so wisdom and diplomacy are shown to share a form.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:656","b":"k:69","type":"develops","note":"69 gives a mother's greatest joy as hearing her son called சான்றோன், greater than at the birth. 656 uses the same two words, ஈன்றாள் and சான்றோர், and places that standing above her hunger. Read together, the son's choice could be seen as what she would have wanted, though neither verse says so.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:657","b":"k:1041","type":"tension","note":"1041 says nothing hurts like poverty. 657 chooses extreme poverty over tainted wealth. The chapter on poverty doesn't withdraw this, so the choice is made in full knowledge of what the poverty will be.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:659","b":"k:319","type":"agrees","note":"Both state a return of harm: 319 in morning and evening, 659 in the taking and the leaving. 659 restricts it to wealth and adds the positive half, that clean gains come back.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:661","b":"k:381","type":"qualifies","note":"381 makes six assets (army, people, wealth, ministers, friends, fortress) the mark of a lion among kings. Parimelazhagar's 'others' in 661 are exactly army, fort and friendship, which he says don't count as firmness. The list of assets is empty without the mind that uses them.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:666","b":"k:540","type":"agrees","note":"Two versions of the same promise: obtaining what one thinks of is easy if one keeps thinking of it (540), and if one is firm (666). The first asks for attention to be sustained, and the second for action to be.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:669","b":"k:628","type":"tension","note":"628 prevents pain by not desiring pleasure. 669 accepts pain for the sake of the pleasure that results. One asks you to stop wanting joy and the other to work for it. They may differ in scope, since 628 speaks of how one meets pain and 669 of which tasks to take up, but neither verse says so.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:671","b":"k:467","type":"agrees","note":"Both separate thinking from having decided. 467 condemns thinking after deciding as a disgrace, and 671 condemns delay after deciding as a fault, the one seeing it as an intellectual lapse and the other a practical one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:672","b":"k:383","type":"qualifies","note":"383 asks a ruler for தூங்காமை, sleeplessness, without condition. 672 divides this: some things should indeed be slept on. Parimelazhagar makes this connection himself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:673","b":"k:546","type":"tension","note":"On Parimelazhagar's reading, 673 ranks force first where one is stronger. 546 says it isn't the spear that gives victory but the king's straight sceptre. The Kural's strategy and its ethics may be speaking here to different questions. Manakkudavar's reading of 673 removes this tension.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:674","b":"k:879","type":"agrees","note":"Both warn about what enmity becomes if left to grow: 879 says fell the thorn tree young, and 674 says the left-over ember flares. 879 addresses timing, and 674 completeness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:679","b":"k:874","type":"develops","note":"874 says the world rests in one who can turn enmity into friendship, as a mark of character. 679 turns this into a question of scheduling: it comes before duties to friends.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:680","b":"k:969","type":"tension","note":"969 praises those who give up life at the loss of honour, like the yak with its hair. 680 describes the weak who bow and take the terms to keep their people steady. Parimelazhagar sees this: the honourable would not choose it, so the verse only reports it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:690","b":"k:586","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Two agents of the king, both ready for death, with opposite methods: the spy says nothing whatever is done to him (586), and the envoy says everything whatever may come (690). They share an unflinching eye: compare 585's கண்ணஞ்சாது with 686's கண் அஞ்சான்.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:684","b":"k:667","type":"tension","note":"667 says never to scorn anyone for their form. 684 makes form (உரு) one of three requirements of the envoy. Parimelazhagar's explanation is that foreign courts respect the look of a man. The Kural is apparently distinguishing what people should do from what they in fact do.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:700","b":"k:803","type":"tension","note":"Both use கெழுதகைமை, the privilege of old intimacy. 803 asks what good long friendship is unless such liberties are accepted as if your own. 700 says to take such liberties with a king brings ruin. Friendship and power apply opposite rules to the same bond.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:697","b":"k:638","type":"tension","note":"638 obliges the man at the king's side to tell him what is good, even if the king destroys wise counsel. 696 and 697 advise saying what is not disliked, in the way that he wants. The same courtier is told to be fearless and to be careful. Parimelazhagar narrows the gap by reading 'wanted' as also greatly beneficial.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:695","b":"k:582","type":"tension","note":"582 makes it a king's work to know everything happening to everyone, swiftly and always. 695 tells those near him not even to listen in on his secrets. Knowledge flows one way, and the Kural presents this imbalance as prudence, not as a problem.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:698","b":"k:388","type":"qualifies","note":"388 says a king who rules justly and protects is held to be a god among people: the title is earned. On Parimelazhagar's reading of 698, the divinity belongs to any king, young or kin, and the courtier mustn't ask. Other readings of ஒளி remove the conflict.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:691","b":"k:563","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Chapter 57 tells the king not to be dreaded, and this chapter tells the courtier how to live with one who is. Both are realistic, and the Kural addresses each party without assuming the other has listened.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:699","b":"k:654","type":"agrees","note":"Almost the same phrase, துளக்கற்ற காட்சி and நடுக்கற்ற காட்சி. In 654 unshaken clarity won't do what is degrading, even in trouble. In 699 it won't presume on favour. Steadiness shows in both failure and success.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:709","b":"k:1100","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Chapters 71 and 110 share the title குறிப்பறிதல். 709 has the eye say what kings won't, and 1100 says that when lovers' eyes agree, words are useless. A single theory of the speaking eye serves both state intelligence and love, and neither treats speech as the deeper channel.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:706","b":"k:585","type":"tension","note":"706 and 710 say the face and eyes can't but show what is within. 585 asks the spy to stand under suspicious eyes and let nothing spill. If the face always reveals, the spy's art should be impossible, and if the spy's art is possible, this chapter overclaims.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:707","b":"k:380","type":"same_image","note":"Both end with தான்முந்துறும், 'it gets there first'. In 380, fate gets ahead of any plan. In 707, the face gets ahead of any words. 1023 uses it again of a deity. The repeated phrase pictures a force that outruns human intention.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:705","b":"k:577","type":"same_image","note":"The Kural keeps asking what eyes are for. 577 answers kindly regard, 393 learning, and 705 reading signs. Each makes the physical eye worthless by that chapter's standard, so 'real sight' is defined differently each time.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:702","b":"k:698","type":"parallel_domain","note":"On Parimelazhagar's reading, the courtier in 698 must treat the king as a god. 702 then gives that same comparison to the man who can read minds, so the servant who can read the god is set beside him.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:718","b":"k:720","type":"same_image","note":"Two poured liquids in three verses: water on a growing bed and nectar in a yard. Nectar is much more precious than water and yet the outcome is reversed. What decides the value of speech is the ground that takes it, not the substance.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:720","b":"k:401","type":"develops","note":"Both use the phrase கோட்டி கொளல், holding forth in company. 401 faults the speaker for lacking learning, and 720 faults the hearers for lacking fitness. Together they give both ways a speech can fail.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:715","b":"k:723","type":"tension","note":"The adjacent chapter says fearless speech in assembly is rarer than courage in battle. 715 says that the best of all good things is holding back among elders. The chapters are a pair and Parimelazhagar reconciles them with precision: only speaking first is restrained.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies"},{"a":"k:723","b":"k:780","type":"tension","note":"780 calls a death that brings tears to one's ruler's eyes worth begging for, in the chapter on martial pride. 723 calls those who die among enemies easy to find. The Kural holds both, and each chapter's supreme value is deflated by the other.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:729","b":"k:410","type":"tension","note":"410 makes the unlearned like beasts beside the learned. 729 places a kind of learned person below the unlearned. Learning that seemed to separate humans from animals is here worth less than nothing if the learned can't speak.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:728","b":"k:650","type":"develops","note":"650 uses the image of a scentless flower for those who can't expound their learning. 728 and 730 give the assembly as the place of the failure, and fear as its cause, adding a diagnosis to the image.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:739","b":"k:619","type":"tension","note":"739 says a land that makes you labour for riches is no land. 619 says bodily labour pays its wage even where the divine does not help. The ideal of the place and the ethic of the person pull in opposite directions.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:732","b":"k:545","type":"develops","note":"545 says rain and crops gather in the just king's land. 732 doesn't mention a king, but Parimelazhagar reads the absence of calamity through the king's virtue, which turns the land's fertility into evidence about its ruler.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:740","b":"k:750","type":"parallel_domain","note":"The same closing move in two chapters: a land with every excellence is useless without accord with its king, and a fort with every excellence is useless without people who can act. Material conditions are never allowed to be sufficient.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:741","b":"k:775","type":"tension","note":"741 allows that a ruler may fear and protect himself behind walls. 775 counts even a blink before a spear as flight. The chapter on the state and the chapter on the warrior hold different standards for fear, and both are the Kural's.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:747","b":"k:421","type":"same_image","note":"747's fort is hard to seize from outside and vulnerable to those within. 421 calls understanding an inner fort (உள்ளழிக்கலாகா அரண்) that enemies cannot destroy from within, the very weakness Parimelazhagar finds in 747.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:750","b":"k:770","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Fort without capable actors and army without leaders end their chapters with the same negative verdict, இல்லது and இல், making human capacity the condition of every material strength.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:750","b":"k:766","type":"qualifies","note":"750 says a fort fails without capable people. In 766 four virtues are the army's ஏமம, and Parimelazhagar concludes that such men need no other fort. Read together, the people are the real wall.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:751","b":"k:400","type":"tension","note":"Both make exclusive claims with the same structure. 400 says learning is true wealth and all others are not. 751 says nothing but wealth is anything. The Kural lets each chapter speak in absolutes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:753","b":"k:299","type":"tension","note":"Both use பொய்யா விளக்கு. In 299 the unfailing lamp of the wise is truthfulness and all other lamps are no lamps. Here the same phrase names money. Whether this is a deliberate reversal can't be proved, but the two verses compete for one title.","grade":"forced","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:755","b":"k:660","type":"agrees","note":"660 likens wealth made by deceit to water stored in an unfired pot, and Parimelazhagar brings that very image into his comment on 755 to explain why such wealth destroys its maker.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:757","b":"k:241","type":"qualifies","note":"241 ranks grace far above material wealth, which even the base have. 757 makes material wealth the nurse that raises grace. The ranking stands but the independence doesn't.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:758","b":"k:678","type":"same_image","note":"Two elephant images for getting a task done without personally bearing the risk. In 678 one elephant is used to catch another, and in 758 the owner watches elephants fight. Both separate the one who directs from the danger.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:760","b":"k:39","type":"tension","note":"39 says only what flows from virtue is pleasure, so virtue is the source. 760 makes rightly made wealth the ground from which virtue and pleasure both come easily. The order of the three aims differs by book.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:761","b":"k:411","type":"parallel_domain","note":"The same formula for two wealths: an army is தலை among a king's riches and listening is தலை among a person's. The phrase shows that 'chief of wealth' is relative to whose life is being described.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:763","b":"k:768","type":"tension","note":"763 mocks the sea-roar of an army without heroes. 768 allows that an army without fight can still gain stature through its show. Noise is nothing in one and display is something in the other.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies"},{"a":"k:765","b":"k:269","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Two ways of confronting Death personified. In 269 ascetic power may leap past கூற்றம், and in 765 an army stands against it together. The renouncer escapes and the soldier faces, and only one of them needs others.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:773","b":"k:148","type":"tension","note":"Both use பேராண்மை. Here the word is first attached to fierceness in battle. In 148 the great manhood is not looking at another's wife. The Kural contests what manhood is, and 773's own turn to helping the fallen foe is partway to 148.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:759","b":"k:773","type":"tension","note":"Two claims about the sharpest edge (எஃகு). In 759 wealth is the sharpest steel for cutting an enemy's pride. In 773 the edge of valour is helping that enemy when he falls. Cutting and sparing are both called sharpness.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:772","b":"k:596","type":"parallel_domain","note":"596 says aim high, since even a failure is as good as not failing. 772 gives the warrior's version: the spear that missed an elephant is better than the arrow that hit a hare. Aim outranks outcome in both.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:776","b":"k:38","type":"tension","note":"Both judge a life day by day and ask for no wasted days. In 38 the good deed makes a day count, and it blocks future births. In 776 the wound makes a day count. Two complete moral accountings use one form.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:780","b":"k:1066","type":"tension","note":"1066 says nothing shames the tongue more than begging, even water for a cow. 780 says this death is worth begging for, with the same root (இர-). Only something beyond the Kural's worst disgrace could justify the word.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:777","b":"k:231","type":"parallel_domain","note":"231 says the only profit for a life is to give and live with fame (இசை). 777 wants the same இசை, and pays for it with life itself. Giving and dying are two routes to one reward.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:784","b":"k:448","type":"parallel_domain","note":"448 says a king with no one to rebuke him falls even without enemies. 784 makes the same office of rebuke the very purpose of friendship. What a counsellor does for a ruler, a friend does for anyone.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:783","b":"k:785","type":"tension","note":"783 says friendship grows with practice and 785 says practice isn't needed. Parimelazhagar reconciles them by ranking three causes of friendship and placing shared feeling first, but the verses themselves sit in plain tension.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:785","b":"k:801","type":"tension","note":"785 makes acquaintance unnecessary, while a whole chapter on old friendship opens at 801. Parimelazhagar's gloss on 801 eases this by saying that பழைமை is not elapsed time but a certain kind of friendship.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:783","b":"k:1110","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Both liken a relationship to learning with a 'the more, the more' (-தொறும்). In 783 friendship deepens like study's delight, and in 1110 love with the beloved is like finding one's ignorance with each new thing learned. Learning gives the Kural its model of an inexhaustible bond.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:791","b":"k:796","type":"qualifies","note":"791 demands inquiry before a friendship begins. 796 says ruin is what truly measures friends, and Manakkudavar adds it is hard to know them otherwise. So the test that matters most can't be run in advance, and this undercuts the chapter's own program.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:791","b":"k:797","type":"tension","note":"791 says those devoted to friendship cannot leave a friend once made. 797 calls leaving a fool's friendship pure gain. Parimelazhagar's 800 offers a way out: leaving is for what one took on without knowing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:793","b":"k:972","type":"tension","note":"793 and 794 screen friends by family. 972 says birth is equal for all living beings and only deeds make the difference. The chapter on choosing friends keeps a criterion that the chapter on greatness dissolves.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:802","b":"k:1302","type":"same_image","note":"Salt in both, and both about intimacy that hurts a little. In 802 the worthy are salt to friendship's liberties, and in 1302 lovers' sulking is salt that spoils when overdone. 1302 supplies the measure 802 leaves out.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:805","b":"k:797","type":"tension","note":"797 says dropping a fool's friendship is gain. 805 says when an old friend hurts you, consider it folly and go on. The same பேதைமை is a reason for leaving in one chapter and an excuse for staying in the next, and the only difference is how old the bond is.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:807","b":"k:800","type":"tension","note":"800 says pay if you must to escape a bad friend. 807 says love does not end even when an old friend ruins you. Between them lies a question the Kural doesn't settle: at what point does a harmful friend stop being an old friend and become one you never should have made?","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:784","b":"k:818","type":"tension","note":"784 says friendship exists to rebuke, openly and in advance. 818 says that with a certain kind of friend, say nothing and let it fade. Whether one rebukes turns out to depend on whether the friend is still worth the rebuke.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:784","b":"k:817","type":"develops","note":"784 says friendship isn't for laughter. 817 raises that to a ten-crore disadvantage against enemies. A verse about purpose becomes a verdict on a whole class of companion.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:813","b":"k:911","type":"agrees","note":"911 says the women who desire wealth rather than the man bring ruin through sweet words. 813 applies the same logic to friends who calculate, and the comparison runs the other way: chapter 92's accusation is made first of men's friendships.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:824","b":"k:706","type":"tension","note":"706 says the face shows what is in the heart as crystal shows what is beside it. 824 fears those whose face shows the reverse. Chapter 71 rests on the reliability of signs and chapter 83 on their failure, and Parimelazhagar's note on 824 calls the face the instrument of reading.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:824","b":"k:826","type":"tension","note":"824 fears hostility that does not show. 826 says an enemy's fine words are soon seen through. The chapter holds two views on whether concealment can succeed.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:827","b":"k:279","type":"same_image","note":"279 says the straight arrow is cruel and the bent lute is kind, so judge by deed. 827 says the bent bow means harm. The two verses reach for the same geometry to make the same point, that shape doesn't reveal intent.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:828","b":"k:71","type":"tension","note":"71 says love can't be locked in, and that tears publish it. 828 says an enemy's tears may hide a weapon. The evidence that the Book of Virtue trusts absolutely is, in the Book of Wealth, a cover.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:829","b":"k:34","type":"tension","note":"34 says all virtue comes to a stainless mind and the rest is noise. 829 teaches outward friendship with inward death. Parimelazhagar accepts the conflict openly and resolves it as a rule for enemies only, which is a substantive claim about how the books of the Kural relate.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:786","b":"k:830","type":"develops","note":"786 says friendship of the face alone is not friendship. 830 tells the reader to use just that kind on a suspect enemy (முகம் நட்டு). What is a failure in one chapter becomes a technique in another.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:835","b":"k:398","type":"parallel_domain","note":"A precise inversion using the same ஒருமை and எழுமை. In 398 learning gained in one birth protects for seven. In 835 folly done in one birth drowns for seven. One life's leverage on all the lives to come works in both directions.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:834","b":"k:823","type":"develops","note":"823 says study doesn't reform the hostile. 834 turns the same point on the student and teacher himself: he is the worst fool. Learning fails as moral change in both, first in the other and then in oneself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:839","b":"k:782","type":"develops","note":"782 says a fool's friendship wanes like the moon. Parimelazhagar uses that waning to explain 839's joke: because it has been shrinking every day, by the time of parting nothing is left to hurt.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:841","b":"k:752","type":"tension","note":"Two reports of what the world does. 752 says everyone despises those with nothing. 841 says the world doesn't count lack of wealth as lack. Both can't be plain observation, so at least one of them is the Kural speaking for how the world ought to judge.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:841","b":"k:1041","type":"tension","note":"Same doubled form (இன்மையுள் இன்மை, இன்மையின் இன்மை). 1041 says the only thing as painful as poverty is poverty. 841 gives the title to lack of understanding. The chapter on poverty and this one rank the worst lack differently.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:850","b":"k:423","type":"tension","note":"850 makes denying the world's consensus demonic. 423 says wisdom is finding the truth in anything, whoever says it. Parimelazhagar's doctrinal list sharpens this, and Kalaignar's 'evidence' moves 850 closer to 423.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:846","b":"k:788","type":"same_image","note":"Both are about the garment and the shame of exposure. In 788 a friend's help comes as fast as the hand to a slipping cloth. In 846 the cloth over the body is exposed as a fraud if faults stay bare. One makes the reflex an ideal and the other mocks it as a substitute.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:852","b":"k:314","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Both tell the injured not to return harm, but their reasons differ. In 314 the wrongdoer is shamed by kindness. In 852, on Parimelazhagar's reading, one's own position is improved. The same act is justified in moral terms in one book and in terms of advantage in another.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:829","b":"k:852","type":"tension","note":"829 tells one to answer hidden contempt with hidden death. 852 says not to return harm even to one who plans division. They are three chapters apart in the same stretch on friends and foes, and the Kural offers both as sound counsel.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:855","b":"k:771","type":"tension","note":"Two kinds of invincibility, each put as 'don't attempt it'. 771's warrior warns that those who stood before his lord now stand as stones. 855's person is unbeatable because he leans away. One's strength is in striking and the other's in declining.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:860","b":"k:784","type":"qualifies","note":"784 says friendship isn't for laughter (நகுதல்). 860 makes நகல் the very source of good ways, which Parimelazhagar glosses as friendship. The same root gets very different valuations in the chapter on what friendship is for and the chapter on hostility.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:861","b":"k:250","type":"tension","note":"The same two words, வலியார் and மெலியார், with opposite advice. 250 says that when going against the weak, remember how you stand before the strong, which is a brake. 861 tells a ruler to go against the weak with appetite. The Book of Virtue and the Book of Wealth are at their furthest apart here.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:861","b":"k:871","type":"tension","note":"Two adjacent chapter openings. 861 tells one to keep enmity with the weak. 871 says enmity shouldn't be desired even in jest. Parimelazhagar's way through is that 871 is about unworthy enmity (மாணாத பகை), but the verses don't draw this distinction.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:867","b":"k:794","type":"parallel_domain","note":"The same phrase, கொடுத்தும் கொளல்வேண்டும். 794 says to pay for the friendship of a person of honour. 867 says to pay for the enmity of a fool. The Kural prices enemies and friends in the same way, and both are investments.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:874","b":"k:830","type":"tension","note":"874 says the world rests on the one who turns enemies into friends. 830 says that when an enemy turns friend, give only your face and drop even that later. If this is the same event seen from both sides, then each party in 874's triumph has reason to be following 830.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:879","b":"k:852","type":"tension","note":"852 and its chapter say it is best not to return harm and to lean away from hostility. 879 says to kill the threat while it is young. One counsels letting enmity die by itself and the other warns that it won't, and both claim the outcome is self-preservation.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:880","b":"k:763","type":"same_image","note":"In 763 the rats die when the cobra merely breathes (உயிர்ப்ப). In 880 those who don't break a foe's pride don't survive his breath (on Manakkudavar's reading). The word for breathing is the unit of how little it takes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:877","b":"k:1200","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Both warn against telling pain to those not attuned to it. In 877 it is a matter of strategy and self-respect, and in 1200 the lover scolds her own heart for telling sorrow to one who doesn't care. The advice carries across books, and it is much sadder in the second one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:735","b":"k:881","type":"qualifies","note":"In 735 the land should be free from உட்பகை, which Parimelazhagar glosses as robbers, slanderers and even wild animals. In 881 the same commentator takes the word as kin. The term shrinks from a realm's internal troubles to a family's, and it hurts more in the smaller frame.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:889","b":"k:104","type":"same_image","note":"Both use a tiny seed against a huge effect. In 104 a millet-seed favour counts as large as a palmyra to those who understand it, and in 889 a sesame-seed enmity holds complete ruin. Small things turn out to be large in the Kural in both moral directions.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:891","b":"k:781","type":"qualifies","note":"781 says no guard is as hard to get past as friendship. 891 says not slighting the able outranks every safeguard. Parimelazhagar lists friendship among those it outranks, so a long section that praised friendship and forts closes by ranking something above them.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:894","b":"k:765","type":"same_image","note":"Death personified, twice. In 765 an army stands united when Death comes raging. In 894 the fool waves Death over with his own hand. The difference between courage and folly is not whether one faces Death, but whether one sent the invitation.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:896","b":"k:129","type":"same_image","note":"Both rank fire below something else: in 129 the burn from fire heals and the burn of the tongue doesn't, and in 896 one may survive the fire but not offence given to the great. Fire is the Kural's standard for what is bad but survivable.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:898","b":"k:29","type":"develops","note":"29 calls the great those who have climbed a hill of virtue, whose anger can't be withstood even for a moment. 898 calls them hill-like, and 896-897 in Parimelazhagar's reading have the same instant destruction. Chapter 90 develops what chapter 3 states about the renunciant, in the political book.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:899","b":"k:25","type":"develops","note":"25 calls Indra sufficient witness to the power of the one who controls the senses. Parimelazhagar makes 899's வேந்தன் Indra as well. The Kural itself only names Indra in 25, so the connection depends on his gloss.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:864","b":"k:899","type":"tension","note":"864 says one who doesn't let go of anger is easy prey for anyone. 899 makes the anger of the exalted able to bring down a king. Anger is weakness or supreme power depending on who has it, and 306 warns that it burns the one who has it.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:906","b":"k:1103","type":"tension","note":"Both set a wife's or beloved's shoulders against heaven. 906 says that fearing those shoulders leaves no worth even in a godlike life, and 1103 asks whether the lotus-eyed god's world is sweeter than sleep on them. Fear and love differ, but the books value the same intimacy in opposite ways.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:907","b":"k:148","type":"develops","note":"Both redefine manliness away from dominance over others. 148 gives பேராண்மை to the man who doesn't look at another's wife, and 907 finds the opposite failure in a man who takes a woman's orders: in each case ஆண்மை is measured by self-command.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:905","b":"k:428","type":"qualifies","note":"428 calls fearing what should be feared the act of the wise. 905 shows the matching error: the fear here lands on doing good, which ought not be feared.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:909","b":"k:1107","type":"qualifies","note":"909 says the man who serves a woman's will loses pleasure with everything else. 1107 compares a beloved's embrace to eating one's own share in one's own home, which suggests that the pleasure 909 says is lost belongs to someone still his own master.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:911","b":"k:91","type":"qualifies","note":"91 defines இன்சொல் as speech soaked in love and free of guile. 911 shows what is left when love is removed: the same sweetness becomes the instrument of harm. So the Kural's praise of sweet speech was never about sweetness alone.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:914","b":"k:241","type":"develops","note":"241 ranks the wealth of grace above material wealth, which even the பூரியார் have. 914 applies the same contrast to choice of company, and 919's பூரியர் are the ones who sink, so these verses share a vocabulary of grace against base wealth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:916","b":"k:149","type":"agrees","note":"Both tie a man's நலம் to whose shoulders he doesn't touch, using the same verb தோய். 149 says it of another's wife and 916 of women who sell themselves, so the two forbidden categories are treated in one idiom of self-possession.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:920","b":"k:617","type":"same_image","note":"Fortune as a goddess who leaves or stays: 617 places her at the feet of the unidle man, and 920 says the ones she has dropped can be known by their company.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:926","b":"k:1201","type":"tension","note":"926 says that drinkers are eating poison. 1201 says love is sweeter than liquor because it delights even in memory, taking liquor's delight as the benchmark. The book of love borrows, without judging, the pleasure that this chapter condemns.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:926","b":"k:339","type":"same_image","note":"Both compare sleep to death. 339 makes this a serious claim about mortality, with waking as birth, and 926 only uses it as a pointer to lost awareness. The commonplace serves metaphysics in one and a warning in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:923","b":"k:69","type":"same_image","note":"Both place a mother and the worthy in a single scene. In 69 her joy peaks when her son is called சான்றோன்; in 923 his drunkenness pains her and is unthinkable before those same people. Read together, 923 is 69 undone.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:929","b":"k:930","type":"qualifies","note":"Other people's reasons can't reach a drunk (929), but his own sober reflection might (930). The chapter separates argument from self-recognition as means of change.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:940","b":"k:345","type":"develops","note":"Under Parimelazhagar's reading of 940 the two are one argument: the soul clings to the body through suffering as a gambler clings through loss, and 345 says that for those ending birth even the body is one attachment too many. 940 is the disease and 345 the cure.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:936","b":"k:617","type":"same_image","note":"Both use முகடி, the goddess of misfortune. 617 lodges her in idleness and 936 equates her with gambling. Parimelazhagar's remark on 936 that her name is too ill-omened to say makes gambling her acceptable alias.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:931","b":"k:932","type":"develops","note":"The hook's bait in 931 becomes the mechanism in 932: the one small win pulls the hundred losses after it. Parimelazhagar explicitly ties them together with அத்தூண்டிற் பொன்.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:943","b":"k:968","type":"tension","note":"943 teaches how to carry the body far. 968 asks whether keeping the flesh alive is 'medicine' when dignity has been destroyed. The health chapter treats long life as the aim, and the honour chapter, two away, makes it conditional.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies"},{"a":"k:943","b":"k:345","type":"tension","note":"The verses value the same body differently. 943's man has received a body and should carry it far, and 345's renouncer finds it one attachment too many.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:949","b":"k:471","type":"parallel_domain","note":"A physician and a ruler think in the same form: 949 weighs patient, illness and time before acting, and 471 weighs the deed, oneself, the opponent and allies. Both end in the same verb of action (செயல்), and both reject a universal prescription.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:947","b":"k:227","type":"same_image","note":"Both use fire for the belly. 947's fire is a measure to observe, and 227's is the disease of hunger that won't touch one who shares food. One is the eater's problem and the other's cure is ethical, which widens what 'not falling ill' means.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:950","b":"k:1102","type":"same_image","note":"950 divides medicine into four roles. 1102 says that other illnesses have other remedies, but the bejewelled woman is herself the remedy for the illness she causes, which collapses the roles of 950 into one.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:951","b":"k:972","type":"tension","note":"951 says that integrity and shame come naturally only to the well-born. 972 says birth is alike for all beings and distinction arises from deeds. This is the Kural's sharpest internal disagreement on lineage, and Kalaignar's reversal of 951 can be read as an attempt to harmonize it with 972.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:957","b":"k:1117","type":"same_image","note":"957 makes the moon's spot the emblem of a visible fault. 1117 asks whether the woman's face, like the moon, carries a spot, and implies it doesn't. The same mark serves moral exposure in one book and the lover's denial of imperfection in another.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:959","b":"k:91","type":"develops","note":"In 91 sweet speech comes from a love-soaked and guileless mind. 959 uses speech as a sign of what lies underneath, as the sprout shows the soil. Together they make speech a surface that discloses the source.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:955","b":"k:223","type":"agrees","note":"223 says that giving before a poor man can utter 'I have nothing' is found only in the well-born. 955 takes the giver's own means shrinking, and holds his nature constant. Giving is the chapter's real measure of lineage in both.","grade":"wrong","better":null},{"a":"k:958","b":"k:133","type":"qualifies","note":"133 says conduct itself is lineage and lapse is low birth. 958 is less radical, since lapse leads people to doubt a man's birth, which keeps birth as the underlying fact while 133 dissolves it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:969","b":"k:131","type":"develops","note":"131 says that conduct must be guarded above life. 969 and 970 turn that ranking into an actual death, and show what the abstract comparison costs.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:961","b":"k:1028","type":"tension","note":"961 gives up even what is indispensable if it would lower one. 1028 warns that a man who is idle and stands on honour will destroy the family he should be building. Honour is the highest thing in one chapter and a hazard in another.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:964","b":"k:969","type":"same_image","note":"Hair appears at both ends of a value scale in the same chapter. Fallen hair is the worthless thing that a degraded person becomes, and the yak's one lost hair is what can't be survived. The first image is about how others see you, the second about how you see yourself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:970","b":"k:780","type":"agrees","note":"Both look at a death through those left behind. 780's soldier dies so that his patron's eyes fill and such death is worth begging for, and 970's man of honour is worshipped by the world. Honour and the warrior ethic share one structure.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:963","b":"k:125","type":"qualifies","note":"125 says bowing is good for all and an added wealth for the rich. 963 keeps the second half and reverses the first for the poor: they are told to stand tall, not to bow.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:974","b":"k:56","type":"develops","note":"56 gives the wife's self-guarding, தற்காத்து, as what makes a woman. 974 makes the same self-holding the condition of greatness in anyone. A gendered virtue is universalized, and the phrasing reaches back across the book of virtue.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:973","b":"k:409","type":"agrees","note":"409 uses மேல் and கீழ் of birth, and ranks the unlearned high-born below the learned low-born. 973 uses the same words of position and wealth. Between them, the Kural detaches worth from both axes of hierarchy.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:975","b":"k:26","type":"agrees","note":"26, in the chapter on renunciants, defines the great as those who do what is hard. 975 repeats that in the book of wealth, with no renunciation. Parimelazhagar himself cites 26 at 971, so this standard crosses from ascetic to householder.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:976","b":"k:443","type":"develops","note":"443 calls cherishing the great and making them one's own the rarest of rare things, using the same பேணி. 976 explains why it is rare: the small don't even conceive of it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:971","b":"k:595","type":"agrees","note":"595 says a person's height is as great as their will, as a lotus stem rises with the water. 971 calls that will one's wealth and radiance. Both make aspiration the measure of a person.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:980","b":"k:984","type":"agrees","note":"Consecutive chapters end up in the same place: 980 ends the chapter on greatness with covering faults, and 984 makes not telling others' faults the defining quality of the worthy.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:986","b":"k:967","type":"tension","note":"Chapters 97 and 99 give different answers to humiliation. 967 prefers being called dead to living behind those who scorn you, and 986 makes accepting defeat even from lesser people the touchstone of worth. The two make most sense as distinct ideals, honour and fullness of character, and not as one code.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:988","b":"k:1044","type":"tension","note":"988 says poverty brings no disgrace to one with worth. 1044 says poverty produces degraded speech even in the well-born. Whether character can hold against poverty is answered one way here and the other in the chapter on poverty.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:990","b":"k:151","type":"develops","note":"151 holds up the earth, bearing those who dig it, as the model of forbearance for people. 990 reverses the dependence: the earth's own bearing relies on the worthy. The model has become the dependent.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:983","b":"k:990","type":"same_image","note":"Two images of bearing a load form a stack: five qualities are pillars carrying worth, and worth carries what the earth bears. The chapter's architecture rests the world on small daily dispositions.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:985","b":"k:225","type":"same_image","note":"The formula ஆற்றுவார் ஆற்றல் recurs. In 225 this strength is enduring hunger, ranked below relieving it, and in 985 it is bowing. The Kural keeps redefining strength as some form of restraint.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:987","b":"k:314","type":"agrees","note":"314 says one punishes those who harm by doing them good until they are ashamed, and 987 makes that same return a test of whether worth has any use. 314 gives it a purpose, and 987 makes it a measure.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:984","b":"k:261","type":"qualifies","note":"261 defines austerity as enduring pain and not hurting living things. 984 picks out not killing as its core, in order to place not speaking of others' faults at the same rank for worth.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"k:995","b":"k:871","type":"tension","note":"The same three words meet. 871 calls enmity the thing that has no பண்பு, not to be wanted even in jest. 995 says that those who know others' feelings have பண்பு even within enmity. Enmity is by definition devoid of courtesy, and yet courtesy can survive inside it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:993","b":"k:1071","type":"develops","note":"993 says that likeness of limbs isn't human likeness, and 1071 gives the unsettling result: the base look exactly like people. The chapter on baseness starts where this verse ends.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:997","b":"k:576","type":"same_image","note":"Both call a person a tree for a moral lack: 997 for lacking human courtesy, 576 for having eyes without kindly regard. Each states a difference between being human and being merely alive.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:996","b":"k:990","type":"agrees","note":"Consecutive chapters end by making the world depend on a moral elite: if the worthy decline the earth can't carry its load (990), and without the courteous the world sinks into it (996). Two images of ground failing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1000","b":"k:216","type":"same_image","note":"Both judge wealth by where it lands. In 216 wealth in the hands of the kind is a fruit tree in the village, and in 1000 wealth in the discourteous spoils in its vessel. The next chapter's 1008 completes the set with a poisonous tree.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"k:992","b":"k:972","type":"tension","note":"972 denies that birth settles distinction, and 992, two chapters later, places birth among courtesy's two sources. Parimelazhagar's gloss, which turns birth into conduct, can be seen as a way of closing the gap.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:991","b":"k:386","type":"parallel_domain","note":"386 says that the land extols a king easy to see and not harsh in speech. 991 gives ordinary people the same recipe. Accessibility is a virtue at every scale in the Kural.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:999","b":"k:786","type":"develops","note":"786 distinguishes the laugh of the face from the laugh of the heart in friendship. Parimelazhagar takes 999's laughter as inner joy in others' company, so a failure of that capacity darkens the world and not merely a friendship.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1008","b":"k:216","type":"same_image","note":"An exact counter-image. 216: wealth in the hands of the kind is a useful tree fruiting in the village. 1008: wealth in the hands of the unwanted is a poison tree fruiting mid-town. Same setting, same act of fruiting, and the holder's character decides whether it feeds or kills.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1001","b":"k:214","type":"agrees","note":"214 places one who doesn't do what's fitting 'among the dead', and 1001 calls one who won't use his wealth 'dead'. Both use death as a civil status, assigned for failure to circulate wealth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1010","b":"k:211","type":"same_image","note":"211 says that duty seeks no return, as with rain. 1010 extends the identification of generous wealth with rain: even their failures are like clouds running dry. The comparison is a consistent one in the Kural.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1005","b":"k:954","type":"same_image","note":"அடுக்கிய கோடி recurs. For the well-born in 954, stacked crores cannot buy a lowering act. For the miser in 1005, they amount to nothing. The same sum measures character in one and its absence in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1003","b":"k:570","type":"qualifies","note":"570 says that a harsh sceptre over the unlearned is the only burden on the earth. 1003 uses the same phrase for hoarders. The same claim to being earth's one burden is made against a tyrant and against a hoarder.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1004","b":"k:233","type":"agrees","note":"233 says nothing is imperishable but high fame, and 1004 asks what the unwanted hoarder imagines will remain. Parimelazhagar's note supplies the link: fame rests only on the giver.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1017","b":"k:1251","type":"tension","note":"1017 says the shame-bearing would give up life before shame. 1251 says that love's axe breaks the door barred by shame. The same supreme barrier falls in the book of love, and the whole book of love depends on that collapse.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1017","b":"k:969","type":"agrees","note":"A nearly identical ranking is made for two virtues. 969 gives up life for மானம் and 1017 for நாண். Kalaignar's gloss of 1017 even uses மானம். The verses suggest honour and shame are the outward and inward faces of a single conviction.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1014","b":"k:1089","type":"same_image","note":"Shame is ornament in both. 1014 calls it the ornament of the worthy, and 1089 asks what other ornaments a woman with shame could need. The same figure crosses gender and books.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1015","b":"k:315","type":"parallel_domain","note":"315 asks what good knowledge is if one doesn't treat another's pain as one's own. 1015 makes the same move for disgrace: treating another's blame as one's own. Both make moral identification the test of the higher quality.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1012","b":"k:972","type":"develops","note":"Both first state what is common to all life, and then find சிறப்பு. For 972 that is deeds, and for 1012 it is shame. Read together, the Kural's account of human distinction becomes specific.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1013","b":"k:73","type":"same_image","note":"The soul's hold on the body is the simile for virtue's bond in both: in 73 love's tie is like that of life to bone, and in 1013 worth holds on to shame in the same way.","grade":"wrong","better":null},{"a":"k:1020","b":"k:997","type":"same_image","note":"Wood is used twice for those without inner quality. The courtesy chapter's man is a tree in 997, and the shameless man here a wooden puppet. The puppet is worse, since it has been shaped to look alive.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1018","b":"k:924","type":"same_image","note":"Personified moral forces flee from the faulty. In 924, Shame the good woman turns her back on the drunk, and in 1018 Virtue herself blushes and departs from one who doesn't blush. Shame is first the one who flees and then the condition for virtue's staying.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1023","b":"k:380","type":"tension","note":"The same words appear with opposite force. In 380 fate comes first (தான்முந் துறும்), defeating whatever one devises (சூழினும்). In 1023 the divine comes first (தான்முந் துறும்) for the one who resolves, and 1024 adds that the end comes without deliberating (சூழாமல்). Priority over human planning works against effort in one and for it in the other.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1023","b":"k:619","type":"develops","note":"619 says that effort pays even when the divine doesn't help. 1023 takes up the other case: effort draws the divine to help. Together they cover both options and in each effort wins.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1024","b":"k:467","type":"tension","note":"467 says deliberate and then resolve, and that deliberating afterwards is a fault. 1024 says that for those who work without delay for their people, the outcome comes without deliberating. The two can be reconciled only by treating family work as a special case.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies"},{"a":"k:1025","b":"k:525","type":"agrees","note":"Both use the same play on சுற்றம் and சுற்றுதல். In 525 giving and sweet speech surround one with layers of kin, and 1025 extends this until the whole world gathers around as kin.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1026","b":"k:148","type":"develops","note":"148's பேராண்மை is not looking at another's wife, and 1026's நல்லாண்மை is ruling one's house. The Kural keeps coining compound forms of manliness that exclude combat, as Parimelazhagar notes here.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1028","b":"k:481","type":"qualifies","note":"481 says that a king needs the right time, like a crow beating an owl by day. Parimelazhagar explicitly cites 481 at 1028 to mark the exception: one who raises his family needs no season. Timing is wisdom for conquest but an excuse for duty.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1029","b":"k:980","type":"agrees","note":"Under Manakkudavar's reading of 1029 the two use the same verb, மறைத்தல்: greatness hides others' weakness, and so does the family's support. He bears the cost in his body.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1029","b":"k:627","type":"same_image","note":"Both see the body as a place for suffering to land: a target in 627 and a vessel in 1029. For 627 this insight is what frees one from distress, and for 1029 the suffering is chosen on others' behalf.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1040","b":"k:151","type":"develops","note":"The same earth responds in two ways. In 151 it supports even those who dig into it, the model of patience. In 1040 it laughs at those who won't dig. Being dug is what it tolerates and even wants, so the virtue chapter's image becomes a joke on the idle.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1040","b":"k:924","type":"same_image","note":"'X என்னும் நல்லாள்' is a shared formula. Shame the good woman turns her back on the drunk, and Land the good woman laughs at the idle. Both personified women judge men.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1039","b":"k:1330","type":"same_image","note":"ஊடல் is love's sweet sulk in 1330, delightful when embrace follows. 1039 borrows the word to warn an absent owner: the land, like a spouse, wants presence, and here the sulk costs him the harvest.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1036","b":"k:42","type":"develops","note":"42 makes the householder the support of renouncers, and 1036 sets the farmer a level below, as the support of renouncing itself. The chain of dependence runs lower than the book of virtue showed.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1033","b":"k:1065","type":"agrees","note":"1065 says even thin gruel earned by one's own effort is the sweetest thing. 1033 makes that a social claim: only those who eat from their own ploughing truly live. The chapters on begging supply this chapter's value.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1035","b":"k:1051","type":"qualifies","note":"1051 tells one to ask of those worth asking and makes refusal their fault, not yours. 1035 presents a person who never needs the permission, and who is on the giving end of that same act.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1046","b":"k:423","type":"tension","note":"423 says the mark of understanding is seeing the truth whoever says it. 1046 reports that a poor man's meaning falls flat however true it is. One gives the ideal, and the other the way the world listens.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1044","b":"k:959","type":"tension","note":"959 says that speech reveals lineage as a sprout reveals soil. 1044 shows the signal failing: poverty brings degraded speech out of the well-born. Speech is a sign of poverty as well as lineage, so the test is less reliable than chapter 96 claims.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1043","b":"k:368","type":"agrees","note":"368 says there is no suffering for those without desire. 1043 makes poverty itself into desire, which Parimelazhagar glosses as: without craving there's no poverty. An ascetic insight enters the chapter on economic misery, while the rest of the chapter denies it.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1050","b":"k:378","type":"develops","note":"378 says that the destitute would renounce if what they must suffer would pass. 1050, with the same துப்புரவு இல்லார், tells them to do it. The verse of fate explains why it doesn't happen, while this verse only mocks them for it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1042","b":"k:247","type":"qualifies","note":"247 keeps two forms of lack apart: no grace loses the next world, no wealth loses this one. 1042 collapses them: poverty takes both worlds. Grace alone turns out not to secure the next world.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1042","b":"k:168","type":"same_image","note":"'X என ஒரு பாவி' appears in both. Envy is the sinner in 168 and poverty in 1042, with both sending one down. Parimelazhagar links them himself. The state of being poor gets the same grammar as a vice.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1047","b":"k:923","type":"same_image","note":"The mother marks a moral limit twice. Her pain at drunkenness (923) still has love in it, because she's the most forgiving audience, and poverty does what drink couldn't by making her look at her son as a stranger.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1051","b":"k:1061","type":"tension","note":"Consecutive chapters open in direct opposition. 1051: ask if you find those worth asking. 1061: not asking, even of those who give gladly and are as dear as eyes, is ten million times better. Parimelazhagar's solution makes 1051 apply only at the edge of death; the verses themselves don't state this.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1058","b":"k:1020","type":"same_image","note":"The wooden puppet (மரப்பாவை) marks a lack of real life in both. In 1020 it's a person without shame, and in 1058 a world without askers. Each chapter finds what makes movement into life, one in an inner quality and one in a social transaction.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1059","b":"k:232","type":"agrees","note":"232 says that all that speakers praise rests on those who give to askers. 1059 makes the dependence explicit: the fame needs the asker as well. The asker turns out to be necessary for a fame that seemed the giver's alone.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:1054","b":"k:1066","type":"tension","note":"Asking from the right person is like giving (1054), yet asking even water for a cow is the most degrading thing the tongue can do (1066). The chapters give opposite values to one act, and the difference of giver doesn't fully explain it, since 1061 refuses even the best givers.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1053","b":"k:963","type":"develops","note":"Parimelazhagar himself cites 963 here: the poor must stand tall, and asking without speaking lets them do so. The honour chapter's demand shapes how begging can be done at all.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1062","b":"k:377","type":"tension","note":"377 says that even those with crores enjoy only as the apportioner apportioned. 1062 curses that very apportioner, if the portion includes begging. The chapter on fate accepts the apportioning, and this chapter refuses it, and Parimelazhagar's gloss makes the two nearly the same figure.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1065","b":"k:64","type":"same_image","note":"Gruel is sweeter than ambrosia twice. 64: when one's child's little hand has stirred it. 1065, in Parimelazhagar's reading: when one's own effort earned it. The two supply the Kural's two sources of value in a poor meal, love and independence.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1068","b":"k:1134","type":"same_image","note":"A flimsy vessel breaks up in both. 1134's raft of shame and good manliness is swept away by the flood of love. 1068's boat of asking is split by the rock of concealment. Each shows a failed crossing, but the vessel in 1134 is what one is proud of.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1064","b":"k:971","type":"agrees","note":"971 calls one's radiance the wealth of aspiration, and 1064 gives its most extreme form. Without means to live, the refusal itself is too large for the world.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1080","b":"k:969","type":"tension","note":"Two responses to the same crisis mark the moral range of the Book of Wealth. When honour or distress comes, those like the yak give up life (969), and the base sell theirs for a price, in haste (1080). The book ends on the second.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:1078","b":"k:1051","type":"qualifies","note":"A scale of giving. In Parimelazhagar's reading of 1051 the worthiest give before anything is said, in 1078 the worthy give when told, and the base give when crushed. 1077 adds that the base give nothing to any who can't hurt them.","grade":"wrong","better":null},{"a":"k:1076","b":"k:587","type":"parallel_domain","note":"A spy in 587 hears the hidden and knows it without doubt, and in 1076 the base also hear it, but proclaim it. Trading in secrets is a skill or a vice according to whether one can hold them.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:1072","b":"k:368","type":"qualifies","note":"368 says there's no suffering for those without desire, the ascetic's freedom from anguish. 1072's base have that freedom, by not knowing what is good. The verse exposes a counterfeit of equanimity: peace of mind can't itself be the measure.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1075","b":"k:1018","type":"tension","note":"1018 sees shame as the condition of virtue, with virtue itself ashamed to approach the shameless. 1075 is a kind of counter-case: the base still have conduct, with fear and desire in place of shame. Their conduct looks alike and differs in its source.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:1071","b":"k:1073","type":"develops","note":"The same satirical move rises in rank: they're like men, and then they're like gods. What makes them like the gods, acting as they please, is what makes them not like men.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1088","b":"k:774","type":"tension","note":"The warrior of 774 pulls a spear from his own body and laughs. The warrior of 1088 has that same reputation and is broken by a forehead. The Kural's courage has no defence against this enemy.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1089","b":"k:1014","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1014 calls modesty the ornament of the great, and without it their proud bearing is a disease. 1089 uses the same idea for a girl's beauty, so the ethical chapter's metaphor returns as a lover's literal claim that jewels are redundant.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1090","b":"k:1281","type":"same_image","note":"Both set love above liquor for delighting through sight. 1090 says it at the first encounter and 1281 says it deep in the relationship, so the comparison holds from the start of love through its maturity.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1097","b":"k:709","type":"qualifies","note":"Both chapters are named குறிப்பறிதல. In 709, a skilled reader of eyes can tell enmity from friendship in a court. 1097 warns that a lover's eyes can put on enmity while friendly, so the statecraft skill has to work harder here.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1091","b":"k:1102","type":"same_image","note":"In 1091 one look wounds and another heals. In 1102 the woman herself is both the disease and its medicine. The paradox grows from a glance to the whole person once they have united.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1101","b":"k:343","type":"tension","note":"343 tells the renunciant to destroy the pull of the five senses and give up all desire at once. 1101 celebrates the same five senses meeting their fill in a single person. The Kural gives each its place in different books without reconciling them.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1104","b":"k:1159","type":"same_image","note":"In 1104 he marvels at a fire that burns at a distance and cools up close. In 1159, a voice in separation says ordinary fire can't burn at a distance at all. The same paradox goes from a delighted question to a complaint.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1107","b":"k:1065","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1065 says even thin gruel earned by your own labour is the sweetest food, for the sake of self-respect. 1107 borrows that dignity of one's own table to describe an embrace.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1109","b":"k:1330","type":"develops","note":"1109 lists the quarrel as love's reward, and the Kural's last verse, 1330, ends the whole work on the same idea. Parimelazhagar's scornful reading of 1109 is hard to square with the fact that 1330 and the final chapters celebrate the sequence.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1110","b":"k:396","type":"parallel_domain","note":"396 says knowledge wells up in proportion to study, like water in a sand well. 1110 takes the same unlimited knowledge as the measure of desire, except that in love more of it reveals a lack and not a supply.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1117","b":"k:957","type":"same_image","note":"957 uses the moon's spot to say the flaws of the high-born show as conspicuously as a mark on the moon. 1117 uses the same spot in praise of an unblemished face. In one, a mark reveals a failing; in the other, its absence is the point.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1114","b":"k:1094","type":"same_image","note":"The lily in 1114 would bow and look at the ground, as the woman herself does in 1094. The lily is imagined acting out the shame of being outdone, in a gesture she makes for love.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1119","b":"k:1112","type":"develops","note":"Both disqualify a comparison because it is பலர் காணும், seen by many. What starts as a heart scolded about flowers becomes an instruction to the moon, showing how his praise rests on exclusivity.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1122","b":"k:338","type":"tension","note":"Both use the same phrase for the friendship of body and life. In 338 it is like a bird flying from an abandoned shell, an image of how easily it ends. In 1122 it is the lover's image for an unbreakable bond. The thing he swears by is what the book on renunciation calls fragile, and in 1124 he seems to know it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1125","b":"k:1207","type":"tension","note":"He says 'I don't know forgetting' with a clever smile. In 1207 the woman uses the same words, மறப்பறியேன், and adds that even remembering burns her. The same claim of constant memory is banter for one and torment for the other.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1129","b":"k:1147","type":"develops","note":"Here the town's talk is a misunderstanding she can rebut. In 1147, the talk has become manure for the love itself. Her stance towards gossip shifts from defence to using it.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1132","b":"k:1017","type":"tension","note":"1017 says those ruled by shame give up life for shame and never give up shame for life. In 1132, the lover sets shame aside to save his body and life. It reverses the ethic exactly, in a book of its own.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1134","b":"k:1164","type":"same_image","note":"For the man, love's flood carries off the raft he had. For the woman in 1164, there is a sea of love and no raft at all. Her situation is worse than his: she has neither the raft nor his palmyra horse.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1137","b":"k:54","type":"qualifies","note":"54 asks what has greater worth than a woman, provided she has the firmness of chastity, using the same words, பெண்ணின் பெருந்தக்க. 1137 gives the worth a specific content: enduring a sea of desire without a public remedy. The praise comes from a man who is announcing that he won't do the same.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1141","b":"k:183","type":"tension","note":"183 says that it is better to die than to live by backbiting, because it is a false kind of life. In 1141 backbiting is what keeps someone else alive. The same act has a very different moral look from the side of its target.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:1145","b":"k:930","type":"parallel_domain","note":"930 asks whether a sober drunkard, seeing another drunk, doesn't recognise his own degradation. 1145 takes the same compulsion, wanting more with each intoxication, and uses it with delight. The book that condemns drink reuses its logic to describe love without condemning it.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1147","b":"k:1093","type":"same_image","note":"In 1093, the water poured into love's plot is her lowered glance. In 1147 the water is her mother's harsh words. What nourishes love moves from the most private gesture to the most hostile.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1153","b":"k:510","type":"parallel_domain","note":"510 warns that trusting without examination and doubting after trust both bring endless grief, for those choosing men to work with. 1153 is that same predicament without any such procedure: there is no way to test the one she has already trusted.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1154","b":"k:1149","type":"same_image","note":"Both quote 'fear not' against him. In 1149 his failure removes her shame before the town. In 1154 it raises the question of fault. One promise is doing a lot of work in her argument.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1152","b":"k:1179","type":"develops","note":"Fear of parting spoils union in 1152. By 1179 it is routine: her eyes can't sleep when he is away or when he is home. Anticipation has become a permanent condition.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1161","b":"k:396","type":"same_image","note":"Both use water that rises as you draw it. In 396, the well is knowledge, and more study brings more. In 1161, the well is pain, and every attempt to bail it out adds more. The image of an inexhaustible resource is the same, and it is a blessing in one and a curse in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1163","b":"k:1132","type":"tension","note":"The man sets shame aside to mount the palmyra horse (1132). The woman carries shame and love in perfect balance and can drop neither (1163). The book states this asymmetry directly: he can choose and she can only bear the weight.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1167","b":"k:1134","type":"develops","note":"The man's flood simply takes his raft. The same phrase, காமக் கடும்புனல், in the woman's verse has her still swimming at midnight with no shore. The image remains and the endurance lengthens.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1180","b":"k:1076","type":"same_image","note":"1076 calls base men a beaten drum because they carry secrets to others. 1180 uses the same அறைபறை for her own eyes. An image of contempt for informers becomes an image of her helplessness: her body is what informs on her.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1175","b":"k:319","type":"parallel_domain","note":"319 says harm done to others in the morning comes back to you by itself in the afternoon. Manakkudavar's gloss on 1175 uses this same moral logic, with the eyes as offenders. A serious doctrine is applied, playfully, to a woman's own eyes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1171","b":"k:1170","type":"develops","note":"1170 wishes the eyes could travel to him as the heart does. Next, 1171 begins the attack on them. Parimelazhagar explicitly calls 1170 a preparation for this chapter, so what looks like a change of subject is a single thought.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1184","b":"k:1130","type":"qualifies","note":"1130 insists he lives in her heart, so he has not gone anywhere. In 1184 her mind and words are full of him, but her skin has its own view. Her body undercuts what chapter 113 argues.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1183","b":"k:1133","type":"same_image","note":"Both are inventories. The man in 1133 once had shame and manliness, and now has the palmyra horse, an instrument he can use. The woman in 1183 had grace and shame, and has been left sickness and pallor. Only his new possession is a tool.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1188","b":"k:1149","type":"develops","note":"In 1149 he has left her and the talk is about her; she can't be ashamed. In 1188 she states the injustice directly: the talk notices the symptom and not who caused it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1192","b":"k:11","type":"parallel_domain","note":"11 says that the rain, sustaining the world, is to be known as ambrosia. 1192 uses the whole opening doctrine for one person's survival. The same dependence that the Kural gives to all life at its start reappears as a lover's condition.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1196","b":"k:1163","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the carrying pole (கா). In 1163, love and shame hang at the ends of her life, and the pole is breaking. In 1196, love should hang on both people. The first is a struggle within one self, and the second is a fault between two.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1198","b":"k:1156","type":"develops","note":"வன்கண் is the same word in both. In 1156 he is hard because he could speak of leaving. In 1198 she calls herself hard for going on living without a word from him. The accusation has moved from him to her.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1202","b":"k:1207","type":"tension","note":"1202 says thinking of the beloved brings no pain, and 1207 says even thinking burns. If Parimelazhagar is right that a man speaks 1202 and a woman 1207, then the chapter makes a pointed contrast between their experience of memory.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1209","b":"k:1122","type":"develops","note":"1122 is the man's oath: the bond is like body and life. 1209 quotes the same promise, வேறல்லம், as the thing killing her. In 1124 he had said her going was death to him, and now she is the one dying.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1210","b":"k:1119","type":"tension","note":"In 1119, the man tells the moon not to appear, as no one else should have her beauty. In 1210, the woman begs the moon to stay, since it's the only thing she may share with him. His moon is a rival to exclusive possession, and hers is the only link she has.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1201","b":"k:1090","type":"develops","note":"1090 says love beats liquor because sight alone is enough. 1201 says memory alone is enough. As he moves away, the comparison holds, and needs less and less of her presence.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1211","b":"k:81","type":"parallel_domain","note":"81 says the whole point of keeping a home is to tend guests. 1211 applies that duty to a dream, which can be neither fed nor refused. A woman with an empty house is still measuring her situation in terms of the householder's virtue.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1212","b":"k:1162","type":"tension","note":"In Parimelazhagar's reading of 1162, sending a message shames her because it implies she's still alive. In 1212, telling him she has survived is just what she wants, provided it's in a dream. The dream permits what her waking shame forbids.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1216","b":"k:339","type":"parallel_domain","note":"339 says death is like sleep and birth is like waking from it, as an image of impermanence. 1216 wishes waking away altogether, for the sake of staying with her lover. If the two were read together, her wish would come close to a wish not to be reborn, though nothing in the verse says this.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:1220","b":"k:1130","type":"agrees","note":"Both end by accusing this town of not understanding. In 1130 he dwells in her heart, while in 1220 he comes in her dreams. The defence moves on as her grounds grow thinner.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1230","b":"k:759","type":"tension","note":"759 tells the reader bluntly to make wealth, for nothing cuts an enemy's pride so sharply. 1230 gives the cost of that advice from where it falls: a man whose nature has become wealth, and a woman who is dying of it. The separate books don't reconcile the claims.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1227","b":"k:1135","type":"agrees","note":"In 1135 the man complains of evening's anguish, and Parimelazhagar says love flowers at evening. 1227 makes that a full image in the woman's voice. Evening afflicts both, so longing's timetable isn't gendered, though only his has the palmyra horse.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1225","b":"k:1152","type":"develops","note":"1152 is the moment when even union was spoiled by the fear of parting, and on the commentators' reading of 1225 it was morning that brought this fear. Now that the feared thing has happened, morning is innocent. Fear has its own hours.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1231","b":"k:1114","type":"tension","note":"In 1114, the man says the lily would bow to the ground for shame before her eyes. In 1231, the friend says her eyes are ashamed before flowers. The same flower and the same shame have changed sides, measuring what absence has done.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1239","b":"k:1108","type":"develops","note":"1108 calls sweet the embrace that even a breeze can't split, using the verb போழ. In 1239 a breeze does split it, with the same verb போழ, and the result is the first sign of her pallor. The ideal of union and its failure are one word apart.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1238","b":"k:1187","type":"same_image","note":"The same incident in two voices: in 1187 she turned slightly in his arms and pallor came, and in 1238 he loosened his arms and her forehead paled. In her telling it happens to her, and in his it is what he did.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1247","b":"k:1163","type":"develops","note":"In 1163, love and shame hang in balance on the pole of her life, and she describes the strain. In 1247 she gives an ultimatum to drop one. Nothing has changed except that she can no longer carry them, and the next chapter tells us which goes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1249","b":"k:1130","type":"tension","note":"In 1130 she uses 'he lives within me' to refute the town's gossip. In 1249 she uses the same fact against her own heart, to stop it from leaving. The argument that once consoled her now traps her.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:1244","b":"k:1177","type":"develops","note":"In 1177 she curses her eyes to weep themselves dry. In 1244 they are eating her, and she wants them gone. The eyes haven't stopped, and her conflict with them has moved from revenge to getting rid of them.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1255","b":"k:966","type":"tension","note":"966 asks what could be gained, neither fame nor heaven, by going after those who despise you. 1255 is a direct reply from inside love: people with this sickness can't know that kind of honour. The ethics chapter on honour isn't refuted, but declared unavailable.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1253","b":"k:1203","type":"same_image","note":"The sneeze appears in two contrary ways: in 1203 it threatens and doesn't come, which she reads as him not thinking of her. In 1253 it comes uncontrollably, like her desire. So his love fails to arrive and hers can't be stopped.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1254","b":"k:1138","type":"agrees","note":"The same phrase, மறையிறந்து மன்று படும், in both. 1138 presents it as a general truth about women's love, with Parimelazhagar seeing her own case in it. In 1254 she says it in the first person, having once thought herself an exception.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1259","b":"k:1284","type":"agrees","note":"Both tell the same story in the first person: she went to sulk, and her heart went to join him. Whichever is read first, the second confirms that this is a repeating pattern, and that she knows it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1270","b":"k:1151","type":"develops","note":"1151 tells him to bring promises of quick return to those who will still be alive. 1270, in Parimelazhagar's reading, has the man finally arriving at that thought himself, from the other end of the journey and perhaps too late. The warning was given at the start of the separation, and he feels it only at the end.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1263","b":"k:591","type":"tension","note":"591 says ஊக்கம், inner resolve, is the only true possession. In 1263, the woman names a very similar quality in உள்ளம் as his companion in leaving her. The virtue the Book of Wealth values most is what she has been left for.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1268","b":"k:1224","type":"tension","note":"If Parimelazhagar's speaker is correct, evening is a coming feast for the man in 1268, and for the woman in 1224 it comes like a killer to the execution ground. They are thinking about the same hour, far apart, and with opposite expectations.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1267","b":"k:1259","type":"develops","note":"1267 is a plan: sulk, embrace or both. 1259 records how such a plan works out: she goes to sulk, and her heart gives way. The planner in 1267 hasn't learned anything from the experience, or she has and enjoys planning anyway.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1279","b":"k:1120","type":"tension","note":"In Parimelazhagar's reading, 1120 is the man refusing to elope because her feet can't bear even swan's down. In 1279 she looks at those same feet, her way of saying she will walk. The compliment had been an excuse, and her glance answers it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1273","b":"k:706","type":"same_image","note":"706 says the face shows what is in the heart, as crystal shows what is next to it, in the chapter on reading signs in court. 1273 uses crystal the same way, with a thread inside it. Courtier and lover both depend on the transparency of the surface.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1276","b":"k:1152","type":"agrees","note":"In 1152 the fear of parting spoils union, and Parimelazhagar cites a poem where unusual fussing is the warning sign. 1276 makes that the very argument: too much tenderness is proof of a departure.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1277","b":"k:1157","type":"same_image","note":"Both have bangles announcing the departure of the lord of the shore. In 1157 they announce it to others, and in 1277 they tell her, before she herself knows. The sign has moved inward.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1282","b":"k:104","type":"same_image","note":"104 says that those who know value a favour the size of a millet seed as if it were a palmyra. 1282 uses the same pair for love and sulking. The scale of ethical gratitude is used for measuring love's excess, and this time the small thing is to be removed altogether.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1283","b":"k:1178","type":"agrees","note":"Both state that he doesn't truly cherish her (பேணாது) and that the eyes can't rest without seeing him (காணாது அமை-). In 1178 this is a complaint on her eyes' folly, and in 1283 it's her reason for not quarrelling. The same fact has moved from grief to an explanation.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1285","b":"k:1127","type":"same_image","note":"In 1127 she won't line her eyes because he's within them and would be hidden. In 1285 the stick is what the eye can't see. In the absence, kohl was an obstacle. In his presence, he himself is like the stick, too close to be seen.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1289","b":"k:90","type":"parallel_domain","note":"90 says that a guest wilts at a cold look as an anicham flower wilts when smelled. 1289 makes love softer still than a flower. In both, what is finest about a relationship fails at a slight change in the other person's face.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1290","b":"k:1259","type":"same_image","note":"1259 tells it in her voice: I went to sulk, my heart gave way. 1290 tells it in his: she sulked with her eyes and came faster than I did. Each tells the other's defeat as a private pleasure, though for her it's defeat, and for him it's a fond memory.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"k:1293","b":"k:796","type":"parallel_domain","note":"796 says ruin has one benefit: it's a measuring rod for friends. 1293 applies this proverb to one's own heart, which she treats as a friend who proves false. The inner dialogue has absorbed a whole social ethic of friendship.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1296","b":"k:1244","type":"same_image","note":"In 1244 her eyes eat her alive with longing, and in 1296 her heart does. The same verb is used for what are, in effect, her own organs, and these chapters keep treating her as a person being consumed by her parts.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1297","b":"k:1247","type":"develops","note":"1247 demands that the heart give up either love or shame. 1297 tells us what happened: shame got forgotten. The chapter between them, on breaking restraint, is how.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1300","b":"k:1130","type":"tension","note":"ஏதிலர், the 'stranger', has been her word for him in the town's mouth, in 1129 and 1130. In 1300 the same word is used for a stranger, and now the one who isn't one's own is one's own heart. By the end, the outsider is inside.","grade":"forced","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1302","b":"k:1328","type":"same_image","note":"உப்பு is the word in both. In 1302 the salt is the sulk, measured carefully. In 1328 the salt is the sweetness of the union that follows, as Parimelazhagar glosses it. The cook's image runs through both chapters, with the same word for the seasoning and the taste.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1306","b":"k:1191","type":"tension","note":"1191 calls requited love the stoneless fruit, the ideal of enjoyment with nothing in the way. 1306 says love without quarrels is fruit gone bad or not yet ripe. So the obstacle that 1191 wishes away, 1306 says is what makes the fruit ripe.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:1310","b":"k:366","type":"parallel_domain","note":"366 warns the renunciant to fear craving (அவா), because it deceives. In 1310, the sulking man names the same craving as what drives his heart to her. He uses the moral vocabulary correctly, with no intention of following it.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1317","b":"k:1203","type":"tension","note":"The same folk belief at two points in the story. In 1203 she longs for a sneeze as proof he's thinking of her. In 1317 he sneezes in front of her, and the same belief proves someone else is thinking of him. A sign that means what one fears can't be falsified.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1316","b":"k:1125","type":"develops","note":"In 1125, the man is clever at the friend's expense: I would remember her if I forgot. 1316 shows the woman turning this logic on him: you say you remembered, so you forgot. His own wit has become the trap.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1315","b":"k:1262","type":"parallel_domain","note":"On Parimelazhagar's reading of 1262, she keeps him in mind at death so as to reach him in the next birth. In 1315, 'this birth' is what makes her cry. Rebirth, for the renunciant a fate to escape, is in this book something lovers want to share.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"k:1320","b":"k:1113","type":"tension","note":"In 1113, he went over her body part by part, comparing it to shoots, pearls and spears, and she was pleased to be praised. In 1320 she treats that very habit of comparing part by part as evidence that he has others in mind. The praise of chapter 112 is the root of the suspicion.","grade":"wrong","better":null},{"a":"k:1323","b":"k:1103","type":"develops","note":"1103 asks whether the Lotus-eyed one's world is sweeter than sleeping on the arms of one's beloved. 1323 repeats the challenge to heaven with a stranger choice: it's the quarrel that outdoes heaven now. Between these two verses, love has come to value its own frictions most.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1327","b":"k:1088","type":"develops","note":"In 1088, a warrior laments his strength being broken by her forehead, and takes it as defeat. In 1327, the man has worked out that losing is the way to win. The book's military imagery, which begins with injury, ends in a pleasantly ruined scoreboard.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1329","b":"k:1169","type":"tension","note":"In 1169, the woman in separation says the long nights are crueller than the cruel man himself. In 1329, the man in reunion prays for night to lengthen. The same long night is torment or wish, depending on whether the other person is there.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1326","b":"k:942","type":"parallel_domain","note":"942 says the body needs no medicine if one eats only after the last meal has digested. 1326 takes the same physician's advice and makes it a rule of love: the pause between meals is what keeps appetite and health.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1324","b":"k:1258","type":"agrees","note":"Both say soft words are the weapon that breaks her defences. In 1258 she says it with bitterness and calls him a thief, and in 1324 she says it with something like eagerness. The weapon hasn't changed, and what she wants from it has.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1","b":"k:543","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 1 says the world has ஆதி பகவன் as its first (முதற்றே உலகு), as letters have அ. Verse 543 uses the same word for the king's sceptre: it stands as the ஆதி of the antaṇar's books and of aram (அறத்திற்கும் ஆதியாய் நின்றது மன்னவன் கோல்), which Parimelazhagar glosses as their cause (காரணம்). The pairing shows the book giving the world one 'first' in its opening and giving aram itself a second, political one in Book 2.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:2","b":"k:315","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 2: what has learning yielded, if one does not bow at the good feet (நற்றாள் தொழாஅர் எனின்)? Verse 315: does understanding achieve anything (அறிவினான் ஆகுவதுண்டோ) if one does not guard against another's pain as one's own? Both are rhetorical questions that judge knowledge by a test outside knowledge, worship in one and compassion in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:2","b":"k:356","type":"develops","note":"Verse 2 asks what use learning is (கற்றதனால் ஆய பயன் என்கொல்) if the learned do not worship the feet of the one of pure knowledge. Verse 356 states the fruit positively: those who learn here and see the true thing (கற்றீண்டு மெய்ப்பொருள் கண்டார்) reach the path of not returning. Parimelazhagar reads verse 2's fruit as ending rebirth too, so 356 spells out what verse 2 leaves as a question.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:3","b":"k:6","type":"same_image","note":"Verses 3 and 6 end on the same words, நீடுவாழ்வார், 'will live long'. Verse 3 gives this to those who reach the feet (மாணடி சேர்ந்தார்); verse 6 gives it to those who stand in the path of conduct free of falsehood (பொய்தீர் ஒழுக்க நெறி நின்றார்) of the one who quenched the five. The repeated ending puts devotion and conduct side by side as routes to the same result.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:4","b":"k:350","type":"develops","note":"Verse 4 describes the one without wanting or not-wanting (வேண்டுதல் வேண்டாமை இலான்) and says those who reach his feet have no affliction. Verse 350 turns the same description into an instruction: hold to the hold of the one without attachment (பற்றற்றான் பற்றினை), in order to let attachment go (பற்று விடற்கு). Parimelazhagar notes that பற்றற்றான் is chosen to match the invocation chapter; 350 supplies the mechanism verse 4 leaves unstated.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:4","b":"k:362","type":"same_image","note":"Both verses turn on the word வேண்டாமை but use it differently. In verse 4 it is paired with வேண்டுதல் as something the Lord lacks, which Parimelazhagar glosses as aversion (வெறுத்தல்). In 362 வேண்டாமை is desirelessness, the thing to want (வேண்டாமை வேண்ட வரும்) so that birthlessness comes. A reader should not carry one sense into the other.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:4","b":"k:628","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 4 says there is no இடும்பை at any time for those who reach the feet of the one free of liking and aversion. Verse 628, in the chapter on not being undone by trouble, says one who does not desire pleasure and calls இடும்பை natural (இன்பம் விழையான் இடும்பை இயல்பென்பான்) does not come to pain. The Book 2 verse keeps the link between absent desire and absent suffering but drops the devotional route, and it concedes that the affliction itself still arrives.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:5","b":"k:38","type":"tension","note":"Verse 5 says both kinds of deed, which belong to darkness (இருள்சேர் இருவினையும்), do not attach to those who dwell on the Lord's praise; Parimelazhagar explains that good deeds too are a cause of birth. Verse 38 says that doing good (நன்றாற்றின்) with no day lost is the stone that blocks the road of one's days, which Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar both read as ending rebirth. On those readings one verse counts good deeds among what binds and the other makes them what frees.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:5","b":"k:352","type":"develops","note":"Verse 5 ties the two kinds of deed to இருள், which Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar gloss as delusion. Verse 352 says that for those of stainless sight who have left confusion (மருள்நீங்கி மாசறு காட்சியவர்க்கு), darkness goes and joy comes (இருள்நீங்கி இன்பம் பயக்கும்). The later verse gives knowledge as what removes the darkness that verse 5 only names.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:5","b":"k:388","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 5 speaks of the praise of இறைவன், the chapter's word for the Lord. Verse 388 says a king who does justice and protects is set down as இறை for the people (மக்கட்கு இறையென்று வைக்கப்படும்). The shared word shows that the title used for the deity in chapter 1 is one a human ruler earns by conduct in Book 2.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:6","b":"k:126","type":"develops","note":"Verse 6 promises long life to those who stand in the path of the one who quenched the five. Verse 126 gives the human practice and its reward: if one can draw in the five like a tortoise in one birth (ஆமைபோல் ஐந்தடக்கல்), it is a protection for seven (எழுமையும் ஏமாப்புடைத்து). Verse 126 says அடக்கல், holding in, where verse 6 says அவித்தான், quenched, so the follower's task is stated more modestly than the exemplar's.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:7","b":"k:622","type":"tension","note":"Verse 7 says it is hard to remove the mind's anxiety (மனக்கவலை மாற்றல் அரிது) except for those who reach the feet of the one without equal. Verse 622 says a flood of affliction perishes when a person of understanding thinks in his own mind (அறிவுடையான் உள்ளத்தின் உள்ளக் கெடும்). The conflict holds if மனக்கவலை is ordinary mental distress, as Manakkudavar's gloss allows; Parimelazhagar narrows it to the sufferings of rebirth, which 622 does not address.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:7","b":"k:1072","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 7 makes freedom from mental anxiety rare, reserved for those who reach the Lord's feet. Verse 1072 says, with irony, that the base are better off than those who know the good, because they have no distress in their hearts (நெஞ்சத்து அவலம் இலர்). Read beside verse 7 it marks that mere absence of worry is not the state the invocation is praising, since the base have it too.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:8","b":"k:30","type":"develops","note":"Verse 8 calls the deity அறவாழி அந்தணன், joining அந்தணன் to aram. Verse 30 defines the word: those called அந்தணர் are the people of aram (அறவோர்), because they act with kindness toward every living being (செந்தண்மை பூண்டொழுகலான்). Verse 30 gives the reason for the pairing of title and virtue that verse 8 assumes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:8","b":"k:989","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 8 uses ஆழி for the deity (அறவாழி), read as 'sea of aram' by Parimelazhagar, who also records the reading 'wheel of aram'. Verse 989 calls the worthy சான்றாண்மைக்கு ஆழி and says they do not shift even if the age shifts; there Parimelazhagar reads ஆழி as the shore of the sea of worth. The same word is attached to a virtue in both verses, to a god in one and to people in the other, and its sense is disputed in both.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:8","b":"k:1164","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 8 says the other seas are hard to swim (பிறவாழி நீந்தல் அரிது) except for those who reach the feet of the antaṇan who is a sea of aram; Parimelazhagar takes the other seas to be wealth and pleasure and the feet to be the raft. In 1164 the woman says the sea of kāmam is there and the safe raft to swim it is not (காமக் கடல் ... அதுநீந்தும் ஏமப் புணை ... இல்). Book 3 shows from inside one of the seas that Parimelazhagar finds in verse 8, with the raft named and missing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:9","b":"k:6","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 9 treats a sense organ that does not grasp (கோளில் பொறி) as the type of uselessness, while verse 6 praises the one who quenched the five of the sense-gates (பொறிவாயில் ஐந்தவித்தான்). Parimelazhagar reads what is quenched in verse 6 as the five desires that travel by the senses, not the senses. Taken together the two verses of the chapter support that reading: a dead sense is a defect, and what is praised is something else.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:9","b":"k:418","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 9 says a head that does not bow at the feet of the one of eight qualities is without worth, like a sense organ that does not grasp (கோளில் பொறியின் குணமிலவே). Verse 418 says ears not pierced by listening are of a deaf kind even though they hear (கேட்பினும் கேளாத் தகையவே). Both measure a part of the body by one proper use and call it dead without that use; the use is worship in one and learning by ear in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:9","b":"k:574","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 9 compares the unbowing head to an organ that is present and does not sense. Verse 574 asks what eyes without kindly regard do, beyond seeming to be in the face (உளபோல் முகத்து எவன் செய்யும்). The figure of the organ that is there and useless is applied in Book 2 to a ruler's compassion.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:10","b":"k:8","type":"same_image","note":"Verses 8 and 10 both speak of swimming a sea with the Lord's feet as the condition. Verse 10 names the sea as birth (பிறவிப் பெருங்கடல்); verse 8 says only 'the other seas' (பிறவாழி), which Parimelazhagar takes as wealth and pleasure. On his reading the chapter has two different seas; without it, verse 8 can look like a first statement of verse 10.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:10","b":"k:349","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 10 makes reaching the Lord's feet the condition for crossing birth: those who do not reach them do not swim it (நீந்தார் இறைவனடி சேராதார்). Verse 349 gives a condition with no deity in it: the moment attachment is cut, that cuts birth (பற்றற்ற கண்ணே பிறப்பறுக்கும்). The next verse, 350, joins the two by telling one to hold to the one without attachment in order to drop attachment, so 349 shows what reaching the feet has to accomplish.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:10","b":"k:1167","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 10 says those who reach the Lord's feet swim the great sea of birth and others do not. In 1167 the woman says she swims the fierce flood of kāmam and sees no shore (நீந்திக் கரைகாணேன்), alone even at midnight. The swimmer who cannot cross, stated as doctrine in verse 10, is spoken in the first person in Book 3.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:31","b":"k:169","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 31 says aram yields wealth and asks what greater gain a living being has. Verse 169 admits the opposite case occurs: the prosperity of the crooked-hearted and the ruin of the upright are to be thought about (நினைக்கப்படும்). Parimelazhagar says such outcomes cannot come from crookedness or uprightness and sends the wise to look for a cause in past deeds; either way 169 limits how far verse 31 can be read as a promise visible in one life. His gloss on 31 itself takes செல்வம் as heavenly goods.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:31","b":"k:754","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 31 says aram yields distinction and yields wealth too (சிறப்பீனும் செல்வமும் ஈனும்). Verse 754 uses the same construction with the terms turned round: wealth that came by known means and without wrong yields aram and yields pleasure too (அறன்ஈனும் இன்பமும் ஈனும்). Book 1 has virtue producing wealth and Book 2 has rightly earned wealth producing virtue, in matching words.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:32","b":"k:117","type":"develops","note":"Verse 32 says no ruin (கேடு) surpasses forgetting aram. Verse 117 gives the other side: the world will not count as ruin (கெடுவாக வையாது) the low state of one who stays fair and rests in the good. Together they define ruin by one's relation to virtue and not by one's circumstances.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:32","b":"k:532","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 32 names forgetting (மறத்தல்) of aram as the greatest ruin. In the chapter on heedlessness, 532 says forgetfulness kills renown (பொச்சாப்புக் கொல்லும் புகழை) as unrelieved poverty kills understanding. The idea that the damage comes through lapse of attention, without any act of wrongdoing, is carried from ethics into statecraft.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:33","b":"k:297","type":"tension","note":"Verse 33 asks for aram everywhere and without a break (ஓவாதே ... எல்லாம் செயல்). Verse 297 says that if one keeps to not lying, then not doing the other virtues is good (அறம்பிற செய்யாமை செய்யாமை நன்று). On Parimelazhagar's reading of 297, that it is better to hold one virtue unfailingly than to take on many and fail in some, this is advice to narrow what verse 33 tells one to spread as widely as possible.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:33","b":"k:477","type":"develops","note":"Verse 33 limits its command with ஒல்லும் வகையான், 'in the way that is possible'. Parimelazhagar explains this for the householder as acting in proportion to one's means, and 477 says so directly: give knowing the measure (அளவறிந்து ஈக), for that is the way to guard wealth while giving. Verse 477 gives the practical content of the qualifier in verse 33. This edition prints அறவறிந்து in 477; the commentary reads அளவு அறிந்து.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:33","b":"k:673","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 33 says to do the work of aram by whatever means one can (ஒல்லும் வகையான்), without ceasing, wherever it can go (செல்லும்வாய் எல்லாம் செயல்). Verse 673, on how to carry out a task, uses the same words: where it is possible the deed is good, and where it is not, act looking for a way that goes (ஒல்லும்வாய் ... செல்லும்வாய் நோக்கிச் செயல்). The rule for virtue and the rule for state action are built from the same vocabulary of the possible. This edition prints the first word of 673 as ஙல்லும், a typo for ஒல்லும், as its own commentary shows.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:34","b":"k:298","type":"develops","note":"Verse 34 sets inner cleanness against outward show. Verse 298 makes the same division and adds how the inner kind is known: outer purity is had by water, and inner purity is seen through truthfulness (அகந்தூய்மை வாய்மையால் காணப்படும்). It gives a test for the state that verse 34 only asserts. This edition prints புறள்தூய்மை for புறந்தூய்மை.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:35","b":"k:161","type":"develops","note":"Verse 35 lists envy (அழுக்காறு) first among what aram must avoid. Verse 161 turns that item into a positive rule: take as your way of conduct (ஒழுக்காறாக் கொள்க) a nature that has no envy in the heart. The chapter on envy opens by treating as a discipline what verse 35 states as an exclusion.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:35","b":"k:431","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 35 defines aram by the absence of four faults. Verse 431 defines a sound prosperity the same way for a ruler: the increase of those without arrogance, anger and smallness (செருக்கும் சினமும் சிறுமையும் இல்லார்) has dignity. Anger is on both lists; Parimelazhagar glosses சிறுமை in 431 as lust, which would bring the two lists closer still.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:36","b":"k:233","type":"tension","note":"Verse 36 calls aram a companion that does not perish (பொன்றாத் துணை). Verse 233 says that apart from high renown nothing in the world stands unperishing (புகழல்லால் பொன்றாது நிற்பதொன்று இல்), using the same word. Read literally the second excludes the first. Parimelazhagar's reading of 36, that the merit goes with the soul into another body, puts it outside 'the world' of 233; Kalaignar reads 36 itself as lasting fame, which removes the conflict another way.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:36","b":"k:398","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 36 says aram done now stays as a companion at death. Verse 398 says the learning one has acquired in one birth is a protection through seven (எழுமையும் ஏமாப்புடைத்து). Both speak of something gained in this life that is not lost with the body; Book 2 says of learning what Book 1 says of virtue.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:37","b":"k:270","type":"agrees","note":"On the reading of Parimelazhagar and Pope, verse 37 explains the difference between palanquin rider and bearer by aram done before. Verse 270 reasons the same way about a whole society: the reason the have-nots are many is that those who do tavam are few and those who do not are many. Both read present inequality as the result of past practice; Parimelazhagar adds that wealth and want in 270 also mean understanding and its lack.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:37","b":"k:973","type":"tension","note":"Verse 37 says there is no need to state the way of aram: look between the one who carries the palanquin and the one who rides (பொறுத்தானொடு ஊர்ந்தான் இடை). Parimelazhagar and Pope take this to mean that the fruit of virtue can be seen in who rides and who carries. Verse 973 says those who are not high are not high even when seated high (மேலிருந்தும் மேலல்லார் மேலல்லர்), and Parimelazhagar reads the seating there as wealth and poverty. On those readings one verse lets position show merit and the other denies that position shows anything about the person. The Tamil of 37 is elliptical and does not itself say which of the two is the virtuous one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:38","b":"k:326","type":"tension","note":"Verse 38 says daily good is a stone that shuts the road of one's வாழ்நாள்; Parimelazhagar takes வாழ்நாள் as the days of living in a body, so that shutting the road means no more births. Verse 326 says Death does not advance on the வாழ்நாள் of one who keeps to not killing. On Parimelazhagar's reading the same word names something virtue ends in 38 and something virtue protects in 326. On Kalaignar's reading of 38, where the stone smooths life's road, there is no conflict.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:38","b":"k:334","type":"develops","note":"Verse 38 treats each day as something that can fall away unused or be filled with good. Verse 334 says what a day is: it shows itself as one thing called a day, and it is a blade sawing through life (உயிர் ஈரும் வாள்), for those who perceive it. Verse 334 gives the reason for the daily urgency of verse 38.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:39","b":"k:156","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 39 says only what comes by aram is joy (அறத்தான் வருவதே இன்பம்) and the rest lies outside and has no renown either. Verse 156 allows that those who retaliate do get joy, for one day (ஒறுத்தார்க்கு ஒருநாளை இன்பம்), while those who forbear have renown for as long as things last (பொன்றுந் துணையும் புகழ்; until the world perishes, in Parimelazhagar's gloss). It keeps verse 39's pairing of joy and renown but concedes a short pleasure outside virtue, so the 'only' of 39 reads as a judgment of worth and not a denial that such pleasure is felt.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:40","b":"k:280","type":"agrees","note":"Verse 40 names blame (பழி) as the thing to escape. Verse 280 says neither shaving nor growing the hair is needed if one gives up what the world has blamed (உலகம் பழித்தது ஒழித்துவிடின்). Both make avoidance of the blameworthy the working test, verse 280 applying it to the ascetic in place of outward marks.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:40","b":"k:466","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 40 gives a two-sided rule: what one should do is aram, and what one should escape is blame (செயற்பாலது ... அறனே, உயற்பாலது ... பழி). Verse 466 gives the same two sides to a ruler as consequences: one is ruined by doing what is not fit to do, and ruined also by not doing what is fit (செய்தக்க செய்யாமையானும் கெடும்). Book 2 restates the pair as policy and makes the omission as costly as the wrong act.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:40","b":"k:656","type":"develops","note":"Verse 40 says blame is what a person should escape. Verse 656 tests that rule at its hardest: even if one must see the hunger of the mother who bore one, do not do the deed the worthy condemn (சான்றோர் பழிக்கும் வினை). It shows that the avoidance of blame in verse 40 is meant to hold against a competing duty, and it names whose blame counts.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:242","b":"k:132","type":"same_image","note":"242 ends பல்லாற்றால் தேரினும் அஃதே துணை: however many ways you examine, அருள் alone is the companion. 132 ends with the same words, தேரினும் அஃதே துணை, said of ஒழுக்கம் (conduct). The identical closing with its exclusive ஏ is given to two different things; read strictly the two claims compete, read as emphasis each chapter is crowning its own subject.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:242","b":"k:324","type":"develops","note":"242 tells one to search by a good path and hold to அருள் (நல்லாற்றான் நாடி அருளாள்க). 324 asks what the good path is (நல்லாறு எனப்படுவது யாதெனின்) and answers: the way that keeps in view not killing anything (யாதொன்றும் கொல்லாமை சூழும் நெறி). 324 gives content to the நல்லாறு that 242 leaves open, and ties அருள் to not killing.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:243","b":"k:5","type":"same_image","note":"243 says those whose hearts are joined with அருள் (அருள்சேர்ந்த நெஞ்சினார்) do not enter the painful world joined with darkness (இருள்சேர்ந்த இன்னா உலகம்). 5 says the two kinds of deed joined with darkness (இருள்சேர் இருவினையும்) do not join (சேரா) those devoted to the Lord's praise. Both build on சேர், what one is joined to: in 5 darkness is a quality of deeds (Parimelazhagar: மயக்கம்), in 243 it is a place one enters, and joining something else keeps it away.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:243","b":"k:121","type":"parallel_domain","note":"243: no entry into the dark painful world for hearts with அருள். 121: restraint leads among the immortals, lack of restraint drives into thick darkness (அடங்காமை ஆரிருள் உய்த்து விடும்). Both use darkness as the destination of a failed virtue, and Parimelazhagar glosses both as a hell; 243 states only the exemption, 121 states both directions.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:244","b":"k:268","type":"same_image","note":"244 pairs மன்னுயிர் and தன்னுயிர்: care for other lives frees one's own life from fear. 268 uses the same pair the other way round: one who has fully gained his own life (தன்னுயிர் தான்அறப் பெற்றானை) is worshipped by all other lives (ஏனைய மன்னுயிரெல்லாம் தொழும்). Together they show the relation running in both directions, from care of others to one's own safety and from self-mastery to others' reverence.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:244","b":"k:326","type":"agrees","note":"244: one who tends living beings and holds to அருள் (மன்னுயிர் ஓம்பி அருளாள்வாற்கு) has no deed his own life need fear (தன்னுயிர் அஞ்சும் வினை இல்). 326: on the days of one who lives by not killing, Death that eats life does not advance (வாழ்நாள்மேல் செல்லாது உயிருண்ணுங் கூற்று). Both say that sparing other lives secures one's own, once in terms of deeds and once in terms of Death.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:244","b":"k:327","type":"qualifies","note":"244 promises that the one who protects lives has nothing his own life need fear. 327 uses the same word, தன்னுயிர், to command not taking another's life even if one's own life goes (தன்னுயிர் நீப்பினும் செய்யற்க). 327 shows that the safety promised in 244 is not the condition for the rule: the rule holds even where one's own life is lost.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:245","b":"k:25","type":"same_image","note":"245 calls the wide earth where the wind moves a witness (ஞாலம் கரி) that those who hold to அருள் have no distress. 25 calls Indra witness enough (இந்திரனே சாலுங் கரி) to the power of one who quenched the five senses. Both close an unprovable claim about a virtue's power by naming a கரி, a witness; 245 appeals to the whole visible world, 25 to one figure from story.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:245","b":"k:169","type":"tension","note":"245 says flatly that those who hold to அருள் have no distress (அல்லல் அருளாள்வார்க்கு இல்லை), and Parimelazhagar takes this to cover this life. 169 says the prosperity of the crooked-hearted and the ruin of the upright (செவ்வியான் கேடும்) are to be pondered, which concedes that the upright do meet ruin. The conflict holds if அல்லல் includes worldly ruin and if 169 is read as admitting such cases occur; Parimelazhagar softens 169 with 'if they occur' (உளவாயின்).","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:246","b":"k:32","type":"agrees","note":"246 says those who leave அருள் and go on doing wrong have, they say, parted from பொருள் and forgotten (பொருள்நீங்கிப் பொச்சாந்தார்). 32 says there is no ruin greater than forgetting virtue (அதனை மறத்தலின் ஊங்கில்லை கேடு). Both treat wrongdoing as a kind of forgetting; Parimelazhagar reads the பொருள் of 246 as the lasting good, i.e. virtue, which brings the two closer, while Kalaignar reads it as being without wealth.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:246","b":"k:250","type":"develops","note":"246 describes the cruel as people who have forgotten (பொச்சாந்தார்); Parimelazhagar says what they forgot is their own suffering. 250 prescribes an act of memory: when going against the weaker, think of yourself before the stronger (வலியார்முன் தன்னை நினைக்க). Within the chapter, 250 supplies the remedy for the forgetting that 246 diagnoses.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:247","b":"k:248","type":"qualifies","note":"247 puts wealth and அருள் in parallel (பொருளில்லார்க்கு இவ்வுலகம் இல்லாகி யாங்கு). 248 then breaks the parallel: those without wealth may flower one day (பூப்பர் ஒருகால்), but those without அருள் are finished and can hardly become anything again (அற்றார் மற்றாதல் அரிது). The simile of 247 holds for what each lack costs, not for whether the loss can be repaired.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:248","b":"k:134","type":"parallel_domain","note":"248: loss of wealth can be reversed, loss of அருள் cannot (அற்றார் மற்றாதல் அரிது). 134: even if forgotten, the recited text can be learned again (மறப்பினும் ஓத்துக் கொளலாகும்), but a Brahmin's birth is ruined when conduct declines (பிறப்பொழுக்கங் குன்றக் கெடும்). Both set a recoverable possession against an unrecoverable loss of character, in the domains of wealth and of learning.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:249","b":"k:355","type":"same_image","note":"249 compares virtue done by one without அருள் to an unclear person seeing the true thing (தெருளாதான் மெய்ப்பொருள் கண்டற்று). 355 defines understanding by the same phrase: seeing the true thing in whatever thing (மெய்ப்பொருள் காண்பது அறிவு). 355 shows why the comparison in 249 bites: seeing the true thing is what clarity consists in, so without clarity it does not happen, or happens only once by chance as Parimelazhagar reads it (ஒருகால்).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:250","b":"k:316","type":"develops","note":"316 gives the general rule: what one has oneself felt to be painful one must not do to another (இன்னா எனத்தான் உணர்ந்தவை ... பிறன்கண் செயல்). 250 applies the same use of one's own experience to a difference in strength: recall your own position before the stronger when you stand over the weaker. 250 is the rule of 316 turned into a specific exercise of memory.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:321","b":"k:254","type":"develops","note":"321 asks what virtuous action is and answers not killing (அறவினை யாதெனின் கொல்லாமை); killing brings all other deeds. 254 uses the same question form for அருள்: what is அருள் and what is not comes down to not killing and killing (யாதெனின் கொல்லாமை கோறல்). Read together, not killing is the content of both அருள் and virtuous action, which joins the chapter on அருள் to the chapter on not killing. (The first letter of 254 is missing in this edition: ருளல்லது for அருளல்லது.)","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:321","b":"k:297","type":"tension","note":"321 makes not killing the answer to what virtuous action is. 297 says that if one keeps up not lying, it is good not to do other virtues (பொய்யாமை ஆற்றின் அறம்பிற செய்யாமை நன்று). Each verse lets one virtue stand for the whole, and they pick different ones; the conflict holds if both are read as claims of sufficiency and not as chapter emphasis. 323 settles the order inside this chapter by putting not lying second.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:322","b":"k:225","type":"develops","note":"322 puts feeding and sustaining lives at the head of the virtues. 225 says the strength of the strong is enduring hunger, and that comes behind the strength of those who remove hunger (அப்பசியை மாற்றுவார் ஆற்றலின் பின்); Parimelazhagar takes the first group to be ascetics. 225 supplies a ranking that fits 322: not harming is completed by actively keeping others alive.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:323","b":"k:292","type":"develops","note":"323 ranks not lying behind not killing. 292 says even falsehood stands in the place of truth if it yields good free of fault (புரைதீர்ந்த நன்மை பயக்கும் எனின்), and Parimelazhagar's example is a false word that saves a life from death. 292 shows the ranking of 323 at work inside the chapter on truth: where truthful speech and a life pull apart, the life decides.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:324","b":"k:291","type":"same_image","note":"324: if asked what is called the good path, it is the way that keeps in view not killing anything (யாதெனின் யாதொன்றும் கொல்லாமை சூழும் நெறி). 291: if asked what is called truth, it is speech with no harm at all in it (யாதெனின் யாதொன்றும் தீமை இலாத சொலல்). The two definitions share their wording, எனப்படுவது யாதெனின் யாதொன்றும், and both define a positive term by the complete absence of harm, in action and in speech.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:325","b":"k:261","type":"develops","note":"325 says that among all who renounced fearing their condition (நிலைஅஞ்சி நீத்தாருள்), the one who fears killing and keeps not killing in view is chief. 261 defines the form of தவம் as bearing one's own pain and doing no harm to living beings (உயிர்க்குறுகண் செய்யாமை). 261 states the two parts of the ascetic's practice; 325 says which part decides rank among ascetics.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:325","b":"k:984","type":"agrees","note":"325 ranks the renouncer who keeps to not killing first. 984 says austerity lies in the goodness of not killing (கொல்லா நலத்தது நோன்மை), and sets it beside worth, which lies in not speaking others' faults. The same claim about renouncers appears in the third part of the book of wealth, used there as the measure for a layman's virtue.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:327","b":"k:656","type":"parallel_domain","note":"327: even at the cost of your own life, do not do the deed that takes a life. 656: even if you must see your mother hungry, do not do the deed the worthy condemn (ஈன்றாள் பசிகாண்பான் ஆயினும் செய்யற்க). Both have the form 'even at this extreme cost, செய்யற்க', and both name a வினை as what is forbidden; the first is addressed to the virtuous life, the second to a minister's conduct of affairs.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:327","b":"k:1017","type":"parallel_domain","note":"327 puts another's life above one's own. 1017 says those ruled by shame give up life for shame and do not give up shame for life (நாணால் உயிரைத் துறப்பர் உயிர்ப்பொருட்டால் நாண்துறவார்). In both, one's own life is not the highest value; what outranks it is another creature's life in 327 and one's own sense of shame in 1017.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:328","b":"k:311","type":"agrees","note":"328 refuses gain that comes by killing, however great. 311 says that even if one could get wealth that brings distinction (சிறப்பீனும் செல்வம் பெறினும்), not doing hurt to others is the settled stand of the stainless. The two adjacent chapters make the same refusal for the lesser harm and the greatest one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:328","b":"k:657","type":"parallel_domain","note":"328: for the worthy (சான்றோர்க்கு), gain got by killing is the lowest (கடை). 657: the utter poverty of the worthy is above gain got with blame (பழிமலைந்து எய்திய ஆக்கத்தின் சான்றோர் கழிநல்குரவே தலை). Both judge ஆக்கம் by its source from the standpoint of the சான்றோர், with கடை in one and தலை in the other; 328 belongs to the book of virtue and 657 to the conduct of state business.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:329","b":"k:551","type":"develops","note":"329 treats killers as the settled example of baseness. 551 takes that example as a measure and says a king who takes up harassing his people and does wrong is crueller than those who have taken up killing (கொலைமேற்கொண்டாரிற் கொடிதே). The book of statecraft uses the low point fixed in 329 to rank a bad ruler below it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:330","b":"k:37","type":"parallel_domain","note":"330 infers a past deed from a present condition: the wretched are those who took life. 37 says there is no need to say 'this is the way of virtue': look between the one who carries the palanquin and the one who rides (சிவிகை பொறுத்தானோடு ஊர்ந்தான் இடை). On Pope's and Parimelazhagar's reading both argue from visible fortune back to deeds, 37 for good fortune and 330 for bad; Kalaignar reads 37 differently, as about how the virtuous bear life.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:330","b":"k:270","type":"parallel_domain","note":"330 explains diseased and failing lives by past killing. 270 explains why the have-nots are many: those who practise austerity are few and those who do not are many (நோற்பார் சிலர் பலர் நோலாதவர்). Both give a cause in conduct for an unequal condition seen in the world, 330 for individuals and 270 for the proportion in a population.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:330","b":"k:326","type":"develops","note":"330 says those with afflicted bodies and a failing, evil life (செயிர் உடம்பின் செல்லாத்தீ வாழ்க்கையவர்) are, they say, people who separated life from body. 326 says Death does not advance on the days of one who lives by not killing. The two verses are the chapter's pair of consequences, both stated in terms of body and lifespan: the non-killer's days are spared and the killer's body and living are spoiled. Parimelazhagar places the killing of 330 in a former birth; the Tamil does not say which birth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:381","b":"k:59","type":"same_image","note":"381 calls the king who has all six limbs (படை, குடி, கூழ், அமைச்சு, நட்பு, அரண்) 'அரசருள் ஏறு', the male beast among kings; 59 says a man whose home lacks a wife who seeks good name has no 'ஏறுபோல் பீடு நடை' before those who scorn him. The same figure of the proud male animal is made to depend on what a man has behind him: a kingdom's six supports in one case, a household's good name in the other. (Parimelazhagar takes ஏறு in 59 as a lion; the word itself only says 'male'.)","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:381","b":"k:740","type":"qualifies","note":"381 measures a king by possession (ஆறும் உடையான்), and குடி, which Parimelazhagar takes as the land, is one of the six. 740 says a land that has every excellence is still without use (பயமின்றே) if it lacks accord with its ruler (வேந்தமைவு இல்லாத நாடு). Read together, having the limb is not enough: the limb and the king must fit each other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:381","b":"k:750","type":"qualifies","note":"381 counts the fort (அரண்) among the six things whose possession makes the leading king. 750 says a fort with every excellence (எனைமாட்சித்து ஆகியக் கண்ணும்) is as nothing with those who lack excellence in action (வினைமாட்சி இல்லார்கண் இல்லது). The later verse adds the condition that 381's bare 'உடையான்' leaves out.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:382","b":"k:863","type":"agrees","note":"382 lists fearlessness, giving, understanding and drive (அஞ்சாமை ஈகை அறிவு ஊக்கம்) as a king's nature. 863 describes the man who is easy prey for an enemy: he fears, does not know, does not fit with others, does not give (அஞ்சும் அறியான் அமைவிலன் ஈகலான்). Three of the four kingly qualities reappear negated, so the weak enemy of chapter 87 is drawn as the reverse of the king of chapter 39.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:383","b":"k:605","type":"agrees","note":"383 gives three things that stay with a ruler, beginning with தூங்காமை. 605 gives four that are the pleasure-boat of those bound for ruin: delay, forgetting, sloth and sleep (நெடுநீர் மறவி மடி துயில்). The two lists are near-opposites, and 605 covers both senses the commentators give தூங்காமை in 383 (not delaying, not being slothful).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:383","b":"k:668","type":"develops","note":"383 names promptness and resolve (தூங்காமை, துணிவுடைமை) as two separate standing qualities, with learning between them. 668 turns them into one instruction for a single task: once a deed has been seen clearly and decided (கலங்காது கண்ட வினை), do it without wavering and with delay driven off (தூக்கம் கடிந்து செயல்). The trait list of the king becomes a sequence: decide, then act at once.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:384","b":"k:766","type":"parallel_domain","note":"384 pairs மறன் and மானம் in the king. 766 opens its list of the four safeguards of an army with the same pair (மறம் மானம்), followed by keeping to the proven path and trustworthiness. The same two qualities are required at the top and in the ranks.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:384","b":"k:773","type":"develops","note":"384 asks for honour that does not fall short of valour. 773 says fierceness (தறுகண்) is called great manliness, but helping the foe when something befalls him (ஒன்று உற்றக்கால் ஊராண்மை) is its edge (எஃகு). Parimelazhagar explains 384's phrase with examples of not striking the unequal or the fleeing, which is the restraint 773 states directly.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:385","b":"k:377","type":"same_image","note":"385 ends the king's four skills with வகுத்தல், apportioning what has been guarded. 377 uses the same verb for what lies outside human skill: even those who have piled up crores can enjoy them only in the way the apportioner has apportioned (வகுத்தான் வகுத்த வகை), which Parimelazhagar takes as fate. The pairing sets the king's apportioning inside a larger one he does not control.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:385","b":"k:756","type":"develops","note":"385 says the king must create ways for wealth to come (இயற்றல்) without naming them. 756 names three that count as the king's wealth: what falls in unowned, customs dues, and what is taken from defeated enemies (உறுபொருள், உல்கு பொருள், ஒன்னார்த் தெறுபொருள்). Parimelazhagar's note on 385 gives a similar list of sources.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:386","b":"k:566","type":"agrees","note":"386 says that if a king is easy to see and not harsh of speech (கடுஞ்சொல்லன் அல்லனேல்), his land is spoken of above others. 566 uses the same word for the reverse case: if he is கடுஞ்சொல்லன் and without kindly regard, his long-standing wealth perishes on the spot. One trait is given both its reward and its penalty.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:387","b":"k:92","type":"develops","note":"92 ranks the two: being pleasant-spoken with a glad face is better than giving with a glad heart (ஈதலின் நன்றே). 387 does not rank them but joins them, giving 'with' pleasant words (இன்சொலால் ஈத்து), and adds protection. The king's version needs both, where the householder's verse lets speech stand above the gift.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:387","b":"k:525","type":"parallel_domain","note":"387 says that for one able to give with pleasant words and protect (இன்சொலால் ஈத்து அளிக்க), the world answers to his word. 525 says one who can give and speak pleasantly (கொடுத்தலும் இன்சொலும் ஆற்றின்) will be surrounded by layers of kin. The same two means work at two scales: they hold a family around a man and the world around a king.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:387","b":"k:648","type":"agrees","note":"387's result clause is that the world becomes as the king says and intends (தன் சொலால் தான் கண்டனைத்து); Manakkudavar reads this as the world lying under his command. 648 says the world quickly takes up the work it is told (விரைந்து தொழில் கேட்கும் ஞாலம்) when it finds those who can speak in order and sweetly. Both make pleasant speech the cause of the world's compliance; 387 adds giving and protection.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:388","b":"k:547","type":"develops","note":"388: the king who does முறை and protects is counted as இறை. 547 uses the same two words in a loop: the இறை guards the whole earth, and முறை guards him if he carries it out without fail (அவனை முறை காக்கும்). Justice is what earns the title in 388 and what keeps its holder safe in 547.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:388","b":"k:702","type":"parallel_domain","note":"388 has the just king set apart as இறை; 702 says one who knows without doubt what is in another's mind should be regarded as equal to a god (தெய்வத்தோடு ஒப்பக் கொளல்). Parimelazhagar glosses both with the same phrase, 'though a man by birth'. The book grants this standing for a function performed, justice in one case and insight in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:389","b":"k:160","type":"parallel_domain","note":"389 praises a king who endures (பொறுக்கும்) bitter words. 160 says those who fast in penance are great, but rank after those who endure the harsh words others speak (இன்னாச்சொல் நோற்பார்). Bearing hard speech is rated as the higher discipline for the ascetic and as the quality that holds a kingdom for the king.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:389","b":"k:447","type":"develops","note":"389 says the world rests under the parasol of a king who can bear words bitter to the ear (செவிகைப்பச் சொற்பொறுக்கும்). 447 asks who can ruin a ruler who keeps as his support people who rebuke him (இடிக்குந் துணையாரை ஆள்வாரை). Parimelazhagar reads 389's bitter words as those of exactly such rebuking companions, so 447 gives the institution and 389 the temperament it needs. Manakkudavar reads 389 differently, as hearing of others' offences and bearing it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:389","b":"k:638","type":"develops","note":"389 states the king's side: he should bear speech that is bitter to hear. 638 states the minister's side: even if the king destroys good counsel and knows nothing himself, the one at his side is bound to say what is sound (உறுதி உழையிருந்தான் கூறல் கடன்). The duty to speak does not wait on the king having 389's quality.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:390","b":"k:556","type":"same_image","note":"390 calls the one who has giving, kindness, a straight sceptre and care of the people 'வேந்தர்க்கு ஒளி', a light to kings. 556 uses the same word: kings endure through just rule, and without it 'மன்னர்க்கு ஒளி' does not endure. 390 makes செங்கோல் one of four sources of that light; 556 makes it the one without which the light goes out.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:390","b":"k:557","type":"develops","note":"390 lists அளி, kindly care, among the four things a king must have. 557 says what its absence is like: as lack of rain is to the earth, so is the king's lack of அளி to the living (துளியின்மை ... வேந்தன் அளியின்மை). An item in a list becomes, in the chapter on the crooked sceptre, something people cannot live without.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:541","b":"k:561","type":"develops","note":"541 gives the steps of justice: examine, favour no one, deliberate, act (ஓர்ந்து ... தேர்ந்து செய்வஃதே முறை). 561 defines the king by the same sequence and adds two things: the punishment must match the offence (ஒத்தாங்கு ஒறுப்பது) and aim at the wrong not recurring (தலைச்செல்லா வண்ணத்தால்). Chapter 57 thus supplies the measure and purpose that 541 leaves unstated.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:541","b":"k:577","type":"tension","note":"541 makes the refusal of கண்ணோட்டம் part of the definition of முறை. 577 says those without கண்ணோட்டம் have no eyes, and those who have eyes are never without it. If 577 is read without exception, the judge of 541 is 'eyeless' in the moment of judging; the conflict disappears only if 577 is limited to dealings outside judgment, as 578 suggests.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:542","b":"k:557","type":"same_image","note":"542: the whole world lives looking to the sky, and the people live looking to the king's sceptre (வானோக்கி ... கோல் நோக்கி). 557 states the same comparison from the side of failure: what lack of rain is to the earth, the king's lack of kindly care is to living beings. The pair shows the rain comparison is about dependence, so that a failed king is a drought.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:542","b":"k:1192","type":"parallel_domain","note":"542 compares subjects' dependence on the sceptre to the world's dependence on rain. In 1192 the woman says the tenderness the loved one gives to the one who loves is like the sky yielding to those who live by it (வாழ்வார்க்கு வானம் பயந்தற்றால்). The same figure of living by the rain is used for a people before their king and a lover before the beloved, and the word அளி serves for both the king's care (557, 390) and the lover's.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:543","b":"k:19","type":"parallel_domain","note":"543 makes the sceptre the precondition of scripture and virtue. 19 says that giving and austerity will not last in the wide world if the sky does not give (தானம் தவம் இரண்டும் தங்கா ... வானம் வழங்காதெனின்). Rain in Book 1 and just rule in Book 2 hold the same position: neither is itself virtue, but virtue does not continue without it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:544","b":"k:387","type":"agrees","note":"544 says the world holds to the king who embraces his people. Parimelazhagar explains the embrace as speaking pleasantly and giving what is needed in hardship, which is what 387 names: to one able to give with pleasant words and protect (இன்சொலால் ஈத்தளிக்க), this world answers. 387 says how the embrace is done; 544 adds that it goes together with the sceptre.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:544","b":"k:1025","type":"parallel_domain","note":"544: the world stands embracing the feet (அடிதழீஇ நிற்கும்) of the king who embraces his people (குடிதழீஇ) while wielding the sceptre. 1025: the world gathers round as kin (சுற்றமாச் சுற்றும் உலகு) the man who raises his family and lives without fault. In both the world's attachment is the return for care of one's own குடி, the word meaning the king's subjects in 544 and a man's family in 1025.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:545","b":"k:55","type":"parallel_domain","note":"545 ties rain to the king's right rule. 55 ties it to a wife's fidelity: she who rises worshipping her husband says 'rain' and it rains (பெய்யெனப் பெய்யும் மழை). In the household and in the kingdom the book makes rain answer to a person keeping to their proper role.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:545","b":"k:239","type":"parallel_domain","note":"545 says harvest (விளையுள்) comes with just rule. 239 says land that bears a body without renown declines in its blameless rich yield (வண்பயன் குன்றும்). Both let the moral state of people show in the fertility of the land; 239 does it for any person, 545 for the king.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:546","b":"k:118","type":"same_image","note":"546's condition is that the கோல் does not bend (கோடாது). 118 praises not leaning to one side (ஒருபால் கோடாமை), like the கோல், here the balance beam, that first levels itself and then weighs. The same word and the same verb serve for the king's sceptre and the scales, so the straightness asked of the ruler is the impartiality asked of the worthy in Book 1.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:547","b":"k:57","type":"parallel_domain","note":"547: the king guards the earth, and justice guards the king (இறை காக்கும் ... முறை காக்கும்). 57: what can the guard of confinement do? The guard that women keep by their own steadfastness is the chief guard (சிறை காக்கும் காப்பு ... நிறை காக்கும் காப்பே தலை). Both verses, in the same rhyming pattern, say that real protection comes from the guarded person's own conduct and not from outside force.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:547","b":"k:448","type":"develops","note":"547 says justice protects the king if he does it without fail. 448 calls the king who has no one to rebuke him 'ஏமரா மன்னன்', a king without a guard, and says he perishes even with no one to ruin him. Together they name what guards a ruler: his own just practice and the people who correct him.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:548","b":"k:386","type":"agrees","note":"548: a king who is not easy of access (எண்பதத்தான் in the negative, as Parimelazhagar construes it) and does not examine and do justice sinks and perishes. 386: a king easy to see (காட்சிக்கு எளியன்) and not harsh in speech has his land praised. Accessibility is stated as a virtue in chapter 39 and its lack as a cause of ruin in chapter 55.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:548","b":"k:448","type":"agrees","note":"548 says the unjust, unapproachable king perishes of himself (தானே கெடும்); Parimelazhagar adds 'even without enemies'. 448 says the king with no one to rebuke him perishes though no one sets out to ruin him (கெடுப்பார் இலானும் கெடும்). Both describe a ruler's fall with no outside agent, caused by shutting out complaint in one case and criticism in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:548","b":"k:553","type":"agrees","note":"548 and 553 share most of a line: a king who does not examine and do justice (ஓரா / நாடி முறைசெய்யா மன்னவன்). In 548 the king himself perishes; in 553 he loses his land day by day (நாடொறும் நாடு கெடும்). The pairing shows the same neglect ruining the ruler and the country.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:549","b":"k:314","type":"qualifies","note":"314 tells a person how to punish those who hurt him: shame them by doing them good and let it go (அவர் நாண நன்னயம் செய்து விடல்). 549 says that for a king, guarding the people and removing offences (குற்றம் கடிதல்) is no stain but his work (வடுவன்று வேந்தன் தொழில்). The words 'no stain' answer the doubt that punishing goes against virtue, which Parimelazhagar spells out; 549 limits 314's rule to one's own injuries and exempts the office.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:549","b":"k:436","type":"qualifies","note":"549 makes removing offences the king's work. 436 sets an order for it: if he first removes his own fault and then looks at others' (தன்குற்றம் நீக்கிப் பிறர்குற்றம் காண்கிற்பின்), what fault can attach to a ruler? The right to punish in 549 is, by 436, clear of blame only for a king who has corrected himself first.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:550","b":"k:1038","type":"same_image","note":"550 compares the king's execution of the cruel to weeding the green crop (பைங்கூழ் களைகட்டதனொடு நேர்). 1038, on farming itself, says that after weeding (கட்டபின்) guarding the crop is better than watering it. In the farmer's own chapter weeding is one step and guarding counts for more, which fits 549's order of guarding the people first and punishing second.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:631","b":"k:483","type":"develops","note":"483 asks whether anything called அருவினை exists for one who acts with கருவி and knowing காலம். 631 uses the same three words but keeps அருவினை as a separate item the minister must be good at. The pair shows where the minister's list comes from: tools and timing are what make a hard task doable.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:631","b":"k:675","type":"develops","note":"631 defines the minister as one excellent in கருவி, காலம், செய்கை and the அருவினை itself. 675 turns nearly the same list into a rule for anyone acting: பொருள், கருவி, காலம், வினை, இடன், 'ஐந்தும் இருள்தீர எண்ணிச் செயல்'. Read together, the minister's defining competence is the checklist of 675, with resources and place added there.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:632","b":"k:382","type":"parallel_domain","note":"632 lists the minister's qualities: வன்கண், குடிகாத்தல், கற்றறிதல், ஆள்வினை. 382 lists the king's: அஞ்சாமை, ஈகை, அறிவு, ஊக்கம் (383 adds கல்வி and துணிவுடைமை). Firmness, knowledge and drive appear on both lists, so the minister is described on the king's pattern; Manakkudavar even glosses வன்கண் as அஞ்சாமை. Giving is on the king's list only.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:633","b":"k:187","type":"parallel_domain","note":"187 says those who cannot make friends with pleasant talk 'கேளிர்ப் பிரிப்பர்', split even their own kin, by divisive speech. 633 uses the same verb, பிரித்தல், for a minister's skill, which Parimelazhagar restricts to detaching an enemy's allies. The same act is a fault at home and a craft in statecraft, and what differs is whose bond is being broken.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:633","b":"k:530","type":"develops","note":"633 names rejoining those who parted as a minister's skill. 530 says how the king should handle such a return: one who left (உழைப்பிரிந்து) and came back for a reason is to be taken in after thought, 'எண்ணிக் கொளல்'. The pair shows that பொருத்தல் is not unconditional welcome; 530 supplies the caution.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:633","b":"k:679","type":"develops","note":"633 says a minister must be able to divide (பிரித்தல்), keep his own side (பேணிக் கொளல்) and rejoin those who broke away (பிரிந்தார்ப் பொருத்தல்). 679 ranks one such act: 'ஒட்டாரை ஒட்டிக் கொளல்', bringing the unattached over, is more urgent than doing good to friends. 679 gives a priority among the alliance skills that 633 only lists.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:634","b":"k:462","type":"develops","note":"462 tells the one acting that nothing is out of reach if he examines with a chosen circle and thinks it over himself: 'தெரிந்த இனத்தொடு தேர்ந்தெண்ணிச் செய்வார்க்கு'. 634 describes the minister with the same two verbs, தெரிதல் and தேர்ந்து செயல். The pairing shows the two sides of one arrangement: 462 is the ruler's need for such company, 634 is what that company must be able to do.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:634","b":"k:671","type":"develops","note":"634 asks of a minister தெரிதல், தேர்ந்து செயல் and 'ஒருதலையாச் சொல்லல்', speaking so that the matter is settled. 671 says 'சூழ்ச்சி முடிவு துணிவெய்தல்': deliberation ends in reaching a resolve, and letting that resolve linger is a fault. Parimelazhagar glosses ஒருதலையாச் சொல்லல் as speaking so that துணிவு arises, which makes the minister's decisive word the point where 671's deliberation closes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:635","b":"k:441","type":"develops","note":"441 tells the king to choose the friendship of those who are 'அறனறிந்து மூத்த அறிவுடையார்', 'திறனறிந்து தேர்ந்து'. 635 describes the counsel-companion in the same words: 'அறனறிந்து ... திறனறிந்தான் தேர்ச்சித் துணை'. 441 is the instruction to seek such a person and 635 is the portrait of him, with settled speech (ஆன்றமைந்த சொல்) added.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:635","b":"k:644","type":"develops","note":"635 asks for one who knows அறன், has full and settled speech, and knows திறன். 644 joins the same three terms in one rule: 'திறனறிந்து சொல்லுக சொல்லை', since no அறன் or பொருள் is beyond that. The next chapter thus takes the speech item of 635 and makes fitted speech itself the place where knowing virtue and knowing method show.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:636","b":"k:503","type":"qualifies","note":"636 implies that nothing withstands the person who has native subtlety and learning. 503 says that even in those who have studied hard texts and are free of fault (அரியகற்று ஆசற்றார்), close examination rarely finds no blankness: 'இன்மை அரிதே வெளிறு'. The praise in 636 concerns outwitting opposing schemes; 503 limits it, since the learned still have gaps.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:637","b":"k:426","type":"agrees","note":"426 defines அறிவு as living with the world in the way the world lives: 'உலகத்தோடு அவ்வது உறைவது'. 637 gives the working form of this for one who already knows the method: 'உலகத்து இயற்கை அறிந்து செயல்'. Together they treat attention to how the world actually goes as part of knowledge and not a concession against it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:638","b":"k:448","type":"develops","note":"638 makes it the duty (கடன்) of the one at the king's side to say what is sound (உறுதி) even if the king rejects knowledge and knows nothing himself. 448 gives the stake: a king without those who rebuke, 'இடிப்பாரை இல்லாத ஏமரா மன்னன்', perishes even with no one to ruin him. 448 shows why 638 does not release the minister when the king will not listen.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:638","b":"k:696","type":"qualifies","note":"638 obliges the minister to speak sound counsel regardless of the king's reception. 696 governs the manner: knowing the king's mood and considering the time, say what is needed without what is disliked, so that it is wanted ('வெறுப்பில வேண்டுப வேட்பச் சொலல்'). The duty of 638 stands, and 696 conditions how and when it is discharged.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:638","b":"k:849","type":"tension","note":"849 says 'காணாதான் காட்டுவான் தான்காணான்': one who tries to show something to a person who does not see ends up not seeing himself, while the other keeps to what he saw. 638 requires exactly this of a minister when the king 'அறிகொன்று அறியான்'. The conflict holds if 849 is read as general advice not to instruct the conceited; Parimelazhagar reads it as a report that the instructor is reviled, which would leave 638 as a duty carried at that cost.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:639","b":"k:817","type":"same_image","note":"639 and 817 use the same construction and the same kind of figure: 'மந்திரியின் ... எழுபது கோடி உறும்' and 'நட்பின் பகைவரால் பத்தடுத்த கோடி உறும்'. In both an inflated count of open enemies is set against one false intimate, a plotting minister in 639 and a friend good only for laughter in 817. Parimelazhagar reads உறும் in 639 as 'that many enemies will come', and notes others read it as 'that many times better', which is how 817 is read.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:639","b":"k:882","type":"agrees","note":"639 says seventy crore enemies are better than a minister at one's side who plots harm ('பழுதெண்ணும் மந்திரி'). 882 gives the general rule: do not fear enemies like swords, fear the bond of enemies who are like kin ('கேள்போல் பகைவர் தொடர்பு'). Parimelazhagar explains 639 on the same ground, that open enemies can be guarded against and the one inside cannot.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:640","b":"k:664","type":"agrees","note":"640 says those without திறப்பாடு leave things unfinished even after planning them in proper order ('முறைப்படச் சூழ்ந்தும் முடிவிலவே செய்வர்'). 664 says saying is easy for anyone and doing as said is hard ('சொல்லிய வண்ணம் செயல்'). Both locate failure in the gap between a correct plan and its execution; Parimelazhagar glosses the 'saying' of 664 as stating in order how the task will be done.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:640","b":"k:728","type":"parallel_domain","note":"640 says sound planning comes to nothing in those who lack the capacity to carry it through. 728 says those who have learned many things are of no use ('பயமிலரே') if they cannot speak well and tellingly in a good assembly. The same pattern appears in two domains: knowledge or planning is counted worthless without the ability that delivers it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:971","b":"k:600","type":"same_image","note":"971 says 'ஒளி ஒருவற்கு உள்ள வெறுக்கை' and 600 says 'உரம் ஒருவற்கு உள்ள வெறுக்கை': the same phrase, abundance of spirit, is called a person's light in one and a person's strength in the other. Each then states the lack: living without it is disgrace (இளி) in 971, and those without it are trees in 600. The repetition shows the chapter on greatness resting on the earlier chapter on ஊக்கம்.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:972","b":"k:133","type":"agrees","note":"133 says having conduct is what good family is ('ஒழுக்கம் உடைமை குடிமை') and a lapse becomes low birth. 972 says birth is equal and distinction follows from deeds. Both move rank from descent to what a person does, though 133 keeps the vocabulary of birth and Parimelazhagar reads it within varna.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:973","b":"k:751","type":"qualifies","note":"751 says nothing but wealth makes people of no account into people of account ('பொருளல்லவரைப் பொருளாகச் செய்யும்'). 973 says the not-high do not become high by being placed high, which Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar both read as a seat given by wealth. On that reading 973 limits 751: wealth gets a person regarded, and it does not make them great.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:974","b":"k:57","type":"develops","note":"57 says confinement does little and the guard a woman keeps by her own steadfastness is the chief guard ('நிறைகாக்கும் காப்பே தலை'). 974 takes this as its comparison: like single-minded women, greatness exists only 'தன்னைத்தான் கொண்டொழுகின்', if one keeps hold of oneself. 974 carries 57's claim about self-guarding into the Porul chapter on greatness, so that greatness, like கற்பு, cannot be kept for someone from outside.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:974","b":"k:965","type":"develops","note":"974 says greatness exists only as long as one keeps oneself. 965 shows the loss: even those like a hill sink if they do a lowering thing the size of a குன்றி seed. Together they present greatness as a condition that continues only through conduct and can be forfeited by a small act.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:975","b":"k:468","type":"develops","note":"975 says the great carry out hard deeds 'ஆற்றின்', in the right way. 468 states the other side with the same word: effort not made in the right way ('ஆற்றின் வருந்தா வருத்தம்') collapses even if many stand guard over it. The pair shows that ஆற்றின் in 975 carries weight: hard deeds done by the wrong route fail.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:976","b":"k:451","type":"agrees","note":"451 says greatness fears small company while smallness takes it as kin ('சுற்றமாச் சூழ்ந்து விடும்'). 976 says the small have no thought of attaching themselves to the great. Both describe the same sorting from two ends: the small seek their own kind and do not look upward.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:977","b":"k:838","type":"parallel_domain","note":"977 says even distinction (சிறப்பு), if it falls to the unworthy, results in acts of excess ('இறப்பே புரிந்த தொழிற்று'). 838 says a fool getting something in hand is like a man already deranged getting drunk. Both say a good thing makes its holder worse when the holder is unfit; 838 says it of possessions and a fool, 977 of distinction and the small. Manakkudavar reads the சிறப்பு of 977 as wealth, which brings the two closer.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:977","b":"k:1000","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1000 says great wealth obtained by one without பண்பு is like good milk turned by the fault of its vessel. 977 says distinction landing on the unworthy produces excess. The logic is the same, that the recipient's quality decides what a gift becomes, but 1000 has the wealth spoiled and useless while 977 has the distinction turn into overbearing action.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:978","b":"k:125","type":"agrees","note":"125 says bowing is good for everyone and for the wealthy it is a further wealth. 978 says greatness bows and smallness decks itself out in self-admiration. Both attach humility to those who have most reason for pride; Parimelazhagar glosses the பணிதல் of 125 as being without பெருமிதம், the word 979 uses.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:978","b":"k:439","type":"develops","note":"439 commands 'வியவற்க எஞ்ஞான்றும் தன்னை', never admire yourself, among faults a ruler must avoid. 978 uses the same verb to define smallness: 'அணியுமாம் தன்னை வியந்து'. What 439 forbids as a fault, 978 treats as the mark by which the small are known.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:978","b":"k:963","type":"qualifies","note":"978 says greatness bows always ('பணியுமாம் என்றும் பெருமை'). 963 says bowing is needed in abundance and standing tall is needed in reduced circumstances ('சுருக்கத்து வேண்டும் உயர்வு'). Taken plainly, 963 limits the என்றும் of 978; Parimelazhagar reads என்றும் in 978 as 'even when distinction has come', which makes the two verses say the same thing about prosperity.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:979","b":"k:431","type":"same_image","note":"979 defines greatness as the absence of பெருமிதம் and smallness as riding it to the end. 431 uses the same word with approval: the prosperity of those free of செருக்கு, anger and சிறுமை is 'பெருமித நீர்த்து', which Parimelazhagar glosses as having the quality of eminence. The word means conceit in 979 and dignity in 431, and both verses agree that the person without pride is the one who has real stature.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:979","b":"k:844","type":"parallel_domain","note":"844 defines வெண்மை, poor understanding, as the pride that says 'we have brightness' ('ஒண்மை உடையம் யாம் என்னும் செருக்கு'). 979 defines smallness as being carried away by பெருமிதம். In both a defect is identified with the claim to its opposite: the foolish are those sure of their intelligence, and the small are those sure of their greatness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:980","b":"k:846","type":"same_image","note":"846 and 980 share the words அற்றம், மறை and குற்றம். In 846 covering one's own nakedness is itself poor understanding while one's faults stay uncovered, which Parimelazhagar takes as faults not removed. In 980 greatness covers the அற்றம் of others. The image of covering what is exposed is used for self-deception in one verse and for consideration toward others in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:980","b":"k:1079","type":"agrees","note":"1079 says the base, on merely seeing others clothed and fed, are able to find a blemish in them ('வடுக்காண வற்றாகும் கீழ்'). 980 says smallness tells only faults ('குற்றமே கூறி விடும்'). 1079 adds a cause the Tamil of 980 leaves out: Parimelazhagar reads the fault-finding of the base as coming from being unable to bear others' prosperity.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1091","b":"k:15","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1091 says the same eyes hold the look that makes him ill and the look that cures it. 15 says rain is both what ruins ('கெடுப்பதூஉம்') and what, as support to the ruined, lifts them again ('எடுப்பதூஉம்'). In both, one source is credited with the harm and with its remedy, once in love and once in the world's subsistence.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1092","b":"k:1090","type":"develops","note":"1090 says brewed liquor gladdens only those who drink it and does not, like love, gladden those who merely see ('கண்டார் மகிழ்செய்தல்'). 1092 puts a measure on that: the small look her eyes steal ('கண்களவு கொள்ளும் சிறுநோக்கம்') is not an even half of love but more ('செம்பாகம் அன்று பெரிது'). Together they make sight alone a large share of love's pleasure; Parimelazhagar takes the 'half' in 1092 to be half of physical union.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1092","b":"k:1326","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1092 ranks a brief stolen look as more than half of love. 1326 ranks sulking above union ('புணர்தலின் ஊடல் இனிது'), as digesting is sweeter than eating. Both verses place the greater part of love's pleasure in something beside the embrace, one before it and one around it.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"k:1093","b":"k:718","type":"same_image","note":"In 1093 her look followed by a lowered head is 'water she poured' ('அட்டிய நீர்') into the 'யாப்பு', which Parimelazhagar reads as the crop of love binding the two. 718 says speaking before those able to understand is like pouring water into the bed of something already growing ('வளர்வதன் பாத்தியுள் நீர்சொரிந்தற்று'). Both use watering for a sign or word given to someone already receptive: the gesture or speech does not start the growth, it feeds it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1096","b":"k:826","type":"same_image","note":"1096: though they speak like those unattached ('உறாஅதவர்போல் சொலினும்'), the words of those without anger are quickly understood ('செறாஅர்சொல் ஒல்லை உணரப்படும்'). 826 has the same frame reversed: though they say good things like friends ('நட்டார்போல் நல்லவை சொல்லினும்'), the words of the unreconciled are quickly understood ('ஒட்டார்சொல் ஒல்லை உணரப்படும்'). The shared wording shows one claim applied in love and in statecraft: a spoken mask, harsh or kind, does not long hide the disposition behind it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1097","b":"k:824","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1097 lists small harsh words spoken without anger and looks like an enemy's ('செற்றார்போல் நோக்கும்') as the signs of those who are close while seeming distant. 824 warns of the opposite pairing: a sweet smile on the face with bitterness inside ('முகத்தின் இனிய நகாஅ அகத்தின்னா'). In both the surface is the reverse of the heart; the lover reads the mismatch with relief, the Book of Wealth reads it with fear.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1098","b":"k:1274","type":"agrees","note":"In 1098, when he looks she softens and smiles gently ('பசையினள் பைய நகும்'), and he says there is a certain grace in her then ('உண்டு ஆண்டு ஓர் ஏஎர்'). 1274 says there is something inside the bud of her smile, like scent inside a closed bud ('நகைமொக்குள் உள்ளதொன்று உண்டு'). Both treat her slight smile as holding an unspoken meaning, and both leave that meaning as an unnamed 'something'.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1099","b":"k:528","type":"same_image","note":"1099 says looking at each other with the common look, like strangers ('ஏதிலார் போலப் பொதுநோக்கு நோக்குதல்'), is found in lovers. 528 uses the same word for a king: he should not look on all in common ('பொதுநோக்கான்') but by rank, and many will live looking to that. In 528 the undifferentiated look is a failure to distinguish; in 1099 it is put on deliberately by the two people who distinguish each other most.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1100","b":"k:705","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1100: when eyes agree with eyes in their look, spoken words are of no use at all ('வாய்ச்சொற்கள் என்ன பயனும் இல'). 705, in the Book of Wealth's chapter of the same name, asks what use eyes are among the limbs if they cannot read a sign by a sign ('என்ன பயத்தவோ கண்'). Both hold that the eye is the organ for knowing another's mind; 705 says so for court service and 1100 for lovers, where it makes speech redundant.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1100","b":"k:1280","type":"develops","note":"1100 says that once the eyes agree, words from the mouth serve no purpose. 1280 says that telling love's sickness with the eyes and pleading ('கண்ணினால் காமநோய் சொல்லி இரவு') adds womanhood to womanhood. 1280 moves from the fact that eyes can replace speech to praise of the woman who chooses to speak that way.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1151","b":"k:1160","type":"develops","note":"1151 tells him to announce his quick return to those who will be alive then ('வாழ்வார்க்கு உரை'), implying she will not be. 1160 says many consent to the hard thing, endure the parting and live on afterwards ('பின்இருந்து வாழ்வார் பலர்'); Parimelazhagar reads this as her ironic reply to the friend, meaning she is not one of them. The chapter opens and closes on the same word, 'வாழ்வார்', for the survivors she sets herself apart from.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1151","b":"k:1263","type":"qualifies","note":"In 1151 the speaker (the friend for Parimelazhagar, the woman for Manakkudavar) says a promise of quick return is only for those who will survive. In 1263 the woman, after he has gone, says she is still alive through wanting his return ('வரல்நசைஇ இன்னும் உளேன்'). The later speech shows the claim of 1151 as said in the moment: the hope of return that was dismissed there is what keeps her alive here.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1152","b":"k:1295","type":"agrees","note":"1152: once even his look was pleasant, but now union itself holds the pain of fearing separation ('பிரிவஞ்சும் புன்கண் உடைத்தால் புணர்வு'). 1295: her heart fears not having him and, having him, fears parting ('பெறின் பிரிவு அஞ்சும்'), so its misery never ends. Both say that the prospect of parting takes away the pleasure of being together; 1295 states it as the heart's standing condition.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1153","b":"k:514","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1153: trust is hard ('அரிதரோ தேற்றம்'), because parting occurs at some point even with those who understand ('அறிவுடையார் கண்ணும்'). 514: even when people have been trusted after every kind of test ('எனைவகையான் தேறியக் கண்ணும்'), many turn different through the nature of the work. Both say that knowing a person's qualities does not secure their future conduct, one about a lover and one about a king's appointee.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1154","b":"k:1209","type":"develops","note":"1154 holds up his words 'fear not' and says the fault, if he leaves, is not the believer's. 1209 holds up another of his sayings, 'we are not separate' ('வேறல்லம் என்பார்'), and says her life is ending from thinking on his lack of grace. 1154 is the argument made before the parting; 1209 is the same contrast of word and conduct felt after it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1155","b":"k:1151","type":"develops","note":"1151 asks him to say he is not going, and leaves talk of return to those who will survive. 1155 turns to the friend: if you would protect, prevent his parting ('பிரிவோம்பல்'), since once he goes union is hard ('நீங்கின் அரிதால் புணர்வு'). Both reject return as a remedy and allow only not-leaving; Parimelazhagar supplies that what is to be protected in 1155 is her life.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1156","b":"k:1222","type":"same_image","note":"1156 names him 'வன்கண்ணர்' for announcing his departure. In 1222 she asks the evening, which looks as wretched as she is, whether its partner too is hard like hers ('எம்கேள்போல் வன்கண்ணதோ'). The epithet given conditionally in 1156 has become her settled description of him once he is gone.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1157","b":"k:1235","type":"same_image","note":"In 1157 the slipping bangles broadcast ('தூற்றா') his leaving. In 1235 the shoulders that have lost bangles and their old beauty tell of the cruel one's cruelty ('கொடியார் கொடுமை உரைக்கும்'). Both make her thinning body a speaker that reports on him without her consent; 1235 adds a judgment of him to the bare news of 1157.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1158","b":"k:839","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1158: living in a town without one's own people is painful, and parting from one who is dear is more painful still ('அதனினும் இன்னாது இனியார்ப் பிரிவு'). 839 says, with irony, that the friendship of fools is very sweet because parting brings no pain at all ('பிரிவின்கண் பீழை தருவதொன்று இல்'). Read together, both verses use the pain of parting as the measure of what a bond was worth: none for a fool's friendship, more than exile among strangers for a lover.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1160","b":"k:1198","type":"agrees","note":"1160 says many agree to the hard thing, put away their pain, endure the parting and live on ('பின்இருந்து வாழ்வார் பலர்'); Parimelazhagar reads it as irony against the friend's advice. 1198 says outright that none in the world are harder than those who live on ('வாழ்வாரின் வன்கணார் இல்') without a kind word from the beloved. Both treat surviving separation as something that needs explaining, not as the expected course.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:11","b":"k:1106","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 11 gives rain the name அமிழ்தம் because the world's life continues through it. In 1106 the lover says her shoulders must be made of amrita (அமிழ்தின் இயன்றன தோள்) because his life puts out new shoots at each touch (உயிர் தளிர்ப்ப). Both verses award the word for the same reason, that the thing keeps life going, once for the whole world and once in a lover's speech about himself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:13","b":"k:734","type":"develops","note":"Verse 13 says that if the sky proves false (விண் பொய்ப்பின்), hunger stays and torments the world (உள்நின்று உடற்றும் பசி), sea-girt though it is. Verse 734 defines a land as one that severe hunger (உறுபசி), unending disease and destructive enemies do not reach. Read together, the first mark of a proper country in the Book of Wealth rests on something no ruler supplies, the rain of chapter 2.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:14","b":"k:1036","type":"develops","note":"Verse 14 says ploughing stops when the rain's bounty declines. Verse 1036 says that if the ploughmen's hands fold (உழவினார் கைம்மடங்கின்), even those who say they have given up desire cannot hold their state. Verse 14 names one cause that would fold those hands, so the failure of rain reaches as far as the renouncer.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:15","b":"k:1102","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 15 says it is rain that ruins (கெடுப்பதூஉம்) and rain again that stands by the ruined and lifts them (எடுப்பதூஉம்); Parimelazhagar explains that it ruins by not falling. In 1102 the lover says that for other diseases the medicine is something else, but she is herself the medicine for the sickness she causes (தன்நோய்க்குத் தானே மருந்து). The pattern of one thing being both the harm and the only remedy appears for rain and the world and, in a lover's speech, for the beloved.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:16","b":"k:557","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 16 says that unless drops fall from the sky (விசும்பின் துளிவீழின் அல்லால்) not even the tip of green grass can be seen. Verse 557 says that what lack of drops (துளியின்மை) is to the earth, the king's lack of tender regard (அளியின்மை) is to those who live under him. Verse 16 supplies the picture that 557's comparison assumes: without the king's அளி, the people are the bare ground.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:17","b":"k:211","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 211 asks what the world does for the rain-cloud in return (மாரிமாட்டு என் ஆற்றுங் கொல்லோ உலகு) and makes it the model of duty that wants no repayment. Verse 17 says even the long sea loses its nature if the cloud, having taken from it (தடிந்து, as Parimelazhagar reads it), does not give back. On that reading the cloud is not a pure giver: it returns what it drew from the sea, and the sea suffers if it withholds. Manakkudavar takes தடிந்து as lightning flashing, and on his reading the qualification falls away.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:17","b":"k:1010","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 17 pictures the cloud not giving (எழிலி தான் நல்காதாகி விடின்) and the sea itself shrinking as a result. Verse 1010 says the brief want of the renowned rich has the nature of the rain-cloud gone dry (மாரி வறங்கூர்ந்தனையது). Both use the withholding cloud; 1010 turns it into a figure for a giver's temporary incapacity, with the suggestion that it is as general a loss and as passing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:18","b":"k:560","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 18 says that if the sky dries up (வானம் வறக்குமேல்), festival and worship for the gods will not go on here (சிறப்பொடு பூசனை செல்லாது). Verse 560 says that if the guardian does not guard (காவலன் காவான் எனின்), the cows' yield shrinks and those of the six duties forget their book (நூல் மறப்பர்). Both say religious practice has a material precondition that can fail; chapter 2 names the rain and chapter 56 names the king.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:19","b":"k:559","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 19 says giving and austerity (தானம் தவம் இரண்டும்) will not stay in the world if the sky does not give. Verse 559 says that if the king acts with justice bent (முறைகோடி), the sky will not pour in season (ஒல்லாது வானம் பெயல்). Chapter 2 makes virtue depend on rain; 559 makes the rain depend in turn on a human virtue, the ruler's justice, so the dependence runs both ways.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:19","b":"k:1036","type":"develops","note":"Verse 19 says austerity (தவம்) cannot last without rain. Verse 1036 gives the step in between: if the ploughmen's hands fold, those who say they have left desire have no standing (விட்டேம் என்பார்க்கும் நிலை இல்லை). The renouncer's practice rests on the farmer's food, and the farmer's on the sky.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:20","b":"k:55","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 20 ends the chapter by saying nothing in the world, for anyone, holds without the sky. Verse 55 says that for the wife who rises worshipping her husband, it rains when she says 'rain' (பெய்யெனப் பெய்யும் மழை). Three chapters after rain is made the condition of everything, the rain is said to answer to one person's steadfastness.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:20","b":"k:542","type":"agrees","note":"Verse 20 says no one's world can do without water (நீர்இன்று அமையாது உலகு), and that ஒழுக்கு cannot exist without the sky. Parimelazhagar takes ஒழுக்கு as the water's unbroken flow; Pope's verse rendering and Kalaignar take it as ordered conduct. Verse 542 states the first half as a given (வானோக்கி வாழும் உலகெல்லாம்) and sets the people's dependence on the sceptre beside it. On either reading of ஒழுக்கு, 542 treats the dependence 20 argues for as the standard case of something one cannot live without.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:21","b":"k:48","type":"tension","note":"Verse 21 says the settled view of the texts (பனுவல் துணிவு) sets the greatness of those who have renounced within their discipline (ஒழுக்கத்து நீத்தார் பெருமை) highest among excellent things. Verse 48 says the household life that keeps others on their path and does not slip from virtue has more endurance than those who do penance (நோற்பாரின் நோன்மை உடைத்து). If 48 is read as ranking the householder above the ascetic, as its plain wording allows, the two chapters give the first place to different lives; if 48 is read as praising only the householder's endurance, they do not collide.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:21","b":"k:533","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 21 rests its claim on பனுவல் துணிவு, the settled conclusion of the texts. Verse 533 uses the same appeal: that the heedless have no fame is the conclusion of authors of every school (எப்பால் நூலோர்க்கும் துணிவு). These are two places where the Kural, which usually argues from observation, cites the agreement of the books as its ground.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:22","b":"k:310","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 22 says that to state the measure of the greatness of renouncers (துறந்தார்) is like counting the dead of the world (இறந்தாரை எண்ணிக்கொண்டற்று). Verse 310 rhymes the same two words: those who exceed in anger (இறந்தார்) are like the dead, and those who have renounced anger are equal to renouncers (துறந்தார் துணை). In 22 the dead supply only a number too large to count; in 310 the same pairing sets renouncers and the dead as opposite ends, and extends the renouncer's standing to anyone who gives up anger.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:23","b":"k:49","type":"tension","note":"Verse 23 says the greatness of those who have discerned the two (இருமை வகைதெரிந்து) and taken on அறம் here stands highest in the world; Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar read the two as birth and release and the அறம் as renunciation. Verse 49 says that what is called அறம் is household life (அறன் எனப்பட்டதே இல்வாழ்க்கை). On the commentators' reading of 23 the two verses attach the word அறம், and the highest standing, to different lives. Kalaignar reads 23 as choosing good over bad in general, and on that reading there is no conflict.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:24","b":"k:361","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 24 calls the one who guards the five senses a seed (வித்து) for the ground called வரன், which Parimelazhagar takes as release. Verse 361 calls craving the seed (வித்து) that bears unceasing birth for all beings. The book uses the seed for both outcomes: restraint of the senses is sown toward release, and craving is what sprouts as rebirth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:26","b":"k:664","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 26 divides people by whether they can do what is hard to do. Verse 664 says saying is easy for anyone and doing as one has said is hard (அரியவாம் சொல்லிய வண்ணம் செயல்). The chapter on firmness in action applies the renouncer's standard to statecraft and names what is hard in a deed: carrying it out as announced.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:27","b":"k:354","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 27 says the world lies within the one who discerns the kinds of the five (ஐந்தின் வகைதெரிவான் கட்டே உலகு): taste, light, touch, sound and smell. Verse 354 says that even having gained the fivefold awareness (ஐயுணர்வு எய்தியக் கண்ணும்) is of no benefit to those without perception of the real (மெய்யுணர்வு). Verse 354 sets a limit on 27: command of the five is not the end, and without true perception it yields nothing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:27","b":"k:1101","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 27 lists the five sense objects (சுவை ஒளி ஊறு ஓசை நாற்றம்) and says the world belongs to the one who discerns them. In 1101 the lover lists the same five as acts, seeing, hearing, tasting, smelling and touching (கண்டுகேட்டு உண்டுயிர்த்து உற்றறியும் ஐம்புலனும்), and says they are all found in this one woman (ஒண்தொடி கண்ணே உள). The same inventory serves the renouncer as a field to be understood and the lover as a field of enjoyment; the second is a speech in the moment and not a counter-teaching.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:28","b":"k:55","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 28 says the greatness of people whose word is full (நிறைமொழி மாந்தர்) is shown on earth by their மறைமொழி; Parimelazhagar explains நிறைமொழி as speech that produces its result whether spoken in grace or in anger. Verse 55 says that when the wife who worships her husband says 'rain', it rains (பெய்யெனப் பெய்யும் மழை). Both give a person's word power over the world as the proof of their standing, for the renouncer through austerity and for the wife through steadfastness at home.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:29","b":"k:310","type":"tension","note":"Verse 29 speaks of the anger (வெகுளி) of those who stand on the hill of good qualities; on Parimelazhagar's reading they are sages whose anger, though it lasts a moment, cannot be warded off by its target. Verse 310 says those who have renounced anger are the equals of renouncers (சினத்தைத் துறந்தார் துறந்தார் துணை), which makes freedom from anger the renouncer's mark. On Parimelazhagar's reading the book's model renouncer still gets angry, and dangerously. Kalaignar reads 29 as saying their anger does not stay even a moment, and on that reading the two verses agree.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:41","b":"k:263","type":"develops","note":"41 makes the householder the support of the three, read by Parimelazhagar as the other orders, ascetics included. 263 asks, with கொல், whether 'the others' (மற்றையவர்கள்) have forgotten தவம் because they want to provide for the renounced (துறந்தார்க்குத் துப்புரவு வேண்டி); Parimelazhagar glosses 'the others' as householders. The Thavam chapter thus looks at the same arrangement from the ascetic side: the householder's support of renouncers is given as the reason he does no austerity himself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:41","b":"k:1036","type":"parallel_domain","note":"41 calls the householder the துணை who stands on the good path (நல்லாற்றின் நின்ற) for 'the three' (இயல்புடைய மூவர்), whom Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar take as the other three orders of life (Kalaignar instead reads parents, spouse and children). 1036 says that if the ploughmen's hands fold (கைம்மடங்கின்), even those who say 'we have let go of desire' (விட்டேம் என்பார்) have no standing (நிலை). On the older commentators' reading both verses make the renouncer's state depend on someone who stays in the world and produces: the householder in Book 1, the farmer in Book 2.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:42","b":"k:41","type":"develops","note":"41 names the householder the support of 'the three of proper nature' (இயல்புடைய மூவர்); 42 repeats the frame இல்வாழ்வான் என்பான் துணை and adds three more: துறந்தார், துவ்வாதவர், இறந்தார். Parimelazhagar takes these as the abandoned, the poor and those who died with no one of their own, so the pair moves from the recognised orders to people with no claim on anyone. Pope and Kalaignar read துறந்தார் as ascetics, which would make 42 overlap 41 rather than extend it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:42","b":"k:226","type":"develops","note":"42 lists துவ்வாதவர், those who have nothing to eat or enjoy, among the people the householder supports. 226 says that ending the destroying hunger of those without means (அற்றார் அழிபசி தீர்த்தல்) is the place where a person who has gained wealth stores it (பொருள் வைப்புழி). 226 turns the duty stated in 42 into a claim about what wealth is for.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:43","b":"k:81","type":"develops","note":"43 lists five quarters to be tended (ஐம்புலத்தாறு ஓம்பல்): ancestors, gods, guests (விருந்து), kin and oneself, and calls this the chief thing (தலை). 81 says that all of staying, guarding one's goods and living at home (இருந்தோம்பி இல்வாழ்வதெல்லாம்) is for the sake of tending guests and doing them good (விருந்தோம்பி வேளாண்மை செய்தற் பொருட்டு). One of the five items of 43 is taken up, with the same verb ஓம்பு, and made the purpose of the whole household.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:43","b":"k:524","type":"parallel_domain","note":"43 puts kin (ஒக்கல்) among the five the householder must tend. 524, in the chapters on kingship, says that living so as to be surrounded by one's kin (சுற்றத்தால் சுற்றப்பட ஒழுகல்) is the fruit of having gained wealth. The household duty of Book 1 reappears in Book 2 as the point of a ruler's prosperity.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:44","b":"k:112","type":"parallel_domain","note":"44 promises an unfailing line (வழி) to the life that fears blame (பழியஞ்சி) and shares its food; Parimelazhagar reads the fear of blame as applying to how the wealth is earned. 112 says the prosperity of the person of rectitude (செப்பம் உடையவன் ஆக்கம்) does not decay and is a security to those who come after (எச்சத்திற்கு ஏமாப்பு). In both, wealth held without wrong is what lasts into the next generation.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:44","b":"k:227","type":"agrees","note":"44 says a life that fears blame and has பாத்தூண், food shared out, never sees its line fail (வழியெஞ்சல் எஞ்ஞான்றும் இல்). 227 uses the same compound: the one habituated to shared food (பாத்தூண் மரீஇயவனை) is hardly touched by the disease called hunger. Both attach a protective result to the same practice, one for the descendants and one for the sharer himself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:44","b":"k:1107","type":"same_image","note":"44 names பாத்தூண், shared food, as the mark of the householder's life. In 1107 the lover says the embrace of the woman is like staying in one's own home and eating one's own after sharing it (தம்மில் இருந்து தமது பாத்து உண்டற்று). The Book of Love takes the householder's sharing meal as a standard of pleasure, where 44 gives it as a duty with a reward; the comparison is the speaker's, not a teaching.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:45","b":"k:75","type":"develops","note":"45 says that if household life has love and virtue (அன்பும் அறனும்), that is its character and its fruit (பண்பும் பயனும்). 75 says the distinction reached by those who have had joy in this world is, 'they say', the result of a way of life settled in love (அன்புற்று அமர்ந்த வழக்கு); Parimelazhagar takes this as household life lived with love, rewarded with heaven. 75 spells out the பயன் that 45 only names.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:46","b":"k:348","type":"tension","note":"46 asks what one who conducts household life in the way of virtue would gain by going to the outside way (புறத்தாற்றில் போஒய்ப் பெறுவது எவன்); Parimelazhagar and Pope take the outside way as renunciation. 348 says those who have renounced completely (தீரத் துறந்தார்) have reached the head, and the rest (மற்றையவர்) are confused and caught in the net. If புறத்தாறு is renunciation and the question in 46 is rhetorical, 46 denies the gain that 348 reserves for renouncers alone; if புறத்தாறு means only ways outside virtue, the conflict disappears.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:47","b":"k:266","type":"tension","note":"47 says the one who lives household life according to its nature is first among all who strive (முயல்வாருள் எல்லாம் தலை). 266 says those who do தவம் are doing their own work and the rest (மற்றல்லார்), caught in desire, do what is futile (அவஞ்செய்வார்). On Parimelazhagar's reading of 47, where the strivers are those working to leave the senses, the householder is ranked above ascetics, while 266 counts everyone but the ascetic as wasting effort. Manakkudavar reads முயல்வார் as those who strive for wealth, and on that reading the two do not collide.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:48","b":"k:225","type":"agrees","note":"48 says a household life that keeps others on their path (ஆற்றின் ஒழுக்கி) and does not slip from virtue has more endurance than those who do penance (நோற்பாரின் நோன்மை உடைத்து). 225 says the strength of the strong is enduring hunger, and that comes behind the strength of those who remove that hunger (மாற்றுவார் ஆற்றலின் பின்). Both rank the one who provides above the one who abstains, and both do it by granting the ascetic's own virtue, endurance, and awarding more of it to the provider.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:49","b":"k:44","type":"qualifies","note":"44 opens with பழியஞ்சி: the household life that fears blame and shares its food keeps its line. 49 calls household life virtue itself, then adds that it is good if there is nothing others blame (பிறன் பழிப்பது இல்லாயின்). On Manakkudavar's reading, where அஃதும் still refers to household life, 49 makes absence of blame the condition on the chapter's whole praise of the householder, which 44 had already built into its description.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"k:49","b":"k:280","type":"develops","note":"49 says what is called virtue is household life, and 'that too' (அஃதும்) is good if it is not something others blame (பிறன் பழிப்பது இல்லாயின்). Parimelazhagar takes அஃதும் as renunciation and the blame as false conduct in ascetics (Manakkudavar keeps it on household life). 280 says neither shaving nor growing the hair is needed if one drops what the world has blamed (உலகம் பழித்தது ஒழித்து விடின்). On Parimelazhagar's reading the chapter on false ascetics works out the condition 49 states in passing: the renouncer's standing rests on being free of blame.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:50","b":"k:413","type":"parallel_domain","note":"50 places the person who lives rightly on earth (வையத்துள்) among the gods of the sky. 413 says those who have listening as food for the ear are, though on earth (நிலத்து), equal to the gods who are fed by oblations. Both verses set 'on earth' against the gods and say a human excellence closes the gap, right living in one case and learning by listening in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:52","b":"k:750","type":"parallel_domain","note":"52 says that if excellence of the home (மனைமாட்சி) is not in the wife, the life is nothing, whatever excellence it has (எனைமாட்சித் தாயினும் இல்). 750 says that whatever excellence a fort has (எனைமாட்சித் தாகியக் கண்ணும்), it is as nothing in those who lack excellence in action (வினைமாட்சி இல்லார்கண் இல்லது). The two share the phrase and the argument: the outward assets of a home or a fortress count for nothing without the quality of the person inside who runs it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:53","b":"k:430","type":"parallel_domain","note":"53 asks what is lacking if the wife has worth (இல்லதென் இல்லவள் மாண்பானால்) and what is there if she does not (உள்ளதென் ... மாணாக் கடை). 430 says those with understanding have everything, and those without it have nothing whatever they have (என்னுடையரேனும் இலர்). The same two-sided formula, one possession that makes all others count or not count, is applied to a wife's character in the home and to understanding in a ruler.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:54","b":"k:988","type":"same_image","note":"54 asks what is greater than a woman, if she has the firmness called கற்பு (கற்பென்னும் திண்மை உண்டாகப் பெறின்). 988 says poverty is no disgrace to a person, if he has the firmness called சால்பு (சால்பென்னும் திண்மை உண்டாகப் பெறின்). The second line is the same but for one word, so the book describes a woman's fidelity and a worthy person's character with the same term, திண்மை, a firmness that outweighs outward circumstances.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:55","b":"k:2","type":"tension","note":"55 praises a wife who does not worship a god (தெய்வம் தொழாஅள்) and worships her husband, and credits her with power over the rain. 2 asks what use learning is if the learned do not worship the feet of the one of pure knowledge (நற்றாள் தொழாஅர் எனின்), with the same verb in the same negative form. Read literally, 55 commends for the wife the not-worshipping that the first chapter treats as making a person's attainments useless. Parimelazhagar removes the conflict by reading 'other gods' (பிற தெய்வம்) and making the husband her god; the tension depends on taking தெய்வம் தொழாஅள் at face value.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:56","b":"k:57","type":"develops","note":"56 defines the woman as one who guards herself (தற்காத்து), cares for her husband and guards the good name (சொற்காத்து) without flagging. 57 asks what guarding by confinement (சிறைகாக்கும் காப்பு) can do, and says the guard women keep by their own நிறை is the chief one. 57 explains why 56 makes her the subject of the verb 'guard': the guarding that counts cannot be done by anyone else.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:56","b":"k:59","type":"develops","note":"56 includes guarding the well-founded word, the couple's good name (தகைசான்ற சொற்காத்து), in its definition of the wife. 59 says those whose home does not seek renown (புகழ்புரிந்த இல் இலோர்க்கு) cannot walk with a proud gait before those who deride them. Parimelazhagar connects them directly, saying 59 states the fault that follows when that good name is not kept: the husband's standing in public depends on it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:58","b":"k:50","type":"parallel_domain","note":"58 says women who 'obtain the one who obtained them' (பெற்றாற் பெறின்) gain great distinction in the world where the gods live (புத்தேளிர் வாழும் உலகு); Parimelazhagar supplies 'honour' and reads it as wives who revere their husbands. 50 says the man who lives on earth as he should is placed among the gods of the sky. The two adjacent chapters end on the same reward, one for the householder and one for the wife, each for fulfilling the household role.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:59","b":"k:237","type":"develops","note":"59 says those whose home does not seek renown (புகழ்புரிந்த இல்) have no proud gait before those who deride them (இகழ்வார்முன்). 237 asks why those who cannot live with renown (புகழ்பட வாழாதார்) resent those who deride them (இகழ்வாரை நோவது எவன்) and not themselves. Both set புகழ் against இகழ்வார்: 59 says that without renown one has no answer to scorners, and 237 adds that the scorn is then one's own doing.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:59","b":"k:1014","type":"same_image","note":"59 says that without a home that seeks renown there is no walking with a bull's (Parimelazhagar: lion's) proud gait, ஏறுபோல் பீடு நடை. 1014 says shame is an ornament to the worthy, and without it their பீடு நடை is a disease. The proud walk appears twice as something that must be earned by an inner or domestic quality, the household's good name in one verse and a sense of shame in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:60","b":"k:61","type":"develops","note":"60 says the excellence of the home is called its blessing (மங்கலம்) and the gaining of good children (நன்மக்கட் பேறு) its fine ornament. 61, opening the next chapter, says that of all things to be gained we know none like the gaining of children who know what should be known (அறிவறிந்த மக்கட்பேறு). 61 picks up the word மக்கட்பேறு from 60 and specifies what makes the children 'good': their understanding.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:61","b":"k:162","type":"tension","note":"Verse 61 says that among all things gained (பெறுமவற்றுள்) 'we' know of nothing to set beside knowing children. Verse 162 says that among excellent gains (விழுப்பேற்றின்) nothing equals (அஃதொப்பது இல்லை) being free of envy. The two top rankings conflict only if each superlative is read literally as a ranking across all gains; read as each chapter's idiom of praise they do not.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:61","b":"k:300","type":"same_image","note":"Both verses rank their subject first by the author's own witness in the first person plural: 61 has யாமறிவது இல்லை ('we know of none') for knowing children, 300 has யாமெய்யாக் கண்டவற்றுள் இல்லை ('among what we have seen as true there is none') for truthfulness. The pairing shows the same rare device, the poet's 'we', used to vouch for a household good and for a virtue of speech.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:62","b":"k:44","type":"develops","note":"Verse 44 says that if a household life fears blame (பழியஞ்சி) and shares its food, its line (வழி) never fails. Verse 62 says that if one gets children in whom blame does not grow (பழிபிறங்கா), no evil touches one for seven births. In 44 the parent's freedom from blame secures the descendants; in 62 the descendants' freedom from blame secures the parent.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:62","b":"k:398","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 62 says evils will not touch a person in all seven births (எழுபிறப்பும் தீயவை தீண்டா) if they get children of character who bring no blame. Verse 398 says the learning gained in one birth is a protection in seven (எழுமையும் ஏமாப்புடைத்து). Both name a good of this life whose protection outlasts it by seven births: in one case one's children, in the other one's own learning.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:62","b":"k:835","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 62 has blameless children keep evil away through seven births (எழுபிறப்பும்). Verse 835 has the fool, in one birth's doing (ஒருமைச் செயல்), make the mire (அளறு) he sinks in for seven (எழுமையும்). The same seven-birth reckoning is applied in the opposite direction, once to a safeguard and once to a ruin made by oneself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:63","b":"k:114","type":"develops","note":"Verse 63 says people call their children their wealth (தம்பொருள் என்ப தம் மக்கள்), and ties that wealth to deeds (வினையான் வரும்). Verse 114 says whether people are worthy or not is seen from their எச்சம், what they leave behind, which Parimelazhagar takes as their offspring and Kalaignar as the name they leave. On Parimelazhagar's reading the pair makes children both a person's property and the evidence by which that person is judged.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:63","b":"k:400","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 63 reports the saying that one's children are one's wealth (தம்பொருள்). Verse 400 says learning is the imperishable wealth (கேடில் விழுச்செல்வம்) and that other things are not wealth (மாடல்ல). Both move the word for property from goods to something else, but 400 denies the title to everything other than learning, while 63 only reports what people say.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:64","b":"k:1103","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 64 sets a child's messy gruel above amrita. In 1103 the lover asks whether the world of the lotus-eyed one (தாமரைக் கண்ணான் உலகு) can be sweeter (இனிதுகொல்) than sleeping on the soft arms of the one he loves; Parimelazhagar has him say this to a companion who reproved him. The same move, a heavenly standard of sweetness outdone by an intimate bodily one, appears in the parent's voice in Book 1 and in a lover's speech in Book 3.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:65","b":"k:1101","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 65 names two sense pleasures a child gives: touching the child's body is joy to the body (உடற்கின்பம்) and hearing its words is joy to the ear (இன்பம் செவிக்கு). In 1101 the lover says the five senses, seeing, hearing, tasting, smelling and touching (கண்டுகேட்டு உண்டுயிர்த்து உற்றறியும்), all have their objects in one woman. Both list pleasures sense by sense and locate them in a single loved person, a child in one and a lover in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:66","b":"k:1228","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 66 says only those who have not heard their own children's prattle call the flute (குழல்) and the lute sweet. In 1228 the woman left alone says the herdsman's flute (ஆயன் குழல்) is now a messenger of the burning evening and a weapon that kills; Parimelazhagar notes that it had been sweet to her before. In both the flute stands for accepted sweetness, and in both that sweetness is made to depend on the hearer's situation.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:67","b":"k:715","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 67 makes it the father's gift that the son sits ahead in the assembly (அவையத்து முந்தி இருப்ப). Verse 715 says the best of good things is the restraint of not speaking first among elders (முதுவருள் முந்து கிளவாச் செறிவு). The shared root முந்து shows the limit: standing first in the assembly is the goal, but being first to speak before one's seniors is what the book warns against.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:67","b":"k:722","type":"develops","note":"Verse 67 says a father's good to his son is to make him sit foremost in the assembly; Parimelazhagar takes this as making him learned beyond the learned there. Verse 722 says those called learned among the learned (கற்றாருள் கற்றார்) are those who can make what they learned go home before the learned. 722 supplies what the front seat of 67 requires in practice: learning that can be delivered in front of that assembly.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:68","b":"k:399","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 68 says one's children's understanding (அறிவுடைமை) is sweet to all beings on the great earth (மன்னுயிர்க்கெல்லாம் இனிது); Parimelazhagar reads தம்மின் as 'more than to oneself', while Kalaignar and Subramuniyaswami take it as children who surpass their parents. Verse 399 says the learned love learning more on seeing that what delights them delights the world (தாமின்புறுவது உலகின்புறக் கண்டு). Both treat knowledge as a pleasure that is not private: the parent's pride in 68 and the scholar's delight in 399 are each measured against the world's share in it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:69","b":"k:1047","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 69 has the mother's joy grow when she hears others call her son worthy (சான்றோன் எனக் கேட்ட தாய்). Verse 1047 says one in poverty unconnected with virtue (அறஞ்சாரா நல்குரவு) is looked on as a stranger even by the mother who bore him (ஈன்ற தாயானும் பிறன்போல நோக்கப்படும்). In both the mother's regard follows the son's standing, rising with his name in 69 and withdrawn in 1047.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:70","b":"k:265","type":"develops","note":"Verse 70 has onlookers explain a good son by asking what the father did as penance (என்நோற்றான்). Verse 265 says தவம் is striven for here because whatever is wanted is obtained as wanted (வேண்டிய வேண்டியாங்கு எய்தலால்). The onlookers' question in 70 assumes the doctrine 265 states, and it applies that doctrine to a son.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:71","b":"k:1180","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 71 says tears raise a public noise (பூசல் தரும்) about the love inside. In 1180 the woman says it is not hard for the town to get at the secret (மறை) of those like her whose eyes are beaten drums (அறைபறை கண்ணார்). In both the eyes make public what the heart holds, and both describe this as sound: a clamour in 71, a drum in 1180.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:72","b":"k:220","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 72 says those who have love belong to others even in their bones (என்பும் உரியர் பிறர்க்கு). Verse 220 says that if ruin comes through ஒப்புரவு, that ruin is worth buying even by selling oneself (விற்றுக்கோள் தக்கது). Both carry giving past one's goods to one's own person, 72 as a description of love and 220 as the price of duty to the community.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:72","b":"k:1009","type":"develops","note":"Verse 72 says the loveless belong to themselves in everything (எல்லாம் தமக்குரியர்) while the loving belong to others (பிறர்க்கு) even in their bones. Verse 1009 says wealth piled up by putting love aside (அன்பொரீஇ), denying oneself and ignoring virtue will be taken by others (கொள்வார் பிறர்). 1009 gives the outcome of the stance in 72: what the loveless keep for themselves ends with பிறர் anyway.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:73","b":"k:80","type":"develops","note":"Verse 73 says the life's connection with the bone (என்போடு) goes with love. Verse 80 says the seat of life (உயிர்நிலை) follows love, and that for those without it the body is bone wrapped in skin (என்புதோல் போர்த்த உடம்பு). The chapter's last verse gives the negative of 73: take away the love and what remains of the union is the bone alone.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:73","b":"k:338","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 73 treats the joining of life and body (என்போடு இயைந்த தொடர்பு) as something fitted to love. Verse 338 says the friendship between body and life (உடம்பொடு உயிரிடை நட்பு) is like a bird flying off and leaving its குடம்பை alone. The pairing sets a limit on 73: the bond that exists for love's sake is, in the chapter on impermanence, one the life will abandon.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:73","b":"k:1122","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 73 says the tie of the life with the body (ஆருயிர்க்கு என்போடு இயைந்த தொடர்பு) is, they say, a matter of being joined with love (அன்போடு இயைந்த வழக்கு); Parimelazhagar takes embodiment as the result of a course meant for joining with love. In 1122 the lover says the bond between him and the woman is what the bond between body and life is (உடம்பொடு உயிரிடை என்ன). 73 explains the union of body and life by love, and the lover in 1122 measures his love by that union.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:74","b":"k:757","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 74 says love gives birth (ஈனும்) to eagerness, and that gives birth to friendship, an unsought distinction (நாடாச் சிறப்பு). Verse 757 calls அருள் the child born of love (அன்பீன் குழவி), raised by the foster-mother wealth. Both use the figure of birth for what comes out of அன்பு, and together they give love two offspring, friendship in the home and grace toward all.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:74","b":"k:785","type":"develops","note":"Verse 74 derives friendship (நண்பு) from love by way of eagerness (ஆர்வம்) and calls it a distinction that is not sought (நாடா). Verse 785 says meeting and long acquaintance are not needed, because feeling itself (உணர்ச்சிதான்) gives the claim of friendship. The friendship chapter of Book 2 keeps the account given in Book 1: friendship comes from an inner disposition and not from arrangement or contact.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:75","b":"k:39","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 39 says only what comes through virtue is joy (அறத்தான் வருவதே இன்பம்) and everything else lies outside it. Verse 75 traces the joy and distinction people reach in the world to a life settled in love (அன்புற்று அமர்ந்த வழக்கு). Set side by side, 75 adds love to the source that 39 confines to அறம், which agrees with verse 45's coupling of the two.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:76","b":"k:862","type":"develops","note":"Verse 76 says the ignorant hold that love supports only virtue, while it is the help (துணை) for மறம் as well; Kalaignar reads மறம் as valour, Parimelazhagar as the hostility that love removes. Verse 862 asks how a man without love (அன்பிலன்), without allies and without strength of his own can wear down an enemy's might. On Kalaignar's reading 862 is the case in point: in Book 2 love is listed first among the things a fighter needs.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:77","b":"k:80","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 77 compares the loveless to a thing without bones (என்பிலது) dried by the sun; Kalaignar and Subramuniyaswami take it as a worm. Verse 80 says the body of those without love is bones wrapped in skin (என்புதோல் போர்த்த உடம்பு). The chapter uses bone twice for the loveless in opposite ways, once as what they lack and once as all they are.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:77","b":"k:204","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 77 says அறம் scorches what has no love as the sun scorches what has no bones (வெயில்போலக் காயுமே). Verse 204 says that if one plots another's ruin, அறம் plots the plotter's ruin (அறஞ்சூழும் சூழ்ந்தவன் கேடு). In both அறம் is an agent that acts against a person, and Parimelazhagar glosses it in both places as அறக்கடவுள், the god of virtue.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:78","b":"k:576","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 78 likens the loveless life to a withered tree on hard ground. Verse 576 says those who have eyes and no kindly regard are like trees fixed in the soil (மண்ணோடியைந்த மரத்தனையர்). In both a person without the inward quality, அன்பு in one and கண்ணோட்டம் in the other, is reduced to a tree, alive in form and without response.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:78","b":"k:1106","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 78 says a life with no love within is like a dried tree putting out shoots on hard ground (வற்றல் மரம் தளிர்த்தற்று), which Parimelazhagar takes as something that does not happen. In 1106 the lover says her touch makes his life sprout each time they meet (உறுதோறு உயிர் தளிர்ப்ப). The verb தளிர் carries both: life sprouts where love is, and 78 uses the same sprouting to mark what a loveless life cannot do.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:79","b":"k:407","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 79 says the outer limbs are of no use without the inner limb, love. Verse 407 says the fine looks (எழில்நலம்) of one without subtle, penetrating understanding are like those of a well-made clay doll (மண்மாண் புனைபாவை). Both set a complete exterior against a missing interior, love in Book 1 and understanding in Book 2.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:79","b":"k:574","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 79 asks what all the outer parts can do (புறத்துறுப்பெல்லாம் எவன்செய்யும்) for those who lack love, the inner part (அகத்துறுப்பு). Verse 574 asks what eyes do (எவன் செய்யும்), beyond seeming to be in the face, when they have no கண்ணோட்டம். The same question in the same words is put to the whole body in 79 and to one organ in 574: a part is judged by the disposition it serves.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:80","b":"k:255","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 80 says the seat of life (உயிர்நிலை) goes by way of love (அன்பின் வழியது). Verse 255 says உயிர்நிலை lies in not eating flesh (உண்ணாமை உள்ளது உயிர்நிலை); Parimelazhagar reads this as life's staying in the body resting on that virtue. The rare word உயிர்நிலை is used in both to make a moral quality, love or not eating flesh, the condition of being alive at all.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:80","b":"k:1020","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 80 says that for those without love the body is only bones wrapped in skin (என்புதோல் போர்த்த உடம்பு). Verse 1020 says the movements of those with no shame within (நாண் அகத்தில்லார்) are like a wooden puppet feigning life by its string (மரப்பாவை ... உயிர்மருட்டியற்று). Both describe a moving body that lacks an inward quality as something without life in it, a frame of bone in one and a puppet in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:82","b":"k:229","type":"agrees","note":"82 says eating alone while the guest is outside (விருந்து புறத்ததாத் தானுண்டல்) is not to be desired even if the food were the medicine against death. 229 says eating by oneself alone (தாமே தமியர் உணல்) to fill up one's store is more bitter than begging. Both judge the solitary meal itself, one by setting it against the best imaginable food and the other against the worst condition.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:83","b":"k:227","type":"agrees","note":"83 says the life of one who tends the arriving guest every day (வைகலும் ஓம்புவான்) does not suffer and go to ruin (பருவந்து பாழ்படுதல் இன்று). 227 says the disease called hunger rarely touches one accustomed to sharing his food (பாத்தூண் மரீஇயவனை). Both promise that habitual sharing of food protects the sharer from want, once for the host of guests and once for the giver to the poor.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:83","b":"k:480","type":"qualifies","note":"83 says daily hospitality never brings a household to ruin (பாழ்படுதல் இன்று), which Parimelazhagar takes to mean the host's goods are not used up. 480 says helpfulness that does not weigh the extent of what one has (உளவரை தூக்காத ஒப்புரவாண்மை) quickly ends one's wealth (வல்லைக் கெடும்). The Book of Wealth thus attaches a condition of measure to the kind of promise 83 makes without one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:84","b":"k:617","type":"same_image","note":"84 has the goddess of fortune (செய்யாள்) living in the home of the glad-faced host. 617 says she of the lotus (தாமரையினாள்) lives in the effort of the unslothful (மடியிலான் தாளுளாள்), and her dark sister in sloth. The same figure is given two different dwellings, generosity in the Book of Virtue and exertion in the Book of Wealth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:85","b":"k:545","type":"parallel_domain","note":"85 implies that the field of the host who feeds guests first yields without sowing. 545 says rain and harvest gather together (பெயலும் விளையுளும் தொக்கு) in the land of the king who wields the sceptre as he should. In both, right conduct is said to make the land fruitful, at the scale of one household's field and at the scale of a kingdom.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:85","b":"k:1039","type":"tension","note":"85 asks whether one who feeds guests and eats what is left (விருந்தோம்பி மிச்சில் மிசைவான்) need even sow his field (வித்தும் இடல்வேண்டும் கொல்லோ); Parimelazhagar supplies that it will bear by itself. 1039 says that if the owner stays away and does not go to it (செல்லான் கிழவன் இருப்பின்), the land sulks like a wife. The conflict holds only if 85 is taken literally, as Parimelazhagar does, and not as a figure of praise; Kalaignar reads 85 quite differently, as asking whether such a host would hold back even his seed-grain.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:86","b":"k:50","type":"develops","note":"86 says one who tends the departing guest and watches for the next (வருவிருந்து பார்த்திருப்பான்) will be a good guest to those in the sky (வானத்தவர்க்கு). 50 says one who lives on earth as one should (வாழ்வாங்கு வாழ்பவன்) will be placed among the gods who dwell in the sky (வான்உறையும் தெய்வத்துள்). 86 names one specific practice that earns what 50 promises the householder in general, and turns it into a reciprocity: the host becomes the guest. (This edition prints நல்வருந்து in 86 and உநற்யும் in 50; both are typos.)","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:87","b":"k:221","type":"tension","note":"On Parimelazhagar's reading, 87 scales the fruit of hospitality to the guest's worth (விருந்தின் துணைத்துணை). 221 says giving to those who have nothing (வறியார்க்கு ஒன்று ஈவதே ஈகை) is the only real giving, and all the rest is like a loan expecting return. One verse values the gift by the receiver's merit and the other by the receiver's need; the conflict depends on reading துணை in 87 as merit and on treating feeding a guest and giving as the same kind of act.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:87","b":"k:259","type":"same_image","note":"87 calls hospitality a வேள்வி (sacrifice) whose fruit has no fixed measure; Parimelazhagar notes it is one of the five great sacrifices. 259 says not taking a life and eating it is better than pouring oblations in a thousand sacrifices (அவிசொரிந்து ஆயிரம் வேட்டலின்). In both verses the ritual word is used to rank an ethical practice: one is named a sacrifice, the other is set above a thousand of them.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:88","b":"k:228","type":"agrees","note":"88 says those who do not take up the sacrifice of tending guests guard their goods painfully (பரிந்தோம்பி) and end by saying 'we have no support' (பற்றற்றேம்). 228 asks whether the hard-eyed who store what they have and lose it (வைத்திழக்கும்) do not know the joy of giving. Both describe the same sequence of hoarding and then loss, and both set it against a use of wealth the hoarder never tried.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:88","b":"k:376","type":"develops","note":"88 pictures people guarding their goods with pain (பரிந்தோம்பி) and still losing them. 376 says what is not allotted to one will not stay even if guarded with pain (பரியினும் ஆகாவாம் பாலல்ல), and what is one's own will not leave even if poured away (சொரியினும் போகா தம). The fate chapter gives a general ground for 88's observation: anxious guarding does not decide whether wealth stays, so spending it on guests risks nothing that guarding would have saved.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:89","b":"k:841","type":"develops","note":"89 says poverty in the midst of wealth is the folly (மடமை) of not caring for guests. 841 says lack of understanding is the poverty of poverties (அறிவின்மை இன்மையுள் இன்மை) and that the world does not count other lack as lack. 841 states as a general principle what 89 shows in one case: the real want is a failure of understanding, whatever one owns.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:90","b":"k:93","type":"develops","note":"90 gives the negative case: a face that turns (முகந்திரிந்து) and looks makes the guest wilt. 93 gives the positive rule in nearly the same terms: with a glad face, looking sweetly (முகத்தான் அமர்ந்து இனிது நோக்கி), then sweet words from the heart. The last verse of the hospitality chapter and the third of the next share the face-and-look sequence; Parimelazhagar on 90 also lists the glad face, the sweet word and the good deed as successive stages of welcome.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:90","b":"k:707","type":"develops","note":"90 says a guest wilts when looked at with an altered face (முகந்திரிந்து நோக்க). 707 says nothing is as knowing as the face, since whether one is pleased or angry it gets there first (உவப்பினும் காயினும் தான்முந்துறும்). 707 explains why 90's guest is hurt before a word is spoken: the face has already reported the host's mind.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:91","b":"k:358","type":"same_image","note":"91 says sweet words are the speech of those who have seen the செம்பொருள் (செம்பொருள் கண்டார் வாய்ச்சொல்), which Parimelazhagar glosses here as those who know virtue. 358 defines understanding as seeing the செம்பொருள் so that the folly of birth departs (செம்பொருள் காண்பது அறிவு). The same phrase links ordinary kind speech to the insight the renunciation chapters describe, though the commentator gives the word a more modest sense in 91.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:92","b":"k:295","type":"parallel_domain","note":"92 says being sweet-spoken with a glad face is better than giving with a glad heart (அகன்அமர்ந்து ஈதலின் நன்றே). 295 says one who speaks truth in accord with his heart (மனத்தொடு வாய்மை மொழியின்) is above those who do austerity together with giving (தவத்தொடு தானஞ்செய்வாரின் தலை). Twice the book ranks a quality of speech above material giving, once for kindness and once for truthfulness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:93","b":"k:34","type":"develops","note":"93 says virtue lies in sweet words that come from within (அகத்தானாம் இன்சொலினதே அறம்), after a glad face and a kind look. 34 says being spotless in mind is the whole of virtue and the rest is noisy show (ஆகுல நீர பிற); Parimelazhagar counts speech and outward guise among that rest. 93's word அகத்தானாம் is what keeps sweet speech on the right side of 34's line: it counts as virtue because it issues from the mind.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:93","b":"k:786","type":"parallel_domain","note":"93 requires the glad face and the sweet word to come from within (முகத்தான் அமர்ந்து ... அகத்தானாம்). 786 says friendship that makes only the face smile is not friendship; friendship that makes the heart within smile is (முகநக ... நெஞ்சத்து அகநக). The same முகம்/அகம் pairing sets the test of genuineness for kind speech and for friendship.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:93","b":"k:824","type":"qualifies","note":"93 places virtue in a glad face followed by sweet words from within (அகத்தானாம்). 824 warns that deceivers who smile sweetly with the face and are bitter within (முகத்தின் இனிய நகாஅ அகத்தின்னா வஞ்சரை) are to be feared. 824 describes the counterfeit of 93's first half, so the outward signs 93 begins with cannot by themselves be taken as proof of the inner part.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:94","b":"k:525","type":"develops","note":"94 asserts that sweet speakers do not fall into want, without saying how. 525 says one who can give and speak sweetly (கொடுத்தலும் இன்சொலும் ஆற்றின்) will be surrounded by layer on layer of kin (அடுக்கிய சுற்றத்தால் சுற்றப்படும்). 525 supplies a worldly mechanism for 94's promise: sweet speech gathers people around one, and a person so surrounded is not left destitute.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"k:94","b":"k:566","type":"agrees","note":"94 says want that brings suffering (துன்புறூஉம் துவ்வாமை) does not come to those whose sweet speech gives joy to everyone. 566 says that if a ruler is harsh of speech and without kindly regard (கடுஞ்சொல்லன் கண்ணிலன்), his long-standing wealth perishes on the spot. The two verses state the same link between speech and prosperity from opposite sides, for anyone in the Book of Virtue and for a king in the Book of Wealth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:95","b":"k:978","type":"same_image","note":"95 makes humility (பணிவு) the true ornament (அணி). 978 says greatness always bows (பணியுமாம்) while smallness adorns itself in self-admiration (அணியுமாம் தன்னை வியந்து). The same two roots are set against each other in 978: the small person's self-adornment is the false ornament, and the bowing 95 praises is what the great actually wear.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:95","b":"k:1014","type":"qualifies","note":"95 says humility and sweet speech are a person's ornament and other things are not (அணியல்ல மற்றுப் பிற). 1014 asks whether shame is not the ornament of the worthy (அணிஅன்றோ நாணுடைமை சான்றோர்க்கு), and 575 names kindly regard as the eye's ornament. The exclusive wording of 95 is therefore the chapter's emphasis: other chapters award the same title to other inner qualities, while agreeing with 95 (as Parimelazhagar reads it) that bodily ornaments are not meant.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:96","b":"k:35","type":"develops","note":"35 defines virtue as what proceeds without slipping into four things, the last being painful speech (இன்னாச்சொல்). 96 says virtue grows and its opposite wears away when one speaks good things sweetly (அல்லவை தேய அறம்பெருகும்). 35 only requires avoiding harsh words; 96 adds that the positive practice of sweet, well-chosen speech actively increases virtue.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:96","b":"k:128","type":"agrees","note":"96 says that if one seeks out good things and says them sweetly (நல்லவை நாடி இனிய சொலின்), what is not virtue wears away and virtue grows. 128 says that if even one harmful word has its effect (ஒன்றானும் தீச்சொல் பொருட்பயன் உண்டாயின்), what was good turns out not good. Together they give speech power over one's stock of virtue in both directions: good words build it and a single bad word undoes it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:97","b":"k:194","type":"same_image","note":"97 says speech that yields benefit and does not part from pleasantness (பயன்ஈன்று பண்பின் தலைப்பிரியாச் சொல்) produces நயன் and brings forth good (நன்றி). 194 says speech with no benefit and no pleasantness (பயன்சாராப் பண்பில்சொல்) is without நயன் and removes one from good (நன்மையின் நீக்கும்). The four terms பயன், பண்பு, நயன் and நன்மை recur in the negative, so 194 is 97 inverted; Parimelazhagar glosses பண்பு as sweetness and நயன் as justice in both.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:97","b":"k:197","type":"qualifies","note":"97 asks for speech that has both benefit (பயன்) and pleasantness (பண்பு), and credits it with producing நயன். 197 says the worthy may say what lacks நயன் if they must, but should not say what lacks பயன் (நயனில சொல்லினும் சொல்லுக ... பயனில சொல்லாமை நன்று). 197 thus ranks the qualities 97 holds together: use is the one that cannot be given up. Parimelazhagar takes நயன் in 197 as justice; Subramuniyaswami takes it as pleasantness, which makes the limit on chapter 10 more direct.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:98","b":"k:291","type":"parallel_domain","note":"98 says sweet speech that is clear of smallness (சிறுமையுள் நீங்கிய இன்சொல்) gives joy in the next life and this one; Parimelazhagar explains சிறுமை as causing pain to others by what the words mean. 291 defines truth as speech containing no harm at all (யாதொன்றும் தீமை இலாத சொலல்). Sweetness and truthfulness are both defined by the same test, the absence of harm to others, and not by sound or by literal accuracy. (This edition prints சிறுமையுவு in 98, a typo for சிறுமையுள்.)","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:98","b":"k:911","type":"qualifies","note":"98 says sweet speech free of smallness gives joy (இன்சொல் ... இன்பம் தரும்) here and hereafter. 911 says the sweet speech of women who desire wealth and not the man brings harm (இன்சொல் இழுக்குத் தரும்). The two predicates answer each other, and the condition in 98 (சிறுமையுள் நீங்கிய) marks the difference: what 98 promises holds only for sweet words that are not serving a mean purpose.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:99","b":"k:318","type":"parallel_domain","note":"99 asks why one who sees how sweetly sweet words yield (இன்சொல் இனிதீன்றல் காண்பான்) would use harsh words (எவன்கொலோ வன்சொல் வழங்குவது); Parimelazhagar takes it as one who has felt the pleasure of others' kind words to himself. 318 asks why one who knows what is painful to his own life does painful things to other lives (என்கொலோ மன்னுயிர்க்கு இன்னா செயல்). The two rhetorical questions have the same form and the same argument from one's own experience to the treatment of others, applied to words in 99 and to harm in general in 318. (The first line of 318 is misprinted in this edition, but the verse is recoverable.)","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:99","b":"k:784","type":"qualifies","note":"99 asks what could lead someone who knows the good that sweet words do to use harsh ones (வன்சொல்). 784 says friendship is not for laughing together but for going ahead to rebuke when a friend oversteps (மிகுதிக்கண் மேற்சென்று இடித்தற் பொருட்டு). The friendship chapter supplies a case where sharp words have a purpose, so 99's question is best read as aimed at needless harshness and not at every hard word.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:100","b":"k:831","type":"parallel_domain","note":"100 pictures someone who has ripe fruit at hand and takes the unripe: unpleasant words chosen while pleasant ones exist (இனிய உளவாக இன்னாத கூறல்). 831 defines folly as taking hold of harm and letting gain go (ஏதங்கொண்டு ஊதியம் போக விடல்). Harsh speech in 100 is an instance of exactly the choice 831 names as folly, which fits the chapter's closing argument that harsh words are a bad bargain for the speaker as well as wrong.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:100","b":"k:1306","type":"same_image","note":"100 says speaking unpleasant things when pleasant ones are available is like taking the unripe fruit when the ripe is there (கனிஇருப்பக் காய்கவர்ந்தற்று). 1306, a speech in the Book of Love, says that love without serious and light quarrels is like over-ripe fruit and unripe fruit (கனியும் கருக்காயும் அற்று). The ripe/unripe pair is used differently: in 100 the ripe fruit is simply the right choice, while the lover treats fruit that is too ripe and fruit that is too raw as two ways of missing the right point.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:101","b":"k:211","type":"agrees","note":"101 says that for help given without any prior help (செய்யாமல் செய்த உதவி) even earth and heaven are hardly a match (ஆற்றல் அரிது). 211 says obligation wants no return (கைம்மாறு வேண்டா) and asks what the world does for the rain in exchange (என் ஆற்றுங் கொல்லோ). The same verb ஆற்று marks both: one verse looks from the receiver who cannot repay, the other from the giver who does not ask to be repaid.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:101","b":"k:313","type":"same_image","note":"Both open with செய்யாமல், 'without (the other) having done anything first'. 101 applies it to help: unprompted help is beyond repayment. 313 applies it to hatred: even toward those who hate you though you did nothing (செய்யாமல் செற்றார்க்கும்), doing hurt brings sorrow with no escape. Read together, the unprovoked good creates a boundless debt while the unprovoked wrong still gives no licence to retaliate.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:102","b":"k:788","type":"develops","note":"102 says help done at the time of need (காலத்தினால் செய்த நன்றி), though small, is greater than the earth. 788 makes that timeliness the definition of friendship: it removes distress there and then (ஆங்கே இடுக்கண் களைவது), like the hand going to a slipping garment. 102 values the help by its moment; 788 says what kind of helper acts in that moment.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:103","b":"k:813","type":"parallel_domain","note":"103 praises help from those who do not weigh the return (பயன்தூக்கார்), calling its good greater than the sea. 813 uses the same weighing verb for the opposite case: friends who weigh what they will get (உறுவது சீர்தூக்கும் நட்பு) are put level with thieves. The pair shows the book judging help and friendship by one test, whether the giver was calculating.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:104","b":"k:109","type":"develops","note":"104 says a millet of good is taken as a palmyra by those who know its worth. 109 says even harm like killing (கொன்றன்ன இன்னா) vanishes when one thinks of a single good the person did (ஒன்று நன்று உள்ளக் கெடும்). Parimelazhagar connects them in his note on 109: because the small good is held as large, that one good can cancel the later harm.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:106","b":"k:800","type":"agrees","note":"Both verses use the phrase மாசற்றார் கேண்மை, the bond of the stainless: 106 says do not forget it (மறவற்க), 800 says keep close to it (மருவுக). Each then adds a second command about a kind of friend, 106 never to abandon those who were a support in distress, 800 to drop the unsuitable (ஒப்பிலார்) even at a cost. The chapter on choosing friends repeats the gratitude chapter's rule and supplies its other half, whom to leave.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:106","b":"k:806","type":"agrees","note":"106 commands: do not abandon (துறவற்க) the friendship of those who were a support in distress. 806 states it as a description: those who keep within the bounds will not abandon (துறவார்) the tie of long-standing friends even in ruin (தொலைவிடத்தும்). The same verb is used in both, with the direction reversed: 106 is loyalty owed for help received in one's own trouble, 806 loyalty kept when trouble comes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:107","b":"k:398","type":"same_image","note":"107 says the friendship of one who removed one's distress is remembered through seven births (எழுமை எழுபிறப்பும்). 398 says learning gained in one birth (ஒருமைக்கண்) is a protection through seven (எழுமையும்). The seven-birth span is the book's figure for what outlasts a single life, used here for a debt of gratitude and there for learning.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:107","b":"k:799","type":"parallel_domain","note":"107 says people will remember (உள்ளுவர்) through seven births the friendship of those who wiped away their distress. 799 says the bond with those who abandoned one in ruin (கெடுங்காலைக் கைவிடுவார்) burns the heart even when thought of (உள்ளினும்) at the hour of death. Both are about how conduct in another's hard time is remembered: the helper for lives to come, the deserter up to the last moment.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:108","b":"k:113","type":"same_image","note":"108 says a wrong done to you should be forgotten that very day (அன்றே மறப்பது). 113 says gain that comes from leaving fairness should be let go that very day (அன்றே ஒழிய விடல்). The shared அன்றே gives both the same urgency: neither a grievance nor an unfair gain is to be kept overnight.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:109","b":"k:807","type":"parallel_domain","note":"109 says that even harm like killing disappears when one recalls a single good the person once did (அவர் செய்த ஒன்று நன்று உள்ள). 807 says those whose bond has come by the way of love do not cut off love even when the friend does ruinous things (அழிவந்த செய்யினும் அன்பறார்). Both have the same 'even if they do grave harm' (செயினும்) clause; what outweighs the harm is a remembered favour in one and long affection in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:110","b":"k:207","type":"same_image","note":"110 says those who kill any good may have an escape (உய்வுண்டாம்), but there is none for one who kills a good done to him (செய்ந்நன்றி கொன்ற மகற்கு). 207 has the same shape: those who incur any enmity (எனைப்பகை உற்றாரும்) escape (உய்வர்), but the enmity of one's deeds follows and kills. Both use 'everything else can be survived' to single out one thing, ingratitude in 110 and evil deeds in general in 207.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:110","b":"k:896","type":"same_image","note":"110 says there is escape (உய்வுண்டாம்) for other wrongs but none (உய்வில்லை) for killing gratitude. 896 says one burned by fire may survive (உய்வுண்டாம்), but those who go on offending the great will not (உய்யார்). The same உய்வு formula marks what each chapter treats as its unforgivable offence, so a reader should take 'only this has no escape' as the book's way of stressing gravity and not as a strict ranking.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:111","b":"k:528","type":"qualifies","note":"111 asks that fairness be kept alike across the divisions of people, on Parimelazhagar's reading toward enemy, stranger and friend. 528 says a king should not look on all in common (பொதுநோக்கான்) but according to rank or merit (வரிசையா நோக்கின்), and many will then stay by him. Together they show that impartiality in 111 does not mean identical treatment: one may not favour by relationship, but one should distinguish by desert.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:111","b":"k:541","type":"parallel_domain","note":"111 says the virtue called தகுதி is good if one keeps to what is due in each case (பகுதியால் பாற்பட்டு ஒழுக); Parimelazhagar takes பகுதி as enemy, stranger and friend. 541 defines a king's justice (முறை) as inquiring, showing favour to no one (யார்மாட்டும் கண்ணோடாது) and acting after consideration. The householder's impartiality of 111 reappears as the procedure of the ruler's court.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:112","b":"k:169","type":"qualifies","note":"112 says the wealth of a person of rectitude (செப்பம் உடையவன் ஆக்கம்) does not decay and is a security to his descendants. 169 admits the opposite case occurs: the prosperity of the crooked-hearted and the ruin of the upright (செவ்வியான் கேடு) are things to be pondered (நினைக்கப்படும்). 169 thus limits 112 to the usual course of things; Parimelazhagar explains the exceptions by deeds of earlier births.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:112","b":"k:171","type":"agrees","note":"112 says a fair person's wealth is a support to his posterity (எச்சத்திற்கு ஏமாப்பு). 171 states the reverse: if one covets others' goods without fairness (நடுவின்றி), the family perishes (குடிபொன்றி) and guilt comes at once. Both tie நடுவு to what happens to one's line, 112 as promise and 171 as warning.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:113","b":"k:328","type":"parallel_domain","note":"113 says to give up at once gain that comes from leaving fairness (நடுவிகந்தாம் ஆக்கம்), even if it yields only good (நன்றே தரினும்). 328 says that, however great the gain that brings good (நன்றாகும் ஆக்கம் பெரிதெனினும்), gain that comes by killing (கொன்றாகும் ஆக்கம்) is lowest to the worthy. The same concession, 'even if it is good and large', is refused in both; only the forbidden means differs.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:115","b":"k:118","type":"same_image","note":"115 and 118 end with the same line, கோடாமை சான்றோர்க்கு அணி, 'not bending is the ornament of the worthy'. In 115 the bending is of the heart under loss and gain (நெஞ்சத்துக் கோடாமை); in 118 it is leaning to one side (ஒருபால் கோடாமை), like a balance. The repetition joins two sides of நடுவு: steadiness under one's own fortunes and impartiality between others.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:115","b":"k:169","type":"develops","note":"115 says loss and increase both occur, and the worthy keep the heart from bending because of them. 169 gives the hard case: the crooked-hearted prosper and the upright are ruined (செவ்வியான் கேடு), which calls for reflection. 169 shows the situation in which 115's advice is tested, when fortune seems to reward bending.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:116","b":"k:204","type":"agrees","note":"116 says that if one's heart leaves fairness and does wrong (நடுவொரீஇ அல்ல செயின்), one should know 'I will perish' (கெடுவல்யான்). 204 says do not plot another's ruin even by a lapse, for அறம் will plot the plotter's ruin (சூழ்ந்தவன் கேடு). Both say that harm intended for others returns as one's own ruin; 116 makes it the wrongdoer's own knowledge, 204 gives the agent as அறம்.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:117","b":"k:752","type":"tension","note":"117 says the world (உலகம்) will not count as ruin the low state (தாழ்வு) of one who stays in virtue by standing fair. 752 says everyone scorns those who have nothing (இல்லாரை எல்லாரும் எள்ளுவர்); Parimelazhagar adds 'even if they have every good quality'. They conflict if உலகம் in 117 means people in general; Parimelazhagar avoids this by reading உலகம் there as the high-minded (உயர்ந்தோர்).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:117","b":"k:988","type":"agrees","note":"117 says the low state of a person who stands fair is not held to be ruin (கெடுவாக வையாது). 988 says poverty is no disgrace (இன்மை இளிவன்று) for one who has the firmness called சால்பு. Both deny that poverty lowers a person of character; 117 says it for fairness in particular, 988 for worth as a whole.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:118","b":"k:561","type":"parallel_domain","note":"118 describes a balance that first makes itself level (சமன்செய்து) and then weighs (சீர்தூக்கும்). 561 says a king is one who examines a case fittingly (தக்காங்கு நாடி) and punishes in proportion (ஒத்தாங்கு ஒறுப்பது); Parimelazhagar glosses the examining as done 'standing in the middle' (நடுவாக நின்று). The two steps of the scale, being level and then measuring, match the two steps of just punishment.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:119","b":"k:279","type":"same_image","note":"119 says rectitude is the absence of crookedness in speech (சொற்கோட்டம்), provided there is none within (உட்கோட்டம்). 279 says the straight arrow is cruel and the bent lute (யாழ்கோடு) is good, so judge people by what their deeds do. Both use straight and bent to separate appearance from substance: 119 will not take straight words as proof without a straight mind, and 279 will not take outward shape as proof at all.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:119","b":"k:295","type":"agrees","note":"119 makes unbent speech count as rectitude only if the mind too is unbent (உட்கோட்டம் இன்மை பெறின்). 295 praises one who speaks truth together with the mind (மனத்தொடு வாய்மை மொழியின்) above those who do austerity and give. Both attach the same condition to good speech, agreement between word and mind, in the fields of fairness and of truthfulness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:120","b":"k:315","type":"parallel_domain","note":"120 says true trade for traders is to look after others' things as their own (பிறவும் தமபோல்). 315 asks what use understanding is if one does not guard against another's pain as one's own (பிறிதின்நோய் தந்நோய்போல்). The same 'as one's own' (தம்போல்) test is applied to goods in the market and to suffering in the chapter on not hurting.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:120","b":"k:1015","type":"parallel_domain","note":"120 asks traders to care for what belongs to others as for what is theirs (பிறவும் தமபோல் பேணி). 1015 says the world calls those who feel shame at others' blame and their own (பிறர்பழியும் தம்பழியும் நாணுவார்) the dwelling of shame; Parimelazhagar reads this as weighing the two alike. Both define a virtue, fairness in one and shame in the other, by removing the difference between oneself and others.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:121","b":"k:50","type":"agrees","note":"121 says restraint brings one among the immortals (அமரருள் உய்க்கும்); 50 says one who lives on earth as one should will be placed among the gods who dwell in the sky (தெய்வத்துள் வைக்கப்படும்). Both promise a place among the gods to a person living an ordinary household life well, without renunciation, and 121 names restraint as the quality that does it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:121","b":"k:168","type":"same_image","note":"121 ends அடங்காமை ஆரிருள் உய்த்து விடும், and 168 ends with envy, called a பாவி, which destroys fortune and தீயுழி உய்த்து விடும். The same closing verb phrase, 'drives and leaves one there', is used for lack of restraint and for envy, so the two vices are given the same power to deliver a person to a bad place.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:122","b":"k:31","type":"develops","note":"31 asks what gain is greater for a living being than virtue (அறத்தினூஉங்கு ஆக்கம் எவனோ உயிர்க்கு); 122 says there is no gain for a living being beyond restraint (ஆக்கம் அதனினூஉங்கு இல்லை உயிர்க்கு) and tells one to guard it as wealth. 122 reuses chapter 4's formula almost word for word and applies it to one particular virtue, so restraint is given the rank the earlier verse gave to virtue as a whole.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:122","b":"k:363","type":"same_image","note":"122 tells one to guard restraint as பொருள், wealth, since nothing is a greater gain; 363 says there is no fine wealth (விழுச்செல்வம்) here like not wanting, and nothing equal to it there either. Both verses count an inner holding-back as the real wealth and both use the 'nothing equals it' form, once for the householder's restraint and once for the renouncer's freedom from craving.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:123","b":"k:715","type":"same_image","note":"123 calls restraint செறிவு and says that, recognised, it yields distinction; 715 calls not speaking first among elders a செறிவு and ranks it the best of good things (முந்து கிளவாச் செறிவு). The shared word shows the general restraint of chapter 13 turning up in the assembly as a concrete act, holding back one's speech before those who know more.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:123","b":"k:834","type":"agrees","note":"123 says that if one knows and then restrains oneself in the proper way (அறிவறிந்து ஆற்றின் அடங்கப் பெறின்), that restraint is recognised and yields distinction; Parimelazhagar takes அறிவறிந்து as knowing that restraint is itself what knowledge is. 834 gives the negative: no fool is greater than one who has studied, understood and taught others but is not himself restrained (தானடங்காப் பேதை). Together they say knowledge is finished only when it turns into restraint.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:124","b":"k:29","type":"same_image","note":"29 speaks of those who have climbed and stand on the hill called good qualities (குணமென்னும் குன்றேறி நின்றார்), whom Parimelazhagar takes to be ascetics; 124 says the restrained person's stature is greater than a mountain (மலையினும் மாணப் பெரிது), and Parimelazhagar reads நிலை there as the householder's state. On his readings the renouncer stands on a hill while the householder who stays restrained in his own station is said to overtop one.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:124","b":"k:965","type":"qualifies","note":"124 says the stature of one who is restrained without shifting from his station (நிலையின் திரியாது அடங்கியான்) is far greater than a mountain's. 965 says even those like a hill will sink if they do a lowering thing as small as a kundri seed (குன்றின் அனையாரும் குன்றுவர்). The mountain stature of 124 holds only while the 'not shifting' holds; 965 states how little it takes to lose it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:124","b":"k:989","type":"agrees","note":"124 praises one who is restrained and does not shift from his station (நிலையின் திரியாது); 989 says those called the ஆழி of worth do not shift even when the age shifts (ஊழி பெயரினும் தாம் பெயரார்). Both measure a person by immovability, 124 against a mountain and 989 against the turning of an age.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:125","b":"k:985","type":"parallel_domain","note":"125 treats bowing (பணிதல்) as an excellence, a second wealth for the rich; 985 says bowing is the strength of those who get things done and the weapon by which the worthy turn opponents around (ஆற்றுவார் ஆற்றல் பணிதல்). The same act is valued as character in the Book of Virtue and as effective power in the Book of Wealth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:126","b":"k:398","type":"same_image","note":"126 says that drawing in the five like a tortoise in one birth is a protection through seven (ஒருமையுள் ... எழுமையும் ஏமாப்புடைத்து); 398 says the learning one gains in one birth is a protection through seven (ஒருமைக்கண் ... எழுமையும் ஏமாப்புடைத்து). The closing formula is identical, so sense-restraint and learning are set side by side as the two acquisitions that carry across births.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:126","b":"k:835","type":"parallel_domain","note":"126 says restraint of the five in one birth protects through seven; 835 says a fool can, by what he does in one birth (ஒருமைச் செயல்), make the mire he will sink in through seven (எழுமையும் தான்புக்கு அழுந்தும் அளறு). The one-birth and seven-births arithmetic runs in both directions: one life is enough to secure the next seven or to ruin them.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:127","b":"k:305","type":"parallel_domain","note":"127 says whatever else they fail to guard, let them guard the tongue, or they will grieve through a fault of speech (நாகாக்க காவாக்கால் சோகாப்பர்); 305 says if you would guard yourself, guard against anger, or anger will kill you (சினங்காக்க காவாக்கால் தன்னையே கொல்லும்). The sentence frame காக்க / காவாக்கால் is the same, and in both the harm of the unguarded thing falls on the person who failed to guard it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:128","b":"k:965","type":"agrees","note":"128 says that if even one bad word has its effect, the good turns out not good (ஒன்றானும் தீச்சொல் ... நன்றாகாதாகி விடும்); Parimelazhagar takes this as the person's other merit turning bad. 965 says even hill-like people sink if they do a lowering thing the size of a kundri seed. Both hold that a single small lapse is enough to cancel a large standing good, in speech in one verse and in honour in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:129","b":"k:159","type":"develops","note":"129 says the scar left by the tongue never heals; 159 says those who bear the harsh words from the mouths of people who exceed all bounds (இறந்தார்வாய் இன்னாச்சொல் நோற்கிற்பவர்) are as pure as renouncers. 129 gives the reason 159's praise is so high: what the forbearing person takes without retaliation is the one kind of wound the book says does not close.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:129","b":"k:1049","type":"same_image","note":"129 sets a fire-burn, which heals, against the tongue's scar, which does not; 1049 says one can even sleep in the middle of fire but cannot close an eye in the middle of poverty (நெருப்பினுள் துஞ்சலும் ஆகும்). The book repeatedly takes fire as the measure of bearable pain and places a non-physical hurt beyond it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:130","b":"k:204","type":"same_image","note":"130 says அறம் itself watches for the right moment and enters the path of one who has guarded against anger, learned, and is restrained (அறம் பார்க்கும் ஆற்றின் நுழைந்து); 204 says that if you plot another's ruin, அறம் plots the ruin of the plotter (அறஞ்சூழும் சூழ்ந்தவன் கேடு). In both verses virtue is an agent that acts on a person, seeking out the restrained and planning against the malicious.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:130","b":"k:594","type":"parallel_domain","note":"130 has virtue looking for its chance and coming along the path to the restrained person; 594 has prosperity asking the way and going to the one of unwavering energy (ஆக்கம் அதர்வினாய்ச் செல்லும்). The same figure, a good that seeks out the person fit for it instead of being pursued, is used for virtue in the first book and for worldly gain in the second. The edition's text of 594 has lost its first letter (க்கம் for ஆக்கம்), but the reading is not in doubt.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:131","b":"k:1017","type":"agrees","note":"131 says conduct is to be guarded more than life because it gives excellence (ஒழுக்கம் உயிரினும் ஓம்பப்படும்); 1017 says those ruled by shame will give up life for shame and will not give up shame for life (நாணால் உயிரைத் துறப்பர்). Both place a quality of character above life itself; 1017 states as what such people actually do what 131 states as a rule.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:133","b":"k:951","type":"tension","note":"133 makes conduct the thing that constitutes good family (ஒழுக்கம் உடைமை குடிமை); 951 says integrity and shame are found together by nature only in those born in a good house (இற்பிறந்தார் கண்அல்லது இல்லை இயல்பாக). If குடிமை in 133 means that conduct alone makes one well-born, 951 runs the other way and makes birth the source of the conduct. The conflict disappears on Parimelazhagar's reading of 133, where conduct only preserves or forfeits a rank that birth has already given.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:133","b":"k:1019","type":"agrees","note":"133 says a lapse from conduct turns into low birth; 1019 says a lapse in கொள்கை burns one's lineage (குலஞ்சுடும் கொள்கை பிழைப்பின்), and Parimelazhagar glosses கொள்கை there as ஒழுக்கம். Both say family standing is lost through the individual's failure in conduct, one calling the result a low birth and the other a burning.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:134","b":"k:560","type":"same_image","note":"134 treats forgetting the Veda (மறப்பினும் ஓத்துக் கொளலாகும்) as a loss that can be made good; 560 lists among the effects of a king who does not protect that those of the six occupations forget their books (அறுதொழிலோர் நூல்மறப்பர்). The same forgetting appears as a private, repairable lapse in 134 and as a sign of public ruin in 560, while 134 keeps conduct as the loss that cannot be repaired.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:135","b":"k:169","type":"qualifies","note":"135 takes it as given that the envious have no gain (ஆக்கம்); 169 says the gain of the crooked-hearted and the ruin of the upright are things to be pondered (அவ்விய நெஞ்சத்தான் ஆக்கமும் ... நினைக்கப்படும்). 169 concedes that the case 135 calls non-existent can occur and needs explaining, which weakens the simile's premise; Parimelazhagar softens it by adding 'if they occur'.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:135","b":"k:170","type":"develops","note":"135 argues by comparison: as there is no gain in the envious, there is no elevation in one without conduct (அழுக்காறுடையான்கண் ஆக்கம் போன்று இல்லை). 170 states the premise on its own: none have grown great through envy (அழுக்கற்று அகன்றாரும் இல்லை). 135 takes chapter 17's claim as already known and builds its point about conduct on it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:136","b":"k:164","type":"same_image","note":"136 says the strong do not slacken in conduct, knowing the harm that comes of a lapse (இழுக்கத்தின் ஏதம் படுபாக்கறிந்து); 164 says people do no wrong out of envy, knowing the harm that comes of the wrong path (இழுக்காற்றின் ஏதம் படுபாக்கு அறிந்து). The second lines are nearly the same words, and both verses ground good behaviour in foreseeing consequences, which 136 counts as strength (உரவோர்).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:137","b":"k:117","type":"develops","note":"137 says that from conduct people reach high standing and from a lapse they reach எய்தாப் பழி, which Parimelazhagar takes as blame that is not properly theirs, laid on them because the lapse makes it believable. 117 says the world will not count as ruin the low state of one who stays in the right by being fair (கெடுவாக வையாது உலகம்). In both the world's verdict follows character more than facts: it discounts the upright person's fall and charges the lapsed person with more than he did.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:138","b":"k:361","type":"same_image","note":"138 calls good conduct the seed of good (நன்றிக்கு வித்தாகும் நல்லொழுக்கம்) and says bad conduct always gives suffering; 361 calls craving the seed that produces unending birth for all beings (தவாஅப் பிறப்பீனும் வித்து). The seed figure is used for a cause whose yield comes later and keeps coming, once for a good crop and once for a bad one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:139","b":"k:199","type":"parallel_domain","note":"139 says speaking evil things even by a slip is not possible for those who have conduct (வழுக்கியும் வாயாற் சொலல் ஒல்லாவே); 199 says those of stainless sight do not say meaningless things even in forgetfulness (பொச்சாந்தும் சொல்லார்). Both claim that a settled quality rules out even the accidental lapse of speech, and they trace that quality to conduct in one case and to clear understanding in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:139","b":"k:959","type":"agrees","note":"139 makes the absence of evil speech a test of having conduct; 959 says that as a sprout shows the ground it lay in, the words from the mouth show the family of the one born in it (குலத்தில் பிறந்தார் வாய்ச்சொல்). Both treat what comes out of the mouth as the evidence of what a person is.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:139","b":"k:1044","type":"qualifies","note":"139 says a person of conduct cannot speak evil even by a slip; 1044 says that even in the well-born, poverty brings the slackness from which degrading words are born (இற்பிறந்தார் கண்ணேயும் இன்மை இளிவந்த சொற்பிறக்கும் சோர்வு தரும்). 1044 names a condition, want, under which the slip of speech that 139 calls impossible does happen to good people.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:140","b":"k:280","type":"parallel_domain","note":"140 makes accord with the world the test that learning must pass; 280 says neither shaving nor matted hair is needed if one gives up what the world condemns (உலகம் பழித்தது ஒழித்துவிடின்). In both, the world's standard outranks a formal qualification, learning for the scholar and outward marks for the ascetic. Parimelazhagar takes உலகம் in both verses as the worthy and not people at large.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:141","b":"k:832","type":"develops","note":"832 defines the folly of all follies (பேதைமையுள் எல்லாம் பேதைமை) as setting one's love on what is not proper to one (கையல்லதன்கண் காதன்மை). 141 names one case of exactly that: the பேதைமை of desiring (பெட்டு ஒழுகும்) a woman who is another's. Read together, 141 is an instance of the general definition that 832 gives much later.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:141","b":"k:910","type":"parallel_domain","note":"141 says the folly (பேதைமை) of desiring a woman who is another's (பிறன்பொருளாள்) is not found (இல்) in those who have seen aram and porul; 910 says the folly that comes of attachment to a woman (பெண்சேர்ந்தாம் பேதைமை) is never found (இல்) in those with a deliberating mind. Both end on 'பேதைமை ... இல்' and treat the lapse as a failure of understanding that knowledge excludes, once about another's wife in the Book of Virtue and once about subservience to one's own in the Book of Wealth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:142","b":"k:150","type":"agrees","note":"142 says that among all who stand outside virtue (அறன்கடை நின்றாருள் எல்லாம்) none is a greater fool than the one who stands at another's door (பிறன்கடை); 150 says that even one who does not keep to virtue and does wrong (அறன்வரையான் அல்ல செயினும்) does well not to desire another's woman. Both verses rank within the class of the non-virtuous, and both put this one offence at the bottom, 142 by naming the worst case and 150 by naming the one abstention still worth having.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:143","b":"k:214","type":"same_image","note":"143 counts the betrayer of a trusting household as no different from the dead (விளிந்தாரின் வேறல்லர்); 214 says only the one who knows what is fitting lives (உயிர்வாழ்வான்), and anyone else is placed among the dead (செத்தாருள் வைக்கப்படும்). In both, being alive is measured by conduct and not by breath, once for a wrong committed and once for a duty to others left undone.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:143","b":"k:926","type":"same_image","note":"143 says those who go on doing evil in the home of people who trust them (தெளிந்தாரில்) are 'no different from the dead' (விளிந்தாரின் வேறல்லர்); 926 uses the same construction for sleepers (துஞ்சினார் செத்தாரின் வேறல்லர்) and extends it to drinkers. Parimelazhagar explains 926 by the absence of awareness and 143 by the loss of aram, porul and inbam, so the same formula of living death is applied to a moral failure in one verse and to loss of consciousness in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:144","b":"k:433","type":"same_image","note":"Both verses use the millet's measure (தினைத்துணை). In 433 those who are ashamed of blame (பழிநாணுவார்) take a fault of a millet's size as large as a palmyra; in 144 the man entering another's home does not reflect even a millet's worth (தினைத்துணையும் தேரான்). The pair shows the same measure from opposite sides: the careful person enlarges the smallest fault, and the adulterer does not give the smallest thought.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:144","b":"k:965","type":"agrees","note":"144 asks what greatness comes to, however great a person is (எனைத்துணையர் ஆயினும் என்னாம்), if he enters another's home without weighing it even a millet's worth (தினைத்துணையும் தேரான்). 965 says even those like a hill (குன்றின் அனையார்) sink if they do a lowering deed as small as a kunri seed. Both hold that standing does not outweigh a degrading act; 965 gives the general rule and 144 one named act.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:145","b":"k:233","type":"qualifies","note":"233 says that apart from high renown nothing in the world stands without perishing (புகழல்லால் பொன்றாது நிற்பதொன்று இல்). 145 says the blame of the man who violates another's home also stands forever without dying (எஞ்ஞான்றும் விளியாது நிற்கும் பழி). Taken literally 145 names a second undying thing, so the pair shows the book treating lasting blame as the counterpart of lasting fame.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:145","b":"k:284","type":"parallel_domain","note":"145 says one who crosses into another's home thinking it easy (எளிதென இல்லிறப்பான்) gets blame that stands forever without dying (விளியாது நிற்கும் பழி). 284 says a love grown strong in theft (களவின்கண் கன்றிய காதல்) yields at harvest a distress that does not die (வீயா விழுமம்). The same logic, a desire for what is another's that looks light at the start and has a permanent result, is applied to another's wife and to another's goods.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:146","b":"k:171","type":"parallel_domain","note":"146 lists four things that never leave the man who crosses into another's home: enmity, sin, fear and blame (பகை பாவம் அச்சம் பழி). 171 says that one who covets another's good property without fairness finds his family ruined and fault brought on at once (குடிபொன்றிக் குற்றமும் ஆங்கே தரும்). Coveting another's wife and coveting another's goods are both given immediate, compound consequences; Parimelazhagar reads the பழி of 146 as blame on the family (குடிப்பழி), which brings it closer to the குடி of 171.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:147","b":"k:41","type":"develops","note":"Both verses use the formula 'the one called a householder' (இல்வாழ்வான் என்பான்). 41 defines him by what he supports: he is the steady help of the three on their good path. 147 defines him by what he refrains from: he lives the household life with aram (அறனியலான்) who does not desire the womanhood of one who belongs to another (பிறனியலாள் பெண்மை நயவாதவன்). Together they give a positive and a negative test for the same title.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:147","b":"k:49","type":"develops","note":"49 says that what is called aram is household life, and that it is good if there is nothing in it that another blames (பிறன்பழிப்பது இல்லாயின்). 147 says the householder living by aram is the one who does not desire another's woman. 147 can be read as naming a specific condition under which the household life of 49 stays free of others' blame; chapter 15 itself (145, 146) names பழி as the result of the offence.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:148","b":"k:282","type":"parallel_domain","note":"148 praises not even looking at (நோக்காத) another's wife; Parimelazhagar glosses this as not taking her into the mind. 282 says that even thinking in the heart (உள்ளத்தால் உள்ளலும்) of taking another's goods (பிறன்பொருளை) by stealth is wrong. In both chapters the prohibition is moved back from the act to the look or the thought, for another's wife and for another's property.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:150","b":"k:297","type":"parallel_domain","note":"150 says not desiring another's woman is good even in one who practises no aram and does wrong; 297 says that if one can keep to not lying, then not doing the other virtues (அறம்பிற செய்யாமை) is still good. Both isolate a single restraint and say it holds its value without the rest of virtue. Set side by side, the book makes this kind of claim for more than one virtue, so each is better read as emphasis than as a ranking.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:151","b":"k:579","type":"agrees","note":"151 says bearing with those who scorn us is the foremost thing (பொறுத்தல் தலை). 579, in the chapter on kindly regard in the Book of Wealth, says that showing regard and bearing (கண்ணோடிப் பொறுத்தாற்றும் பண்பே தலை) even with those whose nature is to hurt us is the foremost quality. The same ranking, with the same words பொறுத்தல் and தலை, is given for the householder and again for the ruler.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:152","b":"k:301","type":"develops","note":"Parimelazhagar reads 152 as telling one to bear a wrong even when one is able to punish it (தாம் ஒறுத்தற்கு இயன்ற காலத்தும்). 301 states that condition outright: only the one who holds back anger where it can take effect (செல்லிடத்துக் காப்பான்) truly holds it back, and where it cannot, it makes no difference. 301 supplies the reason the commentator's condition matters: forbearance toward those one cannot hurt is not yet forbearance.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:152","b":"k:488","type":"qualifies","note":"152 says to bear transgression always (என்றும்) and better still to forget it. 488, advising a king on timing, says: on seeing enemies, bear them (சுமக்க); when their end is seen to have come, the head goes down (கிழக்காம் தலை). In 488 the bearing is temporary and is kept in mind until the moment to act, where 152 asks for a bearing with no end that passes into forgetting. The Book of Wealth thus limits to a tactic what the Book of Virtue states without condition.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:153","b":"k:444","type":"same_image","note":"153 says the strength within strength (வன்மையுள் வன்மை) is bearing with fools (மடவார்ப் பொறை); 444 says the chief of all strengths (வன்மையுள் எல்லாம் தலை) is to live so that those greater than oneself become one's own. Neither verse puts strength in force. One finds it in enduring those below one in understanding and the other in attaching oneself to those above.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:153","b":"k:841","type":"same_image","note":"Both verses name a 'poverty within poverty' (இன்மையுள் இன்மை) and give different answers: in 153 it is turning away a guest, in 841 it is lack of understanding (அறிவின்மை), with other lack not counted as lack by the world. The same superlative formula serves hospitality in the Book of Virtue and intelligence in the Book of Wealth, which shows the formula working as emphasis within each chapter's topic.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:154","b":"k:864","type":"same_image","note":"154 ties நிறை to forbearance: to keep நிறையுடைமை, live guarding பொறையுடைமை. 864 pairs the same two things in the negative, describing the man who does not leave off anger and has no நிறை (நீங்கான் வெகுளி நிறையிலன்) as easy prey for anyone. The commentators gloss நிறை differently in the two places (Parimelazhagar: சால்பு in 154; Pope, following him, has keeping secrets in 864; Manakkudavar on 154 has a conduct of self-guarding), but in both verses its presence goes with restraint of anger and its absence with unchecked anger.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:154","b":"k:987","type":"develops","note":"154 says that one who wants நிறையுடைமை never to leave should guard forbearance; Parimelazhagar glosses நிறை here as சால்பு. 987 asks what use சால்பு is if one does not do pleasing things even to those who have done one harm (இன்னாசெய்தார்க்கும் இனியவே செய்யாக்கால்). On Parimelazhagar's reading the two say the same thing from both ends: forbearance keeps சால்பு in place, and சால்பு that does not show in the treatment of offenders is worth nothing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:155","b":"k:314","type":"develops","note":"155 says the wise do not count as anything those who punish in return (ஒறுத்தாரை ஒன்றாக வையார்) and keep those who forbear like stored gold. 314 keeps the word ஒறுத்தல் and gives it a new content: the way to punish those who have done harm is to shame them by doing them good (அவர்நாண நன்னயஞ் செய்து விடல்). 314 thus shows the only form of 'punishing' that 155's judgment leaves open. (The Parimelazhagar text displayed for 314 in this edition belongs to 315.)","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:155","b":"k:549","type":"qualifies","note":"155 says those who punish (ஒறுத்தார்) are not counted as anything. 549 says that guarding the people and removing wrongdoing (குற்றம் கடிதல்) is no stain on a king but his work (வடுவன்று வேந்தன் தொழில்). Parimelazhagar restricts 155 to a harm done to oneself, and 549 concerns a ruler acting for his subjects, so 549 marks the limit of 155: the low regard for punishing applies to personal retaliation and not to the office of justice.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:156","b":"k:145","type":"parallel_domain","note":"156 says those who forbear have renown until the end (பொன்றுந் துணையும் புகழ்), against one day's pleasure for those who retaliate; 145 says the man who violates another's home thinking it easy gets blame that never dies (விளியாது நிற்கும் பழி). The adjacent chapters use the same accounting, a brief gratification weighed against a permanent reputation, with புகழ் on one side and பழி on the other. Manakkudavar takes பொன்றுந் துணையும் as until the person's own death, Parimelazhagar as until the world ends.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:156","b":"k:173","type":"parallel_domain","note":"156 sets the one day's joy of those who retaliate (ஒருநாளை இன்பம்) against the lasting renown of those who forbear. 173 says those who want the other joy (மற்றின்பம்) will not do what is not aram out of desire for a small joy (சிற்றின்பம்). Both argue against a wrong by the shortness of the pleasure it buys, for retaliation in one verse and for coveting in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:156","b":"k:856","type":"agrees","note":"156 grants the retaliator one day's pleasure and gives the forbearing renown to the end. 856 says that the life of one who says it is sweet (இனிது) to prevail in hostility is close to failure and ruin (தவலும் கெடலும் நணித்து). Both admit that getting the better of an opponent tastes sweet and both say the sweetness is brief; the verse in the Book of Wealth names ruin where the verse in the Book of Virtue names only the loss of lasting fame.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:157","b":"k:312","type":"agrees","note":"157 says that though others do what is improper to one (திறனல்ல தற்பிறர் செய்யினும்), it is good not to do what is not aram. 312 says that even when someone has done harm out of hatred, not doing harm in return (மறுத்தின்னா செய்யாமை) is the settled principle of the stainless. The same rule appears in the householder's chapter on forbearance and in the ascetic's chapter on not harming, which makes it common to both ways of life.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:157","b":"k:319","type":"develops","note":"Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar read நோநொந்து in 157 as grieving for the suffering that will come to the wrongdoers. 319 states the mechanism behind that expectation: if one does harm to others in the forenoon, harm comes to oneself in the afternoon of its own accord (தாமே வரும்). On the commentators' reading 157 presupposes 319: the injured person pities the offender because the offence will return on him, and for the same reason does not add a wrong of his own. Kalaignar instead takes the pain in 157 as the injured person's own.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:157","b":"k:852","type":"parallel_domain","note":"157: though others do one wrong, not doing what is not aram is good (அறனல்ல செய்யாமை நன்று). 852: though someone does unbearable things intending division (பகல்கருதிப் பற்றா செயினும்), not doing harm with hostility in mind is the chief thing (இன்னாசெய்யாமை தலை). The 'even if they do ... not doing' structure and the refusal to answer in kind recur in the Book of Wealth's chapter on hostility, where the reason given is political prudence as well as virtue.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:158","b":"k:985","type":"agrees","note":"158 says to overcome those who do excessive wrong out of arrogance (மிகுதியான்) by one's own worthiness (தகுதியான் வென்றுவிடல்), which Parimelazhagar glosses as forbearance. 985 says the strength of the able is to bow (பணிதல்), and that this is the weapon by which the worthy change their opponents (மாற்றாரை மாற்றும் படை). Both describe yielding as the means of prevailing and use the vocabulary of contest, வெல்லல் and படை, for it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:158","b":"k:986","type":"qualifies","note":"158 calls forbearance toward the arrogant a victory (வென்றுவிடல்). 986 says the touchstone of சால்பு is to accept defeat (தோல்வி ... கொளல்) even from those who are not one's equals. The same conduct toward an overbearing lesser person is named winning in one verse and taking a loss in the other; 986 adds that it has to be felt and accepted as a defeat, which guards against reading 158 as a way of scoring over the offender. Manakkudavar on 158 says explicitly that forbearing is not defeat but is itself the victory.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:158","b":"k:1327","type":"parallel_domain","note":"158 says one defeats the overbearing by one's own forbearing worth. 1327, spoken by the man in the Book of Love, says that in a lovers' quarrel the one who loses has won (ஊடலில் தோற்றவர் வென்றார்), as is seen in the union that follows. The paradox that giving way is the real victory appears in ethics and again, in a dramatised speech, in love; the second is a lover's remark and not a moral rule, but the shape of the thought is the same.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:159","b":"k:48","type":"agrees","note":"159 says those who endure (நோற்கிற்பவர்) the harsh words of transgressors are as pure as renouncers. 48 says a household life that keeps others on their path and does not slip from aram has more endurance than those who do penance (நோற்பாரின் நோன்மை உடைத்து). Both apply the ascetic's verb நோல் to the householder and set him level with or above the ascetic; Parimelazhagar on 159 adds that these are people who remain in household life.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:159","b":"k:310","type":"same_image","note":"Both verses play on இறந்தார் and துறந்தார். 310: those who go beyond bounds in anger (இறந்தார்) are like the dead, and those who have renounced anger are equal to renouncers (துறந்தார் துணை). 159: those who endure harsh words from the mouths of those who go beyond bounds (இறந்தார்வாய் இன்னாச்சொல்) have the purity of renouncers (துறந்தாரின் தூய்மை). In both, a householder's control over anger, his own or his response to another's, earns the standing of renunciation.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:159","b":"k:389","type":"parallel_domain","note":"159 praises those who endure harsh words (இன்னாச்சொல்) from people who overstep. 389 says the world rests under the parasol of the king who has the character to bear words bitter to the ear (செவிகைப்பச் சொற்பொறுக்கும்). The same capacity is a private virtue in one verse and a quality of rule in the other. Parimelazhagar takes the bitter words of 389 to be the rebukes of advisers, so there the speaker means well, unlike the transgressors of 159.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:160","b":"k:261","type":"develops","note":"261 defines the form of tavam as two things: bearing the pain that comes to one (உற்றநோய் நோன்றல்) and doing no harm to living beings. 160 uses the same verb for both the faster and the one who endures harsh words (நோற்பார்). By 261's definition the person in 160 who bears abuse and does not retaliate already meets both parts of tavam, which explains how 160 can rank him ahead of those who fast.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:160","b":"k:295","type":"parallel_domain","note":"160 ranks those who fast behind those who bear harsh words; 295 says one who speaks truth in accord with his heart is above those who practise austerity together with giving (தவத்தொடு தானஞ்செய்வாரின் தலை). In both a virtue of everyday speech, enduring it in one case and keeping it truthful in the other, is placed above formal religious practice.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:161","b":"k:135","type":"same_image","note":"135 pairs the same two words: as the envious have no ஆக்கம், so one without ஒழுக்கம் has no உயர்வு. 161 fuses them, asking that freedom from அழுக்காறு be taken as ஒழுக்காறு. The rhyme ஒழுக்கு/அழுக்காறு is used in both to set envy and conduct side by side.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:162","b":"k:363","type":"parallel_domain","note":"162 says that among excellent gains (விழுப்பேற்றின்) nothing equals being free of envy toward anyone (அஃதொப்பது இல்லை). 363 says there is no excellent wealth (விழுச்செல்வம்) like வேண்டாமை, here or there (அஃதொப்பது இல்). The same formula ranks an absence, of envy in the householder book and of wanting in the ascetic book, above every positive possession.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:163","b":"k:141","type":"parallel_domain","note":"141 says the folly of desiring a woman who is another's (பிறன்பொருளாள்) is not found in those who have seen அறம் and பொருள். 163 says the envier of பிறனாக்கம் is one who wants neither அறன் nor ஆக்கம். In adjacent chapters a wrong attitude to what is another's is measured against the same pair, virtue and wealth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:163","b":"k:1079","type":"develops","note":"163 says the one who does not cherish another's prosperity (பிறனாக்கம் பேணாது) and envies it is called one who wants neither அறன் nor ஆக்கம். 1079 shows the same reaction in the கீழ்: on seeing others clothed and fed (உடுப்பதூஉம் உண்பதூஉம் காணின்) he is quick to find a fault in them. The later verse gives the behaviour that the earlier one judges.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:165","b":"k:304","type":"parallel_domain","note":"304 asks whether there is any other enemy than anger (சினத்தின் பகையும் உளவோ பிற), which kills smile and joy. 165 says the envious need no enemy since envy itself brings ruin. Two of the four things 35 excludes from virtue, anger and envy, are each described as the person's own enemy.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:165","b":"k:434","type":"agrees","note":"434 says fault itself is the enemy that brings one's end (குற்றமே அற்றம் தரூஉம் பகை). 165 says the same of one fault: envy suffices to ruin its holder without any ஒன்னார். The king's general rule in Book 2 has a specific case already stated in Book 1.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:165","b":"k:448","type":"parallel_domain","note":"165 says envy is enough for the envious: it brings ruin even when enemies fail (ஒன்னார் வழுக்கியும் கேடீன்பது). 448 says a king without people who rebuke him perishes even with no one to ruin him (கெடுப்பார் இலானும் கெடும்). Both describe ruin that needs no outside enemy, once in a private vice and once in a ruler's unguarded state.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:166","b":"k:527","type":"develops","note":"527 says crows do not hide food but call the others and eat, and that ஆக்கம் belongs only to those of that nature (அன்ன நீரார்க்கே). 166 gives the opposite disposition and result: one who grudges giving leaves his சுற்றம் without food or clothing. Both tie a household's kin and its prosperity to the attitude taken toward sharing.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:166","b":"k:1079","type":"same_image","note":"Both verses use the phrase உடுப்பதூஉம் உண்பதூஉம். In 1079 the base man sees others' clothing and food and finds fault with them; in 166 the kin of one who envies what is given (கொடுப்பது அழுக்கறுப்பான்) perish without clothing or food. The pairing shows what is envied returning as what is lost.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:167","b":"k:617","type":"same_image","note":"167 says the fair goddess (செய்யவள்) grudges the envious man and shows him to her elder sister (தவ்வை). 617 names both sisters by residence: மாமுகடி lives in sloth, and தாமரையினாள் in the effort of the unslothful. The same pair of goddesses marks envy in Book 1 and sloth in Book 2 as conditions that hand a person over from fortune to misfortune.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:169","b":"k:117","type":"qualifies","note":"117 says the world will not set down as ruin (கெடுவாக வையாது) the low state of one who stays in what is right through fairness. 169 lists the ruin of the upright (செவ்வியான் கேடு) as something to be pondered. 117 limits how that கேடு should be counted: the upright person's decline is not regarded as real ruin.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:171","b":"k:604","type":"parallel_domain","note":"171 says that coveting another's goods without fairness makes the family perish and brings faults at once (குடிபொன்றிக் குற்றமும் ஆங்கே தரும்). 604 says of the slothful that the family dies down and faults multiply (குடிமடிந்து குற்றம் பெருகும்). The same double penalty, on the குடி and in குற்றம், is attached to greed in Book 1 and to sloth in Book 2.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:172","b":"k:113","type":"develops","note":"172 says those ashamed of unfairness (நடுவன்மை நாணுபவர்) will not do blameworthy things out of desire for the gain to be had (படுபயன் வெஃகி). 113 gives the instruction that goes with it: gain that comes from leaving fairness (நடுவிகந்தாம் ஆக்கம்) is to be dropped that very day, even if it brings only good. 172 describes the character and 113 the rule it follows.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:172","b":"k:164","type":"parallel_domain","note":"164 says people do no wrong out of envy because they know the harm that comes of the wrong path (ஏதம் படுபாக்கு அறிந்து). 172 says people do nothing blameworthy out of coveting because they are ashamed of unfairness (நாணுபவர்). The neighbouring chapters use the same sentence shape, ending in செய்யார், with knowledge of consequences as the check in one and shame in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:173","b":"k:754","type":"develops","note":"754 says wealth that comes without wrong and with knowledge of the means (தீதின்றி வந்த பொருள்) yields both அறன் and இன்பம். 173 says the small joy got by coveting is refused by those who want the other joy. Together they show the choice in 173 is not between wealth and joy: wealth rightly got delivers the joy that coveting forfeits.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:174","b":"k:205","type":"agrees","note":"174 says those of faultless sight who have conquered the senses will not covet on the thought 'we have nothing' (இலமென்று வெஃகுதல் செய்யார்). 205 forbids doing evil on the same plea (இலன் என்று தீயவை செய்யற்க) and adds that doing so makes one poor again. Both name poverty as the excuse and reject it; 205 gives the consequence that 174 leaves unstated.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:174","b":"k:656","type":"develops","note":"656 says that even if one must see the mother who bore one go hungry (ஈன்றாள் பசி காண்பான் ஆயினும்), one should not do what the worthy condemn. 174 says the thought of being without does not make the self-controlled covet. 656 pushes the same refusal to its hardest case, where the want is another's and not one's own.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:174","b":"k:1040","type":"same_image","note":"1040 says the good woman called Land laughs on seeing those who sit idle saying 'we have nothing' (இலமென்று அசைஇ இருப்பாரை). 174 has the same words, இலமென்று, as the thought that does not move the self-controlled to covet. The plea of having nothing is refused twice: it excuses neither taking from others nor sitting idle.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:175","b":"k:315","type":"parallel_domain","note":"175 asks what fine and wide understanding comes to (அஃகி அகன்ற அறிவு என்னாம்) if one covets and acts senselessly toward everyone. 315 asks whether anything comes of understanding (அறிவினான் ஆகுவதுண்டோ) if one does not guard against another's pain as one's own. Both test அறிவு by how its owner treats other people, in coveting and in causing hurt.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:175","b":"k:834","type":"agrees","note":"834 says no one is a greater fool than the person who has studied, understood and taught others and yet does not restrain himself (தானடங்கா). 175 says subtle and wide understanding is worth nothing if its owner covets and does வெறிய things. Both hold that learning which does not govern conduct does not count.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:176","b":"k:204","type":"develops","note":"204 says not to plot another's ruin even by a lapse (சூழற்க), because அறம் will plot the ruin of the plotter (அறஞ்சூழும் சூழ்ந்தவன் கேடு). 176 says that one who plots wrongs for wealth (பொல்லாத சூழ) will perish (கெடும்). The shared verb சூழ் places the ruin at the stage of planning, before any act; 204 names the agent of that ruin.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:176","b":"k:285","type":"agrees","note":"285 says a loving nature with அருள் in view (அருள்கருதி) is not found in those who, with goods in view (பொருள்கருதி), watch for another's lapse. 176 says one who stands on the path desiring அருள் (அருள்வெஃகி) perishes if he desires பொருள் (பொருள்வெஃகி) and plots wrongs. Both verses set அருள் and பொருள் as rival objects of one desire and say the second drives out the first.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:177","b":"k:113","type":"qualifies","note":"177 argues from the outcome: gain got by coveting does not turn out well when enjoyed. 113 says gain that comes from leaving fairness is to be given up that very day even if it brings only good (நன்றே தரினும்). 113 removes the dependence on outcome, so the prohibition would stand even where 177's prediction failed.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:177","b":"k:284","type":"same_image","note":"177 says not to want gain that comes by coveting, since at its ripening (விளைவயின்) the fruit is hard to make good. 284 says the love grown strong in stealth yields, at its ripening (விளைவின்கண்), a sorrow that does not die. Coveting in the householder book and theft in the ascetic book are both judged by the harvest image, where the gain looks sound until it ripens.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:177","b":"k:931","type":"parallel_domain","note":"931 opens with the same prohibition, வேண்டற்க: do not want gambling even if you win, because the winnings are like the hook a fish has swallowed. 177 says வேண்டற்க of the gain got by coveting, whose fruit turns out badly. Both warn against a gain that is real at the moment of taking and harmful afterwards.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:178","b":"k:283","type":"develops","note":"283 says gain made by stealth perishes beyond measure even as it seems to grow (ஆவது போலக் கெடும்). 178 says the way to keep wealth from shrinking is not to covet another's goods. 283 gives the negative side of the same claim: what is added by taking another's property is not a lasting addition.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:179","b":"k:167","type":"develops","note":"167 says the fair goddess grudges the envious man and shows him to her elder sister. 179 says திரு goes to those who know virtue and do not covet. The two neighbouring chapters use the goddess in opposite directions: she leaves the one who resents others' goods and comes to the one who does not want them.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:179","b":"k:594","type":"same_image","note":"179 says திரு, knowing the way (திறன் அறிந்து), goes to those who know virtue and do not covet. 594 says ஆக்கம் asks the way (அதர்வினாய்) and goes to the one of unwavering drive. In both, fortune is a traveller that seeks the person out; Book 1 gives non-coveting as what draws it and Book 2 gives energy.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:180","b":"k:692","type":"parallel_domain","note":"692 says that not desiring what kings desire (மன்னர் விழைப விழையாமை) brings lasting prosperity through those kings. 180 says not wanting others' goods yields victory, while coveting without thought yields destruction. The courtier's rule in Book 2 follows the same logic as 180: refraining from what belongs to another is what secures gain.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:181","b":"k:297","type":"parallel_domain","note":"181 holds that not backbiting is still good in a man who otherwise does wrong (அல்ல செயினும் ... இனிது). 297 says that if one keeps to not lying, not doing the other virtues is good (அறம்பிற செய்யாமை செய்யாமை நன்று). Both single out one restraint of speech and weigh it against the whole of the rest of conduct, though 297 goes further and treats it as sufficient.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:182","b":"k:820","type":"develops","note":"182 calls it worse than denying virtue to slander someone when absent and smile at him when present. 820 tells one not to come near those who are friendly in the house and blame you in the assembly (மனைக்கெழீஇ மன்றில் பழிப்பார்). 820 turns the moral verdict of 182 into practical advice about which friendships to refuse.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:183","b":"k:230","type":"parallel_domain","note":"183 ranks death above a life kept up by backbiting and pretence. 230 says nothing is more bitter than death (சாதலின் இன்னாத தில்லை), yet even it is sweet when one cannot give. Each verse measures a fault or lack by setting death beside it and finding death the better; the pairing shows how the book uses that comparison for both a wrong done and a good left undone.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:183","b":"k:967","type":"parallel_domain","note":"183 says that dying yields more of the gain the books of virtue speak of than living by backbiting and falsehood (பொய்த்துயிர் வாழ்தலின் சாதல்). 967 says that rather than live by following those who scorn you (வாழ்தலின்), it is better to be called ruined as you are. Both use the comparative 'than living thus' to say that a kind of life can cost more than its end, once for the slanderer's two faces and once for lost honour.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:184","b":"k:197","type":"parallel_domain","note":"184: though you speak harshly to his face (சொல்லினும்), do not speak (சொல்லற்க) the heedless word behind his back. 197: let the worthy say what lacks நயன் if they must (சொல்லினுஞ் சொல்லுக), it is good that they not say what lacks use. The two neighbouring chapters use the same concessive move, granting a lesser fault of speech in order to isolate the one the chapter is about.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:184","b":"k:795","type":"develops","note":"184 concedes speaking without kindly regard to a person's face (கண்ணின்று கண்ணறச் சொல்லினும்) and forbids only the word said in his absence that does not look to what follows (முன்னின்று பின்நோக்காச் சொல்). 795 goes further and tells one to seek out friends able to speak so that one weeps and to rebuke (அழச்சொல்லி ... இடித்து). What 184 merely tolerates, hard speech face to face, 795 treats as a mark of the friend worth choosing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:185","b":"k:193","type":"parallel_domain","note":"185 says that a man's heart does not really speak virtue is seen from the meanness of his backbiting (புறஞ்சொல்லும் புன்மையாற் காணப்படும்). 193 says that expansive talk of useless things itself declares the speaker to be without நயன் (நயனிலன் என்பது சொல்லும்). In both a kind of speech is treated as evidence about the speaker, whatever he professes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:185","b":"k:959","type":"parallel_domain","note":"185 reads a man's inner lack of virtue from his backbiting. 959 says that as the sprout shows what lies in the soil, the words of the mouth show the family one is born in (காட்டும் குலத்தில் பிறந்தார் வாய்ச்சொல்). Both treat speech as the visible sign of something hidden, the heart in one case and lineage in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:186","b":"k:319","type":"parallel_domain","note":"186 says the one who tells another's blame (பிறன்பழி கூறுவான்) will have his own faults sorted through and told (தன்பழியுள்ளும் திறன்தெரிந்து கூறப்படும்). 319 says that hurt done to others in the forenoon comes back on oneself in the afternoon (பிற்பகல் தாமே வரும்). The same return of the deed appears for speech and for injury; in 186 the return is carried out by other people's tongues.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:186","b":"k:980","type":"develops","note":"186 warns the teller of others' faults that his own will be told. 980 says greatness covers another's weakness while smallness only tells faults (சிறுமைதான் குற்றமே கூறிவிடும்). 186 gives the consequence of the habit and 980 gives its diagnosis: fault-telling is what smallness does.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:187","b":"k:525","type":"agrees","note":"187 says those who do not know how to make friends by words that bring laughter (நகச்சொல்லி நட்பாடல் தேற்றாதவர்) will part even their own kin by words that divide (பகச்சொல்லிக் கேளிர்ப் பிரிப்பர்). 525 says one who can give and speak pleasantly will be surrounded by kin in layers (இன்சொலும் ஆற்றின் ... சுற்றத்தால் சுற்றப்படும்). The two state the same link between speech and keeping one's people, from its negative and positive sides, in the Book of Virtue and the Book of Wealth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:187","b":"k:784","type":"qualifies","note":"187 treats speaking so that others laugh (நகச்சொல்லி) as the way friendship is made. 784 says friendship is not for laughing (நகுதற் பொருட்டன்று நட்டல்) but for going ahead to rebuke when the friend oversteps. 784 limits 187: pleasant talk may begin a friendship, but it is not what the friendship is for.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:188","b":"k:1076","type":"develops","note":"188 describes people whose habit is to broadcast (தூற்றும்) the faults of those near them. 1076 likens the base to a beaten drum because they carry any secret they have heard to others (கேட்ட மறை பிறர்க்கு உய்த்துரைக்கலான்). 1076 places the habit of 188 in the Kural's portrait of the base person.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:188","b":"k:1165","type":"parallel_domain","note":"188 asks what those whose way is to spread even the faults of people close to them (துன்னியார் குற்றமும் தூற்றும்) would do to strangers (என்னைகொல் ஏதிலார் மாட்டு). In 1165 the woman asks what those who can bring grief within friendship (நட்பினுள்) would become in enmity. Both argue from conduct toward intimates to what must follow toward others; in 1165 it is a lover's complaint, not a general teaching.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:189","b":"k:572","type":"same_image","note":"189 speaks of the backbiter as a weight the earth bears (வையம் ... பொறை). 572 says the existence of those without kindly regard is a burden to the earth (நிலக்குப் பொறை). The figure of a person as dead weight on the ground is used in both for someone who fails others in ordinary dealings, by malicious speech in one and by lack of regard in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:189","b":"k:990","type":"same_image","note":"189 wonders that the earth bears the backbiter's weight at all. 990 says that if the worthy decline in worth, the great earth will not bear its load (இருநிலந்தான் தாங்காது மன்னோ பொறை). Together they show the image used at both ends: the earth strains under the bad man, and is able to carry its load only while the worthy stay worthy.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:190","b":"k:930","type":"parallel_domain","note":"190 wishes that people would see their own faults as they see a stranger's. 930 asks whether a drinker, when sober and seeing another man drunk, does not think of his own lapse when he drinks (உள்ளான்கொல் உண்டதன் சோர்வு). 930 is a concrete instance of the failure 190 names: the other man's fault is seen clearly and one's own identical fault is not.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:193","b":"k:959","type":"parallel_domain","note":"193 says that a man's spreading out useless matter in speech itself announces that he is without நயன் (நயனிலன் என்பது சொல்லும்). 959 says the words of the mouth show the family one comes from, as a sprout shows the soil. In both the speech testifies about the speaker; 193 makes the talk itself the subject of the verb 'says', which Parimelazhagar notes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:195","b":"k:957","type":"develops","note":"195 says useless talk costs people of fine nature their distinction (சீர்மை சிறப்பொடு நீங்கும்). 957 says a fault in the well-born shows up high, like the spot on the moon (மதிக்கண் மறுப்போல் உயர்ந்து). 957 supplies a reason for 195: a fault that would pass unnoticed in others is conspicuous in those held high.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:195","b":"k:965","type":"parallel_domain","note":"195 says that if people of fine nature (நீர்மையுடையார்) speak useless things, their distinction and the regard it earned leave them. 965 says even those like a hill will sink if they do a lowering thing the size of a kunri seed. Both hold that standing does not protect the eminent from a small lapse; 195 applies this to idle speech.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:196","b":"k:406","type":"parallel_domain","note":"196 calls the habitual idle talker chaff, a husk with nothing in it. 406 says the unlearned can only be said to exist and are like barren saline land that yields nothing (பயவாக் களரனையர்). Both take a farming image of something that looks like the useful thing and gives no yield, and both turn on பயன், what a person produces.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:197","b":"k:193","type":"qualifies","note":"193 says that useless talk at length shows the speaker to be without நயன், so the two faults go together. 197 separates them and ranks them: speech without நயன் may be let pass in the worthy, speech without use may not. Read together, 197 makes uselessness the graver of the two; Parimelazhagar draws the same ranking from சொல்லினும்.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:197","b":"k:697","type":"parallel_domain","note":"197 lets the worthy say even what lacks நயன் but holds that they should not say what lacks use (பயனில சொல்லாமை நன்று). 697 tells one who serves a king to say what is wanted and never to say what is without use even if asked (வினையில எஞ்ஞான்றும் கேட்பினும் சொல்லாவிடல்); Parimelazhagar glosses வினையில as matters without benefit. The rule against useless speech is kept as firmly at court as in private ethics, and neither verse lets circumstances excuse it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:199","b":"k:258","type":"parallel_domain","note":"199 says what those of stainless sight will not say. 258 says those whose sight has parted from fault (செயிரின் தலைப்பிரிந்த காட்சியார்) will not eat flesh that has parted from life. The book repeatedly states a restraint as what people of clear காட்சி simply do not do; here the same form covers speech and diet.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:201","b":"k:180","type":"same_image","note":"Both end on the phrase 'என்னும் செருக்கு'. In 201 it is 'தீவினை என்னும் செருக்கு', which Parimelazhagar glosses as a delusion (மயக்கம்) and Manakkudavar as an exhilaration (களிப்பு) that the good fear; in 180 it is 'வேண்டாமை என்னும் செருக்கு', the pride of not wanting, which yields victory (விறல்). The same word names a heady state to be dreaded in one verse and a state worth having in the other, depending on what it rests on.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:201","b":"k:244","type":"qualifies","note":"201 makes fear of evil deeds the mark of the excellent. 244 says that for one who sustains living beings and practises அருள் there are no deeds his own life need fear ('தன்னுயிர் அஞ்சும் வினை இல்'). The fear 201 commends is aimed at one's own possible deeds, and 244 names the condition under which nothing of that kind remains to be feared.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:201","b":"k:428","type":"develops","note":"201 says those practised in evil do not fear 'தீவினை என்னும் செருக்கு' while the excellent (விழுமியார்) do. 428 gives the general rule in the chapter on understanding: not fearing what is to be feared (அஞ்சுவது அஞ்சாமை) is folly, and fearing it is 'அறிவார் தொழில்'. Read together, the fear in 201 is a working of knowledge and not timidity; Parimelazhagar on 428 lists sin, blame and ruin among the things to be feared.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:202","b":"k:129","type":"same_image","note":"202 says evil deeds are to be feared more than fire ('தீயினும் அஞ்சப்படும்') because they yield evil. 129 sets fire against the tongue: a wound burned by fire heals, the scar burned by the tongue does not ('ஆறாதே நாவினாற் சுட்ட வடு'). In both, fire is the harm that ends, used to measure a harm that lasts; Parimelazhagar on 202 makes this explicit, saying fire cannot follow one into another time, place or body.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:202","b":"k:896","type":"parallel_domain","note":"202 ranks evil deeds above fire as a thing to fear. 896, in the Book of Wealth, says one burned by fire may still survive ('எரியால் சுடப்படினும் உய்வுண்டாம்') but those who keep wronging the great will not. The same comparison, fire as the survivable danger, is applied once to one's own deeds and once to the anger of the powerful.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:203","b":"k:312","type":"agrees","note":"203 calls it the chief of all understanding to refrain from doing evil even to those who hate one ('செறுவார்க்கும் செய்யா விடல்'). 312 says that not hurting in return even one who has hurt you out of hatred ('கறுத்து இன்னா செய்த அக்கண்ணும்') is the settled stand of the stainless. The same rule is called knowledge (அறிவு) in the householder's chapter and purity (மாசற்றார் கோள்) in the ascetic's.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:203","b":"k:987","type":"develops","note":"203 asks only for restraint: do no evil even to enemies. 987 asks what worth (சால்பு) is good for if it does not do pleasant things even to those who did one harm ('இன்னா செய்தார்க்கும் இனியவே செய்யாக்கால்'). The Book of Wealth's ideal of the சான்றோர் goes one step beyond the householder's rule, from not returning harm to returning good.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:204","b":"k:319","type":"develops","note":"204 says that if one plots another's ruin, virtue plots one's own. 319 states the return in terms of time: harm done to others in the forenoon brings harm to oneself in the afternoon, of itself ('பிற்பகல் தாமே வரும்'). 319's 'தாமே' leaves the return impersonal where 204 gives it an agent, and Parimelazhagar on 204 adds the same point about speed: virtue begins plotting at the very moment the plotter does.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:205","b":"k:656","type":"develops","note":"205 rules out poverty as a ground for evil deeds. 656 takes the hardest case: even if one must see the mother who bore one go hungry ('ஈன்றாள் பசி காண்பான் ஆயினும்'), one should not do what the worthy condemn. Parimelazhagar notes that 656 sets aside a general rule of the dharma texts allowing wrong to feed one's dependants, which shows how far 205's principle is taken.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:205","b":"k:1040","type":"same_image","note":"205 quotes the plea 'இலன் என்று' as an excuse for wrongdoing. 1040 quotes the same plea, 'இலமென்று', as an excuse for sitting idle, and says the good woman called Land laughs at those who make it. The book hears 'I have nothing' twice as a pretext, once for evil and once for sloth, and rejects it both times.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:206","b":"k:320","type":"agrees","note":"206: one who does not want afflicting things to overpower him ('நோய்ப்பால தன்னை அடல் வேண்டாதான்') should not do evil to others. 320: all pains rest on those who caused pain, so those who want freedom from pain cause none ('நோய் செய்யார் நோயின்மை வேண்டுபவர்'). The claim and its form (நோய், வேண்டு-) are nearly the same, stated once for the householder in the chapter on fearing evil deeds and once among the ascetic virtues.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:207","b":"k:208","type":"develops","note":"207 says the enmity of deeds does not leave and follows behind to kill ('வீயாது பின்சென்று அடும்'). 208 gives the image for it: the ruin of evildoers is like a person's shadow that does not leave and stays at the foot ('வீயாது அடி உறைந்தற்று'). The repeated 'வீயாது' ties them, and both Manakkudavar and Parimelazhagar say 208 illustrates what 207 asserted.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:207","b":"k:434","type":"agrees","note":"207 treats one's evil deeds as an enemy ('வினைப்பகை') worse than any outward one. 434, advising the king, says fault itself is the enemy that brings ruin ('குற்றமே அற்றம் தரூஉம் பகை') and so guarding against fault should be one's aim. The two verses, one in the Book of Virtue and one in the Book of Wealth, both move the real enemy from outside to one's own conduct.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:208","b":"k:36","type":"parallel_domain","note":"208: the ruin of those who did evil is like a shadow that never leaves a person. 36: virtue done now is, at the time one perishes, a companion that does not perish ('பொன்றுங்கால் பொன்றாத் துணை'). Both say a person's deeds stay with him; the good deed stays as a companion and the evil deed's ruin as a shadow at his heel.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:208","b":"k:307","type":"parallel_domain","note":"208 uses a simile of something that cannot fail to happen: ruin stays with the evildoer as his shadow stays at his foot. 307 does the same for anger: the ruin of one who treats anger as a thing of worth is like the hand of one who strikes the ground, which cannot miss ('கை பிழையாதற்று'). Both verses take 'கேடு' as their subject and end on a simile of certainty, one for evil deeds and one for anger.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:209","b":"k:843","type":"develops","note":"209 assumes that doing evil is acting against oneself: one who loves himself stays away from it. 843 says the suffering that those without understanding inflict on themselves is hard even for their enemies ('செறுவார்க்கும்') to inflict. 843 describes the person who fails 209's test, and Parimelazhagar's comment there names poverty, blame and sin as what such people bring on themselves.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:210","b":"k:116","type":"agrees","note":"210: know ('அறிக') that one who does not turn aside to do evil has no ruin. 116: if one's heart leaves what is fair and does wrong, let one know ('அறிக') 'I shall perish' ('கெடுவல் யான்'). They state the same law from its two sides with the same imperative, 210 for the one who keeps straight and 116 for the one who leaves the middle.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:210","b":"k:117","type":"qualifies","note":"210 promises that the one who does no evil will not come to ruin. 117 says the world will not count as ruin ('கெடுவாக வையாது') the low state (தாழ்வு) of one who stays in virtue by standing fair. 117 allows that the upright may be brought low and changes what counts as 'கேடு', which limits how literally 210's promise can be taken.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:211","b":"k:221","type":"develops","note":"211 says duty-giving seeks no return (கைம்மாறு). 221, opening the next chapter, defines giving as giving to those who have nothing, and says everything else has the nature of 'குறியெதிர்ப்பை', a measured loan to be returned. 221 turns 211's point into a test: a gift that looks to a return is not giving.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:212","b":"k:613","type":"develops","note":"212 says all the wealth produced by exertion ('தாளாற்றித் தந்த பொருள்') is for doing 'வேளாண்மை', benefaction. 613 says the proud distinction called benefaction ('வேளாண்மை என்னும் செருக்கு') rests in the quality called exertion ('தாளாண்மை'). The same two words are paired from opposite ends: in the Book of Virtue helping others is the purpose of earning, and in the Book of Wealth effort is the basis that makes helping possible.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:212","b":"k:1006","type":"develops","note":"212 says wealth is for benefaction, with 'தக்கார்க்கு' read by Manakkudavar and Kalaignar as the worthy who receive it and by Parimelazhagar as the worthy who own it. 1006 describes the failure of this purpose: one who neither enjoys his great wealth nor gives anything to the worthy ('தக்கார்க்கு ஒன்று ஈதல் இயல்பிலாதான்') is an affliction (ஏதம்) to it. 1006 uses 'தக்கார்க்கு' plainly of recipients, which supports the Manakkudavar reading of 212.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:213","b":"k:222","type":"agrees","note":"213 ranks ஒப்புரவு above anything to be had in the gods' world or this one. 222 says giving is good even if there were no higher world ('மேலுலகம் இல்லெனினும் ஈதலே நன்று'). Both detach the worth of helping others from heaven as a reward: in 213 heaven holds nothing better, and in 222 the good stands even without heaven.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:213","b":"k:363","type":"parallel_domain","note":"213: neither in the world of the gods nor here ('புத்தேள் உலகத்தும் ஈண்டும்') is it easy to find another good like ஒப்புரவு. 363: there is no excellent wealth here like not wanting, and nothing there equals it either ('ஈண்டில்லை ஆண்டும் அஃதொப்பது இல்'). The same two-world formula crowns benevolence in the householder's section and desirelessness in the renouncer's; taken literally the two superlatives compete, and the pairing shows each section naming its own highest good.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:214","b":"k:140","type":"agrees","note":"Parimelazhagar reads 'ஒத்தது' in 214 as the way of the world (உலகநடை): one who knows and follows it lives. 140 says those who have not learned to conduct themselves in accord with the world ('உலகத்தோடு ஒட்ட ஒழுகல்') lack understanding however much they have studied. On that reading both make accord with the world the test of a person, of being alive in 214 and of having understanding in 140; Manakkudavar instead takes ஒத்தது as ஒப்புரவு itself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:217","b":"k:576","type":"same_image","note":"217 makes a tree the figure of a generous man's wealth: a tree that is medicine and does not fail ('மருந்தாகித் தப்பா மரம்'). 576 makes a tree the figure of people who have eyes but no kindly regard: they are like trees joined to the soil ('மண்ணோடு இயைந்த மரத்தனையர்'). The book uses the tree in two ways, for what gives to all who come to it and for what stands unmoved, so the image alone carries no fixed value.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:218","b":"k:955","type":"agrees","note":"218: even at a time without means ('இடனில் பருவத்தும்'), those who see what is due do not slacken in ஒப்புரவு. 955: even when what they have to give has shrunk ('வழங்குவது உள்வீழ்ந்தக் கண்ணும்'), an old family does not part from its character. The Book of Virtue credits this constancy to insight into duty ('கடனறி காட்சி'), and the Book of Wealth credits it to lineage ('பழங்குடி').","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:219","b":"k:89","type":"parallel_domain","note":"219 redefines poverty for the benevolent man: it is not lacking goods but being unable to do good with them. 89 redefines it from the other side: poverty in the midst of having ('உடைமையுள் இன்மை') is the folly of not tending guests. In both verses poverty is measured by what one does for others and not by what one holds.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:220","b":"k:780","type":"parallel_domain","note":"220: if ruin comes through ஒப்புரவு, that ruin is worth getting even by selling oneself ('விற்றுக்கோள் தக்கது உடைத்து'). 780: a death at which the eyes of one's lord fill with tears is worth getting even by begging ('இரந்துகோள் தக்கது உடைத்து'). The same closing formula takes something normally shunned, ruin or death, and declares it worth a degrading price, once for the benefactor and once for the soldier.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:220","b":"k:1080","type":"same_image","note":"220 speaks of a man selling himself ('ஒருவன் விற்றுக்கோள்') as a price worth paying for the ruin that benevolence brings. 1080 says the base are fit for one thing: when something befalls them, to sell themselves quickly ('விற்றற்கு உரியர் விரைந்து'). Self-sale is honourable in the first as the cost of giving and contemptible in the second as an escape from one's own trouble.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:222","b":"k:1054","type":"tension","note":"222 says taking is bad even if it were called a good path (நல்லாறு எனினும் கொளல் தீது). 1054 says that asking from those who do not know concealment even in a dream is itself like giving (இரத்தலும் ஈதலே போலும்). They conflict if கொளல் is read, with Parimelazhagar, as accepting alms without exception; on Kalaignar's reading of கொளல் as taking goods from others by whatever fair means, the two address different acts.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:222","b":"k:1061","type":"agrees","note":"222 holds receiving to be bad whatever is said in its favour. 1061 says that not asking is worth a crore even from those dear as one's eyes who give gladly without hiding (கரவாது உவந்தீயும் கண்ணன்னார் கண்ணும் இரவாமை). Both refuse the best case that could be made for receiving: 222 the claim that it is a good path, 1061 the excellence of the giver.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:223","b":"k:205","type":"same_image","note":"Both verses turn on the quoted plea 'I have nothing': 223 has இலன் என்னும் எவ்வம், 205 has இலன் என்று தீயவை செய்யற்க. In 205 the plea is an excuse for doing evil and is answered with the warning that one will have nothing all over again; in 223 it is a plea the well-born do not voice. In both the book refuses to let want serve as a reason.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:223","b":"k:1044","type":"tension","note":"On Parimelazhagar's first reading, 223 says the well-born do not speak to others the beggar's degrading word 'I am poor'. 1044 says that even in the well-born (இற்பிறந்தார் கண்ணேயும்) poverty produces the lapse from which degrading words are born (இளிவந்த சொற்பிறக்கும் சோர்வு). The conflict depends on that reading of 223; if 'இலன்' is the giver's refusal to an asker, as Pope takes it, the two speak of different utterances.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:224","b":"k:90","type":"same_image","note":"In 224 the giver watches the asker's face and is uneasy until it turns glad. In 90 the guest watches the host's face and wilts, like the anicham flower when smelled, if that face changes (முகந்திரிந்து நோக்க). The face is the measure of the exchange in both, read in opposite directions.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:224","b":"k:228","type":"develops","note":"224 says the unease of being asked lasts until the asker's face is glad. 228 names what follows as a pleasure in its own right, ஈத்துவக்கும் இன்பம், which Parimelazhagar explains as the joy the giver has from the receiver's rejoicing. 228 then uses that pleasure as a charge against those who hoard and lose.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:224","b":"k:1057","type":"parallel_domain","note":"224 gives the giver's side: being asked is painful (இன்னாது இரக்கப்படுதல்) until one sees the asker's glad face (இன்முகம்). 1057 gives the asker's side: on seeing those who give without scorn or contempt, the heart rejoices deep within (உள்ளுள் உவப்பது உடைத்து). The two describe one moment from both ends, and the asker's gladness in 1057 is what ends the giver's discomfort in 224.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:226","b":"k:228","type":"same_image","note":"Both verses use the act of putting wealth away (வை). In 226 feeding the destitute is the வைப்புழி, the place of deposit; in 228 the hard-eyed 'keep and lose' what they own (வைத்திழக்கும்). The chapter sets a store that holds beside a store that is lost.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:227","b":"k:85","type":"agrees","note":"227 promises that the one who shares his food will not meet hunger. 85 asks whether the field of one who feeds guests and eats what is left even needs sowing (வித்தும் இடல் வேண்டும் கொல்லோ). Both state as a rule that feeding others first secures one's own food.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:227","b":"k:1107","type":"same_image","note":"227 praises habitual shared eating (பாத்தூண்). In 1107 the man compares his beloved's embrace to living in one's own home and eating one's own food after sharing it (தமது பாத்து உண்டற்று). The lover's simile takes the householder's sharing of 227 as its standard of pleasure.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:228","b":"k:598","type":"parallel_domain","note":"228 speaks of a pleasure the hoarder never learns, ஈத்துவக்கும் இன்பம். 598, which Parimelazhagar applies to kings, says those without inner drive never reach the proud joy of saying 'we have been generous' (வள்ளியம் என்னும் செருக்கு). The pleasure of giving is missed through hardness in the one verse and through lack of energy in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:228","b":"k:1009","type":"develops","note":"228 says only that the hoarder keeps his goods and loses them. 1009 says how: bright wealth gathered by giving up love, denying oneself and ignoring virtue is taken by others (கொள்வார் பிறர்). The later verse fills in the 'losing' of 228.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:229","b":"k:527","type":"parallel_domain","note":"229 condemns those who eat alone to build up their own wealth. 527 says crows do not hide their food but call and eat (காக்கை கரவா கரைந்துண்ணும்), and that gain belongs only to those of that nature. The rule against eating alone appears in the ethics of giving in 229 and as advice on keeping kin and prosperity in 527.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:230","b":"k:968","type":"parallel_domain","note":"230 holds death sweet for one who can no longer give. 968 asks whether a life of tending the flesh is any medicine once greatness has lost its dignity (பீடழிய வந்த இடத்து). In both a life is not worth keeping once the thing that gave it worth is gone: giving in 230, honour in 968.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:230","b":"k:1041","type":"qualifies","note":"1041 says that the only thing as painful as poverty is poverty itself (இன்மையின் இன்மையே இன்னாதது). 230 opens by saying nothing is more painful than death (சாதலின் இன்னாதது இல்லை), then reverses it for the person who cannot give. Taken together, 230's second half brings it into line with 1041: the want that stops giving is placed above death in bitterness.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"k:231","b":"k:31","type":"same_image","note":"231 ends 'ஊதியம் இல்லை உயிர்க்கு' and 31 ends 'ஆக்கம் எவனோ உயிர்க்கு': both use the figure of profit to a living being. 31 gives the place to virtue in general, which yields distinction and wealth; 231 gives it to renown that comes from giving. The shared formula shows 231 naming one particular form of the gain that 31 states broadly.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:231","b":"k:122","type":"tension","note":"231 says that apart from living with renown through giving there is no profit for a living being (அது அல்லது ஊதியம் இல்லை உயிர்க்கு). 122 says no gain for a living being is greater than restraint (ஆக்கம் அதனினூஉங்கு இல்லை உயிர்க்கு). Read literally the two exclusive claims cannot both hold; the conflict disappears if each is taken as the emphasis its own chapter gives its subject.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:232","b":"k:296","type":"tension","note":"232 makes giving the subject of all praise. 296 says there is no renown like not lying (பொய்யாமை அன்ன புகழில்லை). They conflict if both are read as naming the single greatest source of renown; Parimelazhagar's gloss on 296, that truthfulness is the cause of renown in this life, keeps the claim just as strong.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:232","b":"k:387","type":"parallel_domain","note":"232 says the world's speech settles as praise on the giver. 387 says that for one able to give and protect with sweet words (இன்சொலால் ஈத்தளிக்க வல்லார்க்கு), this world is as his own word would have it. In the chapter on kingship the same giving that earns the world's words in 232 earns its compliance.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:233","b":"k:156","type":"agrees","note":"233 says only renown stands undying. 156 says those who punish have a day's pleasure, while those who forbear have renown பொன்றுந் துணையும், which Pope and Parimelazhagar take as until the world ends. 156 measures a virtue's reward by the lasting quality that 233 attributes to renown, and gives it a source other than giving.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:234","b":"k:966","type":"same_image","note":"234 joins earthly renown (புகழ்) to honour in the gods' world (புத்தேள் உலகு). 966 names the same pair as the two things lost by following those who scorn you: it brings no renown and does not carry one to the gods' land (புகழ் இன்றால் புத்தேள் நாட்டு உய்யாதால்). In both verses renown here and the gods' world there are counted together, as gained in 234 and forfeited in 966.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:235","b":"k:117","type":"agrees","note":"235 says a loss can be an increase. 117 says the world will not set down as ruin (கெடுவாக வையாது உலகம்) the low state of one who stays in virtue by standing fair. Both deny that the decline of a good person's fortunes is a real loss, 235 from the side of renown and 117 from the world's judgement.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:235","b":"k:780","type":"parallel_domain","note":"235 calls rare a death that is really a coming into existence. 780 says that if one can die so that the eyes of those who sustained one fill with tears, that death (சாக்காடு) is worth getting even by begging. The soldier's death in 780 is an instance, in the domain of war, of the death 235 says only the skilled achieve.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:236","b":"k:362","type":"tension","note":"On Parimelazhagar's reading of தோன்றுதல் as birth, 236 treats being born with renown as good and not being born as only the better alternative for those without it. 362 says that if one wants anything it should be not to be born (வேண்டுங்கால் வேண்டும் பிறவாமை), with no exception. The conflict depends on the birth reading; on Kalaignar's reading, where 236 is about entering a field of activity, the two do not meet.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:236","b":"k:972","type":"qualifies","note":"Parimelazhagar reads 236 as: if born human, be born with the qualities that lead to renown. 972 says birth is alike for all living beings (பிறப்பொக்கும் எல்லா உயிர்க்கும்) and distinction differs through the difference in what they do. On the birth reading, 972 limits 236: no one is born with renown, so the 'appearing with renown' has to be made by deeds.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:236","b":"k:1003","type":"same_image","note":"236 says: if you appear, appear with renown, and for those without it not appearing is better than appearing (தோன்றலின் தோன்றாமை நன்று). 1003 says the appearance (தோற்றம்) of men who cling to accumulation and do not want renown (இசை வேண்டா) is a burden to the land. Both judge a person's coming into the world by renown, and 1003 names the misers as a case of those who 'appear' without it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:237","b":"k:1060","type":"parallel_domain","note":"237 tells those without renown to blame themselves and not their scorners. 1060 tells the asker not to be angry at a refusal, since the affliction of his own want is witness enough (நிரப்பிடும்பை தானேயும் சாலும் கரி). In both the grievance against another person is turned back to one's own condition.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:238","b":"k:1004","type":"develops","note":"238 says it is a disgrace to leave no legacy of renown. 1004 asks what the man whom no one wants (நச்சப்படாதவன்) supposes will remain as his எச்சம்; Parimelazhagar explains that he is unwanted because he has never given. 1004 applies 238's standard to the miser and finds nothing left.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:239","b":"k:189","type":"same_image","note":"In 239 the land bears (பொறுத்த) a body without renown and yields less. 189 asks whether it is out of regard for virtue that the earth carries the weight (பொறை) of one who speaks ill of a person behind his back. Both picture the earth as bearing a moral load, and they differ in that 189's earth endures while 239's earth declines.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:239","b":"k:560","type":"parallel_domain","note":"239 says the land's rich yield shrinks (வண்பயன் குன்றும்) under a person without renown. 560 says the yield of cows shrinks (ஆபயன் குன்றும்) if the guardian does not guard. The same phrase ties natural yield to human conduct, a private person's in 239 and the king's in 560.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:239","b":"k:1003","type":"agrees","note":"239 says land that has borne a body without renown (இசையிலா யாக்கை பொறுத்த நிலம்) will decline in its yield. 1003 says the appearance of men who cling to accumulation and do not want renown is a burden to the land (நிலக்குப் பொறை). Both make the person without renown a weight the earth carries; 239 adds the cost to the earth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:240","b":"k:970","type":"develops","note":"240 says that to live without renown is not to live. 970 says the world worships and praises the radiance of those with honour who will not live when disgrace comes (இளிவரின் வாழாத மானம் உடையார்). 970 shows a person acting on 240's equation, and the result is the renown 240 demands.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:240","b":"k:1033","type":"same_image","note":"240 uses the formula வாழ்வாரே வாழ்வார் for those who live without blame. 1033 uses the same formula for those who live by eating what they plough (உழுதுண்டு வாழ்வாரே வாழ்வார்), with all others following and bowing for their food. The phrase 'they alone live' is awarded on two different grounds, a good name in 240 and self-reliance in 1033.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:251","b":"k:72","type":"same_image","note":"72 says the loveless keep everything for themselves, while the loving belong to others even in their bones (என்பும் உரியர் பிறர்க்கு). 251 describes the reverse movement: another's body (பிறிது ஊன்) taken to build one's own. Read together, the body is the measure in both: love gives it over, and the eater of flesh takes another's.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:251","b":"k:315","type":"develops","note":"315 asks what understanding has achieved if one does not guard another's pain as one's own (பிறிதின்நோய் தந்நோய்போல்). 251 sets the same pair, self and other (தன் ஊன் / பிறிது ஊன்), the wrong way round: the other's flesh serves one's own. 315 gives the general test that 251's rhetorical question about அருள் applies to eating.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:251","b":"k:968","type":"same_image","note":"251 asks how one who eats another's flesh to enlarge his own (தன்னூன் பெருக்கற்கு) can exercise அருள்; 968 asks whether a life that nurses the flesh (ஊன் ஓம்பும் வாழ்க்கை) is any medicine once dignity is lost. Both use ஊன், one's own flesh, for a body tended at the cost of something higher: அருள் in one verse, honour in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:252","b":"k:247","type":"same_image","note":"Both verses explain அருள் by putting it beside பொருள்: 247 says that world is not for those without அருள் just as this world is not for those without wealth, and 252 says holding அருள் (அருளாட்சி) is not for flesh-eaters just as holding wealth (பொருளாட்சி) is not for those who do not guard it. 252 takes the comparison one step on: அருள், like wealth, is something that can be lost through carelessness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:252","b":"k:376","type":"tension","note":"252 takes it as given that holding wealth is not for those who do not look after it (பொருளாட்சி போற்றாதார்க்கு இல்லை). 376 says what is not one's portion will not stay however anxiously it is guarded, and what is one's own will not leave even if carried out and poured away (பரியினும் ஆகாவாம் ... சொரியினும் போகா தம). The conflict holds if ஆட்சி is read as possession, as Manakkudavar and Pope read it; Parimelazhagar reads it as getting the benefit of wealth, which softens it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:253","b":"k:285","type":"parallel_domain","note":"253 says the mind of one who has eaten a creature's body with relish does not turn to good (நன்னூக்காது), like the heart of one holding a weapon. 285 says a loving nature with அருள் in view (அருள்கருதி) is not found in those who, with goods in view (பொருள்கருதி), watch for another's lapse. In both, what the mind is fixed on, flesh or another's property, leaves no room for அருள்; Parimelazhagar glosses 253 the same way, as a mind that looks only to the meat.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:254","b":"k:246","type":"same_image","note":"Both verses use பொருள் in an unusual, moral sense alongside அருள். 246 says those who leave அருள் and do what should not be done have left பொருள் and forgotten (பொருள்நீங்கிப் பொச்சாந்தார்); 254 calls eating flesh பொருளல்லது. Parimelazhagar takes பொருள் in 254 as virtue (so that பொருளல்லது is sin) and in 246 as the lasting good one should have done.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:255","b":"k:326","type":"same_image","note":"326 says Death, the eater of life (உயிருண்ணுங் கூற்று), does not advance on the days of one who lives by not killing. 255 gives hell a mouth (அண்ணாத்தல்), which on Parimelazhagar's reading has swallowed the eater of flesh and will not open again. In both the language of eating is turned on the human: the one who spares life is not eaten, and the one who eats is.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:255","b":"k:835","type":"same_image","note":"255 says that if one eats flesh, அளறு (the mire, hell) will not open its mouth (அண்ணாத்தல் செய்யாது), which Parimelazhagar explains as not opening to let him out again. 835 says a fool can in one birth make the அளறு he will enter and sink in for seven (எழுமையும் தான் புக்கழுந்தும் அளறு). Both use the same rare word for a hell one makes for oneself and does not get out of.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:256","b":"k:1059","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1059 asks what distinction givers could have if there were none who wished to ask and receive (இரந்துகோள் மேவார் இலாஅக் கடை). 256 says none would bring flesh to sell if the world did not kill for eating. Both reason from a counterfactual about one party to the existence of the other: askers make givers possible, and eaters make sellers of flesh possible.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:257","b":"k:575","type":"same_image","note":"575 says that without kindly regard the eye is perceived as a wound (புண்ணென்று உணரப்படும்); 257 says flesh would not be eaten if people perceived it as another creature's wound (புண்ணது உணர்வார்). Both use புண் with உணர் to strip something of its usual standing, and both are about compassion: the eye without it is a sore, and food seen with it is a sore.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:258","b":"k:174","type":"parallel_domain","note":"174 says those of faultless vision who have conquered the senses (புலம்வென்ற புன்மையில் காட்சியவர்) will not covet, even thinking 'we have nothing'. 258 says those whose vision is free of fault do not eat flesh. Both attach an abstention that costs something, from another's goods or from meat, to காட்சி, and 174 adds the mastery of the senses that 258 leaves unstated.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:259","b":"k:295","type":"parallel_domain","note":"295 says one who speaks truth in accord with his heart is above those who practise austerity together with giving (தவத்தொடு தானஞ்செய்வாரின் தலை). 259 says not killing one creature and eating it is better than a thousand sacrifices. Both rank a plain restraint, not lying and not killing, above the religious practices that carry the most prestige.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:260","b":"k:326","type":"agrees","note":"326 says Death, the eater of life, does not advance on the days of one who lives by not killing; 260 says all living beings fold their hands (கைகூப்பி) and worship the one who does not kill and refuses flesh. Both state what the non-killer receives in return, and both put it as a response of life itself: living beings honour him, and the taker of life leaves him alone.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:261","b":"k:157","type":"parallel_domain","note":"157 says that though others do you wrong, it is good to feel the pain and not do what is contrary to virtue (நோநொந்து அறனல்ல செய்யாமை). 261 defines தவம் by the same two parts: bearing pain, and causing none to living beings. The chapter on forbearance asks of the householder, in dealings with those who wrong him, what 261 gives as the whole form of austerity.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:262","b":"k:273","type":"develops","note":"262 says தவம் comes to those who already have தவம், and that for those without it taking it up is futile; Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar both read this as merit from earlier births. 273 pictures a person without strength (வலியில் நிலைமையான்) who takes on the form of the strong: a cow grazing in a tiger's skin. 273 shows what the futile undertaking of 262 looks like in practice. (The edition's text of 262 lacks the word அவம் at the start of the second line.)","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:262","b":"k:373","type":"parallel_domain","note":"373 says that however many subtle books one studies, the understanding that is truly one's own still prevails (தன் உண்மையறிவே மிகும்). 262 says austerity too succeeds only for those who already have it. Both hold that a discipline taken up now, study or austerity, yields only according to what the person brings to it; Parimelazhagar refers both to what earlier births have fixed.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:262","b":"k:620","type":"tension","note":"262 says that for those without தவம், undertaking it is futile. 620 says those who toil on without flagging or delay will see the back even of fate (ஊழையும் உப்பக்கம் காண்பர்). If the தவம் one must already have is merit from former births, as Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar read it, then 262 makes present effort useless without a prior endowment, which is what 620 denies. On Kalaignar's reading, where it means present firmness and self-control, the conflict disappears.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:263","b":"k:42","type":"develops","note":"42 names the householder as the support of those who have renounced (துறந்தார்க்கும் ... இல்வாழ்வான் என்பான் துணை), along with the destitute and the dead. 263 asks, with a wry கொல், whether the rest have forgotten தவம் out of a wish to provide for renouncers (துறந்தார்க்குத் துப்புரவு வேண்டி). It turns the duty stated in 42 into an explanation, or an excuse, for why householders do no austerity themselves.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:263","b":"k:48","type":"qualifies","note":"263 speaks of the others, householders on Parimelazhagar's reading, as having forgotten தவம் while they provide for renouncers. 48 says a household life that keeps others on their way and does not slip from virtue has more endurance than those who do penance (நோற்பாரின் நோன்மை உடைத்து); Parimelazhagar reads 'keeps on their way' as supporting ascetics. On 48's account the provider has not forgotten austerity: his life already contains more of it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:263","b":"k:1036","type":"agrees","note":"1036 says that if ploughmen fold their hands, even those who say 'we have given up desire' (விட்டேம் என்பார்) cannot keep their state. 263 assumes the same dependence: renouncers need others to supply their needs (துப்புரவு). Both verses note that renunciation rests on people who have not renounced.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:264","b":"k:29","type":"agrees","note":"29 says the anger of those who stand on the hill of good qualities cannot be withstood even for a moment (கணமேயும் காத்தல் அரிது), on Parimelazhagar's and Pope's reading; Kalaignar takes it to mean their anger does not last a moment. On the first reading it agrees with 264 that renouncers hold a power to ruin those who oppose them, though 264 makes it a matter of intention (எண்ணின்) and 29 of anger.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:264","b":"k:261","type":"tension","note":"261 says the form of தவம் is just this: bearing one's own pain and doing no harm to living beings (உயிர்க்குறுகண் செய்யாமை அற்றே). 264 says crushing foes (ஒன்னார்த் தெறல்) and raising friends come through தவம், if one so intends. Read plainly, austerity defined by harmlessness is credited three verses later with the power to destroy. Parimelazhagar eases this by taking the foes as enemies of virtue and by reading எண்ணின் as implying that ascetics do not normally think of such things.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:264","b":"k:899","type":"agrees","note":"899 says that if those of exalted resolve flare up (ஏந்திய கொள்கையார் சீறின்), even the king breaks midway and loses his rule. Parimelazhagar reads them as people of high vows, and Pope likewise. This is the power of 264, ஒன்னார்த் தெறல் through austerity, seen from the side of the king who must not provoke it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:265","b":"k:362","type":"qualifies","note":"265 says austerity is practised because through it whatever is wanted is obtained as wanted. 362 says that if one must want, one should want not to be born, and that this comes by wanting not to want (வேண்டாமை வேண்ட வரும்). 362 narrows what the வேண்டிய of 265 should be; Parimelazhagar reads 265 itself as referring to results in the next life and release, while Manakkudavar takes it to include enjoyments.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:265","b":"k:666","type":"parallel_domain","note":"The two verses share a formula: வேண்டிய வேண்டியாங்கு எய்தலால் (265), what is wanted is obtained as it is wanted, and எண்ணிய எண்ணியாங்கு எய்துப (666), what is thought of is obtained as it was thought. 265 gives this as the reason austerity is striven for; 666 makes it depend on the planner's firmness (திண்ணியர் ஆகப் பெறின்). The promise made for austerity in the book of virtue is made for resolute action in the book of statecraft.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:266","b":"k:348","type":"agrees","note":"348 divides people the same way: those who have thoroughly renounced have reached the head, and the rest, confused, are caught in the net (மயங்கி வலைப்பட்டார் மற்றையவர்). 266 says those who do தவம் do their own work and the rest act in vain, caught in desire (ஆசையுட் பட்டு). Both use பட்டு / பட்டார், being caught, for everyone outside the ascetic's path; Parimelazhagar supplies 'the net of desire' in his gloss on 266.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:267","b":"k:308","type":"same_image","note":"308 says that even if harm is done to you like the touch of massed flames (இணர்எரி தோய்வன்ன), not being angry is good if one can manage it. 267 says pain that burns (துன்பஞ் சுடச்சுட) makes those who can endure it shine like gold in fire. Both picture suffering as fire applied to a person; 308 asks only that it be borne without anger, and 267 says what the bearing produces.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:267","b":"k:630","type":"parallel_domain","note":"267 says that for those able to endure, pain burning them makes them shine (ஒளிவிடும்), as gold shines the more it is heated. 630 says that if one takes what hurts as pleasure (இன்னாமை இன்பம் எனக்கொளின்), there comes a distinction even one's enemies admire. The ascetic's gain from pain in the first book has a worldly counterpart in the second: in both, pain borne in the right way adds to the person.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:269","b":"k:620","type":"parallel_domain","note":"620 says those who toil on without flagging will see the back even of fate (ஊழையும் உப்பக்கம் காண்பர்); 269 says even getting past Death (கூற்றம் குதித்தலும்) is within reach of those with the power of austerity. Each uses the particle உம், 'even', to name the one thing thought unbeatable and then says sustained effort beats it: labour in the book of statecraft, austerity in the book of virtue. (The edition's text of 269 ends with a stray letter, தலைப்பட்டவர்க்குல்.)","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:270","b":"k:616","type":"parallel_domain","note":"616 says effort creates prosperity and the absence of effort brings in want (முயற்றின்மை இன்மை புகுத்தி விடும்). 270 says the have-nots (இலர்) are many because few practise austerity (நோற்பார் சிலர்). Both trace poverty to something people fail to do, but they name different things: present exertion in the chapter on labour, and austerity here, which the commentators take to bear fruit across births.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:270","b":"k:842","type":"agrees","note":"842 says that when a person without understanding gives gladly, the cause is nothing other than the receiver's தவம் (பெறுவான் தவம்). 270 says the have-nots are many because those who practise austerity are few. Both explain who gets and who lacks by austerity, and 842 shows the idea used in passing in the second book, as an accepted explanation.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:271","b":"k:293","type":"develops","note":"271 says the five elements laugh within (பூதங்கள் ஐந்தும் அகத்தே நகும்) at the concealed conduct of a deceitful mind; 293 says that after a lie about what one's own heart knows, that heart itself burns one (தன்நெஞ்சே தன்னைச் சுடும்). Both place the witness to a hidden fault inside the person; 293 moves it from the body's elements, which only laugh, to the heart, which punishes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:271","b":"k:1040","type":"same_image","note":"In 271 the five elements laugh within at a hypocrite's hidden conduct; in 1040 the good woman called Land laughs (நிலமென்னும் நல்லாள் நகும்) at those who sit idle saying 'we have nothing'. In both a part of the material world is the one that laughs at a human pretence, and Parimelazhagar glosses the laugh in 1040 too as inward (தன்னுள்ளே).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:272","b":"k:34","type":"develops","note":"34 says being spotless in mind is the whole of virtue and the rest is noisy show (ஆகுல நீர பிற); 272 says a sky-high appearance (வானுயர் தோற்றம்) does nothing when the heart is in known fault. 272 applies the general rule of 34 to the specific case of the ascetic's appearance.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:273","b":"k:24","type":"develops","note":"24 praises the one who guards the five senses with the goad called firmness (உரனென்னும் தோட்டியான்); 273 likens one of strengthless nature (வலியில் நிலைமையான்) who takes the form of the strong to a cow grazing in a tiger's skin. Parimelazhagar reads the missing strength in 273 as the power to bring the mind under control, which is what 24 names as the mark of the real renouncer, so 273 shows the outward form of 24 without its substance.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:274","b":"k:261","type":"develops","note":"261 gives the form of தவம் as bearing one's own pain and doing no harm to living beings (உயிர்க்குறுகண் செய்யாமை); 274 speaks of doing 'what is not it' (அல்லவை) under cover of தவம் and compares this to a hunter snaring birds. Read together, the hunter simile shows the hypocrite doing the very thing, harm to creatures, whose absence 261 makes the definition of austerity.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:274","b":"k:828","type":"parallel_domain","note":"274 has the hunter hidden in the bush as the image of evil done under cover of austerity; 828 says a weapon hides even in an enemy's hands folded in worship (தொழுதகை யுள்ளும் படையொடுங்கும்), and likewise in his tears. In both a gesture of piety is the cover for harm, once in ascetic life and once in false friendship.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:275","b":"k:655","type":"same_image","note":"275 says the concealed conduct of those who say 'we are free of attachment' brings many harms with the cry எற்றெற்று; 655 says do not do what will make you grieve saying எற்று ('what is this I have done'). The same exclamation of regret marks the test in both, in the renouncer's conduct and in the minister's action; Manakkudavar, however, takes the cry in 275 as other people's, not the doer's.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:276","b":"k:42","type":"develops","note":"42 makes the householder the support of those who have renounced (துறந்தார்க்கும் ... துணை); 276 says none are more hard-eyed than those who, unrenounced at heart, live by deceiving as if they had renounced (துறந்தார்போல் வஞ்சித்து வாழ்வார்). Parimelazhagar takes the deceived to be the givers, so the pairing shows what the false renouncer exploits: the duty of support that 42 lays on the householder.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:276","b":"k:1198","type":"same_image","note":"276 ends 'வாழ்வாரின் வன்கணார் இல்': none are harder than those who live by posing as renouncers. 1198, spoken by the woman in separation, ends with the same words: none in the world are harder than those who go on living without a kind word from the one they love. The shared formula uses வன்கண் in two senses, the cruelty of the deceiver in 276 and the grim endurance of the one left behind in 1198.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:277","b":"k:706","type":"qualifies","note":"706 says the face shows what is strongest in the heart, as crystal shows what is next to it (நெஞ்சம் கடுத்தது காட்டும் முகம்); 277 says there are people red outside and black inside. 277 limits 706: the face may betray a passing feeling to a skilled reader, but a settled outward appearance can be the opposite of the inside.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:277","b":"k:824","type":"parallel_domain","note":"277 says the world has people who look outwardly like the red kunri seed but are inwardly black like its tip (புறம் ... அகம்); 824 says to fear deceivers who smile sweetly with the face and are bitter within (முகத்தின் இனிய ... அகத்தின்னா வஞ்சர்). The same outside/inside split is described for the false ascetic and for the false friend.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:277","b":"k:965","type":"same_image","note":"277 uses the kunri seed for its two colours, red body and black tip; 965 uses it for its size, saying even those like a hill sink if they do a lowering thing as small as a kunri (குன்றி அனைய). The same seed serves both verses as a figure for a small dark flaw in something that otherwise looks fine, by colour in one and by measure in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:279","b":"k:505","type":"agrees","note":"279 says the straight arrow is cruel and the bent lute is upright, so take people by the nature of their deeds (வினைபடு பாலால் கொளல்); 505 says one's own deeds are the touchstone of greatness and smallness (கருமமே கட்டளைக் கல்). The same rule appears as a test for ascetics in the book of virtue and as a test for choosing officers in the book of statecraft.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:279","b":"k:667","type":"agrees","note":"279 warns against reading uprightness from shape, with the arrow and lute as examples; 667 says do not scorn on seeing someone's form (உருவுகண்டு எள்ளாமை), because the great chariot has its small linchpin. One verse guards against being impressed by form and the other against despising it, and both replace form with function.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:281","b":"k:178","type":"parallel_domain","note":"281 tells one who wants not to be scorned to guard his heart from stealing anything at all (கள்ளாமை காக்க தன் நெஞ்சு); 178 says that what keeps one's wealth from shrinking is not coveting the goods in another's hand (வெஃகாமை ... பிறன்கைப் பொருள்). Each gives restraint toward others' property as the means to something one wants for oneself, standing in 281 and one's own wealth in 178; Parimelazhagar reads 281 differently, as about one esteemed for not despising release.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:282","b":"k:317","type":"agrees","note":"282 says even thinking in the mind (உள்ளத்தால் உள்ளலும்) of taking another's goods by stealth is wrong; 317 says the highest thing is not to do, with the mind (மனத்தானாம்), anything ill to anyone at any time in any degree. Both place the fault at the level of intention before any act, for theft in one and for harm in general in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:283","b":"k:660","type":"agrees","note":"283 says gain made by stealth perishes beyond measure while seeming to grow (ஆவது போலக் கெடும்); 660 says making wealth by deceit (சலத்தால் பொருள்செய்து) and feeling secure in it is like keeping water in an unfired clay pot. The same claim, that wrongly got wealth looks held and is being lost, is made to the renouncer in one book and to the minister in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:284","b":"k:368","type":"develops","note":"368 says those without craving have no sorrow, and where craving exists sorrow comes on and on without fail (தவாஅது மேன்மேல் வரும்); 284 says desire hardened in stealing (கன்றிய காதல்) yields sorrow that does not die (வீயா விழுமம்). 284 is the general law of 368 applied to one desire, with the same stress on sorrow that does not end.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:284","b":"k:1092","type":"same_image","note":"284 speaks of காதல் grown in களவு and means desire for stealing, which ends in undying sorrow; in 1092 the man says the small look her eyes steal (கண் களவு கொள்ளும் சிறுநோக்கம்) is more than half of love's pleasure. The same word களவு is condemned as theft of goods in the book of virtue and prized as the stolen glance in the book of love, where it is a lover's speech and not a teaching about theft.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:285","b":"k:251","type":"parallel_domain","note":"285 says அருள் with love is not found in one who watches to take another's goods; 251 asks how one who eats another creature's flesh to swell his own can hold to அருள் (எங்ஙனம் ஆளும் அருள்). Both test a claim to அருள் against a practice of taking from another for oneself, property in one case and the body in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:285","b":"k:757","type":"qualifies","note":"285 opposes having அருள் in view to having பொருள் in view; 757 says the child அருள், born of அன்பு, is reared by the wealthy foster-mother பொருள். 757 limits how far 285 can be taken: the opposition holds for goods taken from others by watching for their lapses, not for wealth as such, which 757 makes the support of the same அருள் and அன்பு that 285 names.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:286","b":"k:474","type":"parallel_domain","note":"286 says those with hardened desire for stealing cannot stand and walk within அளவு (அளவின்கண் நின்றொழுகல் ஆற்றார்); 474 says the one who does not live in accord, does not know his measure (அளவறியான்) and admires himself perishes quickly. On Kalaignar's reading of அளவு in 286 as a limit one lives within, the two apply one idea, conduct kept inside a known measure, to the renouncer and to the king; Parimelazhagar instead takes அளவு in 286 as the reasoned measuring of the soul and its ends, on which reading the link is only verbal.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:287","b":"k:175","type":"agrees","note":"287 calls stealing a dark knowledge (களவென்னும் காரறி வாண்மை) that is not found in those set on the strength called அளவு; 175 asks what a fine and wide understanding (அஃகி அகன்ற அறிவு) comes to if, coveting, one acts senselessly toward everyone. Both deny that cleverness in the service of taking others' goods counts as real understanding.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:287","b":"k:422","type":"develops","note":"422 defines understanding as what does not let the mind go wherever it has gone, but turns it from evil toward good (தீதொரீஇ நன்றின்பால் உய்ப்பது அறிவு); 287 grants stealing the name அறிவு only with the qualifier கார், dark. By 422's definition the thief's skill fails as அறிவு, which explains why 287 marks it as darkened.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:288","b":"k:34","type":"develops","note":"34 locates virtue in the mind being without stain; 288 says virtue stands in the heart of those who know அளவு just as concealment stands in the heart of those who know stealing (நெஞ்சத்து அறம் ... நெஞ்சில் கரவு). 288 adds to 34 that the heart is occupied either way: what one practises, measure or theft, decides whether virtue or deceit takes up residence there.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:289","b":"k:479","type":"parallel_domain","note":"289 says those who know nothing but stealing do what is beyond measure and perish right there (அளவல்ல செய்தாங்கே வீவர்); 479 says the life of one who does not live knowing the measure seems to exist, then is not, and perishes without trace. Both make ignoring அளவு the cause of ruin, in the renouncer's conduct and in the management of means; Parimelazhagar reads அளவல்ல in 289 as wrong thoughts and not as excess of spending, so the likeness is of pattern, not of subject.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:290","b":"k:121","type":"parallel_domain","note":"290 gives the two outcomes of stealing and not stealing: loss of உயிர்நிலை, and the world of the gods (புத்தேள் உலகு) not failing; 121 gives the two outcomes of restraint and its lack: a place among the immortals (அமரருள்) and thick darkness. Each closes or opens its chapter with the same two-sided reward and penalty attached to a single virtue.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:290","b":"k:255","type":"same_image","note":"290 says that for those who steal the உயிர்நிலை fails, while for those who do not steal the world of the gods does not fail; 255 says உயிர்நிலை rests in not eating flesh, and that hell will not open its mouth for one who eats it. Both verses hang உயிர்நிலை, the standing of life in the body on Parimelazhagar's gloss, on one abstention and pair it with a fate after death; Manakkudavar reads the word in 290 as release itself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:291","b":"k:292","type":"develops","note":"291 defines truth (வாய்மை) not as reporting what happened but as speech with no harm in it at all (யாதொன்றும் தீமை இலாத சொலல்). 292 draws the consequence: even a falsehood stands in truth's place (பொய்மையும் வாய்மை யிடத்த) if it yields faultless good. Read together, the second verse is the first's definition applied to its hardest case.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:292","b":"k:113","type":"tension","note":"292 makes good results decisive: a falsehood that yields faultless good (நன்மை பயக்கும் எனின்) counts as truth. 113 says gain got by leaving fairness is to be dropped that very day even if it yields nothing but good (நன்றே தரினும்). The conflict holds only if both are read as answers to whether a good outcome can license a breach of a norm; 113 is about gain and 292 about speech, and 292's புரைதீர்ந்த (free of fault) may already exclude what 113 forbids.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:293","b":"k:129","type":"same_image","note":"In 129 a burn from fire heals inside but the scar burned by the tongue (நாவினாற் சுட்ட வடு) does not; in 293 the liar's own heart burns him (தன்நெஞ்சே தன்னைச் சுடும்). Both use burning for the lasting damage of speech: the harsh word burns the hearer, the false word burns the speaker.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:294","b":"k:119","type":"parallel_domain","note":"294 says one who lives without falsehood in the heart (உள்ளத்தாற் பொய்யாது) is present in the hearts of all the world. 119 says rectitude is speech without bend, provided there is surely no bend within (உட்கோட்டம் இன்மை). In truthfulness and in impartiality alike, straight speech is counted only when the inside matches it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:295","b":"k:48","type":"parallel_domain","note":"295 sets the truthful speaker above those who practise austerity and giving. 48 says a household life that keeps others on their way and does not slip from virtue has more endurance than those who do penance (நோற்பாரின் நோன்மை உடைத்து). In both, the ascetic is the standard of comparison and an ordinary life rightly lived is placed above it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:296","b":"k:297","type":"develops","note":"296 says not lying gives all the virtues (எல்லா அறமுந் தரும்); Parimelazhagar takes எய்யாமை as 'without wearying the body'. 297 then concludes that one who keeps steadily to not lying does well even without doing the other virtues (அறம்பிற செய்யாமை நன்று). The second verse depends on the first: the others can be left undone because this one already supplies them.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:299","b":"k:601","type":"same_image","note":"299 names not lying the one true lamp for the worthy (பொய்யா விளக்கே விளக்கு). 601 calls lineage an undimming lamp (குடியென்னும் குன்றா விளக்கம்) that nevertheless goes out when the grime of sloth covers it. The lamp figure marks what a person's standing depends on, and 601 adds that such a lamp can be put out by neglect.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:301","b":"k:153","type":"agrees","note":"301 counts restraint of anger only where the anger could have its way. 153 says the strength of strengths is bearing with fools (வன்மையுள் வன்மை மடவார்ப் பொறை). Both locate the real test of forbearance where one has the upper hand and nothing outside compels it.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:302","b":"k:861","type":"tension","note":"302 says anger is bad where it cannot take effect, and where it can, nothing is worse (செல்லிடத்தும் இல்அதனின் தீய பிற); Parimelazhagar reads the two places as the stronger and the weaker. 861 advises avoiding opposition to the strong and pressing enmity against the weak (மெலியார்மேல் மேக பகை). They conflict if 302 is taken as a rule for everyone; if the anger chapter is addressed to ascetics, as Parimelazhagar holds, and 861 to a king, they are advice for different people.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:302","b":"k:895","type":"develops","note":"302's first half says anger where it cannot take effect is bad, which Parimelazhagar explains as harm coming back from the stronger party. 895 states that harm at its extreme: those a king of fierce might is angry with (வேந்து செறப்பட்டவர்) will not survive wherever they go. The chapter on not offending the great fills in what 302 leaves as a bare 'bad'.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:303","b":"k:152","type":"develops","note":"152 says always bear with an excess done to you, and that forgetting it is better still (அதனை மறத்தல் அதனினும் நன்று). 303 uses the same verb for the anger itself: forget anger toward anyone (மறத்தல் வெகுளியை யார்மாட்டும்), since evils are born from it. One forgets the offence and the other the anger at it; 303 adds the reason.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:303","b":"k:360","type":"develops","note":"303 says evils are born from anger (தீய பிறத்தல் அதனான் வரும்). 360 names anger (வெகுளி) with desire and confusion as the three whose very names must perish for pain to perish. What 303 says of anger as a source of evils, 360 places in a full account of the roots of suffering.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:304","b":"k:999","type":"develops","note":"304 counts the death of laughter and joy (நகையும் உவகையும்) as the harm that makes anger the worst enemy. 999 says that for those not capable of laughing (நகல்வல்லர் அல்லார்க்கு) the great world lies in darkness even by day. 999 describes the state that 304 says anger produces.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:306","b":"k:526","type":"develops","note":"306 says anger, the fire that kills what it adjoins (சேர்ந்தாரைக் கொல்லி), burns even the safe raft of one's own people (இனமென்னும் ஏமப் புணை). 526 says no one on earth has more people around him than one who gives greatly and does not cherish anger (பேணான் வெகுளி). The two give the loss and the gain of the same fact: kin stay with the one who is free of anger.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:306","b":"k:1164","type":"same_image","note":"306 calls one's people a safe raft (ஏமப் புணை) that anger burns. In 1164 the woman says the sea of love certainly exists and the safe raft to swim it (ஏமப் புணை) does not. The same phrase names what keeps a person afloat; in one verse a passion destroys it and in the other a passion leaves the speaker without one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:308","b":"k:312","type":"develops","note":"308 says that even if someone does harm like the touch of massed flames (இணர்எரி தோய்வன்ன இன்னா செயினும்), not being angry is good, if one can manage it (புணரின்). 312 says that even when someone has done hurt in hatred, not doing hurt in return is the settled stand of the stainless (மறுத்தின்னா செய்யாமை). 308 governs the feeling and 312 the act that would follow from it; 308 alone concedes that it may not be possible.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:308","b":"k:852","type":"parallel_domain","note":"308 asks for no anger even under harm like fire. 852, in the chapter on hostility in the Book of Wealth, says that even if someone does what cannot be borne, meaning division, not doing harm in return is best (இன்னா செய்யாமை தலை). The same advice, with the same 'even if ... செயினும்' form, is given as virtue in one book and as policy in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:309","b":"k:487","type":"tension","note":"309 praises one who does not think of anger in the heart at all (உள்ளத்தால் உள்ளான் வெகுளி). 487 says the bright do not flare up outwardly on the spot but, watching for the time, burn within (காலம்பார்த்து உள்வேர்ப்பர்). They conflict if both describe how a wise person treats anger inwardly, one removing it and the other keeping it for later; the conflict lessens if 309 is for the ascetic, as Parimelazhagar reads it, and 487 for a ruler facing enemies.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:309","b":"k:540","type":"parallel_domain","note":"309 says all one has thought of is gained at once (உள்ளியதெல்லாம் உடனெய்தும்) if one never thinks of anger in the heart. 540 says gaining what one has thought of is easy (உள்ளியது எய்தல் எளிது) if one can keep thinking of it. Both promise the same result in nearly the same words and set a condition on the mind: one removes a thought, the other holds one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:311","b":"k:113","type":"agrees","note":"113 tells one to let go, that very day, of gain that comes from leaving fairness even if it brings only good (நன்றே தரினும் நடுவிகந்தாம் ஆக்கத்தை). 311 refuses wealth that would require hurting others (பிறர்க்கு இன்னா செய்யாமை). Both grant that the gain is real and still rule out the means, one in the householder's ethics and one in the renouncer's.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:311","b":"k:755","type":"parallel_domain","note":"755, in the chapter on making wealth, says wealth that does not come with grace and love (அருளொடும் அன்பொடும் வாராப் பொருளாக்கம்) should be let go and not embraced. 311 makes not hurting others the settled stand of the stainless even at the cost of wealth. The condition that 311 states for the pure is repeated in the Book of Wealth as a rule for acquisition itself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:312","b":"k:852","type":"parallel_domain","note":"852, on hostility, says that even if someone does unbearable things intending division, not doing hurt in return out of hostility is the highest course (இகல்கருதி இன்னாசெய்யாமை தலை). 312 says not hurting in return (மறுத்தின்னா செய்யாமை) is the stand of the stainless. The same rule, in nearly the same words, is carried from personal purity into the politics of faction.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:313","b":"k:207","type":"develops","note":"313 says that doing hurt, even to those who hated you though you did nothing to them (செய்யாமல் செற்றார்க்கும்), brings sorrow with no escape (உய்யா விழுமம்). 207 says people escape any enmity (உய்வர்) but the enmity of their own deeds follows and kills (வினைப்பகை வீயாது பின்சென்று அடும்). The shared root உய் shows the reasoning: an enemy can be survived, one's own retaliation cannot.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:314","b":"k:152","type":"develops","note":"152 says bearing an excess done to you is good and forgetting it is better still (அதனை மறத்தல் அதனினும் நன்று). 314 ends on விடல்: do good and let it go. Parimelazhagar takes this விடல் as forgetting both the hurt and the good done in return, which would make 314 the fuller form of 152's advice; the Tamil itself says only 'do and leave'.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:314","b":"k:158","type":"develops","note":"158 says to defeat those who did wrong out of arrogance by one's own worthiness (தம் தகுதியான் வென்று விடல்), which Parimelazhagar reads as forbearance. 314 goes a step further: do them a kindness so that they feel shame. Both keep the vocabulary of contest (winning, punishing) while replacing retaliation, and 314 adds the active good that 158 leaves unstated.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:317","b":"k:34","type":"develops","note":"34 says being spotless in mind is the whole of virtue (மனத்துக்கண் மாசிலன் ஆதல் அனைத்து அறன்). 317 makes the highest thing the absence of any mind-born ill toward anyone (மனத்தானாம் மாணா செய்யாமை தலை), in a chapter whose first two verses speak of the மாசற்றார். 317 gives 34's general claim a specific content: the unstained mind is one that harbours no hurt.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:318","b":"k:209","type":"parallel_domain","note":"209 says that if a person loves himself (தன்னைத்தான் காதலனாயின்) he should not come near evil deeds, however small. 318 asks why one who knows what hurts his own life does hurt to other lives. Both reason from self-regard, by different routes: 209 from the doer's own interest, 318 from the doer's own knowledge of pain.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:318","b":"k:316","type":"develops","note":"316 gives the rule: what one has perceived as painful, do not do to another (துன்னாமை வேண்டும் பிறன்கண் செயல்). 318 turns it into a question: one who knows what hurts his own life (தன்னுயிர்க்கு இன்னாமை தானறிவான்), why (என்கொலோ) does he hurt other lives? The second verse treats breaking the rule as something needing explanation; Parimelazhagar suggests delusion as the implied answer. (The edition's text of 318 has typos in its first line.)","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:319","b":"k:208","type":"same_image","note":"208 says the ruin of those who do evil is like a shadow that never leaves and rests at the foot (நிழல் ... அடி உறைந்தற்று). 319 puts the same inevitability in time: forenoon and afternoon (முற்பகல், பிற்பகல்). One verse uses space and the other the day's two halves to say that consequence is attached to the deed; the day figure also fits a shadow lengthening after noon, though the text does not say so.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"k:320","b":"k:169","type":"qualifies","note":"320 says all pains fall on those who caused pain (நோயெல்லாம் நோய்செய்தார் மேலவாம்). 169 admits that the crooked-hearted prosper and the upright meet ruin, and says only that this is to be pondered (நினைக்கப் படும்). 169 concedes cases that 320's rule does not show on its face; Manakkudavar's reading of 320, that present pain comes from hurt done earlier, and Parimelazhagar's of 169, that such cases are to be examined for causes, are the way the commentators reconcile them.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:331","b":"k:349","type":"develops","note":"349 says the moment attachment ends births are cut off, and otherwise impermanence is what is seen (மற்று நிலையாமை காணப்படும்). 331 faults the understanding that takes the unlasting for lasting. 349 explains why the error in 331 matters: for one still attached, impermanence is the standing condition, and Parimelazhagar on 331 likewise ties the error to attachment and rebirth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:331","b":"k:351","type":"parallel_domain","note":"331 calls lowest the paltry understanding that takes what does not last for lasting (நில்லாதவற்றை நிலையின என்றுணரும்). 351 says a joyless birth comes from the confusion that takes what is not real for real (பொருளல்லவற்றைப் பொருளென்று உணரும் மருள்). The two verses are built on the same sentence frame; 331 applies the misperception to duration and 351 to reality, and 351 adds the consequence, rebirth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:332","b":"k:112","type":"tension","note":"332 says great wealth (பெருஞ்செல்வம்) arrives and leaves like a theatre crowd, with no exception stated. 112 says the wealth of a person of rectitude does not decay and is a support to his descendants (சிதைவின்றி எச்சத்திற்கு ஏமாப்பு உடைத்து). If 332 is read as a claim about all wealth, 112's durable wealth contradicts it; the conflict eases if 332 is taken, with Parimelazhagar, as wealth that stays only while its cause in good deeds lasts.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:332","b":"k:376","type":"qualifies","note":"376 says what is not one's portion will not stay however it is guarded, and what is one's own will not go even if thrown out (பரியினும் ஆகாவாம் பாலல்ல ... சொரியினும் போகா தம). 332 says wealth comes and goes like a crowd at a show. 376 puts a cause behind 332's picture: coming and going follow one's allotted portion; Parimelazhagar reads 332 the same way, the crowd coming for the dance and leaving when it ends.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:332","b":"k:592","type":"agrees","note":"332 likens the coming of great wealth to a crowd gathering at a dance hall and its going to that crowd dispersing. 592 says possession of goods does not stay and slips away (பொருளுடைமை நில்லாது நீங்கிவிடும்), while possession of inner drive is real possession. The impermanence of wealth taught to the renouncer is restated in the Book of Wealth, where it becomes a reason to value energy and not a reason to withdraw.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:333","b":"k:437","type":"agrees","note":"437 says the wealth of the miser who does not do with it what should be done (செயற்பால செய்யாது) perishes without anything surviving. 333 says wealth does not stay and should be turned at once into what does. The Book of Wealth gives the same instruction negatively: unused wealth is simply lost.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:334","b":"k:326","type":"tension","note":"334 presents each day as a blade cutting life, with no one exempted. 326 says Death, the eater of life, does not advance on the days (வாழ்நாள்மேல் செல்லாது) of one who lives by not killing. Read literally, 326 exempts the non-killer from the daily loss of life that 334 treats as universal; the conflict depends on taking 326 as a promise about lifespan and not as praise of the vow.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:335","b":"k:342","type":"parallel_domain","note":"342 says if you want what renunciation brings, renounce while there is still something (உண்டாகத் துறக்க), which Parimelazhagar takes as 'while there is time'. 335 says to hurry to good deeds before the death rattle. On that reading the next chapter applies the same urgency to renouncing that 335 applies to good deeds.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:336","b":"k:233","type":"qualifies","note":"336 says this world has the 'greatness' that one who was here yesterday is not here today (நெருநல் உளனொருவன் இன்றில்லை). 233 says nothing in the world stands undying except high renown (புகழல்லால் பொன்றாது நிற்பதொன்றில்). 233 agrees with 336 about everything else and names one exception; the renouncer's chapter offers none.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:336","b":"k:235","type":"qualifies","note":"336 says a person who was yesterday is not today (இன்றில்லை). 235 speaks of a death that is really existence (உளதாகும் சாக்காடு), possible only for the skilled, which Parimelazhagar explains as the body of fame surviving. The two verses use the same pair, உள and இல், in opposite directions: 336 for the plain fact of death and 235 for the rare death that leaves something that is.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:338","b":"k:340","type":"same_image","note":"338 pictures the body as a குடம்பை, shell or nest, that the bird leaves empty; 340 pictures it as a lodging (துச்சில்) where life stays as a tenant without a home of its own (புக்கில்). Both make the body a dwelling and life its occupant. 338 shows the departure, and 340 asks why there has to be one; Manakkudavar takes the permanent home to be liberation.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:339","b":"k:356","type":"qualifies","note":"339 presents death and birth as alternating like sleep and waking, which both Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar read as a cycle that keeps turning. 356 says those who have learned here and seen the true thing reach the path of not coming back here (மற்றீண்டு வாரா நெறி). 356 limits 339: the waking that follows each death is the rule only for those who have not seen the real.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:341","b":"k:343","type":"qualifies","note":"341's doubled யாதனின் யாதனின் ... அதனின் அதனின் allows release thing by thing: each thing left stops hurting. 343 demands that everything wanted be let go together (வேண்டிய வெல்லாம் ஒருங்கு). Parimelazhagar notices the difference under 341, calling giving up everything at once the chief way while granting that giving things up one by one still removes the pain each brings.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:341","b":"k:626","type":"parallel_domain","note":"341 says that whatever one has left (யாதனின் நீங்கியான்), from that one has no pain (நோதல் இலன்). 626, in the chapter on not being undone by trouble, asks whether those who never guarded wealth saying 'we have got it' (பெற்றேமென்று ஓம்புதல் தேற்றாதவர்) would grieve saying 'we have lost it' (அற்றேமென்று). The renouncer's rule reappears in the book of wealth as a habit of mind for someone who still owns things.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:341","b":"k:629","type":"develops","note":"341 ties freedom from pain to having left the thing itself (நீங்கியான்). 629 says one who does not crave the pleasure in pleasure (இன்பத்துள் இன்பம் விழையாதான்) does not suffer the pain in pain; Parimelazhagar reads this as someone who goes on experiencing the pleasure without wanting it. The pairing shows the same result claimed for an inward letting go, without the outward leaving.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:342","b":"k:36","type":"parallel_domain","note":"36 tells one to do virtue without saying 'we will see to it then' (அன்றறிவாம் என்னாது). 342's உண்டாகத் துறக்க is read by Parimelazhagar as 'renounce while there is time', i.e. in youth, so the joys last long; Manakkudavar instead reads it as 'if you want gain for your life'. On Parimelazhagar's reading the chapter on renunciation repeats, for renouncing, the earlier rule against postponing virtue.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:342","b":"k:46","type":"tension","note":"46 asks what a person who runs household life by the way of virtue would gain by going to the outside way (புறத்தாற்றில் போஒய்ப் பெறுவது எவன்). 342 says that after renouncing there are many things to be had here, and urges renouncing if one wants them (வேண்டின் ... துறக்க). The conflict depends on reading புறத்தாறு as the ascetic path, as Pope does: on that reading 46's rhetorical 'what is gained?' gets the answer 'much' in 342.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:342","b":"k:369","type":"agrees","note":"342 says that after renouncing, many things come about here (ஈண்டு இயற்பால பல), which Parimelazhagar takes as joys in this life. 369 says joy is unbroken even here (இன்பம் இடையறாது ஈண்டும்) once craving, the sorrow of sorrows, is gone. Both place the reward of letting go in this world (ஈண்டு), not only after death.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:343","b":"k:6","type":"agrees","note":"6 describes God as the one who quenched the five whose gates are the senses (பொறிவாயில் ஐந்தவித்தான்) and promises long life to those who keep to his path. 343 lays the same thing on the renouncer as a duty (அடல்வேண்டும் ஐந்தன் புலத்தை). The renouncer is asked to do what the invocation names as the mark of the Lord.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:343","b":"k:126","type":"develops","note":"126, in the householder's chapter on restraint, speaks of drawing in the five like a tortoise (ஆமைபோல் ஐந்தடக்கல்), which protects through seven births. 343 uses a harder verb for the renouncer: the field of the five must be killed or crushed (அடல்வேண்டும் ஐந்தன் புலத்தை), and every wanted thing dropped at once. Read together they show two degrees of the same discipline, withdrawal that can be reversed and destruction that cannot.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:344","b":"k:263","type":"qualifies","note":"344 makes possessionlessness the nature of austerity. 263 asks whether the others have forgotten austerity out of a wish to provide for those who have renounced (துறந்தார்க்குத் துப்புரவு வேண்டி). The pairing shows that the ascetic's having nothing is sustained by householders who keep possessions in order to give.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:344","b":"k:1036","type":"qualifies","note":"344 says having nothing at all is natural to the vow (இயல்பாகும் நோன்பிற்கு ஒன்றின்மை) and that owning anything brings back confusion. 1036 says that if ploughmen fold their hands, even those who say 'we have given up what is desired' have no standing (விட்டேம் என்பார்க்கும் நிலை இல்லை). The renouncer's owning nothing rests on others who produce food.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:344","b":"k:1050","type":"qualifies","note":"344 treats having nothing (ஒன்றின்மை) as the proper state of the vow. 1050 speaks of those who have nothing to enjoy (துப்புரவில்லார்) and yet do not fully renounce (துவரத் துறவாமை), calling them death to salt and gruel. Lacking things is therefore not the same as renouncing: poverty without the inner giving up is counted as a burden, not as austerity.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:345","b":"k:73","type":"qualifies","note":"73 says the bond of the precious life with the body (ஆருயிர்க்கு என்போடு இயைந்த தொடர்பு) is the way that came to be joined with love, so embodiment is given a purpose. 345 says that for those undertaking to cut off births (பிறப்பறுக்கல் உற்றார்க்கு) the body itself is excess and other ties (தொடர்ப்பாடு) pointless. The value 73 gives the body-bond holds within the life of love; 345 marks where, for the renouncer, it stops holding.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:345","b":"k:627","type":"parallel_domain","note":"345 says even the body is too much for one seeking to end birth. 627, in the book of wealth, says the great do not take trouble as trouble, knowing the body is a target for suffering (இலக்கம் உடம்பு இடும்பைக்கு). Both start from the body as a liability, but 627 uses that view to keep steady in worldly trouble while 345 uses it to argue for giving up every tie.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:346","b":"k:50","type":"develops","note":"50 says one who lives on earth as life should be lived will be placed among the gods who dwell in the sky (வானுறையும் தெய்வத்துள் வைக்கப்படும்). 346 promises the one who ends 'I' and 'mine' a world higher than the gods' (வானோர்க்கு உயர்ந்த உலகம்). Set side by side the two verses give a scale: the good householder reaches the gods, the renouncer goes beyond them.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:346","b":"k:72","type":"parallel_domain","note":"346 says one who cuts off the pride of saying 'I' and 'mine' (யான் எனது என்னும் செருக்கு) enters a world above the gods. 72 says the loveless keep everything for themselves (எல்லாம் தமக்குரியர்) while the loving belong to others even to their bones (என்பும் உரியர் பிறர்க்கு). The householder's love already loosens 'mine', and even the body as 'I', by giving it to others; renunciation does it by cutting the notion itself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:347","b":"k:438","type":"parallel_domain","note":"347 uses பற்று for attachment in general and says its holders are held by griefs. 438, on a ruler's faults, names miserliness as 'the clinging heart' (பற்றுள்ளம் என்னும் இவறன்மை) and says it is not to be counted with any other fault. The same word and the same refusal to let go appear in the domain of wealth, where Parimelazhagar glosses it as not releasing goods when they ought to be released.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:347","b":"k:940","type":"develops","note":"347 says griefs hold and do not let go of those who hold attachment and do not let go (பற்றினைப் பற்றி விடாஅதவர்க்கு). 940 says that as a gambler loves the game more with each loss, the life grows more fond with each suffering it undergoes (துன்பம் உழத்தொறூஉம் காதற்று உயிர்); Parimelazhagar and Pope take the object of that fondness to be the body. 940 supplies what 347 leaves unexplained: suffering does not loosen the grip but tightens it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:348","b":"k:276","type":"qualifies","note":"348 sets those who have fully renounced above everyone else. 276 says none are harder-hearted than those who have not renounced in the heart (நெஞ்சின் துறவார்) and live by deceiving as though they had (துறந்தார்போல் வஞ்சித்து). The praise in 348 therefore applies to renunciation of the heart; the outward form without it is placed among the worst.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:348","b":"k:365","type":"develops","note":"348 gives the prize only to those who renounce thoroughly (தீரத் துறந்தார்). 365 says those called 'freed' are those freed of craving, and the others are not freed in that way (மற்றையார் அற்றாக அற்றது இலர்), whatever else they have given up. 365 specifies what 348's 'thoroughly' requires: the giving up must reach craving, not only things.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:349","b":"k:361","type":"develops","note":"361 says craving is the seed that yields unceasing birth for all beings (தவாஅப் பிறப்பீனும் வித்து). 349 says the moment attachment is cut off, that cuts off birth (பற்றற்ற கண்ணே பிறப்பறுக்கும்). One verse names the cause and the other states what follows when the cause is removed; Parimelazhagar makes the same cause-and-effect point under 349.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:350","b":"k:359","type":"develops","note":"350 says to hold to the hold of the one who has none. 359 says that if one knows the support and lives so that supports perish (சார்புணர்ந்து சார்புகெட ஒழுகின்), the pains due to come will not come. The two are built alike on a repeated word, பற்று and சார்பு, and Parimelazhagar reads the சார்பு that is known as the true reality on which all rests, so 359 states the result of the practice 350 prescribes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:351","b":"k:1002","type":"same_image","note":"351 says an inglorious birth comes from the delusion that takes what is not real for real (மருளானாம் மாணாப் பிறப்பு). 1002 ends with the same words: the delusion of thinking everything comes by wealth, and so hoarding without giving, produces an inglorious birth. The book of wealth applies the metaphysical formula to one concrete error, treating money as the thing that matters.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:352","b":"k:243","type":"parallel_domain","note":"352 has darkness leave and joy arise for those who see truly. 243 says those whose hearts are joined with compassion do not enter the painful world joined with darkness (இருள்சேர்ந்த இன்னா உலகம்). 243's wording supports Parimelazhagar's reading of இருள் in 352 as a dark world of suffering, and shows the book promising escape from it by compassion as well as by knowledge.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:353","b":"k:50","type":"parallel_domain","note":"50 says one who lives on earth (வையத்துள்) as one should is placed among the gods who dwell in the sky. 353 uses the same pair, earth and sky (வையத்தின் வானம்), and says the sky is the nearer of the two for one who is clear of doubt. The householder gets there by right living and the knower by certainty, and in both the sky is brought within reach of someone still on earth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:353","b":"k:702","type":"parallel_domain","note":"353 says that for those who have left doubt and become clear (ஐயத்தின் நீங்கித் தெளிந்தார்க்கு) the sky is nearer than the earth. 702 says one who knows without doubt what is within another (ஐயப்படாஅது அகத்தது உணர்வானை) should be held equal to a god. In both, knowing free of doubt lifts a person toward the divine, once in the knowledge of reality and once in a courtier's reading of minds.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:354","b":"k:2","type":"parallel_domain","note":"2 asks what learning has achieved (கற்றதனால் ஆய பயனென்கொல்) if the learned do not bow at the feet of the one of pure knowledge. 354 says command of the five senses has no benefit (பயமின்றே) for those without perception of the real. Both verses grant an attainment and then deny its fruit when the final step is missing, learning without worship in one and discipline without insight in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:354","b":"k:126","type":"qualifies","note":"126 says that drawing in the five like a tortoise in one birth is a protection for seven. 354 says the attained fivefold awareness yields no fruit without true perception; Parimelazhagar reads ஐயுணர்வு as the mind withdrawn from the senses and held under control, and the missing fruit as release, while Manakkudavar reads it as knowing all that the five senses can know. On Parimelazhagar's reading sense-restraint earns protection across births, as 126 says, but not the end of birth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:355","b":"k:857","type":"parallel_domain","note":"355 makes seeing the true thing the definition of understanding. 857 says those of bitter understanding who hold to hostility do not see the true thing (மெய்ப்பொருள் காணார்); Parimelazhagar takes it there as the substance of the texts on right policy that bring success. The same phrase marks, in the book of wealth, what a mind bent on quarrel cannot perceive.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:356","b":"k:398","type":"develops","note":"398 says the learning acquired in one birth protects through seven (எழுமையும் ஏமாப்புடைத்து). 356 says those who have learned here and seen the true thing (கற்றீண்டு மெய்ப்பொருள் கண்டார்) reach the path of not coming back here (மற்றீண்டு வாரா நெறி). Learning in the book of wealth is an asset carried across births; learning that ends in seeing the real is said to end the births themselves.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:357","b":"k:356","type":"develops","note":"356 speaks of those who learn and see the true thing; 357 adds the mind's own work: if the mind examines and knows with certainty what is (ஓர்த்து ... உள்ளது உணரின் ஒருதலையா), one need not think of birth again. Parimelazhagar reads the two as successive means, hearing instruction in 356 and reasoned examination in 357. The printed text here has உணரான், but Parimelazhagar's and Manakkudavar's glosses read உணரின், which the sense requires.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:358","b":"k:351","type":"develops","note":"351 says a joyless birth comes from the delusion (மருள்) that takes the unreal for real. 358 goes a step further and calls birth itself a folly (பிறப்பென்னும் பேதைமை); Parimelazhagar explains this as naming the effect in place of its cause. The chapter moves from delusion as the cause of birth to birth and delusion spoken of as one thing, removed together by seeing the real.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:358","b":"k:835","type":"parallel_domain","note":"358 speaks of 'the folly called birth' departing when the real is seen. 835, in the chapter on folly in the book of wealth, says a fool can in one birth's doing make the mire he will sink into for seven (எழுமையும் தான்புக் கழுந்தும் அளறு). Both tie பேதைமை to the chain of births, 358 as its root and 835 through the fool's deeds.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:359","b":"k:5","type":"parallel_domain","note":"5 says the two kinds of deed do not attach (சேரா) to those devoted to the Lord's praise. 359 says the pains due to attach will not attach (சார்தரா சார்தரு நோய்) to one who knows the support and lives so that supports perish; Parimelazhagar takes these as the fruits of stored deeds still awaiting experience, and Manakkudavar takes சார்பு itself as the hold of deeds. Devotion in 5 and knowledge with practice in 359 are credited with the same effect on karma.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:359","b":"k:347","type":"develops","note":"347 says griefs hold and do not let go of those who hold on to attachment. 359 gives the other case: for one who knows the support and lives so that supports fall away (சார்புகெட ஒழுகின்), the pains that were due do not come to cling. 347 states the bondage and 359 the way out, in the same language of things that cling.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:360","b":"k:4","type":"agrees","note":"360 says pain perishes when desire, anger and confusion perish down to their names (காமம் வெகுளி மயக்கம் ... நாமம் கெட); Parimelazhagar glosses the three as wanting, aversion and ignorance. 4 describes God as the one without wanting or not-wanting (வேண்டுதல் வேண்டாமை இலான்) and says those who reach his feet have no suffering. The state 360 sets as the goal is the nature 4 gives to the Lord, with the same result, the end of suffering.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:360","b":"k:431","type":"parallel_domain","note":"360 lists desire, anger and confusion as what must perish for pain to end. 431 lists three faults whose absence gives a ruler's prosperity its dignity: arrogance, anger and smallness (செருக்கும் சினமும் சிறுமையும்), which Parimelazhagar glosses as pride, anger and lust. A similar triad, sharing anger and, on that gloss, desire, is the obstacle to release in one book and to good rule in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:364","b":"k:34","type":"develops","note":"34 says being spotless in mind is the whole of virtue (மனத்துக்கண் மாசிலன் ஆதல் அனைத்து அறன்). 364 defines purity as the absence of craving (அவாவின்மை). Taken together, 364 names the stain that 34 leaves unnamed.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:365","b":"k:275","type":"develops","note":"275 warns that the hidden conduct of those who say 'we are free of attachment' (பற்றற்றேம் என்பார்) brings many harms. 365 says who may rightly be called freed (அற்றவர் என்பார்): only those freed of craving. 275 shows the claim made falsely; 365 gives the inner criterion that the claim has to meet.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:366","b":"k:35","type":"develops","note":"35 defines virtue as what proceeds without slipping into four things, one of which is craving (அழுக்காறு அவா வெகுளி இன்னாச்சொல்). 366 says craving is what deceives a person (வஞ்சிப்பது அவா), and that fearing it is itself virtue (அஞ்சுவதோரும் அறனே). 366 takes one item from the list in 35 and gives the reason for singling it out: it works by deceit, so it needs constant watch.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:366","b":"k:428","type":"develops","note":"428 says fearing what should be feared is the work of the knowing (அஞ்சுவது அஞ்சல் அறிவார் தொழில்) and leaves the object open. 366 names an object, craving, and calls the fear of it virtue (அஞ்சுவதோரும் அறனே). The general rule about right fear in the book of statecraft gets a specific content in the renouncer's chapter.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:366","b":"k:1075","type":"same_image","note":"Fear and craving appear together in both verses with opposite roles. In 1075 fear is the whole conduct of the base (அச்சமே கீழ்களது ஆசாரம்), with a little more if there is something they crave (அவாவுண்டேல்); Parimelazhagar takes the fear as fear of punishment. In 366 fear is directed at craving itself and is called virtue. The pairing separates fear that serves craving from fear that guards against it.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:367","b":"k:266","type":"agrees","note":"266 says those who do austerity do their own work, and the others, caught in desire, do what is futile (அவஞ்செய்வார் ஆசையுட் பட்டு). 367 says that if craving is cut off thoroughly, the deed that does not fail (தவாவினை) comes in the way one wishes. They state the same link from opposite sides: desire makes action futile, and its removal makes action unfailing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:367","b":"k:309","type":"parallel_domain","note":"309 says all that one has thought of is reached at once if one does not think of anger in the heart (உள்ளியதெல்லாம் உடனெய்தும்). 367 says the unfailing deed comes as one wishes (தான் வேண்டுமாற்றான் வரும்) once craving is cut. Each verse removes one passion, anger or craving, and promises that what one intends then arrives.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:368","b":"k:341","type":"develops","note":"341 says a person has no pain from each thing he has left (யாதனின் யாதனின் நீங்கியான் நோதல் அதனின் அதனின் இலன்), taking objects one by one. 368 says those without craving have no sorrow at all (அவாஇல்லார்க்கு இல்லாகுந் துன்பம்). 368 moves from the objects given up to the craving behind them, so the relief is complete instead of item by item.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:368","b":"k:347","type":"agrees","note":"347 says sorrows hold and do not let go of those who hold attachment and do not let go (பற்றி விடாஅ இடும்பைகள்). 368 says that where craving exists sorrow keeps coming without fail, one after another (தவாஅது மேன்மேல் வரும்). Both describe suffering as continuing for as long as its cause is kept.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:368","b":"k:628","type":"parallel_domain","note":"628, in the chapter on not being undone by trouble, says one who does not desire pleasure (இன்பம் விழையான்) and calls hardship natural does not come to sorrow (துன்பம் உறுதல் இலன்). 368 says those without craving have no sorrow. The renouncer's doctrine reappears as practical advice for a person at work in the world, where Parimelazhagar relates it to the pains of effort.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:369","b":"k:854","type":"parallel_domain","note":"The two verses share one sentence frame. 369: joy is unbroken if the sorrow of sorrows called craving perishes (அவாவென்னும் துன்பத்துள் துன்பங் கெடின்). 854: it yields the joy of joys if the sorrow of sorrows called hostility perishes (இகலென்னும் துன்பத்துள் துன்பங் கெடின்). The same title, sorrow of sorrows, is given to craving in the book of virtue and to hostility in the book of statecraft, each as the worst thing in its own field.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:370","b":"k:349","type":"agrees","note":"370 says that giving up craving gives, in that very state (அந்நிலையே), a nature that does not shift (பேரா இயற்கை). 349 says the moment attachment ends (பற்றற்ற கண்ணே) it cuts off birth. Both make the result immediate on the cause ending, with no further step between.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:370","b":"k:940","type":"develops","note":"370 says craving has a nature that is never filled (ஆரா இயற்கை அவா). 940 gives a picture of that: as the gambler loves the game more with each loss (இழத்தொறூஉம் காதலிக்கும்), a living being's love grows with each suffering (துன்பம் உழத்தொறூஉம் காதற்று உயிர்); Parimelazhagar takes this as love of the body. 940 shows why satisfying craving cannot end it, which is why 370 says it has to be given up.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:371","b":"k:617","type":"qualifies","note":"617 says the dark goddess of misfortune lives in sloth (மடியுளாள் மாமுகடி) and the lotus goddess lives in the effort of the one without sloth. 371 says sloth (மடி) and its opposite are themselves produced by the fate of loss and the fate of gain. In 617 fortune depends on whether one is slothful; 371 adds that whether one is slothful depends on fate.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:371","b":"k:618","type":"tension","note":"371 says tireless effort arises from a fate of gain and sloth arises from a fate of loss (போகூழால் தோன்றும் மடி). 618 says lacking good fortune (பொறியின்மை) is no one's disgrace, but lacking effort after learning what should be known is a disgrace (ஆள்வினை இன்மை பழி). If sloth itself is produced by fate, as 371 says on a plain reading, the line 618 draws between blameless fortune and blameworthy idleness is hard to hold.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:372","b":"k:429","type":"qualifies","note":"429 says those whose understanding guards against what is coming (எதிரதாக் காக்கும் அறிவினார்) meet no affliction that shakes them. 372 says a fate of loss makes a person foolish (பேதைப் படுக்கும் இழவூழ்); Parimelazhagar adds that this happens even to one who has understanding. The protection 429 promises rests on understanding, and 372 says understanding itself can be taken away by fate.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:372","b":"k:859","type":"agrees","note":"372 says a fate of loss makes a person foolish and a fate of gain, when it arrives, widens understanding (அறிவகற்றும் ஆகலூழ் உற்றக் கடை). 859 says that when gain is coming a person does not look at hostility (இகல்காணான் ஆக்கம் வருங்கால்), and to bring ruin on himself he looks to excel at it. In both, approaching gain or ruin shows first as a change in the person's own judgement.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:373","b":"k:823","type":"parallel_domain","note":"823 says that even after learning many good things it is hard for the unworthy to become good in heart (பலநல்ல கற்றக் கடைத்தும் மனநல்லர் ஆகுதல் மாணார்க்கு அரிது); Parimelazhagar applies this to enemies. 373 says that after much study a person's own understanding still prevails. The same limit on what books can change is stated once for understanding and once for the heart.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:374","b":"k:169","type":"parallel_domain","note":"169 says the prosperity of the crooked-hearted and the ruin of the upright are things to be pondered (நினைக்கப்படும்), and gives no answer; Parimelazhagar says the wise look for the earlier deed behind it. 374 notes a like mismatch between wealth and understanding and gives it as the two-fold nature of the world. One verse separates wealth from goodness and the other separates it from wisdom.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:374","b":"k:408","type":"develops","note":"374 states as a general fact that wealth and clear understanding are separate lots. 408 judges one result of that: wealth lodged with the unlearned is more painful than poverty lodged with the good (கல்லார்கண் பட்ட திரு). 408 takes for granted the mismatch that 374 explains.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:375","b":"k:483","type":"tension","note":"483 asks whether anything is hard for one who acts with the right means and knowing the time (கருவியான் காலம் அறிந்து செயின்). 375 says that in making wealth all that is good turns bad and bad turns good (நல்லவை எல்லாஅந் தீயவாம்); Parimelazhagar takes the good and bad things to be time, place, means and action, reversed by fate. On that reading the favourable conditions 483 relies on do not secure the result, and the two conflict. Kalaignar reads 375 differently, as good and bad deeds turning out contrary to intent.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:376","b":"k:659","type":"qualifies","note":"376 says what is not one's portion will not stay however carefully it is guarded (பரியினும் ஆகாவாம் பாலல்ல), and what is one's own will not leave even if thrown out. 659 says all that is taken with others' tears leaves with tears, and what is of the good kind (நற்பாலவை) pays later even if lost. Both say some goods stay and some go whatever one does; 659 gives a test the person controls, the way the wealth was gained, where 376 gives only fate's allotment.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:377","b":"k:1005","type":"qualifies","note":"1005 says those who neither give nor enjoy have nothing even with crores piled up (அடுக்கிய கோடியுண்டாயினும் இல்), and treats this as the miser's own failing. 377 says even those who have gathered crores can enjoy them only as the apportioner has apportioned (வகுத்தான் வகுத்த வகையல்லால் ... துய்த்தல் அரிது). Both separate owning from enjoying; 377 places part of enjoyment outside the owner's choice, which limits the blame in 1005.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:378","b":"k:344","type":"develops","note":"344 says having nothing is natural to austerity (இயல்பாகும் நோன்பிற்கொன்று இன்மை). 378 looks at those who already have nothing through poverty and says they would renounce, but for what fate makes them undergo. Parimelazhagar makes the connection: they have already lost what a renouncer gives up, and still suffer because their intent differs. Having nothing is the condition of austerity in 344, and 378 shows it is not enough by itself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:379","b":"k:626","type":"agrees","note":"379 asks why those who see things as good when good comes should grieve (அல்லற்படுவது எவன்) when it does not. 626 asks whether those who never clutched at wealth saying 'we have got it' would grieve (அல்லற்படுபவோ) saying 'we have lost it'. Both are rhetorical questions with the same verb and the same symmetry between gain and loss. 626 adds the practical point that grief at loss depends on how one held the gain.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:380","b":"k:619","type":"qualifies","note":"380 says fate is the strongest thing and gets ahead of any plan. 619 grants that a thing may not come about because of fate (தெய்வத்தான் ஆகாதெனினும்), and adds that effort still pays the wage of the body's labour (மெய்வருத்தக் கூலி தரும்). 619 accepts the limit that 380 states and marks out what remains for effort within it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:391","b":"k:823","type":"qualifies","note":"391 treats right conduct as what should follow flawless learning. 823, on false friends, says that even after studying many good things (பலநல்ல கற்றக் கடைத்தும்) it is hard for the unworthy (மாணார்) to become good at heart. It limits 391: study does not by itself produce the standing-by-it, which is why 391 has to command it separately.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:391","b":"k:834","type":"develops","note":"391 asks for two things in order: learn without flaw (கசடறக் கற்க), then stand in keeping with it (நிற்க அதற்குத் தக). 834 names the person who does the first and skips the second: one who has studied, understood and even taught others but is not himself restrained (தானடங்கா) is the greatest of fools. The pair shows that the second half of 391 is not an afterthought: learning without it is classed as folly.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:392","b":"k:445","type":"same_image","note":"392 makes numbers and letters the eyes of the living. 445 says a king goes about with his counsellors as his eyes (சூழ்வார் கண்ணாக) and so must choose them with care. Both treat sight as something acquired from outside oneself, through learning in one case and through chosen advisers in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:393","b":"k:418","type":"parallel_domain","note":"393 denies real eyes to the unlearned. 418 says ears not pierced by listening (கேள்வியால் தோட்கப்படாத செவி) are of a deaf kind even though they hear. The next chapter repeats the pattern for the other sense: an organ counts only when it has been trained by learning or listening.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:394","b":"k:643","type":"develops","note":"394 describes the learned as people whose company others want again after it ends. 643 defines speech as what binds those who heard it and makes even those who did not hear want it (கேளாரும் வேட்ப). 643 gives the means, speech, for the effect 394 describes.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:394","b":"k:839","type":"parallel_domain","note":"394 says the work of the learned is to meet so that others are glad and to part so that others think of them (உள்ளப் பிரிதல்). 839 says, ironically, that friendship with fools is very sweet because parting from them (பிரிவின்கண்) brings no pain at all. Both judge a person by what the parting leaves behind; the fool is the case where it leaves nothing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:395","b":"k:724","type":"develops","note":"395 praises learning in the stance of one who lacks before one who has. 724 tells even the learned to state what they have learned before the learned and take more from those who know more (மிக்காருள் மிக்க கொளல்). The asking stance of 395 is thus not only for beginners; it continues among the learned.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:395","b":"k:1066","type":"qualifies","note":"395 uses the poor standing before the rich (உடையார்முன் இல்லார்போல்) as its figure for the learner, and on Parimelazhagar's reading those who learned standing so are foremost while those who would not are lowest. 1066 says nothing is more degrading to the tongue than asking (இரவின் இளிவந்தது இல்), even asking water for a cow. Read together, the posture the book calls degrading when the object is goods is the one it honours when the object is learning.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:397","b":"k:753","type":"parallel_domain","note":"397 says that for the learned any land is one's land and any town one's town (யாதானும் நாடாம் ஊராம்). 753 says the unfailing lamp called wealth goes to whatever country one has in mind (எண்ணிய தேயத்துச் சென்று) and cuts away the darkness there. Learning and wealth are each credited with making foreign places open to a person.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:399","b":"k:650","type":"qualifies","note":"399 assumes the learned person's learning gives the world joy. 650 says those who cannot unfold what they have learned so that others grasp it (கற்றது உணர விரித்துரையாதார்) are like a cluster of flowers in bloom without scent. It adds the condition 399 leaves unstated: the world's delight depends on being able to convey the learning.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:400","b":"k:241","type":"parallel_domain","note":"400 gives the title of real wealth to learning. 241 gives it to grace (அருட்செல்வம் செல்வத்துள் செல்வம்), with the reason that wealth of goods is found even among the base. The same move appears in the Book of Virtue with a different winner, and 241's reason, who can hold it, differs from 400's, whether it can be lost.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:400","b":"k:411","type":"same_image","note":"400 says learning is the imperishable, excellent wealth and other things are not wealth (மாடல்ல மற்றையவை). 411 says the wealth of the ear is the wealth among wealths and the head of them all (செல்வத்துளெல்லாம் தலை). Adjacent chapters use the same 'true wealth' figure for study and then for listening, without ranking the two against each other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:401","b":"k:728","type":"parallel_domain","note":"401 says holding forth in an assembly without full book-learning (நிரம்பிய நூலின்றிக் கோட்டி கொளல்) is like playing dice without a marked board. 728 says those who have learned many things are still of no use if they cannot speak well and tellingly in a good assembly. Together they give both halves: speech without learning has nothing to stand on, and learning without speech yields nothing.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:402","b":"k:727","type":"parallel_domain","note":"402 compares the unlearned man's desire to speak (சொற்காமுறுதல், as Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar read it) to a woman without breasts desiring womanhood. 727 compares the learning of one who fears the assembly to a bright sword in the hand of a பேடி among enemies. Both use a figure of lacking what one's role requires, and they cover the two opposite failures: wanting to speak without learning, and having learning without the nerve to speak. Kalaignar reads 402 differently, as desiring to hear the unlearned man's words.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:403","b":"k:715","type":"develops","note":"403 says even the unlearned are very good if they can keep from speaking before the learned (கற்றார்முன் சொல்லாதிருக்கப் பெறின்). 715 says the best of all good things is the restraint of not speaking first among elders (முதுவருள் முந்து கிளவாச் செறிவு). 715 turns the rule 403 gives the unlearned into a general virtue of the assembly that binds capable speakers too.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:403","b":"k:729","type":"qualifies","note":"403 counts silence before the learned as the unlearned person's merit. 729 says those who have studied and understood yet fear an assembly of the good are lower than the unlearned (கல்லாதவரின் கடை). Silence is therefore not good in itself: it is right for the one with nothing to say and a failing in the one who has something.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:404","b":"k:423","type":"tension","note":"404 says that however good an unlearned person's brightness (ஒட்பம்) may be, those with understanding do not accept it. 423 says understanding is seeing the true substance of a thing from whoever's mouth it is heard (யார்யார்வாய்க் கேட்பினும்). If 404 means the wise set aside what the unlearned say even when it is good, it conflicts with 423's rule to judge the matter and not the speaker; on Parimelazhagar's reading, that they only decline to credit him with real brightness because an occasional hit is accidental, the conflict is smaller.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:404","b":"k:636","type":"develops","note":"404 says native brightness without learning is not accepted by the wise. 636 says nothing over-subtle can stand before those who have keen native intelligence together with book-learning (மதிநுட்பம் நூலோடு உடையார்). 636 states positively what 404 implies: natural intelligence gains its force when joined to study.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:405","b":"k:717","type":"parallel_domain","note":"405 says an unlearned person's standing collapses once he converses. 717 says the learning of those who have studied and understood shines out (விளங்கும்) among those able to examine words without fault. Conversation with the competent is the test in both, with opposite results for the unlearned and the learned.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:405","b":"k:1046","type":"same_image","note":"405 says the unlearned person's self-regard gives way when he engages and speaks (சொல்லாடச் சோர்வு படும்); Parimelazhagar supplies that the talk is with a learned person. 1046 says that even when the poor speak of good matters well understood, their words go slack (சொற்பொருள் சோர்வு படும்). The same closing phrase marks two ways speech fails: in 405 for want of learning in the speaker, in 1046 for want of standing with the hearers, though the content is sound.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:406","b":"k:572","type":"parallel_domain","note":"406 grants the unlearned existence only and compares them to land that yields nothing (பயவாக் களர்). 572 says the existence of those without kindly regard is a burden to the earth (உண்மை நிலக்குப் பொறை). Both reduce a person lacking a quality to mere existence and express it through land, as barren soil in one and as weight on the ground in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:406","b":"k:730","type":"same_image","note":"406 says the unlearned are no more than 'they exist' (உளரென்னும் மாத்திரையர்) and are like barren saline ground that yields nothing. 730 says those who, fearing the arena, cannot convey what they have learned are, though they exist, equal to those who do not (உளரெனினும் இல்லாரொடு ஒப்பர்). The same bare-existence wording puts the learned who cannot speak in nearly the place of the unlearned.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:407","b":"k:667","type":"agrees","note":"407 says good looks without understanding are worth what a clay doll's are. 667 says not to scorn anyone on seeing their form (உருவுகண்டு எள்ளாமை), since the great chariot has its small linchpin. Both deny that appearance measures a person, 407 for the handsome and empty, 667 for the slight and capable.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:407","b":"k:1020","type":"same_image","note":"407 compares the good looks of one without fine, penetrating understanding to a well-decorated clay doll (மண்மாண் புனைபாவை). 1020 compares the movements of those with no shame within to a wooden puppet (மரப்பாவை) made to seem alive by its string. Both use a doll for a human form missing its inner part, understanding in one and shame in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:408","b":"k:558","type":"parallel_domain","note":"408 says wealth is worse than poverty when it sits with the unlearned (வறுமையின் இன்னாதே). 558 says having is more painful than not having (இன்மையின் இன்னாது உடைமை) under a ruler who does no justice. Both invert the usual ranking of wealth over poverty under a stated condition: the holder's lack of learning in one, the king's injustice in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:408","b":"k:837","type":"develops","note":"408 says wealth lodged with the unlearned (கல்லார்கண் பட்ட திரு) is more painful than poverty lodged with the good. 837 shows what happens when a fool gets great wealth: strangers feast and his own people go hungry. 837 gives one concrete form of the harm; Manakkudavar likewise reads 408 as harm done to others.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:409","b":"k:133","type":"parallel_domain","note":"409 says the unlearned, though born high, do not have the worth (பாடு) of the learned born low. 133 says having conduct is good family, and lapse from it becomes low birth (இழிந்த பிறப்பாய் விடும்). Both make birth give way to something acquired, learning in 409 and conduct in 133.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:410","b":"k:420","type":"develops","note":"410 says others stand to those who have studied the shining books as beasts stand to people (விலங்கொடு மக்கள் அனையர்). 420 calls those who know taste by the mouth and not by the ear மாக்கள், and asks what it matters whether they die or live. The next chapter carries the comparison on, from those who have not studied to those who do not even listen.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:410","b":"k:1012","type":"parallel_domain","note":"410 makes book-learning the difference between people and beasts. 1012 says food, clothing and the rest are no different for any living being, and that the distinction of human beings is having shame (நாணுடைமை மாந்தர் சிறப்பு). The two verses give different answers, learning and shame, to what sets humans apart.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:410","b":"k:1071","type":"parallel_domain","note":"410 says the unlearned differ from the learned as beasts from people, though all are human in form. 1071 says the base look exactly like people (மக்களே போல்வர் கயவர்), a likeness the speaker has seen nowhere else. Both deny full human standing to some who have the human shape, for lack of learning in one and for baseness in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:412","b":"k:420","type":"develops","note":"412 treats listening as food for the ear (செவிக்குணவு) and lets the stomach have a little (சிறிது) only when that food is absent. 420 turns the same ear-and-mouth pairing into a judgment: for those who know taste by the mouth and not by the ear (செவியிற் சுவையுணரா வாயுணர்வின் மாக்கள்), living and dying come to the same. The chapter's ranking of the two appetites starts as a rule for mealtimes and ends as a measure of a life.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:412","b":"k:946","type":"parallel_domain","note":"412 allows the stomach only a little (சிறிது), and Parimelazhagar explains the word by saying heavy eating multiplies disease and desire. 946, in the chapter on medicine, says well-being stays with the one who eats knowing to stop short (இழிவறிந்து உண்பான்) and disease with the heavy eater (கழிபேர் இரையான்). The same restraint at the table is asked for in the name of listening in one verse and in the name of health in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:413","b":"k:702","type":"parallel_domain","note":"413 makes listeners equal to the oblation-fed (ஆன்றாரோடொப்பர்). 702 says one who knows without doubt what is inside another (அகத்தது உணர்வான்) should be taken as equal to a god (தெய்வத்தோடொப்பக் கொளல்). In both, a human being is ranked with the gods for a faculty of taking in what others carry: their teaching in one, their unspoken thought in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:414","b":"k:403","type":"develops","note":"403 says even the unlearned are very good if they can keep from speaking before the learned (கற்றார்முன் சொல்லாதிருக்கப் பெறின்). 414 addresses the same person (கற்றிலனாயினும்) and tells him to listen (கேட்க). The silence that 403 only recommends as damage control becomes in 414 the means of gaining what he lacks.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:415","b":"k:139","type":"develops","note":"415 says the words from the mouth of those with conduct (ஒழுக்கமுடையார் வாய்ச்சொல்) are like a staff where the ground is slippery (இழுக்கல் உடையுழி). 139 says of the same people (ஒழுக்கமுடையவர்க்கு) that speaking evil with the mouth, even by a slip (வழுக்கியும் வாயாற் சொலல்), is not possible. 139 gives the reason 415 needs: those who do not slip in what they say are the ones whose words can steady someone who is slipping.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:415","b":"k:447","type":"parallel_domain","note":"415 presents the words of people of conduct as the staff that keeps a person upright on slippery ground. 447 asks who can ruin a ruler who keeps as his own those who will rebuke him (இடிக்குந் துணையார்). Both verses locate safety in another person's speech; 447 applies it to a king and makes the helpful words explicitly unwelcome ones.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:417","b":"k:139","type":"parallel_domain","note":"417 says well-grounded listeners do not speak folly even when they have misunderstood (பிழைத்துணர்ந்தும்). 139 says that for those who have conduct, speaking evil even by a slip (வழுக்கியும்) is not possible. The same guarantee, speech that holds even under error, is attached to listening in the Book of Wealth and to conduct in the Book of Virtue.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:417","b":"k:199","type":"parallel_domain","note":"417 says those whose wide listening rests on their own close inquiry (இழைத்துணர்ந்து ஈண்டிய கேள்வியவர்) do not speak folly even when they have misunderstood (பிழைத்துணர்ந்தும் பேதைமை சொல்லார்). 199 says those of stainless vision, free of delusion (மருள்தீர்ந்த மாசறு காட்சியவர்), do not say empty things even in forgetfulness (பொச்சாந்தும் சொல்லார்). The two verses have the same shape, a kind of person whose speech stays sound even when the mind lapses, and they credit it to different sources: listening in one, clear vision in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:418","b":"k:577","type":"parallel_domain","note":"418 denies real hearing to ears that listening has not opened. 577 says those without kindly regard have no eyes (கண்ணோட்டம் இல்லவர் கண்ணிலர்), and those who have eyes do not lack it. The form of argument is the same, an organ is counted as present only when it is used for its higher purpose, applied to instruction in 418 and to kindness in 577.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:419","b":"k:95","type":"qualifies","note":"95 says being humble and sweet-spoken (பணிவுடையன் இன்சொலன் ஆதல்) is a person's ornament, and nothing else is. 419 says a mouth that bows (வணங்கிய வாய்) is hard to come by without fine listening. 95 holds humble speech up for everyone, and 419 adds a condition: it has to be prepared for by listening.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:419","b":"k:844","type":"develops","note":"419 says humble speech is hardly to be had without fine listening. 844 defines வெண்மை, shallowness of understanding, as the conceit that says 'we have brightness' (ஒண்மை உடையம் யாம் என்னும் செருக்கு). The pair links lack of instruction and self-praise from both ends: 419 says the uninstructed will not speak humbly, and 844 says boasting of one's understanding is itself the mark of lacking it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:420","b":"k:214","type":"parallel_domain","note":"420 treats those without the ear's taste as people whose death and life are the same. 214 says only one who knows what is fitting truly lives (உயிர்வாழ்வான்), and anyone else is counted among the dead (செத்தாருள் வைக்கப்படும்). The book repeatedly denies real life to those who lack one defining quality; here the quality is listening, and in 214 it is knowing one's duty to others.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:420","b":"k:406","type":"agrees","note":"406 says the unlearned can only be said to exist (உளரென்னும் மாத்திரையர்) and are otherwise like saline ground that yields nothing (பயவாக் களர்). 420 says of those who have no taste for what the ear takes in that their dying or living makes no difference (அவியினும் வாழினும் என்). The chapters on not learning and on listening reach the same verdict, a life that merely exists; Manakkudavar reads 420, as Parimelazhagar reads 406, as about being of no use to others.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:420","b":"k:1012","type":"parallel_domain","note":"420 asks what it matters whether they die or live, those மாக்கள் who know taste by the mouth and not by the ear. 1012 says food, clothing and the rest are no different for all living beings (ஊணுடை எச்சம் உயிர்க்கெல்லாம் வேறல்ல), and the distinction of மாந்தர் is having shame. Both verses treat eating as what humans share with every creature and put human worth elsewhere, in the ear's taste for one and in shame for the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:421","b":"k:781","type":"parallel_domain","note":"421 names understanding as the tool that guards against ruin (அற்றங் காக்குங் கருவி). 781 asks what guard is as hard for hostile action to get past as friendship (வினைக்கரிய யாவுள காப்பு). The Book of Wealth offers two protections that are not walls or weapons, one within the person and one between persons, and praises each in the vocabulary of defence.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:422","b":"k:24","type":"develops","note":"24 says one who guards the five with the goad called firmness (உரனென்னும் தோட்டியான் ஓரைந்தும் காப்பான்) is a seed for the highest ground; the goad makes the senses elephants, as Parimelazhagar spells out. 422 gives understanding a similar job, not letting one go where one has gone (சென்ற இடத்தால் செலவிடா), which Parimelazhagar explains with a rider directing a horse. 422 goes a step past 24's holding back: it also sorts good from evil and steers toward the good.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:422","b":"k:787","type":"parallel_domain","note":"422 says understanding does not let one go wherever the mind has gone, but draws away from evil and directs toward good (தீதொரீஇ நன்றின்பால் உய்ப்பது). 787 says friendship removes one from what ruins and sets one on the path (அழிவினவை நீக்கி ஆறுய்த்து). The two verses use the same pair of actions and the same verb of steering (உய்); what understanding does for a person from inside, the friend does from outside.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:424","b":"k:650","type":"develops","note":"424 makes it part of understanding to put things so that hearers take them in easily. 650 says those who cannot lay out what they have learned so that others understand (கற்றது உணர விரித்துரையாதார்) are like a cluster of flowers in bloom with no scent. 650 gives the failure case of 424's first half: learning that cannot be conveyed is counted as lacking something essential. The first word of 650 is misprinted in this edition, though the image is clear from the commentary.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:424","b":"k:701","type":"develops","note":"424 says understanding sees the fine meaning in what others say (பிறர்வாய் நுண்பொருள்); Manakkudavar takes this as seeing what they mean without their spelling it out. 701 praises one who knows what is intended by looking, without its being said (கூறாமை நோக்கிக் குறிப்பறிவான்). 701 carries the second half of 424 one step further, from reading what lies within words to reading what is never put into words.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:425","b":"k:115","type":"agrees","note":"115 says loss and increase are not absent from life, and that not bending in the heart (நெஞ்சத்துக் கோடாமை) is the ornament of the worthy. Parimelazhagar records a second reading of 425, in which the opening and closing are expanding in wealth and shrinking in want. On that reading the two verses say the same thing, that a person should stay the same through gain and loss, one under the heading of fairness and the other of understanding.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:425","b":"k:544","type":"same_image","note":"425 says embracing the world is brightness (உலகம் தழீஇயது ஒட்பம்). 544 uses the same verb twice: the world stands embracing the feet (அடிதழீஇ நிற்கும் உலகு) of the king who rules embracing his people (குடிதழீஇ). In 544 the embrace is returned, which gives a concrete reason why 425 counts drawing the world close as intelligence. Parimelazhagar narrows 'the world' in 425 to the high-minded.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:425","b":"k:782","type":"parallel_domain","note":"425 says understanding is what has neither opening nor closing (மலர்தலும் கூம்பலும் இல்லது), which Parimelazhagar reads as steadiness in friendship with the high-minded, unlike a pond flower that opens and shuts. 782 says the friendship of the wise fills like the crescent moon and that of fools shrinks like the moon after full. Both verses judge attachment by a natural cycle; 782 praises the bond that grows, while 425 asks for one that does not swing in either direction.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:426","b":"k:850","type":"develops","note":"426 makes accord with the world part of what understanding is. 850 says one who says 'it is not' of what the world says is (உலகத்தார் உண்டென்பது இல்லென்பான்) will be set down as a demon on earth. 850, in the chapter on shallow understanding, extends 426 from conduct to belief and states the cost of breaking with the world's view.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:427","b":"k:372","type":"qualifies","note":"427 makes knowing what is to come the mark of having understanding, as if it were a trait of the person. 372 says a fate of loss makes a person foolish (பேதைப்படுக்கும் இழவூழ்) and a fate of gain, when it arrives, widens understanding (அறிவகற்றும் ஆகலூழ்). Read with 372, the division in 427 is not fixed: the same person may see ahead or fail to, depending on what fate is at work.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:427","b":"k:429","type":"develops","note":"427 defines those with understanding as those who know what is going to be (ஆவதறிவார்). 429 says those whose understanding guards against what is ahead (எதிரதாக் காக்கும் அறிவினார்) have no affliction that comes to shake them. 429 gives the use of the foresight that 427 only defines: knowing beforehand matters because it lets one guard beforehand.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:427","b":"k:461","type":"develops","note":"427 separates the wise from the unwise by whether they know what will be (ஆவது). 461 tells one to act after weighing what will be lost, what will come of it (ஆவதூஉம்), and the gain that follows later. 461 turns the foresight of 427 into a procedure, a reckoning of outcomes made before acting.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:428","b":"k:1075","type":"qualifies","note":"428 makes fear of the right things the work of the knowing (அறிவார் தொழில்). 1075 says fear is the whole basis of proper behaviour among the base (அச்சமே கீழ்களது ஆசாரம்), which Parimelazhagar takes as fear of harm from the king. Together they show that fear in itself does not separate the wise from the low; what separates them is that the wise fear what deserves it and the base behave only for fear of being punished.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:429","b":"k:435","type":"agrees","note":"429 says those whose understanding guards against what is ahead have no affliction that comes to shake them (அதிர வருவதோர் நோய்). 435 says the life of one who does not guard before it comes (வருமுன்னர்க் காவாதான்) perishes like a straw-heap before fire. They are the positive and negative statements of one rule; 435 stands in the chapter on faults, where Parimelazhagar supplies 'fault' as what comes, while 429 speaks of harm in general.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:429","b":"k:442","type":"develops","note":"429 credits freedom from shock to an understanding that guards against what is coming. 442 tells a ruler to cherish and keep those who can remove the trouble that has come and guard beforehand against what has not (உறாஅமை முற்காக்கும் பெற்றியார்). 442 shows that the foresight of 429 need not be the ruler's own: it can be secured by choosing the right people.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:430","b":"k:591","type":"parallel_domain","note":"430 defines having by understanding: the wise have all, and the unwise have nothing whatever they own. 591 uses the same turn for energy of mind: it is what makes someone 'one who has' (உடையர் எனப்படுவது ஊக்கம்), and those without it do not really have what they have (உடையது உடையரோ). The book redefines possession in the same words for two different inner qualities.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:430","b":"k:841","type":"agrees","note":"430 says those with understanding have everything, and those without it have nothing whatever they have (என்னுடையரேனும் இலர்). 841 says lack of understanding is the lack among lacks (அறிவின்மை இன்மையுள் இன்மை), and the world does not count other lack as lack. 841, opening the chapter on shallow understanding, restates 430 from the side of poverty.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:431","b":"k:180","type":"same_image","note":"431 lists செருக்கு first among the faults whose absence gives prosperity its dignity. 180 names a செருக்கு it approves of, 'வேண்டாமை என்னும் செருக்கு', the pride of not wanting, which yields victory (விறல் ஈனும்); Parimelazhagar there glosses the word as wealth. Read together they separate arrogance from a pride that consists in refusing to covet.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:432","b":"k:531","type":"develops","note":"432 lists 'மாணா உவகை' among a ruler's faults; Parimelazhagar takes it as joy beyond measure. 531 says why such joy is dangerous: the slackness that comes from the elation of great joy (சிறந்த உவகை மகிழ்ச்சியிற் சோர்வு) is worse than excessive anger. 531 supplies the mechanism for the fault 432 only names.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:432","b":"k:1028","type":"agrees","note":"432 calls honour that has passed beyond the fine a fault in a ruler. 1028 says that for one raising his family, idling and minding his மானம் brings ruin (மடிசெய்து மானங் கருதக் கெடும்). Both treat a misplaced sense of honour as a fault, one in the king and one in the head of a house.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:433","b":"k:957","type":"develops","note":"433 describes how those ashamed of blame weigh a small fault of their own. 957 says a fault in the well-born shows up raised high, like the spot on the moon (மதிக்கண் மறுப்போல் உயர்ந்து). 957 adds the public side: in a person of standing a small fault is seen large by others too.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:433","b":"k:965","type":"agrees","note":"433 says the blame-fearing treat a millet-sized fault as palmyra-sized. 965 says even people like a hill are diminished if they do a lowering thing the size of a குன்றி seed. 965 gives the outward fact, that a tiny act does lower the great, which makes the inward magnifying in 433 an accurate estimate and not an exaggeration.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:434","b":"k:304","type":"agrees","note":"434 says fault itself is the enemy that brings ruin. 304 asks whether there is any other enemy (பகையும் உளவோ பிற) than anger, which kills laughter and joy. 304 applies to one named fault the claim 434 makes of fault in general: the real enemy is inside.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:435","b":"k:535","type":"agrees","note":"435: the life of one who doesn't guard before it comes (வருமுன்னர்க் காவாதான்) perishes like straw before fire. 535: one who slipped by not guarding beforehand (முன்னுறக் காவாது இழுக்கியான்) will later grieve over his own error. The same failure is given two outcomes, sudden destruction in 435 and late regret in 535.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:437","b":"k:228","type":"agrees","note":"437: the wealth of one who clings to it without doing what should be done with it (செயற்பால செய்யாது இவறியான்) perishes without lasting. 228 asks whether the hard-eyed who keep what they have and lose it (வைத்திழக்கும்) don't know the joy of giving. Both assume hoarded wealth is lost anyway; 228 adds what the hoarder missed in the meantime.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:437","b":"k:466","type":"parallel_domain","note":"437 says wealth perishes when its owner does not do the things that ought to be done (செயற்பால செய்யாது ... கெடும்). 466 says one perishes by doing what is unfit and also by not doing what is fit (செய்தக்க செய்யாமை யானுங் கெடும்). The miser of 437 is a case of 466's second half: ruin by omission.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:437","b":"k:1006","type":"develops","note":"437 says the miser's wealth perishes because he does not do what should be done with it; Parimelazhagar lists these as virtue, increase of wealth and enjoyment. 1006 says one who neither uses his wealth himself nor gives to the deserving (தான்துவ்வான் ... ஈதல் இயல்பிலாதான்) is an ஏதம், a harm, to great wealth. 1006 spells out the two uses left undone and turns the miser himself into the wealth's affliction.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:438","b":"k:432","type":"qualifies","note":"432 lists இவறல் as one of three faults in a ruler, beside excessive honour and excessive joy. 438, on Parimelazhagar's reading, takes it out of any such list as greater than the rest (எற்றுள்ளும் எண்ணப்படுவதொன்று அன்று). Manakkudavar reads 438 differently, as saying miserliness is not to be valued as anything, and on that reading the two verses simply agree.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:438","b":"k:1002","type":"develops","note":"438 says the miserliness called the clinging heart (பற்றுள்ளம் என்னும் இவறன்மை) is not to be counted among any others; Parimelazhagar takes this to mean it exceeds all other faults. 1002 names what lies behind it, the delusion (மருள்) that everything comes by wealth, so that one clings without giving (ஈயாது இவறும்), and gives its result as an inglorious birth. 1002 supplies a cause and a consequence for the fault 438 ranks.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:439","b":"k:474","type":"develops","note":"439 commands: never admire yourself (வியவற்க எஞ்ஞான்றும் தன்னை). 474 gives the outcome for one who doesn't live in accord with others, doesn't know his measure and admires himself (தன்னை வியந்தான்): he perishes quickly. Parimelazhagar connects them in his note on 439, saying that self-admiration keeps one from knowing place, time and strength.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:439","b":"k:652","type":"same_image","note":"439 ends 'நயவற்க நன்றி பயவா வினை', do not desire a deed that yields no good. 652 ends with the same phrase and extends it: one must always avoid a deed that yields neither renown nor good (புகழொடு நன்றி பயவா வினை). The rule is given in 439 as part of a ruler's guarding against faults and in 652, on Parimelazhagar's reading, as a rule for ministers.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:440","b":"k:866","type":"develops","note":"440: if a ruler can enjoy what he loves without the love being known (காதல் அறியாமை உய்க்கிற்பின்), his enemies' designs come to nothing. 866, from the enemy's side, says one of blinding anger and excessive desire (கழிபெருங் காமத்தான்) is an opponent whose enmity is worth having. Together they show the reasoning: a visible passion is an opening for an enemy, which Parimelazhagar states in his note on 440.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:441","b":"k:795","type":"parallel_domain","note":"441 tells a ruler to choose, after weighing, the friendship of those who know virtue and are mature in understanding (கேண்மை ... தேர்ந்து கொளல்). 795 tells anyone to examine and take the friendship (நட்பு ஆய்ந்து கொளல்) of those able to make one weep, to rebuke, and to teach the right way. The same instruction, to select friends for their capacity to guide, is given for the king's advisers and for ordinary friendship.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:442","b":"k:435","type":"develops","note":"435 says the life of one who does not guard before it comes will burn like straw. 442 tells the ruler to cherish and take as his own those whose nature is to remove the affliction that has come and to guard beforehand against what has not (உறாஅமை முற்காக்கும் பெற்றியார்). The foresight 435 demands of the ruler is, in 442, something he obtains through the people he keeps.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:442","b":"k:787","type":"parallel_domain","note":"442 describes the people a ruler should keep: they remove the trouble that has come and guard in advance against more. 787 describes friendship in similar terms: it removes what leads to ruin (அழிவினவை நீக்கி), sets one on the path, and shares the suffering when ruin comes. The great adviser and the friend are given the same protective work, though 787 adds a sharing of suffering that 442 does not ask of the adviser.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:443","b":"k:781","type":"parallel_domain","note":"443 calls winning the great as one's own the rarest of rare things (அரியவற்றுள் எல்லாம் அரிது). 781 asks what is as hard to make as friendship (செயற்கரிய யாவுள நட்பின்), and what guard is as hard for an enemy's work to get past. Both rank a bond with others as the hardest thing to obtain, and 781 adds that it is also the strongest protection, which is what chapter 45 goes on to claim for the great.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:444","b":"k:891","type":"parallel_domain","note":"444: to conduct oneself so that those greater than oneself are one's own is the chief of all strengths (வன்மையுள் எல்லாம் தலை). 891: not slighting the power of the able is the chief of all the safeguards of those who guard themselves (போற்றலுள் எல்லாம் தலை). The two verses use the same ranking formula for the two sides of dealing with the great, winning them as the highest strength and not offending them as the highest protection.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:444","b":"k:985","type":"agrees","note":"444 places the chief strength in attaching oneself to those greater than oneself; Parimelazhagar adds that this means following their lead. 985 says the strength of those who accomplish things is to bow (ஆற்றுவார் ஆற்றல் பணிதல்). Both locate strength in deference and not in standing alone.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:445","b":"k:462","type":"agrees","note":"445 tells the ruler to choose his counsellors after deliberation (சூழ்வாரைச் சூழ்ந்து கொளல்), since they serve as his eyes. 462 says nothing is out of reach for those who act after considering a matter with chosen company (தெரிந்த இனத்தொடு தேர்ந்தெண்ணி). Both put the selection of the people one deliberates with before the deliberation itself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:445","b":"k:581","type":"same_image","note":"445 says a ruler proceeds with his counsellors as his eyes (சூழ்வார் கண்ணாக) and so must choose them with thought. 581 says a ruler should hold spies and the respected treatise as his eyes (மன்னவன் கண்). The king's 'eyes' are advisers in one verse and intelligence plus learning in the other, and in both he sees through something other than himself. This follows Parimelazhagar's reading of 445; Manakkudavar takes its second line differently.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:445","b":"k:639","type":"develops","note":"445 says counsellors must be chosen with care because the ruler sees by them. 639 states the cost of choosing badly: a minister at one's side who plots harm (பழுதெண்ணும் மந்திரி) is worse than seventy crore enemies. 639 shows what the care in 445 protects against.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:446","b":"k:460","type":"agrees","note":"446 says enemies can do nothing against one who keeps the company of the worthy. 460 says there is no support greater than good company (நல்லினத்தின் ஊங்கும் துணையில்லை) and nothing that brings more affliction than bad company. 460 states as a general rule what 446 applies to a ruler facing enemies.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:446","b":"k:868","type":"agrees","note":"446: against one who can conduct himself as having the worthy for his company (தக்கார் இனத்தனாய்), enemies have nothing left to do. 868 gives the reverse case: one with no good qualities and many faults is without company (இனன் இலன்), and that is a protection to his opponents. Both verses use the word இனம் and both make a ruler's exposure to enemies depend on who stands with him.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:447","b":"k:638","type":"develops","note":"447 speaks of the ruler who keeps rebukers. 638 gives the matching duty of the one beside him: even if the king rejects knowledge and knows nothing himself, the one at his side is bound to tell him what is sound (உறுதி உழையிருந்தான் கூறல் கடன்). 638 extends the idea to the case where the king is not the willing listener 447 assumes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:447","b":"k:784","type":"parallel_domain","note":"447 values supporters for their rebuking (இடிக்கும்) and says a ruler who has them cannot be ruined. 784 defines friendship by the same act: it is not for laughing but for going ahead to rebuke when one oversteps (மேற்சென்று இடித்தற் பொருட்டு). Rebuke is treated as the purpose of the bond in both statecraft and friendship.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:449","b":"k:1030","type":"parallel_domain","note":"449 says there is no standing (நிலை) for those without the support of a prop (மதலையாம் சார்பு). 1030 says a family with no good person to stand by and prop it (அடுத்தூன்றும் நல்லாள் இலாத குடி) falls when trouble cuts at its base. The image of a structure that needs a prop is applied to a king in one verse and to a household in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:450","b":"k:800","type":"agrees","note":"450 says abandoning one's tie with the good (நல்லார் தொடர் கைவிடல்) is ten times worse than the enmity of many. 800 says to hold close the friendship of the stainless (மருவுக மாசற்றார் கேண்மை) and to be rid of the friendship of the unfit even at a price. 800 gives the same instruction for friendship and adds its other half, which bonds to drop.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:450","b":"k:816","type":"parallel_domain","note":"450 weighs losing the good against the enmity of many and finds it ten times worse. 816 weighs close friendship with a fool against being a stranger to the wise and finds the second ten million times better (கோடி உறும்). Both rank relationships by a multiplied comparison, and in both the quality of the person outweighs whether the relation is friendly or hostile.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:450","b":"k:873","type":"develops","note":"873 says one who, being alone, takes on the enmity of many (பல்லார் பகை கொள்பவன்) is more wretched than the mad. 450 uses the same phrase as its measure: letting go the tie with the good is ten times worse than that (பல்லார் பகை கொளலிற் பத்தடுத்த தீமைத்து). Read together, what 873 treats as an extreme of folly becomes the smaller of two harms in 450.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:451","b":"k:201","type":"parallel_domain","note":"451 says greatness fears small company (சிற்றினம் அஞ்சும் பெருமை) while smallness takes it for kin. 201 says those given to evil deeds do not fear them but the excellent do (தீவினையார் அஞ்சார் விழுமியார் அஞ்சுவர்). In both, what marks the better person is fear of the thing the worse person is at ease with, once about company and once about deeds.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:452","b":"k:959","type":"parallel_domain","note":"452 says water takes the nature of the land, and understanding the nature of one's company (இனத்தியல்பு). 959 says the sprout shows what lay in the ground (நிலத்தில் கிடந்தமை கால் காட்டும்) and speech shows the family one was born in. Both treat a person as carrying the mark of the ground they stand in, 452 for the company kept and 959 for birth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:452","b":"k:1323","type":"same_image","note":"452 uses water changing with the nature of the land it meets (நிலத்தியல்பால் நீர் திரிந்து) for understanding taking on the nature of one's company. In 1323 a lover calls the pair those joined as water with earth (நிலத்தொடு நீர் இயைந்தன்னார்), and Parimelazhagar explains that image by water taking on the land's nature. The same picture of water absorbed into soil is a caution about company in one place and a figure of union in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:453","b":"k:505","type":"tension","note":"453 says perception comes from the mind, but the word that one is 'such a person' comes from one's company (இன்னான் எனப்படும் சொல் இனத்தான் ஆம்). 505 says the touchstone of greatness and smallness is each person's own deeds (தத்தம் கருமமே கட்டளைக் கல்), which Parimelazhagar glosses as that and nothing else. If 453 is read as saying what a person really is, and not only what people call them, the two name different tests of character: company or deeds.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies"},{"a":"k:455","b":"k:298","type":"develops","note":"298 says outer purity comes by water and inner purity is seen through truthfulness (அகந்தூய்மை வாய்மையால் காணப்படும்). 455 says purity of mind comes resting on purity of company. The book thus gives inner purity two supports, one's own truthfulness and the people one keeps.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:456","b":"k:462","type":"develops","note":"456 says no deed fails to turn out well for those whose company is pure (இனந்தூயார்க்கு இல்லை நன்றாகா வினை), and Parimelazhagar gives the reason: their deeds are thought through with good company. 462 states that mechanism directly: nothing is hard to attain for those who act after examining the matter with chosen company (தெரிந்த இனத்தொடு தேர்ந்தெண்ணி).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:457","b":"k:651","type":"qualifies","note":"457 says goodness of mind gives gain (ஆக்கம்) and goodness of company gives all praise as well (இனநலம் எல்லாப் புகழும் தரும்). 651 has the same two-step form but says goodness of companions gives gain and goodness of action gives all that is wanted (துணைநலம் ஆக்கம் தரூஉம் வினைநலம் வேண்டிய எல்லாம் தரும்); Parimelazhagar there reads companions as giving wealth only. Company holds the higher place in 457 and the lower in 651, where one's own action outranks it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:458","b":"k:448","type":"develops","note":"458 says that even for the worthy who have goodness of mind in full, goodness of company is a safeguard (ஏமாப்பு உடைத்து). 448 says a king without the guard of people who rebuke him (இடிப்பாரை இல்லாத ஏமரா மன்னன்) perishes even with no one to ruin him. Both use the same word for protection (ஏமம்), and 448 shows what the company protects against: one's own unchecked faults.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:459","b":"k:243","type":"develops","note":"243 says those whose hearts are joined to அருள் do not enter the dark world of pain (அருள் சேர்ந்த நெஞ்சினார்க்கு இல்லை இருள் சேர்ந்த இன்னா உலகம் புகல்). 459 agrees that the next world comes from goodness of mind (மனநலத்தின் ஆகும் மறுமை) and adds that even this is made secure by goodness of company. The afterlife that 243 rests on the heart alone is given a second support in 459.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:460","b":"k:32","type":"parallel_domain","note":"460 is built on the form 'nothing beyond X as support, nothing beyond Y as affliction' (நல்லினத்தின் ஊங்கும் துணையில்லை). 32 uses the same form and the same word ஊங்கு for virtue: no gain beyond virtue, no ruin beyond forgetting it (அறத்தினூஉங்கு ஆக்கமும் இல்லை). The pairing shows good company being given, in the book on statecraft, the kind of unqualified ranking the opening chapters give to virtue.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:460","b":"k:450","type":"develops","note":"460 says there is no support greater than good company and nothing that afflicts more than bad company (தீயினத்தின் அல்லற் படுப்பதூஉம் இல்). 450 says letting go the tie with the good is ten times worse than taking on the enmity of many (பல்லார் பகை கொளலிற் பத்தடுத்த தீமைத்து). 460 names keeping bad company as the worst harm and 450 names losing good company; both rank these above open enemies, and Parimelazhagar glosses 460's second half as enmity.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:461","b":"k:471","type":"develops","note":"461 lists the outcomes to be weighed before acting: loss, gain and later yield. 471, opening the next chapter with the same imperative, lists the strengths to be weighed: of the deed, oneself, the opponent and the allies (வினைவலியும் தன்வலியும் மாற்றான் வலியும் துணைவலியும் தூக்கிச் செயல்). The second supplies what the first's calculation of gain and loss depends on.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:461","b":"k:676","type":"develops","note":"461 tells one to weigh what will be lost, what will be gained and the later yield, and then act (அழிவதூஉம் ஆவதூஉம் ... ஊதியமும் சூழ்ந்து செயல்). 676 has the same closing command and also three items, but different ones: what it takes to finish, the obstacles, and the great yield at completion (முடிவும் இடையூறும் ... படுபயனும் பார்த்துச் செயல்). 461 is an account of cost and return; 676 keeps the return and adds the course of the work itself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:462","b":"k:568","type":"agrees","note":"462 says nothing is hard to attain for those who act after examining a matter with chosen company and thinking it through themselves (தெரிந்த இனத்தொடு தேர்ந்தெண்ணி). 568 gives the negative case: the fortune of a king who does not think matters through with his circle (இனத்தாற்றி எண்ணாத வேந்தன்) and then rages will shrink. Both make deliberation with one's இனம் the condition of success, stated once as promise and once as warning.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:463","b":"k:932","type":"parallel_domain","note":"463 says the wise do not undertake what loses the principal in hope of gain. 932 asks whether gamblers, who get one and lose a hundred (ஒன்றெய்தி நூறிழக்கும் சூதர்), have any way of living well. The gambler is the plain case of the venture 463 rules out, a small gain pursued at the cost of what one already had.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:464","b":"k:172","type":"parallel_domain","note":"464 says those who fear the harm called disgrace do not begin what is not clear to them (தெளிவிலதனைத் தொடங்கார் ... அஞ்சுபவர்). 172 has the same form: those ashamed of unfairness do not do blameworthy things out of desire for the gain (பழிப்படுவ செய்யார் நடுவன்மை நாணுபவர்). In both a fear or shame is what holds a person back, in 172 from a wrong act and in 464 from an unexamined one.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:464","b":"k:428","type":"develops","note":"428 says not fearing what should be feared is folly and fearing it is the work of the knowing (அஞ்சுவது அஞ்சல் அறிவார் தொழில்). 464 gives one such fear and what it does: fear of disgrace (இளிவென்னும் ஏதப்பாடு) keeps people from beginning what they have not made clear. The general rule of 428 is applied in 464 to the start of an undertaking.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:465","b":"k:473","type":"develops","note":"465 says rising without full deliberation (வகையறச் சூழாது எழுதல்) benefits the enemy. 473 says many who did not know their own strength pressed on in enthusiasm and broke midway (ஊக்கத்தின் ஊக்கி இடைக்கண் முரிந்தார் பலர்). 473 names one of the things left unconsidered, one's own strength, and shows the cost to oneself where 465 shows the gain to the enemy.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:465","b":"k:718","type":"same_image","note":"465 says setting out without thinking through every aspect is a way of setting one's enemies in a planting bed (பகைவரைப் பாத்திப் படுப்பது); Parimelazhagar reads this as establishing them in ground where they grow. 718 says speaking before those who understand is like pouring water into the bed of a growing plant (வளர்வதன் பாத்தியுள் நீர் சொரிந்தற்று). The same garden bed stands for fostering growth in both, wanted in 718 and brought on oneself in 465.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:465","b":"k:879","type":"same_image","note":"465 pictures a rash attack as planting the enemy in a bed where he will grow. 879, in a chapter on enemies, says to cut a thorn tree while it is young, since when hardened it wounds the hand that cuts it (இளைதாக முள்மரம் கொல்க). Both treat the enemy as a plant whose growth is the danger: 879 says to stop it early, 465 warns against tending it by one's own haste.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:466","b":"k:510","type":"parallel_domain","note":"466 says one is ruined by doing what is not fit to do, and also by not doing what is fit (செய்தக்க செய்யாமை யானும் கெடும்). 510 says trusting someone unexamined and doubting someone already examined and trusted both bring unending trouble (தேரான் தெளிவும் தெளிந்தான்கண் ஐயுறவும்). Each verse sets an error of excess beside an error of defect and gives them the same result, once for action and once for trust.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:466","b":"k:672","type":"develops","note":"466 says both doing the unfit and leaving the fit undone bring ruin. 672 gives the same two-sided rule for timing: delay what should be done slowly, and do not delay what must be done without delay (தூங்குக தூங்கிச் செயற்பால தூங்கற்க தூங்காது செய்யும் வினை). 672 carries 466's point, that holding back is no safer than acting, into the question of when to act.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:467","b":"k:509","type":"parallel_domain","note":"467 fixes the order for action: think first, then resolve, and no thinking postponed until after. 509 fixes the same order for trusting people: trust no one without examining, and after examining, trust what is to be trusted (தேறற்க யாரையும் தேராது தேர்ந்தபின் தேறுக). In both the examination belongs wholly before the commitment, and the commitment is then not to be reopened.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:468","b":"k:616","type":"qualifies","note":"616 says effort produces fortune and lack of effort brings want (முயற்சி திருவினை ஆக்கும்). 468 says exertion that is not made by the right way (ஆற்றின் வருந்தா வருத்தம்) will give way even if many stand guarding it. 468 puts a condition on 616's praise of effort: it has to follow the proper method, and neither strain nor helpers make up for that.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:468","b":"k:640","type":"parallel_domain","note":"468 says effort without the right method fails whatever support it has (பலர் நின்று போற்றினும் பொத்துப்படும்). 640 says those without practical capacity leave things unfinished even after planning them in proper order (முறைப்படச் சூழ்ந்தும் முடிவிலவே செய்வர் திறப்பாடு இலாதவர்). The two are complementary failures: effort without method in 468, and method on paper without the ability to carry it out in 640.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies"},{"a":"k:469","b":"k:987","type":"tension","note":"469 says doing good can itself be a mistake if the character of the person is not taken into account. 987 asks what worth has achieved if it does not do what is sweet even to those who have done one harm (இன்னா செய்தார்க்கும் இனியவே செய்யாக்கால்), which makes the recipient's conduct irrelevant. The conflict holds if 469's நன்றாற்றல் is read as doing good in general, as Pope and Kalaignar take it; Parimelazhagar narrows it to a king's use of gifts and soft words toward other kings, and on that reading the two do not meet.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:469","b":"k:1078","type":"develops","note":"469 says there is error even in doing good when it is done without knowing each person's character (அவரவர் பண்பறிந்து ஆற்றாக்கடை). 1078 says the worthy are of use at a word, while the base, like sugar-cane, are of use only when crushed (சொல்லப் பயன்படுவர் சான்றோர் கரும்புபோல் கொல்லப் பயன்படும் கீழ்). 1078 is an instance of the difference in character 469 says must be known first: the approach that works on one kind of person does nothing with another.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:470","b":"k:140","type":"agrees","note":"470 says to think and do what will not be scorned, because the world does not accept what does not accord with oneself (கொள்ளாத கொள்ளாது உலகு). 140 says those who have not learned to conduct themselves in accord with the world lack understanding, however much they have studied (உலகத்தோடு ஒட்ட ஒழுகல்). Both make the world's acceptance a test that sound judgement has to meet, 470 for a ruler's choice of action and 140 for conduct in general.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:470","b":"k:280","type":"parallel_domain","note":"470 tells the one who acts to avoid what the world will scorn and not accept. 280 tells the ascetic that neither shaving nor matted hair is needed if he gives up what the world has condemned (உலகம் பழித்தது ஒழித்து விடின்); Parimelazhagar takes 'the world' there as the high-minded. The same measure, what the world rejects, governs a king's policy in one verse and a renouncer's conduct in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:471","b":"k:250","type":"qualifies","note":"471 makes comparing strengths the test of whether to act. 250, in the chapter on compassion, uses the same comparison as a brake: when going against those weaker than oneself (தன்னின் மெலியார்மேல் செல்லுமிடத்து), one should think of oneself before the stronger (வலியார்முன் தன்னை நினைக்க). Being the stronger party permits action in 471, while 250 asks the stronger party to remember what that position feels like from below.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:471","b":"k:675","type":"develops","note":"471 tells one to weigh four strengths before acting: of the deed, oneself, the opponent and the allies (வினைவலி, தன்வலி, மாற்றான் வலி, துணைவலி ... தூக்கிச் செயல்). 675 gives a second checklist with the same closing command, five things to think through until the darkness clears: resources, means, time, the deed and the place (பொருள் கருவி காலம் வினை இடன் ... எண்ணிச் செயல்). Read together, 471 measures the parties to a contest and 675 adds the circumstances of the act, and only the deed (வினை) is on both lists.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:471","b":"k:861","type":"develops","note":"471 says to weigh one's own strength against the opponent's before acting (தன்வலியும் மாற்றான் வலியும் தூக்கிச் செயல்). 861 states what follows from that weighing when choosing an enemy: avoid opposing the stronger (வலியார்க்கு மாறேற்றல் ஓம்புக) and pursue enmity with the weaker (மெலியார்மேல் மேக பகை). 861 is 471's comparison turned into a rule.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:472","b":"k:462","type":"agrees","note":"472 says nothing fails for those who know what they can do and what must be known and proceed resting in that (ஒல்வது அறிவது அறிந்து ... செல்லாதது இல்). 462 says nothing is out of reach for those who act after examining the matter with chosen company and thinking it over themselves (தேர்ந்தெண்ணிச் செய்வார்க்கு அரும்பொருள் யாதொன்றும் இல்). The promise is the same in both and the condition differs: knowledge of one's own capacity in 472, counsel and deliberation in 462.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:472","b":"k:483","type":"parallel_domain","note":"472 promises that nothing fails for one who knows his capacity (ஒல்வது அறிந்து ... செல்லாதது இல்). 483 asks whether anything is hard for one who acts with the means and knows the time (அருவினை என்ப உளவோ கருவியான் காலம் அறிந்து செயின்). The chapters on knowing strength and knowing time each make the same claim for their own subject, which suggests each is meant as one necessary condition, not a sufficient one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:473","b":"k:594","type":"qualifies","note":"594 says gain asks the way and goes to one whose ஊக்கம் does not waver (அசைவிலா ஊக்கமுடையான் உழை). 473 says many who did not know their own strength set out on ஊக்கம் and broke midway (வலியறியார் ஊக்கத்தின் ஊக்கி இடைக்கண் முரிந்தார் பலர்). The same word names the quality praised in chapter 60 and the cause of failure here, so 473 limits 594: drive pays only when it is matched to a measured strength.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:473","b":"k:612","type":"develops","note":"612 warns against letting a task perish in the middle of doing it (வினைக்கண் வினைகெடல் ஓம்பல்), because the world gives up on those who leave work unfinished. 473 describes how that happens: people start on enthusiasm without knowing their strength and break midway (இடைக்கண் முரிந்தார்). 612 demands finishing; 473 puts the remedy before the start, in measuring one's strength.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:473","b":"k:899","type":"same_image","note":"473 says many who did not know their strength broke midway (இடைக்கண் முரிந்தார்). 899 uses the same figure for a king: if those of exalted resolve are angered, even the king breaks midway and loses his kingship (இடைமுரிந்து வேந்தனும் வேந்து கெடும்). Parimelazhagar reads 473 as starting something against those stronger than oneself, and 899 gives one such case, a ruler who misjudges the power of the great.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:474","b":"k:863","type":"agrees","note":"474 lists three failings that bring quick ruin: not living in accord (அமைந்தாங்கு ஒழுகான்), not knowing one's measure (அளவறியான்) and admiring oneself. 863 lists the traits of one who is easy for an enemy, and two of them match: he does not know (அறியான்) and does not fit in with others (அமைவிலன்). The chapter on what makes a good enemy to have describes, from the other side, the man 474 says will perish.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:474","b":"k:978","type":"same_image","note":"474 says the one who admires himself (தன்னை வியந்தான்) perishes quickly. 978 uses the same phrase to define smallness: greatness always bows, and smallness adorns itself in self-admiration (சிறுமை அணியுமாம் தன்னை வியந்து). In 978 self-admiration is a mark of character, and in 474 it is a miscalculation of strength with a practical result.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:475","b":"k:667","type":"same_image","note":"475 says even a cart loaded with peacock feathers breaks its axle if too many are piled on (பீலிபெய் சாகாடும் அச்சிறும்). 667 says not to scorn people for their appearance, since there are those who are like the linchpin of a great rolling chariot (உருள்பெருந்தேர்க்கு அச்சாணி அன்னார்). Both use the axle of a vehicle to say that size misleads: the light thing can break it and the small thing holds it.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:475","b":"k:873","type":"develops","note":"475 says a light load breaks the axle when there is too much of it (அப்பண்டம் சால மிகுத்துப் பெயின்). Parimelazhagar takes the verse as an indirect statement that a strong king who makes enemies of many because each is weak loses his strength when they combine. 873 says this outright: one who, alone, takes on the enmity of many (தமியனாய்ப் பல்லார் பகைகொள்பவன்) is more foolish than the mad.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:475","b":"k:889","type":"parallel_domain","note":"475 says that feathers, light as they are, break an axle when piled too high. 889 says that enmity within, though as small as a split sesame seed (எட்பகவன்ன சிறுமைத்தே ஆயினும்), has ruin in it. Both warn against judging a danger by the smallness of its unit, 475 because small things add up and 889 because of where the small thing sits.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:476","b":"k:596","type":"qualifies","note":"596 says that whatever one thinks of, one should think of rising (உள்ளுவதெல்லாம் உயர்வுள்ளல்), and that even if this is thwarted it is as if it were not. 476 says that those who have climbed to the tip of a branch and push beyond it (அஃதிறந்து ஊக்கின்) end their lives. 596 sets no ceiling on aspiration, while 476 sets one on action: there is a point past which striving higher is fatal.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:476","b":"k:858","type":"parallel_domain","note":"476 says pushing on past the tip of the branch (இறந்து ஊக்கின்) ends one's life. 858 uses the same verb of hostility: leaning away from it is gain, and if one pushes on to excel in it (மிகல் ஊக்கின்), ruin pushes on in turn (ஊக்குமாம் கேடு). In both, the drive that the book elsewhere praises becomes the cause of ruin when it is carried past a limit.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:477","b":"k:943","type":"parallel_domain","note":"477 says to give knowing the measure, for that is the way of guarding wealth while bestowing it (அளவறிந்து ஈக அது பொருள் போற்றி வழங்கு நெறி). 943 says to eat knowing the measure once the last meal is digested, for that is the way to carry the body far (அளவறிந்து உண்க அஃது உடம்பு பெற்றான் நெடிதுய்க்கும் ஆறு). The two verses have the same build, a command to know the measure followed by 'that is the way', applied to wealth in one and the body in the other. Both verses are printed with அறவறிந்து in this edition; the reading அளவு is from Parimelazhagar's gloss.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:478","b":"k:463","type":"agrees","note":"478 says a small income does no harm if spending does not exceed it. 463 says those with understanding do not undertake a deed that loses the capital for the sake of gain (ஆக்கம் கருதி முதலிழக்கும் செய்வினை ஊக்கார்). Both put keeping what one has ahead of getting more: 478 for the regular flow of income and spending, 463 for a single venture.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:478","b":"k:512","type":"qualifies","note":"512 says the work should go to one who widens the sources of income (வாரி பெருக்கி) and increases the yield. 478 says that even if the channel of income is narrow (ஆகாறு அளவிட்டிதாயினும்) there is no ruin, as long as the channel of outgo is not wider (போகாறு அகலாக்கடை). 512 values enlarging income, and 478 adds that safety depends on the relation between the two channels and not on the size of the first.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:479","b":"k:947","type":"parallel_domain","note":"479 says the life of one who lives without knowing the measure (அளவறிந்து வாழாதான்) perishes. 947 says that one who eats a great deal without regard to the measure of his digestive fire (தீயளவன்றித் தெரியான் பெரிதுண்ணின்) gets disease without measure (நோய் அளவின்றிப் படும்). Ignoring a measure brings ruin of wealth in one verse and of health in the other. 479 is printed with a corrupted character in அளவறிந்து in this edition.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:480","b":"k:218","type":"qualifies","note":"218 says those who see what is due do not slacken in ஒப்புரவு even in a time without means (இடனில் பருவத்தும் ஒப்புரவிற்கு ஒல்கார்). 480 says ஒப்புரவு that does not weigh the limit of one's means (உளவரை தூக்காத) quickly destroys those means. The Book of Virtue praises not letting scarcity stop one's helpfulness, and the Book of Wealth, speaking of a ruler's resources, makes the weighing of means a condition of it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:481","b":"k:500","type":"parallel_domain","note":"481 says the crow beats the owl by day (பகல்வெல்லும் கூகையைக் காக்கை), and Parimelazhagar notes that the owl is the stronger bird. 500 says a jackal kills a fearless war elephant in mud where its feet sink (காலாழ் களரில் நரியடும்). In both a weaker animal defeats a stronger one through a circumstance, time in 481 and ground in 500, which is how the two adjacent chapters make their cases.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:482","b":"k:617","type":"parallel_domain","note":"482 pictures fortune (திரு) as something that leaves unless tied, and names timely action as the rope (தீராமை ஆர்க்கும் கயிறு). 617 pictures her as living in the effort of the one without sloth (மடியிலான் தாளுளாள் தாமரையினாள்), with her dark elder sister living in sloth. Both treat fortune as a visitor kept by conduct, and they name different conduct: timing in 482, steady effort in 617.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:482","b":"k:1028","type":"tension","note":"482 says that acting in accord with the season (பருவத்தோடு ஒட்ட ஒழுகல்) is the rope that keeps fortune from leaving. 1028 says those who raise their family have no season (குடிசெய்வார்க்கு இல்லை பருவம்), and that idling and thinking of one's dignity ruins it. The two conflict if பருவம் in 1028 means the right moment for action; Parimelazhagar instead takes it there as seasons of heat, rain and cold used as an excuse to put work off, which removes the conflict.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies"},{"a":"k:483","b":"k:675","type":"develops","note":"483 names two conditions that make a hard deed possible, means and time (கருவியான் காலம் அறிந்து). 675 lists five things to think through before acting: resources, means, time, the deed and the place (பொருள் கருவி காலம் வினை இடன்). 675 keeps 483's pair and sets it inside a fuller list.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:484","b":"k:485","type":"develops","note":"484 says that even if one has the world in mind, it comes to hand when one considers the time and acts by the place (ஞாலம் கருதினும் கைகூடும் காலம் கருதி இடத்தாற் செயின்). 485 reverses the same words: those who have the world in mind wait, unshaken, considering the time (காலம் கருதி இருப்பர் கலங்காது ஞாலம் கருதுபவர்). 484 states the condition for acting and 485 its other side, that until the time comes the ambitious do not move.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:484","b":"k:497","type":"develops","note":"484 says the world comes to hand if one considers the time and acts by the place (இடத்தாற் செயின்). 497, in the next chapter, uses the same phrase: one needs no support but fearlessness if one thinks everything through and acts by the place (எண்ணி இடத்தால் செயின்). 484 names place as a second condition beside time, and the following chapter, with 497, takes it up as its own subject.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:484","b":"k:610","type":"parallel_domain","note":"484 says a king who considers time and place can gain even the world (ஞாலம் கருதினும் கைகூடும்). 610 says a king without sloth gains at once all the expanse crossed by the one who measured with his foot (மடியிலா மன்னவன் எய்தும் அடியளந்தான் தாஅயதெல்லாம்). Both promise the whole earth, one to timing and the other to unremitting effort, so the two chapters make the same claim for different qualities.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:485","b":"k:671","type":"qualifies","note":"485 praises waiting for the right time. 671 says deliberation ends in reaching a resolve, and it is bad for that resolve to rest in delay (அத்துணிவு தாழ்ச்சியுள் தங்குதல் தீது). 671 marks the limit of 485: waiting that is fixed on the time (காலம் கருதி) is part of the plan, and holding back after the decision, for no such reason, is a fault.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:485","b":"k:672","type":"agrees","note":"485 says those who aim at the world wait, unshaken, with their mind on the time (காலம் கருதி இருப்பர் கலங்காது). 672 says to take one's time over what should be done slowly (தூங்குக தூங்கிச் செயற்பால) and not to delay what should be done without delay. Both treat waiting as a decision that fits some tasks, and 672 adds the second half that 485 leaves out.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:486","b":"k:827","type":"parallel_domain","note":"486 says the holding back of a person of energy is like a fighting ram stepping back in order to charge (பொருதகர் தாக்கற்குப் பேருந் தகைத்து). 827 says not to trust humble words from an enemy, because the bending of a bow means harm (வில்வணக்கம் தீங்கு குறித்தமையான்). Both use a drawing back that comes before a blow: 486 recommends it to oneself, and 827 teaches one to see it in an opponent.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:487","b":"k:152","type":"tension","note":"152 says always to bear with an excess done to one, and that forgetting it is better still (பொறுத்தல் இறப்பினை என்றும் அதனை மறத்தல் அதனினும் நன்று). 487 says the bright do not flare up outwardly at once but, watching for the time, burn within (காலம் பார்த்து உள்வேர்ப்பர்). Both hold back the outward response, and they differ on what happens inside: 152 wants the wrong forgotten, 487 wants the anger kept. The conflict holds if both are read as general advice; 152 belongs to the householder's virtues and Parimelazhagar takes 487 as addressed to kings facing enemies.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:487","b":"k:301","type":"tension","note":"301 says that the one who truly guards against anger guards it where it can take effect (செல்லிடத்துக் காப்பான் சினங்காப்பான்); where it cannot, guarding or not makes no difference. 487 has the bright hold back anger now, when showing it would not serve, and keep it inside for the right time. On the reading that 487's anger is to be released when it can succeed, it describes the restraint that 301 says does not count, and withholds the one that does.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:488","b":"k:151","type":"tension","note":"151 says that bearing with those who scorn one, as the earth holds up those who dig into it (அகழ்வாரைத் தாங்கும் நிலம்போல), is the highest thing. 488 uses a verb of carrying for the same act, bearing with those who would harm one (சுமக்க), and makes it a means: one bears them until their end comes and their head goes down. The two conflict if both are read as answers to how one should treat those who wrong one, since forbearance is the goal in 151 and a way to the enemy's fall in 488. 151 is addressed to the householder and 488 to a king, which is the other way to read them.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies"},{"a":"k:488","b":"k:879","type":"qualifies","note":"488 says to bear with enemies until their end comes (இறுவரை காணின்). 879 says to cut down a thorn tree while it is young, because once it has hardened it destroys the hand that cuts it (இளைதாக முள்மரம் கொல்க ... காழ்த்த இடத்து). 879 limits 488's patience: waiting serves when time works against the enemy, and not when the enemy is growing stronger.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:489","b":"k:26","type":"develops","note":"26 says the great are those who do what is hard to do, and the small are those who cannot (செயற்கரிய செய்வார் பெரியர்). 489 uses the same phrase and adds a condition of time: hard deeds are to be done at the moment a rare opportunity arrives (செய்தற்கரிய செயல்). 26 places the ability in the person; 489 says it also depends on a moment that is itself hard to come by (எய்தற்கரியது).","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:489","b":"k:333","type":"parallel_domain","note":"489 says that when what is hard to get has come, one should at that very point do what is hard to do (இயைந்தக்கால் அந்நிலையே செய்தற்கரிய செயல்). 333 says wealth has the nature of not staying, and that on getting it one should at once do what stays (அதுபெற்றால் அற்குப ஆங்கே செயல்). Both reason from something that will not last, an opportunity in 489 and wealth in 333, to acting at once, and 333 turns the king's rule of timing toward lasting good.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:490","b":"k:126","type":"parallel_domain","note":"490 says to be like the heron in the season for folding up (கூம்பும் பருவத்து), and like its strike when the moment is right. 126 says that one who can draw in the five senses like a tortoise (ஆமைபோல் ஐந்தடக்கல்) in one birth has protection for seven. Both teach restraint through an animal that draws itself in. The tortoise's withdrawal is the discipline itself, and the heron's is half of a pair that ends in a strike (குத்து).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:490","b":"k:274","type":"parallel_domain","note":"490 holds up the heron, which stays still until the moment and then strikes, as a model for acting on enemies. 274 says that doing wrong under cover of austerity is like a hunter hiding in a bush to snare birds (புதல்மறைந்து வேட்டுவன் புள்சிமிழ்த்தற்று). The pattern of stillness that hides a coming strike is the same in both. It is recommended to the king waiting for his time, and it is the figure of fraud in one who wears the ascetic's appearance.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:491","b":"k:464","type":"develops","note":"464 says those who fear disgrace do not begin what is not clear to them (தெளிவு இலதனைத் தொடங்கார்). 491 gives the same ban on beginning (தொடங்கற்க எவ்வினையும்) a concrete test: until the place has been seen (இடங்கண்ட பின் அல்லது). Read together, knowing the ground is one specific form of the clarity 464 demands before any start.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:491","b":"k:747","type":"same_image","note":"Parimelazhagar reads 491's முற்றும் இடம் as a place from which the enemy can be encircled, and tells the king neither to act nor to scorn the foe before finding one. 747 defines a fort from the other side as what cannot be taken even by encircling (முற்றியும் ... பற்றற்கு அரியது). The shared siege verb shows attacker and defender reasoning about the same ground; Manakkudavar instead reads 491 as 'the place where the task can be completed', which would loosen this link.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:492","b":"k:741","type":"agrees","note":"492 says that even for those with fighting strength (முரண்சேர்ந்த மொய்ம்பினவர்க்கும்) the advantage of joining a fort (அரண்சேர்ந்தாம் ஆக்கம்) yields many gains. 741 says a fort is of value to those able to go on the offensive (ஆற்றுபவர்க்கும்) as well as to those who guard themselves in fear. Both deny that a fort is only for the weak: the chapter on place and the chapter on forts make the same point about the strong.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:493","b":"k:861","type":"qualifies","note":"861 advises avoiding opposition to the stronger (வலியார்க்கு மாறேற்றல் ஓம்புக) and pursuing enmity against the weaker. 493 says the weaker side can still become strong and win when it knows the ground and protects itself. 493 therefore sets a condition under which 861's rule about relative strength does not settle the outcome.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:493","b":"k:878","type":"develops","note":"493 says even those without strength win (ஆற்றாரும் ஆற்றி அடுப) if they know the place and act against foes while guarding themselves (போற்றிச் செயின்). 878 says the enemy's pride dies when one knows the way, builds oneself up and guards oneself (வகையறிந்து தற்செய்து தற்காப்ப). Self-guarding plus knowledge is the common recipe; 878 generalises 493's 'place' to knowing the way of acting.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:494","b":"k:440","type":"parallel_domain","note":"494 says those who had planned (against you) lose their plan (எண்ணியார் எண்ணம் இழப்பர்) when you advance knowing the place and hold close to it. 440 says the enemies' scheme becomes void (ஏதில ஏதிலார் நூல்) when a ruler enjoys what he loves without letting the love be known. In both, the opponent's design is defeated before any fight by removing what it relied on: bad ground in one case, a known weakness in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:495","b":"k:500","type":"develops","note":"495 shows a strong animal losing outside its own element: the crocodile out of water is killed by others. 500 gives the sharper case, where a jackal kills a fearless war elephant in mud where its feet sink (காலாழ் களரில் நரியடும்). Together they state both halves: one's own ground makes one win, and the wrong ground lets even a much weaker creature win.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:496","b":"k:675","type":"develops","note":"496 says chariots with strong wheels do not run on the sea and sea-going ships do not run on land (கடலோடா ... நாவாயும் ஓடா நிலத்து); Manakkudavar takes it to mean that instruments must be made to suit the place. 675 lists means and place together (கருவி ... இடனொடு ஐந்தும்) among the five things to think through before acting. 496 shows why the two items in 675's list cannot be weighed separately: a tool is only a tool in its own medium.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:497","b":"k:534","type":"develops","note":"497 says that one who thinks everything through and acts by the place needs no support but fearlessness (அஞ்சாமை அல்லால் துணைவேண்டா). 534 says the fearful have no fortress (அச்சமுடையார்க்கு அரணில்லை): Parimelazhagar explains that forest or hill forts are of no use to a frightened mind. The pair shows the two sides of one claim: place is enough given courage, and place is worthless without it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:497","b":"k:875","type":"qualifies","note":"497 says no ally is needed (துணைவேண்டா) beyond fearlessness when the plan is complete and the ground right. 875 advises one who has no ally and two enemies (தன்துணை இன்றால் பகையிரண்டால்) to make one of the enemies a pleasing ally. 875 shows that the book does not treat being without allies as generally safe; 497's claim holds only under its stated condition of complete planning and the right place.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:498","b":"k:473","type":"parallel_domain","note":"473 says many who did not know their own strength pressed on out of zeal and broke midway (ஊக்கத்தின் ஊக்கி இடைக்கண் முரிந்தார்). 498 says the large army's zeal (ஊக்கம்) is destroyed when it enters the small army's ground. In both, drive that outruns a sober measure, of one's strength in one chapter and of the ground in the other, is what gets broken.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:498","b":"k:744","type":"develops","note":"498 says the drive of a commander with a large army is destroyed (உறுபடையான் ஊக்கம் அழிந்து விடும்) if he enters ground suited to a small army. 744 defines a fort as having little to guard and much room, and as destroying the drive of the foe who comes against it (உறுபகை ஊக்கம் அழிப்பது அரண்). The shared phrase shows that a fort is the built form of 498's principle: ground chosen so that numbers stop counting.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:499","b":"k:495","type":"develops","note":"495 gives the image: the crocodile wins in its own deep water. 499 states the human case without an image: even people with no fortress and no great standing (சிறைநலனும் சீரும் இலரெனினும்) are hard to engage on the land where they live (உறைநிலத்தோடு ஒட்டல் அரிது). 499 applies the crocodile's lesson to an invader's estimate of a weak-looking people.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:501","b":"k:513","type":"develops","note":"501 names four tests, virtue, wealth, pleasure and fear for life (அறம்பொருள் இன்பம் உயிரச்சம்), by which a person is examined before being trusted (தேறப்படும்). 513 names four qualities, love, understanding, clarity and freedom from desire, in whoever has which trust lies (நன்குடையான் கட்டே தெளிவு). The first four are trials applied from outside (Parimelazhagar describes staged temptations); the second four are the traits such trials are meant to find.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:501","b":"k:690","type":"develops","note":"501's fourth test is fear for one's life (உயிரச்சம்). 690 defines the envoy as one who, even if it brings his end (இறுதி பயப்பினும்), does not fall short in securing his lord's good. The envoy chapter shows what passing that fourth test looks like in service.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:502","b":"k:133","type":"qualifies","note":"502 makes birth in a good family (குடிப்பிறந்து) a ground for trust alongside freedom from fault. 133 says having good conduct is itself good birth, and a lapse in conduct becomes low birth (ஒழுக்கம் உடைமை குடிமை இழுக்கம் இழிந்த பிறப்பாய் விடும்). On 133's definition, 502's first criterion cannot be checked apart from its second: family does not vouch for a person whose conduct fails.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:502","b":"k:794","type":"parallel_domain","note":"502 places trust in one born of good family, free of faults, with a shame that recoils from blemish (குடிப்பிறந்து ... வடுப்பரியும் நாணுடையான்). 794 says the friendship of one born of good family who is ashamed of blame on himself (குடிப்பிறந்து தன்கண் பழிநாணுவானை) should be had even at a price. The same two criteria, birth and shame, are used to choose a king's officer and to choose a friend.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:502","b":"k:951","type":"develops","note":"502 lists good family first and shame last among the grounds for trust. 951 says integrity and shame are found together by nature (இயல்பாகச் செப்பமும் நாணும் ஒருங்கு) only in those born to a good house. 951 supplies the reasoning 502 leaves unstated: family is asked for because it is taken to be where shame comes naturally.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:503","b":"k:373","type":"agrees","note":"503 says hard learning and freedom from fault still leave some ignorance when one looks closely. 373 says that however many subtle books one studies, one's own inherent understanding still prevails (தன் உண்மை அறிவே மிகும்). Both set a limit on what study can change in a person, one in the chapter on choosing officers and one in the chapter on fate.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:503","b":"k:504","type":"develops","note":"503 observes that even in those who have learned hard things and are free of fault, it is rare on examination to find no blankness (இன்மை அரிதே வெளிறு). 504 gives the rule that follows: examine the qualities and the faults and judge by what predominates (மிகைநாடி மிக்க கொளல்). Parimelazhagar on 504 makes the bridge explicit, saying no one in the world has only qualities or only faults.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:504","b":"k:793","type":"parallel_domain","note":"504 tells the king to examine a person's qualities and faults (குணம்நாடிக் குற்றமும் நாடி) and take whichever predominates. 793 tells one to know a person's qualities, family, faults and kin (குணமும் குடிமையும் குற்றமும் ... இனனும்) before forming a friendship. The same inventory is used in both domains, but only 504 says how to decide when the inventory is mixed.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:504","b":"k:965","type":"qualifies","note":"504 lets the predominant side decide what a person is. 965 says even those like a hill are diminished if they do a lowering thing the size of a kundri seed (குன்றி அனைய செயின்). 965 names a kind of fault, the degrading act, that is not outweighed by the mass of a person's merits, which limits the simple weighing of 504.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:505","b":"k:986","type":"same_image","note":"505 says a person's own deeds are the touchstone (கருமமே கட்டளைக் கல்) of greatness and smallness. 986 asks what the touchstone of worth is (சால்பிற்குக் கட்டளை யாதெனின்) and answers: accepting defeat even from those not one's equal. Both use the assayer's stone for testing people; 505 names the general test, deeds, and 986 picks one particular deed as the decisive one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:506","b":"k:350","type":"same_image","note":"506 treats having no attachment (பற்றிலர்) as a reason not to trust a person, since such a one does not fear blame. 350 tells the renouncer to hold to the one without attachment (பற்றற்றான்) in order to let attachment go. The same word is a disqualification in the chapter on choosing officers and the goal in the chapter on renunciation; the contexts differ, so this is a contrast of valuation across the books and not a contradiction.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:506","b":"k:502","type":"develops","note":"502 puts trust in one who has a shame that recoils from blemish (நாணுடையான்). 506 warns against trusting those who are cut off (அற்றார்), because they have no attachment and are not ashamed of blame (பற்றிலர் நாணார் பழி); Parimelazhagar takes அற்றார் as those without kin, Manakkudavar as those without conduct. The two verses are the positive and negative of one criterion, and 506 adds a cause: shame depends on having ties.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:507","b":"k:515","type":"agrees","note":"507 says trusting the ignorant with affection as the ground (காதன்மை கந்தா அறிவறியார்த் தேறுதல்) yields every kind of folly. 515 says work is not to be assigned to someone because he is dear (சிறந்தானென்று ஏவற்பாற்றன்று), only to one who knows, endures and can do it. Both exclude affection as a ground of appointment, one at the stage of trust and the other at the stage of assigning work.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:507","b":"k:836","type":"develops","note":"507 warns that trusting those without understanding out of fondness brings all folly on the one who trusts. 836 describes the result from the other side: when a fool who does not know how takes up a task (கையறியாப் பேதை வினைமேற் கொளின்), it fails and he ends in fetters. 836 shows the outcome that 507's warning anticipates.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:508","b":"k:510","type":"develops","note":"508 and 510 share their closing line (தீரா இடும்பை தரும்) and their first fault, trusting without examining. 510 adds a second fault of equal weight: doubting one who has been trusted (தெளிந்தான்கண் ஐயுறவும்). Read after 508, 510 shows that the chapter's warning is not simply 'trust less': misplaced doubt is punished in the same words as misplaced trust.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:508","b":"k:792","type":"parallel_domain","note":"508: one who trusts an outsider without examining (தேரான் பிறனைத் தெளிந்தான்) gets trouble that does not end, extending to his descendants (வழிமுறை தீரா இடும்பை). 792: the friendship of one who does not inquire again and again ends in a grief that kills him (கடைமுறை தான்சாம் துயரம்). The same structure, unexamined acceptance followed by lasting harm, is stated for a king's officer and for a friend.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:509","b":"k:518","type":"develops","note":"509 says trust no one without examining, and after examining, trust what is to be trusted (தேர்ந்தபின் தேறுக தேறும் பொருள்). 518 says that after examining a person's fitness for the work (வினைக்குரிமை நாடிய பின்றை), make him fully entitled to it (அதற்குரியனாகச் செயல்). 518 turns 509's second half into an act: the trust that follows examination is shown by handing over real authority.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:509","b":"k:588","type":"qualifies","note":"509 says that once a person has been examined, what is to be trusted should be trusted. 588 says even what a spy has found and brought back should be checked through another spy (மற்றுமோர் ஒற்றினால் ஒற்றிக் கொளல்). For intelligence reports the book keeps verifying after the agent is chosen, which bears on 509's phrase தேறும் பொருள்: Manakkudavar reads it as the matters in which the person is reliable, not everything.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:509","b":"k:791","type":"parallel_domain","note":"509 orders examination first and commitment after. 791 says nothing ruins like befriending without inquiry, because for those who keep friendship there is no letting go once it is made (நட்டபின் வீடில்லை). Friendship follows the same sequence as appointment, and 791 states the reason the order matters: the commitment is hard to reverse.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:510","b":"k:519","type":"agrees","note":"510 says doubting one who has been trusted (தெளிந்தான்கண் ஐயுறவு) brings endless trouble. 519 says fortune leaves the one who thinks otherwise of the closeness of a man devoted to his work (கேண்மை வேறாக நினைப்பானை நீங்கும் திரு). Both make the ruler's unfounded suspicion of a proven servant a cause of his own loss.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:510","b":"k:693","type":"parallel_domain","note":"510 speaks to the ruler: doubt of one already trusted brings trouble that does not end. 693 speaks to the courtier: guard against grave faults, because once suspicion has arisen it is hard for anyone to clear it (கடுத்தபின் தேற்றுதல் யார்க்கும் அரிது). The two verses describe the same event, a ruler's suspicion, as lasting damage from each side of the relationship.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:511","b":"k:422","type":"parallel_domain","note":"511 says the man to employ is one whose nature, having looked into good and bad (நன்மையும் தீமையும் நாடி), has taken to the good (நலம்புரிந்த தன்மையான்). 422 defines understanding as not letting the mind go wherever it goes but turning it from bad to good (தீதொரீஇ நன்றின்பால் உய்ப்பது). The test for a king's officer is the book's general definition of அறிவு applied to a person at work.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:511","b":"k:504","type":"develops","note":"504 tells the king to examine a man's qualities and faults and judge by what predominates (குணம்நாடிக் குற்றமும் நாடி ... மிகைநாடி). 511 uses the same verb நாடி, but now it is the candidate who examines good and bad and settles on the good. The king's weighing of the man in chapter 51 becomes, in chapter 52, a check on how the man himself weighs.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:513","b":"k:682","type":"agrees","note":"513 places trust in one who has love, understanding, clarity and freedom from desire (அன்பறிவு தேற்றம் அவாவின்மை). 682 opens with the same pair, அன்பறிவு, and adds considered eloquence as the three things an envoy cannot lack. The two lists share a core of loyalty and knowledge and then differ by office: the officer handling affairs needs steadiness and no greed, the envoy needs speech.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:514","b":"k:505","type":"develops","note":"505 makes each person's own deeds the touchstone of greatness and smallness (தத்தம் கருமமே கட்டளைக் கல்). 514 says tests of every kind before the appointment still leave many who change once in the work. Both put the decisive evidence in action, and 514 adds that the action that counts is the work itself, after trust has been given.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:514","b":"k:510","type":"qualifies","note":"510 says that doubting a man one has examined and trusted (தெளிந்தான்கண் ஐயுறவு) brings endless trouble. 514 says that even when tested in every way (எனைவகையான் தேறியக் கண்ணும்), many people turn different through the nature of the work (வினைவகையான் வேறாகும்). Read together, 514 limits 510: testing does not settle the matter, so the ban on doubt cannot mean a ban on watching how the man changes in office.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:514","b":"k:520","type":"develops","note":"514 observes that many who passed every test turn different through the work (வினைவகையான் வேறாகும் மாந்தர் பலர்). 520 tells the king to look into the one doing the work every day (நாடோறும் நாடுக மன்னன்). The chapter's closing instruction answers the problem 514 raises: since office changes people, examination has to continue after appointment.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:515","b":"k:640","type":"develops","note":"515 requires for a task one who knows and endures and can carry it through (அறிந்தாற்றிச் செய்கிற்பாற்கு). 640 says that those without practical ability (திறப்பாடு இலாதவர்), though they plan in proper order, do things that never reach completion. 640 shows what 515's last requirement guards against: knowing is not enough without the capacity to finish.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:516","b":"k:675","type":"develops","note":"516 asks the king to examine the doer, examine the deed, and act seeing how they fit the time (செய்வானை நாடி வினைநாடிக் காலத்தோடு எய்த உணர்ந்து செயல்). 675 lists five things to think through before acting: resources, means, time, the deed and the place (பொருள்கருவி காலம் வினையிடனொடு ஐந்தும்). Deed and time are in both lists; 516 adds the one item 675 lacks, the person who will do it, which is the concern of a chapter on employing people.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:516","b":"k:949","type":"parallel_domain","note":"516: examine the doer, the deed and the fit with the time, then act (காலத்தோடு எய்த உணர்ந்து செயல்). 949: the trained physician acts after considering the measure of the patient, the measure of the illness and the time (உற்றான் அளவும் பிணியளவும் காலமும் ... கருதிச் செயல்). Both end in செயல் after a three-part assessment of person, matter and time, the same procedure in statecraft and in medicine.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:517","b":"k:510","type":"agrees","note":"517 says that once the king has reasoned 'this man will finish this by this means', he should leave it with him (அதனை அவன்கண் விடல்). 510 says that doubting one who has been examined and trusted brings endless trouble. Leaving the task with the man is the practical form of 510's rule: examination comes first and is then followed by a real handing over.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:517","b":"k:520","type":"qualifies","note":"517 tells the king to leave the task to the chosen man (அவன்கண் விடல்). 520, in the same chapter, tells the king to look into the one doing the work daily (நாடோறும் நாடுக), because the world stays straight only if he does. The delegation in 517 is therefore not a withdrawal of attention; Parimelazhagar's note on 520 says the checking should be done privately so that the man's standing is not damaged.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:519","b":"k:700","type":"qualifies","note":"519 warns the king against taking amiss the familiarity of a hard-working officer (வினைக்கண் வினையுடையான் கேண்மை). 700, addressed to the courtier, says that familiarity which does improper things on the thought 'we are old associates' (பழையம் எனக்கருதிப் பண்பல்ல செய்யும் கெழுதகைமை) brings ruin. The king must tolerate the liberty and the servant must not presume on it; each verse limits the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:519","b":"k:808","type":"parallel_domain","note":"519 says fortune leaves the one who thinks otherwise of the closeness of a man devoted to the work (கேண்மை வேறாக நினைப்பானை நீங்கும் திரு). 808 praises those with the strength of intimacy not to listen to talk of a friend's lapse (கேளிழுக்கம் கேளாக் கெழுதகைமை). The duty not to reinterpret a close person's conduct appears in kingship and in friendship; Parimelazhagar reads 519 as the king being turned by the words of those who resent the officer, which is the situation 808 names.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:520","b":"k:546","type":"same_image","note":"520 says that if the one doing the work does not bend, the world will not bend (கோடாமை கோடாது உலகு). 546 says victory comes from the king's sceptre, provided it does not bend (கோலதூஉங் கோடாதெனின்). கோடாமை, not bending, is the mark of the just sceptre, and 520 applies the same word to the officer, on whose straightness the world's straightness is made to depend.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:520","b":"k:553","type":"agrees","note":"520: let the king look daily into the one doing his work (நாடோறும் நாடுக மன்னன்). 553: the king who does not look daily and do justice loses his land daily (நாடொறும் நாடி முறைசெய்யா மன்னவன் நாடொறும் நாடு கெடும்). The same phrase of daily examination is used for officers and for justice, and both tie the state of the land to that routine.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:520","b":"k:584","type":"develops","note":"520 requires the king to examine the worker daily. 584 defines the spy as one who examines everyone, beginning with those who do the king's work (வினைசெய்வார்), then his kin and his enemies. 584 supplies the means by which the daily examination of 520 is carried out.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:521","b":"k:806","type":"parallel_domain","note":"521: kin keep honouring old ties (பழைமை) even when all is lost. 806: those who keep within the bounds do not give up, even in ruin (தொலைவிடத்தும்), the bond of those who have stood with them from of old (தொல்லைக்கண் நின்றார் தொடர்பு). Loyalty to a long tie when fortune is gone is claimed as the mark of kin in one chapter and of true old friends in the other.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:521","b":"k:1047","type":"tension","note":"521 says that honouring the old tie even when a man's means are gone (பற்றற்ற கண்ணும் பழைமை பாராட்டுதல்) is found among kin. 1047 says that one in poverty will be looked on as a stranger even by the mother who bore him (ஈன்ற தாயானும் பிறன்போல நோக்கப்படும்). The conflict holds if 1047 is read as Pope reads it, of poverty in general; the verse's own qualifier அறஞ்சாரா, poverty unconnected with virtue, which Parimelazhagar and Subramuniyaswami keep, narrows it and reduces the conflict.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:522","b":"k:884","type":"qualifies","note":"522 says that if a man gets kin whose love does not lapse (விருப்பறாச் சுற்றம் இயையின்), they give many gains (ஆக்கம் பலவும் தரும்). 884 says that if hidden enmity of unreformed mind appears, it gives many ills that turn his circle bad (இனமாணா ஏதம் பலவும் தரும்). The two verses have the same shape with opposite content, and Parimelazhagar notes that 'whose love does not lapse' in 522 is said to exclude the inner enmity of chapter 89: kin are an asset only on that condition.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:524","b":"k:837","type":"develops","note":"524 says the fruit of having gained wealth is to live surrounded by one's kin (சுற்றத்தால் சுற்றப்பட ஒழுகல்). 837 says that when a fool gets great wealth, strangers eat their fill and his own people go hungry (ஏதிலார் ஆரத் தமர்பசிப்பர்). 837 shows the failure of exactly the use 524 names: wealth that does not reach one's own people has missed its purpose.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:526","b":"k:525","type":"develops","note":"525 names giving and pleasant speech as what surrounds a man with kin. 526 keeps giving, now great giving (பெருங்கொடை), and replaces pleasant speech with not cherishing anger (பேணான் வெகுளி), and raises the result to a following unmatched on earth. The pair is not a repetition: the second verse moves from a positive manner to the removal of what drives people away.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:527","b":"k:227","type":"agrees","note":"527 ties gain (ஆக்கம்) to those who, like the crow, call others before eating. 227 says the disease of hunger does not touch one who is habituated to sharing his food (பாத்தூண் மரீஇயவனை). Both hold that sharing food protects the sharer's own fortune, stated in the Book of Virtue as freedom from hunger and here as prosperity.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:527","b":"k:1035","type":"same_image","note":"527 praises the crow for not hiding (கரவா) its food and for calling others to eat. 1035 says those who live by eating what their own hands produce do not beg and give to those who ask without hiding (கரவாது). Not concealing what one has, கரவு being the book's word for the withholder's fault in chapters 106 and 107, is the shared mark of the crow and of the farmer.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:528","b":"k:542","type":"same_image","note":"528 ends: many will live looking to that (அதுநோக்கி வாழ்வார் பலர்). 542 says the world lives looking to the sky and the people live looking to the king's sceptre (கோல்நோக்கி வாழுங் குடி). The phrase 'live looking to' makes the king's conduct something people depend on as they depend on rain; 528 uses it for his recognition of each person's worth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:528","b":"k:972","type":"develops","note":"528 says that if the king looks at people not in common but according to rank (பொதுநோக்கான் வேந்தன் வரிசையா நோக்கின்), many will live looking to that. 972 says birth is alike for all, but distinction is not alike, because of the difference in what people do (சிறப்பொவ்வா செய்தொழில் வேற்றுமையான்). 972 gives a basis for the differential regard of 528: Parimelazhagar reads வரிசை there as each person's merit, and 972 locates such difference in conduct, not birth.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:529","b":"k:530","type":"qualifies","note":"529 says estranged kin return of themselves once the cause of discord is gone. 530 turns to one who left and has come back for a reason (காரணத்தின் வந்தானை) and tells the king to attend to it and accept him only after reflection (இழைத்திருந்து எண்ணிக் கொளல்). The pair separates two kinds of return, one that follows from the king's own correction and one prompted by the returner's interest, and prescribes welcome for the first and caution for the second.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:529","b":"k:633","type":"develops","note":"529 says those who were one's own and left will return to kinship when the cause of the discord is absent (அமராமைக் காரணம் இன்றி வரும்). 633 lists among a minister's abilities reuniting those who have parted (பிரிந்தார்ப் பொருத்தல்). 529 says how that reunion comes about: the ground of the estrangement has to be removed, which Parimelazhagar takes as a fault on the king's own side.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:530","b":"k:509","type":"develops","note":"509 gives the rule: trust no one without examining, then trust (தேறற்க யாரையும் தேராது). 530 applies examination to a man who was already one's own, left, and came back for a reason: he is to be received after reflection (எண்ணிக் கொளல்). Leaving resets the earlier trust, so the test of chapter 51 has to be done again.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:530","b":"k:830","type":"parallel_domain","note":"530 says the king should attend to the reason for which a man who left has come back, wait, and accept him after thinking it over (இழைத்திருந்து எண்ணிக் கொளல்). 830 says that when an enemy turns friend, one should be friendly in face and withhold the heart (முகநட்டு அகநட்பு ஒரீஇ விடல்). Both deal with someone who comes over after having been apart and both withhold immediate trust, but 530 ends in acceptance of the kinsman while 830 ends in letting the former enemy go.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:531","b":"k:539","type":"develops","note":"531 states the danger: slackness from the elation of great joy is worse than excessive anger. 539 gives the practice against it: when one grows strong in one's own joy (மகிழ்ச்சியின் மைந்துறும் போழ்து), call to mind those ruined by negligence (இகழ்ச்சியின் கெட்டாரை உள்ளுக).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:532","b":"k:601","type":"parallel_domain","note":"532 says heedlessness kills fame (பொச்சாப்புக் கொல்லும் புகழை). 601 says the undimmed lamp called family goes out when the grime called sloth creeps over it (மடியென்னும் மாசூர மாய்ந்து கெடும்). In both, an inner lapse, not an outside enemy, destroys a standing built up over time.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:532","b":"k:1046","type":"develops","note":"532 uses as its comparison that constant poverty kills understanding (அறிவினை நிச்ச நிரப்புக் கொன்றாங்கு). 1046 describes one form of it: even when the poor speak good matter well understood, their words and meaning go slack (சொற்பொருள் சோர்வு படும்); Parimelazhagar says nobody listens, so their learning yields nothing. Read together, the 'killing' of understanding in 532 includes its losing all effect in the world.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:534","b":"k:430","type":"parallel_domain","note":"430 says those without understanding have nothing, whatever they possess (என்னுடைய ரேனும் இலர்). 534 says the heedless have no good (பொச்சாப் புடையார்க்கு நன்கு இல்லை), which Parimelazhagar reads as: even with every kind of wealth, they get no benefit from it. The same logic of having without having is applied to lack of understanding in one verse and to lack of vigilance in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:534","b":"k:741","type":"tension","note":"534 says the fearful have no fortress (அச்ச முடையார்க்கு அரணில்லை). 741 says a fort is of value both to those who march out and to those who, in fear, protect themselves (அஞ்சித் தற்போற்றுபவர்க்கும் பொருள்). The conflict holds if 534's first half is taken as a plain claim about forts and the fearful; Parimelazhagar's readings ease it, since he takes 534 as fear in the mind making a fort useless and 741 as the weaker side prudently taking refuge.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:534","b":"k:750","type":"agrees","note":"750 says a fort, whatever its excellences, is as nothing with those who lack excellence in action (வினைமாட்சி இல்லார்கண் இல்லது அரண்). 534 says the fearful have no fort and the heedless have no good (நன்கு). Both hold that an outward advantage counts for nothing without the inner quality needed to use it, and both use the fort to say so.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:535","b":"k:429","type":"agrees","note":"429 gives the positive case: those whose understanding guards against what is coming (எதிரதாக் காக்கும் அறிவினார்) meet no affliction that shakes them. 535 gives the negative: one who did not guard beforehand later grieves over his own fault (தன்பிழை பின்னூறு இரங்கி விடும்). Together they show foresight treated as part of understanding and its absence as heedlessness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:535","b":"k:655","type":"develops","note":"655 tells one not to do deeds over which one will grieve, saying 'what have I done' (எற்றென்று இரங்குவ செய்யற்க). 535 uses the same verb, இரங்கு, for the grief of one who failed to guard in advance. The pairing shows regret following from omission in 535 as it follows from commission in 655.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:536","b":"k:582","type":"develops","note":"536 says nothing equals unfailing vigilance toward everyone at all times (யார்மாட்டும் என்றும்). 582 says it is the king's work to know quickly everything that happens to everyone at all times (எல்லார்க்கும் எல்லாம் நிகழ்பவை எஞ்ஞான்றும்). The same universal scope appears in both, and 582 gives the general vigilance of 536 a concrete form in intelligence gathering.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:537","b":"k:611","type":"agrees","note":"611 says one must not lose heart on the thought that a thing is hard (அருமை உடைத்தென்று அசாவாமை வேண்டும்), since effort gives the greatness needed. 537 says nothing is beyond doing as 'hard' (அரிய என்று ஆகாத இல்லை) when done with unforgetting care. Each denies that difficulty is a reason to give up, and each names its own remedy: effort in 611, sustained attention in 537.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:538","b":"k:466","type":"develops","note":"466 says one is ruined by not doing what is fit to do (செய்தக்க செய்யாமை யானுங் கெடும்). 538 says what has been praised must be carefully done, and those who slight it and do not do it (செய்யாது இகழ்ந்தார்க்கு) have nothing through seven births. 538 takes the omission half of 466 and extends its cost beyond this life.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:538","b":"k:835","type":"parallel_domain","note":"835 says a fool can make, in one birth, the mire he will sink into for seven (எழுமையும் தான்புக் கழுந்தும் அளறு). 538 says those who neglect what is praised have no good through seven births (எழுமையும் இல்). Both attach a seven-birth consequence to conduct in this life, folly in one and neglect of duty in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:539","b":"k:250","type":"parallel_domain","note":"250 says: when you move against those weaker than you, think of yourself before those stronger (வலியார்முன் தன்னை நினைக்க). 539 says: when you grow strong in your own joy, think of those ruined by negligence (இகழ்ச்சியின் கெட்டாரை உள்ளுக). Both prescribe a deliberate act of recollection at the moment of feeling powerful, for the sake of compassion in 250 and of vigilance in 539.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:539","b":"k:930","type":"parallel_domain","note":"930 asks whether a drinker, when sober and seeing another man drunk (களித்தானைக் காணுங்கால்), does not reflect on the slackness of his own drinking (உள்ளான்கொல் உண்டதன் சோர்வு). 539 tells one elated with joy to reflect (உள்ளுக) on those whom negligence ruined. Both correct an intoxicated state by looking at its effect in other people, and 930's word சோர்வு is the one 531 uses for the slackness of joy.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:551","b":"k:552","type":"develops","note":"551 compares the oppressive king to murderers and finds him worse. 552 compares the king who demands wealth to a man standing with a spear saying 'hand it over' (வேலொடு நின்றான் இடுவென்றது). Together the two opening verses of the chapter set the unjust ruler beside common criminals, first the killer and then the robber.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:552","b":"k:756","type":"qualifies","note":"756 names what is the king's wealth: what comes unowned, customs duty, and tribute from enemies (உறுபொருளும் உல்கு பொருளும் தன் ஒன்னார்த் தெறுபொருளும்). 552 treats a sceptre-holder's 'asking' (இரவு) of his people as highway robbery; Parimelazhagar notes that the word இரவு marks it as something other than the due tax. 756 shows the lawful sources against which 552's demand is measured.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:554","b":"k:381","type":"develops","note":"381 lists the six things a king must have, among them people and resources (படைகுடி கூழ்...). 554 says a king who bends the sceptre and acts without reflection loses resources and people together (கூழுங் குடியும் ஒருங்கிழக்கும்). The two things injustice costs him in 554 are two of the six that make a king in 381.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:554","b":"k:568","type":"parallel_domain","note":"568 says the fortune of a king who does not think things through with his circle and then rages in anger will shrink (எண்ணாத வேந்தன் ... சிறுகும் திரு). 554 says a king who acts without reflection (சூழாது) and bends the sceptre loses wealth and people. In both, the king's loss is traced to acting without deliberation, shown in anger in 568 and in injustice in 554.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:555","b":"k:567","type":"same_image","note":"567 says harsh words and punishment beyond bounds are a file that wears down a king's conquering strength (அடுமுரண் தேய்க்கும் அரம்). 555 says the tears of the afflicted are a tool that wears down wealth (செல்வத்தைத் தேய்க்கும் படை). Both describe the effect of a king's cruelty as slow abrasion by a tool, using the same verb தேய்க்கும்.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:555","b":"k:828","type":"same_image","note":"828 says a weapon hides within an enemy's folded hands, and his shed tears are the same (படையொடுங்கும் ... அழுத கண்ணீரும் அனைத்து). 555 calls the tears shed by the afflicted the weapon that wears away wealth (அழுத கண்ணீர் ... படை). Both join the same two words, tears and weapon: in 828 the tears conceal a weapon, and in 555 the tears are the weapon.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:556","b":"k:563","type":"agrees","note":"563 says a king of cruel sceptre who goes on doing what terrifies will certainly and quickly perish (ஒருவந்தம் ஒல்லைக் கெடும்). 556 says kings endure through just rule (மன்னர்க்கு மன்னுதல் செங்கோன்மை) and without it their lustre does not endure. Both tie how long a king lasts to how he rules; Parimelazhagar takes what endures in 556 to be the king's fame.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:557","b":"k:1192","type":"parallel_domain","note":"In 1192 a woman says that the tender care a lover gives the one who loves him (வீழ்வார் அளிக்கும் அளி) is like the sky giving rain to those who live by it (வாழ்வார்க்கு வானம் பயந்தற்று). 557 says a king's lack of அளி is to living beings what lack of rain is to the earth. The same word அளி and the same rain comparison describe dependence on a ruler in one book and on a beloved in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:559","b":"k:55","type":"parallel_domain","note":"55 says that when a wife who worships her husband says 'rain', it rains (பெய்யெனப் பெய்யும் மழை). 559 says that when a king bends justice, the sky will not rain (ஒல்லாது வானம் பெயல்). In both, rain responds to a person's keeping or failing the duty of their station, the wife's in the household and the king's in the realm.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:559","b":"k:239","type":"parallel_domain","note":"239 says land that bears a body without renown declines in its blameless rich yield (வண்பயன் குன்றும்). 559 says the sky holds back rain when the king bends justice, and the next verse, 560, uses the same verb for cows' yield (ஆபயன் குன்றும்). In each, nature's yield falls in response to a human moral failing, an individual's in 239 and a ruler's in 559.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:560","b":"k:19","type":"parallel_domain","note":"19 says that if the sky does not give, giving and austerity will not remain in the wide world (தானம் தவம்இரண்டும் தங்கா). 560 says that if the guardian does not guard, cows' yield declines and the six-occupation men forget their book. In both, religious and learned life depends on a provider, the rain in 19 and the king in 560, and fails when the provider fails.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:561","b":"k:579","type":"qualifies","note":"561 makes the king the one who punishes in proportion (ஒத்தாங்கு ஒறுப்பது வேந்து), while 579 calls it the highest quality to show kindly regard and bear with even those whose way is to hurt (ஒறுத்தாற்றும் பண்பினார் கண்ணும் ... பொறுத்தாற்றும் பண்பே தலை). The same root ஒறு sits on opposite sides. Parimelazhagar separates the cases: 561 concerns wrongs done to weaker subjects, 579 wrongs done to the ruler himself, so forbearance is limited to injuries that are the ruler's own to forgive.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:562","b":"k:550","type":"qualifies","note":"562 asks for a light stroke after a stern show (மெல்ல எறிக); Manakkudavar takes this to mean the penalty should fall short of what the offence deserves. 550 says a king's punishing the cruel with death (கொலையிற் கொடியாரை வேந்தொறுத்தல்) is like weeding the young crop. The pairing marks where gentleness stops: for those 550 calls கொடியார், the book accepts the severest penalty.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:562","b":"k:567","type":"develops","note":"562 tells those who want lasting prosperity to raise the rod sharply but strike gently (கடிதோச்சி மெல்ல எறிக). 567 names what happens when the stroke is not gentle: punishment past the bound (கையிகந்த தண்டம்), with harsh words, is a file that wears down the king's strength. 562 gives the practice and 567 the cost of abandoning it; the 'long prosperity' (நெடிதாக்கம்) of one is the 'conquering strength' (அடுமுரண்) eroded in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:563","b":"k:548","type":"agrees","note":"563 says a cruel-sceptred king who keeps doing what terrifies (வெருவந்த செய்தொழுகும் வெங்கோலன்) will certainly and quickly perish (ஒருவந்தம் ஒல்லைக் கெடும்). 548 says a king who is not easy of access and does not inquire and do justice will sink and perish of himself (தானே கெடும்). Neither verse needs an enemy to bring the king down: in both the misrule is itself the cause of the fall.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:564","b":"k:386","type":"agrees","note":"386 says that if a king is easy to see and not harsh of speech, his land is spoken of above others (மீக்கூறும் மன்னன் நிலம்). 564 gives the reverse case: where people say 'our lord is harsh' (இறைகடியன்), the king quickly perishes. Both verses measure a reign by what is said about it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:564","b":"k:555","type":"develops","note":"564 says the king of whom the painful words 'our lord is harsh' (இறைகடியன் என்றுரைக்கும் இன்னாச்சொல்) are spoken will quickly perish. 555 says the tears of those who cannot bear their affliction are the weapon that wears away wealth (செல்வத்தைத் தேய்க்கும் படை). In both, what destroys the ruler comes from the subjects' suffering, as speech in one verse and as tears in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:565","b":"k:1000","type":"parallel_domain","note":"565 says the great wealth (பெருஞ்செல்வம்) of an inaccessible, grim-faced man is as if seen by a demon; Parimelazhagar explains that no one approaches him, so the wealth is of use neither to him nor to others. 1000 says the great wealth (பெருஞ்செல்வம்) gained by one without courtesy (பண்பிலான்) is like good milk spoiled by its vessel. Outside the court the same judgment holds: the holder's manner ruins wealth that is sound in itself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:566","b":"k:577","type":"develops","note":"566 says that if the ruler is harsh of speech and 'eyeless' (கண்ணிலன்), his long-standing wealth perishes at once; Parimelazhagar takes கண் here to stand for கண்ணோட்டம். 577, in the next chapter, states the equation directly: those without kindly regard have no eyes (கண்ணோட்டம் இல்லவர் கண்ணிலர்). The single word in 566 is the idea the following chapter spells out.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:567","b":"k:888","type":"same_image","note":"567 calls harsh words and excessive punishment a file (அரம்) that wears down (தேய்க்கும்) the king's conquering strength. 888 says a family with enmity inside it wears away (தேயும்) like metal rubbed by a file (அரம்பொருத பொன்போல), its strength ground down. In both the file stands for slow loss of strength from within, by the king's own conduct in one and by a house's own members in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:568","b":"k:306","type":"parallel_domain","note":"306 says anger, the fire that kills what it touches, burns even the safe raft called one's people (இனமென்னும் ஏமப் புணையைச் சுடும்). 568 shows this at court: the king who neglects his circle (இனம்) and then turns his anger on them (சினத்தாற்றிச் சீறின்) loses his fortune; Parimelazhagar adds that the frightened ministers leave him. The general warning in the book of virtue becomes a specific danger for a ruler.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:569","b":"k:435","type":"parallel_domain","note":"435 says the life of one who does not guard before a fault arrives (வருமுன்னர்க் காவாதான்) perishes like straw before fire. 569 applies the same logic to war: the king who has not prepared his defence beforehand is overtaken when the battle arrives (செருவந்த போழ்தில்) and perishes. Both place the failure in the time before the danger, not in the moment of it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:569","b":"k:534","type":"qualifies","note":"569 says a king who has made no defence (சிறைசெய்யா வேந்தன்) will be seized by fear when war comes and quickly perish. 534 says that for the fearful there is no fort (அச்ச முடையார்க்கு அரணில்லை); Parimelazhagar explains that defences are no use to a frightened mind. One verse says lack of a fort produces fear, the other that fear cancels a fort, so the fort is necessary but not sufficient.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:570","b":"k:447","type":"develops","note":"447 asks who can ruin a ruler who keeps as his own those who will rebuke him (இடிக்குந் துணையாரை யாள்வாரை). 570 shows the opposite court: the harsh sceptre gathers the unlearned around it (கல்லார்ப் பிணிக்கும்), because, as Parimelazhagar says, such a ruler admits only those who will go along with him. The two verses together make a ruler's choice of companions a sign of the kind of rule he runs.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:571","b":"k:557","type":"agrees","note":"557 says that a king's lack of tender care (அளியின்மை) is to those who live under him what lack of rain is to the earth. 571 states the positive side: the world exists because kindly regard exists; Parimelazhagar reads this of kings. In both verses the ruler's kindness is treated as a condition of life for the people, not as an ornament only.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:571","b":"k:996","type":"agrees","note":"571 says this world exists because the great beauty called kindly regard exists (கண்ணோட்டம் ... உண்மையான் உண்டிவ் வுலகு). 996 says the world exists by resting on people of courtesy, and would otherwise sink into the earth and perish (பண்புடையார்ப் பட்டுண்டு உலகம்). Both use the same construction, உண்டு உலகு, to say that a human quality and not power keeps the world going; 571 says it in a chapter on kings and 996 of people in general.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:572","b":"k:983","type":"develops","note":"983 lists kindly regard (கண்ணோட்டம்) with love, shame, obligingness and truth as the five pillars that hold up worth (சால்பு). 572 gives it a wider load: the conduct of the world rests in it (கண்ணோட்டத் துள்ளது உலகியல்). The quality that is one support of personal character in 983 is the support of social life in 572.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:572","b":"k:1003","type":"same_image","note":"572 says the existence of those without kindly regard is a burden to the earth (அஃதிலார் உண்மை நிலக்குப் பொறை). 1003 says the very appearance of men who cling to accumulation and do not want renown is a burden to the earth (தோற்றம் நிலக்குப் பொறை). The phrase is identical, and in both the people called a burden are those who are of no use to others.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:573","b":"k:79","type":"parallel_domain","note":"79 asks what all the outer limbs can do for those who lack love, the inner limb (புறத்துறுப் பெல்லாம் எவன்செய்யும் ... அன்பிலவர்க்கு). 573 asks the same kind of question of the eye that lacks kindly regard. In both a visible organ is counted useless without the inner quality it should serve.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:573","b":"k:705","type":"parallel_domain","note":"573 asks what use an eye is without kindly regard (கண்என்னாம் கண்ணோட்டம் இல்லாத கண்), as a melody is no use that does not fit the song. 705 asks what use eyes are among the limbs if they cannot read another's sign (என்ன பயத்தவோ கண்). Both judge the eye by what it does for another person, kindness in one verse and understanding in the other, and not by sight.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:574","b":"k:406","type":"parallel_domain","note":"406 says the unlearned can only be said to exist (உளரென்னும் மாத்திரையர்) and are otherwise like barren saline land. 574 says eyes without kindly regard only seem to be there in the face (உளபோல் முகத்து) and do nothing more. Both verses grant bare existence and deny any use, of unlearned persons in one case and of unkind eyes in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:574","b":"k:578","type":"develops","note":"574 says eyes without kindly regard in due measure (அளவினால் கண்ணோட்டம் இல்லாத கண்) do nothing but seem to be in the face; Parimelazhagar takes அளவினால் as excluding excessive indulgence. 578 says what the measure is: kindly regard shown without ruining the task (கருமம் சிதையாமல் கண்ணோட வல்லார்). The one word of limit in 574 becomes the whole subject of 578.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:575","b":"k:1014","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1014 asks whether shame is not an ornament (அணி) to the worthy, and whether without it their proud walk is not a disease (அஃதின்றேல் பிணி). 575 has the same shape: kindly regard is the eye's ornament (அணிகலம்), and without it (அஃதின்றேல்) the eye is a sore (புண்). In both the missing virtue does not leave the thing plain but turns it into an affliction.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:577","b":"k:393","type":"parallel_domain","note":"393 says those who are called 'people with eyes' are the learned (கண்ணுடையர் என்பவர் கற்றோர்). 577 says those without kindly regard are eyeless and those who have eyes do not lack it (கண்ணுடையார் கண்ணோட்டம் இன்மையும் இல்). The same title, கண்ணுடையார், is awarded on two different tests, learning and kindly regard; the verses do not conflict, but a reader should see that the book defines 'having eyes' twice.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:579","b":"k:152","type":"develops","note":"152 says always bear with excess against you (பொறுத்தல் இறப்பினை என்றும்); Parimelazhagar adds 'even when you are able to punish'. 579 is that case in the ruler, who has the power to punish and is asked to bear with those who harm him. 152 goes a step further than 579 by preferring forgetting the wrong to bearing it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:580","b":"k:805","type":"develops","note":"805 advises that when friends do something painful, one should take it as either folly or great intimacy (பேதைமை ஒன்றோ பெருங்கிழமை என்றுணர்க). 580 describes people who drink poison they have seen poured. 805 supplies a way of construing a friend's injury that makes the conduct in 580 intelligible, though 580's own stated motive is the wish for நாகரிகம் and not a charitable reading of the giver.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:582","b":"k:520","type":"agrees","note":"520 tells the king to look daily (நாடோறும் நாடுக) into the one doing his work, since the world stays straight if he does; 582 makes constant, quick knowledge of everyone's affairs the king's own occupation (வேந்தன் தொழில்). Both treat continuous inquiry, not occasional inspection, as the core of rule; 582 widens the object from the king's officers to everyone.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:582","b":"k:553","type":"develops","note":"582 makes it the king's work to know quickly, at all times (எஞ்ஞான்றும் வல்லறிதல்), all that happens to everyone; 553 says a ruler who does not inquire daily and do justice (நாடொறும் நாடி முறைசெய்யா) loses his land day by day. 553 gives the purpose and the cost: the daily knowing of 582 exists to feed daily justice, and its absence is paid for in the kingdom itself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:583","b":"k:423","type":"parallel_domain","note":"583 denies victory to a ruler who has things spied out but does not discern their meaning (ஒற்றிப் பொருள்தெரியா); 423 defines understanding as seeing the true substance of whatever is heard from anyone (அப்பொருள் மெய்ப்பொருள் காண்பது). The pairing shows the spy chapter applying the general rule about hearsay to intelligence: collecting reports is not yet knowing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:584","b":"k:510","type":"tension","note":"510 says that doubting a person one has examined and trusted (தெளிந்தான்கண் ஐயுறவும்) brings unending trouble; 584 has spies examine everyone (அனைவரையும் ஆராய்வது), including the king's own officers. The conflict holds if standing surveillance of appointed officers counts as the ஐயுறவு that 510 forbids; if spying is read as routine inquiry and not suspicion of a particular person, the two can stand together.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:584","b":"k:514","type":"develops","note":"584 defines the spy as one who examines everyone, beginning with those who do the king's work (வினைசெய்வார்); 514 observes that even people trusted after every test often turn different through the nature of the work (வினைவகையான் வேறாகும்). 514 supplies the reason for watching one's own appointed officers: the testing done before appointment does not settle how they behave afterwards.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:584","b":"k:882","type":"develops","note":"584 puts the king's own kin (தம் சுற்றம்) among those a spy examines, alongside his enemies (வேண்டாதார்); 882 says not to fear enemies who are like a sword but to fear the tie with enemies who are like kin (கேள்போல் பகைவர்). The chapter on internal enmity explains why 584 does not exempt relatives from scrutiny.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:585","b":"k:686","type":"same_image","note":"585 requires of a spy an unsuspected appearance, not flinching before eyes (கண்ணஞ்சாது) and never letting anything out; 686 requires of an envoy that he not fear the eye (கண் அஞ்சான்), which Parimelazhagar glosses in both verses as the angry look of the other side. The open agent and the covert one are given the same nerve; what differs is that the envoy must speak so it goes home, and the spy must give nothing away.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:585","b":"k:689","type":"parallel_domain","note":"585 ends with the spy's ability never to let anything spill (யாண்டும் உகாஅமை); 689 wants the bearer of a king's message to be a hard man whose mouth does not let slip a damaging word (the edition prints வாய்சேரா; Parimelazhagar reads வாய் சோரா). Both make control of what leaves the mouth under pressure the test of the king's agents abroad.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:586","b":"k:276","type":"tension","note":"276 says none are more hard-hearted than those who, unrenounced at heart, live by deceiving in the likeness of renouncers (துறந்தார்போல் வஞ்சித்து வாழ்வார்); 586 has the spy take the guise of renouncers (துறந்தார் படிவத்தராகி) to get into places and investigate. The conflict holds if 276 condemns the false ascetic guise as such; Parimelazhagar narrows 276 to cheating donors, and on that reading the spy's disguise in the king's service falls outside it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:587","b":"k:695","type":"qualifies","note":"695 tells those who serve a king not to listen in on or press after anything when a secret (மறை) is in progress, and to hear it only when he releases it; 587 makes the ability to hear concealed things (மறைந்தவை கேட்க வற்றாகி) the definition of a spy. The pairing marks a boundary: prying into secrets is a skill the king directs at others, and a fault when his own attendants turn it on him.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:587","b":"k:702","type":"parallel_domain","note":"587 defines the spy as able to learn what was concealed and to hold what he has learned without doubt (ஐயப்பாடு இல்லதே); 702 ranks with a god the one who knows without doubt what is within another (ஐயப்படாஅது அகத்தது உணர்வானை). Both prize certain knowledge of what others hide, one gained by inquiry among informants and the other by reading a person's mind from signs.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:588","b":"k:587","type":"qualifies","note":"587 requires the spy to be free of doubt about what he has learned (அறிந்தவை ஐயப்பாடு இல்லதே); 588 tells the king to check even such a report (தந்த பொருளையும்) through another spy. The spy's certainty is a quality of the source and not a warrant for the king, who is to keep doubting until reports agree.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:588","b":"k:589","type":"develops","note":"588 says that even what one spy found and brought should be confirmed through another spy (மற்றுமோர் ஒற்றினால்); 589 adds that spies should be run so that one does not know of another, and that what three say in agreement is to be accepted (மூவர் சொற்றொக்க தேறப்படும்). 589 turns the second opinion of 588 into a procedure: sources kept independent, and a threshold of agreement.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:589","b":"k:509","type":"agrees","note":"509 says to trust no one without examining and, after examining, to trust what is to be trusted (தேர்ந்தபின் தேறுக); 589 says to run spies unknown to one another and to accept what three say in agreement (தேறப்படும்). Both give the same two-step rule, test first and then commit, 509 for persons and 589 for reports.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:590","b":"k:440","type":"parallel_domain","note":"440 says that if a ruler can enjoy what he loves without others knowing of his love (காதல் அறியாமை), his enemies' schemes come to nothing; 590 forbids showing favour to a spy where others can know of it. Both treat a ruler's visible attachments and rewards as information that others can use against him.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:590","b":"k:663","type":"parallel_domain","note":"590 warns that a king who honours a spy so that others know of it (சிறப்பறிய) has himself let the secret out (புறப்படுத்தான் ஆகும் மறை); 663 says a deed should be done so that it shows only at the end, since showing midway brings lasting trouble (இடைக்கொட்கின்). In both the actor's own conduct, not an enemy's skill, is what exposes the undertaking.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:591","b":"k:1005","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1005 says those who neither give nor enjoy have nothing even with crores piled up (கோடியுண்டாயினும் இல்); 591 asks whether those without energy really have what they have. Both separate holding goods from having them, 1005 by the test of use and 591 by the test of the holder's drive.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:592","b":"k:333","type":"develops","note":"333 says wealth has the nature of not staying (அற்கா இயல்பிற்று) and concludes that on getting it one should at once do what stays; 592 also says goods do not stay (நில்லாது நீங்கிவிடும்) and concludes that the lasting possession is the inner drive (உள்ளம் உடைமை). Starting from the same fact, the Book of Virtue points to enduring deeds and the Book of Wealth to an inner resource that can make wealth again.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:594","b":"k:617","type":"agrees","note":"617 says the lotus goddess lives in the effort of the unslothful (மடியிலான் தாளுளாள்); 594 says prosperity finds its own way to the person of unwavering energy. Both personify fortune as choosing where it lives by the person's exertion, and neither leaves it to chance.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:595","b":"k:396","type":"parallel_domain","note":"396 says a sand-well yields water as far as it is dug, and understanding as far as one has learned (தொட்டனைத்து... கற்றனைத்து); 595 says a water-flower's stem is as long as the water is deep, and a person's height as great as their inner drive (வெள்ளத்தனைய... உள்ளத்தனையது). Both use a water image and the same 'as much as' construction to state a strict proportion, one for learning and knowledge, the other for drive and stature.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:596","b":"k:619","type":"parallel_domain","note":"619 says that even if a thing cannot come about because of fate (தெய்வத்தான் ஆகாதெனினும்), effort pays the wage of the body's strain; 596 says a high aim keeps its worth even when it is thrust aside, which Parimelazhagar takes as being denied by fate. Both hold that what fate withholds in outcome does not cancel the value of the striving or the aim.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:597","b":"k:774","type":"parallel_domain","note":"774 shows a warrior who has thrown his spear at an elephant pulling out the spear lodged in his own body and laughing; 597 shows the elephant itself holding firm when struck by arrows, as the image of those who do not give way in ruin. In both, a wound taken is the occasion that shows firmness, and the Book of Wealth draws the general rule in 597 that the war chapter acts out in 774.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:598","b":"k:614","type":"develops","note":"598 says the person without drive does not attain the pride of generosity; 614 says the beneficence of one without effort comes to nothing, like a sword in the hand of a பேடி. 614 adds what happens when such a person tries anyway: the means are present but the capacity to use them is not.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:599","b":"k:763","type":"parallel_domain","note":"599 says an elephant, though huge and sharp-tusked, fears when a tiger attacks; 763 says a sea of roaring rats perishes when the cobra merely breathes. Both animal pairs teach that bulk or numbers do not decide a contest against an opponent with spirit, 599 of a person's drive and 763 of an army.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:600","b":"k:997","type":"same_image","note":"997 says those without human courtesy are like trees even if sharp as a file (மரம்போல்வர்); 600 says those without abundance of drive are trees, differing only in having human form (மரம் மக்களாதலே வேறு). The tree marks the person who lacks what makes one human, but the two verses name different lacks: fellow-feeling in 997, inner drive in 600.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:601","b":"k:753","type":"same_image","note":"601 calls the family a lamp that does not dwindle (குடியென்னும் குன்றா விளக்கம்) which dies when the grime of sloth spreads over it (மடியென்னும் மாசூர). 753 calls wealth a lamp that does not fail (பொருளென்னும் பொய்யா விளக்கம்) which goes where its owner intends and cuts the darkness. The same 'X என்னும் ... விளக்கம்' figure is used twice in the Book of Wealth, and Parimelazhagar glosses both as a நந்தா விளக்கு; one lamp is shown dispelling darkness abroad, the other being put out from within.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:602","b":"k:1022","type":"develops","note":"602 states the negative condition for a family's standing: remove sloth. 1022 states the positive one: the family lengthens (நீளும் குடி) through long work in two things, effort and mature knowledge (ஆள்வினையும் ஆன்ற அறிவும்). Read together, absence of sloth is necessary but 1022 adds knowledge beside effort.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:602","b":"k:1028","type":"develops","note":"602 tells those who want their family to be a family (குடியைக் குடியாக வேண்டுபவர்) to live treating sloth as sloth, or killing it (மடியை மடியா ஒழுகல்). 1028 says those who build the family have no season to wait for, and that if they idle and think of their dignity it is ruined (மடிசெய்து மானங் கருதக் கெடும்). 1028 gives 602's rule its practical form: for the family-builder sloth shows up as waiting for the right time and standing on dignity.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:605","b":"k:371","type":"qualifies","note":"605 treats sloth and its companions as something the ruin-bound choose and desire (காமக் கலன்). 371 says sloth itself arises from a fate of loss (போகூழால் தோன்றும் மடி). 371 puts a cause behind what 605 describes as a preference, which matches 605's own word கெடுநீரார், people whose nature is already to perish.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:605","b":"k:920","type":"parallel_domain","note":"605 lists delay, forgetting, sloth and sleep as the craft that those of a ruin-bound nature desire (கெடுநீரார் காமக் கலன்). 920 lists double-minded women, liquor and dice as the company of those Fortune has cast off (திருநீக்கப் பட்டார் தொடர்பு). Both verses give a short catalogue and tie it to people already marked for ruin, leaving open whether the listed things cause the ruin or are its sign.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:605","b":"k:1068","type":"same_image","note":"605 makes four habits a boat (கலன்) that the ruin-bound love to board; Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar both read it as a vessel that drops its passengers mid-sea, though Parimelazhagar notes others read கலன் as an ornament. 1068 makes begging an unsafe boat (ஏமாப்பில் தோணி) that splits on the rock of refusal. In both a way of living is a vessel trusted by the person in it and bound to fail him.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:606","b":"k:591","type":"agrees","note":"606: even when the holdings of those who own the earth come to them (படியுடையார் பற்றமைந்தக் கண்ணும்), the slothful rarely reach an excellent fruit. 591: what makes one 'a possessor' is energy; do those without it possess even what they possess (உடையது உடையரோ)? Both deny that having things amounts to benefiting from them when the inner quality is missing. Parimelazhagar notes another reading of 606 in which the earth-owners' support, not their wealth, is what comes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:606","b":"k:750","type":"parallel_domain","note":"606 says the greatest possessions yield the slothful no excellent fruit (மாண்பயன் எய்தல் அரிது). 750 says a fort, whatever excellences it has, is as nothing with those who lack excellence in action (வினைமாட்சி இல்லார்கண் இல்லது அரண்). The two share the 'ஆகியக் கண்ணும் / அமைந்தக் கண்ணும்' concession: resources do not work without the action of those who hold them.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:607","b":"k:1040","type":"agrees","note":"607: the slothful end up hearing words of scorn (எள்ளுஞ் சொல்). 1040: seeing those who sit idle saying 'we have nothing', the good woman called Land laughs (நிலமென்னும் நல்லாள் நகும்). Both give idleness the same penalty, being laughed at, once from people and once from the personified earth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:608","b":"k:616","type":"same_image","note":"608: if sloth lodges in one of family standing it puts him into slavery to his enemies (அடிமை புகுத்தி விடும்). 616: absence of effort puts one into want (இன்மை புகுத்தி விடும்). The identical closing phrase makes sloth an agent that ushers a person into a condition; the neighbouring chapters thus name two destinations, poverty and subjection, and Parimelazhagar links them in his note on 616, where want leads to destruction by enemies.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:609","b":"k:604","type":"qualifies","note":"604 says that for the slothful the family dies and faults multiply (குற்றம் பெருகும்). 609 says the fault is destroyed (குற்றம் ... கெடும்) once a man changes his sloth. 609 makes 604's decline reversible; Parimelazhagar goes further and takes 609's faults to include ones that arose from other causes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:609","b":"k:1026","type":"develops","note":"609: when a man ends the rule of sloth over him (மடியாண்மை மாற்ற), the fault that came into his குடியாண்மை is gone; Parimelazhagar splits the word into family and manhood, Manakkudavar reads it as the management of the family. 1026 says good manhood is making the management of the house one was born in one's own (நல்லாண்மை ... இல்லாண்மை ஆக்கிக் கொளல்). 1026 supports reading the two terms of 609 together: manhood is measured in the household, and sloth is what unseats it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:610","b":"k:606","type":"qualifies","note":"610 gives the unslothful king the whole measured world. 606, in the same chapter, says that even when the wealth of earth-owners arrives the slothful get no excellent fruit from it. Together they separate two things: sloth does not only block gaining the world, it also empties the world of value when it is handed over.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:611","b":"k:26","type":"develops","note":"611: do not flag saying 'it is hard' (அருமை உடைத்தென்று அசாவாமை), for effort will give the greatness (பெருமை முயற்சி தரும்). 26: those who do what is hard to do are great, those who cannot are small (செயற்கரிய செய்வார் பெரியர்). 26 defines greatness by hard deeds as if it were a fixed difference between people; 611 says the greatness needed is itself produced by trying.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:612","b":"k:670","type":"agrees","note":"612: guard against the work failing in the doing, because the world has left those who left the work's need undone (வினைக்குறை தீர்ந்தாரின் தீர்ந்தன்று உலகு), on Parimelazhagar's reading; Manakkudavar takes the second line the other way, that the world does not leave those who finish. 670: whatever other firmness they have, the world does not want those who do not want firmness in action (வேண்டாரை வேண்டாது உலகு). Both make the world's regard answer in kind to how a person treats the task.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:612","b":"k:674","type":"develops","note":"612 warns against abandoning a task midway (வினைக்கண் வினைகெடல் ஓம்பல்). 674 says the remainder of a task, like the remainder of an enmity, burns like the remainder of a fire (தீயெச்சம் போலத் தெறும்). 674 supplies a reason 612 does not give: the unfinished part is not inert but goes on doing damage.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:613","b":"k:1035","type":"parallel_domain","note":"613 says the standing of a benefactor rests on labour. 1035 says those whose way is to eat what their hands produce do not beg and give to those who beg without hiding anything (கைசெய்தூண் மாலையவர்). The farming chapter shows 613's general claim in one occupation: the person who works with his hands is the one in a position to give.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:614","b":"k:219","type":"qualifies","note":"614 says the generosity of a man without effort comes to nothing. 219 says that for a man of good feeling, being poor just is not being able to do the helpful things he would do (செயும்நீர செய்யாது அமைகலா வாறு). 219 shows the same blocked intention from the inside and without blame, so 614's scorn falls only on the case where the lack of means comes from not working.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:615","b":"k:901","type":"agrees","note":"615 praises the one who wants the work (வினைவிழைவான்) and not pleasure. 901 says those who desire the wife do not reach the excellent fruit, and that this is the very thing those who desire action do not want (வினைவிழையார் வேண்டாப் பொருளும் அது). Both set the desire for action against a desire for enjoyment and use nearly the same word for the man of action.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:615","b":"k:1030","type":"develops","note":"615 makes the work-loving man a pillar that props his kin (துடைத்தூன்றும் தூண்). 1030 says a family with no good person to stand by and prop it (அடுத்தூன்றும் நல்லாள் இலாத குடி) falls when trouble cuts at its foot. 1030 states the cost of the absence of the figure 615 praises, with the same verb ஊன்று.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:616","b":"k:371","type":"qualifies","note":"616: effort makes fortune (முயற்சி திருவினை ஆக்கும்) and lack of effort brings one into want. 371: unflagging effort arises from a fate of gain, and sloth from a fate of loss (ஆகூழால் தோன்றும் அசைவின்மை ... போகூழால் தோன்றும் மடி). 616 makes effort the cause of wealth; 371 makes fate the cause of effort, so on 371's account the causal chain in 616 starts one step earlier and is not in the person's hands.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:616","b":"k:594","type":"agrees","note":"616 says effort produces fortune. 594 says increase asks the way and goes to the one of unwavering drive (அதர்வினாய்ச் செல்லும் அசைவிலா ஊக்கமுடையானுழை). Both place the source of prosperity in the person's exertion, and 594 pictures it as prosperity doing the seeking. The first word of 594 is truncated in this edition (க்கம் for ஆக்கம்).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:618","b":"k:988","type":"parallel_domain","note":"618 separates what is no disgrace (having no luck) from what is (not working). 988 says want is no disgrace to a person who has the firmness called worth (இன்மை ஒருவற்கு இளிவன்று). Both refuse to count an outward lack as shame and move the test to something in the person, effort in one and character in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:620","b":"k:1024","type":"same_image","note":"620 ends 'தாழாது உஞற்றுபவர்', those who labour without delay, and says they see fate's back. 1024 ends with the same phrase, 'தம்குடியைத் தாழாது உஞற்றுபவர்க்கு', and says that for them the outcome arrives by itself without deliberation (சூழாமல் தானே முடிவெய்தும்). The same people are described twice, once as defeating an opposing power and once as finding the result come unsought.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:621","b":"k:774","type":"parallel_domain","note":"621 says to laugh when trouble comes (இடுக்கண் வருங்கால் நகுக), since nothing presses it back as well. 774 shows a warrior who, having thrown his spear at an elephant, pulls out the one lodged in his own body and laughs (மெய்வேல் பறியா நகும்). The battlefield verse acts out the advice of 621: the laugh comes at the moment of injury, and the injury is turned to use.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:622","b":"k:309","type":"same_image","note":"Both verses turn on the same play of உள்ளம் (mind) and உள்ளுதல் (to think): in 622 a flood of suffering perishes when the wise person thinks with the mind (உள்ளத்தின் உள்ளக் கெடும்), and in 309 all one has thought of is gained if one does not think anger in the mind (உள்ளத்தால் உள்ளான் வெகுளி எனின்). Read together they make a single act of thought decide the outcome, once by what is thought and once by what is left unthought.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:622","b":"k:429","type":"develops","note":"429 says those whose understanding guards against what is coming (எதிரதாக் காக்கும் அறிவினார்) have no affliction that arrives to shake them; 622 says that for the person of understanding (அறிவுடையான்) even a flood of suffering already present is ended by thinking. The pair gives understanding two jobs, forestalling trouble and dissolving the trouble that came anyway, which Parimelazhagar takes in 622 to be trouble from fate.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:623","b":"k:261","type":"parallel_domain","note":"623 says those who do not suffer at suffering (இடும்பைக்கு இடும்பை படாஅதவர்) make suffering itself suffer; Parimelazhagar places this in the middle of an undertaking. 261 makes bearing the pain that comes (உற்றநோய் நோன்றல்) half of the form of தவம். The same endurance is a means to finishing work in one book and the definition of austerity in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:624","b":"k:597","type":"agrees","note":"624 likens the person who pulls through every obstruction to a draught ox (மடுத்தவாயெல்லாம் பகடன்னான்) and says the trouble he meets is itself troubled. 597 says the strong do not give way where things break down (சிதைவிடத்து ஒல்கார்), like an elephant standing firm under arrows. Both make the same claim through a working or fighting animal; the ox adds forward motion to the elephant's holding of ground.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:624","b":"k:620","type":"develops","note":"624 says trouble is troubled by one who hauls on like an ox through every blocked place. 620 carries the claim to its limit: those who labour on without flagging (தாழாது உஞற்றுபவர்) see the back even of fate (ஊழையும் உப்பக்கம் காண்பர்). What 624 says of obstacles in the road, 620 says of the power behind them.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:625","b":"k:989","type":"agrees","note":"625 says that though troubles come stacked (அடுக்கி வரினும்), those of one who is not undone (அழிவிலான்) are themselves in trouble. 989 says those called the shore of worth do not shift even if the age shifts (ஊழி பெயரினும் தாம் பெயரார்). Both define a person by not moving under pressure, one in the chapter on a king's undertakings and one in the chapter on character.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:625","b":"k:1045","type":"qualifies","note":"1045 states as plain fact that within the one suffering called poverty many sufferings arrive and settle (பல்குரைத் துன்பங்கள் சென்று படும்), and offers no remedy. 625 addresses exactly such piling up (அடுக்கி வரினும்) and says it fails against one who is not undone. Set side by side, 625's confidence is conditional on the person, while 1045 describes what the stacking is like for those in want.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:629","b":"k:4","type":"parallel_domain","note":"629 makes freedom from pain depend on not desiring pleasure when it is present (இன்பத்துள் இன்பம் விழையாதான்). 4 describes the Lord as one without wanting or not-wanting (வேண்டுதல் வேண்டாமை இலான்) and says those who reach his feet never have இடும்பை. The same link between having no pull toward or away from things and having no suffering appears as a divine attribute in Book 1 and as a human discipline here.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:629","b":"k:115","type":"agrees","note":"629 says one who does not crave pleasure in the midst of pleasure does not suffer in the midst of pain. 115 says loss and increase are both part of life and that not letting the heart bend (நெஞ்சத்துக் கோடாமை) is the ornament of the worthy. Both ask for the same evenness across good and bad turns; 115 treats it as fairness of mind, 629 as protection against pain.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:630","b":"k:669","type":"develops","note":"669 says to do with resolve the deed that yields joy even though pain comes thick at first (துன்பம் உறவரினும் ... இன்பம் பயக்கும் வினை): pain now, joy later. 630 goes a step further and has the person take the pain itself as joy (இன்னாமை இன்பம் எனக்கொளின்). Parimelazhagar reads the pain in both as the strain of effort.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:630","b":"k:810","type":"same_image","note":"630 measures the reward by the enemy: a distinction one's foes desire or esteem (ஒன்னார் விழையும் சிறப்பு). 810 uses the same measure with the same verb: those who do not depart from their nature toward old friends are wanted even by those who do not want them (விழையார் விழையப்படுப). Each verse marks an unusual constancy, under pain or toward friends who have erred, by saying that even the hostile regard it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:641","b":"k:411","type":"parallel_domain","note":"641 sets the good of the tongue above every other good. 411 calls the wealth of the ear the wealth among wealths and the head of them all (செல்வத்துள் எல்லாம் தலை). The book gives the top place once to listening and once to speaking, in chapters addressed to the learner and to the minister.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:641","b":"k:982","type":"same_image","note":"The two verses end with the same formula, 'அந்நலம்/பிறநலம் யா/எந்நலத்து உள்ளதூஉம் அன்று', used in opposite directions. In 641 the goodness of the tongue (நாநலம்) is not contained in any other goodness, which Parimelazhagar takes to mean it exceeds them all; in 982 every goodness other than the goodness of character (குணநலம்) is not counted among goodnesses at all. One chapter ranks eloquence highest for a minister, the other ranks character alone for the worthy.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:642","b":"k:128","type":"develops","note":"642 says ruin as well as gain comes from speech and asks for vigilance against slips. 128 gives the scale of the risk: if even one harmful word has its effect (ஒன்றானும் தீச்சொல்), the good turns to no good (நன்றாகாதாகி விடும்). 128 supplies the reason why 642 treats a lapse (சோர்வு), and not only deliberate bad speech, as the danger.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:643","b":"k:187","type":"develops","note":"Parimelazhagar reads 643 as speech that binds those who have accepted one as a friend (கேட்டார்ப் பிணிக்கும்) and makes even those who have not want friendship. 187 gives the failure of this skill: those who do not know how to make friends with pleasing talk (நகச்சொல்லி நட்பாடல் தேற்றாதவர்) part even their own kin with divisive talk. On that reading the two verses are the positive and negative of one ability, speech as what holds or breaks alliances.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:644","b":"k:469","type":"parallel_domain","note":"644 says to speak after knowing the திறன் of those concerned. 469 says that even in doing good there is error when it is done without knowing each person's character (அவரவர் பண்பறிந்து ஆற்றாக்கடை). Both make a good thing, a word or a deed, depend on fitting it to the particular person receiving it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:644","b":"k:696","type":"develops","note":"644 asks for speech fitted to its hearers and says no virtue or gain exceeds it. 696 applies this to a single hearer, the king: know his mood, consider the time, and say what is needed in a way he will want (குறிப்பறிந்து காலம் கருதி ... வேட்பச் சொலல்). The general rule for a minister's speech becomes a specific rule for speech to power.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:644","b":"k:711","type":"develops","note":"644 says to speak knowing the திறன், which Parimelazhagar takes as the relative standing of speaker and hearers. 711 gives the method: those who know the stock of words should speak after knowing the assembly and examining (அவையறிந்து ஆராய்ந்து சொல்லுக). Chapter 72 works out as a practice what 644 states as a principle.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:645","b":"k:467","type":"parallel_domain","note":"645 says to speak a word only after knowing that no other word can defeat it (வெல்லுஞ்சொல் இன்மை அறிந்து). 467 says to think and then commit to a task, and that saying 'we will think after committing' is a fault (எண்ணித் துணிக). Both put the testing before the irreversible step; 645 applies to an utterance the rule 467 gives for action.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:646","b":"k:724","type":"develops","note":"646 pairs speaking well with drawing the benefit from others' words. 724 sets that pair in the assembly of the learned: make what you have learned go home to them (கற்ற செலச்சொல்லி) and take what is more from those who know more (மிக்காருள் மிக்க கொளல்). 724 adds what 646 leaves open, that listening is how the speaker's own knowledge grows.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:647","b":"k:686","type":"agrees","note":"647 lists three things that make a person hard to beat in a contest: able to speak, free of lapses, unafraid (சொலல்வல்லன் சோர்விலன் அஞ்சான்). 686 defines the envoy with nearly the same set: learned, not afraid of hostile eyes (கண் அஞ்சான்), speaking so it goes home, and knowing what fits the time. The envoy chapter puts the general portrait of 647 into the one post where it is tested before an enemy.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:647","b":"k:872","type":"develops","note":"647 says no one can easily defeat a fearless, unfaltering speaker in a contest (இகல் வெல்லல் யார்க்கும் அரிது). 872 gives the advice that follows for the other side: take on the enmity of those whose plough is the bow if you must, but not of those whose plough is the word (சொல்லேர் உழவர் பகை). Parimelazhagar takes the latter to be ministers, the people 647 describes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:648","b":"k:1046","type":"qualifies","note":"648 says the world listens readily to skilled, pleasing speakers. 1046 says the words of the poor go slack even when they speak good matter that they have understood well (நற்பொருள் நன்குணர்ந்து சொல்லினும் ... சோர்வு படும்). 1046 limits 648: how the world receives speech depends on the speaker's means as well as his skill.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:649","b":"k:193","type":"agrees","note":"649 treats wanting to say many things as the sign of someone unable to say a few well. 193 says spread-out talk on useless matters (பயனில பாரித்துரைக்கும் உரை) itself announces that the speaker lacks நயன். In both, length of speech is evidence against the speaker; 193 judges his character, 649 his skill.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:649","b":"k:650","type":"qualifies","note":"649 faults many words and praises a few faultless ones; the next verse faults those who cannot expound what they have learned so that others understand (உணர விரித்துரையாதார்). Manakkudavar reads the pair together: speak briefly, but also expand where it is needed. 650 keeps 649 from being taken as a rule of brevity alone.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:649","b":"k:685","type":"develops","note":"649 says those who cannot say a few faultless words (மாசற்ற சில சொல்லல் தேற்றாதவர்) long to say many. 685 makes brevity the envoy's first mark: he speaks compactly (தொகச் சொல்லி), drops what is harsh, and speaks so as to please. 685 shows the positive skill whose absence 649 describes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:650","b":"k:1274","type":"same_image","note":"Both verses use a flower's scent (நாற்றம்) for what is inside a person. In 650 the flower is fully open and gives no scent, like the learned person who cannot convey what he knows; in 1274 the scent is held inside a closed bud, like the unspoken meaning inside the girl's budding smile. The two uses mirror each other: an open display with nothing reaching others, and a closed one whose content the lover already perceives.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:651","b":"k:455","type":"qualifies","note":"651 sets வினைநலம் above துணைநலம்: support gives only ஆக்கம், good action gives all that is wanted. 455 says purity of mind and purity of action (செய்வினை தூய்மை) both come leaning on purity of company (இனந்தூய்மை தூவா வரும்). Read together, the action that 651 ranks above one's support is itself said to depend on the people one keeps.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:653","b":"k:962","type":"develops","note":"653 tells those who say 'ஆஅதும்' (we shall rise) to stop doing what dims their ஒளி. 962 says those who want சீர் together with பேராண்மை will not do 'சீரல்ல' even for the sake of சீர். 962 takes the harder case 653 leaves open: the dimming deed may itself be a route to fame, and it is still refused.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:653","b":"k:971","type":"develops","note":"653 treats ஒளி as something the ambitious ('ஆஅதும் என்னுமவர்') must protect by refraining from certain deeds. 971 defines ஒளி as 'உள்ள வெறுக்கை', abundance of spirit, and இளி as saying one will live without it. Together they give ஒளி two sides: it is lost by what one does (653) and it consists in what one aspires to (971).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:654","b":"k:174","type":"parallel_domain","note":"654 says those of unshaken sight do nothing degrading even in distress (இடுக்கண் படினும்). 174 says those of 'புன்மையில் காட்சி' who have conquered the senses will not covet on the plea 'இலம்' (we have nothing). Both verses name a clear-sighted person (காட்சியவர்) and deny that need is an excuse; 174 applies it to coveting, 654 to action generally.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:654","b":"k:218","type":"parallel_domain","note":"654: clear-sighted people do not do what is degrading even when trouble comes. 218: those of 'கடனறி காட்சி' do not falter in ஒப்புரவு even in a season without means (இடனில் பருவத்தும் ... ஒல்கார்). One verse says hardship does not make them do wrong, the other that it does not make them stop doing good; the two are the negative and positive halves of the same steadiness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:655","b":"k:467","type":"parallel_domain","note":"655 asks that one not do what will be regretted afterwards. 467 says to think and then resolve (எண்ணித் துணிக), and calls it a lapse to say 'we will think after resolving'. Both put the reflection before the act: 467 as a rule of planning, 655 as a rule about the purity of the deed.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:656","b":"k:961","type":"agrees","note":"656 forbids the blameworthy deed even when the need is a mother's hunger. 961 says to give up things that would diminish one (குன்ற வருப விடல்) even if they are indispensable (இன்றி அமையாச் சிறப்பின ஆயினும்). Both deny that necessity licenses a degrading act; 961 states the principle abstractly under honour, 656 gives it a concrete and painful instance.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:656","b":"k:1047","type":"same_image","note":"656 uses the mother who bore one (ஈன்றாள்) as the strongest pull toward wrongdoing for the sake of relief. 1047 says that one in poverty not joined with virtue (அறஞ்சாரா நல்குரவு) is looked on as a stranger even by the mother who bore him (ஈன்ற தாயானும்). The mother marks the limit case in both: 656 holds conduct firm against her need, while 1047 says poverty without virtue loses even her regard.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:657","b":"k:408","type":"parallel_domain","note":"657: the extreme poverty of the worthy (சான்றோர் கழிநல்குரவு) is better than wealth got by taking on blame (பழிமலைந்து எய்திய ஆக்கம்). 408: wealth lodged with the unlearned (கல்லார்கண் பட்ட திரு) is more painful than poverty lodged with the good (நல்லார்கண் பட்ட வறுமை). The same comparison of good people's poverty with bad people's wealth is made from each side, once about how wealth was got and once about who holds it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:657","b":"k:988","type":"agrees","note":"657 ranks the utter poverty of the worthy above blame-laden wealth. 988 says poverty (இன்மை) is no disgrace (இளிவன்று) to one who has the firmness called சால்பு. 988 supplies the ground for 657's ranking: for the சான்றோர் poverty carries no இளிவு, whereas the wealth in 657 carries பழி.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:658","b":"k:113","type":"agrees","note":"658: forbidden deeds done without shunning them bring anguish even if they succeed (முடிந்தாலும் பீழை தரும்). 113: give up at once gain that comes from leaving fairness, even if it brings nothing but good (நன்றே தரினும்). Both concede success in advance and still reject the act, so the objection is not that such means fail.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:658","b":"k:177","type":"agrees","note":"658 warns that the gain of a forbidden deed turns to பீழை later. 177 says not to want gain that comes by coveting (வெஃகியாம் ஆக்கம்), because at the time of enjoying it the fruit is hard to make good (விளைவயின் மாண்டற்கு அரிதாம் பயன்). Both place the harm at the stage of the result and not at the stage of getting.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:658","b":"k:931","type":"parallel_domain","note":"658 says forbidden actions bring anguish even when they are accomplished. 931 says not to want gambling even if one wins (வென்றிடினும்), since the winnings are like the hook a fish has swallowed. The gambling verse gives an image for 658's claim: the success itself is what carries the harm.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:659","b":"k:112","type":"agrees","note":"The second half of 659 says what comes from good ways (நற்பாலவை) yields later even if it is lost (இழப்பினும் பிற்பயக்கும்). 112 says the wealth of a person of rectitude (செப்பம் உடையவன் ஆக்கம்) does not decay and is a security for those who come after (எச்சத்திற்கு ஏமாப்பு). Both give rightly got wealth a benefit that outlasts the moment, 659 for the person later and 112 for descendants.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:660","b":"k:1000","type":"same_image","note":"660: wealth made by deceit is water kept in an unfired clay vessel. 1000: great wealth obtained by one without character is good milk spoiled by the fault of its vessel (கலந்தீமையால் திரிந்தற்று). Both use a faulty கலம் for wealth that cannot be kept, but the fault differs: in 660 it is how the wealth was made, in 1000 it is the holder's character.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:661","b":"k:34","type":"parallel_domain","note":"661: firmness in action is a person's firmness of mind (மனத்திட்பம்); everything else is something other (மற்றைய எல்லாம் பிற). 34: being spotless in mind (மனத்துக்கண் மாசிலன் ஆதல்) is all of virtue; the rest is noisy show (ஆகுல நீர பிற). The two verses have the same shape, reducing a whole domain to a state of mind and dismissing the remainder as 'பிற', once for virtue and once for effective action.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:661","b":"k:600","type":"agrees","note":"661 locates firmness in action in the mind and sets other things aside. 600 says a person's real strength (உரம்) is abundance of spirit (உள்ள வெறுக்கை), and those without it are trees. Both place strength within the person; Parimelazhagar's note on 661 lists the outer things meant by 'மற்றைய' as the firmness of army, fort and allies.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:662","b":"k:429","type":"qualifies","note":"429 says those whose understanding guards against what is coming (எதிரதாக் காக்கும் அறிவினார்) have no affliction that comes to shake them. 662 keeps that first rule (ஊறொரால்) but adds a second for when harm arrives anyway (உற்றபின் ஒல்காமை). 662 thus allows that foresight is not always enough; Parimelazhagar reads the second case as a task spoiled by fate.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:662","b":"k:442","type":"parallel_domain","note":"662 gives two rules as the way of those who have studied the matter: avoid harm (ஊறொரால்) and do not give way once it has come (உற்றபின் ஒல்காமை). 442 tells a king to cherish those who remove the trouble that has come (உற்றநோய் நீக்கி) and guard beforehand against what has not (உறாஅமை முற்காக்கும்). The same before-and-after pair appears as the doer's own discipline in 662 and as the quality to look for in advisers in 442.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:664","b":"k:819","type":"develops","note":"664: saying is easy for anyone; doing as one has said (சொல்லிய வண்ணம் செயல்) is hard. 819: the bond with those whose deed is one thing and word another (வினைவேறு சொல்வேறு பட்டார்) is bitter even in a dream. 664 states the gap between word and deed as a difficulty of execution; 819 judges the same gap in a friend as a fault of character.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:666","b":"k:309","type":"parallel_domain","note":"666: what one has thought of is obtained as thought, if one is firm. 309: all one has thought of is obtained at once (உள்ளிய தெல்லாம் உடனெய்தும்), if one does not think of anger in the heart. The same promise is attached to two different inner conditions, one positive (firmness) and one negative (absence of anger).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:667","b":"k:1032","type":"same_image","note":"667: do not scorn anyone on seeing their form (உருவுகண்டு எள்ளாமை), for there are people like the linchpin (அச்சாணி) of a great rolling chariot. 1032: those who plough are the ஆணி of the world's people, because they bear all who cannot plough and take up other work. Both use the pin that holds the wheel for someone small in appearance on whom everything turns; 667 leaves the person unnamed and 1032 names the farmer. Parimelazhagar supplies the smallness in 667 from the simile.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:668","b":"k:467","type":"develops","note":"467 says to think and then resolve on a task, and that saying 'we will think after resolving' is a lapse. 668 takes up from that point: once the task has been seen without confusion (கலங்காது கண்ட), act without wavering (துளங்காது). Together they give a sequence in which doubt belongs before the decision and not after it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:668","b":"k:671","type":"agrees","note":"668: in a task one has seen clearly and decided on (கலங்காது கண்ட வினை), act without wavering and without delay (தூக்கங் கடிந்து). 671, opening the next chapter, says deliberation ends in reaching resolve (துணிவு) and it is bad for that resolve to rest in delay (தாழ்ச்சியுள் தங்குதல் தீது). The same rule closes the chapter on firmness and opens the chapter on method: once the thinking is done, delay is the fault.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:669","b":"k:611","type":"agrees","note":"669 says to carry through the worthwhile deed despite the pain it brings at first. 611 says one must not slacken on the thought that a thing is hard (அருமை உடைத்தென்று அசாவாமை), since effort gives the greatness needed. Both refuse the difficulty of a task as a reason to hold back; 611 speaks of effort and 669 of resolve (துணிவு).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:669","b":"k:615","type":"develops","note":"669: do with resolve the deed that yields joy (இன்பம் பயக்கும் வினை), even though pain comes close at first (துன்பம் உறவரினும்). 615: one who does not want pleasure but wants the work (இன்பம் விழையான் வினை விழைவான்) is a pillar who wipes away his kin's துன்பம். Both set இன்பம், துன்பம் and வினை against each other; 669 justifies bearing pain by the joy the deed yields, while 615 describes a doer who is not after pleasure at all and whose work removes others' pain.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:670","b":"k:750","type":"parallel_domain","note":"670: whatever other firmness they have attained (எனைத்திட்பம் எய்தியக் கண்ணும்), the world does not want those who do not want firmness in action. 750: whatever excellences it has (எனைமாட்சித்து ஆகியக் கண்ணும்), a fort is nothing with those who lack excellence in action (வினைமாட்சி இல்லார்கண்). The two chapter-closing verses are built alike and make the same point, that other strengths count for nothing without the capacity to act, once of a minister and once of a fort's defenders.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:672","b":"k:490","type":"agrees","note":"490 says to be like the heron in the season for folding up (கூம்பும் பருவத்து) and like its strike when the moment is right (சீர்த்த இடத்து). 672 gives the same double instruction without the image: take time over what is to be done slowly, and do not delay what must not be delayed. Both hold that waiting and speed are each correct in their place.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:672","b":"k:671","type":"qualifies","note":"671 calls it a fault when a resolved deed lingers in delay (தாழ்ச்சியுள் தங்குதல் தீது). 672 immediately limits this: there are deeds to be done slowly, and over those one should take time (தூங்குக தூங்கிச் செயற்பால); only the deed that must be done without delay must not be slept on. Delay is therefore wrong by the nature of the task, not in itself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:673","b":"k:485","type":"develops","note":"485 says those who have the world in mind wait, unshaken, with their mind on the time (காலம் கருதி இருப்பர்). Manakkudavar reads the second half of 673 in that sense: when the deed is not possible, keep it in mind and act on seeing the place where it becomes possible. Parimelazhagar reads 673 differently, as open force where one is stronger and one of the other three expedients where one is not, so the link holds on Manakkudavar's reading.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:677","b":"k:462","type":"develops","note":"462 says nothing is hard to attain for those who act after examining a matter with chosen company (தெரிந்த இனத்தொடு) and thinking it over themselves. 677 narrows whose counsel counts for a particular task: the one who knows that deed from inside (அவ்வினை உள்ளறிவான்), whom Parimelazhagar takes to be someone who has done it before. General consultation in 462 becomes, in 677, learning the mind of the person with experience of the task.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:678","b":"k:875","type":"develops","note":"678 says to get one deed done by means of another, as one binds an elephant with a rutting elephant (யானையால் யானையாத் தற்று). Manakkudavar takes this as setting an enemy's own enemy against him. On that reading 875 is the case worked out: one who is alone with two enemies should take one of them as an ally (இன்துணையாக் கொள்க அவற்றின் ஒன்று). Parimelazhagar reads 678 more generally, as making a task in hand the means to another like it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:679","b":"k:875","type":"agrees","note":"875 says that one without allies and facing two enemies should take one of them as a pleasing ally. 679 says that winning over ஒட்டார் is more urgent than favours to friends; Manakkudavar takes ஒட்டார் as one's enemies, Parimelazhagar as those not allied with one's enemy. On either reading both verses treat alliance as something to be made out of the hostile or uncommitted when one is pressed.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:680","b":"k:488","type":"develops","note":"488 says to bear with enemies on meeting them (செறுநரைக் காணின் சுமக்க), for when their end comes their head will go down; Parimelazhagar glosses சுமக்க as bowing. 680 has the small bow to the great to obtain terms. 488 adds what 680 does not say: that such bowing can be a way of waiting for the stronger side's fall.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:680","b":"k:861","type":"agrees","note":"861 says to avoid standing against the strong (வலியார்க்கு மாறேற்றல் ஓம்புக). 680 describes what that means for a weak party: those of small domain, fearing the trembling of their own side (உள்நடுங்கல் அஞ்சி), bow to the greater and accept terms if they can get them (குறைபெறின் கொள்வர் பெரியார்ப் பணிந்து). 861 gives the rule and 680 the settlement that follows from it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:680","b":"k:967","type":"tension","note":"967, on honour, says it is better to be called ruined as one stands than to live by going behind those who are hostile to one (ஒட்டார்பின் சென்று வாழ்தலின்). 680 says the small will bow to the great and take what terms they can. The conflict depends on reading 967's ஒட்டார் as covering a stronger enemy; Parimelazhagar limits it to following scorners for a living, but he also remarks on 680 that bowing is not the wish of those with honour, which is why the verse says 'they will accept' as a report of worldly practice.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:681","b":"k:502","type":"agrees","note":"502 places trust in one born in a good family (குடிப்பிறந்து), free of faults and with a shame that recoils from blemish. 681 makes birth in a fit family (ஆன்ற குடிப்பிறத்தல்) one of the envoy's three marks. Family is used in both as evidence about a person before he is given a charge, for trust in general in 502 and for the embassy in 681.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:681","b":"k:692","type":"develops","note":"681 asks of the envoy 'qualities that kings desire' (வேந்தவாம் பண்புடைமை) without saying what they are. Manakkudavar glosses the phrase as not desiring what the king desires, which is the content of 692 (மன்னர் விழைப விழையாமை), and Parimelazhagar says this quality is obtained from the following chapter on conduct around kings. 692 therefore supplies, on the commentators' reading, what 681 leaves unstated.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:681","b":"k:992","type":"same_image","note":"The first line of 681 and of 992 is the same: அன்புடைமை ஆன்ற குடிப்பிறத்தல். 992 says these two are what is called courtesy (பண்புடைமை என்னும் வழக்கு); 681 lists them and adds a third, the qualities kings desire (வேந்தவாம் பண்புடைமை), as the nature of one who carries a message. The envoy's qualification is thus the book's general definition of courtesy with a courtly requirement added.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:683","b":"k:722","type":"agrees","note":"722 says those who can make what they have learned go home before the learned are called learned among the learned (கற்றாருள் கற்றார்). 683 uses the same construction for the envoy: he must be strong in the treatises among those who know treatises (நூலாருள் நூல்வல்லன்). Both measure learning by standing among the learned, not against the unlearned.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:684","b":"k:407","type":"qualifies","note":"684 counts presence (உரு) among the three things an envoy needs. 407 says the fine looks of one without fine, penetrating understanding are those of a well-made clay doll (மண்மாண் புனைபாவை). 407 supplies the limit on 684's உரு: appearance counts only in the union (செறிவு) with understanding and learning that 684 itself demands.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:684","b":"k:636","type":"agrees","note":"636 asks what subtle scheme can stand before those who have keen intelligence together with the treatises (மதிநுட்பம் நூலோடு உடையார்க்கு). 684 requires of the envoy understanding and well-examined learning (அறிவு ... ஆராய்ந்த கல்வி) held together. Parimelazhagar glosses the first term in each verse as natural intelligence and the second as acquired learning, so both verses ask for the two in combination, for the minister and for the envoy.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:685","b":"k:696","type":"parallel_domain","note":"696 tells one who serves a king to say what is needed and not disliked, in a way that is wanted (வெறுப்பில வேண்டுப வேட்பச் சொலல்). 685 has the envoy remove what offends and speak so as to please (தூவாத நீக்கி நகச்சொல்லி) while securing good for his own side. The same manner of speech is prescribed toward one's own king in 696 and toward a foreign king in 685; in both the pleasing manner serves what has to be said.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:686","b":"k:724","type":"agrees","note":"724 says to make what one has learned go home before the learned (கற்றார்முன் கற்ற செலச்சொல்லி). 686 uses the same two terms for the envoy: having learned (கற்று), he speaks so that it goes home (செலச்சொல்லி). In both, learning is complete only when it can be delivered to a demanding audience, the assembly in 724 and a hostile court in 686.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:687","b":"k:675","type":"parallel_domain","note":"675 says to act after thinking through resources, means, time, deed and place (காலம் ... இடனொடு ... எண்ணிச் செயல்). 687 uses the same terms for speech: consider the time, know the place, think it through and then speak (காலங் கருதி இடனறிந்து எண்ணி உரைப்பான்). The envoy's speech is planned by the same checklist the book gives for any deed.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:687","b":"k:696","type":"parallel_domain","note":"696 tells the courtier to read the king's mood and consider the time (குறிப்பறிந்து காலங் கருதி) before speaking. 687 has the best envoy know what is due, consider the time (காலங் கருதி) and know the place before he speaks. The phrase காலங் கருதி is common to both, so the discipline for speaking to one's own king is the same as for speaking to another's.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:687","b":"k:712","type":"agrees","note":"712 says those who know how words work should discern the opening (இடைதெரிந்து), understand well and then speak; Parimelazhagar takes இடை as the receptive moment of the assembly. 687 asks the envoy to consider the time, which Parimelazhagar likewise glosses as the listener's receptive state (செவ்வி). Both make the listener's readiness a condition of speaking.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:688","b":"k:298","type":"develops","note":"298 says outer purity comes by water and inner purity is seen through truth (அகந்தூய்மை வாய்மையால் காணப்படும்). 688 names தூய்மை first among the envoy's marks and then requires வாய்மை along with all three. 298 gives the reason the two words belong together: truthfulness is how inner purity shows.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:688","b":"k:383","type":"parallel_domain","note":"383 names three things that never leave a ruler: promptness, learning and resolve (தூங்காமை கல்வி துணிவுடைமை இம்மூன்றும்). 688 has the same frame for the envoy who repeats the king's message: purity, support and resolve, these three (தூய்மை துணைமை துணிவுடைமை இம்மூன்றின்). துணிவுடைமை is the term common to king and envoy; the other two differ with the office.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:688","b":"k:501","type":"develops","note":"501 says a man is to be trusted after being tested by virtue, wealth, pleasure and fear for life. 688 lists the faithful envoy's marks as purity, support, resolve and truthfulness (தூய்மை துணைமை துணிவுடைமை ... வாய்மை). Parimelazhagar explains தூய்மை as being clean in respect of wealth and pleasure and துணிவு as not holding back for fear of harm, which makes 688's qualities the passes of 501's tests.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:689","b":"k:139","type":"parallel_domain","note":"139 says that for people of good conduct it is not possible to speak evil with the mouth even by a slip (வழுக்கியும் வாயாற் சொலல்). 689 asks the same of the envoy in his own field: a firmness whose mouth does not let slip a word that brings blemish (வடுமாற்றம் வாய்சோரா). What Book 1 treats as a result of character, 689 makes a condition for carrying the king's word. (This edition prints வாய்சேரா; the commentaries read வாய்சோரா.)","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:689","b":"k:642","type":"develops","note":"642 says to guard against a lapse in speech (சொல்லின்கட் சோர்வு) because both gain and ruin come from it. 689 is that rule at the point where the stakes are the king's: the envoy must not let a word that discredits slip from his mouth (வாய்சோரா); Parimelazhagar adds that the slip in question would come from fear for himself. The verb சோர் is shared, and 689 names the hardness (வன்கண்) that prevents the lapse 642 warns of.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:690","b":"k:638","type":"agrees","note":"638 says that even if the king destroys good counsel and knows nothing himself, the one at his side owes it to him to say what is good for him (உறுதி ... கூறல் கடன்). 690 says the envoy secures the lord's good (உறுதி) even at the cost of his own life. Both use உறுதி for the king's real interest and hold the servant to it under threat, from his own king in 638 and from a foreign one in 690.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:690","b":"k:723","type":"qualifies","note":"723 says those who will die among enemies are easy to find and those who do not fear in an assembly are rare (பகையகத்துச் சாவார் எளியர் அரியர் அவையகத்து அஞ்சாதவர்). 690 asks the envoy to face death (இறுதி) while speaking in a hostile court. 723 separates the two kinds of courage and rates the second as rarer; the envoy of 690 needs both together, which indicates how much the verse asks.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:690","b":"k:778","type":"parallel_domain","note":"778 says warriors who do not fear for their life when battle comes (உறின் உயிர் அஞ்சா மறவர்) do not lose their distinction even if their lord is angry. 690 says the envoy is one who, even if it brings his end (இறுதி பயப்பினும்), does not fall short and secures what is good for his lord (இறைவற்கு உறுதி). The envoy's office is given the soldier's standard, with both verses naming the lord (இறைவன்) as the one served.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:691","b":"k:896","type":"develops","note":"691 compares the king to a fire one warms at from a measured distance. 896 says one burned by fire may still survive, but those who go on offending the great will not (எரியால் சுடப்படினும் உய்வுண்டாம் உய்யார் பெரியார்ப் பிழைத்தொழுகுவார்). Read together, the fire in 691 understates the danger: 896 ranks the offended great above fire, though Parimelazhagar takes its பெரியார் as those great in austerity, not kings.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:691","b":"k:1104","type":"same_image","note":"691 tells those who serve quarrelsome kings (இகல்வேந்தர்) to be like people warming at a fire, neither moving off nor closing in (அகலாது அணுகாது தீக்காய்வார் போல்க). In 1104 a lover asks where she got a fire that burns when he withdraws and is cool when he comes near (நீங்கின் தெறூஉம் குறுகுங்கால் தண்ணென்னும் தீ). The king's fire burns at close range and the lover's reverses that rule, so the court calls for a kept middle distance and the love speech for none.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:692","b":"k:179","type":"parallel_domain","note":"692 says that not desiring what kings desire (மன்னர் விழைப விழையாமை) brings lasting prosperity through those same kings; Parimelazhagar takes விழைப as the enjoyments, adornment and precedence proper to a king. 179 says Fortune herself comes to the wise who, knowing virtue, do not covet (அறனறிந்து வெஃகா அறிவுடையார்ச் சேரும் ... திரு). Both make not-wanting the route by which wealth arrives, once as court prudence and once as virtue.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:693","b":"k:891","type":"agrees","note":"693 says those who would guard themselves should guard against the gravest things (போற்றின் அரியவை போற்றல்), which Parimelazhagar reads as faults a king cannot forgive. 891 says not slighting the power of those able to act is the chief of all the guarding done by those who guard themselves (போற்றுவார் போற்றலுள் எல்லாம் தலை). Both use போற்றல் for self-protection and locate its first object in one's dealings with the powerful.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:694","b":"k:715","type":"parallel_domain","note":"694 says that in the presence of the great one should suppress whispering and shared smiles (செவிச்சொல்லும் சேர்ந்த நகையும் அவித்தொழுகல்). 715 calls the restraint of not speaking first among elders (முதுவருள் முந்து கிளவாச் செறிவு) the best of good things. Court and assembly get the same rule of holding speech back before superiors; 694 extends it to private asides and laughter, which Parimelazhagar says the king would take as aimed at himself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:695","b":"k:1076","type":"develops","note":"695 treats the king's secret (மறை) as something to be heard only when he lets it go. 1076 says the base are like a beaten drum because they carry to others the secrets they have heard (தாம் கேட்ட மறை பிறர்க்கு உய்த்துரைக்கலான்). The two give the two ends of handling a secret: restraint in acquiring it in 695, and the failure to hold it in 1076.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:696","b":"k:701","type":"develops","note":"696 opens with குறிப்பறிந்து, knowing the king's mood, as the first condition of speaking to him. 701 praises the one who looks and knows what is meant without its being said (கூறாமை நோக்கிக் குறிப்பறிவான்) as an ornament to the earth. The skill 696 names in passing is taken up as the subject of the next chapter, beginning with 701.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:697","b":"k:200","type":"agrees","note":"697 says to speak what is wanted and never to say what has no use, even if asked (வினையில எஞ்ஞான்றும் கேட்பினும் சொல்லாவிடல்). 200 gives the general rule: say words that have use, do not say words that have none (சொல்லிற் பயனிலாச் சொல்). The court verse applies the household rule and tightens it, since here even the king's own request does not license useless talk.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:698","b":"k:667","type":"parallel_domain","note":"698 forbids belittling a king on the grounds of youth or kinship (இளையர் இனமுறையர் என்றிகழார்). 667 says one must not scorn by outward form, since the great rolling chariot depends on its linchpin (உருவுகண்டு எள்ளாமை வேண்டும்). Both reject judging a person by a surface measure, whether age, family relation or size, and point instead to the function he carries.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:700","b":"k:801","type":"qualifies","note":"700 says the familiarity (கெழுதகைமை) that does improper things on the thought 'we are old associates' (பழையம் எனக்கருதி) brings ruin. 801 defines old friendship (பழைமை) as friendship that in no way cuts down the liberties of intimacy (கிழமையைக் கீழ்ந்திடா நட்பு). The liberty that defines friendship between equals is thus withdrawn where the other party is a king, as Parimelazhagar reads 700.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:700","b":"k:805","type":"tension","note":"700 says intimacy that does what is improper brings ruin (பண்பல்ல செய்யும் கெழுதகைமை கேடு தரும்). 805 says that when friends do something hurtful one should understand it as folly or as great intimacy (பேதைமை ஒன்றோ பெருங்கிழமை என்றுணர்க நோதக்க நட்டார் செயின்). The conflict holds on Manakkudavar's gloss of 700, which speaks of the nature of friendship in general; on Parimelazhagar's, 700 concerns a king and the two verses address different relationships.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies"},{"a":"k:701","b":"k:1100","type":"parallel_domain","note":"701 praises the one who knows what is meant by looking, without its being said (கூறாமை நோக்கிக் குறிப்பறிவான்). 1100, in the love chapter that has the same title குறிப்பறிதல், says that when eyes agree with eyes in their look, spoken words have no use (வாய்ச்சொற்கள் என்ன பயனும் இல). Reading without speech is a minister's rare skill in 701 and the ordinary condition of two lovers in 1100.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:703","b":"k:445","type":"develops","note":"703 urges the king to pay any price for those who read signs. 445 says that since counsellors serve as the ruler's eyes (சூழ்வார் கண்ணாக ஒழுகலான்), he must choose them with care. 445 gives the reason behind 703: the king sees through his advisers, so an adviser who can read what is unsaid is worth what Parimelazhagar lists as wealth, land, elephants and horses.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:703","b":"k:794","type":"same_image","note":"703 says to give whatever it takes from one's resources and secure those who read a sign by a sign (யாது கொடுத்தும் கொளல்). 794 says the friendship of one of good family who is ashamed of blame should be had even by giving (கொடுத்தும் கொளல்வேண்டும் நட்பு). The shared formula marks the two kinds of person the book thinks worth paying for, the perceptive adviser and the friend with a sense of shame.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:704","b":"k:410","type":"develops","note":"704 stops at saying that those who cannot grasp the unspoken are 'different' (வேறு) from those who can. 410 says others stand to those who have studied the shining books as beasts stand to people (விலங்கொடு மக்கள் அனையர்). Parimelazhagar glosses the வேறு of 704 with this very comparison, the idea being that they are animals, so 410 states outright what 704 leaves implied.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:704","b":"k:993","type":"agrees","note":"704 says those who grasp what is meant without being told and the rest are alike in limbs (உறுப்போ ரனையரால்) and yet different. 993 says likeness of limbs is not likeness among people (உறுப்பொத்தல் மக்களொப்பு அன்றால்), and true likeness is likeness of character. Both deny that the shared body makes people alike, with perception the dividing line in 704 and பண்பு in 993.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:704","b":"k:1071","type":"parallel_domain","note":"704 says readers of the unspoken and others share the same limbs but are different (வேறு). 1071 says the base look just like people (மக்களே போல்வர் கயவர்), a resemblance the speaker has seen nowhere else. The same figure of outward human likeness hiding an inner difference serves a point about intelligence in 704 and about moral baseness in 1071.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:705","b":"k:393","type":"parallel_domain","note":"705 says eyes that cannot read another's meaning are of no use. 393 says only the learned have eyes, and the unlearned have two sores in the face (முகத்திரண்டு புண்ணுடையர் கல்லாதவர்). In both the physical eye is treated as worthless without a trained capacity behind it, learning in one and the reading of signs in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:705","b":"k:574","type":"parallel_domain","note":"705 asks what use eyes are among the limbs if they cannot read a sign by a sign (என்ன பயத்தவோ கண்). 574 asks what eyes without measured kindly regard do, beyond seeming to be there in the face (உளபோல் முகத்தெவன் செய்யும் ... கண்ணோட்டம் இல்லாத கண்). Each denies the eye its name unless it does more than see, perceiving another's mind in 705 and looking kindly in 574.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:706","b":"k:959","type":"parallel_domain","note":"706 says the face shows what fills the heart, as crystal shows what is beside it. 959 says the sprout shows what lay in the ground, and the spoken word shows the lineage of the one born in it (நிலத்தில் கிடந்தமை கால்காட்டும்). Both rest on காட்டும்: something visible discloses a hidden source, mood in the face and family in speech.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:707","b":"k:830","type":"tension","note":"707 says nothing is as knowing as the face, since whether one is pleased or angry it gets there first (உவப்பினும் காயினும் தான்முந்துறும்). 830 advises that when an enemy turns friend one should befriend with the face and let go in the heart (முகநட்டு அகநட்பு ஒரீஇ விடல்). If the face always runs ahead of the mind, as 707 says, the control 830 recommends should not be possible; the two agree only if 707 describes the unguarded face.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:707","b":"k:1277","type":"parallel_domain","note":"707 says the face is ahead of the person in showing pleasure or anger (தான் முந்துறும்). In 1277 a woman says her bangles knew before she did that her lover had left (நம்மினும் முன்னம் உணர்ந்த வளை). Both give the body a knowledge that arrives before the mind's own, a general observation in 707 and a lover's remark on her thinning arms in 1277.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:708","b":"k:1100","type":"parallel_domain","note":"708 says that if one has people who look within and understand what has happened, it is enough to stand looking them in the face (முகம்நோக்கி நிற்க அமையும்). 1100 says that once eyes agree with eyes, spoken words are of no use. Both make speech unnecessary between the right pair; Parimelazhagar adds for 708 that going on to speak would lessen both parties, a concern for dignity the love verse does not have.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:710","b":"k:796","type":"same_image","note":"710 says the measuring rod (அளக்குங்கோல்) of those who call themselves subtle is nothing but the eye. 796 says ruin has one benefit: it is a rod that measures one's people to full length (கிளைஞரை நீட்டி அளப்பதோர் கோல்). The book uses the measuring rod twice for gauging other people, the eye taking a present reading of a mind in 710 and adversity testing loyalty over time in 796.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:711","b":"k:721","type":"develops","note":"Both verses end with the identical line சொல்லின் தொகையறிந்த தூய்மையவர். 711 commands such people to know the assembly before speaking; 721 says that, having known the kinds (வகையறிந்து), they do not let their speech slip before the capable (வல்லவை வாய்சோரார்). The repeated line makes 721 the outcome of 711's instruction: knowing the assembly is what removes the fear that causes slips.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:712","b":"k:696","type":"parallel_domain","note":"712 asks the speaker in an assembly to discern the opening (இடைதெரிந்து), which Parimelazhagar glosses as the hearers' readiness to listen. 696 asks the same of one speaking to a king: know his mood and consider the time (குறிப்பறிந்து காலங் கருதி) and say it so that it is wanted. The timing rule is the same before an assembly and before a single ruler.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:713","b":"k:401","type":"agrees","note":"713 says those who take up speaking without knowing the assembly (அவையறியார்) do not know the kinds of speech and have no ability at all. 401 says holding forth in an assembly (கோட்டி கொளல்) without full learning is like playing dice with no marked board. Each names a different missing precondition for speaking in an assembly, knowledge of the hearers in 713 and knowledge of the books in 401, and each treats the speech that results as empty.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:714","b":"k:425","type":"tension","note":"714 tells the speaker to change with the audience, bright before the bright and blank before the blank. 425 says understanding is what does not open and close (மலர்தலும் கூம்பலும் இல்லது அறிவு). If 425's second half is read generally, as steadiness of bearing, the two disagree about whether the wise adapt their display; on Parimelazhagar's reading, where 425 is about constancy in friendship with the world, the conflict disappears.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:714","b":"k:844","type":"same_image","note":"714 uses a colour pair for intelligence: be bright (ஒள்ளியர்) before the bright, and take the colour of white plaster (வான்சுதை வண்ணம்) before the blank (வெளியார்). 844 uses the same pair: whiteness (வெண்மை) is the conceit that says 'we have brightness' (ஒண்மை உடையம் யாம்). Read together, the learned person of 714 puts on whiteness knowingly before those who, by 844, are white because they think themselves bright.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:715","b":"k:419","type":"agrees","note":"715 praises the restraint (செறிவு) of not speaking first among elders. 419 says a mouth that bows (வணங்கிய வாய்) is hard to have for those without fine listening. Both treat modest speech as a mark of what a person has learned, and 419 supplies the source 715 leaves unstated: the habit of listening.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:716","b":"k:642","type":"develops","note":"642 gives the general rule: since gain and ruin come from speech, guard against a lapse in it (சொல்லின்கட் சோர்வு). 716 applies it to one setting, a slip before the widely learned, and measures the damage by comparing it to falling from the path (ஆற்றின் நிலைதளர்ந்தற்று), which Parimelazhagar reads as a renouncer's lapse from the good path.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:716","b":"k:957","type":"parallel_domain","note":"716 says a slip (இழுக்கு) before those who have taken in wide learning is like faltering from one's footing on the path. 957 says a fault in the well-born shows up high, like the spot on the moon (மதிக்கண் மறுப்போல்). In both, the weight of a fault depends on where it is seen, the standing of the witnesses in 716 and of the person in 957, and not only on its size.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:717","b":"k:404","type":"qualifies","note":"717 says the learning of those who have studied and understood shines among those able to examine words without fault (சொல்தெரிதல் வல்லார் அகத்து). 404 says the wise do not accept an unlearned person's brightness (ஒட்பம்), however good it is. The same expert hearers decide both cases: before them real learning becomes visible and untaught cleverness is not accepted.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"k:719","b":"k:728","type":"qualifies","note":"719 and 728 share the phrase நல்லவையுள் நன்கு செலச் சொல்லு-. 728 says those who cannot speak tellingly in a good assembly are of no use though they have learned much; 719 tells those who can do so not to speak in a mean assembly even by forgetting (பொச்சாந்தும் சொல்லற்க). Together they set the limits: silence in a good assembly is a failure, and silence in a mean one is required.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:719","b":"k:849","type":"agrees","note":"719 forbids speaking good things in a mean assembly. 849 gives a reason in another chapter: one who shows something to a person who does not see becomes himself unseeing (காணாதான் காட்டுவான் தான்காணான்), while that person goes on seeing as he saw. Parimelazhagar makes the same point on 719, that both assemblies would then look down on the speaker.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:720","b":"k:1000","type":"parallel_domain","note":"720 says holding forth before those not of one's kind is like nectar spilled in a dirty yard (அங்கணத்துள் உக்க அமிழ்து). 1000 says great wealth in the hands of one without courtesy is like good milk spoiled by the fault of its vessel (கலந்தீமையால் திரிந்தற்று). Both describe a fine liquid ruined by where it lands, and so place the loss in the receiver and not in the thing given.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:721","b":"k:647","type":"agrees","note":"721 says those who know the body of words and the kinds of assembly do not let their speech slip before the capable (வாய்சோரார்), which Parimelazhagar reads as not slipping out of fear. 647 lists the same qualities, able to speak, without slips, without fear (சொலல்வல்லன் சோர்விலன் அஞ்சான்), and says such a person is hard for anyone to defeat. 647 states the payoff of the fearlessness that 721 describes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:721","b":"k:716","type":"develops","note":"716 says a slip (இழுக்கு) before those of wide learning is like falling from the path. 721 answers from the next chapter: those who know words and know the assembly do not slip before the capable (வல்லவை வாய்சோரார்). One verse gives the cost of the slip and the other says what prevents it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:722","b":"k:424","type":"agrees","note":"722 says those who make what they have learned go home before the learned (கற்ற செலச் சொல்லுவார்) are called learned among the learned. 424 defines understanding with the same verb: saying things so that they go in easily (எண்பொருளவாகச் செலச்சொல்லி) and seeing the subtle meaning in what others say. Both measure learning or understanding by whether it reaches the hearer, not by what the speaker holds.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:722","b":"k:650","type":"agrees","note":"722 gives the title 'learned among the learned' to those who can convey their learning to the learned. 650 gives the reverse case: those who cannot unfold what they have learned so that others understand (கற்றது உணர விரித்துரையாதார்) are like flowers that bloom in clusters without scent. Together they treat communication as part of learning itself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:723","b":"k:762","type":"tension","note":"723 calls those who die amid enemies எளியர், easy to find. 762 says the hardness that does not fear wounds when the king falters is rare except in an old army (தொல்படைக்கல்லால் அரிது). If 723's first half is read as saying battle courage is common, the two disagree about how rare it is; if 762 is limited to courage in defeat and with reduced numbers, they do not.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:723","b":"k:872","type":"parallel_domain","note":"723 says those who will die amid enemies are easy to find, and those who do not fear within an assembly are hard to find (அவையகத்து அஞ்சாதவர்). 872 says one may take on the enmity of those who plough with the bow but not of those who plough with the word (சொல்லேர் உழவர்). Both rank the word above the weapon, 723 by how rare the courage is and 872 by how dangerous the opponent is.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:724","b":"k:424","type":"agrees","note":"724 joins two acts: convey your learning (செலச்சொல்லி) and take in what those more learned have. 424 joins the same two as its definition of understanding: say things so that they go in (செலச்சொல்லி) and see the subtle meaning in what others say (பிறர்வாய் நுண்பொருள் காண்பது). What 424 calls understanding, 724 presents as what a fearless speaker gains from an assembly.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:725","b":"k:391","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Both verses open with the command to learn. 391 says learn without flaw and then stand in keeping with it (கற்க கசடற ... நிற்க அதற்குத் தக); 725 says learn with measure so as to give a fearless reply (மாற்றங் கொடுத்தற் பொருட்டு). 391 gives learning its end in conduct, and 725 gives it a second end in public speech.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:725","b":"k:645","type":"develops","note":"725 says to learn by the proper path and with measure (ஆற்றின் அளவறிந்து கற்க) in order to reply in the assembly without fear; Parimelazhagar takes அளவு as the science of logic. 645 says to speak a word only after knowing no other word can defeat it (வெல்லுஞ்சொல் இன்மை அறிந்து). 725 names the training that makes 645's test possible.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:726","b":"k:534","type":"parallel_domain","note":"726 asks what a sword is to those who are not hard-eyed, and what books are to those who fear a subtle assembly (நுண்ணவை அஞ்சுபவர்க்கு). 534 says the fearful have no fortress (அச்சமுடையார்க்கு அரணில்லை), which Parimelazhagar explains as a fortress being of no use to them. In each, fear cancels an instrument that is sound in itself: sword, books, fort.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:726","b":"k:647","type":"agrees","note":"726 says books are nothing to one who fears a subtle assembly. 647 says the one who can speak, does not slip and does not fear (அஞ்சான்) is hard for anyone to defeat, and Parimelazhagar takes that fearlessness as fearlessness of the assembly. 647 states positively the condition whose absence 726 says makes learning useless.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:728","b":"k:140","type":"parallel_domain","note":"728 and 140 have the same form: 'though they have learned much'. In 140, those who have not learned to live in accord with the world are without understanding though they have learned much (பலகற்றும்). In 728, those who cannot speak well in a good assembly are without use though they have learned many things (பல்லவை கற்றும்). Each names a practical ability without which the amount learned does not count.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:729","b":"k:395","type":"qualifies","note":"395 calls the unlearned the lowest (கடையரே கல்லாதவர்). 729 uses the same word to put someone beneath them: those who have studied and understood and still fear an assembly of the good are lower than the unlearned (கல்லாதவரின் கடை). 729 thus limits 395's ranking, since learning that is not voiced does not lift a person above the bottom rank and, as Parimelazhagar explains, leaves them with only the labour of having studied.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:730","b":"k:214","type":"same_image","note":"730 counts those who cannot voice their learning as equal to those who are not, though they exist. 214 says one who knows what is fitting truly lives and anyone else is placed among the dead (செத்தாருள் வைக்கப்படும்). Both apply the figure of a living person counted as dead or absent to someone who gives nothing to others, wealth in 214 and learning in 730; Parimelazhagar reads 730's இல்லார் as the dead.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:731","b":"k:239","type":"develops","note":"731 sets unfailing yield and worthy people (தக்கார்) side by side as marks of a land. 239 says land that bears a body without renown (இசையிலா யாக்கை பொறுத்த நிலம்) declines in its blameless rich produce (வண்பயன் குன்றும்). 239 makes a causal claim where 731 only lists: the land's yield depends on the quality of the people it carries.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:731","b":"k:545","type":"develops","note":"731 makes unfailing yield (தள்ளா விளையுள்) one of three things that must come together (சேர்வது) for a land. 545 says rain and yield (பெயலும் விளையுளும்) gather (தொக்கு) in the land of the king who wields the sceptre as it should be wielded. Read together, the yield that defines a land in 731 is traced in 545 to just rule.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:731","b":"k:1032","type":"develops","note":"Parimelazhagar takes விளையுள் in 731 to stand for the cultivators, since the verse lists it beside two kinds of people (தக்கார், செல்வர்). 1032 calls those who plough the linchpin (ஆணி) of the world because they carry all who go off to other work. 1032 gives the reason the cultivators come first in 731's list.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:732","b":"k:15","type":"develops","note":"732 says a land yields greatly because ruin is rare there (அருங்கேட்டால் ஆற்ற விளைவது). 15 says rain is what ruins (கெடுப்பதூஉம்) and also what raises up the ruined. Parimelazhagar's list of the கேடு meant in 732 begins with too much rain and no rain, so 15 names the chief source of the ruin that 732 wants to be rare.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:732","b":"k:736","type":"develops","note":"732 asks that ruin be rare (அருங்கேடு) so that the land yields abundantly. 736 goes a step further: the foremost land does not know ruin, and its richness does not shrink even where ruin has come (கெட்ட இடத்தும் வளங்குன்றா). 732 states the ordinary standard and 736 the highest one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:733","b":"k:552","type":"qualifies","note":"733 defines a land as one that bears burdens arriving all at once and still agrees to give the king his whole due (இறையொருங்கு நேர்வது). 552 says that asking (இரவு) by one who stands with the sceptre is like a man standing with a spear saying 'hand it over'. The land's willingness to pay in 733 is not a licence for the king to demand: Manakkudavar reads 733's burdens as levies beyond the fixed due, which is the situation 552 condemns.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:733","b":"k:990","type":"same_image","note":"In 733 the land bears the load that comes upon it (பொறை ... தாங்கி). In 990 the great earth will not bear its load (தாங்காது ... பொறை) if the worthy decline in worth. The same words describe a land's capacity to carry weight, and 990 makes that capacity depend on the character of its best people.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:734","b":"k:735","type":"develops","note":"734 lists what must not reach a land from outside or by nature: hunger, disease and the enemy who destroys (செறுபகை). 735 lists what must not exist inside it: many factions (பல்குழு), the inner enemy that lays waste (உட்பகை) and murderous bands that harass the king. The two verses divide the threats into external and internal.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:735","b":"k:550","type":"develops","note":"735 says a land has no murderous bands that harass the king (வேந்தலைக்கும் கொல்குறும்பு). 550 says a king's punishing the cruel with death (கொலையிற் கொடியாரை வேந்தொறுத்தல்) is like weeding the young crop. 550 gives the ruler's means of producing the condition 735 describes; Parimelazhagar on 735 likewise says these three are to be removed by the king and the inhabitants.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:735","b":"k:889","type":"develops","note":"735 requires a land to be without the inner enemy that lays waste (பாழ்செய்யும் உட்பகை). 889 says inner enmity, though as small as a split sesame seed (எட்பகவு), holds ruin within it (உள்ளதாங் கேடு). 889 explains why 735 demands its complete absence and not just its reduction.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:736","b":"k:955","type":"parallel_domain","note":"736 calls foremost the land whose richness does not shrink even where ruin has come (கெட்ட இடத்தும் வளங்குன்றா). 955 says an old family does not part from its character even when what it has to give has fallen away (வழங்குவது உள்வீழ்ந்தக் கண்ணும் ... பண்பில் தலைப்பிரிதல் இன்று). Both use the same 'even when' construction to test excellence by what remains after loss, one for a land and one for a family.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:737","b":"k:381","type":"develops","note":"737 names a strong fort (வல்லரண்) among the limbs (உறுப்பு) of a land, with its waters and hill. 381 names the fort (அரண்) and the people or land (குடி) as two of the six things a king must have, which Parimelazhagar calls his six limbs (அங்கம்). 737 nests one of the king's limbs inside another, which fits the order of the chapters: land, then fort.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:738","b":"k:734","type":"agrees","note":"738 lists five ornaments (அணி) of a land: freedom from disease (பிணியின்மை), wealth, yield, happiness and protection (ஏமம்). 734 defines a land by three absences: severe hunger, unceasing disease (ஓவாப் பிணி) and destructive enemies. The two lists match as positive and negative: yield answers hunger, பிணியின்மை answers disease, and ஏமம் answers the enemy, while 738 adds wealth and happiness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:739","b":"k:594","type":"parallel_domain","note":"739 says lands are those whose riches come without being sought (நாடா வளத்தன). 594 says prosperity itself asks the way and goes (ஆக்கம் அதர்வினாய்ச் செல்லும்) to the person of unwavering drive. Both picture wealth arriving unsought, but 594 makes it the result of the person's energy, where 739 makes it a property of the land.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:739","b":"k:1040","type":"qualifies","note":"739 praises land whose riches need no seeking. 1040 says the good woman called Land laughs (நிலமென்னும் நல்லாள் நகும்) at those who say 'we have nothing' and sit idle (அசைஇ இருப்பாரை). 1040 limits 739: the land has riches to give, but not to those who do nothing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:740","b":"k:558","type":"develops","note":"740 says a land's excellences bring no benefit (பயமின்று) without accord between it and its king. 558 says that under the sceptre of a king who does not do justice, having is more painful than not having (இன்மையின் இன்னாது உடைமை). 558 shows one way the wealth praised in this chapter turns useless or worse under the wrong ruler.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:740","b":"k:770","type":"parallel_domain","note":"740 says that a land with every excellence is of no use without accord with its king. 770 says that though an army has plenty of steadfast men (நிலைமக்கள் சால உடைத்தெனினும்), there is no army where there are no leaders (தலைமக்கள் இல்வழி இல்). In both, a body that is complete in its own parts is counted as nothing without its head.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:741","b":"k:569","type":"develops","note":"741 says a fort matters to those who fear attack and look to their own safety. 569 says a king who has not made his defences (சிறைசெய்யா வேந்தன்) will, when war comes, be struck with fear and quickly perish (வெருவந்து வெய்து கெடும்). 569 states the cost of ignoring 741: without a fort prepared beforehand, fear has nowhere to turn.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:742","b":"k:737","type":"same_image","note":"742 says a fort has clear water (மணிநீர்), open ground, a hill (மலை) and a forest of fine shade. 737 lists as limbs of a land the two waters (இருபுனல்), a well-placed hill (வாய்ந்த மலை), the water that comes from it, and a strong fort. The same features of the terrain, water and hill, are counted first as a land's resources and then as a fort's defences, and 737 already names the fort as part of the land.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:743","b":"k:421","type":"parallel_domain","note":"743 defines the fort as a wall complete in four measures: height, breadth, strength and difficulty of approach (உயர்வு அகலம் திண்மை அருமை). 421 calls understanding the tool that guards against ruin and an inner fort that enemies cannot destroy (செறுவார்க்கும் உள்ளழிக்கலாகா அரண்). 421 moves the fort inside the person: the quality 743 seeks in masonry, that it cannot be broken into, 421 claims for understanding.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:745","b":"k:499","type":"develops","note":"745 says a fort is hard to take, holds stored food (கொண்ட கூழ்த்து) and is easy for those inside to hold their position in (அகத்தார் நிலைக்கு எளிது). 499 says that even people with no good defences or standing (சிறைநலனும் சீரும் இலரெனினும்) are hard to take on in the land where they live (உறைநிலத்தோடு ஒட்டல் அரிது). 499 shows that the insiders' advantage of position, which 745 asks the fort to give, already belongs to home ground without walls.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:746","b":"k:762","type":"parallel_domain","note":"746 wants in a fort good men who help at the place where it would otherwise fall; Parimelazhagar glosses இடத்து as the point of destruction by those outside. 762 says the hardness that does not fear a wound when things give way and the force is reduced (உலைவிடத்து ஊறஞ்சா வன்கண்) is rare except in an ancient army. Both measure fighting men by what they do at the moment of collapse, in the fort and in the field.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:746","b":"k:1030","type":"same_image","note":"746 says a fort has every needed thing and good men who help at the critical place (இடத்துதவும் நல்லாள்). 1030 says a family with no good man to stand by and prop it (அடுத்தூன்றும் நல்லாள் இலாத குடி) falls when trouble cuts at its foot. The same word நல்லாள் names the person on whom a fort and a family each depend at the moment of danger; goods alone hold up neither.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:747","b":"k:764","type":"parallel_domain","note":"747 says a fort is hard to seize by encircling, by assault without encircling, or by subversion (அறைப்படுத்தும்), which Parimelazhagar explains as undermining those inside through people they trust. 764 defines an army as one that does not break and cannot be subverted (அறைபோகாதாகி). The same word அறை marks treachery from within as a danger that fort and army must both be proof against, beside open force.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:748","b":"k:493","type":"agrees","note":"748 says a fort is where those who hold it keep their hold (பற்றாற்றி) and defeat even those strong enough to besiege them; Parimelazhagar notes that the smaller force does not just endure but wins. 493 says even those without strength become strong and win (ஆற்றாரும் ஆற்றி அடுப) if they know the place and act against foes while guarding themselves. 748 is 493's rule about place applied to the fort.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:749","b":"k:750","type":"qualifies","note":"749 says a fort has its distinction from what is done in action (வினைமுகத்து வீறெய்தி) so that enemies fall at the front of battle. 750 says that whatever excellence a fort has, it is nothing with those who lack excellence in action (வினைமாட்சி இல்லார்கண் இல்லது). 750 turns 749's praise into a condition; Parimelazhagar says the two verses together show that a fort cannot do without its defenders.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:749","b":"k:767","type":"parallel_domain","note":"749 places a fort's distinction in the actions by which enemies are brought down as soon as battle is joined (முனைமுகத்து); Parimelazhagar explains these as recognising the attack begun and choosing the acts that defeat it. 767 says an army is one that knows the way to bear the battle coming at it (தலைவந்த போர்தாங்கும் தன்மை அறிந்து) and advances. Both ask for trained response to the enemy's opening move, from the walls and in the field.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:750","b":"k:727","type":"parallel_domain","note":"750 says a fort's excellences do not exist for those who lack excellence in action. 727 says the treatise learned by one who fears the assembly is like a bright sword in the hand of a பேடி amid enemies (பகையகத்துப் பேடிகை ஒள்வாள்). In both a good instrument, a fort or learning, is worth nothing in the hands of someone who cannot act when the test comes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:751","b":"k:241","type":"qualifies","note":"751 treats wealth's power to give standing to persons of no account as its distinction. 241 notes the same fact, that material wealth is found even among the base (பொருட்செல்வம் பூரியார் கண்ணும் உள), and uses it to rank wealth below grace, the 'wealth among wealths'. What 751 counts in wealth's favour, 241 counts against it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:752","b":"k:988","type":"qualifies","note":"752 reports that all scorn the poor and all honour the rich. 988 says lack is no disgrace to one who has the firmness called worth (இன்மை ஒருவற்கு இளிவன்று சால்பென்னும் திண்மை). 752 describes how the world behaves; 988 denies that the scorn is deserved where character is present, which Parimelazhagar's gloss on 752 (scorned 'even if they have every good') makes pointed.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:752","b":"k:1046","type":"develops","note":"752 says everyone scorns those who have nothing (இல்லாரை எல்லாரும் எள்ளுவர்). 1046 gives a particular case: even when the poor understand a good matter well and say it, their words go slack (சொற்பொருள் சோர்வு படும்). The general contempt of 752 is shown reaching what a poor person says.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:752","b":"k:1047","type":"develops","note":"752's 'everyone' (எல்லாரும்) scorns the have-nots. 1047 carries that to the limit case: one in poverty not joined to virtue is looked on as a stranger even by the mother who bore him (ஈன்ற தாயானும் பிறன்போல). The pairing shows how far 'everyone' reaches, with 1047 adding the condition அறஞ்சாரா.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:753","b":"k:759","type":"agrees","note":"753 says wealth cuts away darkness (இருளறுக்கும்), which Parimelazhagar takes to be enmity. 759 says no steel is sharper than wealth for cutting the pride of foes (செறுநர் செருக்கறுக்கும்). Both use the verb அறு for what wealth does, once as a lamp and once as a blade; on Parimelazhagar's reading of 753 they make the same claim.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:754","b":"k:39","type":"develops","note":"754 says wealth that came by knowing the right means and without wrong yields virtue and also pleasure (அறன்ஈனும் இன்பமும் ஈனும்). 39 says only what comes through virtue is pleasure (அறத்தான் வருவதே இன்பம்). 754 fits wealth into 39's rule: wealth produces real pleasure only when it was itself acquired without wrong.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:754","b":"k:659","type":"agrees","note":"754 limits its promise to wealth that came without wrong (தீதின்றி வந்த பொருள்). 659 says what was taken making others weep leaves with weeping, while what came by good ways pays later even if lost (பிற்பயக்கும் நற்பாலவை). Both make the manner of acquisition decide what wealth yields.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:755","b":"k:113","type":"agrees","note":"755 says a gain of wealth that does not come with grace and love should be let go, not embraced (புல்லார் புரள விடல்). 113 says gain that comes from leaving fairness should be let go that very day even if it brings only good (அன்றே யொழிய விடல்). Both end on the same command, விடல், for gain acquired the wrong way, one naming love and grace as the test and the other fairness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:755","b":"k:1009","type":"develops","note":"755 tells one to refuse wealth that comes without grace and love. 1009 says what becomes of such wealth: bright wealth amassed by setting love aside and not looking to virtue (அன்பொரீஇ ... அறநோக்காது ஈட்டிய) is taken by others. 1009 supplies a consequence for the wealth 755 rejects.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:757","b":"k:247","type":"agrees","note":"757 says the child grace, born of love, is reared by the nurse called wealth (பொருளென்னும் செல்வச் செவிலி). 247 sets the two side by side: that world is not for those without grace, as this world is not for those without wealth. Both pair அருள் with பொருள்; 247 keeps them parallel, while 757 makes grace depend on wealth to grow.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:757","b":"k:755","type":"qualifies","note":"757 says grace is raised by wealth. 755, in the same chapter, says wealth that does not come with grace and love (அருளொடும் அன்பொடும் வாரா) is to be let go. The dependence runs both ways: grace needs wealth, but only wealth that itself came with grace and love.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:758","b":"k:449","type":"agrees","note":"758 says doing a task with something in one's own hand (தன்கைத்தொன்று உண்டாக) is like watching an elephant fight from a hilltop, which Parimelazhagar explains as acting without fear or strain. 449 says those without capital have no profit (முதலிலார்க்கு ஊதியம் இல்லை). Both make resources in hand the precondition of an undertaking; 758 adds the safety they give.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:758","b":"k:593","type":"same_image","note":"758 praises acting with wealth in hand (கைத்து உண்டாக). 593 says those who hold steady energy as what is in their hand (ஊக்கம் ஒருவந்தம் கைத்துடையார்) do not grieve that they have lost their gains. The same word கைத்து is used for money in 758 and for drive in 593, which treats energy as the holding that matters when the money is gone.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:759","b":"k:546","type":"parallel_domain","note":"759 says wealth is sharper than any steel against foes. 546 says it is not the spear that gives victory but the king's sceptre, if it does not bend (வேலன்று வென்றி தருவது). In both the literal weapon is set below something else, wealth in one and just rule in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:759","b":"k:878","type":"develops","note":"759 says to make wealth, since no steel is sharper for cutting the pride of foes (செறுநர் செருக்கறுக்கும்). 878 says the pride found in enemies dies (மாயும் பகைவர்கண் பட்ட செருக்கு) when one knows the way, builds oneself up and guards oneself (தற்செய்து தற்காப்ப). Both aim at the enemy's செருக்கு; Parimelazhagar glosses 878's தற்செய்து as enlarging oneself, and 759 names wealth as the means.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:759","b":"k:985","type":"parallel_domain","note":"759 calls wealth the sharpest steel (எஃகு) against enemies' pride. 985 says bowing is the weapon (படை) by which the worthy turn their opponents (மாற்றாரை மாற்றும் படை). Each names a non-military thing as the real weapon against foes: resources for the ruler, humility for the person of character.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:760","b":"k:31","type":"same_image","note":"760 makes wealth the route to the other two aims, virtue and pleasure. 31 makes virtue the source of distinction and wealth (சிறப்பு ஈனும் செல்வமும் ஈனும்). Each verse, speaking from within its own book, puts its own aim first as the one from which the others follow.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:760","b":"k:754","type":"qualifies","note":"760 says that for those who have made bright wealth in full, the other two, virtue and pleasure, are easy to have together (ஏனை இரண்டும் ஒருங்கு). 754 says the wealth that yields virtue and pleasure is wealth that came without wrong (தீதின்றி வந்த பொருள்). 754 supplies the condition; Parimelazhagar reads it into 760's ஒண்பொருள் as wealth that comes by the right way.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:760","b":"k:1002","type":"qualifies","note":"760 says abundant wealth makes virtue and pleasure easy to get. 1002 calls it a delusion to hoard without giving on the thought that everything comes from wealth (பொருளானாம் எல்லாமென்று ஈயாது இவறும் மருள்), and says it leads to a low birth. 760's claim holds for wealth that is made and used; held back by a miser, the same belief is called delusion.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:761","b":"k:381","type":"agrees","note":"761 says a conquering army, complete in its parts and unafraid of wounds, is the chief of all a king's wealth (வெறுக்கையுள் எல்லாம் தலை). 381 lists the six things a king must have and puts the army first (படைகுடி கூழ் ...). The placing in 381's list and the ranking in 761 agree.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:761","b":"k:759","type":"same_image","note":"761 counts the army as a form of the king's wealth (வெறுக்கை). 759 counts wealth as a weapon, the sharpest steel against foes (எஃகு). The two neighbouring chapters describe each thing in terms of the other: wealth is a weapon and the army is wealth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:762","b":"k:764","type":"develops","note":"762 says steadfastness in defeat is rare except in an old army (தொல்படை). 764 defines an army by courage that has come down to it (வழிவந்த வன்கண்), along with not collapsing and not being turned by subversion. 764 makes a requirement of what 762 observes, and both use வன்கண் for the quality.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:762","b":"k:806","type":"parallel_domain","note":"762 says the hardness that does not fear harm in a time of decline (தொலைவிடத்து) is found only in an old army (தொல்படை). 806 says those who keep to the bounds do not give up, even in decline (தொலைவிடத்தும்), the bond of those who stood by them of old (தொல்லைக்கண் நின்றார்). Both use the same two words to tie loyalty in bad times to length of standing, in an army and in friendship.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:762","b":"k:955","type":"parallel_domain","note":"762 says only a long-established army stays firm when it is reduced and the king is faltering. 955 says an old family (பழங்குடி) does not part from its nature even when its means of giving have shrunk. In both, age of standing is what keeps character intact under loss.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:763","b":"k:873","type":"qualifies","note":"763 says a crowd of weak enemies is nothing to one strong fighter. 873 says one who, being alone, takes on the enmity of many is more foolish than the mad (தமியனாய்ப் பல்லார் பகைகொள்பவன்). 763 holds only where the one is a cobra and the many are rats; 873 is the warning for a lone person who assumes that.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:764","b":"k:766","type":"develops","note":"764 says an army has courage that has come down to it (வழிவந்த வன்கண்). 766 lists the four things that protect an army: valour, honour, walking the excellent path of those before (மாண்ட வழிச்செலவு) and தேற்றம். 766 spells out the inherited quality of 764 as following the path of earlier warriors, which is how Parimelazhagar reads வழிச்செலவு.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:768","b":"k:750","type":"tension","note":"768 says an army gains stature through its array (படைத்தகையால் பாடு பெறும்) even if it lacks striking power and endurance; Parimelazhagar takes படைத்தகை as splendour of appearance. 750 says a fort, whatever excellences it has, is as nothing with people who lack excellence in action (வினைமாட்சி இல்லார்கண் இல்லது). They conflict if 768's பாடு is read as real military worth; if it means only repute, 768 is a concession and 750 still stands.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:769","b":"k:567","type":"develops","note":"769 names a resentment that will not leave (செல்லாத் துனி) among the things an army must be free of to win; Parimelazhagar says it arises from degrading treatment. 567 says harsh words and punishment beyond bounds are a file that wears down a king's conquering strength (அடுமுரண் தேய்க்கும் அரம்). 567 gives a cause on the king's side for the condition 769 warns of.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:769","b":"k:759","type":"develops","note":"769 says an army wins if it is free of dwindling, of resentment that does not leave, and of poverty (வறுமை). 759 says to make wealth because it is the sharpest weapon against foes. Parimelazhagar links them from both sides: under 769 he says desertion and want arise when the king does not pay, and under 759 that making wealth brings a large army.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:770","b":"k:1030","type":"parallel_domain","note":"770 says an army does not exist without leaders. 1030 says a family with no good person to prop it (அடுத்தூன்றும் நல்லாள் இலாத குடி) falls when trouble cuts at its foot. Both make a group's survival depend on a capable person at its head, in the army and in the household.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:771","b":"k:763","type":"agrees","note":"771 dismisses the enemy by pointing to the many (பலர்) who faced his lord and became stones. 763 asks what it matters if a host of rats roars like the sea, since they perish when the cobra merely breathes (நாகம் உயிர்ப்பக் கெடும்). Both say numbers on the other side count for nothing against one real fighter; Parimelazhagar reads 763 as the hero not fearing a crowd of non-heroes.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:771","b":"k:894","type":"agrees","note":"771 has a warrior warn enemies not to stand before his lord (என்ஐமுன் நில்லன்மின்), since many who did now stand as memorial stones (கல்நின்றவர்). 894 states the rule behind the warning: for the unable to do harm to the able is like beckoning Death with the hand (கூற்றத்தைக் கையால் விளித்தற்று). The battlefield taunt and the political maxim give the same counsel, one from the strong side and one addressed to the weak.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:771","b":"k:1088","type":"same_image","note":"771 is a warrior's boast about a might that turns opponents into memorial stones. In 1088 a lover says his பீடு, which even enemies who had not met him feared in battle (ஞாட்பினுள் நண்ணாரும் உட்கும்), has broken before her bright forehead. The Book of Love borrows the same figure of the warrior whose name alone frightens foes, here as a speaker's way of measuring her effect on him.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:772","b":"k:861","type":"tension","note":"772 prefers the failed throw at an elephant to the successful shot at a hare, and Manakkudavar takes it with 771 as saying the warrior should send the weak away and fight those who can oppose him. 861 advises the reverse: avoid meeting the strong (வலியார்க்கு மாறேற்றல் ஓம்புக) and keep up enmity against the weak (மெலியார்மேல் மேக பகை). The conflict holds if 772 is read as a rule for choosing opponents and 861 as Parimelazhagar reads it; it is the warrior's pride against the king's policy. Kalaignar's gloss of 861 turns it into fighting the strong, which would remove the conflict.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:773","b":"k:987","type":"parallel_domain","note":"773 makes help to a foe in trouble the cutting edge of valour; Parimelazhagar illustrates it with the king of Ayodhya telling his disarmed enemy to go and come back tomorrow. 987 asks what use worth (சால்பு) is if one does not do what is sweet even to those who did one harm (இன்னா செய்தார்க்கும் இனியவே). The ideal of the worthy person in the chapter on சால்பு appears here as the height of a soldier's manhood.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:775","b":"k:686","type":"parallel_domain","note":"775 asks whether it is not a rout (ஒட்டு in this edition, which Parimelazhagar glosses as turning the back) for the fierce if the eye glaring at the enemy closes in a blink when a spear is thrown. 686 requires the envoy to be one who does not fear the eye (கண் அஞ்சான்), which Parimelazhagar explains as the hostile king's angry stare; 585 says the same of the spy. Holding one's gaze under threat is a test in the court as on the field.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:775","b":"k:1129","type":"same_image","note":"775 treats a single blink (இமைப்பின்) before a flung spear as equal to flight. In 1129 the woman says she will not blink because her lover, who lives in her eyes, would be hidden (இமைப்பின் கரப்பாக்கு அறிவல்). Both make the refusal to blink a measure of devotion, to valour in one and, in the speaker's conceit, to the beloved in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:777","b":"k:235","type":"develops","note":"777 says that for those who want fame that circles the earth and do not want life (வேண்டா உயிரார்), tying on the war-anklet is an adornment. 235 says a ruin that is growth and a death that is existence (உளதாகும் சாக்காடு) are possible only for the skilled, which Parimelazhagar explains by the body of fame outliving the body of flesh. 235 gives the reasoning for the warrior's choice in 777.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:777","b":"k:1017","type":"parallel_domain","note":"777 describes warriors who prefer fame (இசை) to life. 1017 says those ruled by shame give up life for shame and will not give up shame for life (நாணால் உயிரைத் துறப்பர்). Both rank a value above staying alive; in one it is public renown won in battle, in the other an inner restraint.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:779","b":"k:772","type":"develops","note":"779 asks who could fault for failure (பிழைத்தது ஒறுக்கிற்பவர்) those who die so as not to break what they vowed (இழைத்தது இகவாமை); Parimelazhagar explains that dying before the vow is accomplished still counts as keeping it. 772 says the spear that missed (பிழைத்த வேல்) the elephant is better to hold than the arrow that hit a hare. Both verses turn on பிழை, a miss, and deny that it is a disgrace when the attempt was great.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:781","b":"k:815","type":"qualifies","note":"781 praises friendship as the best protection. 815 says the poor friendship of small people, which gives no safety even after it is made (செய்து ஏமம் சாரா), is better not had than had. The protection claimed in 781 belongs only to friendship with the right people; made with the wrong ones it guards nothing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:782","b":"k:811","type":"develops","note":"782 contrasts friendships that grow and friendships that wane according to the kind of person. 811 says that with people without character, however ardent they look, it is better for the friendship to shrink than to grow (பெருகலிற் குன்றல் இனிது). The waning that 782 observes in bad friendships is in 811 what one should want for them.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:783","b":"k:811","type":"qualifies","note":"783 says that with people of character (பண்புடையாளர்) the bond grows sweeter with practice. 811 says that with those who lack character (பண்பிலார்), friendship is better shrinking than growing, even if they seem to drink one up with affection. The reward of long practice in 783 depends on பண்பு; without it, more closeness is worse.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:784","b":"k:795","type":"develops","note":"784 gives rebuke as friendship's purpose. 795 turns it into a test for choosing friends: examine and take the friendship of those able to speak so that one weeps, to rebuke (இடித்து) and to teach what is proper. What 784 says a friend is for, 795 tells the reader to look for beforehand.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:785","b":"k:792","type":"tension","note":"Manakkudavar reads 785 as saying many days of acquaintance are not needed to judge a friendship, because for those of the same understanding (உணர்ச்சி) one meeting gives the claim. 792 says the friendship of one who does not examine again and again (ஆய்ந்தாய்ந்து கொள்ளாதான்) ends in grief that kills him. On Manakkudavar's reading the two differ on whether friendship can safely be formed quickly; on Parimelazhagar's, 785 only says that feeling, more than contact, is what makes the bond, and there is no conflict.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:786","b":"k:824","type":"develops","note":"786 says friendship in which only the face smiles is not friendship (முகநக நட்பது நட்பன்று), and that friendship in which the heart within smiles is. 824 says to fear deceivers who smile sweetly with the face and are bitter within (முகத்தின் இனிய நகாஅ அகத்தின்னா). The face and heart contrast that defines true friendship in 786 becomes in 824 the mark of a false friend to be feared.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:787","b":"k:799","type":"develops","note":"787 ends with the friend who suffers along in ruin (அழிவின்கண் அல்லல் உழப்பது). 799 says the friendship of those who abandon one in ruin (கெடுங்காலைக் கைவிடுவார்) burns the heart even when remembered at the hour of death. 799 shows the cost of the failure of the third duty that 787 lists.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:788","b":"k:1023","type":"same_image","note":"In 788 the hand goes at once to the slipping garment (உடுக்கை). In 1023, for one who says he will raise his family, தெய்வம் tucks up its garment (மடிதற்று) and goes ahead of him. Both use a gesture with one's clothing for help that comes unasked and promptly, from a friend in one and from the divine in the other.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:789","b":"k:449","type":"parallel_domain","note":"789 defines the height of friendship as a firm state of propping the other up (ஊன்றும் நிலை). 449 says those without the support of a prop (மதலையாம் சார்பு) have no standing (நிலை), as those without capital have no profit. 789 describes the supporter and 449 the one who needs supporting, in the chapter on a king's reliance on the great.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:789","b":"k:818","type":"agrees","note":"789 places friendship's throne in supporting the friend without wavering wherever it is possible (ஒல்லும்வாய் ஊன்றும் நிலை). 818 says to let slip without a word the friendship of those who make difficult a task they could do (ஒல்லும் கருமம் உடற்றுபவர்). The same word for what lies within one's power sets the standard in 789 and identifies the friend who fails it in 818.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:790","b":"k:978","type":"parallel_domain","note":"790 says friendship looks shabby (புல்லென்னும்) even when friends dress it up in words (புனையினும்) such as 'they are this to us and we are that to them'. 978 says greatness always bows while smallness adorns itself in self-admiration (அணியுமாம் தன்னை வியந்து). In both, the verbal ornament is the sign of the lesser thing: real friendship, like real greatness, does not advertise itself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:791","b":"k:467","type":"parallel_domain","note":"791: inquire first, since after befriending there is no leaving (நட்டபின் வீடில்லை). 467: think and then resolve on a task, and to say 'we will think after resolving' (துணிந்தபின் எண்ணுவம்) is a lapse. The same order of thought before an irreversible step is applied to friendship and to action.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:791","b":"k:800","type":"tension","note":"791 grounds the need for inquiry on the claim that for those who keep friendship there is no leaving once it is formed (நட்டபின் வீடில்லை). 800 tells one to leave the friendship of the ஒப்பிலார், even by giving something (ஒன்றீத்தும் ஒருவுக), and Parimelazhagar takes this as the remedy for a friendship taken up unknowingly. If வீடில்லை is read as a flat 'there is no release', the two conflict; if it describes only what a true friend will not do, 800 is the exception the chapter allows.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:793","b":"k:453","type":"develops","note":"793 includes a person's இனன், his circle, among the things to learn before befriending him (Parimelazhagar takes it as kin). 453 says that the word 'he is such a one' comes from a person's company (இனத்தானாம் இன்னான் எனப்படுஞ் சொல்). 453 gives the reason the circle belongs on 793's list: it is where a person's character is read from.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:794","b":"k:951","type":"develops","note":"794 picks out as a friend the well-born person who is ashamed of blame (பழிநாணுவான்). 951 says that integrity and shame together are found by nature only in those born in a good house (இற்பிறந்தார் கண்அல்லது இல்லை ... செப்பமும் நாணும்). 951 gives the book's reason for 794 pairing birth with shame as the test.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:795","b":"k:448","type":"parallel_domain","note":"795 says to choose as friends those who can rebuke (இடித்து) and bring one to tears over a wrong. 448 says a king without those who rebuke him (இடிப்பாரை இல்லாத) perishes even with no one to ruin him. The rebuker counts as a safeguard both for the private person choosing friends and for the king choosing his support.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:797","b":"k:450","type":"qualifies","note":"797 counts leaving the friendship of fools as gain (ஊதியம்). 450 says that letting go the tie with the good (நல்லார் தொடர் கைவிடல்) is ten times worse than taking on the enmity of many. Whether breaking a tie is profit or harm depends on who is at the other end of it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:797","b":"k:816","type":"agrees","note":"797 says leaving a fool's friendship is gain. 816 says that being a stranger to the wise (அறிவுடையார் ஏதின்மை) is ten million times better than close friendship with a fool (பேதை பெருங்கெழீஇ நட்பு); Pope and Subramuniyaswami render ஏதின்மை as enmity. Both verses put a fool's friendship below having no tie at all.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:797","b":"k:839","type":"same_image","note":"797 calls it gain to give up and leave the friendship of fools (பேதையார் கேண்மை ஒரீஇ விடல்). 839 uses the same phrase, பேதையார் கேண்மை, and says it is very sweet because there is no pain in parting from it. Read together, 839 is the ironic version of 797: the one good thing about such a friendship is the leaving of it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:798","b":"k:106","type":"qualifies","note":"798 says not to take the friendship of those who cut away in distress (அல்லற்கண் ஆற்றறுப்பார்). 106 says not to give up the friendship of those who were one's strength in sorrow (துன்பத்துள் துப்பாயார் நட்பு துறவற்க). The two prohibitions, கொள்ளற்க and துறவற்க, are complementary: how a friend behaves in trouble decides both whom not to take and whom not to drop.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:798","b":"k:596","type":"agrees","note":"798 begins: do not think of what makes the spirit shrink (உள்ளற்க உள்ளம் சிறுகுவ). 596 gives the positive form: in all one thinks, think of rising (உள்ளுவதெல்லாம் உயர்வுள்ளல்). Both treat the direction of one's thoughts as something to be chosen for the sake of the spirit; 798 then sets fair-weather friends beside the shrinking thoughts as things to refuse.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:798","b":"k:814","type":"same_image","note":"798 uses ஆற்றறுப்பார் for friends who fail one in distress. 814 uses the same expression of the untrained horse that fails its rider in battle (அமரகத்து ஆற்றறுக்கும் கல்லாமா) and says solitude is better than such people as one's own. 814 supplies the picture for 798's word: support that holds until the moment it is needed.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:799","b":"k:293","type":"same_image","note":"799 says the thought of false friends burns the heart (உள்ளஞ் சுடும்). 293 says that after a lie one's own heart burns one (தன்நெஞ்சே தன்னைச் சுடும்). In 293 the heart burns for one's own fault, in 799 for another's desertion, so the same inner fire is given two different causes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:799","b":"k:1207","type":"same_image","note":"799 ends உள்ளினும் உள்ளஞ் சுடும்: even to think of the deserter's friendship burns the heart. 1207 ends with the same words in the mouth of the parted woman, for whom even remembering her lover burns. The phrase is used once for betrayal and once for separation in love; in both the pain comes from memory of a bond, not from a present injury.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:800","b":"k:794","type":"same_image","note":"794 says the friendship of the well-born man who fears blame is worth getting even by giving (கொடுத்தும் கொளல்). 800 says the friendship of the ஒப்பிலார் is worth leaving even by giving something (ஒன்றீத்தும் ஒருவுக). The chapter uses the figure of payment in both directions, for acquiring a good friend and for getting free of a bad one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:800","b":"k:818","type":"develops","note":"800 says to leave the friendship of the unfit even at a cost. 818 says how to leave one kind of bad friend, those who make hard work of what they could do: without speaking of it, let the friendship slip (சொல்லாடார் சோர விடல்). 818 adds a manner of leaving to 800's instruction to leave.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:802","b":"k:981","type":"develops","note":"802 calls it the duty of the worthy (சான்றோர் கடன்) to take a friend's liberties well. 981 says that for those who take on worthiness knowing what is due, all good things are கடன். 802 is one instance of 981's general statement, with the same word கடன்.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:803","b":"k:790","type":"develops","note":"803 asks what use long friendship is if one does not accept a friend's liberty as though one had done it oneself (செய்தாங்கு அமையாக் கடை). 790 says friendship looks shabby even when friends describe each other as 'they are this to us, we are that to them'. Both deny a separation of 'mine' and 'theirs' between friends: 790 in how they speak of each other, 803 in whose act an act is counted to be.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:804","b":"k:788","type":"develops","note":"804 says that when friends act without asking, out of intimacy (கெழுதகையாற் கேளாது), it is welcomed. 788 compares friendship to the hand of a man whose garment slips, removing the distress there and then (ஆங்கே). The hand in 788 does not ask leave before acting; 804 states the same thing from the receiving side, that such unasked action is to be wanted and not resented.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:805","b":"k:109","type":"parallel_domain","note":"805 says a hurtful act by a friend is to be understood as folly or as great intimacy (பெருங்கிழமை). 109 says that even an injury like killing is cancelled when one thinks of a single good the person did. Both teach a way of reading an injury so that the bond survives it; 109 does it by remembering a past benefit, 805 by a charitable account of the cause.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:808","b":"k:187","type":"develops","note":"187 says that those who cannot make friends by pleasant speech will divide even their own people by divisive talk (பகச்சொல்லிக் கேளிர்ப் பிரிப்பர்). 808 describes the friend on whom that does not work, who does not listen to reports of a friend's lapse. 187 gives the talebearer's side and 808 the hearer's refusal.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:808","b":"k:510","type":"parallel_domain","note":"808 speaks of those with the strength of intimacy not to listen to talk of a friend's lapse (கேளிழுக்கம் கேளா); Parimelazhagar takes this as not accepting it even when others report it. 510 says that doubting one who has been trusted after examination (தெளிந்தான்கண் ஐயுறவு) brings endless trouble. Both rule out reopening a settled trust, in friendship and in a king's appointments. The second half of 808 is read in opposite ways by Parimelazhagar (the day of the lapse is a day well spent) and Kalaignar (the days are wasted), so the link rests only on the first half.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:809","b":"k:791","type":"develops","note":"791 says that for those who keep friendship there is no leaving once it is formed (வீடில்லை). 809 says the world wants (விழையும் உலகு) those who do not leave (விடாஅர்) the friendship of friends of unbroken long standing. What 791 gives as a reason for caution before befriending, 809 presents as what earns a person general regard.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:811","b":"k:1266","type":"same_image","note":"In 1266 the waiting wife says that when her husband returns she will drink him in (பருகுவன்) so that her pain ends. 811 uses the same verb for friends who look as though they would drink one up (பருகுவார் போலினும்), which Parimelazhagar glosses as from excess of love. The pair shows 'drinking' as the book's figure for consuming affection, sincere in the wife's mouth and only an appearance in the bad friend.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:812","b":"k:799","type":"qualifies","note":"812 treats the friendship of those who leave when the gain ends (அறின் ஒரூஉம்) as a matter of indifference, gained or lost. 799 says that the friendship of those who abandon one in ruin (கெடுங்காலைக் கைவிடுவார்) burns the heart even when recalled at the hour of death (உள்ளினும் உள்ளஞ் சுடும்). Read together, losing such a friend is no loss, yet having trusted one is not painless.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:812","b":"k:800","type":"develops","note":"Both verses name the ஒப்பிலார். 812 says it makes no difference whether their friendship is had or lost; 800 goes further and says to leave it even by giving something (ஒன்றீத்தும் ஒருவுக). Parimelazhagar explains the word differently in each place: here as those who fit with no one because they look only to their own gain, there as those who do not accord with the world.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:812","b":"k:839","type":"agrees","note":"839 says, with irony, that the friendship of fools is very sweet because parting from them brings no pain (பிரிவின்கண் பீழை தருவதொன்றில்). 812 asks what it matters whether one gains or loses (பெறினும் இழப்பினும் என்) the friendship of those who befriend when there is profit and leave when there is none. Both measure a worthless friendship by the same test: its loss costs nothing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:813","b":"k:912","type":"develops","note":"813 sets friends who weigh what will come to them (உறுவது சீர்தூக்கும் நட்பு) beside 'those who take what they get' (பெறுவது கொள்வார்), whom Parimelazhagar identifies as courtesans, and thieves. 912 describes those women directly: they weigh the profit and then speak of character (பயன்தூக்கிப் பண்புரைக்கும்). The later chapter spells out the comparison 813 makes in a phrase, with the same verb தூக்கு for the calculating look.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:814","b":"k:788","type":"develops","note":"788 defines friendship by the moment of need: like the hand of a man whose garment slips, it removes distress there and then (ஆங்கே இடுக்கண் களைவது). 814 pictures the reverse, a horse that gives way in the battle itself (அமரகத்து). Both locate the test of a friend at the instant of crisis, one by the hand that acts unasked and one by the mount that fails.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:815","b":"k:106","type":"develops","note":"106 says not to give up the friendship of those who were one's strength in sorrow (துன்பத்துள் துப்பாயார் நட்பு). 815 says the friendship of the small, which gives no safety (ஏமம்), is better not obtained at all. The same measure, support in trouble, decides in one verse which friends must be kept and in the other which should never be acquired.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:819","b":"k:799","type":"develops","note":"799 says the friendship of those who abandon one in ruin burns the heart even to think of at the time of death (அடுங்காலை உள்ளினும்). 819 says the bond with those whose words and deeds differ is painful even in a dream (கனவினும் இன்னாது). Each verse extends the harm of a false friend beyond ordinary waking life, to the deathbed in one and to sleep in the other.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"k:820","b":"k:188","type":"agrees","note":"188 asks what people whose habit is to broadcast even the faults of those close to them (துன்னியார் குற்றமும் தூற்றும்) would do to strangers. 820 warns against the bond with those who are friendly in private and blame one in public. Both take the public exposure of an intimate as the mark of a person no one should be near.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:821","b":"k:487","type":"parallel_domain","note":"487 says the bright do not flare up outwardly at once but watch for the time and burn within (காலம்பார்த்து உள்வேர்ப்பர்). 821 warns that those who keep close without inner consent will strike when they see the right place. The patience the book recommends to a ruler in 487 is the same conduct it tells him to expect from a false friend in 821.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:821","b":"k:883","type":"agrees","note":"883 says to fear the enemy within, because at the moment of weakness (உலைவிடத்து) it cuts like the tool that cuts the potter's clay (மட்பகை). 821 says the friendship of those who join without inner accord (நேரா நிரந்தவர்) is an anvil (பட்டடை) for striking once they find the right opening (சீரிடம் காணின்). Both verses use a craftsman's tool for the hidden enemy and both place the blow at the moment that suits him.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:822","b":"k:882","type":"agrees","note":"882 says not to fear enemies who are like swords but to fear the bond with enemies who are like kin (கேள்போல் பகைவர் தொடர்பு). 822 says the friendship of those who seem one's own and are not (இனம்போன்று இனமல்லார்) will turn. Both identify the enemy in the guise of kin as the dangerous one; 882 gives the instruction and 822 the reason, that such a bond does not stay what it looks like.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:822","b":"k:920","type":"same_image","note":"920 lists two-minded women (இருமனப் பெண்டிர்) with liquor and dice as the company of those fortune has left. 822 compares false friendship to women's minds that become other (வேறுபடும்). The divided or shifting female mind serves in both as the book's figure for a tie that cannot be relied on, and 920 suggests which women the figure has in view.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:822","b":"k:974","type":"tension","note":"822 uses women's minds as its example of something that changes (மகளிர் மனம்போல வேறுபடும்); Parimelazhagar takes this of women in general and supports it with a line from the Valaiyapathi. 974 takes single-minded women (ஒருமை மகளிர்) as its model of the constancy that greatness needs. On Parimelazhagar's general reading of 822 the two figures conflict; if 822's மகளிர் is limited to the courtesans of chapter 92, they do not.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:825","b":"k:509","type":"develops","note":"509 says to trust no one without examining them and, having examined, to trust what is to be trusted (தேறற்க யாரையும் தேராது). 825 says that those who are not at one with you in mind are not to be judged trustworthy by their words in any matter (சொல்லினால் தேறற்பாற்று அன்று). 825 adds to the rule of examination a statement of what cannot count as evidence.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:825","b":"k:890","type":"agrees","note":"890 says that life with those who are not in accord (உடம்பாடு இலாதவர்) is like living with a snake in a hut. 825 says those whose minds are not settled with yours (மனத்தின் அமையாதவர்) are not to be trusted on their words. Both make inner agreement, not outward closeness or speech, the thing that decides whether a person near you is safe.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:826","b":"k:709","type":"agrees","note":"709 says the eye itself tells of enmity and friendship (பகைமையும் கேண்மையும் கண்ணுரைக்கும்) to those who know how to read it. 826 says an enemy's words are soon recognised even when they sound like a friend's. Both hold that hostility shows through the surface; 709 makes this depend on a skilled reader, a condition 826 leaves unstated.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:826","b":"k:828","type":"qualifies","note":"826 says the words of the unreconciled are quickly seen through although they say good things as friends do. 828, in the same chapter, says a weapon can lie hidden in hands joined in worship (தொழுதகை யுள்ளும் படையொடுங்கும்) and in an enemy's tears. 828 limits the confidence of 826: the speech may give itself away, but gestures of submission and grief can still conceal the intent.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:827","b":"k:488","type":"develops","note":"488 advises that on meeting an enemy one should bear him, which Parimelazhagar glosses as bowing, until his end is in sight (செறுநரைக் காணின் சுமக்க இறுவரை). 827 says not to accept an enemy's bowing speech (சொல்வணக்கம்), because the bending of a bow signals harm. The humility 488 teaches as a tactic is the humility 827 teaches one to distrust in the other side.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:827","b":"k:985","type":"qualifies","note":"985 says that bowing (பணிதல்) is the strength of those who accomplish and the weapon by which the worthy change their opponents (மாற்றாரை மாற்றும் படை). 827 says that an enemy's verbal bowing should not be accepted, since it points to harm as a bent bow does. Bowing is a means of working on an adversary in both; 985 praises it in the worthy, and 827 says that from an enemy it must not be taken as goodwill.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:829","b":"k:488","type":"agrees","note":"488 says to bear an enemy when you meet him until his final limit comes, and then his head goes down. 829 says to keep the false friend pleased and hold him in the appearance of friendship while it dies inwardly. Both advise outward accommodation of an enemy who cannot yet be opposed openly, in the chapter on timing and in the chapter on false friendship.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:829","b":"k:824","type":"develops","note":"824 says to fear deceivers who smile sweetly with the face while bitter within. 829 says that those who make much of you outwardly and despise you (மிகச்செய்து தம்மெள்ளுவார்) should be made to smile and be embraced within the friendship so that it dies (நகச்செய்து நட்பினுள் சாப்புல்லல்). The response 829 prescribes has the same form as the conduct 824 warns against; Parimelazhagar notes that it is allowed as royal policy although such doubleness is otherwise unfitting.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:830","b":"k:488","type":"agrees","note":"488 advises bearing with enemies when one meets them until their end is in view. 830 advises a friendly face toward enemies at a time when, as Parimelazhagar explains, they cannot be openly put aside. Both treat outward civility toward an enemy as a matter of timing and not of feeling.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:831","b":"k:422","type":"agrees","note":"831 defines folly as taking up what harms and letting gain go (ஏதங்கொண்டு ஊதியம் போக விடல்). 422 defines understanding as the reverse movement: turning away from evil and steering toward good (தீதொரீஇ நன்றின்பால் உய்ப்பது). Read together, the two definitions are mirror images, so folly in 831 is not a lack of information but the wrong choice between two things in view.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:831","b":"k:797","type":"same_image","note":"831 says folly is to let gain go (ஊதியம் போக விடல்). 797 uses the same two words to say what gain is: giving up and leaving the friendship of fools (ஊதியம் என்பது ... பேதையார் கேண்மை ஒரீஇ விடல்). The fool lets gain go, and for everyone else letting the fool go is the gain.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:831","b":"k:925","type":"parallel_domain","note":"831 says folly is accepting harm and letting gain go. 925 gives a concrete case in drinking: handing over wealth to receive ignorance of one's own body (பொருள்கொடுத்து மெய்யறியாமை கொளல்), which it traces to கையறியாமை, not knowing what to do. The drinker's purchase is 831's exchange carried out literally.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:832","b":"k:439","type":"agrees","note":"832 names as the worst folly a liking (காதன்மை) for what is not fit for one. 439 gives the matching instruction: do not set your desire on a deed that yields no good (நயவற்க நன்றி பயவா வினை). Both locate the error in the wanting, before any deed is done.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:833","b":"k:428","type":"agrees","note":"833 lists four absences as the fool's work: no shame, no inquiry, no tenderness, no care for anything (நாணாமை நாடாமை நாரின்மை யாதொன்றும் பேணாமை பேதை தொழில்). 428 defines folly as not fearing what should be feared and calls fearing it the work of the knowing (அறிவார் தொழில்). Both define folly as a missing response where one is due, and both set it out as a characteristic தொழில்.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:833","b":"k:865","type":"parallel_domain","note":"833 describes the fool by four negations. 865 describes the ideal enemy to have by a similar string: he does not look to the way, does not do what is fitting, does not look to blame, has no character (வழிநோக்கான் ... பழிநோக்கான் பண்பிலன்), and so is sweet to his foes. The same profile that 833 treats as a moral portrait is, in the chapter on enmity, a strategic weakness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:833","b":"k:1018","type":"develops","note":"833 puts shamelessness (நாணாமை) first among the fool's traits. 1018 says what that trait costs: if one is not ashamed of what makes others ashamed, the fault is one that virtue itself would be ashamed of (அறம் நாணத் தக்கது உடைத்து); Parimelazhagar reads it as virtue leaving him. 1018 supplies the consequence that 833 leaves unstated.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:834","b":"k:130","type":"agrees","note":"130 praises the one who guards against anger and, having learned, is restrained (கற்றடங்கல் ஆற்றுவான்): virtue itself seeks him out. 834 names the one who has read, understood and taught but is not restrained (ஓதி உணர்ந்தும் ... தானடங்கா) the greatest of fools. Both treat learning and அடங்கல் as a pair in which the learning is judged by the restraint.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:834","b":"k:373","type":"develops","note":"834 presents a puzzle: a person who has studied, understood and taught, and still does not restrain himself. 373 offers the chapter on fate's account of such a person: however many subtle books one studies, one's own underlying understanding prevails (தன் உண்மையறிவே மிகும்), which Parimelazhagar takes as the folly given by fate. 373 explains how the fool of 834 is possible.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:835","b":"k:919","type":"same_image","note":"835 calls the fool's destination an அளறு, a mire or hell, into which he enters and sinks (புக்கழுந்தும்). 919 calls the shoulders of women who draw no line the அளறு in which base men sink (பூரியர்கள் ஆழும் அளறு). 919 uses for one present indulgence the image that 835 uses for the result of a whole life of folly.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:836","b":"k:468","type":"agrees","note":"836 says that when a fool who does not know the way of doing (கையறியாப் பேதை) takes up a task, the task fails (பொய்படும்) and he himself ends in fetters. 468 says effort not made by the right means will collapse (பொத்துப் படும்) even with many standing by to protect it. Parimelazhagar glosses both verbs as புரைபடும்; 468 states the rule about method and 836 adds the cost to the doer.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:836","b":"k:925","type":"same_image","note":"836 describes the fool as கையறியா, not knowing how to act. 925 uses the same compound, கையறியாமை, as the cause of paying money to lose awareness of oneself in drink. In both verses the word names a practical ignorance that ends in the person binding or losing himself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:837","b":"k:1000","type":"agrees","note":"837 speaks of great wealth that has come to a fool (பெருஞ்செல்வம் உற்றக்கடை) and benefits the wrong people. 1000 says great wealth obtained by one without character (பண்பிலான் பெற்ற பெருஞ்செல்வம்) is like good milk spoiled by its vessel. Parimelazhagar notes on both verses that the verb marks wealth received and not made; both hold that the holder's defect ruins what the wealth could do.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:837","b":"k:1009","type":"parallel_domain","note":"837 says a fool's great wealth feeds strangers (ஏதிலார்) while his own go hungry. 1009 says wealth amassed by giving up love, denying oneself and ignoring virtue will be taken by others (கொள்வார் பிறர்). The miser and the fool arrive at the same result by opposite routes, one by holding wealth back and one by letting it go to the wrong people.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:838","b":"k:408","type":"agrees","note":"838 likens a fool's possession to drunkenness added to derangement. 408 says fortune lodged with the unlearned is more painful than poverty lodged with the good (கல்லார்கண் பட்ட திரு). Both deny that wealth is a good independent of its holder; 838 gives the image and 408 the comparative judgment.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:840","b":"k:403","type":"qualifies","note":"840 treats the fool's mere entry (புகல்) into the company of the worthy as a defilement; Parimelazhagar adds that the assembly itself is lowered by it. 403 allows that even the unlearned are very good if they can stay silent before the learned (கற்றார்முன் சொல்லாதிருக்கப் பெறின்). 403 leaves a condition under which presence is acceptable, though its subject is the unlearned and 840's is the fool.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:840","b":"k:720","type":"same_image","note":"840 likens a fool entering an assembly of the worthy to setting an unwashed foot on a bed (கழாஅக்கால் பள்ளியுள் வைத்தற்று). 720 likens speaking before those not of one's own kind to nectar spilled in an unclean yard (அங்கணத்துள் உக்க அமிழ்து). The two are mirror images of the same mismatch: the unclean thing brought into the clean place, and the precious thing let fall in the unclean place.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:842","b":"k:70","type":"parallel_domain","note":"842 says that when a person without understanding gives gladly, the cause is nothing but the receiver's தவம். 70 says a son's service to his father is to make people ask what the father did as penance (என்நோற்றான் கொல்) to have him. Both use the same figure, explaining a good by the stored merit of the one who benefits; 70 uses it as praise of the son and 842 to deny the giver any credit.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:842","b":"k:92","type":"qualifies","note":"92 treats giving with a glad heart (அகனமர்ந்து ஈதல்) as a real good, surpassed only by a glad face and sweet speech. 842 takes the same act, giving with a glad heart (நெஞ்சுவந்து ஈதல்), and says that in a person without understanding it reflects only the receiver's merit. 842 limits 92's valuation: the glad gift counts for the giver only when understanding is behind it, which is how Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar both read it.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:843","b":"k:165","type":"parallel_domain","note":"843 says the suffering those without understanding inflict on themselves is hard even for their enemies to inflict (செறுவார்க்கும் செய்தல் அரிது). 165 says that for the envious, envy is enough: it brings ruin even when enemies fail to (ஒன்னார் வழுக்கியும் கேடீன்பது). Both measure an inner defect against an outside enemy and find the defect the more effective.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:843","b":"k:434","type":"agrees","note":"843 says the fool harms himself beyond what an enemy could. 434 says one's own fault is the enemy that brings ruin (குற்றமே அற்றந் தரூஉம் பகை) and is therefore the thing to guard against. 434 gives the general principle of which 843 is the case of the person who cannot do the guarding.","grade":"sound","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:843","b":"k:847","type":"develops","note":"843 states generally that those without understanding torment themselves (தாந்தம்மைப் பீழிக்கும் பீழை). 847, in the same chapter, names one way it happens: the person without understanding lets the precious மறை slip and so does great harm to himself by himself (பெருமிறை தானே தனக்கு). 847 is a specific instance of 843's claim, with the same stress on தானே.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:844","b":"k:404","type":"develops","note":"844 says the claim to ஒண்மை is itself what வெண்மை is. 404 says that however good an unlearned person's ஒட்பம் may be, those with understanding do not accept it (கொள்ளார் அறிவுடையார்). 404 shows the other side of 844's self-assessment: the brightness one credits to oneself is not credited by those able to judge.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:844","b":"k:978","type":"agrees","note":"844 locates want of understanding in the pride (செருக்கு) of saying 'we are bright'. 978 says smallness adorns itself in admiration of itself (சிறுமை அணியுமாம் தன்னை வியந்து) while greatness bows. Both make self-admiration the identifying mark of the deficient state, one for understanding and one for stature.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:845","b":"k:128","type":"parallel_domain","note":"845 says one false claim to learning casts doubt on what one has mastered without flaw. 128 says that if even one bad word has its effect, the good comes to nothing (நன்றாகாதாகி விடும்). Both describe a single lapse spreading backward over what was sound, in knowledge and in speech.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:845","b":"k:391","type":"same_image","note":"391 tells one to learn what is to be learned without flaw (கற்க கசடற) and then stand by it. 845 uses the same phrase for what a person has truly mastered (கசடற வல்லதூஉம்) and says that carrying on as if one knew what one has not learned (கல்லாத மேற்கொண்டொழுகல்) brings doubt even on that. Pretence undoes the standing of the very learning 391 asks for.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:845","b":"k:405","type":"develops","note":"405 says an unlearned person's standing gives way once he meets others and speaks (சொல்லாடச் சோர்வு படும்). 845 describes a further loss for one who has some real learning: by taking on what he has not learned, he puts even his genuine mastery in doubt (ஐயம் தரும்). 405 is the exposure of a claim; 845 is the damage the exposure does to what was true.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:846","b":"k:278","type":"parallel_domain","note":"846 asks whether covering one's nakedness is not itself small-minded when one's faults are left uncovered (தம்வயின் குற்றம் மறையா வழி); Parimelazhagar takes covering faults to mean removing them. 278 speaks of many who, with stain in the mind, bathe and go about concealed (மனத்தது மாசாக ... நீராடி மறைந்தொழுகு). Both set a bodily propriety, clothing or bathing, against an inner fault it does nothing about.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:846","b":"k:1012","type":"develops","note":"846 treats clothing the body as trivial beside dealing with one's faults. 1012 says food, clothing and the rest are no different for any living being, and the distinction of humans is having shame (ஊணுடை எச்சம் உயிர்க்கெல்லாம் வேறல்ல நாணுடைமை மாந்தர் சிறப்பு). 1012 gives the reason behind 846: dress is common to all, and what marks a person is shame at fault.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:847","b":"k:418","type":"agrees","note":"Parimelazhagar reads 847 as a person who receives rare instruction and lets it run out without taking it in, harming himself. 418 says ears not pierced by listening are deaf even though they hear (கேட்பினுங் கேளாத் தகையவே). On that reading both describe teaching that reaches a person and does not enter; 847 adds that the loss is self-inflicted.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:847","b":"k:1076","type":"parallel_domain","note":"847 says a person without understanding lets the precious மறை slip (அருமறை சோரும்) and so harms himself. Manakkudavar takes மறை as a secret let out to others, a reading Parimelazhagar reports and rejects. On that reading the verse meets 1076, where the base are like a beaten drum because they carry every மறை they hear to others; 1076 notes the harm to others, 847 the harm to the leaker.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:848","b":"k:448","type":"develops","note":"448 says a king without people who rebuke him will perish even with no one to ruin him (கெடுப்பாரிலானுங் கெடும்). 848 takes the case one step further: a person who has people to direct him and still does not act on it, and cannot work it out alone. 448 makes having correctors the safeguard; 848 shows the safeguard is useless to one who will not be corrected.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:848","b":"k:572","type":"same_image","note":"848 calls the life of one who neither heeds nor understands a disease until it leaves (போஒம் அளவுமோர் நோய்); Parimelazhagar takes this as a burden hard for the earth to bear, while Kalaignar takes the disease as the person's own. 572 says the existence of those without kindly regard is a weight on the earth (உண்மை நிலக்குப் பொறை). On Parimelazhagar's reading the two share the figure of a person whose mere being alive is an affliction to the world.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"k:848","b":"k:638","type":"parallel_domain","note":"848 describes one who does not act when told and does not see for himself (ஏவவும் செய்கலான் தான் தேறான்), and calls that life a disease until it departs. 638 describes a king in the same two terms, one who destroys what the knowing say and does not know himself (அறிகொன்று அறியான்), and says the minister must still tell him what is sound. The same double failing appears in both; 848 gives the verdict on the person and 638 the duty of those beside him.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:849","b":"k:373","type":"agrees","note":"849 ends with the unseeing person continuing to see as he saw (தான்கண்டவாறு). 373 says that however many subtle books one studies, one's own underlying understanding prevails (தன் உண்மையறிவே மிகும்). Both hold that what is brought from outside, a teacher in 849 and books in 373, does not displace the understanding a person already has.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:849","b":"k:929","type":"parallel_domain","note":"849 says one who tries to show something to a person who does not see ends up the unseeing one, while that person goes on having seen it his own way (கண்டானாம் தான்கண்டவாறு). 929 says giving reasons to a drunk man is like searching with a flame for someone sunk under water (கீழ்நீர்க் குளித்தானைத் தீத்துரீஇ அற்று). Both describe instruction that cannot reach its target, and in both the effort reflects on the one making it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:850","b":"k:140","type":"agrees","note":"850 condemns the one who denies what the world affirms. 140 says those who have not learned to conduct themselves in accord with the world (உலகத்தோடு ஒட்ட ஒழுகல்) lack understanding however much they have studied. Parimelazhagar takes 'the world' in both as the high-minded many, so both make their consensus a test that private cleverness does not override.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:851","b":"k:187","type":"same_image","note":"851 names the fault as பகல், splitting apart, and its chapter closes (860) by setting நகல் against இகல். 187 uses the same pair of roots: those who do not know how to make friends by நகச்சொல்லி will பகச்சொல்லி and separate even their kin. The chapter on backbiting already holds the contrast between dividing and laughing together that the chapter on hostility is built on.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:851","b":"k:886","type":"develops","note":"851 defines இகல் as the disease (நோய்) that spreads பகல், the ill nature of division, among all living beings. 886 takes the same division to its end inside one's own circle: if ஒன்றாமை arises among ஒன்றியார், escaping death (பொன்றாமை) is hard at any time. The general diagnosis of 851 becomes a specific warning about a ruler's own people; Parimelazhagar reads 886 of a king.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:852","b":"k:203","type":"agrees","note":"852 ranks not harming a provoker as தலை, the best course. 203 uses the same ranking word: the head of all understanding (அறிவினுள் எல்லாம் தலை) is not to do evil even to those who hate you (செறுவார்க்கும்). Together they show the text treating non-retaliation as a matter of good sense as well as of virtue; Parimelazhagar on 852 explains that retaliating feeds the enmity while refraining lets it die out.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:853","b":"k:156","type":"agrees","note":"853 says that removing the painful disease of hostility gives a brightness that neither fails nor is flawed (தவலில்லாத் தாவில் விளக்கம்), which Parimelazhagar takes as lasting fame. 156 says those who punish have one day's pleasure while those who forbear have praise until the end (பொன்றுந் துணையும் புகழ்). Both make enduring renown the result of declining the quarrel, one in the householder's ethics and one in public life.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:854","b":"k:629","type":"qualifies","note":"854 offers இன்பத்துள் இன்பம், the joy of joys, as what follows when hostility goes. 629 uses the same two phrases differently: one who does not crave pleasure within pleasure (இன்பத்துள் இன்பம் விழையாதான்) does not feel pain within pain (துன்பத்துள் துன்பம் உறுதல் இலன்). Read together, the joy 854 promises is a result of removing a cause of suffering, and 629 warns against making such joy an object of desire.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:855","b":"k:874","type":"develops","note":"855 says no one sets out to defeat a person who can lean away from hostility. 874 goes a step further: the world rests in the greatness of the person of character who conducts himself taking enmity as friendship (பகை நட்பாக் கொண்டொழுகும்). 855 describes safety gained by not answering hostility; 874 describes actively turning the enemy into a friend and gives it a public value.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:855","b":"k:985","type":"agrees","note":"855 asks who would be inclined to overcome those able to lean away when hostility confronts them (இகலெதிர் சாய்ந்தொழுக வல்லாரை). 985 says the strength of the capable is to bow (பணிதல்), and that this is the weapon by which the worthy change their opponents (மாற்றாரை மாற்றும் படை). Both treat yielding as what disarms an opponent; 985 states it as a trait of the சான்றோர், 855 as a skill (வல்லார்), which Parimelazhagar says is hard for kings.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:855","b":"k:1327","type":"parallel_domain","note":"855 holds that the one who gives way before hostility cannot be beaten. In 1327 the man says that in a lovers' sulk the one who loses has won (ஊடலில் தோற்றவர் வென்றார்), as the reunion shows. The speaker in 1327 is a lover talking about play-quarrels, so this is the same pattern of winning by yielding in a different setting, not a doctrine shared with 855.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:856","b":"k:307","type":"parallel_domain","note":"856 says the life of one who declares it sweet to excel in hostility (இகலின் மிகல் இனிது என்பவன்) is close to failure and ruin. 307 says the ruin of one who takes anger to be a thing of worth (சினத்தைப் பொருளென்று கொண்டவன்) is as sure as the hand of one who strikes the ground. In both the fault is the valuing of the passion, and both attach certain or imminent ruin to it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:857","b":"k:423","type":"develops","note":"423 defines understanding as seeing the true thing (மெய்ப்பொருள் காண்பது அறிவு) in whatever one hears from anyone. 857 says those of harmful understanding who are attached to hostility (இகல்மேவல் இன்னா அறிவினவர்) do not see the மெய்ப்பொருள் that carries victory. So 857 names hostility as one thing that turns அறிவு against its defining task; Parimelazhagar narrows மெய்ப்பொருள் here to the content of the treatises on statecraft and says hostility clouds the understanding.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:860","b":"k:180","type":"same_image","note":"860 says all ills come from hostility, and from friendly gladness (நகல்) comes 'the pride called good conduct' (நன்னயம் என்னும் செருக்கு). 180 has the same shape and the same closing phrase: coveting without thought yields destruction, and 'the pride called not-wanting' (வேண்டாமை என்னும் செருக்கு) yields victory. In both the verse sets a ruinous impulse against its absence and calls that absence a செருக்கு, which Parimelazhagar glosses in both places as wealth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:860","b":"k:303","type":"agrees","note":"860 says இகலான் ஆம் இன்னாத எல்லாம்: every painful thing arises from hostility. 303 says to forget anger toward anyone, because the birth of evils comes from it (தீய பிறத்தல் அதனான் வரும்). The Book of Virtue makes anger the source of evils and the Book of Wealth makes hostility the source, with nearly the same construction.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:860","b":"k:999","type":"develops","note":"860 credits நகல், glad friendliness, with producing good conduct and its dignity. 999 describes those without the capacity: for people not able to laugh with others (நகல் வல்லர் அல்லார்) the wide world lies in darkness even by day. 860 gives what நகல் yields and 999 what its absence costs; Parimelazhagar reads நகல் in both as rejoicing in company with others.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:862","b":"k:449","type":"agrees","note":"862 asks how a man with no love, no worthy allies (ஆன்ற துணையிலன்) and no strength of his own can wear down an enemy's might. 449 states the principle: those without capital have no profit, and those without the prop of supporters (மதலையாஞ் சார்பிலார்) have no standing. 862 applies to a contest with an enemy what 449 says of a ruler's position in general.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:862","b":"k:873","type":"develops","note":"862 describes the man without love, allies or strength as unable to resist an enemy. 873 judges the man in that position who nevertheless provokes: one who, alone (தமியனாய்), takes on the enmity of many is more foolish than the mad. 862 gives the weak position seen by an attacker; 873 gives the advice that follows for the one who is in it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:863","b":"k:534","type":"agrees","note":"863 puts fear first among the traits that make a man easy for his enemy. 534 says the fearful have no fortress (அச்சமுடையார்க்கு அரணில்லை); Parimelazhagar explains that defences are of no use to one with fear in his mind. Both say that fear cancels whatever other protection a person has.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:863","b":"k:869","type":"develops","note":"863 lists fear and ignorance among the weaknesses that make a man easy prey (அஞ்சும் அறியான்). 869 keeps just those two and states them from the other side: joy does not go far from those who fight if they get enemies who are without understanding and afraid (அறிவிலா அஞ்சும் பகைவர்ப் பெறின்). The chapter moves from describing the weak enemy to stating what the attacker gains from him.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:864","b":"k:305","type":"agrees","note":"864 says one who does not leave off anger (நீங்கான் வெகுளி) and has no நிறை is easy for anyone, at any time, anywhere. 305 says that if you would guard yourself, guard against anger, because unguarded anger kills oneself (தன்னையே கொல்லுஞ் சினம்). 305 states the self-destruction as an ethical fact; 864 shows how it works in a contest, by making the angry man open to any opponent.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:864","b":"k:526","type":"develops","note":"864 says the man who cannot leave anger is easy for anyone to overcome. 526 gives the opposite case: no one on earth has more people at his side than one who gives greatly and does not cherish anger (பேணான் வெகுளி). Put together, anger decides whether a man stands with many around him or exposed to all.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:866","b":"k:360","type":"parallel_domain","note":"866 says the enmity of a man of blind anger and excessive lust (காணாச் சினத்தான் கழிபெருங் காமத்தான்) is to be welcomed. 360 names the same two, with delusion, as what must perish for suffering to perish (காமம் வெகுளி மயக்கம்). What the chapter on true knowledge treats as obstacles to release, the chapter on enemies treats as what makes an opponent beatable; Parimelazhagar says such men destroy themselves.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:867","b":"k:800","type":"parallel_domain","note":"867 advises paying to have a certain man as an enemy rather than keep him near. 800 advises paying to be rid of a certain friend: give something (ஒன்று ஈத்தும்) and leave the friendship of those who are not a match. Both accept a cost to end a harmful closeness; the commentators differ on who the man in 867 is, Parimelazhagar taking him as one who begins a task and acts unsuitably, Kalaignar as one who stays beside you.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:868","b":"k:504","type":"develops","note":"504 tells the ruler to examine a man's qualities, then his faults, and judge by what predominates (மிகை நாடி மிக்க கொளல்). 868 describes the result when the tally is all on one side: with no qualities and many faults (குணனிலனாய்க் குற்றம் பலவாயின்) he is left without companions, and that is a support to his opponents. The test used for choosing servants in 504 is applied by 868 to the ruler himself, with his enemies as the ones who benefit.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:868","b":"k:525","type":"develops","note":"868 says a man without qualities and with many faults comes to have no one with him (இனனிலனாம்). 525 gives the positive case: one who can give and speak pleasantly will be surrounded by kin in rows (அடுக்கிய சுற்றத்தால் சுற்றப்படும்). Both make a person's following a product of his character; 868 adds the military consequence of losing it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:869","b":"k:772","type":"tension","note":"869 says joy does not go far from those who fight if they get enemies who are ignorant and afraid. 772 says holding the spear that missed an elephant is sweeter (இனிது) than holding the arrow that hit a forest hare. One locates the pleasure of fighting in the easy opponent and the other in the hard one; the conflict holds if both are read as claims about what a fighter should want, and eases if 869 is a king's reckoning of advantage and 772 an individual warrior's sense of honour.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:871","b":"k:867","type":"tension","note":"871 says enmity, a thing without good in it (பண்பிலதனை), is not to be wanted even in jest (நகையேயும் வேண்டற்பாற்று அன்று). 867 says the enmity of one who stays close and does unfitting things must certainly be got, even by paying for it (கொடுத்தும் கொளல்வேண்டும் மன்ற). The conflict holds if 871 is read as a rule about all enmity; Parimelazhagar narrows it to enmity that brings no benefit, which would leave room for 867.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:872","b":"k:129","type":"parallel_domain","note":"872 says that even if you take on the enmity of those whose plough is the bow (வில்லேர் உழவர்), you should not take on that of those whose plough is the word (சொல்லேர் உழவர்). 129 says a wound burned by fire heals within, but the scar burned by the tongue (நாவினாற் சுட்ட வடு) does not. Both rank harm done by words above harm done by a physical instrument, one in statecraft and one in personal conduct.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:873","b":"k:875","type":"develops","note":"873 states the folly of standing alone (தமியனாய்) against many enemies. 875 gives the working rule for the smallest such case: with no ally and two enemies (தன்துணை இன்றால் பகையிரண்டால்), make one of them a pleasing ally (இன்துணையாக் கொள்க). 875 turns 873's condemnation into a remedy.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:874","b":"k:985","type":"develops","note":"874 praises the one who takes enmity as friendship (பகைநட்பாக் கொண்டொழுகும்) without saying how it is done. 985 names a means: bowing (பணிதல்) is the weapon by which the worthy change their opponents (மாற்றாரை மாற்றும் படை). Parimelazhagar glosses this as ending the opponents' enmity, so 985 reads as the method behind 874.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:874","b":"k:996","type":"agrees","note":"874 says the world rests in the greatness of the person of character who conducts himself taking enmity as friendship (பண்புடையாளன் தகைமைக்கண் தங்கிற்று உலகு). 996 says the world exists by resting on those of character (பண்புடையார்ப் பட்டுண்டு உலகம்), and would otherwise sink into the earth. Both make பண்பு the thing the world stands on; 874, in the chapter on enmity, specifies it as the capacity to turn enemies into friends.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:875","b":"k:633","type":"parallel_domain","note":"875 advises a ruler caught alone between two enemies to make one of them an ally. 633 lists among a minister's abilities dividing (பிரித்தல்), which Parimelazhagar explains as separating the enemy's supporters from him, and rejoining those who broke away (பிரிந்தார்ப் பொருத்தல்). The advice to the king in 875 is the skill required of the minister in 633.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:876","b":"k:691","type":"parallel_domain","note":"876 says that whether or not one has trusted the enemy before, in a time of ruin (அழிவின்கண்) one should neither join him nor break from him (தேறான் பகாஅன் விடல்); Parimelazhagar explains that joining lets him destroy from inside and breaking makes him add to the ruin. 691 tells those who serve quarrelsome kings to be like people warming at a fire, neither moving off nor coming close (அகலாது அணுகாது). Both prescribe a deliberate middle distance from a dangerous party, once toward an enemy and once toward one's own king.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:877","b":"k:440","type":"parallel_domain","note":"877 says not to let softness show before enemies (மேவற்க மென்மை பகைவர் அகத்து); Parimelazhagar takes மென்மை as lack of strength, which enemies watch for. 440 says that if a ruler can enjoy what he loves without that love being known (காதல் அறியாமை), his enemies' designs come to nothing (ஏதில ஏதிலார் நூல்). In both, what an enemy can use is what he is allowed to learn about one's inner state, weakness in one verse and desire in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:879","b":"k:550","type":"same_image","note":"879 speaks of those who remove the thorn tree with the word களையுநர், weeders. 550 compares a king's putting the cruel to death to weeding the green crop (பைங்கூழ் களைகட்டதனொடு நேர்). Both picture the removal of dangerous people as a cultivator's weeding; 879 adds that timing matters, since the weed grown hard injures the weeder's hand.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:880","b":"k:759","type":"develops","note":"880 states the cost of not breaking the pride of one's enemies (செயிர்ப்பவர் செம்மல் சிதைக்கலாதார்): one cannot be counted as living. 759 names the instrument for that task: wealth, the sharpest steel for cutting the pride of foes (செறுநர் செருக்கறுக்கும் எஃகு). One verse gives the necessity and the other the means.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:881","b":"k:522","type":"qualifies","note":"881 says the nature of one's own people is bad if it does harm (தமர்நீரும் இன்னாவாம் இன்னா செயின்). 522 says kin whose affection does not lapse (விருப்பறாச் சுற்றம்) yield many gains that keep branching; Parimelazhagar notes that this wording is chosen to exclude inner enmity. The condition written into 522 is the case 881 describes, so each verse marks the limit of the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:881","b":"k:1309","type":"same_image","note":"881 says even shade and water are bad when they do harm (நிழல்நீரும் இன்னாத இன்னா), and so is the nature of one's own people when it does harm. In 1309 the man says water is sweet in the shade (நீரும் நிழலது இனிதே), and sulking is sweet only with those who love. Both take shade and water as the standard of what is pleasant and then make the pleasantness depend on a condition, which in both cases is the real disposition of those closest to one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:882","b":"k:824","type":"agrees","note":"882 uses the verb of fear for kin-like enemies (அஞ்சுக கேள்போல் பகைவர் தொடர்பு). 824 uses it for deceivers who smile sweetly with the face and are bitter within (முகத்தின் இனிய நகாஅ அகத்தின்னா வஞ்சரை அஞ்சப்படும்). The chapter on false friendship and the chapter on inner enmity reach the same instruction: what is to be feared is hostility presented as closeness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:882","b":"k:828","type":"same_image","note":"882 contrasts the enemy who is like a drawn sword (வாள்போல் பகைவர்) with the one who looks like kin, and fears the second. 828 says a weapon hides even within an enemy's hands folded in worship (தொழுதகையுள்ளும் படையொடுங்கும்), and his tears are the same. In 882 the visible sword is the safer one; 828 shows where the other sword is kept.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:883","b":"k:305","type":"parallel_domain","note":"883 says to fear the enemy within and guard oneself (உட்பகை அஞ்சித் தற்காக்க), or it will destroy one. 305 says that if you would guard yourself, guard against anger (தன்னைத்தான் காக்கின் சினங்காக்க); unguarded, anger kills oneself. The same formula of self-guarding against something lodged inside is applied in one verse to people within the household or court and in the other to a passion within the mind.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:885","b":"k:735","type":"parallel_domain","note":"885 says an inner enmity that comes in the form of kinship (உறல்முறையான் உட்பகை) brings many ills of a deadly kind (இறல்முறையான் ஏதம்) to the ruler. 735 defines a land as one without many factions, without ruinous inner enmity (பாழ்செய்யும் உட்பகை) and without murderous marauders who harass the king. The danger 885 describes for the king's person and household is, in 735, something whose absence is part of what makes a country a country.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:886","b":"k:764","type":"agrees","note":"886 says that if disunity arises among those who are one with a ruler (ஒன்றாமை ஒன்றியார் கட்படின்), it is always hard for him to escape perishing. 764 defines an army as one that is not destroyed, cannot be undermined from within (அறைபோகாதாகி) and has inherited hardness. Both treat the loyalty of one's own side as a condition of survival, stated once as a warning and once as a requirement.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:887","b":"k:277","type":"parallel_domain","note":"887 uses the casket and its lid for a family that fits together outwardly while divided within. 277 uses the kunri seed for false ascetics, red on the outside (புறங்குன்றி கண்டனையர்) and black within like its tip. Each verse chooses an everyday object to show an outside that does not match the inside, one for kin and one for renouncers.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:887","b":"k:786","type":"parallel_domain","note":"887 says a family with inner enmity is not joined, though it joins like a casket and its lid (செப்பின் புணர்ச்சிபோல் கூடினும் கூடாதே). 786 says friendship that makes only the face smile is not friendship; friendship is what makes the heart within smile (நெஞ்சத்து அகநக). Both deny the name of union to a joining that is only on the surface, in the family and in friendship.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:889","b":"k:433","type":"agrees","note":"889 holds that inner enmity is ruinous even at the size of a split sesame seed. 433 says those who are ashamed of blame take a fault the size of a millet seed (தினைத்துணை) to be as large as a palmyra. 433 describes the attitude that 889 calls for: treating the small thing at its eventual size.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:889","b":"k:879","type":"agrees","note":"889 says inner enmity carries ruin even when it is as small as a split sesame seed. 879 says to cut a thorn tree while it is young (இளைதாக), since grown hard it destroys the cutter's hand. The two neighbouring chapters, on open and on inner enmity, make the same point that hostility should be judged by what it will grow into; only 879 goes on to say what to do about it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:889","b":"k:965","type":"parallel_domain","note":"889 says that inner enmity, though as small as a split sesame seed (எட்பகவன்ன சிறுமைத்தே ஆயினும்), has ruin in it. 965 says even those like a hill will be lowered if they do a lowering thing the size of a kunri seed (குன்றி அனைய செயின்). Both use a tiny seed as the measure of something that brings down what is large, a family or kingdom in 889 and a person's standing in 965.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:891","b":"k:861","type":"agrees","note":"891 makes not slighting the able the chief safeguard; 861 tells the ruler to avoid standing against the stronger (வலியார்க்கு மாறேற்றல் ஓம்புக). The chapter on enmity gives as a tactical rule what 891 gives as a rule of self-protection.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:892","b":"k:450","type":"agrees","note":"892 says disregard of the great brings unending trouble (பேரா இடும்பை); 450 says letting go the tie with the good (நல்லார் தொடர் கைவிடல்) is ten times worse than taking on the enmity of many. Both chapters on the great rank a bad relation to them above ordinary dangers, 450 by a number and 892 by permanence.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:892","b":"k:976","type":"develops","note":"892 warns that going on without cherishing the great (பெரியாரைப் பேணாது ஒழுகின்) brings, through them, ills that do not leave. 976 says the intent to cherish and win the great (பெரியாரைப் பேணிக் கொள்வேம் என்னும் நோக்கு) is simply absent from the minds of the small. The shared verb பேணு ties them: 976 names who it is that fails to cherish, 892 what the failure costs.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:893","b":"k:861","type":"agrees","note":"893 states as irony what 861 states as a plain command: offending those with the power to destroy (அடல்வேண்டின் ஆற்றுபவர்) is a way to seek one's own ruin, and 861 says to avoid opposing the stronger (வலியார்க்கு மாறேற்றல் ஓம்புக).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:893","b":"k:922","type":"parallel_domain","note":"893 says: if you want ruin (கெடல்வேண்டின்), go ahead without listening and wrong those able to destroy. 922 says: do not drink, but if anyone will, let it be those who do not want the regard of the worthy (எண்ணப்பட வேண்டாதார்). Both forbid by an ironic permission addressed to whoever wants the bad result, once about offending the powerful and once about liquor.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:894","b":"k:250","type":"parallel_domain","note":"894 says the unable doing harm to the able (ஆற்றுவார்க்கு ஆற்றாதார் இன்னா செயல்) is like beckoning Death with the hand. 250 asks one about to go against someone weaker (மெலியார்மேல் செல்லுமிடத்து) to think of oneself before the stronger (வலியார்முன்). Both reason from the weak facing the strong: 894 uses it as prudence in the Book of Wealth, 250 turns the same experience into a ground for compassion in the Book of Virtue.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:895","b":"k:691","type":"develops","note":"895 says those who draw the anger of a king of fierce might (வெந்துப்பின் வேந்து செறப்பட்டவர்) cannot go on existing anywhere they go. 691 gives the working rule for those who serve such kings: like people warming at a fire, neither move off nor come close (அகலாது அணுகாது தீக்காய்வார் போல்க). 895 states the danger, and 691 the conduct that lives beside it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:895","b":"k:899","type":"develops","note":"895 makes the king's anger inescapable for those under him; 899 says that if those of exalted resolve flare up (ஏந்திய கொள்கையார் சீறின்), the king himself (வேந்தனும்) breaks midway and loses his kingship. Within the chapter the pair sets up an order of power: the king who destroys others can himself be destroyed by the anger of the great. Parimelazhagar takes வேந்தன் in 899 as Indra.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:896","b":"k:207","type":"tension","note":"207 says those who incur any enmity whatever survive it (எனைப்பகை உற்றாரும் உய்வர்), and only the enmity of one's own evil deeds follows and kills. 896 says those who keep offending the great do not survive (உய்யார்). If எனைப்பகை is taken literally as every human enmity, the two conflict; the conflict eases if 207's phrase is read as a rhetorical foil for the enmity of deeds, or if the harm in 896 is understood as itself the fruit of a wrong deed.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:897","b":"k:444","type":"qualifies","note":"444 says living so that those greater than oneself become one's own is the head of all strengths (வன்மையுள் எல்லாம் தலை). 897 gives the other side: a fully appointed life and great wealth count for nothing (என்னாம்) once the worthy great are angered. Together they make one's standing with the great, and not one's possessions, the deciding strength.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"k:897","b":"k:900","type":"agrees","note":"897 asks what a life excellent in every part and lofty wealth (வகைமாண்ட வாழ்க்கையும் வான்பொருளும்) come to if the worthy of great stature are angered (தக்கார் செறின்). 900 says even those with support beyond measure (இறந்தமைந்த சார்பு) do not survive if those of the highest distinction are angered (சீரார் செறின்). The two close on the same word செறின் and between them cancel a person's own assets and then his outside supports.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:898","b":"k:965","type":"same_image","note":"898 plays on குன்று (hill) and குன்ற (to diminish): if the hill-like intend diminishment, the seemingly permanent vanish. 965 uses the same play: even the hill-like (குன்றின் அனையாரும்) will diminish (குன்றுவர்) if they do a lowering thing as small as a kunri seed. In 898 the hill-like bring others low; in 965 they themselves can be brought low by their own act, so height gives power over others but no protection from one's own lapse.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:900","b":"k:29","type":"agrees","note":"900 says that even those with support beyond measure will not survive (உய்யார்) if those of the highest distinction are angered (சிறந்தமைந்த சீரார் செறின்). 29 says the anger (வெகுளி) of those standing on the hill of good qualities is hard to ward off (காத்தல் அரிது) even for a moment. The Book of Wealth applies to kings and their resources what the opening chapters said in praise of renouncers.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:900","b":"k:303","type":"tension","note":"303 says to forget anger toward anyone at all (யார்மாட்டும்), because every evil is born from it. 900 treats the anger of the most distinguished (சீரார் செறின்) as a force no support can save one from, and Parimelazhagar and Pope take these as people of great austerity. If they are ascetics, the chapter on not being angry would have them without anger; the conflict depends on that identification and on reading 900 as accepting such anger, where it may only be warning the offender of what follows.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:901","b":"k:45","type":"qualifies","note":"45 says that when household life has love and virtue (அன்பும் அறனும்), these are its character and its fruit (பண்பும் பயனும்); Parimelazhagar takes the love as that owed to one's wife. 901 says those who fix their desire on the wife (மனைவிழைவார்) miss the excellent fruit (மாண்பயன்). The pair marks a line between love within the household, which is its fruit, and விழைவு that lets the wife rule the man, which loses it; Parimelazhagar reads 901 as men who follow the wife's lead for pleasure's sake.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:903","b":"k:55","type":"qualifies","note":"903 calls a man's stooping before his wife (இல்லாள்கண் தாழ்ந்த) இயல்பின்மை, something against the natural order, which shames him among the good. 55 praises the wife who rises worshipping her husband (கொழுநன் தொழுதெழுவாள்). The two show that the book fixes the direction of deference in marriage: it is a merit in the wife and a disgrace in the husband.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:903","b":"k:978","type":"qualifies","note":"978 says greatness always bows (பணியுமாம் என்றும் பெருமை), and elsewhere the book calls bowing good for everyone. 903 names one bowing that brings shame: lowering oneself before one's wife (தாழ்ந்த இயல்பின்மை), which Parimelazhagar explains as arising from fear. The pair separates humility that comes from the absence of pride from submission that comes from fear.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:904","b":"k:665","type":"same_image","note":"904 denies வீறு (distinction) to the action of the wife-fearing man: வினையாண்மை வீறெய்தல் இன்று. 665 says the firmness in action (வினைத்திட்பம்) of those who have attained distinction (வீறெய்தி மாண்டார்) reaches the king and is held in regard. The same phrase வீறு எய்து names what capable action normally earns and what, in 904, fear of the wife withholds.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:904","b":"k:1026","type":"develops","note":"904 says the man who fears his wife gets no distinction from his command of action (வினையாண்மை வீறெய்தல் இன்று). 1026 defines good manliness (நல்லாண்மை) as making the mastery of one's house one's own (இல்லாண்மை ஆக்கிக் கொளல்), which Parimelazhagar takes as leading one's family. Both build on ஆண்மை as mastery: the man of 904 has given up mastery at home, and his mastery of work earns no honour.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:905","b":"k:908","type":"agrees","note":"905 says the man who fears his wife fears to do good to the good; 908 says those who act as she wishes (பெட்டாங்கு ஒழுகுபவர்) neither meet their friends' needs nor do good (நன்றாற்றார்). Both state the cost to others: the wife-ruled man fails the good in 905 and his friends in 908.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:906","b":"k:1088","type":"parallel_domain","note":"906 says that even if they live like the gods (இமையாரின் வாழினும்), men who fear their wife's bamboo-like shoulders (அமையார்தோள் அஞ்சுபவர்) have no dignity (பாடிலர்). In 1088 a lover exclaims that his might (பீடு), feared by enemies in battle, has broken before her bright forehead alone. Both show male standing giving way before a woman's bodily feature; the Book of Wealth counts it a disgrace, while in the Book of Love it is a lover's speech in the moment and carries no judgment.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:907","b":"k:1011","type":"qualifies","note":"1011 ranks shame at one's deeds (கருமத்தால் நாணுதல்) as true shame and sets aside the shame of fair-browed women (திருநுதல் நல்லவர் நாண்) as another kind. 907 says a woman who has shame (நாணுடைப் பெண்) has more greatness than the manhood of one who lives doing a woman's bidding. So the woman's modesty that 1011 treats as the lesser kind still outranks, in 907, manhood that has given up command.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:907","b":"k:1134","type":"same_image","note":"907 pairs நாண் and ஆண்மை: manhood that obeys a woman is worth less than womanhood with shame. In 1134 a lover says the fierce flood of desire carries off the raft made of shame and good manliness (நாணொடு நல்லாண்மை என்னும் புணை). Both treat shame and manliness as the two things a man loses together under a woman's hold; 907 states it as a judgment, 1134 as a lover's own complaint.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:907","b":"k:1137","type":"agrees","note":"907 gives greatness to the woman with shame over the submissive man's manhood (பெண்ணே பெருமை உடைத்து). 1137, a speech in the Book of Love, says nothing has more worth than womanhood that suffers a sea of desire and still does not mount the palmyra horse (மடலேறாப் பெண்ணின் பெருந்தக்கது இல்). In both, a woman's restraint is measured against a man's lack of it and ranked higher.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:908","b":"k:141","type":"same_image","note":"908 describes men who live as the woman wishes, பெட்டாங்கு ஒழுகுபவர்; 141 speaks of the folly of living in desire for another man's woman, பெட்டொழுகும் பேதைமை. The same pairing of பெட்டு (desire, wish) with ஒழுகு (to conduct oneself) describes a life steered by desire in both, in 141 the man's own desire for a forbidden woman and in 908 the wishes of his own wife.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:908","b":"k:788","type":"qualifies","note":"788 defines friendship as removing a friend's distress on the spot (ஆங்கே இடுக்கண் களைவது), like the hand of a man whose garment slips. 908 says those who live as the woman of the fine brow wishes (நன்னுதலாள் பெட்டாங்கு ஒழுகுபவர்) do not see their friends' needs through (நட்டார் குறைமுடியார்). 908 names a condition under which the duty of 788 goes unmet: the man's actions are no longer his own to direct.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:909","b":"k:754","type":"parallel_domain","note":"754 says wealth that comes by right means and without wrong yields virtue and also pleasure (அறன் ஈனும் இன்பமும் ஈனும்). 909 lists the same three goods as missing in men who do a woman's bidding (பெண்ஏவல் செய்வார்கண் இல்). Where 754 treats the three as flowing from one sound source, 909 treats them as shut off by one fault.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:909","b":"k:760","type":"qualifies","note":"760 says that for those who have made abundant wealth the other two, virtue and pleasure, are easily had together (ஏனை இரண்டும் ஒருங்கு). 909 says that deeds of virtue, ample wealth and the other pursuits (அறவினையும் ஆன்ற பொருளும் பிறவினையும்) are all absent in those who do a woman's bidding; Parimelazhagar and Pope take பிறவினை as pleasure. Both use the three aims as the measure of a life: 760 shows how they are gained together, 909 a way they are lost together.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:910","b":"k:917","type":"agrees","note":"910 says those with deliberation and firmness of heart never fall into the folly of attachment to a woman. 917, in the next chapter, says it is those without a firm heart (நிறைநெஞ்சம் இல்லவர்) who touch the shoulders of women who embrace with their minds on other things. Both locate the safeguard in the man's own நெஞ்சம், stated positively in 910 for the wife-ruled man and negatively in 917 for the client of courtesans.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:911","b":"k:1258","type":"parallel_domain","note":"911 warns that the இன்சொல் of women who want a man's wealth brings him harm. In 1258 a woman calls the soft speech (பணிமொழி) of her lover, a 'thief of many deceptions' (பன்மாயக் கள்வன்), the weapon that breaks her womanly reserve (பெண்மை உடைக்கும் படை). Both treat gentle words as an instrument that undoes the hearer, though 1258 is a lover's complaint in the Book of Love, not a warning the book gives in its own voice.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:912","b":"k:103","type":"same_image","note":"103 says help given by those who do not weigh the return (பயன்தூக்கார்) proves, when its worth is weighed (நயன்தூக்கின்), greater than the sea. 912 uses the same pair of words in the same order: the women weigh the gain (பயன்தூக்கி), and the man is to weigh their conduct (நயன்தூக்கி) and not join them. Weighing the return is what spoils a relationship in both verses, and weighing worth is the observer's proper response.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:913","b":"k:80","type":"develops","note":"80 says the seat of life follows love, and that for those without it the body is bone wrapped in skin (என்புதோல் போர்த்த உடம்பு). 913 likens the false embrace (பொய்ம்மை முயக்கம்) of women who want wealth to embracing an unknown corpse in a dark room (ஏதில் பிணம்). 913 applies 80's premise to a particular case: a body offered without love is, for the one who holds it, a dead one.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:914","b":"k:285","type":"parallel_domain","note":"285 says that a loving nature with grace in view (அருள்கருதி) is not found in those who, with goods in view (பொருள்கருதி), watch for another's unguarded moment. 914 opposes those who examine அருட்பொருள் to women for whom பொருள் is the only thing of value. In the chapter on theft and in this one, the same pair, aiming at grace or aiming at goods, sorts people into two kinds that do not mix.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:915","b":"k:636","type":"agrees","note":"636 asks what subtle scheme can stand before those who have native acuteness together with book-learning (மதிநுட்பம் நூலோடு உடையார்). 915 says those whose knowledge is made excellent by goodness of intellect (மதிநலத்தின் மாண்ட அறிவினவர்) do not touch the favours of women common to all; Parimelazhagar reads this too as natural intellect perfecting acquired knowledge. The pairing shows the same compound of mind being credited in statecraft with seeing through stratagems and here with keeping a man from a private vice.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:915","b":"k:1311","type":"same_image","note":"915 calls the women பொதுநலத்தார், those whose favours are common to all, and says the wise will not touch them. In 1311 a wife tells her husband that all women feast on him in common with their eyes (கண்ணின் பொதுஉண்பர்), calls him பரத்த, and refuses his chest. The reproach of being 'common' is turned on the man there, and it is a sulking wife's charge in the Book of Love, not a judgement the book makes itself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:917","b":"k:57","type":"parallel_domain","note":"57 says confinement does little to guard women, and that the guard kept by their own நிறை, firmness, is the chief one. 917 says it is men without a heart of நிறை (நிறைநெஞ்சம் இல்லவர்) who touch the shoulders of women who embrace while their hearts tend other things. The same inner firmness that 57 names as a wife's real protection is here named as what a man lacks when he goes to such women.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:917","b":"k:864","type":"parallel_domain","note":"864 says the man who does not let go of anger and has no நிறை (நிறையிலன்) is easy prey for anyone, at any time and place. 917 says those without நிறைநெஞ்சம் are the ones who go to women whose hearts are set on something else. In both the lack of self-command is what exposes a man, to enemies in 864 and to women who want what he has in 917.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:918","b":"k:910","type":"agrees","note":"910 says the folly that comes of attachment to a woman never exists in those whose hearts hold deliberation (எண்சேர்ந்த நெஞ்சத்து இடனுடையார்). 918 says the embrace of deceiving women is an affliction to those who lack examining understanding (ஆயும் அறிவினர் அல்லார்க்கு). The neighbouring chapters, on subservience to a wife and on courtesans, give the same safeguard: a mind that thinks the matter through.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:918","b":"k:1082","type":"same_image","note":"918 says that for those without discerning understanding the embrace of deceiving women is an அணங்கு, which Parimelazhagar glosses as the touch of a spirit-woman who takes life through desire. In 1082 a lover says that her returning his look is like an attacking அணங்கு (தாக்கணங்கு) arriving with an army. The same figure of the woman as an afflicting spirit is a lover's praise in the Book of Love and a warning in 918.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:920","b":"k:936","type":"same_image","note":"920 says double-minded women, liquor and dice are the companions of those whom Fortune has removed from herself (திருநீக்கப்பட்டார்). 936 says those covered by the goddess of misfortune named gambling (சூதென்னும் முகடி) do not eat their fill and suffer. Both verses describe a vice through the two sister goddesses: in 920 the vices attend the man Tiru has already left, in 936 one of them is Mugadi herself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:920","b":"k:974","type":"same_image","note":"920 names women of two minds (இருமனப் பெண்டிர்), which Parimelazhagar explains as a mind that joins and does not join a man at once. 974 says greatness, like women of one mind (ஒருமை மகளிர்), exists only by guarding oneself. The two phrases are opposites, and where single-mindedness is 974's model for keeping greatness, doubleness in 920 goes with dice and drink as the company of the unfortunate.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:921","b":"k:653","type":"develops","note":"653 tells those who mean to rise to leave off any deed that dims their lustre (ஒளிமாழ்கும் செய்வினை). 921 says those who live in love of liquor (கட்காதல் கொண்டொழுகுவார்) are never held in awe and lose their lustre (ஒளியிழப்பர்). 921 gives a named instance of the kind of act 653 warns against in general.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:921","b":"k:939","type":"parallel_domain","note":"939 says that clothing, wealth, food, lustre (ஒளி) and learning do not come near a man who takes to gaming. 921 says those devoted to liquor lose their ஒளி and are not feared. The adjoining chapters on drink and dice both count the loss of ஒளி, the standing that makes a man regarded, among the costs.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:922","b":"k:656","type":"agrees","note":"922 forbids drinking and adds that if anyone drinks, it should be those who do not want to be counted by the worthy (சான்றோரான் எண்ணப்பட வேண்டாதார்). 656 forbids any act the worthy condemn (சான்றோர் பழிக்கும் வினை), even to relieve one's own mother's hunger. In both the judgement of the சான்றோர் is the measure of conduct; 922 states it as the price a drinker must be willing to pay.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:924","b":"k:1288","type":"same_image","note":"924 says shame turns away from the drinker. In 1288 the speaker tells her lover that his chest is to her as liquor is to those who have enjoyed it, wanted even though it does harm that brings disgrace (இளித்தக்க இன்னா செயினும்). The simile in the Book of Love takes for granted what 924 teaches, that drink disgraces, and uses it to describe a desire that persists in spite of shame.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:925","b":"k:838","type":"same_image","note":"838 says a fool getting something into his hand is like a deranged man getting drunk (மையல் ஒருவன் களித்தற்று). 925 says paying out wealth to buy oblivion shows ignorance. 838 uses drunkenness as the image for what a fool does with property, and 925 describes drunkenness itself as a fool's use of property.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:927","b":"k:271","type":"same_image","note":"271 says the five elements laugh inwardly (அகத்தே நகும்) at the hidden conduct of a man with a deceitful mind. 927 says those who drink and droop are seen through and always laughed at by their own town (உள்ளொற்றி உள்ளூர் நகப்படுவர்); Parimelazhagar takes கள்ளொற்றி as drinking in concealment. In both, concealed conduct has a witness that laughs, the elements within the body in 271 and the neighbours who infer what is going on in 927.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:928","b":"k:293","type":"develops","note":"293 says not to lie about what one's own heart knows, for the heart will burn the liar afterwards. 928 concerns one such lie, 'I have never been drunk' (களித்தறியேன்), about something the speaker's heart knows, and says that drink brings the hidden thing out. 293 gives the inner penalty for this kind of lie and 928 adds an outward one, exposure.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:928","b":"k:1161","type":"parallel_domain","note":"928 tells the drinker to stop saying he has never been drunk, because what he hid in his heart shows all the more at that moment (ஒளித்ததூஉம் ஆங்கே மிகும்). In 1161 a woman says she would hide her pain, but like spring water for those who draw it, it increases (ஊற்றுநீர் போல மிகும்). Both end on மிகும்: the attempt to conceal an inner state is answered by its overflowing, in drink and in love.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:931","b":"k:113","type":"agrees","note":"113 says to let go, that very day, of gain that comes from leaving fairness even if it yields only good (நன்றே தரினும்). 931 uses the same concession, 'even if you win' (வென்றிடினும்), and still refuses the gain, calling it a swallowed hook. In both the apparent success of a wrong source is conceded and set aside.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:933","b":"k:385","type":"qualifies","note":"385 defines a king as one able to create sources of wealth, gather it, guard it and apportion it (இயற்றலும் ஈட்டலும் காத்தலும்). 933, which Parimelazhagar reads of a king, says both the gathered wealth and its sources (பொருள் ஆயம்) leave him if he keeps at the dice. One habit undoes the gathering and the guarding that 385 makes the mark of rule.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:933","b":"k:1009","type":"parallel_domain","note":"933 says that if one keeps calling the rolling dice-throw (உருளாயம்), wealth and its income (பொருளாயம்) go and fall outside (புறமே படும்); Parimelazhagar takes 'outside' as into enemies' hands. 1009 says the wealth a miser gathered without love, self-regard or virtue is taken by others (கொள்வார் பிறர்). The gambler and the hoarder, by opposite habits, both end with their wealth in strangers' hands.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:934","b":"k:1045","type":"develops","note":"934 says nothing gives poverty like gambling, which does many small harms and ruins standing (சிறுமை பலசெய்து சீரழிக்கும்). 1045 says that within the one affliction called poverty many sufferings arrive (பல்குரைத் துன்பங்கள் சென்று படும்). Read together they form a chain: gambling brings harms on the way to poverty, and poverty then brings its own.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:935","b":"k:437","type":"same_image","note":"935 says those who clung (இவறியார்) in pride to dice, gaming hall and skilled hand became people with nothing (இல்லாகியார்). 437 uses the same word of the miser: the wealth of one who clings (இவறியான்) without doing what should be done perishes. இவறுதல், holding on and not letting go, ends in loss in both verses, whether the thing held is money or the game.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:935","b":"k:539","type":"develops","note":"539 advises thinking, when swollen with one's own joy, of those who were ruined by negligence (இகழ்ச்சியின் கெட்டாரை உள்ளுக). 935 supplies such a memory: those who took pride (தருக்கி) in the dice and came to nothing; Parimelazhagar reads them as kings of former times and names the Pandavas. 935 does for gambling what 539 asks a ruler to do in general.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:936","b":"k:167","type":"same_image","note":"167 says the fair goddess, grudging the envious man, shows him to her elder sister (தவ்வையைக் காட்டி விடும்). 936 names that sister, முகடி, as gambling itself, covering those who play. Envy hands a person over to misfortune; gambling, in 936, is misfortune under another name.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:937","b":"k:1043","type":"parallel_domain","note":"937 says time spent in the gaming hall ruins long-held wealth and character (பழகிய செல்வமும் பண்பும் கெடுக்கும்). 1043 says poverty destroys together one's ancient descent and one's தோல் (தொல்வரவும் தோலும் கெடுக்கும்), which Pope takes as dignity of speech. The two sentences have the same shape: an inherited standing and a personal quality lost at one stroke, with gambling as a cause and poverty as a cause.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:938","b":"k:248","type":"develops","note":"938 lists what gambling does: it destroys wealth (பொருள் கெடுத்து), makes one take up lying, destroys compassion (அருள் கெடுத்து) and drives one through misery. 248 ranks two of these losses: those without wealth may flower again one day (பூப்பர் ஒருகால்), but those without அருள் are finished. By 248's measure the third item in 938's list is graver than the first.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:938","b":"k:757","type":"develops","note":"757 calls அருள் a child born of love that is raised by the wealthy foster-mother called பொருள். 938 has gambling destroy both பொருள் and அருள். Parimelazhagar assigns the losses in 938 to separate causes (losing, winning, resentment), but 757 suggests a link between them: with the nurse gone, the child is harder to raise.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:939","b":"k:166","type":"parallel_domain","note":"166 says the kin of one who envies a gift perish without anything to wear or eat (உடுப்பதூஉம் உண்பதூஉம் இன்றி). 939 begins its list of what gaming keeps away with the same pair, clothing and food (உடை ... ஊண்). Envy and gambling are both measured by the loss of the plainest necessities.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:939","b":"k:400","type":"tension","note":"939 names five things, among them learning (கல்வி), that 'அடையா' the one who takes to gaming. Parimelazhagar glosses this as 'do not come to him', but Pope's verse rendering and Kalaignar take the five as departing from him. On that second reading 939 conflicts with 400, which calls learning the wealth that cannot perish (கேடில் விழுச்செல்வம்); on Parimelazhagar's reading there is no conflict, since the gambler never acquires it.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:940","b":"k:1145","type":"parallel_domain","note":"In 1145 a lover says love grows sweeter each time it is exposed, as drinking is wanted more each time one is drunk (களித்தொறும் கள்ளுண்டல் வேட்டற்றால்). 940 has the same 'each time ... the more' construction with another vice, the dice, as the comparison (இழத்தொறூஉம் காதலிக்கும் சூதேபோல்). The lover uses the figure to praise his state, while 940 uses it to describe an attachment fed by its own losses.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:941","b":"k:466","type":"parallel_domain","note":"941 says the three humours counted by the learned, beginning with wind, cause disease whether they exceed or fall short (மிகினும் குறையினும்); Parimelazhagar refers the excess and shortfall to food and activity. 466 says one is ruined by doing what is unfit and also by not doing what is fit (செயக் கெடும் ... செய்யாமையானும் கெடும்). Both verses locate harm on two sides of a right amount, in the body and in action.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:943","b":"k:479","type":"parallel_domain","note":"943 says to eat knowing the measure once digestion is complete, as the way one who has a body can carry it long (நெடிதுய்க்கும் ஆறு). 479 says the life of one who does not live knowing the measure (அளவறிந்து வாழாதான்) seems to exist and then perishes; Parimelazhagar refers that measure to one's wealth. The same phrase governs both the stomach and the purse. (This edition prints 'அறவறிந்து' in 943; Parimelazhagar's gloss reads அளவு அறிந்து.)","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:946","b":"k:420","type":"parallel_domain","note":"946 says disease stays with the one of very great feed (கழிபேர் இரையான்), as pleasure stays with the one who eats knowing to stop short (இழிவறிந்து உண்பான்). 420 asks what it matters whether those live or die who know taste only by the mouth and not by the ear, and calls them மாக்கள். Both verses use a word with an animal sense for the person ruled by the mouth: இரை, feed, and மாக்கள்.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:947","b":"k:289","type":"parallel_domain","note":"289 says those who know nothing but stealth do what is beyond measure and perish right there (அளவல்ல செய்தாங்கே வீவர்). 947 says the one who eats beyond the measure of his fire gets disease beyond measure. Chapter 29 uses அளவு as a moral measure and chapter 95 as a bodily one, and in each case overstepping it brings its own penalty.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:947","b":"k:475","type":"parallel_domain","note":"947 says that if one eats greatly without discerning the measure of the fire within (தீயளவு), disease arises without measure (நோயளவின்றிப் படும்). 475 says even a cart loaded with peacock feathers breaks its axle if they are piled too high (சால மிகுத்துப் பெயின்). In both a fixed capacity, the digestive fire or the axle, is what excess breaks, whatever the quality of the load.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:948","b":"k:360","type":"parallel_domain","note":"948 tells the healer to look for the root of the disease (நோய்முதல்) before the remedy. 360 does this for suffering in general: when desire, anger and confusion perish even in name, the நோய் perishes. The chapter on true perception treats pain as chapter 95 treats illness, by naming causes and removing them.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:948","b":"k:504","type":"parallel_domain","note":"948 sets out an order of enquiry: look into the disease, then its root, then the means that subdue it, and act so that it works (நோய்நாடி நோய்முதல் நாடி ... வாய்நாடி). 504 has the same threefold நாடி for judging a person: examine the qualities, examine the faults, examine which predominates, and take that. Diagnosis of a patient and assessment of a candidate are given the same grammar of step-by-step examination before a decision.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:949","b":"k:637","type":"agrees","note":"637 says that even when one knows the method of doing a thing, one should act knowing how the world goes (செயற்கை அறிந்தக் கடைத்தும் உலகத்து இயற்கை அறிந்து செயல்); Parimelazhagar glosses the method as what the treatises teach. 949 addresses the one who has learned (கற்றான்) and still requires him to consider this patient, this illness and this moment. Both hold that book learning has to be fitted to the particular case.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:952","b":"k:133","type":"qualifies","note":"952 says the well-born do not slip (இழுக்கார்) in conduct, truthfulness and shame. 133 says having conduct is itself குடிமை and that a lapse (இழுக்கம்) turns into low birth (இழிந்த பிறப்பாய் விடும்). Read together, 952's claim is close to a definition: whoever slips has by 133 already left the class of the well-born, a point Parimelazhagar makes on 133 while keeping it within varna.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:952","b":"k:983","type":"parallel_domain","note":"952 lists three things the well-born keep: ஒழுக்கம், வாய்மை, நாண். 983 lists five pillars that hold up சால்பு: love, shame (நாண்), obligingness, kindly regard and truthfulness (வாய்மை). Two of the three marks of lineage reappear as supports of personal worth, so the same core is described once as inherited and once as a structure a person stands on.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:953","b":"k:92","type":"develops","note":"953 names a smile, giving, sweet words and not scorning (நகை ஈகை இன்சொல் இகழாமை) as the share of a true lineage. 92 takes three of these and ranks them: being sweet-spoken with a glad face (முகனமர்ந்து இன்சொலன்) is better than giving with a glad heart (அகனமர்ந்து ஈதல்). The list in 953 is flat; 92 shows the book elsewhere weighing its items against one another.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:953","b":"k:1057","type":"agrees","note":"953 puts giving and not scorning (ஈகை ... இகழாமை) side by side among the marks of a good family; Parimelazhagar takes all four as the way one meets the poor who come. 1057 gives the receiver's side: on seeing those who give without scorn or contempt (இகழ்ந்தெள்ளாது ஈவார்), the asker's heart rejoices within. The pairing shows why 'not scorning' sits next to 'giving' in the list.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:954","b":"k:311","type":"parallel_domain","note":"954 says the well-born will not do diminishing things (குன்றுவ) even if they get crores piled up (அடுக்கிய கோடி பெறினும்). 311 says the stainless hold to not hurting others even if it would bring wealth that gives distinction (சிறப்பீனும் செல்வம் பெறினும்). Both use the same 'even if they gained' (பெறினும்) form to set a fixed refusal against a large reward, once for family standing and once for non-harm.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:954","b":"k:657","type":"agrees","note":"954 says no amount of wealth makes the well-born do what lowers them. 657 says the extreme poverty of the worthy (சான்றோர் கழிநல்குரவு) ranks above gain reached by taking on blame (பழிமலைந்து எய்திய ஆக்கம்). Both rank standing above wealth; 657 states the comparison outright, in the context of a minister's clean action.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:954","b":"k:965","type":"develops","note":"954 says the well-born do not do things that diminish (குன்றுவ செய்தல் இலர்), whatever the gain. 965 uses the same word to say what follows if they do: even those like a hill sink (குன்றுவர்) if they do a diminishing thing (குன்றுவ) the size of a kundri seed. 954 gives the rule and 965 the cost of breaking it, at a scale far below crores.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:955","b":"k:1043","type":"tension","note":"955 says an ancient family (பழங்குடி) does not part from its character when its means shrink. 1043 says poverty destroys together one's ancient descent and one's speech (தொல்வரவும் தோலும் கெடுக்கும்). They conflict if 1043 is read as plain poverty undoing lineage; the conflict eases on Parimelazhagar's reading, which stresses that 1043 calls poverty a craving (நசை), so it is the wanting, not the lack, that ruins.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:956","b":"k:656","type":"agrees","note":"956 says those who mean to keep to a spotless line do not do what lacks worth (சால்பில செய்யார்); Parimelazhagar adds 'even in poverty'. 656 says one should not do what the worthy condemn (சான்றோர் பழிக்கும் வினை) even if one must see one's mother hungry. Both set a standard of worthy conduct above need, 656 naming the hardest case.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:956","b":"k:660","type":"develops","note":"956 says those resolved to live by their spotless lineage will not do unworthy things by taking to deceit (சலம் பற்றி). 660 uses the same word for wealth-making: securing wealth made by deceit (சலத்தால் பொருள் செய்து) is like keeping water in an unfired clay pot. 956 rules deceit out on grounds of family honour; 660 adds that its gains do not hold anyway.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:958","b":"k:992","type":"agrees","note":"958 says that if lack of affection (நாரின்மை) shows in a man, people will doubt his lineage (குலத்தின்கண் ஐயப்படும்). 992 names having love and being born in a seasoned family (அன்புடைமை ஆன்ற குடிப்பிறத்தல்) as the two things that make up courtesy. 992 pairs love and birth as partners; 958 shows that when the first is missing the second is no longer believed.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:960","b":"k:125","type":"agrees","note":"960 says one who wants lineage must want humility toward everyone (யார்க்கும் பணிவு). 125 says bowing is good for all (எல்லார்க்கும் நன்றாம் பணிதல்) and is a further wealth for the rich. Both make humility general, and each singles out the highly placed, by family or by wealth, as those it most becomes.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:960","b":"k:963","type":"qualifies","note":"960 asks for humility toward all (யார்க்கும் பணிவு) from anyone who wants to keep a lineage. 963, in the next chapter, limits when bowing is called for: in abundance one must bow (பெருக்கத்து வேண்டும் பணிதல்), but in reduced circumstances one must stand high (சுருக்கத்து வேண்டும் உயர்வு). The humility of 960 is thus not meant to cover bending under want.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:960","b":"k:1019","type":"same_image","note":"960 pairs நலம் with shame and குலம் with humility: want நலம், then want நாணுடைமை; want குலம், then want பணிவு toward all. 1019 uses the same pair in negative form: a lapse in principle burns one's குலம், and settled shamelessness burns one's நலம் (நலஞ்சுடும் நாணின்மை). The two verses share the குலம்/நலம் pairing and agree that நலம் depends on shame.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:961","b":"k:131","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Parimelazhagar reads 961's இன்றி அமையா as things without which one would not survive, so the verse puts standing above life. 131 says conduct is to be guarded more than life (உயிரினும் ஓம்பப்படும்) because it gives excellence. The same ranking, a value kept ahead of survival, is made for honour in one chapter and for conduct in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:962","b":"k:113","type":"parallel_domain","note":"962 refuses unworthy acts even when they would bring renown (சீரினும்). 113 says to let go at once of gain that departs from fairness even if it brings nothing but good (நன்றே தரினும் நடுவிகந்தாம் ஆக்கம்). Both have the form 'even if it yields the thing you want, not by this means', applied to fame in one and to profit in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:962","b":"k:652","type":"qualifies","note":"652 says to avoid any action that yields neither renown nor good (புகழொடு நன்றி பயவா வினை), which makes renown one of the tests of an act. 962 says those who want renown together with great manliness will not do unworthy things even for renown (சீரினும் சீரல்ல செய்யார்). 962 therefore narrows 652's test: bringing fame does not by itself make an act fit to do.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:963","b":"k:680","type":"tension","note":"963 says that in small and shrunken circumstances one must hold oneself high (சிறிய சுருக்கத்து வேண்டும் உயர்வு). 680 says those whose domain is small (உறைசிறியார்), fearing their own people's trembling, will bow to the greater and accept terms (கொள்வர் பெரியார்ப் பணிந்து). They conflict if 963's 'reduced state' includes weakness of position before a stronger party; if 963 is only about poverty and personal bearing, 680 is separate advice on statecraft.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:963","b":"k:988","type":"develops","note":"963 asks for an upright bearing (உயர்வு) when one's means have shrunk. 988 says poverty is no disgrace (இன்மை ஒருவற்கு இளிவன்று) for one who has the firmness called worth (சால்பென்னும் திண்மை). 988 supplies the ground for 963's demand: want does not itself lower a person, so there is no reason to stoop in it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:964","b":"k:124","type":"develops","note":"124 says the stature of one who is restrained without shifting from his position (நிலையின் திரியாது அடங்கியான்) is far greater than a mountain. 964 uses the same word for the opposite case: those who descend from their position (நிலையின் இழிந்த) are like hair off the head. Together they give the two ends of one scale, both measured by whether a person keeps to their நிலை.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:964","b":"k:973","type":"tension","note":"964 says people who have come down from their position (நிலையின் இழிந்தக் கடை) are like hair fallen from the head. 973 says those who are not low are not low even when placed low (கீழிருந்தும் கீழல்லார் கீழல்லவர்). They conflict if 964's நிலை means outward rank or station; on Parimelazhagar's reading it means the high standard the well-born hold themselves to, so the fall is their own doing and the verses agree.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:966","b":"k:1033","type":"same_image","note":"966 asks what is gained by going and standing behind those who scorn you (இகழ்வார் பின் சென்று நிலை). 1033 says only those who eat by their own ploughing truly live, and all others bow to eat and go behind (தொழுதுண்டு பின்செல்பவர்). Both use 'going behind' another as the picture of a dependent life, and 1033 names the livelihood that avoids it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:966","b":"k:1053","type":"qualifies","note":"966 says standing in the train of those who scorn you brings neither renown nor the gods' land. 1053 says that even asking has a certain beauty (ஏஎர் உடைத்து) when one stands before those of unconcealing heart who know what is due (கரப்பிலா நெஞ்சின் கடனறிவார் முன்நின்று). What 966 rejects is therefore not dependence as such but dependence on people who despise the one who comes to them.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:967","b":"k:230","type":"parallel_domain","note":"967 prefers being called ruined to a life got by following those who reject you. 230 says nothing is more bitter than dying, yet even that is sweet when giving is out of reach (ஈதல் இயையாக் கடை). In both, a life that cannot be lived on one's own terms, without honour or without the power to give, is ranked below death.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:968","b":"k:82","type":"same_image","note":"968 asks whether a life that tends the flesh is a medicine (மருந்தோ) once greatness has lost its dignity; Parimelazhagar takes this as a medicine against dying. 82 says that eating while the guest waits outside is not to be wanted even if the food were the medicine of deathlessness (சாவா மருந்தெனினும்). Both raise the idea of an immortality-medicine only to rank it below a duty, honour in one and hospitality in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:968","b":"k:1017","type":"agrees","note":"968 questions the worth of a life that merely feeds the body (ஊன் ஓம்பும் வாழ்க்கை) when dignity is gone. 1017 says those governed by shame give up life for shame and do not give up shame for life (நாணால் உயிரைத் துறப்பர் ... நாண் துறவார்). The chapters on honour and on shame reach the same ordering of life and self-respect.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:969","b":"k:779","type":"parallel_domain","note":"969 says those like the கவரிமா give up life when their honour is at stake (உயிர் நீப்பர் மானம் வரின்). 779 asks who could reproach as failures those who die so that what they vowed is not broken (இழைத்தது இகவாமைச் சாவார்). Dying to avoid going back on what one stands for appears as a matter of civil honour in 969 and as a soldier's vow in 779.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:970","b":"k:235","type":"develops","note":"970 says the world worships and praises the radiance of the honourable who do not live on in disgrace. 235 says that a ruin which is growth and a death which is existence (உளதாகும் சாக்காடு) are hard to reach except for the skilled. 235 gives the general idea that 970 applies: a death can leave a person present in fame.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:970","b":"k:971","type":"same_image","note":"970 sets disgrace against radiance: the world worships the ஒளி of those who will not live when இளி comes. 971 defines the same two words: one's ஒளி is abundance of aspiration, and one's இளி is saying 'we will live without it' (அஃதிறந்து வாழ்தும் எனல்). In both, disgrace is a way of going on living, and radiance belongs to those who refuse it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:981","b":"k:218","type":"develops","note":"981 says that for those who know what is due (கடன் அறிந்து) and take on worth, every good thing is கடன்; Parimelazhagar glosses this as the good qualities being natural to them. 218 uses the same phrase, கடனறி காட்சியவர், for people who do not falter in ஒப்புரவு even in a season without means (இடனில் பருவத்தும்). 218 shows one concrete case of 981's general claim: a duty that is felt as one's own does not wait on circumstances.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:982","b":"k:79","type":"parallel_domain","note":"982 says the worthy have only the goodness of character (குணநலம்), and that other goodness is not counted among goodnesses; Parimelazhagar takes பிறநலம் to be the goodness of the bodily limbs. 79 asks what all the outer limbs (புறத்துறுப்பு) can do for those who lack love, the inner limb (அகத்துறுப்பு). Both verses rank an inner quality over the body's outward parts, one for worth and one for love.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:982","b":"k:407","type":"agrees","note":"982 denies that any goodness other than character counts for the worthy. 407 says the handsome looks (எழில்நலம்) of one without fine, penetrating understanding are those of a decorated clay doll (மண்மாண் புனைபாவை). Both dismiss outward நலம் that has nothing inside it, though 407 measures by understanding and 982 by character.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:983","b":"k:615","type":"same_image","note":"983 calls its five qualities the pillar planted to bear worth (சால்பு ஊன்றிய தூண்). 615 calls the man who wants the work and not pleasure a pillar that wipes away his kin's troubles and props them (துடைத்து ஊன்றும் தூண்). The same figure, with the same verb ஊன்று, is used once for qualities holding up a character and once for a person holding up a family.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:983","b":"k:1013","type":"develops","note":"983 lists five pillars that bear worth: அன்பு, நாண், ஒப்புரவு, கண்ணோட்டம், வாய்மை. 1013 singles out one of them: as all lives are fixed on the body (ஊனைக் குறித்த), சால்பு is fixed on the good called shame (நாண் என்னும் நன்மை குறித்தது). Read together, shame is one support among five in 983 and the seat of worth itself in 1013.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:985","b":"k:978","type":"agrees","note":"985 says the strength of those who accomplish is bowing (பணிதல்), and that this is the weapon by which the worthy turn foes. 978 says greatness always bows (பணியுமாம் என்றும் பெருமை) while smallness adorns itself in self-admiration. Both attach bowing to the strong and not the weak; 985 adds that it has an effect on opponents.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:986","b":"k:1327","type":"parallel_domain","note":"986 says the touchstone of worth is accepting defeat (தோல்வி கொளல்) even from those who are not one's equal. In 1327 the man says that in a lovers' quarrel the one who loses has won (ஊடலில் தோற்றவர் வென்றார்), as the reunion shows. Both treat yielding as the better outcome; 1327 is a lover's remark about a sulk, while 986 states a test of character.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:987","b":"k:998","type":"agrees","note":"987 says worth is useless unless it does good even to those who did harm. 998, in the next chapter, says failing in courtesy even toward those who act without friendship or kindness (நயமில செய்வார்க்கும்) is lowness (கடை). Both verses use the particle 'even to' (-க்கும்) to extend the virtue to hostile people, one for சால்பு and one for பண்பு.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:988","b":"k:1064","type":"develops","note":"988 calls சால்பு a firmness (திண்மை) that keeps poverty from being a disgrace. 1064 describes that firmness at work: the worth that will not consent to begging even when no means remain (இடமில்லாக் காலும் இரவொல்லாச் சால்பு) is too large for all space to hold. 1064 shows what the poor but worthy person of 988 does and does not do.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:990","b":"k:572","type":"same_image","note":"990 says the earth cannot carry its burden (பொறை) once the worthy lose their worth. 572 says those without kindly regard are, by merely existing, a burden to the earth (நிலக்குப் பொறை). The same figure is used from two sides: the worthy are what lets the earth carry its load, and people without கண்ணோட்டம் are dead weight on it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:991","b":"k:548","type":"parallel_domain","note":"991 and 548 share the word எண்பதம், easy access. In 548 a king who is not accessible (எண்பதத்தான் ஓரா) and does not examine and do justice falls and perishes by himself; in 991 being accessible to all makes courtesy easy to attain. The quality whose absence ruins a ruler is the one whose presence makes a person courteous.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:994","b":"k:97","type":"same_image","note":"994 says the world honours the character (பண்பு) of those whose usefulness (பயன்) comes from doing good with fairness (நயனொடு நன்றி புரிந்த). 97 uses the same four words of speech: words that yield benefit (பயன்) and do not part from பண்பு produce நயன் and bring forth நன்றி. Parimelazhagar glosses நயன் as justice in both, so the cluster describes good speech in 97 and a good life in 994.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:994","b":"k:216","type":"develops","note":"994 praises people of நயன் whose doing good makes them useful (பயனுடையார்). 216 says wealth falling to one who has நயன் (நயனுடையான்) is like a fruit-bearing tree (பயன்மரம்) ripening in the middle of the town. 216 supplies the picture for 994: the person of நயன் is of பயன் to everyone within reach.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:995","b":"k:874","type":"develops","note":"995 says courtesy is present even in enmity (பகையுள்ளும் பண்புள) in those who understand others. 874 says the world rests in the greatness of the person of courtesy who treats enmity as friendship (பகை நட்பாக் கொண்டொழுகும் பண்புடையாளன்). 874 takes the step beyond 995, from staying courteous toward an enemy to turning him into a friend; Parimelazhagar reads 874 as a matter of policy.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:995","b":"k:953","type":"agrees","note":"995 warns that இகழ்ச்சி, scorn, wounds even as a joke. 953 lists not scorning (இகழாமை) with a smile, giving and sweet words as the four marks of a true family. 953 names the trait as a mark of good birth, and 995 gives the reason for it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:998","b":"k:579","type":"agrees","note":"998 calls it lowness (கடை) to fail in courtesy even toward those who act without friendship or kindness. 579 calls it the highest thing (தலை) to extend kindly regard and bear with even those whose nature is to hurt (ஒறுத்தாற்றும் பண்பினார் கண்ணும்). The verses mark the two ends of the same scale: good conduct toward the hostile is தலை, and its failure is கடை.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:998","b":"k:852","type":"agrees","note":"998 requires courtesy even toward those who do unfriendly, unkind things. 852 says that even if someone does what one cannot bear, intending division (பகல் கருதிப் பற்றா செயினும்), it is best not to do harm in return out of hostility. 852 gives the minimum, no retaliation, and 998 asks for more, that courtesy itself not lapse.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:999","b":"k:523","type":"develops","note":"999 says that for those not able to laugh (நகல் வல்லர் அல்லார்) the great world lies in darkness even by day; Parimelazhagar takes நகல் as mixing with others and being glad at heart. 523 says the life of one who does not mingle freely (அளவளாவு இல்லாதான்) is like a tank filling without a bank. On Parimelazhagar's reading both describe the cost of not mixing with people, as lost wealth in 523 and a darkened world in 999.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:1000","b":"k:1008","type":"agrees","note":"1000 says great wealth obtained by one without courtesy is like good milk spoiled by the fault of its vessel (கலந்தீமையால் திரிந்தற்று). 1008 says the wealth of a man nobody seeks out is a poison tree bearing fruit in the middle of the village (நச்சு மரம் பழுத்தற்று). Both say a good thing is made useless or harmful by the character of its holder; Parimelazhagar reads 1000 as wealth that benefits no one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1001","b":"k:229","type":"develops","note":"229 says eating alone in order to fill up one's store (நிரப்பிய தாமே தமியர் உணல்) is more bitter than begging; 1001 takes the case one step further, the man who does not even eat it (அஃதுண்ணான்), and calls him dead. Read together they grade the misuse of wealth: unshared use is wretched, no use at all is not living.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1002","b":"k:751","type":"qualifies","note":"751 says nothing but wealth makes people of no account into people of account (பொருளல்லது இல்லை பொருள்). 1002 names nearly the same thought, பொருளானாம் எல்லாம், as what the miser thinks, and says that when it leads to not giving (ஈயாது இவறும்) it is delusion. The chapter on making wealth is thus limited here: the claim holds only for wealth that is given and used.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1004","b":"k:114","type":"same_image","note":"114 says whether people were worthy or not is seen from their எச்சம்; 1004 asks what எச்சம் the unwanted man imagines for himself. Parimelazhagar takes the word as offspring in 114 and as what remains after death, i.e. renown, in 1004, so the pairing shows the same test of a life by what it leaves, applied with two senses of the word.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1005","b":"k:430","type":"parallel_domain","note":"430 says those without understanding have nothing, whatever they have (என்னுடையரேனும் இலர்); 1005 says the same of those who neither give nor enjoy, even with crores. The same form of argument, that owning is not having unless some further quality is present, is used for understanding in one place and for use and giving in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1006","b":"k:1000","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1000 says great wealth got by a man without courtesy is like good milk turned by the fault of its vessel (கலந்தீமையால் திரிந்தற்று); 1006 says the man who neither uses nor gives is ஏதம் to பெருஞ்செல்வம். In both the wealth is good in itself and the holder is what spoils it, which is how Parimelazhagar reads 1006 (others take the wealth to be a misery to him).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1007","b":"k:226","type":"develops","note":"226 says ending the ruinous hunger of the destitute (அற்றார் அழிபசி தீர்த்தல்) is the place where one who has got wealth stores it. 1007 says the wealth of one who does nothing for the destitute (அற்றார்க்கொன்று ஆற்றாதான்) is like a very beautiful woman growing old alone. 226 says where wealth is kept safe; 1007 shows what happens to wealth kept anywhere else: it passes its time unused.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1007","b":"k:1039","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1039 says land whose owner stays away sulks like a wife (இல்லாளின் ஊடிவிடும்); 1007 likens ungiven wealth to a beautiful woman who ages alone (தமியள் மூத்தற்று). Both picture a good left unattended as a woman without her partner, for farmland in one verse and for money in the other; Parimelazhagar notes for 1007 that the wealth itself loses its fruit.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1009","b":"k:760","type":"qualifies","note":"760 says that for those who have made bright wealth in plenty (ஒண்பொருள் காழ்ப்ப இயற்றியார்க்கு) the other two, virtue and pleasure, come easily. 1009 uses the same word, ஒண்பொருள், for wealth amassed by dropping love, denying oneself and not looking to virtue, and says others will take it. So the promise of 760 fails when the two ends it names are given up in the earning; Parimelazhagar reads 760 as wealth that comes by the right way.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1010","b":"k:219","type":"agrees","note":"On Parimelazhagar's reading, 1010 says the brief want (சிறுதுனி) of the rich who have renown is like the rain cloud running dry (மாரி வறங்கூர்ந்தனையது). 219 says the poverty of a man of நயன் is just his not being able to do what he is used to doing. Both treat the giver's poverty as a loss to others, not to himself, and 1010 adds by its image that it passes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1011","b":"k:201","type":"parallel_domain","note":"201 says the excellent fear the intoxication called evil deed (விழுமியார் அஞ்சுவர்); 1011 defines shame proper as shame on account of deeds (கருமத்தால் நாணுதல்). Fear and shame are given the same object, one's own wrong action, in two chapters, and Parimelazhagar glosses நாணுதல் elsewhere (433, 172) as அஞ்சுதல்.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1011","b":"k:1251","type":"develops","note":"1011 mentions the other shame, that of women, without describing it. In 1251 a woman in the Book of Love describes it from inside: shame is the bolt (நாணுத்தாழ்) on the door of her restraint, which the axe of desire breaks. This is a speech in character, but it shows what the shame 1011 calls பிற is: a guard on conduct in love, not a recoil from a wrong deed.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1012","b":"k:993","type":"agrees","note":"993 says likeness of limbs is not likeness among people; the likeness that counts is of character (பண்பொத்தல்). 1012 says the bodily needs are shared by all and shame is what sets people apart. Both refuse to take what is physically common as the measure of a human being.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1013","b":"k:80","type":"parallel_domain","note":"80 says the seat of life goes by way of love (அன்பின் வழியது உயிர்நிலை) and without it a body is bones wrapped in skin; 1013 says worth depends on shame as life depends on flesh. Both use the life-and-body relation to say a quality is what a thing lives by, love for the person in 80 and shame for சால்பு in 1013. Parimelazhagar notes a variant ஊணை ('food') in 1013, which Manakkudavar follows; the link rests on the reading ஊனை.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1014","b":"k:118","type":"agrees","note":"118 (with 115) calls not leaning to one side the ornament of the worthy (கோடாமை சான்றோர்க்கு அணி); 1014 calls shame the ornament of the worthy (அணியன்றோ நாணுடைமை சான்றோர்க்கு). The same phrase names two inner restraints, and neither is an outward decoration.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1015","b":"k:980","type":"develops","note":"980 says greatness covers others' exposure (அற்றம் மறைக்கும்) and smallness only speaks their faults. 1015 gives the feeling behind that conduct: being ashamed of another's disgrace as of one's own. One who feels the other's பழி in this way has no reason to tell it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1015","b":"k:1018","type":"develops","note":"1015 praises one who feels shame even for others' disgrace; 1018 describes one who does not feel shame at what makes others ashamed (பிறர் நாணத்தக்கது தான் நாணான்). The two set out the ends of a scale inside the chapter: shame that extends beyond oneself, and shame that fails even for one's own act.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1016","b":"k:57","type":"parallel_domain","note":"57 says confinement does little to guard women and the guard kept by their own firmness (நிறைகாக்கும் காப்பு) is best; 1016 says the high have shame for their fence (நாண்வேலி). In both the effective guard is a quality inside the person and not an outer barrier, for a wife's fidelity in one and for the conduct of the high in the other.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1016","b":"k:311","type":"agrees","note":"311 says the spotless hold to not hurting others even if it would bring wealth that gives distinction. 1016, on Parimelazhagar's reading, says the high take shame as their fence and do not want the wide world (வியன்ஞாலம் பேணலர்) where the two conflict. Both state a refusal of great gain at the price of a restraint; the other reading he reports, that they will not want the world without first having the fence of shame, gives the same result.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1016","b":"k:421","type":"same_image","note":"421 calls understanding an inner fortress enemies cannot destroy (உள்ளழிக்கலாகா அரண்); 1016 calls shame a fence (வேலி), which Parimelazhagar explains as keeping blame and sin from entering. Both give an inner quality the work of a defensive structure, one against ruin and one against disgrace.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:1018","b":"k:130","type":"develops","note":"130 says virtue watches for the right moment and comes by the path to the one who guards against anger and has learned restraint (அறம் பார்க்கும் ஆற்றின் நுழைந்து). 1018, as the commentators read it, has virtue ashamed to come near the shameless. Together they give the two directions of the same figure: virtue seeks out the restrained and shrinks from those without shame.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1018","b":"k:204","type":"parallel_domain","note":"204 says that if one plots another's ruin, அறம் plots the plotter's ruin; 1018 says that if one is not ashamed, அறம் is ashamed. In both, virtue is an agent that answers the person's act in the terms of that act, with a penalty in 204 and with withdrawal in 1018.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1019","b":"k:952","type":"develops","note":"952 says the well-born do not slip in three things: conduct, truthfulness and shame (ஒழுக்கமும் வாய்மையும் நாணும்). 1019 says what happens when two of those three fail: a lapse in conduct burns the family, and shamelessness that stays burns every good. The chapter on good family gives the mark and 1019 gives the cost of losing it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1021","b":"k:611","type":"agrees","note":"1021 says no greatness has more dignity (பீடு) than that of the one who says 'I will not stay my hand' (கைதூவேன்) from the work. 611 says not to slacken at a task's difficulty because effort itself gives greatness (பெருமை முயற்சி தரும்). Both locate பெருமை in persistence rather than in rank or result; 1021 applies it to the work of raising one's family.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1021","b":"k:612","type":"develops","note":"1021 praises the resolve never to stop work once begun (கைதூவேன்). 612 gives the other side: guard against the work dying in mid-course (வினைக்கண் வினைகெடல் ஓம்பல்), since, in Parimelazhagar's reading, the world gives up on those who give up their unfinished work. The pair shows the same refusal to stop as honour in one verse and as a condition of the world's regard in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1022","b":"k:604","type":"agrees","note":"1022 says the family (குடி) lengthens through long-continued work that has both effort (ஆள்வினை) and mature knowledge. 604 states the reverse case: for those sunk in sloth, without worthy exertion (மாண்ட உஞற்றிலவர்), the family dies down and faults multiply (குடிமடிந்து குற்றம் பெருகும்). Together they make one claim from both sides: the fortunes of a குடி follow the exertion of its members.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1022","b":"k:618","type":"agrees","note":"1022 pairs effort with full knowledge (ஆள்வினையும் ஆன்ற அறிவும்) as the two things that extend a family. 618 uses the same pairing when it assigns blame: lacking fortune is no disgrace, but lacking effort after knowing what should be known (அறிவறிந்து ஆள்வினை இன்மை) is. In both verses knowledge and effort are named together as what a person can be held to, as against fortune.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1023","b":"k:617","type":"agrees","note":"1023 has தெய்வம் tying up its garment (மடிதற்று) to go before the family-builder. 617 says the dark goddess lives in sloth (மடி) and the lotus goddess in the effort of the one without sloth (மடியிலான் தாள்). Both put divine favour on the side of the one who works; 1023's மடி (cloth) also echoes the மடி (sloth) that 617 and the chapter on sloth condemn.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1026","b":"k:773","type":"parallel_domain","note":"773 says fierceness is called great manliness (பேராண்மை), and that helping a foe in distress (ஊராண்மை) is its edge. 1026 says good manliness (நல்லாண்மை) is making oneself the one who manages the house one was born in (இல்லாண்மை). Each verse takes ஆண்மை and coins a second compound that turns it toward care for others; Parimelazhagar notes that 'நல்' in 1026 is there to set it apart from the manliness of war.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1027","b":"k:770","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1027 says that as in battle the load falls on the stout-hearted (வன்கண்ணர்), so among kin the burden (பொறை) falls on those able to carry it. 770 says an army has no existence without leaders, however many steady men it has (தலைமக்கள் இல்வழி இல்). 770 is the military fact that 1027 uses as its simile: numbers do not carry a force or a family, a few capable people do.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1029","b":"k:615","type":"develops","note":"615 describes one who does not want pleasure but wants the work (இன்பம் விழையான் வினைவிழைவான்) as the pillar who wipes away his kin's sorrows. 1029 looks at that same person from the inside: his body seems to hold nothing but suffering. 1029 states the cost of the role that 615 praises; Parimelazhagar takes the question as noting that pleasure never reaches him.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1032","b":"k:42","type":"parallel_domain","note":"42 says the householder is the support (துணை) of renouncers, the destitute and the dead. 1032 says the ploughmen bear (பொறுத்து) everyone who does not plough. Both verses identify one group whose ordinary work holds up all the others: the householder in the Book of Virtue, the farmer in the Book of Wealth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1033","b":"k:967","type":"develops","note":"1033 describes everyone but the farmer as those who bow, eat and walk behind others (தொழுதுண்டு பின்செல்பவர்). 967 says it is better to be called ruined than to live by going behind those who do not accept one (ஒட்டார் பின்சென்று வாழ்தல்). Both treat 'going behind' another for a living as a lesser life; 967 judges it by honour, and 1033 names farming as the livelihood that avoids it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1034","b":"k:389","type":"same_image","note":"389 says the world rests under the umbrella (கவிகை) of a king who can bear bitter words. 1034 says those who have the shade of grain will see the shade of many umbrellas come under their own umbrella (தங்குடைக்கீழ்), which Parimelazhagar takes as their own king's. Both use the royal umbrella's shade for dominion, but 389 credits the king's character and 1034 credits the farmers' harvest.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1034","b":"k:545","type":"develops","note":"545 says rain and harvest gather in the land of the king who rules justly (பெயலும் விளையுளும் தொக்கு). 1034 says those who hold the grain bring many kings' umbrellas under their own. The dependence runs in both directions: in 545 good rule yields harvest, and in 1034 harvest yields the king's wider rule.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1035","b":"k:1061","type":"develops","note":"1061 says that not begging (இரவாமை), even from those who give gladly without concealing (கரவாது), is worth ten million times more. 1035 says those whose way is to eat what their own hands produce do not beg (இரவார்) and give to askers without concealing (கரவாது ஈவர்). 1035 names the livelihood that makes the ideal of 1061 possible, and puts the farmer on the giving side of the same exchange.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1037","b":"k:1038","type":"qualifies","note":"1037 says that if ploughed soil is dried until a measure of dust (தொடிப்புழுதி) falls to a quarter (கஃசு), the crop thrives without even a handful of manure. 1038 says manuring is better than ploughing (ஏரினும் நன்றால் எருவிடுதல்). The two set tillage and manure against each other from opposite sides: thorough working of the soil can replace manure, yet as a rule manure counts for more than ploughing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1038","b":"k:1147","type":"same_image","note":"1038 treats manure (எரு) and water (நீர்) as what makes a crop grow. In 1147 the woman says her love-sickness grows with the town's gossip as manure and her mother's words as water (கெளவை எருவாக அன்னைசொல் நீராக). The Book of Love takes the two inputs that 1038 gives as farming advice and uses them as a figure for what feeds her passion.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1039","b":"k:553","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1039 says that if the owner (கிழவன்) stays away and does not go to it, the land turns against him and sulks like a wife. 553 says the king who does not look into things and do justice every day loses his land day by day (நாடொறும் நாடு கெடும்). In both, land is lost through the owner's or ruler's lack of personal attention; Parimelazhagar reads 1039 too as requiring a daily visit.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1039","b":"k:1040","type":"same_image","note":"1039 compares the land to a wife (இல்லாள்) who sulks when her owner stays away. 1040 calls the land a good woman (நிலமென்னும் நல்லாள்) who laughs at those who sit idle pleading poverty. The chapter closes with two verses that make the land a woman who responds to how a man treats her, by sulking in one and by laughing in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1039","b":"k:1304","type":"same_image","note":"1039 describes neglected land with the Book of Love's words for a lovers' sulk: it resents and sulks like a wife (புலந்து ... ஊடி விடும்). 1304, in the chapter on sulking, says that not appeasing one who has sulked (ஊடியவரை உணராமை) is like cutting a withered creeper at the root. The figures run in opposite directions: the land is compared to a sulking woman in 1039, and the neglected sulking woman to a plant in 1304.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1040","b":"k:616","type":"agrees","note":"616 says effort creates prosperity and lack of effort brings in want (முயற்றின்மை இன்மை புகுத்திவிடும்). 1040 pictures the Land laughing at people who claim poverty while sitting idle (அசைஇ இருப்பார்). Both hold that such poverty comes from not working; 1040 states it as mockery from the land that would have paid them.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1043","b":"k:171","type":"develops","note":"171 says that coveting another's goods without fairness ruins the family (வெஃகின் குடிபொன்றி). 1043 names poverty itself a craving (நல்குரவு என்னும் நசை) and gives it the same effect on ancient descent. Read together, 1043 treats poverty as harmful to a lineage through the desire it breeds, the mechanism 171 states for covetousness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1045","b":"k:1056","type":"develops","note":"1045 says many sufferings arrive within the one affliction called poverty (நல்குரவு என்னும் இடும்பையுள் பல்குரைத் துன்பங்கள்). 1056 says that on seeing those free of the affliction of concealing, all the afflictions of poverty perish together (நிரப்பிடும்பை எல்லாம் ஒருங்கு கெடும்). The next chapter answers this verse in its own terms: the sufferings that come as a group also leave as a group.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1047","b":"k:117","type":"qualifies","note":"1047 says the person in poverty unconnected with virtue (அறஞ்சாரா நல்குரவு) is looked on as a stranger even by his own mother. 117 says the world does not count as ruin the low state of one who stays in virtue (நன்றிக்கண் தங்கியான் தாழ்வு). The qualifier அறஞ்சாரா in 1047 marks the boundary 117 draws: estrangement follows poverty without virtue, while poverty with virtue is not held against a person.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1048","b":"k:532","type":"same_image","note":"1048 is a poor man's cry: will the want that as good as killed me yesterday come today too (நெருநலும் கொன்றது போலும் நிரப்பு)? 532 uses the same noun and verb in a simile, constant poverty killing understanding (நிச்ச நிரப்புக் கொன்றாங்கு). Both treat நிரப்பு as something that kills and that returns daily; Parimelazhagar glosses both with a man who fills his stomach each day only with great pain.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1049","b":"k:896","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1049 says one can even sleep in the midst of fire, but in want there is no closing the eyes (நெருப்பினுள் துஞ்சலும் ஆகும்). 896 says one burned by fire may yet survive, but those who go on offending the great will not (எரியால் சுடப்படினும் உய்வுண்டாம்). Both measure a non-physical danger by granting that fire can be endured and denying the same of the thing compared.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1049","b":"k:1136","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1049 says that in poverty the eyes cannot close by any means (யாதொன்றும் கண்பாடு அரிது). In 1136 the man says his eyes will not close because of the girl, even at midnight (படல் ஒல்லா ... என் கண்). The same sign, eyes that cannot close, marks want in the Book of Wealth and longing in the Book of Love; 1136 is a lover's speech, so this is a shared figure and not a shared teaching.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1051","b":"k:1067","type":"agrees","note":"1051 says ask when you find those fit to be asked (இரத்தக்கார்க் காணின்), and if they withhold, the blame is theirs. 1067, in the chapter on the dread of begging, says: if you must beg, do not beg from those who conceal (கரப்பார் இரவன்மின்). The two chapters take opposite attitudes to begging but give the same rule about whom to ask.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1052","b":"k:224","type":"parallel_domain","note":"224 speaks from the giver's side: being asked is painful (இன்னாது இரக்கப்படுதல்) until one sees the asker's glad face. 1052 speaks from the asker's side: asking is a pleasure if what is asked comes without suffering (துன்பம் உறாஅ வரின்). Each verse makes the pain or pleasure of one party depend on how the exchange goes for the other.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1052","b":"k:1066","type":"tension","note":"1052 says even asking is pleasure to a person (இன்பம் ஒருவற்கு இரத்தல்) when the thing comes without pain. 1066 says nothing is more degrading to the tongue than asking, even asking water for a cow (நாவிற்கு இரவின் இளிவந்தது இல்). They conflict if 1052 is read as commending asking under good conditions; 1052 speaks of pleasure and 1066 of disgrace, so the conflict depends on taking a disgraceful act as unable to be a pleasant one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1053","b":"k:218","type":"develops","note":"218 says those whose sight knows what is due (கடனறி காட்சியவர்) do not falter in ஒப்புரவு even when they lack means. 1053 uses the same description, கடனறிவார், for the people before whom asking has a beauty; Manakkudavar glosses them as those who know ஒப்புரவு. The pairing identifies the fit giver of the begging chapter with the person 218 describes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1053","b":"k:708","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1053 says there is a beauty even in asking while standing before those of unconcealing heart who know what is due (கடனறிவார் முன்நின்று இரப்பும்). 708 says that if one has people who look within and understand, it is enough to stand looking at their face (முகம்நோக்கி நிற்க அமையும்). Parimelazhagar reads 1053 the same way: standing before such people is enough and nothing need be said, which is why the asking keeps some grace.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1054","b":"k:1061","type":"qualifies","note":"1054 says asking from those who do not know concealing even in a dream is like giving (இரத்தலும் ஈதலே போலும்). 1061 takes the same givers, who give gladly without concealing and are dear as one's eyes (கரவாது உவந்தீயும் கண்ணன்னார்), and says not asking even of them is worth ten million times more (இரவாமை கோடி உறும்). 1061 limits the praise in 1054: the best case of asking still ranks below not asking.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1055","b":"k:571","type":"parallel_domain","note":"571 says the world exists because the beauty called kindly regard exists (கண்ணோட்டம் ... உண்மையான் உண்டிவ் வுலகு). 1055 uses the same word and construction: asking goes on because the unconcealing exist (உண்மையால்). In both, a practice or the world itself is said to rest on the existence of a disposition in some people.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1055","b":"k:1059","type":"develops","note":"1055 says askers take up standing before others because people who do not conceal exist in the world (கரப்பிலார் வையகத்து உண்மையால்). 1059 says givers would have no renown if there were none who wished to ask and receive (இரந்துகோள் மேவார் இலாஅக் கடை). The two verses state the dependence in both directions: asking exists because of givers, and givers' standing exists because of askers.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1056","b":"k:1063","type":"tension","note":"1056 says that on seeing those free of the affliction of concealing, all the affliction of poverty perishes at once (நிரப்பிடும்பை எல்லாம் ஒருங்கு கெடும்). 1063 says nothing is harder than the hardness of thinking 'we will end the affliction of want by begging' (இன்மை இடும்பை இரந்து தீர்வாம்). They conflict on whether asking can end poverty's distress, if 1056 is read as a general claim; Parimelazhagar removes the conflict by reading 1063 as blaming the choice of begging over effort.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1056","b":"k:1068","type":"qualifies","note":"1056 gives the good outcome of asking: meeting those without concealment ends the afflictions of want. 1068 gives the other outcome: the unsafe boat called asking splits when it strikes the rock called concealment (கரவென்னும் பார்தாக்க). Both make the result of asking turn on கரவு in the person asked, so 1068 states the risk that 1056 leaves out.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1057","b":"k:228","type":"parallel_domain","note":"228 speaks of the giver's joy, the pleasure of giving and being glad (ஈத்துவக்கும் இன்பம்), which hoarders do not know. 1057 speaks of the asker's joy, the heart glad within (உள்ளுள் உவப்பது), when the giving is free of scorn. The same verb உவ marks gladness on both sides of one act of giving.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1058","b":"k:1062","type":"tension","note":"1058 says that without askers the world would be puppets coming and going, which makes askers part of a living world; Manakkudavar draws the conclusion that asking is therefore not to be held low. 1062 says that if one must live even by begging, let the maker of the world wander and perish (பரந்து கெடுக உலகியற்றியான்). They conflict on whether a world that contains begging is as it should be; the conflict depends on reading 1058 as approval and not only as a remark about what givers need.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1060","b":"k:250","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1060 tells the asker not to be angry at a refusal, since the affliction of his own want is witness enough (நிரப்பிடும்பை தானேயும் சாலும் கரி); Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar take it as witness that means are hard to come by for anyone. 250 says that when going against someone weaker, think of yourself before those stronger (வலியார்முன் தன்னை நினைக்க). Both restrain a person by turning him to his own experience as evidence of the other's position.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1063","b":"k:616","type":"develops","note":"616 says effort makes fortune and lack of effort brings want in (முயற்றின்மை இன்மை புகுத்தி விடும்); Parimelazhagar applies it to kings. 1063 calls it hardness (வன்மை) to think poverty's affliction can be ended by begging, and Parimelazhagar explains that the proper path, effort, is being set aside. 1063 applies 616's rule to the poor man and names begging as the wrong substitute for effort.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1063","b":"k:1068","type":"develops","note":"1063 calls hard the thought that poverty can be ended by begging (இரந்து தீர்வாம்). 1068 gives a picture of why: begging is a boat without safety (ஏமாப்பில் தோணி) that splits when it strikes the rock of concealment (கரவென்னும் பார்). Manakkudavar says 1068 states the cause of poverty not ending, which makes it the reason behind 1063's claim.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1064","b":"k:218","type":"parallel_domain","note":"218 says those who see their duty do not slacken in ஒப்புரவு even in a season without means (இடனில் பருவத்தும்). 1064 says worth does not consent to asking even at a time without means (இடமில்லாக் காலும்). The same test, what a person does when resources are gone, is applied to giving in 218 and to asking in 1064: the good neither stop giving nor start begging.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1066","b":"k:222","type":"agrees","note":"222 says taking is bad even if it is called a good path (நல்லாறு எனினும் கொளல் தீது). 1066 says that even if one asks for water for a cow (ஆவிற்கு நீரென்று இரப்பினும்), nothing is more degrading to the tongue than asking; Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar both take the cow as a case of asking for the sake of virtue. Both verses deny that a good purpose removes what is wrong with asking or receiving.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1066","b":"k:656","type":"parallel_domain","note":"656 says that even if one must see one's own mother hungry (ஈன்றாள் பசி காண்பான் ஆயினும்), one should not do what the worthy condemn. 1066 says that even asking water for a cow is degrading to the tongue (நாவிற்கு இளிவந்தது). Each picks the most sympathetic motive available, a hungry mother or a thirsty cow, and holds that it does not excuse the act.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1066","b":"k:1053","type":"tension","note":"1053 says that even asking, standing before those of unconcealing heart who know their duty, has a certain beauty (இரப்பும் ஓர் ஏஎர் உடைத்து). 1066 says nothing is more degrading to the tongue than asking (இரவின் இளிவந்தது இல்), even for a cow's water. They conflict if 1066 is read as covering every act of asking; on a narrower reading 1053 speaks only of asking from the right giver, which 1066 does not mention.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1067","b":"k:1077","type":"develops","note":"1067 asks beggars not to beg of those who conceal (கரப்பார்). 1077 describes such people from the other side: the base will not even shake a wet hand (ஈர்ங்கை விதிரார்) for anyone but those whose fist would break their jaw. 1077 shows what asking a concealer comes to, and why 1067 pleads against it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1068","b":"k:306","type":"same_image","note":"306 says anger burns even the safe raft called one's own people (இனமென்னும் ஏமப் புணை). 1068 calls asking a boat without safety (ஏமாப்பில் தோணி) that breaks on concealment. The same word for safety (ஏமம், ஏமாப்பு) marks the vessel in both: one's people are the safe one in 306, and asking is named the unsafe one in 1068.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1068","b":"k:1164","type":"same_image","note":"1068 calls asking a boat without safety (ஏமாப்பில் தோணி) that splits on the rock of concealment; the commentators supply poverty as the sea it was meant to cross. In 1164 the woman says the sea of love exists but the safe raft to swim it (ஏமப் புணை) does not. Both use the figure of a sea that must be crossed and a vessel that fails, 1068 as a teaching about begging and 1164 as a lover's complaint.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1069","b":"k:224","type":"agrees","note":"224 says being asked is unpleasant (இன்னாது இரக்கப் படுதல்) until one sees the asker's glad face. 1069 says the heart melts at the thought of asking. Taken together they say asking is painful on both sides, for the one asked in 224 and for the one who asks or contemplates it in 1069, and that only the gift ends the pain.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:1069","b":"k:1057","type":"develops","note":"1057 says that on seeing those who give without scorn, the heart rejoices deep within (உள்ளம் உள்ளுள் உவப்பது உடைத்து). 1069 says that at the thought of asking the heart melts (உள்ளம் உருகும்), and at the thought of concealment even what remains is gone (உள்ளதூஉம் இன்றிக் கெடும்). Both follow the heart (உள்ளம்) through the act of asking; 1057 gives its state before a good giver and 1069 its state before a refusal. Parimelazhagar takes the heart in 1069 as the speaker's, while he reports others take it as the asker's.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1070","b":"k:223","type":"agrees","note":"223 says not uttering the misery of 'I have nothing' (இலனென்னும் எவ்வம் உரையாமை), and giving, are found in one of good family; Pope takes the words as the giver's excuse, while Parimelazhagar first takes them as the asker's plea. 1070 says the asker's life leaves when the word is spoken (சொல்லாட); Parimelazhagar takes this as the concealer saying 'no'. On Pope's reading of 223 both verses are about the word of refusal, which the well-born never say and which 1070 says kills the hearer.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1070","b":"k:230","type":"develops","note":"230 says nothing is more bitter than dying, yet even dying is sweet when giving is not possible (ஈதல் இயையாக் கடை). 1070 says the asker's life goes at the word of refusal (சொல்லாடப் போஒம் உயிர்) and asks where the concealer's life hides (யாங்கொளிக்கும் கொல்லோ). 230 gives the giver's view that life without giving is not worth keeping; 1070 puts that standard to the one who can give and refuses, and wonders that his life stays.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1071","b":"k:600","type":"parallel_domain","note":"600 says those without inner drive are trees, and their being human in form is the only difference (மரம் மக்களாதலே வேறு). 1071 says the base resemble people exactly (மக்களே போல்வர்). Both treat the human shape as something that can be present without what makes a person, lacking drive in 600 and lacking character in 1071.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1072","b":"k:201","type":"develops","note":"201 says those given to evil deeds do not fear, and the excellent do fear, the intoxication called evil deed (தீவினையார் அஞ்சார் விழுமியார் அஞ்சுவர்). 1072 says the base are more fortunate than those who know what is good, because they have no distress in the heart (நெஞ்சத்து அவலம் இலர்). 1072 puts 201's fact in mock praise: Parimelazhagar says the verse scorns by implication, and that the point is their not fearing blame.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1072","b":"k:839","type":"parallel_domain","note":"839 says the friendship of fools is very sweet (பெரிதினிது), because parting from it gives no pain. 1072 says the base are fortunate (திருவுடையர்), because their hearts have no distress. Both verses praise a bad thing for the absence of a pain that only something of value would cause.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1073","b":"k:1072","type":"agrees","note":"1072 calls the base fortunate (திருவுடையர்) for having no distress of heart, and 1073 calls them like the gods (தேவர் அனையர்) for doing as they please. Both give praise that is meant as blame; Parimelazhagar says so of each. The two together describe one condition, no inner check in 1072 and no outer one in 1073.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1073","b":"k:1075","type":"qualifies","note":"1073 says the base are like the gods because they too act as they please (மேவன செய்தொழுகலான்). 1075 says that where the base show proper conduct (ஆசாரம்) it is fear, and beyond that a little if there is something they desire. 1075 marks the limit on 1073's freedom: their doing as they please stops only at fear or gain; Parimelazhagar says doing what they like is their nature and conduct is not.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1074","b":"k:978","type":"agrees","note":"978 says greatness always bows and smallness adorns itself in admiration of itself (சிறுமை அணியுமாம் தன்னை வியந்து). 1074 says the base man, on seeing those worse than himself, outdoes them and swaggers (மிகப்பட்டுச் செம்மாக்கும்); this follows Parimelazhagar's reading of அகப்பட்டி as those of looser conduct, while Manakkudavar reads the word quite differently. On Parimelazhagar's reading 1074 shows where the self-admiration of 978 finds its material: in comparison with someone lower.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1075","b":"k:1016","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1016 says the high do not care for the wide world unless they have shame as their fence (நாண்வேலி). 1075 says the conduct of the base is fear (அச்சமே கீழ்களது ஆசாரம்), which Parimelazhagar takes as fear of harm from the king. Each verse names what keeps a kind of person within bounds: shame from inside for the high, fear from outside for the base.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1075","b":"k:1078","type":"develops","note":"1075 says the base behave properly out of fear. 1078 says the base, like sugar-cane, are of use only when crushed (கொல்லப் பயன்படும் கீழ்). 1078 applies the rule of 1075 to giving: what fear does for their conduct, force does for their generosity.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1077","b":"k:221","type":"develops","note":"221 says giving to those who have nothing is what giving is (வறியார்க்கொன்று ஈவதே ஈகை), and all other giving has the nature of a loan to be returned. 1077 says the base will not even shake a wet hand (ஈர்ங்கை விதிரார்) except for those whose bent fist would break their jaw; Parimelazhagar sums up that they give nothing to the weak and everything to those who hurt them. The base man's giving in 1077 is the reverse of 221's definition: it goes only to the strong.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1077","b":"k:1078","type":"develops","note":"1077 says the base give only to those who would break their jaw. 1078 states the same with a contrast and a figure: the worthy are of use at a word (சொல்லப் பயன்படுவர்), and the base, like sugar-cane, when crushed (கொல்லப் பயன்படும்). Parimelazhagar treats the two verses as a pair on how the base give.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1078","b":"k:216","type":"parallel_domain","note":"216 says wealth with a person of நயன் is like a fruit-bearing tree ripening in the middle of the town (பயன்மரம் உள்ளூர்ப் பழுத்தற்று). 1078 says the base are like sugar-cane (கரும்புபோல்), of use only when crushed. Both use a plant's yield (பயன்) as the figure for how a person's wealth reaches others: the tree offers its fruit in the open, and the cane gives nothing until it is pressed.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1078","b":"k:223","type":"develops","note":"1078 says the worthy are of use as soon as they are told (சொல்லப் பயன்படுவர் சான்றோர்). 223 says not uttering the misery of 'I have nothing', and giving, belong to one of good family; Parimelazhagar records a second reading on which the giver gives before the asker has to say it. On that reading 223 goes one step beyond 1078: the worthy of 1078 give at a word, and the well-born of 223 give before the word.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1081","b":"k:918","type":"same_image","note":"In 1081 the man cannot decide whether she is an அணங்கு, a choice peahen or a woman, and his heart reels (மாலும் என் நெஞ்சு). 918 says the embrace of deceiving women (மாய மகளிர் முயக்கு) is called an அணங்கு for those without discerning sense. The same word names a spirit that overpowers a man in both books: here it is a lover's wonder, there a warning, and Parimelazhagar glosses it in 918 as a divine woman who takes life through desire.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1081","b":"k:1082","type":"develops","note":"1081 only asks whether she is an அணங்கு. In 1082, once she returns his look (நோக்கெதிர் நோக்குதல்), he says it is like an attacking அணங்கு (தாக்கணங்கு) who has also brought an army. The doubt of the first verse becomes a felt assault in the second, and Parimelazhagar reads 1082 as spoken after he has settled that she is human.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1081","b":"k:1112","type":"develops","note":"In 1081 his heart reels (மாலும் நெஞ்சு) because it cannot place her among goddess, peahen and woman. In 1112 he scolds the same heart for being confused at flowers (மலர்காணின் மையாத்தி நெஞ்சே), as if her eyes were like what many can see. The confusion moves from what she is to what could be compared with her, and by 1112 he treats it as the heart's error.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1082","b":"k:775","type":"parallel_domain","note":"In 775 a warrior whose glaring eye (விழித்த கண்) blinks when a spear is thrown counts that as running away. In 1082 the man looks, and her look answering his (நோக்கெதிர் நோக்குதல்) is like an army brought against him. Both treat meeting another's gaze as combat, one on the battlefield and one at first sight in love.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1082","b":"k:1091","type":"develops","note":"1082 says her returning look is like an attacking spirit come with an army (தாக்கணங்கு தானைக்கொண் டன்னது): the look only wounds. 1091 finds two looks in her eyes (இருநோக்கு), one that gives the sickness and one that is its medicine (நோய்நோக்கு ... அந்நோய் மருந்து). The next chapter adds the healing side that 1082 leaves out.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1082","b":"k:1113","type":"same_image","note":"1082 makes her answering glance an armed host (தானை). 1113, listing her beauties, calls her kohl-lined eyes spears (வேலுண்கண்). The eye as weapon is a fixed figure of the man's praise, used first as his alarm and later as a plain item of description.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1083","b":"k:765","type":"same_image","note":"765 defines an army as the force that can stand together even if Death comes raging at it (கூற்றுடன்று மேல்வரினும்). In 1083 the man says he now knows what is called Death (கூற்று): it has womanly grace and large warring eyes (பேரமர்க் கட்டு). Both set கூற்று in a battle; the Book of Wealth imagines standing against it, and the lover says he has met it in her eyes.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1083","b":"k:1226","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1083: 'I did not know before (பண்டறியேன்) what Death is; now I know'. 1226: the woman did not know (அறிந்ததிலேன்), while her husband had not left, that evening brings sickness. In both a lover says love has taught what was only a word before, the man at first sight and the woman in separation.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1084","b":"k:279","type":"parallel_domain","note":"279: the arrow is straight but cruel, the lute bent but sweet, so judge by what a thing does (வினைபடு பாலால் கொளல்). 1084 sets a girl of womanly gentleness (பெண்டகைப் பேதை) against eyes that look as if they eat lives; Parimelazhagar takes அமர்த்தன as 'were at odds' with her nature. Both verses turn on a form that does not match its effect, one as a rule for judging ascetics and one as a lover's surprise.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1084","b":"k:326","type":"same_image","note":"326 says Death that eats life (உயிருண்ணுங் கூற்று) does not advance on the days of one who lives by not killing. 1084 says the eyes of this womanly, artless girl have a look that eats the life of those who see them (கண்டார் உயிருண்ணும் தோற்றம்). The lover gives her eyes the standing epithet of Death, which the previous verse (1083) has just named.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1084","b":"k:1221","type":"same_image","note":"1084: her eyes have a look that eats the life of those who see them (உயிருண்ணும்). 1221: the woman tells evening it is not evening but a time that eats the life of those once wed (மணந்தார் உயிருண்ணும் வேலை). The same phrase marks what kills each lover: for the man her eyes at first sight, for the woman the evening hour in his absence.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1085","b":"k:1089","type":"same_image","note":"In 1085 the doe (பிணை) is one of three things her look might be, beside Death. In 1089 her doe-like artless look (பிணையேர் மடநோக்கு) and her modesty are ornament enough, so added jewels are pointless. The same doe glance is first one part of a threat and then her natural adornment.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1085","b":"k:1091","type":"develops","note":"1085 asks whether her look is Death, an eye or a doe, and says it has all three (இம்மூன்றும் உடைத்து); Parimelazhagar comments that it gives pleasure and pain together. 1091 states that plainly as two looks, one the sickness and one its cure. The puzzled question of 1085 is resolved into a doubleness he can name.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1086","b":"k:1087","type":"develops","note":"1086 wishes her brows would screen her eyes (மறைப்பின்), for then the eyes would not cause him trembling pain. 1087 says the cloth over her breasts is like the face-cloth over a rutting elephant's eyes (கட்படாம்). Both are about a covering over something that harms him: one he wishes for and one he sees, and Parimelazhagar says of the second that the cloth keeps them from killing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1086","b":"k:1171","type":"parallel_domain","note":"In 1086 the man says her eyes cause him trembling pain (நடுங்கஞர்) and wishes them screened. In 1171 the woman asks why her own eyes weep, since they showed him to her and so gave her this unending sickness (தண்டாநோய் தாம்காட்ட). Each lover names eyes as the cause of the pain; for him it is hers, for her it is her own.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1087","b":"k:24","type":"same_image","note":"1087: the cloth over her firm breasts is the face-cloth on a rutting bull elephant (கடாஅக் களிற்றின்மேற் கட்படாம்). 24: the renouncer guards the five senses with the goad of firmness (உரனென்னும் தோட்டியான்), a goad being the elephant-driver's tool. Both draw on the dangerous elephant held in check; the ascetic chapter makes the senses the elephant, and the lover makes her covered breasts the elephant.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1088","b":"k:1134","type":"develops","note":"1088 reports the first loss: his battlefield strength breaks at the sight of her forehead. 1134 says the fierce flood of love carries off the raft of shame and good manliness (நாணொடு நல்லாண்மை என்னும் புணை). The later verse generalises what 1088 exclaims, with manliness now lost along with shame.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1088","b":"k:1258","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1088: his might (பீடு), feared even by enemies in battle, has broken (உடைந்ததே) before her bright forehead alone. 1258: the woman says the soft words of that deceiving thief are the weapon that breaks her womanly firmness (பெண்மை உடைக்கும் படை). Each lover uses the language of war and breaking for the loss of the quality they prized in themselves, his strength and her restraint.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1089","b":"k:95","type":"parallel_domain","note":"95: humility and sweet speech are a person's ornament and nothing else is (அணியல்ல மற்றுப் பிற). 1089: what use are foreign ornaments (ஏதில தந்து அணி) to one who has a doe's artless look and modesty? Both deny that added adornment counts beside an inborn quality, 95 as a rule of conduct and 1089 as a lover's praise.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1090","b":"k:1288","type":"qualifies","note":"1090 uses liquor as the lesser pleasure that love outdoes. 1288 uses it as an exact likeness: 'your chest is to us as liquor is to those who are drunk on it (களித்தார்க்குக் கள்ளற்றே), though it does shaming harm (இளித்தக்க இன்னா)'. The later verse grants what 1090 leaves unsaid, that the pull likened to drink goes on despite disgrace.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1102","b":"k:948","type":"qualifies","note":"948 tells the healer to examine the disease, then its root (நோய்முதல்), then the means that subdue it (அது தணிக்கும் வாய்), as three separate things. 1102 first grants the ordinary rule, that the medicine is other than the disease, and then says love is the exception, where cause and remedy are the same person. The lover's verse marks love's sickness as a case the method of 948 does not cover.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1103","b":"k:213","type":"parallel_domain","note":"213 says that in the world of the gods or here (புத்தேளுலகத்தும் ஈண்டும்) it is hard to find another good like ஒப்புரவு, doing one's part for others. 1103 asks whether the lotus-eyed one's world is as sweet as sleeping on the beloved's shoulders. Both measure an earthly thing against heaven and prefer it; in the Book of Virtue it is a duty, and here it is a lover's own claim about his pleasure.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1105","b":"k:265","type":"parallel_domain","note":"265: austerity is practised here because whatever is wanted is obtained as it is wanted (வேண்டிய வேண்டியாங்கு எய்தலால்). 1105: her shoulders are like each desired thing at the moment of desiring it (வேட்ட பொழுதின் அவையவை போலுமே). Both describe desire met exactly as it arises, promised to the ascetic through தவம் and claimed by the lover in her embrace.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1105","b":"k:1101","type":"develops","note":"1101 says the objects of all five senses are found in her alone. 1105 says her shoulders are like whatever one craves, at the time one craves it. The first claim is about range, that she holds every kind of pleasure; the second adds timing, that she answers each wish as it comes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1106","b":"k:64","type":"same_image","note":"64: gruel stirred by the small hands of one's own children is far sweeter than ambrosia (அமிழ்தினும் ஆற்ற இனிதே). 1106: her shoulders are made of ambrosia (அமிழ்தின் இயன்றன) because their touch revives his life. Parent and lover both reach for அமிழ்து to measure the nearness of someone of their own; the parent ranks the child's gruel above it and the lover equates her with it.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1106","b":"k:1124","type":"agrees","note":"1106: his life sprouts afresh whenever he touches her. 1124: she is to his life what living is when they are together, and what dying is when she leaves (வாழ்தல் உயிர்க்கன்னள் ... சாதல் அதற்கன்னள்). Both tie his உயிர் to her presence; 1124 states the other half, which 1106's 'each time' (உறுதோறு) implies.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1107","b":"k:229","type":"parallel_domain","note":"229: eating alone to fill one's own store (தாமே தமியர் உணல்) is more bitter than begging. 1107: eating one's own after sharing it, in one's own house, is the model of sweetness, and the embrace is like it. Read together they give both halves of one judgement on eating: bitter when hoarded, sweet when shared.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1108","b":"k:1187","type":"develops","note":"1108 praises the embrace with no gap. In 1187 the woman says she lay embracing, shifted a little (புடைபெயர்ந்தேன்), and at once pallor was on her thick enough to scoop up. The woman's verse gives a reason from her side why the unbroken embrace is the sweet one: the least separation brings the pallor of parting.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1108","b":"k:1196","type":"agrees","note":"1108 specifies 'two who desire' (வீழும் இருவர்க்கு இனிதே): the close embrace is sweet to both. 1196: love on one side only is painful, and like a carrying pole it is sweet on both ends (இருதலையானும் இனிது). Both make sweetness depend on the love being mutual, 1108 in fulfilment and 1196 from the woman who lacks it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1109","b":"k:1302","type":"qualifies","note":"1109 counts sulking (ஊடல்) among the fruits of love. 1302 says sulking is like salt in the right amount, and letting it run a little long is like a little too much salt (சிறிது மிக்கற்றால் நீள விடல்). The later verse sets a limit on the first item of 1109's list: it is a gain only in measure.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1109","b":"k:1326","type":"develops","note":"1109 names quarrel, making up and union together as rewards without ranking them. 1326 ranks them: as digesting is sweeter than eating, sulking is sweeter than union (புணர்தலின் ஊடல் இனிது). The later verse takes one item from 1109's list and gives it first place.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1110","b":"k:940","type":"parallel_domain","note":"940: as a gambler loves the game more with each loss (இழத்தொறூஉம்), life loves itself more with each suffering (உழத்தொறூஉம்). 1110: each closeness shows love to be more than he knew, as each learning shows ignorance. Both describe an attachment that grows through its own repetition; the gambling chapter gives it as a warning and the lover as a delight.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1110","b":"k:1145","type":"agrees","note":"1145: as drinking is wanted more each time one gets drunk (களித்தொறும் கள்ளுண்டல் வேட்டற்று), love is sweeter each time it is exposed. 1110: love is like finding one's ignorance each time one learns, each time he is close to her. Both say love grows by repetition and does not wear out; 1110 takes its likeness from knowledge and 1145 from drink, and they differ in what is repeated, union or exposure by gossip.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1111","b":"k:1289","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1111 says she is more delicate than the anicham (நின்னினும் மென்னீரள்); 1289 says love itself is more delicate than a flower (மலரினும் மெல்லிது காமம்) and few catch its right moment. The same comparative, softer than a flower, is applied first to the beloved's body and later to the experience of love.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1113","b":"k:906","type":"same_image","note":"1113 praises her as bamboo-shouldered (வேய்த்தோளவட்கு). 906, in the chapter on men ruled by their wives, says those who fear the wife's bamboo-like shoulders (அமையார்தோள் அஞ்சுபவர்) have no standing even if they live like the gods. The same stock epithet of beauty is admired in the Book of Love and named in the Book of Wealth as the thing an unmanned husband fears.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1113","b":"k:1084","type":"develops","note":"1113 lists her features as metaphors: body a tender shoot (முறி), teeth pearls, and kohl-lined eyes that are spears (வேலுண்கண்). 1084 had already noticed the mismatch this list contains: her eyes, with a look that eats the life of those who see them, are at war (அமர்த்தன) with her gentle womanly nature. Read together, the spear in the inventory of soft things is the same contradiction 1084 names outright.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1115","b":"k:475","type":"parallel_domain","note":"475 warns that even a cart loaded with peacock feathers breaks its axle if the load is piled too high (அச்சிறும்). 1115 says she has worn anicham flowers without removing the stalks (கால்களையாள்), so no good drums will sound for her waist; Parimelazhagar explains that the waist will break under the weight and get the funeral drum. Both make the lightest possible load the breaking one, as counsel on overreach in 475 and as playful hyperbole about her slenderness in 1115.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1115","b":"k:1111","type":"develops","note":"1111 says she is more delicate than the anicham. 1115 pushes the claim to a comic limit: the unremoved stalks of that same flower (அனிச்சப்பூக் கால்களையாள்) are too heavy for her waist. The flower that was the standard of softness becomes a burden.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1116","b":"k:1081","type":"parallel_domain","note":"In 1081 the man, at first sight, cannot tell whether she is a goddess, a peahen or a woman, and his heart reels (மாலும் என் நெஞ்சு). In 1116 the confusion is moved to the sky: the stars cannot tell the moon from her face (அறியா) and wander from their places (பதியின் கலங்கிய). The lover's own bewilderment about what she is becomes, in the praise chapter, a bewilderment he attributes to the stars.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1116","b":"k:1117","type":"qualifies","note":"1116 says the stars cannot distinguish the moon from her face. 1117 immediately denies that the two are alike: the moon fills out from loss and has a spot, and her face has none (மறுவுண்டோ). Parimelazhagar reads 1117 as mocking the stars for missing an obvious difference, so the second verse withdraws the equality the first seemed to grant.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1117","b":"k:782","type":"same_image","note":"782 uses the moon's phases for friendship: with the wise it fills like the crescent, with fools it shrinks like the moon after full. 1117 mentions the same cycle, a moon that shines only after filling its lost parts (அறுவாய் நிறைந்த), as a defect that her face does not share. The waxing that is a hopeful figure in 782 counts in 1117 as evidence of the moon's inconstancy.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1118","b":"k:1210","type":"same_image","note":"Both verses address the moon with வாழி மதி. In 1118 the man tells it that it could have his love if it could shine like her face; Parimelazhagar hears the blessing as mockery. In 1210 the woman, left behind, asks it not to set so that her eyes may find the man who has gone without leaving her heart. The moon is a rival to be dismissed in the time of union and a helper to be begged in separation.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1119","b":"k:1311","type":"parallel_domain","note":"In 1119 the man tells the moon that to resemble her face it must not appear for many to see (பலர்காணத் தோன்றல்). In 1311 the woman refuses the man's chest because all women feast on him in common with their eyes (கண்ணின் பொதுஉண்பர்). Each lover applies the same standard from the other side: what is shared with every onlooker is no longer one's own.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1120","b":"k:1111","type":"develops","note":"1111 opens the chapter by ranking her above the anicham in softness. 1120 closes it by making the anicham and swan's down hurt her feet like the nerunji fruit, a thorn (மாதர் அடிக்கு நெருஞ்சிப் பழம்). The flower moves from being less soft than she is to being painful to her. Parimelazhagar sets the verse in a refusal to take her on a desert elopement.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1121","b":"k:64","type":"parallel_domain","note":"64 says the gruel one's children have stirred with their small hands is far sweeter than amrita (அமிழ்தினும் ஆற்ற இனிது). 1121 says the moisture at her white teeth is like milk mixed with honey (பாலொடு தேன்கலந் தற்று). In both, affection makes something bodily and ordinary taste better than the finest food, for a parent in one verse and a lover in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1121","b":"k:1258","type":"same_image","note":"1121 calls her பணிமொழி, the woman of soft, low speech, in a verse about the sweetness of her kiss. In 1258 the woman uses the same word for his speech: the soft words (பணிமொழி) of that thief of many deceits are the weapon that breaks her womanly reserve. The quality he savours in her is the one she names in him as a weapon.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1123","b":"k:1126","type":"parallel_domain","note":"In 1123 the man orders the image in the pupil of his eye (கருமணியிற் பாவாய்) to leave, since otherwise there is no room for the woman he loves. In 1126 the woman says her lover never leaves her eyes (கண்ணுள்ளின் போகார்) and is too subtle to be hurt when she blinks. Both lovers place the other inside the eye; he has to make room for her, and she has him there already.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1124","b":"k:1122","type":"develops","note":"1122 says their bond is like that of body and life. 1124 draws the consequence: together she is to his life what living is (வாழ்தல்), and when she leaves she is what dying is (சாதல்). The comparison in 1122 becomes a statement of what separation will mean.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1124","b":"k:1151","type":"agrees","note":"In 1124 the man says her leaving is death to his life (சாதல் அதற்கன்னள் நீங்கும் இடத்து). In 1151 the reply to his announced journey is that news of his quick return should be told to those who will be alive then (வாழ்வார்க் குரை); Parimelazhagar gives the line to her friend, Manakkudavar to the woman. Each side says that separation is not survivable, he at a brief parting after a meeting and her side when he leaves on a long journey.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1126","b":"k:1129","type":"qualifies","note":"In 1126 she says her lover does not leave her eyes and is not hurt even if she blinks (இமைப்பின் பருவரார்), because he is so subtle. In 1129 she will not blink, knowing he would be hidden (இமைப்பின் கரப்பாக்கு அறிவல்). The same conceit is used both ways within one chapter: blinking is harmless in the first verse and a risk in the second, which shows these are playful defences of him and not one consistent picture.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1126","b":"k:1218","type":"agrees","note":"1126 says he never leaves her eyes though the town thinks him gone. 1218 says that when she sleeps he is on her shoulder and when she wakes he hurries into her heart (நெஞ்சத்தர் ஆவர் விரைந்து). Parimelazhagar frames both as her defence of him against the charge of absence: she answers by locating him somewhere inside herself at every moment.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1128","b":"k:1205","type":"qualifies","note":"In 1128 his presence in her heart is something she protects, to the point of avoiding hot food. In 1205 the same presence is a complaint: he keeps her out of his own heart and yet is not ashamed to come into hers without pause (எம்நெஞ்சத்து ஓவா வரல்). The later verse adds what 1128 leaves out, the question whether the dwelling is mutual.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1128","b":"k:1249","type":"agrees","note":"1128 says that since her lover is in her heart (நெஞ்சத்தார் காதலவராக) she fears eating anything hot, knowing it would burn him. 1249 uses almost the same opening, உள்ளத்தார் காதலவராக, to ask her heart whom it goes out looking for when he is within it. The playful premise of 1128, said to defend him, is used in 1249 to rebuke her own restless heart.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1129","b":"k:1130","type":"develops","note":"1129 and 1130 end with the same line, ஏதிலர் என்னும் இவ் வூர். 1129 gives the town's ground for blame, her sleeplessness, which she explains as refusing to blink. 1130 states her answer directly: he lives gladly in her heart always (உவந்துறைவர் உள்ளத்துள் என்றும்), while the town says he lives apart.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1129","b":"k:1220","type":"agrees","note":"In 1129 she does not blink for fear of losing sight of him, and for this the town calls him a stranger without love (ஏதிலர் என்னும் இவ் வூர்). In 1220 the people of the town say he has left her in waking life, and she asks whether they do not see him in dreams (கனவினால் காணார்கொல்). In both she sets the town's outward evidence against her inner experience of his presence, first in the eye and then in the dream.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1130","b":"k:1204","type":"qualifies","note":"In 1130 she is sure that he lives in her heart, and gladly (உவந்துறைவர்), against the town's talk. In 1204 she affirms the same thing, that he is always in her heart (எந்நெஞ்சத்து ஓஒ உளரே அவர்), and then asks whether she too has a place in his (யாமும் உளேங்கொல் அவர்நெஞ்சத்து). The confidence she shows the town in 1130 does not answer the doubt she admits in 1204: his presence in her heart says nothing about her place in his.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1131","b":"k:1137","type":"qualifies","note":"1131 calls the palmyra horse the only safeguard for those who suffer in love. 1137 praises womanhood that suffers a sea of desire (கடலன்ன காமம் உழந்தும்) and still does not mount it (மடலேறா). The same verb உழந்து marks the same suffering, so 1137 limits 1131: the remedy is one a woman forgoes, and forgoing it is counted as worth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1131","b":"k:1164","type":"qualifies","note":"1131 has the man say that for those worn down by love there is no strength that is a safeguard (ஏமம்) except the palmyra horse (மடலல்லது இல்லை வலி). In 1164 the woman says the sea of love exists but the safe raft to swim it does not (ஏமப் புணைமன்னும் இல்). The one ஏமம் he names is a man's recourse; her verse shows a sufferer for whom even that is missing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1132","b":"k:1247","type":"develops","note":"In 1247 the woman tells her heart to give up either desire or shame, since she cannot bear the two together (காமம் விடு ஒன்றோ நாண்விடு). 1132 is that choice made: the man keeps desire and puts shame aside (நாணினை நீக்கி) to ride the palmyra horse. Read together, 1247 states the dilemma and 1132 shows one way out of it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1133","b":"k:1026","type":"same_image","note":"1026 defines நல்லாண்மை as making oneself fit to manage the house one was born into (இல்லாண்மை ஆக்கிக் கொளல்). 1133 uses the same word for what the lover had before (நல்லாண்மை பண்டுடையேன்) and has now exchanged for the palmyra horse. Set beside 1026's definition, the lover's loss is of the capacity that makes a man of use to his family, not only of pride.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1133","b":"k:1088","type":"develops","note":"In 1088 the man, on first seeing her, says his might (பீடு), which enemies dreaded in battle, has broken before her bright forehead. In 1133 he says the shame and good manliness he once had are replaced by the palmyra horse. The later verse carries the first collapse through to its end: what broke at first sight is now given up publicly.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1133","b":"k:1254","type":"agrees","note":"In 1133 the man says he once had shame and good manliness (நாணொடு நல்லாண்மை பண்டுடையேன்) and today has the palmyra horse. In 1254 the woman says she used to say she had restraint (நிறையுடையேன் என்பேன்மன்), yet her desire passes concealment and comes out in public. Each speaker sets a former self-possession against its loss to love; his loss is நாண் and ஆண்மை, hers is நிறை.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1134","b":"k:306","type":"parallel_domain","note":"306 says anger burns the safe raft that is one's own people (இனமென்னும் ஏமப் புணையைச் சுடும்). 1134 says the flood of love sweeps off the raft that is shame and good manliness (என்னும் புணை). In both a passion destroys the thing figured as the raft that keeps a person afloat: anger in the chapter on not being angry, desire here.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1134","b":"k:1251","type":"agrees","note":"1134 has the flood of love carry away the raft called shame and good manliness (நாணொடு நல்லாண்மை என்னும் புணை). 1251 has the axe of desire break the door called restraint, bolted with the bar of shame (நிறையென்னும் நாணுத்தாழ் வீழ்த்த கதவு). Both make shame a built defence that love's force destroys, one in the man's voice and one in the woman's.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1135","b":"k:1183","type":"agrees","note":"In 1135 the man says the girl with small bangles gave him (தந்தாள்) the palmyra horse and the grief that evening brings. In 1183 the woman says he took her grace and shame and gave in return (கைம்மாறா ... தந்து) sickness and pallor. Each lover describes the affliction as a gift from the other, and in both verses shame is part of what is lost.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1136","b":"k:1252","type":"agrees","note":"In 1136 the man's eyes will not close (படல் ஒல்லா) for the girl, so even at midnight (யாமத்தும்) he thinks of riding the palmyra horse. In 1252 the woman says desire, having no pity, rules her heart at its work even at midnight (யாமத்தும் ஆளும் தொழில்). Both use midnight, when all else rests, as the proof that love allows no rest.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1138","b":"k:71","type":"parallel_domain","note":"71 asks whether there is a bolt that can shut love in (அன்பிற்கும் உண்டோ அடைக்குந்தாழ்), since the small tears of the loving make it known. 1138 says காமம் breaks through secrecy and appears in public. The Book of Virtue says of அன்பு what the Book of Love says of காமம்: neither can be kept hidden, and each publishes itself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1139","b":"k:1180","type":"develops","note":"In 1139 the woman's love, thinking 'not everyone knows' (அறிகிலார் எல்லாரும் என்றே), goes reeling through the street (மறுகின் மறுகும்). In 1180 she says the town easily learns the secret of those whose eyes are beaten drums (அறைபறை கண்ணார்). In both the secret is given away by the lover herself and not by the town: by her love going about in 1139, by her eyes in 1180.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1140","b":"k:877","type":"agrees","note":"877 advises not telling one's pain to those who do not know it (நோவற்க நொந்தது அறியார்க்கு). 1140 shows the reason from inside: those who have not suffered what the speaker suffered (யாம்பட்ட தாம்படா) laugh at her in plain sight. The statecraft maxim and the lover's complaint agree that pain gets no hearing from those who have not felt it.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1140","b":"k:1219","type":"agrees","note":"In 1140 the woman says the unknowing laugh where she can see, because they have not undergone what she has (யாம்பட்ட தாம்படா ஆறு). In 1219 she says those who blame him for not being gracious in waking life are those who have not seen a lover in dreams (காதலர்க் காணாதவர்). Both explain the town's judgement by its lack of the lover's experience.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1141","b":"k:1213","type":"agrees","note":"1141 says that because gossip has risen the precious life holds on (அலரெழ ஆருயிர் நிற்கும்). 1213 says the woman's life exists because she sees in dreams the one who gives nothing in waking life (காண்டலின் உண்டென் உயிர்). In each a lover's life is kept going by something that only stands in for the beloved: the town's talk in one, a dream in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1141","b":"k:1263","type":"agrees","note":"1141 says life stays because the gossip has risen. 1263 says the woman still exists by longing for his return (வரல்நசைஇ இன்னும் உளேன்). Both verses name the one thing that keeps life from leaving; in 1141 it comes from outside, from the town, and in 1263 from her own hope.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1142","b":"k:1150","type":"agrees","note":"In 1142 the man says the town, not knowing how hard the flower-eyed woman is to reach (அருமை அறியாது), has given him gossip (அலரெமக்கு ஈந்தது). In 1150 the speaker says the town is raising the very talk 'we want' (யாம்வேண்டும் கெளவை எடுக்கும் இவ்வூர்). Both treat the town's gossip as an unintended service; Parimelazhagar reads ஈந்தது as help given, and reads 1150 as the friend counting on it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1143","b":"k:1213","type":"parallel_domain","note":"In 1143 the man says the gossip the town knows is like having obtained what he has not obtained (பெறாஅது பெற்றன்ன நீர்த்து). In 1213 the woman sees in dreams the man who gives nothing in waking life, and lives by it. Both take a substitute for union as if it were union: public talk for him, a dream for her.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1144","b":"k:1147","type":"agrees","note":"In 1144 the man says his love thrives through gossip (கவ்வையால் கவ்விது காமம்) and without it would lose its nature and go slack. In 1147 the woman says the town's gossip is manure and her mother's words water to 'this sickness' (நீளும் இந்நோய்). Both say gossip feeds love; he calls what grows காமம் and welcomes it, she calls it நோய்.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1145","b":"k:1281","type":"qualifies","note":"1145 takes drinking as the model for love: each exposure makes love sweeter as each bout makes drink wanted. 1281 says being intoxicated at a thought and glad at a sight belong to love and not to liquor (கள்ளுக்கில் காமத்திற்கு உண்டு). So the likeness in 1145 has a limit the book itself states: liquor works only when drunk, love without contact.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1146","b":"k:957","type":"same_image","note":"In 1146 the woman saw him one day only, and the gossip has spread like the serpent seizing the moon (திங்களைப் பாம்புகொண்டற்று), which Parimelazhagar glosses as spreading everywhere. 957 says a fault in the well-born shows high, like the spot on the moon in the sky (மதிக்கண் மறுப்போல்). Both use the moon as the thing whose blemish everyone sees: a brief event or a small fault becomes public because of where it sits.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1147","b":"k:1227","type":"same_image","note":"1147 makes the sickness of love a crop, manured by gossip and watered by a mother's words, that grows long (நீளும் இந்நோய்). 1227 makes the same இந்நோய் a flower that buds in the morning and opens in the evening. In both the woman describes her lovesickness as a plant with its own growth, fed in 1147 and timed in 1227.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1148","b":"k:1144","type":"develops","note":"1144 says love thrives by gossip and would droop without it. 1148 puts the same point as a rebuttal of those who expect gossip to end the affair: it is ghee on fire. 1148 gives the image and the opposing view that 1144's plain statement lacks.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1148","b":"k:1260","type":"same_image","note":"1148 says that to think of putting out love with gossip is like thinking to put out fire with ghee (நெய்யால் எரி நுதுப்பேம்). 1260 asks whether those whose hearts are like fat put in fire (நிணம் தீயில் இட்டன்ன) can say they will hold out in a sulk. Both use fat meeting fire for love: in 1148 the fat feeds the fire, in 1260 the heart is the fat that melts.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1149","b":"k:1297","type":"agrees","note":"In 1149 the woman asks whether she can be ashamed at gossip (அலர்நாண ஒல்வதோ) now that he has left her. In 1297 she says she has forgotten even shame (நாணும் மறந்தேன்), caught in a heart that cannot forget him. In both her shame gives way, in 1149 because his leaving has made it pointless and in 1297 because her own heart overrides it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1150","b":"k:1143","type":"agrees","note":"1150 says the town is raising the gossip 'we want' (யாம்வேண்டும் கெளவை), and the lover will grant what is asked if he wishes. In 1143 the man asks whether the gossip the town knows is not his to have, since it is like gaining what he has not gained. Both sides welcome the talk: for him it stands in for union, and in 1150, on Parimelazhagar's reading, it is the ground for asking him to take her away.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1161","b":"k:1253","type":"agrees","note":"Both open with the same words, மறைப்பேன்மன் ('I would hide it'), and both say the attempt fails: in 1161 the pain rises like spring water under baling, in 1253 desire shows itself without her intending it, like a sneeze (குறிப்பின்றித் தும்மல்போல்). Read together, one figure says concealment makes it grow and the other that it escapes her will altogether.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1162","b":"k:1200","type":"develops","note":"1162: she cannot hide the sickness, and telling it to the one who caused it brings shame (உரைத்தலும் நாணுத் தரும்). 1200: she tells her heart that it means to tell its pain to one who is not close (உறாஅர்க்கு உறுநோய் உரைப்பாய்) and might as well fill up the sea. Parimelazhagar reads both as about sending him word; 1200 adds futility to the shame that 1162 gives as the obstacle.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1163","b":"k:1017","type":"develops","note":"1017: those ruled by shame give up life for shame and do not give up shame for life (நாணால் உயிரைத் துறப்பர்). 1163 shows that ranking from inside: she drops neither love nor shame, and it is her life, the pole (உயிர் காவா), that takes the strain; Parimelazhagar adds that the life will snap.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1164","b":"k:10","type":"parallel_domain","note":"10: the great sea of birth is swum by those who reach the Lord's feet and not by others (பிறவிப் பெருங்கடல் நீந்துவர்); Parimelazhagar supplies the feet as the raft. 1164: the sea of love exists and the raft to swim it does not (அது நீந்தும் ஏமப்புணை). The same structure of a sea crossed only with a support appears in the first chapter and in a separated woman's complaint.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1165","b":"k:805","type":"qualifies","note":"1165: she asks what those who bring grief in friendship would do as enemies; Parimelazhagar says this is aimed, in a sulk, at the friend who has not sent a message. 805 advises that when friends do something hurtful (நோதக்க நட்டார் செயின்) it should be understood as folly or as great intimacy. 805 offers the charitable reading that her question refuses.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:1166","b":"k:629","type":"parallel_domain","note":"629: one who does not crave the pleasure within pleasure does not come to pain within pain (இன்பத்துள் இன்பம் விழையாதான்). 1166: the pleasure of love is a sea and its pain when it afflicts is larger still (துன்பம் அதனிற் பெரிது). The woman's experience fits the link between relishing pleasure and suffering pain that 629 states as a rule in the Book of Wealth.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1166","b":"k:1137","type":"same_image","note":"1137 speaks of suffering a love like the sea (கடலன்ன காமம் உழந்தும்) and praises womanhood that still does not mount the palmyra horse. 1166 uses the sea twice over: love's pleasure is a sea, and when it afflicts (அடுங்கால்) the pain is greater than that. The sea is the book's measure for love, and 1166 says the measure is exceeded.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1167","b":"k:1136","type":"agrees","note":"Both lovers are awake at midnight, with the same word யாமத்தும். The man in 1136 says his eyes will not close and even at midnight he thinks of mounting the palmyra horse; the woman in 1167 says that even at midnight she alone is there (யானே உளேன்), swimming without sight of shore. Sleeplessness in the dead of night is given to both sides.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1167","b":"k:1164","type":"develops","note":"1164 says the sea of love exists and there is no raft to swim it. 1167 continues the figure: she has swum the fierce flood (காமக் கடும்புனல் நீந்தி) and sees no shore (கரை காணேன்), and at midnight there is only herself. The second verse turns the missing raft into an actual, endless swim.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1168","b":"k:1222","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1168: the night has put all living beings to sleep and, she says with pity (அளித்து), has no companion but her. 1222: she tells the evening it looks sad-eyed (புன்கண்ணை) and asks whether its partner is as hard as hers. In both she treats a time of day as a fellow sufferer and puts her own state onto it; Parimelazhagar says as much of 1222.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1169","b":"k:1235","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the phrase கொடியார் கொடுமை, 'the cruelty of the cruel one', for the absent man. In 1235 her shoulders, their old beauty faded, declare it; in 1169 she uses it as the measure the long nights exceed (கொடுமையின் தாம் கொடிய). 1169 moves the charge of cruelty from the man to the time itself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1169","b":"k:1269","type":"agrees","note":"1169: the nights that pass so long these days (நெடிய கழியும் இரா) are crueller than the cruel one's cruelty. 1269: for those who pine with a set day of return in mind, one day passes like seven (ஒருநாள் எழுநாள்போல்). Both say that time stretches for the one who waits.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1170","b":"k:1167","type":"same_image","note":"In 1167 she herself swims the fierce flood of love and sees no shore; in 1170 it is her eyes that swim in flood water (வெள்ளநீர் நீந்தல), which is her tears. The chapter's water figure for love ends as literal water in the eyes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1170","b":"k:1244","type":"develops","note":"1170: if her eyes could go where he is as her heart does (உள்ளம்போன்று உள்வழிச் செல்கிற்பின்), they would not be swimming in a flood of tears. 1244 turns the wish into a request: heart, take the eyes along when you go (கண்ணும் கொளச்சேறி), for they consume her in wanting to see him. Both rest on the idea that the heart can travel to him and the eyes cannot.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1171","b":"k:1091","type":"same_image","note":"1091 (the man): her kohl-lined eyes hold two looks, one that gives the sickness and one that is its cure (நோய்நோக்கு ... மருந்து). 1171 (the woman): her own eyes showed him to her and so she came to know an unabating sickness (தண்டாநோய்), and she asks why they weep. Eyes are the source of love's sickness in both, the other's eyes for the man and her own for the woman, and only his verse has a cure.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1171","b":"k:1244","type":"agrees","note":"1171: she asks why her eyes weep, since they showed him to her (தாம் காட்ட யாம் கண்டது). 1244: the eyes consume her in their wish to see him (அவர்க்காணல் உற்று). In both she speaks of her eyes as agents apart from herself, with wants of their own that she pays for.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1172","b":"k:535","type":"parallel_domain","note":"535: one who slipped by not guarding beforehand will grieve afterwards over his own error (முன்னுறக் காவாது ... பின்னூறு இரங்கிவிடும்). 1172: the eyes that looked without foresight now toil in misery (பைதல் உழப்பது). Parimelazhagar's comment on 1172 uses the same terms, that those who do not guard against what is coming can only bear it when it arrives.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1172","b":"k:792","type":"parallel_domain","note":"792: the friendship of one who does not take it after examining again and again ends in grief that kills him (ஆய்ந்தாய்ந்து கொள்ளாதான் கேண்மை). 1172, on Parimelazhagar's reading: the eyes looked that day without examining and understanding what would follow (தெரிந்துணரா நோக்கிய) and now suffer. The rule about forming ties without scrutiny is applied to a look. Manakkudavar reads the first phrase differently, as having looked with knowledge.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1173","b":"k:435","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1173: the eyes looked hastily of themselves (கதுமெனத் தாம் நோக்கி) and now weep of themselves, which is laughable. 435: the life of one who does not guard before a fault comes is destroyed like straw before fire (வருமுன்னர்க் காவாதான்). Parimelazhagar explains the laughter in 1173 with that same phrase: it is laughable to those who guard before it comes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1173","b":"k:1140","type":"same_image","note":"1140: the ignorant laugh where she can see it (நகுப அறிவில்லார்), because they have not suffered what she has. 1173: she finds her own eyes' plight fit to be laughed at (நகத்தக்கது உடைத்து). Laughter at a lover's suffering comes from outsiders in one verse and from the sufferer herself in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1174","b":"k:320","type":"parallel_domain","note":"320: all pain falls on those who caused pain (நோயெல்லாம் நோய்செய்தார் மேலவாம்). 1174: the eyes that fixed an inescapable sickness in her (உய்வில்நோய் என்கண் நிறுத்து) have themselves dried up and cannot weep. Parimelazhagar reads 1174 in these terms: having done her harm first, harm came to them of itself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1175","b":"k:1166","type":"develops","note":"1166 says love's pain, when it afflicts, is greater than the sea (அதனிற் பெரிது). 1175 repeats the measure as an epithet, a love-sickness the sea cannot hold (கடலாற்றாக் காமநோய்), and goes on to assign blame for it to her eyes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1176","b":"k:1173","type":"develops","note":"1173 finds it laughable that the eyes looked of themselves and weep of themselves. 1176 goes a step further: it is sweet (ஓஒ இனிதே) that the eyes which gave her this sickness have fallen into it too (தாஅம் இதற்பட்டது). The sequence moves from mockery of her eyes to satisfaction at their suffering.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1177","b":"k:1174","type":"develops","note":"1174 reports that the eyes have dried up and cannot weep (நீருலந்த). 1177 wishes exactly that on them: may the eyes that longed and melted to see him suffer on until their inner water is gone (உள்நீர் அறுக). What one verse states as their condition the other pronounces as a curse.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1179","b":"k:1295","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1179: when he does not come the eyes do not sleep, and when he comes they do not sleep (வாராக்கால் துஞ்சா வரின் துஞ்சா). 1295: the heart fears not having him and, having him, fears parting (பெறாஅமை அஞ்சும் பெறின் பிரிவு அஞ்சும்). The same two-sided bind is said of the eyes and of the heart, and Parimelazhagar explains 1179 by the fear of parting that 1295 names.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1180","b":"k:1271","type":"agrees","note":"1180 (the woman): the town can easily learn the secret of those whose eyes are beaten drums. 1271 (the man): though she hides it, her kohl-lined eyes go beyond her control (கரப்பினும் கையிகந்து) and have something to tell. Both lovers say her eyes disclose what she means to conceal.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1181","b":"k:1160","type":"develops","note":"1160 speaks, with irony, of the many who agree to the hard thing, endure the parting and live on afterwards (அரிதாற்றி ... பிரிவாற்றிப் பின்இருந்து வாழ்வார் பலர்); Parimelazhagar glosses அரிது ஆற்றி as consenting to the parting. 1181 is the woman who did consent (நல்காமை நேர்ந்தேன்) and now cannot bear the result. Read together, 1181 shows the cost of the consent that 1160 doubted anyone could really give.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1181","b":"k:1162","type":"develops","note":"In 1162 she cannot hide the sickness, and telling the one who caused it brings shame (உரைத்தலும் நாணுத் தரும்). In 1181 the question widens to யார்க்கு உரைக்கோ, to whom can I tell it at all, because she herself agreed to his going; Parimelazhagar adds that telling a self-made sorrow would itself be blame. The pair traces how the possible hearers for her complaint run out.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1182","b":"k:1183","type":"develops","note":"1182 has the pallor ride over her body with the pride of saying 'he gave me' (அவர்தந்தார் என்னும் தகையால்). 1183 uses the same verb of giving for an exchange: he took her grace and modesty and gave sickness and pallor (நோயும் பசலையும் தந்து). The pallor that boasts of being his gift in one verse is entered as his payment in the next.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1183","b":"k:211","type":"same_image","note":"211 says obligation wants no return, கைம்மாறு வேண்டா கடப்பாடு, and points to the rain, which the world cannot repay. 1183 uses the same word bitterly: as கைம்மாறு for the grace and modesty he took, he gave sickness and pallor. The word that names the return a good giver does not ask for is here the name of a bad bargain.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1184","b":"k:1125","type":"same_image","note":"1125 and 1184 open with the same words, உள்ளுவன் மன்யான். The man in 1125 says he would remember her qualities if he ever forgot them, and he does not know forgetting; the woman in 1184 says she thinks of him and yet turns pale. The same claim of constant thought is his proof of love and her complaint that such thought gives no protection.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1184","b":"k:1250","type":"develops","note":"In 1184 she thinks of him and speaks only of his merits (உள்ளுவன் ... உரைப்பது அவர்திறம்), and calls it deceit (கள்ளம்) that the pallor comes all the same. In 1250 she tells her heart that by keeping in it the man who left, she will lose still more beauty (இன்னும் இழத்தும் கவின்). 1250 states as cause and effect what 1184 finds baffling: the faithful thinking does not hold the pallor off.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1185","b":"k:1277","type":"develops","note":"In 1277 her bangles understood before she did that the lord of the shore had parted from her (நம்மினும் முன்னம் உணர்ந்த வளை). In 1185 the pallor spreads while he is still in sight. Both make the body register the parting faster than the mind or the facts do; 1277 pushes it to before the event.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1185","b":"k:1278","type":"agrees","note":"1185 points there to the lover going and here to the pallor spreading (உவக்காண் ... இவக்காண்); Parimelazhagar takes it as an earlier occasion when he had gone only a short way. 1278 says he left only yesterday and her body has been pale seven days (நெருநற்றுச் சென்றார் ... எழுநாளேம் மேனி பசந்து). One verse measures the pallor's speed in space and the other in time.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1186","b":"k:285","type":"parallel_domain","note":"285 describes those who, with goods in view, watch for another's lapse (பொச்சாப்புப் பார்ப்பார்), and denies them a loving nature. 1186 gives pallor the same posture: it watches for the weak moment (அற்றம் பார்க்கும்), which Parimelazhagar notes is a figurative use of 'watch'. The book's picture of the thief waiting for inattention is the picture the woman uses for what takes her colour.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1186","b":"k:1238","type":"agrees","note":"1186 is the woman's account: pallor watches for a gap in her husband's embrace (முயக்கற்றம் பார்க்கும்) as darkness watches for a lamp to fail. 1238 is the man's memory of the same thing: he loosened the arms that held her and her forehead turned pale (முயங்கிய கைகளை ஊக்கப் பசந்தது). The two speakers confirm each other from either side of the embrace.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1187","b":"k:1239","type":"agrees","note":"In 1187 she lay embracing him, shifted a little (புடைபெயர்ந்தேன்), and at once the pallor was thick enough to scoop up. In 1239 the man recalls that a cool breeze cutting between them in the embrace (முயக்கிடைத் தண்வளி போழ) was enough to pale her eyes. Both verses, from the two partners, set the threshold of separation at the smallest physical gap.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1188","b":"k:1190","type":"qualifies","note":"1188 complains that the talk fixes on her pallor and not on his leaving. 1190 says it is good to get the name of pallor, provided they do not spread talk of his unkindness (நல்காமை தூற்றார் எனின்); Parimelazhagar sets it as her reply when the friend has blamed him. The complaint of 1188 should therefore not be read as a wish to have him blamed: in 1190 she accepts the very imbalance she resents.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1188","b":"k:1220","type":"qualifies","note":"1188 says people say only 'she has grown pale' and no one says 'he left her' (துறந்தார் அவர் என்பார் இல்). In 1220 the townspeople do say he has left her in waking life (நனவினால் நம்நீத்தார் என்பர்), and she answers that they must not see him in dreams. These are speeches at different moments, but together they show her objecting both when the town blames only her and when it blames him.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1189","b":"k:1181","type":"develops","note":"1181 puts the consent on herself: நல்காமை நேர்ந்தேன், I agreed to his absence. 1189 names him as the one who brought her to agree (நயப்பித்தார்) and says let her body pale as it will if he stands well; Parimelazhagar reads நன்னிலையர் as free of fault, while Manakkudavar reads the condition as his suffering too. On Parimelazhagar's reading the two verses share out the responsibility and she keeps the cost.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1189","b":"k:1236","type":"agrees","note":"In 1189, on Parimelazhagar's reading, she accepts the pallor so long as the man who persuaded her is held blameless. In 1236 her arms thin and her bangles slip, and what pains her is hearing him called cruel (அவரைக் கொடியர் எனக்கூறல் நொந்து). In both she ranks his good name above her own wasting.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1190","b":"k:1232","type":"qualifies","note":"1190 hopes that her pallor can be talked of without his நல்காமை being talked of. In 1232 the friend says her pale, weeping eyes themselves seem to tell of the unkindness of the one she desired (நயந்தவர் நல்காமை சொல்லுவ போலும்). The same word நல்காமை shows the limit of her wish: the pallor is itself the report against him.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1190","b":"k:1236","type":"agrees","note":"1190 says being named for her pallor is good if people do not broadcast his unkindness (நல்காமை தூற்றார் எனின்). 1236 says the hurt is in hearing him called cruel (கொடியர் எனக்கூறல் நொந்து), more than in the slipping bangles. Both verses make the talk against him, and not her own disfigurement, the thing she cannot bear.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1191","b":"k:1108","type":"agrees","note":"1108, spoken in union, says the close embrace is sweet to two who desire each other (வீழும் இருவர்க்கு இனிதே). 1191, spoken in separation, says those loved by the ones they love (தாம்வீழ்வார் தம்வீழப் பெற்றவர்) have the stoneless fruit. Both place love's sweetness in the desire being mutual, using the same verb வீழ்; one says it from inside and the other from outside.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1193","b":"k:598","type":"same_image","note":"598 says those without drive never reach the pride of saying 'we are generous' (வள்ளியம் என்னும் செருக்கு). 1193 says the pride of saying 'we shall live' (வாழுநம் என்னும் செருக்கு) suits only those loved by the ones they love. The same construction makes a justified pride depend on a prior condition: inner drive in the one case, being loved in return in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1194","b":"k:1191","type":"develops","note":"1191 says those loved by the ones they love have 'obtained' (பெற்றவர் பெற்றாரே) love's stoneless fruit; Parimelazhagar ties the word to earlier good deeds. 1194 gives the negative: even those who are esteemed are கெழீஇயிலர் if the one they love does not love them, which Parimelazhagar glosses as lacking good deeds and Manakkudavar as being without what they desire. The pair states the same condition from both sides, and shows the commentators reading it as fortune and not merit.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1195","b":"k:1245","type":"qualifies","note":"1195 asks what the one she loves can do for her when he has no love for her (தாம்காதல் கொள்ளாக் கடை). 1245 asks her heart whether a man who does not respond when she draws near (உற்றால் உறாஅதவர்) can be dropped as one who hates her (செற்றார் எனக் கைவிடல் உண்டோ). The second speech limits the first: finding no benefit in an unreturned love does not make her able to give it up.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1196","b":"k:118","type":"parallel_domain","note":"118 praises being level like the scale-beam and not leaning to one side (ஒருபால் கோடாமை) as the ornament of the worthy. 1196 applies the same test to love: borne on one end (ஒருதலையான்) it hurts, and on both it is sweet. Evenness between two sides is a mark of justice in the Book of Virtue and a condition of happiness here.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1197","b":"k:1252","type":"develops","note":"1252 says the thing called desire has no eye, that is no pity (கண்ணின்று), since it works her heart even at midnight. 1197 asks whether Kaman, who stays and acts on one person only, does not see her pain and misery (காணான்கொல்). The complaint moves from the feeling to the god, and both use sight as the test of his mercy.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1197","b":"k:1308","type":"parallel_domain","note":"In 1308 the man asks what use suffering is when there is no lover who knows 'this one is suffering' (அஃதறியும் காதலர் இல்லா வழி). In 1197 the woman asks whether Kaman fails to see her suffering. Both speakers treat pain as needing a witness, and each finds the one who ought to notice absent.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1199","b":"k:1198","type":"develops","note":"1198 calls hard those who live without receiving a sweet word from the beloved (இன்சொல் பெறாஅது). 1199 defines how little would count: though he gives no grace, any report of him is sweet to her ears (அவர்மாட்டு இசையும் இனிய செவிக்கு). Parimelazhagar reads இசை as any word at all, even that he is not coming, and links the two verses on that basis; Kalaignar takes இசை as his praise.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1199","b":"k:1213","type":"agrees","note":"In 1213 her life continues because in dreams she sees the man who gives no grace in waking life (நனவினால் நல்காதவரை). In 1199 the man who gives no grace (நல்கார் எனினும்) still sweetens her ears by any word of him. In both she is sustained by a substitute for his presence, a dream or a report, while saying outright that he withholds himself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1200","b":"k:1164","type":"same_image","note":"1164 says the sea of love exists and the raft to cross it does not (காமக் கடல் ... ஏமப் புணை இல்). 1200 tells the heart to fill up the sea (கடலைச் செறாஅஅய்) instead of telling its pain to the unloving; Pope takes this sea as her own sorrow, while Parimelazhagar takes it as the sea that torments her. On Pope's reading the two verses share the sea as the measure of her grief, with crossing it and filling it both out of reach.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1201","b":"k:1207","type":"qualifies","note":"Both verses turn on the word உள்ளினும். In 1201 the man says remembering gives unending joy (தீராப் பெருமகிழ்); in 1207 the woman says that even while she remembers, her heart burns (உள்ளினும் உள்ளம் சுடும்), which Parimelazhagar explains as the separation burning her despite the memory. These are two speakers in two moods, so it is a limit on the first claim and not a contradiction in the book's teaching.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1201","b":"k:1281","type":"agrees","note":"1201 (the man, for Parimelazhagar) says love is sweeter than toddy because it gives great joy that does not end even when only thought of (உள்ளினும் தீராப் பெருமகிழ்). 1281, spoken by the woman, makes the same comparison: elation at a thought and delight at a sight belong to love and not to toddy (கள்ளுக்கு இல் காமத்திற்கு உண்டு). The pairing shows the same judgment given from both sides of the couple, built on the same verb உள்ளுதல்.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1202","b":"k:1209","type":"qualifies","note":"1202 says love is sweet in every way since no pain comes from thinking of the one you love (நினைப்ப வருவதொன்று இல், as the commentators read this edition's garbled line). In 1209 the woman's dear life is wasting because she thinks hard on his lack of tenderness (அளியின்மை ஆற்ற நினைந்து). The pairing shows that the sweetness of 1202 depends on what is remembered: the shared days, not the present neglect. Manakkudavar in fact reads 1202 as conditional, 'if no pain comes'.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1202","b":"k:1296","type":"qualifies","note":"1202 claims that thinking of the beloved brings no pain. In 1296 the woman says that when she sat alone and thought (தனியே இருந்து நினைத்தக்கால்), her heart stayed only to eat her (என்னைத் தினிய); Parimelazhagar specifies that what she thought of was his cruelties. The two speeches mark out the cases in which remembering comforts and in which it wounds.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1204","b":"k:1205","type":"develops","note":"1204 asks whether she too is in his heart, since he is always in hers (யாமும் உளேங்கொல் அவர்நெஞ்சத்து). 1205 gives the answer as an accusation: he has put a guard on his heart against her (தம்நெஞ்சத்து எம்மைக் கடிகொண்டார்) and yet comes unceasingly into hers. Manakkudavar reads 1205 as the woman, having doubted, now deciding that he does not think of her.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1205","b":"k:1291","type":"agrees","note":"1205 complains that he keeps her out of his heart while entering hers without pause (எம்நெஞ்சத்து ஓவா வரல்). 1291 states the same imbalance to her own heart: his heart stays his (அவர்நெஞ்சு அவர்க்காதல்), so why is hers not hers? In 1205 she blames him for the unequal exchange, and in 1291 she blames her own heart.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1206","b":"k:1213","type":"agrees","note":"1206: she exists only by remembering the days she had with him (உற்றநாள் உள்ள உளேன்), and asks what else she could live by. 1213: her life exists because she sees in dreams the man who gives nothing in waking (கனவினால் காண்டலின் உண்டென் உயிர்). Both make survival in separation depend on a mental substitute for his presence, memory by day and dream by night.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1206","b":"k:1263","type":"develops","note":"1206 says she lives by looking back at the days of union (உள்ள உளேன்). 1263 says she still lives by longing for his return (வரல் நசைஇ இன்னும் உளேன்). The same verb உளேன் is supported in one verse by the past and in the other by the expected future; Parimelazhagar on 1206 notes that the other supports, such as a messenger from him, are missing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1207","b":"k:1159","type":"develops","note":"1207 says that even as she remembers, her heart is burned (உள்ளம் சுடும்). 1159 describes the same burning as a property of love's sickness: fire burns only when touched, and cannot burn when one moves away as love does (விடிற்சுடல் ஆற்றுமோ தீ). 1159 gives the general paradox of a fire that burns at a distance, and 1207 shows it at work inside memory.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1207","b":"k:1262","type":"agrees","note":"In both verses the woman rejects the idea of forgetting him, which Parimelazhagar says the friend had advised in each case. 1207 asks what she would become if she forgot (மறப்பின் எவனாவன்); 1262 answers concretely that if she forgot today (இன்று மறப்பின்) her beauty would go and the bangles slip from her arms. Remembering hurts, but both verses hold that forgetting would be worse.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1208","b":"k:1199","type":"develops","note":"1199 says that though the one she longs for gives nothing (நல்கார் எனினும்), even a word about him is sweet to her ear. 1208 measures what an ungiving lover leaves her more sharply: his whole gift (சிறப்பு) is that he does not object to being thought of. Parimelazhagar takes 1208 as scorn of the friend's promise that he will come and be kind, whereas Manakkudavar takes the not-being-angry as real grace.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1208","b":"k:1320","type":"same_image","note":"The verses share the words நினை and காய். In 1208 the woman says that however much she thinks of him he is not angry (எனைத்து நினைப்பினும் காயார்), and that this is all the honour he does her. In 1320 the man reports that when he merely sits thinking and looks at her she is angry (நினைத்திருந்து நோக்கினும் காயும்) and asks whom he is thinking of. The absent lover tolerates everything because he is not there to care, while the present one is questioned about every thought.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1210","b":"k:1170","type":"develops","note":"1170 says that if her eyes could go where he is, as her mind does (உள்ளம்போன்று உள்வழிச் செல்கிற்பின்), they would not be swimming in tears. 1210 looks for a way round that limit: on Parimelazhagar's reading, since both of them look at the moon, her eyes meet his there (கண்ணினால் காண). Manakkudavar reads it differently, as a complaint that the moon will not set and let her sleep and see him in a dream.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1211","b":"k:1212","type":"develops","note":"In 1211 the dream arrives as the carrier of his message (காதலர் தூதொடு வந்த கனவு). In 1212 she wants to use the dream in the other direction: if her eyes would sleep, she would herself declare to him that she survives (உயலுண்மை சாற்றுவேன்). Parimelazhagar says this is spoken when she was thinking of sending a messenger, and that she would tell him in full what she would hold back from one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1211","b":"k:1268","type":"same_image","note":"Both verses end on the word விருந்து, a feast. In 1211 the woman (for Parimelazhagar) asks what feast she can make for the dream that came bringing her lover's messenger (யாதுசெய் வேன்கொல் விருந்து). In 1268 the man, away on the king's work, looks forward to the evening feast at home once the king has won (மாலை அயர்கம் விருந்து). The real feast waits on his return, and meanwhile she can offer hospitality only to a dream.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1212","b":"k:1179","type":"agrees","note":"1212 makes everything depend on a condition: if her eyes would sleep when she begs them (யானிரப்பத் துஞ்சின்). 1179 says why the condition fails: her eyes do not sleep when he does not come and do not sleep when he does (வாராக்கால் துஞ்சா வரின்துஞ்சா). Set beside chapter 122, this shows that the dream which consoles her in the following verses is not available to her at will.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:1213","b":"k:1198","type":"qualifies","note":"1198 says no one in the world is harder than those who go on living without getting even a kind word from the one they love (வாழ்வாரின் வன்கணார் இல்). 1213 has the woman living on though he gives her nothing in waking life (நனவினால் நல்காதவரை), and explains it: her life exists because she sees him in dreams (கனவினால் காண்டலின்). The second verse answers the first's charge of hardness by naming what keeps her alive.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1214","b":"k:1170","type":"develops","note":"1170 regrets that her eyes cannot travel to where he is as her mind can. In 1214 the dream does the travelling: it seeks out the man who gives nothing in waking life and brings him (நல்காரை நாடித் தரற்கு), and so there is pleasure in the dream. Parimelazhagar spells it out: the dream goes to the country he went to, finds him and delivers him.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1215","b":"k:1201","type":"qualifies","note":"1201 says love gives a joy that does not end even in recollection (உள்ளினும் தீரா). 1215 says that what she saw in waking was sweet then, and the dream too is sweet just while it is seen (கண்ட பொழுதே இனிது). Manakkudavar glosses both pleasures as lasting for that moment; Parimelazhagar takes her point to be that dream and waking are therefore equal for her. On either reading the woman's sweetness is tied to the moment of seeing, where the man's persists in thought.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1216","b":"k:1169","type":"qualifies","note":"1169 calls the long nights of separation crueller than the cruel man himself (கொடியார் கொடுமையின் தாம்கொடிய). 1216 wants the night's state never to end, with waking itself abolished (நனவென ஒன்றில்லை ஆயின்). The difference is sleep: the night is an enemy when she lies awake and a refuge when it brings the dream, and Parimelazhagar accordingly has her call waking a villain (பாவி).","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1216","b":"k:1329","type":"parallel_domain","note":"In 1216 the woman says that if there were no such thing as waking, her lover would not leave her in the dream (கனவினால் காதலர் நீங்கலர் மன்). In 1329 the man, in the middle of a lovers' quarrel, wishes the night would lengthen so that he can go on pleading (நீடுக மன்னோ இரா). Both wish away the end of the night and both close on the wistful particle மன்; hers is said of a dream meeting and his of a real one.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1217","b":"k:819","type":"parallel_domain","note":"819, on bad friendship, says the tie with those whose deeds are one thing and words another is bitter even in a dream (கனவினும் இன்னாது). In 1217 the lover who gives nothing in waking life hurts her in her dreams as well (கனவினால் ... பீழிப்பது). Both take the dream as the last place a person's unreliability should reach, and both find that it reaches there: one verse in the book of polity, the other in a lover's complaint.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1217","b":"k:1213","type":"qualifies","note":"The two verses open with nearly the same words, நனவினால் நல்காதவரை and நனவினால் நல்காக் கொடியார், and reach opposite verdicts on the dream. In 1213 seeing him in dreams keeps her alive; in 1217 she asks why the cruel one torments her in dreams (எம்மைப் பீழிப்பது). Parimelazhagar places 1217 after she has woken and found him gone, so the dream comforts while it lasts and wounds once it has ended.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1218","b":"k:1217","type":"qualifies","note":"Both verses describe the same event: he is with her in sleep and gone on waking. In 1217 this is his cruelty; Parimelazhagar explains the torment as his being on her shoulder in the dream and vanishing when she wakes. In 1218 she tells it in his favour: asleep he is on her shoulder (தோள்மேலர்), and awake he hurries into her heart (நெஞ்சத்தர் ஆவர் விரைந்து), so he never leaves her at all. Parimelazhagar says 1218 is her defence of him after the friend had spoken against him.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1218","b":"k:1249","type":"agrees","note":"1218 says that on waking he quickly becomes one who is in her heart (நெஞ்சத்தர் ஆவர்). 1249 tells her own heart that the lover is inside it (உள்ளத்தார் காதலவர்), so there is no one to go out looking for. Both use his dwelling in her heart to deny that he is absent; 1218 adds the dream so that both night and day are covered.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1219","b":"k:66","type":"parallel_domain","note":"The two verses have the same construction: an opinion is held only by those who lack an experience. In 66, 'the flute is sweet, the lute is sweet' is said by those who have not heard their own children's prattle (மழலைச்சொல் கேளாதவர்). In 1219 those who blame the man for giving nothing in waking life are those who have not seen a lover in dreams (கனவினால் காதலர்க் காணாதவர்). Parental love in the first book and a lover's dream in the third both discredit a judgment by pointing to what its holders have not known.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1219","b":"k:1236","type":"develops","note":"1236: with bangles and arms slackening, what pains her is hearing him called cruel (அவரைக் கொடியர் எனக்கூறல் நொந்து). 1219 goes from that pain to a rebuttal: those who blame him (நோவர்) are people who have not seen a lover in their dreams. Parimelazhagar gives both verses the same occasion, the friend speaking ill of the man, and says she puts her reply in 1219 on strangers out of displeasure with the friend.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1221","b":"k:326","type":"same_image","note":"326 calls Death 'the life-eating' (உயிருண்ணுங் கூற்று) and says it does not advance on the days of one who does not kill. 1221 gives the same epithet, உயிருண்ணும், to the evening that comes on women parted from their husbands. The pairing shows the woman addressing evening in the book's own language for Death; Parimelazhagar's note on 1229 says she thinks of evening as கூற்றம்.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1221","b":"k:334","type":"parallel_domain","note":"334 says what shows itself as a 'day' (நாளென ஒன்றுபோற் காட்டி) is a blade that saws through life (உயிர் ஈரும் வாள்), for those who perceive it. 1221 has the parted woman tell the hour it is not evening (மாலையோ அல்லை) but a thing that eats the life of those once wed (உயிருண்ணும் வேலை; Parimelazhagar takes வேலை as a final hour, Manakkudavar and Kalaignar as a spear). Both unmask a unit of time as something that consumes life, one as a general truth of impermanence, the other as one lover's experience of separation.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1223","b":"k:1227","type":"same_image","note":"1223 says the evening that used to come budding with chill and looking wan (பனி அரும்பிப் பைதல்கொள் மாலை) now comes with revulsion budding and sorrow growing (துனி அரும்பித் துன்பம் வளர). 1227 says her sickness buds in the morning (காலை அரும்பி), swells through the day and opens in the evening. Both use அரும்பு, budding, for her pain, and together they give evening as both what brings the bud and when it opens.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1224","b":"k:1169","type":"develops","note":"1169 says the long nights now passing are crueller than the cruelty of the cruel one (கொடியார் கொடுமையின் தாம்கொடிய). 1224 says that where her lover is absent (காதலர் இல்வழி) evening comes like ஏதிலர், pitiless strangers or foes, on a killing ground (கொலைக்களத்து). Both make a stretch of time into a cruel agent set beside human cruelty, and 1224 names the condition: it is so only in his absence.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"k:1225","b":"k:319","type":"parallel_domain","note":"319 states a rule: hurt done to others in the forenoon (முற்பகல்) comes back to oneself in the afternoon (பிற்பகல்), of itself. In 1225 the woman asks what good she did to morning (காலைக்குச் செய்த நன்று) and what enmity she did to evening (மாலைக்குச் செய்த பகை). Her question assumes the same logic of return, that suffering answers something one did, and finds no deed of hers to explain evening's hostility.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1228","b":"k:1258","type":"parallel_domain","note":"In 1258 the woman says the soft speech (பணிமொழி) of her deceiving lover is the weapon that breaks her womanly reserve (பெண்மை உடைக்கும் படை). In 1228 the herdsman's flute is a weapon that kills (கொல்லும் படை). Both call a gentle sound a படை, one because it draws her to him and the other because it announces the evening without him.","grade":"sound","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1229","b":"k:1168","type":"qualifies","note":"In 1168 the night has put all living beings to sleep (மன்னுயிர் எல்லாம் துயிற்றி) and she alone is its companion, so her suffering is solitary. In 1229 she says that when evening spreads, bewildering the mind (மதிமருண்டு; Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar take this as her own mind), the whole town is bewildered and suffers (பதிமருண்டு பைதல் உழக்கும்). The two speeches picture the world around her in opposite ways, asleep and untouched in one and sharing her distress in the other.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1230","b":"k:1209","type":"agrees","note":"In 1209 the woman says her dear life is perishing (விளியும் என் இன்னுயிர்) as she thinks much on the lack of kindness of one who said they were not separate. In 1230 her life, which had not died till now (மாயா உயிர்), is dying in the dim evening as she thinks of the man whose nature is wealth (பொருள்மாலையாளரை உள்ளி). Both say that thinking of him is what wears her life away, and each names him by a reproach: his broken word in one, his preference for wealth in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1230","b":"k:1263","type":"qualifies","note":"In 1263 she says he left desiring victory with his own resolve as companion (உரன் நசைஇ உள்ளம் துணையாகச் சென்றார்), and that she still lives by desiring his return (வரல் நசைஇ இன்னும் உளேன்). In 1230 thinking of the man given to wealth makes her life die. Both name him by the aim he left for, but in one speech the thought of him keeps her alive and in the other it kills; these are two moments of the same speaker, not a doctrine.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1232","b":"k:71","type":"parallel_domain","note":"71 says love cannot be bolted in, because the small tears of the loving make it public (புன்கணீர் பூசல் தரும்). 1232 says her eyes, pale and running with tears (பசந்து பனிவாரும் கண்), seem to tell of the unkindness of the one she desired (நல்காமை சொல்லுவ போலும்). The same mechanism, tears that disclose, works in both; in 71 they reveal the weeper's love, in 1232 they also accuse the absent lover.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1232","b":"k:1180","type":"agrees","note":"In 1180 the woman says the town easily learns the secret of those like her whose eyes are beaten drums (அறைபறை கண்ணார்). In 1232 the friend says her pale, weeping eyes seem to be telling of his unkindness. Both treat the eyes as announcing what she would keep hidden; the woman says it of herself and the friend says it to urge her to bear up, as Parimelazhagar frames it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1233","b":"k:1157","type":"agrees","note":"In 1157 the woman asks whether the bangles slipping past her wrist do not proclaim (தூற்றாகொல்) that the lord of the shore has left her. In 1233 the shoulders that swelled on the day of union (மணந்தநாள் வீங்கிய தோள்) seem to announce fully that he has parted (தணந்தமை சால அறிவிப்ப போலும்). In both her body makes the separation public without her speaking; 1233 adds the contrast with how the same shoulders looked at union.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1233","b":"k:1277","type":"same_image","note":"In 1277 her bangles understood before she did that the lord of the cool shore had parted (தணந்தமை ... முன்னம் உணர்ந்த வளை). In 1233 her shoulders announce that he has parted, with the same word தணந்தமை. In 1277 the body is first to know of the parting, and in 1233 it is the one that tells others.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1234","b":"k:1235","type":"develops","note":"1234 and 1235 share the line தொல்கவின் வாடிய தோள், shoulders whose old beauty has faded. 1234 describes the loss: with her partner gone (துணை நீங்கி) they have lost their fullness and the bangles slip. 1235 turns the same shoulders into testimony that declares the cruelty of the cruel one (கொடியார் கொடுமை உரைக்கும்), so the repeated line moves from description to accusation.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1234","b":"k:1262","type":"qualifies","note":"In 1262 the woman tells her friend that if she forgets him today (இன்று மறப்பின்), her beauty will leave her shoulders and her ornaments will slip off (கலங்கழியும் காரிகை நீத்து). In 1234 the beauty has faded and the bangles are slipping because her partner is gone (துணை நீங்கி). Read together, the bodily loss follows his absence whether she remembers or forgets, though in 1262 she holds that remembering is what prevents it.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1235","b":"k:1188","type":"qualifies","note":"In 1188 the woman complains that people say only 'she has grown pale' and no one says 'he has left her' (துறந்தார் அவர் என்பார் இல்). In 1235 the wasted shoulders themselves declare his cruelty. One speech says the visible change brings talk about her alone, and the other says the same kind of change speaks against him; the difference is in who is reading the body, the town or the friend.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1235","b":"k:1236","type":"qualifies","note":"In 1235 the friend, in Parimelazhagar's reading, says the shoulders with bangles lost and old beauty faded declare the cruelty of the cruel one (கொடியார் கொடுமை உரைக்கும்). In 1236 the woman answers that with bangles and shoulders slackening, what pains her is the saying that he is cruel (அவரைக் கொடியர் எனக்கூறல் நொந்து). The second verse limits the first: the body's evidence stands, but she refuses the charge drawn from it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1236","b":"k:1169","type":"qualifies","note":"In 1169 the woman herself speaks of the cruelty of the cruel one (கொடியார் கொடுமை) when she says the long nights are crueller still. In 1236 she is pained when he is called cruel (கொடியர்). The pair shows that she uses the word in her own lament and does not accept it from another's mouth; these are different moments of a dramatised speaker, not a contradiction in teaching.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1237","b":"k:1162","type":"qualifies","note":"In 1162 she says she cannot hide this sickness, and that telling it to the one who caused it brings shame (உரைத்தலும் நாணுத் தரும்). In 1237 she asks her heart whether it can gain honour by telling him of her wasting shoulders. The two give opposite values to the same act of telling him, shame in one and possible honour (பாடு) in the other; in 1237 it is the heart and not she herself who would carry the word.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1240","b":"k:1182","type":"same_image","note":"In 1182 the woman says pallor spreads and rides over her body with the pride (தகையால்) that he was the one who gave it. In 1240 the man says the pallor of her eyes felt distress (பருவரல்) on seeing what her forehead had done. Both give the pallor feelings of its own, pride in one and distress in the other, so that பசப்பு is spoken of as a party to the love and not only its symptom.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1240","b":"k:1238","type":"develops","note":"1238 says that when he loosened the embracing arms her forehead turned pale. 1240 says the pallor of her eyes itself came to distress (கண்ணின் பசப்போ பருவரல் எய்தின்றே) on seeing what the bright forehead had done (ஒண்ணுதல் செய்தது கண்டு). 1240 depends on 1238 for what the forehead did; Parimelazhagar explains that the eyes' pallor was abashed that the forehead had paled sooner than they did, at a smaller cause.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1241","b":"k:1102","type":"develops","note":"In 1102 the man says that medicines for other ailments are things other than the ailment (பிணிக்கு மருந்து பிற), but she is herself the medicine for the sickness she causes (தன்நோய்க்குத் தானே மருந்து). In 1241 the woman, apart from him, asks her heart to think of any medicine at all (எனைத்தொன்றும் ... மருந்து) for her sickness. Read together, 1102 explains why her heart has nothing to offer: the only cure is the absent person who caused the illness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1242","b":"k:1195","type":"agrees","note":"1195 asks what those we love can do for us if they do not love in return (தாம் காதல் கொள்ளாக் கடை). 1242 draws the conclusion and turns it on her own heart: since he has no love (காதல் அவரிலர் ஆக), its suffering is folly (நோவது பேதைமை). The first states the uselessness of one-sided love, the second names persisting in it as foolishness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1242","b":"k:1308","type":"agrees","note":"1308, which Parimelazhagar gives to the man during a sulk, asks what use suffering is where there is no lover who recognises that one suffers (நோதல் எவன் ... அஃதறியும் காதலர் இல்லா வழி). 1242 has the woman tell her heart that suffering is folly when he has no love (நோவது பேதைமை). Both speakers, from either side of the relationship, make pain depend for its point on the other's loving awareness of it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1243","b":"k:1204","type":"develops","note":"1204 asks whether she too is in his heart, since he is always in hers (யாமும் உளேங்கொல் அவர் நெஞ்சத்து). 1243 gives her own bleak answer: a compassionate thought of her does not exist in the one who caused the sickness (பரிந்துள்ளல் பைதல்நோய் செய்தார்கண் இல்). The question of 1204 has by chapter 125 become a settled premise for scolding her heart.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1243","b":"k:1296","type":"agrees","note":"1243 asks the heart why it sits and suffers in thought (இருந்துள்ளி என் பரிதல்) when he has no pitying thought. 1296 says that when she sits alone and thinks (தனியே இருந்து நினைத்தக்கால்) her heart is there only to eat her (என்னைத் தினிய). Both describe sitting and remembering as self-inflicted harm, and each blames the heart for it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1244","b":"k:1283","type":"agrees","note":"1283 says that though he disregards her and does only as he pleases, her eyes will not rest without seeing him (கொண்கனைக் காணாது அமையல கண்). 1244 says the eyes, wanting to see him (அவர்க் காணல் உற்று), eat her. Both make the eyes an appetite of their own that her judgment of him does not govern.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1245","b":"k:807","type":"parallel_domain","note":"807, on old friendship, says that those whose bond came by the way of love do not cut off love even when ruinous things are done to them (அழிவந்த செய்யினும் அன்பறார்). 1245 asks whether she can let him go as an enemy merely because he does not return her closeness. The logic of long friendship in the Book of Wealth, that injury does not license abandonment, reappears as a lover's rhetorical question.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1245","b":"k:1292","type":"agrees","note":"1245 asks the heart whether one can abandon as an enemy (செற்றார் எனக் கைவிடல் உண்டோ) the man who does not respond when she draws close (யாம் உற்றால் உறாஅதவர்). 1292 complains that the heart, even having seen that he is not close (உறாஅதவர்க் கண்ட கண்ணும்), goes to him on the thought that he will not be hostile (செறாஅர் என). The two share the pair உறாதவர் / செறு, and show the same refusal to treat coldness as enmity, once owned by her and once blamed on the heart.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1246","b":"k:1286","type":"develops","note":"1286 says that when she sees him she sees no faults, and when she does not see him she sees nothing but faults (காணுங்கால் காணேன் தவறாய காணாக்கால் காணேன் தவறல்லவை). 1246 calls the heart's anger in his absence false because it never survives the sight of him (காதலர்க் கண்டால் புலந்துணராய்). 1286 states the pattern plainly; 1246 uses it to discredit the anger felt while apart.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1246","b":"k:1294","type":"agrees","note":"1246 tells the heart that when it sees the lover it cannot even sulk and then be reconciled (புலந்துணராய்), so its present anger is a false heat (பொய்க்காய்வு காய்தி). 1294 makes the same charge: the heart will not first raise a quarrel and then enjoy (துனிசெய்து துவ்வாய்), so no one will plan with it. In both the heart is accused of being incapable of the sulk it pretends to.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1247","b":"k:1251","type":"develops","note":"1247 leaves open which of desire and shame is to go (காமம் விடு ஒன்றோ நாண் விடு). 1251, opening the next chapter, reports the outcome: the axe of desire breaks the door of restraint that shame had bolted (காமக் கணிச்சி உடைக்கும் ... நாணுத்தாழ் வீழ்த்த கதவு). The choice she offers her heart in 1247 is settled in 1251 by force, not by decision.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1248","b":"k:967","type":"parallel_domain","note":"967, on honour, says that being called ruined is better than living by going behind those who do not accept one (ஒட்டார் பின் சென்று ஒருவன் வாழ்தலின்); Parimelazhagar takes this of following scorners for gain. 1248 calls her heart a fool for going behind the one who left without grace (பிரிந்தவர் பின்செல்வாய் பேதை). The standard of honour from the Book of Wealth is the one she applies to her own heart, though in love she cannot enforce it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1248","b":"k:1293","type":"agrees","note":"1248 calls the heart a fool for going after the man who left (பிரிந்தவர் பின்செல்வாய்) while grieving that he gives no grace. 1293 asks the heart whether it follows him as it likes (பெட்டாங்கு அவர் பின் செலல்) because the ruined have no friends. Both speak of the heart 'going behind him' as a desertion of her that she can see but not stop.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1250","b":"k:1207","type":"agrees","note":"1207 says she does not know how to forget, and that even remembering burns her heart (உள்ளினும் உள்ளம் சுடும்). 1250 says that holding him in her heart will cost her yet more beauty. Both count remembering him as a harm to herself, while 1207 adds that forgetting is not available to her.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1250","b":"k:1262","type":"tension","note":"In 1250 she says that if she keeps in her heart the man who left without coming close (துறந்தாரை நெஞ்சத்து உடையேமா), she will lose still more beauty (இன்னும் இழத்தும் கவின்); Parimelazhagar frames this as a case for forgetting him. In 1262 she tells her friend that if she forgets him today, beauty will leave her and the bangles slip from her arms (இன்று மறப்பின் ... கலங்கழியும் காரிகை நீத்து). The two speeches give opposite accounts of whether remembering or forgetting costs her beauty; both are a speaker's words in the moment, and the conflict holds on Parimelazhagar's reading of the settings.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1252","b":"k:1167","type":"agrees","note":"1167 says she swims the flood of desire and sees no shore, and even at midnight only she is awake (யாமத்தும் யானே உளேன்). 1252 says desire has no pity (கண் இன்று), for it sets her heart to work even at midnight (யாமத்தும் ஆளும் தொழில்). Both mark midnight, when Parimelazhagar notes all others rest, as the hour that shows desire does not release her.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1253","b":"k:1318","type":"same_image","note":"1253 likens desire to a sneeze that appears without one's intending it; Parimelazhagar glosses that it cannot be held in. In 1318 the man does stifle a sneeze (தும்முச் செறுப்ப), and she weeps that he is hiding from her that his people are thinking of him. The sneeze is in both an involuntary sign of what is within, and 1318 shows that even suppressing it is read as a disclosure.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1254","b":"k:1180","type":"develops","note":"1180 says it is not hard for the town to get the secret of those like her, whose eyes are beaten drums (அறைபறை கண்ணார்). 1254 says her desire goes past concealment into the public place (மன்று படும்). 1180 supplies a means by which the exposure described in 1254 happens: her own eyes announce it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1255","b":"k:1245","type":"develops","note":"1245 asks whether she can abandon as hostile (செற்றார் எனக் கைவிடல் உண்டோ) the man who does not answer her love. 1255 turns that private question into a general statement: not following the hostile (செற்றார் பின் செல்லா) is a dignity unknown to those with love's sickness (காமநோய் உற்றார் அறிவதொன்று அன்று). The word செற்றார் links them, and the second explains why the first could only be a rhetorical question.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1256","b":"k:1168","type":"same_image","note":"In 1168 she calls the night அளித்து, pitiable or kindly, because having put every living thing to sleep it has no companion but her. In 1256 she uses the same word of her grief (அளித்தரோ ... துயர்), which Parimelazhagar takes as scorn. In both she speaks to her tormentor in the language of grace and pity, which is how these monologues voice bitterness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1256","b":"k:1255","type":"develops","note":"1255 states in general terms that the lovesick do not know the dignity of not going after one who is hostile. 1256 applies it to herself with irony: how gracious (அளித்தரோ) is this grief, which wants her to go after the one who is hostile (செற்றவர் பின் சேறல் வேண்டி). The pair moves from a maxim about lovers to her own case, and Parimelazhagar reads அளித்து as mockery.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1257","b":"k:1247","type":"develops","note":"In 1247 she cannot bear desire and shame together and asks her heart to give up one (காமம் விடு ஒன்றோ நாண் விடு). In 1257 the matter is settled in his presence: when he does what she longs for, shame is simply not known to her. The conflict that is unbearable in his absence does not arise once he is there.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1257","b":"k:1297","type":"agrees","note":"1297 says that, caught up with a foolish heart that cannot forget him, she has forgotten even shame (நாணும் மறந்தேன்). 1257 says she knows nothing of a thing called shame (நாண் என ஒன்றோ அறியலம்) when the one she cherishes does out of desire what she longs for. The two verses give the same loss of shame from two moments, separation in 1297 and reunion in 1257.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1258","b":"k:555","type":"same_image","note":"555 asks whether the tears of those who suffer under a ruler are not the weapon that wears away his wealth (கண்ணீர் அன்றே செல்வத்தைத் தேய்க்கும் படை). 1258 has the same form of question: are his humble words not the weapon that breaks our womanhood (பணிமொழி அன்றோ ... உடைக்கும் படை). In both a soft thing, tears or gentle speech, is named as the real weapon against something that looks strong.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1258","b":"k:985","type":"parallel_domain","note":"985 says that bowing is the strength of those who accomplish things and the weapon by which the worthy turn their opponents (பணிதல் ... மாற்றாரை மாற்றும் படை). 1258 calls her lover's lowly speech (பணிமொழி) the weapon that breaks her firmness. Humility as a படை is praised in the ethics of the worthy, and in the love poems the woman sees the same device used on her and calls its user a thief.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1260","b":"k:1259","type":"develops","note":"1259 narrates one occasion: she went away to sulk and embraced him when she saw her heart joining him. 1260 turns it into a general question about people whose hearts melt like fat in fire, and whether 'we will stand in a sulk' (ஊடி நிற்பேம் எனல்) is even open to them. The second verse gives the reason for the first.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1260","b":"k:1287","type":"agrees","note":"1287 asks what use it is to sulk when one knows the sulk will prove false (பொய்த்தல் அறிந்து என் புலந்து), like those who jump into a stream knowing it will carry them off. 1260 asks whether those with hearts like fat put in fire (நிணம் தீயில் இட்டன்ன நெஞ்சினார்) can ever say they will hold out in a sulk. One image is of being swept away and the other of melting, and both conclude that the sulk cannot be sustained.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1261","b":"k:776","type":"parallel_domain","note":"In 776 the warrior takes up his days (தன் நாளை எடுத்து) and files every day without a wound as wasted; in 1261 the wife marks off the days of his absence (நாளொற்றி) until her finger wears down. Both keep a ledger of days, the soldier's reckoned by wounds received and the waiting wife's by absence endured.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1261","b":"k:1269","type":"develops","note":"1261 shows the woman's finger worn (தேய்ந்த விரல்) from marking the days since he left (அவர்சென்ற நாளொற்றி); 1269 says that for those pining with the return day in mind (வருநாள்வைத்து ஏங்குபவர்க்கு) one day passes like seven. 1269 gives the felt arithmetic behind 1261's tally: the days she counts are each lived as many.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1263","b":"k:615","type":"develops","note":"615 praises the man who wants the work and not pleasure (இன்பம் விழையான் வினைவிழைவான்) as a pillar to his kin; in 1263 the wife describes her husband as gone desiring victory with his own resolve for company (உரன்நசைஇ உள்ளம் துணையாக), which Parimelazhagar glosses as not desiring enjoyment. 1263 shows the same man from inside the house: she stays alive only by desiring his return (வரல்நசைஇ இன்னும் உளேன்), the same verb நசைஇ set against his.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1263","b":"k:1268","type":"develops","note":"In 1263 she waits for the man who left wanting victory; in 1268, which Parimelazhagar assigns to the man away on the king's service, he wishes the king would engage and win (வினைகலந்து வென்றீக வேந்தன்) so that he can join his wife for the evening. On that reading the pair gives both ends of one separation: her longing for his return and his impatience for the work to finish.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1264","b":"k:476","type":"same_image","note":"476 warns that one who has climbed to the tip of a branch (நுனிக்கொம்பர் ஏறினார்) and pushes further loses his life; in 1264 her heart, thinking of his return, கோடுகொடு ஏறும், which Manakkudavar and Kalaignar read as climbing a tree's branches to look for him (Parimelazhagar reads it as swelling up like a tree putting out branches). On the climbing reading the same image marks reckless overreach in the book on statecraft and eager hope here.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1264","b":"k:1170","type":"develops","note":"In 1170 her heart goes to where he is, and she wishes her eyes could follow (உள்ளம்போன்று உள்வழிச் செல்கிற்பின்); in 1264 her heart rises thinking of his return (வரவுள்ளி). Both make the heart the part of her that moves toward him while she stays put; on Manakkudavar's reading of 1264 it even climbs to do the seeing her eyes cannot.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1265","b":"k:1187","type":"develops","note":"In 1187 pallor seizes her the moment she shifts a little in his embrace (புடைபெயர்ந்தேன் அவ்வளவில்); in 1265 she says the pallor on her shoulders will leave once she has seen him to her eyes' fill (கண்ணாரக் கண்டபின் நீங்கும்). Together they give pallor's rule in the Book of Love: it comes with the slightest distance and goes only with his presence, which Parimelazhagar underlines by noting that merely hearing of his return will not do.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1265","b":"k:1283","type":"agrees","note":"1283 says her eyes will not rest without seeing her husband (கொண்கனைக் காணாது அமையல கண்) even though he does as he pleases; 1265 prays to see the same கொண்கன் until her eyes are full. Both make the eyes' need to see him the thing that must be satisfied before anything else can mend.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1266","b":"k:1101","type":"develops","note":"1101 says all five senses find their objects in her; in 1266 she will 'drink' him (பருகுவன்), which Parimelazhagar explains as drinking that nectar through all five gateways of sense. On his reading 1266 is the woman's version of 1101, with the five-sense enjoyment compressed into a single verb.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1266","b":"k:1102","type":"agrees","note":"In 1102 the man says that medicine for a disease is normally something else, but she is herself the medicine for the sickness she causes (தன்நோய்க்குத் தானே மருந்து); in 1266 the woman says that if he comes for one day she will drink him in so that all her sickness is destroyed (பைதல்நோய் எல்லாம் கெட). Each lover names the other as both the cause and the only cure.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1267","b":"k:1109","type":"develops","note":"1109 lists sulking, reconciling and uniting (ஊடல் உணர்தல் புணர்தல்) as the rewards of those joined in love; 1267 shows the woman, before he has even returned, unable to choose between sulking and embracing and wondering whether to combine them. What 1109 states as a sequence of pleasures, 1267 shows as one impatient wish for all of them at once, which is how Parimelazhagar explains its place in this chapter.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1269","b":"k:1278","type":"same_image","note":"1269 says one day passes like seven (ஒருநாள் எழுநாள்போல்) for those awaiting the return day; in 1278 she says he left only yesterday (நெருநற்று) and she has been pale for seven days (எழுநாளேம்). Both use seven days to measure how separation distorts time, stretching a day of waiting in 1269 and running ahead of the departure itself in 1278.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1271","b":"k:71","type":"agrees","note":"71 asks if there is any bolt to shut love in, since the small tears of the loving announce it (புன்கணீர் பூசல் தரும்); 1271 says her eyes tell something despite her concealment. The claim that affection cannot be locked away and that the eyes give it out appears in the book on virtue as a general truth about அன்பு and in the Book of Love as a scene.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:1271","b":"k:709","type":"parallel_domain","note":"709 says the eye itself tells enmity and friendship (கண்ணுரைக்கும்) to those who know its kinds; in 1271 the man tells her that though she hides it, her eyes have slipped her control and have something to say (உண்கண் உரைக்கல் உறுவது). The minister's skill of reading the eye in the book on statecraft is the lover's skill here, with the same verb உரை for what the eye does.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1272","b":"k:1280","type":"develops","note":"1272 says this girl has a great measure of womanly nature (பெண்நிறைந்த நீர்மை பெரிது), which Parimelazhagar takes as artlessness beyond the usual; 1280 says that telling love's sickness with the eyes and pleading is womanhood added to womanhood (பெண்ணினால் பெண்மை உடைத்து). The chapter's first observation about her is given its content at the end: her womanliness is her saying it with the eyes and not in words.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1273","b":"k:1274","type":"develops","note":"1273 compares her unspoken meaning to a thread that shows through the gems (திகழ்தரு); 1274 compares it to the scent inside a closed bud (முகைமொக்குள் உள்ளது நாற்றம்). Parimelazhagar distinguishes them: the first lies within yet is visible outside, the second is not visible outside at all, so the pair moves from a sign that can be seen to one that can only be inferred.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1274","b":"k:1227","type":"same_image","note":"1227 describes love's sickness as budding in the morning (காலை அரும்பி), swelling through the day and flowering at evening; 1274 places an unspoken meaning inside the bud of her smile (நகைமொக்குள்). Both use the bud for a feeling that is already there and has not yet opened, pain in the one and a hidden intention in the other.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1275","b":"k:1091","type":"agrees","note":"1091 finds two looks in her eyes, one that gives the sickness and one that is its medicine (அந்நோய் மருந்து); 1275 finds in her covert behaviour a medicine that ends great sorrow (உறுதுயர் தீர்க்கும் மருந்து). In both the remedy is not anything she says but something to be read in a sign she gives.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1275","b":"k:1092","type":"same_image","note":"1092 values the small look her eyes steal (கண்களவு கொள்ளும் சிறுநோக்கம்) at more than half of love; 1275 says her கள்ளம், her stealth, contains a medicine. Theft is the figure for her indirect signalling in both, at first meeting in 1092 and after reunion in 1275.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1275","b":"k:1279","type":"develops","note":"1275 says the stealth with which she acted and withdrew (செய்திறந்த கள்ளம்) holds a medicine for great sorrow; 1279 states what she did (அஃதாண்டவள் செய்தது): she looked at her bangles, her shoulders and her feet. Parimelazhagar ties the two explicitly, taking 1279's அஃது to point back to 1275, so that the silent gesture is the 'stealth' and its meaning, going with him, is the cure.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1276","b":"k:827","type":"parallel_domain","note":"827 warns not to take an enemy's bowing words at face value, because a bow's bending signals harm (வில்வணக்கம் தீங்கு குறித்தமையான்); in 1276 the woman reads his unusually great tenderness (பெரிதாற்றிப் பெட்பக் கலத்தல்) as a sign of coming absence. The same inference, that excess of kindness points to its opposite, is advice about false friends in one book and a wife's reading of her husband in the other, though she alleges no enmity.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1278","b":"k:1277","type":"agrees","note":"1277 says her bangles knew of his parting before she did; 1278 says her body has been pale for seven days though he left only yesterday. The two adjacent speeches make the same point with different signs: her body responds to his intention to leave before the leaving is an acknowledged fact.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1279","b":"k:1093","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1093 reports a wordless act and reads it: she looked, and having looked bowed her head (நோக்கினாள் நோக்கி இறைஞ்சினாள்), and that (அஃது) was water poured on their bond; 1279 reports that she looked at her bangles, her shoulders and her feet, and that (அஃது) is what she did there. Both verses consist of a sequence of looks followed by அஃது, leaving the meaning to be drawn from the gesture, at courtship in 1093 and at the threat of parting in 1279.","grade":"sound","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1279","b":"k:1234","type":"develops","note":"1234 describes separation's effect: the shoulders lose their fullness and old beauty and the bangles slip (பைந்தொடி சோரும்… தோள்); in 1279 she looks at bangles and soft shoulders (தொடிநோக்கி மென்தோளும் நோக்கி) and then at her feet. Read with 1234, her first two glances name what will happen if she is left, and the third, as Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar take it, names her alternative of walking with him.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1280","b":"k:1053","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1053 says that even asking has a beauty of its own (இரப்புமோர் ஏஎர் உடைத்து) when done before those who know their duty and do not withhold; 1280 says her asking (இரவு), done with the eyes, has a womanliness of its own (பெண்மை உடைத்து). The word for begging is the same, and both grant it a grace under conditions, though the chapter on begging means alms and 1280 means a lover's plea.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1280","b":"k:1137","type":"agrees","note":"1137 says nothing has greater worth than womanhood that suffers a sea of desire and still does not mount the palmyra horse (மடலேறாப் பெண்ணின் பெருந்தக்கது இல்); 1280 says that telling love's sickness with the eyes and pleading (கண்ணினால் காமநோய் சொல்லி இரவு) is womanhood upon womanhood. Both praise as specifically womanly the refusal to declare desire openly, 1137 by what she will not do and 1280 by the silent means she uses instead.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1281","b":"k:1288","type":"same_image","note":"In 1281 love is set above liquor: it delights at a thought and a look, which liquor cannot do. In 1288, in the same chapter, his chest is itself like liquor to those who have got drunk on it (களித்தார்க்குக் கள்ளற்றே), wanted though it does shameful harm (இளித்தக்க இன்னா செயினும்). The chapter uses the one image both ways, first to say love exceeds drink and then to say it compels like drink.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1282","b":"k:433","type":"same_image","note":"433 says those ashamed of blame treat a fault as small as a millet as if it were a palmyra (தினைத்துணையாங் குற்றம்... பனைத்துணையாக் கொள்வர்). 1282 uses the same two measures in the other direction: with love as large as a palmyra, even a millet of sulking over his fault is to be avoided. In 433 the small fault is magnified; in 1282 the small grievance is to be dropped.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1282","b":"k:1302","type":"qualifies","note":"1302 says sulking is like salt in the right amount and is spoiled only by being prolonged a little (உப்பமைந் தற்றால் புலவி). 1282 says that when love is full to a palmyra's measure, not even a millet of sulking is wanted (தினைத்துணையும் ஊடாமை வேண்டும்). Read together, 1282 states a condition, காமம் நிறைய வரின், under which the measured sulk that 1302 recommends falls to nothing; Parimelazhagar takes 1282 as the woman voicing her own haste in the form of general advice.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1283","b":"k:1257","type":"qualifies","note":"1257 says she knows nothing of shame when the one she cherishes does, in desire, what she wants (பேணியார் பெட்ப செயின்). 1283 reuses the two words with the sense turned round: even if he does only what he himself wants, without cherishing her (பேணாது பெட்பவே செய்யினும்), her eyes will not rest without seeing him. The second verse removes the condition of the first: her yielding does not depend on his doing what pleases her.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1284","b":"k:1246","type":"develops","note":"1246 tells the heart that when it sees the lover it cannot stay in a sulk, so its present anger is false (பொய்க்காய்வு). 1284 is that prediction coming true: she set out to sulk and the heart forgot it and went to unite (அதுமறந்து கூடற்கண் சென்றது).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1284","b":"k:1294","type":"develops","note":"1284 reports to her friend that her heart forgot the sulk and went to unite. 1294 gives the consequence: since the heart will not first sulk and then enjoy (துனிசெய்து துவ்வாய்), who will consult with it about such things again (நின்னொடு சூழ்வார் யார்). The report of the heart's desertion in one chapter becomes the quarrel with the heart in the next.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1285","b":"k:190","type":"parallel_domain","note":"190 asks what harm could come if people saw their own faults as they see strangers' faults (ஏதிலார் குற்றம்போல் தங்குற்றங் காண்கிற்பின்). 1285 says she cannot see her husband's fault when he is before her, as the eye cannot see the kohl-stick touching it. In both, what is nearest is what goes unseen; 190 treats this as a failing to correct, and 1285 as a fact about love.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1285","b":"k:1286","type":"develops","note":"1285 gives the image: the eye does not see the stick that is lining it, and she does not see his blame when she sees him (பழிகாணேன் கண்ட இடத்து). 1286 states the other half that the image implies: when she does not see him she sees nothing but his faults (காணாக்கால் காணேன் தவறல்லவை). Parimelazhagar's gloss on 1285 already supplies this, saying the eye has seen the stick at every other time.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1286","b":"k:1297","type":"develops","note":"1286: seeing him, she sees no faults; not seeing him, she sees nothing else. In 1297 she calls her heart மடநெஞ்சு, and Parimelazhagar defines that மடமை as exactly this reversal: remembering when he is absent, and forgetting when he is present, faults that ought to be remembered at sight and forgotten in absence. On his reading 1297 names and blames what 1286 only describes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1287","b":"k:1134","type":"same_image","note":"1134: the fierce flood of love carries off the raft of shame and manliness (காமக் கடும்புனல் உய்க்கும்). 1287: why sulk knowing it will prove false, like those who leap into a stream knowing it will carry them off (உய்த்தல் அறிந்து புனல்பாய்பவர்). The same water and the same verb, உய்த்தல், are used by the man of his restraint in 1134 and by the woman of her sulk here.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1288","b":"k:940","type":"parallel_domain","note":"940: as the gambler loves the game more with each loss (இழத்தொறூஉம் காதலிக்கும் சூதேபோல்), the living being loves life more with each suffering. 1288: liquor is wanted by the drunk even as it does them shameful harm, and so is his chest. Both explain an attachment that grows through injury by a vice from the same group of chapters (dice in 940, liquor in 1288).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1288","b":"k:1145","type":"same_image","note":"1145: as drinking is wanted more each time one gets drunk (களித்தொறும் கள்ளுண்டல் வேட்டற்றால்), love is sweeter each time it is exposed. 1288: his chest is as liquor is to those who have got drunk on it (களித்தார்க்குக் கள்ளற்றே), though it does shameful harm. Both take the drinker's returning appetite as the likeness for love; 1288 adds the harm and the shame that 1145 leaves out.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1289","b":"k:1306","type":"develops","note":"1289 says few attain love's செவ்வி, its right moment or condition. 1306 gives a figure for missing it on either side: without துனி and புலவி love is like fruit over-ripe or unripe (கனியும் கருக்காயும் அற்று). Parimelazhagar places 1289 in a sulk that will not yield to appeasement, so the fineness it speaks of is the same fineness of timing that 1306 puts in terms of ripeness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1290","b":"k:1327","type":"develops","note":"1290 remembers her sulk ending because her eagerness to embrace was greater than his. 1327 states the rule the man later draws: in a lovers' quarrel the one who loses has won, as is seen in the union (ஊடலில் தோற்றவர் வென்றார்). Her giving way in 1290 is a case of the defeat that 1327 counts as victory.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1291","b":"k:72","type":"parallel_domain","note":"72: the loveless belong wholly to themselves, and the loving belong to others even in their bones (அன்பிலார் எல்லாம் தமக்குரியர்). 1291: his heart is for him (அவர்நெஞ்சு அவர்க்காதல்), so why, heart, are you not for me (நீ எமக்கு ஆகாதது). The woman's complaint is 72's distinction seen from the inside: the one who loves no longer owns her own heart, and the one who does not love keeps his.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1292","b":"k:1255","type":"agrees","note":"1255 says the dignity of not going after one who has turned away (செற்றார்பின் செல்லாப் பெருந்தகைமை) is something the lovesick cannot know. 1292 shows it: her heart goes to him (சேறி) though it has seen that he is not attached to her. 1255 gives the general statement and 1292 the instance, addressed to the heart that does the going.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1293","b":"k:752","type":"agrees","note":"752 says everyone scorns those who have nothing and everyone honours the rich (இல்லாரை எல்லாரும் எள்ளுவர்). 1293 has the woman quote the same worldly observation as a saying, கெட்டார்க்கு நட்டார் இல், and ask her heart whether that is why it leaves her and follows him. The pairing shows a hard fact from the chapter on wealth being used in the Book of Love as a taunt, with the woman as the ruined one and her heart as the fair-weather friend.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1293","b":"k:787","type":"qualifies","note":"787 defines friendship as turning a friend from ruin and, when ruin comes, sharing the suffering (அழிவின்கண் அல்லல் உழப்பதாம் நட்பு). The saying in 1293, that the ruined have no friends, describes how the world behaves and not what friendship is; by the standard of 787 her heart, which goes after him as it pleases (பெட்டாங்கு அவர்பின் செலல்), has failed as a friend. The woman's question, என்பதோ, shows she is quoting the saying to reproach, not endorsing it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1293","b":"k:798","type":"develops","note":"798 warns against taking the friendship of those who cut away in distress (அல்லற்கண் ஆற்றறுப்பார் நட்பு). In 1293 the one who deserts in distress is her own heart, which cannot be declined as a friend. The verse turns the advice of the friendship chapters into a predicament with no remedy.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1294","b":"k:1260","type":"agrees","note":"1260 asks whether those with hearts like fat in fire can say they will hold out in a sulk (புணர்ந்தூடி நிற்பேம் எனல்). 1294 tells the heart that it will not sulk first and enjoy afterwards, so no one will plan such things with it. Both say the intention to sulk cannot survive her own heart; 1294 adds that she will stop consulting it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1294","b":"k:1330","type":"develops","note":"1330 says sulking is the pleasure of love, and the pleasure of sulking is to come together and embrace (ஊடுதல் காமத்திற்கு இன்பம்). 1294 has the woman want exactly that order, first make a quarrel and then enjoy (துனிசெய்து துவ்வாய்), and complain that her heart will not do it. The book's closing statement of how a sulk should work is, in 1294, a plan she cannot carry out.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1296","b":"k:293","type":"parallel_domain","note":"293: do not lie about what your heart knows, for after the lie your own heart will burn you (தன்நெஞ்சே தன்னைச் சுடும்). 1296: thinking alone, she finds her own heart is there to eat her. In both the tormentor is one's own heart and the setting is solitude with what one knows; 293 makes this the working of conscience, and 1296 gives it as the experience of the abandoned lover, with no fault of hers involved.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1296","b":"k:1207","type":"agrees","note":"1207 says she does not know forgetting, and even remembering burns her heart (உள்ளினும் உள்ளம் சுடும்). 1296 says that when she sits alone and thinks (நினைத்தக்கால்), her heart consumes her. Both make thinking of him in his absence the source of the pain; in 1207 the heart is what is burned, in 1296 it is what does the harm.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1297","b":"k:1017","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1017 says those ruled by shame give up life for shame's sake and do not give up shame for life's sake (நாண்துறவார்). In 1297 the woman has forgotten shame because her heart cannot forget him, and Parimelazhagar glosses the shame as dearer to her than life, the valuation of 1017. The pairing sets the rule from the chapter on shame beside a speaker in the Book of Love who holds that rule and reports failing it; it is her confession, not a contrary teaching.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1297","b":"k:1132","type":"parallel_domain","note":"In 1132 the man says his body and life, unable to bear it, will mount the palmyra horse, with shame set aside (நாணினை நீக்கி நிறுத்து). In 1297 the woman says she has forgotten shame through her heart's inability to forget him. Each sex is shown losing நாண் to love; the man sets it aside to act in public, and the woman finds it gone.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1298","b":"k:967","type":"parallel_domain","note":"967 says it is better to be called ruined where one stands than to live by following those who do not accept one (ஒட்டார்பின் சென்றொருவன் வாழ்தலின்). In 1298 the heart, described as loving life (உயிர்க்காதல் நெஞ்சு), counts scorning him as the disgrace and goes on dwelling on him. The chapter on honour ranks honour above life; the heart in 1298 keeps the vocabulary of disgrace (இளிவு) but, out of attachment to life, applies it so as to stay with the one who slighted her. This is the heart's reasoning as she reports it, not a rival doctrine.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1298","b":"k:1184","type":"same_image","note":"1184: she thinks of him and what she speaks of is his merit (உரைப்பது அவர்திறம்), and still the pallor comes. 1298: her heart thinks of what pertains to him (அவர்திறம் உள்ளும்), judging that to scorn him would be a disgrace. The phrase அவர் திறம் with உள்ளுதல் appears in both; in 1184 it is her own loyal practice that goes unrewarded, and in 1298 it is something her heart does that she stands apart from.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1298","b":"k:1255","type":"qualifies","note":"1255 says the dignity of not going after one who has turned away is unknown to the lovesick (செற்றார்பின் செல்லாப் பெருந்தகைமை). In 1298 her heart reasons the opposite way about where disgrace lies: it thinks that to scorn him would be a disgrace (எள்ளின் இளிவாம் என்று எண்ணி) and so keeps thinking of his side. 1298 shows the lovesick heart not merely ignorant of that dignity, as 1255 has it, but supplied with its own argument from honour for doing without it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1299","b":"k:36","type":"parallel_domain","note":"36 says virtue, done without delay, is the companion that does not die when one dies (பொன்றுங்கால் பொன்றாத் துணை). 1299 says that in grief there is no companion if one's own heart is not one. Both verses ask what stays with a person when other support is gone; Book One answers virtue, and the lover's verse names the heart and finds it missing.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1299","b":"k:1263","type":"same_image","note":"1263 describes the man as having left with his own heart or resolve for companion (உள்ளம் துணையாகச் சென்றார்). 1299 asks who can be a companion in grief when one's own heart is no companion (நெஞ்சந் துணையல் வழி). The heart as துணை is what he had on setting out and, on Parimelazhagar's assignment of 1299 to the man, what he lacks when she will not be reconciled; Manakkudavar gives 1299 to the woman.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1299","b":"k:1300","type":"develops","note":"1299 asks who will be a companion in grief when one's own heart is not (தாமுடைய நெஞ்சந் துணையல் வழி). 1300 repeats the frame, தாமுடைய நெஞ்சம் தமரல் வழி, and draws the further step: then it is a small matter that strangers are not one's own. The pair moves from the loss of the nearest help to an inference about everyone else, and Parimelazhagar gives both to the man, where the first eight verses of the chapter are the woman's.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1300","b":"k:188","type":"parallel_domain","note":"188 asks what those who spread even the faults of their closest (துன்னியார்) will do to strangers (ஏதிலார் மாட்டு). 1300 argues that when one's own heart is not one's own, strangers (ஏதிலார்) not being one's own is a slight thing. Both reason from the nearest relation to ஏதிலார்: if the bond fails at the closest point, nothing can be expected further out.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1300","b":"k:882","type":"parallel_domain","note":"882 says do not fear enemies who are like swords, but fear the tie with enemies who are like kin (கேள்போல் பகைவர்). 1300 finds the same reversal inside one person: the heart that should be தமர், one's own people, is not, and beside that the distance of outsiders hardly counts. The lesson of the chapter on enmity within, that the danger is in what is closest, is applied in 1300 to the heart.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1300","b":"k:1291","type":"agrees","note":"1291 opens the chapter with the woman asking her heart why it is not hers when his heart is his own (நீ எமக்கு ஆகாதது). 1300 closes it with the general form: when one's own heart is not one's kin (நெஞ்சம் தமரல் வழி), it is nothing that strangers are not. Parimelazhagar gives the first to the woman and the last to the man, so the chapter begins and ends with the same complaint from each side.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1301","b":"k:1302","type":"qualifies","note":"1301 has the friend urge the woman to hold back her embrace and sulk (புல்லாது இராப் புலத்தை) so they can watch his pain 'a little' (சிறிது). 1302 states the limit that word implies: sulking is like salt in right measure, and letting it run on (நீள விடல்) is like salt in excess. Parimelazhagar reads the சிறிது of 1301 as already pointing to this rule.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1301","b":"k:1303","type":"develops","note":"1301 proposes not embracing (புல்லாது) in order to see his அல்லல்நோய். 1303 uses the same two words from the other side: leaving the one who sulks unembraced (புல்லா விடல்) is like giving அல்லல்நோய் to those already in distress (அலந்தாரை). Read together, the pain that 1301 treats as a brief test is the pain 1303 says must not be left standing.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1301","b":"k:1329","type":"agrees","note":"In 1301 the friend wants the sulk kept up so that his suffering can be seen for a while. In 1329 the man himself wishes she would go on sulking (ஊடுக மன்னோ) and the night grow long so that he may keep pleading (யாம் இரப்ப). Both sides treat the sulk as something to prolong on purpose, hers to watch and his to beg.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1302","b":"k:1306","type":"develops","note":"1302 gives the measure: a little sulking seasons, a little too long spoils. 1306 gives the two failures by name: without துனி and புலவி, love is like கனி and கருக்காய், which Manakkudavar reads as overripe fruit and unripe fruit. Together they make sulking a matter of degree, judged by taste, in both verses through a food image.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1303","b":"k:1304","type":"agrees","note":"1303 says that leaving unembraced those who have sulked (புலந்தாரைப் புல்லா விடல்) is like giving sharp pain to those already suffering (அலந்தாரை அல்லல்நோய் செய்தற்று). 1304 says that not appeasing those who sulk (ஊடியவரை உணராமை) is like cutting a withered creeper at the root (வாடிய வள்ளி முதல் அரிந்தற்று). The pair states one claim twice, once as added hurt and once as a finishing cut. Parimelazhagar takes both as the wife's taunt sending the man back to his other women.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1304","b":"k:1030","type":"same_image","note":"1304 compares leaving a sulking lover unappeased to cutting an already withered creeper at its root (வாடிய வள்ளி முதல் அரிந்தற்று). 1030 says a family with no good man to prop it falls when trouble cuts it at the foot (இடுக்கண் கால் கொன்றிட வீழும்); Parimelazhagar takes this as an axe at the tree's base. Both picture a weakened plant that needed support and got the cut instead, once in love and once in the household.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1305","b":"k:1321","type":"agrees","note":"1305, on Parimelazhagar's reading, has the man find beauty in her sulk even though he is good and blameless. 1321 gives her side: he has no fault (இல்லை தவறு அவர்க்கு), yet the way he shows tenderness (அவர் அளிக்குமாறு) is what makes sulking possible. Both verses detach the sulk from any real offence.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1305","b":"k:1325","type":"agrees","note":"1305 says that for men good in their qualities (நலத்தகை நல்லவர்க்கு) the beauty (ஏர்) is the sulking in flower-eyed women; Parimelazhagar reads this as 'even for the faultless, sulking is sweet'. 1325 says the same in plainer terms: though without fault (தவறிலர் ஆயினும்), being kept from the soft shoulders of the one they love has something in it (ஆங்கொன்று உடைத்து). Manakkudavar reads 1305 differently, taking நல்லவர் as the other women.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1306","b":"k:1330","type":"agrees","note":"1306 puts it negatively: without quarrels, serious and light, love is fruit at the wrong stage. 1330 puts it positively: sulking is the pleasure of love (ஊடுதல் காமத்திற்கு இன்பம்), and the pleasure of sulking is the embrace that follows. The chapter 133 verse states as a conclusion what 1306 argues by image.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1307","b":"k:1152","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1307 finds a pain inside sulking (ஊடலின் உண்டு ஆங்கு ஓர் துன்பம்): the thought of whether union will be delayed or not. In 1152 the woman says union itself holds the misery of fearing separation (பிரிவு அஞ்சும் புன்கண் உடைத்தால் புணர்வு). In both, a state counted as pleasant carries worry about what comes next.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1307","b":"k:1330","type":"qualifies","note":"1330 calls sulking the pleasure of love, provided the embrace follows (கூடி முயங்கப் பெறின்). 1307 fastens on that proviso: because one cannot be sure the union will not be long delayed (புணர்வது நீடுவது அன்றுகொல்), the sulk has its own pain. 1307 is the reservation built into 1330's praise.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1308","b":"k:877","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1308 asks what use it is to suffer where there is no lover who knows 'this one is hurt' (நொந்தார் என்று அஃது அறியும் காதலர் இல்லா வழி). 877, in the chapter on enemies, advises: do not voice your hurt to those who do not know you are hurt (நோவற்க நொந்தது அறியார்க்கு). The same roots நோ and அறி carry one rule in love and in statecraft: displayed pain is wasted on someone who does not perceive it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1308","b":"k:1200","type":"agrees","note":"In 1308 suffering is pointless when the other does not know or care that one suffers. In 1200 the woman tells her heart that reporting its great pain to one who is not close (உறாஅர்க்கு உறுநோய் உரைப்பாய்) is like trying to fill in the sea. Man and woman reach the same judgment from opposite sides of the couple.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1308","b":"k:1309","type":"develops","note":"1308 says the man's hurt is useless without a lover who recognises it. 1309 gives the general form: as water is sweet in shade, sulking is sweet only with those who love (வீழுநர் கண்ணே இனிது). Parimelazhagar assigns both to the man, in a sulk that she is not answering.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1309","b":"k:1323","type":"agrees","note":"1309 limits the sweetness of sulking to those who love (வீழுநர் கண்ணே). 1323 asks whether any heaven matches sulking with those joined like water with earth (நிலத்தொடு நீர் இயைந்தன்னார் அகத்து). Both say the value of the sulk depends on the closeness of the two, and both use water to say it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1310","b":"k:1255","type":"agrees","note":"1310 calls the heart's wish to join with one who neglects it mere craving. In 1255 the woman says the dignity of not going after those who have turned away (செற்றார் பின் செல்லாப் பெருந்தகைமை) is something the love-sick do not know. Both admit that desire overrides self-respect toward an indifferent partner.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1310","b":"k:1292","type":"agrees","note":"In 1310 the man, on Parimelazhagar's reading, says that his heart's intent to join with one who leaves him to wither in the sulk (ஊடல் உணங்க விடுவாரோடு) is just craving (அவா). In 1292 the woman scolds her heart for going to him even after seeing that he is not close to her (உறாஅதவர்க் கண்ட கண்ணும்). Each blames the heart for running to someone who has shown no care.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1310","b":"k:1304","type":"same_image","note":"1304 compares the unappeased sulker to a withered creeper (வாடிய வள்ளி) cut at the root. 1310 speaks of being left to dry up in the sulk (ஊடல் உணங்க விடுவார்). The wife's warning and the man's complaint use the same picture of a plant left without water; Parimelazhagar places 1310 in a sulk she does not answer.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1312","b":"k:1253","type":"same_image","note":"In 1312 the woman says he sneezed while she was sulking, knowing (அறிந்து) she would have to say 'live long' and so speak to him. In 1253 the sneeze is the example of what happens without intention: desire comes out குறிப்பின்றித் தும்மல்போல். Parimelazhagar notes that 1312 treats a natural sneeze as a deliberate one, which is the subtlety the chapter title names.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1313","b":"k:1311","type":"develops","note":"1311 gives her stated ground: every woman takes him in with her eyes (கண்ணின் பொது உண்பர்). 1313 shows the same worry applied to a trifle: a wreath must have been put on to be shown to another woman (ஒருத்தியைக் காட்டிய சூடினீர்). Her jealousy in both is about who sees him.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1313","b":"k:1321","type":"develops","note":"In 1313 the man reports that even wearing a wreath makes her angry (காயும்): 'you wore it to show some woman'. In 1321 the woman says he has no fault (இல்லை தவறு அவர்க்கு), yet his way of showing tenderness can bring on a sulk. 1321 supplies from her mouth what the man's complaints in chapter 132 imply: the grounds are invented.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1314","b":"k:645","type":"parallel_domain","note":"In 1314 he says 'we love more than anyone' (யாரினும் காதலம்) and she turns it on him: 'than whom, than whom?' (யாரினும் யாரினும்). 645 advises speaking a word only after knowing there is no other word that can defeat it (வெல்லுஞ்சொல் இன்மை அறிந்து). The love scene shows that rule failing in a comic way: his own word, repeated back, is the one that beats him.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1315","b":"k:107","type":"parallel_domain","note":"In 1315 a promise limited to this birth (இம்மை) makes her weep. 107 says the good remember through all seven births (எழுமை எழுபிறப்பும் உள்ளுவர்) the friendship of those who removed their distress. Her complaint measures his promise against the span the book itself uses for lasting bonds.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1315","b":"k:1314","type":"agrees","note":"In 1314 'more than anyone' is heard as implying other loves; in 1315 'we will not part in this birth' (இம்மைப் பிறப்பில் பிரியலம்) fills her eyes with tears, because she hears a parting in the next. Parimelazhagar describes the move in 1315: she takes a plain statement as a hinting one. Both verses turn a strong assurance into an offence by what it leaves unsaid.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1316","b":"k:1207","type":"agrees","note":"1316 has her treat remembering as proof of forgetting. In 1207 the woman, alone, says she does not know forgetting (மறப்பறியேன்) and that even remembering burns. The standard she holds him to in 1316, never having forgotten at all, is the one she claims for herself in separation.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1317","b":"k:1312","type":"develops","note":"In 1312 she suspects his sneeze was a trick to get the customary 'live long' out of her. In 1317 she gives that blessing (வழுத்தினாள்) and at once takes it back (அழித்து அழுதாள்) with a new suspicion. One sneeze is read two ways in the chapter, as a stratagem and as proof of another woman.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1318","b":"k:1317","type":"develops","note":"In 1317 sneezing brings the charge that some woman is thinking of him. In 1318 he holds the sneeze back (தும்முச் செறுப்ப) and she weeps anyway: 'are you hiding from me that your people think of you?' (நுமர் உள்ளல் எம்மை மறைத்திரோ). The two verses close both exits: sneezing and not sneezing are both faults.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1319","b":"k:693","type":"parallel_domain","note":"In 1319 even his effort to appease her makes her angry (உணர்த்தினும் காயும்): 'you must be like this with others too'. 693 warns those who serve kings that once suspicion has arisen, clearing it is hard for anyone (கடுத்தபின் தேற்றுதல் யார்க்கும் அரிது). The court maxim and the bedroom scene describe the same trap: reassurance offered to a suspicious mind is taken as more evidence.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1319","b":"k:1304","type":"qualifies","note":"1304 says not appeasing one who sulks (ஊடியவரை உணராமை) is like cutting a withered creeper at the root. In 1319 he does appease her (தன்னை உணர்த்தினும்) and that too is a fault. Taken together, the man is blamed in chapter 131 for not doing what chapter 132 shows cannot be done to her satisfaction.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:1320","b":"k:1311","type":"develops","note":"In 1311 her objection is to other women's eyes on him (கண்ணின் பொது உண்பர்). In 1320 it is to his eyes on her, which she thinks are really on another woman; Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar both explain that she supposes he is matching her limbs against someone else's. The chapter opens and closes on the look as the place where she locates unfaithfulness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1320","b":"k:1313","type":"agrees","note":"1313, 1319 and 1320 share the frame '…even if I do X, she is angry (…இனும் காயும்), saying …'. In 1313 the act is wearing a wreath; in 1320 it is only sitting and looking at her (நினைத்திருந்து நோக்கினும்), which she takes as comparing her with someone (யார் உள்ளி நோக்கினீர்). The series runs from adornment down to a silent look, so that no act of his is left free of suspicion.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1321","b":"k:1311","type":"develops","note":"In 1311 the woman refuses his chest because all women 'feast on it in common with their eyes' (கண்ணின் பொதுஉண்பர்), a sulk with a stated ground. In 1321 she grants there is no fault at all and says his tenderness itself brings the sulk on; Parimelazhagar explains this as her thought that others too might receive that same tenderness. On his reading the jealousy of 1311 survives in 1321 with its ground removed.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1321","b":"k:1325","type":"agrees","note":"In 1321 the woman says he has no fault (இல்லை தவறவர்க்கு), yet the way he shows tenderness (அவர் அளிக்குமாறு) is able to produce a sulk. In 1325 the man says that even when men are faultless (தவறிலர் ஆயினும்), being kept from the soft arms of the one they love 'has a certain something' (ஆங்கொன்று உடைத்து). The chapter opens each speaker's side with the same concession: the sulk it praises needs no offence.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1322","b":"k:1010","type":"same_image","note":"Both verses use the rare word சிறுதுனி for a brief bad spell in something good. In 1010 the short want of the renowned rich is like the rain-cloud going dry (மாரி வறங்கூர்ந்தனையது); in 1322 the small pain that sulking brings may make his good tenderness droop (நல்லளி வாடினும்), yet it gains worth (பாடு பெறும்). In each the dryness is temporary and belongs to a giver whose giving is not in doubt.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1322","b":"k:1304","type":"qualifies","note":"1322 says tenderness may wilt (வாடினும்) under the small pain of a sulk and still come to greatness. 1304 uses the same verb for the limit: not reconciling one who has sulked is like cutting an already wilted creeper at the root (வாடிய வள்ளி முதலரிந்தற்று). The wilting is harmless only if the sulk is ended; Parimelazhagar reads 1304 as the woman speaking of his other women.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1322","b":"k:1306","type":"develops","note":"1306 says love without துனி and புலவி is like fruit over-ripe or unripe (கனியும் கருக்காயும் அற்று), so some quarrel is needed. 1322 names the amount: only the 'small துனி' that arises in a sulk (ஊடலின் தோன்றும் சிறுதுனி), which leaves his tenderness greater even if it droops for a while. Parimelazhagar on 1306 takes துனி as the full-grown quarrel and புலவி as the young one, so 1322's சிறு marks the tolerable dose.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1324","b":"k:828","type":"parallel_domain","note":"828 warns that a weapon lies hidden inside an enemy's hands folded in worship (தொழுதகையுள்ளும் படையொடுங்கும்), and his tears are the same. 1324 finds a weapon (படை) inside the lovers' sulk, which Parimelazhagar explains as the other's bowing and humble words. Submission as a concealed weapon is a danger to be detected in false friendship and, in love, the means by which the sulk is ended.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1324","b":"k:1251","type":"develops","note":"1251 says the axe of desire breaks the door of restraint bolted with shame (காமக் கணிச்சி உடைக்கும் நிறையென்னும் ... கதவு). 1324 uses the same verb for the resolve to keep sulking: a weapon arises that breaks the heart (உள்ளம் உடைக்கும் படை). What was a breach forced during separation in 1251 becomes, inside the sulk, a breaking that leads to the embrace that does not let go (புல்லி விடா).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1325","b":"k:1103","type":"same_image","note":"Both verses turn on the same phrase, 'the soft arms of the one they love' (தாம்வீழ்வார் மென்றோள்). In 1103 sleeping on them is set against the world of the lotus-eyed one; in 1325 being kept away from them (அகறலின்), though one is faultless, 'has a certain something' (ஆங்கொன்று உடைத்து). The repeated phrase shows the book's last chapter finding a pleasure in the very absence of what the first union praised.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1325","b":"k:1307","type":"qualifies","note":"1325 says that being kept from her arms by a sulk has a pleasure of its own (ஆங்கொன்று உடைத்து). 1307 says sulking also holds one pain: the thought of whether union will be delayed or not (புணர்வது நீடுவது அன்றுகொல் என்று). Parimelazhagar gives both to the man, 1325 after the making up and 1307 during the sulk, so the pleasure of 1325 is one judged in hindsight.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1325","b":"k:1319","type":"develops","note":"In 1319 the man reports that even when he tries to appease her she is angry (உணர்த்தினும் காயும்), saying he is like this with others too: a complaint that he is blamed without cause. In 1325 he grants the same situation, a man without fault (தவறிலர் ஆயினும்) kept from her arms, and finds something to enjoy in it. The groundless sulks listed in chapter 132 are re-read as a pleasure in chapter 133.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1326","b":"k:944","type":"parallel_domain","note":"944 instructs: know that the earlier meal is digested (அற்றது அறிந்து) and eat only when thoroughly hungry (துவரப் பசித்து). 1326 says the digesting of what was eaten is sweeter than eating (உணலினும் உண்டது அறல் இனிது), and so sulking is sweeter than union. The chapter on medicine states as a rule of health what the man uses as his simile; Parimelazhagar on 1326 draws the same line, that one who eats when hungry eats more and with more relish.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1326","b":"k:1330","type":"qualifies","note":"1326 ranks sulking above union: ஊடல் is sweeter than புணர்தல் for love. 1330 calls sulking love's pleasure but adds that the pleasure of the sulk itself lies in being able to come together and embrace (கூடி முயங்கப் பெறின்). The closing verse makes the sulk's sweetness depend on the union that 1326 placed below it, as digestion has value for the next meal.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1327","b":"k:931","type":"parallel_domain","note":"931 says not to want gambling even if you win, since what is won (வென்றதூஉம்) is like the hook a fish has swallowed. 1327 says that in a sulk the loser is the winner (தோற்றவர் வென்றார்). The two verses reverse the apparent result of a contest in opposite directions: the gambler's win is a loss that shows later, and the lover's loss is a win that shows later in union (கூடலிற் காணப்படும்).","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1328","b":"k:802","type":"same_image","note":"802 says intimate liberty is a limb of friendship and that 'being உப்பு to it' (அதற்கு உப்பாதல்) is the duty of the worthy; Parimelazhagar reads this as being pleasant toward that liberty. 1328 uses உப்பு for the savour of union after a sulk, which Parimelazhagar again glosses as sweetness. In both, salt stands for the relish that makes something a close companion does, a liberty or a sulk, enjoyable and not an offence.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1328","b":"k:1238","type":"same_image","note":"In 1238 the man recalls that when he loosened the arms that embraced her, her forehead turned pale (பசந்தது ... பேதை நுதல்). In 1328 he recalls union so intense that her forehead sweated (நுதல் வெயர்ப்ப). Her forehead is the place where he reads both the first sign of separation and the fullness of union.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1329","b":"k:1052","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1052 says even begging is a pleasure to a person if what is begged for comes without pain (இன்பம் ஒருவற்கு இரத்தல்). In 1329 the man wants the sulk and the night prolonged so that he may beg (யாம் இரப்ப), treating the pleading itself as something to enjoy. Begging, which chapter 107 calls degrading, is here a pleasure because the giver is not in doubt, which is the condition 1052 sets.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1329","b":"k:1307","type":"qualifies","note":"1307 names the one pain within sulking: the thought of whether union will be delayed or not (புணர்வது நீடுவது அன்றுகொல்). 1329 wishes for that delay, asking that she keep sulking and the night grow long (ஊடுக ... நீடுக). Parimelazhagar gives both to the man, 1307 during a sulk and 1329 after one has ended well, so the delay feared in the moment is desired in retrospect.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1330","b":"k:1302","type":"qualifies","note":"1302 says a sulk is like salt in the right amount, and letting it run long (நீள விடல்) is like a little too much. 1330 makes the sulk love's pleasure only if embrace follows (கூடி முயங்கப் பெறின்). Parimelazhagar reads 1330's condition through this measure: the sulk must be ended at the right point, between a quarrel too ripe and one too young, and that is hard to judge.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1330","b":"k:1304","type":"qualifies","note":"1330 says the sulk's own pleasure comes if the lovers get to unite and embrace (பெறின்). 1304 states the other case: not reconciling one who has sulked (ஊடியவரை உணராமை) is like cutting a wilted creeper at the root. The conditional in 1330 is a real one, and 1304 shows what a sulk is when the condition fails.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:1","b":"k:332","type":"agrees","note":"Both books make wealth's coming and going a spectacle: the Kural's crowd gathering and dispersing at a show, the Naladiyar's feaster turned beggar.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:2","b":"k:333","type":"agrees","note":"Both draw the same conclusion from wealth's instability: act on it at once (Kural 333) or share it from the day it comes (here).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:3","b":"n:21","type":"same_image","note":"The king on the elephant's neck under the royal umbrella appears in both. Here he falls when his deeds turn; in 21 he simply dies like everyone else.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:4","b":"k:335","type":"agrees","note":"Both urge good deeds before death stops them. The Kural pictures the hiccup that silences the tongue; this quatrain the angry approach of Death.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:5","b":"k:36","type":"agrees","note":"Both reject 'later': the Kural says virtue is the companion that does not perish when you do, and this quatrain says those who gave first escape Death's road.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:6","b":"k:269","type":"tension","note":"Kural 269 says those with the power of austerity can leap past Death (கூற்றம் குதித்தலும்); this quatrain says no one here has leapt past Death and escaped. The conflict holds on Parimelazhagar's literal reading of 269; Kalaignar's figurative 'conquering death' softens it. Mudaliar cites 269 here.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:8","b":"k:331","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 331 calls taking the impermanent for permanent the lowest புல்லறிவு; this quatrain shows the புல்லறிவாளர் at it, with wealth that flashes and vanishes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:9","b":"k:1005","type":"agrees","note":"Both say that wealth neither enjoyed nor given is as good as none: in the Kural such a man has nothing, here he is thought to have lost it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:10","b":"k:1009","type":"agrees","note":"Both say wealth amassed by denying oneself (Kural தற்செற்று, here உடம்பு செற்றும்) and neglecting virtue goes to others. The Naladiyar adds the honey bee as witness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:11","b":"k:46","type":"tension","note":"Here the wise renounce the world even in childhood. Kural 46 asks what a person living household life by virtue would gain by going the outer way. The conflict depends on reading புறத்தாறு as the ascetic path, as Parimelazhagar and Pope do, and துறந்தார் here as world-renunciation, as Shanmuganar does.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:11","b":"k:342","type":"develops","note":"Kural 342 says renounce while there is time; this quatrain pushes the right time back to infancy, because age is certain.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:17","b":"n:44","type":"same_image","note":"Both take the spear-like eye of conventional praise and undo it: 17 by age, which makes it a staff-guided eye, 44 by anatomy, which makes it a scooped-out palm fruit.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:19","b":"k:36","type":"agrees","note":"Both quote the procrastinator's words, 'we will know it later' (மற்றறிவாம் here, அன்றறிவாம் in the Kural), and reject them. The Naladiyar's reason is that the young die too.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:22","b":"k:38","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the phrase வீழ்நாள் படா- for a day not lost. In the Kural it is the good deed done daily; here it is the sun that never fails to rise and so measures out a life. The same words point to opposite sides of the account.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:22","b":"n:7","type":"same_image","note":"Both make the sun the measuring vessel of one's days; in 7 Death does the measuring and eating, in 22 the measure alone is warning enough to do ஒப்புரவு.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:23","b":"n:6","type":"same_image","note":"The funeral drum sounds in both: in 6 it may sound tomorrow for the hoarder, in 23 it replaces the wedding drum on the same day.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:26","b":"k:340","type":"agrees","note":"Both treat the life as a temporary occupant of the body, a lodger in the Kural and a dancer in a skin bag here, so the body is not its home.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:27","b":"k:349","type":"agrees","note":"Both join seeing impermanence to ending the round of births; on Mudaliar's reading of தடுமாற்றம், the wise here resolve to end what Kural 349 says ends when attachment ends.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:28","b":"n:34","type":"agrees","note":"Both say the rare body must be used to win its fruit before it goes; 34 names that fruit as virtue and adds the sugar-cane image.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:29","b":"k:336","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 336 says one here yesterday is gone today; this quatrain shrinks the span to a sentence, stood, sat, lay down, gone, and draws the command to act now.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:30","b":"k:338","type":"same_image","note":"Both picture the life leaving the body as a bird leaving its nest. The Kural makes it a comment on body and life; the Naladiyar applies it to kinsfolk, who leave the body behind for their family.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:31","b":"k:37","type":"agrees","note":"Both read present fortune as the visible fruit of virtue: the Kural's palanquin rider and bearer, the Naladiyar's insider and the one at the gate. The Naladiyar adds that the insider's place is lost when the merit is not renewed.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:32","b":"k:32","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 32 says no ruin exceeds forgetting virtue (மறத்தல்); this quatrain stages the heart that forgets virtue (அறமறந்து) for wealth and asks it what it will do now.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:33","b":"k:379","type":"agrees","note":"Both make the arrival of past deeds' fruit an occasion for equanimity; the Naladiyar goes further and makes that acceptance the way out of rebirth's confusion.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:34","b":"k:1078","type":"same_image","note":"Both use crushed sugar-cane. In the Kural the base yield only when crushed, a reproach; here the body should be crushed for its juice, a counsel.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:35","b":"k:36","type":"agrees","note":"Both say virtue gathered in time stays when the body perishes; the Kural calls it an undying companion, the Naladiyar the jaggery kept when the cane refuse burns.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:36","b":"k:337","type":"agrees","note":"Both turn on not knowing when death comes: the Kural mocks those who plan crores of things without knowing they will live another moment, and this quatrain answers with Death standing behind.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:37","b":"n:22","type":"qualifies","note":"22 urges ஒப்புரவு before one's days end; 37 warns against an ஒப்புரவு done only for the body. Together they mark the word's proper object as others, not oneself.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"n:38","b":"k:104","type":"agrees","note":"Both make a small good large: the Kural's millet-sized help counted as a palmyra by those who know its worth, the Naladiyar's banyan seed. The Naladiyar places the growth in the worthiness of the recipient.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"n:39","b":"k:334","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 334 says the day looks like a thing but is a saw cutting life, to those who see it; this quatrain describes those who do not see it, and rejoice at each day.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:40","b":"k:968","type":"agrees","note":"Both argue that keeping the body alive at the cost of dignity buys nothing, since the body does not last: the Kural asks if it is a medicine, the Naladiyar makes the bargain conditional on a body that never dies.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:41","b":"k:340","type":"same_image","note":"Both call the body a புக்கில், a lodging; Mudaliar even glosses with துச்சில், the Kural's word. The Kural uses it to show the life has no home of its own; the Naladiyar to show the body is a mean hut not worth desiring.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:42","b":"k:80","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 80 says a body without love is bone wrapped in skin, implying love makes it more. The Naladiyar treats every body as filth wrapped in skin and allows no such exception.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"n:44","b":"k:1114","type":"same_image","note":"The water-lily as a figure for the eye appears in both. In Kural 1114 the lover says the lily would bow before her eyes; here the renunciant calls those who make that comparison eyeless.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:45","b":"k:1113","type":"tension","note":"Kural 1113's lover calls her teeth pearls and her eyes spears; this quatrain calls those who praise teeth as pearls untaught and petty, and answers with bones in the burning ground. The conflict holds if the Kural's Book of Love is read as valuing the lover's praise, not only as dramatised speech.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"n:48","b":"k:913","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 913 likens the embrace of women who sell themselves to embracing a corpse in the dark; the Naladiyar extends the corpse to every admired body, which the Kural does not.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"n:49","b":"n:50","type":"same_image","note":"In both the skulls laugh and teach: in 49 they speak to all the living, in 50 they cure the infatuated in particular.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:50","b":"k:345","type":"agrees","note":"Both conclude that the body is not worth valuing: for those ending births even the body is excess in Kural 345, and those cured by the skulls here count themselves as nothing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:51","b":"n:93","type":"develops","note":"Both make good fortune last only as long as the merit behind it: in 51 evil returns when good deeds are exhausted, and in 93 wealth leaves when the deeds that sent it run out (வினையுலந்தக்கால்).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:52","b":"k:266","type":"agrees","note":"Mudaliar quotes Kural 266 here: those who do austerity do their own work (தங்கருமம்), and the rest waste themselves. 52 names the waste as endless scholarship.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:52","b":"k:397","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 397 asks why anyone stays unlearned until death. 52 calls lifelong study of grammar and astrology madness. They are compatible only if the Kural's learning is not the instrumental lore 52 mocks; read together, 52 limits which study is worth a life.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:52","b":"n:135","type":"develops","note":"Mudaliar points to 135, where learning has no shore and life is short, so one must choose what to learn. 52 draws the harder conclusion that endless study is folly when death is near.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:53","b":"k:342","type":"agrees","note":"Both urge renouncing while there is time: 342 says renounce while it is still possible, and 53 has the wise renounce without delay once they see that everything fails to last.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:54","b":"k:46","type":"tension","note":"Kural 46 asks what one would gain by leaving a household life lived in virtue. 54 says the wise, seeing the pain in household pleasure, keep off the household way. Both answer whether household life is a path the wise should stay on, and they answer opposite ways. The conflict holds unless 54 is read as describing only the fools' pleasure-seeking household, not a virtuous one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:55","b":"k:335","type":"agrees","note":"Both press for the good before the body fails: 335 says do good before the death-rattle stops the tongue, and 55 urges the heart to set out now that youth is gone and age and disease are near.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:56","b":"k:52","type":"develops","note":"Kural 52 says a household is nothing if the wife lacks household excellence. 56 starts from the same case and adds that she can't be put away, then draws a conclusion the Kural never draws: marriage itself is to be shunned.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:57","b":"k:267","type":"agrees","note":"Both make hardship the test of austerity: 267 has the ascetic shine like gold in the fire, and 57 counts as blessed only those who hold their vows when unbearable troubles strike.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:58","b":"k:157","type":"agrees","note":"Parimelazhagar glosses the pity of Kural 157 (நோநொந்து) by quoting this quatrain: grieving that the wrongdoer may fall into the fiery hell.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:59","b":"k:24","type":"agrees","note":"Both make control of the five senses the way to release: 24 calls the one who guards the five with firmness a seed for the highest ground, and 59 says the one who steers their craving reaches வீடு.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:60","b":"n:54","type":"agrees","note":"The two share a line and an argument. 54 ends with leaving household life and 60 with refusing to cling to pleasure as it comes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:60","b":"k:629","type":"agrees","note":"Both tie freedom from pain to not craving pleasure: 629 says one who does not want the pleasure within pleasure feels no pain within pain, and 60 has the higher ones see the pain in each pleasure and let it go.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:61","b":"k:151","type":"agrees","note":"Both find the model of bearing scorn in something below us: 151 in the earth that holds up those who dig it, 61 in the fly that climbs on one's head.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:62","b":"k:969","type":"tension","note":"Kural 969 praises those who, like the yak, give up life when honour is lost. 62 says those set on finishing their work don't give up life whenever disgrace comes. Both answer whether disgrace is a reason to die. The conflict holds if 969's மானம் covers the humiliation 62 has in mind; 62 limits itself to those with a task to complete.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:63","b":"k:129","type":"same_image","note":"Both make speech a burn. In 129 the tongue's burn on the hearer never heals; in 63 careless words burn the speaker without end.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:64","b":"k:153","type":"agrees","note":"153 calls bearing with the ignorant the strength of strengths; 64 shows the excellent doing it and the base failing at it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:65","b":"k:301","type":"agrees","note":"Both count restraint only where it costs something: 301 says one who checks anger where it could take effect truly checks it, and 65 that true patience belongs to one with the power to punish.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:65","b":"n:91","type":"agrees","note":"65 calls the gift of one without wealth the true gift; 91 promises open doors in the next world to those who give gladly even when they have little.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:66","b":"k:159","type":"agrees","note":"Both praise bearing the bitter words of those who overstep. 159 likens such people to renunciants in purity; 66 credits their lineage with the restraint.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:67","b":"k:312","type":"agrees","note":"Both make not returning harm to one who has harmed you the mark of the good. 67 adds that this is not weakness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:68","b":"k:29","type":"qualifies","note":"Both make the anger of the good brief. Kural 29, in Parimelazhagar's reading, adds that even while it lasts it can't be withstood; 68 stresses only that it cools of itself, leaving out its force.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"n:69","b":"k:987","type":"agrees","note":"987 asks what worth is for if it doesn't do good even to those who did harm; 69 says the well-born do exactly that, even to the ungrateful.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:71","b":"k:797","type":"agrees","note":"Both make getting away from fools a gain: 797 calls leaving a fool's friendship profit, and 71 advises slipping away rather than talking with one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:72","b":"n:64","type":"agrees","note":"Both open with the same situation in nearly the same words (நீரல்ல சொல்லியக்கால்): 64 shows the excellent not growing angry, and 72 adds that the world counts retaliation as disgrace.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:72","b":"k:155","type":"agrees","note":"Both make public regard the measure: 155 says people count those who retaliate as nothing and keep those who forbear like gold, and 72 that the world takes retaliation as disgrace.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:73","b":"k:784","type":"agrees","note":"784 says friendship exists to rebuke a friend who oversteps, not to laugh together. 73 values those harsh words above an outsider's pleasant ones.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:74","b":"k:428","type":"agrees","note":"Both use the phrase 'fear what should be feared' (அஞ்சுவது அஞ்சு-): 428 makes it the work of the wise, and 74 lists it among the marks of those who live free of suffering.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:75","b":"n:76","type":"tension","note":"Adjacent quatrains on a friend who wrongs you: 75 allows quiet withdrawal once one can bear no more, and 76 forbids abandoning a close friend and says to blame oneself. The conflict holds if both concern the same wrong; it weakens if 75's தேற்றா ஒழுக்கம் is the friend's general misconduct rather than hurt done to oneself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:75","b":"k:188","type":"agrees","note":"Both forbid broadcasting a close friend's faults with the same verb (தூற்று-): 188 condemns those who spread even their intimates' faults, and 75 says to withdraw without doing so.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:76","b":"k:807","type":"agrees","note":"Both hold that close friends are not cast off for the harm they do: 807 says those bound by love keep it even when friends bring ruin, and 76 says to blame oneself rather than leave.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:78","b":"k:1067","type":"agrees","note":"Both say that if one must ask, ask only the right people: 1067 pleads with beggars not to ask those who conceal, and 78 forbids telling one's want to the graceless.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:78","b":"k:1064","type":"agrees","note":"1064 praises the worth that won't consent to begging even with nothing left; 78 implies the same ranking, since only those who lack the resolve to die tell their need at all.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies"},{"a":"n:79","b":"k:656","type":"agrees","note":"Both put blamelessness above the strongest pull: 656 forbids a deed the worthy would blame even to feed one's starving mother, and 79 forbids blameworthy pleasure even if it would never end.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:80","b":"k:205","type":"agrees","note":"Both refuse to let ruin excuse wrongdoing: 205 says not to do evil because one is poor, and 80 says not to plot the ruin of the worthy even when one is ruined oneself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:82","b":"k:146","type":"develops","note":"146 lists four things that never leave the adulterer: enmity, sin, fear and blame. 82 keeps the same four and pairs them with four goods he loses: virtue, renown, friendship and greatness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:84","b":"k:148","type":"agrees","note":"148 calls not looking at another's wife great manliness (பேராண்மை); 84 calls the act unmanliness (ஆணின்மை).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:86","b":"k:148","type":"agrees","note":"Both turn on the look (நோக்கு): 148 praises the manliness that does not look at another's wife, and 86 asks why a married man would look at all.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:88","b":"k:1134","type":"tension","note":"In 1134 the flood of desire carries off the raft of shame and manliness. In 88 the strong, fearing shame, keep desire hidden until it subsides. Both make claims about whether shame can hold desire, and they answer opposite ways. The conflict depends on reading 1134 as a general claim; it is a lover's plea in the Book of Love, while 88 states a rule about the strong.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies"},{"a":"n:89","b":"k:129","type":"same_image","note":"Both contrast an outer burn with an inner one: in 129 the fire's wound heals but the tongue's scar does not, and in 89 arrow, fire and sun burn the body while desire burns the mind.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:90","b":"k:1159","type":"same_image","note":"Both say desire burns where fire can't: 1159 says fire burns only when touched while love burns at a distance, and 90 that fire can be escaped in water but desire can't.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:90","b":"k:1049","type":"parallel_domain","note":"The same figure in another domain: 1049 says one can sleep in the midst of fire but not of poverty, and 90 that one can escape fire but not desire. Each makes fire the lesser affliction.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:91","b":"k:218","type":"agrees","note":"Both hold that giving does not wait for wealth: 218 says those who know their duty don't falter in helping even when means are gone, and 91 rewards those who give gladly when they have little.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:92","b":"k:333","type":"agrees","note":"Both argue from impermanence: 333 says wealth does not stay, so do what lasts at once, and 92 says death, age and disease are near, so share while wealth is in hand.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:93","b":"k:376","type":"agrees","note":"Both deny that guarding keeps wealth: 376 says what is not one's own won't stay however it is guarded, and 93 that wealth leaves when its merit runs out however tightly held. 376 names fate; 93 names past deeds.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:94","b":"k:270","type":"qualifies","note":"Both explain the many poor by what was not done: 270 by the few who practise austerity, and 94 in Mudaliar's reading by the failure to give in a former birth. They give different causes for the same fact without denying each other.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"n:95","b":"k:1061","type":"agrees","note":"Both put a number on not begging: 1061 says it is worth ten million times more even when givers are glad, and 95 that it is worth twice as much as giving.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:96","b":"k:216","type":"same_image","note":"Both put the giver's wealth in a fruiting tree at the town's centre, where all can reach it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:96","b":"k:1008","type":"same_image","note":"1008 keeps the tree in the town's centre but makes the miser's wealth a poison tree fruiting there; 96 moves the miser's barren palm out to the burning ground.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:97","b":"k:211","type":"develops","note":"211 makes the rain the model of help that seeks no return. 97 sets rain and the good's giving side by side as the two things the world can't survive without.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:97","b":"k:19","type":"qualifies","note":"In 19 giving depends on rain: if the sky fails, giving and austerity cease. In 97 the two are parallel supports, each needed on its own.","grade":"weak","better":"tension"},{"a":"n:98","b":"k:221","type":"agrees","note":"Both say giving to those who can repay is not giving but lending: 221 calls it a measured return (குறியெதிர்ப்பை), and 98 a profitable loan (பொலிகடன்).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:99","b":"k:475","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Both turn on small things adding up: in 475 peacock feathers, piled high enough, break a cart's axle, and in 99 small gifts fill the bowl. One warns, the other encourages.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:100","b":"k:232","type":"agrees","note":"Both make giving the source of lasting praise: 232 says all that speakers praise rests on those who give to the asker, and 100 that the word 'they gave' is heard through three worlds.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:101","b":"k:208","type":"agrees","note":"Both make a deed's consequence inseparable from its doer, with different images: the Kural's ruin stays at the heels like a shadow, and the Naladiyar's deed goes looking like a calf for its mother.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:101","b":"n:120","type":"develops","note":"101 says the deed finds its doer among many; 120 says it is the only thing that goes with him at death. Together they make the deed both pursuer and sole companion.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:102","b":"k:333","type":"agrees","note":"Both draw the same conclusion from impermanent wealth: do the lasting good at once. 102 adds beauty, youth and standing to the list and condemns the life that does nothing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:103","b":"k:377","type":"agrees","note":"Both say that enjoyment is measured out in advance: the Kural even for those who amass millions, the Naladiyar for all who want prosperity.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:104","b":"k:269","type":"tension","note":"On Mudaliar's reading, 104 says that even sages cannot remove what is bound to befall. 269 says that those who attain the power of austerity can even leap past death. Both concern whether ascetic power can overcome what is coming; the tension holds if உறுவர் are ascetics and 269's கூற்றம் is fated death.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:104","b":"k:620","type":"tension","note":"104 says no one can remove what is destined, as no one can make rain fall; 620 says those who strive without flagging see even fate's back. The tension depends on reading 620 as effort overcoming fate, as Pope and Kalaignar do.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:105","b":"n:3","type":"agrees","note":"Both describe the great brought low and give the same cause: in 3 the elephant-riders fall when their deeds are spent, and in 105 the palm-sized become millet-sized through former deeds.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:106","b":"k:406","type":"agrees","note":"Both deny that the unlearned truly live: the Kural says they can only be said to exist, like barren land, and the Naladiyar says Death will not even take them, as husks.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:107","b":"k:1062","type":"tension","note":"107 makes begging the outworking of the beggar's own former deeds, so the order that produces it is just. 1062 calls down ruin on whoever ordained that anyone should live by begging, treating it as an unjust order. The tension depends on 107's vinai being the beggar's own deeds.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:108","b":"k:834","type":"qualifies","note":"Both describe one who knows and still does wrong. The Kural calls him the greatest fool; the Naladiyar traces the act to former deeds, which explains it without removing the blame.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:109","b":"k:376","type":"agrees","note":"Both say that a person's will has no say in what comes: in the Kural, care cannot keep what is not one's own; here, wishing cannot keep off what is due.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:110","b":"k:379","type":"agrees","note":"Both end a chapter on fate by asking why anyone grieves when the bad arrives, since it is as fixed as the good.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:111","b":"k:110","type":"develops","note":"The Kural makes killing a kindness received the sin with no escape. On both commentators' reading, 111 names something worse: a promise held out and then broken.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:111","b":"k:223","type":"qualifies","note":"223 praises never uttering 'I have nothing' and giving instead. 111 allows that saying 'no' is blameless when one truly cannot give; the fault lies in false promising.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:112","b":"k:514","type":"tension","note":"112 says the worthy and unworthy never fall from their natures at any time. 514 says that many people, though trusted after every test, change through the nature of the work. The tension holds if 514 means people really change, as Parimelazhagar says, rather than that the tests misjudged them.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:113","b":"k:796","type":"develops","note":"113 observes that kin vanish in trouble; 796 turns that into a benefit, saying ruin is a rod that measures one's people.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:114","b":"k:760","type":"agrees","note":"Both say that whoever secures wealth gets the other two aims of life as well. The Naladiyar adds the cost of failing: suffering like being boiled.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:114","b":"n:1","type":"qualifies","note":"114 makes wealth the key to virtue and pleasure; 1, opening the book, shows the rich reduced to begging for gruel and says wealth should not be counted on. Wealth secures the other two only while it lasts.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:115","b":"k:1046","type":"agrees","note":"Both say that the poor are not heard however sound their words. The Naladiyar adds the other half: the rich are heard even when unlearned.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:116","b":"k:834","type":"agrees","note":"Both describe study that leaves a person unrestrained. The Kural calls such a person the greatest fool; the Naladiyar says it is their nature, as bitterness is the gourd's.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:117","b":"k:151","type":"tension","note":"117 says to scorn those who scorn you; 151 says that bearing with them, as the earth bears those who dig it, is the highest thing. Both concern the same situation. The tension depends on reading 117 as advice, as Mudaliar, Shanmuganar and Pope do, and not as irony.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:117","b":"n:72","type":"tension","note":"Within the Naladiyar: 72 says that when unworthy people speak improperly, bearing it in silence is fitting and the world thinks retaliation shameful. 117 says to scorn the scorners first. The tension holds if 117's scorners and 72's speakers are the same kind of person.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:118","b":"k:34","type":"develops","note":"118 says virtue is one in nature whatever form its ways take. 34 says what that one thing is: a spotless mind, everything else being show.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:120","b":"k:36","type":"qualifies","note":"36 makes virtue the companion that does not die with you. 120 says the only thing that goes along is the deed, which includes bad deeds; so the companion can also be a burden.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"n:121","b":"k:46","type":"tension","note":"On Mudaliar's reading, 121 makes household life itself the sorrow in which those who fail to renounce end as corpses. 46 asks what anyone would gain by leaving a household life lived in virtue for the other path. The tension depends on Mudaliar's gloss of துக்கம்; on Shanmuganar's it is weaker.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:121","b":"k:251","type":"agrees","note":"Both condemn eating flesh. The Kural asks how one who fattens his flesh on others' can have grace; the Naladiyar makes his belly a burning ground.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:122","b":"k:319","type":"agrees","note":"Both say harm done to others returns to the doer. The Naladiyar makes the return copy the harm: those who caged birds wear irons.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:123","b":"k:330","type":"agrees","note":"Both explain a wretched diseased body as the result of having taken life. The Naladiyar names the creature and the disease.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:124","b":"k:452","type":"agrees","note":"Both say a person takes on the nature of their company, the Kural with water changing with the soil and the Naladiyar with ghee turning to flame.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:124","b":"n:112","type":"tension","note":"112 says the worthy never fall from their nature, as jaggery is never bitter. 124 says even the upright turn to cruel deeds when they join the wicked. Both concern whether good character can be changed; the tension holds if 124's கோடார் are truly good, as Mudaliar's நடுவுநிலைமையுடையார் implies.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:124","b":"n:139","type":"develops","note":"124 shows company corrupting the upright; 139 shows it improving the unlearned, like a new pot taking on the scent of flowers. The same mechanism works in both directions.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:125","b":"k:782","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the waxing and waning moon for good and bad friendship. The Kural sets the wise against fools, and the Naladiyar the great against the small.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:126","b":"k:792","type":"agrees","note":"Both warn of friendship formed without examination; the Kural says it ends in mortal grief, the Naladiyar finds a snake where sandal was expected.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:127","b":"k:706","type":"tension","note":"127 says no one can know another's mind, because minds and deeds differ. 706 says the face shows what is uppermost in the heart, as crystal shows what is near it. The tension depends on taking 127 as a general claim rather than a warning about deceivers.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:127","b":"k:702","type":"qualifies","note":"702 grants that someone may know another's mind without doubt, but calls such a person equal to a god. 127 says no one has that power, which agrees that it is beyond ordinary people.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"n:128","b":"k:821","type":"agrees","note":"Both describe friendship without inner consent. The Kural calls it an anvil waiting for the right moment to strike; the Naladiyar says it becomes a stain.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:129","b":"k:727","type":"same_image","note":"Both use a bright sword in the wrong hands. In the Kural it is learning held by one who fears the assembly; in the Naladiyar it is one's own strength lost through association with the unlearned.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:129","b":"k:816","type":"agrees","note":"Both prefer distance from fools to closeness: the Kural rates the enmity of the wise far above a fool's intimacy, and the Naladiyar says the good course is to get away from the unlearned.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:130","b":"k:61","type":"tension","note":"61 says no gain is known greater than children who learn what should be known. 130 rebukes the heart for pining for children and says there is no gain for the soul but good done. The tension softens if 61 speaks of worldly gains and 130 only of the soul's.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:131","b":"k:407","type":"agrees","note":"Both deny that outward beauty is worth anything without learning: the Kural compares it to a well-made clay doll, and the Naladiyar refuses it the name of beauty.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:132","b":"k:400","type":"agrees","note":"Both say learning does not perish when other possessions do. The Naladiyar spells out the ways it differs from wealth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:132","b":"k:398","type":"qualifies","note":"On Mudaliar's reading, 132 says learning lasts as long as its owner lives; 398 says learning gained in one birth protects through seven. Shanmuganar's reading, that it lasts through fame, is closer to the Kural's.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"n:133","b":"k:409","type":"agrees","note":"Both rank the low-born learned above the unlearned, the Kural by comparing them to the high-born unlearned and the Naladiyar with salt from brackish soil.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:133","b":"n:142","type":"qualifies","note":"133 sets the low-born learned in the highest place; 142 says excellence, gentleness and good conduct belong only to the high-born, whatever wealth others gain. Read together, learning can raise one's rank but, in 142, not those qualities.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:134","b":"k:67","type":"agrees","note":"Both make learning the best thing a father gives his children: the Kural as a place at the front of the assembly, the Naladiyar as an inheritance that cannot be taken.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:135","b":"k:391","type":"agrees","note":"Both insist on learning what is to be learned. The Naladiyar adds the reason: learning is endless and life is short.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:136","b":"k:423","type":"agrees","note":"Both say the worth of what is taught does not depend on who teaches it: the Kural says to see the truth in whatever is heard from anyone, and the Naladiyar says to learn from the learned whatever their caste.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:137","b":"k:394","type":"agrees","note":"Both make the meeting of the learned a delight. The Kural describes how they part; the Naladiyar ranks their gathering above heaven.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:138","b":"k:783","type":"agrees","note":"Both say that friendship with people of character grows sweeter with time, the Kural like a book reread and the Naladiyar like cane eaten from the tip.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:139","b":"k:452","type":"agrees","note":"Both say understanding takes on the nature of one's company, the Kural with water and soil and the Naladiyar with water and flowers.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:140","b":"k:356","type":"agrees","note":"Both make the purpose of learning the perception of what is true, which leads out of rebirth on Mudaliar's reading of 140. The Naladiyar adds that worldly learning cannot do this.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:141","b":"k:969","type":"agrees","note":"Both use an animal that will not go against its nature in extremity: the yak that dies when it loses a hair, and the lion that will not eat grass. The Kural speaks of honour, the Naladiyar of the principles of the well-born.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:142","b":"k:952","type":"agrees","note":"Both name three qualities the well-born never lose. The Kural's are conduct, truthfulness and shame; the Naladiyar's are fullness of character, gentleness and conduct, and it adds that wealth cannot buy them.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:144","b":"k:957","type":"agrees","note":"Both say a fault in the well-born is seen by everyone, the Kural like the spot on the moon and the Naladiyar as blame spread by many.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:145","b":"k:997","type":"same_image","note":"Both call people who lack a human moral quality trees or wood: in the Kural those without courtesy, however sharp, and in the Naladiyar the low-born who lack the four fears.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:145","b":"k:951","type":"agrees","note":"Both say a moral fear or shame is found naturally only in the well-born.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:146","b":"k:953","type":"agrees","note":"Both list four qualities of a good lineage, and both include giving and sweet speech.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:146","b":"k:972","type":"tension","note":"146 says that good company, sweet speech, giving and goodness of mind are found only among the well-born. 972 says birth is alike for all and distinction comes from the deeds one does. The two disagree about whether excellence follows birth or deeds. The tension holds if 972's distinction means worth of character, as Pope and Kalaignar take it, and if 146's qualities belong to that worth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:147","b":"k:219","type":"qualifies","note":"219 says that, for a good man, poverty is being unable to do what should be done. 147 says the well-born still do it even in hardship, so for them poverty in that sense never fully arrives.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"n:148","b":"k:218","type":"agrees","note":"The two share the phrase ஒப்புரவிற்கு ஒல்கார்: those who know their duty, or the well-born, do not slacken in serving others when they lack the means. The Naladiyar adds the half-eclipsed moon.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:149","b":"k:1077","type":"agrees","note":"Both say the base do not give even when they could: in the Kural they won't shake off their wet hands for anyone but those who would break their jaws.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:150","b":"k:955","type":"agrees","note":"Both say that the well-born keep their generous nature when their means have shrunk.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:151","b":"k:957","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the spot on the moon. In 957 the fault of the well-born shows as plainly as the moon's spot; in 151 the noble differ from the moon in being unable to live with the spot at all.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:151","b":"k:969","type":"agrees","note":"Both make honour a matter of life and death: in 969 the honourable give up life like the yak that loses its hair, and in 151 the noble waste away at a single stain.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:152","b":"k:596","type":"agrees","note":"Aim high regardless of outcome: 596 says a lofty aim, even if thwarted, keeps its worth, and 152 says the arrow that misses a lion beats the one that kills a jackal.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:153","b":"k:656","type":"agrees","note":"Want does not license wrongdoing. 656 holds even if one's mother starves; 153 holds even when one's own body is reduced to sinew.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:154","b":"k:785","type":"agrees","note":"785 says long acquaintance is not needed for friendship, only shared feeling; 154 shows the worthy treating a single meeting as an old bond.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:154","b":"k:791","type":"tension","note":"791 warns that nothing ruins like befriending without inquiry, since the true friend cannot later withdraw. 154 praises binding someone seen once as if an old friend. The tension holds if 154 means any passer-by; Pope's parenthetical '(worthy) men' softens it, but the Tamil has no such qualifier.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:155","b":"k:153","type":"agrees","note":"153 calls bearing with fools the strength of strengths; 155 shows that strength as patient listening to an ignorant speaker so as not to shame him.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:155","b":"k:403","type":"qualifies","note":"403 puts the burden on the unlearned: they are good if they stay silent before the learned. 155 takes the case where they do not, and puts the burden on the learned to endure it kindly.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:156","b":"k:1078","type":"same_image","note":"Both use crushed sugar-cane, to opposite ends. In 1078 the base are like cane, giving only when crushed; in 156 the well-born are like cane, still sweet however crushed.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:156","b":"k:159","type":"agrees","note":"159 makes bearing harsh words from the insolent as pure as renunciation; 156 makes it the mark of good birth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:157","b":"k:139","type":"agrees","note":"Both say the virtuous cannot speak evil even by a slip: வழுக்கியும் in 139, தள்ளியும் in 157.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:158","b":"k:148","type":"agrees","note":"148 calls not looking at another's wife both virtue and complete conduct; 158 makes that blindness one of three abstentions that leave nothing more to teach.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:158","b":"k:181","type":"agrees","note":"Both give one abstention near-sufficient moral weight: 181 says it is good to be called one who does not backbite even if one does wrong, and 158 counts silence in backbiting among the restraints that make virtue-teaching unnecessary.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:159","b":"k:224","type":"qualifies","note":"224 admits that being asked is painful until one sees the asker's glad face; 159 describes the excellent as treating the prospect of being asked as good from the outset.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:159","b":"k:1057","type":"agrees","note":"1057 is the asker's side: the heart rejoices at givers who do not scorn. 159 is the giver's side: the excellent honour those who may ask instead of scorning them.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:160","b":"k:443","type":"agrees","note":"443 calls winning the great as one's own the rarest of all things; 160 calls meeting the well-born a treasure cave, against the base who chase the rich.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:161","b":"k:700","type":"parallel_domain","note":"700 warns a minister that doing improper things on the strength of long familiarity with the king brings ruin. 161 makes the same point about presuming on the forbearance of the great.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:161","b":"k:896","type":"agrees","note":"896 says one may survive fire but not a lasting offence against the great; 161 says the damage of offending them cannot be undone.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:162","b":"k:443","type":"develops","note":"443 calls winning the great the rarest of things. 162 imagines that rarity handed over free and wasted by people who cannot use it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:163","b":"n:165","type":"develops","note":"Both locate worth in the judgment of the worthy. 163 dismisses the verdict of the base, and 165 dismisses one's own verdict on oneself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:164","b":"k:895","type":"agrees","note":"895: those who have drawn a fierce king's anger exist nowhere, wherever they go. This matches Mudaliar's reading of 164, where the angered great are kings.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:164","b":"k:900","type":"agrees","note":"900: even those with overwhelming support perish if the eminent are angered. This matches the reading of 164 in which the great are the morally great and the fortress is no defence.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:165","b":"k:978","type":"agrees","note":"978 says smallness adorns itself by admiring itself; 165 gives the words of that self-admiration and denies it the name of esteem.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:166","b":"k:782","type":"agrees","note":"The same claim with a different measure of time: 782 has the waxing and waning moon, 166 the shortening and lengthening shadow.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:167","b":"k:691","type":"qualifies","note":"691 advises those who serve kings to stay neither too near nor too far, like people warming at a fire. 167 observes that those who stay close are the ones who enjoy a king's wealth, without the warning.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:168","b":"k:839","type":"tension","note":"839 says, with irony, that friendship with fools is sweet because parting from them brings no pain. 168 says parting even from the undiscerning brings great sorrow. The two make opposite claims about the pain of parting from fools, if தெரிவிலார் is read as fools, as Mudaliar's gloss அறிவிலார் has it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:168","b":"k:816","type":"qualifies","note":"Both end with கோடி உறும். 816 says being a stranger to the wise is ten million times better than close friendship with a fool, a ranking of companions. 168 drops the ranking: mixing with no one is ten million times better.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"n:168","b":"n:174","type":"tension","note":"168 concludes that not mixing with anyone is far better because of the pain of parting. 174 says no one would hate even painful birth if they could keep friendship with the noble. One makes withdrawal the remedy for life's pain, the other makes companionship the remedy.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:169","b":"k:38","type":"agrees","note":"Both count days: 38 says letting no day fall idle without good blocks the road of births, and 169 says the good have no day without learning, visiting the great or giving. Both use படாஅ for days that do not occur.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:170","b":"k:978","type":"agrees","note":"Both define greatness as bowing: பணியுமாம் என்றும் பெருமை in 978, பெரியார் பெருமை சிறுதகைமை in 170.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:170","b":"k:226","type":"agrees","note":"226 says removing the hunger of the destitute is where one should store wealth; 170 says only those who relieve their dependants' distress are truly wealthy.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:171","b":"k:455","type":"agrees","note":"455 says purity of mind and deed both lean on purity of company; 171 shows company acting on past deeds as well, clearing the faults of youth.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"n:172","b":"k:415","type":"agrees","note":"415 calls the words of the good a staff on slippery ground; 172 closes its six precepts with taking in the words of the great every day.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:172","b":"k:159","type":"agrees","note":"Both commend bearing others' harsh words; 159 ranks those who do it with renunciants, 172 lists it among the basic precepts.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:173","b":"k:362","type":"agrees","note":"Both treat birth as the thing to escape: 362 says if one must want, want not to be born, and 173 seeks out those who know birth is bitter.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:174","b":"n:173","type":"qualifies","note":"173 declares birth bitter from the start. 174, placed next to it, grants that and adds that friendship with the good makes birth something no one would resent.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:174","b":"k:460","type":"develops","note":"460 says there is no greater support than good company. 174 raises the stakes: good company is what reconciles a person to being born at all.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:175","b":"k:452","type":"same_image","note":"Both use water changed by what it joins. In 452 water takes on the nature of its soil, as a mind takes on its company; in 175 sewage takes the name of the sacred river, as the lowly take the standing of the good.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:176","b":"n:151","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the moon's dark spot. In 151 the moon tolerates its flaw and the noble cannot; in 176 the flaw is worshipped because it sits in the moon.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:177","b":"k:1000","type":"same_image","note":"Milk and its mixture, in opposite directions: in 1000 good milk is spoiled by a bad vessel, as wealth by a graceless owner; in 177 water is absorbed into milk, as the small into the great.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:178","b":"k:446","type":"agrees","note":"446: enemies can do nothing to one who walks with the worthy. 178 gives the image of grass the plough cannot uproot because it grows against a stump.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:179","b":"k:458","type":"agrees","note":"458 says that even the worthy, whose minds are good, are protected by good company. 179 gives the converse: their worth is wrecked by bad company.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:180","b":"k:453","type":"agrees","note":"453 says what a person is called comes from their company; 180 says the pure in mind are scorned because of theirs.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:180","b":"n:177","type":"develops","note":"The same mechanism run both ways: in 177 the mean are hidden by the great, in 180 the pure are blackened by the bad.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:181","b":"k:1050","type":"agrees","note":"1050 says the destitute who do not renounce merely consume salt and gruel. 181 has someone who can no longer give conclude that renunciation is the fitting course.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:182","b":"k:331","type":"agrees","note":"331 calls taking the impermanent for permanent the lowest understanding; 182 gives the example of the householder who thinks himself settled for good.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:182","b":"k:46","type":"tension","note":"46 asks what one gains by leaving a virtuous household life for the ascetic way. 182 calls those content in home life fools and praises those who see its supports as passing. The conflict holds on Mudaliar's reading, where the failing supports are household attachments themselves; if 182 targets only heedlessness, the two are compatible.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:183","b":"k:335","type":"agrees","note":"Both urge good deeds now because the body may fail without warning: 335 before the death-hiccup stops the tongue, 183 because the body changes without cause.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:184","b":"k:215","type":"same_image","note":"Both use a village water source for the generous: 215 compares a wise man's wealth to a full town tank, 184 compares the great in decline to a spring-fed well that still serves in drought.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:184","b":"k:218","type":"agrees","note":"218 says those who know their duty do not slacken in serving others even when they have no means; 184 says the great keep giving in decline.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:185","b":"n:184","type":"same_image","note":"Consecutive water images for giving in decline: a spring-fed well in 184, a dry riverbed that yields to digging in 185.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:185","b":"n:153","type":"develops","note":"Both end with செய்வர் செயற்பாலவை. In 153 the poor worthy do their duty within their means; in 185 the once-rich keep doing it after ruin, for fewer people.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:185","b":"k:955","type":"agrees","note":"955: even when what they can give has shrunk, an old family keeps its nature. 185 says the same of the ruined giver who still gives to a few.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:186","b":"k:957","type":"agrees","note":"957 says a fault in the well-born stands out like the moon's spot; 186 says the same with a brand on a white bull, and adds that the crimes of the small go unseen.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:187","b":"k:816","type":"agrees","note":"816: being a stranger to the wise is ten million times better than intimacy with a fool. 187 goes further: even enmity with the wise brings dignity, while intimacy with the mean brings grief.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:188","b":"k:987","type":"tension","note":"987 asks what worth is for if it cannot do good even to those who do harm. 188 says to meet foes with terror and the false with falseness. The conflict is direct on Mudaliar's reading of சலவர் as liars; on Pope's reading the third clause is milder, but the clause on foes still conflicts.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:188","b":"k:830","type":"agrees","note":"830 advises friendship of the face, not the heart, with a foe who turns friendly. 188 likewise answers each person in their own coin rather than with uniform goodness.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"n:190","b":"k:360","type":"develops","note":"360 says suffering ends when desire, anger and delusion perish. On Shanmuganar's reading of the three faults, 190 offers a practice that removes them: feeding others before oneself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:190","b":"k:227","type":"agrees","note":"227: hunger does not touch one who habitually shares food. 190 says sharing the first-served food keeps the worthy from sorrow till the end.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:191","b":"k:615","type":"agrees","note":"615 calls the one who prefers work to pleasure a pillar who wipes away his kin's troubles. On Shanmuganar's reading of 191, kin rest easy on what the energetic one provides.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:192","b":"k:611","type":"agrees","note":"611 says effort brings the greatness the task requires; 192 shows it as a twig hardening into a post strong enough for an elephant.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:193","b":"k:618","type":"agrees","note":"618 says bad fortune is no disgrace but failing to work is; 193 tells the knowledgeable not to scorn humble work when fortune is thin.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:193","b":"n:198","type":"qualifies","note":"198 says the lion-hearted would die rather than do anything disgraceful; 193 says even the tiger eats a frog when hungry. Read together, humble work is not disgrace, and dishonourable deeds are.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:194","b":"k:619","type":"agrees","note":"619 says effort pays its wage even when fate denies the goal; 194 makes persistence through failure the very definition of manliness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:194","b":"n:152","type":"agrees","note":"Both begin from success or failure (இசையும் எனினும் இசையாதெனினும் in 152, இசையாதெனினும் in 194) and judge a person by how they act regardless of outcome.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:195","b":"k:972","type":"agrees","note":"972: birth is alike for all beings, and distinction differs by the work done. 195 says good and bad caste are mere words and lineage is made by austerity, learning and effort.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:195","b":"k:951","type":"tension","note":"951 says integrity and shame exist together naturally only in the well-born. 195 says good and bad lineage are words without substance. Both concern whether birth carries real moral qualities, and they answer opposite ways unless 951's இற்பிறந்தார் is read as a lineage made by conduct, as 195 defines it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:195","b":"n:179","type":"tension","note":"On Shanmuganar's reading of 179, the worthy are formed by the family they are born into, as paddy by its soil; 195 says good and bad lineage are only words. On Mudaliar's reading of குலம் in 179 as one's company, there is no conflict.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:196","b":"k:663","type":"agrees","note":"663 says true manliness lets a deed be known only when finished, since revealing it midway brings grief; 196 says the perceptive keep their plans to themselves until the work is done.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:196","b":"k:701","type":"agrees","note":"701 calls one who reads what is unsaid an ornament to the world; 196 says the world is under the sign of those who read others' drive from their features.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:197","b":"k:1030","type":"same_image","note":"Both picture a structure eaten at the base and propped up. In 1030 a family whose foot is cut by trouble falls if no good person props it; in 197 the son props the decaying father like banyan roots.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:197","b":"k:70","type":"develops","note":"70 says a son repays his father by making people wonder what penance the father did. 197 gives a more practical repayment: covering the father's weakness when he falters.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:198","b":"k:656","type":"agrees","note":"656: even seeing one's mother starve, do not do what the worthy condemn. 198: even dying in want at home, the strong do not do what brings disgrace.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:198","b":"k:969","type":"agrees","note":"Both set death against dishonour: 969's yak dies when it loses its hair, 198's lion-hearted would die in want rather than be disgraced.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:199","b":"k:601","type":"agrees","note":"601 says the lamp of lineage dies when sloth covers it; 199 says high birth is worth nothing without great manliness, like a flower that has lost its scent.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:200","b":"k:1065","type":"agrees","note":"Nearly the same claim: 1065 says even watery gruel got by one's own effort (தாள்தந்தது) is the sweetest food, and 200 says water won by one's own effort (தாளாண்மை) becomes nectar.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:200","b":"n:296","type":"develops","note":"In 200 the Mutharaiyar are patrons whose feasts the base enjoy; in 296 the poor who never go begging to the rich are themselves called Mutharaiyar. The patron's name moves from giver to the self-reliant.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:201","b":"k:521","type":"develops","note":"521 says that only kin keep honouring old ties when a man has lost everything; 201 describes what that loyalty does inside the sufferer, likening it to a mother forgetting the pains of birth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:202","b":"k:216","type":"same_image","note":"Both make the good man a fruiting tree that the many enjoy. 216 says his wealth is like a tree ripening in the middle of town; 202 adds the shade tree and insists he toils for it himself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:203","b":"k:1027","type":"agrees","note":"Both put the burden of those who depend on a household on its capable members: 1027 compares them to the brave in battle, 203 to a branch that never fails to bear its own fruit.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:204","b":"k:782","type":"agrees","note":"Both contrast a friendship that holds or grows with one that soon fails; 782 uses the waxing and waning moon, 204 the ascetic's unswerving path.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:205","b":"k:790","type":"agrees","note":"Both distrust speech that labels a relation, and their wording is close (இனையர், இன்னர்/இன்னம்). 790 says friendship looks shabby when friends describe what they are to each other; 205 says the best people never sort others into ours and theirs.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"n:206","b":"k:64","type":"same_image","note":"Both say that plain gruel (கூழ், புற்கை) shared with one's own is sweeter than the finest food. The Kural says it is sweeter than ambrosia when one's children's little hands have stirred it; 206 says it is sweeter than a golden-dish feast from those who are not one's own.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:206","b":"n:217","type":"same_image","note":"The kin chapter and the chapter on testing friendship use the same contrast of poor food given with love and rich food given without it. 217 ends in the bitter strychnos fruit, 206 and 210 in neem.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:207","b":"k:90","type":"agrees","note":"Both say that hospitality without warmth spoils the meal for the guest. 90 says a guest withers like the anicham flower at an averted face; 207 says the punctual feast in an unloving house tastes of neem.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:208","b":"k:799","type":"agrees","note":"Both single out those who leave when ruin comes: 208 compares them to tongs that let go in the fire, and 799 says the memory of such friends burns the heart at the hour of death.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:209","b":"k:787","type":"agrees","note":"787 defines friendship partly as sharing the suffering in ruin; 209 says a friendship that shares only joys can do nothing even in the next world.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:211","b":"n:138","type":"same_image","note":"A near doublet: both compare friendship with the learned to eating sugarcane from the tip and the other kind to eating it from the root. 138 ends on those without character or kindness, 211 on those without sweetness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:211","b":"k:783","type":"agrees","note":"Both say friendship with the right people grows sweeter with use. 783 compares it to a book whose delight grows with study; 211 to sugarcane eaten from the tip.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:212","b":"n:245","type":"tension","note":"212 relies on family as the only workable guarantee that a friend won't change, since minds can't be known. 245 says people are not like their இனம் but like their minds. The tension holds if 245's இனம் includes family, as Mudaliar's 'இனத்தார்' allows; if it means only the company one keeps, the two are about different things.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:212","b":"k:793","type":"qualifies","note":"793 tells one to know a man's qualities, family, faults and connections before befriending him. 212 keeps family but doubts the rest: the mind can't be known, so family is the one dependable ground.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:213","b":"k:807","type":"agrees","note":"Both prize love that survives injury from the friend: 807 says those bound by love don't stop loving even when the friend ruins them, and 213 pictures the dog wagging its tail with the spear in its body.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:214","b":"k:785","type":"agrees","note":"Both say that a shared heart, not meeting or long acquaintance, makes friendship. 214 adds the reverse case: long absence doesn't break it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:215","b":"k:425","type":"same_image","note":"Both use a flower's opening and closing for inconstant friendship. 425 says understanding is not opening and closing; 215 contrasts tree flowers that stay open with pond flowers that close.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:216","b":"k:104","type":"agrees","note":"104 says those who know its value count a millet-sized help as palmyra-sized. On Mudaliar's reading the palmyra in 216 stands for the best friends, who form a great friendship from one small help.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:219","b":"n:187","type":"agrees","note":"Both prefer hostility from the right people to friendship with the wrong. 187 says the enmity of the wise brings dignity; 219 says enmity is better than the friendship of those without clear understanding.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:219","b":"k:816","type":"agrees","note":"816 says being a stranger to the wise is ten million times better than close friendship with a fool. 219 goes further: open enmity is better than a fool's friendship.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:220","b":"k:791","type":"agrees","note":"Both argue for testing before befriending because a formed bond can't be cleanly undone. 791 gives the reason as the friend's own loyalty; 220 gives it as the pain of parting, even from a snake.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:220","b":"n:168","type":"agrees","note":"Both say parting from a bad bond still hurts. 168 concludes that it is best to mix with no one; 220 concludes that one must test long before mixing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:221","b":"n:75","type":"qualifies","note":"75 says to bear a friend's unkind conduct as far as one can and, if one cannot, to withdraw without speaking ill. 221 offers no such exit: keep them and keep their faults inside.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:221","b":"k:805","type":"agrees","note":"Both ask for a charitable account of a friend's wrongs. 805 says to put them down to folly or great intimacy; 221 says every good thing has its husk.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:222","b":"k:151","type":"agrees","note":"Both make forbearance toward those who damage you an image from the land: the earth bears those who dig into it (151), and the farmer re-dams the stream that keeps breaking through (222).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:223","b":"k:803","type":"agrees","note":"803 asks what use an old friendship is if one doesn't accept a friend's liberties as one's own acts. 223 puts the same demand as a maxim: one person's patience keeps two in friendship.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:224","b":"k:799","type":"same_image","note":"Both picture a wrong friendship as burning the heart. In 799 it is the memory of friends who deserted one; in 224 it is a friend one cannot leave who lacks good nature.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:225","b":"n:224","type":"same_image","note":"Adjacent quatrains turn fire both ways: in 224 a bad friend is a fire lit to burn the heart, and in 225 the fire that burned the house is still needed daily, so the friend who hurt one is kept.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:226","b":"n:76","type":"agrees","note":"Both forbid casting off a close friend for the hurt they cause. 76 says to blame oneself, since even beasts find parting hard; 226 says no one cuts off the hand that poked their eye.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:227","b":"k:980","type":"agrees","note":"980 says greatness hides others' faults and smallness only tells them. 227 applies this to friends and ranks the teller below the wrongdoer.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:227","b":"k:188","type":"agrees","note":"188 asks what those who spread the faults of their intimates would do to strangers. 227 makes the same judgement on those who publish a friend's wrongs.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:228","b":"k:805","type":"agrees","note":"Both reinterpret a friend's hurtful act. 805 reads it as folly or intimacy; 228 says it becomes good once held in the heart.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:229","b":"k:830","type":"qualifies","note":"830 says to befriend a former enemy with the face, let go in the heart, and then drop the friendship. 229 also hides an inner judgement behind outward regard, but toward a friend who has proved false, and it never says to drop him.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"n:230","b":"k:504","type":"qualifies","note":"504 tells one to examine a person's qualities and faults and take what predominates. 230 forbids that examination once the friendship is made, so the two divide the labour: examine before, never after.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:230","b":"n:238","type":"same_image","note":"Both are first-person curses with the same frame: may I go where the worst betrayer of a friend goes, while the world laughs. 230 names the one who did not keep a friend's secret, and 238 the one who violated a friend's wife.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:231","b":"k:812","type":"agrees","note":"Both describe friends who stay while there is something to gain. 812 asks what it matters whether one keeps or loses them; 231 shows them putting up with discomfort until their business is done.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:231","b":"n:284","type":"agrees","note":"284 has crowds gathering like crows while wealth lasts and no one asking after you when it goes; 231 shows the same self-interest from inside the friendship.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:232","b":"k:811","type":"agrees","note":"Both say the friendship of the worthless is barren even at its fullest. 811 says it is better shrinking than growing; 232 says even at its height it is like failed rain.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:233","b":"n:137","type":"agrees","note":"Both set the company of the learned against heaven. 137 doubts heaven could be sweeter; 233 makes the comparison explicit and adds that bad company is one of the hells.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:233","b":"k:460","type":"agrees","note":"Both put the company one keeps at the extremes: no greater support than good company and no greater affliction than bad (460), heaven and hell (233).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:234","b":"k:782","type":"agrees","note":"Both describe a friendship that seems to grow and then shrinks: the moon past full (782), fire in straw (234).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:235","b":"k:818","type":"agrees","note":"818 says to let slip the friendship of those who make difficulties of what they could do. 235 names the same delay, with empty promising, and says it brings suffering even to ascetics.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:236","b":"n:177","type":"qualifies","note":"177 says the smallness of the small stops showing when they join the great, as water in milk looks like milk. 236 says their deeds stay different though they grow up beside the great. Read together, association changes how the small look, not what they do.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:236","b":"k:823","type":"agrees","note":"Both say an outside good doesn't change an inner nature: 823 says the unworthy rarely become good at heart however many good books they study, and 236 says the same of their friendship with the large-natured.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:237","b":"k:825","type":"agrees","note":"Both make inner accord the condition of friendship. 825 says not to trust the words of those not one with you in mind; 237 says friendship with them is painful.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:238","b":"k:787","type":"agrees","note":"787 says friendship shares the suffering when ruin comes. 238 raises the stake to one's own life and curses the friend who holds back.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"n:239","b":"k:797","type":"agrees","note":"797 calls leaving the friendship of fools a gain. 239 describes the reverse exchange, leaving the discerning for the petty-minded, as ghee swapped for neem oil.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:240","b":"n:244","type":"qualifies","note":"240 says the discerning who take up with bad company are ruined; 244 says the good-natured don't turn bad in mind though their company is bad. On Mudaliar's reading of 240 as ruin, the two fit: such men may keep their minds and still lose everything else, as 180 says of the sandal tree in a forest fire.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:240","b":"n:179","type":"agrees","note":"Both say bad company ruins the good: 179 compares it to a storm wrecking a ship, and 240 to a cobra mating with a viper.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:241","b":"k:773","type":"agrees","note":"773 calls fierceness valour and kindness to a foe who has fallen (ஊராண்மை) its keen edge. 241 says the worthy feel shame to attack a humbled foe.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:241","b":"k:879","type":"tension","note":"879 says to cut a thorn tree while it is young, before it can wound the cutter; 241 says the worthy don't attack an enemy at his weakest. Both concern whether to strike a foe while he is weak. The tension holds if 879's thorn tree is an enemy, as Parimelazhagar reads it in the chapter on enmity.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:241","b":"n:148","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the eclipse snake and the moon. In 148 the moon still lights the world while the snake holds half of it, an image of the nobly born in poverty; in 241 the snake spares the crescent, an image of the worthy sparing a humbled foe.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:242","b":"k:963","type":"tension","note":"963 says humility suits prosperity and standing tall suits adversity; 242 says restraint is the ornament of the poor and that living without humility shames one's family. Both concern how the poor should bear themselves. The tension depends on taking 963's உயர்வு as not bowing, as Parimelazhagar glosses it, and 242's அடக்கம் as bowing; if 242 condemns only living beyond one's means, they fit.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:242","b":"k:125","type":"qualifies","note":"125 says humility is good for all and an extra wealth for the rich. 242 makes it the ornament of the poor.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"n:243","b":"k:972","type":"agrees","note":"Both deny that origin decides worth: 972 says all are alike in birth and differ by their deeds, and 243 says the next world comes from oneself, not from one's region.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:243","b":"n:195","type":"agrees","note":"195 calls good and bad caste mere words and puts worth in austerity, learning and effort; 243 makes the same claim about the region of one's birth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:244","b":"n:179","type":"tension","note":"179 says goodness perishes when it comes near bad company, as a storm wrecks a ship; 244 says the good-natured do not turn bad in mind though their company is bad. Both concern whether bad company corrupts the good, and the commentators read 244's இனம் as company, which makes the conflict direct.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:244","b":"k:458","type":"qualifies","note":"458 says that even those with full goodness of mind are strengthened by good company. 244 says the good-natured hold firm without it, even among bad company.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:245","b":"k:452","type":"tension","note":"The same image of water leads to opposite claims. 452 says water changes with the soil, so a person's understanding takes on the nature of their company (454 adds that it only seems to be in the mind); 245 says sweet water rises by the sea, so people are as their minds, not their kind. The tension holds if 245's இனம் means the people around one, as the image of surroundings suggests. Shanmuganar's 'not only by their group' softens it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:246","b":"n:215","type":"agrees","note":"Both condemn friendship that comes and goes. 215 contrasts the tree flower with the pond flower; 246 says people of steady mind don't leave and rejoin.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:247","b":"n:168","type":"tension","note":"168 says parting even from those without discernment brings great pain, so it is best to mix with none; 247 says that parting from the undiscerning is how the pain parts. Both concern whether leaving the undiscerning hurts, and they share the phrase தெரியத் தெரியும் தெரிவிலா. The tension rests on reading 247 as both commentators and Pope do.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:247","b":"k:460","type":"agrees","note":"Both tie joy to good company and affliction to bad: no greater support than good company and nothing more afflicting than bad (460).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:248","b":"n:192","type":"agrees","note":"192 says a pliant twig becomes a post for tethering elephants, and so does a life if a man makes himself without slackening; 248 says a person alone raises or lowers himself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:248","b":"k:505","type":"agrees","note":"505 makes one's own deeds the touchstone of greatness and smallness; 248 makes the self the agent of rising and falling.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"n:249","b":"n:193","type":"agrees","note":"Both permit the capable to stoop for a purpose: the tiger eats a frog when there is no other meat (193), and the great walk behind the unlearned for a task (249).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:249","b":"k:967","type":"qualifies","note":"967 says it is better to be ruined than to live by following those who scorn you. 249 allows following the unlearned for the sake of a task, which marks a limit on that pride: following for a purpose is not following for a living.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:250","b":"n:114","type":"develops","note":"114 names the three ends of life and says gaining the middle one, wealth, brings the other two. 250 pictures a life that completes all three without hindrance, as a ship reaching port.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:250","b":"k:754","type":"agrees","note":"754 says rightly gained wealth yields virtue and pleasure. 250 counts the life that achieves work, pleasure and virtuous giving together as a voyage completed.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:251","b":"k:841","type":"agrees","note":"Both call want of understanding the true poverty. Kural 841 adds that the world does not count other lack as lack; 251 adds the image of jewels on one who lacks the essential thing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:251","b":"k:402","type":"same_image","note":"Both use a person lacking the marks of a sex to picture a lack that ornament cannot cover: the unlearned wanting to speak (Kural), the senseless rich in their jewels (here).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:252","b":"k:374","type":"agrees","note":"Both separate wealth from wisdom. The Kural states it as two natures of the world under fate; 252 makes a comic myth of the same fact, the two goddesses quarrelling.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:252","b":"n:266","type":"develops","note":"252 explains in jest why Fortune avoids the learned; 266 turns on her and curses her for going to the base and leaving the good.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:253","b":"k:405","type":"agrees","note":"Both show the unlearned exposed in company. In the Kural his standing wilts as he speaks; here he is asked to read and answers with a stick.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:254","b":"k:403","type":"tension","note":"Kural 403 says the unlearned are very good if they can stay silent before the learned; 254 says that even sitting quietly among the wise, such a man is a dog sitting there. Same situation, opposite verdict. The tension holds on Parimelazhagar's plain reading of 403 (silence keeps them from exposure and may lead them to learning), not if 403 is taken as irony.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:254","b":"k:840","type":"agrees","note":"Both make the fool's mere presence among the worthy a defilement: unwashed feet on a bed (Kural), a dog sitting among them (here).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:255","b":"k:719","type":"agrees","note":"Both tell those who speak well not to speak in a mean assembly. 255 adds the reason, that the hearers would not take the meaning in, and pictures the low talking freely there.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:256","b":"k:642","type":"agrees","note":"Both use சோர்வு for the lapse in speech to be guarded against. The Kural says it because gain and ruin come from speech; 256 shows the learned guarding against it by silence.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:257","b":"k:720","type":"same_image","note":"Good teaching spilt where it is wasted: nectar spilt in a dirty yard (Kural), mango juice pressed into a pig's trough (here).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:258","b":"k:373","type":"agrees","note":"Both say teaching cannot override what a person already is: in the Kural one's own understanding prevails over many books; here understanding cannot be driven into a body without merit. On the commentators' reading both rest on past deeds.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:260","b":"n:255","type":"develops","note":"Both use புன்கோட்டி, the petty assembly. 255 shows the low talking in such assemblies; 260 shows how one is founded, by a man who rejects the learned and finds his like.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:261","b":"k:1008","type":"agrees","note":"Both picture the ungiving man's wealth as a fruiting tree no one can use: a poison tree in the middle of town (Kural), a wood-apple near at hand that the bats ignore (here). Parimelazhagar also stresses that the man is near the poor.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:263","b":"k:215","type":"same_image","note":"Both picture wealth as drinking water. The Kural's wise man's wealth is the full village tank; here the rich man is the undrinkable sea and the giver the small sweet well.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:264","b":"k:374","type":"agrees","note":"Both say that being rich and being wise are separate fortunes. 264 makes the sharper point that the senseless live in silk while the wise are passed over.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:265","b":"k:169","type":"develops","note":"Kural 169 says the prosperity of the crooked and the ruin of the upright are to be pondered. 265 gives the answer such pondering reaches: the fruit of old deeds, and nothing else.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:267","b":"k:169","type":"agrees","note":"Both invite wonder at poverty staying with the good and wealth with the unworthy. Neither verse explains it; Parimelazhagar and 265 explain it by past deeds.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:266","b":"k:179","type":"tension","note":"Kural 179 says Fortune knows the way and goes to the wise who understand virtue; 266 says she leaves the good and goes to the worthless. Both speak of the goddess choosing whom to join. The tension holds if 179 is a claim about how fortune actually goes, as Parimelazhagar takes it, and not only a promise.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:270","b":"k:140","type":"agrees","note":"Both say study does not make one learned without understanding: the Kural names that understanding as living in accord with the world.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:263","b":"n:275","type":"same_image","note":"Both set the sea against a small well. In 263 people seek out the sweet well as they seek out givers; in 275 they wait at a nearly dry well, as the poverty of the worthy is better than wealth that forgets the next world.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:271","b":"k:229","type":"agrees","note":"Both condemn eating alone to keep one's store full. The Kural ranks it more bitter than begging; 271 shuts the door of the next world on it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:272","b":"k:36","type":"agrees","note":"Both reject putting virtue off: the Kural's அன்று அறிவாம் என்னாது ('without saying we'll see to it later') answers the very words of 272's delayers, பின் அறிதும்.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:273","b":"k:1001","type":"agrees","note":"Both describe the man who stores wealth, does not use it and dies: the Kural says he achieved nothing, 273 that his own wealth laughs at him.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:274","b":"k:1007","type":"same_image","note":"Both liken the miser's wealth to a beautiful young woman, with opposite outcomes: in the Kural she grows old alone, unused; here she is enjoyed in due season by a stranger. Unused or lost, the miser has none of it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:276","b":"k:1005","type":"agrees","note":"Both say that those who neither give nor enjoy have nothing, whatever they hold. 276 makes it a joke in which the speaker owns the miser's wealth as much as he does.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:277","b":"k:1041","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1041 says nothing is more painful than poverty. 277 names troubles poverty spares, but only in comparison with the ungiving rich, so it limits 1041 rather than contradicting it.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:278","b":"n:274","type":"develops","note":"274 says the miser's wealth goes to a stranger; 278 follows it to the heirs, who repeat his avarice, so the wealth does no good in either generation.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:279","b":"k:1078","type":"agrees","note":"Both say the base yield only when crushed: like sugar-cane (Kural), like a cow milked by force (here). Both use கொல்ல for the crushing. The Kural's worthy give at a word; here the giver yields like a cow to its calf.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:280","b":"k:754","type":"tension","note":"Kural 754 says wealth gained by right means and without wrong yields virtue and pleasure; 280 says wealth at every stage, earning included, is the dwelling of suffering. Both speak of wealth as such and its yield. The tension holds if 280 is about wealth in general, as its wording suggests, and not only the miser's, as the chapter setting might imply.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:277","b":"n:280","type":"agrees","note":"277 lists the miser's troubles of guarding, burying and clutching; 280 generalizes them into a sequence of earning, guarding, dwindling and loss.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:281","b":"k:752","type":"agrees","note":"Both say everyone scorns those who have nothing and honours the rich. 281 adds that noble birth does not help: the penniless are lower than a corpse.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:281","b":"k:841","type":"tension","note":"Kural 841 says the world does not count lack of wealth as real lack; 281 says the world counts those who lack it lower than a corpse. Both describe how the world regards the poor. The tension holds on Parimelazhagar's reading of 841's second half as a claim about the world's judgment; Kural 752 sides with 281, so the Kural itself is divided.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:283","b":"k:1047","type":"qualifies","note":"Both say the poor lose their own people. Kural 1047 limits it to poverty without virtue, which even a mother treats as a stranger; 283 makes no such exception.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:284","b":"k:527","type":"same_image","note":"The same bird with opposite morals: the Kural's crows call their kin to share their food, an image of the generous; here crows crowding a carcass are the flatterers round the rich.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:285","b":"k:1043","type":"agrees","note":"Both say poverty destroys the standing of one's ancient family at a stroke. 285 adds valour and learning to what is lost.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:287","b":"k:1046","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1046 says the words of the poor lose force even when they understand well. 287 goes further: the understanding itself loses its sharpness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:286","b":"n:288","type":"agrees","note":"Mudaliar says 288 repeats 286: better to leave the village than live there unable to give. The agreement holds on his reading of 286 as advice to a poor man, not Shanmuganar's reading of it as scorn for a miser.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:288","b":"k:229","type":"agrees","note":"Both rank begging above a life that does not give: the Kural the hoarder eating alone, 288 the poor man who stays home unable to give.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:288","b":"k:1050","type":"tension","note":"For the destitute who cannot give, Kural 1050 says the fitting course is to renounce completely, the body included on Parimelazhagar's reading, rather than live off others' salt and gruel. 288 recommends going away and living by begging. Same person, opposite remedies; here the Jain book is the less renunciatory one. The tension depends on Parimelazhagar's reading of 1050.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:289","b":"n:1","type":"agrees","note":"Both show fallen fortune through food: those who once refused a second helping beg for gruel (1), and hands that wore bracelets cook unsalted greens (289).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:290","b":"n:283","type":"agrees","note":"The same image and conclusion, bees leaving a flowerless plant and the poor left without kin; Mudaliar notes the repeat.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:291","b":"n:298","type":"same_image","note":"Both make the honourable mind a fire lit by the rich: a forest fire at their arrogance (291), a forge fire at their contemptuous pity (298).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:292","b":"k:966","type":"agrees","note":"Both refuse 'going behind' those who scorn (Kural இகழ்வார்பின் சென்று, here இயல்பிலார் பின்சென்று). The Kural says it brings neither praise nor heaven; 292 says the honourable go to those who understand unasked.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:292","b":"k:1053","type":"agrees","note":"Both allow asking before the right people: the Kural finds a beauty in asking those of open heart who know their duty, and 292 sends the honourable to those who grasp the need before it is spoken.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:293","b":"k:1057","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 1057 delights in givers who give without scorn; 293 shows the giver who feeds the poor at the back door with contempt, and draws the matching conclusion: forget such friendship.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:295","b":"k:656","type":"agrees","note":"Both set an absolute limit: the worthy will not do what brings blame, not to save a starving mother (Kural) and not to save their own lives (here).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:296","b":"n:270","type":"agrees","note":"Both say the rich who do not give are poor and the poor who do not beg are rich. 296 adds a name for the honourable poor, the great Muttaraiyar.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:297","b":"k:433","type":"agrees","note":"Both make fear of blame the mark of the highest: the Kural's பழிநாணுவார் see a millet-sized fault as a palmyra; here only blame frightens the best, while lower people fear hunger and pain.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:300","b":"k:969","type":"same_image","note":"Each book gives honour an animal that dies rather than compromise: the yak that will not live once its hair is gone (Kural), the tiger that starves rather than eat prey fallen on its left (here).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:300","b":"k:966","type":"develops","note":"Kural 966 says that following those who scorn you does not lead to the gods' land. 300 goes further: even if heaven were in hand, the excellent would refuse it at honour's cost.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:301","b":"k:966","type":"agrees","note":"Both ask why a person of worth should stand behind those who look down on him. 966 counts the loss in renown and in heaven; 301 in clear understanding.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:302","b":"k:656","type":"agrees","note":"656 forbids deeds the wise condemn even to relieve one's mother's hunger. 302 makes the same choice for one's own hunger, and grounds it in the shortness of life rather than in the judgement of the wise.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:302","b":"k:339","type":"same_image","note":"Both make death and rebirth as brief as an ordinary act of the eyes, and Mudaliar cites 339 here. The Kural uses the image to show impermanence; 302 uses it to make blameless hunger the cheaper choice.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:303","b":"k:1053","type":"agrees","note":"1053 finds a beauty even in asking before unconcealing givers who know their duty. 303 narrows this further: the worthy approach only givers who first welcome them in, so asking becomes hospitality.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:304","b":"k:963","type":"agrees","note":"963 asks for dignity when circumstances shrink; 304 shows it, with fortune and the gods both gone and the worthy still refusing to bow to the hoarding rich.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:305","b":"k:1061","type":"agrees","note":"The first half restates 1061: not asking even of givers dear as one's eyes, who give without holding back, is far better. Both use the same phrase, கண்ணன்னார்.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:305","b":"k:1069","type":"same_image","note":"Both have the heart melt (உள்ளம் உருகும்) at the thought of begging. 1069 goes on to the one who refuses; 305 to the beggar at the moment of taking.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:306","b":"k:1063","type":"qualifies","note":"Both reject begging as a cure for poverty. In Parimelazhagar's reading the Kural's alternative is effort; 306's is to want less and consent to hardship. They differ on the remedy, not on the refusal, and the difference shows the Naladiyar's turn toward renunciation.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"n:307","b":"k:1057","type":"tension","note":"1057 speaks of beggars rejoicing when they meet givers who give without scorn, which assumes such givers exist. 307 says the man who does not scorn beggars has never been born. The conflict holds only if 307 is taken literally; Mudaliar's explanations treat it as overstatement or wordplay.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"n:308","b":"k:1070","type":"agrees","note":"In both, the asker's life goes out at the word of refusal. 1070 turns the point against the refuser; 308 stays with the asker.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:309","b":"k:1066","type":"agrees","note":"1066 finds nothing more degrading to the tongue than asking, even for water for a cow. 309 also places the disgrace in the spoken request rather than in dependence.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:310","b":"k:803","type":"agrees","note":"803 asks what use old friendship is if a friend's liberties aren't accepted as one's own. On Shanmuganar's reading, 310 shows the cost of that failure to the friend who relied on it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:311","b":"k:719","type":"agrees","note":"Both tell those who speak well in a good assembly not to speak in a mean one; 311 describes the mean assembly in detail.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:312","b":"n:315","type":"develops","note":"312 warns that the bad scholar will rise to fight; 315 shows the cost to whoever stays to argue: his teeth in his hand.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:313","b":"k:649","type":"agrees","note":"649 says those who cannot say a few faultless words long to say many; 313 shows the same many-worded speaker in debate.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:314","b":"k:403","type":"agrees","note":"403 says even the unlearned are good if they stay silent before the learned; 314's fool fails at exactly that.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:315","b":"k:857","type":"agrees","note":"857 says those of bitter understanding who hold to hostility do not see the truth that brings victory; 315's disputants want victory and will not accept the truth (மெய் கொள்ளார்).","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:316","b":"k:69","type":"same_image","note":"In 69 a mother rejoices more than at his birth when she hears her son called சான்றோன். In 316 the சான்றோர் themselves grieve for the mother of a fool. The two verses are mirror images.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:317","b":"n:318","type":"develops","note":"317 separates a book's text from its meaning; 318 separates those who keep the text from those who grasp the meaning and teach it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:318","b":"k:650","type":"agrees","note":"650 likens those who cannot explain what they learned to flowers without scent; 318 sets them apart from scholars who make others understand.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:319","b":"n:318","type":"develops","note":"318 says true scholars make a book's meaning clear to others; 319 sets out what that explanation must contain.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:320","b":"k:409","type":"tension","note":"409 says the learned of low birth have a worth the unlearned of high birth lack. 320 says that however much they learn, those without good birth cannot do what the well-born do. The conflict depends on reading இற்பிறப்பு as birth into a good family, as both commentators do, and on counting the capacity 320 denies as part of the worth 409 gives to learning.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:320","b":"k:992","type":"agrees","note":"992 makes birth in a good family one of the two parts of courtesy; 320 credits good birth with the restraint to overlook another's petty understanding.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:321","b":"n:322","type":"same_image","note":"Both use milk-rice whose taste goes unknown: the ladle that stirs it and the dog that prefers leather.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:321","b":"k:415","type":"agrees","note":"415 makes the words of the good a staff on slippery ground; in 321 the learned value such words while the fool mocks them.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:322","b":"k:418","type":"agrees","note":"Both describe hearing that isn't hearing: 418's ear unpierced by instruction is deaf though it hears, and 322's listeners lend an ear and still do not hear (செவிகொடுத்தும் கேட்கலார்).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:323","b":"k:420","type":"same_image","note":"Both end on the same verdict, 'what matter whether they die or live'. The Kural aims it at those who taste with the mouth and not the ear; 323 at those who see death coming and do no good.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:324","b":"k:999","type":"agrees","note":"999 says the world is dark even by day for those who cannot laugh with others; 324 asks why anyone would refuse to smile with those he meets.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:325","b":"k:158","type":"agrees","note":"158 tells one to defeat those who wrong one out of arrogance by one's own forbearance; 325 shows that defeat in public, though it doesn't name forbearance.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:326","b":"n:332","type":"develops","note":"326 shows the cost of putting off virtue until old age; 332 names the usual excuse, that the household's needs come first.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:327","b":"k:1006","type":"agrees","note":"1006 names the same two failures, not using one's wealth and not giving to the deserving. 327 adds a third: not taking the good path that protects.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:328","b":"k:335","type":"same_image","note":"Both picture the end, when the tongue no longer works. 335 urges good deeds before that moment; 328 shows the gesture that comes too late.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"n:328","b":"n:332","type":"agrees","note":"Both mock the words of postponement: பின்னறிவாம் here, மற்றறிவாம் in 332.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:329","b":"k:333","type":"agrees","note":"333 says wealth doesn't stay, so do lasting deeds as soon as you have it. 329 shows people who think of such deeds only when their means are gone.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:330","b":"k:336","type":"agrees","note":"336 states the general fact that one here yesterday is gone today. 330 makes it the death of one's own loved ones and still finds people unmoved.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"n:331","b":"k:337","type":"agrees","note":"337 mocks those who cannot know they will live another moment yet plan in crores; 331's turtle is a picture of that ignorance.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:332","b":"k:36","type":"agrees","note":"36 says to do virtue without saying 'we'll see to it then' (அன்றறிவாம்); 332 mocks the household version of the same words (மற்றறிவாம்).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:333","b":"k:140","type":"agrees","note":"On Shanmuganar's reading the two make the same claim: learning without knowing how to live with the world is still ignorance. On Mudaliar's reading, where உலகம் is the worlds after death, the link does not hold.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:334","b":"k:997","type":"same_image","note":"Both compare a person without human quality to an inert thing, a tree in 997 and a stone in 334. 334 goes further and ranks the stone above the person for its usefulness.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"n:335","b":"n:313","type":"same_image","note":"Both put the fool's urge to speak in an itching tongue (சுனை): in 313 the itch to debate, here the itch to abuse.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:336","b":"k:967","type":"agrees","note":"Both warn against following those who do not accept you. 967 counts the cost in honour; 336 in futility.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:337","b":"n:304","type":"qualifies","note":"304 says the worthy will not stand behind the hoarding rich; 337 says people at large do. 304 describes the exception to 337's rule.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:338","b":"n:327","type":"agrees","note":"Both list the goods a fool's life lacks: 327 enjoyment, giving and the good path; 338 adds love and renown.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:339","b":"k:816","type":"agrees","note":"816 rates being a stranger to the wise far above close friendship with a fool; 339 says friendship with the undiscerning is painful even if it brought the whole earth.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:340","b":"k:978","type":"agrees","note":"978 says smallness adorns itself in self-admiration; 340 shows the self-praiser mocked as mad.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:341","b":"k:373","type":"agrees","note":"Both say study does not override what a person already is. In Parimelazhagar's reading 373 credits the lasting understanding to fate; 341 credits it to base nature.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:341","b":"n:345","type":"same_image","note":"Both use a domestic animal that keeps its habit despite good feeding: the hen to its rubbish heap, the dog to others' leftovers.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:342","b":"k:976","type":"agrees","note":"976 says the small have no thought of cherishing the great and taking them as guides; 342 shows the base man inventing excuses not to go to them.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:343","b":"k:973","type":"agrees","note":"On Shanmuganar's reading 343 matches 973: being placed high does not make the base high, nor does it change the worthy.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:343","b":"k:977","type":"agrees","note":"On Mudaliar's reading, prosperity turns the base man to a new course, as 977 says distinction falling to the unworthy turns into excess.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:344","b":"k:104","type":"agrees","note":"The first half restates 104 with the same millet and palm, and Mudaliar cites it here.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:344","b":"k:105","type":"agrees","note":"105 says a benefit's measure is the worth of the one who receives it; 344's second half shows that measure at zero, with palm-sized help counting for nothing among the ungrateful.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:346","b":"k:978","type":"agrees","note":"978 says greatness always bows and smallness admires itself; 346 shows both, with an emperor's wealth on one side and a fraction of a coin on the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:346","b":"k:963","type":"agrees","note":"963 asks for humility in abundance; 346's worthy keep it even with an emperor's wealth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:347","b":"k:505","type":"agrees","note":"505 makes one's deeds the touchstone of greatness and smallness; 347 and 350 apply it to the base who have grown rich, whose wealth cannot hide what their deeds show.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:348","b":"k:1079","type":"develops","note":"1079's base man finds fault on seeing others well fed and clothed; 348's rejoices when others suffer. They are the same envy seen from both sides.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:349","b":"k:810","type":"agrees","note":"810 praises those who do not change toward old friends; 349 gives the worthy that constancy and shows the base without it.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:350","b":"n:347","type":"agrees","note":"The two share their closing lines word for word: 347 makes the point with an object, the jewelled sandal, and 350 with an animal, the young bullocks.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:352","b":"k:823","type":"agrees","note":"Both say study does not change what a person is: 823 says the unworthy rarely become good at heart however many good books they learn, and 352 says those without fine discernment never gain it from flawless books.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:353","b":"k:184","type":"qualifies","note":"184 ranks harsh words to a man's face above careless words behind his back. 353 doesn't let face-to-face speech off: the base are shameless precisely because they list a man's faults standing beside him.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:354","b":"k:978","type":"agrees","note":"On Mudaliar's reading of 354 as a figure, both make self-display the mark of the small: 978 says smallness adorns itself in self-admiration, and 354's wantons adorn and vaunt themselves.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:355","b":"k:1077","type":"agrees","note":"Both say the base give only to force: 1077's base won't shake their wet hands except for those who would break their jaws, and 355's chisel only moves when struck.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:355","b":"k:1078","type":"same_image","note":"1078's sugar-cane yields only when crushed, and 355's chisel cuts only when struck: two tools of labour for the same claim about the base.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"n:356","b":"k:108","type":"agrees","note":"108 tells one to keep a good done in mind and forget the bad that very day. 356 says the worthy do exactly this and the base do the reverse, dwelling on abuse.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:357","b":"k:109","type":"agrees","note":"Both make one kindness outweigh great injury: in 109 a wrong as painful as killing vanishes at the thought of one good, and in 357 the worthy bear a hundred faults for one kindness. 357 adds the base, for whom the scale runs the other way.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:358","b":"k:955","type":"agrees","note":"955 says an old family doesn't part from its nature even when its means shrink, and 358 says the well-born do in poverty what the base won't do in wealth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:359","b":"k:337","type":"agrees","note":"Both mock plans made without regard to life's shortness: 337's unwise think crores of thoughts without knowing they'll live a moment more, and 359's dreamers say 'today, now, soon' and perish.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:360","b":"k:576","type":"same_image","note":"Both picture people who have the means of feeling and none of the feeling: 576's men have eyes but no kind regard and are like trees in the soil, and 360's rich live in wealth but stay dry as the netti's pith.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"n:361","b":"k:53","type":"agrees","note":"53 asks what is lacking if the wife has worth and what is present if she lacks it. 361 gives the second half a picture: every grandeur present, and the house a wilderness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:362","b":"k:57","type":"qualifies","note":"Both say guards can't keep women chaste. 57 concludes that a woman's own steadfastness is the best guard; 362, on Mudaliar's reading, doubts that most women have it.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:363","b":"k:52","type":"develops","note":"52 says a life has nothing if the wife lacks household excellence. 363 names three such failings and calls each a weapon that kills the husband.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:364","b":"k:46","type":"tension","note":"46 asks what one gains by leaving a virtuous household life for the ascetic's way. 364 calls returning to married life a fault like stoning oneself, and Mudaliar takes it as the wise telling one to give household life up. The tension holds on Mudaliar's reading; if the verse is only about remarriage after a wife's death, as Shanmuganar's gloss allows, it is narrower.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:364","b":"n:56","type":"develops","note":"56 explains why the learned call marriage கடி, a dread; 364 opens by citing that name, and Mudaliar points back to 56.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:365","b":"k:47","type":"tension","note":"47 puts the one who lives household life according to its nature first among all who strive; 365 puts austerity first and household life in the middle. Parimelazhagar limits 47's 'all who strive' to those in the third stage, not full renunciants, which softens the conflict.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:365","b":"n:366","type":"develops","note":"Two ranked lists of lives side by side. Both put enjoyment of what one has in the middle and craving at the bottom, but the top changes from austerity to learning.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:367","b":"k:959","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the sprout to read what lies behind it: in 959 the sprout shows the soil as speech shows the lineage, and in 367 the paddy breeds true as a son's mind follows his father's.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:368","b":"k:169","type":"agrees","note":"169 calls the prosperity of the crooked and the ruin of the upright a thing to ponder. 368 describes that world: the worthy fallen and the base risen, the umbrella turned upside down.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:369","b":"k:788","type":"agrees","note":"788 says friendship removes distress at once, like a hand catching a slipping garment. 369 says a heart that doesn't respond so is not worth keeping alive.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:370","b":"n:371","type":"same_image","note":"The same frame, word for word in places, with two images of a love that lasts only while it is fed: the flood that ends with the rain, and the lamp that ends with the oil.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:371","b":"k:911","type":"agrees","note":"911 says the sweet words of women who desire wealth, not the man, bring harm. 371 gives the mechanism: their love burns only while the money feeds it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:372","b":"n:376","type":"agrees","note":"Both are the dupe's own speech recalling a vow of inseparable love, a death pact in 372 and a promise never to part in 376, broken when his money ran out.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:374","b":"k:912","type":"agrees","note":"912's women weigh the profit before they speak of their virtue; 374's women weigh only wealth, so the poor man is poison and the oil-presser with money is sugar.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:375","b":"k:920","type":"agrees","note":"920 names இருமனப் பெண்டிர், double-minded women, among the company of the ruined; 375's eel, showing one face to the snake and another to the fish, pictures the double mind.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:376","b":"n:378","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the fighting ram's horn, which twists away, for the courtesan's love turned to hostility.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:377","b":"k:918","type":"agrees","note":"918 says the embrace of deceiving women is an அணங்கு to those without discernment; 377's wild cow, whose lick pleases and then kills in Mudaliar's gloss, is the same danger, with public ridicule added.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:378","b":"k:914","type":"agrees","note":"914 says those who seek the wealth of grace won't touch the base charms of women who count only wealth; 378 says those who mean to reach the right path won't embrace them, which Mudaliar takes as the path to liberation.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:379","b":"k:913","type":"agrees","note":"913 compares the false embrace to holding a stranger's corpse in a dark room; 379 says the women belong to no one but their bodies. Both deny that anyone is present in the embrace.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:380","b":"k:917","type":"agrees","note":"917 says those without firmness of heart embrace women whose hearts are on other things; 380 says those full of sin don't grasp the guile of women whose minds are with another man.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:381","b":"k:57","type":"agrees","note":"On the commentators' reading, both place a wife's chastity in her own keeping: 57 calls the guard of her own steadfastness the best, and 381 praises the woman whose conduct leaves no one pursuing her. On Pope's reading, 381 instead credits the absence of temptation.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:382","b":"k:51","type":"qualifies","note":"51's good wife lives within her husband's means; 382's good wife keeps her duties of hospitality even when the means are one pot of water and the guests could drink the sea.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:383","b":"n:361","type":"develops","note":"The two halves of one claim: 361's mansion without a good wife is a wilderness, and 383's leaking hut with one is the only true home. Mudaliar's gloss on 383, that other houses are forests, makes the pairing explicit.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:384","b":"k:1302","type":"agrees","note":"1302 says sulking is right in measure like salt and spoils if prolonged; 384's good wife sulks at the right moment and relents sweetly.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:385","b":"k:1257","type":"qualifies","note":"385's wife never loses her shyness with her husband however often they meet; 1257's woman knows nothing of shame when her beloved does what she desires. The second describes desire overriding reserve after separation, so the two set limits on each other rather than conflict.","grade":"weak","better":"tension"},{"a":"n:387","b":"k:1311","type":"agrees","note":"Both wives refuse a chest that other women have shared: 1311 because all women feast on him with their eyes, and 387 because he comes from the courtesans unbathed.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"n:388","b":"n:390","type":"same_image","note":"The same speech to the minstrel in the same frame, with two images of the neglected side: the unstruck face of the drum and the sapless end joints of the sugar-cane.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:389","b":"n:387","type":"agrees","note":"Both wives read the courtesans' embraces on the husband's chest, unwashed in 387 and smeared with sandal in 389.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"n:391","b":"k:1302","type":"same_image","note":"Both make sulking love's salt: 1302 measures it, with too much spoiling the dish, and 391 says love without it has no salt at all.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:391","b":"k:1306","type":"agrees","note":"1306 says love without quarrels, serious and light, is like over-ripe or unripe fruit; 391 says that union and sulking together are love's way.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:384","b":"n:391","type":"agrees","note":"The good wife of 384 sulks at the right moment and relents sweetly; 391 gives the reason, that without sulking love has no salt.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:392","b":"k:1228","type":"same_image","note":"In both, a sound of the season becomes a killing instrument for the separated woman: the rain's thunder is a funeral drum in 392, and the herdsman's flute a weapon in 1228.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:392","b":"n:364","type":"same_image","note":"The funeral drum appears in both: in 364 it should teach a man to give up married life, and in 392 the thunder sounds like it to a woman whose husband is away. The same sound serves renunciation in one book and love in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:393","b":"k:1229","type":"agrees","note":"Both make evening the bewildering hour of separation: 393's மம்மர்கொள் மாலை, and 1229's evening that confuses the mind of the whole town.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:393","b":"n:397","type":"same_image","note":"Two evening scenes in one frame: the day's work falls silent, and the woman casts off her garland and weeps for the absent man.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:394","b":"k:1261","type":"same_image","note":"Both have the woman count the days of absence on her fingers, and Mudaliar cites 1261 with 394. In 1261 she speaks; in 394 the man imagines it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:395","b":"k:1086","type":"same_image","note":"Both make her brow a bow that guards her eyes: in 1086 the brows, if they would only screen her eyes, would spare him his pain, and in 395 they frighten off the kingfisher.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:396","b":"n:398","type":"qualifies","note":"The mother of 396 doubts that feet which shrank from soft dye can bear the stony path; the girl of 398 answers that the strength comes with the man, as riding comes with the horse.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:396","b":"n:399","type":"develops","note":"Both are the mother's speech after her daughter's flight: 396 fears for the girl's feet on the way, and 399 rereads the farewell embrace she didn't understand.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:400","b":"k:1225","type":"develops","note":"1225's woman asks what she did to morning and to evening, looking for the cause of her pain in the hours. 400 runs through such suspects and acquits them, blaming only the road her lover took for wealth.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies"},{"a":"t:1","b":"k:6","type":"agrees","note":"Both invocations name God as the one who conquered the five: the Kural's one who quenched the five sense-gates, Tirumular's ஐந்து வென்றனன்.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1","b":"k:9","type":"same_image","note":"Both invocations close on the number eight: the Kural's எண்குணத்தான், one of eight qualities, and Tirumular's உணர்ந்தெட்டே. Tradition reads both as the eight divine qualities, though Tirumular's eight may also be the eightfold form.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:7","b":"k:7","type":"agrees","note":"Both make God the one with no likeness: the Kural's தனக்குவமை இல்லாதான், Tirumular's தன்னைஒப்பாய் ஒன்றும் இல்லாத் தலைமகன். The Kural adds that only reaching his feet ends the mind's anxiety; Tirumular adds that he becomes father to whoever calls him so.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:33","b":"k:2","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural asks what learning is worth if it does not bow at God's feet; Tirumular turns the test on the worshippers, saying that even those who can recite many hymns lack understanding. Bowing is necessary in one, not enough in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:47","b":"k:48","type":"agrees","note":"Both rank the householder with or above the ascetic: the Kural's virtuous household life endures more than those who do penance, Tirumular's householders are equal to great ascetics.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:47","b":"n:54","type":"tension","note":"The Naladiyar's wise, seeing the pain in pleasure, keep off the household way; Tirumular says those who stay in the household equal great ascetics. The conflict holds if 47's first line is a general claim about householders, not only those who keep the Lord in mind.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:49","b":"k:10","type":"same_image","note":"Both picture salvation as swimming a sea: the Kural's sea of births, crossed by reaching the Lord's feet, and Tirumular's sea of sin, crossed by thinking of the Lord and reaching the shore beyond pasu and pasam.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:14","b":"t:13","type":"develops","note":"13 says the gods cannot measure the Lord; 14 states the result in a refrain, he stands beyond Brahma, beyond Vishnu and beyond everything, yet sees all.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:19","b":"t:18","type":"develops","note":"18 has the Lord make Kubera lord of wealth for his penance and say 'take this lordship'; 19 opens on the same இதுபதி and gives another seat granted for true penance.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:51","b":"k:543","type":"qualifies","note":"Tirumular finds all dharma within the Veda; the Kural makes the king's just sceptre the ஆதி of both the Brahmins' books and dharma. If ஆதி is read as support they agree that the Veda needs a guardian; if read as origin, the Kural places dharma's source in rule, not in scripture.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:55","b":"k:134","type":"agrees","note":"Both separate holding the Veda from living by it. The Kural says the Veda can be relearned but lost conduct ruins a Brahmin; Tirumular complains that reciters keep the six-limbed Veda but practise none of its qualities.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:64","b":"t:60","type":"same_image","note":"Both end in நீர்மேல் எழுத்து, writing on water. 60 leaves open what is futile; 64 states the condition: Agamas without the knowledge the Lord declared.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:64","b":"k:2","type":"agrees","note":"The Kural asks what learning is worth if one does not bow at the feet of the one of pure knowledge; Tirumular says crores of Agamas are writing on water without the knowledge the Lord declared. Both measure scripture by what it leads to.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:64","b":"n:140","type":"agrees","note":"The Naladiyar says worldly books without the books of wisdom are only clamour that never removes confusion; Tirumular says even the Agamas, if their knowledge is missed, are writing on water. Both deny that bulk of learning is the point.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:60","b":"n:135","type":"qualifies","note":"Both face writing beyond counting. The Naladiyar's answer is to choose what is fitting, like the bird that drinks the milk and leaves the water; Tirumular's is that the countless Agamas are hard even for the gods and their count is writing on water.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"t:66","b":"t:65","type":"develops","note":"65 says the Lord spoke Sanskrit and Tamil together; 66 says what both teach, bondage, release and death, and that either leads to knowing him.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:59","b":"t:66","type":"agrees","note":"59 widens the languages of the Lord's dharma to eighteen; 66 names two, Tamil and Sanskrit. Both refuse to tie revelation to one tongue.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:68","b":"t:92","type":"develops","note":"Both open 'by Nandi's grace' and both say Tirumular sought Mulan. 68 credits Nandi with his name and his stay in the land; 92 adds what grace did after: he became Sadasiva and entered true wisdom.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:81","b":"k:265","type":"agrees","note":"The Kural says tapas should be striven for here, since it gains what one wishes; Tirumular asks why be born again when people do not strive at tapas beforehand. Both use முயல் and place the effort now.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:85","b":"k:399","type":"agrees","note":"The Kural's learned love learning more when they see the world delighting in what delights them; Tirumular wishes the bliss he gained on the whole world. Both make a private good complete only when it is shared.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:85","b":"t:70","type":"agrees","note":"70 has the four masters told 'may you gain all that I gained'; 85 widens the same wish from four disciples to the whole world.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:95","b":"t:88","type":"same_image","note":"88 tells how Brahma and Vishnu could not find the foot and crown of the column of fire; 95 turns the same flame into the poet's own confession that he does not know its root.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:102","b":"t:69","type":"develops","note":"69 names Kalangi among Tirumular's seven disciples; 102 makes Kalangar the head of a line through Aghorar and others, carrying the succession one generation further.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:112","b":"t:116","type":"develops","note":"112 names one part of the Lord as the one who stands in the body and breathes; 116 makes the body a temple where Nandi has taken up residence like fire latent in bamboo.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:133","b":"k:126","type":"same_image","note":"Tirumular's third line nearly repeats the Kural's: ஒருமையுள் ஆமைபோல் ... ஐந்து அடக்கி. The Kural makes the restraint a protection through seven births (எழுமையும்); Tirumular's version ends instead with those who 'heard both' (இருமையும்) and stand flawless.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:120","b":"n:135","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the bird that drinks milk and leaves water. The Naladiyar makes it the wise learning only what is fitting; Tirumular makes it the parting that turns deeds into seeds that cannot sprout into births.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:140","b":"n:59","type":"qualifies","note":"The Naladiyar makes guarding the craving that enters through the five gates the effort that reaches release; Tirumular says the five come under control of themselves (தானே) once one has met the Lord. Restraint is a cause in one, a result in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:138","b":"k:10","type":"agrees","note":"The Kural says those who reach the Lord's feet cross the sea of births; Tirumular makes the feet the refuge, the path and the goal itself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:116","b":"t:725","type":"agrees","note":"Both call the body the Lord's temple: 116 where Nandi lives like fire in bamboo, 725 where Tirumular, who once thought the body a defect, found the Lord inside it and so tends it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:116","b":"n:48","type":"tension","note":"The Naladiyar calls the body a stinking cart that only the ignorant adorn and praise; Tirumular calls it the temple where the Lord dwells. The conflict holds if கோயில் is read as giving the body worth, not only as locating the Lord in it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:126","b":"t:1924","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1924 counts the thirty-six tattvas as they unfold from bindu and nada; 126 climbs the same thirty-six as the rungs of a ladder to release.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"t:128","b":"k:605","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural counts sloth and sleep among the pleasure-boats of those bound for ruin; Tirumular gives the same words (சோம்பு, தூக்கம்) to the freed resting in Sivam. The Kural's verdict is on worldly duty; Tirumular names a state beyond it.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"t:167","b":"n:26","type":"agrees","note":"The two verses share most of their words: the dancer in the skin bag who worked it and has left, and the indifference of what is then done to the body. Tirumular lists crows, revilers, milk drops and praisers; the Naladiyar dragging, careful burial, casting out and revilers.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:146","b":"t:161","type":"same_image","note":"Both build the body as a hut with two posts and one ridge beam. 146 says the life can't re-enter once the roof comes apart; 161 adds that it was thatched by a maker, not by ordinary thatchers.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:148","b":"k:336","type":"agrees","note":"The Kural says the world's 'greatness' is that one who was here yesterday is gone today; Tirumular shows it in a single evening: a meal, a talk with his wife, a small ache, and he is gone.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:148","b":"n:29","type":"same_image","note":"Both tell a death as a short row of ordinary acts: the Naladiyar's 'he stood, he sat, he lay down, he went', Tirumular's cooking, eating, talking and lying down. The Naladiyar draws the lesson to do good now; Tirumular draws none.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:144","b":"k:36","type":"agrees","note":"The Kural calls virtue the companion that does not perish when one perishes; Tirumular says that when the body falls only vows and knowledge go on the road with the dead, not wife or children.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:153","b":"n:21","type":"agrees","note":"Both take a king to make the point: the Naladiyar's kings under moon-white umbrellas were carried off and called dead, Tirumular's lord of the land rides a bier with the town behind and drums in front.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:158","b":"t:143","type":"same_image","note":"Both make the body a clay pot. 143 has it melt back to clay in the rain; 158 says even a broken pot is kept as shards, but a broken body is not kept a moment.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:157","b":"t:145","type":"develops","note":"145 ends the funeral with the bath that ends remembering; 157 retells it with the kin turning back at the town's edge and calls those who bathe and leave 'people without justice'.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:171","b":"n:10","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the honey bee whose stored honey others take. The Naladiyar spells out the lesson, that those who hoard without giving lose it all; Tirumular gives only the image.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:172","b":"n:6","type":"tension","note":"Both tell the rich to give their wealth away in view of Death, but the Naladiyar says no one has ever leapt past Death and escaped, while Tirumular says one who sheds his wealth may leap past him. The conflict holds if Tirumular's leap means escaping death; if it means dying free of fear or bondage, the two agree.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:172","b":"k:269","type":"agrees","note":"Both use the phrase of leaping past Death (கூற்றம் குதித்தல்) as something attainable: the Kural through the power of austerity, Tirumular through letting go of wealth.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"t:175","b":"t:171","type":"same_image","note":"The two verses end with the same line (காட்டிக் கொடுத்தவர் கைவிட்ட வாறே): the bees' hoard taken by the strong, and the body left by the kin who raised it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:173","b":"n:8","type":"agrees","note":"Both make wealth vanish without trace through a sudden image, the Naladiyar's lightning in a night cloud and Tirumular's capsizing boat; Tirumular adds a treasure in the body that does not sink.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:177","b":"n:22","type":"agrees","note":"Both read the sun's daily course as a measure of passing life: the Naladiyar urges help to others before one's days run out, Tirumular faults those who see the sun set and do not understand.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:184","b":"n:7","type":"same_image","note":"In the Naladiyar Death uses the sun as a measure to eat one's days; in Tirumular the sun and moon measure out the world from within. Both make the heavenly lights the counters of a life.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:179","b":"n:19","type":"agrees","note":"The Naladiyar says not to put off good deeds because one is young; Tirumular says to hold to Nandi while life lasts, since youth and strength wear away. The urgency is the same, the act urged differs.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:180","b":"n:17","type":"qualifies","note":"The Naladiyar warns men not to desire a woman who will one day stoop over a staff; Tirumular turns the lesson on the man, who was cane to women and is now bitter fruit.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"t:196","b":"k:227","type":"agrees","note":"The Kural says hunger never touches one used to sharing his food; Tirumular asks for a ladleful given before each good meal, the same habit in its smallest form.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:196","b":"n:2","type":"agrees","note":"Both make shared food the right use of plenty: the Naladiyar tells one who gains wealth to eat with many from that day, since wealth turns like a cart wheel; Tirumular asks for a ladleful before one eats.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:189","b":"t:167","type":"same_image","note":"In 167 the dancer leaves the skin bag, in 189 the king leaves the drum and it turns to clay. Both make the life a performer or ruler inside an instrument that is nothing once he goes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:188","b":"t:151","type":"develops","note":"151 has the five who ate rice with ghee depart at death; 188 makes the five guardians of a field who abandon it when their master's summons comes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:191","b":"t:184","type":"agrees","note":"Both say people do not understand that the sun measures out their lives, using the same verb (அளக்கின்றது) and the same complaint (அறிகிலார்).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:192","b":"n:11","type":"agrees","note":"Both take grey hair as the sign to heed: the Naladiyar's wise renounced in childhood knowing it would come, Tirumular faults those who see black hair turn white and still do not grasp it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:197","b":"k:259","type":"agrees","note":"Both rank not killing above ritual offering: the Kural above a thousand oblations of ghee, Tirumular as the brightest of the flowers of worship.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:198","b":"k:330","type":"agrees","note":"Both say killing is paid for after this life, but differently: the Kural through rebirth in a wretched, diseased body, Tirumular through hell; and Tirumular extends the guilt to those who only urged the killing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:199","b":"k:255","type":"agrees","note":"Both send the flesh-eater to hell; the Kural says hell's mouth will not open to let him out, Tirumular describes his being flung into fire-mouthed hell.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:199","b":"n:123","type":"agrees","note":"Both make eating creatures a deed that returns on the eater; the Naladiyar's crab-eater gets a wasting disease in a later life, Tirumular's flesh-eater hell.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:201","b":"n:86","type":"agrees","note":"Both argue from the wife already at home: the Naladiyar asks why a man with a loving wife looks at another's, Tirumular calls it leaving ripe jackfruit for a wild date.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:202","b":"n:84","type":"same_image","note":"Both end the adulterer with a lost leg: in the Naladiyar the leg is cut off as the king's punishment if he is caught, in Tirumular he breaks it falling from the sour mango he climbed.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:204","b":"n:42","type":"agrees","note":"Both say a woman's attractive surface hides what should repel: the Naladiyar the body's foulness under the skin, Tirumular poison under fair leaves and fruit.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:205","b":"n:81","type":"agrees","note":"Both shrink the adulterer's pleasure: small against great fear in the Naladiyar, a dream and a whirlpool in Tirumular.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:206","b":"n:376","type":"agrees","note":"Both describe a woman who promises closeness and then turns away; the Naladiyar's said she would never part, Tirumular's bids the man stay near and leaves talking to others.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:207","b":"n:138","type":"same_image","note":"Both use sugarcane to show sweetness changing over time: the Naladiyar's cane eaten from the tip grows sweeter like the friendship of the learned, Tirumular's cane juice in the hand turns to neem in the body.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:209","b":"n:284","type":"agrees","note":"Both say kin and dependants fall away from the poor: the Naladiyar's crows leave the carcass and no one asks 'are you well?', Tirumular's dependants turn unloving.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:209","b":"k:1047","type":"agrees","note":"Both describe the poor man estranged from those closest to him; the Kural goes as far as his own mother.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:210","b":"k:370","type":"agrees","note":"Both say what can never be filled is ended not by filling it but by removing its cause: craving in the Kural, impurity in Tirumular. This depends on reading the pit as desire and not only the belly.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:212","b":"k:345","type":"agrees","note":"Both treat ties as obstacles to ending births: the Kural asks why one bent on that would keep other ties when even the body is excess, Tirumular calls clinging kin worse than deeds.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:213","b":"k:343","type":"agrees","note":"Both name the five senses as what must be overcome; the Kural says their domain must be crushed, Tirumular describes the suffering they cause until one turns to the Lord.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:214","b":"k:259","type":"qualifies","note":"Tirumular praises the oblation as sustaining sky, earth and gods; the Kural ranks not killing above a thousand oblations. Read together, the rite keeps its worth but not the first place.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:215","b":"n:190","type":"agrees","note":"If புறங்கொடுத்து உண்ணுவர means eating after giving, both describe the worthy as feeding others first and eating after.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:228","b":"k:30","type":"agrees","note":"Both define the Brahmin's state by regard for every living being: the Kural's அந்தணர் are clothed in kindness to all lives, Tirumular's Brahman includes knowing all lives as one's equal.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:229","b":"k:365","type":"agrees","note":"Both define the goal by the end of craving: the Kural says the freed are those freed of craving, Tirumular that Vedanta is the place where craving ends.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:230","b":"k:280","type":"agrees","note":"Both deny that marks of the body make the holy man: the Kural says neither shaving nor long hair is needed, Tirumular that the thread is cotton and the tuft hair.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:231","b":"k:134","type":"agrees","note":"Both make the Brahmin depend on conduct, not birth: the Kural says his birth is ruined when conduct declines, Tirumular denies the name to those without truth and knowledge.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:234","b":"k:30","type":"agrees","note":"Tirumular's அந்தண்மை பூண்ட echoes the Kural's செந்தண்மை பூண்டு: both define the அந்தணர் by a coolness of heart they wear. Tirumular adds that the land where they live prospers.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:237","b":"k:346","type":"agrees","note":"If the two attachments are 'I' and 'mine', both say cutting them raises one above the gods: the Kural to a world higher than theirs, Tirumular to an offering they receive.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"t:232","b":"t:224","type":"qualifies","note":"224 defines Brahmins by the daily rites; 232 makes the highest state one reached once the rites are set aside, so the rites are a stage, not the goal.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:238","b":"k:326","type":"agrees","note":"Both say Death does not approach the good: the Kural that it does not advance on one who keeps from killing, Tirumular that it spares the good while the unlearned king kills.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:239","b":"k:545","type":"agrees","note":"Both tie the land's prosperity to the king's conduct: the Kural rain and harvest to just rule, Tirumular the land's decline to a king who neglects austerity.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:240","b":"k:274","type":"develops","note":"Both condemn holy garb worn without the life it stands for; Tirumular adds that the king should force such men to live by it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:241","b":"t:230","type":"develops","note":"230 says the thread and tuft are only cotton and hair; 241 says they should be cut off from the ignorant who wear them, a ruler's remedy for what 230 diagnoses.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:243","b":"k:560","type":"agrees","note":"Both say the king who fails to guard harms cows and the virtuous; the Kural gives the cost to the land (less milk, forgotten books), Tirumular the cost to the king (hell).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:247","b":"k:549","type":"agrees","note":"Both make punishing wrongdoers a king's proper work, not a stain on him; Tirumular sets it beside punishment in the next world.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:248","b":"k:11","type":"agrees","note":"Both call rain nectar (அமிழ்தம், அமுது): the Kural because the world is sustained by it, Tirumular because it makes the trees flow with nectar.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:250","b":"k:527","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the crow that calls its flock before eating: the Kural for the wealth that comes to those who keep kin close, Tirumular as a lesson in giving.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:250","b":"n:271","type":"agrees","note":"Both say to share food with all before eating, friend or not; the Naladiyar warns that the door of the other world is shut on those who shut their door and eat.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:252","b":"n:94","type":"agrees","note":"Both set the bar for giving so low no one can refuse it: the Naladiyar asks for even a fragment of a rice grain daily before eating, Tirumular a handful before eating, with a leaf for God and a kind word added.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:253","b":"k:226","type":"agrees","note":"Both make feeding those who have nothing the very form of virtue; the Kural calls it where the wealthy store their wealth, Tirumular says the learned call such food virtue itself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:254","b":"k:335","type":"agrees","note":"Both press good deeds by picturing the deathbed: the Kural's hiccup that stops the tongue, Tirumular's fever and eyes that no longer open.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:255","b":"n:19","type":"agrees","note":"Both reject youth as a reason to put off virtue: the Naladiyar's storm drops unripe fruit, Tirumular's Death does not ask whether one is young.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:258","b":"k:10","type":"same_image","note":"Both picture a great sea to be crossed. In the Kural it is the sea of births, swum by reaching the Lord's feet; Tirumular makes it the sea of deeds, crossed in the boat of austerity and virtue.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:260","b":"k:1008","type":"same_image","note":"Both compare unshared wealth to the fruit of the poisonous etti. In the Kural it ripens in the middle of town where no one can use it; in Tirumular it ripens and falls, and the usurer buries his store in the ground.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:261","b":"n:102","type":"agrees","note":"Both fault the person who watches beauty, youth and bodies pass and still does no good thing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:263","b":"k:227","type":"develops","note":"The Kural says the fiery disease of hunger will not touch one who shares his food; Tirumular extends the promise to cough, fever and tumour, taking literally what the Kural keeps to hunger.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:267","b":"k:37","type":"agrees","note":"Both read present fortune as the fruit of past virtue: the Kural points to the palanquin's bearer and rider, Tirumular to joy and sorrow settled by former deeds.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:268","b":"k:113","type":"agrees","note":"Both refuse gain got by leaving fairness: the Kural says to give it up the same day, Tirumular that the Lord will not let pleasure sought that way stand.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:270","b":"k:76","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Both correct what 'the ignorant' (அறியார், அறிவிலார்) say about love: the Kural that love serves not only virtue but also against wrong, Tirumular that love is not a second thing beside Siva. Each widens love past a limit set on it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:272","b":"k:77","type":"same_image","note":"Both join bones, burning and love. In the Kural virtue scorches the loveless as the sun a boneless worm; in Tirumular a man burns his own bones and flesh, and without love it still does not reach God.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:279","b":"t:270","type":"develops","note":"270 says love is Siva; 279 spells out what follows: he dwells within love and is close support only to those who dwell in it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:287","b":"t:270","type":"qualifies","note":"270 says those who know love is Siva rest as that love; 287 warns that people who claim to know him through love, while blind to their own births and deaths, do not.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:284","b":"t:272","type":"agrees","note":"Both set love above attainment: 272 says self-burning austerity does not reach him without love, 284 that the siddhas never discern him while devotees receive release.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:291","b":"k:393","type":"same_image","note":"Both give the learned eyes. The Kural says the unlearned have two sores for eyes; Tirumular places an eye in the thought of the learned.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:294","b":"k:398","type":"agrees","note":"Both make learning something that goes with a person: the Kural through seven births, Tirumular as a companion, without saying how far.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:298","b":"k:350","type":"agrees","note":"Both turn attachment toward the Lord: the Kural says cling to the one free of clinging in order to let go, Tirumular says if you would cling, cling to the Supreme.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:292","b":"t:252","type":"develops","note":"252 asks of everyone a leaf for God; 292 makes such worship of his feet the content of learning itself.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"t:302","b":"k:413","type":"agrees","note":"Both raise listeners to the gods: the Kural's listeners, though on earth, equal the gods fed by oblation; Tirumular's hearers of Siva's word become gods.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:305","b":"k:415","type":"same_image","note":"Both picture the moral life as walking on slippery ground. The Kural gives the words of the virtuous as a staff there; Tirumular asks the mind not to slip from listening and conduct.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:307","b":"k:414","type":"agrees","note":"Both call listening a துணை, a support. The Kural makes it a staff in faltering; Tirumular places it between the body and the mind on Siva's feet in a ladder of supports.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:308","b":"k:407","type":"same_image","note":"An image without life: the Kural likens the unlearned man's good looks to a clay doll; Tirumular makes the Lord a stone cow, giving no milk, to those who neither recite nor realise him.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"t:318","b":"k:2","type":"agrees","note":"Both deny learning its fruit without the Lord: the Kural asks what learning gains if one does not worship the feet of the one of pure knowledge, Tirumular calls the learned without Siva-knowledge wretches and fools.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:310","b":"k:393","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural makes learning the eyes and gives the unlearned two sores; 310, if read as it seems, gives sight to the unlearned who have the grace-eye and denies the learned their joy. The contrast depends on reading கல்லாதவர் as the book-unlearned.","grade":"weak","better":"tension"},{"a":"t:317","b":"k:403","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural finds the unlearned very good if they keep silent before the learned; Tirumular will not even look at or listen to unlearned fools, though he too grants that the merely unlearned are good.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:316","b":"t:315","type":"qualifies","note":"315 mocks those who reckoned and wrote till they wore out; 316 keeps reckoning (கணக்கு) but makes the true one the only way to see. Reckoning is not rejected, only the wrong kind.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:320","b":"k:117","type":"develops","note":"The Kural says the world won't count as ruin the low estate of one who stays fair; Tirumular promises those who stand in the middle knowledge, no hell and godhood. The same virtue, given rewards beyond this world.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:332","b":"k:925","type":"agrees","note":"Both find the fault of drinking in self-forgetting: the Kural's drinker pays to buy self-oblivion (மெய்யறியாமை), Tirumular's sectarians lose the power they sought because they forget themselves (தம்மை மறத்தல்).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:335","b":"t:325","type":"develops","note":"325 names the nectar of bliss that comes in samadhi; 335 says how, by binding the breath to taste the moon's nectar, and sets it against liquor's loss of understanding.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:324","b":"k:922","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural simply says do not drink. Tirumular agrees, but keeps the vocabulary of drink: the clear toddy (தேறல்) he commends is Siva's bliss. Abstinence becomes a substitution.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"t:337","b":"t:320","type":"parallel_domain","note":"The same phrase in two domains: in 320 those who stand in the middle (நடுவுநின்றார்) gain knowledge and godhood; in 337 the earth that does not stand level (நடுவுநில்லாது) tilts toward ruin until a sage restores it.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:337","b":"k:118","type":"same_image","note":"The Kural's fair man is a balance whose beam leans to no side; Tirumular's world, leaning to one side, is set level by Agastya. One image of rightness as balance, moral in one, cosmic in the other.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:345","b":"t:343","type":"develops","note":"343 says the myth of the three cities is about the soul's impurities; 345 does the same for the slaying of Death, turning it into breath and fire raised in the body.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:346","b":"t:343","type":"develops","note":"A third deed read inwardly: after the cities as impurities (343), the burning of Kama becomes the yogi's restraint of the mind and the linga's way.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:340","b":"t:353","type":"agrees","note":"Both tell Daksha's punishment by Siva: 340 the head cut and burned and restored, 353 the sacrifice broken by Siva's anger and the gods scattering.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:348","b":"t:343","type":"qualifies","note":"343 calls fools those who say he burned three cities, meaning the cities are the impurities; 348 still names him the destroyer of the three cities, so the epithet stands and only its literal sense is questioned.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:351","b":"t:197","type":"tension","note":"197 makes not killing the finest flower of worship; 351 tells, with approval shown by the reward, of a devotee who cut down his own father to protect his worship. The conflict depends on reading 351 as commending the act rather than only reporting a legend.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:362","b":"t:372","type":"develops","note":"362 mentions in passing the two who quarrel over who is king while the Lord stands as light; 372 tells the same quarrel as the search for the head and feet of the column of fire, naming Brahma and Vishnu and their folly.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:366","b":"t:371","type":"same_image","note":"Both turn on Siva carrying a skull: 366, as tradition reads it, the head he took from Brahma; 371 the bones and skulls of the gods he bears so they won't crumble to dust.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:368","b":"t:349","type":"develops","note":"349 says only that Siva gave the discus to Achyuta; 368 and 369 tell how Vishnu could not bear it until he worshipped Siva, who then divided its power.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:370","b":"t:353","type":"develops","note":"353 tells of Daksha's sacrifice breaking down at Siva's anger; 370 adds an episode from it, Vishnu turning the discus Siva gave him against Siva's side.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:371","b":"n:49","type":"same_image","note":"Both turn to skulls. The Naladiyar's skulls in the burning ground grin at the living as proof of what the body comes to; Tirumular's are borne by Siva, and his bearing them is what keeps them from becoming dust. One image teaches the body's end, the other the Lord's hold even over what is dead.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:372","b":"k:844","type":"agrees","note":"The Kural defines folly as the pride that says 'we are bright'; Tirumular shows it at the top of the cosmos, Brahma and Vishnu each saying 'I am the Lord' through folly (பேதைமை), and failing before the fire.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:379","b":"t:349","type":"develops","note":"349 records Siva's giving the sword to Brahma and the discus to Vishnu; 379 judges such gifts: the gods who worship the giver of the sword do not reach his feet, as those who give themselves do.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:380","b":"t:349","type":"same_image","note":"Both open on circling through the aeon (ஊழி வலஞ்செய்): in 349 Brahma and Vishnu circle Siva and receive weapons, in 380 one who circles is answered as Siva outshines the sun of dissolution.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:404","b":"k:1","type":"agrees","note":"The Kural says the world has the Primal Lord as its first, as letters have 'a'; Tirumular says one alone creates, protects and removes the worlds. The Kural names only primacy; Tirumular spells it out as the three acts.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"t:404","b":"k:1062","type":"qualifies","note":"Both speak of the one who made the world. The Kural, conditionally, curses him if he meant some to live by begging; Tirumular praises him without reserve. Because the Kural's curse is conditional, this is a difference of attitude, not a contradiction.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"t:407","b":"t:404","type":"same_image","note":"The same quatrain twice, with ஓராயமே (one host) in place of ஒருவனுமே (one alone): if ஆயம் is the assembled powers, the second recasts the one agent as a company.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:386","b":"t:404","type":"qualifies","note":"386 gives creation to one He and one She with five sons; 404 to one alone. Read with 396 and 401, the She is his Shakti, so the two agree that the agent is one.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"t:396","b":"t:386","type":"develops","note":"386 names the pair who create; 396 says what their creating is: play that bears fruit in every season.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:382","b":"t:1923","type":"develops","note":"382 has bindu rise from nada and the will; 1923 lists what unfolds from bindu as it rises: kundalini, the gods, the powers and speech.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:410","b":"t:1930","type":"agrees","note":"Both make the bindu of Maya the source of everything: 410 by listing sun, moon, elements, speech and mind, 1930 by calling bindu the cause in all its effects, as clay in a pot.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:398","b":"t:1927","type":"qualifies","note":"1927 gives the five acts to the Supreme; 398 says the five lords who carry them out are born of bindu and still bound by anava. The acts are his, the agents are not.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:417","b":"t:1930","type":"qualifies","note":"Both use the pot. In 1930 bindu is the clay that stays in the pot, the material cause; in 417 the Lord is the potter who shapes it. Read together they separate maker and material.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"t:422","b":"n:331","type":"same_image","note":"Both put the world in the pot on the fire (உலை). The Naladiyar's turtle plays in the water not knowing it is being cooked, as men revel while Death waits; Tirumular sees the whole world softened and burnt like rice, a cosmic act he says he has come to know, not a warning to the heedless.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:426","b":"t:425","type":"qualifies","note":"The same four names of dissolution, defined differently: daily dissolution is sleep's stupor in 425 and shedding the two bodies in 426. Only the last, grace, stays fixed across 425 to 428.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"t:433","b":"t:372","type":"agrees","note":"372 has Brahma and Vishnu, each claiming lordship, searching in vain for Hara's feet; 433 says the three gods together do not know how he placed the body. The gods' ignorance of Siva is the same theme, turned here to concealment.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:438","b":"t:386","type":"develops","note":"386 gives the world-maker five sons and calls the creator the holy one; 438 names the gods (Maheshvara, Hara, Tirumal, Ayan) as forms the one Lord becomes and works through.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:440","b":"t:1930","type":"same_image","note":"Both use clay and pot. In 1930 bindu stays in the worlds as clay stays in the pot, the material cause; in 440 one clay making many vessels is the one Lord behind all wombs, who like the eye is not seen.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:444","b":"k:9","type":"develops","note":"Kural 9 calls the one to be worshipped எண்குணத்தான், possessor of eight qualities, without naming him; Tirumular gives the eight qualities to the matted-haired Siva who keeps the world and stays near the mind.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:447","b":"t:404","type":"agrees","note":"404 repeats ஒருவனுமே: one alone made, kept and removed the worlds. 445 to 447 use the same refrain form for creation, and 447 ends, like 404's second line, with his keeping what he made.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:450","b":"n:47","type":"same_image","note":"Both see the body as a pot. In the Naladiyar it is a woman's body, a pot oozing filth that fools admire for its skin; in Tirumular it is the vessel in which the Lord's form stands like milk in water, so to see within it is bliss.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:450","b":"n:135","type":"same_image","note":"Both use milk mixed in water. The Naladiyar's swan separates them, as the wise sort what is worth learning; Tirumular's milk stays in the water, the Lord present throughout the body and seen only by steady sight.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:450","b":"t:431","type":"develops","note":"431, opening the chapter on concealment, says the heart lives with the Lord and does not know his form; 450, closing the chapter on grace, has the poet see that form standing in the body like milk in water.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:443","b":"t:440","type":"same_image","note":"Both turn to clay. In 440 one clay making many vessels shows one Lord behind many wombs; in 443 the potter shaping clay as he wishes shows the world becoming whatever Nandi wills.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:457","b":"t:2926","type":"agrees","note":"The same verse appears twice: 2926 reads ஆகிப் படைத்தன in line 2 where 457 reads மூழ்கின்ற முத்தனும், and 457's recorded variants are 2926's readings.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:461","b":"n:46","type":"same_image","note":"Both list the body as bone, sinew and flesh. The Naladiyar asks which of these parts is the woman a man desires; Tirumular sees in the same parts a house built by the Lord and seeks its builder.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:470","b":"n:47","type":"tension","note":"Both describe the body by its nine openings. The Naladiyar makes it a pot seeping filth and calls the man who admires it a fool; Tirumular makes the nine gates sluices set by the Lord, who dwells at its crown and is met there. The conflict is over the body's worth and depends on reading 470 as valuing the body, not only describing how it was made.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"t:470","b":"t:725","type":"agrees","note":"725 says Tirumular once thought the body a blemish, then found the Lord had made it his temple, and so cherishes it; 470 already finds the Lord set within the body he built.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:489","b":"t:1932","type":"agrees","note":"Both use the seed or tuber and its sprout: 1932 says seed and sprout exist only together, 489 that the Lord is present as each stage of the growth.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"t:497","b":"t:115","type":"develops","note":"115 states that when Pati draws near, Pasu and Pasam do not remain; 497 describes those in whom this has happened: become Siva, free of பசுபாசம், the malas destroyed.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:492","b":"t:474","type":"agrees","note":"Both make embodiment serve release: in 474 the body is fashioned so that the bond may leave the soul, in 492 Shakti and Siva yoke souls to maya so as to make them full of Siva.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:501","b":"n:38","type":"agrees","note":"Both say a small gift in worthy hands grows beyond its size: the Naladiyar's banyan seed shades the sky, Tirumular's sesame of gold yields release and siddhi.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:501","b":"k:221","type":"tension","note":"Both say which gift truly counts. The Kural makes the recipient's need the test, calling all other giving a measured loan; Tirumular makes it the recipient's knowledge of Siva, and says gifts to fools bear no fruit however large. The conflict depends on taking மூடர் as the spiritually ignorant whatever their need.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:501","b":"t:505","type":"develops","note":"505 continues the point from the other side: a gift to those without conduct or vows is like milking a barren cow or a crop past its season.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:505","b":"n:38","type":"agrees","note":"The converse case: the Naladiyar's small gift that reaches the hands of the worthy grows like a banyan seed until the sky looks small; Tirumular's gift to the undisciplined is a crop sown after its season. Both make the recipient's worth decide the gift's fruit.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:506","b":"t:250","type":"tension","note":"250 tells the giver to give to everyone and not ask who they are; 506 calls giving to those without discipline and love a great fault. The two conflict if 506's ஈவது means giving in general; if it means imparting yoga teaching, they speak of different gifts.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:506","b":"k:221","type":"tension","note":"The Kural defines true giving as giving to those who have nothing, all else being a loan expecting return; Tirumular judges the recipient by discipline and love, not by need. The conflict holds on the reading of 506 as about material giving.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:509","b":"k:278","type":"agrees","note":"The Kural's many who bathe with an air of distinction while their minds are stained, and Tirumular's deceitful who tramp from water to water: both deny that outer washing reaches the stain within.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:513","b":"t:116","type":"develops","note":"116 says Nandi has made his home in the temple called this body; 513 draws the consequence for pilgrims, who search outside for one already inside.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:510","b":"t:512","type":"qualifies","note":"510 says bathers alone cannot join the Lord; 512 lets the gods become meritorious by bathing in the Ganga. Bathing gains merit but not union, if the two are read together.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:517","b":"k:18","type":"parallel_domain","note":"The same link between rain and worship, run in opposite directions: in the Kural, if the sky dries up, festival and worship cease even for the gods; in Tirumular, if the pujas lapse, the earth has no rain.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"t:518","b":"k:560","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Both list what declines when a guardian fails: in the Kural, if the king does not guard, cows give less and Brahmins forget their books; in Tirumular, if the temple pujas stop, kings come to harm and theft grows. The king is the cause in one and the casualty in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:519","b":"t:241","type":"agrees","note":"241 says that when those whose ignorance remains take up the tuft and thread, the land withers and the king loses standing; 519 says the same of a Brahmin in name only who worships in the temple.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:525","b":"t:524","type":"develops","note":"524 makes the downward face the lower cosmos and the Lord of the aeon; 525 makes it the flower that opens a hundredfold and the endless Shakti.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:529","b":"t:229","type":"agrees","note":"229 says Brahmins who heard Vedanta and kept their craving have not truly heard it; 529 says Vedic Brahmins who dwell on pleasure forget the Lord. In both, Vedic standing is undone by desire.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:534","b":"k:899","type":"agrees","note":"Commentators read the Kural's வேந்தன் as Indra, who falls midway if those of exalted vows are angered; Tirumular names Indra's throne (வாசவன் பீடம்) and great kings' thrones as ruined when devotees are troubled.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:530","b":"k:892","type":"agrees","note":"Both start from not honouring the great (பேணாது, பேணார்); the Kural warns of ills that do not leave, Tirumular calls those who do it base.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:536","b":"t:201","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the good thing at hand left for a worse one: the ripe jackfruit for the wild date in 201, the gem for a stone and ghee and curd for bitter food in 536. One is about another man's wife, the other about leaving the guru's knowledge.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"t:537","b":"t:534","type":"agrees","note":"534 threatens land, kings and Indra's throne when devotees are troubled; 537 sends those who speak ill of them to low hell. One names the public ruin, the other the personal one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:539","b":"k:152","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural asks for bearing wrongs done to one and then forgetting them; Tirumular places forbearance inward, as checking the creature of craving that grips the senses. Both call it forbearance, but the Kural's is toward others and Tirumular's toward oneself.","grade":"forced","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"t:545","b":"n:175","type":"agrees","note":"The Naladiyar's drain water that joins a great river becomes holy water, and the lowborn who join the good stand like a hill; Tirumular says joining the great is supreme bliss. Both make the company one joins transform the joiner.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:548","b":"k:443","type":"agrees","note":"The Kural calls cherishing the great and making them one's own the rarest of rare things; Tirumular's verse begins with அருமை and ends with 'I have joined those strong in holiness'.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:547","b":"t:543","type":"develops","note":"543 resolves to go along with those who run to the Lord and reach his feet with them; 547 tells it happening, going with the devotees' devotees and being called in at the gate.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:551","b":"t:219","type":"tension","note":"219 says that when the fire is worshipped and rises, the ills of deeds burn away like a wick; 551 calls sacrifice a mean path with no way through. The conflict depends on reading யாகத்திற் as the printed text has it, not the variant யாக்கத்திற்.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:552","b":"t:554","type":"develops","note":"552 names yama as the first limb; 554 lists what the man of yama is: one who does not kill, lie or steal, shares his food and is free of drink and lust.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:554","b":"k:323","type":"agrees","note":"The Kural puts not killing first and not lying right behind it; Tirumular's list of yama opens in the same order, கொல்லான் then பொய் கூறான்.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:554","b":"k:322","type":"agrees","note":"Both name sharing one's food (பகுத்துண்டு, பகுந்துண்பான்) among the chief virtues; the Kural sets it beside protecting all life, Tirumular makes it part of the yogi's yama.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:554","b":"t:197","type":"develops","note":"197 makes not killing the brightest flower in the worship of the guru; 554 puts it at the head of the yogi's restraints.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:557","b":"t:551","type":"tension","note":"557 counts sacrifice (மகம்) among the ten niyamas the yogi must keep; 551 calls the path of sacrifice a mean path with no way through. The conflict holds if 551's யாகத்திற் is read as printed; on the variant யாக்கத்திற் it vanishes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:556","b":"t:554","type":"qualifies","note":"554 lists not killing, not stealing and freedom from lust under yama; 556 repeats them under niyama with purity, compassion and forbearance. The division between the two limbs is not kept strictly.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"t:571","b":"k:269","type":"agrees","note":"Both say a discipline can overcome Death (கூற்று): the Kural credits the power of tapas with leaping past Death, Tirumular the measure of the held breath with kicking him.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:575","b":"n:11","type":"tension","note":"The Naladiyar's wise renounce in childhood because grey hair will surely come; Tirumular says purified breath turns the hair black. Read literally, they disagree about whether ageing can be undone; read symbolically, 575 is not about hair, and the tension dissolves.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies"},{"a":"t:577","b":"t:576","type":"develops","note":"Both turn on twelve: 576 says the breath goes out twelve finger-breadths, and 577 makes the twelve elephants that, once known, no longer keep day and night.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:578","b":"k:126","type":"develops","note":"The Kural asks one to draw in the five senses like a tortoise for protection through seven births; Tirumular's withdrawal goes on past restraint to what is found inside: the one the Veda searched for.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:584","b":"t:580","type":"same_image","note":"Both place the inner power two fingers above the lower opening and two below the genital one: 580 calls it kundalini, 584 only a light.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:586","b":"t:575","type":"agrees","note":"Both end with the same line, that Siva will not leave, and both make it the fruit of the breath held within.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:591","b":"k:490","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the heron that waits motionless: in the Kural it is the king biding his time before striking, in Tirumular the yogi who holds still in breath and mind.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:596","b":"n:21","type":"agrees","note":"Both say no one who came before has escaped death: the Naladiyar shows it in kings under their umbrellas, Tirumular in all who came before.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:590","b":"t:580","type":"develops","note":"580 places the coiled kundalini at the root; 590 has the power rising from the root region roused and joined above, with the return to youth as its fruit.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:611","b":"t:594","type":"same_image","note":"Both call the yogic body a bedchamber (பள்ளி அறை): in 594 it is the great chamber with two doors where held breath lets one live long, in 611 the chamber where it is always day.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:612","b":"t:591","type":"agrees","note":"Both promise the yogi the body's survival for an aeon (ஊழி): 591 through binding the breath at the lower door, 612 through rising up the spine through the three mandalas.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:616","b":"t:580","type":"same_image","note":"Both measure the yogic body from the navel in fingers: 580 places coiled kundalini four fingers below it, 616 places the point of contemplation four fingers above.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:618","b":"t:552","type":"develops","note":"552 lists the eight limbs from yama to samadhi; 618 draws the consequence that samadhi is reached only through yama and the rest, so the ethical limbs are the way to the highest.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:631","b":"t:618","type":"qualifies","note":"618 makes samadhi the fruit of the limbs; 631 says that for one who goes with the Lord samadhi is not needed and for one who becomes him there is none, so the chapter's goal is itself relative.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:622","b":"t:583","type":"agrees","note":"583 calls closing the lower door and fixing the mind on the upper one the way to conquer time; 622 says that once the upper door opens Death's word is not heard even in dreams.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:622","b":"k:269","type":"agrees","note":"The Kural says those who have won the power of austerity can even leap past Death; Tirumular names a yogic means, the upper door seen open, for the same escape.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:622","b":"n:6","type":"tension","note":"The Naladiyar says no one here has leapt clear of Death and escaped, so give your wealth now; Tirumular says the yogi who sees the upper door will not hear Death even in dreams. The conflict holds on the literal reading of 622, that the yogi escapes death; if it means only freedom from fear of death, it dissolves.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:624","b":"t:604","type":"develops","note":"604 sets the two gazes at the middle of the nose and lists what ceases, seeking (தேட்டம்) among them; 624 turns the gaze back to the eye and settles the breath in the ground 'without seeking' (தேட்டற்ற), and names the fruit.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:632","b":"k:3","type":"same_image","note":"Both speak of the one who goes upon the flower and of those who reach his feet. The Kural promises they live long upon the earth (or, as Parimelazhagar takes it, in the world of release); Tirumular promises Amaravati, then in the following verses the Siva-state.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:639","b":"t:597","type":"develops","note":"597 defines dharana as holding with the Supreme (தரித்தது தாரணை தற்பரத்தோடே) after dissolving body into elements and mind; 639 defines samadhi as joining the Supreme (தற்பரஞ் சேர்தல்) after going beyond all upadhi. The two limbs differ as holding and joining.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:639","b":"t:585","type":"develops","note":"585 makes pratyahara the separating and dissolving of upadhi from the inner light; 639 makes samadhi the going beyond upadhi altogether, so the two verses mark stages on one line.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:711","b":"k:269","type":"agrees","note":"Both say a discipline can carry one past Death: the Kural's power gained through tapas leaps over Yama (கூற்றம் குதித்தல்); Tirumular's yogis of the brow and staff have no Yama (நமனில்லை).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:711","b":"n:6","type":"tension","note":"The Naladiyar says no one has leapt clear of Death and escaped, so give while you can; Tirumular says those who know the yogic way have no Yama. The conflict holds if நமனில்லை means escape from bodily death; if it means freedom from Yama's judgement after death, the two need not clash.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:647","b":"n:11","type":"tension","note":"The Naladiyar's wise renounce in childhood because grey hair will surely come; Tirumular says that by the eighth stage of practice grey hair and wrinkles do not appear. The conflict depends on reading 647 literally, as a claim about the body, and not as a figure for inner freshness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:683","b":"k:299","type":"same_image","note":"Both say not every lamp is the lamp: the Kural's one true lamp is truthfulness, Tirumular's is the higher inner light that makes release easy. The image is shared, the light is moral in one and yogic in the other.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:670","b":"t:669","type":"qualifies","note":"669 says the eight siddhis come by eightfold yoga and mastered breath; 670 adds that they come into being only when Shakti Tripurai grants grace. Technique is necessary but not sufficient.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:668","b":"t:649","type":"agrees","note":"Two lists of the eight siddhis in one chapter. 668's is closer to the standard anima list; set side by side they show the items were not fixed in wording.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:707","b":"t:641","type":"develops","note":"641 credits the siddhis and the end of birth to grace given to one who sought the Lord's feet; 707 says outward travel finds nothing, and love alone knows where the Lord dwells.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:720","b":"t:1936","type":"parallel_domain","note":"The same charge in two parts of the yogic body: no one knows how the breath is wasted (அழியும் படியை அறிகிலர், 720), people do not know how much bindu is lost (1936). In both the knowing is the remedy: 720 says the one who learns it can join the breath to Nandi.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:725","b":"n:47","type":"tension","note":"On what the body is worth: the Naladiyar calls it a pot seeping filth from nine openings, attractive only through its skin, and the man who praises it a fool; Tirumular says he once held the body base (இழுக்கு) but found the Lord had made it his temple, and so cherishes it. The conflict holds if the Naladiyar's verse is read as a verdict on the body and not only as a remedy for lust.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:724","b":"n:34","type":"qualifies","note":"Both value the body for what it yields, but the Naladiyar has the body thrown away like crushed cane once its juice is taken, while Tirumular says the body must be nurtured because life and true knowledge perish with it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:727","b":"k:941","type":"develops","note":"Both rest on the three humours of the medical writers, wind among them: the Kural says excess or lack of them brings disease, Tirumular names the time of day at which practice removes each, and promises the end of age as well.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:735","b":"k:943","type":"agrees","note":"The Kural says that eating in measure is how one who has a body keeps it long; Tirumular counts the reduction of food among the means of perfecting the body.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:737","b":"k:269","type":"agrees","note":"Both claim that Death can be overcome: the Kural by the power gained through austerity, Tirumular by seeing the Lord's feet in the inner fire before Death strikes.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:741","b":"k:334","type":"agrees","note":"Both make time the quiet cutter of life: the Kural's day is a saw for those who understand it, Tirumular's naazhigai run out while people know everything else.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:764","b":"k:269","type":"agrees","note":"Both say that discipline can carry a person past death: the Kural credits the power of austerity, Tirumular the ability to seek the Siva-state within.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:765","b":"t:1926","type":"agrees","note":"Both analyse Om into A, U and M; 1926 adds bindu and nada's end, 765 places M in the inner channel where the Lord settles.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:762","b":"t:786","type":"develops","note":"762 says the unpassing thing is found by looking within the passing; 786 says no one knows the perishing thing, and that the poet has come to know the knowing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:758","b":"t:724","type":"develops","note":"724 says Tirumular learned how to nourish the body and so the life; 758 takes the claim to its limit, the yogi's body kept a thousand years and more.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:789","b":"k:340","type":"same_image","note":"Both make the body a temporary dwelling: the Kural's life has only borrowed shelter in it, Tirumular's body is a fair hut the Lord has shaped. The Kural laments the lack of a home; Tirumular looks to loosening the bond of the old dwelling.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:786","b":"t:741","type":"develops","note":"741 says people perish without noticing their time run out; 786 names what measures it, fire and breath, and claims the knowledge others lack.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:797","b":"n:52","type":"qualifies","note":"The Naladiyar counts those who prattle astrology among the greatest fools, beside those who neglect their real task; Tirumular sets weekday omens inside a yoga tantra. Whether this is a true conflict depends on whether the Naladiyar's சோதிடம் includes such day lore or means endless study that displaces the work of release.","grade":"weak","better":"tension"},{"a":"t:799","b":"t:574","type":"same_image","note":"Both end on the same words, நட்டம் இருக்க நமனில்லை: in 574 after holding the breath by measure, in 799 after sealing the body for khechari. Tirumular gives the same promise to two practices.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:799","b":"k:269","type":"agrees","note":"The Kural says those who have gained the power of austerity can even leap past Death; Tirumular says Yama has no hold on the yogi who seals the body. Both make death conquerable, the Kural by austerity in general, Tirumular by a named technique.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:805","b":"t:734","type":"same_image","note":"The same closing lines, grey hair and wrinkles undone and the yogi made a child by Nandi's command, follow two different means: union with a woman in 734, the breath set in the palate in 805.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:820","b":"t:725","type":"agrees","note":"725 says Tirumular once held the body worthless, then found the Lord's temple inside it; 820 shows how that temple is lit, with held breath as its gem lamp.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"t:820","b":"n:42","type":"tension","note":"Both call the body a bag (பை). The Naladiyar says it should be looked at as a bag turned inside out, a covered foulness to be shunned; Tirumular says that inside the same bag a light shines and it becomes a temple. The conflict holds if 820's பை is read as the body, as tradition takes it.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"t:811","b":"k:987","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural asks what worth is if one does not do sweet things even to those who harm one. Tirumular's inward yogis are good even to the hostile, but worse than fire to doers of evil, so their goodness has a limit the Kural's does not state.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:826","b":"t:790","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the weekday planets. In 790 வெள்ளி and பொன் name Friday and Thursday in a rule for the breath; in 826 and 836 the same words become silver and gold, a code for the bindu and the body.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"t:828","b":"t:1936","type":"develops","note":"1936 laments that people neither know how much bindu is lost nor how to guard it; 828 gives one answer: guard it in union itself, so that the very act that spends it keeps it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:841","b":"k:1106","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Both make union life-giving. In the Kural the lover feels his life put out shoots at each touch, as if her shoulders were ambrosia; in Tirumular union practised as yoga lets one live on earth for many ages. Love's hyperbole in one is a technique in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:848","b":"t:727","type":"agrees","note":"Both promise the end of grey hair and wrinkles (நரைதிரை) from a bodily practice: 727 from breath held at fixed hours, 848 from drinking the shore-water the witless shun.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:848","b":"n:11","type":"tension","note":"The Naladiyar treats grey hair as certain to come, so the wise renounce in childhood; Tirumular says that for those who drink this water grey hair and wrinkles change and Yama is absent. The conflict holds if 848 is read literally of the body, not as a figure for spiritual youth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:877","b":"t:1936","type":"agrees","note":"1936 faults people who do not know how bindu is lost or how to guard it; 877 describes the yogi in whom bindu does not go out, held at the base for want of desire.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:879","b":"t:724","type":"agrees","note":"724 says that if the body perishes life perishes, so Tirumular learned to nourish the body; 879 gives the condition for their not parting: awareness and body kept in balance.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"t:861","b":"k:782","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the moon's two fortnights: the Kural for friendship with the good that fills and with fools that wanes, Tirumular for the inner moon whose growing and shrinking the yogi must learn.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:871","b":"n:241","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the eclipse serpent that seizes the moon: the Naladiyar's spares the young crescent, as the worthy spare a fallen enemy; Tirumular's enmity of serpent and moon is ended and the two held together, an inner joining.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:905","b":"k:10","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural says only those who reach the Lord's feet cross the sea of births; 905 promises no further birth to whoever chants the five letters, and 898 has already made the Lord's feet the letters. The means is narrowed from the feet to the mantra that is the feet.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"t:909","b":"k:5","type":"agrees","note":"Both free a person from deeds through the Lord's name: the two kinds of deed do not cling to those who delight in his true praise (Kural), and subtle karma is destroyed by chanting the subtle mantra (909). Tirumular adds a count and a technique.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:895","b":"t:115","type":"develops","note":"115 names Pati, Pasu and Pasam and says Pasu and Pasam do not stand when Pati draws near; 895 names the three impurities (anava, maya, karma) and sets the stainless dance among them, on the traditional reading that the dance is where they end.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:886","b":"t:589","type":"same_image","note":"In 589 the holy dance goes on in a hall reached by a waterfall through the long nadi, an image usually read as inside the body; 886 names the outer hall at Chidambaram. Together they show the hall Tirumular means is both.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:906","b":"t:725","type":"develops","note":"725 turns from despising the body to cherishing it as the Lord's temple; 906 goes further and promises that the mantra makes the capable one's body gold.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:975","b":"k:1","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the letter A to point beyond itself. The Kural makes A, first of letters, the image of the Primal Lord first in the world; Tirumular makes A the soul and gives the Supreme to U.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:977","b":"k:24","type":"same_image","note":"Both put a goad over 'the five'. In the Kural it is firmness of mind (உரன்) that guards the five; in Tirumular the five elephants in the forest are goaded by the five syllables.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:977","b":"n:59","type":"agrees","note":"Both make control of the five senses the way in: the Naladiyar's one who steers the craving of the five gates reaches release, Tirumular's one who holds the five together lets the First enter.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:967","b":"k:5","type":"agrees","note":"Both say deeds lose their hold on one devoted to the Lord: the Kural through his true praise, Tirumular through his name kept up without tiring, with the Lord coming to remove the grief of deeds.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:940","b":"k:30","type":"qualifies","note":"Both define the Brahmin by something other than birth, if 940 is read that way: the Kural by kindness to all life, Tirumular by the five syllables dwelling in him.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"t:965","b":"t:2698","type":"agrees","note":"The same verse recurs in the ninth tantra's gross pancakshara, with 'once the order of the fifty is known' for 'once what the fifty become is known'.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:972","b":"t:1115","type":"agrees","note":"A near-doublet in the Bhairavi mantra chapter; there the lady herself departs and cuts away the babble, here the speaker does.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:930","b":"t:2712","type":"agrees","note":"Both make the recited syllables drive out the impurities: here the three malams flee crying out, in 2712 sivaya removes all impurity and yields Siva-jnana yoga.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:962","b":"t:885","type":"develops","note":"885 builds a ladder of one, two and three letters from the one letter that becomes the world; 962 says the six letters must be recited as one, and that by one letter life is won.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"t:952","b":"t:2709","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the alchemist's pill (குளிகை) that makes gold: here the realised true object is the pill like purified gold, in 2709 sivayanama is the pill that turns the body's cage to gold.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:919","b":"t:1823","type":"agrees","note":"Both move the temple inward: 1823 makes the body a shrine and the heart the great temple, 919 makes the five syllables and the subtle within the temple where the Lord sits.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:958","b":"t:978","type":"develops","note":"958 mocks those who perform the twilight rite without knowing the mantra within; 978 says those who join with Shakti at the root need no rite at all.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:976","b":"t:982","type":"agrees","note":"Near-doublets: the same breath-joined recitation with O, ending on 'the first Lord MA', one starting from NA and one from SI.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1000","b":"t:198","type":"tension","note":"198 sends those who say 'kill, strike' bound to hell; 1000 gives a rite for killing by mantra and diagram. The conflict holds if 1000 is read as offering the rite for use; read as a catalogue of what mantra can do, it is a report, not an endorsement.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1008","b":"t:1003","type":"qualifies","note":"1003 to 1006 prescribe flowers, pastes and lamps and promise every fruit; 1008 says the Lord's servants, the Siva-yogis and the desireless do not want such ritual work, only love.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1013","b":"t:1012","type":"develops","note":"1012 places the mantra's syllables in the body; 1013 says what the parts of நமசிவாய stand for: நம the bound soul, சிவ the Lord.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:1005","b":"k:4","type":"agrees","note":"Both promise the end of sorrow to those who come to the Lord's feet; Tirumular reaches it through worship with lamps and offerings, the Kural through reaching the feet of the one without liking or aversion.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:1009","b":"k:6","type":"agrees","note":"The Kural praises the path of the one who quenched the five senses; Tirumular says those who seek through the senses (பொறிவழி) only lament, and the Lord is found within.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:1031","b":"t:1030","type":"qualifies","note":"1030 and the verses around it give the Lord counted feet, faces and eyes in the fire; 1031 says the endless one has no place, no measure and no word that reaches him. The forms are aids, not limits.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1042","b":"t:724","type":"agrees","note":"724 says Tirumular learned to nurture the body so as to nurture life; 1042 says those who guarded the body and dwelt within it lived through a crore of ages.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1019","b":"k:5","type":"agrees","note":"Both say deeds cannot cling to the one turned to the Lord: the Kural through his true praise, Tirumular through seeing the fire rise within.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1045","b":"t:1925","type":"agrees","note":"Both name Mahamaya and speech among the first powers; in 1925 they shine within bindu, in 1045 they and the bindu-born (வயிந்தவம்) are all forms of one Tripura. The bindu chapter's cosmology here gets a goddess's face.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"t:1048","b":"t:1138","type":"agrees","note":"Both make the Goddess the one who unfolds as nada and bindu: 1048 has Tripura grant nada and nada's end while the supreme bindu becomes the worlds, 1138 has Sivashakti spread out as bindu and nada within the mind.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1049","b":"t:1081","type":"same_image","note":"Both portraits give the Goddess the noose and goad. Tripura (1049) adds the sugarcane bow and flower arrows; Bhairavi (1081) adds the trident and skull. Same emblems, gentle and fierce forms.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1053","b":"t:1087","type":"agrees","note":"1053 says no god fails to know her and nothing the five do is done without her; 1087 says the many gods, Siva, Brahma and Vishnu among them, look to the Lady as cause. Both put the Goddess behind every god.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1066","b":"t:1137","type":"agrees","note":"1066 has her stand as his body and life; 1137 says the lady and he stand in one form and people do not see it. Both deny any gap between Siva and Shakti.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1067","b":"k:9","type":"agrees","note":"The Kural calls a head that does not bow at the feet of the Lord of eight qualities as useless as a sense that cannot sense; Tirumular tells the head to wear the Goddess's feet and the mouth to praise her. The same claim for the head, turned to the Goddess.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1071","b":"k:351","type":"develops","note":"The Kural traces joyless birth to the delusion (மருள்) that takes what is not real as real (பொருள்). Tirumular uses the same pair: the Goddess turns the deluded mind into one that has found the true thing, so grace is the remedy for the Kural's diagnosis.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1124","b":"t:1075","type":"develops","note":"The chapter opens with Bhairavi as the first of twelve kalas, a count in a mantra, and closes with her as the first of the twelve again; by the end the count is a name of the goddess praised as beginning and beginningless.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"t:1107","b":"t:1106","type":"develops","note":"1106 leaves the maiden asleep in the middle channel; 1107 has Manonmani come in sleep, embrace and say 'do not sleep'. Read together, the waking is hers and his at once.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1119","b":"t:1118","type":"develops","note":"1118 ends calling maya darkness; 1119 opens by saying darkness is Shakti and light is the Lord, so the darkness is not rejected but given to her as one of two powers.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1116","b":"t:1115","type":"develops","note":"1115 says she cut away the poet's babble with the five-syllable mantra; 1116 says the foolish who went on babbling passed away, while she stands before those whose effort is complete.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1120","b":"t:1114","type":"agrees","note":"The same line of names (woman, samadhi, Manonmani, the auspicious one) in both; in 1114 she joins the heart, in 1120 she blossoms in it, a refrain that marks the hymn's stages.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1103","b":"k:10","type":"agrees","note":"Both make reaching or bowing at the divine feet the end of births: the Kural's sea of births, Tirumular's cruel births. Tirumular gives it to the goddess, the Kural to the unnamed Lord.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1122","b":"k:5","type":"agrees","note":"The Kural says the two kinds of deed do not cling to those who praise the Lord truly; 1122 praises her by name after name and ends that she drove out the poet's deeds.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1130","b":"t:1141","type":"same_image","note":"The two verses share a frame (தார்மேல்/தாமேல் உறைவிடம், பார்மேல், பூமேல் உறைகின்ற ... வந்தனள்): 1130 fills it with Brahma and the goddess of speech, 1141 with the lotus petals and the bangled lady. They may be variants of one verse.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1138","b":"t:1925","type":"agrees","note":"Both make the goddess and bindu one: 1925 sets Mahamaya, the goddess of speech and kundalini within bindu, 1138 has Shakti spread out as bindu and nada.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:1129","b":"k:346","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Both cut a pride (செருக்கு) voiced as possession: the Kural's 'I' and 'mine', Tirumular's 'my mother, my father' said of the divine parents. Tirumular extends the Kural's renunciation of ownership to the soul's claim on God.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:1128","b":"k:80","type":"same_image","note":"Both set love (அன்பு) against bone (என்பு). For the Kural a body without love is bone wrapped in skin; Tirumular has the father reached through love and the mother present in the bone within bondage. The pairing depends on reading என்பிற் as 'in the bone'.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:1150","b":"k:269","type":"agrees","note":"Both join penance, power and the defeat of death: the Kural says those who gain the power (ஆற்றல்) of austerity can leap past death; Tirumular's goddess of rare penance stands in his power (ஆற்றல்) and drives Death (கூற்றம்) away.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"t:1421","b":"k:356","type":"agrees","note":"Both make learning the start of a way that ends in seeing the true: the Kural's learners who see the true thing (மெய்ப்பொருள்) take the road of no return; Tirumular's learn what is to be learned, then pass through yoga and knowledge to see the Supreme.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1422","b":"t:1435","type":"develops","note":"Both say those who saw Nadanta are unwavering (நாதாந்தங் கண்டோர் நடுக்கற்ற). 1422 sets Vedanta and Siddhanta side by side; 1435 distinguishes what the seers of Vedanta, of Nadanta and of Siddhanta each hold.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1423","b":"t:1438","type":"qualifies","note":"1423 commends earrings, seals and beads for those of service and rite; 1438 says the pure Saiva goes beyond outward garb (வேடம் கடந்து), without show. The signs are fitting at one stage and passed beyond at another.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1436","b":"t:115","type":"develops","note":"115 states that pasu and pasam cannot reach the Lord; 1436 repeats the line and gives it images, clouds that never reach the sky and sights that never reach the eye. It leaves out 115's other half, that when the Lord approaches they cease.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1432","b":"t:115","type":"develops","note":"115 sets out Pati, Pasu and Pasam as beginningless; 1432 makes that triad, with impurity and release, the matter the pure Saiva contemplates.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:1429","b":"t:63","type":"agrees","note":"63 names the nine Agamas Nandi received (Karanam, Kamikam and the rest); 1429 begins from the same nine before naming the twenty-eight.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1431","b":"t:1420","type":"develops","note":"1420's pure Saiva discerns the real, the unreal and what is both, and keeps clear of the sentient and the insentient; 1431's has passed beyond them, into a state where they are no longer told apart.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1437","b":"k:10","type":"develops","note":"Both make the feet the way: in the Kural reaching the Lord's feet takes one across the sea of births; in Tirumular going by the feet of the Supreme power one becomes Siva.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"t:1442","b":"k:343","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural says the senses must be crushed as part of renouncing; Tirumular says the five, hard to curb, subside once the truth beyond speech is attained. The same subduing is a task in one and a result in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1438","b":"k:350","type":"agrees","note":"Both cut the bonds of desire by turning to the Lord: the Kural by clinging to the one without clinging, Tirumular by going to Siva's side and crushing ஆசாபாசம்.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:1441","b":"k:346","type":"parallel_domain","note":"The Kural ends the pride of 'I' and 'mine' between a person and his goods; Tirumular ends the speech of 'I' and 'He' between the soul and the Lord. Both close with the 'I' given up.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1445","b":"t:1823","type":"develops","note":"1445 ends the pilgrim's search with the Lord taking the heart as his temple; 1823 builds the whole temple inside: heart as shrine, body as temple, mouth as gate, the soul as linga.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1449","b":"t:1478","type":"agrees","note":"Both make Nandi the giver of the paths: in 1449 he sets the four paths for deluded men, in 1478 he lays down the guru-path so that those on earth may be saved.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1451","b":"k:10","type":"same_image","note":"Both make the Lord's feet the only crossing of a sea: the Kural's great sea of births, Tirumular's leaping sea of deeds. Reaching the feet is the condition in both.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1452","b":"k:259","type":"agrees","note":"Both set one abstention above a mass of offerings: the Kural puts not killing and eating above a thousand fire sacrifices, Tirumular puts giving up flesh above crores of sandal and flowers.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1452","b":"t:199","type":"develops","note":"199 threatens meat-eaters with hell; 1452 adds that their worship, however rich, does not reach the Lord's feet.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1456","b":"t:272","type":"same_image","note":"272 says even burning one's bones as firewood wins nothing without melting in love; in 1456 the devotee melts in love and the Lord shines within the bones.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1458","b":"t:375","type":"develops","note":"375 tells how Hari and Brahma went to the crown and the foot and could not reach him; 1458 recalls their ages of striving and places him in the crown, perceived only by those who perceive.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1466","b":"t:1450","type":"parallel_domain","note":"The same four grades, samaya, visesha, nirvana and abhisheka, are initiations in 1450 and stages of yoga in 1466.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1461","b":"k:356","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural says those who learn and know the truth here do not come back; Tirumular says letters, songs and the sixty-four arts do not remove birth, and only the yogic way does. The difference may lie in what each means by learning.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1467","b":"k:31","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural asks what gain is greater than aram, which yields distinction and wealth; Tirumular says no path of aram is higher than jnana. They conflict only if the Kural's aram is meant to rank above liberating knowledge; Tirumular keeps aram but places it below jnana.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1474","b":"k:346","type":"agrees","note":"Both set the cutting off of 'I' and 'mine' at the summit: the Kural says whoever cuts that pride enters a world above the gods', Tirumular makes it jnana within jnana itself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1475","b":"k:5","type":"agrees","note":"Both free the one who knows or praises the Lord from both kinds of deed: the Kural's twofold deeds of darkness do not cling to him, Tirumular's knower has passed beyond merit and sin alike.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1476","b":"t:1466","type":"parallel_domain","note":"The same four grades are applied to yoga and to jnana; yoga's summit is siddhi (1466), jnana's is the good guru's feet (1476).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1477","b":"k:269","type":"agrees","note":"Both promise victory over death to those of disciplined practice: the Kural to those who have gained the power of tapas, Tirumular to Siva-yoga siddhas who have put away anger.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1480","b":"k:357","type":"agrees","note":"Both make clear knowledge of what is the condition of ending birth: the Kural's mind that has examined and known what is real need not think of another birth; Tirumular says those without clarity do not end their births.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1486","b":"t:2406","type":"agrees","note":"2406 says that when the soul's bound nature is removed and its bonds cut, souls cling to their Lord; 1486 calls that removal and joining sanmarga, and adds the melting heart.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1490","b":"t:631","type":"qualifies","note":"631 says samadhis are not needed for one who goes with the Lord and there is no samadhi once one becomes Him; 1490 praises yoga-samadhi as holding space, light and Shakti. Samadhi is honoured as a means and set below union.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1493","b":"k:24","type":"same_image","note":"Both arm the mind against the five senses: the Kural's goad of firmness guards them like elephants, Tirumular's sharp sword of the mind cuts them down. The Kural guides the five; Tirumular strikes them.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"t:1498","b":"k:10","type":"same_image","note":"Both make reaching the Lord's feet the crossing of a sea. The Kural names the great sea of births; Tirumular names only the shore, four times over, and makes it both the shade of the feet and the Lord's command.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1499","b":"k:9","type":"agrees","note":"The Kural calls a head that does not bow to the Lord's feet as useless as a sense that cannot sense; Tirumular says bowing and serving at Hara's feet is the very fruit of birth. One states the loss, the other the gain.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1500","b":"t:1505","type":"agrees","note":"Both make the Lord answer worship itself: 1500 says he comes forth each time one worships, 1505 that he stands held by the great love of those who make him their eye.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:1506","b":"t:1496","type":"qualifies","note":"1496 counts worship and recitation as the faultless good son's path; 1506 says the same acts, done by a mind like a stone in moss, do not know the Lord. The acts are necessary, not sufficient.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1503","b":"t:1545","type":"agrees","note":"Both set the quarrel of 'this one or that one is supreme' against seeking the Lord himself: here by the ordained way of service, in 1545 by leaving the delusion of those who praise one sect over another.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1508","b":"t:725","type":"agrees","note":"725 finds that the Highest has made the body his temple; 1508 makes the worshipper's own mind the temple in kiriyai. Both move the shrine inside the person.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:1509","b":"t:1507","type":"develops","note":"1507 matches the states of release to the paths; 1509 ranks the same states by what happens to the bond in each, ending in its dissolving.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1511","b":"n:176","type":"agrees","note":"Both make lesser things gain worth by nearness: in the Naladiyar the hare is worshipped with the moon, and the slight gain worth through friends steady as a hill; in Tirumular all that nears the golden mountain turns its colour, and the disciple takes the Lord's form.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"t:1518","b":"t:1514","type":"develops","note":"1518 repeats 1514's line, 'removing blindness and showing many good qualities', but with the Lord as the doer where 1514 had the old woman, and adds that he cut away deeds and gave knowledge. It unlocks part of the riddle: the woman's act is the Lord's.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1517","b":"n:51","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the lamp entering a dark room. The Naladiyar makes it austerity before which sin cannot stand, and warns the dark returns when the oil runs out; Tirumular makes it the Lord and the Mother entering delusion, with no warning that the light fails.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1527","b":"k:5","type":"agrees","note":"Both treat good and bad deeds alike as what must be overcome, not good deeds as a ladder. The Kural says the two dark deeds do not cling to those who praise the Lord truly; Tirumular says grace descends when the two stand in balance.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1530","b":"t:725","type":"agrees","note":"725 says the Highest has made the body his temple; 1530 faults the six sects for not seeing the God who stands within the body. The doctrine of 725 becomes the charge against the sects.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1547","b":"t:1530","type":"develops","note":"1530 says the sects miss the God within the body; 1547 says that once one knows how to reach the light standing within, the quarrel among sects ends of itself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1545","b":"k:355","type":"agrees","note":"The Kural calls understanding seeing the true thing in whatever thing, of whatever kind; Tirumular tells people to drop the claim that this or that sect is good and seek the God beyond the forms. Both look past the label to the thing.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:1555","b":"k:7","type":"agrees","note":"Both name the Lord as the one without equal and make reaching him the cure for the mind: the Kural's தனக்குவமை இல்லாதான் removes the mind's anxiety, Tirumular's துணையிலி is reached by the mind that draws near.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:1556","b":"t:329","type":"agrees","note":"329 calls those held by the mala of deluding sects great fools; 1556 says those who never leave their sect die before reaching freedom from birth. Both treat staying in a sect as the obstacle.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1558","b":"t:1568","type":"agrees","note":"Both mock sectarian ranking with the image of one city: 1558's six roads and barking dog, 1568's Nandi laughing at those who name 'this penance, that penance' while all who understand enter the city.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1558","b":"t:1556","type":"qualifies","note":"1556 condemns those who stay inside their sect and perish; 1558 says all six sects lead to one city. The road is not the fault; refusing to go beyond it, or attacking the others, is.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1571","b":"k:2","type":"agrees","note":"Both make worship of the feet the fruit of knowing: the Kural asks what learning has achieved without it, Tirumular says his examining succeeded where the gods' failed because it ended in worship.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1585","b":"t:1932","type":"same_image","note":"Both use seed and sprout. 1932 makes them the inseparable cause and effect that reveal Hara's way; 1585 makes devotion and dispassion the seed and release-knowledge the sprout, which Shakti's grace makes grow.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1581","b":"t:1576","type":"develops","note":"1576 argues that the guru is pure Siva because the one beyond the worlds comes here and saves; 1581 states it flatly on Nandi's word and adds that people fail to grasp it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1584","b":"k:2","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural asks what learning has achieved if it doesn't lead to the Lord's feet; Tirumular says even the knowledge of Vedanta cannot be known unless the Lord comes as guru. Learning is not enough in either, but for Tirumular the grace must come first.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1598","b":"t:138","type":"same_image","note":"Both are four-line anaphoras on திருவடி. 138 makes the feet Sivam, Sivaloka, the path and the refuge; 1598 makes knowledge of the feet the agent that makes one Siva, brings to Sivaloka, frees from mala and is release.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1593","b":"t:134","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the same words for the end state: beyond word (உரையற்று), without waves (திரையற்ற), shoreless (கரையற்ற). 134 describes those who understand the teacher's word; 1593 is the speaker's own passing beyond words.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1597","b":"t:113","type":"agrees","note":"Both narrate the guru placing his feet on the speaker's head: in 113 as a guard that melts him and removes the verdigris, in 1597 so that the path back to the womb is drained.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1603","b":"k:9","type":"agrees","note":"Both join the head to the Lord's feet: the Kural calls a head that does not bow to them useless, Tirumular says the old sages set the feet on their heads.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1602","b":"k:5","type":"agrees","note":"Both say the two deeds, good and bad, cease for one devoted to the Lord: the Kural through praising him, Tirumular through holding his feet in the mind.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1595","b":"t:1907","type":"agrees","note":"Both make a death of the self the way to become Siva: 1907's living who have died to the three malas, 1595's speaker made Siva and made to die in the bliss beyond speech.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1611","b":"t:1634","type":"agrees","note":"Both make silence the mark of the finished state: 1611 says silence brings release and powers, 1634 that chant and sound are no longer needed once samadhi is in hand.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:1615","b":"n:51","type":"same_image","note":"Both set a light against darkness in the soul. The Naladiyar's lamp is a person's own tapas, which drives out sin until its oil runs out; Tirumular's light is Siva's grace that comes to the renounced soul at its ripe time.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:1620","b":"k:346","type":"agrees","note":"Both put the full renouncer above the heavens: the Kural says he who cuts 'I' and 'mine' enters a world higher than the gods', Tirumular that only he who renounces the earth reaches the Lord's state.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:1624","b":"k:269","type":"agrees","note":"Both put the ascetic beyond death: the Kural says those who gain the power of tapas can leap past Death, Tirumular that for those who have withdrawn and let go, Yama is simply not there.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1626","b":"k:265","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural commends tapas because whatever one wants is gained as one wants it. Tirumular separates getting what one wants, which mere asking achieves, from tapas, whose greatness is ending birth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1630","b":"n:57","type":"agrees","note":"Both define the ascetic by firmness under pressure: the Naladiyar's blessed push aside troubles that would break their vows, Tirumular's stay unblinking amid kings at war.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1631","b":"n:52","type":"agrees","note":"Both set inner work against learned talk: the Naladiyar calls those who prattle of grammar and astrology the greatest fools, Tirumular tells the reader to leave clever shastra-recitation for a moment's inward look.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1644","b":"t:1632","type":"develops","note":"1632 says tapas falls away once the samadhi of knowledge is reached; 1644 adds a second way past it, fellowship with those already in Siva's bliss, and names the end both serve: the mind becoming Siva.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1642","b":"k:270","type":"agrees","note":"Both tie want to the lack of tapas: the Kural says the poor are many because few practise austerity, Tirumular pictures those without tapas or learning whirling around for rice.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1642","b":"n:31","type":"agrees","note":"Both picture those who do no tapas as shut out and reduced to want: in the Naladiyar they cling to the outer door of others' houses, in Tirumular they run from crocodile to bear in search of rice.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1633","b":"k:391","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural asks for flawless learning followed by living by it; Tirumular says recitation (ஓதல்) is no longer needed once the Life of life is within. The two speak of different stages, so this limits learning rather than contradicting it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1645","b":"k:241","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Both make அருள் the wealth above wealth. The Kural's அருள் is compassion toward living beings, and its chapter is named அருளுடைமை; Tirumular takes the same chapter name for the Lord's grace, the source of wealth, knowledge and divinity.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:1645","b":"t:1625","type":"agrees","note":"1625 says that only those with the Lord's grace know what tapas is; 1645 widens the claim: knowledge of every kind, with wealth and greatness, comes from grace.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"t:1647","b":"k:5","type":"agrees","note":"Both say that both kinds of deed, good as well as bad, must cease to cling, and both tie this to the Lord: the Kural to those who love his true praise, Tirumular to seeking his dwelling beyond the two.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1654","b":"k:350","type":"agrees","note":"The Kural says cling to the one without clinging in order to let clinging go; Tirumular vows not to let go of Nandi until release, a clinging that is the way out.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1655","b":"k:276","type":"agrees","note":"Both condemn those who live by others while posing as renouncers: the Kural calls none more hard-eyed than they, Tirumular says the show of devotion they put on for food finds no feet of Siva.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1656","b":"t:241","type":"agrees","note":"241 says the land withers when the unawakened take up tuft and thread and that it is good to cut them off; 1656 repeats the harm, adding famine, and calls stripping the garb of the base a joy.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1657","b":"k:520","type":"agrees","note":"Both put the world's welfare on the ruler's daily scrutiny (நாள்தோறும் நாடுக, நாடி நாடோறும): the Kural of those who do his work, Tirumular of all his people's conduct.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1660","b":"t:240","type":"agrees","note":"240 says the garb is true only for those who keep its way; 1660 restates the distinction of false and true garb and puts the test in knowledge.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1661","b":"k:262","type":"agrees","note":"Both say austerity's garb belongs only to those who already have austerity, and set தவம் against அவம்: for the Kural taking it up without it is futile, for Tirumular those great in futility cannot carry it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1661","b":"k:274","type":"same_image","note":"Both make the false ascetic a hunter: in the Kural one hiding in a bush to snare birds, in Tirumular a வேடர் who wears the garb to kill, punning on the word for garb-wearer.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1665","b":"t:230","type":"agrees","note":"Nearly the same verse: 230 asks whether thread and tuft are Brahman and says the real thread is Vedanta and the real tuft knowledge; 1665 repeats this and adds Om as the means of seeing the supreme soul.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1668","b":"t:240","type":"qualifies","note":"240 says the garb is the true garb for those who keep its way; 1668 goes further, that the knower is released even without it, so the garb is not needed at all where knowledge is.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1668","b":"n:131","type":"parallel_domain","note":"The same move in another field: the Naladiyar says the beauty of hair and garment is not beauty and learning's is; Tirumular says garb does not save and knowledge does.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1679","b":"k:280","type":"agrees","note":"Both say the ascetic's outward marks are not needed: the Kural, neither shaving nor long hair if one gives up what the world condemns; Tirumular, no garb if one reins in the running horse and seeks Nandi.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1679","b":"k:24","type":"same_image","note":"Both make self-control the handling of animals: the Kural's goad of firmness guards the five senses as elephants, Tirumular's firm reins hold the running horse.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"t:1677","b":"t:1660","type":"develops","note":"1660 says a garb saves only the knower; 1677 gives the reason: the garb is put on the body and falls away with it, so it cannot belong to the soul.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1680","b":"t:1693","type":"develops","note":"1680 shows the unripe choosing a teacher who cannot remove blindness and falling with him; 1693 gives the remedy: if you take a guru, take a good one, and give him body, life and goods.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1683","b":"k:819","type":"agrees","note":"Both condemn the split between word and deed: the Kural calls the bond with such people bitter even in a dream, Tirumular warns against saying one thing, thinking another and doing a third.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1684","b":"k:550","type":"agrees","note":"Both make the king's harsh punishment of the wicked a protection of the land: the Kural likens it to weeding the young crop, Tirumular says without it the earth falls to famine.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1684","b":"t:1656","type":"agrees","note":"Both tie the land's famine to tolerating the wrong people: in 1656 the ignorant who wear the garb, in 1684 the sinners the king fails to drive out.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1703","b":"k:208","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the shadow that clings to the feet and never leaves: in the Kural it is the ruin that follows the evildoer, in Tirumular the true disciple's place at the guru's feet.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1701","b":"t:1823","type":"qualifies","note":"1823 calls the body the Lord's temple for those who have seen clearly; 1701 calls it a prison the teacher shows the disciple how to escape. The two can stand together if the body is a prison to the unreleased and a temple to the knower, but the images pull apart.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1707","b":"t:1492","type":"agrees","note":"The same verse appears twice. 1492's last line reads சாதா ரணங்கெட லாஞ்ச மார்க்கமே, which supports reading 1707's as 'this is the companion's path' (சகமார்க்கம்) rather than a statement about one person.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1711","b":"t:2359","type":"agrees","note":"Nearly the same verse in a later tantra: the sixteen-petalled flower and pure knowledge becoming Siva's bliss. 2359 ends with the Lord united there, 1711 with knowledge ripening, so the second differs in who is the subject.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1725","b":"t:1712","type":"develops","note":"1712 says the world has taken form as the linga; 1725 shows it in worship, with the earth as Shakti's base, the sky as the linga, the sea as its bath and the stars as its garland.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1722","b":"t:725","type":"same_image","note":"Both say the Lord took up the body as his temple (கோயில் கொண்டான்). In 725 that discovery turns Tirumular from scorning the body to caring for it; in 1722 it follows from the Lord being beyond all knowing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1721","b":"t:2104","type":"qualifies","note":"2104 declares one kulam and one God; 1721 still gives each varna its own linga. The oneness of 2104 does not, in this chapter, remove ritual distinctions by birth, though 1721 claims no difference in worth.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:1726","b":"n:46","type":"tension","note":"On what the human body is: the Naladiyar breaks it into entrails, fat, blood, bone and sinew and asks which part is the beautiful woman; Tirumular says the body's very form is the Sivalinga, Chidambaram and Siva's dance. The conflict holds if the Naladiyar's verse is read as a verdict on the body and not only as a remedy for desire.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1726","b":"t:1823","type":"agrees","note":"1823, in the chapter on Siva worship, makes the heart the temple, the body the shrine and the soul the linga; 1726 goes further and makes the body's own form the linga.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"t:1729","b":"k:6","type":"same_image","note":"Both speak of the five senses as gates (வாயில்) to be mastered. The Kural praises the one who quenched the five sense-gates and the long life of those who follow him; Tirumular has Nandi enter the body by its gates and receive worship once the senses are put behind.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:1750","b":"t:1726","type":"develops","note":"1726 says the human body's form is the Sivalinga, Sadasiva and the dance; 1750 says it of one's own body (தன்மேனி) and goes on to Siva-bliss and the Supreme itself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1751","b":"k:1","type":"same_image","note":"Both start from the letter A. The Kural compares: as A comes first among letters, the Primal Lord comes first for the world; Tirumular makes the Lord himself the letter A, with Shakti as U, and the two become the earth and sound. The simile becomes an identity.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1752","b":"t:1926","type":"agrees","note":"Both analyse Om into A, U and M with bindu and nada. 1926 places them in the ascent of the kalas; 1752 maps them onto the parts of the linga.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"t:1753","b":"k:1","type":"develops","note":"Both open with A as first: the Kural uses it as a simile, as A is first of letters so the Lord is first of the world. Tirumular takes the letter literally: A and U, known rightly, are themselves the lingam.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1757","b":"t:1754","type":"develops","note":"1754 makes bindu the first pedestal and the union the lingam; 1757 names the parts plainly, bindu as pedestal and nada as lingam, and gives the pair the five acts.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1762","b":"t:1760","type":"agrees","note":"Both end on the Lord dwelling in the speaker's mind; 1762 adds the contrast with the gods who seek him outwardly across the eight directions.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1764","b":"t:1776","type":"agrees","note":"1764 counts four forms, four formless and one in the middle without naming them; 1776 names nine: Brahma, Vishnu, Rudra, Mahesa, the five-faced one, bindu, nada, Shakti and Siva, and calls them the lingam.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1772","b":"t:1755","type":"agrees","note":"Both make Shakti with Siva the தாபரம்: 1755 of the installed lingam, 1772 of everything fixed, with no other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1774","b":"t:1773","type":"develops","note":"1773 says the Lord is too vast to be walked round; 1774 answers that water, flowers and his name, with knowing, are the way to circle him.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1783","b":"t:1778","type":"agrees","note":"Both describe the same rite: taking body, wealth and life, setting the feet and cutting the clinging; 1783 adds the power of grace and jnana and the guru becoming one with the disciple.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1784","b":"t:1594","type":"agrees","note":"In 1594 the guru takes hold of the soul's three forms and takes the disciple to save him; 1784 makes the same taking of life, wealth and body, and adds that what he took was his own gift.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1778","b":"t:1590","type":"agrees","note":"Both make the guru's hand on the disciple the moment of release: in 1590 the red guru places his hand on the head; in 1778 he places hand and feet and shows the way out of birth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1785","b":"k:850","type":"same_image","note":"Both call a failure of understanding ghostly: the Kural's man who denies what the world holds to be is counted a demon, Tirumular's who don't know how body, breath and soul join are like ghosts.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:1786","b":"k:359","type":"agrees","note":"Both make true knowing the end of suffering: the Kural's one who knows the support and lives so that clinging ends is not touched by the pains, Tirumular's who have awareness have no suffering.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1788","b":"t:1791","type":"agrees","note":"Both describe the union by the image of flesh, or body, and the life within it, and both deny any 'I' and 'you' apart.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1803","b":"k:8","type":"agrees","note":"Both name the Lord அறவாழி அந்தணன். In the Kural only those who reach his feet cross the other seas; in Tirumular he himself gives the way of no birth and comes into the heart as kin.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1809","b":"t:1927","type":"agrees","note":"Both give the Lord the five acts. 1927 says it of the Supreme as seed and root of cause and effect; 1809 says it with தானே in every line, adding that he also grants release.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1810","b":"t:1807","type":"develops","note":"1807 lists the nine forms he dances as; 1810 sorts them into four formless, Sadasiva as form-formless, and four with form, while keeping him one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1813","b":"t:1930","type":"agrees","note":"Both make bindu the source that becomes Mahamaya and all the worlds; 1813 adds the gods, mantras and Veda to what ripens from it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1817","b":"k:2","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural asks what learning has achieved if it does not bow at the feet of the one of pure knowledge, so bowing is learning's fruit. Tirumular bows by leaving what he learned (கற்றன விட்டேன்): the feet are reached by setting learning down.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1818","b":"k:299","type":"same_image","note":"Both set a true lamp above ordinary lamps. For the Kural it is truthfulness; for Tirumular it is the lamp that lights the lamp, and its bearer becomes the lamp.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:1823","b":"t:725","type":"agrees","note":"725 tells how Tirumular once thought the body a flaw, then found the Highest had made it his temple and so guards it; 1823 maps the temple onto the body part by part.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1823","b":"n:47","type":"tension","note":"The Naladiyar calls the body a pot seeping filth from nine openings, dressed in skin that fools the eye; Tirumular makes the same flesh body (ஊனுடம்பு) the Lord's shrine and the mouth its gate. The conflict holds if the Naladiyar is read as judging the body's worth itself, not only rebuking lust.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1826","b":"k:298","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural says water gives outer purity and truth inner purity. Tirumular makes truthful speech and thought the condition without which the Lord is not seen, and only then counts flowers and water as praise.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1828","b":"t:252","type":"agrees","note":"Both say worship needs almost nothing: 252 offers the Lord one green leaf, open to anyone; 1828 says flowers and water are always there, so those who fail to approach are evading.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1829","b":"t:1857","type":"agrees","note":"Both redirect offering from the Lord's image to his living devotees: 1857 says what is given to the walking temple reaches the Lord, 1829 that the water washing a jnani's feet is the Lord's bath in the nine holy waters.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1834","b":"k:10","type":"same_image","note":"Both make the sea of births what drowns those who do not turn to the Lord: the Kural's who do not reach his feet cannot swim it, Tirumular's who do not worship sink (அழுந்துகின்றார்) in it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1841","b":"k:34","type":"agrees","note":"The Kural says a spotless mind is the whole of virtue and the rest is noisy show; Tirumular says daily adorning and praise do not bring knowledge of the Lord until the inner stain is gone.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1853","b":"t:1852","type":"develops","note":"1852 says worship belongs where moon and sun do not shine; 1853 says what moon and sun become there, bindu and nada, and where the worship is done.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1856","b":"t:1855","type":"develops","note":"1855 says the Lord takes the worship not done by day and night; 1856 speaks from that place, where night and day are not and the worship is drinking bliss.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1857","b":"t:1823","type":"develops","note":"1823 makes the body a temple and the soul a Siva-linga; 1857 draws the consequence for giving: the devotee is a walking temple, and what he receives reaches the god of the built one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1860","b":"t:1851","type":"agrees","note":"Both rank feeding a jnani above the great religious gifts; 1851 by multiples of merit, 1860 against a thousand villages and a thousand temple towers.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1860","b":"k:259","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Both set one simple act above a thousand great rites: the Kural puts not killing and eating above a thousand sacrifices, Tirumular puts one meal for a jnani above a thousand gifts to Brahmins.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1861","b":"k:263","type":"agrees","note":"The Kural sees householders as those who provide for renunciants, perhaps at the cost of their own austerity; Tirumular makes that provision the highest merit, above feeding Brahmins.","grade":"wrong","better":null},{"a":"t:1866","b":"t:1858","type":"develops","note":"1858 says what the ascetic eats the three worlds eat; 1866 says the three gods eat it, and adds that his leavings give release.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1882","b":"t:1868","type":"agrees","note":"Both say the land where a devotee lives is blessed: 1868 with peace and rain, 1882 with true knowers and good order, and release from births.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1868","b":"k:19","type":"parallel_domain","note":"The Kural makes giving and austerity depend on rain; Tirumular runs it the other way, making rain depend on the presence of one who seeks the Lord.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"t:1876","b":"t:1863","type":"agrees","note":"Both speak of Tirumular as bound and sent by the Lord: 1863 as Nandi's message, 1876 by a deed of servitude written before birth.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:1885","b":"t:1857","type":"agrees","note":"Both place the Lord in the devotee: 1857 tells the giver that food for the walking temple reaches the god, 1885 tells the devotee that he begs and eats for the Lord in the temple within.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1888","b":"k:1062","type":"tension","note":"Both put the world's creator beside begging. The Kural says that if the maker of the world decreed that people live by begging he should wander and perish; Tirumular says the maker of the seven worlds begged himself so that his devotees could reach him by begging. The conflict holds if the Kural is read as condemning all living by alms, not only the beggary of the poor.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1886","b":"n:306","type":"agrees","note":"Both fault begging that comes from desire when the remedy lies within: the Naladiyar asks why one should beg of strangers when poverty can be ended by asking of oneself, Tirumular faults those who beg for their desires instead of reaping the field already ploughed.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1890","b":"k:360","type":"agrees","note":"The Kural says pain ends when desire, anger and confusion are gone; Tirumular says hunger, craving and anger do not stay in Siva's devotees, and adds that they pass beyond the heavens too.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1891","b":"t:1889","type":"same_image","note":"Both use வர இருந்தான் (waiting so as to come or be come to): in 1889 the Lord waits on the way, in 1891 the ascetic waits and the world comes to him.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:1894","b":"t:1893","type":"develops","note":"1893 names shambhavi and khechari among the mudras; 1894 says what each one is: the Lord's glance, and the truth that rises with Om.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1900","b":"t:1895","type":"agrees","note":"Both put the mudra of silence highest: 1895 calls it release beyond release, 1900 the seal of the liberated.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1896","b":"k:127","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural says whatever else one fails to guard, one should guard the tongue; Tirumular says guarding the tongue alone only makes one mute, and silence is true only when the mind is still too.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:1906","b":"t:1905","type":"qualifies","note":"The Siva-yogi of 1905 goes to a higher world and comes back; the faultless jnani of 1906 does not return. Together they rank the yogi below the jnani in what death brings.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:1906","b":"k:356","type":"agrees","note":"Both make release a matter of not coming back: the Kural's மற்றீண்டு வாரா நெறி and Tirumular's மீளார். Tirumular adds the setting of a silent samadhi at death; the Kural says only that seeing the true thing here leads there.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1910","b":"n:26","type":"tension","note":"Both speak of what is done with a corpse once its life has left. The Naladiyar asks what it matters whether it is dragged off, buried or thrown anywhere; Tirumular says that burning or exposing a jnani's body ruins the land. The conflict holds if the Naladiyar's indifference covers every body; if it speaks only of ordinary people, Tirumular's exception for the jnani escapes it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1911","b":"k:559","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Both tie the failure of rain to a wrong: in the Kural a king's crooked rule, in Tirumular the burning of a jnani's body. The same belief that the sky answers moral or sacred offence is applied to kingship and to the rites of the dead.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1911","b":"t:1823","type":"develops","note":"1823 makes the body of flesh a temple of the Lord; 1911 draws the consequence for the jnani's corpse: to burn it is to set fire to the Lord's temple.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1923","b":"t:1934","type":"parallel_domain","note":"The chapter's two bindus: the cosmic one from which kundalini, gods and speech unfold (1923), and the drop distilled from food in the body (1934). Tirumular gives both the same name and the same generative role.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:1932","b":"t:1931","type":"develops","note":"1931 says the Lord is in the seed unperceived; 1932 states the logic: no sprout apart from its seed, and seed and sprout only together.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1934","b":"n:29","type":"same_image","note":"Both use dew on a grass blade. The Naladiyar makes it the body's brief life and urges good deeds now; Tirumular makes it the rare drop of bindu from which the body's strength grows, something to be kept rather than a reminder of loss.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1936","b":"t:724","type":"agrees","note":"724 says that if the body perishes the life perishes, and that Tirumular learned how to nourish the body; 1936 names one way it perishes, the unguarded loss of bindu.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1936","b":"n:27","type":"qualifies","note":"Both start from the perishing body. The Naladiyar's wise see it as a bubble and resolve to end their births; Tirumular faults people for not guarding what keeps the body. The facts agree, the response differs.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:1948","b":"t:1936","type":"develops","note":"1936 laments that people do not know how bindu is lost or how to guard it; 1948 lists what stays when it is kept: breath, strength, intellect, tapas, japa, silence and siddhi.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1949","b":"t:1962","type":"agrees","note":"Both give the same sequence: kindle the fire at the root, raise it through the sealed central way, and drink the nectar. 1949 calls those who do it Siva-yogis; 1962 calls it bindu's victory by Shakti.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1964","b":"t:1946","type":"develops","note":"1946 says there is no harvest except for the sower; 1964 turns this into a riddle of three men who pound, roast or sow the seed, and only the sower is apart.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1966","b":"t:1933","type":"develops","note":"1933 traces food into body and mind, and 1934 into bindu; 1966 names the bindu of food and the bindu of breath and makes knowing both the way to the golden body.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:1969","b":"t:1923","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1923 makes cosmic bindu the source of powers, gods and speech; 1969 makes bindu and nada the source of all lives and mantras, and then turns to the bodily bindu whose containment brings Sivoham.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"t:1953","b":"k:1083","type":"same_image","note":"Both see woman as கூற்று, Death. In the Kural the lover discovers it, struck by her eyes; Tirumular makes it a deliberate meditation that ends desire and with it death.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1960","b":"n:46","type":"qualifies","note":"The Naladiyar quells desire by taking the woman's body apart into entrails and blood. Tirumular does not reject union: kept with bindu unspent, it is Siva's own pleasure, and only the fool's infatuation is condemned.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:1984","b":"t:1982","type":"same_image","note":"Both open on the sun's hidden road under the earth (பகலோன் வரும்வழி) and say no one knows it; 1984 adds what the learned say, that it circles the mountain, and that those who know the town know.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1981","b":"t:1986","type":"develops","note":"1981 says the world's divided talk babbles and passes while the truth in the light goes unseen; 1986, in the body's sun, says those who find where the sun settles still all that babble with it. Same words, பேதித்து and பிதற்று.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:1985","b":"t:754","type":"same_image","note":"Both put the decisive event in the குழல்வழி, the tube or central channel: in 754 the Dancer stands there for those who see the feet, in 1985 a victorious light shines there once the malams are killed.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:1993","b":"t:1923","type":"develops","note":"1923 lists what comes from bindu; 1993 divides bindu into lower and higher and pictures the unfolding as a dawn in the heart, setting up the chapter's sun of knowledge.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:2002","b":"t:2010","type":"agrees","note":"2002 says Siva is in each body like the sun in many pots, not shut in by them; 2010 says he is in the soul and the soul in him, while being everywhere. Presence within without confinement, in two images.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2006","b":"t:2017","type":"agrees","note":"2006 has the two swans that never part and faults the swan that holds itself apart; 2017 says there is no difference of soul and Siva once Siva is known. Both deny the soul a separate standing.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:2008","b":"t:2011","type":"same_image","note":"Both measure subtlety by repeated division: 2008 divides the atom into a thousand to reach the Lord in it, 2011 divides a cow's hair into a hundred and then a thousand to give the soul's size.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2016","b":"t:2025","type":"same_image","note":"Both picture the senses as fields where animals graze: 2016 has cows that must be led past the boundary, 2025 birds feeding in five fields and a herdsman with a staff.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:2020","b":"t:1924","type":"develops","note":"1924 counts the thirty-six tattvas; 2020 says the count is learned from Nandi by grace and is the step to standing beyond them.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:2021","b":"k:280","type":"agrees","note":"Both judge outward ascetic marks worthless without inner change: the Kural says neither shaving nor matted hair is needed if one gives up what is condemned (Parimelazhagar counts hair-plucking under shaving); Tirumular says hair-tearers do not know the impurities or grace.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2023","b":"k:24","type":"qualifies","note":"Both picture the senses as five elephants to be controlled. The Kural says the one who guards them with the goad of firmness is a seed for release; Tirumular shows the mahout exhausted and confesses he does not know how to manage it. The goal agrees; the confidence that it can be done does not.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2026","b":"k:126","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Two animal images for the same discipline: the Kural's tortoise draws its five limbs in; Tirumular's five lions go out to feed and must have claws and teeth cut. Withdrawal against disarming.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"t:2025","b":"n:59","type":"agrees","note":"Both count five senses and the gates through which craving comes and goes, and make guarding them the way to release; the Naladiyar names the five gates, Tirumular adds the nine openings and a herdsman.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:2028","b":"t:2024","type":"same_image","note":"2024 shows a mahout who cannot steer the leaping beast; in 2028 the poet says he himself has become that unruly horse.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2030","b":"k:380","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural asks what is stronger than fate; Tirumular gives fate's great strength the earth but assigns heaven to praise and human life to the mind, so fate is one power among several. The tension depends on reading 2030's domains as limits.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:2033","b":"k:343","type":"tension","note":"The Kural says the field of the five must be crushed (அடல்வேண்டும்); Tirumular calls those who say 'curb the five' ignorant and says full curbing makes one inert. The conflict holds if 2033 is read as rejecting suppression of the senses, as its wording says, rather than only a wrong method of it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2033","b":"t:2035","type":"qualifies","note":"The same chapter that mocks curbing the five (அடக்கு) praises subsiding in the five (ஒடுங்கு) as tapas and Hara's state: the objection is to forcing the senses, not to their stilling.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2034","b":"k:24","type":"same_image","note":"Both treat the five senses as elephants to be held, the Kural's guarded by the goad of firmness (on Parimelazhagar's reading), Tirumular's by a fort of knowledge. In the Kural the guard works; in Tirumular the elephants break out.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2034","b":"t:977","type":"same_image","note":"In 977 the five elephants of the forest are held by the goad of the five letters; in 2034 a fort of knowledge fails to hold them. Mantra succeeds where bare knowledge did not.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2035","b":"k:25","type":"agrees","note":"Both make mastery of the five a supreme power: the Kural calls Indra witness to it, Tirumular calls it the wide world, tapas and Hara's state.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2040","b":"k:334","type":"agrees","note":"Both count life away by its units of time, the Kural's day a saw cutting life, Tirumular's nazhigais and days already gone; Tirumular draws the lesson of conquering the senses now.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2048","b":"t:1680","type":"same_image","note":"Both end with blind and blind falling into the pit; 1680 blames the pupils who choose a guru who cannot cure blindness, 2048 the blind guru who shows the stick.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2058","b":"t:1573","type":"agrees","note":"Both define the Siva-guru by the same act, showing the real, the unreal and the real-unreal (சத்தும் அசத்தும் சதசத்தும்).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2054","b":"t:1511","type":"same_image","note":"Both use transmutation by contact: whatever comes to the golden mountain takes its colour (1511), whatever the philosopher's stone touches becomes gold (2054); both make the guru's presence change the one who comes near.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2051","b":"t:2054","type":"develops","note":"2051 says the iron once turned to gold never turns back; 2054 names the agent, the guru as a touchstone, and widens it to all the world he touches.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:2056","b":"t:1478","type":"agrees","note":"Both make the guru's path the one road that Nandi set out for salvation.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2067","b":"k:271","type":"agrees","note":"Both deny that hidden wrong is hidden: in the Kural the five elements within laugh at the deceiver's concealed conduct, in Tirumular the Lord stands everywhere as overseer. The Kural's witness is in the body, Tirumular's is the Lord.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2070","b":"t:2039","type":"agrees","note":"2039 makes north and south the temple of the mind; 2070 says the world gathered in the north is within, against those who walk north to find it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2071","b":"t:1729","type":"agrees","note":"1729 says Nandi took the body as his temple; 2071 faults those who search the land for the one fragrant within the body.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2074","b":"t:2067","type":"develops","note":"Both end in களவொழிந்தாரே; 2067 says stealth ends when the overseer is seen, 2074 says how he is seen: not with the eye, whose film hides the light.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2085","b":"k:2","type":"agrees","note":"Both ask what is gained by learning or argument that does not end in bowing at the Lord's feet; Tirumular names disputation (வாது) where the Kural names learning.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2090","b":"k:10","type":"agrees","note":"The Kural says only those who reach the Lord's feet cross the sea of births; Tirumular laments those who, given a rare human birth, never cherish those feet and so lose what that birth was for.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2099","b":"k:335","type":"agrees","note":"Both press for action before death arrives: the Kural before the hiccup stops the tongue, Tirumular while the porridge still boils and death runs near.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:2104","b":"k:972","type":"agrees","note":"Both deny that birth ranks people: the Kural says birth is alike for all and distinction comes from deeds; Tirumular says kin is one and God is one. The Kural keeps a distinction by deeds that Tirumular does not mention.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2120","b":"t:1581","type":"agrees","note":"1581 says Nandi taught that the guru is Siva; 2120 adds the condition, a guru who is a flawless knower, and says such a one is to be called nothing but the Supreme.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies"},{"a":"t:2108","b":"k:10","type":"agrees","note":"Both make reaching the Lord the end of births: the Kural's crossing of the sea of births, Tirumular's freedom from all birth for the soul that holds the Lord in mind at death.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2122","b":"t:167","type":"same_image","note":"Both make the body a bag left behind when its occupant goes: in 167 the dancer leaves the leather bag, so what crows do to it no longer matters; in 2122 the thief leaves and the maya-bag turns to earth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2124","b":"t:473","type":"agrees","note":"2124 repeats 473 almost word for word; in the chapter on conception it describes the forming embryo, here the subtle body of eight.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2125","b":"n:46","type":"same_image","note":"Both list the body's stuffs, fat, blood, bone, flesh, skin. The Naladiyar asks which of these is the beloved, to cure desire; Tirumular names them as the gross adjunct beneath the subtle bodies, without disgust.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:2136","b":"t:494","type":"develops","note":"494 names the classes of soul (விஞ்ஞானர், எஞ்ஞானர், மெய்ஞ்ஞானர்) by the malas they have left; 2136 gives each its body: anava, maya, karma, or Siva.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:2137","b":"t:725","type":"agrees","note":"725 says Tirumular once thought the body a flaw, then found the Lord's temple within it and now tends it; 2137 calls those who dismiss the body as filth dull.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2137","b":"n:43","type":"tension","note":"The Naladiyar says the great saw the body's foulness and abandoned it; Tirumular calls those who disregard the body as filth dull and finds the cosmos in it. The conflict holds if மலம் in 2137 is read as bodily filth, as here.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2140","b":"k:36","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural calls virtue the companion that does not perish when one perishes; Tirumular says vows and gifts are no sure support for the perishing flesh. The two can agree if the Kural speaks of the person and Tirumular of the body; the verse does not settle which it means.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2147","b":"t:2165","type":"same_image","note":"Both picture the body as a king's realm with horses and a company of five: in 2147 as the arrayed camp of the waking instruments, in 2165 as a city whose five servants sleep when the king rides in.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2154","b":"t:2142","type":"develops","note":"2142 places dream at the throat with fourteen instruments; 2154 says which ten fall away and that dream is built from the impressions of household life.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2163","b":"t:2162","type":"develops","note":"2162 describes anava as a stupor from which the soul falls into the states; 2163 gives the Lord's side: the states are his waking of the sleeper through maya.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2166","b":"t:2160","type":"qualifies","note":"2160 says the five malas keep every soul in endless birth and death; 2166 says the same five, seen plainly in waking, are the way to stand with the Supreme.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2148","b":"k:338","type":"agrees","note":"Tirumular takes the Kural's own phrase, உடம்பொடு உயிரிடை நட்பு. The Kural says that bond ends as a bird leaves its shell; Tirumular faults people who never come to know it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2148","b":"n:26","type":"agrees","note":"Both separate the life from the body it lives in: the Naladiyar's dancer leaves the skin bag, and Tirumular's fools cling to bodies without knowing the life inside.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:2158","b":"k:126","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the tortoise drawing in for the five senses held in. In the Kural it is self-restraint that guards through seven births; in Tirumular it follows when the silent and spoken letters meet.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2163","b":"k:339","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Both treat birth as a waking from sleep. The Kural uses it to show how quickly birth and death alternate; Tirumular makes embodiment the Lord's waking of the soul from anava's deeper sleep.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2176","b":"t:2172","type":"agrees","note":"Both reach the Vedanta count of twenty-eight from the twenty-four, but add different items to it: 2172 purusha, the Supreme and the void, 2176 turiya, inner time, the void and the higher turiya.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2179","b":"t:2178","type":"develops","note":"2178 reduces the countless tattvas to ninety-six and twenty-five; 2179 says which school counts which, making ninety-six common and thirty-six the Saiva count.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2181","b":"t:2170","type":"qualifies","note":"2170 pictures one who sits in the web of the senses and consumes their objects; 2181 separates the knower from all thirty-six inert tattvas, a knowledge the soul gets only from Nandi.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"t:2180","b":"k:1","type":"same_image","note":"Both make the letter A stand for what is first: the Kural compares God's place in the world to A among letters, and Tirumular names the real tattva the letter A.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2183","b":"t:2160","type":"develops","note":"2160 says the five malas bind every soul; 2183 grades them by the rank of beings, from five in the lowest to anava alone in Sadasiva.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2186","b":"t:2182","type":"develops","note":"2182 removes the five states, the five mala-states and the false thirty-six so that the soul stands as one; 2186 names where that ends, in the dance and in becoming Siva, with the five made Sivaya.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2200","b":"t:2142","type":"agrees","note":"A near-repetition: both place waking, dream, deep sleep and turiya at the brow, throat, heart and navel with fewer instruments in each. They differ in the counts (ஐஐந்தில் against பன்னான்கில்) and in the third line (பொய்கண்ட மூவர் against பொய்கண்டிலாத புருடன்), which may show a corrupt copy in one or both.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2193","b":"t:2015","type":"same_image","note":"Both use பசு in its double sense of cattle and bound soul. 2015 sorts the cows by what they yield, only the mature ones giving milk; 2193 makes them all one in their milk and one in their herdsman.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2211","b":"t:2192","type":"develops","note":"2192 names the three malas and calls for separating the soul from them; 2211 says the Lord gave the tattvas and instruments for exactly that, so that the malas might be cut.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2213","b":"k:352","type":"agrees","note":"Both make release the end of an obscuring mixture: the Kural's stainless sight, freed from confusion, removes darkness and brings joy; Tirumular says Shakti, knowledge and Siva are hidden by mala and light appears when the mixture ends. Tirumular names what hides; the Kural names only confusion.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2194","b":"k:351","type":"agrees","note":"Both trace a bad end to delusion: the Kural's confusion that takes the unreal as real yields a joyless birth, and in 2194 the soul whose delusion grows reaches the seven hells.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2215","b":"k:24","type":"same_image","note":"The Kural guards the five senses with the elephant goad of firmness; Tirumular ends his verse on standing in the true way before the mind scatters with a charging elephant. The pairing holds if 2215's elephant is the mastered senses or mind; if it is the strength one gains, the images only touch.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"t:2224","b":"t:2209","type":"develops","note":"2209 says that after knowing the knowledge that knows, one becomes that knower; 2224 turns this into a riddle: who can know the knower who knows without knowing?","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:2228","b":"n:248","type":"agrees","note":"Both say a person is the maker of his own rise and fall: the Naladiyar names who sets a man up and casts him down and answers 'himself', Tirumular says he is his own enemy, friend, next world and master.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2264","b":"k:351","type":"agrees","note":"The Kural traces joyless birth to taking the unreal for the real; Tirumular's first error is the same kind, taking the body for the self, and it too ends in birth again.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:2264","b":"t:2014","type":"qualifies","note":"2014 has the soul enter turiya and become knowledge; 2264 adds that turiya not joined to the Lord still leads back to birth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2262","b":"t:1527","type":"agrees","note":"Near-repetition: both open with the two deeds balancing and grace-power coming as or through the guru, and end with the impurities removed, as becoming Siva in 1527 and as non-birth here.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2267","b":"t:1924","type":"develops","note":"1924's numbers for the thirty-six tattvas do not add up as read; 2267 gives the standard grouping clearly, twenty-four impure, seven pure-impure and five pure.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:2290","b":"t:2289","type":"same_image","note":"Paired images of mutual concealment: gold and ornament for the soul and its faculties, wood and carved elephant for the Lord and the elements.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2279","b":"t:2288","type":"qualifies","note":"2288 makes knowing oneself the way to know the Lord; 2279 adds that knowing oneself alone doesn't end birth unless one knows the pervading Lord.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2274","b":"k:350","type":"agrees","note":"Both play on பற்று: the Kural bids one cling to the one without clinging in order to let go, Tirumular says the Supreme is grasped as attachment ends.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2303","b":"k:301","type":"agrees","note":"Both place the giving up of anger where one has the power to act on it: the Kural says restraint counts only where anger can take effect, Tirumular says let it go while you can still win.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:2304","b":"k:126","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the tortoise drawing in its five. The Kural makes restraint of the senses in one birth a shelter through seven; Tirumular puts the tortoise in a silent well and promises it a thousand years if it withdraws into truth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2316","b":"k:24","type":"same_image","note":"Both treat the five senses as elephants to be mastered (in the Kural through the goad of firmness, as Parimelazhagar reads it). The Kural's reward is the field of release; Tirumular adds a lamp of knowledge lit inside the body.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2318","b":"k:355","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural defines understanding as seeing the true thing in whatever thing appears; Tirumular narrows it to knowing oneself, and calls every other knowing a ghoul's.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2318","b":"t:251","type":"agrees","note":"251 says those who know themselves bow to the Lord and find him kin; 2318 says such self-knowing is the only knowledge that counts.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2329","b":"t:2323","type":"qualifies","note":"2323 has Nandi tell the soul it is the knower; 2329 adds that the soul does not know by itself and knows only when grace illumines it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2341","b":"t:2309","type":"same_image","note":"Both use fire and its heat. In 2309 the heat left in iron after the fire is gone is karma's residue; in 2341 heat inseparable from fire is grace inseparable from the Lord.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2351","b":"t:417","type":"qualifies","note":"417 makes the Lord the potter who shapes pots and births; 2351 likens him instead to the one clay in all vessels, and to the eye that sees all but itself. The two images give maker and inner presence.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2355","b":"t:1512","type":"agrees","note":"1512 defines Saivam as knowing oneself and reaching Siva; 2355 opens the chapter on knowledge with the same claim from the other side: not to know oneself is ruin.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:2366","b":"t:2355","type":"develops","note":"2355 states the rule that knowing oneself brings no harm; 2366 tells it as the speaker's own history, from long not knowing to a knowing after which nothing else is known.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2362","b":"k:358","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural sets understanding against the folly (பேதைமை) that is birth. Tirumular says the knowledge that frees lies beyond both the world's knowledge and its ignorance, so the line falls not between them but past them.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:2364","b":"k:430","type":"agrees","note":"Both make understanding a kind of total possession: the Kural says those with it have everything, Tirumular that their heart is the wide world itself and the place where the first Lord stays.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:2370","b":"t:1449","type":"develops","note":"1449 mentions the six of wide Vedanta and Siddhanta without listing them, if மாறும் is read as ஆறும்; 2370 names the six ends, Vedanta and Siddhanta first.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:2382","b":"t:2381","type":"develops","note":"2381 turns the ends into the triad of knowing, known and knower; 2382 then dissolves the triad itself, ends into known, knower into known, knowledge gone, leaving silence and bliss.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2386","b":"t:1900","type":"same_image","note":"Both make silence a mudra, a seal: 1900 calls the seal of silence the mark of the liberated, and 2386 ends the unity of Vedanta and Siddhanta in sinking into the silence of the seal.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2392","b":"t:1509","type":"develops","note":"1509 ends the grades of release when the bond dissolves and the Lord is sayujya; 2392 reads tat tvam asi onto the same scheme: 'thou' is the soul, 'that' the Lord left when the bond goes, and their union sayujya.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2393","b":"t:1435","type":"agrees","note":"1435 calls Siddhanta not other than Vedanta; 2393 says both are the one aim of becoming Siva, each in vain apart from it.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:2399","b":"t:2400","type":"develops","note":"2399 counts the ends as five, not six; 2400 holds the five together with Vedanta and Siddhanta made one, which explains the count.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2397","b":"t:2404","type":"agrees","note":"Both make the Veda and the Agama the Lord's own speech and, read rightly, the source of one Vedanta-Siddhanta; 2397 adds that the Veda is general and the Agama special.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2402","b":"t:2017","type":"agrees","note":"2017 says soul and Siva are not different and that souls who know Siva remain as Siva; 2402 calls that knowing of soul and Siva the meaning of tat tvam asi.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2394","b":"k:358","type":"agrees","note":"Both call what true knowledge reaches செம்பொருள், the perfect thing, and tie it to release. The Kural leaves it unnamed; Tirumular says Siddhanta and Vedanta are that perfect meaning and show Siva.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"t:2411","b":"t:2422","type":"develops","note":"2411 calls the bond the good bond, the altar before the lingam; 2422 explains why it is good: like dirt put on cloth to clean it, the bond is grace's instrument.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2416","b":"t:1927","type":"agrees","note":"Both name the Lord as the one who performs the five acts (ஐங்கருமம்) through bindu; 1927 calls him the seed and root, 2416 places the acts in the subtle order of bindu and nada.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:2421","b":"k:346","type":"agrees","note":"The Kural asks for cutting the pride that says 'I' and 'mine'; Tirumular names that 'I'-saying as anava, the root bond itself, and adds that grace is the means of its removal.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"t:2427","b":"k:9","type":"same_image","note":"Both join the Lord's feet to the head. The Kural calls a head that does not bow to them useless; in Tirumular the feet come down onto the head, as grace given rather than worship offered.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2433","b":"t:2427","type":"develops","note":"2427 says Nandi's feet came onto the poet's head; 2433 says what they did there: cut his bonds and his delusion and took him.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2425","b":"t:2421","type":"agrees","note":"2421 makes grace the means to reach the Lord's feet; 2425 makes the feet themselves grace, which shows the soul knowledge.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2436","b":"k:360","type":"agrees","note":"Tirumular takes the Kural's three, காமம் வெகுளி மயக்கம், word for word. The Kural says that when their very names perish, suffering perishes; Tirumular says that with them driven out he reached the abode within Om.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2439","b":"t:2425","type":"agrees","note":"2425 makes the Lord's foot Shakti as grace, which brings the soul to knowledge; 2439 makes 'art', the word that joins soul and Lord, the station of Shakti.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:2444","b":"t:2443","type":"develops","note":"2443 has the Lord setting going waking and the other states; 2444 places them inside the supreme turiya and names its rare turiya as the joining word.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:2448","b":"k:350","type":"agrees","note":"Both turn on பற்று: the Kural bids one cling to the one without clinging in order to let go of clinging; Tirumular repeats 'clinging so that clinging ends' in every line and makes it the way to the supreme beyond the supreme.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2446","b":"n:101","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the cow and her calf. In the Naladiyar the calf finds its mother among many cows as an old deed finds its doer; in Tirumular the mother rises at the thought of her calf, as the Lord comes to the soul. One image carries karma, the other grace.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2446","b":"t:2407","type":"same_image","note":"Both end on a lamp on a hill: in 2407 worship makes the knowledge like a lamp on the western hill, in 2446 Nandi becomes a lamp of virtue filling the hill.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2450","b":"t:2409","type":"agrees","note":"Both have the freed soul passing through kevala to the state beyond while still in waking (நனவில் அதீதம்).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2457","b":"t:2456","type":"develops","note":"2456 says Nandi cut away the tattvas, the two mayas and fear; 2457 adds what followed: he led the speaker beyond knowing and gave himself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2464","b":"t:2459","type":"agrees","note":"Both make Shakti the Supreme's body or form: in 2459 as his body moving in will and knowledge, in 2464 as the open space within the five elements.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2455","b":"k:706","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the crystal that shows what is near it. The Kural makes it the face showing the mind; Tirumular makes it the soul taking the colour of the Supreme it comes within.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2466","b":"t:2488","type":"develops","note":"2466 matches the three turiyas with the three padas; 2488 restates it as soul, Supreme and Siva appearing in turiya as tvam tat asi.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"t:2473","b":"k:358","type":"qualifies","note":"Both name the goal செம்பொருள். For the Kural, seeing it is the work of knowledge that ends birth; for Tirumular, Nandi as the true reality takes him and gives it by grace.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"t:2475","b":"k:351","type":"agrees","note":"Both trace birth to not knowing what truly is: the Kural to the delusion that takes the unreal as real, Tirumular to souls that do not know what is. Tirumular adds that those who know come to grace.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2483","b":"t:2482","type":"same_image","note":"Both use a gem whose light, lustre and rays belong to it: 2482 for the three forms resting in the Lord, 2483 for the experience in the three voids.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2493","b":"t:2485","type":"same_image","note":"Both argue identity through one man seen differently: 2485 one man in three roles, 2493 Devadatta recognised as the same man once changed circumstances are set aside.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2489","b":"k:360","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Both name a set of three whose ending ends the trouble: the Kural desire, anger and delusion, whose very names must perish; Tirumular the impurities and qualities, whose dying lets Siva appear.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"t:2500","b":"t:2497","type":"qualifies","note":"2497 ends at rest in a void beyond imagining; 2500 says that for those who know, there is no void either, nor heart nor form.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2499","b":"t:2503","type":"develops","note":"2503 maps the letters சி, வா, ய to Siva, the Supreme and the soul; 2499 drops நம to leave those letters as the clear form of the mantra.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2505","b":"t:2495","type":"develops","note":"2495 lets go the seven effects and seven causes to reach the voids; 2505 says the bound soul mixes in the seven effects and Parasiva in the seven causes, and the Supreme is in the void beyond both.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2501","b":"t:2463","type":"agrees","note":"Both say the Supreme too has adjuncts: 2463 that even the Supreme clinging to a causal adjunct is not pure, 2501 that the seven adjuncts of the Supreme must be cut away with the soul's.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:2511","b":"k:10","type":"same_image","note":"Both speak of a sea to be crossed. The Kural's sea is births, swum only by those at the Lord's feet; Tirumular's knower has already seen the shore, the waves of the soul settled to clear water.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"t:2512","b":"k:844","type":"agrees","note":"The Kural defines folly as the conceit 'we are wise'; Tirumular's heartless men say 'we know fully' while blind to the Lord, as futile as cutting the hare from the moon.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2525","b":"k:346","type":"agrees","note":"Both name the pride of 'I' and 'mine' as what must be cut. The Kural promises a world beyond the gods' to the one who cuts it; Tirumular makes cutting it, with the lesser releases set aside, the mark of the Siva-siddha.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2517","b":"k:6","type":"agrees","note":"Both make mastering the five senses the condition of reaching the Lord: the Kural's one who quenched the five senses, Tirumular's one who conquers them and knows him as one.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:2535","b":"t:2530","type":"develops","note":"2530 names a river and an inner tank; 2535 places the tank on a hilltop, filled without a river, and adds the lotus without mud worn by Siva.","grade":"sound","better":"same_image"},{"a":"t:2536","b":"t:2517","type":"agrees","note":"Both set standing and sitting and many words against the one who conquers and seeks the Lord: talk and posture do not reach him, mastery does.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2545","b":"t:2537","type":"develops","note":"2537 only names the nine presiding selves; 2545 sets them in the nine states and makes passing beyond their three turiyas into Siva the goal.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2550","b":"t:2071","type":"agrees","note":"The same verse appears word for word as 2071; the repetition places the search for Nandi within the body under the heading of purity as well.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2550","b":"t:725","type":"agrees","note":"725 tells how Tirumular once thought the body a flaw, then found the Lord's temple within it and began to tend it; 2550 faults those who search the land for the Lord fragrant within the body.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2550","b":"n:48","type":"tension","note":"Both say people do not know what the body is. For the Naladiyar its truth is the stinking cart the vultures tear when life leaves it; for Tirumular it holds Nandi, fragrant within. The tension holds if the Naladiyar's verse is read as a judgement of the body itself, not only of vanity about adorning it.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:2552","b":"k:298","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural sets outer purity by water against inner purity by truthfulness; Tirumular also moves purity inward but keeps ritual among its sources: discipline, worship, the fire rite and knowledge free one from ritual impurity.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:2546","b":"t:581","type":"same_image","note":"Both fix a place by finger-breadths from the nose: 581 twelve below it, as a point of meditation that keeps the body; 2546 three or four from its tip, as the Lord's unknown dwelling.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2556","b":"k:351","type":"agrees","note":"Both set the Real (பொருள்) against delusion (மருள்): the Kural makes taking the unreal for the real the cause of base births; Tirumular leaves all but the Lord's devotees whirling in delusion.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2566","b":"t:2570","type":"develops","note":"2566 names the third mode, implication that leaves and keeps (விட்டுவிடாத இலக்கணை); 2570 applies it to the great saying, as in the Vedantic example 'this is that Devadatta', to make the soul the Supreme.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2577","b":"t:2568","type":"develops","note":"2568 sets out the saying analytically, word by word and turiya by turiya; 2577 says what completes it: 'you are that' must become 'I have become that', and this happens by Nandi's grace.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2581","b":"t:2580","type":"develops","note":"2580 tells the seeker to put away both knowing and not-knowing before Siva who is knowledge itself; 2581 gives the reason: the soul knows the elements, but knows the Lord only as the Lord makes it know.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2587","b":"n:27","type":"same_image","note":"Both see the body as a bubble. The Naladiyar's wise draw from it the resolve to end their births; Tirumular sets the bubble beside camphor in fire and has the body go into the Supreme.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2593","b":"t:2590","type":"develops","note":"2590 says the Supreme eats the world and spits it out again; 2593 says what is swallowed, soul, Supreme, turiya and the cosmos, and that it stays outwardly whole like the emptied wood-apple.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2600","b":"k:294","type":"parallel_domain","note":"The Kural says one who is not false in his heart dwells in the hearts of all people; Tirumular says the Lord dwells with those mingled in truth and does not enter before the false. Truthfulness opens the heart in both: to others in the Kural, to the Lord in Tirumular.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2596","b":"k:354","type":"agrees","note":"The Kural says knowledge from the five senses is no gain without true perception; Tirumular says knowing everything is no gain apart from the knowledge that knows everything.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2596","b":"k:2","type":"agrees","note":"Both deny that knowledge profits by itself: the Kural asks what learning gains without worship of the one of pure knowledge, Tirumular says knowing all things gains nothing apart from the all-knowing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2608","b":"k:350","type":"agrees","note":"Both make the way out of bondage a following: the Kural says cling to the one without clinging in order to let clinging go, Tirumular says cut the senses' bond and follow the one who cut away confusion.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2608","b":"k:6","type":"agrees","note":"The Kural praises those who keep to the path of the one who quenched the five senses; Tirumular tells the seeker to cut the five senses' bond and follow the one who has done so.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2611","b":"t:2610","type":"develops","note":"2610 says how the jnani deals with former and later karma; 2611 says by what power: Siva's grace placed on the head.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2610","b":"n:33","type":"agrees","note":"Both have the wise meet arriving karma calmly: the Naladiyar's wise take it as old debt and are freed of confusion, Tirumular's jnanis live it out and are rid of it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2610","b":"k:380","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural says nothing is stronger than what is apportioned, which arrives before any scheme to avoid it. Tirumular grants that former karma must be lived out, but adds that the jnani can stop karma not yet made, which the Kural's verse does not address.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2611","b":"k:5","type":"agrees","note":"Both free the Lord's own from the hold of deeds: in the Kural the two kinds of deed do not cling to those who praise him, in Tirumular the jnani unties former karma by Siva's grace.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2613","b":"k:365","type":"agrees","note":"Both make cutting desire the one test that counts: the Kural says only those free of desire are truly free, Tirumular says yogic talk of breath is useless until desire and attachment are cut.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2615","b":"t:298","type":"tension","note":"In verse 298 Tirumular says if you cling, cling to the Supreme; here he says cut desire even if it is for the Lord. The conflict holds if ஆசை here is the same as பற்று there; if 2615 means desire for gains from the Lord, the two fit.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2615","b":"k:350","type":"tension","note":"The Kural tells the renouncer to cling to the one free of clinging as the means of letting go; Tirumular tells him to cut desire even for the Lord. It depends on reading ஈசனோடாயினும் as desire directed at the Lord himself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2615","b":"k:369","type":"agrees","note":"Both say desire is the source of sorrow and its ending brings unbroken joy: the Kural calls desire the sorrow of sorrows, Tirumular says bliss grows as desire is let go.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2616","b":"n:59","type":"agrees","note":"Both tie release to mastering the longing (வேட்கை) that works through the five: the Naladiyar has it guarded at the five gates of the senses, Tirumular has it flung away with the five elements.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:2617","b":"k:361","type":"agrees","note":"Both set அவா (desire) beside தவா (unfailing): the Kural calls desire the seed of unfailing births, Tirumular has humans sunk in the sea of desire while the Lord holds the unfailing sea.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"t:2614","b":"t:2650","type":"agrees","note":"Both deny the Lord a dwelling outside the person: 2614 says he is in no mansion, hall or temple but in the desireless heart, 2650 says this one's house is his only house.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2640","b":"t:2615","type":"qualifies","note":"2615 says cut desire even for the Lord; here the creeper reaches the Lord joined with longing (விருப்பொடு கூடி). Read together, longing that seeks the Lord is not the desire 2615 cuts, unless 2640's longing belongs to Uma alone.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"t:2641","b":"t:2614","type":"develops","note":"2614 says the Lord is in no temple but in the desireless heart; 2641 tells it as experience: Nandi comes and takes the inner self as his temple.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2657","b":"t:2940","type":"agrees","note":"2940, in the chapter on the samadhi of silence, opens with the same line, the light shining beyond the three turiyas, and also speaks of expanding and contracting; here the state is the guru's and cannot be spoken, there it is a state of experience beyond speech.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2658","b":"k:9","type":"agrees","note":"The Kural calls a head that does not bow to the Lord's feet as useless as a sense that cannot perceive; Tirumular says the same from the other side, that his head, mouth, eyes and mind were given to him for Nandi.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2667","b":"n:176","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Both make the small great by what they cling to: in the Naladiyar the hare is worshipped with the moon it lives in, so the slight gain worth by friendship with steady men; in Tirumular those as small as a split millet seed become high by reaching the Lord with a firm mind.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2667","b":"k:505","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural makes one's own deeds the touchstone of greatness and smallness; Tirumular names another measure, the mind that reaches the Lord, by which even the smallest become high. The two need not conflict, but the test differs.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2680","b":"t:2664","type":"develops","note":"2664 says that one who stands in the sixteen-fold path (சோடச மார்க்கம்) sees as other what seemed oneself; 2680 places the same path among the sanmargis and names the stages they pass beyond to rest at the end of knowing.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"t:2677","b":"t:2699","type":"develops","note":"2677 makes Om the source of elements, world and souls; 2699, on the five letters, takes Om apart as A, U and M and turns from it to Na, the first letter of Nandi's name.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:2684","b":"t:2774","type":"same_image","note":"Both give Siva three eyes that are three lights: here sun, moon and the forehead fire, in 2774 the three eyes in the dancer's body whose limbs are space and the eight directions.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2694","b":"n:51","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Both use light driving out darkness: in the Naladiyar austerity drives out sin as a lamp does darkness, and evil returns when good deeds run out like a lamp's oil; in Tirumular the Lord's light casts off maya's darkness through renunciation, with no word of its return.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:2698","b":"t:965","type":"agrees","note":"965, in the chapter on the chakra of the holy hall, is nearly the same verse; there the fifty letters 'go' and become the five (எழுத்தும்போய்), here they simply are the five. The repetition ties the five letters of this chapter to the earlier letter diagrams.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2699","b":"k:1","type":"same_image","note":"Both begin from the letter A as the first of all letters. The Kural uses it as a simile for the Lord as first of the world; Tirumular traces the letters from A through Om to Na, the first letter of Nandi's name, so the alphabet itself leads to the Lord.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2707","b":"t:2094","type":"agrees","note":"2094 tells people to worship the Lord daily until they sleep (துஞ்சும் அளவும்); 2707 gives the words for that hour, calling him Lord and his feet refuge.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2704","b":"t:2709","type":"develops","note":"2704 says that saying Sivaya nama makes nectar well up; 2709, opening the next chapter, makes the same formula an alchemical pill that turns the body to gold. Both give the recited name a bodily effect.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2709","b":"t:905","type":"same_image","note":"Both make the five letters an alchemy: chanting turns copper to gold in 905, and in 2709 the mantra is a குளிகை that turns the body to gold, which makes the body the metal transformed.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2712","b":"t:1013","type":"agrees","note":"Both drop நம from the mantra as the soul's bound part: 1013 calls it the soul and says when it falls away the search ends, 2712 calls it impurity and leaves சிவாய as the truth free of it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2711","b":"t:979","type":"develops","note":"979 says சிவாய becomes the soul, its enjoyment and knowledge and ends in becoming Siva; 2711 spells out what the full name holds (Siva, grace, soul, concealment, impurity) and how the last are stilled before the soul becomes the supreme Siva.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2716","b":"k:5","type":"agrees","note":"Both free a person from deeds through the Lord's name: the Kural says the two kinds of deed do not cling to those who love his true praise, Tirumular that saying Siva Siva makes evil deeds die. Tirumular adds the converse, that evil-doers do not say it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2720","b":"n:135","type":"agrees","note":"Both answer endless learning with a choice: the Naladiyar says learning has no shore and life is short, so take only what is fitting, as the swan takes milk from water; Tirumular names the one thing to take, the Lord's letter, and makes it the vessel that does reach the shore.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2720","b":"k:10","type":"same_image","note":"Both picture release as crossing to a shore: the Kural's great sea of births is crossed only by those who reach the Lord's feet, and in 2720 the one who masters the Lord's letter is a vessel that has reached the farther shore.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:2721","b":"k:392","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural calls letters and numbers the two eyes of the living; 2721 calls those who boast of knowing letters fools, because they don't know the letter that masters letters. The difference rests on எழுத்து meaning script in the Kural and the mantra in Tirumular, so it qualifies rather than contradicts.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:2779","b":"k:1090","type":"same_image","note":"Both use liquor to measure an intoxication that comes without drinking. The Kural says brewed liquor delights only those who drink it, while love delights those who merely see; Tirumular says the frenzy of Siva's bliss comes to those who saw the dance and even to those who only heard of it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2768","b":"n:26","type":"same_image","note":"Both put a dancer inside the body. The Naladiyar's dancer is the soul in its skin bag, and once it leaves, the body does not matter; Tirumular's dancers in every embodied life are Siva and Shakti themselves.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:2740","b":"k:10","type":"develops","note":"The Kural says only those who reach the Lord's feet cross the sea of births; Tirumular uses the same 'reaching the feet' to define the devotee, and makes the feet those of the dancer seen in the golden hall.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"t:2770","b":"t:1726","type":"develops","note":"1726 says the human body's form is Chidambaram and the holy dance; 2770 locates the small hall at one point in it, the space between the brows reached by fixed gaze.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2775","b":"t:919","type":"agrees","note":"919 says the five letters are seated as the temple; 2775 says the hall of the dance is the five letters. Both make the mantra the shrine.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2760","b":"t:2317","type":"same_image","note":"Two verses of nearly the same shape: the dancing foot, the sound that rises with it and the songs, and then 'this is how I saw'. 2317 sees them within the nadi; 2760 sees them within at the guru's word and ends the search there.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2799","b":"t:2727","type":"develops","note":"2727 says the five dances are the five acts; 2799 assigns each act to a part of the dancer: drum, hand, fire and the two feet.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2754","b":"t:2747","type":"agrees","note":"Both map the body's three channels onto the land, with Meru and Tillai as the central channel and the Himalaya and Lanka at the sides; 2754 states the matching more plainly.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2798","b":"t:2797","type":"develops","note":"2797 reads the dancer's hands as syllables; 2798 adds the foot and tells the reader to recite சிவாயநம in that form.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2796","b":"t:2787","type":"agrees","note":"The same four-line pattern: what the ignorant say, that no one knows the dance, and what the dance is once known. In 2787 it is the Shakti of grace; in 2796 it is the end of the self.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2779","b":"t:2778","type":"develops","note":"2778 says seeing the dance works as the sight of tamarind does on the mouth; 2779 extends the effect from seeing to hearing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2812","b":"t:2685","type":"develops","note":"2685 ranks the lights of the five elements, with space among them, and gathers all five into one; 2812 takes space alone and grades it from the outer worlds to the knowing within and the light in Siva.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"t:2816","b":"t:1818","type":"same_image","note":"Both verses are built on repeated விளக்கு (lamp): in 1818 lighting the lamp lets one know the open space and those who light it shine in it; in 2816 the lamp lit within the lamp leads to the feet of the lamp's owner.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2815","b":"t:2823","type":"develops","note":"2815 names the churning, Siva taking the poison so the gods could drink nectar; 2823 turns that nectar into what Siva himself is to those who can see.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"t:2825","b":"t:2592","type":"develops","note":"In 2592 the Lord takes the poet into himself like water into hot iron and swallows the light of the three voids; 2825 describes the bliss of one who has gone past the three voids into supreme knowledge-bliss.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:2826","b":"t:2437","type":"develops","note":"2437 names the three padas, 'thou', 'that' and 'art', which bring one to Siva; 2826 gives each its turiya and puts the good in the third.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2829","b":"t:2843","type":"same_image","note":"The water lily (உற்பலம்) whose form, colour, scent and beauty cannot be separated: in 2829 for truth and the rest in the soul's union, in 2843 for the guru in whom form and quality are swallowed into the true form.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2846","b":"t:2820","type":"develops","note":"2820 says the Lord granted that 'I' and 'He' are not two and the poet's thinking ceased; 2846 speaks from the far side of that gift: the First can now be called 'I'.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2847","b":"k:379","type":"agrees","note":"Both ground equanimity in fate: the Kural asks why those who accept good fortune grieve at bad; Tirumular's knowers are unchanged whether anointed with sandal or struck with a chisel, since all comes by Nandi's decree.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2848","b":"k:377","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural says one enjoys only as the apportioner (வகுத்தான்) apportioned; Tirumular says nothing is laid by from heaven except by the decree one has made oneself, placing the apportioning in one's own former deeds.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2848","b":"n:101","type":"agrees","note":"Both make fate one's own past deeds: the Naladiyar's old deed finds its doer as a calf finds its mother among many cows; Tirumular's decree is the one 'one made oneself before'.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2852","b":"k:338","type":"same_image","note":"Both picture the body as a bird's nest or shell the life leaves; the Kural uses it to show how slight the bond of body and life is, Tirumular to say the Lord will build another nest.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2852","b":"k:340","type":"develops","note":"The Kural asks whether the life lodging in the body has found no home of its own; Tirumular accepts the body as a passing house and draws comfort from it: losing one is like entering another, for those who know Nandi.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2854","b":"k:357","type":"agrees","note":"If 2854's 'no going, no coming' is read of the knower, both say that examining and grasping what truly is ends birth: the Kural's mind that ponders and knows what is need not think of birth again.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2853","b":"t:2804","type":"develops","note":"2804 places the Lord as Om, fire and right order within the heart; 2853 goes further and says the mind and Siva are not different, Siva showing within the mind as it clears.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:2856","b":"t:2831","type":"same_image","note":"Both make the bliss bodily: in 2831 turiya, sweet as milk and honey, soaks every hair-root; in 2856 nectar wells up at each hair-root.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2857","b":"t:304","type":"same_image","note":"Both end on the Lord who stays as scent in a flower (மலர்க்கந்தம் ... நின்றானே); 304 sets the image after the devotee's babbling praise, 2857 after naming him as all things yet beyond thought.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2857","b":"k:1109","type":"parallel_domain","note":"The Kural names sulking and union as what lovers gain from love; Tirumular, using the same pair (புலவி, புணர்வு), says the Lord is both, carrying love's moments over to the soul and God.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2861","b":"t:2857","type":"qualifies","note":"2857 affirms that he is awareness, life, union; 2861 denies him every name, then reconciles the two: being all these, it is none of them.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2863","b":"t:2498","type":"agrees","note":"Both call the state where turiya subsides the void where words cease (சொல்லறும் பாழ்) and say it is hard to know or tell.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2865","b":"k:350","type":"agrees","note":"Both make the one without attachment the only thing to cling to: the Kural bids one hold to his hold to let go of holding, Tirumular says the unattached hold to the Supreme and the kinless find him their kin.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2865","b":"t:38","type":"qualifies","note":"In 38 the poet vows never to stop babbling Nandi's name; 2865 says those who gain his light stop babbling. If பிதற்று is the same praise in both, the vow belongs to the seeker and the silence to the one who has arrived.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2883","b":"t:2025","type":"agrees","note":"Both put a single herdsman over the five (senses): 2025 has one man graze them with a staff, 2883 says that once herded and checked the five cows give milk.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"t:2883","b":"k:24","type":"agrees","note":"The Kural guards the five with the goad of firmness and calls that man a seed for release; Tirumular herds them and they yield milk. Both make the senses useful once controlled.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2883","b":"k:343","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural says the field of the five must be destroyed (அடல், which Parimelazhagar glosses as கெடுத்தல்); Tirumular wants the five herded so they give milk. If அடல் means only 'conquer', the two agree; on the stronger reading they differ on whether the senses are ended or tamed.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2901","b":"t:201","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the man who passes over sweet fruit at hand to toil for a poor one: in 201 the jackfruit and the wild date stand for wife and another's wife; in 2901 the banana, jaggery and honey against the poisonous etti are left unnamed.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2901","b":"k:100","type":"same_image","note":"The Kural uses the ripe fruit left for the raw one for harsh speech when sweet words are at hand; Tirumular sets the sweet beside the poisonous etti, and his riddle leaves the referent open.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2880","b":"t:2872","type":"develops","note":"2872 ends with those who plough barren land with the breath-yoke; 2880 says their intent is unknowable and what sprouts in such soil may wither.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2903","b":"t:2883","type":"develops","note":"2883 has the wild cows herded so they give milk; 2903 drives the grazing cows beyond the field to the Lord's feet.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"t:2919","b":"k:349","type":"agrees","note":"The Kural says birth is cut only when attachment is cut; Tirumular says the bud's flowering is seen only by those who have let the bond go.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2939","b":"t:129","type":"same_image","note":"Both make the highest state a kind of sleep: in 129 the wise saw Siva-bhoga within while sleeping; here the Lord and the lady sleep in an enjoyment that has no sleep.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2942","b":"t:1987","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the same phrase for the hidden way in, the ford no one knows (துறை அறியோம், துறையறிவார் இல்லை): in 1987 the breath that circles and lies plugged, here the place where devotees drew bliss.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2944","b":"t:2946","type":"agrees","note":"Two arguments for the same claim, that the state is known only by having it: the mother can't tell her daughter her joy, and the taste of the fruit and the nature of a wife are known only to the eater and the husband.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2940","b":"t:2981","type":"same_image","note":"Both use spreading and drawing in (விரிவு குவிவு, விரிந்து குவிந்தது): here the light beyond turiya swallows and spits out the movement; in 2981 it is the mind's movement, whose end with speech is release.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:2951","b":"k:346","type":"develops","note":"The Kural promises a world above the gods to one who cuts off the pride of 'I' and 'mine'; Tirumular describes the cutting from inside, the self, wealth, body and mind undone until even the 'I' that would notice is gone.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2955","b":"t:1896","type":"same_image","note":"Both use muteness against false silence: in 1896 one whose speech alone stops is merely mute (மூங்கை) while true silence stills the mind too; here those who talk of the unspeakable are the mutes, and the Lord is seen by the mind gone still.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2962","b":"t:2718","type":"develops","note":"2718 says repeating சிவாய நம and சிவசிவ fixes the mind and is bliss; here the mantra has become a fruit already eaten, and the speaker reports its sweetness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2968","b":"k:269","type":"agrees","note":"Both say death can be beaten by those who have the power of tapas: the Kural speaks of leaping past Yama, Tirumular of striking him with the sword of knowledge in a mind where tapas has come.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2980","b":"t:272","type":"agrees","note":"272 says only those who melt with love can reach 'my gem' (என்பொன் மணி), whatever austerity others do; here the speaker melts to the bone, weeping, and holds 'my golden gem' as his own.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2982","b":"t:1823","type":"agrees","note":"1823 makes the body of flesh the temple and the heart the shrine; 2982 tells the same as a story of search: those who chased the Lord across lands lost him, and the speaker found him in the body.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2985","b":"t:27","type":"agrees","note":"Both end on the same phrase, புகுந்துநின்றான்: in 27 the Lord enters the minds of those who bow to Nandi daily, in 2985 he enters and stays in his devotees' hearts.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2987","b":"t:34","type":"agrees","note":"Both make the Lord's thousand names (ஆயிர நாமம்) a practice: 34 praises them going and sitting, 2987 promises protection and the end of desire to those who recite them.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:2995","b":"k:5","type":"agrees","note":"The Kural says the two kinds of deed do not cling to those devoted to the Lord's praise; Tirumular says the lord whom the worshipper keeps in mind cuts away deeds and the planets' sway.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:3016","b":"k:10","type":"agrees","note":"If அள்ளற் கடல் is the sea of births, the two verses agree: the Kural's sea of births is crossed by those who reach the Lord's feet, and Tirumular's sea of mire is cut for those who proclaim his bounty.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:3019","b":"t:13","type":"agrees","note":"Both set the Lord beyond those who would know him: in 13 Vishnu, Brahma and the gods cannot measure him, in 3019 the siddhas and gods think on him through all time without knowing him.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:3021","b":"t:11","type":"same_image","note":"Both end with the Lord as rain: in 11 Nandi is the rain-pouring cloud, in 3021 the Lord is everywhere like rain, answering the question of where he is.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:3023","b":"t:10","type":"develops","note":"10 lists the Lord as bearer of the earth, fire, sun, moon, mountain and sea, ending தடவரை தண்கடலாமே; 3023 repeats the frame, ending தடம்கடலாமே, but grounds each line in his own strength and adds that he is lighter than an atom.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:3035","b":"t:2857","type":"agrees","note":"Nearly the same verse. 2857 makes the Lord union and lovers' sulking (புணரும், புலவி); 3035 makes him union and the senses (புணர்வும், புலனும்), so the love pair becomes a list of faculties he pervades. Both end on the scent in the flower cluster.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:3044","b":"t:1722","type":"agrees","note":"Nearly the same verse: the Lord stood where nothing could know him, and the eight worlds. 1722 ends with his taking the poet's body as his temple; 3044 ends with Nandi as the one who knows this, our lord.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:3027","b":"t:1797","type":"develops","note":"Both turn on the same line, the bright flame that knows the flesh and breathes within. 1797 asks who could know the Lord if that flame did not; 3027 answers that when it knows, one can reach him.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:3040","b":"t:449","type":"same_image","note":"Both place the Lord by the same four stations, within (உண்ணின்ற), in the sky (விண்ணின்று), on earth (மண்ணின்ற) and in the eye (கண்ணின்ற). 449 names him the light within and the gem in the eye; 3040 the breath within, the sun, the wind and thought.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:3041","b":"k:392","type":"parallel_domain","note":"The Kural calls number and letter (எண், எழுத்து) the eyes of living beings; Tirumular makes the Supreme their maker and goes on to the eye that takes things in. Learning's two eyes become his gift.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"t:3043","b":"k:1","type":"agrees","note":"Both name the Lord as ஆதி, the first: the Kural makes the world have Adi Bhagavan as its first, as letters have அ; Tirumular calls Sankara 'the first, our first', present with all the world yet one.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"t:3046","b":"t:99","type":"develops","note":"Both open with the same line on the three thousand Tamil verses Mulan spoke. 99 makes the work Nandi's grace and promises the Lord to those who recite it; 3046 adds three hundred mantras and thirty teachings and declares the three one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:3047","b":"t:73","type":"agrees","note":"The book's frame: in 73 Tirumular puts Nandi's holy feet on his head before telling the Agama; in 3047 the work ends by blessing the same feet (நந்தி திருவடி).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"t:3047","b":"k:2","type":"agrees","note":"Both honour the feet of the Lord named by his knowledge: the Kural's வாலறிவன் (one of pure knowledge), whose feet give learning its point, and Tirumular's மெய்ஞ்ஞானத்தவன் (one of true knowledge) and மலமிலான் (the one without impurity).","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"p:2","b":"p:4","type":"same_image","note":"Both songs are built on a long list of Ganapati's food offerings (fruits, appam, sugarcane, dal, sesame, puffed rice, tender coconut). In 2 the list is the god's own table; in 4 it is what the worshipper sets before him with sit-ups and ear-holding.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:8","b":"t:176","type":"agrees","note":"Both set the Lord against Death's messengers at the hour of death. Tirumular says those who think of the Lord will not be harmed by them; 8 is the prayer of one who has not done the thinking and asks Murugan to come anyway, on the peacock, at that moment.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies"},{"a":"p:15","b":"k:1069","type":"agrees","note":"The Kural says the heart melts at the thought of begging; 15 confesses the act, a poet praising patrons as Tamil's refuge 'with a wounded heart', and asks to trade that service for service at Murugan's feet.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:19","b":"k:911","type":"agrees","note":"Both describe women who want a man for his wealth and whose sweet invitations harm him; the Kural warns, 19 confesses to having trusted them.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:12","b":"p:6","type":"same_image","note":"Both make Murugan Siva's teacher: in 6 he teaches what precedes the Veda, in 12 he punishes Brahma and gives the knowledge to Siva.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:23","b":"n:25","type":"same_image","note":"Both hear the funeral drum over the corpse while kin wail. The Naladiyar's drum mocks the man who still marries and seeks joy; 23 draws a different lesson, that one should share even a grain before the summons comes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:23","b":"k:322","type":"agrees","note":"The Kural makes sharing one's food the chief of all duties; 23 arrives at the same act, sharing even a millet grain, through fear of death rather than compassion.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:33","b":"n:371","type":"agrees","note":"Both make the courtesan's love last exactly as long as a man's money: the Naladiyar's lamp goes out with its oil, and in 33 the woman who melted him while wealth lasted beats him from the door when it ends.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:34","b":"n:14","type":"develops","note":"Both turn desire away by showing the desired woman old. The Naladiyar gives one picture, the woman bent on her mother's staff; 34 takes each charm the songs praise, hair, eyes, breasts, and shows its ruin, then names a different remedy, service at the Lord's feet.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:30","b":"n:48","type":"same_image","note":"Both see the living body as a stinking corpse: the Naladiyar's cart torn by vultures once its axle breaks, and 30's body that already stinks, rots and rattles with worms and bones.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:28","b":"p:8","type":"agrees","note":"Both pray at the hour of death: 8 asks Murugan to come on the peacock when Death's men strike, 28 that Death not call him while his body fails and his mother weeps, and that he reach the feet.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:24","b":"k:1229","type":"same_image","note":"Both make evening the hour when love's sickness deepens; the Kural's woman says it bewilders the whole town, while in 24 a mother names the crescent, the south wind and the anril bird as what wears her daughter down.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:43","b":"k:913","type":"same_image","note":"Both bring a corpse into the courtesan's bed: in the Kural the man embracing her is embracing a stranger's corpse in a dark room; in 43 it is she who would embrace even a corpse for money.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:48","b":"n:46","type":"agrees","note":"Both list the body's insides, guts, blood, bone, sinews and skin, almost word for word (குடர், குருதி, என்பு, நரம்பு, தோல்). The Naladiyar turns the list on the desired woman; 48 turns it on the poet's own body, which he still fondles.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"p:38","b":"n:24","type":"same_image","note":"Both follow the drums of a funeral to the moment the body is left: the Naladiyar ends with the dying carrying the dead; 38 ends with the mourners leaving it on the road, and calls the whole scene mithya, a false life to escape.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:46","b":"p:8","type":"agrees","note":"Both ask Murugan to come on the peacock at the hour when Death's men arrive with the noose; 46 adds the kin following to the burning ground and asks above all to see his form.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:54","b":"k:350","type":"agrees","note":"The Kural bids one cling to the clinging of the one without clinging, to be free of all other clinging; 54 asks, after confessing his infatuation, to attain clinging to Murugan (நின் பற்று அடைவேனோ).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:52","b":"k:9","type":"same_image","note":"Both name the Lord the eight-qualitied (எண்குணத்தான், எண்குணன்): the Kural makes the head that does not bow to his feet useless; in 52 the eight-qualitied Siva is himself the one who bows, worshipping Murugan.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:57","b":"n:44","type":"same_image","note":"Both list the conventional comparisons for a woman's eyes, the carp, the lily, the spear. The Naladiyar names them as the talk of blind petty men to be ignored; 57 piles them up as the delusion that holds the poet, then asks to be freed of it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:58","b":"p:57","type":"same_image","note":"The two songs are built alike: a confession that catalogues women's adornments, then a praise made of a chain of epithets ending -க்கார, each naming one role of Murugan. Read together they show the formula at work.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:60","b":"t:269","type":"agrees","note":"Tirumular warns against withering in praise of petty men for their wealth and bids one praise the Lord instead; 60 asks to give up praising impure men and to worship Murugan's feet.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:55","b":"p:15","type":"same_image","note":"Both quote the patron's flattery, the giver called a cloud and a lord of red or lotus hands. In 15 the poet confesses having begged so; in 55 he asks to keep such names for Murugan alone.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:64","b":"p:24","type":"qualifies","note":"64 scorns poets who sing a girl's love-sickness, with Kama's arrows, the south wind and the crescent, without naming Thiruchendur; 24 uses the same devices, but for a girl in love with the lord of Senthil. The scorn falls on poems that leave out the shrine, not on the akam convention itself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:63","b":"p:46","type":"develops","note":"46 asks Murugan to come on the peacock when Death's messengers drag the poet off; 63 asks further that he speak to Death and claim the man as his own devotee.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:68","b":"n:13","type":"develops","note":"Both picture old age with speech failing, teeth gone and a staff in hand. The Naladiyar's old man still keeps his eyes on desire; in 68 the women laugh at him, and the poet turns the scene into a prayer for Murugan to come at the hour of death.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"p:69","b":"k:1066","type":"agrees","note":"The Kural holds nothing more degrading to the tongue than asking; 69 confesses a tongue put to exactly that use, poems of praise carried from one patron's door to another.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:76","b":"p:69","type":"develops","note":"Both list the poet's genres, கோவை, உலா, மடல் and the rest. In 69 they are tools for begging at patrons' doors; in 76 they are titles of honour and the source of the poet's pride, which he asks to have removed.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:75","b":"p:8","type":"agrees","note":"Both draw Death on his buffalo with noose and club and ask Murugan to come on the peacock at that hour; 75 grounds the claim in hereditary service, 8 in the confession that he has not served.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:73","b":"k:918","type":"agrees","note":"The Kural calls the embrace of deceiving women an அணங்கு, a possessing spirit; 73 gives the deceit a literal form, the love-pellets and the charm hidden in the betel.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:88","b":"k:920","type":"agrees","note":"The Kural puts double-minded women, liquor and dice together as the company of those Fortune has abandoned; 88 joins two of the three in one figure, the courtesan who herself drinks palm toddy.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:94","b":"t:335","type":"agrees","note":"Both describe the yogi who binds the breath and tastes the nectar of bliss from the moon; Tirumular sets it against the liquor of fools, 94 against a life of delusion and the elements' illusions.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:93","b":"p:68","type":"same_image","note":"Both catalogue old age through the failing senses, cough and phlegm before death; 68 adds the mocking women and the family, 93 asks for more than rescue, the removal of Death's very principle.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:91","b":"p:69","type":"agrees","note":"Both confess wandering from door to door of worthless patrons with poems; 91 adds that Murugan himself is the true owner of the Tamil garlands the poet wasted.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:83","b":"p:76","type":"qualifies","note":"76 asks to be rid of the poet's titles and honours as pride; 83 asks for the poet's title and fame, but only so that his service and song to Murugan may flourish.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:103","b":"k:1085","type":"same_image","note":"Both put a woman's eye to a doubting question with Death among the answers. The Kural asks whether it is Death, an eye or a doe and finds all three; 103 lengthens the list to poison, fish, Kama's arrow and Yama's messengers, and turns the doubt into the poet's own confusion before he asks for Murugan's feet.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:115","b":"n:25","type":"same_image","note":"Both follow the corpse to the burning ground with drums while the kin wail. The Naladiyar's drum mocks the man who still believes in household joy; 115 adds the mourners washing and letting go of the bond, and asks Murugan to come before that day.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:111","b":"k:913","type":"agrees","note":"Both say the paid embrace is false. The Kural likens it to embracing a stranger's corpse in the dark; 111 shows it from inside, the price bargained, the tight embrace and the man melting like wax, and calls it a madness from which only release frees him.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:108","b":"n:378","type":"qualifies","note":"The Naladiyar says those set on the right path will not embrace such women; 108 says their laughter makes even renouncers weaken. If 108's துறவினர் சோர is taken literally, it doubts the Naladiyar's confidence; it may be only the poet's hyperbole.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:116","b":"p:101","type":"same_image","note":"Both speak for a girl sick with love for Murugan: the moon that burns like the sun, Kama's arrows and the blaming women torment her, and the speaker asks him to come to her. 101 asks for his garland, 116 for his embrace on the peacock that is Om.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:117","b":"p:15","type":"same_image","note":"Both confess begging from patrons by praising them in Tamil. In 15 the plea is to trade that service for service at Murugan's feet; in 117 the begging is tied to desire for women and to the ruined body, and the fear is another birth.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:104","b":"p:17","type":"same_image","note":"Both identify Murugan with the Tamil saint who sent the Jains to the stakes at Madurai; 104 adds the cure of the king's fever, holy ash and Pugali, the details of Sambandar's story.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:110","b":"p:9","type":"same_image","note":"Both run a life in one breath from infancy to youth and desire for women. 9 begins in the womb and ends in disease; 110 stops at sixteen and names the omission, never praising the devotees' feet.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:121","b":"n:376","type":"agrees","note":"Both turn on a courtesan's vow never to part: the Naladiyar woman who promised to be like the gem on its thread turns like a ram's horn, and 121's women who swear union until death strip the man and go to meet the next. The Naladiyar asks the heart to leave; 121 asks Murugan to come.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:126","b":"k:1085","type":"same_image","note":"The Kural cannot decide whether the girl's look is Death, an eye or a doe; 126 claims the eyes have beaten the deer into the forest and trampled Yama himself. The same comparisons, but the Kural's lover wonders and the poet of 126 fears to fall.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:129","b":"p:8","type":"agrees","note":"Both set Murugan against Death's buffalo and noose at the hour of death; 8 asks him to come on the peacock, 129 asks that Death not come near at all and adds the jackals and fire that claim the corpse.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:123","b":"p:8","type":"same_image","note":"Both are confessions of worship never done, built as a list of negatives (தொழுதிலன், சேவித்தறியேனே); 8 ends at the hour of death, 123 at the wish to end birth.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"p:132","b":"p:17","type":"same_image","note":"Both give Murugan the impaling of the Jains, the Madurai story told of Sambandar: 17 names the Tamil mouth that set them on stakes, 132 has him settle at Palani after seeing them there.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:133","b":"p:12","type":"develops","note":"12 praises Murugan for giving the knowledge to Siva; 133 asks whether the poet, as a servant, will receive the same teaching of the Veda's meaning.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:134","b":"p:15","type":"agrees","note":"Both confess begging from patrons: 15 praises them in Tamil with a wounded heart, 134 stands without shame at the gates of those who give food. 134 sets the begging at the end of a life that runs from the womb through women.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:135","b":"n:44","type":"agrees","note":"Both reject the poets' comparisons for a woman's eyes as carp, spear and the like: the Naladiyar ascetic answers them with the eye as a scooped-out palm fruit, 135 asks not to drown in their maya and turns to studying Murugan's poems.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:138","b":"t:1547","type":"agrees","note":"Tirumular says that if one knows how to reach the inner light there is no quarrel at all; 138 lists the clamouring schools and asks for the teaching and vision of knowledge that would end the dispute for him.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:138","b":"p:124","type":"agrees","note":"Both open with the sects that cannot agree and ask Murugan for knowledge and his feet instead; 124 adds the ash, 138 names the schools one by one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:141","b":"k:915","type":"same_image","note":"The Kural's பொதுநலத்தார், women whose favours are common to all, are 141's women who set a price so that all may take their pleasure; the Kural says the wise will not touch them, 141 confesses that he did.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:144","b":"k:919","type":"agrees","note":"The Kural calls the soft shoulders of women who draw no line the mire in which base men sink; 144 confesses to desiring those golden shoulders and sinking low (தாழ்வடைந்து).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:145","b":"n:46","type":"agrees","note":"Both list the body's contents, guts, fat, blood, bone and flesh. The Naladiyar asks where among them the garlanded woman is; 145 applies the list to the body the poet himself lives in, and answers with the Lord's grace.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"p:137","b":"p:132","type":"same_image","note":"Both give Murugan a deed of Sambandar: 132 the impaling of the Jains, 137 the milk of Parvati given to the child who then sang Tamil.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:152","b":"p:149","type":"agrees","note":"Both name Seypathi (Vayalur) as the place where Murugan acted on the poet: in 149 he stopped him and made him his servant, in 152 he gave him the love of singing Thiruppugazh daily. Read together they are the book's account of its own beginning.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:157","b":"n:24","type":"same_image","note":"Both hear the funeral drums and see the body covered and the fire taken up. The Naladiyar ends in irony, the dying carrying the dead; 157 asks for release before that hour comes, while the hut of the body still stands.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:158","b":"n:46","type":"agrees","note":"Both take the body apart into blood, bone, nerves, skin and flesh. The Naladiyar asks which part is the woman one desires; 158 turns the list on the poet's own body and confesses that, knowing this, he still desires women.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"p:160","b":"t:460","type":"agrees","note":"Both place the soul's bondage at conception: Tirumular has maya join the soul in the womb, turiya broken and karma take hold; 160 has the form grow in the womb from seed and breath, divided by the three gunas and covered in deceit, and contrasts it with the turiya state of the liberated.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:163","b":"k:1","type":"develops","note":"The Kural's first line makes A the first of letters as the Lord is first of the world; 163 borrows the words (அகர முதல்) and asks for the meaning that is first in that way, the secret that 173 says Murugan told Siva.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:164","b":"p:24","type":"same_image","note":"Both are love-laments in the same short metre over a lovesick girl, worn down by Kama's arrows, the sea or south wind, and evening; both name Murugan as the one who spoke a word to Siva. 24 is plainly the mother's voice; 164 asks Murugan for his garland for the girl.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:175","b":"p:15","type":"agrees","note":"Both confess the poet's trade of praising patrons for gifts. 15 regrets it with a wounded heart and asks for service instead; 175 adds the patrons' contempt and asks whether men who do not think of Murugan deserve to be sung at all.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:171","b":"p:149","type":"same_image","note":"Both credit Murugan with Sambandar's victory over the Jains, who are impaled, and with the holy ash: in 149 the ash is spread through the town, in 171 a pinch of it cures the Pandya king.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:176","b":"p:153","type":"develops","note":"153 alludes to the race round the world in one line; 176 tells the whole story, including Murugan's anger at losing the fruit, and makes it the reason he dwells at Palani.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:172","b":"k:912","type":"agrees","note":"The Kural warns of women who weigh the profit and then speak of their goodness; 172 describes the same pretence from inside, women who take every possession 'with great friendship'.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:178","b":"n:374","type":"agrees","note":"Both make a man's welcome depend on what he brings: the Naladiyar's courtesans find a moneyed oil-presser sugar, and 178's women question at the door those who come with property and carry it off.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:180","b":"k:911","type":"agrees","note":"Both say the courtesan's sweet talk serves her wish for wealth, not love. The Kural states it as a warning; 180 shows the sequence, talk, trust, then the wealth snatched, and asks for the Lord's love in its place.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:188","b":"t:857","type":"agrees","note":"Both raise the breath from the root through the channel between ida and pingala to the head and arrive at the Lord's feet. Tirumular states the method; 188 asks for it as a gift, and hears the goal as the anklet sound of the dancing foot.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:190","b":"p:27","type":"qualifies","note":"27 asks to see how to leave the embrace and gain the feet; 190 describes the same embrace and vows not to forget the feet even there. Read as a vow, 190 allows a remembrance inside desire that 27 seeks by leaving it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:194","b":"t:2709","type":"agrees","note":"Both set the Lord above the alchemist's work. Tirumular makes the five-letter mantra the alchemist's pill that turns the body to gold; 194 says mercury and the nine metals end as charcoal, while knowing Murugan as the gem brings everything.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:195","b":"p:181","type":"same_image","note":"Both tell the poet's life as one arc from the womb and the breast to the houses of women. 181 adds Brahma sending the soul out to eat its deeds; 195 adds the age of sixteen and the wealth lost.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:199","b":"t:2980","type":"agrees","note":"Both make weeping the sign of devotion: Tirumular melts in love and cries day and night, and 199 asks to wear the feet on his head and weep and weep for grace.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:200","b":"p:179","type":"same_image","note":"Both songs of Avinankudi call Murugan the giver of போதகம், the teaching: 179 opens by hailing him as such, and 200 ends with Siva bowing to receive it.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:211","b":"t:570","type":"tension","note":"Tirumular teaches breath held on the left so that the body does not decay; 211 asks to be rid of the anxious yoga that turns back the breath because it will not accept that the body must go, and seeks silence instead. The conflict holds if 211's participles describe the yoga it rejects, which is the likelier reading.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:207","b":"k:1020","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the string-puppet for a life with no inner agency: in the Kural the man without shame only seems alive, in 207 the poet dances on the strings of his two deeds.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:212","b":"t:1459","type":"same_image","note":"Tirumular says the knowers perceive Siva within the soul 'like a painting'; 212's obscure plea for the end 'in the painting' may ask for the same still perception.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"p:210","b":"p:220","type":"agrees","note":"Both complain of the poet's life at patrons' gates: 210 of the men who slip away once praised in song, 220 of genres wasted on fools who toss a coin; both turn to the Lord's feet instead.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:217","b":"n:47","type":"develops","note":"The Naladiyar gives one picture, a pot of filth leaking from nine openings under a dark skin; 217 takes the body apart organ by organ and disease by disease, and adds the hut of five elements taken up birth after birth.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:218","b":"k:65","type":"develops","note":"The Kural finds the body's joy in touching one's children; 218 asks for that very joy with the Lord as the child on the lap, and puns the child's kiss (முத்தி) into release.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:221","b":"p:24","type":"same_image","note":"Both are songs of a girl lovesick for Murugan, voiced by someone near her, with the evening moon and Kama's arrows; 221 adds the street's gossip.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:223","b":"t:57","type":"agrees","note":"Tirumular counts the Agamas twenty-eight, heard from Siva; 223 asks for the release that runs through the same twenty-eight, so the plea rests on the Siddhanta canon.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"p:227","b":"k:913","type":"agrees","note":"Both call the courtesan's embrace false (பொய்ம்மை முயக்கம், பொய்க்கலவி) because it is for wealth; the Kural likens it to a corpse in a dark room, 227 adds poison and wealth snatched.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:239","b":"k:1005","type":"agrees","note":"The Kural says one who neither gives nor enjoys has nothing however rich; 239 is the confession of such a man, who kept his wealth for his hands and youth and shared nothing with the hungry, and finds it at the funeral.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:239","b":"p:23","type":"agrees","note":"Both set sharing food against the funeral drum and the wailing kin: 23 resolves to share even a grain before the summons, 239 confesses that he never did.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:252","b":"k:913","type":"same_image","note":"Both put the courtesan's embrace for money in a dark room (இருட்டறை). The Kural likens it to embracing a stranger's corpse; 252 keeps the room and the seized wealth but not the corpse, and confesses the pull instead of warning.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:248","b":"n:46","type":"same_image","note":"Both take the body apart into bone, blood, fat and entrails to break desire. The Naladiyar asks which part is the garlanded woman; 248 turns the inventory on the poet's own body, a house he still longs to carry.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:245","b":"p:15","type":"agrees","note":"Both confess the poet's trade of praising patrons in Tamil for gifts; 245 adds the rebuff, 'come tomorrow' and the turned face, and asks, like 15, for the feet instead.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:243","b":"p:232","type":"same_image","note":"Both are catalogues of diseases set as the plea; 232 ties them to sin and women, while 243 asks to be spared them in every birth.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:249","b":"p:242","type":"same_image","note":"Both name the Thiruppugazh within itself as a means of grace: 242 says reading it cuts affliction, 249 asks to live among those who sing it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:247","b":"t:199","type":"develops","note":"Tirumular says the eaters of flesh are seized by Yama's messengers and thrown into hell; 247 counts the daily eating of killed flesh among the bonds and asks for release from them, without the threat.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"p:257","b":"t:1535","type":"agrees","note":"Both say the six sects lead back to birth: in Tirumular they perish where they stand, not knowing the way to leave the earth, and in 257 the sectarians who install many gods wander only to enter the womb again.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:258","b":"n:47","type":"develops","note":"Both call the body a pot to turn the mind from women. The Naladiyar's pot is the woman's body seeping filth; 258 turns the image on the poet's own body, a false pot of flesh, skin and semen, and asks not just to leave desire but to have that birth cut off.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:262","b":"k:1147","type":"same_image","note":"Both make the town's gossip feed love's sickness: the Kural's woman calls it the manure of her disease, and in 262 the women's talk is one of the torments, with cuckoo, moon and sea, that waste the girl's arm.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:265","b":"p:256","type":"same_image","note":"Both laments end by asking Murugan to give the wasting girl his garland; 265 calls it a garland of compassion, so the love token becomes grace.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:266","b":"p:33","type":"agrees","note":"The same formula of the courtesan's love that lasts as long as the money: in 33 the man is beaten from the door when it ends, in 266 he is left groaning there.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:263","b":"p:261","type":"agrees","note":"Both identify Murugan with Sambandar defeating the Jains: 261 tells the cure of the Pandyan and the crushing of the Jains, 263 the impaling.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:284","b":"k:10","type":"develops","note":"The Kural makes the Lord's feet the way across the great sea of births; 284 makes them a raft (புணை) and adds two more seas to cross, desire for women and the arts of learning.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:279","b":"t:318","type":"agrees","note":"Both say learning without knowledge of the Lord leaves one a fool; 279 confesses it in the first person, having read Vedas and Puranas and mocked the silent knowers as dumb.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:279","b":"t:95","type":"same_image","note":"Both measure the Lord by breadth and length and find no measure: Tirumular asks who knows them, and 279 names the reality whose breadth and length cannot be told, which the poet's learning scorned.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:280","b":"p:258","type":"same_image","note":"Both take the body apart as a false pot, a bellows and a home of diseases, and both ask not for health but for the end of the birth that brings such a body.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:277","b":"p:269","type":"agrees","note":"Both make the Thiruppugazh itself what saves: 269 gives it the power to end birth, and 277 asks that devotees live speaking it without forgetting.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:286","b":"p:24","type":"same_image","note":"Both are laments for a girl in love with Murugan, with the moon, the south wind and Kama's arrows as what wears her down; 24 is a mother's grief, 286 adds the town's women quarrelling in the street and asks for the god's garland.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:292","b":"p:15","type":"agrees","note":"Both confess begging praise of patrons in the language of gods and clouds; 292 goes further, quoting the poet comparing a poor patron to the six-faced Murugan himself, the god he should have praised.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:298","b":"t:144","type":"agrees","note":"Tirumular says the women and children who ate from the house do not follow the dead; 298 asks for that knowledge in advance, that wife, children, mother and goods become alien to the mind before the buffalo comes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:296","b":"n:11","type":"qualifies","note":"The Naladiyar's wise renounce in childhood, knowing grey hair will come; 296 is the prayer of one who did not, asking even when his hair is grey and his body ripens and falls to be taken into the supreme light.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:299","b":"k:920","type":"agrees","note":"The Kural makes women of divided heart the company of those whom Fortune has left; 299 lives the sequence, infatuation, all money given away, then hunger burning in the belly.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:301","b":"p:299","type":"same_image","note":"Both close by naming the poet's own work at Thanigai: 299 has Murugan delight in the Tamil garland of Arunagirinathan, and 301 has Thanigai as the place where those who chant Thiruppugazh gather.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:306","b":"p:292","type":"agrees","note":"Both renounce the poet's trade of praising patrons; 292 quotes the comparisons he made, and 306 names the misers and the bards' claim that they are Pari and Kari, and asks instead for one word from Murugan.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:306","b":"k:1067","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural begs those who beg not to beg of those who hide what they have; 306 confesses doing exactly that, praising misers as generous, and its answer is not to choose better patrons but to give patrons up for Murugan.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:306","b":"p:299","type":"same_image","note":"Both make Murugan the Tamil poet who sent the Jains to the stake, i.e. Sambandar; 299 adds the woman's husband brought back, 306 the Pandya's hunch and fever cured by the holy ash.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:305","b":"t:619","type":"qualifies","note":"Tirumular teaches that bindu and nada, raised in the body, join in samadhi; 305 uses the same terms but says the state cannot be reached even by jnanis and silent sages, and asks Murugan to come and give it.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:310","b":"p:15","type":"develops","note":"Both confess the poet's begging trade, praising patrons as wishing trees and clouds; 310 adds the name of the Sangam giver Kumanan and calls the flattery a blind theft of the words owed to the Lord.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:314","b":"t:1533","type":"agrees","note":"Tirumular says the Lord is not the thing the six sects hold; 314 makes him the truth hard for the six sects who quarrel daily, and yet the common ground (பொது) that holds all.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:314","b":"p:257","type":"agrees","note":"Both set the Lord who is one with everything yet apart against the confusion of the six sects; 257 mocks their images and wealth, 314 their angry argument, and asks to teach the truth in every direction.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:316","b":"n:27","type":"same_image","note":"Both see through the bubble: the Naladiyar's body is a bubble in the rain that forms and bursts, while 316 sees the whole world (ப்ரபஞ்சம்) as a bubble, and asks for the ripeness to reach the Lord.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:319","b":"n:13","type":"same_image","note":"Both picture the old man with failing speech or senses, leaning on a stick, staggering, teeth gone; the Naladiyar's still has eyes for desire, while 319 adds the disgust of his kin and wife and asks for the feet before that day.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:319","b":"p:68","type":"agrees","note":"Both picture the poet's old age with stick, white hair and loose teeth while others mock; 68 asks Murugan to come at the hour of death, 319 to reach the feet before that despised birth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:337","b":"p:101","type":"same_image","note":"Both speak for a girl sick with love for Murugan, wounded by Kama's flower arrows and a burning moon, and ask him to come with his garland; 337 adds that the moon rose with the poison of the sea.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:334","b":"p:24","type":"same_image","note":"Both are laments for a lovesick girl in another's voice, with the classical torments of the evening world: 24 the anril bird, south wind and crescent, 334 the roaring sea and the crescent seized by the serpent.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:329","b":"k:350","type":"agrees","note":"The Kural bids one cling to the clinging of the one without clinging in order to let clinging go; 329 asks for the clinging that does not suffer, set against the clinging to food and wealth, if its line is divided so.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:324","b":"p:68","type":"same_image","note":"Both have the heirs at the deathbed thinking of property: in 68 the young sons ask about debts and goods, in 324 the kin cry 'a little for me, a little for you' over the wealth and gold plate.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:331","b":"p:310","type":"agrees","note":"Both confess worshipping patrons for gold; 310 tells the flattery at length, 331 sets the patrons' feet (தருவோர் தாள்) against the Lord's in a few beats.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:341","b":"n:13","type":"same_image","note":"Both picture old age by teeth lost and a body bent on a stick. The Naladiyar uses it against the old man who still desires; 341 carries the picture on to the corpse burned and the kin going home.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:344","b":"p:24","type":"same_image","note":"Both are laments in another voice for a girl lovesick for Murugan, worn down by the night's sights and sounds: in 24 the crescent, the south wind and the anril bird, in 344 the stained moon and the roaring sea.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:345","b":"k:911","type":"develops","note":"The Kural warns that women who desire a man for his wealth bring him harm; 345 tells the harm from inside, the wealth given away and a poverty that burns like the undersea fire.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:355","b":"t:722","type":"tension","note":"Tirumular, by Nandi's command, promises that breath tied fast keeps the body for thousands of years; 355 calls the yogi who holds the breath to make the impermanent flesh last a fool. The conflict holds if 722 is read as bodily longevity sought for its own sake and 355 as rejecting that practice, not only its misuse.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:357","b":"k:911","type":"agrees","note":"Both say the courtesan desires the wealth and not the man; 357 sharpens it into a measure, the desire acted exactly as far as the gold goes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:358","b":"p:352","type":"agrees","note":"Both confess the poet's trade of praising unworthy patrons for gold: 352 tells it in summary, 358 quotes the begging speech itself, the boast of prosody and the call for jewels.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:362","b":"n:46","type":"same_image","note":"Both take the body apart into entrails, fat, blood, bone, sinew and skin. The Naladiyar asks which part is the desired woman; 362 makes the inventory the hut in which the poet's own senses run wild.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:359","b":"p:347","type":"agrees","note":"Both speak of the Absolute by what cannot reach it: 347 by learning, the mind, the tattvas and the Vedas, 359 by chariyai, kiriyai and the turiya state.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:369","b":"t:1503","type":"agrees","note":"Both see the sects as people wandering off, each crying its own 'this is supreme'. Tirumular's remedy is to go the ordained way and seek the King; 369's is to melt in devotion and drop 'mine', when the reality the Vedas proclaim is easily gained.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:370","b":"k:919","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the Kural's word for courtesans (வரைவிலா, வரைவில்) and picture their pleasure as something a man sinks into: the Kural's mire (அளறு), 370's sea of pleasure, which 370 sets against a second sea, the bliss of Siva.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:373","b":"k:912","type":"same_image","note":"Both make the courtesan a woman who weighs: in the Kural she weighs the profit and speaks of her virtue to match, in 373 she measures her heart to the money (திரவியம் அளவளவு அளவியர்).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:373","b":"p:371","type":"same_image","note":"Both end the plea with the same vow, not to forget the word and the two feet given at Arunai; 371 confesses every pleasure first, 373 every deceit of the women, and both keep the vow inside the confession.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:374","b":"k:1083","type":"develops","note":"The Kural's lover discovers that Death has a woman's eyes, half in wonder; 374 extends the discovery to every charm, breasts as Yama and the pearls on them as Yama's rope, and turns it into a reason to turn away.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:378","b":"p:376","type":"same_image","note":"Both open the hour of death with the same formula, Yama casting his thick-handed noose (பரியகைப் பாசம் விட்டு எறியும்); 376 asks for the life to be saved, 378 for the fault to be forgiven.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:380","b":"k:1228","type":"same_image","note":"Both count the flute among the torments of the lovesick woman: the Kural's herdsman's flute is a weapon that kills, and 380's sweet lone flute is one of four things she asks to survive.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:384","b":"p:21","type":"same_image","note":"Both quote the courtesan's invitation, 'come to my house' (21's இங்கு நின்றது என் வீடே, 384's அருகு வீடு இதுதான் அதில் வாரும்); 384 adds that it is spoken to men of wealth and sworn on oaths.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:386","b":"k:1084","type":"same_image","note":"Both give a woman's eyes the power to eat the life of those who look: the Kural's lover says it of one girl with wonder, 386 of women's eyes in general, likened to Siva's throat-poison and Murugan's vel.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:387","b":"p:386","type":"develops","note":"Both liken women's eyes to poison and end with Murugan hiding his own form to follow the hunter girl; 387 takes the simile further, to the poison of the churned sea that cracked the cosmic egg.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:388","b":"p:376","type":"same_image","note":"Both stage the death-bed with wife and sons weeping before Yama comes; 376 asks for the life to be saved, 388 for knowledge to clear his troubles so that he can think of the feet.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:394","b":"p:369","type":"agrees","note":"Both set the uproar of the sects (கோலாகலச் சமயம், கலக விபரித பர சமயிகள்) against a knowledge they cannot reach: 369's reality without birth or death, 394's seal of silence.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:397","b":"k:1147","type":"same_image","note":"Both make the town's talk feed the girl's love-sickness: in the Kural gossip is the manure and the mother's words the water, in 397 the town reproaches her alongside the north wind.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:398","b":"n:371","type":"agrees","note":"Both make the courtesan's love last exactly as long as the money: the Naladiyar's lamp goes out with its oil, and 398's women of magic arts cling only as far as the wealth goes (பொருளது அளவும் மருவுறு).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:402","b":"n:47","type":"same_image","note":"Both call the body a pot (கும்பம்) of filth: the Naladiyar's pot seeps through nine openings under its dark skin, 402's is a pot where stench and liquor pool. The Naladiyar turns the image on the desired woman; 402 turns it on the poet's own body.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:403","b":"p:420","type":"agrees","note":"Both describe the embrace in full and then refuse to forget the feet within it: 403 says 'even then I will never forget', 420 asks 'even in union, will I forget your golden feet?'. Unlike most of the book, they ask for remembrance, not escape.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:404","b":"p:24","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the evening torments of love, the moon and Kama's arrows, for a girl in love with Murugan. In 24 her mother speaks; in 404 the girl herself asks him to come before she dies.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:405","b":"t:1949","type":"agrees","note":"Both describe the same yoga: kindling the fire and raising it until the nectar of the moon is drunk. Tirumular calls those who do it Siva-yogis; 405 confesses that the poet has not done it and wanders after women instead.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:409","b":"p:2","type":"same_image","note":"Both list the foods Ganapati takes, gram or dal, sesame, plantains and other fruits; in 2 they lead into the poet's call to sing, in 409 into pure praise.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:412","b":"n:13","type":"develops","note":"Both show the old man with failing speech and a staff. The Naladiyar's still keeps his eyes on desire; 412 follows the man spent on courtesans to the end, deaf and on a mat, while his own wife, children and kin call him a curse.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"p:413","b":"p:101","type":"same_image","note":"Both speak for a girl sick with love for Murugan, tormented by Kama's five flower arrows, and ask him to come with his garland; 101 is spoken for her, 413 by her, and adds that one night feels like an age.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:423","b":"p:9","type":"same_image","note":"Both run a life from the embryo in the womb through birth, nursing and crawling to desire for women. 9 ends in disease; 423 counts learning among the stages, calling it empty knowledge.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:425","b":"t:923","type":"agrees","note":"Tirumular lists the rotations of the five-letter mantra in order from SI: சிவயநம, மசிவயந, நமசிவய, யநமசிவ, வயநமசி. 425 chants the same rotations in the same order, each joined to the name of Arunachala, making a teaching of the mantra into a hymn to the hill.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:426","b":"p:104","type":"same_image","note":"Both make Murugan the Tamil saint who sent the Jains of Madurai to the stakes. 104 adds Pugali and the cure of the king's fever; 426 adds the milk the mountain's daughter gave, the other mark of Sambandar's story.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:422","b":"n:48","type":"same_image","note":"Both show vultures tearing at the body. The Naladiyar's stinking cart is shown to men who praise sandal and garlands; 422 sees the poet's own body as the feast of dogs, jackals, vultures and worms, and turns from it to the company of devotees.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:428","b":"k:280","type":"agrees","note":"The Kural says neither the shaved head nor long matted hair is needed if one gives up what the world condemns; 428 confesses to both marks, the shaved head and the grown locks, with the condemned conduct, the hunt for gold, kept underneath.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:428","b":"t:1660","type":"agrees","note":"Tirumular says the false garb is worn for the sake of eating; in 428 the poet confesses to wearing it to make gold, and asks for pity rather than claiming the garb.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:441","b":"p:243","type":"develops","note":"Both list the same diseases (scrofula, the great belly, phlegm, colic, urinary flux). 243 asks that they not afflict him through his births; 441 gives up on the body and asks instead for Siva-knowledge and the power to sing the Thiruppugazh.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"p:444","b":"t:485","type":"develops","note":"Tirumular says the form grows ten months, comes into the world in its season and grows mingled with maya, and asks who knows how. 444 follows that same growth month by month to birth in the tenth, and then on past it to the pyre.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:444","b":"n:25","type":"same_image","note":"Both hear the funeral drum while kin wail over the corpse; the Naladiyar's drum mocks the living, while 444 notes that the kin weep each for their own share.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:446","b":"n:48","type":"same_image","note":"Both end the cherished body as carrion: the Naladiyar's vultures tear the stinking cart once its axle breaks, and 446 lays the householder in a pit for vultures and dogs. The Naladiyar's target is the praise of perfumes and garlands; 446's is family life.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:458","b":"n:377","type":"agrees","note":"Both give the courtesan's course in one sequence: the Naladiyar's woman licks like a wild cow, takes the wealth from a man's hands and then balks like an ox; 458's coaxes, takes gold until the wealth is gone, then drives him off with deceitful words.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:461","b":"p:456","type":"agrees","note":"Both carry the courtesan song past the bed to the diseases it leaves, sores with pus and festering; 456 follows them into Death's city, 461 to the onlookers who cover their eyes.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:469","b":"n:27","type":"same_image","note":"Both see the body as a bubble that forms and bursts; the Naladiyar praises the firm who resolve from this to end their wandering, and 469 asks from it for the one release complete in wisdom, silence and bliss.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:467","b":"k:1","type":"develops","note":"The Kural makes the letter A first of letters as the Lord is first of the world; 467 takes the letter itself as a name of the Goddess, calling Sivakami the அகரம் and the life of all.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"p:468","b":"p:467","type":"agrees","note":"Both ask not to leave the bed but to hold the Lord there: 468 vows to keep the twelve eyes and twelve arms even while sporting with women, and 467 asks for the moon faces and feet in the same embrace.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:477","b":"n:370","type":"agrees","note":"Both make poverty the end of the courtesan's love: the Naladiyar's flood ends when the income ends, and 477's women first show delusion and then show poverty.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:487","b":"p:196","type":"same_image","note":"Both join the catalogue of the body's diseases to the ninety-six tattvas living off the body; 487 adds the house-building and desire for wealth that keep the poet from the space where the peacock dances.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:487","b":"t:154","type":"same_image","note":"Tirumular counts the ninety-six as tenants of a walled temple who run off when it falls; 487 makes the same ninety-six the eaters of the body, a picture of consumption rather than desertion.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:485","b":"p:146","type":"same_image","note":"Both turn the body into a dwelling whose real occupants are diseases, listed at length; 485 names the fault as taking that dwelling for permanent.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:490","b":"t:1671","type":"develops","note":"Tirumular's Siva-jnanis go about as dead while their senses and body remain; 490 asks for exactly this, the goal won by dying in the mind before the body is carried to the burning ground, but offers the Lord the alternative of living on in understanding.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:493","b":"n:26","type":"qualifies","note":"The Naladiyar says it matters nothing whether the body is dragged, buried or thrown away once its dweller has left; 493 lists the same receivers of the corpse, vultures, jackals, fire and earth, but as weary claimants, so that the repetition across countless births is itself the burden.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"p:488","b":"p:24","type":"same_image","note":"Both are laments for a girl sick with love for Murugan, told by another voice, with the wind, the birds and the signs of evening wearing her down; 24 is the mother's, and 488 asks Murugan for his garland.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:481","b":"n:374","type":"agrees","note":"Both make the courtesan's only question whose money there is: the Naladiyar's women find even an oil-presser sweet if he has wealth, and 481's women, with hearts dry as cane waste, think only whose wealth it will be.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:497","b":"k:280","type":"agrees","note":"The Kural says neither shaving nor matted hair is needed if one gives up what the world condemns; 497 asks to be spared ochre, matted hair, forest wandering and fasting and to be given instead the inner knowledge in which the self subsides. Both set the ascetic's outward marks aside, the Kural for conduct, 497 for grace and knowledge.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:508","b":"p:9","type":"same_image","note":"Both run a life from the womb through infancy and youth to women and then decline without pause; 9 ends in disease, 508 in Death's arrival, and both ask for release from the cycle.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:510","b":"n:48","type":"same_image","note":"Both picture the body as food for scavengers: Naladiyar 48 has vultures tearing the stinking cart once its axle breaks, 510 the ghoul, the dog and the fly. The Naladiyar uses it to shame those who praise the body; 510 asks to be taken out of it into release.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:512","b":"n:24","type":"same_image","note":"Both follow the corpse to the burning ground with drums (பறை), and both make the funeral a lesson: Naladiyar 24 notes that the dying carry the dead; 512 sets the funeral at the end of a life spent on courtesans.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"p:513","b":"n:55","type":"same_image","note":"Both speakers address their own mind (நெஞ்சே, மனமே) and urge it to set out with them on the good path. Naladiyar 55 gives the reason as youth wasted and old age coming; 513 gives only the Lord, who cuts the root of impurity.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:520","b":"p:508","type":"same_image","note":"Both begin the life with a drop like dew (பனிபோல், பனியின் விந்துளி) and follow it head down out of the womb to the crawling child; 508 goes on to women and Death, 520 to past deeds and a ruined mind.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:524","b":"n:11","type":"agrees","note":"Both look ahead to grey hair and the staff and draw the lesson that one should act before they come: the Naladiyar praises those who renounced in childhood, 524 asks to serve the Lord's servants before the body ripens.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:527","b":"p:528","type":"same_image","note":"The two songs end with the same two lines, Murugan dwelling on the Venkatam hill and giving his devotees whatever they ask when they ask it; a formula of this shrine.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:534","b":"n:42","type":"same_image","note":"Both see the body as a bag full of holes (Naladiyar தொளைபலவாய், 534 தொள்ளைப் புலாற்பை). The Naladiyar tells the lover to look at the desired body as a bag turned inside out; 534 turns the image on the poet's own body and asks to be rid of it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:534","b":"p:280","type":"same_image","note":"Both take the poet's body apart as a list of containers, pot, bellows and bag in 280, a dozen kinds of bag in 534, and both ask for the end of that body rather than its health.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:536","b":"p:534","type":"same_image","note":"Both songs call the body கள்வைத்த தோற்பை, a skin bag holding toddy, a formula the poet reuses. In 534 it is one of a dozen bags; 536 sets it against the formless form seen by the inner eye.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:535","b":"p:124","type":"agrees","note":"Both set the sect-disputers' learning aside: 124 opens with sectarians who cannot see one another's truth, 535 asks not to be tossed in the wrangling of the six sects' logicians. 535's reading rests on one clear line in a dense song.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:537","b":"p:101","type":"same_image","note":"Both speak for a girl sick with love for Murugan, tormented by the moon and the breeze, and ask him for his garland as the cure; 537 adds a plea that he not blame her old karma.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:541","b":"n:13","type":"agrees","note":"Both picture a man who clings to house and desire into a failing old age. The Naladiyar's old man, toothless on his staff, still has his eyes on desire and so has no path to safety; 541's clings to children and gold until he is grey and breathless, and asks for grace before Yama's rope catches him.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:546","b":"p:103","type":"same_image","note":"Both open with a riddle of what a woman's eye is, a string of 'or's of weapons, poison and fish. In 103 Yama's messengers are one of the answers; 546 keeps them out of the list and sets them, with club and noose, at the end of the plea.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:550","b":"n:373","type":"agrees","note":"Both make the courtesan's welcome depend on the gift in hand. The Naladiyar's women would send away even Vishnu with folded hands if he brought nothing; 550's will not speak to a man on the day he brings no gold.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:553","b":"n:375","type":"same_image","note":"Both picture the courtesan's double dealing as a body turned to more than one man at once: the Naladiyar's eel shows its head to the snake and its tail to the fish; 553 gives the eyes to one man, the breasts to another, the hands to a third.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"p:558","b":"t:198","type":"agrees","note":"Both show Yama's servants binding the soul with ropes and driving it into fire. Tirumular gives the punishment to those who urged killing; 558 names no sin, and lists the tortures at length only to ask Murugan to end them.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"p:561","b":"t:2380","type":"agrees","note":"Both describe the Lord as beyond speech and conduct: Tirumular's great one is beyond speech, word and feeling (பேசா உரையுணர்வற்ற); 561's Reality cannot be read, worshipped or spoken (வாய்விட்டுப் பேசொணாதது).","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:563","b":"p:108","type":"same_image","note":"Both measure women's charms by their effect on ascetics: in 108 their laughter makes renouncers weaken (துறவினர் சோர), in 563 their breasts make the ascetics of the great principle faint (தத்துவத்துத் தவர் சோர), the same verb.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:564","b":"p:558","type":"develops","note":"Both list the tortures Yama's messengers inflict, binding, dragging, burning. 558 lists them without a reason; 564 gives them one, an account of the poet's deeds opened and totalled, with the torture matched to the sum.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:574","b":"k:919","type":"same_image","note":"Both call the courtesan's body a mire (அளறு) into which men sink; the Kural's word also means hell, and 574 asks to be taken by the Lord so as not to sink there.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:574","b":"n:47","type":"same_image","note":"Both counter desire by naming the body's openings and their seepage: the Naladiyar's pot oozing filth from nine holes, 574's rotting, cloth-stopped hole. The Naladiyar calls the lover a fool; 574 asks for rescue.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:578","b":"k:1085","type":"same_image","note":"Both make the woman's eye Death: the Kural's lover cannot tell if her look is Death, an eye or a doe, while 578 calls the eye-corners Yama's messengers in a confession it was saved from.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:573","b":"t:33","type":"agrees","note":"Both say rites and recitation do not yield the truth: Tirumular's men perform many rites and recite hymns yet wither without understanding; 573 doubts that japa, fire-offerings and Agamic learning passed down by gurus can be a refuge without Siva's own gift.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:575","b":"t:122","type":"develops","note":"Both define Sivayoga. Tirumular makes it knowing the conscient and non-conscient and becoming one's own light; 575 makes it seeing every element, scripture, letter and god abide as one form, and adds that the malas must go first.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:582","b":"p:27","type":"qualifies","note":"27 asks to see clearly how to leave the embrace and reach the feet; 582 vows to remember the feet even asleep on the breasts after union, so devotion is placed inside desire rather than after it, if the vow is not read as hyperbole.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:589","b":"p:579","type":"agrees","note":"Both confess the court poet's begging: 579 praised the man who never gives as Kubera, 589 sang correct Tamil verse to iron-hearted men who show no pity. Both make this, not only desire, a reason he did not seek the Lord.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:589","b":"k:1066","type":"agrees","note":"The Kural says nothing degrades the tongue more than begging, even water for a cow; 589 shows that degradation in the poet himself, trembling and cringing as he sings for gifts, and asks whether such a tongue can gain love for the Lord.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:602","b":"p:2","type":"agrees","note":"Both thank Murugan for the grace of being told to sing the Thiruppugazh (செப்பென); 2 lists what the song must hold, and 602 already hears it recited by devotees in all four directions.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:603","b":"k:331","type":"agrees","note":"The Kural calls it the lowest folly to take what does not last for what lasts; 603 confesses exactly that folly, living as if a bubble-like life with wife, sons and house would stand for a crore of aeons.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:603","b":"n:27","type":"same_image","note":"Both see life as a bubble (புற்புதம்). The Naladiyar praises those who see it so and resolve to end their wandering; 603 confesses that he knows the image and still lives as if it lasted.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:605","b":"p:8","type":"develops","note":"8 asks Murugan to come on the peacock when Death's men strike and end his fear; 605 turns the same hour into a battle the servant wins with Murugan's grace, and asks him to come and watch.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:609","b":"k:920","type":"agrees","note":"The Kural names double-hearted women, liquor and dice as the company of those whom fortune has left; 609 opens its list with gamblers and money-loving women side by side and adds disease.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:613","b":"p:15","type":"develops","note":"15 confesses that the poet himself begged from patrons, praising them as clouds in giving; 613 looks at the same trade from outside, at poets who praise men as the cloud-dark Vishnu and steal verses, and disowns their way.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:626","b":"n:376","type":"agrees","note":"Both give the courtesan's vow of oneness and show it false: the Naladiyar's woman swore never to part, like gem and thread, and turned away; 626's women swear 'you are me and I am you' without shame.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:625","b":"p:8","type":"develops","note":"8 asks Murugan to come when Death's servants strike; 625 shows those same servants trembling at his vel, so the fear of the plea is answered in the praise.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:628","b":"p:14","type":"same_image","note":"Both are love-sickness told with the moon, Kama's arrows and the cuckoo; 14 names the speaker as a deceiver longing for women, while 628 leaves its speaker unnamed and ends on Valli at Murugan's chest.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:631","b":"p:610","type":"same_image","note":"Both picture the poet laughed at by his own: in 610 wife, town and kin mock him, in 631 mother, kin and clan laugh as disease wastes his body; both answer by asking to be drawn to the Lord.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:633","b":"n:377","type":"agrees","note":"Both describe women who take the wealth from a man's hand once he is caught; the Naladiyar adds that he is laughed at, 633 that his very strength of mind is destroyed.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:632","b":"p:15","type":"agrees","note":"Both confess the humiliation of pleading before patrons; 15 tells the flattery, 632 says he can no longer bear to plead his poverty to base and cruel men after women took his wealth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:634","b":"k:10","type":"agrees","note":"The Kural says only those who join the Lord's feet swim the great sea of births; 634 asks in the same image whether he will swim the long wave of birth and reach the pure feet.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:645","b":"p:337","type":"same_image","note":"Both speak for a girl sick with love for Murugan, wounded by Kama's flowers and burned by the moon, and plead with him for her: 337 asks for his garland, 645 that he come and give her life, adding the tears she weeps as pearls through the night.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:647","b":"k:1228","type":"same_image","note":"The Kural's woman hears the cowherd's flute as the herald of a burning evening and a weapon that kills; in 647 the cowherds' bamboo flute is one of the evening torments, with the sea and the moon, of a girl sick for Murugan.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:643","b":"t:1513","type":"agrees","note":"Both place sayujya past the ordinary states of consciousness: Tirumular calls it reaching the state beyond waking and becoming Siva; 643 sets it above turiya and beyond every path and sect.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:652","b":"p:520","type":"same_image","note":"Both trace the embryo growing through the sizes of a seed, palm fruit and coconut and slipping head down into the world, a formula the book repeats; 520 goes on to virtue forgotten and a stinking body, 652 to the women who ruin the grown child.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:652","b":"t:485","type":"agrees","note":"Both make the ten months in the womb the start of bondage: Tirumular's form grows mingled with maya, and 652's child, grown by the way of deeds, ends among women.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:662","b":"k:223","type":"agrees","note":"Both define the giver by his shame at the word 'none': the Kural's man of good family does not utter the misery of 'I have nothing' and gives without refusing, and 662 names those ashamed to say 'there is none', who do not refuse from what they have, using the same word for misery (எவ்வம்).","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:664","b":"k:1131","type":"same_image","note":"The Kural calls the palmyra horse the only defence of those who suffer in love; 664 has Murugan himself, infatuated in Valli's field, mount it, the god taking the lover's last resort.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:665","b":"n:46","type":"same_image","note":"Both take the body apart into its stuff, skin, flesh, blood and bone: the Naladiyar to ask which part is the desired woman, 665 to show the hut the life lives in before it asks for the strength to praise.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:666","b":"n:373","type":"agrees","note":"Both make the courtesan's welcome depend on the gift: the Naladiyar's women send away even Mal if his hand is empty, and 666's women show delight to the man who gives much and drive off one who does not.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:666","b":"p:653","type":"develops","note":"653 gives Sambandar's defeat of the Jains as one epithet of Murugan; 666 tells the whole Madurai episode, the ash on the king, the hump straightened, the leaf against the current, as Murugan's deed.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"p:671","b":"t:252","type":"agrees","note":"Both make a single handful of food for the hungry the measure of dharma; 671 confesses the poet did not give even that one handful (ஒருபிடி) while he spent himself on courtesans, where Tirumular sets it as what anyone can do.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:677","b":"p:358","type":"agrees","note":"Both confess the poet's trade of singing to patrons who do not deserve it; 358 quotes the begging speech, 677 asks only whether he must go on giving endless songs to sinners blind to the Lord's protection.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:679","b":"n:48","type":"same_image","note":"Both set vultures on the body to answer its admirers: the Naladiyar shows them tearing at the stinking cart once its axle breaks, 679 calls the living body the hut they will feed on and makes it the reason to stop carrying it from town to town.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:683","b":"k:911","type":"agrees","note":"Both warn that the courtesan's sweet words serve her wish for wealth; 683 shows the words in action, the talk of old intimacy leading to the house where the wealth is plundered.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:693","b":"p:24","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Both are akam songs in another voice built from the classical torments of the lovesick girl, the moon and Kama's arrows; 24 is the mother's lament for her daughter, 693 is the girl's own plea that the god come to her bed.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"p:700","b":"n:25","type":"agrees","note":"Both set marriage and the household inside the march to the burning ground: the Naladiyar mocks the man who sees the corpse carried yet marries and thinks there is joy here, and 700 lists embracing women and bearing the household's burden between crawling and burning.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:702","b":"p:33","type":"agrees","note":"Both tell the courtesan's cycle to its end, the man welcomed while his wealth lasts and then beaten and driven out; 702 adds the bargaining at the start and the new man taken up afterwards.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:703","b":"p:704","type":"same_image","note":"Twin songs in one metre: both walk a life from the womb through childhood, women and the hunt for wealth, and both end in the same plea for love that reaches the feet before one falls into hell. 704 dwells longer on infancy (the birth rite, crawling), 703 on youth and roaming.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:707","b":"k:166","type":"agrees","note":"Both condemn the man who begrudges another's giving: the Kural says his kin will be left without food or clothing, and 707 puts him among those who fall into Yama's pit.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:707","b":"k:110","type":"agrees","note":"The Kural says there is no escape for one who kills a good done to him; 707 lists those who forget the good done them among the brutes who fall into Yama's pit, giving that 'no escape' a place.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:709","b":"t:567","type":"agrees","note":"Both make the stilled and turned breath the end of dying: Tirumular says that when breath stays with the mind there is no birth or death, and 709 asks that, with the breath turned into the skull and samadhi gained, the endless dying cease.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:718","b":"k:10","type":"same_image","note":"Both make the Lord's feet the way across the sea of births: the Kural says only those who join his feet swim it, and 718 calls the twin lotus feet a raft (புணை) to be grasped instead of the leaking body.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:723","b":"k:911","type":"agrees","note":"Both see the courtesan's sweetness as aimed at a man's money: the Kural warns that such women's sweet words bring harm, and 723 shows the flattery on the bed with the hand already reaching for the purse.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:730","b":"k:915","type":"agrees","note":"Both use the courtesan's being common to all as the charge: the Kural's women 'whose goodness belongs to everyone' (பொதுநலத்தார்) and 730's பொதுவிகள். The Kural says the wise don't plunge in; 730 is the confession of one who did.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:731","b":"k:912","type":"agrees","note":"The Kural's women weigh the profit and then speak of virtue; 731's women have minds set on the coin and lying speech. Both make the calculation the mark of the courtesan.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:731","b":"t:2957","type":"agrees","note":"Tirumular says that for those who hold Nandi in love there is no caste; 731 asks that the lowness of being sunk in caste and clan end when he reaches the feet. Both place caste among what falls away at release.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:734","b":"t:1611","type":"agrees","note":"Both make silence the door of release: Tirumular says release comes to those to whom silence has come and all speech ends, and 734 asks for the house of silence (மோன வீடு) beyond the mind, where nothing else is spoken.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:736","b":"t:1737","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Tirumular gives form and formlessness and the thirty-six tattvas to Shakti and Siva; 736 gives the same pairs, form and formless, difference and non-difference, bindu and nada, to Murugan and ends 'the one is you'.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"p:738","b":"p:722","type":"agrees","note":"Both ask to be set among the Lord's devotees as the way out of the round of births: 722 asks to be counted in the throng of servants, 738 to have those who think of Murugan as companions so as not to drown in the sea of seven births.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:742","b":"k:380","type":"qualifies","note":"The Kural says nothing is stronger than fate, which arrives before any scheme to avert it; 742 cites the same elders' word and grants it (தகவாம் அது), yet asks for the Lord's grace to free him from what fate brings. Not a tension, since the poet does not deny the saying: grace is set beside fate as the one thing no human scheme is.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:743","b":"p:739","type":"same_image","note":"Both are love-sickness songs with the stock torments of moon, Kama's arrows and night or evening, sung to Murugan in a lover's voice rather than as the poet's confession.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:744","b":"p:762","type":"develops","note":"Both turn the plea into a yogic programme, breath, the sun's path, the sky and the dance in 744; muladhara, the three mandalas, bindu and nada and the central pillar in 762; and both praise Murugan as the saint who sent the Jains to the stake.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"p:745","b":"n:47","type":"same_image","note":"Both see the body as a vessel of filth with nine openings; the Naladiyar uses it to mock the man who praises a woman's arms, 745 to confess the poet's own frame and ask, before thought fails, for teaching.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:748","b":"p:15","type":"agrees","note":"Both confess the poet's trade of praising patrons in verse for gifts, likening men to gods (Vishnu of the milk sea here, clouds in giving in 15), and ask to be turned from it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:749","b":"p:15","type":"develops","note":"15 confesses praising patrons as Tamil's refuge; 749 lists the patrons, fools, misers, gamblers, and the result, the poet ruined and lying in the fire of poverty.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:749","b":"k:1066","type":"agrees","note":"The Kural says nothing is more shameful to the tongue than begging; 749 confesses a tongue spent praising unworthy men and a life left in poverty.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:752","b":"n:13","type":"agrees","note":"Both picture old age by faltering steps, the staff and fallen teeth; the Naladiyar faults the old man still clinging to desire, 752 asks to be freed from the birth that brings such an end.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"p:750","b":"n:374","type":"agrees","note":"Both tie the courtesan's favour to money: the Naladiyar says a rich oil-presser is sugar to her, 750 that she strips of wealth the men she kisses.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:756","b":"t:176","type":"agrees","note":"Both set the Lord against Death's servants at the hour of death; Tirumular says they will not harm those who think of the Lord, 756 confesses a life without that love and asks Murugan to keep Kalan away.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:757","b":"n:371","type":"agrees","note":"The Naladiyar says the harlot's love ends when the hand is empty, like a lamp without oil; 757 shows the moment, the woman leaving the bed because the cash and cloth are not enough.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:761","b":"k:920","type":"agrees","note":"The Kural names double-minded women with liquor and gambling as the company of those whom fortune has left; 761 calls the women குடிகேடிகள், ruiners of families.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:762","b":"t:612","type":"same_image","note":"Both picture the yogi running up the central staff to the head through the three mandalas; Tirumular promises the body will last an aeon, 762 hopes for the light of a hundred crore moons.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:763","b":"n:373","type":"agrees","note":"The Naladiyar says courtesans would bow to Mal himself yet send away a man with nothing in his hand; 763 shows them handing the ruined man a begging bowl and staff.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:774","b":"n:21","type":"agrees","note":"The Naladiyar's kings who rode elephants under moon-white umbrellas were carried off and spoken of as dead; 774 gives the same royal splendour, walls, singers and chowries, and asks only whether it ends as a handful of ash.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:772","b":"p:24","type":"same_image","note":"Both are laments in another's voice for a girl sick with love for Murugan, worn down by the moon, the south wind and the evening; 772 is a bare eight lines, 24 adds the mother's question about bearing daughters.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:783","b":"t:1958","type":"agrees","note":"Tirumular's bindu in the root centre is raised by fire past the throat and becomes cool nectar at the moon; 783 asks Murugan for the same path, root fire through the six supports to the moon's honeyed nectar, as a gift of grace rather than a discipline.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:786","b":"t:345","type":"develops","note":"Tirumular reads the Kadavur story as yoga: the fire at the root turned upward with the breath burns Death. 786, sung at Kadavur, asks for exactly this ascent, breath held, darkness burnt and the space above entered, with the kicking of Death in its praise.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:785","b":"n:48","type":"same_image","note":"The Naladiyar's vultures tear at the stinking cart once its axle breaks; 785 makes the fallen body a packed meal that vultures and crows greet as their food, the end that adorning it as if it were Kama ignores.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:797","b":"n:371","type":"agrees","note":"The Naladiyar's harlots' love ends when the hand is empty, as the lamp's light ends with the oil; 797 shows the same end acted out, the man twisted round, kicked and abused once his wealth is gone.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:798","b":"p:488","type":"same_image","note":"Both are pleas in another's voice for a lovesick girl, worn by the flower bed and by women's blame, asking Murugan for his garland; 798 adds the neighbours' slander and asks for the kadamba garland by name.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:798","b":"p:797","type":"same_image","note":"The fragrant flower bed (மருக்குலாவிய மலரணை) is in 797 the bed where Murugan embraces Valli and in 798 the bed that burns the girl who longs for him; the two songs share the kura tree and the vel in their closing lines.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:812","b":"n:3","type":"agrees","note":"Both use kings to show impermanence: the Naladiyar's commanders on elephants under the royal umbrella fall when their deeds turn, and 812's lords of elephant armies and conquered directions end as a handful of ash; 812 adds that they never thought to worship.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:815","b":"n:47","type":"same_image","note":"Both name the body's nine openings and the filth inside a skin covering: the Naladiyar's fool praises a pot that seeps from nine holes, and 815 calls the body a hut with nine gates, its filth sloshing under the skin.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:815","b":"t:724","type":"tension","note":"Tirumular says that if the body perishes the life perishes before knowledge is won, so he learned the means of nurturing the body; 815 calls the same effort, many medicines and yogas tried on a body that bursts quicker than a bubble, an anguish to be given up for the Lord's feet. The tension holds if 815's yogas are read as practices for preserving the body, as the context of அவாவி suggests.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:821","b":"k:541","type":"agrees","note":"The Kural defines justice as investigating and showing favour to no one; the praise in 821 tells of the Chola king who heard the cow's bell and drove the chariot over his own son, the extreme case of 'no one' including one's own child.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"p:822","b":"p:818","type":"same_image","note":"Both open with பாலோ தேனோ, the string of sweet questions: in 818 they are a lullaby's endearments for a child, in 822 a lover's praise of a woman's speech, which the song then turns into questions about the inner light.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:824","b":"t:1611","type":"develops","note":"Tirumular says that for those whom silence reaches, release and the powers come and all speech ends in a mute word; 824 makes that silent word the guru's teaching, a sword that drives off Death's servants.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"p:827","b":"k:1067","type":"agrees","note":"The Kural begs every beggar not to beg from those who hide what they have; 827 is the confession of a poet who did just that, flattering misers to end his poverty, and turns to the Lord's feet instead.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:828","b":"p:827","type":"same_image","note":"Both repent of patron praise: 827 quotes the flattery, calling a miser the kalpaka tree that protects poets, and 828 lists its inversions, the dumb called famous, fools capable and the base lords, with the patron set beside cloud and kalpaka tree.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"p:834","b":"p:822","type":"same_image","note":"Both build a woman's charms as questions (மொழி பாலோ தேனோ, விழி வேலோ; சேலோ வேலோ, பாலோ பாகோ). In 822 the questions are turned to the inner light; in 834 they lead to shame and to the Lord's mark.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:837","b":"p:2","type":"agrees","note":"Both credit the poet's singing to Murugan's own teaching: in 2 Murugan told him to sing the Thiruppugazh; in 837 he 'became guru and made me speak this', which the poet wonders at as the fruit of some former day.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:838","b":"n:380","type":"agrees","note":"Both picture the courtesan whose mind is with another man while she embraces this one; the Naladiyar marvels that men still don't see it, and 838 says she leaves the lover of many days as soon as another brings more wealth.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:841","b":"p:840","type":"parallel_domain","note":"The two songs share their last lines and their Rama praise; 840 asks for the feet that end maya, while 841 only asks whether it must lose its tapas, so the pair shows one shrine-formula carrying two pleas.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"p:843","b":"t:2125","type":"same_image","note":"Both list the body's stuff, blood, flesh, fat, bone and marrow; the Tirumanthiram counts it as the perishing adjunct of the soul, while 843 makes it a house of earth and water the soul moves into and must watch rot.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:846","b":"p:2","type":"agrees","note":"Both recall the moment Murugan told the poet to sing the Thiruppugazh; 846 adds that he called him as one taking refuge and gave him the experience of release first.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:846","b":"n:371","type":"develops","note":"The Naladiyar says the courtesan's love ends when the man's money ends; 846 goes further, that no amount, thousands of thousands of gold coins, ever satisfies her.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:848","b":"p:292","type":"agrees","note":"Both confess praising unworthy men as the kalpaka tree and as Arjuna to get money; 848 sets that trade beside the courtesans as two forms of one failure to praise Murugan.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:855","b":"p:153","type":"agrees","note":"Both promise to keep Murugan in mind even while sunk in women's pleasure; 153 says he will never forget, 855 names what he will hold, the feet, the twelve arms and the chest.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:858","b":"t:1934","type":"same_image","note":"Both begin the body as a drop like dew on a blade of grass; the Tirumanthiram follows the drop through the body's elements, 858 follows it through a whole life to the cremation fire.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:858","b":"p:508","type":"agrees","note":"Both trace life from a dewlike drop to death; 508 ends asking whether the sorrow will never end, 858 asks for a union in which 'you and I' become one.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"p:860","b":"p:23","type":"agrees","note":"Both name as remedy the sharing of even a millet grain (தினையளவு); in 23 it is drawn from the fear of death, in 860 it stands with singing and tapas among the poet's omissions.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:863","b":"k:9","type":"develops","note":"The Kural names God only as the one of eight qualities, whose feet the head must bow to; 863 takes the same epithet, எண்குணமுற்றோன், and places that Lord dancing in a hall within, to be seen in yogic vision.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"p:868","b":"n:13","type":"agrees","note":"Both picture old age with failing speech, lost teeth and a staff; the Naladiyar faults the old man who still stays in the house eyeing desire, while 868 adds the household's scorn and asks for the feet that end birth.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:870","b":"p:855","type":"agrees","note":"Both ask not to escape the women but to keep Murugan in mind amid them; 870 widens it to every state, union, sleep and talk, and asks for the knowledge to recite him.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:873","b":"k:1001","type":"agrees","note":"The Kural counts the man who heaps up wealth and does not use it as already dead; 873 puts those who hoarded and buried without giving (ஈயாது தேடிப் புதைத்த) among the wicked bound for the lowest births. Both condemn wealth withheld, the Kural as a life wasted, 873 as a sin with a sequel.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:874","b":"t:252","type":"agrees","note":"Tirumular says anyone can give God a green leaf; 874 asks to worship with one flower or leaf if not many flowers. Both make the smallest offering enough, Tirumular as a duty open to all, 874 as a plea that love, not abundance, will be accepted.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:876","b":"k:1085","type":"same_image","note":"The Kural's lover asks whether the girl's look is Death, an eye or a doe, and finds it all three; 876 asks the same question through a dozen guesses, Death (மறலி) and the deer (மான்) among them. The Kural's doubt is a lover's wonder; in 876 it ends by calling such women demons to be avoided.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:881","b":"n:13","type":"same_image","note":"Both picture the old man walking unsteadily on a staff after a life of desire; the Naladiyar says those still set on desire then have no way to safety, while 881 asks the golden feet even so, even if he is born again.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:894","b":"p:898","type":"same_image","note":"Both name the scavengers that will eat the body, foxes, dogs, ghouls and vultures, with owls in 894 and flies, ants and crows in 898; 894 uses them to end its catalogue of disease, 898 to shame those who still swagger in palanquins.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:895","b":"n:25","type":"agrees","note":"The Naladiyar's man sees kin wailing over a corpse carried to the burning ground and still marries believing there is joy here; 895 follows the mourners home from the pyre, where Kama soon sets them laughing again. Both find the same forgetting at the edge of the burning ground.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:896","b":"k:360","type":"agrees","note":"The Kural says pain perishes when even the names of desire, anger and delusion perish; 896 asks to remove the three faults (முக்குற்றம்) as the first step toward the shoreless sea of release. The link depends on reading முக்குற்றம் as the Kural's three.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:907","b":"p:339","type":"agrees","note":"Both set aside the quarrelling sects and ask instead for one wordless certainty, 339 for the true form filled with silence, 907 for jnana that does not waver; 907 goes further and names the Saiva paths of chariyai, kiriyai and yoga among the places to be lost.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:907","b":"t:1446","type":"qualifies","note":"The Tirumanthiram assigns chariyai, kiriyai, yoga and jnana each to its own people, with the siddhas reaching Siva-jnana at the top. 907 keeps the order but treats the first three as traps rather than steps, asking to be given jnana without perishing in them.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:908","b":"p:368","type":"same_image","note":"Both songs are built on the refrain அலமலம், 'enough, enough'. 368 sweeps away women, patron-poetry and the sects; 908 the body itself, food, bed, wife, children and kin, keeping only kinship with those who think of the Lord.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:911","b":"k:107","type":"agrees","note":"The Kural says the good remember through seven births those who wiped away their distress; 911 makes the same vow, never to forget, toward the Lord who cut away the stain of desire and gave jnana.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:913","b":"p:920","type":"same_image","note":"Both credit Murugan with Sambandar's victory over the Jains: 913 names Sirkazhi, the Kaundinya clan and the leaf that survived the fire; 920 the leaf that swam against the current, the eight thousand impaled Jains and the Pandya king and queen.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:915","b":"p:901","type":"same_image","note":"Two Vayalur songs on the one refrain காட்டி, 'showing': in both the courtesans show their bodies and arts to deceive, and 915 turns the same verb on Murugan, who shows jnana.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:920","b":"p:306","type":"same_image","note":"Both make Murugan the saint who cured the Pandya's hunch and sent the Jains to the stake; 306 sets it against court poetry, 920 against the courtesans, and adds the leaf against the current and the queen who lived on at Alavai.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:928","b":"n:21","type":"agrees","note":"Both take kings riding elephants under the white umbrella as the case that proves death spares no one; the Naladiyar says they too were carried off, 928 that in their music and pride they never know the death Brahma has written.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:934","b":"p:24","type":"same_image","note":"Both lament a girl sick with love for Murugan in another's voice, wounded by the moon, the wind and a bird's call; 934 adds the lone flute and ends asking the god to come and embrace her.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:935","b":"p:306","type":"agrees","note":"Both refuse the bard's flattery of base patrons: 306 mocks calling a miser Pari or Kari, 935 calling sinners the equal of the twenty-one ancient patrons, after listing the poet's own honours.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:940","b":"n:11","type":"agrees","note":"Both take the coming of grey hair as the reason to turn now: the Naladiyar's wise renounce in childhood knowing old age will come, and 940 asks for service at the feet while youth lasts, before the hair whitens like a crane.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:941","b":"p:951","type":"agrees","note":"Both turn from the uproar of the six faiths and the arts of argument: 941 says 'enough' even to Siva's mantra and yantra lore and asks for the highest state, 951 asks to leave the attachment to argument and know the knowledge that cannot be pointed to.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:945","b":"t:176","type":"agrees","note":"Both meet Death's messengers by turning to the Lord in time: the Tirumanthiram says that for one who thinks of the Lord the messengers will not even think of burning him, and 945 asks for the feet before the messengers with noose and trident seize him.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:945","b":"p:955","type":"same_image","note":"Both draw old age in the same order, grey hair, failing eyes and teeth, phlegm and cough, then the family's contempt: in 945 kin mock, in 955 the wife reviles and all call him unclean, and both ask for grace at the hour that follows.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:948","b":"k:1227","type":"develops","note":"Both make evening the hour when love's sickness is fullest: the Kural's woman says it buds in the morning and opens at evening; 948 gives that hour to the poet himself and arms it as Kama's army of breeze, moon, cuckoo and sea.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:952","b":"k:912","type":"agrees","note":"Both describe the courtesan's charm as calculated: the Kural's women weigh the profit before speaking of their virtue, and in 952 they name the price and learn their arts of deceit.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:954","b":"p:2","type":"agrees","note":"Both name Murugan's command to sing the Thiruppugazh: 2 remembers the grace of being told to sing, and 954 adds that the command came with lordship, poetic gift and a guru's authority.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:955","b":"n:13","type":"same_image","note":"Both picture the old man whose teeth are gone and whose body is scorned; the Naladiyar faults him for keeping his eyes on desire still, while 955 lists the decay and asks only for grace at the hour of death.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:959","b":"k:913","type":"same_image","note":"Both make the courtesan's embrace an embrace of a corpse, but in opposite directions: in the Kural the man who embraces her holds a stranger's corpse in a dark room; in 959 the women, looking only at money, will embrace even a corpse.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:956","b":"p:1","type":"develops","note":"1 says only that Ganapati first wrote on the first mountain; 956 tells the story fully, that he wrote the Bharata with one tusk on Meru when he was asked.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:967","b":"p:6","type":"same_image","note":"967 recasts the book's opening song almost formula by formula, the three-eyed Lord's chant of the seed of release, the thirty-three gods, the arrow at the ten heads and the chariot driven by the green-cloud Vishnu, and names the treasure sought as Sivam in devotees' hearts.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:966","b":"n:375","type":"agrees","note":"Both fault the courtesan's double dealing: the Naladiyar's eel shows one end to the snake and the other to the fish, and 966's women talk as if on a man's side while hooking him for his wealth.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:970","b":"n:371","type":"agrees","note":"Both tie the courtesan's company to money: the Naladiyar's love lasts as long as a lamp's oil, and 970 ends with the wealth sought now wasted and no goal left.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"p:974","b":"p:24","type":"same_image","note":"Both speak for a girl in love with Murugan who wastes at evening under Kama's arrows and the wind; 24 is her mother's lament against the anril bird, the south wind and the crescent, while 974 blames the north wind and asks the god to come today.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:978","b":"n:21","type":"agrees","note":"Both take the death of kings as the proof of impermanence: the Naladiyar's kings under moon-white umbrellas are carried off, and 978's sword-bearing kings reach the burning ground in a moment; 978 draws from it the renunciant's resolve to end birth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:981","b":"n:24","type":"same_image","note":"Both follow the corpse from the wailing and the drums to the fire; the Naladiyar ends with the dying carrying the dead, 981 with the ash and a prayer not to be born again.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:983","b":"n:13","type":"develops","note":"Both show the man whose speech fails and who walks with a staff; the Naladiyar's old man still keeps his eyes on desire, while 983 traces the whole road from the price-women of youth to the staff and a catalogue of diseases.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:985","b":"t:2528","type":"agrees","note":"Both lead the yogi through the sushumna to a light within; Tirumular's eight-petalled flower holds maya's city, and 985 names the nectar of Sivayoga in the sushumna of the sky as the state it asks Murugan to give.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:987","b":"k:912","type":"agrees","note":"The Kural's women weigh the profit before they speak of virtue; 987 makes the weighing literal, the courtesans assaying the gold they take and counting the hours before the quarrel that ends the bargain.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:988","b":"p:974","type":"agrees","note":"Both are akam songs for a girl in love with Murugan, burned by Kama's arrows and the night, that ask him to come to her now.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"p:996","b":"p:995","type":"agrees","note":"Twin songs with one refrain: both set the pomp of riding elephants and horses as a king, and pleasure with women, against the supreme truth (பரமார்த்தம்) never grasped, and both name Murugan as Siva's teacher.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:1004","b":"p:979","type":"qualifies","note":"In 979 the poet longs to leave praise-poetry for a life of chariyai and kiriyai; in 1004 those who call chariyai and kiriyai penance are among the half-knowers whose path he asks to leave for the experience beyond deeds and malas. The two fit as stages, not as a contradiction.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:1004","b":"t:1547","type":"agrees","note":"Both set the quarrels of the sects against knowing the light within: Tirumular says that for one who reaches it there is no quarrel at all, and 1004 asks to leave the quarrelling sectarians for an experience where 'mine' and 'yours' cease.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:1001","b":"n:25","type":"agrees","note":"Both stand the lover of pleasure beside the funeral: the Naladiyar's drum answers the man who sees the corpse carried yet thinks there is joy here, and 1001 asks at the drum and the burning ground whether that is sweet.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:1006","b":"p:23","type":"same_image","note":"Both measure the failure to give by a millet grain: 23 asks to share even a grain of millet before Death's summons, and 1006 opens its self-condemnation with having not a millet-grain's thought of sharing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:1028","b":"t:2085","type":"agrees","note":"Tirumular asks what men gain by arguing when time slips away, since arguers cannot bow at the Stainless one's feet; 1028 sends those who learn books to quarrel to Death's town with those who do not melt for the Lord.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:1028","b":"n:273","type":"agrees","note":"The Naladiyar's hoarder who neither enjoys nor gives dies mocked by his own wealth; 1028 adds that such men, who seek money and live without giving, go to Death's town.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:1025","b":"p:1027","type":"develops","note":"Two songs of the same refrain-set turn on disease: 1025 asks for the feet at the hour when humoral disease brings death, 1027 lists diseases at length and asks to be kept from them and set in release.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:1041","b":"t:145","type":"same_image","note":"Both follow the funeral to its end: the kin burn the body and then bathe in the water (நீரினில் மூழ்கி; போதா நீருடே போய் மூழ்கா). The Tirumanthiram adds that they stop thinking of the dead; 1041 says they act as strangers.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:1044","b":"t:567","type":"qualifies","note":"The Tirumanthiram teaches that if the breath stays still with the mind there is no birth or death; 1044 rejects the yogi who thinks turning back the breath gives liberation but lacks grace, and asks instead for the feet. The point of difference depends on reading 1044 as faulting yoga without grace, not breath control as such.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:1054","b":"p:1049","type":"same_image","note":"Both close with the same question, பரம ஞான வீடு ஏது புகல்வாயே. 1049 defines that abode by what cannot touch it; 1054 sets it against the rotting body and against rituals performed for show.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:1054","b":"n:27","type":"same_image","note":"Both see the body as a bubble that forms and bursts (the Naladiyar's மொக்குள், 1054's குமிழி); the Naladiyar praises those who see it so and resolve to end birth, and 1054 confesses cherishing it anyway.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:1066","b":"n:120","type":"agrees","note":"Both say nothing goes with a person at death; the Naladiyar names deeds as the one thing that does, while 1066 lists wealth, servants and wife that do not and turns to praising the feet.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:1078","b":"t:1597","type":"same_image","note":"Tirumular says Nandi set his holy feet on his head and so drained the path to the womb; 1078 asks Murugan to set his feet on the poet's head in friendship, the same act of the guru's grace.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:1079","b":"p:1089","type":"same_image","note":"Both songs give the kin's words at death, 'take it quickly' (கடுகென எடும், எடும் எடு), and the body laid in the fire at the burning ground.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:1093","b":"n:46","type":"agrees","note":"Both list the body's gut, fat, blood and flesh (குடர், கொழு, குருதி, தடி) to undo desire; the Naladiyar asks which part is the garlanded woman, 1093 shows women perfuming and adorning that same body.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:1094","b":"t:1530","type":"agrees","note":"Tirumular says the six sects do not see the God within the body; 1094 calls the crore of rival faiths blind to the one Thing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:1092","b":"k:1061","type":"develops","note":"The Kural says not asking even from glad givers is ten million times better; 1092 shows the poet who begged from patrons who gave nothing, and seeks the end of that poverty not in better patrons but in the one Thing.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:1094","b":"p:1078","type":"develops","note":"In 1078 the poet says he has escaped the crumbling other faiths; 1094 says why: they are blind to the one Thing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:1110","b":"t:157","type":"same_image","note":"Both follow the dead man to the pyre and the mourners' plunge into the water (நீர்த்தலை மூழ்குவர்; அலைநீரிற் புக்கு முழுகா). Tirumular calls the mourners people without justice, who forget once they have bathed; 1110 lets the bath end their grief and asks for a life of praise before that day.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:1112","b":"t:154","type":"same_image","note":"Both count the tattvas as thirty, thirty and thirty-six (முப்பதும் முப்பதும் முப்பத்தறுவரும்; முப்பதும் ஆறாறும் முப்பதும்). Tirumular has them flee when the body-temple falls; 1112 asks for a silence that is other than all of them, reached before death.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:1112","b":"t:1707","type":"agrees","note":"Both describe the ascent through the body's centres (ஆதார சோதனை; சக்ர சடாதாரம் முட்டவும் மீதேறி) to a state in which the senses and inner instruments stop working (புலன் கரணம் புத்தி சாதாரணங் கெட்டான்; சொற்கரணாதீதம்).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:1115","b":"t:144","type":"agrees","note":"Both say that wife, children and goods do not follow the dead. Tirumular names what does go along, vows and knowledge; 1115 asks for the mind to praise the Lord's feet.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:1117","b":"t:157","type":"agrees","note":"In both the kin who surround the dead turn back and leave him. 1117 draws the question 'who is kin then?', which its praise answers with Murugan.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:1125","b":"p:1105","type":"develops","note":"1105 confesses praising patrons as the rain cloud, the parijata tree and Meru; 1125 shows who those patrons are, deceitful, loveless and bound for hell, and names the same kind of praise, as Vishnu, Kama or the gods' tree, as the waste of a life.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:1125","b":"n:78","type":"agrees","note":"The Naladiyar says even the starving should not tell their need to men without courtesy; 1125 confesses doing exactly that, going in verse to the cruel and loveless, and asks to be stopped.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:1141","b":"k:920","type":"agrees","note":"Both put the faithless woman beside liquor and make her the mark of lost fortune: the Kural's women, drink and dice are the company of those Fortune has left; 1141 calls the drinking courtesans themselves மூதேவிகள், Misfortune, who bring poverty.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:1140","b":"t:157","type":"qualifies","note":"Both picture the wailing kin at the funeral. Tirumular's kin turn back at the edge of town and leave the dead man to the fire; 1140 lets the kin wail and asks that Murugan come at that moment with his devotees, so that the dead man is not left alone.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:1131","b":"p:1140","type":"develops","note":"Both stage the deathbed, the kin who lament and leave, and Yama's coming. 1131 asks only to praise Murugan before the end; 1140 asks him to arrive at the funeral itself and fight Death's army.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:1154","b":"n:371","type":"agrees","note":"Both make the courtesan's love last as long as the man's money: the Naladiyar's lamp goes out when the oil ends; in 1154 the wealth in his hand is gone and everyone drives him off with 'chi, chi'.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:1155","b":"k:978","type":"agrees","note":"The Kural says smallness adorns itself in self-praise; 1155 gives the examples, the coward praising his valour and the miser calling himself Dharmaputra, and then applies the charge to the poet himself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:1155","b":"n:195","type":"tension","note":"The Naladiyar says 'good caste' and 'bad caste' are words with nothing behind them; 1155 mocks men of base lineage (இழிகுலத்தர்) for claiming a high one, which treats birth-rank as real. The conflict holds if இழிகுலத்தர் is read literally as low caste rather than as a term of abuse for base men.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:1146","b":"t:264","type":"agrees","note":"Both send to hell those who give nothing to the one who begs. Tirumular adds those who do not praise the Lord or tend groves; 1146 adds those who do not pay their teacher.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:1146","b":"t:537","type":"qualifies","note":"Both put in deep hell those who speak ill of the Lord's devotees. Tirumular means Siva's devotees; 1146 names the servants of both Siva and Vishnu (ஈசர் விஷ்ணுவை சேவைசெய்வோர்).","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"p:1158","b":"n:24","type":"same_image","note":"Both hear the funeral drum and see the corpse covered. The Naladiyar counts the beats with detachment and ends 'the dying carry the dead'; 1158 is the dead man's own view of his mourners, and turns the scene into a plea for the feet at that hour.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:1163","b":"p:9","type":"same_image","note":"Both tell a life in one breath from the embryo in the womb. 9 stops at youth, women and disease; 1163 goes on to the stick, the bed, Death and the pyre, and so asks not for grace but for an end to birth.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:1166","b":"n:13","type":"agrees","note":"Both show an old man with failing speech or teeth and a staff whose eyes are still on desire. The Naladiyar says such a man has no way to safety; 1166 is the old man's own confession and still hopes his mind will turn to song.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:1167","b":"k:335","type":"agrees","note":"Both put good deeds before the body fails: the Kural before the hiccup stops the tongue, 1167 before the day the wife spits and life slips away. 1167 joins dharma to bowing before Murugan.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:1170","b":"p:14","type":"same_image","note":"Both name Vishnu as the god who followed after a poet's song (பாவலோர்கள் பாடல் கண்டு ஏகும் மால், வண்டமிழ் பயில்வோர் பின் திரிகின்றவன்): a repeated formula for the uncle.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:1174","b":"k:1131","type":"same_image","note":"Both make the palmyra horse the end point of love's suffering. In the Kural it is the lover's only strength; in 1174 it is what a learned renouncer and yogi is reduced to, and he asks whether fate or grace brought him there.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:1175","b":"p:1157","type":"agrees","note":"Both describe the truth that scriptures, practices and debaters cannot reach and call it a great ocean of bliss (மகோததி), and both answer with the feet. 1175 builds the whole plea from negatives; 1157 sets the four Saiva paths themselves among those that fail.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:1183","b":"p:1176","type":"same_image","note":"Both build the courtesan's arts on the verb of showing (காட்டுவர், காட்டி). 1183 adds robbery and the love-drug; 1176 turns the verb back on Siva and Murugan, asking to be shown the feet instead.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:1183","b":"n:377","type":"agrees","note":"Both describe women who take all the wealth from a man's hands while he believes in their love. The Naladiyar ends in the town's laughter at the fool; 1183 ends in a plea to be taken out of that union.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:1181","b":"p:1180","type":"same_image","note":"Both give Murugan the love-sickness the poet confesses: the south wind and Kama's arrows wound him until he goes to Valli in the millet field. 1181 puts in his mouth the plea to save his life from love's fever.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:1189","b":"p:24","type":"same_image","note":"Both are a mother's lament for a daughter lovesick for Murugan, worn by the moon, the wind and the evening; 24 adds the anril bird, 1189 the gossiping women and the herdsman's flute.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:1189","b":"k:1228","type":"same_image","note":"The herdsman's flute as a weapon of evening: the Kural's woman calls it a messenger of the burning dusk that kills, and in 1189 the cowherd's flute is among the things wearing the girl down.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:1194","b":"n:13","type":"develops","note":"The Naladiyar sketches the old man whose speech fails and teeth fall but who still keeps his eyes on desire; 1194 lists every stage of that decay down to the stench of the sickbed and turns it into a plea for grace at the last moment.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"p:1215","b":"p:23","type":"same_image","note":"Both picture Yama's palm-leaf summons with drums, the bier and wailing kin; 23 draws the lesson of sharing before it comes, 1215 asks instead for love of the anklets.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:1214","b":"k:911","type":"agrees","note":"Both name the courtesan's want as money, not love: the Kural warns that the sweet words of women who desire wealth bring harm, and 1214 calls them women whose minds seek coins.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:1227","b":"t:157","type":"same_image","note":"Both follow the corpse from the house: the kin strip him or wrap him in torn cloth, light the pyre and then bathe. The Tirumanthiram calls them people without justice; 1227 calls life itself pure falsehood and asks for the state of Siva.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:1231","b":"k:912","type":"agrees","note":"The Kural's women who weigh the profit before they speak match 1231's women who sell love after reckoning the coins in a man's hand (கைக்காசின் அளவறிந்து); 1231 adds the poets who sing such women's bodies.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:1226","b":"p:1022","type":"agrees","note":"Both confess the court poet's sin of singing a patron in borrowed praise for gain; 1226 calls the stock comparisons stolen, 1022 the patron undeserving, and both turn the song to the feet.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:1221","b":"t:1507","type":"agrees","note":"Tirumular explains salokam, samipam and sarupam as stages gained through chariyai and the paths after it; 1221 names Murugan lord of the devotees of exactly these three, stopping short of sayujyam.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"p:1235","b":"t:155","type":"agrees","note":"Tirumular's dead man leaves the honey-haired woman, the wealth and the house and is carried to the common burning ground; 1235 lists wife, servants, clan and gathered wealth and ends with the body in a small old rag at the burning ground.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:1246","b":"t:1556","type":"agrees","note":"Tirumular's sectarians stay inside their sect and never gain the experience free of ego; 1246 asks whether one should speak endlessly as scripture instead of seeking the one cause known by experience (அநுபூதி), having said 'enough' to the sects.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:1247","b":"t:1503","type":"agrees","note":"Tirumular says people wander off calling this or that the supreme and that going the ordained way stills the mind's 'that or this' (அது இது); 1247 asks to know, without quarrelling with the sects, the Lord beyond he, she and it, this and that.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:1252","b":"n:27","type":"same_image","note":"The Naladiyar's wise see the body as a bubble in pelting rain and resolve to end their wandering; 1252 calls the poet's own life a bubble (புற்புதப் பிராணன்) and asks for the true word instead.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:1254","b":"p:24","type":"same_image","note":"Both are akam laments at dusk with the south wind, the moon, the anril bird and Kama's arrows; 24 is in a mother's voice, 1254 in the girl's own, asking for the hero's garland.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:1265","b":"n:29","type":"same_image","note":"The Naladiyar's 'he stood, he sat, he lay, and with kin wailing he was gone' urges virtue now; 1265 extends the same list of verbs from birth to corpse (பிறந்தார் கிடந்தார் இருந்தார் தவழ்ந்தார் நடந்தார்) and turns it into a plea for the Lord's 'fear not'.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:1270","b":"n:46","type":"agrees","note":"The Naladiyar lists entrails, blood, bone, sinew and skin and asks where the woman is among them; 1270 calls the body a cage of filth, skin, water and bone, but draws service to the Lord from it rather than disgust at women.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"p:1275","b":"t:857","type":"agrees","note":"Tirumular rouses the root and joins it to the head through the middle channel to reach the holy feet; 1275 asks for the light above the root centre and the steady gem-light in the middle path.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:1266","b":"p:1267","type":"develops","note":"Both set a list of kin against Death's coming: 1266 with Death's rope and a household quarrelling over property, 1267 with Yama on the buffalo and a grief for kin called illusory.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"p:1276","b":"p:1255","type":"agrees","note":"Two vows rather than pleas: 1255 will speak only of the feet whether king or poor, 1276 will not forget them even in the deluded embrace of women.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:1268","b":"p:1254","type":"same_image","note":"Both akam laments end with a request for the hero's flowers, his garland in 1254 and kura blossoms in 1268, against Kama's arrows and the sea.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:1291","b":"p:1285","type":"same_image","note":"The same four lines of plea, Kama's arrows, the blue sea, the cuckoo of the grove and 'embrace the deer', with only the epithets changed; the two read as variants of one song.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:1287","b":"p:1282","type":"same_image","note":"Both songs end by naming Murugan as Sambandar: 1282 by name and by his child's dance, 1287 by the milk of Uma and the impaling of the Jains, and both credit him with filling the land with Tamil.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:1289","b":"k:911","type":"agrees","note":"The Kural warns that the sweet words of women who want wealth, not love, bring harm; 1289 dramatizes those words (laughing talk, soothing care) and ends with the plunder of wealth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:1292","b":"k:346","type":"agrees","note":"Both name 'I' and 'mine' (யான் எனது) as what must be cut; the Kural promises a world above the gods to whoever cuts them, and 1292 asks the cutting itself as a gift of knowledge.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:1297","b":"k:1147","type":"agrees","note":"In both the town's gossip feeds the girl's love-sickness rather than ending it; the Kural makes it manure for the sickness, and 1297 sets the reproach of women beside Kama and the moon as what sinks her in sorrow.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:1303","b":"p:1290","type":"same_image","note":"The same frame, the moon, Kama and a question ending -லாமோ, closing on தம்பிரானே; 1290 asks about the girl, 1303 is her own voice and adds the world's reproach.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:1307","b":"k:1","type":"same_image","note":"The Kural opens with A as first of letters and God as first of the world; 1307 makes Murugan the letter A itself (அகரமுமாகி), and the one above the three gods.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:1313","b":"p:1306","type":"same_image","note":"Both turn into a catalogue of shrines; 1306 is wholly the list, while 1313 sets the list after a yogic ascent, as if the outer pilgrimage answered the inner one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:1317","b":"k:911","type":"agrees","note":"The Kural warns that the sweet words of women who want wealth, not love, bring harm; 1317 names their lavish lying speech and love set at a price, and adds the brass polished to pass for gold.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"p:1321","b":"n:13","type":"develops","note":"The Naladiyar sees the old man with failed speech, a staff and lost teeth still eyeing desire; 1321 gives the same old age in detail, adds the medicines, and draws the remedy not from renunciation but from song and the feet.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"p:1323","b":"p:1092","type":"agrees","note":"Both confess the poet's craft spent for wealth: 1092 the praise-songs sung to patrons who gave nothing, 1323 the arts and poems learned and sung while running the streets after money.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:1325","b":"t:151","type":"same_image","note":"Both mark the end by the physician giving up (Tirumular's lets go the pulse, 1325's says no remedy will work) while wife and wealth stay behind or wail; Tirumular leaves it there, while 1325 asks for Murugan to come at that hour.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"p:1326","b":"t:1","type":"same_image","note":"Tirumular's first verse counts the Lord from one to eight (one, two by grace, within three, knowing four, conquering five); 1326 uses the same counting device through seven to tell Murugan's acts, so the numbers become theology in one and narrative in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null}],"threads":[{"id":"thread:body_carcass_or_temple","title":"Carcass, temple, or neither: what the body is","claim":"The Naladiyar mostly treats the body as a pot seeping filth from nine openings, a stinking cart for vultures, and a skin bag the dancer leaves behind; seeing it so is meant to cool desire and turn one to renunciation. The Tirumanthiram holds a different view beside an echo of that one. Its chapter on the body's impermanence speaks of death and mourning, and one verse there nearly repeats Naladiyar 26 (167), though the same chapter also calls the body a walled temple (154); the teaching chapter just before it makes the body the temple where Nandi dwells (116). Later tantras build on the temple: the body of flesh is the Lord's shrine (1823), its very form is the Sivalinga and the dance (1726), and those who dismiss the body as மலம் are dull (2137, if மலம் means bodily filth rather than the soul's impurity). In 725 Tirumular tells the change as his own: he once held the body base, then saw that the Highest had made it his temple, and now he tends it. He does not drop the older view: to the unreleased the body is still a prison (1701), and only a jnani's corpse is exempt from the Naladiyar's indifference to the dead body (1910, 1911). Even the Naladiyar values the body as a means: it is rare to obtain and its fruit should be taken (34), as Tirumular nourishes it because without it one cannot attain true knowledge (724). The Thiruppugazh adds a third, unsettled voice. Arunagirinathar shares the Naladiyar's disgust (815 lists gut, fat, bone and blood in a hut of nine gates) and in several songs rejects Tirumular's remedy: the yogi who holds the breath thinking the impermanent flesh will last is a fool (355, against 722), the medicines and yogas tried on the body are an anguish to be given up (815, against 724), and the anxious yoga that will not accept that the body must go should leave him (211, against 570). Yet other songs ask for that very yoga, a body made diamond-hard and steady through the rising breath (471, 517), so what he rejects is an anxious clinging to the body, not bodily yoga as such.","grade":"partly_supported","links":[["t:167","n:26"],["t:116","n:48"],["t:1823","n:47"],["t:1726","n:46"],["t:2137","n:43"],["t:724","n:34"],["t:725","n:47"],["t:116","t:725"],["t:2137","t:725"],["t:1701","t:1823"],["t:1910","n:26"],["t:1911","t:1823"],["p:355","t:722"],["p:815","t:724"],["p:211","t:570"]],"books":["Naladiyar","Tirumanthiram","Thiruppugazh"],"limits":"The Naladiyar's body verses mostly serve to cure lust, so each tension holds only if they are read as a verdict on the body itself. 725's 'once' is a turn in the speaker's understanding, not a sign that the tantras are in the order he wrote them, and whether the Naladiyar or the Tirumanthiram borrowed the dancer-in-the-bag verse is not known. Each Thiruppugazh tension holds only if its song is read as rejecting the practice of preserving the body, and the Tirumanthiram verse as promising bodily longevity, not a figure for it.","issue":"The 2137-Naladiyar 43 link is weak: after 2136, மலம் is likelier the soul's impurity, and what the great abandon in Naladiyar 43 is மயல் rather than the body as such. Several Thiruppugazh songs also ask for breath yoga outright (82, 94, 188, 416)."},{"id":"thread:leaping_past_death","title":"Leaping past Death","claim":"The Kural says the power won by austerity can even leap past Death (கூற்றம் குதித்தல், 269); the Naladiyar answers with the same verb that no one here has leapt past Death and escaped, so give your wealth now (6), and that grey hair will surely come (11). The Tirumanthiram holds both views. Several first-tantra verses side with the Naladiyar: Death does not ask whether one is young (255), black hair turns white (192), and a man can die between supper and bed (148, with Kural 336); a verse in the third tantra adds that all who came before have ended (596). The third tantra's yoga sides with the Kural and goes further: those who know the way have no Yama (711), Death's word does not reach the yogi even in dreams (622), and practice undoes grey hair and wrinkles (647, 848, 727). Only once does Tirumular use the disputed verb itself, in the first tantra and against the Naladiyar's use of it: shed your wealth and you may leap past Death (172).","grade":"partly_supported","links":[["n:6","k:269"],["t:596","n:21"],["t:255","n:19"],["t:192","n:11"],["t:148","k:336"],["t:622","n:6"],["t:711","n:6"],["t:622","k:269"],["t:711","k:269"],["t:647","n:11"],["t:848","n:11"],["t:848","t:727"],["t:172","n:6"]],"books":["Kural","Naladiyar","Tirumanthiram"],"limits":"Every tension holds only on a literal reading of Tirumular's promises. Tradition also reads them as freedom from fear of death or from Yama's judgement after death, and on that reading the books need not clash. Kural 269 is itself read literally by Parimelazhagar and figuratively by Kalaignar.","issue":"The first-tantra/third-tantra split is loose: 176, in the first tantra's chapter on wealth, already says Yama's messengers do not come near those who think of the Lord. 622 reads more naturally as freedom from Death's threat than as bodily escape, and 192's last line is too obscure to make greying a stated warning."},{"id":"thread:competing_supremes","title":"Superlatives that compete","claim":"The formula recurs beyond wealth and sometimes collides: nothing surpasses virtue, yet nothing surpasses apt speech; nothing beats truth, yet not killing is ranked above it; poverty is unmatched pain, yet wealth under a tyrant hurts more. Commentators reconcile the first two, and 323 is a deliberate ranking, so these superlatives need case-by-case reading.","grade":"partly_supported","links":[["k:644","k:32"],["k:300","k:323"],["k:841","k:1041"],["k:558","k:1041"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"","issue":"Fails on restraint (32-122 is only a shared formula; restraint is a kind of virtue) and 92-225 is forced. The last sentence overreaches: 323 is an explicit ranking and Parimelazhagar treats it as settling the order, so not every superlative is mere praise."},{"id":"thread:ethics_vs_statecraft","title":"Absolute ethics and the needs of the state","claim":"Five rules stated without exception in the Book of Virtue meet specific verses in the Book of Wealth that suspend or limit them: killing, anger, hypocrisy, generosity and purity of mind. The verses do not acknowledge this; Parimelazhagar reconciles some by adding limits such as royal duty and enemies only. The Book of Wealth elsewhere upholds ethics (546, 657).","grade":"supported","links":[["k:327","k:550"],["k:487","k:303"],["k:586","k:274"],["k:480","k:220"],["k:829","k:34"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"","issue":"All five named areas have a sound link, though two links are the same pair. Overstated in three ways: 480 limits generosity rather than reversing it; 'often' for commentators' added limits rests on clear cases at 550 and 829; and Parimelazhagar directs 303 at renunciants, so some rules were not universal for him. Counterevidence against a book-wide pattern: 546 and 657 show the Book of Wealth upholding ethics."},{"id":"thread:love_overturns","title":"Echoes of the earlier books in the Book of Love","claim":"A few lover-speakers reuse the earlier books' language against its grain: shame worth dying for is set aside to save life (1132/1017), the body-soul bond called flimsy in 338 becomes the image of union (1122), and honour's ban on following those who slight you is called unavailable to the lovesick (1255/966). These are dramatised speeches and do not overturn the earlier teaching.","grade":"partly_supported","links":[["k:1132","k:1017"],["k:1122","k:338"],["k:1255","k:966"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"","issue":"Of the four named examples only shame holds. Senses and warrior are contrasts without incompatibility, and backbiting is forced because அலர் is not புறங்கூறல். 'Overturns' is false as a claim about the book: these are dramatised speakers in stated situations, on Parimelazhagar's and Manakkudavar's framing, and do not revise the earlier teaching."},{"id":"thread:gender_asymmetry","title":"Where the man and woman differ","claim":"On Parimelazhagar's speaker assignments, the man and woman differ over shame (1132/1163) and memory (1202/1207), and 1137 states the first contrast outright. Manakkudavar assigns some speakers differently, and most other differences in the Book of Love follow union and separation rather than gender.","grade":"partly_supported","links":[["k:1163","k:1132"],["k:1202","k:1207"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"","issue":"Only shame and memory hold, and memory's gender contrast fails on Manakkudavar, who gives 1202 to the woman. Evening, night and leaving fail: most contrasts are union against separation, not man against woman, and two links lie outside the Book of Love. Counterevidence: 1251 has the woman's shame break, and the man suffers in 1132 and 1268. 1137, the verse that states the asymmetry outright, is not among the links."},{"id":"thread:truth_by_effect","title":"Truth defined by what it does","claim":"On Parimelazhagar's reading, chapter 30 defines truthfulness by harmlessness of effect and explicitly sets aside mere statement of what happened, though 293 still treats lying as going against what one knows. That definition carries real weight: without it, 297's unbroken not-lying and 292's blameless falsehood would conflict.","grade":"partly_supported","links":[["k:292","k:297"],["k:300","k:323"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"","issue":"The second sentence holds (292-297). 'Not agreement with fact' is Parimelazhagar's explicit gloss on 291, not the verse's wording, and 291 appears only in a forced link, so most links do not test the claim. Counterevidence: 293 still treats lying as going against what one's own heart knows, in the same chapter."},{"id":"thread:knowing_and_learning","title":"Where does understanding come from?","claim":"The book names at least three sources of understanding that do not sit easily together: study (396), one's own or fated nature (373) and company (454). Parimelazhagar reconciles study with fate by limiting study's reach to where fate does not oppose it. Separately, it is divided on whether a speaker's standing matters (423, 415, 850).","grade":"partly_supported","links":[["k:373","k:396"],["k:454","k:373"],["k:423","k:415"],["k:850","k:423"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"","issue":"Only study against fate or innate nature and against company hold. Hearing rests on the wrong 396-416, and poverty's absence has no link in this thread. The two sound links on 423 concern a speaker's credibility, not sources of understanding. Parimelazhagar explicitly reconciles 396 with 373, which the thread does not mention."},{"id":"thread:justice_of_the_world","title":"Does virtue pay? The promise and its escape clauses","claim":"The Book of Virtue states the reward of virtue as a rule: aram yields wealth (31), the fair person's wealth does not decay (112), the envious have no gain (135, 170), the one who does no evil meets no ruin (210), and all pain returns on those who caused it (320). The same book then admits the counter-case: 169 calls the prosperity of the crooked and the ruin of the upright things to be pondered (நினைக்கப்படும்), and 332 says great wealth comes and goes like a theatre crowd, with no exception for the virtuous. The book keeps the promise alive by two moves. Verse 117 changes what counts as ruin: the world does not set down as கேடு the low state of one who stays in virtue, which Parimelazhagar narrows to the judgement of the high-minded (உயர்ந்தோர்) and Manakkudavar reads as the world counting such loss with his gains. The commentators supply the other move, past deeds: Parimelazhagar sends the wise to look for a cause in earlier births at 169, and Manakkudavar reads 320's returning pain as pain done in a former time.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:31","k:169"],["k:112","k:169"],["k:112","k:117"],["k:135","k:169"],["k:169","k:170"],["k:210","k:169"],["k:210","k:117"],["k:320","k:169"],["k:169","k:117"],["k:332","k:112"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"This is a claim about chapters 4 to 34 of the Book of Virtue, and the escape clauses at 117 and in the commentaries are not stated by 169 itself, which leaves the puzzle open. The earlier version of this thread also drew in the apportioner verses (63, 377) and the curse at 1062; those are not in this cluster but belong to the same question.","issue":"The claim says the commentators supply the past-deeds move, which is true inside chapters 4 to 34, but the Book of Virtue itself makes that move in chapter 38 (ஊழ், e.g. 375, 377), right after this range; a reader should not take the text as silent on it. At 117 Parimelazhagar also adds the implied 'செல்வம் என்று கொள்ளுவர்', so he too reads the low state as counted among gains, not only Manakkudavar."},{"id":"thread:poverty","title":"Does character survive poverty?","claim":"Two verses say the poor person of character is not lowered: 117 for the fair-minded (the world does not count his தாழ்வு as ruin) and 988 for the one with the firmness called சால்பு (இன்மை இளிவன்று). Verse 752 reports the opposite as fact: everyone scorns the have-nots and honours the rich, which Parimelazhagar sharpens to 'even if they have every good quality', while 841 says the world does not count lack of wealth as lack at all. The poverty chapter then shows character itself giving way: 1044 says poverty produces degrading speech even in the well-born, though 139 called evil speech impossible for those with conduct and 959 made speech the reliable sign of lineage; on Parimelazhagar's reading of 223, the well-born never utter the beggar's 'I have nothing', which 1044 denies. Verse 1047 draws the line the book seems to want: the poverty that estranges even one's mother is அறஞ்சாரா, poverty not joined to virtue, which matches 117's boundary and runs against 521's claim that kin honour old ties even when all is gone.","grade":"partly_supported","links":[["k:117","k:988"],["k:117","k:752"],["k:752","k:988"],["k:988","k:1044"],["k:841","k:752"],["k:139","k:1044"],["k:1044","k:959"],["k:223","k:1044"],["k:1047","k:117"],["k:752","k:1047"],["k:521","k:1047"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"The reconciliation through அறஞ்சாரா at 1047 is the verse's own qualifier, but Pope drops it and reads the verse of poverty in general; and 752, 841 and 1044 are not qualified by virtue, so the disagreement over whether the world and the person hold up under want remains. The earlier version of this thread noted that இன்மை also names the ascetic's chosen possessionlessness (408, 532), a different condition not covered by this cluster.","issue":"841 does not say the world counts lack of wealth as no lack 'at all': its point is ranking lack of wisdom as the worst lack, and Parimelazhagar glosses that the world does not count other lack as lack of that kind. On Parimelazhagar's reading the degrading words of 1044 are begging ('give to us'), not the தீய speech (harmful lies etc.) that 139 rules out, so the 139 and 959 contrasts are weaker than stated. His note on 1047 also says all kin abandon the poor man because he must take and cannot give, which sharpens rather than softens the clash with 521."},{"id":"thread:fate_and_agency","title":"Fate and agency: who moves the mover?","claim":"Chapter 38 places fate one step behind every human cause: effort and sloth arise from a fate of gain and a fate of loss (371), and fate is stronger than any plan (380). Chapters 61 and 62 treat the same மடி as a thing a person chooses (605, காமக் கலன்) and can depose, after which the faults it brought are gone (609 against 604); they make effort the cause of wealth (616) and house the two goddesses in sloth and labour (617), and 618 blames idleness but not misfortune, a line 371 makes hard to draw if sloth is fate's product. Chapter 62 then both concedes fate and defies it: 619 grants that a thing may fail through fate and keeps the wage of the body's strain for effort, while 620 says the tireless see fate's back. Verse 262 extends the regress to austerity itself, which on Parimelazhagar's and Manakkudavar's reading needs prior-birth தவம் to be undertaken at all; Kalaignar reads it as present self-control and removes the conflict. Parimelazhagar narrows the gap at 620 by saying fate blocks only a few attempts, and 468 adds that effort counts only when made by the right method.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:371","k:620"],["k:620","k:380"],["k:609","k:371"],["k:371","k:617"],["k:371","k:618"],["k:616","k:371"],["k:605","k:371"],["k:380","k:619"],["k:609","k:604"],["k:262","k:620"],["k:468","k:616"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"The verses do not acknowledge one another; the reconciliations are the commentators'. 371 is a general claim about the mind, whereas 616 to 620 are addressed to the king, so the Book of Wealth may be stating a working rule rather than a doctrine. The related question of whether foresight shields a person from fate (429, 372, 380) is treated in its own thread.","issue":"605 is a weak witness for choice: காமக் கலன் says the four are desired, but கெடுநீரார் ('those whose nature is to perish') points toward a predisposition, closer to 371 than against it. Parimelazhagar also glosses 609's மடியாண்மை as the தாமத குணம் behind sloth, a disposition rather than a single act. Kalaignar reads ஊழ் in 371 and 380 as இயற்கை நிலை (natural condition), which softens the regress."},{"id":"thread:foresight_and_fate","title":"Can foresight guard against what is coming?","claim":"Chapter 43 defines understanding as knowing what will be (427, ஆவது அறிவார்) and promises that those whose understanding guards ahead meet no affliction that shakes them (429). Chapter 38 undercuts both ends of that promise: 380 says fate arrives before any counter-plan one devises, and 372 says a fate of loss makes a person foolish, which Parimelazhagar reads as happening even to one with every kind of understanding, so the trait 427 treats as fixed is itself at fate's disposal. Chapter 67 quietly adjusts 429: 662 keeps avoiding harm beforehand as the first rule but adds not giving way once it has come, which Parimelazhagar reads as the case of a task spoiled by fate. Manakkudavar meets the conflict at 429 directly, allowing that early guarding softens even what comes from past deeds.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:427","k:429"],["k:372","k:429"],["k:427","k:372"],["k:429","k:380"],["k:662","k:429"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"The verses are addressed to different readers (chapter 38 to all, chapters 43 and 67 to the king and his ministers), and 429 says 'no affliction that shakes', not 'no affliction', so a narrow reading leaves room for 662. None of the verses names the other.","issue":"Parimelazhagar sets 372 in the chapter's wealth context: the loss-fate is the fate of losing what one holds (கைப்பொருள்). That narrows the claim that understanding is 'at fate's disposal'. Parimelazhagar reads 662's ஊறொரால் as not undertaking acts that will come to grief, not as foreseeing harm. And his gloss on 430 gives understanding the work of rebuilding once fate has destroyed, which is the book's own reconciliation of chapters 38 and 43."},{"id":"thread:household_or_renunciation","title":"Which life stands highest: the household or renunciation?","claim":"The Book of Virtue awards the first place twice. Chapter 3 says the texts' settled view sets the renouncer's greatness above every other excellence (21) and that those who have discerned the two states and taken on அறம் here stand highest in the world (23), and chapters 27 and 35 divide humanity into those who do தவம் or renounce completely and 'the rest', who are caught in desire or in the net (266, 348). Chapter 5 answers in the same register: the householder who lives by virtue gains nothing by going to the outside way (46), stands first among all who strive (47), has more endurance than those who do penance (48), and his life is the thing the word அறம் names (49). Both Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar read 46's புறத்தாறு as renunciation, so the collision between 46 and 342/348 is in the commentaries, not only in Pope; Parimelazhagar fits the two chapters together as a ladder (fame and heaven for householders in 50, release for full renouncers in 348), and reads 49's 'அஃதும் ... நன்று' as granting renunciation parity with the household.","grade":"partly_supported","links":[["k:21","k:48"],["k:21","k:49"],["k:23","k:49"],["k:23","k:46"],["k:46","k:348"],["k:47","k:266"],["k:266","k:46"],["k:342","k:46"],["k:266","k:348"],["k:348","k:50"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"The conflict softens on some readings: Manakkudavar takes 47's முயல்வார் as those who strive for wealth, so 47 does not rank the householder above ascetics, and he reads the 'அஃதும்' of 49 as the household life itself (good if the wife is not blameworthy), not as renunciation. Kalaignar reads 23 as choosing good over bad in general. The thread does not claim the Kural contradicts itself knowingly; it claims each chapter speaks in superlatives for its own subject and leaves the ranking to the commentators.","issue":"The claim misreports Parimelazhagar on 46 and 47. He glosses புறத்தாறு as 'இல்லை விட்டு வனத்துச் செல்லும் நிலை', the forest-dweller stage, and takes 47's முயல்வார் as those in the third stage, explicitly leaving out the full renouncers (முற்றத் துறந்தவர் விட்டமையின்). That is how he avoids the collision with 348, so the conflict between 46 and 342/348 rests on Manakkudavar's தவம் and on Pope, not on both commentators. The 23-46 link note concedes this, and the claim contradicts it."},{"id":"thread:renunciation_rests_on_providers","title":"Renunciation rests on those who have not renounced","claim":"The Kural says the ascetic's state is sustained by people who keep what he gives up. Having nothing is the nature of the vow (344), yet those who say 'we have given up what is desired' have no standing if the ploughmen fold their hands (1036), and 263 assumes that 'the others' hold possessions in order to provide for renouncers (துறந்தார்க்குத் துப்புரவு வேண்டி). On who ranks higher, the book splits by chapter: the chapters on the household and on giving grant the ascetic his own virtue of endurance and award more of it to the provider (48: நோற்பாரின் நோன்மை உடைத்து; 225: enduring hunger comes behind removing it), while 263, on both Parimelazhagar's and Manakkudavar's reading, concludes that தவம் exceeds giving (Manakkudavar: தானத்தினும் தவம் மிகுதியுடைத்து).","grade":"supported","links":[["k:263","k:1036"],["k:344","k:263"],["k:344","k:1036"],["k:263","k:48"],["k:48","k:225"],["k:263","k:225"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"263 is phrased as a question (மறந்தார்கொல்) and its ranking of தவம் over giving is the commentators' inference, not the verse's statement. 1036 is in the Book of Wealth and praises farmers; its point about ascetics is incidental. The thread claims a dependence the book notes, not a doctrine it argues.","issue":"Kalaignar reads 263 as a warning that the others should not forget their own austerity while helping renouncers, so the dependence and the ranking both rest on the Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar construal of மற்றையவர்கள் as householders who give in place of doing தவம். 344 states the vow's possessionlessness but says nothing about who sustains it; the dependence is carried by 263 and 1036 alone."},{"id":"thread:true_wealth","title":"Which wealth is the true wealth?","claim":"Four chapters crown a different thing as the wealth among wealths or the excellent wealth: grace (241, அருட்செல்வம் செல்வத்துள் செல்வம்), not wanting (363, விழுச்செல்வம்), learning (400, கேடில் விழுச்செல்வம்) and hearing (411, செவிச்செல்வம் ... செல்வத்துள் எல்லாம் தலை), while 751 says nothing but wealth is wealth (பொருளல்லது இல்லை பொருள்). Each verse argues against material wealth by a different defect: 241 by who can hold it (even the base have it), 400 by whether it can be lost (Parimelazhagar: heirs, thieves, the strong and kings cannot take learning), 411 on Parimelazhagar's reading by its impermanence and painful fruit, and 363 by having nothing above it in either world. The form is the same and the winners and the reasons differ, which suggests a rhetorical figure that each chapter fills for its own subject rather than a single ranking. 751 inverts the move: the fact that wealth is found among the base, which 241 counts against it, is exactly the power 751 counts in its favour.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:363","k:400"],["k:400","k:241"],["k:400","k:411"],["k:411","k:241"],["k:751","k:241"],["k:751","k:400"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"The verses do not rank the crowned wealths against one another; 400 and 411 sit in adjacent chapters and neither mentions the other. 751 is in the Book of Wealth and speaks of standing in the world, not of the goods the Book of Virtue values, so its absolute is in a different register. No commentator treats these as a contradiction.","issue":"The figure has further instances the claim does not count, notably 592 (உள்ளம் உடைமை உடைமை, with material wealth நில்லாது நீங்கி விடும, an impermanence argument) and the child as wealth in 63; these strengthen the 'rhetorical figure' reading. The reasons attributed to 400 and 411 are Parimelazhagar's, as the claim says; the verses themselves give no reason beyond கேடில் in 400."},{"id":"thread:wealth_on_conditions","title":"Wealth gives everything, on conditions the chapter does not state","claim":"Chapter 76 makes large claims for wealth: nothing else makes persons of no account into persons of account (751), grace itself is a child nursed by wealth (757), and those who have made bright wealth in full have virtue and pleasure together for the asking (760, ஒண்பொருள் ... ஏனை இரண்டும் ஒருங்கு). The conditions come from inside the same chapter and from later ones. 754 requires wealth that came by right means and without wrong, 755 tells one to let go wealth that does not come with grace and love, 1009 uses 760's own word ஒண்பொருள் for wealth amassed by setting love aside and ignoring virtue and says others will take it, and 1002 names nearly 751's thought, பொருளானாம் எல்லாம், as the miser's delusion when it leads to not giving. 973, on Parimelazhagar's and Manakkudavar's reading of the high and low seats as wealth and poverty, limits 751 further: wealth gets a person regarded without making them great. Read together, chapter 76's promises hold for wealth rightly earned and spent, and the book supplies that limit elsewhere rather than in the promising verses.","grade":"partly_supported","links":[["k:760","k:754"],["k:760","k:1002"],["k:1009","k:760"],["k:757","k:755"],["k:755","k:1009"],["k:1002","k:751"],["k:973","k:751"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"Parimelazhagar reads chapter 76 as the king's revenue (754's தீது as tyranny, 755's grace and love as the one-sixth tax), so on his reading 751-760 and the private miser of 1002 and 1009 concern different agents. 757's dependence of grace on wealth is not withdrawn anywhere; 755 only conditions which wealth may nurse it. The thread does not claim the Kural disparages wealth.","issue":"The title says the chapter does not state the conditions, but the claim itself draws them from 754 and 755 inside chapter 76, and on Parimelazhagar (and Pope's 'honestly acquired') 760's ஒண்பொருள் already means rightly earned wealth, so the 'promising verses' are not unconditioned. In 1002 Parimelazhagar takes பொருளானாம் எல்லாம் as correct knowledge (என்றறிந்து) and makes the delusion the later clinging, so 1002 grants 751's premise rather than limiting it."},{"id":"thread:giving_and_asking","title":"Asking: praised in one chapter, refused in the next","claim":"Chapter 106 (இரவு) and chapter 107 (இரவச்சம்) judge the same act from opposite sides, and the difference is not explained by who the giver is. Chapter 106 praises asking only under a condition on the giver: those worth asking (1051), a gift that comes without pain (1052), a heart without concealment that knows what is due (1053), one who would not conceal even in a dream (1054), where asking is itself 'like giving' (இரத்தலும் ஈதலே போலும்). Chapter 107 takes exactly those best givers, who give gladly without concealing and are dear as eyes, and says not asking is worth a crore more (1061); 1066 then says no purpose, not even water for a dying cow, lifts the disgrace from the tongue. 1066 repeats the move of 222 in the Book of Virtue, which calls taking bad 'even if it were called a good path' (நல்லாறு எனினும் கொளல் தீது): both verses refuse the best case that could be made for receiving. Parimelazhagar narrows the gap by reading chapter 106 as addressed to the destitute (நல்கூர்ந்தார்) and by glossing 1054's 'like giving' as merely not losing the honour one already has; on his reading even the best asking is neutral toward honour, never a gain.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:222","k:1051"],["k:222","k:1054"],["k:222","k:1061"],["k:1051","k:1061"],["k:1052","k:1066"],["k:1054","k:1061"],["k:1054","k:1066"],["k:1066","k:222"],["k:1066","k:1053"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"The verses themselves state no such restriction; the reconciliation is the commentator's. 1052 speaks of pleasure and 1066 of disgrace, so they conflict only if a disgraceful act cannot also be a pleasant one. Kalaignar reads கொளல் in 222 as taking goods by any fair means, which would make 222 broader than begging. This thread covers asking; the valuation of giving for itself (chapters 22-23) is not argued here beyond 222.","issue":"Chapter 106 already partly frames asking as a survival exception: Parimelazhagar on 1055 says those who ask by standing silently do so to preserve life and would otherwise die rather than lose honour, so the chapter's praise is less unconditional than the claim's opening suggests."},{"id":"thread:standing_before","title":"Standing before another: when is the humble posture honourable?","claim":"The book uses one posture, standing in want before someone (முன் நின்று, பின் சென்று நிலை), and awards it opposite values according to what is sought and before whom one stands. For learning the posture is honoured: on Parimelazhagar's and Kalaignar's reading of 395, those who learned while yearning 'like the poor before the rich' (உடையார்முன் இல்லார்போல் ஏக்கற்றும்) are foremost and those who would not stoop are lowest (கடையர்); 403 makes the unlearned 'very good' if they stay silent before the learned. For goods the same posture is degrading (1066), except that 1053 allows 'a certain beauty' in standing before a giver of unconcealing heart. 966 names the bad case exactly: standing behind those who scorn you (இகழ்வார்பின் சென்று நிலை) wins neither fame nor heaven. 729 turns the ranking word of 395 around: the one who learned yet fears the assembly of the good is lower than the unlearned (கல்லாதவரின் கடை), so the deference praised in 395 and 403 is right only for the one with nothing yet to say. The Book of Love then puts the honour of 966 out of reach: the dignity of not following one who has turned away is 'not something the lovesick know' (1255), and in 1298 the heart argues from honour the other way, that to scorn him would itself be the disgrace (எள்ளின் இளிவாம்).","grade":"partly_supported","links":[["k:395","k:1066"],["k:403","k:729"],["k:729","k:395"],["k:966","k:1053"],["k:1255","k:966"],["k:1298","k:1255"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"Pope reads 395 as the unlearned standing before the learned, not the learner before the teacher; the honour-of-learning point depends on the Tamil commentators' construal of ஏக்கற்றும் கற்றார். 1255 and 1298 are dramatised speeches, not teaching, and 1255-966 is also read in thread:love_overturns. The verses never state the rule 'judge the posture by its object'; it is the pattern of the cases.","issue":"The 'one posture' is the writer's construct. 403 and 729 are about speaking or staying silent in an assembly, and 1066 is about asking; none of the three is standing in want before someone. The inference that the deference of 395 is right 'only for the one with nothing yet to say' misreads 395: its learners stand before a teacher in order to learn, not because they are silent. In 1255 Parimelazhagar takes பின் செல்லல் as thinking of him without pause, not physically following."},{"id":"thread:sulk_in_measure","title":"The sulk: love's fruit, but only in measure and only if union follows","claim":"The last three chapters of the book treat the lovers' sulk (ஊடல்) as a good, and then fence it on three sides. 1109 lists sulk, making-up and union together as the fruit of love; 1326 ranks the sulk above union, as digesting is sweeter than eating; the book's final verse, 1330, calls the sulk love's pleasure. But 1330 adds the condition 'if they get to come together and embrace' (கூடி முயங்கப் பெறின்), so the sulk's sweetness rests on the union 1326 placed beneath it. 1302 gives the measure: salt in proportion, and a little too long is a little too much; 1307 names the pain inside the sulk, the fear that union will be delayed; 1304 shows the failed case, leaving the sulker unreconciled is cutting a withered creeper at the root. 1282 states the limit from the other direction: when love is full to a palmyra's measure, not a millet of sulking is wanted. Parimelazhagar reads the whole sequence through அளவு, measure: already at 1109 he glosses உணர்தல் as leaving the sulk 'knowing its measure', and at 1330 he makes the embrace depend on ending the sulk between one too ripe and one too young. 1329 and 1307, both the man's on his assignment, show the same delay feared in the moment and wished for afterwards.","grade":"partly_supported","links":[["k:1109","k:1302"],["k:1109","k:1326"],["k:1109","k:1330"],["k:1282","k:1302"],["k:1301","k:1302"],["k:1307","k:1330"],["k:1326","k:1330"],["k:1329","k:1307"],["k:1330","k:1302"],["k:1330","k:1304"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"Parimelazhagar reads 1304 as the wife telling the man to go reconcile his courtesans, a taunt rather than a rule, so its use as the sulk's failed case follows Kalaignar's plain reading. He also reads 1282 as the woman's own haste dressed as general advice, not a doctrine. The chapters do not say which of 1326 and 1330 is the final word; they stand side by side.","issue":"The claim reads 1307 as fear that union will be delayed, but Parimelazhagar glosses புணர்வது நீடுவது அன்றுகொல் as worry about whether the sweet union will last long (நீட்டிக்குமோ நீட்டியாதோ), and Kalaignar agrees. On that reading 1329's wish that the night be long (நீடுக) matches 1307's worry, so the 'feared then desired' contrast depends on a reading the claim does not name. The claim's sentence 'both the man's on his assignment' is also garbled."},{"id":"thread:who_should_fear","title":"Fear: the mark of the excellent and the conduct of the base","claim":"The book awards fear and fearlessness to opposite ranks of people. Fearlessness (அஞ்சாமை) is one of the four things a king must never lack (382), yet not fearing what should be feared is folly and fearing it is the work of the knowing (அறிவார் தொழில், 428), and the excellent fear evil deeds that the practised wrongdoer does not (201). At the other end, fear is the whole of the base person's good conduct (அச்சமே கீழ்களது ஆசாரம், 1075), the only check on a freedom 1073 compares to the gods'. Taken together, the verses make fear itself morally neutral: what ranks a person is its object. Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar both notice the collision at 428 and resolve it the same way, saying that since fearlessness was called a king's excellence, 428 names the things that must be feared (sin, blame, ruin), while Parimelazhagar reads 1075's fear as fear of the king's punishment.","grade":"partly_supported","links":[["k:201","k:428"],["k:201","k:1075"],["k:382","k:428"],["k:428","k:1075"],["k:1073","k:1075"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"This does not claim the book has one doctrine of fear; the reconciliation by object is the commentators', and 382 and 1075 do not themselves say what is or is not feared. 201's 'fear' is of a sin-induced intoxication on Parimelazhagar's reading, which is further from 1075 than the shared word suggests.","issue":"1075 does not make fear the whole of the base person's conduct or the only check on 1073's freedom: its second half (எச்சம் அவாவுண்டேல் உண்டாம் சிறிது) names desire for gain as a second motive, and Parimelazhagar glosses it as mostly fear, sometimes gain. Manakkudavar on 428 notices the tension with fearlessness (மேல் அஞ்சாமை வேண்டு மென்றாராயினும்) but does not list sin, blame and ruin; that list is Parimelazhagar's alone."},{"id":"thread:fort_and_fear","title":"Does a fort cure fear, or does fear cancel a fort?","claim":"Three verses in the Book of Wealth tie fortification and fear together in a loop. A fort is of value to those who, afraid, look to their own safety (அஞ்சித் தற்போற்றுபவர்க்கும் பொருள், 741); a king who has made no defence will be seized by fear when war comes and quickly perish (569); yet the fearful have no fort at all (அச்சமுடையார்க்கு அரணில்லை, 534). Read together they make the fort necessary but not sufficient: its absence produces fear, but fear in the mind makes it useless, which is how Parimelazhagar reads 534 (forests and hills give no profit to a frightened mind). This also gives the kingly fearlessness of 382 a material condition, since 569 shows even a king coming to fear without preparation.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:534","k:741"],["k:569","k:534"],["k:741","k:569"],["k:569","k:382"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"534's fort is a simile for the heedless losing their wealth, so its first half is not primarily a claim about statecraft; and 741's 'afraid' is, on Parimelazhagar's reading, the weaker party prudently taking refuge, not the cowardice 534 condemns. The loop is a reader's construction across chapters 54, 57 and 75, not something any verse states.","issue":"The extension to 382 is the reader's inference: 569 says a king without சிறை comes to fear, but nothing in 382 or 569 makes fearlessness depend on defences."},{"id":"thread:when_to_bow","title":"Bowing: good for all, yet shameful in some","claim":"The book commends பணிதல் without restriction in three places: bowing is good for everyone and a further wealth for the rich (125), whoever wants a lineage must be humble toward all (யார்க்கும் பணிவு, 960), and greatness always bows while smallness admires itself (978). Other verses then draw lines the general rule does not. 963 makes bowing the duty of abundance and standing tall the duty of want; 903 names a bowing that brings lasting shame, stooping before one's wife; and the honour chapter prefers being called ruined to living by following those who slight you (967), while 680 reports that rulers of small domains do bow to the great and take terms. Read together, the praised humility is the absence of pride in those who could be proud (Parimelazhagar glosses 125 and 978 by பெருமிதம் and தருக்கு), and bowing from fear or for a living is a different act the book does not praise: Parimelazhagar says of 680 that bowing is not the wish of the honourable, so the verse says 'they will accept' as worldly practice.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:963","k:125"],["k:978","k:125"],["k:960","k:963"],["k:978","k:963"],["k:903","k:978"],["k:680","k:967"],["k:963","k:680"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"The unifying distinction between humility and submission is the commentators' more than the text's; Parimelazhagar reads 978's என்றும் as 'even when distinction has come', which removes its tension with 963, and Manakkudavar takes 680 as plain prescription for the weak, not a reluctant report. 963's 'reduced circumstances' is poverty on both commentators' readings, so its clash with 680 holds only if personal honour extends to statecraft.","issue":"Parimelazhagar restricts 960's யார்க்கும் to those who deserve reverence (பணியப்படுவார் யாவர் மாட்டும்), so 'without restriction' holds only on the plain text there as well as at 978. The 680 and 967 links bear on the claim only indirectly."},{"id":"thread:birth_across_book","title":"Birth: cause of conduct, or its name?","claim":"The Kural's verses on lineage (குடி, பிறப்பு) pull in two directions. 951 and 952 make birth the source of conduct: integrity and shame are natural (இயல்பாக) only to the well-born, who do not slip in conduct, truth and shame; 502 and 793 treat family as a criterion to check before trusting a minister or making a friend, and 992 names birth as one of courtesy's two sources. 133 reverses the direction: having conduct is itself குடிமை, and a lapse becomes low birth, so that by 133 whoever slips has already left the class 952 describes; 958 sits between, where a lapse in affection makes people doubt a man's birth but leaves birth as the underlying fact; and 972 denies that birth settles distinction at all, leaving only deeds. The commentators divide along the same line: Parimelazhagar keeps 133 within varna (conduct raises or lowers one's standing within the rank one is born to) and reads 951 as saying the well-born need no teaching, while Kalaignar reads 951 and 952 backwards, as tests by which the well-born are recognised, which aligns them with 133 and 972.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:133","k:951"],["k:502","k:133"],["k:502","k:951"],["k:793","k:972"],["k:951","k:972"],["k:952","k:133"],["k:958","k:133"],["k:972","k:133"],["k:992","k:972"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"This does not claim the book rejects birth: most verses here (502, 793, 951, 952, 958, 992) treat it as real and informative, and only 972 denies it outright. Nor does 133 abolish the vocabulary of birth; it redefines it, and on Parimelazhagar's reading does so only within a fixed scheme of varna.","issue":"Parimelazhagar reads 972's எல்லா உயிர்க்கும் as all human lives (மக்களுயிர்) and its சிறப்பு as greatness or smallness from deeds, so 972 levels birth without naming lineage. Kalaignar also reads 502 backwards, as a test by which the well-born are recognised, which the claim does not mention."},{"id":"thread:strong_and_weak","title":"வலியார் and மெலியார்: one pair of words, three kinds of advice","claim":"The book gives opposite advice about the stronger and the weaker in the same words. In the Book of Virtue, 250 tells one who moves against the weaker (மெலியார்மேல் செல்லுமிடத்து) to remember standing before the stronger, and 302 says anger is bad where it cannot take effect and worst where it can, which Parimelazhagar glosses as anger against the stronger and the weaker. In the Book of Wealth, 861 tells a ruler to avoid opposing the stronger and to want enmity with the weaker without holding back (ஓம்பா மெலியார்மேல் மேக பகை), and the warrior's song in 771-772 prefers the spear that missed an elephant to the arrow that hit a hare, which Manakkudavar reads as sending the weak away to fight those who can resist. 493 adds that strength is not fixed: the weaker side wins if it knows the ground and guards itself. Parimelazhagar separates the audiences, taking chapter 31 as addressed to ascetics and 861 to a king; Kalaignar instead rewrites 861 as a wish to fight the strong, which removes the conflict by changing the verse.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:861","k:250"],["k:302","k:861"],["k:772","k:861"],["k:493","k:861"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"The conflict depends on reading 250 and 302 as rules for everyone; if chapter 31 addresses ascetics, as Parimelazhagar holds, the Book of Virtue and 861 speak to different people. 772 is a warrior's boast, not a counsel of policy, and 772 and 861 both favour fighting only when read as rules for choosing an opponent. This thread is a sibling of ethics_vs_statecraft, with its own verses.","issue":"The title's 'same words' holds only for 250 and 861. 302's stronger and weaker are Parimelazhagar's gloss on செல்லா இடம் and செல்லிடம், and 772 uses neither word. Parimelazhagar's gloss on 861 counts allies and sound counsel within strength, which makes வலியார் and மெலியார் a reckoning of resources. 773, beside 772 in the same chapter, praises helping an enemy who has fallen, which counts against reading chapter 78 as a mere wish to fight."},{"id":"thread:enmity_chosen_and_refused","title":"Enmity: chosen, bought, and refused","claim":"Chapter 87 (பகைமாட்சி) treats enmity as a thing to be chosen and kept: 861 tells the ruler to want enmity with the weaker, and 867 says the enmity of one who stays close and acts unfittingly must be acquired even at a price (கொடுத்தும் கொளல்வேண்டும் மன்ற). The next chapter opens, in 871, by calling enmity a thing without பண்பு that no one should want even in jest. 995 then says those who know how others feel keep பண்பு even inside enmity, so the quality enmity is said to lack can survive within it. The commentators settle 871 differently: Parimelazhagar narrows it to unworthy enmity (மாணாத பகை), which leaves room for 861 and 867, while Manakkudavar takes it as saying that taking up enmity is bad everywhere (எவ்விடத்தும் பகைகோடல் தீது).","grade":"partly_supported","links":[["k:861","k:871"],["k:871","k:867"],["k:995","k:871"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"The verses themselves do not distinguish worthy from unworthy enmity; that distinction is Parimelazhagar's. 995 is about conduct toward an enemy one already has and does not say whether enmity should be sought, so it qualifies 871's wording rather than contradicting 871's advice.","issue":"Only two links carry the claim. The 995 step trades on a shift in பண்பு: in 871 பண்பிலது means 'yielding no good' (Parimelazhagar: தீமை பயப்பது), while 995's பண்பு means courteous qualities. Saying the 'quality enmity is said to lack' survives inside it is wordplay, not the same quality. Also, neither commentator reads 867's அடுத்திருந்து as 'staying close': Parimelazhagar takes it as having begun a work, and Kalaignar reads 861 the opposite way (fight the strong)."},{"id":"thread:one_virtue_for_all","title":"Can one virtue stand for all of them?","claim":"Verse 33 asks for virtue to be done without pause wherever it can reach (ஓவாதே ... எல்லாம் செயல்). Two later verses each let a single virtue stand for the whole, and they pick different ones: 297 says that if one keeps up not lying, leaving the other virtues undone is good (அறம்பிற செய்யாமை செய்யாமை நன்று), and 321 answers the question 'what is virtuous action?' with not killing, since killing brings every other sin; 323 then ranks the two with not killing first. Whether 297 is really a claim of sufficiency is a dispute the commentators settle differently: Parimelazhagar reads it as advice to hold one virtue unfailingly rather than take on many and fail in some, and records that others construe it the other way; Manakkudavar is one of them, reading it as 'only if one keeps not-lying unbroken is doing other virtues good, otherwise harmful', on which 297 states a precondition, not a sufficiency, and the tension with 33 and 321 largely dissolves. Even the one virtue 297 singles out carries an exception, since 292 counts a falsehood yielding faultless good as truth, so its 'unbroken' not-lying is unbroken only under chapter 30's definition of truth by effect.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:33","k:297"],["k:321","k:297"],["k:292","k:297"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"This does not claim the book is incoherent: 323 is a deliberate ranking within the chapter, and on Parimelazhagar's reading of 321 அறவினை names the whole class of virtues rather than one deed. The superlative form of 300 and 323 is treated in thread:competing_supremes; this thread concerns sufficiency, not superlatives.","issue":"The commentary already raises this conflict itself: Parimelazhagar on 323 says the ranking was given because 297 and 300 had left in doubt which of the two virtues is higher. He also says of both 297 and 321 that the one virtue yields the fruit of all the others, which strengthens the sufficiency reading the claim treats as disputed."},{"id":"thread:good_results_as_licence","title":"Can a good result excuse the breach?","claim":"The Book of Virtue answers this differently for speech and for gain. For speech, 292 says yes: a falsehood that yields faultless good (புரைதீர்ந்த நன்மை பயக்கும் எனின்) belongs with truth, and this is what keeps 297's unbroken not-lying consistent with it. For gain, 113 says no: gain got by leaving fairness is to be dropped that very day even if it brings nothing but good (நன்றே தரினும்), while 177, three chapters later, refuses covetous gain by arguing from its outcome, that its fruit is not good when enjoyed, an argument 113's concession makes unnecessary. Parimelazhagar narrows the gap from both sides: on 113 he takes நன்றே தரினும் as a concession made for the sake of argument, since such gain in fact yields no good, and on 292 the good that licenses falsehood is itself அறம், such as saving a life from ruin or death; for him the outcome that counts is harm or benefit to others, never advantage to oneself.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:177","k:113"],["k:292","k:113"],["k:292","k:297"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"113 concerns wealth and 292 speech, and 292's புரைதீர்ந்த (free of fault) may already exclude the gain 113 forbids, so the verses are not a flat contradiction. The internal logic of chapter 30 is covered by thread:truth_by_effect; this thread adds the comparison with the treatment of gain.","issue":"Chapter 12 itself argues gain from outcome as well (116: கெடுவல்யான் என்பது அறிக ... நடுவொரீஇ அல்ல செயின்), and Parimelazhagar sums up 112 and 113 as fair wealth yielding good and the other yielding evil, so the contrast between outcome-free 113 and outcome-based 177 holds only on 113's literal concession."},{"id":"thread:trust_and_watching","title":"Trust once given, and the watching that goes on","claim":"Chapter 51 ends by forbidding two errors, trusting one not examined and doubting one examined and trusted (510, தெளிந்தான்கண் ஐயுறவு), after 501 has set out the fourfold test, and 517 draws the practical conclusion: having reasoned who will finish what by what means, leave the work with him (அவன்கண் விடல்). The next chapter and chapter 59 pull the other way: 514 says many who passed every test change through the nature of the work itself (வினைவகையான் வேறாகும் மாந்தர் பலர்), 520 tells the king to look into the one doing the work every day (நாடோறும் நாடுக), and 584 makes the king's own officers the first object of the spy's inquiry. Testing before appointment therefore does not end examination, and the book's rule against doubt has to coexist with daily inspection. The commentators reconcile them by distinguishing watching from visible suspicion: Parimelazhagar on 510 says the forbidden doubt is suspicion without a fault found, which the man notices and which slackens his work and invites enemies to detach him; on 520 the daily inquiry is to be made within the king himself so that the man's standing (உரிமை) is not destroyed; on 514 he recommends a middle-sized task to expose those who change after appointment. Manakkudavar reads 514 as a warning not to grow careless because one has already trusted.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:514","k:501"],["k:514","k:510"],["k:514","k:520"],["k:517","k:510"],["k:517","k:520"],["k:520","k:510"],["k:520","k:584"],["k:584","k:510"],["k:584","k:514"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"The verses themselves never say the inspection must be private; that is the commentators' addition. 584's spy examines kin and enemies as well as officers, so spying may be routine inquiry rather than the particular suspicion 510 forbids, and 520-510 is a tension only on the stricter reading.","issue":"584 lists வினைசெய்வார் first but gives no priority among the three groups. Manakkudavar takes 517 as aimed mainly at the army commander."},{"id":"thread:memory_sweet_or_burning","title":"Does remembering the beloved comfort or burn?","claim":"Chapter 121, the lament of those who remember, holds two opposite accounts of memory. 1201 and 1202, which Parimelazhagar gives to the man, make recollection a joy that does not fade even in thought (உள்ளினும் தீராப் பெருமகிழ்) and say no pain comes from thinking of the one you love. 1207, 1209 and 1296, spoken by the woman, make the same act the wound: even remembering burns her heart (உள்ளினும் உள்ளம் சுடும்), thinking hard on his lack of tenderness (அளியின்மை ஆற்ற நினைந்து) wastes her life, and thinking alone leaves her heart there only to eat her. The verses together locate the difference in what is remembered: Parimelazhagar glosses the object in 1201-1202 as the pleasure of past union and in 1209 and 1296 as his neglect and cruelties, and Manakkudavar reads 1202 as conditional, sweet only if no pain comes from the thinking. 1215 adds that for the woman even the sweetness of seeing him, awake or in a dream, lasts only for the moment of seeing, where the man's persists in thought.","grade":"partly_supported","links":[["k:1201","k:1207"],["k:1202","k:1207"],["k:1202","k:1209"],["k:1202","k:1296"],["k:1215","k:1201"],["k:1296","k:1207"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"These are dramatised speeches in different moods, not a doctrine the book states and then retracts. The gender contrast rests on Parimelazhagar's speaker assignments; Manakkudavar gives 1202 to the woman, which keeps the condition but dissolves the contrast. The 1202/1207 pair is already cited in thread:gender_asymmetry, which this thread does not replace since it treats memory rather than gender.","issue":"1296 is in chapter 130, not 121. Parimelazhagar takes the object remembered in 1207 to be the same past pleasure as in 1201 (அவ்வின்பத்தை மறத்தலறியேன்) and says separation burns despite the memory, so 1207 does not fit the claim's resolution by 'what is remembered'. On 1215 the 'only for the moment' reading is Manakkudavar's (அப்பொழுதைக்கு இன்பமாம்); Parimelazhagar reads the verse as saying waking and dream are equal for her, and the contrast with the man's lasting joy is the writer's construction."},{"id":"thread:body_as_testimony","title":"Who may call him cruel?","claim":"In separation the woman's body is read as public testimony, and the verses dispute what it testifies to and whether she wants it heard. In 1188 she complains that the town says only that she has grown pale and no one says he has left her. Yet in 1190 she calls the name of pallor good provided people do not broadcast his unkindness (நல்காமை தூற்றார் எனின்), and in 1236 what hurts her, more than her slackening bangles and shoulders, is hearing him called cruel (கொடியர் எனக்கூறல் நொந்து). The friend reads the same body the other way: in 1232 the pale weeping eyes seem to tell of the lover's unkindness, and in 1235 the wasted shoulders declare the cruelty of the cruel one; both commentators frame 1232, 1235 as the friend's blaming of the man to console her (இயற்பழித்தல்), and 1190, 1236 as her refusals. The asymmetry is exact: she herself uses கொடியார் கொடுமை of him in 1169, where the long nights outdo his cruelty, but will not accept the word from another mouth.","grade":"partly_supported","links":[["k:1188","k:1190"],["k:1190","k:1232"],["k:1190","k:1236"],["k:1235","k:1188"],["k:1235","k:1236"],["k:1236","k:1169"],["k:1169","k:1235"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"This is a consistency in one dramatised speaker across chapters 117, 119 and 124, not a teaching stated by the book. 1188 has no aligned commentary in the corpus, so its speaker is inferred from context; and 1169 is tied to 1235 by shared phrasing alone, so its weight in the thread is the word she uses, not a link of argument.","issue":"The claim that 'both commentators frame 1232, 1235 as the friend's blaming of the man (இயற்பழித்தல்)' is wrong for 1232. Manakkudavar has the friend warn that the tears are becoming gossip so that she will bear up, and Parimelazhagar has the friend console her (இனி நீ ஆற்றல் வேண்டும்). Only Manakkudavar on 1235 says இயற்பழித்து outright; Parimelazhagar's 1235 is 'how shall we hide it'. Manakkudavar takes தூற்றார் in 1190 as the townspeople, not the friend."},{"id":"thread:fortune_goddess","title":"The goddess of fortune and her elder sister: one figure, many doorsteps","claim":"The Kural personifies fortune as a goddess (செய்யாள் in 84, செய்யவள் in 167, திரு in 179, 482 and 920, தாமரையினாள் in 617) and misfortune as her elder sister (தவ்வை in 167, மாமுகடி in 617, முகடி in 936), and the figure recurs across both books with a different moral trigger each time. She settles in the home of the glad-faced host (84), leaves the envious man and hands him to her sister (167), seeks out those who know virtue and do not covet (179), lives in the labour of the unslothful while her sister lives in sloth (617), is tied down by timely action (482), has already abandoned those who keep two-minded women, drink and dice (920), and in 936 gambling is her sister under another name, which Parimelazhagar explains by saying the sister's real name is too ill-omened to speak. 594 gives the same personification without the goddess: ஆக்கம் asks the way to the man of unwavering energy. Taken together, the verses show the goddess is a portable image that each chapter lends to its own virtue, generosity, non-envy, non-coveting, exertion and timing, not a doctrine about what in fact draws wealth; and 371 undercuts the residence rule of 617 by making sloth and effort themselves products of fate.","grade":"partly_supported","links":[["k:84","k:167"],["k:84","k:617"],["k:167","k:617"],["k:179","k:167"],["k:179","k:594"],["k:482","k:617"],["k:594","k:617"],["k:920","k:617"],["k:920","k:936"],["k:936","k:167"],["k:936","k:617"],["k:371","k:617"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"The thread claims a shared figure, not a theology: Kalaignar reads the two goddesses of 617 as mere names for the industrious and the idle. The verses never rank the triggers against each other, and the fate question raised by 371 belongs to thread:fate_and_agency. The first word of 594 is truncated in this edition (க்கம் for ஆக்கம்).","issue":"The recurring figure is well documented, but the conclusion that it is 'a portable image ... not a doctrine about what in fact draws wealth' goes beyond the verses. Having many triggers does not make them non-causal, and Parimelazhagar treats 617 as stating the cause (ஏது) of fortune and misfortune. In 482 he glosses திரு simply as செல்வம் with no goddess, so counting it among the goddess's names is a reading."},{"id":"thread:ill_gotten_gain","title":"Wrongly got gain: refused because it fails, and refused even if it succeeds","claim":"The Kural refuses wealth got by wrong means on two different grounds that sit side by side. One set argues from outcome: gain by coveting turns out badly when enjoyed (177), stolen gain perishes while seeming to grow (283), what is taken with others' tears leaves with tears (659), wealth made by deceit is water in an unfired pot (660), and wrongdoing to escape poverty only renews it (205). The other set concedes that the gain is real and good and still rules it out, using a fixed concessive form: நன்றே தரினும் (113), நன்றாகும் ஆக்கம் பெரிதெனினும் (328), செல்வம் பெறினும் (311), அடுக்கிய கோடி பெறினும் (954), வென்றிடினும் (931), and 657 ranks the utter poverty of the worthy above blame-laden wealth. The concessive verses make the prohibition independent of the prediction, so it stands where 177 or 205 might fail. The Book of Wealth carries the rule into acquisition itself: the promise that wealth yields virtue and pleasure (754, 760) is restricted to wealth that came திறனறிந்து தீதின்றி, 755 orders wealth without grace and love to be let go with the same verb விடல் as 113, and 1009 says such wealth is taken by others.","grade":"partly_supported","links":[["k:177","k:113"],["k:205","k:657"],["k:113","k:328"],["k:311","k:113"],["k:328","k:311"],["k:931","k:113"],["k:113","k:657"],["k:328","k:657"],["k:954","k:311"],["k:755","k:113"],["k:283","k:659"],["k:283","k:660"],["k:754","k:659"],["k:755","k:660"],["k:760","k:754"],["k:1009","k:760"],["k:755","k:1009"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"The two grounds do not contradict each other and the book never says which is primary. On Parimelazhagar, 311 concerns yogic powers and 328 sacrificial killing by renunciants, so reading them as general rules about wealth follows Pope and Kalaignar rather than his gloss. 754 and 760 are addressed to the king.","issue":"931 is misfiled in the concessive set: it grants வென்றிடினும் but then argues from the outcome (வென்றதூஉம் தூண்டிற்பொன் மீன்விழுங்கி அற்று), so it does not show the prohibition holding apart from a prediction. 1009 sits in the chapter on wealth that does no good (நன்றியில்செல்வம்), and Parimelazhagar reads it as the miser's unspent hoard, not wrongly acquired wealth; only Manakkudavar and Kalaignar put its அன்பொரீஇ ... அறநோக்காது at the stage of acquisition. 954's குன்றுவ is any degrading act, not a way of getting wealth."},{"id":"thread:wealth_and_holder","title":"Owning is not having: wealth measured by its holder","claim":"In both books the Kural denies that holding goods amounts to having them unless the holder has some further quality, and it states this in one recurring form, 'whatever they have, they have nothing': a wife without worth (53, உள்ளதென் இல்லவள் மாணாக்கடை), a person without understanding (430, என்னுடையரேனும் இலர்; 841 calls want of understanding இன்மையுள் இன்மை), one without energy (591, உடையது உடையரோ), one who neither gives nor enjoys (1005, கோடியுண்டாயினும் இல்). The same judgment is pushed further into an inversion of wealth over poverty when the holder is wrong: wealth with the unlearned hurts more than poverty with the good (408), having hurts more than not having under an unjust king (558), great wealth with the discourteous is milk spoiled by its vessel (1000), with the grim-faced it is as if seen by a ghost (565), with a fool it feeds strangers while kin starve (837). The paired images fix where the fault lies: 660 blames the making (unfired pot) and 1000 the holder (bad vessel, good milk); 216 and 1008 put the same fruiting tree in the same village and let the holder's character decide whether it feeds or poisons.","grade":"partly_supported","links":[["k:53","k:430"],["k:430","k:591"],["k:430","k:841"],["k:1005","k:430"],["k:591","k:1005"],["k:408","k:558"],["k:408","k:1000"],["k:408","k:837"],["k:837","k:1000"],["k:1000","k:1008"],["k:1008","k:216"],["k:660","k:1000"],["k:565","k:1000"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"This is a claim about the condition under which பொருள் counts as உடைமை, not about which thing deserves the name செல்வம் (thread:true_wealth). The chapter on making wealth pulls the other way, 751 and 752 saying wealth alone gives standing and everyone honours the rich, though 973 and 1002 qualify those in turn. Parimelazhagar grounds 430 and 591 alike in the holder's inability to keep or remake wealth, which is narrower than the verses' wording.","issue":"558 does not fit 'when the holder is wrong': its condition is the unjust king (முறைசெய்யா மன்னவன்), not any defect in the one who has the wealth. 53 likewise puts the deciding quality in the wife, not the holder. 216 and 1008 are a counter-image of two different trees (பயன்மரம், நச்சு மரம்), not 'the same fruiting tree'."},{"id":"thread:foresight_as_understanding","title":"Understanding as foresight, and its limits","claim":"In the Book of Wealth அறிவு is repeatedly defined as knowing and guarding against what is coming: 427 makes knowing what will be the mark of the wise, 429 promises them no shaking affliction, and 435 and 535 give the failure two outcomes, sudden ruin and late regret. The book then qualifies this in three directions: the foresight can be obtained through others the ruler keeps (442), understanding also dissolves trouble that has already come (622), and 372 says a fate of loss makes a person foolish, which Parimelazhagar extends to one who has every understanding. Read together, foresight is the working core of understanding in statecraft, but neither wholly one's own nor proof against fate.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:427","k:429"],["k:429","k:435"],["k:435","k:535"],["k:429","k:442"],["k:622","k:429"],["k:372","k:429"],["k:427","k:372"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"The verses do not say foresight is the whole of அறிவு; elsewhere the term is tested morally (203, 315) and by seeing the real (355). The pull between 372 and 429 belongs with the fate-and-agency conflict and is not resolved in the verses.","issue":"Only 427 and 429 tie the foresight to அறிவு; 435 and 535 speak of guarding before (வருமுன்னர், முன்னுற) without the word, and Parimelazhagar reads them as guarding against faults and against enemies exploiting a lapse. Parimelazhagar also records an alternative reading of 427's ஆவதறிவார் as 'know what is good for them'."},{"id":"thread:world_as_measure","title":"Does understanding follow the world or test it?","claim":"Several verses make accord with the world part of what understanding is: the much-studied who cannot move with the world lack understanding (140), to live as the world lives is அறிவு (426), and even after knowing a method one should act knowing how the world goes (637). 850 extends this from conduct to belief: one who denies what the world affirms is set down as a demon. Yet 423, in the same chapter as 426, defines understanding as finding the true substance of a thing whoever says it. Parimelazhagar narrows 'the world' in 140 and 850 to the high-minded many and reads 426 as advice to a king, and Kalaignar's 'shown by evidence' moves 850 toward 423.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:140","k:426"],["k:637","k:140"],["k:637","k:426"],["k:426","k:850"],["k:850","k:140"],["k:850","k:423"],["k:214","k:140"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"On Parimelazhagar's reading 214 also makes knowing the way of the world the test of being alive, but Manakkudavar takes ஒத்தது there as ஒப்புரவு, so it is weak support. The 850-423 conflict is already noted under knowing_and_learning; this thread concerns the world as a standard, not the speaker's standing.","issue":"The narrowing of 'world' to the high-minded is not Parimelazhagar's alone: Kalaignar also glosses 140 and 426 by உயர்ந்தோர். Parimelazhagar on 637 grounds the rule in avoiding blame (செய்யின் அது பழிக்கும்), which is prudential rather than epistemic."},{"id":"thread:measure_of_the_sulk","title":"The sulk: love's best part, in measure","claim":"The Book of Love keeps returning to ஊடல், and its verses raise and then bound it. 1109 lists sulking with reconciliation and union as love's rewards; 1326 ranks sulking above union, as digestion above eating; 1330, the book's last verse, calls it love's pleasure but places its own pleasure in the embrace that follows. 1302 and 1306 make it a matter of degree, salt that seasons and spoils if prolonged, fruit unripe or overripe without it, and 1282 says that when love is full not a millet-seed of sulking is wanted. Together they treat the sulk as valuable only as a measured step toward union, which Parimelazhagar makes explicit in glossing 1109 and 1330 as ending it at the right point.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:1109","k:1302"],["k:1109","k:1326"],["k:1326","k:1330"],["k:1330","k:1302"],["k:1302","k:1306"],["k:1306","k:1330"],["k:1282","k:1302"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"The verses come from different speakers and situations (1282 is the woman's impatience voiced as general advice, per Parimelazhagar), so this is a pattern across voices, not one doctrine. Pope's translation of 1109 as lust is a translator's reading.","issue":"'1330, the book's last verse' holds only in Parimelazhagar's order; Manakkudavar closes the Book of Love at 1323. The commentators assign கனி and கருக்காய் in 1306 to துனி and புலவி differently: Parimelazhagar takes கனி as rich ripeness, not spoilage."},{"id":"thread:love_and_liquor","title":"Love measured against liquor","claim":"The Book of Love uses liquor, which chapter 93 condemns, as its standard comparison for love, and uses it both ways. In 1090, 1201 and 1281 love surpasses toddy: liquor delights only when drunk, love at a look or a thought, from first sight (1090) through separation (1201) to the woman's longing (1281). In 1145 and 1288 love instead works like drink: wanted more with each exposure, and in 1288 craved though it does shameful harm, which Parimelazhagar spells out as loss of shame, restraint, conduct and awareness, the very charges laid against drink in chapter 93.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:1090","k:1281"],["k:1201","k:1090"],["k:1201","k:1281"],["k:1145","k:1281"],["k:1281","k:1288"],["k:1288","k:1145"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"The chapter 93 verses are not in this packet; the link to them rests on Parimelazhagar's gloss of 1288. Speakers differ by commentator for 1090, 1145 and 1288, so the thread does not claim a single voice holds both views.","issue":"In 1145 the thing that grows sweeter is love's becoming rumour (Parimelazhagar: காமம் அலராந்தோறும் இனிது), not love at each meeting, so 'wanted more with each exposure' means public exposure."},{"id":"thread:deeds_come_back","title":"How does a harmful deed return to the doer?","claim":"Across the chapters on fearing evil deeds, not harming, not coveting, not stealing and clean action, the return of harm is described in several distinct ways rather than one. Its inescapability is spatial in 207 and 208 (the enmity of deeds follows and kills, ruin stays at the foot like a shadow, both with வீயாது), and 313 sharpens it with உய்யா: an enemy can be survived, one's own hurtful act cannot. It is temporal in 319 (forenoon harm comes back in the afternoon, தாமே, of itself), an agent's act in 204 (அறம் plots the plotter's ruin), and a near-tautology of desire in 206 and 320 (whoever wants no pain causes none). A separate figure, the ripening, places the harm at the harvest and not at the getting: coveted gain in 177, theft in 284, and forbidden deeds that succeed in 658 all go bad at விளைவு, and 659 extends the return to wealth taken with tears, while adding that rightly got wealth pays later even if lost.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:207","k:208"],["k:313","k:207"],["k:319","k:207"],["k:204","k:319"],["k:206","k:320"],["k:177","k:284"],["k:658","k:177"],["k:659","k:319"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"The thread describes how the return is pictured, not whether the book thinks it always happens; 169 concedes that the crooked prosper and the upright fall, which thread:justice_of_the_world takes up. Who or what returns the harm (அறம் in 204, nobody in 319, Parimelazhagar's அறக்கடவுள் and pāvam in his glosses) is left unsettled by the verses.","issue":"658 does not use விளைவு; its timing comes only from முடிந்தாலும் பீழை தரும் (even when the deed succeeds), so it belongs to the ripening figure by sense rather than by wording. 284's harvest is of love of stealing (களவின்கண் கன்றிய காதல்), not of stolen gain as such."},{"id":"thread:checklist_for_action","title":"One checklist for action, from the king's deed to the minister's definition","claim":"A short list of conditions for successful action recurs across five Porul chapters and changes role as it moves. In 483 means and time (கருவி, காலம்) make any 'hard deed' (அருவினை) possible; 631 reuses the same three words but turns them into the definition of a minister, adding method (செய்கை); 675 expands them into a five-item rule for anyone acting (resources, means, time, deed, place); and 516, in the chapter on employing people, keeps deed and time and adds the one item the others lack, the doer. A parallel pair, 461 and 676, shares the closing command (சூழ்ந்து / பார்த்துச் செயல்) and the yield as its last term, but 461 counts loss against gain while 676 weighs the course of the work, its completion and obstacles. Read together, the minister's defining skill is the general checklist for action, and the book adjusts the list to the chapter it sits in.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:483","k:675"],["k:631","k:483"],["k:631","k:675"],["k:516","k:675"],["k:461","k:676"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"This is a claim about recurring vocabulary and list structure, not that the lists are meant as one doctrine; Parimelazhagar glosses the items differently in each place (in 675 each term covers both one's own side and the enemy's). It does not cover the separate theme of execution failing after sound planning (640, 664).","issue":"The 461 and 676 pair shares none of the கருவி/காலம் vocabulary, so it is a second, parallel list rather than part of the same checklist, and its closing verbs differ (சூழ்ந்து, பார்த்து). Parimelazhagar reads 483's actor as the king (அரசரால்)."},{"id":"thread:one_virtue_alone","title":"Can a single virtue stand without the rest?","claim":"Several verses isolate one restraint and say it keeps its value even when the rest of virtue is absent, using a shared 'even if he does wrong' frame: not desiring another's wife in 150 (அறன்வரையான் அல்ல செயினும்) and not backbiting in 181 (அறங்கூறான் அல்ல செயினும்), with 142 ranking the adulterer lowest among those outside virtue. 297 goes further for not lying, saying the other virtues may be left undone, and 296 gives the reason: not lying yields all the virtues by itself. Yet 321 answers 'what is virtuous action?' with not killing, and 33 tells one to do virtue everywhere and without break. Because the book makes this move for different virtues, the verses read best as emphasis on each chapter's virtue, and Parimelazhagar on 150 and 181 says only that the one quality 'stands out' despite the faults, not that it suffices.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:150","k:181"],["k:142","k:150"],["k:150","k:297"],["k:181","k:297"],["k:296","k:297"],["k:321","k:297"],["k:33","k:297"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"Only 296-297 claim sufficiency; 150 and 181 claim only that the one abstention is still good. Parimelazhagar reconciles 297 with 33 by reading it as advice to keep one virtue unfailingly rather than fail at many, and 323 itself ranks not killing above not lying (see thread:competing_supremes and thread:truth_by_effect).","issue":"Parimelazhagar narrows 142's அறன்கடை நின்றார் to those in sin through desire, so it ranks adulterers among lovers gone wrong rather than among all who stand outside virtue. Kalaignar reads 150 as a comparison (desiring another's wife is worse than straying from virtue), not as the 'even if' frame."},{"id":"thread:virtue_and_wealth_reversed","title":"Does virtue yield wealth, or wealth yield virtue?","claim":"Book 1 says virtue yields distinction and wealth (31, சிறப்பு ஈனும் செல்வமும் ஈனும்), and Book 2 turns the same construction round: wealth that came by knowing the means and without wrong yields virtue and pleasure (754, அறன்ஈனும் இன்பமும் ஈனும்), and for those who amass bright wealth the other two come easily together (760). The reversal holds only with a condition on how the wealth was got: 754's தீதின்றி fits wealth into 39's rule that only pleasure through virtue is pleasure, 659 says what is taken with tears leaves with tears, and 1009 uses 760's own word ஒண்பொருள் for wealth amassed by ignoring virtue, which others carry off. So the two books do not rank the ends differently; they make the causal arrow run both ways and let the manner of acquisition decide which way it runs.","grade":"partly_supported","links":[["k:31","k:754"],["k:754","k:39"],["k:760","k:754"],["k:1009","k:760"],["k:754","k:659"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"The condition is explicit in 754 but only read into 760 by Parimelazhagar, who glosses ஒண்பொருள் there as wealth that comes by the right way; on its face 760 promises virtue to abundance as such. Parimelazhagar also reads 754 as a king's revenue raised without tyranny, so its scope may be royal wealth only.","issue":"Parimelazhagar reads 31's சிறப்பு as liberation and its செல்வம் as heaven and similar rewards (துறக்கம் முதலிய செல்வம்), so on his reading 31 is not about worldly wealth and the 'reversal' with 754 is only verbal. 1009's fault is not how the wealth was got but its hoarding: Parimelazhagar glosses அறநோக்காது as not giving to the poor and அன்பொரீஇ as dropping love so as not to give. 1009 therefore shows use, not manner of acquisition, deciding the outcome, and its own ஒண்பொருள் cuts against reading 760's ஒண்பொருள் as 'rightly got'."},{"id":"thread:sulk_on_conditions","title":"Is the lovers' sulk a pleasure, and on what terms?","claim":"The Book of Love praises the sulk in rising terms: one of love's rewards (1109), sweeter than union (1326), beyond heaven (1323), love's very pleasure (1330). Almost every praise is hedged by a condition stated nearby. It must be measured like salt and spoils if prolonged (1302), only a small துனி is wanted (1322), yet love without any quarrel is fruit at the wrong stage (1306). It is sweet only if the embrace follows (1330, கூடி முயங்கப் பெறின்), the doubt about that sequel is its own pain (1307), and it is sweet only with one who loves (1309). 1326 puts the sulk above union, and 1330 makes its sweetness depend on union.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:1109","k:1302"],["k:1282","k:1302"],["k:1302","k:1306"],["k:1322","k:1306"],["k:1326","k:1330"],["k:1307","k:1330"],["k:1330","k:1302"],["k:1309","k:1323"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"These are speeches by characters, not a treatise; 1282, which says no sulk at all is wanted when love is full, is on Parimelazhagar's reading the woman's impatience dressed as advice. The conditions limit the praise but are never offered as a retraction of it.","issue":"1322 does not say that 'only a small துனி is wanted'. It says the small துனி that a sulk brings leads to greatness even if his grace droops; 'only' comes from pairing it with Parimelazhagar's துனி/புலவி distinction on 1306. Manakkudavar reads 1322 as the sulk being pleasant even without union, which cuts against the claim that union is the condition."},{"id":"thread:sulk_without_fault","title":"A sulk that needs no offence","claim":"In chapter 132 the man lists sulks with thinner and thinner grounds: a wreath (1313), his attempt to appease her (1319), a silent look (1320), each drawing anger (காயும்). Only 1311 states a ground, other women's eyes on him. Chapter 133 then has both speakers concede there is no fault: she says he has none, yet his tenderness can bring on a sulk (1321, இல்லை தவறவர்க்கு), and he says that though men are faultless, being kept from her arms has its own savour (1325, தவறிலர் ஆயினும்); on Parimelazhagar's reading 1305 says the same. Read together, the book detaches the sulk from wrongdoing and treats the groundless sulk of chapter 132 as a pleasure in chapter 133.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:1305","k:1321"],["k:1321","k:1325"],["k:1305","k:1325"],["k:1313","k:1321"],["k:1325","k:1319"],["k:1321","k:1311"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"Manakkudavar reads 1305's நல்லவர் as the other women, and Parimelazhagar explains 1321 by her thought that others too may receive his tenderness, so jealousy survives with its ground removed. Real offence is not absent from these chapters: 1311 names him பரத்த.","issue":"'Only 1311 states a ground' undersells chapter 132: in 1313, 1319 and 1320 she also names a ground, always another woman (ஒருத்தியைக் காட்டிய, பிறர்க்கும், யாருள்ளி). The difference is that the man calls those grounds baseless, not that she gives none."},{"id":"thread:living_on_in_separation","title":"Can one live through separation, and why does she?","claim":"Several speeches say separation cannot be survived: parting is death to his life (1124), news of his quick return is for those still alive then (1151), and on Parimelazhagar's reading 1160's 'many endure the parting and live on' is irony. 1198 turns survival into a charge: none are harder (வன்கண்) than those who live on without a kind word, the word 1156 had applied to him for announcing the parting. Yet the same speaker is later alive and gives reasons: she lives by seeing him in dreams (1213) and by longing for his return (1263), the very return 1151 dismissed. Taken together, the verses deny that she can survive, then accuse her of surviving, then name what keeps her alive.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:1124","k:1151"],["k:1151","k:1160"],["k:1160","k:1198"],["k:1213","k:1198"],["k:1151","k:1263"],["k:1198","k:1156"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"This is a dramatic sequence, not a teaching about grief. Speakers vary by commentator (Manakkudavar gives 1151 to the woman), and Parimelazhagar takes 1213 as said to reassure the friend, so its explanation may be partly for show.","issue":"1124 is a simile (சாதல் அதற்கன்னள்) spoken by the man at a brief daytime parting on Parimelazhagar's reading, so it says separation feels like death more than that it cannot be survived. On Parimelazhagar, 1198 is the woman's self-reproach (யான் வன்கண்ணேனாகலின்), which is what lets it count as the charge against her."},{"id":"thread:body_as_witness","title":"The body testifies; who decides what it proves?","claim":"In the separation chapters her body speaks before she does: slipping bangles broadcast his leaving (1157, தூற்றா), shoulders announce it (1233), the bangles knew it before she did (1277), and she has been pale seven days though he left yesterday (1278; also 1185). All parties accept this evidence, but they dispute its verdict. The town reads the pallor as news about her and never says he left her (1188); the friend reads the wasted shoulders as declaring the cruel one's cruelty (1235); and she refuses that inference, pained to hear him called cruel (1236).","grade":"supported","links":[["k:1157","k:1235"],["k:1233","k:1157"],["k:1277","k:1157"],["k:1278","k:1277"],["k:1185","k:1277"],["k:1235","k:1188"],["k:1235","k:1236"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"Speaker assignments differ (Parimelazhagar gives 1233 and 1235 to the friend, Manakkudavar to the woman), and Parimelazhagar's 'before' in 1277 depends on reading the parting as one of mind while they are still together. She herself uses கொடியார் கொடுமை of him in 1169, so her refusal in 1236 is of the charge from another's mouth.","issue":"Parimelazhagar takes 1157's bangles as telling the friend (உனக்கு அறிவியாவோ), not broadcasting to the town, and he reads 1185 as recalling an earlier departure, so the public and immediate force the claim gives these two rests on the plain wording."},{"id":"thread:limits_of_friendship","title":"What can friendship survive?","claim":"The book says those devoted to friendship do not leave a friend once made (791, நட்டபின் வீடில்லை) and that the world loves those who do not give up old friends (809), yet it calls leaving a fool's friendship gain (797) and orders the unfit dropped even at a price (800, ஒன்றீத்தும் ஒருவுக). It also says the bond grows with practice (783) yet does not need it (785), and on Manakkudavar's reading 785's quick bond sits against 792's demand to examine again and again. The growth question is settled by character, not time: the bond with பண்புடையார் deepens with practice (783), while with பண்பிலார் shrinking is better than growing (811), the waning that 782 observes in fools' friendship. Parimelazhagar supplies the limit on leaving: exit is for a friendship taken up without knowing (800).","grade":"partly_supported","links":[["k:791","k:797"],["k:791","k:800"],["k:809","k:791"],["k:783","k:785"],["k:785","k:792"],["k:783","k:811"],["k:782","k:811"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"On Parimelazhagar's reading 785 only says feeling, more than contact, makes the bond, and there is no conflict with 783 or 792. Old friends who harm you are kept (807), which he treats as a separate case.","issue":"The claim says the growth question is 'settled by character, not time', but 783 against 785 is about whether contact is needed to make the bond (புணர்ச்சி பழகுதல் வேண்டா), which 811's பண்பிலார் does not address; character settles only whether growth is desirable. 783 itself speaks of growing delight (நயம்), not of a bond that needs practice."},{"id":"thread:friend_in_ruin","title":"The friend is judged at the hour of ruin","claim":"In all three books one moment decides a friendship: what the friend does when the other falls. The Book of Virtue says never abandon those who were a support in sorrow (106) and that their help is remembered through seven births (107); chapter 79 defines friendship as sharing the suffering in ruin (787) and acting there and then like a hand catching a slipping garment (788). The same measure condemns: the deserter's bond burns the heart even at death (799), those who fail at the crisis are an untrained horse worse than solitude (814, 798's ஆற்றறுப்பார்), and friends who give no safety are better never acquired (815). In the Book of Love, 1293 turns the saying that the ruined have no friends against the speaker's own heart, a deserter she cannot decline.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:106","k:806"],["k:107","k:799"],["k:787","k:799"],["k:814","k:788"],["k:798","k:814"],["k:815","k:106"],["k:1293","k:787"],["k:1293","k:798"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"This is one standard among several: other verses make rebuke (784, 795) or inner feeling (785, 786) the mark of friendship. 1293 is a lover's dramatised reproach quoting a saying, not a teaching about friends.","issue":""},{"id":"thread:learning_judged_by_conduct","title":"Learning that conduct cancels","claim":"391 asks for two things in order: learn without flaw, then stand in keeping with it. Several verses across the Books of Virtue and Wealth enforce the second half by withdrawing the status of the learned from those who skip it: whoever has learned much but cannot move in accord with the world is unlearned and without understanding (140); whoever has read, understood and taught but is not restrained is the greatest fool (834); wide understanding is worth nothing if one covets (175); and restraint after learning is what virtue itself seeks out (130). In statecraft and medicine the same rule becomes professional: knowing the method from the treatises, act knowing the world (637), and the learned physician must weigh this patient, this illness, this time (949).","grade":"supported","links":[["k:391","k:140"],["k:391","k:834"],["k:175","k:834"],["k:834","k:130"],["k:637","k:140"],["k:949","k:637"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"The verses do not claim that study produces good conduct; 823 says study rarely makes the unworthy good at heart, which is why 391 has to command conduct separately. The thread does not cover the question of where understanding comes from, treated in thread:knowing_and_learning.","issue":"637 and 949 are about fitting treatise knowledge to circumstances, not about conduct cancelling learning, so 'the same rule becomes professional' stretches the thread's rule. Parimelazhagar reads மாணார் in 823 as enemies who do not turn friend through study, not as the unworthy in general, so the limits lean on a broader reading than the chapter gives."},{"id":"thread:learning_must_be_voiced","title":"Learning that is not spoken","claim":"The book ranks learned people by whether they can deliver their learning before an audience, and is harsh to those who cannot. Those who cannot unfold what they learned are scentless flowers (650); those who cannot speak tellingly in a good assembly are useless however much they learned (728); and those who learned yet fear the assembly are lower than the unlearned (729), using the very word கடை that 395 gives the unlearned. Silence is therefore graded by who keeps it: it is the unlearned person's merit before the learned (403) and the learned person's disgrace. The positive title follows the same measure: 'learned among the learned' is whoever makes learning go home before the learned (722), and the envoy is judged the same way among treatise-holders and in a hostile court (683, 686, 724).","grade":"supported","links":[["k:728","k:650"],["k:722","k:650"],["k:729","k:395"],["k:403","k:729"],["k:683","k:722"],["k:686","k:724"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"These verses concern speech before assemblies and courts, mostly in the Book of Wealth's chapters on speech and assemblies; they do not say learning has no private worth (398 makes it a protection across births). 720 adds that an unfit audience wastes even good speech.","issue":"683 concerns the envoy's mastery of statecraft treatises among ministers (நூலாருள் நூல்வல்லன்), not delivering speech, so it adds little to the claim about voicing. Parimelazhagar puts the failure in 728 and 729 down to fear (அச்சத்தான்), which separates the chapter on the assembly from 650's plain inability to expound."},{"id":"thread:hoard_and_store","title":"Where is wealth kept safe?","claim":"Across the Book of Virtue and the Book of Wealth, a run of verses turns the hoarder's own logic of keeping against him. Feeding the destitute is the place to deposit wealth (226, வைப்புழி; Parimelazhagar: what is so given comes back to help the giver), while those who keep what they own lose it (228, வைத்திழக்கும்), eating alone to fill one's store is worse than begging (229), the man who does not even use his heap is as dead (1001), and wealth withheld from the destitute ages unused like a beauty growing old alone (1007). The positive side recurs in three chapters: shared food keeps hunger from the sharer (227), keeps his line from failing (44), and gain belongs only to those who, like the crow, call others before eating (527).","grade":"supported","links":[["k:226","k:228"],["k:1007","k:226"],["k:228","k:1001"],["k:1001","k:229"],["k:229","k:527"],["k:527","k:227"],["k:44","k:227"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"The book does not rest giving on return alone: 228 names giving's own joy and 222 calls giving good even if there were no higher world. 82 and 229 condemn the solitary meal by comparison rather than by consequence, and the protective results in 44, 227 and 527 are asserted, not argued.","issue":"Only 226 and 228 turn the hoarder's logic of keeping (வை-) against him in their own words; 1001 and 1007 condemn wealth left unused rather than wealth lost by being kept. 82, cited only in the limits, concerns hospitality to a guest and does not bear on storing wealth."},{"id":"thread:manner_of_giving","title":"Giving and the manner that goes with it","claim":"The book rarely praises giving bare: it pairs it with a glad face, sweet words, no scorn or no anger, and once ranks the manner above the gift, since 92 says a glad face and sweet speech are better than giving with a glad heart. 953 lists smile, giving, sweet words and not scorning flatly as marks of a good lineage (Parimelazhagar: the way one meets the poor who come), and 1057 shows why scorn matters, since the asker rejoices at giving without contempt, while 224 has the giver's own unease end only at the asker's glad face. In the Book of Wealth the same pair becomes an instrument: giving with sweet words wins the world for a king (387) and surrounds a man with kin (525), and 526 swaps sweet speech for not cherishing anger; Parimelazhagar on 525 matches the pair to the northern texts' dana and sama.","grade":"partly_supported","links":[["k:387","k:92"],["k:953","k:92"],["k:953","k:1057"],["k:224","k:1057"],["k:387","k:525"],["k:526","k:525"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"92 sits in the chapter praising sweet speech and may be that chapter's emphasis rather than a fixed ranking. Other verses define real giving by its recipient, not its manner (221, 223), and 387 and 525 treat giving as a means to loyalty, which is not the same claim as 92's ethical ranking.","issue":"'The book rarely praises giving bare' is overstated: most of chapter 23 (ஈகை) and 231 to 232 praise giving with no word on manner, which is more than the limits' recipient verses 221 and 223. 224 is about the giver's discomfort ending when the asker is satisfied, not about the manner of giving."},{"id":"thread:two_shames","title":"One word for two shames","claim":"1011 splits நாண் in two: real shame is shame at one's deeds, and the rest is the shame of women of fair brow (Parimelazhagar: restraint of mind, speech and body proper to women of family). The Book of Love's shame is the second kind: in 1163 and 1247 it is, on Parimelazhagar's reading, what stops the woman telling him or going to him, and in 1251 it is the bolt on the door of restraint. Yet the Book of Love prizes that shame in the first kind's terms: it is the ornament that makes jewels needless (1089, as moral shame is the ornament of the worthy in 1014), and it is held dearer than life (Parimelazhagar on 1297; 1163's life strained on the pole), which is the valuation 1017 gives moral shame.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:1011","k:1251"],["k:1089","k:1014"],["k:1297","k:1017"],["k:1163","k:1017"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"The love verses are dramatised speeches, and the bridge to 1014 and 1017 is partly the commentator's: 1297 itself does not say shame is dearer than life. Kalaignar reads 1014 as remorse for a fault and Pope translates நாண் in 1017 as modesty, so the line between the two shames is drawn differently by different readers.","issue":"1163 does not rank shame above life: on Parimelazhagar love and shame hang in equal balance (ஒன்றினொன்று மிகாது) on the life-pole, so it supports 1017's valuation only loosely."},{"id":"thread:shame_gives_way","title":"In the Book of Love shame never holds","claim":"Several lover-speakers set shame against desire, and in none of them does shame survive; what varies is how it goes. 1247 puts the choice to the heart (give up desire or shame, I cannot bear both); 1132 makes it by decision, setting shame aside to ride the palmyra horse; in 1134 the flood of love carries shame off and in 1251 the axe of desire breaks the door it bolted; in 1297 it is forgotten in his absence, and in 1257 it is simply not felt in his presence (Parimelazhagar: on his return from another woman). Read together, the dilemma 1247 states is settled by force, forgetting or reunion as often as by choice.","grade":"partly_supported","links":[["k:1132","k:1247"],["k:1134","k:1251"],["k:1247","k:1251"],["k:1257","k:1247"],["k:1297","k:1247"],["k:1257","k:1297"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"These are separate dramatised moments, and reading them as one sequence follows the chapter order, not any statement in the text. 1163 holds love and shame in balance without a loss, and 1149 is about shame at gossip rather than at desire. The man also loses shame by force in 1134, which qualifies the existing gender_asymmetry contrast between his choosing and her bearing.","issue":"The title 'never holds' overreaches the Book of Love: 1137 praises the woman who suffers a sea of desire and still does not mount the மடல் (கடலன்ன காமம் உழந்தும் மடலேறாப் பெண்), so her restraint holds there. The claim is accurate only for the verses it cites. The 1297-1247 note's 'the chapter between them' is wrong, since 1247 is in chapter 125 and 1297 in chapter 130."},{"id":"thread:inside_decides","title":"The inside decides what counts","claim":"Several verses in different domains apply one test: an outward act counts only if the mind behind it matches. Virtue is wholly a spotless mind and the rest is noisy show (34), and firmness in action is wholly firmness of mind with all else 'பிற' (661), in the same shape. Sweet speech is virtue because it comes from within (93), friendship is the smile of the heart, not the face (786), straight speech is rectitude only if there is no bend within (119), and truth spoken with the mind outranks austerity and giving (295, 294). Ritual bathing is the case in point: water reaches only the outside (298), and many bathe with impure minds (278).","grade":"supported","links":[["k:93","k:34"],["k:661","k:34"],["k:93","k:786"],["k:294","k:119"],["k:119","k:295"],["k:278","k:34"],["k:298","k:278"],["k:825","k:890"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"The book does not make the mind self-sufficient: 455 and 459 say purity and goodness of mind rest on purity of company. It also asks elsewhere whether the inside can be read from outside at all (thread:signs_and_faces), and 829 permits a show of friendship toward enemies (thread:ethics_vs_statecraft).","issue":"661 is about firmness, not about outward acts being genuine; it shares only the shape of 34. 298 does not say water reaches 'only' the outside; it pairs outer purity from water with inner purity seen through truth."},{"id":"thread:what_one_thinks_one_gets","title":"One promise, several conditions: you obtain what you think of","claim":"The book makes nearly the same promise, that one obtains what one has thought of (உள்ளியது / எண்ணிய ... எய்தும்), three times and attaches a different inner condition each time: never harbouring anger (309), keeping one's mind on the goal without forgetting (540), and firmness (666). Parimelazhagar assigns these to different people, 309 to the ascetic and 540 to the king, so one formula serves both Aram and Porul. The power of thought reaches suffering too: a flood of trouble perishes when the wise person merely thinks it through (622), yet 7 says the mind's anxiety is hard to remove except for those who reach the feet of the incomparable one.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:309","k:540"],["k:666","k:309"],["k:666","k:540"],["k:622","k:309"],["k:7","k:622"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"The three conditions are not rivals; commentators treat each as sufficient within its own chapter's concern. The 7-622 conflict holds only on Manakkudavar's wider reading of மனக்கவலை; Parimelazhagar narrows 7 to the sorrows of rebirth, which 622 does not address.","issue":""},{"id":"thread:lover_as_cure","title":"Is the beloved the cure for love's sickness?","claim":"Several verses make the one who causes the sickness its only medicine: one look in her eyes wounds and the other heals (1091), she is herself the medicine for the disease she gives (1102), and the woman says one day of his return would destroy all her sickness (1266). This explains why her heart can name no medicine at all in 1241, and 1275 finds the remedy only in a sign. Yet other verses deny that presence cures: union itself now holds the pain of fearing separation (1152), her eyes do not sleep whether he comes or not (1179), and her heart fears not having him and, having him, fears parting (1295).","grade":"partly_supported","links":[["k:1091","k:1102"],["k:1266","k:1102"],["k:1241","k:1102"],["k:1275","k:1091"],["k:1152","k:1295"],["k:1179","k:1295"],["k:1152","k:1179"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"The cure verses 1091 and 1102 belong to the man in the first meetings, while the denials are the woman's in a settled relationship facing separation, so the difference may track the stage of love rather than a contradiction. The speaker of 1275 is the man for Parimelazhagar and the friend for Manakkudavar.","issue":"1241 only asks the heart for a medicine (நினைத்தொன்று சொல்லாயோ); it does not say the heart can name none, and the claim's 'this explains why' reads 1102 into it. 1275 does not place the cure only in a sign of the beloved: on Parimelazhagar the medicine is the friend's assurance that the beloved will not part, and on Manakkudavar the friend is telling the man his suit will succeed."},{"id":"thread:blaming_own_organs","title":"The woman turns on her own eyes and heart","claim":"Where the man finds the source of love's sickness in her eyes (1091), the woman in separation finds it in her own: her eyes showed him to her (1171), and she mocks them as laughable (1173), rejoices that they now suffer the sickness they gave (1176), and curses them to weep until their water is gone, which 1174 reports has happened (1177). Her heart is treated the same way across later chapters: thinking of him alone, her heart is there only to eat her (1296), remembering burns it (1207), and she rebukes it for sitting and suffering over one who has no pity (1243).","grade":"supported","links":[["k:1171","k:1091"],["k:1176","k:1173"],["k:1177","k:1174"],["k:1243","k:1296"],["k:1296","k:1207"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"Chapters 118, 125 and 130 are built around addressing the eyes or the heart, so the pattern partly follows the book's chapter scheme; the claim is that the same self-division runs across them, not that it is a hidden theme. On Parimelazhagar's reading of 1296 the heart's harm comes from dwelling on his cruelties, not from memory as such.","issue":"1207 does not turn on the heart: in உள்ளினும் உள்ளம் சுடும் the heart is what is burned, the victim of memory and separation, so it fits the self-division theme less than 1243 and 1296, where the heart is blamed."},{"id":"thread:remembering_the_absent","title":"Does remembering the absent lover comfort or wound?","claim":"Chapter 121 opens by calling remembrance an unfailing joy: love is sweeter than toddy because even thinking of it gives delight that does not end (1201), and thinking of the beloved brings no pain (1202); the woman makes the same toddy comparison in 1281. The same chapter and later ones then make thinking the source of pain: even remembering burns (1207), her life wastes as she dwells on his want of grace (1209), and when she sits alone and thinks, her heart eats her (1296). Parimelazhagar separates the cases by what is remembered: past union in 1201, his neglect in 1209, his cruelties in 1296. Yet forgetting is refused as worse (1207, 1262), while 1250 argues that keeping him in her heart costs her beauty, the very loss 1262 attributes to forgetting.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:1201","k:1207"],["k:1202","k:1207"],["k:1202","k:1209"],["k:1202","k:1296"],["k:1296","k:1207"],["k:1201","k:1281"],["k:1207","k:1262"],["k:1250","k:1262"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"These are dramatised speeches in different moods, not a doctrine of memory, and the speaker assignments (man in 1201 and 1202) are Parimelazhagar's; Manakkudavar gives 1202 to the woman and reads it as conditional. The gender side of 1202/1207 is already in thread:gender_asymmetry.","issue":"On Parimelazhagar, what the woman remembers in 1207 is the past union (அவ்வின்பத்தை), the same object as 1201, and it is the separation that burns, so 'what is remembered' does not separate every case. He also reads 1250's கவின் as the inner beauty left after the outer is lost, so it is not quite 'the very loss' of 1262's காரிகை and bangles."},{"id":"thread:lover_in_her_heart","title":"He lives in her heart: a defence that becomes a doubt","claim":"In chapters 113 and 122 the woman answers the charge that her lover is absent and loveless by locating him inside herself: in her eyes (1126), in her heart gladly and always while the town says he lives apart (1129, 1130), on her shoulder in sleep and in her heart on waking (1218), so that her heart has no one to go looking for (1249). Parimelazhagar frames several of these as replies to the friend's blame of him. In chapter 121 the same image turns: he is always in her heart, but is she in his (1204)? He bars her from his heart and is not ashamed to keep entering hers (1205), and by 1243 his lack of any pitying thought is a settled premise. Her body also contradicts the claim of presence in 1184, where constant thought does not stop the pallor.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:1129","k:1130"],["k:1126","k:1218"],["k:1218","k:1249"],["k:1130","k:1204"],["k:1204","k:1205"],["k:1128","k:1205"],["k:1243","k:1204"],["k:1184","k:1130"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"The sequence is the reader's arrangement across chapters, not a stated argument; 1205's guarded heart is read as accusation by Manakkudavar. The thread does not claim the man's verses share this doubt.","issue":"1249 is in chapter 125, not 113 or 122. Parimelazhagar ties 1184 to her recalling his parting promises (தெளிவித்த சொற்கள்) and treats the pallor as the body betraying mind and speech; reading it as undercutting 'presence in her heart' is the writer's framing."},{"id":"thread:conduct_makes_family","title":"Is good family the source of conduct or its name?","claim":"133 defines good family as conduct (ஒழுக்கம் உடைமை குடிமை) and says a lapse becomes low birth, and 1019 agrees that a lapse in principle burns one's lineage. Chapter 96 runs the other way: integrity and shame are natural only to the well-born (951), the well-born do not slip in conduct, truth and shame (952), and a lapse only makes people doubt a man's lineage (958), keeping birth as the underlying fact. The selection verses rely on chapter 96's direction, asking for good family alongside shame when choosing an officer (502) or a friend (794); on 133's definition that first test cannot be checked apart from the second.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:133","k:951"],["k:952","k:133"],["k:958","k:133"],["k:502","k:133"],["k:133","k:1019"],["k:502","k:951"],["k:794","k:951"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"The conflict depends on reading 133 as dissolving birth into conduct; Parimelazhagar reads it within varna, as conduct keeping or losing a rank birth already gave, which removes it. This complements thread:birth_across_book (972 and the screening verses) rather than replacing it, since its 972 links are not in this packet.","issue":"Kalaignar reads 951, 952 and 502 in 133's direction (those who keep these are to be counted well-born), so chapter 96's 'birth first' direction is Parimelazhagar's and Manakkudavar's construal more than the bare Tamil. 958's failing is specifically நாரின்மை, lack of kindness (Parimelazhagar: not giving, harsh speech), not any lapse in conduct."},{"id":"thread:family_built_by_effort","title":"The family as something built, not inherited","claim":"Where chapter 96 treats குடி as inherited character, chapters 61 and 103 treat it as something kept up or raised by work: those who want their family to be a family must drive out sloth (602), the family lengthens through long effort and mature knowledge (1022), and for the slothful it dies down while faults multiply (604). The same double penalty on the family and in fault is attached to covetousness in 171, and 609 makes the decline reversible once a man ends sloth's rule over him. 1028 turns this against honour: the family-builder who idles and stands on dignity (மானம்) ruins the family, whereas 961, as Parimelazhagar glosses it, gives up even indispensable things that would lower one's birth.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:602","k:1022"],["k:1022","k:604"],["k:171","k:604"],["k:609","k:604"],["k:602","k:1028"],["k:961","k:1028"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"The two views are not set against each other by the text; one chapter says what a family is, the other what its members owe it, and 1022/1028 can be read as about a clan's prosperity rather than its rank. The 961/1028 tension depends on reading both as concerning the family's standing.","issue":"The opening contrast with chapter 96, where குடி is inherited, has no link of its own and is asserted rather than shown. Kalaignar reads 1028's மானம் as being shaken by insults rather than standing on dignity, which would weaken the 961 tension."},{"id":"thread:greatness_forfeited","title":"Can greatness survive one small lapse?","claim":"Several verses in different books grant a person great stature and then make it hang on a single act. 124 sets the restrained householder's stature above a mountain, and 974 says greatness exists only while one keeps hold of oneself; 965 states the loss as a rule, that even the hill-like sink (குன்றுவர்) for a lowering act the size of a kunri seed, while 144 (entering another's home) and 195 (idle speech) name particular acts that undo a person's worth however great. 898 uses the same hill pun for the opposite direction: the hill-like can bring others to nothing, so in the book's imagery height gives power over others but no protection against one's own lapse.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:124","k:965"],["k:974","k:965"],["k:144","k:965"],["k:195","k:965"],["k:898","k:965"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"The verses do not say greatness can never be regained; they only make its loss quick and cheap. Parimelazhagar reads the hill-like of 965 as those great by birth and those of 898 as ascetics, so the 'height' is not one kind of standing throughout.","issue":"On Manakkudavar, 898's குன்ற மதிப்பின் means others slighting the hill-like (அவமதித்தல்), so the hill-like are the ones diminished there too and the 'opposite direction' contrast holds only on Parimelazhagar's reading of them as ascetics who wish ruin."},{"id":"thread:greatness_by_deed","title":"Greatness measured by hard deeds, from renouncer to householder","claim":"26, in the chapter on renouncers, defines the great as those who do what is hard to do and the small as those who cannot; 975, in the Book of Wealth, says the same of those who have greatness, with no renunciation in view (Parimelazhagar adds: even when they are poor). 611 turns the definition into a process, saying effort itself produces the greatness a hard task needs, and 1021 puts the highest dignity in the resolve not to stop work. 505 and 972 generalise the test: deeds are the touchstone of greatness and smallness, and distinction differs by the work done though birth is alike.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:975","k:26"],["k:611","k:26"],["k:1021","k:611"],["k:505","k:972"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"Parimelazhagar narrows 26's 'hard things' to yogic disciplines and reads 26 as a ranking among those of equal birth, and he takes 965's hill-like as great by birth; the book elsewhere treats birth as a real factor (see thread:birth_across_book). 26 can also be read as a fixed difference between people, which 611 does not say.","issue":"Parimelazhagar's gloss on 505 counts birth, quality and knowledge (பிறப்பு குணம் அறிவு) as sources of greatness and makes deeds only the test of them, so 505 does not by itself say birth is irrelevant."},{"id":"thread:effort_never_wasted","title":"Effort that pays whatever fate does","claim":"Against 380, where fate outruns every plan, several verses give effort a return in each case fate can take. If fate denies the outcome, effort still pays the wage of the body's strain (619), and a high aim thrust aside keeps the quality of not having been thrust aside (596, which Parimelazhagar reads as thwarted by fate); if the divine helps, it girds itself and goes ahead of the one resolved to raise his family (1023), and for those who toil without delay the end comes of itself (1024). Persistence is said to trouble trouble itself (624) and to see even fate's back (620). Parimelazhagar draws the conclusion at 619: since effort is not wasted either way, one should strive without waiting on fate.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:380","k:619"],["k:596","k:619"],["k:1023","k:619"],["k:620","k:1024"],["k:624","k:620"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"This thread is about how the book secures effort's value, not whether effort is free; 371 makes effort itself a product of fate, a conflict taken up in thread:fate_and_agency. Parimelazhagar narrows 620 to fate blocking only one or two attempts.","issue":""},{"id":"thread:conditions_on_effort","title":"Effort praised without condition, then bounded","claim":"616 says flatly that effort makes fortune and its absence brings want, and 596 sets no ceiling on aspiration, but verses elsewhere fix effort's conditions. 468 says exertion not made by the right method collapses however many guard it; 476 says that pushing beyond the tip of the branch ends one's life; 14 makes the plough, which 1031 says the whole world follows, itself depend on the rain. 739 even defines the true land as one whose wealth comes without seeking, though 1040 has the land mock those who sit idle claiming poverty.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:468","k:616"],["k:476","k:596"],["k:14","k:1031"],["k:739","k:1040"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"None of these verses denies that effort is necessary; they add method, measure of strength and natural supply as further conditions. The fate-based limit (371) is treated separately in thread:fate_and_agency.","issue":"Parimelazhagar reads 596 as addressed to kings and takes its தள்ளினும் as thwarting by fate (பால்வகை), so 596 itself concedes a limit. 739 concerns a country's natural endowment for its people, not the sufficiency of individual effort, so its pairing with 1040 is loose."},{"id":"thread:sulk_versus_union","title":"Which is sweeter, union or the sulk?","claim":"The first union chapter sets sleeping on the beloved's soft arms above the lotus-eyed god's world (1103) and lists sulking, making up and uniting as love's rewards without ranking them (1109). The closing chapter re-ranks: sulking with one joined as water to earth outdoes heaven (1323), being kept from those same soft arms (தாம்வீழ்வார் மென்றோள், the phrase of 1103) 'has a certain something' (1325), and sulking is sweeter than union as digesting is sweeter than eating (1326). Yet the last verse makes the sulk's pleasure depend on the embrace that follows (கூடி முயங்கப் பெறின், 1330), and 1307 finds pain inside the sulk in the fear that union will be delayed, so the sulk outranks union only as a way back to it. Parimelazhagar already reads 1109's ஊடல் as needed so that union becomes sweet.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:1323","k:1103"],["k:1325","k:1103"],["k:1109","k:1326"],["k:1326","k:1330"],["k:1307","k:1330"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"These are lovers' speeches, mostly the man's in the joy-after-sulking chapter, not the author ranking pleasures in his own voice; and the ranking is of moments within one marriage, not of union against sulking as rival goods.","issue":"'The last verse' (1330) is Parimelazhagar's order; Manakkudavar ends the book at 1323. Manakkudavar takes the world in 1103 as Indra's heaven, while Parimelazhagar takes it as the world ascetics attain."},{"id":"thread:smallest_gap","title":"How small a gap counts as separation?","claim":"1108 calls sweet the embrace that not even a breeze can split (வளியிடை போழப் படாஅ). Later verses test that ideal and find the threshold of separation at exactly that size: a cool breeze splitting the embrace, with the same verb போழ, pales her eyes (1239); his loosening his arms pales her forehead (1238); she shifted slightly and pallor came thick enough to scoop up (1187); pallor watches for any lapse in the embrace as darkness watches for a lamp to fail (1186). The woman and the man report it from either side. On Parimelazhagar's reading the man recalls 1238 and 1239 on his way home from a campaign, so the tiny gap is the measure against which the present separation by hills and forests is felt.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:1108","k:1187"],["k:1187","k:1239"],["k:1239","k:1108"],["k:1186","k:1238"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"1108 itself says nothing of pallor, and Parimelazhagar frames it as a plea for marriage (union without interruption); the pallor verses are hyperbole from the separation chapters, not a separate doctrine.","issue":"Parimelazhagar reads 1238 and 1239 as one remembered incident (the breeze entered because he loosened his arms), so they are one threshold seen twice, not two. He reads 1186-1187 and 1238-1239 alike as a fortiori arguments: if this for a slight gap, what now in separation? That reading strengthens the claim."},{"id":"thread:eyes_tell_secrets","title":"Eyes that say what the mouth withholds","claim":"Across the Book of Love, man, woman and friend all treat the eyes as a channel that speaks where the mouth does not, and that cannot be kept quiet. When eyes agree, words are useless (1100); telling love's sickness with the eyes and pleading adds womanhood to womanhood (1280, which Parimelazhagar glosses as not saying it by mouth even to her friends); eyes like beaten drums let the town learn her secret (1180); though she hides it, her kohl-lined eyes go beyond her control and have something to say (1271); her pale, weeping eyes seem to tell others of his unkindness (1232). In love, concealment fails at the eyes.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:1100","k:1280"],["k:1180","k:1271"],["k:1232","k:1180"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"This holds for lovers' eyes; the Book of Wealth says enemies' smiles can hide hostility even from the face (824, see thread:signs_and_faces), so it is not a general claim that faces are honest. 1232 says only that the eyes seem to tell, and the speakers of 1100 and 1280 differ between Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar.","issue":"1100 and 1280 are about eyes chosen as the channel, not eyes that betray; the claim's 'cannot be kept quiet' rests on 1180, 1271 and 1232 alone."},{"id":"thread:seen_by_many","title":"What many eyes see is no longer one's own","claim":"The man disqualifies flowers as likenesses of her eyes because many people see them (பலர்காணும் பூ, 1112), and tells the moon it may resemble her face only if it stops appearing for many to see (பலர்காணத் தோன்றல், 1119); Parimelazhagar glosses both as his having her beauty for himself alone. The woman applies the same standard to him: she refuses his chest because all women feast on him in common with their eyes (கண்ணின் பொதுஉண்பர், 1311), which Parimelazhagar takes as making it others' leftovers, and she suspects even his gaze at her of being on another woman (1320). Looking is treated as a kind of possession, by both lovers.","grade":"partly_supported","links":[["k:1119","k:1112"],["k:1119","k:1311"],["k:1320","k:1311"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"1311 and 1320 come from the chapter on subtle sulking, where Parimelazhagar presents the woman's charges as pretexts for a lover's sulk, and 1112 and 1119 are praise hyperbole; neither side states a rule about fidelity.","issue":"Only two links carry the 'seen by many' standard; 1320 is about whom he is thinking of as he looks (யாருள்ளி நோக்கினீர்), not about being seen by many, so its fit with 'looking as possession' is loose."},{"id":"thread:one_birth_seven_births","title":"One birth's leverage on seven","claim":"Several verses across Aram and Porul share a fixed arithmetic: what is done in one birth (ஒருமை) settles the next seven (எழுமை). Restraint of the five senses (126) and learning (398) end in the identical formula எழுமையும் ஏமாப்புடைத்து, and blameless children keep evil off for seven births (62); the same reckoning runs the other way when the fool makes in one birth the mire he sinks in for seven (835) and neglect of what is praised leaves nothing through seven (538). Taken together they make a single life the lever on all later ones, for good or ill, and place sense-restraint, learning and children side by side as goods that outlast the body.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:126","k:398"],["k:835","k:398"],["k:126","k:835"],["k:62","k:398"],["k:62","k:835"],["k:538","k:835"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"This is a recurring formula in five verses, not a doctrine the book argues for. Parimelazhagar reads எழுமை as seven future births, while Kalaignar renders it as seven generations or simply all time, which removes rebirth from these verses.","issue":"Only 126, 398 and 835 pair ஒருமை with எழுமை; 62 (எழுபிறப்பும்) and 538 (எழுமையும்) give the seven-birth consequence without the one-birth term. 107 (எழுமை எழுபிறப்பும் உள்ளுவர்) is another எழுமை verse the thread does not mention."},{"id":"thread:births_to_secure_or_end","title":"Births: a span to secure or a cycle to end?","claim":"The chapters on renunciation and true knowledge treat birth itself as the evil: craving is its seed (361) and cutting attachment cuts it off (349); delusion produces a joyless birth (351) and birth is then called a folly (358); learning that sees the real leads to the path of not returning (356), and certain knowledge makes further birth unthinkable (357), against 339's endless round of sleep and waking. Elsewhere births are a span to protect, and the two attitudes meet over learning: in 398 it protects through seven births, in 356 it ends them. Likewise 73 gives the body-bond a purpose in love, while for one set on cutting births even the body is excess (345).","grade":"partly_supported","links":[["k:356","k:398"],["k:339","k:356"],["k:349","k:361"],["k:358","k:351"],["k:357","k:356"],["k:345","k:73"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"The verses never set the two attitudes against each other; on Parimelazhagar's reading they belong to different stages, the householder and the renouncer, so this is a shift of aim rather than a contradiction. Kalaignar reads 356 as not returning to household life, which drops rebirth from it.","issue":"The second attitude is thin: 398 values learning carried across births (எழுமையும் ஏமாப்பு), not births as a span to secure, and Kalaignar reads எழுமை as generations. 73 values the life-body bond as the fruit of love, which is the only real counterweight. The 'birth as evil' half is well supported; the contrast the title draws rests on two weak verses."},{"id":"thread:king_falls_by_himself","title":"Who guards the king?","claim":"Several verses across chapters 39, 45, 55 and 56 place a ruler's safety and ruin inside his own conduct rather than with enemies. Justice, which earns him the name இறை in 388, is what guards him in 547 if he practises it without fail. A king without rebukers perishes with no one to destroy him (448), and the unapproachable king who does not examine and do justice perishes by himself (548), while neglect of daily justice ruins his land day by day (553). The temperament that prevents this is the ability to bear bitter words (389), which Parimelazhagar reads as the speech of the rebuking companions of 447 and 448.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:388","k:547"],["k:547","k:448"],["k:548","k:448"],["k:548","k:553"],["k:389","k:448"],["k:389","k:447"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"The claim is about how these verses frame the king's fall; the book elsewhere treats foreign enemies and fortifications at length. Manakkudavar reads 389 differently, so the 389 links depend on Parimelazhagar.","issue":"553 has the land ruined day by day, not the king perishing by himself, so it widens the claim from the ruler's safety to the realm's."},{"id":"thread:sceptre_as_rain","title":"The sceptre as rain","claim":"Chapters 55 and 56 build the king's rule onto the image of rain. People live looking to the sceptre as the world lives looking to the sky (542); the just sceptre brings rain and harvest together (545), and the bent one stops the rain (559); the king's lack of kindness is to his people what drought is to the earth (557), and when the guardian does not guard, cows yield less and the learned forget their books (560), the books whose origin 543 makes the sceptre. Read together, the verses present just rule not only as like rain but, on Parimelazhagar's reading of 545 and 559, as a cause of the actual weather.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:559","k:545"],["k:542","k:557"],["k:560","k:543"],["k:390","k:557"],["k:528","k:542"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"Whether the weather verses are literal is a commentators' dispute: Kalaignar turns 545 into a comparison and 559 into neglected reservoirs. 542 and 557 are explicit comparisons and do not by themselves claim moral weather.","issue":"The links from 390 and 528 add little: 390 only lists அளி, and 528's 'live looking to' concerns kin and the king's recognition of rank, not rain."},{"id":"thread:enmity_shunned_and_sought","title":"Is enmity to be shunned or sought?","claim":"Chapters 86 to 88 give both answers. 871 says enmity, having no good in it, should not be wanted even in jest, and 852 and 855 praise not returning harm and leaning away from hostility. Yet 861 says to keep enmity with the weak, 867 says a certain man's enmity must be got even by paying for it, 879 says to fell the thorn tree while young, and 880 says those who do not break a foe's pride are not even alive, with 759 naming wealth as the blade for it. The same section prizes turning enmity into friendship (874), while 830 says an enemy who turns friend gets only one's face.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:861","k:871"],["k:871","k:867"],["k:879","k:852"],["k:880","k:759"],["k:855","k:874"],["k:874","k:830"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"Parimelazhagar reconciles 871 with 861 and 867 by limiting it to enmity that brings no gain (மாணாத பகை); the verses do not draw that line. Kalaignar reads 861 the other way, as fighting the strong rather than the weak, which removes one side of the conflict on his reading.","issue":"874 and 830 are less opposed than the claim suggests: Parimelazhagar reads 874's turning enmity to friendship as a king's policy used when needed (வேண்டியவழி), and Manakkudavar reads it as treating enemies like friends, which is close to 830's face-only friendship. Manakkudavar narrows 867 too, saying such a foe cannot do real harm, so the conflict with 871 is eased by both commentators, not by Parimelazhagar alone."},{"id":"thread:enemy_within","title":"The enemy inside is worse than the enemy outside","claim":"Across the chapters on ministers, the land, bad friendship and inner enmity, the book ranks the hidden or intimate enemy below the open one. 639 and 817 share one construction, setting crores of open enemies against a single plotting minister or a laughing friend; 882 gives the rule (fear not the sword-like foe but the kin-like one), 828 shows the weapon hidden in folded hands, and 735 adds inner enmity (உட்பகை) to 734's outside threats to a land.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:639","k:817"],["k:639","k:882"],["k:882","k:828"],["k:734","k:735"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"Parimelazhagar reads உறும் in 639 as 'that many enemies will arise' rather than 'is better', which weakens its parallel with 817. 734-735 only lists the inner enemy beside the outer one and does not rank them.","issue":"'Ranks the hidden enemy below the open one' means 'as worse than', which the wording leaves ambiguous. Chapter 89 (883, 884, 889) adds direct support the thread does not use."},{"id":"thread:reading_signs_court_and_love","title":"One art of reading signs, in court and in love","claim":"Chapters 71 and 110 share the title குறிப்பறிதல், and the verses apply one theory of the speaking eye and face in both books. The minister who knows the king's mind without being told (701), the eye that reads a sign by a sign (705), the face one need only stand before (708) and the eye that tells enmity and friendship (709) return in love as eyes that agree and make words useless (1100), eyes that speak against the beloved's will (1271, using the same verb உரை as 709), and the crystal image of 706 in 1273. In court the reading is a rare skill a subordinate exercises on a ruler; in love it is mutual and replaces speech.","grade":"partly_supported","links":[["k:701","k:1100"],["k:708","k:1100"],["k:709","k:1100"],["k:1100","k:705"],["k:1271","k:709"],["k:1273","k:706"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"The book does not cross-refer between the chapters; the parallel rests on shared title, vocabulary and images. Parimelazhagar adds for 708 that speaking would lessen both parties, a concern for dignity the love verses do not share.","issue":"The love-side verses 1271 and 1273 are not mutual: the man reads a sign she hides, and in 1273 Parimelazhagar has him admit he cannot read it and ask the friend. In court the reading is not only of a ruler: in 709 Parimelazhagar's ministers read foreign kings' eyes, and in 708 the one read is a petitioner. Parimelazhagar takes 1100's வாய்ச்சொற்கள் as words not meant in the heart, so the eyes expose speech rather than simply replacing it."},{"id":"thread:signs_and_faces","title":"Can signs be trusted?","claim":"The face shows what is strongest in the heart (706) and gets there before the mind (707), and the love books push this further: bangles know of the lover's parting before the woman does (1277), and desire breaks out like a sneeze without intent (1253). Yet signs can be worn against the heart: deceivers smile sweetly while bitter within (824, against 706), lovers put on hostile looks while close (1097), which complicates 709's reading of enmity in the eye, and in 1312 the woman takes his sneeze, the type of the involuntary in 1253, as calculated. The book holds both that the body betrays the heart and that it can be made to lie, and leaves the reader's skill to decide.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:824","k:706"],["k:1097","k:824"],["k:1097","k:709"],["k:707","k:1277"],["k:1312","k:1253"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"The feigned signs differ in kind: 824 is an enemy's deception, 1097 a lover's play, read with relief rather than fear. This extends the existing thread; its link 824-826 (enemies' words soon seen through) lies outside this packet and should be kept when merging, as should the note that 71 and 828 on tears do not conflict.","issue":"Parimelazhagar reads 1277's தணந்தமை as his mental separation while still physically with her, so the bangles detect an inner change, which strengthens the 'signs before mind' point."},{"id":"thread:sulk_defeated_by_heart","title":"Why the planned sulk always fails","claim":"Across four chapters of the Book of Love the woman sets out to sulk and her own heart defeats the plan. 1246 predicts it (the heart's anger is false heat, since it cannot hold out once it sees him), 1259 and 1284 report it as something that happened (she went to sulk; the heart went to unite), 1260 gives the reason as a general nature (hearts like fat in fire cannot say 'we will hold out in a sulk'), and 1294 draws the consequence that she will no longer plan with her heart. Together they treat ஊடல், the sulk that a lover's art requires, as a plan the speaker's own heart reliably breaks.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:1259","k:1284"],["k:1260","k:1259"],["k:1284","k:1246"],["k:1284","k:1294"],["k:1246","k:1294"],["k:1294","k:1260"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"These are dramatised speeches of one speaker and do not claim the sulk is never kept; the man's 1310 calls his own heart's wish to join mere craving, so the pattern is not confined to the woman, but this thread rests on her verses.","issue":"In 1246 it is the heart's own anger, refusing to go to him over his cruelty in Parimelazhagar's frame, that she calls false. So 1246 is not her plan to sulk but the heart's pretended sulk, which still fits the pattern."},{"id":"thread:whose_heart_is_whose","title":"Whose heart belongs to whom?","claim":"A complaint about the ownership of hearts runs from chapter 121 to the close of chapter 130. 1204 asks whether she has a place in his heart since he is always in hers; 1205 answers that he bars her from his heart yet keeps entering hers, and 1243 takes his lack of pitying thought as settled. 1291 turns the same imbalance inward: his heart stays his, so why is hers not hers? 1299 and 1300 generalise it (when one's own heart is no companion or kin, no stranger can be), and Parimelazhagar gives these two to the man, so the chapter ends with the complaint voiced from the other side.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:1204","k:1205"],["k:1243","k:1204"],["k:1205","k:1291"],["k:1300","k:1291"],["k:1299","k:1300"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"Pope reads 1205 the other way (he has imprisoned her in his heart), and Manakkudavar gives 1299 to the woman, which removes the two-sided ending. Elsewhere his dwelling in her heart is something she cherishes, not a grievance (1128, 1218, 1249).","issue":""},{"id":"thread:renown_as_remainder","title":"A life judged by what remains of it","claim":"Several verses measure a life by what is left after it (எச்சம்) and make renown that remainder: 238 calls it a disgrace to leave no legacy of renown, 1004 asks what the unwanted hoarder imagines will remain of him, and 233 says nothing but renown stands undying. The same logic reaches death in war: 777's warrior wants fame more than life, and 235, as Parimelazhagar glosses it, explains a death that is existence through the body of fame outliving the body of flesh; 970 shows the world praising those who choose death over disgrace, as 240 says a life without renown is no life.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:238","k:114"],["k:238","k:1004"],["k:1004","k:114"],["k:1004","k:233"],["k:777","k:235"],["k:970","k:235"],["k:240","k:970"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"The word எச்சம் is read differently: Parimelazhagar takes it as offspring in 114 but as surviving renown in 1004, so 114 belongs to the thread only on the Kalaignar-style reading. The link from 235 to the warrior depends on Parimelazhagar's gloss of a compressed verse.","issue":"970 is in the chapter on honour (மானம்): what it describes is refusing to live under disgrace and being praised for it, so it says nothing directly about fame outliving the body. The war-death strand depends on Parimelazhagar's gloss of 235 and also on reading 970 through 235."},{"id":"thread:sources_of_renown","title":"Many roads to renown, and one it may not take","claim":"The book names renown as the reward of different virtues: giving (231, 232), not lying (296, 'no renown like it'), forbearance (156) and dropping hostility (853, renown on Parimelazhagar's reading), and dying in battle (777, set beside giving in 231). 232 and 296 both speak as if naming the chief source, though Parimelazhagar narrows 296 to the cause of renown in this life. Then the Book of Wealth limits renown as a motive: 652 makes it one test of an act, but 962 says those who want honour will not do the unworthy even for renown, taking up the case 653 leaves open.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:232","k:296"],["k:777","k:231"],["k:233","k:156"],["k:853","k:156"],["k:962","k:652"],["k:653","k:962"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"The superlatives may be rhetorical rather than a ranking (compare thread:competing_supremes). 652 pairs renown with good (or, for Parimelazhagar, with virtue hereafter), so it never made renown alone sufficient; 962 sharpens rather than reverses it.","issue":"Parimelazhagar sends several of these roads back through giving: he grounds 233's unmatched renown in giving life, limbs and goods, and 853's renown in friends, wealth and then கொடை. On his reading the sources converge more than the claim suggests."},{"id":"thread:maanam_virtue_and_fault","title":"Honour as virtue and as fault","claim":"The same word மானம் is required of the king, paired with valour (384), and of the army (766), and chapter 97 ranks it above even what is indispensable (961, which Parimelazhagar reads as life itself). Yet 432 lists மாண்பிறந்த மானம், honour that has passed beyond the fine, among a ruler's faults, and 1028 says the one raising his family ruins it if he idles and minds his மானம். The book marks the line between the virtue and the fault by a qualifier or by context, not by a separate word, so the term cannot be read as uniformly praised.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:384","k:766"],["k:432","k:384"],["k:432","k:1028"],["k:961","k:1028"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"Commentators fill the boundary differently: Parimelazhagar makes 432's fault refusing to bow to elders and forcing through what was resolved, and 1028's a resentment at suffering for the family's sake, while Kalaignar reads 1028 as being shaken by insult. On those readings 961 and 1028 do not strictly conflict.","issue":"961 does not use the word மானம்; its place in the honour chapter and Parimelazhagar's gloss (lowering of one's birth) supply the link. 384's மானம் is also qualified (மறனிழுக்கா), so qualifiers mark the virtue as well as the fault."},{"id":"thread:life_ranked_below","title":"What is worth more than staying alive?","claim":"Four chapters rank continued life below another value in the same form: one gives up life rather than honour (969, 968, 970), rather than shame (1017), or to keep a vow in battle (779). 971 turns the ranking into a definition: disgrace is saying 'we will live without it'. Parimelazhagar gives the shared reason at 969: the body passes and honour lasts. Read together, these verses make 'choosing to live on' itself the mark of the lesser person across civil, military and inner virtue.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:969","k:779"],["k:968","k:1017"],["k:1017","k:969"],["k:970","k:971"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"971's 'it' is aspiration (Parimelazhagar's ஊக்கம்), not honour, and 779 concerns a soldier's vow. In the Book of Love 1132 sets shame aside to save life (see thread:love_overturns), and 963 and 988 answer shrunken circumstances with bearing, not death.","issue":"Parimelazhagar ties 968's பெருந்தகைமை to the honour that comes with high birth (உயர்குடிப் பிறப்பு), so on his reading part of this ranking belongs to the well-born rather than to everyone."},{"id":"thread:body_betrays_secret","title":"Who gives the secret away?","claim":"Although chapter 115 is about the town's gossip, verses across chapters 114, 116, 118, 124 and 126 locate the leak in the lover herself: her love reels through the street (1139), her eyes are beaten drums that make her secret easy to learn (1180), slipping bangles and wasted shoulders announce his leaving (1157, 1233, 1235), and the same line, மறையிறந்து மன்று படும், is said of love in general (1138) and then of herself after she thought herself firm (1254). Together they make the town's talk downstream of what her own body publishes.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:1139","k:1180"],["k:1254","k:1180"],["k:1254","k:1138"],["k:1232","k:1180"],["k:1157","k:1235"],["k:1233","k:1157"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"Most of these are framed as 'seems to' (போலும்) or rhetorical questions, and Parimelazhagar gives 1233 and 1235 to the friend (Manakkudavar gives 1233 to the woman). The thread does not claim she wishes the secret known.","issue":"Both commentators read 1157's bangles as telling the woman or her friend of his leaving (Parimelazhagar: உனக்கு அறிவியாவோ; Manakkudavar: எமக்கு அறிவியாவோ), not as broadcasting it to the town. 1139 has her love, not her body, reeling through the street."},{"id":"thread:blame_for_the_absent_lover","title":"Should the absent lover be blamed?","claim":"Several speeches have the woman rebut the town's verdict that he is loveless or has left her, by appeal to a presence it cannot see: in her unblinking eye (1129), her heart (1130) and her dreams (1219, 1220); Parimelazhagar frames 1129 and 1219 as replies to the friend's blaming him. But 1188 complains the opposite, that people talk only of her pallor and no one says he left her, and 1190 then accepts that name if only his unkindness is not spread. The friend's voice in 1232 and 1235 (on Parimelazhagar's assignment) says her body itself reports his unkindness. The book keeps her divided between wanting his fault named and shielding him from it.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:1129","k:1130"],["k:1129","k:1220"],["k:1220","k:1130"],["k:1140","k:1219"],["k:1188","k:1190"],["k:1190","k:1232"],["k:1235","k:1188"]],"books":["Kural"],"limits":"These are dramatised speeches in different situations, and 1188's 'no one' and 1220's townspeople need not be the same audience. On Parimelazhagar's reading the laughers of 1140 are the friend's teasing, not the town.","issue":"The 'wanting his fault named' side rests on 1188 alone, and this edition has no Parimelazhagar or Manakkudavar gloss for that verse, so its reading follows Pope and Kalaignar. In 1190 Parimelazhagar's தூற்றார் are நட்டோர, close friends, not the town."}]};
